if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-2701"]="This text is a combination of etexts, one from the now-defunct ERIS project at Virginia Tech and one from Project Gutenberg’s archives. The proofreaders of this version are indebted to The University of Adelaide Library for preserving the Virginia Tech version. The resulting etext was compared with a public domain hard copy version of the text.\n\n(Supplied by a Late Consumptive Usher to a Grammar School.)\n\nThe pale Usher—threadbare in coat, heart, body, and brain; I see him now. He was ever dusting his old lexicons and grammars, with a queer handkerchief, mockingly embellished with all the gay flags of all the known nations of the world. He loved to dust his old grammars; it somehow mildly reminded him of his mortality.\n\n“While you take in hand to school others, and to teach them by what name a whale-fish is to be called in our tongue, leaving out, through ignorance, the letter H, which almost alone maketh up the signification of the word, you deliver that which is not true.” —Hackluyt.\n\n“WHALE. * * * Sw. and Dan. hval. This animal is named from roundness or rolling; for in Dan. hvalt is arched or vaulted.” —Webster’s Dictionary.\n\n“WHALE. * * * It is more immediately from the Dut. and Ger. Wallen; A.S. Walw-ian, to roll, to wallow.” —Richardson’s Dictionary.\n\n‏חו‎, Hebrew. ϰητος, Greek. CETUS, Latin. WHŒL, Anglo-Saxon. HVALT, Danish. WAL, Dutch. HWAL, Swedish. HVALUR, Icelandic. WHALE, English. BALEINE, French. BALLENA, Spanish. PEKEE-NUEE-NUEE, Fegee. PEHEE-NUEE-NUEE, Erromangoan.\n\nEXTRACTS. (Supplied by a Sub-Sub-Librarian).\n\nIt will be seen that this mere painstaking burrower and grub-worm of a poor devil of a Sub-Sub appears to have gone through the long Vaticans and street-stalls of the earth, picking up whatever random allusions to whales he could anyways find in any book whatsoever, sacred or profane. Therefore you must not, in every case at least, take the higgledy-piggledy whale statements, however authentic, in these extracts, for veritable gospel cetology. Far from it. As touching the ancient authors generally, as well as the poets here appearing, these extracts are solely valuable or entertaining, as affording a glancing bird’s eye view of what has been promiscuously said, thought, fancied, and sung of Leviathan, by many nations and generations, including our own.\n\nSo fare thee well, poor devil of a Sub-Sub, whose commentator I am. Thou belongest to that hopeless, sallow tribe which no wine of this world will ever warm; and for whom even Pale Sherry would be too rosy-strong; but with whom one sometimes loves to sit, and feel poor-devilish, too; and grow convivial upon tears; and say to them bluntly, with full eyes and empty glasses, and in not altogether unpleasant sadness—Give it up, Sub-Subs! For by how much the more pains ye take to please the world, by so much the more shall ye for ever go thankless! Would that I could clear out Hampton Court and the Tuileries for ye! But gulp down your tears and hie aloft to the royal-mast with your hearts; for your friends who have gone before are clearing out the seven-storied heavens, and making refugees of long-pampered Gabriel, Michael, and Raphael, against your coming. Here ye strike but splintered hearts together—there, ye shall strike unsplinterable glasses!\n\n“And God created great whales.” —Genesis.\n\n“Leviathan maketh a path to shine after him; One would think the deep to be hoary.” —Job.\n\n“Now the Lord had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah.” —Jonah.\n\n“There go the ships; there is that Leviathan whom thou hast made to play therein.” —Psalms.\n\n“In that day, the Lord with his sore, and great, and strong sword, shall punish Leviathan the piercing serpent, even Leviathan that crooked serpent; and he shall slay the dragon that is in the sea.” —Isaiah.\n\n“And what thing soever besides cometh within the chaos of this monster’s mouth, be it beast, boat, or stone, down it goes all incontinently that foul great swallow of his, and perisheth in the bottomless gulf of his paunch.” —Holland’s Plutarch’s Morals.\n\n“The Indian Sea breedeth the most and the biggest fishes that are: among which the Whales and Whirlpooles called Balaene, take up as much in length as four acres or arpens of land.” —Holland’s Pliny.\n\n“Scarcely had we proceeded two days on the sea, when about sunrise a great many Whales and other monsters of the sea, appeared. Among the former, one was of a most monstrous size.... This came towards us, open-mouthed, raising the waves on all sides, and beating the sea before him into a foam.” —Tooke’s Lucian. “The True History.”\n\n“He visited this country also with a view of catching horse-whales, which had bones of very great value for their teeth, of which he brought some to the king.... The best whales were catched in his own country, of which some were forty-eight, some fifty yards long. He said that he was one of six who had killed sixty in two days.” —Other or Other’s verbal narrative taken down from his mouth by King Alfred, A.D. 890.\n\n“And whereas all the other things, whether beast or vessel, that enter into the dreadful gulf of this monster’s (whale’s) mouth, are immediately lost and swallowed up, the sea-gudgeon retires into it in great security, and there sleeps.” —MONTAIGNE. —Apology for Raimond Sebond.\n\n“Let us fly, let us fly! Old Nick take me if it is not Leviathan described by the noble prophet Moses in the life of patient Job.” —Rabelais.\n\n“This whale’s liver was two cartloads.” —Stowe’s Annals.\n\n“The great Leviathan that maketh the seas to seethe like boiling pan.” —Lord Bacon’s Version of the Psalms.\n\n“Touching that monstrous bulk of the whale or ork we have received nothing certain. They grow exceeding fat, insomuch that an incredible quantity of oil will be extracted out of one whale.” —Ibid. “History of Life and Death.”\n\n“The sovereignest thing on earth is parmacetti for an inward bruise.” —King Henry.\n\n“Very like a whale.” —Hamlet.\n\n“Which to secure, no skill of leach’s art Mote him availle, but to returne againe To his wound’s worker, that with lowly dart, Dinting his breast, had bred his restless paine, Like as the wounded whale to shore flies thro’ the maine.” —The Fairie Queen.\n\n“Immense as whales, the motion of whose vast bodies can in a peaceful calm trouble the ocean till it boil.” —Sir William Davenant. Preface to Gondibert.\n\n“What spermacetti is, men might justly doubt, since the learned Hosmannus in his work of thirty years, saith plainly, Nescio quid sit.” —Sir T. Browne. Of Sperma Ceti and the Sperma Ceti Whale. Vide his V. E.\n\n“Like Spencer’s Talus with his modern flail He threatens ruin with his ponderous tail. ... Their fixed jav’lins in his side he wears, And on his back a grove of pikes appears.” —Waller’s Battle of the Summer Islands.\n\n“By art is created that great Leviathan, called a Commonwealth or State—(in Latin, Civitas) which is but an artificial man.” —Opening sentence of Hobbes’s Leviathan.\n\n“Silly Mansoul swallowed it without chewing, as if it had been a sprat in the mouth of a whale.” —Pilgrim’s Progress.\n\n“That sea beast Leviathan, which God of all his works Created hugest that swim the ocean stream.” —Paradise Lost.\n\n—“There Leviathan, Hugest of living creatures, in the deep Stretched like a promontory sleeps or swims, And seems a moving land; and at his gills Draws in, and at his breath spouts out a sea.” —Ibid.\n\n“The mighty whales which swim in a sea of water, and have a sea of oil swimming in them.” —Fuller’s Profane and Holy State.\n\n“So close behind some promontory lie The huge Leviathan to attend their prey, And give no chance, but swallow in the fry, Which through their gaping jaws mistake the way.” —Dryden’s Annus Mirabilis.\n\n“While the whale is floating at the stern of the ship, they cut off his head, and tow it with a boat as near the shore as it will come; but it will be aground in twelve or thirteen feet water.” —Thomas Edge’s Ten Voyages to Spitzbergen, in Purchas.\n\n“In their way they saw many whales sporting in the ocean, and in wantonness fuzzing up the water through their pipes and vents, which nature has placed on their shoulders.” —Sir T. Herbert’s Voyages into Asia and Africa. Harris Coll.\n\n“Here they saw such huge troops of whales, that they were forced to proceed with a great deal of caution for fear they should run their ship upon them.” —Schouten’s Sixth Circumnavigation.\n\n“We set sail from the Elbe, wind N.E. in the ship called The Jonas-in-the-Whale.... Some say the whale can’t open his mouth, but that is a fable.... They frequently climb up the masts to see whether they can see a whale, for the first discoverer has a ducat for his pains.... I was told of a whale taken near Shetland, that had above a barrel of herrings in his belly.... One of our harpooneers told me that he caught once a whale in Spitzbergen that was white all over.” —A Voyage to Greenland, A.D. 1671. Harris Coll.\n\n“Several whales have come in upon this coast (Fife) Anno 1652, one eighty feet in length of the whale-bone kind came in, which (as I was informed), besides a vast quantity of oil, did afford 500 weight of baleen. The jaws of it stand for a gate in the garden of Pitferren.” —Sibbald’s Fife and Kinross.\n\n“Myself have agreed to try whether I can master and kill this Sperma-ceti whale, for I could never hear of any of that sort that was killed by any man, such is his fierceness and swiftness.” —Richard Strafford’s Letter from the Bermudas. Phil. Trans. A.D. 1668.\n\n“Whales in the sea God’s voice obey.” —N. E. Primer.\n\n“We saw also abundance of large whales, there being more in those southern seas, as I may say, by a hundred to one; than we have to the northward of us.” —Captain Cowley’s Voyage round the Globe, A.D. 1729.\n\n“... and the breath of the whale is frequently attended with such an insupportable smell, as to bring on a disorder of the brain.” —Ulloa’s South America.\n\n“To fifty chosen sylphs of special note, We trust the important charge, the petticoat. Oft have we known that seven-fold fence to fail, Tho’ stuffed with hoops and armed with ribs of whale.” —Rape of the Lock.\n\n“If we compare land animals in respect to magnitude, with those that take up their abode in the deep, we shall find they will appear contemptible in the comparison. The whale is doubtless the largest animal in creation.” —Goldsmith, Nat. Hist.\n\n“If you should write a fable for little fishes, you would make them speak like great whales.” —Goldsmith to Johnson.\n\n“In the afternoon we saw what was supposed to be a rock, but it was found to be a dead whale, which some Asiatics had killed, and were then towing ashore. They seemed to endeavor to conceal themselves behind the whale, in order to avoid being seen by us.” —Cook’s Voyages.\n\n“The larger whales, they seldom venture to attack. They stand in so great dread of some of them, that when out at sea they are afraid to mention even their names, and carry dung, lime-stone, juniper-wood, and some other articles of the same nature in their boats, in order to terrify and prevent their too near approach.” —Uno Von Troil’s Letters on Banks’s and Solander’s Voyage to Iceland in 1772.\n\n“The Spermacetti Whale found by the Nantuckois, is an active, fierce animal, and requires vast address and boldness in the fishermen.” —Thomas Jefferson’s Whale Memorial to the French minister in 1778.\n\n“And pray, sir, what in the world is equal to it?” —Edmund Burke’s reference in Parliament to the Nantucket Whale-Fishery.\n\n“Spain—a great whale stranded on the shores of Europe.” —Edmund Burke. (somewhere.)\n\n“A tenth branch of the king’s ordinary revenue, said to be grounded on the consideration of his guarding and protecting the seas from pirates and robbers, is the right to royal fish, which are whale and sturgeon. And these, when either thrown ashore or caught near the coast, are the property of the king.” —Blackstone.\n\n“Soon to the sport of death the crews repair: Rodmond unerring o’er his head suspends The barbed steel, and every turn attends.” —Falconer’s Shipwreck.\n\n“Bright shone the roofs, the domes, the spires, And rockets blew self driven, To hang their momentary fire Around the vault of heaven.\n\n“So fire with water to compare, The ocean serves on high, Up-spouted by a whale in air, To express unwieldy joy.” —Cowper, on the Queen’s Visit to London.\n\n“Ten or fifteen gallons of blood are thrown out of the heart at a stroke, with immense velocity.” —John Hunter’s account of the dissection of a whale. (A small sized one.)\n\n“The aorta of a whale is larger in the bore than the main pipe of the water-works at London Bridge, and the water roaring in its passage through that pipe is inferior in impetus and velocity to the blood gushing from the whale’s heart.” —Paley’s Theology.\n\n“The whale is a mammiferous animal without hind feet.” —Baron Cuvier.\n\n“In 40 degrees south, we saw Spermacetti Whales, but did not take any till the first of May, the sea being then covered with them.” —Colnett’s Voyage for the Purpose of Extending the Spermaceti Whale Fishery.\n\n“In the free element beneath me swam, Floundered and dived, in play, in chace, in battle, Fishes of every colour, form, and kind; Which language cannot paint, and mariner Had never seen; from dread Leviathan To insect millions peopling every wave: Gather’d in shoals immense, like floating islands, Led by mysterious instincts through that waste And trackless region, though on every side Assaulted by voracious enemies, Whales, sharks, and monsters, arm’d in front or jaw, With swords, saws, spiral horns, or hooked fangs.” —Montgomery’s World before the Flood.\n\n“Io! Paean! Io! sing. To the finny people’s king. Not a mightier whale than this In the vast Atlantic is; Not a fatter fish than he, Flounders round the Polar Sea.” —Charles Lamb’s Triumph of the Whale.\n\n“In the year 1690 some persons were on a high hill observing the whales spouting and sporting with each other, when one observed: there—pointing to the sea—is a green pasture where our children’s grand-children will go for bread.” —Obed Macy’s History of Nantucket.\n\n“I built a cottage for Susan and myself and made a gateway in the form of a Gothic Arch, by setting up a whale’s jaw bones.” —Hawthorne’s Twice Told Tales.\n\n“She came to bespeak a monument for her first love, who had been killed by a whale in the Pacific ocean, no less than forty years ago.” —Ibid.\n\n“No, Sir, ’tis a Right Whale,” answered Tom; “I saw his sprout; he threw up a pair of as pretty rainbows as a Christian would wish to look at. He’s a raal oil-butt, that fellow!” —Cooper’s Pilot.\n\n“The papers were brought in, and we saw in the Berlin Gazette that whales had been introduced on the stage there.” —Eckermann’s Conversations with Goethe.\n\n“My God! Mr. Chace, what is the matter?” I answered, “we have been stove by a whale.” —“Narrative of the Shipwreck of the Whale Ship Essex of Nantucket, which was attacked and finally destroyed by a large Sperm Whale in the Pacific Ocean.” By Owen Chace of Nantucket, first mate of said vessel. New York, 1821.\n\n“A mariner sat in the shrouds one night, The wind was piping free; Now bright, now dimmed, was the moonlight pale, And the phospher gleamed in the wake of the whale, As it floundered in the sea.” —Elizabeth Oakes Smith.\n\n“The quantity of line withdrawn from the boats engaged in the capture of this one whale, amounted altogether to 10,440 yards or nearly six English miles....\n\n“Sometimes the whale shakes its tremendous tail in the air, which, cracking like a whip, resounds to the distance of three or four miles.” —Scoresby.\n\n“Mad with the agonies he endures from these fresh attacks, the infuriated Sperm Whale rolls over and over; he rears his enormous head, and with wide expanded jaws snaps at everything around him; he rushes at the boats with his head; they are propelled before him with vast swiftness, and sometimes utterly destroyed.... It is a matter of great astonishment that the consideration of the habits of so interesting, and, in a commercial point of view, so important an animal (as the Sperm Whale) should have been so entirely neglected, or should have excited so little curiosity among the numerous, and many of them competent observers, that of late years, must have possessed the most abundant and the most convenient opportunities of witnessing their habitudes.” —Thomas Beale’s History of the Sperm Whale, 1839.\n\n“The Cachalot” (Sperm Whale) “is not only better armed than the True Whale” (Greenland or Right Whale) “in possessing a formidable weapon at either extremity of its body, but also more frequently displays a disposition to employ these weapons offensively and in manner at once so artful, bold, and mischievous, as to lead to its being regarded as the most dangerous to attack of all the known species of the whale tribe.” —Frederick Debell Bennett’s Whaling Voyage Round the Globe, 1840.\n\nOctober 13. “There she blows,” was sung out from the mast-head. “Where away?” demanded the captain. “Three points off the lee bow, sir.” “Raise up your wheel. Steady!” “Steady, sir.” “Mast-head ahoy! Do you see that whale now?” “Ay ay, sir! A shoal of Sperm Whales! There she blows! There she breaches!” “Sing out! sing out every time!” “Ay Ay, sir! There she blows! there—there—thar she blows—bowes—bo-o-os!” “How far off?” “Two miles and a half.” “Thunder and lightning! so near! Call all hands.” —J. Ross Browne’s Etchings of a Whaling Cruize. 1846.\n\n“The Whale-ship Globe, on board of which vessel occurred the horrid transactions we are about to relate, belonged to the island of Nantucket.” —“Narrative of the Globe Mutiny,” by Lay and Hussey survivors. A.D. 1828.\n\nBeing once pursued by a whale which he had wounded, he parried the assault for some time with a lance; but the furious monster at length rushed on the boat; himself and comrades only being preserved by leaping into the water when they saw the onset was inevitable.” —Missionary Journal of Tyerman and Bennett.\n\n“Nantucket itself,” said Mr. Webster, “is a very striking and peculiar portion of the National interest. There is a population of eight or nine thousand persons living here in the sea, adding largely every year to the National wealth by the boldest and most persevering industry.” —Report of Daniel Webster’s Speech in the U. S. Senate, on the application for the Erection of a Breakwater at Nantucket. 1828.\n\n“The whale fell directly over him, and probably killed him in a moment.” —“The Whale and his Captors, or The Whaleman’s Adventures and the Whale’s Biography, gathered on the Homeward Cruise of the Commodore Preble.” By Rev. Henry T. Cheever.\n\n“If you make the least damn bit of noise,” replied Samuel, “I will send you to hell.” —Life of Samuel Comstock (the mutineer), by his brother, William Comstock. Another Version of the whale-ship Globe narrative.\n\n“The voyages of the Dutch and English to the Northern Ocean, in order, if possible, to discover a passage through it to India, though they failed of their main object, laid-open the haunts of the whale.” —McCulloch’s Commercial Dictionary.\n\n“These things are reciprocal; the ball rebounds, only to bound forward again; for now in laying open the haunts of the whale, the whalemen seem to have indirectly hit upon new clews to that same mystic North-West Passage.” —From “Something” unpublished.\n\n“It is impossible to meet a whale-ship on the ocean without being struck by her near appearance. The vessel under short sail, with look-outs at the mast-heads, eagerly scanning the wide expanse around them, has a totally different air from those engaged in regular voyage.” —Currents and Whaling. U.S. Ex. Ex.\n\n“Pedestrians in the vicinity of London and elsewhere may recollect having seen large curved bones set upright in the earth, either to form arches over gateways, or entrances to alcoves, and they may perhaps have been told that these were the ribs of whales.” —Tales of a Whale Voyager to the Arctic Ocean.\n\n“It was not till the boats returned from the pursuit of these whales, that the whites saw their ship in bloody possession of the savages enrolled among the crew.” —Newspaper Account of the Taking and Retaking of the Whale-Ship Hobomack.\n\n“It is generally well known that out of the crews of Whaling vessels (American) few ever return in the ships on board of which they departed.” —Cruise in a Whale Boat.\n\n“Suddenly a mighty mass emerged from the water, and shot up perpendicularly into the air. It was the whale.” —Miriam Coffin or the Whale Fisherman.\n\n“The Whale is harpooned to be sure; but bethink you, how you would manage a powerful unbroken colt, with the mere appliance of a rope tied to the root of his tail.” —A Chapter on Whaling in Ribs and Trucks.\n\n“On one occasion I saw two of these monsters (whales) probably male and female, slowly swimming, one after the other, within less than a stone’s throw of the shore” (Terra Del Fuego), “over which the beech tree extended its branches.” —Darwin’s Voyage of a Naturalist.\n\n“‘Stern all!’ exclaimed the mate, as upon turning his head, he saw the distended jaws of a large Sperm Whale close to the head of the boat, threatening it with instant destruction;—‘Stern all, for your lives!’” —Wharton the Whale Killer.\n\n“So be cheery, my lads, let your hearts never fail, While the bold harpooneer is striking the whale!” —Nantucket Song.\n\n“Oh, the rare old Whale, mid storm and gale In his ocean home will be A giant in might, where might is right, And King of the boundless sea.” —Whale Song.\n\n# CHAPTER 1. Loomings.\n\nCall me Ishmael. Some years ago—never mind how long precisely—having little or no money in my purse, and nothing particular to interest me on shore, I thought I would sail about a little and see the watery part of the world. It is a way I have of driving off the spleen and regulating the circulation. Whenever I find myself growing grim about the mouth; whenever it is a damp, drizzly November in my soul; whenever I find myself involuntarily pausing before coffin warehouses, and bringing up the rear of every funeral I meet; and especially whenever my hypos get such an upper hand of me, that it requires a strong moral principle to prevent me from deliberately stepping into the street, and methodically knocking people’s hats off—then, I account it high time to get to sea as soon as I can. This is my substitute for pistol and ball. With a philosophical flourish Cato throws himself upon his sword; I quietly take to the ship. There is nothing surprising in this. If they but knew it, almost all men in their degree, some time or other, cherish very nearly the same feelings towards the ocean with me.\n\nThere now is your insular city of the Manhattoes, belted round by wharves as Indian isles by coral reefs—commerce surrounds it with her surf. Right and left, the streets take you waterward. Its extreme downtown is the battery, where that noble mole is washed by waves, and cooled by breezes, which a few hours previous were out of sight of land. Look at the crowds of water-gazers there.\n\nCircumambulate the city of a dreamy Sabbath afternoon. Go from Corlears Hook to Coenties Slip, and from thence, by Whitehall, northward. What do you see?—Posted like silent sentinels all around the town, stand thousands upon thousands of mortal men fixed in ocean reveries. Some leaning against the spiles; some seated upon the pier-heads; some looking over the bulwarks of ships from China; some high aloft in the rigging, as if striving to get a still better seaward peep. But these are all landsmen; of week days pent up in lath and plaster—tied to counters, nailed to benches, clinched to desks. How then is this? Are the green fields gone? What do they here?\n\nBut look! here come more crowds, pacing straight for the water, and seemingly bound for a dive. Strange! Nothing will content them but the extremest limit of the land; loitering under the shady lee of yonder warehouses will not suffice. No. They must get just as nigh the water as they possibly can without falling in. And there they stand—miles of them—leagues. Inlanders all, they come from lanes and alleys, streets and avenues—north, east, south, and west. Yet here they all unite. Tell me, does the magnetic virtue of the needles of the compasses of all those ships attract them thither?\n\nOnce more. Say you are in the country; in some high land of lakes. Take almost any path you please, and ten to one it carries you down in a dale, and leaves you there by a pool in the stream. There is magic in it. Let the most absent-minded of men be plunged in his deepest reveries—stand that man on his legs, set his feet a-going, and he will infallibly lead you to water, if water there be in all that region. Should you ever be athirst in the great American desert, try this experiment, if your caravan happen to be supplied with a metaphysical professor. Yes, as every one knows, meditation and water are wedded for ever.\n\nBut here is an artist. He desires to paint you the dreamiest, shadiest, quietest, most enchanting bit of romantic landscape in all the valley of the Saco. What is the chief element he employs? There stand his trees, each with a hollow trunk, as if a hermit and a crucifix were within; and here sleeps his meadow, and there sleep his cattle; and up from yonder cottage goes a sleepy smoke. Deep into distant woodlands winds a mazy way, reaching to overlapping spurs of mountains bathed in their hill-side blue. But though the picture lies thus tranced, and though this pine-tree shakes down its sighs like leaves upon this shepherd’s head, yet all were vain, unless the shepherd’s eye were fixed upon the magic stream before him. Go visit the Prairies in June, when for scores on scores of miles you wade knee-deep among Tiger-lilies—what is the one charm wanting?—Water—there is not a drop of water there! Were Niagara but a cataract of sand, would you travel your thousand miles to see it? Why did the poor poet of Tennessee, upon suddenly receiving two handfuls of silver, deliberate whether to buy him a coat, which he sadly needed, or invest his money in a pedestrian trip to Rockaway Beach? Why is almost every robust healthy boy with a robust healthy soul in him, at some time or other crazy to go to sea? Why upon your first voyage as a passenger, did you yourself feel such a mystical vibration, when first told that you and your ship were now out of sight of land? Why did the old Persians hold the sea holy? Why did the Greeks give it a separate deity, and own brother of Jove? Surely all this is not without meaning. And still deeper the meaning of that story of Narcissus, who because he could not grasp the tormenting, mild image he saw in the fountain, plunged into it and was drowned. But that same image, we ourselves see in all rivers and oceans. It is the image of the ungraspable phantom of life; and this is the key to it all.\n\nNow, when I say that I am in the habit of going to sea whenever I begin to grow hazy about the eyes, and begin to be over conscious of my lungs, I do not mean to have it inferred that I ever go to sea as a passenger. For to go as a passenger you must needs have a purse, and a purse is but a rag unless you have something in it. Besides, passengers get sea-sick—grow quarrelsome—don’t sleep of nights—do not enjoy themselves much, as a general thing;—no, I never go as a passenger; nor, though I am something of a salt, do I ever go to sea as a Commodore, or a Captain, or a Cook. I abandon the glory and distinction of such offices to those who like them. For my part, I abominate all honorable respectable toils, trials, and tribulations of every kind whatsoever. It is quite as much as I can do to take care of myself, without taking care of ships, barques, brigs, schooners, and what not. And as for going as cook,—though I confess there is considerable glory in that, a cook being a sort of officer on ship-board—yet, somehow, I never fancied broiling fowls;—though once broiled, judiciously buttered, and judgmatically salted and peppered, there is no one who will speak more respectfully, not to say reverentially, of a broiled fowl than I will. It is out of the idolatrous dotings of the old Egyptians upon broiled ibis and roasted river horse, that you see the mummies of those creatures in their huge bake-houses the pyramids.\n\nNo, when I go to sea, I go as a simple sailor, right before the mast, plumb down into the forecastle, aloft there to the royal mast-head. True, they rather order me about some, and make me jump from spar to spar, like a grasshopper in a May meadow. And at first, this sort of thing is unpleasant enough. It touches one’s sense of honor, particularly if you come of an old established family in the land, the Van Rensselaers, or Randolphs, or Hardicanutes. And more than all, if just previous to putting your hand into the tar-pot, you have been lording it as a country schoolmaster, making the tallest boys stand in awe of you. The transition is a keen one, I assure you, from a schoolmaster to a sailor, and requires a strong decoction of Seneca and the Stoics to enable you to grin and bear it. But even this wears off in time.\n\nWhat of it, if some old hunks of a sea-captain orders me to get a broom and sweep down the decks? What does that indignity amount to, weighed, I mean, in the scales of the New Testament? Do you think the archangel Gabriel thinks anything the less of me, because I promptly and respectfully obey that old hunks in that particular instance? Who ain’t a slave? Tell me that. Well, then, however the old sea-captains may order me about—however they may thump and punch me about, I have the satisfaction of knowing that it is all right; that everybody else is one way or other served in much the same way—either in a physical or metaphysical point of view, that is; and so the universal thump is passed round, and all hands should rub each other’s shoulder-blades, and be content.\n\nAgain, I always go to sea as a sailor, because they make a point of paying me for my trouble, whereas they never pay passengers a single penny that I ever heard of. On the contrary, passengers themselves must pay. And there is all the difference in the world between paying and being paid. The act of paying is perhaps the most uncomfortable infliction that the two orchard thieves entailed upon us. But being paid,—what will compare with it? The urbane activity with which a man receives money is really marvellous, considering that we so earnestly believe money to be the root of all earthly ills, and that on no account can a monied man enter heaven. Ah! how cheerfully we consign ourselves to perdition!\n\nFinally, I always go to sea as a sailor, because of the wholesome exercise and pure air of the fore-castle deck. For as in this world, head winds are far more prevalent than winds from astern (that is, if you never violate the Pythagorean maxim), so for the most part the Commodore on the quarter-deck gets his atmosphere at second hand from the sailors on the forecastle. He thinks he breathes it first; but not so. In much the same way do the commonalty lead their leaders in many other things, at the same time that the leaders little suspect it. But wherefore it was that after having repeatedly smelt the sea as a merchant sailor, I should now take it into my head to go on a whaling voyage; this the invisible police officer of the Fates, who has the constant surveillance of me, and secretly dogs me, and influences me in some unaccountable way—he can better answer than any one else. And, doubtless, my going on this whaling voyage, formed part of the grand programme of Providence that was drawn up a long time ago. It came in as a sort of brief interlude and solo between more extensive performances. I take it that this part of the bill must have run something like this:\n\n“Grand Contested Election for the Presidency of the United States. “WHALING VOYAGE BY ONE ISHMAEL. “BLOODY BATTLE IN AFFGHANISTAN.”\n\nThough I cannot tell why it was exactly that those stage managers, the Fates, put me down for this shabby part of a whaling voyage, when others were set down for magnificent parts in high tragedies, and short and easy parts in genteel comedies, and jolly parts in farces—though I cannot tell why this was exactly; yet, now that I recall all the circumstances, I think I can see a little into the springs and motives which being cunningly presented to me under various disguises, induced me to set about performing the part I did, besides cajoling me into the delusion that it was a choice resulting from my own unbiased freewill and discriminating judgment.\n\nChief among these motives was the overwhelming idea of the great whale himself. Such a portentous and mysterious monster roused all my curiosity. Then the wild and distant seas where he rolled his island bulk; the undeliverable, nameless perils of the whale; these, with all the attending marvels of a thousand Patagonian sights and sounds, helped to sway me to my wish. With other men, perhaps, such things would not have been inducements; but as for me, I am tormented with an everlasting itch for things remote. I love to sail forbidden seas, and land on barbarous coasts. Not ignoring what is good, I am quick to perceive a horror, and could still be social with it—would they let me—since it is but well to be on friendly terms with all the inmates of the place one lodges in.\n\nBy reason of these things, then, the whaling voyage was welcome; the great flood-gates of the wonder-world swung open, and in the wild conceits that swayed me to my purpose, two and two there floated into my inmost soul, endless processions of the whale, and, mid most of them all, one grand hooded phantom, like a snow hill in the air.\n\n# CHAPTER 2. The Carpet-Bag.\n\nI stuffed a shirt or two into my old carpet-bag, tucked it under my arm, and started for Cape Horn and the Pacific. Quitting the good city of old Manhatto, I duly arrived in New Bedford. It was a Saturday night in December. Much was I disappointed upon learning that the little packet for Nantucket had already sailed, and that no way of reaching that place would offer, till the following Monday.\n\nAs most young candidates for the pains and penalties of whaling stop at this same New Bedford, thence to embark on their voyage, it may as well be related that I, for one, had no idea of so doing. For my mind was made up to sail in no other than a Nantucket craft, because there was a fine, boisterous something about everything connected with that famous old island, which amazingly pleased me. Besides though New Bedford has of late been gradually monopolising the business of whaling, and though in this matter poor old Nantucket is now much behind her, yet Nantucket was her great original—the Tyre of this Carthage;—the place where the first dead American whale was stranded. Where else but from Nantucket did those aboriginal whalemen, the Red-Men, first sally out in canoes to give chase to the Leviathan? And where but from Nantucket, too, did that first adventurous little sloop put forth, partly laden with imported cobblestones—so goes the story—to throw at the whales, in order to discover when they were nigh enough to risk a harpoon from the bowsprit?\n\nNow having a night, a day, and still another night following before me in New Bedford, ere I could embark for my destined port, it became a matter of concernment where I was to eat and sleep meanwhile. It was a very dubious-looking, nay, a very dark and dismal night, bitingly cold and cheerless. I knew no one in the place. With anxious grapnels I had sounded my pocket, and only brought up a few pieces of silver,—So, wherever you go, Ishmael, said I to myself, as I stood in the middle of a dreary street shouldering my bag, and comparing the gloom towards the north with the darkness towards the south—wherever in your wisdom you may conclude to lodge for the night, my dear Ishmael, be sure to inquire the price, and don’t be too particular.\n\nWith halting steps I paced the streets, and passed the sign of “The Crossed Harpoons”—but it looked too expensive and jolly there. Further on, from the bright red windows of the “Sword-Fish Inn,” there came such fervent rays, that it seemed to have melted the packed snow and ice from before the house, for everywhere else the congealed frost lay ten inches thick in a hard, asphaltic pavement,—rather weary for me, when I struck my foot against the flinty projections, because from hard, remorseless service the soles of my boots were in a most miserable plight. Too expensive and jolly, again thought I, pausing one moment to watch the broad glare in the street, and hear the sounds of the tinkling glasses within. But go on, Ishmael, said I at last; don’t you hear? get away from before the door; your patched boots are stopping the way. So on I went. I now by instinct followed the streets that took me waterward, for there, doubtless, were the cheapest, if not the cheeriest inns.\n\nSuch dreary streets! blocks of blackness, not houses, on either hand, and here and there a candle, like a candle moving about in a tomb. At this hour of the night, of the last day of the week, that quarter of the town proved all but deserted. But presently I came to a smoky light proceeding from a low, wide building, the door of which stood invitingly open. It had a careless look, as if it were meant for the uses of the public; so, entering, the first thing I did was to stumble over an ash-box in the porch. Ha! thought I, ha, as the flying particles almost choked me, are these ashes from that destroyed city, Gomorrah? But “The Crossed Harpoons,” and “The Sword-Fish?”—this, then must needs be the sign of “The Trap.” However, I picked myself up and hearing a loud voice within, pushed on and opened a second, interior door.\n\nIt seemed the great Black Parliament sitting in Tophet. A hundred black faces turned round in their rows to peer; and beyond, a black Angel of Doom was beating a book in a pulpit. It was a negro church; and the preacher’s text was about the blackness of darkness, and the weeping and wailing and teeth-gnashing there. Ha, Ishmael, muttered I, backing out, Wretched entertainment at the sign of ‘The Trap!’\n\nMoving on, I at last came to a dim sort of light not far from the docks, and heard a forlorn creaking in the air; and looking up, saw a swinging sign over the door with a white painting upon it, faintly representing a tall straight jet of misty spray, and these words underneath—“The Spouter Inn:—Peter Coffin.”\n\nCoffin?—Spouter?—Rather ominous in that particular connexion, thought I. But it is a common name in Nantucket, they say, and I suppose this Peter here is an emigrant from there. As the light looked so dim, and the place, for the time, looked quiet enough, and the dilapidated little wooden house itself looked as if it might have been carted here from the ruins of some burnt district, and as the swinging sign had a poverty-stricken sort of creak to it, I thought that here was the very spot for cheap lodgings, and the best of pea coffee.\n\nIt was a queer sort of place—a gable-ended old house, one side palsied as it were, and leaning over sadly. It stood on a sharp bleak corner, where that tempestuous wind Euroclydon kept up a worse howling than ever it did about poor Paul’s tossed craft. Euroclydon, nevertheless, is a mighty pleasant zephyr to any one in-doors, with his feet on the hob quietly toasting for bed. “In judging of that tempestuous wind called Euroclydon,” says an old writer—of whose works I possess the only copy extant—“it maketh a marvellous difference, whether thou lookest out at it from a glass window where the frost is all on the outside, or whether thou observest it from that sashless window, where the frost is on both sides, and of which the wight Death is the only glazier.” True enough, thought I, as this passage occurred to my mind—old black-letter, thou reasonest well. Yes, these eyes are windows, and this body of mine is the house. What a pity they didn’t stop up the chinks and the crannies though, and thrust in a little lint here and there. But it’s too late to make any improvements now. The universe is finished; the copestone is on, and the chips were carted off a million years ago. Poor Lazarus there, chattering his teeth against the curbstone for his pillow, and shaking off his tatters with his shiverings, he might plug up both ears with rags, and put a corn-cob into his mouth, and yet that would not keep out the tempestuous Euroclydon. Euroclydon! says old Dives, in his red silken wrapper—(he had a redder one afterwards) pooh, pooh! What a fine frosty night; how Orion glitters; what northern lights! Let them talk of their oriental summer climes of everlasting conservatories; give me the privilege of making my own summer with my own coals.\n\nBut what thinks Lazarus? Can he warm his blue hands by holding them up to the grand northern lights? Would not Lazarus rather be in Sumatra than here? Would he not far rather lay him down lengthwise along the line of the equator; yea, ye gods! go down to the fiery pit itself, in order to keep out this frost?\n\nNow, that Lazarus should lie stranded there on the curbstone before the door of Dives, this is more wonderful than that an iceberg should be moored to one of the Moluccas. Yet Dives himself, he too lives like a Czar in an ice palace made of frozen sighs, and being a president of a temperance society, he only drinks the tepid tears of orphans.\n\nBut no more of this blubbering now, we are going a-whaling, and there is plenty of that yet to come. Let us scrape the ice from our frosted feet, and see what sort of a place this “Spouter” may be.\n\n# CHAPTER 3. The Spouter-Inn.\n\nEntering that gable-ended Spouter-Inn, you found yourself in a wide, low, straggling entry with old-fashioned wainscots, reminding one of the bulwarks of some condemned old craft. On one side hung a very large oilpainting so thoroughly besmoked, and every way defaced, that in the unequal crosslights by which you viewed it, it was only by diligent study and a series of systematic visits to it, and careful inquiry of the neighbors, that you could any way arrive at an understanding of its purpose. Such unaccountable masses of shades and shadows, that at first you almost thought some ambitious young artist, in the time of the New England hags, had endeavored to delineate chaos bewitched. But by dint of much and earnest contemplation, and oft repeated ponderings, and especially by throwing open the little window towards the back of the entry, you at last come to the conclusion that such an idea, however wild, might not be altogether unwarranted.\n\nBut what most puzzled and confounded you was a long, limber, portentous, black mass of something hovering in the centre of the picture over three blue, dim, perpendicular lines floating in a nameless yeast. A boggy, soggy, squitchy picture truly, enough to drive a nervous man distracted. Yet was there a sort of indefinite, half-attained, unimaginable sublimity about it that fairly froze you to it, till you involuntarily took an oath with yourself to find out what that marvellous painting meant. Ever and anon a bright, but, alas, deceptive idea would dart you through.—It’s the Black Sea in a midnight gale.—It’s the unnatural combat of the four primal elements.—It’s a blasted heath.—It’s a Hyperborean winter scene.—It’s the breaking-up of the icebound stream of Time. But at last all these fancies yielded to that one portentous something in the picture’s midst. That once found out, and all the rest were plain. But stop; does it not bear a faint resemblance to a gigantic fish? even the great leviathan himself?\n\nIn fact, the artist’s design seemed this: a final theory of my own, partly based upon the aggregated opinions of many aged persons with whom I conversed upon the subject. The picture represents a Cape-Horner in a great hurricane; the half-foundered ship weltering there with its three dismantled masts alone visible; and an exasperated whale, purposing to spring clean over the craft, is in the enormous act of impaling himself upon the three mast-heads.\n\nThe opposite wall of this entry was hung all over with a heathenish array of monstrous clubs and spears. Some were thickly set with glittering teeth resembling ivory saws; others were tufted with knots of human hair; and one was sickle-shaped, with a vast handle sweeping round like the segment made in the new-mown grass by a long-armed mower. You shuddered as you gazed, and wondered what monstrous cannibal and savage could ever have gone a death-harvesting with such a hacking, horrifying implement. Mixed with these were rusty old whaling lances and harpoons all broken and deformed. Some were storied weapons. With this once long lance, now wildly elbowed, fifty years ago did Nathan Swain kill fifteen whales between a sunrise and a sunset. And that harpoon—so like a corkscrew now—was flung in Javan seas, and run away with by a whale, years afterwards slain off the Cape of Blanco. The original iron entered nigh the tail, and, like a restless needle sojourning in the body of a man, travelled full forty feet, and at last was found imbedded in the hump.\n\nCrossing this dusky entry, and on through yon low-arched way—cut through what in old times must have been a great central chimney with fireplaces all round—you enter the public room. A still duskier place is this, with such low ponderous beams above, and such old wrinkled planks beneath, that you would almost fancy you trod some old craft’s cockpits, especially of such a howling night, when this corner-anchored old ark rocked so furiously. On one side stood a long, low, shelf-like table covered with cracked glass cases, filled with dusty rarities gathered from this wide world’s remotest nooks. Projecting from the further angle of the room stands a dark-looking den—the bar—a rude attempt at a right whale’s head. Be that how it may, there stands the vast arched bone of the whale’s jaw, so wide, a coach might almost drive beneath it. Within are shabby shelves, ranged round with old decanters, bottles, flasks; and in those jaws of swift destruction, like another cursed Jonah (by which name indeed they called him), bustles a little withered old man, who, for their money, dearly sells the sailors deliriums and death.\n\nAbominable are the tumblers into which he pours his poison. Though true cylinders without—within, the villanous green goggling glasses deceitfully tapered downwards to a cheating bottom. Parallel meridians rudely pecked into the glass, surround these footpads’ goblets. Fill to this mark, and your charge is but a penny; to this a penny more; and so on to the full glass—the Cape Horn measure, which you may gulp down for a shilling.\n\nUpon entering the place I found a number of young seamen gathered about a table, examining by a dim light divers specimens of skrimshander. I sought the landlord, and telling him I desired to be accommodated with a room, received for answer that his house was full—not a bed unoccupied. “But avast,” he added, tapping his forehead, “you haint no objections to sharing a harpooneer’s blanket, have ye? I s’pose you are goin’ a-whalin’, so you’d better get used to that sort of thing.”\n\nI told him that I never liked to sleep two in a bed; that if I should ever do so, it would depend upon who the harpooneer might be, and that if he (the landlord) really had no other place for me, and the harpooneer was not decidedly objectionable, why rather than wander further about a strange town on so bitter a night, I would put up with the half of any decent man’s blanket.\n\n“I thought so. All right; take a seat. Supper?—you want supper? Supper’ll be ready directly.”\n\nI sat down on an old wooden settle, carved all over like a bench on the Battery. At one end a ruminating tar was still further adorning it with his jack-knife, stooping over and diligently working away at the space between his legs. He was trying his hand at a ship under full sail, but he didn’t make much headway, I thought.\n\nAt last some four or five of us were summoned to our meal in an adjoining room. It was cold as Iceland—no fire at all—the landlord said he couldn’t afford it. Nothing but two dismal tallow candles, each in a winding sheet. We were fain to button up our monkey jackets, and hold to our lips cups of scalding tea with our half frozen fingers. But the fare was of the most substantial kind—not only meat and potatoes, but dumplings; good heavens! dumplings for supper! One young fellow in a green box coat, addressed himself to these dumplings in a most direful manner.\n\n“My boy,” said the landlord, “you’ll have the nightmare to a dead sartainty.”\n\n“Landlord,” I whispered, “that aint the harpooneer is it?”\n\n“Oh, no,” said he, looking a sort of diabolically funny, “the harpooneer is a dark complexioned chap. He never eats dumplings, he don’t—he eats nothing but steaks, and he likes ’em rare.”\n\n“The devil he does,” says I. “Where is that harpooneer? Is he here?”\n\n“He’ll be here afore long,” was the answer.\n\nI could not help it, but I began to feel suspicious of this “dark complexioned” harpooneer. At any rate, I made up my mind that if it so turned out that we should sleep together, he must undress and get into bed before I did.\n\nSupper over, the company went back to the bar-room, when, knowing not what else to do with myself, I resolved to spend the rest of the evening as a looker on.\n\nPresently a rioting noise was heard without. Starting up, the landlord cried, “That’s the Grampus’s crew. I seed her reported in the offing this morning; a three years’ voyage, and a full ship. Hurrah, boys; now we’ll have the latest news from the Feegees.”\n\nA tramping of sea boots was heard in the entry; the door was flung open, and in rolled a wild set of mariners enough. Enveloped in their shaggy watch coats, and with their heads muffled in woollen comforters, all bedarned and ragged, and their beards stiff with icicles, they seemed an eruption of bears from Labrador. They had just landed from their boat, and this was the first house they entered. No wonder, then, that they made a straight wake for the whale’s mouth—the bar—when the wrinkled little old Jonah, there officiating, soon poured them out brimmers all round. One complained of a bad cold in his head, upon which Jonah mixed him a pitch-like potion of gin and molasses, which he swore was a sovereign cure for all colds and catarrhs whatsoever, never mind of how long standing, or whether caught off the coast of Labrador, or on the weather side of an ice-island.\n\nThe liquor soon mounted into their heads, as it generally does even with the arrantest topers newly landed from sea, and they began capering about most obstreperously.\n\nI observed, however, that one of them held somewhat aloof, and though he seemed desirous not to spoil the hilarity of his shipmates by his own sober face, yet upon the whole he refrained from making as much noise as the rest. This man interested me at once; and since the sea-gods had ordained that he should soon become my shipmate (though but a sleeping-partner one, so far as this narrative is concerned), I will here venture upon a little description of him. He stood full six feet in height, with noble shoulders, and a chest like a coffer-dam. I have seldom seen such brawn in a man. His face was deeply brown and burnt, making his white teeth dazzling by the contrast; while in the deep shadows of his eyes floated some reminiscences that did not seem to give him much joy. His voice at once announced that he was a Southerner, and from his fine stature, I thought he must be one of those tall mountaineers from the Alleghanian Ridge in Virginia. When the revelry of his companions had mounted to its height, this man slipped away unobserved, and I saw no more of him till he became my comrade on the sea. In a few minutes, however, he was missed by his shipmates, and being, it seems, for some reason a huge favourite with them, they raised a cry of “Bulkington! Bulkington! where’s Bulkington?” and darted out of the house in pursuit of him.\n\nIt was now about nine o’clock, and the room seeming almost supernaturally quiet after these orgies, I began to congratulate myself upon a little plan that had occurred to me just previous to the entrance of the seamen.\n\nNo man prefers to sleep two in a bed. In fact, you would a good deal rather not sleep with your own brother. I don’t know how it is, but people like to be private when they are sleeping. And when it comes to sleeping with an unknown stranger, in a strange inn, in a strange town, and that stranger a harpooneer, then your objections indefinitely multiply. Nor was there any earthly reason why I as a sailor should sleep two in a bed, more than anybody else; for sailors no more sleep two in a bed at sea, than bachelor Kings do ashore. To be sure they all sleep together in one apartment, but you have your own hammock, and cover yourself with your own blanket, and sleep in your own skin.\n\nThe more I pondered over this harpooneer, the more I abominated the thought of sleeping with him. It was fair to presume that being a harpooneer, his linen or woollen, as the case might be, would not be of the tidiest, certainly none of the finest. I began to twitch all over. Besides, it was getting late, and my decent harpooneer ought to be home and going bedwards. Suppose now, he should tumble in upon me at midnight—how could I tell from what vile hole he had been coming?\n\n“Landlord! I’ve changed my mind about that harpooneer.—I shan’t sleep with him. I’ll try the bench here.”\n\n“Just as you please; I’m sorry I can’t spare ye a tablecloth for a mattress, and it’s a plaguy rough board here”—feeling of the knots and notches. “But wait a bit, Skrimshander; I’ve got a carpenter’s plane there in the bar—wait, I say, and I’ll make ye snug enough.” So saying he procured the plane; and with his old silk handkerchief first dusting the bench, vigorously set to planing away at my bed, the while grinning like an ape. The shavings flew right and left; till at last the plane-iron came bump against an indestructible knot. The landlord was near spraining his wrist, and I told him for heaven’s sake to quit—the bed was soft enough to suit me, and I did not know how all the planing in the world could make eider down of a pine plank. So gathering up the shavings with another grin, and throwing them into the great stove in the middle of the room, he went about his business, and left me in a brown study.\n\nI now took the measure of the bench, and found that it was a foot too short; but that could be mended with a chair. But it was a foot too narrow, and the other bench in the room was about four inches higher than the planed one—so there was no yoking them. I then placed the first bench lengthwise along the only clear space against the wall, leaving a little interval between, for my back to settle down in. But I soon found that there came such a draught of cold air over me from under the sill of the window, that this plan would never do at all, especially as another current from the rickety door met the one from the window, and both together formed a series of small whirlwinds in the immediate vicinity of the spot where I had thought to spend the night.\n\nThe devil fetch that harpooneer, thought I, but stop, couldn’t I steal a march on him—bolt his door inside, and jump into his bed, not to be wakened by the most violent knockings? It seemed no bad idea; but upon second thoughts I dismissed it. For who could tell but what the next morning, so soon as I popped out of the room, the harpooneer might be standing in the entry, all ready to knock me down!\n\nStill, looking round me again, and seeing no possible chance of spending a sufferable night unless in some other person’s bed, I began to think that after all I might be cherishing unwarrantable prejudices against this unknown harpooneer. Thinks I, I’ll wait awhile; he must be dropping in before long. I’ll have a good look at him then, and perhaps we may become jolly good bedfellows after all—there’s no telling.\n\nBut though the other boarders kept coming in by ones, twos, and threes, and going to bed, yet no sign of my harpooneer.\n\n“Landlord!” said I, “what sort of a chap is he—does he always keep such late hours?” It was now hard upon twelve o’clock.\n\nThe landlord chuckled again with his lean chuckle, and seemed to be mightily tickled at something beyond my comprehension. “No,” he answered, “generally he’s an early bird—airley to bed and airley to rise—yes, he’s the bird what catches the worm. But to-night he went out a peddling, you see, and I don’t see what on airth keeps him so late, unless, may be, he can’t sell his head.”\n\n“Can’t sell his head?—What sort of a bamboozingly story is this you are telling me?” getting into a towering rage. “Do you pretend to say, landlord, that this harpooneer is actually engaged this blessed Saturday night, or rather Sunday morning, in peddling his head around this town?”\n\n“That’s precisely it,” said the landlord, “and I told him he couldn’t sell it here, the market’s overstocked.”\n\n“With what?” shouted I.\n\n“With heads to be sure; ain’t there too many heads in the world?”\n\n“I tell you what it is, landlord,” said I quite calmly, “you’d better stop spinning that yarn to me—I’m not green.”\n\n“May be not,” taking out a stick and whittling a toothpick, “but I rayther guess you’ll be done brown if that ere harpooneer hears you a slanderin’ his head.”\n\n“I’ll break it for him,” said I, now flying into a passion again at this unaccountable farrago of the landlord’s.\n\n“Broke,” said I—“broke, do you mean?”\n\n“Sartain, and that’s the very reason he can’t sell it, I guess.”\n\n“Landlord,” said I, going up to him as cool as Mt. Hecla in a snow-storm—“landlord, stop whittling. You and I must understand one another, and that too without delay. I come to your house and want a bed; you tell me you can only give me half a one; that the other half belongs to a certain harpooneer. And about this harpooneer, whom I have not yet seen, you persist in telling me the most mystifying and exasperating stories tending to beget in me an uncomfortable feeling towards the man whom you design for my bedfellow—a sort of connexion, landlord, which is an intimate and confidential one in the highest degree. I now demand of you to speak out and tell me who and what this harpooneer is, and whether I shall be in all respects safe to spend the night with him. And in the first place, you will be so good as to unsay that story about selling his head, which if true I take to be good evidence that this harpooneer is stark mad, and I’ve no idea of sleeping with a madman; and you, sir, you I mean, landlord, you, sir, by trying to induce me to do so knowingly, would thereby render yourself liable to a criminal prosecution.”\n\n“Wall,” said the landlord, fetching a long breath, “that’s a purty long sarmon for a chap that rips a little now and then. But be easy, be easy, this here harpooneer I have been tellin’ you of has just arrived from the south seas, where he bought up a lot of ’balmed New Zealand heads (great curios, you know), and he’s sold all on ’em but one, and that one he’s trying to sell to-night, cause to-morrow’s Sunday, and it would not do to be sellin’ human heads about the streets when folks is goin’ to churches. He wanted to, last Sunday, but I stopped him just as he was goin’ out of the door with four heads strung on a string, for all the airth like a string of inions.”\n\nThis account cleared up the otherwise unaccountable mystery, and showed that the landlord, after all, had had no idea of fooling me—but at the same time what could I think of a harpooneer who stayed out of a Saturday night clean into the holy Sabbath, engaged in such a cannibal business as selling the heads of dead idolators?\n\n“Depend upon it, landlord, that harpooneer is a dangerous man.”\n\n“He pays reg’lar,” was the rejoinder. “But come, it’s getting dreadful late, you had better be turning flukes—it’s a nice bed; Sal and me slept in that ere bed the night we were spliced. There’s plenty of room for two to kick about in that bed; it’s an almighty big bed that. Why, afore we give it up, Sal used to put our Sam and little Johnny in the foot of it. But I got a dreaming and sprawling about one night, and somehow, Sam got pitched on the floor, and came near breaking his arm. Arter that, Sal said it wouldn’t do. Come along here, I’ll give ye a glim in a jiffy;” and so saying he lighted a candle and held it towards me, offering to lead the way. But I stood irresolute; when looking at a clock in the corner, he exclaimed “I vum it’s Sunday—you won’t see that harpooneer to-night; he’s come to anchor somewhere—come along then; do come; won’t ye come?”\n\nI considered the matter a moment, and then up stairs we went, and I was ushered into a small room, cold as a clam, and furnished, sure enough, with a prodigious bed, almost big enough indeed for any four harpooneers to sleep abreast.\n\n“There,” said the landlord, placing the candle on a crazy old sea chest that did double duty as a wash-stand and centre table; “there, make yourself comfortable now, and good night to ye.” I turned round from eyeing the bed, but he had disappeared.\n\nFolding back the counterpane, I stooped over the bed. Though none of the most elegant, it yet stood the scrutiny tolerably well. I then glanced round the room; and besides the bedstead and centre table, could see no other furniture belonging to the place, but a rude shelf, the four walls, and a papered fireboard representing a man striking a whale. Of things not properly belonging to the room, there was a hammock lashed up, and thrown upon the floor in one corner; also a large seaman’s bag, containing the harpooneer’s wardrobe, no doubt in lieu of a land trunk. Likewise, there was a parcel of outlandish bone fish hooks on the shelf over the fire-place, and a tall harpoon standing at the head of the bed.\n\nBut what is this on the chest? I took it up, and held it close to the light, and felt it, and smelt it, and tried every way possible to arrive at some satisfactory conclusion concerning it. I can compare it to nothing but a large door mat, ornamented at the edges with little tinkling tags something like the stained porcupine quills round an Indian moccasin. There was a hole or slit in the middle of this mat, as you see the same in South American ponchos. But could it be possible that any sober harpooneer would get into a door mat, and parade the streets of any Christian town in that sort of guise? I put it on, to try it, and it weighed me down like a hamper, being uncommonly shaggy and thick, and I thought a little damp, as though this mysterious harpooneer had been wearing it of a rainy day. I went up in it to a bit of glass stuck against the wall, and I never saw such a sight in my life. I tore myself out of it in such a hurry that I gave myself a kink in the neck.\n\nI sat down on the side of the bed, and commenced thinking about this head-peddling harpooneer, and his door mat. After thinking some time on the bed-side, I got up and took off my monkey jacket, and then stood in the middle of the room thinking. I then took off my coat, and thought a little more in my shirt sleeves. But beginning to feel very cold now, half undressed as I was, and remembering what the landlord said about the harpooneer’s not coming home at all that night, it being so very late, I made no more ado, but jumped out of my pantaloons and boots, and then blowing out the light tumbled into bed, and commended myself to the care of heaven.\n\nWhether that mattress was stuffed with corn-cobs or broken crockery, there is no telling, but I rolled about a good deal, and could not sleep for a long time. At last I slid off into a light doze, and had pretty nearly made a good offing towards the land of Nod, when I heard a heavy footfall in the passage, and saw a glimmer of light come into the room from under the door.\n\nLord save me, thinks I, that must be the harpooneer, the infernal head-peddler. But I lay perfectly still, and resolved not to say a word till spoken to. Holding a light in one hand, and that identical New Zealand head in the other, the stranger entered the room, and without looking towards the bed, placed his candle a good way off from me on the floor in one corner, and then began working away at the knotted cords of the large bag I before spoke of as being in the room. I was all eagerness to see his face, but he kept it averted for some time while employed in unlacing the bag’s mouth. This accomplished, however, he turned round—when, good heavens! what a sight! Such a face! It was of a dark, purplish, yellow colour, here and there stuck over with large blackish looking squares. Yes, it’s just as I thought, he’s a terrible bedfellow; he’s been in a fight, got dreadfully cut, and here he is, just from the surgeon. But at that moment he chanced to turn his face so towards the light, that I plainly saw they could not be sticking-plasters at all, those black squares on his cheeks. They were stains of some sort or other. At first I knew not what to make of this; but soon an inkling of the truth occurred to me. I remembered a story of a white man—a whaleman too—who, falling among the cannibals, had been tattooed by them. I concluded that this harpooneer, in the course of his distant voyages, must have met with a similar adventure. And what is it, thought I, after all! It’s only his outside; a man can be honest in any sort of skin. But then, what to make of his unearthly complexion, that part of it, I mean, lying round about, and completely independent of the squares of tattooing. To be sure, it might be nothing but a good coat of tropical tanning; but I never heard of a hot sun’s tanning a white man into a purplish yellow one. However, I had never been in the South Seas; and perhaps the sun there produced these extraordinary effects upon the skin. Now, while all these ideas were passing through me like lightning, this harpooneer never noticed me at all. But, after some difficulty having opened his bag, he commenced fumbling in it, and presently pulled out a sort of tomahawk, and a seal-skin wallet with the hair on. Placing these on the old chest in the middle of the room, he then took the New Zealand head—a ghastly thing enough—and crammed it down into the bag. He now took off his hat—a new beaver hat—when I came nigh singing out with fresh surprise. There was no hair on his head—none to speak of at least—nothing but a small scalp-knot twisted up on his forehead. His bald purplish head now looked for all the world like a mildewed skull. Had not the stranger stood between me and the door, I would have bolted out of it quicker than ever I bolted a dinner.\n\nEven as it was, I thought something of slipping out of the window, but it was the second floor back. I am no coward, but what to make of this head-peddling purple rascal altogether passed my comprehension. Ignorance is the parent of fear, and being completely nonplussed and confounded about the stranger, I confess I was now as much afraid of him as if it was the devil himself who had thus broken into my room at the dead of night. In fact, I was so afraid of him that I was not game enough just then to address him, and demand a satisfactory answer concerning what seemed inexplicable in him.\n\nMeanwhile, he continued the business of undressing, and at last showed his chest and arms. As I live, these covered parts of him were checkered with the same squares as his face; his back, too, was all over the same dark squares; he seemed to have been in a Thirty Years’ War, and just escaped from it with a sticking-plaster shirt. Still more, his very legs were marked, as if a parcel of dark green frogs were running up the trunks of young palms. It was now quite plain that he must be some abominable savage or other shipped aboard of a whaleman in the South Seas, and so landed in this Christian country. I quaked to think of it. A peddler of heads too—perhaps the heads of his own brothers. He might take a fancy to mine—heavens! look at that tomahawk!\n\nBut there was no time for shuddering, for now the savage went about something that completely fascinated my attention, and convinced me that he must indeed be a heathen. Going to his heavy grego, or wrapall, or dreadnaught, which he had previously hung on a chair, he fumbled in the pockets, and produced at length a curious little deformed image with a hunch on its back, and exactly the colour of a three days’ old Congo baby. Remembering the embalmed head, at first I almost thought that this black manikin was a real baby preserved in some similar manner. But seeing that it was not at all limber, and that it glistened a good deal like polished ebony, I concluded that it must be nothing but a wooden idol, which indeed it proved to be. For now the savage goes up to the empty fire-place, and removing the papered fire-board, sets up this little hunch-backed image, like a tenpin, between the andirons. The chimney jambs and all the bricks inside were very sooty, so that I thought this fire-place made a very appropriate little shrine or chapel for his Congo idol.\n\nI now screwed my eyes hard towards the half hidden image, feeling but ill at ease meantime—to see what was next to follow. First he takes about a double handful of shavings out of his grego pocket, and places them carefully before the idol; then laying a bit of ship biscuit on top and applying the flame from the lamp, he kindled the shavings into a sacrificial blaze. Presently, after many hasty snatches into the fire, and still hastier withdrawals of his fingers (whereby he seemed to be scorching them badly), he at last succeeded in drawing out the biscuit; then blowing off the heat and ashes a little, he made a polite offer of it to the little negro. But the little devil did not seem to fancy such dry sort of fare at all; he never moved his lips. All these strange antics were accompanied by still stranger guttural noises from the devotee, who seemed to be praying in a sing-song or else singing some pagan psalmody or other, during which his face twitched about in the most unnatural manner. At last extinguishing the fire, he took the idol up very unceremoniously, and bagged it again in his grego pocket as carelessly as if he were a sportsman bagging a dead woodcock.\n\nAll these queer proceedings increased my uncomfortableness, and seeing him now exhibiting strong symptoms of concluding his business operations, and jumping into bed with me, I thought it was high time, now or never, before the light was put out, to break the spell in which I had so long been bound.\n\nBut the interval I spent in deliberating what to say, was a fatal one. Taking up his tomahawk from the table, he examined the head of it for an instant, and then holding it to the light, with his mouth at the handle, he puffed out great clouds of tobacco smoke. The next moment the light was extinguished, and this wild cannibal, tomahawk between his teeth, sprang into bed with me. I sang out, I could not help it now; and giving a sudden grunt of astonishment he began feeling me.\n\nStammering out something, I knew not what, I rolled away from him against the wall, and then conjured him, whoever or whatever he might be, to keep quiet, and let me get up and light the lamp again. But his guttural responses satisfied me at once that he but ill comprehended my meaning.\n\n“Who-e debel you?”—he at last said—“you no speak-e, dam-me, I kill-e.” And so saying the lighted tomahawk began flourishing about me in the dark.\n\n“Landlord, for God’s sake, Peter Coffin!” shouted I. “Landlord! Watch! Coffin! Angels! save me!”\n\n“Speak-e! tell-ee me who-ee be, or dam-me, I kill-e!” again growled the cannibal, while his horrid flourishings of the tomahawk scattered the hot tobacco ashes about me till I thought my linen would get on fire. But thank heaven, at that moment the landlord came into the room light in hand, and leaping from the bed I ran up to him.\n\n“Don’t be afraid now,” said he, grinning again, “Queequeg here wouldn’t harm a hair of your head.”\n\n“Stop your grinning,” shouted I, “and why didn’t you tell me that that infernal harpooneer was a cannibal?”\n\n“I thought ye know’d it;—didn’t I tell ye, he was a peddlin’ heads around town?—but turn flukes again and go to sleep. Queequeg, look here—you sabbee me, I sabbee—you this man sleepe you—you sabbee?”\n\n“Me sabbee plenty”—grunted Queequeg, puffing away at his pipe and sitting up in bed.\n\n“You gettee in,” he added, motioning to me with his tomahawk, and throwing the clothes to one side. He really did this in not only a civil but a really kind and charitable way. I stood looking at him a moment. For all his tattooings he was on the whole a clean, comely looking cannibal. What’s all this fuss I have been making about, thought I to myself—the man’s a human being just as I am: he has just as much reason to fear me, as I have to be afraid of him. Better sleep with a sober cannibal than a drunken Christian.\n\n“Landlord,” said I, “tell him to stash his tomahawk there, or pipe, or whatever you call it; tell him to stop smoking, in short, and I will turn in with him. But I don’t fancy having a man smoking in bed with me. It’s dangerous. Besides, I ain’t insured.”\n\nThis being told to Queequeg, he at once complied, and again politely motioned me to get into bed—rolling over to one side as much as to say—“I won’t touch a leg of ye.”\n\n“Good night, landlord,” said I, “you may go.”\n\nI turned in, and never slept better in my life.\n\n# CHAPTER 4. The Counterpane.\n\nUpon waking next morning about daylight, I found Queequeg’s arm thrown over me in the most loving and affectionate manner. You had almost thought I had been his wife. The counterpane was of patchwork, full of odd little parti-coloured squares and triangles; and this arm of his tattooed all over with an interminable Cretan labyrinth of a figure, no two parts of which were of one precise shade—owing I suppose to his keeping his arm at sea unmethodically in sun and shade, his shirt sleeves irregularly rolled up at various times—this same arm of his, I say, looked for all the world like a strip of that same patchwork quilt. Indeed, partly lying on it as the arm did when I first awoke, I could hardly tell it from the quilt, they so blended their hues together; and it was only by the sense of weight and pressure that I could tell that Queequeg was hugging me.\n\nMy sensations were strange. Let me try to explain them. When I was a child, I well remember a somewhat similar circumstance that befell me; whether it was a reality or a dream, I never could entirely settle. The circumstance was this. I had been cutting up some caper or other—I think it was trying to crawl up the chimney, as I had seen a little sweep do a few days previous; and my stepmother who, somehow or other, was all the time whipping me, or sending me to bed supperless,—my mother dragged me by the legs out of the chimney and packed me off to bed, though it was only two o’clock in the afternoon of the 21st June, the longest day in the year in our hemisphere. I felt dreadfully. But there was no help for it, so up stairs I went to my little room in the third floor, undressed myself as slowly as possible so as to kill time, and with a bitter sigh got between the sheets.\n\nI lay there dismally calculating that sixteen entire hours must elapse before I could hope for a resurrection. Sixteen hours in bed! the small of my back ached to think of it. And it was so light too; the sun shining in at the window, and a great rattling of coaches in the streets, and the sound of gay voices all over the house. I felt worse and worse—at last I got up, dressed, and softly going down in my stockinged feet, sought out my stepmother, and suddenly threw myself at her feet, beseeching her as a particular favour to give me a good slippering for my misbehaviour; anything indeed but condemning me to lie abed such an unendurable length of time. But she was the best and most conscientious of stepmothers, and back I had to go to my room. For several hours I lay there broad awake, feeling a great deal worse than I have ever done since, even from the greatest subsequent misfortunes. At last I must have fallen into a troubled nightmare of a doze; and slowly waking from it—half steeped in dreams—I opened my eyes, and the before sun-lit room was now wrapped in outer darkness. Instantly I felt a shock running through all my frame; nothing was to be seen, and nothing was to be heard; but a supernatural hand seemed placed in mine. My arm hung over the counterpane, and the nameless, unimaginable, silent form or phantom, to which the hand belonged, seemed closely seated by my bed-side. For what seemed ages piled on ages, I lay there, frozen with the most awful fears, not daring to drag away my hand; yet ever thinking that if I could but stir it one single inch, the horrid spell would be broken. I knew not how this consciousness at last glided away from me; but waking in the morning, I shudderingly remembered it all, and for days and weeks and months afterwards I lost myself in confounding attempts to explain the mystery. Nay, to this very hour, I often puzzle myself with it.\n\nNow, take away the awful fear, and my sensations at feeling the supernatural hand in mine were very similar, in their strangeness, to those which I experienced on waking up and seeing Queequeg’s pagan arm thrown round me. But at length all the past night’s events soberly recurred, one by one, in fixed reality, and then I lay only alive to the comical predicament. For though I tried to move his arm—unlock his bridegroom clasp—yet, sleeping as he was, he still hugged me tightly, as though naught but death should part us twain. I now strove to rouse him—“Queequeg!”—but his only answer was a snore. I then rolled over, my neck feeling as if it were in a horse-collar; and suddenly felt a slight scratch. Throwing aside the counterpane, there lay the tomahawk sleeping by the savage’s side, as if it were a hatchet-faced baby. A pretty pickle, truly, thought I; abed here in a strange house in the broad day, with a cannibal and a tomahawk! “Queequeg!—in the name of goodness, Queequeg, wake!” At length, by dint of much wriggling, and loud and incessant expostulations upon the unbecomingness of his hugging a fellow male in that matrimonial sort of style, I succeeded in extracting a grunt; and presently, he drew back his arm, shook himself all over like a Newfoundland dog just from the water, and sat up in bed, stiff as a pike-staff, looking at me, and rubbing his eyes as if he did not altogether remember how I came to be there, though a dim consciousness of knowing something about me seemed slowly dawning over him. Meanwhile, I lay quietly eyeing him, having no serious misgivings now, and bent upon narrowly observing so curious a creature. When, at last, his mind seemed made up touching the character of his bedfellow, and he became, as it were, reconciled to the fact; he jumped out upon the floor, and by certain signs and sounds gave me to understand that, if it pleased me, he would dress first and then leave me to dress afterwards, leaving the whole apartment to myself. Thinks I, Queequeg, under the circumstances, this is a very civilized overture; but, the truth is, these savages have an innate sense of delicacy, say what you will; it is marvellous how essentially polite they are. I pay this particular compliment to Queequeg, because he treated me with so much civility and consideration, while I was guilty of great rudeness; staring at him from the bed, and watching all his toilette motions; for the time my curiosity getting the better of my breeding. Nevertheless, a man like Queequeg you don’t see every day, he and his ways were well worth unusual regarding.\n\nHe commenced dressing at top by donning his beaver hat, a very tall one, by the by, and then—still minus his trowsers—he hunted up his boots. What under the heavens he did it for, I cannot tell, but his next movement was to crush himself—boots in hand, and hat on—under the bed; when, from sundry violent gaspings and strainings, I inferred he was hard at work booting himself; though by no law of propriety that I ever heard of, is any man required to be private when putting on his boots. But Queequeg, do you see, was a creature in the transition stage—neither caterpillar nor butterfly. He was just enough civilized to show off his outlandishness in the strangest possible manners. His education was not yet completed. He was an undergraduate. If he had not been a small degree civilized, he very probably would not have troubled himself with boots at all; but then, if he had not been still a savage, he never would have dreamt of getting under the bed to put them on. At last, he emerged with his hat very much dented and crushed down over his eyes, and began creaking and limping about the room, as if, not being much accustomed to boots, his pair of damp, wrinkled cowhide ones—probably not made to order either—rather pinched and tormented him at the first go off of a bitter cold morning.\n\nSeeing, now, that there were no curtains to the window, and that the street being very narrow, the house opposite commanded a plain view into the room, and observing more and more the indecorous figure that Queequeg made, staving about with little else but his hat and boots on; I begged him as well as I could, to accelerate his toilet somewhat, and particularly to get into his pantaloons as soon as possible. He complied, and then proceeded to wash himself. At that time in the morning any Christian would have washed his face; but Queequeg, to my amazement, contented himself with restricting his ablutions to his chest, arms, and hands. He then donned his waistcoat, and taking up a piece of hard soap on the wash-stand centre table, dipped it into water and commenced lathering his face. I was watching to see where he kept his razor, when lo and behold, he takes the harpoon from the bed corner, slips out the long wooden stock, unsheathes the head, whets it a little on his boot, and striding up to the bit of mirror against the wall, begins a vigorous scraping, or rather harpooning of his cheeks. Thinks I, Queequeg, this is using Rogers’s best cutlery with a vengeance. Afterwards I wondered the less at this operation when I came to know of what fine steel the head of a harpoon is made, and how exceedingly sharp the long straight edges are always kept.\n\nThe rest of his toilet was soon achieved, and he proudly marched out of the room, wrapped up in his great pilot monkey jacket, and sporting his harpoon like a marshal’s baton.\n\n# CHAPTER 5. Breakfast.\n\nI quickly followed suit, and descending into the bar-room accosted the grinning landlord very pleasantly. I cherished no malice towards him, though he had been skylarking with me not a little in the matter of my bedfellow.\n\nHowever, a good laugh is a mighty good thing, and rather too scarce a good thing; the more’s the pity. So, if any one man, in his own proper person, afford stuff for a good joke to anybody, let him not be backward, but let him cheerfully allow himself to spend and be spent in that way. And the man that has anything bountifully laughable about him, be sure there is more in that man than you perhaps think for.\n\nThe bar-room was now full of the boarders who had been dropping in the night previous, and whom I had not as yet had a good look at. They were nearly all whalemen; chief mates, and second mates, and third mates, and sea carpenters, and sea coopers, and sea blacksmiths, and harpooneers, and ship keepers; a brown and brawny company, with bosky beards; an unshorn, shaggy set, all wearing monkey jackets for morning gowns.\n\nYou could pretty plainly tell how long each one had been ashore. This young fellow’s healthy cheek is like a sun-toasted pear in hue, and would seem to smell almost as musky; he cannot have been three days landed from his Indian voyage. That man next him looks a few shades lighter; you might say a touch of satin wood is in him. In the complexion of a third still lingers a tropic tawn, but slightly bleached withal; he doubtless has tarried whole weeks ashore. But who could show a cheek like Queequeg? which, barred with various tints, seemed like the Andes’ western slope, to show forth in one array, contrasting climates, zone by zone.\n\n“Grub, ho!” now cried the landlord, flinging open a door, and in we went to breakfast.\n\nThey say that men who have seen the world, thereby become quite at ease in manner, quite self-possessed in company. Not always, though: Ledyard, the great New England traveller, and Mungo Park, the Scotch one; of all men, they possessed the least assurance in the parlor. But perhaps the mere crossing of Siberia in a sledge drawn by dogs as Ledyard did, or the taking a long solitary walk on an empty stomach, in the negro heart of Africa, which was the sum of poor Mungo’s performances—this kind of travel, I say, may not be the very best mode of attaining a high social polish. Still, for the most part, that sort of thing is to be had anywhere.\n\nThese reflections just here are occasioned by the circumstance that after we were all seated at the table, and I was preparing to hear some good stories about whaling; to my no small surprise, nearly every man maintained a profound silence. And not only that, but they looked embarrassed. Yes, here were a set of sea-dogs, many of whom without the slightest bashfulness had boarded great whales on the high seas—entire strangers to them—and duelled them dead without winking; and yet, here they sat at a social breakfast table—all of the same calling, all of kindred tastes—looking round as sheepishly at each other as though they had never been out of sight of some sheepfold among the Green Mountains. A curious sight; these bashful bears, these timid warrior whalemen!\n\nBut as for Queequeg—why, Queequeg sat there among them—at the head of the table, too, it so chanced; as cool as an icicle. To be sure I cannot say much for his breeding. His greatest admirer could not have cordially justified his bringing his harpoon into breakfast with him, and using it there without ceremony; reaching over the table with it, to the imminent jeopardy of many heads, and grappling the beefsteaks towards him. But that was certainly very coolly done by him, and every one knows that in most people’s estimation, to do anything coolly is to do it genteelly.\n\nWe will not speak of all Queequeg’s peculiarities here; how he eschewed coffee and hot rolls, and applied his undivided attention to beefsteaks, done rare. Enough, that when breakfast was over he withdrew like the rest into the public room, lighted his tomahawk-pipe, and was sitting there quietly digesting and smoking with his inseparable hat on, when I sallied out for a stroll.\n\n# CHAPTER 6. The Street.\n\nIf I had been astonished at first catching a glimpse of so outlandish an individual as Queequeg circulating among the polite society of a civilized town, that astonishment soon departed upon taking my first daylight stroll through the streets of New Bedford.\n\nIn thoroughfares nigh the docks, any considerable seaport will frequently offer to view the queerest looking nondescripts from foreign parts. Even in Broadway and Chestnut streets, Mediterranean mariners will sometimes jostle the affrighted ladies. Regent Street is not unknown to Lascars and Malays; and at Bombay, in the Apollo Green, live Yankees have often scared the natives. But New Bedford beats all Water Street and Wapping. In these last-mentioned haunts you see only sailors; but in New Bedford, actual cannibals stand chatting at street corners; savages outright; many of whom yet carry on their bones unholy flesh. It makes a stranger stare.\n\nBut, besides the Feegeeans, Tongatobooarrs, Erromanggoans, Pannangians, and Brighggians, and, besides the wild specimens of the whaling-craft which unheeded reel about the streets, you will see other sights still more curious, certainly more comical. There weekly arrive in this town scores of green Vermonters and New Hampshire men, all athirst for gain and glory in the fishery. They are mostly young, of stalwart frames; fellows who have felled forests, and now seek to drop the axe and snatch the whale-lance. Many are as green as the Green Mountains whence they came. In some things you would think them but a few hours old. Look there! that chap strutting round the corner. He wears a beaver hat and swallow-tailed coat, girdled with a sailor-belt and sheath-knife. Here comes another with a sou’-wester and a bombazine cloak.\n\nNo town-bred dandy will compare with a country-bred one—I mean a downright bumpkin dandy—a fellow that, in the dog-days, will mow his two acres in buckskin gloves for fear of tanning his hands. Now when a country dandy like this takes it into his head to make a distinguished reputation, and joins the great whale-fishery, you should see the comical things he does upon reaching the seaport. In bespeaking his sea-outfit, he orders bell-buttons to his waistcoats; straps to his canvas trowsers. Ah, poor Hay-Seed! how bitterly will burst those straps in the first howling gale, when thou art driven, straps, buttons, and all, down the throat of the tempest.\n\nBut think not that this famous town has only harpooneers, cannibals, and bumpkins to show her visitors. Not at all. Still New Bedford is a queer place. Had it not been for us whalemen, that tract of land would this day perhaps have been in as howling condition as the coast of Labrador. As it is, parts of her back country are enough to frighten one, they look so bony. The town itself is perhaps the dearest place to live in, in all New England. It is a land of oil, true enough: but not like Canaan; a land, also, of corn and wine. The streets do not run with milk; nor in the spring-time do they pave them with fresh eggs. Yet, in spite of this, nowhere in all America will you find more patrician-like houses; parks and gardens more opulent, than in New Bedford. Whence came they? how planted upon this once scraggy scoria of a country?\n\nGo and gaze upon the iron emblematical harpoons round yonder lofty mansion, and your question will be answered. Yes; all these brave houses and flowery gardens came from the Atlantic, Pacific, and Indian oceans. One and all, they were harpooned and dragged up hither from the bottom of the sea. Can Herr Alexander perform a feat like that?\n\nIn New Bedford, fathers, they say, give whales for dowers to their daughters, and portion off their nieces with a few porpoises a-piece. You must go to New Bedford to see a brilliant wedding; for, they say, they have reservoirs of oil in every house, and every night recklessly burn their lengths in spermaceti candles.\n\nIn summer time, the town is sweet to see; full of fine maples—long avenues of green and gold. And in August, high in air, the beautiful and bountiful horse-chestnuts, candelabra-wise, proffer the passer-by their tapering upright cones of congregated blossoms. So omnipotent is art; which in many a district of New Bedford has superinduced bright terraces of flowers upon the barren refuse rocks thrown aside at creation’s final day.\n\nAnd the women of New Bedford, they bloom like their own red roses. But roses only bloom in summer; whereas the fine carnation of their cheeks is perennial as sunlight in the seventh heavens. Elsewhere match that bloom of theirs, ye cannot, save in Salem, where they tell me the young girls breathe such musk, their sailor sweethearts smell them miles off shore, as though they were drawing nigh the odorous Moluccas instead of the Puritanic sands.\n\n# CHAPTER 7. The Chapel.\n\nIn this same New Bedford there stands a Whaleman’s Chapel, and few are the moody fishermen, shortly bound for the Indian Ocean or Pacific, who fail to make a Sunday visit to the spot. I am sure that I did not.\n\nReturning from my first morning stroll, I again sallied out upon this special errand. The sky had changed from clear, sunny cold, to driving sleet and mist. Wrapping myself in my shaggy jacket of the cloth called bearskin, I fought my way against the stubborn storm. Entering, I found a small scattered congregation of sailors, and sailors’ wives and widows. A muffled silence reigned, only broken at times by the shrieks of the storm. Each silent worshipper seemed purposely sitting apart from the other, as if each silent grief were insular and incommunicable. The chaplain had not yet arrived; and there these silent islands of men and women sat steadfastly eyeing several marble tablets, with black borders, masoned into the wall on either side the pulpit. Three of them ran something like the following, but I do not pretend to quote:—\n\nSACRED TO THE MEMORY OF JOHN TALBOT, Who, at the age of eighteen, was lost overboard, Near the Isle of Desolation, off Patagonia, November 1st, 1836. THIS TABLET Is erected to his Memory BY HIS SISTER.\n\nSACRED TO THE MEMORY OF ROBERT LONG, WILLIS ELLERY, NATHAN COLEMAN, WALTER CANNY, SETH MACY, AND SAMUEL GLEIG, Forming one of the boats’ crews OF THE SHIP ELIZA Who were towed out of sight by a Whale, On the Off-shore Ground in the PACIFIC, December 31st, 1839. THIS MARBLE Is here placed by their surviving SHIPMATES.\n\nSACRED TO THE MEMORY OF The late CAPTAIN EZEKIEL HARDY, Who in the bows of his boat was killed by a Sperm Whale on the coast of Japan, August 3d, 1833. THIS TABLET Is erected to his Memory BY HIS WIDOW.\n\nShaking off the sleet from my ice-glazed hat and jacket, I seated myself near the door, and turning sideways was surprised to see Queequeg near me. Affected by the solemnity of the scene, there was a wondering gaze of incredulous curiosity in his countenance. This savage was the only person present who seemed to notice my entrance; because he was the only one who could not read, and, therefore, was not reading those frigid inscriptions on the wall. Whether any of the relatives of the seamen whose names appeared there were now among the congregation, I knew not; but so many are the unrecorded accidents in the fishery, and so plainly did several women present wear the countenance if not the trappings of some unceasing grief, that I feel sure that here before me were assembled those, in whose unhealing hearts the sight of those bleak tablets sympathetically caused the old wounds to bleed afresh.\n\nOh! ye whose dead lie buried beneath the green grass; who standing among flowers can say—here, here lies my beloved; ye know not the desolation that broods in bosoms like these. What bitter blanks in those black-bordered marbles which cover no ashes! What despair in those immovable inscriptions! What deadly voids and unbidden infidelities in the lines that seem to gnaw upon all Faith, and refuse resurrections to the beings who have placelessly perished without a grave. As well might those tablets stand in the cave of Elephanta as here.\n\nIn what census of living creatures, the dead of mankind are included; why it is that a universal proverb says of them, that they tell no tales, though containing more secrets than the Goodwin Sands; how it is that to his name who yesterday departed for the other world, we prefix so significant and infidel a word, and yet do not thus entitle him, if he but embarks for the remotest Indies of this living earth; why the Life Insurance Companies pay death-forfeitures upon immortals; in what eternal, unstirring paralysis, and deadly, hopeless trance, yet lies antique Adam who died sixty round centuries ago; how it is that we still refuse to be comforted for those who we nevertheless maintain are dwelling in unspeakable bliss; why all the living so strive to hush all the dead; wherefore but the rumor of a knocking in a tomb will terrify a whole city. All these things are not without their meanings.\n\nBut Faith, like a jackal, feeds among the tombs, and even from these dead doubts she gathers her most vital hope.\n\nIt needs scarcely to be told, with what feelings, on the eve of a Nantucket voyage, I regarded those marble tablets, and by the murky light of that darkened, doleful day read the fate of the whalemen who had gone before me. Yes, Ishmael, the same fate may be thine. But somehow I grew merry again. Delightful inducements to embark, fine chance for promotion, it seems—aye, a stove boat will make me an immortal by brevet. Yes, there is death in this business of whaling—a speechlessly quick chaotic bundling of a man into Eternity. But what then? Methinks we have hugely mistaken this matter of Life and Death. Methinks that what they call my shadow here on earth is my true substance. Methinks that in looking at things spiritual, we are too much like oysters observing the sun through the water, and thinking that thick water the thinnest of air. Methinks my body is but the lees of my better being. In fact take my body who will, take it I say, it is not me. And therefore three cheers for Nantucket; and come a stove boat and stove body when they will, for stave my soul, Jove himself cannot.\n\n# CHAPTER 8. The Pulpit.\n\nI had not been seated very long ere a man of a certain venerable robustness entered; immediately as the storm-pelted door flew back upon admitting him, a quick regardful eyeing of him by all the congregation, sufficiently attested that this fine old man was the chaplain. Yes, it was the famous Father Mapple, so called by the whalemen, among whom he was a very great favourite. He had been a sailor and a harpooneer in his youth, but for many years past had dedicated his life to the ministry. At the time I now write of, Father Mapple was in the hardy winter of a healthy old age; that sort of old age which seems merging into a second flowering youth, for among all the fissures of his wrinkles, there shone certain mild gleams of a newly developing bloom—the spring verdure peeping forth even beneath February’s snow. No one having previously heard his history, could for the first time behold Father Mapple without the utmost interest, because there were certain engrafted clerical peculiarities about him, imputable to that adventurous maritime life he had led. When he entered I observed that he carried no umbrella, and certainly had not come in his carriage, for his tarpaulin hat ran down with melting sleet, and his great pilot cloth jacket seemed almost to drag him to the floor with the weight of the water it had absorbed. However, hat and coat and overshoes were one by one removed, and hung up in a little space in an adjacent corner; when, arrayed in a decent suit, he quietly approached the pulpit.\n\nLike most old fashioned pulpits, it was a very lofty one, and since a regular stairs to such a height would, by its long angle with the floor, seriously contract the already small area of the chapel, the architect, it seemed, had acted upon the hint of Father Mapple, and finished the pulpit without a stairs, substituting a perpendicular side ladder, like those used in mounting a ship from a boat at sea. The wife of a whaling captain had provided the chapel with a handsome pair of red worsted man-ropes for this ladder, which, being itself nicely headed, and stained with a mahogany colour, the whole contrivance, considering what manner of chapel it was, seemed by no means in bad taste. Halting for an instant at the foot of the ladder, and with both hands grasping the ornamental knobs of the man-ropes, Father Mapple cast a look upwards, and then with a truly sailor-like but still reverential dexterity, hand over hand, mounted the steps as if ascending the main-top of his vessel.\n\nThe perpendicular parts of this side ladder, as is usually the case with swinging ones, were of cloth-covered rope, only the rounds were of wood, so that at every step there was a joint. At my first glimpse of the pulpit, it had not escaped me that however convenient for a ship, these joints in the present instance seemed unnecessary. For I was not prepared to see Father Mapple after gaining the height, slowly turn round, and stooping over the pulpit, deliberately drag up the ladder step by step, till the whole was deposited within, leaving him impregnable in his little Quebec.\n\nI pondered some time without fully comprehending the reason for this. Father Mapple enjoyed such a wide reputation for sincerity and sanctity, that I could not suspect him of courting notoriety by any mere tricks of the stage. No, thought I, there must be some sober reason for this thing; furthermore, it must symbolize something unseen. Can it be, then, that by that act of physical isolation, he signifies his spiritual withdrawal for the time, from all outward worldly ties and connexions? Yes, for replenished with the meat and wine of the word, to the faithful man of God, this pulpit, I see, is a self-containing stronghold—a lofty Ehrenbreitstein, with a perennial well of water within the walls.\n\nBut the side ladder was not the only strange feature of the place, borrowed from the chaplain’s former sea-farings. Between the marble cenotaphs on either hand of the pulpit, the wall which formed its back was adorned with a large painting representing a gallant ship beating against a terrible storm off a lee coast of black rocks and snowy breakers. But high above the flying scud and dark-rolling clouds, there floated a little isle of sunlight, from which beamed forth an angel’s face; and this bright face shed a distinct spot of radiance upon the ship’s tossed deck, something like that silver plate now inserted into the Victory’s plank where Nelson fell. “Ah, noble ship,” the angel seemed to say, “beat on, beat on, thou noble ship, and bear a hardy helm; for lo! the sun is breaking through; the clouds are rolling off—serenest azure is at hand.”\n\nNor was the pulpit itself without a trace of the same sea-taste that had achieved the ladder and the picture. Its panelled front was in the likeness of a ship’s bluff bows, and the Holy Bible rested on a projecting piece of scroll work, fashioned after a ship’s fiddle-headed beak.\n\nWhat could be more full of meaning?—for the pulpit is ever this earth’s foremost part; all the rest comes in its rear; the pulpit leads the world. From thence it is the storm of God’s quick wrath is first descried, and the bow must bear the earliest brunt. From thence it is the God of breezes fair or foul is first invoked for favourable winds. Yes, the world’s a ship on its passage out, and not a voyage complete; and the pulpit is its prow.\n\n# CHAPTER 9. The Sermon.\n\nFather Mapple rose, and in a mild voice of unassuming authority ordered the scattered people to condense. “Starboard gangway, there! side away to larboard—larboard gangway to starboard! Midships! midships!”\n\nThere was a low rumbling of heavy sea-boots among the benches, and a still slighter shuffling of women’s shoes, and all was quiet again, and every eye on the preacher.\n\nHe paused a little; then kneeling in the pulpit’s bows, folded his large brown hands across his chest, uplifted his closed eyes, and offered a prayer so deeply devout that he seemed kneeling and praying at the bottom of the sea.\n\nThis ended, in prolonged solemn tones, like the continual tolling of a bell in a ship that is foundering at sea in a fog—in such tones he commenced reading the following hymn; but changing his manner towards the concluding stanzas, burst forth with a pealing exultation and joy—\n\n“The ribs and terrors in the whale, Arched over me a dismal gloom, While all God’s sun-lit waves rolled by, And lift me deepening down to doom.\n\n“I saw the opening maw of hell, With endless pains and sorrows there; Which none but they that feel can tell— Oh, I was plunging to despair.\n\n“In black distress, I called my God, When I could scarce believe him mine, He bowed his ear to my complaints— No more the whale did me confine.\n\n“With speed he flew to my relief, As on a radiant dolphin borne; Awful, yet bright, as lightning shone The face of my Deliverer God.\n\n“My song for ever shall record That terrible, that joyful hour; I give the glory to my God, His all the mercy and the power.”\n\nNearly all joined in singing this hymn, which swelled high above the howling of the storm. A brief pause ensued; the preacher slowly turned over the leaves of the Bible, and at last, folding his hand down upon the proper page, said: “Beloved shipmates, clinch the last verse of the first chapter of Jonah—‘And God had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah.’”\n\n“Shipmates, this book, containing only four chapters—four yarns—is one of the smallest strands in the mighty cable of the Scriptures. Yet what depths of the soul does Jonah’s deep sealine sound! what a pregnant lesson to us is this prophet! What a noble thing is that canticle in the fish’s belly! How billow-like and boisterously grand! We feel the floods surging over us; we sound with him to the kelpy bottom of the waters; sea-weed and all the slime of the sea is about us! But what is this lesson that the book of Jonah teaches? Shipmates, it is a two-stranded lesson; a lesson to us all as sinful men, and a lesson to me as a pilot of the living God. As sinful men, it is a lesson to us all, because it is a story of the sin, hard-heartedness, suddenly awakened fears, the swift punishment, repentance, prayers, and finally the deliverance and joy of Jonah. As with all sinners among men, the sin of this son of Amittai was in his wilful disobedience of the command of God—never mind now what that command was, or how conveyed—which he found a hard command. But all the things that God would have us do are hard for us to do—remember that—and hence, he oftener commands us than endeavors to persuade. And if we obey God, we must disobey ourselves; and it is in this disobeying ourselves, wherein the hardness of obeying God consists.\n\n“With this sin of disobedience in him, Jonah still further flouts at God, by seeking to flee from Him. He thinks that a ship made by men will carry him into countries where God does not reign, but only the Captains of this earth. He skulks about the wharves of Joppa, and seeks a ship that’s bound for Tarshish. There lurks, perhaps, a hitherto unheeded meaning here. By all accounts Tarshish could have been no other city than the modern Cadiz. That’s the opinion of learned men. And where is Cadiz, shipmates? Cadiz is in Spain; as far by water, from Joppa, as Jonah could possibly have sailed in those ancient days, when the Atlantic was an almost unknown sea. Because Joppa, the modern Jaffa, shipmates, is on the most easterly coast of the Mediterranean, the Syrian; and Tarshish or Cadiz more than two thousand miles to the westward from that, just outside the Straits of Gibraltar. See ye not then, shipmates, that Jonah sought to flee world-wide from God? Miserable man! Oh! most contemptible and worthy of all scorn; with slouched hat and guilty eye, skulking from his God; prowling among the shipping like a vile burglar hastening to cross the seas. So disordered, self-condemning is his look, that had there been policemen in those days, Jonah, on the mere suspicion of something wrong, had been arrested ere he touched a deck. How plainly he’s a fugitive! no baggage, not a hat-box, valise, or carpet-bag,—no friends accompany him to the wharf with their adieux. At last, after much dodging search, he finds the Tarshish ship receiving the last items of her cargo; and as he steps on board to see its Captain in the cabin, all the sailors for the moment desist from hoisting in the goods, to mark the stranger’s evil eye. Jonah sees this; but in vain he tries to look all ease and confidence; in vain essays his wretched smile. Strong intuitions of the man assure the mariners he can be no innocent. In their gamesome but still serious way, one whispers to the other—“Jack, he’s robbed a widow;” or, “Joe, do you mark him; he’s a bigamist;” or, “Harry lad, I guess he’s the adulterer that broke jail in old Gomorrah, or belike, one of the missing murderers from Sodom.” Another runs to read the bill that’s stuck against the spile upon the wharf to which the ship is moored, offering five hundred gold coins for the apprehension of a parricide, and containing a description of his person. He reads, and looks from Jonah to the bill; while all his sympathetic shipmates now crowd round Jonah, prepared to lay their hands upon him. Frighted Jonah trembles, and summoning all his boldness to his face, only looks so much the more a coward. He will not confess himself suspected; but that itself is strong suspicion. So he makes the best of it; and when the sailors find him not to be the man that is advertised, they let him pass, and he descends into the cabin.\n\n“‘Who’s there?’ cries the Captain at his busy desk, hurriedly making out his papers for the Customs—‘Who’s there?’ Oh! how that harmless question mangles Jonah! For the instant he almost turns to flee again. But he rallies. ‘I seek a passage in this ship to Tarshish; how soon sail ye, sir?’ Thus far the busy Captain had not looked up to Jonah, though the man now stands before him; but no sooner does he hear that hollow voice, than he darts a scrutinizing glance. ‘We sail with the next coming tide,’ at last he slowly answered, still intently eyeing him. ‘No sooner, sir?’—‘Soon enough for any honest man that goes a passenger.’ Ha! Jonah, that’s another stab. But he swiftly calls away the Captain from that scent. ‘I’ll sail with ye,’—he says,—‘the passage money how much is that?—I’ll pay now.’ For it is particularly written, shipmates, as if it were a thing not to be overlooked in this history, ‘that he paid the fare thereof’ ere the craft did sail. And taken with the context, this is full of meaning.\n\n“Now Jonah’s Captain, shipmates, was one whose discernment detects crime in any, but whose cupidity exposes it only in the penniless. In this world, shipmates, sin that pays its way can travel freely, and without a passport; whereas Virtue, if a pauper, is stopped at all frontiers. So Jonah’s Captain prepares to test the length of Jonah’s purse, ere he judge him openly. He charges him thrice the usual sum; and it’s assented to. Then the Captain knows that Jonah is a fugitive; but at the same time resolves to help a flight that paves its rear with gold. Yet when Jonah fairly takes out his purse, prudent suspicions still molest the Captain. He rings every coin to find a counterfeit. Not a forger, any way, he mutters; and Jonah is put down for his passage. ‘Point out my state-room, Sir,’ says Jonah now, ‘I’m travel-weary; I need sleep.’ ‘Thou lookest like it,’ says the Captain, ‘there’s thy room.’ Jonah enters, and would lock the door, but the lock contains no key. Hearing him foolishly fumbling there, the Captain laughs lowly to himself, and mutters something about the doors of convicts’ cells being never allowed to be locked within. All dressed and dusty as he is, Jonah throws himself into his berth, and finds the little state-room ceiling almost resting on his forehead. The air is close, and Jonah gasps. Then, in that contracted hole, sunk, too, beneath the ship’s water-line, Jonah feels the heralding presentiment of that stifling hour, when the whale shall hold him in the smallest of his bowels’ wards.\n\n“Screwed at its axis against the side, a swinging lamp slightly oscillates in Jonah’s room; and the ship, heeling over towards the wharf with the weight of the last bales received, the lamp, flame and all, though in slight motion, still maintains a permanent obliquity with reference to the room; though, in truth, infallibly straight itself, it but made obvious the false, lying levels among which it hung. The lamp alarms and frightens Jonah; as lying in his berth his tormented eyes roll round the place, and this thus far successful fugitive finds no refuge for his restless glance. But that contradiction in the lamp more and more appals him. The floor, the ceiling, and the side, are all awry. ‘Oh! so my conscience hangs in me!’ he groans, ‘straight upwards, so it burns; but the chambers of my soul are all in crookedness!’\n\n“Like one who after a night of drunken revelry hies to his bed, still reeling, but with conscience yet pricking him, as the plungings of the Roman race-horse but so much the more strike his steel tags into him; as one who in that miserable plight still turns and turns in giddy anguish, praying God for annihilation until the fit be passed; and at last amid the whirl of woe he feels, a deep stupor steals over him, as over the man who bleeds to death, for conscience is the wound, and there’s naught to staunch it; so, after sore wrestlings in his berth, Jonah’s prodigy of ponderous misery drags him drowning down to sleep.\n\n“And now the time of tide has come; the ship casts off her cables; and from the deserted wharf the uncheered ship for Tarshish, all careening, glides to sea. That ship, my friends, was the first of recorded smugglers! the contraband was Jonah. But the sea rebels; he will not bear the wicked burden. A dreadful storm comes on, the ship is like to break. But now when the boatswain calls all hands to lighten her; when boxes, bales, and jars are clattering overboard; when the wind is shrieking, and the men are yelling, and every plank thunders with trampling feet right over Jonah’s head; in all this raging tumult, Jonah sleeps his hideous sleep. He sees no black sky and raging sea, feels not the reeling timbers, and little hears he or heeds he the far rush of the mighty whale, which even now with open mouth is cleaving the seas after him. Aye, shipmates, Jonah was gone down into the sides of the ship—a berth in the cabin as I have taken it, and was fast asleep. But the frightened master comes to him, and shrieks in his dead ear, ‘What meanest thou, O, sleeper! arise!’ Startled from his lethargy by that direful cry, Jonah staggers to his feet, and stumbling to the deck, grasps a shroud, to look out upon the sea. But at that moment he is sprung upon by a panther billow leaping over the bulwarks. Wave after wave thus leaps into the ship, and finding no speedy vent runs roaring fore and aft, till the mariners come nigh to drowning while yet afloat. And ever, as the white moon shows her affrighted face from the steep gullies in the blackness overhead, aghast Jonah sees the rearing bowsprit pointing high upward, but soon beat downward again towards the tormented deep.\n\n“Terrors upon terrors run shouting through his soul. In all his cringing attitudes, the God-fugitive is now too plainly known. The sailors mark him; more and more certain grow their suspicions of him, and at last, fully to test the truth, by referring the whole matter to high Heaven, they fall to casting lots, to see for whose cause this great tempest was upon them. The lot is Jonah’s; that discovered, then how furiously they mob him with their questions. ‘What is thine occupation? Whence comest thou? Thy country? What people? But mark now, my shipmates, the behavior of poor Jonah. The eager mariners but ask him who he is, and where from; whereas, they not only receive an answer to those questions, but likewise another answer to a question not put by them, but the unsolicited answer is forced from Jonah by the hard hand of God that is upon him.\n\n“‘I am a Hebrew,’ he cries—and then—‘I fear the Lord the God of Heaven who hath made the sea and the dry land!’ Fear him, O Jonah? Aye, well mightest thou fear the Lord God then! Straightway, he now goes on to make a full confession; whereupon the mariners became more and more appalled, but still are pitiful. For when Jonah, not yet supplicating God for mercy, since he but too well knew the darkness of his deserts,—when wretched Jonah cries out to them to take him and cast him forth into the sea, for he knew that for his sake this great tempest was upon them; they mercifully turn from him, and seek by other means to save the ship. But all in vain; the indignant gale howls louder; then, with one hand raised invokingly to God, with the other they not unreluctantly lay hold of Jonah.\n\n“And now behold Jonah taken up as an anchor and dropped into the sea; when instantly an oily calmness floats out from the east, and the sea is still, as Jonah carries down the gale with him, leaving smooth water behind. He goes down in the whirling heart of such a masterless commotion that he scarce heeds the moment when he drops seething into the yawning jaws awaiting him; and the whale shoots-to all his ivory teeth, like so many white bolts, upon his prison. Then Jonah prayed unto the Lord out of the fish’s belly. But observe his prayer, and learn a weighty lesson. For sinful as he is, Jonah does not weep and wail for direct deliverance. He feels that his dreadful punishment is just. He leaves all his deliverance to God, contenting himself with this, that spite of all his pains and pangs, he will still look towards His holy temple. And here, shipmates, is true and faithful repentance; not clamorous for pardon, but grateful for punishment. And how pleasing to God was this conduct in Jonah, is shown in the eventual deliverance of him from the sea and the whale. Shipmates, I do not place Jonah before you to be copied for his sin but I do place him before you as a model for repentance. Sin not; but if you do, take heed to repent of it like Jonah.”\n\nWhile he was speaking these words, the howling of the shrieking, slanting storm without seemed to add new power to the preacher, who, when describing Jonah’s sea-storm, seemed tossed by a storm himself. His deep chest heaved as with a ground-swell; his tossed arms seemed the warring elements at work; and the thunders that rolled away from off his swarthy brow, and the light leaping from his eye, made all his simple hearers look on him with a quick fear that was strange to them.\n\nThere now came a lull in his look, as he silently turned over the leaves of the Book once more; and, at last, standing motionless, with closed eyes, for the moment, seemed communing with God and himself.\n\nBut again he leaned over towards the people, and bowing his head lowly, with an aspect of the deepest yet manliest humility, he spake these words:\n\n“Shipmates, God has laid but one hand upon you; both his hands press upon me. I have read ye by what murky light may be mine the lesson that Jonah teaches to all sinners; and therefore to ye, and still more to me, for I am a greater sinner than ye. And now how gladly would I come down from this mast-head and sit on the hatches there where you sit, and listen as you listen, while some one of you reads me that other and more awful lesson which Jonah teaches to me, as a pilot of the living God. How being an anointed pilot-prophet, or speaker of true things, and bidden by the Lord to sound those unwelcome truths in the ears of a wicked Nineveh, Jonah, appalled at the hostility he should raise, fled from his mission, and sought to escape his duty and his God by taking ship at Joppa. But God is everywhere; Tarshish he never reached. As we have seen, God came upon him in the whale, and swallowed him down to living gulfs of doom, and with swift slantings tore him along ‘into the midst of the seas,’ where the eddying depths sucked him ten thousand fathoms down, and ‘the weeds were wrapped about his head,’ and all the watery world of woe bowled over him. Yet even then beyond the reach of any plummet—‘out of the belly of hell’—when the whale grounded upon the ocean’s utmost bones, even then, God heard the engulphed, repenting prophet when he cried. Then God spake unto the fish; and from the shuddering cold and blackness of the sea, the whale came breeching up towards the warm and pleasant sun, and all the delights of air and earth; and ‘vomited out Jonah upon the dry land;’ when the word of the Lord came a second time; and Jonah, bruised and beaten—his ears, like two sea-shells, still multitudinously murmuring of the ocean—Jonah did the Almighty’s bidding. And what was that, shipmates? To preach the Truth to the face of Falsehood! That was it!\n\n“This, shipmates, this is that other lesson; and woe to that pilot of the living God who slights it. Woe to him whom this world charms from Gospel duty! Woe to him who seeks to pour oil upon the waters when God has brewed them into a gale! Woe to him who seeks to please rather than to appal! Woe to him whose good name is more to him than goodness! Woe to him who, in this world, courts not dishonor! Woe to him who would not be true, even though to be false were salvation! Yea, woe to him who, as the great Pilot Paul has it, while preaching to others is himself a castaway!”\n\nHe dropped and fell away from himself for a moment; then lifting his face to them again, showed a deep joy in his eyes, as he cried out with a heavenly enthusiasm,—“But oh! shipmates! on the starboard hand of every woe, there is a sure delight; and higher the top of that delight, than the bottom of the woe is deep. Is not the main-truck higher than the kelson is low? Delight is to him—a far, far upward, and inward delight—who against the proud gods and commodores of this earth, ever stands forth his own inexorable self. Delight is to him whose strong arms yet support him, when the ship of this base treacherous world has gone down beneath him. Delight is to him, who gives no quarter in the truth, and kills, burns, and destroys all sin though he pluck it out from under the robes of Senators and Judges. Delight,—top-gallant delight is to him, who acknowledges no law or lord, but the Lord his God, and is only a patriot to heaven. Delight is to him, whom all the waves of the billows of the seas of the boisterous mob can never shake from this sure Keel of the Ages. And eternal delight and deliciousness will be his, who coming to lay him down, can say with his final breath—O Father!—chiefly known to me by Thy rod—mortal or immortal, here I die. I have striven to be Thine, more than to be this world’s, or mine own. Yet this is nothing: I leave eternity to Thee; for what is man that he should live out the lifetime of his God?”\n\nHe said no more, but slowly waving a benediction, covered his face with his hands, and so remained kneeling, till all the people had departed, and he was left alone in the place.\n\n# CHAPTER 10. A Bosom Friend.\n\nReturning to the Spouter-Inn from the Chapel, I found Queequeg there quite alone; he having left the Chapel before the benediction some time. He was sitting on a bench before the fire, with his feet on the stove hearth, and in one hand was holding close up to his face that little negro idol of his; peering hard into its face, and with a jack-knife gently whittling away at its nose, meanwhile humming to himself in his heathenish way.\n\nBut being now interrupted, he put up the image; and pretty soon, going to the table, took up a large book there, and placing it on his lap began counting the pages with deliberate regularity; at every fiftieth page—as I fancied—stopping a moment, looking vacantly around him, and giving utterance to a long-drawn gurgling whistle of astonishment. He would then begin again at the next fifty; seeming to commence at number one each time, as though he could not count more than fifty, and it was only by such a large number of fifties being found together, that his astonishment at the multitude of pages was excited.\n\nWith much interest I sat watching him. Savage though he was, and hideously marred about the face—at least to my taste—his countenance yet had a something in it which was by no means disagreeable. You cannot hide the soul. Through all his unearthly tattooings, I thought I saw the traces of a simple honest heart; and in his large, deep eyes, fiery black and bold, there seemed tokens of a spirit that would dare a thousand devils. And besides all this, there was a certain lofty bearing about the Pagan, which even his uncouthness could not altogether maim. He looked like a man who had never cringed and never had had a creditor. Whether it was, too, that his head being shaved, his forehead was drawn out in freer and brighter relief, and looked more expansive than it otherwise would, this I will not venture to decide; but certain it was his head was phrenologically an excellent one. It may seem ridiculous, but it reminded me of General Washington’s head, as seen in the popular busts of him. It had the same long regularly graded retreating slope from above the brows, which were likewise very projecting, like two long promontories thickly wooded on top. Queequeg was George Washington cannibalistically developed.\n\nWhilst I was thus closely scanning him, half-pretending meanwhile to be looking out at the storm from the casement, he never heeded my presence, never troubled himself with so much as a single glance; but appeared wholly occupied with counting the pages of the marvellous book. Considering how sociably we had been sleeping together the night previous, and especially considering the affectionate arm I had found thrown over me upon waking in the morning, I thought this indifference of his very strange. But savages are strange beings; at times you do not know exactly how to take them. At first they are overawing; their calm self-collectedness of simplicity seems a Socratic wisdom. I had noticed also that Queequeg never consorted at all, or but very little, with the other seamen in the inn. He made no advances whatever; appeared to have no desire to enlarge the circle of his acquaintances. All this struck me as mighty singular; yet, upon second thoughts, there was something almost sublime in it. Here was a man some twenty thousand miles from home, by the way of Cape Horn, that is—which was the only way he could get there—thrown among people as strange to him as though he were in the planet Jupiter; and yet he seemed entirely at his ease; preserving the utmost serenity; content with his own companionship; always equal to himself. Surely this was a touch of fine philosophy; though no doubt he had never heard there was such a thing as that. But, perhaps, to be true philosophers, we mortals should not be conscious of so living or so striving. So soon as I hear that such or such a man gives himself out for a philosopher, I conclude that, like the dyspeptic old woman, he must have “broken his digester.”\n\nAs I sat there in that now lonely room; the fire burning low, in that mild stage when, after its first intensity has warmed the air, it then only glows to be looked at; the evening shades and phantoms gathering round the casements, and peering in upon us silent, solitary twain; the storm booming without in solemn swells; I began to be sensible of strange feelings. I felt a melting in me. No more my splintered heart and maddened hand were turned against the wolfish world. This soothing savage had redeemed it. There he sat, his very indifference speaking a nature in which there lurked no civilized hypocrisies and bland deceits. Wild he was; a very sight of sights to see; yet I began to feel myself mysteriously drawn towards him. And those same things that would have repelled most others, they were the very magnets that thus drew me. I’ll try a pagan friend, thought I, since Christian kindness has proved but hollow courtesy. I drew my bench near him, and made some friendly signs and hints, doing my best to talk with him meanwhile. At first he little noticed these advances; but presently, upon my referring to his last night’s hospitalities, he made out to ask me whether we were again to be bedfellows. I told him yes; whereat I thought he looked pleased, perhaps a little complimented.\n\nWe then turned over the book together, and I endeavored to explain to him the purpose of the printing, and the meaning of the few pictures that were in it. Thus I soon engaged his interest; and from that we went to jabbering the best we could about the various outer sights to be seen in this famous town. Soon I proposed a social smoke; and, producing his pouch and tomahawk, he quietly offered me a puff. And then we sat exchanging puffs from that wild pipe of his, and keeping it regularly passing between us.\n\nIf there yet lurked any ice of indifference towards me in the Pagan’s breast, this pleasant, genial smoke we had, soon thawed it out, and left us cronies. He seemed to take to me quite as naturally and unbiddenly as I to him; and when our smoke was over, he pressed his forehead against mine, clasped me round the waist, and said that henceforth we were married; meaning, in his country’s phrase, that we were bosom friends; he would gladly die for me, if need should be. In a countryman, this sudden flame of friendship would have seemed far too premature, a thing to be much distrusted; but in this simple savage those old rules would not apply.\n\nAfter supper, and another social chat and smoke, we went to our room together. He made me a present of his embalmed head; took out his enormous tobacco wallet, and groping under the tobacco, drew out some thirty dollars in silver; then spreading them on the table, and mechanically dividing them into two equal portions, pushed one of them towards me, and said it was mine. I was going to remonstrate; but he silenced me by pouring them into my trowsers’ pockets. I let them stay. He then went about his evening prayers, took out his idol, and removed the paper fireboard. By certain signs and symptoms, I thought he seemed anxious for me to join him; but well knowing what was to follow, I deliberated a moment whether, in case he invited me, I would comply or otherwise.\n\nI was a good Christian; born and bred in the bosom of the infallible Presbyterian Church. How then could I unite with this wild idolator in worshipping his piece of wood? But what is worship? thought I. Do you suppose now, Ishmael, that the magnanimous God of heaven and earth—pagans and all included—can possibly be jealous of an insignificant bit of black wood? Impossible! But what is worship?—to do the will of God—that is worship. And what is the will of God?—to do to my fellow man what I would have my fellow man to do to me—that is the will of God. Now, Queequeg is my fellow man. And what do I wish that this Queequeg would do to me? Why, unite with me in my particular Presbyterian form of worship. Consequently, I must then unite with him in his; ergo, I must turn idolator. So I kindled the shavings; helped prop up the innocent little idol; offered him burnt biscuit with Queequeg; salamed before him twice or thrice; kissed his nose; and that done, we undressed and went to bed, at peace with our own consciences and all the world. But we did not go to sleep without some little chat.\n\nHow it is I know not; but there is no place like a bed for confidential disclosures between friends. Man and wife, they say, there open the very bottom of their souls to each other; and some old couples often lie and chat over old times till nearly morning. Thus, then, in our hearts’ honeymoon, lay I and Queequeg—a cosy, loving pair.\n\n# CHAPTER 11. Nightgown.\n\nWe had lain thus in bed, chatting and napping at short intervals, and Queequeg now and then affectionately throwing his brown tattooed legs over mine, and then drawing them back; so entirely sociable and free and easy were we; when, at last, by reason of our confabulations, what little nappishness remained in us altogether departed, and we felt like getting up again, though day-break was yet some way down the future.\n\nYes, we became very wakeful; so much so that our recumbent position began to grow wearisome, and by little and little we found ourselves sitting up; the clothes well tucked around us, leaning against the head-board with our four knees drawn up close together, and our two noses bending over them, as if our kneepans were warming-pans. We felt very nice and snug, the more so since it was so chilly out of doors; indeed out of bed-clothes too, seeing that there was no fire in the room. The more so, I say, because truly to enjoy bodily warmth, some small part of you must be cold, for there is no quality in this world that is not what it is merely by contrast. Nothing exists in itself. If you flatter yourself that you are all over comfortable, and have been so a long time, then you cannot be said to be comfortable any more. But if, like Queequeg and me in the bed, the tip of your nose or the crown of your head be slightly chilled, why then, indeed, in the general consciousness you feel most delightfully and unmistakably warm. For this reason a sleeping apartment should never be furnished with a fire, which is one of the luxurious discomforts of the rich. For the height of this sort of deliciousness is to have nothing but the blanket between you and your snugness and the cold of the outer air. Then there you lie like the one warm spark in the heart of an arctic crystal.\n\nWe had been sitting in this crouching manner for some time, when all at once I thought I would open my eyes; for when between sheets, whether by day or by night, and whether asleep or awake, I have a way of always keeping my eyes shut, in order the more to concentrate the snugness of being in bed. Because no man can ever feel his own identity aright except his eyes be closed; as if darkness were indeed the proper element of our essences, though light be more congenial to our clayey part. Upon opening my eyes then, and coming out of my own pleasant and self-created darkness into the imposed and coarse outer gloom of the unilluminated twelve-o’clock-at-night, I experienced a disagreeable revulsion. Nor did I at all object to the hint from Queequeg that perhaps it were best to strike a light, seeing that we were so wide awake; and besides he felt a strong desire to have a few quiet puffs from his Tomahawk. Be it said, that though I had felt such a strong repugnance to his smoking in the bed the night before, yet see how elastic our stiff prejudices grow when love once comes to bend them. For now I liked nothing better than to have Queequeg smoking by me, even in bed, because he seemed to be full of such serene household joy then. I no more felt unduly concerned for the landlord’s policy of insurance. I was only alive to the condensed confidential comfortableness of sharing a pipe and a blanket with a real friend. With our shaggy jackets drawn about our shoulders, we now passed the Tomahawk from one to the other, till slowly there grew over us a blue hanging tester of smoke, illuminated by the flame of the new-lit lamp.\n\nWhether it was that this undulating tester rolled the savage away to far distant scenes, I know not, but he now spoke of his native island; and, eager to hear his history, I begged him to go on and tell it. He gladly complied. Though at the time I but ill comprehended not a few of his words, yet subsequent disclosures, when I had become more familiar with his broken phraseology, now enable me to present the whole story such as it may prove in the mere skeleton I give.\n\n# CHAPTER 12. Biographical.\n\nQueequeg was a native of Rokovoko, an island far away to the West and South. It is not down in any map; true places never are.\n\nWhen a new-hatched savage running wild about his native woodlands in a grass clout, followed by the nibbling goats, as if he were a green sapling; even then, in Queequeg’s ambitious soul, lurked a strong desire to see something more of Christendom than a specimen whaler or two. His father was a High Chief, a King; his uncle a High Priest; and on the maternal side he boasted aunts who were the wives of unconquerable warriors. There was excellent blood in his veins—royal stuff; though sadly vitiated, I fear, by the cannibal propensity he nourished in his untutored youth.\n\nA Sag Harbor ship visited his father’s bay, and Queequeg sought a passage to Christian lands. But the ship, having her full complement of seamen, spurned his suit; and not all the King his father’s influence could prevail. But Queequeg vowed a vow. Alone in his canoe, he paddled off to a distant strait, which he knew the ship must pass through when she quitted the island. On one side was a coral reef; on the other a low tongue of land, covered with mangrove thickets that grew out into the water. Hiding his canoe, still afloat, among these thickets, with its prow seaward, he sat down in the stern, paddle low in hand; and when the ship was gliding by, like a flash he darted out; gained her side; with one backward dash of his foot capsized and sank his canoe; climbed up the chains; and throwing himself at full length upon the deck, grappled a ring-bolt there, and swore not to let it go, though hacked in pieces.\n\nIn vain the captain threatened to throw him overboard; suspended a cutlass over his naked wrists; Queequeg was the son of a King, and Queequeg budged not. Struck by his desperate dauntlessness, and his wild desire to visit Christendom, the captain at last relented, and told him he might make himself at home. But this fine young savage—this sea Prince of Wales, never saw the Captain’s cabin. They put him down among the sailors, and made a whaleman of him. But like Czar Peter content to toil in the shipyards of foreign cities, Queequeg disdained no seeming ignominy, if thereby he might happily gain the power of enlightening his untutored countrymen. For at bottom—so he told me—he was actuated by a profound desire to learn among the Christians, the arts whereby to make his people still happier than they were; and more than that, still better than they were. But, alas! the practices of whalemen soon convinced him that even Christians could be both miserable and wicked; infinitely more so, than all his father’s heathens. Arrived at last in old Sag Harbor; and seeing what the sailors did there; and then going on to Nantucket, and seeing how they spent their wages in that place also, poor Queequeg gave it up for lost. Thought he, it’s a wicked world in all meridians; I’ll die a pagan.\n\nAnd thus an old idolator at heart, he yet lived among these Christians, wore their clothes, and tried to talk their gibberish. Hence the queer ways about him, though now some time from home.\n\nBy hints, I asked him whether he did not propose going back, and having a coronation; since he might now consider his father dead and gone, he being very old and feeble at the last accounts. He answered no, not yet; and added that he was fearful Christianity, or rather Christians, had unfitted him for ascending the pure and undefiled throne of thirty pagan Kings before him. But by and by, he said, he would return,—as soon as he felt himself baptized again. For the nonce, however, he proposed to sail about, and sow his wild oats in all four oceans. They had made a harpooneer of him, and that barbed iron was in lieu of a sceptre now.\n\nI asked him what might be his immediate purpose, touching his future movements. He answered, to go to sea again, in his old vocation. Upon this, I told him that whaling was my own design, and informed him of my intention to sail out of Nantucket, as being the most promising port for an adventurous whaleman to embark from. He at once resolved to accompany me to that island, ship aboard the same vessel, get into the same watch, the same boat, the same mess with me, in short to share my every hap; with both my hands in his, boldly dip into the Potluck of both worlds. To all this I joyously assented; for besides the affection I now felt for Queequeg, he was an experienced harpooneer, and as such, could not fail to be of great usefulness to one, who, like me, was wholly ignorant of the mysteries of whaling, though well acquainted with the sea, as known to merchant seamen.\n\nHis story being ended with his pipe’s last dying puff, Queequeg embraced me, pressed his forehead against mine, and blowing out the light, we rolled over from each other, this way and that, and very soon were sleeping.\n\n# CHAPTER 13. Wheelbarrow.\n\nNext morning, Monday, after disposing of the embalmed head to a barber, for a block, I settled my own and comrade’s bill; using, however, my comrade’s money. The grinning landlord, as well as the boarders, seemed amazingly tickled at the sudden friendship which had sprung up between me and Queequeg—especially as Peter Coffin’s cock and bull stories about him had previously so much alarmed me concerning the very person whom I now companied with.\n\nWe borrowed a wheelbarrow, and embarking our things, including my own poor carpet-bag, and Queequeg’s canvas sack and hammock, away we went down to “the Moss,” the little Nantucket packet schooner moored at the wharf. As we were going along the people stared; not at Queequeg so much—for they were used to seeing cannibals like him in their streets,—but at seeing him and me upon such confidential terms. But we heeded them not, going along wheeling the barrow by turns, and Queequeg now and then stopping to adjust the sheath on his harpoon barbs. I asked him why he carried such a troublesome thing with him ashore, and whether all whaling ships did not find their own harpoons. To this, in substance, he replied, that though what I hinted was true enough, yet he had a particular affection for his own harpoon, because it was of assured stuff, well tried in many a mortal combat, and deeply intimate with the hearts of whales. In short, like many inland reapers and mowers, who go into the farmers’ meadows armed with their own scythes—though in no wise obliged to furnish them—even so, Queequeg, for his own private reasons, preferred his own harpoon.\n\nShifting the barrow from my hand to his, he told me a funny story about the first wheelbarrow he had ever seen. It was in Sag Harbor. The owners of his ship, it seems, had lent him one, in which to carry his heavy chest to his boarding house. Not to seem ignorant about the thing—though in truth he was entirely so, concerning the precise way in which to manage the barrow—Queequeg puts his chest upon it; lashes it fast; and then shoulders the barrow and marches up the wharf. “Why,” said I, “Queequeg, you might have known better than that, one would think. Didn’t the people laugh?”\n\nUpon this, he told me another story. The people of his island of Rokovoko, it seems, at their wedding feasts express the fragrant water of young cocoanuts into a large stained calabash like a punchbowl; and this punchbowl always forms the great central ornament on the braided mat where the feast is held. Now a certain grand merchant ship once touched at Rokovoko, and its commander—from all accounts, a very stately punctilious gentleman, at least for a sea captain—this commander was invited to the wedding feast of Queequeg’s sister, a pretty young princess just turned of ten. Well; when all the wedding guests were assembled at the bride’s bamboo cottage, this Captain marches in, and being assigned the post of honor, placed himself over against the punchbowl, and between the High Priest and his majesty the King, Queequeg’s father. Grace being said,—for those people have their grace as well as we—though Queequeg told me that unlike us, who at such times look downwards to our platters, they, on the contrary, copying the ducks, glance upwards to the great Giver of all feasts—Grace, I say, being said, the High Priest opens the banquet by the immemorial ceremony of the island; that is, dipping his consecrated and consecrating fingers into the bowl before the blessed beverage circulates. Seeing himself placed next the Priest, and noting the ceremony, and thinking himself—being Captain of a ship—as having plain precedence over a mere island King, especially in the King’s own house—the Captain coolly proceeds to wash his hands in the punchbowl;—taking it I suppose for a huge finger-glass. “Now,” said Queequeg, “what you tink now?—Didn’t our people laugh?”\n\nAt last, passage paid, and luggage safe, we stood on board the schooner. Hoisting sail, it glided down the Acushnet river. On one side, New Bedford rose in terraces of streets, their ice-covered trees all glittering in the clear, cold air. Huge hills and mountains of casks on casks were piled upon her wharves, and side by side the world-wandering whale ships lay silent and safely moored at last; while from others came a sound of carpenters and coopers, with blended noises of fires and forges to melt the pitch, all betokening that new cruises were on the start; that one most perilous and long voyage ended, only begins a second; and a second ended, only begins a third, and so on, for ever and for aye. Such is the endlessness, yea, the intolerableness of all earthly effort.\n\nGaining the more open water, the bracing breeze waxed fresh; the little Moss tossed the quick foam from her bows, as a young colt his snortings. How I snuffed that Tartar air!—how I spurned that turnpike earth!—that common highway all over dented with the marks of slavish heels and hoofs; and turned me to admire the magnanimity of the sea which will permit no records.\n\nAt the same foam-fountain, Queequeg seemed to drink and reel with me. His dusky nostrils swelled apart; he showed his filed and pointed teeth. On, on we flew; and our offing gained, the Moss did homage to the blast; ducked and dived her bows as a slave before the Sultan. Sideways leaning, we sideways darted; every ropeyarn tingling like a wire; the two tall masts buckling like Indian canes in land tornadoes. So full of this reeling scene were we, as we stood by the plunging bowsprit, that for some time we did not notice the jeering glances of the passengers, a lubber-like assembly, who marvelled that two fellow beings should be so companionable; as though a white man were anything more dignified than a whitewashed negro. But there were some boobies and bumpkins there, who, by their intense greenness, must have come from the heart and centre of all verdure. Queequeg caught one of these young saplings mimicking him behind his back. I thought the bumpkin’s hour of doom was come. Dropping his harpoon, the brawny savage caught him in his arms, and by an almost miraculous dexterity and strength, sent him high up bodily into the air; then slightly tapping his stern in mid-somerset, the fellow landed with bursting lungs upon his feet, while Queequeg, turning his back upon him, lighted his tomahawk pipe and passed it to me for a puff.\n\n“Capting! Capting!” yelled the bumpkin, running towards that officer; “Capting, Capting, here’s the devil.”\n\n“Hallo, you sir,” cried the Captain, a gaunt rib of the sea, stalking up to Queequeg, “what in thunder do you mean by that? Don’t you know you might have killed that chap?”\n\n“What him say?” said Queequeg, as he mildly turned to me.\n\n“He say,” said I, “that you came near kill-e that man there,” pointing to the still shivering greenhorn.\n\n“Kill-e,” cried Queequeg, twisting his tattooed face into an unearthly expression of disdain, “ah! him bevy small-e fish-e; Queequeg no kill-e so small-e fish-e; Queequeg kill-e big whale!”\n\n“Look you,” roared the Captain, “I’ll kill-e you, you cannibal, if you try any more of your tricks aboard here; so mind your eye.”\n\nBut it so happened just then, that it was high time for the Captain to mind his own eye. The prodigious strain upon the main-sail had parted the weather-sheet, and the tremendous boom was now flying from side to side, completely sweeping the entire after part of the deck. The poor fellow whom Queequeg had handled so roughly, was swept overboard; all hands were in a panic; and to attempt snatching at the boom to stay it, seemed madness. It flew from right to left, and back again, almost in one ticking of a watch, and every instant seemed on the point of snapping into splinters. Nothing was done, and nothing seemed capable of being done; those on deck rushed towards the bows, and stood eyeing the boom as if it were the lower jaw of an exasperated whale. In the midst of this consternation, Queequeg dropped deftly to his knees, and crawling under the path of the boom, whipped hold of a rope, secured one end to the bulwarks, and then flinging the other like a lasso, caught it round the boom as it swept over his head, and at the next jerk, the spar was that way trapped, and all was safe. The schooner was run into the wind, and while the hands were clearing away the stern boat, Queequeg, stripped to the waist, darted from the side with a long living arc of a leap. For three minutes or more he was seen swimming like a dog, throwing his long arms straight out before him, and by turns revealing his brawny shoulders through the freezing foam. I looked at the grand and glorious fellow, but saw no one to be saved. The greenhorn had gone down. Shooting himself perpendicularly from the water, Queequeg, now took an instant’s glance around him, and seeming to see just how matters were, dived down and disappeared. A few minutes more, and he rose again, one arm still striking out, and with the other dragging a lifeless form. The boat soon picked them up. The poor bumpkin was restored. All hands voted Queequeg a noble trump; the captain begged his pardon. From that hour I clove to Queequeg like a barnacle; yea, till poor Queequeg took his last long dive.\n\nWas there ever such unconsciousness? He did not seem to think that he at all deserved a medal from the Humane and Magnanimous Societies. He only asked for water—fresh water—something to wipe the brine off; that done, he put on dry clothes, lighted his pipe, and leaning against the bulwarks, and mildly eyeing those around him, seemed to be saying to himself—“It’s a mutual, joint-stock world, in all meridians. We cannibals must help these Christians.”\n\n# CHAPTER 14. Nantucket.\n\nNothing more happened on the passage worthy the mentioning; so, after a fine run, we safely arrived in Nantucket.\n\nNantucket! Take out your map and look at it. See what a real corner of the world it occupies; how it stands there, away off shore, more lonely than the Eddystone lighthouse. Look at it—a mere hillock, and elbow of sand; all beach, without a background. There is more sand there than you would use in twenty years as a substitute for blotting paper. Some gamesome wights will tell you that they have to plant weeds there, they don’t grow naturally; that they import Canada thistles; that they have to send beyond seas for a spile to stop a leak in an oil cask; that pieces of wood in Nantucket are carried about like bits of the true cross in Rome; that people there plant toadstools before their houses, to get under the shade in summer time; that one blade of grass makes an oasis, three blades in a day’s walk a prairie; that they wear quicksand shoes, something like Laplander snow-shoes; that they are so shut up, belted about, every way inclosed, surrounded, and made an utter island of by the ocean, that to their very chairs and tables small clams will sometimes be found adhering, as to the backs of sea turtles. But these extravaganzas only show that Nantucket is no Illinois.\n\nLook now at the wondrous traditional story of how this island was settled by the red-men. Thus goes the legend. In olden times an eagle swooped down upon the New England coast, and carried off an infant Indian in his talons. With loud lament the parents saw their child borne out of sight over the wide waters. They resolved to follow in the same direction. Setting out in their canoes, after a perilous passage they discovered the island, and there they found an empty ivory casket,—the poor little Indian’s skeleton.\n\nWhat wonder, then, that these Nantucketers, born on a beach, should take to the sea for a livelihood! They first caught crabs and quohogs in the sand; grown bolder, they waded out with nets for mackerel; more experienced, they pushed off in boats and captured cod; and at last, launching a navy of great ships on the sea, explored this watery world; put an incessant belt of circumnavigations round it; peeped in at Behring’s Straits; and in all seasons and all oceans declared everlasting war with the mightiest animated mass that has survived the flood; most monstrous and most mountainous! That Himmalehan, salt-sea Mastodon, clothed with such portentousness of unconscious power, that his very panics are more to be dreaded than his most fearless and malicious assaults!\n\nAnd thus have these naked Nantucketers, these sea hermits, issuing from their ant-hill in the sea, overrun and conquered the watery world like so many Alexanders; parcelling out among them the Atlantic, Pacific, and Indian oceans, as the three pirate powers did Poland. Let America add Mexico to Texas, and pile Cuba upon Canada; let the English overswarm all India, and hang out their blazing banner from the sun; two thirds of this terraqueous globe are the Nantucketer’s. For the sea is his; he owns it, as Emperors own empires; other seamen having but a right of way through it. Merchant ships are but extension bridges; armed ones but floating forts; even pirates and privateers, though following the sea as highwaymen the road, they but plunder other ships, other fragments of the land like themselves, without seeking to draw their living from the bottomless deep itself. The Nantucketer, he alone resides and riots on the sea; he alone, in Bible language, goes down to it in ships; to and fro ploughing it as his own special plantation. There is his home; there lies his business, which a Noah’s flood would not interrupt, though it overwhelmed all the millions in China. He lives on the sea, as prairie cocks in the prairie; he hides among the waves, he climbs them as chamois hunters climb the Alps. For years he knows not the land; so that when he comes to it at last, it smells like another world, more strangely than the moon would to an Earthsman. With the landless gull, that at sunset folds her wings and is rocked to sleep between billows; so at nightfall, the Nantucketer, out of sight of land, furls his sails, and lays him to his rest, while under his very pillow rush herds of walruses and whales.\n\n# CHAPTER 15. Chowder.\n\nIt was quite late in the evening when the little Moss came snugly to anchor, and Queequeg and I went ashore; so we could attend to no business that day, at least none but a supper and a bed. The landlord of the Spouter-Inn had recommended us to his cousin Hosea Hussey of the Try Pots, whom he asserted to be the proprietor of one of the best kept hotels in all Nantucket, and moreover he had assured us that Cousin Hosea, as he called him, was famous for his chowders. In short, he plainly hinted that we could not possibly do better than try pot-luck at the Try Pots. But the directions he had given us about keeping a yellow warehouse on our starboard hand till we opened a white church to the larboard, and then keeping that on the larboard hand till we made a corner three points to the starboard, and that done, then ask the first man we met where the place was: these crooked directions of his very much puzzled us at first, especially as, at the outset, Queequeg insisted that the yellow warehouse—our first point of departure—must be left on the larboard hand, whereas I had understood Peter Coffin to say it was on the starboard. However, by dint of beating about a little in the dark, and now and then knocking up a peaceable inhabitant to inquire the way, we at last came to something which there was no mistaking.\n\nTwo enormous wooden pots painted black, and suspended by asses’ ears, swung from the cross-trees of an old top-mast, planted in front of an old doorway. The horns of the cross-trees were sawed off on the other side, so that this old top-mast looked not a little like a gallows. Perhaps I was over sensitive to such impressions at the time, but I could not help staring at this gallows with a vague misgiving. A sort of crick was in my neck as I gazed up to the two remaining horns; yes, two of them, one for Queequeg, and one for me. It’s ominous, thinks I. A Coffin my Innkeeper upon landing in my first whaling port; tombstones staring at me in the whalemen’s chapel; and here a gallows! and a pair of prodigious black pots too! Are these last throwing out oblique hints touching Tophet?\n\nI was called from these reflections by the sight of a freckled woman with yellow hair and a yellow gown, standing in the porch of the inn, under a dull red lamp swinging there, that looked much like an injured eye, and carrying on a brisk scolding with a man in a purple woollen shirt.\n\n“Get along with ye,” said she to the man, “or I’ll be combing ye!”\n\n“Come on, Queequeg,” said I, “all right. There’s Mrs. Hussey.”\n\nAnd so it turned out; Mr. Hosea Hussey being from home, but leaving Mrs. Hussey entirely competent to attend to all his affairs. Upon making known our desires for a supper and a bed, Mrs. Hussey, postponing further scolding for the present, ushered us into a little room, and seating us at a table spread with the relics of a recently concluded repast, turned round to us and said—“Clam or Cod?”\n\n“What’s that about Cods, ma’am?” said I, with much politeness.\n\n“Clam or Cod?” she repeated.\n\n“A clam for supper? a cold clam; is that what you mean, Mrs. Hussey?” says I, “but that’s a rather cold and clammy reception in the winter time, ain’t it, Mrs. Hussey?”\n\nBut being in a great hurry to resume scolding the man in the purple Shirt, who was waiting for it in the entry, and seeming to hear nothing but the word “clam,” Mrs. Hussey hurried towards an open door leading to the kitchen, and bawling out “clam for two,” disappeared.\n\n“Queequeg,” said I, “do you think that we can make out a supper for us both on one clam?”\n\nHowever, a warm savory steam from the kitchen served to belie the apparently cheerless prospect before us. But when that smoking chowder came in, the mystery was delightfully explained. Oh, sweet friends! hearken to me. It was made of small juicy clams, scarcely bigger than hazel nuts, mixed with pounded ship biscuit, and salted pork cut up into little flakes; the whole enriched with butter, and plentifully seasoned with pepper and salt. Our appetites being sharpened by the frosty voyage, and in particular, Queequeg seeing his favourite fishing food before him, and the chowder being surpassingly excellent, we despatched it with great expedition: when leaning back a moment and bethinking me of Mrs. Hussey’s clam and cod announcement, I thought I would try a little experiment. Stepping to the kitchen door, I uttered the word “cod” with great emphasis, and resumed my seat. In a few moments the savoury steam came forth again, but with a different flavor, and in good time a fine cod-chowder was placed before us.\n\nWe resumed business; and while plying our spoons in the bowl, thinks I to myself, I wonder now if this here has any effect on the head? What’s that stultifying saying about chowder-headed people? “But look, Queequeg, ain’t that a live eel in your bowl? Where’s your harpoon?”\n\nFishiest of all fishy places was the Try Pots, which well deserved its name; for the pots there were always boiling chowders. Chowder for breakfast, and chowder for dinner, and chowder for supper, till you began to look for fish-bones coming through your clothes. The area before the house was paved with clam-shells. Mrs. Hussey wore a polished necklace of codfish vertebra; and Hosea Hussey had his account books bound in superior old shark-skin. There was a fishy flavor to the milk, too, which I could not at all account for, till one morning happening to take a stroll along the beach among some fishermen’s boats, I saw Hosea’s brindled cow feeding on fish remnants, and marching along the sand with each foot in a cod’s decapitated head, looking very slip-shod, I assure ye.\n\nSupper concluded, we received a lamp, and directions from Mrs. Hussey concerning the nearest way to bed; but, as Queequeg was about to precede me up the stairs, the lady reached forth her arm, and demanded his harpoon; she allowed no harpoon in her chambers. “Why not?” said I; “every true whaleman sleeps with his harpoon—but why not?” “Because it’s dangerous,” says she. “Ever since young Stiggs coming from that unfort’nt v’y’ge of his, when he was gone four years and a half, with only three barrels of ile, was found dead in my first floor back, with his harpoon in his side; ever since then I allow no boarders to take sich dangerous weepons in their rooms at night. So, Mr. Queequeg” (for she had learned his name), “I will just take this here iron, and keep it for you till morning. But the chowder; clam or cod to-morrow for breakfast, men?”\n\n“Both,” says I; “and let’s have a couple of smoked herring by way of variety.”\n\n# CHAPTER 16. The Ship.\n\nIn bed we concocted our plans for the morrow. But to my surprise and no small concern, Queequeg now gave me to understand, that he had been diligently consulting Yojo—the name of his black little god—and Yojo had told him two or three times over, and strongly insisted upon it everyway, that instead of our going together among the whaling-fleet in harbor, and in concert selecting our craft; instead of this, I say, Yojo earnestly enjoined that the selection of the ship should rest wholly with me, inasmuch as Yojo purposed befriending us; and, in order to do so, had already pitched upon a vessel, which, if left to myself, I, Ishmael, should infallibly light upon, for all the world as though it had turned out by chance; and in that vessel I must immediately ship myself, for the present irrespective of Queequeg.\n\nI have forgotten to mention that, in many things, Queequeg placed great confidence in the excellence of Yojo’s judgment and surprising forecast of things; and cherished Yojo with considerable esteem, as a rather good sort of god, who perhaps meant well enough upon the whole, but in all cases did not succeed in his benevolent designs.\n\nNow, this plan of Queequeg’s, or rather Yojo’s, touching the selection of our craft; I did not like that plan at all. I had not a little relied upon Queequeg’s sagacity to point out the whaler best fitted to carry us and our fortunes securely. But as all my remonstrances produced no effect upon Queequeg, I was obliged to acquiesce; and accordingly prepared to set about this business with a determined rushing sort of energy and vigor, that should quickly settle that trifling little affair. Next morning early, leaving Queequeg shut up with Yojo in our little bedroom—for it seemed that it was some sort of Lent or Ramadan, or day of fasting, humiliation, and prayer with Queequeg and Yojo that day; how it was I never could find out, for, though I applied myself to it several times, I never could master his liturgies and XXXIX Articles—leaving Queequeg, then, fasting on his tomahawk pipe, and Yojo warming himself at his sacrificial fire of shavings, I sallied out among the shipping. After much prolonged sauntering and many random inquiries, I learnt that there were three ships up for three-years’ voyages—The Devil-dam, the Tit-bit, and the Pequod. Devil-Dam, I do not know the origin of; Tit-bit is obvious; Pequod, you will no doubt remember, was the name of a celebrated tribe of Massachusetts Indians; now extinct as the ancient Medes. I peered and pryed about the Devil-dam; from her, hopped over to the Tit-bit; and finally, going on board the Pequod, looked around her for a moment, and then decided that this was the very ship for us.\n\nYou may have seen many a quaint craft in your day, for aught I know;—square-toed luggers; mountainous Japanese junks; butter-box galliots, and what not; but take my word for it, you never saw such a rare old craft as this same rare old Pequod. She was a ship of the old school, rather small if anything; with an old-fashioned claw-footed look about her. Long seasoned and weather-stained in the typhoons and calms of all four oceans, her old hull’s complexion was darkened like a French grenadier’s, who has alike fought in Egypt and Siberia. Her venerable bows looked bearded. Her masts—cut somewhere on the coast of Japan, where her original ones were lost overboard in a gale—her masts stood stiffly up like the spines of the three old kings of Cologne. Her ancient decks were worn and wrinkled, like the pilgrim-worshipped flag-stone in Canterbury Cathedral where Becket bled. But to all these her old antiquities, were added new and marvellous features, pertaining to the wild business that for more than half a century she had followed. Old Captain Peleg, many years her chief-mate, before he commanded another vessel of his own, and now a retired seaman, and one of the principal owners of the Pequod,—this old Peleg, during the term of his chief-mateship, had built upon her original grotesqueness, and inlaid it, all over, with a quaintness both of material and device, unmatched by anything except it be Thorkill-Hake’s carved buckler or bedstead. She was apparelled like any barbaric Ethiopian emperor, his neck heavy with pendants of polished ivory. She was a thing of trophies. A cannibal of a craft, tricking herself forth in the chased bones of her enemies. All round, her unpanelled, open bulwarks were garnished like one continuous jaw, with the long sharp teeth of the sperm whale, inserted there for pins, to fasten her old hempen thews and tendons to. Those thews ran not through base blocks of land wood, but deftly travelled over sheaves of sea-ivory. Scorning a turnstile wheel at her reverend helm, she sported there a tiller; and that tiller was in one mass, curiously carved from the long narrow lower jaw of her hereditary foe. The helmsman who steered by that tiller in a tempest, felt like the Tartar, when he holds back his fiery steed by clutching its jaw. A noble craft, but somehow a most melancholy! All noble things are touched with that.\n\nNow when I looked about the quarter-deck, for some one having authority, in order to propose myself as a candidate for the voyage, at first I saw nobody; but I could not well overlook a strange sort of tent, or rather wigwam, pitched a little behind the main-mast. It seemed only a temporary erection used in port. It was of a conical shape, some ten feet high; consisting of the long, huge slabs of limber black bone taken from the middle and highest part of the jaws of the right-whale. Planted with their broad ends on the deck, a circle of these slabs laced together, mutually sloped towards each other, and at the apex united in a tufted point, where the loose hairy fibres waved to and fro like the top-knot on some old Pottowottamie Sachem’s head. A triangular opening faced towards the bows of the ship, so that the insider commanded a complete view forward.\n\nAnd half concealed in this queer tenement, I at length found one who by his aspect seemed to have authority; and who, it being noon, and the ship’s work suspended, was now enjoying respite from the burden of command. He was seated on an old-fashioned oaken chair, wriggling all over with curious carving; and the bottom of which was formed of a stout interlacing of the same elastic stuff of which the wigwam was constructed.\n\nThere was nothing so very particular, perhaps, about the appearance of the elderly man I saw; he was brown and brawny, like most old seamen, and heavily rolled up in blue pilot-cloth, cut in the Quaker style; only there was a fine and almost microscopic net-work of the minutest wrinkles interlacing round his eyes, which must have arisen from his continual sailings in many hard gales, and always looking to windward;—for this causes the muscles about the eyes to become pursed together. Such eye-wrinkles are very effectual in a scowl.\n\n“Is this the Captain of the Pequod?” said I, advancing to the door of the tent.\n\n“Supposing it be the captain of the Pequod, what dost thou want of him?” he demanded.\n\n“I was thinking of shipping.”\n\n“Thou wast, wast thou? I see thou art no Nantucketer—ever been in a stove boat?”\n\n“No, Sir, I never have.”\n\n“Dost know nothing at all about whaling, I dare say—eh?\n\n“Nothing, Sir; but I have no doubt I shall soon learn. I’ve been several voyages in the merchant service, and I think that—”\n\n“Merchant service be damned. Talk not that lingo to me. Dost see that leg?—I’ll take that leg away from thy stern, if ever thou talkest of the marchant service to me again. Marchant service indeed! I suppose now ye feel considerable proud of having served in those marchant ships. But flukes! man, what makes thee want to go a whaling, eh?—it looks a little suspicious, don’t it, eh?—Hast not been a pirate, hast thou?—Didst not rob thy last Captain, didst thou?—Dost not think of murdering the officers when thou gettest to sea?”\n\nI protested my innocence of these things. I saw that under the mask of these half humorous innuendoes, this old seaman, as an insulated Quakerish Nantucketer, was full of his insular prejudices, and rather distrustful of all aliens, unless they hailed from Cape Cod or the Vineyard.\n\n“But what takes thee a-whaling? I want to know that before I think of shipping ye.”\n\n“Well, sir, I want to see what whaling is. I want to see the world.”\n\n“Want to see what whaling is, eh? Have ye clapped eye on Captain Ahab?”\n\n“Who is Captain Ahab, sir?”\n\n“Aye, aye, I thought so. Captain Ahab is the Captain of this ship.”\n\n“I am mistaken then. I thought I was speaking to the Captain himself.”\n\n“Thou art speaking to Captain Peleg—that’s who ye are speaking to, young man. It belongs to me and Captain Bildad to see the Pequod fitted out for the voyage, and supplied with all her needs, including crew. We are part owners and agents. But as I was going to say, if thou wantest to know what whaling is, as thou tellest ye do, I can put ye in a way of finding it out before ye bind yourself to it, past backing out. Clap eye on Captain Ahab, young man, and thou wilt find that he has only one leg.”\n\n“What do you mean, sir? Was the other one lost by a whale?”\n\n“Lost by a whale! Young man, come nearer to me: it was devoured, chewed up, crunched by the monstrousest parmacetty that ever chipped a boat!—ah, ah!”\n\nI was a little alarmed by his energy, perhaps also a little touched at the hearty grief in his concluding exclamation, but said as calmly as I could, “What you say is no doubt true enough, sir; but how could I know there was any peculiar ferocity in that particular whale, though indeed I might have inferred as much from the simple fact of the accident.”\n\n“Look ye now, young man, thy lungs are a sort of soft, d’ye see; thou dost not talk shark a bit. Sure, ye’ve been to sea before now; sure of that?”\n\n“Sir,” said I, “I thought I told you that I had been four voyages in the merchant—”\n\n“Hard down out of that! Mind what I said about the marchant service—don’t aggravate me—I won’t have it. But let us understand each other. I have given thee a hint about what whaling is; do ye yet feel inclined for it?”\n\n“I do, sir.”\n\n“Very good. Now, art thou the man to pitch a harpoon down a live whale’s throat, and then jump after it? Answer, quick!”\n\n“I am, sir, if it should be positively indispensable to do so; not to be got rid of, that is; which I don’t take to be the fact.”\n\n“Good again. Now then, thou not only wantest to go a-whaling, to find out by experience what whaling is, but ye also want to go in order to see the world? Was not that what ye said? I thought so. Well then, just step forward there, and take a peep over the weather-bow, and then back to me and tell me what ye see there.”\n\nFor a moment I stood a little puzzled by this curious request, not knowing exactly how to take it, whether humorously or in earnest. But concentrating all his crow’s feet into one scowl, Captain Peleg started me on the errand.\n\nGoing forward and glancing over the weather bow, I perceived that the ship swinging to her anchor with the flood-tide, was now obliquely pointing towards the open ocean. The prospect was unlimited, but exceedingly monotonous and forbidding; not the slightest variety that I could see.\n\n“Well, what’s the report?” said Peleg when I came back; “what did ye see?”\n\n“Not much,” I replied—“nothing but water; considerable horizon though, and there’s a squall coming up, I think.”\n\n“Well, what does thou think then of seeing the world? Do ye wish to go round Cape Horn to see any more of it, eh? Can’t ye see the world where you stand?”\n\nI was a little staggered, but go a-whaling I must, and I would; and the Pequod was as good a ship as any—I thought the best—and all this I now repeated to Peleg. Seeing me so determined, he expressed his willingness to ship me.\n\n“And thou mayest as well sign the papers right off,” he added—“come along with ye.” And so saying, he led the way below deck into the cabin.\n\nSeated on the transom was what seemed to me a most uncommon and surprising figure. It turned out to be Captain Bildad, who along with Captain Peleg was one of the largest owners of the vessel; the other shares, as is sometimes the case in these ports, being held by a crowd of old annuitants; widows, fatherless children, and chancery wards; each owning about the value of a timber head, or a foot of plank, or a nail or two in the ship. People in Nantucket invest their money in whaling vessels, the same way that you do yours in approved state stocks bringing in good interest.\n\nNow, Bildad, like Peleg, and indeed many other Nantucketers, was a Quaker, the island having been originally settled by that sect; and to this day its inhabitants in general retain in an uncommon measure the peculiarities of the Quaker, only variously and anomalously modified by things altogether alien and heterogeneous. For some of these same Quakers are the most sanguinary of all sailors and whale-hunters. They are fighting Quakers; they are Quakers with a vengeance.\n\nSo that there are instances among them of men, who, named with Scripture names—a singularly common fashion on the island—and in childhood naturally imbibing the stately dramatic thee and thou of the Quaker idiom; still, from the audacious, daring, and boundless adventure of their subsequent lives, strangely blend with these unoutgrown peculiarities, a thousand bold dashes of character, not unworthy a Scandinavian sea-king, or a poetical Pagan Roman. And when these things unite in a man of greatly superior natural force, with a globular brain and a ponderous heart; who has also by the stillness and seclusion of many long night-watches in the remotest waters, and beneath constellations never seen here at the north, been led to think untraditionally and independently; receiving all nature’s sweet or savage impressions fresh from her own virgin voluntary and confiding breast, and thereby chiefly, but with some help from accidental advantages, to learn a bold and nervous lofty language—that man makes one in a whole nation’s census—a mighty pageant creature, formed for noble tragedies. Nor will it at all detract from him, dramatically regarded, if either by birth or other circumstances, he have what seems a half wilful overruling morbidness at the bottom of his nature. For all men tragically great are made so through a certain morbidness. Be sure of this, O young ambition, all mortal greatness is but disease. But, as yet we have not to do with such an one, but with quite another; and still a man, who, if indeed peculiar, it only results again from another phase of the Quaker, modified by individual circumstances.\n\nLike Captain Peleg, Captain Bildad was a well-to-do, retired whaleman. But unlike Captain Peleg—who cared not a rush for what are called serious things, and indeed deemed those self-same serious things the veriest of all trifles—Captain Bildad had not only been originally educated according to the strictest sect of Nantucket Quakerism, but all his subsequent ocean life, and the sight of many unclad, lovely island creatures, round the Horn—all that had not moved this native born Quaker one single jot, had not so much as altered one angle of his vest. Still, for all this immutableness, was there some lack of common consistency about worthy Captain Bildad. Though refusing, from conscientious scruples, to bear arms against land invaders, yet himself had illimitably invaded the Atlantic and Pacific; and though a sworn foe to human bloodshed, yet had he in his straight-bodied coat, spilled tuns upon tuns of leviathan gore. How now in the contemplative evening of his days, the pious Bildad reconciled these things in the reminiscence, I do not know; but it did not seem to concern him much, and very probably he had long since come to the sage and sensible conclusion that a man’s religion is one thing, and this practical world quite another. This world pays dividends. Rising from a little cabin-boy in short clothes of the drabbest drab, to a harpooneer in a broad shad-bellied waistcoat; from that becoming boat-header, chief-mate, and captain, and finally a ship owner; Bildad, as I hinted before, had concluded his adventurous career by wholly retiring from active life at the goodly age of sixty, and dedicating his remaining days to the quiet receiving of his well-earned income.\n\nNow, Bildad, I am sorry to say, had the reputation of being an incorrigible old hunks, and in his sea-going days, a bitter, hard task-master. They told me in Nantucket, though it certainly seems a curious story, that when he sailed the old Categut whaleman, his crew, upon arriving home, were mostly all carried ashore to the hospital, sore exhausted and worn out. For a pious man, especially for a Quaker, he was certainly rather hard-hearted, to say the least. He never used to swear, though, at his men, they said; but somehow he got an inordinate quantity of cruel, unmitigated hard work out of them. When Bildad was a chief-mate, to have his drab-coloured eye intently looking at you, made you feel completely nervous, till you could clutch something—a hammer or a marling-spike, and go to work like mad, at something or other, never mind what. Indolence and idleness perished before him. His own person was the exact embodiment of his utilitarian character. On his long, gaunt body, he carried no spare flesh, no superfluous beard, his chin having a soft, economical nap to it, like the worn nap of his broad-brimmed hat.\n\nSuch, then, was the person that I saw seated on the transom when I followed Captain Peleg down into the cabin. The space between the decks was small; and there, bolt-upright, sat old Bildad, who always sat so, and never leaned, and this to save his coat tails. His broad-brim was placed beside him; his legs were stiffly crossed; his drab vesture was buttoned up to his chin; and spectacles on nose, he seemed absorbed in reading from a ponderous volume.\n\n“Bildad,” cried Captain Peleg, “at it again, Bildad, eh? Ye have been studying those Scriptures, now, for the last thirty years, to my certain knowledge. How far ye got, Bildad?”\n\nAs if long habituated to such profane talk from his old shipmate, Bildad, without noticing his present irreverence, quietly looked up, and seeing me, glanced again inquiringly towards Peleg.\n\n“He says he’s our man, Bildad,” said Peleg, “he wants to ship.”\n\n“Dost thee?” said Bildad, in a hollow tone, and turning round to me.\n\n“I dost,” said I unconsciously, he was so intense a Quaker.\n\n“What do ye think of him, Bildad?” said Peleg.\n\n“He’ll do,” said Bildad, eyeing me, and then went on spelling away at his book in a mumbling tone quite audible.\n\nI thought him the queerest old Quaker I ever saw, especially as Peleg, his friend and old shipmate, seemed such a blusterer. But I said nothing, only looking round me sharply. Peleg now threw open a chest, and drawing forth the ship’s articles, placed pen and ink before him, and seated himself at a little table. I began to think it was high time to settle with myself at what terms I would be willing to engage for the voyage. I was already aware that in the whaling business they paid no wages; but all hands, including the captain, received certain shares of the profits called lays, and that these lays were proportioned to the degree of importance pertaining to the respective duties of the ship’s company. I was also aware that being a green hand at whaling, my own lay would not be very large; but considering that I was used to the sea, could steer a ship, splice a rope, and all that, I made no doubt that from all I had heard I should be offered at least the 275th lay—that is, the 275th part of the clear net proceeds of the voyage, whatever that might eventually amount to. And though the 275th lay was what they call a rather long lay, yet it was better than nothing; and if we had a lucky voyage, might pretty nearly pay for the clothing I would wear out on it, not to speak of my three years’ beef and board, for which I would not have to pay one stiver.\n\nIt might be thought that this was a poor way to accumulate a princely fortune—and so it was, a very poor way indeed. But I am one of those that never take on about princely fortunes, and am quite content if the world is ready to board and lodge me, while I am putting up at this grim sign of the Thunder Cloud. Upon the whole, I thought that the 275th lay would be about the fair thing, but would not have been surprised had I been offered the 200th, considering I was of a broad-shouldered make.\n\nBut one thing, nevertheless, that made me a little distrustful about receiving a generous share of the profits was this: Ashore, I had heard something of both Captain Peleg and his unaccountable old crony Bildad; how that they being the principal proprietors of the Pequod, therefore the other and more inconsiderable and scattered owners, left nearly the whole management of the ship’s affairs to these two. And I did not know but what the stingy old Bildad might have a mighty deal to say about shipping hands, especially as I now found him on board the Pequod, quite at home there in the cabin, and reading his Bible as if at his own fireside. Now while Peleg was vainly trying to mend a pen with his jack-knife, old Bildad, to my no small surprise, considering that he was such an interested party in these proceedings; Bildad never heeded us, but went on mumbling to himself out of his book, “Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth, where moth—”\n\n“Well, Captain Bildad,” interrupted Peleg, “what d’ye say, what lay shall we give this young man?”\n\n“Thou knowest best,” was the sepulchral reply, “the seven hundred and seventy-seventh wouldn’t be too much, would it?—‘where moth and rust do corrupt, but lay—’”\n\nLay, indeed, thought I, and such a lay! the seven hundred and seventy-seventh! Well, old Bildad, you are determined that I, for one, shall not lay up many lays here below, where moth and rust do corrupt. It was an exceedingly long lay that, indeed; and though from the magnitude of the figure it might at first deceive a landsman, yet the slightest consideration will show that though seven hundred and seventy-seven is a pretty large number, yet, when you come to make a teenth of it, you will then see, I say, that the seven hundred and seventy-seventh part of a farthing is a good deal less than seven hundred and seventy-seven gold doubloons; and so I thought at the time.\n\n“Why, blast your eyes, Bildad,” cried Peleg, “thou dost not want to swindle this young man! he must have more than that.”\n\n“Seven hundred and seventy-seventh,” again said Bildad, without lifting his eyes; and then went on mumbling—“for where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.”\n\n“I am going to put him down for the three hundredth,” said Peleg, “do ye hear that, Bildad! The three hundredth lay, I say.”\n\nBildad laid down his book, and turning solemnly towards him said, “Captain Peleg, thou hast a generous heart; but thou must consider the duty thou owest to the other owners of this ship—widows and orphans, many of them—and that if we too abundantly reward the labors of this young man, we may be taking the bread from those widows and those orphans. The seven hundred and seventy-seventh lay, Captain Peleg.”\n\n“Thou Bildad!” roared Peleg, starting up and clattering about the cabin. “Blast ye, Captain Bildad, if I had followed thy advice in these matters, I would afore now had a conscience to lug about that would be heavy enough to founder the largest ship that ever sailed round Cape Horn.”\n\n“Captain Peleg,” said Bildad steadily, “thy conscience may be drawing ten inches of water, or ten fathoms, I can’t tell; but as thou art still an impenitent man, Captain Peleg, I greatly fear lest thy conscience be but a leaky one; and will in the end sink thee foundering down to the fiery pit, Captain Peleg.”\n\n“Fiery pit! fiery pit! ye insult me, man; past all natural bearing, ye insult me. It’s an all-fired outrage to tell any human creature that he’s bound to hell. Flukes and flames! Bildad, say that again to me, and start my soul-bolts, but I’ll—I’ll—yes, I’ll swallow a live goat with all his hair and horns on. Out of the cabin, ye canting, drab-coloured son of a wooden gun—a straight wake with ye!”\n\nAs he thundered out this he made a rush at Bildad, but with a marvellous oblique, sliding celerity, Bildad for that time eluded him.\n\nAlarmed at this terrible outburst between the two principal and responsible owners of the ship, and feeling half a mind to give up all idea of sailing in a vessel so questionably owned and temporarily commanded, I stepped aside from the door to give egress to Bildad, who, I made no doubt, was all eagerness to vanish from before the awakened wrath of Peleg. But to my astonishment, he sat down again on the transom very quietly, and seemed to have not the slightest intention of withdrawing. He seemed quite used to impenitent Peleg and his ways. As for Peleg, after letting off his rage as he had, there seemed no more left in him, and he, too, sat down like a lamb, though he twitched a little as if still nervously agitated. “Whew!” he whistled at last—“the squall’s gone off to leeward, I think. Bildad, thou used to be good at sharpening a lance, mend that pen, will ye. My jack-knife here needs the grindstone. That’s he; thank ye, Bildad. Now then, my young man, Ishmael’s thy name, didn’t ye say? Well then, down ye go here, Ishmael, for the three hundredth lay.”\n\n“Captain Peleg,” said I, “I have a friend with me who wants to ship too—shall I bring him down to-morrow?”\n\n“To be sure,” said Peleg. “Fetch him along, and we’ll look at him.”\n\n“What lay does he want?” groaned Bildad, glancing up from the book in which he had again been burying himself.\n\n“Oh! never thee mind about that, Bildad,” said Peleg. “Has he ever whaled it any?” turning to me.\n\n“Killed more whales than I can count, Captain Peleg.”\n\n“Well, bring him along then.”\n\nAnd, after signing the papers, off I went; nothing doubting but that I had done a good morning’s work, and that the Pequod was the identical ship that Yojo had provided to carry Queequeg and me round the Cape.\n\nBut I had not proceeded far, when I began to bethink me that the Captain with whom I was to sail yet remained unseen by me; though, indeed, in many cases, a whale-ship will be completely fitted out, and receive all her crew on board, ere the captain makes himself visible by arriving to take command; for sometimes these voyages are so prolonged, and the shore intervals at home so exceedingly brief, that if the captain have a family, or any absorbing concernment of that sort, he does not trouble himself much about his ship in port, but leaves her to the owners till all is ready for sea. However, it is always as well to have a look at him before irrevocably committing yourself into his hands. Turning back I accosted Captain Peleg, inquiring where Captain Ahab was to be found.\n\n“And what dost thou want of Captain Ahab? It’s all right enough; thou art shipped.”\n\n“Yes, but I should like to see him.”\n\n“But I don’t think thou wilt be able to at present. I don’t know exactly what’s the matter with him; but he keeps close inside the house; a sort of sick, and yet he don’t look so. In fact, he ain’t sick; but no, he isn’t well either. Any how, young man, he won’t always see me, so I don’t suppose he will thee. He’s a queer man, Captain Ahab—so some think—but a good one. Oh, thou’lt like him well enough; no fear, no fear. He’s a grand, ungodly, god-like man, Captain Ahab; doesn’t speak much; but, when he does speak, then you may well listen. Mark ye, be forewarned; Ahab’s above the common; Ahab’s been in colleges, as well as ’mong the cannibals; been used to deeper wonders than the waves; fixed his fiery lance in mightier, stranger foes than whales. His lance! aye, the keenest and the surest that out of all our isle! Oh! he ain’t Captain Bildad; no, and he ain’t Captain Peleg; he’s Ahab, boy; and Ahab of old, thou knowest, was a crowned king!”\n\n“And a very vile one. When that wicked king was slain, the dogs, did they not lick his blood?”\n\n“Come hither to me—hither, hither,” said Peleg, with a significance in his eye that almost startled me. “Look ye, lad; never say that on board the Pequod. Never say it anywhere. Captain Ahab did not name himself. ’Twas a foolish, ignorant whim of his crazy, widowed mother, who died when he was only a twelvemonth old. And yet the old squaw Tistig, at Gayhead, said that the name would somehow prove prophetic. And, perhaps, other fools like her may tell thee the same. I wish to warn thee. It’s a lie. I know Captain Ahab well; I’ve sailed with him as mate years ago; I know what he is—a good man—not a pious, good man, like Bildad, but a swearing good man—something like me—only there’s a good deal more of him. Aye, aye, I know that he was never very jolly; and I know that on the passage home, he was a little out of his mind for a spell; but it was the sharp shooting pains in his bleeding stump that brought that about, as any one might see. I know, too, that ever since he lost his leg last voyage by that accursed whale, he’s been a kind of moody—desperate moody, and savage sometimes; but that will all pass off. And once for all, let me tell thee and assure thee, young man, it’s better to sail with a moody good captain than a laughing bad one. So good-bye to thee—and wrong not Captain Ahab, because he happens to have a wicked name. Besides, my boy, he has a wife—not three voyages wedded—a sweet, resigned girl. Think of that; by that sweet girl that old man has a child: hold ye then there can be any utter, hopeless harm in Ahab? No, no, my lad; stricken, blasted, if he be, Ahab has his humanities!”\n\nAs I walked away, I was full of thoughtfulness; what had been incidentally revealed to me of Captain Ahab, filled me with a certain wild vagueness of painfulness concerning him. And somehow, at the time, I felt a sympathy and a sorrow for him, but for I don’t know what, unless it was the cruel loss of his leg. And yet I also felt a strange awe of him; but that sort of awe, which I cannot at all describe, was not exactly awe; I do not know what it was. But I felt it; and it did not disincline me towards him; though I felt impatience at what seemed like mystery in him, so imperfectly as he was known to me then. However, my thoughts were at length carried in other directions, so that for the present dark Ahab slipped my mind.\n\n# CHAPTER 17. The Ramadan.\n\nAs Queequeg’s Ramadan, or Fasting and Humiliation, was to continue all day, I did not choose to disturb him till towards night-fall; for I cherish the greatest respect towards everybody’s religious obligations, never mind how comical, and could not find it in my heart to undervalue even a congregation of ants worshipping a toad-stool; or those other creatures in certain parts of our earth, who with a degree of footmanism quite unprecedented in other planets, bow down before the torso of a deceased landed proprietor merely on account of the inordinate possessions yet owned and rented in his name.\n\nI say, we good Presbyterian Christians should be charitable in these things, and not fancy ourselves so vastly superior to other mortals, pagans and what not, because of their half-crazy conceits on these subjects. There was Queequeg, now, certainly entertaining the most absurd notions about Yojo and his Ramadan;—but what of that? Queequeg thought he knew what he was about, I suppose; he seemed to be content; and there let him rest. All our arguing with him would not avail; let him be, I say: and Heaven have mercy on us all—Presbyterians and Pagans alike—for we are all somehow dreadfully cracked about the head, and sadly need mending.\n\nTowards evening, when I felt assured that all his performances and rituals must be over, I went up to his room and knocked at the door; but no answer. I tried to open it, but it was fastened inside. “Queequeg,” said I softly through the key-hole:—all silent. “I say, Queequeg! why don’t you speak? It’s I—Ishmael.” But all remained still as before. I began to grow alarmed. I had allowed him such abundant time; I thought he might have had an apoplectic fit. I looked through the key-hole; but the door opening into an odd corner of the room, the key-hole prospect was but a crooked and sinister one. I could only see part of the foot-board of the bed and a line of the wall, but nothing more. I was surprised to behold resting against the wall the wooden shaft of Queequeg’s harpoon, which the landlady the evening previous had taken from him, before our mounting to the chamber. That’s strange, thought I; but at any rate, since the harpoon stands yonder, and he seldom or never goes abroad without it, therefore he must be inside here, and no possible mistake.\n\n“Queequeg!—Queequeg!”—all still. Something must have happened. Apoplexy! I tried to burst open the door; but it stubbornly resisted. Running down stairs, I quickly stated my suspicions to the first person I met—the chamber-maid. “La! la!” she cried, “I thought something must be the matter. I went to make the bed after breakfast, and the door was locked; and not a mouse to be heard; and it’s been just so silent ever since. But I thought, may be, you had both gone off and locked your baggage in for safe keeping. La! la, ma’am!—Mistress! murder! Mrs. Hussey! apoplexy!”—and with these cries, she ran towards the kitchen, I following.\n\nMrs. Hussey soon appeared, with a mustard-pot in one hand and a vinegar-cruet in the other, having just broken away from the occupation of attending to the castors, and scolding her little black boy meantime.\n\n“Wood-house!” cried I, “which way to it? Run for God’s sake, and fetch something to pry open the door—the axe!—the axe! he’s had a stroke; depend upon it!”—and so saying I was unmethodically rushing up stairs again empty-handed, when Mrs. Hussey interposed the mustard-pot and vinegar-cruet, and the entire castor of her countenance.\n\n“What’s the matter with you, young man?”\n\n“Get the axe! For God’s sake, run for the doctor, some one, while I pry it open!”\n\n“Look here,” said the landlady, quickly putting down the vinegar-cruet, so as to have one hand free; “look here; are you talking about prying open any of my doors?”—and with that she seized my arm. “What’s the matter with you? What’s the matter with you, shipmate?”\n\nIn as calm, but rapid a manner as possible, I gave her to understand the whole case. Unconsciously clapping the vinegar-cruet to one side of her nose, she ruminated for an instant; then exclaimed—“No! I haven’t seen it since I put it there.” Running to a little closet under the landing of the stairs, she glanced in, and returning, told me that Queequeg’s harpoon was missing. “He’s killed himself,” she cried. “It’s unfort’nate Stiggs done over again—there goes another counterpane—God pity his poor mother!—it will be the ruin of my house. Has the poor lad a sister? Where’s that girl?—there, Betty, go to Snarles the Painter, and tell him to paint me a sign, with—“no suicides permitted here, and no smoking in the parlor;”—might as well kill both birds at once. Kill? The Lord be merciful to his ghost! What’s that noise there? You, young man, avast there!”\n\nAnd running up after me, she caught me as I was again trying to force open the door.\n\n“I don’t allow it; I won’t have my premises spoiled. Go for the locksmith, there’s one about a mile from here. But avast!” putting her hand in her side-pocket, “here’s a key that’ll fit, I guess; let’s see.” And with that, she turned it in the lock; but, alas! Queequeg’s supplemental bolt remained unwithdrawn within.\n\n“Have to burst it open,” said I, and was running down the entry a little, for a good start, when the landlady caught at me, again vowing I should not break down her premises; but I tore from her, and with a sudden bodily rush dashed myself full against the mark.\n\nWith a prodigious noise the door flew open, and the knob slamming against the wall, sent the plaster to the ceiling; and there, good heavens! there sat Queequeg, altogether cool and self-collected; right in the middle of the room; squatting on his hams, and holding Yojo on top of his head. He looked neither one way nor the other way, but sat like a carved image with scarce a sign of active life.\n\n“Queequeg,” said I, going up to him, “Queequeg, what’s the matter with you?”\n\n“He hain’t been a sittin’ so all day, has he?” said the landlady.\n\nBut all we said, not a word could we drag out of him; I almost felt like pushing him over, so as to change his position, for it was almost intolerable, it seemed so painfully and unnaturally constrained; especially, as in all probability he had been sitting so for upwards of eight or ten hours, going too without his regular meals.\n\n“Mrs. Hussey,” said I, “he’s alive at all events; so leave us, if you please, and I will see to this strange affair myself.”\n\nClosing the door upon the landlady, I endeavored to prevail upon Queequeg to take a chair; but in vain. There he sat; and all he could do—for all my polite arts and blandishments—he would not move a peg, nor say a single word, nor even look at me, nor notice my presence in the slightest way.\n\nI wonder, thought I, if this can possibly be a part of his Ramadan; do they fast on their hams that way in his native island. It must be so; yes, it’s part of his creed, I suppose; well, then, let him rest; he’ll get up sooner or later, no doubt. It can’t last for ever, thank God, and his Ramadan only comes once a year; and I don’t believe it’s very punctual then.\n\nI went down to supper. After sitting a long time listening to the long stories of some sailors who had just come from a plum-pudding voyage, as they called it (that is, a short whaling-voyage in a schooner or brig, confined to the north of the line, in the Atlantic Ocean only); after listening to these plum-puddingers till nearly eleven o’clock, I went up stairs to go to bed, feeling quite sure by this time Queequeg must certainly have brought his Ramadan to a termination. But no; there he was just where I had left him; he had not stirred an inch. I began to grow vexed with him; it seemed so downright senseless and insane to be sitting there all day and half the night on his hams in a cold room, holding a piece of wood on his head.\n\n“For heaven’s sake, Queequeg, get up and shake yourself; get up and have some supper. You’ll starve; you’ll kill yourself, Queequeg.” But not a word did he reply.\n\nDespairing of him, therefore, I determined to go to bed and to sleep; and no doubt, before a great while, he would follow me. But previous to turning in, I took my heavy bearskin jacket, and threw it over him, as it promised to be a very cold night; and he had nothing but his ordinary round jacket on. For some time, do all I would, I could not get into the faintest doze. I had blown out the candle; and the mere thought of Queequeg—not four feet off—sitting there in that uneasy position, stark alone in the cold and dark; this made me really wretched. Think of it; sleeping all night in the same room with a wide awake pagan on his hams in this dreary, unaccountable Ramadan!\n\nBut somehow I dropped off at last, and knew nothing more till break of day; when, looking over the bedside, there squatted Queequeg, as if he had been screwed down to the floor. But as soon as the first glimpse of sun entered the window, up he got, with stiff and grating joints, but with a cheerful look; limped towards me where I lay; pressed his forehead again against mine; and said his Ramadan was over.\n\nNow, as I before hinted, I have no objection to any person’s religion, be it what it may, so long as that person does not kill or insult any other person, because that other person don’t believe it also. But when a man’s religion becomes really frantic; when it is a positive torment to him; and, in fine, makes this earth of ours an uncomfortable inn to lodge in; then I think it high time to take that individual aside and argue the point with him.\n\nAnd just so I now did with Queequeg. “Queequeg,” said I, “get into bed now, and lie and listen to me.” I then went on, beginning with the rise and progress of the primitive religions, and coming down to the various religions of the present time, during which time I labored to show Queequeg that all these Lents, Ramadans, and prolonged ham-squattings in cold, cheerless rooms were stark nonsense; bad for the health; useless for the soul; opposed, in short, to the obvious laws of Hygiene and common sense. I told him, too, that he being in other things such an extremely sensible and sagacious savage, it pained me, very badly pained me, to see him now so deplorably foolish about this ridiculous Ramadan of his. Besides, argued I, fasting makes the body cave in; hence the spirit caves in; and all thoughts born of a fast must necessarily be half-starved. This is the reason why most dyspeptic religionists cherish such melancholy notions about their hereafters. In one word, Queequeg, said I, rather digressively; hell is an idea first born on an undigested apple-dumpling; and since then perpetuated through the hereditary dyspepsias nurtured by Ramadans.\n\nI then asked Queequeg whether he himself was ever troubled with dyspepsia; expressing the idea very plainly, so that he could take it in. He said no; only upon one memorable occasion. It was after a great feast given by his father the king, on the gaining of a great battle wherein fifty of the enemy had been killed by about two o’clock in the afternoon, and all cooked and eaten that very evening.\n\n“No more, Queequeg,” said I, shuddering; “that will do;” for I knew the inferences without his further hinting them. I had seen a sailor who had visited that very island, and he told me that it was the custom, when a great battle had been gained there, to barbecue all the slain in the yard or garden of the victor; and then, one by one, they were placed in great wooden trenchers, and garnished round like a pilau, with breadfruit and cocoanuts; and with some parsley in their mouths, were sent round with the victor’s compliments to all his friends, just as though these presents were so many Christmas turkeys.\n\nAfter all, I do not think that my remarks about religion made much impression upon Queequeg. Because, in the first place, he somehow seemed dull of hearing on that important subject, unless considered from his own point of view; and, in the second place, he did not more than one third understand me, couch my ideas simply as I would; and, finally, he no doubt thought he knew a good deal more about the true religion than I did. He looked at me with a sort of condescending concern and compassion, as though he thought it a great pity that such a sensible young man should be so hopelessly lost to evangelical pagan piety.\n\nAt last we rose and dressed; and Queequeg, taking a prodigiously hearty breakfast of chowders of all sorts, so that the landlady should not make much profit by reason of his Ramadan, we sallied out to board the Pequod, sauntering along, and picking our teeth with halibut bones.\n\n# CHAPTER 18. His Mark.\n\nAs we were walking down the end of the wharf towards the ship, Queequeg carrying his harpoon, Captain Peleg in his gruff voice loudly hailed us from his wigwam, saying he had not suspected my friend was a cannibal, and furthermore announcing that he let no cannibals on board that craft, unless they previously produced their papers.\n\n“What do you mean by that, Captain Peleg?” said I, now jumping on the bulwarks, and leaving my comrade standing on the wharf.\n\n“I mean,” he replied, “he must show his papers.”\n\n“Yes,” said Captain Bildad in his hollow voice, sticking his head from behind Peleg’s, out of the wigwam. “He must show that he’s converted. Son of darkness,” he added, turning to Queequeg, “art thou at present in communion with any Christian church?”\n\n“Why,” said I, “he’s a member of the first Congregational Church.” Here be it said, that many tattooed savages sailing in Nantucket ships at last come to be converted into the churches.\n\n“First Congregational Church,” cried Bildad, “what! that worships in Deacon Deuteronomy Coleman’s meeting-house?” and so saying, taking out his spectacles, he rubbed them with his great yellow bandana handkerchief, and putting them on very carefully, came out of the wigwam, and leaning stiffly over the bulwarks, took a good long look at Queequeg.\n\n“How long hath he been a member?” he then said, turning to me; “not very long, I rather guess, young man.”\n\n“No,” said Peleg, “and he hasn’t been baptized right either, or it would have washed some of that devil’s blue off his face.”\n\n“Do tell, now,” cried Bildad, “is this Philistine a regular member of Deacon Deuteronomy’s meeting? I never saw him going there, and I pass it every Lord’s day.”\n\n“I don’t know anything about Deacon Deuteronomy or his meeting,” said I; “all I know is, that Queequeg here is a born member of the First Congregational Church. He is a deacon himself, Queequeg is.”\n\n“Young man,” said Bildad sternly, “thou art skylarking with me—explain thyself, thou young Hittite. What church dost thee mean? answer me.”\n\nFinding myself thus hard pushed, I replied. “I mean, sir, the same ancient Catholic Church to which you and I, and Captain Peleg there, and Queequeg here, and all of us, and every mother’s son and soul of us belong; the great and everlasting First Congregation of this whole worshipping world; we all belong to that; only some of us cherish some queer crotchets no ways touching the grand belief; in that we all join hands.”\n\n“Splice, thou mean’st splice hands,” cried Peleg, drawing nearer. “Young man, you’d better ship for a missionary, instead of a fore-mast hand; I never heard a better sermon. Deacon Deuteronomy—why Father Mapple himself couldn’t beat it, and he’s reckoned something. Come aboard, come aboard; never mind about the papers. I say, tell Quohog there—what’s that you call him? tell Quohog to step along. By the great anchor, what a harpoon he’s got there! looks like good stuff that; and he handles it about right. I say, Quohog, or whatever your name is, did you ever stand in the head of a whale-boat? did you ever strike a fish?”\n\nWithout saying a word, Queequeg, in his wild sort of way, jumped upon the bulwarks, from thence into the bows of one of the whale-boats hanging to the side; and then bracing his left knee, and poising his harpoon, cried out in some such way as this:—\n\n“Cap’ain, you see him small drop tar on water dere? You see him? well, spose him one whale eye, well, den!” and taking sharp aim at it, he darted the iron right over old Bildad’s broad brim, clean across the ship’s decks, and struck the glistening tar spot out of sight.\n\n“Now,” said Queequeg, quietly hauling in the line, “spos-ee him whale-e eye; why, dad whale dead.”\n\n“Quick, Bildad,” said Peleg, his partner, who, aghast at the close vicinity of the flying harpoon, had retreated towards the cabin gangway. “Quick, I say, you Bildad, and get the ship’s papers. We must have Hedgehog there, I mean Quohog, in one of our boats. Look ye, Quohog, we’ll give ye the ninetieth lay, and that’s more than ever was given a harpooneer yet out of Nantucket.”\n\nSo down we went into the cabin, and to my great joy Queequeg was soon enrolled among the same ship’s company to which I myself belonged.\n\nWhen all preliminaries were over and Peleg had got everything ready for signing, he turned to me and said, “I guess, Quohog there don’t know how to write, does he? I say, Quohog, blast ye! dost thou sign thy name or make thy mark?”\n\nBut at this question, Queequeg, who had twice or thrice before taken part in similar ceremonies, looked no ways abashed; but taking the offered pen, copied upon the paper, in the proper place, an exact counterpart of a queer round figure which was tattooed upon his arm; so that through Captain Peleg’s obstinate mistake touching his appellative, it stood something like this:—\n\nQuohog. his X mark.\n\nMeanwhile Captain Bildad sat earnestly and steadfastly eyeing Queequeg, and at last rising solemnly and fumbling in the huge pockets of his broad-skirted drab coat, took out a bundle of tracts, and selecting one entitled “The Latter Day Coming; or No Time to Lose,” placed it in Queequeg’s hands, and then grasping them and the book with both his, looked earnestly into his eyes, and said, “Son of darkness, I must do my duty by thee; I am part owner of this ship, and feel concerned for the souls of all its crew; if thou still clingest to thy Pagan ways, which I sadly fear, I beseech thee, remain not for aye a Belial bondsman. Spurn the idol Bell, and the hideous dragon; turn from the wrath to come; mind thine eye, I say; oh! goodness gracious! steer clear of the fiery pit!”\n\nSomething of the salt sea yet lingered in old Bildad’s language, heterogeneously mixed with Scriptural and domestic phrases.\n\n“Avast there, avast there, Bildad, avast now spoiling our harpooneer,” cried Peleg. “Pious harpooneers never make good voyagers—it takes the shark out of ’em; no harpooneer is worth a straw who aint pretty sharkish. There was young Nat Swaine, once the bravest boat-header out of all Nantucket and the Vineyard; he joined the meeting, and never came to good. He got so frightened about his plaguy soul, that he shrinked and sheered away from whales, for fear of after-claps, in case he got stove and went to Davy Jones.”\n\n“Peleg! Peleg!” said Bildad, lifting his eyes and hands, “thou thyself, as I myself, hast seen many a perilous time; thou knowest, Peleg, what it is to have the fear of death; how, then, can’st thou prate in this ungodly guise. Thou beliest thine own heart, Peleg. Tell me, when this same Pequod here had her three masts overboard in that typhoon on Japan, that same voyage when thou went mate with Captain Ahab, did’st thou not think of Death and the Judgment then?”\n\n“Hear him, hear him now,” cried Peleg, marching across the cabin, and thrusting his hands far down into his pockets,—“hear him, all of ye. Think of that! When every moment we thought the ship would sink! Death and the Judgment then? What? With all three masts making such an everlasting thundering against the side; and every sea breaking over us, fore and aft. Think of Death and the Judgment then? No! no time to think about Death then. Life was what Captain Ahab and I was thinking of; and how to save all hands—how to rig jury-masts—how to get into the nearest port; that was what I was thinking of.”\n\nBildad said no more, but buttoning up his coat, stalked on deck, where we followed him. There he stood, very quietly overlooking some sailmakers who were mending a top-sail in the waist. Now and then he stooped to pick up a patch, or save an end of tarred twine, which otherwise might have been wasted.\n\n“Shipmates, have ye shipped in that ship?”\n\nQueequeg and I had just left the Pequod, and were sauntering away from the water, for the moment each occupied with his own thoughts, when the above words were put to us by a stranger, who, pausing before us, levelled his massive forefinger at the vessel in question. He was but shabbily apparelled in faded jacket and patched trowsers; a rag of a black handkerchief investing his neck. A confluent small-pox had in all directions flowed over his face, and left it like the complicated ribbed bed of a torrent, when the rushing waters have been dried up.\n\n“Have ye shipped in her?” he repeated.\n\n“You mean the ship Pequod, I suppose,” said I, trying to gain a little more time for an uninterrupted look at him.\n\n“Aye, the Pequod—that ship there,” he said, drawing back his whole arm, and then rapidly shoving it straight out from him, with the fixed bayonet of his pointed finger darted full at the object.\n\n“Yes,” said I, “we have just signed the articles.”\n\n“Anything down there about your souls?”\n\n“About what?”\n\n“Oh, perhaps you hav’n’t got any,” he said quickly. “No matter though, I know many chaps that hav’n’t got any,—good luck to ’em; and they are all the better off for it. A soul’s a sort of a fifth wheel to a wagon.”\n\n“What are you jabbering about, shipmate?” said I.\n\n“He’s got enough, though, to make up for all deficiencies of that sort in other chaps,” abruptly said the stranger, placing a nervous emphasis upon the word he.\n\n“Queequeg,” said I, “let’s go; this fellow has broken loose from somewhere; he’s talking about something and somebody we don’t know.”\n\n“Stop!” cried the stranger. “Ye said true—ye hav’n’t seen Old Thunder yet, have ye?”\n\n“Who’s Old Thunder?” said I, again riveted with the insane earnestness of his manner.\n\n“Captain Ahab.”\n\n“What! the captain of our ship, the Pequod?”\n\n“Aye, among some of us old sailor chaps, he goes by that name. Ye hav’n’t seen him yet, have ye?”\n\n“No, we hav’n’t. He’s sick they say, but is getting better, and will be all right again before long.”\n\n“All right again before long!” laughed the stranger, with a solemnly derisive sort of laugh. “Look ye; when Captain Ahab is all right, then this left arm of mine will be all right; not before.”\n\n“What do you know about him?”\n\n“What did they tell you about him? Say that!”\n\n“They didn’t tell much of anything about him; only I’ve heard that he’s a good whale-hunter, and a good captain to his crew.”\n\n“That’s true, that’s true—yes, both true enough. But you must jump when he gives an order. Step and growl; growl and go—that’s the word with Captain Ahab. But nothing about that thing that happened to him off Cape Horn, long ago, when he lay like dead for three days and nights; nothing about that deadly skrimmage with the Spaniard afore the altar in Santa?—heard nothing about that, eh? Nothing about the silver calabash he spat into? And nothing about his losing his leg last voyage, according to the prophecy. Didn’t ye hear a word about them matters and something more, eh? No, I don’t think ye did; how could ye? Who knows it? Not all Nantucket, I guess. But hows’ever, mayhap, ye’ve heard tell about the leg, and how he lost it; aye, ye have heard of that, I dare say. Oh yes, that every one knows a’most—I mean they know he’s only one leg; and that a parmacetti took the other off.”\n\n“My friend,” said I, “what all this gibberish of yours is about, I don’t know, and I don’t much care; for it seems to me that you must be a little damaged in the head. But if you are speaking of Captain Ahab, of that ship there, the Pequod, then let me tell you, that I know all about the loss of his leg.”\n\n“All about it, eh—sure you do?—all?”\n\n“Pretty sure.”\n\nWith finger pointed and eye levelled at the Pequod, the beggar-like stranger stood a moment, as if in a troubled reverie; then starting a little, turned and said:—“Ye’ve shipped, have ye? Names down on the papers? Well, well, what’s signed, is signed; and what’s to be, will be; and then again, perhaps it won’t be, after all. Anyhow, it’s all fixed and arranged a’ready; and some sailors or other must go with him, I suppose; as well these as any other men, God pity ’em! Morning to ye, shipmates, morning; the ineffable heavens bless ye; I’m sorry I stopped ye.”\n\n“Look here, friend,” said I, “if you have anything important to tell us, out with it; but if you are only trying to bamboozle us, you are mistaken in your game; that’s all I have to say.”\n\n“And it’s said very well, and I like to hear a chap talk up that way; you are just the man for him—the likes of ye. Morning to ye, shipmates, morning! Oh! when ye get there, tell ’em I’ve concluded not to make one of ’em.”\n\n“Ah, my dear fellow, you can’t fool us that way—you can’t fool us. It is the easiest thing in the world for a man to look as if he had a great secret in him.”\n\n“Morning to ye, shipmates, morning.”\n\n“Morning it is,” said I. “Come along, Queequeg, let’s leave this crazy man. But stop, tell me your name, will you?”\n“Elijah.”\n\nElijah! thought I, and we walked away, both commenting, after each other’s fashion, upon this ragged old sailor; and agreed that he was nothing but a humbug, trying to be a bugbear. But we had not gone perhaps above a hundred yards, when chancing to turn a corner, and looking back as I did so, who should be seen but Elijah following us, though at a distance. Somehow, the sight of him struck me so, that I said nothing to Queequeg of his being behind, but passed on with my comrade, anxious to see whether the stranger would turn the same corner that we did. He did; and then it seemed to me that he was dogging us, but with what intent I could not for the life of me imagine. This circumstance, coupled with his ambiguous, half-hinting, half-revealing, shrouded sort of talk, now begat in me all kinds of vague wonderments and half-apprehensions, and all connected with the Pequod; and Captain Ahab; and the leg he had lost; and the Cape Horn fit; and the silver calabash; and what Captain Peleg had said of him, when I left the ship the day previous; and the prediction of the squaw Tistig; and the voyage we had bound ourselves to sail; and a hundred other shadowy things.\n\nI was resolved to satisfy myself whether this ragged Elijah was really dogging us or not, and with that intent crossed the way with Queequeg, and on that side of it retraced our steps. But Elijah passed on, without seeming to notice us. This relieved me; and once more, and finally as it seemed to me, I pronounced him in my heart, a humbug.\n\n# CHAPTER 20. All Astir.\n\nA day or two passed, and there was great activity aboard the Pequod. Not only were the old sails being mended, but new sails were coming on board, and bolts of canvas, and coils of rigging; in short, everything betokened that the ship’s preparations were hurrying to a close. Captain Peleg seldom or never went ashore, but sat in his wigwam keeping a sharp look-out upon the hands: Bildad did all the purchasing and providing at the stores; and the men employed in the hold and on the rigging were working till long after night-fall.\n\nOn the day following Queequeg’s signing the articles, word was given at all the inns where the ship’s company were stopping, that their chests must be on board before night, for there was no telling how soon the vessel might be sailing. So Queequeg and I got down our traps, resolving, however, to sleep ashore till the last. But it seems they always give very long notice in these cases, and the ship did not sail for several days. But no wonder; there was a good deal to be done, and there is no telling how many things to be thought of, before the Pequod was fully equipped.\n\nEvery one knows what a multitude of things—beds, sauce-pans, knives and forks, shovels and tongs, napkins, nut-crackers, and what not, are indispensable to the business of housekeeping. Just so with whaling, which necessitates a three-years’ housekeeping upon the wide ocean, far from all grocers, costermongers, doctors, bakers, and bankers. And though this also holds true of merchant vessels, yet not by any means to the same extent as with whalemen. For besides the great length of the whaling voyage, the numerous articles peculiar to the prosecution of the fishery, and the impossibility of replacing them at the remote harbors usually frequented, it must be remembered, that of all ships, whaling vessels are the most exposed to accidents of all kinds, and especially to the destruction and loss of the very things upon which the success of the voyage most depends. Hence, the spare boats, spare spars, and spare lines and harpoons, and spare everythings, almost, but a spare Captain and duplicate ship.\n\nAt the period of our arrival at the Island, the heaviest storage of the Pequod had been almost completed; comprising her beef, bread, water, fuel, and iron hoops and staves. But, as before hinted, for some time there was a continual fetching and carrying on board of divers odds and ends of things, both large and small.\n\nChief among those who did this fetching and carrying was Captain Bildad’s sister, a lean old lady of a most determined and indefatigable spirit, but withal very kindhearted, who seemed resolved that, if she could help it, nothing should be found wanting in the Pequod, after once fairly getting to sea. At one time she would come on board with a jar of pickles for the steward’s pantry; another time with a bunch of quills for the chief mate’s desk, where he kept his log; a third time with a roll of flannel for the small of some one’s rheumatic back. Never did any woman better deserve her name, which was Charity—Aunt Charity, as everybody called her. And like a sister of charity did this charitable Aunt Charity bustle about hither and thither, ready to turn her hand and heart to anything that promised to yield safety, comfort, and consolation to all on board a ship in which her beloved brother Bildad was concerned, and in which she herself owned a score or two of well-saved dollars.\n\nBut it was startling to see this excellent hearted Quakeress coming on board, as she did the last day, with a long oil-ladle in one hand, and a still longer whaling lance in the other. Nor was Bildad himself nor Captain Peleg at all backward. As for Bildad, he carried about with him a long list of the articles needed, and at every fresh arrival, down went his mark opposite that article upon the paper. Every once in a while Peleg came hobbling out of his whalebone den, roaring at the men down the hatchways, roaring up to the riggers at the mast-head, and then concluded by roaring back into his wigwam.\n\nDuring these days of preparation, Queequeg and I often visited the craft, and as often I asked about Captain Ahab, and how he was, and when he was going to come on board his ship. To these questions they would answer, that he was getting better and better, and was expected aboard every day; meantime, the two captains, Peleg and Bildad, could attend to everything necessary to fit the vessel for the voyage. If I had been downright honest with myself, I would have seen very plainly in my heart that I did but half fancy being committed this way to so long a voyage, without once laying my eyes on the man who was to be the absolute dictator of it, so soon as the ship sailed out upon the open sea. But when a man suspects any wrong, it sometimes happens that if he be already involved in the matter, he insensibly strives to cover up his suspicions even from himself. And much this way it was with me. I said nothing, and tried to think nothing.\n\nAt last it was given out that some time next day the ship would certainly sail. So next morning, Queequeg and I took a very early start.\n\n# CHAPTER 21. Going Aboard.\n\nIt was nearly six o’clock, but only grey imperfect misty dawn, when we drew nigh the wharf.\n\n“There are some sailors running ahead there, if I see right,” said I to Queequeg, “it can’t be shadows; she’s off by sunrise, I guess; come on!”\n\n“Avast!” cried a voice, whose owner at the same time coming close behind us, laid a hand upon both our shoulders, and then insinuating himself between us, stood stooping forward a little, in the uncertain twilight, strangely peering from Queequeg to me. It was Elijah.\n\n“Going aboard?”\n\n“Hands off, will you,” said I.\n\n“Lookee here,” said Queequeg, shaking himself, “go ’way!”\n\n“Ain’t going aboard, then?”\n\n“Yes, we are,” said I, “but what business is that of yours? Do you know, Mr. Elijah, that I consider you a little impertinent?”\n\n“No, no, no; I wasn’t aware of that,” said Elijah, slowly and wonderingly looking from me to Queequeg, with the most unaccountable glances.\n\n“Elijah,” said I, “you will oblige my friend and me by withdrawing. We are going to the Indian and Pacific Oceans, and would prefer not to be detained.”\n\n“Ye be, be ye? Coming back afore breakfast?”\n\n“He’s cracked, Queequeg,” said I, “come on.”\n\n“Holloa!” cried stationary Elijah, hailing us when we had removed a few paces.\n\n“Never mind him,” said I, “Queequeg, come on.”\n\nBut he stole up to us again, and suddenly clapping his hand on my shoulder, said—“Did ye see anything looking like men going towards that ship a while ago?”\n\nStruck by this plain matter-of-fact question, I answered, saying, “Yes, I thought I did see four or five men; but it was too dim to be sure.”\n\n“Very dim, very dim,” said Elijah. “Morning to ye.”\n\nOnce more we quitted him; but once more he came softly after us; and touching my shoulder again, said, “See if you can find ’em now, will ye?\n“Find who?”\n\n“Morning to ye! morning to ye!” he rejoined, again moving off. “Oh! I was going to warn ye against—but never mind, never mind—it’s all one, all in the family too;—sharp frost this morning, ain’t it? Good-bye to ye. Shan’t see ye again very soon, I guess; unless it’s before the Grand Jury.” And with these cracked words he finally departed, leaving me, for the moment, in no small wonderment at his frantic impudence.\n\nAt last, stepping on board the Pequod, we found everything in profound quiet, not a soul moving. The cabin entrance was locked within; the hatches were all on, and lumbered with coils of rigging. Going forward to the forecastle, we found the slide of the scuttle open. Seeing a light, we went down, and found only an old rigger there, wrapped in a tattered pea-jacket. He was thrown at whole length upon two chests, his face downwards and inclosed in his folded arms. The profoundest slumber slept upon him.\n\n“Those sailors we saw, Queequeg, where can they have gone to?” said I, looking dubiously at the sleeper. But it seemed that, when on the wharf, Queequeg had not at all noticed what I now alluded to; hence I would have thought myself to have been optically deceived in that matter, were it not for Elijah’s otherwise inexplicable question. But I beat the thing down; and again marking the sleeper, jocularly hinted to Queequeg that perhaps we had best sit up with the body; telling him to establish himself accordingly. He put his hand upon the sleeper’s rear, as though feeling if it was soft enough; and then, without more ado, sat quietly down there.\n\n“Gracious! Queequeg, don’t sit there,” said I.\n\n“Oh! perry dood seat,” said Queequeg, “my country way; won’t hurt him face.”\n\n“Face!” said I, “call that his face? very benevolent countenance then; but how hard he breathes, he’s heaving himself; get off, Queequeg, you are heavy, it’s grinding the face of the poor. Get off, Queequeg! Look, he’ll twitch you off soon. I wonder he don’t wake.”\n\nQueequeg removed himself to just beyond the head of the sleeper, and lighted his tomahawk pipe. I sat at the feet. We kept the pipe passing over the sleeper, from one to the other. Meanwhile, upon questioning him in his broken fashion, Queequeg gave me to understand that, in his land, owing to the absence of settees and sofas of all sorts, the king, chiefs, and great people generally, were in the custom of fattening some of the lower orders for ottomans; and to furnish a house comfortably in that respect, you had only to buy up eight or ten lazy fellows, and lay them round in the piers and alcoves. Besides, it was very convenient on an excursion; much better than those garden-chairs which are convertible into walking-sticks; upon occasion, a chief calling his attendant, and desiring him to make a settee of himself under a spreading tree, perhaps in some damp marshy place.\n\nWhile narrating these things, every time Queequeg received the tomahawk from me, he flourished the hatchet-side of it over the sleeper’s head.\n\n“What’s that for, Queequeg?”\n\n“Perry easy, kill-e; oh! perry easy!”\n\nHe was going on with some wild reminiscences about his tomahawk-pipe, which, it seemed, had in its two uses both brained his foes and soothed his soul, when we were directly attracted to the sleeping rigger. The strong vapor now completely filling the contracted hole, it began to tell upon him. He breathed with a sort of muffledness; then seemed troubled in the nose; then revolved over once or twice; then sat up and rubbed his eyes.\n\n“Holloa!” he breathed at last, “who be ye smokers?”\n\n“Shipped men,” answered I, “when does she sail?”\n\n“Aye, aye, ye are going in her, be ye? She sails to-day. The Captain came aboard last night.”\n\n“What Captain?—Ahab?”\n“Who but him indeed?”\n\nI was going to ask him some further questions concerning Ahab, when we heard a noise on deck.\n\n“Holloa! Starbuck’s astir,” said the rigger. “He’s a lively chief mate, that; good man, and a pious; but all alive now, I must turn to.” And so saying he went on deck, and we followed.\n\nIt was now clear sunrise. Soon the crew came on board in twos and threes; the riggers bestirred themselves; the mates were actively engaged; and several of the shore people were busy in bringing various last things on board. Meanwhile Captain Ahab remained invisibly enshrined within his cabin.\n\n# CHAPTER 22. Merry Christmas.\n\nAt length, towards noon, upon the final dismissal of the ship’s riggers, and after the Pequod had been hauled out from the wharf, and after the ever-thoughtful Charity had come off in a whale-boat, with her last gift—a night-cap for Stubb, the second mate, her brother-in-law, and a spare Bible for the steward—after all this, the two Captains, Peleg and Bildad, issued from the cabin, and turning to the chief mate, Peleg said:\n\n“Now, Mr. Starbuck, are you sure everything is right? Captain Ahab is all ready—just spoke to him—nothing more to be got from shore, eh? Well, call all hands, then. Muster ’em aft here—blast ’em!”\n\n“No need of profane words, however great the hurry, Peleg,” said Bildad, “but away with thee, friend Starbuck, and do our bidding.”\n\nHow now! Here upon the very point of starting for the voyage, Captain Peleg and Captain Bildad were going it with a high hand on the quarter-deck, just as if they were to be joint-commanders at sea, as well as to all appearances in port. And, as for Captain Ahab, no sign of him was yet to be seen; only, they said he was in the cabin. But then, the idea was, that his presence was by no means necessary in getting the ship under weigh, and steering her well out to sea. Indeed, as that was not at all his proper business, but the pilot’s; and as he was not yet completely recovered—so they said—therefore, Captain Ahab stayed below. And all this seemed natural enough; especially as in the merchant service many captains never show themselves on deck for a considerable time after heaving up the anchor, but remain over the cabin table, having a farewell merry-making with their shore friends, before they quit the ship for good with the pilot.\n\nBut there was not much chance to think over the matter, for Captain Peleg was now all alive. He seemed to do most of the talking and commanding, and not Bildad.\n\n“Aft here, ye sons of bachelors,” he cried, as the sailors lingered at the main-mast. “Mr. Starbuck, drive ’em aft.”\n\n“Strike the tent there!”—was the next order. As I hinted before, this whalebone marquee was never pitched except in port; and on board the Pequod, for thirty years, the order to strike the tent was well known to be the next thing to heaving up the anchor.\n\n“Man the capstan! Blood and thunder!—jump!”—was the next command, and the crew sprang for the handspikes.\n\nNow in getting under weigh, the station generally occupied by the pilot is the forward part of the ship. And here Bildad, who, with Peleg, be it known, in addition to his other officers, was one of the licensed pilots of the port—he being suspected to have got himself made a pilot in order to save the Nantucket pilot-fee to all the ships he was concerned in, for he never piloted any other craft—Bildad, I say, might now be seen actively engaged in looking over the bows for the approaching anchor, and at intervals singing what seemed a dismal stave of psalmody, to cheer the hands at the windlass, who roared forth some sort of a chorus about the girls in Booble Alley, with hearty good will. Nevertheless, not three days previous, Bildad had told them that no profane songs would be allowed on board the Pequod, particularly in getting under weigh; and Charity, his sister, had placed a small choice copy of Watts in each seaman’s berth.\n\nMeantime, overseeing the other part of the ship, Captain Peleg ripped and swore astern in the most frightful manner. I almost thought he would sink the ship before the anchor could be got up; involuntarily I paused on my handspike, and told Queequeg to do the same, thinking of the perils we both ran, in starting on the voyage with such a devil for a pilot. I was comforting myself, however, with the thought that in pious Bildad might be found some salvation, spite of his seven hundred and seventy-seventh lay; when I felt a sudden sharp poke in my rear, and turning round, was horrified at the apparition of Captain Peleg in the act of withdrawing his leg from my immediate vicinity. That was my first kick.\n\n“Is that the way they heave in the marchant service?” he roared. “Spring, thou sheep-head; spring, and break thy backbone! Why don’t ye spring, I say, all of ye—spring! Quohog! spring, thou chap with the red whiskers; spring there, Scotch-cap; spring, thou green pants. Spring, I say, all of ye, and spring your eyes out!” And so saying, he moved along the windlass, here and there using his leg very freely, while imperturbable Bildad kept leading off with his psalmody. Thinks I, Captain Peleg must have been drinking something to-day.\n\nAt last the anchor was up, the sails were set, and off we glided. It was a short, cold Christmas; and as the short northern day merged into night, we found ourselves almost broad upon the wintry ocean, whose freezing spray cased us in ice, as in polished armor. The long rows of teeth on the bulwarks glistened in the moonlight; and like the white ivory tusks of some huge elephant, vast curving icicles depended from the bows.\n\nLank Bildad, as pilot, headed the first watch, and ever and anon, as the old craft deep dived into the green seas, and sent the shivering frost all over her, and the winds howled, and the cordage rang, his steady notes were heard,—\n\n“Sweet fields beyond the swelling flood, Stand dressed in living green. So to the Jews old Canaan stood, While Jordan rolled between.”\n\nNever did those sweet words sound more sweetly to me than then. They were full of hope and fruition. Spite of this frigid winter night in the boisterous Atlantic, spite of my wet feet and wetter jacket, there was yet, it then seemed to me, many a pleasant haven in store; and meads and glades so eternally vernal, that the grass shot up by the spring, untrodden, unwilted, remains at midsummer.\n\nAt last we gained such an offing, that the two pilots were needed no longer. The stout sail-boat that had accompanied us began ranging alongside.\n\nIt was curious and not unpleasing, how Peleg and Bildad were affected at this juncture, especially Captain Bildad. For loath to depart, yet; very loath to leave, for good, a ship bound on so long and perilous a voyage—beyond both stormy Capes; a ship in which some thousands of his hard earned dollars were invested; a ship, in which an old shipmate sailed as captain; a man almost as old as he, once more starting to encounter all the terrors of the pitiless jaw; loath to say good-bye to a thing so every way brimful of every interest to him,—poor old Bildad lingered long; paced the deck with anxious strides; ran down into the cabin to speak another farewell word there; again came on deck, and looked to windward; looked towards the wide and endless waters, only bounded by the far-off unseen Eastern Continents; looked towards the land; looked aloft; looked right and left; looked everywhere and nowhere; and at last, mechanically coiling a rope upon its pin, convulsively grasped stout Peleg by the hand, and holding up a lantern, for a moment stood gazing heroically in his face, as much as to say, “Nevertheless, friend Peleg, I can stand it; yes, I can.”\n\nAs for Peleg himself, he took it more like a philosopher; but for all his philosophy, there was a tear twinkling in his eye, when the lantern came too near. And he, too, did not a little run from cabin to deck—now a word below, and now a word with Starbuck, the chief mate.\n\nBut, at last, he turned to his comrade, with a final sort of look about him,—“Captain Bildad—come, old shipmate, we must go. Back the main-yard there! Boat ahoy! Stand by to come close alongside, now! Careful, careful!—come, Bildad, boy—say your last. Luck to ye, Starbuck—luck to ye, Mr. Stubb—luck to ye, Mr. Flask—good-bye and good luck to ye all—and this day three years I’ll have a hot supper smoking for ye in old Nantucket. Hurrah and away!”\n\n“God bless ye, and have ye in His holy keeping, men,” murmured old Bildad, almost incoherently. “I hope ye’ll have fine weather now, so that Captain Ahab may soon be moving among ye—a pleasant sun is all he needs, and ye’ll have plenty of them in the tropic voyage ye go. Be careful in the hunt, ye mates. Don’t stave the boats needlessly, ye harpooneers; good white cedar plank is raised full three per cent. within the year. Don’t forget your prayers, either. Mr. Starbuck, mind that cooper don’t waste the spare staves. Oh! the sail-needles are in the green locker! Don’t whale it too much a’ Lord’s days, men; but don’t miss a fair chance either, that’s rejecting Heaven’s good gifts. Have an eye to the molasses tierce, Mr. Stubb; it was a little leaky, I thought. If ye touch at the islands, Mr. Flask, beware of fornication. Good-bye, good-bye! Don’t keep that cheese too long down in the hold, Mr. Starbuck; it’ll spoil. Be careful with the butter—twenty cents the pound it was, and mind ye, if—”\n\n“Come, come, Captain Bildad; stop palavering,—away!” and with that, Peleg hurried him over the side, and both dropt into the boat.\n\nShip and boat diverged; the cold, damp night breeze blew between; a screaming gull flew overhead; the two hulls wildly rolled; we gave three heavy-hearted cheers, and blindly plunged like fate into the lone Atlantic.\n\n# CHAPTER 23. The Lee Shore.\n\nSome chapters back, one Bulkington was spoken of, a tall, newlanded mariner, encountered in New Bedford at the inn.\n\nWhen on that shivering winter’s night, the Pequod thrust her vindictive bows into the cold malicious waves, who should I see standing at her helm but Bulkington! I looked with sympathetic awe and fearfulness upon the man, who in mid-winter just landed from a four years’ dangerous voyage, could so unrestingly push off again for still another tempestuous term. The land seemed scorching to his feet. Wonderfullest things are ever the unmentionable; deep memories yield no epitaphs; this six-inch chapter is the stoneless grave of Bulkington. Let me only say that it fared with him as with the storm-tossed ship, that miserably drives along the leeward land. The port would fain give succor; the port is pitiful; in the port is safety, comfort, hearthstone, supper, warm blankets, friends, all that’s kind to our mortalities. But in that gale, the port, the land, is that ship’s direst jeopardy; she must fly all hospitality; one touch of land, though it but graze the keel, would make her shudder through and through. With all her might she crowds all sail off shore; in so doing, fights ’gainst the very winds that fain would blow her homeward; seeks all the lashed sea’s landlessness again; for refuge’s sake forlornly rushing into peril; her only friend her bitterest foe!\n\nKnow ye now, Bulkington? Glimpses do ye seem to see of that mortally intolerable truth; that all deep, earnest thinking is but the intrepid effort of the soul to keep the open independence of her sea; while the wildest winds of heaven and earth conspire to cast her on the treacherous, slavish shore?\n\nBut as in landlessness alone resides highest truth, shoreless, indefinite as God—so, better is it to perish in that howling infinite, than be ingloriously dashed upon the lee, even if that were safety! For worm-like, then, oh! who would craven crawl to land! Terrors of the terrible! is all this agony so vain? Take heart, take heart, O Bulkington! Bear thee grimly, demigod! Up from the spray of thy ocean-perishing—straight up, leaps thy apotheosis!\n\n# CHAPTER 24. The Advocate.\n\nAs Queequeg and I are now fairly embarked in this business of whaling; and as this business of whaling has somehow come to be regarded among landsmen as a rather unpoetical and disreputable pursuit; therefore, I am all anxiety to convince ye, ye landsmen, of the injustice hereby done to us hunters of whales.\n\nIn the first place, it may be deemed almost superfluous to establish the fact, that among people at large, the business of whaling is not accounted on a level with what are called the liberal professions. If a stranger were introduced into any miscellaneous metropolitan society, it would but slightly advance the general opinion of his merits, were he presented to the company as a harpooneer, say; and if in emulation of the naval officers he should append the initials S.W.F. (Sperm Whale Fishery) to his visiting card, such a procedure would be deemed pre-eminently presuming and ridiculous.\n\nDoubtless one leading reason why the world declines honoring us whalemen, is this: they think that, at best, our vocation amounts to a butchering sort of business; and that when actively engaged therein, we are surrounded by all manner of defilements. Butchers we are, that is true. But butchers, also, and butchers of the bloodiest badge have been all Martial Commanders whom the world invariably delights to honor. And as for the matter of the alleged uncleanliness of our business, ye shall soon be initiated into certain facts hitherto pretty generally unknown, and which, upon the whole, will triumphantly plant the sperm whale-ship at least among the cleanliest things of this tidy earth. But even granting the charge in question to be true; what disordered slippery decks of a whale-ship are comparable to the unspeakable carrion of those battle-fields from which so many soldiers return to drink in all ladies’ plaudits? And if the idea of peril so much enhances the popular conceit of the soldier’s profession; let me assure ye that many a veteran who has freely marched up to a battery, would quickly recoil at the apparition of the sperm whale’s vast tail, fanning into eddies the air over his head. For what are the comprehensible terrors of man compared with the interlinked terrors and wonders of God!\n\nBut, though the world scouts at us whale hunters, yet does it unwittingly pay us the profoundest homage; yea, an all-abounding adoration! for almost all the tapers, lamps, and candles that burn round the globe, burn, as before so many shrines, to our glory!\n\nBut look at this matter in other lights; weigh it in all sorts of scales; see what we whalemen are, and have been.\n\nWhy did the Dutch in De Witt’s time have admirals of their whaling fleets? Why did Louis XVI. of France, at his own personal expense, fit out whaling ships from Dunkirk, and politely invite to that town some score or two of families from our own island of Nantucket? Why did Britain between the years 1750 and 1788 pay to her whalemen in bounties upwards of £1,000,000? And lastly, how comes it that we whalemen of America now outnumber all the rest of the banded whalemen in the world; sail a navy of upwards of seven hundred vessels; manned by eighteen thousand men; yearly consuming 4,000,000 of dollars; the ships worth, at the time of sailing, $20,000,000! and every year importing into our harbors a well reaped harvest of $7,000,000. How comes all this, if there be not something puissant in whaling?\n\nBut this is not the half; look again.\n\nI freely assert, that the cosmopolite philosopher cannot, for his life, point out one single peaceful influence, which within the last sixty years has operated more potentially upon the whole broad world, taken in one aggregate, than the high and mighty business of whaling. One way and another, it has begotten events so remarkable in themselves, and so continuously momentous in their sequential issues, that whaling may well be regarded as that Egyptian mother, who bore offspring themselves pregnant from her womb. It would be a hopeless, endless task to catalogue all these things. Let a handful suffice. For many years past the whale-ship has been the pioneer in ferreting out the remotest and least known parts of the earth. She has explored seas and archipelagoes which had no chart, where no Cook or Vancouver had ever sailed. If American and European men-of-war now peacefully ride in once savage harbors, let them fire salutes to the honor and glory of the whale-ship, which originally showed them the way, and first interpreted between them and the savages. They may celebrate as they will the heroes of Exploring Expeditions, your Cooks, your Krusensterns; but I say that scores of anonymous Captains have sailed out of Nantucket, that were as great, and greater than your Cook and your Krusenstern. For in their succourless empty-handedness, they, in the heathenish sharked waters, and by the beaches of unrecorded, javelin islands, battled with virgin wonders and terrors that Cook with all his marines and muskets would not willingly have dared. All that is made such a flourish of in the old South Sea Voyages, those things were but the life-time commonplaces of our heroic Nantucketers. Often, adventures which Vancouver dedicates three chapters to, these men accounted unworthy of being set down in the ship’s common log. Ah, the world! Oh, the world!\n\nUntil the whale fishery rounded Cape Horn, no commerce but colonial, scarcely any intercourse but colonial, was carried on between Europe and the long line of the opulent Spanish provinces on the Pacific coast. It was the whaleman who first broke through the jealous policy of the Spanish crown, touching those colonies; and, if space permitted, it might be distinctly shown how from those whalemen at last eventuated the liberation of Peru, Chili, and Bolivia from the yoke of Old Spain, and the establishment of the eternal democracy in those parts.\n\nThat great America on the other side of the sphere, Australia, was given to the enlightened world by the whaleman. After its first blunder-born discovery by a Dutchman, all other ships long shunned those shores as pestiferously barbarous; but the whale-ship touched there. The whale-ship is the true mother of that now mighty colony. Moreover, in the infancy of the first Australian settlement, the emigrants were several times saved from starvation by the benevolent biscuit of the whale-ship luckily dropping an anchor in their waters. The uncounted isles of all Polynesia confess the same truth, and do commercial homage to the whale-ship, that cleared the way for the missionary and the merchant, and in many cases carried the primitive missionaries to their first destinations. If that double-bolted land, Japan, is ever to become hospitable, it is the whale-ship alone to whom the credit will be due; for already she is on the threshold.\n\nBut if, in the face of all this, you still declare that whaling has no æsthetically noble associations connected with it, then am I ready to shiver fifty lances with you there, and unhorse you with a split helmet every time.\n\nThe whale has no famous author, and whaling no famous chronicler, you will say.\n\nThe whale no famous author, and whaling no famous chronicler? Who wrote the first account of our Leviathan? Who but mighty Job! And who composed the first narrative of a whaling-voyage? Who, but no less a prince than Alfred the Great, who, with his own royal pen, took down the words from Other, the Norwegian whale-hunter of those times! And who pronounced our glowing eulogy in Parliament? Who, but Edmund Burke!\n\nTrue enough, but then whalemen themselves are poor devils; they have no good blood in their veins.\n\nNo good blood in their veins? They have something better than royal blood there. The grandmother of Benjamin Franklin was Mary Morrel; afterwards, by marriage, Mary Folger, one of the old settlers of Nantucket, and the ancestress to a long line of Folgers and harpooneers—all kith and kin to noble Benjamin—this day darting the barbed iron from one side of the world to the other.\n\nGood again; but then all confess that somehow whaling is not respectable.\n\nWhaling not respectable? Whaling is imperial! By old English statutory law, the whale is declared “a royal fish.” *\n\nOh, that’s only nominal! The whale himself has never figured in any grand imposing way.\n\nThe whale never figured in any grand imposing way? In one of the mighty triumphs given to a Roman general upon his entering the world’s capital, the bones of a whale, brought all the way from the Syrian coast, were the most conspicuous object in the cymballed procession.*\n\n*See subsequent chapters for something more on this head.\n\nGrant it, since you cite it; but, say what you will, there is no real dignity in whaling.\n\nNo dignity in whaling? The dignity of our calling the very heavens attest. Cetus is a constellation in the South! No more! Drive down your hat in presence of the Czar, and take it off to Queequeg! No more! I know a man that, in his lifetime, has taken three hundred and fifty whales. I account that man more honorable than that great captain of antiquity who boasted of taking as many walled towns.\n\nAnd, as for me, if, by any possibility, there be any as yet undiscovered prime thing in me; if I shall ever deserve any real repute in that small but high hushed world which I might not be unreasonably ambitious of; if hereafter I shall do anything that, upon the whole, a man might rather have done than to have left undone; if, at my death, my executors, or more properly my creditors, find any precious MSS. in my desk, then here I prospectively ascribe all the honor and the glory to whaling; for a whale-ship was my Yale College and my Harvard.\n\n# CHAPTER 25. Postscript.\n\nIn behalf of the dignity of whaling, I would fain advance naught but substantiated facts. But after embattling his facts, an advocate who should wholly suppress a not unreasonable surmise, which might tell eloquently upon his cause—such an advocate, would he not be blameworthy?\n\nIt is well known that at the coronation of kings and queens, even modern ones, a certain curious process of seasoning them for their functions is gone through. There is a saltcellar of state, so called, and there may be a castor of state. How they use the salt, precisely—who knows? Certain I am, however, that a king’s head is solemnly oiled at his coronation, even as a head of salad. Can it be, though, that they anoint it with a view of making its interior run well, as they anoint machinery? Much might be ruminated here, concerning the essential dignity of this regal process, because in common life we esteem but meanly and contemptibly a fellow who anoints his hair, and palpably smells of that anointing. In truth, a mature man who uses hair-oil, unless medicinally, that man has probably got a quoggy spot in him somewhere. As a general rule, he can’t amount to much in his totality.\n\nBut the only thing to be considered here, is this—what kind of oil is used at coronations? Certainly it cannot be olive oil, nor macassar oil, nor castor oil, nor bear’s oil, nor train oil, nor cod-liver oil. What then can it possibly be, but sperm oil in its unmanufactured, unpolluted state, the sweetest of all oils?\n\nThink of that, ye loyal Britons! we whalemen supply your kings and queens with coronation stuff!\n\n# CHAPTER 26. Knights and Squires.\n\nThe chief mate of the Pequod was Starbuck, a native of Nantucket, and a Quaker by descent. He was a long, earnest man, and though born on an icy coast, seemed well adapted to endure hot latitudes, his flesh being hard as twice-baked biscuit. Transported to the Indies, his live blood would not spoil like bottled ale. He must have been born in some time of general drought and famine, or upon one of those fast days for which his state is famous. Only some thirty arid summers had he seen; those summers had dried up all his physical superfluousness. But this, his thinness, so to speak, seemed no more the token of wasting anxieties and cares, than it seemed the indication of any bodily blight. It was merely the condensation of the man. He was by no means ill-looking; quite the contrary. His pure tight skin was an excellent fit; and closely wrapped up in it, and embalmed with inner health and strength, like a revivified Egyptian, this Starbuck seemed prepared to endure for long ages to come, and to endure always, as now; for be it Polar snow or torrid sun, like a patent chronometer, his interior vitality was warranted to do well in all climates. Looking into his eyes, you seemed to see there the yet lingering images of those thousand-fold perils he had calmly confronted through life. A staid, steadfast man, whose life for the most part was a telling pantomime of action, and not a tame chapter of sounds. Yet, for all his hardy sobriety and fortitude, there were certain qualities in him which at times affected, and in some cases seemed well nigh to overbalance all the rest. Uncommonly conscientious for a seaman, and endued with a deep natural reverence, the wild watery loneliness of his life did therefore strongly incline him to superstition; but to that sort of superstition, which in some organizations seems rather to spring, somehow, from intelligence than from ignorance. Outward portents and inward presentiments were his. And if at times these things bent the welded iron of his soul, much more did his far-away domestic memories of his young Cape wife and child, tend to bend him still more from the original ruggedness of his nature, and open him still further to those latent influences which, in some honest-hearted men, restrain the gush of dare-devil daring, so often evinced by others in the more perilous vicissitudes of the fishery. “I will have no man in my boat,” said Starbuck, “who is not afraid of a whale.” By this, he seemed to mean, not only that the most reliable and useful courage was that which arises from the fair estimation of the encountered peril, but that an utterly fearless man is a far more dangerous comrade than a coward.\n\n“Aye, aye,” said Stubb, the second mate, “Starbuck, there, is as careful a man as you’ll find anywhere in this fishery.” But we shall ere long see what that word “careful” precisely means when used by a man like Stubb, or almost any other whale hunter.\n\nStarbuck was no crusader after perils; in him courage was not a sentiment; but a thing simply useful to him, and always at hand upon all mortally practical occasions. Besides, he thought, perhaps, that in this business of whaling, courage was one of the great staple outfits of the ship, like her beef and her bread, and not to be foolishly wasted. Wherefore he had no fancy for lowering for whales after sun-down; nor for persisting in fighting a fish that too much persisted in fighting him. For, thought Starbuck, I am here in this critical ocean to kill whales for my living, and not to be killed by them for theirs; and that hundreds of men had been so killed Starbuck well knew. What doom was his own father’s? Where, in the bottomless deeps, could he find the torn limbs of his brother?\n\nWith memories like these in him, and, moreover, given to a certain superstitiousness, as has been said; the courage of this Starbuck which could, nevertheless, still flourish, must indeed have been extreme. But it was not in reasonable nature that a man so organized, and with such terrible experiences and remembrances as he had; it was not in nature that these things should fail in latently engendering an element in him, which, under suitable circumstances, would break out from its confinement, and burn all his courage up. And brave as he might be, it was that sort of bravery chiefly, visible in some intrepid men, which, while generally abiding firm in the conflict with seas, or winds, or whales, or any of the ordinary irrational horrors of the world, yet cannot withstand those more terrific, because more spiritual terrors, which sometimes menace you from the concentrating brow of an enraged and mighty man.\n\nBut were the coming narrative to reveal in any instance, the complete abasement of poor Starbuck’s fortitude, scarce might I have the heart to write it; for it is a thing most sorrowful, nay shocking, to expose the fall of valour in the soul. Men may seem detestable as joint stock-companies and nations; knaves, fools, and murderers there may be; men may have mean and meagre faces; but man, in the ideal, is so noble and so sparkling, such a grand and glowing creature, that over any ignominious blemish in him all his fellows should run to throw their costliest robes. That immaculate manliness we feel within ourselves, so far within us, that it remains intact though all the outer character seem gone; bleeds with keenest anguish at the undraped spectacle of a valor-ruined man. Nor can piety itself, at such a shameful sight, completely stifle her upbraidings against the permitting stars. But this august dignity I treat of, is not the dignity of kings and robes, but that abounding dignity which has no robed investiture. Thou shalt see it shining in the arm that wields a pick or drives a spike; that democratic dignity which, on all hands, radiates without end from God; Himself! The great God absolute! The centre and circumference of all democracy! His omnipresence, our divine equality!\n\nIf, then, to meanest mariners, and renegades and castaways, I shall hereafter ascribe high qualities, though dark; weave round them tragic graces; if even the most mournful, perchance the most abased, among them all, shall at times lift himself to the exalted mounts; if I shall touch that workman’s arm with some ethereal light; if I shall spread a rainbow over his disastrous set of sun; then against all mortal critics bear me out in it, thou just Spirit of Equality, which hast spread one royal mantle of humanity over all my kind! Bear me out in it, thou great democratic God! who didst not refuse to the swart convict, Bunyan, the pale, poetic pearl; Thou who didst clothe with doubly hammered leaves of finest gold, the stumped and paupered arm of old Cervantes; Thou who didst pick up Andrew Jackson from the pebbles; who didst hurl him upon a war-horse; who didst thunder him higher than a throne! Thou who, in all Thy mighty, earthly marchings, ever cullest Thy selectest champions from the kingly commons; bear me out in it, O God!\n\n# CHAPTER 27. Knights and Squires.\n\nStubb was the second mate. He was a native of Cape Cod; and hence, according to local usage, was called a Cape-Cod-man. A happy-go-lucky; neither craven nor valiant; taking perils as they came with an indifferent air; and while engaged in the most imminent crisis of the chase, toiling away, calm and collected as a journeyman joiner engaged for the year. Good-humored, easy, and careless, he presided over his whale-boat as if the most deadly encounter were but a dinner, and his crew all invited guests. He was as particular about the comfortable arrangement of his part of the boat, as an old stage-driver is about the snugness of his box. When close to the whale, in the very death-lock of the fight, he handled his unpitying lance coolly and off-handedly, as a whistling tinker his hammer. He would hum over his old rigadig tunes while flank and flank with the most exasperated monster. Long usage had, for this Stubb, converted the jaws of death into an easy chair. What he thought of death itself, there is no telling. Whether he ever thought of it at all, might be a question; but, if he ever did chance to cast his mind that way after a comfortable dinner, no doubt, like a good sailor, he took it to be a sort of call of the watch to tumble aloft, and bestir themselves there, about something which he would find out when he obeyed the order, and not sooner.\n\nWhat, perhaps, with other things, made Stubb such an easy-going, unfearing man, so cheerily trudging off with the burden of life in a world full of grave pedlars, all bowed to the ground with their packs; what helped to bring about that almost impious good-humor of his; that thing must have been his pipe. For, like his nose, his short, black little pipe was one of the regular features of his face. You would almost as soon have expected him to turn out of his bunk without his nose as without his pipe. He kept a whole row of pipes there ready loaded, stuck in a rack, within easy reach of his hand; and, whenever he turned in, he smoked them all out in succession, lighting one from the other to the end of the chapter; then loading them again to be in readiness anew. For, when Stubb dressed, instead of first putting his legs into his trowsers, he put his pipe into his mouth.\n\nI say this continual smoking must have been one cause, at least, of his peculiar disposition; for every one knows that this earthly air, whether ashore or afloat, is terribly infected with the nameless miseries of the numberless mortals who have died exhaling it; and as in time of the cholera, some people go about with a camphorated handkerchief to their mouths; so, likewise, against all mortal tribulations, Stubb’s tobacco smoke might have operated as a sort of disinfecting agent.\n\nThe third mate was Flask, a native of Tisbury, in Martha’s Vineyard. A short, stout, ruddy young fellow, very pugnacious concerning whales, who somehow seemed to think that the great leviathans had personally and hereditarily affronted him; and therefore it was a sort of point of honor with him, to destroy them whenever encountered. So utterly lost was he to all sense of reverence for the many marvels of their majestic bulk and mystic ways; and so dead to anything like an apprehension of any possible danger from encountering them; that in his poor opinion, the wondrous whale was but a species of magnified mouse, or at least water-rat, requiring only a little circumvention and some small application of time and trouble in order to kill and boil. This ignorant, unconscious fearlessness of his made him a little waggish in the matter of whales; he followed these fish for the fun of it; and a three years’ voyage round Cape Horn was only a jolly joke that lasted that length of time. As a carpenter’s nails are divided into wrought nails and cut nails; so mankind may be similarly divided. Little Flask was one of the wrought ones; made to clinch tight and last long. They called him King-Post on board of the Pequod; because, in form, he could be well likened to the short, square timber known by that name in Arctic whalers; and which by the means of many radiating side timbers inserted into it, serves to brace the ship against the icy concussions of those battering seas.\n\nNow these three mates—Starbuck, Stubb, and Flask, were momentous men. They it was who by universal prescription commanded three of the Pequod’s boats as headsmen. In that grand order of battle in which Captain Ahab would probably marshal his forces to descend on the whales, these three headsmen were as captains of companies. Or, being armed with their long keen whaling spears, they were as a picked trio of lancers; even as the harpooneers were flingers of javelins.\n\nAnd since in this famous fishery, each mate or headsman, like a Gothic Knight of old, is always accompanied by his boat-steerer or harpooneer, who in certain conjunctures provides him with a fresh lance, when the former one has been badly twisted, or elbowed in the assault; and moreover, as there generally subsists between the two, a close intimacy and friendliness; it is therefore but meet, that in this place we set down who the Pequod’s harpooneers were, and to what headsman each of them belonged.\n\nFirst of all was Queequeg, whom Starbuck, the chief mate, had selected for his squire. But Queequeg is already known.\n\nNext was Tashtego, an unmixed Indian from Gay Head, the most westerly promontory of Martha’s Vineyard, where there still exists the last remnant of a village of red men, which has long supplied the neighboring island of Nantucket with many of her most daring harpooneers. In the fishery, they usually go by the generic name of Gay-Headers. Tashtego’s long, lean, sable hair, his high cheek bones, and black rounding eyes—for an Indian, Oriental in their largeness, but Antarctic in their glittering expression—all this sufficiently proclaimed him an inheritor of the unvitiated blood of those proud warrior hunters, who, in quest of the great New England moose, had scoured, bow in hand, the aboriginal forests of the main. But no longer snuffing in the trail of the wild beasts of the woodland, Tashtego now hunted in the wake of the great whales of the sea; the unerring harpoon of the son fitly replacing the infallible arrow of the sires. To look at the tawny brawn of his lithe snaky limbs, you would almost have credited the superstitions of some of the earlier Puritans, and half-believed this wild Indian to be a son of the Prince of the Powers of the Air. Tashtego was Stubb the second mate’s squire.\n\nThird among the harpooneers was Daggoo, a gigantic, coal-black negro-savage, with a lion-like tread—an Ahasuerus to behold. Suspended from his ears were two golden hoops, so large that the sailors called them ring-bolts, and would talk of securing the top-sail halyards to them. In his youth Daggoo had voluntarily shipped on board of a whaler, lying in a lonely bay on his native coast. And never having been anywhere in the world but in Africa, Nantucket, and the pagan harbors most frequented by whalemen; and having now led for many years the bold life of the fishery in the ships of owners uncommonly heedful of what manner of men they shipped; Daggoo retained all his barbaric virtues, and erect as a giraffe, moved about the decks in all the pomp of six feet five in his socks. There was a corporeal humility in looking up at him; and a white man standing before him seemed a white flag come to beg truce of a fortress. Curious to tell, this imperial negro, Ahasuerus Daggoo, was the Squire of little Flask, who looked like a chess-man beside him. As for the residue of the Pequod’s company, be it said, that at the present day not one in two of the many thousand men before the mast employed in the American whale fishery, are Americans born, though pretty nearly all the officers are. Herein it is the same with the American whale fishery as with the American army and military and merchant navies, and the engineering forces employed in the construction of the American Canals and Railroads. The same, I say, because in all these cases the native American liberally provides the brains, the rest of the world as generously supplying the muscles. No small number of these whaling seamen belong to the Azores, where the outward bound Nantucket whalers frequently touch to augment their crews from the hardy peasants of those rocky shores. In like manner, the Greenland whalers sailing out of Hull or London, put in at the Shetland Islands, to receive the full complement of their crew. Upon the passage homewards, they drop them there again. How it is, there is no telling, but Islanders seem to make the best whalemen. They were nearly all Islanders in the Pequod, Isolatoes too, I call such, not acknowledging the common continent of men, but each Isolato living on a separate continent of his own. Yet now, federated along one keel, what a set these Isolatoes were! An Anacharsis Clootz deputation from all the isles of the sea, and all the ends of the earth, accompanying Old Ahab in the Pequod to lay the world’s grievances before that bar from which not very many of them ever come back. Black Little Pip—he never did—oh, no! he went before. Poor Alabama boy! On the grim Pequod’s forecastle, ye shall ere long see him, beating his tambourine; prelusive of the eternal time, when sent for, to the great quarter-deck on high, he was bid strike in with angels, and beat his tambourine in glory; called a coward here, hailed a hero there!\n\n# CHAPTER 28. Ahab.\n\nFor several days after leaving Nantucket, nothing above hatches was seen of Captain Ahab. The mates regularly relieved each other at the watches, and for aught that could be seen to the contrary, they seemed to be the only commanders of the ship; only they sometimes issued from the cabin with orders so sudden and peremptory, that after all it was plain they but commanded vicariously. Yes, their supreme lord and dictator was there, though hitherto unseen by any eyes not permitted to penetrate into the now sacred retreat of the cabin.\n\nEvery time I ascended to the deck from my watches below, I instantly gazed aft to mark if any strange face were visible; for my first vague disquietude touching the unknown captain, now in the seclusion of the sea, became almost a perturbation. This was strangely heightened at times by the ragged Elijah’s diabolical incoherences uninvitedly recurring to me, with a subtle energy I could not have before conceived of. But poorly could I withstand them, much as in other moods I was almost ready to smile at the solemn whimsicalities of that outlandish prophet of the wharves. But whatever it was of apprehensiveness or uneasiness—to call it so—which I felt, yet whenever I came to look about me in the ship, it seemed against all warrantry to cherish such emotions. For though the harpooneers, with the great body of the crew, were a far more barbaric, heathenish, and motley set than any of the tame merchant-ship companies which my previous experiences had made me acquainted with, still I ascribed this—and rightly ascribed it—to the fierce uniqueness of the very nature of that wild Scandinavian vocation in which I had so abandonedly embarked. But it was especially the aspect of the three chief officers of the ship, the mates, which was most forcibly calculated to allay these colourless misgivings, and induce confidence and cheerfulness in every presentment of the voyage. Three better, more likely sea-officers and men, each in his own different way, could not readily be found, and they were every one of them Americans; a Nantucketer, a Vineyarder, a Cape man. Now, it being Christmas when the ship shot from out her harbor, for a space we had biting Polar weather, though all the time running away from it to the southward; and by every degree and minute of latitude which we sailed, gradually leaving that merciless winter, and all its intolerable weather behind us. It was one of those less lowering, but still grey and gloomy enough mornings of the transition, when with a fair wind the ship was rushing through the water with a vindictive sort of leaping and melancholy rapidity, that as I mounted to the deck at the call of the forenoon watch, so soon as I levelled my glance towards the taffrail, foreboding shivers ran over me. Reality outran apprehension; Captain Ahab stood upon his quarter-deck.\n\nThere seemed no sign of common bodily illness about him, nor of the recovery from any. He looked like a man cut away from the stake, when the fire has overrunningly wasted all the limbs without consuming them, or taking away one particle from their compacted aged robustness. His whole high, broad form, seemed made of solid bronze, and shaped in an unalterable mould, like Cellini’s cast Perseus. Threading its way out from among his grey hairs, and continuing right down one side of his tawny scorched face and neck, till it disappeared in his clothing, you saw a slender rod-like mark, lividly whitish. It resembled that perpendicular seam sometimes made in the straight, lofty trunk of a great tree, when the upper lightning tearingly darts down it, and without wrenching a single twig, peels and grooves out the bark from top to bottom, ere running off into the soil, leaving the tree still greenly alive, but branded. Whether that mark was born with him, or whether it was the scar left by some desperate wound, no one could certainly say. By some tacit consent, throughout the voyage little or no allusion was made to it, especially by the mates. But once Tashtego’s senior, an old Gay-Head Indian among the crew, superstitiously asserted that not till he was full forty years old did Ahab become that way branded, and then it came upon him, not in the fury of any mortal fray, but in an elemental strife at sea. Yet, this wild hint seemed inferentially negatived, by what a grey Manxman insinuated, an old sepulchral man, who, having never before sailed out of Nantucket, had never ere this laid eye upon wild Ahab. Nevertheless, the old sea-traditions, the immemorial credulities, popularly invested this old Manxman with preternatural powers of discernment. So that no white sailor seriously contradicted him when he said that if ever Captain Ahab should be tranquilly laid out—which might hardly come to pass, so he muttered—then, whoever should do that last office for the dead, would find a birth-mark on him from crown to sole.\n\nSo powerfully did the whole grim aspect of Ahab affect me, and the livid brand which streaked it, that for the first few moments I hardly noted that not a little of this overbearing grimness was owing to the barbaric white leg upon which he partly stood. It had previously come to me that this ivory leg had at sea been fashioned from the polished bone of the sperm whale’s jaw. “Aye, he was dismasted off Japan,” said the old Gay-Head Indian once; “but like his dismasted craft, he shipped another mast without coming home for it. He has a quiver of ’em.”\n\nI was struck with the singular posture he maintained. Upon each side of the Pequod’s quarter deck, and pretty close to the mizzen shrouds, there was an auger hole, bored about half an inch or so, into the plank. His bone leg steadied in that hole; one arm elevated, and holding by a shroud; Captain Ahab stood erect, looking straight out beyond the ship’s ever-pitching prow. There was an infinity of firmest fortitude, a determinate, unsurrenderable wilfulness, in the fixed and fearless, forward dedication of that glance. Not a word he spoke; nor did his officers say aught to him; though by all their minutest gestures and expressions, they plainly showed the uneasy, if not painful, consciousness of being under a troubled master-eye. And not only that, but moody stricken Ahab stood before them with a crucifixion in his face; in all the nameless regal overbearing dignity of some mighty woe.\n\nEre long, from his first visit in the air, he withdrew into his cabin. But after that morning, he was every day visible to the crew; either standing in his pivot-hole, or seated upon an ivory stool he had; or heavily walking the deck. As the sky grew less gloomy; indeed, began to grow a little genial, he became still less and less a recluse; as if, when the ship had sailed from home, nothing but the dead wintry bleakness of the sea had then kept him so secluded. And, by and by, it came to pass, that he was almost continually in the air; but, as yet, for all that he said, or perceptibly did, on the at last sunny deck, he seemed as unnecessary there as another mast. But the Pequod was only making a passage now; not regularly cruising; nearly all whaling preparatives needing supervision the mates were fully competent to, so that there was little or nothing, out of himself, to employ or excite Ahab, now; and thus chase away, for that one interval, the clouds that layer upon layer were piled upon his brow, as ever all clouds choose the loftiest peaks to pile themselves upon.\n\nNevertheless, ere long, the warm, warbling persuasiveness of the pleasant, holiday weather we came to, seemed gradually to charm him from his mood. For, as when the red-cheeked, dancing girls, April and May, trip home to the wintry, misanthropic woods; even the barest, ruggedest, most thunder-cloven old oak will at least send forth some few green sprouts, to welcome such glad-hearted visitants; so Ahab did, in the end, a little respond to the playful allurings of that girlish air. More than once did he put forth the faint blossom of a look, which, in any other man, would have soon flowered out in a smile.\n\n# CHAPTER 29. Enter Ahab; to Him, Stubb.\n\nSome days elapsed, and ice and icebergs all astern, the Pequod now went rolling through the bright Quito spring, which, at sea, almost perpetually reigns on the threshold of the eternal August of the Tropic. The warmly cool, clear, ringing, perfumed, overflowing, redundant days, were as crystal goblets of Persian sherbet, heaped up—flaked up, with rose-water snow. The starred and stately nights seemed haughty dames in jewelled velvets, nursing at home in lonely pride, the memory of their absent conquering Earls, the golden helmeted suns! For sleeping man, ’twas hard to choose between such winsome days and such seducing nights. But all the witcheries of that unwaning weather did not merely lend new spells and potencies to the outward world. Inward they turned upon the soul, especially when the still mild hours of eve came on; then, memory shot her crystals as the clear ice most forms of noiseless twilights. And all these subtle agencies, more and more they wrought on Ahab’s texture.\n\nOld age is always wakeful; as if, the longer linked with life, the less man has to do with aught that looks like death. Among sea-commanders, the old greybeards will oftenest leave their berths to visit the night-cloaked deck. It was so with Ahab; only that now, of late, he seemed so much to live in the open air, that truly speaking, his visits were more to the cabin, than from the cabin to the planks. “It feels like going down into one’s tomb,”—he would mutter to himself—“for an old captain like me to be descending this narrow scuttle, to go to my grave-dug berth.”\n\nSo, almost every twenty-four hours, when the watches of the night were set, and the band on deck sentinelled the slumbers of the band below; and when if a rope was to be hauled upon the forecastle, the sailors flung it not rudely down, as by day, but with some cautiousness dropt it to its place for fear of disturbing their slumbering shipmates; when this sort of steady quietude would begin to prevail, habitually, the silent steersman would watch the cabin-scuttle; and ere long the old man would emerge, gripping at the iron banister, to help his crippled way. Some considering touch of humanity was in him; for at times like these, he usually abstained from patrolling the quarter-deck; because to his wearied mates, seeking repose within six inches of his ivory heel, such would have been the reverberating crack and din of that bony step, that their dreams would have been on the crunching teeth of sharks. But once, the mood was on him too deep for common regardings; and as with heavy, lumber-like pace he was measuring the ship from taffrail to mainmast, Stubb, the old second mate, came up from below, with a certain unassured, deprecating humorousness, hinted that if Captain Ahab was pleased to walk the planks, then, no one could say nay; but there might be some way of muffling the noise; hinting something indistinctly and hesitatingly about a globe of tow, and the insertion into it, of the ivory heel. Ah! Stubb, thou didst not know Ahab then.\n\n“Am I a cannon-ball, Stubb,” said Ahab, “that thou wouldst wad me that fashion? But go thy ways; I had forgot. Below to thy nightly grave; where such as ye sleep between shrouds, to use ye to the filling one at last.—Down, dog, and kennel!”\n\nStarting at the unforseen concluding exclamation of the so suddenly scornful old man, Stubb was speechless a moment; then said excitedly, “I am not used to be spoken to that way, sir; I do but less than half like it, sir.”\n\n“Avast! gritted Ahab between his set teeth, and violently moving away, as if to avoid some passionate temptation.\n\n“No, sir; not yet,” said Stubb, emboldened, “I will not tamely be called a dog, sir.”\n\n“Then be called ten times a donkey, and a mule, and an ass, and begone, or I’ll clear the world of thee!”\n\nAs he said this, Ahab advanced upon him with such overbearing terrors in his aspect, that Stubb involuntarily retreated.\n\n“I was never served so before without giving a hard blow for it,” muttered Stubb, as he found himself descending the cabin-scuttle. “It’s very queer. Stop, Stubb; somehow, now, I don’t well know whether to go back and strike him, or—what’s that?—down here on my knees and pray for him? Yes, that was the thought coming up in me; but it would be the first time I ever did pray. It’s queer; very queer; and he’s queer too; aye, take him fore and aft, he’s about the queerest old man Stubb ever sailed with. How he flashed at me!—his eyes like powder-pans! is he mad? Anyway there’s something on his mind, as sure as there must be something on a deck when it cracks. He aint in his bed now, either, more than three hours out of the twenty-four; and he don’t sleep then. Didn’t that Dough-Boy, the steward, tell me that of a morning he always finds the old man’s hammock clothes all rumpled and tumbled, and the sheets down at the foot, and the coverlid almost tied into knots, and the pillow a sort of frightful hot, as though a baked brick had been on it? A hot old man! I guess he’s got what some folks ashore call a conscience; it’s a kind of Tic-Dolly-row they say—worse nor a toothache. Well, well; I don’t know what it is, but the Lord keep me from catching it. He’s full of riddles; I wonder what he goes into the after hold for, every night, as Dough-Boy tells me he suspects; what’s that for, I should like to know? Who’s made appointments with him in the hold? Ain’t that queer, now? But there’s no telling, it’s the old game—Here goes for a snooze. Damn me, it’s worth a fellow’s while to be born into the world, if only to fall right asleep. And now that I think of it, that’s about the first thing babies do, and that’s a sort of queer, too. Damn me, but all things are queer, come to think of ’em. But that’s against my principles. Think not, is my eleventh commandment; and sleep when you can, is my twelfth—So here goes again. But how’s that? didn’t he call me a dog? blazes! he called me ten times a donkey, and piled a lot of jackasses on top of that! He might as well have kicked me, and done with it. Maybe he did kick me, and I didn’t observe it, I was so taken all aback with his brow, somehow. It flashed like a bleached bone. What the devil’s the matter with me? I don’t stand right on my legs. Coming afoul of that old man has a sort of turned me wrong side out. By the Lord, I must have been dreaming, though—How? how? how?—but the only way’s to stash it; so here goes to hammock again; and in the morning, I’ll see how this plaguey juggling thinks over by daylight.”\n\n# CHAPTER 30. The Pipe.\n\nWhen Stubb had departed, Ahab stood for a while leaning over the bulwarks; and then, as had been usual with him of late, calling a sailor of the watch, he sent him below for his ivory stool, and also his pipe. Lighting the pipe at the binnacle lamp and planting the stool on the weather side of the deck, he sat and smoked.\n\nIn old Norse times, the thrones of the sea-loving Danish kings were fabricated, saith tradition, of the tusks of the narwhale. How could one look at Ahab then, seated on that tripod of bones, without bethinking him of the royalty it symbolized? For a Khan of the plank, and a king of the sea, and a great lord of Leviathans was Ahab.\n\nSome moments passed, during which the thick vapor came from his mouth in quick and constant puffs, which blew back again into his face. “How now,” he soliloquized at last, withdrawing the tube, “this smoking no longer soothes. Oh, my pipe! hard must it go with me if thy charm be gone! Here have I been unconsciously toiling, not pleasuring—aye, and ignorantly smoking to windward all the while; to windward, and with such nervous whiffs, as if, like the dying whale, my final jets were the strongest and fullest of trouble. What business have I with this pipe? This thing that is meant for sereneness, to send up mild white vapors among mild white hairs, not among torn iron-grey locks like mine. I’ll smoke no more—”\n\nHe tossed the still lighted pipe into the sea. The fire hissed in the waves; the same instant the ship shot by the bubble the sinking pipe made. With slouched hat, Ahab lurchingly paced the planks.\n\nNext morning Stubb accosted Flask.\n\n“Such a queer dream, King-Post, I never had. You know the old man’s ivory leg, well I dreamed he kicked me with it; and when I tried to kick back, upon my soul, my little man, I kicked my leg right off! And then, presto! Ahab seemed a pyramid, and I, like a blazing fool, kept kicking at it. But what was still more curious, Flask—you know how curious all dreams are—through all this rage that I was in, I somehow seemed to be thinking to myself, that after all, it was not much of an insult, that kick from Ahab. ‘Why,’ thinks I, ‘what’s the row? It’s not a real leg, only a false leg.’ And there’s a mighty difference between a living thump and a dead thump. That’s what makes a blow from the hand, Flask, fifty times more savage to bear than a blow from a cane. The living member—that makes the living insult, my little man. And thinks I to myself all the while, mind, while I was stubbing my silly toes against that cursed pyramid—so confoundedly contradictory was it all, all the while, I say, I was thinking to myself, ‘what’s his leg now, but a cane—a whalebone cane. Yes,’ thinks I, ‘it was only a playful cudgelling—in fact, only a whaleboning that he gave me—not a base kick. Besides,’ thinks I, ‘look at it once; why, the end of it—the foot part—what a small sort of end it is; whereas, if a broad footed farmer kicked me, there’s a devilish broad insult. But this insult is whittled down to a point only.’ But now comes the greatest joke of the dream, Flask. While I was battering away at the pyramid, a sort of badger-haired old merman, with a hump on his back, takes me by the shoulders, and slews me round. ‘What are you ’bout?’ says he. Slid! man, but I was frightened. Such a phiz! But, somehow, next moment I was over the fright. ‘What am I about?’ says I at last. ‘And what business is that of yours, I should like to know, Mr. Humpback? Do you want a kick?’ By the lord, Flask, I had no sooner said that, than he turned round his stern to me, bent over, and dragging up a lot of seaweed he had for a clout—what do you think, I saw?—why thunder alive, man, his stern was stuck full of marlinspikes, with the points out. Says I, on second thoughts, ‘I guess I won’t kick you, old fellow.’ ‘Wise Stubb,’ said he, ‘wise Stubb;’ and kept muttering it all the time, a sort of eating of his own gums like a chimney hag. Seeing he wasn’t going to stop saying over his ‘wise Stubb, wise Stubb,’ I thought I might as well fall to kicking the pyramid again. But I had only just lifted my foot for it, when he roared out, ‘Stop that kicking!’ ‘Halloa,’ says I, ‘what’s the matter now, old fellow?’ ‘Look ye here,’ says he; ‘let’s argue the insult. Captain Ahab kicked ye, didn’t he?’ ‘Yes, he did,’ says I—‘right here it was.’ ‘Very good,’ says he—‘he used his ivory leg, didn’t he?’ ‘Yes, he did,’ says I. ‘Well then,’ says he, ‘wise Stubb, what have you to complain of? Didn’t he kick with right good will? it wasn’t a common pitch pine leg he kicked with, was it? No, you were kicked by a great man, and with a beautiful ivory leg, Stubb. It’s an honor; I consider it an honor. Listen, wise Stubb. In old England the greatest lords think it great glory to be slapped by a queen, and made garter-knights of; but, be your boast, Stubb, that ye were kicked by old Ahab, and made a wise man of. Remember what I say; be kicked by him; account his kicks honors; and on no account kick back; for you can’t help yourself, wise Stubb. Don’t you see that pyramid?’ With that, he all of a sudden seemed somehow, in some queer fashion, to swim off into the air. I snored; rolled over; and there I was in my hammock! Now, what do you think of that dream, Flask?”\n\n“I don’t know; it seems a sort of foolish to me, tho.’”\n\n“May be; may be. But it’s made a wise man of me, Flask. D’ye see Ahab standing there, sideways looking over the stern? Well, the best thing you can do, Flask, is to let the old man alone; never speak to him, whatever he says. Halloa! What’s that he shouts? Hark!”\n\n“Mast-head, there! Look sharp, all of ye! There are whales hereabouts!\n\n“If ye see a white one, split your lungs for him!\n\n“What do you think of that now, Flask? ain’t there a small drop of something queer about that, eh? A white whale—did ye mark that, man? Look ye—there’s something special in the wind. Stand by for it, Flask. Ahab has that that’s bloody on his mind. But, mum; he comes this way.”\n\n# CHAPTER 32. Cetology.\n\nAlready we are boldly launched upon the deep; but soon we shall be lost in its unshored, harbourless immensities. Ere that come to pass; ere the Pequod’s weedy hull rolls side by side with the barnacled hulls of the leviathan; at the outset it is but well to attend to a matter almost indispensable to a thorough appreciative understanding of the more special leviathanic revelations and allusions of all sorts which are to follow.\n\nIt is some systematized exhibition of the whale in his broad genera, that I would now fain put before you. Yet is it no easy task. The classification of the constituents of a chaos, nothing less is here essayed. Listen to what the best and latest authorities have laid down.\n\n“No branch of Zoology is so much involved as that which is entitled Cetology,” says Captain Scoresby, A.D. 1820.\n\n“It is not my intention, were it in my power, to enter into the inquiry as to the true method of dividing the cetacea into groups and families. * * * Utter confusion exists among the historians of this animal” (sperm whale), says Surgeon Beale, A.D. 1839.\n\n“Unfitness to pursue our research in the unfathomable waters.” “Impenetrable veil covering our knowledge of the cetacea.” “A field strewn with thorns.” “All these incomplete indications but serve to torture us naturalists.”\n\nThus speak of the whale, the great Cuvier, and John Hunter, and Lesson, those lights of zoology and anatomy. Nevertheless, though of real knowledge there be little, yet of books there are a plenty; and so in some small degree, with cetology, or the science of whales. Many are the men, small and great, old and new, landsmen and seamen, who have at large or in little, written of the whale. Run over a few:—The Authors of the Bible; Aristotle; Pliny; Aldrovandi; Sir Thomas Browne; Gesner; Ray; Linnæus; Rondeletius; Willoughby; Green; Artedi; Sibbald; Brisson; Marten; Lacépède; Bonneterre; Desmarest; Baron Cuvier; Frederick Cuvier; John Hunter; Owen; Scoresby; Beale; Bennett; J. Ross Browne; the Author of Miriam Coffin; Olmstead; and the Rev. T. Cheever. But to what ultimate generalizing purpose all these have written, the above cited extracts will show.\n\nOf the names in this list of whale authors, only those following Owen ever saw living whales; and but one of them was a real professional harpooneer and whaleman. I mean Captain Scoresby. On the separate subject of the Greenland or right-whale, he is the best existing authority. But Scoresby knew nothing and says nothing of the great sperm whale, compared with which the Greenland whale is almost unworthy mentioning. And here be it said, that the Greenland whale is an usurper upon the throne of the seas. He is not even by any means the largest of the whales. Yet, owing to the long priority of his claims, and the profound ignorance which, till some seventy years back, invested the then fabulous or utterly unknown sperm-whale, and which ignorance to this present day still reigns in all but some few scientific retreats and whale-ports; this usurpation has been every way complete. Reference to nearly all the leviathanic allusions in the great poets of past days, will satisfy you that the Greenland whale, without one rival, was to them the monarch of the seas. But the time has at last come for a new proclamation. This is Charing Cross; hear ye! good people all,—the Greenland whale is deposed,—the great sperm whale now reigneth!\n\nThere are only two books in being which at all pretend to put the living sperm whale before you, and at the same time, in the remotest degree succeed in the attempt. Those books are Beale’s and Bennett’s; both in their time surgeons to English South-Sea whale-ships, and both exact and reliable men. The original matter touching the sperm whale to be found in their volumes is necessarily small; but so far as it goes, it is of excellent quality, though mostly confined to scientific description. As yet, however, the sperm whale, scientific or poetic, lives not complete in any literature. Far above all other hunted whales, his is an unwritten life.\n\nNow the various species of whales need some sort of popular comprehensive classification, if only an easy outline one for the present, hereafter to be filled in all its departments by subsequent laborers. As no better man advances to take this matter in hand, I hereupon offer my own poor endeavors. I promise nothing complete; because any human thing supposed to be complete, must for that very reason infallibly be faulty. I shall not pretend to a minute anatomical description of the various species, or—in this place at least—to much of any description. My object here is simply to project the draught of a systematization of cetology. I am the architect, not the builder.\n\nBut it is a ponderous task; no ordinary letter-sorter in the Post-Office is equal to it. To grope down into the bottom of the sea after them; to have one’s hands among the unspeakable foundations, ribs, and very pelvis of the world; this is a fearful thing. What am I that I should essay to hook the nose of this leviathan! The awful tauntings in Job might well appal me. Will he (the leviathan) make a covenant with thee? Behold the hope of him is vain! But I have swam through libraries and sailed through oceans; I have had to do with whales with these visible hands; I am in earnest; and I will try. There are some preliminaries to settle.\n\nFirst: The uncertain, unsettled condition of this science of Cetology is in the very vestibule attested by the fact, that in some quarters it still remains a moot point whether a whale be a fish. In his System of Nature, A.D. 1776, Linnæus declares, “I hereby separate the whales from the fish.” But of my own knowledge, I know that down to the year 1850, sharks and shad, alewives and herring, against Linnæus’s express edict, were still found dividing the possession of the same seas with the Leviathan.\n\nThe grounds upon which Linnæus would fain have banished the whales from the waters, he states as follows: “On account of their warm bilocular heart, their lungs, their movable eyelids, their hollow ears, penem intrantem feminam mammis lactantem,” and finally, “ex lege naturæ jure meritoque.” I submitted all this to my friends Simeon Macey and Charley Coffin, of Nantucket, both messmates of mine in a certain voyage, and they united in the opinion that the reasons set forth were altogether insufficient. Charley profanely hinted they were humbug.\n\nBe it known that, waiving all argument, I take the good old fashioned ground that the whale is a fish, and call upon holy Jonah to back me. This fundamental thing settled, the next point is, in what internal respect does the whale differ from other fish. Above, Linnæus has given you those items. But in brief, they are these: lungs and warm blood; whereas, all other fish are lungless and cold blooded.\n\nNext: how shall we define the whale, by his obvious externals, so as conspicuously to label him for all time to come? To be short, then, a whale is a spouting fish with a horizontal tail. There you have him. However contracted, that definition is the result of expanded meditation. A walrus spouts much like a whale, but the walrus is not a fish, because he is amphibious. But the last term of the definition is still more cogent, as coupled with the first. Almost any one must have noticed that all the fish familiar to landsmen have not a flat, but a vertical, or up-and-down tail. Whereas, among spouting fish the tail, though it may be similarly shaped, invariably assumes a horizontal position.\n\nBy the above definition of what a whale is, I do by no means exclude from the leviathanic brotherhood any sea creature hitherto identified with the whale by the best informed Nantucketers; nor, on the other hand, link with it any fish hitherto authoritatively regarded as alien.* Hence, all the smaller, spouting, and horizontal tailed fish must be included in this ground-plan of Cetology. Now, then, come the grand divisions of the entire whale host.\n\n*I am aware that down to the present time, the fish styled Lamatins and Dugongs (Pig-fish and Sow-fish of the Coffins of Nantucket) are included by many naturalists among the whales. But as these pig-fish are a noisy, contemptible set, mostly lurking in the mouths of rivers, and feeding on wet hay, and especially as they do not spout, I deny their credentials as whales; and have presented them with their passports to quit the Kingdom of Cetology.\n\nFirst: According to magnitude I divide the whales into three primary BOOKS (subdivisible into CHAPTERS), and these shall comprehend them all, both small and large.\n\nI. THE FOLIO WHALE; II. the OCTAVO WHALE; III. the DUODECIMO WHALE.\n\nAs the type of the FOLIO I present the Sperm Whale; of the OCTAVO, the Grampus; of the DUODECIMO, the Porpoise.\n\nFOLIOS. Among these I here include the following chapters:—I. The Sperm Whale; II. the Right Whale; III. the Fin-Back Whale; IV. the Hump-backed Whale; V. the Razor Back Whale; VI. the Sulphur Bottom Whale.\n\nBOOK I. (Folio), CHAPTER I. (Sperm Whale).—This whale, among the English of old vaguely known as the Trumpa whale, and the Physeter whale, and the Anvil Headed whale, is the present Cachalot of the French, and the Pottsfich of the Germans, and the Macrocephalus of the Long Words. He is, without doubt, the largest inhabitant of the globe; the most formidable of all whales to encounter; the most majestic in aspect; and lastly, by far the most valuable in commerce; he being the only creature from which that valuable substance, spermaceti, is obtained. All his peculiarities will, in many other places, be enlarged upon. It is chiefly with his name that I now have to do. Philologically considered, it is absurd. Some centuries ago, when the Sperm whale was almost wholly unknown in his own proper individuality, and when his oil was only accidentally obtained from the stranded fish; in those days spermaceti, it would seem, was popularly supposed to be derived from a creature identical with the one then known in England as the Greenland or Right Whale. It was the idea also, that this same spermaceti was that quickening humor of the Greenland Whale which the first syllable of the word literally expresses. In those times, also, spermaceti was exceedingly scarce, not being used for light, but only as an ointment and medicament. It was only to be had from the druggists as you nowadays buy an ounce of rhubarb. When, as I opine, in the course of time, the true nature of spermaceti became known, its original name was still retained by the dealers; no doubt to enhance its value by a notion so strangely significant of its scarcity. And so the appellation must at last have come to be bestowed upon the whale from which this spermaceti was really derived.\n\nBOOK I. (Folio), CHAPTER II. (Right Whale).—In one respect this is the most venerable of the leviathans, being the one first regularly hunted by man. It yields the article commonly known as whalebone or baleen; and the oil specially known as “whale oil,” an inferior article in commerce. Among the fishermen, he is indiscriminately designated by all the following titles: The Whale; the Greenland Whale; the Black Whale; the Great Whale; the True Whale; the Right Whale. There is a deal of obscurity concerning the identity of the species thus multitudinously baptised. What then is the whale, which I include in the second species of my Folios? It is the Great Mysticetus of the English naturalists; the Greenland Whale of the English whalemen; the Baleine Ordinaire of the French whalemen; the Growlands Walfish of the Swedes. It is the whale which for more than two centuries past has been hunted by the Dutch and English in the Arctic seas; it is the whale which the American fishermen have long pursued in the Indian ocean, on the Brazil Banks, on the Nor’ West Coast, and various other parts of the world, designated by them Right Whale Cruising Grounds.\n\nSome pretend to see a difference between the Greenland whale of the English and the right whale of the Americans. But they precisely agree in all their grand features; nor has there yet been presented a single determinate fact upon which to ground a radical distinction. It is by endless subdivisions based upon the most inconclusive differences, that some departments of natural history become so repellingly intricate. The right whale will be elsewhere treated of at some length, with reference to elucidating the sperm whale.\n\nBOOK I. (Folio), CHAPTER III. (Fin-Back).—Under this head I reckon a monster which, by the various names of Fin-Back, Tall-Spout, and Long-John, has been seen almost in every sea and is commonly the whale whose distant jet is so often descried by passengers crossing the Atlantic, in the New York packet-tracks. In the length he attains, and in his baleen, the Fin-back resembles the right whale, but is of a less portly girth, and a lighter colour, approaching to olive. His great lips present a cable-like aspect, formed by the intertwisting, slanting folds of large wrinkles. His grand distinguishing feature, the fin, from which he derives his name, is often a conspicuous object. This fin is some three or four feet long, growing vertically from the hinder part of the back, of an angular shape, and with a very sharp pointed end. Even if not the slightest other part of the creature be visible, this isolated fin will, at times, be seen plainly projecting from the surface. When the sea is moderately calm, and slightly marked with spherical ripples, and this gnomon-like fin stands up and casts shadows upon the wrinkled surface, it may well be supposed that the watery circle surrounding it somewhat resembles a dial, with its style and wavy hour-lines graved on it. On that Ahaz-dial the shadow often goes back. The Fin-Back is not gregarious. He seems a whale-hater, as some men are man-haters. Very shy; always going solitary; unexpectedly rising to the surface in the remotest and most sullen waters; his straight and single lofty jet rising like a tall misanthropic spear upon a barren plain; gifted with such wondrous power and velocity in swimming, as to defy all present pursuit from man; this leviathan seems the banished and unconquerable Cain of his race, bearing for his mark that style upon his back. From having the baleen in his mouth, the Fin-Back is sometimes included with the right whale, among a theoretic species denominated Whalebone whales, that is, whales with baleen. Of these so called Whalebone whales, there would seem to be several varieties, most of which, however, are little known. Broad-nosed whales and beaked whales; pike-headed whales; bunched whales; under-jawed whales and rostrated whales, are the fishermen’s names for a few sorts.\n\nIn connection with this appellative of “Whalebone whales,” it is of great importance to mention, that however such a nomenclature may be convenient in facilitating allusions to some kind of whales, yet it is in vain to attempt a clear classification of the Leviathan, founded upon either his baleen, or hump, or fin, or teeth; notwithstanding that those marked parts or features very obviously seem better adapted to afford the basis for a regular system of Cetology than any other detached bodily distinctions, which the whale, in his kinds, presents. How then? The baleen, hump, back-fin, and teeth; these are things whose peculiarities are indiscriminately dispersed among all sorts of whales, without any regard to what may be the nature of their structure in other and more essential particulars. Thus, the sperm whale and the humpbacked whale, each has a hump; but there the similitude ceases. Then, this same humpbacked whale and the Greenland whale, each of these has baleen; but there again the similitude ceases. And it is just the same with the other parts above mentioned. In various sorts of whales, they form such irregular combinations; or, in the case of any one of them detached, such an irregular isolation; as utterly to defy all general methodization formed upon such a basis. On this rock every one of the whale-naturalists has split.\n\nBut it may possibly be conceived that, in the internal parts of the whale, in his anatomy—there, at least, we shall be able to hit the right classification. Nay; what thing, for example, is there in the Greenland whale’s anatomy more striking than his baleen? Yet we have seen that by his baleen it is impossible correctly to classify the Greenland whale. And if you descend into the bowels of the various leviathans, why there you will not find distinctions a fiftieth part as available to the systematizer as those external ones already enumerated. What then remains? nothing but to take hold of the whales bodily, in their entire liberal volume, and boldly sort them that way. And this is the Bibliographical system here adopted; and it is the only one that can possibly succeed, for it alone is practicable. To proceed.\n\nBOOK I. (Folio) CHAPTER IV. (Hump Back).—This whale is often seen on the northern American coast. He has been frequently captured there, and towed into harbor. He has a great pack on him like a peddler; or you might call him the Elephant and Castle whale. At any rate, the popular name for him does not sufficiently distinguish him, since the sperm whale also has a hump though a smaller one. His oil is not very valuable. He has baleen. He is the most gamesome and light-hearted of all the whales, making more gay foam and white water generally than any other of them.\n\nBOOK I. (Folio), CHAPTER V. (Razor Back).—Of this whale little is known but his name. I have seen him at a distance off Cape Horn. Of a retiring nature, he eludes both hunters and philosophers. Though no coward, he has never yet shown any part of him but his back, which rises in a long sharp ridge. Let him go. I know little more of him, nor does anybody else. BOOK I. (Folio), CHAPTER VI. (Sulphur Bottom).—Another retiring gentleman, with a brimstone belly, doubtless got by scraping along the Tartarian tiles in some of his profounder divings. He is seldom seen; at least I have never seen him except in the remoter southern seas, and then always at too great a distance to study his countenance. He is never chased; he would run away with rope-walks of line. Prodigies are told of him. Adieu, Sulphur Bottom! I can say nothing more that is true of ye, nor can the oldest Nantucketer.\n\nThus ends BOOK I. (Folio), and now begins BOOK II. (Octavo).\n\nOCTAVOES.*—These embrace the whales of middling magnitude, among which present may be numbered:—I., the Grampus; II., the Black Fish; III., the Narwhale; IV., the Thrasher; V., the Killer.\n\n*Why this book of whales is not denominated the Quarto is very plain. Because, while the whales of this order, though smaller than those of the former order, nevertheless retain a proportionate likeness to them in figure, yet the bookbinder’s Quarto volume in its dimensioned form does not preserve the shape of the Folio volume, but the Octavo volume does.\n\nBOOK II. (Octavo), CHAPTER I. (Grampus).—Though this fish, whose loud sonorous breathing, or rather blowing, has furnished a proverb to landsmen, is so well known a denizen of the deep, yet is he not popularly classed among whales. But possessing all the grand distinctive features of the leviathan, most naturalists have recognised him for one. He is of moderate octavo size, varying from fifteen to twenty-five feet in length, and of corresponding dimensions round the waist. He swims in herds; he is never regularly hunted, though his oil is considerable in quantity, and pretty good for light. By some fishermen his approach is regarded as premonitory of the advance of the great sperm whale.\n\nBOOK II. (Octavo), CHAPTER II. (Black Fish).—I give the popular fishermen’s names for all these fish, for generally they are the best. Where any name happens to be vague or inexpressive, I shall say so, and suggest another. I do so now, touching the Black Fish, so-called, because blackness is the rule among almost all whales. So, call him the Hyena Whale, if you please. His voracity is well known, and from the circumstance that the inner angles of his lips are curved upwards, he carries an everlasting Mephistophelean grin on his face. This whale averages some sixteen or eighteen feet in length. He is found in almost all latitudes. He has a peculiar way of showing his dorsal hooked fin in swimming, which looks something like a Roman nose. When not more profitably employed, the sperm whale hunters sometimes capture the Hyena whale, to keep up the supply of cheap oil for domestic employment—as some frugal housekeepers, in the absence of company, and quite alone by themselves, burn unsavory tallow instead of odorous wax. Though their blubber is very thin, some of these whales will yield you upwards of thirty gallons of oil.\n\nBOOK II. (Octavo), CHAPTER III. (Narwhale), that is, Nostril whale.—Another instance of a curiously named whale, so named I suppose from his peculiar horn being originally mistaken for a peaked nose. The creature is some sixteen feet in length, while its horn averages five feet, though some exceed ten, and even attain to fifteen feet. Strictly speaking, this horn is but a lengthened tusk, growing out from the jaw in a line a little depressed from the horizontal. But it is only found on the sinister side, which has an ill effect, giving its owner something analogous to the aspect of a clumsy left-handed man. What precise purpose this ivory horn or lance answers, it would be hard to say. It does not seem to be used like the blade of the sword-fish and bill-fish; though some sailors tell me that the Narwhale employs it for a rake in turning over the bottom of the sea for food. Charley Coffin said it was used for an ice-piercer; for the Narwhale, rising to the surface of the Polar Sea, and finding it sheeted with ice, thrusts his horn up, and so breaks through. But you cannot prove either of these surmises to be correct. My own opinion is, that however this one-sided horn may really be used by the Narwhale—however that may be—it would certainly be very convenient to him for a folder in reading pamphlets. The Narwhale I have heard called the Tusked whale, the Horned whale, and the Unicorn whale. He is certainly a curious example of the Unicornism to be found in almost every kingdom of animated nature. From certain cloistered old authors I have gathered that this same sea-unicorn’s horn was in ancient days regarded as the great antidote against poison, and as such, preparations of it brought immense prices. It was also distilled to a volatile salts for fainting ladies, the same way that the horns of the male deer are manufactured into hartshorn. Originally it was in itself accounted an object of great curiosity. Black Letter tells me that Sir Martin Frobisher on his return from that voyage, when Queen Bess did gallantly wave her jewelled hand to him from a window of Greenwich Palace, as his bold ship sailed down the Thames; “when Sir Martin returned from that voyage,” saith Black\n\nLetter, “on bended knees he presented to her highness a prodigious long horn of the Narwhale, which for a long period after hung in the castle at Windsor.” An Irish author avers that the Earl of Leicester, on bended knees, did likewise present to her highness another horn, pertaining to a land beast of the unicorn nature.\n\nThe Narwhale has a very picturesque, leopard-like look, being of a milk-white ground colour, dotted with round and oblong spots of black. His oil is very superior, clear and fine; but there is little of it, and he is seldom hunted. He is mostly found in the circumpolar seas.\n\nBOOK II. (Octavo), CHAPTER IV. (Killer).—Of this whale little is precisely known to the Nantucketer, and nothing at all to the professed naturalist. From what I have seen of him at a distance, I should say that he was about the bigness of a grampus. He is very savage—a sort of Feegee fish. He sometimes takes the great Folio whales by the lip, and hangs there like a leech, till the mighty brute is worried to death. The Killer is never hunted. I never heard what sort of oil he has. Exception might be taken to the name bestowed upon this whale, on the ground of its indistinctness. For we are all killers, on land and on sea; Bonapartes and Sharks included.\n\nBOOK II. (Octavo), CHAPTER V. (Thrasher).—This gentleman is famous for his tail, which he uses for a ferule in thrashing his foes. He mounts the Folio whale’s back, and as he swims, he works his passage by flogging him; as some schoolmasters get along in the world by a similar process. Still less is known of the Thrasher than of the Killer. Both are outlaws, even in the lawless seas.\n\nThus ends BOOK II. (Octavo), and begins BOOK III. (Duodecimo).\n\nDUODECIMOES.—These include the smaller whales. I. The Huzza Porpoise. II. The Algerine Porpoise. III. The Mealy-mouthed Porpoise.\n\nTo those who have not chanced specially to study the subject, it may possibly seem strange, that fishes not commonly exceeding four or five feet should be marshalled among WHALES—a word, which, in the popular sense, always conveys an idea of hugeness. But the creatures set down above as Duodecimoes are infallibly whales, by the terms of my definition of what a whale is—i.e. a spouting fish, with a horizontal tail.\n\nBOOK III. (Duodecimo), CHAPTER 1. (Huzza Porpoise).—This is the common porpoise found almost all over the globe. The name is of my own bestowal; for there are more than one sort of porpoises, and something must be done to distinguish them. I call him thus, because he always swims in hilarious shoals, which upon the broad sea keep tossing themselves to heaven like caps in a Fourth-of-July crowd. Their appearance is generally hailed with delight by the mariner. Full of fine spirits, they invariably come from the breezy billows to windward. They are the lads that always live before the wind. They are accounted a lucky omen. If you yourself can withstand three cheers at beholding these vivacious fish, then heaven help ye; the spirit of godly gamesomeness is not in ye. A well-fed, plump Huzza Porpoise will yield you one good gallon of good oil. But the fine and delicate fluid extracted from his jaws is exceedingly valuable. It is in request among jewellers and watchmakers. Sailors put it on their hones. Porpoise meat is good eating, you know. It may never have occurred to you that a porpoise spouts. Indeed, his spout is so small that it is not very readily discernible. But the next time you have a chance, watch him; and you will then see the great Sperm whale himself in miniature.\n\nBOOK III. (Duodecimo), CHAPTER II. (Algerine Porpoise).—A pirate. Very savage. He is only found, I think, in the Pacific. He is somewhat larger than the Huzza Porpoise, but much of the same general make. Provoke him, and he will buckle to a shark. I have lowered for him many times, but never yet saw him captured. BOOK III. (Duodecimo), CHAPTER III. (Mealy-mouthed Porpoise).—The largest kind of Porpoise; and only found in the Pacific, so far as it is known. The only English name, by which he has hitherto been designated, is that of the fishers—Right-Whale Porpoise, from the circumstance that he is chiefly found in the vicinity of that Folio. In shape, he differs in some degree from the Huzza Porpoise, being of a less rotund and jolly girth; indeed, he is of quite a neat and gentleman-like figure. He has no fins on his back (most other porpoises have), he has a lovely tail, and sentimental Indian eyes of a hazel hue. But his mealy-mouth spoils all. Though his entire back down to his side fins is of a deep sable, yet a boundary line, distinct as the mark in a ship’s hull, called the “bright waist,” that line streaks him from stem to stern, with two separate colours, black above and white below. The white comprises part of his head, and the whole of his mouth, which makes him look as if he had just escaped from a felonious visit to a meal-bag. A most mean and mealy aspect! His oil is much like that of the common porpoise.\n\nBeyond the DUODECIMO, this system does not proceed, inasmuch as the Porpoise is the smallest of the whales. Above, you have all the Leviathans of note. But there are a rabble of uncertain, fugitive, half-fabulous whales, which, as an American whaleman, I know by reputation, but not personally. I shall enumerate them by their fore-castle appellations; for possibly such a list may be valuable to future investigators, who may complete what I have here but begun. If any of the following whales, shall hereafter be caught and marked, then he can readily be incorporated into this System, according to his Folio, Octavo, or Duodecimo magnitude:—The Bottle-Nose Whale; the Junk Whale; the Pudding-Headed Whale; the Cape Whale; the Leading Whale; the Cannon Whale; the Scragg Whale; the Coppered Whale; the Elephant Whale; the Iceberg Whale; the Quog Whale; the Blue Whale; etc. From Icelandic, Dutch, and old English authorities, there might be quoted other lists of uncertain whales, blessed with all manner of uncouth names. But I omit them as altogether obsolete; and can hardly help suspecting them for mere sounds, full of Leviathanism, but signifying nothing.\n\nFinally: It was stated at the outset, that this system would not be here, and at once, perfected. You cannot but plainly see that I have kept my word. But I now leave my cetological System standing thus unfinished, even as the great Cathedral of Cologne was left, with the crane still standing upon the top of the uncompleted tower. For small erections may be finished by their first architects; grand ones, true ones, ever leave the copestone to posterity. God keep me from ever completing anything. This whole book is but a draught—nay, but the draught of a draught. Oh, Time, Strength, Cash, and Patience!\n\n# CHAPTER 33. The Specksnyder.\n\nConcerning the officers of the whale-craft, this seems as good a place as any to set down a little domestic peculiarity on ship-board, arising from the existence of the harpooneer class of officers, a class unknown of course in any other marine than the whale-fleet.\n\nThe large importance attached to the harpooneer’s vocation is evinced by the fact, that originally in the old Dutch Fishery, two centuries and more ago, the command of a whale ship was not wholly lodged in the person now called the captain, but was divided between him and an officer called the Specksnyder. Literally this word means Fat-Cutter; usage, however, in time made it equivalent to Chief Harpooneer. In those days, the captain’s authority was restricted to the navigation and general management of the vessel; while over the whale-hunting department and all its concerns, the Specksnyder or Chief Harpooneer reigned supreme. In the British Greenland Fishery, under the corrupted title of Specksioneer, this old Dutch official is still retained, but his former dignity is sadly abridged. At present he ranks simply as senior Harpooneer; and as such, is but one of the captain’s more inferior subalterns. Nevertheless, as upon the good conduct of the harpooneers the success of a whaling voyage largely depends, and since in the American Fishery he is not only an important officer in the boat, but under certain circumstances (night watches on a whaling ground) the command of the ship’s deck is also his; therefore the grand political maxim of the sea demands, that he should nominally live apart from the men before the mast, and be in some way distinguished as their professional superior; though always, by them, familiarly regarded as their social equal.\n\nNow, the grand distinction drawn between officer and man at sea, is this—the first lives aft, the last forward. Hence, in whale-ships and merchantmen alike, the mates have their quarters with the captain; and so, too, in most of the American whalers the harpooneers are lodged in the after part of the ship. That is to say, they take their meals in the captain’s cabin, and sleep in a place indirectly communicating with it.\n\nThough the long period of a Southern whaling voyage (by far the longest of all voyages now or ever made by man), the peculiar perils of it, and the community of interest prevailing among a company, all of whom, high or low, depend for their profits, not upon fixed wages, but upon their common luck, together with their common vigilance, intrepidity, and hard work; though all these things do in some cases tend to beget a less rigorous discipline than in merchantmen generally; yet, never mind how much like an old Mesopotamian family these whalemen may, in some primitive instances, live together; for all that, the punctilious externals, at least, of the quarter-deck are seldom materially relaxed, and in no instance done away. Indeed, many are the Nantucket ships in which you will see the skipper parading his quarter-deck with an elated grandeur not surpassed in any military navy; nay, extorting almost as much outward homage as if he wore the imperial purple, and not the shabbiest of pilot-cloth.\n\nAnd though of all men the moody captain of the Pequod was the least given to that sort of shallowest assumption; and though the only homage he ever exacted, was implicit, instantaneous obedience; though he required no man to remove the shoes from his feet ere stepping upon the quarter-deck; and though there were times when, owing to peculiar circumstances connected with events hereafter to be detailed, he addressed them in unusual terms, whether of condescension or in terrorem, or otherwise; yet even Captain Ahab was by no means unobservant of the paramount forms and usages of the sea.\n\nNor, perhaps, will it fail to be eventually perceived, that behind those forms and usages, as it were, he sometimes masked himself; incidentally making use of them for other and more private ends than they were legitimately intended to subserve. That certain sultanism of his brain, which had otherwise in a good degree remained unmanifested; through those forms that same sultanism became incarnate in an irresistible dictatorship. For be a man’s intellectual superiority what it will, it can never assume the practical, available supremacy over other men, without the aid of some sort of external arts and entrenchments, always, in themselves, more or less paltry and base. This it is, that for ever keeps God’s true princes of the Empire from the world’s hustings; and leaves the highest honors that this air can give, to those men who become famous more through their infinite inferiority to the choice hidden handful of the Divine Inert, than through their undoubted superiority over the dead level of the mass. Such large virtue lurks in these small things when extreme political superstitions invest them, that in some royal instances even to idiot imbecility they have imparted potency. But when, as in the case of Nicholas the Czar, the ringed crown of geographical empire encircles an imperial brain; then, the plebeian herds crouch abased before the tremendous centralization. Nor, will the tragic dramatist who would depict mortal indomitableness in its fullest sweep and direct swing, ever forget a hint, incidentally so important in his art, as the one now alluded to.\n\nBut Ahab, my Captain, still moves before me in all his Nantucket grimness and shagginess; and in this episode touching Emperors and Kings, I must not conceal that I have only to do with a poor old whale-hunter like him; and, therefore, all outward majestical trappings and housings are denied me. Oh, Ahab! what shall be grand in thee, it must needs be plucked at from the skies, and dived for in the deep, and featured in the unbodied air!\n\n# CHAPTER 34. The Cabin-Table.\n\nIt is noon; and Dough-Boy, the steward, thrusting his pale loaf-of-bread face from the cabin-scuttle, announces dinner to his lord and master; who, sitting in the lee quarter-boat, has just been taking an observation of the sun; and is now mutely reckoning the latitude on the smooth, medallion-shaped tablet, reserved for that daily purpose on the upper part of his ivory leg. From his complete inattention to the tidings, you would think that moody Ahab had not heard his menial. But presently, catching hold of the mizen shrouds, he swings himself to the deck, and in an even, unexhilarated voice, saying, “Dinner, Mr. Starbuck,” disappears into the cabin.\n\nWhen the last echo of his sultan’s step has died away, and Starbuck, the first Emir, has every reason to suppose that he is seated, then Starbuck rouses from his quietude, takes a few turns along the planks, and, after a grave peep into the binnacle, says, with some touch of pleasantness, “Dinner, Mr. Stubb,” and descends the scuttle. The second Emir lounges about the rigging awhile, and then slightly shaking the main brace, to see whether it will be all right with that important rope, he likewise takes up the old burden, and with a rapid “Dinner, Mr. Flask,” follows after his predecessors.\n\nBut the third Emir, now seeing himself all alone on the quarter-deck, seems to feel relieved from some curious restraint; for, tipping all sorts of knowing winks in all sorts of directions, and kicking off his shoes, he strikes into a sharp but noiseless squall of a hornpipe right over the Grand Turk’s head; and then, by a dexterous sleight, pitching his cap up into the mizentop for a shelf, he goes down rollicking so far at least as he remains visible from the deck, reversing all other processions, by bringing up the rear with music. But ere stepping into the cabin doorway below, he pauses, ships a new face altogether, and, then, independent, hilarious little Flask enters King Ahab’s presence, in the character of Abjectus, or the Slave.\n\nIt is not the least among the strange things bred by the intense artificialness of sea-usages, that while in the open air of the deck some officers will, upon provocation, bear themselves boldly and defyingly enough towards their commander; yet, ten to one, let those very officers the next moment go down to their customary dinner in that same commander’s cabin, and straightway their inoffensive, not to say deprecatory and humble air towards him, as he sits at the head of the table; this is marvellous, sometimes most comical. Wherefore this difference? A problem? Perhaps not. To have been Belshazzar, King of Babylon; and to have been Belshazzar, not haughtily but courteously, therein certainly must have been some touch of mundane grandeur. But he who in the rightly regal and intelligent spirit presides over his own private dinner-table of invited guests, that man’s unchallenged power and dominion of individual influence for the time; that man’s royalty of state transcends Belshazzar’s, for Belshazzar was not the greatest. Who has but once dined his friends, has tasted what it is to be Cæsar. It is a witchery of social czarship which there is no withstanding. Now, if to this consideration you superadd the official supremacy of a ship-master, then, by inference, you will derive the cause of that peculiarity of sea-life just mentioned.\n\nOver his ivory-inlaid table, Ahab presided like a mute, maned sea-lion on the white coral beach, surrounded by his warlike but still deferential cubs. In his own proper turn, each officer waited to be served. They were as little children before Ahab; and yet, in Ahab, there seemed not to lurk the smallest social arrogance. With one mind, their intent eyes all fastened upon the old man’s knife, as he carved the chief dish before him. I do not suppose that for the world they would have profaned that moment with the slightest observation, even upon so neutral a topic as the weather. No! And when reaching out his knife and fork, between which the slice of beef was locked, Ahab thereby motioned Starbuck’s plate towards him, the mate received his meat as though receiving alms; and cut it tenderly; and a little started if, perchance, the knife grazed against the plate; and chewed it noiselessly; and swallowed it, not without circumspection. For, like the Coronation banquet at Frankfort, where the German Emperor profoundly dines with the seven Imperial Electors, so these cabin meals were somehow solemn meals, eaten in awful silence; and yet at table old Ahab forbade not conversation; only he himself was dumb. What a relief it was to choking Stubb, when a rat made a sudden racket in the hold below. And poor little Flask, he was the youngest son, and little boy of this weary family party. His were the shinbones of the saline beef; his would have been the drumsticks. For Flask to have presumed to help himself, this must have seemed to him tantamount to larceny in the first degree. Had he helped himself at that table, doubtless, never more would he have been able to hold his head up in this honest world; nevertheless, strange to say, Ahab never forbade him. And had Flask helped himself, the chances were Ahab had never so much as noticed it. Least of all, did Flask presume to help himself to butter. Whether he thought the owners of the ship denied it to him, on account of its clotting his clear, sunny complexion; or whether he deemed that, on so long a voyage in such marketless waters, butter was at a premium, and therefore was not for him, a subaltern; however it was, Flask, alas! was a butterless man!\n\nAnother thing. Flask was the last person down at the dinner, and Flask is the first man up. Consider! For hereby Flask’s dinner was badly jammed in point of time. Starbuck and Stubb both had the start of him; and yet they also have the privilege of lounging in the rear. If Stubb even, who is but a peg higher than Flask, happens to have but a small appetite, and soon shows symptoms of concluding his repast, then Flask must bestir himself, he will not get more than three mouthfuls that day; for it is against holy usage for Stubb to precede Flask to the deck. Therefore it was that Flask once admitted in private, that ever since he had arisen to the dignity of an officer, from that moment he had never known what it was to be otherwise than hungry, more or less. For what he ate did not so much relieve his hunger, as keep it immortal in him. Peace and satisfaction, thought Flask, have for ever departed from my stomach. I am an officer; but, how I wish I could fish a bit of old-fashioned beef in the forecastle, as I used to when I was before the mast. There’s the fruits of promotion now; there’s the vanity of glory: there’s the insanity of life! Besides, if it were so that any mere sailor of the Pequod had a grudge against Flask in Flask’s official capacity, all that sailor had to do, in order to obtain ample vengeance, was to go aft at dinner-time, and get a peep at Flask through the cabin sky-light, sitting silly and dumfoundered before awful Ahab.\n\nNow, Ahab and his three mates formed what may be called the first table in the Pequod’s cabin. After their departure, taking place in inverted order to their arrival, the canvas cloth was cleared, or rather was restored to some hurried order by the pallid steward. And then the three harpooneers were bidden to the feast, they being its residuary legatees. They made a sort of temporary servants’ hall of the high and mighty cabin.\n\nIn strange contrast to the hardly tolerable constraint and nameless invisible domineerings of the captain’s table, was the entire care-free license and ease, the almost frantic democracy of those inferior fellows the harpooneers. While their masters, the mates, seemed afraid of the sound of the hinges of their own jaws, the harpooneers chewed their food with such a relish that there was a report to it. They dined like lords; they filled their bellies like Indian ships all day loading with spices. Such portentous appetites had Queequeg and Tashtego, that to fill out the vacancies made by the previous repast, often the pale Dough-Boy was fain to bring on a great baron of salt-junk, seemingly quarried out of the solid ox. And if he were not lively about it, if he did not go with a nimble hop-skip-and-jump, then Tashtego had an ungentlemanly way of accelerating him by darting a fork at his back, harpoon-wise. And once Daggoo, seized with a sudden humor, assisted Dough-Boy’s memory by snatching him up bodily, and thrusting his head into a great empty wooden trencher, while Tashtego, knife in hand, began laying out the circle preliminary to scalping him. He was naturally a very nervous, shuddering sort of little fellow, this bread-faced steward; the progeny of a bankrupt baker and a hospital nurse. And what with the standing spectacle of the black terrific Ahab, and the periodical tumultuous visitations of these three savages, Dough-Boy’s whole life was one continual lip-quiver. Commonly, after seeing the harpooneers furnished with all things they demanded, he would escape from their clutches into his little pantry adjoining, and fearfully peep out at them through the blinds of its door, till all was over.\n\nIt was a sight to see Queequeg seated over against Tashtego, opposing his filed teeth to the Indian’s: crosswise to them, Daggoo seated on the floor, for a bench would have brought his hearse-plumed head to the low carlines; at every motion of his colossal limbs, making the low cabin framework to shake, as when an African elephant goes passenger in a ship. But for all this, the great negro was wonderfully abstemious, not to say dainty. It seemed hardly possible that by such comparatively small mouthfuls he could keep up the vitality diffused through so broad, baronial, and superb a person. But, doubtless, this noble savage fed strong and drank deep of the abounding element of air; and through his dilated nostrils snuffed in the sublime life of the worlds. Not by beef or by bread, are giants made or nourished. But Queequeg, he had a mortal, barbaric smack of the lip in eating—an ugly sound enough—so much so, that the trembling Dough-Boy almost looked to see whether any marks of teeth lurked in his own lean arms. And when he would hear Tashtego singing out for him to produce himself, that his bones might be picked, the simple-witted steward all but shattered the crockery hanging round him in the pantry, by his sudden fits of the palsy. Nor did the whetstone which the harpooneers carried in their pockets, for their lances and other weapons; and with which whetstones, at dinner, they would ostentatiously sharpen their knives; that grating sound did not at all tend to tranquillize poor Dough-Boy. How could he forget that in his Island days, Queequeg, for one, must certainly have been guilty of some murderous, convivial indiscretions. Alas! Dough-Boy! hard fares the white waiter who waits upon cannibals. Not a napkin should he carry on his arm, but a buckler. In good time, though, to his great delight, the three salt-sea warriors would rise and depart; to his credulous, fable-mongering ears, all their martial bones jingling in them at every step, like Moorish scimetars in scabbards.\n\nBut, though these barbarians dined in the cabin, and nominally lived there; still, being anything but sedentary in their habits, they were scarcely ever in it except at mealtimes, and just before sleeping-time, when they passed through it to their own peculiar quarters.\n\nIn this one matter, Ahab seemed no exception to most American whale captains, who, as a set, rather incline to the opinion that by rights the ship’s cabin belongs to them; and that it is by courtesy alone that anybody else is, at any time, permitted there. So that, in real truth, the mates and harpooneers of the Pequod might more properly be said to have lived out of the cabin than in it. For when they did enter it, it was something as a street-door enters a house; turning inwards for a moment, only to be turned out the next; and, as a permanent thing, residing in the open air. Nor did they lose much hereby; in the cabin was no companionship; socially, Ahab was inaccessible. Though nominally included in the census of Christendom, he was still an alien to it. He lived in the world, as the last of the Grisly Bears lived in settled Missouri. And as when Spring and Summer had departed, that wild Logan of the woods, burying himself in the hollow of a tree, lived out the winter there, sucking his own paws; so, in his inclement, howling old age, Ahab’s soul, shut up in the caved trunk of his body, there fed upon the sullen paws of its gloom!\n\n# CHAPTER 35. The Mast-Head.\n\nIt was during the more pleasant weather, that in due rotation with the other seamen my first mast-head came round.\n\nIn most American whalemen the mast-heads are manned almost simultaneously with the vessel’s leaving her port; even though she may have fifteen thousand miles, and more, to sail ere reaching her proper cruising ground. And if, after a three, four, or five years’ voyage she is drawing nigh home with anything empty in her—say, an empty vial even—then, her mast-heads are kept manned to the last; and not till her skysail-poles sail in among the spires of the port, does she altogether relinquish the hope of capturing one whale more.\n\nNow, as the business of standing mast-heads, ashore or afloat, is a very ancient and interesting one, let us in some measure expatiate here. I take it, that the earliest standers of mast-heads were the old Egyptians; because, in all my researches, I find none prior to them. For though their progenitors, the builders of Babel, must doubtless, by their tower, have intended to rear the loftiest mast-head in all Asia, or Africa either; yet (ere the final truck was put to it) as that great stone mast of theirs may be said to have gone by the board, in the dread gale of God’s wrath; therefore, we cannot give these Babel builders priority over the Egyptians. And that the Egyptians were a nation of mast-head standers, is an assertion based upon the general belief among archæologists, that the first pyramids were founded for astronomical purposes: a theory singularly supported by the peculiar stair-like formation of all four sides of those edifices; whereby, with prodigious long upliftings of their legs, those old astronomers were wont to mount to the apex, and sing out for new stars; even as the look-outs of a modern ship sing out for a sail, or a whale just bearing in sight. In Saint Stylites, the famous Christian hermit of old times, who built him a lofty stone pillar in the desert and spent the whole latter portion of his life on its summit, hoisting his food from the ground with a tackle; in him we have a remarkable instance of a dauntless stander-of-mast-heads; who was not to be driven from his place by fogs or frosts, rain, hail, or sleet; but valiantly facing everything out to the last, literally died at his post. Of modern standers-of-mast-heads we have but a lifeless set; mere stone, iron, and bronze men; who, though well capable of facing out a stiff gale, are still entirely incompetent to the business of singing out upon discovering any strange sight. There is Napoleon; who, upon the top of the column of Vendome, stands with arms folded, some one hundred and fifty feet in the air; careless, now, who rules the decks below; whether Louis Philippe, Louis Blanc, or Louis the Devil. Great Washington, too, stands high aloft on his towering main-mast in Baltimore, and like one of Hercules’ pillars, his column marks that point of human grandeur beyond which few mortals will go. Admiral Nelson, also, on a capstan of gun-metal, stands his mast-head in Trafalgar Square; and ever when most obscured by that London smoke, token is yet given that a hidden hero is there; for where there is smoke, must be fire. But neither great Washington, nor Napoleon, nor Nelson, will answer a single hail from below, however madly invoked to befriend by their counsels the distracted decks upon which they gaze; however it may be surmised, that their spirits penetrate through the thick haze of the future, and descry what shoals and what rocks must be shunned.\n\nIt may seem unwarrantable to couple in any respect the mast-head standers of the land with those of the sea; but that in truth it is not so, is plainly evinced by an item for which Obed Macy, the sole historian of Nantucket, stands accountable. The worthy Obed tells us, that in the early times of the whale fishery, ere ships were regularly launched in pursuit of the game, the people of that island erected lofty spars along the sea-coast, to which the look-outs ascended by means of nailed cleats, something as fowls go upstairs in a hen-house. A few years ago this same plan was adopted by the Bay whalemen of New Zealand, who, upon descrying the game, gave notice to the ready-manned boats nigh the beach. But this custom has now become obsolete; turn we then to the one proper mast-head, that of a whale-ship at sea. The three mast-heads are kept manned from sun-rise to sun-set; the seamen taking their regular turns (as at the helm), and relieving each other every two hours. In the serene weather of the tropics it is exceedingly pleasant the mast-head; nay, to a dreamy meditative man it is delightful. There you stand, a hundred feet above the silent decks, striding along the deep, as if the masts were gigantic stilts, while beneath you and between your legs, as it were, swim the hugest monsters of the sea, even as ships once sailed between the boots of the famous Colossus at old Rhodes. There you stand, lost in the infinite series of the sea, with nothing ruffled but the waves. The tranced ship indolently rolls; the drowsy trade winds blow; everything resolves you into languor. For the most part, in this tropic whaling life, a sublime uneventfulness invests you; you hear no news; read no gazettes; extras with startling accounts of commonplaces never delude you into unnecessary excitements; you hear of no domestic afflictions; bankrupt securities; fall of stocks; are never troubled with the thought of what you shall have for dinner—for all your meals for three years and more are snugly stowed in casks, and your bill of fare is immutable.\n\nIn one of those southern whalesmen, on a long three or four years’ voyage, as often happens, the sum of the various hours you spend at the mast-head would amount to several entire months. And it is much to be deplored that the place to which you devote so considerable a portion of the whole term of your natural life, should be so sadly destitute of anything approaching to a cosy inhabitiveness, or adapted to breed a comfortable localness of feeling, such as pertains to a bed, a hammock, a hearse, a sentry box, a pulpit, a coach, or any other of those small and snug contrivances in which men temporarily isolate themselves. Your most usual point of perch is the head of the t’ gallant-mast, where you stand upon two thin parallel sticks (almost peculiar to whalemen) called the t’ gallant cross-trees. Here, tossed about by the sea, the beginner feels about as cosy as he would standing on a bull’s horns. To be sure, in cold weather you may carry your house aloft with you, in the shape of a watch-coat; but properly speaking the thickest watch-coat is no more of a house than the unclad body; for as the soul is glued inside of its fleshy tabernacle, and cannot freely move about in it, nor even move out of it, without running great risk of perishing (like an ignorant pilgrim crossing the snowy Alps in winter); so a watch-coat is not so much of a house as it is a mere envelope, or additional skin encasing you. You cannot put a shelf or chest of drawers in your body, and no more can you make a convenient closet of your watch-coat.\n\nConcerning all this, it is much to be deplored that the mast-heads of a southern whale ship are unprovided with those enviable little tents or pulpits, called crow’s-nests, in which the look-outs of a Greenland whaler are protected from the inclement weather of the frozen seas. In the fireside narrative of Captain Sleet, entitled “A Voyage among the Icebergs, in quest of the Greenland Whale, and incidentally for the re-discovery of the Lost Icelandic Colonies of Old Greenland;” in this admirable volume, all standers of mast-heads are furnished with a charmingly circumstantial account of the then recently invented crow’s-nest of the Glacier, which was the name of Captain Sleet’s good craft. He called it the Sleet’s crow’s-nest, in honor of himself; he being the original inventor and patentee, and free from all ridiculous false delicacy, and holding that if we call our own children after our own names (we fathers being the original inventors and patentees), so likewise should we denominate after ourselves any other apparatus we may beget. In shape, the Sleet’s crow’s-nest is something like a large tierce or pipe; it is open above, however, where it is furnished with a movable side-screen to keep to windward of your head in a hard gale. Being fixed on the summit of the mast, you ascend into it through a little trap-hatch in the bottom. On the after side, or side next the stern of the ship, is a comfortable seat, with a locker underneath for umbrellas, comforters, and coats. In front is a leather rack, in which to keep your speaking trumpet, pipe, telescope, and other nautical conveniences. When Captain Sleet in person stood his mast-head in this crow’s-nest of his, he tells us that he always had a rifle with him (also fixed in the rack), together with a powder flask and shot, for the purpose of popping off the stray narwhales, or vagrant sea unicorns infesting those waters; for you cannot successfully shoot at them from the deck owing to the resistance of the water, but to shoot down upon them is a very different thing. Now, it was plainly a labor of love for Captain Sleet to describe, as he does, all the little detailed conveniences of his crow’s-nest; but though he so enlarges upon many of these, and though he treats us to a very scientific account of his experiments in this crow’s-nest, with a small compass he kept there for the purpose of counteracting the errors resulting from what is called the “local attraction” of all binnacle magnets; an error ascribable to the horizontal vicinity of the iron in the ship’s planks, and in the Glacier’s case, perhaps, to there having been so many broken-down blacksmiths among her crew; I say, that though the Captain is very discreet and scientific here, yet, for all his learned “binnacle deviations,” “azimuth compass observations,” and “approximate errors,” he knows very well, Captain Sleet, that he was not so much immersed in those profound magnetic meditations, as to fail being attracted occasionally towards that well replenished little case-bottle, so nicely tucked in on one side of his crow’s nest, within easy reach of his hand. Though, upon the whole, I greatly admire and even love the brave, the honest, and learned Captain; yet I take it very ill of him that he should so utterly ignore that case-bottle, seeing what a faithful friend and comforter it must have been, while with mittened fingers and hooded head he was studying the mathematics aloft there in that bird’s nest within three or four perches of the pole.\n\nBut if we Southern whale-fishers are not so snugly housed aloft as Captain Sleet and his Greenlandmen were; yet that disadvantage is greatly counter-balanced by the widely contrasting serenity of those seductive seas in which we South fishers mostly float. For one, I used to lounge up the rigging very leisurely, resting in the top to have a chat with Queequeg, or any one else off duty whom I might find there; then ascending a little way further, and throwing a lazy leg over the top-sail yard, take a preliminary view of the watery pastures, and so at last mount to my ultimate destination.\n\nLet me make a clean breast of it here, and frankly admit that I kept but sorry guard. With the problem of the universe revolving in me, how could I—being left completely to myself at such a thought-engendering altitude—how could I but lightly hold my obligations to observe all whale-ships’ standing orders, “Keep your weather eye open, and sing out every time.”\n\nAnd let me in this place movingly admonish you, ye ship-owners of Nantucket! Beware of enlisting in your vigilant fisheries any lad with lean brow and hollow eye; given to unseasonable meditativeness; and who offers to ship with the Phædon instead of Bowditch in his head. Beware of such an one, I say; your whales must be seen before they can be killed; and this sunken-eyed young Platonist will tow you ten wakes round the world, and never make you one pint of sperm the richer. Nor are these monitions at all unneeded. For nowadays, the whale-fishery furnishes an asylum for many romantic, melancholy, and absent-minded young men, disgusted with the carking cares of earth, and seeking sentiment in tar and blubber. Childe Harold not unfrequently perches himself upon the mast-head of some luckless disappointed whale-ship, and in moody phrase ejaculates:—\n\n“Roll on, thou deep and dark blue ocean, roll! Ten thousand blubber-hunters sweep over thee in vain.”\n\nVery often do the captains of such ships take those absent-minded young philosophers to task, upbraiding them with not feeling sufficient “interest” in the voyage; half-hinting that they are so hopelessly lost to all honorable ambition, as that in their secret souls they would rather not see whales than otherwise. But all in vain; those young Platonists have a notion that their vision is imperfect; they are short-sighted; what use, then, to strain the visual nerve? They have left their opera-glasses at home.\n\n“Why, thou monkey,” said a harpooneer to one of these lads, “we’ve been cruising now hard upon three years, and thou hast not raised a whale yet. Whales are scarce as hen’s teeth whenever thou art up here.” Perhaps they were; or perhaps there might have been shoals of them in the far horizon; but lulled into such an opium-like listlessness of vacant, unconscious reverie is this absent-minded youth by the blending cadence of waves with thoughts, that at last he loses his identity; takes the mystic ocean at his feet for the visible image of that deep, blue, bottomless soul, pervading mankind and nature; and every strange, half-seen, gliding, beautiful thing that eludes him; every dimly-discovered, uprising fin of some undiscernible form, seems to him the embodiment of those elusive thoughts that only people the soul by continually flitting through it. In this enchanted mood, thy spirit ebbs away to whence it came; becomes diffused through time and space; like Cranmer’s sprinkled Pantheistic ashes, forming at last a part of every shore the round globe over.\n\nThere is no life in thee, now, except that rocking life imparted by a gently rolling ship; by her, borrowed from the sea; by the sea, from the inscrutable tides of God. But while this sleep, this dream is on ye, move your foot or hand an inch; slip your hold at all; and your identity comes back in horror. Over Descartian vortices you hover. And perhaps, at mid-day, in the fairest weather, with one half-throttled shriek you drop through that transparent air into the summer sea, no more to rise for ever. Heed it well, ye Pantheists!\n\n(Enter Ahab: Then, all.)\n\nIt was not a great while after the affair of the pipe, that one morning shortly after breakfast, Ahab, as was his wont, ascended the cabin-gangway to the deck. There most sea-captains usually walk at that hour, as country gentlemen, after the same meal, take a few turns in the garden.\n\nSoon his steady, ivory stride was heard, as to and fro he paced his old rounds, upon planks so familiar to his tread, that they were all over dented, like geological stones, with the peculiar mark of his walk. Did you fixedly gaze, too, upon that ribbed and dented brow; there also, you would see still stranger foot-prints—the foot-prints of his one unsleeping, ever-pacing thought.\n\nBut on the occasion in question, those dents looked deeper, even as his nervous step that morning left a deeper mark. And, so full of his thought was Ahab, that at every uniform turn that he made, now at the main-mast and now at the binnacle, you could almost see that thought turn in him as he turned, and pace in him as he paced; so completely possessing him, indeed, that it all but seemed the inward mould of every outer movement.\n\n“D’ye mark him, Flask?” whispered Stubb; “the chick that’s in him pecks the shell. ’Twill soon be out.”\n\nThe hours wore on;—Ahab now shut up within his cabin; anon, pacing the deck, with the same intense bigotry of purpose in his aspect.\n\nIt drew near the close of day. Suddenly he came to a halt by the bulwarks, and inserting his bone leg into the auger-hole there, and with one hand grasping a shroud, he ordered Starbuck to send everybody aft.\n\n“Sir!” said the mate, astonished at an order seldom or never given on ship-board except in some extraordinary case.\n\n“Send everybody aft,” repeated Ahab. “Mast-heads, there! come down!”\n\nWhen the entire ship’s company were assembled, and with curious and not wholly unapprehensive faces, were eyeing him, for he looked not unlike the weather horizon when a storm is coming up, Ahab, after rapidly glancing over the bulwarks, and then darting his eyes among the crew, started from his standpoint; and as though not a soul were nigh him resumed his heavy turns upon the deck. With bent head and half-slouched hat he continued to pace, unmindful of the wondering whispering among the men; till Stubb cautiously whispered to Flask, that Ahab must have summoned them there for the purpose of witnessing a pedestrian feat. But this did not last long. Vehemently pausing, he cried:—\n\n“What do ye do when ye see a whale, men?”\n\n“Sing out for him!” was the impulsive rejoinder from a score of clubbed voices.\n\n“Good!” cried Ahab, with a wild approval in his tones; observing the hearty animation into which his unexpected question had so magnetically thrown them.\n\n“And what do ye next, men?”\n\n“Lower away, and after him!”\n\n“And what tune is it ye pull to, men?”\n\n“A dead whale or a stove boat!”\n\nMore and more strangely and fiercely glad and approving, grew the countenance of the old man at every shout; while the mariners began to gaze curiously at each other, as if marvelling how it was that they themselves became so excited at such seemingly purposeless questions.\n\nBut, they were all eagerness again, as Ahab, now half-revolving in his pivot-hole, with one hand reaching high up a shroud, and tightly, almost convulsively grasping it, addressed them thus:—\n\n“All ye mast-headers have before now heard me give orders about a white whale. Look ye! d’ye see this Spanish ounce of gold?”—holding up a broad bright coin to the sun—“it is a sixteen dollar piece, men. D’ye see it? Mr. Starbuck, hand me yon top-maul.”\n\nWhile the mate was getting the hammer, Ahab, without speaking, was slowly rubbing the gold piece against the skirts of his jacket, as if to heighten its lustre, and without using any words was meanwhile lowly humming to himself, producing a sound so strangely muffled and inarticulate that it seemed the mechanical humming of the wheels of his vitality in him.\n\nReceiving the top-maul from Starbuck, he advanced towards the main-mast with the hammer uplifted in one hand, exhibiting the gold with the other, and with a high raised voice exclaiming: “Whosoever of ye raises me a white-headed whale with a wrinkled brow and a crooked jaw; whosoever of ye raises me that white-headed whale, with three holes punctured in his starboard fluke—look ye, whosoever of ye raises me that same white whale, he shall have this gold ounce, my boys!”\n\n“Huzza! huzza!” cried the seamen, as with swinging tarpaulins they hailed the act of nailing the gold to the mast.\n\n“It’s a white whale, I say,” resumed Ahab, as he threw down the topmaul: “a white whale. Skin your eyes for him, men; look sharp for white water; if ye see but a bubble, sing out.”\n\nAll this while Tashtego, Daggoo, and Queequeg had looked on with even more intense interest and surprise than the rest, and at the mention of the wrinkled brow and crooked jaw they had started as if each was separately touched by some specific recollection.\n\n“Captain Ahab,” said Tashtego, “that white whale must be the same that some call Moby Dick.”\n\n“Moby Dick?” shouted Ahab. “Do ye know the white whale then, Tash?”\n\n“Does he fan-tail a little curious, sir, before he goes down?” said the Gay-Header deliberately.\n\n“And has he a curious spout, too,” said Daggoo, “very bushy, even for a parmacetty, and mighty quick, Captain Ahab?”\n\n“And he have one, two, three—oh! good many iron in him hide, too, Captain,” cried Queequeg disjointedly, “all twiske-tee be-twisk, like him—him—” faltering hard for a word, and screwing his hand round and round as though uncorking a bottle—“like him—him—”\n\n“Corkscrew!” cried Ahab, “aye, Queequeg, the harpoons lie all twisted and wrenched in him; aye, Daggoo, his spout is a big one, like a whole shock of wheat, and white as a pile of our Nantucket wool after the great annual sheep-shearing; aye, Tashtego, and he fan-tails like a split jib in a squall. Death and devils! men, it is Moby Dick ye have seen—Moby Dick—Moby Dick!”\n\n“Captain Ahab,” said Starbuck, who, with Stubb and Flask, had thus far been eyeing his superior with increasing surprise, but at last seemed struck with a thought which somewhat explained all the wonder. “Captain Ahab, I have heard of Moby Dick—but it was not Moby Dick that took off thy leg?”\n\n“Who told thee that?” cried Ahab; then pausing, “Aye, Starbuck; aye, my hearties all round; it was Moby Dick that dismasted me; Moby Dick that brought me to this dead stump I stand on now. Aye, aye,” he shouted with a terrific, loud, animal sob, like that of a heart-stricken moose; “Aye, aye! it was that accursed white whale that razeed me; made a poor pegging lubber of me for ever and a day!” Then tossing both arms, with measureless imprecations he shouted out: “Aye, aye! and I’ll chase him round Good Hope, and round the Horn, and round the Norway Maelstrom, and round perdition’s flames before I give him up. And this is what ye have shipped for, men! to chase that white whale on both sides of land, and over all sides of earth, till he spouts black blood and rolls fin out. What say ye, men, will ye splice hands on it, now? I think ye do look brave.”\n\n“Aye, aye!” shouted the harpooneers and seamen, running closer to the excited old man: “A sharp eye for the white whale; a sharp lance for Moby Dick!”\n\n“God bless ye,” he seemed to half sob and half shout. “God bless ye, men. Steward! go draw the great measure of grog. But what’s this long face about, Mr. Starbuck; wilt thou not chase the white whale? art not game for Moby Dick?”\n\n“I am game for his crooked jaw, and for the jaws of Death too, Captain Ahab, if it fairly comes in the way of the business we follow; but I came here to hunt whales, not my commander’s vengeance. How many barrels will thy vengeance yield thee even if thou gettest it, Captain Ahab? it will not fetch thee much in our Nantucket market.”\n\n“Nantucket market! Hoot! But come closer, Starbuck; thou requirest a little lower layer. If money’s to be the measurer, man, and the accountants have computed their great counting-house the globe, by girdling it with guineas, one to every three parts of an inch; then, let me tell thee, that my vengeance will fetch a great premium here!”\n\n“He smites his chest,” whispered Stubb, “what’s that for? methinks it rings most vast, but hollow.”\n\n“Vengeance on a dumb brute!” cried Starbuck, “that simply smote thee from blindest instinct! Madness! To be enraged with a dumb thing, Captain Ahab, seems blasphemous.”\n\n“Hark ye yet again—the little lower layer. All visible objects, man, are but as pasteboard masks. But in each event—in the living act, the undoubted deed—there, some unknown but still reasoning thing puts forth the mouldings of its features from behind the unreasoning mask. If man will strike, strike through the mask! How can the prisoner reach outside except by thrusting through the wall? To me, the white whale is that wall, shoved near to me. Sometimes I think there’s naught beyond. But ’tis enough. He tasks me; he heaps me; I see in him outrageous strength, with an inscrutable malice sinewing it. That inscrutable thing is chiefly what I hate; and be the white whale agent, or be the white whale principal, I will wreak that hate upon him. Talk not to me of blasphemy, man; I’d strike the sun if it insulted me. For could the sun do that, then could I do the other; since there is ever a sort of fair play herein, jealousy presiding over all creations. But not my master, man, is even that fair play. Who’s over me? Truth hath no confines. Take off thine eye! more intolerable than fiends’ glarings is a doltish stare! So, so; thou reddenest and palest; my heat has melted thee to anger-glow. But look ye, Starbuck, what is said in heat, that thing unsays itself. There are men from whom warm words are small indignity. I meant not to incense thee. Let it go. Look! see yonder Turkish cheeks of spotted tawn—living, breathing pictures painted by the sun. The Pagan leopards—the unrecking and unworshipping things, that live; and seek, and give no reasons for the torrid life they feel! The crew, man, the crew! Are they not one and all with Ahab, in this matter of the whale? See Stubb! he laughs! See yonder Chilian! he snorts to think of it. Stand up amid the general hurricane, thy one tost sapling cannot, Starbuck! And what is it? Reckon it. ’Tis but to help strike a fin; no wondrous feat for Starbuck. What is it more? From this one poor hunt, then, the best lance out of all Nantucket, surely he will not hang back, when every foremast-hand has clutched a whetstone? Ah! constrainings seize thee; I see! the billow lifts thee! Speak, but speak!—Aye, aye! thy silence, then, that voices thee. (Aside) Something shot from my dilated nostrils, he has inhaled it in his lungs. Starbuck now is mine; cannot oppose me now, without rebellion.”\n\n“God keep me!—keep us all!” murmured Starbuck, lowly.\n\nBut in his joy at the enchanted, tacit acquiescence of the mate, Ahab did not hear his foreboding invocation; nor yet the low laugh from the hold; nor yet the presaging vibrations of the winds in the cordage; nor yet the hollow flap of the sails against the masts, as for a moment their hearts sank in. For again Starbuck’s downcast eyes lighted up with the stubbornness of life; the subterranean laugh died away; the winds blew on; the sails filled out; the ship heaved and rolled as before. Ah, ye admonitions and warnings! why stay ye not when ye come? But rather are ye predictions than warnings, ye shadows! Yet not so much predictions from without, as verifications of the foregoing things within. For with little external to constrain us, the innermost necessities in our being, these still drive us on.\n\n“The measure! the measure!” cried Ahab.\n\nReceiving the brimming pewter, and turning to the harpooneers, he ordered them to produce their weapons. Then ranging them before him near the capstan, with their harpoons in their hands, while his three mates stood at his side with their lances, and the rest of the ship’s company formed a circle round the group; he stood for an instant searchingly eyeing every man of his crew. But those wild eyes met his, as the bloodshot eyes of the prairie wolves meet the eye of their leader, ere he rushes on at their head in the trail of the bison; but, alas! only to fall into the hidden snare of the Indian.\n\n“Drink and pass!” he cried, handing the heavy charged flagon to the nearest seaman. “The crew alone now drink. Round with it, round! Short draughts—long swallows, men; ’tis hot as Satan’s hoof. So, so; it goes round excellently. It spiralizes in ye; forks out at the serpent-snapping eye. Well done; almost drained. That way it went, this way it comes. Hand it me—here’s a hollow! Men, ye seem the years; so brimming life is gulped and gone. Steward, refill!\n\n“Attend now, my braves. I have mustered ye all round this capstan; and ye mates, flank me with your lances; and ye harpooneers, stand there with your irons; and ye, stout mariners, ring me in, that I may in some sort revive a noble custom of my fisherman fathers before me. O men, you will yet see that—Ha! boy, come back? bad pennies come not sooner. Hand it me. Why, now, this pewter had run brimming again, wer’t not thou St. Vitus’ imp—away, thou ague!\n\n“Advance, ye mates! Cross your lances full before me. Well done! Let me touch the axis.” So saying, with extended arm, he grasped the three level, radiating lances at their crossed centre; while so doing, suddenly and nervously twitched them; meanwhile, glancing intently from Starbuck to Stubb; from Stubb to Flask. It seemed as though, by some nameless, interior volition, he would fain have shocked into them the same fiery emotion accumulated within the Leyden jar of his own magnetic life. The three mates quailed before his strong, sustained, and mystic aspect. Stubb and Flask looked sideways from him; the honest eye of Starbuck fell downright.\n\n“In vain!” cried Ahab; “but, maybe, ’tis well. For did ye three but once take the full-forced shock, then mine own electric thing, that had perhaps expired from out me. Perchance, too, it would have dropped ye dead. Perchance ye need it not. Down lances! And now, ye mates, I do appoint ye three cupbearers to my three pagan kinsmen there—yon three most honorable gentlemen and noblemen, my valiant harpooneers. Disdain the task? What, when the great Pope washes the feet of beggars, using his tiara for ewer? Oh, my sweet cardinals! your own condescension, that shall bend ye to it. I do not order ye; ye will it. Cut your seizings and draw the poles, ye harpooneers!”\n\nSilently obeying the order, the three harpooneers now stood with the detached iron part of their harpoons, some three feet long, held, barbs up, before him.\n\n“Stab me not with that keen steel! Cant them; cant them over! know ye not the goblet end? Turn up the socket! So, so; now, ye cup-bearers, advance. The irons! take them; hold them while I fill!” Forthwith, slowly going from one officer to the other, he brimmed the harpoon sockets with the fiery waters from the pewter.\n\n“Now, three to three, ye stand. Commend the murderous chalices! Bestow them, ye who are now made parties to this indissoluble league. Ha! Starbuck! but the deed is done! Yon ratifying sun now waits to sit upon it. Drink, ye harpooneers! drink and swear, ye men that man the deathful whaleboat’s bow—Death to Moby Dick! God hunt us all, if we do not hunt Moby Dick to his death!” The long, barbed steel goblets were lifted; and to cries and maledictions against the white whale, the spirits were simultaneously quaffed down with a hiss. Starbuck paled, and turned, and shivered. Once more, and finally, the replenished pewter went the rounds among the frantic crew; when, waving his free hand to them, they all dispersed; and Ahab retired within his cabin.\n\n# CHAPTER 37. Sunset.\n\nThe cabin; by the stern windows; Ahab sitting alone, and gazing out.\n\nI leave a white and turbid wake; pale waters, paler cheeks, where’er I sail. The envious billows sidelong swell to whelm my track; let them; but first I pass.\n\nYonder, by ever-brimming goblet’s rim, the warm waves blush like wine. The gold brow plumbs the blue. The diver sun—slow dived from noon—goes down; my soul mounts up! she wearies with her endless hill. Is, then, the crown too heavy that I wear? this Iron Crown of Lombardy. Yet is it bright with many a gem; I the wearer, see not its far flashings; but darkly feel that I wear that, that dazzlingly confounds. ’Tis iron—that I know—not gold. ’Tis split, too—that I feel; the jagged edge galls me so, my brain seems to beat against the solid metal; aye, steel skull, mine; the sort that needs no helmet in the most brain-battering fight!\n\nDry heat upon my brow? Oh! time was, when as the sunrise nobly spurred me, so the sunset soothed. No more. This lovely light, it lights not me; all loveliness is anguish to me, since I can ne’er enjoy. Gifted with the high perception, I lack the low, enjoying power; damned, most subtly and most malignantly! damned in the midst of Paradise! Good night—good night! (waving his hand, he moves from the window.)\n\n’Twas not so hard a task. I thought to find one stubborn, at the least; but my one cogged circle fits into all their various wheels, and they revolve. Or, if you will, like so many ant-hills of powder, they all stand before me; and I their match. Oh, hard! that to fire others, the match itself must needs be wasting! What I’ve dared, I’ve willed; and what I’ve willed, I’ll do! They think me mad—Starbuck does; but I’m demoniac, I am madness maddened! That wild madness that’s only calm to comprehend itself! The prophecy was that I should be dismembered; and—Aye! I lost this leg. I now prophesy that I will dismember my dismemberer. Now, then, be the prophet and the fulfiller one. That’s more than ye, ye great gods, ever were. I laugh and hoot at ye, ye cricket-players, ye pugilists, ye deaf Burkes and blinded Bendigoes! I will not say as schoolboys do to bullies—Take some one of your own size; don’t pommel me! No, ye’ve knocked me down, and I am up again; but ye have run and hidden. Come forth from behind your cotton bags! I have no long gun to reach ye. Come, Ahab’s compliments to ye; come and see if ye can swerve me. Swerve me? ye cannot swerve me, else ye swerve yourselves! man has ye there. Swerve me? The path to my fixed purpose is laid with iron rails, whereon my soul is grooved to run. Over unsounded gorges, through the rifled hearts of mountains, under torrents’ beds, unerringly I rush! Naught’s an obstacle, naught’s an angle to the iron way!\n\nBy the Mainmast; Starbuck leaning against it.\nMy soul is more than matched; she’s overmanned; and by a madman! Insufferable sting, that sanity should ground arms on such a field! But he drilled deep down, and blasted all my reason out of me! I think I see his impious end; but feel that I must help him to it. Will I, nill I, the ineffable thing has tied me to him; tows me with a cable I have no knife to cut. Horrible old man! Who’s over him, he cries;—aye, he would be a democrat to all above; look, how he lords it over all below! Oh! I plainly see my miserable office,—to obey, rebelling; and worse yet, to hate with touch of pity! For in his eyes I read some lurid woe would shrivel me up, had I it. Yet is there hope. Time and tide flow wide. The hated whale has the round watery world to swim in, as the small gold-fish has its glassy globe. His heaven-insulting purpose, God may wedge aside. I would up heart, were it not like lead. But my whole clock’s run down; my heart the all-controlling weight, I have no key to lift again.\n\n[A burst of revelry from the forecastle.]\n\nOh, God! to sail with such a heathen crew that have small touch of human mothers in them! Whelped somewhere by the sharkish sea. The white whale is their demigorgon. Hark! the infernal orgies! that revelry is forward! mark the unfaltering silence aft! Methinks it pictures life. Foremost through the sparkling sea shoots on the gay, embattled, bantering bow, but only to drag dark Ahab after it, where he broods within his sternward cabin, builded over the dead water of the wake, and further on, hunted by its wolfish gurglings. The long howl thrills me through! Peace! ye revellers, and set the watch! Oh, life! ’tis in an hour like this, with soul beat down and held to knowledge,—as wild, untutored things are forced to feed—Oh, life! ’tis now that I do feel the latent horror in thee! but ’tis not me! that horror’s out of me! and with the soft feeling of the human in me, yet will I try to fight ye, ye grim, phantom futures! Stand by me, hold me, bind me, O ye blessed influences!\n\n(Stubb solus, and mending a brace.)\nHa! ha! ha! ha! hem! clear my throat!—I’ve been thinking over it ever since, and that ha, ha’s the final consequence. Why so? Because a laugh’s the wisest, easiest answer to all that’s queer; and come what will, one comfort’s always left—that unfailing comfort is, it’s all predestinated. I heard not all his talk with Starbuck; but to my poor eye Starbuck then looked something as I the other evening felt. Be sure the old Mogul has fixed him, too. I twigged it, knew it; had had the gift, might readily have prophesied it—for when I clapped my eye upon his skull I saw it. Well, Stubb, wise Stubb—that’s my title—well, Stubb, what of it, Stubb? Here’s a carcase. I know not all that may be coming, but be it what it will, I’ll go to it laughing. Such a waggish leering as lurks in all your horribles! I feel funny. Fa, la! lirra, skirra! What’s my juicy little pear at home doing now? Crying its eyes out?—Giving a party to the last arrived harpooneers, I dare say, gay as a frigate’s pennant, and so am I—fa, la! lirra, skirra! Oh—\n\nWe’ll drink to-night with hearts as light, To love, as gay and fleeting As bubbles that swim, on the beaker’s brim, And break on the lips while meeting.\n\nA brave stave that—who calls? Mr. Starbuck? Aye, aye, sir—(Aside) he’s my superior, he has his too, if I’m not mistaken.—Aye, aye, sir, just through with this job—coming.\n\n# HARPOONEERS AND SAILORS.\n\n(Foresail rises and discovers the watch standing, lounging, leaning, and lying in various attitudes, all singing in chorus.)\n\nFarewell and adieu to you, Spanish ladies! Farewell and adieu to you, ladies of Spain! Our captain’s commanded.—\n\n1ST NANTUCKET SAILOR. Oh, boys, don’t be sentimental; it’s bad for the digestion! Take a tonic, follow me!\n\n(Sings, and all follow.)\n\nOur captain stood upon the deck, A spy-glass in his hand, A viewing of those gallant whales That blew at every strand. Oh, your tubs in your boats, my boys, And by your braces stand, And we’ll have one of those fine whales, Hand, boys, over hand! So, be cheery, my lads! may your hearts never fail! While the bold harpooner is striking the whale!\n\nMATE’S VOICE FROM THE QUARTER-DECK. Eight bells there, forward!\n\n2ND NANTUCKET SAILOR. Avast the chorus! Eight bells there! d’ye hear, bell-boy? Strike the bell eight, thou Pip! thou blackling! and let me call the watch. I’ve the sort of mouth for that—the hogshead mouth. So, so, (thrusts his head down the scuttle,) Star-bo-l-e-e-n-s, a-h-o-y! Eight bells there below! Tumble up!\n\nDUTCH SAILOR. Grand snoozing to-night, maty; fat night for that. I mark this in our old Mogul’s wine; it’s quite as deadening to some as filliping to others. We sing; they sleep—aye, lie down there, like ground-tier butts. At ’em again! There, take this copper-pump, and hail ’em through it. Tell ’em to avast dreaming of their lasses. Tell ’em it’s the resurrection; they must kiss their last, and come to judgment. That’s the way—that’s it; thy throat ain’t spoiled with eating Amsterdam butter.\n\nFRENCH SAILOR. Hist, boys! let’s have a jig or two before we ride to anchor in Blanket Bay. What say ye? There comes the other watch. Stand by all legs! Pip! little Pip! hurrah with your tambourine!\n\nPIP. (Sulky and sleepy.) Don’t know where it is.\n\nFRENCH SAILOR. Beat thy belly, then, and wag thy ears. Jig it, men, I say; merry’s the word; hurrah! Damn me, won’t you dance? Form, now, Indian-file, and gallop into the double-shuffle? Throw yourselves! Legs! legs!\n\nICELAND SAILOR. I don’t like your floor, maty; it’s too springy to my taste. I’m used to ice-floors. I’m sorry to throw cold water on the subject; but excuse me.\n\nMALTESE SAILOR. Me too; where’s your girls? Who but a fool would take his left hand by his right, and say to himself, how d’ye do? Partners! I must have partners!\n\nSICILIAN SAILOR. Aye; girls and a green!—then I’ll hop with ye; yea, turn grasshopper!\n\nLONG-ISLAND SAILOR. Well, well, ye sulkies, there’s plenty more of us. Hoe corn when you may, say I. All legs go to harvest soon. Ah! here comes the music; now for it!\n\nAZORE SAILOR. (Ascending, and pitching the tambourine up the scuttle.) Here you are, Pip; and there’s the windlass-bitts; up you mount! Now, boys! (The half of them dance to the tambourine; some go below; some sleep or lie among the coils of rigging. Oaths a-plenty.)\n\nAZORE SAILOR. (Dancing) Go it, Pip! Bang it, bell-boy! Rig it, dig it, stig it, quig it, bell-boy! Make fire-flies; break the jinglers!\n\nPIP. Jinglers, you say?—there goes another, dropped off; I pound it so.\n\nCHINA SAILOR. Rattle thy teeth, then, and pound away; make a pagoda of thyself.\n\nFRENCH SAILOR. Merry-mad! Hold up thy hoop, Pip, till I jump through it! Split jibs! tear yourselves!\n\nTASHTEGO. (Quietly smoking.) That’s a white man; he calls that fun: humph! I save my sweat.\n\nOLD MANX SAILOR. I wonder whether those jolly lads bethink them of what they are dancing over. I’ll dance over your grave, I will—that’s the bitterest threat of your night-women, that beat head-winds round corners. O Christ! to think of the green navies and the green-skulled crews! Well, well; belike the whole world’s a ball, as you scholars have it; and so ’tis right to make one ballroom of it. Dance on, lads, you’re young; I was once.\n\n3D NANTUCKET SAILOR. Spell oh!—whew! this is worse than pulling after whales in a calm—give us a whiff, Tash.\n\n(They cease dancing, and gather in clusters. Meantime the sky darkens—the wind rises.)\n\nLASCAR SAILOR. By Brahma! boys, it’ll be douse sail soon. The sky-born, high-tide Ganges turned to wind! Thou showest thy black brow, Seeva!\n\nMALTESE SAILOR. (Reclining and shaking his cap.) It’s the waves—the snow’s caps turn to jig it now. They’ll shake their tassels soon. Now would all the waves were women, then I’d go drown, and chassee with them evermore! There’s naught so sweet on earth—heaven may not match it!—as those swift glances of warm, wild bosoms in the dance, when the over-arboring arms hide such ripe, bursting grapes.\n\nSICILIAN SAILOR. (Reclining.) Tell me not of it! Hark ye, lad—fleet interlacings of the limbs—lithe swayings—coyings—flutterings! lip! heart! hip! all graze: unceasing touch and go! not taste, observe ye, else come satiety. Eh, Pagan? (Nudging.)\n\nTAHITAN SAILOR. (Reclining on a mat.) Hail, holy nakedness of our dancing girls!—the Heeva-Heeva! Ah! low veiled, high palmed Tahiti! I still rest me on thy mat, but the soft soil has slid! I saw thee woven in the wood, my mat! green the first day I brought ye thence; now worn and wilted quite. Ah me!—not thou nor I can bear the change! How then, if so be transplanted to yon sky? Hear I the roaring streams from Pirohitee’s peak of spears, when they leap down the crags and drown the villages?—The blast! the blast! Up, spine, and meet it! (Leaps to his feet.)\n\nPORTUGUESE SAILOR. How the sea rolls swashing ’gainst the side! Stand by for reefing, hearties! the winds are just crossing swords, pell-mell they’ll go lunging presently.\n\nDANISH SAILOR. Crack, crack, old ship! so long as thou crackest, thou holdest! Well done! The mate there holds ye to it stiffly. He’s no more afraid than the isle fort at Cattegat, put there to fight the Baltic with storm-lashed guns, on which the sea-salt cakes!\n\n4TH NANTUCKET SAILOR. He has his orders, mind ye that. I heard old Ahab tell him he must always kill a squall, something as they burst a waterspout with a pistol—fire your ship right into it!\n\nENGLISH SAILOR. Blood! but that old man’s a grand old cove! We are the lads to hunt him up his whale!\n\nALL. Aye! aye!\n\nOLD MANX SAILOR. How the three pines shake! Pines are the hardest sort of tree to live when shifted to any other soil, and here there’s none but the crew’s cursed clay. Steady, helmsman! steady. This is the sort of weather when brave hearts snap ashore, and keeled hulls split at sea. Our captain has his birthmark; look yonder, boys, there’s another in the sky—lurid-like, ye see, all else pitch black.\n\nDAGGOO. What of that? Who’s afraid of black’s afraid of me! I’m quarried out of it!\n\nSPANISH SAILOR. (Aside.) He wants to bully, ah!—the old grudge makes me touchy (Advancing.) Aye, harpooneer, thy race is the undeniable dark side of mankind—devilish dark at that. No offence.\n\nDAGGOO (grimly). None.\n\nST. JAGO’S SAILOR. That Spaniard’s mad or drunk. But that can’t be, or else in his one case our old Mogul’s fire-waters are somewhat long in working.\n\n5TH NANTUCKET SAILOR. What’s that I saw—lightning? Yes.\n\nSPANISH SAILOR. No; Daggoo showing his teeth.\n\nDAGGOO (springing). Swallow thine, mannikin! White skin, white liver!\n\nSPANISH SAILOR (meeting him). Knife thee heartily! big frame, small spirit!\n\nALL. A row! a row! a row!\n\nTASHTEGO (with a whiff). A row a’low, and a row aloft—Gods and men—both brawlers! Humph!\n\nBELFAST SAILOR. A row! arrah a row! The Virgin be blessed, a row! Plunge in with ye!\n\nENGLISH SAILOR. Fair play! Snatch the Spaniard’s knife! A ring, a ring!\n\nOLD MANX SAILOR. Ready formed. There! the ringed horizon. In that ring Cain struck Abel. Sweet work, right work! No? Why then, God, mad’st thou the ring?\n\nMATE’S VOICE FROM THE QUARTER-DECK. Hands by the halyards! in top-gallant sails! Stand by to reef topsails!\n\nALL. The squall! the squall! jump, my jollies! (They scatter.)\n\nPIP (shrinking under the windlass). Jollies? Lord help such jollies! Crish, crash! there goes the jib-stay! Blang-whang! God! Duck lower, Pip, here comes the royal yard! It’s worse than being in the whirled woods, the last day of the year! Who’d go climbing after chestnuts now? But there they go, all cursing, and here I don’t. Fine prospects to ’em; they’re on the road to heaven. Hold on hard! Jimmini, what a squall! But those chaps there are worse yet—they are your white squalls, they. White squalls? white whale, shirr! shirr! Here have I heard all their chat just now, and the white whale—shirr! shirr!—but spoken of once! and only this evening—it makes me jingle all over like my tambourine—that anaconda of an old man swore ’em in to hunt him! Oh, thou big white God aloft there somewhere in yon darkness, have mercy on this small black boy down here; preserve him from all men that have no bowels to feel fear!\n\n# CHAPTER 41. Moby Dick.\n\nI, Ishmael, was one of that crew; my shouts had gone up with the rest; my oath had been welded with theirs; and stronger I shouted, and more did I hammer and clinch my oath, because of the dread in my soul. A wild, mystical, sympathetical feeling was in me; Ahab’s quenchless feud seemed mine. With greedy ears I learned the history of that murderous monster against whom I and all the others had taken our oaths of violence and revenge.\n\nFor some time past, though at intervals only, the unaccompanied, secluded White Whale had haunted those uncivilized seas mostly frequented by the Sperm Whale fishermen. But not all of them knew of his existence; only a few of them, comparatively, had knowingly seen him; while the number who as yet had actually and knowingly given battle to him, was small indeed. For, owing to the large number of whale-cruisers; the disorderly way they were sprinkled over the entire watery circumference, many of them adventurously pushing their quest along solitary latitudes, so as seldom or never for a whole twelvemonth or more on a stretch, to encounter a single news-telling sail of any sort; the inordinate length of each separate voyage; the irregularity of the times of sailing from home; all these, with other circumstances, direct and indirect, long obstructed the spread through the whole world-wide whaling-fleet of the special individualizing tidings concerning Moby Dick. It was hardly to be doubted, that several vessels reported to have encountered, at such or such a time, or on such or such a meridian, a Sperm Whale of uncommon magnitude and malignity, which whale, after doing great mischief to his assailants, had completely escaped them; to some minds it was not an unfair presumption, I say, that the whale in question must have been no other than Moby Dick. Yet as of late the Sperm Whale fishery had been marked by various and not unfrequent instances of great ferocity, cunning, and malice in the monster attacked; therefore it was, that those who by accident ignorantly gave battle to Moby Dick; such hunters, perhaps, for the most part, were content to ascribe the peculiar terror he bred, more, as it were, to the perils of the Sperm Whale fishery at large, than to the individual cause. In that way, mostly, the disastrous encounter between Ahab and the whale had hitherto been popularly regarded.\n\nAnd as for those who, previously hearing of the White Whale, by chance caught sight of him; in the beginning of the thing they had every one of them, almost, as boldly and fearlessly lowered for him, as for any other whale of that species. But at length, such calamities did ensue in these assaults—not restricted to sprained wrists and ankles, broken limbs, or devouring amputations—but fatal to the last degree of fatality; those repeated disastrous repulses, all accumulating and piling their terrors upon Moby Dick; those things had gone far to shake the fortitude of many brave hunters, to whom the story of the White Whale had eventually come.\n\nNor did wild rumors of all sorts fail to exaggerate, and still the more horrify the true histories of these deadly encounters. For not only do fabulous rumors naturally grow out of the very body of all surprising terrible events,—as the smitten tree gives birth to its fungi; but, in maritime life, far more than in that of terra firma, wild rumors abound, wherever there is any adequate reality for them to cling to. And as the sea surpasses the land in this matter, so the whale fishery surpasses every other sort of maritime life, in the wonderfulness and fearfulness of the rumors which sometimes circulate there. For not only are whalemen as a body unexempt from that ignorance and superstitiousness hereditary to all sailors; but of all sailors, they are by all odds the most directly brought into contact with whatever is appallingly astonishing in the sea; face to face they not only eye its greatest marvels, but, hand to jaw, give battle to them. Alone, in such remotest waters, that though you sailed a thousand miles, and passed a thousand shores, you would not come to any chiseled hearth-stone, or aught hospitable beneath that part of the sun; in such latitudes and longitudes, pursuing too such a calling as he does, the whaleman is wrapped by influences all tending to make his fancy pregnant with many a mighty birth.\n\nNo wonder, then, that ever gathering volume from the mere transit over the widest watery spaces, the outblown rumors of the White Whale did in the end incorporate with themselves all manner of morbid hints, and half-formed fœtal suggestions of supernatural agencies, which eventually invested Moby Dick with new terrors unborrowed from anything that visibly appears. So that in many cases such a panic did he finally strike, that few who by those rumors, at least, had heard of the White Whale, few of those hunters were willing to encounter the perils of his jaw.\n\nBut there were still other and more vital practical influences at work. Not even at the present day has the original prestige of the Sperm Whale, as fearfully distinguished from all other species of the leviathan, died out of the minds of the whalemen as a body. There are those this day among them, who, though intelligent and courageous enough in offering battle to the Greenland or Right whale, would perhaps—either from professional inexperience, or incompetency, or timidity, decline a contest with the Sperm Whale; at any rate, there are plenty of whalemen, especially among those whaling nations not sailing under the American flag, who have never hostilely encountered the Sperm Whale, but whose sole knowledge of the leviathan is restricted to the ignoble monster primitively pursued in the North; seated on their hatches, these men will hearken with a childish fireside interest and awe, to the wild, strange tales of Southern whaling. Nor is the pre-eminent tremendousness of the great Sperm Whale anywhere more feelingly comprehended, than on board of those prows which stem him.\n\nAnd as if the now tested reality of his might had in former legendary times thrown its shadow before it; we find some book naturalists—Olassen and Povelson—declaring the Sperm Whale not only to be a consternation to every other creature in the sea, but also to be so incredibly ferocious as continually to be athirst for human blood. Nor even down to so late a time as Cuvier’s, were these or almost similar impressions effaced. For in his Natural History, the Baron himself affirms that at sight of the Sperm Whale, all fish (sharks included) are “struck with the most lively terrors,” and “often in the precipitancy of their flight dash themselves against the rocks with such violence as to cause instantaneous death.” And however the general experiences in the fishery may amend such reports as these; yet in their full terribleness, even to the bloodthirsty item of Povelson, the superstitious belief in them is, in some vicissitudes of their vocation, revived in the minds of the hunters.\n\nSo that overawed by the rumors and portents concerning him, not a few of the fishermen recalled, in reference to Moby Dick, the earlier days of the Sperm Whale fishery, when it was oftentimes hard to induce long practised Right whalemen to embark in the perils of this new and daring warfare; such men protesting that although other leviathans might be hopefully pursued, yet to chase and point lance at such an apparition as the Sperm Whale was not for mortal man. That to attempt it, would be inevitably to be torn into a quick eternity. On this head, there are some remarkable documents that may be consulted.\n\nNevertheless, some there were, who even in the face of these things were ready to give chase to Moby Dick; and a still greater number who, chancing only to hear of him distantly and vaguely, without the specific details of any certain calamity, and without superstitious accompaniments, were sufficiently hardy not to flee from the battle if offered.\n\nOne of the wild suggestions referred to, as at last coming to be linked with the White Whale in the minds of the superstitiously inclined, was the unearthly conceit that Moby Dick was ubiquitous; that he had actually been encountered in opposite latitudes at one and the same instant of time.\n\nNor, credulous as such minds must have been, was this conceit altogether without some faint show of superstitious probability. For as the secrets of the currents in the seas have never yet been divulged, even to the most erudite research; so the hidden ways of the Sperm Whale when beneath the surface remain, in great part, unaccountable to his pursuers; and from time to time have originated the most curious and contradictory speculations regarding them, especially concerning the mystic modes whereby, after sounding to a great depth, he transports himself with such vast swiftness to the most widely distant points.\n\nIt is a thing well known to both American and English whale-ships, and as well a thing placed upon authoritative record years ago by Scoresby, that some whales have been captured far north in the Pacific, in whose bodies have been found the barbs of harpoons darted in the Greenland seas. Nor is it to be gainsaid, that in some of these instances it has been declared that the interval of time between the two assaults could not have exceeded very many days. Hence, by inference, it has been believed by some whalemen, that the Nor’ West Passage, so long a problem to man, was never a problem to the whale. So that here, in the real living experience of living men, the prodigies related in old times of the inland Strello mountain in Portugal (near whose top there was said to be a lake in which the wrecks of ships floated up to the surface); and that still more wonderful story of the Arethusa fountain near Syracuse (whose waters were believed to have come from the Holy Land by an underground passage); these fabulous narrations are almost fully equalled by the realities of the whalemen.\n\nForced into familiarity, then, with such prodigies as these; and knowing that after repeated, intrepid assaults, the White Whale had escaped alive; it cannot be much matter of surprise that some whalemen should go still further in their superstitions; declaring Moby Dick not only ubiquitous, but immortal (for immortality is but ubiquity in time); that though groves of spears should be planted in his flanks, he would still swim away unharmed; or if indeed he should ever be made to spout thick blood, such a sight would be but a ghastly deception; for again in unensanguined billows hundreds of leagues away, his unsullied jet would once more be seen.\n\nBut even stripped of these supernatural surmisings, there was enough in the earthly make and incontestable character of the monster to strike the imagination with unwonted power. For, it was not so much his uncommon bulk that so much distinguished him from other sperm whales, but, as was elsewhere thrown out—a peculiar snow-white wrinkled forehead, and a high, pyramidical white hump. These were his prominent features; the tokens whereby, even in the limitless, uncharted seas, he revealed his identity, at a long distance, to those who knew him.\n\nThe rest of his body was so streaked, and spotted, and marbled with the same shrouded hue, that, in the end, he had gained his distinctive appellation of the White Whale; a name, indeed, literally justified by his vivid aspect, when seen gliding at high noon through a dark blue sea, leaving a milky-way wake of creamy foam, all spangled with golden gleamings.\n\nNor was it his unwonted magnitude, nor his remarkable hue, nor yet his deformed lower jaw, that so much invested the whale with natural terror, as that unexampled, intelligent malignity which, according to specific accounts, he had over and over again evinced in his assaults. More than all, his treacherous retreats struck more of dismay than perhaps aught else. For, when swimming before his exulting pursuers, with every apparent symptom of alarm, he had several times been known to turn round suddenly, and, bearing down upon them, either stave their boats to splinters, or drive them back in consternation to their ship.\n\nAlready several fatalities had attended his chase. But though similar disasters, however little bruited ashore, were by no means unusual in the fishery; yet, in most instances, such seemed the White Whale’s infernal aforethought of ferocity, that every dismembering or death that he caused, was not wholly regarded as having been inflicted by an unintelligent agent.\n\nJudge, then, to what pitches of inflamed, distracted fury the minds of his more desperate hunters were impelled, when amid the chips of chewed boats, and the sinking limbs of torn comrades, they swam out of the white curds of the whale’s direful wrath into the serene, exasperating sunlight, that smiled on, as if at a birth or a bridal.\n\nHis three boats stove around him, and oars and men both whirling in the eddies; one captain, seizing the line-knife from his broken prow, had dashed at the whale, as an Arkansas duellist at his foe, blindly seeking with a six inch blade to reach the fathom-deep life of the whale. That captain was Ahab. And then it was, that suddenly sweeping his sickle-shaped lower jaw beneath him, Moby Dick had reaped away Ahab’s leg, as a mower a blade of grass in the field. No turbaned Turk, no hired Venetian or Malay, could have smote him with more seeming malice. Small reason was there to doubt, then, that ever since that almost fatal encounter, Ahab had cherished a wild vindictiveness against the whale, all the more fell for that in his frantic morbidness he at last came to identify with him, not only all his bodily woes, but all his intellectual and spiritual exasperations. The White Whale swam before him as the monomaniac incarnation of all those malicious agencies which some deep men feel eating in them, till they are left living on with half a heart and half a lung. That intangible malignity which has been from the beginning; to whose dominion even the modern Christians ascribe one-half of the worlds; which the ancient Ophites of the east reverenced in their statue devil;—Ahab did not fall down and worship it like them; but deliriously transferring its idea to the abhorred white whale, he pitted himself, all mutilated, against it. All that most maddens and torments; all that stirs up the lees of things; all truth with malice in it; all that cracks the sinews and cakes the brain; all the subtle demonisms of life and thought; all evil, to crazy Ahab, were visibly personified, and made practically assailable in Moby Dick. He piled upon the whale’s white hump the sum of all the general rage and hate felt by his whole race from Adam down; and then, as if his chest had been a mortar, he burst his hot heart’s shell upon it.\n\nIt is not probable that this monomania in him took its instant rise at the precise time of his bodily dismemberment. Then, in darting at the monster, knife in hand, he had but given loose to a sudden, passionate, corporal animosity; and when he received the stroke that tore him, he probably but felt the agonizing bodily laceration, but nothing more. Yet, when by this collision forced to turn towards home, and for long months of days and weeks, Ahab and anguish lay stretched together in one hammock, rounding in mid winter that dreary, howling Patagonian Cape; then it was, that his torn body and gashed soul bled into one another; and so interfusing, made him mad. That it was only then, on the homeward voyage, after the encounter, that the final monomania seized him, seems all but certain from the fact that, at intervals during the passage, he was a raving lunatic; and, though unlimbed of a leg, yet such vital strength yet lurked in his Egyptian chest, and was moreover intensified by his delirium, that his mates were forced to lace him fast, even there, as he sailed, raving in his hammock. In a strait-jacket, he swung to the mad rockings of the gales. And, when running into more sufferable latitudes, the ship, with mild stun’sails spread, floated across the tranquil tropics, and, to all appearances, the old man’s delirium seemed left behind him with the Cape Horn swells, and he came forth from his dark den into the blessed light and air; even then, when he bore that firm, collected front, however pale, and issued his calm orders once again; and his mates thanked God the direful madness was now gone; even then, Ahab, in his hidden self, raved on. Human madness is oftentimes a cunning and most feline thing. When you think it fled, it may have but become transfigured into some still subtler form. Ahab’s full lunacy subsided not, but deepeningly contracted; like the unabated Hudson, when that noble Northman flows narrowly, but unfathomably through the Highland gorge. But, as in his narrow-flowing monomania, not one jot of Ahab’s broad madness had been left behind; so in that broad madness, not one jot of his great natural intellect had perished. That before living agent, now became the living instrument. If such a furious trope may stand, his special lunacy stormed his general sanity, and carried it, and turned all its concentred cannon upon its own mad mark; so that far from having lost his strength, Ahab, to that one end, did now possess a thousand fold more potency than ever he had sanely brought to bear upon any one reasonable object.\n\nThis is much; yet Ahab’s larger, darker, deeper part remains unhinted. But vain to popularize profundities, and all truth is profound. Winding far down from within the very heart of this spiked Hotel de Cluny where we here stand—however grand and wonderful, now quit it;—and take your way, ye nobler, sadder souls, to those vast Roman halls of Thermes; where far beneath the fantastic towers of man’s upper earth, his root of grandeur, his whole awful essence sits in bearded state; an antique buried beneath antiquities, and throned on torsoes! So with a broken throne, the great gods mock that captive king; so like a Caryatid, he patient sits, upholding on his frozen brow the piled entablatures of ages. Wind ye down there, ye prouder, sadder souls! question that proud, sad king! A family likeness! aye, he did beget ye, ye young exiled royalties; and from your grim sire only will the old State-secret come.\n\nNow, in his heart, Ahab had some glimpse of this, namely: all my means are sane, my motive and my object mad. Yet without power to kill, or change, or shun the fact; he likewise knew that to mankind he did long dissemble; in some sort, did still. But that thing of his dissembling was only subject to his perceptibility, not to his will determinate. Nevertheless, so well did he succeed in that dissembling, that when with ivory leg he stepped ashore at last, no Nantucketer thought him otherwise than but naturally grieved, and that to the quick, with the terrible casualty which had overtaken him.\n\nThe report of his undeniable delirium at sea was likewise popularly ascribed to a kindred cause. And so too, all the added moodiness which always afterwards, to the very day of sailing in the Pequod on the present voyage, sat brooding on his brow. Nor is it so very unlikely, that far from distrusting his fitness for another whaling voyage, on account of such dark symptoms, the calculating people of that prudent isle were inclined to harbor the conceit, that for those very reasons he was all the better qualified and set on edge, for a pursuit so full of rage and wildness as the bloody hunt of whales. Gnawed within and scorched without, with the infixed, unrelenting fangs of some incurable idea; such an one, could he be found, would seem the very man to dart his iron and lift his lance against the most appalling of all brutes. Or, if for any reason thought to be corporeally incapacitated for that, yet such an one would seem superlatively competent to cheer and howl on his underlings to the attack. But be all this as it may, certain it is, that with the mad secret of his unabated rage bolted up and keyed in him, Ahab had purposely sailed upon the present voyage with the one only and all-engrossing object of hunting the White Whale. Had any one of his old acquaintances on shore but half dreamed of what was lurking in him then, how soon would their aghast and righteous souls have wrenched the ship from such a fiendish man! They were bent on profitable cruises, the profit to be counted down in dollars from the mint. He was intent on an audacious, immitigable, and supernatural revenge.\n\nHere, then, was this grey-headed, ungodly old man, chasing with curses a Job’s whale round the world, at the head of a crew, too, chiefly made up of mongrel renegades, and castaways, and cannibals—morally enfeebled also, by the incompetence of mere unaided virtue or right-mindedness in Starbuck, the invulnerable jollity of indifference and recklessness in Stubb, and the pervading mediocrity in Flask. Such a crew, so officered, seemed specially picked and packed by some infernal fatality to help him to his monomaniac revenge. How it was that they so aboundingly responded to the old man’s ire—by what evil magic their souls were possessed, that at times his hate seemed almost theirs; the White Whale as much their insufferable foe as his; how all this came to be—what the White Whale was to them, or how to their unconscious understandings, also, in some dim, unsuspected way, he might have seemed the gliding great demon of the seas of life,—all this to explain, would be to dive deeper than Ishmael can go. The subterranean miner that works in us all, how can one tell whither leads his shaft by the ever shifting, muffled sound of his pick? Who does not feel the irresistible arm drag? What skiff in tow of a seventy-four can stand still? For one, I gave myself up to the abandonment of the time and the place; but while yet all a-rush to encounter the whale, could see naught in that brute but the deadliest ill.\n\n# CHAPTER 42. The Whiteness of the Whale.\n\nWhat the white whale was to Ahab, has been hinted; what, at times, he was to me, as yet remains unsaid.\n\nAside from those more obvious considerations touching Moby Dick, which could not but occasionally awaken in any man’s soul some alarm, there was another thought, or rather vague, nameless horror concerning him, which at times by its intensity completely overpowered all the rest; and yet so mystical and well nigh ineffable was it, that I almost despair of putting it in a comprehensible form. It was the whiteness of the whale that above all things appalled me. But how can I hope to explain myself here; and yet, in some dim, random way, explain myself I must, else all these chapters might be naught.\n\nThough in many natural objects, whiteness refiningly enhances beauty, as if imparting some special virtue of its own, as in marbles, japonicas, and pearls; and though various nations have in some way recognised a certain royal preeminence in this hue; even the barbaric, grand old kings of Pegu placing the title “Lord of the White Elephants” above all their other magniloquent ascriptions of dominion; and the modern kings of Siam unfurling the same snow-white quadruped in the royal standard; and the Hanoverian flag bearing the one figure of a snow-white charger; and the great Austrian Empire, Cæsarian, heir to overlording Rome, having for the imperial colour the same imperial hue; and though this pre-eminence in it applies to the human race itself, giving the white man ideal mastership over every dusky tribe; and though, besides, all this, whiteness has been even made significant of gladness, for among the Romans a white stone marked a joyful day; and though in other mortal sympathies and symbolizings, this same hue is made the emblem of many touching, noble things—the innocence of brides, the benignity of age; though among the Red Men of America the giving of the white belt of wampum was the deepest pledge of honor; though in many climes, whiteness typifies the majesty of Justice in the ermine of the Judge, and contributes to the daily state of kings and queens drawn by milk-white steeds; though even in the higher mysteries of the most august religions it has been made the symbol of the divine spotlessness and power; by the Persian fire worshippers, the white forked flame being held the holiest on the altar; and in the Greek mythologies, Great Jove himself being made incarnate in a snow-white bull; and though to the noble Iroquois, the midwinter sacrifice of the sacred White Dog was by far the holiest festival of their theology, that spotless, faithful creature being held the purest envoy they could send to the Great Spirit with the annual tidings of their own fidelity; and though directly from the Latin word for white, all Christian priests derive the name of one part of their sacred vesture, the alb or tunic, worn beneath the cassock; and though among the holy pomps of the Romish faith, white is specially employed in the celebration of the Passion of our Lord; though in the Vision of St. John, white robes are given to the redeemed, and the four-and-twenty elders stand clothed in white before the great white throne, and the Holy One that sitteth there white like wool; yet for all these accumulated associations, with whatever is sweet, and honorable, and sublime, there yet lurks an elusive something in the innermost idea of this hue, which strikes more of panic to the soul than that redness which affrights in blood.\n\nThis elusive quality it is, which causes the thought of whiteness, when divorced from more kindly associations, and coupled with any object terrible in itself, to heighten that terror to the furthest bounds. Witness the white bear of the poles, and the white shark of the tropics; what but their smooth, flaky whiteness makes them the transcendent horrors they are? That ghastly whiteness it is which imparts such an abhorrent mildness, even more loathsome than terrific, to the dumb gloating of their aspect. So that not the fierce-fanged tiger in his heraldic coat can so stagger courage as the white-shrouded bear or shark.*\n\n*With reference to the Polar bear, it may possibly be urged by him who would fain go still deeper into this matter, that it is not the whiteness, separately regarded, which heightens the intolerable hideousness of that brute; for, analysed, that heightened hideousness, it might be said, only rises from the circumstance, that the irresponsible ferociousness of the creature stands invested in the fleece of celestial innocence and love; and hence, by bringing together two such opposite emotions in our minds, the Polar bear frightens us with so unnatural a contrast. But even assuming all this to be true; yet, were it not for the whiteness, you would not have that intensified terror.\n\nAs for the white shark, the white gliding ghostliness of repose in that creature, when beheld in his ordinary moods, strangely tallies with the same quality in the Polar quadruped. This peculiarity is most vividly hit by the French in the name they bestow upon that fish. The Romish mass for the dead begins with “Requiem eternam” (eternal rest), whence Requiem denominating the mass itself, and any other funeral music. Now, in allusion to the white, silent stillness of death in this shark, and the mild deadliness of his habits, the French call him Requin.\n\nBethink thee of the albatross, whence come those clouds of spiritual wonderment and pale dread, in which that white phantom sails in all imaginations? Not Coleridge first threw that spell; but God’s great, unflattering laureate, Nature.*\n\n*I remember the first albatross I ever saw. It was during a prolonged gale, in waters hard upon the Antarctic seas. From my forenoon watch below, I ascended to the overclouded deck; and there, dashed upon the main hatches, I saw a regal, feathery thing of unspotted whiteness, and with a hooked, Roman bill sublime. At intervals, it arched forth its vast archangel wings, as if to embrace some holy ark. Wondrous flutterings and throbbings shook it. Though bodily unharmed, it uttered cries, as some king’s ghost in supernatural distress. Through its inexpressible, strange eyes, methought I peeped to secrets which took hold of God. As Abraham before the angels, I bowed myself; the white thing was so white, its wings so wide, and in those for ever exiled waters, I had lost the miserable warping memories of traditions and of towns. Long I gazed at that prodigy of plumage. I cannot tell, can only hint, the things that darted through me then. But at last I awoke; and turning, asked a sailor what bird was this. A goney, he replied. Goney! never had heard that name before; is it conceivable that this glorious thing is utterly unknown to men ashore! never! But some time after, I learned that goney was some seaman’s name for albatross. So that by no possibility could Coleridge’s wild Rhyme have had aught to do with those mystical impressions which were mine, when I saw that bird upon our deck. For neither had I then read the Rhyme, nor knew the bird to be an albatross. Yet, in saying this, I do but indirectly burnish a little brighter the noble merit of the poem and the poet.\n\nI assert, then, that in the wondrous bodily whiteness of the bird chiefly lurks the secret of the spell; a truth the more evinced in this, that by a solecism of terms there are birds called grey albatrosses; and these I have frequently seen, but never with such emotions as when I beheld the Antarctic fowl.\n\nBut how had the mystic thing been caught? Whisper it not, and I will tell; with a treacherous hook and line, as the fowl floated on the sea. At last the Captain made a postman of it; tying a lettered, leathern tally round its neck, with the ship’s time and place; and then letting it escape. But I doubt not, that leathern tally, meant for man, was taken off in Heaven, when the white fowl flew to join the wing-folding, the invoking, and adoring cherubim!\n\nMost famous in our Western annals and Indian traditions is that of the White Steed of the Prairies; a magnificent milk-white charger, large-eyed, small-headed, bluff-chested, and with the dignity of a thousand monarchs in his lofty, overscorning carriage. He was the elected Xerxes of vast herds of wild horses, whose pastures in those days were only fenced by the Rocky Mountains and the Alleghanies. At their flaming head he westward trooped it like that chosen star which every evening leads on the hosts of light. The flashing cascade of his mane, the curving comet of his tail, invested him with housings more resplendent than gold and silver-beaters could have furnished him. A most imperial and archangelical apparition of that unfallen, western world, which to the eyes of the old trappers and hunters revived the glories of those primeval times when Adam walked majestic as a god, bluff-browed and fearless as this mighty steed. Whether marching amid his aides and marshals in the van of countless cohorts that endlessly streamed it over the plains, like an Ohio; or whether with his circumambient subjects browsing all around at the horizon, the White Steed gallopingly reviewed them with warm nostrils reddening through his cool milkiness; in whatever aspect he presented himself, always to the bravest Indians he was the object of trembling reverence and awe. Nor can it be questioned from what stands on legendary record of this noble horse, that it was his spiritual whiteness chiefly, which so clothed him with divineness; and that this divineness had that in it which, though commanding worship, at the same time enforced a certain nameless terror.\n\nBut there are other instances where this whiteness loses all that accessory and strange glory which invests it in the White Steed and Albatross.\n\nWhat is it that in the Albino man so peculiarly repels and often shocks the eye, as that sometimes he is loathed by his own kith and kin! It is that whiteness which invests him, a thing expressed by the name he bears. The Albino is as well made as other men—has no substantive deformity—and yet this mere aspect of all-pervading whiteness makes him more strangely hideous than the ugliest abortion. Why should this be so?\n\nNor, in quite other aspects, does Nature in her least palpable but not the less malicious agencies, fail to enlist among her forces this crowning attribute of the terrible. From its snowy aspect, the gauntleted ghost of the Southern Seas has been denominated the White Squall. Nor, in some historic instances, has the art of human malice omitted so potent an auxiliary. How wildly it heightens the effect of that passage in Froissart, when, masked in the snowy symbol of their faction, the desperate White Hoods of Ghent murder their bailiff in the market-place!\n\nNor, in some things, does the common, hereditary experience of all mankind fail to bear witness to the supernaturalism of this hue. It cannot well be doubted, that the one visible quality in the aspect of the dead which most appals the gazer, is the marble pallor lingering there; as if indeed that pallor were as much like the badge of consternation in the other world, as of mortal trepidation here. And from that pallor of the dead, we borrow the expressive hue of the shroud in which we wrap them. Nor even in our superstitions do we fail to throw the same snowy mantle round our phantoms; all ghosts rising in a milk-white fog—Yea, while these terrors seize us, let us add, that even the king of terrors, when personified by the evangelist, rides on his pallid horse.\n\nTherefore, in his other moods, symbolize whatever grand or gracious thing he will by whiteness, no man can deny that in its profoundest idealized significance it calls up a peculiar apparition to the soul.\n\nBut though without dissent this point be fixed, how is mortal man to account for it? To analyse it, would seem impossible. Can we, then, by the citation of some of those instances wherein this thing of whiteness—though for the time either wholly or in great part stripped of all direct associations calculated to impart to it aught fearful, but nevertheless, is found to exert over us the same sorcery, however modified;—can we thus hope to light upon some chance clue to conduct us to the hidden cause we seek?\n\nLet us try. But in a matter like this, subtlety appeals to subtlety, and without imagination no man can follow another into these halls. And though, doubtless, some at least of the imaginative impressions about to be presented may have been shared by most men, yet few perhaps were entirely conscious of them at the time, and therefore may not be able to recall them now.\n\nWhy to the man of untutored ideality, who happens to be but loosely acquainted with the peculiar character of the day, does the bare mention of Whitsuntide marshal in the fancy such long, dreary, speechless processions of slow-pacing pilgrims, down-cast and hooded with new-fallen snow? Or, to the unread, unsophisticated Protestant of the Middle American States, why does the passing mention of a White Friar or a White Nun, evoke such an eyeless statue in the soul?\n\nOr what is there apart from the traditions of dungeoned warriors and kings (which will not wholly account for it) that makes the White Tower of London tell so much more strongly on the imagination of an untravelled American, than those other storied structures, its neighbors—the Byward Tower, or even the Bloody? And those sublimer towers, the White Mountains of New Hampshire, whence, in peculiar moods, comes that gigantic ghostliness over the soul at the bare mention of that name, while the thought of Virginia’s Blue Ridge is full of a soft, dewy, distant dreaminess? Or why, irrespective of all latitudes and longitudes, does the name of the White Sea exert such a spectralness over the fancy, while that of the Yellow Sea lulls us with mortal thoughts of long lacquered mild afternoons on the waves, followed by the gaudiest and yet sleepiest of sunsets? Or, to choose a wholly unsubstantial instance, purely addressed to the fancy, why, in reading the old fairy tales of Central Europe, does “the tall pale man” of the Hartz forests, whose changeless pallor unrustlingly glides through the green of the groves—why is this phantom more terrible than all the whooping imps of the Blocksburg?\n\nNor is it, altogether, the remembrance of her cathedral-toppling earthquakes; nor the stampedoes of her frantic seas; nor the tearlessness of arid skies that never rain; nor the sight of her wide field of leaning spires, wrenched cope-stones, and crosses all adroop (like canted yards of anchored fleets); and her suburban avenues of house-walls lying over upon each other, as a tossed pack of cards;—it is not these things alone which make tearless Lima, the strangest, saddest city thou can’st see. For Lima has taken the white veil; and there is a higher horror in this whiteness of her woe. Old as Pizarro, this whiteness keeps her ruins for ever new; admits not the cheerful greenness of complete decay; spreads over her broken ramparts the rigid pallor of an apoplexy that fixes its own distortions.\n\nI know that, to the common apprehension, this phenomenon of whiteness is not confessed to be the prime agent in exaggerating the terror of objects otherwise terrible; nor to the unimaginative mind is there aught of terror in those appearances whose awfulness to another mind almost solely consists in this one phenomenon, especially when exhibited under any form at all approaching to muteness or universality. What I mean by these two statements may perhaps be respectively elucidated by the following examples.\n\nFirst: The mariner, when drawing nigh the coasts of foreign lands, if by night he hear the roar of breakers, starts to vigilance, and feels just enough of trepidation to sharpen all his faculties; but under precisely similar circumstances, let him be called from his hammock to view his ship sailing through a midnight sea of milky whiteness—as if from encircling headlands shoals of combed white bears were swimming round him, then he feels a silent, superstitious dread; the shrouded phantom of the whitened waters is horrible to him as a real ghost; in vain the lead assures him he is still off soundings; heart and helm they both go down; he never rests till blue water is under him again. Yet where is the mariner who will tell thee, “Sir, it was not so much the fear of striking hidden rocks, as the fear of that hideous whiteness that so stirred me?”\n\nSecond: To the native Indian of Peru, the continual sight of the snow-howdahed Andes conveys naught of dread, except, perhaps, in the mere fancying of the eternal frosted desolateness reigning at such vast altitudes, and the natural conceit of what a fearfulness it would be to lose oneself in such inhuman solitudes. Much the same is it with the backwoodsman of the West, who with comparative indifference views an unbounded prairie sheeted with driven snow, no shadow of tree or twig to break the fixed trance of whiteness. Not so the sailor, beholding the scenery of the Antarctic seas; where at times, by some infernal trick of legerdemain in the powers of frost and air, he, shivering and half shipwrecked, instead of rainbows speaking hope and solace to his misery, views what seems a boundless churchyard grinning upon him with its lean ice monuments and splintered crosses.\n\nBut thou sayest, methinks that white-lead chapter about whiteness is but a white flag hung out from a craven soul; thou surrenderest to a hypo, Ishmael.\n\nTell me, why this strong young colt, foaled in some peaceful valley of Vermont, far removed from all beasts of prey—why is it that upon the sunniest day, if you but shake a fresh buffalo robe behind him, so that he cannot even see it, but only smells its wild animal muskiness—why will he start, snort, and with bursting eyes paw the ground in phrensies of affright? There is no remembrance in him of any gorings of wild creatures in his green northern home, so that the strange muskiness he smells cannot recall to him anything associated with the experience of former perils; for what knows he, this New England colt, of the black bisons of distant Oregon?\n\nNo: but here thou beholdest even in a dumb brute, the instinct of the knowledge of the demonism in the world. Though thousands of miles from Oregon, still when he smells that savage musk, the rending, goring bison herds are as present as to the deserted wild foal of the prairies, which this instant they may be trampling into dust.\n\nThus, then, the muffled rollings of a milky sea; the bleak rustlings of the festooned frosts of mountains; the desolate shiftings of the windrowed snows of prairies; all these, to Ishmael, are as the shaking of that buffalo robe to the frightened colt!\n\nThough neither knows where lie the nameless things of which the mystic sign gives forth such hints; yet with me, as with the colt, somewhere those things must exist. Though in many of its aspects this visible world seems formed in love, the invisible spheres were formed in fright.\n\nBut not yet have we solved the incantation of this whiteness, and learned why it appeals with such power to the soul; and more strange and far more portentous—why, as we have seen, it is at once the most meaning symbol of spiritual things, nay, the very veil of the Christian’s Deity; and yet should be as it is, the intensifying agent in things the most appalling to mankind.\n\nIs it that by its indefiniteness it shadows forth the heartless voids and immensities of the universe, and thus stabs us from behind with the thought of annihilation, when beholding the white depths of the milky way? Or is it, that as in essence whiteness is not so much a colour as the visible absence of colour; and at the same time the concrete of all colours; is it for these reasons that there is such a dumb blankness, full of meaning, in a wide landscape of snows—a colourless, all-colour of atheism from which we shrink? And when we consider that other theory of the natural philosophers, that all other earthly hues—every stately or lovely emblazoning—the sweet tinges of sunset skies and woods; yea, and the gilded velvets of butterflies, and the butterfly cheeks of young girls; all these are but subtile deceits, not actually inherent in substances, but only laid on from without; so that all deified Nature absolutely paints like the harlot, whose allurements cover nothing but the charnel-house within; and when we proceed further, and consider that the mystical cosmetic which produces every one of her hues, the great principle of light, for ever remains white or colorless in itself, and if operating without medium upon matter, would touch all objects, even tulips and roses, with its own blank tinge—pondering all this, the palsied universe lies before us a leper; and like wilful travellers in Lapland, who refuse to wear coloured and colouring glasses upon their eyes, so the wretched infidel gazes himself blind at the monumental white shroud that wraps all the prospect around him. And of all these things the Albino whale was the symbol. Wonder ye then at the fiery hunt?\n\n“HIST! Did you hear that noise, Cabaco?”\n\nIt was the middle-watch: a fair moonlight; the seamen were standing in a cordon, extending from one of the fresh-water butts in the waist, to the scuttle-butt near the taffrail. In this manner, they passed the buckets to fill the scuttle-butt. Standing, for the most part, on the hallowed precincts of the quarter-deck, they were careful not to speak or rustle their feet. From hand to hand, the buckets went in the deepest silence, only broken by the occasional flap of a sail, and the steady hum of the unceasingly advancing keel.\n\nIt was in the midst of this repose, that Archy, one of the cordon, whose post was near the after-hatches, whispered to his neighbor, a Cholo, the words above.\n\n“Hist! did you hear that noise, Cabaco?”\n\n“Take the bucket, will ye, Archy? what noise d’ye mean?”\n\n“There it is again—under the hatches—don’t you hear it—a cough—it sounded like a cough.”\n\n“Cough be damned! Pass along that return bucket.”\n\n“There again—there it is!—it sounds like two or three sleepers turning over, now!”\n\n“Caramba! have done, shipmate, will ye? It’s the three soaked biscuits ye eat for supper turning over inside of ye—nothing else. Look to the bucket!”\n\n“Say what ye will, shipmate; I’ve sharp ears.”\n\n“Aye, you are the chap, ain’t ye, that heard the hum of the old Quakeress’s knitting-needles fifty miles at sea from Nantucket; you’re the chap.”\n\n“Grin away; we’ll see what turns up. Hark ye, Cabaco, there is somebody down in the after-hold that has not yet been seen on deck; and I suspect our old Mogul knows something of it too. I heard Stubb tell Flask, one morning watch, that there was something of that sort in the wind.”\n\n# CHAPTER 44. The Chart.\n\nHad you followed Captain Ahab down into his cabin after the squall that took place on the night succeeding that wild ratification of his purpose with his crew, you would have seen him go to a locker in the transom, and bringing out a large wrinkled roll of yellowish sea charts, spread them before him on his screwed-down table. Then seating himself before it, you would have seen him intently study the various lines and shadings which there met his eye; and with slow but steady pencil trace additional courses over spaces that before were blank. At intervals, he would refer to piles of old log-books beside him, wherein were set down the seasons and places in which, on various former voyages of various ships, sperm whales had been captured or seen.\n\nWhile thus employed, the heavy pewter lamp suspended in chains over his head, continually rocked with the motion of the ship, and for ever threw shifting gleams and shadows of lines upon his wrinkled brow, till it almost seemed that while he himself was marking out lines and courses on the wrinkled charts, some invisible pencil was also tracing lines and courses upon the deeply marked chart of his forehead.\n\nBut it was not this night in particular that, in the solitude of his cabin, Ahab thus pondered over his charts. Almost every night they were brought out; almost every night some pencil marks were effaced, and others were substituted. For with the charts of all four oceans before him, Ahab was threading a maze of currents and eddies, with a view to the more certain accomplishment of that monomaniac thought of his soul.\n\nNow, to any one not fully acquainted with the ways of the leviathans, it might seem an absurdly hopeless task thus to seek out one solitary creature in the unhooped oceans of this planet. But not so did it seem to Ahab, who knew the sets of all tides and currents; and thereby calculating the driftings of the sperm whale’s food; and, also, calling to mind the regular, ascertained seasons for hunting him in particular latitudes; could arrive at reasonable surmises, almost approaching to certainties, concerning the timeliest day to be upon this or that ground in search of his prey.\n\nSo assured, indeed, is the fact concerning the periodicalness of the sperm whale’s resorting to given waters, that many hunters believe that, could he be closely observed and studied throughout the world; were the logs for one voyage of the entire whale fleet carefully collated, then the migrations of the sperm whale would be found to correspond in invariability to those of the herring-shoals or the flights of swallows. On this hint, attempts have been made to construct elaborate migratory charts of the sperm whale.*\n\n*Since the above was written, the statement is happily borne out by an official circular, issued by Lieutenant Maury, of the National Observatory, Washington, April 16th, 1851. By that circular, it appears that precisely such a chart is in course of completion; and portions of it are presented in the circular. “This chart divides the ocean into districts of five degrees of latitude by five degrees of longitude; perpendicularly through each of which districts are twelve columns for the twelve months; and horizontally through each of which districts are three lines; one to show the number of days that have been spent in each month in every district, and the two others to show the number of days in which whales, sperm or right, have been seen.”\n\nBesides, when making a passage from one feeding-ground to another, the sperm whales, guided by some infallible instinct—say, rather, secret intelligence from the Deity—mostly swim in veins, as they are called; continuing their way along a given ocean-line with such undeviating exactitude, that no ship ever sailed her course, by any chart, with one tithe of such marvellous precision. Though, in these cases, the direction taken by any one whale be straight as a surveyor’s parallel, and though the line of advance be strictly confined to its own unavoidable, straight wake, yet the arbitrary vein in which at these times he is said to swim, generally embraces some few miles in width (more or less, as the vein is presumed to expand or contract); but never exceeds the visual sweep from the whale-ship’s mast-heads, when circumspectly gliding along this magic zone. The sum is, that at particular seasons within that breadth and along that path, migrating whales may with great confidence be looked for.\n\nAnd hence not only at substantiated times, upon well known separate feeding-grounds, could Ahab hope to encounter his prey; but in crossing the widest expanses of water between those grounds he could, by his art, so place and time himself on his way, as even then not to be wholly without prospect of a meeting.\n\nThere was a circumstance which at first sight seemed to entangle his delirious but still methodical scheme. But not so in the reality, perhaps. Though the gregarious sperm whales have their regular seasons for particular grounds, yet in general you cannot conclude that the herds which haunted such and such a latitude or longitude this year, say, will turn out to be identically the same with those that were found there the preceding season; though there are peculiar and unquestionable instances where the contrary of this has proved true. In general, the same remark, only within a less wide limit, applies to the solitaries and hermits among the matured, aged sperm whales. So that though Moby Dick had in a former year been seen, for example, on what is called the Seychelle ground in the Indian ocean, or Volcano Bay on the Japanese Coast; yet it did not follow, that were the Pequod to visit either of those spots at any subsequent corresponding season, she would infallibly encounter him there. So, too, with some other feeding grounds, where he had at times revealed himself. But all these seemed only his casual stopping-places and ocean-inns, so to speak, not his places of prolonged abode. And where Ahab’s chances of accomplishing his object have hitherto been spoken of, allusion has only been made to whatever way-side, antecedent, extra prospects were his, ere a particular set time or place were attained, when all possibilities would become probabilities, and, as Ahab fondly thought, every possibility the next thing to a certainty. That particular set time and place were conjoined in the one technical phrase—the Season-on-the-Line. For there and then, for several consecutive years, Moby Dick had been periodically descried, lingering in those waters for awhile, as the sun, in its annual round, loiters for a predicted interval in any one sign of the Zodiac. There it was, too, that most of the deadly encounters with the white whale had taken place; there the waves were storied with his deeds; there also was that tragic spot where the monomaniac old man had found the awful motive to his vengeance. But in the cautious comprehensiveness and unloitering vigilance with which Ahab threw his brooding soul into this unfaltering hunt, he would not permit himself to rest all his hopes upon the one crowning fact above mentioned, however flattering it might be to those hopes; nor in the sleeplessness of his vow could he so tranquillize his unquiet heart as to postpone all intervening quest.\n\nNow, the Pequod had sailed from Nantucket at the very beginning of the Season-on-the-Line. No possible endeavor then could enable her commander to make the great passage southwards, double Cape Horn, and then running down sixty degrees of latitude arrive in the equatorial Pacific in time to cruise there. Therefore, he must wait for the next ensuing season. Yet the premature hour of the Pequod’s sailing had, perhaps, been correctly selected by Ahab, with a view to this very complexion of things. Because, an interval of three hundred and sixty-five days and nights was before him; an interval which, instead of impatiently enduring ashore, he would spend in a miscellaneous hunt; if by chance the White Whale, spending his vacation in seas far remote from his periodical feeding-grounds, should turn up his wrinkled brow off the Persian Gulf, or in the Bengal Bay, or China Seas, or in any other waters haunted by his race. So that Monsoons, Pampas, Nor’-Westers, Harmattans, Trades; any wind but the Levanter and Simoon, might blow Moby Dick into the devious zig-zag world-circle of the Pequod’s circumnavigating wake.\n\nBut granting all this; yet, regarded discreetly and coolly, seems it not but a mad idea, this; that in the broad boundless ocean, one solitary whale, even if encountered, should be thought capable of individual recognition from his hunter, even as a white-bearded Mufti in the thronged thoroughfares of Constantinople? Yes. For the peculiar snow-white brow of Moby Dick, and his snow-white hump, could not but be unmistakable. And have I not tallied the whale, Ahab would mutter to himself, as after poring over his charts till long after midnight he would throw himself back in reveries—tallied him, and shall he escape? His broad fins are bored, and scalloped out like a lost sheep’s ear! And here, his mad mind would run on in a breathless race; till a weariness and faintness of pondering came over him; and in the open air of the deck he would seek to recover his strength. Ah, God! what trances of torments does that man endure who is consumed with one unachieved revengeful desire. He sleeps with clenched hands; and wakes with his own bloody nails in his palms.\n\nOften, when forced from his hammock by exhausting and intolerably vivid dreams of the night, which, resuming his own intense thoughts through the day, carried them on amid a clashing of phrensies, and whirled them round and round and round in his blazing brain, till the very throbbing of his life-spot became insufferable anguish; and when, as was sometimes the case, these spiritual throes in him heaved his being up from its base, and a chasm seemed opening in him, from which forked flames and lightnings shot up, and accursed fiends beckoned him to leap down among them; when this hell in himself yawned beneath him, a wild cry would be heard through the ship; and with glaring eyes Ahab would burst from his state room, as though escaping from a bed that was on fire. Yet these, perhaps, instead of being the unsuppressable symptoms of some latent weakness, or fright at his own resolve, were but the plainest tokens of its intensity. For, at such times, crazy Ahab, the scheming, unappeasedly steadfast hunter of the white whale; this Ahab that had gone to his hammock, was not the agent that so caused him to burst from it in horror again. The latter was the eternal, living principle or soul in him; and in sleep, being for the time dissociated from the characterizing mind, which at other times employed it for its outer vehicle or agent, it spontaneously sought escape from the scorching contiguity of the frantic thing, of which, for the time, it was no longer an integral. But as the mind does not exist unless leagued with the soul, therefore it must have been that, in Ahab’s case, yielding up all his thoughts and fancies to his one supreme purpose; that purpose, by its own sheer inveteracy of will, forced itself against gods and devils into a kind of self-assumed, independent being of its own. Nay, could grimly live and burn, while the common vitality to which it was conjoined, fled horror-stricken from the unbidden and unfathered birth. Therefore, the tormented spirit that glared out of bodily eyes, when what seemed Ahab rushed from his room, was for the time but a vacated thing, a formless somnambulistic being, a ray of living light, to be sure, but without an object to colour, and therefore a blankness in itself. God help thee, old man, thy thoughts have created a creature in thee; and he whose intense thinking thus makes him a Prometheus; a vulture feeds upon that heart for ever; that vulture the very creature he creates.\n\n# CHAPTER 45. The Affidavit.\n\nSo far as what there may be of a narrative in this book; and, indeed, as indirectly touching one or two very interesting and curious particulars in the habits of sperm whales, the foregoing chapter, in its earlier part, is as important a one as will be found in this volume; but the leading matter of it requires to be still further and more familiarly enlarged upon, in order to be adequately understood, and moreover to take away any incredulity which a profound ignorance of the entire subject may induce in some minds, as to the natural verity of the main points of this affair.\n\nI care not to perform this part of my task methodically; but shall be content to produce the desired impression by separate citations of items, practically or reliably known to me as a whaleman; and from these citations, I take it—the conclusion aimed at will naturally follow of itself.\n\nFirst: I have personally known three instances where a whale, after receiving a harpoon, has effected a complete escape; and, after an interval (in one instance of three years), has been again struck by the same hand, and slain; when the two irons, both marked by the same private cypher, have been taken from the body. In the instance where three years intervened between the flinging of the two harpoons; and I think it may have been something more than that; the man who darted them happening, in the interval, to go in a trading ship on a voyage to Africa, went ashore there, joined a discovery party, and penetrated far into the interior, where he travelled for a period of nearly two years, often endangered by serpents, savages, tigers, poisonous miasmas, with all the other common perils incident to wandering in the heart of unknown regions. Meanwhile, the whale he had struck must also have been on its travels; no doubt it had thrice circumnavigated the globe, brushing with its flanks all the coasts of Africa; but to no purpose. This man and this whale again came together, and the one vanquished the other. I say I, myself, have known three instances similar to this; that is in two of them I saw the whales struck; and, upon the second attack, saw the two irons with the respective marks cut in them, afterwards taken from the dead fish. In the three-year instance, it so fell out that I was in the boat both times, first and last, and the last time distinctly recognised a peculiar sort of huge mole under the whale’s eye, which I had observed there three years previous. I say three years, but I am pretty sure it was more than that. Here are three instances, then, which I personally know the truth of; but I have heard of many other instances from persons whose veracity in the matter there is no good ground to impeach.\n\nSecondly: It is well known in the Sperm Whale Fishery, however ignorant the world ashore may be of it, that there have been several memorable historical instances where a particular whale in the ocean has been at distant times and places popularly cognisable. Why such a whale became thus marked was not altogether and originally owing to his bodily peculiarities as distinguished from other whales; for however peculiar in that respect any chance whale may be, they soon put an end to his peculiarities by killing him, and boiling him down into a peculiarly valuable oil. No: the reason was this: that from the fatal experiences of the fishery there hung a terrible prestige of perilousness about such a whale as there did about Rinaldo Rinaldini, insomuch that most fishermen were content to recognise him by merely touching their tarpaulins when he would be discovered lounging by them on the sea, without seeking to cultivate a more intimate acquaintance. Like some poor devils ashore that happen to know an irascible great man, they make distant unobtrusive salutations to him in the street, lest if they pursued the acquaintance further, they might receive a summary thump for their presumption.\n\nBut not only did each of these famous whales enjoy great individual celebrity—Nay, you may call it an ocean-wide renown; not only was he famous in life and now is immortal in forecastle stories after death, but he was admitted into all the rights, privileges, and distinctions of a name; had as much a name indeed as Cambyses or Cæsar. Was it not so, O Timor Tom! thou famed leviathan, scarred like an iceberg, who so long did’st lurk in the Oriental straits of that name, whose spout was oft seen from the palmy beach of Ombay? Was it not so, O New Zealand Jack! thou terror of all cruisers that crossed their wakes in the vicinity of the Tattoo Land? Was it not so, O Morquan! King of Japan, whose lofty jet they say at times assumed the semblance of a snow-white cross against the sky? Was it not so, O Don Miguel! thou Chilian whale, marked like an old tortoise with mystic hieroglyphics upon the back! In plain prose, here are four whales as well known to the students of Cetacean History as Marius or Sylla to the classic scholar.\n\nBut this is not all. New Zealand Tom and Don Miguel, after at various times creating great havoc among the boats of different vessels, were finally gone in quest of, systematically hunted out, chased and killed by valiant whaling captains, who heaved up their anchors with that express object as much in view, as in setting out through the Narragansett Woods, Captain Butler of old had it in his mind to capture that notorious murderous savage Annawon, the headmost warrior of the Indian King Philip.\n\nI do not know where I can find a better place than just here, to make mention of one or two other things, which to me seem important, as in printed form establishing in all respects the reasonableness of the whole story of the White Whale, more especially the catastrophe. For this is one of those disheartening instances where truth requires full as much bolstering as error. So ignorant are most landsmen of some of the plainest and most palpable wonders of the world, that without some hints touching the plain facts, historical and otherwise, of the fishery, they might scout at Moby Dick as a monstrous fable, or still worse and more detestable, a hideous and intolerable allegory.\n\nFirst: Though most men have some vague flitting ideas of the general perils of the grand fishery, yet they have nothing like a fixed, vivid conception of those perils, and the frequency with which they recur. One reason perhaps is, that not one in fifty of the actual disasters and deaths by casualties in the fishery, ever finds a public record at home, however transient and immediately forgotten that record. Do you suppose that that poor fellow there, who this moment perhaps caught by the whale-line off the coast of New Guinea, is being carried down to the bottom of the sea by the sounding leviathan—do you suppose that that poor fellow’s name will appear in the newspaper obituary you will read to-morrow at your breakfast? No: because the mails are very irregular between here and New Guinea. In fact, did you ever hear what might be called regular news direct or indirect from New Guinea? Yet I tell you that upon one particular voyage which I made to the Pacific, among many others we spoke thirty different ships, every one of which had had a death by a whale, some of them more than one, and three that had each lost a boat’s crew. For God’s sake, be economical with your lamps and candles! not a gallon you burn, but at least one drop of man’s blood was spilled for it.\n\nSecondly: People ashore have indeed some indefinite idea that a whale is an enormous creature of enormous power; but I have ever found that when narrating to them some specific example of this two-fold enormousness, they have significantly complimented me upon my facetiousness; when, I declare upon my soul, I had no more idea of being facetious than Moses, when he wrote the history of the plagues of Egypt.\n\nBut fortunately the special point I here seek can be established upon testimony entirely independent of my own. That point is this: The Sperm Whale is in some cases sufficiently powerful, knowing, and judiciously malicious, as with direct aforethought to stave in, utterly destroy, and sink a large ship; and what is more, the Sperm Whale has done it.\n\nFirst: In the year 1820 the ship Essex, Captain Pollard, of Nantucket, was cruising in the Pacific Ocean. One day she saw spouts, lowered her boats, and gave chase to a shoal of sperm whales. Ere long, several of the whales were wounded; when, suddenly, a very large whale escaping from the boats, issued from the shoal, and bore directly down upon the ship. Dashing his forehead against her hull, he so stove her in, that in less than “ten minutes” she settled down and fell over. Not a surviving plank of her has been seen since. After the severest exposure, part of the crew reached the land in their boats. Being returned home at last, Captain Pollard once more sailed for the Pacific in command of another ship, but the gods shipwrecked him again upon unknown rocks and breakers; for the second time his ship was utterly lost, and forthwith forswearing the sea, he has never tempted it since. At this day Captain Pollard is a resident of Nantucket. I have seen Owen Chace, who was chief mate of the Essex at the time of the tragedy; I have read his plain and faithful narrative; I have conversed with his son; and all this within a few miles of the scene of the catastrophe.*\n\n*The following are extracts from Chace’s narrative: “Every fact seemed to warrant me in concluding that it was anything but chance which directed his operations; he made two several attacks upon the ship, at a short interval between them, both of which, according to their direction, were calculated to do us the most injury, by being made ahead, and thereby combining the speed of the two objects for the shock; to effect which, the exact manœuvres which he made were necessary. His aspect was most horrible, and such as indicated resentment and fury. He came directly from the shoal which we had just before entered, and in which we had struck three of his companions, as if fired with revenge for their sufferings.” Again: “At all events, the whole circumstances taken together, all happening before my own eyes, and producing, at the time, impressions in my mind of decided, calculating mischief, on the part of the whale (many of which impressions I cannot now recall), induce me to be satisfied that I am correct in my opinion.”\n\nHere are his reflections some time after quitting the ship, during a black night in an open boat, when almost despairing of reaching any hospitable shore. “The dark ocean and swelling waters were nothing; the fears of being swallowed up by some dreadful tempest, or dashed upon hidden rocks, with all the other ordinary subjects of fearful contemplation, seemed scarcely entitled to a moment’s thought; the dismal looking wreck, and the horrid aspect and revenge of the whale, wholly engrossed my reflections, until day again made its appearance.”\n\nIn another place—p. 45,—he speaks of “the mysterious and mortal attack of the animal.”\n\nSecondly: The ship Union, also of Nantucket, was in the year 1807 totally lost off the Azores by a similar onset, but the authentic particulars of this catastrophe I have never chanced to encounter, though from the whale hunters I have now and then heard casual allusions to it.\n\nThirdly: Some eighteen or twenty years ago Commodore J——, then commanding an American sloop-of-war of the first class, happened to be dining with a party of whaling captains, on board a Nantucket ship in the harbor of Oahu, Sandwich Islands. Conversation turning upon whales, the Commodore was pleased to be sceptical touching the amazing strength ascribed to them by the professional gentlemen present. He peremptorily denied for example, that any whale could so smite his stout sloop-of-war as to cause her to leak so much as a thimbleful. Very good; but there is more coming. Some weeks after, the Commodore set sail in this impregnable craft for Valparaiso. But he was stopped on the way by a portly sperm whale, that begged a few moments’ confidential business with him. That business consisted in fetching the Commodore’s craft such a thwack, that with all his pumps going he made straight for the nearest port to heave down and repair. I am not superstitious, but I consider the Commodore’s interview with that whale as providential. Was not Saul of Tarsus converted from unbelief by a similar fright? I tell you, the sperm whale will stand no nonsense.\n\nI will now refer you to Langsdorff’s Voyages for a little circumstance in point, peculiarly interesting to the writer hereof. Langsdorff, you must know by the way, was attached to the Russian Admiral Krusenstern’s famous Discovery Expedition in the beginning of the present century. Captain Langsdorff thus begins his seventeenth chapter:\n\n“By the thirteenth of May our ship was ready to sail, and the next day we were out in the open sea, on our way to Ochotsh. The weather was very clear and fine, but so intolerably cold that we were obliged to keep on our fur clothing. For some days we had very little wind; it was not till the nineteenth that a brisk gale from the northwest sprang up. An uncommon large whale, the body of which was larger than the ship itself, lay almost at the surface of the water, but was not perceived by any one on board till the moment when the ship, which was in full sail, was almost upon him, so that it was impossible to prevent its striking against him. We were thus placed in the most imminent danger, as this gigantic creature, setting up its back, raised the ship three feet at least out of the water. The masts reeled, and the sails fell altogether, while we who were below all sprang instantly upon the deck, concluding that we had struck upon some rock; instead of this we saw the monster sailing off with the utmost gravity and solemnity. Captain D’Wolf applied immediately to the pumps to examine whether or not the vessel had received any damage from the shock, but we found that very happily it had escaped entirely uninjured.”\n\nNow, the Captain D’Wolf here alluded to as commanding the ship in question, is a New Englander, who, after a long life of unusual adventures as a sea-captain, this day resides in the village of Dorchester near Boston. I have the honor of being a nephew of his. I have particularly questioned him concerning this passage in Langsdorff. He substantiates every word. The ship, however, was by no means a large one: a Russian craft built on the Siberian coast, and purchased by my uncle after bartering away the vessel in which he sailed from home.\n\nIn that up and down manly book of old-fashioned adventure, so full, too, of honest wonders—the voyage of Lionel Wafer, one of ancient Dampier’s old chums—I found a little matter set down so like that just quoted from Langsdorff, that I cannot forbear inserting it here for a corroborative example, if such be needed.\n\nLionel, it seems, was on his way to “John Ferdinando,” as he calls the modern Juan Fernandes. “In our way thither,” he says, “about four o’clock in the morning, when we were about one hundred and fifty leagues from the Main of America, our ship felt a terrible shock, which put our men in such consternation that they could hardly tell where they were or what to think; but every one began to prepare for death. And, indeed, the shock was so sudden and violent, that we took it for granted the ship had struck against a rock; but when the amazement was a little over, we cast the lead, and sounded, but found no ground. * * * * * The suddenness of the shock made the guns leap in their carriages, and several of the men were shaken out of their hammocks. Captain Davis, who lay with his head on a gun, was thrown out of his cabin!” Lionel then goes on to impute the shock to an earthquake, and seems to substantiate the imputation by stating that a great earthquake, somewhere about that time, did actually do great mischief along the Spanish land. But I should not much wonder if, in the darkness of that early hour of the morning, the shock was after all caused by an unseen whale vertically bumping the hull from beneath.\n\nI might proceed with several more examples, one way or another known to me, of the great power and malice at times of the sperm whale. In more than one instance, he has been known, not only to chase the assailing boats back to their ships, but to pursue the ship itself, and long withstand all the lances hurled at him from its decks. The English ship Pusie Hall can tell a story on that head; and, as for his strength, let me say, that there have been examples where the lines attached to a running sperm whale have, in a calm, been transferred to the ship, and secured there; the whale towing her great hull through the water, as a horse walks off with a cart. Again, it is very often observed that, if the sperm whale, once struck, is allowed time to rally, he then acts, not so often with blind rage, as with wilful, deliberate designs of destruction to his pursuers; nor is it without conveying some eloquent indication of his character, that upon being attacked he will frequently open his mouth, and retain it in that dread expansion for several consecutive minutes. But I must be content with only one more and a concluding illustration; a remarkable and most significant one, by which you will not fail to see, that not only is the most marvellous event in this book corroborated by plain facts of the present day, but that these marvels (like all marvels) are mere repetitions of the ages; so that for the millionth time we say amen with Solomon—Verily there is nothing new under the sun.\n\nIn the sixth Christian century lived Procopius, a Christian magistrate of Constantinople, in the days when Justinian was Emperor and Belisarius general. As many know, he wrote the history of his own times, a work every way of uncommon value. By the best authorities, he has always been considered a most trustworthy and unexaggerating historian, except in some one or two particulars, not at all affecting the matter presently to be mentioned.\n\nNow, in this history of his, Procopius mentions that, during the term of his prefecture at Constantinople, a great sea-monster was captured in the neighboring Propontis, or Sea of Marmora, after having destroyed vessels at intervals in those waters for a period of more than fifty years. A fact thus set down in substantial history cannot easily be gainsaid. Nor is there any reason it should be. Of what precise species this sea-monster was, is not mentioned. But as he destroyed ships, as well as for other reasons, he must have been a whale; and I am strongly inclined to think a sperm whale. And I will tell you why. For a long time I fancied that the sperm whale had been always unknown in the Mediterranean and the deep waters connecting with it. Even now I am certain that those seas are not, and perhaps never can be, in the present constitution of things, a place for his habitual gregarious resort. But further investigations have recently proved to me, that in modern times there have been isolated instances of the presence of the sperm whale in the Mediterranean. I am told, on good authority, that on the Barbary coast, a Commodore Davis of the British navy found the skeleton of a sperm whale. Now, as a vessel of war readily passes through the Dardanelles, hence a sperm whale could, by the same route, pass out of the Mediterranean into the Propontis.\n\nIn the Propontis, as far as I can learn, none of that peculiar substance called brit is to be found, the aliment of the right whale. But I have every reason to believe that the food of the sperm whale—squid or cuttle-fish—lurks at the bottom of that sea, because large creatures, but by no means the largest of that sort, have been found at its surface. If, then, you properly put these statements together, and reason upon them a bit, you will clearly perceive that, according to all human reasoning, Procopius’s sea-monster, that for half a century stove the ships of a Roman Emperor, must in all probability have been a sperm whale.\n\n# CHAPTER 46. Surmises.\n\nThough, consumed with the hot fire of his purpose, Ahab in all his thoughts and actions ever had in view the ultimate capture of Moby Dick; though he seemed ready to sacrifice all mortal interests to that one passion; nevertheless it may have been that he was by nature and long habituation far too wedded to a fiery whaleman’s ways, altogether to abandon the collateral prosecution of the voyage. Or at least if this were otherwise, there were not wanting other motives much more influential with him. It would be refining too much, perhaps, even considering his monomania, to hint that his vindictiveness towards the White Whale might have possibly extended itself in some degree to all sperm whales, and that the more monsters he slew by so much the more he multiplied the chances that each subsequently encountered whale would prove to be the hated one he hunted. But if such an hypothesis be indeed exceptionable, there were still additional considerations which, though not so strictly according with the wildness of his ruling passion, yet were by no means incapable of swaying him.\n\nTo accomplish his object Ahab must use tools; and of all tools used in the shadow of the moon, men are most apt to get out of order. He knew, for example, that however magnetic his ascendency in some respects was over Starbuck, yet that ascendency did not cover the complete spiritual man any more than mere corporeal superiority involves intellectual mastership; for to the purely spiritual, the intellectual but stand in a sort of corporeal relation. Starbuck’s body and Starbuck’s coerced will were Ahab’s, so long as Ahab kept his magnet at Starbuck’s brain; still he knew that for all this the chief mate, in his soul, abhorred his captain’s quest, and could he, would joyfully disintegrate himself from it, or even frustrate it. It might be that a long interval would elapse ere the White Whale was seen. During that long interval Starbuck would ever be apt to fall into open relapses of rebellion against his captain’s leadership, unless some ordinary, prudential, circumstantial influences were brought to bear upon him. Not only that, but the subtle insanity of Ahab respecting Moby Dick was noways more significantly manifested than in his superlative sense and shrewdness in foreseeing that, for the present, the hunt should in some way be stripped of that strange imaginative impiousness which naturally invested it; that the full terror of the voyage must be kept withdrawn into the obscure background (for few men’s courage is proof against protracted meditation unrelieved by action); that when they stood their long night watches, his officers and men must have some nearer things to think of than Moby Dick. For however eagerly and impetuously the savage crew had hailed the announcement of his quest; yet all sailors of all sorts are more or less capricious and unreliable—they live in the varying outer weather, and they inhale its fickleness—and when retained for any object remote and blank in the pursuit, however promissory of life and passion in the end, it is above all things requisite that temporary interests and employments should intervene and hold them healthily suspended for the final dash.\n\nNor was Ahab unmindful of another thing. In times of strong emotion mankind disdain all base considerations; but such times are evanescent. The permanent constitutional condition of the manufactured man, thought Ahab, is sordidness. Granting that the White Whale fully incites the hearts of this my savage crew, and playing round their savageness even breeds a certain generous knight-errantism in them, still, while for the love of it they give chase to Moby Dick, they must also have food for their more common, daily appetites. For even the high lifted and chivalric Crusaders of old times were not content to traverse two thousand miles of land to fight for their holy sepulchre, without committing burglaries, picking pockets, and gaining other pious perquisites by the way. Had they been strictly held to their one final and romantic object—that final and romantic object, too many would have turned from in disgust. I will not strip these men, thought Ahab, of all hopes of cash—aye, cash. They may scorn cash now; but let some months go by, and no perspective promise of it to them, and then this same quiescent cash all at once mutinying in them, this same cash would soon cashier Ahab.\n\nNor was there wanting still another precautionary motive more related to Ahab personally. Having impulsively, it is probable, and perhaps somewhat prematurely revealed the prime but private purpose of the Pequod’s voyage, Ahab was now entirely conscious that, in so doing, he had indirectly laid himself open to the unanswerable charge of usurpation; and with perfect impunity, both moral and legal, his crew if so disposed, and to that end competent, could refuse all further obedience to him, and even violently wrest from him the command. From even the barely hinted imputation of usurpation, and the possible consequences of such a suppressed impression gaining ground, Ahab must of course have been most anxious to protect himself. That protection could only consist in his own predominating brain and heart and hand, backed by a heedful, closely calculating attention to every minute atmospheric influence which it was possible for his crew to be subjected to.\n\nFor all these reasons then, and others perhaps too analytic to be verbally developed here, Ahab plainly saw that he must still in a good degree continue true to the natural, nominal purpose of the Pequod’s voyage; observe all customary usages; and not only that, but force himself to evince all his well known passionate interest in the general pursuit of his profession.\n\nBe all this as it may, his voice was now often heard hailing the three mast-heads and admonishing them to keep a bright look-out, and not omit reporting even a porpoise. This vigilance was not long without reward.\n\n# CHAPTER 47. The Mat-Maker.\n\nIt was a cloudy, sultry afternoon; the seamen were lazily lounging about the decks, or vacantly gazing over into the lead-coloured waters. Queequeg and I were mildly employed weaving what is called a sword-mat, for an additional lashing to our boat. So still and subdued and yet somehow preluding was all the scene, and such an incantation of reverie lurked in the air, that each silent sailor seemed resolved into his own invisible self.\n\nI was the attendant or page of Queequeg, while busy at the mat. As I kept passing and repassing the filling or woof of marline between the long yarns of the warp, using my own hand for the shuttle, and as Queequeg, standing sideways, ever and anon slid his heavy oaken sword between the threads, and idly looking off upon the water, carelessly and unthinkingly drove home every yarn: I say so strange a dreaminess did there then reign all over the ship and all over the sea, only broken by the intermitting dull sound of the sword, that it seemed as if this were the Loom of Time, and I myself were a shuttle mechanically weaving and weaving away at the Fates. There lay the fixed threads of the warp subject to but one single, ever returning, unchanging vibration, and that vibration merely enough to admit of the crosswise interblending of other threads with its own. This warp seemed necessity; and here, thought I, with my own hand I ply my own shuttle and weave my own destiny into these unalterable threads. Meantime, Queequeg’s impulsive, indifferent sword, sometimes hitting the woof slantingly, or crookedly, or strongly, or weakly, as the case might be; and by this difference in the concluding blow producing a corresponding contrast in the final aspect of the completed fabric; this savage’s sword, thought I, which thus finally shapes and fashions both warp and woof; this easy, indifferent sword must be chance—aye, chance, free will, and necessity—nowise incompatible—all interweavingly working together. The straight warp of necessity, not to be swerved from its ultimate course—its every alternating vibration, indeed, only tending to that; free will still free to ply her shuttle between given threads; and chance, though restrained in its play within the right lines of necessity, and sideways in its motions directed by free will, though thus prescribed to by both, chance by turns rules either, and has the last featuring blow at events.\n\nThus we were weaving and weaving away when I started at a sound so strange, long drawn, and musically wild and unearthly, that the ball of free will dropped from my hand, and I stood gazing up at the clouds whence that voice dropped like a wing. High aloft in the cross-trees was that mad Gay-Header, Tashtego. His body was reaching eagerly forward, his hand stretched out like a wand, and at brief sudden intervals he continued his cries. To be sure the same sound was that very moment perhaps being heard all over the seas, from hundreds of whalemen’s look-outs perched as high in the air; but from few of those lungs could that accustomed old cry have derived such a marvellous cadence as from Tashtego the Indian’s.\n\nAs he stood hovering over you half suspended in air, so wildly and eagerly peering towards the horizon, you would have thought him some prophet or seer beholding the shadows of Fate, and by those wild cries announcing their coming.\n\n“There she blows! there! there! there! she blows! she blows!”\n\n“Where-away?”\n\n“On the lee-beam, about two miles off! a school of them!”\n\nInstantly all was commotion.\n\nThe Sperm Whale blows as a clock ticks, with the same undeviating and reliable uniformity. And thereby whalemen distinguish this fish from other tribes of his genus.\n\n“There go flukes!” was now the cry from Tashtego; and the whales disappeared.\n\n“Quick, steward!” cried Ahab. “Time! time!”\n\nDough-Boy hurried below, glanced at the watch, and reported the exact minute to Ahab.\n\nThe ship was now kept away from the wind, and she went gently rolling before it. Tashtego reporting that the whales had gone down heading to leeward, we confidently looked to see them again directly in advance of our bows. For that singular craft at times evinced by the Sperm Whale when, sounding with his head in one direction, he nevertheless, while concealed beneath the surface, mills round, and swiftly swims off in the opposite quarter—this deceitfulness of his could not now be in action; for there was no reason to suppose that the fish seen by Tashtego had been in any way alarmed, or indeed knew at all of our vicinity. One of the men selected for shipkeepers—that is, those not appointed to the boats, by this time relieved the Indian at the main-mast head. The sailors at the fore and mizzen had come down; the line tubs were fixed in their places; the cranes were thrust out; the mainyard was backed, and the three boats swung over the sea like three samphire baskets over high cliffs. Outside of the bulwarks their eager crews with one hand clung to the rail, while one foot was expectantly poised on the gunwale. So look the long line of man-of-war’s men about to throw themselves on board an enemy’s ship.\n\nBut at this critical instant a sudden exclamation was heard that took every eye from the whale. With a start all glared at dark Ahab, who was surrounded by five dusky phantoms that seemed fresh formed out of air.\n\n# CHAPTER 48. The First Lowering.\n\nThe phantoms, for so they then seemed, were flitting on the other side of the deck, and, with a noiseless celerity, were casting loose the tackles and bands of the boat which swung there. This boat had always been deemed one of the spare boats, though technically called the captain’s, on account of its hanging from the starboard quarter. The figure that now stood by its bows was tall and swart, with one white tooth evilly protruding from its steel-like lips. A rumpled Chinese jacket of black cotton funereally invested him, with wide black trowsers of the same dark stuff. But strangely crowning this ebonness was a glistening white plaited turban, the living hair braided and coiled round and round upon his head. Less swart in aspect, the companions of this figure were of that vivid, tiger-yellow complexion peculiar to some of the aboriginal natives of the Manillas;—a race notorious for a certain diabolism of subtilty, and by some honest white mariners supposed to be the paid spies and secret confidential agents on the water of the devil, their lord, whose counting-room they suppose to be elsewhere.\n\nWhile yet the wondering ship’s company were gazing upon these strangers, Ahab cried out to the white-turbaned old man at their head, “All ready there, Fedallah?”\n\n“Ready,” was the half-hissed reply.\n\n“Lower away then; d’ye hear?” shouting across the deck. “Lower away there, I say.”\n\nSuch was the thunder of his voice, that spite of their amazement the men sprang over the rail; the sheaves whirled round in the blocks; with a wallow, the three boats dropped into the sea; while, with a dexterous, off-handed daring, unknown in any other vocation, the sailors, goat-like, leaped down the rolling ship’s side into the tossed boats below.\n\nHardly had they pulled out from under the ship’s lee, when a fourth keel, coming from the windward side, pulled round under the stern, and showed the five strangers rowing Ahab, who, standing erect in the stern, loudly hailed Starbuck, Stubb, and Flask, to spread themselves widely, so as to cover a large expanse of water. But with all their eyes again riveted upon the swart Fedallah and his crew, the inmates of the other boats obeyed not the command.\n\n“Captain Ahab?—” said Starbuck.\n\n“Spread yourselves,” cried Ahab; “give way, all four boats. Thou, Flask, pull out more to leeward!”\n\n“Aye, aye, sir,” cheerily cried little King-Post, sweeping round his great steering oar. “Lay back!” addressing his crew. “There!—there!—there again! There she blows right ahead, boys!—lay back!”\n\n“Never heed yonder yellow boys, Archy.”\n\n“Oh, I don’t mind ’em, sir,” said Archy; “I knew it all before now. Didn’t I hear ’em in the hold? And didn’t I tell Cabaco here of it? What say ye, Cabaco? They are stowaways, Mr. Flask.”\n\n“Pull, pull, my fine hearts-alive; pull, my children; pull, my little ones,” drawlingly and soothingly sighed Stubb to his crew, some of whom still showed signs of uneasiness. “Why don’t you break your backbones, my boys? What is it you stare at? Those chaps in yonder boat? Tut! They are only five more hands come to help us—never mind from where—the more the merrier. Pull, then, do pull; never mind the brimstone—devils are good fellows enough. So, so; there you are now; that’s the stroke for a thousand pounds; that’s the stroke to sweep the stakes! Hurrah for the gold cup of sperm oil, my heroes! Three cheers, men—all hearts alive! Easy, easy; don’t be in a hurry—don’t be in a hurry. Why don’t you snap your oars, you rascals? Bite something, you dogs! So, so, so, then:—softly, softly! That’s it—that’s it! long and strong. Give way there, give way! The devil fetch ye, ye ragamuffin rapscallions; ye are all asleep. Stop snoring, ye sleepers, and pull. Pull, will ye? pull, can’t ye? pull, won’t ye? Why in the name of gudgeons and ginger-cakes don’t ye pull?—pull and break something! pull, and start your eyes out! Here!” whipping out the sharp knife from his girdle; “every mother’s son of ye draw his knife, and pull with the blade between his teeth. That’s it—that’s it. Now ye do something; that looks like it, my steel-bits. Start her—start her, my silver-spoons! Start her, marling-spikes!”\n\nStubb’s exordium to his crew is given here at large, because he had rather a peculiar way of talking to them in general, and especially in inculcating the religion of rowing. But you must not suppose from this specimen of his sermonizings that he ever flew into downright passions with his congregation. Not at all; and therein consisted his chief peculiarity. He would say the most terrific things to his crew, in a tone so strangely compounded of fun and fury, and the fury seemed so calculated merely as a spice to the fun, that no oarsman could hear such queer invocations without pulling for dear life, and yet pulling for the mere joke of the thing. Besides he all the time looked so easy and indolent himself, so loungingly managed his steering-oar, and so broadly gaped—open-mouthed at times—that the mere sight of such a yawning commander, by sheer force of contrast, acted like a charm upon the crew. Then again, Stubb was one of those odd sort of humorists, whose jollity is sometimes so curiously ambiguous, as to put all inferiors on their guard in the matter of obeying them.\n\nIn obedience to a sign from Ahab, Starbuck was now pulling obliquely across Stubb’s bow; and when for a minute or so the two boats were pretty near to each other, Stubb hailed the mate.\n\n“Mr. Starbuck! larboard boat there, ahoy! a word with ye, sir, if ye please!”\n\n“Halloa!” returned Starbuck, turning round not a single inch as he spoke; still earnestly but whisperingly urging his crew; his face set like a flint from Stubb’s.\n\n“What think ye of those yellow boys, sir!”\n\n“Smuggled on board, somehow, before the ship sailed. (Strong, strong, boys!)” in a whisper to his crew, then speaking out loud again: “A sad business, Mr. Stubb! (seethe her, seethe her, my lads!) but never mind, Mr. Stubb, all for the best. Let all your crew pull strong, come what will. (Spring, my men, spring!) There’s hogsheads of sperm ahead, Mr. Stubb, and that’s what ye came for. (Pull, my boys!) Sperm, sperm’s the play! This at least is duty; duty and profit hand in hand.”\n\n“Aye, aye, I thought as much,” soliloquized Stubb, when the boats diverged, “as soon as I clapt eye on ’em, I thought so. Aye, and that’s what he went into the after hold for, so often, as Dough-Boy long suspected. They were hidden down there. The White Whale’s at the bottom of it. Well, well, so be it! Can’t be helped! All right! Give way, men! It ain’t the White Whale to-day! Give way!”\n\nNow the advent of these outlandish strangers at such a critical instant as the lowering of the boats from the deck, this had not unreasonably awakened a sort of superstitious amazement in some of the ship’s company; but Archy’s fancied discovery having some time previous got abroad among them, though indeed not credited then, this had in some small measure prepared them for the event. It took off the extreme edge of their wonder; and so what with all this and Stubb’s confident way of accounting for their appearance, they were for the time freed from superstitious surmisings; though the affair still left abundant room for all manner of wild conjectures as to dark Ahab’s precise agency in the matter from the beginning. For me, I silently recalled the mysterious shadows I had seen creeping on board the Pequod during the dim Nantucket dawn, as well as the enigmatical hintings of the unaccountable Elijah.\n\nMeantime, Ahab, out of hearing of his officers, having sided the furthest to windward, was still ranging ahead of the other boats; a circumstance bespeaking how potent a crew was pulling him. Those tiger yellow creatures of his seemed all steel and whalebone; like five trip-hammers they rose and fell with regular strokes of strength, which periodically started the boat along the water like a horizontal burst boiler out of a Mississippi steamer. As for Fedallah, who was seen pulling the harpooneer oar, he had thrown aside his black jacket, and displayed his naked chest with the whole part of his body above the gunwale, clearly cut against the alternating depressions of the watery horizon; while at the other end of the boat Ahab, with one arm, like a fencer’s, thrown half backward into the air, as if to counterbalance any tendency to trip; Ahab was seen steadily managing his steering oar as in a thousand boat lowerings ere the White Whale had torn him. All at once the outstretched arm gave a peculiar motion and then remained fixed, while the boat’s five oars were seen simultaneously peaked. Boat and crew sat motionless on the sea. Instantly the three spread boats in the rear paused on their way. The whales had irregularly settled bodily down into the blue, thus giving no distantly discernible token of the movement, though from his closer vicinity Ahab had observed it.\n\n“Every man look out along his oars!” cried Starbuck. “Thou, Queequeg, stand up!”\n\nNimbly springing up on the triangular raised box in the bow, the savage stood erect there, and with intensely eager eyes gazed off towards the spot where the chase had last been descried. Likewise upon the extreme stern of the boat where it was also triangularly platformed level with the gunwale, Starbuck himself was seen coolly and adroitly balancing himself to the jerking tossings of his chip of a craft, and silently eyeing the vast blue eye of the sea.\n\nNot very far distant Flask’s boat was also lying breathlessly still; its commander recklessly standing upon the top of the loggerhead, a stout sort of post rooted in the keel, and rising some two feet above the level of the stern platform. It is used for catching turns with the whale line. Its top is not more spacious than the palm of a man’s hand, and standing upon such a base as that, Flask seemed perched at the mast-head of some ship which had sunk to all but her trucks. But little King-Post was small and short, and at the same time little King-Post was full of a large and tall ambition, so that this loggerhead stand-point of his did by no means satisfy King-Post.\n\n“I can’t see three seas off; tip us up an oar there, and let me on to that.”\n\nUpon this, Daggoo, with either hand upon the gunwale to steady his way, swiftly slid aft, and then erecting himself volunteered his lofty shoulders for a pedestal.\n\n“Good a mast-head as any, sir. Will you mount?”\n\n“That I will, and thank ye very much, my fine fellow; only I wish you fifty feet taller.”\n\nWhereupon planting his feet firmly against two opposite planks of the boat, the gigantic negro, stooping a little, presented his flat palm to Flask’s foot, and then putting Flask’s hand on his hearse-plumed head and bidding him spring as he himself should toss, with one dexterous fling landed the little man high and dry on his shoulders. And here was Flask now standing, Daggoo with one lifted arm furnishing him with a breastband to lean against and steady himself by.\n\nAt any time it is a strange sight to the tyro to see with what wondrous habitude of unconscious skill the whaleman will maintain an erect posture in his boat, even when pitched about by the most riotously perverse and cross-running seas. Still more strange to see him giddily perched upon the loggerhead itself, under such circumstances. But the sight of little Flask mounted upon gigantic Daggoo was yet more curious; for sustaining himself with a cool, indifferent, easy, unthought of, barbaric majesty, the noble negro to every roll of the sea harmoniously rolled his fine form. On his broad back, flaxen-haired Flask seemed a snow-flake. The bearer looked nobler than the rider. Though truly vivacious, tumultuous, ostentatious little Flask would now and then stamp with impatience; but not one added heave did he thereby give to the negro’s lordly chest. So have I seen Passion and Vanity stamping the living magnanimous earth, but the earth did not alter her tides and her seasons for that.\n\nMeanwhile Stubb, the third mate, betrayed no such far-gazing solicitudes. The whales might have made one of their regular soundings, not a temporary dive from mere fright; and if that were the case, Stubb, as his wont in such cases, it seems, was resolved to solace the languishing interval with his pipe. He withdrew it from his hatband, where he always wore it aslant like a feather. He loaded it, and rammed home the loading with his thumb-end; but hardly had he ignited his match across the rough sandpaper of his hand, when Tashtego, his harpooneer, whose eyes had been setting to windward like two fixed stars, suddenly dropped like light from his erect attitude to his seat, crying out in a quick phrensy of hurry, “Down, down all, and give way!—there they are!”\n\nTo a landsman, no whale, nor any sign of a herring, would have been visible at that moment; nothing but a troubled bit of greenish white water, and thin scattered puffs of vapor hovering over it, and suffusingly blowing off to leeward, like the confused scud from white rolling billows. The air around suddenly vibrated and tingled, as it were, like the air over intensely heated plates of iron. Beneath this atmospheric waving and curling, and partially beneath a thin layer of water, also, the whales were swimming. Seen in advance of all the other indications, the puffs of vapor they spouted, seemed their forerunning couriers and detached flying outriders.\n\nAll four boats were now in keen pursuit of that one spot of troubled water and air. But it bade fair to outstrip them; it flew on and on, as a mass of interblending bubbles borne down a rapid stream from the hills.\n\n“Pull, pull, my good boys,” said Starbuck, in the lowest possible but intensest concentrated whisper to his men; while the sharp fixed glance from his eyes darted straight ahead of the bow, almost seemed as two visible needles in two unerring binnacle compasses. He did not say much to his crew, though, nor did his crew say anything to him. Only the silence of the boat was at intervals startlingly pierced by one of his peculiar whispers, now harsh with command, now soft with entreaty.\n\nHow different the loud little King-Post. “Sing out and say something, my hearties. Roar and pull, my thunderbolts! Beach me, beach me on their black backs, boys; only do that for me, and I’ll sign over to you my Martha’s Vineyard plantation, boys; including wife and children, boys. Lay me on—lay me on! O Lord, Lord! but I shall go stark, staring mad! See! see that white water!” And so shouting, he pulled his hat from his head, and stamped up and down on it; then picking it up, flirted it far off upon the sea; and finally fell to rearing and plunging in the boat’s stern like a crazed colt from the prairie.\n\n“Look at that chap now,” philosophically drawled Stubb, who, with his unlighted short pipe, mechanically retained between his teeth, at a short distance, followed after—“He’s got fits, that Flask has. Fits? yes, give him fits—that’s the very word—pitch fits into ’em. Merrily, merrily, hearts-alive. Pudding for supper, you know;—merry’s the word. Pull, babes—pull, sucklings—pull, all. But what the devil are you hurrying about? Softly, softly, and steadily, my men. Only pull, and keep pulling; nothing more. Crack all your backbones, and bite your knives in two—that’s all. Take it easy—why don’t ye take it easy, I say, and burst all your livers and lungs!”\n\nBut what it was that inscrutable Ahab said to that tiger-yellow crew of his—these were words best omitted here; for you live under the blessed light of the evangelical land. Only the infidel sharks in the audacious seas may give ear to such words, when, with tornado brow, and eyes of red murder, and foam-glued lips, Ahab leaped after his prey.\n\nMeanwhile, all the boats tore on. The repeated specific allusions of Flask to “that whale,” as he called the fictitious monster which he declared to be incessantly tantalizing his boat’s bow with its tail—these allusions of his were at times so vivid and life-like, that they would cause some one or two of his men to snatch a fearful look over the shoulder. But this was against all rule; for the oarsmen must put out their eyes, and ram a skewer through their necks; usage pronouncing that they must have no organs but ears, and no limbs but arms, in these critical moments.\n\nIt was a sight full of quick wonder and awe! The vast swells of the omnipotent sea; the surging, hollow roar they made, as they rolled along the eight gunwales, like gigantic bowls in a boundless bowling-green; the brief suspended agony of the boat, as it would tip for an instant on the knife-like edge of the sharper waves, that almost seemed threatening to cut it in two; the sudden profound dip into the watery glens and hollows; the keen spurrings and goadings to gain the top of the opposite hill; the headlong, sled-like slide down its other side;—all these, with the cries of the headsmen and harpooneers, and the shuddering gasps of the oarsmen, with the wondrous sight of the ivory Pequod bearing down upon her boats with outstretched sails, like a wild hen after her screaming brood;—all this was thrilling.\n\nNot the raw recruit, marching from the bosom of his wife into the fever heat of his first battle; not the dead man’s ghost encountering the first unknown phantom in the other world;—neither of these can feel stranger and stronger emotions than that man does, who for the first time finds himself pulling into the charmed, churned circle of the hunted sperm whale.\n\nThe dancing white water made by the chase was now becoming more and more visible, owing to the increasing darkness of the dun cloud-shadows flung upon the sea. The jets of vapor no longer blended, but tilted everywhere to right and left; the whales seemed separating their wakes. The boats were pulled more apart; Starbuck giving chase to three whales running dead to leeward. Our sail was now set, and, with the still rising wind, we rushed along; the boat going with such madness through the water, that the lee oars could scarcely be worked rapidly enough to escape being torn from the row-locks.\n\nSoon we were running through a suffusing wide veil of mist; neither ship nor boat to be seen.\n\n“Give way, men,” whispered Starbuck, drawing still further aft the sheet of his sail; “there is time to kill a fish yet before the squall comes. There’s white water again!—close to! Spring!”\n\nSoon after, two cries in quick succession on each side of us denoted that the other boats had got fast; but hardly were they overheard, when with a lightning-like hurtling whisper Starbuck said: “Stand up!” and Queequeg, harpoon in hand, sprang to his feet.\n\nThough not one of the oarsmen was then facing the life and death peril so close to them ahead, yet with their eyes on the intense countenance of the mate in the stern of the boat, they knew that the imminent instant had come; they heard, too, an enormous wallowing sound as of fifty elephants stirring in their litter. Meanwhile the boat was still booming through the mist, the waves curling and hissing around us like the erected crests of enraged serpents.\n\n“That’s his hump. There, there, give it to him!” whispered Starbuck.\n\nA short rushing sound leaped out of the boat; it was the darted iron of Queequeg. Then all in one welded commotion came an invisible push from astern, while forward the boat seemed striking on a ledge; the sail collapsed and exploded; a gush of scalding vapor shot up near by; something rolled and tumbled like an earthquake beneath us. The whole crew were half suffocated as they were tossed helter-skelter into the white curdling cream of the squall. Squall, whale, and harpoon had all blended together; and the whale, merely grazed by the iron, escaped.\n\nThough completely swamped, the boat was nearly unharmed. Swimming round it we picked up the floating oars, and lashing them across the gunwale, tumbled back to our places. There we sat up to our knees in the sea, the water covering every rib and plank, so that to our downward gazing eyes the suspended craft seemed a coral boat grown up to us from the bottom of the ocean.\n\nThe wind increased to a howl; the waves dashed their bucklers together; the whole squall roared, forked, and crackled around us like a white fire upon the prairie, in which, unconsumed, we were burning; immortal in these jaws of death! In vain we hailed the other boats; as well roar to the live coals down the chimney of a flaming furnace as hail those boats in that storm. Meanwhile the driving scud, rack, and mist, grew darker with the shadows of night; no sign of the ship could be seen. The rising sea forbade all attempts to bale out the boat. The oars were useless as propellers, performing now the office of life-preservers. So, cutting the lashing of the waterproof match keg, after many failures Starbuck contrived to ignite the lamp in the lantern; then stretching it on a waif pole, handed it to Queequeg as the standard-bearer of this forlorn hope. There, then, he sat, holding up that imbecile candle in the heart of that almighty forlornness. There, then, he sat, the sign and symbol of a man without faith, hopelessly holding up hope in the midst of despair.\n\nWet, drenched through, and shivering cold, despairing of ship or boat, we lifted up our eyes as the dawn came on. The mist still spread over the sea, the empty lantern lay crushed in the bottom of the boat. Suddenly Queequeg started to his feet, hollowing his hand to his ear. We all heard a faint creaking, as of ropes and yards hitherto muffled by the storm. The sound came nearer and nearer; the thick mists were dimly parted by a huge, vague form. Affrighted, we all sprang into the sea as the ship at last loomed into view, bearing right down upon us within a distance of not much more than its length.\n\nFloating on the waves we saw the abandoned boat, as for one instant it tossed and gaped beneath the ship’s bows like a chip at the base of a cataract; and then the vast hull rolled over it, and it was seen no more till it came up weltering astern. Again we swam for it, were dashed against it by the seas, and were at last taken up and safely landed on board. Ere the squall came close to, the other boats had cut loose from their fish and returned to the ship in good time. The ship had given us up, but was still cruising, if haply it might light upon some token of our perishing,—an oar or a lance pole.\n\n# CHAPTER 49. The Hyena.\n\nThere are certain queer times and occasions in this strange mixed affair we call life when a man takes this whole universe for a vast practical joke, though the wit thereof he but dimly discerns, and more than suspects that the joke is at nobody’s expense but his own. However, nothing dispirits, and nothing seems worth while disputing. He bolts down all events, all creeds, and beliefs, and persuasions, all hard things visible and invisible, never mind how knobby; as an ostrich of potent digestion gobbles down bullets and gun flints. And as for small difficulties and worryings, prospects of sudden disaster, peril of life and limb; all these, and death itself, seem to him only sly, good-natured hits, and jolly punches in the side bestowed by the unseen and unaccountable old joker. That odd sort of wayward mood I am speaking of, comes over a man only in some time of extreme tribulation; it comes in the very midst of his earnestness, so that what just before might have seemed to him a thing most momentous, now seems but a part of the general joke. There is nothing like the perils of whaling to breed this free and easy sort of genial, desperado philosophy; and with it I now regarded this whole voyage of the Pequod, and the great White Whale its object.\n\n“Queequeg,” said I, when they had dragged me, the last man, to the deck, and I was still shaking myself in my jacket to fling off the water; “Queequeg, my fine friend, does this sort of thing often happen?” Without much emotion, though soaked through just like me, he gave me to understand that such things did often happen.\n\n“Mr. Stubb,” said I, turning to that worthy, who, buttoned up in his oil-jacket, was now calmly smoking his pipe in the rain; “Mr. Stubb, I think I have heard you say that of all whalemen you ever met, our chief mate, Mr. Starbuck, is by far the most careful and prudent. I suppose then, that going plump on a flying whale with your sail set in a foggy squall is the height of a whaleman’s discretion?”\n\n“Certain. I’ve lowered for whales from a leaking ship in a gale off Cape Horn.”\n\n“Mr. Flask,” said I, turning to little King-Post, who was standing close by; “you are experienced in these things, and I am not. Will you tell me whether it is an unalterable law in this fishery, Mr. Flask, for an oarsman to break his own back pulling himself back-foremost into death’s jaws?”\n\n“Can’t you twist that smaller?” said Flask. “Yes, that’s the law. I should like to see a boat’s crew backing water up to a whale face foremost. Ha, ha! the whale would give them squint for squint, mind that!”\n\nHere then, from three impartial witnesses, I had a deliberate statement of the entire case. Considering, therefore, that squalls and capsizings in the water and consequent bivouacks on the deep, were matters of common occurrence in this kind of life; considering that at the superlatively critical instant of going on to the whale I must resign my life into the hands of him who steered the boat—oftentimes a fellow who at that very moment is in his impetuousness upon the point of scuttling the craft with his own frantic stampings; considering that the particular disaster to our own particular boat was chiefly to be imputed to Starbuck’s driving on to his whale almost in the teeth of a squall, and considering that Starbuck, notwithstanding, was famous for his great heedfulness in the fishery; considering that I belonged to this uncommonly prudent Starbuck’s boat; and finally considering in what a devil’s chase I was implicated, touching the White Whale: taking all things together, I say, I thought I might as well go below and make a rough draft of my will. “Queequeg,” said I, “come along, you shall be my lawyer, executor, and legatee.”\n\nIt may seem strange that of all men sailors should be tinkering at their last wills and testaments, but there are no people in the world more fond of that diversion. This was the fourth time in my nautical life that I had done the same thing. After the ceremony was concluded upon the present occasion, I felt all the easier; a stone was rolled away from my heart. Besides, all the days I should now live would be as good as the days that Lazarus lived after his resurrection; a supplementary clean gain of so many months or weeks as the case might be. I survived myself; my death and burial were locked up in my chest. I looked round me tranquilly and contentedly, like a quiet ghost with a clean conscience sitting inside the bars of a snug family vault.\n\nNow then, thought I, unconsciously rolling up the sleeves of my frock, here goes for a cool, collected dive at death and destruction, and the devil fetch the hindmost.\n\n# CHAPTER 50. Ahab’s Boat and Crew. Fedallah.\n\n“Who would have thought it, Flask!” cried Stubb; “if I had but one leg you would not catch me in a boat, unless maybe to stop the plug-hole with my timber toe. Oh! he’s a wonderful old man!”\n\n“I don’t think it so strange, after all, on that account,” said Flask. “If his leg were off at the hip, now, it would be a different thing. That would disable him; but he has one knee, and good part of the other left, you know.”\n\n“I don’t know that, my little man; I never yet saw him kneel.”\n\nAmong whale-wise people it has often been argued whether, considering the paramount importance of his life to the success of the voyage, it is right for a whaling captain to jeopardize that life in the active perils of the chase. So Tamerlane’s soldiers often argued with tears in their eyes, whether that invaluable life of his ought to be carried into the thickest of the fight.\n\nBut with Ahab the question assumed a modified aspect. Considering that with two legs man is but a hobbling wight in all times of danger; considering that the pursuit of whales is always under great and extraordinary difficulties; that every individual moment, indeed, then comprises a peril; under these circumstances is it wise for any maimed man to enter a whale-boat in the hunt? As a general thing, the joint-owners of the Pequod must have plainly thought not.\n\nAhab well knew that although his friends at home would think little of his entering a boat in certain comparatively harmless vicissitudes of the chase, for the sake of being near the scene of action and giving his orders in person, yet for Captain Ahab to have a boat actually apportioned to him as a regular headsman in the hunt—above all for Captain Ahab to be supplied with five extra men, as that same boat’s crew, he well knew that such generous conceits never entered the heads of the owners of the Pequod. Therefore he had not solicited a boat’s crew from them, nor had he in any way hinted his desires on that head. Nevertheless he had taken private measures of his own touching all that matter. Until Cabaco’s published discovery, the sailors had little foreseen it, though to be sure when, after being a little while out of port, all hands had concluded the customary business of fitting the whaleboats for service; when some time after this Ahab was now and then found bestirring himself in the matter of making thole-pins with his own hands for what was thought to be one of the spare boats, and even solicitously cutting the small wooden skewers, which when the line is running out are pinned over the groove in the bow: when all this was observed in him, and particularly his solicitude in having an extra coat of sheathing in the bottom of the boat, as if to make it better withstand the pointed pressure of his ivory limb; and also the anxiety he evinced in exactly shaping the thigh board, or clumsy cleat, as it is sometimes called, the horizontal piece in the boat’s bow for bracing the knee against in darting or stabbing at the whale; when it was observed how often he stood up in that boat with his solitary knee fixed in the semi-circular depression in the cleat, and with the carpenter’s chisel gouged out a little here and straightened it a little there; all these things, I say, had awakened much interest and curiosity at the time. But almost everybody supposed that this particular preparative heedfulness in Ahab must only be with a view to the ultimate chase of Moby Dick; for he had already revealed his intention to hunt that mortal monster in person. But such a supposition did by no means involve the remotest suspicion as to any boat’s crew being assigned to that boat.\n\nNow, with the subordinate phantoms, what wonder remained soon waned away; for in a whaler wonders soon wane. Besides, now and then such unaccountable odds and ends of strange nations come up from the unknown nooks and ash-holes of the earth to man these floating outlaws of whalers; and the ships themselves often pick up such queer castaway creatures found tossing about the open sea on planks, bits of wreck, oars, whaleboats, canoes, blown-off Japanese junks, and what not; that Beelzebub himself might climb up the side and step down into the cabin to chat with the captain, and it would not create any unsubduable excitement in the forecastle.\n\nBut be all this as it may, certain it is that while the subordinate phantoms soon found their place among the crew, though still as it were somehow distinct from them, yet that hair-turbaned Fedallah remained a muffled mystery to the last. Whence he came in a mannerly world like this, by what sort of unaccountable tie he soon evinced himself to be linked with Ahab’s peculiar fortunes; nay, so far as to have some sort of a half-hinted influence; Heaven knows, but it might have been even authority over him; all this none knew. But one cannot sustain an indifferent air concerning Fedallah. He was such a creature as civilized, domestic people in the temperate zone only see in their dreams, and that but dimly; but the like of whom now and then glide among the unchanging Asiatic communities, especially the Oriental isles to the east of the continent—those insulated, immemorial, unalterable countries, which even in these modern days still preserve much of the ghostly aboriginalness of earth’s primal generations, when the memory of the first man was a distinct recollection, and all men his descendants, unknowing whence he came, eyed each other as real phantoms, and asked of the sun and the moon why they were created and to what end; when though, according to Genesis, the angels indeed consorted with the daughters of men, the devils also, add the uncanonical Rabbins, indulged in mundane amours.\n\n# CHAPTER 51. The Spirit-Spout.\n\nDays, weeks passed, and under easy sail, the ivory Pequod had slowly swept across four several cruising-grounds; that off the Azores; off the Cape de Verdes; on the Plate (so called), being off the mouth of the Rio de la Plata; and the Carrol Ground, an unstaked, watery locality, southerly from St. Helena.\n\nIt was while gliding through these latter waters that one serene and moonlight night, when all the waves rolled by like scrolls of silver; and, by their soft, suffusing seethings, made what seemed a silvery silence, not a solitude; on such a silent night a silvery jet was seen far in advance of the white bubbles at the bow. Lit up by the moon, it looked celestial; seemed some plumed and glittering god uprising from the sea. Fedallah first descried this jet. For of these moonlight nights, it was his wont to mount to the main-mast head, and stand a look-out there, with the same precision as if it had been day. And yet, though herds of whales were seen by night, not one whaleman in a hundred would venture a lowering for them. You may think with what emotions, then, the seamen beheld this old Oriental perched aloft at such unusual hours; his turban and the moon, companions in one sky. But when, after spending his uniform interval there for several successive nights without uttering a single sound; when, after all this silence, his unearthly voice was heard announcing that silvery, moon-lit jet, every reclining mariner started to his feet as if some winged spirit had lighted in the rigging, and hailed the mortal crew. “There she blows!” Had the trump of judgment blown, they could not have quivered more; yet still they felt no terror; rather pleasure. For though it was a most unwonted hour, yet so impressive was the cry, and so deliriously exciting, that almost every soul on board instinctively desired a lowering.\n\nWalking the deck with quick, side-lunging strides, Ahab commanded the t’gallant sails and royals to be set, and every stunsail spread. The best man in the ship must take the helm. Then, with every mast-head manned, the piled-up craft rolled down before the wind. The strange, upheaving, lifting tendency of the taffrail breeze filling the hollows of so many sails, made the buoyant, hovering deck to feel like air beneath the feet; while still she rushed along, as if two antagonistic influences were struggling in her—one to mount direct to heaven, the other to drive yawingly to some horizontal goal. And had you watched Ahab’s face that night, you would have thought that in him also two different things were warring. While his one live leg made lively echoes along the deck, every stroke of his dead limb sounded like a coffin-tap. On life and death this old man walked. But though the ship so swiftly sped, and though from every eye, like arrows, the eager glances shot, yet the silvery jet was no more seen that night. Every sailor swore he saw it once, but not a second time.\n\nThis midnight-spout had almost grown a forgotten thing, when, some days after, lo! at the same silent hour, it was again announced: again it was descried by all; but upon making sail to overtake it, once more it disappeared as if it had never been. And so it served us night after night, till no one heeded it but to wonder at it. Mysteriously jetted into the clear moonlight, or starlight, as the case might be; disappearing again for one whole day, or two days, or three; and somehow seeming at every distinct repetition to be advancing still further and further in our van, this solitary jet seemed for ever alluring us on.\n\nNor with the immemorial superstition of their race, and in accordance with the preternaturalness, as it seemed, which in many things invested the Pequod, were there wanting some of the seamen who swore that whenever and wherever descried; at however remote times, or in however far apart latitudes and longitudes, that unnearable spout was cast by one self-same whale; and that whale, Moby Dick. For a time, there reigned, too, a sense of peculiar dread at this flitting apparition, as if it were treacherously beckoning us on and on, in order that the monster might turn round upon us, and rend us at last in the remotest and most savage seas.\n\nThese temporary apprehensions, so vague but so awful, derived a wondrous potency from the contrasting serenity of the weather, in which, beneath all its blue blandness, some thought there lurked a devilish charm, as for days and days we voyaged along, through seas so wearily, lonesomely mild, that all space, in repugnance to our vengeful errand, seemed vacating itself of life before our urn-like prow.\n\nBut, at last, when turning to the eastward, the Cape winds began howling around us, and we rose and fell upon the long, troubled seas that are there; when the ivory-tusked Pequod sharply bowed to the blast, and gored the dark waves in her madness, till, like showers of silver chips, the foam-flakes flew over her bulwarks; then all this desolate vacuity of life went away, but gave place to sights more dismal than before.\n\nClose to our bows, strange forms in the water darted hither and thither before us; while thick in our rear flew the inscrutable sea-ravens. And every morning, perched on our stays, rows of these birds were seen; and spite of our hootings, for a long time obstinately clung to the hemp, as though they deemed our ship some drifting, uninhabited craft; a thing appointed to desolation, and therefore fit roosting-place for their homeless selves. And heaved and heaved, still unrestingly heaved the black sea, as if its vast tides were a conscience; and the great mundane soul were in anguish and remorse for the long sin and suffering it had bred.\n\nCape of Good Hope, do they call ye? Rather Cape Tormentoso, as called of yore; for long allured by the perfidious silences that before had attended us, we found ourselves launched into this tormented sea, where guilty beings transformed into those fowls and these fish, seemed condemned to swim on everlastingly without any haven in store, or beat that black air without any horizon. But calm, snow-white, and unvarying; still directing its fountain of feathers to the sky; still beckoning us on from before, the solitary jet would at times be descried.\n\nDuring all this blackness of the elements, Ahab, though assuming for the time the almost continual command of the drenched and dangerous deck, manifested the gloomiest reserve; and more seldom than ever addressed his mates. In tempestuous times like these, after everything above and aloft has been secured, nothing more can be done but passively to await the issue of the gale. Then Captain and crew become practical fatalists. So, with his ivory leg inserted into its accustomed hole, and with one hand firmly grasping a shroud, Ahab for hours and hours would stand gazing dead to windward, while an occasional squall of sleet or snow would all but congeal his very eyelashes together. Meantime, the crew driven from the forward part of the ship by the perilous seas that burstingly broke over its bows, stood in a line along the bulwarks in the waist; and the better to guard against the leaping waves, each man had slipped himself into a sort of bowline secured to the rail, in which he swung as in a loosened belt. Few or no words were spoken; and the silent ship, as if manned by painted sailors in wax, day after day tore on through all the swift madness and gladness of the demoniac waves. By night the same muteness of humanity before the shrieks of the ocean prevailed; still in silence the men swung in the bowlines; still wordless Ahab stood up to the blast. Even when wearied nature seemed demanding repose he would not seek that repose in his hammock. Never could Starbuck forget the old man’s aspect, when one night going down into the cabin to mark how the barometer stood, he saw him with closed eyes sitting straight in his floor-screwed chair; the rain and half-melted sleet of the storm from which he had some time before emerged, still slowly dripping from the unremoved hat and coat. On the table beside him lay unrolled one of those charts of tides and currents which have previously been spoken of. His lantern swung from his tightly clenched hand. Though the body was erect, the head was thrown back so that the closed eyes were pointed towards the needle of the tell-tale that swung from a beam in the ceiling.*\n\n*The cabin-compass is called the tell-tale, because without going to the compass at the helm, the Captain, while below, can inform himself of the course of the ship.\n\nTerrible old man! thought Starbuck with a shudder, sleeping in this gale, still thou steadfastly eyest thy purpose.\n\n# CHAPTER 52. The Albatross.\n\nSouth-eastward from the Cape, off the distant Crozetts, a good cruising ground for Right Whalemen, a sail loomed ahead, the Goney (Albatross) by name. As she slowly drew nigh, from my lofty perch at the fore-mast-head, I had a good view of that sight so remarkable to a tyro in the far ocean fisheries—a whaler at sea, and long absent from home.\n\nAs if the waves had been fullers, this craft was bleached like the skeleton of a stranded walrus. All down her sides, this spectral appearance was traced with long channels of reddened rust, while all her spars and her rigging were like the thick branches of trees furred over with hoar-frost. Only her lower sails were set. A wild sight it was to see her long-bearded look-outs at those three mast-heads. They seemed clad in the skins of beasts, so torn and bepatched the raiment that had survived nearly four years of cruising. Standing in iron hoops nailed to the mast, they swayed and swung over a fathomless sea; and though, when the ship slowly glided close under our stern, we six men in the air came so nigh to each other that we might almost have leaped from the mast-heads of one ship to those of the other; yet, those forlorn-looking fishermen, mildly eyeing us as they passed, said not one word to our own look-outs, while the quarter-deck hail was being heard from below.\n\n“Ship ahoy! Have ye seen the White Whale?”\n\nBut as the strange captain, leaning over the pallid bulwarks, was in the act of putting his trumpet to his mouth, it somehow fell from his hand into the sea; and the wind now rising amain, he in vain strove to make himself heard without it. Meantime his ship was still increasing the distance between. While in various silent ways the seamen of the Pequod were evincing their observance of this ominous incident at the first mere mention of the White Whale’s name to another ship, Ahab for a moment paused; it almost seemed as though he would have lowered a boat to board the stranger, had not the threatening wind forbade. But taking advantage of his windward position, he again seized his trumpet, and knowing by her aspect that the stranger vessel was a Nantucketer and shortly bound home, he loudly hailed—“Ahoy there! This is the Pequod, bound round the world! Tell them to address all future letters to the Pacific ocean! and this time three years, if I am not at home, tell them to address them to ——”\n\nAt that moment the two wakes were fairly crossed, and instantly, then, in accordance with their singular ways, shoals of small harmless fish, that for some days before had been placidly swimming by our side, darted away with what seemed shuddering fins, and ranged themselves fore and aft with the stranger’s flanks. Though in the course of his continual voyagings Ahab must often before have noticed a similar sight, yet, to any monomaniac man, the veriest trifles capriciously carry meanings.\n\n“Swim away from me, do ye?” murmured Ahab, gazing over into the water. There seemed but little in the words, but the tone conveyed more of deep helpless sadness than the insane old man had ever before evinced. But turning to the steersman, who thus far had been holding the ship in the wind to diminish her headway, he cried out in his old lion voice,—“Up helm! Keep her off round the world!”\n\nRound the world! There is much in that sound to inspire proud feelings; but whereto does all that circumnavigation conduct? Only through numberless perils to the very point whence we started, where those that we left behind secure, were all the time before us.\n\nWere this world an endless plain, and by sailing eastward we could for ever reach new distances, and discover sights more sweet and strange than any Cyclades or Islands of King Solomon, then there were promise in the voyage. But in pursuit of those far mysteries we dream of, or in tormented chase of that demon phantom that, some time or other, swims before all human hearts; while chasing such over this round globe, they either lead us on in barren mazes or midway leave us whelmed.\n\n# CHAPTER 53. The Gam.\n\nThe ostensible reason why Ahab did not go on board of the whaler we had spoken was this: the wind and sea betokened storms. But even had this not been the case, he would not after all, perhaps, have boarded her—judging by his subsequent conduct on similar occasions—if so it had been that, by the process of hailing, he had obtained a negative answer to the question he put. For, as it eventually turned out, he cared not to consort, even for five minutes, with any stranger captain, except he could contribute some of that information he so absorbingly sought. But all this might remain inadequately estimated, were not something said here of the peculiar usages of whaling-vessels when meeting each other in foreign seas, and especially on a common cruising-ground.\n\nIf two strangers crossing the Pine Barrens in New York State, or the equally desolate Salisbury Plain in England; if casually encountering each other in such inhospitable wilds, these twain, for the life of them, cannot well avoid a mutual salutation; and stopping for a moment to interchange the news; and, perhaps, sitting down for a while and resting in concert: then, how much more natural that upon the illimitable Pine Barrens and Salisbury Plains of the sea, two whaling vessels descrying each other at the ends of the earth—off lone Fanning’s Island, or the far away King’s Mills; how much more natural, I say, that under such circumstances these ships should not only interchange hails, but come into still closer, more friendly and sociable contact. And especially would this seem to be a matter of course, in the case of vessels owned in one seaport, and whose captains, officers, and not a few of the men are personally known to each other; and consequently, have all sorts of dear domestic things to talk about.\n\nFor the long absent ship, the outward-bounder, perhaps, has letters on board; at any rate, she will be sure to let her have some papers of a date a year or two later than the last one on her blurred and thumb-worn files. And in return for that courtesy, the outward-bound ship would receive the latest whaling intelligence from the cruising-ground to which she may be destined, a thing of the utmost importance to her. And in degree, all this will hold true concerning whaling vessels crossing each other’s track on the cruising-ground itself, even though they are equally long absent from home. For one of them may have received a transfer of letters from some third, and now far remote vessel; and some of those letters may be for the people of the ship she now meets. Besides, they would exchange the whaling news, and have an agreeable chat. For not only would they meet with all the sympathies of sailors, but likewise with all the peculiar congenialities arising from a common pursuit and mutually shared privations and perils.\n\nNor would difference of country make any very essential difference; that is, so long as both parties speak one language, as is the case with Americans and English. Though, to be sure, from the small number of English whalers, such meetings do not very often occur, and when they do occur there is too apt to be a sort of shyness between them; for your Englishman is rather reserved, and your Yankee, he does not fancy that sort of thing in anybody but himself. Besides, the English whalers sometimes affect a kind of metropolitan superiority over the American whalers; regarding the long, lean Nantucketer, with his nondescript provincialisms, as a sort of sea-peasant. But where this superiority in the English whalemen does really consist, it would be hard to say, seeing that the Yankees in one day, collectively, kill more whales than all the English, collectively, in ten years. But this is a harmless little foible in the English whale-hunters, which the Nantucketer does not take much to heart; probably, because he knows that he has a few foibles himself.\n\nSo, then, we see that of all ships separately sailing the sea, the whalers have most reason to be sociable—and they are so. Whereas, some merchant ships crossing each other’s wake in the mid-Atlantic, will oftentimes pass on without so much as a single word of recognition, mutually cutting each other on the high seas, like a brace of dandies in Broadway; and all the time indulging, perhaps, in finical criticism upon each other’s rig. As for Men-of-War, when they chance to meet at sea, they first go through such a string of silly bowings and scrapings, such a ducking of ensigns, that there does not seem to be much right-down hearty good-will and brotherly love about it at all. As touching Slave-ships meeting, why, they are in such a prodigious hurry, they run away from each other as soon as possible. And as for Pirates, when they chance to cross each other’s cross-bones, the first hail is—“How many skulls?”—the same way that whalers hail—“How many barrels?” And that question once answered, pirates straightway steer apart, for they are infernal villains on both sides, and don’t like to see overmuch of each other’s villanous likenesses.\n\nBut look at the godly, honest, unostentatious, hospitable, sociable, free-and-easy whaler! What does the whaler do when she meets another whaler in any sort of decent weather? She has a “Gam,” a thing so utterly unknown to all other ships that they never heard of the name even; and if by chance they should hear of it, they only grin at it, and repeat gamesome stuff about “spouters” and “blubber-boilers,” and such like pretty exclamations. Why it is that all Merchant-seamen, and also all Pirates and Man-of-War’s men, and Slave-ship sailors, cherish such a scornful feeling towards Whale-ships; this is a question it would be hard to answer. Because, in the case of pirates, say, I should like to know whether that profession of theirs has any peculiar glory about it. It sometimes ends in uncommon elevation, indeed; but only at the gallows. And besides, when a man is elevated in that odd fashion, he has no proper foundation for his superior altitude. Hence, I conclude, that in boasting himself to be high lifted above a whaleman, in that assertion the pirate has no solid basis to stand on.\n\nBut what is a Gam? You might wear out your index-finger running up and down the columns of dictionaries, and never find the word. Dr. Johnson never attained to that erudition; Noah Webster’s ark does not hold it. Nevertheless, this same expressive word has now for many years been in constant use among some fifteen thousand true born Yankees. Certainly, it needs a definition, and should be incorporated into the Lexicon. With that view, let me learnedly define it.\n\nGAM. NOUN—A social meeting of two (or more) Whaleships, generally on a cruising-ground; when, after exchanging hails, they exchange visits by boats’ crews: the two captains remaining, for the time, on board of one ship, and the two chief mates on the other.\n\nThere is another little item about Gamming which must not be forgotten here. All professions have their own little peculiarities of detail; so has the whale fishery. In a pirate, man-of-war, or slave ship, when the captain is rowed anywhere in his boat, he always sits in the stern sheets on a comfortable, sometimes cushioned seat there, and often steers himself with a pretty little milliner’s tiller decorated with gay cords and ribbons. But the whale-boat has no seat astern, no sofa of that sort whatever, and no tiller at all. High times indeed, if whaling captains were wheeled about the water on castors like gouty old aldermen in patent chairs. And as for a tiller, the whale-boat never admits of any such effeminacy; and therefore as in gamming a complete boat’s crew must leave the ship, and hence as the boat steerer or harpooneer is of the number, that subordinate is the steersman upon the occasion, and the captain, having no place to sit in, is pulled off to his visit all standing like a pine tree. And often you will notice that being conscious of the eyes of the whole visible world resting on him from the sides of the two ships, this standing captain is all alive to the importance of sustaining his dignity by maintaining his legs. Nor is this any very easy matter; for in his rear is the immense projecting steering oar hitting him now and then in the small of his back, the after-oar reciprocating by rapping his knees in front. He is thus completely wedged before and behind, and can only expand himself sideways by settling down on his stretched legs; but a sudden, violent pitch of the boat will often go far to topple him, because length of foundation is nothing without corresponding breadth. Merely make a spread angle of two poles, and you cannot stand them up. Then, again, it would never do in plain sight of the world’s riveted eyes, it would never do, I say, for this straddling captain to be seen steadying himself the slightest particle by catching hold of anything with his hands; indeed, as token of his entire, buoyant self-command, he generally carries his hands in his trowsers’ pockets; but perhaps being generally very large, heavy hands, he carries them there for ballast. Nevertheless there have occurred instances, well authenticated ones too, where the captain has been known for an uncommonly critical moment or two, in a sudden squall say—to seize hold of the nearest oarsman’s hair, and hold on there like grim death.\n\n(As told at the Golden Inn.)\nThe Cape of Good Hope, and all the watery region round about there, is much like some noted four corners of a great highway, where you meet more travellers than in any other part.\n\nIt was not very long after speaking the Goney that another homeward-bound whaleman, the Town-Ho,* was encountered. She was manned almost wholly by Polynesians. In the short gam that ensued she gave us strong news of Moby Dick. To some the general interest in the White Whale was now wildly heightened by a circumstance of the Town-Ho’s story, which seemed obscurely to involve with the whale a certain wondrous, inverted visitation of one of those so called judgments of God which at times are said to overtake some men. This latter circumstance, with its own particular accompaniments, forming what may be called the secret part of the tragedy about to be narrated, never reached the ears of Captain Ahab or his mates. For that secret part of the story was unknown to the captain of the Town-Ho himself. It was the private property of three confederate white seamen of that ship, one of whom, it seems, communicated it to Tashtego with Romish injunctions of secrecy, but the following night Tashtego rambled in his sleep, and revealed so much of it in that way, that when he was wakened he could not well withhold the rest. Nevertheless, so potent an influence did this thing have on those seamen in the Pequod who came to the full knowledge of it, and by such a strange delicacy, to call it so, were they governed in this matter, that they kept the secret among themselves so that it never transpired abaft the Pequod’s main-mast. Interweaving in its proper place this darker thread with the story as publicly narrated on the ship, the whole of this strange affair I now proceed to put on lasting record.\n\n*The ancient whale-cry upon first sighting a whale from the mast-head, still used by whalemen in hunting the famous Gallipagos terrapin.\n\nFor my humor’s sake, I shall preserve the style in which I once narrated it at Lima, to a lounging circle of my Spanish friends, one saint’s eve, smoking upon the thick-gilt tiled piazza of the Golden Inn. Of those fine cavaliers, the young Dons, Pedro and Sebastian, were on the closer terms with me; and hence the interluding questions they occasionally put, and which are duly answered at the time.\n\n“Some two years prior to my first learning the events which I am about rehearsing to you, gentlemen, the Town-Ho, Sperm Whaler of Nantucket, was cruising in your Pacific here, not very many days’ sail eastward from the eaves of this good Golden Inn. She was somewhere to the northward of the Line. One morning upon handling the pumps, according to daily usage, it was observed that she made more water in her hold than common. They supposed a sword-fish had stabbed her, gentlemen. But the captain, having some unusual reason for believing that rare good luck awaited him in those latitudes; and therefore being very averse to quit them, and the leak not being then considered at all dangerous, though, indeed, they could not find it after searching the hold as low down as was possible in rather heavy weather, the ship still continued her cruisings, the mariners working at the pumps at wide and easy intervals; but no good luck came; more days went by, and not only was the leak yet undiscovered, but it sensibly increased. So much so, that now taking some alarm, the captain, making all sail, stood away for the nearest harbor among the islands, there to have his hull hove out and repaired.\n\n“Though no small passage was before her, yet, if the commonest chance favoured, he did not at all fear that his ship would founder by the way, because his pumps were of the best, and being periodically relieved at them, those six-and-thirty men of his could easily keep the ship free; never mind if the leak should double on her. In truth, well nigh the whole of this passage being attended by very prosperous breezes, the Town-Ho had all but certainly arrived in perfect safety at her port without the occurrence of the least fatality, had it not been for the brutal overbearing of Radney, the mate, a Vineyarder, and the bitterly provoked vengeance of Steelkilt, a Lakeman and desperado from Buffalo.\n\n“‘Lakeman!—Buffalo! Pray, what is a Lakeman, and where is Buffalo?’ said Don Sebastian, rising in his swinging mat of grass.\n\n“On the eastern shore of our Lake Erie, Don; but—I crave your courtesy—may be, you shall soon hear further of all that. Now, gentlemen, in square-sail brigs and three-masted ships, well-nigh as large and stout as any that ever sailed out of your old Callao to far Manilla; this Lakeman, in the land-locked heart of our America, had yet been nurtured by all those agrarian freebooting impressions popularly connected with the open ocean. For in their interflowing aggregate, those grand fresh-water seas of ours,—Erie, and Ontario, and Huron, and Superior, and Michigan,—possess an ocean-like expansiveness, with many of the ocean’s noblest traits; with many of its rimmed varieties of races and of climes. They contain round archipelagoes of romantic isles, even as the Polynesian waters do; in large part, are shored by two great contrasting nations, as the Atlantic is; they furnish long maritime approaches to our numerous territorial colonies from the East, dotted all round their banks; here and there are frowned upon by batteries, and by the goat-like craggy guns of lofty Mackinaw; they have heard the fleet thunderings of naval victories; at intervals, they yield their beaches to wild barbarians, whose red painted faces flash from out their peltry wigwams; for leagues and leagues are flanked by ancient and unentered forests, where the gaunt pines stand like serried lines of kings in Gothic genealogies; those same woods harboring wild Afric beasts of prey, and silken creatures whose exported furs give robes to Tartar Emperors; they mirror the paved capitals of Buffalo and Cleveland, as well as Winnebago villages; they float alike the full-rigged merchant ship, the armed cruiser of the State, the steamer, and the beech canoe; they are swept by Borean and dismasting blasts as direful as any that lash the salted wave; they know what shipwrecks are, for out of sight of land, however inland, they have drowned full many a midnight ship with all its shrieking crew. Thus, gentlemen, though an inlander, Steelkilt was wild-ocean born, and wild-ocean nurtured; as much of an audacious mariner as any. And for Radney, though in his infancy he may have laid him down on the lone Nantucket beach, to nurse at his maternal sea; though in after life he had long followed our austere Atlantic and your contemplative Pacific; yet was he quite as vengeful and full of social quarrel as the backwoods seaman, fresh from the latitudes of buck-horn handled Bowie-knives. Yet was this Nantucketer a man with some good-hearted traits; and this Lakeman, a mariner, who though a sort of devil indeed, might yet by inflexible firmness, only tempered by that common decency of human recognition which is the meanest slave’s right; thus treated, this Steelkilt had long been retained harmless and docile. At all events, he had proved so thus far; but Radney was doomed and made mad, and Steelkilt—but, gentlemen, you shall hear.\n\n“It was not more than a day or two at the furthest after pointing her prow for her island haven, that the Town-Ho’s leak seemed again increasing, but only so as to require an hour or more at the pumps every day. You must know that in a settled and civilized ocean like our Atlantic, for example, some skippers think little of pumping their whole way across it; though of a still, sleepy night, should the officer of the deck happen to forget his duty in that respect, the probability would be that he and his shipmates would never again remember it, on account of all hands gently subsiding to the bottom. Nor in the solitary and savage seas far from you to the westward, gentlemen, is it altogether unusual for ships to keep clanging at their pump-handles in full chorus even for a voyage of considerable length; that is, if it lie along a tolerably accessible coast, or if any other reasonable retreat is afforded them. It is only when a leaky vessel is in some very out of the way part of those waters, some really landless latitude, that her captain begins to feel a little anxious.\n\n“Much this way had it been with the Town-Ho; so when her leak was found gaining once more, there was in truth some small concern manifested by several of her company; especially by Radney the mate. He commanded the upper sails to be well hoisted, sheeted home anew, and every way expanded to the breeze. Now this Radney, I suppose, was as little of a coward, and as little inclined to any sort of nervous apprehensiveness touching his own person as any fearless, unthinking creature on land or on sea that you can conveniently imagine, gentlemen. Therefore when he betrayed this solicitude about the safety of the ship, some of the seamen declared that it was only on account of his being a part owner in her. So when they were working that evening at the pumps, there was on this head no small gamesomeness slily going on among them, as they stood with their feet continually overflowed by the rippling clear water; clear as any mountain spring, gentlemen—that bubbling from the pumps ran across the deck, and poured itself out in steady spouts at the lee scupper-holes.\n\n“Now, as you well know, it is not seldom the case in this conventional world of ours—watery or otherwise; that when a person placed in command over his fellow-men finds one of them to be very significantly his superior in general pride of manhood, straightway against that man he conceives an unconquerable dislike and bitterness; and if he have a chance he will pull down and pulverize that subaltern’s tower, and make a little heap of dust of it. Be this conceit of mine as it may, gentlemen, at all events Steelkilt was a tall and noble animal with a head like a Roman, and a flowing golden beard like the tasseled housings of your last viceroy’s snorting charger; and a brain, and a heart, and a soul in him, gentlemen, which had made Steelkilt Charlemagne, had he been born son to Charlemagne’s father. But Radney, the mate, was ugly as a mule; yet as hardy, as stubborn, as malicious. He did not love Steelkilt, and Steelkilt knew it.\n\n“Espying the mate drawing near as he was toiling at the pump with the rest, the Lakeman affected not to notice him, but unawed, went on with his gay banterings.\n\n“‘Aye, aye, my merry lads, it’s a lively leak this; hold a cannikin, one of ye, and let’s have a taste. By the Lord, it’s worth bottling! I tell ye what, men, old Rad’s investment must go for it! he had best cut away his part of the hull and tow it home. The fact is, boys, that sword-fish only began the job; he’s come back again with a gang of ship-carpenters, saw-fish, and file-fish, and what not; and the whole posse of ’em are now hard at work cutting and slashing at the bottom; making improvements, I suppose. If old Rad were here now, I’d tell him to jump overboard and scatter ’em. They’re playing the devil with his estate, I can tell him. But he’s a simple old soul,—Rad, and a beauty too. Boys, they say the rest of his property is invested in looking-glasses. I wonder if he’d give a poor devil like me the model of his nose.’\n\n“‘Damn your eyes! what’s that pump stopping for?’ roared Radney, pretending not to have heard the sailors’ talk. ‘Thunder away at it!’\n\n“‘Aye, aye, sir,’ said Steelkilt, merry as a cricket. ‘Lively, boys, lively, now!’ And with that the pump clanged like fifty fire-engines; the men tossed their hats off to it, and ere long that peculiar gasping of the lungs was heard which denotes the fullest tension of life’s utmost energies.\n\n“Quitting the pump at last, with the rest of his band, the Lakeman went forward all panting, and sat himself down on the windlass; his face fiery red, his eyes bloodshot, and wiping the profuse sweat from his brow. Now what cozening fiend it was, gentlemen, that possessed Radney to meddle with such a man in that corporeally exasperated state, I know not; but so it happened. Intolerably striding along the deck, the mate commanded him to get a broom and sweep down the planks, and also a shovel, and remove some offensive matters consequent upon allowing a pig to run at large.\n\n“Now, gentlemen, sweeping a ship’s deck at sea is a piece of household work which in all times but raging gales is regularly attended to every evening; it has been known to be done in the case of ships actually foundering at the time. Such, gentlemen, is the inflexibility of sea-usages and the instinctive love of neatness in seamen; some of whom would not willingly drown without first washing their faces. But in all vessels this broom business is the prescriptive province of the boys, if boys there be aboard. Besides, it was the stronger men in the Town-Ho that had been divided into gangs, taking turns at the pumps; and being the most athletic seaman of them all, Steelkilt had been regularly assigned captain of one of the gangs; consequently he should have been freed from any trivial business not connected with truly nautical duties, such being the case with his comrades. I mention all these particulars so that you may understand exactly how this affair stood between the two men.\n\n“But there was more than this: the order about the shovel was almost as plainly meant to sting and insult Steelkilt, as though Radney had spat in his face. Any man who has gone sailor in a whale-ship will understand this; and all this and doubtless much more, the Lakeman fully comprehended when the mate uttered his command. But as he sat still for a moment, and as he steadfastly looked into the mate’s malignant eye and perceived the stacks of powder-casks heaped up in him and the slow-match silently burning along towards them; as he instinctively saw all this, that strange forbearance and unwillingness to stir up the deeper passionateness in any already ireful being—a repugnance most felt, when felt at all, by really valiant men even when aggrieved—this nameless phantom feeling, gentlemen, stole over Steelkilt.\n\n“Therefore, in his ordinary tone, only a little broken by the bodily exhaustion he was temporarily in, he answered him saying that sweeping the deck was not his business, and he would not do it. And then, without at all alluding to the shovel, he pointed to three lads as the customary sweepers; who, not being billeted at the pumps, had done little or nothing all day. To this, Radney replied with an oath, in a most domineering and outrageous manner unconditionally reiterating his command; meanwhile advancing upon the still seated Lakeman, with an uplifted cooper’s club hammer which he had snatched from a cask near by.\n\n“Heated and irritated as he was by his spasmodic toil at the pumps, for all his first nameless feeling of forbearance the sweating Steelkilt could but ill brook this bearing in the mate; but somehow still smothering the conflagration within him, without speaking he remained doggedly rooted to his seat, till at last the incensed Radney shook the hammer within a few inches of his face, furiously commanding him to do his bidding.\n\n“Steelkilt rose, and slowly retreating round the windlass, steadily followed by the mate with his menacing hammer, deliberately repeated his intention not to obey. Seeing, however, that his forbearance had not the slightest effect, by an awful and unspeakable intimation with his twisted hand he warned off the foolish and infatuated man; but it was to no purpose. And in this way the two went once slowly round the windlass; when, resolved at last no longer to retreat, bethinking him that he had now forborne as much as comported with his humor, the Lakeman paused on the hatches and thus spoke to the officer:\n\n“‘Mr. Radney, I will not obey you. Take that hammer away, or look to yourself.’ But the predestinated mate coming still closer to him, where the Lakeman stood fixed, now shook the heavy hammer within an inch of his teeth; meanwhile repeating a string of insufferable maledictions. Retreating not the thousandth part of an inch; stabbing him in the eye with the unflinching poniard of his glance, Steelkilt, clenching his right hand behind him and creepingly drawing it back, told his persecutor that if the hammer but grazed his cheek he (Steelkilt) would murder him. But, gentlemen, the fool had been branded for the slaughter by the gods. Immediately the hammer touched the cheek; the next instant the lower jaw of the mate was stove in his head; he fell on the hatch spouting blood like a whale.\n\n“Ere the cry could go aft Steelkilt was shaking one of the backstays leading far aloft to where two of his comrades were standing their mastheads. They were both Canallers.\n\n“‘Canallers!’ cried Don Pedro. ‘We have seen many whale-ships in our harbours, but never heard of your Canallers. Pardon: who and what are they?’\n\n“‘Canallers, Don, are the boatmen belonging to our grand Erie Canal. You must have heard of it.’\n\n“‘Nay, Senor; hereabouts in this dull, warm, most lazy, and hereditary land, we know but little of your vigorous North.’\n\n“‘Aye? Well then, Don, refill my cup. Your chicha’s very fine; and ere proceeding further I will tell ye what our Canallers are; for such information may throw side-light upon my story.’\n\n“For three hundred and sixty miles, gentlemen, through the entire breadth of the state of New York; through numerous populous cities and most thriving villages; through long, dismal, uninhabited swamps, and affluent, cultivated fields, unrivalled for fertility; by billiard-room and bar-room; through the holy-of-holies of great forests; on Roman arches over Indian rivers; through sun and shade; by happy hearts or broken; through all the wide contrasting scenery of those noble Mohawk counties; and especially, by rows of snow-white chapels, whose spires stand almost like milestones, flows one continual stream of Venetianly corrupt and often lawless life. There’s your true Ashantee, gentlemen; there howl your pagans; where you ever find them, next door to you; under the long-flung shadow, and the snug patronising lee of churches. For by some curious fatality, as it is often noted of your metropolitan freebooters that they ever encamp around the halls of justice, so sinners, gentlemen, most abound in holiest vicinities.\n\n“‘Is that a friar passing?’ said Don Pedro, looking downwards into the crowded plazza, with humorous concern.\n\n“‘Well for our northern friend, Dame Isabella’s Inquisition wanes in Lima,’ laughed Don Sebastian. ‘Proceed, Senor.’\n\n“‘A moment! Pardon!’ cried another of the company. ‘In the name of all us Limeese, I but desire to express to you, sir sailor, that we have by no means overlooked your delicacy in not substituting present Lima for distant Venice in your corrupt comparison. Oh! do not bow and look surprised; you know the proverb all along this coast—“Corrupt as Lima.” It but bears out your saying, too; churches more plentiful than billiard-tables, and for ever open—and “Corrupt as Lima.” So, too, Venice; I have been there; the holy city of the blessed evangelist, St. Mark!—St. Dominic, purge it! Your cup! Thanks: here I refill; now, you pour out again.’\n\n“Freely depicted in his own vocation, gentlemen, the Canaller would make a fine dramatic hero, so abundantly and picturesquely wicked is he. Like Mark Antony, for days and days along his green-turfed, flowery Nile, he indolently floats, openly toying with his red-cheeked Cleopatra, ripening his apricot thigh upon the sunny deck. But ashore, all this effeminacy is dashed. The brigandish guise which the Canaller so proudly sports; his slouched and gaily-ribboned hat betoken his grand features. A terror to the smiling innocence of the villages through which he floats; his swart visage and bold swagger are not unshunned in cities. Once a vagabond on his own canal, I have received good turns from one of these Canallers; I thank him heartily; would fain be not ungrateful; but it is often one of the prime redeeming qualities of your man of violence, that at times he has as stiff an arm to back a poor stranger in a strait, as to plunder a wealthy one. In sum, gentlemen, what the wildness of this canal life is, is emphatically evinced by this; that our wild whale-fishery contains so many of its most finished graduates, and that scarce any race of mankind, except Sydney men, are so much distrusted by our whaling captains. Nor does it at all diminish the curiousness of this matter, that to many thousands of our rural boys and young men born along its line, the probationary life of the Grand Canal furnishes the sole transition between quietly reaping in a Christian corn-field, and recklessly ploughing the waters of the most barbaric seas.\n\n“‘I see! I see!’ impetuously exclaimed Don Pedro, spilling his chicha upon his silvery ruffles. ‘No need to travel! The world’s one Lima. I had thought, now, that at your temperate North the generations were cold and holy as the hills.—But the story.’\n\n“I left off, gentlemen, where the Lakeman shook the backstay. Hardly had he done so, when he was surrounded by the three junior mates and the four harpooneers, who all crowded him to the deck. But sliding down the ropes like baleful comets, the two Canallers rushed into the uproar, and sought to drag their man out of it towards the forecastle. Others of the sailors joined with them in this attempt, and a twisted turmoil ensued; while standing out of harm’s way, the valiant captain danced up and down with a whale-pike, calling upon his officers to manhandle that atrocious scoundrel, and smoke him along to the quarter-deck. At intervals, he ran close up to the revolving border of the confusion, and prying into the heart of it with his pike, sought to prick out the object of his resentment. But Steelkilt and his desperadoes were too much for them all; they succeeded in gaining the forecastle deck, where, hastily slewing about three or four large casks in a line with the windlass, these sea-Parisians entrenched themselves behind the barricade.\n\n“‘Come out of that, ye pirates!’ roared the captain, now menacing them with a pistol in each hand, just brought to him by the steward. ‘Come out of that, ye cut-throats!’\n\n“Steelkilt leaped on the barricade, and striding up and down there, defied the worst the pistols could do; but gave the captain to understand distinctly, that his (Steelkilt’s) death would be the signal for a murderous mutiny on the part of all hands. Fearing in his heart lest this might prove but too true, the captain a little desisted, but still commanded the insurgents instantly to return to their duty.\n\n“‘Will you promise not to touch us, if we do?’ demanded their ringleader.\n\n“‘Turn to! turn to!—I make no promise;—to your duty! Do you want to sink the ship, by knocking off at a time like this? Turn to!’ and he once more raised a pistol.\n\n“‘Sink the ship?’ cried Steelkilt. ‘Aye, let her sink. Not a man of us turns to, unless you swear not to raise a rope-yarn against us. What say ye, men?’ turning to his comrades. A fierce cheer was their response.\n\n“The Lakeman now patrolled the barricade, all the while keeping his eye on the Captain, and jerking out such sentences as these:—‘It’s not our fault; we didn’t want it; I told him to take his hammer away; it was boy’s business; he might have known me before this; I told him not to prick the buffalo; I believe I have broken a finger here against his cursed jaw; ain’t those mincing knives down in the forecastle there, men? look to those handspikes, my hearties. Captain, by God, look to yourself; say the word; don’t be a fool; forget it all; we are ready to turn to; treat us decently, and we’re your men; but we won’t be flogged.’\n\n“‘Turn to! I make no promises, turn to, I say!’\n\n“‘Look ye, now,’ cried the Lakeman, flinging out his arm towards him, ‘there are a few of us here (and I am one of them) who have shipped for the cruise, d’ye see; now as you well know, sir, we can claim our discharge as soon as the anchor is down; so we don’t want a row; it’s not our interest; we want to be peaceable; we are ready to work, but we won’t be flogged.’\n\n“‘Turn to!’ roared the Captain.\n\n“Steelkilt glanced round him a moment, and then said:—‘I tell you what it is now, Captain, rather than kill ye, and be hung for such a shabby rascal, we won’t lift a hand against ye unless ye attack us; but till you say the word about not flogging us, we don’t do a hand’s turn.’\n\n“‘Down into the forecastle then, down with ye, I’ll keep ye there till ye’re sick of it. Down ye go.’\n\n“‘Shall we?’ cried the ringleader to his men. Most of them were against it; but at length, in obedience to Steelkilt, they preceded him down into their dark den, growlingly disappearing, like bears into a cave.\n\n“As the Lakeman’s bare head was just level with the planks, the Captain and his posse leaped the barricade, and rapidly drawing over the slide of the scuttle, planted their group of hands upon it, and loudly called for the steward to bring the heavy brass padlock belonging to the companionway. Then opening the slide a little, the Captain whispered something down the crack, closed it, and turned the key upon them—ten in number—leaving on deck some twenty or more, who thus far had remained neutral.\n\n“All night a wide-awake watch was kept by all the officers, forward and aft, especially about the forecastle scuttle and fore hatchway; at which last place it was feared the insurgents might emerge, after breaking through the bulkhead below. But the hours of darkness passed in peace; the men who still remained at their duty toiling hard at the pumps, whose clinking and clanking at intervals through the dreary night dismally resounded through the ship.\n\n“At sunrise the Captain went forward, and knocking on the deck, summoned the prisoners to work; but with a yell they refused. Water was then lowered down to them, and a couple of handfuls of biscuit were tossed after it; when again turning the key upon them and pocketing it, the Captain returned to the quarter-deck. Twice every day for three days this was repeated; but on the fourth morning a confused wrangling, and then a scuffling was heard, as the customary summons was delivered; and suddenly four men burst up from the forecastle, saying they were ready to turn to. The fetid closeness of the air, and a famishing diet, united perhaps to some fears of ultimate retribution, had constrained them to surrender at discretion. Emboldened by this, the Captain reiterated his demand to the rest, but Steelkilt shouted up to him a terrific hint to stop his babbling and betake himself where he belonged. On the fifth morning three others of the mutineers bolted up into the air from the desperate arms below that sought to restrain them. Only three were left.\n\n“‘Better turn to, now?’ said the Captain with a heartless jeer.\n\n“‘Shut us up again, will ye!’ cried Steelkilt.\n\n“‘Oh certainly,’ said the Captain, and the key clicked.\n\n“It was at this point, gentlemen, that enraged by the defection of seven of his former associates, and stung by the mocking voice that had last hailed him, and maddened by his long entombment in a place as black as the bowels of despair; it was then that Steelkilt proposed to the two Canallers, thus far apparently of one mind with him, to burst out of their hole at the next summoning of the garrison; and armed with their keen mincing knives (long, crescentic, heavy implements with a handle at each end) run amuck from the bowsprit to the taffrail; and if by any devilishness of desperation possible, seize the ship. For himself, he would do this, he said, whether they joined him or not. That was the last night he should spend in that den. But the scheme met with no opposition on the part of the other two; they swore they were ready for that, or for any other mad thing, for anything in short but a surrender. And what was more, they each insisted upon being the first man on deck, when the time to make the rush should come. But to this their leader as fiercely objected, reserving that priority for himself; particularly as his two comrades would not yield, the one to the other, in the matter; and both of them could not be first, for the ladder would but admit one man at a time. And here, gentlemen, the foul play of these miscreants must come out.\n\n“Upon hearing the frantic project of their leader, each in his own separate soul had suddenly lighted, it would seem, upon the same piece of treachery, namely: to be foremost in breaking out, in order to be the first of the three, though the last of the ten, to surrender; and thereby secure whatever small chance of pardon such conduct might merit. But when Steelkilt made known his determination still to lead them to the last, they in some way, by some subtle chemistry of villany, mixed their before secret treacheries together; and when their leader fell into a doze, verbally opened their souls to each other in three sentences; and bound the sleeper with cords, and gagged him with cords; and shrieked out for the Captain at midnight.\n\n“Thinking murder at hand, and smelling in the dark for the blood, he and all his armed mates and harpooneers rushed for the forecastle. In a few minutes the scuttle was opened, and, bound hand and foot, the still struggling ringleader was shoved up into the air by his perfidious allies, who at once claimed the honor of securing a man who had been fully ripe for murder. But all these were collared, and dragged along the deck like dead cattle; and, side by side, were seized up into the mizzen rigging, like three quarters of meat, and there they hung till morning. ‘Damn ye,’ cried the Captain, pacing to and fro before them, ‘the vultures would not touch ye, ye villains!’\n\n“At sunrise he summoned all hands; and separating those who had rebelled from those who had taken no part in the mutiny, he told the former that he had a good mind to flog them all round—thought, upon the whole, he would do so—he ought to—justice demanded it; but for the present, considering their timely surrender, he would let them go with a reprimand, which he accordingly administered in the vernacular.\n\n“‘But as for you, ye carrion rogues,’ turning to the three men in the rigging—‘for you, I mean to mince ye up for the try-pots;’ and, seizing a rope, he applied it with all his might to the backs of the two traitors, till they yelled no more, but lifelessly hung their heads sideways, as the two crucified thieves are drawn.\n\n“‘My wrist is sprained with ye!’ he cried, at last; ‘but there is still rope enough left for you, my fine bantam, that wouldn’t give up. Take that gag from his mouth, and let us hear what he can say for himself.’\n\n“For a moment the exhausted mutineer made a tremulous motion of his cramped jaws, and then painfully twisting round his head, said in a sort of hiss, ‘What I say is this—and mind it well—if you flog me, I murder you!’\n\n“‘Say ye so? then see how ye frighten me’—and the Captain drew off with the rope to strike.\n\n“‘Best not,’ hissed the Lakeman.\n\n“‘But I must,’—and the rope was once more drawn back for the stroke.\n\n“Steelkilt here hissed out something, inaudible to all but the Captain; who, to the amazement of all hands, started back, paced the deck rapidly two or three times, and then suddenly throwing down his rope, said, ‘I won’t do it—let him go—cut him down: d’ye hear?’\n\n“But as the junior mates were hurrying to execute the order, a pale man, with a bandaged head, arrested them—Radney the chief mate. Ever since the blow, he had lain in his berth; but that morning, hearing the tumult on the deck, he had crept out, and thus far had watched the whole scene. Such was the state of his mouth, that he could hardly speak; but mumbling something about his being willing and able to do what the captain dared not attempt, he snatched the rope and advanced to his pinioned foe.\n\n“‘You are a coward!’ hissed the Lakeman.\n\n“‘So I am, but take that.’ The mate was in the very act of striking, when another hiss stayed his uplifted arm. He paused: and then pausing no more, made good his word, spite of Steelkilt’s threat, whatever that might have been. The three men were then cut down, all hands were turned to, and, sullenly worked by the moody seamen, the iron pumps clanged as before.\n\n“Just after dark that day, when one watch had retired below, a clamor was heard in the forecastle; and the two trembling traitors running up, besieged the cabin door, saying they durst not consort with the crew. Entreaties, cuffs, and kicks could not drive them back, so at their own instance they were put down in the ship’s run for salvation. Still, no sign of mutiny reappeared among the rest. On the contrary, it seemed, that mainly at Steelkilt’s instigation, they had resolved to maintain the strictest peacefulness, obey all orders to the last, and, when the ship reached port, desert her in a body. But in order to insure the speediest end to the voyage, they all agreed to another thing—namely, not to sing out for whales, in case any should be discovered. For, spite of her leak, and spite of all her other perils, the Town-Ho still maintained her mast-heads, and her captain was just as willing to lower for a fish that moment, as on the day his craft first struck the cruising ground; and Radney the mate was quite as ready to change his berth for a boat, and with his bandaged mouth seek to gag in death the vital jaw of the whale.\n\n“But though the Lakeman had induced the seamen to adopt this sort of passiveness in their conduct, he kept his own counsel (at least till all was over) concerning his own proper and private revenge upon the man who had stung him in the ventricles of his heart. He was in Radney the chief mate’s watch; and as if the infatuated man sought to run more than half way to meet his doom, after the scene at the rigging, he insisted, against the express counsel of the captain, upon resuming the head of his watch at night. Upon this, and one or two other circumstances, Steelkilt systematically built the plan of his revenge.\n\n“During the night, Radney had an unseamanlike way of sitting on the bulwarks of the quarter-deck, and leaning his arm upon the gunwale of the boat which was hoisted up there, a little above the ship’s side. In this attitude, it was well known, he sometimes dozed. There was a considerable vacancy between the boat and the ship, and down between this was the sea. Steelkilt calculated his time, and found that his next trick at the helm would come round at two o’clock, in the morning of the third day from that in which he had been betrayed. At his leisure, he employed the interval in braiding something very carefully in his watches below.\n\n“‘What are you making there?’ said a shipmate.\n\n“‘What do you think? what does it look like?’\n\n“‘Like a lanyard for your bag; but it’s an odd one, seems to me.’\n\n“‘Yes, rather oddish,’ said the Lakeman, holding it at arm’s length before him; ‘but I think it will answer. Shipmate, I haven’t enough twine,—have you any?’\n\n“But there was none in the forecastle.\n\n“‘Then I must get some from old Rad;’ and he rose to go aft.\n\n“‘You don’t mean to go a begging to him!’ said a sailor.\n\n“‘Why not? Do you think he won’t do me a turn, when it’s to help himself in the end, shipmate?’ and going to the mate, he looked at him quietly, and asked him for some twine to mend his hammock. It was given him—neither twine nor lanyard were seen again; but the next night an iron ball, closely netted, partly rolled from the pocket of the Lakeman’s monkey jacket, as he was tucking the coat into his hammock for a pillow. Twenty-four hours after, his trick at the silent helm—nigh to the man who was apt to doze over the grave always ready dug to the seaman’s hand—that fatal hour was then to come; and in the fore-ordaining soul of Steelkilt, the mate was already stark and stretched as a corpse, with his forehead crushed in.\n\n“But, gentlemen, a fool saved the would-be murderer from the bloody deed he had planned. Yet complete revenge he had, and without being the avenger. For by a mysterious fatality, Heaven itself seemed to step in to take out of his hands into its own the damning thing he would have done.\n\n“It was just between daybreak and sunrise of the morning of the second day, when they were washing down the decks, that a stupid Teneriffe man, drawing water in the main-chains, all at once shouted out, ‘There she rolls! there she rolls!’ Jesu, what a whale! It was Moby Dick.\n\n“‘Moby Dick!’ cried Don Sebastian; ‘St. Dominic! Sir sailor, but do whales have christenings? Whom call you Moby Dick?’\n\n“‘A very white, and famous, and most deadly immortal monster, Don;—but that would be too long a story.’\n\n“‘How? how?’ cried all the young Spaniards, crowding.\n\n“‘Nay, Dons, Dons—nay, nay! I cannot rehearse that now. Let me get more into the air, Sirs.’\n\n“‘The chicha! the chicha!’ cried Don Pedro; ‘our vigorous friend looks faint;—fill up his empty glass!’\n\n“No need, gentlemen; one moment, and I proceed.—Now, gentlemen, so suddenly perceiving the snowy whale within fifty yards of the ship—forgetful of the compact among the crew—in the excitement of the moment, the Teneriffe man had instinctively and involuntarily lifted his voice for the monster, though for some little time past it had been plainly beheld from the three sullen mast-heads. All was now a phrensy. ‘The White Whale—the White Whale!’ was the cry from captain, mates, and harpooneers, who, undeterred by fearful rumours, were all anxious to capture so famous and precious a fish; while the dogged crew eyed askance, and with curses, the appalling beauty of the vast milky mass, that lit up by a horizontal spangling sun, shifted and glistened like a living opal in the blue morning sea. Gentlemen, a strange fatality pervades the whole career of these events, as if verily mapped out before the world itself was charted. The mutineer was the bowsman of the mate, and when fast to a fish, it was his duty to sit next him, while Radney stood up with his lance in the prow, and haul in or slacken the line, at the word of command. Moreover, when the four boats were lowered, the mate’s got the start; and none howled more fiercely with delight than did Steelkilt, as he strained at his oar. After a stiff pull, their harpooneer got fast, and, spear in hand, Radney sprang to the bow. He was always a furious man, it seems, in a boat. And now his bandaged cry was, to beach him on the whale’s topmost back. Nothing loath, his bowsman hauled him up and up, through a blinding foam that blent two whitenesses together; till of a sudden the boat struck as against a sunken ledge, and keeling over, spilled out the standing mate. That instant, as he fell on the whale’s slippery back, the boat righted, and was dashed aside by the swell, while Radney was tossed over into the sea, on the other flank of the whale. He struck out through the spray, and, for an instant, was dimly seen through that veil, wildly seeking to remove himself from the eye of Moby Dick. But the whale rushed round in a sudden maelstrom; seized the swimmer between his jaws; and rearing high up with him, plunged headlong again, and went down.\n\n“Meantime, at the first tap of the boat’s bottom, the Lakeman had slackened the line, so as to drop astern from the whirlpool; calmly looking on, he thought his own thoughts. But a sudden, terrific, downward jerking of the boat, quickly brought his knife to the line. He cut it; and the whale was free. But, at some distance, Moby Dick rose again, with some tatters of Radney’s red woollen shirt, caught in the teeth that had destroyed him. All four boats gave chase again; but the whale eluded them, and finally wholly disappeared.\n\n“In good time, the Town-Ho reached her port—a savage, solitary place—where no civilized creature resided. There, headed by the Lakeman, all but five or six of the foremastmen deliberately deserted among the palms; eventually, as it turned out, seizing a large double war-canoe of the savages, and setting sail for some other harbor.\n\n“The ship’s company being reduced to but a handful, the captain called upon the Islanders to assist him in the laborious business of heaving down the ship to stop the leak. But to such unresting vigilance over their dangerous allies was this small band of whites necessitated, both by night and by day, and so extreme was the hard work they underwent, that upon the vessel being ready again for sea, they were in such a weakened condition that the captain durst not put off with them in so heavy a vessel. After taking counsel with his officers, he anchored the ship as far off shore as possible; loaded and ran out his two cannon from the bows; stacked his muskets on the poop; and warning the Islanders not to approach the ship at their peril, took one man with him, and setting the sail of his best whale-boat, steered straight before the wind for Tahiti, five hundred miles distant, to procure a reinforcement to his crew.\n\n“On the fourth day of the sail, a large canoe was descried, which seemed to have touched at a low isle of corals. He steered away from it; but the savage craft bore down on him; and soon the voice of Steelkilt hailed him to heave to, or he would run him under water. The captain presented a pistol. With one foot on each prow of the yoked war-canoes, the Lakeman laughed him to scorn; assuring him that if the pistol so much as clicked in the lock, he would bury him in bubbles and foam.\n\n“‘What do you want of me?’ cried the captain.\n\n“‘Where are you bound? and for what are you bound?’ demanded Steelkilt; ‘no lies.’\n\n“‘I am bound to Tahiti for more men.’\n\n“‘Very good. Let me board you a moment—I come in peace.’ With that he leaped from the canoe, swam to the boat; and climbing the gunwale, stood face to face with the captain.\n\n“‘Cross your arms, sir; throw back your head. Now, repeat after me. As soon as Steelkilt leaves me, I swear to beach this boat on yonder island, and remain there six days. If I do not, may lightnings strike me!’\n\n“‘A pretty scholar,’ laughed the Lakeman. ‘Adios, Senor!’ and leaping into the sea, he swam back to his comrades.\n\n“Watching the boat till it was fairly beached, and drawn up to the roots of the cocoa-nut trees, Steelkilt made sail again, and in due time arrived at Tahiti, his own place of destination. There, luck befriended him; two ships were about to sail for France, and were providentially in want of precisely that number of men which the sailor headed. They embarked; and so for ever got the start of their former captain, had he been at all minded to work them legal retribution.\n\n“Some ten days after the French ships sailed, the whale-boat arrived, and the captain was forced to enlist some of the more civilized Tahitians, who had been somewhat used to the sea. Chartering a small native schooner, he returned with them to his vessel; and finding all right there, again resumed his cruisings.\n\n“Where Steelkilt now is, gentlemen, none know; but upon the island of Nantucket, the widow of Radney still turns to the sea which refuses to give up its dead; still in dreams sees the awful white whale that destroyed him. * * * *\n\n“‘Are you through?’ said Don Sebastian, quietly.\n\n“‘I am, Don.’\n\n“‘Then I entreat you, tell me if to the best of your own convictions, this your story is in substance really true? It is so passing wonderful! Did you get it from an unquestionable source? Bear with me if I seem to press.’\n\n“‘Also bear with all of us, sir sailor; for we all join in Don Sebastian’s suit,’ cried the company, with exceeding interest.\n\n“‘Is there a copy of the Holy Evangelists in the Golden Inn, gentlemen?’\n\n“‘Nay,’ said Don Sebastian; ‘but I know a worthy priest near by, who will quickly procure one for me. I go for it; but are you well advised? this may grow too serious.’\n\n“‘Will you be so good as to bring the priest also, Don?’\n\n“‘Though there are no Auto-da-Fés in Lima now,’ said one of the company to another; ‘I fear our sailor friend runs risk of the archiepiscopacy. Let us withdraw more out of the moonlight. I see no need of this.’\n\n“‘Excuse me for running after you, Don Sebastian; but may I also beg that you will be particular in procuring the largest sized Evangelists you can.’\n\n“‘This is the priest, he brings you the Evangelists,’ said Don Sebastian, gravely, returning with a tall and solemn figure.\n\n“‘Let me remove my hat. Now, venerable priest, further into the light, and hold the Holy Book before me that I may touch it.\n\n“‘So help me Heaven, and on my honor the story I have told ye, gentlemen, is in substance and its great items, true. I know it to be true; it happened on this ball; I trod the ship; I knew the crew; I have seen and talked with Steelkilt since the death of Radney.’”\n\n# CHAPTER 55. Of the Monstrous Pictures of Whales.\n\nI shall ere long paint to you as well as one can without canvas, something like the true form of the whale as he actually appears to the eye of the whaleman when in his own absolute body the whale is moored alongside the whale-ship so that he can be fairly stepped upon there. It may be worth while, therefore, previously to advert to those curious imaginary portraits of him which even down to the present day confidently challenge the faith of the landsman. It is time to set the world right in this matter, by proving such pictures of the whale all wrong.\n\nIt may be that the primal source of all those pictorial delusions will be found among the oldest Hindoo, Egyptian, and Grecian sculptures. For ever since those inventive but unscrupulous times when on the marble panellings of temples, the pedestals of statues, and on shields, medallions, cups, and coins, the dolphin was drawn in scales of chain-armor like Saladin’s, and a helmeted head like St. George’s; ever since then has something of the same sort of license prevailed, not only in most popular pictures of the whale, but in many scientific presentations of him.\n\nNow, by all odds, the most ancient extant portrait anyways purporting to be the whale’s, is to be found in the famous cavern-pagoda of Elephanta, in India. The Brahmins maintain that in the almost endless sculptures of that immemorial pagoda, all the trades and pursuits, every conceivable avocation of man, were prefigured ages before any of them actually came into being. No wonder then, that in some sort our noble profession of whaling should have been there shadowed forth. The Hindoo whale referred to, occurs in a separate department of the wall, depicting the incarnation of Vishnu in the form of leviathan, learnedly known as the Matse Avatar. But though this sculpture is half man and half whale, so as only to give the tail of the latter, yet that small section of him is all wrong. It looks more like the tapering tail of an anaconda, than the broad palms of the true whale’s majestic flukes.\n\nBut go to the old Galleries, and look now at a great Christian painter’s portrait of this fish; for he succeeds no better than the antediluvian Hindoo. It is Guido’s picture of Perseus rescuing Andromeda from the sea-monster or whale. Where did Guido get the model of such a strange creature as that? Nor does Hogarth, in painting the same scene in his own “Perseus Descending,” make out one whit better. The huge corpulence of that Hogarthian monster undulates on the surface, scarcely drawing one inch of water. It has a sort of howdah on its back, and its distended tusked mouth into which the billows are rolling, might be taken for the Traitors’ Gate leading from the Thames by water into the Tower. Then, there are the Prodromus whales of old Scotch Sibbald, and Jonah’s whale, as depicted in the prints of old Bibles and the cuts of old primers. What shall be said of these? As for the book-binder’s whale winding like a vine-stalk round the stock of a descending anchor—as stamped and gilded on the backs and title-pages of many books both old and new—that is a very picturesque but purely fabulous creature, imitated, I take it, from the like figures on antique vases. Though universally denominated a dolphin, I nevertheless call this book-binder’s fish an attempt at a whale; because it was so intended when the device was first introduced. It was introduced by an old Italian publisher somewhere about the 15th century, during the Revival of Learning; and in those days, and even down to a comparatively late period, dolphins were popularly supposed to be a species of the Leviathan.\n\nIn the vignettes and other embellishments of some ancient books you will at times meet with very curious touches at the whale, where all manner of spouts, jets d’eau, hot springs and cold, Saratoga and Baden-Baden, come bubbling up from his unexhausted brain. In the title-page of the original edition of the “Advancement of Learning” you will find some curious whales.\n\nBut quitting all these unprofessional attempts, let us glance at those pictures of leviathan purporting to be sober, scientific delineations, by those who know. In old Harris’s collection of voyages there are some plates of whales extracted from a Dutch book of voyages, A.D. 1671, entitled “A Whaling Voyage to Spitzbergen in the ship Jonas in the Whale, Peter Peterson of Friesland, master.” In one of those plates the whales, like great rafts of logs, are represented lying among ice-isles, with white bears running over their living backs. In another plate, the prodigious blunder is made of representing the whale with perpendicular flukes.\n\nThen again, there is an imposing quarto, written by one Captain Colnett, a Post Captain in the English navy, entitled “A Voyage round Cape Horn into the South Seas, for the purpose of extending the Spermaceti Whale Fisheries.” In this book is an outline purporting to be a “Picture of a Physeter or Spermaceti whale, drawn by scale from one killed on the coast of Mexico, August, 1793, and hoisted on deck.” I doubt not the captain had this veracious picture taken for the benefit of his marines. To mention but one thing about it, let me say that it has an eye which applied, according to the accompanying scale, to a full grown sperm whale, would make the eye of that whale a bow-window some five feet long. Ah, my gallant captain, why did ye not give us Jonah looking out of that eye!\n\nNor are the most conscientious compilations of Natural History for the benefit of the young and tender, free from the same heinousness of mistake. Look at that popular work “Goldsmith’s Animated Nature.” In the abridged London edition of 1807, there are plates of an alleged “whale” and a “narwhale.” I do not wish to seem inelegant, but this unsightly whale looks much like an amputated sow; and, as for the narwhale, one glimpse at it is enough to amaze one, that in this nineteenth century such a hippogriff could be palmed for genuine upon any intelligent public of schoolboys.\n\nThen, again, in 1825, Bernard Germain, Count de Lacépède, a great naturalist, published a scientific systemized whale book, wherein are several pictures of the different species of the Leviathan. All these are not only incorrect, but the picture of the Mysticetus or Greenland whale (that is to say, the Right whale), even Scoresby, a long experienced man as touching that species, declares not to have its counterpart in nature.\n\nBut the placing of the cap-sheaf to all this blundering business was reserved for the scientific Frederick Cuvier, brother to the famous Baron. In 1836, he published a Natural History of Whales, in which he gives what he calls a picture of the Sperm Whale. Before showing that picture to any Nantucketer, you had best provide for your summary retreat from Nantucket. In a word, Frederick Cuvier’s Sperm Whale is not a Sperm Whale, but a squash. Of course, he never had the benefit of a whaling voyage (such men seldom have), but whence he derived that picture, who can tell? Perhaps he got it as his scientific predecessor in the same field, Desmarest, got one of his authentic abortions; that is, from a Chinese drawing. And what sort of lively lads with the pencil those Chinese are, many queer cups and saucers inform us.\n\nAs for the sign-painters’ whales seen in the streets hanging over the shops of oil-dealers, what shall be said of them? They are generally Richard III. whales, with dromedary humps, and very savage; breakfasting on three or four sailor tarts, that is whaleboats full of mariners: their deformities floundering in seas of blood and blue paint.\n\nBut these manifold mistakes in depicting the whale are not so very surprising after all. Consider! Most of the scientific drawings have been taken from the stranded fish; and these are about as correct as a drawing of a wrecked ship, with broken back, would correctly represent the noble animal itself in all its undashed pride of hull and spars. Though elephants have stood for their full-lengths, the living Leviathan has never yet fairly floated himself for his portrait. The living whale, in his full majesty and significance, is only to be seen at sea in unfathomable waters; and afloat the vast bulk of him is out of sight, like a launched line-of-battle ship; and out of that element it is a thing eternally impossible for mortal man to hoist him bodily into the air, so as to preserve all his mighty swells and undulations. And, not to speak of the highly presumable difference of contour between a young sucking whale and a full-grown Platonian Leviathan; yet, even in the case of one of those young sucking whales hoisted to a ship’s deck, such is then the outlandish, eel-like, limbered, varying shape of him, that his precise expression the devil himself could not catch.\n\nBut it may be fancied, that from the naked skeleton of the stranded whale, accurate hints may be derived touching his true form. Not at all. For it is one of the more curious things about this Leviathan, that his skeleton gives very little idea of his general shape. Though Jeremy Bentham’s skeleton, which hangs for candelabra in the library of one of his executors, correctly conveys the idea of a burly-browed utilitarian old gentleman, with all Jeremy’s other leading personal characteristics; yet nothing of this kind could be inferred from any leviathan’s articulated bones. In fact, as the great Hunter says, the mere skeleton of the whale bears the same relation to the fully invested and padded animal as the insect does to the chrysalis that so roundingly envelopes it. This peculiarity is strikingly evinced in the head, as in some part of this book will be incidentally shown. It is also very curiously displayed in the side fin, the bones of which almost exactly answer to the bones of the human hand, minus only the thumb. This fin has four regular bone-fingers, the index, middle, ring, and little finger. But all these are permanently lodged in their fleshy covering, as the human fingers in an artificial covering. “However recklessly the whale may sometimes serve us,” said humorous Stubb one day, “he can never be truly said to handle us without mittens.”\n\nFor all these reasons, then, any way you may look at it, you must needs conclude that the great Leviathan is that one creature in the world which must remain unpainted to the last. True, one portrait may hit the mark much nearer than another, but none can hit it with any very considerable degree of exactness. So there is no earthly way of finding out precisely what the whale really looks like. And the only mode in which you can derive even a tolerable idea of his living contour, is by going a whaling yourself; but by so doing, you run no small risk of being eternally stove and sunk by him. Wherefore, it seems to me you had best not be too fastidious in your curiosity touching this Leviathan.\n\nCHAPTER 56. Of the Less Erroneous Pictures of Whales, and the True Pictures of Whaling Scenes.\nIn connexion with the monstrous pictures of whales, I am strongly tempted here to enter upon those still more monstrous stories of them which are to be found in certain books, both ancient and modern, especially in Pliny, Purchas, Hackluyt, Harris, Cuvier, etc. But I pass that matter by.\n\nI know of only four published outlines of the great Sperm Whale; Colnett’s, Huggins’s, Frederick Cuvier’s, and Beale’s. In the previous chapter Colnett and Cuvier have been referred to. Huggins’s is far better than theirs; but, by great odds, Beale’s is the best. All Beale’s drawings of this whale are good, excepting the middle figure in the picture of three whales in various attitudes, capping his second chapter. His frontispiece, boats attacking Sperm Whales, though no doubt calculated to excite the civil scepticism of some parlor men, is admirably correct and life-like in its general effect. Some of the Sperm Whale drawings in J. Ross Browne are pretty correct in contour; but they are wretchedly engraved. That is not his fault though.\n\nOf the Right Whale, the best outline pictures are in Scoresby; but they are drawn on too small a scale to convey a desirable impression. He has but one picture of whaling scenes, and this is a sad deficiency, because it is by such pictures only, when at all well done, that you can derive anything like a truthful idea of the living whale as seen by his living hunters.\n\nBut, taken for all in all, by far the finest, though in some details not the most correct, presentations of whales and whaling scenes to be anywhere found, are two large French engravings, well executed, and taken from paintings by one Garnery. Respectively, they represent attacks on the Sperm and Right Whale. In the first engraving a noble Sperm Whale is depicted in full majesty of might, just risen beneath the boat from the profundities of the ocean, and bearing high in the air upon his back the terrific wreck of the stoven planks. The prow of the boat is partially unbroken, and is drawn just balancing upon the monster’s spine; and standing in that prow, for that one single incomputable flash of time, you behold an oarsman, half shrouded by the incensed boiling spout of the whale, and in the act of leaping, as if from a precipice. The action of the whole thing is wonderfully good and true. The half-emptied line-tub floats on the whitened sea; the wooden poles of the spilled harpoons obliquely bob in it; the heads of the swimming crew are scattered about the whale in contrasting expressions of affright; while in the black stormy distance the ship is bearing down upon the scene. Serious fault might be found with the anatomical details of this whale, but let that pass; since, for the life of me, I could not draw so good a one.\n\nIn the second engraving, the boat is in the act of drawing alongside the barnacled flank of a large running Right Whale, that rolls his black weedy bulk in the sea like some mossy rock-slide from the Patagonian cliffs. His jets are erect, full, and black like soot; so that from so abounding a smoke in the chimney, you would think there must be a brave supper cooking in the great bowels below. Sea fowls are pecking at the small crabs, shell-fish, and other sea candies and maccaroni, which the Right Whale sometimes carries on his pestilent back. And all the while the thick-lipped leviathan is rushing through the deep, leaving tons of tumultuous white curds in his wake, and causing the slight boat to rock in the swells like a skiff caught nigh the paddle-wheels of an ocean steamer. Thus, the foreground is all raging commotion; but behind, in admirable artistic contrast, is the glassy level of a sea becalmed, the drooping unstarched sails of the powerless ship, and the inert mass of a dead whale, a conquered fortress, with the flag of capture lazily hanging from the whale-pole inserted into his spout-hole.\n\nWho Garnery the painter is, or was, I know not. But my life for it he was either practically conversant with his subject, or else marvellously tutored by some experienced whaleman. The French are the lads for painting action. Go and gaze upon all the paintings of Europe, and where will you find such a gallery of living and breathing commotion on canvas, as in that triumphal hall at Versailles; where the beholder fights his way, pell-mell, through the consecutive great battles of France; where every sword seems a flash of the Northern Lights, and the successive armed kings and Emperors dash by, like a charge of crowned centaurs? Not wholly unworthy of a place in that gallery, are these sea battle-pieces of Garnery.\n\nThe natural aptitude of the French for seizing the picturesqueness of things seems to be peculiarly evinced in what paintings and engravings they have of their whaling scenes. With not one tenth of England’s experience in the fishery, and not the thousandth part of that of the Americans, they have nevertheless furnished both nations with the only finished sketches at all capable of conveying the real spirit of the whale hunt. For the most part, the English and American whale draughtsmen seem entirely content with presenting the mechanical outline of things, such as the vacant profile of the whale; which, so far as picturesqueness of effect is concerned, is about tantamount to sketching the profile of a pyramid. Even Scoresby, the justly renowned Right whaleman, after giving us a stiff full length of the Greenland whale, and three or four delicate miniatures of narwhales and porpoises, treats us to a series of classical engravings of boat hooks, chopping knives, and grapnels; and with the microscopic diligence of a Leuwenhoeck submits to the inspection of a shivering world ninety-six fac-similes of magnified Arctic snow crystals. I mean no disparagement to the excellent voyager (I honor him for a veteran), but in so important a matter it was certainly an oversight not to have procured for every crystal a sworn affidavit taken before a Greenland Justice of the Peace.\n\nIn addition to those fine engravings from Garnery, there are two other French engravings worthy of note, by some one who subscribes himself “H. Durand.” One of them, though not precisely adapted to our present purpose, nevertheless deserves mention on other accounts. It is a quiet noon-scene among the isles of the Pacific; a French whaler anchored, inshore, in a calm, and lazily taking water on board; the loosened sails of the ship, and the long leaves of the palms in the background, both drooping together in the breezeless air. The effect is very fine, when considered with reference to its presenting the hardy fishermen under one of their few aspects of oriental repose. The other engraving is quite a different affair: the ship hove-to upon the open sea, and in the very heart of the Leviathanic life, with a Right Whale alongside; the vessel (in the act of cutting-in) hove over to the monster as if to a quay; and a boat, hurriedly pushing off from this scene of activity, is about giving chase to whales in the distance. The harpoons and lances lie levelled for use; three oarsmen are just setting the mast in its hole; while from a sudden roll of the sea, the little craft stands half-erect out of the water, like a rearing horse. From the ship, the smoke of the torments of the boiling whale is going up like the smoke over a village of smithies; and to windward, a black cloud, rising up with earnest of squalls and rains, seems to quicken the activity of the excited seamen.\n\nCHAPTER 57. Of Whales in Paint; in Teeth; in Wood; in Sheet-Iron; in Stone; in Mountains; in Stars.\nOn Tower-hill, as you go down to the London docks, you may have seen a crippled beggar (or kedger, as the sailors say) holding a painted board before him, representing the tragic scene in which he lost his leg. There are three whales and three boats; and one of the boats (presumed to contain the missing leg in all its original integrity) is being crunched by the jaws of the foremost whale. Any time these ten years, they tell me, has that man held up that picture, and exhibited that stump to an incredulous world. But the time of his justification has now come. His three whales are as good whales as were ever published in Wapping, at any rate; and his stump as unquestionable a stump as any you will find in the western clearings. But, though for ever mounted on that stump, never a stump-speech does the poor whaleman make; but, with downcast eyes, stands ruefully contemplating his own amputation.\n\nThroughout the Pacific, and also in Nantucket, and New Bedford, and Sag Harbor, you will come across lively sketches of whales and whaling-scenes, graven by the fishermen themselves on Sperm Whale-teeth, or ladies’ busks wrought out of the Right Whale-bone, and other like skrimshander articles, as the whalemen call the numerous little ingenious contrivances they elaborately carve out of the rough material, in their hours of ocean leisure. Some of them have little boxes of dentistical-looking implements, specially intended for the skrimshandering business. But, in general, they toil with their jack-knives alone; and, with that almost omnipotent tool of the sailor, they will turn you out anything you please, in the way of a mariner’s fancy.\n\nLong exile from Christendom and civilization inevitably restores a man to that condition in which God placed him, i.e. what is called savagery. Your true whale-hunter is as much a savage as an Iroquois. I myself am a savage, owning no allegiance but to the King of the Cannibals; and ready at any moment to rebel against him.\n\nNow, one of the peculiar characteristics of the savage in his domestic hours, is his wonderful patience of industry. An ancient Hawaiian war-club or spear-paddle, in its full multiplicity and elaboration of carving, is as great a trophy of human perseverance as a Latin lexicon. For, with but a bit of broken sea-shell or a shark’s tooth, that miraculous intricacy of wooden net-work has been achieved; and it has cost steady years of steady application.\n\nAs with the Hawaiian savage, so with the white sailor-savage. With the same marvellous patience, and with the same single shark’s tooth, of his one poor jack-knife, he will carve you a bit of bone sculpture, not quite as workmanlike, but as close packed in its maziness of design, as the Greek savage, Achilles’s shield; and full of barbaric spirit and suggestiveness, as the prints of that fine old Dutch savage, Albert Durer.\n\nWooden whales, or whales cut in profile out of the small dark slabs of the noble South Sea war-wood, are frequently met with in the forecastles of American whalers. Some of them are done with much accuracy.\n\nAt some old gable-roofed country houses you will see brass whales hung by the tail for knockers to the road-side door. When the porter is sleepy, the anvil-headed whale would be best. But these knocking whales are seldom remarkable as faithful essays. On the spires of some old-fashioned churches you will see sheet-iron whales placed there for weather-cocks; but they are so elevated, and besides that are to all intents and purposes so labelled with “Hands off!” you cannot examine them closely enough to decide upon their merit.\n\nIn bony, ribby regions of the earth, where at the base of high broken cliffs masses of rock lie strewn in fantastic groupings upon the plain, you will often discover images as of the petrified forms of the Leviathan partly merged in grass, which of a windy day breaks against them in a surf of green surges.\n\nThen, again, in mountainous countries where the traveller is continually girdled by amphitheatrical heights; here and there from some lucky point of view you will catch passing glimpses of the profiles of whales defined along the undulating ridges. But you must be a thorough whaleman, to see these sights; and not only that, but if you wish to return to such a sight again, you must be sure and take the exact intersecting latitude and longitude of your first stand-point, else so chance-like are such observations of the hills, that your precise, previous stand-point would require a laborious re-discovery; like the Soloma Islands, which still remain incognita, though once high-ruffed Mendanna trod them and old Figuera chronicled them.\n\nNor when expandingly lifted by your subject, can you fail to trace out great whales in the starry heavens, and boats in pursuit of them; as when long filled with thoughts of war the Eastern nations saw armies locked in battle among the clouds. Thus at the North have I chased Leviathan round and round the Pole with the revolutions of the bright points that first defined him to me. And beneath the effulgent Antarctic skies I have boarded the Argo-Navis, and joined the chase against the starry Cetus far beyond the utmost stretch of Hydrus and the Flying Fish.\n\nWith a frigate’s anchors for my bridle-bitts and fasces of harpoons for spurs, would I could mount that whale and leap the topmost skies, to see whether the fabled heavens with all their countless tents really lie encamped beyond my mortal sight!\n\n# CHAPTER 58. Brit.\n\nSteering north-eastward from the Crozetts, we fell in with vast meadows of brit, the minute, yellow substance, upon which the Right Whale largely feeds. For leagues and leagues it undulated round us, so that we seemed to be sailing through boundless fields of ripe and golden wheat.\n\nOn the second day, numbers of Right Whales were seen, who, secure from the attack of a Sperm Whaler like the Pequod, with open jaws sluggishly swam through the brit, which, adhering to the fringing fibres of that wondrous Venetian blind in their mouths, was in that manner separated from the water that escaped at the lip.\n\nAs morning mowers, who side by side slowly and seethingly advance their scythes through the long wet grass of marshy meads; even so these monsters swam, making a strange, grassy, cutting sound; and leaving behind them endless swaths of blue upon the yellow sea.*\n\n*That part of the sea known among whalemen as the “Brazil Banks” does not bear that name as the Banks of Newfoundland do, because of there being shallows and soundings there, but because of this remarkable meadow-like appearance, caused by the vast drifts of brit continually floating in those latitudes, where the Right Whale is often chased.\n\nBut it was only the sound they made as they parted the brit which at all reminded one of mowers. Seen from the mast-heads, especially when they paused and were stationary for a while, their vast black forms looked more like lifeless masses of rock than anything else. And as in the great hunting countries of India, the stranger at a distance will sometimes pass on the plains recumbent elephants without knowing them to be such, taking them for bare, blackened elevations of the soil; even so, often, with him, who for the first time beholds this species of the leviathans of the sea. And even when recognised at last, their immense magnitude renders it very hard really to believe that such bulky masses of overgrowth can possibly be instinct, in all parts, with the same sort of life that lives in a dog or a horse.\n\nIndeed, in other respects, you can hardly regard any creatures of the deep with the same feelings that you do those of the shore. For though some old naturalists have maintained that all creatures of the land are of their kind in the sea; and though taking a broad general view of the thing, this may very well be; yet coming to specialties, where, for example, does the ocean furnish any fish that in disposition answers to the sagacious kindness of the dog? The accursed shark alone can in any generic respect be said to bear comparative analogy to him.\n\nBut though, to landsmen in general, the native inhabitants of the seas have ever been regarded with emotions unspeakably unsocial and repelling; though we know the sea to be an everlasting terra incognita, so that Columbus sailed over numberless unknown worlds to discover his one superficial western one; though, by vast odds, the most terrific of all mortal disasters have immemorially and indiscriminately befallen tens and hundreds of thousands of those who have gone upon the waters; though but a moment’s consideration will teach, that however baby man may brag of his science and skill, and however much, in a flattering future, that science and skill may augment; yet for ever and for ever, to the crack of doom, the sea will insult and murder him, and pulverize the stateliest, stiffest frigate he can make; nevertheless, by the continual repetition of these very impressions, man has lost that sense of the full awfulness of the sea which aboriginally belongs to it.\n\nThe first boat we read of, floated on an ocean, that with Portuguese vengeance had whelmed a whole world without leaving so much as a widow. That same ocean rolls now; that same ocean destroyed the wrecked ships of last year. Yea, foolish mortals, Noah’s flood is not yet subsided; two thirds of the fair world it yet covers.\n\nWherein differ the sea and the land, that a miracle upon one is not a miracle upon the other? Preternatural terrors rested upon the Hebrews, when under the feet of Korah and his company the live ground opened and swallowed them up for ever; yet not a modern sun ever sets, but in precisely the same manner the live sea swallows up ships and crews.\n\nBut not only is the sea such a foe to man who is an alien to it, but it is also a fiend to its own off-spring; worse than the Persian host who murdered his own guests; sparing not the creatures which itself hath spawned. Like a savage tigress that tossing in the jungle overlays her own cubs, so the sea dashes even the mightiest whales against the rocks, and leaves them there side by side with the split wrecks of ships. No mercy, no power but its own controls it. Panting and snorting like a mad battle steed that has lost its rider, the masterless ocean overruns the globe.\n\nConsider the subtleness of the sea; how its most dreaded creatures glide under water, unapparent for the most part, and treacherously hidden beneath the loveliest tints of azure. Consider also the devilish brilliance and beauty of many of its most remorseless tribes, as the dainty embellished shape of many species of sharks. Consider, once more, the universal cannibalism of the sea; all whose creatures prey upon each other, carrying on eternal war since the world began.\n\nConsider all this; and then turn to this green, gentle, and most docile earth; consider them both, the sea and the land; and do you not find a strange analogy to something in yourself? For as this appalling ocean surrounds the verdant land, so in the soul of man there lies one insular Tahiti, full of peace and joy, but encompassed by all the horrors of the half known life. God keep thee! Push not off from that isle, thou canst never return!\n\n# CHAPTER 59. Squid.\n\nSlowly wading through the meadows of brit, the Pequod still held on her way north-eastward towards the island of Java; a gentle air impelling her keel, so that in the surrounding serenity her three tall tapering masts mildly waved to that languid breeze, as three mild palms on a plain. And still, at wide intervals in the silvery night, the lonely, alluring jet would be seen.\n\nBut one transparent blue morning, when a stillness almost preternatural spread over the sea, however unattended with any stagnant calm; when the long burnished sun-glade on the waters seemed a golden finger laid across them, enjoining some secrecy; when the slippered waves whispered together as they softly ran on; in this profound hush of the visible sphere a strange spectre was seen by Daggoo from the main-mast-head.\n\nIn the distance, a great white mass lazily rose, and rising higher and higher, and disentangling itself from the azure, at last gleamed before our prow like a snow-slide, new slid from the hills. Thus glistening for a moment, as slowly it subsided, and sank. Then once more arose, and silently gleamed. It seemed not a whale; and yet is this Moby Dick? thought Daggoo. Again the phantom went down, but on re-appearing once more, with a stiletto-like cry that startled every man from his nod, the negro yelled out—“There! there again! there she breaches! right ahead! The White Whale, the White Whale!”\n\nUpon this, the seamen rushed to the yard-arms, as in swarming-time the bees rush to the boughs. Bare-headed in the sultry sun, Ahab stood on the bowsprit, and with one hand pushed far behind in readiness to wave his orders to the helmsman, cast his eager glance in the direction indicated aloft by the outstretched motionless arm of Daggoo.\n\nWhether the flitting attendance of the one still and solitary jet had gradually worked upon Ahab, so that he was now prepared to connect the ideas of mildness and repose with the first sight of the particular whale he pursued; however this was, or whether his eagerness betrayed him; whichever way it might have been, no sooner did he distinctly perceive the white mass, than with a quick intensity he instantly gave orders for lowering.\n\nThe four boats were soon on the water; Ahab’s in advance, and all swiftly pulling towards their prey. Soon it went down, and while, with oars suspended, we were awaiting its reappearance, lo! in the same spot where it sank, once more it slowly rose. Almost forgetting for the moment all thoughts of Moby Dick, we now gazed at the most wondrous phenomenon which the secret seas have hitherto revealed to mankind. A vast pulpy mass, furlongs in length and breadth, of a glancing cream-colour, lay floating on the water, innumerable long arms radiating from its centre, and curling and twisting like a nest of anacondas, as if blindly to clutch at any hapless object within reach. No perceptible face or front did it have; no conceivable token of either sensation or instinct; but undulated there on the billows, an unearthly, formless, chance-like apparition of life.\n\nAs with a low sucking sound it slowly disappeared again, Starbuck still gazing at the agitated waters where it had sunk, with a wild voice exclaimed—“Almost rather had I seen Moby Dick and fought him, than to have seen thee, thou white ghost!”\n\n“What was it, Sir?” said Flask.\n\n“The great live squid, which, they say, few whale-ships ever beheld, and returned to their ports to tell of it.”\n\nBut Ahab said nothing; turning his boat, he sailed back to the vessel; the rest as silently following.\n\nWhatever superstitions the sperm whalemen in general have connected with the sight of this object, certain it is, that a glimpse of it being so very unusual, that circumstance has gone far to invest it with portentousness. So rarely is it beheld, that though one and all of them declare it to be the largest animated thing in the ocean, yet very few of them have any but the most vague ideas concerning its true nature and form; notwithstanding, they believe it to furnish to the sperm whale his only food. For though other species of whales find their food above water, and may be seen by man in the act of feeding, the spermaceti whale obtains his whole food in unknown zones below the surface; and only by inference is it that any one can tell of what, precisely, that food consists. At times, when closely pursued, he will disgorge what are supposed to be the detached arms of the squid; some of them thus exhibited exceeding twenty and thirty feet in length. They fancy that the monster to which these arms belonged ordinarily clings by them to the bed of the ocean; and that the sperm whale, unlike other species, is supplied with teeth in order to attack and tear it.\n\nThere seems some ground to imagine that the great Kraken of Bishop Pontoppodan may ultimately resolve itself into Squid. The manner in which the Bishop describes it, as alternately rising and sinking, with some other particulars he narrates, in all this the two correspond. But much abatement is necessary with respect to the incredible bulk he assigns it.\n\nBy some naturalists who have vaguely heard rumors of the mysterious creature, here spoken of, it is included among the class of cuttle-fish, to which, indeed, in certain external respects it would seem to belong, but only as the Anak of the tribe.\n\n# CHAPTER 60. The Line.\n\nWith reference to the whaling scene shortly to be described, as well as for the better understanding of all similar scenes elsewhere presented, I have here to speak of the magical, sometimes horrible whale-line.\n\nThe line originally used in the fishery was of the best hemp, slightly vapored with tar, not impregnated with it, as in the case of ordinary ropes; for while tar, as ordinarily used, makes the hemp more pliable to the rope-maker, and also renders the rope itself more convenient to the sailor for common ship use; yet, not only would the ordinary quantity too much stiffen the whale-line for the close coiling to which it must be subjected; but as most seamen are beginning to learn, tar in general by no means adds to the rope’s durability or strength, however much it may give it compactness and gloss.\n\nOf late years the Manilla rope has in the American fishery almost entirely superseded hemp as a material for whale-lines; for, though not so durable as hemp, it is stronger, and far more soft and elastic; and I will add (since there is an æsthetics in all things), is much more handsome and becoming to the boat, than hemp. Hemp is a dusky, dark fellow, a sort of Indian; but Manilla is as a golden-haired Circassian to behold.\n\nThe whale-line is only two-thirds of an inch in thickness. At first sight, you would not think it so strong as it really is. By experiment its one and fifty yarns will each suspend a weight of one hundred and twenty pounds; so that the whole rope will bear a strain nearly equal to three tons. In length, the common sperm whale-line measures something over two hundred fathoms. Towards the stern of the boat it is spirally coiled away in the tub, not like the worm-pipe of a still though, but so as to form one round, cheese-shaped mass of densely bedded “sheaves,” or layers of concentric spiralizations, without any hollow but the “heart,” or minute vertical tube formed at the axis of the cheese. As the least tangle or kink in the coiling would, in running out, infallibly take somebody’s arm, leg, or entire body off, the utmost precaution is used in stowing the line in its tub. Some harpooneers will consume almost an entire morning in this business, carrying the line high aloft and then reeving it downwards through a block towards the tub, so as in the act of coiling to free it from all possible wrinkles and twists.\n\nIn the English boats two tubs are used instead of one; the same line being continuously coiled in both tubs. There is some advantage in this; because these twin-tubs being so small they fit more readily into the boat, and do not strain it so much; whereas, the American tub, nearly three feet in diameter and of proportionate depth, makes a rather bulky freight for a craft whose planks are but one half-inch in thickness; for the bottom of the whale-boat is like critical ice, which will bear up a considerable distributed weight, but not very much of a concentrated one. When the painted canvas cover is clapped on the American line-tub, the boat looks as if it were pulling off with a prodigious great wedding-cake to present to the whales.\n\nBoth ends of the line are exposed; the lower end terminating in an eye-splice or loop coming up from the bottom against the side of the tub, and hanging over its edge completely disengaged from everything. This arrangement of the lower end is necessary on two accounts. First: In order to facilitate the fastening to it of an additional line from a neighboring boat, in case the stricken whale should sound so deep as to threaten to carry off the entire line originally attached to the harpoon. In these instances, the whale of course is shifted like a mug of ale, as it were, from the one boat to the other; though the first boat always hovers at hand to assist its consort. Second: This arrangement is indispensable for common safety’s sake; for were the lower end of the line in any way attached to the boat, and were the whale then to run the line out to the end almost in a single, smoking minute as he sometimes does, he would not stop there, for the doomed boat would infallibly be dragged down after him into the profundity of the sea; and in that case no town-crier would ever find her again.\n\nBefore lowering the boat for the chase, the upper end of the line is taken aft from the tub, and passing round the loggerhead there, is again carried forward the entire length of the boat, resting crosswise upon the loom or handle of every man’s oar, so that it jogs against his wrist in rowing; and also passing between the men, as they alternately sit at the opposite gunwales, to the leaded chocks or grooves in the extreme pointed prow of the boat, where a wooden pin or skewer the size of a common quill, prevents it from slipping out. From the chocks it hangs in a slight festoon over the bows, and is then passed inside the boat again; and some ten or twenty fathoms (called box-line) being coiled upon the box in the bows, it continues its way to the gunwale still a little further aft, and is then attached to the short-warp—the rope which is immediately connected with the harpoon; but previous to that connexion, the short-warp goes through sundry mystifications too tedious to detail.\n\nThus the whale-line folds the whole boat in its complicated coils, twisting and writhing around it in almost every direction. All the oarsmen are involved in its perilous contortions; so that to the timid eye of the landsman, they seem as Indian jugglers, with the deadliest snakes sportively festooning their limbs. Nor can any son of mortal woman, for the first time, seat himself amid those hempen intricacies, and while straining his utmost at the oar, bethink him that at any unknown instant the harpoon may be darted, and all these horrible contortions be put in play like ringed lightnings; he cannot be thus circumstanced without a shudder that makes the very marrow in his bones to quiver in him like a shaken jelly. Yet habit—strange thing! what cannot habit accomplish?—Gayer sallies, more merry mirth, better jokes, and brighter repartees, you never heard over your mahogany, than you will hear over the half-inch white cedar of the whale-boat, when thus hung in hangman’s nooses; and, like the six burghers of Calais before King Edward, the six men composing the crew pull into the jaws of death, with a halter around every neck, as you may say.\n\nPerhaps a very little thought will now enable you to account for those repeated whaling disasters—some few of which are casually chronicled—of this man or that man being taken out of the boat by the line, and lost. For, when the line is darting out, to be seated then in the boat, is like being seated in the midst of the manifold whizzings of a steam-engine in full play, when every flying beam, and shaft, and wheel, is grazing you. It is worse; for you cannot sit motionless in the heart of these perils, because the boat is rocking like a cradle, and you are pitched one way and the other, without the slightest warning; and only by a certain self-adjusting buoyancy and simultaneousness of volition and action, can you escape being made a Mazeppa of, and run away with where the all-seeing sun himself could never pierce you out.\n\nAgain: as the profound calm which only apparently precedes and prophesies of the storm, is perhaps more awful than the storm itself; for, indeed, the calm is but the wrapper and envelope of the storm; and contains it in itself, as the seemingly harmless rifle holds the fatal powder, and the ball, and the explosion; so the graceful repose of the line, as it silently serpentines about the oarsmen before being brought into actual play—this is a thing which carries more of true terror than any other aspect of this dangerous affair. But why say more? All men live enveloped in whale-lines. All are born with halters round their necks; but it is only when caught in the swift, sudden turn of death, that mortals realize the silent, subtle, ever-present perils of life. And if you be a philosopher, though seated in the whale-boat, you would not at heart feel one whit more of terror, than though seated before your evening fire with a poker, and not a harpoon, by your side.\n\n# CHAPTER 61. Stubb Kills a Whale.\n\nIf to Starbuck the apparition of the Squid was a thing of portents, to Queequeg it was quite a different object.\n\n“When you see him ’quid,” said the savage, honing his harpoon in the bow of his hoisted boat, “then you quick see him ’parm whale.”\n\nThe next day was exceedingly still and sultry, and with nothing special to engage them, the Pequod’s crew could hardly resist the spell of sleep induced by such a vacant sea. For this part of the Indian Ocean through which we then were voyaging is not what whalemen call a lively ground; that is, it affords fewer glimpses of porpoises, dolphins, flying-fish, and other vivacious denizens of more stirring waters, than those off the Rio de la Plata, or the in-shore ground off Peru.\n\nIt was my turn to stand at the foremast-head; and with my shoulders leaning against the slackened royal shrouds, to and fro I idly swayed in what seemed an enchanted air. No resolution could withstand it; in that dreamy mood losing all consciousness, at last my soul went out of my body; though my body still continued to sway as a pendulum will, long after the power which first moved it is withdrawn.\n\nEre forgetfulness altogether came over me, I had noticed that the seamen at the main and mizzen-mast-heads were already drowsy. So that at last all three of us lifelessly swung from the spars, and for every swing that we made there was a nod from below from the slumbering helmsman. The waves, too, nodded their indolent crests; and across the wide trance of the sea, east nodded to west, and the sun over all.\n\nSuddenly bubbles seemed bursting beneath my closed eyes; like vices my hands grasped the shrouds; some invisible, gracious agency preserved me; with a shock I came back to life. And lo! close under our lee, not forty fathoms off, a gigantic Sperm Whale lay rolling in the water like the capsized hull of a frigate, his broad, glossy back, of an Ethiopian hue, glistening in the sun’s rays like a mirror. But lazily undulating in the trough of the sea, and ever and anon tranquilly spouting his vapory jet, the whale looked like a portly burgher smoking his pipe of a warm afternoon. But that pipe, poor whale, was thy last. As if struck by some enchanter’s wand, the sleepy ship and every sleeper in it all at once started into wakefulness; and more than a score of voices from all parts of the vessel, simultaneously with the three notes from aloft, shouted forth the accustomed cry, as the great fish slowly and regularly spouted the sparkling brine into the air.\n\n“Clear away the boats! Luff!” cried Ahab. And obeying his own order, he dashed the helm down before the helmsman could handle the spokes.\n\nThe sudden exclamations of the crew must have alarmed the whale; and ere the boats were down, majestically turning, he swam away to the leeward, but with such a steady tranquillity, and making so few ripples as he swam, that thinking after all he might not as yet be alarmed, Ahab gave orders that not an oar should be used, and no man must speak but in whispers. So seated like Ontario Indians on the gunwales of the boats, we swiftly but silently paddled along; the calm not admitting of the noiseless sails being set. Presently, as we thus glided in chase, the monster perpendicularly flitted his tail forty feet into the air, and then sank out of sight like a tower swallowed up.\n\n“There go flukes!” was the cry, an announcement immediately followed by Stubb’s producing his match and igniting his pipe, for now a respite was granted. After the full interval of his sounding had elapsed, the whale rose again, and being now in advance of the smoker’s boat, and much nearer to it than to any of the others, Stubb counted upon the honor of the capture. It was obvious, now, that the whale had at length become aware of his pursuers. All silence of cautiousness was therefore no longer of use. Paddles were dropped, and oars came loudly into play. And still puffing at his pipe, Stubb cheered on his crew to the assault.\n\nYes, a mighty change had come over the fish. All alive to his jeopardy, he was going “head out”; that part obliquely projecting from the mad yeast which he brewed.*\n\n*It will be seen in some other place of what a very light substance the entire interior of the sperm whale’s enormous head consists. Though apparently the most massive, it is by far the most buoyant part about him. So that with ease he elevates it in the air, and invariably does so when going at his utmost speed. Besides, such is the breadth of the upper part of the front of his head, and such the tapering cut-water formation of the lower part, that by obliquely elevating his head, he thereby may be said to transform himself from a bluff-bowed sluggish galliot into a sharppointed New York pilot-boat.\n\n“Start her, start her, my men! Don’t hurry yourselves; take plenty of time—but start her; start her like thunder-claps, that’s all,” cried Stubb, spluttering out the smoke as he spoke. “Start her, now; give ’em the long and strong stroke, Tashtego. Start her, Tash, my boy—start her, all; but keep cool, keep cool—cucumbers is the word—easy, easy—only start her like grim death and grinning devils, and raise the buried dead perpendicular out of their graves, boys—that’s all. Start her!”\n\n“Woo-hoo! Wa-hee!” screamed the Gay-Header in reply, raising some old war-whoop to the skies; as every oarsman in the strained boat involuntarily bounced forward with the one tremendous leading stroke which the eager Indian gave.\n\nBut his wild screams were answered by others quite as wild. “Kee-hee! Kee-hee!” yelled Daggoo, straining forwards and backwards on his seat, like a pacing tiger in his cage.\n\n“Ka-la! Koo-loo!” howled Queequeg, as if smacking his lips over a mouthful of Grenadier’s steak. And thus with oars and yells the keels cut the sea. Meanwhile, Stubb retaining his place in the van, still encouraged his men to the onset, all the while puffing the smoke from his mouth. Like desperadoes they tugged and they strained, till the welcome cry was heard—“Stand up, Tashtego!—give it to him!” The harpoon was hurled. “Stern all!” The oarsmen backed water; the same moment something went hot and hissing along every one of their wrists. It was the magical line. An instant before, Stubb had swiftly caught two additional turns with it round the loggerhead, whence, by reason of its increased rapid circlings, a hempen blue smoke now jetted up and mingled with the steady fumes from his pipe. As the line passed round and round the loggerhead; so also, just before reaching that point, it blisteringly passed through and through both of Stubb’s hands, from which the hand-cloths, or squares of quilted canvas sometimes worn at these times, had accidentally dropped. It was like holding an enemy’s sharp two-edged sword by the blade, and that enemy all the time striving to wrest it out of your clutch.\n\n“Wet the line! wet the line!” cried Stubb to the tub oarsman (him seated by the tub) who, snatching off his hat, dashed sea-water into it.* More turns were taken, so that the line began holding its place. The boat now flew through the boiling water like a shark all fins. Stubb and Tashtego here changed places—stem for stern—a staggering business truly in that rocking commotion.\n\n*Partly to show the indispensableness of this act, it may here be stated, that, in the old Dutch fishery, a mop was used to dash the running line with water; in many other ships, a wooden piggin, or bailer, is set apart for that purpose. Your hat, however, is the most convenient.\n\nFrom the vibrating line extending the entire length of the upper part of the boat, and from its now being more tight than a harpstring, you would have thought the craft had two keels—one cleaving the water, the other the air—as the boat churned on through both opposing elements at once. A continual cascade played at the bows; a ceaseless whirling eddy in her wake; and, at the slightest motion from within, even but of a little finger, the vibrating, cracking craft canted over her spasmodic gunwale into the sea. Thus they rushed; each man with might and main clinging to his seat, to prevent being tossed to the foam; and the tall form of Tashtego at the steering oar crouching almost double, in order to bring down his centre of gravity. Whole Atlantics and Pacifics seemed passed as they shot on their way, till at length the whale somewhat slackened his flight.\n\n“Haul in—haul in!” cried Stubb to the bowsman! and, facing round towards the whale, all hands began pulling the boat up to him, while yet the boat was being towed on. Soon ranging up by his flank, Stubb, firmly planting his knee in the clumsy cleat, darted dart after dart into the flying fish; at the word of command, the boat alternately sterning out of the way of the whale’s horrible wallow, and then ranging up for another fling.\n\nThe red tide now poured from all sides of the monster like brooks down a hill. His tormented body rolled not in brine but in blood, which bubbled and seethed for furlongs behind in their wake. The slanting sun playing upon this crimson pond in the sea, sent back its reflection into every face, so that they all glowed to each other like red men. And all the while, jet after jet of white smoke was agonizingly shot from the spiracle of the whale, and vehement puff after puff from the mouth of the excited headsman; as at every dart, hauling in upon his crooked lance (by the line attached to it), Stubb straightened it again and again, by a few rapid blows against the gunwale, then again and again sent it into the whale.\n\n“Pull up—pull up!” he now cried to the bowsman, as the waning whale relaxed in his wrath. “Pull up!—close to!” and the boat ranged along the fish’s flank. When reaching far over the bow, Stubb slowly churned his long sharp lance into the fish, and kept it there, carefully churning and churning, as if cautiously seeking to feel after some gold watch that the whale might have swallowed, and which he was fearful of breaking ere he could hook it out. But that gold watch he sought was the innermost life of the fish. And now it is struck; for, starting from his trance into that unspeakable thing called his “flurry,” the monster horribly wallowed in his blood, overwrapped himself in impenetrable, mad, boiling spray, so that the imperilled craft, instantly dropping astern, had much ado blindly to struggle out from that phrensied twilight into the clear air of the day.\n\nAnd now abating in his flurry, the whale once more rolled out into view; surging from side to side; spasmodically dilating and contracting his spout-hole, with sharp, cracking, agonized respirations. At last, gush after gush of clotted red gore, as if it had been the purple lees of red wine, shot into the frighted air; and falling back again, ran dripping down his motionless flanks into the sea. His heart had burst!\n\n“He’s dead, Mr. Stubb,” said Daggoo.\n\n“Yes; both pipes smoked out!” and withdrawing his own from his mouth, Stubb scattered the dead ashes over the water; and, for a moment, stood thoughtfully eyeing the vast corpse he had made.\n\nA word concerning an incident in the last chapter.\n\nAccording to the invariable usage of the fishery, the whale-boat pushes off from the ship, with the headsman or whale-killer as temporary steersman, and the harpooneer or whale-fastener pulling the foremost oar, the one known as the harpooneer-oar. Now it needs a strong, nervous arm to strike the first iron into the fish; for often, in what is called a long dart, the heavy implement has to be flung to the distance of twenty or thirty feet. But however prolonged and exhausting the chase, the harpooneer is expected to pull his oar meanwhile to the uttermost; indeed, he is expected to set an example of superhuman activity to the rest, not only by incredible rowing, but by repeated loud and intrepid exclamations; and what it is to keep shouting at the top of one’s compass, while all the other muscles are strained and half started—what that is none know but those who have tried it. For one, I cannot bawl very heartily and work very recklessly at one and the same time. In this straining, bawling state, then, with his back to the fish, all at once the exhausted harpooneer hears the exciting cry—“Stand up, and give it to him!” He now has to drop and secure his oar, turn round on his centre half way, seize his harpoon from the crotch, and with what little strength may remain, he essays to pitch it somehow into the whale. No wonder, taking the whole fleet of whalemen in a body, that out of fifty fair chances for a dart, not five are successful; no wonder that so many hapless harpooneers are madly cursed and disrated; no wonder that some of them actually burst their blood-vessels in the boat; no wonder that some sperm whalemen are absent four years with four barrels; no wonder that to many ship owners, whaling is but a losing concern; for it is the harpooneer that makes the voyage, and if you take the breath out of his body how can you expect to find it there when most wanted!\n\nAgain, if the dart be successful, then at the second critical instant, that is, when the whale starts to run, the boatheader and harpooneer likewise start to running fore and aft, to the imminent jeopardy of themselves and every one else. It is then they change places; and the headsman, the chief officer of the little craft, takes his proper station in the bows of the boat.\n\nNow, I care not who maintains the contrary, but all this is both foolish and unnecessary. The headsman should stay in the bows from first to last; he should both dart the harpoon and the lance, and no rowing whatever should be expected of him, except under circumstances obvious to any fisherman. I know that this would sometimes involve a slight loss of speed in the chase; but long experience in various whalemen of more than one nation has convinced me that in the vast majority of failures in the fishery, it has not by any means been so much the speed of the whale as the before described exhaustion of the harpooneer that has caused them.\n\nTo insure the greatest efficiency in the dart, the harpooneers of this world must start to their feet from out of idleness, and not from out of toil.\n\n# CHAPTER 63. The Crotch.\n\nOut of the trunk, the branches grow; out of them, the twigs. So, in productive subjects, grow the chapters.\n\nThe crotch alluded to on a previous page deserves independent mention. It is a notched stick of a peculiar form, some two feet in length, which is perpendicularly inserted into the starboard gunwale near the bow, for the purpose of furnishing a rest for the wooden extremity of the harpoon, whose other naked, barbed end slopingly projects from the prow. Thereby the weapon is instantly at hand to its hurler, who snatches it up as readily from its rest as a backwoodsman swings his rifle from the wall. It is customary to have two harpoons reposing in the crotch, respectively called the first and second irons.\n\nBut these two harpoons, each by its own cord, are both connected with the line; the object being this: to dart them both, if possible, one instantly after the other into the same whale; so that if, in the coming drag, one should draw out, the other may still retain a hold. It is a doubling of the chances. But it very often happens that owing to the instantaneous, violent, convulsive running of the whale upon receiving the first iron, it becomes impossible for the harpooneer, however lightning-like in his movements, to pitch the second iron into him. Nevertheless, as the second iron is already connected with the line, and the line is running, hence that weapon must, at all events, be anticipatingly tossed out of the boat, somehow and somewhere; else the most terrible jeopardy would involve all hands. Tumbled into the water, it accordingly is in such cases; the spare coils of box line (mentioned in a preceding chapter) making this feat, in most instances, prudently practicable. But this critical act is not always unattended with the saddest and most fatal casualties.\n\nFurthermore: you must know that when the second iron is thrown overboard, it thenceforth becomes a dangling, sharp-edged terror, skittishly curvetting about both boat and whale, entangling the lines, or cutting them, and making a prodigious sensation in all directions. Nor, in general, is it possible to secure it again until the whale is fairly captured and a corpse.\n\nConsider, now, how it must be in the case of four boats all engaging one unusually strong, active, and knowing whale; when owing to these qualities in him, as well as to the thousand concurring accidents of such an audacious enterprise, eight or ten loose second irons may be simultaneously dangling about him. For, of course, each boat is supplied with several harpoons to bend on to the line should the first one be ineffectually darted without recovery. All these particulars are faithfully narrated here, as they will not fail to elucidate several most important, however intricate passages, in scenes hereafter to be painted.\n\n# CHAPTER 64. Stubb’s Supper.\n\nStubb’s whale had been killed some distance from the ship. It was a calm; so, forming a tandem of three boats, we commenced the slow business of towing the trophy to the Pequod. And now, as we eighteen men with our thirty-six arms, and one hundred and eighty thumbs and fingers, slowly toiled hour after hour upon that inert, sluggish corpse in the sea; and it seemed hardly to budge at all, except at long intervals; good evidence was hereby furnished of the enormousness of the mass we moved. For, upon the great canal of Hang-Ho, or whatever they call it, in China, four or five laborers on the foot-path will draw a bulky freighted junk at the rate of a mile an hour; but this grand argosy we towed heavily forged along, as if laden with pig-lead in bulk.\n\nDarkness came on; but three lights up and down in the Pequod’s main-rigging dimly guided our way; till drawing nearer we saw Ahab dropping one of several more lanterns over the bulwarks. Vacantly eyeing the heaving whale for a moment, he issued the usual orders for securing it for the night, and then handing his lantern to a seaman, went his way into the cabin, and did not come forward again until morning.\n\nThough, in overseeing the pursuit of this whale, Captain Ahab had evinced his customary activity, to call it so; yet now that the creature was dead, some vague dissatisfaction, or impatience, or despair, seemed working in him; as if the sight of that dead body reminded him that Moby Dick was yet to be slain; and though a thousand other whales were brought to his ship, all that would not one jot advance his grand, monomaniac object. Very soon you would have thought from the sound on the Pequod’s decks, that all hands were preparing to cast anchor in the deep; for heavy chains are being dragged along the deck, and thrust rattling out of the port-holes. But by those clanking links, the vast corpse itself, not the ship, is to be moored. Tied by the head to the stern, and by the tail to the bows, the whale now lies with its black hull close to the vessel’s and seen through the darkness of the night, which obscured the spars and rigging aloft, the two—ship and whale, seemed yoked together like colossal bullocks, whereof one reclines while the other remains standing.*\n\n*A little item may as well be related here. The strongest and most reliable hold which the ship has upon the whale when moored alongside, is by the flukes or tail; and as from its greater density that part is relatively heavier than any other (excepting the side-fins), its flexibility even in death, causes it to sink low beneath the surface; so that with the hand you cannot get at it from the boat, in order to put the chain round it. But this difficulty is ingeniously overcome: a small, strong line is prepared with a wooden float at its outer end, and a weight in its middle, while the other end is secured to the ship. By adroit management the wooden float is made to rise on the other side of the mass, so that now having girdled the whale, the chain is readily made to follow suit; and being slipped along the body, is at last locked fast round the smallest part of the tail, at the point of junction with its broad flukes or lobes.\n\nIf moody Ahab was now all quiescence, at least so far as could be known on deck, Stubb, his second mate, flushed with conquest, betrayed an unusual but still good-natured excitement. Such an unwonted bustle was he in that the staid Starbuck, his official superior, quietly resigned to him for the time the sole management of affairs. One small, helping cause of all this liveliness in Stubb, was soon made strangely manifest. Stubb was a high liver; he was somewhat intemperately fond of the whale as a flavorish thing to his palate.\n\n“A steak, a steak, ere I sleep! You, Daggoo! overboard you go, and cut me one from his small!”\n\nHere be it known, that though these wild fishermen do not, as a general thing, and according to the great military maxim, make the enemy defray the current expenses of the war (at least before realizing the proceeds of the voyage), yet now and then you find some of these Nantucketers who have a genuine relish for that particular part of the Sperm Whale designated by Stubb; comprising the tapering extremity of the body.\n\nAbout midnight that steak was cut and cooked; and lighted by two lanterns of sperm oil, Stubb stoutly stood up to his spermaceti supper at the capstan-head, as if that capstan were a sideboard. Nor was Stubb the only banqueter on whale’s flesh that night. Mingling their mumblings with his own mastications, thousands on thousands of sharks, swarming round the dead leviathan, smackingly feasted on its fatness. The few sleepers below in their bunks were often startled by the sharp slapping of their tails against the hull, within a few inches of the sleepers’ hearts. Peering over the side you could just see them (as before you heard them) wallowing in the sullen, black waters, and turning over on their backs as they scooped out huge globular pieces of the whale of the bigness of a human head. This particular feat of the shark seems all but miraculous. How at such an apparently unassailable surface, they contrive to gouge out such symmetrical mouthfuls, remains a part of the universal problem of all things. The mark they thus leave on the whale, may best be likened to the hollow made by a carpenter in countersinking for a screw.\n\nThough amid all the smoking horror and diabolism of a sea-fight, sharks will be seen longingly gazing up to the ship’s decks, like hungry dogs round a table where red meat is being carved, ready to bolt down every killed man that is tossed to them; and though, while the valiant butchers over the deck-table are thus cannibally carving each other’s live meat with carving-knives all gilded and tasselled, the sharks, also, with their jewel-hilted mouths, are quarrelsomely carving away under the table at the dead meat; and though, were you to turn the whole affair upside down, it would still be pretty much the same thing, that is to say, a shocking sharkish business enough for all parties; and though sharks also are the invariable outriders of all slave ships crossing the Atlantic, systematically trotting alongside, to be handy in case a parcel is to be carried anywhere, or a dead slave to be decently buried; and though one or two other like instances might be set down, touching the set terms, places, and occasions, when sharks do most socially congregate, and most hilariously feast; yet is there no conceivable time or occasion when you will find them in such countless numbers, and in gayer or more jovial spirits, than around a dead sperm whale, moored by night to a whaleship at sea. If you have never seen that sight, then suspend your decision about the propriety of devil-worship, and the expediency of conciliating the devil.\n\nBut, as yet, Stubb heeded not the mumblings of the banquet that was going on so nigh him, no more than the sharks heeded the smacking of his own epicurean lips.\n\n“Cook, cook!—where’s that old Fleece?” he cried at length, widening his legs still further, as if to form a more secure base for his supper; and, at the same time darting his fork into the dish, as if stabbing with his lance; “cook, you cook!—sail this way, cook!”\n\nThe old black, not in any very high glee at having been previously roused from his warm hammock at a most unseasonable hour, came shambling along from his galley, for, like many old blacks, there was something the matter with his knee-pans, which he did not keep well scoured like his other pans; this old Fleece, as they called him, came shuffling and limping along, assisting his step with his tongs, which, after a clumsy fashion, were made of straightened iron hoops; this old Ebony floundered along, and in obedience to the word of command, came to a dead stop on the opposite side of Stubb’s sideboard; when, with both hands folded before him, and resting on his two-legged cane, he bowed his arched back still further over, at the same time sideways inclining his head, so as to bring his best ear into play.\n\n“Cook,” said Stubb, rapidly lifting a rather reddish morsel to his mouth, “don’t you think this steak is rather overdone? You’ve been beating this steak too much, cook; it’s too tender. Don’t I always say that to be good, a whale-steak must be tough? There are those sharks now over the side, don’t you see they prefer it tough and rare? What a shindy they are kicking up! Cook, go and talk to ’em; tell ’em they are welcome to help themselves civilly, and in moderation, but they must keep quiet. Blast me, if I can hear my own voice. Away, cook, and deliver my message. Here, take this lantern,” snatching one from his sideboard; “now then, go and preach to ’em!”\n\nSullenly taking the offered lantern, old Fleece limped across the deck to the bulwarks; and then, with one hand dropping his light low over the sea, so as to get a good view of his congregation, with the other hand he solemnly flourished his tongs, and leaning far over the side in a mumbling voice began addressing the sharks, while Stubb, softly crawling behind, overheard all that was said.\n\n“Fellow-critters: I’se ordered here to say dat you must stop dat dam noise dare. You hear? Stop dat dam smackin’ ob de lip! Massa Stubb say dat you can fill your dam bellies up to de hatchings, but by Gor! you must stop dat dam racket!”\n\n“Cook,” here interposed Stubb, accompanying the word with a sudden slap on the shoulder,—“Cook! why, damn your eyes, you mustn’t swear that way when you’re preaching. That’s no way to convert sinners, cook!”\n\n“Who dat? Den preach to him yourself,” sullenly turning to go.\n\n“No, cook; go on, go on.”\n\n“Well, den, Belubed fellow-critters:”—\n\n“Right!” exclaimed Stubb, approvingly, “coax ’em to it; try that,” and Fleece continued.\n\n“Do you is all sharks, and by natur wery woracious, yet I zay to you, fellow-critters, dat dat woraciousness—’top dat dam slappin’ ob de tail! How you tink to hear, spose you keep up such a dam slappin’ and bitin’ dare?”\n\n“Cook,” cried Stubb, collaring him, “I won’t have that swearing. Talk to ’em gentlemanly.”\n\nOnce more the sermon proceeded.\n\n“Your woraciousness, fellow-critters, I don’t blame ye so much for; dat is natur, and can’t be helped; but to gobern dat wicked natur, dat is de pint. You is sharks, sartin; but if you gobern de shark in you, why den you be angel; for all angel is not’ing more dan de shark well goberned. Now, look here, bred’ren, just try wonst to be cibil, a helping yourselbs from dat whale. Don’t be tearin’ de blubber out your neighbour’s mout, I say. Is not one shark dood right as toder to dat whale? And, by Gor, none on you has de right to dat whale; dat whale belong to some one else. I know some o’ you has berry brig mout, brigger dan oders; but den de brig mouts sometimes has de small bellies; so dat de brigness of de mout is not to swaller wid, but to bit off de blubber for de small fry ob sharks, dat can’t get into de scrouge to help demselves.”\n\n“Well done, old Fleece!” cried Stubb, “that’s Christianity; go on.”\n\n“No use goin’ on; de dam willains will keep a scougin’ and slappin’ each oder, Massa Stubb; dey don’t hear one word; no use a-preachin’ to such dam g’uttons as you call ’em, till dare bellies is full, and dare bellies is bottomless; and when dey do get ’em full, dey wont hear you den; for den dey sink in de sea, go fast to sleep on de coral, and can’t hear not’ing at all, no more, for eber and eber.”\n\n“Upon my soul, I am about of the same opinion; so give the benediction, Fleece, and I’ll away to my supper.”\n\nUpon this, Fleece, holding both hands over the fishy mob, raised his shrill voice, and cried—\n\n“Cussed fellow-critters! Kick up de damndest row as ever you can; fill your dam’ bellies ’till dey bust—and den die.”\n\n“Now, cook,” said Stubb, resuming his supper at the capstan; “stand just where you stood before, there, over against me, and pay particular attention.”\n\n“All dention,” said Fleece, again stooping over upon his tongs in the desired position.\n\n“Well,” said Stubb, helping himself freely meanwhile; “I shall now go back to the subject of this steak. In the first place, how old are you, cook?”\n\n“What dat do wid de ’teak,” said the old black, testily.\n\n“Silence! How old are you, cook?”\n\n“’Bout ninety, dey say,” he gloomily muttered.\n\n“And you have lived in this world hard upon one hundred years, cook, and don’t know yet how to cook a whale-steak?” rapidly bolting another mouthful at the last word, so that morsel seemed a continuation of the question. “Where were you born, cook?”\n\n“’Hind de hatchway, in ferry-boat, goin’ ober de Roanoke.”\n\n“Born in a ferry-boat! That’s queer, too. But I want to know what country you were born in, cook!”\n\n“Didn’t I say de Roanoke country?” he cried sharply.\n\n“No, you didn’t, cook; but I’ll tell you what I’m coming to, cook. You must go home and be born over again; you don’t know how to cook a whale-steak yet.”\n\n“Bress my soul, if I cook noder one,” he growled, angrily, turning round to depart.\n\n“Come back, cook;—here, hand me those tongs;—now take that bit of steak there, and tell me if you think that steak cooked as it should be? Take it, I say”—holding the tongs towards him—“take it, and taste it.”\n\nFaintly smacking his withered lips over it for a moment, the old negro muttered, “Best cooked ’teak I eber taste; joosy, berry joosy.”\n\n“Cook,” said Stubb, squaring himself once more; “do you belong to the church?”\n\n“Passed one once in Cape-Down,” said the old man sullenly.\n\n“And you have once in your life passed a holy church in Cape-Town, where you doubtless overheard a holy parson addressing his hearers as his beloved fellow-creatures, have you, cook! And yet you come here, and tell me such a dreadful lie as you did just now, eh?” said Stubb. “Where do you expect to go to, cook?”\n\n“Go to bed berry soon,” he mumbled, half-turning as he spoke.\n\n“Avast! heave to! I mean when you die, cook. It’s an awful question. Now what’s your answer?”\n\n“When dis old brack man dies,” said the negro slowly, changing his whole air and demeanor, “he hisself won’t go nowhere; but some bressed angel will come and fetch him.”\n\n“Fetch him? How? In a coach and four, as they fetched Elijah? And fetch him where?”\n\n“Up dere,” said Fleece, holding his tongs straight over his head, and keeping it there very solemnly.\n\n“So, then, you expect to go up into our main-top, do you, cook, when you are dead? But don’t you know the higher you climb, the colder it gets? Main-top, eh?”\n\n“Didn’t say dat t’all,” said Fleece, again in the sulks.\n\n“You said up there, didn’t you? and now look yourself, and see where your tongs are pointing. But, perhaps you expect to get into heaven by crawling through the lubber’s hole, cook; but, no, no, cook, you don’t get there, except you go the regular way, round by the rigging. It’s a ticklish business, but must be done, or else it’s no go. But none of us are in heaven yet. Drop your tongs, cook, and hear my orders. Do ye hear? Hold your hat in one hand, and clap t’other a’top of your heart, when I’m giving my orders, cook. What! that your heart, there?—that’s your gizzard! Aloft! aloft!—that’s it—now you have it. Hold it there now, and pay attention.”\n\n“All ’dention,” said the old black, with both hands placed as desired, vainly wriggling his grizzled head, as if to get both ears in front at one and the same time.\n\n“Well then, cook, you see this whale-steak of yours was so very bad, that I have put it out of sight as soon as possible; you see that, don’t you? Well, for the future, when you cook another whale-steak for my private table here, the capstan, I’ll tell you what to do so as not to spoil it by overdoing. Hold the steak in one hand, and show a live coal to it with the other; that done, dish it; d’ye hear? And now to-morrow, cook, when we are cutting in the fish, be sure you stand by to get the tips of his fins; have them put in pickle. As for the ends of the flukes, have them soused, cook. There, now ye may go.”\n\nBut Fleece had hardly got three paces off, when he was recalled.\n\n“Cook, give me cutlets for supper to-morrow night in the mid-watch. D’ye hear? away you sail, then.—Halloa! stop! make a bow before you go.—Avast heaving again! Whale-balls for breakfast—don’t forget.”\n\n“Wish, by gor! whale eat him, ’stead of him eat whale. I’m bressed if he ain’t more of shark dan Massa Shark hisself,” muttered the old man, limping away; with which sage ejaculation he went to his hammock.\n\n# CHAPTER 65. The Whale as a Dish.\n\nThat mortal man should feed upon the creature that feeds his lamp, and, like Stubb, eat him by his own light, as you may say; this seems so outlandish a thing that one must needs go a little into the history and philosophy of it.\n\nIt is upon record, that three centuries ago the tongue of the Right Whale was esteemed a great delicacy in France, and commanded large prices there. Also, that in Henry VIIIth’s time, a certain cook of the court obtained a handsome reward for inventing an admirable sauce to be eaten with barbacued porpoises, which, you remember, are a species of whale. Porpoises, indeed, are to this day considered fine eating. The meat is made into balls about the size of billiard balls, and being well seasoned and spiced might be taken for turtle-balls or veal balls. The old monks of Dunfermline were very fond of them. They had a great porpoise grant from the crown.\n\nThe fact is, that among his hunters at least, the whale would by all hands be considered a noble dish, were there not so much of him; but when you come to sit down before a meat-pie nearly one hundred feet long, it takes away your appetite. Only the most unprejudiced of men like Stubb, nowadays partake of cooked whales; but the Esquimaux are not so fastidious. We all know how they live upon whales, and have rare old vintages of prime old train oil. Zogranda, one of their most famous doctors, recommends strips of blubber for infants, as being exceedingly juicy and nourishing. And this reminds me that certain Englishmen, who long ago were accidentally left in Greenland by a whaling vessel—that these men actually lived for several months on the mouldy scraps of whales which had been left ashore after trying out the blubber. Among the Dutch whalemen these scraps are called “fritters”; which, indeed, they greatly resemble, being brown and crisp, and smelling something like old Amsterdam housewives’ dough-nuts or oly-cooks, when fresh. They have such an eatable look that the most self-denying stranger can hardly keep his hands off.\n\nBut what further depreciates the whale as a civilized dish, is his exceeding richness. He is the great prize ox of the sea, too fat to be delicately good. Look at his hump, which would be as fine eating as the buffalo’s (which is esteemed a rare dish), were it not such a solid pyramid of fat. But the spermaceti itself, how bland and creamy that is; like the transparent, half-jellied, white meat of a cocoanut in the third month of its growth, yet far too rich to supply a substitute for butter. Nevertheless, many whalemen have a method of absorbing it into some other substance, and then partaking of it. In the long try watches of the night it is a common thing for the seamen to dip their ship-biscuit into the huge oil-pots and let them fry there awhile. Many a good supper have I thus made.\n\nIn the case of a small Sperm Whale the brains are accounted a fine dish. The casket of the skull is broken into with an axe, and the two plump, whitish lobes being withdrawn (precisely resembling two large puddings), they are then mixed with flour, and cooked into a most delectable mess, in flavor somewhat resembling calves’ head, which is quite a dish among some epicures; and every one knows that some young bucks among the epicures, by continually dining upon calves’ brains, by and by get to have a little brains of their own, so as to be able to tell a calf’s head from their own heads; which, indeed, requires uncommon discrimination. And that is the reason why a young buck with an intelligent looking calf’s head before him, is somehow one of the saddest sights you can see. The head looks a sort of reproachfully at him, with an “Et tu Brute!” expression.\n\nIt is not, perhaps, entirely because the whale is so excessively unctuous that landsmen seem to regard the eating of him with abhorrence; that appears to result, in some way, from the consideration before mentioned: i.e. that a man should eat a newly murdered thing of the sea, and eat it too by its own light. But no doubt the first man that ever murdered an ox was regarded as a murderer; perhaps he was hung; and if he had been put on his trial by oxen, he certainly would have been; and he certainly deserved it if any murderer does. Go to the meat-market of a Saturday night and see the crowds of live bipeds staring up at the long rows of dead quadrupeds. Does not that sight take a tooth out of the cannibal’s jaw? Cannibals? who is not a cannibal? I tell you it will be more tolerable for the Fejee that salted down a lean missionary in his cellar against a coming famine; it will be more tolerable for that provident Fejee, I say, in the day of judgment, than for thee, civilized and enlightened gourmand, who nailest geese to the ground and feastest on their bloated livers in thy paté-de-foie-gras.\n\nBut Stubb, he eats the whale by its own light, does he? and that is adding insult to injury, is it? Look at your knife-handle, there, my civilized and enlightened gourmand dining off that roast beef, what is that handle made of?—what but the bones of the brother of the very ox you are eating? And what do you pick your teeth with, after devouring that fat goose? With a feather of the same fowl. And with what quill did the Secretary of the Society for the Suppression of Cruelty to Ganders formally indite his circulars? It is only within the last month or two that that society passed a resolution to patronize nothing but steel pens.\n\n# CHAPTER 66. The Shark Massacre.\n\nWhen in the Southern Fishery, a captured Sperm Whale, after long and weary toil, is brought alongside late at night, it is not, as a general thing at least, customary to proceed at once to the business of cutting him in. For that business is an exceedingly laborious one; is not very soon completed; and requires all hands to set about it. Therefore, the common usage is to take in all sail; lash the helm a’lee; and then send every one below to his hammock till daylight, with the reservation that, until that time, anchor-watches shall be kept; that is, two and two for an hour, each couple, the crew in rotation shall mount the deck to see that all goes well.\n\nBut sometimes, especially upon the Line in the Pacific, this plan will not answer at all; because such incalculable hosts of sharks gather round the moored carcase, that were he left so for six hours, say, on a stretch, little more than the skeleton would be visible by morning. In most other parts of the ocean, however, where these fish do not so largely abound, their wondrous voracity can be at times considerably diminished, by vigorously stirring them up with sharp whaling-spades, a procedure notwithstanding, which, in some instances, only seems to tickle them into still greater activity. But it was not thus in the present case with the Pequod’s sharks; though, to be sure, any man unaccustomed to such sights, to have looked over her side that night, would have almost thought the whole round sea was one huge cheese, and those sharks the maggots in it.\n\nNevertheless, upon Stubb setting the anchor-watch after his supper was concluded; and when, accordingly, Queequeg and a forecastle seaman came on deck, no small excitement was created among the sharks; for immediately suspending the cutting stages over the side, and lowering three lanterns, so that they cast long gleams of light over the turbid sea, these two mariners, darting their long whaling-spades, kept up an incessant murdering of the sharks,* by striking the keen steel deep into their skulls, seemingly their only vital part. But in the foamy confusion of their mixed and struggling hosts, the marksmen could not always hit their mark; and this brought about new revelations of the incredible ferocity of the foe. They viciously snapped, not only at each other’s disembowelments, but like flexible bows, bent round, and bit their own; till those entrails seemed swallowed over and over again by the same mouth, to be oppositely voided by the gaping wound. Nor was this all. It was unsafe to meddle with the corpses and ghosts of these creatures. A sort of generic or Pantheistic vitality seemed to lurk in their very joints and bones, after what might be called the individual life had departed. Killed and hoisted on deck for the sake of his skin, one of these sharks almost took poor Queequeg’s hand off, when he tried to shut down the dead lid of his murderous jaw.\n\n*The whaling-spade used for cutting-in is made of the very best steel; is about the bigness of a man’s spread hand; and in general shape, corresponds to the garden implement after which it is named; only its sides are perfectly flat, and its upper end considerably narrower than the lower. This weapon is always kept as sharp as possible; and when being used is occasionally honed, just like a razor. In its socket, a stiff pole, from twenty to thirty feet long, is inserted for a handle.\n\n“Queequeg no care what god made him shark,” said the savage, agonizingly lifting his hand up and down; “wedder Fejee god or Nantucket god; but de god wat made shark must be one dam Ingin.”\n\n# CHAPTER 67. Cutting In.\n\nIt was a Saturday night, and such a Sabbath as followed! Ex officio professors of Sabbath breaking are all whalemen. The ivory Pequod was turned into what seemed a shamble; every sailor a butcher. You would have thought we were offering up ten thousand red oxen to the sea gods.\n\nIn the first place, the enormous cutting tackles, among other ponderous things comprising a cluster of blocks generally painted green, and which no single man can possibly lift—this vast bunch of grapes was swayed up to the main-top and firmly lashed to the lower mast-head, the strongest point anywhere above a ship’s deck. The end of the hawser-like rope winding through these intricacies, was then conducted to the windlass, and the huge lower block of the tackles was swung over the whale; to this block the great blubber hook, weighing some one hundred pounds, was attached. And now suspended in stages over the side, Starbuck and Stubb, the mates, armed with their long spades, began cutting a hole in the body for the insertion of the hook just above the nearest of the two side-fins. This done, a broad, semicircular line is cut round the hole, the hook is inserted, and the main body of the crew striking up a wild chorus, now commence heaving in one dense crowd at the windlass. When instantly, the entire ship careens over on her side; every bolt in her starts like the nail-heads of an old house in frosty weather; she trembles, quivers, and nods her frighted mast-heads to the sky. More and more she leans over to the whale, while every gasping heave of the windlass is answered by a helping heave from the billows; till at last, a swift, startling snap is heard; with a great swash the ship rolls upwards and backwards from the whale, and the triumphant tackle rises into sight dragging after it the disengaged semicircular end of the first strip of blubber. Now as the blubber envelopes the whale precisely as the rind does an orange, so is it stripped off from the body precisely as an orange is sometimes stripped by spiralizing it. For the strain constantly kept up by the windlass continually keeps the whale rolling over and over in the water, and as the blubber in one strip uniformly peels off along the line called the “scarf,” simultaneously cut by the spades of Starbuck and Stubb, the mates; and just as fast as it is thus peeled off, and indeed by that very act itself, it is all the time being hoisted higher and higher aloft till its upper end grazes the main-top; the men at the windlass then cease heaving, and for a moment or two the prodigious blood-dripping mass sways to and fro as if let down from the sky, and every one present must take good heed to dodge it when it swings, else it may box his ears and pitch him headlong overboard.\n\nOne of the attending harpooneers now advances with a long, keen weapon called a boarding-sword, and watching his chance he dexterously slices out a considerable hole in the lower part of the swaying mass. Into this hole, the end of the second alternating great tackle is then hooked so as to retain a hold upon the blubber, in order to prepare for what follows. Whereupon, this accomplished swordsman, warning all hands to stand off, once more makes a scientific dash at the mass, and with a few sidelong, desperate, lunging slicings, severs it completely in twain; so that while the short lower part is still fast, the long upper strip, called a blanket-piece, swings clear, and is all ready for lowering. The heavers forward now resume their song, and while the one tackle is peeling and hoisting a second strip from the whale, the other is slowly slackened away, and down goes the first strip through the main hatchway right beneath, into an unfurnished parlor called the blubber-room. Into this twilight apartment sundry nimble hands keep coiling away the long blanket-piece as if it were a great live mass of plaited serpents. And thus the work proceeds; the two tackles hoisting and lowering simultaneously; both whale and windlass heaving, the heavers singing, the blubber-room gentlemen coiling, the mates scarfing, the ship straining, and all hands swearing occasionally, by way of assuaging the general friction.\n\n# CHAPTER 68. The Blanket.\n\nI have given no small attention to that not unvexed subject, the skin of the whale. I have had controversies about it with experienced whalemen afloat, and learned naturalists ashore. My original opinion remains unchanged; but it is only an opinion.\n\nThe question is, what and where is the skin of the whale? Already you know what his blubber is. That blubber is something of the consistence of firm, close-grained beef, but tougher, more elastic and compact, and ranges from eight or ten to twelve and fifteen inches in thickness.\n\nNow, however preposterous it may at first seem to talk of any creature’s skin as being of that sort of consistence and thickness, yet in point of fact these are no arguments against such a presumption; because you cannot raise any other dense enveloping layer from the whale’s body but that same blubber; and the outermost enveloping layer of any animal, if reasonably dense, what can that be but the skin? True, from the unmarred dead body of the whale, you may scrape off with your hand an infinitely thin, transparent substance, somewhat resembling the thinnest shreds of isinglass, only it is almost as flexible and soft as satin; that is, previous to being dried, when it not only contracts and thickens, but becomes rather hard and brittle. I have several such dried bits, which I use for marks in my whale-books. It is transparent, as I said before; and being laid upon the printed page, I have sometimes pleased myself with fancying it exerted a magnifying influence. At any rate, it is pleasant to read about whales through their own spectacles, as you may say. But what I am driving at here is this. That same infinitely thin, isinglass substance, which, I admit, invests the entire body of the whale, is not so much to be regarded as the skin of the creature, as the skin of the skin, so to speak; for it were simply ridiculous to say, that the proper skin of the tremendous whale is thinner and more tender than the skin of a new-born child. But no more of this.\n\nAssuming the blubber to be the skin of the whale; then, when this skin, as in the case of a very large Sperm Whale, will yield the bulk of one hundred barrels of oil; and, when it is considered that, in quantity, or rather weight, that oil, in its expressed state, is only three fourths, and not the entire substance of the coat; some idea may hence be had of the enormousness of that animated mass, a mere part of whose mere integument yields such a lake of liquid as that. Reckoning ten barrels to the ton, you have ten tons for the net weight of only three quarters of the stuff of the whale’s skin.\n\nIn life, the visible surface of the Sperm Whale is not the least among the many marvels he presents. Almost invariably it is all over obliquely crossed and re-crossed with numberless straight marks in thick array, something like those in the finest Italian line engravings. But these marks do not seem to be impressed upon the isinglass substance above mentioned, but seem to be seen through it, as if they were engraved upon the body itself. Nor is this all. In some instances, to the quick, observant eye, those linear marks, as in a veritable engraving, but afford the ground for far other delineations. These are hieroglyphical; that is, if you call those mysterious cyphers on the walls of pyramids hieroglyphics, then that is the proper word to use in the present connexion. By my retentive memory of the hieroglyphics upon one Sperm Whale in particular, I was much struck with a plate representing the old Indian characters chiselled on the famous hieroglyphic palisades on the banks of the Upper Mississippi. Like those mystic rocks, too, the mystic-marked whale remains undecipherable. This allusion to the Indian rocks reminds me of another thing. Besides all the other phenomena which the exterior of the Sperm Whale presents, he not seldom displays the back, and more especially his flanks, effaced in great part of the regular linear appearance, by reason of numerous rude scratches, altogether of an irregular, random aspect. I should say that those New England rocks on the sea-coast, which Agassiz imagines to bear the marks of violent scraping contact with vast floating icebergs—I should say, that those rocks must not a little resemble the Sperm Whale in this particular. It also seems to me that such scratches in the whale are probably made by hostile contact with other whales; for I have most remarked them in the large, full-grown bulls of the species.\n\nA word or two more concerning this matter of the skin or blubber of the whale. It has already been said, that it is stript from him in long pieces, called blanket-pieces. Like most sea-terms, this one is very happy and significant. For the whale is indeed wrapt up in his blubber as in a real blanket or counterpane; or, still better, an Indian poncho slipt over his head, and skirting his extremity. It is by reason of this cosy blanketing of his body, that the whale is enabled to keep himself comfortable in all weathers, in all seas, times, and tides. What would become of a Greenland whale, say, in those shuddering, icy seas of the North, if unsupplied with his cosy surtout? True, other fish are found exceedingly brisk in those Hyperborean waters; but these, be it observed, are your cold-blooded, lungless fish, whose very bellies are refrigerators; creatures, that warm themselves under the lee of an iceberg, as a traveller in winter would bask before an inn fire; whereas, like man, the whale has lungs and warm blood. Freeze his blood, and he dies. How wonderful is it then—except after explanation—that this great monster, to whom corporeal warmth is as indispensable as it is to man; how wonderful that he should be found at home, immersed to his lips for life in those Arctic waters! where, when seamen fall overboard, they are sometimes found, months afterwards, perpendicularly frozen into the hearts of fields of ice, as a fly is found glued in amber. But more surprising is it to know, as has been proved by experiment, that the blood of a Polar whale is warmer than that of a Borneo negro in summer.\n\nIt does seem to me, that herein we see the rare virtue of a strong individual vitality, and the rare virtue of thick walls, and the rare virtue of interior spaciousness. Oh, man! admire and model thyself after the whale! Do thou, too, remain warm among ice. Do thou, too, live in this world without being of it. Be cool at the equator; keep thy blood fluid at the Pole. Like the great dome of St. Peter’s, and like the great whale, retain, O man! in all seasons a temperature of thine own.\n\nBut how easy and how hopeless to teach these fine things! Of erections, how few are domed like St. Peter’s! of creatures, how few vast as the whale!\n\n“Haul in the chains! Let the carcase go astern!”\n\nThe vast tackles have now done their duty. The peeled white body of the beheaded whale flashes like a marble sepulchre; though changed in hue, it has not perceptibly lost anything in bulk. It is still colossal. Slowly it floats more and more away, the water round it torn and splashed by the insatiate sharks, and the air above vexed with rapacious flights of screaming fowls, whose beaks are like so many insulting poniards in the whale. The vast white headless phantom floats further and further from the ship, and every rod that it so floats, what seem square roods of sharks and cubic roods of fowls, augment the murderous din. For hours and hours from the almost stationary ship that hideous sight is seen. Beneath the unclouded and mild azure sky, upon the fair face of the pleasant sea, wafted by the joyous breezes, that great mass of death floats on and on, till lost in infinite perspectives.\n\nThere’s a most doleful and most mocking funeral! The sea-vultures all in pious mourning, the air-sharks all punctiliously in black or speckled. In life but few of them would have helped the whale, I ween, if peradventure he had needed it; but upon the banquet of his funeral they most piously do pounce. Oh, horrible vultureism of earth! from which not the mightiest whale is free.\n\nNor is this the end. Desecrated as the body is, a vengeful ghost survives and hovers over it to scare. Espied by some timid man-of-war or blundering discovery-vessel from afar, when the distance obscuring the swarming fowls, nevertheless still shows the white mass floating in the sun, and the white spray heaving high against it; straightway the whale’s unharming corpse, with trembling fingers is set down in the log—shoals, rocks, and breakers hereabouts: beware! And for years afterwards, perhaps, ships shun the place; leaping over it as silly sheep leap over a vacuum, because their leader originally leaped there when a stick was held. There’s your law of precedents; there’s your utility of traditions; there’s the story of your obstinate survival of old beliefs never bottomed on the earth, and now not even hovering in the air! There’s orthodoxy!\n\nThus, while in life the great whale’s body may have been a real terror to his foes, in his death his ghost becomes a powerless panic to a world.\n\nAre you a believer in ghosts, my friend? There are other ghosts than the Cock-Lane one, and far deeper men than Doctor Johnson who believe in them.\n\n# CHAPTER 70. The Sphynx.\n\nIt should not have been omitted that previous to completely stripping the body of the leviathan, he was beheaded. Now, the beheading of the Sperm Whale is a scientific anatomical feat, upon which experienced whale surgeons very much pride themselves: and not without reason.\n\nConsider that the whale has nothing that can properly be called a neck; on the contrary, where his head and body seem to join, there, in that very place, is the thickest part of him. Remember, also, that the surgeon must operate from above, some eight or ten feet intervening between him and his subject, and that subject almost hidden in a discoloured, rolling, and oftentimes tumultuous and bursting sea. Bear in mind, too, that under these untoward circumstances he has to cut many feet deep in the flesh; and in that subterraneous manner, without so much as getting one single peep into the ever-contracting gash thus made, he must skilfully steer clear of all adjacent, interdicted parts, and exactly divide the spine at a critical point hard by its insertion into the skull. Do you not marvel, then, at Stubb’s boast, that he demanded but ten minutes to behead a sperm whale?\n\nWhen first severed, the head is dropped astern and held there by a cable till the body is stripped. That done, if it belong to a small whale it is hoisted on deck to be deliberately disposed of. But, with a full grown leviathan this is impossible; for the sperm whale’s head embraces nearly one third of his entire bulk, and completely to suspend such a burden as that, even by the immense tackles of a whaler, this were as vain a thing as to attempt weighing a Dutch barn in jewellers’ scales.\n\nThe Pequod’s whale being decapitated and the body stripped, the head was hoisted against the ship’s side—about half way out of the sea, so that it might yet in great part be buoyed up by its native element. And there with the strained craft steeply leaning over to it, by reason of the enormous downward drag from the lower mast-head, and every yard-arm on that side projecting like a crane over the waves; there, that blood-dripping head hung to the Pequod’s waist like the giant Holofernes’s from the girdle of Judith.\n\nWhen this last task was accomplished it was noon, and the seamen went below to their dinner. Silence reigned over the before tumultuous but now deserted deck. An intense copper calm, like a universal yellow lotus, was more and more unfolding its noiseless measureless leaves upon the sea.\n\nA short space elapsed, and up into this noiselessness came Ahab alone from his cabin. Taking a few turns on the quarter-deck, he paused to gaze over the side, then slowly getting into the main-chains he took Stubb’s long spade—still remaining there after the whale’s decapitation—and striking it into the lower part of the half-suspended mass, placed its other end crutch-wise under one arm, and so stood leaning over with eyes attentively fixed on this head.\n\nIt was a black and hooded head; and hanging there in the midst of so intense a calm, it seemed the Sphynx’s in the desert. “Speak, thou vast and venerable head,” muttered Ahab, “which, though ungarnished with a beard, yet here and there lookest hoary with mosses; speak, mighty head, and tell us the secret thing that is in thee. Of all divers, thou hast dived the deepest. That head upon which the upper sun now gleams, has moved amid this world’s foundations. Where unrecorded names and navies rust, and untold hopes and anchors rot; where in her murderous hold this frigate earth is ballasted with bones of millions of the drowned; there, in that awful water-land, there was thy most familiar home. Thou hast been where bell or diver never went; hast slept by many a sailor’s side, where sleepless mothers would give their lives to lay them down. Thou saw’st the locked lovers when leaping from their flaming ship; heart to heart they sank beneath the exulting wave; true to each other, when heaven seemed false to them. Thou saw’st the murdered mate when tossed by pirates from the midnight deck; for hours he fell into the deeper midnight of the insatiate maw; and his murderers still sailed on unharmed—while swift lightnings shivered the neighboring ship that would have borne a righteous husband to outstretched, longing arms. O head! thou hast seen enough to split the planets and make an infidel of Abraham, and not one syllable is thine!”\n\n“Sail ho!” cried a triumphant voice from the main-mast-head.\n\n“Aye? Well, now, that’s cheering,” cried Ahab, suddenly erecting himself, while whole thunder-clouds swept aside from his brow. “That lively cry upon this deadly calm might almost convert a better man.—Where away?”\n\n“Three points on the starboard bow, sir, and bringing down her breeze to us!\n\n“Better and better, man. Would now St. Paul would come along that way, and to my breezelessness bring his breeze! O Nature, and O soul of man! how far beyond all utterance are your linked analogies! not the smallest atom stirs or lives on matter, but has its cunning duplicate in mind.”\n\n# CHAPTER 71. The Jeroboam’s Story.\n\nHand in hand, ship and breeze blew on; but the breeze came faster than the ship, and soon the Pequod began to rock.\n\nBy and by, through the glass the stranger’s boats and manned mast-heads proved her a whale-ship. But as she was so far to windward, and shooting by, apparently making a passage to some other ground, the Pequod could not hope to reach her. So the signal was set to see what response would be made.\n\nHere be it said, that like the vessels of military marines, the ships of the American Whale Fleet have each a private signal; all which signals being collected in a book with the names of the respective vessels attached, every captain is provided with it. Thereby, the whale commanders are enabled to recognise each other upon the ocean, even at considerable distances and with no small facility.\n\nThe Pequod’s signal was at last responded to by the stranger’s setting her own; which proved the ship to be the Jeroboam of Nantucket. Squaring her yards, she bore down, ranged abeam under the Pequod’s lee, and lowered a boat; it soon drew nigh; but, as the side-ladder was being rigged by Starbuck’s order to accommodate the visiting captain, the stranger in question waved his hand from his boat’s stern in token of that proceeding being entirely unnecessary. It turned out that the Jeroboam had a malignant epidemic on board, and that Mayhew, her captain, was fearful of infecting the Pequod’s company. For, though himself and boat’s crew remained untainted, and though his ship was half a rifle-shot off, and an incorruptible sea and air rolling and flowing between; yet conscientiously adhering to the timid quarantine of the land, he peremptorily refused to come into direct contact with the Pequod.\n\nBut this did by no means prevent all communications. Preserving an interval of some few yards between itself and the ship, the Jeroboam’s boat by the occasional use of its oars contrived to keep parallel to the Pequod, as she heavily forged through the sea (for by this time it blew very fresh), with her main-topsail aback; though, indeed, at times by the sudden onset of a large rolling wave, the boat would be pushed some way ahead; but would be soon skilfully brought to her proper bearings again. Subject to this, and other the like interruptions now and then, a conversation was sustained between the two parties; but at intervals not without still another interruption of a very different sort.\n\nPulling an oar in the Jeroboam’s boat, was a man of a singular appearance, even in that wild whaling life where individual notabilities make up all totalities. He was a small, short, youngish man, sprinkled all over his face with freckles, and wearing redundant yellow hair. A long-skirted, cabalistically-cut coat of a faded walnut tinge enveloped him; the overlapping sleeves of which were rolled up on his wrists. A deep, settled, fanatic delirium was in his eyes.\n\nSo soon as this figure had been first descried, Stubb had exclaimed—“That’s he! that’s he!—the long-togged scaramouch the Town-Ho’s company told us of!” Stubb here alluded to a strange story told of the Jeroboam, and a certain man among her crew, some time previous when the Pequod spoke the Town-Ho. According to this account and what was subsequently learned, it seemed that the scaramouch in question had gained a wonderful ascendency over almost everybody in the Jeroboam. His story was this:\n\nHe had been originally nurtured among the crazy society of Neskyeuna Shakers, where he had been a great prophet; in their cracked, secret meetings having several times descended from heaven by the way of a trap-door, announcing the speedy opening of the seventh vial, which he carried in his vest-pocket; but, which, instead of containing gunpowder, was supposed to be charged with laudanum. A strange, apostolic whim having seized him, he had left Neskyeuna for Nantucket, where, with that cunning peculiar to craziness, he assumed a steady, common-sense exterior, and offered himself as a green-hand candidate for the Jeroboam’s whaling voyage. They engaged him; but straightway upon the ship’s getting out of sight of land, his insanity broke out in a freshet. He announced himself as the archangel Gabriel, and commanded the captain to jump overboard. He published his manifesto, whereby he set himself forth as the deliverer of the isles of the sea and vicar-general of all Oceanica. The unflinching earnestness with which he declared these things;—the dark, daring play of his sleepless, excited imagination, and all the preternatural terrors of real delirium, united to invest this Gabriel in the minds of the majority of the ignorant crew, with an atmosphere of sacredness. Moreover, they were afraid of him. As such a man, however, was not of much practical use in the ship, especially as he refused to work except when he pleased, the incredulous captain would fain have been rid of him; but apprised that that individual’s intention was to land him in the first convenient port, the archangel forthwith opened all his seals and vials—devoting the ship and all hands to unconditional perdition, in case this intention was carried out. So strongly did he work upon his disciples among the crew, that at last in a body they went to the captain and told him if Gabriel was sent from the ship, not a man of them would remain. He was therefore forced to relinquish his plan. Nor would they permit Gabriel to be any way maltreated, say or do what he would; so that it came to pass that Gabriel had the complete freedom of the ship. The consequence of all this was, that the archangel cared little or nothing for the captain and mates; and since the epidemic had broken out, he carried a higher hand than ever; declaring that the plague, as he called it, was at his sole command; nor should it be stayed but according to his good pleasure. The sailors, mostly poor devils, cringed, and some of them fawned before him; in obedience to his instructions, sometimes rendering him personal homage, as to a god. Such things may seem incredible; but, however wondrous, they are true. Nor is the history of fanatics half so striking in respect to the measureless self-deception of the fanatic himself, as his measureless power of deceiving and bedevilling so many others. But it is time to return to the Pequod.\n\n“I fear not thy epidemic, man,” said Ahab from the bulwarks, to Captain Mayhew, who stood in the boat’s stern; “come on board.”\n\nBut now Gabriel started to his feet.\n\n“Think, think of the fevers, yellow and bilious! Beware of the horrible plague!”\n\n“Gabriel! Gabriel!” cried Captain Mayhew; “thou must either—” But that instant a headlong wave shot the boat far ahead, and its seethings drowned all speech.\n\n“Hast thou seen the White Whale?” demanded Ahab, when the boat drifted back.\n\n“Think, think of thy whale-boat, stoven and sunk! Beware of the horrible tail!”\n\n“I tell thee again, Gabriel, that—” But again the boat tore ahead as if dragged by fiends. Nothing was said for some moments, while a succession of riotous waves rolled by, which by one of those occasional caprices of the seas were tumbling, not heaving it. Meantime, the hoisted sperm whale’s head jogged about very violently, and Gabriel was seen eyeing it with rather more apprehensiveness than his archangel nature seemed to warrant.\n\nWhen this interlude was over, Captain Mayhew began a dark story concerning Moby Dick; not, however, without frequent interruptions from Gabriel, whenever his name was mentioned, and the crazy sea that seemed leagued with him.\n\nIt seemed that the Jeroboam had not long left home, when upon speaking a whale-ship, her people were reliably apprised of the existence of Moby Dick, and the havoc he had made. Greedily sucking in this intelligence, Gabriel solemnly warned the captain against attacking the White Whale, in case the monster should be seen; in his gibbering insanity, pronouncing the White Whale to be no less a being than the Shaker God incarnated; the Shakers receiving the Bible. But when, some year or two afterwards, Moby Dick was fairly sighted from the mast-heads, Macey, the chief mate, burned with ardour to encounter him; and the captain himself being not unwilling to let him have the opportunity, despite all the archangel’s denunciations and forewarnings, Macey succeeded in persuading five men to man his boat. With them he pushed off; and, after much weary pulling, and many perilous, unsuccessful onsets, he at last succeeded in getting one iron fast. Meantime, Gabriel, ascending to the main-royal mast-head, was tossing one arm in frantic gestures, and hurling forth prophecies of speedy doom to the sacrilegious assailants of his divinity. Now, while Macey, the mate, was standing up in his boat’s bow, and with all the reckless energy of his tribe was venting his wild exclamations upon the whale, and essaying to get a fair chance for his poised lance, lo! a broad white shadow rose from the sea; by its quick, fanning motion, temporarily taking the breath out of the bodies of the oarsmen. Next instant, the luckless mate, so full of furious life, was smitten bodily into the air, and making a long arc in his descent, fell into the sea at the distance of about fifty yards. Not a chip of the boat was harmed, nor a hair of any oarsman’s head; but the mate for ever sank.\n\nIt is well to parenthesize here, that of the fatal accidents in the Sperm-Whale Fishery, this kind is perhaps almost as frequent as any. Sometimes, nothing is injured but the man who is thus annihilated; oftener the boat’s bow is knocked off, or the thigh-board, in which the headsman stands, is torn from its place and accompanies the body. But strangest of all is the circumstance, that in more instances than one, when the body has been recovered, not a single mark of violence is discernible; the man being stark dead.\n\nThe whole calamity, with the falling form of Macey, was plainly descried from the ship. Raising a piercing shriek—“The vial! the vial!” Gabriel called off the terror-stricken crew from the further hunting of the whale. This terrible event clothed the archangel with added influence; because his credulous disciples believed that he had specifically fore-announced it, instead of only making a general prophecy, which any one might have done, and so have chanced to hit one of many marks in the wide margin allowed. He became a nameless terror to the ship.\n\nMayhew having concluded his narration, Ahab put such questions to him, that the stranger captain could not forbear inquiring whether he intended to hunt the White Whale, if opportunity should offer. To which Ahab answered—“Aye.” Straightway, then, Gabriel once more started to his feet, glaring upon the old man, and vehemently exclaimed, with downward pointed finger—“Think, think of the blasphemer—dead, and down there!—beware of the blasphemer’s end!”\n\nAhab stolidly turned aside; then said to Mayhew, “Captain, I have just bethought me of my letter-bag; there is a letter for one of thy officers, if I mistake not. Starbuck, look over the bag.”\n\nEvery whale-ship takes out a goodly number of letters for various ships, whose delivery to the persons to whom they may be addressed, depends upon the mere chance of encountering them in the four oceans. Thus, most letters never reach their mark; and many are only received after attaining an age of two or three years or more.\n\nSoon Starbuck returned with a letter in his hand. It was sorely tumbled, damp, and covered with a dull, spotted, green mould, in consequence of being kept in a dark locker of the cabin. Of such a letter, Death himself might well have been the post-boy.\n\n“Can’st not read it?” cried Ahab. “Give it me, man. Aye, aye, it’s but a dim scrawl;—what’s this?” As he was studying it out, Starbuck took a long cutting-spade pole, and with his knife slightly split the end, to insert the letter there, and in that way, hand it to the boat, without its coming any closer to the ship.\n\nMeantime, Ahab holding the letter, muttered, “Mr. Har—yes, Mr. Harry—(a woman’s pinny hand,—the man’s wife, I’ll wager)—Aye—Mr. Harry Macey, Ship Jeroboam;—why it’s Macey, and he’s dead!”\n\n“Poor fellow! poor fellow! and from his wife,” sighed Mayhew; “but let me have it.”\n\n“Nay, keep it thyself,” cried Gabriel to Ahab; “thou art soon going that way.”\n\n“Curses throttle thee!” yelled Ahab. “Captain Mayhew, stand by now to receive it”; and taking the fatal missive from Starbuck’s hands, he caught it in the slit of the pole, and reached it over towards the boat. But as he did so, the oarsmen expectantly desisted from rowing; the boat drifted a little towards the ship’s stern; so that, as if by magic, the letter suddenly ranged along with Gabriel’s eager hand. He clutched it in an instant, seized the boat-knife, and impaling the letter on it, sent it thus loaded back into the ship. It fell at Ahab’s feet. Then Gabriel shrieked out to his comrades to give way with their oars, and in that manner the mutinous boat rapidly shot away from the Pequod.\n\nAs, after this interlude, the seamen resumed their work upon the jacket of the whale, many strange things were hinted in reference to this wild affair.\n\n# CHAPTER 72. The Monkey-Rope.\n\nIn the tumultuous business of cutting-in and attending to a whale, there is much running backwards and forwards among the crew. Now hands are wanted here, and then again hands are wanted there. There is no staying in any one place; for at one and the same time everything has to be done everywhere. It is much the same with him who endeavors the description of the scene. We must now retrace our way a little. It was mentioned that upon first breaking ground in the whale’s back, the blubber-hook was inserted into the original hole there cut by the spades of the mates. But how did so clumsy and weighty a mass as that same hook get fixed in that hole? It was inserted there by my particular friend Queequeg, whose duty it was, as harpooneer, to descend upon the monster’s back for the special purpose referred to. But in very many cases, circumstances require that the harpooneer shall remain on the whale till the whole flensing or stripping operation is concluded. The whale, be it observed, lies almost entirely submerged, excepting the immediate parts operated upon. So down there, some ten feet below the level of the deck, the poor harpooneer flounders about, half on the whale and half in the water, as the vast mass revolves like a tread-mill beneath him. On the occasion in question, Queequeg figured in the Highland costume—a shirt and socks—in which to my eyes, at least, he appeared to uncommon advantage; and no one had a better chance to observe him, as will presently be seen.\n\nBeing the savage’s bowsman, that is, the person who pulled the bow-oar in his boat (the second one from forward), it was my cheerful duty to attend upon him while taking that hard-scrabble scramble upon the dead whale’s back. You have seen Italian organ-boys holding a dancing-ape by a long cord. Just so, from the ship’s steep side, did I hold Queequeg down there in the sea, by what is technically called in the fishery a monkey-rope, attached to a strong strip of canvas belted round his waist.\n\nIt was a humorously perilous business for both of us. For, before we proceed further, it must be said that the monkey-rope was fast at both ends; fast to Queequeg’s broad canvas belt, and fast to my narrow leather one. So that for better or for worse, we two, for the time, were wedded; and should poor Queequeg sink to rise no more, then both usage and honor demanded, that instead of cutting the cord, it should drag me down in his wake. So, then, an elongated Siamese ligature united us. Queequeg was my own inseparable twin brother; nor could I any way get rid of the dangerous liabilities which the hempen bond entailed.\n\nSo strongly and metaphysically did I conceive of my situation then, that while earnestly watching his motions, I seemed distinctly to perceive that my own individuality was now merged in a joint stock company of two; that my free will had received a mortal wound; and that another’s mistake or misfortune might plunge innocent me into unmerited disaster and death. Therefore, I saw that here was a sort of interregnum in Providence; for its even-handed equity never could have so gross an injustice. And yet still further pondering—while I jerked him now and then from between the whale and ship, which would threaten to jam him—still further pondering, I say, I saw that this situation of mine was the precise situation of every mortal that breathes; only, in most cases, he, one way or other, has this Siamese connexion with a plurality of other mortals. If your banker breaks, you snap; if your apothecary by mistake sends you poison in your pills, you die. True, you may say that, by exceeding caution, you may possibly escape these and the multitudinous other evil chances of life. But handle Queequeg’s monkey-rope heedfully as I would, sometimes he jerked it so, that I came very near sliding overboard. Nor could I possibly forget that, do what I would, I only had the management of one end of it.*\n\n*The monkey-rope is found in all whalers; but it was only in the Pequod that the monkey and his holder were ever tied together. This improvement upon the original usage was introduced by no less a man than Stubb, in order to afford the imperilled harpooneer the strongest possible guarantee for the faithfulness and vigilance of his monkey-rope holder.\n\nI have hinted that I would often jerk poor Queequeg from between the whale and the ship—where he would occasionally fall, from the incessant rolling and swaying of both. But this was not the only jamming jeopardy he was exposed to. Unappalled by the massacre made upon them during the night, the sharks now freshly and more keenly allured by the before pent blood which began to flow from the carcass—the rabid creatures swarmed round it like bees in a beehive.\n\nAnd right in among those sharks was Queequeg; who often pushed them aside with his floundering feet. A thing altogether incredible were it not that attracted by such prey as a dead whale, the otherwise miscellaneously carnivorous shark will seldom touch a man.\n\nNevertheless, it may well be believed that since they have such a ravenous finger in the pie, it is deemed but wise to look sharp to them. Accordingly, besides the monkey-rope, with which I now and then jerked the poor fellow from too close a vicinity to the maw of what seemed a peculiarly ferocious shark—he was provided with still another protection. Suspended over the side in one of the stages, Tashtego and Daggoo continually flourished over his head a couple of keen whale-spades, wherewith they slaughtered as many sharks as they could reach. This procedure of theirs, to be sure, was very disinterested and benevolent of them. They meant Queequeg’s best happiness, I admit; but in their hasty zeal to befriend him, and from the circumstance that both he and the sharks were at times half hidden by the blood-muddled water, those indiscreet spades of theirs would come nearer amputating a leg than a tail. But poor Queequeg, I suppose, straining and gasping there with that great iron hook—poor Queequeg, I suppose, only prayed to his Yojo, and gave up his life into the hands of his gods.\n\nWell, well, my dear comrade and twin-brother, thought I, as I drew in and then slacked off the rope to every swell of the sea—what matters it, after all? Are you not the precious image of each and all of us men in this whaling world? That unsounded ocean you gasp in, is Life; those sharks, your foes; those spades, your friends; and what between sharks and spades you are in a sad pickle and peril, poor lad.\n\nBut courage! there is good cheer in store for you, Queequeg. For now, as with blue lips and blood-shot eyes the exhausted savage at last climbs up the chains and stands all dripping and involuntarily trembling over the side; the steward advances, and with a benevolent, consolatory glance hands him—what? Some hot Cognac? No! hands him, ye gods! hands him a cup of tepid ginger and water!\n\n“Ginger? Do I smell ginger?” suspiciously asked Stubb, coming near. “Yes, this must be ginger,” peering into the as yet untasted cup. Then standing as if incredulous for a while, he calmly walked towards the astonished steward slowly saying, “Ginger? ginger? and will you have the goodness to tell me, Mr. Dough-Boy, where lies the virtue of ginger? Ginger! is ginger the sort of fuel you use, Dough-boy, to kindle a fire in this shivering cannibal? Ginger!—what the devil is ginger? Sea-coal? firewood?—lucifer matches?—tinder?—gunpowder?—what the devil is ginger, I say, that you offer this cup to our poor Queequeg here.”\n\n“There is some sneaking Temperance Society movement about this business,” he suddenly added, now approaching Starbuck, who had just come from forward. “Will you look at that kannakin, sir: smell of it, if you please.” Then watching the mate’s countenance, he added, “The steward, Mr. Starbuck, had the face to offer that calomel and jalap to Queequeg, there, this instant off the whale. Is the steward an apothecary, sir? and may I ask whether this is the sort of bitters by which he blows back the life into a half-drowned man?”\n\n“I trust not,” said Starbuck, “it is poor stuff enough.”\n\n“Aye, aye, steward,” cried Stubb, “we’ll teach you to drug a harpooneer; none of your apothecary’s medicine here; you want to poison us, do ye? You have got out insurances on our lives and want to murder us all, and pocket the proceeds, do ye?”\n\n“It was not me,” cried Dough-Boy, “it was Aunt Charity that brought the ginger on board; and bade me never give the harpooneers any spirits, but only this ginger-jub—so she called it.”\n\n“Ginger-jub! you gingerly rascal! take that! and run along with ye to the lockers, and get something better. I hope I do no wrong, Mr. Starbuck. It is the captain’s orders—grog for the harpooneer on a whale.”\n\n“Enough,” replied Starbuck, “only don’t hit him again, but—”\n\n“Oh, I never hurt when I hit, except when I hit a whale or something of that sort; and this fellow’s a weazel. What were you about saying, sir?”\n\n“Only this: go down with him, and get what thou wantest thyself.”\n\nWhen Stubb reappeared, he came with a dark flask in one hand, and a sort of tea-caddy in the other. The first contained strong spirits, and was handed to Queequeg; the second was Aunt Charity’s gift, and that was freely given to the waves.\n\n# CHAPTER 73. Stubb and Flask kill a Right Whale; and Then Have a Talk over Him.\n\nIt must be borne in mind that all this time we have a Sperm Whale’s prodigious head hanging to the Pequod’s side. But we must let it continue hanging there a while till we can get a chance to attend to it. For the present other matters press, and the best we can do now for the head, is to pray heaven the tackles may hold.\n\nNow, during the past night and forenoon, the Pequod had gradually drifted into a sea, which, by its occasional patches of yellow brit, gave unusual tokens of the vicinity of Right Whales, a species of the Leviathan that but few supposed to be at this particular time lurking anywhere near. And though all hands commonly disdained the capture of those inferior creatures; and though the Pequod was not commissioned to cruise for them at all, and though she had passed numbers of them near the Crozetts without lowering a boat; yet now that a Sperm Whale had been brought alongside and beheaded, to the surprise of all, the announcement was made that a Right Whale should be captured that day, if opportunity offered.\n\nNor was this long wanting. Tall spouts were seen to leeward; and two boats, Stubb’s and Flask’s, were detached in pursuit. Pulling further and further away, they at last became almost invisible to the men at the mast-head. But suddenly in the distance, they saw a great heap of tumultuous white water, and soon after news came from aloft that one or both the boats must be fast. An interval passed and the boats were in plain sight, in the act of being dragged right towards the ship by the towing whale. So close did the monster come to the hull, that at first it seemed as if he meant it malice; but suddenly going down in a maelstrom, within three rods of the planks, he wholly disappeared from view, as if diving under the keel. “Cut, cut!” was the cry from the ship to the boats, which, for one instant, seemed on the point of being brought with a deadly dash against the vessel’s side. But having plenty of line yet in the tubs, and the whale not sounding very rapidly, they paid out abundance of rope, and at the same time pulled with all their might so as to get ahead of the ship. For a few minutes the struggle was intensely critical; for while they still slacked out the tightened line in one direction, and still plied their oars in another, the contending strain threatened to take them under. But it was only a few feet advance they sought to gain. And they stuck to it till they did gain it; when instantly, a swift tremor was felt running like lightning along the keel, as the strained line, scraping beneath the ship, suddenly rose to view under her bows, snapping and quivering; and so flinging off its drippings, that the drops fell like bits of broken glass on the water, while the whale beyond also rose to sight, and once more the boats were free to fly. But the fagged whale abated his speed, and blindly altering his course, went round the stern of the ship towing the two boats after him, so that they performed a complete circuit.\n\nMeantime, they hauled more and more upon their lines, till close flanking him on both sides, Stubb answered Flask with lance for lance; and thus round and round the Pequod the battle went, while the multitudes of sharks that had before swum round the Sperm Whale’s body, rushed to the fresh blood that was spilled, thirstily drinking at every new gash, as the eager Israelites did at the new bursting fountains that poured from the smitten rock.\n\nAt last his spout grew thick, and with a frightful roll and vomit, he turned upon his back a corpse.\n\nWhile the two headsmen were engaged in making fast cords to his flukes, and in other ways getting the mass in readiness for towing, some conversation ensued between them.\n\n“I wonder what the old man wants with this lump of foul lard,” said Stubb, not without some disgust at the thought of having to do with so ignoble a leviathan.\n\n“Wants with it?” said Flask, coiling some spare line in the boat’s bow, “did you never hear that the ship which but once has a Sperm Whale’s head hoisted on her starboard side, and at the same time a Right Whale’s on the larboard; did you never hear, Stubb, that that ship can never afterwards capsize?”\n\n“Why not?\n“I don’t know, but I heard that gamboge ghost of a Fedallah saying so, and he seems to know all about ships’ charms. But I sometimes think he’ll charm the ship to no good at last. I don’t half like that chap, Stubb. Did you ever notice how that tusk of his is a sort of carved into a snake’s head, Stubb?”\n\n“Sink him! I never look at him at all; but if ever I get a chance of a dark night, and he standing hard by the bulwarks, and no one by; look down there, Flask”—pointing into the sea with a peculiar motion of both hands—“Aye, will I! Flask, I take that Fedallah to be the devil in disguise. Do you believe that cock and bull story about his having been stowed away on board ship? He’s the devil, I say. The reason why you don’t see his tail, is because he tucks it up out of sight; he carries it coiled away in his pocket, I guess. Blast him! now that I think of it, he’s always wanting oakum to stuff into the toes of his boots.”\n\n“He sleeps in his boots, don’t he? He hasn’t got any hammock; but I’ve seen him lay of nights in a coil of rigging.”\n\n“No doubt, and it’s because of his cursed tail; he coils it down, do ye see, in the eye of the rigging.”\n\n“What’s the old man have so much to do with him for?”\n\n“Striking up a swap or a bargain, I suppose.”\n\n“Bargain?—about what?”\n\n“Why, do ye see, the old man is hard bent after that White Whale, and the devil there is trying to come round him, and get him to swap away his silver watch, or his soul, or something of that sort, and then he’ll surrender Moby Dick.”\n\n“Pooh! Stubb, you are skylarking; how can Fedallah do that?”\n\n“I don’t know, Flask, but the devil is a curious chap, and a wicked one, I tell ye. Why, they say as how he went a sauntering into the old flag-ship once, switching his tail about devilish easy and gentlemanlike, and inquiring if the old governor was at home. Well, he was at home, and asked the devil what he wanted. The devil, switching his hoofs, up and says, ‘I want John.’ ‘What for?’ says the old governor. ‘What business is that of yours,’ says the devil, getting mad,—‘I want to use him.’ ‘Take him,’ says the governor—and by the Lord, Flask, if the devil didn’t give John the Asiatic cholera before he got through with him, I’ll eat this whale in one mouthful. But look sharp—ain’t you all ready there? Well, then, pull ahead, and let’s get the whale alongside.”\n\n“I think I remember some such story as you were telling,” said Flask, when at last the two boats were slowly advancing with their burden towards the ship, “but I can’t remember where.”\n\n“Three Spaniards? Adventures of those three bloody-minded soldadoes? Did ye read it there, Flask? I guess ye did?”\n\n“No: never saw such a book; heard of it, though. But now, tell me, Stubb, do you suppose that that devil you was speaking of just now, was the same you say is now on board the Pequod?”\n\n“Am I the same man that helped kill this whale? Doesn’t the devil live for ever; who ever heard that the devil was dead? Did you ever see any parson a wearing mourning for the devil? And if the devil has a latch-key to get into the admiral’s cabin, don’t you suppose he can crawl into a porthole? Tell me that, Mr. Flask?”\n\n“How old do you suppose Fedallah is, Stubb?”\n\n“Do you see that mainmast there?” pointing to the ship; “well, that’s the figure one; now take all the hoops in the Pequod’s hold, and string along in a row with that mast, for oughts, do you see; well, that wouldn’t begin to be Fedallah’s age. Nor all the coopers in creation couldn’t show hoops enough to make oughts enough.”\n\n“But see here, Stubb, I thought you a little boasted just now, that you meant to give Fedallah a sea-toss, if you got a good chance. Now, if he’s so old as all those hoops of yours come to, and if he is going to live for ever, what good will it do to pitch him overboard—tell me that?\n\n“Give him a good ducking, anyhow.”\n\n“But he’d crawl back.”\n“Duck him again; and keep ducking him.”\n\n“Suppose he should take it into his head to duck you, though—yes, and drown you—what then?”\n\n“I should like to see him try it; I’d give him such a pair of black eyes that he wouldn’t dare to show his face in the admiral’s cabin again for a long while, let alone down in the orlop there, where he lives, and hereabouts on the upper decks where he sneaks so much. Damn the devil, Flask; so you suppose I’m afraid of the devil? Who’s afraid of him, except the old governor who daresn’t catch him and put him in double-darbies, as he deserves, but lets him go about kidnapping people; aye, and signed a bond with him, that all the people the devil kidnapped, he’d roast for him? There’s a governor!”\n\n“Do you suppose Fedallah wants to kidnap Captain Ahab?”\n\n“Do I suppose it? You’ll know it before long, Flask. But I am going now to keep a sharp look-out on him; and if I see anything very suspicious going on, I’ll just take him by the nape of his neck, and say—Look here, Beelzebub, you don’t do it; and if he makes any fuss, by the Lord I’ll make a grab into his pocket for his tail, take it to the capstan, and give him such a wrenching and heaving, that his tail will come short off at the stump—do you see; and then, I rather guess when he finds himself docked in that queer fashion, he’ll sneak off without the poor satisfaction of feeling his tail between his legs.”\n\n“And what will you do with the tail, Stubb?”\n\n“Do with it? Sell it for an ox whip when we get home;—what else?”\n\n“Now, do you mean what you say, and have been saying all along, Stubb?”\n\n“Mean or not mean, here we are at the ship.”\n\nThe boats were here hailed, to tow the whale on the larboard side, where fluke chains and other necessaries were already prepared for securing him.\n\n“Didn’t I tell you so?” said Flask; “yes, you’ll soon see this right whale’s head hoisted up opposite that parmacetti’s.”\n\nIn good time, Flask’s saying proved true. As before, the Pequod steeply leaned over towards the sperm whale’s head, now, by the counterpoise of both heads, she regained her even keel; though sorely strained, you may well believe. So, when on one side you hoist in Locke’s head, you go over that way; but now, on the other side, hoist in Kant’s and you come back again; but in very poor plight. Thus, some minds for ever keep trimming boat. Oh, ye foolish! throw all these thunder-heads overboard, and then you will float light and right.\n\nIn disposing of the body of a right whale, when brought alongside the ship, the same preliminary proceedings commonly take place as in the case of a sperm whale; only, in the latter instance, the head is cut off whole, but in the former the lips and tongue are separately removed and hoisted on deck, with all the well known black bone attached to what is called the crown-piece. But nothing like this, in the present case, had been done. The carcases of both whales had dropped astern; and the head-laden ship not a little resembled a mule carrying a pair of overburdening panniers.\n\nMeantime, Fedallah was calmly eyeing the right whale’s head, and ever and anon glancing from the deep wrinkles there to the lines in his own hand. And Ahab chanced so to stand, that the Parsee occupied his shadow; while, if the Parsee’s shadow was there at all it seemed only to blend with, and lengthen Ahab’s. As the crew toiled on, Laplandish speculations were bandied among them, concerning all these passing things.\n\n# CHAPTER 74. The Sperm Whale’s Head—Contrasted View.\n\nHere, now, are two great whales, laying their heads together; let us join them, and lay together our own.\n\nOf the grand order of folio leviathans, the Sperm Whale and the Right Whale are by far the most noteworthy. They are the only whales regularly hunted by man. To the Nantucketer, they present the two extremes of all the known varieties of the whale. As the external difference between them is mainly observable in their heads; and as a head of each is this moment hanging from the Pequod’s side; and as we may freely go from one to the other, by merely stepping across the deck:—where, I should like to know, will you obtain a better chance to study practical cetology than here?\n\nIn the first place, you are struck by the general contrast between these heads. Both are massive enough in all conscience; but there is a certain mathematical symmetry in the Sperm Whale’s which the Right Whale’s sadly lacks. There is more character in the Sperm Whale’s head. As you behold it, you involuntarily yield the immense superiority to him, in point of pervading dignity. In the present instance, too, this dignity is heightened by the pepper and salt colour of his head at the summit, giving token of advanced age and large experience. In short, he is what the fishermen technically call a “grey-headed whale.”\n\nLet us now note what is least dissimilar in these heads—namely, the two most important organs, the eye and the ear. Far back on the side of the head, and low down, near the angle of either whale’s jaw, if you narrowly search, you will at last see a lashless eye, which you would fancy to be a young colt’s eye; so out of all proportion is it to the magnitude of the head.\n\nNow, from this peculiar sideway position of the whale’s eyes, it is plain that he can never see an object which is exactly ahead, no more than he can one exactly astern. In a word, the position of the whale’s eyes corresponds to that of a man’s ears; and you may fancy, for yourself, how it would fare with you, did you sideways survey objects through your ears. You would find that you could only command some thirty degrees of vision in advance of the straight side-line of sight; and about thirty more behind it. If your bitterest foe were walking straight towards you, with dagger uplifted in broad day, you would not be able to see him, any more than if he were stealing upon you from behind. In a word, you would have two backs, so to speak; but, at the same time, also, two fronts (side fronts): for what is it that makes the front of a man—what, indeed, but his eyes?\n\nMoreover, while in most other animals that I can now think of, the eyes are so planted as imperceptibly to blend their visual power, so as to produce one picture and not two to the brain; the peculiar position of the whale’s eyes, effectually divided as they are by many cubic feet of solid head, which towers between them like a great mountain separating two lakes in valleys; this, of course, must wholly separate the impressions which each independent organ imparts. The whale, therefore, must see one distinct picture on this side, and another distinct picture on that side; while all between must be profound darkness and nothingness to him. Man may, in effect, be said to look out on the world from a sentry-box with two joined sashes for his window. But with the whale, these two sashes are separately inserted, making two distinct windows, but sadly impairing the view. This peculiarity of the whale’s eyes is a thing always to be borne in mind in the fishery; and to be remembered by the reader in some subsequent scenes.\n\nA curious and most puzzling question might be started concerning this visual matter as touching the Leviathan. But I must be content with a hint. So long as a man’s eyes are open in the light, the act of seeing is involuntary; that is, he cannot then help mechanically seeing whatever objects are before him. Nevertheless, any one’s experience will teach him, that though he can take in an undiscriminating sweep of things at one glance, it is quite impossible for him, attentively, and completely, to examine any two things—however large or however small—at one and the same instant of time; never mind if they lie side by side and touch each other. But if you now come to separate these two objects, and surround each by a circle of profound darkness; then, in order to see one of them, in such a manner as to bring your mind to bear on it, the other will be utterly excluded from your contemporary consciousness. How is it, then, with the whale? True, both his eyes, in themselves, must simultaneously act; but is his brain so much more comprehensive, combining, and subtle than man’s, that he can at the same moment of time attentively examine two distinct prospects, one on one side of him, and the other in an exactly opposite direction? If he can, then is it as marvellous a thing in him, as if a man were able simultaneously to go through the demonstrations of two distinct problems in Euclid. Nor, strictly investigated, is there any incongruity in this comparison.\n\nIt may be but an idle whim, but it has always seemed to me, that the extraordinary vacillations of movement displayed by some whales when beset by three or four boats; the timidity and liability to queer frights, so common to such whales; I think that all this indirectly proceeds from the helpless perplexity of volition, in which their divided and diametrically opposite powers of vision must involve them.\n\nBut the ear of the whale is full as curious as the eye. If you are an entire stranger to their race, you might hunt over these two heads for hours, and never discover that organ. The ear has no external leaf whatever; and into the hole itself you can hardly insert a quill, so wondrously minute is it. It is lodged a little behind the eye. With respect to their ears, this important difference is to be observed between the sperm whale and the right. While the ear of the former has an external opening, that of the latter is entirely and evenly covered over with a membrane, so as to be quite imperceptible from without.\n\nIs it not curious, that so vast a being as the whale should see the world through so small an eye, and hear the thunder through an ear which is smaller than a hare’s? But if his eyes were broad as the lens of Herschel’s great telescope; and his ears capacious as the porches of cathedrals; would that make him any longer of sight, or sharper of hearing? Not at all.—Why then do you try to “enlarge” your mind? Subtilize it.\n\nLet us now with whatever levers and steam-engines we have at hand, cant over the sperm whale’s head, that it may lie bottom up; then, ascending by a ladder to the summit, have a peep down the mouth; and were it not that the body is now completely separated from it, with a lantern we might descend into the great Kentucky Mammoth Cave of his stomach. But let us hold on here by this tooth, and look about us where we are. What a really beautiful and chaste-looking mouth! from floor to ceiling, lined, or rather papered with a glistening white membrane, glossy as bridal satins.\n\nBut come out now, and look at this portentous lower jaw, which seems like the long narrow lid of an immense snuff-box, with the hinge at one end, instead of one side. If you pry it up, so as to get it overhead, and expose its rows of teeth, it seems a terrific portcullis; and such, alas! it proves to many a poor wight in the fishery, upon whom these spikes fall with impaling force. But far more terrible is it to behold, when fathoms down in the sea, you see some sulky whale, floating there suspended, with his prodigious jaw, some fifteen feet long, hanging straight down at right-angles with his body, for all the world like a ship’s jib-boom. This whale is not dead; he is only dispirited; out of sorts, perhaps; hypochondriac; and so supine, that the hinges of his jaw have relaxed, leaving him there in that ungainly sort of plight, a reproach to all his tribe, who must, no doubt, imprecate lock-jaws upon him.\n\nIn most cases this lower jaw—being easily unhinged by a practised artist—is disengaged and hoisted on deck for the purpose of extracting the ivory teeth, and furnishing a supply of that hard white whalebone with which the fishermen fashion all sorts of curious articles, including canes, umbrella-stocks, and handles to riding-whips.\n\nWith a long, weary hoist the jaw is dragged on board, as if it were an anchor; and when the proper time comes—some few days after the other work—Queequeg, Daggoo, and Tashtego, being all accomplished dentists, are set to drawing teeth. With a keen cutting-spade, Queequeg lances the gums; then the jaw is lashed down to ringbolts, and a tackle being rigged from aloft, they drag out these teeth, as Michigan oxen drag stumps of old oaks out of wild wood lands. There are generally forty-two teeth in all; in old whales, much worn down, but undecayed; nor filled after our artificial fashion. The jaw is afterwards sawn into slabs, and piled away like joists for building houses.\n\n# CHAPTER 75. The Right Whale’s Head—Contrasted View.\n\nCrossing the deck, let us now have a good long look at the Right Whale’s head.\n\nAs in general shape the noble Sperm Whale’s head may be compared to a Roman war-chariot (especially in front, where it is so broadly rounded); so, at a broad view, the Right Whale’s head bears a rather inelegant resemblance to a gigantic galliot-toed shoe. Two hundred years ago an old Dutch voyager likened its shape to that of a shoemaker’s last. And in this same last or shoe, that old woman of the nursery tale, with the swarming brood, might very comfortably be lodged, she and all her progeny.\n\nBut as you come nearer to this great head it begins to assume different aspects, according to your point of view. If you stand on its summit and look at these two F-shaped spoutholes, you would take the whole head for an enormous bass-viol, and these spiracles, the apertures in its sounding-board. Then, again, if you fix your eye upon this strange, crested, comb-like incrustation on the top of the mass—this green, barnacled thing, which the Greenlanders call the “crown,” and the Southern fishers the “bonnet” of the Right Whale; fixing your eyes solely on this, you would take the head for the trunk of some huge oak, with a bird’s nest in its crotch. At any rate, when you watch those live crabs that nestle here on this bonnet, such an idea will be almost sure to occur to you; unless, indeed, your fancy has been fixed by the technical term “crown” also bestowed upon it; in which case you will take great interest in thinking how this mighty monster is actually a diademed king of the sea, whose green crown has been put together for him in this marvellous manner. But if this whale be a king, he is a very sulky looking fellow to grace a diadem. Look at that hanging lower lip! what a huge sulk and pout is there! a sulk and pout, by carpenter’s measurement, about twenty feet long and five feet deep; a sulk and pout that will yield you some 500 gallons of oil and more.\n\nA great pity, now, that this unfortunate whale should be hare-lipped. The fissure is about a foot across. Probably the mother during an important interval was sailing down the Peruvian coast, when earthquakes caused the beach to gape. Over this lip, as over a slippery threshold, we now slide into the mouth. Upon my word were I at Mackinaw, I should take this to be the inside of an Indian wigwam. Good Lord! is this the road that Jonah went? The roof is about twelve feet high, and runs to a pretty sharp angle, as if there were a regular ridge-pole there; while these ribbed, arched, hairy sides, present us with those wondrous, half vertical, scimetar-shaped slats of whalebone, say three hundred on a side, which depending from the upper part of the head or crown bone, form those Venetian blinds which have elsewhere been cursorily mentioned. The edges of these bones are fringed with hairy fibres, through which the Right Whale strains the water, and in whose intricacies he retains the small fish, when openmouthed he goes through the seas of brit in feeding time. In the central blinds of bone, as they stand in their natural order, there are certain curious marks, curves, hollows, and ridges, whereby some whalemen calculate the creature’s age, as the age of an oak by its circular rings. Though the certainty of this criterion is far from demonstrable, yet it has the savor of analogical probability. At any rate, if we yield to it, we must grant a far greater age to the Right Whale than at first glance will seem reasonable.\n\nIn old times, there seem to have prevailed the most curious fancies concerning these blinds. One voyager in Purchas calls them the wondrous “whiskers” inside of the whale’s mouth;* another, “hogs’ bristles”; a third old gentleman in Hackluyt uses the following elegant language: “There are about two hundred and fifty fins growing on each side of his upper chop, which arch over his tongue on each side of his mouth.”\n\n*This reminds us that the Right Whale really has a sort of whisker, or rather a moustache, consisting of a few scattered white hairs on the upper part of the outer end of the lower jaw. Sometimes these tufts impart a rather brigandish expression to his otherwise solemn countenance.\n\nAs every one knows, these same “hogs’ bristles,” “fins,” “whiskers,” “blinds,” or whatever you please, furnish to the ladies their busks and other stiffening contrivances. But in this particular, the demand has long been on the decline. It was in Queen Anne’s time that the bone was in its glory, the farthingale being then all the fashion. And as those ancient dames moved about gaily, though in the jaws of the whale, as you may say; even so, in a shower, with the like thoughtlessness, do we nowadays fly under the same jaws for protection; the umbrella being a tent spread over the same bone.\n\nBut now forget all about blinds and whiskers for a moment, and, standing in the Right Whale’s mouth, look around you afresh. Seeing all these colonnades of bone so methodically ranged about, would you not think you were inside of the great Haarlem organ, and gazing upon its thousand pipes? For a carpet to the organ we have a rug of the softest Turkey—the tongue, which is glued, as it were, to the floor of the mouth. It is very fat and tender, and apt to tear in pieces in hoisting it on deck. This particular tongue now before us; at a passing glance I should say it was a six-barreler; that is, it will yield you about that amount of oil.\n\nEre this, you must have plainly seen the truth of what I started with—that the Sperm Whale and the Right Whale have almost entirely different heads. To sum up, then: in the Right Whale’s there is no great well of sperm; no ivory teeth at all; no long, slender mandible of a lower jaw, like the Sperm Whale’s. Nor in the Sperm Whale are there any of those blinds of bone; no huge lower lip; and scarcely anything of a tongue. Again, the Right Whale has two external spout-holes, the Sperm Whale only one.\n\nLook your last, now, on these venerable hooded heads, while they yet lie together; for one will soon sink, unrecorded, in the sea; the other will not be very long in following.\n\nCan you catch the expression of the Sperm Whale’s there? It is the same he died with, only some of the longer wrinkles in the forehead seem now faded away. I think his broad brow to be full of a prairie-like placidity, born of a speculative indifference as to death. But mark the other head’s expression. See that amazing lower lip, pressed by accident against the vessel’s side, so as firmly to embrace the jaw. Does not this whole head seem to speak of an enormous practical resolution in facing death? This Right Whale I take to have been a Stoic; the Sperm Whale, a Platonian, who might have taken up Spinoza in his latter years.\n\n# CHAPTER 76. The Battering-Ram.\n\nEre quitting, for the nonce, the Sperm Whale’s head, I would have you, as a sensible physiologist, simply—particularly remark its front aspect, in all its compacted collectedness. I would have you investigate it now with the sole view of forming to yourself some unexaggerated, intelligent estimate of whatever battering-ram power may be lodged there. Here is a vital point; for you must either satisfactorily settle this matter with yourself, or for ever remain an infidel as to one of the most appalling, but not the less true events, perhaps anywhere to be found in all recorded history.\n\nYou observe that in the ordinary swimming position of the Sperm Whale, the front of his head presents an almost wholly vertical plane to the water; you observe that the lower part of that front slopes considerably backwards, so as to furnish more of a retreat for the long socket which receives the boom-like lower jaw; you observe that the mouth is entirely under the head, much in the same way, indeed, as though your own mouth were entirely under your chin. Moreover you observe that the whale has no external nose; and that what nose he has—his spout hole—is on the top of his head; you observe that his eyes and ears are at the sides of his head, nearly one third of his entire length from the front. Wherefore, you must now have perceived that the front of the Sperm Whale’s head is a dead, blind wall, without a single organ or tender prominence of any sort whatsoever. Furthermore, you are now to consider that only in the extreme, lower, backward sloping part of the front of the head, is there the slightest vestige of bone; and not till you get near twenty feet from the forehead do you come to the full cranial development. So that this whole enormous boneless mass is as one wad. Finally, though, as will soon be revealed, its contents partly comprise the most delicate oil; yet, you are now to be apprised of the nature of the substance which so impregnably invests all that apparent effeminacy. In some previous place I have described to you how the blubber wraps the body of the whale, as the rind wraps an orange. Just so with the head; but with this difference: about the head this envelope, though not so thick, is of a boneless toughness, inestimable by any man who has not handled it. The severest pointed harpoon, the sharpest lance darted by the strongest human arm, impotently rebounds from it. It is as though the forehead of the Sperm Whale were paved with horses’ hoofs. I do not think that any sensation lurks in it.\n\nBethink yourself also of another thing. When two large, loaded Indiamen chance to crowd and crush towards each other in the docks, what do the sailors do? They do not suspend between them, at the point of coming contact, any merely hard substance, like iron or wood. No, they hold there a large, round wad of tow and cork, enveloped in the thickest and toughest of ox-hide. That bravely and uninjured takes the jam which would have snapped all their oaken handspikes and iron crow-bars. By itself this sufficiently illustrates the obvious fact I drive at. But supplementary to this, it has hypothetically occurred to me, that as ordinary fish possess what is called a swimming bladder in them, capable, at will, of distension or contraction; and as the Sperm Whale, as far as I know, has no such provision in him; considering, too, the otherwise inexplicable manner in which he now depresses his head altogether beneath the surface, and anon swims with it high elevated out of the water; considering the unobstructed elasticity of its envelope; considering the unique interior of his head; it has hypothetically occurred to me, I say, that those mystical lung-celled honeycombs there may possibly have some hitherto unknown and unsuspected connexion with the outer air, so as to be susceptible to atmospheric distension and contraction. If this be so, fancy the irresistibleness of that might, to which the most impalpable and destructive of all elements contributes.\n\nNow, mark. Unerringly impelling this dead, impregnable, uninjurable wall, and this most buoyant thing within; there swims behind it all a mass of tremendous life, only to be adequately estimated as piled wood is—by the cord; and all obedient to one volition, as the smallest insect. So that when I shall hereafter detail to you all the specialities and concentrations of potency everywhere lurking in this expansive monster; when I shall show you some of his more inconsiderable braining feats; I trust you will have renounced all ignorant incredulity, and be ready to abide by this; that though the Sperm Whale stove a passage through the Isthmus of Darien, and mixed the Atlantic with the Pacific, you would not elevate one hair of your eye-brow. For unless you own the whale, you are but a provincial and sentimentalist in Truth. But clear Truth is a thing for salamander giants only to encounter; how small the chances for the provincials then? What befell the weakling youth lifting the dread goddess’s veil at Lais?\n\n# CHAPTER 77. The Great Heidelburgh Tun.\n\nNow comes the Baling of the Case. But to comprehend it aright, you must know something of the curious internal structure of the thing operated upon.\n\nRegarding the Sperm Whale’s head as a solid oblong, you may, on an inclined plane, sideways divide it into two quoins,* whereof the lower is the bony structure, forming the cranium and jaws, and the upper an unctuous mass wholly free from bones; its broad forward end forming the expanded vertical apparent forehead of the whale. At the middle of the forehead horizontally subdivide this upper quoin, and then you have two almost equal parts, which before were naturally divided by an internal wall of a thick tendinous substance.\n\n*Quoin is not a Euclidean term. It belongs to the pure nautical mathematics. I know not that it has been defined before. A quoin is a solid which differs from a wedge in having its sharp end formed by the steep inclination of one side, instead of the mutual tapering of both sides.\n\nThe lower subdivided part, called the junk, is one immense honeycomb of oil, formed by the crossing and recrossing, into ten thousand infiltrated cells, of tough elastic white fibres throughout its whole extent. The upper part, known as the Case, may be regarded as the great Heidelburgh Tun of the Sperm Whale. And as that famous great tierce is mystically carved in front, so the whale’s vast plaited forehead forms innumerable strange devices for the emblematical adornment of his wondrous tun. Moreover, as that of Heidelburgh was always replenished with the most excellent of the wines of the Rhenish valleys, so the tun of the whale contains by far the most precious of all his oily vintages; namely, the highly-prized spermaceti, in its absolutely pure, limpid, and odoriferous state. Nor is this precious substance found unalloyed in any other part of the creature. Though in life it remains perfectly fluid, yet, upon exposure to the air, after death, it soon begins to concrete; sending forth beautiful crystalline shoots, as when the first thin delicate ice is just forming in water. A large whale’s case generally yields about five hundred gallons of sperm, though from unavoidable circumstances, considerable of it is spilled, leaks, and dribbles away, or is otherwise irrevocably lost in the ticklish business of securing what you can.\n\nI know not with what fine and costly material the Heidelburgh Tun was coated within, but in superlative richness that coating could not possibly have compared with the silken pearl-coloured membrane, like the lining of a fine pelisse, forming the inner surface of the Sperm Whale’s case.\n\nIt will have been seen that the Heidelburgh Tun of the Sperm Whale embraces the entire length of the entire top of the head; and since—as has been elsewhere set forth—the head embraces one third of the whole length of the creature, then setting that length down at eighty feet for a good sized whale, you have more than twenty-six feet for the depth of the tun, when it is lengthwise hoisted up and down against a ship’s side.\n\nAs in decapitating the whale, the operator’s instrument is brought close to the spot where an entrance is subsequently forced into the spermaceti magazine; he has, therefore, to be uncommonly heedful, lest a careless, untimely stroke should invade the sanctuary and wastingly let out its invaluable contents. It is this decapitated end of the head, also, which is at last elevated out of the water, and retained in that position by the enormous cutting tackles, whose hempen combinations, on one side, make quite a wilderness of ropes in that quarter.\n\nThus much being said, attend now, I pray you, to that marvellous and—in this particular instance—almost fatal operation whereby the Sperm Whale’s great Heidelburgh Tun is tapped.\n\n# CHAPTER 78. Cistern and Buckets.\n\nNimble as a cat, Tashtego mounts aloft; and without altering his erect posture, runs straight out upon the overhanging mainyard-arm, to the part where it exactly projects over the hoisted Tun. He has carried with him a light tackle called a whip, consisting of only two parts, travelling through a single-sheaved block. Securing this block, so that it hangs down from the yard-arm, he swings one end of the rope, till it is caught and firmly held by a hand on deck. Then, hand-over-hand, down the other part, the Indian drops through the air, till dexterously he lands on the summit of the head. There—still high elevated above the rest of the company, to whom he vivaciously cries—he seems some Turkish Muezzin calling the good people to prayers from the top of a tower. A short-handled sharp spade being sent up to him, he diligently searches for the proper place to begin breaking into the Tun. In this business he proceeds very heedfully, like a treasure-hunter in some old house, sounding the walls to find where the gold is masoned in. By the time this cautious search is over, a stout iron-bound bucket, precisely like a well-bucket, has been attached to one end of the whip; while the other end, being stretched across the deck, is there held by two or three alert hands. These last now hoist the bucket within grasp of the Indian, to whom another person has reached up a very long pole. Inserting this pole into the bucket, Tashtego downward guides the bucket into the Tun, till it entirely disappears; then giving the word to the seamen at the whip, up comes the bucket again, all bubbling like a dairy-maid’s pail of new milk. Carefully lowered from its height, the full-freighted vessel is caught by an appointed hand, and quickly emptied into a large tub. Then remounting aloft, it again goes through the same round until the deep cistern will yield no more. Towards the end, Tashtego has to ram his long pole harder and harder, and deeper and deeper into the Tun, until some twenty feet of the pole have gone down.\n\nNow, the people of the Pequod had been baling some time in this way; several tubs had been filled with the fragrant sperm; when all at once a queer accident happened. Whether it was that Tashtego, that wild Indian, was so heedless and reckless as to let go for a moment his one-handed hold on the great cabled tackles suspending the head; or whether the place where he stood was so treacherous and oozy; or whether the Evil One himself would have it to fall out so, without stating his particular reasons; how it was exactly, there is no telling now; but, on a sudden, as the eightieth or ninetieth bucket came suckingly up—my God! poor Tashtego—like the twin reciprocating bucket in a veritable well, dropped head-foremost down into this great Tun of Heidelburgh, and with a horrible oily gurgling, went clean out of sight!\n\n“Man overboard!” cried Daggoo, who amid the general consternation first came to his senses. “Swing the bucket this way!” and putting one foot into it, so as the better to secure his slippery hand-hold on the whip itself, the hoisters ran him high up to the top of the head, almost before Tashtego could have reached its interior bottom. Meantime, there was a terrible tumult. Looking over the side, they saw the before lifeless head throbbing and heaving just below the surface of the sea, as if that moment seized with some momentous idea; whereas it was only the poor Indian unconsciously revealing by those struggles the perilous depth to which he had sunk.\n\nAt this instant, while Daggoo, on the summit of the head, was clearing the whip—which had somehow got foul of the great cutting tackles—a sharp cracking noise was heard; and to the unspeakable horror of all, one of the two enormous hooks suspending the head tore out, and with a vast vibration the enormous mass sideways swung, till the drunk ship reeled and shook as if smitten by an iceberg. The one remaining hook, upon which the entire strain now depended, seemed every instant to be on the point of giving way; an event still more likely from the violent motions of the head.\n\n“Come down, come down!” yelled the seamen to Daggoo, but with one hand holding on to the heavy tackles, so that if the head should drop, he would still remain suspended; the negro having cleared the foul line, rammed down the bucket into the now collapsed well, meaning that the buried harpooneer should grasp it, and so be hoisted out.\n\n“In heaven’s name, man,” cried Stubb, “are you ramming home a cartridge there?—Avast! How will that help him; jamming that iron-bound bucket on top of his head? Avast, will ye!”\n\n“Stand clear of the tackle!” cried a voice like the bursting of a rocket.\n\nAlmost in the same instant, with a thunder-boom, the enormous mass dropped into the sea, like Niagara’s Table-Rock into the whirlpool; the suddenly relieved hull rolled away from it, to far down her glittering copper; and all caught their breath, as half swinging—now over the sailors’ heads, and now over the water—Daggoo, through a thick mist of spray, was dimly beheld clinging to the pendulous tackles, while poor, buried-alive Tashtego was sinking utterly down to the bottom of the sea! But hardly had the blinding vapor cleared away, when a naked figure with a boarding-sword in his hand, was for one swift moment seen hovering over the bulwarks. The next, a loud splash announced that my brave Queequeg had dived to the rescue. One packed rush was made to the side, and every eye counted every ripple, as moment followed moment, and no sign of either the sinker or the diver could be seen. Some hands now jumped into a boat alongside, and pushed a little off from the ship.\n\n“Ha! ha!” cried Daggoo, all at once, from his now quiet, swinging perch overhead; and looking further off from the side, we saw an arm thrust upright from the blue waves; a sight strange to see, as an arm thrust forth from the grass over a grave.\n\n“Both! both!—it is both!”—cried Daggoo again with a joyful shout; and soon after, Queequeg was seen boldly striking out with one hand, and with the other clutching the long hair of the Indian. Drawn into the waiting boat, they were quickly brought to the deck; but Tashtego was long in coming to, and Queequeg did not look very brisk.\n\nNow, how had this noble rescue been accomplished? Why, diving after the slowly descending head, Queequeg with his keen sword had made side lunges near its bottom, so as to scuttle a large hole there; then dropping his sword, had thrust his long arm far inwards and upwards, and so hauled out poor Tash by the head. He averred, that upon first thrusting in for him, a leg was presented; but well knowing that that was not as it ought to be, and might occasion great trouble;—he had thrust back the leg, and by a dexterous heave and toss, had wrought a somerset upon the Indian; so that with the next trial, he came forth in the good old way—head foremost. As for the great head itself, that was doing as well as could be expected.\n\nAnd thus, through the courage and great skill in obstetrics of Queequeg, the deliverance, or rather, delivery of Tashtego, was successfully accomplished, in the teeth, too, of the most untoward and apparently hopeless impediments; which is a lesson by no means to be forgotten. Midwifery should be taught in the same course with fencing and boxing, riding and rowing.\n\nI know that this queer adventure of the Gay-Header’s will be sure to seem incredible to some landsmen, though they themselves may have either seen or heard of some one’s falling into a cistern ashore; an accident which not seldom happens, and with much less reason too than the Indian’s, considering the exceeding slipperiness of the curb of the Sperm Whale’s well.\n\nBut, peradventure, it may be sagaciously urged, how is this? We thought the tissued, infiltrated head of the Sperm Whale, was the lightest and most corky part about him; and yet thou makest it sink in an element of a far greater specific gravity than itself. We have thee there. Not at all, but I have ye; for at the time poor Tash fell in, the case had been nearly emptied of its lighter contents, leaving little but the dense tendinous wall of the well—a double welded, hammered substance, as I have before said, much heavier than the sea water, and a lump of which sinks in it like lead almost. But the tendency to rapid sinking in this substance was in the present instance materially counteracted by the other parts of the head remaining undetached from it, so that it sank very slowly and deliberately indeed, affording Queequeg a fair chance for performing his agile obstetrics on the run, as you may say. Yes, it was a running delivery, so it was.\n\nNow, had Tashtego perished in that head, it had been a very precious perishing; smothered in the very whitest and daintiest of fragrant spermaceti; coffined, hearsed, and tombed in the secret inner chamber and sanctum sanctorum of the whale. Only one sweeter end can readily be recalled—the delicious death of an Ohio honey-hunter, who seeking honey in the crotch of a hollow tree, found such exceeding store of it, that leaning too far over, it sucked him in, so that he died embalmed. How many, think ye, have likewise fallen into Plato’s honey head, and sweetly perished there?\n\n# CHAPTER 79. The Prairie.\n\nTo scan the lines of his face, or feel the bumps on the head of this Leviathan; this is a thing which no Physiognomist or Phrenologist has as yet undertaken. Such an enterprise would seem almost as hopeful as for Lavater to have scrutinized the wrinkles on the Rock of Gibraltar, or for Gall to have mounted a ladder and manipulated the Dome of the Pantheon. Still, in that famous work of his, Lavater not only treats of the various faces of men, but also attentively studies the faces of horses, birds, serpents, and fish; and dwells in detail upon the modifications of expression discernible therein. Nor have Gall and his disciple Spurzheim failed to throw out some hints touching the phrenological characteristics of other beings than man. Therefore, though I am but ill qualified for a pioneer, in the application of these two semi-sciences to the whale, I will do my endeavor. I try all things; I achieve what I can.\n\nPhysiognomically regarded, the Sperm Whale is an anomalous creature. He has no proper nose. And since the nose is the central and most conspicuous of the features; and since it perhaps most modifies and finally controls their combined expression; hence it would seem that its entire absence, as an external appendage, must very largely affect the countenance of the whale. For as in landscape gardening, a spire, cupola, monument, or tower of some sort, is deemed almost indispensable to the completion of the scene; so no face can be physiognomically in keeping without the elevated open-work belfry of the nose. Dash the nose from Phidias’s marble Jove, and what a sorry remainder! Nevertheless, Leviathan is of so mighty a magnitude, all his proportions are so stately, that the same deficiency which in the sculptured Jove were hideous, in him is no blemish at all. Nay, it is an added grandeur. A nose to the whale would have been impertinent. As on your physiognomical voyage you sail round his vast head in your jolly-boat, your noble conceptions of him are never insulted by the reflection that he has a nose to be pulled. A pestilent conceit, which so often will insist upon obtruding even when beholding the mightiest royal beadle on his throne.\n\nIn some particulars, perhaps the most imposing physiognomical view to be had of the Sperm Whale, is that of the full front of his head. This aspect is sublime.\n\nIn thought, a fine human brow is like the East when troubled with the morning. In the repose of the pasture, the curled brow of the bull has a touch of the grand in it. Pushing heavy cannon up mountain defiles, the elephant’s brow is majestic. Human or animal, the mystical brow is as that great golden seal affixed by the German emperors to their decrees. It signifies—“God: done this day by my hand.” But in most creatures, nay in man himself, very often the brow is but a mere strip of alpine land lying along the snow line. Few are the foreheads which like Shakespeare’s or Melancthon’s rise so high, and descend so low, that the eyes themselves seem clear, eternal, tideless mountain lakes; and all above them in the forehead’s wrinkles, you seem to track the antlered thoughts descending there to drink, as the Highland hunters track the snow prints of the deer. But in the great Sperm Whale, this high and mighty god-like dignity inherent in the brow is so immensely amplified, that gazing on it, in that full front view, you feel the Deity and the dread powers more forcibly than in beholding any other object in living nature. For you see no one point precisely; not one distinct feature is revealed; no nose, eyes, ears, or mouth; no face; he has none, proper; nothing but that one broad firmament of a forehead, pleated with riddles; dumbly lowering with the doom of boats, and ships, and men. Nor, in profile, does this wondrous brow diminish; though that way viewed its grandeur does not domineer upon you so. In profile, you plainly perceive that horizontal, semi-crescentic depression in the forehead’s middle, which, in man, is Lavater’s mark of genius.\n\nBut how? Genius in the Sperm Whale? Has the Sperm Whale ever written a book, spoken a speech? No, his great genius is declared in his doing nothing particular to prove it. It is moreover declared in his pyramidical silence. And this reminds me that had the great Sperm Whale been known to the young Orient World, he would have been deified by their child-magian thoughts. They deified the crocodile of the Nile, because the crocodile is tongueless; and the Sperm Whale has no tongue, or at least it is so exceedingly small, as to be incapable of protrusion. If hereafter any highly cultured, poetical nation shall lure back to their birth-right, the merry May-day gods of old; and livingly enthrone them again in the now egotistical sky; in the now unhaunted hill; then be sure, exalted to Jove’s high seat, the great Sperm Whale shall lord it.\n\nChampollion deciphered the wrinkled granite hieroglyphics. But there is no Champollion to decipher the Egypt of every man’s and every being’s face. Physiognomy, like every other human science, is but a passing fable. If then, Sir William Jones, who read in thirty languages, could not read the simplest peasant’s face in its profounder and more subtle meanings, how may unlettered Ishmael hope to read the awful Chaldee of the Sperm Whale’s brow? I but put that brow before you. Read it if you can.\n\n# CHAPTER 80. The Nut.\n\nIf the Sperm Whale be physiognomically a Sphinx, to the phrenologist his brain seems that geometrical circle which it is impossible to square.\n\nIn the full-grown creature the skull will measure at least twenty feet in length. Unhinge the lower jaw, and the side view of this skull is as the side of a moderately inclined plane resting throughout on a level base. But in life—as we have elsewhere seen—this inclined plane is angularly filled up, and almost squared by the enormous superincumbent mass of the junk and sperm. At the high end the skull forms a crater to bed that part of the mass; while under the long floor of this crater—in another cavity seldom exceeding ten inches in length and as many in depth—reposes the mere handful of this monster’s brain. The brain is at least twenty feet from his apparent forehead in life; it is hidden away behind its vast outworks, like the innermost citadel within the amplified fortifications of Quebec. So like a choice casket is it secreted in him, that I have known some whalemen who peremptorily deny that the Sperm Whale has any other brain than that palpable semblance of one formed by the cubic-yards of his sperm magazine. Lying in strange folds, courses, and convolutions, to their apprehensions, it seems more in keeping with the idea of his general might to regard that mystic part of him as the seat of his intelligence.\n\nIt is plain, then, that phrenologically the head of this Leviathan, in the creature’s living intact state, is an entire delusion. As for his true brain, you can then see no indications of it, nor feel any. The whale, like all things that are mighty, wears a false brow to the common world.\n\nIf you unload his skull of its spermy heaps and then take a rear view of its rear end, which is the high end, you will be struck by its resemblance to the human skull, beheld in the same situation, and from the same point of view. Indeed, place this reversed skull (scaled down to the human magnitude) among a plate of men’s skulls, and you would involuntarily confound it with them; and remarking the depressions on one part of its summit, in phrenological phrase you would say—This man had no self-esteem, and no veneration. And by those negations, considered along with the affirmative fact of his prodigious bulk and power, you can best form to yourself the truest, though not the most exhilarating conception of what the most exalted potency is.\n\nBut if from the comparative dimensions of the whale’s proper brain, you deem it incapable of being adequately charted, then I have another idea for you. If you attentively regard almost any quadruped’s spine, you will be struck with the resemblance of its vertebræ to a strung necklace of dwarfed skulls, all bearing rudimental resemblance to the skull proper. It is a German conceit, that the vertebræ are absolutely undeveloped skulls. But the curious external resemblance, I take it the Germans were not the first men to perceive. A foreign friend once pointed it out to me, in the skeleton of a foe he had slain, and with the vertebræ of which he was inlaying, in a sort of basso-relievo, the beaked prow of his canoe. Now, I consider that the phrenologists have omitted an important thing in not pushing their investigations from the cerebellum through the spinal canal. For I believe that much of a man’s character will be found betokened in his backbone. I would rather feel your spine than your skull, whoever you are. A thin joist of a spine never yet upheld a full and noble soul. I rejoice in my spine, as in the firm audacious staff of that flag which I fling half out to the world.\n\nApply this spinal branch of phrenology to the Sperm Whale. His cranial cavity is continuous with the first neck-vertebra; and in that vertebra the bottom of the spinal canal will measure ten inches across, being eight in height, and of a triangular figure with the base downwards. As it passes through the remaining vertebræ the canal tapers in size, but for a considerable distance remains of large capacity. Now, of course, this canal is filled with much the same strangely fibrous substance—the spinal cord—as the brain; and directly communicates with the brain. And what is still more, for many feet after emerging from the brain’s cavity, the spinal cord remains of an undecreasing girth, almost equal to that of the brain. Under all these circumstances, would it be unreasonable to survey and map out the whale’s spine phrenologically? For, viewed in this light, the wonderful comparative smallness of his brain proper is more than compensated by the wonderful comparative magnitude of his spinal cord.\n\nBut leaving this hint to operate as it may with the phrenologists, I would merely assume the spinal theory for a moment, in reference to the Sperm Whale’s hump. This august hump, if I mistake not, rises over one of the larger vertebræ, and is, therefore, in some sort, the outer convex mould of it. From its relative situation then, I should call this high hump the organ of firmness or indomitableness in the Sperm Whale. And that the great monster is indomitable, you will yet have reason to know.\n\n# CHAPTER 81. The Pequod Meets The Virgin.\n\nThe predestinated day arrived, and we duly met the ship Jungfrau, Derick De Deer, master, of Bremen.\n\nAt one time the greatest whaling people in the world, the Dutch and Germans are now among the least; but here and there at very wide intervals of latitude and longitude, you still occasionally meet with their flag in the Pacific.\n\nFor some reason, the Jungfrau seemed quite eager to pay her respects. While yet some distance from the Pequod, she rounded to, and dropping a boat, her captain was impelled towards us, impatiently standing in the bows instead of the stern.\n\n“What has he in his hand there?” cried Starbuck, pointing to something wavingly held by the German. “Impossible!—a lamp-feeder!”\n\n“Not that,” said Stubb, “no, no, it’s a coffee-pot, Mr. Starbuck; he’s coming off to make us our coffee, is the Yarman; don’t you see that big tin can there alongside of him?—that’s his boiling water. Oh! he’s all right, is the Yarman.”\n\n“Go along with you,” cried Flask, “it’s a lamp-feeder and an oil-can. He’s out of oil, and has come a-begging.”\n\nHowever curious it may seem for an oil-ship to be borrowing oil on the whale-ground, and however much it may invertedly contradict the old proverb about carrying coals to Newcastle, yet sometimes such a thing really happens; and in the present case Captain Derick De Deer did indubitably conduct a lamp-feeder as Flask did declare.\n\nAs he mounted the deck, Ahab abruptly accosted him, without at all heeding what he had in his hand; but in his broken lingo, the German soon evinced his complete ignorance of the White Whale; immediately turning the conversation to his lamp-feeder and oil can, with some remarks touching his having to turn into his hammock at night in profound darkness—his last drop of Bremen oil being gone, and not a single flying-fish yet captured to supply the deficiency; concluding by hinting that his ship was indeed what in the Fishery is technically called a clean one (that is, an empty one), well deserving the name of Jungfrau or the Virgin.\n\nHis necessities supplied, Derick departed; but he had not gained his ship’s side, when whales were almost simultaneously raised from the mast-heads of both vessels; and so eager for the chase was Derick, that without pausing to put his oil-can and lamp-feeder aboard, he slewed round his boat and made after the leviathan lamp-feeders.\n\nNow, the game having risen to leeward, he and the other three German boats that soon followed him, had considerably the start of the Pequod’s keels. There were eight whales, an average pod. Aware of their danger, they were going all abreast with great speed straight before the wind, rubbing their flanks as closely as so many spans of horses in harness. They left a great, wide wake, as though continually unrolling a great wide parchment upon the sea.\n\nFull in this rapid wake, and many fathoms in the rear, swam a huge, humped old bull, which by his comparatively slow progress, as well as by the unusual yellowish incrustations overgrowing him, seemed afflicted with the jaundice, or some other infirmity. Whether this whale belonged to the pod in advance, seemed questionable; for it is not customary for such venerable leviathans to be at all social. Nevertheless, he stuck to their wake, though indeed their back water must have retarded him, because the white-bone or swell at his broad muzzle was a dashed one, like the swell formed when two hostile currents meet. His spout was short, slow, and laborious; coming forth with a choking sort of gush, and spending itself in torn shreds, followed by strange subterranean commotions in him, which seemed to have egress at his other buried extremity, causing the waters behind him to upbubble.\n\n“Who’s got some paregoric?” said Stubb, “he has the stomach-ache, I’m afraid. Lord, think of having half an acre of stomach-ache! Adverse winds are holding mad Christmas in him, boys. It’s the first foul wind I ever knew to blow from astern; but look, did ever whale yaw so before? it must be, he’s lost his tiller.”\n\nAs an overladen Indiaman bearing down the Hindostan coast with a deck load of frightened horses, careens, buries, rolls, and wallows on her way; so did this old whale heave his aged bulk, and now and then partly turning over on his cumbrous rib-ends, expose the cause of his devious wake in the unnatural stump of his starboard fin. Whether he had lost that fin in battle, or had been born without it, it were hard to say.\n\n“Only wait a bit, old chap, and I’ll give ye a sling for that wounded arm,” cried cruel Flask, pointing to the whale-line near him.\n\n“Mind he don’t sling thee with it,” cried Starbuck. “Give way, or the German will have him.”\n\nWith one intent all the combined rival boats were pointed for this one fish, because not only was he the largest, and therefore the most valuable whale, but he was nearest to them, and the other whales were going with such great velocity, moreover, as almost to defy pursuit for the time. At this juncture the Pequod’s keels had shot by the three German boats last lowered; but from the great start he had had, Derick’s boat still led the chase, though every moment neared by his foreign rivals. The only thing they feared, was, that from being already so nigh to his mark, he would be enabled to dart his iron before they could completely overtake and pass him. As for Derick, he seemed quite confident that this would be the case, and occasionally with a deriding gesture shook his lamp-feeder at the other boats.\n\n“The ungracious and ungrateful dog!” cried Starbuck; “he mocks and dares me with the very poor-box I filled for him not five minutes ago!”—then in his old intense whisper—“Give way, greyhounds! Dog to it!”\n\n“I tell ye what it is, men”—cried Stubb to his crew—“it’s against my religion to get mad; but I’d like to eat that villainous Yarman—Pull—won’t ye? Are ye going to let that rascal beat ye? Do ye love brandy? A hogshead of brandy, then, to the best man. Come, why don’t some of ye burst a blood-vessel? Who’s that been dropping an anchor overboard—we don’t budge an inch—we’re becalmed. Halloo, here’s grass growing in the boat’s bottom—and by the Lord, the mast there’s budding. This won’t do, boys. Look at that Yarman! The short and long of it is, men, will ye spit fire or not?”\n\n“Oh! see the suds he makes!” cried Flask, dancing up and down—“What a hump—Oh, do pile on the beef—lays like a log! Oh! my lads, do spring—slap-jacks and quahogs for supper, you know, my lads—baked clams and muffins—oh, do, do, spring,—he’s a hundred barreller—don’t lose him now—don’t oh, don’t!—see that Yarman—Oh, won’t ye pull for your duff, my lads—such a sog! such a sogger! Don’t ye love sperm? There goes three thousand dollars, men!—a bank!—a whole bank! The bank of England!—Oh, do, do, do!—What’s that Yarman about now?”\n\nAt this moment Derick was in the act of pitching his lamp-feeder at the advancing boats, and also his oil-can; perhaps with the double view of retarding his rivals’ way, and at the same time economically accelerating his own by the momentary impetus of the backward toss.\n\n“The unmannerly Dutch dogger!” cried Stubb. “Pull now, men, like fifty thousand line-of-battle-ship loads of red-haired devils. What d’ye say, Tashtego; are you the man to snap your spine in two-and-twenty pieces for the honor of old Gayhead? What d’ye say?”\n\n“I say, pull like god-dam,”—cried the Indian.\n\nFiercely, but evenly incited by the taunts of the German, the Pequod’s three boats now began ranging almost abreast; and, so disposed, momentarily neared him. In that fine, loose, chivalrous attitude of the headsman when drawing near to his prey, the three mates stood up proudly, occasionally backing the after oarsman with an exhilarating cry of, “There she slides, now! Hurrah for the white-ash breeze! Down with the Yarman! Sail over him!”\n\nBut so decided an original start had Derick had, that spite of all their gallantry, he would have proved the victor in this race, had not a righteous judgment descended upon him in a crab which caught the blade of his midship oarsman. While this clumsy lubber was striving to free his white-ash, and while, in consequence, Derick’s boat was nigh to capsizing, and he thundering away at his men in a mighty rage;—that was a good time for Starbuck, Stubb, and Flask. With a shout, they took a mortal start forwards, and slantingly ranged up on the German’s quarter. An instant more, and all four boats were diagonically in the whale’s immediate wake, while stretching from them, on both sides, was the foaming swell that he made.\n\nIt was a terrific, most pitiable, and maddening sight. The whale was now going head out, and sending his spout before him in a continual tormented jet; while his one poor fin beat his side in an agony of fright. Now to this hand, now to that, he yawed in his faltering flight, and still at every billow that he broke, he spasmodically sank in the sea, or sideways rolled towards the sky his one beating fin. So have I seen a bird with clipped wing making affrighted broken circles in the air, vainly striving to escape the piratical hawks. But the bird has a voice, and with plaintive cries will make known her fear; but the fear of this vast dumb brute of the sea, was chained up and enchanted in him; he had no voice, save that choking respiration through his spiracle, and this made the sight of him unspeakably pitiable; while still, in his amazing bulk, portcullis jaw, and omnipotent tail, there was enough to appal the stoutest man who so pitied.\n\nSeeing now that but a very few moments more would give the Pequod’s boats the advantage, and rather than be thus foiled of his game, Derick chose to hazard what to him must have seemed a most unusually long dart, ere the last chance would for ever escape.\n\nBut no sooner did his harpooneer stand up for the stroke, than all three tigers—Queequeg, Tashtego, Daggoo—instinctively sprang to their feet, and standing in a diagonal row, simultaneously pointed their barbs; and darted over the head of the German harpooneer, their three Nantucket irons entered the whale. Blinding vapors of foam and white-fire! The three boats, in the first fury of the whale’s headlong rush, bumped the German’s aside with such force, that both Derick and his baffled harpooneer were spilled out, and sailed over by the three flying keels.\n\n“Don’t be afraid, my butter-boxes,” cried Stubb, casting a passing glance upon them as he shot by; “ye’ll be picked up presently—all right—I saw some sharks astern—St. Bernard’s dogs, you know—relieve distressed travellers. Hurrah! this is the way to sail now. Every keel a sunbeam! Hurrah!—Here we go like three tin kettles at the tail of a mad cougar! This puts me in mind of fastening to an elephant in a tilbury on a plain—makes the wheel-spokes fly, boys, when you fasten to him that way; and there’s danger of being pitched out too, when you strike a hill. Hurrah! this is the way a fellow feels when he’s going to Davy Jones—all a rush down an endless inclined plane! Hurrah! this whale carries the everlasting mail!”\n\nBut the monster’s run was a brief one. Giving a sudden gasp, he tumultuously sounded. With a grating rush, the three lines flew round the loggerheads with such a force as to gouge deep grooves in them; while so fearful were the harpooneers that this rapid sounding would soon exhaust the lines, that using all their dexterous might, they caught repeated smoking turns with the rope to hold on; till at last—owing to the perpendicular strain from the lead-lined chocks of the boats, whence the three ropes went straight down into the blue—the gunwales of the bows were almost even with the water, while the three sterns tilted high in the air. And the whale soon ceasing to sound, for some time they remained in that attitude, fearful of expending more line, though the position was a little ticklish. But though boats have been taken down and lost in this way, yet it is this “holding on,” as it is called; this hooking up by the sharp barbs of his live flesh from the back; this it is that often torments the Leviathan into soon rising again to meet the sharp lance of his foes. Yet not to speak of the peril of the thing, it is to be doubted whether this course is always the best; for it is but reasonable to presume, that the longer the stricken whale stays under water, the more he is exhausted. Because, owing to the enormous surface of him—in a full grown sperm whale something less than 2000 square feet—the pressure of the water is immense. We all know what an astonishing atmospheric weight we ourselves stand up under; even here, above-ground, in the air; how vast, then, the burden of a whale, bearing on his back a column of two hundred fathoms of ocean! It must at least equal the weight of fifty atmospheres. One whaleman has estimated it at the weight of twenty line-of-battle ships, with all their guns, and stores, and men on board.\n\nAs the three boats lay there on that gently rolling sea, gazing down into its eternal blue noon; and as not a single groan or cry of any sort, nay, not so much as a ripple or a bubble came up from its depths; what landsman would have thought, that beneath all that silence and placidity, the utmost monster of the seas was writhing and wrenching in agony! Not eight inches of perpendicular rope were visible at the bows. Seems it credible that by three such thin threads the great Leviathan was suspended like the big weight to an eight day clock. Suspended? and to what? To three bits of board. Is this the creature of whom it was once so triumphantly said—“Canst thou fill his skin with barbed irons? or his head with fish-spears? The sword of him that layeth at him cannot hold, the spear, the dart, nor the habergeon: he esteemeth iron as straw; the arrow cannot make him flee; darts are counted as stubble; he laugheth at the shaking of a spear!” This the creature? this he? Oh! that unfulfilments should follow the prophets. For with the strength of a thousand thighs in his tail, Leviathan had run his head under the mountains of the sea, to hide him from the Pequod’s fish-spears!\n\nIn that sloping afternoon sunlight, the shadows that the three boats sent down beneath the surface, must have been long enough and broad enough to shade half Xerxes’ army. Who can tell how appalling to the wounded whale must have been such huge phantoms flitting over his head!\n\n“Stand by, men; he stirs,” cried Starbuck, as the three lines suddenly vibrated in the water, distinctly conducting upwards to them, as by magnetic wires, the life and death throbs of the whale, so that every oarsman felt them in his seat. The next moment, relieved in great part from the downward strain at the bows, the boats gave a sudden bounce upwards, as a small icefield will, when a dense herd of white bears are scared from it into the sea.\n\n“Haul in! Haul in!” cried Starbuck again; “he’s rising.”\n\nThe lines, of which, hardly an instant before, not one hand’s breadth could have been gained, were now in long quick coils flung back all dripping into the boats, and soon the whale broke water within two ship’s lengths of the hunters.\n\nHis motions plainly denoted his extreme exhaustion. In most land animals there are certain valves or flood-gates in many of their veins, whereby when wounded, the blood is in some degree at least instantly shut off in certain directions. Not so with the whale; one of whose peculiarities it is to have an entire non-valvular structure of the blood-vessels, so that when pierced even by so small a point as a harpoon, a deadly drain is at once begun upon his whole arterial system; and when this is heightened by the extraordinary pressure of water at a great distance below the surface, his life may be said to pour from him in incessant streams. Yet so vast is the quantity of blood in him, and so distant and numerous its interior fountains, that he will keep thus bleeding and bleeding for a considerable period; even as in a drought a river will flow, whose source is in the well-springs of far-off and undiscernible hills. Even now, when the boats pulled upon this whale, and perilously drew over his swaying flukes, and the lances were darted into him, they were followed by steady jets from the new made wound, which kept continually playing, while the natural spout-hole in his head was only at intervals, however rapid, sending its affrighted moisture into the air. From this last vent no blood yet came, because no vital part of him had thus far been struck. His life, as they significantly call it, was untouched.\n\nAs the boats now more closely surrounded him, the whole upper part of his form, with much of it that is ordinarily submerged, was plainly revealed. His eyes, or rather the places where his eyes had been, were beheld. As strange misgrown masses gather in the knot-holes of the noblest oaks when prostrate, so from the points which the whale’s eyes had once occupied, now protruded blind bulbs, horribly pitiable to see. But pity there was none. For all his old age, and his one arm, and his blind eyes, he must die the death and be murdered, in order to light the gay bridals and other merry-makings of men, and also to illuminate the solemn churches that preach unconditional inoffensiveness by all to all. Still rolling in his blood, at last he partially disclosed a strangely discoloured bunch or protuberance, the size of a bushel, low down on the flank.\n\n“A nice spot,” cried Flask; “just let me prick him there once.”\n\n“Avast!” cried Starbuck, “there’s no need of that!”\n\nBut humane Starbuck was too late. At the instant of the dart an ulcerous jet shot from this cruel wound, and goaded by it into more than sufferable anguish, the whale now spouting thick blood, with swift fury blindly darted at the craft, bespattering them and their glorying crews all over with showers of gore, capsizing Flask’s boat and marring the bows. It was his death stroke. For, by this time, so spent was he by loss of blood, that he helplessly rolled away from the wreck he had made; lay panting on his side, impotently flapped with his stumped fin, then over and over slowly revolved like a waning world; turned up the white secrets of his belly; lay like a log, and died. It was most piteous, that last expiring spout. As when by unseen hands the water is gradually drawn off from some mighty fountain, and with half-stifled melancholy gurglings the spray-column lowers and lowers to the ground—so the last long dying spout of the whale.\n\nSoon, while the crews were awaiting the arrival of the ship, the body showed symptoms of sinking with all its treasures unrifled. Immediately, by Starbuck’s orders, lines were secured to it at different points, so that ere long every boat was a buoy; the sunken whale being suspended a few inches beneath them by the cords. By very heedful management, when the ship drew nigh, the whale was transferred to her side, and was strongly secured there by the stiffest fluke-chains, for it was plain that unless artificially upheld, the body would at once sink to the bottom.\n\nIt so chanced that almost upon first cutting into him with the spade, the entire length of a corroded harpoon was found imbedded in his flesh, on the lower part of the bunch before described. But as the stumps of harpoons are frequently found in the dead bodies of captured whales, with the flesh perfectly healed around them, and no prominence of any kind to denote their place; therefore, there must needs have been some other unknown reason in the present case fully to account for the ulceration alluded to. But still more curious was the fact of a lance-head of stone being found in him, not far from the buried iron, the flesh perfectly firm about it. Who had darted that stone lance? And when? It might have been darted by some Nor’ West Indian long before America was discovered.\n\nWhat other marvels might have been rummaged out of this monstrous cabinet there is no telling. But a sudden stop was put to further discoveries, by the ship’s being unprecedentedly dragged over sideways to the sea, owing to the body’s immensely increasing tendency to sink. However, Starbuck, who had the ordering of affairs, hung on to it to the last; hung on to it so resolutely, indeed, that when at length the ship would have been capsized, if still persisting in locking arms with the body; then, when the command was given to break clear from it, such was the immovable strain upon the timber-heads to which the fluke-chains and cables were fastened, that it was impossible to cast them off. Meantime everything in the Pequod was aslant. To cross to the other side of the deck was like walking up the steep gabled roof of a house. The ship groaned and gasped. Many of the ivory inlayings of her bulwarks and cabins were started from their places, by the unnatural dislocation. In vain handspikes and crows were brought to bear upon the immovable fluke-chains, to pry them adrift from the timberheads; and so low had the whale now settled that the submerged ends could not be at all approached, while every moment whole tons of ponderosity seemed added to the sinking bulk, and the ship seemed on the point of going over.\n\n“Hold on, hold on, won’t ye?” cried Stubb to the body, “don’t be in such a devil of a hurry to sink! By thunder, men, we must do something or go for it. No use prying there; avast, I say with your handspikes, and run one of ye for a prayer book and a pen-knife, and cut the big chains.”\n\n“Knife? Aye, aye,” cried Queequeg, and seizing the carpenter’s heavy hatchet, he leaned out of a porthole, and steel to iron, began slashing at the largest fluke-chains. But a few strokes, full of sparks, were given, when the exceeding strain effected the rest. With a terrific snap, every fastening went adrift; the ship righted, the carcase sank.\n\nNow, this occasional inevitable sinking of the recently killed Sperm Whale is a very curious thing; nor has any fisherman yet adequately accounted for it. Usually the dead Sperm Whale floats with great buoyancy, with its side or belly considerably elevated above the surface. If the only whales that thus sank were old, meagre, and broken-hearted creatures, their pads of lard diminished and all their bones heavy and rheumatic; then you might with some reason assert that this sinking is caused by an uncommon specific gravity in the fish so sinking, consequent upon this absence of buoyant matter in him. But it is not so. For young whales, in the highest health, and swelling with noble aspirations, prematurely cut off in the warm flush and May of life, with all their panting lard about them; even these brawny, buoyant heroes do sometimes sink.\n\nBe it said, however, that the Sperm Whale is far less liable to this accident than any other species. Where one of that sort go down, twenty Right Whales do. This difference in the species is no doubt imputable in no small degree to the greater quantity of bone in the Right Whale; his Venetian blinds alone sometimes weighing more than a ton; from this incumbrance the Sperm Whale is wholly free. But there are instances where, after the lapse of many hours or several days, the sunken whale again rises, more buoyant than in life. But the reason of this is obvious. Gases are generated in him; he swells to a prodigious magnitude; becomes a sort of animal balloon. A line-of-battle ship could hardly keep him under then. In the Shore Whaling, on soundings, among the Bays of New Zealand, when a Right Whale gives token of sinking, they fasten buoys to him, with plenty of rope; so that when the body has gone down, they know where to look for it when it shall have ascended again.\n\nIt was not long after the sinking of the body that a cry was heard from the Pequod’s mast-heads, announcing that the Jungfrau was again lowering her boats; though the only spout in sight was that of a Fin-Back, belonging to the species of uncapturable whales, because of its incredible power of swimming. Nevertheless, the Fin-Back’s spout is so similar to the Sperm Whale’s, that by unskilful fishermen it is often mistaken for it. And consequently Derick and all his host were now in valiant chase of this unnearable brute. The Virgin crowding all sail, made after her four young keels, and thus they all disappeared far to leeward, still in bold, hopeful chase.\n\nOh! many are the Fin-Backs, and many are the Dericks, my friend.\n\n# CHAPTER 82. The Honor and Glory of Whaling.\n\nThere are some enterprises in which a careful disorderliness is the true method.\n\nThe more I dive into this matter of whaling, and push my researches up to the very spring-head of it so much the more am I impressed with its great honorableness and antiquity; and especially when I find so many great demi-gods and heroes, prophets of all sorts, who one way or other have shed distinction upon it, I am transported with the reflection that I myself belong, though but subordinately, to so emblazoned a fraternity.\n\nThe gallant Perseus, a son of Jupiter, was the first whaleman; and to the eternal honor of our calling be it said, that the first whale attacked by our brotherhood was not killed with any sordid intent. Those were the knightly days of our profession, when we only bore arms to succor the distressed, and not to fill men’s lamp-feeders. Every one knows the fine story of Perseus and Andromeda; how the lovely Andromeda, the daughter of a king, was tied to a rock on the sea-coast, and as Leviathan was in the very act of carrying her off, Perseus, the prince of whalemen, intrepidly advancing, harpooned the monster, and delivered and married the maid. It was an admirable artistic exploit, rarely achieved by the best harpooneers of the present day; inasmuch as this Leviathan was slain at the very first dart. And let no man doubt this Arkite story; for in the ancient Joppa, now Jaffa, on the Syrian coast, in one of the Pagan temples, there stood for many ages the vast skeleton of a whale, which the city’s legends and all the inhabitants asserted to be the identical bones of the monster that Perseus slew. When the Romans took Joppa, the same skeleton was carried to Italy in triumph. What seems most singular and suggestively important in this story, is this: it was from Joppa that Jonah set sail.\n\nAkin to the adventure of Perseus and Andromeda—indeed, by some supposed to be indirectly derived from it—is that famous story of St. George and the Dragon; which dragon I maintain to have been a whale; for in many old chronicles whales and dragons are strangely jumbled together, and often stand for each other. “Thou art as a lion of the waters, and as a dragon of the sea,” saith Ezekiel; hereby, plainly meaning a whale; in truth, some versions of the Bible use that word itself. Besides, it would much subtract from the glory of the exploit had St. George but encountered a crawling reptile of the land, instead of doing battle with the great monster of the deep. Any man may kill a snake, but only a Perseus, a St. George, a Coffin, have the heart in them to march boldly up to a whale.\n\nLet not the modern paintings of this scene mislead us; for though the creature encountered by that valiant whaleman of old is vaguely represented of a griffin-like shape, and though the battle is depicted on land and the saint on horseback, yet considering the great ignorance of those times, when the true form of the whale was unknown to artists; and considering that as in Perseus’ case, St. George’s whale might have crawled up out of the sea on the beach; and considering that the animal ridden by St. George might have been only a large seal, or sea-horse; bearing all this in mind, it will not appear altogether incompatible with the sacred legend and the ancientest draughts of the scene, to hold this so-called dragon no other than the great Leviathan himself. In fact, placed before the strict and piercing truth, this whole story will fare like that fish, flesh, and fowl idol of the Philistines, Dagon by name; who being planted before the ark of Israel, his horse’s head and both the palms of his hands fell off from him, and only the stump or fishy part of him remained. Thus, then, one of our own noble stamp, even a whaleman, is the tutelary guardian of England; and by good rights, we harpooneers of Nantucket should be enrolled in the most noble order of St. George. And therefore, let not the knights of that honorable company (none of whom, I venture to say, have ever had to do with a whale like their great patron), let them never eye a Nantucketer with disdain, since even in our woollen frocks and tarred trowsers we are much better entitled to St. George’s decoration than they.\n\nWhether to admit Hercules among us or not, concerning this I long remained dubious: for though according to the Greek mythologies, that antique Crockett and Kit Carson—that brawny doer of rejoicing good deeds, was swallowed down and thrown up by a whale; still, whether that strictly makes a whaleman of him, that might be mooted. It nowhere appears that he ever actually harpooned his fish, unless, indeed, from the inside. Nevertheless, he may be deemed a sort of involuntary whaleman; at any rate the whale caught him, if he did not the whale. I claim him for one of our clan.\n\nBut, by the best contradictory authorities, this Grecian story of Hercules and the whale is considered to be derived from the still more ancient Hebrew story of Jonah and the whale; and vice versâ; certainly they are very similar. If I claim the demi-god then, why not the prophet?\n\nNor do heroes, saints, demigods, and prophets alone comprise the whole roll of our order. Our grand master is still to be named; for like royal kings of old times, we find the head waters of our fraternity in nothing short of the great gods themselves. That wondrous oriental story is now to be rehearsed from the Shaster, which gives us the dread Vishnoo, one of the three persons in the godhead of the Hindoos; gives us this divine Vishnoo himself for our Lord;—Vishnoo, who, by the first of his ten earthly incarnations, has for ever set apart and sanctified the whale. When Brahma, or the God of Gods, saith the Shaster, resolved to recreate the world after one of its periodical dissolutions, he gave birth to Vishnoo, to preside over the work; but the Vedas, or mystical books, whose perusal would seem to have been indispensable to Vishnoo before beginning the creation, and which therefore must have contained something in the shape of practical hints to young architects, these Vedas were lying at the bottom of the waters; so Vishnoo became incarnate in a whale, and sounding down in him to the uttermost depths, rescued the sacred volumes. Was not this Vishnoo a whaleman, then? even as a man who rides a horse is called a horseman?\n\nPerseus, St. George, Hercules, Jonah, and Vishnoo! there’s a member-roll for you! What club but the whaleman’s can head off like that?\n\n# CHAPTER 83. Jonah Historically Regarded.\n\nReference was made to the historical story of Jonah and the whale in the preceding chapter. Now some Nantucketers rather distrust this historical story of Jonah and the whale. But then there were some sceptical Greeks and Romans, who, standing out from the orthodox pagans of their times, equally doubted the story of Hercules and the whale, and Arion and the dolphin; and yet their doubting those traditions did not make those traditions one whit the less facts, for all that.\n\nOne old Sag-Harbor whaleman’s chief reason for questioning the Hebrew story was this:—He had one of those quaint old-fashioned Bibles, embellished with curious, unscientific plates; one of which represented Jonah’s whale with two spouts in his head—a peculiarity only true with respect to a species of the Leviathan (the Right Whale, and the varieties of that order), concerning which the fishermen have this saying, “A penny roll would choke him”; his swallow is so very small. But, to this, Bishop Jebb’s anticipative answer is ready. It is not necessary, hints the Bishop, that we consider Jonah as tombed in the whale’s belly, but as temporarily lodged in some part of his mouth. And this seems reasonable enough in the good Bishop. For truly, the Right Whale’s mouth would accommodate a couple of whist-tables, and comfortably seat all the players. Possibly, too, Jonah might have ensconced himself in a hollow tooth; but, on second thoughts, the Right Whale is toothless.\n\nAnother reason which Sag-Harbor (he went by that name) urged for his want of faith in this matter of the prophet, was something obscurely in reference to his incarcerated body and the whale’s gastric juices. But this objection likewise falls to the ground, because a German exegetist supposes that Jonah must have taken refuge in the floating body of a dead whale—even as the French soldiers in the Russian campaign turned their dead horses into tents, and crawled into them. Besides, it has been divined by other continental commentators, that when Jonah was thrown overboard from the Joppa ship, he straightway effected his escape to another vessel near by, some vessel with a whale for a figure-head; and, I would add, possibly called “The Whale,” as some craft are nowadays christened the “Shark,” the “Gull,” the “Eagle.” Nor have there been wanting learned exegetists who have opined that the whale mentioned in the book of Jonah merely meant a life-preserver—an inflated bag of wind—which the endangered prophet swam to, and so was saved from a watery doom. Poor Sag-Harbor, therefore, seems worsted all round. But he had still another reason for his want of faith. It was this, if I remember right: Jonah was swallowed by the whale in the Mediterranean Sea, and after three days he was vomited up somewhere within three days’ journey of Nineveh, a city on the Tigris, very much more than three days’ journey across from the nearest point of the Mediterranean coast. How is that?\n\nBut was there no other way for the whale to land the prophet within that short distance of Nineveh? Yes. He might have carried him round by the way of the Cape of Good Hope. But not to speak of the passage through the whole length of the Mediterranean, and another passage up the Persian Gulf and Red Sea, such a supposition would involve the complete circumnavigation of all Africa in three days, not to speak of the Tigris waters, near the site of Nineveh, being too shallow for any whale to swim in. Besides, this idea of Jonah’s weathering the Cape of Good Hope at so early a day would wrest the honor of the discovery of that great headland from Bartholomew Diaz, its reputed discoverer, and so make modern history a liar.\n\nBut all these foolish arguments of old Sag-Harbor only evinced his foolish pride of reason—a thing still more reprehensible in him, seeing that he had but little learning except what he had picked up from the sun and the sea. I say it only shows his foolish, impious pride, and abominable, devilish rebellion against the reverend clergy. For by a Portuguese Catholic priest, this very idea of Jonah’s going to Nineveh via the Cape of Good Hope was advanced as a signal magnification of the general miracle. And so it was. Besides, to this day, the highly enlightened Turks devoutly believe in the historical story of Jonah. And some three centuries ago, an English traveller in old Harris’s Voyages, speaks of a Turkish Mosque built in honor of Jonah, in which Mosque was a miraculous lamp that burnt without any oil.\n\n# CHAPTER 84. Pitchpoling.\n\nTo make them run easily and swiftly, the axles of carriages are anointed; and for much the same purpose, some whalers perform an analogous operation upon their boat; they grease the bottom. Nor is it to be doubted that as such a procedure can do no harm, it may possibly be of no contemptible advantage; considering that oil and water are hostile; that oil is a sliding thing, and that the object in view is to make the boat slide bravely. Queequeg believed strongly in anointing his boat, and one morning not long after the German ship Jungfrau disappeared, took more than customary pains in that occupation; crawling under its bottom, where it hung over the side, and rubbing in the unctuousness as though diligently seeking to insure a crop of hair from the craft’s bald keel. He seemed to be working in obedience to some particular presentiment. Nor did it remain unwarranted by the event.\n\nTowards noon whales were raised; but so soon as the ship sailed down to them, they turned and fled with swift precipitancy; a disordered flight, as of Cleopatra’s barges from Actium.\n\nNevertheless, the boats pursued, and Stubb’s was foremost. By great exertion, Tashtego at last succeeded in planting one iron; but the stricken whale, without at all sounding, still continued his horizontal flight, with added fleetness. Such unintermitted strainings upon the planted iron must sooner or later inevitably extract it. It became imperative to lance the flying whale, or be content to lose him. But to haul the boat up to his flank was impossible, he swam so fast and furious. What then remained?\n\nOf all the wondrous devices and dexterities, the sleights of hand and countless subtleties, to which the veteran whaleman is so often forced, none exceed that fine manœuvre with the lance called pitchpoling. Small sword, or broad sword, in all its exercises boasts nothing like it. It is only indispensable with an inveterate running whale; its grand fact and feature is the wonderful distance to which the long lance is accurately darted from a violently rocking, jerking boat, under extreme headway. Steel and wood included, the entire spear is some ten or twelve feet in length; the staff is much slighter than that of the harpoon, and also of a lighter material—pine. It is furnished with a small rope called a warp, of considerable length, by which it can be hauled back to the hand after darting.\n\nBut before going further, it is important to mention here, that though the harpoon may be pitchpoled in the same way with the lance, yet it is seldom done; and when done, is still less frequently successful, on account of the greater weight and inferior length of the harpoon as compared with the lance, which in effect become serious drawbacks. As a general thing, therefore, you must first get fast to a whale, before any pitchpoling comes into play.\n\nLook now at Stubb; a man who from his humorous, deliberate coolness and equanimity in the direst emergencies, was specially qualified to excel in pitchpoling. Look at him; he stands upright in the tossed bow of the flying boat; wrapt in fleecy foam, the towing whale is forty feet ahead. Handling the long lance lightly, glancing twice or thrice along its length to see if it be exactly straight, Stubb whistlingly gathers up the coil of the warp in one hand, so as to secure its free end in his grasp, leaving the rest unobstructed. Then holding the lance full before his waistband’s middle, he levels it at the whale; when, covering him with it, he steadily depresses the butt-end in his hand, thereby elevating the point till the weapon stands fairly balanced upon his palm, fifteen feet in the air. He minds you somewhat of a juggler, balancing a long staff on his chin. Next moment with a rapid, nameless impulse, in a superb lofty arch the bright steel spans the foaming distance, and quivers in the life spot of the whale. Instead of sparkling water, he now spouts red blood.\n\n“That drove the spigot out of him!” cried Stubb. “’Tis July’s immortal Fourth; all fountains must run wine today! Would now, it were old Orleans whiskey, or old Ohio, or unspeakable old Monongahela! Then, Tashtego, lad, I’d have ye hold a canakin to the jet, and we’d drink round it! Yea, verily, hearts alive, we’d brew choice punch in the spread of his spout-hole there, and from that live punch-bowl quaff the living stuff.”\n\nAgain and again to such gamesome talk, the dexterous dart is repeated, the spear returning to its master like a greyhound held in skilful leash. The agonized whale goes into his flurry; the tow-line is slackened, and the pitchpoler dropping astern, folds his hands, and mutely watches the monster die.\n\n# CHAPTER 85. The Fountain.\n\nThat for six thousand years—and no one knows how many millions of ages before—the great whales should have been spouting all over the sea, and sprinkling and mistifying the gardens of the deep, as with so many sprinkling or mistifying pots; and that for some centuries back, thousands of hunters should have been close by the fountain of the whale, watching these sprinklings and spoutings—that all this should be, and yet, that down to this blessed minute (fifteen and a quarter minutes past one o’clock P.M. of this sixteenth day of December, A.D. 1851), it should still remain a problem, whether these spoutings are, after all, really water, or nothing but vapor—this is surely a noteworthy thing.\n\nLet us, then, look at this matter, along with some interesting items contingent. Every one knows that by the peculiar cunning of their gills, the finny tribes in general breathe the air which at all times is combined with the element in which they swim; hence, a herring or a cod might live a century, and never once raise its head above the surface. But owing to his marked internal structure which gives him regular lungs, like a human being’s, the whale can only live by inhaling the disengaged air in the open atmosphere. Wherefore the necessity for his periodical visits to the upper world. But he cannot in any degree breathe through his mouth, for, in his ordinary attitude, the Sperm Whale’s mouth is buried at least eight feet beneath the surface; and what is still more, his windpipe has no connexion with his mouth. No, he breathes through his spiracle alone; and this is on the top of his head.\n\nIf I say, that in any creature breathing is only a function indispensable to vitality, inasmuch as it withdraws from the air a certain element, which being subsequently brought into contact with the blood imparts to the blood its vivifying principle, I do not think I shall err; though I may possibly use some superfluous scientific words. Assume it, and it follows that if all the blood in a man could be aerated with one breath, he might then seal up his nostrils and not fetch another for a considerable time. That is to say, he would then live without breathing. Anomalous as it may seem, this is precisely the case with the whale, who systematically lives, by intervals, his full hour and more (when at the bottom) without drawing a single breath, or so much as in any way inhaling a particle of air; for, remember, he has no gills. How is this? Between his ribs and on each side of his spine he is supplied with a remarkable involved Cretan labyrinth of vermicelli-like vessels, which vessels, when he quits the surface, are completely distended with oxygenated blood. So that for an hour or more, a thousand fathoms in the sea, he carries a surplus stock of vitality in him, just as the camel crossing the waterless desert carries a surplus supply of drink for future use in its four supplementary stomachs. The anatomical fact of this labyrinth is indisputable; and that the supposition founded upon it is reasonable and true, seems the more cogent to me, when I consider the otherwise inexplicable obstinacy of that leviathan in having his spoutings out, as the fishermen phrase it. This is what I mean. If unmolested, upon rising to the surface, the Sperm Whale will continue there for a period of time exactly uniform with all his other unmolested risings. Say he stays eleven minutes, and jets seventy times, that is, respires seventy breaths; then whenever he rises again, he will be sure to have his seventy breaths over again, to a minute. Now, if after he fetches a few breaths you alarm him, so that he sounds, he will be always dodging up again to make good his regular allowance of air. And not till those seventy breaths are told, will he finally go down to stay out his full term below. Remark, however, that in different individuals these rates are different; but in any one they are alike. Now, why should the whale thus insist upon having his spoutings out, unless it be to replenish his reservoir of air, ere descending for good? How obvious is it, too, that this necessity for the whale’s rising exposes him to all the fatal hazards of the chase. For not by hook or by net could this vast leviathan be caught, when sailing a thousand fathoms beneath the sunlight. Not so much thy skill, then, O hunter, as the great necessities that strike the victory to thee!\n\nIn man, breathing is incessantly going on—one breath only serving for two or three pulsations; so that whatever other business he has to attend to, waking or sleeping, breathe he must, or die he will. But the Sperm Whale only breathes about one seventh or Sunday of his time.\n\nIt has been said that the whale only breathes through his spout-hole; if it could truthfully be added that his spouts are mixed with water, then I opine we should be furnished with the reason why his sense of smell seems obliterated in him; for the only thing about him that at all answers to his nose is that identical spout-hole; and being so clogged with two elements, it could not be expected to have the power of smelling. But owing to the mystery of the spout—whether it be water or whether it be vapor—no absolute certainty can as yet be arrived at on this head. Sure it is, nevertheless, that the Sperm Whale has no proper olfactories. But what does he want of them? No roses, no violets, no Cologne-water in the sea.\n\nFurthermore, as his windpipe solely opens into the tube of his spouting canal, and as that long canal—like the grand Erie Canal—is furnished with a sort of locks (that open and shut) for the downward retention of air or the upward exclusion of water, therefore the whale has no voice; unless you insult him by saying, that when he so strangely rumbles, he talks through his nose. But then again, what has the whale to say? Seldom have I known any profound being that had anything to say to this world, unless forced to stammer out something by way of getting a living. Oh! happy that the world is such an excellent listener!\n\nNow, the spouting canal of the Sperm Whale, chiefly intended as it is for the conveyance of air, and for several feet laid along, horizontally, just beneath the upper surface of his head, and a little to one side; this curious canal is very much like a gas-pipe laid down in a city on one side of a street. But the question returns whether this gas-pipe is also a water-pipe; in other words, whether the spout of the Sperm Whale is the mere vapor of the exhaled breath, or whether that exhaled breath is mixed with water taken in at the mouth, and discharged through the spiracle. It is certain that the mouth indirectly communicates with the spouting canal; but it cannot be proved that this is for the purpose of discharging water through the spiracle. Because the greatest necessity for so doing would seem to be, when in feeding he accidentally takes in water. But the Sperm Whale’s food is far beneath the surface, and there he cannot spout even if he would. Besides, if you regard him very closely, and time him with your watch, you will find that when unmolested, there is an undeviating rhyme between the periods of his jets and the ordinary periods of respiration.\n\nBut why pester one with all this reasoning on the subject? Speak out! You have seen him spout; then declare what the spout is; can you not tell water from air? My dear sir, in this world it is not so easy to settle these plain things. I have ever found your plain things the knottiest of all. And as for this whale spout, you might almost stand in it, and yet be undecided as to what it is precisely.\n\nThe central body of it is hidden in the snowy sparkling mist enveloping it; and how can you certainly tell whether any water falls from it, when, always, when you are close enough to a whale to get a close view of his spout, he is in a prodigious commotion, the water cascading all around him. And if at such times you should think that you really perceived drops of moisture in the spout, how do you know that they are not merely condensed from its vapor; or how do you know that they are not those identical drops superficially lodged in the spout-hole fissure, which is countersunk into the summit of the whale’s head? For even when tranquilly swimming through the mid-day sea in a calm, with his elevated hump sun-dried as a dromedary’s in the desert; even then, the whale always carries a small basin of water on his head, as under a blazing sun you will sometimes see a cavity in a rock filled up with rain.\n\nNor is it at all prudent for the hunter to be over curious touching the precise nature of the whale spout. It will not do for him to be peering into it, and putting his face in it. You cannot go with your pitcher to this fountain and fill it, and bring it away. For even when coming into slight contact with the outer, vapory shreds of the jet, which will often happen, your skin will feverishly smart, from the acridness of the thing so touching it. And I know one, who coming into still closer contact with the spout, whether with some scientific object in view, or otherwise, I cannot say, the skin peeled off from his cheek and arm. Wherefore, among whalemen, the spout is deemed poisonous; they try to evade it. Another thing; I have heard it said, and I do not much doubt it, that if the jet is fairly spouted into your eyes, it will blind you. The wisest thing the investigator can do then, it seems to me, is to let this deadly spout alone.\n\nStill, we can hypothesize, even if we cannot prove and establish. My hypothesis is this: that the spout is nothing but mist. And besides other reasons, to this conclusion I am impelled, by considerations touching the great inherent dignity and sublimity of the Sperm Whale; I account him no common, shallow being, inasmuch as it is an undisputed fact that he is never found on soundings, or near shores; all other whales sometimes are. He is both ponderous and profound. And I am convinced that from the heads of all ponderous profound beings, such as Plato, Pyrrho, the Devil, Jupiter, Dante, and so on, there always goes up a certain semi-visible steam, while in the act of thinking deep thoughts. While composing a little treatise on Eternity, I had the curiosity to place a mirror before me; and ere long saw reflected there, a curious involved worming and undulation in the atmosphere over my head. The invariable moisture of my hair, while plunged in deep thought, after six cups of hot tea in my thin shingled attic, of an August noon; this seems an additional argument for the above supposition.\n\nAnd how nobly it raises our conceit of the mighty, misty monster, to behold him solemnly sailing through a calm tropical sea; his vast, mild head overhung by a canopy of vapor, engendered by his incommunicable contemplations, and that vapor—as you will sometimes see it—glorified by a rainbow, as if Heaven itself had put its seal upon his thoughts. For, d’ye see, rainbows do not visit the clear air; they only irradiate vapor. And so, through all the thick mists of the dim doubts in my mind, divine intuitions now and then shoot, enkindling my fog with a heavenly ray. And for this I thank God; for all have doubts; many deny; but doubts or denials, few along with them, have intuitions. Doubts of all things earthly, and intuitions of some things heavenly; this combination makes neither believer nor infidel, but makes a man who regards them both with equal eye.\n\n# CHAPTER 86. The Tail.\n\nOther poets have warbled the praises of the soft eye of the antelope, and the lovely plumage of the bird that never alights; less celestial, I celebrate a tail.\n\nReckoning the largest sized Sperm Whale’s tail to begin at that point of the trunk where it tapers to about the girth of a man, it comprises upon its upper surface alone, an area of at least fifty square feet. The compact round body of its root expands into two broad, firm, flat palms or flukes, gradually shoaling away to less than an inch in thickness. At the crotch or junction, these flukes slightly overlap, then sideways recede from each other like wings, leaving a wide vacancy between. In no living thing are the lines of beauty more exquisitely defined than in the crescentic borders of these flukes. At its utmost expansion in the full grown whale, the tail will considerably exceed twenty feet across.\n\nThe entire member seems a dense webbed bed of welded sinews; but cut into it, and you find that three distinct strata compose it:—upper, middle, and lower. The fibres in the upper and lower layers, are long and horizontal; those of the middle one, very short, and running crosswise between the outside layers. This triune structure, as much as anything else, imparts power to the tail. To the student of old Roman walls, the middle layer will furnish a curious parallel to the thin course of tiles always alternating with the stone in those wonderful relics of the antique, and which undoubtedly contribute so much to the great strength of the masonry.\n\nBut as if this vast local power in the tendinous tail were not enough, the whole bulk of the leviathan is knit over with a warp and woof of muscular fibres and filaments, which passing on either side the loins and running down into the flukes, insensibly blend with them, and largely contribute to their might; so that in the tail the confluent measureless force of the whole whale seems concentrated to a point. Could annihilation occur to matter, this were the thing to do it.\n\nNor does this—its amazing strength, at all tend to cripple the graceful flexion of its motions; where infantileness of ease undulates through a Titanism of power. On the contrary, those motions derive their most appalling beauty from it. Real strength never impairs beauty or harmony, but it often bestows it; and in everything imposingly beautiful, strength has much to do with the magic. Take away the tied tendons that all over seem bursting from the marble in the carved Hercules, and its charm would be gone. As devout Eckerman lifted the linen sheet from the naked corpse of Goethe, he was overwhelmed with the massive chest of the man, that seemed as a Roman triumphal arch. When Angelo paints even God the Father in human form, mark what robustness is there. And whatever they may reveal of the divine love in the Son, the soft, curled, hermaphroditical Italian pictures, in which his idea has been most successfully embodied; these pictures, so destitute as they are of all brawniness, hint nothing of any power, but the mere negative, feminine one of submission and endurance, which on all hands it is conceded, form the peculiar practical virtues of his teachings.\n\nSuch is the subtle elasticity of the organ I treat of, that whether wielded in sport, or in earnest, or in anger, whatever be the mood it be in, its flexions are invariably marked by exceeding grace. Therein no fairy’s arm can transcend it.\n\nFive great motions are peculiar to it. First, when used as a fin for progression; Second, when used as a mace in battle; Third, in sweeping; Fourth, in lobtailing; Fifth, in peaking flukes.\n\nFirst: Being horizontal in its position, the Leviathan’s tail acts in a different manner from the tails of all other sea creatures. It never wriggles. In man or fish, wriggling is a sign of inferiority. To the whale, his tail is the sole means of propulsion. Scroll-wise coiled forwards beneath the body, and then rapidly sprung backwards, it is this which gives that singular darting, leaping motion to the monster when furiously swimming. His side-fins only serve to steer by.\n\nSecond: It is a little significant, that while one sperm whale only fights another sperm whale with his head and jaw, nevertheless, in his conflicts with man, he chiefly and contemptuously uses his tail. In striking at a boat, he swiftly curves away his flukes from it, and the blow is only inflicted by the recoil. If it be made in the unobstructed air, especially if it descend to its mark, the stroke is then simply irresistible. No ribs of man or boat can withstand it. Your only salvation lies in eluding it; but if it comes sideways through the opposing water, then partly owing to the light buoyancy of the whale-boat, and the elasticity of its materials, a cracked rib or a dashed plank or two, a sort of stitch in the side, is generally the most serious result. These submerged side blows are so often received in the fishery, that they are accounted mere child’s play. Some one strips off a frock, and the hole is stopped.\n\nThird: I cannot demonstrate it, but it seems to me, that in the whale the sense of touch is concentrated in the tail; for in this respect there is a delicacy in it only equalled by the daintiness of the elephant’s trunk. This delicacy is chiefly evinced in the action of sweeping, when in maidenly gentleness the whale with a certain soft slowness moves his immense flukes from side to side upon the surface of the sea; and if he feel but a sailor’s whisker, woe to that sailor, whiskers and all. What tenderness there is in that preliminary touch! Had this tail any prehensile power, I should straightway bethink me of Darmonodes’ elephant that so frequented the flower-market, and with low salutations presented nosegays to damsels, and then caressed their zones. On more accounts than one, a pity it is that the whale does not possess this prehensile virtue in his tail; for I have heard of yet another elephant, that when wounded in the fight, curved round his trunk and extracted the dart.\n\nFourth: Stealing unawares upon the whale in the fancied security of the middle of solitary seas, you find him unbent from the vast corpulence of his dignity, and kitten-like, he plays on the ocean as if it were a hearth. But still you see his power in his play. The broad palms of his tail are flirted high into the air; then smiting the surface, the thunderous concussion resounds for miles. You would almost think a great gun had been discharged; and if you noticed the light wreath of vapor from the spiracle at his other extremity, you would think that that was the smoke from the touch-hole.\n\nFifth: As in the ordinary floating posture of the leviathan the flukes lie considerably below the level of his back, they are then completely out of sight beneath the surface; but when he is about to plunge into the deeps, his entire flukes with at least thirty feet of his body are tossed erect in the air, and so remain vibrating a moment, till they downwards shoot out of view. Excepting the sublime breach—somewhere else to be described—this peaking of the whale’s flukes is perhaps the grandest sight to be seen in all animated nature. Out of the bottomless profundities the gigantic tail seems spasmodically snatching at the highest heaven. So in dreams, have I seen majestic Satan thrusting forth his tormented colossal claw from the flame Baltic of Hell. But in gazing at such scenes, it is all in all what mood you are in; if in the Dantean, the devils will occur to you; if in that of Isaiah, the archangels. Standing at the mast-head of my ship during a sunrise that crimsoned sky and sea, I once saw a large herd of whales in the east, all heading towards the sun, and for a moment vibrating in concert with peaked flukes. As it seemed to me at the time, such a grand embodiment of adoration of the gods was never beheld, even in Persia, the home of the fire worshippers. As Ptolemy Philopater testified of the African elephant, I then testified of the whale, pronouncing him the most devout of all beings. For according to King Juba, the military elephants of antiquity often hailed the morning with their trunks uplifted in the profoundest silence.\n\nThe chance comparison in this chapter, between the whale and the elephant, so far as some aspects of the tail of the one and the trunk of the other are concerned, should not tend to place those two opposite organs on an equality, much less the creatures to which they respectively belong. For as the mightiest elephant is but a terrier to Leviathan, so, compared with Leviathan’s tail, his trunk is but the stalk of a lily. The most direful blow from the elephant’s trunk were as the playful tap of a fan, compared with the measureless crush and crash of the sperm whale’s ponderous flukes, which in repeated instances have one after the other hurled entire boats with all their oars and crews into the air, very much as an Indian juggler tosses his balls.*\n\n*Though all comparison in the way of general bulk between the whale and the elephant is preposterous, inasmuch as in that particular the elephant stands in much the same respect to the whale that a dog does to the elephant; nevertheless, there are not wanting some points of curious similitude; among these is the spout. It is well known that the elephant will often draw up water or dust in his trunk, and then elevating it, jet it forth in a stream.\n\nThe more I consider this mighty tail, the more do I deplore my inability to express it. At times there are gestures in it, which, though they would well grace the hand of man, remain wholly inexplicable. In an extensive herd, so remarkable, occasionally, are these mystic gestures, that I have heard hunters who have declared them akin to Free-Mason signs and symbols; that the whale, indeed, by these methods intelligently conversed with the world. Nor are there wanting other motions of the whale in his general body, full of strangeness, and unaccountable to his most experienced assailant. Dissect him how I may, then, I but go skin deep; I know him not, and never will. But if I know not even the tail of this whale, how understand his head? much more, how comprehend his face, when face he has none? Thou shalt see my back parts, my tail, he seems to say, but my face shall not be seen. But I cannot completely make out his back parts; and hint what he will about his face, I say again he has no face.\n\n# CHAPTER 87. The Grand Armada.\n\nThe long and narrow peninsula of Malacca, extending south-eastward from the territories of Birmah, forms the most southerly point of all Asia. In a continuous line from that peninsula stretch the long islands of Sumatra, Java, Bally, and Timor; which, with many others, form a vast mole, or rampart, lengthwise connecting Asia with Australia, and dividing the long unbroken Indian ocean from the thickly studded oriental archipelagoes. This rampart is pierced by several sally-ports for the convenience of ships and whales; conspicuous among which are the straits of Sunda and Malacca. By the straits of Sunda, chiefly, vessels bound to China from the west, emerge into the China seas.\n\nThose narrow straits of Sunda divide Sumatra from Java; and standing midway in that vast rampart of islands, buttressed by that bold green promontory, known to seamen as Java Head; they not a little correspond to the central gateway opening into some vast walled empire: and considering the inexhaustible wealth of spices, and silks, and jewels, and gold, and ivory, with which the thousand islands of that oriental sea are enriched, it seems a significant provision of nature, that such treasures, by the very formation of the land, should at least bear the appearance, however ineffectual, of being guarded from the all-grasping western world. The shores of the Straits of Sunda are unsupplied with those domineering fortresses which guard the entrances to the Mediterranean, the Baltic, and the Propontis. Unlike the Danes, these Orientals do not demand the obsequious homage of lowered top-sails from the endless procession of ships before the wind, which for centuries past, by night and by day, have passed between the islands of Sumatra and Java, freighted with the costliest cargoes of the east. But while they freely waive a ceremonial like this, they do by no means renounce their claim to more solid tribute.\n\nTime out of mind the piratical proas of the Malays, lurking among the low shaded coves and islets of Sumatra, have sallied out upon the vessels sailing through the straits, fiercely demanding tribute at the point of their spears. Though by the repeated bloody chastisements they have received at the hands of European cruisers, the audacity of these corsairs has of late been somewhat repressed; yet, even at the present day, we occasionally hear of English and American vessels, which, in those waters, have been remorselessly boarded and pillaged.\n\nWith a fair, fresh wind, the Pequod was now drawing nigh to these straits; Ahab purposing to pass through them into the Javan sea, and thence, cruising northwards, over waters known to be frequented here and there by the Sperm Whale, sweep inshore by the Philippine Islands, and gain the far coast of Japan, in time for the great whaling season there. By these means, the circumnavigating Pequod would sweep almost all the known Sperm Whale cruising grounds of the world, previous to descending upon the Line in the Pacific; where Ahab, though everywhere else foiled in his pursuit, firmly counted upon giving battle to Moby Dick, in the sea he was most known to frequent; and at a season when he might most reasonably be presumed to be haunting it.\n\nBut how now? in this zoned quest, does Ahab touch no land? does his crew drink air? Surely, he will stop for water. Nay. For a long time, now, the circus-running sun has raced within his fiery ring, and needs no sustenance but what’s in himself. So Ahab. Mark this, too, in the whaler. While other hulls are loaded down with alien stuff, to be transferred to foreign wharves; the world-wandering whale-ship carries no cargo but herself and crew, their weapons and their wants. She has a whole lake’s contents bottled in her ample hold. She is ballasted with utilities; not altogether with unusable pig-lead and kentledge. She carries years’ water in her. Clear old prime Nantucket water; which, when three years afloat, the Nantucketer, in the Pacific, prefers to drink before the brackish fluid, but yesterday rafted off in casks, from the Peruvian or Indian streams. Hence it is, that, while other ships may have gone to China from New York, and back again, touching at a score of ports, the whale-ship, in all that interval, may not have sighted one grain of soil; her crew having seen no man but floating seamen like themselves. So that did you carry them the news that another flood had come; they would only answer—“Well, boys, here’s the ark!”\n\nNow, as many Sperm Whales had been captured off the western coast of Java, in the near vicinity of the Straits of Sunda; indeed, as most of the ground, roundabout, was generally recognised by the fishermen as an excellent spot for cruising; therefore, as the Pequod gained more and more upon Java Head, the look-outs were repeatedly hailed, and admonished to keep wide awake. But though the green palmy cliffs of the land soon loomed on the starboard bow, and with delighted nostrils the fresh cinnamon was snuffed in the air, yet not a single jet was descried. Almost renouncing all thought of falling in with any game hereabouts, the ship had well nigh entered the straits, when the customary cheering cry was heard from aloft, and ere long a spectacle of singular magnificence saluted us.\n\nBut here be it premised, that owing to the unwearied activity with which of late they have been hunted over all four oceans, the Sperm Whales, instead of almost invariably sailing in small detached companies, as in former times, are now frequently met with in extensive herds, sometimes embracing so great a multitude, that it would almost seem as if numerous nations of them had sworn solemn league and covenant for mutual assistance and protection. To this aggregation of the Sperm Whale into such immense caravans, may be imputed the circumstance that even in the best cruising grounds, you may now sometimes sail for weeks and months together, without being greeted by a single spout; and then be suddenly saluted by what sometimes seems thousands on thousands.\n\nBroad on both bows, at the distance of some two or three miles, and forming a great semicircle, embracing one half of the level horizon, a continuous chain of whale-jets were up-playing and sparkling in the noon-day air. Unlike the straight perpendicular twin-jets of the Right Whale, which, dividing at top, fall over in two branches, like the cleft drooping boughs of a willow, the single forward-slanting spout of the Sperm Whale presents a thick curled bush of white mist, continually rising and falling away to leeward.\n\nSeen from the Pequod’s deck, then, as she would rise on a high hill of the sea, this host of vapory spouts, individually curling up into the air, and beheld through a blending atmosphere of bluish haze, showed like the thousand cheerful chimneys of some dense metropolis, descried of a balmy autumnal morning, by some horseman on a height.\n\nAs marching armies approaching an unfriendly defile in the mountains, accelerate their march, all eagerness to place that perilous passage in their rear, and once more expand in comparative security upon the plain; even so did this vast fleet of whales now seem hurrying forward through the straits; gradually contracting the wings of their semicircle, and swimming on, in one solid, but still crescentic centre.\n\nCrowding all sail the Pequod pressed after them; the harpooneers handling their weapons, and loudly cheering from the heads of their yet suspended boats. If the wind only held, little doubt had they, that chased through these Straits of Sunda, the vast host would only deploy into the Oriental seas to witness the capture of not a few of their number. And who could tell whether, in that congregated caravan, Moby Dick himself might not temporarily be swimming, like the worshipped white-elephant in the coronation procession of the Siamese! So with stun-sail piled on stun-sail, we sailed along, driving these leviathans before us; when, of a sudden, the voice of Tashtego was heard, loudly directing attention to something in our wake.\n\nCorresponding to the crescent in our van, we beheld another in our rear. It seemed formed of detached white vapors, rising and falling something like the spouts of the whales; only they did not so completely come and go; for they constantly hovered, without finally disappearing. Levelling his glass at this sight, Ahab quickly revolved in his pivot-hole, crying, “Aloft there, and rig whips and buckets to wet the sails;—Malays, sir, and after us!”\n\nAs if too long lurking behind the headlands, till the Pequod should fairly have entered the straits, these rascally Asiatics were now in hot pursuit, to make up for their over-cautious delay. But when the swift Pequod, with a fresh leading wind, was herself in hot chase; how very kind of these tawny philanthropists to assist in speeding her on to her own chosen pursuit,—mere riding-whips and rowels to her, that they were. As with glass under arm, Ahab to-and-fro paced the deck; in his forward turn beholding the monsters he chased, and in the after one the bloodthirsty pirates chasing him; some such fancy as the above seemed his. And when he glanced upon the green walls of the watery defile in which the ship was then sailing, and bethought him that through that gate lay the route to his vengeance, and beheld, how that through that same gate he was now both chasing and being chased to his deadly end; and not only that, but a herd of remorseless wild pirates and inhuman atheistical devils were infernally cheering him on with their curses;—when all these conceits had passed through his brain, Ahab’s brow was left gaunt and ribbed, like the black sand beach after some stormy tide has been gnawing it, without being able to drag the firm thing from its place.\n\nBut thoughts like these troubled very few of the reckless crew; and when, after steadily dropping and dropping the pirates astern, the Pequod at last shot by the vivid green Cockatoo Point on the Sumatra side, emerging at last upon the broad waters beyond; then, the harpooneers seemed more to grieve that the swift whales had been gaining upon the ship, than to rejoice that the ship had so victoriously gained upon the Malays. But still driving on in the wake of the whales, at length they seemed abating their speed; gradually the ship neared them; and the wind now dying away, word was passed to spring to the boats. But no sooner did the herd, by some presumed wonderful instinct of the Sperm Whale, become notified of the three keels that were after them,—though as yet a mile in their rear,—than they rallied again, and forming in close ranks and battalions, so that their spouts all looked like flashing lines of stacked bayonets, moved on with redoubled velocity.\n\nStripped to our shirts and drawers, we sprang to the white-ash, and after several hours’ pulling were almost disposed to renounce the chase, when a general pausing commotion among the whales gave animating token that they were now at last under the influence of that strange perplexity of inert irresolution, which, when the fishermen perceive it in the whale, they say he is gallied. The compact martial columns in which they had been hitherto rapidly and steadily swimming, were now broken up in one measureless rout; and like King Porus’ elephants in the Indian battle with Alexander, they seemed going mad with consternation. In all directions expanding in vast irregular circles, and aimlessly swimming hither and thither, by their short thick spoutings, they plainly betrayed their distraction of panic. This was still more strangely evinced by those of their number, who, completely paralysed as it were, helplessly floated like water-logged dismantled ships on the sea. Had these Leviathans been but a flock of simple sheep, pursued over the pasture by three fierce wolves, they could not possibly have evinced such excessive dismay. But this occasional timidity is characteristic of almost all herding creatures. Though banding together in tens of thousands, the lion-maned buffaloes of the West have fled before a solitary horseman. Witness, too, all human beings, how when herded together in the sheepfold of a theatre’s pit, they will, at the slightest alarm of fire, rush helter-skelter for the outlets, crowding, trampling, jamming, and remorselessly dashing each other to death. Best, therefore, withhold any amazement at the strangely gallied whales before us, for there is no folly of the beasts of the earth which is not infinitely outdone by the madness of men.\n\nThough many of the whales, as has been said, were in violent motion, yet it is to be observed that as a whole the herd neither advanced nor retreated, but collectively remained in one place. As is customary in those cases, the boats at once separated, each making for some one lone whale on the outskirts of the shoal. In about three minutes’ time, Queequeg’s harpoon was flung; the stricken fish darted blinding spray in our faces, and then running away with us like light, steered straight for the heart of the herd. Though such a movement on the part of the whale struck under such circumstances, is in no wise unprecedented; and indeed is almost always more or less anticipated; yet does it present one of the more perilous vicissitudes of the fishery. For as the swift monster drags you deeper and deeper into the frantic shoal, you bid adieu to circumspect life and only exist in a delirious throb.\n\nAs, blind and deaf, the whale plunged forward, as if by sheer power of speed to rid himself of the iron leech that had fastened to him; as we thus tore a white gash in the sea, on all sides menaced as we flew, by the crazed creatures to and fro rushing about us; our beset boat was like a ship mobbed by ice-isles in a tempest, and striving to steer through their complicated channels and straits, knowing not at what moment it may be locked in and crushed.\n\nBut not a bit daunted, Queequeg steered us manfully; now sheering off from this monster directly across our route in advance; now edging away from that, whose colossal flukes were suspended overhead, while all the time, Starbuck stood up in the bows, lance in hand, pricking out of our way whatever whales he could reach by short darts, for there was no time to make long ones. Nor were the oarsmen quite idle, though their wonted duty was now altogether dispensed with. They chiefly attended to the shouting part of the business. “Out of the way, Commodore!” cried one, to a great dromedary that of a sudden rose bodily to the surface, and for an instant threatened to swamp us. “Hard down with your tail, there!” cried a second to another, which, close to our gunwale, seemed calmly cooling himself with his own fan-like extremity.\n\nAll whaleboats carry certain curious contrivances, originally invented by the Nantucket Indians, called druggs. Two thick squares of wood of equal size are stoutly clenched together, so that they cross each other’s grain at right angles; a line of considerable length is then attached to the middle of this block, and the other end of the line being looped, it can in a moment be fastened to a harpoon. It is chiefly among gallied whales that this drugg is used. For then, more whales are close round you than you can possibly chase at one time. But sperm whales are not every day encountered; while you may, then, you must kill all you can. And if you cannot kill them all at once, you must wing them, so that they can be afterwards killed at your leisure. Hence it is, that at times like these the drugg, comes into requisition. Our boat was furnished with three of them. The first and second were successfully darted, and we saw the whales staggeringly running off, fettered by the enormous sidelong resistance of the towing drugg. They were cramped like malefactors with the chain and ball. But upon flinging the third, in the act of tossing overboard the clumsy wooden block, it caught under one of the seats of the boat, and in an instant tore it out and carried it away, dropping the oarsman in the boat’s bottom as the seat slid from under him. On both sides the sea came in at the wounded planks, but we stuffed two or three drawers and shirts in, and so stopped the leaks for the time.\n\nIt had been next to impossible to dart these drugged-harpoons, were it not that as we advanced into the herd, our whale’s way greatly diminished; moreover, that as we went still further and further from the circumference of commotion, the direful disorders seemed waning. So that when at last the jerking harpoon drew out, and the towing whale sideways vanished; then, with the tapering force of his parting momentum, we glided between two whales into the innermost heart of the shoal, as if from some mountain torrent we had slid into a serene valley lake. Here the storms in the roaring glens between the outermost whales, were heard but not felt. In this central expanse the sea presented that smooth satin-like surface, called a sleek, produced by the subtle moisture thrown off by the whale in his more quiet moods. Yes, we were now in that enchanted calm which they say lurks at the heart of every commotion. And still in the distracted distance we beheld the tumults of the outer concentric circles, and saw successive pods of whales, eight or ten in each, swiftly going round and round, like multiplied spans of horses in a ring; and so closely shoulder to shoulder, that a Titanic circus-rider might easily have over-arched the middle ones, and so have gone round on their backs. Owing to the density of the crowd of reposing whales, more immediately surrounding the embayed axis of the herd, no possible chance of escape was at present afforded us. We must watch for a breach in the living wall that hemmed us in; the wall that had only admitted us in order to shut us up. Keeping at the centre of the lake, we were occasionally visited by small tame cows and calves; the women and children of this routed host.\n\nNow, inclusive of the occasional wide intervals between the revolving outer circles, and inclusive of the spaces between the various pods in any one of those circles, the entire area at this juncture, embraced by the whole multitude, must have contained at least two or three square miles. At any rate—though indeed such a test at such a time might be deceptive—spoutings might be discovered from our low boat that seemed playing up almost from the rim of the horizon. I mention this circumstance, because, as if the cows and calves had been purposely locked up in this innermost fold; and as if the wide extent of the herd had hitherto prevented them from learning the precise cause of its stopping; or, possibly, being so young, unsophisticated, and every way innocent and inexperienced; however it may have been, these smaller whales—now and then visiting our becalmed boat from the margin of the lake—evinced a wondrous fearlessness and confidence, or else a still becharmed panic which it was impossible not to marvel at. Like household dogs they came snuffling round us, right up to our gunwales, and touching them; till it almost seemed that some spell had suddenly domesticated them. Queequeg patted their foreheads; Starbuck scratched their backs with his lance; but fearful of the consequences, for the time refrained from darting it.\n\nBut far beneath this wondrous world upon the surface, another and still stranger world met our eyes as we gazed over the side. For, suspended in those watery vaults, floated the forms of the nursing mothers of the whales, and those that by their enormous girth seemed shortly to become mothers. The lake, as I have hinted, was to a considerable depth exceedingly transparent; and as human infants while suckling will calmly and fixedly gaze away from the breast, as if leading two different lives at the time; and while yet drawing mortal nourishment, be still spiritually feasting upon some unearthly reminiscence;—even so did the young of these whales seem looking up towards us, but not at us, as if we were but a bit of Gulfweed in their new-born sight. Floating on their sides, the mothers also seemed quietly eyeing us. One of these little infants, that from certain queer tokens seemed hardly a day old, might have measured some fourteen feet in length, and some six feet in girth. He was a little frisky; though as yet his body seemed scarce yet recovered from that irksome position it had so lately occupied in the maternal reticule; where, tail to head, and all ready for the final spring, the unborn whale lies bent like a Tartar’s bow. The delicate side-fins, and the palms of his flukes, still freshly retained the plaited crumpled appearance of a baby’s ears newly arrived from foreign parts.\n\n“Line! line!” cried Queequeg, looking over the gunwale; “him fast! him fast!—Who line him! Who struck?—Two whale; one big, one little!”\n\n“What ails ye, man?” cried Starbuck.\n\n“Look-e here,” said Queequeg, pointing down.\n\nAs when the stricken whale, that from the tub has reeled out hundreds of fathoms of rope; as, after deep sounding, he floats up again, and shows the slackened curling line buoyantly rising and spiralling towards the air; so now, Starbuck saw long coils of the umbilical cord of Madame Leviathan, by which the young cub seemed still tethered to its dam. Not seldom in the rapid vicissitudes of the chase, this natural line, with the maternal end loose, becomes entangled with the hempen one, so that the cub is thereby trapped. Some of the subtlest secrets of the seas seemed divulged to us in this enchanted pond. We saw young Leviathan amours in the deep.*\n\n*The sperm whale, as with all other species of the Leviathan, but unlike most other fish, breeds indifferently at all seasons; after a gestation which may probably be set down at nine months, producing but one at a time; though in some few known instances giving birth to an Esau and Jacob:—a contingency provided for in suckling by two teats, curiously situated, one on each side of the anus; but the breasts themselves extend upwards from that. When by chance these precious parts in a nursing whale are cut by the hunter’s lance, the mother’s pouring milk and blood rivallingly discolour the sea for rods. The milk is very sweet and rich; it has been tasted by man; it might do well with strawberries. When overflowing with mutual esteem, the whales salute more hominum.\n\nAnd thus, though surrounded by circle upon circle of consternations and affrights, did these inscrutable creatures at the centre freely and fearlessly indulge in all peaceful concernments; yea, serenely revelled in dalliance and delight. But even so, amid the tornadoed Atlantic of my being, do I myself still for ever centrally disport in mute calm; and while ponderous planets of unwaning woe revolve round me, deep down and deep inland there I still bathe me in eternal mildness of joy.\n\nMeanwhile, as we thus lay entranced, the occasional sudden frantic spectacles in the distance evinced the activity of the other boats, still engaged in drugging the whales on the frontier of the host; or possibly carrying on the war within the first circle, where abundance of room and some convenient retreats were afforded them. But the sight of the enraged drugged whales now and then blindly darting to and fro across the circles, was nothing to what at last met our eyes. It is sometimes the custom when fast to a whale more than commonly powerful and alert, to seek to hamstring him, as it were, by sundering or maiming his gigantic tail-tendon. It is done by darting a short-handled cutting-spade, to which is attached a rope for hauling it back again. A whale wounded (as we afterwards learned) in this part, but not effectually, as it seemed, had broken away from the boat, carrying along with him half of the harpoon line; and in the extraordinary agony of the wound, he was now dashing among the revolving circles like the lone mounted desperado Arnold, at the battle of Saratoga, carrying dismay wherever he went.\n\nBut agonizing as was the wound of this whale, and an appalling spectacle enough, any way; yet the peculiar horror with which he seemed to inspire the rest of the herd, was owing to a cause which at first the intervening distance obscured from us. But at length we perceived that by one of the unimaginable accidents of the fishery, this whale had become entangled in the harpoon-line that he towed; he had also run away with the cutting-spade in him; and while the free end of the rope attached to that weapon, had permanently caught in the coils of the harpoon-line round his tail, the cutting-spade itself had worked loose from his flesh. So that tormented to madness, he was now churning through the water, violently flailing with his flexible tail, and tossing the keen spade about him, wounding and murdering his own comrades.\n\nThis terrific object seemed to recall the whole herd from their stationary fright. First, the whales forming the margin of our lake began to crowd a little, and tumble against each other, as if lifted by half spent billows from afar; then the lake itself began faintly to heave and swell; the submarine bridal-chambers and nurseries vanished; in more and more contracting orbits the whales in the more central circles began to swim in thickening clusters. Yes, the long calm was departing. A low advancing hum was soon heard; and then like to the tumultuous masses of block-ice when the great river Hudson breaks up in Spring, the entire host of whales came tumbling upon their inner centre, as if to pile themselves up in one common mountain. Instantly Starbuck and Queequeg changed places; Starbuck taking the stern.\n\n“Oars! Oars!” he intensely whispered, seizing the helm—“gripe your oars, and clutch your souls, now! My God, men, stand by! Shove him off, you Queequeg—the whale there!—prick him!—hit him! Stand up—stand up, and stay so! Spring, men—pull, men; never mind their backs—scrape them!—scrape away!”\n\nThe boat was now all but jammed between two vast black bulks, leaving a narrow Dardanelles between their long lengths. But by desperate endeavor we at last shot into a temporary opening; then giving way rapidly, and at the same time earnestly watching for another outlet. After many similar hair-breadth escapes, we at last swiftly glided into what had just been one of the outer circles, but now crossed by random whales, all violently making for one centre. This lucky salvation was cheaply purchased by the loss of Queequeg’s hat, who, while standing in the bows to prick the fugitive whales, had his hat taken clean from his head by the air-eddy made by the sudden tossing of a pair of broad flukes close by.\n\nRiotous and disordered as the universal commotion now was, it soon resolved itself into what seemed a systematic movement; for having clumped together at last in one dense body, they then renewed their onward flight with augmented fleetness. Further pursuit was useless; but the boats still lingered in their wake to pick up what drugged whales might be dropped astern, and likewise to secure one which Flask had killed and waifed. The waif is a pennoned pole, two or three of which are carried by every boat; and which, when additional game is at hand, are inserted upright into the floating body of a dead whale, both to mark its place on the sea, and also as token of prior possession, should the boats of any other ship draw near.\n\nThe result of this lowering was somewhat illustrative of that sagacious saying in the Fishery,—the more whales the less fish. Of all the drugged whales only one was captured. The rest contrived to escape for the time, but only to be taken, as will hereafter be seen, by some other craft than the Pequod.\n\n# CHAPTER 88. Schools and Schoolmasters.\n\nThe previous chapter gave account of an immense body or herd of Sperm Whales, and there was also then given the probable cause inducing those vast aggregations.\n\nNow, though such great bodies are at times encountered, yet, as must have been seen, even at the present day, small detached bands are occasionally observed, embracing from twenty to fifty individuals each. Such bands are known as schools. They generally are of two sorts; those composed almost entirely of females, and those mustering none but young vigorous males, or bulls, as they are familiarly designated.\n\nIn cavalier attendance upon the school of females, you invariably see a male of full grown magnitude, but not old; who, upon any alarm, evinces his gallantry by falling in the rear and covering the flight of his ladies. In truth, this gentleman is a luxurious Ottoman, swimming about over the watery world, surroundingly accompanied by all the solaces and endearments of the harem. The contrast between this Ottoman and his concubines is striking; because, while he is always of the largest leviathanic proportions, the ladies, even at full growth, are not more than one-third of the bulk of an average-sized male. They are comparatively delicate, indeed; I dare say, not to exceed half a dozen yards round the waist. Nevertheless, it cannot be denied, that upon the whole they are hereditarily entitled to en bon point.\n\nIt is very curious to watch this harem and its lord in their indolent ramblings. Like fashionables, they are for ever on the move in leisurely search of variety. You meet them on the Line in time for the full flower of the Equatorial feeding season, having just returned, perhaps, from spending the summer in the Northern seas, and so cheating summer of all unpleasant weariness and warmth. By the time they have lounged up and down the promenade of the Equator awhile, they start for the Oriental waters in anticipation of the cool season there, and so evade the other excessive temperature of the year.\n\nWhen serenely advancing on one of these journeys, if any strange suspicious sights are seen, my lord whale keeps a wary eye on his interesting family. Should any unwarrantably pert young Leviathan coming that way, presume to draw confidentially close to one of the ladies, with what prodigious fury the Bashaw assails him, and chases him away! High times, indeed, if unprincipled young rakes like him are to be permitted to invade the sanctity of domestic bliss; though do what the Bashaw will, he cannot keep the most notorious Lothario out of his bed; for, alas! all fish bed in common. As ashore, the ladies often cause the most terrible duels among their rival admirers; just so with the whales, who sometimes come to deadly battle, and all for love. They fence with their long lower jaws, sometimes locking them together, and so striving for the supremacy like elks that warringly interweave their antlers. Not a few are captured having the deep scars of these encounters,—furrowed heads, broken teeth, scolloped fins; and in some instances, wrenched and dislocated mouths.\n\nBut supposing the invader of domestic bliss to betake himself away at the first rush of the harem’s lord, then is it very diverting to watch that lord. Gently he insinuates his vast bulk among them again and revels there awhile, still in tantalizing vicinity to young Lothario, like pious Solomon devoutly worshipping among his thousand concubines. Granting other whales to be in sight, the fishermen will seldom give chase to one of these Grand Turks; for these Grand Turks are too lavish of their strength, and hence their unctuousness is small. As for the sons and the daughters they beget, why, those sons and daughters must take care of themselves; at least, with only the maternal help. For like certain other omnivorous roving lovers that might be named, my Lord Whale has no taste for the nursery, however much for the bower; and so, being a great traveller, he leaves his anonymous babies all over the world; every baby an exotic. In good time, nevertheless, as the ardour of youth declines; as years and dumps increase; as reflection lends her solemn pauses; in short, as a general lassitude overtakes the sated Turk; then a love of ease and virtue supplants the love for maidens; our Ottoman enters upon the impotent, repentant, admonitory stage of life, forswears, disbands the harem, and grown to an exemplary, sulky old soul, goes about all alone among the meridians and parallels saying his prayers, and warning each young Leviathan from his amorous errors.\n\nNow, as the harem of whales is called by the fishermen a school, so is the lord and master of that school technically known as the schoolmaster. It is therefore not in strict character, however admirably satirical, that after going to school himself, he should then go abroad inculcating not what he learned there, but the folly of it. His title, schoolmaster, would very naturally seem derived from the name bestowed upon the harem itself, but some have surmised that the man who first thus entitled this sort of Ottoman whale, must have read the memoirs of Vidocq, and informed himself what sort of a country-schoolmaster that famous Frenchman was in his younger days, and what was the nature of those occult lessons he inculcated into some of his pupils.\n\nThe same secludedness and isolation to which the schoolmaster whale betakes himself in his advancing years, is true of all aged Sperm Whales. Almost universally, a lone whale—as a solitary Leviathan is called—proves an ancient one. Like venerable moss-bearded Daniel Boone, he will have no one near him but Nature herself; and her he takes to wife in the wilderness of waters, and the best of wives she is, though she keeps so many moody secrets.\n\nThe schools composing none but young and vigorous males, previously mentioned, offer a strong contrast to the harem schools. For while those female whales are characteristically timid, the young males, or forty-barrel-bulls, as they call them, are by far the most pugnacious of all Leviathans, and proverbially the most dangerous to encounter; excepting those wondrous grey-headed, grizzled whales, sometimes met, and these will fight you like grim fiends exasperated by a penal gout.\n\nThe Forty-barrel-bull schools are larger than the harem schools. Like a mob of young collegians, they are full of fight, fun, and wickedness, tumbling round the world at such a reckless, rollicking rate, that no prudent underwriter would insure them any more than he would a riotous lad at Yale or Harvard. They soon relinquish this turbulence though, and when about three-fourths grown, break up, and separately go about in quest of settlements, that is, harems.\n\nAnother point of difference between the male and female schools is still more characteristic of the sexes. Say you strike a Forty-barrel-bull—poor devil! all his comrades quit him. But strike a member of the harem school, and her companions swim around her with every token of concern, sometimes lingering so near her and so long, as themselves to fall a prey.\n\n# CHAPTER 89. Fast-Fish and Loose-Fish.\n\nThe allusion to the waif and waif-poles in the last chapter but one, necessitates some account of the laws and regulations of the whale fishery, of which the waif may be deemed the grand symbol and badge.\n\nIt frequently happens that when several ships are cruising in company, a whale may be struck by one vessel, then escape, and be finally killed and captured by another vessel; and herein are indirectly comprised many minor contingencies, all partaking of this one grand feature. For example,—after a weary and perilous chase and capture of a whale, the body may get loose from the ship by reason of a violent storm; and drifting far away to leeward, be retaken by a second whaler, who, in a calm, snugly tows it alongside, without risk of life or line. Thus the most vexatious and violent disputes would often arise between the fishermen, were there not some written or unwritten, universal, undisputed law applicable to all cases.\n\nPerhaps the only formal whaling code authorized by legislative enactment, was that of Holland. It was decreed by the States-General in A.D. 1695. But though no other nation has ever had any written whaling law, yet the American fishermen have been their own legislators and lawyers in this matter. They have provided a system which for terse comprehensiveness surpasses Justinian’s Pandects and the By-laws of the Chinese Society for the Suppression of Meddling with other People’s Business. Yes; these laws might be engraven on a Queen Anne’s farthing, or the barb of a harpoon, and worn round the neck, so small are they.\n\nI. A Fast-Fish belongs to the party fast to it.\n\nII. A Loose-Fish is fair game for anybody who can soonest catch it.\n\nBut what plays the mischief with this masterly code is the admirable brevity of it, which necessitates a vast volume of commentaries to expound it.\n\nFirst: What is a Fast-Fish? Alive or dead a fish is technically fast, when it is connected with an occupied ship or boat, by any medium at all controllable by the occupant or occupants,—a mast, an oar, a nine-inch cable, a telegraph wire, or a strand of cobweb, it is all the same. Likewise a fish is technically fast when it bears a waif, or any other recognised symbol of possession; so long as the party waifing it plainly evince their ability at any time to take it alongside, as well as their intention so to do.\n\nThese are scientific commentaries; but the commentaries of the whalemen themselves sometimes consist in hard words and harder knocks—the Coke-upon-Littleton of the fist. True, among the more upright and honorable whalemen allowances are always made for peculiar cases, where it would be an outrageous moral injustice for one party to claim possession of a whale previously chased or killed by another party. But others are by no means so scrupulous.\n\nSome fifty years ago there was a curious case of whale-trover litigated in England, wherein the plaintiffs set forth that after a hard chase of a whale in the Northern seas; and when indeed they (the plaintiffs) had succeeded in harpooning the fish; they were at last, through peril of their lives, obliged to forsake not only their lines, but their boat itself. Ultimately the defendants (the crew of another ship) came up with the whale, struck, killed, seized, and finally appropriated it before the very eyes of the plaintiffs. And when those defendants were remonstrated with, their captain snapped his fingers in the plaintiffs’ teeth, and assured them that by way of doxology to the deed he had done, he would now retain their line, harpoons, and boat, which had remained attached to the whale at the time of the seizure. Wherefore the plaintiffs now sued for the recovery of the value of their whale, line, harpoons, and boat.\n\nMr. Erskine was counsel for the defendants; Lord Ellenborough was the judge. In the course of the defence, the witty Erskine went on to illustrate his position, by alluding to a recent crim. con. case, wherein a gentleman, after in vain trying to bridle his wife’s viciousness, had at last abandoned her upon the seas of life; but in the course of years, repenting of that step, he instituted an action to recover possession of her. Erskine was on the other side; and he then supported it by saying, that though the gentleman had originally harpooned the lady, and had once had her fast, and only by reason of the great stress of her plunging viciousness, had at last abandoned her; yet abandon her he did, so that she became a loose-fish; and therefore when a subsequent gentleman re-harpooned her, the lady then became that subsequent gentleman’s property, along with whatever harpoon might have been found sticking in her.\n\nNow in the present case Erskine contended that the examples of the whale and the lady were reciprocally illustrative of each other.\n\nThese pleadings, and the counter pleadings, being duly heard, the very learned judge in set terms decided, to wit,—That as for the boat, he awarded it to the plaintiffs, because they had merely abandoned it to save their lives; but that with regard to the controverted whale, harpoons, and line, they belonged to the defendants; the whale, because it was a Loose-Fish at the time of the final capture; and the harpoons and line because when the fish made off with them, it (the fish) acquired a property in those articles; and hence anybody who afterwards took the fish had a right to them. Now the defendants afterwards took the fish; ergo, the aforesaid articles were theirs.\n\nA common man looking at this decision of the very learned Judge, might possibly object to it. But ploughed up to the primary rock of the matter, the two great principles laid down in the twin whaling laws previously quoted, and applied and elucidated by Lord Ellenborough in the above cited case; these two laws touching Fast-Fish and Loose-Fish, I say, will, on reflection, be found the fundamentals of all human jurisprudence; for notwithstanding its complicated tracery of sculpture, the Temple of the Law, like the Temple of the Philistines, has but two props to stand on.\n\nIs it not a saying in every one’s mouth, Possession is half of the law: that is, regardless of how the thing came into possession? But often possession is the whole of the law. What are the sinews and souls of Russian serfs and Republican slaves but Fast-Fish, whereof possession is the whole of the law? What to the rapacious landlord is the widow’s last mite but a Fast-Fish? What is yonder undetected villain’s marble mansion with a door-plate for a waif; what is that but a Fast-Fish? What is the ruinous discount which Mordecai, the broker, gets from poor Woebegone, the bankrupt, on a loan to keep Woebegone’s family from starvation; what is that ruinous discount but a Fast-Fish? What is the Archbishop of Savesoul’s income of £100,000 seized from the scant bread and cheese of hundreds of thousands of broken-backed laborers (all sure of heaven without any of Savesoul’s help) what is that globular £100,000 but a Fast-Fish? What are the Duke of Dunder’s hereditary towns and hamlets but Fast-Fish? What to that redoubted harpooneer, John Bull, is poor Ireland, but a Fast-Fish? What to that apostolic lancer, Brother Jonathan, is Texas but a Fast-Fish? And concerning all these, is not Possession the whole of the law?\n\nBut if the doctrine of Fast-Fish be pretty generally applicable, the kindred doctrine of Loose-Fish is still more widely so. That is internationally and universally applicable.\n\nWhat was America in 1492 but a Loose-Fish, in which Columbus struck the Spanish standard by way of waifing it for his royal master and mistress? What was Poland to the Czar? What Greece to the Turk? What India to England? What at last will Mexico be to the United States? All Loose-Fish.\n\nWhat are the Rights of Man and the Liberties of the World but Loose-Fish? What all men’s minds and opinions but Loose-Fish? What is the principle of religious belief in them but a Loose-Fish? What to the ostentatious smuggling verbalists are the thoughts of thinkers but Loose-Fish? What is the great globe itself but a Loose-Fish? And what are you, reader, but a Loose-Fish and a Fast-Fish, too?\n\n# CHAPTER 90. Heads or Tails.\n\n“De balena vero sufficit, si rex habeat caput, et regina caudam.” Bracton, l. 3, c. 3.\n\nLatin from the books of the Laws of England, which taken along with the context, means, that of all whales captured by anybody on the coast of that land, the King, as Honorary Grand Harpooneer, must have the head, and the Queen be respectfully presented with the tail. A division which, in the whale, is much like halving an apple; there is no intermediate remainder. Now as this law, under a modified form, is to this day in force in England; and as it offers in various respects a strange anomaly touching the general law of Fast and Loose-Fish, it is here treated of in a separate chapter, on the same courteous principle that prompts the English railways to be at the expense of a separate car, specially reserved for the accommodation of royalty. In the first place, in curious proof of the fact that the above-mentioned law is still in force, I proceed to lay before you a circumstance that happened within the last two years.\n\nIt seems that some honest mariners of Dover, or Sandwich, or some one of the Cinque Ports, had after a hard chase succeeded in killing and beaching a fine whale which they had originally descried afar off from the shore. Now the Cinque Ports are partially or somehow under the jurisdiction of a sort of policeman or beadle, called a Lord Warden. Holding the office directly from the crown, I believe, all the royal emoluments incident to the Cinque Port territories become by assignment his. By some writers this office is called a sinecure. But not so. Because the Lord Warden is busily employed at times in fobbing his perquisites; which are his chiefly by virtue of that same fobbing of them.\n\nNow when these poor sun-burnt mariners, bare-footed, and with their trowsers rolled high up on their eely legs, had wearily hauled their fat fish high and dry, promising themselves a good £150 from the precious oil and bone; and in fantasy sipping rare tea with their wives, and good ale with their cronies, upon the strength of their respective shares; up steps a very learned and most Christian and charitable gentleman, with a copy of Blackstone under his arm; and laying it upon the whale’s head, he says—“Hands off! this fish, my masters, is a Fast-Fish. I seize it as the Lord Warden’s.” Upon this the poor mariners in their respectful consternation—so truly English—knowing not what to say, fall to vigorously scratching their heads all round; meanwhile ruefully glancing from the whale to the stranger. But that did in nowise mend the matter, or at all soften the hard heart of the learned gentleman with the copy of Blackstone. At length one of them, after long scratching about for his ideas, made bold to speak,\n\n“Please, sir, who is the Lord Warden?”\n\n“The Duke.”\n\n“But the duke had nothing to do with taking this fish?”\n“It is his.”\n\n“We have been at great trouble, and peril, and some expense, and is all that to go to the Duke’s benefit; we getting nothing at all for our pains but our blisters?”\n\n“It is his.”\n\n“Is the Duke so very poor as to be forced to this desperate mode of getting a livelihood?”\n\n“It is his.”\n\n“I thought to relieve my old bed-ridden mother by part of my share of this whale.”\n\n“It is his.”\n\n“Won’t the Duke be content with a quarter or a half?”\n\n“It is his.”\n\nIn a word, the whale was seized and sold, and his Grace the Duke of Wellington received the money. Thinking that viewed in some particular lights, the case might by a bare possibility in some small degree be deemed, under the circumstances, a rather hard one, an honest clergyman of the town respectfully addressed a note to his Grace, begging him to take the case of those unfortunate mariners into full consideration. To which my Lord Duke in substance replied (both letters were published) that he had already done so, and received the money, and would be obliged to the reverend gentleman if for the future he (the reverend gentleman) would decline meddling with other people’s business. Is this the still militant old man, standing at the corners of the three kingdoms, on all hands coercing alms of beggars?\n\nIt will readily be seen that in this case the alleged right of the Duke to the whale was a delegated one from the Sovereign. We must needs inquire then on what principle the Sovereign is originally invested with that right. The law itself has already been set forth. But Plowdon gives us the reason for it. Says Plowdon, the whale so caught belongs to the King and Queen, “because of its superior excellence.” And by the soundest commentators this has ever been held a cogent argument in such matters.\n\nBut why should the King have the head, and the Queen the tail? A reason for that, ye lawyers!\n\nIn his treatise on “Queen-Gold,” or Queen-pinmoney, an old King’s Bench author, one William Prynne, thus discourseth: “Ye tail is ye Queen’s, that ye Queen’s wardrobe may be supplied with ye whalebone.” Now this was written at a time when the black limber bone of the Greenland or Right whale was largely used in ladies’ bodices. But this same bone is not in the tail; it is in the head, which is a sad mistake for a sagacious lawyer like Prynne. But is the Queen a mermaid, to be presented with a tail? An allegorical meaning may lurk here.\n\nThere are two royal fish so styled by the English law writers—the whale and the sturgeon; both royal property under certain limitations, and nominally supplying the tenth branch of the crown’s ordinary revenue. I know not that any other author has hinted of the matter; but by inference it seems to me that the sturgeon must be divided in the same way as the whale, the King receiving the highly dense and elastic head peculiar to that fish, which, symbolically regarded, may possibly be humorously grounded upon some presumed congeniality. And thus there seems a reason in all things, even in law.\n\n# CHAPTER 91. The Pequod Meets The Rose-Bud.\n\n“In vain it was to rake for Ambergriese in the paunch of this Leviathan, insufferable fetor denying not inquiry.” Sir T. Browne, V.E.\n\nIt was a week or two after the last whaling scene recounted, and when we were slowly sailing over a sleepy, vapory, mid-day sea, that the many noses on the Pequod’s deck proved more vigilant discoverers than the three pairs of eyes aloft. A peculiar and not very pleasant smell was smelt in the sea.\n\n“I will bet something now,” said Stubb, “that somewhere hereabouts are some of those drugged whales we tickled the other day. I thought they would keel up before long.”\n\nPresently, the vapors in advance slid aside; and there in the distance lay a ship, whose furled sails betokened that some sort of whale must be alongside. As we glided nearer, the stranger showed French colours from his peak; and by the eddying cloud of vulture sea-fowl that circled, and hovered, and swooped around him, it was plain that the whale alongside must be what the fishermen call a blasted whale, that is, a whale that has died unmolested on the sea, and so floated an unappropriated corpse. It may well be conceived, what an unsavory odor such a mass must exhale; worse than an Assyrian city in the plague, when the living are incompetent to bury the departed. So intolerable indeed is it regarded by some, that no cupidity could persuade them to moor alongside of it. Yet are there those who will still do it; notwithstanding the fact that the oil obtained from such subjects is of a very inferior quality, and by no means of the nature of attar-of-rose.\n\nComing still nearer with the expiring breeze, we saw that the Frenchman had a second whale alongside; and this second whale seemed even more of a nosegay than the first. In truth, it turned out to be one of those problematical whales that seem to dry up and die with a sort of prodigious dyspepsia, or indigestion; leaving their defunct bodies almost entirely bankrupt of anything like oil. Nevertheless, in the proper place we shall see that no knowing fisherman will ever turn up his nose at such a whale as this, however much he may shun blasted whales in general.\n\nThe Pequod had now swept so nigh to the stranger, that Stubb vowed he recognised his cutting spade-pole entangled in the lines that were knotted round the tail of one of these whales.\n\n“There’s a pretty fellow, now,” he banteringly laughed, standing in the ship’s bows, “there’s a jackal for ye! I well know that these Crappoes of Frenchmen are but poor devils in the fishery; sometimes lowering their boats for breakers, mistaking them for Sperm Whale spouts; yes, and sometimes sailing from their port with their hold full of boxes of tallow candles, and cases of snuffers, foreseeing that all the oil they will get won’t be enough to dip the Captain’s wick into; aye, we all know these things; but look ye, here’s a Crappo that is content with our leavings, the drugged whale there, I mean; aye, and is content too with scraping the dry bones of that other precious fish he has there. Poor devil! I say, pass round a hat, some one, and let’s make him a present of a little oil for dear charity’s sake. For what oil he’ll get from that drugged whale there, wouldn’t be fit to burn in a jail; no, not in a condemned cell. And as for the other whale, why, I’ll agree to get more oil by chopping up and trying out these three masts of ours, than he’ll get from that bundle of bones; though, now that I think of it, it may contain something worth a good deal more than oil; yes, ambergris. I wonder now if our old man has thought of that. It’s worth trying. Yes, I’m for it;” and so saying he started for the quarter-deck.\n\nBy this time the faint air had become a complete calm; so that whether or no, the Pequod was now fairly entrapped in the smell, with no hope of escaping except by its breezing up again. Issuing from the cabin, Stubb now called his boat’s crew, and pulled off for the stranger. Drawing across her bow, he perceived that in accordance with the fanciful French taste, the upper part of her stem-piece was carved in the likeness of a huge drooping stalk, was painted green, and for thorns had copper spikes projecting from it here and there; the whole terminating in a symmetrical folded bulb of a bright red colour. Upon her head boards, in large gilt letters, he read “Bouton de Rose,”—Rose-button, or Rose-bud; and this was the romantic name of this aromatic ship.\n\nThough Stubb did not understand the Bouton part of the inscription, yet the word rose, and the bulbous figure-head put together, sufficiently explained the whole to him.\n\n“A wooden rose-bud, eh?” he cried with his hand to his nose, “that will do very well; but how like all creation it smells!”\n\nNow in order to hold direct communication with the people on deck, he had to pull round the bows to the starboard side, and thus come close to the blasted whale; and so talk over it.\n\nArrived then at this spot, with one hand still to his nose, he bawled—“Bouton-de-Rose, ahoy! are there any of you Bouton-de-Roses that speak English?”\n\n“Yes,” rejoined a Guernsey-man from the bulwarks, who turned out to be the chief-mate.\n\n“Well, then, my Bouton-de-Rose-bud, have you seen the White Whale?”\n\n“What whale?”\n\n“The White Whale—a Sperm Whale—Moby Dick, have ye seen him?\n\n“Never heard of such a whale. Cachalot Blanche! White Whale—no.”\n\n“Very good, then; good bye now, and I’ll call again in a minute.”\n\nThen rapidly pulling back towards the Pequod, and seeing Ahab leaning over the quarter-deck rail awaiting his report, he moulded his two hands into a trumpet and shouted—“No, Sir! No!” Upon which Ahab retired, and Stubb returned to the Frenchman.\n\nHe now perceived that the Guernsey-man, who had just got into the chains, and was using a cutting-spade, had slung his nose in a sort of bag.\n\n“What’s the matter with your nose, there?” said Stubb. “Broke it?”\n\n“I wish it was broken, or that I didn’t have any nose at all!” answered the Guernsey-man, who did not seem to relish the job he was at very much. “But what are you holding yours for?”\n\n“Oh, nothing! It’s a wax nose; I have to hold it on. Fine day, ain’t it? Air rather gardenny, I should say; throw us a bunch of posies, will ye, Bouton-de-Rose?”\n\n“What in the devil’s name do you want here?” roared the Guernseyman, flying into a sudden passion.\n\n“Oh! keep cool—cool? yes, that’s the word! why don’t you pack those whales in ice while you’re working at ’em? But joking aside, though; do you know, Rose-bud, that it’s all nonsense trying to get any oil out of such whales? As for that dried up one, there, he hasn’t a gill in his whole carcase.”\n\n“I know that well enough; but, d’ye see, the Captain here won’t believe it; this is his first voyage; he was a Cologne manufacturer before. But come aboard, and mayhap he’ll believe you, if he won’t me; and so I’ll get out of this dirty scrape.”\n\n“Anything to oblige ye, my sweet and pleasant fellow,” rejoined Stubb, and with that he soon mounted to the deck. There a queer scene presented itself. The sailors, in tasselled caps of red worsted, were getting the heavy tackles in readiness for the whales. But they worked rather slow and talked very fast, and seemed in anything but a good humor. All their noses upwardly projected from their faces like so many jib-booms. Now and then pairs of them would drop their work, and run up to the mast-head to get some fresh air. Some thinking they would catch the plague, dipped oakum in coal-tar, and at intervals held it to their nostrils. Others having broken the stems of their pipes almost short off at the bowl, were vigorously puffing tobacco-smoke, so that it constantly filled their olfactories.\n\nStubb was struck by a shower of outcries and anathemas proceeding from the Captain’s round-house abaft; and looking in that direction saw a fiery face thrust from behind the door, which was held ajar from within. This was the tormented surgeon, who, after in vain remonstrating against the proceedings of the day, had betaken himself to the Captain’s round-house (cabinet he called it) to avoid the pest; but still, could not help yelling out his entreaties and indignations at times.\n\nMarking all this, Stubb argued well for his scheme, and turning to the Guernsey-man had a little chat with him, during which the stranger mate expressed his detestation of his Captain as a conceited ignoramus, who had brought them all into so unsavory and unprofitable a pickle. Sounding him carefully, Stubb further perceived that the Guernsey-man had not the slightest suspicion concerning the ambergris. He therefore held his peace on that head, but otherwise was quite frank and confidential with him, so that the two quickly concocted a little plan for both circumventing and satirizing the Captain, without his at all dreaming of distrusting their sincerity. According to this little plan of theirs, the Guernsey-man, under cover of an interpreter’s office, was to tell the Captain what he pleased, but as coming from Stubb; and as for Stubb, he was to utter any nonsense that should come uppermost in him during the interview.\n\nBy this time their destined victim appeared from his cabin. He was a small and dark, but rather delicate looking man for a sea-captain, with large whiskers and moustache, however; and wore a red cotton velvet vest with watch-seals at his side. To this gentleman, Stubb was now politely introduced by the Guernsey-man, who at once ostentatiously put on the aspect of interpreting between them.\n\n“What shall I say to him first?” said he.\n\n“Why,” said Stubb, eyeing the velvet vest and the watch and seals, “you may as well begin by telling him that he looks a sort of babyish to me, though I don’t pretend to be a judge.”\n\n“He says, Monsieur,” said the Guernsey-man, in French, turning to his captain, “that only yesterday his ship spoke a vessel, whose captain and chief-mate, with six sailors, had all died of a fever caught from a blasted whale they had brought alongside.”\n\nUpon this the captain started, and eagerly desired to know more.\n\n“What now?” said the Guernsey-man to Stubb.\n\n“Why, since he takes it so easy, tell him that now I have eyed him carefully, I’m quite certain that he’s no more fit to command a whale-ship than a St. Jago monkey. In fact, tell him from me he’s a baboon.”\n\n“He vows and declares, Monsieur, that the other whale, the dried one, is far more deadly than the blasted one; in fine, Monsieur, he conjures us, as we value our lives, to cut loose from these fish.”\n\nInstantly the captain ran forward, and in a loud voice commanded his crew to desist from hoisting the cutting-tackles, and at once cast loose the cables and chains confining the whales to the ship.\n\n“What now?” said the Guernsey-man, when the Captain had returned to them.\n\n“Why, let me see; yes, you may as well tell him now that—that—in fact, tell him I’ve diddled him, and (aside to himself) perhaps somebody else.”\n\n“He says, Monsieur, that he’s very happy to have been of any service to us.”\n\nHearing this, the captain vowed that they were the grateful parties (meaning himself and mate) and concluded by inviting Stubb down into his cabin to drink a bottle of Bordeaux.\n\n“He wants you to take a glass of wine with him,” said the interpreter.\n\n“Thank him heartily; but tell him it’s against my principles to drink with the man I’ve diddled. In fact, tell him I must go.”\n\n“He says, Monsieur, that his principles won’t admit of his drinking; but that if Monsieur wants to live another day to drink, then Monsieur had best drop all four boats, and pull the ship away from these whales, for it’s so calm they won’t drift.”\n\nBy this time Stubb was over the side, and getting into his boat, hailed the Guernsey-man to this effect,—that having a long tow-line in his boat, he would do what he could to help them, by pulling out the lighter whale of the two from the ship’s side. While the Frenchman’s boats, then, were engaged in towing the ship one way, Stubb benevolently towed away at his whale the other way, ostentatiously slacking out a most unusually long tow-line.\n\nPresently a breeze sprang up; Stubb feigned to cast off from the whale; hoisting his boats, the Frenchman soon increased his distance, while the Pequod slid in between him and Stubb’s whale. Whereupon Stubb quickly pulled to the floating body, and hailing the Pequod to give notice of his intentions, at once proceeded to reap the fruit of his unrighteous cunning. Seizing his sharp boat-spade, he commenced an excavation in the body, a little behind the side fin. You would almost have thought he was digging a cellar there in the sea; and when at length his spade struck against the gaunt ribs, it was like turning up old Roman tiles and pottery buried in fat English loam. His boat’s crew were all in high excitement, eagerly helping their chief, and looking as anxious as gold-hunters.\n\nAnd all the time numberless fowls were diving, and ducking, and screaming, and yelling, and fighting around them. Stubb was beginning to look disappointed, especially as the horrible nosegay increased, when suddenly from out the very heart of this plague, there stole a faint stream of perfume, which flowed through the tide of bad smells without being absorbed by it, as one river will flow into and then along with another, without at all blending with it for a time.\n\n“I have it, I have it,” cried Stubb, with delight, striking something in the subterranean regions, “a purse! a purse!”\n\nDropping his spade, he thrust both hands in, and drew out handfuls of something that looked like ripe Windsor soap, or rich mottled old cheese; very unctuous and savory withal. You might easily dent it with your thumb; it is of a hue between yellow and ash colour. And this, good friends, is ambergris, worth a gold guinea an ounce to any druggist. Some six handfuls were obtained; but more was unavoidably lost in the sea, and still more, perhaps, might have been secured were it not for impatient Ahab’s loud command to Stubb to desist, and come on board, else the ship would bid them good bye.\n\n# CHAPTER 92. Ambergris.\n\nNow this ambergris is a very curious substance, and so important as an article of commerce, that in 1791 a certain Nantucket-born Captain Coffin was examined at the bar of the English House of Commons on that subject. For at that time, and indeed until a comparatively late day, the precise origin of ambergris remained, like amber itself, a problem to the learned. Though the word ambergris is but the French compound for grey amber, yet the two substances are quite distinct. For amber, though at times found on the sea-coast, is also dug up in some far inland soils, whereas ambergris is never found except upon the sea. Besides, amber is a hard, transparent, brittle, odorless substance, used for mouth-pieces to pipes, for beads and ornaments; but ambergris is soft, waxy, and so highly fragrant and spicy, that it is largely used in perfumery, in pastiles, precious candles, hair-powders, and pomatum. The Turks use it in cooking, and also carry it to Mecca, for the same purpose that frankincense is carried to St. Peter’s in Rome. Some wine merchants drop a few grains into claret, to flavor it.\n\nWho would think, then, that such fine ladies and gentlemen should regale themselves with an essence found in the inglorious bowels of a sick whale! Yet so it is. By some, ambergris is supposed to be the cause, and by others the effect, of the dyspepsia in the whale. How to cure such a dyspepsia it were hard to say, unless by administering three or four boat loads of Brandreth’s pills, and then running out of harm’s way, as laborers do in blasting rocks.\n\nI have forgotten to say that there were found in this ambergris, certain hard, round, bony plates, which at first Stubb thought might be sailors’ trowsers buttons; but it afterwards turned out that they were nothing more than pieces of small squid bones embalmed in that manner.\n\nNow that the incorruption of this most fragrant ambergris should be found in the heart of such decay; is this nothing? Bethink thee of that saying of St. Paul in Corinthians, about corruption and incorruption; how that we are sown in dishonor, but raised in glory. And likewise call to mind that saying of Paracelsus about what it is that maketh the best musk. Also forget not the strange fact that of all things of ill-savor, Cologne-water, in its rudimental manufacturing stages, is the worst.\n\nI should like to conclude the chapter with the above appeal, but cannot, owing to my anxiety to repel a charge often made against whalemen, and which, in the estimation of some already biased minds, might be considered as indirectly substantiated by what has been said of the Frenchman’s two whales. Elsewhere in this volume the slanderous aspersion has been disproved, that the vocation of whaling is throughout a slatternly, untidy business. But there is another thing to rebut. They hint that all whales always smell bad. Now how did this odious stigma originate?\n\nI opine, that it is plainly traceable to the first arrival of the Greenland whaling ships in London, more than two centuries ago. Because those whalemen did not then, and do not now, try out their oil at sea as the Southern ships have always done; but cutting up the fresh blubber in small bits, thrust it through the bung holes of large casks, and carry it home in that manner; the shortness of the season in those Icy Seas, and the sudden and violent storms to which they are exposed, forbidding any other course. The consequence is, that upon breaking into the hold, and unloading one of these whale cemeteries, in the Greenland dock, a savor is given forth somewhat similar to that arising from excavating an old city grave-yard, for the foundations of a Lying-in Hospital.\n\nI partly surmise also, that this wicked charge against whalers may be likewise imputed to the existence on the coast of Greenland, in former times, of a Dutch village called Schmerenburgh or Smeerenberg, which latter name is the one used by the learned Fogo Von Slack, in his great work on Smells, a text-book on that subject. As its name imports (smeer, fat; berg, to put up), this village was founded in order to afford a place for the blubber of the Dutch whale fleet to be tried out, without being taken home to Holland for that purpose. It was a collection of furnaces, fat-kettles, and oil sheds; and when the works were in full operation certainly gave forth no very pleasant savor. But all this is quite different with a South Sea Sperm Whaler; which in a voyage of four years perhaps, after completely filling her hold with oil, does not, perhaps, consume fifty days in the business of boiling out; and in the state that it is casked, the oil is nearly scentless. The truth is, that living or dead, if but decently treated, whales as a species are by no means creatures of ill odor; nor can whalemen be recognised, as the people of the middle ages affected to detect a Jew in the company, by the nose. Nor indeed can the whale possibly be otherwise than fragrant, when, as a general thing, he enjoys such high health; taking abundance of exercise; always out of doors; though, it is true, seldom in the open air. I say, that the motion of a Sperm Whale’s flukes above water dispenses a perfume, as when a musk-scented lady rustles her dress in a warm parlor. What then shall I liken the Sperm Whale to for fragrance, considering his magnitude? Must it not be to that famous elephant, with jewelled tusks, and redolent with myrrh, which was led out of an Indian town to do honor to Alexander the Great?\n\n# CHAPTER 93. The Castaway.\n\nIt was but some few days after encountering the Frenchman, that a most significant event befell the most insignificant of the Pequod’s crew; an event most lamentable; and which ended in providing the sometimes madly merry and predestinated craft with a living and ever accompanying prophecy of whatever shattered sequel might prove her own.\n\nNow, in the whale ship, it is not every one that goes in the boats. Some few hands are reserved called ship-keepers, whose province it is to work the vessel while the boats are pursuing the whale. As a general thing, these ship-keepers are as hardy fellows as the men comprising the boats’ crews. But if there happen to be an unduly slender, clumsy, or timorous wight in the ship, that wight is certain to be made a ship-keeper. It was so in the Pequod with the little negro Pippin by nick-name, Pip by abbreviation. Poor Pip! ye have heard of him before; ye must remember his tambourine on that dramatic midnight, so gloomy-jolly.\n\nIn outer aspect, Pip and Dough-Boy made a match, like a black pony and a white one, of equal developments, though of dissimilar colour, driven in one eccentric span. But while hapless Dough-Boy was by nature dull and torpid in his intellects, Pip, though over tender-hearted, was at bottom very bright, with that pleasant, genial, jolly brightness peculiar to his tribe; a tribe, which ever enjoy all holidays and festivities with finer, freer relish than any other race. For blacks, the year’s calendar should show naught but three hundred and sixty-five Fourth of Julys and New Year’s Days. Nor smile so, while I write that this little black was brilliant, for even blackness has its brilliancy; behold yon lustrous ebony, panelled in king’s cabinets. But Pip loved life, and all life’s peaceable securities; so that the panic-striking business in which he had somehow unaccountably become entrapped, had most sadly blurred his brightness; though, as ere long will be seen, what was thus temporarily subdued in him, in the end was destined to be luridly illumined by strange wild fires, that fictitiously showed him off to ten times the natural lustre with which in his native Tolland County in Connecticut, he had once enlivened many a fiddler’s frolic on the green; and at melodious even-tide, with his gay ha-ha! had turned the round horizon into one star-belled tambourine. So, though in the clear air of day, suspended against a blue-veined neck, the pure-watered diamond drop will healthful glow; yet, when the cunning jeweller would show you the diamond in its most impressive lustre, he lays it against a gloomy ground, and then lights it up, not by the sun, but by some unnatural gases. Then come out those fiery effulgences, infernally superb; then the evil-blazing diamond, once the divinest symbol of the crystal skies, looks like some crown-jewel stolen from the King of Hell. But let us to the story.\n\nIt came to pass, that in the ambergris affair Stubb’s after-oarsman chanced so to sprain his hand, as for a time to become quite maimed; and, temporarily, Pip was put into his place.\n\nThe first time Stubb lowered with him, Pip evinced much nervousness; but happily, for that time, escaped close contact with the whale; and therefore came off not altogether discreditably; though Stubb observing him, took care, afterwards, to exhort him to cherish his courageousness to the utmost, for he might often find it needful.\n\nNow upon the second lowering, the boat paddled upon the whale; and as the fish received the darted iron, it gave its customary rap, which happened, in this instance, to be right under poor Pip’s seat. The involuntary consternation of the moment caused him to leap, paddle in hand, out of the boat; and in such a way, that part of the slack whale line coming against his chest, he breasted it overboard with him, so as to become entangled in it, when at last plumping into the water. That instant the stricken whale started on a fierce run, the line swiftly straightened; and presto! poor Pip came all foaming up to the chocks of the boat, remorselessly dragged there by the line, which had taken several turns around his chest and neck.\n\nTashtego stood in the bows. He was full of the fire of the hunt. He hated Pip for a poltroon. Snatching the boat-knife from its sheath, he suspended its sharp edge over the line, and turning towards Stubb, exclaimed interrogatively, “Cut?” Meantime Pip’s blue, choked face plainly looked, Do, for God’s sake! All passed in a flash. In less than half a minute, this entire thing happened.\n\n“Damn him, cut!” roared Stubb; and so the whale was lost and Pip was saved.\n\nSo soon as he recovered himself, the poor little negro was assailed by yells and execrations from the crew. Tranquilly permitting these irregular cursings to evaporate, Stubb then in a plain, business-like, but still half humorous manner, cursed Pip officially; and that done, unofficially gave him much wholesome advice. The substance was, Never jump from a boat, Pip, except—but all the rest was indefinite, as the soundest advice ever is. Now, in general, Stick to the boat, is your true motto in whaling; but cases will sometimes happen when Leap from the boat, is still better. Moreover, as if perceiving at last that if he should give undiluted conscientious advice to Pip, he would be leaving him too wide a margin to jump in for the future; Stubb suddenly dropped all advice, and concluded with a peremptory command, “Stick to the boat, Pip, or by the Lord, I won’t pick you up if you jump; mind that. We can’t afford to lose whales by the likes of you; a whale would sell for thirty times what you would, Pip, in Alabama. Bear that in mind, and don’t jump any more.” Hereby perhaps Stubb indirectly hinted, that though man loved his fellow, yet man is a money-making animal, which propensity too often interferes with his benevolence.\n\nBut we are all in the hands of the Gods; and Pip jumped again. It was under very similar circumstances to the first performance; but this time he did not breast out the line; and hence, when the whale started to run, Pip was left behind on the sea, like a hurried traveller’s trunk. Alas! Stubb was but too true to his word. It was a beautiful, bounteous, blue day; the spangled sea calm and cool, and flatly stretching away, all round, to the horizon, like gold-beater’s skin hammered out to the extremest. Bobbing up and down in that sea, Pip’s ebon head showed like a head of cloves. No boat-knife was lifted when he fell so rapidly astern. Stubb’s inexorable back was turned upon him; and the whale was winged. In three minutes, a whole mile of shoreless ocean was between Pip and Stubb. Out from the centre of the sea, poor Pip turned his crisp, curling, black head to the sun, another lonely castaway, though the loftiest and the brightest.\n\nNow, in calm weather, to swim in the open ocean is as easy to the practised swimmer as to ride in a spring-carriage ashore. But the awful lonesomeness is intolerable. The intense concentration of self in the middle of such a heartless immensity, my God! who can tell it? Mark, how when sailors in a dead calm bathe in the open sea—mark how closely they hug their ship and only coast along her sides.\n\nBut had Stubb really abandoned the poor little negro to his fate? No; he did not mean to, at least. Because there were two boats in his wake, and he supposed, no doubt, that they would of course come up to Pip very quickly, and pick him up; though, indeed, such considerations towards oarsmen jeopardized through their own timidity, is not always manifested by the hunters in all similar instances; and such instances not unfrequently occur; almost invariably in the fishery, a coward, so called, is marked with the same ruthless detestation peculiar to military navies and armies.\n\nBut it so happened, that those boats, without seeing Pip, suddenly spying whales close to them on one side, turned, and gave chase; and Stubb’s boat was now so far away, and he and all his crew so intent upon his fish, that Pip’s ringed horizon began to expand around him miserably. By the merest chance the ship itself at last rescued him; but from that hour the little negro went about the deck an idiot; such, at least, they said he was. The sea had jeeringly kept his finite body up, but drowned the infinite of his soul. Not drowned entirely, though. Rather carried down alive to wondrous depths, where strange shapes of the unwarped primal world glided to and fro before his passive eyes; and the miser-merman, Wisdom, revealed his hoarded heaps; and among the joyous, heartless, ever-juvenile eternities, Pip saw the multitudinous, God-omnipresent, coral insects, that out of the firmament of waters heaved the colossal orbs. He saw God’s foot upon the treadle of the loom, and spoke it; and therefore his shipmates called him mad. So man’s insanity is heaven’s sense; and wandering from all mortal reason, man comes at last to that celestial thought, which, to reason, is absurd and frantic; and weal or woe, feels then uncompromised, indifferent as his God.\n\nFor the rest, blame not Stubb too hardly. The thing is common in that fishery; and in the sequel of the narrative, it will then be seen what like abandonment befell myself.\n\n# CHAPTER 94. A Squeeze of the Hand.\n\nThat whale of Stubb’s, so dearly purchased, was duly brought to the Pequod’s side, where all those cutting and hoisting operations previously detailed, were regularly gone through, even to the baling of the Heidelburgh Tun, or Case.\n\nWhile some were occupied with this latter duty, others were employed in dragging away the larger tubs, so soon as filled with the sperm; and when the proper time arrived, this same sperm was carefully manipulated ere going to the try-works, of which anon.\n\nIt had cooled and crystallized to such a degree, that when, with several others, I sat down before a large Constantine’s bath of it, I found it strangely concreted into lumps, here and there rolling about in the liquid part. It was our business to squeeze these lumps back into fluid. A sweet and unctuous duty! No wonder that in old times this sperm was such a favourite cosmetic. Such a clearer! such a sweetener! such a softener! such a delicious molifier! After having my hands in it for only a few minutes, my fingers felt like eels, and began, as it were, to serpentine and spiralise.\n\nAs I sat there at my ease, cross-legged on the deck; after the bitter exertion at the windlass; under a blue tranquil sky; the ship under indolent sail, and gliding so serenely along; as I bathed my hands among those soft, gentle globules of infiltrated tissues, woven almost within the hour; as they richly broke to my fingers, and discharged all their opulence, like fully ripe grapes their wine; as I snuffed up that uncontaminated aroma,—literally and truly, like the smell of spring violets; I declare to you, that for the time I lived as in a musky meadow; I forgot all about our horrible oath; in that inexpressible sperm, I washed my hands and my heart of it; I almost began to credit the old Paracelsan superstition that sperm is of rare virtue in allaying the heat of anger; while bathing in that bath, I felt divinely free from all ill-will, or petulance, or malice, of any sort whatsoever.\n\nSqueeze! squeeze! squeeze! all the morning long; I squeezed that sperm till I myself almost melted into it; I squeezed that sperm till a strange sort of insanity came over me; and I found myself unwittingly squeezing my co-laborers’ hands in it, mistaking their hands for the gentle globules. Such an abounding, affectionate, friendly, loving feeling did this avocation beget; that at last I was continually squeezing their hands, and looking up into their eyes sentimentally; as much as to say,—Oh! my dear fellow beings, why should we longer cherish any social acerbities, or know the slightest ill-humor or envy! Come; let us squeeze hands all round; nay, let us all squeeze ourselves into each other; let us squeeze ourselves universally into the very milk and sperm of kindness.\n\nWould that I could keep squeezing that sperm for ever! For now, since by many prolonged, repeated experiences, I have perceived that in all cases man must eventually lower, or at least shift, his conceit of attainable felicity; not placing it anywhere in the intellect or the fancy; but in the wife, the heart, the bed, the table, the saddle, the fireside, the country; now that I have perceived all this, I am ready to squeeze case eternally. In thoughts of the visions of the night, I saw long rows of angels in paradise, each with his hands in a jar of spermaceti.\n\nNow, while discoursing of sperm, it behooves to speak of other things akin to it, in the business of preparing the sperm whale for the try-works.\n\nFirst comes white-horse, so called, which is obtained from the tapering part of the fish, and also from the thicker portions of his flukes. It is tough with congealed tendons—a wad of muscle—but still contains some oil. After being severed from the whale, the white-horse is first cut into portable oblongs ere going to the mincer. They look much like blocks of Berkshire marble.\n\nPlum-pudding is the term bestowed upon certain fragmentary parts of the whale’s flesh, here and there adhering to the blanket of blubber, and often participating to a considerable degree in its unctuousness. It is a most refreshing, convivial, beautiful object to behold. As its name imports, it is of an exceedingly rich, mottled tint, with a bestreaked snowy and golden ground, dotted with spots of the deepest crimson and purple. It is plums of rubies, in pictures of citron. Spite of reason, it is hard to keep yourself from eating it. I confess, that once I stole behind the foremast to try it. It tasted something as I should conceive a royal cutlet from the thigh of Louis le Gros might have tasted, supposing him to have been killed the first day after the venison season, and that particular venison season contemporary with an unusually fine vintage of the vineyards of Champagne.\n\nThere is another substance, and a very singular one, which turns up in the course of this business, but which I feel it to be very puzzling adequately to describe. It is called slobgollion; an appellation original with the whalemen, and even so is the nature of the substance. It is an ineffably oozy, stringy affair, most frequently found in the tubs of sperm, after a prolonged squeezing, and subsequent decanting. I hold it to be the wondrously thin, ruptured membranes of the case, coalescing.\n\nGurry, so called, is a term properly belonging to right whalemen, but sometimes incidentally used by the sperm fishermen. It designates the dark, glutinous substance which is scraped off the back of the Greenland or right whale, and much of which covers the decks of those inferior souls who hunt that ignoble Leviathan.\n\nNippers. Strictly this word is not indigenous to the whale’s vocabulary. But as applied by whalemen, it becomes so. A whaleman’s nipper is a short firm strip of tendinous stuff cut from the tapering part of Leviathan’s tail: it averages an inch in thickness, and for the rest, is about the size of the iron part of a hoe. Edgewise moved along the oily deck, it operates like a leathern squilgee; and by nameless blandishments, as of magic, allures along with it all impurities.\n\nBut to learn all about these recondite matters, your best way is at once to descend into the blubber-room, and have a long talk with its inmates. This place has previously been mentioned as the receptacle for the blanket-pieces, when stript and hoisted from the whale. When the proper time arrives for cutting up its contents, this apartment is a scene of terror to all tyros, especially by night. On one side, lit by a dull lantern, a space has been left clear for the workmen. They generally go in pairs,—a pike-and-gaffman and a spade-man. The whaling-pike is similar to a frigate’s boarding-weapon of the same name. The gaff is something like a boat-hook. With his gaff, the gaffman hooks on to a sheet of blubber, and strives to hold it from slipping, as the ship pitches and lurches about. Meanwhile, the spade-man stands on the sheet itself, perpendicularly chopping it into the portable horse-pieces. This spade is sharp as hone can make it; the spademan’s feet are shoeless; the thing he stands on will sometimes irresistibly slide away from him, like a sledge. If he cuts off one of his own toes, or one of his assistants’, would you be very much astonished? Toes are scarce among veteran blubber-room men.\n\n# CHAPTER 95. The Cassock.\n\nHad you stepped on board the Pequod at a certain juncture of this post-mortemizing of the whale; and had you strolled forward nigh the windlass, pretty sure am I that you would have scanned with no small curiosity a very strange, enigmatical object, which you would have seen there, lying along lengthwise in the lee scuppers. Not the wondrous cistern in the whale’s huge head; not the prodigy of his unhinged lower jaw; not the miracle of his symmetrical tail; none of these would so surprise you, as half a glimpse of that unaccountable cone,—longer than a Kentuckian is tall, nigh a foot in diameter at the base, and jet-black as Yojo, the ebony idol of Queequeg. And an idol, indeed, it is; or, rather, in old times, its likeness was. Such an idol as that found in the secret groves of Queen Maachah in Judea; and for worshipping which, King Asa, her son, did depose her, and destroyed the idol, and burnt it for an abomination at the brook Kedron, as darkly set forth in the 15th chapter of the First Book of Kings.\n\nLook at the sailor, called the mincer, who now comes along, and assisted by two allies, heavily backs the grandissimus, as the mariners call it, and with bowed shoulders, staggers off with it as if he were a grenadier carrying a dead comrade from the field. Extending it upon the forecastle deck, he now proceeds cylindrically to remove its dark pelt, as an African hunter the pelt of a boa. This done he turns the pelt inside out, like a pantaloon leg; gives it a good stretching, so as almost to double its diameter; and at last hangs it, well spread, in the rigging, to dry. Ere long, it is taken down; when removing some three feet of it, towards the pointed extremity, and then cutting two slits for arm-holes at the other end, he lengthwise slips himself bodily into it. The mincer now stands before you invested in the full canonicals of his calling. Immemorial to all his order, this investiture alone will adequately protect him, while employed in the peculiar functions of his office.\n\nThat office consists in mincing the horse-pieces of blubber for the pots; an operation which is conducted at a curious wooden horse, planted endwise against the bulwarks, and with a capacious tub beneath it, into which the minced pieces drop, fast as the sheets from a rapt orator’s desk. Arrayed in decent black; occupying a conspicuous pulpit; intent on bible leaves; what a candidate for an archbishopric, what a lad for a Pope were this mincer!*\n\n*Bible leaves! Bible leaves! This is the invariable cry from the mates to the mincer. It enjoins him to be careful, and cut his work into as thin slices as possible, inasmuch as by so doing the business of boiling out the oil is much accelerated, and its quantity considerably increased, besides perhaps improving it in quality.\n\n# CHAPTER 96. The Try-Works.\n\nBesides her hoisted boats, an American whaler is outwardly distinguished by her try-works. She presents the curious anomaly of the most solid masonry joining with oak and hemp in constituting the completed ship. It is as if from the open field a brick-kiln were transported to her planks.\n\nThe try-works are planted between the foremast and mainmast, the most roomy part of the deck. The timbers beneath are of a peculiar strength, fitted to sustain the weight of an almost solid mass of brick and mortar, some ten feet by eight square, and five in height. The foundation does not penetrate the deck, but the masonry is firmly secured to the surface by ponderous knees of iron bracing it on all sides, and screwing it down to the timbers. On the flanks it is cased with wood, and at top completely covered by a large, sloping, battened hatchway. Removing this hatch we expose the great try-pots, two in number, and each of several barrels’ capacity. When not in use, they are kept remarkably clean. Sometimes they are polished with soapstone and sand, till they shine within like silver punch-bowls. During the night-watches some cynical old sailors will crawl into them and coil themselves away there for a nap. While employed in polishing them—one man in each pot, side by side—many confidential communications are carried on, over the iron lips. It is a place also for profound mathematical meditation. It was in the left hand try-pot of the Pequod, with the soapstone diligently circling round me, that I was first indirectly struck by the remarkable fact, that in geometry all bodies gliding along the cycloid, my soapstone for example, will descend from any point in precisely the same time.\n\nRemoving the fire-board from the front of the try-works, the bare masonry of that side is exposed, penetrated by the two iron mouths of the furnaces, directly underneath the pots. These mouths are fitted with heavy doors of iron. The intense heat of the fire is prevented from communicating itself to the deck, by means of a shallow reservoir extending under the entire inclosed surface of the works. By a tunnel inserted at the rear, this reservoir is kept replenished with water as fast as it evaporates. There are no external chimneys; they open direct from the rear wall. And here let us go back for a moment.\n\nIt was about nine o’clock at night that the Pequod’s try-works were first started on this present voyage. It belonged to Stubb to oversee the business.\n\n“All ready there? Off hatch, then, and start her. You cook, fire the works.” This was an easy thing, for the carpenter had been thrusting his shavings into the furnace throughout the passage. Here be it said that in a whaling voyage the first fire in the try-works has to be fed for a time with wood. After that no wood is used, except as a means of quick ignition to the staple fuel. In a word, after being tried out, the crisp, shrivelled blubber, now called scraps or fritters, still contains considerable of its unctuous properties. These fritters feed the flames. Like a plethoric burning martyr, or a self-consuming misanthrope, once ignited, the whale supplies his own fuel and burns by his own body. Would that he consumed his own smoke! for his smoke is horrible to inhale, and inhale it you must, and not only that, but you must live in it for the time. It has an unspeakable, wild, Hindoo odor about it, such as may lurk in the vicinity of funereal pyres. It smells like the left wing of the day of judgment; it is an argument for the pit.\n\nBy midnight the works were in full operation. We were clear from the carcase; sail had been made; the wind was freshening; the wild ocean darkness was intense. But that darkness was licked up by the fierce flames, which at intervals forked forth from the sooty flues, and illuminated every lofty rope in the rigging, as with the famed Greek fire. The burning ship drove on, as if remorselessly commissioned to some vengeful deed. So the pitch and sulphur-freighted brigs of the bold Hydriote, Canaris, issuing from their midnight harbors, with broad sheets of flame for sails, bore down upon the Turkish frigates, and folded them in conflagrations.\n\nThe hatch, removed from the top of the works, now afforded a wide hearth in front of them. Standing on this were the Tartarean shapes of the pagan harpooneers, always the whale-ship’s stokers. With huge pronged poles they pitched hissing masses of blubber into the scalding pots, or stirred up the fires beneath, till the snaky flames darted, curling, out of the doors to catch them by the feet. The smoke rolled away in sullen heaps. To every pitch of the ship there was a pitch of the boiling oil, which seemed all eagerness to leap into their faces. Opposite the mouth of the works, on the further side of the wide wooden hearth, was the windlass. This served for a sea-sofa. Here lounged the watch, when not otherwise employed, looking into the red heat of the fire, till their eyes felt scorched in their heads. Their tawny features, now all begrimed with smoke and sweat, their matted beards, and the contrasting barbaric brilliancy of their teeth, all these were strangely revealed in the capricious emblazonings of the works. As they narrated to each other their unholy adventures, their tales of terror told in words of mirth; as their uncivilized laughter forked upwards out of them, like the flames from the furnace; as to and fro, in their front, the harpooneers wildly gesticulated with their huge pronged forks and dippers; as the wind howled on, and the sea leaped, and the ship groaned and dived, and yet steadfastly shot her red hell further and further into the blackness of the sea and the night, and scornfully champed the white bone in her mouth, and viciously spat round her on all sides; then the rushing Pequod, freighted with savages, and laden with fire, and burning a corpse, and plunging into that blackness of darkness, seemed the material counterpart of her monomaniac commander’s soul.\n\nSo seemed it to me, as I stood at her helm, and for long hours silently guided the way of this fire-ship on the sea. Wrapped, for that interval, in darkness myself, I but the better saw the redness, the madness, the ghastliness of others. The continual sight of the fiend shapes before me, capering half in smoke and half in fire, these at last begat kindred visions in my soul, so soon as I began to yield to that unaccountable drowsiness which ever would come over me at a midnight helm.\n\nBut that night, in particular, a strange (and ever since inexplicable) thing occurred to me. Starting from a brief standing sleep, I was horribly conscious of something fatally wrong. The jaw-bone tiller smote my side, which leaned against it; in my ears was the low hum of sails, just beginning to shake in the wind; I thought my eyes were open; I was half conscious of putting my fingers to the lids and mechanically stretching them still further apart. But, spite of all this, I could see no compass before me to steer by; though it seemed but a minute since I had been watching the card, by the steady binnacle lamp illuminating it. Nothing seemed before me but a jet gloom, now and then made ghastly by flashes of redness. Uppermost was the impression, that whatever swift, rushing thing I stood on was not so much bound to any haven ahead as rushing from all havens astern. A stark, bewildered feeling, as of death, came over me. Convulsively my hands grasped the tiller, but with the crazy conceit that the tiller was, somehow, in some enchanted way, inverted. My God! what is the matter with me? thought I. Lo! in my brief sleep I had turned myself about, and was fronting the ship’s stern, with my back to her prow and the compass. In an instant I faced back, just in time to prevent the vessel from flying up into the wind, and very probably capsizing her. How glad and how grateful the relief from this unnatural hallucination of the night, and the fatal contingency of being brought by the lee!\n\nLook not too long in the face of the fire, O man! Never dream with thy hand on the helm! Turn not thy back to the compass; accept the first hint of the hitching tiller; believe not the artificial fire, when its redness makes all things look ghastly. To-morrow, in the natural sun, the skies will be bright; those who glared like devils in the forking flames, the morn will show in far other, at least gentler, relief; the glorious, golden, glad sun, the only true lamp—all others but liars!\n\nNevertheless the sun hides not Virginia’s Dismal Swamp, nor Rome’s accursed Campagna, nor wide Sahara, nor all the millions of miles of deserts and of griefs beneath the moon. The sun hides not the ocean, which is the dark side of this earth, and which is two thirds of this earth. So, therefore, that mortal man who hath more of joy than sorrow in him, that mortal man cannot be true—not true, or undeveloped. With books the same. The truest of all men was the Man of Sorrows, and the truest of all books is Solomon’s, and Ecclesiastes is the fine hammered steel of woe. “All is vanity.” ALL. This wilful world hath not got hold of unchristian Solomon’s wisdom yet. But he who dodges hospitals and jails, and walks fast crossing graveyards, and would rather talk of operas than hell; calls Cowper, Young, Pascal, Rousseau, poor devils all of sick men; and throughout a care-free lifetime swears by Rabelais as passing wise, and therefore jolly;—not that man is fitted to sit down on tomb-stones, and break the green damp mould with unfathomably wondrous Solomon.\n\nBut even Solomon, he says, “the man that wandereth out of the way of understanding shall remain” (i.e., even while living) “in the congregation of the dead.” Give not thyself up, then, to fire, lest it invert thee, deaden thee; as for the time it did me. There is a wisdom that is woe; but there is a woe that is madness. And there is a Catskill eagle in some souls that can alike dive down into the blackest gorges, and soar out of them again and become invisible in the sunny spaces. And even if he for ever flies within the gorge, that gorge is in the mountains; so that even in his lowest swoop the mountain eagle is still higher than other birds upon the plain, even though they soar.\n\n# CHAPTER 97. The Lamp.\n\nHad you descended from the Pequod’s try-works to the Pequod’s forecastle, where the off duty watch were sleeping, for one single moment you would have almost thought you were standing in some illuminated shrine of canonized kings and counsellors. There they lay in their triangular oaken vaults, each mariner a chiselled muteness; a score of lamps flashing upon his hooded eyes.\n\nIn merchantmen, oil for the sailor is more scarce than the milk of queens. To dress in the dark, and eat in the dark, and stumble in darkness to his pallet, this is his usual lot. But the whaleman, as he seeks the food of light, so he lives in light. He makes his berth an Aladdin’s lamp, and lays him down in it; so that in the pitchiest night the ship’s black hull still houses an illumination.\n\nSee with what entire freedom the whaleman takes his handful of lamps—often but old bottles and vials, though—to the copper cooler at the try-works, and replenishes them there, as mugs of ale at a vat. He burns, too, the purest of oil, in its unmanufactured, and, therefore, unvitiated state; a fluid unknown to solar, lunar, or astral contrivances ashore. It is sweet as early grass butter in April. He goes and hunts for his oil, so as to be sure of its freshness and genuineness, even as the traveller on the prairie hunts up his own supper of game.\n\n# CHAPTER 98. Stowing Down and Clearing Up.\n\nAlready has it been related how the great leviathan is afar off descried from the mast-head; how he is chased over the watery moors, and slaughtered in the valleys of the deep; how he is then towed alongside and beheaded; and how (on the principle which entitled the headsman of old to the garments in which the beheaded was killed) his great padded surtout becomes the property of his executioner; how, in due time, he is condemned to the pots, and, like Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, his spermaceti, oil, and bone pass unscathed through the fire;—but now it remains to conclude the last chapter of this part of the description by rehearsing—singing, if I may—the romantic proceeding of decanting off his oil into the casks and striking them down into the hold, where once again leviathan returns to his native profundities, sliding along beneath the surface as before; but, alas! never more to rise and blow.\n\nWhile still warm, the oil, like hot punch, is received into the six-barrel casks; and while, perhaps, the ship is pitching and rolling this way and that in the midnight sea, the enormous casks are slewed round and headed over, end for end, and sometimes perilously scoot across the slippery deck, like so many land slides, till at last man-handled and stayed in their course; and all round the hoops, rap, rap, go as many hammers as can play upon them, for now, ex officio, every sailor is a cooper.\n\nAt length, when the last pint is casked, and all is cool, then the great hatchways are unsealed, the bowels of the ship are thrown open, and down go the casks to their final rest in the sea. This done, the hatches are replaced, and hermetically closed, like a closet walled up.\n\nIn the sperm fishery, this is perhaps one of the most remarkable incidents in all the business of whaling. One day the planks stream with freshets of blood and oil; on the sacred quarter-deck enormous masses of the whale’s head are profanely piled; great rusty casks lie about, as in a brewery yard; the smoke from the try-works has besooted all the bulwarks; the mariners go about suffused with unctuousness; the entire ship seems great leviathan himself; while on all hands the din is deafening.\n\nBut a day or two after, you look about you, and prick your ears in this self-same ship; and were it not for the tell-tale boats and try-works, you would all but swear you trod some silent merchant vessel, with a most scrupulously neat commander. The unmanufactured sperm oil possesses a singularly cleansing virtue. This is the reason why the decks never look so white as just after what they call an affair of oil. Besides, from the ashes of the burned scraps of the whale, a potent lye is readily made; and whenever any adhesiveness from the back of the whale remains clinging to the side, that lye quickly exterminates it. Hands go diligently along the bulwarks, and with buckets of water and rags restore them to their full tidiness. The soot is brushed from the lower rigging. All the numerous implements which have been in use are likewise faithfully cleansed and put away. The great hatch is scrubbed and placed upon the try-works, completely hiding the pots; every cask is out of sight; all tackles are coiled in unseen nooks; and when by the combined and simultaneous industry of almost the entire ship’s company, the whole of this conscientious duty is at last concluded, then the crew themselves proceed to their own ablutions; shift themselves from top to toe; and finally issue to the immaculate deck, fresh and all aglow, as bridegrooms new-leaped from out the daintiest Holland.\n\nNow, with elated step, they pace the planks in twos and threes, and humorously discourse of parlors, sofas, carpets, and fine cambrics; propose to mat the deck; think of having hanging to the top; object not to taking tea by moonlight on the piazza of the forecastle. To hint to such musked mariners of oil, and bone, and blubber, were little short of audacity. They know not the thing you distantly allude to. Away, and bring us napkins!\n\nBut mark: aloft there, at the three mast heads, stand three men intent on spying out more whales, which, if caught, infallibly will again soil the old oaken furniture, and drop at least one small grease-spot somewhere. Yes; and many is the time, when, after the severest uninterrupted labors, which know no night; continuing straight through for ninety-six hours; when from the boat, where they have swelled their wrists with all day rowing on the Line,—they only step to the deck to carry vast chains, and heave the heavy windlass, and cut and slash, yea, and in their very sweatings to be smoked and burned anew by the combined fires of the equatorial sun and the equatorial try-works; when, on the heel of all this, they have finally bestirred themselves to cleanse the ship, and make a spotless dairy room of it; many is the time the poor fellows, just buttoning the necks of their clean frocks, are startled by the cry of “There she blows!” and away they fly to fight another whale, and go through the whole weary thing again. Oh! my friends, but this is man-killing! Yet this is life. For hardly have we mortals by long toilings extracted from this world’s vast bulk its small but valuable sperm; and then, with weary patience, cleansed ourselves from its defilements, and learned to live here in clean tabernacles of the soul; hardly is this done, when—There she blows!—the ghost is spouted up, and away we sail to fight some other world, and go through young life’s old routine again.\n\nOh! the metempsychosis! Oh! Pythagoras, that in bright Greece, two thousand years ago, did die, so good, so wise, so mild; I sailed with thee along the Peruvian coast last voyage—and, foolish as I am, taught thee, a green simple boy, how to splice a rope!\n\n# CHAPTER 99. The Doubloon.\n\nEre now it has been related how Ahab was wont to pace his quarter-deck, taking regular turns at either limit, the binnacle and mainmast; but in the multiplicity of other things requiring narration it has not been added how that sometimes in these walks, when most plunged in his mood, he was wont to pause in turn at each spot, and stand there strangely eyeing the particular object before him. When he halted before the binnacle, with his glance fastened on the pointed needle in the compass, that glance shot like a javelin with the pointed intensity of his purpose; and when resuming his walk he again paused before the mainmast, then, as the same riveted glance fastened upon the riveted gold coin there, he still wore the same aspect of nailed firmness, only dashed with a certain wild longing, if not hopefulness.\n\nBut one morning, turning to pass the doubloon, he seemed to be newly attracted by the strange figures and inscriptions stamped on it, as though now for the first time beginning to interpret for himself in some monomaniac way whatever significance might lurk in them. And some certain significance lurks in all things, else all things are little worth, and the round world itself but an empty cipher, except to sell by the cartload, as they do hills about Boston, to fill up some morass in the Milky Way.\n\nNow this doubloon was of purest, virgin gold, raked somewhere out of the heart of gorgeous hills, whence, east and west, over golden sands, the head-waters of many a Pactolus flows. And though now nailed amidst all the rustiness of iron bolts and the verdigris of copper spikes, yet, untouchable and immaculate to any foulness, it still preserved its Quito glow. Nor, though placed amongst a ruthless crew and every hour passed by ruthless hands, and through the livelong nights shrouded with thick darkness which might cover any pilfering approach, nevertheless every sunrise found the doubloon where the sunset left it last. For it was set apart and sanctified to one awe-striking end; and however wanton in their sailor ways, one and all, the mariners revered it as the white whale’s talisman. Sometimes they talked it over in the weary watch by night, wondering whose it was to be at last, and whether he would ever live to spend it.\n\nNow those noble golden coins of South America are as medals of the sun and tropic token-pieces. Here palms, alpacas, and volcanoes; sun’s disks and stars; ecliptics, horns-of-plenty, and rich banners waving, are in luxuriant profusion stamped; so that the precious gold seems almost to derive an added preciousness and enhancing glories, by passing through those fancy mints, so Spanishly poetic.\n\nIt so chanced that the doubloon of the Pequod was a most wealthy example of these things. On its round border it bore the letters, REPUBLICA DEL ECUADOR: QUITO. So this bright coin came from a country planted in the middle of the world, and beneath the great equator, and named after it; and it had been cast midway up the Andes, in the unwaning clime that knows no autumn. Zoned by those letters you saw the likeness of three Andes’ summits; from one a flame; a tower on another; on the third a crowing cock; while arching over all was a segment of the partitioned zodiac, the signs all marked with their usual cabalistics, and the keystone sun entering the equinoctial point at Libra.\n\nBefore this equatorial coin, Ahab, not unobserved by others, was now pausing.\n\n“There’s something ever egotistical in mountain-tops and towers, and all other grand and lofty things; look here,—three peaks as proud as Lucifer. The firm tower, that is Ahab; the volcano, that is Ahab; the courageous, the undaunted, and victorious fowl, that, too, is Ahab; all are Ahab; and this round gold is but the image of the rounder globe, which, like a magician’s glass, to each and every man in turn but mirrors back his own mysterious self. Great pains, small gains for those who ask the world to solve them; it cannot solve itself. Methinks now this coined sun wears a ruddy face; but see! aye, he enters the sign of storms, the equinox! and but six months before he wheeled out of a former equinox at Aries! From storm to storm! So be it, then. Born in throes, ’tis fit that man should live in pains and die in pangs! So be it, then! Here’s stout stuff for woe to work on. So be it, then.”\n\n“No fairy fingers can have pressed the gold, but devil’s claws must have left their mouldings there since yesterday,” murmured Starbuck to himself, leaning against the bulwarks. “The old man seems to read Belshazzar’s awful writing. I have never marked the coin inspectingly. He goes below; let me read. A dark valley between three mighty, heaven-abiding peaks, that almost seem the Trinity, in some faint earthly symbol. So in this vale of Death, God girds us round; and over all our gloom, the sun of Righteousness still shines a beacon and a hope. If we bend down our eyes, the dark vale shows her mouldy soil; but if we lift them, the bright sun meets our glance half way, to cheer. Yet, oh, the great sun is no fixture; and if, at midnight, we would fain snatch some sweet solace from him, we gaze for him in vain! This coin speaks wisely, mildly, truly, but still sadly to me. I will quit it, lest Truth shake me falsely.”\n\n“There now’s the old Mogul,” soliloquized Stubb by the try-works, “he’s been twigging it; and there goes Starbuck from the same, and both with faces which I should say might be somewhere within nine fathoms long. And all from looking at a piece of gold, which did I have it now on Negro Hill or in Corlaer’s Hook, I’d not look at it very long ere spending it. Humph! in my poor, insignificant opinion, I regard this as queer. I have seen doubloons before now in my voyagings; your doubloons of old Spain, your doubloons of Peru, your doubloons of Chili, your doubloons of Bolivia, your doubloons of Popayan; with plenty of gold moidores and pistoles, and joes, and half joes, and quarter joes. What then should there be in this doubloon of the Equator that is so killing wonderful? By Golconda! let me read it once. Halloa! here’s signs and wonders truly! That, now, is what old Bowditch in his Epitome calls the zodiac, and what my almanac below calls ditto. I’ll get the almanac and as I have heard devils can be raised with Daboll’s arithmetic, I’ll try my hand at raising a meaning out of these queer curvicues here with the Massachusetts calendar. Here’s the book. Let’s see now. Signs and wonders; and the sun, he’s always among ’em. Hem, hem, hem; here they are—here they go—all alive:—Aries, or the Ram; Taurus, or the Bull and Jimimi! here’s Gemini himself, or the Twins. Well; the sun he wheels among ’em. Aye, here on the coin he’s just crossing the threshold between two of twelve sitting-rooms all in a ring. Book! you lie there; the fact is, you books must know your places. You’ll do to give us the bare words and facts, but we come in to supply the thoughts. That’s my small experience, so far as the Massachusetts calendar, and Bowditch’s navigator, and Daboll’s arithmetic go. Signs and wonders, eh? Pity if there is nothing wonderful in signs, and significant in wonders! There’s a clue somewhere; wait a bit; hist—hark! By Jove, I have it! Look you, Doubloon, your zodiac here is the life of man in one round chapter; and now I’ll read it off, straight out of the book. Come, Almanack! To begin: there’s Aries, or the Ram—lecherous dog, he begets us; then, Taurus, or the Bull—he bumps us the first thing; then Gemini, or the Twins—that is, Virtue and Vice; we try to reach Virtue, when lo! comes Cancer the Crab, and drags us back; and here, going from Virtue, Leo, a roaring Lion, lies in the path—he gives a few fierce bites and surly dabs with his paw; we escape, and hail Virgo, the Virgin! that’s our first love; we marry and think to be happy for aye, when pop comes Libra, or the Scales—happiness weighed and found wanting; and while we are very sad about that, Lord! how we suddenly jump, as Scorpio, or the Scorpion, stings us in the rear; we are curing the wound, when whang come the arrows all round; Sagittarius, or the Archer, is amusing himself. As we pluck out the shafts, stand aside! here’s the battering-ram, Capricornus, or the Goat; full tilt, he comes rushing, and headlong we are tossed; when Aquarius, or the Water-bearer, pours out his whole deluge and drowns us; and to wind up with Pisces, or the Fishes, we sleep. There’s a sermon now, writ in high heaven, and the sun goes through it every year, and yet comes out of it all alive and hearty. Jollily he, aloft there, wheels through toil and trouble; and so, alow here, does jolly Stubb. Oh, jolly’s the word for aye! Adieu, Doubloon! But stop; here comes little King-Post; dodge round the try-works, now, and let’s hear what he’ll have to say. There; he’s before it; he’ll out with something presently. So, so; he’s beginning.”\n\n“I see nothing here, but a round thing made of gold, and whoever raises a certain whale, this round thing belongs to him. So, what’s all this staring been about? It is worth sixteen dollars, that’s true; and at two cents the cigar, that’s nine hundred and sixty cigars. I won’t smoke dirty pipes like Stubb, but I like cigars, and here’s nine hundred and sixty of them; so here goes Flask aloft to spy ’em out.”\n\n“Shall I call that wise or foolish, now; if it be really wise it has a foolish look to it; yet, if it be really foolish, then has it a sort of wiseish look to it. But, avast; here comes our old Manxman—the old hearse-driver, he must have been, that is, before he took to the sea. He luffs up before the doubloon; halloa, and goes round on the other side of the mast; why, there’s a horse-shoe nailed on that side; and now he’s back again; what does that mean? Hark! he’s muttering—voice like an old worn-out coffee-mill. Prick ears, and listen!”\n\n“If the White Whale be raised, it must be in a month and a day, when the sun stands in some one of these signs. I’ve studied signs, and know their marks; they were taught me two score years ago, by the old witch in Copenhagen. Now, in what sign will the sun then be? The horse-shoe sign; for there it is, right opposite the gold. And what’s the horse-shoe sign? The lion is the horse-shoe sign—the roaring and devouring lion. Ship, old ship! my old head shakes to think of thee.”\n\n“There’s another rendering now; but still one text. All sorts of men in one kind of world, you see. Dodge again! here comes Queequeg—all tattooing—looks like the signs of the Zodiac himself. What says the Cannibal? As I live he’s comparing notes; looking at his thigh bone; thinks the sun is in the thigh, or in the calf, or in the bowels, I suppose, as the old women talk Surgeon’s Astronomy in the back country. And by Jove, he’s found something there in the vicinity of his thigh—I guess it’s Sagittarius, or the Archer. No: he don’t know what to make of the doubloon; he takes it for an old button off some king’s trowsers. But, aside again! here comes that ghost-devil, Fedallah; tail coiled out of sight as usual, oakum in the toes of his pumps as usual. What does he say, with that look of his? Ah, only makes a sign to the sign and bows himself; there is a sun on the coin—fire worshipper, depend upon it. Ho! more and more. This way comes Pip—poor boy! would he had died, or I; he’s half horrible to me. He too has been watching all of these interpreters—myself included—and look now, he comes to read, with that unearthly idiot face. Stand away again and hear him. Hark!”\n\n“I look, you look, he looks; we look, ye look, they look.”\n\n“Upon my soul, he’s been studying Murray’s Grammar! Improving his mind, poor fellow! But what’s that he says now—hist!”\n\n“I look, you look, he looks; we look, ye look, they look.”\n\n“Why, he’s getting it by heart—hist! again.”\n\n“I look, you look, he looks; we look, ye look, they look.”\n\n“Well, that’s funny.”\n\n“And I, you, and he; and we, ye, and they, are all bats; and I’m a crow, especially when I stand a’top of this pine tree here. Caw! caw! caw! caw! caw! caw! Ain’t I a crow? And where’s the scare-crow? There he stands; two bones stuck into a pair of old trowsers, and two more poked into the sleeves of an old jacket.”\n\n“Wonder if he means me?—complimentary!—poor lad!—I could go hang myself. Any way, for the present, I’ll quit Pip’s vicinity. I can stand the rest, for they have plain wits; but he’s too crazy-witty for my sanity. So, so, I leave him muttering.”\n\n“Here’s the ship’s navel, this doubloon here, and they are all on fire to unscrew it. But, unscrew your navel, and what’s the consequence? Then again, if it stays here, that is ugly, too, for when aught’s nailed to the mast it’s a sign that things grow desperate. Ha, ha! old Ahab! the White Whale; he’ll nail ye! This is a pine tree. My father, in old Tolland county, cut down a pine tree once, and found a silver ring grown over in it; some old darkey’s wedding ring. How did it get there? And so they’ll say in the resurrection, when they come to fish up this old mast, and find a doubloon lodged in it, with bedded oysters for the shaggy bark. Oh, the gold! the precious, precious, gold! the green miser’ll hoard ye soon! Hish! hish! God goes ’mong the worlds blackberrying. Cook! ho, cook! and cook us! Jenny! hey, hey, hey, hey, hey, Jenny, Jenny! and get your hoe-cake done!”\n\n# CHAPTER 100. Leg and Arm.\n\nThe Pequod, of Nantucket, Meets the Samuel Enderby, of London.\n\n“Ship, ahoy! Hast seen the White Whale?”\n\nSo cried Ahab, once more hailing a ship showing English colours, bearing down under the stern. Trumpet to mouth, the old man was standing in his hoisted quarter-boat, his ivory leg plainly revealed to the stranger captain, who was carelessly reclining in his own boat’s bow. He was a darkly-tanned, burly, good-natured, fine-looking man, of sixty or thereabouts, dressed in a spacious roundabout, that hung round him in festoons of blue pilot-cloth; and one empty arm of this jacket streamed behind him like the broidered arm of a hussar’s surcoat.\n\n“Hast seen the White Whale?”\n\n“See you this?” and withdrawing it from the folds that had hidden it, he held up a white arm of sperm whale bone, terminating in a wooden head like a mallet.\n\n“Man my boat!” cried Ahab, impetuously, and tossing about the oars near him—“Stand by to lower!”\n\nIn less than a minute, without quitting his little craft, he and his crew were dropped to the water, and were soon alongside of the stranger. But here a curious difficulty presented itself. In the excitement of the moment, Ahab had forgotten that since the loss of his leg he had never once stepped on board of any vessel at sea but his own, and then it was always by an ingenious and very handy mechanical contrivance peculiar to the Pequod, and a thing not to be rigged and shipped in any other vessel at a moment’s warning. Now, it is no very easy matter for anybody—except those who are almost hourly used to it, like whalemen—to clamber up a ship’s side from a boat on the open sea; for the great swells now lift the boat high up towards the bulwarks, and then instantaneously drop it half way down to the kelson. So, deprived of one leg, and the strange ship of course being altogether unsupplied with the kindly invention, Ahab now found himself abjectly reduced to a clumsy landsman again; hopelessly eyeing the uncertain changeful height he could hardly hope to attain.\n\nIt has before been hinted, perhaps, that every little untoward circumstance that befell him, and which indirectly sprang from his luckless mishap, almost invariably irritated or exasperated Ahab. And in the present instance, all this was heightened by the sight of the two officers of the strange ship, leaning over the side, by the perpendicular ladder of nailed cleets there, and swinging towards him a pair of tastefully-ornamented man-ropes; for at first they did not seem to bethink them that a one-legged man must be too much of a cripple to use their sea bannisters. But this awkwardness only lasted a minute, because the strange captain, observing at a glance how affairs stood, cried out, “I see, I see!—avast heaving there! Jump, boys, and swing over the cutting-tackle.”\n\nAs good luck would have it, they had had a whale alongside a day or two previous, and the great tackles were still aloft, and the massive curved blubber-hook, now clean and dry, was still attached to the end. This was quickly lowered to Ahab, who at once comprehending it all, slid his solitary thigh into the curve of the hook (it was like sitting in the fluke of an anchor, or the crotch of an apple tree), and then giving the word, held himself fast, and at the same time also helped to hoist his own weight, by pulling hand-over-hand upon one of the running parts of the tackle. Soon he was carefully swung inside the high bulwarks, and gently landed upon the capstan head. With his ivory arm frankly thrust forth in welcome, the other captain advanced, and Ahab, putting out his ivory leg, and crossing the ivory arm (like two sword-fish blades) cried out in his walrus way, “Aye, aye, hearty! let us shake bones together!—an arm and a leg!—an arm that never can shrink, d’ye see; and a leg that never can run. Where did’st thou see the White Whale?—how long ago?”\n\n“The White Whale,” said the Englishman, pointing his ivory arm towards the East, and taking a rueful sight along it, as if it had been a telescope; “there I saw him, on the Line, last season.”\n\n“And he took that arm off, did he?” asked Ahab, now sliding down from the capstan, and resting on the Englishman’s shoulder, as he did so.\n\n“Aye, he was the cause of it, at least; and that leg, too?”\n\n“Spin me the yarn,” said Ahab; “how was it?”\n\n“It was the first time in my life that I ever cruised on the Line,” began the Englishman. “I was ignorant of the White Whale at that time. Well, one day we lowered for a pod of four or five whales, and my boat fastened to one of them; a regular circus horse he was, too, that went milling and milling round so, that my boat’s crew could only trim dish, by sitting all their sterns on the outer gunwale. Presently up breaches from the bottom of the sea a bouncing great whale, with a milky-white head and hump, all crows’ feet and wrinkles.”\n\n“It was he, it was he!” cried Ahab, suddenly letting out his suspended breath.\n\n“And harpoons sticking in near his starboard fin.”\n\n“Aye, aye—they were mine—my irons,” cried Ahab, exultingly—“but on!”\n\n“Give me a chance, then,” said the Englishman, good-humoredly. “Well, this old great-grandfather, with the white head and hump, runs all afoam into the pod, and goes to snapping furiously at my fast-line!\n\n“Aye, I see!—wanted to part it; free the fast-fish—an old trick—I know him.”\n\n“How it was exactly,” continued the one-armed commander, “I do not know; but in biting the line, it got foul of his teeth, caught there somehow; but we didn’t know it then; so that when we afterwards pulled on the line, bounce we came plump on to his hump! instead of the other whale’s; that went off to windward, all fluking. Seeing how matters stood, and what a noble great whale it was—the noblest and biggest I ever saw, sir, in my life—I resolved to capture him, spite of the boiling rage he seemed to be in. And thinking the hap-hazard line would get loose, or the tooth it was tangled to might draw (for I have a devil of a boat’s crew for a pull on a whale-line); seeing all this, I say, I jumped into my first mate’s boat—Mr. Mounttop’s here (by the way, Captain—Mounttop; Mounttop—the captain);—as I was saying, I jumped into Mounttop’s boat, which, d’ye see, was gunwale and gunwale with mine, then; and snatching the first harpoon, let this old great-grandfather have it. But, Lord, look you, sir—hearts and souls alive, man—the next instant, in a jiff, I was blind as a bat—both eyes out—all befogged and bedeadened with black foam—the whale’s tail looming straight up out of it, perpendicular in the air, like a marble steeple. No use sterning all, then; but as I was groping at midday, with a blinding sun, all crown-jewels; as I was groping, I say, after the second iron, to toss it overboard—down comes the tail like a Lima tower, cutting my boat in two, leaving each half in splinters; and, flukes first, the white hump backed through the wreck, as though it was all chips. We all struck out. To escape his terrible flailings, I seized hold of my harpoon-pole sticking in him, and for a moment clung to that like a sucking fish. But a combing sea dashed me off, and at the same instant, the fish, taking one good dart forwards, went down like a flash; and the barb of that cursed second iron towing along near me caught me here” (clapping his hand just below his shoulder); “yes, caught me just here, I say, and bore me down to Hell’s flames, I was thinking; when, when, all of a sudden, thank the good God, the barb ript its way along the flesh—clear along the whole length of my arm—came out nigh my wrist, and up I floated;—and that gentleman there will tell you the rest (by the way, captain—Dr. Bunger, ship’s surgeon: Bunger, my lad,—the captain). Now, Bunger boy, spin your part of the yarn.”\n\nThe professional gentleman thus familiarly pointed out, had been all the time standing near them, with nothing specific visible, to denote his gentlemanly rank on board. His face was an exceedingly round but sober one; he was dressed in a faded blue woollen frock or shirt, and patched trowsers; and had thus far been dividing his attention between a marlingspike he held in one hand, and a pill-box held in the other, occasionally casting a critical glance at the ivory limbs of the two crippled captains. But, at his superior’s introduction of him to Ahab, he politely bowed, and straightway went on to do his captain’s bidding.\n\n“It was a shocking bad wound,” began the whale-surgeon; “and, taking my advice, Captain Boomer here, stood our old Sammy—”\n\n“Samuel Enderby is the name of my ship,” interrupted the one-armed captain, addressing Ahab; “go on, boy.”\n\n“Stood our old Sammy off to the northward, to get out of the blazing hot weather there on the Line. But it was no use—I did all I could; sat up with him nights; was very severe with him in the matter of diet—”\n\n“Oh, very severe!” chimed in the patient himself; then suddenly altering his voice, “Drinking hot rum toddies with me every night, till he couldn’t see to put on the bandages; and sending me to bed, half seas over, about three o’clock in the morning. Oh, ye stars! he sat up with me indeed, and was very severe in my diet. Oh! a great watcher, and very dietetically severe, is Dr. Bunger. (Bunger, you dog, laugh out! why don’t ye? You know you’re a precious jolly rascal.) But, heave ahead, boy, I’d rather be killed by you than kept alive by any other man.”\n\n“My captain, you must have ere this perceived, respected sir”—said the imperturbable godly-looking Bunger, slightly bowing to Ahab—“is apt to be facetious at times; he spins us many clever things of that sort. But I may as well say—en passant, as the French remark—that I myself—that is to say, Jack Bunger, late of the reverend clergy—am a strict total abstinence man; I never drink—”\n\n“Water!” cried the captain; “he never drinks it; it’s a sort of fits to him; fresh water throws him into the hydrophobia; but go on—go on with the arm story.”\n\n“Yes, I may as well,” said the surgeon, coolly. “I was about observing, sir, before Captain Boomer’s facetious interruption, that spite of my best and severest endeavors, the wound kept getting worse and worse; the truth was, sir, it was as ugly gaping wound as surgeon ever saw; more than two feet and several inches long. I measured it with the lead line. In short, it grew black; I knew what was threatened, and off it came. But I had no hand in shipping that ivory arm there; that thing is against all rule”—pointing at it with the marlingspike—“that is the captain’s work, not mine; he ordered the carpenter to make it; he had that club-hammer there put to the end, to knock some one’s brains out with, I suppose, as he tried mine once. He flies into diabolical passions sometimes. Do ye see this dent, sir”—removing his hat, and brushing aside his hair, and exposing a bowl-like cavity in his skull, but which bore not the slightest scarry trace, or any token of ever having been a wound—“Well, the captain there will tell you how that came here; he knows.”\n\n“No, I don’t,” said the captain, “but his mother did; he was born with it. Oh, you solemn rogue, you—you Bunger! was there ever such another Bunger in the watery world? Bunger, when you die, you ought to die in pickle, you dog; you should be preserved to future ages, you rascal.”\n\n“What became of the White Whale?” now cried Ahab, who thus far had been impatiently listening to this by-play between the two Englishmen.\n\n“Oh!” cried the one-armed captain, “oh, yes! Well; after he sounded, we didn’t see him again for some time; in fact, as I before hinted, I didn’t then know what whale it was that had served me such a trick, till some time afterwards, when coming back to the Line, we heard about Moby Dick—as some call him—and then I knew it was he.”\n\n“Did’st thou cross his wake again?”\n\n“Twice.”\n\n“But could not fasten?”\n\n“Didn’t want to try to: ain’t one limb enough? What should I do without this other arm? And I’m thinking Moby Dick doesn’t bite so much as he swallows.”\n\n“Well, then,” interrupted Bunger, “give him your left arm for bait to get the right. Do you know, gentlemen”—very gravely and mathematically bowing to each Captain in succession—“Do you know, gentlemen, that the digestive organs of the whale are so inscrutably constructed by Divine Providence, that it is quite impossible for him to completely digest even a man’s arm? And he knows it too. So that what you take for the White Whale’s malice is only his awkwardness. For he never means to swallow a single limb; he only thinks to terrify by feints. But sometimes he is like the old juggling fellow, formerly a patient of mine in Ceylon, that making believe swallow jack-knives, once upon a time let one drop into him in good earnest, and there it stayed for a twelvemonth or more; when I gave him an emetic, and he heaved it up in small tacks, d’ye see. No possible way for him to digest that jack-knife, and fully incorporate it into his general bodily system. Yes, Captain Boomer, if you are quick enough about it, and have a mind to pawn one arm for the sake of the privilege of giving decent burial to the other, why in that case the arm is yours; only let the whale have another chance at you shortly, that’s all.”\n\n“No, thank ye, Bunger,” said the English Captain, “he’s welcome to the arm he has, since I can’t help it, and didn’t know him then; but not to another one. No more White Whales for me; I’ve lowered for him once, and that has satisfied me. There would be great glory in killing him, I know that; and there is a ship-load of precious sperm in him, but, hark ye, he’s best let alone; don’t you think so, Captain?”—glancing at the ivory leg.\n\n“He is. But he will still be hunted, for all that. What is best let alone, that accursed thing is not always what least allures. He’s all a magnet! How long since thou saw’st him last? Which way heading?”\n\n“Bless my soul, and curse the foul fiend’s,” cried Bunger, stoopingly walking round Ahab, and like a dog, strangely snuffing; “this man’s blood—bring the thermometer!—it’s at the boiling point!—his pulse makes these planks beat!—sir!”—taking a lancet from his pocket, and drawing near to Ahab’s arm.\n\n“Avast!” roared Ahab, dashing him against the bulwarks—“Man the boat! Which way heading?”\n\n“Good God!” cried the English Captain, to whom the question was put. “What’s the matter? He was heading east, I think.—Is your Captain crazy?” whispering Fedallah.\n\nBut Fedallah, putting a finger on his lip, slid over the bulwarks to take the boat’s steering oar, and Ahab, swinging the cutting-tackle towards him, commanded the ship’s sailors to stand by to lower.\n\nIn a moment he was standing in the boat’s stern, and the Manilla men were springing to their oars. In vain the English Captain hailed him. With back to the stranger ship, and face set like a flint to his own, Ahab stood upright till alongside of the Pequod.\n\n# CHAPTER 101. The Decanter.\n\nEre the English ship fades from sight, be it set down here, that she hailed from London, and was named after the late Samuel Enderby, merchant of that city, the original of the famous whaling house of Enderby & Sons; a house which in my poor whaleman’s opinion, comes not far behind the united royal houses of the Tudors and Bourbons, in point of real historical interest. How long, prior to the year of our Lord 1775, this great whaling house was in existence, my numerous fish-documents do not make plain; but in that year (1775) it fitted out the first English ships that ever regularly hunted the Sperm Whale; though for some score of years previous (ever since 1726) our valiant Coffins and Maceys of Nantucket and the Vineyard had in large fleets pursued that Leviathan, but only in the North and South Atlantic: not elsewhere. Be it distinctly recorded here, that the Nantucketers were the first among mankind to harpoon with civilized steel the great Sperm Whale; and that for half a century they were the only people of the whole globe who so harpooned him.\n\nIn 1778, a fine ship, the Amelia, fitted out for the express purpose, and at the sole charge of the vigorous Enderbys, boldly rounded Cape Horn, and was the first among the nations to lower a whale-boat of any sort in the great South Sea. The voyage was a skilful and lucky one; and returning to her berth with her hold full of the precious sperm, the Amelia’s example was soon followed by other ships, English and American, and thus the vast Sperm Whale grounds of the Pacific were thrown open. But not content with this good deed, the indefatigable house again bestirred itself: Samuel and all his Sons—how many, their mother only knows—and under their immediate auspices, and partly, I think, at their expense, the British government was induced to send the sloop-of-war Rattler on a whaling voyage of discovery into the South Sea. Commanded by a naval Post-Captain, the Rattler made a rattling voyage of it, and did some service; how much does not appear. But this is not all. In 1819, the same house fitted out a discovery whale ship of their own, to go on a tasting cruise to the remote waters of Japan. That ship—well called the “Syren”—made a noble experimental cruise; and it was thus that the great Japanese Whaling Ground first became generally known. The Syren in this famous voyage was commanded by a Captain Coffin, a Nantucketer.\n\nAll honor to the Enderbies, therefore, whose house, I think, exists to the present day; though doubtless the original Samuel must long ago have slipped his cable for the great South Sea of the other world.\n\nThe ship named after him was worthy of the honor, being a very fast sailer and a noble craft every way. I boarded her once at midnight somewhere off the Patagonian coast, and drank good flip down in the forecastle. It was a fine gam we had, and they were all trumps—every soul on board. A short life to them, and a jolly death. And that fine gam I had—long, very long after old Ahab touched her planks with his ivory heel—it minds me of the noble, solid, Saxon hospitality of that ship; and may my parson forget me, and the devil remember me, if I ever lose sight of it. Flip? Did I say we had flip? Yes, and we flipped it at the rate of ten gallons the hour; and when the squall came (for it’s squally off there by Patagonia), and all hands—visitors and all—were called to reef topsails, we were so top-heavy that we had to swing each other aloft in bowlines; and we ignorantly furled the skirts of our jackets into the sails, so that we hung there, reefed fast in the howling gale, a warning example to all drunken tars. However, the masts did not go overboard; and by and by we scrambled down, so sober, that we had to pass the flip again, though the savage salt spray bursting down the forecastle scuttle, rather too much diluted and pickled it to my taste.\n\nThe beef was fine—tough, but with body in it. They said it was bull-beef; others, that it was dromedary beef; but I do not know, for certain, how that was. They had dumplings too; small, but substantial, symmetrically globular, and indestructible dumplings. I fancied that you could feel them, and roll them about in you after they were swallowed. If you stooped over too far forward, you risked their pitching out of you like billiard-balls. The bread—but that couldn’t be helped; besides, it was an anti-scorbutic; in short, the bread contained the only fresh fare they had. But the forecastle was not very light, and it was very easy to step over into a dark corner when you ate it. But all in all, taking her from truck to helm, considering the dimensions of the cook’s boilers, including his own live parchment boilers; fore and aft, I say, the Samuel Enderby was a jolly ship; of good fare and plenty; fine flip and strong; crack fellows all, and capital from boot heels to hat-band.\n\nBut why was it, think ye, that the Samuel Enderby, and some other English whalers I know of—not all though—were such famous, hospitable ships; that passed round the beef, and the bread, and the can, and the joke; and were not soon weary of eating, and drinking, and laughing? I will tell you. The abounding good cheer of these English whalers is matter for historical research. Nor have I been at all sparing of historical whale research, when it has seemed needed.\n\nThe English were preceded in the whale fishery by the Hollanders, Zealanders, and Danes; from whom they derived many terms still extant in the fishery; and what is yet more, their fat old fashions, touching plenty to eat and drink. For, as a general thing, the English merchant-ship scrimps her crew; but not so the English whaler. Hence, in the English, this thing of whaling good cheer is not normal and natural, but incidental and particular; and, therefore, must have some special origin, which is here pointed out, and will be still further elucidated.\n\nDuring my researches in the Leviathanic histories, I stumbled upon an ancient Dutch volume, which, by the musty whaling smell of it, I knew must be about whalers. The title was, “Dan Coopman,” wherefore I concluded that this must be the invaluable memoirs of some Amsterdam cooper in the fishery, as every whale ship must carry its cooper. I was reinforced in this opinion by seeing that it was the production of one “Fitz Swackhammer.” But my friend Dr. Snodhead, a very learned man, professor of Low Dutch and High German in the college of Santa Claus and St. Pott’s, to whom I handed the work for translation, giving him a box of sperm candles for his trouble—this same Dr. Snodhead, so soon as he spied the book, assured me that “Dan Coopman” did not mean “The Cooper,” but “The Merchant.” In short, this ancient and learned Low Dutch book treated of the commerce of Holland; and, among other subjects, contained a very interesting account of its whale fishery. And in this chapter it was, headed, “Smeer,” or “Fat,” that I found a long detailed list of the outfits for the larders and cellars of 180 sail of Dutch whalemen; from which list, as translated by Dr. Snodhead, I transcribe the following:\n\n400,000 lbs. of beef. 60,000 lbs. Friesland pork. 150,000 lbs. of stock fish. 550,000 lbs. of biscuit. 72,000 lbs. of soft bread. 2,800 firkins of butter. 20,000 lbs. Texel & Leyden cheese. 144,000 lbs. cheese (probably an inferior article). 550 ankers of Geneva. 10,800 barrels of beer.\n\nMost statistical tables are parchingly dry in the reading; not so in the present case, however, where the reader is flooded with whole pipes, barrels, quarts, and gills of good gin and good cheer.\n\nAt the time, I devoted three days to the studious digesting of all this beer, beef, and bread, during which many profound thoughts were incidentally suggested to me, capable of a transcendental and Platonic application; and, furthermore, I compiled supplementary tables of my own, touching the probable quantity of stock-fish, etc., consumed by every Low Dutch harpooneer in that ancient Greenland and Spitzbergen whale fishery. In the first place, the amount of butter, and Texel and Leyden cheese consumed, seems amazing. I impute it, though, to their naturally unctuous natures, being rendered still more unctuous by the nature of their vocation, and especially by their pursuing their game in those frigid Polar Seas, on the very coasts of that Esquimaux country where the convivial natives pledge each other in bumpers of train oil.\n\nThe quantity of beer, too, is very large, 10,800 barrels. Now, as those polar fisheries could only be prosecuted in the short summer of that climate, so that the whole cruise of one of these Dutch whalemen, including the short voyage to and from the Spitzbergen sea, did not much exceed three months, say, and reckoning 30 men to each of their fleet of 180 sail, we have 5,400 Low Dutch seamen in all; therefore, I say, we have precisely two barrels of beer per man, for a twelve weeks’ allowance, exclusive of his fair proportion of that 550 ankers of gin. Now, whether these gin and beer harpooneers, so fuddled as one might fancy them to have been, were the right sort of men to stand up in a boat’s head, and take good aim at flying whales; this would seem somewhat improbable. Yet they did aim at them, and hit them too. But this was very far North, be it remembered, where beer agrees well with the constitution; upon the Equator, in our southern fishery, beer would be apt to make the harpooneer sleepy at the mast-head and boozy in his boat; and grievous loss might ensue to Nantucket and New Bedford.\n\nBut no more; enough has been said to show that the old Dutch whalers of two or three centuries ago were high livers; and that the English whalers have not neglected so excellent an example. For, say they, when cruising in an empty ship, if you can get nothing better out of the world, get a good dinner out of it, at least. And this empties the decanter.\n\n# CHAPTER 102. A Bower in the Arsacides.\n\nHitherto, in descriptively treating of the Sperm Whale, I have chiefly dwelt upon the marvels of his outer aspect; or separately and in detail upon some few interior structural features. But to a large and thorough sweeping comprehension of him, it behooves me now to unbutton him still further, and untagging the points of his hose, unbuckling his garters, and casting loose the hooks and the eyes of the joints of his innermost bones, set him before you in his ultimatum; that is to say, in his unconditional skeleton.\n\nBut how now, Ishmael? How is it, that you, a mere oarsman in the fishery, pretend to know aught about the subterranean parts of the whale? Did erudite Stubb, mounted upon your capstan, deliver lectures on the anatomy of the Cetacea; and by help of the windlass, hold up a specimen rib for exhibition? Explain thyself, Ishmael. Can you land a full-grown whale on your deck for examination, as a cook dishes a roast-pig? Surely not. A veritable witness have you hitherto been, Ishmael; but have a care how you seize the privilege of Jonah alone; the privilege of discoursing upon the joists and beams; the rafters, ridge-pole, sleepers, and under-pinnings, making up the frame-work of leviathan; and belike of the tallow-vats, dairy-rooms, butteries, and cheeseries in his bowels.\n\nI confess, that since Jonah, few whalemen have penetrated very far beneath the skin of the adult whale; nevertheless, I have been blessed with an opportunity to dissect him in miniature. In a ship I belonged to, a small cub Sperm Whale was once bodily hoisted to the deck for his poke or bag, to make sheaths for the barbs of the harpoons, and for the heads of the lances. Think you I let that chance go, without using my boat-hatchet and jack-knife, and breaking the seal and reading all the contents of that young cub?\n\nAnd as for my exact knowledge of the bones of the leviathan in their gigantic, full grown development, for that rare knowledge I am indebted to my late royal friend Tranquo, king of Tranque, one of the Arsacides. For being at Tranque, years ago, when attached to the trading-ship Dey of Algiers, I was invited to spend part of the Arsacidean holidays with the lord of Tranque, at his retired palm villa at Pupella; a sea-side glen not very far distant from what our sailors called Bamboo-Town, his capital.\n\nAmong many other fine qualities, my royal friend Tranquo, being gifted with a devout love for all matters of barbaric vertu, had brought together in Pupella whatever rare things the more ingenious of his people could invent; chiefly carved woods of wonderful devices, chiselled shells, inlaid spears, costly paddles, aromatic canoes; and all these distributed among whatever natural wonders, the wonder-freighted, tribute-rendering waves had cast upon his shores.\n\nChief among these latter was a great Sperm Whale, which, after an unusually long raging gale, had been found dead and stranded, with his head against a cocoa-nut tree, whose plumage-like, tufted droopings seemed his verdant jet. When the vast body had at last been stripped of its fathom-deep enfoldings, and the bones become dust dry in the sun, then the skeleton was carefully transported up the Pupella glen, where a grand temple of lordly palms now sheltered it.\n\nThe ribs were hung with trophies; the vertebræ were carved with Arsacidean annals, in strange hieroglyphics; in the skull, the priests kept up an unextinguished aromatic flame, so that the mystic head again sent forth its vapory spout; while, suspended from a bough, the terrific lower jaw vibrated over all the devotees, like the hair-hung sword that so affrighted Damocles.\n\nIt was a wondrous sight. The wood was green as mosses of the Icy Glen; the trees stood high and haughty, feeling their living sap; the industrious earth beneath was as a weaver’s loom, with a gorgeous carpet on it, whereof the ground-vine tendrils formed the warp and woof, and the living flowers the figures. All the trees, with all their laden branches; all the shrubs, and ferns, and grasses; the message-carrying air; all these unceasingly were active. Through the lacings of the leaves, the great sun seemed a flying shuttle weaving the unwearied verdure. Oh, busy weaver! unseen weaver!—pause!—one word!—whither flows the fabric? what palace may it deck? wherefore all these ceaseless toilings? Speak, weaver!—stay thy hand!—but one single word with thee! Nay—the shuttle flies—the figures float from forth the loom; the freshet-rushing carpet for ever slides away. The weaver-god, he weaves; and by that weaving is he deafened, that he hears no mortal voice; and by that humming, we, too, who look on the loom are deafened; and only when we escape it shall we hear the thousand voices that speak through it. For even so it is in all material factories. The spoken words that are inaudible among the flying spindles; those same words are plainly heard without the walls, bursting from the opened casements. Thereby have villainies been detected. Ah, mortal! then, be heedful; for so, in all this din of the great world’s loom, thy subtlest thinkings may be overheard afar.\n\nNow, amid the green, life-restless loom of that Arsacidean wood, the great, white, worshipped skeleton lay lounging—a gigantic idler! Yet, as the ever-woven verdant warp and woof intermixed and hummed around him, the mighty idler seemed the cunning weaver; himself all woven over with the vines; every month assuming greener, fresher verdure; but himself a skeleton. Life folded Death; Death trellised Life; the grim god wived with youthful Life, and begat him curly-headed glories.\n\nNow, when with royal Tranquo I visited this wondrous whale, and saw the skull an altar, and the artificial smoke ascending from where the real jet had issued, I marvelled that the king should regard a chapel as an object of vertu. He laughed. But more I marvelled that the priests should swear that smoky jet of his was genuine. To and fro I paced before this skeleton—brushed the vines aside—broke through the ribs—and with a ball of Arsacidean twine, wandered, eddied long amid its many winding, shaded colonnades and arbours. But soon my line was out; and following it back, I emerged from the opening where I entered. I saw no living thing within; naught was there but bones.\n\nCutting me a green measuring-rod, I once more dived within the skeleton. From their arrow-slit in the skull, the priests perceived me taking the altitude of the final rib, “How now!” they shouted; “Dar’st thou measure this our god! That’s for us.” “Aye, priests—well, how long do ye make him, then?” But hereupon a fierce contest rose among them, concerning feet and inches; they cracked each other’s sconces with their yard-sticks—the great skull echoed—and seizing that lucky chance, I quickly concluded my own admeasurements.\n\nThese admeasurements I now propose to set before you. But first, be it recorded, that, in this matter, I am not free to utter any fancied measurement I please. Because there are skeleton authorities you can refer to, to test my accuracy. There is a Leviathanic Museum, they tell me, in Hull, England, one of the whaling ports of that country, where they have some fine specimens of fin-backs and other whales. Likewise, I have heard that in the museum of Manchester, in New Hampshire, they have what the proprietors call “the only perfect specimen of a Greenland or River Whale in the United States.” Moreover, at a place in Yorkshire, England, Burton Constable by name, a certain Sir Clifford Constable has in his possession the skeleton of a Sperm Whale, but of moderate size, by no means of the full-grown magnitude of my friend King Tranquo’s.\n\nIn both cases, the stranded whales to which these two skeletons belonged, were originally claimed by their proprietors upon similar grounds. King Tranquo seizing his because he wanted it; and Sir Clifford, because he was lord of the seignories of those parts. Sir Clifford’s whale has been articulated throughout; so that, like a great chest of drawers, you can open and shut him, in all his bony cavities—spread out his ribs like a gigantic fan—and swing all day upon his lower jaw. Locks are to be put upon some of his trap-doors and shutters; and a footman will show round future visitors with a bunch of keys at his side. Sir Clifford thinks of charging twopence for a peep at the whispering gallery in the spinal column; threepence to hear the echo in the hollow of his cerebellum; and sixpence for the unrivalled view from his forehead.\n\nThe skeleton dimensions I shall now proceed to set down are copied verbatim from my right arm, where I had them tattooed; as in my wild wanderings at that period, there was no other secure way of preserving such valuable statistics. But as I was crowded for space, and wished the other parts of my body to remain a blank page for a poem I was then composing—at least, what untattooed parts might remain—I did not trouble myself with the odd inches; nor, indeed, should inches at all enter into a congenial admeasurement of the whale.\n\n# CHAPTER 103. Measurement of The Whale’s Skeleton.\n\nIn the first place, I wish to lay before you a particular, plain statement, touching the living bulk of this leviathan, whose skeleton we are briefly to exhibit. Such a statement may prove useful here.\n\nAccording to a careful calculation I have made, and which I partly base upon Captain Scoresby’s estimate, of seventy tons for the largest sized Greenland whale of sixty feet in length; according to my careful calculation, I say, a Sperm Whale of the largest magnitude, between eighty-five and ninety feet in length, and something less than forty feet in its fullest circumference, such a whale will weigh at least ninety tons; so that, reckoning thirteen men to a ton, he would considerably outweigh the combined population of a whole village of one thousand one hundred inhabitants.\n\nThink you not then that brains, like yoked cattle, should be put to this leviathan, to make him at all budge to any landsman’s imagination?\n\nHaving already in various ways put before you his skull, spout-hole, jaw, teeth, tail, forehead, fins, and divers other parts, I shall now simply point out what is most interesting in the general bulk of his unobstructed bones. But as the colossal skull embraces so very large a proportion of the entire extent of the skeleton; as it is by far the most complicated part; and as nothing is to be repeated concerning it in this chapter, you must not fail to carry it in your mind, or under your arm, as we proceed, otherwise you will not gain a complete notion of the general structure we are about to view.\n\nIn length, the Sperm Whale’s skeleton at Tranque measured seventy-two feet; so that when fully invested and extended in life, he must have been ninety feet long; for in the whale, the skeleton loses about one fifth in length compared with the living body. Of this seventy-two feet, his skull and jaw comprised some twenty feet, leaving some fifty feet of plain back-bone. Attached to this back-bone, for something less than a third of its length, was the mighty circular basket of ribs which once enclosed his vitals.\n\nTo me this vast ivory-ribbed chest, with the long, unrelieved spine, extending far away from it in a straight line, not a little resembled the hull of a great ship new-laid upon the stocks, when only some twenty of her naked bow-ribs are inserted, and the keel is otherwise, for the time, but a long, disconnected timber.\n\nThe ribs were ten on a side. The first, to begin from the neck, was nearly six feet long; the second, third, and fourth were each successively longer, till you came to the climax of the fifth, or one of the middle ribs, which measured eight feet and some inches. From that part, the remaining ribs diminished, till the tenth and last only spanned five feet and some inches. In general thickness, they all bore a seemly correspondence to their length. The middle ribs were the most arched. In some of the Arsacides they are used for beams whereon to lay footpath bridges over small streams.\n\nIn considering these ribs, I could not but be struck anew with the circumstance, so variously repeated in this book, that the skeleton of the whale is by no means the mould of his invested form. The largest of the Tranque ribs, one of the middle ones, occupied that part of the fish which, in life, is greatest in depth. Now, the greatest depth of the invested body of this particular whale must have been at least sixteen feet; whereas, the corresponding rib measured but little more than eight feet. So that this rib only conveyed half of the true notion of the living magnitude of that part. Besides, for some way, where I now saw but a naked spine, all that had been once wrapped round with tons of added bulk in flesh, muscle, blood, and bowels. Still more, for the ample fins, I here saw but a few disordered joints; and in place of the weighty and majestic, but boneless flukes, an utter blank!\n\nHow vain and foolish, then, thought I, for timid untravelled man to try to comprehend aright this wondrous whale, by merely poring over his dead attenuated skeleton, stretched in this peaceful wood. No. Only in the heart of quickest perils; only when within the eddyings of his angry flukes; only on the profound unbounded sea, can the fully invested whale be truly and livingly found out.\n\nBut the spine. For that, the best way we can consider it is, with a crane, to pile its bones high up on end. No speedy enterprise. But now it’s done, it looks much like Pompey’s Pillar.\n\nThere are forty and odd vertebræ in all, which in the skeleton are not locked together. They mostly lie like the great knobbed blocks on a Gothic spire, forming solid courses of heavy masonry. The largest, a middle one, is in width something less than three feet, and in depth more than four. The smallest, where the spine tapers away into the tail, is only two inches in width, and looks something like a white billiard-ball. I was told that there were still smaller ones, but they had been lost by some little cannibal urchins, the priest’s children, who had stolen them to play marbles with. Thus we see how that the spine of even the hugest of living things tapers off at last into simple child’s play.\n\n# CHAPTER 104. The Fossil Whale.\n\nFrom his mighty bulk the whale affords a most congenial theme whereon to enlarge, amplify, and generally expatiate. Would you, you could not compress him. By good rights he should only be treated of in imperial folio. Not to tell over again his furlongs from spiracle to tail, and the yards he measures about the waist; only think of the gigantic involutions of his intestines, where they lie in him like great cables and hawsers coiled away in the subterranean orlop-deck of a line-of-battle-ship.\n\nSince I have undertaken to manhandle this Leviathan, it behooves me to approve myself omnisciently exhaustive in the enterprise; not overlooking the minutest seminal germs of his blood, and spinning him out to the uttermost coil of his bowels. Having already described him in most of his present habitatory and anatomical peculiarities, it now remains to magnify him in an archæological, fossiliferous, and antediluvian point of view. Applied to any other creature than the Leviathan—to an ant or a flea—such portly terms might justly be deemed unwarrantably grandiloquent. But when Leviathan is the text, the case is altered. Fain am I to stagger to this emprise under the weightiest words of the dictionary. And here be it said, that whenever it has been convenient to consult one in the course of these dissertations, I have invariably used a huge quarto edition of Johnson, expressly purchased for that purpose; because that famous lexicographer’s uncommon personal bulk more fitted him to compile a lexicon to be used by a whale author like me.\n\nOne often hears of writers that rise and swell with their subject, though it may seem but an ordinary one. How, then, with me, writing of this Leviathan? Unconsciously my chirography expands into placard capitals. Give me a condor’s quill! Give me Vesuvius’ crater for an inkstand! Friends, hold my arms! For in the mere act of penning my thoughts of this Leviathan, they weary me, and make me faint with their outreaching comprehensiveness of sweep, as if to include the whole circle of the sciences, and all the generations of whales, and men, and mastodons, past, present, and to come, with all the revolving panoramas of empire on earth, and throughout the whole universe, not excluding its suburbs. Such, and so magnifying, is the virtue of a large and liberal theme! We expand to its bulk. To produce a mighty book, you must choose a mighty theme. No great and enduring volume can ever be written on the flea, though many there be who have tried it.\n\nEre entering upon the subject of Fossil Whales, I present my credentials as a geologist, by stating that in my miscellaneous time I have been a stone-mason, and also a great digger of ditches, canals and wells, wine-vaults, cellars, and cisterns of all sorts. Likewise, by way of preliminary, I desire to remind the reader, that while in the earlier geological strata there are found the fossils of monsters now almost completely extinct; the subsequent relics discovered in what are called the Tertiary formations seem the connecting, or at any rate intercepted links, between the antichronical creatures, and those whose remote posterity are said to have entered the Ark; all the Fossil Whales hitherto discovered belong to the Tertiary period, which is the last preceding the superficial formations. And though none of them precisely answer to any known species of the present time, they are yet sufficiently akin to them in general respects, to justify their taking rank as Cetacean fossils.\n\nDetached broken fossils of pre-adamite whales, fragments of their bones and skeletons, have within thirty years past, at various intervals, been found at the base of the Alps, in Lombardy, in France, in England, in Scotland, and in the States of Louisiana, Mississippi, and Alabama. Among the more curious of such remains is part of a skull, which in the year 1779 was disinterred in the Rue Dauphine in Paris, a short street opening almost directly upon the palace of the Tuileries; and bones disinterred in excavating the great docks of Antwerp, in Napoleon’s time. Cuvier pronounced these fragments to have belonged to some utterly unknown Leviathanic species.\n\nBut by far the most wonderful of all Cetacean relics was the almost complete vast skeleton of an extinct monster, found in the year 1842, on the plantation of Judge Creagh, in Alabama. The awe-stricken credulous slaves in the vicinity took it for the bones of one of the fallen angels. The Alabama doctors declared it a huge reptile, and bestowed upon it the name of Basilosaurus. But some specimen bones of it being taken across the sea to Owen, the English Anatomist, it turned out that this alleged reptile was a whale, though of a departed species. A significant illustration of the fact, again and again repeated in this book, that the skeleton of the whale furnishes but little clue to the shape of his fully invested body. So Owen rechristened the monster Zeuglodon; and in his paper read before the London Geological Society, pronounced it, in substance, one of the most extraordinary creatures which the mutations of the globe have blotted out of existence.\n\nWhen I stand among these mighty Leviathan skeletons, skulls, tusks, jaws, ribs, and vertebræ, all characterized by partial resemblances to the existing breeds of sea-monsters; but at the same time bearing on the other hand similar affinities to the annihilated antichronical Leviathans, their incalculable seniors; I am, by a flood, borne back to that wondrous period, ere time itself can be said to have begun; for time began with man. Here Saturn’s grey chaos rolls over me, and I obtain dim, shuddering glimpses into those Polar eternities; when wedged bastions of ice pressed hard upon what are now the Tropics; and in all the 25,000 miles of this world’s circumference, not an inhabitable hand’s breadth of land was visible. Then the whole world was the whale’s; and, king of creation, he left his wake along the present lines of the Andes and the Himmalehs. Who can show a pedigree like Leviathan? Ahab’s harpoon had shed older blood than the Pharaoh’s. Methuselah seems a school-boy. I look round to shake hands with Shem. I am horror-struck at this antemosaic, unsourced existence of the unspeakable terrors of the whale, which, having been before all time, must needs exist after all humane ages are over.\n\nBut not alone has this Leviathan left his pre-adamite traces in the stereotype plates of nature, and in limestone and marl bequeathed his ancient bust; but upon Egyptian tablets, whose antiquity seems to claim for them an almost fossiliferous character, we find the unmistakable print of his fin. In an apartment of the great temple of Denderah, some fifty years ago, there was discovered upon the granite ceiling a sculptured and painted planisphere, abounding in centaurs, griffins, and dolphins, similar to the grotesque figures on the celestial globe of the moderns. Gliding among them, old Leviathan swam as of yore; was there swimming in that planisphere, centuries before Solomon was cradled.\n\nNor must there be omitted another strange attestation of the antiquity of the whale, in his own osseous post-diluvian reality, as set down by the venerable John Leo, the old Barbary traveller.\n\n“Not far from the Sea-side, they have a Temple, the Rafters and Beams of which are made of Whale-Bones; for Whales of a monstrous size are oftentimes cast up dead upon that shore. The Common People imagine, that by a secret Power bestowed by God upon the Temple, no Whale can pass it without immediate death. But the truth of the Matter is, that on either side of the Temple, there are Rocks that shoot two Miles into the Sea, and wound the Whales when they light upon ’em. They keep a Whale’s Rib of an incredible length for a Miracle, which lying upon the Ground with its convex part uppermost, makes an Arch, the Head of which cannot be reached by a Man upon a Camel’s Back. This Rib (says John Leo) is said to have layn there a hundred Years before I saw it. Their Historians affirm, that a Prophet who prophesy’d of Mahomet, came from this Temple, and some do not stand to assert, that the Prophet Jonas was cast forth by the Whale at the Base of the Temple.”\n\nIn this Afric Temple of the Whale I leave you, reader, and if you be a Nantucketer, and a whaleman, you will silently worship there.\n\n# CHAPTER 105. Does the Whale’s Magnitude Diminish?—Will He Perish?\n\nInasmuch, then, as this Leviathan comes floundering down upon us from the head-waters of the Eternities, it may be fitly inquired, whether, in the long course of his generations, he has not degenerated from the original bulk of his sires.\n\nBut upon investigation we find, that not only are the whales of the present day superior in magnitude to those whose fossil remains are found in the Tertiary system (embracing a distinct geological period prior to man), but of the whales found in that Tertiary system, those belonging to its latter formations exceed in size those of its earlier ones.\n\nOf all the pre-adamite whales yet exhumed, by far the largest is the Alabama one mentioned in the last chapter, and that was less than seventy feet in length in the skeleton. Whereas, we have already seen, that the tape-measure gives seventy-two feet for the skeleton of a large sized modern whale. And I have heard, on whalemen’s authority, that Sperm Whales have been captured near a hundred feet long at the time of capture.\n\nBut may it not be, that while the whales of the present hour are an advance in magnitude upon those of all previous geological periods; may it not be, that since Adam’s time they have degenerated?\n\nAssuredly, we must conclude so, if we are to credit the accounts of such gentlemen as Pliny, and the ancient naturalists generally. For Pliny tells us of whales that embraced acres of living bulk, and Aldrovandus of others which measured eight hundred feet in length—Rope Walks and Thames Tunnels of Whales! And even in the days of Banks and Solander, Cooke’s naturalists, we find a Danish member of the Academy of Sciences setting down certain Iceland Whales (reydan-siskur, or Wrinkled Bellies) at one hundred and twenty yards; that is, three hundred and sixty feet. And Lacépède, the French naturalist, in his elaborate history of whales, in the very beginning of his work (page 3), sets down the Right Whale at one hundred metres, three hundred and twenty-eight feet. And this work was published so late as A.D. 1825.\n\nBut will any whaleman believe these stories? No. The whale of to-day is as big as his ancestors in Pliny’s time. And if ever I go where Pliny is, I, a whaleman (more than he was), will make bold to tell him so. Because I cannot understand how it is, that while the Egyptian mummies that were buried thousands of years before even Pliny was born, do not measure so much in their coffins as a modern Kentuckian in his socks; and while the cattle and other animals sculptured on the oldest Egyptian and Nineveh tablets, by the relative proportions in which they are drawn, just as plainly prove that the high-bred, stall-fed, prize cattle of Smithfield, not only equal, but far exceed in magnitude the fattest of Pharaoh’s fat kine; in the face of all this, I will not admit that of all animals the whale alone should have degenerated.\n\nBut still another inquiry remains; one often agitated by the more recondite Nantucketers. Whether owing to the almost omniscient look-outs at the mast-heads of the whale-ships, now penetrating even through Behring’s straits, and into the remotest secret drawers and lockers of the world; and the thousand harpoons and lances darted along all continental coasts; the moot point is, whether Leviathan can long endure so wide a chase, and so remorseless a havoc; whether he must not at last be exterminated from the waters, and the last whale, like the last man, smoke his last pipe, and then himself evaporate in the final puff.\n\nComparing the humped herds of whales with the humped herds of buffalo, which, not forty years ago, overspread by tens of thousands the prairies of Illinois and Missouri, and shook their iron manes and scowled with their thunder-clotted brows upon the sites of populous river-capitals, where now the polite broker sells you land at a dollar an inch; in such a comparison an irresistible argument would seem furnished, to show that the hunted whale cannot now escape speedy extinction.\n\nBut you must look at this matter in every light. Though so short a period ago—not a good lifetime—the census of the buffalo in Illinois exceeded the census of men now in London, and though at the present day not one horn or hoof of them remains in all that region; and though the cause of this wondrous extermination was the spear of man; yet the far different nature of the whale-hunt peremptorily forbids so inglorious an end to the Leviathan. Forty men in one ship hunting the Sperm Whales for forty-eight months think they have done extremely well, and thank God, if at last they carry home the oil of forty fish. Whereas, in the days of the old Canadian and Indian hunters and trappers of the West, when the far west (in whose sunset suns still rise) was a wilderness and a virgin, the same number of moccasined men, for the same number of months, mounted on horse instead of sailing in ships, would have slain not forty, but forty thousand and more buffaloes; a fact that, if need were, could be statistically stated.\n\nNor, considered aright, does it seem any argument in favour of the gradual extinction of the Sperm Whale, for example, that in former years (the latter part of the last century, say) these Leviathans, in small pods, were encountered much oftener than at present, and, in consequence, the voyages were not so prolonged, and were also much more remunerative. Because, as has been elsewhere noticed, those whales, influenced by some views to safety, now swim the seas in immense caravans, so that to a large degree the scattered solitaries, yokes, and pods, and schools of other days are now aggregated into vast but widely separated, unfrequent armies. That is all. And equally fallacious seems the conceit, that because the so-called whale-bone whales no longer haunt many grounds in former years abounding with them, hence that species also is declining. For they are only being driven from promontory to cape; and if one coast is no longer enlivened with their jets, then, be sure, some other and remoter strand has been very recently startled by the unfamiliar spectacle.\n\nFurthermore: concerning these last mentioned Leviathans, they have two firm fortresses, which, in all human probability, will for ever remain impregnable. And as upon the invasion of their valleys, the frosty Swiss have retreated to their mountains; so, hunted from the savannas and glades of the middle seas, the whale-bone whales can at last resort to their Polar citadels, and diving under the ultimate glassy barriers and walls there, come up among icy fields and floes; and in a charmed circle of everlasting December, bid defiance to all pursuit from man.\n\nBut as perhaps fifty of these whale-bone whales are harpooned for one cachalot, some philosophers of the forecastle have concluded that this positive havoc has already very seriously diminished their battalions. But though for some time past a number of these whales, not less than 13,000, have been annually slain on the nor’ west coast by the Americans alone; yet there are considerations which render even this circumstance of little or no account as an opposing argument in this matter.\n\nNatural as it is to be somewhat incredulous concerning the populousness of the more enormous creatures of the globe, yet what shall we say to Harto, the historian of Goa, when he tells us that at one hunting the King of Siam took 4,000 elephants; that in those regions elephants are numerous as droves of cattle in the temperate climes. And there seems no reason to doubt that if these elephants, which have now been hunted for thousands of years, by Semiramis, by Porus, by Hannibal, and by all the successive monarchs of the East—if they still survive there in great numbers, much more may the great whale outlast all hunting, since he has a pasture to expatiate in, which is precisely twice as large as all Asia, both Americas, Europe and Africa, New Holland, and all the Isles of the sea combined.\n\nMoreover: we are to consider, that from the presumed great longevity of whales, their probably attaining the age of a century and more, therefore at any one period of time, several distinct adult generations must be contemporary. And what that is, we may soon gain some idea of, by imagining all the grave-yards, cemeteries, and family vaults of creation yielding up the live bodies of all the men, women, and children who were alive seventy-five years ago; and adding this countless host to the present human population of the globe.\n\nWherefore, for all these things, we account the whale immortal in his species, however perishable in his individuality. He swam the seas before the continents broke water; he once swam over the site of the Tuileries, and Windsor Castle, and the Kremlin. In Noah’s flood he despised Noah’s Ark; and if ever the world is to be again flooded, like the Netherlands, to kill off its rats, then the eternal whale will still survive, and rearing upon the topmost crest of the equatorial flood, spout his frothed defiance to the skies.\n\n# CHAPTER 106. Ahab’s Leg.\n\nThe precipitating manner in which Captain Ahab had quitted the Samuel Enderby of London, had not been unattended with some small violence to his own person. He had lighted with such energy upon a thwart of his boat that his ivory leg had received a half-splintering shock. And when after gaining his own deck, and his own pivot-hole there, he so vehemently wheeled round with an urgent command to the steersman (it was, as ever, something about his not steering inflexibly enough); then, the already shaken ivory received such an additional twist and wrench, that though it still remained entire, and to all appearances lusty, yet Ahab did not deem it entirely trustworthy.\n\nAnd, indeed, it seemed small matter for wonder, that for all his pervading, mad recklessness, Ahab did at times give careful heed to the condition of that dead bone upon which he partly stood. For it had not been very long prior to the Pequod’s sailing from Nantucket, that he had been found one night lying prone upon the ground, and insensible; by some unknown, and seemingly inexplicable, unimaginable casualty, his ivory limb having been so violently displaced, that it had stake-wise smitten, and all but pierced his groin; nor was it without extreme difficulty that the agonizing wound was entirely cured.\n\nNor, at the time, had it failed to enter his monomaniac mind, that all the anguish of that then present suffering was but the direct issue of a former woe; and he too plainly seemed to see, that as the most poisonous reptile of the marsh perpetuates his kind as inevitably as the sweetest songster of the grove; so, equally with every felicity, all miserable events do naturally beget their like. Yea, more than equally, thought Ahab; since both the ancestry and posterity of Grief go further than the ancestry and posterity of Joy. For, not to hint of this: that it is an inference from certain canonic teachings, that while some natural enjoyments here shall have no children born to them for the other world, but, on the contrary, shall be followed by the joy-childlessness of all hell’s despair; whereas, some guilty mortal miseries shall still fertilely beget to themselves an eternally progressive progeny of griefs beyond the grave; not at all to hint of this, there still seems an inequality in the deeper analysis of the thing. For, thought Ahab, while even the highest earthly felicities ever have a certain unsignifying pettiness lurking in them, but, at bottom, all heartwoes, a mystic significance, and, in some men, an archangelic grandeur; so do their diligent tracings-out not belie the obvious deduction. To trail the genealogies of these high mortal miseries, carries us at last among the sourceless primogenitures of the gods; so that, in the face of all the glad, hay-making suns, and soft cymballing, round harvest-moons, we must needs give in to this: that the gods themselves are not for ever glad. The ineffaceable, sad birth-mark in the brow of man, is but the stamp of sorrow in the signers.\n\nUnwittingly here a secret has been divulged, which perhaps might more properly, in set way, have been disclosed before. With many other particulars concerning Ahab, always had it remained a mystery to some, why it was, that for a certain period, both before and after the sailing of the Pequod, he had hidden himself away with such Grand-Lama-like exclusiveness; and, for that one interval, sought speechless refuge, as it were, among the marble senate of the dead. Captain Peleg’s bruited reason for this thing appeared by no means adequate; though, indeed, as touching all Ahab’s deeper part, every revelation partook more of significant darkness than of explanatory light. But, in the end, it all came out; this one matter did, at least. That direful mishap was at the bottom of his temporary recluseness. And not only this, but to that ever-contracting, dropping circle ashore, who, for any reason, possessed the privilege of a less banned approach to him; to that timid circle the above hinted casualty—remaining, as it did, moodily unaccounted for by Ahab—invested itself with terrors, not entirely underived from the land of spirits and of wails. So that, through their zeal for him, they had all conspired, so far as in them lay, to muffle up the knowledge of this thing from others; and hence it was, that not till a considerable interval had elapsed, did it transpire upon the Pequod’s decks.\n\nBut be all this as it may; let the unseen, ambiguous synod in the air, or the vindictive princes and potentates of fire, have to do or not with earthly Ahab, yet, in this present matter of his leg, he took plain practical procedures;—he called the carpenter.\n\nAnd when that functionary appeared before him, he bade him without delay set about making a new leg, and directed the mates to see him supplied with all the studs and joists of jaw-ivory (Sperm Whale) which had thus far been accumulated on the voyage, in order that a careful selection of the stoutest, clearest-grained stuff might be secured. This done, the carpenter received orders to have the leg completed that night; and to provide all the fittings for it, independent of those pertaining to the distrusted one in use. Moreover, the ship’s forge was ordered to be hoisted out of its temporary idleness in the hold; and, to accelerate the affair, the blacksmith was commanded to proceed at once to the forging of whatever iron contrivances might be needed.\n\n# CHAPTER 107. The Carpenter.\n\nSeat thyself sultanically among the moons of Saturn, and take high abstracted man alone; and he seems a wonder, a grandeur, and a woe. But from the same point, take mankind in mass, and for the most part, they seem a mob of unnecessary duplicates, both contemporary and hereditary. But most humble though he was, and far from furnishing an example of the high, humane abstraction; the Pequod’s carpenter was no duplicate; hence, he now comes in person on this stage.\n\nLike all sea-going ship carpenters, and more especially those belonging to whaling vessels, he was, to a certain off-handed, practical extent, alike experienced in numerous trades and callings collateral to his own; the carpenter’s pursuit being the ancient and outbranching trunk of all those numerous handicrafts which more or less have to do with wood as an auxiliary material. But, besides the application to him of the generic remark above, this carpenter of the Pequod was singularly efficient in those thousand nameless mechanical emergencies continually recurring in a large ship, upon a three or four years’ voyage, in uncivilized and far-distant seas. For not to speak of his readiness in ordinary duties:—repairing stove boats, sprung spars, reforming the shape of clumsy-bladed oars, inserting bull’s eyes in the deck, or new tree-nails in the side planks, and other miscellaneous matters more directly pertaining to his special business; he was moreover unhesitatingly expert in all manner of conflicting aptitudes, both useful and capricious.\n\nThe one grand stage where he enacted all his various parts so manifold, was his vice-bench; a long rude ponderous table furnished with several vices, of different sizes, and both of iron and of wood. At all times except when whales were alongside, this bench was securely lashed athwartships against the rear of the Try-works.\n\nA belaying pin is found too large to be easily inserted into its hole: the carpenter claps it into one of his ever-ready vices, and straightway files it smaller. A lost land-bird of strange plumage strays on board, and is made a captive: out of clean shaved rods of right-whale bone, and cross-beams of sperm whale ivory, the carpenter makes a pagoda-looking cage for it. An oarsman sprains his wrist: the carpenter concocts a soothing lotion. Stubb longed for vermillion stars to be painted upon the blade of his every oar; screwing each oar in his big vice of wood, the carpenter symmetrically supplies the constellation. A sailor takes a fancy to wear shark-bone ear-rings: the carpenter drills his ears. Another has the toothache: the carpenter out pincers, and clapping one hand upon his bench bids him be seated there; but the poor fellow unmanageably winces under the unconcluded operation; whirling round the handle of his wooden vice, the carpenter signs him to clap his jaw in that, if he would have him draw the tooth.\n\nThus, this carpenter was prepared at all points, and alike indifferent and without respect in all. Teeth he accounted bits of ivory; heads he deemed but top-blocks; men themselves he lightly held for capstans. But while now upon so wide a field thus variously accomplished and with such liveliness of expertness in him, too; all this would seem to argue some uncommon vivacity of intelligence. But not precisely so. For nothing was this man more remarkable, than for a certain impersonal stolidity as it were; impersonal, I say; for it so shaded off into the surrounding infinite of things, that it seemed one with the general stolidity discernible in the whole visible world; which while pauselessly active in uncounted modes, still eternally holds its peace, and ignores you, though you dig foundations for cathedrals. Yet was this half-horrible stolidity in him, involving, too, as it appeared, an all-ramifying heartlessness;—yet was it oddly dashed at times, with an old, crutch-like, antediluvian, wheezing humorousness, not unstreaked now and then with a certain grizzled wittiness; such as might have served to pass the time during the midnight watch on the bearded forecastle of Noah’s ark. Was it that this old carpenter had been a life-long wanderer, whose much rolling, to and fro, not only had gathered no moss; but what is more, had rubbed off whatever small outward clingings might have originally pertained to him? He was a stript abstract; an unfractioned integral; uncompromised as a new-born babe; living without premeditated reference to this world or the next. You might almost say, that this strange uncompromisedness in him involved a sort of unintelligence; for in his numerous trades, he did not seem to work so much by reason or by instinct, or simply because he had been tutored to it, or by any intermixture of all these, even or uneven; but merely by a kind of deaf and dumb, spontaneous literal process. He was a pure manipulator; his brain, if he had ever had one, must have early oozed along into the muscles of his fingers. He was like one of those unreasoning but still highly useful, multum in parvo, Sheffield contrivances, assuming the exterior—though a little swelled—of a common pocket knife; but containing, not only blades of various sizes, but also screw-drivers, cork-screws, tweezers, awls, pens, rulers, nail-filers, countersinkers. So, if his superiors wanted to use the carpenter for a screw-driver, all they had to do was to open that part of him, and the screw was fast: or if for tweezers, take him up by the legs, and there they were.\n\nYet, as previously hinted, this omnitooled, open-and-shut carpenter, was, after all, no mere machine of an automaton. If he did not have a common soul in him, he had a subtle something that somehow anomalously did its duty. What that was, whether essence of quicksilver, or a few drops of hartshorn, there is no telling. But there it was; and there it had abided for now some sixty years or more. And this it was, this same unaccountable, cunning life-principle in him; this it was, that kept him a great part of the time soliloquizing; but only like an unreasoning wheel, which also hummingly soliloquizes; or rather, his body was a sentry-box and this soliloquizer on guard there, and talking all the time to keep himself awake.\n\nThe Deck—First Night Watch.\n\n(Carpenter standing before his vice-bench, and by the light of two lanterns busily filing the ivory joist for the leg, which joist is firmly fixed in the vice. Slabs of ivory, leather straps, pads, screws, and various tools of all sorts lying about the bench. Forward, the red flame of the forge is seen, where the blacksmith is at work.)\n\nDrat the file, and drat the bone! That is hard which should be soft, and that is soft which should be hard. So we go, who file old jaws and shinbones. Let’s try another. Aye, now, this works better (sneezes). Halloa, this bone dust is (sneezes)—why it’s (sneezes)—yes it’s (sneezes)—bless my soul, it won’t let me speak! This is what an old fellow gets now for working in dead lumber. Saw a live tree, and you don’t get this dust; amputate a live bone, and you don’t get it (sneezes). Come, come, you old Smut, there, bear a hand, and let’s have that ferule and buckle-screw; I’ll be ready for them presently. Lucky now (sneezes) there’s no knee-joint to make; that might puzzle a little; but a mere shinbone—why it’s easy as making hop-poles; only I should like to put a good finish on. Time, time; if I but only had the time, I could turn him out as neat a leg now as ever (sneezes) scraped to a lady in a parlor. Those buckskin legs and calves of legs I’ve seen in shop windows wouldn’t compare at all. They soak water, they do; and of course get rheumatic, and have to be doctored (sneezes) with washes and lotions, just like live legs. There; before I saw it off, now, I must call his old Mogulship, and see whether the length will be all right; too short, if anything, I guess. Ha! that’s the heel; we are in luck; here he comes, or it’s somebody else, that’s certain.\n\nAHAB (advancing). (During the ensuing scene, the carpenter continues sneezing at times.)\n\nWell, manmaker!\n\nJust in time, sir. If the captain pleases, I will now mark the length. Let me measure, sir.\n\nMeasured for a leg! good. Well, it’s not the first time. About it! There; keep thy finger on it. This is a cogent vice thou hast here, carpenter; let me feel its grip once. So, so; it does pinch some.\n\nOh, sir, it will break bones—beware, beware!\n\nNo fear; I like a good grip; I like to feel something in this slippery world that can hold, man. What’s Prometheus about there?—the blacksmith, I mean—what’s he about?\n\nHe must be forging the buckle-screw, sir, now.\n\nRight. It’s a partnership; he supplies the muscle part. He makes a fierce red flame there!\n\nAye, sir; he must have the white heat for this kind of fine work.\n\nUm-m. So he must. I do deem it now a most meaning thing, that that old Greek, Prometheus, who made men, they say, should have been a blacksmith, and animated them with fire; for what’s made in fire must properly belong to fire; and so hell’s probable. How the soot flies! This must be the remainder the Greek made the Africans of. Carpenter, when he’s through with that buckle, tell him to forge a pair of steel shoulder-blades; there’s a pedlar aboard with a crushing pack.\n\nSir?\nHold; while Prometheus is about it, I’ll order a complete man after a desirable pattern. Imprimis, fifty feet high in his socks; then, chest modelled after the Thames Tunnel; then, legs with roots to ’em, to stay in one place; then, arms three feet through the wrist; no heart at all, brass forehead, and about a quarter of an acre of fine brains; and let me see—shall I order eyes to see outwards? No, but put a sky-light on top of his head to illuminate inwards. There, take the order, and away.\n\nNow, what’s he speaking about, and who’s he speaking to, I should like to know? Shall I keep standing here? (aside).\n\n’Tis but indifferent architecture to make a blind dome; here’s one. No, no, no; I must have a lantern.\n\nHo, ho! That’s it, hey? Here are two, sir; one will serve my turn.\n\nWhat art thou thrusting that thief-catcher into my face for, man? Thrusted light is worse than presented pistols.\n\nI thought, sir, that you spoke to carpenter.\n\nCarpenter? why that’s—but no;—a very tidy, and, I may say, an extremely gentlemanlike sort of business thou art in here, carpenter;—or would’st thou rather work in clay?\n\nSir?—Clay? clay, sir? That’s mud; we leave clay to ditchers, sir.\n\nThe fellow’s impious! What art thou sneezing about?\n\nBone is rather dusty, sir.\n\nTake the hint, then; and when thou art dead, never bury thyself under living people’s noses.\n\nSir?—oh! ah!—I guess so;—yes—oh, dear!\n\nLook ye, carpenter, I dare say thou callest thyself a right good workmanlike workman, eh? Well, then, will it speak thoroughly well for thy work, if, when I come to mount this leg thou makest, I shall nevertheless feel another leg in the same identical place with it; that is, carpenter, my old lost leg; the flesh and blood one, I mean. Canst thou not drive that old Adam away?\n\nTruly, sir, I begin to understand somewhat now. Yes, I have heard something curious on that score, sir; how that a dismasted man never entirely loses the feeling of his old spar, but it will be still pricking him at times. May I humbly ask if it be really so, sir?\n\nIt is, man. Look, put thy live leg here in the place where mine once was; so, now, here is only one distinct leg to the eye, yet two to the soul. Where thou feelest tingling life; there, exactly there, there to a hair, do I. Is’t a riddle?\n\nI should humbly call it a poser, sir.\n\nHist, then. How dost thou know that some entire, living, thinking thing may not be invisibly and uninterpenetratingly standing precisely where thou now standest; aye, and standing there in thy spite? In thy most solitary hours, then, dost thou not fear eavesdroppers? Hold, don’t speak! And if I still feel the smart of my crushed leg, though it be now so long dissolved; then, why mayst not thou, carpenter, feel the fiery pains of hell for ever, and without a body? Hah!\n\nGood Lord! Truly, sir, if it comes to that, I must calculate over again; I think I didn’t carry a small figure, sir.\n\nLook ye, pudding-heads should never grant premises.—How long before the leg is done?\n\nPerhaps an hour, sir.\n\nBungle away at it then, and bring it to me (turns to go). Oh, Life! Here I am, proud as Greek god, and yet standing debtor to this blockhead for a bone to stand on! Cursed be that mortal inter-indebtedness which will not do away with ledgers. I would be free as air; and I’m down in the whole world’s books. I am so rich, I could have given bid for bid with the wealthiest Prætorians at the auction of the Roman empire (which was the world’s); and yet I owe for the flesh in the tongue I brag with. By heavens! I’ll get a crucible, and into it, and dissolve myself down to one small, compendious vertebra. So.\n\nCARPENTER (resuming his work).\n\nWell, well, well! Stubb knows him best of all, and Stubb always says he’s queer; says nothing but that one sufficient little word queer; he’s queer, says Stubb; he’s queer—queer, queer; and keeps dinning it into Mr. Starbuck all the time—queer—sir—queer, queer, very queer. And here’s his leg! Yes, now that I think of it, here’s his bedfellow! has a stick of whale’s jaw-bone for a wife! And this is his leg; he’ll stand on this. What was that now about one leg standing in three places, and all three places standing in one hell—how was that? Oh! I don’t wonder he looked so scornful at me! I’m a sort of strange-thoughted sometimes, they say; but that’s only haphazard-like. Then, a short, little old body like me, should never undertake to wade out into deep waters with tall, heron-built captains; the water chucks you under the chin pretty quick, and there’s a great cry for life-boats. And here’s the heron’s leg! long and slim, sure enough! Now, for most folks one pair of legs lasts a lifetime, and that must be because they use them mercifully, as a tender-hearted old lady uses her roly-poly old coach-horses. But Ahab; oh he’s a hard driver. Look, driven one leg to death, and spavined the other for life, and now wears out bone legs by the cord. Halloa, there, you Smut! bear a hand there with those screws, and let’s finish it before the resurrection fellow comes a-calling with his horn for all legs, true or false, as brewery-men go round collecting old beer barrels, to fill ’em up again. What a leg this is! It looks like a real live leg, filed down to nothing but the core; he’ll be standing on this to-morrow; he’ll be taking altitudes on it. Halloa! I almost forgot the little oval slate, smoothed ivory, where he figures up the latitude. So, so; chisel, file, and sand-paper, now!\n\n# CHAPTER 109. Ahab and Starbuck in the Cabin.\n\nAccording to usage they were pumping the ship next morning; and lo! no inconsiderable oil came up with the water; the casks below must have sprung a bad leak. Much concern was shown; and Starbuck went down into the cabin to report this unfavourable affair.*\n\n*In Sperm-whalemen with any considerable quantity of oil on board, it is a regular semi-weekly duty to conduct a hose into the hold, and drench the casks with sea-water; which afterwards, at varying intervals, is removed by the ship’s pumps. Hereby the casks are sought to be kept damply tight; while by the changed character of the withdrawn water, the mariners readily detect any serious leakage in the precious cargo.\n\nNow, from the South and West the Pequod was drawing nigh to Formosa and the Bashee Isles, between which lies one of the tropical outlets from the China waters into the Pacific. And so Starbuck found Ahab with a general chart of the oriental archipelagoes spread before him; and another separate one representing the long eastern coasts of the Japanese islands—Niphon, Matsmai, and Sikoke. With his snow-white new ivory leg braced against the screwed leg of his table, and with a long pruning-hook of a jack-knife in his hand, the wondrous old man, with his back to the gangway door, was wrinkling his brow, and tracing his old courses again.\n\n“Who’s there?” hearing the footstep at the door, but not turning round to it. “On deck! Begone!”\n\n“Captain Ahab mistakes; it is I. The oil in the hold is leaking, sir. We must up Burtons and break out.”\n\n“Up Burtons and break out? Now that we are nearing Japan; heave-to here for a week to tinker a parcel of old hoops?”\n\n“Either do that, sir, or waste in one day more oil than we may make good in a year. What we come twenty thousand miles to get is worth saving, sir.”\n\n“So it is, so it is; if we get it.”\n\n“I was speaking of the oil in the hold, sir.”\n\n“And I was not speaking or thinking of that at all. Begone! Let it leak! I’m all aleak myself. Aye! leaks in leaks! not only full of leaky casks, but those leaky casks are in a leaky ship; and that’s a far worse plight than the Pequod’s, man. Yet I don’t stop to plug my leak; for who can find it in the deep-loaded hull; or how hope to plug it, even if found, in this life’s howling gale? Starbuck! I’ll not have the Burtons hoisted.”\n\n“What will the owners say, sir?”\n\n“Let the owners stand on Nantucket beach and outyell the Typhoons. What cares Ahab? Owners, owners? Thou art always prating to me, Starbuck, about those miserly owners, as if the owners were my conscience. But look ye, the only real owner of anything is its commander; and hark ye, my conscience is in this ship’s keel.—On deck!”\n\n“Captain Ahab,” said the reddening mate, moving further into the cabin, with a daring so strangely respectful and cautious that it almost seemed not only every way seeking to avoid the slightest outward manifestation of itself, but within also seemed more than half distrustful of itself; “A better man than I might well pass over in thee what he would quickly enough resent in a younger man; aye, and in a happier, Captain Ahab.”\n\n“Devils! Dost thou then so much as dare to critically think of me?—On deck!”\n\n“Nay, sir, not yet; I do entreat. And I do dare, sir—to be forbearing! Shall we not understand each other better than hitherto, Captain Ahab?”\n\nAhab seized a loaded musket from the rack (forming part of most South-Sea-men’s cabin furniture), and pointing it towards Starbuck, exclaimed: “There is one God that is Lord over the earth, and one Captain that is lord over the Pequod.—On deck!”\n\nFor an instant in the flashing eyes of the mate, and his fiery cheeks, you would have almost thought that he had really received the blaze of the levelled tube. But, mastering his emotion, he half calmly rose, and as he quitted the cabin, paused for an instant and said: “Thou hast outraged, not insulted me, sir; but for that I ask thee not to beware of Starbuck; thou wouldst but laugh; but let Ahab beware of Ahab; beware of thyself, old man.”\n\n“He waxes brave, but nevertheless obeys; most careful bravery that!” murmured Ahab, as Starbuck disappeared. “What’s that he said—Ahab beware of Ahab—there’s something there!” Then unconsciously using the musket for a staff, with an iron brow he paced to and fro in the little cabin; but presently the thick plaits of his forehead relaxed, and returning the gun to the rack, he went to the deck.\n\n“Thou art but too good a fellow, Starbuck,” he said lowly to the mate; then raising his voice to the crew: “Furl the t’gallant-sails, and close-reef the top-sails, fore and aft; back the main-yard; up Burton, and break out in the main-hold.”\n\nIt were perhaps vain to surmise exactly why it was, that as respecting Starbuck, Ahab thus acted. It may have been a flash of honesty in him; or mere prudential policy which, under the circumstance, imperiously forbade the slightest symptom of open disaffection, however transient, in the important chief officer of his ship. However it was, his orders were executed; and the Burtons were hoisted.\n\n# CHAPTER 110. Queequeg in His Coffin.\n\nUpon searching, it was found that the casks last struck into the hold were perfectly sound, and that the leak must be further off. So, it being calm weather, they broke out deeper and deeper, disturbing the slumbers of the huge ground-tier butts; and from that black midnight sending those gigantic moles into the daylight above. So deep did they go; and so ancient, and corroded, and weedy the aspect of the lowermost puncheons, that you almost looked next for some mouldy corner-stone cask containing coins of Captain Noah, with copies of the posted placards, vainly warning the infatuated old world from the flood. Tierce after tierce, too, of water, and bread, and beef, and shooks of staves, and iron bundles of hoops, were hoisted out, till at last the piled decks were hard to get about; and the hollow hull echoed under foot, as if you were treading over empty catacombs, and reeled and rolled in the sea like an air-freighted demijohn. Top-heavy was the ship as a dinnerless student with all Aristotle in his head. Well was it that the Typhoons did not visit them then.\n\nNow, at this time it was that my poor pagan companion, and fast bosom-friend, Queequeg, was seized with a fever, which brought him nigh to his endless end.\n\nBe it said, that in this vocation of whaling, sinecures are unknown; dignity and danger go hand in hand; till you get to be Captain, the higher you rise the harder you toil. So with poor Queequeg, who, as harpooneer, must not only face all the rage of the living whale, but—as we have elsewhere seen—mount his dead back in a rolling sea; and finally descend into the gloom of the hold, and bitterly sweating all day in that subterraneous confinement, resolutely manhandle the clumsiest casks and see to their stowage. To be short, among whalemen, the harpooneers are the holders, so called.\n\nPoor Queequeg! when the ship was about half disembowelled, you should have stooped over the hatchway, and peered down upon him there; where, stripped to his woollen drawers, the tattooed savage was crawling about amid that dampness and slime, like a green spotted lizard at the bottom of a well. And a well, or an ice-house, it somehow proved to him, poor pagan; where, strange to say, for all the heat of his sweatings, he caught a terrible chill which lapsed into a fever; and at last, after some days’ suffering, laid him in his hammock, close to the very sill of the door of death. How he wasted and wasted away in those few long-lingering days, till there seemed but little left of him but his frame and tattooing. But as all else in him thinned, and his cheek-bones grew sharper, his eyes, nevertheless, seemed growing fuller and fuller; they became of a strange softness of lustre; and mildly but deeply looked out at you there from his sickness, a wondrous testimony to that immortal health in him which could not die, or be weakened. And like circles on the water, which, as they grow fainter, expand; so his eyes seemed rounding and rounding, like the rings of Eternity. An awe that cannot be named would steal over you as you sat by the side of this waning savage, and saw as strange things in his face, as any beheld who were bystanders when Zoroaster died. For whatever is truly wondrous and fearful in man, never yet was put into words or books. And the drawing near of Death, which alike levels all, alike impresses all with a last revelation, which only an author from the dead could adequately tell. So that—let us say it again—no dying Chaldee or Greek had higher and holier thoughts than those, whose mysterious shades you saw creeping over the face of poor Queequeg, as he quietly lay in his swaying hammock, and the rolling sea seemed gently rocking him to his final rest, and the ocean’s invisible flood-tide lifted him higher and higher towards his destined heaven.\n\nNot a man of the crew but gave him up; and, as for Queequeg himself, what he thought of his case was forcibly shown by a curious favour he asked. He called one to him in the grey morning watch, when the day was just breaking, and taking his hand, said that while in Nantucket he had chanced to see certain little canoes of dark wood, like the rich war-wood of his native isle; and upon inquiry, he had learned that all whalemen who died in Nantucket, were laid in those same dark canoes, and that the fancy of being so laid had much pleased him; for it was not unlike the custom of his own race, who, after embalming a dead warrior, stretched him out in his canoe, and so left him to be floated away to the starry archipelagoes; for not only do they believe that the stars are isles, but that far beyond all visible horizons, their own mild, uncontinented seas, interflow with the blue heavens; and so form the white breakers of the milky way. He added, that he shuddered at the thought of being buried in his hammock, according to the usual sea-custom, tossed like something vile to the death-devouring sharks. No: he desired a canoe like those of Nantucket, all the more congenial to him, being a whaleman, that like a whale-boat these coffin-canoes were without a keel; though that involved but uncertain steering, and much lee-way adown the dim ages.\n\nNow, when this strange circumstance was made known aft, the carpenter was at once commanded to do Queequeg’s bidding, whatever it might include. There was some heathenish, coffin-coloured old lumber aboard, which, upon a long previous voyage, had been cut from the aboriginal groves of the Lackaday islands, and from these dark planks the coffin was recommended to be made. No sooner was the carpenter apprised of the order, than taking his rule, he forthwith with all the indifferent promptitude of his character, proceeded into the forecastle and took Queequeg’s measure with great accuracy, regularly chalking Queequeg’s person as he shifted the rule.\n\n“Ah! poor fellow! he’ll have to die now,” ejaculated the Long Island sailor.\n\nGoing to his vice-bench, the carpenter for convenience sake and general reference, now transferringly measured on it the exact length the coffin was to be, and then made the transfer permanent by cutting two notches at its extremities. This done, he marshalled the planks and his tools, and to work.\n\nWhen the last nail was driven, and the lid duly planed and fitted, he lightly shouldered the coffin and went forward with it, inquiring whether they were ready for it yet in that direction.\n\nOverhearing the indignant but half-humorous cries with which the people on deck began to drive the coffin away, Queequeg, to every one’s consternation, commanded that the thing should be instantly brought to him, nor was there any denying him; seeing that, of all mortals, some dying men are the most tyrannical; and certainly, since they will shortly trouble us so little for evermore, the poor fellows ought to be indulged.\n\nLeaning over in his hammock, Queequeg long regarded the coffin with an attentive eye. He then called for his harpoon, had the wooden stock drawn from it, and then had the iron part placed in the coffin along with one of the paddles of his boat. All by his own request, also, biscuits were then ranged round the sides within: a flask of fresh water was placed at the head, and a small bag of woody earth scraped up in the hold at the foot; and a piece of sail-cloth being rolled up for a pillow, Queequeg now entreated to be lifted into his final bed, that he might make trial of its comforts, if any it had. He lay without moving a few minutes, then told one to go to his bag and bring out his little god, Yojo. Then crossing his arms on his breast with Yojo between, he called for the coffin lid (hatch he called it) to be placed over him. The head part turned over with a leather hinge, and there lay Queequeg in his coffin with little but his composed countenance in view. “Rarmai” (it will do; it is easy), he murmured at last, and signed to be replaced in his hammock.\n\nBut ere this was done, Pip, who had been slily hovering near by all this while, drew nigh to him where he lay, and with soft sobbings, took him by the hand; in the other, holding his tambourine.\n\n“Poor rover! will ye never have done with all this weary roving? where go ye now? But if the currents carry ye to those sweet Antilles where the beaches are only beat with water-lilies, will ye do one little errand for me? Seek out one Pip, who’s now been missing long: I think he’s in those far Antilles. If ye find him, then comfort him; for he must be very sad; for look! he’s left his tambourine behind;—I found it. Rig-a-dig, dig, dig! Now, Queequeg, die; and I’ll beat ye your dying march.”\n\n“I have heard,” murmured Starbuck, gazing down the scuttle, “that in violent fevers, men, all ignorance, have talked in ancient tongues; and that when the mystery is probed, it turns out always that in their wholly forgotten childhood those ancient tongues had been really spoken in their hearing by some lofty scholars. So, to my fond faith, poor Pip, in this strange sweetness of his lunacy, brings heavenly vouchers of all our heavenly homes. Where learned he that, but there?—Hark! he speaks again: but more wildly now.”\n\n“Form two and two! Let’s make a General of him! Ho, where’s his harpoon? Lay it across here.—Rig-a-dig, dig, dig! huzza! Oh for a game cock now to sit upon his head and crow! Queequeg dies game!—mind ye that; Queequeg dies game!—take ye good heed of that; Queequeg dies game! I say; game, game, game! but base little Pip, he died a coward; died all a’shiver;—out upon Pip! Hark ye; if ye find Pip, tell all the Antilles he’s a runaway; a coward, a coward, a coward! Tell them he jumped from a whale-boat! I’d never beat my tambourine over base Pip, and hail him General, if he were once more dying here. No, no! shame upon all cowards—shame upon them! Let ’em go drown like Pip, that jumped from a whale-boat. Shame! shame!”\n\nDuring all this, Queequeg lay with closed eyes, as if in a dream. Pip was led away, and the sick man was replaced in his hammock.\n\nBut now that he had apparently made every preparation for death; now that his coffin was proved a good fit, Queequeg suddenly rallied; soon there seemed no need of the carpenter’s box: and thereupon, when some expressed their delighted surprise, he, in substance, said, that the cause of his sudden convalescence was this;—at a critical moment, he had just recalled a little duty ashore, which he was leaving undone; and therefore had changed his mind about dying: he could not die yet, he averred. They asked him, then, whether to live or die was a matter of his own sovereign will and pleasure. He answered, certainly. In a word, it was Queequeg’s conceit, that if a man made up his mind to live, mere sickness could not kill him: nothing but a whale, or a gale, or some violent, ungovernable, unintelligent destroyer of that sort.\n\nNow, there is this noteworthy difference between savage and civilized; that while a sick, civilized man may be six months convalescing, generally speaking, a sick savage is almost half-well again in a day. So, in good time my Queequeg gained strength; and at length after sitting on the windlass for a few indolent days (but eating with a vigorous appetite) he suddenly leaped to his feet, threw out his arms and legs, gave himself a good stretching, yawned a little bit, and then springing into the head of his hoisted boat, and poising a harpoon, pronounced himself fit for a fight.\n\nWith a wild whimsiness, he now used his coffin for a sea-chest; and emptying into it his canvas bag of clothes, set them in order there. Many spare hours he spent, in carving the lid with all manner of grotesque figures and drawings; and it seemed that hereby he was striving, in his rude way, to copy parts of the twisted tattooing on his body. And this tattooing had been the work of a departed prophet and seer of his island, who, by those hieroglyphic marks, had written out on his body a complete theory of the heavens and the earth, and a mystical treatise on the art of attaining truth; so that Queequeg in his own proper person was a riddle to unfold; a wondrous work in one volume; but whose mysteries not even himself could read, though his own live heart beat against them; and these mysteries were therefore destined in the end to moulder away with the living parchment whereon they were inscribed, and so be unsolved to the last. And this thought it must have been which suggested to Ahab that wild exclamation of his, when one morning turning away from surveying poor Queequeg—“Oh, devilish tantalization of the gods!”\n\n# CHAPTER 111. The Pacific.\n\nWhen gliding by the Bashee isles we emerged at last upon the great South Sea; were it not for other things, I could have greeted my dear Pacific with uncounted thanks, for now the long supplication of my youth was answered; that serene ocean rolled eastwards from me a thousand leagues of blue.\n\nThere is, one knows not what sweet mystery about this sea, whose gently awful stirrings seem to speak of some hidden soul beneath; like those fabled undulations of the Ephesian sod over the buried Evangelist St. John. And meet it is, that over these sea-pastures, wide-rolling watery prairies and Potters’ Fields of all four continents, the waves should rise and fall, and ebb and flow unceasingly; for here, millions of mixed shades and shadows, drowned dreams, somnambulisms, reveries; all that we call lives and souls, lie dreaming, dreaming, still; tossing like slumberers in their beds; the ever-rolling waves but made so by their restlessness.\n\nTo any meditative Magian rover, this serene Pacific, once beheld, must ever after be the sea of his adoption. It rolls the midmost waters of the world, the Indian ocean and Atlantic being but its arms. The same waves wash the moles of the new-built Californian towns, but yesterday planted by the recentest race of men, and lave the faded but still gorgeous skirts of Asiatic lands, older than Abraham; while all between float milky-ways of coral isles, and low-lying, endless, unknown Archipelagoes, and impenetrable Japans. Thus this mysterious, divine Pacific zones the world’s whole bulk about; makes all coasts one bay to it; seems the tide-beating heart of earth. Lifted by those eternal swells, you needs must own the seductive god, bowing your head to Pan.\n\nBut few thoughts of Pan stirred Ahab’s brain, as standing like an iron statue at his accustomed place beside the mizen rigging, with one nostril he unthinkingly snuffed the sugary musk from the Bashee isles (in whose sweet woods mild lovers must be walking), and with the other consciously inhaled the salt breath of the new found sea; that sea in which the hated White Whale must even then be swimming. Launched at length upon these almost final waters, and gliding towards the Japanese cruising-ground, the old man’s purpose intensified itself. His firm lips met like the lips of a vice; the Delta of his forehead’s veins swelled like overladen brooks; in his very sleep, his ringing cry ran through the vaulted hull, “Stern all! the White Whale spouts thick blood!”\n\n# CHAPTER 112. The Blacksmith.\n\nAvailing himself of the mild, summer-cool weather that now reigned in these latitudes, and in preparation for the peculiarly active pursuits shortly to be anticipated, Perth, the begrimed, blistered old blacksmith, had not removed his portable forge to the hold again, after concluding his contributory work for Ahab’s leg, but still retained it on deck, fast lashed to ringbolts by the foremast; being now almost incessantly invoked by the headsmen, and harpooneers, and bowsmen to do some little job for them; altering, or repairing, or new shaping their various weapons and boat furniture. Often he would be surrounded by an eager circle, all waiting to be served; holding boat-spades, pike-heads, harpoons, and lances, and jealously watching his every sooty movement, as he toiled. Nevertheless, this old man’s was a patient hammer wielded by a patient arm. No murmur, no impatience, no petulance did come from him. Silent, slow, and solemn; bowing over still further his chronically broken back, he toiled away, as if toil were life itself, and the heavy beating of his hammer the heavy beating of his heart. And so it was.—Most miserable!\n\nA peculiar walk in this old man, a certain slight but painful appearing yawing in his gait, had at an early period of the voyage excited the curiosity of the mariners. And to the importunity of their persisted questionings he had finally given in; and so it came to pass that every one now knew the shameful story of his wretched fate.\n\nBelated, and not innocently, one bitter winter’s midnight, on the road running between two country towns, the blacksmith half-stupidly felt the deadly numbness stealing over him, and sought refuge in a leaning, dilapidated barn. The issue was, the loss of the extremities of both feet. Out of this revelation, part by part, at last came out the four acts of the gladness, and the one long, and as yet uncatastrophied fifth act of the grief of his life’s drama.\n\nHe was an old man, who, at the age of nearly sixty, had postponedly encountered that thing in sorrow’s technicals called ruin. He had been an artisan of famed excellence, and with plenty to do; owned a house and garden; embraced a youthful, daughter-like, loving wife, and three blithe, ruddy children; every Sunday went to a cheerful-looking church, planted in a grove. But one night, under cover of darkness, and further concealed in a most cunning disguisement, a desperate burglar slid into his happy home, and robbed them all of everything. And darker yet to tell, the blacksmith himself did ignorantly conduct this burglar into his family’s heart. It was the Bottle Conjuror! Upon the opening of that fatal cork, forth flew the fiend, and shrivelled up his home. Now, for prudent, most wise, and economic reasons, the blacksmith’s shop was in the basement of his dwelling, but with a separate entrance to it; so that always had the young and loving healthy wife listened with no unhappy nervousness, but with vigorous pleasure, to the stout ringing of her young-armed old husband’s hammer; whose reverberations, muffled by passing through the floors and walls, came up to her, not unsweetly, in her nursery; and so, to stout Labor’s iron lullaby, the blacksmith’s infants were rocked to slumber.\n\nOh, woe on woe! Oh, Death, why canst thou not sometimes be timely? Hadst thou taken this old blacksmith to thyself ere his full ruin came upon him, then had the young widow had a delicious grief, and her orphans a truly venerable, legendary sire to dream of in their after years; and all of them a care-killing competency. But Death plucked down some virtuous elder brother, on whose whistling daily toil solely hung the responsibilities of some other family, and left the worse than useless old man standing, till the hideous rot of life should make him easier to harvest.\n\nWhy tell the whole? The blows of the basement hammer every day grew more and more between; and each blow every day grew fainter than the last; the wife sat frozen at the window, with tearless eyes, glitteringly gazing into the weeping faces of her children; the bellows fell; the forge choked up with cinders; the house was sold; the mother dived down into the long church-yard grass; her children twice followed her thither; and the houseless, familyless old man staggered off a vagabond in crape; his every woe unreverenced; his grey head a scorn to flaxen curls!\n\nDeath seems the only desirable sequel for a career like this; but Death is only a launching into the region of the strange Untried; it is but the first salutation to the possibilities of the immense Remote, the Wild, the Watery, the Unshored; therefore, to the death-longing eyes of such men, who still have left in them some interior compunctions against suicide, does the all-contributed and all-receptive ocean alluringly spread forth his whole plain of unimaginable, taking terrors, and wonderful, new-life adventures; and from the hearts of infinite Pacifics, the thousand mermaids sing to them—“Come hither, broken-hearted; here is another life without the guilt of intermediate death; here are wonders supernatural, without dying for them. Come hither! bury thyself in a life which, to your now equally abhorred and abhorring, landed world, is more oblivious than death. Come hither! put up thy gravestone, too, within the churchyard, and come hither, till we marry thee!”\n\nHearkening to these voices, East and West, by early sunrise, and by fall of eve, the blacksmith’s soul responded, Aye, I come! And so Perth went a-whaling.\n\n# CHAPTER 113. The Forge.\n\nWith matted beard, and swathed in a bristling shark-skin apron, about mid-day, Perth was standing between his forge and anvil, the latter placed upon an iron-wood log, with one hand holding a pike-head in the coals, and with the other at his forge’s lungs, when Captain Ahab came along, carrying in his hand a small rusty-looking leathern bag. While yet a little distance from the forge, moody Ahab paused; till at last, Perth, withdrawing his iron from the fire, began hammering it upon the anvil—the red mass sending off the sparks in thick hovering flights, some of which flew close to Ahab.\n\n“Are these thy Mother Carey’s chickens, Perth? they are always flying in thy wake; birds of good omen, too, but not to all;—look here, they burn; but thou—thou liv’st among them without a scorch.”\n\n“Because I am scorched all over, Captain Ahab,” answered Perth, resting for a moment on his hammer; “I am past scorching; not easily can’st thou scorch a scar.”\n\n“Well, well; no more. Thy shrunk voice sounds too calmly, sanely woeful to me. In no Paradise myself, I am impatient of all misery in others that is not mad. Thou should’st go mad, blacksmith; say, why dost thou not go mad? How can’st thou endure without being mad? Do the heavens yet hate thee, that thou can’st not go mad?—What wert thou making there?”\n\n“Welding an old pike-head, sir; there were seams and dents in it.”\n\n“And can’st thou make it all smooth again, blacksmith, after such hard usage as it had?”\n\n“I think so, sir.”\n\n“And I suppose thou can’st smoothe almost any seams and dents; never mind how hard the metal, blacksmith?”\n\n“Aye, sir, I think I can; all seams and dents but one.”\n\n“Look ye here, then,” cried Ahab, passionately advancing, and leaning with both hands on Perth’s shoulders; “look ye here—here—can ye smoothe out a seam like this, blacksmith,” sweeping one hand across his ribbed brow; “if thou could’st, blacksmith, glad enough would I lay my head upon thy anvil, and feel thy heaviest hammer between my eyes. Answer! Can’st thou smoothe this seam?”\n\n“Oh! that is the one, sir! Said I not all seams and dents but one?”\n\n“Aye, blacksmith, it is the one; aye, man, it is unsmoothable; for though thou only see’st it here in my flesh, it has worked down into the bone of my skull—that is all wrinkles! But, away with child’s play; no more gaffs and pikes to-day. Look ye here!” jingling the leathern bag, as if it were full of gold coins. “I, too, want a harpoon made; one that a thousand yoke of fiends could not part, Perth; something that will stick in a whale like his own fin-bone. There’s the stuff,” flinging the pouch upon the anvil. “Look ye, blacksmith, these are the gathered nail-stubbs of the steel shoes of racing horses.”\n\n“Horse-shoe stubbs, sir? Why, Captain Ahab, thou hast here, then, the best and stubbornest stuff we blacksmiths ever work.”\n\n“I know it, old man; these stubbs will weld together like glue from the melted bones of murderers. Quick! forge me the harpoon. And forge me first, twelve rods for its shank; then wind, and twist, and hammer these twelve together like the yarns and strands of a tow-line. Quick! I’ll blow the fire.”\n\nWhen at last the twelve rods were made, Ahab tried them, one by one, by spiralling them, with his own hand, round a long, heavy iron bolt. “A flaw!” rejecting the last one. “Work that over again, Perth.”\n\nThis done, Perth was about to begin welding the twelve into one, when Ahab stayed his hand, and said he would weld his own iron. As, then, with regular, gasping hems, he hammered on the anvil, Perth passing to him the glowing rods, one after the other, and the hard pressed forge shooting up its intense straight flame, the Parsee passed silently, and bowing over his head towards the fire, seemed invoking some curse or some blessing on the toil. But, as Ahab looked up, he slid aside.\n\n“What’s that bunch of lucifers dodging about there for?” muttered Stubb, looking on from the forecastle. “That Parsee smells fire like a fusee; and smells of it himself, like a hot musket’s powder-pan.”\n\nAt last the shank, in one complete rod, received its final heat; and as Perth, to temper it, plunged it all hissing into the cask of water near by, the scalding steam shot up into Ahab’s bent face.\n\n“Would’st thou brand me, Perth?” wincing for a moment with the pain; “have I been but forging my own branding-iron, then?”\n\n“Pray God, not that; yet I fear something, Captain Ahab. Is not this harpoon for the White Whale?”\n\n“For the white fiend! But now for the barbs; thou must make them thyself, man. Here are my razors—the best of steel; here, and make the barbs sharp as the needle-sleet of the Icy Sea.”\n\nFor a moment, the old blacksmith eyed the razors as though he would fain not use them.\n\n“Take them, man, I have no need for them; for I now neither shave, sup, nor pray till—but here—to work!”\n\nFashioned at last into an arrowy shape, and welded by Perth to the shank, the steel soon pointed the end of the iron; and as the blacksmith was about giving the barbs their final heat, prior to tempering them, he cried to Ahab to place the water-cask near.\n\n“No, no—no water for that; I want it of the true death-temper. Ahoy, there! Tashtego, Queequeg, Daggoo! What say ye, pagans! Will ye give me as much blood as will cover this barb?” holding it high up. A cluster of dark nods replied, Yes. Three punctures were made in the heathen flesh, and the White Whale’s barbs were then tempered.\n\n“Ego non baptizo te in nomine patris, sed in nomine diaboli!” deliriously howled Ahab, as the malignant iron scorchingly devoured the baptismal blood.\n\nNow, mustering the spare poles from below, and selecting one of hickory, with the bark still investing it, Ahab fitted the end to the socket of the iron. A coil of new tow-line was then unwound, and some fathoms of it taken to the windlass, and stretched to a great tension. Pressing his foot upon it, till the rope hummed like a harp-string, then eagerly bending over it, and seeing no strandings, Ahab exclaimed, “Good! and now for the seizings.”\n\nAt one extremity the rope was unstranded, and the separate spread yarns were all braided and woven round the socket of the harpoon; the pole was then driven hard up into the socket; from the lower end the rope was traced half-way along the pole’s length, and firmly secured so, with intertwistings of twine. This done, pole, iron, and rope—like the Three Fates—remained inseparable, and Ahab moodily stalked away with the weapon; the sound of his ivory leg, and the sound of the hickory pole, both hollowly ringing along every plank. But ere he entered his cabin, light, unnatural, half-bantering, yet most piteous sound was heard. Oh, Pip! thy wretched laugh, thy idle but unresting eye; all thy strange mummeries not unmeaningly blended with the black tragedy of the melancholy ship, and mocked it!\n\n# CHAPTER 114. The Gilder.\n\nPenetrating further and further into the heart of the Japanese cruising ground, the Pequod was soon all astir in the fishery. Often, in mild, pleasant weather, for twelve, fifteen, eighteen, and twenty hours on the stretch, they were engaged in the boats, steadily pulling, or sailing, or paddling after the whales, or for an interlude of sixty or seventy minutes calmly awaiting their uprising; though with but small success for their pains.\n\nAt such times, under an abated sun; afloat all day upon smooth, slow heaving swells; seated in his boat, light as a birch canoe; and so sociably mixing with the soft waves themselves, that like hearth-stone cats they purr against the gunwale; these are the times of dreamy quietude, when beholding the tranquil beauty and brilliancy of the ocean’s skin, one forgets the tiger heart that pants beneath it; and would not willingly remember, that this velvet paw but conceals a remorseless fang.\n\nThese are the times, when in his whale-boat the rover softly feels a certain filial, confident, land-like feeling towards the sea; that he regards it as so much flowery earth; and the distant ship revealing only the tops of her masts, seems struggling forward, not through high rolling waves, but through the tall grass of a rolling prairie: as when the western emigrants’ horses only show their erected ears, while their hidden bodies widely wade through the amazing verdure.\n\nThe long-drawn virgin vales; the mild blue hill-sides; as over these there steals the hush, the hum; you almost swear that play-wearied children lie sleeping in these solitudes, in some glad May-time, when the flowers of the woods are plucked. And all this mixes with your most mystic mood; so that fact and fancy, half-way meeting, interpenetrate, and form one seamless whole.\n\nNor did such soothing scenes, however temporary, fail of at least as temporary an effect on Ahab. But if these secret golden keys did seem to open in him his own secret golden treasuries, yet did his breath upon them prove but tarnishing.\n\nOh, grassy glades! oh, ever vernal endless landscapes in the soul; in ye,—though long parched by the dead drought of the earthy life,—in ye, men yet may roll, like young horses in new morning clover; and for some few fleeting moments, feel the cool dew of the life immortal on them. Would to God these blessed calms would last. But the mingled, mingling threads of life are woven by warp and woof: calms crossed by storms, a storm for every calm. There is no steady unretracing progress in this life; we do not advance through fixed gradations, and at the last one pause:—through infancy’s unconscious spell, boyhood’s thoughtless faith, adolescence’ doubt (the common doom), then scepticism, then disbelief, resting at last in manhood’s pondering repose of If. But once gone through, we trace the round again; and are infants, boys, and men, and Ifs eternally. Where lies the final harbor, whence we unmoor no more? In what rapt ether sails the world, of which the weariest will never weary? Where is the foundling’s father hidden? Our souls are like those orphans whose unwedded mothers die in bearing them: the secret of our paternity lies in their grave, and we must there to learn it.\n\nAnd that same day, too, gazing far down from his boat’s side into that same golden sea, Starbuck lowly murmured:—\n\n“Loveliness unfathomable, as ever lover saw in his young bride’s eye!—Tell me not of thy teeth-tiered sharks, and thy kidnapping cannibal ways. Let faith oust fact; let fancy oust memory; I look deep down and do believe.”\n\nAnd Stubb, fish-like, with sparkling scales, leaped up in that same golden light:—\n\n“I am Stubb, and Stubb has his history; but here Stubb takes oaths that he has always been jolly!”\n\n# CHAPTER 115. The Pequod Meets The Bachelor.\n\nAnd jolly enough were the sights and the sounds that came bearing down before the wind, some few weeks after Ahab’s harpoon had been welded.\n\nIt was a Nantucket ship, the Bachelor, which had just wedged in her last cask of oil, and bolted down her bursting hatches; and now, in glad holiday apparel, was joyously, though somewhat vain-gloriously, sailing round among the widely-separated ships on the ground, previous to pointing her prow for home.\n\nThe three men at her mast-head wore long streamers of narrow red bunting at their hats; from the stern, a whale-boat was suspended, bottom down; and hanging captive from the bowsprit was seen the long lower jaw of the last whale they had slain. Signals, ensigns, and jacks of all colours were flying from her rigging, on every side. Sideways lashed in each of her three basketed tops were two barrels of sperm; above which, in her top-mast cross-trees, you saw slender breakers of the same precious fluid; and nailed to her main truck was a brazen lamp.\n\nAs was afterwards learned, the Bachelor had met with the most surprising success; all the more wonderful, for that while cruising in the same seas numerous other vessels had gone entire months without securing a single fish. Not only had barrels of beef and bread been given away to make room for the far more valuable sperm, but additional supplemental casks had been bartered for, from the ships she had met; and these were stowed along the deck, and in the captain’s and officers’ state-rooms. Even the cabin table itself had been knocked into kindling-wood; and the cabin mess dined off the broad head of an oil-butt, lashed down to the floor for a centrepiece. In the forecastle, the sailors had actually caulked and pitched their chests, and filled them; it was humorously added, that the cook had clapped a head on his largest boiler, and filled it; that the steward had plugged his spare coffee-pot and filled it; that the harpooneers had headed the sockets of their irons and filled them; that indeed everything was filled with sperm, except the captain’s pantaloons pockets, and those he reserved to thrust his hands into, in self-complacent testimony of his entire satisfaction.\n\nAs this glad ship of good luck bore down upon the moody Pequod, the barbarian sound of enormous drums came from her forecastle; and drawing still nearer, a crowd of her men were seen standing round her huge try-pots, which, covered with the parchment-like poke or stomach skin of the black fish, gave forth a loud roar to every stroke of the clenched hands of the crew. On the quarter-deck, the mates and harpooneers were dancing with the olive-hued girls who had eloped with them from the Polynesian Isles; while suspended in an ornamented boat, firmly secured aloft between the foremast and mainmast, three Long Island negroes, with glittering fiddle-bows of whale ivory, were presiding over the hilarious jig. Meanwhile, others of the ship’s company were tumultuously busy at the masonry of the try-works, from which the huge pots had been removed. You would have almost thought they were pulling down the cursed Bastille, such wild cries they raised, as the now useless brick and mortar were being hurled into the sea.\n\nLord and master over all this scene, the captain stood erect on the ship’s elevated quarter-deck, so that the whole rejoicing drama was full before him, and seemed merely contrived for his own individual diversion.\n\nAnd Ahab, he too was standing on his quarter-deck, shaggy and black, with a stubborn gloom; and as the two ships crossed each other’s wakes—one all jubilations for things passed, the other all forebodings as to things to come—their two captains in themselves impersonated the whole striking contrast of the scene.\n\n“Come aboard, come aboard!” cried the gay Bachelor’s commander, lifting a glass and a bottle in the air.\n\n“Hast seen the White Whale?” gritted Ahab in reply.\n\n“No; only heard of him; but don’t believe in him at all,” said the other good-humoredly. “Come aboard!”\n\n“Thou art too damned jolly. Sail on. Hast lost any men?”\n\n“Not enough to speak of—two islanders, that’s all;—but come aboard, old hearty, come along. I’ll soon take that black from your brow. Come along, will ye (merry’s the play); a full ship and homeward-bound.”\n\n“How wondrous familiar is a fool!” muttered Ahab; then aloud, “Thou art a full ship and homeward bound, thou sayst; well, then, call me an empty ship, and outward-bound. So go thy ways, and I will mine. Forward there! Set all sail, and keep her to the wind!”\n\nAnd thus, while the one ship went cheerily before the breeze, the other stubbornly fought against it; and so the two vessels parted; the crew of the Pequod looking with grave, lingering glances towards the receding Bachelor; but the Bachelor’s men never heeding their gaze for the lively revelry they were in. And as Ahab, leaning over the taffrail, eyed the homeward-bound craft, he took from his pocket a small vial of sand, and then looking from the ship to the vial, seemed thereby bringing two remote associations together, for that vial was filled with Nantucket soundings.\n\n# CHAPTER 116. The Dying Whale.\n\nNot seldom in this life, when, on the right side, fortune’s favourites sail close by us, we, though all adroop before, catch somewhat of the rushing breeze, and joyfully feel our bagging sails fill out. So seemed it with the Pequod. For next day after encountering the gay Bachelor, whales were seen and four were slain; and one of them by Ahab.\n\nIt was far down the afternoon; and when all the spearings of the crimson fight were done: and floating in the lovely sunset sea and sky, sun and whale both stilly died together; then, such a sweetness and such plaintiveness, such inwreathing orisons curled up in that rosy air, that it almost seemed as if far over from the deep green convent valleys of the Manilla isles, the Spanish land-breeze, wantonly turned sailor, had gone to sea, freighted with these vesper hymns.\n\nSoothed again, but only soothed to deeper gloom, Ahab, who had sterned off from the whale, sat intently watching his final wanings from the now tranquil boat. For that strange spectacle observable in all sperm whales dying—the turning sunwards of the head, and so expiring—that strange spectacle, beheld of such a placid evening, somehow to Ahab conveyed a wondrousness unknown before.\n\n“He turns and turns him to it,—how slowly, but how steadfastly, his homage-rendering and invoking brow, with his last dying motions. He too worships fire; most faithful, broad, baronial vassal of the sun!—Oh that these too-favouring eyes should see these too-favouring sights. Look! here, far water-locked; beyond all hum of human weal or woe; in these most candid and impartial seas; where to traditions no rocks furnish tablets; where for long Chinese ages, the billows have still rolled on speechless and unspoken to, as stars that shine upon the Niger’s unknown source; here, too, life dies sunwards full of faith; but see! no sooner dead, than death whirls round the corpse, and it heads some other way.\n\n“Oh, thou dark Hindoo half of nature, who of drowned bones hast builded thy separate throne somewhere in the heart of these unverdured seas; thou art an infidel, thou queen, and too truly speakest to me in the wide-slaughtering Typhoon, and the hushed burial of its after calm. Nor has this thy whale sunwards turned his dying head, and then gone round again, without a lesson to me.\n\n“Oh, trebly hooped and welded hip of power! Oh, high aspiring, rainbowed jet!—that one strivest, this one jettest all in vain! In vain, oh whale, dost thou seek intercedings with yon all-quickening sun, that only calls forth life, but gives it not again. Yet dost thou, darker half, rock me with a prouder, if a darker faith. All thy unnamable imminglings float beneath me here; I am buoyed by breaths of once living things, exhaled as air, but water now.\n\n“Then hail, for ever hail, O sea, in whose eternal tossings the wild fowl finds his only rest. Born of earth, yet suckled by the sea; though hill and valley mothered me, ye billows are my foster-brothers!”\n\n# CHAPTER 117. The Whale Watch.\n\nThe four whales slain that evening had died wide apart; one, far to windward; one, less distant, to leeward; one ahead; one astern. These last three were brought alongside ere nightfall; but the windward one could not be reached till morning; and the boat that had killed it lay by its side all night; and that boat was Ahab’s.\n\nThe waif-pole was thrust upright into the dead whale’s spout-hole; and the lantern hanging from its top, cast a troubled flickering glare upon the black, glossy back, and far out upon the midnight waves, which gently chafed the whale’s broad flank, like soft surf upon a beach.\n\nAhab and all his boat’s crew seemed asleep but the Parsee; who crouching in the bow, sat watching the sharks, that spectrally played round the whale, and tapped the light cedar planks with their tails. A sound like the moaning in squadrons over Asphaltites of unforgiven ghosts of Gomorrah, ran shuddering through the air.\n\nStarted from his slumbers, Ahab, face to face, saw the Parsee; and hooped round by the gloom of the night they seemed the last men in a flooded world. “I have dreamed it again,” said he.\n\n“Of the hearses? Have I not said, old man, that neither hearse nor coffin can be thine?”\n\n“And who are hearsed that die on the sea?”\n\n“But I said, old man, that ere thou couldst die on this voyage, two hearses must verily be seen by thee on the sea; the first not made by mortal hands; and the visible wood of the last one must be grown in America.”\n\n“Aye, aye! a strange sight that, Parsee:—a hearse and its plumes floating over the ocean with the waves for the pall-bearers. Ha! Such a sight we shall not soon see.”\n\n“Believe it or not, thou canst not die till it be seen, old man.”\n\n“And what was that saying about thyself?”\n\n“Though it come to the last, I shall still go before thee thy pilot.”\n\n“And when thou art so gone before—if that ever befall—then ere I can follow, thou must still appear to me, to pilot me still?—Was it not so? Well, then, did I believe all ye say, oh my pilot! I have here two pledges that I shall yet slay Moby Dick and survive it.”\n\n“Take another pledge, old man,” said the Parsee, as his eyes lighted up like fire-flies in the gloom—“Hemp only can kill thee.”\n\n“The gallows, ye mean.—I am immortal then, on land and on sea,” cried Ahab, with a laugh of derision;—“Immortal on land and on sea!”\n\nBoth were silent again, as one man. The grey dawn came on, and the slumbering crew arose from the boat’s bottom, and ere noon the dead whale was brought to the ship.\n\n# CHAPTER 118. The Quadrant.\n\nThe season for the Line at length drew near; and every day when Ahab, coming from his cabin, cast his eyes aloft, the vigilant helmsman would ostentatiously handle his spokes, and the eager mariners quickly run to the braces, and would stand there with all their eyes centrally fixed on the nailed doubloon; impatient for the order to point the ship’s prow for the equator. In good time the order came. It was hard upon high noon; and Ahab, seated in the bows of his high-hoisted boat, was about taking his wonted daily observation of the sun to determine his latitude.\n\nNow, in that Japanese sea, the days in summer are as freshets of effulgences. That unblinkingly vivid Japanese sun seems the blazing focus of the glassy ocean’s immeasurable burning-glass. The sky looks lacquered; clouds there are none; the horizon floats; and this nakedness of unrelieved radiance is as the insufferable splendors of God’s throne. Well that Ahab’s quadrant was furnished with coloured glasses, through which to take sight of that solar fire. So, swinging his seated form to the roll of the ship, and with his astrological-looking instrument placed to his eye, he remained in that posture for some moments to catch the precise instant when the sun should gain its precise meridian. Meantime while his whole attention was absorbed, the Parsee was kneeling beneath him on the ship’s deck, and with face thrown up like Ahab’s, was eyeing the same sun with him; only the lids of his eyes half hooded their orbs, and his wild face was subdued to an earthly passionlessness. At length the desired observation was taken; and with his pencil upon his ivory leg, Ahab soon calculated what his latitude must be at that precise instant. Then falling into a moment’s revery, he again looked up towards the sun and murmured to himself: “Thou sea-mark! thou high and mighty Pilot! thou tellest me truly where I am—but canst thou cast the least hint where I shall be? Or canst thou tell where some other thing besides me is this moment living? Where is Moby Dick? This instant thou must be eyeing him. These eyes of mine look into the very eye that is even now beholding him; aye, and into the eye that is even now equally beholding the objects on the unknown, thither side of thee, thou sun!”\n\nThen gazing at his quadrant, and handling, one after the other, its numerous cabalistical contrivances, he pondered again, and muttered: “Foolish toy! babies’ plaything of haughty Admirals, and Commodores, and Captains; the world brags of thee, of thy cunning and might; but what after all canst thou do, but tell the poor, pitiful point, where thou thyself happenest to be on this wide planet, and the hand that holds thee: no! not one jot more! Thou canst not tell where one drop of water or one grain of sand will be to-morrow noon; and yet with thy impotence thou insultest the sun! Science! Curse thee, thou vain toy; and cursed be all the things that cast man’s eyes aloft to that heaven, whose live vividness but scorches him, as these old eyes are even now scorched with thy light, O sun! Level by nature to this earth’s horizon are the glances of man’s eyes; not shot from the crown of his head, as if God had meant him to gaze on his firmament. Curse thee, thou quadrant!” dashing it to the deck, “no longer will I guide my earthly way by thee; the level ship’s compass, and the level dead-reckoning, by log and by line; these shall conduct me, and show me my place on the sea. Aye,” lighting from the boat to the deck, “thus I trample on thee, thou paltry thing that feebly pointest on high; thus I split and destroy thee!”\n\nAs the frantic old man thus spoke and thus trampled with his live and dead feet, a sneering triumph that seemed meant for Ahab, and a fatalistic despair that seemed meant for himself—these passed over the mute, motionless Parsee’s face. Unobserved he rose and glided away; while, awestruck by the aspect of their commander, the seamen clustered together on the forecastle, till Ahab, troubledly pacing the deck, shouted out—“To the braces! Up helm!—square in!”\n\nIn an instant the yards swung round; and as the ship half-wheeled upon her heel, her three firm-seated graceful masts erectly poised upon her long, ribbed hull, seemed as the three Horatii pirouetting on one sufficient steed.\n\nStanding between the knight-heads, Starbuck watched the Pequod’s tumultuous way, and Ahab’s also, as he went lurching along the deck.\n\n“I have sat before the dense coal fire and watched it all aglow, full of its tormented flaming life; and I have seen it wane at last, down, down, to dumbest dust. Old man of oceans! of all this fiery life of thine, what will at length remain but one little heap of ashes!”\n\n“Aye,” cried Stubb, “but sea-coal ashes—mind ye that, Mr. Starbuck—sea-coal, not your common charcoal. Well, well; I heard Ahab mutter, ‘Here some one thrusts these cards into these old hands of mine; swears that I must play them, and no others.’ And damn me, Ahab, but thou actest right; live in the game, and die in it!”\n\n# CHAPTER 119. The Candles.\n\nWarmest climes but nurse the cruellest fangs: the tiger of Bengal crouches in spiced groves of ceaseless verdure. Skies the most effulgent but basket the deadliest thunders: gorgeous Cuba knows tornadoes that never swept tame northern lands. So, too, it is, that in these resplendent Japanese seas the mariner encounters the direst of all storms, the Typhoon. It will sometimes burst from out that cloudless sky, like an exploding bomb upon a dazed and sleepy town.\n\nTowards evening of that day, the Pequod was torn of her canvas, and bare-poled was left to fight a Typhoon which had struck her directly ahead. When darkness came on, sky and sea roared and split with the thunder, and blazed with the lightning, that showed the disabled masts fluttering here and there with the rags which the first fury of the tempest had left for its after sport.\n\nHolding by a shroud, Starbuck was standing on the quarter-deck; at every flash of the lightning glancing aloft, to see what additional disaster might have befallen the intricate hamper there; while Stubb and Flask were directing the men in the higher hoisting and firmer lashing of the boats. But all their pains seemed naught. Though lifted to the very top of the cranes, the windward quarter boat (Ahab’s) did not escape. A great rolling sea, dashing high up against the reeling ship’s high teetering side, stove in the boat’s bottom at the stern, and left it again, all dripping through like a sieve.\n\n“Bad work, bad work! Mr. Starbuck,” said Stubb, regarding the wreck, “but the sea will have its way. Stubb, for one, can’t fight it. You see, Mr. Starbuck, a wave has such a great long start before it leaps, all round the world it runs, and then comes the spring! But as for me, all the start I have to meet it, is just across the deck here. But never mind; it’s all in fun: so the old song says;”—(sings.)\n\nOh! jolly is the gale, And a joker is the whale, A’ flourishin’ his tail,— Such a funny, sporty, gamy, jesty, joky, hoky-poky lad, is the Ocean, oh!\n\nThe scud all a flyin’, That’s his flip only foamin’; When he stirs in the spicin’,— Such a funny, sporty, gamy, jesty, joky, hoky-poky lad, is the Ocean, oh!\n\nThunder splits the ships, But he only smacks his lips, A tastin’ of this flip,— Such a funny, sporty, gamy, jesty, joky, hoky-poky lad, is the Ocean, oh!\n\n“Avast Stubb,” cried Starbuck, “let the Typhoon sing, and strike his harp here in our rigging; but if thou art a brave man thou wilt hold thy peace.”\n\n“But I am not a brave man; never said I was a brave man; I am a coward; and I sing to keep up my spirits. And I tell you what it is, Mr. Starbuck, there’s no way to stop my singing in this world but to cut my throat. And when that’s done, ten to one I sing ye the doxology for a wind-up.”\n\n“Madman! look through my eyes if thou hast none of thine own.”\n\n“What! how can you see better of a dark night than anybody else, never mind how foolish?”\n\n“Here!” cried Starbuck, seizing Stubb by the shoulder, and pointing his hand towards the weather bow, “markest thou not that the gale comes from the eastward, the very course Ahab is to run for Moby Dick? the very course he swung to this day noon? now mark his boat there; where is that stove? In the stern-sheets, man; where he is wont to stand—his stand-point is stove, man! Now jump overboard, and sing away, if thou must!\n\n“I don’t half understand ye: what’s in the wind?”\n\n“Yes, yes, round the Cape of Good Hope is the shortest way to Nantucket,” soliloquized Starbuck suddenly, heedless of Stubb’s question. “The gale that now hammers at us to stave us, we can turn it into a fair wind that will drive us towards home. Yonder, to windward, all is blackness of doom; but to leeward, homeward—I see it lightens up there; but not with the lightning.”\n\nAt that moment in one of the intervals of profound darkness, following the flashes, a voice was heard at his side; and almost at the same instant a volley of thunder peals rolled overhead.\n\n“Who’s there?”\n\n“Old Thunder!” said Ahab, groping his way along the bulwarks to his pivot-hole; but suddenly finding his path made plain to him by elbowed lances of fire.\n\nNow, as the lightning rod to a spire on shore is intended to carry off the perilous fluid into the soil; so the kindred rod which at sea some ships carry to each mast, is intended to conduct it into the water. But as this conductor must descend to considerable depth, that its end may avoid all contact with the hull; and as moreover, if kept constantly towing there, it would be liable to many mishaps, besides interfering not a little with some of the rigging, and more or less impeding the vessel’s way in the water; because of all this, the lower parts of a ship’s lightning-rods are not always overboard; but are generally made in long slender links, so as to be the more readily hauled up into the chains outside, or thrown down into the sea, as occasion may require.\n\n“The rods! the rods!” cried Starbuck to the crew, suddenly admonished to vigilance by the vivid lightning that had just been darting flambeaux, to light Ahab to his post. “Are they overboard? drop them over, fore and aft. Quick!”\n\n“Avast!” cried Ahab; “let’s have fair play here, though we be the weaker side. Yet I’ll contribute to raise rods on the Himmalehs and Andes, that all the world may be secured; but out on privileges! Let them be, sir.”\n\n“Look aloft!” cried Starbuck. “The corpusants! the corpusants!”\n\nAll the yard-arms were tipped with a pallid fire; and touched at each tri-pointed lightning-rod-end with three tapering white flames, each of the three tall masts was silently burning in that sulphurous air, like three gigantic wax tapers before an altar.\n\n“Blast the boat! let it go!” cried Stubb at this instant, as a swashing sea heaved up under his own little craft, so that its gunwale violently jammed his hand, as he was passing a lashing. “Blast it!”—but slipping backward on the deck, his uplifted eyes caught the flames; and immediately shifting his tone he cried—“The corpusants have mercy on us all!”\n\nTo sailors, oaths are household words; they will swear in the trance of the calm, and in the teeth of the tempest; they will imprecate curses from the topsail-yard-arms, when most they teeter over to a seething sea; but in all my voyagings, seldom have I heard a common oath when God’s burning finger has been laid on the ship; when His “Mene, Mene, Tekel Upharsin” has been woven into the shrouds and the cordage.\n\nWhile this pallidness was burning aloft, few words were heard from the enchanted crew; who in one thick cluster stood on the forecastle, all their eyes gleaming in that pale phosphorescence, like a far away constellation of stars. Relieved against the ghostly light, the gigantic jet negro, Daggoo, loomed up to thrice his real stature, and seemed the black cloud from which the thunder had come. The parted mouth of Tashtego revealed his shark-white teeth, which strangely gleamed as if they too had been tipped by corpusants; while lit up by the preternatural light, Queequeg’s tattooing burned like Satanic blue flames on his body.\n\nThe tableau all waned at last with the pallidness aloft; and once more the Pequod and every soul on her decks were wrapped in a pall. A moment or two passed, when Starbuck, going forward, pushed against some one. It was Stubb. “What thinkest thou now, man; I heard thy cry; it was not the same in the song.”\n\n“No, no, it wasn’t; I said the corpusants have mercy on us all; and I hope they will, still. But do they only have mercy on long faces?—have they no bowels for a laugh? And look ye, Mr. Starbuck—but it’s too dark to look. Hear me, then: I take that mast-head flame we saw for a sign of good luck; for those masts are rooted in a hold that is going to be chock a’ block with sperm-oil, d’ye see; and so, all that sperm will work up into the masts, like sap in a tree. Yes, our three masts will yet be as three spermaceti candles—that’s the good promise we saw.”\n\nAt that moment Starbuck caught sight of Stubb’s face slowly beginning to glimmer into sight. Glancing upwards, he cried: “See! see!” and once more the high tapering flames were beheld with what seemed redoubled supernaturalness in their pallor.\n\n“The corpusants have mercy on us all,” cried Stubb, again.\n\nAt the base of the mainmast, full beneath the doubloon and the flame, the Parsee was kneeling in Ahab’s front, but with his head bowed away from him; while near by, from the arched and overhanging rigging, where they had just been engaged securing a spar, a number of the seamen, arrested by the glare, now cohered together, and hung pendulous, like a knot of numbed wasps from a drooping, orchard twig. In various enchanted attitudes, like the standing, or stepping, or running skeletons in Herculaneum, others remained rooted to the deck; but all their eyes upcast.\n\n“Aye, aye, men!” cried Ahab. “Look up at it; mark it well; the white flame but lights the way to the White Whale! Hand me those mainmast links there; I would fain feel this pulse, and let mine beat against it; blood against fire! So.”\n\nThen turning—the last link held fast in his left hand, he put his foot upon the Parsee; and with fixed upward eye, and high-flung right arm, he stood erect before the lofty tri-pointed trinity of flames.\n\n“Oh! thou clear spirit of clear fire, whom on these seas I as Persian once did worship, till in the sacramental act so burned by thee, that to this hour I bear the scar; I now know thee, thou clear spirit, and I now know that thy right worship is defiance. To neither love nor reverence wilt thou be kind; and e’en for hate thou canst but kill; and all are killed. No fearless fool now fronts thee. I own thy speechless, placeless power; but to the last gasp of my earthquake life will dispute its unconditional, unintegral mastery in me. In the midst of the personified impersonal, a personality stands here. Though but a point at best; whencesoe’er I came; wheresoe’er I go; yet while I earthly live, the queenly personality lives in me, and feels her royal rights. But war is pain, and hate is woe. Come in thy lowest form of love, and I will kneel and kiss thee; but at thy highest, come as mere supernal power; and though thou launchest navies of full-freighted worlds, there’s that in here that still remains indifferent. Oh, thou clear spirit, of thy fire thou madest me, and like a true child of fire, I breathe it back to thee.”\n\n[Sudden, repeated flashes of lightning; the nine flames leap lengthwise to thrice their previous height; Ahab, with the rest, closes his eyes, his right hand pressed hard upon them.]\n\n“I own thy speechless, placeless power; said I not so? Nor was it wrung from me; nor do I now drop these links. Thou canst blind; but I can then grope. Thou canst consume; but I can then be ashes. Take the homage of these poor eyes, and shutter-hands. I would not take it. The lightning flashes through my skull; mine eye-balls ache and ache; my whole beaten brain seems as beheaded, and rolling on some stunning ground. Oh, oh! Yet blindfold, yet will I talk to thee. Light though thou be, thou leapest out of darkness; but I am darkness leaping out of light, leaping out of thee! The javelins cease; open eyes; see, or not? There burn the flames! Oh, thou magnanimous! now I do glory in my genealogy. But thou art but my fiery father; my sweet mother, I know not. Oh, cruel! what hast thou done with her? There lies my puzzle; but thine is greater. Thou knowest not how came ye, hence callest thyself unbegotten; certainly knowest not thy beginning, hence callest thyself unbegun. I know that of me, which thou knowest not of thyself, oh, thou omnipotent. There is some unsuffusing thing beyond thee, thou clear spirit, to whom all thy eternity is but time, all thy creativeness mechanical. Through thee, thy flaming self, my scorched eyes do dimly see it. Oh, thou foundling fire, thou hermit immemorial, thou too hast thy incommunicable riddle, thy unparticipated grief. Here again with haughty agony, I read my sire. Leap! leap up, and lick the sky! I leap with thee; I burn with thee; would fain be welded with thee; defyingly I worship thee!”\n\n“The boat! the boat!” cried Starbuck, “look at thy boat, old man!”\n\nAhab’s harpoon, the one forged at Perth’s fire, remained firmly lashed in its conspicuous crotch, so that it projected beyond his whale-boat’s bow; but the sea that had stove its bottom had caused the loose leather sheath to drop off; and from the keen steel barb there now came a levelled flame of pale, forked fire. As the silent harpoon burned there like a serpent’s tongue, Starbuck grasped Ahab by the arm—“God, God is against thee, old man; forbear! ’tis an ill voyage! ill begun, ill continued; let me square the yards, while we may, old man, and make a fair wind of it homewards, to go on a better voyage than this.”\n\nOverhearing Starbuck, the panic-stricken crew instantly ran to the braces—though not a sail was left aloft. For the moment all the aghast mate’s thoughts seemed theirs; they raised a half mutinous cry. But dashing the rattling lightning links to the deck, and snatching the burning harpoon, Ahab waved it like a torch among them; swearing to transfix with it the first sailor that but cast loose a rope’s end. Petrified by his aspect, and still more shrinking from the fiery dart that he held, the men fell back in dismay, and Ahab again spoke:—\n\n“All your oaths to hunt the White Whale are as binding as mine; and heart, soul, and body, lungs and life, old Ahab is bound. And that ye may know to what tune this heart beats; look ye here; thus I blow out the last fear!” And with one blast of his breath he extinguished the flame.\n\nAs in the hurricane that sweeps the plain, men fly the neighborhood of some lone, gigantic elm, whose very height and strength but render it so much the more unsafe, because so much the more a mark for thunderbolts; so at those last words of Ahab’s many of the mariners did run from him in a terror of dismay.\n\nAhab standing by the helm. Starbuck approaching him.\n\n“We must send down the main-top-sail yard, sir. The band is working loose and the lee lift is half-stranded. Shall I strike it, sir?”\n\n“Strike nothing; lash it. If I had sky-sail poles, I’d sway them up now.”\n\n“Sir!—in God’s name!—sir?”\n“Well.”\n\n“The anchors are working, sir. Shall I get them inboard?”\n\n“Strike nothing, and stir nothing, but lash everything. The wind rises, but it has not got up to my table-lands yet. Quick, and see to it.—By masts and keels! he takes me for the hunch-backed skipper of some coasting smack. Send down my main-top-sail yard! Ho, gluepots! Loftiest trucks were made for wildest winds, and this brain-truck of mine now sails amid the cloud-scud. Shall I strike that? Oh, none but cowards send down their brain-trucks in tempest time. What a hooroosh aloft there! I would e’en take it for sublime, did I not know that the colic is a noisy malady. Oh, take medicine, take medicine!”\n\n# CHAPTER 121. Midnight.—The Forecastle Bulwarks.\n\nStubb and Flask mounted on them, and passing additional lashings over the anchors there hanging.\n“No, Stubb; you may pound that knot there as much as you please, but you will never pound into me what you were just now saying. And how long ago is it since you said the very contrary? Didn’t you once say that whatever ship Ahab sails in, that ship should pay something extra on its insurance policy, just as though it were loaded with powder barrels aft and boxes of lucifers forward? Stop, now; didn’t you say so?”\n\n“Well, suppose I did? What then? I’ve part changed my flesh since that time, why not my mind? Besides, supposing we are loaded with powder barrels aft and lucifers forward; how the devil could the lucifers get afire in this drenching spray here? Why, my little man, you have pretty red hair, but you couldn’t get afire now. Shake yourself; you’re Aquarius, or the water-bearer, Flask; might fill pitchers at your coat collar. Don’t you see, then, that for these extra risks the Marine Insurance companies have extra guarantees? Here are hydrants, Flask. But hark, again, and I’ll answer ye the other thing. First take your leg off from the crown of the anchor here, though, so I can pass the rope; now listen. What’s the mighty difference between holding a mast’s lightning-rod in the storm, and standing close by a mast that hasn’t got any lightning-rod at all in a storm? Don’t you see, you timber-head, that no harm can come to the holder of the rod, unless the mast is first struck? What are you talking about, then? Not one ship in a hundred carries rods, and Ahab,—aye, man, and all of us,—were in no more danger then, in my poor opinion, than all the crews in ten thousand ships now sailing the seas. Why, you King-Post, you, I suppose you would have every man in the world go about with a small lightning-rod running up the corner of his hat, like a militia officer’s skewered feather, and trailing behind like his sash. Why don’t ye be sensible, Flask? it’s easy to be sensible; why don’t ye, then? any man with half an eye can be sensible.”\n\n“I don’t know that, Stubb. You sometimes find it rather hard.”\n\n“Yes, when a fellow’s soaked through, it’s hard to be sensible, that’s a fact. And I am about drenched with this spray. Never mind; catch the turn there, and pass it. Seems to me we are lashing down these anchors now as if they were never going to be used again. Tying these two anchors here, Flask, seems like tying a man’s hands behind him. And what big generous hands they are, to be sure. These are your iron fists, hey? What a hold they have, too! I wonder, Flask, whether the world is anchored anywhere; if she is, she swings with an uncommon long cable, though. There, hammer that knot down, and we’ve done. So; next to touching land, lighting on deck is the most satisfactory. I say, just wring out my jacket skirts, will ye? Thank ye. They laugh at long-togs so, Flask; but seems to me, a long tailed coat ought always to be worn in all storms afloat. The tails tapering down that way, serve to carry off the water, d’ye see. Same with cocked hats; the cocks form gable-end eave-troughs, Flask. No more monkey-jackets and tarpaulins for me; I must mount a swallow-tail, and drive down a beaver; so. Halloa! whew! there goes my tarpaulin overboard; Lord, Lord, that the winds that come from heaven should be so unmannerly! This is a nasty night, lad.”\n\n# CHAPTER 122. Midnight Aloft.—Thunder and Lightning.\n\nThe main-top-sail yard.—Tashtego passing new lashings around it.\n\n“Um, um, um. Stop that thunder! Plenty too much thunder up here. What’s the use of thunder? Um, um, um. We don’t want thunder; we want rum; give us a glass of rum. Um, um, um!” CHAPTER 123. The Musket.\n\nDuring the most violent shocks of the Typhoon, the man at the Pequod’s jaw-bone tiller had several times been reelingly hurled to the deck by its spasmodic motions, even though preventer tackles had been attached to it—for they were slack—because some play to the tiller was indispensable.\n\nIn a severe gale like this, while the ship is but a tossed shuttlecock to the blast, it is by no means uncommon to see the needles in the compasses, at intervals, go round and round. It was thus with the Pequod’s; at almost every shock the helmsman had not failed to notice the whirling velocity with which they revolved upon the cards; it is a sight that hardly anyone can behold without some sort of unwonted emotion.\n\nSome hours after midnight, the Typhoon abated so much, that through the strenuous exertions of Starbuck and Stubb—one engaged forward and the other aft—the shivered remnants of the jib and fore and main-top-sails were cut adrift from the spars, and went eddying away to leeward, like the feathers of an albatross, which sometimes are cast to the winds when that storm-tossed bird is on the wing.\n\nThe three corresponding new sails were now bent and reefed, and a storm-trysail was set further aft; so that the ship soon went through the water with some precision again; and the course—for the present, East-south-east—which he was to steer, if practicable, was once more given to the helmsman. For during the violence of the gale, he had only steered according to its vicissitudes. But as he was now bringing the ship as near her course as possible, watching the compass meanwhile, lo! a good sign! the wind seemed coming round astern; aye, the foul breeze became fair!\n\nInstantly the yards were squared, to the lively song of “Ho! the fair wind! oh-ye-ho, cheerly men!” the crew singing for joy, that so promising an event should so soon have falsified the evil portents preceding it.\n\nIn compliance with the standing order of his commander—to report immediately, and at any one of the twenty-four hours, any decided change in the affairs of the deck,—Starbuck had no sooner trimmed the yards to the breeze—however reluctantly and gloomily,—than he mechanically went below to apprise Captain Ahab of the circumstance.\n\nEre knocking at his state-room, he involuntarily paused before it a moment. The cabin lamp—taking long swings this way and that—was burning fitfully, and casting fitful shadows upon the old man’s bolted door,—a thin one, with fixed blinds inserted, in place of upper panels. The isolated subterraneousness of the cabin made a certain humming silence to reign there, though it was hooped round by all the roar of the elements. The loaded muskets in the rack were shiningly revealed, as they stood upright against the forward bulkhead. Starbuck was an honest, upright man; but out of Starbuck’s heart, at that instant when he saw the muskets, there strangely evolved an evil thought; but so blent with its neutral or good accompaniments that for the instant he hardly knew it for itself.\n\n“He would have shot me once,” he murmured, “yes, there’s the very musket that he pointed at me;—that one with the studded stock; let me touch it—lift it. Strange, that I, who have handled so many deadly lances, strange, that I should shake so now. Loaded? I must see. Aye, aye; and powder in the pan;—that’s not good. Best spill it?—wait. I’ll cure myself of this. I’ll hold the musket boldly while I think.—I come to report a fair wind to him. But how fair? Fair for death and doom,—that’s fair for Moby Dick. It’s a fair wind that’s only fair for that accursed fish.—The very tube he pointed at me!—the very one; this one—I hold it here; he would have killed me with the very thing I handle now.—Aye and he would fain kill all his crew. Does he not say he will not strike his spars to any gale? Has he not dashed his heavenly quadrant? and in these same perilous seas, gropes he not his way by mere dead reckoning of the error-abounding log? and in this very Typhoon, did he not swear that he would have no lightning-rods? But shall this crazed old man be tamely suffered to drag a whole ship’s company down to doom with him?—Yes, it would make him the wilful murderer of thirty men and more, if this ship come to any deadly harm; and come to deadly harm, my soul swears this ship will, if Ahab have his way. If, then, he were this instant—put aside, that crime would not be his. Ha! is he muttering in his sleep? Yes, just there,—in there, he’s sleeping. Sleeping? aye, but still alive, and soon awake again. I can’t withstand thee, then, old man. Not reasoning; not remonstrance; not entreaty wilt thou hearken to; all this thou scornest. Flat obedience to thy own flat commands, this is all thou breathest. Aye, and say’st the men have vow’d thy vow; say’st all of us are Ahabs. Great God forbid!—But is there no other way? no lawful way?—Make him a prisoner to be taken home? What! hope to wrest this old man’s living power from his own living hands? Only a fool would try it. Say he were pinioned even; knotted all over with ropes and hawsers; chained down to ring-bolts on this cabin floor; he would be more hideous than a caged tiger, then. I could not endure the sight; could not possibly fly his howlings; all comfort, sleep itself, inestimable reason would leave me on the long intolerable voyage. What, then, remains? The land is hundreds of leagues away, and locked Japan the nearest. I stand alone here upon an open sea, with two oceans and a whole continent between me and law.—Aye, aye, ’tis so.—Is heaven a murderer when its lightning strikes a would-be murderer in his bed, tindering sheets and skin together?—And would I be a murderer, then, if”—and slowly, stealthily, and half sideways looking, he placed the loaded musket’s end against the door.\n\n“On this level, Ahab’s hammock swings within; his head this way. A touch, and Starbuck may survive to hug his wife and child again.—Oh Mary! Mary!—boy! boy! boy!—But if I wake thee not to death, old man, who can tell to what unsounded deeps Starbuck’s body this day week may sink, with all the crew! Great God, where art Thou? Shall I? shall I?—The wind has gone down and shifted, sir; the fore and main topsails are reefed and set; she heads her course.”\n\n“Stern all! Oh Moby Dick, I clutch thy heart at last!”\n\nSuch were the sounds that now came hurtling from out the old man’s tormented sleep, as if Starbuck’s voice had caused the long dumb dream to speak.\n\nThe yet levelled musket shook like a drunkard’s arm against the panel; Starbuck seemed wrestling with an angel; but turning from the door, he placed the death-tube in its rack, and left the place.\n\n“He’s too sound asleep, Mr. Stubb; go thou down, and wake him, and tell him. I must see to the deck here. Thou know’st what to say.”\n\n# CHAPTER 124. The Needle.\n\nNext morning the not-yet-subsided sea rolled in long slow billows of mighty bulk, and striving in the Pequod’s gurgling track, pushed her on like giants’ palms outspread. The strong, unstaggering breeze abounded so, that sky and air seemed vast outbellying sails; the whole world boomed before the wind. Muffled in the full morning light, the invisible sun was only known by the spread intensity of his place; where his bayonet rays moved on in stacks. Emblazonings, as of crowned Babylonian kings and queens, reigned over everything. The sea was as a crucible of molten gold, that bubblingly leaps with light and heat.\n\nLong maintaining an enchanted silence, Ahab stood apart; and every time the tetering ship loweringly pitched down her bowsprit, he turned to eye the bright sun’s rays produced ahead; and when she profoundly settled by the stern, he turned behind, and saw the sun’s rearward place, and how the same yellow rays were blending with his undeviating wake.\n\n“Ha, ha, my ship! thou mightest well be taken now for the sea-chariot of the sun. Ho, ho! all ye nations before my prow, I bring the sun to ye! Yoke on the further billows; hallo! a tandem, I drive the sea!”\n\nBut suddenly reined back by some counter thought, he hurried towards the helm, huskily demanding how the ship was heading.\n\n“East-sou-east, sir,” said the frightened steersman.\n\n“Thou liest!” smiting him with his clenched fist. “Heading East at this hour in the morning, and the sun astern?”\n\nUpon this every soul was confounded; for the phenomenon just then observed by Ahab had unaccountably escaped every one else; but its very blinding palpableness must have been the cause.\n\nThrusting his head half way into the binnacle, Ahab caught one glimpse of the compasses; his uplifted arm slowly fell; for a moment he almost seemed to stagger. Standing behind him Starbuck looked, and lo! the two compasses pointed East, and the Pequod was as infallibly going West.\n\nBut ere the first wild alarm could get out abroad among the crew, the old man with a rigid laugh exclaimed, “I have it! It has happened before. Mr. Starbuck, last night’s thunder turned our compasses—that’s all. Thou hast before now heard of such a thing, I take it.”\n\n“Aye; but never before has it happened to me, sir,” said the pale mate, gloomily.\n\nHere, it must needs be said, that accidents like this have in more than one case occurred to ships in violent storms. The magnetic energy, as developed in the mariner’s needle, is, as all know, essentially one with the electricity beheld in heaven; hence it is not to be much marvelled at, that such things should be. Instances where the lightning has actually struck the vessel, so as to smite down some of the spars and rigging, the effect upon the needle has at times been still more fatal; all its loadstone virtue being annihilated, so that the before magnetic steel was of no more use than an old wife’s knitting needle. But in either case, the needle never again, of itself, recovers the original virtue thus marred or lost; and if the binnacle compasses be affected, the same fate reaches all the others that may be in the ship; even were the lowermost one inserted into the kelson.\n\nDeliberately standing before the binnacle, and eyeing the transpointed compasses, the old man, with the sharp of his extended hand, now took the precise bearing of the sun, and satisfied that the needles were exactly inverted, shouted out his orders for the ship’s course to be changed accordingly. The yards were hard up; and once more the Pequod thrust her undaunted bows into the opposing wind, for the supposed fair one had only been juggling her.\n\nMeanwhile, whatever were his own secret thoughts, Starbuck said nothing, but quietly he issued all requisite orders; while Stubb and Flask—who in some small degree seemed then to be sharing his feelings—likewise unmurmuringly acquiesced. As for the men, though some of them lowly rumbled, their fear of Ahab was greater than their fear of Fate. But as ever before, the pagan harpooneers remained almost wholly unimpressed; or if impressed, it was only with a certain magnetism shot into their congenial hearts from inflexible Ahab’s.\n\nFor a space the old man walked the deck in rolling reveries. But chancing to slip with his ivory heel, he saw the crushed copper sight-tubes of the quadrant he had the day before dashed to the deck.\n\n“Thou poor, proud heaven-gazer and sun’s pilot! yesterday I wrecked thee, and to-day the compasses would fain have wrecked me. So, so. But Ahab is lord over the level loadstone yet. Mr. Starbuck—a lance without a pole; a top-maul, and the smallest of the sail-maker’s needles. Quick!”\n\nAccessory, perhaps, to the impulse dictating the thing he was now about to do, were certain prudential motives, whose object might have been to revive the spirits of his crew by a stroke of his subtile skill, in a matter so wondrous as that of the inverted compasses. Besides, the old man well knew that to steer by transpointed needles, though clumsily practicable, was not a thing to be passed over by superstitious sailors, without some shudderings and evil portents.\n\n“Men,” said he, steadily turning upon the crew, as the mate handed him the things he had demanded, “my men, the thunder turned old Ahab’s needles; but out of this bit of steel Ahab can make one of his own, that will point as true as any.”\n\nAbashed glances of servile wonder were exchanged by the sailors, as this was said; and with fascinated eyes they awaited whatever magic might follow. But Starbuck looked away.\n\nWith a blow from the top-maul Ahab knocked off the steel head of the lance, and then handing to the mate the long iron rod remaining, bade him hold it upright, without its touching the deck. Then, with the maul, after repeatedly smiting the upper end of this iron rod, he placed the blunted needle endwise on the top of it, and less strongly hammered that, several times, the mate still holding the rod as before. Then going through some small strange motions with it—whether indispensable to the magnetizing of the steel, or merely intended to augment the awe of the crew, is uncertain—he called for linen thread; and moving to the binnacle, slipped out the two reversed needles there, and horizontally suspended the sail-needle by its middle, over one of the compass-cards. At first, the steel went round and round, quivering and vibrating at either end; but at last it settled to its place, when Ahab, who had been intently watching for this result, stepped frankly back from the binnacle, and pointing his stretched arm towards it, exclaimed,—“Look ye, for yourselves, if Ahab be not lord of the level loadstone! The sun is East, and that compass swears it!”\n\nOne after another they peered in, for nothing but their own eyes could persuade such ignorance as theirs, and one after another they slunk away.\n\nIn his fiery eyes of scorn and triumph, you then saw Ahab in all his fatal pride.\n\n# CHAPTER 125. The Log and Line.\n\nWhile now the fated Pequod had been so long afloat this voyage, the log and line had but very seldom been in use. Owing to a confident reliance upon other means of determining the vessel’s place, some merchantmen, and many whalemen, especially when cruising, wholly neglect to heave the log; though at the same time, and frequently more for form’s sake than anything else, regularly putting down upon the customary slate the course steered by the ship, as well as the presumed average rate of progression every hour. It had been thus with the Pequod. The wooden reel and angular log attached hung, long untouched, just beneath the railing of the after bulwarks. Rains and spray had damped it; sun and wind had warped it; all the elements had combined to rot a thing that hung so idly. But heedless of all this, his mood seized Ahab, as he happened to glance upon the reel, not many hours after the magnet scene, and he remembered how his quadrant was no more, and recalled his frantic oath about the level log and line. The ship was sailing plungingly; astern the billows rolled in riots.\n\n“Forward, there! Heave the log!”\n\nTwo seamen came. The golden-hued Tahitian and the grizzly Manxman. “Take the reel, one of ye, I’ll heave.”\n\nThey went towards the extreme stern, on the ship’s lee side, where the deck, with the oblique energy of the wind, was now almost dipping into the creamy, sidelong-rushing sea.\n\nThe Manxman took the reel, and holding it high up, by the projecting handle-ends of the spindle, round which the spool of line revolved, so stood with the angular log hanging downwards, till Ahab advanced to him.\n\nAhab stood before him, and was lightly unwinding some thirty or forty turns to form a preliminary hand-coil to toss overboard, when the old Manxman, who was intently eyeing both him and the line, made bold to speak.\n\n“Sir, I mistrust it; this line looks far gone, long heat and wet have spoiled it.”\n\n“’Twill hold, old gentleman. Long heat and wet, have they spoiled thee? Thou seem’st to hold. Or, truer perhaps, life holds thee; not thou it.”\n\n“I hold the spool, sir. But just as my captain says. With these grey hairs of mine ’tis not worth while disputing, ’specially with a superior, who’ll ne’er confess.”\n\n“What’s that? There now’s a patched professor in Queen Nature’s granite-founded College; but methinks he’s too subservient. Where wert thou born?”\n\n“In the little rocky Isle of Man, sir.”\n\n“Excellent! Thou’st hit the world by that.”\n\n“I know not, sir, but I was born there.”\n\n“In the Isle of Man, hey? Well, the other way, it’s good. Here’s a man from Man; a man born in once independent Man, and now unmanned of Man; which is sucked in—by what? Up with the reel! The dead, blind wall butts all inquiring heads at last. Up with it! So.”\n\nThe log was heaved. The loose coils rapidly straightened out in a long dragging line astern, and then, instantly, the reel began to whirl. In turn, jerkingly raised and lowered by the rolling billows, the towing resistance of the log caused the old reelman to stagger strangely.\n\n“Hold hard!”\n\nSnap! the overstrained line sagged down in one long festoon; the tugging log was gone.\n\n“I crush the quadrant, the thunder turns the needles, and now the mad sea parts the log-line. But Ahab can mend all. Haul in here, Tahitian; reel up, Manxman. And look ye, let the carpenter make another log, and mend thou the line. See to it.”\n\n“There he goes now; to him nothing’s happened; but to me, the skewer seems loosening out of the middle of the world. Haul in, haul in, Tahitian! These lines run whole, and whirling out: come in broken, and dragging slow. Ha, Pip? come to help; eh, Pip?”\n\n“Pip? whom call ye Pip? Pip jumped from the whale-boat. Pip’s missing. Let’s see now if ye haven’t fished him up here, fisherman. It drags hard; I guess he’s holding on. Jerk him, Tahiti! Jerk him off; we haul in no cowards here. Ho! there’s his arm just breaking water. A hatchet! a hatchet! cut it off—we haul in no cowards here. Captain Ahab! sir, sir! here’s Pip, trying to get on board again.”\n\n“Peace, thou crazy loon,” cried the Manxman, seizing him by the arm. “Away from the quarter-deck!”\n\n“The greater idiot ever scolds the lesser,” muttered Ahab, advancing. “Hands off from that holiness! Where sayest thou Pip was, boy?\n\n“Astern there, sir, astern! Lo! lo!”\n\n“And who art thou, boy? I see not my reflection in the vacant pupils of thy eyes. Oh God! that man should be a thing for immortal souls to sieve through! Who art thou, boy?”\n\n“Bell-boy, sir; ship’s-crier; ding, dong, ding! Pip! Pip! Pip! One hundred pounds of clay reward for Pip; five feet high—looks cowardly—quickest known by that! Ding, dong, ding! Who’s seen Pip the coward?”\n\n“There can be no hearts above the snow-line. Oh, ye frozen heavens! look down here. Ye did beget this luckless child, and have abandoned him, ye creative libertines. Here, boy; Ahab’s cabin shall be Pip’s home henceforth, while Ahab lives. Thou touchest my inmost centre, boy; thou art tied to me by cords woven of my heart-strings. Come, let’s down.”\n\n“What’s this? here’s velvet shark-skin,” intently gazing at Ahab’s hand, and feeling it. “Ah, now, had poor Pip but felt so kind a thing as this, perhaps he had ne’er been lost! This seems to me, sir, as a man-rope; something that weak souls may hold by. Oh, sir, let old Perth now come and rivet these two hands together; the black one with the white, for I will not let this go.”\n\n“Oh, boy, nor will I thee, unless I should thereby drag thee to worse horrors than are here. Come, then, to my cabin. Lo! ye believers in gods all goodness, and in man all ill, lo you! see the omniscient gods oblivious of suffering man; and man, though idiotic, and knowing not what he does, yet full of the sweet things of love and gratitude. Come! I feel prouder leading thee by thy black hand, than though I grasped an Emperor’s!”\n\n“There go two daft ones now,” muttered the old Manxman. “One daft with strength, the other daft with weakness. But here’s the end of the rotten line—all dripping, too. Mend it, eh? I think we had best have a new line altogether. I’ll see Mr. Stubb about it.”\n\n# CHAPTER 126. The Life-Buoy.\n\nSteering now south-eastward by Ahab’s levelled steel, and her progress solely determined by Ahab’s level log and line; the Pequod held on her path towards the Equator. Making so long a passage through such unfrequented waters, descrying no ships, and ere long, sideways impelled by unvarying trade winds, over waves monotonously mild; all these seemed the strange calm things preluding some riotous and desperate scene.\n\nAt last, when the ship drew near to the outskirts, as it were, of the Equatorial fishing-ground, and in the deep darkness that goes before the dawn, was sailing by a cluster of rocky islets; the watch—then headed by Flask—was startled by a cry so plaintively wild and unearthly—like half-articulated wailings of the ghosts of all Herod’s murdered Innocents—that one and all, they started from their reveries, and for the space of some moments stood, or sat, or leaned all transfixedly listening, like the carved Roman slave, while that wild cry remained within hearing. The Christian or civilized part of the crew said it was mermaids, and shuddered; but the pagan harpooneers remained unappalled. Yet the grey Manxman—the oldest mariner of all—declared that the wild thrilling sounds that were heard, were the voices of newly drowned men in the sea.\n\nBelow in his hammock, Ahab did not hear of this till grey dawn, when he came to the deck; it was then recounted to him by Flask, not unaccompanied with hinted dark meanings. He hollowly laughed, and thus explained the wonder.\n\nThose rocky islands the ship had passed were the resort of great numbers of seals, and some young seals that had lost their dams, or some dams that had lost their cubs, must have risen nigh the ship and kept company with her, crying and sobbing with their human sort of wail. But this only the more affected some of them, because most mariners cherish a very superstitious feeling about seals, arising not only from their peculiar tones when in distress, but also from the human look of their round heads and semi-intelligent faces, seen peeringly uprising from the water alongside. In the sea, under certain circumstances, seals have more than once been mistaken for men.\n\nBut the bodings of the crew were destined to receive a most plausible confirmation in the fate of one of their number that morning. At sun-rise this man went from his hammock to his mast-head at the fore; and whether it was that he was not yet half waked from his sleep (for sailors sometimes go aloft in a transition state), whether it was thus with the man, there is now no telling; but, be that as it may, he had not been long at his perch, when a cry was heard—a cry and a rushing—and looking up, they saw a falling phantom in the air; and looking down, a little tossed heap of white bubbles in the blue of the sea.\n\nThe life-buoy—a long slender cask—was dropped from the stern, where it always hung obedient to a cunning spring; but no hand rose to seize it, and the sun having long beat upon this cask it had shrunken, so that it slowly filled, and that parched wood also filled at its every pore; and the studded iron-bound cask followed the sailor to the bottom, as if to yield him his pillow, though in sooth but a hard one.\n\nAnd thus the first man of the Pequod that mounted the mast to look out for the White Whale, on the White Whale’s own peculiar ground; that man was swallowed up in the deep. But few, perhaps, thought of that at the time. Indeed, in some sort, they were not grieved at this event, at least as a portent; for they regarded it, not as a foreshadowing of evil in the future, but as the fulfilment of an evil already presaged. They declared that now they knew the reason of those wild shrieks they had heard the night before. But again the old Manxman said nay.\n\nThe lost life-buoy was now to be replaced; Starbuck was directed to see to it; but as no cask of sufficient lightness could be found, and as in the feverish eagerness of what seemed the approaching crisis of the voyage, all hands were impatient of any toil but what was directly connected with its final end, whatever that might prove to be; therefore, they were going to leave the ship’s stern unprovided with a buoy, when by certain strange signs and inuendoes Queequeg hinted a hint concerning his coffin.\n\n“A life-buoy of a coffin!” cried Starbuck, starting.\n\n“Rather queer, that, I should say,” said Stubb.\n\n“It will make a good enough one,” said Flask, “the carpenter here can arrange it easily.”\n\n“Bring it up; there’s nothing else for it,” said Starbuck, after a melancholy pause. “Rig it, carpenter; do not look at me so—the coffin, I mean. Dost thou hear me? Rig it.”\n\n“And shall I nail down the lid, sir?” moving his hand as with a hammer.\n\n“Aye.”\n\n“And shall I caulk the seams, sir?” moving his hand as with a caulking-iron.\n\n“Aye.”\n\n“And shall I then pay over the same with pitch, sir?” moving his hand as with a pitch-pot.\n\n“Away! what possesses thee to this? Make a life-buoy of the coffin, and no more.—Mr. Stubb, Mr. Flask, come forward with me.”\n\n“He goes off in a huff. The whole he can endure; at the parts he baulks. Now I don’t like this. I make a leg for Captain Ahab, and he wears it like a gentleman; but I make a bandbox for Queequeg, and he won’t put his head into it. Are all my pains to go for nothing with that coffin? And now I’m ordered to make a life-buoy of it. It’s like turning an old coat; going to bring the flesh on the other side now. I don’t like this cobbling sort of business—I don’t like it at all; it’s undignified; it’s not my place. Let tinkers’ brats do tinkerings; we are their betters. I like to take in hand none but clean, virgin, fair-and-square mathematical jobs, something that regularly begins at the beginning, and is at the middle when midway, and comes to an end at the conclusion; not a cobbler’s job, that’s at an end in the middle, and at the beginning at the end. It’s the old woman’s tricks to be giving cobbling jobs. Lord! what an affection all old women have for tinkers. I know an old woman of sixty-five who ran away with a bald-headed young tinker once. And that’s the reason I never would work for lonely widow old women ashore, when I kept my job-shop in the Vineyard; they might have taken it into their lonely old heads to run off with me. But heigh-ho! there are no caps at sea but snow-caps. Let me see. Nail down the lid; caulk the seams; pay over the same with pitch; batten them down tight, and hang it with the snap-spring over the ship’s stern. Were ever such things done before with a coffin? Some superstitious old carpenters, now, would be tied up in the rigging, ere they would do the job. But I’m made of knotty Aroostook hemlock; I don’t budge. Cruppered with a coffin! Sailing about with a grave-yard tray! But never mind. We workers in woods make bridal-bedsteads and card-tables, as well as coffins and hearses. We work by the month, or by the job, or by the profit; not for us to ask the why and wherefore of our work, unless it be too confounded cobbling, and then we stash it if we can. Hem! I’ll do the job, now, tenderly. I’ll have me—let’s see—how many in the ship’s company, all told? But I’ve forgotten. Any way, I’ll have me thirty separate, Turk’s-headed life-lines, each three feet long hanging all round to the coffin. Then, if the hull go down, there’ll be thirty lively fellows all fighting for one coffin, a sight not seen very often beneath the sun! Come hammer, caulking-iron, pitch-pot, and marling-spike! Let’s to it.”\n\n# CHAPTER 127. The Deck.\n\nThe coffin laid upon two line-tubs, between the vice-bench and the open hatchway; the Carpenter caulking its seams; the string of twisted oakum slowly unwinding from a large roll of it placed in the bosom of his frock.—Ahab comes slowly from the cabin-gangway, and hears Pip following him.\n\n“Back, lad; I will be with ye again presently. He goes! Not this hand complies with my humor more genially than that boy.—Middle aisle of a church! What’s here?”\n\n“Life-buoy, sir. Mr. Starbuck’s orders. Oh, look, sir! Beware the hatchway!”\n\n“Thank ye, man. Thy coffin lies handy to the vault.”\n\n“Sir? The hatchway? oh! So it does, sir, so it does.”\n\n“Art not thou the leg-maker? Look, did not this stump come from thy shop?”\n\n“I believe it did, sir; does the ferrule stand, sir?”\n\n“Well enough. But art thou not also the undertaker?”\n\n“Aye, sir; I patched up this thing here as a coffin for Queequeg; but they’ve set me now to turning it into something else.”\n\n“Then tell me; art thou not an arrant, all-grasping, intermeddling, monopolising, heathenish old scamp, to be one day making legs, and the next day coffins to clap them in, and yet again life-buoys out of those same coffins? Thou art as unprincipled as the gods, and as much of a jack-of-all-trades.”\n\n“But I do not mean anything, sir. I do as I do.”\n\n“The gods again. Hark ye, dost thou not ever sing working about a coffin? The Titans, they say, hummed snatches when chipping out the craters for volcanoes; and the grave-digger in the play sings, spade in hand. Dost thou never?”\n\n“Sing, sir? Do I sing? Oh, I’m indifferent enough, sir, for that; but the reason why the grave-digger made music must have been because there was none in his spade, sir. But the caulking mallet is full of it. Hark to it.”\n\n“Aye, and that’s because the lid there’s a sounding-board; and what in all things makes the sounding-board is this—there’s naught beneath. And yet, a coffin with a body in it rings pretty much the same, Carpenter. Hast thou ever helped carry a bier, and heard the coffin knock against the churchyard gate, going in?\n\n“Faith, sir, I’ve——”\n\n“Faith? What’s that?”\n\n“Why, faith, sir, it’s only a sort of exclamation-like—that’s all, sir.”\n\n“Um, um; go on.”\n\n“I was about to say, sir, that——”\n\n“Art thou a silk-worm? Dost thou spin thy own shroud out of thyself? Look at thy bosom! Despatch! and get these traps out of sight.”\n\n“He goes aft. That was sudden, now; but squalls come sudden in hot latitudes. I’ve heard that the Isle of Albemarle, one of the Gallipagos, is cut by the Equator right in the middle. Seems to me some sort of Equator cuts yon old man, too, right in his middle. He’s always under the Line—fiery hot, I tell ye! He’s looking this way—come, oakum; quick. Here we go again. This wooden mallet is the cork, and I’m the professor of musical glasses—tap, tap!”\n\n(Ahab to himself.)\n\n“There’s a sight! There’s a sound! The greyheaded woodpecker tapping the hollow tree! Blind and dumb might well be envied now. See! that thing rests on two line-tubs, full of tow-lines. A most malicious wag, that fellow. Rat-tat! So man’s seconds tick! Oh! how immaterial are all materials! What things real are there, but imponderable thoughts? Here now’s the very dreaded symbol of grim death, by a mere hap, made the expressive sign of the help and hope of most endangered life. A life-buoy of a coffin! Does it go further? Can it be that in some spiritual sense the coffin is, after all, but an immortality-preserver! I’ll think of that. But no. So far gone am I in the dark side of earth, that its other side, the theoretic bright one, seems but uncertain twilight to me. Will ye never have done, Carpenter, with that accursed sound? I go below; let me not see that thing here when I return again. Now, then, Pip, we’ll talk this over; I do suck most wondrous philosophies from thee! Some unknown conduits from the unknown worlds must empty into thee!”\n\n# CHAPTER 128. The Pequod Meets The Rachel.\n\nNext day, a large ship, the Rachel, was descried, bearing directly down upon the Pequod, all her spars thickly clustering with men. At the time the Pequod was making good speed through the water; but as the broad-winged windward stranger shot nigh to her, the boastful sails all fell together as blank bladders that are burst, and all life fled from the smitten hull.\n\n“Bad news; she brings bad news,” muttered the old Manxman. But ere her commander, who, with trumpet to mouth, stood up in his boat; ere he could hopefully hail, Ahab’s voice was heard.\n\n“Hast seen the White Whale?”\n\n“Aye, yesterday. Have ye seen a whale-boat adrift?”\n\nThrottling his joy, Ahab negatively answered this unexpected question; and would then have fain boarded the stranger, when the stranger captain himself, having stopped his vessel’s way, was seen descending her side. A few keen pulls, and his boat-hook soon clinched the Pequod’s main-chains, and he sprang to the deck. Immediately he was recognised by Ahab for a Nantucketer he knew. But no formal salutation was exchanged.\n\n“Where was he?—not killed!—not killed!” cried Ahab, closely advancing. “How was it?”\n\nIt seemed that somewhat late on the afternoon of the day previous, while three of the stranger’s boats were engaged with a shoal of whales, which had led them some four or five miles from the ship; and while they were yet in swift chase to windward, the white hump and head of Moby Dick had suddenly loomed up out of the water, not very far to leeward; whereupon, the fourth rigged boat—a reserved one—had been instantly lowered in chase. After a keen sail before the wind, this fourth boat—the swiftest keeled of all—seemed to have succeeded in fastening—at least, as well as the man at the mast-head could tell anything about it. In the distance he saw the diminished dotted boat; and then a swift gleam of bubbling white water; and after that nothing more; whence it was concluded that the stricken whale must have indefinitely run away with his pursuers, as often happens. There was some apprehension, but no positive alarm, as yet. The recall signals were placed in the rigging; darkness came on; and forced to pick up her three far to windward boats—ere going in quest of the fourth one in the precisely opposite direction—the ship had not only been necessitated to leave that boat to its fate till near midnight, but, for the time, to increase her distance from it. But the rest of her crew being at last safe aboard, she crowded all sail—stunsail on stunsail—after the missing boat; kindling a fire in her try-pots for a beacon; and every other man aloft on the look-out. But though when she had thus sailed a sufficient distance to gain the presumed place of the absent ones when last seen; though she then paused to lower her spare boats to pull all around her; and not finding anything, had again dashed on; again paused, and lowered her boats; and though she had thus continued doing till daylight; yet not the least glimpse of the missing keel had been seen.\n\nThe story told, the stranger Captain immediately went on to reveal his object in boarding the Pequod. He desired that ship to unite with his own in the search; by sailing over the sea some four or five miles apart, on parallel lines, and so sweeping a double horizon, as it were.\n\n“I will wager something now,” whispered Stubb to Flask, “that some one in that missing boat wore off that Captain’s best coat; mayhap, his watch—he’s so cursed anxious to get it back. Who ever heard of two pious whale-ships cruising after one missing whale-boat in the height of the whaling season? See, Flask, only see how pale he looks—pale in the very buttons of his eyes—look—it wasn’t the coat—it must have been the—”\n\n“My boy, my own boy is among them. For God’s sake—I beg, I conjure”—here exclaimed the stranger Captain to Ahab, who thus far had but icily received his petition. “For eight-and-forty hours let me charter your ship—I will gladly pay for it, and roundly pay for it—if there be no other way—for eight-and-forty hours only—only that—you must, oh, you must, and you shall do this thing.”\n\n“His son!” cried Stubb, “oh, it’s his son he’s lost! I take back the coat and watch—what says Ahab? We must save that boy.”\n\n“He’s drowned with the rest on ’em, last night,” said the old Manx sailor standing behind them; “I heard; all of ye heard their spirits.”\n\nNow, as it shortly turned out, what made this incident of the Rachel’s the more melancholy, was the circumstance, that not only was one of the Captain’s sons among the number of the missing boat’s crew; but among the number of the other boat’s crews, at the same time, but on the other hand, separated from the ship during the dark vicissitudes of the chase, there had been still another son; as that for a time, the wretched father was plunged to the bottom of the cruellest perplexity; which was only solved for him by his chief mate’s instinctively adopting the ordinary procedure of a whale-ship in such emergencies, that is, when placed between jeopardized but divided boats, always to pick up the majority first. But the captain, for some unknown constitutional reason, had refrained from mentioning all this, and not till forced to it by Ahab’s iciness did he allude to his one yet missing boy; a little lad, but twelve years old, whose father with the earnest but unmisgiving hardihood of a Nantucketer’s paternal love, had thus early sought to initiate him in the perils and wonders of a vocation almost immemorially the destiny of all his race. Nor does it unfrequently occur, that Nantucket captains will send a son of such tender age away from them, for a protracted three or four years’ voyage in some other ship than their own; so that their first knowledge of a whaleman’s career shall be unenervated by any chance display of a father’s natural but untimely partiality, or undue apprehensiveness and concern.\n\nMeantime, now the stranger was still beseeching his poor boon of Ahab; and Ahab still stood like an anvil, receiving every shock, but without the least quivering of his own.\n\n“I will not go,” said the stranger, “till you say aye to me. Do to me as you would have me do to you in the like case. For you too have a boy, Captain Ahab—though but a child, and nestling safely at home now—a child of your old age too—Yes, yes, you relent; I see it—run, run, men, now, and stand by to square in the yards.”\n\n“Avast,” cried Ahab—“touch not a rope-yarn”; then in a voice that prolongingly moulded every word—“Captain Gardiner, I will not do it. Even now I lose time. Good-bye, good-bye. God bless ye, man, and may I forgive myself, but I must go. Mr. Starbuck, look at the binnacle watch, and in three minutes from this present instant warn off all strangers: then brace forward again, and let the ship sail as before.”\n\nHurriedly turning, with averted face, he descended into his cabin, leaving the strange captain transfixed at this unconditional and utter rejection of his so earnest suit. But starting from his enchantment, Gardiner silently hurried to the side; more fell than stepped into his boat, and returned to his ship.\n\nSoon the two ships diverged their wakes; and long as the strange vessel was in view, she was seen to yaw hither and thither at every dark spot, however small, on the sea. This way and that her yards were swung round; starboard and larboard, she continued to tack; now she beat against a head sea; and again it pushed her before it; while all the while, her masts and yards were thickly clustered with men, as three tall cherry trees, when the boys are cherrying among the boughs.\n\nBut by her still halting course and winding, woeful way, you plainly saw that this ship that so wept with spray, still remained without comfort. She was Rachel, weeping for her children, because they were not.\n\n# CHAPTER 129. The Cabin.\n\n(Ahab moving to go on deck; Pip catches him by the hand to follow.)\n\n“Lad, lad, I tell thee thou must not follow Ahab now. The hour is coming when Ahab would not scare thee from him, yet would not have thee by him. There is that in thee, poor lad, which I feel too curing to my malady. Like cures like; and for this hunt, my malady becomes my most desired health. Do thou abide below here, where they shall serve thee, as if thou wert the captain. Aye, lad, thou shalt sit here in my own screwed chair; another screw to it, thou must be.”\n\n“No, no, no! ye have not a whole body, sir; do ye but use poor me for your one lost leg; only tread upon me, sir; I ask no more, so I remain a part of ye.”\n\n“Oh! spite of million villains, this makes me a bigot in the fadeless fidelity of man!—and a black! and crazy!—but methinks like-cures-like applies to him too; he grows so sane again.”\n\n“They tell me, sir, that Stubb did once desert poor little Pip, whose drowned bones now show white, for all the blackness of his living skin. But I will never desert ye, sir, as Stubb did him. Sir, I must go with ye.”\n\n“If thou speakest thus to me much more, Ahab’s purpose keels up in him. I tell thee no; it cannot be.”\n\n“Oh good master, master, master!\n\n“Weep so, and I will murder thee! have a care, for Ahab too is mad. Listen, and thou wilt often hear my ivory foot upon the deck, and still know that I am there. And now I quit thee. Thy hand!—Met! True art thou, lad, as the circumference to its centre. So: God for ever bless thee; and if it come to that,—God for ever save thee, let what will befall.”\n\n(Ahab goes; Pip steps one step forward.)\n\n“Here he this instant stood; I stand in his air,—but I’m alone. Now were even poor Pip here I could endure it, but he’s missing. Pip! Pip! Ding, dong, ding! Who’s seen Pip? He must be up here; let’s try the door. What? neither lock, nor bolt, nor bar; and yet there’s no opening it. It must be the spell; he told me to stay here: Aye, and told me this screwed chair was mine. Here, then, I’ll seat me, against the transom, in the ship’s full middle, all her keel and her three masts before me. Here, our old sailors say, in their black seventy-fours great admirals sometimes sit at table, and lord it over rows of captains and lieutenants. Ha! what’s this? epaulets! epaulets! the epaulets all come crowding! Pass round the decanters; glad to see ye; fill up, monsieurs! What an odd feeling, now, when a black boy’s host to white men with gold lace upon their coats!—Monsieurs, have ye seen one Pip?—a little negro lad, five feet high, hang-dog look, and cowardly! Jumped from a whale-boat once;—seen him? No! Well then, fill up again, captains, and let’s drink shame upon all cowards! I name no names. Shame upon them! Put one foot upon the table. Shame upon all cowards.—Hist! above there, I hear ivory—Oh, master! master! I am indeed down-hearted when you walk over me. But here I’ll stay, though this stern strikes rocks; and they bulge through; and oysters come to join me.”\n\n# CHAPTER 130. The Hat.\n\nAnd now that at the proper time and place, after so long and wide a preliminary cruise, Ahab,—all other whaling waters swept—seemed to have chased his foe into an ocean-fold, to slay him the more securely there; now, that he found himself hard by the very latitude and longitude where his tormenting wound had been inflicted; now that a vessel had been spoken which on the very day preceding had actually encountered Moby Dick;—and now that all his successive meetings with various ships contrastingly concurred to show the demoniac indifference with which the white whale tore his hunters, whether sinning or sinned against; now it was that there lurked a something in the old man’s eyes, which it was hardly sufferable for feeble souls to see. As the unsetting polar star, which through the livelong, arctic, six months’ night sustains its piercing, steady, central gaze; so Ahab’s purpose now fixedly gleamed down upon the constant midnight of the gloomy crew. It domineered above them so, that all their bodings, doubts, misgivings, fears, were fain to hide beneath their souls, and not sprout forth a single spear or leaf.\n\nIn this foreshadowing interval too, all humor, forced or natural, vanished. Stubb no more strove to raise a smile; Starbuck no more strove to check one. Alike, joy and sorrow, hope and fear, seemed ground to finest dust, and powdered, for the time, in the clamped mortar of Ahab’s iron soul. Like machines, they dumbly moved about the deck, ever conscious that the old man’s despot eye was on them.\n\nBut did you deeply scan him in his more secret confidential hours; when he thought no glance but one was on him; then you would have seen that even as Ahab’s eyes so awed the crew’s, the inscrutable Parsee’s glance awed his; or somehow, at least, in some wild way, at times affected it. Such an added, gliding strangeness began to invest the thin Fedallah now; such ceaseless shudderings shook him; that the men looked dubious at him; half uncertain, as it seemed, whether indeed he were a mortal substance, or else a tremulous shadow cast upon the deck by some unseen being’s body. And that shadow was always hovering there. For not by night, even, had Fedallah ever certainly been known to slumber, or go below. He would stand still for hours: but never sat or leaned; his wan but wondrous eyes did plainly say—We two watchmen never rest.\n\nNor, at any time, by night or day could the mariners now step upon the deck, unless Ahab was before them; either standing in his pivot-hole, or exactly pacing the planks between two undeviating limits,—the main-mast and the mizen; or else they saw him standing in the cabin-scuttle,—his living foot advanced upon the deck, as if to step; his hat slouched heavily over his eyes; so that however motionless he stood, however the days and nights were added on, that he had not swung in his hammock; yet hidden beneath that slouching hat, they could never tell unerringly whether, for all this, his eyes were really closed at times; or whether he was still intently scanning them; no matter, though he stood so in the scuttle for a whole hour on the stretch, and the unheeded night-damp gathered in beads of dew upon that stone-carved coat and hat. The clothes that the night had wet, the next day’s sunshine dried upon him; and so, day after day, and night after night; he went no more beneath the planks; whatever he wanted from the cabin that thing he sent for.\n\nHe ate in the same open air; that is, his two only meals,—breakfast and dinner: supper he never touched; nor reaped his beard; which darkly grew all gnarled, as unearthed roots of trees blown over, which still grow idly on at naked base, though perished in the upper verdure. But though his whole life was now become one watch on deck; and though the Parsee’s mystic watch was without intermission as his own; yet these two never seemed to speak—one man to the other—unless at long intervals some passing unmomentous matter made it necessary. Though such a potent spell seemed secretly to join the twain; openly, and to the awe-struck crew, they seemed pole-like asunder. If by day they chanced to speak one word; by night, dumb men were both, so far as concerned the slightest verbal interchange. At times, for longest hours, without a single hail, they stood far parted in the starlight; Ahab in his scuttle, the Parsee by the mainmast; but still fixedly gazing upon each other; as if in the Parsee Ahab saw his forethrown shadow, in Ahab the Parsee his abandoned substance.\n\nAnd yet, somehow, did Ahab—in his own proper self, as daily, hourly, and every instant, commandingly revealed to his subordinates,—Ahab seemed an independent lord; the Parsee but his slave. Still again both seemed yoked together, and an unseen tyrant driving them; the lean shade siding the solid rib. For be this Parsee what he may, all rib and keel was solid Ahab.\n\nAt the first faintest glimmering of the dawn, his iron voice was heard from aft,—“Man the mast-heads!”—and all through the day, till after sunset and after twilight, the same voice every hour, at the striking of the helmsman’s bell, was heard—“What d’ye see?—sharp! sharp!”\n\nBut when three or four days had slided by, after meeting the children-seeking Rachel; and no spout had yet been seen; the monomaniac old man seemed distrustful of his crew’s fidelity; at least, of nearly all except the Pagan harpooneers; he seemed to doubt, even, whether Stubb and Flask might not willingly overlook the sight he sought. But if these suspicions were really his, he sagaciously refrained from verbally expressing them, however his actions might seem to hint them.\n\n“I will have the first sight of the whale myself,”—he said. “Aye! Ahab must have the doubloon!” and with his own hands he rigged a nest of basketed bowlines; and sending a hand aloft, with a single sheaved block, to secure to the main-mast head, he received the two ends of the downward-reeved rope; and attaching one to his basket prepared a pin for the other end, in order to fasten it at the rail. This done, with that end yet in his hand and standing beside the pin, he looked round upon his crew, sweeping from one to the other; pausing his glance long upon Daggoo, Queequeg, Tashtego; but shunning Fedallah; and then settling his firm relying eye upon the chief mate, said,—“Take the rope, sir—I give it into thy hands, Starbuck.” Then arranging his person in the basket, he gave the word for them to hoist him to his perch, Starbuck being the one who secured the rope at last; and afterwards stood near it. And thus, with one hand clinging round the royal mast, Ahab gazed abroad upon the sea for miles and miles,—ahead, astern, this side, and that,—within the wide expanded circle commanded at so great a height.\n\nWhen in working with his hands at some lofty almost isolated place in the rigging, which chances to afford no foothold, the sailor at sea is hoisted up to that spot, and sustained there by the rope; under these circumstances, its fastened end on deck is always given in strict charge to some one man who has the special watch of it. Because in such a wilderness of running rigging, whose various different relations aloft cannot always be infallibly discerned by what is seen of them at the deck; and when the deck-ends of these ropes are being every few minutes cast down from the fastenings, it would be but a natural fatality, if, unprovided with a constant watchman, the hoisted sailor should by some carelessness of the crew be cast adrift and fall all swooping to the sea. So Ahab’s proceedings in this matter were not unusual; the only strange thing about them seemed to be, that Starbuck, almost the one only man who had ever ventured to oppose him with anything in the slightest degree approaching to decision—one of those too, whose faithfulness on the look-out he had seemed to doubt somewhat;—it was strange, that this was the very man he should select for his watchman; freely giving his whole life into such an otherwise distrusted person’s hands.\n\nNow, the first time Ahab was perched aloft; ere he had been there ten minutes; one of those red-billed savage sea-hawks which so often fly incommodiously close round the manned mast-heads of whalemen in these latitudes; one of these birds came wheeling and screaming round his head in a maze of untrackably swift circlings. Then it darted a thousand feet straight up into the air; then spiralized downwards, and went eddying again round his head.\n\nBut with his gaze fixed upon the dim and distant horizon, Ahab seemed not to mark this wild bird; nor, indeed, would any one else have marked it much, it being no uncommon circumstance; only now almost the least heedful eye seemed to see some sort of cunning meaning in almost every sight.\n\n“Your hat, your hat, sir!” suddenly cried the Sicilian seaman, who being posted at the mizen-mast-head, stood directly behind Ahab, though somewhat lower than his level, and with a deep gulf of air dividing them.\n\nBut already the sable wing was before the old man’s eyes; the long hooked bill at his head: with a scream, the black hawk darted away with his prize.\n\nAn eagle flew thrice round Tarquin’s head, removing his cap to replace it, and thereupon Tanaquil, his wife, declared that Tarquin would be king of Rome. But only by the replacing of the cap was that omen accounted good. Ahab’s hat was never restored; the wild hawk flew on and on with it; far in advance of the prow: and at last disappeared; while from the point of that disappearance, a minute black spot was dimly discerned, falling from that vast height into the sea.\n\n# CHAPTER 131. The Pequod Meets The Delight.\n\nThe intense Pequod sailed on; the rolling waves and days went by; the life-buoy-coffin still lightly swung; and another ship, most miserably misnamed the Delight, was descried. As she drew nigh, all eyes were fixed upon her broad beams, called shears, which, in some whaling-ships, cross the quarter-deck at the height of eight or nine feet; serving to carry the spare, unrigged, or disabled boats.\n\nUpon the stranger’s shears were beheld the shattered, white ribs, and some few splintered planks, of what had once been a whale-boat; but you now saw through this wreck, as plainly as you see through the peeled, half-unhinged, and bleaching skeleton of a horse.\n\n“Hast seen the White Whale?”\n\n“Look!” replied the hollow-cheeked captain from his taffrail; and with his trumpet he pointed to the wreck.\n\n“Hast killed him?”\n\n“The harpoon is not yet forged that ever will do that,” answered the other, sadly glancing upon a rounded hammock on the deck, whose gathered sides some noiseless sailors were busy in sewing together.\n\n“Not forged!” and snatching Perth’s levelled iron from the crotch, Ahab held it out, exclaiming—“Look ye, Nantucketer; here in this hand I hold his death! Tempered in blood, and tempered by lightning are these barbs; and I swear to temper them triply in that hot place behind the fin, where the White Whale most feels his accursed life!”\n\n“Then God keep thee, old man—see’st thou that”—pointing to the hammock—“I bury but one of five stout men, who were alive only yesterday; but were dead ere night. Only that one I bury; the rest were buried before they died; you sail upon their tomb.” Then turning to his crew—“Are ye ready there? place the plank then on the rail, and lift the body; so, then—Oh! God”—advancing towards the hammock with uplifted hands—“may the resurrection and the life——”\n\n“Brace forward! Up helm!” cried Ahab like lightning to his men.\n\nBut the suddenly started Pequod was not quick enough to escape the sound of the splash that the corpse soon made as it struck the sea; not so quick, indeed, but that some of the flying bubbles might have sprinkled her hull with their ghostly baptism.\n\nAs Ahab now glided from the dejected Delight, the strange life-buoy hanging at the Pequod’s stern came into conspicuous relief.\n\n“Ha! yonder! look yonder, men!” cried a foreboding voice in her wake. “In vain, oh, ye strangers, ye fly our sad burial; ye but turn us your taffrail to show us your coffin!”\n\n# CHAPTER 132. The Symphony.\n\nIt was a clear steel-blue day. The firmaments of air and sea were hardly separable in that all-pervading azure; only, the pensive air was transparently pure and soft, with a woman’s look, and the robust and man-like sea heaved with long, strong, lingering swells, as Samson’s chest in his sleep.\n\nHither, and thither, on high, glided the snow-white wings of small, unspeckled birds; these were the gentle thoughts of the feminine air; but to and fro in the deeps, far down in the bottomless blue, rushed mighty leviathans, sword-fish, and sharks; and these were the strong, troubled, murderous thinkings of the masculine sea.\n\nBut though thus contrasting within, the contrast was only in shades and shadows without; those two seemed one; it was only the sex, as it were, that distinguished them.\n\nAloft, like a royal czar and king, the sun seemed giving this gentle air to this bold and rolling sea; even as bride to groom. And at the girdling line of the horizon, a soft and tremulous motion—most seen here at the equator—denoted the fond, throbbing trust, the loving alarms, with which the poor bride gave her bosom away.\n\nTied up and twisted; gnarled and knotted with wrinkles; haggardly firm and unyielding; his eyes glowing like coals, that still glow in the ashes of ruin; untottering Ahab stood forth in the clearness of the morn; lifting his splintered helmet of a brow to the fair girl’s forehead of heaven.\n\nOh, immortal infancy, and innocency of the azure! Invisible winged creatures that frolic all round us! Sweet childhood of air and sky! how oblivious were ye of old Ahab’s close-coiled woe! But so have I seen little Miriam and Martha, laughing-eyed elves, heedlessly gambol around their old sire; sporting with the circle of singed locks which grew on the marge of that burnt-out crater of his brain.\n\nSlowly crossing the deck from the scuttle, Ahab leaned over the side and watched how his shadow in the water sank and sank to his gaze, the more and the more that he strove to pierce the profundity. But the lovely aromas in that enchanted air did at last seem to dispel, for a moment, the cankerous thing in his soul. That glad, happy air, that winsome sky, did at last stroke and caress him; the step-mother world, so long cruel—forbidding—now threw affectionate arms round his stubborn neck, and did seem to joyously sob over him, as if over one, that however wilful and erring, she could yet find it in her heart to save and to bless. From beneath his slouched hat Ahab dropped a tear into the sea; nor did all the Pacific contain such wealth as that one wee drop.\n\nStarbuck saw the old man; saw him, how he heavily leaned over the side; and he seemed to hear in his own true heart the measureless sobbing that stole out of the centre of the serenity around. Careful not to touch him, or be noticed by him, he yet drew near to him, and stood there.\n\nAhab turned.\n\n“Starbuck!”\n“Sir.”\n\n“Oh, Starbuck! it is a mild, mild wind, and a mild looking sky. On such a day—very much such a sweetness as this—I struck my first whale—a boy-harpooneer of eighteen! Forty—forty—forty years ago!—ago! Forty years of continual whaling! forty years of privation, and peril, and storm-time! forty years on the pitiless sea! for forty years has Ahab forsaken the peaceful land, for forty years to make war on the horrors of the deep! Aye and yes, Starbuck, out of those forty years I have not spent three ashore. When I think of this life I have led; the desolation of solitude it has been; the masoned, walled-town of a Captain’s exclusiveness, which admits but small entrance to any sympathy from the green country without—oh, weariness! heaviness! Guinea-coast slavery of solitary command!—when I think of all this; only half-suspected, not so keenly known to me before—and how for forty years I have fed upon dry salted fare—fit emblem of the dry nourishment of my soil!—when the poorest landsman has had fresh fruit to his daily hand, and broken the world’s fresh bread to my mouldy crusts—away, whole oceans away, from that young girl-wife I wedded past fifty, and sailed for Cape Horn the next day, leaving but one dent in my marriage pillow—wife? wife?—rather a widow with her husband alive! Aye, I widowed that poor girl when I married her, Starbuck; and then, the madness, the frenzy, the boiling blood and the smoking brow, with which, for a thousand lowerings old Ahab has furiously, foamingly chased his prey—more a demon than a man!—aye, aye! what a forty years’ fool—fool—old fool, has old Ahab been! Why this strife of the chase? why weary, and palsy the arm at the oar, and the iron, and the lance? how the richer or better is Ahab now? Behold. Oh, Starbuck! is it not hard, that with this weary load I bear, one poor leg should have been snatched from under me? Here, brush this old hair aside; it blinds me, that I seem to weep. Locks so grey did never grow but from out some ashes! But do I look very old, so very, very old, Starbuck? I feel deadly faint, bowed, and humped, as though I were Adam, staggering beneath the piled centuries since Paradise. God! God! God!—crack my heart!—stave my brain!—mockery! mockery! bitter, biting mockery of grey hairs, have I lived enough joy to wear ye; and seem and feel thus intolerably old? Close! stand close to me, Starbuck; let me look into a human eye; it is better than to gaze into sea or sky; better than to gaze upon God. By the green land; by the bright hearth-stone! this is the magic glass, man; I see my wife and my child in thine eye. No, no; stay on board, on board!—lower not when I do; when branded Ahab gives chase to Moby Dick. That hazard shall not be thine. No, no! not with the far away home I see in that eye!”\n\n“Oh, my Captain! my Captain! noble soul! grand old heart, after all! why should any one give chase to that hated fish! Away with me! let us fly these deadly waters! let us home! Wife and child, too, are Starbuck’s—wife and child of his brotherly, sisterly, play-fellow youth; even as thine, sir, are the wife and child of thy loving, longing, paternal old age! Away! let us away!—this instant let me alter the course! How cheerily, how hilariously, O my Captain, would we bowl on our way to see old Nantucket again! I think, sir, they have some such mild blue days, even as this, in Nantucket.”\n\n“They have, they have. I have seen them—some summer days in the morning. About this time—yes, it is his noon nap now—the boy vivaciously wakes; sits up in bed; and his mother tells him of me, of cannibal old me; how I am abroad upon the deep, but will yet come back to dance him again.”\n\n“’Tis my Mary, my Mary herself! She promised that my boy, every morning, should be carried to the hill to catch the first glimpse of his father’s sail! Yes, yes! no more! it is done! we head for Nantucket! Come, my Captain, study out the course, and let us away! See, see! the boy’s face from the window! the boy’s hand on the hill!”\n\nBut Ahab’s glance was averted; like a blighted fruit tree he shook, and cast his last, cindered apple to the soil.\n\n“What is it, what nameless, inscrutable, unearthly thing is it; what cozening, hidden lord and master, and cruel, remorseless emperor commands me; that against all natural lovings and longings, I so keep pushing, and crowding, and jamming myself on all the time; recklessly making me ready to do what in my own proper, natural heart, I durst not so much as dare? Is Ahab, Ahab? Is it I, God, or who, that lifts this arm? But if the great sun move not of himself; but is as an errand-boy in heaven; nor one single star can revolve, but by some invisible power; how then can this one small heart beat; this one small brain think thoughts; unless God does that beating, does that thinking, does that living, and not I. By heaven, man, we are turned round and round in this world, like yonder windlass, and Fate is the handspike. And all the time, lo! that smiling sky, and this unsounded sea! Look! see yon Albicore! who put it into him to chase and fang that flying-fish? Where do murderers go, man! Who’s to doom, when the judge himself is dragged to the bar? But it is a mild, mild wind, and a mild looking sky; and the air smells now, as if it blew from a far-away meadow; they have been making hay somewhere under the slopes of the Andes, Starbuck, and the mowers are sleeping among the new-mown hay. Sleeping? Aye, toil we how we may, we all sleep at last on the field. Sleep? Aye, and rust amid greenness; as last year’s scythes flung down, and left in the half-cut swaths—Starbuck!”\n\nBut blanched to a corpse’s hue with despair, the Mate had stolen away.\n\nAhab crossed the deck to gaze over on the other side; but started at two reflected, fixed eyes in the water there. Fedallah was motionlessly leaning over the same rail.\n\n# CHAPTER 133. The Chase—First Day.\n\nThat night, in the mid-watch, when the old man—as his wont at intervals—stepped forth from the scuttle in which he leaned, and went to his pivot-hole, he suddenly thrust out his face fiercely, snuffing up the sea air as a sagacious ship’s dog will, in drawing nigh to some barbarous isle. He declared that a whale must be near. Soon that peculiar odor, sometimes to a great distance given forth by the living sperm whale, was palpable to all the watch; nor was any mariner surprised when, after inspecting the compass, and then the dog-vane, and then ascertaining the precise bearing of the odor as nearly as possible, Ahab rapidly ordered the ship’s course to be slightly altered, and the sail to be shortened.\n\nThe acute policy dictating these movements was sufficiently vindicated at daybreak, by the sight of a long sleek on the sea directly and lengthwise ahead, smooth as oil, and resembling in the pleated watery wrinkles bordering it, the polished metallic-like marks of some swift tide-rip, at the mouth of a deep, rapid stream.\n\n“Man the mast-heads! Call all hands!”\n\nThundering with the butts of three clubbed handspikes on the forecastle deck, Daggoo roused the sleepers with such judgment claps that they seemed to exhale from the scuttle, so instantaneously did they appear with their clothes in their hands.\n\n“What d’ye see?” cried Ahab, flattening his face to the sky.\n\n“Nothing, nothing sir!” was the sound hailing down in reply.\n\n“T’gallant sails!—stunsails! alow and aloft, and on both sides!”\n\nAll sail being set, he now cast loose the life-line, reserved for swaying him to the main royal-mast head; and in a few moments they were hoisting him thither, when, while but two thirds of the way aloft, and while peering ahead through the horizontal vacancy between the main-top-sail and top-gallant-sail, he raised a gull-like cry in the air. “There she blows!—there she blows! A hump like a snow-hill! It is Moby Dick!”\n\nFired by the cry which seemed simultaneously taken up by the three look-outs, the men on deck rushed to the rigging to behold the famous whale they had so long been pursuing. Ahab had now gained his final perch, some feet above the other look-outs, Tashtego standing just beneath him on the cap of the top-gallant-mast, so that the Indian’s head was almost on a level with Ahab’s heel. From this height the whale was now seen some mile or so ahead, at every roll of the sea revealing his high sparkling hump, and regularly jetting his silent spout into the air. To the credulous mariners it seemed the same silent spout they had so long ago beheld in the moonlit Atlantic and Indian Oceans.\n\n“And did none of ye see it before?” cried Ahab, hailing the perched men all around him.\n\n“I saw him almost that same instant, sir, that Captain Ahab did, and I cried out,” said Tashtego.\n\n“Not the same instant; not the same—no, the doubloon is mine, Fate reserved the doubloon for me. I only; none of ye could have raised the White Whale first. There she blows!—there she blows!—there she blows! There again!—there again!” he cried, in long-drawn, lingering, methodic tones, attuned to the gradual prolongings of the whale’s visible jets. “He’s going to sound! In stunsails! Down top-gallant-sails! Stand by three boats. Mr. Starbuck, remember, stay on board, and keep the ship. Helm there! Luff, luff a point! So; steady, man, steady! There go flukes! No, no; only black water! All ready the boats there? Stand by, stand by! Lower me, Mr. Starbuck; lower, lower,—quick, quicker!” and he slid through the air to the deck.\n\n“He is heading straight to leeward, sir,” cried Stubb, “right away from us; cannot have seen the ship yet.”\n\n“Be dumb, man! Stand by the braces! Hard down the helm!—brace up! Shiver her!—shiver her!—So; well that! Boats, boats!”\n\nSoon all the boats but Starbuck’s were dropped; all the boat-sails set—all the paddles plying; with rippling swiftness, shooting to leeward; and Ahab heading the onset. A pale, death-glimmer lit up Fedallah’s sunken eyes; a hideous motion gnawed his mouth.\n\nLike noiseless nautilus shells, their light prows sped through the sea; but only slowly they neared the foe. As they neared him, the ocean grew still more smooth; seemed drawing a carpet over its waves; seemed a noon-meadow, so serenely it spread. At length the breathless hunter came so nigh his seemingly unsuspecting prey, that his entire dazzling hump was distinctly visible, sliding along the sea as if an isolated thing, and continually set in a revolving ring of finest, fleecy, greenish foam. He saw the vast, involved wrinkles of the slightly projecting head beyond. Before it, far out on the soft Turkish-rugged waters, went the glistening white shadow from his broad, milky forehead, a musical rippling playfully accompanying the shade; and behind, the blue waters interchangeably flowed over into the moving valley of his steady wake; and on either hand bright bubbles arose and danced by his side. But these were broken again by the light toes of hundreds of gay fowl softly feathering the sea, alternate with their fitful flight; and like to some flag-staff rising from the painted hull of an argosy, the tall but shattered pole of a recent lance projected from the white whale’s back; and at intervals one of the cloud of soft-toed fowls hovering, and to and fro skimming like a canopy over the fish, silently perched and rocked on this pole, the long tail feathers streaming like pennons.\n\nA gentle joyousness—a mighty mildness of repose in swiftness, invested the gliding whale. Not the white bull Jupiter swimming away with ravished Europa clinging to his graceful horns; his lovely, leering eyes sideways intent upon the maid; with smooth bewitching fleetness, rippling straight for the nuptial bower in Crete; not Jove, not that great majesty Supreme! did surpass the glorified White Whale as he so divinely swam.\n\nOn each soft side—coincident with the parted swell, that but once leaving him, then flowed so wide away—on each bright side, the whale shed off enticings. No wonder there had been some among the hunters who namelessly transported and allured by all this serenity, had ventured to assail it; but had fatally found that quietude but the vesture of tornadoes. Yet calm, enticing calm, oh, whale! thou glidest on, to all who for the first time eye thee, no matter how many in that same way thou may’st have bejuggled and destroyed before.\n\nAnd thus, through the serene tranquillities of the tropical sea, among waves whose hand-clappings were suspended by exceeding rapture, Moby Dick moved on, still withholding from sight the full terrors of his submerged trunk, entirely hiding the wrenched hideousness of his jaw. But soon the fore part of him slowly rose from the water; for an instant his whole marbleized body formed a high arch, like Virginia’s Natural Bridge, and warningly waving his bannered flukes in the air, the grand god revealed himself, sounded, and went out of sight. Hoveringly halting, and dipping on the wing, the white sea-fowls longingly lingered over the agitated pool that he left.\n\nWith oars apeak, and paddles down, the sheets of their sails adrift, the three boats now stilly floated, awaiting Moby Dick’s reappearance.\n\n“An hour,” said Ahab, standing rooted in his boat’s stern; and he gazed beyond the whale’s place, towards the dim blue spaces and wide wooing vacancies to leeward. It was only an instant; for again his eyes seemed whirling round in his head as he swept the watery circle. The breeze now freshened; the sea began to swell.\n\n“The birds!—the birds!” cried Tashtego.\n\nIn long Indian file, as when herons take wing, the white birds were now all flying towards Ahab’s boat; and when within a few yards began fluttering over the water there, wheeling round and round, with joyous, expectant cries. Their vision was keener than man’s; Ahab could discover no sign in the sea. But suddenly as he peered down and down into its depths, he profoundly saw a white living spot no bigger than a white weasel, with wonderful celerity uprising, and magnifying as it rose, till it turned, and then there were plainly revealed two long crooked rows of white, glistening teeth, floating up from the undiscoverable bottom. It was Moby Dick’s open mouth and scrolled jaw; his vast, shadowed bulk still half blending with the blue of the sea. The glittering mouth yawned beneath the boat like an open-doored marble tomb; and giving one sidelong sweep with his steering oar, Ahab whirled the craft aside from this tremendous apparition. Then, calling upon Fedallah to change places with him, went forward to the bows, and seizing Perth’s harpoon, commanded his crew to grasp their oars and stand by to stern.\n\nNow, by reason of this timely spinning round the boat upon its axis, its bow, by anticipation, was made to face the whale’s head while yet under water. But as if perceiving this stratagem, Moby Dick, with that malicious intelligence ascribed to him, sidelingly transplanted himself, as it were, in an instant, shooting his pleated head lengthwise beneath the boat.\n\nThrough and through; through every plank and each rib, it thrilled for an instant, the whale obliquely lying on his back, in the manner of a biting shark, slowly and feelingly taking its bows full within his mouth, so that the long, narrow, scrolled lower jaw curled high up into the open air, and one of the teeth caught in a row-lock. The bluish pearl-white of the inside of the jaw was within six inches of Ahab’s head, and reached higher than that. In this attitude the White Whale now shook the slight cedar as a mildly cruel cat her mouse. With unastonished eyes Fedallah gazed, and crossed his arms; but the tiger-yellow crew were tumbling over each other’s heads to gain the uttermost stern.\n\nAnd now, while both elastic gunwales were springing in and out, as the whale dallied with the doomed craft in this devilish way; and from his body being submerged beneath the boat, he could not be darted at from the bows, for the bows were almost inside of him, as it were; and while the other boats involuntarily paused, as before a quick crisis impossible to withstand, then it was that monomaniac Ahab, furious with this tantalizing vicinity of his foe, which placed him all alive and helpless in the very jaws he hated; frenzied with all this, he seized the long bone with his naked hands, and wildly strove to wrench it from its gripe. As now he thus vainly strove, the jaw slipped from him; the frail gunwales bent in, collapsed, and snapped, as both jaws, like an enormous shears, sliding further aft, bit the craft completely in twain, and locked themselves fast again in the sea, midway between the two floating wrecks. These floated aside, the broken ends drooping, the crew at the stern-wreck clinging to the gunwales, and striving to hold fast to the oars to lash them across.\n\nAt that preluding moment, ere the boat was yet snapped, Ahab, the first to perceive the whale’s intent, by the crafty upraising of his head, a movement that loosed his hold for the time; at that moment his hand had made one final effort to push the boat out of the bite. But only slipping further into the whale’s mouth, and tilting over sideways as it slipped, the boat had shaken off his hold on the jaw; spilled him out of it, as he leaned to the push; and so he fell flat-faced upon the sea.\n\nRipplingly withdrawing from his prey, Moby Dick now lay at a little distance, vertically thrusting his oblong white head up and down in the billows; and at the same time slowly revolving his whole spindled body; so that when his vast wrinkled forehead rose—some twenty or more feet out of the water—the now rising swells, with all their confluent waves, dazzlingly broke against it; vindictively tossing their shivered spray still higher into the air.* So, in a gale, the but half baffled Channel billows only recoil from the base of the Eddystone, triumphantly to overleap its summit with their scud.\n\n*This motion is peculiar to the sperm whale. It receives its designation (pitchpoling) from its being likened to that preliminary up-and-down poise of the whale-lance, in the exercise called pitchpoling, previously described. By this motion the whale must best and most comprehensively view whatever objects may be encircling him.\n\nBut soon resuming his horizontal attitude, Moby Dick swam swiftly round and round the wrecked crew; sideways churning the water in his vengeful wake, as if lashing himself up to still another and more deadly assault. The sight of the splintered boat seemed to madden him, as the blood of grapes and mulberries cast before Antiochus’s elephants in the book of Maccabees. Meanwhile Ahab half smothered in the foam of the whale’s insolent tail, and too much of a cripple to swim,—though he could still keep afloat, even in the heart of such a whirlpool as that; helpless Ahab’s head was seen, like a tossed bubble which the least chance shock might burst. From the boat’s fragmentary stern, Fedallah incuriously and mildly eyed him; the clinging crew, at the other drifting end, could not succor him; more than enough was it for them to look to themselves. For so revolvingly appalling was the White Whale’s aspect, and so planetarily swift the ever-contracting circles he made, that he seemed horizontally swooping upon them. And though the other boats, unharmed, still hovered hard by; still they dared not pull into the eddy to strike, lest that should be the signal for the instant destruction of the jeopardized castaways, Ahab and all; nor in that case could they themselves hope to escape. With straining eyes, then, they remained on the outer edge of the direful zone, whose centre had now become the old man’s head.\n\nMeantime, from the beginning all this had been descried from the ship’s mast heads; and squaring her yards, she had borne down upon the scene; and was now so nigh, that Ahab in the water hailed her!—“Sail on the”—but that moment a breaking sea dashed on him from Moby Dick, and whelmed him for the time. But struggling out of it again, and chancing to rise on a towering crest, he shouted,—“Sail on the whale!—Drive him off!”\n\nThe Pequod’s prows were pointed; and breaking up the charmed circle, she effectually parted the white whale from his victim. As he sullenly swam off, the boats flew to the rescue.\n\nDragged into Stubb’s boat with blood-shot, blinded eyes, the white brine caking in his wrinkles; the long tension of Ahab’s bodily strength did crack, and helplessly he yielded to his body’s doom: for a time, lying all crushed in the bottom of Stubb’s boat, like one trodden under foot of herds of elephants. Far inland, nameless wails came from him, as desolate sounds from out ravines.\n\nBut this intensity of his physical prostration did but so much the more abbreviate it. In an instant’s compass, great hearts sometimes condense to one deep pang, the sum total of those shallow pains kindly diffused through feebler men’s whole lives. And so, such hearts, though summary in each one suffering; still, if the gods decree it, in their life-time aggregate a whole age of woe, wholly made up of instantaneous intensities; for even in their pointless centres, those noble natures contain the entire circumferences of inferior souls.\n\n“The harpoon,” said Ahab, half way rising, and draggingly leaning on one bended arm—“is it safe?”\n\n“Aye, sir, for it was not darted; this is it,” said Stubb, showing it.\n\n“Lay it before me;—any missing men?”\n\n“One, two, three, four, five;—there were five oars, sir, and here are five men.”\n\n“That’s good.—Help me, man; I wish to stand. So, so, I see him! there! there! going to leeward still; what a leaping spout!—Hands off from me! The eternal sap runs up in Ahab’s bones again! Set the sail; out oars; the helm!”\n\nIt is often the case that when a boat is stove, its crew, being picked up by another boat, help to work that second boat; and the chase is thus continued with what is called double-banked oars. It was thus now. But the added power of the boat did not equal the added power of the whale, for he seemed to have treble-banked his every fin; swimming with a velocity which plainly showed, that if now, under these circumstances, pushed on, the chase would prove an indefinitely prolonged, if not a hopeless one; nor could any crew endure for so long a period, such an unintermitted, intense straining at the oar; a thing barely tolerable only in some one brief vicissitude. The ship itself, then, as it sometimes happens, offered the most promising intermediate means of overtaking the chase. Accordingly, the boats now made for her, and were soon swayed up to their cranes—the two parts of the wrecked boat having been previously secured by her—and then hoisting everything to her side, and stacking her canvas high up, and sideways outstretching it with stun-sails, like the double-jointed wings of an albatross; the Pequod bore down in the leeward wake of Moby-Dick. At the well known, methodic intervals, the whale’s glittering spout was regularly announced from the manned mast-heads; and when he would be reported as just gone down, Ahab would take the time, and then pacing the deck, binnacle-watch in hand, so soon as the last second of the allotted hour expired, his voice was heard.—“Whose is the doubloon now? D’ye see him?” and if the reply was, No, sir! straightway he commanded them to lift him to his perch. In this way the day wore on; Ahab, now aloft and motionless; anon, unrestingly pacing the planks.\n\nAs he was thus walking, uttering no sound, except to hail the men aloft, or to bid them hoist a sail still higher, or to spread one to a still greater breadth—thus to and fro pacing, beneath his slouched hat, at every turn he passed his own wrecked boat, which had been dropped upon the quarter-deck, and lay there reversed; broken bow to shattered stern. At last he paused before it; and as in an already over-clouded sky fresh troops of clouds will sometimes sail across, so over the old man’s face there now stole some such added gloom as this.\n\nStubb saw him pause; and perhaps intending, not vainly, though, to evince his own unabated fortitude, and thus keep up a valiant place in his Captain’s mind, he advanced, and eyeing the wreck exclaimed—“The thistle the ass refused; it pricked his mouth too keenly, sir; ha! ha!”\n\n“What soulless thing is this that laughs before a wreck? Man, man! did I not know thee brave as fearless fire (and as mechanical) I could swear thou wert a poltroon. Groan nor laugh should be heard before a wreck.”\n\n“Aye, sir,” said Starbuck drawing near, “’tis a solemn sight; an omen, and an ill one.”\n\n“Omen? omen?—the dictionary! If the gods think to speak outright to man, they will honorably speak outright; not shake their heads, and give an old wives’ darkling hint.—Begone! Ye two are the opposite poles of one thing; Starbuck is Stubb reversed, and Stubb is Starbuck; and ye two are all mankind; and Ahab stands alone among the millions of the peopled earth, nor gods nor men his neighbors! Cold, cold—I shiver!—How now? Aloft there! D’ye see him? Sing out for every spout, though he spout ten times a second!”\n\nThe day was nearly done; only the hem of his golden robe was rustling. Soon, it was almost dark, but the look-out men still remained unset.\n\n“Can’t see the spout now, sir;—too dark”—cried a voice from the air.\n\n“How heading when last seen?”\n\n“As before, sir,—straight to leeward.”\n\n“Good! he will travel slower now ’tis night. Down royals and top-gallant stun-sails, Mr. Starbuck. We must not run over him before morning; he’s making a passage now, and may heave-to a while. Helm there! keep her full before the wind!—Aloft! come down!—Mr. Stubb, send a fresh hand to the fore-mast head, and see it manned till morning.”—Then advancing towards the doubloon in the main-mast—“Men, this gold is mine, for I earned it; but I shall let it abide here till the White Whale is dead; and then, whosoever of ye first raises him, upon the day he shall be killed, this gold is that man’s; and if on that day I shall again raise him, then, ten times its sum shall be divided among all of ye! Away now!—the deck is thine, sir!”\n\nAnd so saying, he placed himself half way within the scuttle, and slouching his hat, stood there till dawn, except when at intervals rousing himself to see how the night wore on.\n\n# CHAPTER 134. The Chase—Second Day.\n\nAt day-break, the three mast-heads were punctually manned afresh.\n\n“D’ye see him?” cried Ahab after allowing a little space for the light to spread.\n\n“See nothing, sir.”\n\n“Turn up all hands and make sail! he travels faster than I thought for;—the top-gallant sails!—aye, they should have been kept on her all night. But no matter—’tis but resting for the rush.”\n\nHere be it said, that this pertinacious pursuit of one particular whale, continued through day into night, and through night into day, is a thing by no means unprecedented in the South sea fishery. For such is the wonderful skill, prescience of experience, and invincible confidence acquired by some great natural geniuses among the Nantucket commanders; that from the simple observation of a whale when last descried, they will, under certain given circumstances, pretty accurately foretell both the direction in which he will continue to swim for a time, while out of sight, as well as his probable rate of progression during that period. And, in these cases, somewhat as a pilot, when about losing sight of a coast, whose general trending he well knows, and which he desires shortly to return to again, but at some further point; like as this pilot stands by his compass, and takes the precise bearing of the cape at present visible, in order the more certainly to hit aright the remote, unseen headland, eventually to be visited: so does the fisherman, at his compass, with the whale; for after being chased, and diligently marked, through several hours of daylight, then, when night obscures the fish, the creature’s future wake through the darkness is almost as established to the sagacious mind of the hunter, as the pilot’s coast is to him. So that to this hunter’s wondrous skill, the proverbial evanescence of a thing writ in water, a wake, is to all desired purposes well nigh as reliable as the steadfast land. And as the mighty iron Leviathan of the modern railway is so familiarly known in its every pace, that, with watches in their hands, men time his rate as doctors that of a baby’s pulse; and lightly say of it, the up train or the down train will reach such or such a spot, at such or such an hour; even so, almost, there are occasions when these Nantucketers time that other Leviathan of the deep, according to the observed humor of his speed; and say to themselves, so many hours hence this whale will have gone two hundred miles, will have about reached this or that degree of latitude or longitude. But to render this acuteness at all successful in the end, the wind and the sea must be the whaleman’s allies; for of what present avail to the becalmed or windbound mariner is the skill that assures him he is exactly ninety-three leagues and a quarter from his port? Inferable from these statements, are many collateral subtile matters touching the chase of whales.\n\nThe ship tore on; leaving such a furrow in the sea as when a cannon-ball, missent, becomes a plough-share and turns up the level field.\n\n“By salt and hemp!” cried Stubb, “but this swift motion of the deck creeps up one’s legs and tingles at the heart. This ship and I are two brave fellows!—Ha, ha! Some one take me up, and launch me, spine-wise, on the sea,—for by live-oaks! my spine’s a keel. Ha, ha! we go the gait that leaves no dust behind!”\n\n“There she blows—she blows!—she blows!—right ahead!” was now the mast-head cry.\n\n“Aye, aye!” cried Stubb, “I knew it—ye can’t escape—blow on and split your spout, O whale! the mad fiend himself is after ye! blow your trump—blister your lungs!—Ahab will dam off your blood, as a miller shuts his watergate upon the stream!”\n\nAnd Stubb did but speak out for well nigh all that crew. The frenzies of the chase had by this time worked them bubblingly up, like old wine worked anew. Whatever pale fears and forebodings some of them might have felt before; these were not only now kept out of sight through the growing awe of Ahab, but they were broken up, and on all sides routed, as timid prairie hares that scatter before the bounding bison. The hand of Fate had snatched all their souls; and by the stirring perils of the previous day; the rack of the past night’s suspense; the fixed, unfearing, blind, reckless way in which their wild craft went plunging towards its flying mark; by all these things, their hearts were bowled along. The wind that made great bellies of their sails, and rushed the vessel on by arms invisible as irresistible; this seemed the symbol of that unseen agency which so enslaved them to the race.\n\nThey were one man, not thirty. For as the one ship that held them all; though it was put together of all contrasting things—oak, and maple, and pine wood; iron, and pitch, and hemp—yet all these ran into each other in the one concrete hull, which shot on its way, both balanced and directed by the long central keel; even so, all the individualities of the crew, this man’s valor, that man’s fear; guilt and guiltiness, all varieties were welded into oneness, and were all directed to that fatal goal which Ahab their one lord and keel did point to.\n\nThe rigging lived. The mast-heads, like the tops of tall palms, were outspreadingly tufted with arms and legs. Clinging to a spar with one hand, some reached forth the other with impatient wavings; others, shading their eyes from the vivid sunlight, sat far out on the rocking yards; all the spars in full bearing of mortals, ready and ripe for their fate. Ah! how they still strove through that infinite blueness to seek out the thing that might destroy them!\n\n“Why sing ye not out for him, if ye see him?” cried Ahab, when, after the lapse of some minutes since the first cry, no more had been heard. “Sway me up, men; ye have been deceived; not Moby Dick casts one odd jet that way, and then disappears.”\n\nIt was even so; in their headlong eagerness, the men had mistaken some other thing for the whale-spout, as the event itself soon proved; for hardly had Ahab reached his perch; hardly was the rope belayed to its pin on deck, when he struck the key-note to an orchestra, that made the air vibrate as with the combined discharges of rifles. The triumphant halloo of thirty buckskin lungs was heard, as—much nearer to the ship than the place of the imaginary jet, less than a mile ahead—Moby Dick bodily burst into view! For not by any calm and indolent spoutings; not by the peaceable gush of that mystic fountain in his head, did the White Whale now reveal his vicinity; but by the far more wondrous phenomenon of breaching. Rising with his utmost velocity from the furthest depths, the Sperm Whale thus booms his entire bulk into the pure element of air, and piling up a mountain of dazzling foam, shows his place to the distance of seven miles and more. In those moments, the torn, enraged waves he shakes off, seem his mane; in some cases, this breaching is his act of defiance.\n\n“There she breaches! there she breaches!” was the cry, as in his immeasurable bravadoes the White Whale tossed himself salmon-like to Heaven. So suddenly seen in the blue plain of the sea, and relieved against the still bluer margin of the sky, the spray that he raised, for the moment, intolerably glittered and glared like a glacier; and stood there gradually fading and fading away from its first sparkling intensity, to the dim mistiness of an advancing shower in a vale.\n\n“Aye, breach your last to the sun, Moby Dick!” cried Ahab, “thy hour and thy harpoon are at hand!—Down! down all of ye, but one man at the fore. The boats!—stand by!”\n\nUnmindful of the tedious rope-ladders of the shrouds, the men, like shooting stars, slid to the deck, by the isolated backstays and halyards; while Ahab, less dartingly, but still rapidly was dropped from his perch.\n\n“Lower away,” he cried, so soon as he had reached his boat—a spare one, rigged the afternoon previous. “Mr. Starbuck, the ship is thine—keep away from the boats, but keep near them. Lower, all!”\n\nAs if to strike a quick terror into them, by this time being the first assailant himself, Moby Dick had turned, and was now coming for the three crews. Ahab’s boat was central; and cheering his men, he told them he would take the whale head-and-head,—that is, pull straight up to his forehead,—a not uncommon thing; for when within a certain limit, such a course excludes the coming onset from the whale’s sidelong vision. But ere that close limit was gained, and while yet all three boats were plain as the ship’s three masts to his eye; the White Whale churning himself into furious speed, almost in an instant as it were, rushing among the boats with open jaws, and a lashing tail, offered appalling battle on every side; and heedless of the irons darted at him from every boat, seemed only intent on annihilating each separate plank of which those boats were made. But skilfully manœuvred, incessantly wheeling like trained chargers in the field; the boats for a while eluded him; though, at times, but by a plank’s breadth; while all the time, Ahab’s unearthly slogan tore every other cry but his to shreds.\n\nBut at last in his untraceable evolutions, the White Whale so crossed and recrossed, and in a thousand ways entangled the slack of the three lines now fast to him, that they foreshortened, and, of themselves, warped the devoted boats towards the planted irons in him; though now for a moment the whale drew aside a little, as if to rally for a more tremendous charge. Seizing that opportunity, Ahab first paid out more line: and then was rapidly hauling and jerking in upon it again—hoping that way to disencumber it of some snarls—when lo!—a sight more savage than the embattled teeth of sharks!\n\nCaught and twisted—corkscrewed in the mazes of the line, loose harpoons and lances, with all their bristling barbs and points, came flashing and dripping up to the chocks in the bows of Ahab’s boat. Only one thing could be done. Seizing the boat-knife, he critically reached within—through—and then, without—the rays of steel; dragged in the line beyond, passed it, inboard, to the bowsman, and then, twice sundering the rope near the chocks—dropped the intercepted fagot of steel into the sea; and was all fast again. That instant, the White Whale made a sudden rush among the remaining tangles of the other lines; by so doing, irresistibly dragged the more involved boats of Stubb and Flask towards his flukes; dashed them together like two rolling husks on a surf-beaten beach, and then, diving down into the sea, disappeared in a boiling maelstrom, in which, for a space, the odorous cedar chips of the wrecks danced round and round, like the grated nutmeg in a swiftly stirred bowl of punch.\n\nWhile the two crews were yet circling in the waters, reaching out after the revolving line-tubs, oars, and other floating furniture, while aslope little Flask bobbed up and down like an empty vial, twitching his legs upwards to escape the dreaded jaws of sharks; and Stubb was lustily singing out for some one to ladle him up; and while the old man’s line—now parting—admitted of his pulling into the creamy pool to rescue whom he could;—in that wild simultaneousness of a thousand concreted perils,—Ahab’s yet unstricken boat seemed drawn up towards Heaven by invisible wires,—as, arrow-like, shooting perpendicularly from the sea, the White Whale dashed his broad forehead against its bottom, and sent it, turning over and over, into the air; till it fell again—gunwale downwards—and Ahab and his men struggled out from under it, like seals from a sea-side cave.\n\nThe first uprising momentum of the whale—modifying its direction as he struck the surface—involuntarily launched him along it, to a little distance from the centre of the destruction he had made; and with his back to it, he now lay for a moment slowly feeling with his flukes from side to side; and whenever a stray oar, bit of plank, the least chip or crumb of the boats touched his skin, his tail swiftly drew back, and came sideways smiting the sea. But soon, as if satisfied that his work for that time was done, he pushed his pleated forehead through the ocean, and trailing after him the intertangled lines, continued his leeward way at a traveller’s methodic pace.\n\nAs before, the attentive ship having descried the whole fight, again came bearing down to the rescue, and dropping a boat, picked up the floating mariners, tubs, oars, and whatever else could be caught at, and safely landed them on her decks. Some sprained shoulders, wrists, and ankles; livid contusions; wrenched harpoons and lances; inextricable intricacies of rope; shattered oars and planks; all these were there; but no fatal or even serious ill seemed to have befallen any one. As with Fedallah the day before, so Ahab was now found grimly clinging to his boat’s broken half, which afforded a comparatively easy float; nor did it so exhaust him as the previous day’s mishap.\n\nBut when he was helped to the deck, all eyes were fastened upon him; as instead of standing by himself he still half-hung upon the shoulder of Starbuck, who had thus far been the foremost to assist him. His ivory leg had been snapped off, leaving but one short sharp splinter.\n\n“Aye, aye, Starbuck, ’tis sweet to lean sometimes, be the leaner who he will; and would old Ahab had leaned oftener than he has.”\n\n“The ferrule has not stood, sir,” said the carpenter, now coming up; “I put good work into that leg.”\n\n“But no bones broken, sir, I hope,” said Stubb with true concern.\n\n“Aye! and all splintered to pieces, Stubb!—d’ye see it.—But even with a broken bone, old Ahab is untouched; and I account no living bone of mine one jot more me, than this dead one that’s lost. Nor white whale, nor man, nor fiend, can so much as graze old Ahab in his own proper and inaccessible being. Can any lead touch yonder floor, any mast scrape yonder roof?—Aloft there! which way?”\n\n“Dead to leeward, sir.”\n\n“Up helm, then; pile on the sail again, ship keepers! down the rest of the spare boats and rig them—Mr. Starbuck away, and muster the boat’s crews.”\n\n“Let me first help thee towards the bulwarks, sir.”\n\n“Oh, oh, oh! how this splinter gores me now! Accursed fate! that the unconquerable captain in the soul should have such a craven mate!”\n\n“Sir?”\n\n“My body, man, not thee. Give me something for a cane—there, that shivered lance will do. Muster the men. Surely I have not seen him yet. By heaven it cannot be!—missing?—quick! call them all.”\n\nThe old man’s hinted thought was true. Upon mustering the company, the Parsee was not there.\n\n“The Parsee!” cried Stubb—“he must have been caught in——”\n\n“The black vomit wrench thee!—run all of ye above, alow, cabin, forecastle—find him—not gone—not gone!”\n\nBut quickly they returned to him with the tidings that the Parsee was nowhere to be found.\n\n“Aye, sir,” said Stubb—“caught among the tangles of your line—I thought I saw him dragging under.”\n\n“My line! my line? Gone?—gone? What means that little word?—What death-knell rings in it, that old Ahab shakes as if he were the belfry. The harpoon, too!—toss over the litter there,—d’ye see it?—the forged iron, men, the white whale’s—no, no, no,—blistered fool! this hand did dart it!—’tis in the fish!—Aloft there! Keep him nailed—Quick!—all hands to the rigging of the boats—collect the oars—harpooneers! the irons, the irons!—hoist the royals higher—a pull on all the sheets!—helm there! steady, steady for your life! I’ll ten times girdle the unmeasured globe; yea and dive straight through it, but I’ll slay him yet!”\n\n“Great God! but for one single instant show thyself,” cried Starbuck; “never, never wilt thou capture him, old man—In Jesus’ name no more of this, that’s worse than devil’s madness. Two days chased; twice stove to splinters; thy very leg once more snatched from under thee; thy evil shadow gone—all good angels mobbing thee with warnings:—what more wouldst thou have?—Shall we keep chasing this murderous fish till he swamps the last man? Shall we be dragged by him to the bottom of the sea? Shall we be towed by him to the infernal world? Oh, oh,—Impiety and blasphemy to hunt him more!”\n\n“Starbuck, of late I’ve felt strangely moved to thee; ever since that hour we both saw—thou know’st what, in one another’s eyes. But in this matter of the whale, be the front of thy face to me as the palm of this hand—a lipless, unfeatured blank. Ahab is for ever Ahab, man. This whole act’s immutably decreed. ’Twas rehearsed by thee and me a billion years before this ocean rolled. Fool! I am the Fates’ lieutenant; I act under orders. Look thou, underling! that thou obeyest mine.—Stand round me, men. Ye see an old man cut down to the stump; leaning on a shivered lance; propped up on a lonely foot. ’Tis Ahab—his body’s part; but Ahab’s soul’s a centipede, that moves upon a hundred legs. I feel strained, half stranded, as ropes that tow dismasted frigates in a gale; and I may look so. But ere I break, ye’ll hear me crack; and till ye hear that, know that Ahab’s hawser tows his purpose yet. Believe ye, men, in the things called omens? Then laugh aloud, and cry encore! For ere they drown, drowning things will twice rise to the surface; then rise again, to sink for evermore. So with Moby Dick—two days he’s floated—tomorrow will be the third. Aye, men, he’ll rise once more,—but only to spout his last! D’ye feel brave men, brave?”\n\n“As fearless fire,” cried Stubb.\n\n“And as mechanical,” muttered Ahab. Then as the men went forward, he muttered on: “The things called omens! And yesterday I talked the same to Starbuck there, concerning my broken boat. Oh! how valiantly I seek to drive out of others’ hearts what’s clinched so fast in mine!—The Parsee—the Parsee!—gone, gone? and he was to go before:—but still was to be seen again ere I could perish—How’s that?—There’s a riddle now might baffle all the lawyers backed by the ghosts of the whole line of judges:—like a hawk’s beak it pecks my brain. I’ll, I’ll solve it, though!”\n\nWhen dusk descended, the whale was still in sight to leeward.\n\nSo once more the sail was shortened, and everything passed nearly as on the previous night; only, the sound of hammers, and the hum of the grindstone was heard till nearly daylight, as the men toiled by lanterns in the complete and careful rigging of the spare boats and sharpening their fresh weapons for the morrow. Meantime, of the broken keel of Ahab’s wrecked craft the carpenter made him another leg; while still as on the night before, slouched Ahab stood fixed within his scuttle; his hid, heliotrope glance anticipatingly gone backward on its dial; sat due eastward for the earliest sun.\n\n# CHAPTER 135. The Chase.—Third Day.\n\nThe morning of the third day dawned fair and fresh, and once more the solitary night-man at the fore-mast-head was relieved by crowds of the daylight look-outs, who dotted every mast and almost every spar.\n\n“D’ye see him?” cried Ahab; but the whale was not yet in sight.\n\n“In his infallible wake, though; but follow that wake, that’s all. Helm there; steady, as thou goest, and hast been going. What a lovely day again! were it a new-made world, and made for a summer-house to the angels, and this morning the first of its throwing open to them, a fairer day could not dawn upon that world. Here’s food for thought, had Ahab time to think; but Ahab never thinks; he only feels, feels, feels; that’s tingling enough for mortal man! to think’s audacity. God only has that right and privilege. Thinking is, or ought to be, a coolness and a calmness; and our poor hearts throb, and our poor brains beat too much for that. And yet, I’ve sometimes thought my brain was very calm—frozen calm, this old skull cracks so, like a glass in which the contents turned to ice, and shiver it. And still this hair is growing now; this moment growing, and heat must breed it; but no, it’s like that sort of common grass that will grow anywhere, between the earthy clefts of Greenland ice or in Vesuvius lava. How the wild winds blow it; they whip it about me as the torn shreds of split sails lash the tossed ship they cling to. A vile wind that has no doubt blown ere this through prison corridors and cells, and wards of hospitals, and ventilated them, and now comes blowing hither as innocent as fleeces. Out upon it!—it’s tainted. Were I the wind, I’d blow no more on such a wicked, miserable world. I’d crawl somewhere to a cave, and slink there. And yet, ’tis a noble and heroic thing, the wind! who ever conquered it? In every fight it has the last and bitterest blow. Run tilting at it, and you but run through it. Ha! a coward wind that strikes stark naked men, but will not stand to receive a single blow. Even Ahab is a braver thing—a nobler thing than that. Would now the wind but had a body; but all the things that most exasperate and outrage mortal man, all these things are bodiless, but only bodiless as objects, not as agents. There’s a most special, a most cunning, oh, a most malicious difference! And yet, I say again, and swear it now, that there’s something all glorious and gracious in the wind. These warm Trade Winds, at least, that in the clear heavens blow straight on, in strong and steadfast, vigorous mildness; and veer not from their mark, however the baser currents of the sea may turn and tack, and mightiest Mississippies of the land swift and swerve about, uncertain where to go at last. And by the eternal Poles! these same Trades that so directly blow my good ship on; these Trades, or something like them—something so unchangeable, and full as strong, blow my keeled soul along! To it! Aloft there! What d’ye see?”\n\n“Nothing, sir.”\n“Nothing! and noon at hand! The doubloon goes a-begging! See the sun! Aye, aye, it must be so. I’ve oversailed him. How, got the start? Aye, he’s chasing me now; not I, him—that’s bad; I might have known it, too. Fool! the lines—the harpoons he’s towing. Aye, aye, I have run him by last night. About! about! Come down, all of ye, but the regular look outs! Man the braces!”\n\nSteering as she had done, the wind had been somewhat on the Pequod’s quarter, so that now being pointed in the reverse direction, the braced ship sailed hard upon the breeze as she rechurned the cream in her own white wake.\n\n“Against the wind he now steers for the open jaw,” murmured Starbuck to himself, as he coiled the new-hauled main-brace upon the rail. “God keep us, but already my bones feel damp within me, and from the inside wet my flesh. I misdoubt me that I disobey my God in obeying him!”\n\n“Stand by to sway me up!” cried Ahab, advancing to the hempen basket. “We should meet him soon.”\n\n“Aye, aye, sir,” and straightway Starbuck did Ahab’s bidding, and once more Ahab swung on high.\n\nA whole hour now passed; gold-beaten out to ages. Time itself now held long breaths with keen suspense. But at last, some three points off the weather bow, Ahab descried the spout again, and instantly from the three mast-heads three shrieks went up as if the tongues of fire had voiced it.\n\n“Forehead to forehead I meet thee, this third time, Moby Dick! On deck there!—brace sharper up; crowd her into the wind’s eye. He’s too far off to lower yet, Mr. Starbuck. The sails shake! Stand over that helmsman with a top-maul! So, so; he travels fast, and I must down. But let me have one more good round look aloft here at the sea; there’s time for that. An old, old sight, and yet somehow so young; aye, and not changed a wink since I first saw it, a boy, from the sand-hills of Nantucket! The same!—the same!—the same to Noah as to me. There’s a soft shower to leeward. Such lovely leewardings! They must lead somewhere—to something else than common land, more palmy than the palms. Leeward! the white whale goes that way; look to windward, then; the better if the bitterer quarter. But good bye, good bye, old mast-head! What’s this?—green? aye, tiny mosses in these warped cracks. No such green weather stains on Ahab’s head! There’s the difference now between man’s old age and matter’s. But aye, old mast, we both grow old together; sound in our hulls, though, are we not, my ship? Aye, minus a leg, that’s all. By heaven this dead wood has the better of my live flesh every way. I can’t compare with it; and I’ve known some ships made of dead trees outlast the lives of men made of the most vital stuff of vital fathers. What’s that he said? he should still go before me, my pilot; and yet to be seen again? But where? Will I have eyes at the bottom of the sea, supposing I descend those endless stairs? and all night I’ve been sailing from him, wherever he did sink to. Aye, aye, like many more thou told’st direful truth as touching thyself, O Parsee; but, Ahab, there thy shot fell short. Good-bye, mast-head—keep a good eye upon the whale, the while I’m gone. We’ll talk to-morrow, nay, to-night, when the white whale lies down there, tied by head and tail.”\n\nHe gave the word; and still gazing round him, was steadily lowered through the cloven blue air to the deck.\n\nIn due time the boats were lowered; but as standing in his shallop’s stern, Ahab just hovered upon the point of the descent, he waved to the mate,—who held one of the tackle-ropes on deck—and bade him pause.\n\n“Starbuck!”\n“Sir?”\n\n“For the third time my soul’s ship starts upon this voyage, Starbuck.”\n\n“Aye, sir, thou wilt have it so.”\n\n“Some ships sail from their ports, and ever afterwards are missing, Starbuck!”\n\n“Truth, sir: saddest truth.”\n\n“Some men die at ebb tide; some at low water; some at the full of the flood;—and I feel now like a billow that’s all one crested comb, Starbuck. I am old;—shake hands with me, man.”\n\nTheir hands met; their eyes fastened; Starbuck’s tears the glue.\n\n“Oh, my captain, my captain!—noble heart—go not—go not!—see, it’s a brave man that weeps; how great the agony of the persuasion then!”\n\n“Lower away!”—cried Ahab, tossing the mate’s arm from him. “Stand by the crew!”\n\nIn an instant the boat was pulling round close under the stern.\n\n“The sharks! the sharks!” cried a voice from the low cabin-window there; “O master, my master, come back!”\n\nBut Ahab heard nothing; for his own voice was high-lifted then; and the boat leaped on.\n\nYet the voice spake true; for scarce had he pushed from the ship, when numbers of sharks, seemingly rising from out the dark waters beneath the hull, maliciously snapped at the blades of the oars, every time they dipped in the water; and in this way accompanied the boat with their bites. It is a thing not uncommonly happening to the whale-boats in those swarming seas; the sharks at times apparently following them in the same prescient way that vultures hover over the banners of marching regiments in the east. But these were the first sharks that had been observed by the Pequod since the White Whale had been first descried; and whether it was that Ahab’s crew were all such tiger-yellow barbarians, and therefore their flesh more musky to the senses of the sharks—a matter sometimes well known to affect them,—however it was, they seemed to follow that one boat without molesting the others.\n\n“Heart of wrought steel!” murmured Starbuck gazing over the side, and following with his eyes the receding boat—“canst thou yet ring boldly to that sight?—lowering thy keel among ravening sharks, and followed by them, open-mouthed to the chase; and this the critical third day?—For when three days flow together in one continuous intense pursuit; be sure the first is the morning, the second the noon, and the third the evening and the end of that thing—be that end what it may. Oh! my God! what is this that shoots through me, and leaves me so deadly calm, yet expectant,—fixed at the top of a shudder! Future things swim before me, as in empty outlines and skeletons; all the past is somehow grown dim. Mary, girl! thou fadest in pale glories behind me; boy! I seem to see but thy eyes grown wondrous blue. Strangest problems of life seem clearing; but clouds sweep between—Is my journey’s end coming? My legs feel faint; like his who has footed it all day. Feel thy heart,—beats it yet? Stir thyself, Starbuck!—stave it off—move, move! speak aloud!—Mast-head there! See ye my boy’s hand on the hill?—Crazed;—aloft there!—keep thy keenest eye upon the boats:—mark well the whale!—Ho! again!—drive off that hawk! see! he pecks—he tears the vane”—pointing to the red flag flying at the main-truck—“Ha! he soars away with it!—Where’s the old man now? see’st thou that sight, oh Ahab!—shudder, shudder!”\n\nThe boats had not gone very far, when by a signal from the mast-heads—a downward pointed arm, Ahab knew that the whale had sounded; but intending to be near him at the next rising, he held on his way a little sideways from the vessel; the becharmed crew maintaining the profoundest silence, as the head-beat waves hammered and hammered against the opposing bow.\n\n“Drive, drive in your nails, oh ye waves! to their uttermost heads drive them in! ye but strike a thing without a lid; and no coffin and no hearse can be mine:—and hemp only can kill me! Ha! ha!”\n\nSuddenly the waters around them slowly swelled in broad circles; then quickly upheaved, as if sideways sliding from a submerged berg of ice, swiftly rising to the surface. A low rumbling sound was heard; a subterraneous hum; and then all held their breaths; as bedraggled with trailing ropes, and harpoons, and lances, a vast form shot lengthwise, but obliquely from the sea. Shrouded in a thin drooping veil of mist, it hovered for a moment in the rainbowed air; and then fell swamping back into the deep. Crushed thirty feet upwards, the waters flashed for an instant like heaps of fountains, then brokenly sank in a shower of flakes, leaving the circling surface creamed like new milk round the marble trunk of the whale.\n\n“Give way!” cried Ahab to the oarsmen, and the boats darted forward to the attack; but maddened by yesterday’s fresh irons that corroded in him, Moby Dick seemed combinedly possessed by all the angels that fell from heaven. The wide tiers of welded tendons overspreading his broad white forehead, beneath the transparent skin, looked knitted together; as head on, he came churning his tail among the boats; and once more flailed them apart; spilling out the irons and lances from the two mates’ boats, and dashing in one side of the upper part of their bows, but leaving Ahab’s almost without a scar.\n\nWhile Daggoo and Queequeg were stopping the strained planks; and as the whale swimming out from them, turned, and showed one entire flank as he shot by them again; at that moment a quick cry went up. Lashed round and round to the fish’s back; pinioned in the turns upon turns in which, during the past night, the whale had reeled the involutions of the lines around him, the half torn body of the Parsee was seen; his sable raiment frayed to shreds; his distended eyes turned full upon old Ahab.\n\nThe harpoon dropped from his hand.\n\n“Befooled, befooled!”—drawing in a long lean breath—“Aye, Parsee! I see thee again.—Aye, and thou goest before; and this, this then is the hearse that thou didst promise. But I hold thee to the last letter of thy word. Where is the second hearse? Away, mates, to the ship! those boats are useless now; repair them if ye can in time, and return to me; if not, Ahab is enough to die—Down, men! the first thing that but offers to jump from this boat I stand in, that thing I harpoon. Ye are not other men, but my arms and my legs; and so obey me.—Where’s the whale? gone down again?”\n\nBut he looked too nigh the boat; for as if bent upon escaping with the corpse he bore, and as if the particular place of the last encounter had been but a stage in his leeward voyage, Moby Dick was now again steadily swimming forward; and had almost passed the ship,—which thus far had been sailing in the contrary direction to him, though for the present her headway had been stopped. He seemed swimming with his utmost velocity, and now only intent upon pursuing his own straight path in the sea.\n\n“Oh! Ahab,” cried Starbuck, “not too late is it, even now, the third day, to desist. See! Moby Dick seeks thee not. It is thou, thou, that madly seekest him!”\n\nSetting sail to the rising wind, the lonely boat was swiftly impelled to leeward, by both oars and canvas. And at last when Ahab was sliding by the vessel, so near as plainly to distinguish Starbuck’s face as he leaned over the rail, he hailed him to turn the vessel about, and follow him, not too swiftly, at a judicious interval. Glancing upwards, he saw Tashtego, Queequeg, and Daggoo, eagerly mounting to the three mast-heads; while the oarsmen were rocking in the two staved boats which had but just been hoisted to the side, and were busily at work in repairing them. One after the other, through the port-holes, as he sped, he also caught flying glimpses of Stubb and Flask, busying themselves on deck among bundles of new irons and lances. As he saw all this; as he heard the hammers in the broken boats; far other hammers seemed driving a nail into his heart. But he rallied. And now marking that the vane or flag was gone from the main-mast-head, he shouted to Tashtego, who had just gained that perch, to descend again for another flag, and a hammer and nails, and so nail it to the mast.\n\nWhether fagged by the three days’ running chase, and the resistance to his swimming in the knotted hamper he bore; or whether it was some latent deceitfulness and malice in him: whichever was true, the White Whale’s way now began to abate, as it seemed, from the boat so rapidly nearing him once more; though indeed the whale’s last start had not been so long a one as before. And still as Ahab glided over the waves the unpitying sharks accompanied him; and so pertinaciously stuck to the boat; and so continually bit at the plying oars, that the blades became jagged and crunched, and left small splinters in the sea, at almost every dip.\n\n“Heed them not! those teeth but give new rowlocks to your oars. Pull on! ’tis the better rest, the shark’s jaw than the yielding water.”\n\n“But at every bite, sir, the thin blades grow smaller and smaller!”\n\n“They will last long enough! pull on!—But who can tell”—he muttered—“whether these sharks swim to feast on the whale or on Ahab?—But pull on! Aye, all alive, now—we near him. The helm! take the helm! let me pass,”—and so saying two of the oarsmen helped him forward to the bows of the still flying boat.\n\nAt length as the craft was cast to one side, and ran ranging along with the White Whale’s flank, he seemed strangely oblivious of its advance—as the whale sometimes will—and Ahab was fairly within the smoky mountain mist, which, thrown off from the whale’s spout, curled round his great, Monadnock hump; he was even thus close to him; when, with body arched back, and both arms lengthwise high-lifted to the poise, he darted his fierce iron, and his far fiercer curse into the hated whale. As both steel and curse sank to the socket, as if sucked into a morass, Moby Dick sideways writhed; spasmodically rolled his nigh flank against the bow, and, without staving a hole in it, so suddenly canted the boat over, that had it not been for the elevated part of the gunwale to which he then clung, Ahab would once more have been tossed into the sea. As it was, three of the oarsmen—who foreknew not the precise instant of the dart, and were therefore unprepared for its effects—these were flung out; but so fell, that, in an instant two of them clutched the gunwale again, and rising to its level on a combing wave, hurled themselves bodily inboard again; the third man helplessly dropping astern, but still afloat and swimming.\n\nAlmost simultaneously, with a mighty volition of ungraduated, instantaneous swiftness, the White Whale darted through the weltering sea. But when Ahab cried out to the steersman to take new turns with the line, and hold it so; and commanded the crew to turn round on their seats, and tow the boat up to the mark; the moment the treacherous line felt that double strain and tug, it snapped in the empty air!\n\n“What breaks in me? Some sinew cracks!—’tis whole again; oars! oars! Burst in upon him!”\n\nHearing the tremendous rush of the sea-crashing boat, the whale wheeled round to present his blank forehead at bay; but in that evolution, catching sight of the nearing black hull of the ship; seemingly seeing in it the source of all his persecutions; bethinking it—it may be—a larger and nobler foe; of a sudden, he bore down upon its advancing prow, smiting his jaws amid fiery showers of foam.\n\nAhab staggered; his hand smote his forehead. “I grow blind; hands! stretch out before me that I may yet grope my way. Is’t night?”\n\n“The whale! The ship!” cried the cringing oarsmen.\n\n“Oars! oars! Slope downwards to thy depths, O sea, that ere it be for ever too late, Ahab may slide this last, last time upon his mark! I see: the ship! the ship! Dash on, my men! Will ye not save my ship?”\n\nBut as the oarsmen violently forced their boat through the sledge-hammering seas, the before whale-smitten bow-ends of two planks burst through, and in an instant almost, the temporarily disabled boat lay nearly level with the waves; its half-wading, splashing crew, trying hard to stop the gap and bale out the pouring water.\n\nMeantime, for that one beholding instant, Tashtego’s mast-head hammer remained suspended in his hand; and the red flag, half-wrapping him as with a plaid, then streamed itself straight out from him, as his own forward-flowing heart; while Starbuck and Stubb, standing upon the bowsprit beneath, caught sight of the down-coming monster just as soon as he.\n\n“The whale, the whale! Up helm, up helm! Oh, all ye sweet powers of air, now hug me close! Let not Starbuck die, if die he must, in a woman’s fainting fit. Up helm, I say—ye fools, the jaw! the jaw! Is this the end of all my bursting prayers? all my life-long fidelities? Oh, Ahab, Ahab, lo, thy work. Steady! helmsman, steady. Nay, nay! Up helm again! He turns to meet us! Oh, his unappeasable brow drives on towards one, whose duty tells him he cannot depart. My God, stand by me now!”\n\n“Stand not by me, but stand under me, whoever you are that will now help Stubb; for Stubb, too, sticks here. I grin at thee, thou grinning whale! Who ever helped Stubb, or kept Stubb awake, but Stubb’s own unwinking eye? And now poor Stubb goes to bed upon a mattrass that is all too soft; would it were stuffed with brushwood! I grin at thee, thou grinning whale! Look ye, sun, moon, and stars! I call ye assassins of as good a fellow as ever spouted up his ghost. For all that, I would yet ring glasses with ye, would ye but hand the cup! Oh, oh! oh, oh! thou grinning whale, but there’ll be plenty of gulping soon! Why fly ye not, O Ahab! For me, off shoes and jacket to it; let Stubb die in his drawers! A most mouldy and over salted death, though;—cherries! cherries! cherries! Oh, Flask, for one red cherry ere we die!”\n\n“Cherries? I only wish that we were where they grow. Oh, Stubb, I hope my poor mother’s drawn my part-pay ere this; if not, few coppers will now come to her, for the voyage is up.”\n\nFrom the ship’s bows, nearly all the seamen now hung inactive; hammers, bits of plank, lances, and harpoons, mechanically retained in their hands, just as they had darted from their various employments; all their enchanted eyes intent upon the whale, which from side to side strangely vibrating his predestinating head, sent a broad band of overspreading semicircular foam before him as he rushed. Retribution, swift vengeance, eternal malice were in his whole aspect, and spite of all that mortal man could do, the solid white buttress of his forehead smote the ship’s starboard bow, till men and timbers reeled. Some fell flat upon their faces. Like dislodged trucks, the heads of the harpooneers aloft shook on their bull-like necks. Through the breach, they heard the waters pour, as mountain torrents down a flume.\n\n“The ship! The hearse!—the second hearse!” cried Ahab from the boat; “its wood could only be American!”\n\nDiving beneath the settling ship, the whale ran quivering along its keel; but turning under water, swiftly shot to the surface again, far off the other bow, but within a few yards of Ahab’s boat, where, for a time, he lay quiescent.\n\n“I turn my body from the sun. What ho, Tashtego! let me hear thy hammer. Oh! ye three unsurrendered spires of mine; thou uncracked keel; and only god-bullied hull; thou firm deck, and haughty helm, and Pole-pointed prow,—death-glorious ship! must ye then perish, and without me? Am I cut off from the last fond pride of meanest shipwrecked captains? Oh, lonely death on lonely life! Oh, now I feel my topmost greatness lies in my topmost grief. Ho, ho! from all your furthest bounds, pour ye now in, ye bold billows of my whole foregone life, and top this one piled comber of my death! Towards thee I roll, thou all-destroying but unconquering whale; to the last I grapple with thee; from hell’s heart I stab at thee; for hate’s sake I spit my last breath at thee. Sink all coffins and all hearses to one common pool! and since neither can be mine, let me then tow to pieces, while still chasing thee, though tied to thee, thou damned whale! Thus, I give up the spear!”\n\nThe harpoon was darted; the stricken whale flew forward; with igniting velocity the line ran through the grooves;—ran foul. Ahab stooped to clear it; he did clear it; but the flying turn caught him round the neck, and voicelessly as Turkish mutes bowstring their victim, he was shot out of the boat, ere the crew knew he was gone. Next instant, the heavy eye-splice in the rope’s final end flew out of the stark-empty tub, knocked down an oarsman, and smiting the sea, disappeared in its depths.\n\nFor an instant, the tranced boat’s crew stood still; then turned. “The ship? Great God, where is the ship?” Soon they through dim, bewildering mediums saw her sidelong fading phantom, as in the gaseous Fata Morgana; only the uppermost masts out of water; while fixed by infatuation, or fidelity, or fate, to their once lofty perches, the pagan harpooneers still maintained their sinking lookouts on the sea. And now, concentric circles seized the lone boat itself, and all its crew, and each floating oar, and every lance-pole, and spinning, animate and inanimate, all round and round in one vortex, carried the smallest chip of the Pequod out of sight.\n\nBut as the last whelmings intermixingly poured themselves over the sunken head of the Indian at the mainmast, leaving a few inches of the erect spar yet visible, together with long streaming yards of the flag, which calmly undulated, with ironical coincidings, over the destroying billows they almost touched;—at that instant, a red arm and a hammer hovered backwardly uplifted in the open air, in the act of nailing the flag faster and yet faster to the subsiding spar. A sky-hawk that tauntingly had followed the main-truck downwards from its natural home among the stars, pecking at the flag, and incommoding Tashtego there; this bird now chanced to intercept its broad fluttering wing between the hammer and the wood; and simultaneously feeling that etherial thrill, the submerged savage beneath, in his death-gasp, kept his hammer frozen there; and so the bird of heaven, with archangelic shrieks, and his imperial beak thrust upwards, and his whole captive form folded in the flag of Ahab, went down with his ship, which, like Satan, would not sink to hell till she had dragged a living part of heaven along with her, and helmeted herself with it.\n\nNow small fowls flew screaming over the yet yawning gulf; a sullen white surf beat against its steep sides; then all collapsed, and the great shroud of the sea rolled on as it rolled five thousand years ago.\n\nEpilogue\n\nThe drama’s done. Why then here does any one step forth?—Because one did survive the wreck.\n\nIt so chanced, that after the Parsee’s disappearance, I was he whom the Fates ordained to take the place of Ahab’s bowsman, when that bowsman assumed the vacant post; the same, who, when on the last day the three men were tossed from out of the rocking boat, was dropped astern. So, floating on the margin of the ensuing scene, and in full sight of it, when the halfspent suction of the sunk ship reached me, I was then, but slowly, drawn towards the closing vortex. When I reached it, it had subsided to a creamy pool. Round and round, then, and ever contracting towards the button-like black bubble at the axis of that slowly wheeling circle, like another Ixion I did revolve. Till, gaining that vital centre, the black bubble upward burst; and now, liberated by reason of its cunning spring, and, owing to its great buoyancy, rising with great force, the coffin life-buoy shot lengthwise from the sea, fell over, and floated by my side. Buoyed up by that coffin, for almost one whole day and night, I floated on a soft and dirgelike main. The unharming sharks, they glided by as if with padlocks on their mouths; the savage sea-hawks sailed with sheathed beaks. On the second day, a sail drew near, nearer, and picked me up at last. It was the devious-cruising Rachel, that in her retracing search after her missing children, only found another orphan.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-2701"]="이 텍스트는 두 개의 전자 텍스트를 결합한 것으로, 하나는 지금은 폐지된 버지니아 공대의 ERIS 프로젝트에서, 다른 하나는 프로젝트 구텐베르크의 자료실에서 가져온 것이다. 이 판본의 교정자들은 버지니아 공대 판본을 보존해 준 애들레이드 대학교 도서관에 감사를 표한다. 그렇게 만들어진 전자 텍스트는 퍼블릭 도메인의 인쇄본과 대조 검토되었다.\n\n어원.\n(어느 문법학교의 작고한 폐병 걸린 보조교사가 제공함.)\n\n창백한 보조교사 — 외투도, 마음도, 몸도, 머릿속도 모두 닳아 해진 그 사람이 지금도 눈앞에 선하다. 그는 늘 낡은 사전과 문법책의 먼지를 떨어내곤 했는데, 세상에 알려진 모든 나라의 화려한 깃발들로 우습게 장식된 기묘한 손수건을 썼다. 그는 낡은 문법책의 먼지 떠는 일을 좋아했다. 어쩐지 그 일이 그에게 자신의 죽을 운명을 은근히 일깨워 주었던 것이다.\n\n\"그대가 다른 이들을 가르치려 손을 들고, 우리 말로 고래라는 물고기를 무어라 불러야 하는지 일러주면서, 무지로 인해 H라는 글자를 빠뜨린다면 — 그 글자야말로 그 단어의 의미를 거의 홀로 이루어 내는 것이거늘 — 그대는 진실 아닌 것을 전하는 셈이다.\" —해클루트(Hackluyt).\n\n\"고래(WHALE). * * * 스웨덴어와 덴마크어의 hval. 이 동물은 둥글거나 구르는 모양에서 이름이 붙여졌다. 덴마크어의 hvalt는 아치형 또는 둥근 천장 모양을 뜻하기 때문이다.\" —웹스터 사전(Webster's Dictionary).\n\n\"고래(WHALE). * * * 더 직접적으로는 네덜란드어와 독일어의 Wallen, 그리고 앵글로색슨어의 Walw-ian에서 왔으며, 구르다, 뒹굴다라는 뜻이다.\" —리처드슨 사전(Richardson's Dictionary).\n\nחו, 히브리어. ϰητος, 그리스어. CETUS, 라틴어. WHŒL, 앵글로색슨어. HVALT, 덴마크어. WAL, 네덜란드어. HWAL, 스웨덴어. HVALUR, 아이슬란드어. WHALE, 영어. BALEINE, 프랑스어. BALLENA, 스페인어. PEKEE-NUEE-NUEE, 피지어. PEHEE-NUEE-NUEE, 에로망고어.\n\n발췌록. (어느 보조 사서가 제공함.)\n\n보다시피 가련한 이 보조 사서, 부지런히 땅을 파헤치는 굼벵이 같은 이 불쌍한 작자는 신성한 것이든 세속적인 것이든 그 어떤 책에서든 닥치는 대로 찾아낼 수 있는 고래에 관한 온갖 잡다한 언급을 주워 모으느라, 세상의 기나긴 바티칸 도서관들과 거리의 헌책 가판대들을 두루 헤맨 듯하다. 그러므로 적어도 모든 경우에 있어, 이 발췌록 안에 담긴 뒤죽박죽 고래 진술들이 아무리 진본이라 한들 그것을 참된 고래학의 복음으로 여겨서는 안 된다. 결코 그래서는 안 된다. 여기 등장하는 시인들은 물론 일반적으로 고대의 저자들에 관해서도, 이 발췌록들은 오로지 우리 자신을 포함한 여러 민족과 세대가 리바이어던(Leviathan)에 관해 두서없이 말하고, 생각하고, 상상하고, 노래해 온 바를 새가 하늘에서 슬쩍 굽어보듯 일별하게 해 준다는 점에서만 가치가 있거나 흥미로운 것이다.\n\n그러니 잘 가시게, 가련한 보조 사서여, 나는 그대의 주석가일 뿐이다. 그대는 이 세상 그 어떤 술로도 결코 데워지지 않을 저 희망 없고 누렇게 뜬 부족(部族)에 속해 있다. 그들에게는 창백한 셰리주조차 너무 붉고 독할 터이지만, 그럼에도 더러는 그들과 더불어 앉아 함께 가련해지고 싶어지기도 하는 법, 눈물로 흥에 겨워, 그렁그렁한 눈과 빈 잔을 들고서, 아주 불쾌하지만은 않은 슬픔에 잠겨 그들에게 무뚝뚝하게 말하고 싶어지는 것이다 — 그만두게, 보조 사서들이여! 그대들이 세상을 기쁘게 하려 애를 쓰면 쓸수록, 그만큼 더 영영 감사 한마디 받지 못하리니! 그대들을 위해 햄프턴 궁과 튀일리 궁을 통째로 비워 줄 수 있다면 얼마나 좋으련만! 그러나 눈물일랑 꿀꺽 삼키고, 마음만은 최상부 돛대 꼭대기로 날아오르게나. 그대들보다 먼저 떠나간 벗들이 일곱 층 하늘을 비워 두고, 오래도록 호강하던 가브리엘과 미카엘과 라파엘을 그대들의 도래에 대비해 피난민으로 내몰고 있으니. 이 세상에서 그대들은 깨어진 마음끼리 부딪칠 뿐이지만 — 저 세상에서는 결코 깨지지 않을 잔들을 마주쳐 울리게 되리라!\n발췌록.\n\n\"하느님께서 큰 고래들을 창조하셨다.\" —창세기.\n\n\"리바이어던은 자기가 지나간 뒤에 빛나는 길을 남기니, 깊은 바다가 백발이 된 듯하다.\" —욥기.\n\n\"이제 주께서 큰 물고기를 예비하사 요나(Jonah)를 삼키게 하셨더라.\" —요나서.\n\n\"저기 배들이 다니고, 주께서 거기서 놀라고 지으신 저 리바이어던이 있나이다.\" —시편.\n\n\"그날에 주께서 그의 매섭고 크고 강한 칼로 꿰뚫는 뱀 리바이어던, 곧 꼬불꼬불한 뱀 리바이어던을 벌하시며, 바다에 있는 용을 죽이시리라.\" —이사야서.\n\n\"그 밖에 무엇이든 이 괴물의 입이 이루는 혼돈 속으로 들어오는 것은, 짐승이든 배든 돌이든, 그 더럽고 거대한 목구멍 속으로 거침없이 곤두박질쳐 들어가, 밑바닥 없는 그 위장의 심연 속에서 스러지고 만다.\" —홀랜드 역(譯) 플루타르코스의 모랄리아.\n\n\"인도양은 가장 많고 가장 큰 물고기들을 길러 내는데, 그중에서도 발라이네(Balaene)라 불리는 고래와 소용돌이 고래는 그 길이가 네 에이커, 곧 네 아르팡의 땅에 맞먹는다.\" —홀랜드 역 플리니우스.\n\n\"바다로 나선 지 겨우 이틀이 지났을 무렵, 해 뜰 즈음에 수많은 고래들과 다른 바다 괴물들이 나타났다. 그중 한 마리는 더없이 괴물 같은 크기였다.... 그것이 입을 벌린 채 사방으로 파도를 일으키며, 앞바다를 거품으로 휘몰아치며 우리 쪽으로 다가왔다.\" —투크 역 루키아노스. \"참된 역사.\"\n\n\"그는 또한 이 나라를 찾아왔는데, 이는 이빨로 인해 매우 값진 뼈를 지닌 바다코끼리를 잡으려는 뜻에서였으며, 그 이빨 몇 개를 왕에게 가져다 바쳤다.... 가장 좋은 고래들은 그의 고향에서 잡혔는데, 그중 어떤 것은 마흔여덟 야드, 어떤 것은 쉰 야드 길이였다. 그는 자신이 여섯 사람 중 하나로서 이틀 동안 예순 마리를 잡았노라고 말했다.\" —890년에 앨프레드 대왕이 오테르(혹은 오테르)의 입에서 직접 받아 적은 구술 이야기.\n\n\"그리고 다른 모든 것들은, 짐승이든 배든, 이 괴물(고래)의 입이 이루는 무시무시한 심연 속으로 들어가면 곧바로 사라지고 삼켜지지만, 바다 모샘치는 그 안으로 아주 안전하게 들어가 거기서 잠을 잔다.\" —몽테뉴. 레몽 스봉을 위한 변호.\n\n\"달아나자, 달아나자! 인내심 깊은 욥의 생애 속에서 고귀한 예언자 모세가 묘사한 그 리바이어던이 아니라면 악마가 날 잡아가도 좋다.\" —라블레.\n\n\"이 고래의 간은 짐수레 두 대 분량이었다.\" —스토우의 연대기.\n\n\"바다를 끓는 냄비처럼 들끓게 하는 위대한 리바이어던.\" —베이컨 경의 시편 번역.\n\n\"고래 혹은 오르크라는 저 괴물 같은 거구에 관해서는 우리가 확실히 받아들인 바가 없다. 그것들은 엄청나게 살이 쪄서, 고래 한 마리에서 믿기 어려운 양의 기름이 추출된다.\" —같은 책. \"삶과 죽음의 역사.\"\n\n\"속의 멍에는 향유고래 기름이 이 세상에서 으뜸가는 약이다.\" —헨리 왕.\n\n\"꼭 고래 같구나.\" —햄릿.\n\n\"그것을 막아 보려 한들 의원의 어떤 솜씨도 그에게 소용없으니, 다만 제 상처를 낸 자에게로 되돌아갈 뿐, 낮은 작살이 그 가슴을 찔러 그칠 줄 모르는 고통을 낳았기에, 상처 입은 고래가 망망대해를 가르며 뭍으로 달아나듯이.\" —요정 여왕.\n\n\"고래처럼 거대하여, 그 거대한 몸뚱이의 움직임이 고요한 정적 속에서도 바다를 들끓을 만큼 휘저어 놓는다.\" —윌리엄 데버넌트 경. 곤디버트 서문.\n\n\"향유고래 기름이 무엇인지는 사람들이 마땅히 의심할 만하니, 학식 깊은 호스만누스조차 삼십 년에 걸친 저작에서 분명히 말하기를, '그것이 무엇인지 나는 모른다(Nescio quid sit)'고 하였다.\" —토머스 브라운 경. 향유고래 기름과 향유고래에 관하여. 그의 V. E. 참조.\n\n\"스펜서의 탈루스가 그 현대적 도리깨를 휘두르듯, 그 묵직한 꼬리로 파멸을 위협한다. ... 그 옆구리에는 박힌 투창들을 지니고, 그 등에는 창들의 숲이 솟아 있다.\" —월러의 여름 섬 전투.\n\n\"기예로써 저 위대한 리바이어던, 곧 코먼웰스 또는 국가(라틴어로 Civitas)라 불리는 것이 창조되는데, 그것은 다만 인공의 인간일 따름이다.\" —홉스의 리바이어던 첫 문장.\n\n\"어리석은 맨소울은 그것을 씹지도 않고 삼켰으니, 마치 고래 입 속의 청어 새끼와도 같았다.\" —천로역정.\n\n\"하느님께서 당신의 모든 작품 가운데 바다의 물줄기를 헤엄치는 것 중 가장 거대하게 지으신 저 바다 짐승 리바이어던.\" —실낙원.\n\n—\"저기 리바이어던, 살아 있는 피조물 가운데 가장 거대한 것이, 깊은 바다 속에 곶처럼 길게 뻗어 잠들거나 헤엄치며, 움직이는 땅덩어리처럼 보이고, 아가미로는 물을 빨아들이고 숨결로는 바다를 뿜어낸다.\" —같은 책.\n\n\"물의 바다 속을 헤엄치며, 제 몸속에는 기름의 바다를 헤엄치게 하는 저 거대한 고래들.\" —풀러의 세속과 성스러운 나라.\n\n\"어느 곶 뒤에 바싹 붙어, 거대한 리바이어던이 먹이를 노리며 도사리고서, 한 치의 기회도 주지 않은 채, 그 떡 벌린 아가리 속으로 길을 잘못 든 작은 물고기 떼를 통째로 삼켜 버린다.\" —드라이든의 기적의 해.\n\n\"고래가 배의 고물 쪽에 떠 있는 동안, 그들은 그 머리를 잘라 내어 보트로 뭍에 닿을 수 있는 데까지 최대한 가까이 끌고 가는데, 다만 물 깊이 열두 자나 열세 자쯤 되는 곳에서 바닥에 닿고 만다.\" —토머스 에지의 스피츠베르겐 열 차례 항해, 퍼처스 수록.\n\n\"그들은 가는 길에 수많은 고래들이 바다에서 노니는 것을 보았는데, 그것들은 멋대로 장난을 치며 자연이 그 어깨 위에 마련해 준 관과 구멍으로 물을 뿜어 올리고 있었다.\" —토머스 허버트 경의 아시아 아프리카 항해기. 해리스 총서.\n\n\"여기서 그들은 어찌나 거대한 고래 떼를 보았던지, 행여 배가 그것들 위로 올라타지나 않을까 두려워 몹시 조심하며 나아갈 수밖에 없었다.\" —스호우텐의 여섯 번째 세계 일주.\n\n\"우리는 북동풍을 받으며 '고래 뱃속의 요나'라는 이름의 배를 타고 엘베강에서 출항했다.... 어떤 이들은 고래가 입을 벌리지 못한다고 하나, 그것은 꾸며 낸 이야기다.... 그들은 고래가 보이는지 살피러 자주 돛대 위로 기어오르는데, 처음 발견한 자에게는 그 수고의 대가로 두카트 금화 한 닢이 주어지기 때문이다.... 셰틀랜드 근처에서 잡힌 어느 고래는 뱃속에 청어가 한 통이 넘게 들어 있었다는 이야기를 들었다.... 우리 작살잡이 중 하나가 내게 말하기를, 자기가 한번은 스피츠베르겐에서 온몸이 새하얀 고래를 잡은 적이 있다고 했다.\" —그린란드 항해기, 1671년. 해리스 총서.\n\n\"1652년에 이 해안(파이프)으로 고래 몇 마리가 떠밀려 왔는데, 그중 길이 여든 자나 되는 고래수염 종류 한 마리가 들어왔다. (내가 들은 바로는) 이놈은 막대한 양의 기름 외에도 고래수염 500웨이트를 내주었다고 한다. 그 턱뼈는 핏페런 정원에서 문 노릇을 하고 있다.\" —시발드의 파이프와 킨로스.\n\n\"나 자신이 이 향유고래를 제압하여 죽일 수 있는지 시험해 보기로 했으니, 이는 그토록 사납고 빠른 까닭에 그런 종류를 어느 누가 죽였다는 소리를 내 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없기 때문이다.\" —리처드 스트래퍼드가 버뮤다에서 보낸 편지. 철학회보, 1668년.\n\n\"바다의 고래들은 하느님의 목소리에 복종하느니라.\" —뉴잉글랜드 입문서.\n\n\"우리는 또한 큰 고래들을 무수히 보았는데, 말하자면 우리 북쪽 바다보다 그 남쪽 바다에 백 배는 더 많았다.\" —카울리 선장의 세계 일주 항해기, 1729년.\n\n\"... 그리고 고래의 숨결에는 흔히 견딜 수 없는 악취가 따라붙어, 뇌에 이상을 일으킬 정도다.\" —우요아의 남아메리카.\n\n\"특별히 이름난 쉰 명의 정예 실프에게 우리는 그 중대한 임무, 곧 페티코트를 맡기노라. 후프로 채워 넣고 고래 갈빗대로 무장한들, 그 일곱 겹 울타리가 무너지는 일을 우리는 종종 보아 왔으니.\" —머리타래의 강탈.\n\n\"육지의 동물을 그 크기로 깊은 바다에 깃들어 사는 것들과 견주어 보면, 그 비교에서 육지 동물은 보잘것없어 보일 것이다. 고래는 의심할 바 없이 만물 가운데 가장 큰 동물이다.\" —골드스미스, 박물지.\n\n\"작은 물고기들을 위한 우화를 쓴다면, 자네는 그것들이 큰 고래처럼 말하게 만들 걸세.\" —골드스미스가 존슨에게.\n\n\"오후에 우리는 바위로 짐작되는 것을 보았으나, 그것이 죽은 고래임이 드러났다. 어떤 아시아인들이 그것을 죽여 뭍으로 끌고 가던 중이었다. 그들은 우리 눈에 띄지 않으려고 고래 뒤에 몸을 숨기려 애쓰는 듯했다.\" —쿡의 항해기.\n\n\"더 큰 고래들은 그들이 좀처럼 공격하려 들지 않는다. 그중 어떤 것들은 어찌나 무서워하는지, 바다에 나가면 그 이름조차 입에 올리기를 꺼리며, 그것들이 너무 가까이 다가오는 것을 겁주어 막으려고 똥과 석회석과 향나무, 그 밖에 같은 종류의 물건들을 보트에 싣고 다닌다.\" —우노 폰 트로일의 편지, 1772년 뱅크스와 솔란데르의 아이슬란드 항해에 관하여.\n\n\"낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들이 발견한 향유고래는 활기차고 사나운 동물이어서, 어부들에게 막대한 기량과 담력을 요한다.\" —토머스 제퍼슨이 1778년 프랑스 대신에게 보낸 고래 각서.\n\n\"그런데 선생, 도대체 세상에 무엇이 그것에 견줄 수 있단 말이오?\" —에드먼드 버크가 의회에서 낸터킷 고래잡이를 언급하며.\n\n\"스페인 — 유럽의 해안에 좌초한 거대한 고래.\" —에드먼드 버크. (어딘가에서.)\n\n\"국왕의 통상 세입 가운데 열 번째 항목은, 바다를 해적과 도적으로부터 지키고 보호하는 데 대한 고려에서 비롯되었다고 하는데, 바로 '왕실' 어류에 대한 권리로서, 이는 고래와 철갑상어다. 그리고 이것들이 뭍으로 떠밀려 오거나 해안 가까이에서 잡히면 국왕의 소유가 된다.\" —블랙스톤.\n\n\"머지않아 선원들은 죽음의 놀이를 향해 모여드니, 로드먼드는 빗나감 없이 미늘 박힌 강철을 머리 위로 치켜들고, 매 순간을 노린다.\" —팰코너의 난파선.\n\n\"지붕도, 둥근 천장도, 첨탑도 환히 빛나고, 로켓들은 스스로 솟구쳐 올라, 그 찰나의 불꽃을 하늘 궁창 둘레에 매달았네.\n\n그리하여 불을 물에 견주노라면, 바다가 저 높은 곳에서 한몫을 하니, 고래가 허공으로 뿜어 올리는 그 물줄기로, 주체할 수 없는 기쁨을 드러내는도다.\" —쿠퍼, 여왕의 런던 방문에 부쳐.\n\n\"한 번 박동할 때마다 심장에서 열 갤런 내지 열다섯 갤런의 피가 어마어마한 속도로 뿜어져 나온다.\" —존 헌터의 고래 해부 기록. (작은 크기의 것.)\n\n\"고래의 대동맥은 그 구멍이 런던 다리 상수도의 본관보다 더 굵으며, 그 관을 통해 우렁차게 흐르는 물조차 고래 심장에서 솟구치는 피의 기세와 속도에는 미치지 못한다.\" —페일리의 신학.\n\n\"고래는 뒷다리가 없는 포유동물이다.\" —퀴비에 남작.\n\n\"남위 40도에서 우리는 향유고래들을 보았으나, 5월 1일에 이르러서야 비로소 한 마리를 잡았는데, 그때는 바다가 온통 그것들로 뒤덮여 있었다.\" —콜넷의 향유고래잡이 확장을 위한 항해.\n\n\"내 발밑의 자유로운 원소 속에서, 온갖 빛깔과 모양과 종류의 물고기들이 헤엄치고, 허우적대며 자맥질하니, 놀이로, 추격으로, 싸움으로 — 그 모습은 언어로도 그려 낼 수 없고 어떤 뱃사람도 본 적 없는 것이라. 무시무시한 리바이어던에서부터 물결마다 가득 채운 수백만의 곤충에 이르기까지, 떠다니는 섬처럼 거대한 무리를 이루어 모여들어, 신비로운 본능에 이끌려 저 황량하고 길 없는 영역을 가로지르며, 사방에서 게걸스러운 적들에게 — 고래와 상어와 괴물들, 그 앞머리나 턱에 칼과 톱과 나선 뿔과 갈고리 송곳니로 무장한 것들에게 공격받으면서도.\" —몽고메리의 대홍수 이전의 세계.\n\n\"이오! 파이안! 이오! 노래하라. 지느러미 백성의 왕에게. 이놈보다 더 힘센 고래는 저 광대한 대서양에도 없으며, 이놈보다 더 살진 물고기는 극지의 바다를 헤매어도 없으리라.\" —찰스 램의 고래의 개선.\n\n\"1690년에 어떤 이들이 높은 언덕 위에서 고래들이 물을 뿜고 서로 어울려 노니는 것을 바라보고 있었는데, 그중 한 사람이 바다를 가리키며 이렇게 말했다. 저기가 — 우리 자손의 자손이 빵을 구하러 갈 푸른 초원이라네.\" —오베드 메이시의 낸터킷 역사.\n\n\"나는 수전과 나를 위해 오두막 한 채를 지었고, 고래 턱뼈를 세워 고딕식 아치 모양의 출입문을 만들었다.\" —호손의 두 번 들려준 이야기.\n\n\"그녀는 자신의 첫사랑을 위한 비석을 부탁하러 왔는데, 그 사람은 무려 사십 년 전에 태평양에서 고래에게 목숨을 잃었다고 했다.\" —같은 책.\n\n\"아니올시다, 나리, 저건 참고래입니다.\" 톰이 대답했다. \"물줄기를 봤습죠. 기독교인이라면 누구나 보고 싶어 할 만큼 어여쁜 무지개를 한 쌍이나 뿜어 올렸거든요. 저놈은 진짜 기름통이올시다, 저 녀석은!\" —쿠퍼의 도선사.\n\n\"신문이 들어왔고, 우리는 베를린 가제트에서 그곳 무대에 고래가 등장했다는 기사를 보았다.\" —에커만의 괴테와의 대화.\n\n\"맙소사! 체이스 씨, 무슨 일이오?\" 나는 대답했다. \"고래에게 들이받혔습니다.\" —\"태평양에서 거대한 향유고래에게 공격받아 끝내 파괴된 낸터킷 포경선 에식스호의 난파에 관한 이야기.\" 그 배의 일등 항해사였던 낸터킷의 오언 체이스 지음. 뉴욕, 1821년.\n\n\"어느 밤 한 뱃사람이 삭구 사이에 앉아 있었네, 바람은 거침없이 휘파람을 불었고, 창백한 달빛은 밝아졌다 흐려졌다 하였으며, 고래가 바다에서 허우적댈 때 그 항적에는 인광이 번득였네.\" —엘리자베스 오크스 스미스.\n\n\"이 고래 한 마리를 잡는 데 가담한 보트들에서 풀려나간 밧줄의 길이는 통틀어 1만 440야드, 곧 거의 영국 거리로 6마일에 달했다....\n\n\"때로 고래는 그 무시무시한 꼬리를 허공에 흔들어 대는데, 그것이 채찍처럼 철썩 소리를 내며 삼사 마일 거리까지 울려 퍼진다.\" —스코어스비.\n\n\"이 새로운 공격으로 겪는 고통에 미쳐 날뛰는 성난 향유고래는 몇 번이고 몸을 뒤집으며, 그 거대한 머리를 쳐들고, 떡 벌린 아가리로 주위의 모든 것을 물어뜯는다. 그것은 머리로 보트들을 향해 돌진하니, 보트들은 그 앞에서 엄청난 속도로 떠밀려 나가고, 때로는 완전히 박살 나고 만다.... 참으로 놀라운 일은, 이토록 흥미로우면서도 상업적 관점에서 그토록 중요한 동물(향유고래)의 습성에 대한 고찰이 그처럼 철저히 등한시되어 왔다는 점, 또는 근래에 그 습성을 관찰할 가장 풍부하고 가장 편리한 기회를 분명히 누렸을 수많은 관찰자들, 그것도 그중 다수가 유능한 관찰자들이었음에도 그토록 적은 호기심밖에 불러일으키지 못했다는 점이다.\" —토머스 빌의 향유고래의 역사, 1839년.\n\n\"카샬로트('향유고래')는 참고래('그린란드 고래' 또는 '참고래')보다 더 잘 무장되어 있을 뿐 아니라 — 몸의 양쪽 끝에 가공할 무기를 지니고 있기 때문이거니와 — 그 무기들을 공격적으로, 그것도 더없이 교묘하고 대담하며 짓궂은 방식으로 즐겨 휘두르는 기질을 더 자주 드러내는 까닭에, 알려진 모든 고래 종족 가운데 공격하기에 가장 위험한 것으로 여겨지기에 이른다.\" —프레더릭 데벨 베닛의 세계 일주 포경 항해, 1840년.\n\n10월 13일. \"저기 물 뿜는다!\" 돛대 꼭대기에서 외치는 소리가 울려 퍼졌다. \"어디냐?\" 선장이 물었다. \"바람 불어 가는 쪽 이물에서 세 점 떨어진 곳입니다, 나리.\" \"키를 돌려라. 침착하게!\" \"침착하게, 나리.\" \"돛대 꼭대기! 이제 그 고래가 보이느냐?\" \"예, 예, 나리! 향유고래 떼입니다! 저기 물 뿜는다! 저기 솟구쳐 오른다!\" \"외쳐라! 보일 때마다 외쳐!\" \"예, 예, 나리! 저기 물 뿜는다! 저기 — 저기 — 저어기 물 뿜는다 — 뿜는다 — 뿌우운다!\" \"거리는?\" \"이 마일 반입니다.\" \"천둥 번개로다! 그토록 가깝다니! 전원 집합시켜라.\" —J. 로스 브라운의 포경 항해 스케치. 1846년.\n\n\"이제부터 우리가 이야기하려는 그 끔찍한 사건들이 벌어진 포경선 글로브호는 낸터킷섬 소속이었다.\" —\"글로브호 반란 이야기.\" 생존자 레이와 허시 지음. 1828년.\n\n\"그는 자기가 부상을 입힌 고래에게 한번 쫓기던 차에, 한동안 창으로 그 공격을 막아 냈다. 그러나 성난 괴물이 끝내 보트로 돌진해 왔고, 그와 동료들은 그 돌격이 피할 수 없음을 깨닫고 물속으로 뛰어든 덕분에 가까스로 목숨을 건졌다.\" —타이어먼과 베닛의 선교 일지.\n\n\"낸터킷 그 자체가,\" 웹스터 씨가 말했다, \"국가적 이익에서 매우 두드러지고 특이한 부분입니다. 여기 바다 위에 팔구천 명의 인구가 살면서, 가장 대담하고 가장 끈질긴 근면으로 해마다 국부에 크게 보태고 있습니다.\" —낸터킷에 방파제를 세우자는 청원에 관한, 미국 상원에서 행한 대니얼 웹스터의 연설 보고서. 1828년.\n\n\"고래가 그의 바로 위로 떨어졌고, 아마도 한순간에 그를 죽였을 것이다.\" —\"고래와 그 포획자들, 혹은 포경꾼의 모험과 고래의 전기, 커모도어 프레블호의 귀항 항해에서 모은 이야기.\" 헨리 T. 치버 목사 지음.\n\n\"네가 손톱만큼이라도 소리를 낸다면,\" 새뮤얼이 대답했다, \"널 지옥으로 보내 버리겠다.\" —새뮤얼 콤스톡(그 반란자)의 생애, 그의 동생 윌리엄 콤스톡 지음. 포경선 글로브호 이야기의 또 다른 판본.\n\n\"네덜란드인과 영국인이 가능하다면 인도로 통하는 항로를 발견하려고 북극해로 떠난 항해들은, 비록 그 주된 목적은 이루지 못했으나, 고래의 서식처를 활짝 열어 보였다.\" —매컬럭의 상업 사전.\n\n\"이런 일들은 서로 주고받는 것이다. 공은 다시 앞으로 튀어 나가려 되튀어 오르는 법, 이제 고래의 서식처를 열어 보이는 가운데, 포경꾼들은 다름 아닌 저 신비로운 북서 항로로 향하는 새로운 실마리를 우연히 짚어 낸 듯하다.\" —미간행 \"어떤 글\"에서.\n\n\"바다에서 포경선과 마주치고서 그 가까운 모습에 강한 인상을 받지 않기란 불가능하다. 돛을 줄이고, 돛대 꼭대기마다 망꾼을 세워 둔 채 주위의 광활한 바다를 열심히 살피는 그 배는, 통상적인 항해에 나선 배들과는 전혀 다른 분위기를 풍긴다.\" —해류와 포경. 미국 탐사 원정대.\n\n\"런던 근교와 그 밖의 여러 곳을 거니는 사람들이라면, 땅 위에 곧추세워진 커다랗게 휘어진 뼈들을 본 기억이 있을 것이다. 그것들은 문 위에 아치를 이루거나 정자의 입구를 이루고 있었는데, 어쩌면 그들은 그것이 고래의 갈빗대라는 이야기를 들었을지도 모른다.\" —북극해로 떠난 어느 고래 항해자의 이야기.\n\n\"바로 이 고래들을 쫓아 떠났던 보트들이 돌아오고 나서야, 백인들은 자기네 배가, 선원으로 받아들인 야만인들에게 피투성이로 점령당한 것을 보았다.\" —포경선 호보맥호의 탈취와 탈환에 관한 신문 기사.\n\n\"(미국) 포경선의 선원들 가운데 자기가 떠날 때 탔던 그 배를 타고 돌아오는 자가 거의 없다는 것은 일반적으로 잘 알려진 사실이다.\" —포경 보트에서의 항해.\n\n\"별안간 거대한 덩어리가 물속에서 솟아올라, 허공으로 수직으로 치솟았다. 그것은 고래였다.\" —미리엄 코핀, 혹은 고래잡이 어부.\n\n\"고래는 물론 작살에 찔린다. 그러나 한번 생각해 보라, 그저 꼬리 밑동에 밧줄 한 가닥을 묶었을 뿐인데 힘세고 길들지 않은 망아지를 어떻게 다스리겠는가.\" —갈빗대와 수레바퀴 속 포경에 관한 한 장(章).\n\n\"한번은 이 괴물(고래) 두 마리, 아마도 수컷과 암컷인 듯한 것들이 돌 던지면 닿을 만한 거리도 안 되는 해안 가까이에서, 한 마리가 다른 한 마리를 뒤따르며 천천히 헤엄치는 것을 보았다(티에라델푸에고). 그 위로는 너도밤나무가 가지를 드리우고 있었다.\" —다윈의 어느 박물학자의 항해.\n\n\"'전원 뒤로 저어라!' 항해사가 고개를 돌리다가, 거대한 향유고래가 떡 벌린 아가리를 보트 머리 바로 곁에 들이대고 당장이라도 박살 낼 듯 위협하는 것을 보고 외쳤다 — '전원 뒤로 저어라, 목숨이 아깝거든!'\" —고래 잡이 와튼.\n\n\"그러니 기운을 내게, 친구들이여, 대담한 작살잡이가 고래를 찔러 대는 동안, 그대들의 마음일랑 결코 꺾이지 말기를!\" —낸터킷 노래.\n\n\"오, 진귀하고 늙은 고래여, 폭풍과 강풍 한가운데 그 바다 보금자리에서, 힘이 곧 정의인 곳의 강자가 되어, 가없는 바다의 왕이 되리라.\" —고래의 노래.\n\n# CHAPTER 1. Loomings.\n\n나를 이슈메일이라 불러 다오. 몇 해 전 — 정확히 얼마나 됐는지는 아무래도 좋다 — 지갑에는 돈이 거의 혹은 전혀 없었고, 뭍에서는 딱히 흥미를 끄는 일도 없었기에, 나는 잠시 배를 타고 떠돌며 이 세상의 바닷물 진 부분이나 구경해 볼까 생각했다. 그것은 내가 우울을 떨쳐 내고 피의 순환을 다스리는 방식이다. 입가가 험상궂게 일그러지는 것을 느낄 때마다, 내 영혼에 축축하고 보슬비 내리는 11월이 깃들 때마다, 나도 모르게 관(棺) 파는 가게 앞에서 발을 멈추고 마주치는 장례 행렬마다 그 꽁무니를 따라가게 될 때마다, 그리고 특히 내 우울증이 어찌나 나를 짓누르는지 거리로 성큼 걸어 나가 사람들의 모자를 차례차례 후려쳐 떨어뜨리지 못하도록 막으려면 굳건한 도덕적 원칙이 필요해질 때마다 — 그럴 때면 나는 되도록 빨리 바다로 나가야 할 때가 되었다고 여긴다. 이것이 권총과 총알을 대신하는 나의 방편이다. 카토는 철학자다운 멋을 부리며 제 칼 위로 몸을 던졌지만, 나는 조용히 배에 오른다. 여기에 놀랄 것은 하나도 없다. 알고 보면, 거의 모든 사람이 저마다의 정도에 따라 언젠가 한 번쯤은 바다를 향해 나와 거의 똑같은 감정을 품는 법이다.\n\n자, 여기 섬처럼 떠 있는 그대들의 도시 맨해튼이 있다. 산호초가 인도양의 섬들을 두르듯 부두가 그 둘레를 띠처럼 감싸고 있고, 상업이 제 물결로 도시를 에워싸고 있다. 좌우로 뻗은 거리들은 그대를 물가로 데려간다. 도시의 맨 끝, 가장 남쪽에는 배터리 공원이 있어, 그 고귀한 방파제가 파도에 씻기고, 몇 시간 전만 해도 뭍이 보이지 않는 먼바다에 있던 산들바람에 식혀진다. 거기 바다를 바라보는 사람들의 무리를 보라.\n\n꿈결 같은 안식일 오후에 도시를 한 바퀴 거닐어 보라. 콜리어스 훅에서 코엔티스 슬립까지, 거기서 다시 화이트홀을 지나 북쪽으로 가 보라. 무엇이 보이는가? 말없는 보초처럼 도시 곳곳에 늘어서서, 바다의 몽상에 붙박인 수천수만의 인간들이 서 있다. 어떤 이는 말뚝에 기대어 있고, 어떤 이는 부두 끝머리에 앉아 있으며, 어떤 이는 중국에서 온 배의 뱃전 너머를 굽어보고, 어떤 이는 더 나은 바다 풍경을 엿보려는 듯 삭구 높은 곳에 올라가 있다. 그러나 이들은 모두 뭍사람이다. 평일에는 욋가지와 회벽 안에 갇혀 — 계산대에 묶이고, 의자에 못 박히고, 책상에 들러붙어 지내는 자들이다. 그렇다면 이게 어찌 된 일인가? 푸른 들판은 다 사라졌단 말인가? 이들은 여기서 무엇을 하고 있는가?\n\n그런데 보라! 더 많은 무리가 곧장 물가를 향해 걸어오는데, 마치 물에 뛰어들기라도 할 작정인 듯하다. 이상한 일이다! 뭍의 가장 끝자락이 아니고서는 그 무엇도 이들을 만족시키지 못한다. 저 창고들의 그늘진 바람막이 아래서 어슬렁거리는 것만으로는 충분치 않은 것이다. 안 된다. 이들은 물에 빠지지 않을 한도 내에서 되도록 물에 가까이 다가서야만 한다. 그리하여 거기 서 있다 — 몇 마일에 걸쳐, 몇 리그에 걸쳐. 모두 내륙 사람들이다. 골목과 샛길에서, 거리와 큰길에서 — 북쪽에서, 동쪽에서, 남쪽에서, 서쪽에서 몰려온다. 그런데도 여기서는 모두 하나로 모인다. 말해 보라, 저 모든 배에 달린 나침반 바늘의 자력이 이들을 그리로 끌어당기기라도 한단 말인가?\n\n다시 한번. 그대가 시골에, 호수가 많은 어느 고지대에 있다고 하자. 마음 내키는 대로 거의 아무 길이나 골라 잡아 보라. 십중팔구 그 길은 그대를 골짜기로 내려보내, 시냇물 곁 웅덩이 옆에 두고 갈 것이다. 거기에는 마법이 깃들어 있다. 세상에서 가장 정신이 멍한 사람을 가장 깊은 몽상에 빠뜨려 보라 — 그자를 두 발로 세워 발걸음을 떼게 하면, 그 일대에 물이라는 게 있기만 하다면 그는 어김없이 그대를 물가로 이끌 것이다. 혹시라도 광활한 아메리카 사막에서 목이 마르거든, 그대의 대상(隊商)에 형이상학 교수가 한 명 끼어 있을 경우 이 실험을 해 보라. 그렇다, 누구나 알다시피, 명상과 물은 영원히 맺어진 부부 사이다.\n\n그런데 여기 한 화가가 있다. 그는 사코 계곡을 통틀어 가장 꿈결 같고, 가장 그늘지고, 가장 고요하며, 가장 매혹적인 낭만적 풍경 한 자락을 그려 그대에게 보여 주고자 한다. 그가 쓰는 으뜸가는 요소는 무엇인가? 저기 그의 나무들이 서 있는데, 저마다 줄기 속이 텅 비어 마치 그 안에 은자와 십자가상이라도 들어 있는 듯하다. 그리고 여기 그의 풀밭이 잠들어 있고, 저기 그의 가축들이 잠들어 있으며, 저 멀리 오두막에서는 나른한 연기가 피어오른다. 멀리 깊은 숲속으로는 구불구불한 길이 미로처럼 휘감겨 들어가, 산허리의 푸른 빛에 잠긴 채 겹겹이 포개진 산자락에 가닿는다. 그러나 그림이 이처럼 황홀경에 잠겨 있고, 이 소나무가 이 목동의 머리 위로 잎사귀 같은 한숨을 떨군다 한들, 그 목동의 눈길이 제 앞의 마법 같은 시냇물에 붙박여 있지 않다면 이 모든 것은 헛될 따름이다. 6월의 대초원을 찾아가 보라. 그곳에서 수십, 수백 마일에 걸쳐 무릎까지 차오르는 참나리꽃 사이를 헤치고 나아갈 때 — 모자라는 단 하나의 매력은 무엇인가? 물이다 — 거기엔 물 한 방울 없는 것이다! 나이아가라가 모래 폭포에 지나지 않는다면, 그대는 그것을 보겠다고 천 마일을 여행하겠는가? 테네시의 가난한 시인은 어쩌다 은화 두 줌을 손에 쥐었을 때, 절실히 필요했던 외투를 사 입을지 아니면 그 돈을 록어웨이 해변으로 가는 도보 여행에 쓸지를 두고 어찌하여 망설였는가? 어찌하여 건강하고 다부진 영혼을 지닌 건장한 사내아이라면 거의 누구나 언젠가 한 번쯤은 바다로 나가고 싶어 안달이 나는가? 어찌하여 그대 자신도 승객으로서 첫 항해에 나섰을 때, 이제 그대와 그대의 배가 뭍에서 보이지 않는 곳에 이르렀다는 말을 처음 들었을 때, 그토록 신비로운 떨림을 느꼈는가? 어찌하여 옛 페르시아인들은 바다를 신성하게 여겼는가? 어찌하여 그리스인들은 바다에 따로 신격을 부여하여 제우스의 친형제로 삼았는가? 진실로 이 모든 것에는 뜻이 없지 않다. 그리고 더더욱 깊은 뜻이 나르키소스 이야기 속에 담겨 있으니, 그는 샘물에 비친, 괴롭도록 부드러운 그 영상을 붙잡을 수 없어 그 속으로 뛰어들었다가 빠져 죽고 말았다. 그러나 바로 그 영상을, 우리 자신도 모든 강과 바다 속에서 보고 있는 것이다. 그것은 붙잡을 수 없는 생명의 환영이 빚어낸 영상이며, 이것이야말로 그 모든 것을 푸는 열쇠다.\n\n자, 내가 눈앞이 흐릿해지고 폐가 지나치게 의식되기 시작할 때마다 바다로 나가는 버릇이 있다고 말한다고 해서, 내가 승객으로 바다에 나간다는 뜻으로 받아들여서는 안 된다. 승객으로 가려면 모름지기 지갑이 있어야 하는데, 지갑이란 그 안에 든 것이 없으면 한낱 헝겊 조각에 지나지 않기 때문이다. 게다가 승객은 뱃멀미를 한다 — 신경질을 부리고 — 밤에 잠을 이루지 못하며 — 대체로 그다지 즐거운 시간을 보내지 못한다. 그러니 안 된다, 나는 결코 승객으로는 가지 않는다. 또한 내가 제법 뱃사람티가 난다고 해서, 제독이나 선장이나 요리사로서 바다에 나가는 일도 결코 없다. 그런 직위의 영예와 위엄일랑, 그것을 좋아하는 자들에게 넘겨준다. 나로 말하자면, 명예롭고 점잖다는 온갖 종류의 고역과 시련과 고난을 죄다 질색한다. 배든, 바크든, 브리그든, 스쿠너든 그 무엇이든 돌볼 것 없이, 나 자신 하나 건사하는 것만으로도 내게는 벅찬 일이다. 그리고 요리사로 가는 것으로 말하자면 — 거기에 상당한 영예가 따른다는 것은 인정한다, 요리사란 배 위에서 일종의 사관(士官)이니까 — 그래도 어쩐지 나는 가금류를 굽는 일이 영 내키지 않았다. 물론 일단 잘 구워서, 적절히 버터를 바르고, 사리에 맞게 소금과 후추로 간을 한 구운 새고기라면, 나만큼 그것을 정중하게, 아니 경건하다 할 만큼 떠받들어 말할 사람도 없을 테지만 말이다. 옛 이집트인들이 구운 따오기와 통째로 구운 하마를 우상처럼 떠받들며 애지중지했던 까닭에, 그 거대한 빵 굽는 가마와도 같은 피라미드 속에서 그 짐승들의 미라를 보게 되는 것이다.\n\n아니다, 나는 바다에 나갈 때 그저 평범한 선원으로, 돛대 바로 앞에 서서, 곧장 앞갑판 선실로 내려갔다가, 다시 저 높은 곳 최상부 돛대 꼭대기로 올라간다. 사실 그들은 나를 꽤나 이리저리 부려 먹고, 5월 풀밭의 메뚜기처럼 이 활대에서 저 활대로 뛰어다니게 만든다. 처음에는 이런 일이 영 불쾌하다. 특히 그대가 이 땅의 유서 깊은 명문가 출신, 이를테면 판 렌셀러가, 랜돌프가, 혹은 하르디크누트가 출신이라면, 그것은 자존심을 건드린다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 타르 통에 손을 담그기 바로 전까지 그대가 시골 학교 선생으로 군림하며 키 큰 학생들조차 그대를 두려워하게 만들었던 처지라면 더욱 그러하다. 장담하건대, 학교 선생에서 선원으로 옮겨 가는 그 전환은 뼈저린 것이어서, 그것을 이 악물고 견뎌 내려면 세네카와 스토아 철학자들의 진한 탕약이 필요하다. 그러나 이마저도 시간이 지나면 차츰 가신다.\n\n심술궂은 늙은 선장이 내게 빗자루를 가져다 갑판을 쓸라고 명령한들 그게 무슨 대수란 말인가? 신약성경의 저울에 달아 본다면, 그런 모욕이 대체 얼마나 나간단 말인가? 내가 그 특정한 경우에 그 심술궂은 늙은이에게 선뜻 공손하게 복종한다고 해서, 대천사 가브리엘이 나를 조금이라도 낮추어 본다고 생각하는가? 노예 아닌 자가 누가 있는가? 그것을 내게 말해 보라. 자, 그러니, 늙은 선장들이 나를 아무리 이리저리 부려 먹든 — 나를 아무리 쥐어박고 두들기든, 나는 그것이 다 괜찮다는 것을, 다른 모든 사람도 이런저런 방식으로 거의 똑같은 대접을 받고 있다는 것을 — 그러니까 육체적 관점에서든 형이상학적 관점에서든 말이다 — 아는 데서 오는 만족을 누린다. 그렇게 보편적인 쥐어박음이 차례로 돌고 도는 것이니, 모두가 서로의 어깻죽지를 문질러 주며 만족할 일이다.\n\n또한 나는 늘 선원으로 바다에 나가는데, 이는 그들이 내 수고에 대해 어김없이 삯을 치러 주기 때문이다. 반면에 승객에게는 단 한 푼이라도 치러 준다는 말을 나는 들어 본 적이 없다. 오히려 승객은 제 손으로 돈을 내야 한다. 그리고 돈을 내는 것과 돈을 받는 것 사이에는 천양지차가 있다. 돈을 내는 행위란, 그 두 과수원 도둑이 우리에게 물려준 가장 거북한 형벌일 것이다. 그러나 돈을 받는 것은 — 무엇이 이에 견줄 수 있겠는가? 사람이 돈을 받을 때 보이는 그 점잖은 활기란 참으로 놀라운 것인데, 우리가 돈이야말로 이 세상 모든 악의 뿌리라고 그토록 진지하게 믿고, 돈 많은 자는 결코 천국에 들어갈 수 없다고 믿는 것을 생각하면 더욱 그렇다. 아! 우리는 어쩌면 그토록 즐거이 우리 자신을 파멸에 내맡기는가!\n\n끝으로, 나는 늘 선원으로 바다에 나가는데, 이는 앞갑판에서 누리는 건강한 운동과 맑은 공기 때문이다. 이 세상에서는 고물에서 불어오는 바람보다 맞바람이 훨씬 잦은 법이므로 (다시 말해, 그대가 피타고라스의 금언을 결코 어기지 않는다면 말이다), 후갑판의 제독은 대개 앞갑판 선원들에게서 한 번 걸러진 공기를 얻어 마시는 셈이다. 그는 자기가 그 공기를 맨 먼저 들이마신다고 여기지만, 실은 그렇지 않다. 이와 거의 같은 방식으로, 평민들은 다른 여러 일에서도 자기네 지도자를 이끌고 있건만, 정작 지도자들은 그것을 좀처럼 눈치채지 못한다. 그러나 어찌하여, 상선 선원으로 여러 차례 바다 냄새를 맡고 난 내가 이제 와서 고래잡이 항해에 나설 생각을 머릿속에 품게 되었는가 — 이것은 늘 나를 감시하고, 은밀히 내 뒤를 밟으며, 무어라 설명할 수 없는 방식으로 나를 좌우하는 저 운명의 보이지 않는 경찰관이라야 그 누구보다도 잘 답할 수 있을 것이다. 그리고 의심할 바 없이, 내가 이 고래잡이 항해에 나선 것은 아주 오래전에 짜인 저 섭리의 거창한 공연 차례표 가운데 한 부분이었다. 그것은 더 방대한 공연들 사이에 끼인 일종의 짧은 막간극이자 독주(獨奏)로서 끼어든 셈이다. 짐작건대 그 공연 차례표의 이 대목은 대략 다음과 같이 적혀 있었으리라.\n\n“미합중국 대통령직을 둘러싼 거대한 경합 선거.\n“어느 이슈메일이라는 자의 고래잡이 항해. “아프가니스탄에서의 피비린내 나는 전투.”\n\n저 무대 감독들, 곧 운명의 여신들이 어찌하여 다른 이들에게는 장엄한 비극의 화려한 배역을, 점잖은 희극의 짧고 수월한 배역을, 익살극의 흥겨운 배역을 맡겨 두고서, 하필 내게는 이 초라한 고래잡이 항해 배역을 정해 주었는지 — 그 까닭을 나는 정확히 말할 수 없다. 그렇긴 해도, 이제 모든 정황을 돌이켜 보면, 나는 그 동기와 동력의 속내를 조금쯤은 들여다볼 수 있을 듯하다. 그것들은 갖가지 변장을 하고 교묘하게 내 앞에 나타나, 내가 그 배역을 맡아 연기하도록 부추겼고, 게다가 그것이 어떠한 치우침도 없는 나 자신의 자유의지와 분별 있는 판단에서 비롯된 선택이라는 착각에 빠지도록 나를 구슬렸던 것이다.\n\n이 동기들 가운데 으뜸가는 것은 저 거대한 고래 자체에 대한 압도적인 상념이었다. 그토록 불길하고 신비로운 괴물이 내 모든 호기심을 들쑤셨다. 그리고 그놈이 섬덩이 같은 제 몸을 굴리며 노니는 거칠고 머나먼 바다들, 도무지 벗어날 길 없는 이름 모를 고래의 위험들 — 이 모든 것이, 천 가지 파타고니아의 풍경과 소리가 자아내는 온갖 경이로움과 더불어, 내 소망 쪽으로 나를 기울게 했다. 다른 사람들에게라면 어쩌면 그런 것들이 유인이 되지 못했을지도 모른다. 그러나 나로 말하자면, 머나먼 것들을 향한 그칠 줄 모르는 갈망에 시달리는 사람이다. 나는 금단의 바다를 항해하고, 야만의 해안에 상륙하기를 좋아한다. 좋은 것을 외면하지는 않으면서도, 나는 공포를 재빨리 알아차리며, 또한 그것과 더불어 어울려 지낼 수도 있다 — 그것이 허락만 해 준다면 말이다 — 자기가 묵는 곳의 모든 식구와 사이좋게 지내는 것이야말로 좋은 일이니까.\n\n그리하여 이런 까닭들로 해서, 고래잡이 항해는 반가운 것이었다. 경이로운 세계의 거대한 수문이 활짝 열렸고, 나를 그 뜻으로 기울게 한 거친 공상들 속에서, 끝없이 이어지는 고래들의 행렬이 둘씩 둘씩 짝지어 내 영혼 가장 깊은 곳으로 떠밀려 들어왔으며, 그 모든 것의 한복판에는 허공에 솟은 눈 덮인 언덕과도 같은, 두건을 쓴 거대한 환영 하나가 떠 있었다.\n\n# 제3권(12절판) 제2장(알제리 돌고래). 해적이다.\n\n셔츠 한두 장을 낡은 융단 가방에 쑤셔 넣고는 그것을 옆구리에 낀 채, 나는 케이프 혼과 태평양을 향해 길을 나섰다. 유서 깊은 옛 도시 맨해튼을 떠나, 나는 무사히 뉴베드퍼드(New Bedford)에 닿았다. 때는 십이월의 어느 토요일 밤이었다. 낸터킷(Nantucket)으로 가는 작은 우편선이 이미 떠나 버렸고, 다음 월요일이 되기 전에는 그곳에 닿을 방도가 전혀 없다는 사실을 알게 되었을 때, 나는 적잖이 실망했다.\n\n고래잡이의 고통과 형벌을 자청하는 젊은 지원자들 대부분이 바로 이 뉴베드퍼드에 머물렀다가 그곳에서 항해를 떠나는 까닭에, 나로 말하자면 그럴 생각이 전혀 없었음을 여기서 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠다. 내 마음은 오로지 낸터킷의 배를 타고 떠나리라는 쪽으로 굳어 있었으니, 그 유명한 옛 섬에 얽힌 모든 것에는 어딘가 멋지고 떠들썩한 무언가가 있어 나를 놀랍도록 흡족하게 했기 때문이다. 게다가 뉴베드퍼드가 근래에 들어 고래잡이 사업을 차츰 독차지하고 있고, 이 일에 있어 가련한 옛 낸터킷이 이제 그 도시에 한참 뒤처져 있다 한들, 낸터킷은 그 도시의 위대한 원조였다 — 이 카르타고의 티레였던 것이다. 곧 최초의 죽은 아메리카 고래가 떠밀려 와 좌초한 곳이 바로 낸터킷이었다. 저 토착 고래잡이들, 곧 붉은 피부의 사람들이 카누를 타고 처음으로 리바이어던(Leviathan)을 쫓아 나선 곳이 낸터킷 아니고 또 어디였단 말인가? 그리고 — 이야기에 따르면 — 고래가 뱃머리 작살을 던질 만큼 가까이 왔는지 알아보려고 던질 요량으로 수입한 자갈을 절반쯤 싣고서 처음으로 모험에 나선 그 작은 외돛배가 출항한 곳 또한 낸터킷 아니고 어디였겠는가?\n\n이제 정해진 항구를 향해 떠날 수 있기까지 뉴베드퍼드에서 하룻밤과 하루, 그리고 또 하룻밤을 앞에 두고 보니, 그동안 어디서 먹고 잘 것인가 하는 문제가 신경 쓰이기 시작했다. 매우 미덥지 못해 보이는, 아니 매우 어둡고 음산한 밤이었으며, 살을 에듯 춥고 쓸쓸했다. 나는 그곳에 아는 사람이 하나도 없었다. 불안한 갈고리를 드리워 내 호주머니의 깊이를 재어 보았으나, 건져 올린 것이라곤 은화 몇 닢뿐이었다 — 그러니, 이스마엘, 어디로 가든, 황량한 거리 한복판에 서서 가방을 어깨에 둘러멘 채 북쪽의 어둠과 남쪽의 어둠을 견주어 보며 나는 스스로에게 말했다 — 사랑하는 이스마엘이여, 그대의 지혜로 어디서 하룻밤 묵기로 결정하든, 부디 값부터 물어보고, 너무 까다롭게 굴지는 말지어다.\n\n머뭇거리는 걸음으로 거리를 거닐다가 \"엇갈린 작살\" 간판을 지났는데 — 그곳은 너무 값비싸고 흥겨워 보였다. 좀 더 가니 \"황새치 여관\"의 환한 붉은 창문에서 어찌나 뜨거운 빛줄기가 쏟아져 나왔던지, 집 앞에 다져진 눈과 얼음마저 녹여 버린 듯했다. 다른 곳은 어디나 얼어붙은 서리가 단단한 아스팔트 포장처럼 열 인치 두께로 깔려 있었으니 — 내겐 다소 고된 노릇이었다. 부싯돌처럼 솟은 모서리에 발을 부딪칠 때마다, 모질고 가차 없는 혹사 탓에 내 장화 밑창이 더없이 비참한 꼴이 되어 있었던 까닭이다. 또 너무 값비싸고 흥겹군, 나는 다시 생각하며 잠시 걸음을 멈추고 거리에 넘실대는 환한 불빛을 바라보고 안에서 잔이 부딪치며 쟁그랑거리는 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 하지만 가자, 이스마엘, 나는 마침내 말했다. 들리지 않느냐? 문 앞에서 비켜라. 그대의 누덕누덕한 장화가 길을 막고 있다. 그래서 나는 다시 걸음을 옮겼다. 이제 나는 본능에 이끌려 바다 쪽으로 나를 데려가는 거리를 따라갔으니, 가장 흥겨운 여관은 아닐지언정 가장 값싼 여관이 그곳에 있으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었기 때문이다.\n\n이 얼마나 황량한 거리인가! 양옆으로는 집이 아니라 시커먼 어둠의 덩어리뿐이고, 여기저기 촛불 하나가, 마치 무덤 속을 떠도는 촛불처럼 보였다. 한 주의 마지막 날, 이 야심한 시각에 그 동네는 거의 인적이 끊겨 있었다. 그러나 이윽고 나는 나지막하고 널찍한 건물에서 새어 나오는 자욱한 불빛에 이르렀는데, 그 문은 어서 들어오라는 듯 활짝 열려 있었다. 아무렇게나 내버려 둔 듯한 모양새가 마치 누구든 드나들라고 마련된 곳 같았다. 그래서 안으로 들어서며 내가 맨 처음 한 일은 현관에 놓인 잿더미 상자에 걸려 비틀거린 것이었다. 하! 나는 생각했다, 하, 흩날리는 재가 거의 숨통을 막을 지경이 되자, 이것은 저 멸망한 도시 고모라의 재란 말인가? 그러나 \"엇갈린 작살\"과 \"황새치\"라니 — 그렇다면 이곳은 분명 \"덫\"이라는 간판을 단 곳이렷다. 그래도 나는 몸을 추스르고는 안에서 우렁찬 목소리가 들려오기에, 안쪽으로 밀고 들어가 두 번째 안문을 열었다.\n\n마치 토페트에 자리한 위대한 흑인 의회 같았다. 백 개의 검은 얼굴이 줄지어 앉은 채 일제히 고개를 돌려 빤히 쳐다보았고, 그 너머에서는 검은 운명의 천사가 강단에서 책을 두드리고 있었다. 그곳은 흑인 교회였으니, 설교자의 본문은 어둠의 캄캄함과, 그곳에서의 울부짖음과 통곡과 이 가는 소리에 관한 것이었다. 하, 이스마엘, 나는 뒷걸음질로 빠져나오며 중얼거렸다. \"덫\"이라는 간판에 걸맞은 참담한 환대로구나!\n\n다시 걸음을 옮겨, 나는 마침내 부두에서 멀지 않은 곳에서 희미한 빛 같은 것을 만났고, 허공에서 처량하게 삐걱대는 소리를 들었다. 위를 올려다보니 문 위에 흔들리는 간판이 걸려 있었는데, 그 위에는 흰 그림이 그려져 있어 자욱한 물보라가 길고 곧게 솟구치는 모양을 어렴풋이 나타내고 있었고, 그 아래에는 이런 글귀가 있었다 — \"물뿜이 여관: 피터 코핀.\"\n\n코핀(Coffin)? — 물뿜이라고? — 이런 특정한 맥락에서는 다소 불길하군, 나는 생각했다. 하지만 그것이 낸터킷에서는 흔한 이름이라고들 하니, 여기 이 피터라는 자도 그곳에서 온 이주민이려니 싶었다. 불빛이 어찌나 흐릿한지, 그리고 그곳이 적어도 그때만큼은 제법 조용해 보였고, 그 다 허물어져 가는 작은 목조 집 자체가 마치 어느 불탄 동네의 잔해에서 수레로 실어다 놓은 듯한 데다, 흔들리는 간판이 가난에 찌든 듯 삐걱대고 있었기에, 나는 여기야말로 값싼 숙소와 둘도 없는 완두콩 커피를 얻을 바로 그 자리라고 생각했다.\n\n그곳은 기묘한 곳이었다 — 박공지붕을 인 낡은 집으로, 한쪽 면이 마치 중풍에 걸린 듯 슬프게 기울어져 있었다. 그것은 매섭고 황량한 모퉁이에 서 있었는데, 그곳에서는 저 사나운 바람 유로클리돈이, 일찍이 가련한 바울의 요동치던 배를 둘러싸고 울부짖던 때보다 더 거세게 울부짖고 있었다. 그렇지만 유로클리돈도 집 안에서 두 발을 난로 선반에 올려놓고 잠자리에 들 채비로 한가로이 몸을 덥히는 사람에게는 더없이 기분 좋은 산들바람이다. \"유로클리돈이라 불리는 저 사나운 바람을 헤아릴진대\" 하고 어느 옛 저술가는 — 현존하는 그의 저작이라곤 내가 가진 단 한 부뿐이거니와 — 이렇게 말한다. \"그것은 참으로 놀라운 차이를 빚어내나니, 그대가 바깥에만 온통 서리가 낀 유리창에서 그것을 내다보느냐, 아니면 양쪽 다 서리가 끼고 죽음이라는 자가 유일한 유리장이 노릇을 하는 저 창틀 없는 창에서 그것을 바라보느냐에 따라 그러하니라.\" 참으로 옳도다, 이 구절이 내 머릿속에 떠오를 때 나는 생각했다 — 늙은 고딕 활자여, 그대의 논변이 훌륭하구나. 그렇다, 이 두 눈은 창이요, 이 내 몸뚱이는 집이로다. 그래도 그들이 틈새와 구멍을 막지 않고, 여기저기 솜털 한 줌이라도 쑤셔 넣지 않은 것은 참으로 안타까운 노릇이다. 하지만 이제 와서 무엇을 고치기란 너무 늦었다. 우주는 이미 완성되었으니, 갓돌은 얹혔고 부스러기들은 백만 년 전에 수레로 실려 나가 버렸다. 저기 가련한 나사로는, 베개 삼은 연석에 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 떨고, 떨리는 몸으로 누더기를 들썩이고 있으니, 그는 두 귀를 헝겊으로 틀어막고 옥수수 속대를 입에 물어 보아도, 그 사나운 유로클리돈을 막아 내지는 못하리라. 유로클리돈이라! 붉은 비단 가운을 걸친 늙은 디베스가 말한다 — (훗날 그는 더 붉은 가운을 걸쳤다) 쳇, 쳇! 어찌나 멋진 서리 내린 밤인가. 오리온은 어찌나 반짝이며, 저 북극광은 또 어떠한가! 영원토록 온실로 가득한 동방의 여름 기후를 떠들고 싶거든 떠들라지. 내게는 내 석탄으로 내 여름을 손수 빚어내는 특권을 다오.\n\n그러나 나사로는 무엇을 생각할까? 그가 푸르뎅뎅한 두 손을 저 장엄한 북극광에 치켜든다고 그 손이 따뜻해질까? 나사로는 여기보다 차라리 수마트라에 있고 싶지 않을까? 그는 차라리 적도의 선을 따라 몸을 죽 뻗고 눕고 싶지 않을까. 그렇다, 오 신들이시여! 이 서리를 막아 내기 위해서라면 차라리 불구덩이 그 자체로 내려가고 싶지 않을까?\n\n그런데 나사로가 디베스의 문 앞 연석 위에 좌초한 듯 누워 있다는 것은, 빙산 하나가 몰루카 제도의 어느 섬에 닻을 내리고 매여 있다는 것보다 더 놀라운 일이다. 그렇지만 디베스 그 자신 또한 얼어붙은 한숨으로 지어 올린 얼음 궁전 안에서 차르처럼 살고 있으며, 금주회의 회장인 까닭에 그가 마시는 것이라곤 고아들의 미적지근한 눈물뿐이다.\n\n하지만 이런 눈물 짜는 이야기는 이제 그만하자, 우리는 고래잡이를 떠나려는 참이고, 그런 이야기는 앞으로도 얼마든지 나올 테니까. 서리 낀 우리 발에서 얼음이나 긁어내고, 이 \"물뿜이\"라는 곳이 도대체 어떤 곳인지나 살펴보자.\n\n# CHAPTER 3. The Spouter-Inn.\n\n그 박공지붕의 물보라 여관(Spouter-Inn)으로 들어서면, 폭이 넓고 천장이 낮은 데다 어수선하게 뻗은 현관이 나타났는데, 구식 징두리 판벽으로 둘러져 있어 어딘가 폐선 선고를 받은 낡은 배의 뱃전이 떠오르게 했다. 한쪽 벽에는 매우 커다란 유화 한 점이 걸려 있었는데, 어찌나 그을음으로 뒤덮이고 사방이 훼손되었던지, 고르지 못한 빛이 엇갈려 비치는 가운데서 그것을 들여다보자면, 부지런히 살펴보고 체계적으로 여러 차례 찾아가 보며 이웃들에게 꼼꼼히 물어본 뒤에야 비로소 그 그림이 무엇을 그린 것인지 어렴풋이나마 이해에 다다를 수 있었다. 도무지 설명할 수 없는 음영과 그림자 덩어리들이 뒤엉켜 있어, 처음에는 뉴잉글랜드(New England)의 마녀 시대에 어느 야심 찬 젊은 화가가 마법에 걸린 혼돈을 그려 보려 애쓴 것이 아닐까 하는 생각마저 들 정도였다. 그러나 거듭 진지하게 응시하고 몇 번이고 곰곰이 헤아린 끝에, 특히 현관 안쪽으로 난 작은 창을 활짝 열어젖힌 뒤에는, 그런 발상이 아무리 황당하다 한들 전혀 근거 없는 것만은 아닐지도 모른다는 결론에 이르게 되었다.\n\n그러나 무엇보다도 사람을 어리둥절하게 하고 혼란에 빠뜨린 것은, 그림 한가운데에 어른거리는 길고 흐느적거리며 불길한 검은 덩어리였으니, 그것은 이름 모를 효모 같은 거품 속에 떠 있는 푸르고 흐릿한 세 개의 수직선 위로 드리워져 있었다. 그것은 참으로 질척하고 축축하며 흐물거리는 그림이어서, 신경이 예민한 사람이라면 정신을 놓을 만했다. 그럼에도 거기에는 어딘가 막연하고 절반쯤만 잡힐 듯한, 상상조차 할 수 없는 숭고함이 깃들어 있어, 그것을 보는 이를 그 자리에 얼어붙게 만들었고, 마침내 저 경이로운 그림이 대체 무엇을 뜻하는지 기어이 알아내고야 말겠다고 자신도 모르게 맹세하게 만들었다. 이따금 밝은, 그러나 아아, 허망하게도 빗나간 생각이 번개처럼 머릿속을 스쳤다. — 한밤중 폭풍 속의 흑해(Black Sea)로구나. — 네 가지 원초적 원소들의 부자연스러운 싸움이로구나. — 벼락 맞은 황야로구나. — 극북(極北)의 겨울 풍경이로구나. — 얼음에 갇혀 있던 시간의 강이 풀리며 깨어지는 광경이로구나. 그러나 마침내 이 모든 공상은 그림 한복판의 그 불길한 무언가에 자리를 내주고 말았다. 일단 그것의 정체를 알아내고 나면 나머지는 모두 분명해질 터였다. 그러나 잠깐 — 저것은 거대한 물고기와 어렴풋이 닮지 않았는가? 다름 아닌 저 위대한 리바이어던(Leviathan) 그 자체와?\n\n사실 그 화가의 의도는 이러했던 것 같다. 이는 내가 이 주제를 두고 이야기를 나눈 여러 나이 든 사람들의 의견을 한데 모아, 부분적으로 거기에 근거를 두고 세운 나만의 최종적인 견해다. 그 그림은 거대한 허리케인을 만난 케이프 혼(Cape Horn) 항해선을 그린 것이었다. 반쯤 가라앉은 배가 그 자리에서 뒤척이며, 장비를 다 잃은 세 개의 돛대만이 겨우 보일 뿐이었다. 그리고 격분한 고래(Whale) 한 마리가 그 배를 단숨에 뛰어넘으려다가, 세 돛대 꼭대기에 제 몸을 꿰뚫는 그 어마어마한 동작의 한가운데에 있었다.\n\n이 현관의 맞은편 벽에는 괴물 같은 곤봉과 창들이 이교적인 모양새로 빼곡히 걸려 있었다. 어떤 것들은 상아 톱을 닮은 반짝이는 이빨들이 촘촘히 박혀 있었고, 어떤 것들은 사람의 머리카락 다발이 술처럼 매달려 있었으며, 또 하나는 낫 모양으로, 팔이 긴 풀 베는 사람이 갓 벤 풀밭에 그려 내는 호선(弧線)처럼 빙 돌아가는 커다란 자루가 달려 있었다. 그것을 응시하노라면 몸서리가 쳐졌고, 대체 어떤 괴물 같은 식인종과 야만인이 이토록 끔찍하게 베어 대는 무시무시한 도구로 죽음의 수확에 나섰을까 궁금해졌다. 이것들 사이에는 녹슨 옛 포경용 작은창과 작살들이 죄다 부러지고 일그러진 채 섞여 있었다. 그중 어떤 것들은 사연이 깃든 무기였다. 지금은 마구 꺾인 이 한때 길었던 작은창으로, 오십 년 전에 네이선 스웨인이 해돋이부터 해넘이 사이에 고래 열다섯 마리를 잡았다. 그리고 저 작살은 — 지금은 코르크 마개뽑이를 닮았지만 — 자바해(Java seas)에서 던져졌다가 어느 고래에게 박힌 채 끌려갔고, 여러 해 뒤에 블랑코곶(Cape of Blanco) 앞바다에서 그 고래가 잡혔다. 처음에 작살이 꽂힌 곳은 꼬리 근처였는데, 마치 사람의 몸속을 쉬지 않고 떠도는 바늘처럼 꼬박 사십 피트(약 12미터)를 옮겨 다니다가, 마침내 등의 혹 속에 박혀 있는 것이 발견되었다.\n\n이 어둑한 현관을 가로질러, 저 낮은 아치형 통로 — 옛날에는 둘레마다 벽난로가 딸린 거대한 중앙 굴뚝이었을 곳을 뚫어 만든 길 — 를 지나면 공용실로 들어서게 된다. 이곳은 한층 더 어둑한 곳으로, 위로는 묵직하고 낮은 들보들이, 아래로는 오래되어 주름진 널빤지들이 깔려 있어, 어느 낡은 배의 조타실을 밟고 있는 듯한 착각이 들 지경이었으니, 특히나 이렇게 바람이 울부짖는 밤, 한 귀퉁이에 닻을 내린 이 낡은 방주가 그토록 사납게 흔들릴 때면 더욱 그러했다. 한쪽에는 길고 낮은 선반 같은 탁자가 놓여 있었는데, 그 위에는 금이 간 유리 진열장들이 덮여 있었고, 그 안에는 이 넓은 세상의 가장 외진 구석구석에서 모아 온 먼지투성이 진귀품들이 가득 차 있었다. 방의 저쪽 모퉁이에서 튀어나온 곳에는 어둑한 굴 같은 곳 — 바로 술청이 — 서 있었는데, 참고래의 머리를 어설프게 흉내 낸 것이었다. 어찌 됐든 거기에는 고래 턱뼈의 거대한 아치가 서 있었으니, 어찌나 넓던지 마차 한 대가 그 밑으로 지나갈 수 있을 듯했다. 그 안에는 허름한 선반들이 있어, 둘레로 낡은 유리병과 병들과 술병들이 늘어서 있었다. 그리고 그 신속한 파멸의 턱 속에서, 또 다른 저주받은 요나(Jonah)처럼 (실제로 사람들은 그를 그 이름으로 불렀다), 자그마하고 쪼글쪼글한 늙은이가 부산을 떨며, 선원들에게서 돈을 받고 광란과 죽음을 비싸게 팔아넘기고 있었다.\n\n그가 독약을 따라 붓는 술잔들이란 가증스럽기 짝이 없었다. 겉으로는 영락없는 원통 모양이지만 — 안쪽으로는, 그 음흉한 초록빛 둥근 유리잔들이 교활하게도 아래로 갈수록 좁아져 사람을 속이는 바닥으로 이어졌다. 유리잔에는 자오선처럼 가지런한 눈금들이 거칠게 새겨져 이 노상강도들의 술잔을 빙 둘러쌌다. 이 눈금까지 채우면 값은 고작 한 푼, 저 눈금까지면 한 푼 더, 그렇게 가다가 잔을 가득 채우면 — 그것이 바로 케이프 혼 잔으로, 한 실링이면 단숨에 들이켤 수 있었다.\n\n그곳에 들어서니 젊은 선원 여럿이 탁자 둘레에 모여 어슴푸레한 불빛 아래 여러 가지 고래 뼈 세공품(skrimshander)을 들여다보고 있었다. 나는 여관 주인을 찾아 방 하나를 묵게 해 달라고 청했더니, 집이 꽉 차서 빈 침대가 하나도 없다는 대답이 돌아왔다. \"하지만 잠깐만,\" 그가 이마를 톡톡 두드리며 덧붙였다. \"작살잡이하고 담요를 나눠 쓰는 건 마다하지 않으시겠죠, 안 그렇소? 보아하니 고래잡이를 나서실 모양인데, 그런 일에 익숙해지는 게 좋을 거요.\"\n\n나는 결코 한 침대에서 둘이 자는 것을 좋아한 적이 없으며, 설령 그런 일을 하게 된다 해도 그건 그 작살잡이가 어떤 사람이냐에 달린 일이고, 만약 그(여관 주인)에게 정말로 나를 위한 다른 자리가 없고, 그 작살잡이가 딱히 거슬리는 자가 아니라면, 이토록 매서운 밤에 낯선 도시를 더 헤매고 다니느니 차라리 멀쩡한 사람의 담요 절반을 나눠 쓰는 편을 택하겠노라고 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았소. 좋소. 자리에 앉으시오. 저녁? — 저녁을 드시겠소? 저녁은 곧 준비될 거요.\"\n\n나는 배터리 공원의 벤치처럼 온통 조각이 새겨진 낡은 나무 의자에 앉았다. 한쪽 끝에서는 무언가 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 늙은 뱃사람 하나가 주머니칼로 그 의자를 한층 더 꾸미고 있었는데, 몸을 잔뜩 수그린 채 두 다리 사이의 공간을 부지런히 깎아 내고 있었다. 그는 돛을 한껏 펼친 배 한 척을 새겨 보려 애쓰는 중이었지만, 내가 보기에 별 진척은 없는 듯했다.\n\n마침내 우리 중 너덧 명이 옆방으로 식사를 하라는 부름을 받았다. 그곳은 아이슬란드처럼 추웠고 — 불이라곤 전혀 없었다 — 여관 주인은 그럴 형편이 못 된다고 했다. 음울한 수지 양초 두 자루가 각기 수의(壽衣) 같은 촛농에 싸여 있을 뿐이었다. 우리는 하는 수 없이 두꺼운 선원용 재킷의 단추를 잠그고, 반쯤 얼어붙은 손가락으로 펄펄 끓는 차 잔을 입술에 가져다 댔다. 그러나 음식만큼은 더없이 푸짐한 종류였으니 — 고기와 감자뿐 아니라 경단까지 있었다. 맙소사! 저녁상에 경단이라니! 초록빛 박스코트를 걸친 한 젊은이가 그 경단을 더없이 무시무시한 기세로 공략하기 시작했다.\n\n\"이보게, 젊은이,\" 여관 주인이 말했다. \"자넨 틀림없이 가위에 눌릴 걸세, 그건 확실하네.\"\n\n\"주인장,\" 내가 속삭였다. \"저 친구가 그 작살잡이는 아니죠?\"\n\n\"아, 아니오,\" 그가 어딘가 악마처럼 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"그 작살잡이는 피부가 가무잡잡한 친구요. 그자는 경단 따위는 절대 먹지 않소. 절대로 — 그자는 오로지 스테이크만 먹고, 그것도 설익은 걸 좋아하지.\"\n\n\"그것 참 별난 친구로군,\" 내가 말했다. \"그 작살잡이는 어디 있소? 여기 있소?\"\n\n\"머지않아 올 거요,\" 하는 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n나로서는 어쩔 수 없이, 이 \"가무잡잡한\" 작살잡이에 대해 점점 의심이 들기 시작했다. 어찌 됐든, 만약 우리가 함께 자게 되는 상황이 벌어진다면 그자가 나보다 먼저 옷을 벗고 침대에 들어가야 한다고 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나자 사람들은 술청으로 돌아갔고, 나는 달리 무엇을 해야 할지 몰라 그날 저녁의 나머지를 구경꾼으로 보내기로 작정했다.\n\n이윽고 밖에서 떠들썩한 소리가 들려왔다. 여관 주인은 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"저건 그램퍼스호 선원들이로군. 오늘 아침에 앞바다에 그 배가 들어왔다는 소식을 들었지. 삼 년짜리 항해에다 배가 가득 찼다더군. 야, 이보게들, 이제 피지(Feegees)의 최신 소식을 들을 수 있겠어.\"\n\n현관에서 바다 장화 소리가 쿵쿵 울렸다. 문이 벌컥 열리더니, 더없이 거친 한 무리의 뱃사람들이 우르르 굴러들어 왔다. 텁수룩한 당직용 외투를 두르고, 머리에는 온통 기워지고 너덜너덜한 모직 목도리를 칭칭 감았으며, 수염은 고드름으로 뻣뻣하게 굳은 그들은, 마치 래브라도에서 쏟아져 나온 곰 떼 같았다. 그들은 방금 보트에서 내린 참이었고, 이곳이 그들이 처음 들어선 집이었다. 그러니 그들이 곧장 고래의 입 — 술청 — 을 향해 항로를 잡은 것도 놀랄 일이 아니었으니, 거기서 일을 맡아보던 쪼글쪼글한 작은 늙은이 요나가 이내 그들 모두에게 잔이 넘치도록 술을 따라 주었다. 한 사람이 머리에 지독한 감기가 들었다고 투덜대자, 요나는 그에게 진과 당밀을 섞은 역청 같은 물약을 만들어 주면서, 그것이 아무리 오래된 것이든, 래브라도 해안에서 걸렸든 빙산의 바람받이 쪽에서 걸렸든, 온갖 감기와 카타르에 듣는 만병통치약이라고 장담했다.\n\n술은 곧 그들의 머리로 올라갔으니, 바다에서 갓 내린 지독한 술꾼들조차 대개 그러하듯이, 그들은 더없이 요란스럽게 까불며 날뛰기 시작했다.\n\n그러나 나는 그들 가운데 한 사람이 어쩐지 떨어져 있는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그는 제 진지한 얼굴 때문에 동료들의 흥을 깨고 싶지 않은 듯했지만, 전체적으로는 나머지 사람들만큼 떠들썩하게 굴기를 삼가고 있었다. 이 사람은 단번에 내 흥미를 끌었다. 그리고 바다의 신들이 그가 곧 나의 뱃동무가 되도록 정해 놓았으므로 (이 이야기에 관한 한 그저 잠든 동업자에 지나지 않지만), 나는 여기서 그를 조금 묘사해 보고자 한다. 그는 키가 족히 육 피트(약 1.8미터)는 되었고, 어깨가 떡 벌어졌으며, 가슴은 방죽처럼 단단했다. 나는 그토록 우람한 근육을 가진 사람을 좀처럼 본 적이 없었다. 그의 얼굴은 깊이 그을려 거뭇했는데, 그 대비로 하얀 이가 눈부시게 빛났다. 한편 그의 눈 깊은 그림자 속에는 그에게 그다지 기쁨을 주지 않는 듯한 어떤 추억들이 떠다니고 있었다. 그의 목소리는 단번에 그가 남부 사람임을 알려 주었고, 그 훤칠한 체격으로 미루어 나는 그가 버지니아의 앨러게니 산맥에서 온 키 큰 산사람들 가운데 하나임에 틀림없다고 생각했다. 동료들의 흥청거림이 절정에 달했을 때, 이 사람은 아무도 모르게 슬며시 빠져나갔고, 나는 그가 바다에서 나의 동료가 되기 전까지는 그를 다시 보지 못했다. 그러나 몇 분 지나지 않아 동료들은 그가 사라진 것을 알아챘고, 무슨 까닭에선지 그를 무척이나 좋아했던 모양인지, \"벌킹턴! 벌킹턴! 벌킹턴은 어디 갔어?\" 하고 외쳐 대더니 그를 찾아 집 밖으로 우르르 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n이제 시각은 아홉 시쯤 되었고, 그 흥청거림이 지나간 뒤 방 안은 거의 초자연적일 만큼 고요해진 듯했다. 나는 선원들이 들어오기 바로 직전에 떠올랐던 자그마한 계획을 두고 스스로 흐뭇해하기 시작했다.\n\n한 침대에서 둘이 자는 것을 좋아할 사람은 아무도 없다. 사실 친형제와도 함께 자지 않는 편을 훨씬 더 바랄 것이다. 어찌 된 영문인지는 모르겠으나, 사람들은 잠을 잘 때만큼은 혼자이고 싶어 한다. 더구나 낯선 여관, 낯선 도시에서, 알지도 못하는 낯선 자와 함께 자야 한다고 하면, 게다가 그 낯선 자가 작살잡이라면, 거부감은 한도 끝도 없이 불어난다. 게다가 선원인 나라고 해서 다른 누구보다 더 한 침대에서 둘이 자야 할 까닭은 이 세상 어디에도 없었다. 바다에서 선원들이 한 침대에서 둘이 자는 일은, 뭍에서 독신 왕들이 그러는 것만큼이나 없는 일이니까. 물론 그들은 모두 한 선실에서 함께 잠을 자지만, 저마다 제 해먹을 갖고 있고, 제 담요로 몸을 덮고, 제 살갗 속에서 잠을 잔다.\n\n그 작살잡이에 대해 곰곰이 생각하면 할수록, 그자와 함께 잔다는 생각이 점점 더 끔찍하게 여겨졌다. 작살잡이인 만큼, 그의 속옷이 무명이든 모직이든 간에 그것이 가장 깨끗하지는 않으리라고, 하물며 가장 고급은 결코 아니리라고 짐작하는 것이 타당했다. 나는 온몸이 근질거리기 시작했다. 게다가 시간도 점점 늦어졌으니, 멀쩡한 작살잡이라면 마땅히 집에 돌아와 잠자리에 들 시각이었다. 그런데 만약 그자가 한밤중에 내 위로 굴러들어 온다면 — 그자가 대체 어떤 더러운 소굴에서 오는 길인지 내가 무슨 수로 알겠는가?\n\n\"주인장! 그 작살잡이에 대해 마음을 바꿨소. — 그자하고는 자지 않겠소. 여기 이 벤치에서 해 보겠소.\"\n\n\"좋으실 대로 하시오. 매트리스 삼을 식탁보 한 장 내드리지 못해 미안하구려. 그리고 여기 이 판자는 지긋지긋하게 거칠다오.\" 그러면서 그는 옹이와 흠집들을 더듬어 보았다. \"하지만 잠깐만 기다려 보쇼, 뼈 세공쟁이 양반. 술청에 목수용 대패가 하나 있으니 — 기다리라니까, 내 아주 편안하게 만들어 드리리다.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 대패를 가져왔다. 그러고는 낡은 비단 손수건으로 먼저 벤치의 먼지를 턴 다음, 원숭이처럼 히죽거리며 내 잠자리를 힘차게 대패질하기 시작했다. 대팻밥이 좌우로 날렸고, 마침내 대팻날이 도무지 깎이지 않는 옹이에 쾅 부딪혔다. 여관 주인이 손목을 삘 지경이 되자, 나는 제발 그만두라고, 침대는 내게 충분히 부드러우며, 세상의 그 어떤 대패질로도 소나무 널빤지가 오리털이 될 리는 없지 않느냐고 말했다. 그러자 그는 또 한 번 히죽거리며 대팻밥을 그러모아 방 한가운데의 커다란 난로에 던져 넣고는, 제 일을 보러 가 버려 나를 깊은 상념에 잠기게 했다.\n\n나는 이제 벤치의 치수를 재 보았는데, 한 발쯤 짧다는 것을 알았다. 그래도 그건 의자 하나로 메울 수 있었다. 그러나 폭이 한 발쯤 좁았고, 방 안의 다른 벤치는 대패질한 것보다 네 인치쯤 높아서 — 둘을 짝지을 수가 없었다. 그래서 나는 첫 번째 벤치를 벽에 붙은 유일하게 빈 자리에 길게 세로로 놓고, 등을 누일 수 있도록 약간의 틈을 남겨 두었다. 그러나 곧 창틀 밑에서 차가운 바람이 어찌나 들이치는지, 이 계획은 도저히 통하지 않으리라는 것을 깨달았다. 더구나 삐걱거리는 문틈에서 들어오는 또 다른 바람이 창에서 오는 바람과 만나, 둘이 합쳐져 내가 밤을 보내려던 바로 그 자리 근처에 자그마한 회오리바람들을 줄지어 일으켰기 때문이었다.\n\n악마나 그 작살잡이를 잡아가라지, 하고 나는 생각했다. 그러나 잠깐, 그자보다 한발 앞서 선수를 칠 수는 없을까 — 안에서 그자의 방문을 걸어 잠그고, 그자의 침대로 뛰어들어, 아무리 거세게 두드려도 깨지 않으면 어떨까? 나쁘지 않은 생각 같았다. 그러나 다시 곰곰이 따져 보고는 그 생각을 접었다. 이튿날 아침, 내가 방에서 막 튀어나오자마자 그 작살잡이가 현관에 떡 버티고 서서 나를 한 대 후려칠 채비를 갖추고 있지 않으리라고 누가 장담하겠는가!\n\n그래도 다시 한번 주위를 둘러보고, 다른 누군가의 침대가 아니고서는 그럭저럭 견딜 만한 밤을 보낼 가능성이 도무지 없음을 깨닫자, 나는 어쩌면 결국 이 알지도 못하는 작살잡이에 대해 근거 없는 편견을 품고 있는 것은 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 잠시 기다려 보자, 하고 나는 생각했다. 그자도 머지않아 들어올 테니. 그때 그자를 잘 살펴보면, 어쩌면 우리가 결국 더없이 즐거운 잠자리 동무가 될지도 모를 일이다 — 누가 알겠는가.\n\n그러나 다른 투숙객들이 한 명, 두 명, 세 명씩 들어와 잠자리에 드는 동안에도, 내 작살잡이의 기척은 도무지 없었다.\n\n\"주인장!\" 내가 말했다. \"그 친구는 대체 어떤 사람이오 — 늘 이렇게 늦게까지 다니오?\" 시각은 이제 거의 열두 시가 다 되어 가고 있었다.\n\n여관 주인은 또다시 그 메마른 웃음으로 킬킬거렸는데, 내가 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 무언가가 어지간히 우스운 모양이었다. \"아니오,\" 그가 대답했다. \"보통은 일찍 일어나는 새지 — 일찍 자고 일찍 일어나거든 — 그래, 그자야말로 벌레를 잡는 새라오. 한데 오늘 밤엔 행상을 나갔단 말이지, 알겠소. 그러니 대체 무슨 영문으로 이리 늦는지 나도 모르겠소, 혹시 머리를 못 팔고 있는 게 아니라면 말이오.\"\n\n\"머리를 못 팔아? — 대체 무슨 사람 홀리는 소릴 지껄이는 거요?\" 나는 치솟는 분노에 휩싸이며 말했다. \"주인장, 그래 이 작살잡이가 이 거룩한 토요일 밤, 아니 차라리 일요일 새벽에, 이 도시를 돌아다니며 제 머리를 팔고 있다고 말하려는 거요?\"\n\n\"바로 그거요,\" 여관 주인이 말했다. \"그래서 내가 그자한테 여기선 못 판다고 했지, 시장에 물건이 넘쳐 난다고 말이오.\"\n\n\"무엇이 넘쳐 난단 말이오?\" 내가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"머리지, 당연히. 세상에 머리가 너무 많지 않소?\"\n\n\"내 말해 두는데, 주인장,\" 나는 자못 차분하게 말했다. \"그런 허튼소리는 그만두는 게 좋을 거요 — 나는 풋내기가 아니란 말이오.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지,\" 그가 막대기를 꺼내어 이쑤시개를 깎으며 말했다. \"하지만 저 작살잡이가 자기 머리를 헐뜯는 소릴 듣게 되면, 당신이야말로 아주 노릇노릇하게 구워질 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"내가 그자 머리를 박살 내 주지,\" 나는 여관 주인의 이 도무지 알 수 없는 횡설수설에 또다시 발끈하며 말했다.\n\n\"그건 이미 박살 나 있소,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"박살이 나,\" 내가 말했다. \"박살이 났다니, 그게 무슨 소리요?\"\n\n\"틀림없소, 바로 그게 그자가 그걸 못 파는 까닭이지, 내 짐작엔 말이오.\"\n\"주인장,\" 나는 눈보라 속의 헤클라산처럼 차갑게 그에게 다가서며 말했다. \"주인장, 그 이쑤시개 깎는 것 좀 그만두시오. 당신과 나는 서로 이해를 해 둬야겠소, 그것도 지체 없이 말이오. 나는 당신 집에 와서 침대를 원했소. 그런데 당신은 내게 침대 절반밖에 못 준다고, 나머지 절반은 어떤 작살잡이의 것이라고 했소. 그리고 아직 본 적도 없는 이 작살잡이에 관해, 당신은 더없이 사람을 홀리고 약 올리는 이야기들을 끈질기게 늘어놓아, 당신이 내 잠자리 동무로 정해 놓은 사람에 대해 내 마음속에 불편한 감정을 심어 놓고 있소 — 주인장, 그건 더없이 친밀하고 은밀한 관계란 말이오. 이제 나는 당신에게 분명히 말할 것을 요구하오. 이 작살잡이가 누구이며 무엇 하는 자인지, 그리고 내가 모든 면에서 안전하게 그자와 하룻밤을 보낼 수 있을지 말이오. 그리고 무엇보다 먼저, 그자가 머리를 판다는 그 이야기를 부디 거두어 주시오. 만약 그게 사실이라면, 나는 그것을 이 작살잡이가 완전히 미쳤다는 확실한 증거로 받아들일 텐데, 미친 자와 함께 잘 생각은 추호도 없소. 그리고 당신, 당신 말이오, 주인장, 바로 당신이오, 그런 줄 알면서도 나더러 그렇게 하도록 꾀어내려 든다면, 당신은 그로써 형사 소추를 받게 될 거란 말이오.\"\n\n\"흠,\" 여관 주인이 긴 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"이따금 욕설 좀 내뱉는 양반치곤 꽤나 긴 설교로구려. 하지만 진정하쇼, 진정하라니까. 내가 당신한테 얘기해 온 이 작살잡이는 방금 남쪽 바다에서 돌아온 참이라오. 거기서 방부 처리한 뉴질랜드(New Zealand) 머리를 잔뜩 사들였는데 (대단한 골동품이지, 알겠소), 그걸 다 팔고 딱 하나가 남았소. 그래서 그걸 오늘 밤에 팔아 보려는 거요. 내일이 일요일이라, 사람들이 교회에 가는데 거리에서 사람 머리를 팔고 다니는 건 좋지 않을 테니까. 지난 일요일에도 그러려고 했지만, 내가 막 문을 나서려는 그자를 붙들었지. 글쎄 머리 네 개를 줄에 꿰어 들고 있었는데, 영락없이 양파 한 두름 같더라니까.\"\n\n이 설명은 그러지 않았다면 도무지 풀리지 않았을 수수께끼를 명백히 해 주었고, 여관 주인이 결국은 나를 놀려 먹을 생각이 전혀 없었음을 보여 주었다. 그러나 동시에, 죽은 우상숭배자들의 머리를 파는 그런 식인종 같은 일에 매달려, 토요일 밤부터 거룩한 안식일까지 내내 밖으로 나돌아 다니는 작살잡이에 대해, 내가 무슨 생각을 할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n\"틀림없소, 주인장, 그 작살잡이는 위험한 자요.\"\n\n\"돈은 꼬박꼬박 내지,\" 하는 대꾸가 돌아왔다. \"하지만 자, 시간이 끔찍하게 늦어졌으니, 당신도 어서 꼬리를 틀고 자리에 드는 게 좋을 거요 — 좋은 침대라니까. 나랑 샐이 혼례를 올린 날 밤에 바로 그 침대에서 잤지. 둘이 발버둥을 쳐도 남아돌 만큼 자리가 넉넉하다오. 그건 엄청나게 큰 침대거든. 아, 그 침대를 내주기 전엔, 샐이 우리 샘이랑 어린 조니를 그 발치에 재우곤 했지. 그런데 어느 날 밤 내가 꿈을 꾸면서 마구 버둥대는 통에, 어쩌다 샘이 바닥으로 굴러떨어져 하마터면 팔이 부러질 뻔했다오. 그 뒤로 샐이 안 되겠다고 하더군. 자, 이리 오쇼, 내 금세 불을 밝혀 드리리다.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 촛불을 켜서 내게 들이밀며 앞장서겠다고 했다. 그러나 나는 망설이며 서 있었다. 그러자 그는 구석의 시계를 보더니 외쳤다. \"이런, 벌써 일요일이로군 — 오늘 밤엔 그 작살잡이를 못 볼 거요. 그자는 어딘가에 닻을 내렸겠지 — 그러니 어서 오쇼. 자, 오라니까. 안 오겠다는 거요?\"\n\n나는 잠시 그 일을 곱씹어 보고는, 이윽고 우리는 위층으로 올라갔고, 나는 조개처럼 차가운 작은 방으로 안내받았는데, 과연 거기에는 어마어마하게 큰 침대가 갖춰져 있어, 실로 작살잡이 넷이 나란히 누워 잘 수 있을 만큼 컸다.\n\n\"자,\" 여관 주인이 세면대와 가운데 탁자를 겸하는 다 낡은 선원용 궤짝 위에 촛불을 내려놓으며 말했다. \"자, 이제 편히 쉬쇼. 그럼 잘 자시오.\" 나는 침대를 살펴보다가 몸을 돌렸는데, 그는 이미 사라지고 없었다.\n\n침대보를 젖히고, 나는 침대 위로 몸을 수그렸다. 가장 우아한 것은 못 되었지만, 그래도 자세히 살펴보니 그런대로 견딜 만했다. 그런 다음 방 안을 둘러보았다. 침대 틀과 가운데 탁자 말고는, 이 방에 딸린 다른 가구라고는 보이지 않았고, 그저 거친 선반 하나, 사방의 벽들, 그리고 한 남자가 고래를 작살로 찌르는 그림이 그려진 벽지 바른 난로 가림판이 있을 뿐이었다. 본래 이 방에 속하지 않은 물건으로는, 단단히 묶여 한구석 바닥에 던져진 해먹 하나가 있었고, 또 뭍에서 쓰는 여행 가방을 대신하는 것이 분명한, 작살잡이의 옷가지가 든 커다란 선원용 자루도 있었다. 마찬가지로, 벽난로 위 선반에는 기괴한 뼈로 만든 낚싯바늘 한 꾸러미가 있었고, 침대 머리맡에는 키 큰 작살 하나가 세워져 있었다.\n\n그런데 궤짝 위에 놓인 이건 대체 무엇일까? 나는 그것을 집어 들어 불빛에 가까이 대고, 만져 보고, 냄새도 맡아 보며, 그것에 관해 어떤 그럴듯한 결론에 이르고자 가능한 모든 방법을 다 동원해 보았다. 나로서는 그것을 커다란 현관 매트 말고는 달리 비할 데가 없었으니, 그 가장자리에는 인디언(Indian) 모카신 둘레의 물들인 호저 가시 같은, 짤랑거리는 자그마한 술 장식들이 달려 있었다. 이 매트 한가운데에는 구멍, 곧 트인 자국이 하나 있었는데, 남아메리카의 판초에서 볼 수 있는 것과 똑같았다. 그러나 멀쩡한 정신의 작살잡이가 이런 현관 매트를 뒤집어쓰고, 그런 차림새로 어느 기독교 도시의 거리를 활보한다는 것이 과연 있을 법한 일일까? 나는 그것을 한번 걸쳐 보았는데, 보기 드물게 텁수룩하고 두꺼운 데다, 어딘가 조금 축축하기까지 해서 — 마치 이 수수께끼 같은 작살잡이가 비 오는 날 그것을 걸치고 다녔던 것처럼 — 그것은 빨래 광주리처럼 나를 짓눌렀다. 나는 그것을 걸친 채 벽에 붙은 작은 거울 조각 앞으로 다가가 보았는데, 내 평생 그런 꼴은 본 적이 없었다. 어찌나 황급히 그것을 벗어 던졌던지, 그 바람에 목을 삐끗하고 말았다.\n\n나는 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉아, 이 머리를 행상하는 작살잡이와 그의 현관 매트에 관해 생각하기 시작했다. 침대 옆에서 한동안 생각에 잠겨 있다가, 나는 일어나 선원용 재킷을 벗고, 방 한가운데에 서서 생각했다. 그런 다음 윗옷을 벗고, 셔츠 바람으로 조금 더 생각했다. 그러나 이렇게 반쯤 벗은 채로 있자니 이제 몹시 추워지기 시작했고, 시간이 너무 늦어 그 작살잡이가 그날 밤에는 아예 집에 돌아오지 않을 거라던 여관 주인의 말이 떠올라, 나는 더 이상 망설이지 않고 바지와 장화를 훌훌 벗어 던지고는, 불을 끄고 침대 속으로 굴러들어 가, 하늘의 보살핌에 나를 맡겼다.\n\n그 매트리스가 옥수수 속대로 채워졌는지 깨진 사기 조각으로 채워졌는지는 알 길이 없으나, 나는 한참을 이리저리 뒤척이며 오래도록 잠을 이루지 못했다. 마침내 어렴풋한 선잠 속으로 미끄러져 들어가, 거의 잠의 나라를 향해 좋은 항로를 잡아 가던 참이었는데, 복도에서 묵직한 발소리가 들려왔고, 문틈 아래로 한 줄기 빛이 방 안으로 새어 드는 것이 보였다.\n\n하느님 맙소사, 하고 나는 생각했다. 저건 틀림없이 그 작살잡이, 그 지긋지긋한 머리 행상꾼이로구나. 그러나 나는 꼼짝도 하지 않고 가만히 누워, 말을 걸어오기 전에는 한마디도 하지 않으리라 마음먹었다. 한 손에는 불을 들고, 다른 손에는 바로 그 뉴질랜드 머리를 든 채, 그 낯선 자가 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 침대 쪽은 보지도 않고, 촛불을 나에게서 멀찍이 떨어진 한구석 바닥에 내려놓더니, 앞서 이 방에 있다고 말했던 그 커다란 자루의 매듭진 끈을 풀기 시작했다. 나는 그의 얼굴을 보고 싶어 안달이 났지만, 그는 자루 입구의 끈을 푸는 동안 한참이나 얼굴을 돌리고 있었다. 그러나 마침내 그 일을 끝내고 그가 몸을 돌렸을 때 — 맙소사! 그 광경이라니! 그런 얼굴이라니! 그것은 검붉고 누르스름한 빛깔이었고, 군데군데 거뭇거뭇한 커다란 네모꼴들이 박혀 있었다. 그래, 내 생각이 옳았어, 저자는 끔찍한 잠자리 동무로구나. 싸움질을 하다가 지독하게 베인 채로, 이제 막 의사한테서 돌아온 모양이로구나. 그러나 바로 그 순간 그가 우연히 얼굴을 불빛 쪽으로 돌리는 바람에, 나는 그의 뺨에 박힌 그 검은 네모꼴들이 결코 반창고 따위가 아님을 똑똑히 보게 되었다. 그것은 무언가의 얼룩이었다. 처음에는 그것을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 몰랐으나, 곧 진실의 실마리가 떠올랐다. 나는 어느 백인 — 그것도 고래잡이 — 이 식인종들 사이에 떨어져 그들에게 문신을 새겨졌다는 이야기를 떠올렸다. 나는 이 작살잡이도 먼 항해를 다니던 중에 비슷한 일을 겪었음에 틀림없다고 결론지었다. 그래, 결국 그게 무슨 대수겠는가, 하고 나는 생각했다! 그건 그저 그자의 겉모습일 뿐, 사람은 어떤 살갗을 지녔든 정직할 수 있는 법이니까. 그러나 그렇다면, 그의 그 섬뜩한 안색은 또 어떻게 받아들여야 할까. 내 말은, 문신의 네모꼴들과는 완전히 별개로, 그 둘레에 퍼져 있는 그 빛깔 말이다. 물론, 그것이 그저 열대 지방에서 잘 그을린 빛에 지나지 않을 수도 있었다. 그러나 뜨거운 햇볕이 백인을 검붉고 누런색으로 그을렸다는 소리는 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었다. 하기야 나는 남쪽 바다에 가 본 적이 없으니, 어쩌면 그곳의 햇볕은 살갗에 이런 기이한 효과를 내는지도 모를 일이었다. 한데 이 모든 생각이 번개처럼 내 머릿속을 스쳐 지나가는 동안, 이 작살잡이는 나를 전혀 알아채지 못했다. 그러다 한참 애를 쓴 끝에 자루를 열고는, 그 안을 더듬거리더니, 이윽고 일종의 토마호크 같은 것과, 털이 그대로 붙은 물범 가죽 지갑을 꺼냈다. 이것들을 방 한가운데의 낡은 궤짝 위에 올려놓고, 그는 그 뉴질랜드 머리 — 충분히 소름 끼치는 물건이었다 — 를 집어 들어 자루 속으로 쑤셔 넣었다. 그는 이제 모자 — 새 비버 가죽 모자 — 를 벗었는데, 나는 새삼스러운 놀라움에 하마터면 소리를 지를 뻔했다. 그의 머리에는 머리카락이 없었다 — 적어도 머리카락이라고 할 만한 것은 없었다 — 그저 이마 위로 비비 꼬아 올린 자그마한 정수리 매듭 하나뿐이었다. 그 벗어진 검붉은 머리통은 이제 영락없이 곰팡이 핀 해골처럼 보였다. 그 낯선 자가 나와 문 사이를 가로막고 서 있지만 않았더라면, 나는 일찍이 저녁을 후딱 해치우던 것보다도 더 빨리 그 문으로 줄행랑을 쳤을 것이다.\n\n설령 그렇다 해도, 나는 창문으로 빠져나갈까도 잠시 생각했지만, 그곳은 뒤쪽 이층이었다. 나는 겁쟁이가 아니지만, 이 머리 행상하는 검붉은 악당을 통째로 어떻게 받아들여야 할지는 도무지 내 이해의 범위를 벗어나는 일이었다. 무지는 두려움의 어버이이니, 이 낯선 자에 대해 완전히 어찌할 바를 모르고 혼란에 빠진 나는, 솔직히 말해 이제 그자를, 마치 악마 그 자체가 이렇게 한밤중에 내 방에 쳐들어오기라도 한 양 두려워하고 있었다. 사실 나는 그자가 어찌나 무서웠던지, 그 순간에는 그에게 말을 걸어, 그에게서 도무지 알 수 없게만 여겨지던 것에 관해 만족스러운 답을 요구할 만한 배짱조차 없었다.\n\n그러는 사이, 그는 계속 옷을 벗어 갔고, 마침내 가슴과 팔을 드러냈다. 내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 옷에 가려져 있던 그 부분들도 얼굴과 똑같은 네모꼴들로 바둑판처럼 뒤덮여 있었다. 등도 마찬가지로 온통 그 똑같은 거뭇한 네모꼴들투성이였다. 그는 마치 삼십 년 전쟁이라도 치르고는, 반창고로 만든 셔츠만 걸친 채 거기서 막 빠져나온 사람 같았다. 게다가, 다리마저도 표시가 새겨져 있었으니, 마치 짙푸른 개구리 한 무리가 어린 야자나무 줄기를 타고 기어오르는 것 같았다. 이제 그가 남쪽 바다에서 어느 고래잡이배에 실려 와, 이렇게 이 기독교 나라에 내려진, 어떤 가증스러운 야만인임에 틀림없다는 것이 아주 명백해졌다. 나는 그 생각에 몸을 떨었다. 게다가 머리 행상꾼이라니 — 어쩌면 제 형제들의 머리일지도 모르는 것을. 그자가 내 머리에 욕심을 낼 수도 있는 일 아닌가 — 맙소사! 저 토마호크 좀 보라지!\n\n그러나 몸서리칠 겨를도 없었으니, 이제 그 야만인이 내 주의를 완전히 사로잡고, 그가 정녕 이교도임에 틀림없음을 내게 확신시켜 준 무언가에 착수했기 때문이다. 그는 앞서 의자에 걸어 두었던 묵직한 그레고, 곧 외투, 또는 두꺼운 방한복으로 다가가 주머니를 뒤지더니, 이윽고 등에 혹이 달리고 빛깔은 영락없이 사흘 된 콩고 아기 같은, 기이하고 자그마한 일그러진 형상 하나를 꺼냈다. 방부 처리된 머리가 떠올라, 처음에는 이 검은 인형이 비슷한 방식으로 보존된 진짜 아기가 아닐까 하는 생각마저 들 뻔했다. 그러나 그것이 전혀 물렁거리지 않고, 광을 낸 흑단처럼 꽤나 반들거리는 것을 보고는, 그저 나무로 만든 우상에 지나지 않으리라고 결론지었는데, 실제로 그것은 그러했다. 이제 그 야만인이 빈 벽난로로 다가가, 벽지 바른 난로 가림판을 치우더니, 이 자그마한 곱사등이 형상을 볼링 핀처럼 장작 받침쇠 사이에 세워 놓았다. 굴뚝 옆벽과 그 안의 벽돌들이 죄다 그을음투성이여서, 나는 이 벽난로가 그의 콩고 우상을 모실 아주 알맞은 자그마한 사당, 곧 예배소가 되겠구나 하고 생각했다.\n\n나는 이제 반쯤 가려진 그 형상을 향해 눈을 잔뜩 모아 떴는데, 그러는 내내 영 불안한 심정으로 — 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어질지 지켜보았다. 먼저 그는 그레고 주머니에서 대팻밥을 두 줌가량 꺼내어 우상 앞에 조심스레 놓았다. 그런 다음 그 위에 배에서 쓰는 비스킷 한 조각을 얹고, 램프의 불꽃을 갖다 대어 대팻밥을 제물의 불길로 지폈다. 이윽고 황급히 불 속으로 손을 여러 번 집어넣었다가 그보다 더 황급히 손가락을 빼내기를 거듭한 끝에 (그 바람에 손가락을 심하게 데이는 듯했다), 그는 마침내 그 비스킷을 꺼내는 데 성공했다. 그러고는 열기와 재를 조금 불어 낸 다음, 그것을 그 자그마한 흑인에게 정중히 권했다. 그러나 그 자그마한 악마는 그런 메마른 음식 따위는 도무지 마음에 들지 않는 모양이었으니, 입술 하나 까딱하지 않았다. 이 모든 기이한 행동에는, 그 신자가 내는, 더욱더 기이한 목구멍 소리가 곁들여졌는데, 그는 가락을 붙여 기도를 하거나, 아니면 무슨 이교의 찬송가를 부르고 있는 듯했고, 그러는 동안 그의 얼굴은 더없이 부자연스러운 모양으로 씰룩거렸다. 마침내 그는 불을 끄고는, 그 우상을 아주 무람없이 집어 들어, 마치 사냥꾼이 죽은 멧도요를 자루에 챙기듯 무심하게 다시 제 그레고 주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다.\n\n이 모든 괴이한 행위는 나의 불편함을 더욱 키웠고, 이제 그가 일을 마무리 짓고 나와 함께 침대에 뛰어들 강한 기미를 보이는 것을 알아채자, 나는 불이 꺼지기 전에 지금이야말로, 이때가 아니면 영영 기회가 없으리라 여기고, 그토록 오래 나를 옭아매고 있던 그 주술을 깨야겠다고 생각했다.\n\n그러나 무슨 말을 해야 할지 궁리하느라 보낸 그 짬은 치명적인 것이었다. 그는 탁자에서 제 토마호크를 집어 들어, 잠시 그 머리 부분을 살펴보더니, 불빛에 비춰 든 채 자루에 입을 대고 담배 연기를 뭉게뭉게 뿜어 댔다. 다음 순간 불이 꺼졌고, 이 사나운 식인종은, 토마호크를 이 사이에 문 채, 나와 함께 침대로 뛰어들었다. 나는 비명을 질렀다, 이제는 도저히 어쩔 수가 없었다. 그러자 그는 화들짝 놀라 켁 하는 소리를 내더니 나를 더듬기 시작했다.\n\n나는 무슨 말인지도 모를 말을 더듬더듬 내뱉으며, 그에게서 굴러 벽 쪽으로 몸을 피했다. 그러고는 그가 누구든 무엇이든, 제발 조용히 좀 있어 달라고, 그래서 내가 일어나 다시 램프에 불을 켤 수 있게 해 달라고 그에게 애원했다. 그러나 그의 목구멍에서 나오는 대꾸는, 그가 내 말뜻을 거의 알아듣지 못한다는 것을 단번에 내게 확인시켜 주었다.\n\n\"누구냐, 이 악마 같은 놈?\" 그가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"말 안 해, 빌어먹을, 죽여 버린다.\" 이렇게 말하며 그 불붙은 토마호크가 어둠 속에서 내 주위로 마구 휘둘러지기 시작했다.\n\n\"주인장, 제발, 피터 코핀!\" 나는 소리쳤다. \"주인장! 경비! 코핀! 천사들이여! 날 좀 살려 줘요!\"\n\n\"말해! 누군지 말하란 말이야, 안 그러면, 빌어먹을, 죽여 버린다!\" 식인종이 다시 으르렁거렸다. 그러는 동안 그가 토마호크를 끔찍하게 휘둘러 대는 통에 뜨거운 담뱃재가 내 주위로 흩날렸고, 나는 내 속옷에 불이 붙는 줄로만 알았다. 그러나 고맙게도 바로 그 순간 여관 주인이 손에 불을 들고 방 안으로 들어왔고, 나는 침대에서 펄쩍 뛰어내려 그에게로 달려갔다.\n\n\"이제 겁낼 거 없소,\" 그가 또다시 히죽거리며 말했다. \"여기 이 퀴케그(Queequeg)는 당신 머리카락 한 올도 해치지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"그 히죽거리는 것 좀 그만두쇼,\" 나는 소리쳤다. \"그리고 그 지긋지긋한 작살잡이가 식인종이라는 걸 왜 진작 말해 주지 않았소?\"\n\n\"당신이 아는 줄 알았지 — 내가 말하지 않았소, 그자가 시내를 돌며 머리를 행상하고 다닌다고? — 자, 그러니 다시 꼬리를 틀고 자리에 들어 잠이나 자쇼. 퀴케그, 이리 보게 — 자네 내 말 알아듣지(sabbee), 나도 알아 — 자네, 이 사람, 자네하고 잔다(sleepe), 알겠나(sabbee)?\"\n\n\"나 많이 알아들어(sabbee)\" 하고 퀴케그가 침대에서 일어나 앉아 제 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"자네 들어와,\" 그가 토마호크로 나에게 손짓하고 옷가지들을 한쪽으로 치우며 덧붙였다. 그는 정말로 이 일을 정중할 뿐 아니라 참으로 친절하고 자비로운 태도로 행했다. 나는 잠시 그를 바라보며 서 있었다. 그 온갖 문신에도 불구하고, 그는 전체적으로 깨끗하고 보기 좋은 식인종이었다. 내가 그동안 대체 무엇 때문에 이 야단법석을 떨었던가, 하고 나는 혼자 생각했다 — 이 사람도 나와 똑같은 인간 아닌가. 그가 나를 두려워할 까닭이나, 내가 그를 두려워할 까닭이나 매한가지이지 않은가. 술 취한 기독교인하고 자느니 술 안 마신 식인종하고 자는 편이 낫지.\n\n\"주인장,\" 내가 말했다. \"그자한테 그 토마호크인지, 파이프인지, 뭐라고 부르든 간에 그걸 좀 치우라고 하시오. 한마디로 담배 좀 그만 피우라고 말이오. 그러면 그자와 함께 자리에 들겠소. 하지만 나는 나와 함께 침대에서 담배 피우는 사람은 질색이오. 그건 위험하다고. 게다가 나는 보험도 안 들었거든.\"\n\n이 말이 퀴케그에게 전해지자, 그는 즉시 따랐고, 다시 한번 정중하게 내게 침대로 들어오라는 손짓을 했다 — 한쪽으로 잔뜩 굴러가서는, 마치 \"자네 다리 하나 건드리지 않을게\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯이.\n\n\"잘 자시오, 주인장,\" 내가 말했다. \"이제 가 봐도 되오.\"\n\n나는 자리에 들었고, 내 평생 그날 밤보다 더 잘 잔 적이 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 4. The Counterpane.\n\n이튿날 동틀 무렵 잠에서 깨어 보니, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 팔이 더없이 다정하고 애정 어린 모양새로 내 몸을 감싸고 있었다. 누가 보았다면 내가 그의 아내라도 되는 줄 알았으리라. 침대 덮개는 색색의 작고 기묘한 네모와 세모 조각들이 가득한 조각보였다. 그리고 그의 이 팔은 끝없이 이어지는 크레타의 미궁 같은 무늬가 온통 새겨져 있었는데, 그 어느 두 부분도 정확히 같은 빛깔이 아니었다. 짐작건대 바다에서 그가 햇볕과 그늘을 가리지 않고 멋대로 팔을 드러내 두었던 데다, 셔츠 소매를 그때그때 제멋대로 걷어 올렸던 탓이리라. 어쨌든 이 똑같은 팔이 영락없이 저 조각보 이불의 한 자락처럼 보였던 것이다. 사실 처음 잠에서 깼을 때 그의 팔이 이불 위에 절반쯤 걸쳐져 있는 모양이, 그 빛깔이 너무도 자연스레 어우러진 나머지 나는 그것을 이불과 거의 구별할 수 없을 지경이었다. 다만 그 무게와 짓누르는 감촉으로써만 나는 퀴퀘그가 나를 끌어안고 있음을 알 수 있었다.\n\n내 느낌은 기묘했다. 그것을 어떻게든 설명해 보겠다. 어린 시절, 나는 이와 다소 비슷한 일을 겪은 기억이 또렷이 남아 있다. 그것이 실제였는지 꿈이었는지는 끝내 온전히 가려내지 못했다. 사정은 이러했다. 나는 무슨 장난이든 한바탕 벌이고 있었는데 — 며칠 전에 어린 굴뚝 청소부가 하는 것을 본 터라, 아마 굴뚝을 기어오르려 했던 것 같다. 그러자 무슨 까닭에선지 늘 나를 매질하거나 저녁도 안 주고 잠자리로 내쫓던 새어머니가, 내 다리를 붙잡고 굴뚝에서 끌어내려 침대로 처박아 버렸다. 그때는 6월 21일 오후 두 시밖에 안 된 시각이었으니, 우리 반구(半球)에서 일 년 중 해가 가장 긴 날이었다. 나는 끔찍한 기분이었다. 그러나 어쩔 도리가 없었기에, 나는 삼층의 내 작은 방으로 올라가, 시간을 죽이려고 되도록 천천히 옷을 벗고는, 쓰디쓴 한숨과 함께 이불 속으로 기어들었다.\n\n나는 그곳에 누운 채, 부활을 바랄 수 있기까지 꼬박 열여섯 시간이 흘러야 한다는 것을 음울하게 헤아렸다. 침대에서 열여섯 시간이라니! 그 생각만으로도 등허리가 쑤셔 왔다. 게다가 바깥은 어찌나 환한지, 창으로 햇살이 비쳐 들고, 거리에서는 마차들이 요란하게 덜컹거렸으며, 집 안 곳곳에서 흥겨운 목소리들이 울려 퍼졌다. 나는 점점 더 견딜 수 없어졌다. 마침내 나는 일어나 옷을 입고, 양말만 신은 발로 살금살금 아래층으로 내려가 새어머니를 찾아냈다. 그러고는 느닷없이 그분의 발치에 몸을 던지며, 내 잘못을 슬리퍼로 호되게 때려 주십사 특별한 은혜로 간청했다 — 그토록 견딜 수 없이 긴 시간을 침대에 누워 있으라는 형벌만 아니라면 무엇이든 좋다고. 그러나 그분은 더없이 훌륭하고 양심적인 새어머니였기에, 나는 도로 내 방으로 돌아가야만 했다. 나는 몇 시간이고 말똥말똥 깬 채 누워 있었는데, 그 뒤로 겪은 가장 큰 불행들에 견주어도 그때만큼 비참했던 적은 없었다. 마침내 나는 어수선한 악몽 같은 선잠에 빠져들었던 모양이다. 그리고 그 잠에서 천천히 깨어나며 — 꿈에 반쯤 잠긴 채로 — 눈을 떠 보니, 조금 전까지 햇살이 가득하던 방이 이제는 바깥의 어둠에 휩싸여 있었다. 순간 나는 온몸을 관통하는 충격을 느꼈다. 아무것도 보이지 않고, 아무것도 들리지 않았다. 그런데 초자연적인 손 하나가 내 손 안에 놓여 있는 것만 같았다. 내 팔은 침대 덮개 너머로 늘어져 있었고, 그 손의 주인인 이름 없고 상상조차 할 수 없는 고요한 형체 혹은 환영이 내 침대 곁에 바짝 다가앉아 있는 듯했다. 헤아릴 수 없는 세월이 켜켜이 쌓인 듯한 시간 동안, 나는 더없이 끔찍한 공포에 얼어붙어 그곳에 누운 채, 감히 내 손을 빼낼 엄두도 내지 못했다. 그러면서도 단 한 치라도 손을 움직일 수만 있다면 그 무시무시한 주술이 풀리리라는 생각만은 줄곧 했다. 이 의식이 마침내 어떻게 나에게서 스르르 사라져 갔는지는 알지 못한다. 그러나 아침에 깨어나 그 모든 일을 몸서리치며 떠올렸고, 그 뒤로 며칠이고 몇 주고 몇 달이고, 나는 그 수수께끼를 풀어 보려는 갈피 잡을 수 없는 시도 속에서 헤맸다. 아니, 바로 이 순간까지도 나는 종종 그 일로 골머리를 앓는다.\n\n자, 그 끔찍한 공포만 걷어 낸다면, 초자연적인 손이 내 손 안에 있음을 느꼈을 때의 그 감각은, 그 기묘함에 있어 잠에서 깨어나 퀴퀘그의 이교도 팔이 나를 휘감고 있는 것을 보았을 때 겪은 감각과 매우 흡사했다. 그러나 마침내 지난밤의 모든 일들이 하나하나 차분하게, 확고한 현실로 되살아났고, 그제야 나는 이 우스꽝스러운 곤경만을 또렷이 의식하게 되었다. 그의 팔을 치워 보려고 — 그 신랑 같은 포옹을 풀어 보려고 — 애써 보았으나, 잠들어 있으면서도 그는 마치 죽음 말고는 우리 둘을 갈라놓을 것이 없다는 듯 나를 꼭 끌어안고 있었다. 나는 이제 그를 깨워 보려 애썼다. \"퀴퀘그!\" 그러나 그의 대답이라곤 코 고는 소리뿐이었다. 그래서 나는 몸을 돌렸는데, 목이 마치 말의 멍에에라도 끼인 것 같았다. 그러다 문득 살짝 긁히는 느낌이 들었다. 침대 덮개를 젖혀 보니, 미개인의 옆구리에 토마호크가 잠들어 있었다. 마치 도끼 얼굴을 한 아기라도 되는 듯이. 참으로 한심한 꼴이로구나, 하고 나는 생각했다. 대낮에 낯선 집 침대에 누워, 식인종과 토마호크를 곁에 두고 있다니! \"퀴퀘그! — 제발 좀, 퀴퀘그, 일어나라고!\" 마침내 나는 한참을 버둥거리고, 사내끼리 그런 부부 같은 꼴로 끌어안는 것이 얼마나 꼴사나운 짓인지 크고 끊임없이 항의한 끝에, 가까스로 그에게서 끙 하는 소리를 끌어냈다. 이윽고 그는 팔을 거두어들이더니, 물에서 막 나온 뉴펀들랜드 개처럼 온몸을 부르르 떨고는, 침대에 꼿꼿이 앉았다. 작살자루처럼 빳빳하게 앉아 나를 바라보며, 내가 어쩌다 거기 있게 되었는지 도무지 기억나지 않는다는 듯 두 눈을 비벼 댔다. 그러면서도 나에 관한 무언가를 알고 있다는 어렴풋한 의식이 그에게 서서히 동터 오는 듯했다. 그동안 나는 조용히 누운 채 그를 살펴보았다. 이제는 별다른 불안도 없었기에, 이토록 기묘한 피조물을 찬찬히 관찰하는 데 온 마음을 기울였다. 마침내 그가 제 잠자리 동무의 정체에 관해 마음을 정한 듯, 말하자면 그 사실과 화해하게 되었을 때, 그는 바닥으로 풀쩍 뛰어내리더니 어떤 몸짓과 소리로 내게 이렇게 알렸다. 내가 괜찮다면 자기가 먼저 옷을 입고 나가서 방을 통째로 내게 내어 줄 테니, 나는 그다음에 옷을 입으라는 것이었다. 이런 상황에서 보니, 퀴퀘그, 이거야말로 참으로 점잖은 제안이로구나, 하고 나는 생각했다. 하지만 사실 이 미개인들에게는, 뭐라고들 하든 간에, 타고난 섬세함이 있다. 그들이 본질적으로 얼마나 예의 바른지는 참으로 놀라울 정도다. 내가 퀴퀘그에게 이런 각별한 찬사를 보내는 것은, 내가 큰 무례를 저지르고 있는 와중에도 그는 나를 그토록 정중하고 사려 깊게 대해 주었기 때문이다. 나는 침대에서 그를 빤히 쳐다보며 그의 몸단장하는 동작을 일일이 지켜보고 있었으니, 그동안만큼은 호기심이 예의를 이겨 버린 셈이었다. 그렇기는 해도, 퀴퀘그 같은 사내는 날마다 볼 수 있는 것이 아니었으니, 그와 그의 행동거지는 유난스레 눈여겨볼 만한 가치가 충분했다.\n\n그는 머리부터 옷을 입기 시작했는데, 우선 비버 가죽 모자를 썼다. 그건 그렇고, 그것은 아주 높다란 모자였다. 그러고는 — 여전히 바지는 입지 않은 채로 — 자기 장화를 찾아냈다. 도대체 무슨 영문으로 그랬는지는 알 수 없으나, 그의 다음 동작은 — 장화를 손에 들고 모자를 쓴 채로 — 침대 밑으로 제 몸을 욱여넣는 것이었다. 그러고는 격렬하게 헐떡이고 끙끙대는 소리로 미루어, 나는 그가 장화를 신느라 안간힘을 쓰고 있음을 짐작했다. 하지만 내가 일찍이 들어 본 그 어떤 예법으로도, 장화를 신을 때 남의 눈을 피해야 한다고 정한 법은 없다. 그러나 보다시피 퀴퀘그는 과도기 단계의 피조물이었다 — 애벌레도 아니고 나비도 아닌. 그는 더없이 기묘한 방식으로 제 야성을 드러낼 만큼만 딱 적당히 문명화되어 있었다. 그의 교육은 아직 완성되지 않았다. 그는 학부생이었던 셈이다. 만약 그가 조금이라도 문명화되지 않았더라면, 그는 십중팔구 장화 따위로 골머리를 앓지도 않았을 것이다. 하지만 또 그가 여전히 미개인이 아니었더라면, 그것을 신겠다고 침대 밑으로 기어들 생각은 결코 꿈에도 하지 않았으리라. 마침내 그는 모자가 잔뜩 찌그러진 채 눈까지 푹 눌러쓴 모습으로 기어 나오더니, 삐걱대고 절뚝거리며 방 안을 돌아다니기 시작했다. 장화에 그리 익숙하지 않은 데다, 축축하고 주름진 그의 쇠가죽 장화 한 켤레가 — 아마 맞춤으로 지은 것도 아니었으리라 — 살을 에는 추운 아침의 첫걸음부터 어지간히 그를 죄어 들고 괴롭히는 모양이었다.\n\n이제 보니 창에는 커튼이 없었고, 거리가 몹시 좁은 탓에 맞은편 집에서 방 안이 훤히 들여다보였다. 게다가 퀴퀘그가 모자와 장화 말고는 거의 아무것도 걸치지 않은 채 쿵쾅거리며 돌아다니는 꼴이 점점 더 꼴사나워 보이기에, 나는 되도록 좋은 말로 그에게 몸단장을 좀 서둘러 달라고, 특히 바지부터 한시바삐 입어 달라고 청했다. 그는 순순히 따랐고, 그런 다음 세수를 하기 시작했다. 그 시각이면 어떤 기독교도라도 얼굴을 씻었으련만, 놀랍게도 퀴퀘그는 가슴과 팔과 손을 씻는 것으로 제 세정을 끝내는 데 만족했다. 그러고는 조끼를 걸치더니, 세면대 가운데 탁자에 놓인 굳은 비누 한 조각을 집어 들어 물에 적시고는 얼굴에 거품을 내기 시작했다. 나는 그가 면도칼을 어디에 두는지 보려고 지켜보고 있었는데, 그런데 이게 웬일인가, 그는 침대 모서리에서 작살을 집어 들더니, 긴 나무 자루를 빼내고 날의 칼집을 벗긴 뒤, 장화에 슬쩍 갈고는, 벽에 걸린 작은 거울 앞으로 성큼성큼 다가가, 제 두 뺨을 힘차게 긁어 대기 시작했다. 아니, 작살로 면도를 한다고나 할까. 퀴퀘그, 이건 로저스의 최고급 칼붙이를 작정하고 함부로 쓰는 격이로구나, 하고 나는 생각했다. 나중에 작살의 날이 얼마나 좋은 강철로 만들어지는지, 그리고 그 길고 곧은 날이 늘 얼마나 지독히도 날카롭게 갈려 있는지를 알게 되자, 나는 이 광경에 그리 놀라지 않게 되었다.\n\n그의 나머지 몸단장은 이내 마무리되었고, 그는 커다란 도선사용 두툼한 외투를 두르고는, 작살을 마치 원수(元帥)의 지휘봉처럼 멋들어지게 흔들며 의기양양하게 방을 나섰다.\n\n# CHAPTER 5. Breakfast.\n\n나는 곧바로 그를 따라 일어나 술집으로 내려가서, 싱글거리는 주인장에게 아주 유쾌하게 말을 건넸다. 그가 내 잠자리 동무 문제로 나를 적잖이 놀려 댔지만, 나는 그에게 아무런 악감정도 품지 않았다.\n\n어쨌든 한바탕 웃음이란 참으로 좋은 것이며, 그러기에는 너무 드문 좋은 것이기도 하다. 더욱 안타까운 일이다. 그러니 어떤 사람이 제 한 몸으로 누구에게든 좋은 농담거리를 줄 수 있다면, 망설이지 말고 기꺼이 그 방면으로 제 몸을 내주어 마음껏 쓰이게 할 일이다. 그리고 제 안에 어딘가 넉넉히 웃음을 자아내는 데가 있는 사람이라면, 그 사람 안에는 그대가 짐작하는 것보다 더 많은 것이 들어 있음을 믿어도 좋다.\n\n술집은 이제 전날 밤 하나둘 흘러들어 온 하숙객들로 가득 찼는데, 나는 아직 그들을 제대로 살펴보지 못한 터였다. 그들은 거의 모두가 고래잡이였다. 일등 항해사, 이등 항해사, 삼등 항해사, 배 목수, 배 통장이, 배 대장장이, 작살잡이, 그리고 배 지킴이들이었다. 햇볕에 그을려 거무스레하고 억센 무리로, 덥수룩한 수염을 길렀다. 깎지 않은 텁수룩한 패거리가 하나같이 짧은 두꺼운 윗도리를 잠옷 가운 삼아 걸치고 있었다.\n\n저마다 뭍에 올라온 지 얼마나 되었는지는 제법 또렷이 알아볼 수 있었다. 이 젊은 친구의 혈색 좋은 뺨은 햇볕에 익은 배(梨)의 빛깔을 띠어, 거의 그만큼이나 사향 냄새를 풍길 듯했다. 인도양 항해에서 뭍에 내린 지 사흘도 채 되지 않았을 것이다. 그 옆에 있는 자는 몇 단계쯤 더 옅어 보였으니, 새틴우드 결이 살짝 비친다고나 할까. 세 번째 사람의 안색에는 아직 열대의 거무튀튀한 기운이 남아 있었으나, 그래도 조금은 바래 있었다. 이 사람은 분명 몇 주씩이나 뭍에 머물렀을 것이다. 그러나 누가 퀴퀘그(Queequeg) 같은 뺨을 내보일 수 있겠는가? 갖가지 색조로 줄무늬가 진 그 뺨은, 마치 안데스산맥의 서쪽 비탈처럼, 서로 다른 기후를 한 화폭 위에 지대별로 나란히 펼쳐 보이는 듯했다.\n\n\"밥 나왔소!\" 이윽고 주인장이 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 외쳤고, 우리는 안으로 들어가 아침을 먹었다.\n\n세상을 두루 본 사람은 그로 인해 태도가 아주 느긋해지고, 무리 가운데서도 침착해진다고들 한다. 그러나 늘 그런 것은 아니다. 위대한 뉴잉글랜드의 여행가 레디어드(Ledyard)와 스코틀랜드의 여행가 멍고 파크(Mungo Park), 이 두 사람은 누구보다도 응접실에서 자신감이 가장 없는 사람들이었다. 그러나 어쩌면 레디어드가 했던 것처럼 개들이 끄는 썰매를 타고 시베리아를 가로지르는 일, 또는 가련한 멍고가 이룬 일의 전부였던, 굶주린 배를 안고 아프리카의 검은 심장부를 홀로 오래도록 걷는 일 — 말하건대 이런 종류의 여행은 높은 사교적 세련됨을 얻기에는 그리 좋은 방편이 아닐지도 모른다. 그래도 대체로 그런 종류의 것이라면 어디서든 얻을 수 있는 법이다.\n\n마침 여기서 이런 생각이 떠오른 것은, 우리가 모두 식탁에 둘러앉아 내가 고래잡이에 관한 그럴듯한 이야기를 들으려 채비하고 있을 때, 적잖이 놀랍게도 거의 모든 사람이 깊은 침묵을 지켰기 때문이다. 그뿐만 아니라 그들은 어색해 보이기까지 했다. 그렇다, 여기 모인 이 바다의 늙은 개들 가운데 많은 이들은 일찍이 한 점 부끄럼도 없이 망망대해에서 거대한 고래들 — 그들에게는 생판 낯선 상대들 — 의 등에 올라타 눈 하나 깜짝하지 않고 결투를 벌여 숨통을 끊어 놓은 자들이었다. 그런데도 지금 이 화기로운 아침 식탁에 둘러앉아서는 — 모두가 같은 일을 하는, 같은 취향을 지닌 사람들이건만 — 마치 그린마운틴 어느 양 우리를 한 번도 벗어나 본 적 없는 양 서로를 멋쩍게 두리번거리고 있었다. 참으로 진기한 광경이었다. 이 수줍은 곰들, 이 겁 많은 전사 같은 고래잡이들이라니!\n\n그러나 퀴퀘그로 말하자면 — 아니, 퀴퀘그는 그들 가운데 앉아 있었으니, 마침 식탁의 상석에 자리 잡고서, 고드름처럼 태연자약했다. 물론 그의 몸가짐에 대해서야 내가 좋게 말할 거리가 별로 없다. 그를 가장 흠모하는 사람이라 해도, 그가 아침 식탁에까지 작살을 들고 와 거리낌 없이 그것을 휘둘러 댄 일, 곧 식탁 너머로 작살을 쭉 뻗어 여러 사람의 머리를 위태롭게 하면서 비프스테이크를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기던 짓을 진심으로 두둔하지는 못했을 것이다. 그러나 그것을 그는 확실히 아주 침착하게 해냈으니, 누구나 알다시피 대다수 사람들이 보기에 무슨 일이든 침착하게 해낸다는 것은 곧 점잖게 해낸다는 뜻이다.\n\n퀴퀘그의 별난 점을 여기서 다 늘어놓지는 않겠다. 그가 어떻게 커피와 따끈한 롤빵을 마다하고 오로지 설익힌 비프스테이크에만 온 정신을 쏟았는지 따위는. 다만 아침 식사가 끝나자 그도 나머지 사람들처럼 공용실로 물러나 토마호크 담뱃대에 불을 붙이고, 한시도 떼어 놓지 않는 모자를 쓴 채 그 자리에 앉아 조용히 소화를 시키며 담배를 피우고 있었고, 그동안 나는 산책을 하러 밖으로 나섰다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하다.\n\n# CHAPTER 6. The Street.\n\n처음에 퀴케그(Queequeg)처럼 그토록 기이한 인물이 문명화된 도시의 점잖은 사교계 속을 누비는 모습을 흘끗 보았을 때 나는 적잖이 놀랐지만, 그 놀라움은 뉴베드퍼드(New Bedford)의 거리를 처음으로 대낮에 산책하면서 곧 사라져 버렸다.\n\n부두 가까운 큰길에서는, 어지간한 규모의 항구 도시라면 흔히 이국에서 온 더없이 괴상한 모습의 정체불명 인간들을 눈앞에 펼쳐 보이게 마련이다. 브로드웨이나 체스트넛 거리에서조차 지중해의 뱃사람들이 겁에 질린 부인네들과 어깨를 부딪치는 일이 더러 있다. 리젠트 거리도 라스카르인과 말레이인에게 낯설지 않으며, 봄베이의 아폴로 그린에서는 살아 있는 양키들이 곧잘 원주민들을 겁먹게 했다. 그러나 뉴베드퍼드는 워터 거리와 워핑을 죄다 능가한다. 방금 언급한 그런 소굴들에서는 그저 선원들만 보일 뿐이지만, 뉴베드퍼드에서는 진짜 식인종들이 길모퉁이에 서서 잡담을 나누고 있다. 영락없는 야만인들이며, 그중 다수는 아직도 제 뼈 위에 부정한 살점을 붙이고 다닌다. 이방인이라면 빤히 쳐다보지 않을 수 없는 광경이다.\n\n그러나 피지인, 통가타부인, 에로망고인, 파낭인, 브리기인 외에도, 그리고 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 가운데 거리를 비틀거리며 돌아다니는 고래잡이 무리의 야성적인 표본들 외에도, 그대는 그보다 더 진기하고, 확실히 더 우스꽝스러운 광경들을 보게 될 것이다. 이 도시에는 매주 수십 명의 풋내기 버몬트 사람과 뉴햄프셔 사람이 몰려드는데, 하나같이 포경업에서 돈과 영예를 얻으려 목말라하는 자들이다. 대부분 젊고 건장한 체구의 사내들로, 숲을 베어 넘기던 자들이 이제는 도끼를 내던지고 고래 작살을 거머쥐려는 것이다. 다수는 자신들이 떠나온 그린마운틴만큼이나 풋풋하다. 어떤 면에서는 갓 태어난 지 몇 시간밖에 안 된 것처럼 보일 정도다. 저기를 보라! 모퉁이를 으스대며 돌아 나오는 저 친구를. 비버 가죽 모자에 제비꼬리 외투를 걸치고, 선원용 허리띠에 칼집 단검을 차고 있다. 여기 또 한 명이 온다. 사우웨스터 방수모를 쓰고 봄바진 천 망토를 두른 사내다.\n\n도회에서 자란 멋쟁이는 시골에서 자란 멋쟁이를 결코 따를 수 없다. 내가 말하는 것은 영락없는 촌뜨기 멋쟁이로, 삼복더위에도 제 손이 햇볕에 그을릴까 두려워 사슴 가죽 장갑을 끼고 자기 밭 두 에이커를 베어 넘기는 그런 작자다. 그런데 이런 시골 멋쟁이가 명성 한번 떨쳐 보겠다고 마음먹고 위대한 포경업에 뛰어들면, 항구에 닿은 뒤 그가 벌이는 우스꽝스러운 짓거리를 그대도 꼭 보아야 한다. 바다용 장비를 맞추면서 그는 조끼에 종 모양 단추를 달아 달라고 주문하고, 캔버스 천 바지에는 끈을 달아 달라 한다. 아, 가련한 풋내기여! 첫 광풍이 울부짖을 때 그 끈들이 얼마나 비참하게 터져 나갈 것인가. 그때 그대는 끈이고 단추고 죄다 함께 폭풍의 목구멍 속으로 처박히게 될 테니.\n\n그러나 이 이름난 도시가 방문객에게 작살잡이와 식인종과 촌뜨기들만 보여 준다고는 생각지 마라. 결코 그렇지 않다. 그래도 뉴베드퍼드는 묘한 곳이다. 만약 우리 고래잡이들이 없었더라면, 저 땅뙈기는 오늘날 아마 래브라도 해안만큼이나 황량한 처지에 놓여 있었을지도 모른다. 그렇기는 해도, 그 내륙 일부는 어찌나 앙상해 보이는지 사람을 겁먹게 하기에 충분하다. 도시 자체는 아마 온 뉴잉글랜드(New England)를 통틀어 가장 살기 비싼 곳일 것이다. 과연 기름의 땅이기는 하다. 그러나 가나안과 같지는 않으니, 가나안은 또한 곡식과 포도주의 땅이었다. 이곳 거리에는 젖이 흐르지 않으며, 봄철이라 해서 신선한 달걀로 길을 포장하지도 않는다. 그럼에도 불구하고, 온 아메리카를 통틀어 뉴베드퍼드보다 더 귀족적인 저택들과 더 호화로운 공원과 정원을 찾을 수 있는 곳은 어디에도 없다. 그것들은 어디서 왔는가? 한때 이 거칠고 메마른 화산암 같은 땅 위에 어떻게 심어졌단 말인가?\n\n저 높이 솟은 저택 둘레에 상징처럼 둘러진 무쇠 작살들을 가서 한번 바라보라. 그러면 그대의 물음에 답이 나오리라. 그렇다. 이 모든 당당한 저택과 꽃 만발한 정원은 대서양과 태평양과 인도양에서 온 것이다. 하나도 빠짐없이, 그것들은 작살에 꿰여 바다 밑바닥에서 이곳까지 끌어올려졌다. 알렉산더 선생이 이런 묘기를 부릴 수 있겠는가?\n\n뉴베드퍼드에서는, 사람들 말이, 아버지들이 딸에게 지참금으로 고래를 주고, 조카딸들에게는 한 사람당 돌고래 몇 마리씩을 떼어 준다고 한다. 화려한 결혼식을 보려거든 뉴베드퍼드에 가 봐야 한다. 사람들 말로는, 집집마다 기름 저장고가 있어서, 매일 밤 향유고래 기름 양초를 그 길이만큼 아낌없이 태운다고 하기 때문이다.\n\n여름철이면 이 도시는 보기에 감미롭다. 아름다운 단풍나무로 가득하여, 초록빛과 황금빛이 어우러진 긴 가로수 길을 이룬다. 그리고 팔월이면, 높다란 허공으로 아름답고 풍성한 마로니에가 촛대처럼 가지를 뻗어, 무리 지어 핀 꽃송이가 뾰족하게 곧추선 원뿔 모양으로 지나가는 행인에게 그 자태를 내민다. 예술의 힘이란 이토록 전능하니, 뉴베드퍼드의 여러 구역에서 그것은 천지창조의 마지막 날에 한쪽으로 버려졌던 황량한 폐석 위에 환한 꽃의 계단을 일구어 놓았다.\n\n그리고 뉴베드퍼드의 여인들, 그들은 제 고장의 붉은 장미처럼 피어난다. 그러나 장미는 여름에만 피지만, 그들의 뺨에 어린 고운 카네이션 빛깔은 일곱째 하늘의 햇살처럼 사철 변함이 없다. 그들의 그 꽃다움에 견줄 만한 것은 다른 어디서도 찾을 수 없으니, 다만 세일럼만은 예외다. 사람들이 내게 일러 주기를, 그곳의 젊은 처녀들은 어찌나 향긋한 사향 내음을 풍기는지, 뱃사람 연인들이 바다에서 몇 마일 떨어진 데서도 그 향기를 맡아, 마치 청교도의 모래밭이 아니라 향기로운 몰루카 제도에 다가서는 듯한 기분이 든다고 했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 7. The Chapel.\n\n이 같은 뉴베드퍼드에는 고래잡이 예배당이 하나 서 있는데, 머지않아 인도양이나 태평양으로 떠날 침울한 어부들 가운데 일요일에 이곳을 찾지 않는 이는 드물다. 나 역시 빠뜨리지 않았음을 분명히 밝혀 둔다.\n\n첫 아침 산책에서 돌아온 나는, 바로 그 특별한 볼일을 위해 다시 길을 나섰다. 하늘은 맑고 햇살 비치는 추위에서, 휘몰아치는 진눈깨비와 안개로 바뀌어 있었다. 곰가죽이라 불리는 천으로 지은 텁수룩한 외투에 몸을 감싸고, 나는 끈질긴 폭풍을 헤치며 길을 뚫고 나아갔다. 안으로 들어서자 선원들과, 선원의 아내들, 그리고 과부들이 띄엄띄엄 모여 앉아 이루는 작은 회중이 보였다. 숨죽인 정적이 자리를 지배했고, 이따금 폭풍의 비명만이 그것을 깨뜨릴 뿐이었다. 말 없는 예배자들은 저마다 일부러 떨어져 앉아 있는 듯했으니, 마치 그 말 없는 슬픔이 저마다 외딴섬처럼 동떨어져 서로 통할 수 없는 것인 양했다. 목사는 아직 도착하지 않았고, 그곳에서 이 말 없는 남녀의 섬들은 설교단 양옆 벽에 박혀 있는, 검은 테두리를 두른 여러 대리석 명판을 한결같이 응시하고 있었다. 그중 세 개는 대략 다음과 같았으나, 정확히 옮긴다고 장담하지는 않겠다.\n\n존 탤벗(JOHN TALBOT)을 기리며. 그는 열여덟의 나이에, 1836년 11월 1일, 파타고니아 앞바다 황량의 섬 부근에서 바다에 휩쓸려 목숨을 잃었다. 이 명판은 그의 누이가 그를 기리며 세운 것이다.\n\n로버트 롱(ROBERT LONG), 윌리스 엘러리(WILLIS ELLERY), 네이선 콜먼(NATHAN COLEMAN), 월터 캐니(WALTER CANNY), 세스 메이시(SETH MACY), 그리고 새뮤얼 글레이그(SAMUEL GLEIG)를 기리며. 이들은 일라이자(ELIZA)호의 보트 한 척에 탄 선원들로서, 1839년 12월 31일 태평양의 먼바다 어장에서 고래에게 끌려가 시야에서 사라졌다. 이 대리석은 살아남은 동료 선원들이 이곳에 세운 것이다.\n\n작고한 이지키엘 하디(EZEKIEL HARDY) 선장을 기리며. 그는 1833년 8월 3일, 일본 연안에서 자기 보트의 뱃머리에 서 있다가 향유고래에게 목숨을 잃었다. 이 명판은 그의 미망인이 그를 기리며 세운 것이다.\n\n얼음으로 번들거리는 모자와 외투에서 진눈깨비를 털어 내고, 나는 문 가까이에 자리를 잡았는데, 옆으로 고개를 돌리다가 가까이에 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)가 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 장면의 엄숙함에 사로잡힌 그의 얼굴에는, 믿기지 않는다는 듯한 호기심의 의아한 응시가 어려 있었다. 이 야만인은 내가 들어온 것을 알아챈 듯 보이는 유일한 사람이었다. 글을 읽지 못하는 유일한 사람이었기에, 벽에 새겨진 저 싸늘한 비문들을 읽고 있지 않았던 까닭이다. 그곳에 이름이 오른 선원들의 친지 가운데 누군가가 지금 이 회중 속에 있는지 나는 알 수 없었다. 그러나 포경업에는 기록되지 않은 사고가 워낙 많고, 또 그 자리의 여러 여인들이 끊이지 않는 슬픔의 장식까지는 아니더라도 그 표정만은 너무도 또렷이 지니고 있었기에, 나는 분명히 느꼈다. 내 앞에 모여 있는 이들이야말로, 그 아물지 않는 가슴속에서 저 황량한 명판들을 바라보는 것만으로 옛 상처가 새삼 피 흘리고 마는 사람들임을.\n\n오! 그대들, 죽은 이가 푸른 풀밭 아래 묻혀 있는 자들이여. 꽃들 가운데 서서 여기, 바로 여기 내 사랑하는 이가 누워 있노라고 말할 수 있는 그대들이여. 그대들은 이런 가슴속에 도사린 황폐함을 알지 못한다. 재 한 줌 덮지 못한 저 검은 테두리의 대리석 속에 담긴 그 쓰라린 공백이라니! 저 꿈쩍도 않는 비문 속에 담긴 그 절망이라니! 모든 믿음(Faith)을 갉아먹는 듯하며, 무덤도 없이 어디서랄 것도 없이 스러진 존재들에게 부활을 허락하지 않는 그 글줄 속에 담긴, 죽음 같은 공허와 청하지도 않은 불신이라니. 저 명판들은 차라리 이곳보다 엘레판타 동굴 안에 서 있는 편이 나았으리라.\n\n살아 있는 피조물의 그 어떤 인구 조사에 인류의 죽은 자들이 포함되는가. 그들에 관해 어찌하여 만국 공통의 속담은, 그들이 굿윈 모래톱보다 더 많은 비밀을 품고 있으면서도 아무 이야기도 들려주지 않는다고 말하는가. 어제 저세상으로 떠난 이의 이름 앞에는 그토록 의미심장하고 불신에 찬 말을 우리가 붙이면서, 정작 그가 이 살아 있는 땅의 가장 먼 인도(Indies)로 떠나간 것이라면 어찌하여 그리 부르지 않는가. 어찌하여 생명 보험 회사들은 불멸의 존재들에게 사망 보험금을 지급하는가. 예순 세기를 헤아리는 둥근 세월 전에 죽은 저 옛 아담은 어떤 영원하고 미동도 없는 마비 속에, 어떤 죽음 같고 희망 없는 혼수상태 속에 여전히 누워 있는가. 우리가 형언할 수 없는 지복(至福) 속에 머물고 있다고 끝내 주장하면서도, 어찌하여 우리는 그들을 위해 위로받기를 여전히 거부하는가. 어찌하여 살아 있는 모든 자들은 죽은 모든 자들을 그토록 잠재우려 애쓰는가. 무덤 안에서 무언가 두드린다는 소문 하나만으로도 어찌하여 도시 하나가 통째로 공포에 떠는가. 이 모든 것에는 다 그럴 만한 의미가 있는 것이다.\n\n그러나 믿음은 자칼처럼 무덤 사이를 누비며 먹이를 구하고, 이런 죽은 의심들에서조차 가장 생생한 희망을 거두어들인다.\n\n낸터킷(Nantucket) 항해를 앞둔 전날 밤, 어떤 심정으로 내가 저 대리석 명판들을 바라보았는지, 그리고 어둡고 음울한 그날의 침침한 빛 속에서 나보다 앞서 떠나간 고래잡이들의 운명을 읽어 내렸는지는 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라. 그래, 이슈메일, 그 같은 운명이 네 것이 될 수도 있으리라. 그러나 어쩐 일인지 나는 다시 유쾌해졌다. 출항을 부추기는 즐거운 유인책에다, 승진의 더없이 좋은 기회까지 있는 듯하니 말이다. 그렇다, 박살 난 보트 한 척이 나를 약식 임명으로 불멸의 존재로 만들어 주리라. 그렇다, 이 고래잡이라는 일에는 죽음이 도사리고 있다. 한 인간을 말 한마디 못 할 만큼 순식간에 영원(Eternity) 속으로 혼란스레 처박아 넣는 죽음이. 그러나 그래서 어떻단 말인가? 생각건대 우리는 이 삶과 죽음의 문제를 크게 잘못 짚어 온 것이다. 생각건대 이 땅 위에서 사람들이 내 그림자라 부르는 것이야말로 나의 참된 실체다. 생각건대 영적인 것들을 바라볼 때 우리는, 물을 통해 해를 관찰하면서 그 두꺼운 물이 가장 엷은 공기라 여기는 굴(牡蠣)과 너무도 닮았다. 생각건대 내 육신은 더 나은 내 존재가 가라앉고 남은 찌꺼기일 따름이다. 사실 내 육신은 누구든 가져가고 싶은 자가 가져가라, 가져가라니까, 그것은 내가 아니다. 그러므로 낸터킷을 위하여 만세 삼창을. 그리고 박살 난 보트든 박살 난 육신이든 올 테면 언제든 오라, 내 영혼만은 제우스(Jove)라 한들 결코 박살 내지 못할 터이니.\n\n# CHAPTER 8. The Pulpit.\n\n내가 자리에 앉은 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 어딘가 위엄 있는 강건함을 지닌 한 남자가 들어왔다. 폭풍에 두들겨 맞던 문이 그를 맞아들이며 활짝 젖혀지자마자, 회중 전체가 그를 향해 던지는 재빠르고 주의 깊은 눈길이, 이 훌륭한 노인이 바로 그 목사임을 충분히 증명해 주었다. 그렇다, 그는 고래잡이들이 매플 신부(Father Mapple)라 부르는 그 유명한 인물이었으며, 그들 사이에서 대단한 인기를 누리고 있었다. 그는 젊은 시절 선원이자 작살잡이였으나, 지난 여러 해 동안은 자신의 삶을 성직에 바쳐 왔다. 지금 내가 쓰고 있는 이 시절에, 매플 신부는 건강한 노년의 강인한 겨울을 맞고 있었다. 그것은 두 번째로 꽃피는 청춘 속으로 녹아드는 듯한 그런 노년이었으니, 그의 주름 사이 골마다 새로이 피어나는 꽃의 은은한 광채가 어른거렸기 때문이다 — 이월의 눈 아래에서조차 살며시 고개를 내미는 봄의 푸르름 같았다. 그의 내력을 미리 들어 본 적 없는 이라면, 누구도 처음 매플 신부를 보고서 더없는 흥미를 느끼지 않을 수 없었으니, 그가 살아온 그 모험적인 바다 생활에서 비롯된, 성직자다움에 접붙여진 어떤 독특함이 그에게 깃들어 있었던 까닭이다. 그가 들어왔을 때 나는 그가 우산을 들지 않았음을, 그리고 분명 마차를 타고 오지도 않았음을 알아차렸다. 그의 방수모에서는 녹아내리는 진눈깨비가 줄줄 흘러내렸고, 두꺼운 항해용 천으로 지은 큼직한 외투는 빨아들인 물의 무게로 그를 바닥으로 끌어내릴 듯했기 때문이다. 그러나 모자와 외투와 덧신은 하나씩 벗겨져, 옆 모퉁이의 작은 공간에 걸렸다. 그렇게 단정한 차림이 된 그는, 조용히 설교단으로 다가갔다.\n\n대부분의 옛 양식 설교단이 그러하듯, 그것은 매우 높이 솟은 것이었으며, 그만한 높이까지 정규 계단을 놓는다면 바닥과 이루는 긴 경사 탓에 가뜩이나 비좁은 예배당 공간을 한층 옹색하게 만들 터였으므로, 건축가는 매플 신부의 의견을 따른 듯, 계단 없이 설교단을 완성하고, 그 대신 바다에서 보트를 타고 배에 오를 때 쓰는 것 같은 수직 측면 사다리를 놓았다. 어느 포경선 선장의 아내가 이 사다리에 쓰라고 붉은 털실로 짠 멋진 손잡이 밧줄 한 쌍을 예배당에 마련해 주었는데, 그 밧줄은 끝이 곱게 매듭지어지고 마호가니 빛깔로 물들여져 있어, 이 예배당이 어떤 곳인가를 헤아려 보면, 그 전체 장치가 결코 격에 맞지 않는다고는 할 수 없었다. 매플 신부는 사다리 발치에서 잠시 멈추어, 두 손으로 손잡이 밧줄의 장식 매듭을 움켜쥐고 위를 올려다보더니, 이윽고 참으로 선원다우면서도 경건함을 잃지 않은 능숙한 솜씨로, 한 손 한 손 번갈아 짚으며, 마치 제 배의 큰돛대 꼭대기에 오르듯 계단을 올라갔다.\n\n이 측면 사다리의 수직 부분은, 흔들리는 사다리들이 으레 그러하듯 천으로 감싼 밧줄로 되어 있었고, 다만 디딤대만 나무였으므로, 한 단마다 이음매가 있었다. 설교단을 처음 흘끗 보았을 때, 나는 이 이음매들이 배에서는 아무리 편리하다 한들 지금 이 경우에는 불필요해 보인다는 점을 놓치지 않았다. 매플 신부가 그 높이에 오른 뒤, 천천히 몸을 돌려 설교단 너머로 허리를 굽히고는, 한 단 한 단 신중하게 사다리를 끌어올려 마침내 그 전체를 안쪽에 들여놓고, 자신만의 작은 퀘벡 요새 안에 난공불락으로 들어앉으리라고는 미처 예상하지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n나는 한동안 곰곰이 생각해 보았으나, 그 까닭을 온전히 헤아릴 수 없었다. 매플 신부는 그 진실함과 거룩함으로 워낙 널리 명성을 떨치던 인물이라, 한낱 무대 위 속임수 따위로 세간의 이목을 끌려 한다고는 의심할 수 없었다. 아니다, 하고 나는 생각했다, 여기에는 분명 어떤 진지한 이유가 있을 터이며, 더 나아가 이는 눈에 보이지 않는 무언가를 상징하는 것이 틀림없다. 그렇다면 혹시, 그 육체적 격리의 행위를 통해, 그가 한동안 모든 바깥세상의 끈과 인연으로부터 자신의 영혼이 물러섬을 나타내려는 것은 아닐까? 그렇다, 말씀의 고기와 포도주로 그득히 채워진 하느님의 신실한 사람에게, 이 설교단은, 내가 보기에, 그 자체로 완결된 요새이니 — 성벽 안에 마르지 않는 샘물을 품은 드높은 에렌브라이트슈타인이다.\n\n그러나 측면 사다리만이 이곳에서 목사의 옛 항해 생활에서 빌려 온 유일한 기이한 특징은 아니었다. 설교단 양옆의 대리석 기념비 사이로, 그 뒤편을 이루는 벽에는 커다란 그림 한 폭이 걸려 있었는데, 검은 바위와 새하얀 파도로 이루어진 바람 받는 해안을 끼고 무시무시한 폭풍과 맞서 싸우는 늠름한 배 한 척을 그린 것이었다. 그러나 흩날리는 물보라와 검게 뒤채는 구름 위 높은 곳에는, 햇살로 이루어진 작은 섬 하나가 떠 있었고, 그곳에서 한 천사의 얼굴이 환히 빛을 발하고 있었다. 그리고 이 밝은 얼굴은 요동치는 배의 갑판 위로 또렷한 빛의 점 하나를 드리웠으니, 넬슨이 쓰러진 자리에 지금도 박혀 있는 빅토리호 갑판 널의 저 은판과도 같았다. \"아, 고귀한 배여,\" 천사는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다, \"맞서 나아가라, 맞서 나아가라, 그대 고귀한 배여, 키를 굳세게 잡으라. 보라! 해가 뚫고 나오고 있으니, 구름이 걷히고 있으니 — 더없이 맑은 창공이 곧 다가오리라.\"\n\n설교단 자체에도, 사다리와 그림을 빚어낸 그 똑같은 바다의 취향이 깃든 흔적이 없지 않았다. 널판으로 짠 그 정면은 배의 불룩한 이물을 닮아 있었고, 성경은 배의 바이올린머리 모양 뱃부리를 본떠 만든, 앞으로 튀어나온 두루마리 장식 위에 놓여 있었다.\n\n이보다 더 의미로 가득 찬 것이 또 있겠는가? — 설교단이야말로 늘 이 땅의 가장 앞선 부분이며, 나머지 모든 것은 그 뒤를 따라온다. 설교단은 세상을 이끈다. 그곳에서 하느님의 날쌘 진노의 폭풍이 가장 먼저 발견되고, 이물이 그 최초의 맹공을 견뎌 내야 한다. 그곳에서 순풍이든 역풍이든 바람을 다스리는 하느님께 순조로운 바람을 비는 기도가 가장 먼저 올려진다. 그렇다, 세상은 떠나가는 항해 중인 한 척의 배이지 아직 완수된 항해가 아니며, 설교단은 그 뱃머리다.\n\n# CHAPTER 9. The Sermon.\n\n매플 신부가 자리에서 일어나, 거드름 없는 권위가 담긴 부드러운 목소리로 흩어져 앉은 사람들에게 한곳으로 모이라고 일렀다. \"우현 통로에 앉은 분들, 거기! 좌현 쪽으로 옮기시오 — 좌현 통로 분들은 우현으로! 한가운데로! 한가운데로 모이시오!\"\n\n벤치들 사이로 묵직한 바다용 장화들이 낮게 우르릉거리는 소리가 일었고, 그보다 더 가벼운 여인네들의 신발 끄는 소리가 뒤따르더니, 다시금 사방이 고요해졌고, 모든 눈길이 설교자에게로 쏠렸다.\n\n그는 잠시 멈추었다. 그러고는 설교단의 뱃머리 쪽에 무릎을 꿇고, 크고 갈색인 두 손을 가슴 위로 포개고, 감은 눈을 위로 들어 올린 채, 어찌나 깊고 경건한 기도를 올리는지, 마치 바다 밑바닥에 무릎 꿇고 기도하는 사람처럼 보였다.\n\n이 기도가 끝나자, 안개 속에서 바다에 침몰해 가는 배 안에서 끊임없이 울리는 종소리처럼 길게 늘어진 엄숙한 어조로 — 바로 그런 어조로 그는 다음의 찬송가를 읽기 시작했다. 그러나 마지막 연들에 이르러 그 태도를 바꾸어, 종소리 울려 퍼지듯 환희와 기쁨을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"고래의 갈빗대와 공포가, 음울한 어둠으로 나를 둘러쳤고, 하느님의 햇살 어린 물결이 모두 곁을 지나 굴러가는 동안, 나를 깊디깊은 멸망으로 끌어내렸네.\n\n\"나는 지옥의 벌어진 아가리를 보았네, 그 안에 끝없는 고통과 슬픔이 있었으니, 겪어 본 자만이 말할 수 있는 것 — 오, 나는 절망 속으로 곤두박질치고 있었네.\n\n\"검은 고난 속에서 나는 내 하느님을 불렀네, 그분이 내 것이라 차마 믿기 어려운 때에, 그분은 내 탄식에 귀를 기울이셨고 — 더는 고래가 나를 가두지 못했네.\n\n\"그분은 빛나는 돌고래에 실린 듯, 나를 구하러 쏜살같이 날아오셨네. 두려우면서도, 번개처럼 밝게 빛났네, 나를 건지신 하느님의 얼굴이.\n\n\"내 노래는 영원토록 기록하리라, 그 무서웠던, 그 기뻤던 순간을. 나는 내 하느님께 영광을 돌리노니, 모든 자비와 권능이 그분의 것일지라.\"\n\n거의 모든 사람이 이 찬송에 함께 목소리를 보탰고, 그 소리는 폭풍의 울부짖음을 넘어 드높이 솟아올랐다. 짧은 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 설교자는 성경의 책장을 천천히 넘기더니, 마침내 알맞은 쪽에 손을 펴 짚으며 말했다. \"사랑하는 선원 여러분, 요나서 1장의 마지막 구절을 단단히 새기시오 — '하느님께서 큰 물고기를 예비하사 요나를 삼키게 하셨더라.'\"\n\n\"선원 여러분, 단 네 장 — 네 가닥의 이야기 — 으로 이루어진 이 책은 성경이라는 그 거대한 밧줄 가운데 가장 가느다란 가닥 중 하나입니다. 그러나 요나의 깊은 측심줄이 영혼의 그 얼마나 깊은 곳을 재어 내는지요! 이 예언자가 우리에게 주는 가르침은 또 얼마나 깊은 뜻을 품고 있는지요! 물고기 뱃속에서 부른 저 노래는 얼마나 고귀한 것입니까! 얼마나 물결처럼 일렁이며 거세고도 장엄합니까! 우리는 홍수가 우리 머리 위로 밀어닥치는 것을 느낍니다. 우리는 그와 함께 해초 가득한 바다 밑바닥까지 측심줄을 내려 봅니다. 해초와 바다의 온갖 진흙이 우리를 둘러쌉니다! 그런데 요나서가 가르치는 이 교훈이란 대체 무엇입니까? 선원 여러분, 그것은 두 가닥으로 꼬인 교훈입니다. 죄지은 인간으로서 우리 모두에게 주는 교훈이요, 살아 계신 하느님의 키잡이로서 나에게 주는 교훈입니다. 죄지은 인간으로서, 이것은 우리 모두에게 주는 교훈이니, 그것은 곧 죄와 완고함과 갑작스레 깨어난 두려움과 신속한 징벌과 회개와 기도, 그리고 마침내 요나가 얻은 구원과 기쁨에 관한 이야기이기 때문입니다. 사람들 가운데 모든 죄인이 그러하듯, 아미타이의 이 아들이 지은 죄는 하느님의 명령을 제멋대로 거역한 데 있었으니 — 그 명령이 무엇이었는지, 또 어떻게 전해졌는지는 지금 따지지 맙시다 — 그는 그것을 따르기 힘든 명령이라 여겼습니다. 그러나 하느님께서 우리에게 행하라 하시는 모든 일은 우리가 행하기에 힘든 법입니다 — 이것을 기억하십시오 — 그러므로 그분은 우리를 설득하려 애쓰기보다 명령을 내리시는 일이 더 잦은 것입니다. 그리고 우리가 하느님께 순종하려면, 우리 자신에게는 불복해야 합니다. 그리고 바로 이 자기 자신에게 불복하는 일 가운데, 하느님께 순종하는 일의 어려움이 깃들어 있는 것입니다.\n\n\"이 불복종의 죄를 품은 채, 요나는 더 나아가 하느님으로부터 달아나려 함으로써 그분을 조롱합니다. 그는 사람이 만든 배가 자기를 하느님이 다스리지 않는 나라들, 오직 이 땅의 선장들만이 다스리는 곳으로 데려다 주리라 생각합니다. 그는 욥바의 부두 언저리를 몰래 어슬렁거리며, 다시스로 가는 배를 찾습니다. 어쩌면 여기에 지금껏 누구도 주목하지 않은 어떤 뜻이 숨어 있는지도 모릅니다. 모든 정황으로 보아 다시스는 오늘날의 카디스 말고는 다른 도시일 수 없었습니다. 그것이 학자들의 견해입니다. 그런데 선원 여러분, 카디스는 어디에 있습니까? 카디스는 스페인에 있으니, 욥바에서 바닷길로 가자면, 대서양이 아직 거의 알려지지 않은 바다이던 저 옛 시절에 요나가 항해할 수 있었을 가장 먼 거리에 있습니다. 선원 여러분, 욥바 곧 오늘날의 야파는 지중해에서 가장 동쪽 해안인 시리아 쪽에 있고, 다시스 곧 카디스는 그곳에서 서쪽으로 이천 마일도 넘게 떨어진, 지브롤터 해협 바로 바깥에 있기 때문입니다. 그러니 선원 여러분, 요나가 세상 끝까지 하느님으로부터 달아나려 했음을 알지 못하겠습니까? 가련한 인간이여! 오! 더없이 경멸스럽고 온갖 조롱을 받아 마땅한 자, 모자를 푹 눌러쓰고 죄지은 눈빛으로 자기 하느님을 피해 몰래 다니며, 바다를 건너려 서두르는 비열한 강도처럼 선박들 사이를 살금살금 헤매는 자입니다. 그 행색이 어찌나 어수선하고 스스로를 단죄하는 듯하던지, 그 시절에 순경이 있었다면, 요나는 무언가 잘못이 있으리라는 의심만으로도 갑판에 발을 들여놓기도 전에 체포되었을 것입니다. 그가 도망자임이 얼마나 빤히 드러나는지요! 짐 보따리도, 모자 상자도, 여행 가방도, 융단 가방도 없고 — 부두까지 작별 인사를 건네며 그를 배웅하는 벗 하나 없습니다. 마침내 한참을 이리저리 피해 다니며 찾은 끝에, 그는 마지막 화물을 실어 들이는 다시스행 배를 발견합니다. 그리고 그가 선실에서 선장을 만나려고 배에 오르자, 선원들은 모두 잠시 짐 싣는 일을 멈추고 그 낯선 자의 사악한 눈빛을 살핍니다. 요나도 이를 알아챕니다. 그러나 아무리 태연하고 자신만만한 척하려 애써도 헛일이요, 그 비참한 미소를 지어 보려 해도 소용이 없습니다. 뱃사람들은 강렬한 직감으로, 저자가 결코 무고한 자일 리 없다고 확신합니다. 그들은 장난스러우면서도 자못 진지한 투로 서로 귓속말을 주고받습니다 — '잭, 저자는 과부 재산을 털었어.' 혹은 '조, 저자를 잘 봐 둬. 중혼한 놈이야.' 혹은 '해리, 내 짐작엔 저자가 옛 고모라에서 감옥을 깨고 나온 간음한 자거나, 아니면 소돔에서 도망친 살인자들 중 하나일걸.' 또 다른 한 사람은 배가 매여 있는 부두의 말뚝에 붙은 방을 읽으러 달려가는데, 거기에는 어떤 친부 살해범을 붙잡는 자에게 금화 오백 닢을 준다는 내용과 그 자의 인상착의가 적혀 있습니다. 그는 그것을 읽고는, 요나와 방을 번갈아 봅니다. 그러는 사이 동정심 어린 그의 동료들은 이제 요나를 에워싸고, 그에게 손을 대려 합니다. 겁에 질린 요나는 부들부들 떨며, 온 담력을 얼굴에 끌어모으지만, 그럴수록 더욱더 겁쟁이로만 보일 뿐입니다. 그는 자기가 의심받고 있음을 시인하려 하지 않습니다. 그러나 그것 자체가 더욱 강한 의심을 삽니다. 그래서 그는 어떻게든 그 상황을 모면해 보려 애쓰고, 마침내 뱃사람들이 그가 방에 적힌 그 사람이 아님을 알아내자, 그를 그냥 지나가게 두니, 그는 선실로 내려갑니다.\n\n\"'거기 누구요?' 분주한 책상에서 세관에 낼 서류를 서둘러 작성하던 선장이 외칩니다 — '거기 누구요?' 오! 그 악의 없는 물음이 요나를 얼마나 갈가리 찢어 놓는지요! 그 순간 그는 다시 달아나려고 거의 몸을 돌릴 뻔합니다. 그러나 마음을 다잡습니다. '이 배로 다시스까지 가는 뱃길을 청합니다. 언제 떠나시오?' 그때까지 분주한 선장은 그 사람이 이제 자기 앞에 서 있는데도 요나를 올려다보지 않았으나, 그 공허한 목소리를 듣자마자 날카로운 눈초리를 던집니다. '다음 밀물 때 떠나오.' 마침내 그가 여전히 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 느릿느릿 대답합니다. '그보다 더 빨리는 안 되오, 선장?' — '뱃삯 내고 떠나는 점잖은 사람이라면 그쯤이면 충분히 빠르지요.' 하! 요나여, 그것이 또 한 번의 비수로구나. 그러나 그는 재빨리 선장의 그 추궁의 냄새를 다른 데로 돌립니다. '같이 떠나겠소' — 그가 말합니다 — '뱃삯은 얼마요? 지금 치르겠소.' 선원 여러분, 이 이야기에서 결코 흘려보아서는 안 될 일이라는 듯이, '그가 그 삯을 치렀다'고 배가 떠나기에 앞서 특별히 기록되어 있기 때문입니다. 그리고 앞뒤 맥락과 더불어 보면, 이 한 구절은 깊은 뜻으로 가득 차 있습니다.\n\n\"자, 선원 여러분, 요나의 선장은 누구에게서든 죄를 알아채는 분별력을 지녔으되, 그 탐욕 탓에 한 푼 없는 자에게서만 그 죄를 들춰내는 자였습니다. 선원 여러분, 이 세상에서는 제 길값을 치르는 죄는 통행증도 없이 자유로이 다닐 수 있으나, 미덕은 가난한 자라면 모든 국경에서 가로막힙니다. 그래서 요나의 선장은 그를 드러내 놓고 판단하기에 앞서, 먼저 요나의 지갑 길이를 시험해 보려 합니다. 그는 평소의 세 배에 달하는 삯을 요구하고, 요나는 이에 응합니다. 그러자 선장은 요나가 도망자임을 알아챕니다. 그러나 동시에, 그 뒤를 황금으로 깔아 주는 도주라면 도와주기로 마음먹습니다. 그런데도 요나가 막상 지갑을 꺼내자, 신중한 의심이 여전히 선장을 괴롭힙니다. 그는 위조 화폐가 섞였나 싶어 동전을 하나하나 울려 봅니다. '어쨌든 위조범은 아니로군' 하고 그가 중얼거리고는, 요나의 뱃삯이 치러진 것으로 처리됩니다. '내 선실을 일러 주시오, 선장' 하고 이제 요나가 말합니다. '여행에 지쳤소. 잠을 좀 자야겠소.' '그래 보이는구려' 하고 선장이 말합니다. '저기가 당신 방이오.' 요나가 들어가서 문을 잠그려 하지만, 자물쇠에 열쇠가 없습니다. 그가 그곳에서 어리석게 더듬거리는 소리를 듣고는, 선장이 혼자 나직이 웃으며, 죄수들의 감방 문은 결코 안에서 잠그도록 허락되지 않는다는 식으로 무어라 중얼거립니다. 옷도 다 입은 채, 먼지투성이 그대로, 요나는 자기 침상에 몸을 던지고는, 그 작은 선실 천장이 거의 자기 이마에 닿을 듯한 것을 알아챕니다. 공기는 답답하고, 요나는 숨을 헐떡입니다. 그러더니 그 비좁은 구멍 속에서, 그것도 배의 흘수선 아래로 가라앉은 그곳에서, 요나는 고래가 그 내장의 가장 작은 방 안에 자기를 가둘 그 숨 막히는 순간을 미리 알리는 전조를 느낍니다.\n\n\"제 축을 따라 뱃전에 고정된 채, 흔들리는 등불 하나가 요나의 방에서 가볍게 흔들립니다. 그리고 마지막으로 실어 들인 짐짝들의 무게로 배가 부두 쪽으로 기울자, 등불은 불꽃까지 통째로, 비록 미세하게 흔들리기는 하나, 방을 기준으로 보면 한결같이 비스듬한 각도를 유지합니다. 사실 등불 자체는 한 치의 오차도 없이 곧건만, 다만 그것이 매달린 그 거짓되고 기만적인 수평면들을 도리어 또렷이 드러내 보일 뿐입니다. 그 등불이 요나를 놀라게 하고 겁먹게 합니다. 침상에 누운 채 그의 괴로운 눈이 방 안을 굴러다니지만, 여기까지 용케 달아나 온 이 도망자는 그 불안한 시선을 둘 안식처를 찾지 못합니다. 그러나 그 등불의 저 모순이 점점 더 그를 소름 끼치게 합니다. 바닥도, 천장도, 뱃전도, 모두가 비뚜름합니다. '오! 내 양심이 내 안에 이렇게 매달려 있구나!' 하고 그가 신음합니다. '곧장 위를 향하여, 그렇게 타오르건만, 내 영혼의 방들은 모조리 비뚤어져 있구나!'\n\n\"밤새도록 술 취해 흥청거린 끝에, 여전히 비틀거리면서도 양심이 그를 콕콕 찌르는 채로 잠자리로 기어드는 사람처럼 — 로마 경주마가 날뛸수록 그 강철 미늘이 더욱더 살을 파고드는 것처럼 — 그 비참한 처지에서 발작이 지나가기만을 빌며, 하느님께 차라리 자기를 없애 달라 기도하면서 어지러운 고뇌 속에 자꾸만 몸을 뒤척이는 사람처럼, 그러다 마침내 그 비탄의 소용돌이 한가운데서 깊은 혼곤함이 그를 덮쳐 오는 것을 느끼는 사람처럼 — 피를 흘리며 죽어 가는 사람을 덮치는 그 혼곤함처럼, 양심이야말로 그 상처요, 그것을 멎게 할 무엇도 없으니 — 그렇게, 요나는 침상에서 모진 몸부림 끝에, 그 엄청난 비참함의 무게에 짓눌려, 익사하듯 잠 속으로 끌려 내려갑니다.\n\n\"그리고 이제 밀물 때가 왔습니다. 배는 닻줄을 풀고, 인적 없는 부두로부터 그 다시스행 배는 환송하는 이 하나 없이, 온통 기우뚱거리며 바다로 미끄러져 나갑니다. 벗들이여, 그 배야말로 기록에 남은 최초의 밀항선이었습니다! 그 밀수품은 바로 요나였지요. 그러나 바다가 반항합니다. 바다는 그 사악한 짐을 견디려 하지 않습니다. 무시무시한 폭풍이 닥쳐오고, 배는 곧 부서질 듯합니다. 그러나 이제 갑판장이 배를 가볍게 하려고 모든 선원을 불러 모을 때, 상자며 짐짝이며 항아리가 뱃전 너머로 요란하게 던져질 때, 바람이 비명을 지르고 사람들이 고함을 치며, 요나의 머리 바로 위로 갑판 널빤지마다 짓밟는 발길에 천둥처럼 울려 댈 때 — 이 모든 미쳐 날뛰는 아수라장 속에서도, 요나는 그 끔찍한 잠을 자고 있습니다. 그는 검은 하늘도 미쳐 날뛰는 바다도 보지 못하고, 휘청거리는 선재도 느끼지 못하며, 바로 지금 입을 떡 벌린 채 그를 쫓아 바다를 가르며 다가오는 저 거대한 고래의 먼 돌진 소리도 거의 듣지도, 아랑곳하지도 않습니다. 그렇습니다, 선원 여러분, 요나는 배의 옆구리 안쪽으로 — 내가 받아들이기로는 선실 안의 침상으로 — 내려가, 깊이 잠들어 있었던 것입니다. 그러나 겁에 질린 선장이 그에게 다가와, 송장처럼 잠든 그의 귀에 대고 소리칩니다. '오, 잠든 자여, 어찌하여 이러고 있느냐! 일어나라!' 그 무시무시한 외침에 혼수에서 화들짝 깨어난 요나는 비틀비틀 일어나, 휘청거리며 갑판으로 나가 돛 받침줄 하나를 움켜쥐고 바다를 내다봅니다. 그러나 바로 그 순간, 뱃전 위로 뛰어오른 표범 같은 큰 물결이 그를 덮칩니다. 이렇게 파도가 잇따라 배 안으로 뛰어들지만, 빠져나갈 길을 찾지 못해 뱃머리부터 고물까지 으르렁대며 내달리니, 뱃사람들은 아직 물 위에 떠 있는 채로도 익사할 지경에 이릅니다. 그리고 그럴 때마다, 머리 위 어둠의 가파른 골짜기에서 새하얀 달이 그 겁먹은 얼굴을 내밀면, 넋이 나간 요나는 솟구쳐 오른 제1사장(이물 돛대)이 하늘 높이 곧추 솟았다가도 이내 다시 저 고통스러운 심연을 향해 곤두박질치는 것을 봅니다.\n\n\"공포 위에 또 공포가 그의 영혼을 가로질러 소리치며 내달립니다. 그 온갖 움츠린 꼴로 인해, 하느님으로부터 도망친 자임이 이제 너무도 빤히 드러납니다. 뱃사람들은 그를 눈여겨봅니다. 그를 향한 의심은 점점 더 짙어지고, 마침내 진상을 온전히 가려내고자, 그 모든 일을 드높은 하늘에 맡겨, 이 거대한 폭풍이 누구 탓으로 그들에게 닥쳤는지 알아보려고 제비를 뽑기 시작합니다. 제비는 요나에게 떨어집니다. 그것이 밝혀지자, 그들은 얼마나 사납게 그를 에워싸고 질문을 퍼붓는지요. '네 직업이 무엇이냐? 어디서 왔느냐? 네 나라는? 어느 백성이냐?' 그런데 선원 여러분, 이제 가련한 요나의 행동을 눈여겨보십시오. 다급한 뱃사람들은 다만 그가 누구이며 어디서 왔는지를 물을 뿐인데, 그들은 그 물음에 대한 답을 받을 뿐 아니라, 그들이 묻지도 않은 또 다른 물음에 대한 답까지 받게 됩니다. 그러나 그 청하지도 않은 답은, 그를 짓누르는 하느님의 무거운 손에 떠밀려 요나에게서 억지로 터져 나온 것입니다.\n\n\"'나는 히브리 사람이오' 하고 그가 외칩니다 — 그러고는 — '나는 바다와 마른 땅을 지으신 하늘의 주 하느님을 경외하오!' 요나여, 그분을 경외한다고? 그렇다, 그때야말로 너는 주 하느님을 경외함이 마땅하렷다! 곧바로 그는 이제 온전한 고백을 이어 갑니다. 그러자 뱃사람들은 점점 더 소름이 돋지만, 그래도 여전히 가엾이 여깁니다. 요나가 — 자기가 받아 마땅한 어둠을 너무도 잘 알았기에 아직 하느님께 자비를 빌지도 못한 채 — 가련한 요나가, 자기를 들어 바다에 던지라고, 이 거대한 폭풍이 바로 자기 탓으로 그들에게 닥친 것임을 알기에 그렇게 그들에게 외칠 때, 그들은 자비롭게도 그에게서 돌아서서, 다른 방도로 배를 구하려 애씁니다. 그러나 모두 헛일입니다. 분노한 강풍이 더욱 거세게 울부짖습니다. 그러자 그들은 한 손을 하느님께 간구하듯 치켜들고, 다른 손으로는 마지못한 것만도 아닌 손길로 요나를 붙잡습니다.\n\n\"그리고 이제 보십시오, 요나가 닻처럼 들려 올려져 바다에 던져지는 것을. 그러자 곧바로 동쪽으로부터 기름처럼 매끄러운 고요가 떠 와, 요나가 그 강풍을 안고 가라앉아 뒤에 잔잔한 물을 남기자, 바다가 잠잠해집니다. 그는 그 누구도 다스리지 못할 격동의 소용돌이치는 한복판으로 내려가니, 자기를 기다리며 떡 벌린 아가리 속으로 부글부글 끓으며 떨어져 드는 그 순간조차 거의 알아채지 못합니다. 그리고 고래는 그 모든 상아 같은 이빨을, 마치 수많은 흰 빗장처럼, 자기 감옥 위로 탁 다물어 버립니다. 그러자 요나는 물고기 뱃속에서 주께 기도했습니다. 그러나 그 기도를 눈여겨보고, 묵직한 교훈을 배우십시오. 죄인이건만, 요나는 곧장 구원해 달라고 울며 통곡하지 않습니다. 그는 자기가 받는 그 무서운 징벌이 마땅한 것임을 느낍니다. 그는 자기 구원을 온전히 하느님께 맡긴 채, 다만 이것으로 만족합니다. 곧, 그 온갖 아픔과 고통에도 불구하고, 그래도 여전히 주의 거룩한 성전을 향해 바라보겠노라는 것입니다. 그리고 선원 여러분, 바로 여기에 참되고 신실한 회개가 있습니다. 용서를 구하느라 떠들썩한 것이 아니라, 징벌을 감사히 받아들이는 것입니다. 요나의 이러한 처신이 하느님께 얼마나 흡족했는지는, 그가 끝내 바다와 고래로부터 구원받은 일에서 드러납니다. 선원 여러분, 나는 요나를 그 죄를 본받으라고 여러분 앞에 세우는 것이 아니라, 회개의 본보기로서 여러분 앞에 세우는 것입니다. 죄짓지 마십시오. 그러나 만일 죄를 짓거든, 요나처럼 그것을 회개하도록 마음 쓰십시오.\"\n\n그가 이 말들을 하고 있는 동안, 바깥에서 비스듬히 몰아치며 비명을 지르는 폭풍의 울부짖음이 이 설교자에게 새로운 힘을 더해 주는 듯했으니, 요나의 바다 폭풍을 그려 낼 때면 그 자신이 폭풍에 내던져진 듯 보였다. 그의 깊은 가슴팍은 너울이 일렁이듯 들썩였고, 내던지듯 휘젓는 두 팔은 서로 다투는 자연의 원소들이 일하는 것만 같았으며, 거무스름한 이마에서 굴러 나오는 천둥과 눈에서 튀어 오르는 빛은, 소박한 청중 모두로 하여금 그들에게는 생소한 어떤 빠른 두려움을 품고 그를 바라보게 했다.\n\n이제 그의 표정에 잠시 잔잔함이 깃들었으니, 그가 말없이 다시 한번 성경의 책장을 넘기더니, 마침내 눈을 감은 채 잠시 미동도 없이 서서, 하느님과 자기 자신과 더불어 교감하는 듯 보였다.\n\n그러나 그는 다시금 사람들 쪽으로 몸을 기울이더니, 머리를 깊이 숙이고, 더없이 깊으면서도 가장 사내다운 겸손의 빛을 띤 채, 이런 말을 했다.\n\n\"선원 여러분, 하느님께서는 여러분에게는 한 손만을 얹으셨으나, 나에게는 두 손을 다 누르고 계십니다. 나는 나에게 허락된 그 흐릿한 빛으로나마, 요나가 모든 죄인에게 가르치는 그 교훈을 여러분에게 읽어 드렸습니다. 그러므로 여러분에게도, 그러나 나에게는 더더욱 그러하니, 나야말로 여러분보다 더 큰 죄인이기 때문입니다. 그러니 이제 나는 이 돛대 꼭대기에서 기꺼이 내려와, 여러분이 앉은 저 승강구 위에 함께 앉아, 여러분이 귀 기울이듯 귀를 기울이며, 여러분 가운데 누군가가 나에게 저 또 하나의, 한층 더 무서운 교훈을 읽어 주기를 얼마나 바라는지 모릅니다 — 살아 계신 하느님의 키잡이인 나에게 요나가 가르치는 그 교훈을 말입니다. 기름 부음 받은 키잡이 예언자, 곧 참된 것을 전하는 자로서, 주께 명을 받아 그 달갑잖은 진리를 사악한 니네베의 귀에 울려 퍼뜨려야 했건만, 요나는 자기가 불러일으킬 적의에 겁을 먹고, 제 사명에서 달아나, 욥바에서 배를 탐으로써 자기 의무와 자기 하느님을 피하려 했던 것입니다. 그러나 하느님은 어디에나 계십니다. 그는 다시스에 끝내 닿지 못했습니다. 우리가 보았듯이, 하느님은 고래의 모습으로 그에게 닥쳐와 그를 살아 있는 멸망의 심연으로 삼켜 버리시고, 재빠른 비스듬한 움직임으로 그를 '바다 한가운데로' 끌고 가셨으니, 거기서 소용돌이치는 심연이 그를 만 길 아래로 빨아들였고, '바닷풀이 그의 머리를 휘감았으며', 비탄으로 가득 찬 온 물의 세계가 그의 위로 굴러 넘쳤습니다. 그러나 그때에도, 어떤 측심추도 닿지 못할 곳에서 — '지옥의 뱃속에서' — 고래가 대양의 가장 깊은 뼈대에 닿았을 그때조차도, 하느님은 그 삼켜진 채 회개하는 예언자가 부르짖을 때 그 소리를 들으셨습니다. 그러자 하느님이 물고기에게 말씀하셨고, 바다의 몸서리치는 차가움과 어둠으로부터, 고래가 따뜻하고 정겨운 햇빛을 향해, 그리고 공기와 땅의 온갖 기쁨을 향해 솟구쳐 올라, '요나를 마른 땅에 토해 냈습니다.' 그때 주의 말씀이 두 번째로 임했고, 멍들고 두들겨 맞은 요나는 — 그 귀가 두 개의 소라껍데기처럼 여전히 무수히 바다를 웅얼대는 가운데 — 전능하신 분의 분부를 행했습니다. 그런데 그것이 무엇이었습니까, 선원 여러분? 거짓의 면전에 대고 진리를 설교하는 것! 바로 그것이었습니다!\n\n\"선원 여러분, 이것이, 이것이 바로 저 또 하나의 교훈입니다. 그리고 살아 계신 하느님의 키잡이로서 이를 가벼이 여기는 자에게 화가 있을지어다. 이 세상이 복음의 의무로부터 꾀어내는 그 자에게 화가 있을지어다! 하느님께서 물을 휘저어 강풍으로 끓이셨는데도 그 물 위에 기름을 부으려 하는 자에게 화가 있을지어다! 소름 끼치게 하기보다 비위를 맞추려 드는 자에게 화가 있을지어다! 선함보다 제 명성을 더 소중히 여기는 자에게 화가 있을지어다! 이 세상에서 불명예를 자청하지 않는 자에게 화가 있을지어다! 거짓됨이 곧 구원이 되는 한이 있더라도, 참되려 하지 않는 자에게 화가 있을지어다! 그렇습니다, 위대한 키잡이 바울이 말했듯이, 남들에게 설교하면서 정작 자기 자신은 버림받은 자가 되는 그 자에게 화가 있을지어다!\"\n\n그는 잠시 풀썩 주저앉아 제정신을 놓은 듯하더니, 다시 그들에게 얼굴을 들어, 두 눈에 깊은 기쁨을 내보이며, 천상의 열정으로 외쳤다 — \"그러나 오! 선원 여러분! 모든 화의 우현 쪽에는, 확실한 기쁨이 하나 있습니다. 그리고 그 기쁨의 꼭대기는 그 화의 밑바닥이 깊은 것보다 더 높습니다. 대장 돛대 꼭대기가 용골 받침대의 낮음보다 더 높지 않습니까? 기쁨은 그에게 있습니다 — 저 멀고도 드높은, 그리고 안으로 깊은 기쁨은 — 이 땅의 오만한 신들과 사령관들에 맞서, 언제나 굽힐 줄 모르는 제 자신으로 우뚝 서는 그에게 있습니다. 기쁨은, 이 비열하고 음흉한 세상이라는 배가 그의 발밑에서 가라앉아 버렸을 때에도, 여전히 그를 떠받쳐 주는 튼튼한 두 팔을 지닌 그에게 있습니다. 기쁨은, 진리에 있어서는 한 치도 봐주지 않으며, 비록 원로원 의원들과 재판관들의 법복 자락 밑에서 그것을 끄집어내야 할지라도 모든 죄를 죽이고 불사르고 부수어 버리는 그에게 있습니다. 기쁨은, 곧 가장 높은 돛대 꼭대기의 기쁨은, 주 하느님 그분 말고는 그 어떤 법도 주인도 인정하지 않으며, 오직 하늘에만 충성하는 그에게 있습니다. 기쁨은, 거세게 날뛰는 군중이라는 바다의 그 큰 물결의 온갖 파도로도 결코 흔들어 놓을 수 없는, 만세반석이라는 저 든든한 용골 위에 굳건한 그에게 있습니다. 그리고 영원한 기쁨과 감미로움은, 마침내 누우러 와서, 마지막 숨결로 이렇게 말할 수 있는 그의 것이 되리니 — 오 아버지여! — 무엇보다 당신의 채찍으로써 제가 알게 된 분이여 — 죽을 몸이든 죽지 않을 몸이든, 저 여기서 죽나이다. 저는 이 세상의 것이 되기보다, 또 제 자신의 것이 되기보다, 당신의 것이 되고자 애써 왔나이다. 그러나 이것조차 아무것도 아니니, 영원은 당신께 맡기나이다. 한낱 인간이 무엇이기에 제 하느님의 생애만큼 길이 살기를 바라겠나이까?\"\n\n그는 더는 말하지 않고, 다만 천천히 손을 저어 축복을 내린 뒤, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고, 그렇게 무릎 꿇은 채로 머물러 있었으니, 사람들이 모두 떠나가고 그 자리에 그 혼자만 남을 때까지 그러했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 10. A Bosom Friend.\n\n예배당에서 스파우터 여관으로 돌아와 보니 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 혼자 있었다. 그는 축도가 끝나기도 전에 한참 일찍 예배당을 떠나온 것이었다. 그는 난로 앞 벤치에 앉아 두 발을 화덕 위에 올려놓고, 한 손으로는 그 작은 흑인 우상을 얼굴 가까이 바싹 들이댄 채, 그 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 잭나이프로 코를 살살 깎아 내고 있었다. 그러면서 그는 이교도 특유의 곡조를 혼자 흥얼거렸다.\n\n그러나 이제 방해를 받자 그는 그 상을 치워 두었다. 곧이어 그는 탁자로 가서 거기 놓인 커다란 책 한 권을 집어 들고, 그것을 무릎 위에 올려놓더니 신중하고 규칙적인 손놀림으로 책장을 세기 시작했다. 쉰 장째에 이를 때마다—내가 보기엔 그랬다—잠시 멈추고는 멍하니 사방을 둘러보며, 길게 끌어 올리는 듯한 경탄의 휘파람을 그르렁그르렁 내뱉었다. 그러고는 다시 다음 쉰 장을 세기 시작하는데, 매번 처음 하나부터 다시 세는 듯했다. 마치 쉰까지밖에는 셀 줄 모르며, 그렇게 많은 쉰 묶음이 한데 모여 있는 것을 발견했을 때라야 비로소 책장의 엄청난 수에 대한 그의 경탄이 솟아나는 것 같았다.\n\n나는 자못 흥미를 느끼며 앉아서 그를 지켜보았다. 비록 그가 야만인이었고, 얼굴은—적어도 내 취향에는—흉측하게 일그러져 있었지만, 그 용모에는 결코 거슬리지 않는 무언가가 깃들어 있었다. 영혼은 감출 수가 없는 법이다. 그 모든 기괴한 문신 너머로, 나는 소박하고 정직한 마음의 자취를 본 듯했다. 그리고 그 크고 깊은 눈, 불길처럼 검고 담대한 그 눈에는 천 마리 악마라도 맞설 만한 정신의 징표가 어려 있는 듯했다. 게다가 이 모든 것 위에, 그 이교도에게는 어떤 고결한 기품이 있어서, 그의 거친 외양으로도 그것을 온전히 망가뜨릴 수는 없었다. 그는 한 번도 굽실거려 본 적 없고, 빚쟁이를 두어 본 적도 없는 사람처럼 보였다. 또한 그의 머리가 빡빡 깎인 까닭에 이마가 한결 시원하고 환하게 드러나, 본래보다 더 넓어 보이는 것인지, 그것은 내가 감히 단정하지 않겠다. 그러나 그의 머리가 골상학적으로 빼어난 두상임은 분명했다. 우습게 들릴지 모르나, 그것은 흔히 보는 흉상 속 워싱턴(Washington) 장군의 머리를 떠올리게 했다. 똑같이 눈썹 위에서부터 길고 규칙적으로 단계를 이루며 뒤로 물러나는 경사를 지녔는데, 그 눈썹 역시 매우 도드라져, 마치 꼭대기에 나무가 빽빽이 우거진 두 개의 긴 곶 같았다. 퀴케그는 식인종 식으로 발현된 조지 워싱턴(George Washington)이었다.\n\n내가 이렇게 그를 면밀히 살피며, 그러는 동안 한편으로는 창문 너머로 폭풍우를 내다보는 척하고 있었으나, 그는 내 존재를 전혀 개의치 않았으며, 단 한 번 곁눈질조차 하느라 수고하지 않았다. 다만 그 신기한 책의 책장을 세는 일에 온전히 몰두한 듯 보였다. 우리가 전날 밤 그토록 다정하게 함께 잤다는 것을 생각하면, 더구나 아침에 깨어 보니 그의 애정 어린 팔이 내 몸 위에 걸쳐져 있던 것을 생각하면, 나는 이런 그의 무관심이 무척 이상하게 느껴졌다. 그러나 야만인이란 기이한 존재인지라, 때로는 그들을 어떻게 대해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없는 법이다. 처음에는 그들이 위압적으로 다가온다. 그 소박함에서 우러나는 차분한 자기 절제는 마치 소크라테스적 지혜처럼 보인다. 나는 또한 퀴케그가 여관의 다른 선원들과는 전혀, 혹은 거의 어울리지 않는다는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그는 어떤 식으로든 먼저 다가가는 일이 없었고, 자기 교제의 범위를 넓히려는 욕심도 없어 보였다. 이 모든 것이 내게는 몹시 별나게 비쳤다. 하지만 다시 생각해 보니, 거기에는 어딘가 거의 숭고하다 할 무언가가 있었다. 여기 한 사나이가 있으니, 케이프 혼(Cape Horn)을 돌아 고향에서 이만 마일이나 떨어진 곳에—그것이 그가 이리로 올 수 있는 유일한 길이었으니—말하자면 목성에라도 와 있는 듯 자신에게 낯선 사람들 한복판에 내던져졌건만, 그럼에도 그는 더없이 편안해 보였으며, 지극한 평정을 유지한 채 제 자신과의 동무로 만족하며, 언제나 자기 자신과 한결같았다. 이는 분명 훌륭한 철학의 한 자락이었다. 비록 그런 것이 세상에 있다는 말조차 그는 들어 본 적이 없었겠지만 말이다. 그러나 어쩌면, 참된 철학자가 되려면 우리네 인간은 그렇게 살고 있다거나 그렇게 애쓰고 있다는 것을 의식하지 말아야 하는지도 모른다. 누군가가 스스로를 철학자라 내세운다는 말을 듣자마자, 나는 그가 소화불량에 걸린 노파처럼 \"소화기를 망가뜨린\" 사람임에 틀림없다고 결론짓는다.\n\n이제 적막해진 그 방에 앉아 있노라니, 불은 약하게 타들어 가, 처음의 맹렬한 기세가 공기를 데우고 나면 그저 바라보기 좋게 발갛게 빛나기만 하는 그 온화한 단계에 이르러 있었다. 저녁 그림자와 유령들이 창문 둘레로 모여들어, 말없이 외로이 앉은 우리 두 사람을 안으로 들여다보았다. 밖에서는 폭풍우가 장엄한 너울로 우르릉거렸다. 나는 야릇한 감정이 일기 시작하는 것을 느꼈다. 내 안에서 무언가가 녹아내리는 듯했다. 산산이 부서진 내 마음도, 미쳐 날뛰던 내 손도, 더는 이리 떼 같은 세상을 향해 곤두서 있지 않았다. 이 위안을 주는 야만인이 그것을 구원해 준 것이다. 그가 거기 앉아 있었고, 그 무관심 자체가 곧 문명화된 위선이나 매끄러운 기만 따위가 도사리지 않은 천성을 말해 주고 있었다. 그는 거칠었다. 보고 또 보아도 진풍경이었다. 그럼에도 나는 신비롭게 그에게 이끌리는 나 자신을 느끼기 시작했다. 그리고 다른 대부분의 사람이라면 질색했을 바로 그 점들이, 이토록 나를 끌어당기는 자석이었다. 기독교의 친절이라는 것이 결국 속 빈 예의에 지나지 않음이 드러났으니, 어디 이교도 친구를 한번 사귀어 보자, 나는 생각했다. 나는 벤치를 그의 곁으로 끌어다 놓고, 그러는 동안 그와 이야기를 나누어 보려 안간힘을 쓰며 우호적인 몸짓과 신호를 몇 가지 보냈다. 처음에 그는 이런 다가옴에 별 반응이 없었으나, 곧 내가 어젯밤의 환대를 입에 올리자, 우리가 다시 한자리에서 잘 거냐고 그럭저럭 물어 왔다. 나는 그렇다고 답했고, 그러자 그는 기뻐하는 듯, 어쩌면 약간 우쭐해하는 듯 보였다.\n\n그러고 나서 우리는 함께 그 책을 넘겼고, 나는 인쇄라는 것의 목적과, 그 안에 든 몇 안 되는 그림의 뜻을 그에게 설명해 보려 애썼다. 그리하여 나는 곧 그의 흥미를 끌어냈다. 그러고는 이 유명한 도시에서 볼 수 있는 갖가지 바깥 풍경들에 관해, 우리는 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 두서없이 떠들어 댔다. 이윽고 나는 친목 삼아 한 대 피우자고 제안했고, 그는 자기 쌈지와 토마호크 담뱃대를 꺼내 조용히 내게 한 모금 권했다. 그리하여 우리는 그 야성적인 담뱃대로 번갈아 연기를 주고받으며, 그것을 규칙적으로 우리 사이에 돌려 가며 앉아 있었다.\n\n설령 그 이교도의 가슴속에 나를 향한 무관심의 얼음이 아직 얼마쯤 도사리고 있었다 한들, 우리가 함께 나눈 이 흐뭇하고 정겨운 한 대의 담배가 그것을 이내 녹여 버려, 우리를 막역한 벗으로 만들어 놓았다. 그가 내게 끌리는 것은 내가 그에게 끌리는 것만큼이나 자연스럽고 스스럼없어 보였다. 담배를 다 피우고 나자 그는 자기 이마를 내 이마에 맞대고, 내 허리를 두 팔로 끌어안으며, 이제부터 우리는 부부가 된 거라고 말했다. 그것은 자기 나라 말로 우리가 가슴을 터놓는 벗이 되었다는 뜻이었으니, 필요하다면 그는 기꺼이 나를 위해 죽으리라는 것이었다. 같은 나라 사람끼리였다면, 이렇게 갑작스레 타오르는 우정의 불꽃은 너무 성급해 크게 미덥지 못한 것으로 비쳤겠지만, 이 소박한 야만인에게 그 케케묵은 규칙들은 들어맞지 않았다.\n\n저녁 식사를 마치고, 또 한 차례 정겨운 한담과 담배를 나눈 뒤, 우리는 함께 우리 방으로 갔다. 그는 자기가 방부 처리한 머리를 내게 선물로 주었다. 그러고는 그 어마어마하게 큰 담배 지갑을 꺼내, 담배 밑을 더듬어 은화로 삼십 달러쯤을 끄집어냈다. 그러더니 그것을 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓고, 기계적으로 똑같이 두 몫으로 나누어, 그중 한 몫을 내 쪽으로 밀며 그것이 내 것이라고 말했다. 나는 한사코 마다하려 했으나, 그는 그것을 내 바지 주머니에 쏟아 넣어 내 입을 막아 버렸다. 나는 그냥 그대로 두었다. 그러고서 그는 저녁 기도를 올리려고 자기 우상을 꺼내고, 종이로 된 난로 가리개를 치웠다. 어떤 몸짓과 기색으로 보아, 그는 내가 함께하기를 바라는 듯했다. 그러나 무슨 일이 뒤따를지를 잘 아는지라, 나는 만약 그가 나를 청한다면 응할 것인지 말 것인지 한순간 곰곰이 따져 보았다.\n\n나는 어엿한 기독교인이었으니, 오류 없는 장로교회의 품 안에서 나고 자란 사람이었다. 그러니 어찌 내가 이 야성의 우상숭배자와 한패가 되어 그의 나뭇조각을 섬길 수 있단 말인가? 그러나 도대체 예배란 무엇인가? 하고 나는 생각했다. 이스마엘, 너는 정녕 천지의 너그러우신 하느님께서—이교도까지 모두 아우르시는 그분께서—하찮은 검은 나뭇조각 따위를 시샘하실 수 있다고 여기느냐? 있을 수 없는 일이다! 그렇다면 예배란 무엇인가?—하느님의 뜻을 행하는 것, 바로 그것이 예배다. 그러면 하느님의 뜻이란 무엇인가?—내 이웃 사람이 내게 해 주기를 바라는 바를 내가 그 이웃에게 해 주는 것, 바로 그것이 하느님의 뜻이다. 그런데 퀴케그는 내 이웃 사람이다. 그러면 나는 이 퀴케그가 내게 무엇을 해 주기를 바라는가? 그야 물론, 나만의 장로교식 예배에 그가 함께해 주기를 바라는 것이다. 따라서 그렇다면 나도 그의 예배에 함께해야 마땅하다. 고로 나는 우상숭배자가 되어야 한다. 그리하여 나는 대팻밥에 불을 댕기고, 그 천진한 작은 우상을 받쳐 세우는 것을 거들었으며, 퀴케그와 함께 그 우상에게 탄 비스킷을 바치고, 그 앞에서 두세 번 절을 올렸으며, 그 코에 입을 맞추었다. 그 일을 마치고 우리는 옷을 벗고 잠자리에 들었으니, 우리 각자의 양심과도, 온 세상과도 화평했다. 그러나 우리는 자그마한 한담을 나누지 않고는 잠들지 않았다.\n\n어찌 된 영문인지 나는 모르겠으나, 벗끼리 흉금을 털어놓는 데에는 잠자리만 한 곳이 없다. 부부는, 사람들 말로는, 거기서 서로에게 영혼의 맨 밑바닥까지 열어 보인다고 하며, 어떤 늙은 부부는 곧잘 누워서 새벽녘이 다 되도록 옛 시절 이야기로 도란도란 한담을 나눈다고 한다. 그리하여 마음의 신혼을 맞은 우리, 나와 퀴케그는—아늑하고 정다운 한 쌍으로—그렇게 누워 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 11. Nightgown.\n\n우리는 그렇게 침대에 누워 짧은 간격으로 이야기를 나누다 깜빡 졸기를 거듭했고, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)는 이따금 문신 새겨진 갈색 다리를 다정하게 내 다리 위로 척 걸쳐 놓았다가 다시 거두어들이곤 했다. 우리는 그토록 허물없고 자유롭고 편안한 사이가 되어 있었다. 그러다 마침내 끝없는 잡담 탓에 우리 안에 남아 있던 약간의 졸음마저 깡그리 달아나 버렸고, 동이 트려면 아직 한참 남았는데도 다시 일어나고 싶은 기분이 들었다.\n\n그렇다, 우리는 완전히 말똥말똥해졌다. 어찌나 그러했던지 누워 있는 자세가 점점 지겨워지기 시작했고, 우리는 조금씩 몸을 일으켜 어느새 앉아 있었다. 이불을 몸에 단단히 두르고 침대 머리판에 기댄 채, 네 무릎을 바싹 모아 세우고 두 콧등을 그 위로 숙였으니, 마치 무릎이 발 데우는 화로라도 되는 듯했다. 우리는 무척 아늑하고 포근한 기분이었는데, 바깥이 그토록 쌀쌀했기에 더욱 그러했다. 사실 방 안에 불기운이 전혀 없었던 터라 이불 밖 또한 차가웠으니 말이다. 다시 말하거니와 그래서 더욱 그러했던 것이다. 진정으로 몸의 온기를 누리려면 몸의 어느 작은 부분만큼은 차가워야 하기 때문이다. 이 세상에 대비(對比)에 의하지 않고서 본래 그것 자체인 성질이란 없으니, 그 어떤 것도 홀로 존재하지는 않는 법이다. 만약 그대가 온몸이 빈틈없이 편안하다고, 그것도 오랫동안 그러했다고 으스댄다면, 그대는 더 이상 편안하다고 말할 수 없다. 그러나 침대 속의 퀴퀘그와 나처럼 콧등 끝이나 정수리가 살짝 시리다면, 바로 그때야말로 의식 전체가 더없이 즐겁고도 틀림없는 따뜻함을 느끼게 된다. 이런 까닭에 침실에는 결코 불을 들여놓아서는 안 되는 것인데, 그것이야말로 부자들이 누리는 사치스러운 불편 가운데 하나다. 이런 종류의 감미로움이 절정에 이르려면, 그대와 그대의 아늑함, 그리고 바깥의 찬 공기 사이에 담요 한 장 외에는 아무것도 없어야 하기 때문이다. 그러면 그대는 북극의 수정 한가운데 깃든 하나의 따뜻한 불씨처럼 거기 누워 있게 된다.\n\n우리는 그렇게 몸을 웅크리고 한동안 앉아 있었는데, 문득 나는 눈을 떠 보아야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 이부자리 사이에 들면, 낮이든 밤이든, 잠들었든 깨어 있든, 나는 침대 속의 아늑함을 더욱 한데 모으려고 늘 눈을 감아 두는 버릇이 있기 때문이다. 빛이 우리의 흙으로 빚어진 부분에는 더 어울릴지언정, 어둠이야말로 정녕 우리 본질의 본래 거처라는 듯이, 눈을 감지 않고서는 누구도 제 자신의 정체를 제대로 느낄 수 없는 법이다. 그리하여 눈을 뜨고, 내 스스로 만들어 낸 즐거운 어둠에서 빠져나와 불 밝히지 않은 자정의 강요된 거친 바깥 어둠 속으로 나오자, 나는 불쾌한 거부감을 맛보았다. 우리가 이토록 말똥말똥 깨어 있으니 차라리 불을 켜는 편이 낫겠다는 퀴퀘그의 제안에도 나는 조금도 반대하지 않았다. 게다가 그는 자신의 토마호크로 조용히 몇 모금 빨고 싶은 강한 욕구를 느끼고 있었던 것이다. 분명히 말해 두거니와, 전날 밤 나는 그가 침대에서 담배를 피우는 것에 그토록 강한 혐오를 느꼈건만, 일단 애정이 끼어들어 그것을 구부려 놓으면 우리의 뻣뻣한 편견이 얼마나 잘 휘어지는지를 보라. 이제 나는 퀴퀘그가, 그것도 침대 속에서, 내 곁에서 담배 피우는 것보다 더 좋은 일이 없었으니, 그때 그가 그토록 평온한 가정적 기쁨으로 가득 차 보였기 때문이다. 나는 더 이상 주인장의 보험 정책 따위를 지나치게 염려하지 않았다. 다만 진정한 벗과 더불어 파이프 하나와 담요 한 장을 나누는, 응축되고 은밀한 안락함에만 온통 마음이 살아 있었다. 우리는 텁수룩한 웃옷을 어깨에 두른 채 이제 토마호크를 서로 주고받았고, 마침내 우리 머리 위로 갓 켠 등불의 불꽃에 비친 푸른 연기의 천개(天蓋)가 서서히 드리워졌다.\n\n이 일렁이는 천개가 그 야만인을 머나먼 풍경 속으로 실어 보낸 것인지는 나도 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그는 이제 자신의 고향 섬에 관해 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그의 내력을 듣고 싶었던 나는 그에게 어서 계속해서 들려 달라고 청했다. 그는 기꺼이 응했다. 그때는 그의 말을 적잖이 제대로 알아듣지 못했으나, 훗날 그의 서툰 말투에 더 익숙해진 뒤에 알게 된 사실들 덕분에, 이제 나는 여기 뼈대만 추려 내놓는 정도로나마 그 이야기 전체를 들려줄 수 있게 되었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 12. Biographical.\n\n퀴케그는 머나먼 서쪽, 그리고 남쪽에 자리한 로코보코 섬 태생이었다. 그 섬은 어떤 지도에도 나와 있지 않다. 참된 장소란 본래 그런 법이다.\n\n풀로 엮은 허리가리개 하나만 두른 채 고향의 숲을 마구 헤집고 뛰어다니던 갓 태어난 야만인 시절, 마치 그를 어린 묘목으로 여기는 듯 새끼 염소들이 그 뒤를 졸졸 따라다니며 풀을 뜯던 그 시절에도, 퀴케그의 야심 찬 영혼 속에는 포경선 한두 척 표본 따위로는 만족할 수 없는, 기독교 세계를 좀 더 보고 싶다는 강한 열망이 도사리고 있었다. 그의 아버지는 대추장이자 왕이었고, 숙부는 대제사장이었다. 또한 외가 쪽으로는 천하무적의 전사들을 남편으로 둔 고모들을 자랑할 만했다. 그의 핏줄에는 더없이 고귀한 피 — 왕족의 피가 흐르고 있었다. 다만 안타깝게도, 가르침 받지 못한 어린 시절에 그가 키워 온 식인 성향으로 인해 그 피가 적잖이 더럽혀졌으리라 짐작될 뿐이다.\n\n새그하버에서 온 배 한 척이 그의 아버지가 다스리는 만(灣)을 찾아왔고, 퀴케그는 기독교 세계로 가는 뱃길을 청했다. 그러나 그 배는 이미 선원을 가득 채우고 있던 터라 그의 청을 물리쳤다. 그의 아버지인 왕의 영향력을 모두 동원해도 소용없었다. 그러자 퀴케그는 한 가지 맹세를 세웠다. 그는 홀로 카누를 몰아, 배가 섬을 떠날 때 반드시 지나가야 하는 줄을 알고 있던 머나먼 해협으로 노 저어 나아갔다. 한쪽에는 산호초가 있었고, 다른 한쪽에는 물속까지 뻗어 나온 맹그로브 덤불로 뒤덮인 나지막한 곶이 있었다. 그는 여전히 물 위에 떠 있는 카누를 그 덤불 사이에 뱃머리를 바다 쪽으로 향한 채 숨겨 두고는, 고물에 앉아 노를 낮게 쥐었다. 그리고 배가 미끄러지듯 곁을 지나갈 때, 번개처럼 튀어나가 뱃전에 다다랐고, 한 발을 뒤로 차 카누를 뒤집어 가라앉힌 뒤, 사슬을 타고 기어올라 갑판 위에 온몸을 길게 내던지고는, 거기 박힌 고리 볼트를 움켜쥐고서, 산산조각이 나는 한이 있어도 결코 놓지 않겠노라 맹세했다.\n\n선장은 그를 바다에 던져 버리겠다 으름장을 놓고, 드러난 그의 손목 위로 단검을 들이댔지만 모두 헛수고였다. 퀴케그는 왕의 아들이었고, 그는 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다. 그 필사적인 담대함과 기독교 세계를 보고 싶어 하는 거친 열망에 마음이 움직인 선장은 마침내 누그러져, 마음대로 지내도 좋다고 말했다. 그러나 이 늠름한 젊은 야만인 — 이 바다의 웨일스 왕자는 선장실 구경 한 번 못 했다. 사람들은 그를 선원들 틈에 처박아 두고 고래잡이로 만들어 버렸다. 그러나 표트르 황제가 이국 도시의 조선소에서 기꺼이 막일을 마다하지 않았듯, 퀴케그도 그로 인해 가르침 받지 못한 제 동족을 일깨울 힘을 얻을 수만 있다면 겉보기의 그 어떤 굴욕도 마다하지 않았다. 그가 내게 말하기를, 그의 마음 밑바닥에는 기독교인들 사이에서 제 백성을 지금보다 더 행복하게 — 그뿐 아니라 지금보다 더 선하게 만들 기술을 배우고자 하는 깊은 열망이 자리 잡고 있었던 것이다. 그러나, 아! 고래잡이들의 행실은 곧 그에게, 기독교인이라 한들 비참하고 사악할 수 있으며, 그것도 그의 아버지가 다스리던 모든 이교도들보다 한없이 더 심할 수 있음을 깨닫게 해 주었다. 마침내 오래된 새그하버에 다다라 그곳 선원들이 무슨 짓을 하는지 보았고, 이어 낸터킷으로 건너가 거기서도 그들이 품삯을 어떻게 탕진하는지를 본 가련한 퀴케그는, 모든 것을 단념하고 말았다. 그는 생각했다. 어느 자오선 아래든 세상이란 사악한 곳이로구나, 나는 이교도로 죽으리라.\n\n그리하여 마음속으로는 여전히 오래된 우상숭배자이면서도, 그는 이 기독교인들 틈에서 살아가며 그들의 옷을 입고, 그들의 알아들을 수 없는 말을 따라 해 보려 애썼다. 고향을 떠나온 지 어느덧 세월이 흘렀건만 그에게서 풍기는 기이한 구석은 바로 거기서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n나는 넌지시 그에게, 고향으로 돌아가 즉위식을 치를 생각은 없는지 물었다. 마지막으로 들은 소식으로는 그의 아버지가 무척 늙고 쇠약해졌다니, 이제는 세상을 떠났다고 여겨도 좋을 터였다. 그는 아니라고, 아직은 아니라고 답했다. 그러고는 기독교, 아니 차라리 기독교인들이 자신을 더럽혀, 자기 앞서 서른 명의 이교도 왕이 앉았던 순수하고 흠 없는 왕좌에 오르기에는 합당치 않게 만들어 버린 것이 두렵다고 덧붙였다. 하지만 머지않아, 다시 세례받았다는 느낌이 들기만 하면 돌아가겠노라고 그는 말했다. 그러나 당장은 사방을 두루 항해하며 네 대양 모두에 젊은 날의 객기를 뿌려 둘 작정이라고 했다. 사람들은 그를 작살잡이로 만들어 놓았으니, 이제는 그 미늘 박힌 작살이 왕홀(王笏)을 대신하고 있었다.\n\n나는 그에게 앞으로의 행보와 관련하여 당장의 목적이 무엇인지 물었다. 그는 옛 직업 그대로 다시 바다로 나가는 것이라 답했다. 그 말에 나는 고래잡이야말로 내가 품은 계획이라 일러 주었고, 모험을 좇는 고래잡이가 출항하기에 가장 유망한 항구인 낸터킷에서 배를 타고 나갈 작정임을 알려 주었다. 그는 곧장 나와 함께 그 섬으로 가서, 같은 배를 타고, 같은 당직조에 들고, 같은 보트를 타며, 같은 식탁에 앉아 — 한마디로 내 모든 운명을 함께 나누기로 결심했다. 두 손을 맞잡고서, 두 세계 모두의 운수 자루 속으로 대담하게 손을 담그자는 것이었다. 나는 이 모든 것에 기쁘게 동의했다. 이제 퀴케그에게 느끼는 애정은 차치하더라도, 그는 노련한 작살잡이였으니, 상선 선원들이 아는 만큼은 바다에 익숙했어도 고래잡이의 신비에 대해서는 전혀 무지했던 나 같은 사람에게는 더없이 큰 도움이 될 것이 분명했기 때문이다.\n\n그의 파이프에서 마지막 연기가 사그라지며 이야기가 끝나자, 퀴케그는 나를 끌어안고 제 이마를 내 이마에 맞댄 뒤, 불을 끄고는, 우리는 이리저리 서로에게서 몸을 굴려 떨어졌고, 이내 잠들고 말았다.\n\n# CHAPTER 13. Wheelbarrow.\n\n이튿날 아침, 월요일에, 방부 처리된 머리를 어느 이발사에게 가발 받침으로 넘긴 뒤, 나는 내 몫과 동료의 셈을 치렀다. 다만 그 돈은 동료의 것을 썼다. 능글맞게 웃는 여관 주인이며 다른 하숙인들이며 모두, 나와 퀴케그 사이에 갑작스레 싹튼 우정에 놀라우리만치 재미있어하는 눈치였다. 더구나 피터 코핀이 그자에 대해 늘어놓았던 허무맹랑한 이야기들이 일찍이 나를 그토록 겁먹게 했던 바로 그 인물과 내가 지금 함께 다니고 있으니 그럴 만도 했다.\n\n우리는 손수레 하나를 빌려, 내 초라한 융단 가방과 퀴케그의 범포 자루와 해먹까지 짐을 모두 싣고서, 부두에 매여 있는 작은 낸터킷 정기 연락선인 스쿠너, \"모스\"호를 향해 내려갔다. 우리가 길을 걸어가는 동안 사람들이 빤히 쳐다보았다. 퀴케그를 보고 그러는 것은 아니었다. 그들은 거리에서 그 같은 식인종을 보는 데 익숙했으니까. 그보다는 그와 내가 그토록 허물없는 사이로 지내는 모습을 보고 놀란 것이었다. 그러나 우리는 그들을 개의치 않고, 번갈아 손수레를 밀며 나아갔으며, 퀴케그는 이따금 멈춰 서서 작살 미늘에 씌운 칼집을 매만지곤 했다. 나는 그에게 어째서 그토록 성가신 물건을 뭍에까지 들고 다니느냐고, 어느 포경선이든 제 작살은 다 마련해 두지 않느냐고 물었다. 이에 그는 대략 이렇게 대답했다. 내 말이 틀림없는 사실이긴 하나, 그래도 자기는 제 작살에 각별한 애착이 있다는 것이었다. 그것은 믿을 만한 재질로 만들어졌고, 숱한 목숨 건 싸움에서 단단히 검증되었으며, 고래들의 심장과 깊이 친밀한 사이이기 때문이라고 했다. 요컨대, 굳이 제 것을 마련할 의무가 없는데도 제 낫을 들고 농부들의 풀밭으로 들어가는 내륙의 많은 추수꾼과 풀 베는 일꾼들처럼, 퀴케그 역시 제 나름의 사사로운 이유로 제 작살을 더 좋아하는 것이었다.\n\n내 손에서 제 손으로 손수레를 넘겨받으며, 그는 난생처음 손수레를 보았던 때의 우스운 이야기 하나를 들려주었다. 그것은 새그 하버에서의 일이었다. 듣자 하니 그의 배 선주들이 무거운 궤짝을 하숙집까지 나르라고 손수레 하나를 빌려준 모양이었다. 손수레를 다루는 정확한 방법에 대해 사실은 전혀 무지했으면서도, 그에 대해 모르는 티를 내지 않으려고, 퀴케그는 제 궤짝을 손수레 위에 올려놓고 단단히 동여맨 다음, 그 손수레를 어깨에 들쳐 메고 부두를 따라 행진해 올라갔던 것이다. \"이런,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"퀴케그, 그쯤은 알 만도 했을 텐데. 사람들이 웃지 않던가?\"\n\n이 말에 그는 또 다른 이야기를 들려주었다. 듣자 하니, 그의 고향 섬 로코보코의 사람들은 혼인 잔치 때 어린 야자열매의 향긋한 물을 짜내어 펀치 그릇처럼 큼직한, 얼룩진 호리병박 속에 담아 둔다고 했다. 그리고 이 펀치 그릇은 잔치가 벌어지는 엮은 돗자리 위에서 언제나 한가운데를 차지하는 큰 장식이 된다는 것이었다. 그런데 언젠가 어느 거대한 상선 한 척이 로코보코에 들렀고, 그 선장은 — 전해지는 바로는 적어도 바다 선장치고는 매우 위엄 있고 격식을 차리는 신사였다는데 — 그 선장이 갓 열 살을 넘긴 어여쁜 어린 공주, 곧 퀴케그의 누이의 혼인 잔치에 초대되었다. 그런데 모든 혼인 손님들이 신부의 대나무 오두막에 모였을 때, 이 선장이 안으로 들어와 상석에 앉게 되었으니, 펀치 그릇을 마주한 자리, 곧 대제사장과 임금 폐하, 다시 말해 퀴케그의 아버지 사이에 자리를 잡은 것이다. 식전 기도가 올려졌는데 — 그 사람들에게도 우리처럼 식전 기도가 있었으니 — 다만 퀴케그가 내게 말하기를, 그런 때에 제 접시를 내려다보는 우리와는 달리, 그들은 오히려 오리를 흉내 내어 모든 잔치를 베푸시는 위대한 분을 우러러본다고 했다. 아무튼 식전 기도가 올려지자, 대제사장은 그 섬의 까마득한 옛 의식으로 잔치의 막을 열었다. 그것은 곧, 그 복된 음료가 돌려지기 전에 자신의 신성하고 또 신성하게 하는 손가락을 그릇 속에 담그는 일이었다. 자신이 제사장 옆자리에 앉은 것을 보고, 또 그 의식을 눈여겨보며, 스스로를 — 배의 선장인 자신이 — 한낱 섬나라 임금보다, 더구나 임금 자신의 집에서야말로 마땅히 윗자리에 있다고 여긴 선장은, 태연히 그 펀치 그릇에 손을 씻기 시작했다. 짐작건대 그것을 커다란 핑거볼쯤으로 여긴 모양이었다. \"자,\" 하고 퀴케그가 말했다. \"이제 자네는 어떻게 생각하나? — 우리 사람들이 웃지 않았겠나?\"\n\n마침내 뱃삯을 치르고 짐도 무사히 실은 채, 우리는 스쿠너 갑판에 올라섰다. 돛을 올리자 배는 아쿠시넷 강을 미끄러져 내려갔다. 한쪽 편에서는 뉴베드퍼드가 층층이 이어진 거리들로 솟아올랐고, 얼음에 덮인 나무들이 맑고 차가운 공기 속에서 온통 반짝거렸다. 부두마다 술통 위에 술통을 쌓아 올린 거대한 언덕과 산들이 쌓여 있었고, 그 곁에는 온 세계를 떠돌던 포경선들이 마침내 말없이 안전하게 매여 잠잠히 누워 있었다. 한편 다른 배들에서는 목수와 통장이들의 소리가 들려왔고, 역청을 녹이는 불과 풀무 소리가 뒤섞여 들려오니, 이 모든 것이 새로운 항해가 막 시작되려 함을 알리고 있었다. 더없이 위험하고 기나긴 항해 하나가 끝나면 그저 또 다른 항해가 시작될 뿐이며, 두 번째 항해가 끝나면 그저 세 번째가 시작될 뿐, 그렇게 영영, 끝없이 이어지는 것이다. 모든 세속적 수고란 이토록 끝이 없으며, 그렇다, 견디기 힘든 것이다.\n\n좀 더 탁 트인 바다에 이르자 상쾌한 바닷바람이 한결 싱그러워졌다. 작은 모스호는 어린 망아지가 콧김을 내뿜듯 뱃머리에서 잽싼 물거품을 흩뿌렸다. 아, 나는 그 거친 타타르의 공기를 얼마나 들이켰던가! 아, 나는 그 통행세 받는 땅덩이를 얼마나 발로 차 버렸던가! 노예 같은 발뒤꿈치와 말발굽 자국으로 온통 패인 그 흔한 큰길을 떨쳐 버리고, 어떠한 자취도 남기지 않는 바다의 너그러움을 우러러보았다.\n\n바로 그 물거품의 샘에서, 퀴케그도 나와 함께 들이켜며 비틀거리는 듯했다. 거무스름한 그의 콧구멍이 벌름거리며 부풀어 올랐고, 그는 줄로 갈아 뾰족하게 만든 이를 드러내 보였다. 우리는 자꾸자꾸 내달렸다. 그리고 먼바다에 이르자 모스호는 돌풍에 경의를 표하듯, 술탄 앞의 노예처럼 뱃머리를 숙이고 자맥질했다. 옆으로 기울며 우리도 옆으로 쏜살같이 나아갔고, 밧줄 가닥마다 철사처럼 짜릿하게 울렸으며, 두 개의 높은 돛대는 육지의 회오리바람 속 인도산 등나무 줄기처럼 휘어졌다. 곤두박질치는 제1사장 곁에 서 있던 우리는 이 비틀거리는 광경에 어찌나 빠져 있었던지, 한동안 우리는 승객들의 비웃는 눈길을 알아채지도 못했다. 풋내기 뱃사람 같은 그 무리는, 마치 백인이 회칠한 흑인보다 더 품위 있기라도 하다는 듯, 두 인간이 그토록 가까이 어울려 다니는 것을 신기해했다. 그러나 그중에는 멍청이며 촌뜨기도 몇 있었는데, 그 지독한 풋내로 보아 온갖 풋것의 한복판, 그 핵심에서 막 나온 게 틀림없었다. 퀴케그는 그 어린 햇가지 같은 자들 중 하나가 등 뒤에서 자신을 흉내 내는 것을 알아챘다. 나는 그 촌뜨기에게 최후의 순간이 닥쳤구나 싶었다. 작살을 내던진 우람한 야만인은 두 팔로 그를 거머잡더니, 거의 기적과도 같은 날렵함과 힘으로 그를 몸째 공중 높이 날려 버렸다. 그러고는 공중제비 한가운데서 그자의 엉덩이를 가볍게 톡 쳐 주니, 그 사내는 터질 듯한 폐를 안고 두 발로 착지했고, 그동안 퀴케그는 그에게 등을 돌린 채 토마호크 담뱃대에 불을 붙여 내게 한 모금 빨라며 건네주었다.\n\n\"선장님! 선장님!\" 하고 그 촌뜨기가 선장을 향해 달려가며 소리쳤다. \"선장님, 선장님, 여기 악마가 있어요.\"\n\n\"이봐, 자네 말이야,\" 하고 선장이 외쳤다. 그는 비쩍 마른 바다의 갈빗대 같은 사내였는데, 성큼성큼 퀴케그에게 다가서며 말했다. \"도대체 그게 무슨 짓이야? 저 친구를 죽일 뻔했다는 걸 모르나?\"\n\n\"저 사람 뭐라 그래?\" 하고 퀴케그가 순순히 내게 돌아서며 물었다.\n\n\"저 사람 말이,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"자네가 저기 저 사람을 죽일 뻔했다는 거야.\" 하며 나는 아직도 벌벌 떨고 있는 풋내기를 가리켰다.\n\n\"죽이다니,\" 하고 퀴케그가 외쳤다. 그는 문신 새긴 얼굴을 비틀어 이 세상 것 같지 않은 경멸의 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"허! 저놈은 쪼끄만 물고기야. 퀴케그는 그런 쪼끄만 물고기 안 죽여. 퀴케그는 큰 고래 죽여!\"\n\n\"이봐,\" 하고 선장이 으르렁댔다. \"이 식인종아, 이 배 위에서 또 그따위 수작을 부렸다간 내가 너를 죽여 버릴 테다. 그러니 눈 똑바로 뜨고 조심해.\"\n\n그런데 바로 그때, 마침 선장 자신이야말로 제 눈을 똑바로 떠야 할 판이었다. 주돛에 걸린 엄청난 장력이 바람 쪽 돛줄을 끊어 버려, 그 거대한 활대가 좌우로 마구 흔들리며 갑판의 뒷부분을 통째로 휩쓸고 있었던 것이다. 퀴케그가 그토록 거칠게 다루었던 그 가엾은 친구는 바다로 휩쓸려 떨어지고 말았다. 모든 선원이 공황에 빠졌고, 활대를 붙잡아 멈춰 보려는 것은 미친 짓이나 다름없어 보였다. 활대는 시계가 한 번 째깍하는 사이에 거의 오른쪽에서 왼쪽으로, 다시 되돌아오기를 거듭했고, 매 순간 산산조각으로 부러질 듯이 보였다. 아무것도 손쓸 수 없었고, 또 무엇도 할 수 있을 것 같지 않았다. 갑판 위의 사람들은 뱃머리 쪽으로 우르르 몰려가, 마치 그 활대가 성난 고래의 아래턱이라도 되는 양 빤히 노려보며 서 있었다. 이 아수라장 한복판에서, 퀴케그는 날쌔게 무릎을 꿇더니 활대가 지나는 길 아래로 기어들어 밧줄 하나를 낚아채, 그 한쪽 끝을 뱃전에 단단히 매고는, 다른 한쪽 끝을 올가미처럼 휙 던져 머리 위로 휩쓸려 지나가는 활대에 걸었다. 그리고 다음번에 활대가 홱 당겨지는 순간, 그 가로대는 그렇게 옭매여 모든 것이 안전해졌다. 스쿠너는 바람 쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌렸고, 선원들이 고물의 보트를 풀어 내리는 동안, 퀴케그는 허리까지 옷을 벗어젖히고 길고 살아 있는 활처럼 도약하여 뱃전에서 뛰어들었다. 삼 분 남짓 동안, 그가 개처럼 헤엄치며 긴 두 팔을 앞으로 곧게 내뻗고, 번갈아 그 우람한 어깨를 얼어붙을 듯한 물거품 사이로 드러내는 것이 보였다. 나는 그 장대하고 영광스러운 사내를 바라보았으나, 구해야 할 사람은 보이지 않았다. 풋내기는 이미 가라앉아 버린 것이다. 퀴케그는 물에서 몸을 수직으로 솟구쳐 올려, 한순간 주위를 둘러보고는, 형편이 어떠한지 곧바로 알아챈 듯 물속으로 자맥질해 사라졌다. 다시 몇 분이 지나자 그는 한쪽 팔로는 여전히 물살을 가르며, 다른 팔로는 생기 없는 몸뚱이를 끌고서 떠올랐다. 보트가 곧 그들을 끌어올렸다. 가엾은 촌뜨기는 되살아났다. 모든 선원이 퀴케그를 고결한 호걸이라 입을 모았고, 선장은 그에게 용서를 빌었다. 그 시각부터 나는 따개비처럼 퀴케그에게 들러붙었다. 그렇다, 가엾은 퀴케그가 마지막 긴 자맥질을 할 그때까지도.\n\n이토록 자기 행동에 무심한 사람이 또 있었을까? 그는 자신이 인도주의 협회나 자선 협회로부터 훈장을 받을 만하다고는 조금도 생각하지 않는 듯했다. 그는 그저 물을 — 마실 물을 — 소금기를 닦아 낼 무언가를 청할 뿐이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 마른 옷으로 갈아입고 담뱃대에 불을 붙인 뒤, 뱃전에 기대어 주위 사람들을 온화하게 바라보며, 마치 혼잣말로 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"이 세상은 어느 자오선에서나 서로 돕는 합자(合資)의 세상이지. 우리 식인종들도 이 기독교인들을 도와야 하는 법이고.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 14. Nantucket.\n\n항해 중에 더 언급할 만한 일은 일어나지 않았다. 그리하여 우리는 순조롭게 바다를 달려 낸터킷(Nantucket)에 무사히 도착했다.\n\n낸터킷! 지도를 펼쳐 그곳을 보라. 그것이 세상의 얼마나 외진 한구석을 차지하고 있는지를 보라. 어떻게 그렇게 뭍에서 멀찍이 떨어져, 에디스톤 등대보다도 더 쓸쓸하게 서 있는지를 보라. 보라, 그저 작은 언덕 하나, 모래로 굽은 팔꿈치 하나일 뿐이다. 온통 해변일 뿐 배경이라 할 만한 것이 없다. 거기에는 압지(壓紙) 대용으로 이십 년을 써도 남을 만큼의 모래가 있다. 짓궂은 익살꾼들은 이렇게 말할 것이다. 그곳에서는 잡초마저 심어야 하니 저절로는 자라지 않으며, 캐나다엉겅퀴를 수입해 와야 하고, 기름통의 새는 구멍을 막을 마개 하나를 구하려고 바다 건너로 사람을 보내야 한다고. 또 낸터킷에서는 나뭇조각이 로마에서 진짜 십자가 조각처럼 떠받들려 다니며, 사람들은 여름철 그늘을 얻으려고 집 앞에 독버섯을 심고, 풀 한 포기가 오아시스를 이루고 하루를 걸어도 세 포기면 대초원을 이루며, 라플란드 사람의 눈신 비슷한 모래신을 신는다고. 또 그곳 사람들은 바다에 의해 사방이 갇히고, 띠처럼 둘러싸이고, 온통 에워싸여 완전한 섬으로 만들어진 까닭에, 그들의 의자와 탁자에까지도 마치 바다거북의 등딱지에 붙듯 작은 대합조개가 들러붙어 있곤 한다고. 그러나 이런 허풍은 다만 낸터킷이 일리노이 같은 곳이 아님을 보여 줄 뿐이다.\n\n이제 이 섬이 어떻게 인디언들에 의해 정착되었는지를 전하는 그 경이로운 옛이야기를 보라. 전설은 이러하다. 옛적에 독수리 한 마리가 뉴잉글랜드 해안으로 내리꽂혀 인디언 갓난아기 하나를 발톱으로 채어 갔다. 부모는 크게 슬피 울며 제 아이가 드넓은 바다 너머로 실려 가 시야에서 사라지는 것을 지켜보았다. 그들은 같은 방향을 좇기로 결심했다. 카누를 타고 나선 그들은 위태로운 항해 끝에 그 섬을 발견했고, 거기서 텅 빈 상아 함 하나를 찾아냈으니 — 바로 그 가엾은 어린 인디언의 백골이었다.\n\n그러니 해변에서 태어난 이 낸터킷 사람들이 바다로 나아가 생계를 꾸린다 한들 무엇이 놀랍겠는가! 그들은 처음에는 모래밭에서 게와 대합을 잡았다. 더 대담해지자 그물을 들고 물속으로 걸어 들어가 고등어를 잡았다. 더 노련해지자 보트를 밀어내어 대구를 잡았다. 그리고 마침내 거대한 함대를 바다에 띄워 이 물의 세계를 탐험했고, 끊임없이 이어지는 세계 일주의 띠를 그 둘레에 둘렀으며, 베링 해협을 들여다보았고, 모든 계절과 모든 대양에서 대홍수를 견디고 살아남은 가장 강력한 생물 덩어리, 가장 괴물 같고 가장 산더미 같은 그것을 향해 영원한 전쟁을 선포했다! 히말라야 같은 그 소금바다의 마스토돈, 무의식적인 힘의 그토록 무시무시한 위용으로 둘러싸여, 그 가장 두려움 없고 악의에 찬 공격보다도 그것이 겁에 질려 날뛰는 순간이 오히려 더 두려운 그 짐승을!\n\n그리하여 이 헐벗은 낸터킷 사람들, 이 바다의 은둔자들은 바닷속 제 개미집에서 쏟아져 나와, 마치 수많은 알렉산드로스처럼 물의 세계를 휩쓸고 정복했다. 세 해적 강국이 폴란드를 나누어 가졌듯이, 그들끼리 대서양과 태평양과 인도양을 나누어 가진 것이다. 아메리카가 텍사스에 멕시코를 더하고 캐나다 위에 쿠바를 얹는다 해도, 영국인들이 인도 전역을 새카맣게 뒤덮고 태양으로부터 그 이글거리는 깃발을 내건다 해도, 이 물과 뭍으로 이루어진 지구의 삼분의 이는 낸터킷 사람의 것이다. 바다는 그의 것이기 때문이다. 황제가 제국을 소유하듯 그는 바다를 소유한다. 다른 뱃사람들은 그저 그곳을 지나갈 통행권을 가질 뿐이다. 상선이란 그저 늘어뜨린 다리에 지나지 않고, 군함이란 떠다니는 요새에 지나지 않는다. 해적과 사략선조차도 노상강도가 길을 따라 다니듯 바다를 따라 다니지만, 그들은 다만 다른 배들, 곧 자기들과 같은 뭍의 부스러기들을 약탈할 뿐, 밑바닥 없는 깊은 바다 그 자체에서 생계를 끌어내려 하지는 않는다. 낸터킷 사람, 오직 그만이 바다에 거주하며 바다 위에서 마음껏 누린다. 성경의 표현을 빌리자면, 오직 그만이 배를 타고 바다로 내려가, 그것을 제 전용 농장처럼 이리저리 갈아엎는다. 그곳에 그의 집이 있고, 그곳에 그의 생업이 놓여 있으니, 노아의 홍수가 중국의 수백만 백성을 모조리 삼킨다 한들 그의 일은 멈추지 않을 것이다. 대초원의 멧닭이 대초원에 살듯 그는 바다에 산다. 그는 물결 사이에 몸을 숨기고, 샤무아 사냥꾼이 알프스를 오르듯 물결을 타고 오른다. 여러 해 동안 그는 뭍을 모르고 지내니, 마침내 뭍에 닿으면 그것은 또 다른 세계처럼, 뭍사람에게 달이 풍기는 것보다도 더 낯설게 풍겨 온다. 해 질 녘이면 날개를 접고 큰 물결 사이에서 흔들리며 잠드는 저 뭍 없는 갈매기처럼, 낸터킷 사람도 밤이 내리면 뭍이 보이지 않는 곳에서 돛을 거두고 쉼에 든다. 바로 그 베개 밑으로 바다코끼리와 고래의 무리가 떼 지어 내달리는 동안.\n\n# CHAPTER 15. Chowder.\n\n작은 모스호가 아늑하게 닻을 내렸을 때는 저녁도 꽤 늦은 시각이었고, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 나는 뭍으로 올라갔다. 그래서 그날은 아무 볼일도 볼 수 없었으니, 적어도 저녁 식사와 잠자리 외에는 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 물보라 여관의 주인장은 우리에게 자기 사촌인 트라이 포츠의 호세아 허시를 추천해 주었는데, 그가 낸터킷(Nantucket) 전역에서 가장 잘 관리되는 호텔 가운데 하나의 주인이라고 장담했다. 게다가 그는 자기가 호세아 사촌이라 부르는 그 사람이 차우더 솜씨로 유명하다고 우리에게 보증했다. 한마디로, 그는 우리가 트라이 포츠에서 차우더를 맛보는 것보다 더 나은 선택은 결코 없으리라고 노골적으로 넌지시 일러 준 셈이었다. 그러나 그가 우리에게 일러 준 방향이라는 것이 문제였다. 노란 창고를 오른쪽에 끼고 가다가 왼쪽으로 하얀 교회가 보이는 곳까지 간 다음, 그 교회를 왼쪽에 끼고 가다가 오른쪽으로 세 점쯤 트인 모퉁이에 이르거든, 그제야 처음 만나는 사람에게 그곳이 어디인지 물어보라는 것이었다. 이 꼬불꼬불한 방향 안내는 처음에 우리를 무척이나 헷갈리게 했는데, 특히 출발점에서부터 퀴퀘그가 우리의 첫 기점인 노란 창고를 왼쪽에 두고 가야 한다고 우기는 통에 더욱 그러했다. 나는 피터 코핀이 그것을 오른쪽에 둔다고 말한 것으로 알아들었던 것이다. 그렇지만 어둠 속을 조금 헤매고, 이따금 평화로이 잠든 주민을 깨워 길을 묻고 한 끝에, 우리는 마침내 도저히 잘못 볼 수 없는 무언가에 다다랐다.\n\n검게 칠한 거대한 나무 솥 두 개가 당나귀 귀 모양의 고리에 매달려, 낡은 출입구 앞에 박아 세운 오래된 중간 돛대의 가로대에 흔들리고 있었다. 가로대의 한쪽 끝은 톱으로 잘려 나가 있어서, 이 낡은 중간 돛대는 영락없이 교수대처럼 보였다. 어쩌면 그때의 나는 그런 인상에 지나치게 예민했는지도 모르겠다. 그러나 나는 어렴풋한 불안감을 품은 채 이 교수대를 빤히 바라보지 않을 수 없었다. 남아 있는 두 개의 가로대 끝을 올려다보노라니 목덜미가 뻐근하게 결렸다. 그렇다, 두 개였다. 하나는 퀴퀘그의 몫, 하나는 내 몫. 불길하다, 하고 나는 생각했다. 첫 포경 항구에 발을 디디자마자 코핀(관)이라는 자가 내 여관 주인이었고, 고래잡이들의 예배당에서는 묘비들이 나를 노려보았으며, 이제는 여기 교수대까지 있다! 게다가 어마어마한 검은 솥 한 쌍도! 이 마지막 것들은 지옥의 불구덩이에 관해 슬며시 암시를 던지고 있는 것일까?\n\n이런 상념에서 나를 불러낸 것은, 주근깨가 박힌 한 여인의 모습이었다. 노란 머리에 노란 가운을 걸친 그 여인은 여관 현관에 서 있었고, 그 위에는 상처 입은 눈알처럼 보이는 흐릿한 붉은 등불이 흔들리고 있었으며, 그녀는 자줏빛 양모 셔츠를 걸친 한 남자에게 기세 좋게 잔소리를 퍼붓고 있었다.\n\n\"썩 꺼지지 못해,\" 그녀가 그 남자에게 말했다. \"안 그러면 머리채를 잡고 빗어 줄 테다!\"\n\n\"이리 오게, 퀴퀘그,\" 내가 말했다. \"괜찮아. 저기 허시 부인이 있군.\"\n\n그리고 정말 그러했다. 호세아 허시 씨는 집을 비웠으나, 허시 부인이 그의 모든 일을 도맡아 처리할 만큼 완벽히 능숙했다. 우리가 저녁 식사와 잠자리를 원한다고 알리자, 허시 부인은 잔소리를 잠시 미뤄 두고 우리를 자그마한 방으로 안내했다. 그러고는 방금 끝난 식사의 잔해가 널린 식탁에 우리를 앉히더니, 우리 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"조개? 대구?\"\n\n\"대구라니 무슨 말씀이신지요, 부인?\" 나는 무척 공손하게 물었다.\n\n\"조개? 대구?\" 그녀가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"저녁으로 조개라고요? 차가운 조개 말입니까. 그게 부인의 뜻인가요?\" 내가 말했다. \"그렇지만 겨울철에 그건 좀 차갑고 끈적한 대접이 아닌가요, 허시 부인?\"\n\n그러나 허시 부인은 현관에서 잔소리를 기다리고 있는 자줏빛 셔츠의 남자에게 어서 다시 호통을 치고 싶어 안달이 난 데다, \"조개\"라는 말 외에는 아무것도 못 들은 모양이었다. 그녀는 부엌으로 통하는 열린 문 쪽으로 서둘러 가더니 \"둘 다 조개로!\" 하고 크게 외치고는 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"퀴퀘그,\" 내가 말했다. \"자네 생각엔 우리 둘이 조개 하나로 저녁을 때울 수 있을 것 같나?\"\n\n그러나 부엌에서 풍겨 오는 따스하고 구수한 김이, 우리 앞에 놓인 그 음울해 보이던 전망을 거짓으로 만들어 주었다. 그리고 김이 모락모락 나는 차우더가 들어오자, 그 수수께끼는 더없이 흐뭇하게 풀렸다. 오, 다정한 벗들이여! 내 말을 들어 보라. 그것은 개암나무 열매보다 겨우 클까 말까 한 작고 즙 많은 조개에다, 빻은 선박용 비스킷과 잘게 부순 소금 절인 돼지고기를 섞고, 전체에 버터를 듬뿍 넣어 풍미를 더하고 후추와 소금으로 넉넉히 간을 한 것이었다. 서리 내린 항해로 식욕이 한껏 날카로워진 데다, 특히 퀴퀘그는 자기가 좋아하는 생선 음식을 눈앞에 두고 있었고, 차우더는 더없이 훌륭했으므로, 우리는 그것을 잽싸게 해치웠다. 그러고는 잠시 등을 기대고 앉아 허시 부인의 그 조개니 대구니 하던 말을 곰곰이 떠올리다가, 나는 작은 실험을 한번 해 봐야겠다고 생각했다. 부엌문 쪽으로 다가가 \"대구\"라는 단어를 아주 힘주어 내뱉고는 다시 자리로 돌아왔다. 잠시 후 구수한 김이 또다시 피어올랐는데, 이번에는 풍미가 달랐다. 그리고 알맞은 때에 근사한 대구 차우더가 우리 앞에 놓였다.\n\n우리는 다시 식사에 들어갔다. 그릇 속에 숟가락을 부지런히 놀리는 동안, 나는 혼자 생각했다. 자, 이게 머리에 무슨 영향이라도 미치는 게 아닐까? 차우더 머리(차우더처럼 멍청한 머리)라는 그 멍청한 속담은 또 뭐란 말인가? \"그런데 이보게, 퀴퀘그, 자네 그릇 속에 든 게 살아 있는 뱀장어 아닌가? 작살은 어디다 두었나?\"\n\n생선 비린내 나는 온갖 곳들 가운데에서도 가장 비린 곳이 트라이 포츠였으니, 그 이름값을 톡톡히 하는 곳이었다. 그곳의 솥들은 언제나 차우더를 펄펄 끓이고 있었기 때문이다. 아침에도 차우더, 점심에도 차우더, 저녁에도 차우더라, 급기야 자기 옷을 뚫고 생선 가시가 삐져나오지 않나 살피게 될 지경이었다. 집 앞마당은 조개껍데기로 포장되어 있었다. 허시 부인은 대구 척추뼈를 반들반들 갈아 만든 목걸이를 걸고 다녔으며, 호세아 허시는 회계장부를 고급 묵은 상어 가죽으로 장정해 두었다. 우유에서도 비린내가 났는데, 도무지 그 까닭을 알 수 없었다. 그러다 어느 아침, 어부들의 보트 사이로 해변을 거닐다가, 호세아네 얼룩소가 생선 찌꺼기를 뜯어 먹으며 모래밭을 걷는 모습을 보고서야 알게 되었다. 그놈은 두 발 모두 목 잘린 대구 머리에 푹 박은 채 걸어 다니고 있었는데, 단언컨대 그 꼴이 어찌나 칠칠치 못해 보이던지.\n\n저녁 식사를 마치자, 우리는 허시 부인에게서 등불 하나와 침실로 가는 가장 가까운 길에 관한 안내를 받았다. 그러나 퀴퀘그가 막 나를 앞질러 계단을 오르려 할 때, 그 부인은 팔을 쭉 뻗어 그의 작살을 내놓으라고 했다. 그녀는 자기 객실에 작살을 들이는 것을 허락하지 않았다. \"왜 안 됩니까?\" 내가 말했다. \"진정한 고래잡이라면 누구나 작살을 끼고 자는데, 어째서 안 된다는 거죠?\" \"위험하니까요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 운수 사나운 항해에서 돌아온 젊은 스틱스 이후로 말이에요. 그 애가 사 년 반이나 나가 있다가 겨우 기름 세 통만 가지고 돌아와서는, 옆구리에 자기 작살을 박은 채 우리 일 층 뒤쪽 방에서 죽은 채로 발견됐거든요. 그 일이 있은 뒤로는, 어떤 숙박객도 밤에 그런 위험한 흉기를 방에 들이는 걸 허락하지 않아요. 그러니, 퀴퀘그 씨\" (그녀는 그새 그의 이름을 알아 둔 것이다) \"이 작살은 내가 받아 두었다가 아침까지 잘 간수해 드리지요. 그런데 차우더 말인데요. 내일 아침 식사는 조개로 할까요, 대구로 할까요, 손님들?\"\n\n\"둘 다요,\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 색다른 맛을 보게 훈제 청어도 두어 마리 곁들여 주시지요.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 16. The Ship.\n\n침대에 누워 우리는 다음 날의 계획을 짰다. 그런데 놀랍게도, 또 적잖이 걱정스럽게도, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)는 이제 내게 이렇게 일러 주었다. 자신이 요조—그의 작고 검은 신의 이름이었다—와 부지런히 상의해 왔는데, 요조가 두세 번에 걸쳐, 그리고 온갖 방식으로 강력히 주장하기를, 우리가 함께 항구의 포경선단 사이를 누비며 한마음으로 우리의 배를 고르는 대신에, 그러니까 그렇게 하는 대신에, 배를 고르는 일은 전적으로 내게 맡겨져야 한다고 요조가 간곡히 명했다는 것이다. 요조는 우리를 도우려는 뜻을 품고 있었고, 그렇게 하기 위해 이미 어떤 배 한 척을 점찍어 두었는데, 내게 맡겨 두기만 하면 나, 이슈메일은 마치 우연히 그렇게 된 것처럼 틀림없이 그 배를 발견하게 되리라는 것이었다. 그리고 그 배에 나는 당장 올라타야 하며, 당분간은 퀴퀘그와는 상관없이 그래야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n내가 미처 말하지 못한 것이 있는데, 여러 가지 일에서 퀴퀘그는 요조의 판단력이 뛰어나고 앞일을 내다보는 능력이 놀랍다고 크게 신뢰했으며, 요조를 상당한 경의로써 떠받들었다. 요조는 대체로 보아 제법 괜찮은 종류의 신으로, 아마도 전반적으로는 좋은 뜻을 품고 있으나, 늘 그 자비로운 계획을 성공시키지는 못하는 신이라고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n그런데 우리의 배를 고르는 일에 관한 이 퀴퀘그의 계획, 아니 차라리 요조의 계획이 나는 도무지 마음에 들지 않았다. 나는 우리와 우리의 운명을 안전하게 실어 줄 가장 알맞은 포경선을 가려내는 데 퀴퀘그의 명민함에 적잖이 기대를 걸고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 나의 온갖 항변도 퀴퀘그에게는 아무런 효과가 없었으므로, 나는 묵묵히 따를 수밖에 없었다. 그리하여 이 사소한 일을 얼른 처리해 버리려고, 단호하고 거침없는 일종의 기력과 활력으로 이 일에 착수할 준비를 했다. 이튿날 이른 아침, 퀴퀘그를 요조와 함께 우리의 작은 침실에 틀어박히게 둔 채—그날은 퀴퀘그와 요조에게 일종의 사순절이나 라마단, 곧 단식과 참회와 기도의 날인 듯했기 때문이다. 그것이 어떤 것인지 나는 끝내 알아낼 수 없었으니, 몇 차례나 알아보려 애썼건만 그의 예배 의식과 신앙 조문 서른아홉 개를 도무지 통달할 수 없었던 것이다—그렇게 퀴퀘그를 그의 토마호크 파이프 위에서 단식하게 두고, 요조를 대팻밥 제물의 불에 몸을 데우게 둔 채, 나는 배들 사이로 나섰다. 한참을 어슬렁거리고 닥치는 대로 이것저것 물어본 끝에, 나는 삼 년짜리 항해에 나설 배가 세 척 있다는 것을 알아냈다. 데블댐호와 티트빗호, 그리고 피쿼드(Pequod)호였다. 데블댐이라는 이름의 유래는 나도 모른다. 티트빗은 뻔하다. 피쿼드는, 여러분도 분명 기억하겠지만, 매사추세츠 인디언(Indian)의 어느 이름난 부족의 이름으로, 지금은 고대 메디아인들처럼 멸종해 버렸다. 나는 데블댐호를 이리저리 들여다보고 살펴보았고, 거기서 폴짝 건너뛰어 티트빗호로 갔으며, 마침내 피쿼드호에 올라 잠시 둘러본 뒤, 바로 이것이 우리에게 꼭 맞는 배라고 결정했다.\n\n여러분도 한평생 살아오면서 기묘한 배를 숱하게 보았을 것이다, 내가 어찌 알겠는가마는. 코가 네모진 러거선, 산더미 같은 일본 정크선, 버터 상자처럼 생긴 갤리엇선이며 그 밖에 이런저런 것들을. 그러나 내 말을 믿어 두기 바란다, 여러분은 이 진귀하고 오래된 피쿼드호만큼 진귀하고 오래된 배는 결코 본 적이 없을 것이다. 그것은 옛 학파의 배로, 굳이 따지자면 좀 작은 편이었고, 발톱처럼 휘어진 옛날식 다리를 단 듯한 분위기를 풍겼다. 사대양의 태풍과 무풍을 두루 겪으며 오래 묵고 비바람에 물든 그 낡은 선체의 빛깔은, 이집트와 시베리아에서 똑같이 싸워 온 프랑스 척탄병의 얼굴처럼 거뭇하게 그을려 있었다. 그 유서 깊은 이물은 수염이 난 듯 보였다. 그 돛대들은—원래의 것은 강풍에 바다로 잃어버려 일본 해안 어딘가에서 베어 온 것이었다—쾰른의 세 늙은 왕들의 등뼈처럼 빳빳이 솟아 있었다. 그 오래된 갑판은 닳고 주름져, 베킷이 피를 흘린 캔터베리 대성당의, 순례자들이 우러르며 밟은 포석 같았다. 그러나 이 모든 낡은 골동품 같은 면면에 더하여, 반세기 넘게 그 배가 좇아온 거친 일에 걸맞은 새롭고 경이로운 특징들이 덧붙여져 있었다. 늙은 펠레그(Peleg) 선장은 자신의 배를 따로 지휘하기 전 여러 해 동안 이 배의 일등항해사였고, 지금은 은퇴한 뱃사람이자 피쿼드호의 주요 선주 가운데 하나였는데—이 늙은 펠레그가 일등항해사로 있던 동안 그 본래의 기괴함 위에 무언가를 보태고, 그것을 온통 박아 넣어, 재료에서나 고안에서나 진기하기 그지없게 꾸며 놓았으니, 토르킬하케의 새겨진 방패나 침상을 빼고는 무엇과도 견줄 수 없을 정도였다. 그것은 마치 연마된 상아 장신구로 목을 묵직하게 두른 어느 야만적인 에티오피아 황제처럼 치장되어 있었다. 그것은 전리품으로 이루어진 물건이었다. 적의 깎아 낸 뼈로 제 몸을 꾸며 낸, 식인종 같은 배였다. 사방으로, 판자를 대지 않은 채 열려 있는 뱃전은 마치 하나로 이어진 턱주가리처럼, 향유고래(Sperm)의 길고 날카로운 이빨들로 장식되어 있었고, 그 이빨들은 못으로 박혀 있어 낡은 삼줄 힘줄과 밧줄을 단단히 매어 두는 데 쓰였다. 그 힘줄들은 뭍에서 난 목재로 만든 천한 도르래를 거치지 않고, 바다 상아로 깎은 활차 위로 솜씨 좋게 미끄러져 다녔다. 그 위엄 있는 키에 회전식 바퀴 따위는 거들떠보지도 않고, 그 배는 그 자리에 키손잡이를 자랑스레 달고 있었으니, 그 키손잡이는 한 덩어리로, 대대로 내려온 원수의 길고 좁은 아래턱뼈에서 기묘하게 깎아 낸 것이었다. 폭풍 속에서 그 키손잡이로 배를 모는 키잡이는, 사나운 준마의 턱을 움켜쥐어 그 기세를 누르는 타타르인이 된 듯한 기분이 들 터였다. 고귀한 배였으나, 어쩐지 더없이 우수에 잠긴 배였다! 모든 고귀한 것에는 그런 빛이 어려 있는 법이다.\n\n그런데 갑판 위를 둘러보며, 이 항해의 지원자로 나서 보려고 권한을 지닌 누군가를 찾으려 했을 때, 처음에는 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 큰 돛대 조금 뒤편에 쳐 놓은 기묘한 천막, 아니 차라리 위그웜이라 할 만한 것은 도무지 못 본 척할 수가 없었다. 그것은 항구에 있을 때 쓰는 일종의 임시 가설물인 듯했다. 그것은 원뿔 모양으로, 높이가 한 십 피트(약 3미터)쯤 되었고, 참고래의 턱뼈 한가운데와 가장 높은 부분에서 떼어 낸 길고 거대한, 휘어지는 검은 뼈판들로 이루어져 있었다. 그 넓은 끝을 갑판에 박아 놓고, 이 뼈판들을 둥글게 둘러 서로 엮어 비스듬히 맞대어 세웠으며, 꼭대기에서는 한데 모여 술이 달린 뾰족한 점을 이루었는데, 거기서 풀린 털 같은 섬유들이 어느 늙은 포타와토미 추장 머리 위의 상투처럼 이리저리 나부꼈다. 삼각형 입구가 뱃머리 쪽을 향하고 있어, 그 안에 든 자는 앞쪽을 온전히 내다볼 수 있었다.\n\n그리고 이 기묘한 거처 속에 반쯤 몸을 숨긴 채, 나는 마침내 그 풍모로 보아 권한을 지닌 듯한 사람 하나를 찾아냈다. 정오라 배의 일은 멈춰 있었고, 그는 이제 지휘의 짐에서 벗어나 한숨 돌리며 쉬고 있었다. 그는 온통 진기한 조각으로 꿈틀거리는 듯한 옛날식 떡갈나무 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 그 바닥은 위그웜을 짓는 데 쓰인 것과 똑같은 탄력 있는 재료를 튼튼하게 얽어 만든 것이었다.\n\n내가 본 그 노인의 겉모습에는 그리 특별한 데가 없었는지도 모른다. 그는 여느 늙은 뱃사람들처럼 까무잡잡하고 다부졌으며, 퀘이커식으로 재단한 푸른 파일럿 천을 두툼하게 둘러 입고 있었다. 다만 그의 두 눈 둘레에는 더없이 잘디잔 주름이 곱게, 거의 현미경으로나 볼 만큼 촘촘히 그물처럼 엮여 있었는데, 이는 숱한 거센 강풍 속을 끊임없이 항해하며 늘 바람 부는 쪽을 바라본 데서 비롯된 것이 분명했다. 그렇게 하면 눈가의 근육이 한데 오므라들기 때문이다. 그런 눈가 주름은 노려보는 표정에 아주 효과적이다.\n\n\"이 배가 피쿼드호의 선장이신가요?\" 나는 천막 입구로 다가서며 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람이 피쿼드호 선장이라 치자, 그래서 그한테 무얼 바라는 게냐?\" 그가 따져 물었다.\n\n\"배를 타 볼까 하고요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇단 말이지? 보아하니 자네는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람은 아니로군—깨진 보트에 타 본 적은 있나?\"\n\n\"아니요, 선생님, 한 번도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"포경에 대해서는 아무것도 모르겠지, 안 그래—응?\"\n\n\"전혀 모릅니다, 선생님. 그래도 곧 배우게 되리라는 건 의심치 않습니다. 상선 일로 항해를 여러 번 해 봤고, 그래서 제 생각엔—\"\n\n\"상선이고 나발이고 다 집어치워. 그딴 소리는 내게 늘어놓지 마라. 이 다리가 보이느냐?—한 번만 더 내게 상선 일 얘기를 꺼내면, 자네 꽁무니에서 이 다리를 떼어 내 줄 테다. 상선 일은 무슨! 이제 보니 자네는 그런 상선들에서 일했다는 게 어지간히 자랑스러운 모양이로군. 한데 고래꼬리에 맹세코! 이 사람아, 도대체 자네가 무엇 때문에 포경에 나서고 싶다는 거냐, 응?—좀 수상쩍지 않나, 그렇지 않아, 응?—해적질을 한 건 아니겠지, 안 그러냐?—마지막으로 탔던 선장을 털어먹은 건 아니겠지, 그렇지?—바다에 나가면 사관들을 죽일 생각을 품고 있는 건 아니렷다?\"\n\n나는 그런 일들에 대해 결백하다고 항변했다. 나는 이 반쯤 익살스러운 빈정거림의 가면 뒤에서, 외딴섬에 갇힌 퀘이커교도 낸터킷 사람인 이 늙은 뱃사람이 섬사람 특유의 편견으로 가득 차, 케이프코드나 비니어드 출신이 아닌 한 모든 외지인을 어지간히 미더워하지 않고 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"한데 자네는 무엇 때문에 포경에 나서겠다는 거냐? 자네를 배에 태울지 생각하기 전에 그것부터 알아야겠다.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 선생님, 저는 포경이 어떤 것인지 보고 싶습니다. 세상을 보고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"포경이 어떤 것인지 보고 싶다고, 응? 자네 에이해브(Ahab) 선장을 본 적이 있나?\"\n\n\"에이해브 선장이 누구신데요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그럴 줄 알았다. 에이해브 선장이 바로 이 배의 선장이야.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 잘못 알았군요. 저는 선장님과 직접 이야기하고 있는 줄 알았습니다.\"\n\n\"자네는 지금 펠레그 선장과 이야기하고 있는 거다—그게 자네가 지금 말 상대하고 있는 사람이지, 젊은이. 피쿼드호를 항해에 맞게 채비시키고, 선원을 포함해 필요한 모든 것을 갖추어 주는 일은 나와 빌대드(Bildad) 선장의 몫이야. 우리는 공동 선주이자 대리인이지. 한데 내가 말하려던 것처럼, 자네가 말하듯 정말로 포경이 어떤 것인지 알고 싶다면, 발 빼지 못할 만큼 단단히 매이기 전에 미리 그걸 알아낼 길을 일러 줄 수 있다. 에이해브 선장을 한번 똑똑히 보아라, 젊은이. 그러면 그에게 다리가 하나밖에 없다는 걸 알게 될 테니.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신가요, 선생님? 나머지 한쪽은 고래한테 잃은 건가요?\"\n\n\"고래한테 잃었느냐고! 젊은이, 내게 더 가까이 와 보아라. 그건 일찍이 보트를 박살 낸 것 중에서도 가장 괴물 같은 향유고래한테 통째로 먹히고, 씹히고, 으스러진 거다!—아, 아!\"\n\n나는 그의 격한 기세에 약간 놀랐고, 어쩌면 그 마지막 외침에 담긴 진심 어린 슬픔에 조금 마음이 동하기도 했지만, 되도록 차분하게 말했다. \"선생님 말씀이 틀림없이 사실이겠지요. 하지만 비록 그 사고라는 단순한 사실만으로도 그쯤은 짐작할 수 있었을지 모르나, 하필 그 고래에게 무슨 유별난 흉포함이 있었는지를 제가 어찌 알 수 있었겠습니까.\"\n\n\"이봐라, 젊은이, 자네 허파는 어딘가 물러 터졌어, 알겠나. 자네는 상어처럼 거칠게 말할 줄을 도무지 모른단 말이야. 자네, 분명히 전에 바다에 나가 본 적은 있겠지, 그건 확실하냐?\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 내가 말했다. \"제가 상선에서 네 번 항해했다고 말씀드린 줄 알았는데요—\"\n\n\"그딴 소리 당장 거두지 못해! 상선 일에 대해 내가 한 말을 명심하라고—날 더 약 올리지 마—그런 건 못 참아. 그러나 우리 서로 분명히 해 두자. 나는 자네에게 포경이 어떤 것인지 귀띔을 해 줬어. 그래도 여전히 그게 하고 싶은 마음이 드느냐?\"\n\n\"듭니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"아주 좋아. 그렇다면, 자네는 살아 있는 고래의 목구멍에 작살을 내리꽂고, 그러고 나서 그 뒤를 따라 뛰어들 만한 사내인가? 대답해, 어서!\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생님. 그게 정말로 없어서는 안 될 일이라면, 그러니까 도저히 피할 수 없는 일이라면 말입니다. 하지만 저는 그게 실제로 그런 일이라고는 생각지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"또 좋아. 자, 그렇다면 자네는 포경에 나서서 직접 겪어 봄으로써 포경이 어떤 것인지 알아내고 싶을 뿐 아니라, 세상을 보고 싶어서 나서겠다는 거지? 그렇게 말하지 않았나? 그런 줄 알았다. 그럼 저기 앞으로 좀 나가서, 바람 부는 쪽 뱃머리 너머를 한번 슬쩍 내다보고, 그러고는 내게 돌아와서 거기 무엇이 보이는지 말해 보아라.\"\n\n잠시 나는 이 기묘한 요청에 좀 어리둥절해서, 그것을 익살로 받아들여야 할지 진심으로 받아들여야 할지 도무지 알 수 없어 멈칫 서 있었다. 그러나 펠레그 선장은 그의 까마귀 발 같은 눈가 주름을 죄다 한데 모아 노려보며 나를 그 심부름길로 떠밀었다.\n\n앞으로 나아가 바람 부는 쪽 뱃머리 너머를 흘끗 내다보니, 밀물에 닻줄을 따라 흔들리던 배가 이제 비스듬히 망망대해 쪽을 가리키고 있는 것이 보였다. 전망은 끝이 없었으나, 더없이 단조롭고 황량했다. 내 눈에는 조금의 변화도 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 보고는 어떻게 됐나?\" 내가 돌아오자 펠레그가 말했다. \"무얼 보았느냐?\"\n\n\"별것 없었습니다.\" 내가 대답했다. \"물뿐이었어요. 수평선은 꽤 넓었고요, 그리고 돌풍이 몰려오고 있는 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"음, 그럼 세상을 본다는 것에 대해서는 어떻게 생각하나? 그걸 좀 더 보겠다고 케이프 혼을 빙 돌고 싶은 거냐, 응? 자네가 서 있는 그 자리에서도 세상이 보이지 않더냐?\"\n\n나는 좀 말문이 막혔으나, 어쨌든 나는 포경에 나서야 했고, 또 나설 작정이었다. 게다가 피쿼드호는 어느 배 못지않게 좋은 배였다—내 생각엔 가장 좋은 배였다—그래서 나는 이 모든 것을 이제 펠레그에게 거듭 말했다. 내가 그토록 단호한 것을 보고, 그는 나를 기꺼이 배에 태우겠노라고 밝혔다.\n\n\"그럼 자네는 곧장 서류에 서명하는 게 좋겠군.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"이리 따라오게나.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 앞장서서 갑판 아래 선실로 내려갔다.\n\n고물 가로판에 앉아 있는 것은 내게는 더없이 흔치 않고 놀라운 인물처럼 보였다. 그가 바로 빌대드 선장이었으니, 그는 펠레그 선장과 더불어 이 배의 최대 선주 가운데 하나였다. 다른 지분들은, 이런 항구에서 더러 그러하듯, 한 무리의 늙은 연금 수령자들—과부들, 아비 없는 아이들, 그리고 형평법원의 피후견인들—이 나누어 쥐고 있었는데, 저마다 배의 갑목 머리 하나쯤, 또는 널빤지 한 자쯤, 또는 못 한두 개쯤의 값어치를 소유하고 있었다. 낸터킷 사람들은 여러분이 짭짤한 이자가 붙는 검증된 국공채에 돈을 넣듯이, 포경선에 돈을 투자한다.\n\n그런데 빌대드는, 펠레그와 마찬가지로, 또 실은 다른 많은 낸터킷 사람들과 마찬가지로 퀘이커교도였다. 이 섬은 본래 그 종파가 정착해 세운 곳이었고, 오늘날까지도 그 주민들은 대체로 퀘이커교도 특유의 기질을 유난히 짙게 지니고 있으되, 다만 전혀 이질적이고 동떨어진 것들에 의해 가지각색으로, 또 야릇하게 변형되어 있을 따름이다. 바로 이 똑같은 퀘이커교도들 가운데 더러는 모든 뱃사람과 고래잡이를 통틀어 가장 잔혹하기 그지없는 자들이기 때문이다. 그들은 싸우는 퀘이커교도들이요, 앙심 품은 퀘이커교도들이다.\n\n그리하여 그들 가운데에는 이런 사람들의 예가 있다. 성경의 이름을 따서 이름 지어지고—이는 그 섬에서 유난히 흔한 풍습이었다—어린 시절에 자연스레 퀘이커 말투의 위엄 있고 극적인 그대와 너를 몸에 익혔으되, 그 뒤의 삶에서 대담하고 거침없으며 가없는 모험을 거치는 동안, 미처 벗어 버리지 못한 그 기질에 천 가지 대담한 기상의 획을 야릇하게 뒤섞어 놓은 자들이니, 그것은 스칸디나비아의 어느 바다 왕이나 시적인 이교도 로마인에게도 부끄럽지 않을 만한 것이었다. 그리고 이런 것들이, 둥근 머리통과 묵직한 심장을 지닌, 천성적으로 월등히 강한 기력을 타고난 사람 안에서 하나로 어우러질 때—더욱이 그 사람이 가장 외딴 바다에서, 이 북녘에서는 결코 볼 수 없는 별자리들 아래서, 숱한 긴 밤 당직의 정적과 고독을 통해 전통에 얽매이지 않고 독자적으로 사고하게 되고, 자연이 그 처녀같이 자발적이고 거리낌 없는 가슴으로부터 직접 건네는 달콤하거나 사나운 온갖 인상을 생생하게 받아들이며, 주로 그것을 통해, 그러나 우연한 이점들의 도움도 얼마간 받아, 대담하고 신경질적이며 드높은 언어를 익히게 될 때—그런 사람은 한 나라의 인구 전체를 통틀어 단 하나뿐인 존재가 되니, 고귀한 비극을 위해 빚어진 위풍당당한 장관(壯觀)의 피조물이다. 또한 극적으로 보아, 만일 그가 타고난 천성이나 다른 형편으로 말미암아 그 본성 밑바닥에 반쯤은 고의로 모든 것을 짓누르는 듯한 병적인 기질을 지니고 있다 한들, 그것이 결코 그의 품위를 깎아내리지는 않을 것이다. 비극적으로 위대한 모든 사람은 어떤 병적인 기질을 통해 그렇게 되는 법이기 때문이다. 이것만은 분명히 알아 두라, 오 젊은 야망이여, 인간의 모든 위대함이란 한낱 병에 지나지 않는 것임을. 그러나 아직 우리가 다룰 상대는 그런 인물이 아니라, 자못 다른 사람이다. 그래도 역시 한 인간이니, 만일 그가 정말로 별난 데가 있다면, 그것은 다시금 퀘이커교도의 또 다른 면모가 개인적 형편에 의해 변형되어 나온 것일 뿐이다.\n\n펠레그 선장과 마찬가지로, 빌대드 선장도 살림이 넉넉한, 은퇴한 고래잡이였다. 그러나 펠레그 선장과는 달리—이른바 진지한 것들 따위에는 손톱만큼도 마음을 쓰지 않았고, 실로 바로 그 진지하다는 것들을 세상에서 가장 하찮은 것으로 여겼던 펠레그와는 달리—빌대드 선장은 본래 낸터킷 퀘이커교 중에서도 가장 엄격한 종파의 가르침을 따라 자랐을 뿐 아니라, 그 뒤의 온 바다 생활도, 케이프 혼을 돌며 마주친 헐벗고 사랑스러운 숱한 섬 처녀들의 자태도—그 모든 것도 이 토박이 퀘이커교도를 털끝만큼도 움직이지 못했고, 그의 조끼 한 귀퉁이조차 바꿔 놓지 못했다. 그래도 이 모든 흔들림 없음에도 불구하고, 이 훌륭한 빌대드 선장에게는 어딘가 흔한 일관성이 결여된 데가 있었다. 양심상의 거리낌 때문에 뭍을 침범하는 자들에 맞서 무기를 드는 일은 거부하면서도, 정작 자신은 대서양과 태평양을 한없이 침범해 왔고, 사람의 피 흘리는 일이라면 맹세코 원수처럼 여기면서도, 정작 그 곧게 빠진 외투를 걸친 채 리바이어던의 피를 몇 통이고 몇 통이고 쏟아 왔던 것이다. 이제 사색에 잠긴 황혼의 나날에, 경건한 빌대드가 이런 일들을 회상 속에서 어떻게 화해시키는지 나는 모른다. 그러나 그것이 그를 그리 괴롭히는 것 같지는 않았고, 십중팔구 그는 이미 오래전에 현명하고 분별 있는 결론에 다다랐을 것이다—사람의 종교는 별개의 것이요, 이 현실 세계는 또 전혀 별개의 것이라는. 이 세계는 배당금을 지급한다. 가장 칙칙한 칙칙한 빛깔의 짧은 옷을 입은 작은 사환에서 출발하여, 넓은 청어 배처럼 불룩한 조끼를 입은 작살잡이로, 거기서 보트 지휘자로, 일등항해사로, 그리고 선장으로, 마침내 선주로 올라선 빌대드는, 앞서 내가 넌지시 비쳤듯이, 예순이라는 좋은 나이에 현역 생활에서 완전히 물러나 그 모험으로 가득한 인생을 마무리 짓고, 남은 날들을 마땅히 벌어들인 수입을 조용히 거두어들이는 데 바쳤다.\n\n그런데 빌대드는, 말하기 안됐지만, 구제 불능의 늙은 구두쇠라는 평판이 나 있었고, 바다를 누비던 시절에는 모질고 가혹한 감독자였다. 낸터킷에서 사람들이 내게 일러 준 바로는—분명 야릇한 이야기로 들리긴 하지만—그가 옛 카테것호를 몰던 시절, 선원들이 고향에 닿았을 때 거의 모두가 녹초가 되고 기진맥진하여 뭍으로 들려 나가 병원으로 실려 갔다고 한다. 경건한 사람치고는, 더구나 퀘이커교도치고는, 그는 적어도 어지간히 모진 마음을 지닌 자였던 셈이다. 다만 그는 부하들에게 욕설을 퍼붓는 일은 결코 없었다고들 했다. 그러나 어찌 된 영문인지 그는 그들에게서 잔혹하고 가차 없는 고된 일을 터무니없이 많이 짜냈다. 빌대드가 일등항해사였던 시절, 그 칙칙한 빛깔의 눈이 자네를 뚫어지게 바라보면, 자네는 무언가를—망치나 밧줄 꿰는 송곳 같은 것을—움켜쥐고서 무엇이든, 그게 무엇이든 상관없이, 미친 듯이 일에 매달리지 않고는 못 배길 만큼 완전히 안절부절못하게 되고 말았다. 게으름과 빈둥거림은 그의 앞에서 스러져 버렸다. 그 자신의 인품이야말로 그 실리적인 성격을 그대로 빚어낸 화신이었다. 길쭉하고 비쩍 마른 그 몸뚱이에는 군더더기 살 한 점, 군더더기 수염 한 올 없었고, 그 턱에는 챙 넓은 모자의 닳은 보풀처럼 부드럽고 알뜰한 보풀이 돋아 있었다.\n\n펠레그 선장을 따라 선실로 내려갔을 때 고물 가로판에 앉아 있던 사람이 바로 그런 인물이었다. 갑판 사이의 공간은 비좁았다. 그리고 거기, 늙은 빌대드가 꼿꼿이 앉아 있었다. 그는 늘 그렇게 앉았으며 결코 등을 기대지 않았으니, 이는 외투 자락을 아끼기 위해서였다. 그의 챙 넓은 모자는 곁에 놓여 있었고, 두 다리는 빳빳이 꼰 채였으며, 그 칙칙한 빛깔의 옷은 턱밑까지 단추가 채워져 있었다. 그리고 콧등에 안경을 걸친 채, 그는 묵직한 책 한 권을 읽는 데 골몰해 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"빌대드,\" 펠레그 선장이 외쳤다. \"또 그러고 있나, 빌대드, 응? 자네는 지난 삼십 년 동안 줄곧 그 성경을 들여다보고 있는 게야, 내가 똑똑히 아는 바로는. 어디까지 읽었나, 빌대드?\"\n\n오랜 뱃동무에게서 그런 불경스러운 소리를 듣는 데 이골이 난 듯, 빌대드는 그의 이번 불경함도 아랑곳하지 않고 조용히 눈을 들어, 나를 보더니, 다시 펠레그 쪽을 향해 묻는 듯한 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"이 친구가 자기가 우리 사람이라는군, 빌대드,\" 펠레그가 말했다. \"배를 타고 싶다는 거야.\"\n\n\"자네가 그러는가?\" 빌대드가 공허한 어조로 말하며 내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"제가 그러옵니다.\" 나도 모르게 그렇게 말했다. 그가 어찌나 철저한 퀘이커교도였던지.\n\n\"이 친구를 어떻게 보나, 빌대드?\" 펠레그가 말했다.\n\n\"쓸 만하겠어.\" 빌대드가 나를 찬찬히 보며 말하더니, 다시 또렷이 들리는 웅얼거리는 어조로 책을 한 자 한 자 짚어 나가기 시작했다.\n\n나는 그가 내가 본 중에서 가장 별난 늙은 퀘이커교도라고 생각했다. 더구나 그의 벗이자 오랜 뱃동무인 펠레그가 그토록 떠벌이는 자였으니 더욱 그러했다. 그러나 나는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 그저 주위를 날카롭게 둘러보기만 했다. 펠레그는 이제 궤짝 하나를 열어젖히고, 배의 계약 서류를 꺼내어, 펜과 잉크를 자기 앞에 놓더니, 작은 탁자 앞에 앉았다. 나는 이제 이 항해에 어떤 조건으로 응할 마음이 있는지 스스로 정해 둘 때가 되었다고 생각하기 시작했다. 나는 포경업에서는 삯을 지불하지 않는다는 것을 이미 알고 있었다. 다만 선장을 포함한 모든 선원이 레이(lay)라 불리는 이익의 일정한 몫을 받았으며, 이 레이는 배에 탄 사람들 각자의 임무가 지니는 중요도에 비례한다는 것도 알고 있었다. 나는 또한 포경에 풋내기인 만큼 내 몫의 레이는 그리 크지 않으리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러나 내가 바다에 익숙하고, 배를 몰 줄 알며, 밧줄을 이을 줄도 알고 그 밖에도 이런저런 것을 할 줄 안다는 점을 헤아릴 때, 내가 들은 바로 미루어 적어도 275번째 레이는 제안받으리라는 것을—다시 말해 항해의 순이익이 결국 얼마가 되든 그 275분의 1은 받으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않았다. 그리고 275번째 레이라는 것이 이른바 다소 박한 레이이긴 했으나, 그래도 없는 것보다는 나았다. 게다가 운 좋게 항해를 마친다면, 그 항해 동안 닳아 없앨 옷값쯤은 거의 메울 수 있을 만했고, 한 푼도 치를 필요 없는 삼 년치 쇠고기와 숙식은 더 말할 것도 없었다.\n\n이것이 왕후 같은 재산을 모으기에는 보잘것없는 방도라고 여겨질지도 모른다—과연 그러했으니, 참으로 보잘것없는 방도였다. 그러나 나는 왕후 같은 재산 따위로 안달복달하는 법이 없는 부류의 사람이라, 이 음산한 천둥 구름 간판 아래 묵고 있는 동안 세상이 내게 숙식만 베풀어 줄 채비가 되어 있다면 그것으로 흡족하다. 대체로 보아, 나는 275번째 레이가 적당한 셈이리라고 생각했으나, 어깨가 떡 벌어진 체격임을 헤아려 200번째를 제안받았다 한들 놀라지는 않았을 것이다.\n\n그렇지만, 그래도 한 가지, 내가 후한 몫의 이익을 받는 일에 대해 좀 미덥지 않게 여기게 만든 것이 있었으니 바로 이것이었다. 뭍에 있을 때, 나는 펠레그 선장과 그의 까닭 모를 늙은 단짝 빌대드에 관해 얼마간 이야기를 들었다. 그들이 피쿼드호의 주요 소유주인 까닭에, 나머지 더 보잘것없고 흩어진 선주들이 배의 사무 거의 전부를 이 두 사람에게 맡겨 두고 있다는 것이었다. 그리고 나로서는 그 인색한 늙은 빌대드가 선원 고용에 관해 막강한 발언권을 쥐고 있지나 않을까 알 수 없었으니, 더구나 이제 그가 피쿼드호 선상에서, 선실 안에 마치 제 집 난롯가에 있는 양 편안히 자리 잡고 앉아 성경을 읽고 있는 것을 보았으니 더욱 그러했다. 그런데 펠레그가 잭나이프로 펜을 다듬어 보려고 헛되이 애쓰는 동안, 늙은 빌대드는, 그가 이 일에 그토록 이해관계가 걸린 당사자임을 헤아릴 때 나로서는 적잖이 놀랍게도, 우리에게는 도무지 아랑곳하지 않고, 자기 책에서 혼자 웅얼웅얼 읽어 내려갈 따름이었다. \"너희를 위하여 보물을 땅에 쌓아 두지 마라. 거기는 좀이—\"\n\n\"이봐요, 빌대드 선장,\" 펠레그가 끼어들었다. \"어떻게 하겠나, 이 젊은 친구한테 어떤 레이를 줄까?\"\n\n\"자네가 가장 잘 알겠지.\" 무덤 속에서 나오는 듯한 대답이었다. \"칠백칠십칠 번째라면 너무 과하지는 않겠지, 안 그런가?—'거기는 좀과 동록이 해하나, 너희를 위하여—'\"\n\n레이라니, 참, 나는 생각했다, 그것도 그런 레이라니! 칠백칠십칠 번째라! 그래, 늙은 빌대드 양반, 당신은 적어도 나 하나만큼은, 좀과 동록이 해하는 이 땅 아래에 레이를 많이 쌓아 두지 못하게 할 작정인 게로군. 그것은 참으로 더없이 박한 레이였다. 그리고 그 숫자의 크기 때문에 뭍사람이라면 처음엔 속아 넘어갈지도 모르나, 조금만 헤아려 보면 알 수 있으니, 칠백칠십칠은 제법 큰 수이지만, 막상 그것의 몇 분의 일을 따지기 시작하면, 다시 말하건대, 1파딩의 칠백칠십칠 분의 일이 칠백칠십칠 닢의 금화 더블룬보다는 한참 적다는 것이 드러나는 것이다. 그래서 나는 그때 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n\"이런, 눈깔이 멀었나, 빌대드,\" 펠레그가 외쳤다. \"자네 이 젊은 친구를 등쳐 먹을 셈은 아니겠지! 이 친구는 그보다는 더 받아야 해.\"\n\n\"칠백칠십칠 번째.\" 빌대드가 눈도 들지 않고 다시 말하더니, 이어 웅얼웅얼 읊어 나갔다. \"네 보물 있는 그곳에는 네 마음도 있으리라.\"\n\n\"나는 이 친구를 삼백 번째로 적어 넣을 작정이야.\" 펠레그가 말했다. \"그 말 들었나, 빌대드! 삼백 번째 레이라고, 분명히 말하네.\"\n\n빌대드는 책을 내려놓고, 엄숙하게 그를 향해 돌아앉으며 말했다. \"펠레그 선장, 자네는 마음이 후한 사람일세. 하지만 이 배의 다른 선주들—그 가운데에는 과부와 고아도 많지—에게 진 자네의 의무를 헤아려야 하네. 만일 우리가 이 젊은이의 노고에 너무 후한 보답을 한다면, 우리는 그 과부들과 그 고아들의 입에서 빵을 앗아 가는 셈이 될지도 모르거든. 칠백칠십칠 번째 레이일세, 펠레그 선장.\"\n\n\"이런 빌대드 같으니라고!\" 펠레그가 벌떡 일어나 선실 안을 쿵쾅거리며 돌아다니며 고함쳤다. \"젠장맞을, 빌대드 선장, 이런 일에서 내가 자네 충고를 따랐더라면, 나는 지금쯤 케이프 혼을 돌아 항해한 그 어떤 큰 배라도 가라앉힐 만큼 묵직한 양심 덩어리를 짊어지고 다녀야 했을 게야.\"\n\n\"펠레그 선장,\" 빌대드가 차분하게 말했다. \"자네 양심이 물에 열 인치쯤 잠겨 있을지, 열 길쯤 잠겨 있을지, 나야 알 수 없네. 하지만 자네가 여전히 회개를 모르는 사람인지라, 펠레그 선장, 나는 자네 양심이 그저 물이 새는 양심에 지나지 않아, 끝내는 자네를 불구덩이 속으로 가라앉혀 버리지나 않을까 몹시 두렵다네, 펠레그 선장.\"\n\n\"불구덩이! 불구덩이라니! 자네 지금 나를 모욕하는 게야, 이 사람아. 인간이 도저히 참을 수 없을 만큼 나를 모욕하고 있어. 사람을 두고 지옥에 떨어지게 정해져 있다고 말하는 건 천하에 괘씸한 망발이야. 고래꼬리에 불벼락 맞을! 빌대드, 그 말을 내게 한 번만 더 해 봐, 내 영혼의 빗장이 튀어나가게, 그러면 나는—나는—그래, 나는 산 염소를 털이며 뿔이며 통째로 삼켜 버릴 테다. 선실에서 나가게, 이 위선이나 떠는 칙칙한 빛깔의, 나무 대포 같은 작자야—곧장 침로를 잡고 꺼지라고!\"\n\n이렇게 우레같이 내지르며 그는 빌대드에게 달려들었으나, 빌대드는 놀랍도록 비스듬히, 미끄러지듯 잽싸게 그때만큼은 그를 피해 버렸다.\n\n이 배의 가장 주요하고 책임 있는 두 선주 사이에서 벌어진 이 무시무시한 폭발에 깜짝 놀라, 또 이토록 미심쩍게 소유되고 임시로 지휘되는 배에 오를 생각을 죄다 접어 버릴까 하는 마음이 반쯤 들어, 나는 문간에서 비켜서서 빌대드가 나가도록 길을 터 주었다. 그가 깨어난 펠레그의 분노 앞에서 어서 사라지려 안달이 나 있으리라는 것을 나는 추호도 의심치 않았던 것이다. 그러나 놀랍게도, 그는 다시 고물 가로판에 아주 조용히 앉더니, 물러날 생각이라고는 털끝만큼도 없는 듯했다. 그는 회개를 모르는 펠레그와 그의 행태에 자못 이골이 난 모양이었다. 펠레그로 말하자면, 그렇게 분풀이를 다 하고 나니, 안에 더 남은 것이 없는 듯, 그 역시 어린양처럼 얌전히 앉았다. 다만 여전히 신경이 곤두선 듯 이따금 움찔거리기는 했다. \"휘유!\" 마침내 그가 휘파람을 불었다—\"돌풍이 바람 불어 가는 쪽으로 물러간 모양이야, 내 생각엔. 빌대드, 자네 예전에 창날 가는 솜씨가 좋았지, 이 펜 좀 다듬어 주게나. 내 잭나이프는 여기 숫돌이 필요하군. 옳지, 그거야. 고맙네, 빌대드. 자, 그럼, 젊은이, 자네 이름이 이슈메일이라 했던가? 좋아, 그렇다면 여기 적어 넣게나, 이슈메일, 삼백 번째 레이로.\"\n\n\"펠레그 선장,\" 내가 말했다. \"저와 함께 온 친구가 하나 있는데, 그 친구도 배를 타고 싶어 합니다—내일 데려와도 될까요?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\" 펠레그가 말했다. \"데려오게, 그러면 우리가 한번 보지.\"\n\n\"그 친구는 어떤 레이를 원하나?\" 다시 책 속에 파묻혀 있던 빌대드가 눈을 들며 끙 하고 물었다.\n\n\"오! 그건 자네가 신경 쓸 일이 아닐세, 빌대드.\" 펠레그가 말했다. \"그 친구는 포경을 해 본 적이 있나?\" 그가 내게로 돌아서며 물었다.\n\n\"제가 셀 수 없을 만큼 많은 고래를 잡았습니다, 펠레그 선장.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 데려오게나.\"\n\n그리하여 서류에 서명을 마치고 나는 떠났다. 내가 오전을 알차게 잘 보냈다는 것, 그리고 피쿼드호야말로 요조가 퀴퀘그와 나를 케이프 혼 너머로 실어 보내려고 마련해 둔 바로 그 배라는 것을 추호도 의심치 않으면서.\n\n그러나 얼마 가지 않아, 나는 정작 함께 항해할 그 선장을 아직 한 번도 보지 못했다는 데 생각이 미치기 시작했다. 하기야, 많은 경우에 포경선은 선장이 지휘를 맡으러 나타나 모습을 드러내기 전에 채비를 완전히 갖추고 선원들을 죄다 배에 태우기도 한다. 이런 항해는 때로 너무도 길게 이어지고, 고향에서 뭍에 머무는 기간은 더없이 짧은 까닭에, 선장이 가정을 두었거나 그런 종류의 마음 쓰이는 일이 있으면, 항구에 있는 배에는 그리 마음을 쓰지 않고, 출항 채비가 다 갖춰질 때까지 그것을 선주들에게 맡겨 두기 때문이다. 그러나 자신을 돌이킬 수 없이 그의 손에 맡기기 전에, 늘 한번 그를 보아 두는 편이 나은 법이다. 발길을 돌려 나는 펠레그 선장에게 다가가, 에이해브 선장을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 물었다.\n\n\"한데 에이해브 선장한테 무슨 볼일이 있느냐? 다 끝난 일이야. 자네는 배에 오르기로 된 몸이라고.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 그분을 좀 뵙고 싶어서요.\"\n\n\"하지만 지금은 자네가 그러지 못할 것 같군. 나도 그에게 정확히 무슨 일이 있는 건지는 모르겠으나, 그는 집 안에만 틀어박혀 있어. 일종의 병자 같으면서도, 또 그렇게 보이지도 않거든. 사실 그는 아픈 게 아니야. 하지만, 그렇다고 또 멀쩡한 것도 아니지. 어쨌든, 젊은이, 그는 나조차도 늘 만나 주지는 않으니, 자네라고 만나 줄 성싶지가 않군. 그는 별난 사람이야, 에이해브 선장은—더러는 그렇게들 여기지—하지만 좋은 사람이지. 오, 자네도 그를 충분히 좋아하게 될 거야. 걱정 말게, 걱정 마. 그는 위대하고, 불경하면서도, 신과 같은 사람이야, 에이해브 선장은. 말수는 적지만, 일단 입을 열면, 그때는 자네 귀를 기울이는 게 좋을 거야. 명심하게, 미리 일러 두는데, 에이해브는 여느 사람을 넘어선 인물이야. 에이해브는 대학에도 다녔고, 식인종 사이에서도 살아 봤어. 파도보다 더 깊은 경이를 겪어 왔고, 고래보다 더 강하고 더 기이한 적들에게 그 불타는 창을 꽂아 왔지. 그의 창이라니! 그래, 우리 섬을 통틀어 가장 날카롭고 가장 어김없는 창이지! 오! 그는 빌대드 선장이 아니야. 그렇고말고, 펠레그 선장도 아니지. 그는 에이해브야, 이 사람아. 그리고 옛적의 에이해브는, 자네도 알다시피, 왕관을 쓴 왕이었지!\"\n\n\"게다가 아주 사악한 왕이었지요. 그 못된 왕이 죽임을 당했을 때, 개들이 그의 피를 핥지 않았던가요?\"\n\n\"이리 가까이 와 보게나—이리로, 이리로.\" 펠레그가 거의 나를 흠칫하게 할 만큼 의미심장한 눈빛으로 말했다. \"이봐라, 젊은이. 피쿼드호 위에서는 결코 그런 말을 하지 마라. 어디서든 절대 하지 마. 에이해브 선장이 스스로 제 이름을 지은 게 아니야. 그건 그의 미친, 과부가 된 어머니의 어리석고 무지한 변덕이었어. 그 어머니는 그가 겨우 한 살밖에 안 됐을 때 죽었지. 그런데도 게이헤드의 늙은 무녀 티스티그는 그 이름이 어쩐지 예언처럼 들어맞으리라고 했어. 그리고 어쩌면 그 여자 같은 다른 바보들도 자네한테 똑같은 소리를 할지 모르지. 나는 자네에게 미리 일러 두고 싶네. 그건 거짓말이야. 나는 에이해브 선장을 잘 알아. 몇 해 전에 그와 항해사로서 함께 항해했거든. 나는 그가 어떤 사람인지 알아—좋은 사람이지—빌대드처럼 경건한 좋은 사람이 아니라, 욕설을 입에 달고 사는 좋은 사람이야—나하고 좀 비슷한 부류지—다만 그가 나보다 훨씬 더 큰 그릇일 뿐이야. 그래, 그래, 그가 한 번도 그리 유쾌한 사람이 아니었던 건 나도 알아. 그리고 고향으로 돌아오는 항해에서 한동안 그가 좀 제정신이 아니었던 것도 알지. 하지만 그건 그 피 흘리는 다리 토막에서 솟구치는 날카로운 통증 때문에 그리된 거였어, 누구라도 알 만한 일이지. 또 나는 알아, 지난 항해에서 그 저주받은 고래한테 다리를 잃은 뒤로, 그가 줄곧 일종의 침울한—지독하게 침울하고, 때로는 사나운 사람이 되어 버린 것을. 하지만 그것도 다 지나갈 거야. 그리고 마지막으로 단단히 일러 두는데, 젊은이, 잘 새겨 두게나, 웃음 헤픈 나쁜 선장과 함께 항해하느니 침울한 좋은 선장과 함께 항해하는 편이 나은 법이야. 그러니 잘 가게나—그리고 에이해브 선장이 마침 사악한 이름을 지녔다 하여 그를 함부로 깎아내리지 말게. 게다가, 이 사람아, 그에게는 아내가 있어—혼인한 지 세 번의 항해도 채 안 된, 다정하고 순종적인 처녀라네. 생각해 보게나. 그 다정한 처녀를 통해 그 노인에게는 자식도 하나 있어. 그러니 자네, 에이해브에게 어떤 철저하고 가망 없는 악이 깃들어 있을 수 있겠나? 아닐세, 아니야, 이 사람아. 비록 그가 얻어맞고 짓밟혀 망가졌다 한들, 에이해브에게도 인간다운 따스함은 있다네!\"\n\n발걸음을 옮기는 동안, 나는 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 에이해브 선장에 관해 우연히 내게 드러난 것들이, 그에 대한 어떤 거칠고 막연한 고통스러움으로 나를 가득 채웠다. 그리고 어쩐지, 그때 나는 그에게 동정과 슬픔을 느꼈는데, 그것이 무엇 때문이었는지는 모르겠다, 그의 잔혹한 다리 상실 때문이 아니라면 말이다. 그러면서도 나는 그에게 야릇한 경외감 또한 느꼈다. 그러나 그 경외감이라는 것은, 내가 도무지 설명할 수 없는 종류의 것이어서, 정확히 말하면 경외감도 아니었다. 그것이 무엇이었는지 나는 모른다. 그러나 나는 그것을 느꼈다. 그리고 그것이 나를 그에게서 멀어지게 하지는 않았다. 다만 그때 그가 내게 그토록 어렴풋이만 알려진 까닭에, 그에게 깃든 듯한 그 수수께끼 같은 것에 나는 조바심을 느꼈다. 그러나 내 생각은 마침내 다른 방향으로 흘러갔고, 그리하여 당분간 어두운 에이해브는 내 마음에서 미끄러져 나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 17. The Ramadan.\n\n퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 라마단, 곧 단식과 고행은 온종일 이어질 예정이었으므로, 나는 해 질 무렵까지 그를 방해하지 않기로 했다. 나는 그것이 아무리 우스꽝스럽더라도 누구의 종교적 의무든 더없이 존중하는 사람이라, 두꺼비버섯을 숭배하는 개미 떼라 한들 그 모임을 함부로 깔보는 마음을 도무지 품을 수 없는 까닭이다. 또한 우리 지구의 어떤 지역에 사는 또 다른 피조물들, 다른 행성에서는 결코 볼 수 없는 정도의 노예근성으로, 어느 죽은 지주의 몸뚱이 앞에 머리를 조아리는 자들 역시 마찬가지다. 그것도 다만 그자의 이름으로 여전히 소유되고 임대되는 터무니없이 막대한 재산 때문에 그러는 것일 뿐인데도 말이다.\n\n내 말은, 우리 선량한 장로교 기독교인들은 이런 문제에 있어 너그러워야 하며, 이교도든 무엇이든 다른 인간들이 이런 주제에 관해 반쯤 미친 망상을 품고 있다 해서 우리가 그들보다 그토록 까마득히 우월하다고 자만해서는 안 된다는 것이다. 자, 여기 퀴퀘그가 있었다. 그는 분명 요조(Yojo)와 자기 라마단에 관해 더없이 터무니없는 생각들을 품고 있었다 — 그러나 그게 무슨 상관인가? 퀴퀘그는 자기가 무얼 하는지 안다고 여기는 듯했고, 그것으로 만족하는 듯했다. 그러니 그냥 내버려 두자. 우리가 아무리 그와 논쟁한들 아무 소용이 없을 터, 그저 내버려 두자는 것이다. 그리고 하늘이여, 우리 모두에게 — 장로교도든 이교도든 똑같이 — 자비를 베푸소서. 왜냐하면 우리는 모두 어쩐지 머릿속이 지독히도 금이 가 있고, 안타깝게도 손질이 절실히 필요한 자들이니.\n\n저녁 무렵, 그의 모든 의식과 예배가 틀림없이 끝났으리라 확신이 들었을 때, 나는 그의 방으로 올라가 문을 두드렸다. 그러나 대답이 없었다. 문을 열어 보려 했으나 안에서 잠겨 있었다. \"퀴퀘그.\" 나는 열쇠 구멍에 대고 나직이 불렀다. 사방이 고요했다. \"이봐, 퀴퀘그! 왜 말을 안 해? 날세 — 이슈메일이야.\" 그러나 여전히 아까처럼 잠잠했다. 나는 슬슬 겁이 나기 시작했다. 그에게 그토록 넉넉한 시간을 주었으니, 혹시 뇌졸중이라도 일으킨 게 아닐까 싶었다. 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보았으나, 문이 방의 묘한 구석으로 열리게 되어 있어 열쇠 구멍으로 보이는 광경은 비뚜름하고 불길하기만 했다. 침대 발치 판자의 일부와 벽의 한 줄기만 보일 뿐, 그 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 그런데 벽에 기대 세워진 퀴퀘그의 작살 나무 자루가 눈에 들어와 나는 놀랐다. 그것은 전날 저녁 우리가 방으로 올라가기 전에 안주인이 그에게서 빼앗아 두었던 것이었다. 이상한걸, 하고 나는 생각했다. 그러나 어쨌든 작살이 저기 서 있고, 그는 그것 없이는 좀처럼, 아니 결코 밖에 나가는 법이 없으니, 그렇다면 그는 틀림없이 이 안에 있는 것이다. 추호도 틀릴 리 없었다.\n\n\"퀴퀘그! — 퀴퀘그!\" — 여전히 고요했다. 무슨 일이 벌어진 게 분명했다. 뇌졸중이다! 나는 문을 부수어 열려고 했으나, 문은 완강히 버텼다. 아래층으로 달려 내려가 처음 마주친 사람 — 객실 하녀 — 에게 재빨리 내 의심을 털어놓았다. \"어머! 어머나!\" 그녀가 소리쳤다. \"뭔가 일이 났구나 했어요. 아침 식사 후에 침대를 정돈하러 갔더니 문이 잠겨 있고, 쥐새끼 한 마리 소리도 안 나는 거예요. 그 뒤로 줄곧 그렇게 조용했죠. 하지만 전 두 분이 함께 어디 나가시면서 짐을 안전하게 두려고 안에 가둬 잠가 두신 줄로만 알았어요. 어머! 어머나, 마님! — 마님! 살인이에요! 허시 부인! 뇌졸중이라고요!\" — 그러고는 이런 외침과 함께 부엌으로 달려갔고, 나도 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n허시 부인이 곧 나타났는데, 한 손에는 겨자 단지를, 다른 손에는 식초병을 들고 있었다. 마침 양념병 받침대를 손보는 일에서 막 손을 떼고, 그새 자기네 흑인 꼬마를 야단치던 참이었다.\n\n\"장작 헛간!\" 내가 소리쳤다. \"그게 어느 쪽이오? 제발 빨리 달려가서 문 비틀어 열 만한 걸 가져와요 — 도끼! — 도끼 말이오! 그가 졸도했단 말이오. 틀림없소!\" — 그렇게 외치며 나는 빈손으로 다시 정신없이 위층으로 돌진하려 했는데, 허시 부인이 겨자 단지와 식초병을, 그리고 자기 얼굴이라는 양념병 받침대 통째로 내 앞을 가로막았다.\n\n\"젊은이, 대체 무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"도끼를 가져와요! 제발 누구든 의사를 부르러 달려가요, 내가 문을 비틀어 여는 동안!\"\n\n\"여보쇼.\" 안주인은 한 손을 자유롭게 하려고 재빨리 식초병을 내려놓으며 말했다. \"여보쇼, 지금 내 집 문짝을 비틀어 연다는 거요?\" — 그러더니 내 팔을 붙들었다. \"젊은이, 대체 무슨 일이오? 무슨 일이냐니까, 뱃사람 양반?\"\n\n나는 되도록 차분하면서도 빠른 어조로 사정의 전말을 그녀에게 알아듣게 설명했다. 그녀는 무심결에 식초병을 콧등 한쪽에 갖다 댄 채 잠시 곰곰이 생각하더니, 이윽고 외쳤다. \"아니! 내가 거기 두고 나선 그걸 본 적이 없는데.\" 그녀는 계단 층계참 아래의 작은 벽장으로 달려가 안을 흘긋 들여다보더니, 돌아와서는 퀴퀘그의 작살이 없어졌다고 내게 말했다. \"그 사람이 자살했어.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"불운한 스티그스 사건이 또 되풀이되는구먼 — 침대보 한 장 또 날아가게 생겼네 — 하느님, 그 가엾은 어미를 불쌍히 여기소서! — 우리 집안 망조가 들겠어. 그 가엾은 젊은이한테 누이가 있나? 그 계집애 어디 갔어? — 거기, 베티, 화가 스내얼스한테 가서 간판 하나 그려 달라고 해라, 이렇게 — '이곳에서는 자살 금지, 응접실 흡연 금지'라고. 이왕이면 한꺼번에 두 마리 새를 잡는 게 낫지. 죽인다고? 주여, 그 영혼에 자비를 베푸소서! 저기 저 소리는 뭐야? 이봐, 젊은이, 거기 멈춰!\"\n\n그러고는 내 뒤를 쫓아 올라와, 내가 다시 문을 억지로 열려고 하는 참에 나를 붙잡았다.\n\n\"안 돼, 그건 허락 못 해. 내 집을 망가뜨리게 둘 순 없지. 자물쇠장수한테 가요, 여기서 한 마일쯤 가면 하나 있으니까. 아니, 잠깐!\" 그녀는 옆 주머니에 손을 넣으며 말했다. \"여기 맞을 만한 열쇠가 있어, 아마. 어디 보자.\" 그러면서 그녀는 자물쇠에 열쇠를 넣고 돌렸다. 그러나 아아! 퀴퀘그가 안에서 따로 질러 둔 빗장은 풀리지 않은 채였다.\n\n\"부수어 열 수밖에 없겠소.\" 나는 이렇게 말하고, 제대로 도움닫기를 하려고 복도를 조금 뒤로 달려 내려갔다. 그러자 안주인이 다시 내 집을 부수지 못하게 하겠노라 다짐하며 나를 붙들었다. 그러나 나는 그녀에게서 몸을 떼어 내고, 온몸을 돌연 내던지듯 표적을 향해 정면으로 부딪쳐 갔다.\n\n엄청난 소리와 함께 문이 활짝 열렸고, 문손잡이가 벽에 쾅 부딪치며 회반죽이 천장까지 튀어 올랐다. 그리고 거기에 — 맙소사! — 거기에 퀴퀘그가 앉아 있었다. 더없이 침착하고 평온한 모습으로, 방 한복판에 떡하니, 엉덩이를 깔고 쪼그려 앉아, 요조를 자기 머리 꼭대기에 얹은 채로. 그는 이쪽도 저쪽도 보지 않고, 살아 움직이는 기색이라곤 거의 없이, 마치 조각상처럼 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"퀴퀘그.\" 나는 그에게 다가가며 말했다. \"퀴퀘그, 자네 대체 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"이 양반 온종일 저러고 앉아 있었던 거 아니우?\" 안주인이 말했다.\n\n그러나 우리가 무슨 말을 하든, 그에게서는 한마디도 끌어낼 수 없었다. 나는 그의 자세를 바꿔 주려고 그를 밀어 넘어뜨리고 싶은 심정마저 들었다. 그 모습이 거의 견딜 수 없을 만큼, 너무도 고통스럽고 부자연스럽게 옥죄여 보였기 때문이다. 더구나 그는 정해진 끼니마저 거른 채 십중팔구 여덟 시간이고 열 시간이고 그렇게 앉아 있었을 터였다.\n\n\"허시 부인.\" 나는 말했다. \"어쨌든 이 사람은 살아 있소. 그러니 괜찮으시다면 우리를 좀 놔두시오. 이 기묘한 일은 내가 직접 처리하리다.\"\n\n안주인을 내보내고 문을 닫은 뒤, 나는 퀴퀘그에게 의자에 앉으라고 설득해 보려 애썼다. 그러나 헛수고였다. 그는 그대로 앉아 있었다. 내가 아무리 정중한 재간과 살가운 말로 구슬려도 — 그는 손톱만큼도 움직이려 들지 않았고, 단 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 나를 쳐다보지도, 내 존재를 조금도 알은체하지도 않았다.\n\n이것도 어쩌면 그의 라마단의 일부일까, 하고 나는 생각했다. 그의 고향 섬에서는 저렇게 엉덩이를 깔고 단식을 하는 걸까. 그렇겠지. 그래, 이것도 그의 신조의 일부인가 보다. 그렇다면 그냥 내버려 두자. 그도 머잖아 일어날 게 틀림없다. 영원히 갈 수야 없으니 다행이고, 게다가 그의 라마단은 일 년에 한 번뿐이지 않은가. 더구나 그때도 그리 정확하게 지켜지는 것 같지는 않으니.\n\n나는 저녁을 먹으러 내려갔다. 자기들 말로 '건포도 푸딩 항해'라 부르는 항해에서 막 돌아온 몇몇 선원들의 긴 이야기를 한참 듣고 난 뒤였다(그것은 대서양에서만, 적도 이북에 한정해 스쿠너나 브리그선으로 다녀오는 짧은 고래잡이 항해를 일컫는 말이다). 이 건포도 푸딩 선원들의 이야기를 거의 열한 시까지 듣고 나서, 나는 잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갔다. 이쯤이면 퀴퀘그도 분명 자기 라마단을 끝마쳤으리라 굳게 믿으면서. 그러나 아니었다. 그는 내가 두고 간 바로 그 자리에 그대로 있었다. 한 치도 움직이지 않았다. 나는 슬슬 그가 짜증스러워지기 시작했다. 추운 방 안에서 머리에 나무 조각을 얹은 채, 온종일 그리고 한밤중까지 엉덩이를 깔고 앉아 있다니, 너무도 어처구니없고 제정신이 아닌 짓으로만 보였다.\n\n\"제발 좀, 퀴퀘그, 일어나서 몸 좀 추슬러. 일어나서 저녁이라도 먹게. 굶어 죽겠어. 자네 스스로 목숨을 끊는 셈이야, 퀴퀘그.\" 그러나 그는 한마디도 대답하지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 나는 그에게 단념하고, 잠자리에 들어 자기로 마음먹었다. 머잖아 그도 분명 나를 따라 잘 터였다. 그러나 누워 들기 전에, 나는 내 묵직한 곰가죽 외투를 가져다 그에게 덮어 주었다. 밤이 몹시 추워질 듯한데, 그는 평소의 둥근 짧은 외투 하나만 걸치고 있었기 때문이다. 한동안 나는 무슨 짓을 해도 도무지 어렴풋한 졸음조차 들지 못했다. 촛불은 꺼 둔 터였다. 그런데 퀴퀘그가 — 네 자도 채 떨어지지 않은 곳에서 — 그 불편한 자세로, 춥고 어두운 가운데 홀로 외따로 앉아 있다는 생각만으로도, 나는 정말이지 비참한 심정이 되었다. 생각해 보라. 이 음울하고 알 수 없는 라마단을 치르느라 멀쩡히 깨어 엉덩이를 깔고 앉아 있는 이교도와 한방에서 밤새 자야 한다니!\n\n그러나 어찌어찌 나는 마침내 잠이 들었고, 동틀 녘까지 아무것도 알지 못했다. 그러다 침대맡 너머로 내려다보니, 퀴퀘그가 거기 쪼그려 앉아 있었다. 마치 바닥에 나사못으로 박혀 고정된 듯이. 그러나 햇살의 첫 자락이 창으로 들어오자마자 그는 벌떡 일어났다. 뻣뻣하게 굳어 삐걱대는 관절을 하고서도 명랑한 얼굴로, 내가 누운 쪽으로 절뚝거리며 다가와, 다시 자기 이마를 내 이마에 갖다 대고는, 자기 라마단이 끝났노라고 말했다.\n\n자, 앞서 넌지시 말했듯이, 나는 그 어떤 사람의 종교든, 그것이 무엇이든 간에 이의가 없다. 다만 그 사람이 다른 사람이 그 종교를 함께 믿지 않는다는 이유로 그 사람을 죽이거나 모욕하지 않는 한에서다. 그러나 한 사람의 종교가 정말로 광적으로 치닫고, 그것이 그에게 명백한 고문이 되며, 끝내는 우리의 이 땅을 머물기 거북한 여인숙으로 만들어 버린다면 — 그때야말로 그 개인을 한쪽으로 데려가 그 점을 두고 따져 볼 때라고 나는 생각한다.\n\n그리고 바로 그렇게, 나는 이제 퀴퀘그에게 그리했다. \"퀴퀘그.\" 나는 말했다. \"이제 침대로 들어가서 누워 내 말을 좀 들어 보게.\" 그러고는 원시 종교의 발생과 발전에서 시작해 오늘날의 여러 종교에 이르기까지 이야기를 풀어 나갔다. 그러는 동안 나는 이 모든 사순절이며 라마단이며, 춥고 음산한 방에서 엉덩이를 깔고 오래도록 쪼그려 앉는 짓들이 새빨간 헛소리이며, 건강에 해롭고, 영혼에 무익하며, 한마디로 명백한 위생의 법칙과 상식에 어긋나는 것임을 퀴퀘그에게 보여 주려 애썼다. 또한 나는 그에게, 다른 일에 있어서는 그토록 더없이 분별 있고 명민한 야만인인 그가, 지금 이 우스꽝스러운 라마단에 관해서는 이토록 한심하리만치 어리석은 꼴을 보이니 내 마음이 아프다고, 몹시도 아프다고 일러 주었다. 게다가 단식은 몸을 움푹 꺼지게 하고, 따라서 정신도 움푹 꺼지게 하며, 단식에서 태어난 온갖 생각은 필연코 반쯤 굶주린 것일 수밖에 없다고 나는 따져 말했다. 바로 이런 까닭에 소화불량에 걸린 신앙가들 대다수가 자기네 내세에 관해 그토록 우울한 관념을 품는 것이다. 한마디로 말해, 퀴퀘그, 하고 나는 다소 옆길로 새며 말했다. 지옥이란 소화되지 않은 사과 경단에서 처음 생겨난 관념이며, 그 뒤로 라마단이 길러 낸 대물림 소화불량을 통해 줄곧 이어져 온 것이라고.\n\n이어서 나는 퀴퀘그에게 그 자신이 소화불량으로 고생한 적이 있는지 물었다. 그가 알아들을 수 있도록 아주 쉽게 풀어서 표현했다. 그는 없다고 대답했다. 다만 한 번 잊지 못할 일이 있었다고 했다. 그것은 그의 아버지인 왕이 큰 전투에서 승리한 것을 기념해 베푼 성대한 잔치 뒤의 일이었는데, 그 전투에서는 오후 두 시쯤까지 적군 쉰 명이 죽임을 당했고, 바로 그날 저녁에 그 모두를 요리해 먹어 치웠다는 것이었다.\n\n\"그만하게, 퀴퀘그.\" 나는 몸서리치며 말했다. \"그쯤이면 됐어.\" 그가 더 넌지시 일러 주지 않아도 나는 그 결말을 이미 알고 있었기 때문이다. 나는 바로 그 섬을 다녀온 어느 선원을 본 적이 있는데, 그가 내게 말하기를, 거기서는 큰 전투에서 이기면 죽은 자들을 모조리 승리자의 마당이나 정원에서 통째로 구워 내는 풍습이 있다고 했다. 그런 다음 하나씩 커다란 나무 쟁반에 담아, 필라우 요리처럼 빵나무 열매와 야자열매로 둘레를 곁들이고, 입에는 파슬리를 물려, 승리자의 인사와 함께 그의 모든 친구들에게 돌렸다는 것이다. 마치 그 선물들이 크리스마스 칠면조라도 되는 양 말이다.\n\n결국, 종교에 관한 내 이야기가 퀴퀘그에게 그리 큰 인상을 남긴 것 같지는 않다. 왜냐하면, 첫째로 그는 그 중대한 주제에 관해서는 어쩐지 귀가 어두운 듯했기 때문이다 — 그것이 자기 자신의 관점에서 다뤄지지 않는 한에는. 둘째로, 내가 아무리 내 생각을 단순하게 풀어 놓아도 그는 그것을 삼분의 일도 채 이해하지 못했다. 그리고 끝으로, 그는 의심할 바 없이 참된 종교에 관해서라면 나보다 자기가 훨씬 더 많이 안다고 여겼다. 그는 일종의 너그러운 염려와 측은함이 어린 눈길로 나를 바라보았다. 마치 이토록 분별 있는 젊은이가 복음에 충실한 이교도의 경건함에서 이렇게나 가망 없이 벗어나 있다니 참으로 딱하다고 여기는 듯이.\n\n마침내 우리는 일어나 옷을 입었다. 그리고 퀴퀘그는 온갖 종류의 차우더로 엄청나게 푸짐한 아침 식사를 했는데 — 자기 라마단 탓에 안주인이 큰 이득을 보지 못하게 하려는 셈이었다 — 우리는 페쿼드호(Pequod)에 오르러 길을 나섰다. 어슬렁어슬렁 거닐면서, 넙치 뼈로 이를 쑤시며.\n\n# CHAPTER 18. His Mark.\n\n우리가 부두 끝을 따라 배 쪽으로 걸어 내려갈 때, 퀴케그(Queequeg)는 작살을 메고 있었고, 펠레그(Peleg) 선장은 자기 천막 안에서 그 무뚝뚝한 목소리로 우리를 향해 큰 소리로 불러 댔다. 내 친구가 식인종일 줄은 미처 몰랐다며, 게다가 미리 서류를 내보이지 않는 한 그 어떤 식인종도 저 배에 태우지 않겠다고 선언하는 것이었다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 말씀입니까, 펠레그 선장님?\" 나는 이렇게 말하며 뱃전 위로 펄쩍 뛰어올랐고, 내 동료는 부두에 그대로 세워 둔 채였다.\n\n\"내 말은,\" 그가 대답했다. \"저자가 서류를 내보여야 한다는 거다.\"\n\n\"그렇지,\" 빌대드(Bildad) 선장이 그 공허한 목소리로 펠레그의 등 뒤, 천막 안에서 고개를 쑥 내밀며 말했다. \"저자가 개종했다는 걸 증명해야지. 어둠의 자식아,\" 그는 퀴케그를 향해 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"그대는 지금 어느 기독교 교회와 교통(交通)하고 있는가?\"\n\n\"왜요,\" 내가 말했다. \"이 친구는 제일회중교회의 신자입니다.\" 여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 낸터킷(Nantucket) 배를 타는 문신 새긴 야만인들 가운데 많은 수가 끝내 교회로 개종해 들어오게 된다.\n\n\"제일회중교회,\" 빌대드가 외쳤다. \"뭐라고! 듀터로노미 콜먼 집사의 예배당에서 예배 보는 그 교회 말이냐?\" 이렇게 말하면서 그는 안경을 꺼내 그 커다란 노란색 반다나 손수건으로 닦은 다음, 아주 조심스레 쓰고는 천막 밖으로 나와, 뱃전 위로 뻣뻣하게 몸을 기울인 채 퀴케그를 한참 동안 찬찬히 뜯어보았다.\n\n\"신자가 된 지는 얼마나 되었느냐?\" 이윽고 그가 내게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"그리 오래되지는 않았겠지, 내 짐작에는, 젊은이.\"\n\n\"아니지,\" 펠레그가 말했다. \"그리고 세례도 제대로 받지 못했어, 아니었으면 그 얼굴에서 악마의 푸른 물이 얼마쯤은 씻겨 나갔을 텐데 말이야.\"\n\n\"어디 말해 보게나,\" 빌대드가 외쳤다. \"이 필리스티아 사람이 듀터로노미 집사의 예배당에 정식으로 다니는 신자란 말인가? 나는 저 사람이 거기 가는 걸 한 번도 본 적이 없네, 나는 주일마다 그 앞을 지나가는데도 말이야.\"\n\n\"저는 듀터로노미 집사나 그 예배당에 대해서는 아무것도 모릅니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"제가 아는 거라곤 여기 이 퀴케그가 제일회중교회의 타고난 신자라는 것뿐입니다. 퀴케그는 그 자신이 집사예요.\"\n\n\"젊은이,\" 빌대드가 엄하게 말했다. \"그대는 지금 나를 놀리고 있구나 — 똑똑히 밝혀 보아라, 이 젊은 히타이트 사람아. 그대가 말하는 교회가 대체 어떤 교회냐? 내게 대답하라.\"\n\n이렇게 궁지에 몰린 나는 이렇게 대답했다. \"제 말은요, 선장님, 선장님과 저, 그리고 저기 펠레그 선장님, 또 여기 퀴케그, 우리 모두와, 어머니 배에서 난 모든 아들과 영혼이 다 함께 속해 있는 바로 그 오래된 보편 교회 말입니다. 예배 드리는 이 온 세상의 위대하고 영원한 제일회중 말입니다. 우리는 모두 거기에 속해 있어요. 다만 우리 중 더러는 그 위대한 믿음과는 조금도 상관없는 별난 고집을 품고 있을 뿐, 바로 그 믿음 안에서 우리는 모두 손을 맞잡는 거죠.\"\n\n\"맞이음이지, 그대가 말하려는 건 손을 맞이음하는 거야.\" 펠레그가 더 가까이 다가오며 외쳤다. \"젊은이, 자네는 앞돛대 갑판원보다 선교사로 배를 타는 편이 낫겠어. 그렇게 훌륭한 설교는 들어 본 적이 없거든. 듀터로노미 집사라니 — 아니, 매플(Mapple) 신부님조차도 그걸 당해 내지 못할걸세, 그분도 대단한 분으로 쳐주는데 말이야. 자, 배에 오르게, 어서 오르게나. 서류 따위는 신경 쓸 것 없어. 그러니까, 저기 저 쿼호그에게 일러 주게나 — 저 친구를 뭐라고 부른다고 했지? 쿼호그에게 어서 오라고 일러 주게. 저 거대한 닻에 두고 맹세컨대, 정말이지 굉장한 작살을 들고 있군! 좋은 물건 같은데, 다루는 솜씨도 제법이야. 이봐, 쿼호그, 아니 자네 이름이 뭐든 간에, 자네 고래잡이 보트의 뱃머리에 서 본 적이 있나? 물고기를 작살로 찔러 본 적은 있고?\"\n\n퀴케그는 한마디도 하지 않은 채, 그 특유의 거친 동작으로 뱃전 위로 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다가, 거기서 다시 뱃전에 매달린 고래잡이 보트 가운데 하나의 뱃머리로 뛰어내렸다. 그러고는 왼쪽 무릎을 굽혀 버틴 채 작살을 겨누고서 이런 식으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"선장님, 저기 물 위에 타르 작은 방울 떨어진 거 보이지요? 보여요? 좋아요, 저게 고래 눈깔이라 칩시다, 자, 그럼!\" 그러고는 거기에 날카롭게 겨눠, 늙은 빌대드의 챙 넓은 모자 바로 위로, 배의 갑판을 가로질러 작살을 던졌고, 반짝이던 타르 자국을 흔적도 없이 날려 버렸다.\n\n\"자,\" 퀴케그가 줄을 조용히 끌어당기며 말했다. \"저게 고래 눈깔이라 치면, 그럼, 그 고래 죽은 거지요.\"\n\n\"빨리, 빌대드,\" 그의 동업자 펠레그가 말했다. 날아간 작살이 너무 바로 곁을 스쳐 지나간 통에 기겁한 그는 선실 통로 쪽으로 물러나 있었다. \"어서, 이보게 빌대드, 빨리 배의 서류를 가져오게. 저기 저 헤지호그를, 아니 쿼호그를 우리 보트 가운데 하나에 꼭 태워야겠어. 이보게 쿼호그, 자네에게 90분의 1 몫을 주겠네. 낸터킷에서 어떤 작살잡이에게도 일찍이 주어진 적 없는 후한 몫이지.\"\n\n그래서 우리는 선실로 내려갔고, 더없이 기쁘게도 퀴케그는 곧 나 자신이 속한 바로 그 배의 선원 명부에 올랐다.\n\n모든 사전 절차가 끝나고 펠레그가 서명에 필요한 모든 준비를 마치자, 그는 내게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"내 생각에 저기 저 쿼호그는 글씨를 쓸 줄 모르겠지, 안 그런가? 이보게 쿼호그, 빌어먹을! 자네 이름을 직접 서명하겠나, 아니면 표시를 그리겠나?\"\n\n그러나 이 질문에, 전에도 두세 차례 비슷한 의식에 참여해 본 적이 있는 퀴케그는 조금도 당황하는 기색 없이, 건네받은 펜을 들고 종이의 적절한 자리에다, 자기 팔에 새겨진 기묘한 둥근 무늬와 똑같은 모양을 정확히 베껴 그렸다. 그리하여 그의 이름에 관한 펠레그 선장의 고집스러운 착오 덕분에, 그것은 대략 이런 모양이 되었다.\n\n쿼호그. 그의 X 표시.\n\n그동안 빌대드 선장은 퀴케그를 진지하고도 흔들림 없이 응시하고 앉아 있다가, 마침내 엄숙하게 일어나 자신의 폭 넓은 칙칙한 갈색 외투의 커다란 주머니를 더듬어, 소책자 한 묶음을 꺼냈다. 그러고는 그중 \"다가오는 마지막 날, 곧 지체할 시간이 없도다\"라는 제목이 붙은 것을 골라 퀴케그의 손에 쥐여 주고는, 두 손으로 그의 손과 그 책을 한꺼번에 움켜잡은 채, 그의 두 눈을 진지하게 들여다보며 말했다. \"어둠의 자식아, 나는 그대에게 내 의무를 다해야겠다. 나는 이 배의 공동 선주이며, 그 모든 선원의 영혼을 염려하는 마음이 있다. 만일 그대가 여전히 그 이교의 방식에 매달려 있다면 — 슬프게도 나는 그것이 두렵구나 — 부디 간청하노니, 영영 벨리알의 종으로 남지 말라. 우상 벨을, 그리고 그 끔찍한 용을 내쳐 버려라. 다가올 진노에서 돌아서라. 정신을 똑바로 차려라, 내 말 잘 들어라. 오! 자비로우신 하느님이시여! 저 불타는 구렁텅이를 피하라!\"\n\n늙은 빌대드의 말투에는 짠 바닷내가 아직도 얼마간 배어 있어, 성경 구절과 일상의 표현이 잡다하게 뒤섞여 있었다.\n\n\"멈춰, 거기 멈추라고, 빌대드, 우리 작살잡이를 망쳐 놓는 짓은 이제 그만두게.\" 펠레그가 외쳤다. \"경건한 작살잡이는 결코 좋은 항해자가 못 돼 — 경건함은 그자들에게서 상어 기질을 빼내 버리거든. 어지간히 상어 같지 않은 작살잡이는 지푸라기만큼의 값어치도 없는 거야. 옛날에 냇 스웨인이라는 젊은이가 있었지, 한때는 낸터킷과 빈야드 통틀어 가장 용감한 보트 지휘자였어. 그런데 그자가 예배당에 다니기 시작하더니, 영 신통치 못하게 되어 버렸지. 자기의 그 빌어먹을 영혼이 어찌나 무서웠던지, 보트가 부서져 데이비 존스의 바닷속으로 떨어지기라도 하면 뒤탈이 있을까 봐 겁이 나서, 고래만 보면 움츠러들고 슬슬 피해 다녔다네.\"\n\n\"펠레그! 펠레그!\" 빌대드가 두 눈과 두 손을 치켜들며 말했다. \"그대 자신도, 나 자신과 마찬가지로, 숱한 위험한 순간을 겪어 보지 않았는가. 펠레그, 그대도 죽음의 공포를 안다는 것이 어떤 것인지 알 터인데, 어찌 그토록 불경한 모습으로 함부로 지껄일 수 있단 말인가. 그대는 자기 마음을 속이고 있어, 펠레그. 어디 말해 보게, 일본 앞바다의 그 태풍 속에서 여기 이 피쿼드(Pequod)호가 돛대 세 개를 모조리 바다로 날려 버렸을 때, 그러니까 그대가 에이해브(Ahab) 선장 밑에서 일등항해사로 갔던 바로 그 항해 때, 그대는 죽음과 심판을 떠올리지 않았던가?\"\n\n\"저 사람 말 좀 들어 보게, 지금 저 사람 말 좀 들어 보라고.\" 펠레그가 선실을 가로질러 성큼성큼 걸으며 두 손을 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣은 채 외쳤다. \"저 사람 말 좀 들어 보게나, 다들. 생각해 보라고! 순간순간마다 배가 가라앉으리라 여겼던 그때를! 그때 죽음과 심판? 뭐라고? 돛대 세 개가 모조리 뱃전을 향해 그토록 끝없이 우레처럼 부딪쳐 대고, 바닷물은 앞에서 뒤에서 우리 머리 위를 덮쳐 오는데. 그런 때에 죽음과 심판을 생각해? 천만에! 그때는 죽음 따위를 생각할 겨를이 없었어. 에이해브 선장과 내가 생각한 건 오직 삶이었지. 어떻게 모두를 살릴까 — 어떻게 임시 돛대를 세울까 — 어떻게 가장 가까운 항구로 들어갈까, 내가 생각한 건 바로 그런 것들이었어.\"\n\n빌대드는 더는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 외투 단추를 채운 채 갑판으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔고, 우리도 그를 따라 나갔다. 거기서 그는 배 중간께에서 윗돛을 깁고 있는 돛 만드는 일꾼 몇몇을 아주 조용히 내려다보며 서 있었다. 이따금 그는 몸을 굽혀 천 조각을 주워 들거나, 그러지 않았으면 버려졌을 타르 묻은 노끈 끄트머리를 챙겨 두곤 했다.\n\n「선원 양반들, 저 배에 승선 계약을 했소?」\n\n퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 나는 막 피쿼드호(Pequod)를 떠나 물가에서 멀어지며 한가로이 걷고 있던 참이었고, 저마다 제 생각에 잠겨 있었는데, 그때 낯선 사내 하나가 우리 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 서더니 그 육중한 집게손가락을 문제의 그 배 쪽으로 겨누며 위와 같은 말을 던졌다. 그는 빛바랜 웃옷에 기운 바지를 걸친, 초라하기 짝이 없는 행색이었다. 목에는 검은 손수건 누더기 한 조각을 둘러매고 있었다. 천연두 자국이 한데 엉겨 그의 얼굴을 사방으로 뒤덮어, 거센 물살이 말라붙은 뒤에 남은 격류의 골 진 바닥처럼 복잡하게 패어 있었다.\n\n「저 배에 계약을 했냐 말이오?」 그가 거듭 물었다.\n\n「피쿼드호를 말하는 거겠지요.」 나는 그를 방해받지 않고 좀 더 찬찬히 뜯어볼 시간을 벌려고 그렇게 말했다.\n\n「그렇소, 피쿼드호 말이오—저기 저 배.」 그는 팔 전체를 뒤로 당겼다가 다시 재빨리 앞으로 쭉 내뻗으며, 뾰족한 손가락이라는 꽂힌 총검을 그 대상에 정통으로 겨누었다.\n\n「그렇소,」 내가 말했다. 「우린 막 계약서에 서명한 참이오.」\n\n「거기 당신들 영혼에 관한 조항도 있던가?」\n\n「뭐에 관한 거라고요?」\n\n「오, 어쩌면 당신들한텐 영혼이란 게 없는지도 모르지.」 그가 잽싸게 말했다. 「뭐, 그래도 상관없소. 나는 영혼 없는 친구들을 숱하게 알고 있으니까—그자들에게 복이 있기를. 그들은 그 덕에 도리어 형편이 더 낫지. 영혼이란 짐수레에 달린 다섯 번째 바퀴 같은 거니까.」\n\n「대체 무슨 헛소리를 지껄이는 거요, 선원 양반?」 내가 말했다.\n\n「하지만 그 사람은 다른 친구들의 그런 모자람을 죄다 채우고도 남을 만큼 충분히 가졌지.」 낯선 사내가 불쑥 말하며, 그 사람이라는 말에 신경질적으로 힘을 주었다.\n\n「퀴퀘그,」 내가 말했다. 「가자. 이 작자는 어디선가 풀려나온 모양이야. 우리가 알지도 못하는 무언가와 누군가에 대해 떠들어 대고 있어.」\n\n「잠깐!」 낯선 사내가 외쳤다. 「당신 말이 옳소—아직 늙은 천둥(Old Thunder)을 본 적이 없지, 안 그렇소?」\n\n「늙은 천둥이 누구요?」 나는 그 미친 듯한 진지함에 또다시 못 박힌 듯 멈춰 서며 물었다.\n\n「에이해브(Ahab) 선장 말이오.」\n\n「뭐라고! 우리 배 피쿼드호의 선장 말이오?」\n\n「그렇소, 우리 같은 늙은 뱃사람 몇몇은 그분을 그 이름으로 부른다오. 아직 그분을 본 적이 없지, 안 그렇소?」\n\n「그렇소, 아직 못 봤소. 듣자 하니 편찮으시다는데, 차도가 있어서 머지않아 다시 멀쩡해지실 거라더군요.」\n\n「머지않아 다시 멀쩡해진다!」 낯선 사내가 엄숙하면서도 비웃는 듯한 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 「잘 들으시오. 에이해브 선장이 멀쩡해질 때면, 그때는 내 이 왼팔도 멀쩡해질 거요. 그전엔 어림없지.」\n\n「그 사람에 대해 뭘 안다는 거요?」\n\n「그 사람에 대해 그자들이 당신한테 뭐라고 일러주던가? 그걸 말해 보시오!」\n\n「그 사람에 대해 별 얘기는 없었소. 다만 고래잡이 솜씨가 뛰어나고, 선원들에게 좋은 선장이라는 말은 들었지.」\n\n「그건 사실이지, 사실이고말고—그렇소, 둘 다 틀림없는 사실이오. 하지만 그분이 명령을 내리면 당신은 펄쩍 뛰어 움직여야 할 거요. 걸으면서 으르렁대고, 으르렁대면서 가라—그게 에이해브 선장과 함께하는 법칙이오. 하지만 오래전 케이프 혼(Cape Horn) 앞바다에서 그분에게 일어난 그 일, 사흘 밤낮을 죽은 듯이 누워 있던 그 일에 대해선 아무 말도 없었지. 산타(Santa)에서 제단 앞에서 스페인 사람과 벌인 그 목숨을 건 난투극에 대해서도—그것에 대해선 아무것도 못 들었지, 응? 그분이 침을 뱉었던 은 표주박에 대해서도 아무 말 없었고? 그리고 예언대로 지난 항해에서 다리를 잃은 일에 대해서도 아무것도. 그런 일들에 대해, 또 그 밖의 일들에 대해 단 한마디도 못 들었지, 응? 아니, 못 들었을 거요. 어떻게 들을 수 있었겠소? 그걸 누가 알겠소? 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들 전부가 아는 것도 아닐 테니. 하지만 어쩌면 그 다리에 대해선, 그분이 그걸 어떻게 잃었는지에 대해선 들어 봤을지도 모르겠군. 그렇소, 그건 들었을 거요, 틀림없이. 오, 그렇지, 그건 거의 누구나 다 아는 일이오—내 말은, 그분이 다리가 하나뿐이고, 향유고래 한 마리가 다른 한쪽을 떼어 갔다는 걸 안다는 말이오.」\n\n「이보시오,」 내가 말했다. 「당신이 지껄이는 이 모든 헛소리가 대체 무엇에 관한 것인지 나는 모르겠고, 별로 알고 싶지도 않소. 내가 보기엔 당신 머리가 좀 어떻게 된 것 같으니 말이오. 하지만 저기 저 배, 피쿼드호의 에이해브 선장 얘기를 하는 거라면, 분명히 말해 두는데, 나는 그분이 다리를 잃은 일에 대해 죄다 알고 있소.」\n\n「죄다 안다고, 응—정말 그렇소?—죄다?」\n\n「거의 틀림없소.」\n\n거지꼴의 낯선 사내는 손가락을 겨눈 채 피쿼드호에 눈길을 고정하고서, 괴로운 몽상에 잠긴 듯 한순간 가만히 서 있었다. 그러더니 흠칫 놀란 듯 몸을 돌려 말했다.—「계약을 했단 말이지, 응? 이름을 서류에 적었고? 그래, 그래, 서명한 건 서명한 거고, 될 일은 될 테고, 또 어쩌면, 결국 안 될지도 모르지. 어쨌든 그건 이미 죄다 정해지고 짜여 있는 일이오. 그리고 어떤 선원이든 누군가는 그분과 함께 가야 할 테지. 이 사람들이든 다른 누구든 마찬가지일 테고, 하느님(God)이 그들을 가엾이 여기시기를! 잘 가시오, 선원 양반들, 잘 가시오. 형언할 수 없는 하늘이 당신들에게 복을 내리시기를. 붙잡아 세워서 미안하오.」\n\n「이보시오, 친구,」 내가 말했다. 「우리한테 들려줄 중요한 얘기가 있거든 어서 털어놓으시오. 하지만 그저 우리를 어리둥절하게 만들려는 수작이라면, 당신은 헛다리를 짚은 거요. 내가 할 말은 그게 다요.」\n\n「그것참 잘 말했소, 그리고 사내가 그렇게 당차게 받아치는 걸 듣는 게 나는 좋소. 당신이야말로 그분에게 꼭 맞는 사람이오—당신 같은 사람 말이오. 잘 가시오, 선원 양반들, 잘 가시오! 오! 거기 가거든, 그분들에게 전해 주시오. 나는 그들 중 하나가 되지 않기로 결심했다고.」\n\n「아, 이보시오, 그런 식으로는 우리를 속일 수 없소—우릴 속일 순 없지. 자기 속에 무슨 대단한 비밀이라도 품은 듯이 굴기는 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일이니까.」\n\n「잘 가시오, 선원 양반들, 잘 가시오.」\n\n「잘 가라니, 그러지.」 내가 말했다. 「가자, 퀴퀘그, 이 미친 작자는 내버려 두고. 그런데 잠깐, 당신 이름이나 좀 말해 주겠소?」\n\n「일라이저(Elijah)요.」\n일라이저라! 나는 속으로 생각했고, 우리는 저마다 제 식대로 이 누더기 차림의 늙은 뱃사람에 대해 한마디씩 주고받으며 걸어갔다. 그리고 그가 사람을 놀래키려 드는 허풍선이에 지나지 않는다는 데 의견을 모았다. 그러나 백 야드도 채 가지 못했을 무렵, 우연히 모퉁이를 돌면서 돌아보니, 놀랍게도 거기엔 일라이저가, 비록 멀찍이 떨어져서지만 우리를 뒤따라오고 있는 것이 아닌가. 어쩐지 그 모습이 나를 어찌나 충격에 빠뜨렸던지, 나는 그가 뒤따라온다는 것을 퀴퀘그에게는 한마디도 하지 않고, 그저 동무와 함께 걸음을 옮기며, 저 낯선 사내가 우리가 돈 바로 그 모퉁이를 돌아오는지 어떤지 지켜보려고 애를 태웠다. 과연 그는 그렇게 했다. 그러자 내게는 그가 우리를 미행하고 있는 것처럼 여겨졌으나, 도대체 무슨 속셈인지는 죽었다 깨어나도 짐작할 수 없었다. 이 정황은, 그 모호하고, 반쯤 넌지시 비추고 반쯤 드러내는, 안개에 싸인 듯한 말투와 한데 어우러져, 이제 내 안에 온갖 막연한 의혹과 어렴풋한 불안을 자아냈고, 그 모든 것이 피쿼드호와, 에이해브 선장과, 그가 잃은 다리와, 케이프 혼에서의 발작과, 은 표주박과, 전날 내가 배를 떠날 때 펠레그(Peleg) 선장이 그에 대해 했던 말과, 인디언 여인 티스티그(Tistig)의 예언과, 우리가 항해하기로 스스로 묶어 둔 그 항해와, 그 밖에 그림자처럼 어른거리는 수백 가지 일들과 얽혀 있었다.\n\n나는 이 누더기 차림의 일라이저가 정말로 우리를 미행하고 있는지 아닌지 내 눈으로 확인하기로 마음먹고, 그 속셈으로 퀴퀘그와 함께 길을 건너, 그쪽 길을 따라 왔던 걸음을 되짚어 갔다. 그러나 일라이저는 우리를 알아채는 기색도 없이 그냥 지나쳐 갔다. 그것이 나를 안심시켰다. 그리하여 나는 다시 한번, 그리고 내게는 이번이 마지막인 듯이, 그를 마음속으로 허풍선이라 단정 지었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 20. All Astir.\n\n하루 이틀이 지나는 동안 피쿼드(Pequod)호 선상은 분주하기 그지없었다. 낡은 돛을 손보는 일은 물론이고, 새 돛이 배에 실려 올라왔으며, 두루마리 캔버스 천과 둘둘 감은 삭구 뭉치도 들어왔다. 한마디로 모든 것이 배의 출항 준비가 막바지로 치닫고 있음을 알려 주고 있었다. 펠레그(Peleg) 선장은 좀처럼, 아니 거의 뭍에 오르는 법이 없이 자기 천막 안에 앉아 일꾼들을 매섭게 감독했고, 빌대드(Bildad)는 상점들을 돌며 물품을 사들이고 조달하는 일을 도맡았다. 그리고 선창과 삭구에서 일하는 사람들은 밤이 깊도록 한참을 더 일했다.\n\n퀴케그(Queequeg)가 계약서에 서명한 다음 날, 선원들이 묵고 있던 모든 여관에 통지가 내려졌다. 배가 얼마나 빨리 떠나게 될지 알 수 없으니, 밤이 되기 전에 각자의 궤짝을 배에 실어 두라는 것이었다. 그래서 퀴케그와 나는 짐을 챙겨 내리면서도, 마지막 순간까지는 뭍에서 자기로 마음먹었다. 그러나 이런 경우에는 으레 미리 길게 통지를 하는 모양이어서, 배는 며칠이 지나도록 출항하지 않았다. 하기야 놀랄 일도 아니었다. 해야 할 일이 산더미였고, 피쿼드호를 온전히 갖추기까지 챙겨야 할 것이 얼마나 많은지 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었으니까.\n\n살림살이라는 것이 침대며 냄비며 칼과 포크, 부삽과 부집게, 냅킨, 호두까기 따위에 이르기까지 얼마나 많은 물건을 없어서는 안 될 필수품으로 요구하는지는 누구나 알고 있다. 고래잡이도 꼭 그러한데, 이는 모든 식료품상과 행상인, 의사와 제빵사와 은행가로부터 멀리 떨어진 망망대해 위에서 삼 년에 걸친 살림을 꾸려야 하는 일이기 때문이다. 물론 이것은 상선에도 해당되는 이야기지만, 그래도 고래잡이배만큼은 결코 아니다. 고래잡이 항해의 어마어마한 길이는 차치하고라도, 어업을 수행하는 데 필요한 수많은 특수 물품들과, 흔히 드나드는 외딴 항구에서는 그것들을 다시 마련할 수 없다는 점, 그리고 무엇보다도 모든 배 가운데 고래잡이배야말로 온갖 종류의 사고에, 특히 항해의 성패가 가장 크게 달려 있는 바로 그 물건들의 파손과 유실에 가장 많이 노출되어 있다는 사실을 잊어서는 안 된다. 그런 까닭에 예비 보트, 예비 원목, 예비 밧줄과 작살, 그리고 거의 모든 것의 예비품이 갖춰진다. 다만 예비 선장과 똑같은 배 한 척만은 예외였다.\n\n우리가 이 섬에 닿았을 무렵, 피쿼드호에 실을 가장 묵직한 화물은 이미 거의 다 실린 상태였다. 쇠고기와 빵, 물과 연료, 그리고 쇠 테와 통널 따위가 그것이었다. 그러나 앞서 넌지시 말했듯이, 한동안은 크고 작은 온갖 자질구레한 물건들을 끊임없이 배로 가져오고 또 실어 나르는 일이 계속되었다.\n\n이렇게 물건을 가져오고 실어 나르는 사람들 가운데 으뜸가는 이는 빌대드 선장의 누이였다. 더없이 굳센 의지와 지칠 줄 모르는 정신을 지닌, 그러면서도 마음씨가 무척 고운 깡마른 노부인이었는데, 일단 배가 바다로 제대로 나가고 나면, 자기 힘으로 막을 수만 있다면 피쿼드호에 부족한 것이라곤 단 하나도 없게 하리라 단단히 작정한 듯했다. 한 번은 사환의 식료품 저장실에 둘 피클 단지를 들고 배에 올랐고, 또 한 번은 일등항해사가 항해일지를 적어 두는 책상에 둘 깃펜 한 묶음을 들고 왔으며, 세 번째에는 누군가의 류머티즘 앓는 허리께에 댈 플란넬 천 한 두루마리를 들고 나타났다. 그 어떤 여인도 자신의 이름을 이보다 더 마땅히 누린 적은 없었으니, 그 이름은 채러티(Charity), 곧 자비였다. 모두가 그녀를 채러티 아주머니라 불렀다. 그리고 자비의 수녀처럼, 이 자비로운 채러티 아주머니는 이리저리 부산을 떨며, 사랑하는 동생 빌대드가 관여하고 또 자기 자신도 알뜰히 모은 몇 십 달러의 몫을 지닌 그 배 위의 모든 이에게 안전과 안락과 위안을 가져다줄 만한 일이라면 무엇에든 기꺼이 손과 마음을 내어 줄 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n* 채러티(Charity): 자비, 자선을 뜻하는 영어 이름.\n그러나 마음씨 고운 이 퀘이커 교도 부인이, 마지막 날에 그러했듯, 한 손에는 기다란 기름 국자를, 다른 손에는 그보다 더 기다란 고래잡이 창을 들고 배에 오르는 광경은 보기에 자못 놀라웠다. 빌대드 자신도, 펠레그 선장도 결코 뒤지지 않았다. 빌대드로 말하자면, 필요한 물품을 적은 기다란 목록을 늘 지니고 다니다가, 새로운 물건이 도착할 때마다 종이 위의 그 항목 옆에 표시를 해 나갔다. 그리고 펠레그는 이따금 고래뼈로 꾸민 자기 굴에서 절름거리며 나와, 승강구 아래의 일꾼들을 향해 고함을 치고, 돛대 꼭대기의 삭구공들을 향해 고함을 질러 대다가, 끝내는 다시 천막 안으로 고함을 치며 돌아가곤 했다.\n\n이 준비의 나날 동안, 퀴케그와 나는 자주 그 배를 찾았고, 그때마다 나는 에이해브(Ahab) 선장에 관해, 그의 상태가 어떤지, 그리고 언제 배에 오를 것인지를 물었다. 그러면 그들은 그가 점점 나아지고 있으며 날마다 배에 오를 것으로 기대하고 있다고, 그동안에는 두 선장 펠레그와 빌대드가 항해를 위해 배를 채비하는 데 필요한 모든 일을 처리할 수 있다고 대답하곤 했다. 만일 내가 나 자신에게 철저히 솔직했더라면, 배가 망망대해로 나서는 순간부터 이 항해의 절대적 지배자가 될 그 사람을 단 한 번도 직접 보지 못한 채 이토록 긴 항해에 몸을 맡기는 일이 도무지 마음에 차지 않는다는 사실을 내 마음속에서 똑똑히 보았으리라. 그러나 사람이 무언가 잘못된 것을 의심할 때, 이미 그 일에 발을 들여놓은 처지라면, 자기 자신에게마저 그 의심을 덮어 감추려 부지중에 애쓰는 일이 더러 일어난다. 나도 꼭 그러했다. 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 아무 생각도 하지 않으려 애썼다.\n\n마침내 다음 날 어느 때엔가 배가 틀림없이 출항하리라는 통지가 내려졌다. 그래서 이튿날 아침, 퀴케그와 나는 아주 이른 시각에 길을 나섰다.\n\n# CHAPTER 21. Going Aboard.\n\n째 무렵, 우리가 부두에 다가섰을 때는 아직 여섯 시가 채 안 되었고, 잿빛으로 흐릿하게 안개 낀 미명만이 깔려 있었다.\n\n\"저기 앞쪽에 선원 몇 명이 달려가고 있어, 내가 제대로 봤다면 말이야.\" 내가 퀴케그(Queequeg)에게 말했다. \"그림자일 리는 없고, 배는 해 뜰 무렵에 떠나는 모양이야. 자, 어서 가자!\"\n\n\"멈추게!\" 어떤 목소리가 외쳤다. 그 목소리의 주인이 동시에 우리 등 뒤로 바짝 다가와 두 손을 우리 두 사람의 어깨에 얹고는, 우리 사이를 비집고 들어와 흐릿한 어스름 속에서 몸을 약간 앞으로 구부린 채 서서, 퀴케그에게서 나에게로 기이하게 눈길을 던졌다. 일라이저(Elijah)였다.\n\n\"배에 타려는 건가?\"\n\n\"손 좀 떼지 그래.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이봐.\" 퀴케그가 몸을 흔들며 말했다. \"저리 가!\"\n\n\"그럼 배에 안 타는 건가?\"\n\n\"아니, 탈 거요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그런데 그게 당신과 무슨 상관이오? 일라이저 씨, 내가 당신을 좀 무례한 사람이라고 여기고 있다는 걸 아시오?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니지. 그건 내 미처 몰랐군.\" 일라이저가 나에게서 퀴케그에게로 천천히, 의아한 듯, 도무지 알 수 없는 눈빛으로 시선을 옮기며 말했다.\n\n\"일라이저.\" 내가 말했다. \"내 친구와 나를 위해 물러나 주면 고맙겠소. 우리는 인도양과 태평양으로 가는 길이고, 붙잡혀 있고 싶지 않소.\"\n\n\"그렇다, 그런 거지? 아침 먹기 전에 돌아올 텐가?\"\n\n\"이 사람 머리가 좀 돌았어, 퀴케그.\" 내가 말했다. \"어서 가자.\"\n\n\"여보게!\" 그 자리에 가만히 선 일라이저가 우리가 몇 걸음 멀어졌을 때 우리를 불렀다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마.\" 내가 말했다. \"퀴케그, 어서 가자.\"\n\n그러나 그는 다시 슬그머니 우리에게 다가와, 느닷없이 내 어깨에 손을 짚으며 말했다. \"조금 전에 저 배 쪽으로 사람처럼 보이는 무언가가 가는 걸 보지 않았나?\"\n\n이 평범하고 사실적인 물음에 흠칫한 나는 대답했다. \"그래요, 네다섯 사람을 본 것 같소. 하지만 너무 어두워 확실하지는 않았소.\"\n\n\"아주 어둡지, 아주 어두워.\" 일라이저가 말했다. \"잘들 가시게.\"\n\n우리는 또 한 번 그를 떠났다. 그러나 그는 또다시 슬며시 우리 뒤를 따라와, 다시 내 어깨를 건드리며 말했다. \"이제 그자들을 찾을 수 있는지 한번 보게, 그래 줄 텐가?\"\n\n\"누구를 찾으란 말이오?\"\n\"잘들 가시게! 잘들 가시게!\" 그는 다시 떠나가며 대꾸했다. \"오! 자네들에게 경고하려던 참이었는데 — 하지만 됐네, 됐어 — 다 마찬가지지, 다 한 식구이기도 하고. 오늘 아침 서리가 매섭군, 그렇지 않나? 잘들 가시게. 곧 다시 보지는 못할 걸세, 내 짐작에는. 대배심 앞에서가 아니라면 말이야.\" 이 정신 나간 말들을 남기고 그는 마침내 떠나갔고, 나는 그 순간 그의 광적인 뻔뻔함에 적잖이 어리둥절했다.\n\n마침내 페쿼드(Pequod)호에 올라 보니 모든 것이 깊은 정적에 잠겨 있었고, 움직이는 사람 하나 없었다. 선실 입구는 안에서 잠겨 있었고, 승강구는 모두 닫힌 채 둘둘 감긴 삭구 더미로 막혀 있었다. 앞쪽 선수루로 가 보니 갑판 승강구의 미닫이가 열려 있었다. 불빛이 보여 우리는 아래로 내려갔는데, 거기에는 너덜너덜한 두꺼운 모직 외투를 걸친 늙은 삭구장이 한 사람만 있었다. 그는 궤짝 두 개 위에 온몸을 길게 뻗고, 얼굴은 아래로 향한 채 포갠 두 팔에 파묻혀 있었다. 더없이 깊은 잠이 그를 덮고 있었다.\n\n\"우리가 본 그 선원들 말이야, 퀴케그, 어디로 갔을까?\" 잠든 사람을 미심쩍게 바라보며 내가 말했다. 그러나 부두에서 퀴케그는 내가 지금 말한 것을 전혀 알아차리지 못한 듯했다. 그래서 일라이저의 달리 설명할 길 없는 물음만 아니었다면, 나는 그 일에 관해 내 눈이 착각을 일으켰다고 여겼을 것이다. 하지만 나는 그 생각을 눌러 버렸다. 그리고 다시 잠든 사람을 보며, 어쩌면 우리가 시신을 지키며 밤을 새우는 게 좋겠다고 퀴케그에게 농담조로 넌지시 말하고는, 그에 맞게 자리를 잡으라고 일렀다. 그는 그것이 충분히 부드러운지 확인하듯 잠든 사람의 엉덩이에 손을 얹어 보더니, 더 따질 것도 없이 그 위에 조용히 주저앉았다.\n\n\"맙소사! 퀴케그, 거기 앉지 마.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 아주 좋은 자리야.\" 퀴케그가 말했다. \"우리 나라 방식이지. 이 사람 얼굴 안 다쳐.\"\n\n\"얼굴이라고!\" 내가 말했다. \"그걸 얼굴이라 부르는 거야? 그렇다면 참 자비로운 낯짝이군. 그런데 숨을 어찌나 가쁘게 쉬는지, 몸을 들썩이고 있잖아. 내려와, 퀴케그, 너 무거워. 이러다 가난한 자의 얼굴을 짓이기겠다. 내려와, 퀴케그! 봐, 곧 너를 떨쳐 낼 거야. 어째 안 깨는지 신기하군.\"\n\n퀴케그는 잠든 사람의 머리맡 바로 너머로 자리를 옮기고, 도끼 파이프에 불을 붙였다. 나는 발치에 앉았다. 우리는 잠든 사람을 사이에 두고 파이프를 서로 주거니 받거니 했다. 그러는 동안, 그의 더듬거리는 말투로 물어보니, 퀴케그가 내게 일러 준 바로는, 그의 고향에서는 온갖 종류의 긴 의자와 소파가 없는 까닭에, 왕과 추장과 대체로 지체 높은 사람들이 낮은 신분의 몇몇을 살찌워 발걸상으로 쓰는 관습이 있다고 했다. 그래서 그 점에서 집을 안락하게 꾸미려면, 그저 게으른 녀석 여덟이나 열쯤 사들여 마루와 벽감 둘레에 죽 늘어놓기만 하면 된다는 것이었다. 게다가 나들이를 갈 때도 무척 편리해서, 지팡이로 바꿀 수 있는 그 정원용 의자보다 훨씬 낫다고 했다. 이를테면 추장이 시종을 불러, 가지를 넓게 드리운 나무 아래, 어쩌면 축축하고 질척한 곳에서, 제 몸을 긴 의자로 만들라고 명할 수 있으니 말이다.\n\n이런 이야기를 늘어놓는 동안, 퀴케그는 내게서 도끼 파이프를 건네받을 때마다 잠든 사람의 머리 위로 도끼날 쪽을 휘둘러 댔다.\n\n\"그건 왜 그러는 거야, 퀴케그?\"\n\n\"아주 쉽게, 죽여. 오! 아주 쉽게!\"\n\n그는 자신의 도끼 파이프에 얽힌 거친 추억담을 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 보아하니 그것은 두 가지 쓰임새로 적의 골통을 깨부수기도 하고 제 영혼을 달래기도 했던 모양이다. 그때 우리의 눈길은 곧장 잠든 삭구장에게로 쏠렸다. 이제 짙은 연기가 그 비좁은 구멍을 완전히 채우자, 그것이 그에게 영향을 미치기 시작했다. 그는 무언가 막힌 듯이 숨을 쉬더니, 코가 거북한 모양이었고, 그러더니 한두 번 몸을 뒤척이고는, 일어나 앉아 눈을 비볐다.\n\n\"여보게!\" 그가 마침내 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"담배 피우는 자네들은 누군가?\"\n\n\"승선 계약을 한 사람들이오.\" 내가 대답했다. \"이 배는 언제 떠나오?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 자네들도 이 배를 타고 가는 거로군, 그렇지? 배는 오늘 떠나네. 선장이 어젯밤에 배에 올랐지.\"\n\n\"무슨 선장 말이오? 에이해브(Ahab) 말이오?\"\n\n\"그분 말고 또 누가 있겠나?\"\n나는 에이해브에 관해 그에게 몇 가지를 더 물어보려던 참에, 갑판에서 무슨 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"여보게! 스타벅(Starbuck)이 일어났군.\" 삭구장이 말했다. \"저 양반은 활기찬 일등 항해사야. 좋은 사람이고, 신앙심도 깊지. 그런데 이제 다들 움직이는군, 나도 일하러 가야겠어.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 갑판으로 올라갔고, 우리도 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n이제 완연히 해가 떠올랐다. 곧 선원들이 두셋씩 짝지어 배에 올랐고, 삭구장들은 분주히 움직였으며, 항해사들도 활발히 일에 매달렸다. 또 뭍에서 온 사람 몇몇은 갖가지 마지막 물건들을 배에 실어 나르느라 바빴다. 그러는 동안 에이해브 선장은 자신의 선실 안에 모습을 감춘 채 신전에 모셔진 듯 머물러 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 22. Merry Christmas.\n\n마침내 정오 무렵, 배의 의장공(艤裝工)들이 모두 해산되고, 페쿼드(Pequod)호가 부두에서 끌려 나온 뒤, 또한 늘 사려 깊은 채리티가 고래잡이 보트를 타고 마지막 선물을 들고 찾아온 뒤에 — 그 선물이란 이등 항해사이자 그녀의 시동생인 스터브(Stubb)에게 줄 수면모자 하나와, 사환에게 줄 여분의 성경책 한 권이었다 — 이 모든 일이 끝난 다음에, 두 선장 펠레그(Peleg)와 빌다드(Bildad)가 선실에서 나와, 일등 항해사를 돌아보며 펠레그가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 스타벅(Starbuck) 군, 모든 게 다 제대로 되어 있는 게 확실한가? 에이해브(Ahab) 선장도 준비를 다 마쳤네 — 방금 이야기를 나눴어 — 뭍에서 더 가져올 것은 없겠지, 응? 좋아, 그럼 전원을 불러 모으게. 죄다 여기 고물 쪽으로 집합시켜 — 빌어먹을 놈들!\"\n\n\"아무리 급하기로서니 불경한 말을 입에 담을 필요는 없네, 펠레그.\" 빌다드가 말했다. \"어서 가게나, 친구 스타벅, 우리가 시킨 대로 하게.\"\n\n이게 무슨 일인가! 막 항해를 떠나려는 바로 이 순간에, 펠레그 선장과 빌다드 선장은 마치 항구에서뿐만 아니라 바다에서도 공동 지휘관 노릇을 할 사람들인 양, 뒷갑판에서 거리낌 없이 위세를 부리고 있었다. 그리고 에이해브 선장으로 말하자면, 아직 그의 모습은 어디에도 보이지 않았다. 다만 선실에 있다는 말뿐이었다. 그러나 생각해 보면, 배를 출항시키고 능숙하게 먼바다로 몰아 나가는 데 그가 굳이 모습을 드러낼 필요는 전혀 없었다. 사실 그것은 그의 본래 임무가 아니라 도선사의 일이었고, 또 그가 아직 완전히 회복되지 않은 탓에 — 사람들은 그렇게 말했다 — 에이해브 선장은 아래에 머물러 있었다. 이 모든 것이 충분히 자연스러워 보였다. 더욱이 상선에서는 닻을 올린 뒤에도 상당 시간 동안 갑판에 모습을 드러내지 않고, 도선사와 함께 배를 영영 떠나기 전에 선실 탁자에 둘러앉아 뭍의 친구들과 작별의 흥겨운 자리를 갖는 선장이 적지 않았으니 말이다.\n\n그러나 이 일을 곰곰이 따져 볼 겨를은 없었으니, 펠레그 선장이 이제 펄펄 살아 움직이고 있었기 때문이다. 말하고 지휘하는 일은 대부분 빌다드가 아니라 그의 몫인 듯했다.\n\n\"고물로 모이라니까, 이 홀아비 자식들아.\" 선원들이 큰 돛대 곁에서 우물쭈물하자 그가 소리쳤다. \"스타벅 군, 저놈들을 고물로 몰아붙이게.\"\n\n\"거기 천막을 거두어라!\" 다음 명령이 떨어졌다. 앞에서 넌지시 일렀듯이, 이 고래수염 천막은 항구에 있을 때 말고는 결코 치지 않았다. 그리고 페쿼드호에서는 삼십 년 동안 천막을 거두라는 명령이 곧 닻을 올릴 차례임을 알리는 신호로 널리 통했다.\n\n\"양묘기(揚錨機)를 잡아라! 천둥벼락이 칠 노릇이다! — 뛰어!\" 다음 명령이 떨어지자, 선원들은 양묘봉을 향해 달려들었다.\n\n자, 배를 출항시킬 때 도선사가 통상 자리 잡는 곳은 배의 앞부분이다. 그리고 바로 여기서 빌다드의 모습이 보였다. 미리 밝혀 두자면, 빌다드는 펠레그와 더불어 다른 직책 외에도 이 항구의 면허받은 도선사 가운데 한 사람이었는데 — 사람들은 그가 자신이 관여하는 모든 배에 들어가는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 도선료를 아끼려고 일부러 도선사 자격을 얻은 게 아니냐고 의심했으니, 그가 다른 어떤 배도 인도한 적이 없었기 때문이다 — 그 빌다드가, 말하건대, 이제 뱃머리 너머로 다가오는 닻을 살피느라 부지런히 매달려 있었고, 이따금 양묘기에서 일하는 선원들을 북돋우려는 듯 음울한 찬송가 한 소절 같은 것을 흥얼거렸으니, 그 선원들은 한껏 흥에 겨워 부블 골목의 처녀들에 관한 후렴 같은 것을 목청껏 부르고 있었다. 그러나 빌다드는 불과 사흘 전에, 페쿼드호 위에서는, 특히 출항할 때는 불경한 노래를 일절 허용하지 않겠다고 그들에게 일러두었었다. 게다가 그의 누이 채리티는 선원 각자의 침상마다 와츠 찬송가 선집의 자그마한 정선본을 한 권씩 놓아 두었던 터였다.\n\n한편, 펠레그 선장은 배의 다른 쪽을 감독하느라 고물 쪽에서 더없이 무시무시하게 욕설을 퍼붓고 고함을 질렀다. 나는 닻이 올라오기도 전에 그가 배를 가라앉혀 버리지 않을까 싶을 정도였다. 나도 모르게 양묘봉을 잡은 채 멈춰 서서 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)에게도 그렇게 하라 일렀으니, 저런 악마 같은 자를 도선사로 삼고 항해를 떠나는 우리 둘이 무릅쓸 위험들을 떠올렸기 때문이다. 그래도 나는 경건한 빌다드에게서 일말의 구원을 찾을 수 있으리라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로하고 있었다 — 그가 777분의 1이라는 배당 몫을 매겼음에도 불구하고 말이다. 그때 별안간 엉덩이에 날카로운 쿡 찌름이 느껴졌고, 돌아보니 펠레그 선장이 바로 내 곁에서 다리를 거두어들이는 모습이 눈에 들어와 소스라치게 놀랐다. 그것이 내가 처음 걷어차인 발길질이었다.\n\n\"상선에서는 그딴 식으로 닻을 끌어 올리더냐?\" 그가 고함쳤다. \"용을 써, 이 양대가리야. 용을 써, 등뼈가 부러지도록! 왜 용을 안 쓰는 거냐, 이 말이다, 죄다 — 용을 써! 코호그! 용을 써, 거기 붉은 구레나룻 사내. 용을 써, 스코틀랜드 모자. 용을 써, 거기 초록 바지. 용을 쓰라니까, 죄다, 눈알이 튀어나오도록 용을 써!\" 그러고는 양묘기를 따라 이리저리 옮겨 다니며 다리를 거리낌 없이 마구 휘둘렀고, 그동안 태연자약한 빌다드는 줄곧 찬송가를 선창하고 있었다. 나는 속으로 생각했다, 펠레그 선장이 오늘 무언가 술을 좀 걸친 게 틀림없다고.\n\n마침내 닻이 올라오고, 돛이 펼쳐졌으며, 우리는 미끄러지듯 나아갔다. 짧고 추운 성탄절이었다. 그리고 짧은 북녘의 낮이 밤 속으로 잦아들 무렵, 우리는 어느덧 겨울 바다 한가운데로 거의 다 나와 있었으니, 그 얼어붙는 물보라가 윤이 나는 갑옷처럼 우리를 얼음으로 감쌌다. 뱃전을 따라 길게 늘어선 이빨들이 달빛 아래 반짝였고, 거대한 코끼리의 새하얀 상아 엄니처럼, 한껏 굽은 거대한 고드름들이 뱃머리에 매달려 있었다.\n\n깡마른 빌다드는 도선사로서 첫 당직을 이끌었다. 그리고 그 낡은 배가 검푸른 바다 속으로 깊이 곤두박질치며 오슬오슬한 서리를 온몸에 뒤집어쓰고, 바람이 울부짖고 삭구가 윙윙 울릴 때마다, 그의 흔들림 없는 가락이 들려왔다.\n\n\"넘실대는 강물 너머 정다운 들판이, 살아 숨 쉬는 초록빛 옷을 입고 서 있네. 그렇게 옛 유대인들 앞에 가나안이 서 있었으니, 그 사이로 요단강이 굽이쳐 흐를 적에.\"\n\n그 정다운 노랫말이 그때만큼 내게 더없이 정답게 울린 적은 없었다. 그 가락은 희망과 결실로 가득 차 있었다. 사나운 대서양의 이 매서운 겨울밤에도, 젖은 발과 더 흠뻑 젖은 외투에도 불구하고, 그때 내게는 앞으로 다다를 기분 좋은 항구가 수없이 마련되어 있을 것만 같았다. 그리고 영원히 봄빛을 띤 초원과 숲속 빈터들이 있어, 봄에 돋아난 풀이 밟히지도 시들지도 않은 채 한여름까지 그대로 남아 있을 것만 같았다.\n\n마침내 우리는 두 도선사가 더는 필요 없을 만큼 먼바다로 나아갔다. 우리를 뒤따라온 튼튼한 돛단배가 옆으로 바싹 붙어 오기 시작했다.\n\n이 갈림길에서 펠레그와 빌다드가, 특히 빌다드 선장이 어떤 심정에 휩싸였는지는 묘하면서도 보기 싫지 않은 광경이었다. 떠나기가 못내 아쉬웠던 것이다. 그토록 길고 위태로운 항해를 — 폭풍 몰아치는 두 곶을 모두 넘어가야 할 항해를 — 떠나는 배와 영영 작별하기가 못내 아쉬웠던 것이다. 자신이 애써 번 수천 달러를 투자한 배, 한때의 옛 동료가 선장으로 타고 있는 배, 자신과 거의 나이가 엇비슷한 그 사내가 또다시 저 무자비한 아가리의 온갖 공포와 맞서러 떠나는 배, 그에게는 모든 면에서 온갖 관심거리로 넘쳐 나는 그 무엇과 작별 인사를 나누기가 못내 아쉬웠던 것이다 — 가련한 늙은 빌다드는 오래도록 머뭇거렸다. 불안한 걸음으로 갑판을 서성였고, 또 한 번 작별의 말을 건네러 선실로 뛰어 내려갔다가, 다시 갑판으로 올라와 바람 부는 쪽을 바라보았다. 저 멀리 보이지 않는 동방의 대륙들로만 경계 지어진, 넓고 끝없는 바다를 바라보았다. 뭍 쪽을 바라보았다. 위쪽을 올려다보았다. 좌우를 둘러보았다. 사방을 보면서도 아무 데도 보지 않았다. 그러다 마침내, 기계적으로 밧줄을 고정 핀에 둘둘 감고는, 발작하듯 우람한 펠레그의 손을 움켜쥐었고, 등불을 치켜든 채 잠시 그의 얼굴을 영웅적으로 응시하며 서 있었으니, 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"그래도 말일세, 친구 펠레그, 나는 견딜 수 있네. 그래, 나는 견딜 수 있어.\"\n\n펠레그로 말하자면, 그는 이를 좀 더 철학자답게 받아들였다. 그러나 그 모든 철학에도 불구하고, 등불이 너무 가까이 다가오자 그의 눈에는 눈물 한 방울이 반짝이고 있었다. 그 역시 선실과 갑판 사이를 적잖이 오르내렸다 — 아래에서 한마디 건네고, 또 일등 항해사 스타벅과 한마디 나누곤 했다.\n\n그러나 마침내 그는, 마지막을 고하는 듯한 눈빛으로 주위를 둘러보며 동료를 돌아보았다. \"빌다드 선장 — 자, 옛 동료여, 우리는 가야 하네. 거기 큰 활대를 뒤로 돌려라! 보트, 어이! 자, 옆으로 바싹 붙일 준비를 해라! 조심해, 조심하라고! — 자, 빌다드, 이 사람아 — 마지막 인사를 하게. 행운을 비네, 스타벅 — 행운을 비네, 스터브 군 — 행운을 비네, 플래스크(Flask) 군 — 모두들 잘 가게, 행운을 비네 — 그리고 오늘로부터 삼 년 뒤에는 옛 낸터킷에서 김이 모락모락 나는 뜨거운 저녁을 자네들에게 차려 두고 기다리겠네. 만세, 자, 떠나라!\"\n\n\"신의 가호가 함께하길, 신의 거룩한 보살핌 속에 머무르길 비네, 사람들아.\" 늙은 빌다드가 거의 두서없이 중얼거렸다. \"이제 날씨가 좋아져서 에이해브 선장이 어서 자네들 사이를 거닐 수 있게 되길 바라네 — 그분에게 필요한 건 따사로운 햇볕뿐인데, 자네들이 떠나는 열대 항해에서는 그런 날이 넘쳐 날 테지. 사냥할 때 조심들 하게, 항해사들. 보트를 공연히 부수지 말게, 작살잡이들. 좋은 흰 삼나무 판자가 올 한 해에만 값이 꼬박 3퍼센트나 올랐네. 기도하는 것도 잊지 말고. 스타벅 군, 통장이가 여분의 통널을 낭비하지 않도록 살피게. 아! 돛 꿰매는 바늘은 초록색 사물함 속에 있네! 주님의 날에는 너무 고래잡이에 열을 올리지 말게, 사람들아. 하지만 좋은 기회를 놓치지도 말게, 그건 하늘이 주신 좋은 선물을 마다하는 짓이니. 당밀 큰 통을 잘 살피게, 스터브 군. 좀 새는 데가 있던 것 같으니. 섬에 들르거든, 플래스크 군, 음행을 조심하게. 잘 가게, 잘 가! 그 치즈를 선창에 너무 오래 두지 말게, 스타벅 군. 상하고 말 테니. 버터도 조심하고 — 파운드에 20센트나 주고 산 것이니, 명심하게나, 혹시라도 —\"\n\n\"자, 자, 빌다드 선장. 잔소리는 그만두고 — 어서 가세!\" 그 말과 함께 펠레그는 그를 서둘러 뱃전 너머로 내몰았고, 두 사람은 보트 안으로 내려섰다.\n\n배와 보트는 서로 멀어져 갔다. 차고 축축한 밤바람이 그 사이로 불었다. 끼룩끼룩 우는 갈매기 한 마리가 머리 위로 날아갔다. 두 선체가 사납게 일렁였다. 우리는 무거운 마음으로 세 번 환호성을 올린 뒤, 운명처럼 눈먼 채 외로운 대서양 속으로 곤두박질쳐 들어갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 23. The Lee Shore.\n\n몇 장 앞에서 벌킹턴이라는 사람이 언급된 적이 있다. 키가 크고, 갓 뭍에 오른 선원으로, 뉴베드퍼드의 여인숙에서 마주쳤던 인물이다.\n\n그 살을 에는 겨울밤, 피쿼드(Pequod)호가 복수심에 찬 뱃머리를 차갑고 악의에 찬 파도 속으로 들이밀었을 때, 그 키를 잡고 서 있는 사람이 다름 아닌 벌킹턴이 아니겠는가! 나는 연민 어린 경외와 두려움으로 그 사내를 바라보았다. 한겨울에, 사 년에 걸친 위험천만한 항해에서 막 돌아온 그가, 또다시 폭풍우 같은 한 차례의 항해를 향해 그토록 쉴 새 없이 다시 떠밀려 나갈 수 있다니. 뭍은 그의 발을 태워 버릴 듯한 모양이었다. 가장 경이로운 것은 언제나 입에 담을 수 없는 법이니, 깊은 기억은 어떤 묘비명도 내놓지 않는다. 이 여섯 치짜리 장(章)이야말로 벌킹턴의, 비석 없는 무덤이다. 다만 이것만은 말해 두자. 그에게 닥친 일은 폭풍에 시달리는 배의 처지와도 같아서, 그 배는 비참하게도 바람 불어가는 쪽의 뭍을 따라 떠밀려 간다. 항구는 기꺼이 구원의 손길을 내밀려 하고, 항구는 가엾이 여기는 마음으로 가득하며, 항구 안에는 안전과 안락과 화롯가와 저녁 식사와 따뜻한 담요와 벗들, 곧 죽을 운명을 타고난 우리에게 다정한 모든 것이 있다. 그러나 그 폭풍 속에서는 항구가, 그 뭍이 곧 그 배에게 가장 무서운 위험인 것이다. 배는 모든 환대를 피해 달아나야만 한다. 한 번의 뭍과의 닿음은, 설령 그것이 용골을 스치기만 할지라도, 배를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부르르 떨게 만들 것이다. 배는 온 힘을 다해 돛이란 돛을 모조리 펼쳐 뭍에서 멀어진다. 그리하여 그 와중에, 자신을 고향으로 불어 보내고픈 바로 그 바람들과 맞서 싸운다. 채찍질당하는 바다의 뭍 없음을 다시금 찾아 나선다. 피난처를 얻기 위해 쓸쓸히 위험 속으로 돌진해 들어간다. 배의 유일한 벗이 곧 가장 쓰라린 적인 것이다!\n\n이제 알겠는가, 벌킹턴이여? 그대는 저 죽도록 견딜 수 없는 진실의 단편을 언뜻언뜻 보는 듯하다. 곧 모든 깊고 진지한 사색이란, 하늘과 땅의 가장 사나운 바람들이 한데 모의하여 영혼을 저 위험천만하고 비굴한 뭍으로 내동댕이치려 할 때, 그 바다의 탁 트인 독립을 지켜 내려는 영혼의 두려움 없는 분투에 지나지 않는다는 진실을?\n\n그러나 가장 드높은 진실이 오직 뭍 없음 속에만 깃들어, 하느님처럼 물가도 없고 끝도 없는 것이라면 — 그렇다면, 비록 그것이 안전이라 할지라도, 명예롭지 못하게 바람 불어가는 쪽에 부서져 버리느니, 차라리 저 울부짖는 무한 속에서 스러지는 편이 낫다! 그러할진대, 아아! 누가 벌레처럼 비겁하게 뭍을 향해 기어가려 하겠는가! 무시무시한 것 중에서도 무시무시한 것이여! 이 모든 고뇌가 그토록 헛된 것이란 말인가? 용기를 내라, 용기를 내라, 오 벌킹턴이여! 의연히 견뎌 내라, 반신반인(半神半人)이여! 그대를 삼키는 대양의 물보라 속에서 — 곧장 위로, 그대의 신격화가 솟구쳐 오르나니!\n\n# CHAPTER 24. The Advocate.\n\n퀴퀘그와 나는 이제 어엿이 이 포경업이라는 일에 발을 들여놓았다. 그런데 이 포경업이라는 것이 어찌 된 영문인지 뭍사람들 사이에서는 다소 시적이지 못하고 평판 나쁜 생업으로 여겨지게 되었다. 그러니 나는 그대들, 뭍사람들에게 우리 고래 사냥꾼들이 이로 인해 받고 있는 부당함을 꼭 납득시키고 싶어 안달이 난 것이다.\n\n우선, 일반 대중들 사이에서 포경업이 이른바 자유전문직과 동등한 수준으로 평가받지 못한다는 사실을 입증하는 일은 거의 군더더기로 여겨질지도 모른다. 어느 잡다한 대도시 사교계에 낯선 사람 하나가 소개된다고 했을 때, 그가 이를테면 작살잡이로서 그 모임에 소개된다 한들 그의 인물됨에 대한 세간의 평이 조금이라도 올라갈 리 없으며, 만일 그가 해군 장교들을 흉내 내어 자기 명함에 S.W.F.(향유고래 어업)라는 머리글자를 덧붙인다면, 그런 짓은 더없이 주제넘고 가소로운 일로 여겨질 것이다.\n\n세상이 우리 고래잡이들에게 영예를 돌리기를 마다하는 한 가지 주된 이유는 분명 이것이다. 그들은 우리 직업이 기껏해야 도살에 가까운 부류의 일이며, 그 일에 한창 매달려 있을 때 우리는 온갖 더러움에 둘러싸여 있다고 여기는 것이다. 우리가 도살꾼인 것은 사실이다. 그러나 도살꾼이라면, 그것도 가장 피비린내 나는 표지를 단 도살꾼이라면, 세상이 한결같이 기꺼이 떠받드는 저 모든 군사 지휘관들 또한 그러했다. 그리고 우리 일이 불결하다는 그 비난에 관해서라면, 그대들은 머지않아 지금껏 거의 널리 알려지지 않았던 어떤 사실들을 깨우치게 될 것이며, 그 사실들은 대체로 보아 향유고래잡이 배를 적어도 이 단정한 지구상에서 가장 깨끗한 것들 가운데 당당히 세워 놓을 것이다. 그러나 설령 문제의 그 비난을 사실로 인정한다 한들, 고래잡이 배의 어질러지고 미끄러운 갑판이 그토록 많은 병사들이 돌아와 온갖 숙녀들의 갈채를 들이켜는 저 전장의 형언할 수 없는 시체 더미에 비할 만한 것이겠는가? 그리고 위험이라는 관념이 병사라는 직업에 대한 세간의 자부심을 그토록 드높여 준다면, 단언컨대 포대를 향해 거침없이 진격해 본 적 있는 숱한 노병들도 향유고래의 거대한 꼬리가 머리 위 허공을 소용돌이로 부채질하는 그 환영 앞에서는 순식간에 뒷걸음질 칠 것이다. 인간이 헤아릴 수 있는 공포 따위가 신의 그 서로 얽힌 공포와 경이에 어찌 비할 수 있겠는가!\n\n그러나 세상이 우리 고래 사냥꾼들을 비웃을지언정, 그것은 자기도 모르게 우리에게 가장 깊은 경의를 — 그렇다, 넘치고 넘치는 흠모를 바치고 있는 것이다! 지구 곳곳에서 타오르는 거의 모든 양초와 등불과 촛불이, 마치 수많은 신전 앞에서 타오르듯, 우리의 영광을 위해 타오르고 있으니!\n\n그러나 이 문제를 다른 빛으로 비추어 보고, 온갖 저울에 달아 보라. 우리 고래잡이들이 무엇이며, 또 무엇이었는지를 보라.\n\n어찌하여 드 비트 시대의 네덜란드인들은 그들의 포경 선단에 제독을 두었는가? 어찌하여 프랑스의 루이 16세는 사비를 털어 됭케르크에서 포경선을 의장하고, 우리 고향 낸터킷 섬에서 스무남은 가구를 그 도시로 정중히 초청했는가? 어찌하여 영국은 1750년에서 1788년 사이에 자국 고래잡이들에게 100만 파운드가 넘는 장려금을 지불했는가? 그리고 끝으로, 어찌하여 우리 아메리카의 고래잡이들이 이제 세계의 나머지 모든 고래잡이 무리를 합친 수를 능가하며, 700척이 넘는 함대를 띄우고, 1만 8천 명의 사람을 태우며, 해마다 400만 달러를 쓰고, 출항 당시 그 배들의 값어치가 2천만 달러에 이르며, 또 해마다 우리 항구로 700만 달러라는 알찬 수확을 거두어들이게 되었는가? 포경업에 무언가 강력한 힘이 깃들어 있지 않다면, 이 모든 일이 어찌하여 일어난단 말인가?\n\n그러나 이것은 절반에도 미치지 못한다. 다시 보라.\n\n나는 거리낌 없이 단언하건대, 저 세계주의 철학자라 한들 목숨을 걸고서라도 지난 60년 동안 온 넓은 세상에 한데 묶어 통틀어 보아 저 높고도 강력한 포경업보다 더 강력하게 작용한 평화로운 영향력 단 하나조차 짚어 낼 수 없을 것이다. 이러구러 포경업은 그 자체로 너무도 주목할 만하고, 그 잇따른 결과에 있어 끊임없이 중대한 사건들을 낳아 왔으니, 포경업은 가히 저 이집트의 어미에 견줄 만하다. 제 태에서 낳은 자식들이 다시 제 안에 아이를 밴 채로 태어나게 한 그 어미 말이다. 이 모든 것을 일일이 목록으로 정리하는 일은 가망 없고 끝없는 작업이 될 것이다. 한 줌만으로 족하다. 지난 여러 해 동안 고래잡이 배는 지구상에서 가장 멀고 가장 덜 알려진 지역들을 들추어 찾아내는 선구자였다. 그 배는 해도(海圖)에도 없고, 쿡(Cook)이나 밴쿠버조차 한 번도 항해한 적 없는 바다와 군도들을 탐험했다. 만일 아메리카와 유럽의 군함들이 이제 한때 야만스럽던 항구에 평화로이 정박해 있다면, 그들로 하여금 애초에 그 길을 보여 주고 그들과 야만인들 사이에서 처음으로 통역해 준 고래잡이 배의 명예와 영광을 위해 예포를 쏘게 하라. 그들이 탐험 원정대의 영웅들, 그대들의 쿡이며 그대들의 크루젠슈테른을 마음껏 기린들, 나는 말하노니 낸터킷에서 출항한 이름 없는 선장 수십 명이 그대들의 쿡이나 크루젠슈테른만큼, 아니 그보다 더 위대했다. 그들은 도움도 없이 빈손인 채로, 상어가 들끓는 저 이교의 바다에서, 기록에도 없고 투창이나 날아드는 섬들의 해변에서, 쿡이 그 모든 해병과 머스킷 총을 거느리고도 선뜻 무릅쓰려 하지 않았을 처녀지의 경이와 공포에 맞서 싸웠던 것이다. 저 옛 남양 항해기에서 그토록 요란하게 떠벌리는 그 모든 것들이, 우리 영웅적인 낸터킷 사람들에게는 한평생의 예사로운 일에 지나지 않았다. 밴쿠버가 세 장(章)을 통째로 바쳐 적은 모험들을, 이 사람들은 흔히 배의 일지에 적어 넣을 가치조차 없는 것으로 쳤다. 아, 세상이여! 오, 세상이여!\n\n고래잡이 어업이 케이프 혼을 돌아가기 전까지, 유럽과 태평양 연안에 늘어선 저 부유한 스페인 식민지들 사이에는 식민지 무역 외의 어떤 통상도, 식민지 교류 외의 어떤 왕래도 거의 이루어지지 않았다. 그 식민지들에 관한 스페인 왕실의 시샘 어린 정책을 처음으로 깨뜨린 이가 바로 고래잡이였다. 그리고 지면이 허락한다면, 어찌하여 그 고래잡이들로부터 마침내 페루와 칠레와 볼리비아가 옛 스페인의 멍에에서 해방되고, 그 지역에 영원한 민주정이 수립되기에 이르렀는지를 또렷이 보여 줄 수도 있으리라.\n\n지구 저편의 저 또 다른 위대한 아메리카, 곧 오스트레일리아를 깨우친 세상에 안겨 준 것도 고래잡이였다. 어느 네덜란드인이 어쩌다 헛디뎌 그곳을 발견한 뒤로, 다른 모든 배들은 그 해안을 역병처럼 야만스러운 곳으로 여겨 오래도록 피해 다녔으나, 고래잡이 배만은 그곳에 닿았다. 고래잡이 배야말로 이제 강성해진 그 식민지의 참된 어머니다. 게다가 최초의 오스트레일리아 정착지가 갓 생겨났을 무렵, 이주민들은 여러 차례 굶주림에서 구원받았으니, 다행히 그 바다에 닻을 내린 고래잡이 배가 베푼 자비로운 비스킷 덕분이었다. 폴리네시아 전역의 헤아릴 수 없는 섬들도 같은 진실을 고백하며, 선교사와 상인을 위해 길을 터 주고, 많은 경우 초기 선교사들을 그들의 첫 목적지까지 실어 나른 고래잡이 배에 통상의 경의를 바친다. 만일 저 이중으로 빗장을 지른 땅, 일본이 언젠가 손님을 맞아들이게 된다면, 그 공로는 오로지 고래잡이 배에게 돌아갈 것이다. 그 배는 이미 그 문턱에 와 있으니 말이다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 그대가 여전히 포경업에는 미학적으로 고귀하다 할 만한 연상이 조금도 없다고 단언한다면, 그렇다면 나는 그 자리에서 그대와 창 쉰 자루를 부딪칠 각오가 되어 있으며, 매번 그대의 투구를 쪼개어 그대를 말에서 떨어뜨릴 준비가 되어 있다.\n\n고래에게는 이름난 작가가 없고, 포경업에는 이름난 연대기 작가가 없다고 그대는 말할 것이다.\n\n고래에게 이름난 작가가 없고, 포경업에 이름난 연대기 작가가 없다고? 우리의 리바이어던에 관한 최초의 기록을 쓴 이가 누구인가? 다름 아닌 저 위대한 욥이 아니던가! 그리고 포경 항해에 관한 최초의 이야기를 지은 이가 누구인가? 다름 아닌 앨프레드 대왕 그 못지않은 왕이니, 그는 친히 왕의 펜으로 그 시대의 노르웨이 고래 사냥꾼 오테르의 입에서 나온 말을 받아 적었다! 그리고 의회에서 우리에게 그 빛나는 찬사를 바친 이가 누구인가? 다름 아닌 에드먼드 버크가 아니던가!\n\n과연 옳은 말이다, 그러나 고래잡이들 자신은 가련한 작자들이며, 그들의 핏줄에는 고귀한 피가 흐르지 않는다고 할 것이다.\n\n그들의 핏줄에 고귀한 피가 흐르지 않는다고? 거기에는 왕족의 피보다 더 나은 무언가가 흐른다. 벤저민 프랭클린의 할머니는 메리 모렐이었으니, 훗날 혼인으로 메리 폴저가 되어 낸터킷의 옛 정착민 가운데 한 사람이자, 길게 이어지는 폴저 가문과 작살잡이들의 여조상이 되었다. 모두가 고귀한 벤저민의 일가친척이며, 오늘날에도 미늘 박힌 작살을 세계의 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝으로 내던지고 있다.\n\n또 좋은 말이다, 그러나 어쨌든 포경업이 점잖지 못하다는 것은 모두가 인정하지 않느냐고 할 것이다.\n\n포경업이 점잖지 못하다고? 포경업은 제왕의 것이다! 옛 영국 성문법에 따르면 고래는 \"왕실 어류\"로 선포되어 있다.\n\n오, 그것은 그저 이름뿐인 것이다! 고래 그 자체가 대단하고 위엄 있는 방식으로 등장한 적은 한 번도 없다고 할 것이다.\n\n고래가 대단하고 위엄 있는 방식으로 등장한 적이 한 번도 없다고? 어느 로마 장군이 세계의 수도에 입성할 때 베풀어진 저 장엄한 개선식 가운데 하나에서, 멀리 시리아 해안에서부터 가져온 고래의 뼈가 심벌즈 울리는 행렬 속에서 가장 눈에 띄는 대상이었다.\n\n* 이 점에 관한 더 자세한 내용은 이후의 장들을 보라.\n\n그대가 그것을 인용하니 인정하겠다, 그러나 그대가 무어라 하든 포경업에는 진정한 위엄이 없다고 할 것이다.\n\n포경업에 위엄이 없다고? 우리 직업의 위엄은 저 하늘이 친히 증언한다. 고래자리(Cetus)는 남쪽 하늘의 별자리가 아닌가! 더 말할 것도 없다! 차르 앞에서는 모자를 푹 눌러쓰되, 퀴퀘그 앞에서는 모자를 벗으라! 더 말할 것도 없다! 나는 한평생 고래를 350마리나 잡은 사람을 알고 있다. 나는 그 사람을, 그만큼 많은 성곽 도시를 함락했노라 뽐내던 저 고대의 위대한 장군보다 더 명예로운 자로 친다.\n\n그리고 나 자신으로 말하자면, 만일 어떤 식으로든 내 안에 아직 발견되지 않은 으뜸가는 무언가가 있다면, 만일 내가 언젠가 그 작지만 고고하게 침묵하는 세계 — 내가 부당하다 할 수 없을 만큼 야심을 품어 봄 직한 그 세계 — 에서 진정한 명성을 얻을 자격이 생긴다면, 만일 내가 훗날 대체로 보아 하지 않고 내버려 두느니 차라리 해 두는 편이 나을 어떤 일을 해낸다면, 만일 내가 죽을 때 나의 유언 집행인들이, 아니 더 정확히 말해 나의 채권자들이 내 책상에서 무언가 값진 원고를 발견하게 된다면, 그렇다면 나는 여기서 미리 그 모든 명예와 영광을 포경업에 돌리는 바다. 고래잡이 배야말로 나의 예일 대학이요 나의 하버드였으니.\n\n# CHAPTER 25. Postscript.\n\n포경업의 존엄을 위하여, 나는 입증된 사실만을 내세우고 싶다. 그러나 자신의 사실들을 단단히 진열해 놓고서도, 자기 주장에 웅변적으로 보탬이 될 법한 그리 무리하지 않은 추측 하나를 통째로 억눌러 버리는 변호인이 있다면 — 그런 변호인이라면 비난받아 마땅하지 않겠는가?\n\n널리 알려진 바와 같이, 왕과 왕비의 대관식에서는, 심지어 근대의 군주들조차도, 그들을 자기 직무에 맞게 간 맞추는 어떤 기묘한 절차를 거친다. 이른바 국가 의례용 소금그릇이라는 것이 있고, 국가 의례용 양념병도 있을 법하다. 그들이 그 소금을 정확히 어떻게 쓰는지 — 누가 알겠는가? 그러나 내가 확실히 아는 것은, 왕의 머리가 대관식에서 엄숙하게 기름 부음을 받는다는 사실이다, 마치 샐러드 한 접시에 기름을 두르듯이. 하지만 혹시 그들이, 기계에 기름칠을 하듯, 그 머릿속이 잘 돌아가게 하려고 기름을 바르는 것은 아닐까? 이 왕가의 절차가 지닌 본질적 존엄에 관하여는 여기서 곰곰이 되새겨 볼 만한 것이 많다. 일상생활에서 우리는 머리에 기름을 바르고 그 기름 냄새를 노골적으로 풍기는 사내를 천박하고 멸시할 만한 자로 여기기 때문이다. 사실, 다 자란 성인 남자가 약용이 아닌 이상 머릿기름을 쓴다면, 그자는 어딘가에 물렁하고 미심쩍은 구석이 있을 공산이 크다. 일반적으로 말해, 그런 자는 그 전체를 따져 보아도 변변치 못한 인물일 수밖에 없다.\n\n그러나 여기서 따져 볼 단 한 가지는 바로 이것이다 — 대관식에서는 어떤 종류의 기름을 쓰는가? 분명 올리브유는 아닐 것이고, 마카사르유도, 피마자유도, 곰 기름도, 고래 기름도, 대구 간유도 아닐 것이다. 그렇다면 그것이, 정제도 오염도 거치지 않은 본디 그대로의, 모든 기름 가운데 가장 향기로운 향유고래 기름이 아니고서야 대체 무엇일 수 있겠는가?\n\n이를 한번 생각해 보라, 충성스러운 영국인들이여! 그대들의 왕과 왕비에게 대관식에 쓸 물건을 대 주는 것이 바로 우리 고래잡이들이다!\n\n# CHAPTER 26. Knights and Squires.\n\n피쿼드호의 일등항해사는 스타벅으로, 낸터킷 태생이며 가계로 보면 퀘이커 교도였다. 그는 키가 크고 진지한 사람이었으며, 비록 얼음으로 뒤덮인 해안에서 태어났으나 더운 위도를 견디기에 잘 맞는 듯했으니, 그의 살은 두 번 구운 비스킷처럼 단단했다. 서인도제도로 옮겨 놓는다 해도 그의 살아 있는 피는 병에 담긴 맥주처럼 상하지 않을 터였다. 그는 분명 어느 가뭄과 기근이 만연한 시절에, 아니면 그의 고향 주(州)가 유명한 그 금식일 가운데 어느 날에 태어났을 것이다. 그가 본 메마른 여름이라곤 고작 서른 번 남짓이었다. 그 여름들이 그의 육신에서 군더더기를 모조리 말려 버린 것이다. 그러나 이 야윈 몸은, 말하자면, 그를 갉아먹는 근심과 걱정의 표시도, 어떤 육체적 쇠락의 징후도 아닌 듯했다. 그것은 다만 그 사람을 응축해 놓은 것일 뿐이었다. 그는 결코 못난 인물이 아니었다. 오히려 그 반대였다. 깨끗하고 탱탱한 피부는 그의 몸에 더없이 잘 맞았으며, 그 안에 단단히 감싸인 채 내면의 건강과 힘으로 방부 처리된 듯한 이 스타벅은, 마치 되살아난 이집트인처럼 앞으로 오랜 세월을 견디고, 지금처럼 언제까지나 견뎌 낼 채비가 된 듯했다. 극지의 눈이든 작열하는 태양이든, 정밀한 크로노미터처럼 그의 내면의 활력은 어떤 기후에서도 잘 작동하리라 보증된 것이었다. 그의 눈을 들여다보면, 거기에 그가 평생 동안 침착하게 맞서 온 그 수천 겹의 위험들이 아직 어른거리는 모습을 보는 듯했다. 진중하고 한결같은 사람, 그의 삶은 대부분 소리로 이루어진 시시한 한 장(章)이 아니라, 행동으로 말하는 무언극이었다. 그러나 그 모든 강인한 절제와 불굴의 정신에도 불구하고, 그에게는 때때로 마음을 흔들고, 어떤 경우에는 그 나머지 모든 것을 거의 압도하다시피 하는 어떤 자질들이 있었다. 뱃사람치고는 보기 드물게 양심적이었고, 깊고 천성적인 경외심을 타고난 그였기에, 그의 삶이 지닌 거칠고 물에 잠긴 고독은 그를 강하게 미신 쪽으로 기울게 했다. 그러나 그것은 어떤 기질에서는 어쩐 일인지 무지가 아니라 오히려 지성에서 솟아나는 듯한 그런 종류의 미신이었다. 바깥의 전조와 안의 예감이 그의 것이었다. 그리고 때때로 이런 것들이 그의 영혼의 단련된 쇠를 휘어 놓았다면, 멀리 떨어진 고향의 추억들 — 케이프코드의 젊은 아내와 아이에 대한 기억은 그보다 훨씬 더, 본래 거칠던 그의 천성을 휘어 놓았고, 어떤 정직한 마음을 지닌 사람들에게서 무모한 만용의 분출을 억제하는 — 다른 이들이 포경업의 더 위험한 격변 속에서 그토록 자주 드러내는 그 만용을 억누르는 잠재적 영향력에 그를 한층 더 활짝 열어 놓았다. \"나는 고래를 두려워하지 않는 자는 내 보트에 태우지 않겠다.\" 스타벅은 이렇게 말했다. 이 말로써 그가 뜻한 바는, 가장 믿음직하고 쓸모 있는 용기란 마주친 위험을 공정하게 가늠하는 데서 솟아나는 용기라는 것뿐만 아니라, 전혀 두려움을 모르는 사람이야말로 겁쟁이보다 훨씬 더 위험한 동료라는 것인 듯했다.\n\n\"옳소, 옳소.\" 이등항해사 스터브가 말했다. \"저기 저 스타벅은 이 포경업 어디를 가도 찾아보기 힘들 만큼 신중한 사람이지.\" 그러나 스터브 같은 사람이나, 거의 모든 다른 고래잡이가 쓸 때 그 \"신중한\"이라는 말이 정확히 무엇을 뜻하는지는 머지않아 알게 될 것이다.\n\n스타벅은 위험을 찾아 나서는 십자군 같은 사람이 아니었다. 그에게 용기란 하나의 감정이 아니라, 그저 쓸모 있는 것이었으며, 목숨이 걸린 온갖 실제적 상황에서 늘 곁에 두는 것이었다. 게다가 그는 아마도, 이 포경이라는 일에서 용기란 쇠고기나 빵처럼 배가 갖추어야 할 중요한 비품 가운데 하나이므로 어리석게 낭비해서는 안 된다고 여겼던 것이다. 그래서 그는 해가 진 뒤에 고래를 잡으러 보트를 내리는 것을 달가워하지 않았고, 자기에게 너무 끈질기게 맞서 싸우는 고래와 끝까지 싸우는 것도 좋아하지 않았다. 스타벅은 이렇게 생각했던 것이다. 나는 이 험난한 바다에서 내 밥벌이를 위해 고래를 죽이러 온 것이지, 고래의 밥벌이를 위해 그들에게 죽임을 당하러 온 것이 아니다. 그리고 그렇게 죽임을 당한 사람이 수백 명에 이른다는 것을 스타벅은 잘 알고 있었다. 그 자신의 아버지의 운명은 어떠했던가? 저 밑바닥 없는 심연 어디에서 그는 형제의 찢긴 사지를 찾을 수 있단 말인가?\n\n이런 기억들을 품은 데다, 앞서 말했듯 어떤 미신적 기질까지 지닌 그였으니, 그럼에도 여전히 꽃피울 수 있었던 이 스타벅의 용기란 참으로 대단한 것이었음이 틀림없다. 그러나 그처럼 기질이 형성되고, 그토록 끔찍한 경험과 기억을 지닌 사람에게서 이런 것들이, 적절한 상황이 닥치면 그 갇혀 있던 데서 터져 나와 그의 모든 용기를 불살라 버릴 어떤 요소를 은밀히 잉태하지 않으리라는 것은 이치에 맞는 자연의 섭리가 아니었다. 그리고 그가 아무리 용감하다 한들, 그것은 주로 어떤 대담한 사람들에게서 보이는 그런 종류의 용기였으니, 바다나 바람이나 고래와 맞서거나, 세상의 여느 비이성적인 공포와 맞설 때는 대체로 굳건히 버티면서도, 때때로 격노한 강대한 사람의 응집된 이맛살에서 그대를 위협해 오는, 더 무시무시한 — 더욱 영적인 까닭에 무시무시한 — 공포에는 끝내 견뎌 내지 못하는 그런 용기였다.\n\n그러나 만일 앞으로 펼쳐질 이야기가 어느 대목에선가 가련한 스타벅의 불굴의 정신이 완전히 무너져 내리는 모습을 드러낸다면, 나는 그것을 차마 쓸 마음이 좀처럼 들지 않을 것이다. 영혼 속에서 용맹이 추락하는 모습을 드러내 보이는 일은 더없이 슬픈, 아니, 충격적인 일이기 때문이다. 사람들은 합자회사나 국가처럼 가증스러워 보일 수도 있다. 악당과 바보와 살인자가 있을 수도 있다. 사람들은 비열하고 빈약한 얼굴을 하고 있을 수도 있다. 그러나 인간은, 그 이상(理想)에서 보자면 너무나 고귀하고 너무나 찬란하며, 너무나 위대하고 빛나는 피조물이어서, 그에게 있는 어떤 치욕스러운 흠이라도 모든 동료들이 달려와 자신들의 가장 값진 예복을 던져 덮어 주어야 마땅한 것이다. 우리가 우리 자신 안에서, 우리 깊은 곳에서 느끼는 그 티 없는 인간다움은, 바깥의 모든 인격이 사라진 듯 보일지라도 고스란히 남아 있어, 용맹이 무너진 사람의 헐벗은 광경 앞에서 더없이 날카로운 고통으로 피 흘린다. 그처럼 부끄러운 광경 앞에서는 경건함조차도, 그것을 허락한 별들을 향한 책망을 온전히 억누를 수 없다. 그러나 내가 다루는 이 장엄한 존엄은 왕과 예복의 존엄이 아니라, 예복을 걸치지 않은 그 넘쳐흐르는 존엄이다. 그대는 그것이 곡괭이를 휘두르거나 못을 박는 팔에서 빛나는 것을 보게 될 것이다. 그 민주적 존엄은 사방으로, 끝없이 하느님으로부터 뻗어 나오는 것이니, 하느님 바로 그분에게서! 절대자이신 위대한 하느님! 모든 민주주의의 중심이자 둘레! 그분의 편재(遍在)야말로 우리의 신성한 평등이다!\n\n그러므로 만일 내가 앞으로 가장 미천한 뱃사람들과 배교자들과 표류자들에게, 비록 어두울지언정 고귀한 자질을 부여하고, 그들 둘레에 비극적인 기품을 엮어 넣는다면, 만일 그들 모두 가운데 가장 비통하고, 어쩌면 가장 천하게 추락한 자조차도 때로는 스스로를 드높은 봉우리로 들어 올린다면, 만일 내가 저 노동자의 팔에 어떤 천상의 빛을 입힌다면, 만일 내가 그의 재앙으로 저무는 해 위에 무지개를 펼쳐 놓는다면, 그때는 온 세상의 비판자들에 맞서 나를 끝까지 옹호해 다오, 그대 공정한 평등의 정신이여, 내 동족 모두에게 인류라는 하나의 왕다운 망토를 펼쳐 덮어 준 그대여! 나를 끝까지 옹호해 다오, 그대 위대한 민주의 하느님이여! 거무튀튀한 죄수 버니언에게 창백하고 시적인 진주를 마다하지 않으셨던 그대여, 늙은 세르반테스의 잘리고 가난해진 팔을 더없이 고운 황금을 두 번 두드려 편 잎사귀로 입히셨던 그대여, 앤드루 잭슨을 자갈밭에서 주워 올려, 군마(軍馬) 위로 내던지시고, 옥좌보다 더 높이 천둥처럼 끌어올리셨던 그대여! 그대의 모든 위대한 지상의 행진 속에서, 늘 왕다운 평민들 가운데서 가장 빼어난 투사들을 골라내시는 그대여, 나를 끝까지 옹호해 다오, 오 하느님이여!\n\n# CHAPTER 27. Knights and Squires.\n\n스터브(Stubb)는 이등항해사였다. 그는 케이프코드 태생이었고, 그래서 그곳 관습에 따라 케이프코드 사람이라 불렸다. 천하태평한 사내였다. 겁쟁이도 아니고 용맹한 자도 아니어서, 위험이 닥쳐오면 그저 무심한 태도로 받아들였다. 추격이 가장 위급한 절정에 이른 와중에도 그는 일 년 계약으로 고용된 목수가 일하듯 차분하고 침착하게 묵묵히 제 일을 해냈다. 성품이 쾌활하고 느긋하고 무던하여, 가장 치명적인 충돌의 순간조차 한 끼 만찬쯤으로, 그의 선원들을 모두 초대받은 손님쯤으로 여기는 양 자신의 포경 보트를 다스렸다. 그는 보트에서 제 몫의 자리를 편안하게 꾸미는 일에 어찌나 까다로웠던지, 마치 늙은 역마차 마부가 제 마부석의 아늑함에 신경 쓰는 것과 같았다. 고래에 바싹 다가가 사투의 한복판에 들어섰을 때에도, 그는 휘파람 부는 땜장이가 망치를 다루듯 그 무자비한 작살창을 태연하고 거침없이 휘둘렀다. 가장 약이 오른 괴물과 옆구리를 맞댄 채로도 그는 케케묵은 옛 뱃노래 가락을 흥얼거리곤 했다. 오랜 세월의 단련은 이 스터브에게 죽음의 아가리를 안락의자로 바꾸어 놓았다. 그가 죽음 그 자체를 어떻게 생각했는지는 알 길이 없다. 도대체 그것을 생각이나 해 보았는지조차 의문일지 모른다. 그러나 혹시라도 푸짐한 만찬을 마친 뒤 마음을 그쪽으로 돌리는 일이 있었다면, 틀림없이 그는 훌륭한 선원답게 그것을 일종의 당직 호출쯤으로, 곧 돛대 위로 후딱 올라가 그곳에서 무언가를 두고 부산을 떨라는 신호쯤으로 여겼을 것이다. 그 무언가가 무엇인지는 명령에 따라 올라가 본 뒤에야 알게 될 일이지, 그 전에는 알 수 없는 노릇이라고 말이다.\n\n다른 여러 이유와 더불어, 짐 보따리에 짓눌려 땅바닥에 허리를 구부린 음울한 행상꾼들로 가득한 세상에서 스터브를 그토록 느긋하고 두려움 없는 사내로, 삶의 짐을 짊어진 채 그토록 명랑하게 터덜터덜 걸어가는 사내로 만든 것이 무엇이었는가. 그 거의 불경하다 할 만한 쾌활함을 빚어내는 데 한몫한 것이 무엇이었는가. 그것은 틀림없이 그의 파이프였을 것이다. 그의 코와 마찬가지로, 짤막한 검은 그 파이프는 그의 얼굴을 이루는 변함없는 한 부분이었다. 그가 파이프 없이 침상에서 일어나리라 기대하느니 차라리 코 없이 일어나리라 기대하는 편이 나았을 정도다. 그는 미리 담배를 채워 둔 파이프를 한 줄로 가지런히 걸이에 꽂아 손닿는 자리에 두었다. 그래서 잠자리에 들 때마다 하나에 불을 붙여 그것으로 다음 것에 옮겨 붙이는 식으로 끝까지 차례차례 다 피웠고, 그러고는 다시 담배를 채워 새로 채비를 해 두었다. 스터브는 옷을 입을 때 바지에 먼저 다리를 꿰는 대신 입에 파이프부터 물었으니 말이다.\n\n내 말하건대, 이 끊임없는 흡연이야말로 적어도 그의 독특한 기질을 만든 한 가지 원인이었음이 분명하다. 누구나 알다시피 이 지상의 공기는, 뭍에 있든 물 위에 있든, 그것을 내뿜으며 죽어 간 수많은 인간들의 이름 없는 비참함에 끔찍하게 오염되어 있기 때문이다. 콜레라가 도는 때에 어떤 사람들이 입에 장뇌 적신 손수건을 대고 다니듯, 그와 마찬가지로 죽을 운명의 온갖 환난에 맞서 스터브의 담배 연기는 일종의 소독제 노릇을 했는지도 모른다.\n\n삼등항해사는 플래스크(Flask)로, 마서스 비니어드의 티스버리 태생이었다. 키가 작고 다부지며 혈색 좋은 젊은 친구였는데, 고래에 관한 한 무척이나 호전적이어서, 어쩐 일인지 저 거대한 리바이어던(Leviathan)들이 자기를 개인적으로, 또 대대로 모욕해 왔다고 여기는 듯했다. 그래서 마주칠 때마다 그것들을 없애 버리는 일이 그에게는 일종의 명예가 걸린 문제였다. 그는 고래의 장엄한 거구와 신비로운 습성이 자아내는 수많은 경이에 대한 경외감을 깡그리 잃은 사람이었다. 그것들과 마주치는 데서 비롯될지 모를 위험에 대한 두려움 따위도 아예 죽어 버린 사람이었다. 그의 어리석은 소견으로는, 저 경이로운 고래란 그저 몸집을 부풀린 생쥐, 적어도 물쥐 정도의 종류에 지나지 않아서, 죽여서 삶아 내자면 약간의 술수와 자그마한 수고와 품만 들이면 그만인 짐승이었다. 이 무지하고 무의식적인 두려움 없음 탓에 그는 고래에 관해서라면 다소 익살스러운 데가 있었다. 그는 재미 삼아 이 물고기들을 쫓아다녔다. 케이프혼을 도는 삼 년의 항해도 그에게는 그 기간만큼 이어지는 한바탕 유쾌한 농담일 뿐이었다. 목수의 못이 단조한 못과 잘라 만든 못으로 나뉘듯, 인류 또한 그렇게 나뉠 수 있을 것이다. 작은 플래스크는 단조한 못 가운데 하나였으니, 단단히 박혀 오래 버티도록 만들어진 못이었다. 페쿼드(Pequod)호 선상에서 사람들은 그를 킹포스트라 불렀다. 생김새로 보아 그가 북극 포경선에서 그 이름으로 불리는, 짧고 네모진 기둥에 빗댈 만했기 때문이다. 그 기둥은 사방으로 뻗어 박힌 여러 곁기둥의 도움으로, 사정없이 들이치는 저 빙해의 충격에 맞서 배를 떠받치는 역할을 한다.\n\n자, 이 세 항해사, 곧 스타벅(Starbuck)과 스터브와 플래스크는 막중한 인물들이었다. 보편적인 관례에 따라 페쿼드호의 보트 세 척을 선두잡이로서 지휘하는 자들이 바로 이들이었다. 에이해브(Ahab) 선장이 고래들에게 덮쳐들기 위해 자신의 병력을 배치할 저 웅대한 전투 대형에서, 이 세 선두잡이는 중대를 거느린 대장과도 같았다. 또한 길고 날카로운 포경 창으로 무장한 그들은 선발된 세 창기병과도 같았으니, 마치 작살잡이들이 투창을 던지는 자들이었던 것과 같았다.\n\n그리고 이 이름난 포경업에서는, 각 항해사 또는 선두잡이가 옛 고딕 기사처럼 언제나 자신의 키잡이, 곧 작살잡이를 동반한다. 그 작살잡이는 어떤 상황에서 앞서 쓰던 창이 공격 도중에 심하게 휘거나 비틀려 버렸을 때 새 창을 그에게 건네주는 역할을 맡는다. 게다가 이 둘 사이에는 대개 긴밀한 친밀함과 우정이 자리 잡고 있다. 그러므로 이 자리에서 페쿼드호의 작살잡이들이 누구였는지, 또 그들 각자가 어느 선두잡이에게 속해 있었는지를 적어 두는 것이 마땅하겠다.\n\n무엇보다 먼저 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 있었으니, 일등항해사 스타벅이 자신의 종자로 택한 사람이었다. 그러나 퀴케그는 이미 알려진 인물이다.\n\n다음은 태시테고(Tashtego)였다. 마서스 비니어드의 가장 서쪽 곶인 게이헤드 출신의 순수 혈통 인디언이었다. 그곳에는 아직도 붉은 피부 사람들이 살던 마을의 마지막 자취가 남아 있는데, 그 마을은 오래도록 이웃 섬 낸터킷(Nantucket)에 가장 대담한 작살잡이들을 숱하게 공급해 왔다. 포경업에서 그들은 보통 게이헤더라는 통칭으로 불린다. 태시테고의 길고 야윈 칠흑 같은 머리카락, 높이 솟은 광대뼈, 둥글고 검은 두 눈은 — 인디언치고는 동양인처럼 컸으나 그 반짝이는 표정만은 남극처럼 차가웠으니 — 이 모든 것이, 그가 일찍이 활을 들고 저 거대한 뉴잉글랜드 큰사슴을 좇아 본토의 원시림을 누비던 자랑스러운 사냥 전사들의 순수한 피를 물려받은 후예임을 충분히 드러내고 있었다. 그러나 이제 더는 숲속 야수의 발자취를 좇아 코를 킁킁대지 않고, 태시테고는 바다의 거대한 고래들이 남긴 항적을 쫓아 사냥했다. 아들의 빗나감 없는 작살이 선조들의 어김없는 화살을 알맞게 대신한 셈이었다. 뱀처럼 유연한 그의 사지에 어린 황갈색 근육을 바라보고 있노라면, 초기 청교도들의 미신을 거의 곧이듣게 되어, 이 거친 인디언이야말로 공중 권세의 군왕의 아들이 아닐까 반쯤 믿게 될 지경이었다. 태시테고는 이등항해사 스터브의 종자였다.\n\n작살잡이 가운데 셋째는 대구(Daggoo)였다. 거대한 몸집에 석탄처럼 새까만 흑인 야만인으로, 사자처럼 당당히 걸음을 옮기는 — 보기에 아하수에로 같은 사내였다. 그의 귀에는 황금 고리 두 개가 매달려 있었는데 어찌나 컸던지 선원들은 그것을 고리볼트라 부르며 거기에 윗돛 마룻줄을 묶어 두자고 농을 던지곤 했다. 젊은 시절 대구는 제 고향 해안의 외딴 만에 정박해 있던 어느 포경선에 스스로 올라탔다. 그리고 아프리카와 낸터킷, 그리고 고래잡이들이 가장 자주 드나드는 이교도 항구들 말고는 세상 어디에도 가 본 적이 없었으며, 이제는 여러 해 동안, 어떤 부류의 사람을 태우는지에 유달리 신경 쓰는 선주들의 배에서 포경업의 대담한 삶을 이끌어 왔던 터라, 대구는 자신의 야만적 미덕을 고스란히 간직하고 있었다. 그리하여 그는 기린처럼 꼿꼿하게, 양말만 신은 채로도 6피트 5인치(약 1.95미터)에 이르는 위풍당당함을 뽐내며 갑판을 누비고 다녔다. 그를 올려다보노라면 절로 몸이 겸손해졌다. 그의 앞에 선 백인은 마치 요새에 휴전을 구걸하러 온 백기처럼 보였다. 신기하게도 이 제왕 같은 흑인, 아하수에로 같은 대구는 그의 곁에 서면 장기짝처럼 보이는 작은 플래스크의 종자였다. 페쿼드호 선원의 나머지에 관해 말하자면, 오늘날 미국 포경업에 종사하는 수천 명의 평선원 가운데 미국 태생은 둘에 하나꼴도 되지 않는다는 점을 짚어 두어야겠다. 다만 사관들은 거의 모두가 미국 태생이다. 이 점에서 미국 포경업은 미국 육군과 군함대와 상선대, 그리고 미국의 운하와 철도 건설에 동원된 공병대와 매한가지다. 매한가지라고 말하는 까닭은, 이 모든 경우에 미국 태생 사람들이 두뇌를 넉넉히 대고 나머지 세계가 그에 못지않게 후하게 근육을 대 주기 때문이다. 이 포경 선원들 가운데 적지 않은 수가 아조레스 제도 출신인데, 그곳은 출항하는 낸터킷 포경선들이 그 바위 해안의 억센 농민들로 선원을 보충하려고 자주 들르는 곳이다. 마찬가지로 헐이나 런던에서 출항하는 그린란드 포경선들은 셰틀랜드 제도에 들러 선원 정원을 가득 채운다. 귀항하는 길에는 그들을 다시 그곳에 내려놓는다. 어찌 된 영문인지 알 수는 없으나, 섬사람들이 가장 뛰어난 고래잡이가 되는 듯하다. 페쿼드호의 선원도 거의 다 섬사람이었다. 나는 그런 자들을 고립자라 부르는데, 인류 공동의 대륙을 인정하지 않고 저마다 자신만의 별개의 대륙에서 살아가는 까닭이다. 그러나 이제 하나의 용골을 따라 연방을 이룬 이 고립자들은 어떤 무리였던가! 바다의 모든 섬과 땅의 모든 끝에서 모여든 아나카르시스 클로츠의 사절단이라 할 만했으니, 늙은 에이해브를 따라 페쿼드호에 올라, 그들 가운데 그리 많은 수가 다시는 돌아오지 못할 저 심판대 앞에 세상의 원한을 호소하러 가는 길이었다. 검은 꼬마 핍(Pip) — 그는 끝내 돌아오지 못했다. 오, 아니지! 그는 먼저 떠나갔다. 가엾은 앨라배마 소년이여! 음산한 페쿼드호의 앞갑판에서 그대들은 머지않아 그가 탬버린을 두드리는 모습을 보게 되리라. 그것은 영원의 시간을 알리는 전주곡이었으니, 부름을 받아 저 높은 곳 거대한 뒷갑판으로 불려 가, 천사들과 한데 어울려 영광 속에 탬버린을 두드리라는 명을 받을 그날의 전조였다. 이곳에서는 겁쟁이라 불렸으나, 저곳에서는 영웅이라 환호받으리라!\n\n# CHAPTER 28. Ahab.\n\n낸터킷(Nantucket)을 떠난 뒤로 여러 날 동안, 갑판 위에서는 에이해브(Ahab) 선장의 모습을 좀처럼 볼 수 없었다. 항해사들은 정해진 차례에 따라 번갈아 당직을 섰고, 겉으로 드러난 것만 보아서는 그들이 이 배의 유일한 지휘관인 것처럼 보였다. 다만 이따금 그들은 너무도 갑작스럽고 단호한 명령을 들고 선실에서 나오곤 했는데, 결국 그들이 누군가를 대신하여 명령을 내리고 있을 뿐이라는 사실이 분명히 드러나곤 했다. 그렇다, 그들의 지고한 주군이자 독재자가 거기 있었던 것이다. 다만 지금껏 그 신성한 은둔처가 된 선실 안을 들여다볼 허락을 받지 못한 어떤 눈에도 그는 보이지 않았을 뿐이다.\n\n아래에서 당직을 마치고 갑판으로 올라올 때마다, 나는 곧장 고물 쪽을 바라보며 낯선 얼굴이 보이는지 살피곤 했다. 이제 망망대해의 고립 속에 들어앉은 미지의 선장에 대해 처음에는 막연하던 내 불안이 거의 동요라 할 만한 것으로 자라났기 때문이다. 게다가 이 불안은, 누더기를 걸친 일라이저(Elijah)의 그 악마 같은 횡설수설이 청하지도 않았는데 자꾸만 내 머릿속에 되살아남으로써 이따금 기이하게 더해졌으니, 그것은 내가 전에는 상상조차 할 수 없었던 미묘한 힘으로 다가왔다. 그러나 나는 그것들을 거의 견뎌 내지 못했는데, 다른 기분일 때에는 그 부두의 괴상한 예언자가 늘어놓던 엄숙한 변덕에 거의 미소를 지을 뻔하기도 했건만 말이다. 그러나 내가 느낀 것이 무엇이었든 — 두려움이라 하든 불안이라 하든 — 막상 배 안을 둘러볼 때면, 그런 감정을 품는 것이 온갖 이치에 어긋나는 것처럼 여겨졌다. 작살잡이들은 물론 선원 대다수가, 내가 전에 겪어 익히 알던 그 어떤 점잖은 상선의 무리보다 훨씬 더 야만스럽고 이교도적이며 잡다한 패거리였지만, 나는 그것을 — 그리고 마땅히 그렇게 보았듯이 — 내가 그토록 무모하게 몸을 내던진 저 거친 스칸디나비아식 직업의 본성 자체가 지닌 사나운 독특함 탓으로 돌렸던 것이다. 그러나 이 빛바랜 의심들을 가장 강력하게 가라앉히고, 이번 항해의 온갖 정경에 대해 신뢰와 명랑함을 불러일으킨 것은 무엇보다도 이 배의 세 수석 사관, 곧 항해사들의 면모였다. 이들보다 더 낫고, 더 미더운 바다의 사관이자 사내를 각기 제 나름의 방식대로 그리 쉽게 찾아내기는 어려웠을 것이다. 그리고 그들은 하나같이 미국인이었으니, 낸터킷 사람과 비니어드 사람과 케이프 사람이었다. 그런데 배가 항구를 박차고 나선 때가 마침 크리스마스 무렵이었던지라, 한동안 우리는 살을 에는 극지의 날씨를 겪었다. 다만 내내 그것을 피해 남쪽으로 달아나는 중이었으니, 우리가 항해한 위도의 한 도, 한 분마다 그 무자비한 겨울과 그 견딜 수 없는 날씨를 차츰 뒤로 떨쳐 내고 있었다. 그날은 그 전환기의 아침들 가운데 하나로, 음울함이 조금 덜하긴 했으나 여전히 잿빛이고 어둑한 아침이었다. 순풍을 받은 배가 어딘가 복수심에 찬 듯한 도약과 우울한 속도로 물살을 헤치며 내달리고 있을 때, 오전 당직을 부르는 소리에 갑판으로 올라가 고물 난간 쪽으로 시선을 던지는 순간, 불길한 전율이 온몸을 훑고 지나갔다. 현실이 예감을 앞질렀던 것이다. 에이해브 선장이 후갑판 위에 서 있었다.\n\n그에게서는 흔한 육체의 병색도, 그런 병에서 회복된 흔적도 보이지 않았다. 그는 화형대에서 풀려난 사람처럼 보였다. 불길이 사지를 모조리 태워 버리지는 못한 채, 빈틈없이 다져진 그 노년의 강건함에서 단 한 톨도 앗아 가지 못한 채, 그저 다 핥아 버리고 만 그런 사람 말이다. 그의 크고 떡 벌어진 온몸은 단단한 청동으로 빚어, 결코 바꿀 수 없는 거푸집에 부어 만든 것 같았으니, 마치 첼리니가 주조한 페르세우스상 같았다. 그의 잿빛 머리카락 사이로 길을 내며 빠져나와, 햇볕에 그을린 누런 얼굴과 목의 한쪽을 따라 곧장 내려가다 옷 속으로 사라지는, 가느다란 막대 모양의 자국 하나가 보였는데, 그것은 납빛이 도는 희끄무레한 색이었다. 그것은 거대한 나무의 곧고 우뚝한 줄기에 이따금 생기는 그 수직의 흠과 닮아 있었다. 위에서 번개가 내리꽂혀 갈가리 찢으며 내달릴 때, 잔가지 하나 비틀지 않으면서도 꼭대기에서 밑동까지 껍질을 벗기고 홈을 파낸 뒤 땅속으로 빠져나가, 나무는 여전히 푸르게 살아 있되 낙인이 찍힌 채로 남게 되는, 바로 그런 흠 말이다. 그 자국이 태어날 때부터 그와 함께한 것인지, 아니면 어떤 끔찍한 상처가 남긴 흉터인지는 아무도 확실히 말할 수 없었다. 어떤 무언의 합의에 따라, 항해 내내 그것에 관해서는 거의 또는 전혀 언급이 없었으니, 특히 항해사들이 그러했다. 그러나 한번은 태시테고(Tashtego)의 손윗사람으로 선원 중에 끼어 있던 늙은 게이헤드 인디언이, 에이해브가 그렇게 낙인찍히게 된 것은 그가 마흔이 다 차고 나서야 일어난 일이며, 그것도 어떤 목숨을 건 싸움의 광란 속에서가 아니라 바다에서 벌어진 원초적 격투 가운데 그에게 닥친 것이라고 미신적으로 단언했다. 그러나 이 거친 추측은, 잿빛의 늙은 맨섬 사람이 넌지시 흘린 말로 미루어 부정되는 듯했다. 그는 무덤처럼 음침한 늙은이로, 전에 한 번도 낸터킷을 벗어나 항해해 본 적이 없었던 까닭에 이번이 있기 전까지는 거친 에이해브를 본 적이 없었다. 그럼에도 오래된 바다의 전설들, 아득한 옛날부터 내려온 속신들은 이 늙은 맨섬 사람에게 초자연적인 통찰의 힘이 깃들어 있다고 널리 믿게 만들었다. 그래서 그가 말하기를, 만약 언젠가 에이해브 선장이 고요히 염습되어 눕는 날이 온다면 — 그런 일이 좀처럼 일어나지는 않으리라고 그는 중얼거렸지만 — 그때 죽은 자를 위해 그 마지막 의식을 행하는 자가 누구이든, 그의 몸에서 정수리부터 발바닥까지 이어진 태생의 점을 발견하게 될 것이라고 했을 때, 어떤 백인 선원도 그 말에 진지하게 반박하지 못했다.\n\n에이해브의 그 음산한 면모 전체와 그것을 가로지른 납빛 낙인이 어찌나 강렬하게 나를 사로잡았던지, 처음 얼마 동안 나는 그 위압적인 음산함의 적지 않은 부분이 그가 한쪽으로 기대어 선 야만스러운 흰 다리에서 비롯된 것임을 거의 알아채지 못했다. 이 상아 다리가 바다에서 향유고래 턱뼈를 매끄럽게 갈아 만든 것이라는 사실은 전에 이미 내 귀에 들어와 있었다. \"그래, 그는 일본 앞바다에서 돛대를 잃었지.\" 언젠가 그 늙은 게이헤드 인디언이 말했다. \"하지만 돛대를 잃은 자기 배처럼, 그는 고향에 돌아가지 않고도 새 돛대를 갖춰 달았어. 그런 걸 한 통 가득 갖고 있거든.\"\n\n나는 그가 취하고 있던 그 기이한 자세에 깊은 인상을 받았다. 피쿼드호(Pequod)의 후갑판 양편, 뒷돛대 지삭에 꽤 가까운 자리에, 송곳으로 뚫은 구멍이 하나씩 있었는데, 널빤지 속으로 반 인치쯤 파인 것이었다. 그의 뼈다귀 다리가 그 구멍에 단단히 박혀 있었고, 한쪽 팔은 위로 치켜올려 지삭을 붙들고 있었다. 에이해브 선장은 곧추선 채로, 끊임없이 출렁이는 뱃머리 너머 저 먼 곳을 똑바로 응시하고 있었다. 흔들림 없이 두려움 없는, 앞을 향한 그 시선의 헌신 속에는 더없이 굳건한 불굴의 의지가, 단호하고 결코 굴복하지 않을 고집이 한없이 깃들어 있었다. 그는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 그의 사관들도 그에게 아무 말도 건네지 않았다. 다만 그들의 더없이 사소한 몸짓과 표정 하나하나로, 번민에 찬 주인의 눈 아래 놓여 있다는, 고통스럽다고까지는 못해도 불안한 의식을 분명히 드러내 보이고 있었다. 그뿐 아니라, 우울에 짓눌린 에이해브는 얼굴에 십자가형의 고난을 띤 채, 어떤 거대한 비통이 자아내는 이름 붙일 수 없는 제왕적이고 위압적인 위엄을 온통 두른 채로 그들 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n이윽고 그는 처음으로 바깥 공기를 쐬고 나서 다시 선실로 물러났다. 그러나 그날 아침 이후로 그는 날마다 선원들 앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 그 구멍에 다리를 박고 서 있거나, 가지고 있던 상아 의자에 앉아 있거나, 무거운 걸음으로 갑판을 거닐거나 했다. 하늘이 덜 음울해지고, 실로 조금씩 온화해지기 시작하자, 그도 점점 덜 은둔하게 되었다. 마치 배가 고향을 떠났을 때 그를 그토록 칩거하게 만든 것이 오직 죽은 듯 황량한 겨울 바다의 적막뿐이었던 것처럼 말이다. 그리고 차츰, 그는 거의 끊임없이 바깥 공기 속에 머무르게 되었다. 그러나 아직까지는, 마침내 햇살이 비치는 갑판 위에서 그가 한 말이나 눈에 띄게 행한 모든 것을 보더라도, 그곳에서 그는 또 하나의 돛대만큼이나 쓸모없는 존재처럼 보였다. 하지만 지금 피쿼드호는 그저 항로를 지나가고 있을 뿐, 본격적으로 순항하는 것은 아니었다. 거의 모든 고래잡이 준비는 감독이 필요했으되 항해사들이 충분히 감당할 수 있는 일이었으니, 이제 에이해브 자신을 제외하고는 그를 부리거나 자극할 거리가 거의, 아니 전혀 없었다. 그리하여 그 한동안만이라도, 겹겹이 쌓여 그의 이마에 얹혀 있는 구름을 쫓아낼 길이 없었던 것이다. 마치 모든 구름이 가장 높은 봉우리를 골라 그 위에 쌓이기를 택하듯이.\n\n그럼에도 머지않아, 우리가 들어선 그 화창한 휴일 같은 날씨의 따뜻하고 지저귀는 듯한 설득력이 차츰 그를 그 침울한 기분에서 끌어내는 듯했다. 볼이 발그레한 채 춤추는 처녀들, 곧 사월과 오월이 겨울의 인간 혐오에 잠긴 숲으로 사뿐히 돌아올 때, 헐벗고 거칠며 벼락에 갈라진 가장 늙은 떡갈나무조차 그 기쁨에 찬 손님들을 맞이하려 몇 줄기 푸른 새싹을 적어도 내미는 법이듯이, 에이해브도 마침내는 그 소녀 같은 대기의 장난스러운 유혹에 조금은 응답했던 것이다. 그는 한 번 이상, 어떤 표정의 희미한 꽃봉오리를 피워 보였는데, 다른 사람 같았으면 그것은 이내 미소로 활짝 피어났을 그런 표정이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 29. Enter Ahab; to Him, Stubb.\n\n며칠이 흘렀고, 얼음과 빙산도 모두 고물 뒤로 멀어졌다. 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 이제 밝은 키토의 봄을 가르며 일렁일렁 나아갔다. 바다에서는 그 봄이 거의 영구히 열대의 영원한 팔월 문턱을 다스리고 있었다. 따스하면서도 서늘하고, 맑고, 청량하게 울리며, 향기롭고, 넘쳐흐르며, 풍요로운 그 나날들은, 장미수 눈을 켜켜이 쌓아 올린 — 송이송이 얹은 페르시아 셔벗을 담은 수정 잔과도 같았다. 별이 총총하고 위풍당당한 밤들은, 보석 박힌 벨벳을 두른 채 고독한 자긍심 속에 홀로 집을 지키며, 멀리 떠난 정복자 백작들, 곧 황금 투구를 쓴 태양들의 기억을 품어 기르는 도도한 귀부인들 같았다! 잠을 자려는 이에게는 이토록 매혹적인 낮과 이토록 유혹적인 밤 사이에서 어느 하나를 고르기란 쉽지 않았다. 그러나 그 시들 줄 모르는 날씨의 온갖 마법은 단지 바깥 세계에 새로운 주문과 위력을 더해 주는 데 그치지 않았다. 그것들은 안으로 돌아 영혼을 파고들었으니, 특히 저녁의 고요하고 부드러운 시간이 다가올 때 그러했다. 그러면 소리 없는 황혼이 가장 또렷한 얼음을 빚어내듯, 기억은 제 수정들을 쏘아 올렸다. 그리고 이 모든 미묘한 작용들은, 점점 더, 에이해브(Ahab)의 결을 파고들어 갔다.\n\n노년은 늘 잠이 없는 법이다. 마치 삶과 더 오래 이어질수록, 인간은 죽음을 닮은 그 무엇과도 덜 엮이려는 듯이. 바다의 지휘관들 가운데서도 늙고 흰 수염을 기른 자들이 가장 자주 잠자리를 떠나 밤의 장막에 덮인 갑판을 찾는다. 에이해브도 그러했다. 다만 요즈음 들어, 그는 어찌나 바깥 공기 속에 머무는 듯 살았던지, 사실대로 말하자면 그의 발걸음은 선실에서 갑판으로 나오는 일보다 갑판에서 선실로 들어가는 일이 더 많았다. \"나 같은 늙은 선장이 이 좁은 승강구로 내려가, 무덤처럼 파인 잠자리로 향하는 건\" 하고 그는 혼자 중얼거리곤 했다. \"꼭 제 무덤 속으로 내려가는 기분이로군.\"\n\n그리하여 거의 스물네 시간마다, 밤의 당직이 정해지고, 갑판 위의 무리가 아래에서 잠든 무리의 단잠을 지키며 보초를 설 무렵, 그리고 앞갑판에서 밧줄을 끌어당겨야 할 때면 선원들이 낮처럼 거칠게 그것을 내던지지 않고, 잠든 동료들을 깨울까 두려워 조심스레 제자리에 내려놓을 무렵, 이런 한결같은 정적이 으레 깃들기 시작하면, 말없는 키잡이는 선실 승강구를 지켜보았다. 그리고 머지않아 그 노인이, 불편한 걸음을 거들려고 쇠 난간을 움켜쥔 채 모습을 드러내곤 했다. 그에게도 남을 헤아리는 인정의 손길이 어느 정도 남아 있었으니, 이런 때면 그는 대개 후갑판을 거니는 일을 삼갔다. 그의 상아 발꿈치에서 여섯 인치도 안 되는 곳에서 안식을 구하는 지친 항해사들에게, 그 뼈로 된 걸음이 울려대는 우지끈 소리와 소란이라니, 그들의 꿈이 온통 상어가 뼈를 으드득 씹는 소리로 가득 찰 지경이었기 때문이다. 그러나 한번은, 그가 빠져든 기분이 그런 사소한 헤아림에는 미치지 못할 만큼 깊었다. 그가 무겁고 통나무 같은 걸음으로 고물 난간에서 큰돛대까지 배의 길이를 재며 거닐고 있을 때, 늙은 이등 항해사 스터브(Stubb)가 아래에서 올라와, 어딘가 자신 없고 미안해하는 듯한 익살을 띤 채, 만약 에이해브 선장이 갑판을 거니는 게 흡족하시다면야 아무도 안 된다 할 수 없겠으나, 그래도 그 소리를 죽일 무슨 방도가 있지 않겠느냐고 넌지시 말했다. 그러고는 뱃밥 뭉치 한 덩이에다 그 상아 발꿈치를 끼워 넣는 일에 관해 무언가를 어물어물 망설이며 흘렸다. 아! 스터브, 그대는 그때 에이해브를 알지 못했다.\n\n\"내가 포탄이라도 된단 말이냐, 스터브\" 하고 에이해브가 말했다. \"그런 식으로 나를 메워 막으려 하다니? 허나 가던 길이나 가거라. 내가 깜빡했군. 네 밤의 무덤으로 내려가라. 너희 같은 자들이 수의(壽衣) 사이에서 잠을 자며, 끝내 그 안을 채우는 데 익숙해지는 그곳으로. — 내려가라, 개야, 개집으로!\"\n\n그토록 갑작스레 경멸을 내뱉는 노인의, 미처 예상치 못한 끝맺음의 외침에 흠칫 놀란 스터브는 한순간 말을 잃었다. 그러더니 격앙되어 말했다. \"저는 그런 식으로 말을 듣는 데 익숙하지 않습니다, 선장님. 절반도 채 마음에 들지 않는군요, 선장님.\"\n\n\"닥쳐!\" 에이해브가 앙다문 이 사이로 으드득 내뱉으며, 무슨 격정 어린 유혹이라도 피하려는 듯 거세게 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 선장님. 아직은 아닙니다\" 하고 스터브가 대담해져서 말했다. \"저는 순순히 개라 불릴 생각은 없습니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당나귀라고 열 번, 노새라고, 멍청이라고 불러 주마. 그러니 썩 꺼져라, 안 그러면 이 세상에서 너를 쓸어버리겠다!\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 에이해브가 그 표정에 어찌나 압도적인 위세를 띠고 다가들었던지, 스터브는 저도 모르게 물러서고 말았다.\n\n\"이렇게 당하고도 호되게 한 방 갚아 주지 못한 건 난생처음이군\" 하고 스터브는 어느새 선실 승강구를 내려가는 자신을 발견하고는 중얼거렸다. \"참 묘하단 말이야. 가만, 스터브. 어쩐 일인지 지금 나는 다시 올라가서 그를 한 대 갈겨야 할지, 아니면 — 저게 뭐람? — 여기 무릎을 꿇고 그를 위해 기도라도 해야 할지 잘 모르겠단 말이지. 그래, 그런 생각이 내 안에서 떠오르고 있었어. 하지만 그건 내가 기도라는 걸 다 해 보는 첫 번째 일이 될 거야. 묘해, 아주 묘하단 말이지. 그리고 저 양반도 묘해. 그래, 앞에서 보나 뒤에서 보나, 스터브가 함께 항해해 본 늙은이 중에 가장 묘한 양반이야. 어떻게 나한테 그렇게 번뜩였담! — 그 눈이 화약 접시 같았어! 미친 건가? 어쨌든 그의 마음속에 뭔가 걸린 게 있어, 갑판이 우지끈할 때 그 위에 분명 뭔가가 얹혀 있는 것만큼이나 확실하게. 그리고 그 양반은 이제 하루 스물네 시간 중에 세 시간도 침상에 누워 있지 않아. 게다가 그때조차 잠을 안 자. 저 도-보이라는 급사가 나한테 그러지 않았나, 아침마다 보면 늘 그 노인의 해먹 침구가 온통 구겨지고 헝클어져 있고, 시트는 발치까지 흘러내려 있고, 덮개는 거의 매듭이 지도록 비틀려 있고, 베개는 마치 구운 벽돌이라도 얹혀 있었던 것처럼 무섭게 뜨끈하다고 말이야. 뜨거운 늙은이야! 내 짐작엔 저 양반, 뭍에 사는 어떤 사람들이 양심이라 부르는 걸 지니고 있는 것 같아. 그게 일종의 안면 신경통이라던데 — 치통보다 더 고약한 거라잖아. 그래, 그래, 그게 뭔지는 모르겠지만, 주여, 제가 그것에 옮지 않게 지켜 주소서. 그 양반은 온통 수수께끼투성이야. 도-보이가 의심한다고 나한테 일러 준 대로, 밤마다 도대체 무엇 하러 뒤쪽 선창으로 들어가는 걸까. 그게 무엇 때문인지 좀 알고 싶군그래. 누가 선창에서 그 양반과 약속이라도 잡아 놨나? 자, 이거 묘하지 않은가? 하지만 알 길이 없지, 늘 그렇고 그런 노릇이니까. — 자, 이제 한숨 자러 가자. 빌어먹을, 사람이 이 세상에 태어나는 보람이 있다면, 그저 곤히 잠들 수 있다는 것 하나만으로도 그럴 만하지. 그러고 보니 그게 아기들이 하는 거의 첫 번째 일이기도 하군. 그것도 일종의 묘한 일이지. 빌어먹을, 따지고 보면 세상만사가 다 묘하단 말이야. 하지만 그건 내 신조에 어긋나지. 생각하지 말 것, 이게 내 열한 번째 계명이고, 잘 수 있을 때 자 둘 것, 이게 내 열두 번째 계명이거든. — 그러니 자, 다시 가 보자고. 그런데 저게 어찌 된 거지? 그가 나를 개라고 부르지 않았나? 제기랄! 나를 당나귀라고 열 번이나 부르고, 그 위에다 멍청이를 한 무더기 쌓아 올렸어! 차라리 나를 걷어차고 끝내 버리는 편이 나을 뻔했군. 어쩌면 정말로 나를 걷어찼는데, 내가 미처 못 알아챘는지도 몰라, 어쩐지 그 양반 이마에 그만 완전히 넋이 나가 버렸으니까. 그게 바랜 뼈처럼 번뜩였단 말이지. 도대체 나한테 무슨 일이 일어난 거야? 두 다리로 똑바로 서 있지를 못하겠어. 저 늙은이와 부딪힌 게 나를 어쩐지 뒤집어 까 놓은 모양이야. 맙소사, 내가 꿈이라도 꾸고 있었던 게 분명해. — 어떻게? 어떻게? 어떻게? — 하지만 유일한 길은 이걸 다 치워 두는 거지. 그러니 자, 다시 해먹으로 가자. 그리고 아침이 되면, 이 골치 아픈 요술이 환한 대낮에 어떻게 비치는지 한번 따져 봐야겠어.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 30. The Pipe.\n\n스터브가 물러간 뒤, 에이해브는 한동안 뱃전 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 서 있었다. 그러다가 요즈음 들어 버릇처럼 된 대로, 당직 선원 하나를 불러 아래로 내려보내 자신의 상아 의자와 파이프를 가져오게 했다. 그는 나침반함 등불에 파이프를 붙여 불을 댕긴 다음, 갑판의 바람받이 쪽에 의자를 놓고 앉아서 담배를 피웠다.\n\n전해 오는 이야기로는, 옛 노르드 시대에 바다를 사랑하던 덴마크 왕들의 옥좌는 일각고래의 엄니로 만들어졌다고 한다. 그렇다면 그 뼈로 된 삼각 의자 위에 앉은 에이해브를 바라보면서, 그것이 상징하는 왕권을 떠올리지 않을 수 있겠는가? 에이해브야말로 갑판의 칸(汗)이요, 바다의 왕이요, 리바이어던의 위대한 군주였으니 말이다.\n\n얼마간 시간이 흘렀고, 그동안 짙은 연기가 그의 입에서 빠르고도 끊임없는 뭉치로 뿜어져 나왔다가 도로 그의 얼굴로 불려 돌아왔다. \"이게 어찌 된 일인가.\" 마침내 그가 파이프 대롱을 입에서 떼며 혼잣말을 했다. \"이 담배가 더는 마음을 달래 주지 않는구나. 오, 내 파이프여! 네 마법마저 사라졌다면 나는 참으로 견디기 힘들 터인데! 여기서 나는 즐기지도 못한 채 나도 모르게 고역만 치르고 있었구나 — 그래, 게다가 줄곧 어리석게도 바람받이 쪽으로 담배를 피우고 있었으니, 바람 부는 쪽으로, 그것도 그토록 신경질적인 연기를 뿜어 대며, 마치 죽어 가는 고래가 가장 거센, 가장 고통으로 가득 찬 마지막 물줄기를 뿜어 올리듯이 말이다. 내가 이 파이프와 무슨 상관이 있단 말인가? 이 물건은 본디 평온을 위한 것, 부드러운 흰 머리칼 사이로 부드러운 흰 연기를 피워 올리라고 있는 것이지, 나처럼 헝클어진 쇳빛 잿빛 머리채 사이로 피우라고 있는 것이 아니거늘. 나는 더 이상 담배를 피우지 않으리라—\"\n\n그는 아직도 불이 붙은 파이프를 바다로 던져 버렸다. 불이 물결 속에서 쉭 소리를 냈고, 바로 그 순간 배는 가라앉는 파이프가 만든 거품 곁을 스쳐 지나갔다. 모자를 푹 눌러쓴 채, 에이해브는 비틀거리며 갑판을 거닐었다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 스터브가 플라스크에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"이렇게 괴상한 꿈은 처음 꿔 봤네, 킹포스트. 영감님의 그 상아 다리 알지? 글쎄 꿈속에서 그 다리로 날 걷어차지 뭔가. 그래서 나도 되받아 차려고 했더니, 맙소사, 이 사람아, 내가 내 다리를 통째로 차서 떼어 내 버렸지 뭔가! 그러더니 순식간에 에이해브가 피라미드처럼 변하고, 나는 미친 멍청이마냥 그걸 줄곧 걷어차고 있었네. 한데 더 이상한 건 말이야, 플라스크 — 꿈이란 게 다 얼마나 묘한지 자네도 알겠지만 — 그렇게 화가 머리끝까지 치밀어 오른 와중에도, 어쩐지 속으로 이런 생각이 드는 거야. 따지고 보면 에이해브한테 차인 게 그리 대단한 모욕은 아니라고 말일세. '아니,' 하고 나는 생각했지, '뭐가 문제야? 진짜 다리도 아니고, 그저 가짜 다리잖아.' 살아 있는 것한테 맞는 것과 죽은 것한테 맞는 것 사이엔 엄청난 차이가 있는 법이거든. 그래서, 플라스크, 손으로 맞는 게 지팡이로 맞는 것보다 쉰 배는 더 사납게 느껴지는 거라네. 살아 있는 신체 — 그게 바로 살아 있는 모욕을 만드는 거야, 이 사람아. 그리고 명심하게, 내가 그 빌어먹을 피라미드에다 멍청한 발가락을 줄곧 짓찧고 있는 내내 속으로 이렇게 생각하고 있었단 말일세 — 그게 또 어찌나 앞뒤가 안 맞던지 — 어쨌든 내내 속으로 이런 생각을 하고 있었어. '저 영감 다리가 지금 지팡이지 뭐야 — 고래 뼈로 만든 지팡이 말이야. 그래,' 하고 나는 생각했지, '저건 그저 장난삼아 한 대 두들긴 것 — 사실 고래 뼈로 살짝 건드린 것뿐이지 — 비열한 발길질이 아니야. 게다가,' 하고 나는 생각했네, '한번 보라고. 그 끝부분 — 발끝 쪽 말이야 — 얼마나 자그마한 끝인가. 만약 발 넓적한 농부가 날 걷어찼다면, 그건 정말로 지독하게 넓은 모욕일 테지. 한데 이 모욕은 그저 한 점으로 깎여 내려간 거 아닌가.' 그런데 이제 그 꿈에서 제일 우스운 대목이 나오네, 플라스크. 내가 그 피라미드를 한창 두들겨 패고 있는데, 등에 혹이 달리고 오소리 털 같은 머리를 한 늙은 인어 사내 하나가 내 어깨를 붙잡더니 휙 돌려세우지 뭔가. '뭐 하는 거야?' 하고 그자가 묻더군. 이런! 이 사람아, 나는 깜짝 놀랐다네. 그 상판대기라니! 그런데 어찌 된 일인지 다음 순간엔 놀란 게 가시더라고. '내가 뭘 하느냐고?' 마침내 내가 말했지. '그게 당신하고 무슨 상관인지 좀 알았으면 좋겠는데, 꼽추 양반? 당신도 한 대 걷어차여 보고 싶나?' 맙소사, 플라스크, 내가 그 말을 내뱉기가 무섭게 그자가 등짝을 내 쪽으로 돌리더니 허리를 굽혀, 가리개 삼아 두르고 있던 해초 뭉치를 끌어 올리는 거야 — 그래서 내가 뭘 봤겠나? — 세상에 이런 벼락 맞을, 이 사람아, 그자 등짝에 밧줄 송곳이 끝을 바깥으로 향한 채 잔뜩 박혀 있지 뭔가. 나는 다시 생각해 보고 말했지, '아무래도 당신은 안 차는 게 낫겠소, 영감.' '현명한 스터브로군,' 하고 그자가 말하더니, '현명한 스터브야'를 줄곧 중얼거리는데, 마치 굴뚝 마녀가 제 잇몸을 우물거리듯 하더군. 그자가 '현명한 스터브, 현명한 스터브' 하는 소리를 멈출 기색이 없기에, 나는 다시 그 피라미드를 걷어차는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했네. 그런데 막 발을 들어 올린 참인데, 그자가 버럭 소리치는 거야, '그 발길질 멈춰!' '아니,' 하고 내가 말했지, '이번엔 또 뭐가 문제요, 영감?' '여기 좀 보게,' 하고 그자가 말하더군. '그 모욕에 대해 한번 따져 보세. 에이해브 선장이 자넬 걷어찼지, 안 그런가?' '그래, 그랬소,' 하고 내가 말했네 — '바로 여기를 걷어찼지.' '아주 좋아,' 하고 그자가 말했지 — '그가 상아 다리를 썼지, 안 그래?' '그래, 그랬소,' 하고 내가 말했네. '그렇다면,' 하고 그자가 말하더군, '현명한 스터브, 자네가 불평할 게 뭐가 있나? 그가 기꺼운 마음으로 걷어찬 거 아닌가? 그가 흔해 빠진 소나무 다리로 찬 게 아니잖아, 그렇지? 아니지, 자넨 위대한 사람한테, 그것도 아름다운 상아 다리로 걷어차인 거야, 스터브. 그건 영광일세. 나는 그걸 영광이라고 여기네. 들어 보게, 현명한 스터브. 옛 잉글랜드에서는 가장 위대한 영주들이 여왕한테 한 대 얻어맞아 가터 기사로 책봉되는 걸 큰 영예로 여긴다네. 한데 자네는, 스터브, 늙은 에이해브한테 걷어차여 현명한 사람이 되었노라고 자랑하게나. 내 말 명심하게. 그분한테 차이게나. 그분의 발길질을 영광으로 여기게. 그리고 절대로 되받아 차지 말게. 어차피 자네 힘으로는 어쩔 수 없으니, 현명한 스터브. 저 피라미드가 보이지 않나?' 그러더니 그자가 갑자기 어쩐 일인지 묘한 모양새로 허공으로 헤엄쳐 사라지는 거야. 나는 코를 골았지. 몸을 뒤척였지. 그러고 보니 내 해먹 안이지 뭔가! 자, 그 꿈을 어떻게 생각하나, 플라스크?\"\n\n\"모르겠네. 한데 나한테는 좀 어리석은 소리처럼 들리는걸.\"\n\n\"그럴지도, 그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 그 꿈이 날 현명한 사람으로 만들었다네, 플라스크. 저기 에이해브가 옆으로 서서 고물 너머를 바라보고 있는 거 보이나? 그래, 자네가 할 수 있는 최선은 말일세, 플라스크, 저 영감을 가만 내버려 두는 거야. 무슨 말을 하든 절대 말을 걸지 말게. 이런! 저 양반이 뭐라고 외치는 거지? 들어 보게!\"\n\n\"돛대 꼭대기, 거기! 다들 눈 똑바로 떠라! 이 근처에 고래가 있다!\n\n\"하얀 놈이 보이거든, 폐가 터지도록 소리쳐라!\"\n\n\"이번엔 그걸 어떻게 생각하나, 플라스크? 거기엔 뭔가 묘한 구석이 한 방울쯤 들어 있지 않나, 응? 하얀 고래라 — 자네도 그 말을 새겨들었나, 이 사람아? 잘 보게 — 바람결에 뭔가 심상찮은 게 실려 있어. 단단히 대비하게, 플라스크. 에이해브는 마음속에 뭔가 피비린내 나는 걸 품고 있어. 한데, 쉿. 이쪽으로 오고 있네.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 32. Cetology.\n\n세상 이미 우리는 대담하게도 깊은 바다 위로 나아갔다. 그러나 머지않아 우리는 그 기슭도 항구도 없는 광막함 속에서 길을 잃고 말 것이다. 그런 일이 닥치기 전에, 곧 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 해초 낀 선체가 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 따개비 달라붙은 거구와 나란히 뒹굴기 전에, 출항의 첫머리인 지금, 앞으로 이어질 온갖 종류의 더욱 특별한 고래 이야기와 암시들을 철저하고 깊이 있게 이해하는 데 거의 없어서는 안 될 한 가지 문제에 주의를 기울여 두는 것이 좋겠다.\n\n내가 지금 그대들 앞에 기꺼이 펼쳐 보이고자 하는 것은, 고래를 그 폭넓은 종속(種屬)에 따라 어떤 식으로든 체계적으로 정리해 보여 주는 일이다. 그러나 그것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니다. 여기서 시도하려는 것은 다름 아닌 혼돈을 이루는 구성 요소들의 분류 작업이다. 가장 뛰어나고 가장 최근의 권위자들이 무어라 정해 놓았는지 들어 보라.\n\n\"동물학의 어떤 분과도 고래학(Cetology)이라는 이름이 붙은 분야만큼 복잡한 것은 없다.\" 스코어스비 선장이 서기 1820년에 한 말이다.\n\n\"향유고래(Sperm)를 무리와 과(科)로 나누는 참된 방법에 관한 탐구에 들어가는 일은, 설령 내게 그럴 능력이 있다 하더라도 나의 의도가 아니다. * * * 이 동물(향유고래)을 다룬 사가(史家)들 사이에는 완전한 혼란이 존재한다.\" 외과의 빌이 서기 1839년에 한 말이다.\n\n\"헤아릴 수 없는 물속에서 우리의 연구를 밀고 나가기에는 부적합하다.\" \"고래에 관한 우리의 지식을 가린 뚫을 수 없는 장막.\" \"가시가 흩뿌려진 들판.\" \"이 모든 불완전한 단서들은 그저 우리 박물학자들을 괴롭히는 데 쓰일 뿐이다.\"\n\n고래에 관해 이렇게 말한 이들은 저 위대한 퀴비에와 존 헌터와 레송이니, 동물학과 해부학의 등불 같은 인물들이다. 그럼에도 불구하고, 참된 지식은 거의 없을지언정 책만큼은 차고 넘친다. 그러니 고래학, 곧 고래에 관한 학문도 어느 정도까지는 사정이 같다. 작든 크든, 옛사람이든 요즘 사람이든, 뭍사람이든 뱃사람이든, 길게든 짧게든 고래에 관해 글을 쓴 이는 한둘이 아니다. 그 몇몇을 훑어보자. 성경의 저자들, 아리스토텔레스, 플리니우스, 알드로반디, 토머스 브라운 경, 게스너, 레이, 린나이우스, 론델레티우스, 윌러비, 그린, 아르테디, 시발드, 브리송, 마르텐, 라세페드, 보네테르, 데마레, 퀴비에 남작, 프레데리크 퀴비에, 존 헌터, 오언, 스코어스비, 빌, 베넷, J. 로스 브라운, 『미리엄 코핀』의 저자, 옴스테드, 그리고 목사 T. 치버. 그러나 이들 모두가 결국 어떤 일반화의 목적을 두고 글을 썼는지는 위에 인용한 발췌문들이 잘 보여 줄 것이다.\n\n이 고래 저술가 명단에 오른 이름들 가운데, 오언 이후에 오는 자들만이 살아 있는 고래를 직접 본 적이 있으며, 그들 가운데 진짜 직업적인 작살잡이이자 고래잡이였던 이는 단 한 사람뿐이다. 스코어스비 선장을 두고 하는 말이다. 그린란드(Greenland)고래, 곧 참고래(Right Whale)라는 별개의 주제에 관해서라면 그는 현존하는 최고의 권위자다. 그러나 스코어스비는 저 위대한 향유고래에 관해서는 아무것도 알지 못했고 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 향유고래에 견주면 그린란드고래는 거의 입에 올릴 가치조차 없을 정도다. 그리고 여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 그린란드고래는 바다의 옥좌를 찬탈한 자다. 그놈은 결코 고래들 가운데 가장 큰 놈조차도 아니다. 그럼에도 불구하고, 그놈이 옛적부터 오래도록 그 자리를 주장해 온 까닭에, 또한 칠십 년쯤 전까지만 해도 그때까지 전설적이거나 전혀 알려지지 않았던 향유고래를 둘러싸고 있던 깊은 무지, 그리고 오늘날까지도 몇몇 과학적 은신처와 고래잡이 항구를 빼면 여전히 군림하고 있는 그 무지 탓에, 이 찬탈은 어느 모로 보나 완전하게 이루어졌다. 지나간 시대의 위대한 시인들이 남긴 거의 모든 고래 관련 언급을 살펴보면, 그들에게는 그린란드고래가 단 하나의 경쟁자도 없는 바다의 군주였음을 그대들도 수긍하게 될 것이다. 그러나 마침내 새로운 포고를 내릴 때가 왔다. 여기는 채링 크로스다. 모든 백성이여, 들으라! 그린란드고래는 폐위되었노라. 이제는 저 위대한 향유고래가 군림하노라!\n\n살아 있는 향유고래를 그대들 앞에 펼쳐 보이겠노라고 조금이라도 자처하면서, 동시에 그 시도에 아주 미미한 정도나마 성공을 거둔 책은 세상에 단 두 권뿐이다. 그 두 책은 빌의 책과 베넷의 책이다. 둘 다 한 시절 영국 남양(南洋) 포경선의 외과의였으며, 둘 다 정확하고 믿을 만한 사람들이다. 그들의 책에서 찾아볼 수 있는, 향유고래를 다룬 독창적인 내용은 필연적으로 적을 수밖에 없다. 그러나 그 분량이 닿는 데까지는 그 질이 빼어나니, 비록 대부분이 과학적 서술에 한정되어 있긴 하다. 그러나 아직까지도 향유고래는, 과학적으로든 시적으로든, 그 어떤 문헌 속에서도 온전하게 살아 있지 못하다. 사냥당하는 다른 모든 고래들을 훨씬 능가하는 이 고래의 생애는, 아직 쓰이지 않은 생애인 것이다.\n\n자, 그렇다면 고래의 여러 종(種)은 어떤 식으로든 일반인이 두루 이해할 만한 분류가 필요하다. 비록 지금으로서는 손쉬운 개요에 지나지 않을지라도, 나중에 뒤따르는 일꾼들이 그 모든 갈래를 빠짐없이 채워 넣을 수 있도록 하기 위함이다. 나보다 나은 사람이 나서서 이 일을 떠맡지 않으니, 나는 이에 나 자신의 보잘것없는 노력을 바치고자 한다. 나는 무엇 하나 완전하리라고 약속하지 않는다. 무릇 완전하다고 여겨지는 인간의 모든 것은, 바로 그 이유 때문에 어김없이 결함을 지니게 마련인 까닭이다. 나는 여러 종에 대한 세밀한 해부학적 서술을 꾀하지도 않을 것이며, 적어도 이 자리에서만큼은 그 어떤 자세한 서술도 그리 많이 늘어놓지 않을 것이다. 여기서 내 목적은 그저 고래학을 체계화하는 설계도의 초안을 그려 보이는 데 있다. 나는 건축가이지 시공자가 아니다.\n\n그러나 이는 묵직한 과업이다. 우체국의 어떤 평범한 우편물 분류원도 이 일을 감당하지는 못한다. 그놈들을 찾아 바다 밑바닥까지 더듬어 내려가는 일, 이 세상의 형언할 수 없는 토대와 갈빗대와 바로 그 골반에 손을 대는 일, 이것은 두려운 일이다. 도대체 내가 무엇이라고 이 리바이어던의 코에 갈고리를 꿰려 든단 말인가! 욥기에 나오는 저 무시무시한 조롱이 능히 나를 질리게 할 만하다. 그(리바이어던)가 너와 언약을 맺겠느냐? 보라, 그를 잡으려는 희망은 헛되도다! 그러나 나는 도서관들을 헤엄쳐 건넜고 대양들을 항해해 건넜다. 나는 눈에 보이는 이 두 손으로 고래들을 직접 다루어 왔다. 나는 진지하다. 그러니 한번 해 보겠다. 먼저 정리해 두어야 할 몇 가지 예비 사항이 있다.\n\n첫째, 이 고래학이라는 학문이 얼마나 불확실하고 정립되지 못한 상태에 있는지는, 어떤 부류에서는 고래가 과연 물고기인가 하는 것이 아직도 논란거리로 남아 있다는 사실만으로도 그 입구에서부터 입증된다. 린나이우스는 서기 1776년에 펴낸 『자연의 체계』에서 \"나는 이로써 고래를 물고기로부터 분리하노라\"라고 선언했다. 그러나 내가 직접 아는 바로는, 1850년에 이르기까지도 상어와 청어 무리, 그리고 앨와이프와 청어가 린나이우스의 분명한 칙령을 거슬러 여전히 리바이어던과 같은 바다를 나누어 차지하고 있는 것이 발견되었다.\n\n린나이우스가 고래를 물속에서 기어이 추방하고자 했던 근거를 그는 다음과 같이 진술한다. \"그것들이 따뜻하고 두 칸으로 나뉜 심장을 지녔고, 폐를 지녔으며, 움직이는 눈꺼풀과 속이 빈 귀를 지녔고, 페넴 인트란템 페미남 맘미스 락탄템(암컷에게 삽입하는 음경과 젖으로 새끼를 먹임)인 까닭에서다.\" 그리고 끝으로 \"엑스 레게 나투라이 유레 메리토케(자연의 법에 따라 정당하고도 마땅하게)\"라고 했다. 나는 이 모든 것을 내 친구인 낸터킷(Nantucket) 출신의 시므온 메이시와 찰리 코핀에게 내놓았는데, 두 사람 다 어느 항해에서 나와 한솥밥을 먹던 동료였다. 그들은 한목소리로 거기에 제시된 이유들이 도무지 충분치 않다는 의견을 내놓았다. 찰리는 불경스럽게도 그것들이 다 헛소리라고 넌지시 비꼬았다.\n\n분명히 밝혀 두지만, 나는 온갖 논쟁을 다 제쳐 두고서, 고래는 물고기라는 저 정겨운 옛 방식의 입장을 취하며, 거룩한 요나(Jonah)를 불러내어 나를 거들게 하겠다. 이 근본적인 문제가 정해졌으니, 다음으로 따져 볼 점은 고래가 내적으로 어떤 면에서 다른 물고기들과 다른가 하는 것이다. 앞서 린나이우스가 그 항목들을 그대들에게 일러 주었다. 그러나 간략히 말하자면 그것은 이러하다. 폐와 따뜻한 피다. 반면 다른 모든 물고기는 폐가 없고 차가운 피를 지녔다.\n\n다음으로, 우리는 고래를 그 한눈에 드러나는 겉모습으로 어떻게 정의하여, 앞으로 영원토록 또렷이 그 이름표를 붙여 줄 것인가? 그렇다면 짧게 말해, 고래란 수평으로 누운 꼬리를 지니고 물을 뿜는 물고기다. 자, 이것이 고래다. 아무리 압축된 것이라 한들, 그 정의는 한껏 부풀려진 사색의 결실이다. 바다코끼리도 고래와 꽤 비슷하게 물을 뿜지만, 바다코끼리는 물고기가 아니니 양서(兩棲)하는 까닭이다. 그러나 정의의 마지막 항목은 첫 항목과 짝을 이룰 때 한층 더 설득력을 지닌다. 뭍사람들에게 익숙한 모든 물고기가 납작한 꼬리가 아니라 수직으로 선, 곧 위아래로 놓인 꼬리를 지녔다는 것은 거의 누구나 눈여겨보았을 것이다. 반면 물을 뿜는 물고기들 사이에서는, 그 꼬리가 설령 비슷한 모양일지라도 어김없이 수평으로 놓인 자세를 취한다.\n\n고래가 무엇인지에 관한 위의 정의로써, 나는 지금껏 가장 정통한 낸터킷 사람들이 고래와 동일하게 여겨 온 그 어떤 바다 생물도 결코 리바이어던 형제단에서 배제하지 않으며, 또한 반대로 지금껏 권위 있게 이방의 것으로 간주되어 온 그 어떤 물고기도 거기에 결부시키지 않는다.* 그러므로 물을 뿜고 수평으로 누운 꼬리를 지닌 더 작은 물고기들도 모두 이 고래학의 기본 설계도 안에 포함되어야 한다. 자, 그렇다면 이제 고래 무리 전체의 큰 갈래로 들어가 보자.\n\n* 나는 오늘날에 이르기까지도 라마틴과 듀공(낸터킷 코핀 집안 사람들이 부르는 돼지물고기와 암퇘지물고기)이라 불리는 물고기들이 많은 박물학자들에 의해 고래에 포함되고 있음을 알고 있다. 그러나 이 돼지물고기들은 시끄럽고 하찮은 무리로, 대개 강어귀에 숨어 젖은 건초를 뜯어 먹으며, 무엇보다도 물을 뿜지 않는 까닭에, 나는 그것들이 고래라는 자격을 인정하지 않으며, 그것들에게 고래학의 왕국을 떠나라는 통행증을 발급하였다.\n\n첫째, 나는 크기에 따라 고래를 세 권의 으뜸가는 책(다시 장으로 나뉠 수 있다)으로 나누는데, 이 책들이 작은 것이든 큰 것이든 그 모두를 아우르게 될 것이다.\n\nI. 2절판(folio) 고래. II. 8절판(octavo) 고래. III. 12절판(duodecimo) 고래.\n\n2절판의 대표로 나는 향유고래를 내놓고, 8절판의 대표로는 그램퍼스를, 12절판의 대표로는 돌고래를 내놓는다.\n\n2절판. 이 항목 아래 나는 다음과 같은 장들을 포함시킨다. I. 향유고래. II. 참고래. III. 긴수염고래. IV. 혹등고래. V. 면도날등고래. VI. 흰배고래.\n\n제1책(2절판), 제1장(향유고래). 이 고래는 옛 영국인들 사이에서 트럼파 고래, 피세터 고래, 모루머리 고래라고 막연히 알려졌던 것으로, 오늘날 프랑스인들이 부르는 카샬로(Cachalot), 독일인들의 포츠피슈(Pottsfich), 그리고 장황한 학명을 좋아하는 이들의 마크로케팔루스(Macrocephalus)와 같은 것이다. 그것은 의심할 바 없이 지구상에서 가장 거대한 거주자이며, 모든 고래 가운데 맞닥뜨리기에 가장 무시무시하고, 그 모습이 가장 위풍당당하며, 끝으로 상업적으로도 단연 가장 값진 존재이니, 향유(spermaceti)라는 저 귀한 물질을 얻을 수 있는 유일한 피조물이기 때문이다. 그 모든 특이점들은 다른 여러 대목에서 자세히 다루어질 것이다. 지금 내가 다루어야 할 것은 주로 그 이름이다. 어원학적으로 따져 보면 그것은 터무니없는 이름이다. 몇 세기 전, 향유고래가 그 본래의 개별적 실체로는 거의 전혀 알려지지 않았고, 그 기름이 오로지 뭍에 떠밀려 온 물고기에서 우연히 얻어지던 시절에는, 향유라는 것이 당시 영국에서 그린란드 고래 혹은 참고래라 불리던 것과 동일한 피조물에서 나온다고 흔히들 여겼던 모양이다. 또한 이 향유가 그 단어의 첫 음절이 글자 그대로 나타내는 바, 곧 그린란드 고래의 생명을 북돋우는 정액이라는 생각도 있었다. 그 시절에는 향유가 몹시 귀해서 등불용으로 쓰이지 않고 오로지 연고나 약재로만 쓰였다. 오늘날 대황 한 온스를 사듯이, 그것은 오로지 약제상에게서만 구할 수 있었다. 내 짐작으로는, 세월이 흐르며 향유의 참된 본성이 밝혀진 뒤에도 상인들은 그 본래의 이름을 그대로 간직했으니, 이는 분명 그 희소성을 그토록 묘하게 암시하는 이름을 통해 값어치를 한층 높이려는 속셈이었을 것이다. 그리하여 마침내 그 명칭은 이 향유가 실제로 나오는 고래에게 붙여지기에 이르렀을 것이다.\n\n제1책 (이절판), 제2장 (참고래). 한 가지 점에서 이것은 리바이어던 가운데 가장 유서 깊은 종류이니, 인간이 처음으로 정식 사냥을 시작한 대상이기 때문이다. 이놈에게서는 흔히 고래수염 혹은 발린(baleen)이라 불리는 물건이 나오며, 또한 특별히 \"고래 기름\"이라 알려진, 상업적으로는 질이 떨어지는 기름이 나온다. 어부들 사이에서 이놈은 다음과 같은 온갖 이름으로 두루 불린다. 고래, 그린란드고래, 검은고래, 큰고래, 참고래(True Whale), 참고래(Right Whale). 이처럼 무수한 이름으로 세례를 받은 이 종(種)의 정체에 관해서는 모호함이 많다. 그렇다면 내가 이절판의 두 번째 종으로 포함시킨 이 고래는 대체 무엇인가? 그것은 영국 박물학자들이 말하는 큰미스티케투스(Great Mysticetus)요, 영국 고래잡이들이 말하는 그린란드고래요, 프랑스 고래잡이들이 말하는 발렌 오르디네르(Baleine Ordinaire)요, 스웨덴 사람들이 말하는 그뢴란드발피슈(Growlands Walfish)다. 그것은 지난 두 세기가 넘도록 네덜란드인과 영국인이 북극해에서 사냥해 온 고래요, 미국 어부들이 인도양에서, 브라질 어장에서, 북서 해안에서, 그리고 세계의 여러 다른 곳에서 오랫동안 뒤쫓아 온 고래로서, 그들은 그곳들을 참고래 조업 어장이라 부른다.\n\n어떤 이들은 영국인의 그린란드고래와 미국인의 참고래 사이에 차이가 있다고 우긴다. 그러나 그 둘은 모든 중요한 특징에서 정확히 일치하며, 근본적인 구별의 근거가 될 만한 단 하나의 확정적 사실조차 아직 제시된 바 없다. 박물학의 어떤 분야들이 그토록 진저리 나게 복잡해지는 것은, 가장 결론 없는 차이에 기댄 끝없는 세분화 때문이다. 참고래는 다른 곳에서 향유고래를 해설하는 데 견주어 가며 어느 정도 길게 다루어질 것이다.\n\n제1책. (이절판), 제3장. (긴수염고래).—이 항목 아래에 나는 한 마리 괴물을 분류해 넣으니, 긴수염고래, 높은물줄기고래, 긴존이라는 여러 이름으로 거의 모든 바다에서 목격되어 왔으며, 뉴욕 정기선 항로를 따라 대서양을 건너는 승객들이 그 멀리서 솟는 물줄기를 흔히 발견하게 되는 바로 그 고래다. 도달하는 몸길이와 고래수염에 있어서 긴수염고래는 참고래를 닮았으나, 둘레는 그만큼 우람하지 않고, 빛깔은 더 옅어 올리브색에 가깝다. 그 거대한 입술은, 큼직한 주름들이 비스듬히 겹쳐 서로 얽히면서 빚어내는 밧줄 같은 모양을 띤다. 그 이름의 유래가 된 위대한 식별 특징인 지느러미는 흔히 눈에 잘 띄는 대상이다. 이 지느러미는 길이가 서너 자쯤 되며, 등의 뒤쪽에서 수직으로 솟아 모난 형태를 이루고, 끝이 매우 날카롭게 뾰족하다. 설령 이 짐승의 다른 부분이 조금도 보이지 않을 때라도, 이 외따로 떨어진 지느러미만은 이따금 수면 위로 또렷이 솟아오른 것이 목격되곤 한다. 바다가 어지간히 잔잔하고 둥근 잔물결이 잘게 일렁이는 가운데 이 해시계 바늘 같은 지느러미가 곧추서서 주름진 수면 위에 그림자를 드리울 때면, 그것을 에워싼 물의 둥근 테두리가 마치 바늘과 물결무늬 시각선이 새겨진 해시계와 얼마간 닮았다고 여길 만도 하다. 그 아하스의 해시계 위에서 그림자는 흔히 뒤로 물러난다. 긴수염고래는 무리를 짓지 않는다. 어떤 사람들이 인간 혐오자이듯, 이놈은 고래 혐오자처럼 보인다. 몹시 수줍어서 늘 홀로 다니며, 가장 멀고 가장 음울한 바다에서 느닷없이 수면 위로 떠오른다. 그 곧고 외줄기로 높이 솟는 물줄기는 황량한 들판 위에 우뚝 선 키 큰 염세가의 창처럼 치솟고, 헤엄에 있어 어찌나 놀라운 힘과 속도를 타고났는지 인간의 그 어떤 추격도 당장에 비웃어 버린다. 이 리바이어던은 제 종족 가운데 추방된, 결코 정복되지 않는 카인처럼 보이며, 그 표징으로 등에 저 바늘을 지고 다닌다. 입속에 고래수염을 지닌 까닭에, 긴수염고래는 때로 참고래와 더불어 고래수염고래라 불리는 이론상의 종(種), 곧 고래수염을 지닌 고래들 무리에 포함된다. 이른바 고래수염고래라 일컫는 이것들에는 여러 변종이 있는 듯하나, 그 대부분은 거의 알려져 있지 않다. 넓적코고래와 부리고래, 창머리고래, 혹고래, 아래턱고래와 주둥이고래 따위가 몇몇 종류에 대한 어부들의 이름이다.\n\n이 “고래수염고래”라는 호칭과 관련하여 매우 중요하게 언급해 둘 것이 있으니, 그러한 명명법이 어떤 종류의 고래들을 가리키는 일을 수월하게 하는 데에는 편리할지 몰라도, 그 고래수염이든 혹이든 지느러미든 이빨이든 그 어느 것을 근거로 삼아 리바이어던을 명확히 분류하려는 시도는 헛되다는 점이다. 그런 두드러진 부위나 특징들이, 고래가 그 여러 종류에 걸쳐 드러내는 다른 어떤 떨어진 신체적 구별점들보다도 정연한 고래학 체계의 기초를 제공하기에 더 알맞아 보이는 것이 너무도 명백한데도 그러하다. 그러면 어찌 된 일인가? 고래수염, 혹, 등지느러미, 이빨—이런 것들은 그 구조가 다른 더 본질적인 세부에서 어떤 성질을 띠는지와는 아랑곳없이, 그 특이성이 온갖 종류의 고래들 사이에 마구잡이로 흩어져 있는 것들이다. 그리하여 향유고래와 혹등고래는 저마다 혹을 지녔으되, 닮음은 거기서 그친다. 그런가 하면 이 같은 혹등고래와 그린란드고래는 저마다 고래수염을 지녔으되, 또다시 닮음은 거기서 그친다. 그리고 앞서 말한 다른 부위들도 사정은 매한가지다. 여러 종류의 고래들에서 그것들은 그토록 불규칙한 조합을 이루거나, 혹은 그중 어느 하나만 떼어 놓고 보면 그토록 불규칙하게 고립되어 있어서, 그러한 기초 위에 세운 그 어떤 일반적 체계화도 완전히 무색하게 만든다. 고래 박물학자라면 누구나 이 바위에 부딪쳐 좌초하고 말았다.\n\n그러나 어쩌면 고래의 내부, 곧 그 해부 구조 안에서라면—적어도 거기서는 올바른 분류에 다다를 수 있으리라 생각할 수도 있다. 천만에. 이를테면 그린란드고래의 해부 구조에서 그 고래수염보다 더 두드러진 것이 무엇이 있겠는가? 그런데도 우리는 그 고래수염으로는 그린란드고래를 정확히 분류하기가 불가능하다는 것을 이미 보았다. 또한 여러 리바이어던의 내장 속으로 내려가 본들, 거기서는 이미 열거한 저 외부의 특징들에 견주어 오십분의 일만큼도 분류학자에게 쓸모 있는 구별점을 찾아내지 못할 것이다. 그러면 무엇이 남는가? 고래들을 통째로, 그 너그러운 부피 전체로 움켜쥐고서 대담하게 그런 식으로 분류하는 길밖에 없다. 그리고 이것이야말로 여기서 채택한 서지학적 분류 체계이며, 성공할 가망이 있는 유일한 방법이니, 오직 그것만이 실행 가능하기 때문이다. 그럼 계속하자.\n\n제1권. (이절판) 제4장. (혹등고래.)―이 고래는 북아메리카 연안에서 자주 눈에 띈다. 그곳에서 빈번히 잡혀 항구로 끌려오곤 했다. 등에는 행상인처럼 커다란 짐 보따리를 짊어진 듯한 혹이 있어, 코끼리와 성(城) 고래라 불러도 무방할 것이다. 어쨌거나 이 통속적인 이름만으로는 그를 충분히 구별해 내지 못하는데, 향유고래(Sperm Whale) 역시 비록 더 작긴 하나 혹을 지니고 있기 때문이다. 그 기름은 그다지 값나가지 않는다. 고래수염을 지녔다. 모든 고래 가운데 가장 장난기 많고 쾌활한 녀석으로, 대체로 다른 어떤 고래보다도 더 흥겨운 거품과 새하얀 물보라를 일으킨다.\n\n제1책(이절판), 제5장(레이저 백).—이 고래에 관해서는 그 이름 말고는 알려진 바가 거의 없다. 나는 케이프 혼 앞바다에서 멀찍이 그를 본 적이 있다. 천성이 은둔을 즐기는지라, 그는 사냥꾼도 철학자도 모두 따돌린다. 비겁자는 아니지만, 그는 아직껏 길고 날카로운 등마루처럼 솟아오르는 등 말고는 제 몸의 어느 한 부분도 드러내 보인 적이 없다. 가게 내버려 두자. 나도 그에 관해서는 그 이상 아는 바가 없으며, 다른 누구도 마찬가지다.\n제1책(이절판), 제6장(설퍼 보텀).—또 한 명의 은둔자다. 유황빛 배를 지녔는데, 이는 필시 더 깊은 잠수를 하던 어느 때에 저 지옥의 바닥 타일을 긁으며 다닌 탓에 얻은 것이리라. 그는 좀처럼 모습을 보이지 않는다. 적어도 나는 저 멀고 외진 남쪽 바다에서가 아니고는 그를 본 적이 없으며, 그나마도 늘 그 얼굴을 살피기에는 너무 먼 거리에서였다. 그는 결코 추격당하지 않는다. 추격하다가는 밧줄 공장 하나 분량의 작살 줄을 통째로 끌고 달아나 버릴 테니. 그에 관해서는 온갖 기이한 이야기가 전해진다. 잘 가게, 설퍼 보텀이여! 그대에 관해 더는 참된 것을 한마디도 할 수 없으니, 가장 나이 든 낸터킷 사람이라 한들 마찬가지다.\n\n이로써 제1책(이절판)이 끝나고, 이제 제2책(팔절판)이 시작된다.\n\n팔절판류.*—여기에는 중간 크기의 고래들이 속하는데, 현재로서는 다음과 같은 것들을 꼽을 수 있다.—제1, 그램퍼스. 제2, 블랙 피시. 제3, 일각고래. 제4, 스래셔. 제5, 킬러.\n\n*고래에 관한 이 책을 어째서 사절판이라 부르지 않는지는 매우 분명하다. 이 부류의 고래들이 비록 앞 부류의 고래들보다 작기는 하지만 그래도 그 형태에서 비례적인 닮음을 지니고 있는데, 제본업자의 사절판 책은 그 규격화된 형태에서 이절판 책의 모양을 보존하지 못하는 반면, 팔절판 책은 그것을 보존하기 때문이다.\n\n제2서. (8절판), 제1장 (그램퍼스). — 그 우렁차고 낭랑한 숨결, 아니 차라리 물뿜기로써 뭍사람들에게 속담 하나를 마련해 준 이 물고기는, 심해에 사는 친숙한 주민으로 잘 알려져 있으면서도 흔히 고래의 무리에 끼이지는 않는다. 그러나 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 위대한 특징들을 모두 갖추고 있는 까닭에, 대부분의 박물학자들은 이놈을 고래의 일종으로 인정해 왔다. 이놈은 적당한 8절판 크기로, 길이는 15피트(약 4.6미터)에서 25피트(약 7.6미터)까지 다양하며, 허리둘레도 그에 걸맞은 치수를 지닌다. 떼를 지어 헤엄치고, 비록 그 기름이 양으로 보아 제법 상당하고 등불용으로도 꽤 쓸 만하지만 정식으로 사냥당하는 일은 결코 없다. 어떤 어부들은 이놈이 다가오는 것을 보고 거대한 향유고래(Sperm Whale)가 곧 나타날 전조로 여기기도 한다.\n\n제2서 (8절판), 제2장 (흑어).—나는 이 모든 물고기에 대해 어부들이 흔히 쓰는 이름을 그대로 옮긴다. 대체로 그것이 가장 나은 이름이기 때문이다. 어떤 이름이 모호하거나 그 특징을 제대로 드러내지 못할 경우에는 그렇다고 말하고 다른 이름을 제안하겠다. 흔히 흑어(黑魚)라 불리는 이 물고기를 두고 지금 바로 그렇게 하려 한다. 검은빛이라는 것은 거의 모든 고래에게 공통된 특징이기 때문이다. 그러니 원한다면 이놈을 하이에나고래라 부르기로 하자. 그 게걸스러움은 익히 알려져 있고, 입술의 안쪽 끝이 위로 휘어 올라간 까닭에 그 얼굴에는 영원히 메피스토펠레스 같은 능글맞은 웃음이 걸려 있다. 이 고래는 평균 16피트에서 18피트(약 5미터에서 5.5미터) 정도의 길이다. 거의 모든 위도에서 발견된다. 헤엄칠 때 등의 갈고리 모양 지느러미를 드러내는 독특한 방식이 있는데, 그 모습이 어쩐지 로마식 매부리코를 닮았다. 더 수지맞는 일에 매여 있지 않을 때면, 향유고래 사냥꾼들은 가끔 이 하이에나고래를 잡아 가정용으로 쓸 값싼 기름의 공급을 이어 가곤 한다 — 마치 알뜰한 살림꾼이 손님도 없이 혼자 지낼 때면 향기로운 밀랍 대신 냄새 고약한 짐승 기름을 태우는 것과 마찬가지다. 비록 이 고래들의 지방층은 매우 얇지만, 그래도 그중 어떤 놈은 30갤런(약 114리터)이 넘는 기름을 내주기도 할 것이다.\n\n제2권(8절판), 제3장(외뿔고래), 곧 콧구멍 고래다. 기묘하게 이름 붙은 고래의 또 하나의 사례로, 짐작건대 그 독특한 뿔이 본래 뾰족한 코로 잘못 여겨졌던 데서 그런 이름이 붙은 듯하다. 이 동물은 길이가 약 16피트(약 4.9미터)인데, 그 뿔은 평균 5피트(약 1.5미터)에 이르며, 더러는 10피트(약 3미터)를 넘기고, 심지어 15피트(약 4.6미터)에까지 닿는 것도 있다. 엄밀히 말하면 이 뿔은 길게 자라난 엄니에 지나지 않으니, 수평에서 약간 아래로 기운 선을 따라 턱에서 뻗어 나온다. 그런데 그것은 왼쪽에만 나 있어 보기에 좋지 않은 인상을 주는데, 그 주인을 어딘지 서툰 왼손잡이 사내와 비슷한 모습으로 만들어 버린다. 이 상아 뿔, 곧 창이 정확히 어떤 쓸모를 지니는지는 말하기 어렵다. 그것은 황새치나 부리고래의 칼날처럼 쓰이는 것 같지는 않다. 그래도 어떤 선원들은 외뿔고래가 그것을 갈퀴 삼아 바다 밑바닥을 헤집어 먹이를 찾는 데 쓴다고 내게 말해 준다. 찰리 코핀(Charley Coffin)은 그것이 얼음을 뚫는 데 쓰인다고 말했다. 외뿔고래가 북극해의 수면으로 떠오르다 그곳이 온통 얼음으로 덮여 있는 것을 보면, 제 뿔을 위로 치밀어 그렇게 얼음을 깨고 나온다는 것이다. 그러나 이 추측들 중 어느 하나도 옳다고 증명할 수는 없다. 내 개인적인 견해로는, 외뿔고래가 이 한쪽으로만 난 뿔을 실제로 어떻게 쓰든 간에 — 그것이 어떻든 — 소책자를 읽을 때 책장을 누르는 종이칼로는 분명 무척 요긴할 것이라는 점이다.\n외뿔고래는 엄니고래, 뿔고래, 일각수고래라고 불리는 것을 나는 들어 왔다. 그것은 살아 있는 자연의 거의 모든 왕국에서 찾아볼 수 있는 일각수성(一角獸性)의 기묘한 한 본보기임에 틀림없다. 어느 수도원에 틀어박혔던 옛 저자들로부터 나는, 이 바다 일각수의 뿔이 옛날에는 독에 대항하는 위대한 해독제로 여겨졌으며, 그런 까닭에 그것으로 만든 조제약이 어마어마한 값에 팔렸다는 사실을 알게 되었다. 그것은 또 정신을 잃은 귀부인들을 위한 휘발성 소금으로 증류되기도 했으니, 수사슴의 뿔을 녹용정(鹿茸精)으로 만들어 내는 것과 같은 방식이었다. 본래 그것은 그 자체로 커다란 호기심의 대상으로 여겨졌다. 흑체활자본(黑體活字本)이 내게 전하는 바로는, 마틴 프로비셔 경이 그 항해에서 돌아왔을 때 — 그의 대담한 배가 템스강을 따라 내려갈 적에 엘리자베스 여왕이 그리니치 궁전의 창가에서 보석 박힌 손을 늠름하게 흔들어 보였다는 그 항해에서 돌아왔을 때 — \"마틴 경이 그 항해에서 돌아왔을 때,\" 흑체활자본은 이르기를,\n\n무릎을 꿇은 채 \"그는 외뿔고래의 어마어마하게 긴 뿔 하나를 폐하께 바쳤는데, 그것은 그 뒤로도 오랫동안 윈저 성에 걸려 있었다.\" 어느 아일랜드 작가는 레스터 백작이 무릎을 꿇은 채, 외뿔 짐승의 부류에 속하는 또 다른 뿔, 곧 육지 짐승의 뿔을 폐하께 마찬가지로 바쳤다고 주장한다.\n\n외뿔고래는 매우 그림 같은, 표범을 닮은 모습을 하고 있는데, 우윳빛 바탕에 검은 둥근 점과 길쭉한 점들이 흩뿌려져 있기 때문이다. 그 기름은 더없이 우수하여 맑고 고우나, 그 양이 적고, 좀처럼 사냥당하지 않는다. 이놈은 대개 극지방을 둘러싼 바다에서 발견된다.\n\n제2부(8절판), 제4장(킬러). 이 고래에 관해서는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람조차 정확히 아는 바가 거의 없고, 자칭 박물학자에 이르러서는 그야말로 아무것도 알지 못한다. 멀리서 본 바에 따르면, 나로서는 그 크기가 큰돌고래만 하다고 말할 수 있겠다. 이놈은 매우 사나운데, 일종의 피지(Feegee)산 물고기라 할 만하다. 이놈은 이따금 거대한 2절판 고래의 입술을 물고는, 거머리처럼 거기 매달려 있다가, 그 힘센 짐승이 시달리다 못해 죽음에 이르게 만든다. 킬러는 결코 사냥의 대상이 되지 않는다. 이놈이 어떤 종류의 기름을 지녔는지 나는 들어 본 적이 없다.\n이 고래에 붙은 이름에 대해서는, 그 모호함을 이유로 이의를 제기할 만하다. 왜냐하면 우리 모두가, 뭍에서든 바다에서든 살인자들이기 때문이다. 보나파르트도 상어도 거기에 포함된다.\n\n제2서(8절판), 제5장(타작고래). — 이 신사가 이름을 떨치는 것은 그 꼬리 덕분인데, 그는 그것을 적을 후려치는 회초리로 쓴다. 그는 2절판 고래의 등에 올라타, 헤엄을 치면서 상대를 채찍질하여 제 갈 길을 헤쳐 나간다. 마치 어떤 학교 선생들이 비슷한 방식으로 세상을 살아가는 것처럼 말이다. 타작고래에 관해서는 범고래보다도 더 알려진 바가 적다. 둘 다 무법자이니, 무법천지인 바다에서조차 그러하다.\n\n이로써 제2서(8절판)가 끝나고, 제3서(12절판)가 시작된다.\n\n12절판. — 여기에는 비교적 작은 고래들이 포함된다. 첫째, 만세돌고래. 둘째, 알제리돌고래. 셋째, 입조심돌고래.\n\n이 주제를 특별히 연구해 본 적이 없는 이들에게는, 흔히 길이가 네다섯 자를 넘지 않는 물고기들이 고래들 사이에 줄지어 놓이는 것이 어쩌면 이상해 보일지도 모른다. 고래라는 말은 통속적인 의미에서 언제나 거대함의 관념을 전하기 때문이다. 그러나 위에서 12절판으로 분류해 둔 이 생물들은, 고래란 무엇인가에 대한 나의 정의에 따르면 틀림없는 고래다. 곧, 수평으로 누운 꼬리를 지니고 물을 뿜는 물고기인 것이다.\n\n제3권. (12절판), 제1장. (만세돌고래).—이것은 거의 전 세계에 걸쳐 발견되는 흔한 돌고래다. 이 이름은 내가 직접 붙인 것인데, 돌고래에도 여러 종류가 있어 그것들을 구별할 무언가가 필요하기 때문이다. 내가 이놈을 이렇게 부르는 까닭은, 이놈이 언제나 흥에 겨운 무리를 지어 헤엄치며, 드넓은 바다 위에서 독립기념일 군중 속의 모자들처럼 하늘을 향해 제 몸을 끊임없이 솟구쳐 올리기 때문이다. 이놈들의 모습은 대체로 뱃사람들에게 기쁨으로 환영받는다. 활기가 넘치는 이놈들은 어김없이 바람 부는 쪽의 산들거리는 물결에서 다가온다. 이놈들은 늘 바람 앞에 사는 녀석들이다. 행운의 징조로 여겨지기도 한다. 만약 당신이 이 생기발랄한 물고기를 보고도 만세 삼창을 참아 낼 수 있다면, 부디 하늘이 당신을 도우시기를. 당신에게는 경건한 유희의 정신이 깃들어 있지 않은 것이다. 잘 먹어 토실토실한 만세돌고래 한 마리는 질 좋은 기름 한 갤런을 내준다. 그러나 그 턱에서 추출되는 곱고 섬세한 액체는 더없이 귀하다. 보석 세공인과 시계공 사이에서 수요가 있다. 선원들은 그것을 숫돌에 바른다. 돌고래 고기는 먹을 만하다고들 한다. 돌고래가 물을 뿜는다는 사실은 어쩌면 당신이 미처 생각해 본 적 없을지도 모른다. 사실 이놈의 물줄기는 너무 작아서 좀처럼 쉽게 알아보기 어렵다. 그러나 다음에 기회가 닿거든 이놈을 잘 지켜보라. 그러면 저 위대한 향유고래(Sperm whale)를 그대로 축소해 놓은 모습을 보게 될 것이다.\n\n매우 사납다. 내 생각에 이놈은 오로지 태평양에서만 발견된다. 환호 돌고래보다 다소 크지만, 전체적인 생김새는 거의 같다. 이놈을 화나게 하면 상어에게도 덤벼들 것이다. 나는 이놈을 잡으려고 여러 번 보트를 내렸지만, 아직껏 한 번도 포획되는 것을 본 적이 없다.\n제3권(12절판) 제3장(가루입 돌고래). 돌고래 중에서도 가장 큰 종류이며, 알려진 한에서는 오로지 태평양에서만 발견된다. 지금까지 이놈에게 붙여진 유일한 영어 이름은 어부들이 부르는 명칭, 곧 참고래 돌고래인데, 이는 이놈이 주로 그 2절판 고래의 근처에서 발견되기 때문이다. 생김새로 보면 환호 돌고래와는 어느 정도 다른데, 덜 둥글고 덜 풍채 좋은 몸통을 지녔다. 실로 이놈은 제법 단정하고 신사다운 자태를 갖추었다. 등에는 지느러미가 없고(다른 대부분의 돌고래에게는 있다), 사랑스러운 꼬리와 개암빛 도는 감상적인 인디언의 눈을 지녔다. 그러나 그 가루입이 모든 것을 망쳐 놓는다. 이놈의 등 전체는 옆구리 지느러미까지 짙은 흑색이지만, 배의 선체에 그어진 \"밝은 허리\"라 불리는 표시처럼 또렷한 경계선이 이놈을 뱃머리에서 고물까지 두 빛깔로 가르니, 위는 검고 아래는 희다. 흰 부분은 머리의 일부와 입 전체를 차지하는데, 그 때문에 이놈은 마치 밀가루 자루에 흉악하게 들이닥쳤다가 막 빠져나온 것처럼 보인다. 더없이 비루하고 가루투성이인 몰골이다! 이놈의 기름은 보통 돌고래의 것과 거의 같다.\n\n12절판을 넘어서면 이 분류 체계는 더 나아가지 않으니, 돌고래야말로 고래 가운데 가장 작은 것이기 때문이다. 앞에서 그대들은 이름난 리바이어던들을 모두 보았다. 그러나 그 밖에도 불확실하고, 종잡을 수 없으며, 반쯤은 전설에 가까운 고래들의 무리가 있는데, 미국 고래잡이로서 나는 이것들을 소문으로는 알지만 직접 본 적은 없다. 나는 이놈들을 선원들이 앞갑판에서 부르는 이름으로 열거하려 한다. 어쩌면 이러한 목록이, 내가 여기서 다만 시작만 해 놓은 것을 완성해 줄 후세의 연구자들에게 값진 것이 될지도 모르기 때문이다. 만약 다음에 열거하는 고래들 가운데 어느 것이라도 훗날 잡혀서 표시가 매겨진다면, 그때는 그 크기에 따라 2절판이든 8절판이든 12절판이든 이 체계 속에 손쉽게 편입될 수 있을 것이다. 곧 병코고래, 정크고래, 푸딩머리고래, 케이프고래, 길잡이고래, 대포고래, 깡마른고래, 구리고래, 코끼리고래, 빙산고래, 쿽고래, 푸른고래 따위다. 아이슬란드어와 네덜란드어, 그리고 고대 영어 전거에서라면, 온갖 투박한 이름을 단 또 다른 불확실한 고래들의 목록을 끌어올 수도 있을 것이다. 그러나 나는 그것들을 모조리 한물간 것으로 보아 생략하거니와, 그것들이 리바이어던다움으로 가득 차 있되 실은 아무것도 뜻하지 않는 한낱 소리에 지나지 않으리라는 의심을 도무지 떨칠 수가 없다.\n\n끝으로, 처음에 밝혔듯이 이 체계는 이 자리에서 단숨에 완성되지는 않을 것이라고 했다. 그대들은 내가 그 약속을 지켰음을 분명히 보지 않을 수 없으리라. 그러나 나는 이제 나의 고래학 체계를 이렇게 미완인 채로 남겨 두니, 마치 저 위대한 쾰른 대성당이 미완성의 탑 꼭대기에 기중기를 그대로 세워 둔 채로 남겨졌던 것과도 같다. 작은 건축물이야 그것을 처음 세운 건축가가 마무리할 수 있겠지만, 웅대한 것, 참된 것은 언제나 그 마지막 돌을 후세에 넘겨주는 법이다. 하느님이여, 내가 그 무엇이든 결코 완성하지 않게 하소서. 이 책 전체는 한낱 초고에 지나지 않는다. 아니, 초고의 초고일 따름이다. 오, 시간이여, 힘이여, 돈이여, 그리고 인내여!\n\n# CHAPTER 33. The Specksnyder.\n\n포경선의 사관들에 관해 말하자면, 작살잡이라는 사관 계급의 존재에서 비롯된, 배 안에서의 사소한 살림살이상의 특수성을 적어 두기에 이만한 자리도 없을 듯하다. 그것은 물론 포경 선단(船團) 말고는 그 어떤 바다 세계에서도 찾아볼 수 없는 계급이다.\n\n작살잡이라는 직분에 부여된 막중한 중요성은, 본래 두 세기도 더 거슬러 올라가는 옛 네덜란드 포경업에서, 포경선의 지휘권이 오늘날 선장이라 불리는 인물 한 사람에게 온전히 맡겨진 것이 아니라, 그와 슈펙스니데르(Specksnyder)라 불리는 사관 사이에 나뉘어 있었다는 사실로써 입증된다. 이 단어를 글자 그대로 옮기면 기름 베는 자라는 뜻이지만, 세월이 흐르며 관습은 그것을 수석 작살잡이와 같은 말로 만들어 놓았다. 그 시절 선장의 권한은 배의 항해와 전반적인 운영에만 한정되어 있었던 반면, 고래 사냥 부문과 그에 딸린 모든 일에 대해서는 슈펙스니데르, 곧 수석 작살잡이가 최고의 권위로 군림했다. 영국의 그린란드 포경업에서는 이 옛 네덜란드 직책이 슈펙시오니어(Specksioneer)라는 변형된 이름으로 아직 남아 있으나, 예전의 위엄은 안타깝게도 크게 깎여 버렸다. 오늘날 그는 그저 선임 작살잡이로 자리매김할 뿐이며, 그런 자격으로는 선장 휘하의 비교적 낮은 부하 하나에 지나지 않는다. 그럼에도 작살잡이들의 처신 여하에 포경 항해의 성패가 크게 달려 있고, 또 미국 포경업에서 그는 보트에서 중요한 사관일 뿐 아니라 특정한 상황(고래잡이 어장에서의 야간 당직)에서는 갑판의 지휘권마저 그의 몫이 되는 까닭에, 바다의 저 위대한 통치 원칙은 그가 명목상으로나마 앞돛대 앞의 일반 선원들과 떨어져 살며, 어떤 식으로든 그들의 직무상 상급자로 구별되어야 한다고 요구한다. 다만 선원들은 언제나 그를 사회적으로는 자기네와 대등한 동료로 허물없이 여기기는 한다.\n\n자, 바다에서 사관과 일반 선원 사이에 그어지는 그 큰 구별이란 바로 이것이다 — 전자는 고물 쪽에 거하고, 후자는 이물 쪽에 거한다. 그리하여 포경선과 상선을 막론하고 항해사들은 선장과 함께 거처를 두며, 마찬가지로 미국 포경선 대부분에서도 작살잡이들은 배의 고물 쪽에 머문다. 다시 말해 그들은 선장의 선실에서 식사를 하고, 그 선실과 간접적으로 통하는 곳에서 잠을 잔다.\n\n남빙양 포경 항해는 그 기간이 길고(지금이든 예전이든 인간이 행한 모든 항해 가운데 단연 가장 길다), 거기에 따르는 특유의 위험이 있으며, 또 높든 낮든 모든 선원이 고정된 임금이 아니라 다 함께 누리는 운수와 더불어 다 함께 기울이는 경계심과 담대함과 고된 노동에 그 이익을 의지하는 이해 공동체를 이루는 까닭에, 이 모든 사정이 어떤 경우에는 일반 상선보다 덜 엄격한 규율을 낳는 쪽으로 작용하기도 한다. 그렇기는 해도, 이 고래잡이들이 더러 원시적인 사례에서 옛 메소포타미아의 한 가족처럼 어우러져 산다 한들, 그럼에도 불구하고 적어도 후갑판의 격식 차린 외양만큼은 실질적으로 느슨해지는 일이 좀처럼 없으며, 아예 폐기되는 일은 결코 없다. 실로 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 배들 가운데는, 선장이 그 어떤 군함 해군에도 뒤지지 않는 의기양양한 위풍을 떨치며 후갑판을 거니는 광경을 볼 수 있는 배가 한둘이 아니다. 아니, 그가 누더기 같은 수로 안내인용 천이 아니라 황제의 자줏빛 망토라도 두른 듯, 겉으로 바치는 경의를 거의 그만큼이나 강요하기까지 한다.\n\n그리고 페쿼드(Pequod)호의 침울한 선장은 모든 사람 가운데서도 그런 천박하기 짝이 없는 허세와는 가장 거리가 먼 사람이었고, 그가 여태껏 요구한 유일한 경의라고는 두말없는, 즉각적인 복종뿐이었으며, 후갑판에 발을 올리기 전에 신발을 벗으라고 누구에게도 시킨 적이 없었고, 또 앞으로 상세히 풀어 놓을 사건들과 얽힌 특수한 사정으로 인해 때로는 짐짓 겸손한 어조로든 위협을 담은 어조로든 그 밖의 어조로든 평소와 다른 말투로 그들에게 말을 건넨 적이 있기는 했으나, 그런 에이해브(Ahab) 선장조차도 바다의 으뜸가는 격식과 관습을 결코 소홀히 여기지는 않았다.\n\n또한 어쩌면 종국에는 이런 사실이 드러나지 않을 수 없을 것이다 — 그가 때때로 그러한 격식과 관습 뒤에, 말하자면 자신을 가면처럼 가리었고, 그것들이 본디 정당하게 떠받들도록 마련된 목적이 아니라 다른, 더 사사로운 목적을 위해 부수적으로 이용했다는 사실 말이다. 그의 두뇌에 깃든 어떤 술탄적 전제(專制)의 기질은 본래대로라면 상당 부분 겉으로 드러나지 않은 채 머물렀을 터인데, 바로 그러한 격식들을 통하여 그 술탄적 기질은 저항할 수 없는 독재로 화신(化身)했다. 한 인간의 지적 우월함이 제아무리 대단할지라도, 그것은 그 자체로 다소간 하찮고 비루한 어떤 외적 술책과 방벽의 도움 없이는, 다른 사람들에 대한 실질적이고 행사 가능한 지배력으로 결코 굳어질 수 없는 법이다. 바로 이것이 하느님께서 세우신 진정한 제국의 군주들을 이 세상의 선거 연단에서 영영 멀리 떼어 놓으며, 이 세속의 대기가 베풀 수 있는 최고의 영예를, 무리라는 죽은 듯한 평면을 넘어서는 의심할 바 없는 우월함보다는, 오히려 신성한 무위(無爲) 속에 숨겨진 선택받은 소수에 대한 그들 자신의 한없는 열등함을 통해 명성을 얻는 자들에게 넘겨주는 것이다. 극단적인 정치적 미신이 이런 사소한 것들에 옷을 입혀 줄 때면 그 속에는 그토록 큰 위력이 도사리는지라, 어떤 왕가의 사례에서는 백치 같은 천치에게조차 그것들이 권능을 부여하기도 했다. 그러나 차르 니콜라이의 경우처럼, 지리적 제국의 둥근 왕관이 한 제왕적 두뇌를 휘감을 때면, 그때는 평민의 무리가 그 가공할 중앙집권 앞에 비굴하게 웅크리고 만다. 그리고 인간의 불굴함을 그 가장 폭넓은 진폭과 가장 곧은 휘두름으로 그려 내고자 하는 비극 작가라면, 자신의 예술에서 부수적으로나마 그토록 중요한, 지금 여기서 넌지시 가리킨 그 암시를 결코 잊지 않을 것이다.\n\n그러나 나의 선장 에이해브는 지금도 그 모든 낸터킷식 험상궂음과 텁수룩함을 지닌 채 내 눈앞을 거닐고 있다. 그리고 황제와 왕을 다루는 이 대목에서 나는 숨기지 말아야겠다 — 내가 다루는 것은 다만 그와 같은 가난하고 늙은 고래잡이 한 사람일 뿐이며, 따라서 겉으로 드러난 온갖 제왕적 장식과 의례용 천막은 내게 허락되지 않았다는 사실을. 오, 에이해브여! 그대 안에서 무엇이 위대하든, 그것은 모름지기 하늘에서 끌어 내려야 하고, 깊은 바닷속으로 자맥질하여 건져 올려야 하며, 형체 없는 대기 속에서 그려 내야 하리라!\n\n# CHAPTER 34. The Cabin-Table.\n\n정오다. 식당 사환인 도우보이가 선실 승강구로 빵 덩어리처럼 창백한 얼굴을 내밀고, 자기 주인이자 상전에게 식사를 알린다. 그 상전은 바람 아래쪽 사분정에 앉아 막 태양을 관측하던 참이었고, 지금은 그 일과를 위해 마련해 둔, 상아 다리 윗부분의 매끈하고 메달처럼 둥근 판에 위도를 말없이 계산해 적고 있다. 그가 그 전갈에 도무지 주의를 기울이지 않는 모습을 보면, 우울한 에이해브가 하인의 말을 듣지 못했다고 여길 법하다. 그러나 곧 그는 뒷돛대의 삭구를 붙잡고 갑판으로 몸을 휙 내려, 무덤덤하고 들뜸 없는 목소리로 \"식사하지, 스타벅 군\"이라 말한 뒤 선실 안으로 사라진다.\n\n그 술탄 같은 발걸음의 마지막 메아리가 잦아들고, 첫째 에미르인 스타벅이 그가 자리에 앉았으리라 충분히 짐작할 만해지면, 그제야 스타벅은 고요함에서 깨어나 갑판 위를 몇 차례 거닐고, 나침반함을 진지하게 한 번 들여다본 다음, 다소 유쾌한 기색을 담아 \"식사하게, 스텁 군\"이라 말하고 승강구를 내려간다. 둘째 에미르는 잠시 삭구 주변을 어슬렁거리다가, 메인브레이스를 가볍게 흔들어 그 중요한 밧줄이 멀쩡한지 확인한 뒤, 역시 그 오래된 임무를 떠맡아 빠르게 \"식사하게, 플라스크 군\"이라 던지고는 앞선 이들의 뒤를 따른다.\n\n그러나 셋째 에미르는 이제 뒷갑판에 자기 혼자 남은 것을 깨닫고는, 어떤 기묘한 속박에서 풀려난 듯한 기분이 드는 모양이다. 사방으로 온갖 의미심장한 윙크를 날리고 신발을 걷어차 벗어 던지더니, 그는 저 대투르크의 머리 바로 위에서 날카롭되 소리 없는 혼파이프 춤을 한바탕 추어 댄다. 그러고는 능숙한 솜씨로 모자를 뒷돛대 망루로 휙 던져 선반 삼아 걸쳐 놓고, 적어도 갑판에서 보이는 데까지는 흥겹게 까불며 내려간다. 다른 모든 행렬을 거꾸로 뒤집어, 음악과 함께 맨 뒤를 장식하면서 말이다. 그러나 아래쪽 선실 문턱으로 들어서기 직전, 그는 잠시 멈춰 서서 완전히 새로운 얼굴로 갈아입고, 그런 다음 독립적이고 유쾌한 작은 사내 플라스크는 비천한 자, 곧 노예의 역할로 에이해브 왕 앞에 들어선다.\n\n바다 관습의 그 지독한 인위성이 길러 낸 기묘한 일들 가운데서도 결코 사소하다 할 수 없는 것은 이런 것이다. 갑판 위 탁 트인 곳에서는 더러 어떤 사관들이 자극을 받으면 자기 지휘관에게 제법 대담하고 반항적으로 굴기도 하지만, 십중팔구 바로 그 사관들이 다음 순간 그 똑같은 지휘관의 선실로 늘 그렇듯 식사하러 내려가면, 곧장 그가 식탁 상석에 앉아 있는 동안 그에게 거슬리지 않는, 아니 굽실대고 겸손하다 할 만한 태도를 보인다는 점이다. 이는 참으로 놀랍고, 때로는 더없이 우스꽝스럽다. 어째서 이런 차이가 생기는가? 풀기 어려운 문제인가? 아마 아닐 것이다. 바빌론의 왕 벨사살이 되었다는 것, 그것도 거만하게가 아니라 정중하게 벨사살이 되었다는 것에는, 분명 세속적 위엄의 어떤 기운이 깃들어 있었을 것이다. 그러나 진실로 제왕다운, 지혜로운 정신으로 자기 사사로운 식탁에 초대한 손님들을 주재하는 사람, 그 사람이 한동안 누리는 도전받지 않는 권력과 개인적 영향력의 지배, 그 사람의 제왕다운 위세는 벨사살의 것을 능가한다. 벨사살은 가장 위대한 자가 아니었기 때문이다. 단 한 번이라도 벗들에게 식사를 베풀어 본 자는 카이사르가 된다는 것이 무엇인지 맛본 셈이다. 그것은 사교적 황제 노릇이라는 마법이니, 도무지 거역할 수 없는 것이다. 자, 이런 고려에 더하여 선장의 공식적 절대 권위를 덧붙인다면, 그로부터 방금 언급한 바다 생활의 그 특이한 점이 어디서 비롯되는지 추론해 낼 수 있을 것이다.\n\n상아를 박아 넣은 식탁을 두고, 에이해브는 하얀 산호 모래밭 위의 말 없는, 갈기 달린 바다사자처럼, 호전적이지만 여전히 공손한 새끼들에게 둘러싸여 좌중을 주재했다. 각 사관은 저마다 제 차례를 기다려 음식을 받았다. 그들은 에이해브 앞에서 어린아이와 다름없었다. 그럼에도 에이해브 안에는 사교적 오만이라곤 티끌만큼도 도사리고 있지 않은 듯했다. 그가 자기 앞의 주된 요리를 썰 때면, 그들은 한마음으로 골똘한 눈길을 모두 노인의 칼에 붙박았다. 나는 그들이 무슨 일이 있어도 그 순간을, 설령 날씨처럼 무난하기 짝이 없는 화제로라도 사소한 한마디로 더럽히는 일은 없었으리라고 생각한다. 결코 없었다! 그리고 에이해브가 칼과 포크를 내밀어 그 사이에 고기 한 점을 끼운 채 스타벅의 접시를 제 쪽으로 가져오라는 시늉을 하면, 일등항해사는 마치 적선을 받듯 제 몫의 고기를 받았다. 그리고 조심스레 그것을 썰었다. 혹여 칼이 접시에 스치기라도 하면 흠칫 놀랐다. 소리 없이 씹었다. 신중을 기하지 않고는 삼키지도 않았다. 독일 황제가 일곱 선제후와 더불어 장엄하게 식사하는 프랑크푸르트의 대관식 연회처럼, 이 선실의 식사 또한 어쩐지 엄숙한 식사여서, 무서운 침묵 속에 이루어졌다. 그러면서도 늙은 에이해브는 식탁에서 대화를 금하지는 않았으니, 다만 그 자신이 입을 다물 뿐이었다. 숨이 막힐 듯한 스텁에게는, 아래쪽 짐칸에서 쥐 한 마리가 갑자기 소동을 일으켰을 때 그것이 얼마나 큰 위안이 되었던가. 그리고 가련한 작은 플라스크, 그는 이 지친 가족 모임의 막내아들이자 어린 사내아이였다. 소금에 절인 쇠고기의 정강이뼈가 그의 몫이었고, 닭으로 치면 북채 자리가 그의 차지였을 것이다. 플라스크가 감히 제 손으로 음식을 덜어 먹는다는 것은, 그에게는 일급 절도죄나 다름없게 여겨졌으리라. 그가 그 식탁에서 제 손으로 음식을 덜어 먹었다면, 틀림없이 이 정직한 세상에서 다시는 고개를 들고 다닐 수 없었을 것이다. 그럼에도, 이상한 말이지만, 에이해브는 결코 그것을 금한 적이 없었다. 게다가 플라스크가 제 손으로 덜어 먹었더라도, 십중팔구 에이해브는 그것을 알아채지조차 못했을 것이다. 무엇보다도, 플라스크는 감히 버터를 제 손으로 떠 먹을 엄두를 내지 못했다. 배의 선주들이 버터가 그의 맑고 햇살 같은 혈색을 엉기게 한다는 이유로 그에게 버터를 금했다고 여겼던 것인지, 아니면 이렇게 시장이라곤 없는 바다에서 이토록 긴 항해를 하는 동안 버터는 귀한 물건이라 일개 하급자인 자기 몫은 아니라고 생각했던 것인지, 어쨌든 가련한 플라스크는 버터 없는 사내였다!\n\n또 한 가지 있다. 플라스크는 식사 자리에 맨 마지막으로 내려오는 사람이었고, 또 맨 먼저 일어나는 사람이었다. 생각해 보라! 이 때문에 플라스크의 식사 시간은 양쪽으로 고약하게 짓눌렸다. 스타벅과 스텁은 둘 다 그보다 먼저 시작했고, 게다가 뒤에 느긋이 남아 있을 특권까지 누렸다. 플라스크보다 겨우 한 단계 위일 뿐인 스텁조차 어쩌다 입맛이 없어 곧 식사를 마칠 기색을 보이면, 플라스크는 서둘러야 했으니, 그날은 세 입 이상 먹지 못할 판이었다. 스텁이 플라스크보다 먼저 갑판으로 올라가는 것은 신성한 관습에 어긋나는 일이었기 때문이다. 그래서 플라스크는 언젠가 사석에서 이렇게 털어놓은 적이 있었다. 자기가 사관의 지위에 오른 그 순간부터, 정도의 차이는 있을지언정 늘 배고프지 않은 상태라는 게 어떤 것인지 도무지 알지 못하게 되었노라고. 그가 먹는 것은 허기를 달래 준다기보다, 오히려 그 허기를 그의 안에 영원히 살아 있게 할 뿐이었기 때문이다. 평화와 만족은 내 위장에서 영영 떠나 버렸구나, 하고 플라스크는 생각했다. 나는 사관이지만, 돛대 앞 평선원이던 시절 그랬던 것처럼 앞갑판 선원실에서 옛날식 쇠고기 한 점이라도 낚아 올릴 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까. 이게 바로 승진의 열매라는 것이다. 이게 바로 영광의 헛됨이다. 이게 바로 삶의 광기다! 게다가 만일 페쿼드호의 어떤 일개 선원이 사관으로서의 플라스크에게 앙심을 품었다 치자. 그가 충분한 복수를 손에 넣으려면 그저 식사 시간에 고물 쪽으로 가, 선실 천창 너머로 플라스크를 한 번 훔쳐보기만 하면 그만이었다. 무시무시한 에이해브 앞에서 바보같이, 얼이 빠진 채 앉아 있는 그 모습을 말이다.\n\n자, 에이해브와 그의 세 항해사는 페쿼드호 선실에서 이른바 첫째 식탁을 이루었다. 그들이 들어온 순서와 정반대로 자리를 뜨고 나면, 식탁보가 치워지거나, 아니 차라리 창백한 사환의 손에 의해 다소 황급히 어느 정도 정돈되었다. 그런 다음 세 작살잡이가 그 잔치에 불려 들어왔으니, 그들이야말로 그 잔여 유산의 상속자였던 셈이다. 그들은 높고 위엄 있는 선실을 일종의 임시 하인 방쯤으로 만들어 버렸다.\n\n선장의 식탁이 자아내던, 견디기 힘든 그 억압과 이름조차 붙일 수 없는 보이지 않는 위세와는 기묘한 대조를 이루며, 작살잡이라는 저 하급자들은 완전히 근심 없는 방종과 편안함, 거의 광적이다 싶은 민주주의를 누렸다. 그들의 상전인 항해사들이 제 턱 경첩 소리조차 두려워하는 듯했던 반면, 작살잡이들은 어찌나 맛있게 음식을 씹어 대던지 소리가 다 날 지경이었다. 그들은 영주처럼 식사했다. 온종일 향신료를 싣는 인도행 배들처럼 제 배를 채웠다. 퀴케그와 타슈테고의 식욕이 어찌나 불길할 만큼 대단했던지, 앞서 먹어 치운 식사가 남긴 빈자리를 메우려고, 창백한 도우보이는 통째로 깎아 낸 소처럼 보이는 소금에 절인 쇠고기 한 덩이를 큼지막하게 가져와야 할 때가 많았다. 그가 굼뜨게 굴거나 날렵하게 깡충깡충 뛰어다니지 않으면, 타슈테고는 작살 던지듯 포크를 그의 등에 휙 꽂아 그를 재촉하는 신사답지 못한 버릇이 있었다. 한번은 대구가 갑작스러운 장난기에 사로잡혀, 도우보이의 기억을 일깨워 주겠다며 그를 통째로 번쩍 들어 올려 그 머리를 텅 빈 커다란 나무 접시에 처박았고, 그러는 동안 타슈테고는 칼을 손에 쥐고 그의 머릿가죽을 벗기기에 앞서 빙 둘러 금을 긋기 시작했다. 이 빵 덩어리 얼굴을 한 사환은 천성적으로 몹시 신경질적이고 벌벌 떠는 작은 사내였으니, 파산한 빵집 주인과 병원 간호사 사이에서 난 자식이었다. 그리고 시커멓고 무시무시한 에이해브가 늘 버티고 있는 광경과 이 세 야만인의 주기적이고 소란스러운 행패가 겹치니, 도우보이의 삶은 온통 끊임없이 입술을 떠는 나날이었다. 대개 그는 작살잡이들이 요구한 것을 모두 갖춰 준 뒤에는, 그들의 손아귀를 벗어나 바로 곁에 딸린 작은 식료품 저장실로 도망쳐, 그 문의 블라인드 틈으로 모든 일이 끝날 때까지 겁에 질려 그들을 엿보곤 했다.\n\n퀴케그가 타슈테고를 마주 보고 앉아, 자기 줄로 간 뾰족한 이빨을 인디언의 이빨에 맞세우고 있는 광경은 볼만했다. 그 둘과 직각을 이루어 대구는 바닥에 앉아 있었으니, 의자에 앉았다가는 영구차 깃털 장식 같은 그의 머리가 낮은 천장 들보에 닿을 판이었기 때문이다. 그의 거대한 팔다리가 움직일 때마다, 마치 아프리카코끼리가 배에 승객으로 올라탄 듯, 낮은 선실 골조가 흔들렸다. 그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 그 거대한 흑인은 놀랍도록 절제할 줄 알았으니, 까다롭다 할 만큼 정갈하게 먹었다. 저토록 작은 양을 입에 넣으면서, 그토록 넓고 영주답고 당당한 몸뚱이에 두루 퍼진 생기를 어떻게 지탱할 수 있는지 도무지 믿기 어려울 정도였다. 그러나 의심할 바 없이, 이 고귀한 야만인은 사방에 넘쳐 나는 공기라는 원소를 든든히 먹고 깊이 마셨으니, 한껏 벌어진 콧구멍으로 세상의 숭고한 생명을 들이마셨던 것이다. 거인은 쇠고기나 빵으로 만들어지거나 길러지는 것이 아니다. 그러나 퀴케그는 음식을 먹을 때 입맛 다시는 소리가 죽을 듯이 야만스러웠으니, 그 흉한 소리가 어찌나 컸던지, 벌벌 떠는 도우보이는 행여 제 깡마른 팔뚝에 이빨 자국이 남지나 않았나 거의 들여다볼 지경이었다. 그리고 타슈테고가 그를 향해 어서 와서 제 뼈를 발라 가라고 외치는 소리를 들을 때면, 이 단순한 사환은 갑작스러운 경련에 사로잡혀, 식료품 저장실 둘레에 걸린 사기그릇을 죄다 박살 낼 뻔했다. 작살잡이들이 자기네 창과 다른 무기를 갈려고 주머니에 넣고 다니던 숫돌, 그리고 식사 자리에서 보란 듯이 칼을 갈아 대던 그 숫돌, 그 갈리는 소리 또한 가련한 도우보이를 진정시키는 데는 조금도 도움이 되지 않았다. 고향 섬에서 지내던 시절, 적어도 퀴케그만큼은 분명 사람을 죽이고 흥겹게 즐기는 어떤 무분별한 짓을 저질렀으리라는 것을, 그가 어찌 잊을 수 있었겠는가. 아아, 도우보이여! 식인종의 시중을 드는 백인 급사의 신세란 참으로 고달프구나. 그는 팔에 냅킨이 아니라 방패를 걸치고 다녀야 마땅하리라. 그러나 때가 되면, 그가 더없이 기뻐하게도 세 바닷소금 전사들은 일어나 떠나갔다. 잘 속아 넘어가고 허무맹랑한 이야기에 솔깃해하는 그의 귀에는, 그들이 걸음을 옮길 때마다 그 몸속의 호전적인 뼈들이 모조리 쩔렁거리는 소리가, 마치 칼집 속의 무어인 언월도 소리처럼 들렸다.\n\n그러나 이 야만인들이 선실에서 식사하고, 명목상으로는 거기서 산다고는 해도, 그들의 습성은 도무지 한자리에 붙어 있는 법이 없어서, 식사 때와 잠자기 직전, 곧 그곳을 지나 제 고유한 처소로 갈 때를 빼고는 좀처럼 선실 안에 있는 일이 없었다.\n\n이 한 가지 점에서, 에이해브는 대다수 미국 고래잡이 선장들과 다를 바 없어 보였다. 그들은 대체로 배의 선실이란 본디 자기네 것이며, 그 누구든 언제든 거기 들어오도록 허락받는 것은 오로지 호의 덕분이라는 견해 쪽으로 기우는 부류였다. 그러므로 사실대로 말하자면, 페쿼드호의 항해사들과 작살잡이들은 선실 안에서 살았다기보다 선실 바깥에서 살았다고 하는 편이 더 옳을 것이다. 그들이 선실에 들어설 때면, 그것은 마치 거리로 난 현관문이 집 안으로 들어서는 것과도 같았으니, 한순간 안으로 향했다가 다음 순간 곧장 밖으로 향했고, 항구적으로는 탁 트인 바깥에 머물러 있었기 때문이다. 그렇다고 그들이 이로써 크게 잃은 것도 없었다. 선실에는 동료애란 것이 없었고, 사교적으로 에이해브는 다가설 수 없는 사람이었다. 명목상으로는 기독교 세계의 인구에 포함되어 있었으나, 그는 여전히 거기에 이방인이었다. 그는 이 세상을 살아가되, 마치 정착민이 들어찬 미주리에서 마지막 남은 회색곰처럼 살아갔다. 그리고 봄과 여름이 떠나가고 나면, 저 숲속의 거친 로건이 나무 구멍 속에 제 몸을 파묻고 거기서 제 발바닥을 빨며 겨울을 나듯이, 에이해브의 영혼 또한 그 매섭고 울부짖는 노년에 제 몸뚱이라는 동굴 같은 줄기 속에 갇혀, 거기서 제 음울한 발바닥을 빨며 우울함을 양식으로 삼았던 것이다!\n\n# CHAPTER 35. The Mast-Head.\n\n더없이 쾌적한 날씨가 이어지던 무렵, 다른 선원들과 정해진 차례를 따라 돌아가다가 마침내 내 첫 돛대 꼭대기 망보기 차례가 돌아왔다.\n\n미국의 포경선 대부분에서는, 배가 항구를 떠나는 것과 거의 동시에 돛대 꼭대기에 사람이 배치된다. 비록 제대로 된 어장에 닿기까지 1만 5천 마일, 혹은 그 이상을 항해해야 하는 경우라도 그렇다. 그리고 만약 삼 년, 사 년, 혹은 오 년에 걸친 항해 끝에 배 안에 무언가 빈 것이 있는 채로—이를테면 빈 약병 하나라도 있는 채로—고향에 가까워지고 있다면, 그때에도 그 돛대 꼭대기에는 마지막까지 사람이 배치된다. 그래서 그 배의 스카이세일 돛대 끝이 항구의 첨탑들 사이로 미끄러져 들어갈 때에야 비로소, 배는 고래를 한 마리 더 잡으리라는 희망을 완전히 내려놓는 것이다.\n\n자, 뭍에서든 물 위에서든 돛대 꼭대기에 서서 망을 보는 일이란 매우 오래되고 흥미로운 것이니, 여기서 어느 정도 그 이야기를 풀어 보기로 하자. 내가 보기에 가장 이른 시기에 돛대 꼭대기에 선 자들은 옛 이집트인들이었던 듯하다. 내가 아무리 살펴보아도 그들보다 앞선 자들을 찾을 수 없기 때문이다. 물론 그들의 선조인 바벨탑을 세운 자들도, 그 탑으로 아시아든 아프리카든 통틀어 가장 높다란 돛대 꼭대기를 세우려 했음이 분명하다. 그러나 (그 꼭대기에 마지막 장식이 얹히기도 전에) 그 거대한 돌 돛대는 하느님의 진노라는 무서운 폭풍 속에서 배 밖으로 떨어져 나갔다고 할 수 있으니, 따라서 우리는 이 바벨탑 건설자들에게 이집트인들보다 앞선 자리를 내줄 수 없다. 그리고 이집트인들이 돛대 꼭대기에 선 자들의 민족이었다는 주장은, 최초의 피라미드들이 천문학적 목적으로 세워졌다는 고고학자들 사이의 일반적인 믿음에 근거를 둔다. 이 학설은 그 건축물들의 네 면이 모두 계단 모양으로 독특하게 이루어져 있다는 사실로 유달리 뒷받침되는데, 그 계단을 따라 옛 천문학자들은 다리를 엄청나게 높이 들어 올리며 꼭대기까지 기어올라 새로운 별을 발견하면 큰 소리로 외치곤 했던 것이다. 마치 현대 선박의 망꾼들이 돛이나 막 시야에 들어온 고래를 보고 큰 소리로 외치듯이 말이다. 옛 시대의 이름난 기독교 은수자 성(聖) 스틸리테스의 경우, 그는 사막에 높다란 돌기둥을 세우고 그 꼭대기에서 생애의 후반부를 통째로 보냈으며, 도르래로 땅에서 음식을 끌어 올리곤 했다. 그에게서 우리는 두려움을 모르는 돛대 꼭대기 망꾼의 놀라운 본보기를 본다. 그는 안개에도, 서리에도, 비에도, 우박에도, 진눈깨비에도 제자리에서 쫓겨나지 않고, 끝까지 모든 것을 용감히 견뎌 내며, 말 그대로 제 임무의 자리에서 죽었다. 현대의 돛대 꼭대기 망꾼들로는 우리에게 생명 없는 무리뿐이니, 한낱 돌과 쇠와 청동으로 만든 사람들이다. 그들은 거센 폭풍을 견뎌 낼 능력은 충분하지만, 무언가 기이한 광경을 발견하고 큰 소리로 외치는 일에는 전혀 무능하다. 저기 나폴레옹이 있다. 그는 방돔 광장 기둥 꼭대기에서 팔짱을 낀 채, 허공 백오십 피트(약 45미터) 높이에 서 있으되, 이제는 저 아래 갑판을 누가 다스리든—루이 필리프든, 루이 블랑이든, 악마 루이든—아랑곳하지 않는다. 위대한 워싱턴 역시 볼티모어에서 우뚝 솟은 큰 돛대 꼭대기 높은 곳에 서 있으니, 헤라클레스의 기둥 가운데 하나처럼, 그의 기둥은 인간의 위대함이 다다를 수 있는 그 한계점을 표시하는바, 그 너머로 나아갈 인간은 거의 없을 것이다. 넬슨 제독 또한 청동포 금속으로 만든 캡스턴 위에서 트라팔가 광장의 돛대 꼭대기에 서 있으니, 저 런던의 매연에 가장 짙게 가려 있을 때에도 그곳에 숨은 영웅이 있다는 표징은 여전히 주어진다. 연기가 있는 곳에는 반드시 불이 있는 법이니까. 그러나 위대한 워싱턴도, 나폴레옹도, 넬슨도, 저 아래에서 아무리 미친 듯이 불러내어 그들이 굽어보는 어지러운 갑판을 그 지혜로 도와 달라 간청한들, 단 한 번의 외침에도 응답하지 않을 것이다. 비록 그들의 영혼이 미래의 자욱한 안개를 꿰뚫어, 어떤 모래톱과 어떤 암초를 피해야 하는지를 알아본다고 짐작될지라도 말이다.\n\n뭍에서 돛대 꼭대기에 선 자들을 바다에서 그렇게 한 자들과 어떤 식으로든 짝지어 놓는 것이 부당해 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 사실은 그렇지 않다는 것은, 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 유일한 역사가인 오베드 메이시가 책임지고 전하는 한 항목으로 분명히 입증된다. 갸륵한 오베드는 우리에게 이렇게 일러 준다. 고래잡이가 시작되던 초창기, 아직 배들이 정기적으로 사냥감을 좇아 출항하기 전에, 그 섬 사람들은 바닷가를 따라 높다란 기둥들을 세웠고, 망꾼들은 거기에 못으로 박은 디딤대를 밟고 올라갔는데, 그 모습이 마치 닭들이 닭장에서 횃대 위로 올라가는 것과 비슷했다고 한다. 몇 해 전에는 뉴질랜드의 만(灣) 포경꾼들도 이와 똑같은 방식을 채택하여, 사냥감을 발견하면 해변 가까이에 사람을 다 태워 둔 보트들에게 신호를 보냈다. 그러나 이 관습은 이제 한물갔으니, 그렇다면 본래의 제대로 된 돛대 꼭대기, 곧 바다 위 포경선의 돛대 꼭대기로 눈을 돌려 보자. 세 개의 돛대 꼭대기에는 해 뜰 때부터 해 질 때까지 사람이 배치되며, 선원들은 (키를 잡을 때처럼) 정해진 차례를 따라 두 시간마다 서로 교대한다. 열대 지방의 고요한 날씨 속에서 돛대 꼭대기란 더없이 쾌적한 곳이다. 아니, 몽상에 잠겨 사색하기를 좋아하는 사람에게는 그야말로 즐거운 곳이다. 그곳에서 그대는 고요한 갑판으로부터 백 피트(약 30미터) 높이에 서서, 마치 돛대가 거대한 죽마라도 되는 듯 깊은 바다 위를 성큼성큼 누비는데, 그러는 동안 그대의 발밑에서, 말하자면 그대의 두 다리 사이로, 바다에서 가장 거대한 괴물들이 헤엄쳐 가니, 마치 옛 로도스의 그 이름난 거상(巨像)의 두 장화 사이로 배들이 한때 항해했던 것과도 같다. 그곳에서 그대는 바다의 무한한 연속 속에 자신을 잃은 채 서 있고, 물결 외에는 흐트러지는 것이라곤 없다. 황홀경에 빠진 배는 나른하게 흔들리고, 졸음 오는 무역풍이 불어오니, 모든 것이 그대를 나른함 속으로 녹여 든다. 이 열대 포경 생활에서는 대체로, 숭고하리만치 아무 일도 일어나지 않는 평온이 그대를 감싼다. 그대는 어떤 소식도 듣지 못하고, 어떤 신문도 읽지 못하며, 평범한 일들을 깜짝 놀랄 만한 이야기로 부풀린 호외(號外)가 그대를 쓸데없는 흥분으로 꾀어내는 일도 결코 없다. 집안의 우환이나, 부도난 증권이나, 주가 폭락에 관한 소식도 듣지 못하며, 저녁으로 무엇을 먹을까 하는 생각으로 시달리는 일도 결코 없다. 삼 년 넘는 동안 먹을 끼니가 모조리 통 속에 차곡차곡 채워져 있고, 그대의 식단표는 바뀔 줄을 모르기 때문이다.\n\n이러한 남방 포경선들 가운데 하나에서, 흔히 그러하듯 삼 년이나 사 년에 걸친 긴 항해를 한다면, 그대가 돛대 꼭대기에서 보내는 여러 시간을 모두 합치면 몇 달이나 될 것이다. 그리고 참으로 한탄스러운 일은, 그대가 타고난 인생의 전체 기간 가운데 그토록 상당한 부분을 바치는 그 자리가, 아늑한 거주성 비슷한 것이라곤 어이없을 만큼 결여되어 있다는 점이다. 침대나, 해먹이나, 영구차나, 보초막이나, 설교단이나, 마차나, 그 밖에 사람들이 잠시나마 스스로를 고립시키는 자그맣고 아늑한 장치들에 깃든, 편안한 장소감 같은 것을 길러 내기에 알맞은 구석이라곤 없는 것이다. 그대가 가장 흔히 자리 잡는 횃대는 톱갈란트 돛대의 꼭대기인데, 거기서 그대는 톱갈란트 가로대라 불리는 (거의 포경꾼들에게만 특유한) 두 개의 가느다란 평행 막대 위에 선다. 바다에 휘둘려 이리저리 흔들리는 이곳에서, 초심자가 느끼는 아늑함이란 황소의 뿔 위에 선 것과 비슷한 정도다. 물론 추운 날씨에는 당직용 외투의 형태로 제 집을 위로 가지고 올라갈 수도 있다. 그러나 엄밀히 말하면, 아무리 두꺼운 당직용 외투라도 옷 한 점 걸치지 않은 몸뚱이 이상으로 집이 되어 주지는 못한다. 영혼이 제 살로 된 장막 안에 들러붙어, 그 안에서 자유로이 움직이지도 못하고, 멸망의 큰 위험을 무릅쓰지 않고서는 (마치 겨울에 눈 덮인 알프스를 건너는 무지한 순례자처럼) 거기서 빠져나오지도 못하는 것처럼, 당직용 외투란 집이라기보다는 한낱 봉투, 곧 그대를 감싸는 또 하나의 살갗에 지나지 않는다. 그대는 제 몸뚱이 안에 선반이나 서랍장을 들여놓을 수 없고, 마찬가지로 당직용 외투를 편리한 벽장으로 만들 수도 없다.\n\n이 모든 것과 관련하여 참으로 한탄스러운 것은, 남방 포경선의 돛대 꼭대기에는 까마귀 둥지(crow's-nest)라 불리는, 저 부러운 작은 천막 혹은 설교단이 갖추어져 있지 않다는 점이다. 그린란드 포경선의 망꾼들은 그 안에서 얼어붙은 바다의 혹독한 날씨로부터 보호받는다. 슬릿 선장의 노변(爐邊) 이야기, 곧 \"그린란드 고래를 찾아, 그리고 부수적으로는 옛 그린란드의 사라진 아이슬란드 식민지를 다시 발견하기 위한, 빙산 사이의 항해\"라는 제목의 책에서, 이 훌륭한 책에서, 돛대 꼭대기에 서는 모든 자들은 글레이셔호의 당시 갓 발명된 까마귀 둥지에 관한 매혹적이고 상세한 설명을 제공받는다. 글레이셔호란 슬릿 선장의 멋진 배의 이름이었다. 그는 그것을 스스로의 영예를 기리어 \"슬릿의 까마귀 둥지\"라 불렀으니, 그가 본래의 발명가이자 특허권자였기 때문이요, 또한 그가 온갖 우스꽝스러운 거짓 겸양에서 자유로운 사람으로서, 우리가 우리 자신의 자식을 우리 자신의 이름을 따라 부른다면 (우리 아비들이야말로 본래의 발명가이자 특허권자이니), 마찬가지로 우리가 낳은 다른 어떤 장치든 우리 자신을 따라 이름 붙여야 마땅하다고 여겼기 때문이다. 모양으로 보자면, 슬릿의 까마귀 둥지는 커다란 술통이나 큰 통과 비슷한 무엇이다. 다만 위쪽이 열려 있는데, 그곳에는 거센 폭풍 속에서 머리를 바람으로부터 막아 주도록 움직일 수 있는 측면 가림막이 갖추어져 있다. 돛대 꼭대기에 고정되어 있어, 그 바닥의 작은 뚜껑 문을 통해 안으로 올라간다. 뒤쪽, 곧 배의 고물에 가까운 쪽에는 편안한 자리가 있고, 그 아래에는 우산과 목도리와 외투를 넣어 두는 사물함이 있다. 앞쪽에는 가죽으로 된 걸이가 있어, 거기에 그대의 나팔형 확성기와 담뱃대와 망원경과 그 밖의 항해용 편의 물품들을 보관한다. 슬릿 선장이 몸소 이 자신의 까마귀 둥지에서 돛대 꼭대기 망을 설 때면, 그는 늘 소총 한 자루를 (역시 걸이에 고정해서) 화약통과 탄알과 함께 가지고 다녔다고 우리에게 일러 준다. 그 물에 들끓는 길 잃은 일각고래, 곧 떠도는 바다 유니콘을 쏘아 잡기 위함이었으니, 갑판에서는 물의 저항 때문에 그것들을 명중시킬 수 없으나, 위에서 아래로 내리쏘는 것은 전혀 다른 문제이기 때문이다. 자, 슬릿 선장이 그렇게 자신의 까마귀 둥지에 딸린 자잘한 편의 시설들을 빠짐없이 묘사하는 것은 분명 애정에서 우러난 수고였다. 그러나 그가 이런 것들 가운데 많은 것을 그토록 자세히 늘어놓으면서, 또 그가 그 까마귀 둥지 안에서 행한 실험에 관한 매우 과학적인 설명을 우리에게 베풀면서—그곳에 작은 나침반을 하나 두고서, 모든 나침반함 자석의 이른바 \"국지적 인력\"에서 비롯되는 오차를 상쇄하려 했으니, 이 오차는 배의 널빤지 속에 든 쇠붙이가 수평으로 가까이 있는 데서, 그리고 글레이셔호의 경우에는 어쩌면 그 선원들 가운데 몰락한 대장장이가 그토록 많았던 데서 비롯된 것이라 할 만하다—내가 말하건대, 비록 슬릿 선장이 이 대목에서 매우 신중하고 과학적이긴 하나, 그 온갖 박식한 \"나침반함 편차\"와 \"방위 나침반 관측\"과 \"근사 오차\"에도 불구하고, 그는 잘 알고 있다, 슬릿 선장은. 자신이 그 심오한 자기(磁氣) 명상에 그토록 깊이 빠져 있던 나머지, 까마귀 둥지 한쪽에 그토록 산뜻하게 끼워져 손만 뻗으면 닿을 곳에 놓인, 가득 채워진 그 작은 휴대용 술병 쪽으로 이따금 마음이 끌리지 않을 수 없었다는 것을. 비록 전체적으로 보아 나는 이 용감하고 정직하며 학식 깊은 선장을 크게 흠모하고 심지어 사랑하기까지 하지만, 그가 그 휴대용 술병을 그토록 철저히 묵살한 것은 그에게 몹시 못마땅하다. 장갑 낀 손가락과 두건 쓴 머리로, 극점에서 서너 길도 떨어지지 않은 저 위의 새 둥지 안에서 수학을 연구하던 동안, 그 술병이 그에게 얼마나 충실한 벗이요 위로가 되어 주었을 것인가를 생각하면 더욱 그러하다.\n\n그러나 우리 남방 고래잡이들이 슬릿 선장과 그의 그린란드 사람들만큼 위에서 아늑하게 거처를 누리지는 못한다 해도, 그 불리함은 우리 남방 어부들이 주로 떠다니는 그 매혹적인 바다의 사뭇 대조적인 고요함으로 크게 상쇄된다. 한 예로, 나는 아주 느긋하게 삭구를 타고 올라가, 망루에 자리를 잡고 쉬면서 퀴케그(Queequeg)나, 혹은 거기서 마주치는 비번인 다른 누군가와 이야기를 나누곤 했다. 그런 다음 조금 더 위로 올라가, 윗돛 활대 위로 게으른 다리 하나를 척 걸치고서 물의 목초지를 미리 한번 둘러본 뒤에, 마침내 나의 최종 목적지까지 기어올랐다.\n\n여기서 솔직히 털어놓고, 내가 형편없이 망을 보았다는 것을 거리낌 없이 인정하겠다. 우주의 문제가 내 안에서 빙빙 도는 마당에—그토록 사색을 자아내는 높이에서 완전히 혼자 내버려진 채로—내가 어찌 모든 포경선의 상비 명령, 곧 \"바람 부는 쪽 눈을 똑바로 뜨고, 무언가 보일 때마다 큰 소리로 외쳐라\"라는 의무를 가볍게 여기지 않을 수 있었겠는가.\n\n그리고 이 자리에서 그대들에게 간곡히 충고하노라, 낸터킷의 선주들이여! 그대들의 빈틈없는 어업에, 이마가 야위고 눈이 움푹 꺼진, 철 모르는 사색에 빠져들기 일쑤이며, 머릿속에 보디치 대신 파이돈을 담고 승선하겠노라는 젊은이를 결코 끌어들이지 말라. 내가 말하노니, 그런 자를 경계하라. 그대들의 고래는 잡히기에 앞서 먼저 눈에 띄어야 하거늘, 이 눈 꺼진 젊은 플라톤주의자는 그대들을 세상을 열 바퀴 돌도록 헛되이 끌고 다니면서도, 향유고래 기름 한 파인트도 더 채워 주지 못할 것이다. 또한 이런 경고가 전혀 필요 없는 것도 아니다. 요즘 들어 고래잡이는, 세상의 좀먹는 근심에 진저리가 나서 타르와 고래 비계 속에서 감상(感傷)을 찾는, 낭만적이고 우울하며 정신이 딴 데 가 있는 수많은 젊은이들에게 피난처를 제공하기 때문이다. 차일드 해럴드는 운수 사나운, 실망에 찬 어느 포경선의 돛대 꼭대기에 적잖이 자주 올라앉아, 침울한 어조로 이렇게 내뱉는다.\n\n\"굽이쳐라, 그대 깊고 검푸른 대양이여, 굽이쳐라! 일만 명의 비계 사냥꾼들이 그대 위를 휩쓸어도 헛되도다.\"\n\n그런 배의 선장들은 정신이 딴 데 가 있는 이 젊은 철학자들을 곧잘 꾸짖으며, 항해에 충분한 \"관심\"을 느끼지 않는다고 나무란다. 그러면서 그들이 온갖 명예로운 야망을 가망 없이 잃어버린 나머지, 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 차라리 고래를 보지 않는 편을 더 바란다고 은근히 빗대기도 한다. 그러나 모두 헛수고다. 이 젊은 플라톤주의자들은 자신들의 시력이 불완전하다는 생각을 품고 있다. 자기들은 근시라는 것이다. 그렇다면 무엇 하러 시신경을 혹사하겠는가? 그들은 오페라용 쌍안경을 집에 두고 온 것이다.\n\n\"이놈, 이 원숭이 같은 녀석아.\" 어느 작살잡이가 이런 젊은이 가운데 하나에게 말했다. \"우리가 거의 삼 년이 다 되도록 바다를 누비고 다녔건만, 너는 아직 고래 한 마리도 발견하지 못했다. 네가 저 위에 올라가 있을 때면 고래가 닭의 이빨만큼이나 드물어지는구나.\" 어쩌면 정말 그러했을지도 모른다. 아니면 어쩌면 저 먼 수평선에 고래 떼가 있었을지도 모른다. 그러나 이 정신이 딴 데 가 있는 젊은이는, 물결의 율동과 자신의 상념이 어우러진 그 가락에 아편에라도 취한 듯 멍하니 무의식적인 몽상의 나른함에 잠겨 든 나머지, 마침내 자신의 정체성을 잃고 만다. 그는 제 발밑의 신비로운 바다를, 인류와 자연을 두루 적시는 저 깊고 푸르며 밑바닥 없는 영혼의 눈에 보이는 형상으로 여긴다. 그리고 그를 슬며시 비껴가는 온갖 기이하고, 어렴풋이 보이며, 미끄러지듯 흐르는 아름다운 것들, 분간할 수 없는 어떤 형체에서 흐릿하게 발견되어 솟아오르는 모든 지느러미가, 그에게는 끊임없이 영혼을 가로질러 스치며 그곳에 깃들 뿐인 저 붙잡을 수 없는 상념들의 화신으로 보인다. 이 홀린 듯한 기분 속에서, 그대의 영혼은 그것이 비롯된 곳으로 썰물처럼 빠져나가, 시간과 공간을 가로질러 흩어진다. 크랜머의 뿌려진 범신론적 재처럼, 마침내 이 둥근 지구 위 모든 해안의 일부가 되는 것이다.\n\n이제 그대 안에는 생명이라곤 없으니, 다만 부드럽게 흔들리는 배가 전해 주는 그 일렁이는 생명뿐이다. 배는 그것을 바다에서 빌려 왔고, 바다는 그것을 헤아릴 길 없는 하느님의 조수(潮水)에서 빌려 왔다. 그러나 이 잠이, 이 꿈이 그대 위에 드리워 있는 동안, 발이나 손을 한 치라도 움직여 보라. 잡고 있던 손을 조금이라도 놓쳐 보라. 그러면 그대의 정체성이 공포와 더불어 되돌아온다. 그대는 데카르트의 소용돌이 위를 떠돈다. 그리고 어쩌면, 한낮에, 더없이 화창한 날씨 속에서, 반쯤 목 졸린 듯한 외마디 비명과 함께, 그대는 그 투명한 대기를 뚫고 한여름의 바다 속으로 떨어져, 다시는 영영 솟아오르지 못하리라. 이를 명심하라, 그대 범신론자들이여!\n\n(에이해브 등장. 이어서 모두.)\n\n파이프 사건이 있은 지 그리 오래지 않은 어느 날 아침, 식사를 마친 지 얼마 안 되어 에이해브(Ahab)는 여느 때처럼 선실 통로를 거쳐 갑판으로 올라왔다. 시골 신사가 같은 식사를 마치고 정원을 몇 바퀴 거니는 것처럼, 바다의 선장들은 대개 그 시각에 그렇게 갑판을 거닐곤 한다.\n\n곧 그의 한결같은 상아 다리 발걸음 소리가 들려왔다. 그가 오랜 산책길을 오가며 거닐던 그 널빤지는 그의 발길에 너무도 익숙해진 나머지, 마치 지질학적 암석처럼 그 독특한 걸음의 흔적으로 온통 움푹움푹 패어 있었다. 그 골이 파이고 자국이 새겨진 이마를 그대도 가만히 들여다보았더라면, 거기서도 한층 더 기이한 발자국을 보았으리라 — 잠들지 않고 끊임없이 서성이는 그의 한 가지 생각이 남긴 발자국을.\n\n그러나 지금 문제의 그날 아침에는, 그 자국들이 한결 깊어 보였다. 그날따라 그의 초조한 발걸음이 한층 깊은 흔적을 남겼던 것처럼. 그리고 에이해브는 자신의 생각에 어찌나 골똘히 잠겨 있었던지, 그가 큰 돛대 앞에서, 또 나침반함 앞에서 한결같은 걸음으로 돌아설 때마다, 그가 돌아서면 그 생각도 그 안에서 함께 돌아서고, 그가 거닐면 그 생각도 그 안에서 함께 거니는 것이 거의 눈에 보일 듯했다. 그 생각이 어찌나 완전히 그를 사로잡고 있었던지, 그것은 마치 바깥으로 드러나는 모든 동작을 안에서 빚어내는 거푸집인 듯싶었다.\n\n\"저 양반을 좀 보게, 플라스크(Flask).\" 스터브(Stubb)가 속삭였다. \"저 안에 든 병아리가 껍데기를 쪼고 있어. 곧 튀어나올 걸세.\"\n\n시간이 흘러갔다. 에이해브는 이제 선실 안에 틀어박혔다가, 이윽고 다시 갑판을 거닐었는데, 그 얼굴에는 여전히 무서울 만큼 한곳에 집착하는 광기가 어려 있었다.\n\n하루해가 저물어 가고 있었다. 갑자기 그는 뱃전 곁에 멈춰 서더니, 거기 뚫린 송곳 구멍에 뼈다리를 꽂아 넣고, 한 손으로는 돛줄을 움켜쥐고서, 스타벅(Starbuck)에게 모두를 고물 쪽으로 불러 모으라고 명령했다.\n\n\"선장님!\" 일등항해사가 말했다. 무언가 예사롭지 않은 경우가 아니고서는 배 위에서 좀처럼, 아니 결코 내려지지 않는 명령에 그는 놀란 것이었다.\n\n\"모두를 고물로 불러 모아라.\" 에이해브가 거듭 명했다. \"돛대 꼭대기에 있는 자들! 내려와!\"\n\n배의 선원이 모두 모여, 호기심 어린, 그러면서도 약간의 불안을 감추지 못한 얼굴로 그를 바라보았다. 폭풍이 다가올 때의 수평선 같은 그의 모습 때문이었다. 에이해브는 뱃전 너머로 재빨리 한 번 눈길을 던지고는, 다시 선원들 사이로 시선을 쏘아 보내더니, 서 있던 자리에서 다시 걸음을 옮겼다. 그리고 곁에 아무도 없다는 듯이, 묵직한 발걸음으로 갑판을 도는 일을 다시 시작했다. 고개를 숙이고 모자를 반쯤 눌러쓴 채, 그는 선원들 사이에 도는 의아한 속삭임에는 아랑곳없이 계속 거닐었다. 마침내 스터브가 플라스크에게 조심스레 속삭이기를, 에이해브가 사람들을 여기 불러 모은 것은 보행 묘기라도 구경시키려는 셈인 모양이라고 했다. 그러나 그 일은 오래가지 않았다. 그는 거칠게 걸음을 멈추더니 외쳤다.\n\n\"제군들, 고래를 보면 어떻게 하나?\"\n\n\"소리쳐 알립니다!\" 스무 명도 넘는 목소리가 한데 뭉쳐 충동적으로 답했다.\n\n\"좋다!\" 에이해브가 사나운 칭찬이 담긴 목소리로 외쳤다. 자신의 뜻밖의 물음이 그들을 그토록 자석처럼 끌어당겨 일으킨 그 진심 어린 활기를 지켜보면서.\n\n\"그다음엔 어떻게 하나, 제군들?\"\n\n\"보트를 내려서 쫓아갑니다!\"\n\n\"그러면 어떤 가락에 맞춰 노를 젓나, 제군들?\"\n\n\"죽은 고래가 아니면 부서진 보트다!\"\n\n외침이 터질 때마다 노인의 얼굴은 점점 더 기이하고 사납게 기뻐하며 흡족해졌다. 한편 선원들은 호기심 어린 눈으로 서로를 바라보기 시작했다. 도무지 무슨 뜻인지도 모를 그런 물음에 자기들이 어쩌면 이토록 흥분해 버렸는지 의아해하는 듯한 모습이었다.\n\n그러나 그들은 다시 온통 열기에 휩싸였다. 에이해브가 이제 다리를 꽂은 구멍 속에서 반쯤 몸을 돌리고, 한 손으로 돛줄 높은 데를 붙잡아 거의 발작하듯 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 그들에게 이렇게 말한 것이다.\n\n\"돛대 꼭대기에 올라가는 자들은 모두 전에 내가 흰 고래에 관해 명령을 내리는 것을 들었을 것이다. 자, 보라! 이 스페인 금화 한 닢이 보이나?\" 그는 넓적하고 번쩍이는 동전 한 닢을 햇빛에 치켜들었다. \"16달러짜리다, 제군들. 보이나? 스타벅 군, 저 큰 망치를 좀 건네주게.\"\n\n일등항해사가 망치를 가지러 간 사이, 에이해브는 아무 말 없이 그 금화를 윗옷 자락에 천천히 문지르고 있었다. 마치 그 광택을 더하려는 듯이. 그러면서도 아무 말 없이 그동안 낮게 콧노래를 흥얼거리고 있었는데, 그 소리가 어찌나 기이하게 먹먹하고 또렷하지 않았던지, 마치 그의 몸속에서 생명력의 톱니바퀴가 기계처럼 돌아가며 내는 소리 같았다.\n\n스타벅에게서 큰 망치를 받아 든 그는, 한 손에는 망치를 치켜들고 다른 손으로는 금화를 내보이며 큰 돛대 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그리고 한껏 높인 목소리로 외쳤다. \"그대들 가운데 누구든 주름진 이마와 비뚤어진 턱을 가진 흰머리 고래를 내게 발견해 주는 자, 그대들 가운데 누구든 오른쪽 꼬리지느러미에 구멍 세 개가 뚫린 그 흰머리 고래를 내게 발견해 주는 자 — 보라, 그대들 가운데 누구든 그 흰 고래를 내게 발견해 주는 자, 그자는 이 금화 한 닢을 차지하리라, 제군들!\"\n\n\"만세! 만세!\" 선원들이 외치며, 방수모를 흔들어 금화를 돛대에 박는 그 행동에 환호를 보냈다.\n\n\"흰 고래다, 똑똑히 말하노라.\" 에이해브가 큰 망치를 내던지며 말을 이었다. \"흰 고래다. 눈에 불을 켜고 그놈을 찾아라, 제군들. 흰 물보라를 날카롭게 살펴라. 거품 하나라도 보이거든 소리쳐 알려라.\"\n\n이러는 내내 태시테고(Tashtego)와 대구(Daggoo), 그리고 퀴케그(Queequeg)는 다른 누구보다도 더 골똘한 관심과 놀라움으로 지켜보고 있었다. 주름진 이마와 비뚤어진 턱이라는 말이 나오자, 세 사람은 저마다 어떤 특정한 기억에 따로따로 건드려진 듯 움찔했다.\n\n\"에이해브 선장님,\" 태시테고가 말했다. \"그 흰 고래는 어떤 이들이 모비 딕(Moby Dick)이라 부르는 바로 그놈이 틀림없습니다.\"\n\n\"모비 딕이라고?\" 에이해브가 소리쳤다. \"그럼 자네는 그 흰 고래를 아나, 태시?\"\n\n\"녀석은 물속으로 가라앉기 전에 꼬리를 좀 묘하게 부채처럼 펼치지 않습니까, 선장님?\" 게이헤더 출신 태시테고가 신중하게 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 물 뿜는 모양도 묘하지 않습니까,\" 대구가 말했다. \"향유고래치고도 유난히 무성하고, 또 엄청나게 빠르지요, 에이해브 선장님?\"\n\n\"그리고 녀석 가죽에 한 개, 두 개, 세 개 — 오! 작살이 아주 많이 박혀 있어요, 선장님.\" 퀴케그가 띄엄띄엄 외쳤다. \"전부 비비 꼬여서 비틀려 있는데, 꼭 이놈 — 이놈 —\" 그는 적당한 말을 찾느라 몹시 애를 먹으며, 병마개를 따듯 손을 빙빙 돌렸다. \"꼭 이놈 — 이놈 —\"\n\n\"타래송곳!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"그래, 퀴케그, 작살들이 녀석 몸에 온통 비틀리고 뒤틀린 채 박혀 있지. 그래, 대구, 녀석의 물줄기는 큼직해서, 마치 밀단을 통째로 쌓은 것 같고, 해마다 큰 양털 깎기를 마친 뒤의 우리 낸터킷(Nantucket) 양털 무더기처럼 새하얗지. 그래, 태시테고, 녀석은 돌풍에 찢긴 삼각돛처럼 꼬리를 부채꼴로 펼치지. 죽음과 악마여! 제군들, 그대들이 본 것은 모비 딕이다 — 모비 딕 — 모비 딕이란 말이다!\"\n\n\"에이해브 선장님,\" 스타벅이 말했다. 그는 스터브와 플라스크와 더불어 여태껏 점점 커지는 놀라움으로 상관을 지켜보고 있었으나, 마침내 그 모든 의아함을 어느 정도 풀어 주는 어떤 생각에 부딪힌 듯했다. \"에이해브 선장님, 저도 모비 딕에 관해 들은 바가 있습니다 — 그러나 선장님의 다리를 앗아간 것이 모비 딕은 아니었지요?\"\n\n\"누가 자네한테 그런 소리를 했나?\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. 그러고는 잠시 말을 멈췄다가, \"그래, 스타벅. 그래, 사방에 둘러선 나의 친구들이여. 나를 돛대 잃은 배처럼 만든 것이 바로 모비 딕이었다. 지금 내가 디디고 선 이 죽은 그루터기로 나를 끌어내린 것이 바로 모비 딕이었어. 그래, 그래.\" 그는 가슴을 얻어맞은 큰 사슴의 울음 같은, 무시무시하고 우렁찬 짐승의 흐느낌으로 소리쳤다. \"그래, 그렇다! 나를 깡그리 허물어뜨린 것이 그 저주받은 흰 고래였어. 나를 영원토록 의족이나 끼고 비틀거리는 가련한 병신으로 만든 것이!\" 그러더니 그는 두 팔을 내던지며, 한없는 저주를 퍼부으며 외쳤다. \"그래, 그렇다! 나는 녀석을 희망봉을 돌아서도, 혼곶을 돌아서도, 노르웨이 큰 소용돌이를 돌아서도, 지옥의 불길을 돌아서라도 쫓아갈 테다. 녀석을 포기하느니 차라리. 그리고 이것이 바로 그대들이 배에 오른 까닭이다, 제군들! 그 흰 고래를 육지의 이쪽저쪽에서, 또 이 땅의 사방팔방에서 쫓아가, 녀석이 검은 피를 뿜으며 지느러미를 위로 한 채 뒤집힐 때까지 쫓는 것이다. 어떤가, 제군들, 이 일에 손을 맞잡고 함께하겠나? 보아하니 그대들은 용감해 보이는군.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 그렇습니다!\" 작살잡이들과 선원들이 외치며, 흥분한 노인 가까이로 바싹 다가섰다. \"흰 고래를 향한 날카로운 눈! 모비 딕을 향한 날카로운 창!\"\n\n\"하느님께서 그대들을 축복하시길.\" 그가 반쯤 흐느끼며, 반쯤 외치는 듯이 말했다. \"하느님께서 그대들을 축복하시길, 제군들. 급사! 가서 큰 통의 그로그주를 따라 오너라. 한데 그 긴 얼굴은 무슨 까닭인가, 스타벅 군. 자네는 그 흰 고래를 쫓지 않겠다는 건가? 모비 딕을 상대할 배짱이 없는 건가?\"\n\n\"녀석의 비뚤어진 턱이라도, 또 죽음의 아가리라도 저는 마다하지 않습니다, 에이해브 선장님. 그것이 우리가 좇는 일의 떳떳한 길목에 들어선다면 말입니다. 그러나 저는 고래를 사냥하러 여기 왔지, 선장님의 복수를 위해 온 것이 아닙니다. 설령 그 복수를 이룬다 한들, 선장님의 복수가 기름을 몇 통이나 내주겠습니까, 에이해브 선장님? 우리 낸터킷 시장에서는 그것으로 큰돈을 받지 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"낸터킷 시장이라고! 흥! 한데 더 가까이 와 보게, 스타벅. 자네에게는 좀 더 밑바닥에 깔린 속내가 필요하군. 돈이 척도가 되어야 한다면, 그리고 회계사들이 이 지구라는 거대한 회계소의 셈을 매기되, 4분의 1인치마다 기니 금화 한 닢씩으로 그 둘레를 빙 둘러 계산한다면, 내 자네에게 말하지, 나의 복수는 바로 이곳에서 막대한 웃돈을 받아 낼 걸세!\" 그는 자신의 가슴을 쳤다.\n\n\"가슴을 치는군.\" 스터브가 속삭였다. \"저건 무슨 뜻이지? 내 보기엔 어마어마하게 울려 퍼지긴 하는데, 텅 빈 소리야.\"\n\n\"말 못 하는 짐승에게 복수라니!\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"녀석은 그저 가장 눈먼 본능에 따라 선장님을 들이받았을 뿐입니다! 미친 짓입니다! 말 못 하는 것에게 분노한다는 것은, 에이해브 선장님, 신성모독으로 여겨집니다.\"\n\n\"다시 한번 내 말을 들어 보게 — 그 좀 더 밑바닥에 깔린 속내를. 눈에 보이는 모든 사물이란, 이 사람아, 한낱 두꺼운 종이 가면에 지나지 않아. 그러나 모든 사건 속에서 — 살아 있는 행위 속에서, 의심할 바 없는 그 행동 속에서 — 거기, 알 수 없으나 여전히 이성을 지닌 무언가가, 이성 없는 가면 뒤에서 제 모습의 윤곽을 내밀고 있단 말이지. 사람이 내리쳐야 한다면, 그 가면을 꿰뚫어 내리쳐야 해! 갇힌 자가 벽을 뚫고 나아가지 않고서야 어떻게 바깥에 닿을 수 있겠나? 내게는 그 흰 고래가 바로 그 벽이야, 내 코앞에 바싹 들이밀어진. 때로는 그 너머에 아무것도 없으리라는 생각도 드네. 그러나 그것으로 족해. 녀석은 나를 못살게 굴고, 나를 짓눌러. 나는 녀석에게서 터무니없는 힘을 보고, 그것을 떠받치는 헤아릴 수 없는 악의를 보네. 내가 무엇보다도 미워하는 것은 바로 그 헤아릴 수 없는 것이야. 그러니 그 흰 고래가 앞잡이든, 아니면 그 흰 고래가 장본인이든, 나는 그 미움을 녀석에게 퍼부어 줄 테다. 내게 신성모독이라는 말일랑 꺼내지 말게, 이 사람아. 나를 모욕한다면 나는 태양이라도 내리칠 걸세. 태양이 그럴 수 있다면, 나도 그 반대로 할 수 있는 것이니. 여기에는 늘 일종의 공정한 승부라는 것이 있어서, 질투가 모든 창조물을 다스리고 있으니 말이야. 그러나 이 사람아, 그 공정한 승부조차 내 주인은 아니야. 나의 위에 누가 있단 말인가? 진리에는 경계가 없네. 그 눈을 치우게! 악마의 노려봄보다 더 견디기 힘든 것이 바로 멍청한 응시야! 그래, 그래. 자네가 붉어졌다 창백해졌다 하는군. 내 열기가 자네를 녹여 분노의 불빛으로 만들었어. 그러나 보게, 스타벅, 열에 들떠 한 말은 그 말 스스로가 도로 거두어들이는 법이야. 더운 말 한마디쯤은 작은 모욕도 못 되는 사람들이 있지. 자네를 화나게 하려던 것은 아니었네. 그 일은 그냥 흘려보내게. 보게! 저기 얼룩진 황갈색의 튀르크인 같은 뺨을 보게 — 태양이 그려 놓은, 살아 숨 쉬는 그림이지. 이교도의 표범들 — 헤아릴 줄도, 경배할 줄도 모르면서 살아가는 것들, 자기가 느끼는 그 뜨거운 삶에 대해 아무런 까닭도 대지 않고 구하며 살아가는 것들 말이야! 선원들이야, 이 사람아, 저 선원들! 저들은 이 고래의 일에서 하나같이 에이해브와 한마음이 아니던가? 스터브를 보게! 저자는 웃고 있어! 저기 저 칠레 사람을 보게! 그 생각만으로도 콧김을 내뿜고 있지 않나. 스타벅, 온 천지를 휩쓰는 이 태풍 속에서 자네의 외로이 흔들리는 어린나무 한 그루로는 버텨 설 수가 없네! 한데 그게 무어란 말인가? 한번 헤아려 보게. 그저 지느러미 하나 찌르는 일을 돕는 것일 뿐이야. 스타벅에게야 무슨 놀랄 만한 일도 아니지. 그게 그 이상 무어란 말인가? 그렇다면 이 한 번의 변변찮은 사냥에서, 낸터킷을 통틀어 으뜸가는 창잡이가, 앞돛대의 일꾼들이 저마다 숫돌을 거머쥐고 있는 마당에, 설마 뒤로 물러나지는 않겠지? 아! 어떤 속박이 자네를 옭아매는군, 보여! 큰 물결이 자네를 들어 올리고 있어! 말하게, 어서 말하게! — 그래, 그렇다! 그렇다면 자네의 침묵, 그것이 자네를 대변하는군. (혼잣말로) 부풀어 오른 내 콧구멍에서 무언가가 뿜어져 나왔고, 저자가 그것을 제 폐 속으로 들이마셨어. 스타벅은 이제 내 것이야. 이제는 반역하지 않고서는 나를 거스를 수 없지.\"\n\n\"하느님, 저를 지켜 주소서! — 저희 모두를 지켜 주소서!\" 스타벅이 나직이 중얼거렸다.\n\n그러나 일등항해사의, 마치 홀린 듯한 그 말 없는 묵종에 기뻐한 나머지 에이해브는 그 불길한 기도 소리를 듣지 못했다. 선창에서 새어 나오는 나직한 웃음소리도, 삭구를 스치는 바람의 불길한 떨림도, 한순간 모두의 가슴이 철렁 내려앉던 그때 돛이 돛대를 때리는 텅 빈 펄럭임 소리도 듣지 못했다. 다시금 스타벅의 내리깐 눈에 삶의 완강함이 불을 밝혔고, 땅 밑에서 울리던 웃음은 잦아들었으며, 바람은 계속 불었고, 돛은 다시 부풀어 올랐고, 배는 전과 다름없이 출렁이며 흔들렸으니 말이다. 아, 그대들 훈계와 경고여! 어찌하여 그대들은 찾아왔다가도 머물지 않는가? 그대들 그림자들이여, 그러나 그대들은 경고라기보다는 차라리 예언이로다! 그러나 그것도 바깥에서 오는 예언이라기보다는, 이미 안에 깃들어 있던 것들에 대한 확증이라 하겠다. 우리를 옭아맬 외부의 힘이 거의 없을 때라도, 우리 존재의 가장 깊은 속에 자리한 필연들, 바로 그것들이 여전히 우리를 몰아가는 까닭이다.\n\n\"술이다! 술을 가져와라!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다.\n\n찰랑찰랑 가득 찬 백랍 잔을 받아 든 그는, 작살잡이들 쪽으로 돌아서서 그들에게 무기를 가져오라고 명했다. 그러고는 작살을 손에 든 그들을 양묘기 가까이 자기 앞에 늘어세우고, 세 명의 항해사는 창을 든 채 그의 곁에 서게 했으며, 나머지 선원들은 그 무리 둘레로 둥글게 원을 이루게 했다. 그는 한순간 선원 한 사람 한 사람을 샅샅이 훑어보며 서 있었다. 그러나 그 사나운 눈들이 그의 눈과 마주친 모습은, 우두머리가 들소의 자취를 좇아 앞장서서 내달리기 직전, 핏발 선 눈의 초원 이리들이 그 우두머리의 눈과 마주치는 광경과도 같았다 — 그러나, 아아! 그저 인디언이 숨겨 둔 덫에 걸려들고 마는 신세일 뿐이건만.\n\n\"마시고 돌려라!\" 그는 가장 가까이 선 선원에게 묵직하게 가득 찬 술병을 건네며 외쳤다. \"이제 선원들만 마신다. 빙 둘러라, 빙! 짧게 들이켜고 — 길게 삼켜라, 제군들. 사탄의 발굽처럼 뜨거운 놈이지. 그래, 그래. 아주 잘도 돌아가는구나. 그것이 그대들 속에서 소용돌이치고, 뱀처럼 번뜩이는 눈으로 갈래져 솟구치는군. 잘했다. 거의 비워졌어. 저쪽으로 갔다가 이쪽으로 돌아오는구나. 그것을 내게 다오 — 이건 빈 통이로군! 제군들, 그대들은 마치 세월 같구나. 이토록 가득 차오른 생명이 단숨에 들이켜져 사라지니. 급사, 다시 채워라!\n\n\"이제 잘 들어라, 나의 용사들이여. 내가 그대들 모두를 이 양묘기 둘레로 불러 모았다. 항해사들이여, 그대들의 창으로 내 양옆을 지켜라. 작살잡이들이여, 그대들의 작살을 들고 거기 서라. 그리고 그대들, 굳센 선원들이여, 나를 빙 둘러싸라. 나보다 앞서 살았던 어부 조상들의 고귀한 관습을 내 이렇게나마 되살려 보려는 것이니. 오, 제군들, 그대들은 머잖아 보게 될 것이다 — 하! 이놈, 벌써 돌아왔느냐? 못된 동전도 이보다 빨리 굴러오진 않겠다. 그것을 내게 다오. 한데 이런, 이 백랍 잔이 다시 넘치도록 채워졌겠구나, 네놈이 그 성 비투스 무도병에 걸린 도깨비만 아니었던들 — 썩 물러가라, 이 학질 같은 놈아!\n\n\"앞으로 나오라, 항해사들이여! 그대들의 창을 내 앞에 가득히 엇갈리게 하라. 잘했다! 그 축을 내 만져 보게 하라.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 팔을 뻗어, 수평으로 사방을 향해 뻗은 세 자루의 창을 그 엇갈린 중심에서 움켜쥐었다. 그러면서 별안간 신경질적으로 그것들을 홱홱 잡아당겼다. 그러는 동안 그는 스타벅에게서 스터브에게로, 스터브에게서 플라스크에게로 골똘히 눈길을 던졌다. 마치 어떤 이름 붙일 수 없는 내면의 의지로, 자신의 자성 어린 생명력이 깃든 라이덴병 속에 쌓인 그 똑같은 불같은 격정을 그들 속으로 충격을 가하듯 흘려보내고 싶어 하는 듯했다. 세 항해사는 그의 강렬하고 흔들림 없는, 신비로운 모습 앞에서 움츠러들었다. 스터브와 플라스크는 그에게서 곁눈질로 눈을 돌렸고, 스타벅의 정직한 눈은 곧장 아래로 떨어졌다.\n\n\"헛수고로구나!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"그러나, 어쩌면, 그편이 나으리라. 그대들 셋이 단 한 번이라도 그 충격을 온전히 받았더라면, 그때 내 안의 그 전기 같은 것이, 어쩌면 내게서 다 빠져나가 버렸을지도 모르니. 어쩌면 그것이 그대들을 그 자리에서 죽게 만들었을지도 모르지. 어쩌면 그대들에게는 그것이 필요 없을지도. 창을 내려라! 자, 이제 항해사들이여, 내 그대들 셋을 저기 저 나의 이교도 형제 셋의 술잔지기로 임명하노라 — 저기 저 더없이 존귀한 신사이자 귀족인 세 사람, 나의 용맹한 작살잡이들 말이다. 그 일이 하찮다 여기는가? 무어라, 위대한 교황께서도 거지들의 발을 씻겨 주시며, 당신의 삼중관을 물그릇 삼아 쓰시거늘? 오, 나의 어여쁜 추기경들이여! 바로 그대들 자신의 겸양이, 그것이 그대들을 그 일에 굽히게 하리라. 내 그대들에게 명하는 것이 아니다. 그대들이 스스로 그리 원하는 것이지. 묶음줄을 끊고 자루를 뽑아라, 작살잡이들이여!\"\n\n말없이 그 명령에 따라, 세 작살잡이는 이제 자루에서 떼어 낸, 길이 석 자쯤 되는 작살의 쇠 부분을, 미늘을 위로 한 채 그의 앞에 들고 섰다.\n\n\"그 날카로운 강철로 나를 찌르지는 마라! 그것을 기울여라, 거꾸로 기울이란 말이다! 잔으로 쓸 끝이 어느 쪽인지 모르느냐? 그 자루 끼우는 구멍을 위로 향하게 하라! 그래, 그래. 자, 술잔지기들이여, 앞으로 나오라. 그 작살들을! 그것을 받아 들어라. 내가 채우는 동안 단단히 붙들고 있어라!\" 그러고는 곧바로, 한 장교에게서 다른 장교에게로 천천히 옮겨 다니며, 백랍 잔에 든 불같은 물로 작살의 자루 구멍을 찰랑찰랑 채웠다.\n\n\"이제 셋 대 셋으로 그대들이 마주 섰구나. 이 살인의 성배를 받들어라! 그것을 건네라, 이제 이 풀 수 없는 맹약의 당사자가 된 그대들이여. 하! 스타벅! 그러나 일은 이미 이루어졌도다! 저기 저 비준하는 태양이 이제 그 위에 앉으려 기다리고 있으니. 마셔라, 작살잡이들이여! 마시고 맹세하라, 죽음을 부르는 고래잡이 보트의 뱃머리를 책임지는 그대들이여 — 모비 딕에게 죽음을! 우리가 모비 딕을 죽음으로 몰아 사냥하지 못한다면, 하느님께서 우리 모두를 사냥하시기를!\" 길고 미늘 박힌 강철 잔들이 치켜올려졌다. 그리고 흰 고래를 향한 외침과 저주와 더불어, 그 독한 술이 일제히 쉭 소리를 내며 단숨에 들이켜졌다. 스타벅은 창백해지더니, 돌아서서 몸을 떨었다. 다시 한번, 그리고 마지막으로, 새로 채워진 백랍 잔이 광란에 빠진 선원들 사이를 돌았다. 이윽고 에이해브가 한 손을 들어 그들에게 흔들어 보이자, 모두가 흩어졌고, 그는 자신의 선실로 물러갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 37. Sunset.\n\n선실, 고물 쪽 창가. 에이해브가 홀로 앉아 바깥을 응시하고 있다.\n\n나는 희고 탁한 항적을 남긴다. 창백한 물결, 더 창백한 뺨, 내가 어디로 항해하든 그러하다. 시기에 찬 큰 물결이 내 지나간 자리를 삼키려 비스듬히 부풀어 오른다. 그러라지. 하지만 먼저 내가 지나가고 나서다.\n\n저기, 늘 넘칠 듯 가득 찬 술잔의 가장자리에서, 따뜻한 물결이 포도주처럼 붉게 물든다. 황금빛 이마가 푸른 바다를 가늠하며 잠겨 든다. 잠수부 같은 태양이 — 정오부터 천천히 자맥질해 내려온 그 태양이 — 가라앉는다. 한데 내 영혼은 솟아오른다! 영혼은 그 끝없는 언덕에 지쳐 있다. 그렇다면 내가 쓰고 있는 이 관(冠)이 너무 무거운 것인가? 이 롬바르디아의 철관(鐵冠)이. 그래도 그것은 수많은 보석으로 빛난다. 그것을 쓰고 있는 나는 그 머나먼 광채를 보지 못하지만, 눈부시게 사람을 어지럽히는 그 무언가를 쓰고 있음을 어렴풋이 느낀다. 그것은 쇠다 — 그것을 나는 안다 — 금이 아니다. 또한 그것은 쪼개져 있다 — 그것을 나는 느낀다. 들쭉날쭉한 모서리가 어찌나 나를 쓰리게 하는지, 내 뇌가 그 단단한 쇠를 향해 쿵쿵 부딪치는 듯하다. 그렇다, 강철 두개골, 내 것이다. 뇌가 으스러지는 가장 격렬한 싸움에서도 투구 따위 필요 없는 그런 두개골 말이다!\n\n내 이마에 메마른 열기가 도는가? 오! 일찍이 한때는, 해돋이가 나를 고귀하게 분기시켰듯, 해넘이는 나를 달래 주던 시절이 있었다. 이제는 그렇지 않다. 이 사랑스러운 빛은, 나를 비추지 않는다. 온갖 아름다움이 내게는 고통이니, 나는 결코 그것을 누릴 수 없는 까닭이다. 높은 통찰력은 타고났으나, 나는 낮은 것, 곧 누리는 능력이 없다. 저주받았다, 더없이 교묘하고 더없이 악의적으로! 낙원 한복판에서 저주받았다! 잘 자라 — 잘 자라! (손을 흔들며 창가에서 물러난다.)\n\n그리 어려운 일은 아니었다. 적어도 한 놈쯤은 완고한 자가 있으리라 여겼건만, 톱니 박힌 내 둥근 바퀴 하나가 그들 각양각색의 바퀴 모두에 꼭 들어맞으니, 그들은 따라 돈다. 아니면 이렇게 말해도 좋다, 그들은 화약 더미를 쌓은 개미집처럼 모조리 내 앞에 늘어서 있고, 나는 그것들에 불을 댕길 성냥이다. 오, 모진 일이로다! 남들에게 불을 붙이려면, 성냥 그 자신이 다 타 없어져야만 한다니! 내가 감행한 것을 나는 의지했고, 내가 의지한 것을 나는 행하리라! 그들은 나를 미쳤다 여긴다 — 스타벅(Starbuck)이 그렇다. 하지만 나는 악귀에 들렸다, 나는 광기가 더욱 미쳐 날뛰는 것이다! 오직 제 자신을 깨닫기 위해서만 잔잔해지는 그 사나운 광기 말이다! 예언인즉 내가 사지를 잃으리라 하였으니 — 그렇다! 나는 이 다리를 잃었다. 이제 나는 예언하노라, 내가 나를 토막 낸 그놈을 토막 내리라고. 자, 그렇다면 예언하는 자와 이루는 자가 하나가 되게 하라. 그것은 그대들, 위대한 신들이여, 일찍이 그대들이 이루었던 것보다 더한 것이다. 나는 그대들을 비웃고 야유한다, 그대들 크리켓 선수들이여, 그대들 권투꾼들이여, 그대들 귀먹은 버크와 눈먼 벤디고들이여! 나는 학생들이 골목대장에게 하듯 말하지 않으리라 — 네 덩치에 맞는 놈이나 골라라, 나를 두들기지 마! 아니다, 그대들은 나를 쓰러뜨렸으나, 나는 다시 일어섰다. 한데 그대들은 도망쳐 몸을 숨겼다. 그 솜뭉치 자루 뒤에서 썩 나오라! 나는 그대들에게 닿을 장총이 없으니. 오라, 에이해브의 안부를 그대들에게 전하노라. 와서 그대들이 나를 빗나가게 할 수 있는지 보라. 나를 빗나가게 한다고? 그대들은 나를 빗나가게 할 수 없으니, 그러려다간 그대들 자신을 빗나가게 할 뿐이다! 거기서는 인간이 그대들을 이긴다. 나를 빗나가게 한다고? 내 확고한 목적으로 가는 길에는 무쇠 궤도가 놓여 있고, 내 영혼은 그 위를 달리도록 홈이 파여 있다. 깊이를 알 수 없는 협곡을 넘어, 산들의 갈라진 심장을 꿰뚫고, 격류의 바닥 밑을 지나, 나는 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 내달린다! 그 무쇠 길에는 그 무엇도 장애가 아니며, 그 무엇도 굽이가 아니다!\n\n내 영혼은 적수를 넘어선 무엇과 맞붙었다. 압도당했다. 그것도 미치광이에게! 견딜 수 없는 가시 같은 일이로다, 멀쩡한 정신이 이런 싸움터에서 무기를 내려놓아야 하다니! 하지만 그는 깊숙이 파고들어, 내 안의 이성을 모조리 폭파해 버렸다! 나는 그의 불경한 최후가 어렴풋이 보이는 듯하다. 그러나 내가 그를 그 길로 도와야 하리라는 것을 느낀다. 원하든 원치 않든, 그 형언할 수 없는 무엇이 나를 그에게 동여매었다. 끊을 칼조차 없는 밧줄로 나를 끌어당긴다. 끔찍한 늙은이여! 누가 자기 위에 있느냐고 그는 외친다. 그렇다, 그는 자기 위에 있는 모든 것에는 평등을 외치는 민주주의자가 되려 하면서, 보라, 자기 아래에 있는 모든 것 위에는 어찌 그리 군림하는가! 오! 나는 내 비참한 직분을 똑똑히 본다. 반항하면서도 복종하는 일, 더 나쁘게는 연민이 섞인 채로 미워하는 일! 그의 눈 속에서 나는 어떤 음울한 비애를 읽으니, 내가 그것을 지녔더라면 나를 바싹 오그라들게 했을 것이다. 그래도 희망은 있다. 세월과 조수는 넓게 흐른다. 저 가증스러운 고래에게는 작은 금붕어가 유리 어항을 가지듯, 헤엄칠 둥근 바다 세계가 통째로 있다. 하늘을 모욕하는 그의 목적을, 하느님이 옆으로 밀쳐 버리실지도 모른다. 마음이 납덩이만 같지 않다면 나는 기운을 차려 보련만. 그러나 내 시계는 통째로 다 풀려 멈춰 버렸다. 온 시계를 다스리는 추인 내 마음을, 다시 감아올릴 열쇠가 내게는 없다.\n\n[앞갑판에서 흥청거리는 떠들썩한 소리가 터져 나온다.]\n\n오, 하느님! 인간 어미의 정이라곤 거의 깃들지 않은 저런 이교도 선원들과 더불어 항해를 하다니! 어디선가 상어 같은 바다가 낳아 놓은 것들이로구나. 흰고래는 저들의 반신반귀(半神半鬼)다. 들어 보라! 저 지옥 같은 광란을! 저 흥청거림은 앞쪽에 있다! 고물 쪽의 흔들림 없는 침묵을 보라! 내게는 그것이 인생을 그려 보이는 듯하다. 반짝이는 바다를 가르며 가장 앞장서 나아가는 것은, 흥겹고 무장한 듯 위풍당당하며 빈정대는 뱃머리지만, 그것은 다만 어두운 에이해브를 뒤에 끌고 갈 뿐이니, 그는 항적의 죽은 물 위에 지어진 고물 쪽 선실 안에 틀어박혀 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있고, 더 멀리 뒤로는 늑대처럼 부글거리는 물소리에 쫓기고 있다. 저 길게 우짖는 소리가 나를 온통 전율케 한다! 그만들 하라! 흥청대는 자들이여, 당직을 세워라! 오, 인생이여! 영혼이 짓눌린 채 깨달음에 붙들리는 — 마치 야생의 길들지 않은 짐승들이 억지로 먹이를 받아먹도록 내몰리듯 하는 — 바로 이런 순간이로다, 오, 인생이여! 너 속에 잠재한 공포를 내가 느끼는 것은 바로 지금이다! 그러나 그것은 내가 아니다! 그 공포는 내 바깥에 있다! 그리고 내 안에 깃든 인간의 부드러운 감정을 안고서, 그래도 나는 너희와 맞서 싸워 보리라, 너희 음산한 환영 같은 미래들이여! 내 곁에 서라, 나를 붙들라, 나를 동여매라, 오, 너희 축복받은 힘들이여!\n\n하! 하! 하! 하! 흠! 목청 좀 가다듬자!—그때부터 줄곧 그 일을 곱씹어 왔는데, 결국 도달한 결말이 그 하, 하는 웃음이다. 왜냐고? 웃음이야말로 기이한 모든 것에 대한 가장 현명하고 손쉬운 대답이니까. 게다가 무슨 일이 닥치든 위안 하나는 늘 남는 법이다—그 변치 않는 위안이란, 모든 게 다 예정되어 있다는 것이다. 그가 스타벅(Starbuck)과 나눈 이야기를 다 듣지는 못했다. 하지만 내 보잘것없는 눈에 그때 스타벅의 표정은 며칠 전 저녁의 내 심정과 어딘가 닮아 보였다. 틀림없이 저 늙은 무굴 황제가 그도 단단히 옭아맸을 것이다. 나는 그걸 알아챘다, 알고 있었다. 그런 재주가 내게 있었다면 쉽사리 예언이라도 했을 것이다—그의 두개골에 눈길이 꽂히던 순간 나는 그걸 보았으니까. 그래, 스텁(Stubb), 현명한 스텁—이게 내 칭호지—그래, 스텁, 그게 어쨌다는 거냐, 스텁? 여기 송장 하나가 있다. 앞으로 닥칠 모든 일을 다 알지는 못하지만, 그것이 무엇이든 나는 웃으며 맞이하러 가련다. 너희들의 그 온갖 끔찍한 것들 속에 숨어 있는 장난스러운 곁눈질이라니! 어쩐지 우습다. 파, 라! 리라, 스키라! 고향에 두고 온 내 탱탱하고 작은 배(梨) 같은 그 애는 지금 무얼 하고 있을까? 눈이 퉁퉁 붓도록 울고 있으려나?—갓 들어온 작살잡이들에게 잔치라도 베풀고 있겠지, 군함의 깃발처럼 흥겹게, 나도 그러하니—파, 라! 리라, 스키라! 오—\n\n오늘 밤 우리는 가벼운 마음으로 마시리, 사랑을 위하여, 잔 가장자리를 헤엄치다가 입술이 닿는 순간 터져 버리는 거품처럼 흥겹고 덧없이.\n\n멋들어진 한 가락이다—누가 부르나? 스타벅 씨? 예, 예, 알겠습니다—(혼잣말로) 저 사람은 내 상관이지, 헌데 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 그에게도 제 상관이 따로 있지.—예, 예, 알겠습니다, 이 일만 마저 끝내고—갑니다.\n\n# HARPOONEERS AND SAILORS.\n\n작살잡이들과 선원들.\n(앞돛이 솟아오르며, 당직 선원들이 서 있고, 기대어 있고, 비스듬히 누워 있고, 가지각색 자세로 모두 합창하는 모습이 드러난다.)\n\n안녕히, 잘 가시오, 스페인 아가씨들이여! 안녕히, 잘 가시오,\n스페인의 아가씨들이여! 우리 선장이 명을 내리셨으니—\n\n낸터킷 선원 1. 야, 친구들, 너무 감상에 젖지 마라. 소화에 안 좋아! 강장제 한잔이다, 날 따라 해!\n(노래하자, 모두 따라 부른다.)\n\n우리 선장이 갑판 위에 서서, 손에 망원경을 들고, 물줄기마다\n\n물을 뿜어 올리는 저 늠름한 고래들을 살펴보았네. 오, 보트의 통을\n챙겨라, 얘들아, 아딧줄 곁에 서라, 그러면 저 멋진 고래들 가운데\n\n한 놈을 잡으리니, 손에 손을 맞잡고, 얘들아! 그러니, 기운을 내라,\n녀석들아! 그대들의 마음이 결코 꺾이지 않기를! 용맹한 작살잡이가\n\n고래를 내리꽂는 동안!\n뒷갑판에서 들려오는 항해사의 목소리. 거기 앞쪽, 여덟 점 종이다!\n\n낸터킷 선원 2. 합창을 멈춰라! 여덟 점 종이야! 들리느냐, 종지기 소년아? 종을 여덟 번 쳐라, 핍 너 말이다! 이 까만 녀석아! 그리고 내가 당직을 부르게 해 다오. 나야말로 그 일에 알맞은 입을 가졌지—큰 술통 같은 입을 말이야. 자, 자, (승강구 아래로 머리를 들이밀며) 좌현 당직들, 어이! 거기 아래, 여덟 점 종이다! 굴러 나와라!\n네덜란드 선원. 오늘 밤 한숨 푹 자기 딱 좋겠는걸, 친구. 그러기 살진 밤이야. 우리 늙은 모굴의 포도주 탓이라고 봐. 그건 어떤 이에게는 사람 죽이는 약이고, 어떤 이에게는 손가락 튕기듯 자극이 되거든. 우리는 노래하고, 저들은 잠들지—그래, 저기 바닥에 드러누웠군, 맨 아래층에 쟁여 둔 술통처럼 말이야. 다시 깨워 보자고! 자, 이 구리 펌프를 받아라, 이걸 통해 외쳐 봐. 제 계집 꿈일랑 그만 꾸라고 일러. 부활의 날이라고, 마지막 입맞춤을 하고 심판대 앞에 나오라고 일러. 그렇지—바로 그거야. 네 목청은 암스테르담 버터를 먹어 망가진 데가 없구나.\n\n프랑스 선원. 쉿, 얘들아! 담요 만(灣)에 닻을 내리고 잠들기 전에 지그춤이나 한두 번 추자. 어때? 저기 다른 당직이 오는군. 다들 다리를 준비해라! 핍! 꼬마 핍! 탬버린을 들고 한번 신나게 놀아 보자!\n\n핍. (뚱하니 졸린 채로.) 그게 어디 있는지 모르겠어요.\n\n프랑스 선원. 그럼 네 배라도 두드리고, 귀라도 흔들어라. 추라니까, 다들. 흥겨운 게 제일이다, 만세! 빌어먹을, 춤 안 출 거야? 자, 이제, 인디언처럼 한 줄로 늘어서서, 더블 셔플로 신나게 내달려 보자! 몸을 던져라! 다리! 다리!\n\n아이슬란드 선원. 자네 마룻바닥이 영 마음에 안 드는군, 친구. 내 취향엔 너무 물렁물렁해. 난 얼음 바닥에 익숙하거든. 이런 일에 찬물을 끼얹어 미안하네만, 양해해 주게.\n\n몰타 선원. 나도 그래. 자네들 계집애들은 어딨나? 바보가 아니고서야 제 오른손으로 제 왼손을 잡고는 혼잣말로 '안녕하세요?' 하고 인사하겠나? 짝이다! 난 짝이 있어야겠어!\n\n시칠리아 선원. 그래, 계집애들과 풀밭이 있어야지!—그럼 자네들과 한바탕 뛰어 보겠네. 그래, 메뚜기로 변신이라도 하지!\n\n롱아일랜드 선원. 자, 자, 이 뚱한 녀석들아, 우리는 얼마든지 더 있어. 옥수수밭은 김맬 수 있을 때 매라, 나는 그렇게 말한다네. 머지않아 모든 다리들이 추수하러 나갈 테니까. 아! 저기 음악이 오는군. 자, 이제 시작이다!\n\n아조레스 선원. (올라오면서, 탬버린을 승강구 위로 휙 던져 올리며.) 자, 여기 있다, 핍. 그리고 저기 양묘기 기둥이 있으니, 거기 올라타라! 자, 얘들아! (그들 중 절반이 탬버린에 맞춰 춤춘다. 더러는 아래로 내려가고, 더러는 잠들거나 밧줄 사리 사이에 드러눕는다. 욕설이 푸짐하다.)\n\n아조레스 선원. (춤추며) 가자, 핍! 두드려라, 종지기 소년아! 흔들고, 쑤시고, 찌르고, 까불어라, 종지기 소년아! 반딧불을 만들어라, 그 딸랑이들을 깨부숴라!\n\n핍. 딸랑이라고요?—저기 또 하나 떨어져 나가네요, 내가 어찌나 세게 두드리는지.\n\n중국 선원. 그럼 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 마구 두드려라. 네 몸을 탑(塔) 하나로 만들어 봐.\n\n프랑스 선원. 신나게 미쳤구나! 핍, 네 굴렁쇠를 높이 들어라, 내가 그걸 통과해 뛰어넘게! 삼각돛을 찢어라! 몸을 박살 내듯 뛰어라!\n\n태시테고. (조용히 담배를 피우며.) 저게 백인이지. 저런 걸 재미라고 부르는군. 흥! 나는 내 땀이나 아껴 두겠어.\n\n늙은 맨섬 선원. 저 흥겨운 녀석들이 자기네가 무엇 위에서 춤추고 있는지 한 번이라도 생각이나 해 봤을까. 내가 네 무덤 위에서 춤추겠다, 그렇고말고—그게 모퉁이를 돌 때마다 역풍처럼 들이치는 너희 밤 계집들의 가장 모진 저주가 아니던가. 오, 그리스도여! 저 푸른 함대와 푸른 해골이 된 선원들을 생각하면! 흠, 흠. 아마 온 세상이 너희 학자들 말마따나 하나의 공일 테고, 그러니 그것을 하나의 무도장으로 삼는 게 옳기도 하겠지. 계속 춤춰라, 얘들아, 너희는 젊으니. 나도 한때는 그랬다.\n\n낸터킷 선원 3. 잠깐 쉬자!—휴! 이건 잔잔한 바다에서 고래 뒤를 노 저어 쫓는 것보다 더 힘들군—한 모금만 빨게 해 주게, 태시.\n\n(그들이 춤을 멈추고 무리 지어 모인다. 그 사이 하늘이 어두워지고—바람이 인다.)\n\n라스카르 선원. 브라마 신이시여! 얘들아, 곧 돛을 내려야겠어. 하늘에서 태어난, 만조의 갠지스강이 바람으로 변했구나! 그대의 검은 이마를 드러내시는군요, 시바 신이여!\n\n몰타 선원. (비스듬히 누워 모자를 흔들며.) 저건 파도야—눈 같은 물마루들이 이제 춤을 추려고 하는군. 저것들도 곧 술 장식을 흔들어 댈 거야. 지금 저 모든 파도가 여자라면, 나는 기꺼이 빠져 죽으며 그들과 영원토록 춤춰 나가련만! 이 땅에 그토록 달콤한 것은 없어—천국조차 견줄 수 없으리라!—춤출 때 따뜻하고 거친 가슴이 던지는 저 날렵한 눈길만큼은, 머리 위로 뻗은 두 팔이 그 무르익어 터질 듯한 포도송이를 가릴 때 말이야.\n\n시칠리아 선원. (비스듬히 누워.) 그 얘긴 꺼내지도 마! 들어 봐, 친구—날래게 뒤엉키는 팔다리—나긋나긋한 흔들림—수줍은 몸짓—파르르 떨림! 입술! 가슴! 엉덩이! 모두 스칠 듯 말 듯: 끊임없이 닿았다 떨어지는 것! 맛보지는 마, 알겠나, 그러면 곧 물려 버리니까. 그렇지, 이교도 양반? (옆구리를 쿡 찌르며.)\n\n타히티 선원. (돗자리에 비스듬히 누워.) 만세, 우리 무희들의 성스러운 알몸이여!—헤에바-헤에바! 아! 낮게 드리워지고 높이 야자수가 솟은 타히티여! 나는 아직도 네 돗자리 위에서 쉬건만, 부드러운 흙은 이미 미끄러져 떠나 버렸구나! 숲에서 너를 엮는 모습을 내가 보았지, 내 돗자리야! 너를 거기서 가져온 첫날에는 푸르렀건만, 이제는 닳고 시들어 버렸구나. 아, 나여!—너도 나도 이 변화를 견딜 수 없으니! 그렇다면 저 하늘로 옮겨 심긴다면 어떠하랴? 피로히티 봉우리의 창날 같은 정상에서 우렁차게 쏟아지는 물줄기 소리가 들리는가, 그것들이 벼랑을 뛰어내려 마을을 잠기게 할 때?—돌풍이다! 돌풍이야! 일어서라, 등뼈여, 맞서라! (벌떡 일어선다.)\n\n포르투갈 선원. 바다가 어찌나 출렁이며 뱃전에 부딪쳐 오는지! 돛을 줄일 준비를 해라, 친구들아! 바람이 막 칼을 맞부딪치고 있으니, 머지않아 마구 뒤엉켜 찔러 댈 것이다.\n\n덴마크 선원. 삐걱, 삐걱, 늙은 배여! 네가 삐걱대는 한, 너는 버티는 것이다! 잘한다! 저기 항해사가 너를 단단히 붙들고 있구나. 그는 카테가트 해협의 섬 요새보다도 겁이 없어, 폭풍에 두들겨 맞은 대포로 발트해와 맞서 싸우라고 거기 세워진, 바닷소금이 켜켜이 들러붙은 그 요새 말이야!\n\n낸터킷 선원 4. 그는 명령을 받았어, 그 점을 명심해라. 늙은 에이해브가 그에게 이르는 걸 내가 들었지, 언제나 돌풍을 죽여야 한다고, 마치 권총으로 용오름을 터뜨리듯이 말이야—네 배를 곧장 그 속으로 쏘아 넣으라는 거지!\n\n영국 선원. 빌어먹을! 그 늙은이는 정말 대단한 양반이야! 우리야말로 그를 위해 그의 고래를 사냥해 줄 녀석들이지!\n\n모두. 옳소! 옳소!\n\n늙은 맨섬 선원. 저 세 그루 소나무가 어찌나 흔들리는지! 소나무는 다른 흙으로 옮겨지면 살아남기 가장 어려운 나무인데, 여기엔 선원들의 저주받은 진흙밖에 없으니. 침착해라, 키잡이! 침착해. 이런 날씨야말로 용감한 마음이 뭍에서 뚝 꺾이고, 용골 박힌 선체가 바다에서 쪼개지는 그런 날씨다. 우리 선장에게는 그의 모반(母斑)이 있지. 저기 좀 봐라, 얘들아, 하늘에도 또 하나가 있으니—거 봐, 핏빛처럼 음산하고, 그 밖에는 온통 칠흑같이 검구나.\n\n대구. 그게 뭐 어쨌다고? 검은 것을 두려워하는 자는 나를 두려워하는 거다! 나는 검은 것에서 깎여 나온 몸이니까!\n\n스페인 선원. (방백.) 저자가 으스대려는군, 아!—오래된 원한 때문에 신경이 곤두서는걸 (앞으로 나서며.) 그래, 작살잡이여, 자네 종족은 부정할 수 없는 인류의 어두운 면이지—그것도 악마처럼 시커먼 면이고. 기분 상해하진 말게.\n\n대구 (험악하게). 상할 것 없어.\n\n세인트야고 선원. 저 스페인 사람이 미쳤거나 취했군. 하지만 그럴 리 없지, 그게 아니라면 저 한 사람에게만은 우리 늙은 모굴의 독주가 효력을 내는 데 좀 오래 걸리는 셈이니까.\n\n낸터킷 선원 5. 내가 본 게 뭐지—번개인가? 그래.\n\n스페인 선원. 아니. 대구가 이빨을 드러낸 거야.\n\n대구 (벌떡 일어서며). 네 이빨이나 삼켜라, 이 난쟁이야! 흰 살갗에, 흰 간이로구나!\n\n스페인 선원 (그와 맞서며). 네놈을 신나게 칼로 찔러 주마! 덩치만 크고, 배짱은 쥐꼬리만 하구나!\n\n모두. 싸움이다! 싸움이야! 싸움이다!\n\n태시테고 (한 모금 빨며). 아래도 싸움, 위도 싸움—신들도 인간들도—다 싸움질이로군! 흥!\n\n벨파스트 선원. 싸움이다! 어이쿠, 싸움이야! 성모께 축복 있으라, 싸움이다! 다들 뛰어들어라!\n\n영국 선원. 정정당당하게! 스페인 놈의 칼을 빼앗아라! 둥글게, 둥글게 둘러서라!\n\n늙은 맨섬 선원. 벌써 둥글게 만들어졌군. 저기! 둥근 수평선이로다. 저 둥근 테 안에서 카인이 아벨을 쳤지. 달콤한 짓이고, 옳은 짓이야! 아니라고? 그럼 어째서, 하느님이시여, 당신은 저 둥근 테를 만드셨나이까?\n\n뒷갑판에서 들려오는 항해사의 목소리. 모두 활대 밧줄을 잡아라! 윗돛을 거둬라! 큰돛을 줄일 준비를 해라!\n\n모두. 돌풍이다! 돌풍이야! 뛰어라, 흥겨운 친구들아! (그들이 흩어진다.)\n\n핍 (양묘기 밑으로 움츠러들며). 흥겨운 친구들? 주여, 저런 흥겨운 친구들을 도우소서! 우지끈, 와장창! 저기 삼각돛 버팀줄이 끊어지네! 쾅-쾅! 하느님! 더 낮게 숙여라, 핍, 저기 윗활대가 내려온다! 한 해의 마지막 날에 빙빙 도는 숲속에 있는 것보다 더 끔찍해! 이런 판에 누가 밤톨을 따러 올라가겠어? 그런데 저들은 가는군, 모두 욕설을 퍼부으며, 나는 안 가지만. 저들에게는 좋은 앞날이 있지. 천국으로 가는 길에 올랐으니까. 단단히 붙들어라! 맙소사, 무슨 이런 돌풍이! 하지만 저기 저 녀석들은 더 고약해—저것들은 흰 돌풍이야, 정말. 흰 돌풍이라고? 흰 고래, 휙! 휙! 방금 저들이 떠드는 소리를 다 들었는데, 흰 고래는—휙! 휙!—딱 한 번 입에 올렸을 뿐이지! 그것도 바로 오늘 저녁—그 생각만 하면 온몸이 내 탬버린처럼 딸랑딸랑 떨려 온다—저 아나콘다 같은 늙은이가 그놈을 사냥하라고 저들에게 맹세를 시켰어! 오, 저 어둠 속 어딘가 높은 곳에 계신 위대한 흰 신이시여, 이 아래에 있는 이 작은 검둥이 소년에게 자비를 베푸소서. 두려움을 느낄 창자조차 없는 모든 인간들로부터 그를 지켜 주소서!\n\n# CHAPTER 41. Moby Dick.\n\n나, 이슈메일도 그 선원들 가운데 하나였다. 내 함성도 다른 이들의 함성과 함께 솟아올랐고, 내 맹세도 그들의 맹세와 한데 용접되었다. 그리고 나는 영혼 속에 깃든 두려움 때문에 더욱 크게 외쳤으며, 더욱 힘껏 맹세를 두드려 박고 단단히 다졌다. 거칠고 신비롭고 동조하는 감정이 내 안에 있었다. 에이해브의 꺼지지 않는 원한이 마치 나 자신의 것인 양 느껴졌다. 나는 탐욕스러운 귀로, 나와 다른 모든 이들이 폭력과 복수의 맹세를 바친 그 살인적인 괴물의 내력을 들었다.\n\n지난 한동안, 비록 띄엄띄엄이긴 했으나, 홀로 외떨어진 흰 고래는 향유고래잡이 어부들이 주로 드나드는 저 미개한 바다를 떠돌고 있었다. 그러나 그들 모두가 그 존재를 알고 있었던 것은 아니다. 그를 직접 보고 알아본 자는 비교적 소수에 지나지 않았고, 실제로 그를 알아보고 맞서 싸운 자의 수에 이르러서는 참으로 적었다. 그도 그럴 것이, 고래잡이배의 수가 워낙 많고, 그것들이 광대한 바다의 둘레 전체에 어수선하게 흩어져 있는 데다, 그 가운데 다수가 외딴 위도를 따라 모험적으로 사냥을 밀고 나아가서, 한 해 남짓을 내리 항해해도 소식을 전해 줄 돛 한 척조차 좀처럼, 아니 결코 마주치지 못하는 일이 흔했기 때문이다. 게다가 항해 하나하나가 터무니없이 길고, 고향을 떠나는 출항 시기 또한 들쭉날쭉했으니, 이런 사정들이 직간접의 다른 정황들과 더불어, 모비 딕에 관한 특별하고도 구체적인 소식이 전 세계 고래잡이 선단에 두루 퍼지는 일을 오랫동안 가로막았다. 어느 때, 혹은 어느 자오선 위에서 유달리 거대하고 흉포한 향유고래를 만났으며, 그 고래가 공격자들에게 큰 화를 입힌 뒤 완전히 빠져나갔다고 보고한 배가 여럿 있었음은 거의 의심할 여지가 없었다. 내 말하건대, 어떤 이들에게는 그 문제의 고래가 다름 아닌 모비 딕이었으리라는 짐작이 결코 부당한 것이 아니었다. 그러나 근래 들어 향유고래잡이에는 공격받은 괴물이 보인 엄청난 흉포함과 교활함과 악의의 사례들이 다양하게, 그것도 드물지 않게 나타났기에, 우연히 영문도 모른 채 모비 딕과 맞붙은 자들, 즉 그런 사냥꾼들은 대개 그가 빚어내는 그 유별난 공포를, 말하자면 그 개별적인 원인보다는 향유고래잡이 전반의 위험에 돌리는 것으로 만족했다. 에이해브와 그 고래 사이의 참혹한 조우 또한 지금까지는 대체로 그런 식으로 사람들에게 받아들여져 왔다.\n\n그리고 흰 고래에 관해 미리 들어 두었다가 우연히 그를 목격하게 된 자들로 말하자면, 일이 처음 벌어졌을 때 그들은 거의 하나같이 그 종(種)의 다른 어떤 고래를 잡을 때와 다름없이 대담하고 거침없이 그를 향해 보트를 내렸다. 그러나 마침내 이 공격들에는 온갖 재앙이 뒤따르고 말았으니 — 손목이나 발목이 삐고, 사지가 부러지고, 끔찍한 절단을 당하는 정도에 그치지 않고 — 가장 극심한 죽음에까지 이르는 일이 거듭되었다. 그렇게 거듭된 참혹한 격퇴가, 모비 딕에게 그 모든 공포를 차곡차곡 쌓아 올렸으니, 이런 일들은 흰 고래의 이야기가 끝내 흘러든 수많은 용맹한 사냥꾼들의 굳센 기개마저 크게 뒤흔들어 놓았다.\n\n또한 온갖 거친 소문들도 이 치명적인 조우의 참된 내력을 부풀리고 한층 더 끔찍하게 만드는 데 빠지지 않았다. 그도 그럴 것이, 벼락 맞은 나무가 거기서 버섯을 피워 내듯, 놀랍고 무시무시한 사건의 바로 그 몸뚱이에서 황당한 소문이 절로 자라나는 것은 당연하거니와, 게다가 뭍의 삶에서보다 바다의 삶에서, 그 소문이 매달릴 만한 그럴듯한 실체가 조금이라도 있는 곳이면 어디든 거친 소문이 넘쳐 나기 때문이다. 그리고 이 점에서 바다가 뭍을 능가하듯이, 고래잡이는 거기서 이따금 떠도는 소문들의 경이로움과 무시무시함에 있어 다른 모든 종류의 바다 생활을 능가한다. 그도 그럴 것이, 고래잡이들이 한 무리로서 모든 뱃사람에게 대물림된 그 무지와 미신에서 벗어나 있지 못할 뿐만 아니라, 모든 뱃사람 가운데 그들이야말로 단연 바다에서 가장 소름 끼치도록 놀라운 것들과 가장 직접 맞닿아 살아가는 자들이기 때문이다. 그들은 얼굴을 맞대고 바다의 가장 위대한 경이를 두 눈으로 볼 뿐 아니라, 손과 턱을 맞대고 그것들과 싸운다. 천 마일을 항해하고 천 개의 해안을 지나도 정으로 다듬은 화롯돌 하나, 저 햇빛 아래 사람을 맞아 줄 것 하나 만나지 못할 만큼 외딴 바다, 그토록 먼 바다에서 홀로, 그것도 그가 하는 것과 같은 일을 좇으며 살아가니, 고래잡이는 그 상상력을 숱한 거대한 산물로 잉태시키는 온갖 영향력에 휩싸여 있는 것이다.\n\n그러니, 가장 드넓은 바다를 가로질러 흐르는 동안 부피만 점점 불려 나간 끝에, 흰 고래에 관해 퍼져 나간 소문이 마침내 온갖 병적인 암시와, 초자연적 작용을 어렴풋이 빚어내는 태아 같은 추측들까지 제 속에 끌어안게 된 것도 놀랄 일이 아니다. 그것들은 결국 눈에 보이는 그 무엇에서도 빌려 오지 않은 새로운 공포를 모비 딕에게 입혔다. 그리하여 많은 경우 그가 마침내 불러일으킨 공황이 얼마나 컸던지, 적어도 그런 소문을 통해 흰 고래에 관해 들어 본 자들 가운데 그의 턱이 안기는 위험과 기꺼이 맞서려는 사냥꾼은 거의 없었다.\n\n그러나 여기에는 또 다른, 더 본질적이고 실질적인 영향력들이 작용하고 있었다. 오늘날에 이르러서도, 리바이어던의 다른 모든 종과 무섭도록 구별되는 향유고래 본연의 위엄은 고래잡이라는 무리의 마음에서 사그라지지 않았다. 오늘날 그들 가운데에는, 그린란드고래나 참고래에게 싸움을 거는 데는 충분히 영리하고 용감하면서도, 직업적 경험 부족이거나 무능이거나 소심함 때문에 향유고래와의 대결은 마다할 자들이 있을 것이다. 어쨌거나, 특히 미국 깃발 아래 항해하지 않는 고래잡이 나라들 사이에는, 향유고래와 적대적으로 맞붙어 본 적이 한 번도 없고, 리바이어던에 관한 지식이라고는 처음부터 북쪽에서 쫓던 그 변변찮은 괴물에 국한된 고래잡이가 수두룩하다. 이런 자들은 승강구에 걸터앉아, 어린아이가 화롯가에서 듣듯 호기심과 경외에 차서 남쪽 고래잡이의 거칠고 기이한 이야기에 귀를 기울일 것이다. 그리고 위대한 향유고래의 그 압도적인 무시무시함을, 그를 뱃머리로 헤치며 나아가는 그 배들 위에서보다 더 절실히 헤아리는 곳은 어디에도 없다.\n\n그리고 마치 이제 입증된 그 힘의 실체가 옛 전설의 시대에 미리 그 그림자를 드리워 두기라도 한 듯, 우리는 어떤 책상물림 박물학자들 — 올라센과 포벨손 — 이 향유고래를 두고 바다의 다른 모든 피조물에게 공포의 대상일 뿐 아니라, 끊임없이 사람의 피에 목말라할 만큼 믿을 수 없으리만치 흉포하다고 단언한 것을 본다. 그리고 퀴비에의 시대만큼 늦은 무렵까지도 이러한, 혹은 거의 비슷한 인상은 지워지지 않았다. 그도 그럴 것이, 남작 자신은 그의 박물지에서, 향유고래를 보면 모든 물고기가(상어까지 포함하여) \"더없이 생생한 공포에 사로잡히며\" 또 \"달아나려는 황급함 속에서 어찌나 사납게 바위에 제 몸을 부딪치는지 그 자리에서 즉사할 정도\"라고 단언했기 때문이다. 그리고 고래잡이의 일반적 경험이 이러한 보고들을 아무리 바로잡는다 한들, 그것들이 지닌 온전한 무시무시함, 심지어 포벨손이 말한 그 피에 굶주린 대목에 이르기까지, 그것들에 대한 미신적 믿음은 그 직업의 어떤 굴곡진 순간에 사냥꾼들의 마음속에서 되살아나는 것이다.\n\n그리하여 그에 관한 소문과 흉조에 압도된 적지 않은 어부들이, 모비 딕과 관련하여 향유고래잡이의 초기 시절을 떠올렸다. 그때는 오랜 경험을 쌓은 참고래잡이들을 이 새롭고 대담한 싸움의 위험 속으로 끌어들이기가 흔히 어려웠다. 그런 자들은 항변하기를, 다른 리바이어던이라면 희망을 품고 쫓을 만하나, 향유고래 같은 환영을 뒤쫓아 작살을 겨누는 일은 인간의 몫이 아니라고 했다. 그런 짓을 시도하는 것은 곧 산 채로 갈가리 찢겨 순식간에 영원 속으로 떨어지는 일이라는 것이었다. 이 점에 관해서는 참고할 만한 주목할 기록들이 더러 남아 있다.\n\n그럼에도, 이런 일들 앞에서조차 모비 딕을 기꺼이 추격하려는 자들이 더러 있었다. 그리고 그를 멀리서 어렴풋이 들어 보았을 뿐, 어떤 확실한 재앙의 구체적 정황도, 미신적인 꾸밈도 없이 알게 된 까닭에, 싸움이 닥쳐와도 달아나지 않을 만큼 충분히 담대한 자들은 그보다 훨씬 더 많았다.\n\n미신에 기울어진 자들의 마음속에서 끝내 흰 고래와 결부되기에 이른, 앞서 언급한 그 거친 추측들 가운데 하나는, 모비 딕이 어디에나 동시에 존재한다는 섬뜩한 망상이었다. 다시 말해, 그가 같은 시각 정반대의 위도에서 실제로 목격되었다는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그런 마음들이 아무리 잘 속아 넘어가는 것이었다 해도, 이 망상이 미신적 개연성의 어렴풋한 자취조차 전혀 없는 것은 아니었다. 그도 그럴 것이, 바다 해류의 비밀이 아직껏 가장 박식한 연구에조차 밝혀진 바 없듯이, 향유고래가 수면 아래로 잠겼을 때 다니는 그 숨은 길도 대부분은 추격자들로서는 헤아릴 수 없는 것으로 남아 있다. 그리하여 그것들에 관해, 특히 그가 깊은 바닥까지 잠수한 뒤에 그토록 엄청난 속도로 더없이 멀리 떨어진 지점으로 제 몸을 옮겨 가는 그 신비로운 방식에 관해, 더없이 기이하고 모순된 억측들이 이따금 생겨나곤 했다.\n\n어떤 고래들이 태평양 멀리 북쪽에서 잡혔는데, 그 몸뚱이 안에서 그린란드 바다에서 던진 작살의 미늘이 발견되었다는 것은, 미국과 영국 고래잡이배 양쪽에 두루 잘 알려진 사실이며, 또한 여러 해 전에 스코어즈비가 권위 있는 기록으로 남겨 둔 사실이기도 하다. 그리고 이런 사례들 가운데 더러는 두 차례 공격 사이의 시간 간격이 며칠을 그리 넘지 않았으리라고 단언된 바 있음 또한 부인할 수 없다. 그리하여 어떤 고래잡이들은 미루어 짐작하기를, 그토록 오랫동안 인간에게 풀리지 않는 숙제였던 북서 항로가 고래에게는 애초에 숙제였던 적이 없다고 믿었다. 그리하여 여기, 살아 있는 사람들의 실제로 겪은 경험 속에서, 포르투갈 내륙의 스트렐로산에 관해 옛날부터 전해 오는 신기한 이야기(그 꼭대기 가까이에 호수가 있어 그 안으로 난파선의 잔해가 수면 위로 떠올랐다고 한다)와, 그보다 한층 더 놀라운 시라쿠사 근처 아레투사 샘에 관한 이야기(그 샘물이 지하 통로를 거쳐 성지에서 흘러왔다고 믿어졌다) — 이런 황당한 전설들이 고래잡이들의 현실에 거의 고스란히 맞먹게 되는 것이다.\n\n그렇다면, 이런 신기한 일들에 익히 길들여진 데다, 거듭된 용맹한 공격 끝에도 흰 고래가 산 채로 빠져나갔음을 아는 터이니, 어떤 고래잡이들이 그 미신을 한층 더 멀리까지 밀고 나아간다 한들 그리 놀랄 일은 아니다. 그들은 모비 딕이 어디에나 존재할 뿐 아니라 죽지 않는다고 단언했다(불멸이란 곧 시간 속의 편재일 뿐이므로). 그의 옆구리에 창의 숲이 빽빽이 박힌다 해도 그는 여전히 아무 상처 없이 헤엄쳐 달아날 것이며, 설령 정말로 그가 검붉은 피를 뿜게 되는 일이 있다 해도, 그런 광경은 그저 소름 끼치는 속임수에 지나지 않으리니, 수백 리그 떨어진 피 한 점 섞이지 않은 물결 속에서 그의 티 없이 맑은 물줄기가 다시 한 번 보이리라는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 이러한 초자연적 억측들을 모두 벗겨 낸다 해도, 그 괴물의 지상적 생김새와 반박할 수 없는 본성 속에는 상상력을 유례없는 힘으로 후려치는 무언가가 충분히 있었다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그를 다른 향유고래들과 그토록 두드러지게 갈라놓는 것은 비범한 그 거구라기보다는 — 다른 데서 이미 언급했듯이 — 유달리 새하얗게 주름진 이마와, 높고 피라미드처럼 솟은 흰 혹이었다. 이것들이 그의 두드러진 특징이었으니, 끝없이 펼쳐진 해도에도 없는 바다에서조차, 그를 아는 자들에게 먼 거리에서도 제 정체를 드러내 보이는 표지였다.\n\n그의 몸뚱이 나머지 부분도 그 똑같이 수의 같은 빛깔로 줄무늬가 지고, 점이 박히고, 대리석처럼 얼룩져 있어서, 끝내 그는 흰 고래라는 그 독특한 별칭을 얻기에 이르렀다. 그것은 참으로, 한낮에 짙푸른 바다를 미끄러지듯 헤치며, 황금빛 광채가 점점이 반짝이는 크림 같은 거품의 은하수 같은 항적을 남기는 그의 선명한 자태를 보면, 글자 그대로 정당화되는 이름이었다.\n\n그러나 그 고래에게 천부적인 공포를 그토록 짙게 입힌 것은, 비범한 그 크기도, 두드러진 그 빛깔도, 일그러진 그 아래턱조차도 아니었으니, 바로 구체적인 증언들에 따르면 그가 공격할 때 거듭거듭 드러냈다는, 유례없이 지능적인 악의가 그것이었다. 무엇보다도 그의 음흉한 후퇴가 어쩌면 다른 그 무엇보다 더 큰 경악을 안겼다. 그도 그럴 것이, 의기양양한 추격자들 앞에서 온갖 겁먹은 기색을 다 보이며 헤엄쳐 달아나다가, 그가 불쑥 몸을 돌려 그들을 덮쳐서는 보트를 산산조각으로 부숴 버리거나, 그들을 혼비백산하여 제 배로 도로 쫓아 버린 일이 여러 차례 알려져 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이미 그를 쫓는 일에는 여러 차례 죽음이 따랐다. 그러나 그와 비슷한 재앙들이, 뭍에서는 별로 소문나지 않았을지언정, 고래잡이에서는 결코 드문 일이 아니었음에도, 대부분의 경우 흰 고래가 보인 그 흉포함이 어찌나 지옥처럼 미리 계산된 것 같았던지, 그가 일으킨 모든 절단과 죽음이 지능 없는 행위자에 의해 가해진 것이라고만은 결코 여겨지지 않았다.\n\n그러니 헤아려 보라, 더 필사적인 사냥꾼들의 마음이 얼마나 활활 타오르고 갈피를 잃은 분노의 절정으로 몰렸을지를. 그들이 씹어 으깨진 보트 조각들과, 찢긴 동료들의 가라앉는 사지 한가운데서, 고래의 무시무시한 진노가 빚어낸 흰 거품을 헤치고 빠져나와, 마치 탄생이나 혼례를 보고 웃기라도 하는 듯 미소 지으며 사람의 속을 뒤집어 놓는 저 고요한 햇살 속으로 나아갔을 때 말이다.\n\n그를 둘러싸고 세 척의 보트가 박살이 나고, 노와 사람이 모두 소용돌이 속에서 빙빙 도는 가운데, 한 선장이 부서진 뱃머리에서 밧줄칼을 움켜쥐고는, 아칸소의 결투자가 제 원수에게 달려들듯 고래에게 돌진하여, 여섯 인치짜리 칼날로 한 길 깊이에 자리한 고래의 생명을 맹목적으로 더듬어 찾으려 했다. 그 선장이 바로 에이해브였다. 그리고 바로 그때, 모비 딕이 낫 모양의 아래턱을 그의 발밑으로 휙 쓸어 올려, 풀 베는 이가 들판의 풀잎을 베어 내듯 에이해브의 다리를 거두어 가 버렸다. 터번을 두른 어떤 튀르크인도, 돈에 팔린 어떤 베네치아인이나 말레이인도, 그보다 더 악의에 차 보이는 일격을 가하지는 못했으리라. 그렇다면 그 거의 치명적이었던 조우 이후로, 에이해브가 그 고래에 대해 거친 복수심을 품어 왔음을 의심할 까닭은 거의 없었으니, 그 복수심이 한층 더 사나워진 것은, 그가 광적인 병증 속에서 마침내 자신의 모든 육체적 고통뿐 아니라 지적이고 영적인 격분까지 모두 그 고래와 동일시하기에 이르렀기 때문이었다. 흰 고래는, 어떤 깊은 사람들이 제 안에서 갉아먹히는 것을 느끼다가 끝내 반쪽짜리 심장과 반쪽짜리 폐로 연명하게 만드는 그 모든 악의에 찬 작용의, 편집광적 화신(化身)으로서 그의 앞을 헤엄쳐 다녔다. 태초부터 있어 온 그 손에 잡히지 않는 악의, 현대의 기독교인들조차 그 지배에 세상의 절반을 돌리고, 동방의 옛 오피스교도들이 그들의 악마 신상으로 떠받든 그 악의 — 에이해브는 그들처럼 그 앞에 엎드려 숭배하지는 않았으나, 그 관념을 가증스러운 흰 고래에게 미친 듯이 옮겨 놓고는, 사지가 잘린 몸으로 그것에 맞섰다. 사람을 가장 미치게 하고 괴롭히는 모든 것, 사물의 앙금을 휘저어 올리는 모든 것, 악의가 깃든 모든 진실, 힘줄을 끊고 뇌를 굳혀 버리는 모든 것, 삶과 사유의 온갖 교묘한 악마성, 모든 악이, 미쳐 버린 에이해브에게는 모비 딕 안에 눈에 보이게 인격화되어, 실제로 공격할 수 있는 것이 되었다. 그는 아담 이래로 자기 종족 전체가 느껴 온 일반적 분노와 증오의 총합을 그 고래의 흰 혹 위에 쌓아 올렸다. 그러고는 마치 제 가슴이 박격포라도 되는 양, 그 위로 뜨거운 심장의 탄피를 터뜨렸다.\n\n그의 이 편집광이 정확히 그가 육체를 절단당한 그 순간에 곧바로 솟아났을 성싶지는 않다. 그때, 칼을 손에 쥐고 그 괴물에게 돌진했을 때 그는 그저 갑작스럽고 격정적인 육체적 적의를 분출했을 뿐이며, 그를 찢어 놓은 그 일격을 받았을 때도 그는 아마 살을 에는 육체의 찢김만을 느꼈을 뿐, 그 이상의 것은 아니었으리라. 그러나 이 충돌로 말미암아 어쩔 수 없이 고향을 향해 뱃머리를 돌리고, 여러 달에 걸친 나날과 주(週)들 동안, 한겨울 저 황량하고 울부짖는 파타고니아의 곶을 돌아 나오면서 에이해브와 고뇌가 한 해먹 속에 나란히 누워 있었을 때, 바로 그때, 그의 찢긴 몸과 베인 영혼이 서로의 안으로 피를 흘려 보내며, 그렇게 한데 뒤섞여 그를 미치게 만들었다. 그 마지막 편집광이 그를 사로잡은 것이 오직 그때, 그 조우 이후 귀향 항해에서였다는 것은 거의 확실해 보인다. 항해 도중 띄엄띄엄 그는 발광하는 미치광이였으며, 비록 다리 하나를 잃었음에도 그의 이집트인 같은 가슴팍에는 여전히 무서운 활력이 도사리고 있었고, 게다가 그 활력이 광란으로 한층 거세진 까닭에, 항해하는 동안 해먹 속에서 미쳐 날뛰는 그를 항해사들이 거기다 단단히 동여매지 않을 수 없을 정도였다는 사실에서 그러하다. 구속복에 묶인 채 그는 광풍의 미친 흔들림에 따라 흔들렸다. 그러다 한결 견딜 만한 위도로 접어들어, 배가 부드러운 보조돛을 펼치고 고요한 열대 바다를 가로질러 떠가고, 겉보기에 노인의 광란은 케이프 혼의 너울과 함께 뒤에 남겨 두고 온 듯하여, 그가 어두운 굴에서 나와 축복받은 빛과 공기 속으로 걸어 나왔을 때 — 그때조차, 비록 창백하나마 그가 그 굳건하고 차분한 표정을 짓고 다시금 침착한 명령을 내렸을 때, 그리고 항해사들이 그 무시무시한 광기가 이제 사라졌다고 하느님께 감사드렸을 때 — 그때조차, 에이해브는 그 감추어진 자아 속에서 여전히 미쳐 날뛰고 있었다. 인간의 광기란 흔히 교활하고 더없이 고양이 같은 것이다. 그것이 달아났다고 여길 때, 그것은 그저 한층 더 교묘한 형태로 모습을 바꾸었을 뿐일지도 모른다. 에이해브의 온전한 광기는 가라앉은 것이 아니라, 깊어지며 좁혀들었으니, 마치 줄지 않고 흐르는 허드슨강이 저 고결한 북방의 물줄기가 되어 하이랜드 협곡을 비좁게, 그러나 헤아릴 수 없이 깊게 흐르는 것과 같았다. 그러나 그 비좁게 흐르는 편집광 속에 에이해브의 드넓은 광기가 한 톨도 남김없이 담겨 있었듯이, 그 드넓은 광기 속에는 그의 위대한 천부적 지성이 한 톨도 사라지지 않고 그대로였다. 전에는 살아 있는 행위자였던 그것이, 이제는 살아 있는 도구가 되었다. 이런 격렬한 비유가 허락된다면, 그의 특수한 광기가 그의 일반적인 온전한 정신을 폭풍처럼 휩쓸어 점령하고는, 그 모든 응집된 대포를 제 자신의 미친 표적에 겨누어 발사했다고 하겠다. 그리하여 에이해브는 그 힘을 잃기는커녕, 오로지 그 한 가지 목적을 위하여, 온전한 정신으로 그 어떤 분별 있는 대상에 쏟아부었던 것보다 천 배는 더한 위력을 이제 갖추게 된 것이다.\n\n이것만 해도 대단하다. 그러나 에이해브의 더 크고, 더 어둡고, 더 깊은 부분은 아직 암시조차 되지 않은 채로 남아 있다. 그러나 심오한 것을 대중에게 쉽게 풀어 보이려는 것은 헛된 일이며, 모든 진실은 심오한 법이다. 우리가 지금 서 있는 이 첨탑 솟은 클뤼니 호텔의 바로 그 심장부에서 — 그것이 아무리 웅장하고 경이롭더라도, 이제 그곳을 떠나, 멀리 아래로 굽이굽이 내려가라. 그대들, 더 고귀하고 더 슬픈 영혼들이여, 저 광대한 로마의 테르메 대욕장으로 발길을 옮기라. 그곳, 인간의 위쪽 대지에 솟은 환상적인 탑들 저 깊은 아래, 그의 위대함의 뿌리, 그의 온전한 무시무시한 본질이 수염을 기른 위엄 속에 앉아 있나니, 옛것들 아래 묻힌 옛것이요, 토르소들 위에 옥좌를 차지하고 앉은 존재로다! 그렇게, 부서진 옥좌와 함께, 위대한 신들은 그 사로잡힌 왕을 조롱한다. 그렇게 카리아티드처럼, 그는 인내하며 앉아, 그 얼어붙은 이마 위에 쌓인 시대의 들보들을 떠받치고 있다. 그곳으로 굽이굽이 내려가라, 그대들 더 자랑스럽고 더 슬픈 영혼들이여! 저 자랑스럽고 슬픈 왕에게 물으라! 가문의 닮은꼴이라! 그렇다, 그가 그대들을 낳았다, 그대들 추방당한 어린 왕족들이여. 그리고 그대들의 그 험상궂은 아비에게서만, 저 오래된 국가의 비밀이 흘러나오리라.\n\n자, 에이해브는 마음속으로 이것을 어렴풋이 엿보고 있었으니, 곧 나의 모든 수단은 온전하나, 나의 동기와 나의 목적은 미쳐 있다는 것이었다. 그러나 그 사실을 죽이거나, 바꾸거나, 피할 힘이 없었으니, 그는 또한 자신이 인간들에게 오래도록 거짓을 꾸며 왔으며, 어떤 면에서는 지금도 여전히 그러하다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 그가 꾸며 내는 그 가식은 다만 그의 자각에만 매여 있었을 뿐, 그의 결연한 의지에 매여 있는 것은 아니었다. 그럼에도 그는 그 가식에 어찌나 능했던지, 마침내 상아 다리로 뭍에 발을 디뎠을 때, 어떤 낸터킷 사람도 그를, 자신을 덮친 그 끔찍한 재난으로 자연스레, 그것도 뼈저리게 슬퍼하는 사람으로밖에는 달리 여기지 않았다.\n\n바다에서 그가 부정할 수 없이 광란했다는 소문 또한 사람들에게는 그와 같은 부류의 원인으로 돌려졌다. 그리고 그 후로 줄곧, 이번 항해를 위해 피쿼드호로 출항하던 바로 그날까지 그의 이마에 깊이 자리 잡고 있던 그 한층 더해진 침울함 또한 마찬가지였다. 또한, 그토록 어두운 징후들 때문에 그가 또 한 번의 고래잡이 항해에 적합한지를 미심쩍게 여기기는커녕, 그 신중한 섬의 타산적인 사람들이 오히려 이런 생각을 품기 시작했다는 것도 그리 있을 법하지 않은 일은 아니다. 즉, 바로 그런 이유들 때문에 그가 피비린내 나는 고래 사냥처럼 분노와 광기로 가득한 추격에 한층 더 적합하고 한껏 날이 서 있다는 생각 말이다. 어떤 불치의 관념이 박아 넣은 그 가차 없는 송곳니에 안으로는 갉아먹히고 밖으로는 그을린 자, 만약 그런 자를 찾아낼 수 있다면, 그야말로 온갖 짐승 가운데 가장 소름 끼치는 놈에게 작살을 던지고 창을 치켜들 적임자로 보일 것이었다. 혹은 어떤 이유로든 그가 육체적으로 그 일에 부적합하다고 여겨진다 해도, 그런 자야말로 부하들을 격려하고 함성으로 부추겨 공격에 나서게 하는 데는 더없이 뛰어난 적임자로 보일 것이었다. 그러나 이 모든 것이 어찌 되었든, 분명한 것은, 사그라지지 않은 그 분노의 미친 비밀을 제 안에 빗장 질러 단단히 잠가 둔 채로, 에이해브가 오직 흰 고래를 사냥한다는 그 단 하나의, 모든 것을 집어삼키는 목적을 품고 이번 항해에 일부러 나섰다는 사실이다. 뭍에 있는 그의 옛 지인들 가운데 누구라도 그 안에 도사리고 있던 것을 절반이나마 어렴풋이 꿈꿔 보았더라면, 그들의 경악하고 의로운 영혼이 얼마나 재빨리 그토록 악마 같은 사내의 손에서 그 배를 비틀어 빼앗았겠는가! 그들은 돈벌이가 되는 항해에 마음을 두고 있었으니, 그 이문은 조폐국에서 찍어 낸 달러로 헤아릴 수 있는 것이었다. 그러나 그는 대담하고, 누그러뜨릴 수 없으며, 초자연적인 복수에 골몰해 있었다.\n\n그러니 여기, 머리가 희끗희끗한 이 불경한 노인이, 욥의 고래를 저주를 퍼부으며 온 세상을 돌며 쫓고 있었으니, 게다가 그가 거느린 선원들이라는 것도 대개는 잡종 변절자와 표류자와 식인종들로 이루어진 무리였다. 도덕적으로도 약화된 무리였으니, 스타벅에게는 그저 도움받지 못한 미덕이나 올바른 마음만으로는 어쩔 수 없는 무능이 있었고, 스터브에게는 무관심과 무모함이 빚어내는 그 어떤 것에도 끄떡없는 쾌활함이 있었으며, 플래스크에게는 사방에 배어든 범용함이 있었다. 그렇게 짜인 선원들과 그렇게 임명된 항해사들은, 마치 어떤 지옥의 운명이 그의 편집광적 복수를 거들도록 특별히 골라 한데 묶어 놓은 것만 같았다. 그들이 어찌하여 그토록 넘치게 노인의 분노에 호응했는지 — 어떤 사악한 마법에 그들의 영혼이 사로잡혔기에, 때로 그의 증오가 거의 그들 자신의 것처럼 보였고, 흰 고래가 그에게만큼이나 그들에게도 견딜 수 없는 원수처럼 보였는지, 이 모든 것이 어떻게 그리되었는지 — 흰 고래가 그들에게 무엇이었는지, 혹은 그들의 무의식적인 이해 속에서도, 어떤 어렴풋하고 미처 짐작지 못한 방식으로, 그가 삶의 바다를 미끄러져 다니는 거대한 악마처럼 보였을지도 모른다는 것을, 이 모든 것을 설명하려는 것은 이슈메일이 잠수할 수 있는 것보다 더 깊이 잠수하는 일일 터이다. 우리 모두의 안에서 일하는 저 지하의 광부, 끊임없이 자리를 옮기는 그 곡괭이의 둔탁한 소리만으로 그의 갱도가 어디로 이어지는지를 누가 알 수 있겠는가? 그 거역할 수 없는 팔의 끌어당김을 느끼지 못할 자가 어디 있겠는가? 칠십사 문(門) 군함에 끌려가는 작은 거룻배가 어떻게 가만히 멈춰 있을 수 있겠는가? 나로 말하자면, 나는 그 시간과 장소의 방기(放棄) 속에 나 자신을 내맡겨 버렸다. 그러나 온통 고래와 맞붙으러 내달리면서도, 그 짐승에게서 더없이 치명적인 악(惡) 말고는 아무것도 볼 수가 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 42. The Whiteness of the Whale.\n\n흰 고래가 에이해브(Ahab)에게 무엇이었는지는 이미 넌지시 비쳐 두었다. 하지만 그것이 때때로 나에게 무엇이었는지는 아직 말하지 않은 채로 남아 있다.\n\n모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 둘러싼, 누구의 영혼에든 이따금 어떤 경계심을 불러일으키지 않을 수 없는 저 보다 명백한 사정들은 차치하더라도, 그 고래와 관련하여 또 하나의 생각이, 아니 그보다는 막연하고 이름조차 붙일 수 없는 공포가 있었으니, 그것은 때때로 그 강렬함으로 다른 모든 것을 완전히 압도해 버리곤 했다. 그러면서도 그것은 너무도 신비롭고 거의 형언할 수 없는 것이어서, 나는 그것을 이해할 만한 형태로 옮겨 놓는 일에 거의 절망하고 만다. 무엇보다도 나를 소름 끼치게 한 것은 그 고래의 흰빛이었다. 그러나 여기서 내가 어떻게 나 자신을 설명할 수 있기를 바랄 수 있겠는가. 그럼에도 어슴푸레하고 두서없는 방식으로나마, 나는 기어이 나 자신을 설명해 내야만 한다. 그러지 않는다면 이 모든 장(章)이 한낱 헛것이 되고 말 테니까.\n\n비록 수많은 자연의 사물들에서 흰빛이 마치 제 고유의 어떤 특별한 미덕을 부여하기라도 하듯 아름다움을 정련하여 한층 돋보이게 하는 것이 사실이고, 대리석과 동백꽃과 진주가 그러하며, 또한 여러 민족이 어떤 식으로든 이 빛깔 속에서 일종의 제왕적 탁월함을 인정해 온 것도 사실이어서, 저 야만스럽고 위대한 페구의 옛 왕들조차 \"흰 코끼리의 군주\"라는 칭호를 자신들의 온갖 호언장담하는 지배의 칭호들 위에 두었고, 현대 시암의 왕들 또한 그 눈처럼 흰 네발짐승을 왕실의 깃발에 펼쳐 내걸었으며, 하노버의 깃발에도 눈처럼 흰 군마(軍馬) 한 마리가 그려져 있고, 군림하던 로마의 상속자인 저 위대한 오스트리아 제국, 카이사르의 후예인 그 제국 역시 황제의 빛깔로 바로 그 제왕의 색조를 삼았으며, 또한 이 색의 탁월함은 인류 자신에게도 적용되어 백인에게 모든 거무스름한 종족 위에 군림하는 이상적 지배권을 부여하는가 하면, 이 모든 것에 더하여 흰빛은 심지어 기쁨의 표상이 되기도 하여 로마인들 사이에서는 흰 돌이 즐거운 날을 표시했고, 또한 인간의 다른 여러 정서와 상징 속에서도 이 같은 빛깔이 수많은 애틋하고 고결한 것들의 표상으로 — 신부의 순결과 노년의 자애로움의 표상으로 — 쓰였으며, 아메리카의 붉은 사람들 사이에서는 흰 조가비 띠를 건네는 것이 가장 깊은 명예의 서약이었고, 또한 많은 풍토에서 흰빛은 재판관의 흰담비 모피 속에 깃든 정의의 위엄을 상징하며 젖빛처럼 흰 준마들이 끄는 왕과 왕비의 나날의 위의(威儀)에 한몫을 보태고, 심지어 가장 존엄한 종교들의 더 높은 신비 속에서도 그것은 신성한 무결함과 권능의 상징이 되어, 페르시아의 불 숭배자들에게는 흰빛으로 갈라진 불꽃이 제단 위에서 가장 거룩한 것으로 여겨졌으며, 그리스 신화에서는 위대한 제우스 신마저 눈처럼 흰 황소로 화신(化身)했고, 또한 고결한 이로쿼이 족에게는 한겨울에 신성한 흰 개를 제물로 바치는 의식이 그들 신학에서 단연 가장 거룩한 축제였으니, 그 티 없고 충직한 짐승이야말로 자신들의 충성을 알리는 해마다의 소식과 더불어 위대한 정령에게 보낼 수 있는 가장 순결한 사자(使者)로 여겨졌으며, 또한 모든 기독교 사제는 흰빛을 뜻하는 라틴어 단어에서 직접 그들 성스러운 제의의 한 부분, 곧 검은 사제복 아래 입는 흰 장백의(長白衣)의 이름을 따왔고, 로마 가톨릭 신앙의 거룩한 장엄함 속에서도 흰빛은 특별히 우리 주님의 수난을 기리는 데 쓰이며, 성 요한의 묵시 속에서는 구원받은 자들에게 흰 옷이 주어지고 스물네 명의 장로가 위대한 흰 보좌 앞에 흰 옷을 입고 서 있으며 거기 앉으신 거룩한 분은 양털처럼 희다고 하였으되 — 이 모든 누적된 연상에도, 곧 달콤하고 명예롭고 숭고한 그 무엇과 결부되어 있음에도, 이 빛깔의 가장 깊숙한 관념 속에는 여전히 붙잡기 힘든 그 무엇이 도사리고 있어, 피 속의 그 붉음이 자아내는 공포보다도 더한 공황(恐慌)을 영혼에 들이친다.\n\n바로 이 붙잡기 힘든 성질이야말로, 흰빛에 대한 생각이 보다 다정한 연상들에서 떨어져 나와 그 자체로 끔찍한 어떤 대상과 결합될 때, 그 공포를 극한의 경계까지 치솟게 하는 원인이다. 극지의 흰곰과 열대의 흰 상어를 보라. 그 매끄럽고 비늘 같은 흰빛이 아니라면 무엇이 그것들을 그토록 초월적인 공포로 만들겠는가? 그 소름 끼치는 흰빛이야말로, 무언의 음흉한 표정에 그토록 혐오스러운 온화함을 — 무시무시함보다도 더 역겨운 그 온화함을 — 부여하는 것이다. 그리하여 문장(紋章)의 외피를 두른 사나운 송곳니의 호랑이조차도, 흰 수의를 두른 곰이나 상어만큼 용기를 휘청거리게 만들지는 못한다.*\n\n* 북극곰에 관하여, 이 문제를 더 깊이 파고들고자 하는 이는 어쩌면 이렇게 주장할지도 모른다. 곧, 그 짐승의 견딜 수 없는 흉측함을 한층 더 돋우는 것은 따로 떼어 본 흰빛 그 자체가 아니라는 것이다. 분석해 보면, 그 가중된 흉측함은 다만 다음과 같은 사정에서 비롯될 뿐이라고 말할 수 있으리라. 곧 그 짐승의 책임 없는 흉포함이 천상의 무구함과 사랑의 양털을 두르고 있다는 사정, 그리하여 우리 마음속에 그토록 상반된 두 감정을 한데 끌어모음으로써 북극곰이 그토록 부자연스러운 대비로 우리를 두렵게 한다는 것이다. 그러나 이 모든 것이 사실이라 치더라도, 흰빛이 아니었던들 그 강화된 공포는 결코 없었을 것이다.\n\n흰 상어로 말하자면, 그 짐승이 평소의 기분으로 있을 때 보이는 그 희고 미끄러지는 유령 같은 정적은 묘하게도 극지의 네발짐승이 지닌 그 같은 성질과 들어맞는다. 이 독특함을 가장 생생하게 짚어 낸 것은 프랑스인들이 그 물고기에게 붙인 이름이다. 로마 가톨릭의 죽은 자를 위한 미사는 \"레퀴엠 에테르남(영원한 안식)\"으로 시작되며, 거기서 레퀴엠이라는 말이 미사 자체와 온갖 장례 음악을 가리키게 되었다. 그런데 이 상어가 지닌 죽음의 희고 고요한 정적과 그 온화한 치명성을 빗대어, 프랑스인들은 그것을 르캥(Requin)이라 부른다.\n\n신천옹을 떠올려 보라. 모든 상상 속에서 그 흰 유령이 헤쳐 나아가는 저 영적 경이와 창백한 두려움의 구름은 어디서 오는 것인가? 그 주문을 처음 던진 것은 콜리지가 아니다. 그것은 하느님의 위대하고 아첨할 줄 모르는 계관시인, 곧 자연이었다.*\n\n* 내가 난생처음 본 신천옹을 나는 기억한다. 그것은 남극해에 바싹 닿은 바다에서, 한차례 오래 이어진 폭풍이 부는 동안의 일이었다. 오전 비번을 마치고 아래에서 올라와 구름이 잔뜩 낀 갑판에 발을 디뎠는데, 거기 주 승강구 위에 내동댕이쳐진 채로, 티 한 점 없는 흰빛의 제왕다운, 깃털 달린 존재가 보였다. 그것은 갈고리처럼 굽은, 숭고한 로마식 부리를 지니고 있었다. 이따금 그것은 마치 어떤 거룩한 방주를 끌어안기라도 하려는 듯, 그 광대한 대천사의 날개를 활짝 펼쳤다. 경이로운 파닥임과 떨림이 그것을 뒤흔들었다. 몸은 다친 데 없었으나, 그것은 초자연적 고뇌에 빠진 어느 왕의 유령처럼 울음소리를 냈다. 그 형언할 수 없는 기이한 눈을 통해, 나는 하느님을 사로잡은 비밀들을 엿보는 듯한 느낌을 받았다. 천사들 앞에 선 아브라함처럼, 나는 머리를 조아렸다. 그 흰 것은 너무도 희었고, 그 날개는 너무도 넓었으며, 영영 추방된 그 바다 위에서 나는 전통과 도시들에 얽힌 저 비참하게 뒤틀린 기억들을 잃어버렸다. 나는 오래도록 그 깃털의 경이를 바라보았다. 그때 내 안을 꿰뚫고 지나간 것들을 나는 말로 다 할 수 없으며, 다만 넌지시 비칠 수 있을 뿐이다. 그러나 마침내 나는 정신을 차렸고, 돌아서서 한 선원에게 이것이 무슨 새냐고 물었다. 고니(goney)요, 하고 그가 대답했다. 고니라니! 그런 이름은 그때껏 들어 본 적이 없었다. 이토록 영광스러운 존재가 뭍의 사람들에게 전혀 알려져 있지 않다는 것이 과연 있을 수 있는 일인가! 결코 그럴 수 없다! 그러나 얼마 뒤에야 나는 고니가 신천옹을 가리키는 어느 뱃사람의 이름임을 알게 되었다. 그러니 우리 갑판 위에서 그 새를 보았을 때 내가 느낀 저 신비로운 인상들과 콜리지의 격렬한 운율시가 무슨 관계가 있었을 리는 도저히 없는 것이다. 그때 나는 그 시를 읽은 적도 없었고, 그 새가 신천옹임을 알지도 못했으니까. 그러나 이렇게 말함으로써 나는 다만 그 시와 그 시인의 고귀한 공적을 에둘러 조금 더 환히 빛내 줄 따름이다.\n\n그렇다면 나는 단언하노니, 그 주문의 비밀은 무엇보다도 그 새의 경이로운 육신의 흰빛 속에 도사리고 있다. 이 진실은, 어법의 모순으로 회색 신천옹이라 불리는 새들이 있다는 점에서 한층 더 분명하게 드러난다. 나는 이런 새들을 여러 번 보았으나, 저 남극의 새를 보았을 때와 같은 감정을 느낀 적은 한 번도 없었다.\n\n그런데 그 신비로운 것은 어떻게 잡혔던가? 소리 죽여 말할 테니 누구에게도 발설하지 말라. 그것은 새가 바다 위에 떠 있을 때 음흉한 낚싯바늘과 낚싯줄로 잡혔다. 마침내 선장은 그것을 우편배달부로 삼아, 가죽에 글자를 새긴 표찰에 배의 날짜와 위치를 적어 그 목에 매달고는 놓아주었다. 그러나 나는 의심하지 않는다. 사람을 위한 그 가죽 표찰은, 그 흰 새가 날개를 접고 기도하고 흠숭하는 천사들 무리에 합류하러 날아갔을 때, 천국에서 떼어졌으리라는 것을!\n\n우리 서부의 연대기와 인디언 전설 가운데 가장 유명한 것은 대초원의 백마(白馬) 이야기다. 젖빛처럼 흰 그 장려한 군마는 눈이 크고 머리는 작으며 가슴은 떡 벌어졌고, 그 드높고 모든 것을 깔보는 듯한 거동에는 천 명의 군주가 지닌 위엄이 서려 있었다. 그것은 광대한 야생마 무리의 선택된 크세르크세스였으니, 그 시절 그 무리의 목초지는 로키 산맥과 앨러게이니 산맥만이 울타리를 쳐 줄 뿐이었다. 그것은 그 불타는 듯한 무리의 선두에 서서, 매일 저녁 빛의 군대를 이끄는 저 선택된 별처럼 서쪽으로 무리 지어 나아갔다. 번뜩이며 쏟아지는 폭포 같은 갈기와 곡선을 그리는 혜성 같은 꼬리는, 금은 세공장이가 마련해 줄 수 있는 그 어떤 마구보다도 더 찬란한 장식을 그것에 둘러 주었다. 타락하지 않은 그 서부 세계의, 더없이 제왕적이고 대천사 같은 환영(幻影)이었으니, 늙은 덫 사냥꾼들과 수렵꾼들의 눈에는 아담이 신처럼 위엄 있게, 이 강대한 군마처럼 떡 벌어진 이마에 두려움 없이 거닐던 저 태곳적의 영광을 되살려 주는 것이었다. 오하이오 강처럼 끝없이 평원 위로 흘러내리는 무수한 부대의 선봉에서 부관들과 원수들 사이를 행진하든, 혹은 지평선 둘레에서 풀을 뜯는 신민들에게 에워싸인 채 백마가 그 서늘한 젖빛을 뚫고 발그레해지는 따뜻한 콧김을 내뿜으며 그들을 질주하듯 사열하든, 어떤 모습으로 나타나든 간에 그것은 언제나 가장 용감한 인디언들에게조차 떨리는 경외와 외경의 대상이었다. 그리고 이 고귀한 말에 관해 전설로 전해 내려오는 기록을 보건대, 그것에 신성함을 그토록 입혀 준 것이 무엇보다도 그 영적인 흰빛이었으며, 또한 이 신성함 속에는 흠숭을 자아내는 동시에 어떤 이름 없는 공포를 강요하는 그 무엇이 깃들어 있었음은 의심할 여지가 없다.\n\n그러나 이 흰빛이, 백마와 신천옹을 감싸던 저 부수적이고 기이한 영광을 모조리 잃어버리는 다른 사례들도 있다.\n\n백색증 환자에게서 흰빛이 그토록 유난히 눈을 거슬리게 하고 종종 충격을 주어, 때로는 제 살붙이와 핏줄에게마저 혐오를 사게 하는 까닭은 무엇인가! 그것은 그를 감싸고 있는 저 흰빛이니, 그가 지닌 이름 자체가 그것을 말해 준다. 백색증 환자도 여느 사람만큼 잘 빚어진 몸을 지녔고 — 실질적인 기형이라곤 없는데도 — 그럼에도 온몸에 퍼진 흰빛이라는 이 단순한 외양 하나가 가장 추한 기형아보다도 그를 더 기이하게 흉측해 보이게 만든다. 어찌하여 그러한가?\n\n또한 전혀 다른 측면에서, 자연은 그 가장 손에 잡히지 않으면서도 결코 덜 악의적이지는 않은 작용들 속에서, 이 무시무시함의 으뜸가는 속성을 제 군대에 끌어들이기를 빠뜨리지 않는다. 그 눈처럼 흰 모습 때문에, 남쪽 바다의 철갑을 두른 유령은 '하얀 돌풍'이라 이름 붙여졌다. 또한 몇몇 역사적 사례에서, 인간 악의의 술책 역시 이토록 강력한 조력자를 빠뜨리지 않았다. 프루아사르의 그 대목, 곧 헨트의 절망에 찬 '하얀 두건단'이 자기네 파벌의 눈처럼 흰 상징으로 얼굴을 가린 채 시장 한복판에서 그들의 행정관을 살해하는 그 장면이 그 효과를 얼마나 격렬하게 드높이는가!\n\n또한 어떤 것들에서는, 온 인류가 공통으로 물려받은 경험 역시 이 빛깔의 초자연성을 증언하기를 빠뜨리지 않는다. 죽은 자의 모습에서 보는 이를 가장 소름 끼치게 하는 단 하나의 눈에 보이는 성질이 거기 머무는 대리석 같은 창백함이라는 것은 좀처럼 의심할 수 없으니, 마치 그 창백함이 이 세상에서의 죽을 운명의 전율을 나타내는 만큼이나 저세상에서의 경악의 표지(標識)이기라도 한 듯하다. 그리고 죽은 자의 그 창백함에서, 우리는 그들을 감싸는 수의의 저 표현력 짙은 빛깔을 빌려 온다. 심지어 우리의 미신 속에서도 우리는 우리의 유령들에 그 같은 눈빛의 망토를 둘러 주기를 빠뜨리지 않으니, 모든 망령은 젖빛처럼 흰 안개 속에서 일어선다 — 그렇다, 이 공포들이 우리를 사로잡는 동안 한마디 덧붙이자면, 복음 기록자에 의해 인격화된 공포의 왕마저도 그 창백한 말을 타고 달리는 것이다.\n\n그러므로 다른 기분에 잠겼을 때, 그가 흰빛으로 그 어떤 장엄하거나 우아한 것을 상징하든 간에, 그것이 그 가장 깊고 이상화된 의미에서 영혼에 어떤 독특한 환영을 불러일으킨다는 것을 부정할 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없다.\n\n그러나 이 점이 이론(異論) 없이 확정된다 하더라도, 죽을 운명의 인간이 그것을 어떻게 설명할 수 있겠는가? 그것을 분석한다는 것은 불가능해 보인다. 그렇다면 우리는, 이 흰빛이라는 것이 — 비록 한동안은 두려움을 안겨 주도록 짜인 모든 직접적 연상에서 전적으로 혹은 대체로 벗어나 있을지라도, 그럼에도 형태야 달라졌을망정 우리에게 똑같은 마법을 행사하고 있음이 드러나는 — 그런 몇몇 사례를 인용함으로써, 우리가 찾는 저 숨은 원인으로 우리를 이끌어 줄 우연한 실마리 하나를 더듬어 만나기를 바랄 수 있을까?\n\n한번 시도해 보자. 그러나 이런 문제에서는 미묘함이 미묘함에 호소하는 법이어서, 상상력 없이는 그 누구도 다른 이를 따라 이 회랑 속으로 들어설 수 없다. 그리고 이제 제시하려는 상상적 인상들 가운데 적어도 일부는 의심할 바 없이 대다수 사람이 함께 느껴 본 것이겠으나, 그것을 그 당시에 온전히 의식했던 사람은 아마 거의 없을 터이니, 따라서 지금 그것을 떠올리지 못할지도 모른다.\n\n성령강림절이라는 날의 독특한 성격을 그저 어렴풋이밖에 알지 못하는, 가르침받지 못한 관념의 소유자에게, 그저 그 절기의 이름을 입에 올리기만 해도 어찌하여 그토록 길고 음울하며 말 없는 행렬이 — 고개를 떨군 채 갓 내린 눈을 두건처럼 뒤집어쓰고 느릿느릿 걸어가는 순례자들의 행렬이 — 환상 속에 줄지어 늘어서는가? 혹은 중부 아메리카 여러 주의 책 안 읽고 세상 물정 모르는 프로테스탄트에게, 백의(白衣)의 수사나 백의의 수녀를 지나가는 말로 언급하기만 해도 어찌하여 그토록 눈먼 석상(石像)이 영혼 속에 떠오르는가?\n\n혹은, 지하 감옥에 갇힌 전사들과 왕들의 전설을 (그것만으로는 온전히 설명되지 않거니와) 떼어 놓고 보았을 때, 무엇이 있기에 런던의 '하얀 탑'이, 그 이웃에 있는 다른 유서 깊은 건축물들 — 곧 바이워드 탑이나 심지어 '피의 탑'보다도 — 여행해 본 적 없는 아메리카인의 상상에 그토록 더 강하게 작용하는가? 또한 저 한층 더 숭고한 탑들, 곧 뉴햄프셔의 화이트 산맥은 어떠한가. 그 이름을 입에 올리기만 해도 독특한 기분에 잠긴 영혼 위로 그 거대한 유령다움이 덮쳐 오는 반면, 버지니아의 블루리지 산맥을 떠올리면 부드럽고 이슬 맺힌 아득한 몽환으로 가득 차는 것은 어찌 된 일인가? 혹은 위도와 경도를 모두 떠나서, 어찌하여 백해(白海)라는 이름은 환상 위에 그토록 유령 같은 기운을 드리우는 반면, 황해라는 이름은 옻칠한 듯 잔잔한 긴 오후를 물결 위에서 보내고 그 뒤에 더없이 현란하면서도 더없이 졸음 겨운 일몰이 이어지는, 죽을 운명의 인간다운 생각으로 우리를 달래는가? 혹은, 순전히 환상에만 호소하는 전혀 실체 없는 사례를 하나 고르자면, 중부 유럽의 옛 동화들을 읽을 때, 그 변치 않는 창백함으로 숲의 푸름 사이를 바스락 소리 하나 없이 미끄러져 다니는 하르츠 숲의 \"키 큰 창백한 사내\"는 — 어찌하여 이 유령이 블록스부르크의 온갖 고래고래 소리 지르는 작은 마귀들보다 더 무서운가?\n\n또한 리마를 두고 보건대, 그토록 기이하고 슬픈 도시로 만드는 것은, 전적으로 그 대성당을 무너뜨린 지진들에 대한 기억도, 그 미친 듯 날뛰던 바다의 아우성도, 결코 비를 뿌리지 않는 메마른 하늘의 눈물 없음도, 기울어진 첨탑들과 비틀린 갓돌들과 (닻 내린 함대의 기운 활대들처럼) 죄다 늘어진 십자가들이 드넓게 펼쳐진 광경도, 던져 놓은 카드 한 벌처럼 서로의 위로 쓰러진 집 벽들이 늘어선 교외의 거리들도 — 이런 것들만이 눈물 없는 리마를 네가 볼 수 있는 가장 기이하고 가장 슬픈 도시로 만드는 것은 아니다. 리마는 흰 너울을 썼기 때문이니, 그 슬픔의 흰빛 속에는 한층 더 높은 공포가 깃들어 있다. 피사로만큼이나 오래된 이 흰빛은 그 폐허를 영원히 새것인 채로 유지하여, 완전한 부패가 띠는 명랑한 푸르름을 허락하지 않으며, 그 무너진 성벽 위에 제 스스로의 일그러짐을 고정시켜 버리는 뇌졸중의 그 뻣뻣한 창백함을 펼쳐 놓는다.\n\n나는 안다. 흔한 통념으로는, 이 흰빛이라는 현상이 다른 면에서 끔찍한 사물들의 공포를 부풀리는 으뜸가는 작인(作因)으로 인정받지 못한다는 것을. 또한 상상력 없는 정신에게는, 다른 정신에게는 그 무서움이 거의 오로지 이 한 가지 현상에만 — 특히 그것이 무언(無言)이나 보편성에 조금이라도 다가가는 어떤 형태로든 나타날 때 — 있는 그런 외양들 속에서, 아무런 공포도 느껴지지 않는다는 것을. 이 두 가지 진술로 내가 뜻하는 바는, 아마 다음의 두 예로 각각 밝혀질 수 있을 것이다.\n\n첫째. 뱃사람은 낯선 나라의 해안에 다가갈 때, 밤중에 부서지는 파도의 굉음을 들으면 정신이 번쩍 들어 경계 태세에 들어가며, 모든 감각을 날카롭게 벼리기에 꼭 알맞을 만큼의 전율을 느낀다. 그러나 그와 똑같은 상황에서, 그를 해먹에서 불러내어 자기 배가 젖빛처럼 흰 한밤의 바다를 가르며 나아가는 모습을 보게 한다면 — 마치 에워싼 곶들로부터 빗질한 듯한 흰곰 떼가 그의 주위로 헤엄쳐 오기라도 하는 듯한 그 광경을 보게 한다면, 그제야 그는 말 없는 미신적 공포를 느낀다. 흰빛으로 물든 바다의 수의(壽衣)에 싸인 유령은 진짜 망령처럼 그에게 끔찍하니, 측연(測鉛)이 아직 수심이 깊은 곳에 있다고 아무리 안심시킨들 소용없이, 그의 심장과 키는 둘 다 가라앉고 만다. 다시 발밑에 푸른 바다가 펼쳐지기 전까지 그는 결코 마음을 놓지 못한다. 그러나 너에게 이렇게 말해 줄 뱃사람이 어디 있겠는가. \"선생, 나를 그토록 동요시킨 것은 숨은 바위에 부딪칠까 하는 두려움이라기보다, 저 흉측한 흰빛에 대한 두려움이었소\"라고?\n\n둘째. 페루 원주민 인디언에게는, 눈으로 가마를 씌운 듯한 안데스 산맥을 늘 바라보는 것이 아무런 두려움도 안겨 주지 않는다. 다만 어쩌면 그토록 광막한 고도에 군림하는 영원히 얼어붙은 황량함을 그저 상상해 볼 때, 그리고 그토록 비인간적인 적막 속에서 길을 잃는다면 그 얼마나 무서운 일일까 하는 자연스러운 공상에서나 약간의 두려움이 일 뿐이다. 서부의 오지 사냥꾼도 거의 마찬가지여서, 흩날린 눈으로 뒤덮여 나무 한 그루 잔가지 하나의 그림자조차 그 고정된 흰빛의 황홀경을 깨뜨리지 않는, 끝없는 대초원을 비교적 무심하게 바라본다. 그러나 남극해의 풍광을 마주한 뱃사람은 그렇지 않으니, 그곳에서는 때때로 서리와 대기의 힘이 부리는 어떤 지옥 같은 요술 솜씨로 말미암아, 추위에 떨며 반쯤 난파된 그가 자신의 비참함에 희망과 위안을 건네는 무지개 대신, 비쩍 마른 얼음 기념비들과 쪼개진 십자가들을 곁에 거느린 채 그를 향해 히죽 웃는 끝없는 묘지처럼 보이는 것을 바라보게 된다.\n\n그러나 너는 말하겠지. 내 생각엔, 흰빛에 관한 저 백연(白鉛) 같은 장(章)은 겁쟁이 영혼이 내건 한낱 흰 깃발에 지나지 않으며, 이슈마엘, 너는 우울증에 항복하는 것이라고.\n\n말해 보라. 어찌하여 이 힘찬 어린 망아지는, 온갖 맹수로부터 멀리 떨어진 버몬트의 어느 평화로운 골짜기에서 태어났는데도, 더없이 화창한 날에 갓 벗긴 들소 가죽을 그 등 뒤에서 흔들어 — 그것을 보지조차 못하고 그저 그 야생 짐승의 사향 내음만을 맡을 뿐인데도 — 어찌하여 그것은 펄쩍 뛰며 콧김을 내뿜고, 눈을 부릅뜬 채 미친 듯한 공포에 사로잡혀 땅을 긁어 대는가? 그 안에는 푸른 북쪽 고향에서 야생 짐승에게 들이받힌 어떤 기억도 없으니, 그것이 맡는 그 낯선 사향 내음이 지난날의 위험에 얽힌 그 무엇도 떠올려 줄 리가 없다. 이 뉴잉글랜드 망아지가, 머나먼 오리건의 검은 들소에 대해 무엇을 알겠는가?\n\n아니다. 그러나 여기서 너는 말 못 하는 짐승에게서조차 세상에 깃든 악마성을 아는 본능을 목격하는 것이다. 오리건에서 수천 마일 떨어져 있을지언정, 그 야만스러운 사향 내음을 맡는 순간, 그 갈가리 찢고 들이받는 들소 떼는, 바로 이 순간 그것들에게 짓밟혀 먼지가 되고 있을지도 모를 대초원의 버려진 야생 망아지에게만큼이나 생생하게 거기 있는 것이다.\n\n그러므로, 젖빛 바다의 그 둔탁한 일렁임도, 산을 화환처럼 두른 서리의 그 음산한 바스락거림도, 대초원에 줄지어 쌓인 눈의 그 황량한 일렁임도 — 이 모든 것은, 이슈마엘에게는 겁먹은 망아지에게 흔들어 보이는 그 들소 가죽과도 같은 것이다!\n\n비록 그 신비로운 표징이 그토록 넌지시 비치는 저 이름 없는 것들이 어디에 있는지는 둘 다 알지 못하나, 그럼에도 그 망아지와 마찬가지로 나에게도, 그것들은 어딘가에 분명히 존재할 수밖에 없다. 이 눈에 보이는 세계는 여러 모습에서 사랑으로 빚어진 듯 보이지만, 눈에 보이지 않는 영역들은 두려움으로 빚어졌던 것이다.\n\n그러나 우리는 아직 이 흰빛의 주술을 풀지 못했고, 어찌하여 그것이 그토록 강력한 힘으로 영혼에 호소하는지를 알아내지 못했다. 그리고 한층 더 기이하고 한층 더 불길한 것은 — 어찌하여, 우리가 이미 보았듯, 그것이 동시에 영적인 것들의 가장 의미심장한 상징이며, 아니 기독교도의 신을 가리는 바로 그 너울이면서도, 또한 지금 그러하듯 인류에게 가장 소름 끼치는 것들을 한층 더 강화하는 작인이기도 한가 하는 점이다.\n\n그것은 그 막연함으로 말미암아 이 우주의 무정한 공허와 광막함을 어렴풋이 드러내고, 그리하여 우리가 은하수의 흰 심연을 바라볼 때 소멸이라는 생각으로 우리를 등 뒤에서 찌르기 때문인가? 혹은 그것은, 본질에 있어 흰빛이란 하나의 빛깔이라기보다 빛깔의 눈에 보이는 부재(不在)인 동시에 모든 빛깔의 응결체이기 때문인가? 바로 이런 까닭으로, 드넓은 설경 속에는 그토록 말 없는, 의미로 가득 찬 공백이 — 우리가 움츠러들게 되는, 무신론의 무색(無色)이면서 만색(萬色)인 그 빛깔이 — 깃들어 있는 것인가? 또한 자연철학자들의 저 다른 이론, 곧 다른 모든 지상의 빛깔들이 — 모든 장엄하거나 사랑스러운 채색이, 일몰 무렵 하늘과 숲의 그 달콤한 색조가, 그렇다, 나비의 금박을 입힌 듯한 보드라운 빛깔과 어린 소녀들의 나비 같은 두 볼이 — 이 모든 것이 실은 사물 자체에 내재한 것이 아니라 다만 바깥에서 입혀진, 미묘한 기만에 지나지 않는다는 그 이론을 우리가 고려할 때, 그리하여 신격화된 자연 전체가 실은 창부(娼婦)처럼 분칠을 하고 있을 뿐이며 그 유혹이 가리는 것은 그 안의 납골당밖에 없다는 것을 — 그리고 더 나아가, 그녀의 빛깔을 하나하나 빚어내는 그 신비로운 화장품, 곧 빛이라는 저 위대한 원리가 그 자체로는 영원히 희거나 무색으로 남아 있어, 만일 매질(媒質) 없이 물질에 직접 작용한다면 튤립이든 장미든 모든 사물을 제 고유의 공백한 색조로 물들이고 말리라는 것을 우리가 헤아릴 때 — 이 모든 것을 곰곰이 따져 보면, 마비된 우주가 한 사람의 나병 환자처럼 우리 앞에 누워 있고, 색을 입히는 색안경을 눈에 쓰기를 한사코 거부하는 저 라플란드의 고집스러운 여행자들처럼, 가련한 불신자는 제 둘레의 온 전경(全景)을 감싼 그 기념비적인 흰 수의를 노려보다가 끝내 눈이 멀고 마는 것이다. 그리고 이 모든 것의 상징이 바로 그 백색증의 고래였다. 그러니 그대들은 그 불타는 듯한 추격을 이상하게 여기겠는가?\n\n\"쉿! 카바코, 자네 저 소리 들었나?\"\n\n자정 당직 시간이었다. 달빛이 맑게 흐르고 있었다. 선원들은 갑판 중앙의 담수통 하나에서 고물 난간 근처의 식수통까지 한 줄로 늘어서 있었다. 그렇게 그들은 물통을 차례로 건네며 식수통을 채웠다. 대부분은 신성하게 여겨지는 뒷갑판 구역에 서 있었으므로, 말을 하거나 발을 끌어 부스럭거리지 않으려고 조심했다. 손에서 손으로, 물통은 깊디깊은 정적 속을 오갔다. 그 정적을 깨뜨리는 것이라곤 이따금 펄럭이는 돛 소리와, 끊임없이 나아가는 용골이 내는 한결같은 웅웅거림뿐이었다.\n\n바로 이 고요의 한가운데서, 줄지어 선 사람들 중 하나로 뒤쪽 승강구 근처를 맡고 있던 아치가 옆 사람인 촐로 사내에게 위의 말을 속삭였던 것이다.\n\n\"쉿! 카바코, 자네 저 소리 들었나?\"\n\n\"물통이나 받게, 아치, 응? 무슨 소리 말인가?\"\n\n\"또 들린다네 — 승강구 아래에서 — 안 들리나 — 기침 소리야 — 꼭 기침 같았어.\"\n\n\"기침은 무슨 기침! 빈 물통이나 어서 넘기게.\"\n\n\"또야 — 또 들려! — 이번엔 누가 두셋쯤 잠결에 몸을 뒤척이는 소리 같다니까!\"\n\n\"카람바! 그만하게, 여보게, 응? 저녁에 자네가 먹은 그 물에 불린 비스킷 세 조각이 뱃속에서 뒤집히는 소리야 — 그뿐이라고. 물통이나 보게!\"\n\n\"자네야 뭐라 하든 마음대로 하게, 여보게. 난 귀가 밝거든.\"\n\n\"그래, 자네가 바로 그 사내지, 안 그런가? 낸터킷에서 바다로 오십 마일이나 나와서도 늙은 퀘이커 부인의 뜨개바늘 소리를 들었다는 그 사내 말이야. 자네가 그 작자야.\"\n\"실컷 비웃게나. 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 두고 보자고. 잘 듣게, 카바코. 뒤쪽 선창 아래에 아직 갑판에서 한 번도 모습을 보이지 않은 누군가가 있어. 그리고 우리 늙은 무굴 영감도 그걸 뭔가 알고 있는 것 같단 말이지. 새벽 당직 때 스텁이 플라스크한테 그런 낌새가 있다고 말하는 걸 내가 들었거든.\"\n\n\"쳇! 물통이나!\"\n* 무굴 영감: 에이해브 선장을 가리키는 선원들 사이의 별칭. 무굴은 인도를 호령하던 절대 군주를 뜻한다.\n\n# CHAPTER 44. The Chart.\n\n그날 밤, 에이해브가 선원들 앞에서 자신의 목적을 거칠게 다짐받은 그 광란의 의식이 있은 다음 날 밤, 한바탕 돌풍이 지나간 뒤에 그를 따라 선실로 내려가 보았더라면, 당신은 그가 고물 쪽 사물함으로 가서 누렇게 바랜 해도(海圖) 한 무더기를 둘둘 만 채로 꺼내, 나사로 고정된 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓는 광경을 보았을 것이다. 그러고는 그 앞에 앉아, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 갖가지 선과 음영을 골똘히 들여다보며, 느리지만 흔들림 없는 연필 끝으로 전에는 비어 있던 공간 위에 새로운 항로를 그려 넣는 모습을 보았을 것이다. 이따금 그는 곁에 쌓아 둔 낡은 항해일지 더미를 들춰 보았는데, 거기에는 여러 배의 갖가지 지난 항해에서 향유고래를 잡았거나 목격한 계절과 장소가 적혀 있었다.\n\n이렇게 일에 몰두하는 동안, 머리 위 쇠사슬에 매달린 묵직한 백랍 등잔은 배의 흔들림을 따라 끊임없이 좌우로 흔들리며, 그의 주름진 이마 위로 끊임없이 변하는 빛과 선의 그림자를 던졌다. 마침내 그것은 마치, 그 자신이 주름진 해도 위에 선과 항로를 그려 넣는 동안, 어떤 보이지 않는 연필이 그의 이마라는 깊이 새겨진 해도 위에도 선과 항로를 그려 넣고 있는 것처럼 보일 지경이었다.\n\n그러나 에이해브가 선실의 고독 속에서 이처럼 해도를 들여다본 것은 비단 이날 밤만의 일이 아니었다. 거의 매일 밤 해도가 펼쳐졌고, 거의 매일 밤 어떤 연필 자국은 지워지고 다른 자국이 그 자리를 대신했다. 사방 네 대양의 해도를 앞에 놓고서, 에이해브는 자기 영혼의 그 편집광적인 생각을 더욱 확실히 이루려는 일념으로 해류와 소용돌이의 미로를 더듬어 가고 있었던 것이다.\n\n리바이어던의 생태에 완전히 정통하지 못한 사람이라면, 이 행성의 테두리 없는 대양에서 단 한 마리의 고독한 짐승을 이렇게 찾아내려는 일이 터무니없이 가망 없는 짓으로 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 에이해브에게는 그렇지 않았다. 그는 모든 조수와 해류의 흐름을 알았고, 그로써 향유고래의 먹이가 떠도는 경로를 계산해 냈으며, 또한 특정 위도에서 그놈을 사냥하기에 일정하게 검증된 계절을 떠올려, 이러저러한 어장에서 먹잇감을 찾기에 가장 알맞은 날에 관하여, 거의 확신에 가까운 합당한 추정에 이를 수 있었던 것이다.\n\n실제로 향유고래가 일정한 해역을 주기적으로 찾아든다는 사실은 워낙 확실해서, 많은 사냥꾼들은 만약 그놈을 전 세계에 걸쳐 면밀히 관찰하고 연구할 수 있다면, 또 전체 포경 선단의 한 차례 항해 일지를 빠짐없이 대조할 수 있다면, 향유고래의 회유(回遊)가 청어 떼나 제비의 비행만큼이나 변함없는 규칙성을 보이리라고 믿는다. 이러한 암시에 힘입어, 향유고래의 정교한 회유 해도를 작성하려는 시도가 이루어져 왔다.*\n\n* 위 글을 쓴 이후로, 1851년 4월 16일 워싱턴 국립천문대의 모리 중위가 발표한 공식 회람에 의해 이 진술이 다행히도 뒷받침되었다. 그 회람에 따르면, 바로 그러한 해도가 완성 단계에 있으며, 그 일부가 회람에 실려 있다. \"이 해도는 대양을 위도 5도, 경도 5도의 구역으로 나누고, 각 구역을 세로로 가로질러 열두 달을 나타내는 열두 개의 칸을 두며, 가로로 가로질러서는 세 개의 선을 두는데, 하나는 각 구역에서 매달 보낸 날수를 나타내고, 나머지 둘은 향유고래든 참고래든 고래가 목격된 날수를 나타낸다.\"\n\n게다가, 한 먹이터에서 다른 먹이터로 옮겨 갈 때, 향유고래는 어떤 틀림없는 본능에 — 아니, 차라리 신으로부터의 은밀한 계시라고 해야 하리라 — 이끌려 대개 이른바 '맥(脈, vein)'을 따라 헤엄친다. 정해진 대양의 한 선을 따라 어찌나 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 정확하게 나아가는지, 그 어떤 배도 그 어떤 해도를 가지고도 그처럼 경이로운 정확성의 십분의 일도 따라가지 못한다. 비록 이런 경우에 어느 한 마리의 고래가 잡는 방향은 측량사가 그은 평행선처럼 곧고, 그 전진의 선은 피할 수 없는 제 자신의 곧은 항적에 엄격히 한정되지만, 이때 그놈이 헤엄친다고 일컬어지는 그 임의의 '맥'은 대체로 폭이 몇 마일에 이른다(맥이 넓어지거나 좁아지리라 짐작되는 데 따라 더하기도 덜하기도 한다). 그러나 그 폭은, 이 마법의 띠를 따라 신중하게 미끄러져 갈 때 포경선의 돛대 꼭대기에서 둘러볼 수 있는 시야의 범위를 결코 넘지 않는다. 요컨대 특정한 계절에 그 폭 안에서, 그 길을 따라, 회유하는 고래를 큰 확신을 가지고 기다릴 수 있다는 것이다.\n\n따라서 에이해브는 잘 알려진 별개의 먹이터에서 검증된 시기에 먹잇감을 만나기를 바랄 수 있었을 뿐만 아니라, 그 먹이터들 사이의 가장 넓은 바다를 가로지를 때에도, 자기 기량을 발휘하여 길 위의 자기 위치와 시간을 적절히 안배함으로써, 그때조차 마주칠 가망이 아주 없지는 않게끔 할 수 있었다.\n\n처음 보기에 그의 광기 어린, 그러나 여전히 체계적인 계획을 얽어맬 듯한 사정이 하나 있었다. 그러나 실제로는 어쩌면 그렇지 않았다. 무리 짓는 향유고래가 특정 어장을 찾는 일정한 계절을 가지고 있긴 하지만, 일반적으로 올해 이러저러한 위도나 경도에 출몰한 무리가 지난 계절에 그곳에서 발견된 바로 그 무리와 동일하리라고 단정할 수는 없다 — 비록 그 반대가 사실임이 입증된 독특하고 의심할 바 없는 사례들이 있긴 하지만. 일반적으로 같은 이야기가, 다만 그보다 좁은 한계 안에서, 다 자란 늙은 향유고래 가운데 외톨이와 은둔자들에게도 적용된다. 그러므로 가령 모비 딕이 지난 어느 해에 인도양의 이른바 세이셸 어장이나 일본 연안의 화산만(火山灣)에서 목격되었다 하더라도, 피쿼드호가 그 어느 곳이든 그 이후의 상응하는 계절에 찾아간다고 해서 그곳에서 틀림없이 그놈을 마주치리라는 결론이 따라 나오는 것은 아니었다. 그놈이 때때로 모습을 드러낸 다른 몇몇 먹이터에 대해서도 마찬가지였다. 그러나 이 모든 곳은 말하자면 그놈이 우연히 들르는 정거장이요 바다의 여인숙일 뿐, 오래 머무는 거처는 아닌 듯했다. 그리고 지금까지 에이해브가 자기 목적을 이룰 가망에 관해 이야기한 것은, 어떤 특정한 정해진 시기나 장소에 다다르기 전, 길가에서 마주칠 법한 사전적이고 부수적인 가망들에 대한 언급에 불과했다. 그 정해진 시기나 장소에 이르면 모든 가능성이 개연성으로 바뀌고, 에이해브가 흐뭇하게 여겼듯이, 모든 가능성이 확실성에 가장 가까운 것이 되는 것이었다. 그 정해진 특정한 시기와 장소는 하나의 전문적인 표현으로 합쳐져 있었으니 — 바로 '적도선의 계절(Season-on-the-Line)'이다. 거기서, 그때, 여러 해 연달아 모비 딕은 주기적으로 발견되었으며, 마치 태양이 한 해의 순환 속에서 황도 십이궁의 어느 한 별자리에 예고된 기간만큼 머무는 것처럼, 한동안 그 해역에 머물렀던 것이다. 또한 그곳은 흰 고래와의 치명적인 충돌이 대부분 벌어진 곳이었고, 그곳의 파도는 그놈의 행적으로 이야깃거리가 되었으며, 그곳은 또한 그 편집광 노인이 자신의 끔찍한 복수의 동기를 얻은 비극의 자리이기도 했다. 그러나 에이해브가 이 흔들림 없는 사냥에 자신의 곱씹는 영혼을 던져 넣을 때의 그 신중한 포괄성과 게으름 없는 경계심을 가지고서, 그는 앞서 말한 그 으뜸가는 사실 하나에만 모든 희망을 걸어 두려 하지 않았다 — 그것이 그 희망에 아무리 솔깃한 것이라 할지라도. 또한 그 맹세의 잠 못 이루는 집념 속에서, 그는 그사이에 끼어들 온갖 탐색을 미뤄 둘 만큼 자신의 불안한 마음을 가라앉힐 수도 없었다.\n\n그런데 피쿼드호는 바로 적도선의 계절이 막 시작될 무렵에 낸터킷을 출항했다. 그러니 어떤 노력을 기울인다 해도, 그 함장이 남쪽으로 대항해를 감행하여 케이프 혼을 돌고, 그런 뒤 위도 육십 도를 내달려, 적도 부근 태평양에 제때 도착해 그곳을 항해할 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 그러므로 그는 다음에 이어지는 계절을 기다려야 했다. 그러나 피쿼드호가 이처럼 이른 시각에 출항한 것은, 어쩌면 바로 이러한 사정의 국면을 내다보고 에이해브가 정확하게 택한 것일 수도 있었다. 왜냐하면 그의 앞에는 삼백예순닷새의 낮과 밤이라는 기간이 놓여 있었고, 그는 그 기간을 뭍에서 안달하며 견디는 대신 잡다한 사냥으로 보내려 했기 때문이다. 만약 어쩌다 흰 고래가 주기적으로 찾는 먹이터에서 멀리 떨어진 바다에서 휴가를 보내다가, 페르시아 만이나 벵골 만이나 중국해, 혹은 제 동족이 출몰하는 그 밖의 어느 해역에서 그 주름진 이마를 불쑥 쳐들 수도 있을 테니까. 그리하여 계절풍이든, 팜파스의 바람이든, 북서풍이든, 하르마탄이든, 무역풍이든 — 레반테르와 시문을 제외한 어떤 바람이든 — 모비 딕을 피쿼드호가 세계를 일주하는 항적의 그 구불구불한 갈지자 원궤 속으로 몰아넣을 수도 있었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 것을 인정한다 하더라도, 신중하고 냉정하게 따져 보면, 이것은 한낱 미친 생각에 지나지 않는 것이 아닐까? 넓고 끝없는 대양에서, 단 한 마리의 고독한 고래를, 설령 마주친다 한들, 마치 콘스탄티노플의 붐비는 큰길에서 흰 수염의 무프티를 알아보듯, 사냥꾼이 그놈만을 따로 알아볼 수 있으리라고 여기는 것이? 그렇다. 알아볼 수 있다. 모비 딕의 그 독특한 눈처럼 흰 이마와 눈처럼 흰 혹은 결코 못 알아볼 수 없는 것이기 때문이다. 게다가 내가 그 고래를 점찍어 두지 않았던가, 하고 에이해브는 자정이 한참 지나도록 해도를 들여다보다가 몸을 뒤로 젖히고 공상에 잠긴 채 혼잣말로 중얼거리곤 했다 — 그놈을 점찍어 두었거늘, 그놈이 빠져나갈 수 있겠는가? 그 널찍한 지느러미는 잃어버린 양의 귀처럼 구멍이 뚫리고 가장자리가 부채꼴로 패어 있지 않은가! 그러다 그의 광기 어린 정신은 숨 가쁜 질주를 이어 가곤 했고, 마침내 곱씹기에 지친 노곤함과 아득함이 그를 덮쳐 왔다. 그러면 그는 갑판의 트인 바람을 쐬며 기력을 되찾으려 했다. 아, 하느님이시여! 이루지 못한 복수의 욕망 하나에 사로잡혀 타들어 가는 자가 견뎌 내는 그 고통의 황홀경이란 어떤 것이란 말입니까. 그는 두 주먹을 꽉 쥔 채 잠들었다가, 제 손바닥에 제 피투성이 손톱이 박힌 채로 깨어나곤 했다.\n\n밤이면 진을 빼는, 견딜 수 없이 생생한 꿈에 시달려 해먹에서 뛰쳐나오는 일이 잦았다. 그 꿈은 낮 동안의 격렬한 생각을 다시 이어받아, 광란들이 서로 부딪치는 가운데 그것들을 끌고 다니며, 그의 불타는 머릿속에서 빙글빙글 돌리고 또 돌려, 마침내 그의 생명이 깃든 자리의 고동 자체가 견딜 수 없는 고통이 될 지경이었다. 그리고 때때로 그렇듯, 이 영적인 진통이 그의 존재를 그 근저에서부터 들어 올릴 때면, 그의 안에서 심연이 입을 벌리는 듯했고, 그 심연으로부터 갈래진 불꽃과 번개가 솟구쳐 오르며, 저주받은 악귀들이 그 한가운데로 뛰어내리라 그를 손짓해 불렀다. 이렇듯 자기 안의 지옥이 그의 발밑에서 입을 벌릴 때면, 배 안에는 사나운 비명이 울려 퍼지곤 했고, 에이해브는 불붙은 침대에서 달아나기라도 하듯, 이글거리는 눈으로 자기 선실에서 튀어나오는 것이었다. 그러나 이것은 어쩌면 어떤 잠재된 약함이나 자기 결심에 대한 두려움의 억누를 수 없는 징후가 아니라, 도리어 그 결심이 그만큼 강렬하다는 가장 명백한 표시였는지도 모른다. 왜냐하면 그런 순간에, 흰 고래를 노리는 음모 꾸미는, 달랠 길 없이 한결같은 사냥꾼인 미친 에이해브, 해먹에 든 바로 그 에이해브는, 그를 다시금 공포에 질려 거기서 튀어나오게 만든 그 주체가 아니었기 때문이다. 후자는 그의 안에 깃든 영원하고 살아 있는 원리, 곧 영혼이었다. 그리고 잠 속에서 그것은 한동안, 평소에는 그것을 바깥세상에 내보내는 수레나 대리자로 부려 먹는 그 규정하는 정신과 떨어져 나와 있었기에, 그 미쳐 날뛰는 것 — 한동안 더 이상 그것의 한 부분이 아니게 된 그것 — 의 살을 태우는 가까움에서 저절로 달아나려 한 것이었다. 그러나 정신이란 영혼과 결합되지 않으면 존재하지 못하는 까닭에, 에이해브의 경우에는 이러했음이 틀림없다. 곧, 자신의 모든 생각과 공상을 그 하나의 지고한 목적에 내맡겨 버린 결과, 그 목적이 제 의지의 순전한 뿌리 깊음으로 말미암아, 신들과 악마들을 거스르면서까지 스스로 떠맡은 일종의 독립된 존재로 자기 자신을 밀어붙였던 것이다. 아니, 그것은 자신이 결합되어 있던 그 공통의 생명력이 부르지도 않았고 아비도 없는 그 탄생에 질겁하여 달아나는 동안에도, 음산하게 살아남아 불타오를 수 있었던 것이다. 그러므로 에이해브로 보이는 것이 제 선실에서 뛰쳐나올 때, 육신의 두 눈에서 이글거리며 내다보던 그 고통받는 정신은, 한동안 텅 비워진 것, 곧 형체 없는 몽유병적 존재요, 분명 살아 있는 빛의 한 줄기이긴 하되, 물들일 대상이 없는, 그리하여 그 자체로 하나의 공백이었던 것이다. 신이여 그대를 도우소서, 노인이여, 그대의 생각이 그대 안에 한 피조물을 만들어 냈으니. 강렬한 생각으로 자신을 이렇게 프로메테우스로 만든 자, 그자의 그 심장을 한 마리 독수리가 영원히 파먹는다. 그리고 그 독수리는 바로 그자가 만들어 낸 그 피조물이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 45. The Affidavit.\n\n이 책에 담긴 이야기다운 이야기에 관한 한, 그리고 사실 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 습성에 관한 한두 가지 매우 흥미롭고 진기한 세부 사항을 간접적으로 다룬다는 점에서, 앞 장(章)의 앞부분은 이 책 전체에서 찾아볼 수 있는 가장 중요한 대목 가운데 하나다. 그러나 그 핵심 내용은 제대로 이해되기 위해, 그리고 나아가 이 사안의 주요 요점이 자연 그대로의 진실임을 두고 그 주제 전반에 대한 깊은 무지가 어떤 이들의 마음속에 불러일으킬지 모를 온갖 의심을 걷어 내기 위해, 여전히 더 자세하고 더 친근하게 풀어 놓을 필요가 있다.\n\n나는 이 과업의 이 부분을 체계적으로 수행할 마음이 없다. 다만 고래잡이로서 내가 실제로, 또는 믿을 만하게 알고 있는 여러 사례를 따로따로 인용함으로써 원하는 인상을 만들어 내는 것으로 만족하려 한다. 그리고 이러한 인용들로부터 — 내가 보기에 — 목표로 삼은 결론이 자연스럽게 절로 따라 나올 것이다.\n\n첫째: 나는 고래 한 마리가 작살을 맞고도 완벽하게 달아났다가, 일정한 시간이 지난 뒤(한 경우에는 삼 년이 흘렀다) 다시 같은 사람의 손에 작살을 맞고 죽임을 당해, 그 몸에서 동일한 개인 표식이 새겨진 두 개의 작살이 빠져나온 사례를 세 차례나 직접 알고 있다. 두 작살을 던진 사이에 삼 년이 흐른 경우에는 — 어쩌면 그보다 좀 더 되었을지도 모른다 — 작살을 던진 그 사람이 그 사이에 어느 무역선을 타고 아프리카로 항해를 떠났다가 그곳에 상륙하여 어느 탐험대에 합류했고, 내륙 깊숙이 파고들어 거의 이 년에 걸쳐 떠돌았는데, 그동안 그는 뱀과 야만인과 호랑이, 독기 어린 장기(瘴氣), 그리고 미지의 땅 한복판을 헤매는 데 따르는 온갖 흔한 위험에 거듭 목숨을 위협받았다. 한편 그가 작살로 찔렀던 고래 또한 제 나름의 여행을 하고 있었을 것이 분명하니, 의심할 바 없이 그놈은 지구를 세 번이나 일주하며 그 옆구리로 아프리카의 모든 해안을 스치고 다녔을 것이다. 그러나 다 부질없는 일이었다. 이 사람과 이 고래가 다시 만났고, 한쪽이 다른 한쪽을 제압했다. 거듭 말하지만, 나 자신이 이와 비슷한 사례를 세 차례 알고 있다. 즉 그중 두 경우에서 나는 고래가 작살에 찔리는 것을 보았고, 두 번째 공격이 이루어진 뒤 죽은 고래 몸에서 각각의 표식이 새겨진 두 개의 작살이 빠져나오는 것을 보았다. 삼 년의 시간이 흐른 그 경우에는, 마침 내가 처음과 마지막 두 번 다 보트에 타고 있었는데, 마지막 번에 나는 고래의 눈 밑에 난 어떤 특이한 큼직한 사마귀를 또렷이 알아보았으니, 그것은 삼 년 전에 내가 거기서 눈여겨봐 두었던 것이었다. 삼 년이라고 말하지만, 사실 나는 그보다 더 되었으리라고 거의 확신한다. 그러니 여기 내가 직접 그 진실을 아는 세 가지 사례가 있다. 그러나 나는 이 문제에 관한 진실성을 의심할 만한 근거가 전혀 없는 사람들에게서 그 밖에도 많은 사례를 전해 들었다.\n\n둘째: 뭍의 세상 사람들이야 아무리 모를지언정, 향유고래잡이 업계에서는 망망대해의 어떤 특정한 고래가 멀리 떨어진 시기와 장소에서도 널리 알아볼 수 있는 존재였던, 기억할 만한 역사적 사례가 여럿 있었다는 것이 잘 알려져 있다. 그런 고래가 그처럼 이름이 붙게 된 까닭이 처음부터 전적으로 다른 고래들과 구별되는 그 몸의 특이함 때문만은 아니었다. 우연히 마주친 어떤 고래가 그 점에서 아무리 특이하다 한들, 사람들은 그놈을 죽여서 끓여 특별히 값진 기름으로 만들어 버림으로써 그 특이함에 곧장 종지부를 찍어 버리기 때문이다. 아니다. 그 까닭은 이러했다. 고래잡이의 치명적인 경험들로 말미암아, 마치 리날도 리날디니를 둘러싸고 그랬듯, 그런 고래를 둘러싸고 위험함이라는 무시무시한 위세가 감돌았던 것이다. 그리하여 대다수 고래잡이들은 그놈이 바다 위에서 자기들 곁을 어슬렁거리는 것을 발견하더라도 더 친밀한 사귐을 도모하려 들지 않고, 그저 방수모에 손을 갖다 대어 아는 척하는 것으로 만족했다. 마치 뭍에서 어쩌다 성마른 거물 한 사람을 알게 된 가련한 작자들이, 행여 사귐을 더 밀고 나갔다가는 그 주제넘음에 대한 대가로 단번에 한 대 얻어맞을까 봐, 길에서 멀찍이 떨어져 눈에 거슬리지 않게 인사나 건네는 것과 같았다.\n\n그러나 이 이름난 고래들은 저마다 대단한 개인적 명성을 누렸을 뿐 아니라 — 아니, 그것은 온 바다에 두루 퍼진 명망이라 불러야 마땅하다. 살아생전에 유명했을 뿐 아니라 죽은 뒤에도 선원실 이야기 속에서 불멸의 존재가 되었으며, 나아가 하나의 이름이 누리는 온갖 권리와 특권과 영예를 인정받았으니, 실로 캄비세스나 카이사르 못지않은 이름을 지녔던 것이다. 그렇지 않은가, 오 티모르 톰(Timor Tom)이여! 빙산처럼 상처투성이인 그대, 이름난 리바이어던(Leviathan)이여, 그토록 오랫동안 같은 이름의 동방 해협에 숨어 지내며, 옴바이의 야자수 우거진 해변에서 그 물줄기가 자주 목격되던 그대여. 그렇지 않은가, 오 뉴질랜드 잭(New Zealand Jack)이여! 문신의 땅 근방에서 제 항적을 가로지르던 모든 순항선들의 공포였던 그대여. 그렇지 않은가, 오 모르콴(Morquan)이여! 일본의 왕이여, 사람들 말로는 그 드높이 솟구치는 물줄기가 때로 하늘을 배경으로 새하얀 십자가의 형상을 띠곤 했다는 그대여. 그렇지 않은가, 오 돈 미겔(Don Miguel)이여! 등에 신비로운 상형문자가 마치 늙은 거북의 등딱지처럼 새겨진 그대, 칠레의 고래여! 쉽게 말하자면, 여기 고래학사(史)를 공부하는 이들에게 마치 고전학자들에게 마리우스나 술라가 그러하듯 익히 알려진 네 마리 고래가 있는 셈이다.\n\n그러나 이것이 전부가 아니다. 뉴질랜드 톰과 돈 미겔은 여러 시기에 걸쳐 갖가지 선박의 보트들 사이에서 큰 참화를 일으킨 끝에, 마침내 용맹한 고래잡이 선장들에게 표적이 되어 조직적으로 수색되고 추격당해 죽임을 당했다. 그 선장들은 오로지 그 분명한 목적을 염두에 두고 닻을 끌어올렸으니, 이는 옛날 버틀러 선장이 내러갠셋 숲을 뚫고 나서며 저 악명 높고 살기등등한 야만인 애너원, 곧 인디언(Indian) 왕 필립의 으뜸가는 전사를 사로잡으리라 마음먹었던 것과 다르지 않았다.\n\n내가 보기에 중요하다고 여겨지는 한두 가지 다른 일들을 언급하기에, 바로 이 자리보다 더 나은 곳을 찾을 수 없을 듯하다. 그것들은 인쇄된 형태로써 흰 고래(White Whale) 이야기 전체의 타당성을, 특히 그 대참사의 타당성을 모든 면에서 확립해 주기 때문이다. 이는 진실이 오류 못지않게 받침대를 필요로 하는, 저 맥 빠지는 사례들 가운데 하나이기 때문이다. 대다수 뭍사람들은 이 세상의 가장 평범하고도 가장 뚜렷한 경이들 가운데 일부에 대해서조차 너무나 무지하여, 고래잡이의 평범한 사실들, 곧 역사적인 것이든 그 밖의 것이든, 그에 관한 약간의 암시라도 없다면 모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 괴물 같은 우화라고, 아니 그보다 더 나쁘고 더 가증스럽게도 흉측하고 견딜 수 없는 알레고리라고 비웃어 넘길지도 모른다.\n\n첫째: 대다수 사람들은 이 위대한 고래잡이업의 일반적인 위험들에 관해 어렴풋하고 스치는 관념 정도는 지니고 있으나, 그 위험들에 대해서, 그리고 그 위험들이 되풀이되는 빈도에 대해서 확고하고 생생한 개념 같은 것은 전혀 갖고 있지 못하다. 그 한 가지 까닭은 아마도, 고래잡이에서 실제로 일어나는 재난과 우발적 사고로 인한 죽음 가운데 단 쉰에 하나도 본국에서 공적인 기록으로 남는 일이 없기 때문일 것이다 — 설령 남는다 해도 그 기록은 덧없이 곧 잊히고 만다. 지금 이 순간 어쩌면 뉴기니 앞바다에서 고래 밧줄에 걸려, 깊이 잠수하는 리바이어던에게 끌려 바다 밑바닥으로 내려가고 있을 저 가련한 친구의 이름이, 그대가 내일 아침 식사 자리에서 읽을 신문의 부고란에 실리리라고 그대는 생각하는가? 아니다. 여기와 뉴기니 사이의 우편이라는 것이 몹시 불규칙하기 때문이다. 사실 그대는 뉴기니로부터 직접적이든 간접적이든 정규 소식이라 할 만한 것을 들어 본 적이 있는가? 그러나 내가 그대에게 말하건대, 내가 태평양으로 떠났던 어느 항해에서, 여러 다른 배들 가운데 우리는 서른 척의 각기 다른 배와 말을 나누었는데, 그 모든 배마다 고래로 인한 죽음을 겪었고, 그중 더러는 한 번이 아니었으며, 세 척은 저마다 보트 한 척의 선원 전원을 잃은 터였다. 제발 부탁하노니, 그대의 등잔과 양초를 아껴 쓰라! 그대가 태우는 기름 한 갤런마다, 그것을 위해 적어도 사람의 피 한 방울은 흘려졌으니.\n\n둘째: 뭍의 사람들은 실로 고래가 어마어마한 힘을 지닌 어마어마한 생물이라는 막연한 관념 정도는 지니고 있다. 그러나 내가 그들에게 이 이중의 어마어마함을 보여 주는 어떤 구체적인 사례를 들려줄 때마다, 그들은 으레 내 익살스러움을 의미심장하게 추켜세우곤 했다. 그러나 내 영혼을 걸고 단언하건대, 나는 모세가 이집트의 재앙들에 관한 역사를 기록했을 때 그러했던 것 못지않게 익살을 부릴 생각이 조금도 없었다.\n\n그러나 다행스럽게도 내가 여기서 밝히려는 특정한 요점은 전적으로 내 것이 아닌 증언 위에서 확립될 수 있다. 그 요점이란 이것이다. 향유고래는 어떤 경우에는 충분히 강력하고, 영리하며, 판단력을 갖춘 악의로써, 직접적인 사전 계획 아래 커다란 배 한 척을 들이받아 완전히 부수고 침몰시킬 만큼 강하다는 것, 그리고 더 나아가, 향유고래가 실제로 그런 일을 해냈다는 것이다.\n\n첫째: 1820년에 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 폴러드 선장이 지휘하는 에식스호가 태평양에서 항해하고 있었다. 어느 날 그 배는 물줄기를 발견하고 보트들을 내려 향유고래 떼를 추격했다. 머지않아 고래 여러 마리가 부상을 입었는데, 그때 별안간 아주 커다란 고래 한 마리가 보트들로부터 빠져나와 무리에서 솟구쳐 나오더니 곧장 배를 향해 돌진해 왔다. 그놈은 자기 이마를 배의 선체에 들이박아 어찌나 심하게 부숴 놓았던지, \"십 분\"도 채 안 되어 배는 가라앉으며 옆으로 쓰러져 버렸다. 그 뒤로 그 배의 멀쩡한 널빤지 한 조각도 보인 일이 없었다. 가장 혹독한 노출을 견뎌 낸 끝에 선원 일부가 보트를 타고 뭍에 닿았다. 마침내 본국으로 돌아온 폴러드 선장은 또 다른 배를 지휘하여 다시 한번 태평양으로 항해를 떠났으나, 신들은 그를 미지의 암초와 부서지는 파도 위에서 또다시 난파시켰다. 두 번째로 그의 배가 완전히 잃어버렸고, 그는 곧장 바다와 인연을 끊겠노라 맹세하고는 그 뒤로 다시는 바다를 넘보지 않았다. 오늘날 폴러드 선장은 낸터킷에 거주하고 있다. 나는 그 비극이 일어났을 당시 에식스호의 일등 항해사였던 오언 체이스를 만나 본 적이 있다. 나는 그의 꾸밈없고 충실한 기록을 읽어 보았다. 나는 그의 아들과 이야기를 나누어 보았다. 그리고 이 모든 일이 그 대참사의 현장에서 불과 몇 마일 안쪽에서 이루어졌다.*\n\n* 다음은 체이스의 기록에서 발췌한 것이다. \"모든 사실이 그놈의 행동을 이끈 것이 결코 우연 따위가 아니라는 결론으로 나를 몰아가는 듯했다. 그놈은 짧은 간격을 두고 배에 두 차례 따로 공격을 가했는데, 그 방향으로 미루어 보건대 두 번 다 정면에서 이루어짐으로써 두 물체의 속도를 충돌에 합치도록 계산된 것이어서 우리에게 가장 큰 손상을 입히도록 되어 있었으니, 그 일을 이루기 위해서는 그놈이 행한 바로 그 정확한 기동이 필요했다. 그 형상은 더없이 끔찍했으며, 원한과 분노를 드러내는 듯한 것이었다. 그놈은 우리가 바로 직전에 들어갔던, 그리고 우리가 그놈의 동료 셋을 작살로 찌른 바로 그 무리에서 곧장 나왔으니, 마치 동료들이 겪은 고통에 대한 복수심에 불타오르는 듯했다.\" 또 이렇게 적었다. \"어쨌거나, 모든 정황을 한데 모아 보면, 그 모든 것이 내 눈앞에서 벌어졌고, 또 그 당시 내 마음속에 고래 쪽의 단호하고 계산된 악의(그 인상들 가운데 많은 것을 나는 지금 다 떠올릴 수 없다)라는 인상을 자아냈으니, 그것들이 나로 하여금 내 견해가 옳다고 확신하게 만든다.\"\n\n다음은 그가 배를 떠난 뒤 한동안, 어느 환대해 줄 해안에라도 닿을 수 있을지 거의 절망에 빠진 채 칠흑 같은 밤에 지붕도 없는 보트 위에서 떠올린 상념들이다. \"어두운 바다와 부풀어 오르는 물결은 아무것도 아니었다. 어떤 무시무시한 폭풍에 삼켜지지나 않을까, 숨은 암초에 부딪혀 부서지지나 않을까 하는 두려움과, 그 밖에 두려움을 자아내는 온갖 평범한 상념들은 한순간의 생각조차 할 가치가 없는 듯했다. 음산해 보이는 난파선의 잔해와, 그 고래의 끔찍한 형상과 복수심이 온통 내 상념을 사로잡았으니, 마침내 다시 날이 밝아 올 때까지 그러했다.\"\n\n또 다른 대목에서 — 45쪽에서 — 그는 \"그 짐승의 불가사의하고 치명적인 공격\"을 이야기한다.\n\n둘째: 역시 낸터킷 소속인 유니언호가 1807년에 비슷한 습격으로 아조레스 앞바다에서 완전히 침몰했는데, 이 대참사의 믿을 만한 세부 사항을 나는 한 번도 우연히 마주친 적이 없다. 다만 고래잡이들로부터 이따금 그에 대한 우발적인 언급을 들은 일은 있다.\n\n셋째: 한 십팔 년에서 이십 년쯤 전에, 당시 미국 일급 전투용 슬루프함을 지휘하던 J 준장이 마침 샌드위치 제도 오아후 항구에 정박한 어느 낸터킷 배에서 여러 고래잡이 선장들과 함께 식사를 하게 되었다. 이야기가 고래에 미치자, 준장은 그 자리에 있던 직업적인 신사들이 고래에 부여하는 그 놀라운 힘에 대해 기꺼이 회의적인 태도를 보였다. 예컨대 그는 어떤 고래가 자신의 튼튼한 슬루프함을 들이받아 골무 한 개분만큼의 물이라도 새게 할 수 있다는 것을 단호히 부정했다. 좋다. 그러나 뒷이야기가 더 남아 있다. 몇 주 뒤, 준장은 이 난공불락의 함선을 타고 발파라이소를 향해 출항했다. 그러나 그는 도중에 어느 풍채 좋은 향유고래에게 가로막혔으니, 그놈은 잠깐 동안 그와 은밀한 볼일이 있다고 청해 온 셈이었다. 그 볼일이란 준장의 함선에 어찌나 세찬 일격을 가하는 것이었던지, 준장은 펌프란 펌프를 모조리 가동하면서도 곧장 가장 가까운 항구로 달려가 배를 기울여 수리할 수밖에 없었다. 나는 미신을 믿는 사람이 아니지만, 그 고래와 준장의 면담을 천우신조라고 여긴다. 타르수스의 사울도 비슷한 공포로 인해 불신에서 회심하지 않았던가? 내가 그대에게 말하노니, 향유고래는 어떤 허튼수작도 가만두지 않는다.\n\n이제 나는 이 글을 쓰는 사람에게 특히 흥미로운, 여기에 꼭 들어맞는 자그마한 정황 하나를 위해 그대를 랑스도르프의 항해기로 안내하려 한다. 그런데 미리 알아 두어야 할 것은, 랑스도르프가 금세기 초에 러시아 제독 크루젠슈테른의 이름난 발견 탐험대에 소속되어 있었다는 사실이다. 랑스도르프 선장은 그 열일곱 번째 장(章)을 이렇게 시작한다.\n\n\"5월 13일까지 우리 배는 출항할 채비를 마쳤고, 이튿날 우리는 망망대해로 나아가 오호츠크를 향해 가는 길에 올랐다. 날씨는 매우 맑고 좋았으나, 어찌나 견딜 수 없이 추웠던지 우리는 모피옷을 계속 걸치고 있을 수밖에 없었다. 며칠 동안 바람이 거의 없다가, 19일이 되어서야 비로소 북서쪽에서 세찬 강풍이 불어왔다. 몸집이 배 자체보다도 더 큰, 보기 드물게 거대한 고래 한 마리가 수면 바로 아래에 누워 있었으나, 돛을 활짝 편 배가 그놈을 거의 덮칠 지경에 이른 그 순간까지 배에 탄 누구도 그것을 알아차리지 못했으니, 그놈과 부딪치는 것을 막기란 불가능했다. 이리하여 우리는 더없이 절박한 위험에 처하게 되었으니, 이 거대한 생물이 등을 곧추세우는 바람에 배가 물 밖으로 적어도 삼 피트(약 0.9미터)는 들어 올려졌기 때문이다. 돛대들이 휘청거렸고 돛들이 한꺼번에 내려앉았으며, 아래에 있던 우리는 우리가 어떤 암초에 부딪힌 것이라 단정 짓고 모두 즉시 갑판 위로 뛰쳐 올라갔다. 그러나 그게 아니라, 우리는 그 괴물이 더없는 위엄과 엄숙함을 갖추고 떠나가는 모습을 보았다. 더울프 선장은 충돌로 배가 손상을 입었는지 어떤지를 살피려 즉시 펌프를 가동시켰으나, 우리는 매우 다행스럽게도 배가 아무런 손상도 입지 않고 온전히 벗어났음을 알게 되었다.\"\n\n자, 여기서 문제의 배를 지휘하던 인물로 언급된 더울프 선장은 뉴잉글랜드 사람으로, 바다 선장으로서 보기 드문 모험으로 점철된 긴 생애를 보낸 뒤 오늘날 보스턴 근처 도체스터 마을에 거주하고 있다. 나는 영광스럽게도 그의 조카다. 나는 랑스도르프의 이 대목에 관해 그에게 특별히 물어보았다. 그는 한마디 한마디를 다 사실이라고 확인해 주었다. 다만 그 배는 결코 큰 배가 아니었으니, 시베리아 해안에서 건조된 러시아 선박으로, 외삼촌이 고향에서 타고 떠났던 배와 맞바꾼 뒤에 사들인 것이었다.\n\n옛 댐피어의 오랜 벗 가운데 하나인 라이오넬 웨이퍼의 항해기 — 진솔한 경이들로 가득 찬, 거침없고 사내다운 그 구식 모험담 — 에서 나는 방금 랑스도르프에게서 인용한 것과 너무도 흡사한 자그마한 일화 하나를 발견했으니, 혹시라도 그런 것이 필요하다면 보강하는 사례로 삼고자 여기 끼워 넣지 않을 수 없다.\n\n라이오넬은 보아하니 그가 \"존 페르디난도\"라 부르는 곳, 곧 오늘날의 후안 페르난데스로 가는 길이었던 모양이다. \"그리로 가는 도중에,\" 그는 이렇게 말한다, \"새벽 네 시쯤, 우리가 아메리카 본토에서 약 백오십 리그쯤 떨어져 있을 무렵, 우리 배가 끔찍한 충격을 느꼈으니, 그 바람에 우리 선원들은 어찌나 당황했던지 자기들이 어디에 있는지, 무엇을 어찌 생각해야 할지조차 거의 알 수 없을 지경이었다. 그리하여 저마다 죽음을 준비하기 시작했다. 사실 그 충격이 어찌나 갑작스럽고 격렬했던지, 우리는 배가 암초에 부딪혔으려니 하고 당연히 여겼다. 그러나 놀라움이 좀 가시자 우리는 측연을 던져 수심을 재 보았으나, 바닥에 닿지 않았다. * * * * * 그 충격이 어찌나 별안간이었던지 대포들이 포가(砲架) 위에서 펄쩍 뛰어올랐고, 여러 사람이 해먹에서 떨어져 나뒹굴었다. 머리를 대포에 베고 누워 있던 데이비스 선장은 선실 밖으로 내동댕이쳐졌다!\" 그러고 나서 라이오넬은 그 충격을 지진 탓으로 돌리며, 그 무렵 어디선가 큰 지진이 실제로 일어나 스페인 땅 일대에 큰 피해를 입혔다고 진술함으로써 그 추정을 뒷받침하려는 듯하다. 그러나 그 이른 새벽의 어둠 속에서, 그 충격이 결국 보이지 않는 고래가 밑에서 선체를 수직으로 들이받아 일어난 것이라 한들, 나로서는 그다지 놀랄 일이 아닐 것이다.\n\n나는 이런저런 경로로 내가 알고 있는, 향유고래가 때때로 발휘하는 그 거대한 힘과 악의에 관한 사례를 몇 가지 더 들 수도 있을 것이다. 한 번 이상의 경우에서 그놈은 공격해 오는 보트들을 그 모선까지 쫓아 보내는 데 그치지 않고, 배 자체를 추격하며 갑판에서 자신을 향해 던지는 모든 작살을 오래도록 버텨 낸 것으로 알려져 있다. 영국(English) 배 푸지 홀호가 그에 관한 이야기 한 토막을 들려줄 수 있을 것이다. 또한 그 힘으로 말하자면, 잔잔한 바다에서 달아나는 향유고래에 매인 밧줄이 배로 옮겨져 그곳에 단단히 묶이고는, 그 고래가 마치 말이 수레를 끌고 가듯 그 거대한 선체를 물살을 가르며 끌고 간 사례들이 있었다는 것을 말해 두고자 한다. 또한 한 번 작살에 찔린 향유고래가 다시 기운을 추스를 시간을 얻게 되면, 그놈은 흔히 눈먼 분노로 행동하기보다 추격자들을 파괴하려는 고의적이고 신중한 의도로 행동한다는 사실이 매우 자주 목격된다. 또한 그놈이 공격을 받으면 자주 입을 벌리고는 그 무시무시하게 벌어진 상태를 몇 분 동안 잇따라 유지한다는 것도, 그 성정에 관한 어떤 웅변적인 징표를 전해 주지 않는 것이 아니다. 그러나 나는 단 하나의 사례를, 그것도 마무리 짓는 예증을 드는 것으로 만족해야겠다. 그것은 주목할 만하고 더없이 의미심장한 사례로서, 그것을 통해 그대는 이 책에서 가장 경이로운 사건이 오늘날의 평범한 사실들로 뒷받침될 뿐 아니라, 이 경이들이 (모든 경이가 그러하듯) 시대를 거듭한 한낱 반복일 따름이라는 것을 어김없이 보게 될 것이다. 그리하여 우리는 백만 번째로 솔로몬과 더불어 아멘이라 말하노니 — 진실로 해 아래 새것이 없도다.\n\n서력 6세기에 프로코피우스라는 사람이 살았으니, 그는 유스티니아누스가 황제요 벨리사리우스가 장군이던 시절, 콘스탄티노플의 그리스도교 행정관이었다. 많은 이들이 알다시피, 그는 자기 시대의 역사를 기록했는데, 그것은 여러모로 보기 드문 가치를 지닌 저작이다. 가장 권위 있는 전거들에 따르면, 그는 한두 가지 세부 사항을 제외하고는 — 그것들은 지금 언급하려는 사안에 전혀 영향을 미치지 않는다 — 늘 더없이 믿을 만하고 과장하지 않는 역사가로 여겨져 왔다.\n\n자, 그가 쓴 이 역사 속에서 프로코피우스는, 자신이 콘스탄티노플의 행정관으로 재직하던 기간에, 인근 프로폰티스, 곧 마르마라해에서 거대한 바다 괴물 한 마리가 사로잡혔다고 언급한다. 그놈은 그 바다에서 오십 년이 넘는 세월에 걸쳐 이따금 배들을 파괴해 온 끝에 잡힌 것이었다. 이처럼 견실한 역사 속에 적혀 있는 사실은 쉽사리 부인할 수 없다. 또 그래야 할 까닭도 없다. 이 바다 괴물이 정확히 어떤 종류였는지는 언급되어 있지 않다. 그러나 그놈이 배들을 파괴했다는 점에서, 또 다른 여러 이유에서, 그것은 틀림없이 고래였을 것이며, 나는 그것이 향유고래였으리라고 강하게 기우는 바다. 그 까닭을 말해 주겠다. 오랫동안 나는 향유고래가 지중해와 그에 이어지는 깊은 바다에서는 늘 알려진 바가 없었으리라 짐작했다. 지금도 나는 그 바다들이, 현재의 사물 질서 안에서는, 그놈이 습관적으로 무리 지어 즐겨 찾는 곳이 아니며, 어쩌면 결코 그런 곳이 될 수 없으리라고 확신한다. 그러나 더 깊은 조사를 통해 최근에 나는, 근래에 들어 지중해에 향유고래가 출현한 고립된 사례들이 있었음을 알게 되었다. 나는 믿을 만한 전거에 따라, 바르바리 해안에서 영국 해군의 데이비스 준장이 향유고래의 골격을 발견했다는 이야기를 들었다. 자, 전투용 함선이 다르다넬스 해협을 손쉽게 통과하니, 향유고래 또한 같은 경로를 따라 지중해에서 프로폰티스로 빠져나갈 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n내가 알아낼 수 있는 한, 프로폰티스에서는 참고래의 먹이인, 브릿(brit)이라 불리는 그 특이한 물질이 전혀 발견되지 않는다. 그러나 나는 향유고래의 먹이인 오징어 또는 갑오징어가 그 바다 밑바닥에 숨어 있으리라고 믿을 만한 온갖 근거를 지니고 있으니, 그것은 그런 종류 가운데 결코 가장 큰 것은 아니지만 커다란 생물들이 그 수면에서 발견된 적이 있기 때문이다. 그러니 그대가 이러한 진술들을 제대로 한데 모아 조금만 따져 본다면, 인간의 모든 추론에 비추어, 반세기 동안 어느 로마 황제의 배들을 부수었던 프로코피우스의 그 바다 괴물이 십중팔구 향유고래였음에 틀림없다는 것을 그대는 명확히 깨닫게 될 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 46. Surmises.\n\n비록 에이해브는 자신의 목적이 지핀 뜨거운 불길에 휩싸여, 그 모든 생각과 행동에서 늘 모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 끝내 사로잡는 일을 염두에 두었고, 그 한 가지 열정을 위해서라면 인간으로서의 온갖 이해관계를 기꺼이 희생할 듯이 보였지만, 그럼에도 불구하고 그는 본성과 오랜 습관으로 말미암아 불같은 고래잡이의 방식에 너무도 깊이 매여 있었던 까닭에, 항해의 부수적인 본업을 통째로 저버리지는 못했던 것이리라. 혹은 적어도 사정이 그렇지 않았다 하더라도, 그를 훨씬 더 강하게 움직이는 다른 동기들이 없지 않았다. 그의 편집광적 광기를 감안한다 해도, 어쩌면 그가 흰 고래를 향해 품은 복수심이 어느 정도는 모든 향유고래에게로까지 번져 나갔을지 모른다고, 그래서 더 많은 괴물을 죽일수록 그만큼 더, 이후에 마주치는 고래마다 자기가 쫓는 그 증오스러운 놈으로 드러날 가능성을 늘려 갔던 것이라고 넌지시 말하는 것은 지나치게 세밀한 해석일 터다. 그러나 그러한 가설이 설령 받아들이기 어렵다 한들, 비록 그를 지배하는 열정의 광포함과는 그리 엄밀히 맞아떨어지지 않으나 결코 그를 흔들지 못할 것도 아닌, 또 다른 고려 사항들이 여전히 남아 있었다.\n\n자신의 목적을 이루기 위해 에이해브는 도구를 써야만 했다. 그런데 달빛 아래에서 쓰이는 온갖 도구 가운데 가장 탈이 나기 쉬운 것이 바로 사람이다. 이를테면 그는, 어떤 면에서 자기가 스타벅(Starbuck)에게 자석처럼 끌어당기는 우위를 지녔다 하더라도, 그 우위가 오로지 육체적 우월이 곧 지적 지배를 뜻하지 않는 것과 마찬가지로 영적인 인간 전체를 덮지는 못한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 순수하게 영적인 것에 비하면 지적인 것이란 일종의 육체적 관계에 머무를 뿐이기 때문이다. 에이해브가 스타벅의 머릿속에 자석을 들이대고 있는 한, 스타벅의 몸과 억눌린 의지는 에이해브의 것이었다. 그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 일등항해사가 마음속으로는 선장의 추적을 혐오하고 있으며, 할 수만 있다면 거기서 기꺼이 자신을 떼어 내거나 심지어 그 일을 좌절시키려 들리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 흰 고래가 모습을 드러내기까지 오랜 시간이 흐를 수도 있었다. 그 긴 기간 동안, 평범하고 신중하며 정황에 맞는 어떤 영향력이 그에게 가해지지 않는 한, 스타벅은 언제든 선장의 지휘에 맞서 공공연히 반항의 도로 빠져들기 쉬울 터였다. 그뿐만 아니라, 모비 딕에 관한 에이해브의 교묘한 광기는, 당분간 이 추적에서 그것을 자연히 휘감고 있던 저 기이하고 환상적인 불경함을 어떻게든 벗겨 내야 한다는 것을 그가 더없이 예민하고 빈틈없는 감각으로 내다보았다는 점에서 가장 뚜렷이 드러났다. 곧 이 항해의 온전한 공포는 어두운 배경 속으로 물러나 있게 해야 했고(행동으로 풀리지 않는 채 오래 이어지는 사색을 견뎌 낼 만큼 굳센 용기를 지닌 사람은 드물기 때문이다), 그들이 긴 밤 당직을 설 때면 그의 부하 장교들과 선원들은 모비 딕보다 더 가까운 무언가를 생각거리로 가져야만 했다. 아무리 그 야만스러운 선원들이 그의 추적 선언을 열렬하고도 격렬하게 환호로 맞이했다 한들, 온갖 부류의 모든 뱃사람이란 다소간 변덕스럽고 미덥지 못한 법이어서 — 그들은 끊임없이 변하는 바깥 날씨 속에 살며 그 변덕을 들이마시므로 — 끝내 가서는 생명과 열정을 약속하는 일이라 할지라도, 추구하는 동안에는 아득하고 막연한 어떤 목적을 위해 붙들어 둘 때에는, 무엇보다도 임시적인 관심거리와 일거리가 그 사이에 끼어들어 그들을 마지막 돌진을 위해 건강하게 매달려 있도록 유지하는 것이 반드시 필요했다.\n\n또한 에이해브는 다른 한 가지도 잊지 않고 있었다. 강렬한 감정의 순간에는 인간이 온갖 비천한 셈속을 경멸한다. 그러나 그러한 순간은 덧없이 사라진다. 만들어진 인간의 영속적이고 본질적인 상태는 천박함이라고 에이해브는 생각했다. 흰 고래가 이 야만스러운 내 선원들의 가슴을 온전히 부추기고, 그들의 야만성 둘레를 맴돌며 그들에게 일종의 너그러운 기사도적 모험심마저 길러 낸다 치더라도, 그들이 그것을 사랑하여 모비 딕을 뒤쫓는 동안에도 그들은 더 흔하고 일상적인 욕구를 채울 양식 또한 가져야만 했다. 옛날의 드높고 의로운 십자군조차도, 그 거룩한 성묘를 위해 싸우려고 이천 마일의 땅을 가로지르면서도 가는 길에 도둑질을 하고, 소매치기를 하고, 그 밖의 경건한 부수입을 챙기지 않고서는 만족하지 못했다. 만약 그들이 단 하나의 최종적이고 낭만적인 목적에만 엄격히 매여 있었다면 — 너무도 많은 이들이 그 최종적이고 낭만적인 목적을 혐오스러워하며 등을 돌렸으리라. 나는 이 사람들에게서 현금에 대한 온갖 희망을 — 그렇다, 현금에 대한 희망을 — 빼앗지 않으리라고 에이해브는 생각했다. 그들은 지금이야 현금을 비웃을지 모른다. 그러나 몇 달이 흐르고 그들에게 현금에 대한 어떤 전망의 약속도 없다면, 그때는 그 잠잠하던 현금이 한꺼번에 그들 속에서 반란을 일으켜, 바로 그 현금이 머지않아 에이해브를 면직시켜 버릴 것이다.\n\n게다가 에이해브 자신과 더 직접적으로 관련된, 또 하나의 예방적 동기도 없지 않았다. 아마도 충동적으로, 그리고 어쩌면 다소 성급하게 피쿼드호(Pequod) 항해의 으뜸가는 사적 목적을 드러내 버린 까닭에, 에이해브는 이제 그렇게 함으로써 자신이 권한 찬탈이라는 반박할 수 없는 비난에 간접적으로 스스로를 내맡긴 셈이라는 것을 온전히 의식하고 있었다. 그리하여 만약 그의 선원들이 그럴 마음을 먹고 또 그럴 만한 역량을 갖춘다면, 도덕적으로든 법적으로든 아무런 책임도 지지 않은 채 그에 대한 일체의 복종을 거부할 수도 있고, 심지어 폭력으로 지휘권을 빼앗아 갈 수도 있었다. 권한 찬탈이라는, 그저 넌지시 비치기만 한 혐의로부터도, 또 그렇게 억눌려 있던 인상이 점점 힘을 얻어 갈 때 빚어질 수 있는 결과로부터도, 에이해브는 물론 자신을 지키려고 더없이 애를 태웠을 것이 분명하다. 그러한 보호는 오로지, 자신의 압도적인 두뇌와 심장과 손으로써, 그리고 그의 선원들이 휩쓸릴 수 있는 온갖 미세한 분위기의 영향력 하나하나에까지 빈틈없고 면밀히 셈하는 주의를 기울이는 것으로써만 이루어질 수 있었다.\n\n그러니 이 모든 이유에서, 그리고 어쩌면 여기서 말로 풀어내기에는 너무 분석적인 다른 이유들에서, 에이해브는 자신이 여전히 상당한 정도로 피쿼드호 항해의 자연스럽고 명목상의 목적에 충실해야 하며, 온갖 관습적인 관행을 지켜야 하고, 그뿐만 아니라 자기 직업의 일반적인 추구에 대해 익히 알려진 자신의 열정적인 관심을 빠짐없이 내보이도록 스스로를 다그쳐야 한다는 것을 똑똑히 깨달았다.\n\n이 모든 사정이야 어떠하든, 이제 그가 세 개의 돛대 꼭대기를 향해 외치며 환히 망을 보라고, 돌고래 한 마리조차 보고하기를 빠뜨리지 말라고 타이르는 목소리가 자주 들려왔다. 이러한 경계심은 오래지 않아 보답을 받았다.\n\n# CHAPTER 47. The Mat-Maker.\n\n흐리고 무더운 오후였다. 선원들은 갑판 여기저기에 게으르게 늘어져 있거나, 납빛 바닷물을 멍하니 굽어보고 있었다. 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 나는 우리 보트에 덧댈 결박줄로 쓸, 이른바 검매트(sword-mat)라는 것을 짜며 한가로이 일하고 있었다. 온 풍경이 어찌나 고요하고 차분하면서도 어딘가 무언가를 예고하는 듯하던지, 또 공기 속에는 그런 몽상의 주문이 깃들어 있어, 말없는 선원들 하나하나가 저마다 보이지 않는 제 자신 속으로 녹아드는 듯했다.\n\n매트를 짜느라 분주한 동안 나는 퀴퀘그의 조수, 곧 시동(侍童) 노릇을 했다. 내가 날실(warp)을 이루는 기다란 실들 사이로 씨실(woof)이 될 가는 노끈을 손수 북 삼아 가며 거듭 오가게 하는 동안, 퀴퀘그는 옆으로 서서 이따금 묵직한 떡갈나무 검을 실 사이로 밀어 넣으며, 물을 무심히 바라보다가 아무렇게나 생각 없이 실마다 단단히 박아 넣었다. 그러니까 그때 온 배와 온 바다를 뒤덮은 그 기묘한 몽환은 오직 그 검이 간간이 내는 둔탁한 소리에만 깨어졌고, 그것은 마치 이곳이 시간의 베틀(Loom of Time)이며, 나 자신은 운명을 향해 기계적으로 짜고 또 짜 나가는 한 자루의 북인 것만 같았다. 거기 날실의 고정된 실들은 단 하나의, 늘 되돌아오는, 변함없는 진동에만 내맡겨져 있었고, 그 진동은 그저 다른 실들이 제 실과 가로질러 엮여 들 만큼만의 여지를 줄 뿐이었다. 이 날실은 필연인 듯했다. 그리고 여기서, 나는 생각했다, 나는 내 손으로 내 북을 놀려 이 바꿀 수 없는 실들 속에 내 운명을 짜 넣고 있는 것이다. 그러는 동안 퀴퀘그의 충동적이고 무심한 검은, 때로는 씨실을 비스듬히, 때로는 비뚤게, 때로는 세게, 때로는 약하게, 그때그때 형편대로 때렸다. 그리고 그 마지막 타격의 차이로 말미암아 완성된 직물의 최종 모습에 그에 상응하는 차이가 빚어졌다. 이리하여 날실과 씨실 둘 다를 끝내 빚어내고 모양 지우는 이 야만인의 검, 이 거침없고 무심한 검은 곧 우연임에 틀림없다고 나는 생각했다 — 그렇다, 우연과 자유의지와 필연, 이 셋은 결코 서로 어긋나지 않으며, 모두가 서로 엮이며 함께 일하는 것이다. 필연의 곧은 날실은 제 궁극의 행로에서 벗어날 수 없으며, 그 번갈아 오가는 진동 하나하나도 실은 오로지 그 행로를 향할 뿐이다. 자유의지는 여전히 주어진 실들 사이로 제 북을 놀릴 자유를 누린다. 그리고 우연은, 비록 필연의 곧은 선들 안에서만 그 놀이가 허락되고, 그 옆걸음치는 움직임은 자유의지의 지시를 받아, 이처럼 양쪽 모두에게 규정을 받으면서도, 번갈아 가며 그 둘을 다스리고, 사건의 마지막 모양새를 결정짓는 타격을 가하는 것이다.\n\n이렇게 우리가 짜고 또 짜 나가던 그때, 어찌나 기묘하고 길게 늘어지며, 음악처럼 거칠고 이 세상 것 같지 않은 소리가 들려왔던지, 나는 흠칫 놀라 자유의지의 실뭉치를 손에서 떨어뜨리고는, 그 목소리가 날개처럼 떨어져 내린 구름을 올려다보며 서 있었다. 가로돛대 높은 곳에는 저 미치광이 게이헤드 사람, 태시테고(Tashtego)가 있었다. 그의 몸은 간절하게 앞으로 뻗어 나가 있었고, 손은 지팡이처럼 뻗쳐 있었으며, 짧고 갑작스러운 간격마다 그는 외침을 이어 갔다. 분명 그 똑같은 소리는 바로 그 순간 온 바다 곳곳에서, 그처럼 높은 허공에 올라앉은 수백 명의 고래잡이 망꾼들에게서 들려오고 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그 익숙한 옛 외침을, 인디언 태시테고에게서 나오는 것만큼 그토록 경이로운 가락으로 빚어낼 수 있는 폐(肺)는 그중에서도 몇 되지 않았다.\n\n그가 허공에 반쯤 매달린 채 그대 머리 위에 떠 있듯 서서, 그처럼 거칠고 간절하게 수평선 쪽을 응시하는 모습을 보았다면, 그대는 그를 운명의 그림자를 바라보며 그 거친 외침으로 그 도래를 알리는 어떤 예언자나 선견자라 여겼을 것이다.\n\n\"저기 물을 뿜는다! 저기! 저기! 저기! 물을 뿜는다! 물을 뿜어!\"\n\n\"어느 쪽이냐?\"\n\n\"바람 불어가는 쪽 옆으로, 한 이 마일쯤 떨어져서! 떼로 몰려 있어요!\"\n\n순식간에 온통 야단법석이 되었다.\n\n향유고래(Sperm Whale)는 시계가 째깍거리듯, 똑같이 어김없고 믿음직한 규칙으로 물을 뿜는다. 고래잡이들은 바로 이로써 이 고래를 같은 속(屬)의 다른 무리들과 구별해 낸다.\n\n\"꼬리지느러미가 내려간다!\" 이제 태시테고에게서 이런 외침이 들려왔다. 그리고 고래들은 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"빨리, 급사!\" 에이해브(Ahab)가 외쳤다. \"시간을! 시간을 재!\"\n\n도우보이가 서둘러 아래로 내려가 시계를 흘끗 보고는, 정확한 분(分)을 에이해브에게 일러바쳤다.\n\n이제 배는 바람을 등지도록 돌려졌고, 그 앞으로 부드럽게 흔들리며 나아갔다. 고래들이 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 머리를 두고 물속으로 내려갔다는 태시테고의 보고에 따라, 우리는 곧장 뱃머리 바로 앞에서 그것들을 다시 보게 되리라 자신했다. 향유고래가 이따금 보이는 그 독특한 술수, 곧 머리를 한쪽 방향으로 두고 잠수했다가도 수면 아래 몸을 숨긴 채 빙 돌아 정반대 쪽으로 잽싸게 헤엄쳐 달아나는 그 교활함은 이번에는 발휘될 리가 없었다. 태시테고가 본 그 고래가 어떤 식으로든 놀랐거나, 실로 우리가 가까이 있다는 것을 조금이라도 알아챘다고 볼 까닭이 없었기 때문이다. 보트에 배정되지 않은, 곧 배지기로 뽑힌 사람들 가운데 하나가 이제 큰돛대 꼭대기에서 인디언과 교대했다. 앞돛대와 뒷돛대에 있던 선원들은 내려왔다. 밧줄 통들은 제자리에 고정되었고, 크레인들은 바깥으로 내밀어졌으며, 큰돛의 활대는 뒤로 돌려졌고, 세 척의 보트는 높은 절벽 위로 내밀린 세 개의 함초 바구니처럼 바다 위로 흔들려 나갔다. 뱃전 바깥에서 그 열성적인 보트 선원들은 한 손으로는 난간을 붙잡고, 한 발은 기대에 차서 뱃전 위에 올려 둔 채였다. 적함 위로 뛰어들기 직전의 군함 병사들이 길게 늘어선 모습이 꼭 그러할 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 위태로운 찰나에, 모든 이의 눈을 고래에서 떼어 놓는 한 줄기 갑작스러운 외침이 들려왔다. 모두가 흠칫 놀라 어두운 에이해브를 쏘아보았는데, 그는 마치 막 허공에서 빚어져 나온 듯한 거무스름한 다섯 유령에게 둘러싸여 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 48. The First Lowering.\n\n그때는 유령처럼 보였던 그 형체들이 갑판 반대편에서 어른거리며, 소리 하나 없이 날래게 그곳에 매달려 있던 보트의 도르래와 결박을 풀고 있었다. 이 보트는 우현 선미 쪽에 매달려 있는 까닭에 기술적으로는 선장의 보트라 불렸으나, 늘 예비 보트 중 하나로 여겨져 왔다. 지금 그 뱃머리 곁에 선 형체는 키가 크고 거무튀튀했으며, 강철 같은 입술 사이로 새하얀 이 하나가 사악하게 비죽 튀어나와 있었다. 검은 무명으로 지은 구겨진 중국식 윗옷이 장례식이라도 치르듯 그의 몸을 감쌌고, 같은 검은 천으로 지은 헐렁한 검정 바지가 어우러져 있었다. 그런데 이 칠흑 같은 검음 위에 기묘하게 얹힌 것은 반짝이는 새하얀 머릿수건이었으니, 살아 있는 머리카락을 땋아 머리 위에 둘둘 감아올린 것이었다. 이 형체의 동료들은 얼굴빛이 덜 검었으되, 마닐라 원주민 일부에게서만 볼 수 있는 선명한 호랑이 같은 누런 살빛을 띠고 있었다. 그들은 어떤 교묘한 악마성으로 악명이 높은 종족이었고, 정직한 백인 선원들 가운데는 그들을 두고 바다 위에서 자신들의 주인인 악마에게 돈을 받고 일하는 첩자이자 은밀한 심복이며, 그 악마의 회계실은 어딘가 다른 곳에 있으리라 믿는 이들도 있었다.\n\n어리둥절해진 선원들이 아직 이 낯선 자들을 응시하고 있을 때, 에이해브가 그들의 우두머리 격인 흰 머릿수건을 쓴 노인을 향해 외쳤다. \"거기 다 됐나, 페달라?\"\n\n\"됐습니다.\" 반쯤 쉭쉭거리는 듯한 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그럼 내려라. 들었느냐?\" 갑판 너머로 고함이 울렸다. \"거기 내리라니까.\"\n\n그 목소리가 어찌나 우레와 같았던지, 사람들은 놀라움에도 불구하고 난간을 뛰어넘었다. 도르래 바퀴가 활차 안에서 빙글빙글 돌았고, 세 척의 보트가 첨벙거리며 바다로 떨어져 내렸다. 그러는 동안 선원들은 다른 어떤 직업에서도 볼 수 없는 능란하고 거침없는 대담함으로, 염소처럼 출렁이는 뱃전을 따라 아래에서 까딱이는 보트로 뛰어내렸다.\n\n그들이 배 그늘 아래에서 막 노 저어 빠져나오자, 바람이 불어오는 쪽에서 네 번째 보트가 나타나 선미를 돌아 다가왔는데, 거기에는 에이해브를 태우고 노 젓는 다섯 명의 낯선 자들이 보였다. 에이해브는 선미에 꼿꼿이 선 채 스타벅과 스터브, 그리고 플래스크에게 큰소리로 외쳐, 넓은 바다를 덮을 수 있도록 멀찍이 흩어지라고 명했다. 그러나 모두의 눈길이 다시금 거무튀튀한 페달라와 그 부하들에게 못 박힌 탓에, 다른 보트의 선원들은 그 명령을 따르지 않았다.\n\n\"에이해브 선장님—\" 스타벅이 입을 열었다.\n\n\"흩어져라.\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"네 척 모두 노를 저어라. 너, 플래스크, 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 더 빠져나가!\"\n\n\"예, 예, 선장님.\" 작은 킹포스트가 커다란 키잡이 노를 휘돌리며 명랑하게 외쳤다. \"몸을 젖혀라!\" 그가 부하들에게 명했다. \"저기다!—저기!—또 저기! 바로 앞에서 물을 뿜는다, 친구들!—몸을 젖혀라!\"\n\n\"저기 누런 녀석들 따위 신경 쓰지 마라, 아치.\"\n\n\"오, 신경 안 씁니다, 선장님.\" 아치가 말했다. \"전 진작에 다 알고 있었거든요. 선창에서 그놈들 소리를 듣지 않았겠습니까? 그래서 여기 카바코한테 말해 주지 않았습니까? 그렇지, 카바코? 저놈들은 밀항자입니다, 플래스크 씨.\"\n\n\"저어라, 저어라, 살아 펄떡이는 내 사랑들아. 저어라, 내 새끼들아, 저어라, 내 귀여운 것들아.\" 스터브가 부하들을 향해 느릿느릿 달래듯 한숨처럼 내뱉었는데, 그중 몇몇은 아직도 불안한 기색을 보였다. \"어째서 등뼈가 부러지도록 젓지 않는 거냐, 얘들아? 뭘 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보는 거냐? 저기 보트에 탄 녀석들? 쳇! 저들은 그저 우리를 도우러 온 일손 다섯에 불과해—어디서 왔든 무슨 상관이냐—사람이 많을수록 즐거운 법이지. 그러니 저어라, 어서 저어라. 유황 따위 신경 쓰지 마라—악마라도 꽤 괜찮은 친구들이니까. 그렇지, 그렇지. 자, 이제 됐다. 천 파운드짜리 노 젓기다. 판돈을 싹쓸이할 노 젓기야! 향유고래 기름의 황금 잔을 위하여, 내 영웅들아! 세 번 만세를 부르자, 얘들아—모두 살아 펄떡이며! 천천히, 천천히. 서두르지 마라—서두르지 마. 어째서 노를 부러뜨리지 않느냐, 이 악당들아? 뭔가를 물어뜯어라, 이 개 같은 놈들아! 그렇지, 그렇지, 그래—부드럽게, 부드럽게! 바로 그거다—그거야! 길고 힘차게. 저어라, 저어! 악마가 너희를 잡아가기를, 이 누더기 같은 부랑자 놈들아. 죄다 잠들었구나. 코 그만 골고 저어라, 이 잠꾸러기들아. 저으라니까? 저을 줄 모르느냐? 젓기 싫으냐? 모샘치와 생강과자의 이름으로 묻건대 어째서 젓지 않는 거냐?—저어서 뭔가를 부러뜨려라! 저어서 눈깔이 튀어나오게 해라! 자!\" 그가 허리띠에서 날카로운 칼을 휙 뽑아 들었다. \"너희 어미가 낳은 자식들 하나하나 모두 칼을 뽑아 들고, 칼날을 이로 문 채 저어라. 그거다—그거야. 이제야 뭔가 하는구나. 그래야 그림이 나오지, 내 강철 조각들아. 출발해라—출발해, 내 은수저들아! 출발해라, 밧줄 송곳들아!\"\n\n스터브가 부하들에게 늘어놓은 이 서두를 여기 길게 옮긴 것은, 그가 대체로 그들에게 말을 거는 방식이, 특히 노 젓기의 종교를 설파하는 방식이 자못 독특했기 때문이다. 그러나 이 설교 한 토막을 보고서 그가 자기 신도들에게 진짜로 노발대발했으리라 짐작해서는 안 된다. 결코 그렇지 않았고, 바로 거기에 그의 가장 큰 특이함이 있었다. 그는 부하들에게 더없이 끔찍한 말들을 내뱉으면서도, 그 어조가 익살과 분노가 어찌나 묘하게 뒤섞였던지, 그 분노조차 오로지 익살에 곁들이는 양념처럼 계산된 것으로 보일 정도였다. 그래서 어떤 노잡이도 그토록 기묘한 호령을 들으면 죽을힘을 다해 노를 젓지 않을 수 없었으되, 그러면서도 한낱 농담거리로 여기며 젓는 것이었다. 게다가 그는 내내 무척이나 느긋하고 게으른 모습이었고, 키잡이 노도 너무나 빈둥대듯 다루었으며, 어찌나 입을 떡 벌리고—때로는 아예 하품하듯 벌어진 입으로—있었던지, 그렇게 하품하는 지휘관의 모습이 오로지 대조의 힘만으로 부하들에게 마법처럼 작용하는 것이었다. 그런가 하면 스터브는 그 명랑함이 때로 어찌나 야릇하게 모호한지, 명령에 복종하는 일에 있어 아랫사람을 죄다 경계하게 만드는, 그런 기묘한 부류의 익살꾼 가운데 하나였다.\n\n에이해브의 신호에 따라, 스타벅은 이제 스터브의 뱃머리를 비스듬히 가로질러 노를 젓고 있었다. 그리고 잠깐 동안 두 보트가 제법 가까워지자 스터브가 일등항해사를 불렀다.\n\n\"스타벅 씨! 거기 좌현 보트, 어이! 한 말씀 드릴까 하는데요, 선생, 괜찮으시다면요!\"\n\n\"여어!\" 스타벅이 말하면서도 단 한 치도 고개를 돌리지 않은 채 대답했다. 그는 여전히 진지하면서도 속삭이듯 부하들을 다그치고 있었고, 그 얼굴은 스터브 쪽으로는 부싯돌처럼 굳어 있었다.\n\n\"저 누런 녀석들을 어찌 생각하십니까, 선생!\"\n\n\"배가 출항하기 전에 어떻게든 몰래 실린 거지. (힘차게, 힘차게, 얘들아!)\" 부하들에게 속삭인 뒤, 다시 큰 소리로 말했다. \"딱한 일이오, 스터브 씨! (휘저어라, 휘저어, 얘들아!) 하지만 신경 쓰지 마시오, 스터브 씨, 다 잘되자고 하는 일이니. 무슨 일이 닥치든 부하들을 힘차게 젓게 하시오. (튀어 올라라, 얘들아, 튀어!) 앞에 향유고래가 통째로 가득하오, 스터브 씨, 바로 그걸 잡으러 온 게 아니오. (저어라, 얘들아!) 향유고래, 향유고래가 관건이지! 적어도 이건 의무요. 의무와 이익이 손을 맞잡았소.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그럴 줄 알았지.\" 보트들이 갈라지자 스터브가 혼잣말을 했다. \"그놈들을 처음 보자마자 그런 줄 알았다니까. 그래, 그래서 도우보이가 오래전부터 의심했던 대로, 그자가 그토록 자주 뒤쪽 선창으로 내려갔던 거지. 놈들은 거기 아래 숨어 있었던 거야. 흰 고래가 이 일의 밑바닥에 깔려 있어. 자, 자, 그래 좋다! 어쩔 수 없지! 좋아! 저어라, 얘들아! 오늘은 흰 고래가 아니다! 저어라!\"\n\n자, 갑판에서 보트를 내리는 그토록 위중한 순간에 이 별난 낯선 자들이 등장한 것은, 선원들 가운데 몇몇에게 일종의 미신적인 경악을 불러일으켰던 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그러나 아치가 멋대로 발견했다는 이야기가 얼마 전부터 그들 사이에 퍼져 있던 터라—물론 그때는 아무도 믿지 않았지만—이것이 그나마 조금은 그들로 하여금 이 사태에 대비하게 만들어 주었다. 그것이 그들의 놀라움에서 가장 날카로운 모서리를 깎아 냈다. 그리하여 이 모든 것에다 스터브가 그들의 등장을 자신만만하게 설명해 준 덕분에, 그들은 당분간 미신적인 억측에서 벗어났다. 하지만 이 일은 여전히, 애초부터 어두운 에이해브가 이 문제에 정확히 어떻게 관여했는지를 두고 온갖 거친 추측이 일어날 넉넉한 여지를 남겨 두었다. 나로 말하자면, 어슴푸레한 낸터킷의 새벽녘에 피쿼드호로 살그머니 올라타던 그 신비로운 그림자들을, 그리고 도무지 알 수 없던 일라이저의 수수께끼 같은 암시들을 말없이 떠올렸다.\n\n그러는 동안, 부하 항해사들의 목소리가 닿지 않는 곳까지 바람 불어오는 쪽으로 가장 멀리 빠져나간 에이해브는, 여전히 다른 보트들보다 앞서 나아가고 있었으니, 이는 그를 태우고 노 젓는 선원들이 얼마나 강력한지를 말해 주는 정황이었다. 호랑이처럼 누런 그의 부하들은 온통 강철과 고래수염으로 빚은 듯했다. 다섯 개의 증기 해머처럼 그들은 규칙적인 힘의 박자에 맞추어 솟았다 떨어졌고, 그 박자마다 보트는 마치 미시시피 증기선에서 터져 나온 수평으로 누운 보일러처럼 물살을 가르며 튀어 나갔다. 작살잡이의 노를 젓는 모습이 보이던 페달라로 말하자면, 그는 검은 윗옷을 벗어 던지고 맨가슴을 드러낸 채 뱃전 위로 솟은 상반신 전부를, 번갈아 가라앉는 물 수평선을 배경으로 또렷이 새겨 보였다. 한편 보트 반대편 끝에서 에이해브는, 마치 휘청이는 어떤 기미라도 상쇄하려는 듯 검객처럼 한쪽 팔을 반쯤 뒤로 젖혀 허공에 들어 올린 채, 흰 고래가 그의 다리를 찢어 놓기 전 천 번의 보트 내리기 때 그러했던 것처럼 한결같이 키잡이 노를 다루고 있었다. 별안간 그 뻗은 팔이 기묘하게 움직이더니 그대로 멈추었고, 동시에 보트의 다섯 노가 일제히 곤두선 채 멈춰 들리는 것이 보였다. 보트도 선원들도 바다 위에 미동도 없이 앉아 있었다. 그 즉시 뒤쪽에 흩어져 있던 세 척의 보트도 가던 길을 멈추었다. 고래들은 불규칙하게 몸뚱이를 푸른 물속으로 가라앉혔던 터라, 그 움직임을 멀리서 알아챌 만한 기색은 전혀 남기지 않았으되, 더 가까이 있던 에이해브는 그것을 알아보았던 것이다.\n\n\"모두 자기 노를 따라 잘 살펴라!\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"너, 퀴케그, 일어서라!\"\n\n야만인은 뱃머리에 솟은 삼각형 상자 위로 날렵하게 뛰어올라 거기 꼿꼿이 서서는, 사냥감이 마지막으로 보였던 지점을 향해 강렬하고도 간절한 눈으로 응시했다. 마찬가지로 보트의 맨 끝 선미에도 뱃전과 같은 높이로 삼각형 발판이 마련되어 있었는데, 그 위에서 스타벅 자신이 나무 조각 같은 자기 배의 들썩이는 출렁임에 침착하고 능란하게 몸의 균형을 잡으며, 말없이 바다의 광대한 푸른 눈을 응시하고 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 플래스크의 보트 역시 숨죽인 듯 고요히 떠 있었다. 그 지휘관은 무모하게도 로거헤드 꼭대기에 올라서 있었으니, 그것은 용골에 박힌 굵직한 기둥으로 선미 발판 높이보다 두 자(약 60센티미터)쯤 솟아 있었다. 그것은 고래 밧줄을 감아 두는 데 쓰이는 것이었다. 그 꼭대기는 사람 손바닥보다 넓지 않은데, 플래스크는 그런 받침 위에 올라서 있던 까닭에, 마치 돛대 꼭대기만 남기고 침몰해 버린 어느 배의 그 꼭대기에 걸터앉아 있는 듯 보였다. 그러나 작은 킹포스트는 키가 작고 땅딸막했고, 동시에 작은 킹포스트는 크고 드높은 야심으로 가득 차 있었던 까닭에, 이 로거헤드 발판 따위로는 결코 만족하지 못했다.\n\n\"파도 세 마루 너머도 안 보이는군. 거기 노를 하나 세워다오, 그 위로 올라가게.\"\n\n이에 대구가 가는 길을 안정시키려 양손을 뱃전에 짚은 채 잽싸게 선미로 미끄러져 가더니, 몸을 곧추세우고는 자신의 우뚝한 어깨를 받침대로 자청해 내밀었다.\n\n\"여느 돛대 꼭대기 못지않습니다, 선생. 올라타시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그러고말고, 정말 고맙다, 멋진 친구야. 다만 네가 쉰 자(약 15미터)는 더 컸으면 싶구나.\"\n\n그러자 그 거대한 흑인은 보트의 마주 보는 두 판자에 두 발을 단단히 디디고 약간 몸을 숙여, 자신의 펼친 손바닥을 플래스크의 발에 받쳐 주고는, 플래스크의 손을 자신의 영구차 깃털 같은 머리에 얹게 한 뒤 자기가 들어 올릴 때 맞춰 뛰어오르라 이르더니, 능란하게 한 번 던져 그 작은 사내를 자기 어깨 위에 높직이 마른 채로 올려놓았다. 이렇게 플래스크는 이제 그 위에 서 있었고, 대구는 한쪽 팔을 들어 올려 그가 기대어 몸을 가눌 가슴받이를 만들어 주었다.\n\n언제 보아도 풋내기에게는, 더없이 사납고 비뚤어지게 엇갈리며 출렁이는 파도에 마구 흔들리면서도 고래잡이가 제 보트 안에서 꼿꼿한 자세를 유지하는, 그 무의식적인 솜씨의 놀라운 능숙함이 기이한 광경이다. 그런 상황에서 로거헤드 그 자체에 아찔하게 올라선 모습을 보는 것은 더욱 기이하다. 그러나 거대한 대구 위에 올라탄 작은 플래스크의 모습은 한층 더 기묘했다. 차분하고 무심하고 느긋하며 아무 생각 없는, 야만적인 위엄으로 몸을 지탱하면서, 그 고귀한 흑인은 바다가 한 번 출렁일 때마다 자신의 멋진 몸을 조화롭게 함께 출렁였던 것이다. 그 널찍한 등판 위에서 아마빛 머리의 플래스크는 한 송이 눈송이처럼 보였다. 짊어진 자가 올라탄 자보다 더 고귀해 보였다. 참으로 활기차고 부산스럽고 과시적인 작은 플래스크가 이따금 안달이 나서 발을 굴렀으나, 그렇다고 흑인의 당당한 가슴팍을 단 한 번이라도 더 들썩이게 만들지는 못했다. 그렇게 나는 정념과 허영이 살아 있는 너그러운 대지를 발로 구르는 것을 보아 왔으되, 대지는 그런다고 제 조수와 제 계절을 바꾸지 않았다.\n\n그러는 동안 삼등항해사 스터브는 그처럼 멀리 내다보는 노심초사 따위는 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 고래들은 단순히 겁먹어 잠깐 잠수한 것이 아니라, 늘 하듯 깊이 가라앉은 것인지도 몰랐다. 만일 그렇다면, 그런 경우 그의 버릇대로 스터브는 따분하게 늘어지는 그 막간을 자기 파이프로 달래기로 작정한 모양이었다. 그는 늘 깃털처럼 비스듬히 꽂아 두던 모자 띠에서 파이프를 뽑아냈다. 그것에 담배를 채우고 엄지 끝으로 꾹꾹 눌러 다졌다. 그러나 거친 사포 같은 자기 손바닥에 성냥을 그어 막 불을 붙이려던 참에, 두 개의 붙박이별처럼 바람 불어오는 쪽을 향해 눈을 박고 있던 그의 작살잡이 태시테고가, 별안간 꼿꼿한 자세에서 빛처럼 휙 제자리에 주저앉으며 다급한 광기에 휩싸여 외쳤다. \"엎드려, 모두 엎드려, 저어라!—저기 있다!\"\n\n그 순간 뭍사람의 눈에는 고래는커녕 청어 한 마리의 기색조차 보이지 않았으리라. 보이는 것이라곤 어지러이 일렁이는 연녹색의 흰 물결 한 자락과, 그 위를 맴도는 흩어진 옅은 수증기 다발들, 그리고 흰 너울에서 일어나는 어수선한 물보라처럼 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 흩날려 가는 김뿐이었다. 주위의 공기는 별안간 진동하며 따끔거리는 듯했으니, 마치 시뻘겋게 달군 철판 위의 공기와도 같았다. 이렇게 너울거리며 휘감기는 대기 아래, 또 일부는 얇은 물의 층 아래에서, 고래들이 헤엄치고 있었다. 다른 어떤 징후보다 앞서 보이던, 그들이 뿜어 올린 수증기 다발은 마치 그들의 선발 전령이자 떨어져 나와 날아다니는 척후병들처럼 보였다.\n\n이제 네 척의 보트는 모두 그 어지러운 물과 공기의 한 지점을 향해 열렬한 추격에 나섰다. 그러나 그것은 그들을 따돌릴 기세였다. 그것은 마치 산에서 빠른 물살에 떠밀려 내려오는 거품 덩어리처럼, 자꾸자꾸 앞으로 날아갔다.\n\n\"저어라, 저어, 착한 녀석들아.\" 스타벅이 부하들에게 가능한 한 가장 낮으면서도 가장 강렬하게 응축된 속삭임으로 말했다. 그러는 동안 그의 두 눈에서 쏘아져 나오는 날카롭게 고정된 시선은 뱃머리 정면으로 곧장 뻗어, 마치 어김없는 두 개의 나침반 위에 놓인 두 개의 눈에 보이는 바늘 같았다. 그래도 그는 부하들에게 별말을 하지 않았고, 부하들 역시 그에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 다만 보트의 침묵을 이따금 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 꿰뚫는 것은 명령조로 거칠어졌다가 애원조로 부드러워지는, 그의 그 독특한 속삭임 하나뿐이었다.\n\n소리 큰 작은 킹포스트는 이와 얼마나 달랐던가. \"큰 소리로 외치고 뭐라고 말 좀 해라, 친구들아. 포효하며 저어라, 내 벼락들아! 나를 들이박게 해 다오, 저놈들의 검은 등판에 나를 들이박아 다오, 얘들아. 나한테 그것만 해 주면, 내 마서스비니어드 농장을 너희한테 양도해 주마, 얘들아. 처자식까지 다 포함해서 말이다, 얘들아. 나를 갖다 대 다오—나를 갖다 대 다오! 오 주여, 주여! 아무래도 내가 완전히 미쳐 버리고 말 것 같구나! 봐라! 저 흰 물결을 봐라!\" 그렇게 외치며 그는 머리에서 모자를 벗어 발로 짓밟더니, 다시 집어 들어 바다 멀리 휙 던져 버리고는, 마침내 대초원에서 온 미친 망아지처럼 보트 선미에서 날뛰며 곤두박질치기 시작했다.\n\n\"저 친구 좀 보게.\" 스터브가 짧은 거리를 두고 뒤따르며, 불도 붙이지 않은 짧은 파이프를 기계적으로 입에 문 채 철학자처럼 느릿느릿 말했다. \"발작을 일으켰구먼, 저 플래스크 녀석이. 발작이라? 그래, 발작 한번 안겨 주지—그게 딱 맞는 말이야—놈들한테 발작을 처넣어 주는 거야. 즐겁게, 즐겁게, 살아 펄떡이는 녀석들아. 저녁엔 푸딩이 나온다잖느냐—즐거움이 좌우명이지. 저어라, 갓난것들아—저어라, 젖먹이들아—모두 저어라. 그런데 도대체 뭘 그리 서두르는 거냐? 부드럽게, 부드럽게, 그리고 한결같이, 얘들아. 그저 젓기만 해라, 계속 젓기만 해. 그뿐이다. 등뼈를 죄다 으스러뜨리고, 칼을 둘로 깨물어 버려라—그게 전부다. 느긋하게 해라—어째서 느긋하게 안 하는 거냐, 내 말은, 그리고 간이고 폐고 죄다 터뜨려 버려라!\"\n\n그러나 그 헤아릴 길 없는 에이해브가 자기의 호랑이처럼 누런 부하들에게 한 말이 무엇이었는지—그것은 여기서 차라리 빼 두는 편이 나으리라. 그대들은 복음의 빛이 축복처럼 비치는 땅 아래 살고 있으니 말이다. 회오리바람 같은 이마와 시뻘건 살의의 눈, 그리고 거품으로 굳어 버린 입술로 에이해브가 사냥감을 향해 뛰어들 때, 그런 말에 귀 기울일 수 있는 것은 오로지 저 대담무쌍한 바다의 이교도 상어들뿐이리라.\n\n그러는 동안 모든 보트가 맹렬히 내달렸다. 플래스크가 거듭 \"저 고래\"를 구체적으로 들먹였는데—그는 그 꼬리로 자기 보트의 뱃머리를 쉼 없이 약 올린다고 우겨 대는 그 가상의 괴물을 그렇게 불렀다—그의 이런 언급들은 때로 어찌나 생생하고 실감 났던지, 부하 한둘이 어깨 너머로 겁에 질린 시선을 던지게 만들 정도였다. 그러나 이것은 온갖 규칙에 어긋나는 짓이었으니, 노잡이들은 제 눈을 도려내고 목에 꼬챙이를 꿰뚫어야 마땅했다. 관례가 말하기를, 이런 위중한 순간에 그들에게는 귀 말고는 어떤 감각기관도 없어야 하고, 팔 말고는 어떤 사지도 없어야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n그것은 순식간의 경이와 외경으로 가득 찬 광경이었다! 전능한 바다의 거대한 너울들, 끝없는 볼링장의 거대한 공처럼 여덟 개의 뱃전을 따라 굴러가며 일으키는 솟구치고 텅 빈 듯한 그 포효, 더 날카로운 파도가 거의 보트를 둘로 베어 버리겠다고 위협하듯 그 칼날 같은 마루 위에서 한순간 보트가 기우뚱 매달릴 때의 그 짧게 멈추어 선 듯한 고통, 물의 골짜기와 우묵한 곳으로 별안간 깊숙이 곤두박질치는 것, 맞은편 언덕 꼭대기에 오르려고 날카롭게 박차를 가하며 몰아세우는 것, 그 반대편 비탈을 썰매처럼 곤두박질쳐 미끄러져 내려오는 것—이 모든 것이, 우두머리 사냥꾼들과 작살잡이들의 외침, 노잡이들의 부르르 떠는 헐떡임, 그리고 돛을 활짝 펴고 제 보트들을 향해 덮쳐 오는 상아빛 피쿼드호가 마치 비명 지르는 새끼들을 좇는 야생 암탉 같던 그 경이로운 광경과 더불어—이 모든 것이 짜릿하기 그지없었다.\n\n아내의 품에서 나와 제 첫 전투의 열기 속으로 행군해 들어가는 풋내기 신병도, 저세상에서 처음 보는 미지의 유령과 마주치는 죽은 이의 혼령도—이 둘 중 어느 쪽도, 사냥당하는 향유고래의 마법에 걸린 채 휘저어진 원 속으로 처음으로 노 저어 들어가는 자가 느끼는 것보다 더 기이하고 더 강렬한 감정을 느끼지는 못하리라.\n\n추격이 일으킨 춤추는 흰 물결은, 바다 위로 드리워지는 칙칙한 구름 그림자가 점점 더 짙어지는 탓에 이제 점점 더 또렷이 보였다. 수증기 줄기들은 더는 한데 섞이지 않고 여기저기 좌우로 기울었다. 고래들은 제각기 자취를 갈라놓는 듯했다. 보트들은 더 멀찍이 떨어져 노를 저었고, 스타벅은 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 곧장 달아나는 고래 세 마리를 쫓고 있었다. 이제 우리의 돛이 펼쳐졌고, 여전히 거세지는 바람을 받으며 우리는 내달렸다. 보트가 어찌나 미친 듯이 물살을 가르고 나아갔던지, 바람 불어가는 쪽 노들은 노받이에서 뜯겨 나가지 않으려고 빨리 놀려도 도무지 따라잡을 수 없을 지경이었다.\n\n이윽고 우리는 넓게 퍼진 안개의 장막을 뚫고 달리게 되었으니, 배도 보트도 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"저어라, 얘들아.\" 스타벅이 돛의 시트를 더욱 선미 쪽으로 당기며 속삭였다. \"돌풍이 닥치기 전에 아직 고래 한 마리 잡을 시간은 있다. 저기 또 흰 물결이다!—바로 가까이에! 튀어 올라라!\"\n\n곧이어 우리 양옆에서 잇따라 두 차례 터진 외침은 다른 보트들이 고래를 작살로 꽂았음을 알리는 것이었다. 그러나 그 소리가 채 귓전에 닿기도 전에, 스타벅이 번개처럼 휙 스치는 속삭임으로 말했다. \"일어서!\" 그러자 퀴케그가 손에 작살을 든 채 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n그때 노잡이들 가운데 그 누구도 바로 앞에 그토록 가까이 닥친 생사의 위험을 마주하고 있지는 않았으되, 그들은 보트 선미에 선 일등항해사의 긴장된 얼굴에 눈길을 두고서, 절박한 순간이 왔음을 알았다. 또한 그들은 코끼리 쉰 마리가 우리 안에서 뒤척이는 듯한 거대한 출렁임 소리를 들었다. 그러는 동안 보트는 여전히 안개를 뚫고 우르르 내달렸고, 파도는 성난 뱀들이 곧추세운 볏처럼 우리 주위에서 곱이치며 쉭쉭거렸다.\n\n\"저게 그놈의 혹이다. 저기, 저기, 안겨 줘라!\" 스타벅이 속삭였다.\n\n보트에서 짧고 휙 하는 소리가 솟아 나왔다. 퀴케그가 던진 작살이었다. 그러더니 한 덩어리로 뒤엉킨 소동 속에서, 선미 쪽으로부터 보이지 않는 떠밀림이 들이닥쳤고, 한편 뱃머리는 무슨 암붕에라도 부딪치는 듯했다. 돛이 푹 꺼지며 펑 터졌고, 바로 곁에서 펄펄 끓는 듯한 수증기가 확 솟구쳐 올랐으며, 우리 발밑에서는 지진처럼 무언가가 굴러 뒹굴었다. 선원 전원은 돌풍이 빚어낸 하얗게 응어리진 거품 속으로 뒤죽박죽 내동댕이쳐지면서 반쯤 숨이 막혔다. 돌풍과 고래와 작살이 죄다 한데 뒤섞여 버렸고, 작살에 그저 스치기만 한 고래는 달아나 버렸다.\n\n보트는 완전히 물에 잠겼으되, 거의 멀쩡했다. 우리는 그 둘레를 헤엄치며 떠다니는 노들을 건져 올려 뱃전에 가로질러 동여매고는, 굴러떨어지듯 제자리로 돌아갔다. 거기서 우리는 바다에 무릎까지 잠긴 채 앉아 있었으니, 물이 모든 늑재와 판자를 뒤덮어, 아래를 내려다보는 우리 눈에는 그 떠 있는 배가 마치 대양 밑바닥에서 우리를 향해 자라 올라온 산호 보트처럼 보였다.\n\n바람은 울부짖음으로 거세졌고, 파도는 제 방패들을 서로 맞부딪쳤다. 돌풍 전체가 우리 주위에서 포효하고 갈래갈래 갈라지며 탁탁 튀었으니, 마치 대초원 위의 흰 불길 같았고, 우리는 그 속에서 타지 않은 채 타오르고 있었다. 이 죽음의 아가리 속에서 불멸하는 존재로! 우리는 헛되이 다른 보트들을 불렀다. 그 폭풍 속에서 저 보트들을 부른다는 것은 활활 타오르는 용광로의 굴뚝 아래 시뻘건 숯덩이를 향해 고함치는 것이나 마찬가지였다. 그러는 동안 휘몰아치는 물보라와 찢긴 구름과 안개는 밤의 그림자로 점점 어두워졌고, 배의 자취는 어디서도 보이지 않았다. 솟구치는 바다는 보트의 물을 퍼낼 어떤 시도도 허락하지 않았다. 노는 추진기로서는 쓸모가 없어, 이제 구명 도구의 노릇을 하고 있었다. 그래서 스타벅은 방수 처리된 성냥통의 결박을 끊고, 여러 번 실패한 끝에 간신히 등롱 속 램프에 불을 붙였다. 그러고는 그것을 표지 막대에 매달아, 이 가망 없는 사투의 기수로 삼아 퀴케그에게 건넸다. 거기, 그 자리에서, 그는 그 전능한 절망의 한복판에서 저 무력한 촛불을 치켜든 채 앉아 있었다. 거기, 그 자리에서, 그는 믿음 없는 인간의 표지이자 상징으로서, 절망 한가운데서 가망 없이 희망을 치켜든 채 앉아 있었다.\n\n흠뻑 젖어 온몸이 으슬으슬 떨리고, 배도 보트도 만나리란 희망을 잃은 채, 우리는 동이 터 오자 눈을 들어 올렸다. 안개는 여전히 바다 위에 퍼져 있었고, 텅 빈 등롱은 보트 바닥에 짓이겨져 나뒹굴었다. 별안간 퀴케그가 벌떡 일어서더니 손을 오므려 귀에 갖다 댔다. 우리 모두 폭풍에 가려 그때껏 들리지 않던 밧줄과 활대의 희미한 삐걱거림 같은 소리를 들었다. 그 소리는 점점 가까워졌다. 짙은 안개가 거대하고 흐릿한 형체에 의해 어슴푸레 갈라졌다. 우리는 기겁하여 다 함께 바다로 뛰어들었으니, 마침내 모습을 드러낸 배가 제 배 길이보다 그리 멀지 않은 거리 안으로 곧장 우리를 향해 덮쳐 왔기 때문이었다.\n\n파도 위에 떠서 우리는 버려진 보트를 보았는데, 그것은 한순간 폭포 밑바닥의 나무 부스러기처럼 배의 뱃머리 아래에서 까딱이며 입을 떡 벌렸다. 그러더니 그 거대한 선체가 그 위로 굴러 넘어갔고, 보트는 선미 쪽으로 허우적대며 떠오르기 전까지는 다시 보이지 않았다. 우리는 다시 그쪽으로 헤엄쳤고, 파도에 떠밀려 그것에 부딪쳤다가, 마침내 끌어 올려져 무사히 배 위로 옮겨졌다. 돌풍이 가까이 닥치기 전에 다른 보트들은 제 고래를 끊어 버리고 제때 배로 돌아와 있었다. 배는 우리를 단념했으되, 혹시라도 우리가 죽었음을 알리는 어떤 표적—노 한 자루나 창 자루 하나라도—마주칠까 하여 여전히 바다를 누비고 있었던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 49. The Hyena.\n\n우리가 인생이라 부르는 이 기묘하게 뒤섞인 사건 속에는, 한 사내가 이 우주 전체를 거대한 짓궂은 장난으로 여기게 되는 야릇한 순간과 경우가 더러 있다. 비록 그 농담의 묘미를 그는 어렴풋이밖에 알아채지 못하지만, 그 농담이 다른 누구도 아닌 바로 자기 자신을 겨냥한 것임을 어렴풋이 짐작하는 정도를 넘어선다. 그러나 그 무엇도 그의 기를 꺾지 못하고, 그 무엇도 따져 볼 가치가 있어 보이지 않는다. 그는 모든 사건과 모든 신조와 믿음과 신념을, 보이는 것이든 보이지 않는 것이든 온갖 딱딱한 것들을 그것이 아무리 울퉁불퉁하든 아랑곳없이 통째로 삼켜 버린다. 마치 소화력이 강한 타조가 총알과 부싯돌을 꿀꺽 삼키듯이. 그리고 사소한 곤경과 근심거리, 갑작스러운 재앙의 전망, 목숨과 사지가 처한 위험, 이 모든 것이, 그리고 죽음 그 자체마저도, 그에게는 보이지 않고 헤아릴 수 없는 저 늙은 익살꾼이 슬쩍 던지는 짓궂으면서도 마음씨 좋은 한 방, 옆구리를 유쾌하게 쿡 찌르는 농에 지나지 않게 보인다. 내가 말하는 그 묘한 변덕스러운 기분은, 오직 극심한 환난의 시기에만 사람을 찾아온다. 그것은 그가 한창 진지한 와중에 찾아오기에, 바로 조금 전까지만 해도 더없이 중대해 보이던 일이 이제는 그저 저 보편적 농담의 한 부분으로만 보이게 된다. 이런 거리낌 없고 느긋하며 호방한, 무뢰한의 철학을 길러 내는 데에는 고래잡이의 위험만 한 것이 없다. 그리고 나는 이제 그런 마음가짐으로 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 이 항해 전부와, 그 목표인 저 거대한 흰 고래를 바라보았다.\n\n\"퀴퀘그(Queequeg).\" 그들이 맨 마지막으로 나를 갑판 위로 끌어올렸고, 내가 아직도 웃옷을 흔들어 물을 털어 내고 있을 때 나는 말했다. \"퀴퀘그, 나의 멋진 친구여, 이런 일이 자주 일어나는 건가?\" 나와 꼭 마찬가지로 흠뻑 젖었으면서도 그는 별다른 감정 없이, 이런 일이 자주 일어난다는 뜻을 내게 전했다.\n\n\"스터브(Stubb) 씨.\" 나는 그 훌륭한 사내 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. 그는 기름 먹인 웃옷의 단추를 채운 채, 이제 빗속에서 태연히 파이프를 피우고 있었다. \"스터브 씨, 당신이 여태껏 만난 모든 고래잡이 중에서 우리 일등항해사 스타벅(Starbuck) 씨가 단연 가장 조심스럽고 신중한 사람이라고 말하는 걸 들은 것 같은데. 그렇다면 안개 낀 돌풍 속에서 돛을 활짝 펴고 달아나는 고래에게 곧장 돌진하는 것이 고래잡이의 신중함의 극치라는 말이오?\"\n\n\"물론이지. 나는 케이프 혼(Cape Horn) 앞바다 강풍 속에서, 물이 새는 배에서도 고래 잡으러 보트를 내린 적이 있다네.\"\n\n\"플래스크(Flask) 씨.\" 나는 바로 곁에 서 있던 키 작은 킹포스트에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"당신은 이런 일에 경험이 많고 나는 그렇지 못하오. 플래스크 씨, 이 어업에서 노잡이가 자기 등이 부러지도록 노를 저어 거꾸로 죽음의 아가리 속으로 끌려 들어가는 것이 결코 바뀌지 않는 법칙인지 말해 주겠소?\"\n\n\"그걸 좀 더 간단히 비틀 순 없나?\" 플래스크가 말했다. \"그래, 그게 법칙이지. 나는 보트의 선원이 고래를 정면으로 마주 보면서 물을 거꾸로 저어 가는 꼴을 한번 보고 싶군. 하하! 그러면 고래가 그들에게 곁눈질에는 곁눈질로 응수해 줄 걸세, 명심하라고!\"\n\n이렇게 해서 나는 세 사람의 공정한 증인으로부터 이 사건 전체에 관한 신중한 진술을 얻은 셈이었다. 그러니 돌풍과 물속에서의 전복, 그리고 그에 따라 깊은 바다 위에서 야영하듯 떠 있는 일이 이런 종류의 삶에서는 흔히 일어나는 일임을 헤아려 보면. 또한 고래에게 돌진하는 더없이 위태로운 그 순간에, 나는 보트를 모는 자의 손에 내 목숨을 내맡길 수밖에 없으며—그자는 흔히 바로 그 순간 자신의 격렬함에 사로잡혀, 미친 듯이 발을 굴러 제 손으로 배를 침몰시키려는 지경에 이른 사내임을 헤아려 보면. 또한 우리 보트가 당한 특정한 재난이 주로, 거의 돌풍의 정면에 맞서다시피 자신의 고래에게 돌진한 스타벅의 탓으로 돌려야 할 일이었음을 헤아려 보면. 그러면서도 스타벅이 어업에서 더없이 신중하기로 이름난 사람이었음을 헤아려 보면. 또한 내가 이 유달리 사려 깊은 스타벅의 보트에 속해 있었음을 헤아려 보면. 그리고 끝으로, 흰 고래를 두고 내가 얼마나 악마 같은 추격에 얽혀 들었는가를 헤아려 보면. 이 모든 것을 한데 묶어 보건대, 나는 차라리 아래로 내려가 유언장 초안이나 대충 써 두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다. \"퀴퀘그.\" 나는 말했다. \"이리 오게. 자네가 내 변호사이자 유언 집행자이며 유산 상속인이 되어 주게.\"\n\n모든 사람 중에서 하필 뱃사람들이 자기 유언장을 만지작거린다는 것은 이상하게 보일지 모르나, 세상에 그 소일거리를 그들보다 더 좋아하는 사람은 없다. 이번 일은 내가 뱃사람으로 살아오면서 같은 짓을 한 네 번째였다. 이번에도 그 의식을 마치고 나니, 한결 마음이 가벼워졌다. 가슴 위에서 돌덩이 하나가 굴러 떨어진 듯했다. 게다가 이제부터 내가 살아갈 모든 날들은, 부활한 뒤에 나사로가 살았던 날들만큼이나 값진 것이리라. 형편에 따라 몇 달이든 몇 주든, 깨끗이 덤으로 얻은 이득인 셈이었다. 나는 나 자신을 넘어 살아남았다. 내 죽음과 매장은 내 궤짝 속에 단단히 잠겨 있었다. 나는 차분하고 흡족한 마음으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 마치 떳떳한 양심을 지닌 채 아늑한 가족 납골당 창살 안에 앉아 있는 조용한 유령처럼.\n\n자, 이제, 하고 나는 생각하며 나도 모르게 작업복 소매를 걷어 올렸다. 죽음과 파멸을 향해 냉정하고 침착하게 뛰어들 테니, 뒤처지는 놈은 악마에게나 잡혀가라지.\n\n# CHAPTER 50. Ahab’s Boat and Crew. Fedallah.\n\n\"누가 그런 일을 상상이나 했겠나, 플래스크!\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"내가 만약 다리가 하나뿐이었다면, 자네는 결코 나를 보트 안에서 보지 못했을 걸세. 굳이 본다면 내 나무 발가락으로 배수 구멍이나 틀어막을 때쯤이겠지. 아아! 그분은 정말 대단한 노인이야!\"\n\n\"난 그 점에 관해서라면 그렇게 이상하다고는 생각하지 않네.\" 플래스크가 말했다. \"이제 만약 그분 다리가 엉덩이께에서 잘려 나갔다면, 그건 사정이 다르겠지. 그러면 아주 못 쓰게 되는 거니까. 하지만 그분에게는 한쪽 무릎이 멀쩡하고, 다른 쪽도 상당 부분이 남아 있잖나.\"\n\n\"그건 나도 모르겠네, 이 작은 친구야. 나는 아직 그분이 무릎 꿇는 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없으니까.\"\n\n고래에 정통한 사람들 사이에서는 종종 이런 논쟁이 벌어지곤 했다. 선장의 생명이 항해의 성공에 더없이 중대하다는 점을 고려할 때, 고래잡이 선장이 추격의 능동적인 위험 속에 자기 목숨을 내거는 것이 과연 옳은가 하는 문제였다. 마찬가지로 티무르의 병사들도 눈물을 글썽이며 자주 논쟁을 벌였으니, 그토록 값진 그의 생명을 싸움의 가장 치열한 한복판으로 끌고 들어가야 하는가 하는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 에이해브에게 이르러서는 그 문제가 다소 변형된 양상을 띠었다. 인간이란 두 다리가 있어도 위험한 순간이면 모두 절뚝거리는 신세에 지나지 않는다는 점을 고려할 때, 고래 추격이란 언제나 크고 비상한 곤경 아래 이루어진다는 점을, 실로 그때는 매 순간순간이 하나하나 위험을 품고 있다는 점을 고려할 때, 이러한 상황에서 불구의 몸을 한 사람이 사냥에 나선 고래잡이 보트에 오르는 것이 과연 현명한 일인가? 대체로 보아, 피쿼드호의 공동 선주들은 분명 그렇지 않다고 여겼을 것이다.\n\n에이해브는 잘 알고 있었다. 비교적 해롭지 않은 추격의 어떤 우여곡절 속에서, 행동의 현장 가까이에 머물며 몸소 명령을 내리기 위해 그가 보트에 오르는 일이라면, 고향의 친구들은 대수롭지 않게 여길 터였다. 그러나 에이해브 선장에게 정식 작살 두목으로서 보트 한 척이 실제로 배정되고 — 무엇보다 에이해브 선장에게 그 보트의 선원으로 다섯 명의 별도 인원이 주어진다는 것은, 그런 후한 발상 따위가 피쿼드호 선주들의 머릿속에는 결코 떠오르지 않으리라는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었다. 그러므로 그는 선주들에게 보트 선원을 청하지도 않았고, 그 문제에 관한 자신의 바람을 어떤 식으로도 내비치지 않았다. 그럼에도 그는 그 모든 일과 관련하여 은밀히 자기 나름의 조치를 취해 두었다. 카바코가 그 사실을 공공연히 폭로하기 전까지, 선원들은 그것을 거의 예측하지 못했다. 물론 항구를 떠난 지 얼마쯤 지났을 때, 모든 선원이 고래잡이 보트를 작업에 쓸 수 있도록 정비하는 통상적인 일을 마무리한 뒤, 그로부터 얼마간 시간이 흐른 무렵에 예비 보트 중 하나로 여겨지던 것을 위해 에이해브가 이따금 직접 노받이 못을 만드는 일에 분주히 손을 놀리는 모습이 눈에 띄었고, 심지어 밧줄이 풀려 나갈 때 뱃머리의 홈 위에 꽂아 두는 작은 나무 쐐기까지 정성껏 깎고 있는 것이 목격되기도 했다. 이 모든 것이 그에게서 관찰되었을 때, 특히 그가 보트 바닥에 덧대는 판자를 한 겹 더 깔아 두는 데 보이던 그 정성 — 마치 자신의 상아 다리가 가하는 뾰족한 압력을 더 잘 견디게 하려는 듯한 — 이 눈에 띄었을 때, 또한 허벅지받이판, 혹은 흔히 투박한 받침대라고도 불리는, 고래에게 작살을 던지거나 창을 찌를 때 무릎을 받쳐 버티는 데 쓰는 보트 뱃머리의 그 수평 목재를 정확한 모양으로 다듬는 데 그가 보이던 그 노심초사가 목격되었을 때, 그가 얼마나 자주 그 보트 안에 일어서서 외로운 한쪽 무릎을 그 받침대의 반원형 홈에 끼우고는, 목수의 끌로 여기를 조금 파내고 저기를 조금 곧게 다듬었는지가 관찰되었을 때 — 이 모든 일이, 다시 말하건대, 그 당시에 적지 않은 흥미와 호기심을 불러일으켰던 것이다. 그러나 거의 모든 이가 에이해브의 이 유난스러운 준비성과 세심함이 오로지 모비 딕을 향한 최후의 추격을 염두에 둔 것이라고만 짐작했다. 왜냐하면 그는 이미 그 치명적인 괴물을 몸소 사냥하겠다는 의도를 드러낸 바 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그러한 짐작에는 그 보트에 어떤 선원이 배정되리라는 데 대한 일말의 의심조차 결코 포함되어 있지 않았다.\n\n자, 그 부속 유령들에 관해서는 남아 있던 의아함마저 곧 사그라들었다. 고래잡이 배에서는 놀라움이란 금세 시드는 법이니까. 게다가 이따금 그런 도무지 설명할 길 없는 갖가지 잡동사니 같은 이방 인종들이 땅의 알려지지 않은 구석과 재 구덩이에서 솟아 나와, 이 떠다니는 고래잡이 무법자 배들에 인원을 채우곤 한다. 또한 배들 자체도 종종 그런 기이하게 표류하던 생명체들을 건져 올리는데, 그들은 망망대해 위에서 널빤지나 난파선 조각, 노, 고래잡이 보트, 카누, 바람에 떠밀려 온 일본 정크선, 그 밖의 온갖 것들에 매달려 까닥거리고 있던 자들이다. 그러니 바알세불 본인이 뱃전을 기어올라 선실로 내려와 선장과 한담을 나눈다 한들, 그것은 선원실에 그 어떤 누를 수 없는 동요도 일으키지 못할 것이다.\n\n그러나 그 모든 것이 어떠하든 간에, 한 가지 확실한 것은, 그 부속 유령들은 비록 여전히 어쩐지 다른 선원들과 구별되는 듯하기는 했어도 곧 선원들 사이에서 자기 자리를 찾았으나, 머리에 터번을 두른 저 페달라만은 끝끝내 베일에 싸인 수수께끼로 남았다는 점이다. 이처럼 점잖은 세상에 그가 대체 어디서 왔는지, 어떤 종류의 설명할 수 없는 끈으로 그가 그토록 빨리 에이해브의 기이한 운명과 얽혀 있음을 드러냈는지, 아니, 어느 정도까지는 은근히 암시되는 영향력을 — 하늘만이 알겠지만 어쩌면 그에 대한 권위마저도 — 지니게 되었는지, 이 모든 것을 아는 이는 아무도 없었다. 그러나 누구도 페달라에 관해서만은 무관심한 태도를 유지할 수 없었다. 그는 온대 지방의 문명화된 가정적인 사람들이 오직 꿈에서나 볼 법한, 그것도 흐릿하게만 볼 법한 그런 존재였다. 그러나 그와 비슷한 자들은 변함없는 아시아의 공동체들, 특히 대륙 동쪽의 동방 섬들 사이를 이따금 미끄러지듯 오간다. 그 외따로 떨어진, 까마득히 오래된, 결코 변하지 않는 나라들은 이 현대에 이르러서도 여전히 지구의 원초적 세대들이 지녔던 유령 같은 원시성을 상당 부분 간직하고 있으니, 그것은 첫 사람에 대한 기억이 아직 또렷한 회상으로 남아 있던 시절, 그리고 그의 후손인 모든 인간이 자신이 어디서 왔는지도 모른 채 서로를 진짜 유령처럼 바라보며, 해와 달을 향해 어찌하여 그것들이 창조되었으며 무슨 목적을 위함이냐고 묻던 시절의 것이다. 그때는, 창세기에 따르면 천사들이 실로 사람의 딸들과 어울렸고, 또한 외경에 속한 랍비들의 말을 덧붙이자면 악마들조차 이 세상의 사랑놀음에 탐닉했던 시절이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 51. The Spirit-Spout.\n\n여러 날, 여러 주가 흘러갔다. 상아로 무장한 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 편안한 돛을 펼친 채 네 군데의 서로 다른 어장을 천천히 가로질렀다. 아조레스 근해, 카보베르데 근해, 라플라타강 어귀에 있어 흔히 플레이트라 불리는 어장, 그리고 세인트헬레나섬에서 남쪽으로 떨어진, 표지 하나 없는 망망한 바다 캐럴 어장이 그것이었다.\n\n이 마지막 바다를 미끄러지듯 지나던 어느 고요하고 달 밝은 밤이었다. 물결이 모조리 은빛 두루마리처럼 굽이쳐 흘러가고, 그 부드럽게 번져 가는 들끓음으로 인해 적막이라기보다는 차라리 은빛 정적이라 할 만한 것이 펼쳐졌다. 그런 고요한 밤에, 뱃머리의 하얀 거품 떼보다 훨씬 앞쪽에서 은빛 물줄기 하나가 솟구치는 것이 보였다. 달빛을 받아 그것은 천상의 것처럼 보였다. 마치 깃털을 두르고 번쩍이는 어떤 신이 바다에서 솟아오르는 듯했다. 이 물줄기를 가장 먼저 알아본 것은 페달라였다. 이런 달 밝은 밤이면 그는 큰돛대 꼭대기에 올라가, 마치 대낮이기라도 한 듯 똑같이 정확하게 망을 보는 것이 버릇이었기 때문이다. 그런데 밤에 고래 떼가 보인다 한들, 백 명 가운데 한 명도 그것을 잡으러 보트를 내리겠다고 나설 고래잡이는 없었다. 그러니 선원들이 이 늙은 동방인이 그토록 야릇한 시각에 높은 곳에 걸터앉아 있는 모습을 어떤 심정으로 바라보았을지 짐작이 갈 것이다. 그의 터번과 달이 한 하늘 아래 동무로 떠 있었다. 그러나 그가 며칠 밤을 잇따라 한결같은 간격을 지켜 그곳에 머물면서도 단 한 마디 소리도 내지 않다가, 그 모든 침묵 끝에 마침내 그 은빛 달빛 물줄기를 알리는 기괴한 목소리가 들려왔을 때, 누워 있던 선원들은 마치 날개 돋친 정령이 삭구에 내려앉아 죽을 운명의 선원들을 부르기라도 한 듯 저마다 벌떡 일어섰다. \"저기 물을 뿜는다!\" 최후 심판의 나팔이 울렸다 한들 그들이 그보다 더 떨지는 못했으리라. 그러나 그들은 공포를 느끼지 않았다. 오히려 기쁨을 느꼈다. 비록 더없이 별난 시각이었으나, 그 외침은 너무도 인상적이고 너무도 정신없이 흥분되는 것이어서, 배에 탄 거의 모든 영혼이 본능적으로 보트를 내리고 싶어 했던 것이다.\n\n에이해브(Ahab)는 옆으로 몸을 내던지듯 성큼성큼 빠른 걸음으로 갑판을 거닐며, 윗돛과 맨 윗돛을 올리고 보조돛을 빠짐없이 펼치라 명령했다. 배에서 가장 솜씨 좋은 자가 키를 잡아야 했다. 그리하여 돛대 꼭대기마다 망꾼이 배치된 채, 돛을 잔뜩 쌓아 올린 배는 바람을 안고 내달렸다. 고물 쪽에서 불어오는 바람이 그 숱한 돛의 빈 공간을 채우며 야릇하게 솟구쳐 들어 올리는 기세가, 둥실 떠올라 맴도는 듯한 갑판을 발밑에 마치 허공인 양 느끼게 했다. 그러면서도 배는 여전히 앞으로 돌진했으니, 마치 두 개의 상반된 힘이 배 안에서 다투는 듯했다. 하나는 곧장 하늘로 솟구치려는 힘이요, 다른 하나는 좌우로 흔들리며 어떤 수평의 목표를 향해 내달리려는 힘이었다. 그날 밤 에이해브의 얼굴을 보았더라면, 그의 안에서도 두 가지 서로 다른 것이 싸우고 있다고 여겼으리라. 그의 살아 있는 한쪽 다리가 갑판 위에 경쾌한 메아리를 울리는 동안, 죽은 다리가 내딛는 걸음걸음은 관을 두드리는 소리처럼 들렸다. 이 늙은이는 삶과 죽음을 딛고 걸었다. 그러나 배가 그토록 쏜살같이 내달리고, 모든 눈이 화살처럼 간절한 시선을 쏘아 보냈건만, 그 은빛 물줄기는 그날 밤 다시는 보이지 않았다. 선원마다 한 번은 분명히 보았노라 맹세했으나, 두 번째로 본 자는 없었다.\n\n이 한밤중의 물줄기는 거의 잊힌 것이 되어 가던 차에, 며칠 뒤, 보라! 똑같이 고요한 시각에 그것이 또다시 알려졌다. 다시 모두가 그것을 알아보았다. 그러나 돛을 올려 뒤쫓으려 들자, 마치 처음부터 있지도 않았던 것처럼 또 한 번 사라져 버렸다. 그렇게 그것은 밤마다 우리를 농락했고, 마침내 아무도 그것을 마음에 두지 않은 채 그저 의아해할 뿐이었다. 때로는 맑은 달빛 속으로, 때로는 별빛 속으로 신비롭게 솟구쳐 올랐다가, 다시 하루나 이틀, 사흘씩 자취를 감추곤 했다. 그리고 어쩐 일인지 또렷이 되풀이될 때마다 우리 앞쪽으로 점점 더 멀리, 더 멀리 나아가는 듯이 보이는 이 외로운 물줄기는, 영영 우리를 앞으로 꾀어내는 것만 같았다.\n\n그들 종족의 태고 적부터 내려온 미신과, 또 여러 면에서 피쿼드호를 휘감은 듯 보이던 그 초자연적 기운에 걸맞게도, 선원들 가운데에는 그것이 언제 어디서 보이든, 아무리 동떨어진 시각이든, 아무리 멀리 떨어진 위도와 경도든, 그 다가갈 수 없는 물줄기는 단 하나의 똑같은 고래가 뿜어 올린 것이며, 그 고래가 바로 모비 딕(Moby Dick)이라고 맹세하는 자들이 없지 않았다. 한동안은 이 어른거리는 환영에 대해 묘한 공포가 감돌기도 했으니, 마치 그것이 음흉하게 우리를 앞으로, 또 앞으로 손짓해 불러내어, 그 괴물이 마침내 가장 외지고 가장 사나운 바다에서 우리에게 돌아서서 우리를 갈가리 찢어 놓으려는 것만 같았다.\n\n이 막연하지만 너무도 끔찍한 일시적 불안은, 대조를 이루는 날씨의 고요함에서 놀라운 힘을 얻었다. 그 푸르른 부드러움 아래 어떤 악마적 마력이 도사리고 있다고 여기는 자도 있었으니, 우리는 며칠이고 며칠이고, 너무도 지겹고 외롭도록 잔잔한 바다를 가로질러 항해했고, 온 공간이 우리의 복수에 찬 사명을 혐오하기라도 하듯 우리의 항아리 같은 뱃머리 앞에서 스스로 생명을 비워 내는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 마침내 동쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌리자, 곶의 바람이 우리 둘레에서 울부짖기 시작했고, 우리는 그곳의 길고 거친 바다 위로 솟구쳤다 떨어졌다 했다. 상아 엄니를 단 피쿼드호가 돌풍을 향해 날카롭게 머리를 숙이고, 미친 듯이 검은 물결을 들이받자, 마침내 거품 조각이 은빛 부스러기 소나기처럼 뱃전 너머로 흩날렸다. 그러자 이 황량하고 텅 빈 생명의 공허는 사라졌으나, 그 자리에 이전보다 더 음울한 광경들이 들어섰다.\n\n뱃머리 가까이에서는 물속의 기이한 형체들이 우리 앞에서 이리저리 쏜살같이 내달렸고, 한편 우리 고물 쪽으로는 속을 알 수 없는 바다 까마귀들이 빽빽이 날아들었다. 그리고 아침마다 우리 지삭(支索)에 걸터앉은 이 새들이 줄지어 보였다. 우리가 아무리 쫓으려 소리를 질러도 오랫동안 그것들은 삼노끈에 끈질기게 매달려 있었으니, 마치 우리 배를 아무도 살지 않은 채 떠다니는 표류선쯤으로, 황폐함에 운명 지어졌기에 집 없는 제 몸들이 깃들기에 알맞은 자리쯤으로 여기는 듯했다. 그리고 검은 바다는 들썩이고 또 들썩이며, 쉴 줄 모르고 여전히 들썩였으니, 마치 그 광막한 조수가 하나의 양심이기라도 한 듯, 그리고 이 거대한 세상의 영혼이 제가 길러 낸 오랜 죄악과 고통에 괴로워하고 뉘우치기라도 하는 듯했다.\n\n희망봉이라, 그대들은 그곳을 그리 부르는가? 차라리 옛적에 불리던 대로 토르멘토소곶, 곧 고난의 곶이라 부르는 편이 옳으리라. 앞서 우리를 따라다니던 그 음흉한 침묵에 오래도록 홀려 끌려온 끝에, 우리는 이 고난의 바다로 내던져진 자신을 발견했으니, 그곳에서는 저 새와 이 물고기로 둔갑한 죄지은 존재들이, 깃들일 항구 하나 없이 영영 헤엄쳐야 하고, 지평선 하나 없는 저 검은 허공을 끝없이 날갯짓해야 할 운명에 처한 듯 보였다. 그러나 고요하고, 눈처럼 새하얗고, 변함없이, 여전히 깃털 같은 분수를 하늘로 뿜어 올리며, 여전히 앞쪽에서 우리를 손짓해 부르는 그 외로운 물줄기가 때때로 보이곤 했다.\n\n천지자연이 온통 이렇게 캄캄하던 내내, 에이해브는 비록 한동안 물에 흠뻑 젖은 위험한 갑판을 거의 쉴 새 없이 지휘하긴 했으나, 더없이 음울한 침묵을 드러냈고, 그 어느 때보다 드물게 항해사들에게 말을 건넸다. 이런 폭풍우의 시절에는, 갑판 위와 높은 곳의 모든 것을 단단히 매어 두고 나면, 그저 폭풍이 어찌 될지를 가만히 기다리는 것 말고는 더 할 수 있는 일이 없다. 그러면 선장도 선원도 현실적인 운명론자가 된다. 그리하여 상아 다리를 늘 끼우던 구멍에 박아 넣고, 한 손으로는 삭구를 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 에이해브는 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 바람 불어오는 쪽을 죽은 듯 응시하며 서 있었으며, 이따금 진눈깨비나 눈보라가 몰아쳐 그의 속눈썹마저 거의 얼어붙게 했다. 그동안 선원들은 뱃머리 위로 부서지듯 덮쳐 오는 위험한 파도에 밀려 배의 앞쪽에서 쫓겨나, 한가운데 뱃전을 따라 한 줄로 늘어서 있었다. 그리고 펄쩍 뛰어오르는 파도를 더 잘 막아 내려고, 저마다 난간에 묶어 둔 일종의 고리줄에 제 몸을 끼워 넣은 채, 헐거운 허리띠에 매달리듯 그 안에서 흔들거렸다. 오가는 말은 거의, 아니 전혀 없었다. 그리고 마치 밀랍으로 빚어 색칠한 선원들이 부리는 듯한 그 말 없는 배는, 날이면 날마다 악마 같은 파도의 그 거센 광기와 환희를 뚫고 내달렸다. 밤이 되어도 대양의 비명 앞에 선 인간의 그 똑같은 침묵이 지배했다. 여전히 침묵 속에서 사람들은 고리줄에 매달려 흔들렸고, 여전히 말없이 에이해브는 돌풍을 마주하고 서 있었다. 지칠 대로 지친 육신이 휴식을 요구하는 듯할 때조차 그는 해먹에서 그 휴식을 구하려 하지 않았다. 스타벅(Starbuck)은 그 늙은이의 모습을 결코 잊을 수 없었으니, 어느 밤 기압계가 어떤지 살피러 선실로 내려갔다가, 그가 바닥에 나사로 고정된 의자에 두 눈을 감은 채 꼿꼿이 앉아 있는 것을 보았던 것이다. 얼마 전 그가 빠져나온 폭풍의 비와 반쯤 녹은 진눈깨비가, 여태 벗지 않은 모자와 외투에서 여전히 천천히 떨어지고 있었다. 그의 곁 탁자 위에는 앞서 이야기한 바 있는 조수와 해류 해도 가운데 하나가 펼쳐져 있었다. 그의 등잔은 꽉 움켜쥔 손에 매달려 흔들렸다. 몸은 꼿꼿이 세웠으나 머리는 뒤로 젖혀져 있어서, 감긴 두 눈은 천장 들보에 매달려 흔들리는 알림나침반의 바늘을 향하고 있었다.\n\n* 선실 나침반은 알림나침반이라 불리는데, 이는 선장이 아래에 있으면서도 키의 나침반까지 가지 않고도 배의 항로를 알 수 있기 때문이다.\n\n무서운 늙은이로구나! 스타벅은 몸서리치며 생각했다. 이 폭풍 속에 잠들어서도 그대는 여전히 한결같이 그대의 목적을 응시하고 있구나.\n\n# CHAPTER 52. The Albatross.\n\n희망봉에서 남동쪽으로, 멀리 떨어진 크로제 제도 부근, 참고래(Right Whale)잡이들에게는 더없이 좋은 조업 해역에서, 한 척의 배가 앞쪽에 어렴풋이 모습을 드러냈다. 고니(앨버트로스)호라는 이름의 배였다. 그 배가 천천히 다가올 때, 나는 앞돛대 꼭대기 높은 자리에서 먼바다 어장에 갓 들어선 풋내기에게는 더없이 놀라운 광경을 똑똑히 볼 수 있었다. 오랫동안 고향을 떠나 바다 위를 떠도는 고래잡이배의 모습이었다.\n\n마치 파도가 표백공이라도 되는 양, 이 배는 뭍에 좌초된 바다코끼리의 뼈처럼 허옇게 바래 있었다. 옆구리를 따라 아래로 죽 늘어선 붉은 녹의 긴 줄기들이 이 유령 같은 모습을 더해 주었고, 활대와 삭구는 온통 흰 서리로 뒤덮인 나무의 굵은 가지들 같았다. 펼쳐진 돛은 아래쪽 돛뿐이었다. 세 돛대 꼭대기에 자리한 긴 수염의 망꾼들을 바라보는 것은 야릇한 광경이었다. 그들은 짐승의 가죽을 두른 듯 보였으니, 거의 사 년에 걸친 항해를 견뎌 낸 옷가지는 그토록 찢기고 기워져 있었다. 돛대에 못으로 박은 쇠테 안에 선 채로, 그들은 깊이를 알 수 없는 바다 위에서 흔들리고 출렁였다. 배가 천천히 미끄러져 우리 고물 아래로 바싹 다가왔을 때, 공중에 있던 우리 여섯 사내는 서로 어찌나 가까워졌던지 한 배의 돛대 꼭대기에서 다른 배의 돛대 꼭대기로 뛰어 건너갈 수 있을 정도였다. 그럼에도 저 쓸쓸해 보이는 어부들은 스쳐 지나가며 우리를 부드럽게 바라볼 뿐, 우리 망꾼들에게 한마디도 건네지 않았다. 그러는 동안 아래쪽 뒷갑판에서는 외치는 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"어이, 배! 흰 고래(White Whale)를 본 적이 있소?\"\n\n그러나 그 낯선 선장이 핏기 없는 뱃전 너머로 몸을 기울인 채 나팔을 입에 가져가려는 찰나, 어쩐 일인지 그것이 손에서 빠져 바다로 떨어지고 말았다. 게다가 바람이 세차게 거세지면서 그는 나팔도 없이 자기 목소리를 들리게 하려 애썼으나 헛수고였다. 그사이 그의 배는 둘 사이의 거리를 점점 더 벌리고 있었다. 다른 배에 흰 고래의 이름을 처음 입에 올린 그 일이 불길한 사건임을 페쿼드(Pequod)호의 선원들이 갖가지 말 없는 방식으로 드러내고 있는 동안, 에이해브(Ahab)는 한순간 멈칫했다. 위협적인 바람이 막지만 않았더라면 그는 보트를 내려 낯선 배에 올라타기라도 할 것처럼 보였다. 그러나 자신이 바람 위쪽에 있는 이점을 살려, 그는 다시 나팔을 거머쥐었고, 배의 생김새로 보아 그 낯선 배가 낸터킷(Nantucket) 배이며 곧 고향으로 돌아가는 길임을 알아채고는 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"어이, 거기! 이 배는 세계를 일주하는 페쿼드호다! 앞으로 부치는 편지는 모두 태평양으로 보내라고 전해 주시오! 그리고 만 삼 년 뒤에도 내가 집에 없거든, 편지를 ——로 부치라고 전해 주시오!\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 두 배의 물길이 완전히 엇갈렸다. 그러자 그 별난 습성에 따라, 며칠 전부터 우리 곁에서 잔잔히 헤엄치던 작고 해롭지 않은 물고기 떼가 마치 지느러미를 떨듯 쏜살같이 달아나, 낯선 배의 옆구리를 따라 앞뒤로 늘어섰다. 끝없는 항해의 와중에 에이해브도 분명 전에 비슷한 광경을 자주 보았을 터이나, 그럼에도 어떤 편집광에게든 가장 하찮은 것조차 제멋대로 의미를 띠는 법이다.\n\n\"나에게서 헤엄쳐 달아나는구나, 그래?\" 에이해브가 물속을 들여다보며 중얼거렸다. 그 말에는 별것이 담겨 있지 않은 듯했으나, 그 어조는 이 미친 노인이 일찍이 드러낸 그 어떤 것보다도 깊고 어쩔 수 없는 슬픔을 품고 있었다. 그러나 이제껏 배의 속력을 줄이려고 바람을 향해 뱃머리를 붙들고 있던 키잡이를 돌아보며, 그는 예의 그 사자 같은 목소리로 외쳤다. \"키를 올려라! 세계를 돌아 계속 나아가게 하라!\"\n\n세계 일주라! 그 울림에는 자랑스러운 감정을 불러일으키는 데가 많다. 그러나 그 모든 항해는 끝내 우리를 어디로 데려가는가? 그저 헤아릴 수 없는 위험들을 거쳐, 우리가 출발했던 바로 그 지점으로 돌아갈 뿐이다. 그곳에는 우리가 안전하게 두고 떠났던 이들이, 줄곧 우리보다 앞서 있었던 것이다.\n\n이 세상이 끝없는 평원이어서 동쪽으로 항해해 가면 영원히 새로운 거리에 가닿고, 그 어떤 키클라데스 제도나 솔로몬 왕의 섬들보다 더 달콤하고 기이한 광경을 발견할 수 있다면, 그 항해에는 약속이 깃들어 있으리라. 그러나 우리가 꿈꾸는 저 머나먼 신비를 좇거나, 언젠가 모든 인간의 마음 앞을 헤엄쳐 지나가는 그 악마 같은 환영을 괴로이 추격하면서, 이 둥근 지구 위에서 그런 것을 뒤쫓을 때면, 그것들은 우리를 메마른 미궁 속으로 이끌거나 한복판에서 우리를 집어삼킨 채 버려두고 만다.\n\n# CHAPTER 53. The Gam.\n\n에이해브가 우리가 인사를 나눈 그 포경선에 오르지 않은 표면적인 이유는 이러했다. 바람과 바다가 폭풍을 예고하고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 설령 사정이 그렇지 않았다 하더라도, 그는 어쩌면 결국 그 배에 오르지 않았을 것이다. 비슷한 경우에 그가 보인 훗날의 행동으로 미루어 판단하건대, 만약 인사를 주고받는 과정에서 자신이 던진 질문에 부정적인 대답을 얻었더라면 말이다. 결국 드러난 바로는, 그는 자신이 그토록 골똘히 찾아 헤매는 정보를 얼마간이라도 보태 줄 수 없는 한, 어떤 낯선 선장과도 단 오 분조차 어울리기를 원치 않았던 것이다. 그러나 머나먼 바다에서, 특히 공동의 어장에서 포경선들이 서로 마주칠 때의 독특한 관습에 관해 여기서 한마디 해 두지 않는다면, 이 모든 것은 충분히 이해되지 못한 채로 남을 것이다.\n\n뉴욕주의 소나무 황무지(Pine Barrens)나, 그에 못지않게 황량한 영국의 솔즈베리 평원을 가로지르던 낯선 두 사람이, 그토록 인적 드문 황야에서 우연히 마주친다면, 이 두 사람은 무슨 일이 있어도 서로 인사를 나누지 않을 수 없을 것이며, 잠시 멈춰 서서 소식을 주고받고, 어쩌면 한동안 함께 앉아 쉬기도 할 것이다. 그렇다면 바다라는 가없는 소나무 황무지와 솔즈베리 평원 위에서, 세상의 끝자락에서 — 외딴 패닝섬 앞바다나 머나먼 킹스밀스 앞바다에서 — 서로를 알아본 두 포경선이라면, 이런 상황에서 단지 인사만 주고받는 것이 아니라 더 가깝고, 더 다정하며, 더 사교적인 접촉을 나누는 것이 얼마나 더 자연스러운 일이겠는가. 더구나 같은 항구에 선적을 둔 배들, 그리고 그 선장들과 사관들, 적지 않은 선원들이 서로 개인적으로 알고 지내며, 따라서 정겨운 고향 이야깃거리를 온갖 종류로 나눌 수 있는 경우라면, 이는 더더욱 당연한 일로 보일 것이다.\n\n오랫동안 집을 떠나 있던 배에게는, 어쩌면 이제 막 출항한 배가 편지를 싣고 있을지도 모른다. 적어도 그 배는, 흐릿하고 손때 묻은 서류철에 끼워 둔 가장 최근 것보다 한두 해는 더 새로운 신문 몇 부를 틀림없이 건네줄 것이다. 그리고 그 친절에 대한 보답으로, 출항하는 배는 자신이 향하게 될 어장에 관한 가장 최신의 포경 소식을 얻게 되니, 이는 그 배에게 더없이 중요한 일이다. 그리고 정도의 차이는 있을지언정, 이 모든 것은 어장 한복판에서 서로의 항로를 가로지르는 포경선들에게도 똑같이 적용되는데, 비록 두 배 모두 집을 떠난 지 오래되었다 해도 그러하다. 그중 한 배가 어떤 제삼의, 이제는 멀리 떨어진 배로부터 편지를 넘겨받았을 수도 있고, 그 편지들 가운데 일부는 지금 마주친 배의 사람들에게 보내는 것일 수도 있기 때문이다. 게다가 그들은 포경 소식을 주고받고, 즐거운 잡담을 나누기도 할 것이다. 그들은 선원들 사이의 온갖 동질감으로 마주할 뿐만 아니라, 공동의 생업과 함께 나눈 결핍과 위험에서 비롯되는 온갖 독특한 친밀감으로도 마주하기 때문이다.\n\n나라가 다르다고 해서 본질적으로 큰 차이가 생기는 것도 아니다. 다시 말해, 미국인과 영국인의 경우가 그렇듯, 양쪽이 같은 언어를 쓰는 한 그러하다. 물론 영국 포경선의 수가 워낙 적은 탓에 이런 만남이 그리 자주 일어나지는 않으며, 일어난다 해도 둘 사이에는 일종의 서먹함이 깃들기 십상이다. 영국인은 다소 내성적이고, 양키는 그런 식의 내성적인 태도를 자기 말고 다른 누구에게서도 달가워하지 않기 때문이다. 게다가 영국 포경선들은 때때로 미국 포경선들 위에 군림하는 일종의 도회적 우월감을 내비치며, 정체 모를 촌티를 풍기는 길쭉하고 비쩍 마른 낸터킷 사람을 일종의 바다 촌뜨기쯤으로 여기곤 한다. 그러나 양키들이 단 하루에 다 함께 잡는 고래가 영국인 전부가 십 년 동안 다 함께 잡는 것보다 많은 마당에, 영국 포경꾼들의 이 우월감이 대체 어디에 근거하는 것인지는 말하기 어렵다. 그러나 이것은 영국 고래 사냥꾼들의 해롭지 않은 사소한 결점일 뿐이어서, 낸터킷 사람은 이를 그리 마음에 담아 두지 않는다. 아마도 자기에게도 사소한 결점 몇 가지쯤은 있다는 것을 알기 때문이리라.\n\n그러니 바다를 저마다 항해하는 온갖 배들 가운데서도, 포경선이야말로 가장 사교적일 이유를 지녔으며 — 실제로도 그러하다는 것을 우리는 알 수 있다. 반면에 대서양 한복판에서 서로의 항적을 가로지르는 어떤 상선들은, 알아본다는 말 한마디 없이 그대로 지나쳐 버리는 일이 흔하며, 마치 브로드웨이의 멋쟁이 한 쌍처럼 망망대해에서 서로를 본체만체하면서, 그동안 어쩌면 서로의 삭구를 두고 까다로운 흠이나 잡고 있을 것이다. 군함으로 말하자면, 바다에서 우연히 마주치면 먼저 그토록 어리석은 절과 굽실거림을 줄줄이 늘어놓고, 군기를 그토록 까딱까딱 내려 인사하니, 거기에는 진심에서 우러난 정이나 형제애 같은 것이 그다지 깃들어 있는 것 같지가 않다. 노예선들이 마주치는 경우로 말하자면, 그들은 어찌나 엄청나게 서두르는지 되도록 빨리 서로에게서 달아나 버린다. 해적들로 말하자면, 우연히 서로의 해골 깃발이 엇갈리면 첫 인사가 — \"해골이 몇 개냐?\" 인데, 이는 포경선들이 — \"기름이 몇 통이냐?\" 하고 인사하는 것과 똑같은 방식이다. 그리고 그 질문에 답이 나오면, 해적들은 곧장 서로 갈라져 뱃길을 돌린다. 양쪽 다 지옥에서 온 악당들이라, 서로의 악당 같은 몰골을 너무 많이 보고 싶어 하지 않기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 저 경건하고, 정직하고, 꾸밈없고, 손님 대접 후하고, 사교적이며, 격의 없는 포경선을 보라! 포경선은 그럭저럭 괜찮은 날씨에 다른 포경선을 만나면 무엇을 하는가? 그들은 \"갬(Gam)\"을 갖는데, 이는 다른 모든 배들에게는 너무도 생소하여 그 이름조차 들어 본 적이 없는 것이며, 혹 우연히 그 말을 듣게 된다 해도 그저 비죽 웃으며 \"물 뿜는 놈들\"이니 \"기름 끓이는 놈들\"이니 하는 그런 짓궂은 농지거리나 되풀이할 뿐이다. 어찌하여 모든 상선 선원들이, 그리고 모든 해적과 군함 선원들, 노예선 뱃사람들까지도 포경선을 향해 그토록 경멸하는 감정을 품는지, 이는 답하기 어려운 물음이다. 이를테면 해적의 경우, 나는 그들의 그 직업에 무슨 특별한 영광이 깃들어 있는지 알고 싶을 따름이다. 과연 그것이 때때로 유별난 드높음으로 끝나기는 하지만, 그것은 오직 교수대 위에서일 뿐이다. 게다가 사람이 그런 묘한 방식으로 높이 들어 올려질 때는, 그 우월한 높이를 떠받칠 변변한 토대가 없는 법이다. 그러므로 나는 이렇게 결론짓는다. 포경꾼보다 한참 높이 들어 올려져 있노라고 으스대는 그 주장에서, 해적은 발 디딜 단단한 근거가 없다고.\n\n그런데 갬이란 무엇인가? 사전의 갈피갈피를 오르내리며 집게손가락이 닳도록 뒤져 본들, 그 단어를 결코 찾아내지 못할 것이다. 존슨 박사조차 그런 박학에 이르지 못했으며, 노아 웹스터의 방주에도 그것은 실려 있지 않다. 그럼에도 바로 이 함축적인 단어는, 이제 여러 해 동안 한 만오천 명쯤 되는 진짜 토박이 양키들 사이에서 끊임없이 쓰여 왔다. 분명 그것은 정의를 내릴 필요가 있으며, 마땅히 사전에 편입되어야 한다. 그런 취지에서, 내가 학자답게 그것을 정의해 보겠다.\n\n갬. 명사 — 두 척(또는 그 이상)의 포경선이, 대개 어장에서 갖는 사교적 모임. 이때 그들은 인사를 주고받은 뒤 보트의 선원들을 통해 서로를 방문하는데, 두 선장은 한동안 한 배에 함께 머물고, 두 일등항해사는 다른 배에 머문다.\n\n여기서 잊지 말아야 할, 갬에 관한 또 하나의 자그마한 사항이 있다. 모든 직업에는 저마다 세부적인 자잘한 특색이 있는 법인데, 포경업 또한 그러하다. 해적선이든 군함이든 노예선이든, 선장이 보트를 타고 어딘가로 노 저어 갈 때면, 그는 늘 고물의 자리에, 더러는 방석까지 깔린 안락한 좌석에 앉아, 종종 화사한 끈과 리본으로 장식한 예쁘장한 모자 가게풍의 작은 키손잡이로 직접 키를 잡는다. 그러나 포경 보트에는 고물에 좌석도 없고, 그런 종류의 소파 따위는 전혀 없으며, 키손잡이도 아예 없다. 포경선 선장들이 통풍에 시달리는 늙은 시의원들처럼 특허받은 의자에 앉아 바퀴를 달고 물 위를 굴러다닌다면, 참으로 호사스러운 시절이 아니겠는가. 그리고 키손잡이로 말하자면, 포경 보트는 그런 나약한 물건을 결코 허용하지 않는다. 그러므로 갬을 할 때는 한 보트의 선원 전부가 배를 떠나야 하고, 따라서 키잡이 혹은 작살잡이도 그 인원에 포함되므로, 그 부하가 이 경우의 키잡이가 되며, 앉을 자리가 없는 선장은 소나무처럼 꼿꼿이 선 채로 노 저어져 방문길에 오른다. 그리고 당신은 종종 보게 될 것이다. 두 배의 뱃전에서 눈에 보이는 온 세상의 눈길이 자기에게 쏠려 있음을 의식한 채, 이 선 채로 가는 선장이 두 다리를 굳건히 버텨 자신의 위엄을 지키는 일의 중대함에 온 신경을 곤두세우고 있는 모습을 말이다. 게다가 이것은 그리 만만한 일이 아니다. 그의 뒤에서는 엄청나게 길게 튀어나온 조타용 노가 이따금 그의 등허리를 후려치고, 앞에서는 맨 뒤쪽 노가 맞장구치듯 그의 무릎을 탁탁 친다. 그리하여 그는 앞뒤로 완전히 끼인 채, 오로지 쩍 벌린 다리를 낮춰 디딤으로써만 옆으로 몸을 펼 수 있을 뿐이다. 그러나 보트가 갑작스럽고 격렬하게 출렁이면 종종 그를 넘어뜨리고도 남는데, 토대의 길이란 그에 걸맞은 너비가 없으면 아무 소용이 없기 때문이다. 그저 막대 두 개를 벌려 각을 만들어 보라, 그것들을 똑바로 세울 수는 없을 것이다. 또한 온 세상의 못 박힌 듯한 눈길이 빤히 지켜보는 앞에서, 이렇게 다리를 벌리고 선 선장이 손으로 무언가를 붙잡고 단 한 치라도 몸을 가누는 모습을 보인다는 것은 결코 있을 수 없는 일이다, 정말이지 결코 있을 수 없는 일이라고 나는 말한다. 실로 자신의 온전하고 거침없는 자제력의 증표로서, 그는 대개 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣고 다닌다. 그러나 어쩌면 그 손이 대체로 무척 크고 묵직한 손이라, 그는 그것을 균형추 삼아 거기에 넣어 두는 것일지도 모른다. 그렇지만, 충분히 입증된 사례들도 있는바, 선장이 유난히 위태로운 한두 순간에 — 이를테면 느닷없는 돌풍 속에서 — 가장 가까이 있는 노잡이의 머리카락을 움켜쥐고는 죽기 살기로 거기에 매달려 있었던 경우도 있었다.\n\n지브롤터 곶, 곧 희망봉(Cape of Good Hope)과 그 주위를 둘러싼 광대한 바다는 거대한 도로의 이름난 네거리와 무척이나 닮아 있어, 그 어떤 곳보다 더 많은 나그네를 만나게 되는 곳이다.\n\n고니 호(Goney)와 말을 주고받은 지 그리 오래지 않아, 우리는 또 한 척의 귀향길에 오른 포경선 타운호(Town-Ho)*와 마주쳤다. 그 배에는 거의 전부가 폴리네시아 사람들로 채워져 있었다. 뒤이어 벌어진 짧은 가다(gam) 동안, 그 배는 우리에게 모비 딕(Moby Dick)에 관한 강렬한 소식을 전해 주었다. 어떤 이들에게는 흰 고래(White Whale)에 대한 보편적인 관심이 이제 타운호의 이야기에 담긴 한 가지 사정으로 인해 격렬하게 고조되었으니, 그것은 그 고래와 더불어, 사람들이 흔히 말하는바 때때로 어떤 사람들에게 들이닥친다는 저 하느님의 심판(judgments of God) 가운데 하나가, 기이하고도 거꾸로 뒤집힌 방문의 형태로 어렴풋이 얽혀 있는 듯 보였기 때문이다. 이 후자의 사정은, 그 나름의 특별한 부수적인 정황들과 더불어, 이제부터 이야기될 비극의 이른바 비밀스러운 부분을 이루는 것으로서, 결코 에이해브(Ahab) 선장이나 그의 항해사들의 귀에는 닿지 못했다. 그 이야기의 비밀스러운 부분은 타운호의 선장 자신에게조차 알려져 있지 않았기 때문이다. 그것은 그 배의 백인 선원 세 사람이 한패가 되어 간직한 사사로운 소유물이었는데, 그 가운데 하나가 가톨릭식 비밀 엄수의 맹세와 함께 그것을 태시테고(Tashtego)에게 털어놓은 듯하나, 그다음 날 밤 태시테고가 잠결에 헛소리를 하며 그 일을 그렇게 많이 드러내고 말아, 막상 깨어났을 때는 나머지를 차마 감출 수가 없었던 것이다. 그럼에도 불구하고, 이 일은 그것을 온전히 알게 된 피쿼드호(Pequod)의 선원들에게 어찌나 강력한 영향을 미쳤던지, 그리고 그들은 이 문제에 있어 — 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 — 어떤 기묘한 섬세함에 사로잡혀 있었던지라, 그 비밀을 자기들끼리만 간직하여 그것이 피쿼드호의 큰돛대 뒤편으로는 결코 새어 나가지 않게 했다. 배 위에서 공공연하게 이야기된 그 사연 속에, 이 한층 어두운 실가닥을 마땅한 자리에 엮어 넣어, 나는 이제 이 기이한 사건의 전모를 영원한 기록으로 남기고자 한다.\n\n* 돛대 꼭대기에서 고래를 처음 발견했을 때 외치던 옛 포경 함성으로, 지금도 포경선원들이 그 유명한 갈라파고스(Gallipagos) 거북을 사냥할 때 쓴다.\n\n내 취향에 맞추어, 나는 언젠가 리마(Lima)에서 이 이야기를 풀어놓던 그 방식을 그대로 보존하려 한다. 그날은 어느 성인의 전야제로, 나는 골든 인(Golden Inn)의 두꺼운 금박 타일이 깔린 회랑에서 담배를 피우며 빈둥거리던 스페인 친구들 무리에게 이 이야기를 들려주었다. 그 멋진 기사들, 곧 젊은 돈(Don)들 가운데 페드로와 세바스티안이 나와 더 가까운 사이였는데, 그래서 그들이 이따금 끼워 넣던 질문들, 그리고 그때그때 거기에 답한 내용을 함께 적어 둔다.\n\n\"여러분, 내가 지금 여러분께 들려드리려는 이 사건들을 처음 알게 되기 한 이 년쯤 전의 일이오. 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 향유고래잡이 배 타운호가 바로 이곳 여러분의 태평양을 항해하고 있었는데, 이 좋은 골든 인의 처마에서 동쪽으로 며칠 안 되는 뱃길의 거리였소. 그 배는 적도(Line) 어딘가 북쪽에 있었소. 어느 날 아침, 날마다 하는 관례대로 펌프를 다루던 중에, 그 배의 선창에 평소보다 물이 더 많이 차 있는 것이 발견되었소. 사람들은 황새치가 배를 들이받았으리라 짐작했소, 여러분. 그러나 선장은 그 위도에서 보기 드문 행운이 자기를 기다리고 있다고 믿을 만한 무슨 남다른 이유가 있었던지라, 그 해역을 떠나는 것을 몹시 꺼렸고, 또한 당시로서는 그 누수가 전혀 위험하다고 여겨지지 않았소. 사실 험한 날씨 속에서 선창을 가능한 한 깊은 데까지 뒤져 보고도 누수의 자리를 찾아내지 못하긴 했지만 말이오. 그리하여 배는 계속 항해를 이어 갔고, 선원들은 넉넉하고 여유로운 간격으로 펌프 앞에서 일했소. 그러나 행운은 오지 않았소. 날이 더 지나도, 누수의 자리는 여전히 찾지 못했을 뿐 아니라 눈에 띄게 더 심해졌소. 어찌나 심했던지, 이제 적잖이 놀란 선장은 돛을 모조리 펴고서 섬들 사이의 가장 가까운 항구를 향해 뱃머리를 돌렸소. 거기서 선체를 기울여 끌어 올려 수리할 작정이었소.\n\n그 배 앞에 결코 만만찮은 항로가 놓여 있긴 했으나, 그래도 가장 평범한 운만 따라 준다면, 선장은 가는 도중에 배가 침몰하리라고는 조금도 걱정하지 않았소. 그의 펌프가 최상품이었던 데다, 거기에서 번갈아 가며 일을 덜어 가면, 그의 서른여섯 선원들이 능히 배 안을 물 없이 지킬 수 있었기 때문이오. 설령 누수가 곱절로 늘어난다 해도 상관없었소. 사실, 이 항로의 거의 전 구간이 더없이 순조로운 바람을 받았던지라, 타운호는 단 한 차례의 사고도 없이 거의 틀림없이 무사히 항구에 닿았을 것이오. 만일 그 일등항해사인 빈야드(Vineyard) 출신의 래드니(Radney)의 잔인하고 거만한 횡포와, 버펄로(Buffalo) 출신의 호반인(Lakeman)이자 무법자인 스틸킬트(Steelkilt)의 쓰라리게 도발된 복수만 없었더라면 말이오.\n\n'호반인! 버펄로라니! 부디 묻겠소만, 호반인이란 무엇이고, 버펄로는 어디에 있소?' 돈 세바스티안이 풀로 엮은 흔들리는 깔개에서 몸을 일으키며 말했다.\n\n우리 이리(Erie) 호수의 동쪽 기슭이라오, 돈. 하지만 — 너그러이 헤아려 주시오 — 그 모든 사정은 곧 더 듣게 되실 게요. 자, 여러분, 옛 카야오(Callao)에서 머나먼 마닐라(Manilla)까지 항해하던 그 어떤 배 못지않게 크고 튼튼한, 가로돛을 단 쌍돛대 범선과 세 돛대 배를 부리는 일에 있어, 이 호반인은 우리 아메리카(America)의 육지에 둘러싸인 한복판에서 자랐으되, 흔히 망망대해와 결부되곤 하는 그 모든 농경적 약탈자의 기질을 고스란히 길러 받았다오. 왜냐하면 함께 흘러들어 하나로 모인 그 광대한 우리의 담수의 바다들 — 이리와 온타리오와 휴런과 슈피리어와 미시간 — 은 대양과도 같은 광활함을 지녔고, 대양의 가장 고귀한 특질들을 숱하게 갖추었으며, 대양처럼 둘레에 갖가지 인종과 풍토를 거느리고 있기 때문이오. 그것들은 폴리네시아(Polynesia)의 바다가 그러하듯 낭만적인 섬들로 이루어진 둥근 군도(群島)를 품고 있고, 대서양(Atlantic)이 그러하듯 그 상당 부분이 서로 극명히 대비되는 두 위대한 나라에 의해 해안을 이루며, 그 기슭 곳곳에 흩어져 있는 동쪽으로부터의 우리의 수많은 식민 영토로 향하는 긴 해상 통로를 제공하오. 여기저기에서 그 바다들은 포대(砲臺)와, 우뚝 솟은 매키노(Mackinaw)의 산양 같은 험준한 대포들의 위협을 받고, 해전의 승리를 알리는 빠른 천둥소리를 들어 왔으며, 이따금 그 해변을 야만의 미개인들에게 내어 주는데, 그들의 붉게 칠한 얼굴이 모피로 지은 천막에서 번뜩이오. 또한 수십 리, 수백 리에 걸쳐 오래되고 사람의 발길이 닿지 않은 숲이 그 옆구리를 지키는데, 거기에는 야윈 소나무들이 고딕식 족보에 늘어선 왕들의 행렬처럼 서 있소. 바로 그 숲들이 아프리카(Afric)의 사나운 맹수와, 그 모피가 수출되어 타타르(Tartar) 황제들의 어의(御衣)가 되는 비단결 같은 짐승들을 품고 있다오. 그 바다들은 위네바고(Winnebago) 부락의 모습뿐 아니라, 버펄로와 클리블랜드(Cleveland)의 포장된 도시들을 거울처럼 비추고, 완전한 삭구를 갖춘 상선과 국가의 무장 순양함과 증기선과 너도밤나무 카누를 한결같이 띄우며, 짠 바닷물을 후려치는 그 어떤 것 못지않게 무시무시하게 돛대를 부러뜨리는 북풍(Borean)의 돌풍에 휩쓸리오. 그 바다들은 난파가 무엇인지 알고 있으니, 아무리 내륙에 있다 한들 뭍이 보이지 않는 그곳에서, 비명을 지르는 선원을 모조리 태운 한밤의 배들을 숱하게 익사시켜 왔기 때문이오. 그러니, 여러분, 비록 내륙 사람이긴 하나, 스틸킬트는 거친 대양에서 태어나 거친 대양에서 길러진 자로서, 그 누구 못지않게 대담한 뱃사람이었소. 그리고 래드니로 말하자면, 비록 갓난아기 때 외로운 낸터킷 해변에 누워 어머니인 바다의 젖을 빨았을지 모르고, 또 훗날의 삶에서 우리의 준엄한 대서양과 여러분의 사색에 잠긴 태평양을 오래도록 따라다녔을지 모르나, 그럼에도 그는 수사슴 뿔 손잡이가 달린 보위 칼(Bowie-knife)의 위도에서 갓 올라온 오지의 뱃사람만큼이나 복수심에 차 있고 시비 걸기를 일삼는 자였소. 그래도 이 낸터킷 사람에게는 몇 가지 마음씨 좋은 구석이 있었고, 또 이 호반인은, 비록 실은 일종의 악마 같은 자이긴 했으나, 그래도 흔들림 없는 단호함으로 다스리되 — 그 단호함을, 가장 천한 노예조차 누릴 권리인 인간으로서의 최소한의 예우라는 평범한 품위로만 누그러뜨리면 — 그렇게만 대해 주면, 이 스틸킬트는 오래도록 해롭지 않고 고분고분하게 부릴 수 있는 자였소. 적어도 여태까지는 그가 그러함을 입증해 왔소. 그러나 래드니는 파멸할 운명이었고 미쳐 버렸으며, 스틸킬트는 — 하지만, 여러분, 직접 들어 보시오.\n\n타운호가 섬의 피난처를 향해 뱃머리를 돌린 뒤 길어야 하루나 이틀쯤 지났을 무렵, 그 배의 누수가 다시 심해지는 듯했으나, 그래봐야 날마다 한 시간 남짓 펌프질을 해야 하는 정도였소. 여러분은 알아 두셔야 하오. 이를테면 우리 대서양처럼 자리 잡히고 문명화된 바다에서는, 어떤 선장들은 항해 내내 펌프질을 해 가며 건너는 것을 대수롭지 않게 여긴다오. 다만 고요하고 졸음 오는 밤에, 갑판 당직 사관이 어쩌다 그 일을 깜빡 잊기라도 한다면, 십중팔구 그와 그의 동료들은 다시는 그것을 기억해 내지 못하게 될 게요. 모든 사람이 슬그머니 바다 밑바닥으로 가라앉아 버릴 테니 말이오. 또한 여기서 서쪽으로 멀리 떨어진 외롭고 사나운 바다에서도, 여러분, 배들이 상당히 긴 항해 동안에도 펌프 손잡이를 다 함께 한목소리로 절그럭절그럭 울려 대는 일이 아주 드문 것은 아니라오. 그러니까, 어지간히 접근할 만한 해안을 끼고 있거나, 달리 의지할 만한 합당한 피난처가 주어진다면 말이오. 누수가 있는 배가 그 바다의 외진 곳, 정말로 뭍이라곤 없는 위도에 놓였을 때라야 비로소, 그 선장은 조금 불안을 느끼기 시작하는 것이오.\n\n타운호의 사정도 대체로 이러했소. 그래서 누수가 다시 심해지고 있음이 드러나자, 사실 그 배의 몇몇 선원들은 적잖이 걱정하는 기색을 내비쳤는데, 특히 일등항해사 래드니가 그러했소. 그는 윗돛들을 단단히 끌어 올리고, 돛자락을 새로 팽팽히 당겨, 온갖 방법으로 바람을 받도록 활짝 펴라고 명령했소. 그런데 이 래드니라는 자는, 내 짐작으로는, 여러분이 손쉽게 떠올릴 수 있는 뭍이든 바다든 그 어떤 겁 없고 생각 없는 피조물 못지않게 겁이라곤 거의 없고, 또 자기 한 몸의 안위에 대해 어떤 신경질적인 불안 따위도 거의 느끼지 않는 자였소, 여러분. 그러므로 그가 배의 안전을 두고 이런 근심을 드러내자, 선원들 가운데 더러는 그것이 다만 그가 그 배의 지분을 가진 공동 선주이기 때문이라고들 했소. 그래서 그날 저녁 펌프 앞에서 일하던 그들 사이에는, 이 문제를 두고 적잖은 짓궂은 농지거리가 슬그머니 오갔는데, 그동안 그들의 발은 잔물결 이는 맑은 물에 끊임없이 잠겨 있었소. 산골 샘물처럼 맑은 물이었다오, 여러분 — 펌프에서 보글거리며 솟아난 그 물이 갑판을 가로질러 흘러, 바람 아래쪽 배수구를 통해 꾸준한 물줄기로 쏟아져 나갔소.\n\n자, 여러분도 잘 아시다시피, 우리의 이 관습적인 세상에서는 — 물 위든 어디든 — 동료들 위에 명령권자로 앉은 어떤 사람이, 그들 가운데 하나가 사내다운 자존심에 있어 자기보다 현저히 뛰어남을 알아챘을 때, 그 즉시 그 사람을 향해 도무지 누를 수 없는 혐오와 쓰라림을 품게 되는 일이 드물지 않다오. 그리고 기회만 닿으면 그는 그 아랫사람의 탑을 허물어 가루로 만들고, 한 줌 먼지 더미로 빻아 버리려 하지요. 이런 내 생각이야 어찌 됐든, 여러분, 어쨌든 스틸킬트는 키가 크고 고귀한 짐승 같은 사내로, 머리는 로마인 같았고, 흘러내리는 황금빛 수염은 여러분네 최근 부왕(副王)의 콧김 뿜는 군마에 두른 술 달린 마의(馬衣) 같았으며, 그의 안에는 두뇌와 심장과 영혼이 깃들어 있었으니, 만일 그가 샤를마뉴의 아버지의 아들로 태어났더라면 스틸킬트를 능히 샤를마뉴로 만들었을 그런 것이었소. 그러나 일등항해사 래드니는 노새처럼 못생긴 자였소. 그러면서도 노새처럼 단단하고, 고집스럽고, 악의에 차 있었소. 그는 스틸킬트를 좋아하지 않았고, 스틸킬트도 그것을 알고 있었소.\n\n다른 이들과 함께 펌프 앞에서 고되게 일하던 호반인은, 일등항해사가 가까이 다가오는 것을 곁눈으로 보고도 못 본 척하며, 조금도 주눅 들지 않고 흥겨운 농담을 이어 갔소.\n\n'그래, 그래, 유쾌한 친구들아, 이거 참 활기찬 누수로구나. 누가 깡통 하나 받쳐 봐, 맛 좀 보자고. 맙소사, 이건 병에 담아 둘 만한 물이야! 이봐, 친구들, 내 말 좀 들어 보게. 늙은 래드 영감의 투자금이 이걸로 날아가게 생겼어! 영감은 차라리 제 몫의 선체를 잘라 내서 집으로 끌고 가는 게 낫겠다. 사실은 말이야, 친구들, 그 황새치 놈은 일을 시작했을 뿐이라네. 이제 그놈이 배 목수 패거리를 데리고 다시 돌아왔거든. 톱상어에 줄상어에 별별 놈들을 다 데리고 말이지. 그 패거리 전부가 지금 배 밑바닥을 깎고 베면서 한창 일하는 중이라네. 아마 개량 공사를 하는 모양이지. 만일 늙은 래드 영감이 지금 여기 있다면, 나는 바다로 뛰어들어 그놈들을 흩어 버리라고 일러 줄 텐데. 그놈들이 영감 재산을 가지고 아주 야단법석을 떨고 있다고 말해 줄 테야. 하지만 영감은 단순한 늙은이지 — 래드 말이야, 게다가 미남이기까지 하고. 친구들아, 영감 재산 나머지는 죄다 거울에 처박혀 있다더군. 나 같은 가련한 놈한테 그 코의 본을 떠 주지 않으려나 모르겠네.'\n\n'이 망할 눈깔들! 그 펌프가 왜 멈췄어?' 래드니가 선원들의 말을 못 들은 척하며 고함쳤소. '천둥 치듯 두들겨 대란 말이야!'\n\n'예, 예, 나리.' 귀뚜라미처럼 흥겹게 스틸킬트가 말했소. '활기차게, 친구들, 자, 활기차게!' 그러자 펌프가 쉰 대의 소방 펌프처럼 절그럭거렸고, 사람들은 그 위로 모자를 벗어 던졌으며, 이윽고 폐가 헐떡이는 그 독특한 소리가 들려왔으니, 그것은 삶의 가장 극한의 기력을 한껏 짜내고 있음을 알리는 소리였소.\n\n마침내 자기 패거리의 나머지와 함께 펌프를 떠난 호반인은 헐떡이며 앞쪽으로 나아가, 양묘기(揚錨機) 위에 주저앉았소. 얼굴은 불처럼 붉고, 두 눈은 핏발이 섰으며, 이마에서 줄줄 흐르는 땀을 닦고 있었소. 자, 여러분, 도대체 어떤 사람을 속이는 마귀가 래드니에게 들러붙어, 그토록 육체적으로 지쳐 격앙된 상태에 있는 사내를 건드리게 만들었는지 나는 모르오. 하지만 일은 그렇게 벌어졌소. 견딜 수 없는 거드름을 피우며 갑판을 성큼성큼 가로지른 일등항해사는, 빗자루를 가져와 널빤지를 쓸어 내라고, 또 삽도 가져와서, 돼지를 마음대로 돌아다니게 둔 탓에 생긴 더러운 것들을 치우라고 그에게 명령했소.\n\n자, 여러분, 바다에서 배의 갑판을 쓰는 일은, 사나운 폭풍이 몰아치는 때만 빼고는 언제든 저녁마다 으레 행해지는 일종의 집안일이오. 실제로 침몰하는 와중에 있는 배에서도 그 일이 행해진 적이 있다고 알려져 있소. 여러분, 바다의 관습이란 그토록 굽힐 줄 모르는 것이고, 또 선원들에게는 그토록 본능적인 청결에 대한 사랑이 있는 것이오. 그들 가운데 어떤 이들은 먼저 얼굴을 씻지 않고서는 기꺼이 익사하려 들지도 않을 게요. 그러나 어느 배에서든 이 빗자루 일은, 만일 배에 소년들이 있다면, 으레 소년들에게 정해진 몫이오. 게다가 타운호에서는 더 힘센 사내들이 패거리로 나뉘어 번갈아 가며 펌프질을 맡고 있었고, 스틸킬트는 그들 가운데 가장 건장한 뱃사람이었던지라 으레 한 패의 우두머리로 정해져 있었소. 따라서 그는, 그의 동료들이 그러했듯, 참된 선원의 임무와 무관한 어떤 하찮은 일에서도 마땅히 면제되어야 했소. 내가 이 모든 세세한 사정을 말하는 것은, 이 일이 그 두 사람 사이에서 정확히 어떤 형국이었는지를 여러분이 똑똑히 이해하시도록 하기 위함이오.\n\n그러나 이게 다가 아니었소. 삽에 관한 그 명령은, 마치 래드니가 그의 얼굴에 침을 뱉기라도 한 것처럼, 거의 노골적으로 스틸킬트를 찌르고 모욕하려는 뜻이었소. 포경선에서 뱃사람 노릇을 해 본 사람이라면 누구나 이 점을 이해할 게요. 그리고 일등항해사가 그 명령을 내뱉었을 때, 호반인은 이 모든 것을, 아니 의심할 바 없이 그 이상을 온전히 알아챘소. 그러나 그가 잠시 가만히 앉아, 일등항해사의 악의에 찬 눈을 뚫어지게 들여다보며, 그 안에 쌓여 있는 화약통 더미와, 그것을 향해 소리 없이 타들어 가는 도화선을 알아보았을 때 — 본능적으로 이 모든 것을 알아챘을 때 — 이미 노기에 찬 어떤 존재의 더 깊은 격정을 휘저어 놓기를 꺼리는 저 기묘한 자제심과 망설임이, 곧 참으로 용감한 자들이 부당한 일을 당했을 때조차 느끼곤 하는, 혹여 느낀다면 가장 강하게 느끼는 그 거부감이, 이 이름 없는 유령 같은 감정이, 여러분, 스틸킬트를 슬며시 사로잡았소.\n\n그리하여, 잠시 빠져 있던 육체적 탈진으로 조금 끊기긴 했으나 평소의 어조로, 그는 갑판을 쓰는 것은 자기 일이 아니며 그 일을 하지 않겠다고 그에게 답했소. 그러고는 삽에 대해서는 전혀 언급하지 않은 채, 으레 갑판을 쓰는 일을 맡은 세 소년을 가리켰는데, 그들은 펌프에 배치되지 않았던지라 온종일 일을 거의, 아니 전혀 하지 않은 터였소. 이에 래드니는 욕설을 내뱉으며, 더없이 위압적이고 무도한 태도로 자기 명령을 조건 없이 거듭 되풀이했소. 그러는 동안 그는 근처의 통에서 낚아챈 통메장이의 큰 망치를 치켜든 채, 여전히 앉아 있는 호반인을 향해 다가섰소.\n\n펌프질의 발작적인 노동으로 몸이 달아오르고 약이 오를 대로 오른 데다, 그 처음의 이름 없는 자제심에도 불구하고, 땀에 젖은 스틸킬트는 일등항해사의 이 태도를 좀처럼 견딜 수가 없었소. 그러나 그래도 어떻게든 안에서 타오르는 불길을 억누른 채, 그는 말없이 제 자리에 끈질기게 뿌리내린 듯 앉아 있었소. 그러다 마침내 격분한 래드니가 그의 얼굴에서 몇 인치 안 되는 데까지 망치를 휘두르며, 시키는 대로 하라고 사납게 명령하기에 이르렀소.\n\n스틸킬트는 일어나, 위협하는 망치를 든 일등항해사가 끈질기게 뒤따르는 가운데, 천천히 양묘기를 돌아 물러나면서, 복종하지 않겠다는 제 뜻을 차분히 거듭 밝혔소. 그러나 자신의 자제심이 손톱만 한 효과도 없음을 알아채고는, 비틀린 손으로 무섭고 형언할 수 없는 신호를 보내, 어리석고 분별을 잃은 그 사내에게 물러서라고 경고했소. 하지만 그것도 헛수고였소. 그렇게 두 사람은 양묘기를 천천히 한 바퀴 돌았소. 마침내 더는 물러서지 않기로 작정하고, 자기 성미에 어울리는 만큼은 이제 충분히 참았다고 생각한 호반인은, 승강구 뚜껑 위에 멈춰 서서 그 사관에게 이렇게 말했소.\n\n'래드니 씨, 나는 당신 명령을 따르지 않겠소. 그 망치를 치우든지, 아니면 당신 몸이나 조심하시오.' 그러나 운명이 미리 정해 둔 그 일등항해사는 호반인이 우뚝 멈춰 선 자리로 한층 더 바싹 다가서더니, 그 묵직한 망치를 그의 이빨에서 한 치 떨어진 데까지 휘둘러 댔소. 그러면서 차마 견딜 수 없는 저주를 줄줄이 퍼부었소. 스틸킬트는 천분의 일 인치도 물러서지 않고, 흔들림 없는 단도 같은 눈빛으로 상대의 눈을 찌르며, 오른손을 등 뒤로 움켜쥐고 슬그머니 끌어당기면서, 그 망치가 제 뺨을 스치기만 해도 자기가(스틸킬트가) 그를 죽여 버리겠노라고 그 박해자에게 일렀소. 그러나, 여러분, 그 바보는 이미 신들에게 도살의 낙인이 찍힌 자였소. 그 즉시 망치가 뺨에 닿았고, 다음 순간 일등항해사의 아래턱이 머릿속으로 으스러져 박혔소. 그는 고래처럼 피를 뿜으며 승강구 뚜껑 위로 쓰러졌소.\n\n그 외침이 고물 쪽으로 미처 닿기도 전에, 스틸킬트는 저 높은 곳으로 이어진 뒷버팀 밧줄(backstay) 하나를 흔들고 있었으니, 거기 돛대 꼭대기에는 그의 동료 둘이 망보고 서 있었소. 두 사람 다 운하 사람(Canaller)이었소.\n\n'운하 사람들이라니!' 돈 페드로가 외쳤다. '우리 항구에서 포경선이라면 숱하게 보았소만, 운하 사람이란 말은 들어 본 적이 없소. 미안하지만, 그들은 누구이고 무엇이오?'\n\n'운하 사람이란, 돈, 우리의 그 위대한 이리 운하(Erie Canal)에 속한 뱃사람들이라오. 그 운하 이야기는 분명 들어 보셨을 게요.'\n\n'아니오, 세뇨르. 이 무덥고 나른하며 더없이 게으른, 대대로 물려받은 이 땅에서, 우리는 여러분네 활기찬 북녘에 대해 아는 바가 거의 없다오.'\n\n'그렇소? 그럼, 돈, 내 잔이나 다시 채워 주시오. 여러분네 치차(chicha)는 참으로 훌륭하구려. 더 나아가기 전에, 우리 운하 사람들이 어떤 자들인지 일러 드리리다. 그런 정보가 내 이야기에 곁들이는 빛을 던져 줄 수 있을 테니 말이오.'\n\n여러분, 삼백육십 마일에 걸쳐, 뉴욕(New York) 주 전체를 가로질러, 인구로 북적이는 숱한 도시들과 더없이 번창하는 마을들을 지나, 길고 음산한 사람 없는 늪지와, 비옥하기로는 견줄 데 없는 풍요로운 경작지를 지나, 당구장과 술집 곁을 지나, 거대한 숲의 지성소(至聖所)를 지나, 인디언(Indian)의 강 위에 걸린 로마식 아치를 건너, 햇살과 그늘 사이를 지나, 행복한 마음과 부서진 마음 곁을 지나, 저 고귀한 모호크(Mohawk) 군(郡)들의 그 넓디넓고 대조적인 온갖 풍경을 가로질러, 그리고 특히, 그 첨탑이 거의 이정표처럼 늘어선 눈처럼 새하얀 예배당들의 줄을 따라, 베네치아처럼 타락하고 흔히 무법한 삶의 끊이지 않는 물줄기 하나가 흐르고 있다오. 바로 거기 여러분네 진짜 아샨티(Ashantee)가 있소, 여러분. 거기서 여러분네 이교도들이 울부짖고 있소. 여러분이 그들을 발견하는 곳이라면 어디든, 그들은 바로 여러분의 옆집에 있다오. 길게 드리워진 그늘 아래, 교회의 아늑하고 너그러운 바람그늘 속에 말이오. 왜냐하면, 여러분네 대도시의 약탈자들이 늘 재판소 주위에 진을 친다고 흔히들 말하듯, 어떤 기묘한 운명의 장난으로, 죄인들은, 여러분, 가장 거룩한 곳 가까이에 가장 많이 들끓는 법이니 말이오.\n\n'저기 지나가는 게 수사(修士)요?' 돈 페드로가 익살스러운 걱정을 담아, 북적이는 광장을 내려다보며 말했다.\n\n'우리 북녘 친구로서는 다행이지요, 이사벨라 여왕(Dame Isabella)의 종교재판이 리마에서는 시들해졌으니 말이오.' 돈 세바스티안이 웃으며 말했다. '계속하시오, 세뇨르.'\n\n'잠깐만! 미안하오!' 일행 가운데 또 한 사람이 외쳤다. '우리 리마 사람들 모두의 이름으로, 나는 다만 뱃사람 양반께 한마디 하고 싶소. 당신이 그 타락의 비유에서, 가까운 리마를 먼 베네치아로 바꿔 넣는 섬세한 배려를 보여 주신 것을 우리가 결코 놓치지 않았다는 것을 말이오. 오! 고개를 숙이며 놀란 척하지 마시오. 이 해안 일대에 두루 퍼진 그 속담을 당신도 알고 있잖소 — \"리마처럼 타락했다.\" 그 속담이 당신의 그 말마따나 들어맞소. 교회가 당구대보다 더 흔하고, 또 언제나 문이 열려 있으니 — \"리마처럼 타락했다.\" 베네치아도 마찬가지요. 나도 거기 가 본 적이 있소. 복된 복음사가, 성 마르코(St. Mark)의 거룩한 도시 말이오! — 성 도미니쿠스(St. Dominic)여, 그곳을 정화하소서! 당신 잔을 이리 주시오! 고맙소. 자, 채우겠소. 이제 당신이 다시 따라 주시오.'\n\n여러분, 자신의 생업 속에 거리낌 없이 그려 놓고 보면, 운하 사람은 멋들어진 극적 영웅이 될 만하니, 그토록 풍성하고도 그림 같은 악당이기 때문이오. 마르쿠스 안토니우스(Mark Antony)처럼, 그는 푸른 잔디와 꽃이 우거진 자기 나일강을 따라 며칠이고 며칠이고 나른하게 떠다니며, 볼이 발그레한 자기 클레오파트라(Cleopatra)와 드러내 놓고 희롱하고, 햇살 비치는 갑판 위에서 살구 같은 허벅지를 무르익히고 있소. 그러나 뭍에 오르면, 이 모든 나약함은 흔적도 없이 사라지오. 운하 사람이 그토록 자랑스레 뽐내며 두르는 그 도적 같은 차림새, 그 비뚜름하게 눌러쓴, 화려한 리본이 달린 모자가 그의 위풍당당한 면모를 드러내오. 그가 떠다니며 지나는 마을들의 그 미소 짓는 순박함에는 공포의 대상이며, 그 거무튀튀한 얼굴과 거침없는 활갯짓은 도시에서도 사람들이 피하지 않을 수 없는 것이라오. 한때 나 자신도 그 운하 위에서 떠돌이 신세였는데, 이런 운하 사람 가운데 하나에게서 좋은 신세를 진 적이 있소. 나는 그에게 진심으로 고마워하며, 은혜를 모르는 자가 되고 싶지 않소. 다만, 여러분네 폭력배의 으뜸가는 미덕 가운데 하나가 흔히 이것이라오. 그가 때때로 부유한 자를 약탈하는 데 못지않게, 곤경에 빠진 가련한 낯선 이를 떠받쳐 주는 데도 그토록 굳센 팔뚝을 가졌다는 것 말이오. 요컨대, 여러분, 이 운하 생활의 거칢이 어떠한지는 바로 이것으로 분명히 드러나오. 우리의 거친 포경업이 그 운하가 길러 낸 가장 노련한 졸업생들을 그토록 많이 품고 있다는 것, 그리고 시드니(Sydney) 사람들을 빼고는 인류의 그 어떤 족속도 우리 포경선 선장들에게 그토록 불신받는 자들은 없다는 것이 그 증거요. 또한, 그 운하 일대에서 태어난 우리네 수천수만의 시골 소년과 젊은이들에게, 그 거대한 운하의 수습 생활이 그리스도교의 보리밭에서 조용히 곡식을 거두던 삶과, 더없이 야만적인 바다의 물결을 무모하게 헤쳐 가는 삶 사이의 유일한 이행기를 마련해 준다는 사실 또한, 이 문제의 기묘함을 조금도 덜어 주지 않는다오.\n\n'알겠소! 알겠소!' 돈 페드로가 자기 치차를 은빛 주름 장식에 쏟아 가며 격하게 외쳤다. '여행할 필요가 없겠구려! 세상이 온통 하나의 리마니 말이오. 나는 지금껏, 여러분네 온화한 북녘에서는 사람들이 저 언덕들처럼 차갑고 거룩하리라 여겼었소. 하지만 — 그 이야기나 계속하시오.'\n\n여러분, 호반인이 뒷버팀 밧줄을 흔드는 데서 이야기를 멈췄었소. 그가 그렇게 하기가 무섭게, 세 명의 하급 항해사와 네 명의 작살잡이가 그를 에워싸고, 모두가 그를 갑판 위로 밀어붙였소. 그러나 두 운하 사람이 불길한 혜성처럼 밧줄을 타고 미끄러져 내려와 그 소란 속으로 뛰어들더니, 자기네 사내를 거기서 끌어내어 앞갑판 쪽으로 데려가려 했소. 다른 선원들도 이 시도에 가세하자, 뒤엉킨 혼전이 벌어졌소. 그동안 그 용맹한 선장은 위험을 피해 비켜선 채, 고래 작살창을 들고 오르락내리락 날뛰며, 자기 사관들에게 저 흉악한 악당을 거칠게 다루어 뒷갑판으로 몰아오라고 외쳐 댔소. 이따금 그는 빙빙 도는 혼란의 가장자리까지 바싹 다가가, 작살창으로 그 한복판을 쑤시며, 자기 원한의 대상을 찔러 끄집어내려 했소. 그러나 스틸킬트와 그의 무법자들은 그들 전부에게 벅찬 상대였소. 그들은 앞갑판에 오르는 데 성공했고, 거기서 큼직한 통 서너 개를 양묘기와 일렬로 황급히 돌려놓아, 이 바다의 파리지앵들은 그 바리케이드 뒤에 진을 쳤소.\n\n'거기서 나와라, 이 해적 놈들아!' 선장이 이제 양손에 권총을 든 채 그들을 위협하며 고함쳤소. 권총은 방금 사환이 그에게 가져다준 것이었소. '거기서 나오란 말이다, 이 살인자 놈들아!'\n\n스틸킬트는 바리케이드 위로 뛰어올라, 거기서 오르락내리락 성큼성큼 거닐며, 그 권총들이 할 수 있는 최악의 짓을 비웃었소. 그러나 자신(스틸킬트)의 죽음이 곧 전 선원의 살인적 반란을 알리는 신호가 되리라는 것을 선장에게 똑똑히 깨우쳐 주었소. 이 말이 너무도 사실로 드러날까 마음속으로 두려워한 선장은 조금 물러섰으나, 그래도 반란자들에게 당장 제 임무로 돌아가라고 명령했소.\n\n'우리가 돌아가면, 우리한테 손대지 않겠다고 약속하겠소?' 그들의 우두머리가 따져 물었소.\n\n'일이나 해! 일이나 하란 말이다! 약속은 못 한다. 네 임무로 돌아가! 이런 판국에 일손을 놓아 배를 침몰시킬 작정이냐? 일이나 해!' 그러면서 그는 다시 한번 권총을 치켜들었소.\n\n'배를 침몰시키겠다고?' 스틸킬트가 외쳤소. '그래, 가라앉히라지. 우리 가운데 단 한 놈도 돌아가지 않을 거다. 당신이 우리한테 밧줄 한 가닥 들이대지 않겠다고 맹세하지 않는 한은 말이야. 어떤가, 친구들?' 그가 동료들을 돌아보며 말했소. 사나운 환호가 그들의 대답이었소.\n\n호반인은 이제 바리케이드 위를 순찰하듯 거닐며, 줄곧 선장에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 이런 말들을 툭툭 내뱉었소. '우리 잘못이 아니다. 우리가 원한 일도 아니야. 나는 그자한테 망치를 치우라고 일렀다. 그건 소년들이나 할 일이었어. 그자는 진작 나를 알아봤어야 했지. 나는 그자한테 들소를 찌르지 말라고 했단 말이다. 여기 그자의 그 빌어먹을 턱에 부딪쳐 손가락 하나가 부러진 것 같군. 저 앞갑판에 다지는 칼들이 있지 않나, 친구들? 저 지렛대들을 챙겨 둬, 내 용감한 친구들아. 선장, 맹세코, 당신 몸이나 조심하시지. 한마디만 하시오. 바보짓 좀 하지 말고. 다 잊어버리란 말이야. 우리는 일하러 돌아갈 준비가 돼 있어. 우리를 점잖게 대해 주면, 우리는 당신 사람들이야. 하지만 채찍질만은 당하지 않겠다.'\n\n'일이나 해! 나는 약속 따위 안 한다, 일이나 하란 말이다!'\n\n'이봐, 잘 들으시오.' 호반인이 그를 향해 팔을 쭉 뻗으며 외쳤소. '여기 우리 가운데 몇 사람은(나도 그중 하나요) 이번 항해만 하기로 계약하고 탔단 말이오, 알겠소? 그리고 당신도 잘 알다시피, 나리, 우리는 닻이 내려지는 즉시 우리 해고를 요구할 수 있소. 그러니 우리는 소동을 원치 않소. 그건 우리한테도 이로울 게 없으니까. 우리는 평화롭게 지내고 싶소. 우리는 일할 준비가 돼 있소. 하지만 채찍질만은 당하지 않겠다.'\n\n'일이나 해!' 선장이 으르렁거렸소.\n\n스틸킬트는 잠시 주위를 둘러보더니, 이윽고 말했소. '이제 사정이 이렇소, 선장. 당신을 죽이고서 그런 보잘것없는 악당 노릇으로 교수형을 당하느니, 우리는 당신이 우리를 공격하지 않는 한 당신한테 손 하나 까딱하지 않겠소. 하지만 당신이 우리를 채찍질하지 않겠다고 그 한마디를 할 때까지는, 우리는 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않겠소.'\n\n'그럼 앞갑판 밑으로 내려가, 내려가란 말이다. 너희가 진절머리 날 때까지 거기 처박아 두겠다. 내려가.'\n\n'그럴까?' 우두머리가 자기 사내들에게 외쳤소. 그들 대부분은 그것에 반대했소. 그러나 마침내 스틸킬트의 말에 따라, 그들은 그를 앞세워 자기네의 어두운 굴속으로 으르렁거리며, 동굴로 들어가는 곰들처럼 사라졌소.\n\n호반인의 맨머리가 막 갑판 널빤지와 같은 높이에 이르렀을 때, 선장과 그의 패거리가 바리케이드를 뛰어넘어, 재빨리 승강구의 미닫이 뚜껑을 끌어 닫고, 그 위에 여럿이 손을 짚어 누른 채, 사환에게 큰 소리로 외쳐 그 승강구에 딸린 묵직한 놋쇠 자물쇠를 가져오게 했소. 그러고는 미닫이를 조금 열어, 선장이 그 틈으로 무언가를 속삭여 내려보내더니, 다시 닫고 그들에게 자물쇠를 채웠소 — 열 사람이었소. 그리고 갑판에는 여태껏 중립을 지킨 스무 명 남짓을 남겨 두었소.\n\n밤새도록 모든 사관이 배의 앞뒤에서 한순간도 졸지 않고 망을 보았는데, 특히 앞갑판 승강구와 앞쪽 승강구를 살폈소. 반란자들이 아래쪽 격벽을 뚫고서 이 마지막 곳으로 튀어나올까 두려웠던 것이오. 그러나 어둠의 시간은 평온하게 지나갔소. 여전히 제 임무에 남아 있던 자들은 펌프 앞에서 고되게 일했고, 그 절그럭절그럭하는 소리가 음산한 밤 내내 이따금 배 안에 음울하게 울려 퍼졌소.\n\n해 뜰 녘에 선장은 앞쪽으로 가서, 갑판을 두드려 죄수들에게 일하러 나오라고 명했소. 그러나 그들은 고함을 지르며 거부했소. 그러자 물을 그들에게 내려보내고, 그 뒤로 비스킷 두어 줌을 던져 넣었소. 그러고 나서 다시 그들에게 자물쇠를 채워 열쇠를 주머니에 넣은 채, 선장은 뒷갑판으로 돌아갔소. 사흘 동안 하루 두 차례씩 이 일이 되풀이되었소. 그러나 나흘째 아침, 으레 하던 부름을 전하자, 어수선하게 다투는 소리가, 이어서 몸싸움하는 소리가 들리더니, 별안간 네 사람이 앞갑판에서 튀어 올라와, 일하러 돌아갈 준비가 되었다고 말했소. 견딜 수 없이 답답한 공기와 굶주리게 하는 식사가, 어쩌면 끝내 닥칠 응보에 대한 어떤 두려움과 한데 어우러져, 그들로 하여금 무조건 항복하도록 몰아붙였던 것이오. 이에 기세가 오른 선장은 나머지에게 자기 요구를 거듭했으나, 스틸킬트가 그에게 무시무시한 한마디를 외쳐 올려, 그 잔소리를 그만두고 제가 있을 곳으로 꺼지라고 했소. 닷새째 아침에는 반란자 가운데 또 다른 셋이, 자기들을 붙잡으려는 아래쪽의 필사적인 팔들을 뿌리치고 위로 튀어 올라왔소. 이제 셋만 남았소.\n\n'이제는 일하러 돌아가는 게 낫지 않나?' 선장이 매정하게 비웃으며 말했소.\n\n'우리를 다시 처박아 둬, 그럴 테냐!' 스틸킬트가 외쳤소.\n\n'오, 그러고말고.' 선장이 말했고, 자물쇠가 철컥 잠겼소.\n\n여러분, 바로 이 대목에서, 일곱이나 되는 옛 동료의 변절에 격분하고, 마지막으로 자기를 부르던 그 조롱하는 목소리에 찔리고, 절망의 창자처럼 새까만 곳에 오래 갇혀 있던 탓에 미쳐 버린 스틸킬트는, 여태까지는 겉보기에 자기와 한마음인 듯하던 두 운하 사람에게, 그 수비대가 다음번에 부를 때 자기네 굴에서 뛰쳐나가자고 제안했소. 그리고 그들의 예리한 다지는 칼(길고, 초승달 모양에, 양쪽 끝에 손잡이가 달린 묵직한 연장이오)로 무장하고서 이물의 활대(bowsprit)에서 고물 난간(taffrail)까지 닥치는 대로 날뛰며, 만일 어떤 필사적인 악착스러움으로 가능하기만 하다면 배를 빼앗자고 했소. 자기로 말하자면, 그들이 함께하든 아니든 이 일을 하겠다고 그는 말했소. 그날 밤이 그 굴속에서 보내는 마지막 밤이 될 것이라고 했소. 그러나 그 계획에 나머지 두 사람도 반대하지 않았소. 그들은 그 일이든, 다른 어떤 미친 일이든, 요컨대 항복만 빼고는 무엇이든 할 준비가 되어 있다고 맹세했소. 게다가, 그들은 저마다 돌격할 때가 오면 자기가 갑판에 첫 번째로 올라가는 사람이 되겠다고 우겼소. 그러나 이에 대해서는 그들의 우두머리가 그 우선권을 자기 몫으로 남겨 두며 못지않게 사납게 반대했소. 특히 그의 두 동료가 서로 그 일에서 양보하려 들지 않았기 때문이오. 그리고 사다리는 한 번에 한 사람만 받아들이는지라, 둘 다 첫 번째가 될 수는 없었소. 그리고 여기서, 여러분, 이 악당들의 비열한 배반이 드러나게 되오.\n\n자기네 우두머리의 미친 계획을 듣고서, 그들 각자는 저마다 따로따로 제 영혼 속에서, 똑같은 한 가지 배신을 별안간 떠올린 듯했으니, 곧 이것이오. 뛰쳐나가는 데 맨 앞장을 서서, 셋 가운데 첫 번째로, 비록 열 사람 가운데서는 마지막이긴 하나, 항복하는 사람이 되고, 그리하여 그런 처신이 받을 수도 있는 자그마한 사면의 기회나마 확보하자는 것이었소. 그러나 스틸킬트가 끝까지 자기가 그들을 이끌겠다는 결심을 밝히자, 그들은 어떤 식으로든, 악당 짓의 어떤 교묘한 화학 작용으로, 여태 서로에게 감춰 두었던 배신을 한데 뒤섞었소. 그리고 그들의 우두머리가 깜빡 잠이 들자, 단 세 마디로 서로에게 제 영혼을 털어놓고, 잠든 그를 밧줄로 묶고, 밧줄로 입에 재갈을 물리고는, 한밤중에 선장을 향해 비명을 질러 댔소.\n\n살인이 임박했다고 여기고, 어둠 속에서 피 냄새를 맡으며, 선장은 무장한 항해사들과 작살잡이들을 모두 거느리고 앞갑판으로 달려갔소. 몇 분 만에 승강구가 열렸고, 손발이 묶인 채 여전히 몸부림치는 우두머리가, 신의를 저버린 자기 동료들의 손에 떠밀려 위로 올라왔소. 그들은 곧바로, 살인을 저지를 만큼 충분히 무르익었던 사내 하나를 붙잡은 공로를 내세웠소. 그러나 이들 모두 멱살을 잡혀, 죽은 가축처럼 갑판을 따라 끌려갔소. 그리고 고기 네 덩이의 사분의 삼처럼, 나란히 뒤쪽 돛대의 삭구에 끌어 올려져, 거기서 아침까지 매달려 있었소. '이런 망할 놈들.' 선장이 그들 앞에서 오락가락 거닐며 외쳤소. '독수리도 너희한텐 손대지 않을 게다, 이 악당 놈들아!'\n\n해 뜰 녘에 그는 전 선원을 불러 모았소. 그리고 반란을 일으킨 자들을 그 반란에 가담하지 않은 자들로부터 갈라놓고서, 전자에게 이르기를, 자기는 너희를 모조리 돌아가며 채찍질하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같다고, 곰곰이 생각해 보니 그렇게 하겠다고, 마땅히 그래야 한다고, 정의가 그것을 요구한다고 했소. 그러나 지금으로서는 그들이 때맞추어 항복한 것을 헤아려, 견책 한마디로 보내 주겠다고 했고, 그리하여 그 견책을 토속어로 퍼부어 주었소.\n\n'하지만 너희로 말하자면, 이 썩은 고기 같은 악당들아.' 그가 삭구에 매달린 세 사람을 돌아보며 말했소. '너희는, 정유 솥(try-pot)에 넣게 잘게 다져 줄 작정이다.' 그러고는 밧줄 하나를 움켜쥐고, 두 배신자의 등에 온 힘을 다해 후려쳤소. 마침내 그들이 더는 비명도 지르지 못하고, 십자가에 못 박힌 두 도둑을 그린 그림처럼, 숨이 끊어진 듯 고개를 옆으로 떨굴 때까지 말이오.\n\n'네놈들 때문에 내 손목을 삐었구나!' 그가 마침내 외쳤소. '하지만 끝까지 굽히지 않겠다던 내 멋진 싸움닭, 네놈한테 쓸 밧줄은 아직 충분히 남아 있다. 저놈 입에서 그 재갈을 빼내, 제놈이 무슨 변명을 할 수 있는지 들어 보자.'\n\n잠시 그 지쳐 버린 반란자는 경련하는 턱을 떨듯이 움직이더니, 이윽고 고통스레 고개를 비틀어 돌리고는, 일종의 쉭쉭거리는 소리로 말했소. '내 할 말은 이것이오 — 그리고 잘 새겨들으시오 — 만일 당신이 나를 채찍질하면, 나는 당신을 죽이겠소!'\n\n'네놈이 그렇게 말한다? 그럼 내가 얼마나 무서워하는지 어디 봐라.' 그러면서 선장은 후려치려고 밧줄을 뒤로 빼들었소.\n\n'그러지 않는 게 좋을 거요.' 호반인이 쉭쉭거리며 말했소.\n\n'하지만 나는 해야겠다.' 그러면서 밧줄은 다시 한번 후려치려고 뒤로 당겨졌소.\n\n스틸킬트는 여기서 무언가를 쉭쉭거리며 내뱉었는데, 선장 말고는 누구에게도 들리지 않았소. 그러자 선장은, 전 선원이 깜짝 놀라게도, 흠칫 물러서더니, 갑판을 두세 번 빠르게 거닐고는, 별안간 밧줄을 내던지며 말했소. '안 하겠다 — 저놈을 놓아줘라 — 끌어 내려라. 알아들었나?'\n\n그러나 하급 항해사들이 그 명령을 서둘러 실행하려 할 때, 머리에 붕대를 감은 창백한 사내 하나가 그들을 가로막았으니 — 일등항해사 래드니였소. 그 일격을 당한 이래 그는 줄곧 제 침상에 누워 있었으나, 그날 아침 갑판의 소동을 듣고서 기어 나와, 여태껏 그 전모를 지켜본 터였소. 그의 입은 그 지경이어서 거의 말도 할 수 없었소. 그러나 선장이 감히 시도하지 못한 일을 자기는 기꺼이, 또 능히 할 수 있다는 식의 무언가를 웅얼거리며, 그는 밧줄을 낚아채어 묶여 있는 제 원수를 향해 나아갔소.\n\n'당신은 겁쟁이야!' 호반인이 쉭쉭거렸소.\n\n'그래, 나는 겁쟁이다. 하지만 이거나 받아라.' 일등항해사가 막 후려치려는 바로 그 순간, 또 한 번의 쉭쉭거리는 소리가 그의 치켜든 팔을 멈춰 세웠소. 그는 멈칫했소. 그러더니 더는 멈칫하지 않고, 스틸킬트의 위협이 무엇이었든 그것을 무릅쓰고, 제 말을 실행에 옮겼소. 그러고 나서 세 사람은 끌어 내려졌고, 전 선원이 일하러 돌아갔으며, 침울한 선원들이 시무룩하게 다루는 가운데, 그 무쇠 펌프가 전처럼 절그럭거렸소.\n\n그날 어둠이 막 내린 뒤, 한 당직조가 아래로 물러나 있을 때, 앞갑판에서 소란이 일었소. 그러자 부들부들 떠는 두 배신자가 뛰어 올라와, 차마 선원들과 어울릴 수 없다며 선실 문을 에워쌌소. 애원도, 손찌검도, 발길질도 그들을 돌려보내지 못해, 결국 그들 자신의 청에 따라 그들은 목숨을 부지하라고 배의 후미진 선저(船底)로 내려보내졌소. 그래도 나머지 사이에서는 반란의 기미가 다시 나타나지 않았소. 오히려, 주로 스틸킬트의 부추김으로, 그들은 더없이 철저한 평온을 지키고, 끝까지 모든 명령에 복종하며, 배가 항구에 닿으면 다 함께 배를 버리고 달아나기로 결의한 듯했소. 그러나 항해가 가장 빨리 끝나도록 보장하기 위해, 그들은 또 한 가지에 모두 합의했으니 — 곧, 혹여 고래가 발견되더라도 고래라고 외쳐 알리지 않는다는 것이었소. 왜냐하면, 누수에도 불구하고, 또 다른 온갖 위험에도 불구하고, 타운호는 여전히 돛대 꼭대기에 망꾼을 두고 있었고, 그 선장은 자기 배가 어장에 처음 들어선 날 못지않게 그 순간 당장이라도 고래를 잡으러 보트를 내릴 마음이 있었기 때문이오. 그리고 일등항해사 래드니 또한, 자기 침상을 보트로 기꺼이 바꾸어, 붕대 감은 입으로, 고래의 그 목숨 같은 턱을 죽음 속에 틀어막으려 들 만반의 준비가 되어 있었소.\n\n그러나 호반인은 선원들이 처신에 있어 이런 식의 수동성을 받아들이도록 이끌어 놓긴 했으나, 자기 심장의 심실(心室)을 찌른 그 사내에 대한 자신의 정당하고도 사사로운 복수에 관해서는 (적어도 모든 일이 끝날 때까지는) 제 속내를 혼자 간직했소. 그는 일등항해사 래드니의 당직조에 속해 있었소. 그리고 그 분별을 잃은 사내는 마치 제 운명을 마중하러 절반도 넘게 달려 나가려는 듯이, 삭구에서의 그 소동이 있은 뒤, 선장의 분명한 충고에도 아랑곳없이, 밤에 다시 제 당직조의 우두머리 자리에 서겠다고 우겼소. 바로 이 점과, 그 밖의 한두 가지 정황 위에, 스틸킬트는 자기 복수의 계획을 차근차근 쌓아 올렸소.\n\n밤이면 래드니는 뒷갑판의 뱃전에 앉아, 거기 배의 옆구리에서 조금 위쪽으로 끌어 올려져 있던 보트의 뱃전에 팔을 괴는, 선원답지 못한 버릇이 있었소. 이런 자세로 그가 더러 깜빡 졸곤 한다는 것은 잘 알려진 사실이었소. 보트와 배 사이에는 제법 빈틈이 있었고, 그 아래로는 바다였소. 스틸킬트는 자기 시간을 가늠해 보고, 그가 배신당한 그날로부터 사흘째 되는 날 새벽 두 시에 자기가 다음번 키를 잡을 차례가 돌아온다는 것을 알아냈소. 그는 한가한 틈에, 아래 선실에서 쉬는 동안 무언가를 아주 정성스레 땋는 데 그 짬을 썼소.\n\n'거기서 뭘 만들고 있나?' 한 동료 선원이 말했소.\n\n'뭘 만드는 것 같나? 뭐처럼 보이나?'\n\n'자네 자루에 매다는 끈 같긴 한데, 내가 보기엔 이상한 끈이로군.'\n\n'그래, 좀 이상한 끈이지.' 호반인이 그것을 팔 길이만큼 앞으로 들어 보이며 말했소. '하지만 쓸모는 있을 것 같네. 이보게, 내가 노끈이 모자라는데 — 자네한테 좀 있나?'\n\n그러나 앞갑판에는 노끈이 없었소.\n\n'그럼 늙은 래드한테서 좀 얻어 와야겠군.' 그러면서 그는 고물 쪽으로 가려고 일어섰소.\n\n'설마 그자한테 빌어먹으러 가려는 건 아니겠지!' 한 선원이 말했소.\n\n'왜 아니겠나? 그게 결국엔 제놈을 돕는 일인데, 그자가 나한테 호의 하나 베풀지 않을 것 같나, 이 사람아?' 그러면서 일등항해사에게 가서, 그는 그를 조용히 바라보며, 해먹을 손보게 노끈 좀 달라고 청했소. 노끈은 주어졌소 — 그 노끈도 끈도 다시는 눈에 띄지 않았소. 그러나 다음 날 밤, 호반인이 외투를 베개 삼아 해먹 속에 쑤셔 넣을 때, 그의 두꺼운 작업복 주머니에서 촘촘히 그물처럼 엮인 무쇠 공 하나가 반쯤 굴러 나왔소. 스물네 시간 뒤, 그가 그 고요한 키를 잡을 차례가 — 늘 선원의 손에 미리 파인 무덤 위로 깜빡 졸곤 하던 그 사내 가까이서 — 그 운명의 시각이 이제 닥쳐오게 되어 있었소. 그리고 미리 운명을 정해 두는 스틸킬트의 영혼 속에서는, 일등항해사가 이미 이마가 으스러진 채 뻣뻣하게 시체처럼 뻗어 있었소.\n\n그러나, 여러분, 한 바보가 그 살인자가 될 뻔한 자를 그가 꾸민 그 피의 행위로부터 구해 주었소. 그래도 그는 완전한 복수를 거두었으니, 더욱이 자기가 복수의 손이 되지 않고서 그러했소. 어떤 신비로운 운명에 의해, 하늘 그 자체가 나서서, 그가 저지르려던 그 저주받을 짓을 그의 손에서 거두어 제 손으로 가져가는 듯했기 때문이오.\n\n바로 그 이튿날 아침, 동틀 녘과 해 뜰 녘 사이, 그들이 갑판을 씻어 내리고 있을 때였소. 큰 돛대 사슬 쪽에서 물을 긷던 어느 멍청한 테네리페(Teneriffe) 사내가, 별안간 소리쳤소. '저기 구르고 있다! 저기 구르고 있어!' 맙소사, 그 무슨 고래였던지! 그것은 모비 딕이었소.\n\n'모비 딕이라니!' 돈 세바스티안이 외쳤다. '성 도미니쿠스여! 뱃사람 양반, 그래 고래도 세례명을 받는단 말이오? 누구를 모비 딕이라 부르는 거요?'\n\n'아주 새하얗고, 이름나고, 더없이 치명적이며 죽지 않는 괴물이라오, 돈. 하지만 그 이야기는 너무 길어질 게요.'\n\n'어째서요? 어떻게 그렇소?' 젊은 스페인 사람들이 모두 다가서며 외쳤다.\n\n'아니, 돈들, 돈들 — 아니, 아니오! 지금은 그 이야기를 풀어놓을 수가 없소. 좀 더 바람이나 쐬게 해 주시오, 여러분.'\n\n'치차! 치차를!' 돈 페드로가 외쳤다. '우리 기운찬 친구가 어지러워 보이는구려. 빈 잔을 채워 주시오!'\n\n그럴 것 없소, 여러분. 잠깐이면 되오, 계속하리다. 자, 여러분, 그 눈처럼 흰 고래가 배에서 쉰 야드도 안 되는 데 있는 것을 그토록 별안간 알아채고서 — 선원들 사이의 그 약속도 잊은 채 — 그 순간의 흥분에 휩싸여, 테네리페 사내는 본능적으로, 또 저도 모르게 그 괴물을 향해 목청을 높였소. 비록 그 괴물은 얼마 전부터 이미 그 침울한 세 돛대 꼭대기에서 똑똑히 보이고 있었지만 말이오. 이제 온통 광란이었소. '흰 고래다 — 흰 고래!' 선장과 항해사들과 작살잡이들이 외쳐 댔으니, 그들은 그 무시무시한 소문에도 굴하지 않고, 그토록 이름나고 값진 고래를 잡고 싶어 모두 안달이 나 있었소. 한편 고집스러운 선원들은, 수평으로 반짝이는 햇살을 받아 환히 빛나며 푸른 아침 바다 위에서 살아 있는 오팔처럼 일렁이며 반짝이는, 그 거대한 젖빛 덩어리의 소름 끼치는 아름다움을, 곁눈질로, 또 저주를 퍼부으며 바라보았소. 여러분, 이 일련의 사건들이 펼쳐지는 그 모든 과정에는, 마치 세상 그 자체가 지도로 그려지기도 전에 진실로 미리 짜여 있기라도 한 듯, 기묘한 운명이 깃들어 있소. 그 반란자는 일등항해사의 노잡이였고, 고래에 작살이 박혀 줄이 당겨질 때면, 래드니가 이물에 작살을 들고 일어서는 동안 그 곁에 앉아, 명령 한마디에 줄을 끌어들이거나 늦추는 것이 그의 임무였소. 게다가, 네 척의 보트가 내려졌을 때, 일등항해사의 보트가 앞서 나갔소. 그리고 스틸킬트가 제 노를 힘껏 당기며 기뻐 날뛰는 그 으르렁대는 소리보다 더 사납게 울부짖는 자는 아무도 없었소. 한바탕 힘껏 노를 저은 끝에, 그들의 작살잡이가 작살을 박았고, 창을 손에 든 래드니가 이물로 뛰어올랐소. 보트에서는 그가 늘 사나운 사내였던 모양이오. 그리고 이제 그의 붕대 감은 입에서 나온 외침은, 자기를 고래의 가장 높은 등 위에 올려 달라는 것이었소. 그의 노잡이는 거리낌 없이, 두 가지 흰빛을 한데 뒤섞는 눈먼 듯한 거품을 헤치고, 그를 위로 또 위로 끌어 올렸소. 그러다 별안간 보트가 물밑 암초에 부딪치듯 부딪치며, 옆으로 기울어, 서 있던 일등항해사를 쏟아 냈소. 그 순간, 그가 고래의 미끄러운 등 위로 떨어지자, 보트는 바로 서며 너울에 밀려 옆으로 내동댕이쳐졌고, 래드니는 고래의 반대편 옆구리로 바다에 내던져졌소. 그는 물보라를 헤치고 헤엄쳐 나와, 한순간, 그 장막 너머로 어렴풋이 보였으니, 모비 딕의 눈에서 벗어나려고 미친 듯이 발버둥치고 있었소. 그러나 그 고래는 별안간 소용돌이를 일으키며 빙 돌더니, 그 헤엄치는 자를 두 턱 사이에 움켜잡았고, 그를 물고 높이 솟구쳤다가, 다시 거꾸로 곤두박질치며 물속으로 내려갔소.\n\n그동안, 보트 바닥이 처음 부딪친 그 순간, 호반인은 줄을 늦추어, 그 소용돌이에서 고물 쪽으로 빠져나오게 했소. 그는 차분히 지켜보며, 제 생각에 잠겨 있었소. 그러나 별안간 보트가 무섭게 아래로 홱 잡아당겨지자, 그는 재빨리 칼을 줄에 갖다 댔소. 그가 줄을 끊었고, 고래는 풀려났소. 그러나 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 모비 딕이 다시 떠올랐는데, 그를 파멸시킨 그 이빨에는 래드니의 붉은 모직 셔츠의 너덜너덜한 조각이 걸려 있었소. 네 척의 보트가 다시 추격에 나섰소. 그러나 고래는 그들을 따돌렸고, 마침내 완전히 사라져 버렸소.\n\n이윽고, 타운호는 그 항구에 닿았소 — 야만적이고 외딴 곳으로, 문명화된 피조물이라곤 살지 않는 데였소. 거기서, 호반인을 우두머리로 하여, 다섯이나 여섯을 뺀 앞돛대 선원 전부가 야자수 사이로 작정하고 달아났소. 끝내 드러난 바로는, 그들은 미개인들의 큼직한 쌍둥이 전투용 카누 한 척을 빼앗아, 어딘가 다른 항구를 향해 돛을 올렸다 하오.\n\n배의 인원이 한 줌으로 줄어들자, 선장은 섬사람들을 불러, 누수를 막으려 배를 기울여 끌어 내리는 그 고된 일을 거들어 달라고 했소. 그러나 이 한 줌의 백인들은, 위험한 자기네 협력자들을 밤이고 낮이고 한순간도 쉬지 않고 경계해야만 했고, 또 그들이 치른 고역이 어찌나 혹독했던지, 배가 다시 바다로 나갈 채비가 갖추어졌을 무렵, 그들은 너무도 쇠약해진 형편이라, 선장은 그토록 묵직한 배에 그들을 태우고 떠날 엄두를 내지 못했소. 사관들과 의논한 끝에, 그는 배를 가능한 한 해안에서 멀리 떼어 닻을 내리고, 이물의 대포 두 문에 장전하여 밀어 내고, 고물 갑판에 머스킷 총을 쌓아 두었소. 그러고는 섬사람들에게 목숨이 아깝거든 배에 가까이 오지 말라고 경고한 뒤, 사내 하나를 데리고, 자기의 가장 좋은 포경 보트의 돛을 올려, 바람을 받으며 곧장 오백 마일 떨어진 타히티(Tahiti)를 향해 키를 잡았소. 자기 선원을 보충하기 위해서였소.\n\n항해 나흘째 되는 날, 큰 카누 한 척이 눈에 띄었는데, 그것은 산호로 이루어진 낮은 섬에 잠시 들렀던 듯했소. 그는 그것을 피해 키를 돌렸소. 그러나 그 미개인의 배가 그를 향해 다가왔소. 곧 스틸킬트의 목소리가 그에게 외쳐, 배를 멈추라고, 그러지 않으면 그를 물속에 처박아 버리겠다고 했소. 선장은 권총을 들이댔소. 멍에처럼 한데 묶인 전투용 카누의 두 이물에 한 발씩 디딘 채, 호반인은 그를 비웃으며, 만일 그 권총의 공이가 조금이라도 철컥하기만 하면, 그를 거품과 물보라 속에 파묻어 버리겠노라고 장담했소.\n\n'나한테 무얼 원하는 거냐?' 선장이 외쳤소.\n\n'당신은 어디로 가는 거요? 또 무엇 때문에 가는 거요?' 스틸킬트가 따져 물었소. '거짓말은 마시오.'\n\n'나는 사람을 더 구하러 타히티로 가는 길이다.'\n\n'좋소. 잠깐 당신 배에 올라가게 해 주시오 — 나는 평화롭게 가는 거요.' 그러면서 그는 카누에서 뛰어내려, 헤엄쳐 보트로 다가갔소. 그리고 뱃전을 기어올라, 선장과 얼굴을 마주하고 섰소.\n\n'두 팔을 가슴에 포개시오, 나리. 고개를 뒤로 젖히고. 자, 내 말을 따라 하시오. 스틸킬트가 나를 떠나는 즉시, 나는 이 보트를 저기 저 섬에 대고 거기서 엿새 동안 머물 것을 맹세한다. 만일 그러지 않거든, 벼락이 나를 내리쳐도 좋다!'\n\n'제법 학자 양반이로군.' 호반인이 웃었소. '잘 가시오, 세뇨르!' 그러면서 그는 바다로 뛰어들어, 헤엄쳐 자기 동료들에게로 돌아갔소.\n\n그 보트가 마침내 해변에 닿아, 야자나무 뿌리께까지 끌어 올려지는 것을 지켜본 뒤, 스틸킬트는 다시 돛을 올렸고, 때맞추어 자기 목적지인 타히티에 닿았소. 거기서 운이 그를 도왔소. 두 척의 배가 막 프랑스를 향해 떠나려던 참이었는데, 마치 하늘의 섭리인 양 그 뱃사람이 거느린 바로 그만큼의 인원을 마침 필요로 하고 있었소. 그들은 배에 올랐소. 그리하여, 설령 그들의 옛 선장이 그들에게 법대로 응보를 가할 마음을 조금이라도 품었더라 한들, 그들은 영영 그보다 한발 앞서 떠나 버린 것이오.\n\n프랑스 배들이 떠난 지 열흘쯤 뒤에, 그 포경 보트가 도착했고, 선장은 어쩔 수 없이 바다에 어느 정도 익숙해진, 좀 더 문명화된 타히티 사람 몇을 새로 고용했소. 작은 토착 스쿠너 한 척을 빌려, 그는 그들을 데리고 제 배로 돌아갔소. 그리고 거기 모든 것이 무사함을 보고서, 다시 항해를 이어 갔소.\n\n여러분, 스틸킬트가 지금 어디에 있는지는 아무도 모르오. 그러나 낸터킷 섬에서는, 래드니의 미망인이 지금도 제 죽은 자를 내어 주기를 거부하는 그 바다를 향해 발길을 돌리고 있소. 그리고 꿈속에서 지금도, 제 남편을 파멸시킨 그 무서운 흰 고래를 보고 있다오.\n\n'다 끝났소?' 돈 세바스티안이 조용히 말했다.\n\n'다 끝났소, 돈.'\n\n'그럼 부탁이니, 말해 주시오. 당신 자신의 확신에 비추어, 이 당신 이야기가 그 본질에 있어 정말로 참된 것이오? 그토록 지나치게 놀라운 이야기인지라! 의심할 여지없는 출처에서 들은 것이오? 내가 다그치는 듯 보이거든 너그러이 받아 주시오.'\n\n'우리 모두에게도 너그러우시오, 뱃사람 양반. 우리 모두가 돈 세바스티안의 청에 한마음으로 동참하니 말이오.' 일행이 더없이 큰 관심을 담아 외쳤다.\n\n'골든 인에 거룩한 복음서(Holy Evangelists) 한 권이라도 있겠소, 여러분?'\n\n'아니오.' 돈 세바스티안이 말했다. '하지만 근처에 훌륭한 사제 한 분을 아는데, 그분이 얼른 한 권 구해다 주실 게요. 내가 가져오리다. 그런데 당신, 정녕 잘 생각하신 게요? 이게 너무 심각해질 수도 있소.'\n\n'사제님도 함께 모셔다주시면 고맙겠소, 돈?'\n\n'지금이야 리마에 화형식(Auto-da-Fé) 같은 건 없소만.' 일행 가운데 하나가 다른 이에게 말했다. '나는 우리 뱃사람 친구가 대주교청의 미움을 살 위험이 있을까 걱정되는구려. 달빛에서 좀 더 물러나 있읍시다. 이럴 필요까지는 없을 듯한데.'\n\n'쫓아가서 미안하오, 돈 세바스티안. 하지만 한 가지 더 부탁해도 되겠소? 구할 수 있는 가장 큰 판형의 복음서로 각별히 마련해다 주시오.'\n\n'이분이 그 사제님이오, 당신께 복음서를 가져다주실 게요.' 돈 세바스티안이 키 크고 근엄한 인물과 함께 돌아오며 자못 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n'내 모자를 벗겠소. 자, 존경하는 사제님, 빛 속으로 좀 더 가까이 오시어, 그 거룩한 책을 제 앞에 들어 주시오, 제가 거기 손을 댈 수 있도록 말이오.'\n\n'하늘이여 나를 도우소서, 또 내 명예를 걸고, 여러분, 내가 여러분께 들려드린 이 이야기는 그 본질과 그 큰 줄거리에 있어 참된 것이오. 나는 그것이 참됨을 알고 있소. 그 일은 이 지구 위에서 벌어졌소. 나는 그 배를 디뎌 보았고, 그 선원들을 알았으며, 래드니가 죽은 뒤로 스틸킬트를 만나 그와 이야기를 나누었소.'\"\n\n# CHAPTER 55. Of the Monstrous Pictures of Whales.\n\n머지않아 나는 화폭 없이도 그릴 수 있는 한 최대한 충실하게, 고래가 그 자신의 온전한 몸뚱이 그대로 포경선 곁에 매여 있어 그 위에 정통으로 발을 디딜 수 있을 때 고래잡이의 눈에 실제로 비치는 고래의 참된 형상에 가까운 무언가를 그대들에게 그려 보이고자 한다. 그러므로 그에 앞서, 오늘날에 이르기까지도 뭍사람의 믿음을 당당히 시험하는 저 기이하고 상상으로 빚어낸 고래 초상화들을 잠시 짚어 보는 것이 가치 있는 일일 것이다. 그러한 고래 그림들이 모조리 틀렸음을 입증함으로써, 이 문제에 관하여 세상을 바로잡을 때가 되었다.\n\n이 모든 그림 속 망상의 근원은 어쩌면 가장 오래된 힌두와 이집트와 그리스의 조각들 속에서 찾아질지도 모른다. 사원의 대리석 패널과 조각상의 받침대, 그리고 방패와 메달리온과 잔과 동전 위에, 돌고래가 살라딘의 것과 같은 사슬 갑옷 비늘과 성 게오르기우스의 것과 같은 투구 쓴 머리로 그려지던, 저 창의적이지만 거리낌 없던 시절 이래로, 그와 똑같은 종류의 자유분방함이 고래의 가장 대중적인 그림들뿐 아니라 그에 관한 수많은 과학적 묘사들에까지 줄곧 만연해 온 것이다.\n\n자, 무엇보다도, 어떤 식으로든 고래를 그렸노라 자처하는 현존하는 가장 오래된 초상은 인도의 그 유명한 엘레판타 동굴 사원에서 찾아볼 수 있다. 브라만들은 그 태곳적 사원의 거의 끝없는 조각들 속에, 인간의 모든 직업과 일거리, 상상할 수 있는 온갖 생업이 그것들이 실제로 생겨나기 수천 년 전에 미리 그려져 있다고 주장한다. 그러니 우리의 고귀한 포경업이 어떤 식으로든 거기에 어렴풋이 비쳐 있다 한들 놀랄 일이 아니다. 그 힌두 고래는 벽의 별도 구획에 등장하는데, 학식 있는 자들 사이에서 마트세 아바타르로 알려진, 리바이어던의 형상을 한 비슈누의 화신을 묘사한 것이다. 그러나 이 조각은 절반은 사람이고 절반은 고래여서 후자의 꼬리만을 보여 주는데, 그나마 그 작은 부분조차 온통 틀렸다. 그것은 참된 고래의 위풍당당한 꼬리지느러미의 널찍한 손바닥 같은 모양보다는, 차라리 아나콘다의 가늘어지는 꼬리에 더 가까워 보인다.\n\n그러나 옛 화랑들로 가서, 이제 어느 위대한 기독교 화가가 그린 이 물고기의 초상을 보라. 그라고 해서 대홍수 이전의 힌두인보다 더 나을 것이 없으니 말이다. 그것은 페르세우스가 바다 괴물 혹은 고래로부터 안드로메다를 구하는 장면을 그린 귀도의 그림이다. 귀도는 도대체 어디서 그토록 기괴한 피조물의 모델을 얻었단 말인가? 호가스 역시 자신의 작품 \"강림하는 페르세우스\"에서 같은 장면을 그리면서 조금도 나을 바가 없다. 그 호가스적 괴물의 거대한 비만은 수면 위에서 일렁이는데, 물에 잠긴 깊이는 채 한 치도 되지 않는다. 그것은 등에 일종의 가마 같은 것을 얹고 있고, 파도가 굽이쳐 들어가는 그 떡 벌어진 엄니 달린 입은, 템스강에서 물길을 따라 탑으로 통하는 반역자의 문으로 여겨질 만하다. 그다음으로는, 늙은 스코틀랜드인 시발드의 프로드로무스 고래들이 있고, 낡은 성경의 판화와 낡은 입문서의 삽화에 그려진 요나(Jonah)의 고래가 있다. 이것들에 대해서는 무어라 말해야 할까? 내려가는 닻의 몸통을 포도 덩굴처럼 휘감고 있는 — 오래된 책이든 새 책이든 수많은 책의 뒤표지와 표제지에 찍히고 도금된 — 제본업자의 고래로 말하자면, 그것은 매우 그림 같지만 순전히 꾸며 낸 피조물로서, 내 생각으로는 고대 항아리에 그려진 비슷한 형상에서 본떠 온 것이다. 비록 어디서나 돌고래라 불리지만, 그럼에도 나는 이 제본업자의 물고기를 고래를 그리려 한 시도라 부르겠다. 그 도안이 처음 도입되었을 때 그렇게 의도되었기 때문이다. 그것은 학문 부흥기였던 15세기 어름에 어느 늙은 이탈리아 출판업자가 도입한 것인데, 그 시절에는, 아니 비교적 후대에 이르기까지도, 돌고래는 흔히 리바이어던의 일종으로 여겨졌던 것이다.\n\n어떤 옛 책들의 작은 삽화와 그 밖의 장식들 속에서, 그대는 이따금 고래에 관한 매우 기이한 손길과 마주치게 될 터인데, 거기서는 온갖 종류의 물줄기와 분수와 더운 샘과 찬 샘, 새러토가와 바덴바덴이 그것의 마르지 않는 머릿속에서 부글부글 솟아오른다. \"학문의 진보\" 초판본의 표제지에서도 그대는 몇몇 기이한 고래들을 발견하게 될 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 비전문가적 시도들은 접어 두고, 잘 아는 자들이 그렸다고 자처하는, 진중하고 과학적인 묘사를 표방하는 저 리바이어던 그림들을 한번 훑어보자. 늙은 해리스의 항해기 모음집에는 1671년의 어느 네덜란드 항해서에서 발췌한 고래 도판 몇 점이 실려 있는데, 그 책의 제목은 \"고래 뱃속의 요나호를 타고 떠난 스피츠베르겐 포경 항해, 선장 프리슬란트의 페터 페터르손\"이다. 그 도판들 가운데 하나에서는 통나무 뗏목처럼 거대한 고래들이 얼음섬들 사이에 누워 있고, 흰곰들이 그 살아 있는 등 위를 뛰어다니는 모습으로 그려져 있다. 또 다른 도판에서는 고래의 꼬리지느러미를 수직으로 그려 놓는 어처구니없는 실수를 저지르고 있다.\n\n또한, 영국 해군의 함장 콜넷이라는 사람이 쓴, 위풍당당한 사절판 책이 한 권 있는데, 그 제목은 \"향유고래잡이 확장을 위한 케이프 혼을 돌아 남쪽 바다로 가는 항해\"이다. 이 책에는 \"1793년 8월 멕시코 연안에서 잡혀 갑판으로 끌어올려진 한 마리를 척도에 맞추어 그린 피세테르, 곧 향유고래의 그림\"이라 표방하는 윤곽도가 실려 있다. 나는 이 함장이 자기 해병들을 위해 이 진실한 그림을 그리게 한 것이라 믿어 의심치 않는다. 그것에 관해 한 가지만 짚고 넘어가자면, 거기에는 눈이 하나 그려져 있는데, 그 곁에 붙은 척도에 따라 다 자란 향유고래에 적용해 보면, 그 고래의 눈은 길이가 다섯 자쯤 되는 활 모양 내닫이창이 되고 말 것이다. 아, 나의 용맹한 함장이여, 어찌하여 그 눈으로 내다보는 요나는 우리에게 그려 주지 않았단 말이오!\n\n어린이와 여린 마음을 위한, 가장 양심적인 박물지 편찬물조차도 이 가증스러운 오류에서 자유롭지 못하다. 저 대중적인 저작 \"골드스미스의 생동하는 자연\"을 보라. 1807년 런던 축약판에는 이른바 \"고래\"와 \"외뿔고래\"의 도판이 실려 있다. 무례하게 굴고 싶지는 않으나, 이 흉측한 고래는 다리가 잘린 암퇘지를 영락없이 닮았으며, 외뿔고래로 말하자면, 그것을 한번 흘끗 보기만 해도, 이 십구 세기에 그런 히포그리프가 진품인 양 어느 영민한 학동 무리에게 떠넘겨질 수 있다는 사실에 그저 아연실색하게 된다.\n\n또한, 1825년에는 위대한 박물학자 베르나르 제르맹 라세페드 백작이 리바이어던의 여러 종을 그린 그림 몇 점이 담긴, 과학적으로 체계화된 고래 책을 펴냈다. 이것들은 모조리 부정확할뿐더러, 미스티세투스, 곧 그린란드 고래(다시 말해 참고래)의 그림은, 그 종에 관하여 오랜 경험을 쌓은 사람인 스코어즈비조차도 자연계에는 그에 대응하는 것이 존재하지 않는다고 단언하는 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 엉터리 작업에 화룡점정을 찍는 일은, 그 유명한 남작의 동생인 과학자 프레데리크 퀴비에에게 맡겨져 있었다. 1836년에 그는 고래의 박물지를 펴냈는데, 거기서 그는 자신이 향유고래의 그림이라 일컫는 것을 내놓는다. 그 그림을 어느 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람에게 보여 주기 전에, 그대는 낸터킷에서 신속히 달아날 방도부터 마련해 두는 편이 좋을 것이다. 한마디로, 프레데리크 퀴비에의 향유고래는 향유고래가 아니라 호박덩어리다. 물론 그는 포경 항해의 혜택을 누려 본 적이 없었지만(그런 사람들은 좀처럼 그런 경험이 없다), 도대체 그가 그 그림을 어디서 끌어왔는지 누가 알겠는가? 어쩌면 그는 같은 분야에서 그보다 앞선 과학자 데마레가 자신의 진품 같지 않은 졸작 하나를 얻은 것과 같은 방식으로, 곧 어느 중국 그림에서 그것을 얻었는지도 모른다. 그리고 그 중국인들이 붓을 든 얼마나 활기찬 친구들인지는, 수많은 기묘한 잔과 받침 접시들이 우리에게 일러 준다.\n\n기름 장수의 가게 위에 매달려 거리에서 볼 수 있는 간판장이의 고래들로 말하자면, 그것들에 대해서는 무어라 말해야 할까? 그것들은 대개 단봉낙타 같은 혹을 단, 매우 사나운 리처드 3세 고래로서, 세네 개의 선원 파이를, 다시 말해 뱃사람들로 가득 찬 포경 보트를 아침거리로 먹어 치우는데, 그 일그러진 몸뚱이들이 피와 푸른 물감의 바다 속에서 허우적거리고 있다.\n\n그러나 고래를 묘사함에 있어 이토록 갖가지 실수가 빚어진 것도 따지고 보면 그리 놀라운 일은 아니다. 한번 생각해 보라! 과학적 그림들의 대부분은 좌초한 물고기를 보고 그린 것이며, 이는 등이 부러진 난파선을 그린 그림이, 선체와 활대의 한 점 흠 없는 위풍을 온전히 갖춘 그 고귀한 선박 자체를 정확히 그려 낼 수 있는 것과 엇비슷한 정도로만 정확하다. 코끼리는 그 전신을 통째로 그림의 모델로 세웠건만, 살아 있는 리바이어던은 아직 단 한 번도 제 초상을 위해 제대로 몸을 떠올려 본 적이 없다. 살아 있는 고래는, 그 온전한 위엄과 의미를 갖춘 모습으로는, 오직 바다의 헤아릴 수 없는 깊은 물에서만 볼 수 있다. 그리고 물 위에 떠 있을 때 그 거대한 몸뚱이는, 진수된 전열함처럼 시야에서 벗어나 있다. 또한 그 원소 밖으로 그것을 통째로 공중에 들어 올려, 그 위풍당당한 모든 융기와 굴곡을 고스란히 보존하는 일은, 죽을 운명의 인간에게는 영원히 불가능한 노릇이다. 그리고 젖을 빠는 어린 고래와 다 자란 플라톤적 리바이어던 사이에 충분히 짐작될 만한 윤곽의 차이는 말할 것도 없거니와, 설령 그런 젖먹이 어린 고래 가운데 한 마리를 배의 갑판으로 끌어올린다 한들, 그때 그것의 모습은 어찌나 괴상하고 뱀장어처럼 흐물거리며 시시각각 변하는지, 그 정확한 표정만큼은 악마 자신도 붙잡아 낼 수 없을 것이다.\n\n그러나 좌초한 고래의 앙상한 뼈대에서라면 그 참된 형상에 관한 정확한 단서를 얻을 수 있으리라 짐작할 수도 있겠다. 천만의 말씀이다. 이 리바이어던에 관한 더욱 기이한 점 하나는, 그 뼈대가 전체적인 형상에 관해 거의 아무런 단서도 주지 않는다는 것이기 때문이다. 제러미 벤담의 유언 집행인 가운데 하나의 서재에 촛대 노릇으로 걸려 있는 그의 뼈대는 떡 벌어진 이마를 가진 공리주의 노신사의 인상을, 그 밖에 제러미의 다른 주된 개인적 특징들과 함께 정확히 전해 주지만, 그 어떤 리바이어던의 짜 맞춰진 뼈에서도 이런 종류의 것은 추론해 낼 수 없다. 사실, 위대한 헌터가 말하듯, 고래의 뼈대만으로는, 그것이 온전히 살로 둘러싸이고 충전된 동물에 대해 가지는 관계가, 마치 곤충이 그것을 둥글게 감싸고 있는 번데기에 대해 가지는 관계와 같다. 이 특이함은 머리에서 두드러지게 드러나는데, 이는 이 책의 어느 부분에서 곁들여 보이게 될 것이다. 그것은 또한 옆지느러미에서도 매우 흥미롭게 나타나는데, 그 뼈들은 거의 정확히 사람 손의 뼈에 들어맞으며, 다만 엄지만 빠져 있을 뿐이다. 이 지느러미에는 집게손가락, 가운뎃손가락, 약손가락, 새끼손가락에 해당하는 네 개의 가지런한 뼈손가락이 있다. 그러나 이것들은 모두, 사람의 손가락이 인공의 덮개 속에 들어 있듯, 살로 된 덮개 속에 영영 박혀 있다. \"고래가 때로 우리를 아무리 무지막지하게 대한다 한들,\" 익살꾼 스터브가 어느 날 말했다, \"그놈이 장갑도 끼지 않고 우리를 다룬다고는 결코 말할 수 없지.\"\n\n그러므로 이 모든 이유로, 어느 모로 보든, 그대는 이 위대한 리바이어던이야말로 끝끝내 그려지지 않은 채로 남아야 할, 이 세상의 단 하나뿐인 피조물이라는 결론에 다다를 수밖에 없다. 물론 어떤 초상이 다른 초상보다 과녁에 훨씬 더 가까이 맞을 수는 있겠으나, 어느 것도 그다지 대단한 정확성으로는 그것을 맞힐 수 없다. 그러니 고래가 정말로 어떻게 생겼는지 정확히 알아낼 길은 이 세상 어디에도 없다. 그리고 그것의 살아 있는 윤곽에 대해 그나마 그런대로 괜찮은 관념이라도 얻을 수 있는 유일한 방법은, 그대 자신이 직접 고래잡이에 나서는 것이다. 그러나 그렇게 함으로써 그대는 그놈에게 영원히 들이받혀 침몰당할 적잖은 위험을 무릅쓰게 된다. 그러므로, 내 보기에는, 이 리바이어던에 관한 호기심을 두고 그대는 너무 까다롭게 굴지 않는 편이 좋을 듯하다.\n\n고래에 관한 저 괴물 같은 그림들과 관련하여, 나는 여기서 그것들에 얽힌 한층 더 괴물 같은 이야기들로 넘어가고 싶은 강한 유혹을 느낀다. 그런 이야기들은 고대와 근대를 막론하고 어떤 책들 속에서, 특히 플리니우스, 퍼처스, 해클루트, 해리스, 퀴비에 따위의 글 속에서 발견된다. 그러나 그 문제는 그냥 지나치기로 한다.\n\n위대한 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 윤곽을 출판으로 그려낸 것 가운데 내가 아는 것은 단 네 가지뿐이다. 콜넷, 허긴스, 프레더릭 퀴비에, 그리고 빌의 것이다. 앞 장에서 콜넷과 퀴비에는 이미 언급했다. 허긴스의 것은 그들의 것보다 훨씬 낫지만, 단연코 가장 뛰어난 것은 빌의 그림이다. 빌이 그린 이 고래의 그림들은 모두 훌륭한데, 다만 그의 둘째 장 첫머리를 장식한, 여러 자세를 취한 고래 세 마리를 담은 그림 가운데 한가운데 놓인 형상만은 예외다. 그의 권두 삽화는 향유고래를 공격하는 보트들을 그린 것인데, 어느 응접실 신사들의 점잖은 회의를 자아내도록 계산된 것이라 한들, 그 전체적인 효과만큼은 감탄할 만큼 정확하고 생생하다. J. 로스 브라운의 향유고래 그림 가운데 일부는 윤곽이 제법 정확하지만, 판각이 형편없다. 하지만 그것이 그의 잘못은 아니다.\n\n참고래(Right Whale)에 관해서라면 가장 뛰어난 윤곽화는 스코어스비의 것이지만, 너무 작은 축척으로 그려져 있어 바람직한 인상을 전하지 못한다. 그에게는 고래잡이 장면을 담은 그림이 단 하나뿐인데, 이는 안타까운 결함이다. 살아 있는 사냥꾼들의 눈에 비친 살아 있는 고래에 관해 그나마 진실에 가까운 관념을 얻을 수 있는 것은, 오직 그런 그림들을 통해서, 그것도 제대로 그려졌을 때에만 가능하기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 모든 점을 두루 따져 볼 때, 비록 세부에서는 가장 정확하다고 할 수 없을지언정, 어디서든 찾아볼 수 있는 고래와 고래잡이 장면의 묘사 가운데 단연 가장 빼어난 것은 두 점의 큼직한 프랑스 판화다. 솜씨 좋게 새겨진 이 그림들은 가르네리라는 어떤 이의 회화를 바탕으로 한 것이다. 두 그림은 각각 향유고래와 참고래에 대한 공격을 묘사한다. 첫째 판화에는 위풍당당한 향유고래 한 마리가 그 힘의 위엄을 한껏 드러낸 채 그려져 있는데, 방금 대양의 심연에서 보트 밑으로 솟구쳐 올라, 박살 난 널빤지들의 처참한 잔해를 등에 짊어진 채 허공 높이 떠받치고 있다. 보트의 뱃머리는 일부만 부서진 채 그 괴물의 등뼈 위에서 아슬아슬하게 균형을 잡고 있는 모습으로 그려져 있다. 그리고 그 뱃머리에 한 사공이 서 있는데, 셈할 수도 없는 그 단 한 순간의 섬광 같은 찰나에, 고래가 성난 듯 끓어오르며 뿜어내는 물줄기에 반쯤 가려진 채 마치 벼랑에서 뛰어내리려는 듯한 자세를 취하고 있다. 이 모든 동작이 놀랍도록 훌륭하고 진실하다. 반쯤 비어 가는 작살줄 통은 하얗게 부서지는 바다 위에 떠 있고, 쏟아진 작살들의 나무 자루가 그 안에서 비스듬히 까딱거린다. 헤엄치는 선원들의 머리는 고래 주변에 흩어진 채 저마다 다른 공포의 표정을 짓고 있고, 한편 검고 폭풍우 치는 저 먼 곳에서는 배가 그 현장을 향해 내달려 오고 있다. 이 고래의 해부학적 세부에는 자못 심각한 흠을 찾아낼 수도 있겠으나, 그냥 넘어가기로 하자. 나로서는 죽었다 깨어나도 이만큼 잘 그려낼 수 없을 테니 말이다.\n\n둘째 판화에서는 보트가 거대한 참고래의, 따개비로 뒤덮인 옆구리로 막 다가서는 참이다. 이 고래는 검고 해초가 엉긴 거구를 바다 속에서 굴리며 나아가는데, 그 모습이 마치 파타고니아의 절벽에서 이끼 낀 바위가 무너져 내리는 것 같다. 그것이 뿜어 올리는 물줄기는 곧추서서 가득하고 그을음처럼 검다. 굴뚝에서 그토록 자욱한 연기가 피어오르는 것을 보면, 저 거대한 뱃속 아래에서 진수성찬 같은 만찬이 끓고 있으리라 여길 법도 하다. 바닷새들은 작은 게며 조개며, 참고래가 이따금 그 역겨운 등에 싣고 다니는 갖가지 바다 사탕과 마카로니 같은 것들을 쪼아 먹고 있다. 그러는 내내 입술 두툼한 리바이어던(Leviathan)은 깊은 바다를 가르며 돌진하느라, 뒤로는 몇 톤이나 되는 거센 흰 거품을 남기고, 그 작은 보트를 너울 속에서 흔들어 대니, 마치 외륜 증기선의 물갈퀴 바퀴 가까이에 휘말린 거룻배 같다. 이렇듯 화면 앞쪽은 온통 격렬한 소란이지만, 그 뒤에는 감탄스러운 예술적 대비로 잔잔히 가라앉은 바다의 유리알 같은 수면과, 바람 없이 맥없이 늘어진 배의 풀기 빠진 돛, 그리고 죽은 고래의 미동도 없는 거구가 정복당한 요새처럼 놓여 있고, 그 분수공에 꽂힌 고래 깃대에서는 포획의 깃발이 나른하게 늘어져 있다.\n\n화가 가르네리가 누구인지, 혹은 누구였는지 나는 알지 못한다. 그러나 내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 그는 자기가 그리는 대상에 실제로 정통한 사람이었거나, 아니면 노련한 고래잡이에게 놀라우리만치 잘 배운 사람이었음이 틀림없다. 동작을 그리는 데는 프랑스인이 제격이다. 유럽의 모든 회화를 두루 살펴보라. 베르사유의 저 개선 회랑만큼 살아 숨 쉬는 소란을 화폭에 담아낸 화랑을 어디서 찾을 수 있겠는가. 그곳에서는 관람자가 프랑스의 잇따른 대전투들을 헤치며 우왕좌왕 길을 뚫고 나아가고, 칼이란 칼은 모두 북극광의 섬광처럼 번쩍이며, 무장한 왕들과 황제들이 줄지어 내달려 가는 모습이 마치 왕관 쓴 켄타우로스 무리가 돌격하는 것 같다. 가르네리의 이 바다 전투화들은, 저 회랑에 한 자리를 차지하기에 결코 손색이 없다.\n\n사물의 그림 같은 매력을 포착해 내는 프랑스인 특유의 천부적 재능은, 그들이 그린 고래잡이 장면의 회화와 판화에서 유독 두드러지게 드러나는 듯하다. 그들은 고래잡이 경험이 영국의 십분의 일에도, 미국의 천분의 일에도 미치지 못함에도 불구하고, 고래 사냥의 참된 정신을 그나마 전할 수 있는 유일하게 완성된 스케치를 두 나라 모두에게 제공해 왔다. 영국과 미국의 고래 화공들은 대개 고래의 텅 빈 옆모습 같은, 사물의 기계적인 윤곽을 제시하는 것으로 완전히 만족하는 듯한데, 그림 같은 효과로 말할 것 같으면 그것은 피라미드의 옆모습을 스케치하는 것과 별반 다를 바 없다. 정당하게 명성을 떨친 참고래잡이 스코어스비조차도, 그린란드 고래의 뻣뻣한 전신상을 하나 보여 주고 일각고래와 돌고래의 섬세한 세밀화를 서너 점 곁들인 다음에는, 보트 갈고리며 자르는 칼이며 닻갈고리 따위의 고전적인 판화를 줄줄이 내놓는다. 그리고 레이우엔훅 같은 현미경적 근면함으로, 확대한 북극 눈 결정의 모사도 아흔여섯 점을 추위에 떠는 세상의 검열에 부친다. 나는 이 훌륭한 항해자를 폄하할 뜻은 조금도 없으며(오히려 노련한 베테랑으로서 존경한다), 다만 그토록 중대한 사안에 있어, 결정 하나하나에 그린란드 치안판사 앞에서 작성한 선서 진술서를 확보해 두지 않은 것은 분명 한 가지 실수였다 하겠다.\n\n가르네리의 저 빼어난 판화들 외에도, 주목할 만한 프랑스 판화가 두 점 더 있는데, 자신을 \"H. 뒤랑\"이라 서명한 어떤 이의 작품이다. 그중 하나는 우리의 지금 목적에 꼭 들어맞는 것은 아니지만, 다른 이유에서 언급할 만하다. 그것은 태평양 섬들 사이의 고요한 정오 풍경이다. 프랑스 포경선 한 척이 잔잔한 물 위, 해안 가까이에 닻을 내린 채 한가로이 물을 싣고 있고, 풀어 놓은 배의 돛과 배경의 야자수 긴 잎사귀들이 바람 한 점 없는 공기 속에서 함께 늘어져 있다. 그 효과는 자못 빼어난데, 강인한 고래잡이들을 좀처럼 보기 힘든 동방적 휴식의 한 모습으로 담아냈다는 점을 고려하면 더욱 그렇다. 다른 판화는 사뭇 다른 광경이다. 배는 망망대해 위, 바로 리바이어던적 생명의 한복판에서 닻을 풀고 멈춰 선 채, 옆구리에는 참고래 한 마리를 끼고 있다. 배는 (해체 작업을 하느라) 마치 부두에 대듯 그 괴물 쪽으로 기울어져 있고, 보트 한 척은 이 부산한 현장에서 황급히 밀려 나와 저 멀리 있는 고래들을 향해 막 추격에 나서려는 참이다. 작살과 창들은 곧장 쓸 수 있도록 가지런히 누워 있고, 세 사공이 마침 돛대를 그 구멍에 세우고 있는 동안, 갑자기 바다가 너울치는 바람에 그 작은 배는 마치 앞발을 치켜든 말처럼 물 밖으로 반쯤 곧추서 있다. 배 쪽에서는 끓는 고래의 고통에서 피어오르는 연기가 마치 대장간 마을 위로 솟는 연기처럼 올라가고 있고, 바람 불어오는 쪽에서는 검은 구름이 돌풍과 비를 예고하며 피어올라, 흥분한 선원들의 분주함을 한층 재촉하는 듯하다.\n\n런던 부두로 내려가는 길목인 타워힐에서, 자기 앞에 그림 한 폭을 그려 넣은 판자를 받쳐 든 채 서 있는 불구의 거지(선원들 말로는 케저)를 본 적이 있을 것이다. 그 판자에는 그가 다리를 잃었던 비극적인 장면이 그려져 있다. 고래 세 마리와 보트 세 척이 있고, 그중 한 척의 보트(그 안에 잃어버린 다리가 본래의 온전한 모습 그대로 들어 있다고 추정되는)가 맨 앞 고래의 턱에 으스러지고 있다. 사람들이 내게 일러 준 바로는, 지난 십 년 동안 줄곧 그 사내는 그 그림을 치켜들고, 믿으려 들지 않는 세상을 향해 그 잘린 다리를 내보여 왔다고 한다. 그러나 이제 그의 정당성이 입증될 때가 왔다. 그의 고래 세 마리는 적어도 워핑(Wapping)에서 그동안 세상에 내놓은 어떤 고래 못지않게 훌륭한 고래이며, 그의 잘린 다리 또한 서부 개척지에서 보게 될 어떤 그루터기 다리만큼이나 의심할 여지 없는 그루터기다. 그러나, 그 그루터기 위에 영영 올라서 있으면서도, 이 가련한 고래잡이는 결코 그루터기 연설 따위는 하지 않는다. 다만 눈을 내리깐 채, 서글피 제 절단된 다리를 들여다보며 서 있을 뿐이다.\n\n태평양 전역에서, 그리고 낸터킷(Nantucket)과 뉴베드퍼드와 새그하버에서도, 어부들이 손수 향유고래의 이빨에 새겨 넣은 고래와 포경 장면의 생생한 그림들을 마주치게 될 것이다. 또 참고래의 뼈로 깎아 만든 부인용 코르셋 살대며, 그 밖에 비슷한 스크림샌더 세공품들도 보게 되는데, 이는 고래잡이들이 망망대해에서 한가로운 시간을 보낼 적에 거친 재료를 정성껏 깎아 만들어 내는 그 수많은 자그마하고 정교한 물건들을 일컫는 그들 나름의 말이다. 그들 중 더러는 치과 기구처럼 생긴 자그마한 연장 상자를 갖추고 있는데, 오로지 이 스크림샌더 일을 위해 마련한 것이다. 그러나 대체로는 잭나이프 하나만 가지고 작업하며, 선원이 지닌 그 거의 만능에 가까운 연장 하나로, 뱃사람의 상상이 닿는 것이라면 그 무엇이든 원하는 대로 척척 만들어 낸다.\n\n기독교 세계와 문명으로부터 오래 떠나 있다 보면, 사람은 어쩔 수 없이 하느님께서 처음 그를 두셨던 그 상태, 곧 야만이라 불리는 상태로 되돌아가게 마련이다. 진정한 고래 사냥꾼은 이로쿼이족 못지않은 야만인이다. 나 자신도 야만인이어서, 식인종의 왕에게밖에는 그 어떤 충성도 바치지 않으며, 언제라도 그에게 반기를 들 채비가 되어 있다.\n\n그런데 집안에서 시간을 보낼 적에 야만인이 보이는 독특한 특징 가운데 하나는, 그 일에 쏟는 놀라운 인내심이다. 고대 하와이의 전투용 곤봉이나 창 모양의 노는, 그 새김의 온갖 복잡함과 정교함을 따져 보면, 라틴어 사전에 견줄 만한 인간 끈기의 위대한 전리품이다. 왜냐하면 깨진 조개껍데기 한 조각이나 상어 이빨 하나만 가지고, 그 기적 같은 정교한 나무 그물 세공을 이루어 냈으며, 그것은 한결같은 정성을 쏟은 여러 해의 세월을 들인 끝에 얻어진 것이기 때문이다.\n\n하와이의 야만인이 그러하듯, 백인 선원 야만인 역시 마찬가지다. 똑같은 경이로운 인내심으로, 그리고 자신의 보잘것없는 잭나이프 하나에 달린 똑같은 상어 이빨 한 점으로, 그는 당신을 위해 뼈 조각 한 점을 깎아 낼 텐데, 솜씨야 그만 못할지언정 그 무늬의 어지러운 복잡함만큼은 저 그리스의 야만인 아킬레우스의 방패에 못지않게 빽빽이 들어차 있고, 또 저 노련한 네덜란드의 야만인 알브레히트 뒤러의 판화처럼 야만적인 기백과 암시로 가득 차 있다.\n\n나무로 깎은 고래나, 고귀한 남양산 전투용 목재의 작고 검은 판자를 옆모습으로 깎아 낸 고래는 미국 포경선의 선원실에서 흔히 마주칠 수 있다. 그중 더러는 상당히 정확하게 만들어져 있다.\n\n박공지붕을 인 어느 오래된 시골집들에서는, 길가로 난 문에 노커로 꼬리를 매달아 둔 놋쇠 고래를 보게 될 것이다. 문지기가 졸음에 겨워할 때라면, 모루 머리를 한 고래가 가장 안성맞춤일 것이다. 그러나 이렇게 문을 두드리는 고래들이 충실한 작품으로서 눈에 띌 만한 경우는 드물다. 구식 교회의 몇몇 첨탑에서는 풍향계로 거기 얹어 둔 양철 고래를 보게 될 텐데, 워낙 높이 매달려 있는 데다 게다가 어느 모로 보나 \"손대지 마시오!\"라는 딱지를 붙여 둔 셈이라, 그 솜씨의 우열을 가늠할 만큼 가까이서 살펴볼 수는 없다.\n\n뼈가 드러나고 갈빗대 같은 능선이 펼쳐진 땅, 곧 높고 부서진 절벽 기슭에 바위 덩어리들이 기묘한 무리를 이루어 들판 위에 흩어져 있는 곳에서는, 풀밭에 절반쯤 잠긴 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 석화된 형상 같은 모습들을 종종 발견하게 될 것이다. 바람 부는 날이면 풀밭은 초록 너울의 물보라처럼 그 형상들에 부딪쳐 부서진다.\n\n그런가 하면 또, 나그네가 원형극장처럼 둘러선 높은 봉우리들에 끊임없이 에워싸이는 산악 지대에서는, 운 좋은 어느 시점에서 그 굽이치는 능선을 따라 윤곽이 잡힌 고래의 옆모습을 언뜻언뜻 스쳐 보게 될 것이다. 그러나 이런 광경을 보려면 당신은 철저한 고래잡이여야만 한다. 그뿐 아니라, 그러한 광경을 다시 보고자 한다면, 처음 섰던 그 자리의 정확한 위도와 경도가 교차하는 지점을 반드시 알아 두어야 한다. 그러지 않으면 이러한 산봉우리 관찰이란 워낙 우연에 좌우되는 것이어서, 정확히 앞서 섰던 그 자리를 다시 찾아내자면 고된 재발견의 수고를 치러야 할 것이기 때문이다. 마치 솔로마 제도가, 한때 높은 주름깃을 단 멘다냐가 그 땅을 밟고 늙은 피게로아가 그 기록을 남겼음에도 여태껏 미지의 섬으로 남아 있는 것과도 같다.\n\n또한 당신의 주제에 마음이 한껏 부풀어 들려 올라갈 때라면, 별이 총총한 하늘에서 거대한 고래들과 그것들을 뒤쫓는 보트들의 자취를 더듬어 내지 않을 수 없으리라. 마치 오래도록 전쟁 생각에 잠겨 있던 동방의 여러 민족이 구름 사이에서 맞붙어 싸우는 군대를 보았던 것처럼 말이다. 그리하여 나는 북쪽에서, 처음 내게 그 윤곽을 일러 준 밝은 별들의 회전을 좇아, 북극을 돌고 또 돌며 리바이어던을 뒤쫓았다. 또 눈부신 남극의 하늘 아래서는 아르고자리에 올라타, 물뱀자리와 날치자리가 닿는 가장 먼 끝조차 한참 넘어선 곳에서 별이 빛나는 고래자리를 뒤쫓는 추격에 가담하였다.\n\n군함의 닻을 재갈로 삼고 작살 묶음을 박차로 삼아, 저 고래에 올라타 가장 높은 하늘로 뛰어오를 수 있다면 얼마나 좋으랴. 그리하여 전설 속의 하늘이, 그 헤아릴 수 없는 천막들을 모조리 거느린 채, 정녕 내 인간의 시야 너머에 진을 치고 있는지 두 눈으로 확인할 수 있다면!\n\n# CHAPTER 58. Brit.\n\n크로제트 제도에서 북동쪽으로 키를 잡고 나아가던 우리는, 참고래(Right Whale)가 주로 먹고 사는 미세하고 누런 물질인 브릿(brit)의 광활한 초원과 마주쳤다. 그것은 몇 리그를 가도 다시 몇 리그를 가도 우리 주위로 물결치고 있어서, 마치 익어 황금빛으로 물든 끝없는 밀밭 사이를 항해하는 듯했다.\n\n이튿날 수많은 참고래가 모습을 드러냈는데, 피쿼드(Pequod)호 같은 향유고래잡이배의 공격으로부터 안전한 그놈들은 입을 벌린 채 느릿느릿 브릿 사이를 헤엄쳤다. 브릿은 그 입속에 달린 경이로운 베네치아식 발 같은 술 모양 섬유에 들러붙었고, 그렇게 해서 입술 사이로 빠져나가는 물과 걸러졌다.\n\n마치 아침의 풀 베는 일꾼들이 어깨를 나란히 한 채 늪지 풀밭의 길고 축축한 풀 사이로 천천히, 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 낫을 밀어 나가듯이, 이 괴물들도 그렇게 헤엄치며 기묘하고 풀 베는 듯한 절단음을 냈다. 그러고는 누런 바다 위에 끝없이 푸른 자국을 베어 남겼다.*\n\n* 고래잡이들 사이에서 \"브라질 둑(Brazil Banks)\"이라 알려진 그 해역은, 뉴펀들랜드 둑처럼 그곳에 얕은 여울과 측심 가능한 바닥이 있어서 그런 이름이 붙은 것이 아니라, 참고래가 자주 쫓기는 그 위도에서 끊임없이 떠다니는 광대한 브릿 무리가 자아내는 이 두드러진 초원 같은 광경 때문에 그렇게 불린다.\n\n그러나 풀 베는 일꾼을 떠올리게 하는 것은 오직 그놈들이 브릿을 가를 때 내는 소리뿐이었다. 돛대 꼭대기에서 내려다보면, 특히 그놈들이 잠시 멈춰 가만히 머물러 있을 때면, 그 거대하고 검은 형체는 그 무엇보다도 생명 없는 바위 덩어리처럼 보였다. 인도의 저 광대한 수렵지에서 멀리서 온 나그네가 들판에 누워 있는 코끼리들을 그것인 줄도 모르고 그저 헐벗고 거뭇한 흙더미로 여기며 지나치곤 하듯이, 이 바다의 리바이어던(Leviathan) 종족을 처음 보는 사람도 흔히 그러하다. 그리고 마침내 그것이 무엇인지 알아본 뒤에도, 그 어마어마한 크기 때문에 이토록 거대하게 자란 살덩이가 그 온몸 구석구석에 개나 말에게 깃든 것과 같은 생명으로 살아 숨 쉬고 있다는 사실을 정말로 믿기란 무척 어렵다.\n\n실로 다른 점에서도, 깊은 바다의 어떤 생물도 뭍의 생물을 대하는 것과 같은 감정으로 바라보기란 좀처럼 어렵다. 비록 몇몇 옛 박물학자들이 뭍의 모든 생물에는 바다에 그에 상응하는 종류가 있다고 주장해 왔고, 또 이 문제를 폭넓고 일반적인 관점에서 본다면 충분히 그럴 법도 하지만, 막상 세부로 들어가 보면, 이를테면 바다가 그 기질에 있어 개의 영리한 다정함에 견줄 만한 물고기를 어디 내놓는단 말인가? 오직 저주받은 상어만이 어떤 일반적인 면에서나마 개에 비교될 만한 유사성을 지녔다고 말할 수 있을 따름이다.\n\n그러나 비록 일반적으로 뭍사람들에게 바다의 토박이 거주자들이 형언할 수 없으리만치 비사교적이고 거부감을 주는 감정으로 여겨져 왔고, 또 우리가 바다를 영원히 알 수 없는 미지의 땅으로 알고 있어 콜럼버스가 자신의 그 하나의 피상적인 서쪽 세계를 발견하려고 무수한 미지의 세계 위를 항해했으며, 또 헤아릴 수 없이 큰 차이로 모든 인간이 겪는 재난 가운데 가장 무시무시한 것들이 아득한 옛날부터 바다로 나선 수십만 수백만의 사람들에게 가리지 않고 닥쳐왔으며, 또 잠시만 생각해 보아도 어린아이 같은 인간이 제 과학과 기술을 아무리 뽐낸들, 또 흐뭇한 미래에 그 과학과 기술이 아무리 늘어난들, 영원토록, 세상이 끝나는 날까지 바다는 인간을 모욕하고 살해할 것이며 그가 만들 수 있는 가장 위풍당당하고 견고한 군함조차 가루로 빻아 버리리라는 것을 깨닫게 되건만, 그럼에도 불구하고 바로 이런 인상들이 끊임없이 되풀이된 탓에 인간은 본디 바다에 깃들어 있던 그 온전한 두려움의 감각을 잃어버리고 말았다.\n\n우리가 글에서 읽는 최초의 배는, 포르투갈인의 복수처럼 한 세상 전부를 삼켜 과부 하나조차 남기지 않은 그 대양 위에 떠 있었다. 그와 똑같은 대양이 지금도 출렁이고 있다. 그와 똑같은 대양이 지난해의 난파선들을 파괴했다. 그렇다, 어리석은 인간들이여, 노아의 홍수는 아직 가라앉지 않았다. 그것은 아직도 이 아름다운 세상의 삼분의 이를 뒤덮고 있다.\n\n바다와 뭍이 무엇이 다르기에, 한쪽에서 일어난 기적이 다른 쪽에서는 기적이 아니란 말인가? 코라(Korah)와 그 무리의 발밑에서 산 땅이 갈라져 그들을 영영 삼켜 버렸을 때, 초자연적인 공포가 히브리인들 위에 내려앉았다. 그러나 오늘날의 해는 단 한 번도 그와 똑같은 방식으로 산 바다가 배와 선원들을 삼켜 버리지 않는 날에 지는 법이 없다.\n\n그러나 바다는 자신에게 낯선 인간에게만 그런 적인 것이 아니라, 제가 낳은 새끼들에게도 마귀와 같다. 제 손님들을 살해한 페르시아의 군주보다도 더 고약하게, 제 스스로 낳아 놓은 생물들조차 가만두지 않는다. 정글에서 몸을 뒤척이다 제 새끼들을 깔아뭉개는 사나운 암호랑이처럼, 바다는 가장 힘센 고래들조차 바위에 내동댕이쳐서 갈라진 난파선 잔해와 나란히 거기 버려둔다. 그 어떤 자비도, 그 자신 말고는 그 어떤 힘도 바다를 다스리지 못한다. 제 기수를 잃은 미쳐 날뛰는 군마처럼 헐떡이고 콧김을 내뿜으며, 주인 없는 대양이 온 지구를 짓밟고 다닌다.\n\n바다의 교활함을 생각해 보라. 그 가장 무서운 생물들이 대개는 보이지도 않게 물속을 미끄러져 다니며, 더없이 아름다운 쪽빛의 빛깔 아래 음흉하게 몸을 숨기고 있음을. 또한 그 가장 무자비한 무리들 가운데 많은 것들이 지닌, 여러 종의 상어가 보여 주는 우아하게 꾸민 자태처럼 악마적인 광채와 아름다움을 생각해 보라. 다시 한번, 바다의 보편적인 동족상잔을 생각해 보라. 그 모든 생물이 서로를 잡아먹으며 세상이 시작된 이래로 영원한 전쟁을 이어 오고 있음을.\n\n이 모든 것을 생각해 보라. 그런 다음 이 푸르고 온화하며 더없이 순한 땅으로 눈을 돌려 보라. 바다와 뭍, 그 둘을 함께 생각해 보라. 그러면 그대 자신 안의 무언가와 닮은 기묘한 유사성을 발견하지 않겠는가? 이 소름 끼치는 대양이 푸르른 땅을 에워싸고 있듯이, 인간의 영혼 속에도 평화와 기쁨으로 가득 찬 하나의 외딴 타히티 섬이 자리하고 있되, 그것은 절반밖에 알려지지 않은 삶의 온갖 공포에 둘러싸여 있으니. 하느님(God)께서 그대를 지켜 주시기를! 그 섬에서 배를 띄워 떠나지 말라, 그대는 결코 돌아오지 못하리니!\n\n# CHAPTER 59. Squid.\n\n천천히 브릿(brit)의 초원을 헤치고 나아가며, 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 여전히 자바섬을 향해 북동쪽으로 항로를 잡고 있었다. 부드러운 바람이 용골을 밀어 주어, 사방의 고요 속에서 가늘게 뻗어 오른 세 개의 높다란 돛대가 그 나른한 미풍에 맞추어 평원에 선 세 그루의 온순한 야자수처럼 잔잔히 흔들렸다. 그리고 여전히, 은빛으로 빛나는 밤이면 넓은 간격을 두고서 외롭고도 사람을 홀리는 물줄기가 보이곤 했다.\n\n그러나 어느 투명하고 푸른 아침, 거의 초자연적이라 할 만한 정적이 바다 위에 깔렸으나 그렇다고 무거운 무풍의 정체는 아니었던 그때, 수면 위로 길게 늘어선 윤나는 햇살의 띠가 마치 어떤 비밀을 명하듯 바다를 가로질러 놓인 황금 손가락처럼 보였던 그때, 부드럽게 미끄러져 가는 물결들이 사뿐히 흐르며 서로 속삭이던 그때, 눈에 보이는 천지의 이 깊은 침묵 속에서 대구(Daggoo)가 큰 돛대 꼭대기에서 기이한 유령을 보았다.\n\n저 멀리, 거대한 흰 덩어리가 나른하게 솟아오르더니, 점점 더 높이 올라와 짙푸른 바다에서 제 몸을 풀어내며, 마침내 우리 뱃머리 앞에서 언덕에서 갓 미끄러져 내린 눈사태처럼 번쩍였다. 그렇게 한순간 번득이다가, 천천히 가라앉으며 물속으로 잠겨 들었다. 그러더니 다시 한번 떠올라 소리 없이 빛났다. 그것은 고래 같지 않았다. 하지만 이것이 모비 딕(Moby Dick)인가? 대구는 생각했다. 다시금 그 유령은 가라앉았으나, 또 한 번 떠오르자, 단검처럼 날카로운 외침으로 졸고 있던 모든 사람을 화들짝 깨우며 그 흑인은 고함쳤다. \"저기! 저기 또! 솟구쳐 오른다! 바로 앞이다! 흰 고래다, 흰 고래야!\"\n\n이 말에 선원들은, 분봉 철에 벌들이 가지로 몰려들듯, 활대 끝으로 우르르 달려갔다. 무더운 햇볕 아래 모자도 쓰지 않은 채, 에이해브(Ahab)는 제1사장(斜檣) 위에 서서, 키잡이에게 명령을 흔들어 전할 채비로 한 손을 등 뒤로 한껏 젖힌 채, 위쪽에서 대구가 쭉 뻗은 미동 없는 팔로 가리킨 방향으로 간절한 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n홀로 외롭게 떠다니며 따라붙던 그 잔잔한 물줄기가 차츰 에이해브의 마음에 작용을 일으켜, 이제 그가 자신이 쫓는 바로 그 고래를 처음 보는 순간에 온순함과 안식이라는 관념을 결부시킬 채비가 되어 있었던 것인지, 그것이 어떠했든 간에, 혹은 그의 조급함이 그를 속였던 것인지, 그 어느 쪽이었든 간에, 그가 그 흰 덩어리를 또렷이 알아보자마자, 그는 즉시 격렬한 기세로 보트를 내리라는 명령을 내렸다.\n\n네 척의 보트가 곧 물 위에 떴다. 에이해브의 것이 앞장섰고, 모두가 잽싸게 먹잇감을 향해 노를 저었다. 곧 그것이 가라앉았고, 우리가 노를 멈춘 채 그것이 다시 나타나기를 기다리고 있는데, 보라! 그것이 잠겼던 바로 그 자리에서, 또 한 번 천천히 솟아올랐다. 한순간 모비 딕에 관한 모든 생각조차 거의 잊어버린 채, 우리는 이제 비밀의 바다가 여태껏 인류에게 드러내 보인 가장 경이로운 현상을 응시했다. 길이와 너비가 몇 펄롱(furlong)에 이르는, 반짝이는 크림빛의 거대한 물컹한 덩어리가 수면 위에 둥둥 떠 있었고, 그 중심에서 수없이 많은 긴 팔들이 사방으로 뻗어 나와, 마치 손에 닿는 어떤 가엾은 것이든 무턱대고 움켜쥐려는 듯, 한 무리의 아나콘다처럼 휘감기고 비틀렸다. 알아볼 만한 얼굴도 앞면도 없었고, 감각이나 본능이라 할 만한 어떤 짐작 가는 표시도 없었으며, 다만 거기 물결 위에서 일렁이고 있을 뿐이었으니, 이 세상 것 같지 않고 형체 없으며 우연처럼 나타난 생명의 환영이었다.\n\n낮게 빨아들이는 듯한 소리를 내며 그것이 다시금 천천히 사라지자, 스타벅(Starbuck)은 그것이 가라앉은 곳의 일렁이는 물을 여전히 바라보며 거친 목소리로 외쳤다. \"차라리 모비 딕을 보고 그놈과 싸우는 편이, 너를 보는 것보다 나았을 것이다, 이 흰 유령아!\"\n\n\"그게 뭐였습니까, 선장님?\" 플래스크(Flask)가 물었다.\n\n\"저 거대한 살아 있는 오징어다. 사람들 말로는, 그것을 보고도 항구로 돌아와 그 이야기를 전한 고래잡이배가 몇 척 없다더군.\"\n\n그러나 에이해브는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 보트를 돌려 그는 다시 본선으로 돌아갔고, 나머지도 마찬가지로 말없이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n향유고래(Sperm) 잡이들이 일반적으로 이 물체를 본 일에 어떤 미신들을 결부시켜 왔든 간에, 분명한 것은 그것을 언뜻 보는 일조차 너무도 드문지라, 그 사정이 그것을 불길한 전조로 둘러싸는 데 크게 한몫했다는 점이다. 그것이 워낙 드물게 목격되는 까닭에, 그들 모두가 한결같이 그것을 바다에서 가장 거대한 살아 있는 것이라 단언하면서도, 그것의 참된 본성과 형체에 관해서는 더없이 막연한 관념밖에 지니지 못한 자가 대부분이다. 그럼에도 그들은 그것이 향유고래에게 유일한 먹이를 대 준다고 믿는다. 다른 종류의 고래들은 수면 위에서 먹이를 찾으므로 사람이 그것들이 먹이를 먹는 모습을 볼 수 있지만, 향유고래는 그 먹이를 모조리 수면 아래 알려지지 않은 영역에서 구하기에, 그 먹이가 정확히 무엇으로 이루어졌는지는 오로지 추론으로만 누군가 말할 수 있을 따름이다. 때때로 바싹 쫓기면 그놈은 오징어의 떨어져 나온 팔로 짐작되는 것들을 토해 내는데, 그렇게 드러난 것들 가운데 더러는 길이가 20피트, 30피트(약 6미터, 9미터)를 넘는 것도 있다. 그들은 이 팔들이 달린 괴물이 평소 그 팔들로 바다 밑바닥에 들러붙어 있으며, 향유고래는 다른 종들과 달리 그것을 공격해 찢어발기기 위해 이빨을 갖추고 있는 것이라 짐작한다.\n\n폰토피단(Pontoppodan) 주교가 말한 저 거대한 크라켄(Kraken)이 끝내 오징어로 귀결되리라 상상할 만한 어떤 근거가 있는 듯하다. 주교가 그것을 번갈아 떠올랐다 가라앉았다 하는 것으로 묘사한 방식이며, 그가 서술한 그 밖의 몇몇 세부 사항이며, 이 모든 점에서 둘은 들어맞는다. 다만 그가 그것에 부여한 믿기 어려운 거구에 관해서는 상당한 에누리가 필요하다.\n\n여기서 말한 이 신비로운 생물에 관한 소문을 어렴풋이 전해 들은 어떤 박물학자들은 그것을 갑오징어 부류에 포함시키는데, 과연 어떤 외형적 면에서는 거기에 속하는 듯이 보이기는 하나, 그저 그 종족의 아낙(Anak)으로서일 뿐이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 60. The Line.\n\n곧 묘사하게 될 포경 장면과 관련하여, 또한 다른 곳에서 펼쳐질 그와 비슷한 모든 장면을 더 잘 이해할 수 있도록, 나는 여기서 그 마법 같고 때로는 끔찍한 고래 밧줄에 관해 이야기해 두어야겠다.\n\n포경업에서 본래 쓰이던 밧줄은 최상품 삼(麻)으로 만든 것으로, 보통 밧줄처럼 타르를 흠뻑 먹인 것이 아니라 살짝 김을 쐬듯 발라 둔 정도였다. 타르를 통상적으로 쓰면 밧줄 제작자에게는 삼이 더 잘 휘어지게 해 주고, 또 선원이 배에서 일상적으로 쓰기에도 더 편리하게 해 주지만, 통상적인 분량을 쓰면 고래 밧줄이 너무 뻣뻣해져서, 그것이 반드시 거쳐야 하는 빽빽한 사리기에 부적합해질 뿐 아니라, 대다수 선원들이 차츰 깨닫기 시작하듯이, 타르는 밧줄에 단단함과 윤기를 더해 줄지언정 그 내구성이나 강도를 높여 주는 일은 결코 없기 때문이다.\n\n근래에 들어 미국 포경업에서는 마닐라 밧줄이 고래 밧줄의 재료로서 삼을 거의 완전히 대신하게 되었다. 마닐라 밧줄은 삼만큼 오래가지는 못하지만 더 튼튼하고, 훨씬 더 부드럽고 탄력이 있기 때문이다. 게다가 (모든 것에는 나름의 미학이 깃들어 있으니) 한마디 덧붙이자면, 그것은 삼보다 훨씬 보기 좋고 보트에도 잘 어울린다. 삼은 거무튀튀하고 어두운 친구로 일종의 인디언 같다면, 마닐라는 보기에 금발의 체르케스 여인 같다.\n\n고래 밧줄의 굵기는 겨우 3분의 2인치에 지나지 않는다. 언뜻 보아서는 그것이 실제만큼 그렇게 튼튼하리라 여기지 못할 것이다. 실험에 따르면, 그것을 이루는 쉰한 가닥의 실은 저마다 120파운드의 무게를 견뎌 내니, 밧줄 전체로는 거의 3톤에 맞먹는 장력을 버틸 수 있다. 길이로 말하자면, 보통의 향유고래 밧줄은 200패덤 남짓 된다. 보트의 고물 쪽에서 그것은 통 속에 나선형으로 사려져 있는데, 증류기의 나선관처럼 감겨 있는 것이 아니라, 빽빽하게 포개진 \"가닥들\", 곧 동심원을 그리며 나선으로 쌓인 층들이 둥근 치즈 모양의 한 덩어리를 이루도록 사려져 있으며, 그 한가운데에는 치즈의 축에 해당하는 자리에 생긴 \"심장\", 곧 작고 수직으로 뚫린 관 외에는 빈 곳이 없다. 사려 놓은 밧줄에 조금이라도 엉킴이나 꼬임이 있으면, 밧줄이 풀려 나갈 때 누군가의 팔이나 다리, 혹은 온몸을 영락없이 앗아 가 버릴 것이므로, 통 속에 밧줄을 갈무리할 때는 더없이 세심한 주의를 기울인다. 어떤 작살잡이들은 이 일에 거의 한나절을 다 쓰기도 하는데, 밧줄을 높이 들어 올린 다음 도르래를 통해 아래쪽 통을 향해 늘어뜨려, 사리는 과정에서 일어날 수 있는 모든 주름과 뒤틀림을 없애는 것이다.\n\n영국 보트에서는 통을 하나가 아니라 둘을 쓰는데, 같은 밧줄을 두 통에 연이어 사려 둔다. 여기에는 어느 정도 이점이 있다. 이 쌍둥이 통은 워낙 작아서 보트에 더 수월하게 들어맞고, 보트에 무리를 덜 주기 때문이다. 반면 미국식 통은 지름이 거의 3피트에 깊이도 그에 걸맞아서, 널빤지 두께가 겨우 반 인치밖에 안 되는 작은 배에는 제법 부피가 큰 짐이 된다. 고래잡이 보트의 밑바닥은 살얼음과도 같아서, 고루 분산된 무게라면 상당량을 떠받칠 수 있어도 한곳에 집중된 무게는 그리 많이 견디지 못하기 때문이다. 미국식 밧줄 통에 칠한 캔버스 덮개를 씌워 놓으면, 보트는 마치 고래들에게 선사하려고 어마어마하게 큰 결혼식 케이크를 싣고 노 저어 나가는 것처럼 보인다.\n\n밧줄의 양쪽 끝은 모두 밖으로 드러나 있다. 아래쪽 끝은 고리매듭, 곧 통 바닥에서부터 옆면을 타고 올라와 가장자리에 걸쳐진 채 그 무엇에도 매이지 않고 늘어진 올가미 모양으로 끝난다. 아래쪽 끝을 이렇게 해 두는 데에는 두 가지 이유가 있다. 첫째, 작살 맞은 고래가 너무 깊이 잠수하여, 본래 작살에 매여 있던 밧줄 전체를 끌고 가 버릴 위험이 생길 경우, 이웃한 보트에서 가져온 여분의 밧줄을 거기에 손쉽게 잇기 위함이다. 이런 경우 고래는, 말하자면 맥주 한 잔이 옮겨 가듯 한 보트에서 다른 보트로 넘겨지는데, 그래도 처음 보트는 늘 곁에 머물며 짝패 보트를 돕는다. 둘째, 이런 처리는 공동의 안전을 위해 없어서는 안 된다. 밧줄의 아래쪽 끝이 어떤 식으로든 보트에 매여 있다면, 그리고 고래가 이따금 그러하듯 김을 내뿜으며 단 한 순간 만에 밧줄을 끝까지 풀어 내 버린다면, 고래는 거기서 멈추지 않을 것이고, 운명 지어진 그 보트는 영락없이 고래를 따라 바다 깊은 심연 속으로 끌려 내려갈 것이며, 그렇게 되면 그 어떤 마을 포고꾼도 그 보트를 두 번 다시 찾아내지 못할 것이기 때문이다.\n\n추격을 위해 보트를 내리기 전에, 밧줄의 위쪽 끝을 통에서 빼내어 고물 쪽으로 가져가, 거기 있는 밧줄걸이 기둥을 한 바퀴 돌린 다음, 다시 보트 전체 길이만큼 앞으로 끌어가는데, 모든 사람의 노 손잡이 위에 가로질러 얹혀, 노를 저을 때마다 그의 손목에 부딪치게 된다. 또한 양쪽 뱃전에 번갈아 앉은 사람들 사이를 지나, 보트의 뾰족한 이물 맨 끝에 있는 납을 댄 홈 받침에 이르는데, 거기서는 보통 깃펜만 한 굵기의 나무못이나 꼬챙이가 밧줄이 빠져나가지 못하게 막아 준다. 그 받침에서 밧줄은 이물 위로 살짝 늘어진 곡선을 그리며 걸쳐졌다가 다시 보트 안쪽으로 들어오고, 그중 열 패덤 내지 스무 패덤가량(상자 밧줄이라 불린다)이 이물의 상자 위에 사려진 다음, 조금 더 고물 쪽 뱃전을 향해 이어지다가 거기서 짧은 줄, 곧 작살과 곧바로 연결되는 밧줄에 매인다. 다만 그렇게 연결되기에 앞서, 짧은 줄은 일일이 늘어놓기에는 너무 번거로운 갖가지 복잡한 처리 과정을 거친다.\n\n이리하여 고래 밧줄은 그 얽히고설킨 사림으로 보트 전체를 감싸 안고, 거의 모든 방향에서 보트를 휘감으며 뒤틀린다. 모든 노잡이가 그 위태로운 뒤틀림 속에 휘말려 들어, 뭍사람의 겁먹은 눈에는 그들이 마치 가장 치명적인 뱀들을 제 팔다리에 장난스레 늘어뜨린 인디언 곡예사들처럼 보인다. 죽을 운명을 타고난 어떤 여인의 아들이든, 처음으로 그 삼 밧줄의 미궁 한복판에 앉아 노에 온 힘을 쏟는 가운데, 언제 어느 순간에 작살이 던져지고 이 끔찍한 뒤틀림들이 동그라미 친 번개처럼 한꺼번에 작동하기 시작할지 모른다는 생각에 잠겨 보라. 그런 처지에 놓이고서 어찌 몸서리를 치지 않을 수 있겠는가. 그 몸서리는 뼛속 골수마저 흔들린 젤리처럼 떨리게 만든다. 그러나 습관이란 — 참으로 기이한 것이다! 습관이 이루어 내지 못하는 일이 어디 있으랴! — 그대가 마호가니 식탁에 둘러앉아 들어 본 그 어떤 것보다도, 이렇게 교수형 올가미를 두른 채 반 인치짜리 흰 삼나무 보트에 매달려 있을 때, 더 흥겨운 농지거리와 더 명랑한 웃음, 더 재미난 농담과 더 재치 있는 응수를 듣게 될 것이다. 그리하여, 에드워드 왕 앞에 선 칼레의 여섯 시민처럼, 보트의 선원을 이루는 여섯 사내는 말하자면 저마다 목에 올가미를 두른 채로 죽음의 아가리 속을 향해 노를 저어 들어가는 것이다.\n\n이제 조금만 생각해 보면, 그대는 저 거듭되는 포경 참사들 — 그중 몇몇은 우연히 기록으로 남았는데, 이 사람 혹은 저 사람이 밧줄에 끌려 보트 밖으로 내동댕이쳐져 목숨을 잃었다는 이야기들 — 을 능히 헤아릴 수 있을 것이다. 밧줄이 쏜살같이 풀려 나가는 동안 보트에 앉아 있다는 것은, 모든 날아다니는 들보와 축과 바퀴가 그대를 스치며 돌아가는, 한창 가동 중인 증기기관의 갖가지 윙윙거림 한복판에 앉아 있는 것과 같다. 아니, 그보다 더하다. 보트가 요람처럼 흔들리는 통에 그대는 이 위험들의 한가운데에서 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있을 수가 없고, 아무런 예고도 없이 이쪽저쪽으로 내던져지며, 오직 일종의 스스로를 가다듬는 균형 감각과, 의지와 행동의 동시성에 의해서만, 마제파 신세가 되어 그 모든 것을 굽어보는 태양조차 결코 그대를 찾아낼 수 없는 곳으로 끌려가는 일을 면할 수 있을 따름이다.\n\n또한, 폭풍에 겉으로만 앞서며 그것을 예고하는 듯한 그 깊은 고요가 어쩌면 폭풍 그 자체보다 더 무서운 것이듯 — 실로 그 고요란 폭풍을 감싼 포장지요 봉투에 지나지 않으며, 겉으로 무해해 보이는 소총이 치명적인 화약과 탄알과 폭발을 품고 있듯, 그 고요는 폭풍을 제 안에 품고 있으니 — 그와 마찬가지로, 밧줄이 실제로 작동하기 전에 노잡이들 둘레를 소리 없이 뱀처럼 휘감으며 짓는 그 우아한 정적, 이것이야말로 이 위험천만한 일의 그 어떤 면모보다도 더한 진정한 공포를 품고 있다. 그러나 더 말해 무엇 하랴? 모든 인간은 고래 밧줄에 감싸인 채 살아간다. 모두가 목에 올가미를 두른 채 태어난다. 다만 죽음의 그 빠르고 갑작스러운 회전에 사로잡힐 때라야, 인간은 비로소 삶의 소리 없고 은밀하며 늘 곁에 있는 위험들을 깨닫는 것이다. 그리고 만일 그대가 철학자라면, 고래잡이 보트에 앉아 있다 한들, 그대 곁에 작살이 아니라 부지깽이를 두고 저녁 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있을 때와 견주어, 마음속으로 느끼는 공포가 터럭만큼도 더하지 않을 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 61. Stubb Kills a Whale.\n\n스타벅에게 오징어의 출현이 흉조였다면, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)에게 그것은 전혀 다른 의미를 지닌 것이었다.\n\n\"오징어 보이면,\" 그 야만인은 끌어 올린 보트 뱃머리에서 작살을 갈며 말했다. \"곧 향유고래 본다, 빨리.\"\n\n이튿날은 유난히 고요하고 무더웠다. 딱히 할 일이 없었던 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 선원들은 그토록 텅 빈 바다가 자아내는 졸음의 마력을 좀처럼 떨쳐 낼 수 없었다. 우리가 그때 항해하고 있던 인도양의 이 해역은 고래잡이들이 이른바 활기찬 어장이라 부르는 곳이 아니었다. 다시 말해, 리오데라플라타 앞바다나 페루 연안 어장에 비해, 더 출렁이는 바다에 사는 쇠돌고래나 돌고래, 날치, 그 밖의 활달한 주민들을 볼 기회가 훨씬 드문 곳이었다.\n\n앞돛대 꼭대기에 올라가 망을 볼 차례가 내게 돌아왔다. 나는 느슨하게 풀린 윗돛 받침줄에 어깨를 기댄 채, 마법에 걸린 듯한 대기 속에서 멍하니 앞뒤로 흔들거렸다. 그 어떤 결심으로도 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 없었다. 그 몽롱한 기분 속에서 나는 의식을 모두 잃었고, 마침내 내 영혼이 육체를 빠져나갔다. 하지만 내 육체는, 처음 그것을 움직인 힘이 거두어진 지 한참이 지난 뒤에도 진자처럼 계속 흔들리고 있었다.\n\n망각이 완전히 나를 덮치기 전에, 나는 큰돛대와 뒷돛대 꼭대기의 선원들이 이미 졸고 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 그리하여 마침내 우리 셋은 모두 활대에 매달려 생기 없이 흔들렸고, 우리가 한 번 흔들릴 때마다 저 아래에서 졸고 있던 키잡이의 고갯짓이 화답했다. 파도 또한 그 나른한 물마루를 끄덕였고, 바다의 드넓은 혼곤함을 가로질러 동쪽이 서쪽을 향해 끄덕였으며, 그 모든 것 위로 해가 끄덕이고 있었다.\n\n별안간 감은 두 눈 밑에서 거품이 터지는 듯했다. 두 손이 만력기처럼 받침줄을 움켜쥐었다. 보이지 않는 어떤 자비로운 손길이 나를 지켜 주었다. 나는 화들짝 놀라며 생기를 되찾았다. 그리고 보라! 우리 바람 아래쪽, 마흔 길도 채 안 되는 가까운 곳에서, 거대한 향유고래(Sperm Whale) 한 마리가 전복된 프리깃함의 선체처럼 물 위에서 뒹굴고 있었다. 에티오피아인의 피부 같은 빛깔을 띤 그 넓고 윤기 흐르는 등이 햇살을 받아 거울처럼 반짝였다. 하지만 바다의 골을 따라 나른하게 일렁이며 이따금 평온하게 수증기 같은 물줄기를 뿜어 올리는 그 고래는, 따스한 오후에 파이프 담배를 피우는 풍채 좋은 시민처럼 보였다. 그러나 가련한 고래여, 그 파이프가 네 마지막 담배였다. 마법사의 지팡이에라도 맞은 듯, 졸고 있던 배와 그 안에서 잠들어 있던 자들이 일제히 화들짝 깨어났다. 그리고 그 거대한 물고기가 천천히, 규칙적으로 반짝이는 짠물을 허공으로 뿜어 올리는 가운데, 돛대 위에서 들려오는 세 가닥의 외침과 동시에 배의 곳곳에서 스무 명이 넘는 목소리가 익숙한 그 함성을 내질렀다.\n\n\"보트를 내려라! 바람 위로 돌려라!\" 에이해브(Ahab)가 외쳤다. 그러고는 자신이 내린 명령에 스스로 따르듯, 키잡이가 키 자루를 잡을 새도 없이 키를 내리눌렀다.\n\n선원들의 갑작스러운 외침이 고래를 놀라게 한 것이 분명했다. 보트가 내려지기도 전에, 고래는 위풍당당하게 몸을 틀어 바람 아래쪽으로 헤엄쳐 갔다. 하지만 어찌나 한결같이 평온하게, 헤엄치면서 어찌나 잔물결을 적게 일으켰던지, 결국 아직은 놀라지 않았을지도 모른다고 생각한 에이해브는, 노를 단 한 자루도 쓰지 말고 누구든 속삭이는 소리 외에는 입을 열지 말라고 명령했다. 그리하여 우리는 온타리오 인디언처럼 보트의 뱃전에 걸터앉아, 빠르되 소리 없이 노를 저어 나아갔다. 잔잔한 바다라 소리 없는 돛조차 펼 수 없었던 것이다. 이윽고 우리가 그렇게 미끄러지듯 추격하는 동안, 그 괴물이 꼬리를 수직으로 마흔 자 높이까지 휙 치켜올리더니, 이내 삼켜져 버린 탑처럼 시야에서 가라앉았다.\n\n\"꼬리가 들어간다!\" 하는 외침이 터졌고, 이 외침에 곧바로 이어 스텁(Stubb)이 성냥을 꺼내 파이프에 불을 붙였다. 이제 잠시 한숨 돌릴 틈이 주어졌기 때문이다. 고래가 잠수해 있던 시간이 온전히 흐른 뒤, 고래가 다시 떠올랐다. 이제 그놈은 담배 피우는 자의 보트보다 앞쪽에 있었고, 다른 어떤 보트보다도 그 보트에 훨씬 가까웠으므로, 스텁은 포획의 영예를 자기 것이라 셈했다. 이제 고래가 마침내 추격자들의 존재를 알아챘다는 것이 분명해졌다. 따라서 조심스레 지키던 온갖 침묵은 더 이상 쓸모가 없었다. 노 젓는 노들이 내려지고, 본격적인 노들이 요란하게 움직이기 시작했다. 스텁은 여전히 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 부하들에게 돌격을 독려했다.\n\n그렇다, 그 물고기에게 엄청난 변화가 일어났다. 자신이 처한 위기를 온몸으로 깨달은 그놈은 \"머리를 내밀고\" 나아가고 있었다. 다시 말해 자기가 휘저어 일으킨 그 미친 거품 속에서 머리 부분을 비스듬히 내밀고 있었던 것이다.*\n\n* 다른 대목에서 보게 되겠지만, 향유고래의 거대한 머리는 그 내부 전체가 매우 가벼운 물질로 이루어져 있다. 겉보기에는 가장 육중한 부분이지만, 실은 그 몸에서 단연 가장 잘 뜨는 부분이다. 그래서 고래는 손쉽게 그 머리를 공중으로 치켜올리며, 전속력으로 나아갈 때는 반드시 그렇게 한다. 게다가 머리 앞부분 위쪽의 폭이 워낙 넓고 아래쪽이 워낙 뾰족하게 깎인 뱃머리 모양을 이루고 있어서, 머리를 비스듬히 치켜올림으로써 그놈은 뭉툭한 뱃머리의 굼뜬 갈리오트선을 날카롭게 뾰족한 뉴욕 수로 안내선으로 바꾸어 놓는다고 할 만하다.\n\n\"저어라, 저어, 이 사람들아! 서두르진 마라. 시간은 넉넉히 잡되, 그래도 저어라. 벼락 치듯 저으란 말이다, 그게 전부다.\" 스텁이 말하면서 연기를 푹푹 내뿜으며 외쳤다. \"자, 저어라. 길고 힘차게 한 번에 당겨라, 태시테고(Tashtego). 저어라, 태시, 이 친구야. 모두 저어라. 하지만 침착하게, 침착하게 해라. 오이처럼 시원하게가 핵심이다. 편하게, 편하게 해라. 다만 무서운 죽음과 이빨 드러낸 악마처럼 저어라, 그리고 묻힌 망자들을 무덤에서 수직으로 일으켜 세워라, 친구들아. 그게 전부다. 저어라!\"\n\n\"우후! 와히!\" 게이헤드 출신 사내가 화답하며 외쳐, 어떤 오래된 전쟁 함성을 하늘 높이 내질렀다. 그러자 잔뜩 긴장한 보트 안의 노잡이들이 저마다, 그 열성적인 인디언이 내지른 한 번의 어마어마한 선도 동작에 자기도 모르게 앞으로 튕겨 나갔다.\n\n하지만 그의 거친 비명에는 못지않게 거친 다른 비명들이 응답했다. \"키히! 키히!\" 대구(Daggoo)가 우리 안을 어슬렁대는 호랑이처럼 좌석에서 앞뒤로 몸을 잡아당기며 고함쳤다.\n\n\"칼라! 쿨루!\" 퀴퀘그는 척탄병의 스테이크 한 입을 두고 입맛을 다시기라도 하듯 울부짖었다. 그리하여 노와 함성과 더불어 용골들이 바다를 갈랐다. 그동안 스텁은 선두 자리를 지키며 줄곧 입에서 연기를 뿜어 대면서 부하들에게 돌격을 독려했다. 그들은 무법자들처럼 당기고 또 잡아당겼고, 마침내 반가운 외침이 들려왔다. \"일어서라, 태시테고! 한 방 먹여라!\" 작살이 날아갔다. \"모두 뒤로!\" 노잡이들이 노를 거꾸로 저었다. 바로 그 순간 무언가 뜨겁게 쉭쉭거리며 저마다의 손목을 스치고 지나갔다. 그 마법의 밧줄이었다. 한순간 전, 스텁은 그 밧줄을 잽싸게 밧줄걸이 기둥에 두 바퀴 더 감았는데, 밧줄이 점점 빠르게 휘감겨 도는 까닭에, 이제 그 기둥에서 삼실의 푸른 연기가 솟구쳐 올라 그의 파이프에서 꾸준히 피어오르는 연기와 뒤섞였다. 밧줄이 그 기둥을 휘휘 감고 도는 사이, 또한 그 지점에 닿기 직전에, 밧줄은 스텁의 두 손을 사정없이 스치고 또 스치며 살을 데었다. 손에서 손싸개가, 즉 이런 때 더러 끼는 누비 캔버스 천 조각이 어쩌다 떨어져 나간 탓이었다. 그것은 마치 적의 날카로운 양날 검을 칼날 쪽으로 움켜쥔 것 같았고, 그러는 내내 그 적은 그것을 당신의 손아귀에서 비틀어 빼내려 안간힘을 쓰고 있는 셈이었다.\n\n\"밧줄을 적셔라! 밧줄을 적셔!\" 스텁이 통 옆에 앉은 통 담당 노잡이에게 외쳤다. 그러자 그 사내는 모자를 홱 벗어 거기에 바닷물을 퍼 담았다.* 밧줄이 몇 바퀴 더 감기자, 밧줄은 비로소 제자리를 잡기 시작했다. 이제 보트는 온통 지느러미뿐인 상어처럼 끓어오르는 물살을 뚫고 날아갔다. 여기서 스텁과 태시테고는 자리를 바꾸었다. 뱃머리와 고물을 맞바꾼 것인데, 그 출렁이는 소란 속에서 그것은 참으로 아슬아슬한 일이었다.\n\n* 이 행위가 얼마나 필수적인지 어느 정도 보여 주기 위해 여기서 밝혀 두자면, 옛 네덜란드 어업에서는 풀려 나가는 밧줄에 물을 끼얹는 데 자루걸레를 썼다. 다른 여러 배에서는 그 목적을 위해 나무 물통, 즉 물 푸는 바가지를 따로 마련해 둔다. 하지만 모자가 가장 편리하다.\n\n보트 윗부분의 전체 길이에 걸쳐 팽팽하게 뻗은 그 진동하는 밧줄을 보고, 또 그것이 이제 하프 줄보다도 더 팽팽하게 당겨진 것을 보았다면, 당신은 이 배에 용골이 둘 달렸다고 — 하나는 물을 가르고, 다른 하나는 공기를 가른다고 — 여겼을 것이다. 보트가 서로 맞서는 두 원소를 동시에 휘저으며 나아갔으니 말이다. 뱃머리에서는 폭포수가 끊임없이 일었고, 그 항적에서는 소용돌이가 쉴 새 없이 휘돌았다. 그리고 안에서 누군가 새끼손가락 하나라도 까딱 움직이면, 진동하며 삐걱대는 그 배는 경련하듯 한쪽 뱃전을 기울이며 당장이라도 바닷속으로 뒤집힐 듯했다. 그렇게 그들은 내달렸다. 저마다 거품 속으로 내동댕이쳐지지 않으려 온 힘을 다해 자기 좌석에 매달렸고, 키 잡는 노 앞의 키 큰 태시테고는 무게 중심을 낮추려고 거의 두 겹으로 접히다시피 몸을 웅크렸다. 대서양과 태평양을 통째로 지나친 듯한 기세로 그들은 쏜살같이 나아갔고, 마침내 고래가 그 질주를 다소 늦추었다.\n\n\"끌어당겨라, 끌어당겨!\" 스텁이 뱃머리 사내에게 외쳤다. 그리고 모두 고래 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 보트가 아직 끌려가는 와중에도 보트를 고래 쪽으로 끌어당기기 시작했다. 이윽고 고래의 옆구리에 바싹 다가서자, 스텁은 무릎을 그 투박한 받침대에 단단히 박은 채, 달아나는 물고기에게 작살을 연거푸 던져 넣었다. 호령에 따라 보트는 번갈아 가며 고래의 끔찍한 몸부림을 피해 뒤로 물러났다가, 다시 또 한 번 던지기 위해 다가서곤 했다.\n\n이제 그 괴물의 사방에서 붉은 물결이, 언덕을 따라 흘러내리는 시냇물처럼 쏟아져 나왔다. 고통에 시달리는 그 몸뚱이는 이제 짠물이 아니라 피 속에서 뒹굴었고, 그 피는 항적 뒤로 몇 펄롱에 걸쳐 부글거리며 끓어올랐다. 비스듬히 기운 해가 바다의 이 진홍빛 못 위에서 어른거리며 그 반사광을 모두의 얼굴에 되쏘았고, 그리하여 그들은 서로에게 붉은 인간처럼 이글거려 보였다. 그러는 내내, 고래의 분수공에서는 흰 연기가 한 줄기씩 고통스럽게 뿜어 나왔고, 흥분한 작살잡이의 입에서는 격렬한 연기가 한 모금씩 내뿜어졌다. 작살을 한 번 던질 때마다, 스텁은 (거기에 매인 밧줄로) 자신의 휜 창을 끌어당겨, 뱃전에 몇 번 잽싸게 두드려 그것을 거듭거듭 곧게 펴고는, 또다시 고래에게 박아 넣었던 것이다.\n\n\"끌어 올려라, 끌어 올려!\" 그가 이제 뱃머리 사내에게 외쳤다. 점점 기력이 다해 가는 고래가 그 분노를 누그러뜨렸기 때문이다. \"끌어 올려라! 바싹 붙어라!\" 그러자 보트가 고래의 옆구리를 따라 나란히 섰다. 스텁은 뱃머리 너머로 멀찍이 몸을 뻗어, 길고 날카로운 창을 천천히 고래의 몸속에 휘저어 넣고 그대로 둔 채, 조심스레 휘젓고 또 휘저었다. 마치 고래가 삼켰을지 모를 어떤 금시계를 손끝으로 더듬어 찾으면서, 그것을 갈고리로 끌어내기 전에 행여 깨뜨릴까 봐 두려워하는 듯한 손길이었다. 하지만 그가 찾던 그 금시계란 다름 아닌 고래의 가장 깊은 곳에 있는 목숨이었다. 이제 그것이 찔렸다. 혼곤한 잠에서 깨어나 이른바 그 \"발작\"이라 불리는 형언할 수 없는 상태로 접어든 그 괴물은, 제 피 속에서 끔찍하게 뒹굴며 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 미치광이 같은 끓어오르는 물보라로 제 몸을 휘감았다. 그리하여 위태로워진 그 작은 배는 곧장 뒤로 물러나면서, 그 광란의 어스름에서 벗어나 환한 대낮의 맑은 대기 속으로 더듬더듬 빠져나오느라 진땀을 뺐다.\n\n이제 발작이 잦아들면서, 고래는 다시 한번 모습을 드러내며 떠올랐다. 좌우로 출렁이며, 분수공을 발작하듯 벌렸다 오므렸다 하면서, 날카롭고 갈라지는, 고통에 찬 숨을 몰아쉬었다. 마침내, 마치 적포도주의 자줏빛 찌끼라도 되는 양 엉긴 붉은 핏덩이가 한 줄기씩 거듭 겁먹은 허공으로 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그러고는 다시 떨어져 내려, 미동도 없는 그 옆구리를 따라 뚝뚝 흘러 바다로 흘러 들어갔다. 그 심장이 터져 버린 것이다!\n\n\"놈이 죽었습니다, 스텁 씨.\" 대구가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 두 파이프가 다 타 버렸군!\" 스텁은 자기 입에서 파이프를 빼내며 죽은 재를 물 위에 흩뿌렸다. 그러고는 잠시, 자신이 만들어 낸 그 거대한 시체를 생각에 잠긴 눈으로 바라보며 서 있었다.\n\n앞 장에서 일어난 한 가지 사건에 관해 한마디 덧붙여 둔다.\n\n포경업의 변함없는 관습에 따르면, 고래잡이 보트는 머리잡이(headsman), 곧 고래를 죽이는 자가 임시 조타수를 맡고, 작살잡이(harpooneer), 곧 고래에 작살을 박는 자가 맨 앞자리의 노 — 이른바 작살잡이 노라 불리는 것 — 를 저으며 본선에서 떨어져 나간다. 그런데 처음으로 작살을 고래 몸에 꽂으려면 강하고 단단한 팔이 필요하다. 흔히 긴 던지기라 불리는 경우에는, 그 묵직한 도구를 20피트나 30피트(약 6미터 내지 9미터) 거리까지 내던져야 하기 때문이다. 그러나 추격이 아무리 길고 진을 빼는 것일지라도, 작살잡이는 그동안 자기 노를 있는 힘껏 저어야 한다. 아니, 그는 믿기 어려운 노젓기뿐 아니라 거듭 터뜨리는 크고 담대한 외침으로써 나머지 사람들에게 초인적인 활약의 본보기를 보여 주어야 한다. 다른 근육이란 근육이 모두 팽팽하게 당겨지고 반쯤 풀려나는 와중에 목청껏 고함을 계속 질러야 한다는 것이 어떤 일인지 — 그것은 해 본 자가 아니면 아무도 모른다. 나로 말하자면, 한순간에 마음껏 고함을 지르면서 동시에 죽을힘을 다해 일하는 것은 도무지 못 하겠다. 그리하여 이렇게 힘을 짜내며 고래에게 등을 돌린 채 고함치는 상태에서, 기진맥진한 작살잡이는 별안간 가슴 뛰는 외침을 듣는다 — \"일어서서, 놈에게 한 방 먹여!\" 이제 그는 노를 놓고 단단히 묶은 뒤, 제자리에서 몸을 반쯤 돌려, 받침대에서 작살을 움켜쥐고, 남아 있는 얼마 안 되는 힘으로 어떻게든 그것을 고래에게 던져 박으려 안간힘을 쓴다. 그러니 고래잡이 선단 전체를 한데 놓고 볼 때, 던질 기회가 쉰 번 있어도 그중 다섯 번도 성공하지 못한다 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 그토록 많은 가련한 작살잡이들이 미친 듯이 욕을 먹고 강등당한다 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 그들 중 더러는 보트 안에서 실제로 혈관이 터지고 만다 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 어떤 향유고래잡이들이 사 년이나 집을 비우고도 기름 네 통밖에 채우지 못한다 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 많은 선주들에게 포경업이 그저 밑지는 장사일 뿐이라 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 항해의 성패를 가르는 것은 바로 작살잡이이니, 그의 몸에서 숨을 다 빼 놓고서야 어찌 가장 절실한 순간에 그 숨이 남아 있기를 바랄 수 있겠는가!\n\n또한, 던지기가 성공한다 해도, 두 번째 결정적인 순간, 곧 고래가 내달리기 시작할 때, 머리잡이와 작살잡이도 마찬가지로 앞뒤로 자리를 옮기느라 분주해져, 자기 자신은 물론 다른 모든 이들까지 임박한 위험에 빠뜨린다. 바로 이때 그들은 자리를 맞바꾸며, 그 작은 배의 우두머리 사관인 머리잡이가 보트 뱃머리의 제자리를 차지하게 된다.\n\n그런데 나는 누가 그 반대를 주장하든 개의치 않거니와, 이 모든 것이야말로 어리석고 불필요한 짓이다. 머리잡이는 처음부터 끝까지 뱃머리에 머물러 있어야 한다. 그는 작살도 던지고 창도 던져야 하며, 어떤 어부에게나 빤히 보이는 상황을 제외하고는 그에게 노젓는 일을 기대해서는 안 된다. 물론 이렇게 하면 때때로 추격 속도가 약간 떨어지는 일도 있으리란 것을 나는 안다. 그러나 한 나라에 그치지 않는 여러 포경선에서 쌓은 오랜 경험을 통해 나는, 포경업에서 일어나는 실패의 압도적 대다수가, 결코 고래의 속도 때문이라기보다는 앞서 말한 작살잡이의 탈진 때문에 빚어진 것임을 확신하게 되었다.\n\n던지기에서 최대의 효율을 보장하려면, 이 세상의 작살잡이들은 고된 일에서가 아니라 한가로움에서 벌떡 일어서야 하는 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 63. The Crotch.\n\n줄기에서 가지가 자라나고, 그 가지에서 잔가지가 돋는다. 그처럼 결실 많은 주제에서는 여러 장(章)이 자라난다.\n\n앞 쪽에서 언급한 받침대(crotch)는 따로 떼어 설명할 만하다. 그것은 독특한 형태로 홈이 파인 막대인데, 길이는 2피트(약 60센티미터)쯤 되며, 뱃머리 가까운 우현 뱃전에 수직으로 꽂아 두어 작살의 나무 자루 끝을 받쳐 두는 데 쓰인다. 작살의 다른 한쪽, 곧 미늘 박힌 맨살의 끝은 뱃머리에서 비스듬히 앞으로 튀어나오게 된다. 그리하여 그 무기는 던지는 자의 손에 즉각 닿게 되어, 마치 변경의 산사람이 벽에 걸린 소총을 낚아채듯 받침대에서 단숨에 집어 들 수 있다. 받침대에는 작살 두 자루를 얹어 두는 것이 관례인데, 각각 첫째 작살과 둘째 작살이라 부른다.\n\n그러나 이 두 작살은 저마다 제 줄로 본줄에 이어져 있으니, 그 목적은 이러하다. 가능하다면 둘 다, 하나를 던진 직후 곧바로 다른 하나를 같은 고래에 꽂아 넣으려는 것이다. 그리하여 뒤이어 끌려갈 때 만일 하나가 빠져나가더라도 다른 하나가 여전히 박혀 있게 하려는 것이다. 이는 가능성을 곱절로 늘리는 일이다. 그러나 첫째 작살을 맞은 고래가 순식간에 격렬하고 발작적으로 내달리는 까닭에, 작살잡이가 번개처럼 몸을 놀린다 해도 둘째 작살을 그놈에게 박아 넣기란 불가능해지는 경우가 매우 잦다. 그럼에도 둘째 작살은 이미 본줄에 이어져 있고 그 줄은 풀려 나가고 있으니, 그 무기는 어떻게든, 또 어디로든, 어떤 일이 있어도 미리 보트 밖으로 던져 버려야만 한다. 그러지 않으면 더없이 끔찍한 위험이 선원 모두를 덮칠 것이기 때문이다. 그리하여 그런 경우에는 작살이 물속으로 던져지는데, (앞 장에서 언급한) 통에 감겨 있는 여분의 줄 사리가 있어 대개는 이 일을 신중하게 해낼 수 있다. 그러나 이 위태로운 행위에는 더없이 슬프고 치명적인 사고가 따르지 않는 법이 없다.\n\n게다가 알아 두어야 할 것은, 둘째 작살이 배 밖으로 던져지고 나면 그때부터 그것은 대롱대롱 매달려 날뛰는, 날 선 공포의 물건이 된다는 점이다. 그것은 보트와 고래 둘레를 변덕스레 펄쩍거리며 휘돌아, 줄을 엉키게 하거나 끊어 버리고, 사방에 어마어마한 소동을 일으킨다. 그리고 고래가 완전히 잡혀 송장이 되기 전까지는 대개 그것을 다시 거두어들이는 일이 불가능하다.\n\n이제 생각해 보라. 보통보다 훨씬 더 힘세고 날래며 영악한 고래 한 마리를 보트 네 척이 한꺼번에 상대하는 경우가 어떠할지를. 그놈의 그러한 성질에다, 그토록 대담한 일에 으레 뒤따르는 수천 가지 사고가 겹치는 까닭에, 풀려난 둘째 작살 여덟 자루나 열 자루가 한꺼번에 그놈 둘레에서 대롱거리는 사태가 벌어질 수도 있다. 물론 보트마다 작살이 여러 자루씩 갖춰져 있어, 첫째 작살이 헛되이 던져져 거두지 못하게 되면 본줄에 새로 이어 맬 수 있기 때문이다. 이 모든 자세한 사정을 여기에 충실히 적어 두는 까닭은, 앞으로 그려 낼 장면들 속에서 더없이 중요하면서도 까다로운 여러 대목을 밝히는 데 반드시 도움이 될 것이기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 64. Stubb’s Supper.\n\n스터브의 고래는 배에서 제법 떨어진 곳에서 죽어 있었다. 바다는 잔잔했다. 그리하여 우리는 보트 세 척을 한 줄로 잇대어, 그 전리품을 피쿼드(Pequod)호까지 끌고 가는 더딘 일에 착수했다. 그리고 이제 우리 열여덟 명이 서른여섯 개의 팔과 백여든 개의 엄지와 손가락을 놀려, 바다 위의 그 굼뜨고 묵직한 시체를 한 시간 또 한 시간 천천히 끌어당겼으나, 그것은 좀처럼 꿈쩍도 하지 않는 듯했고, 다만 긴 간격을 두고서야 겨우 움직일 뿐이었으니, 우리가 움직이는 그 덩어리가 얼마나 어마어마한지를 여기서 충분히 짐작할 수 있었다. 중국의 항허(Hang-Ho) 대운하인지 뭐라 부르든 그곳에서는, 길가의 인부 네댓 명이 짐을 가득 실은 거룻배를 시속 1마일로 끌어가지만, 우리가 끌고 가는 이 거대한 배는 마치 무게 단위로 잰 납덩이를 가득 실은 것처럼 무겁게 짓이기며 나아갔다.\n\n어둠이 내려앉았다. 그러나 피쿼드호의 큰 돛 삭구에 위아래로 매달린 세 개의 불빛이 우리의 길을 어슴푸레 인도해 주었다. 점점 가까워지자 에이해브(Ahab)가 뱃전 너머로 등불 몇 개를 더 늘어뜨리는 모습이 보였다. 그는 출렁이는 고래를 잠시 멍하니 바라보더니, 밤새 그것을 단단히 붙잡아 두라는 평소의 지시를 내렸고, 그런 다음 등불을 한 선원에게 건네고 선실로 들어가서는, 아침이 될 때까지 다시는 앞으로 나오지 않았다.\n\n이 고래를 추격하는 일을 감독하면서 에이해브 선장(Captain)은 — 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 — 평소의 활력을 보여 주었으나, 이제 그 짐승이 죽고 나자 어떤 막연한 불만이나 초조함이나 절망 같은 것이 그의 안에서 꿈틀대는 듯했다. 마치 그 죽은 몸뚱이를 보는 것이 모비 딕(Moby Dick)은 아직 죽이지 못했음을 그에게 일깨워 주기라도 하는 것처럼, 그리고 천 마리의 다른 고래를 그의 배로 끌어온다 한들, 그 모든 것이 그의 위대한, 편집광적인 목표를 손톱만큼도 진전시키지 못하기라도 하는 것처럼. 머지않아 그대는 피쿼드호 갑판에서 나는 소리만 듣고도, 온 선원이 깊은 바다에 닻을 내릴 채비를 하고 있다고 생각했을 것이다. 묵직한 쇠사슬들이 갑판을 따라 질질 끌려가고, 현문(舷門) 구멍 밖으로 덜컹거리며 밀려 나가고 있었으니 말이다. 그러나 저 철컹대는 사슬 고리에 매여 묶이는 것은 배가 아니라 그 거대한 시체 자체였다. 머리는 고물에, 꼬리는 이물에 묶인 채, 고래는 이제 그 검은 선체를 배의 선체에 바싹 붙인 채 누워 있었고, 위쪽의 활대와 삭구를 가려 버린 밤의 어둠을 통해 바라보면, 그 둘 — 배와 고래는 마치 거대한 황소 한 쌍이 멍에에 함께 묶인 듯, 그중 하나는 누워 있고 다른 하나는 선 채로 있는 것처럼 보였다.*\n\n* 여기서 사소한 이야기 하나를 곁들여도 좋겠다. 고래를 배 옆에 붙들어 맬 때 배가 고래를 붙잡는 가장 튼튼하고 미더운 고리는 꼬리지느러미, 곧 꼬리다. 그 부위는 밀도가 더 높아 다른 어느 부분(옆지느러미를 제외하고)보다도 상대적으로 무겁기에, 죽은 뒤에도 그 유연함 탓에 수면 아래로 깊이 가라앉는다. 그래서 보트에서는 손을 뻗어도 그것에 닿을 수가 없어, 사슬을 둘러 감을 수가 없다. 그러나 이 난관은 기발하게 극복된다. 가늘고 튼튼한 밧줄을 마련하여 바깥쪽 끝에는 나무 부표를 달고 가운데에는 추를 매달며, 다른 쪽 끝은 배에 단단히 고정한다. 능숙하게 다루면 나무 부표가 그 덩어리의 반대편으로 떠오르게 되고, 그리하여 이제 고래를 한 바퀴 둘렀으니 사슬도 손쉽게 그 뒤를 따르게 되며, 몸을 따라 미끄러져 내려가다가 마침내 넓은 꼬리지느러미, 곧 갈래와 맞닿는 지점에서 꼬리의 가장 가느다란 부분을 단단히 감아 잠근다.\n\n침울한 에이해브가 이제 — 적어도 갑판에서 알 수 있는 한 — 온통 고요에 잠겨 있었다면, 그의 이등 항해사 스터브(Stubb)는 승리에 들떠 평소답지 않으면서도 여전히 사람 좋은 흥분을 드러내고 있었다. 그가 어찌나 유난스레 부산을 떨었던지, 그의 공식적인 상관인 진중한 스타벅(Starbuck)은 한동안 모든 일의 지휘권을 그에게 조용히 넘겨주었다. 스터브의 이 모든 활기에 한몫 거든 작은 까닭 하나가 곧 묘하게 드러났다. 스터브는 잘 먹고 잘 마시는 자였고, 입맛을 돋우는 별미로서 고래를 다소 절제 없이 좋아하는 편이었다.\n\n\"스테이크다, 스테이크! 잠들기 전에 한 점 먹어야겠다! 자네, 대구(Daggoo)! 어서 물로 뛰어들어, 저놈의 꼬리 쪽에서 한 덩이 잘라 와!\"\n\n여기서 밝혀 두건대, 이 거친 어부들은 일반적으로, 또 저 위대한 군사 격언에 따라 적에게 전쟁의 당면 비용을 치르게 하는 법이(적어도 항해의 수익을 거두어들이기 전에는) 없지만, 그래도 이따금 이 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들 가운데에는 스터브가 가리킨 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 바로 그 부위, 곧 끝으로 갈수록 가늘어지는 몸뚱이의 끄트머리에 진심으로 입맛을 다시는 자들이 있다.\n\n자정 무렵 그 스테이크는 잘려 익혀졌다. 그리고 향유고래 기름을 채운 등불 두 개의 불빛 아래, 스터브는 캡스턴(capstan) 꼭대기에서 마치 그 캡스턴이 식탁이라도 되는 양 떡 버티고 서서 자신의 향유고래 만찬을 들었다. 그날 밤 고래 살로 잔치를 벌인 것은 스터브뿐만이 아니었다. 죽은 리바이어던(Leviathan) 주위로 떼 지어 몰려든 수천수만 마리의 상어들이, 그의 씹는 소리에 저들의 우물거리는 소리를 뒤섞으며 그 기름진 살을 쩝쩝거리며 포식했다. 아래쪽 침상에서 잠을 청하던 몇 안 되는 자들은, 잠든 이의 심장에서 불과 몇 인치 떨어진 곳에서 선체를 후려치는 상어 꼬리의 날카로운 철썩임에 번번이 화들짝 놀라곤 했다. 뱃전 너머로 내려다보면, (앞서 그 소리를 들었듯이) 그놈들이 음침하고 검은 물속에서 뒹굴며, 사람 머리만 한 크기의 거대하고 둥근 고래 살점을 도려내면서 등을 뒤집는 모습을 어렴풋이 볼 수 있었다. 상어의 이 유별난 재주는 거의 기적과도 같아 보인다. 겉보기에 그토록 비집고 들어갈 데 없어 보이는 표면에서, 어떻게 그처럼 좌우 대칭의 한 입 거리를 후벼 파내는지는 만물에 깃든 보편적 수수께끼의 일부로 남아 있다. 그렇게 해서 고래에 남긴 자국은, 목수가 나사를 박으려고 파 놓은 우묵한 구멍에 견주는 것이 가장 적절하리라.\n\n해전의 매캐한 공포와 악마성 한가운데서도, 상어들이 마치 붉은 고기를 썰어 내는 식탁 둘레의 굶주린 개들처럼 배의 갑판을 갈망 어린 눈길로 올려다보며, 자기들에게 던져지는 죽은 자라면 누구든 덥석 삼킬 채비를 하고 있는 모습을 보게 되고, 또 갑판이라는 식탁 위의 용맹한 백정들이 황금빛에 술까지 달린 칼로 서로의 살아 있는 살점을 식인종처럼 베어 내는 동안, 보석 박힌 칼자루 같은 입을 가진 상어들 역시 식탁 아래에서 죽은 살점을 서로 다투며 베어 가고 있는 모습을 보게 되며, 또 그 모든 광경을 거꾸로 뒤집어 놓는다 한들 여전히 거의 같은 일, 곧 모든 편에게 충격적이리만치 상어 같은 짓거리일 따름이며, 또 상어들은 대서양을 건너는 모든 노예선의 변함없는 호위병으로서, 어딘가로 짐 꾸러미가 옮겨지거나 죽은 노예가 점잖게 매장되어야 할 경우에 대비해 빈틈없이 곁에서 종종걸음을 치며 따라붙고, 또 상어들이 가장 사교적으로 모여들어 가장 흥겹게 잔치를 벌이는 정해진 조건과 장소와 경우에 관해 이와 비슷한 사례를 한둘 더 적어 둘 수도 있겠으나, 그럼에도 밤중에 바다에서 고래잡이배에 붙들어 맨 죽은 향유고래 둘레만큼 상어들이 그토록 헤아릴 수 없는 수효로, 그토록 들뜨고 흥겨운 기분으로 모여드는 때나 경우는 달리 생각해 낼 수가 없다. 그대가 그 광경을 한 번도 본 적이 없다면, 악마 숭배가 과연 온당한지, 또 악마를 달래는 것이 과연 현명한 일인지에 대한 판단일랑 잠시 미뤄 두는 것이 좋으리라.\n\n그러나 아직까지 스터브는, 상어들이 자신의 미식가다운 입술이 쩝쩝대는 소리에 아랑곳하지 않는 것과 마찬가지로, 바로 곁에서 벌어지는 그 잔치의 우물거림에 조금도 신경 쓰지 않았다.\n\n\"요리사, 요리사! 그 늙은 플리스(Fleece) 어디 갔나?\" 그는 마침내 소리쳤다. 마치 자기 만찬을 위해 더 든든한 받침대를 만들기라도 하듯 두 다리를 한층 더 벌리면서, 동시에 작살을 찌르듯 포크를 접시에 푹 꽂으며. \"요리사, 이봐 요리사! 이쪽으로 와, 요리사!\"\n\n이 늙은 흑인은, 가장 한가롭지 못한 시각에 따뜻한 해먹에서 끌려 나온 것이 그리 신이 날 리 없는 터라, 주방에서 비척비척 걸어 나왔다. 다른 많은 늙은 흑인들처럼 그도 무릎 종지에 무슨 탈이 있었는데, 그것만은 다른 냄비들처럼 반들반들 닦아 두지 않았던 것이다. 사람들이 플리스라 부르던 이 노인은 발을 끌며 절뚝절뚝 다가오면서, 곧게 편 쇠테로 어설프게 만든 부젓가락에 의지해 걸음을 옮겼다. 이 늙은 흑단(黑檀) 같은 사내는 허우적허우적 걸어와, 명령에 따라 스터브의 식탁 맞은편에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그러고는 두 손을 앞으로 모아 두 갈래로 된 지팡이에 얹고서, 굽은 등을 한층 더 깊이 숙이는 동시에 머리를 옆으로 기울여 가장 잘 들리는 쪽 귀를 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"요리사,\" 스터브가 불그스름한 살점 한 조각을 재빨리 입으로 가져가며 말했다. \"이 스테이크가 좀 너무 익은 것 같지 않나? 자네 이 스테이크를 너무 두들겼어, 요리사. 너무 물렁물렁해. 내가 늘 말하지 않나, 고래 스테이크가 맛있으려면 질겨야 한다고? 저기 뱃전 너머의 저 상어들 좀 봐, 저놈들이 질기고 설익은 걸 좋아하는 게 안 보여? 저놈들 한바탕 소란을 떨어 대는 꼴이라니! 요리사, 가서 저놈들한테 한마디 하게. 점잖게, 적당히 알아서들 먹는 건 좋다고, 하지만 좀 조용히 해야 한다고 일러 줘. 빌어먹을, 내 목소리도 안 들릴 지경이야. 어서 가, 요리사, 가서 내 말을 전해. 자, 이 등불을 들고 가게.\" 그러면서 그는 식탁에서 하나를 낚아챘다. \"자 그럼, 가서 저놈들한테 설교를 하라고!\"\n\n늙은 플리스는 마지못해 건네받은 등불을 받아 들고, 절뚝거리며 갑판을 가로질러 뱃전으로 갔다. 그러고는 한 손으로 등불을 바다 위로 낮게 늘어뜨려 자기 신도들을 잘 볼 수 있도록 한 다음, 다른 손으로는 부젓가락을 엄숙하게 휘두르며, 뱃전 너머로 몸을 깊이 기울인 채 우물거리는 목소리로 상어들에게 설교를 시작했다. 그동안 스터브는 살그머니 뒤로 기어와 그가 하는 말을 죄다 엿들었다.\n\n\"동포 짐승들이여, 내 여기 명을 받고 와서 이르노니, 너희는 저 빌어먹을 소란을 멈춰야 하느니라. 듣고 있느냐? 저 빌어먹을 입맛 다시는 소리를 멈추란 말이다! 스터브 나리께서 이르시되, 너희 빌어먹을 배때기를 천장까지 그득 채워도 좋다 하셨으나, 맙소사! 저 빌어먹을 야단법석만은 멈춰야 하느니라!\"\n\n\"요리사,\" 여기서 스터브가 끼어들며, 그 말과 함께 별안간 어깨를 탁 치고는 말했다. \"요리사! 아니, 이런 빌어먹을, 설교를 할 때는 그렇게 욕지거리를 해선 안 되지. 그렇게 해선 죄인들을 회개시킬 수가 없어, 요리사!\"\n\n\"누가 그래요? 그럼 나리가 직접 설교를 하든가.\" 그가 시무룩하게 돌아서서 가려 했다.\n\n\"아냐, 요리사. 계속해, 계속하라고.\"\n\n\"그럼 좋소, 사랑하는 동포 짐승들이여—\"\n\n\"옳지!\" 스터브가 흐뭇하게 외쳤다. \"그렇게 어르고 달래야지. 그렇게 해 봐.\" 그러자 플리스가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"너희가 죄다 상어이고, 천성이 무척이나 게걸스러운 줄은 안다만, 그래도 내 너희에게 이르노니, 동포 짐승들이여, 그 게걸스러움이란—저 빌어먹을 꼬리 철썩이는 짓을 멈추라! 너희가 그렇게 빌어먹을 철썩질에 물어뜯기를 계속하면서 어찌 들을 생각을 하느냐?\"\n\n\"요리사,\" 스터브가 그의 멱살을 잡으며 외쳤다. \"그 욕지거리는 안 된다니까. 점잖게 말하란 말이야.\"\n\n또다시 설교가 이어졌다.\n\n\"너희의 게걸스러움은, 동포 짐승들이여, 내 그리 탓하지는 않으마. 그건 천성이고 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이니. 허나 그 못된 천성을 다스리는 것, 그것이 바로 요체니라. 너희는 분명 상어다. 허나 너희 안의 상어를 다스린다면, 그때 너희는 천사가 되느니라. 무릇 천사란 천성을 잘 다스린 상어에 지나지 않으니. 자, 이보게 형제들이여, 단 한 번만이라도 점잖게 굴어 보게, 저 고래에서 제 몫을 챙기되 말일세. 이웃의 입에서 비계를 잡아채지 말란 말이다. 어느 한 상어가 다른 상어보다 저 고래에 더 큰 권리를 가졌다더냐? 게다가 맙소사, 너희 가운데 아무도 저 고래에 권리가 없느니라. 저 고래는 다른 누군가의 것이니. 내 알기로 너희 가운데 몇몇은 주둥이가 무척 커서, 다른 놈들보다 더 크다만, 그러나 주둥이 큰 놈이 때로는 배때기가 작은 법. 그러니 주둥이가 큰 까닭은 삼키라는 것이 아니라, 그 비계를 베어 내어, 비집고 들어와 제 몫을 챙길 수 없는 송사리 같은 작은 상어들에게 나눠 주라는 것이니라.\"\n\n\"잘했어, 늙은 플리스!\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"그게 바로 기독교 정신이야. 계속해.\"\n\n\"계속해 봐야 소용없소, 스터브 나리. 저 빌어먹을 악당들은 서로 떠밀고 후려치기만 할 뿐, 한마디도 안 듣소. 나리가 부르는 대로 저런 빌어먹을 식충이들한테는, 배때기가 그득 찰 때까지는 설교해 봐야 소용없는데, 저놈들 배때기는 바닥이 없소. 그리고 배가 정말 그득 차고 나면, 그때는 또 나리 말을 안 듣소. 그땐 바닷속으로 가라앉아 산호 위에서 곯아떨어져, 영영토록 아무것도, 더는 아무것도 못 듣소.\"\n\n\"내 영혼을 걸고, 나도 거의 같은 생각이네. 그러니 축도나 해 주게, 플리스. 나는 내 만찬으로 돌아가야겠으니.\"\n\n이 말에 플리스는 두 손을 저 물고기 떼 위로 쳐들고, 새된 목소리를 높여 외쳤다—\n\n\"저주받은 동포 짐승들이여! 너희가 부릴 수 있는 가장 빌어먹을 야단법석이나 실컷 부려라. 너희 빌어먹을 배때기를 터지도록 채우고—그러고는 뒈져 버려라.\"\n\n\"자, 요리사,\" 스터브가 캡스턴에서 만찬을 다시 들며 말했다. \"아까 섰던 바로 그 자리, 거기, 내 맞은편에 서서, 똑똑히 귀를 기울이게.\"\n\n\"귀 쫑긋.\" 플리스가 다시 부젓가락에 몸을 기대며 시키는 대로 자세를 잡고 말했다.\n\n\"자,\" 스터브가 그러는 동안 마음껏 먹어 대며 말했다. \"이제 이 스테이크 이야기로 돌아가지. 우선 말이야, 자네 나이가 몇인가, 요리사?\"\n\n\"그게 스테이크하고 무슨 상관이오.\" 늙은 흑인이 퉁명스레 말했다.\n\n\"닥쳐! 나이가 몇이냐고, 요리사?\"\n\n\"아흔쯤 됐다고들 합디다.\" 그가 침울하게 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"이 세상을 백 년 가까이 살아오고도, 아직 고래 스테이크 하나 제대로 구울 줄을 모른단 말인가, 요리사?\" 마지막 말과 함께 또 한 입을 재빨리 삼키는 통에, 그 살점이 마치 질문의 연장처럼 보였다. \"자넨 어디서 태어났나, 요리사?\"\n\n\"승강구 뒤쪽에서요, 로어노크(Roanoke) 건너던 나룻배에서.\"\n\n\"나룻배에서 태어났다고! 그것 참 별나기도 하구먼. 하지만 내가 알고 싶은 건 자네가 어느 나라에서 태어났느냐는 거야, 요리사!\"\n\n\"로어노크 고장이라고 안 했소?\" 그가 날카롭게 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 안 했어, 요리사. 하지만 내가 무슨 말을 하려는지 일러 주지, 요리사. 자넨 고향에 돌아가서 다시 태어나야겠어. 아직 고래 스테이크 굽는 법도 모르니 말이야.\"\n\n\"내 영혼을 걸고, 다시는 안 굽겠소.\" 그가 성을 내며 으르렁대고는 떠나려고 돌아섰다.\n\n\"돌아와, 요리사.—자, 그 부젓가락 이리 주게.—자, 거기 그 스테이크 한 점을 집어서, 그게 제대로 구워졌다고 생각하는지 말해 보게. 집으라니까\"—부젓가락을 그에게 내밀며—\"집어서, 맛을 보란 말이야.\"\n\n늙은 흑인은 잠시 시든 입술로 그것을 희미하게 쩝쩝거려 보더니 중얼거렸다. \"내 평생 맛본 중 제일 잘 구운 스테이크요. 촉촉하고, 아주 촉촉합니다.\"\n\n\"요리사,\" 스터브가 다시 한번 몸을 떡 버티며 말했다. \"자넨 교회에 다니나?\"\n\n\"한번은 케이프타운(Cape-Town)에서 하나 지나친 적이 있소.\" 늙은이가 시무룩하게 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까 자넨 평생에 단 한 번 케이프타운에서 거룩한 교회를 지나쳤고, 거기서 틀림없이 거룩한 목사가 자기 청중을 사랑하는 동포 짐승들이라 부르며 설교하는 걸 엿들었다 이거지, 요리사! 그래 놓고도 자넨 여기 와서, 방금 같은 그런 끔찍한 거짓말을 했다 이 말이지, 응?\" 스터브가 말했다. \"자넨 어디로 갈 작정인가, 요리사?\"\n\n\"곧 잠자리로 갈 작정이오.\" 그가 말하면서 반쯤 돌아서며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"멈춰! 정선하라고! 내 말은 자네가 죽으면 말이야, 요리사. 이건 엄숙한 질문이야. 자, 자네 대답은 뭔가?\"\n\n\"이 늙은 흑인이 죽으면 말이오,\" 흑인이 천천히 말했다. 그의 분위기와 태도가 온통 달라졌다. \"그 자신은 아무 데도 안 가오. 다만 어떤 복된 천사가 와서 그를 데려갈 거요.\"\n\n\"데려가? 어떻게? 네 마리 말이 끄는 마차로, 엘리야를 데려가듯이? 그래서 그를 어디로 데려간다는 건가?\"\n\n\"저 위로요.\" 플리스가 부젓가락을 머리 위로 곧추세워 들고는, 그것을 아주 엄숙하게 그 자리에 그대로 두며 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 자넨 죽으면 우리 큰 돛 꼭대기로 올라갈 작정이다 이거지, 요리사? 하지만 자네 모르나, 높이 올라갈수록 더 추워진다는 걸? 큰 돛 꼭대기라, 응?\"\n\n\"그런 말 한 적 없소.\" 플리스가 다시 부루퉁해져 말했다.\n\n\"저 위라고 했잖아, 안 그래? 자, 이제 자네 자신을 좀 봐, 그리고 자네 부젓가락이 어딜 가리키는지 봐. 하지만 어쩌면 자넨 풋내기 구멍으로 기어 올라 천국에 들어갈 작정인지도 모르지, 요리사. 하지만 안 돼, 안 돼, 요리사, 거긴 정식 길로, 삭구를 빙 돌아가지 않고서는 못 들어가. 까다로운 일이지만, 해내야만 하지, 안 그러면 헛일이야. 하지만 우리 가운데 아직 천국에 가 있는 사람은 아무도 없어. 부젓가락 내려놓게, 요리사, 그리고 내 명령을 듣게. 듣고 있나? 내가 명령을 내릴 때는, 한 손에 모자를 들고, 다른 손은 가슴 위에 척 얹게, 요리사. 뭐야! 그게 자네 가슴이라고, 거기가?—그건 모래주머니잖아! 위로! 위로!—그래, 바로 거기—이제 됐어. 자, 거기에 손을 얹고, 귀를 기울이게.\"\n\n\"귀 쫑긋.\" 늙은 흑인이 시키는 대로 두 손을 얹고서, 두 귀를 한꺼번에 앞쪽으로 가져오려는 듯 희끗희끗한 머리를 부질없이 꿈틀거리며 말했다.\n\n\"자 그럼, 요리사, 자네 이 고래 스테이크가 어찌나 형편없던지, 내가 되도록 빨리 안 보이게 치워 버렸지, 그건 자네도 알지, 안 그래? 자, 앞으로 자네가 여기 내 개인 식탁, 곧 이 캡스턴에 올릴 고래 스테이크를 또 구울 때는, 너무 익혀서 망치지 않도록 어떻게 해야 하는지 내 일러 주지. 한 손에 스테이크를 들고, 다른 손으로 시뻘건 숯덩이를 한 번 보여 주게. 그게 끝나면 접시에 담아. 알겠나? 그리고 자, 내일 말이야, 요리사, 우리가 그 물고기를 토막 낼 때, 그 지느러미 끄트머리를 챙길 수 있게 꼭 곁에 붙어 있게. 그것들을 식초에 절여 두라고. 그리고 꼬리지느러미 끝은, 양념에 푹 담가 두게, 요리사. 자, 이제 가도 좋아.\"\n\n그러나 플리스가 채 세 걸음도 떼기 전에, 그는 다시 불려 왔다.\n\n\"요리사, 내일 밤 중간 당직 때 내 야식으로 커틀릿을 만들어 주게. 듣고 있나? 그럼 어서 가 봐.—어이! 잠깐! 가기 전에 절을 하고 가야지.—또 멈추라니까! 아침으로는 고래 알을—잊지 말게.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 차라리 고래가 저 양반을 잡아먹는 게 낫겠네, 저 양반이 고래를 먹느니. 내 영혼을 걸고, 저 양반은 상어 나리 본인보다 더한 상어야.\" 늙은이가 절뚝거리며 멀어져 가면서 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 이 현명한 탄식을 끝으로 자기 해먹으로 갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 65. The Whale as a Dish.\n\n죽어야 할 인간이 제 등불을 살찌우는 그 짐승을 먹어야 한다니, 게다가 스텁(Stubb)처럼 말하자면 제 등불 빛으로 그놈을 먹어야 한다니, 이것은 너무도 기이한 일이어서 그 역사와 철학을 조금쯤 들여다볼 필요가 있다.\n\n기록에 따르면, 삼백 년 전 프랑스에서는 참고래(Right Whale)의 혀가 진귀한 별미로 여겨져 비싼 값에 거래되었다고 한다. 또한 헨리 8세(Henry) 시절에는 궁정의 어느 요리사가 바비큐로 구운 돌고래에 곁들여 먹을 훌륭한 소스를 고안해 낸 덕분에 후한 상을 받았다고 하는데, 기억하다시피 돌고래도 고래의 한 종류다. 사실 돌고래는 오늘날까지도 훌륭한 식재료로 여겨진다. 그 고기는 당구공만 한 크기의 완자로 빚어지는데, 양념과 향신료를 잘 입히면 자라 완자나 송아지 완자로 착각할 만하다. 던펌린의 늙은 수도사들은 이것을 무척 좋아했다. 그들은 왕실로부터 돌고래에 관한 큰 하사권을 받고 있었다.\n\n사실인즉, 적어도 고래를 사냥하는 자들 사이에서라면 고래는 누구나 인정하는 고귀한 요리로 여겨질 만하지만, 다만 그 양이 너무도 많다는 것이 문제다. 그러나 길이가 거의 백 피트(약 30미터)에 이르는 고기 파이를 앞에 두고 앉게 되면, 그만 입맛이 싹 가셔 버린다. 스텁처럼 편견이라곤 없는 극소수의 사람들만이 요즘 들어 조리한 고래 고기를 먹을 뿐이다. 그러나 에스키모인들은 그렇게 까다롭지 않다. 그들이 고래로 연명하며, 진귀하고 오래 묵은 일등급 고래 기름을 묵은 술처럼 갖추고 있다는 것은 우리 모두가 아는 바다. 그들의 가장 이름난 의사 가운데 하나인 조그란다는 갓난아이에게 고래 지방 띠를 권하는데, 그것이 더없이 즙이 많고 영양가가 높다는 이유에서다. 그러고 보니 떠오르는 이야기가 있다. 오래전 어느 포경선에 의해 그린란드에 우연히 버려진 영국인들이 있었는데, 이 사람들은 실제로 지방을 끓여 내고 난 뒤 해안에 버려진 곰팡이 핀 고래 찌꺼기로 여러 달을 연명했다고 한다. 네덜란드 고래잡이들 사이에서 이 찌꺼기는 \"프리터(fritter)\"라 불리는데, 사실 그 모양이 프리터와 매우 닮았다. 갈색에 바삭하고, 갓 구워 냈을 때는 옛 암스테르담 주부들이 만드는 도넛이나 올리쿡(oly-cook) 같은 냄새가 나기 때문이다. 어찌나 먹음직스러워 보이는지, 아무리 절제심 강한 이방인이라도 좀처럼 거기에 손대지 않고는 못 배긴다.\n\n* 올리쿡: 네덜란드식 튀김 과자로, 오늘날 도넛의 기원이 된 음식.\n그러나 고래를 문명인의 요리로서 한층 더 격을 떨어뜨리는 것은 그 지나친 기름기다. 고래는 바다의 거대한 우량 황소와도 같아서, 너무 기름져 섬세하게 좋은 맛을 내지 못한다. 그 혹을 보라. 만약 그것이 그토록 단단한 기름 덩어리의 피라미드가 아니었다면, 들소의 혹(진귀한 요리로 여겨지는 그것)만큼이나 훌륭한 먹거리가 되었으리라. 그러나 향유(spermaceti) 그 자체는 어찌나 부드럽고 크림처럼 매끄러운지, 자라난 지 석 달째 된 코코넛의 투명하고 반쯤 젤리처럼 굳은 흰 과육과도 같으나, 버터의 대용으로 쓰기에는 그 맛이 너무도 진하다. 그럼에도 많은 고래잡이들은 그것을 다른 어떤 재료에 스며들게 한 뒤 먹는 방법을 알고 있다. 밤의 기나긴 기름 짜는 당직을 서는 동안, 선원들이 선상용 비스킷을 커다란 기름 솥에 담가 잠시 튀겨 내는 것은 흔한 일이다. 나도 이렇게 해서 맛 좋은 저녁을 여러 번 먹었다.\n\n작은 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 경우에는 골이 별미로 여겨진다. 도끼로 두개골이라는 함을 쪼개어 두 개의 통통하고 희끄무레한 뇌엽(꼭 커다란 푸딩 두 덩이를 닮았다)을 꺼낸 다음, 그것을 밀가루와 섞어 더없이 맛깔스러운 요리로 만들어 내는데, 그 풍미가 송아지 머리 고기와 어느 정도 비슷하다. 송아지 머리 고기로 말하자면 어떤 미식가들 사이에서는 제법 인기 있는 요리다. 누구나 알다시피, 미식가 가운데 어떤 젊은 멋쟁이들은 송아지 골을 줄곧 먹어 댄 끝에 차차 제 머릿속에도 골이 조금 생겨나, 송아지 머리와 제 머리를 구별할 수 있게 되는데, 이는 실로 비범한 분별력을 요하는 일이다. 그리고 바로 그런 까닭에, 영리해 보이는 송아지 머리 고기를 앞에 둔 젊은 멋쟁이의 모습은 어쩐지 세상에서 볼 수 있는 가장 서글픈 광경 가운데 하나다. 그 머리는 \"브루투스(Brute), 너마저!\" 하는 표정으로 그를 어딘가 책망하듯 바라보는 것이다.\n\n뭍사람들이 고래 먹기를 혐오스럽게 여기는 것은, 아마도 고래가 그토록 지나치게 기름지기 때문만은 아닐 것이다. 그것은 어떤 식으로든 앞서 말한 고려에서 비롯되는 듯하다. 곧, 인간이 바다에서 갓 죽인 것을 먹어야 한다는 것, 게다가 그것을 그 자신의 빛으로 먹어야 한다는 것 말이다. 그러나 의심할 바 없이, 최초로 황소를 죽인 자는 살인자로 여겨졌으리라. 어쩌면 그는 교수형을 당했을지도 모른다. 그리고 만약 그가 황소들 앞에서 재판을 받았더라면 틀림없이 교수형을 당했을 터이고, 또 어떤 살인자가 그래 마땅하다면 그 역시 마땅히 그러했을 것이다. 토요일 밤 고기 시장에 가서, 길게 늘어선 죽은 네발짐승들을 멍하니 올려다보는 산 두발짐승들의 무리를 보라. 그 광경이 식인종의 턱에서 이빨 하나쯤 빼놓지 않겠는가? 식인종이라고? 도대체 누가 식인종이 아니란 말인가? 내 그대에게 말하노니, 다가올 기근에 대비해 비쩍 마른 선교사를 제 지하실에 소금에 절여 둔 피지(Fejee) 사람이, 곧 그 앞날을 내다본 피지 사람이, 심판의 날에 더 너그러운 처분을 받을 것이다. 거위를 땅에 못 박아 두고 그 부풀어 오른 간을 푸아그라로 즐기는, 문명화되고 계몽된 그대 미식가보다는 말이다.\n\n그런데 스텁은 고래를 그 자신의 빛으로 먹는다고? 그러니 그것은 상처에 모욕까지 더하는 짓이라고? 그대의 칼 손잡이를 보라. 거기, 그 구운 쇠고기를 먹고 있는 문명화되고 계몽된 그대 미식가여, 그 손잡이는 무엇으로 만들어졌는가? 바로 그대가 먹고 있는 그 황소의 형제뻘 되는 짐승의 뼈가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 그리고 그 살진 거위를 다 먹어 치운 뒤에 그대는 무엇으로 이를 쑤시는가? 바로 그 같은 새의 깃털로다. 그리고 거위 학대 방지 협회의 간사는 도대체 어떤 깃펜으로 그 회람을 격식 차려 적어 내려갔단 말인가? 그 협회가 강철 펜 외에는 아무것도 쓰지 않기로 결의한 것은 겨우 한두 달 전의 일이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 66. The Shark Massacre.\n\n남쪽 어장에서 향유고래를 잡은 뒤, 길고 고된 수고 끝에 밤늦게야 배 옆구리에 그것을 끌어다 붙였을 경우, 적어도 일반적인 관례로는 곧바로 해체 작업에 착수하지 않는다. 그 작업이란 지극히 고된 일이어서, 좀처럼 빨리 끝나지 않고, 또 선원 전부가 달려들어야 하기 때문이다. 그러므로 흔히 따르는 방식은 돛을 모두 거두고, 키를 바람 부는 쪽으로 묶어 둔 다음, 날이 밝을 때까지 모두를 갑판 아래 해먹으로 내려보내는 것이다. 다만 그때까지는 정박 당직을 세운다는 조건이 붙는데, 즉 두 사람씩 한 시간 간격으로 짝을 지어, 선원들이 차례로 갑판에 올라가 모든 일이 무사한지 살피는 것이다.\n\n그러나 때로는, 특히 태평양 적도선 부근에서는 이 방식이 전혀 통하지 않는다. 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 상어 떼가 매어 둔 사체 주위로 몰려들기 때문인데, 가령 그것을 여섯 시간쯤 내처 그대로 두었다가는 아침이 되면 뼈대 말고는 거의 아무것도 보이지 않게 될 지경이다. 그렇지만 이 물고기들이 그토록 많지 않은 바다의 대부분 다른 해역에서는, 날카로운 고래잡이 삽으로 그것들을 힘차게 휘저어 쑤셔 대면 그 놀라운 식탐을 때때로 상당히 누그러뜨릴 수 있다. 물론 어떤 경우에는 그런 짓이 도리어 그것들을 자극해 더욱더 기승을 부리게 만드는 것처럼 보이기도 하지만 말이다. 그러나 이번 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 상어들의 경우는 그렇지 않았다. 하기야 그런 광경에 익숙지 않은 사람이 그날 밤 뱃전 너머를 내려다보았다면, 둥근 바다 전체가 하나의 거대한 치즈 덩어리이고 저 상어들이 그 속에서 들끓는 구더기라고 생각했을 법도 하다.\n\n그럼에도 불구하고, 스터브(Stubb)가 저녁 식사를 끝낸 뒤 정박 당직을 세우자, 그리고 그에 따라 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 선수루의 한 선원이 갑판에 올라오자, 상어들 사이에서는 적잖은 소동이 일었다. 두 뱃사람이 즉시 해체 발판을 뱃전 너머로 늘어뜨리고 등불 세 개를 내려, 그 등불이 탁한 바다 위로 긴 빛줄기를 드리우게 한 다음, 자신들의 긴 고래잡이 삽을 내리꽂으며 쉴 새 없이 상어들을 죽여 나갔던 것이다.* 날카로운 강철을 그것들의 두개골 깊숙이 박아 넣었는데, 그곳이야말로 유일한 급소인 듯했다. 그러나 서로 뒤엉켜 발버둥 치는 무리가 일으키는 거품투성이 아수라장 속에서 두 사수는 늘 표적을 명중시킬 수는 없었고, 이로 인해 그 적들의 믿기 어려운 흉포함이 새삼 드러나게 되었다. 그것들은 서로의 쏟아진 창자에 사납게 달려들 뿐 아니라, 유연한 활처럼 몸을 둥글게 구부려 제 창자를 물어뜯었다. 그리하여 그 내장들은 같은 입으로 몇 번이고 거듭 삼켜졌다가, 떡 벌어진 상처 구멍으로 도로 게워져 나오는 듯했다. 이뿐만이 아니었다. 이 짐승들의 시체와 망령을 함부로 건드리는 것은 위험한 일이었다. 이른바 개체의 생명이라 할 만한 것이 떠나간 뒤에도, 그것들의 관절과 뼈마디 자체에는 일종의 종(種) 전체에 깃든, 혹은 범신론적인 생명력이 도사리고 있는 듯했다. 가죽을 벗기려고 죽여서 갑판 위로 끌어 올린 상어 한 마리는, 퀴퀘그가 그 잔혹한 턱의 죽은 뚜껑을 닫아 보려 하자 하마터면 가엾은 퀴퀘그의 손을 잘라 낼 뻔했다.\n\n* 해체 작업에 쓰는 고래잡이 삽은 더없이 좋은 강철로 만들어지며, 크기는 사람이 손을 활짝 편 정도이고, 전체적인 모양은 그 이름의 유래가 된 정원용 연장과 비슷하다. 다만 양옆이 완벽하게 평평하고, 위쪽 끝이 아래쪽보다 상당히 좁다. 이 무기는 늘 가능한 한 날카롭게 갈아 두며, 쓸 때는 면도칼처럼 이따금 숫돌에 갈기도 한다. 그 자루 구멍에는 손잡이로 쓸 스무 자에서 서른 자(약 6미터에서 9미터) 길이의 단단한 장대를 끼운다.\n\n\"퀴퀘그, 어떤 신이 상어 만들었든 상관없어.\" 야만인은 괴로운 듯 손을 위아래로 흔들며 말했다. \"피지의 신이든 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 신이든 상관없어. 하지만 상어 만든 신은 분명 빌어먹을 인디언일 거야.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 67. Cutting In.\n\n토요일 밤이었고, 그 뒤로 이어진 안식일이라니! 안식일을 어기는 일에 관한 한 모든 고래잡이는 직무상의 전문가다. 상아로 뒤덮인 피쿼드호(Pequod)는 마치 도살장처럼 변해 버렸다. 선원들은 하나같이 백정이 되었다. 우리가 바다의 신들에게 붉은 황소 만 마리를 바치는 중이라 해도 믿을 만했을 것이다.\n\n우선, 그 거대한 절단용 도르래 장치가 등장한다. 그것은 다른 묵직한 물건들 가운데서도 흔히 초록색으로 칠해진 한 무더기의 활차(滑車)로 이루어져 있어, 어떤 장사도 혼자서는 결코 들어 올릴 수 없는 것이다. 이 거대한 포도송이 같은 덩어리가 큰 돛대 꼭대기로 끌어 올려진 다음, 갑판 위에서 가장 튼튼한 지점인 아래쪽 돛대머리에 단단히 결박된다. 이 복잡한 장치 사이를 굽이굽이 통과해 나온 닻줄 같은 밧줄의 끝은 양묘기(揚錨機)로 이어지고, 도르래 장치의 거대한 아래쪽 활차는 고래 위로 흔들려 걸쳐진다. 이 활차에는 무게가 백 파운드(약 45킬로그램)쯤 되는 큼직한 지방 갈고리가 매달린다. 이제 뱃전 너머로 발판에 매달린 채, 일등항해사 스타벅(Starbuck)과 이등항해사 스터브(Stubb)가 각자 긴 삽을 들고서, 두 옆지느러미 중 가까운 쪽 바로 위에 갈고리를 끼워 넣을 구멍을 고래 몸통에 파기 시작한다. 이 일이 끝나면 그 구멍 둘레로 넓고 반원을 그리는 선이 새겨지고, 갈고리가 박힌다. 그러면 선원들 대부분이 거친 합창을 터뜨리며 빽빽한 한 무리를 이루어 양묘기를 감아 당기기 시작한다. 그 순간, 배 전체가 한쪽으로 기울어진다. 배에 박힌 모든 볼트가 서리 내린 날씨에 낡은 집의 못대가리가 튀어나오듯 들썩이고, 배는 떨고, 부르르 진동하며, 겁에 질린 돛대머리를 하늘을 향해 끄덕거린다. 배는 점점 더 고래 쪽으로 기울고, 양묘기가 헐떡이며 한 번씩 당겨 올릴 때마다 물결도 도움을 보태듯 한 번씩 출렁여 답한다. 그러다 마침내 빠르고 소스라치는 우지끈 소리가 들린다. 거대한 물보라와 함께 배는 고래로부터 위로, 뒤로 굴러 일어서고, 의기양양한 도르래 장치가 떨어져 나온 첫 번째 지방 띠의 반원형 끄트머리를 끌어당기며 모습을 드러낸다. 자, 지방이 오렌지 껍질이 오렌지를 감싸듯 고래를 정확히 감싸고 있는 까닭에, 그것을 벗겨 내는 일도 오렌지를 때로 나선형으로 돌려 가며 껍질을 벗기듯 정확히 그렇게 이루어진다. 양묘기가 끊임없이 유지하는 장력이 고래를 물속에서 쉴 새 없이 빙글빙글 돌리고, 그동안 항해사 스타벅과 스터브의 삽이 동시에 베어 나가는 이른바 \"스카프\"라 불리는 선을 따라 지방이 한 가닥 띠를 이루며 고르게 벗겨진다. 그리고 그렇게 벗겨지는 그 속도 그대로, 아니 바로 그 벗겨지는 동작 자체에 의해, 지방 띠는 내내 점점 더 높이 위로 끌어 올려져 마침내 그 위쪽 끝이 큰 돛대 꼭대기에 닿을 듯 스친다. 그러면 양묘기에 매달린 사람들은 감아 당기기를 멈추고, 잠깐 동안 그 엄청난, 피가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 덩어리가 하늘에서 내려진 듯이 이리저리 흔들린다. 그곳에 있는 사람은 누구나 그것이 흔들릴 때 잘 피하도록 단단히 조심해야 한다. 그러지 않으면 그것에 따귀를 얻어맞고 뱃전 너머로 곤두박질쳐 떨어질 수도 있기 때문이다.\n\n이제 곁에서 거들던 작살잡이 하나가 보딩소드(boarding-sword)라 불리는 길고 날카로운 무기를 들고 앞으로 나선다. 그는 기회를 노리다가, 흔들리는 덩어리의 아래쪽에 능숙한 솜씨로 제법 큰 구멍을 베어 낸다. 그러고는 다음에 이어질 작업을 준비하기 위해, 지방을 붙들어 두려고 두 번째로 번갈아 쓰는 큰 도르래 장치의 끝을 이 구멍에 건다. 그런 다음 이 솜씨 좋은 검객은 모두에게 물러서라 경고하고는, 다시 한 번 그 덩어리를 향해 정밀하게 돌진하여, 옆으로 비스듬히 몇 차례 필사적으로 내리찍듯 베어, 그것을 완전히 둘로 갈라 놓는다. 그리하여 짧은 아래쪽 부분은 여전히 매여 있는 채로, 담요 조각(blanket-piece)이라 불리는 길고 위쪽인 띠는 자유로이 흔들리며 내릴 채비를 모두 갖추게 된다. 이제 앞쪽의 감아 올리는 사람들이 다시 노래를 시작하고, 한쪽 도르래 장치가 고래에서 두 번째 지방 띠를 벗겨 끌어 올리는 동안, 다른 한쪽은 천천히 풀려 내려가, 첫 번째 띠가 바로 아래에 있는 큰 승강구를 통해 지방 저장실(blubber-room)이라 불리는 텅 빈 방으로 내려간다. 이 어스름한 방 안에서는 여러 날렵한 손길들이 그 긴 담요 조각을, 마치 살아 꿈틀대는 거대한 뱀 다발이라도 되는 양 쉴 새 없이 둘둘 사려 놓는다. 그렇게 작업은 이어진다. 두 도르래 장치가 동시에 끌어 올리고 내리며, 고래도 양묘기도 들썩이고, 끌어당기는 자들은 노래하고, 지방 저장실의 사내들은 사리고, 항해사들은 띠를 베어 내고, 배는 삐걱대며 버틴다. 그리고 모든 선원들은 이따금 욕설을 내뱉는데, 그것은 이 전반적인 마찰을 누그러뜨리기 위한 나름의 방편인 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 68. The Blanket.\n\n내가 적잖이 마음을 쏟아 온 주제가 하나 있으니, 골치깨나 썩이는 그 문제란 바로 고래의 피부다. 나는 이 문제를 두고 바다 위의 노련한 고래잡이들과도, 뭍의 학식 깊은 박물학자들과도 갑론을박을 벌여 왔다. 처음의 내 견해는 지금도 변함이 없으나, 어디까지나 그것은 하나의 견해일 뿐이다.\n\n문제는 이것이다. 고래의 피부는 무엇이며 어디에 있는가? 고래의 지방층(blubber)이 무엇인지는 이미 알고 있을 것이다. 그 지방층은 단단하고 결이 촘촘한 쇠고기와 비슷한 데가 있으나, 그보다 더 질기고 탄력이 있으며 촘촘하고, 두께는 8인치나 10인치에서 12인치, 15인치(약 38센티미터)에 이르기까지 다양하다.\n\n자, 어떤 짐승의 피부를 두고 그런 정도의 단단함과 두께를 지녔다고 말하는 것이 처음에는 아무리 터무니없게 들릴지라도, 사실 그런 추정에 반박할 만한 근거는 전혀 없다. 고래의 몸에서 그 지방층 말고는 다른 어떤 빽빽한 외피층도 벗겨 낼 수 없기 때문이다. 그리고 어느 동물이든 가장 바깥쪽을 감싸는 층이 어지간히 빽빽하다면, 그것을 피부 말고 무엇이라 부를 수 있겠는가? 사실, 흠집 하나 없는 고래의 사체에서라면 손으로 한없이 얇고 투명한 물질을 긁어낼 수 있는데, 그것은 부레풀의 가장 얇은 조각과 다소 비슷하되, 새틴처럼 부드럽고 나긋나긋하다. 다만 이는 마르기 전의 이야기이며, 일단 마르고 나면 수축하여 두꺼워질 뿐만 아니라 제법 단단하고 부서지기 쉬워진다. 나는 그렇게 말린 조각을 몇 개 가지고 있어, 고래에 관한 내 책들의 갈피표로 쓰고 있다. 앞서 말했듯 그것은 투명해서, 인쇄된 책장 위에 얹어 놓으면 마치 글자를 확대해 주는 듯한 느낌이 들어 혼자 흐뭇해할 때가 있다. 어찌 됐든, 말하자면 고래 자신의 안경을 통해 고래에 관한 글을 읽는 것은 즐거운 일이다. 그러나 여기서 내가 말하려는 요지는 이것이다. 내가 인정하건대 고래의 온몸을 감싸고 있는 그 한없이 얇은 부레풀 같은 물질은, 고래의 피부라기보다는 차라리 피부의 피부쯤으로 보아야 한다는 것이다. 그토록 어마어마한 고래의 진짜 피부가 갓난아기의 피부보다 더 얇고 여리다고 말하는 것은 그야말로 우스꽝스러운 노릇이기 때문이다. 하지만 이 이야기는 이쯤 해 두자.\n\n지방층을 고래의 피부라고 가정하자. 그렇다면 이 피부는, 매우 큰 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 경우, 기름 백 배럴에 맞먹는 양을 내준다. 게다가 그 짜낸 기름이 양으로, 아니 무게로 따져 외피 전체 물질의 4분의 3에 불과하고 전부가 아님을 헤아려 본다면, 한낱 외피의 한낱 일부분이 그토록 호수만 한 액체를 내놓는 그 살아 있는 거구가 얼마나 어마어마한지 어렴풋이나마 짐작할 수 있으리라. 1톤을 10배럴로 잡으면, 고래 피부 물질의 4분의 3에 해당하는 것만으로도 순중량 10톤이 나오는 셈이다.\n\n살아 있을 때, 향유고래의 눈에 보이는 표면은 그것이 보여 주는 숱한 경이 가운데서도 결코 작은 것이 아니다. 그 표면은 거의 어김없이 온통, 무수한 곧은 자국들이 빽빽이 늘어서서 비스듬히 가로지르고 또 가로지르는데, 그 모습이 이탈리아의 가장 정교한 선 동판화의 선들과 어딘가 닮았다. 그러나 이 자국들은 앞서 말한 부레풀 같은 물질 위에 새겨진 것이 아니라, 마치 몸뚱이 자체에 새겨진 듯, 그 물질을 투과해 보이는 듯하다. 그뿐이 아니다. 어떤 경우에는, 재빠르고 눈썰미 있는 이의 눈에는 그 선 자국들이 진짜 동판화에서처럼 다른 갖가지 그림을 그려 낼 바탕이 되어 준다. 이 그림들은 상형문자와도 같으니, 다시 말해 피라미드 벽면의 저 신비로운 부호들을 상형문자라 부른다면, 지금 이 경우에도 그 말을 쓰는 것이 마땅하다. 나는 특히 어느 향유고래 한 마리에 새겨진 상형문자를 또렷이 기억하는데, 그 덕분에 어퍼미시시피 강가의 이름난 상형문자 절벽에 새겨진 옛 인디언(Indian) 글자를 그려 놓은 도판을 보고 몹시 놀랐다. 그 신비로운 바위들과 마찬가지로, 신비로운 무늬가 새겨진 고래 역시 끝내 해독할 수 없는 채로 남아 있다. 인디언의 바위 이야기가 나온 김에 또 한 가지가 떠오른다. 향유고래의 외양이 보여 주는 다른 온갖 현상 말고도, 이 고래는 등, 그중에서도 특히 옆구리가, 제멋대로 아무렇게나 난 거친 흠집들이 무수해서 규칙적인 선 무늬가 상당 부분 지워진 모습을 드물지 않게 드러낸다. 거대한 부빙(浮氷)들과 격렬하게 긁히며 부딪친 자국을 지니고 있으리라고 아가시가 상상한 저 뉴잉글랜드(New England) 해안의 바위들이야말로, 바로 이 점에서 향유고래와 적잖이 닮았으리라고 나는 말하고 싶다. 또 내게는, 고래에 난 그런 흠집들이 아마도 다른 고래들과 적대적으로 부딪쳐 생긴 것이 아닐까 싶다. 그런 자국을 나는 이 종의 크고 다 자란 수컷들에게서 가장 많이 보아 왔기 때문이다.\n\n고래의 피부, 곧 지방층이라는 이 문제에 관해 한두 마디 더 보태고자 한다. 앞서 말했듯, 그것은 고래의 몸에서 긴 조각으로 벗겨 내며, 이를 담요 조각(blanket-piece)이라 부른다. 대개의 항해 용어가 그렇듯, 이 말 또한 더없이 절묘하고 함축적이다. 고래는 실제로 마치 진짜 담요나 침대보를 두른 듯이 제 지방층에 둘둘 감싸여 있기 때문이다. 아니, 그보다 더 적절히 말하자면, 머리 위로 푹 뒤집어쓰고 발끝까지 자락이 늘어진 인디언의 판초를 걸친 격이다. 바로 이렇게 제 몸을 아늑하게 감싸 덮은 덕분에, 고래는 어떤 날씨에도, 어떤 바다와 어떤 시간과 어떤 물때에도 제 몸을 편안하게 지킬 수 있다. 가령 그린란드고래가, 그 아늑한 외투를 두르지 못한 채 저 몸서리치도록 차디찬 북녘 바다에 있다면 어떻게 되겠는가? 하기야, 저 극북의 바닷물 속에서도 더없이 활기차게 사는 다른 물고기들이 있긴 하다. 그러나 유념할 것은, 이들이 냉혈에 허파도 없는 물고기여서, 제 배때기 자체가 냉장고나 다름없는 부류라는 점이다. 이런 것들은 겨울 나그네가 여관 난롯불 앞에서 몸을 녹이듯, 빙산의 바람을 막아 주는 그늘에서 제 몸을 덥힌다. 반면에 고래는 사람과 마찬가지로 허파가 있고 더운 피가 흐른다. 그 피가 얼면, 고래는 죽는다. 그러니 — 설명을 듣기 전이라면 — 사람에게 그렇듯 몸의 온기가 없어서는 안 될 이 거대한 괴물이, 입술까지 저 북극 바다에 잠긴 채로 평생을 제집처럼 지낸다는 것이 얼마나 놀라운 일인가! 그 바다에서는 선원들이 떨어지면 더러는 몇 달 뒤에야 발견되는데, 마치 호박(琥珀) 속에 들러붙은 파리처럼, 얼음판의 한복판에 똑바로 선 채 얼어붙은 모습으로 나오기도 한다. 그러나 더욱 놀라운 것은, 실험으로 증명된 바와 같이, 북극고래의 피가 한여름 보르네오 흑인의 피보다 더 따뜻하다는 사실이다.\n\n내게는, 바로 여기서 우리가 강인한 개체적 생명력이라는 보기 드문 미덕과, 두꺼운 벽이라는 보기 드문 미덕과, 내부의 드넓음이라는 보기 드문 미덕을 보게 되는 듯하다. 오, 인간이여! 고래에 감탄하고 고래를 본받으라! 그대 또한 얼음 속에서도 따뜻함을 잃지 말라. 그대 또한 이 세상에 살되 이 세상에 속하지는 말라. 적도에서는 서늘하라. 극지에서는 그대의 피를 흐르게 하라. 성베드로 대성당의 저 거대한 둥근 지붕처럼, 또 저 거대한 고래처럼, 오, 인간이여! 어느 계절에나 그대 자신만의 체온을 지키라.\n\n그러나 이런 멋진 이치를 가르치기란 얼마나 쉽고, 또 얼마나 가망 없는 일인가! 사람이 세운 건축물 중에 성베드로 대성당처럼 둥근 지붕을 인 것이 몇이나 되며, 살아 있는 것들 중에 고래만큼 거대한 것이 몇이나 되겠는가!\n\n\"쇠사슬을 끌어들여라! 시체는 고물 쪽으로 흘려보내라!\"\n\n거대한 도르래 장치들은 이제 제 소임을 다했다. 머리가 잘려 나가고 껍질이 벗겨진 고래의 하얀 몸뚱이는 대리석 무덤처럼 번쩍인다. 빛깔은 달라졌으나, 부피만큼은 눈에 띄게 줄어든 데가 없다. 여전히 거대하다. 그것은 천천히 점점 더 멀리 떠내려가는데, 그 주위의 물은 만족을 모르는 상어들에게 갈가리 찢기고 튀겨 오르며, 그 위의 공중은 비명을 질러대는 게걸스러운 새 떼의 비행으로 어지러운데, 그 부리들은 고래에게 꽂히는 수많은 모욕의 단검과도 같다. 머리 없는 그 거대한 흰 유령은 배에서 점점 더 멀리 떠가고, 그것이 그렇게 떠가는 한 걸음 한 걸음마다, 마치 평방 단위로 펼쳐진 상어 떼와 입방 단위로 들끓는 새 떼가 살기 어린 소란을 더욱 키워 가는 듯하다. 거의 멈춰 선 배에서 그 끔찍한 광경이 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 내내 보인다. 구름 한 점 없이 부드러운 쪽빛 하늘 아래, 즐거운 산들바람에 실려, 잔잔한 바다의 고운 얼굴 위로, 저 거대한 죽음의 덩어리는 끝없는 원경 속으로 사라질 때까지 하염없이 떠가고 또 떠간다.\n\n세상에 이보다 더 구슬프고 더 조롱 어린 장례식은 없으리라! 바다의 독수리들은 모두 경건한 상복을 차려입었고, 하늘의 상어들은 모조리 격식을 갖춰 검정이나 얼룩무늬 옷을 둘렀다. 살아생전 그 고래가 혹여 도움을 필요로 했더라도, 짐작건대 이들 가운데 고래를 도울 자는 거의 없었으리라. 그러나 고래의 장례 연회 앞에서는 더없이 경건하게 달려든다. 오, 이 땅의 끔찍한 독수리 근성이여! 가장 힘센 고래조차 거기서 자유롭지 못하구나.\n\n그러나 이것이 끝은 아니다. 시신은 그토록 능욕당했어도, 복수심에 찬 유령이 살아남아 그 위를 맴돌며 두려움을 자아낸다. 멀리서 어느 겁 많은 군함이나 길을 헤매는 탐사선이 그것을 발견하는데, 거리가 들끓는 새 떼는 가려 주지만 그래도 햇빛 속에 떠 있는 흰 덩어리와 그 위로 높이 솟구치는 흰 물보라만은 여전히 보일 때, 누구도 해치지 않는 그 고래의 시체는 곧장 떨리는 손가락에 의해 항해일지에 기록되는 것이다. 이 부근에 모래톱과 암초와 부서지는 파도 있음. 주의할 것! 그리하여 그 뒤로 여러 해 동안, 어쩌면 배들은 그곳을 피해 다닐 터인데, 이는 어리석은 양들이 텅 빈 허공을 뛰어넘는 격이니, 애초에 우두머리가 막대기를 들이댔을 때 거기서 뛰어넘었기 때문이다. 바로 그것이 그대들의 선례라는 법칙이요, 그것이 그대들의 전통이라는 효용이며, 땅에 뿌리내린 적도 없고 이제는 공중에서 맴돌지조차 않는 낡은 믿음이 끈질기게 살아남는 이야기다! 그것이 바로 정통 교리라는 것이다!\n이렇듯, 살아 있을 때 거대한 고래의 몸뚱이는 그 적들에게 실로 공포의 대상이었을지 모르나, 죽고 나면 그 유령이 온 세상을 향해 아무 힘도 없는 공황(恐慌)이 되고 마는 것이다.\n\n그대는 유령을 믿는 사람인가, 벗이여? 콕레인의 유령 말고도 다른 유령들이 있으며, 그것들을 믿는 자들 중에는 존슨 박사보다 훨씬 더 깊이 있는 사람들도 있다.\n\n* 콕레인의 유령: 18세기 런던 콕레인에서 일어난 유명한 유령 소동으로, 존슨 박사가 진상 조사에 참여했으나 결국 사기극으로 밝혀졌다.\n\n# CHAPTER 70. The Sphynx.\n\n리바이어던의 몸뚱이를 완전히 벗겨 내기에 앞서 먼저 머리를 잘라 냈다는 사실을 빠뜨려서는 안 될 것이다. 그런데 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 머리를 자르는 일은 과학적이고 해부학적인 솜씨를 요하는 작업으로, 노련한 고래 외과의들이 자못 자랑스러워하는 일이다. 그럴 만한 까닭이 없지 않다.\n\n생각해 보라. 고래에게는 제대로 목이라 부를 만한 부위가 전혀 없다. 오히려 머리와 몸이 맞닿은 듯이 보이는 바로 그 자리가 그놈의 몸에서 가장 두꺼운 부분이다. 또한 외과의는 위쪽에서 작업을 해야 하며, 그와 그가 다루는 대상 사이에는 여덟 자에서 열 자(약 2.4미터에서 3미터)나 되는 간격이 가로놓여 있고, 그 대상은 빛깔이 탁하고 출렁이며 때로는 사납게 들끓고 부서지는 바다 속에 거의 가려져 있다는 점을 기억하라. 또 이런 고약한 여건 속에서 그는 살을 여러 자나 깊숙이 베어 들어가야 한다는 것도 명심하라. 그리고 그렇게 땅속을 파듯 작업하면서, 그렇게 낸 칼자국 속을 단 한 번도 들여다보지 못한 채 — 그 틈은 끊임없이 오므라들거니와 — 인접한 모든 금지 부위들을 능숙하게 피해 가면서, 척추가 두개골에 박혀 드는 바로 그 결정적인 지점에서 정확히 등뼈를 갈라내야 한다. 그러니 향유고래의 머리를 자르는 데 십 분이면 충분하다던 스터브(Stubb)의 호언장담에 그대는 경탄하지 않을 수 있겠는가?\n\n머리를 처음 잘라 내면 그것을 고물 쪽으로 떨구어 밧줄로 매어 두었다가, 몸뚱이를 다 벗길 때까지 그대로 둔다. 그 일이 끝났을 때, 만일 그것이 작은 고래의 것이라면 갑판으로 끌어올려 천천히 처리한다. 그러나 다 자란 리바이어던의 경우에는 이것이 불가능하다. 향유고래의 머리는 그 몸 전체의 거의 삼분의 일을 차지하는 까닭에, 포경선의 그 거대한 도르래 장치로도 그런 짐덩이를 통째로 매달아 올리려는 것은, 네덜란드의 헛간을 보석상의 저울로 달아 보려는 것만큼이나 헛된 일이기 때문이다.\n\n피쿼드(Pequod)호가 잡은 고래의 목을 자르고 몸뚱이를 벗겨 낸 뒤, 머리는 뱃전에 매달아 올렸다 — 바다 밖으로 절반쯤 나오게 하여, 여전히 대부분이 제 본래의 원소에 떠받쳐지도록 한 것이다. 그리하여 아래쪽 돛대 꼭대기에서 내리누르는 엄청난 견인력 때문에 무리하게 짐을 진 배가 그쪽으로 가파르게 기울어지고, 그쪽 활대 끝이 죄다 파도 위로 기중기처럼 뻗어 나간 가운데, 피가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 그 머리는 마치 유디트의 허리띠에 매달린 거인 홀로페르네스의 머리처럼 피쿼드호의 허리께에 매달려 있었다.\n\n이 마지막 작업이 끝났을 때는 정오였고, 선원들은 식사를 하러 아래로 내려갔다. 조금 전까지 소란하던, 그러나 이제는 텅 비어 버린 갑판 위로 정적이 감돌았다. 온 세상을 뒤덮은 한 송이 노란 연꽃 같은, 짙은 구릿빛 적막이 소리 없이 헤아릴 수 없는 잎새를 바다 위에 점점 더 활짝 펼쳐 가고 있었다.\n\n잠시 시간이 흐른 뒤, 이 소리 없는 적막 속으로 에이해브(Ahab)가 홀로 선실에서 올라왔다. 뒷갑판을 몇 바퀴 돌던 그는 걸음을 멈추고 뱃전 너머를 바라보았다. 그러고는 천천히 큰 돛대의 쇠사슬로 다가가, 고래의 목을 잘라 낸 뒤로 여태 거기 남아 있던 스터브의 긴 삽을 집어 들더니, 그것을 절반쯤 매달린 그 덩어리의 아래쪽에 박아 넣고 다른 쪽 끝을 목발처럼 한쪽 겨드랑이에 괴고서, 그 머리에 두 눈을 골똘히 못 박은 채 몸을 기울이고 서 있었다.\n\n그것은 검고 두건을 뒤집어쓴 듯한 머리였다. 그토록 깊은 적막의 한복판에 매달려 있는 그것은 마치 사막의 스핑크스 같았다. \"말해 보라, 그대 거대하고 존엄한 머리여.\" 에이해브가 중얼거렸다. \"비록 수염으로 꾸며지지는 않았으나 여기저기 이끼가 끼어 백발이 성성한 듯 보이는 그대여, 말하라, 위대한 머리여, 그대 속에 든 비밀을 우리에게 일러 다오. 모든 잠수부 가운데 그대는 가장 깊은 곳까지 잠겨 보았으리라. 지금 머리 위의 해가 그 위에 어른거리는 그 머리는, 일찍이 이 세상의 토대 사이를 누비고 다녔다. 기록되지 못한 이름들과 함대들이 녹슬어 가고, 알려지지 않은 희망과 닻들이 썩어 가는 곳, 살육의 선창 속에서 이 군함 같은 지구가 익사한 수백만의 뼈를 바닥짐으로 싣고 있는 그곳 — 그 무시무시한 물의 나라야말로 그대에게는 가장 정든 보금자리였으리라. 그대는 종(鐘)도 잠수부도 결코 가 닿지 못한 곳까지 다녀왔다. 잠 못 이루는 어머니들이 자기 목숨을 내주고서라도 눕고 싶어 했을 그 자리에서, 수많은 뱃사람들 곁에 나란히 잠들기도 했으리라. 그대는 불타는 배에서 함께 뛰어내리는, 서로 얽힌 연인들을 보았다. 가슴과 가슴을 맞댄 채 그들은 환호하는 파도 아래로 가라앉았으니, 하늘이 그들을 저버린 듯 보이던 그 순간에도 서로에게만은 한결같았다. 그대는 한밤의 갑판에서 해적들에게 내던져진, 살해당한 항해사를 보았다. 그는 몇 시간이고 저 채울 줄 모르는 아가리의 더 깊은 한밤 속으로 떨어져 내렸으되, 그를 죽인 자들은 여전히 멀쩡히 항해를 이어 갔으니 — 그사이 잽싼 번개가, 의로운 남편을 활짝 벌린 채 애타게 기다리는 두 팔로 데려다주었을 이웃의 배를 산산이 부수어 놓았다. 오, 머리여! 그대는 행성을 쪼개 버리고 아브라함마저 불신자로 만들 만큼 많은 것을 보았건만, 그대에게서는 단 한 마디도 흘러나오지 않는구나!\"\n\n\"돛이다, 어이!\" 큰 돛대 꼭대기에서 의기양양한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래? 그것 참, 반가운 소리로구나.\" 에이해브가 별안간 몸을 곧추세우며 외쳤다. 그러는 사이 먹구름 떼가 그의 이마에서 죄다 걷혀 나갔다. \"이 죽음 같은 적막 속에서 들려오는 저 생기 넘치는 외침이라면, 나보다 나은 사람조차 거의 회심시킬 만하구나. — 어느 쪽이냐?\"\n\n\"우현 이물 쪽으로 세 포인트입니다, 선장님. 게다가 그쪽 바람을 우리 쪽으로 몰고 오고 있습니다!\"\n\n\"갈수록 좋군그래, 이 사람아. 이참에 성 바울이 그 길로 와서, 바람 한 점 없는 내게 그의 바람을 좀 가져다주었으면 좋으련만! 오, 자연이여, 그리고 오, 인간의 영혼이여! 그대들의 그 얽히고설킨 닮은꼴은 그 어떤 말로도 다 담아낼 수 없도다! 아무리 작은 원자 하나라도 물질 위에서 움직이거나 살아 숨 쉬는 것치고, 마음속에 그 교묘한 분신을 지니지 않은 것이 없으니.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 71. The Jeroboam’s Story.\n\n손에 손을 맞잡듯 배와 산들바람이 함께 나아갔다. 그러나 바람이 배보다 빨랐기에, 이내 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 흔들리기 시작했다.\n\n이윽고 망원경 너머로 그 낯선 배의 보트들과 사람이 올라가 있는 돛대 꼭대기가 보였으니, 그 배가 고래잡이배임이 드러났다. 그러나 그 배가 바람 불어오는 쪽으로 너무 멀리 떨어진 채 빠르게 스쳐 지나가며, 보아하니 어느 다른 어장으로 가는 길인 듯하여, 피쿼드호로서는 그 배에 닿기를 바랄 수 없었다. 그리하여 어떤 응답이 올지 보려고 신호기를 내걸었다.\n\n여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 군함과 마찬가지로 미국 포경 선단의 배들도 저마다 고유의 신호를 지니고 있으며, 그 모든 신호가 해당 선박들의 이름과 함께 한 권의 책으로 묶여 모든 선장에게 비치되어 있다. 그 덕분에 고래잡이 선장들은 상당한 거리에서도 적잖이 수월하게 바다 위에서 서로를 알아볼 수 있다.\n\n피쿼드호의 신호에 마침내 그 낯선 배가 제 신호를 내걸어 응답하니, 그 배가 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 제로보암(Jeroboam)호임이 드러났다. 활대를 직각으로 돌린 그 배는 바람을 받으며 다가와 피쿼드호의 바람 아래쪽 옆구리에 나란히 자리를 잡고 보트 한 척을 내렸다. 보트는 곧 가까이 다가왔다. 그러나 스타벅(Starbuck)의 명에 따라 찾아오는 선장을 맞으려고 뱃전 사다리가 내려지는 동안, 문제의 그 낯선 선장은 보트 고물에서 손을 내저어 그런 절차가 전혀 필요 없음을 알렸다. 알고 보니 제로보암호에는 악성 전염병이 돌고 있었고, 그 배의 선장 메이휴(Mayhew)는 피쿼드호의 선원들에게 병을 옮길까 두려워하고 있었던 것이다. 비록 그 자신과 보트의 선원들은 아직 병에 걸리지 않았고, 그의 배가 소총 한 발 거리의 절반쯤 떨어져 있으며, 그 사이로 더럽혀질 수 없는 바다와 공기가 굽이쳐 흐르고 있었음에도, 그는 양심에 따라 뭍에서 행하는 소심한 격리 관습을 고집하며 피쿼드호와 직접 닿는 것을 단호히 거부했다.\n\n그러나 이것이 모든 소통을 가로막은 것은 결코 아니었다. 제 배와 피쿼드호 사이에 몇 야드의 간격을 유지하면서, 제로보암호의 보트는 이따금 노를 저어 가며, 큰 돛대의 윗돛을 뒤로 젖힌 채 무겁게 바다를 헤치며 나아가는 피쿼드호와(이 무렵에는 바람이 자못 거세게 불고 있었다) 나란히 가려고 애썼다. 물론 더러는 큰 너울이 갑작스레 들이치는 통에 보트가 한참 앞으로 떠밀려 가기도 했으나, 곧 능숙하게 제자리로 돌아오곤 했다. 이런 일과 그 밖에 비슷한 방해가 이따금 끼어드는 가운데서도 두 진영 사이에서는 대화가 이어졌다. 그러나 그 간간이 또 다른, 사뭇 다른 종류의 방해가 끼어드는 일도 없지 않았다.\n\n제로보암호의 보트에서 노를 젓고 있는 자 가운데, 개개인의 별난 인물됨이 전부를 이루는 저 거친 포경 생활에서조차 유독 기이한 모습을 한 사내가 하나 있었다. 그는 키 작고 땅딸막한 데다 아직 젊은 사내로, 얼굴 가득 주근깨가 뿌려져 있고 노란 머리칼이 더부룩했다. 빛바랜 호두색의, 자락이 긴 데다 신비주의적으로 재단된 외투가 그를 감싸고 있었으며, 그 겹쳐진 소매는 손목께까지 걷어 올려져 있었다. 그의 두 눈에는 깊이 자리 잡은 광신적 광기가 서려 있었다.\n\n이 인물이 처음 눈에 띄자마자 스터브(Stubb)는 외쳤다. \"저놈이다! 저놈이야! 타운호(Town-Ho)호 선원들이 우리한테 말해 준 그 긴 옷 차림의 어릿광대 놈!\" 스터브가 여기서 빗대어 말한 것은, 얼마 전 피쿼드호가 타운호호와 마주쳤을 때 들었던 제로보암호와 그 선원 중 한 사람에 관한 기이한 이야기였다. 그 이야기와 나중에 알게 된 바에 따르면, 문제의 그 어릿광대는 제로보암호의 거의 모든 이들 위에 놀라운 영향력을 떨치게 된 모양이었다. 그의 내력은 이러했다.\n\n그는 본디 네스큐나의 광신적인 셰이커교도 무리 속에서 자라났으며, 그곳에서 위대한 예언자 노릇을 했다. 그 미치광이 같은 비밀 집회에서 그는 여러 차례 들창문을 통해 하늘에서 내려오는 시늉을 하며, 자신이 조끼 주머니에 지니고 있다는 일곱째 대접을 곧 열겠노라 선포하곤 했는데, 그 대접에는 화약이 아니라 아편 팅크가 담겨 있으리라고들 여겨졌다. 어떤 기이한 사도적 변덕에 사로잡힌 그는 네스큐나를 떠나 낸터킷으로 왔고, 그곳에서 광기 특유의 교활함을 발휘하여 침착하고 분별 있는 겉모습을 꾸미고는, 제로보암호의 포경 항해에 풋내기 지원자로 제 몸을 내놓았다. 사람들은 그를 채용했다. 그러나 배가 뭍을 벗어나 보이지 않게 되자마자 그의 광기는 봇물 터지듯 솟구쳤다. 그는 자신을 대천사 가브리엘이라 선언하며 선장에게 바다로 뛰어들라고 명했다. 그는 선언문을 발표하여 자신을 바다 섬들의 구원자요 온 오세아니아의 총대리(總代理)로 내세웠다. 이런 것들을 선포할 때의 그 흔들림 없는 진지함, 잠들 줄 모르는 흥분된 상상력의 어둡고 대담한 활약, 그리고 진짜 광기가 빚어내는 온갖 초자연적 공포가 한데 어우러져, 이 가브리엘을 무지한 선원 대다수의 마음속에 신성의 분위기로 감싸 놓았다. 더구나 그들은 그를 두려워했다. 그러나 그런 사내는 배에서 그다지 실질적인 쓸모가 없었고, 더욱이 제 내키는 때가 아니면 일하기를 거부했으므로, 그를 믿지 않는 선장은 그를 떨쳐 버리고 싶어 했다. 그러나 선장이 처음 닿는 적당한 항구에 자기를 내려놓을 작정임을 눈치챈 그 대천사는 즉시 제 모든 봉인과 대접을 열어, 만약 그 계획이 실행된다면 배와 선원 전부를 무조건 멸망에 바치겠노라 저주했다. 그가 선원들 가운데 제 제자들에게 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 마침내 그들은 떼를 지어 선장에게 가서, 가브리엘을 배에서 내쫓으면 자기들 중 한 사람도 남지 않겠다고 말했다. 그리하여 선장은 제 계획을 단념할 수밖에 없었다. 또한 그들은 가브리엘이 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 짓을 하든 그가 조금이라도 학대당하는 것을 용납하지 않았다. 그리하여 가브리엘은 배 안에서 완전한 자유를 누리게 되었다. 이 모든 일의 결과로, 그 대천사는 선장이나 항해사들 따위는 거의, 아니 전혀 안중에도 두지 않게 되었다. 그리고 전염병이 돌기 시작한 뒤로 그는 그 어느 때보다 더 큰 권세를 휘둘렀으니, 그가 역병이라 부르는 그것이 오로지 자신의 명령 아래 있으며, 자신의 뜻에 따르지 않고는 결코 그칠 수 없다고 선언했다. 대개 가련한 처지의 선원들은 그 앞에서 굽실거렸고, 더러는 아첨까지 했으며, 그의 지시에 복종하여 때로는 신을 대하듯 그에게 몸소 경배를 바치기도 했다. 이런 일들이 믿기 어렵게 여겨질지도 모른다. 그러나 아무리 놀랍다 한들 그것은 사실이다. 광신자들의 역사에서, 광신자 자신의 헤아릴 수 없는 자기기만보다 더 놀라운 것은, 그토록 많은 다른 이들을 속이고 홀려 내는 그의 헤아릴 수 없는 능력이다. 그러나 이제 피쿼드호로 돌아갈 때다.\n\n\"나는 그대의 전염병 따위 두렵지 않소, 이보시오.\" 에이해브(Ahab)가 뱃전에서 보트 고물에 서 있는 메이휴 선장에게 말했다. \"올라오시오.\"\n\n그러나 이때 가브리엘이 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"생각하시오, 열병을 생각해 보란 말이오, 누렇게 뜨고 담즙이 끓는 열병을! 저 끔찍한 역병을 조심하시오!\"\n\n\"가브리엘! 가브리엘!\" 메이휴 선장이 외쳤다. \"너는 어느 쪽이든—\" 그러나 그 순간 곤두박질치는 파도가 보트를 멀찍이 앞으로 내쏘았고, 그 들끓는 물소리가 모든 말을 삼켜 버렸다.\n\n\"흰 고래를 본 적이 있소?\" 보트가 다시 떠밀려 돌아오자 에이해브가 물었다.\n\n\"생각하시오, 그대의 고래잡이 보트를 생각해 보란 말이오, 부서지고 가라앉은 그것을! 저 끔찍한 꼬리를 조심하시오!\"\n\n\"내 다시 말하건대, 가브리엘, 그—\" 그러나 다시금 보트는 마치 마귀들에게 끌려가듯 앞으로 내달았다. 한동안 아무 말도 오가지 못했으니, 사납게 날뛰는 파도가 잇따라 굽이쳐 지나갔기 때문이다. 그것은 바다가 이따금 부리는 변덕 가운데 하나로, 너울이 솟구치는 것이 아니라 마구 뒹굴며 굴러가는 모양이었다. 그러는 사이 끌어올려 둔 향유고래의 머리가 몹시 격렬하게 흔들거렸고, 가브리엘이 자신의 대천사다운 천성에 어울릴 법한 것보다 사뭇 더 불안한 눈길로 그것을 바라보고 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n이 막간이 끝나자 메이휴 선장은 모비 딕(Moby Dick)에 관한 어두운 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그러나 그 이름이 입에 오를 때마다, 그리고 그와 한패가 된 듯한 저 미친 바다 탓에, 가브리엘의 잦은 방해가 끼어드는 것을 피할 수는 없었다.\n\n이야기인즉슨, 제로보암호가 고향을 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않았을 무렵, 어느 고래잡이배와 마주쳐 이야기를 나누던 중 그 선원들이 모비 딕의 존재와 그가 저질러 온 참화를 확실히 전해 들었다는 것이다. 이 소식을 게걸스레 빨아들인 가브리엘은, 혹시라도 그 괴물이 눈에 띄거든 흰 고래를 공격하지 말라고 선장에게 엄숙히 경고했다. 그는 횡설수설하는 광기 속에서, 흰 고래가 다름 아닌 화신(化身)한 셰이커교의 신이라고 선언했으니, 셰이커교도들도 성경을 받드는 까닭이었다. 그러나 그로부터 한두 해 뒤, 마침내 돛대 꼭대기에서 모비 딕이 또렷이 목격되자, 일등항해사 메이시(Macey)는 그놈과 맞붙고 싶은 열의로 불타올랐다. 그리고 선장 또한 대천사의 온갖 비난과 사전 경고에도 불구하고 그에게 기회를 주는 것을 마다하지 않았으므로, 메이시는 다섯 사람을 설득하여 제 보트에 태우는 데 성공했다. 그는 그들과 함께 배를 밀어 나갔고, 지칠 만큼 노를 젓고 위태롭게 여러 차례 헛되이 달려든 끝에, 마침내 작살 하나를 단단히 박아 넣는 데 성공했다. 그러는 사이 가브리엘은 큰 돛대 맨 꼭대기로 기어올라 한쪽 팔을 미친 듯이 휘저으며, 자신의 신성을 모독하는 불경한 공격자들에게 곧 닥칠 파멸의 예언을 마구 쏟아 내고 있었다. 그런데 항해사 메이시가 보트 이물에 우뚝 선 채, 제 동족 특유의 무모한 기세로 고래를 향해 거친 고함을 내지르며 겨눈 창을 던질 좋은 기회를 노리고 있던 바로 그때, 보라! 넓고 흰 그림자 하나가 바다에서 솟아오르더니, 그 빠르고 부채질하는 듯한 움직임으로 노잡이들의 몸에서 한순간 숨을 앗아 갔다. 다음 순간, 그토록 격렬한 생기로 가득 차 있던 가련한 항해사는 통째로 허공으로 내쳐졌고, 떨어지면서 긴 호를 그리다가 약 오십 야드쯤 떨어진 바다에 떨어졌다. 보트는 나뭇조각 하나 상하지 않았고 노잡이 누구의 머리카락 한 올 다치지 않았으나, 그 항해사는 영영 가라앉고 말았다.\n\n여기서 한마디 덧붙여 두는 것이 좋겠다. 향유고래잡이에서 일어나는 치명적 사고 가운데, 이런 종류는 아마도 어느 것 못지않게 잦은 편이다. 때로는 이렇게 흔적도 없이 사라지는 사람 외에는 아무것도 상하지 않는다. 더 흔하게는 보트의 이물이 부서져 나가거나, 작살잡이가 발을 딛고 서는 넓적다리 받침판이 제자리에서 뜯겨 나가 그 몸과 함께 사라진다. 그러나 무엇보다 기이한 것은, 한두 번이 아니라 여러 차례 그 시신을 건져 냈을 때, 폭력의 흔적이라고는 단 하나도 찾아볼 수 없는데도 그 사람이 완전히 죽어 있더라는 사실이다.\n\n메이시가 떨어지는 모습과 함께 이 모든 참극이 배에서 또렷이 목격되었다. 가브리엘은 찢어지는 듯한 비명을 지르며—\"대접이다! 대접이다!\"—공포에 질린 선원들을 불러들여 더는 그 고래를 쫓지 못하게 했다. 이 무서운 사건은 그 대천사에게 한층 더한 영향력을 입혀 주었다. 잘 속아 넘어가는 그의 제자들은, 그가 그저 누구나 할 수 있을 법한 막연한 예언을 하여 넓게 허용된 여백 속에서 여러 표적 가운데 하나를 우연히 맞힌 것이 아니라, 바로 이 일을 콕 집어 미리 예고했었다고 믿었기 때문이다. 그는 배에서 이름조차 입에 올릴 수 없는 공포의 존재가 되었다.\n\n메이휴가 이야기를 마치자 에이해브가 그에게 이런저런 물음을 던졌고, 그 낯선 선장은 끝내 묻지 않을 수 없어, 만약 기회가 온다면 흰 고래를 사냥할 작정이냐고 되물었다. 이에 에이해브는 \"그렇소\"라고 대답했다. 그러자 곧바로 가브리엘이 다시 한번 벌떡 일어나 노인을 노려보더니, 손가락을 아래로 가리키며 격렬하게 외쳤다. \"생각하라, 저 신성모독자를 생각하라—죽어서 저 아래 가라앉은 그자를! 신성모독자의 최후를 조심하라!\"\n\n에이해브는 무덤덤하게 고개를 돌리더니, 메이휴에게 말했다. \"선장, 마침 내 편지 자루가 떠올랐소. 내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 그대의 사관 중 한 사람에게 온 편지가 있소. 스타벅, 자루를 뒤져 보게.\"\n\n고래잡이배는 저마다 여러 배 앞으로 온 적잖은 편지를 싣고 다니는데, 그 편지를 수신인에게 전하는 일은 오로지 네 대양에서 그들과 마주치는 우연에 달려 있다. 그리하여 대부분의 편지는 끝내 제 목적지에 닿지 못하며, 더러는 두세 해, 혹은 그 이상의 세월을 묵힌 뒤에야 비로소 받아 보게 된다.\n\n이윽고 스타벅이 편지 한 통을 손에 들고 돌아왔다. 선실의 어두운 사물함 속에 보관되어 있던 탓에, 그 편지는 잔뜩 구겨지고 눅눅하며 칙칙한 얼룩무늬의 푸른 곰팡이로 뒤덮여 있었다. 그런 편지라면 죽음 그 자체가 우편배달부 노릇을 했다 해도 능히 어울렸으리라.\n\n\"못 읽겠소?\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"이리 주시오, 이보게. 그래그래, 흐릿하게 갈겨쓴 글씨로군—이건 뭐지?\" 그가 그것을 들여다보며 풀어 가는 동안, 스타벅은 긴 고래 자르는 삽 자루를 가져다 칼로 그 끝을 살짝 쪼개어 편지를 끼워 넣었으니, 그렇게 하면 보트가 배에 더 가까이 다가오지 않고도 편지를 건넬 수 있었다.\n\n그러는 사이 에이해브는 편지를 들고 중얼거렸다. \"해리—그래, 해리 씨—(여자의 가냘픈 필체로군—이 사내의 아내가 분명해, 내기를 걸어도 좋아)—그래—해리 메이시 씨, 제로보암호—아니, 이건 메이시잖나, 그런데 그자는 죽었거늘!\"\n\n\"가련한 사람! 가련한 사람! 게다가 그 아내가 보낸 것이라니.\" 메이휴가 한숨지었다. \"하지만 내게 주시오.\"\n\n\"아니, 네가 가지고 있어라.\" 가브리엘이 에이해브에게 외쳤다. \"너도 머지않아 그 길로 가게 될 테니.\"\n\n\"저주가 네 목을 졸라 버리기를!\" 에이해브가 고함쳤다. \"메이휴 선장, 자, 받을 채비를 하시오.\" 그러고는 스타벅의 손에서 그 불길한 편지를 받아 자루 끝의 쪼개진 틈에 끼우고 보트 쪽으로 내밀었다. 그러나 그가 그리하는 사이, 노잡이들이 무언가를 기대하며 노 젓기를 멈추는 바람에, 보트가 배의 고물 쪽으로 조금 떠밀렸다. 그리하여 마치 마법에라도 걸린 듯, 편지가 별안간 가브리엘의 잽싼 손과 나란히 놓이게 되었다. 그는 순식간에 그것을 움켜쥐더니 보트용 칼을 집어 그 위에 편지를 꿰어, 그렇게 얹은 채로 도로 배 안으로 내던졌다. 편지는 에이해브의 발치에 떨어졌다. 그러자 가브리엘은 동료들에게 노를 저으라고 비명을 질렀고, 그렇게 하여 그 반항적인 보트는 순식간에 피쿼드호로부터 멀어져 갔다.\n\n이 막간이 지난 뒤 선원들이 다시 고래의 껍질을 벗기는 일에 손을 대는 동안, 이 거친 사건을 두고 갖가지 기이한 이야기들이 넌지시 오갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 72. The Monkey-Rope.\n\n고래를 해체하고 돌보는 그 소란스러운 작업 중에는, 선원들 사이에서 앞뒤로 뛰어다니는 일이 무척 많다. 어느 순간에는 이곳에 일손이 필요하고, 또 어느 순간에는 저곳에 일손이 필요하다. 한자리에 머물러 있을 수가 없다. 같은 순간에 모든 일이 사방에서 처리되어야 하기 때문이다. 이 장면을 묘사하려 애쓰는 사람도 사정은 매한가지다. 우리는 이제 왔던 길을 조금 되짚어 가야 한다. 앞서 고래의 등에 처음 작업을 시작할 때, 항해사들의 작업삽으로 잘라 낸 그 첫 구멍에 지방 갈고리를 박아 넣었다는 이야기를 했다. 그런데 그토록 둔중하고 묵직한 덩어리인 그 갈고리가 어떻게 그 구멍에 단단히 박힐 수 있었던 것일까? 그것을 거기에 박아 넣은 사람은 다름 아닌 나의 각별한 친구 퀴케그(Queequeg)였으니, 작살잡이로서 앞서 말한 그 특별한 목적을 위해 괴물의 등으로 내려가는 것이 그의 임무였다.\n그러나 매우 많은 경우, 사정상 작살잡이는 해체 혹은 껍질 벗기는 작업이 모두 끝날 때까지 고래 위에 남아 있어야 한다. 눈여겨볼 것은, 고래가 작업이 이루어지는 바로 그 부분만을 제외하고는 거의 전부 물속에 잠긴 채로 떠 있다는 점이다. 그래서 갑판 높이보다 약 열 자(약 3미터) 아래 그 자리에서, 가련한 작살잡이는 몸의 절반은 고래 위에, 절반은 물속에 둔 채로 허우적거린다. 그러는 동안 그 거대한 덩어리는 발밑에서 디딜방아처럼 빙글빙글 돌아간다. 지금 말하는 그 경우에, 퀴케그는 하일랜드식 복장—셔츠와 양말—을 하고 있었는데, 적어도 내 눈에는 그 차림이 그를 유달리 돋보이게 했다. 그리고 곧 보게 되겠지만, 그를 관찰하기에 나보다 더 좋은 위치에 있는 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n나는 그 야만인의 이물 노잡이, 다시 말해 그의 보트(앞에서 두 번째 보트)에서 이물 노를 젓는 사람이었으므로, 죽은 고래의 등 위에서 그가 그 고된 발버둥질을 하는 동안 그를 보살피는 것이 나의 즐거운 임무였다. 당신들도 긴 줄로 춤추는 원숭이를 붙들고 있는 이탈리아 풍금 소년을 본 적이 있을 것이다. 꼭 그와 같이, 나는 배의 가파른 옆구리에서, 포경업계에서 전문적으로 원숭이 밧줄(monkey-rope)이라 부르는 것으로 저 아래 바닷속의 퀴케그를 붙들고 있었으니, 그 밧줄은 그의 허리에 두른 튼튼한 범포 띠에 매여 있었다.\n\n그것은 우리 둘 모두에게 우스꽝스러우면서도 위태로운 일이었다. 더 나아가기 전에 반드시 말해 두어야 할 것은, 이 원숭이 밧줄이 양쪽 끝이 모두 단단히 매여 있었다는 점이다. 한쪽 끝은 퀴케그의 넓은 범포 띠에, 다른 한쪽 끝은 나의 좁은 가죽 띠에 묶여 있었다. 그리하여 좋든 나쁘든, 우리 둘은 그 순간만큼은 혼인한 사이였다. 만약 가련한 퀴케그가 가라앉아 다시는 떠오르지 못하게 된다면, 관습으로 보나 명예로 보나, 그 밧줄을 끊어 버리는 대신 그것이 그를 따라 나를 함께 끌고 내려가야 마땅했다. 그러니 길게 늘어진 샴쌍둥이의 끈이 우리를 하나로 잇고 있었던 셈이다. 퀴케그는 나의 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 쌍둥이 형제였으며, 그 삼줄의 인연이 안겨 준 위험한 책임에서 나는 결코 어떤 식으로도 벗어날 수 없었다.\n\n그때 나는 내 처지를 어찌나 강렬하게, 또 형이상학적으로 헤아렸던지, 그의 움직임을 골똘히 지켜보면서 분명히 깨닫는 듯했다. 내 자신의 개별성이 이제 둘이 합쳐진 합자회사(合資會社) 속에 녹아들었다는 것을, 내 자유의지가 치명적인 상처를 입었다는 것을, 그리고 남의 실수나 불운이 죄 없는 나를 마땅하지 않은 재앙과 죽음 속으로 빠뜨릴 수도 있다는 것을. 그러므로 나는 여기에 일종의 섭리의 공백기가 있음을 알았다. 섭리의 공정한 형평이라면 결코 이토록 엄청난 불의를 허용할 리 없기 때문이다. 그럼에도 더 깊이 생각을 거듭하면서—이따금 그를 고래와 배 사이에서 홱 끌어올려 주면서, 그렇지 않으면 그 틈에 끼일 위험이 있었기에—더더욱 깊이 생각을 거듭한 끝에, 나는 깨달았다. 이 나의 처지야말로 숨 쉬는 모든 인간의 정확한 처지라는 것을. 다만 대부분의 경우, 인간은 이런저런 방식으로 다른 여러 인간과 이런 샴쌍둥이식 연결을 맺고 있다는 점이 다를 뿐이다. 당신의 은행가가 파산하면 당신도 끊어지고, 당신의 약제사가 실수로 알약 속에 독을 담아 보내면 당신은 죽는다. 물론 당신은 지나칠 만큼 조심하면 이런 일들과, 그 밖의 무수한 삶의 악운들을 피할 수 있을지도 모른다고 말할 수 있다. 그러나 내가 아무리 퀴케그의 원숭이 밧줄을 조심스레 다루어도, 때로는 그가 어찌나 세게 그것을 홱 잡아당기던지, 나는 하마터면 뱃전 너머로 미끄러져 떨어질 뻔했다. 게다가 내가 무슨 수를 쓴들 그 밧줄의 한쪽 끝만 관리할 수 있을 뿐이라는 사실을 결코 잊을 수 없었다.*\n\n* 원숭이 밧줄은 모든 포경선에서 볼 수 있다. 그러나 원숭이와 그를 붙드는 자가 서로 묶여 있었던 것은 오직 피쿼드(Pequod)호뿐이었다. 본래의 관습에 이런 개량을 도입한 사람은 다름 아닌 스터브(Stubb)였으니, 위험에 처한 작살잡이에게 그를 붙드는 원숭이 밧줄잡이의 성실함과 경계심에 대한 가능한 한 가장 강력한 보증을 마련해 주기 위함이었다.\n\n나는 가련한 퀴케그를 고래와 배 사이에서 이따금 홱 끌어올려 주곤 했다는 것을 넌지시 말한 바 있다—그는 양쪽이 끊임없이 흔들리고 출렁이는 통에 때때로 그 틈에 빠지곤 했던 것이다. 그러나 그가 노출된 위험은 그렇게 끼이는 것만이 아니었다. 간밤에 자기들에게 가해진 대량 학살에도 전혀 겁먹지 않은 채, 이제 상어들은 사체에서 흘러나오기 시작한, 그동안 갇혀 있던 피에 새로이, 그리고 더욱 날카롭게 이끌려—그 미쳐 날뛰는 짐승들이 벌집 속의 벌 떼처럼 사체 주위로 우글거리며 몰려들었다.\n\n그리고 바로 그 상어들 한복판에 퀴케그가 있었으니, 그는 허우적거리는 발로 자주 그것들을 밀어내곤 했다. 죽은 고래 같은 먹이에 이끌리면 평소에는 닥치는 대로 무엇이든 먹어 치우는 상어도 좀처럼 사람은 건드리지 않는다는 사실이 아니었다면, 이는 도무지 믿을 수 없는 일이었을 것이다.\n\n그럼에도 상어들이 그토록 게걸스럽게 그 잔치에 손을 들이밀고 있는 만큼, 그것들을 예리하게 경계하는 것이 현명한 일로 여겨지는 것은 충분히 수긍할 만하다. 그리하여 원숭이 밧줄 외에도—나는 그 밧줄로 이따금 가련한 친구를 유난히 사나워 보이는 어느 상어의 아가리에 너무 가까이 다가가지 않도록 홱 끌어당겨 주었다—그에게는 또 다른 보호 장치가 마련되어 있었다. 뱃전 너머로 늘어뜨린 발판 가운데 하나에 매달린 채, 태시테고(Tashtego)와 대구(Daggoo)가 그의 머리 위에서 끊임없이 날카로운 고래삽 두 자루를 휘둘러, 손이 닿는 데까지 닥치는 대로 상어를 도살했다. 물론 그들의 이런 행동은 무척이나 사심 없고 자비로운 것이었다. 그들이 퀴케그의 더없는 행복을 바랐다는 것은 나도 인정한다. 그러나 그를 도우려는 성급한 열의 탓에, 또 그와 상어가 때때로 피로 흐려진 물에 절반쯤 가려져 있던 사정 탓에, 그들의 그 경솔한 삽은 꼬리보다는 다리를 잘라 낼 뻔하기 일쑤였다. 하지만 가련한 퀴케그는, 내 짐작에, 그 커다란 쇠갈고리를 붙들고 안간힘을 쓰며 헐떡이던 그 자리에서—가련한 퀴케그는, 내 짐작에, 그저 자기 요조(Yojo)에게 기도하며 제 목숨을 신들의 손에 맡겼을 따름이다.\n\n자, 자, 나의 소중한 동지이자 쌍둥이 형제여, 나는 바다의 너울에 따라 밧줄을 끌어당겼다가 다시 늦추어 주면서 생각했다—결국 그게 다 무슨 상관이란 말인가? 그대야말로 이 포경 세계 속 우리 모든 인간 하나하나의 소중한 표상이 아니던가? 그대가 그 속에서 헐떡이는 저 깊이를 알 수 없는 대양은 삶이요, 저 상어들은 그대의 적이며, 저 삽들은 그대의 벗이로다. 그리고 상어와 삽 사이에 끼인 그대는, 가련한 친구여, 슬픈 곤경과 위험에 처해 있구나.\n\n그러나 용기를 내라! 그대에게도 좋은 위안이 마련되어 있다네, 퀴케그여. 이제, 새파래진 입술과 핏발 선 눈으로 기진맥진한 그 야만인이 마침내 사슬을 타고 기어올라 온몸에서 물을 뚝뚝 흘리며 저도 모르게 부들부들 떨면서 뱃전에 서자, 사환이 다가와 자애롭고도 위로 어린 눈길로 그에게 무언가를 건넨다—무엇을? 뜨거운 코냑이라도? 천만에! 그가 건넨 것은, 오 신들이여! 그가 건넨 것은 미지근한 생강 물 한 잔이었다!\n\n\"생강? 내가 지금 생강 냄새를 맡은 건가?\" 스터브가 다가오며 미심쩍게 물었다. \"그래, 이건 분명 생강이야.\" 그는 아직 아무도 입을 대지 않은 잔을 들여다보며 말했다. 그러고는 한동안 도무지 믿기지 않는다는 듯이 서 있다가, 어안이 벙벙해진 사환을 향해 차분히 걸어가며 천천히 말했다. \"생강? 생강이라? 도우보이(Dough-Boy)씨, 부디 좀 알려 주시겠나, 생강의 효능이 도대체 어디에 있는지 말일세? 생강이라! 도우보이, 자네는 이 벌벌 떠는 식인종 몸뚱이에 불을 지피겠다고 생강 따위를 연료로 쓰는 건가? 생강이라니!—도대체 생강이 무엇이냔 말이야? 석탄인가? 장작인가?—성냥개비인가?—부싯깃인가?—화약인가?—내 다시 묻건대, 도대체 생강이란 게 무엇이기에 자네는 여기 우리 가련한 퀴케그에게 이런 잔을 내미는 건가.\"\n\n\"이 일에는 뭔가 음흉한 금주협회(禁酒協會)의 책동이 끼어 있구먼.\" 그는 갑자기 이렇게 덧붙이며, 이제 막 앞쪽에서 온 스타벅(Starbuck)에게 다가갔다. \"이 깡통 좀 보십시오, 항해사님. 괜찮으시다면 냄새 한번 맡아 보시지요.\" 그러고는 항해사의 표정을 살피며 덧붙였다. \"스타벅 씨, 저 사환 놈이 글쎄 방금 고래에서 막 올라온 저기 퀴케그에게 이런 감홍(甘汞)이며 약쑥 따위를 내밀 낯짝이 있더란 말입니다. 항해사님, 저 사환이 약제사라도 됩니까? 그리고 묻고 싶습니다만, 반쯤 익사한 사람에게 다시 숨을 불어넣겠다는 게 고작 이런 쓴 약 따위입니까?\"\n\n\"그건 아니길 바라네.\" 스타벅이 말했다. \"정말 형편없는 물건이군.\"\n\n\"옳소, 옳소, 사환.\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"작살잡이한테 약 먹이는 짓이 어떤 건지 우리가 똑똑히 가르쳐 주지. 여기서는 자네 약제사 약 따위는 통하지 않아. 우리를 독살하려는 게냐, 응? 우리 목숨에 보험이라도 들어 놓고 우리 모두를 죽인 다음에 그 돈을 챙기려는 게지, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"제가 한 게 아닙니다.\" 도우보이가 외쳤다. \"그 생강은 채러티(Charity) 아주머니가 배에 실어 놓으신 겁니다. 그러면서 작살잡이들한테는 절대 독한 술을 주지 말고 오직 이 생강 음료만 주라고 분부하셨어요—아주머니가 그렇게 부르시더군요.\"\n\n\"생강 음료라니! 이 생강 같은 비실비실한 녀석아! 이거나 받아라! 그리고 어서 저장고로 달려가서 더 나은 걸 가져와. 스타벅 씨, 제가 잘못하는 건 아니겠지요. 이건 선장의 명령입니다—고래 위에 오른 작살잡이에게는 그로그주를 주라는.\"\n\n\"됐네.\" 스타벅이 대답했다. \"다만 저 친구를 다시 때리지는 말게, 그저—\"\n\n\"아, 저는 때린다고 사람을 다치게 한 적이 없습니다요, 고래나 그 비슷한 걸 칠 때 말고는요. 게다가 이 녀석은 족제비 같은 놈인걸요. 그런데 무슨 말씀을 하시려던 참이었습니까, 항해사님?\"\n\n\"그저 이것뿐일세. 저 친구와 함께 내려가서, 자네가 원하는 걸 직접 가져오게.\"\n\n스터브가 다시 나타났을 때, 그는 한 손에는 거무스름한 술병을, 다른 손에는 차 보관통 비슷한 것을 들고 있었다. 첫 번째 것에는 독한 술이 들어 있었고 그것은 퀴케그에게 건네졌다. 두 번째 것은 채러티 아주머니의 선물이었는데, 그것은 아낌없이 파도에게 내던져졌다.\n\n# CHAPTER 73. Stubb and Flask kill a Right Whale; and Then Have a Talk over Him.\n\n이 모든 시간 동안 우리에게는 페쿼드(Pequod)호의 뱃전에 매달려 있는 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 거대한 머리가 있었다는 사실을 잊지 말아야 한다. 그러나 그 머리를 손볼 기회가 생길 때까지는 잠시 그대로 매달려 있게 두는 수밖에 없다. 지금은 다른 일들이 더 급하게 밀려들고 있으니, 그 머리를 위해 우리가 지금 할 수 있는 최선이란 다만 도르래 장치가 버텨 주기를 하늘에 비는 것뿐이다.\n\n자, 지난밤과 그날 오전 동안 페쿼드호는 차츰차츰 어떤 바다로 흘러들어 갔는데, 그곳은 군데군데 떠 있는 누런 브릿(brit) 무리로 미루어 참고래(Right Whale)가 가까이 있음을 알리는 심상찮은 징조를 드러내고 있었다. 참고래는 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 한 종류로, 바로 이 무렵 이 근처 어딘가에 숨어 있으리라고는 거의 아무도 짐작하지 못한 놈이었다. 비록 선원들 모두가 이런 하등한 짐승을 잡는 일을 흔히 업신여겼고, 페쿼드호 역시 애초에 그것을 좇으라는 명을 받은 바가 전혀 없었으며, 크로제 제도 근처에서 수많은 참고래를 지나치면서도 보트 한 척 내리지 않았건만, 이제 향유고래 한 마리를 뱃전으로 끌어와 목을 베고 보니, 모두가 놀랍게도 기회만 닿는다면 그날 참고래 한 마리를 잡자는 선언이 내려졌다.\n* 브릿(brit): 고래가 먹이로 삼는 작은 바다 생물 무리.\n\n그리고 그 기회는 오래 기다릴 것도 없이 찾아왔다. 바람 불어가는 쪽에서 높이 솟구치는 물줄기가 보였고, 스터브(Stubb)와 플래스크(Flask)의 보트 두 척이 추격에 나섰다. 점점 더 멀리 노를 저어 나아가는 통에, 그들은 마침내 돛대 꼭대기에 있는 사람들의 눈에도 거의 보이지 않게 되었다. 그러나 갑자기 저 멀리서, 그들은 어지러이 들끓는 새하얀 물보라가 크게 솟구치는 것을 보았고, 곧이어 두 보트 중 하나, 혹은 둘 다 고래에 작살을 박아 끌려가고 있다는 소식이 돛대 위에서 들려왔다. 한참 지나자 보트들이 또렷이 보였는데, 끌고 가는 고래에 의해 곧장 배 쪽으로 끌려오는 중이었다. 그 괴물이 어찌나 선체 가까이 다가왔던지, 처음에는 마치 배에 앙심을 품은 것처럼 보였다. 그러나 별안간 뱃전 널빤지에서 세 길도 채 안 되는 거리에서 소용돌이 속으로 가라앉더니, 마치 용골 밑으로 잠수해 들어가기라도 하듯 시야에서 완전히 사라져 버렸다. \"줄을 끊어, 끊으라고!\" 배에서 보트들을 향해 외치는 소리가 터져 나왔다. 한순간 보트들이 배 옆구리에 치명적으로 부딪칠 것만 같았기 때문이다. 그러나 줄통에는 아직 밧줄이 넉넉히 남아 있었고, 고래 또한 그리 빠르게 잠수하지는 않았으므로, 그들은 밧줄을 넉넉히 풀어 주는 동시에 온 힘을 다해 노를 저어 배보다 앞서 나가려 했다. 몇 분 동안 그 싸움은 극도로 아슬아슬했다. 한쪽으로는 팽팽해진 줄을 계속 늦춰 주면서, 다른 쪽으로는 여전히 노를 저어 댔으니, 서로 맞서는 그 힘이 그들을 물속으로 끌어들일 듯 위협했던 것이다. 그러나 그들이 얻으려던 것은 겨우 몇 자(尺)의 전진뿐이었다. 그리고 그들은 그것을 얻을 때까지 끝끝내 버텼다. 그러자 곧장, 번개처럼 빠른 떨림이 용골을 타고 달리는 것이 느껴졌으니, 배 밑을 긁고 지나가던 팽팽한 줄이 갑자기 뱃머리 아래로 떠오르며 탁탁 튀고 부르르 떨면서, 묻은 물방울을 사방으로 털어 냈는데, 그 물방울들이 마치 깨진 유리 조각처럼 수면 위로 떨어졌고, 그 너머에서 고래 또한 모습을 드러냈으니, 이제 보트들은 다시 자유로이 내달릴 수 있게 되었다. 그러나 지친 고래는 속도를 늦추더니, 무작정 방향을 틀어 배의 고물 쪽을 돌아 나가면서 두 보트를 뒤에 매단 채 끌고 가는 통에, 그들은 배 주위를 완전히 한 바퀴 돌게 되었다.\n\n그러는 동안 그들은 점점 더 줄을 끌어당겨, 마침내 고래의 양 옆구리에 바짝 붙어서서, 스터브가 작살을 던지면 플래스크가 작살로 화답하는 식이 되었다. 그렇게 페쿼드호 둘레를 돌고 또 돌며 싸움이 이어지는 동안, 앞서 향유고래의 몸뚱이 둘레를 헤엄치던 무수한 상어 떼가 새로이 흘러나온 피로 몰려들어, 새로 베일 때마다 갈증 난 듯 그 피를 들이켰으니, 그 모습은 마치 바위를 쳐서 솟구쳐 나온 새 샘물에 목마른 이스라엘 백성이 달려들던 것과도 같았다.\n\n마침내 그 물줄기가 걸쭉해지더니, 끔찍하게 한 번 뒤척이며 토악질을 하고는, 고래는 죽은 몸뚱이가 되어 벌렁 나자빠졌다.\n\n두 작살잡이 우두머리가 고래 꼬리에 밧줄을 매고, 그 거대한 덩어리를 끌고 갈 채비를 이런저런 방법으로 갖추는 동안, 둘 사이에 이런 대화가 오갔다.\n\n\"영감님이 이런 고약한 비계 덩어리를 가지고 대체 뭘 하시려는 건지 모르겠군.\" 이렇게도 변변찮은 리바이어던을 상대해야 한다는 생각에 다소 역겨움을 느끼며 스터브가 말했다.\n\n\"이걸 가지고 뭘 하느냐고?\" 플래스크가 보트 뱃머리에 남는 밧줄을 사리며 말했다. \"자네 그런 얘기 한 번도 못 들어 봤나, 한 배가 우현에 향유고래 머리를 매단 동시에 좌현에 참고래 머리를 매달기만 하면, 그 배는 그 뒤로 절대 뒤집히는 일이 없다는 말? 그런 말 못 들어 봤어, 스터브?\"\n\"왜 안 뒤집힌다는 거야?\"\n\n\"나도 모르지. 하지만 저 누리끼리한 유령 같은 페달라(Fedallah)가 그렇게 말하는 걸 들었거든. 그 작자는 배에 관한 부적 따위는 죄다 꿰고 있는 것 같아. 그런데 난 가끔 그 작자가 결국엔 이 배에 좋지 못한 주술을 걸 거라는 생각이 들어. 난 저 친구가 영 마음에 안 들어, 스터브. 자네 그 작자 송곳니가 일종의 뱀 대가리 모양으로 깎여 있는 걸 본 적 있나, 스터브?\"\n\n\"그 작자야 물속에 처박혀 버리라지! 난 아예 쳐다보지도 않아. 하지만 언젠가 캄캄한 밤에 기회가 닿아서, 그 작자가 뱃전 난간 바로 옆에 서 있고 곁에 아무도 없으면, 저기 저 아래를 좀 봐, 플래스크.\" 두 손으로 묘한 동작을 지으며 바다를 가리켰다. \"암, 그렇게 하고말고! 플래스크, 난 그 페달라가 변장한 악마라고 생각해. 그 작자가 배에 몰래 숨어들었다는 그 황당무계한 이야기를 자네는 믿나? 난 그자가 악마라고 단언하네. 자네가 그 작자 꼬리를 못 보는 이유는, 그 작자가 그걸 안 보이게 말아 올려 두기 때문이야. 내 짐작엔 그걸 둘둘 사려서 주머니에 넣고 다니는 거지. 빌어먹을 놈! 이제 생각해 보니, 그 작자는 늘 부츠 발끝에 쑤셔 넣을 뱃밥을 달라고 하거든.\"\n\n\"그 작자는 부츠를 신은 채로 잔다지, 안 그래? 해먹도 없잖아. 난 그자가 밤이면 밧줄 사린 무더기 속에 드러눕는 걸 본 적이 있어.\"\n\n\"틀림없어. 그게 다 그 빌어먹을 꼬리 때문이지. 알겠나, 그 작자는 밧줄 사린 그 구멍 속에 꼬리를 둘둘 사려 넣는 거야.\"\n\n\"영감님은 대체 무슨 일로 그 작자랑 그렇게 자주 어울리는 거야?\"\n\n\"무슨 거래나 흥정을 트는 거겠지, 내 짐작엔.\"\n\n\"흥정?—뭘 가지고?\"\n\n\"그게 말이지, 알겠나, 영감님은 저 흰 고래(White Whale)를 잡겠다고 단단히 작정하셨잖아. 그러니까 저 악마가 영감님한테 접근해서, 은시계든 영혼이든 뭐 그런 걸 넘기게 꾀어내려는 거지. 그러면 그 대가로 자기가 모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 넘겨주겠다는 거야.\"\n\n\"흥! 스터브, 자네 지금 장난치는 거지. 페달라가 어떻게 그런 일을 한단 말이야?\"\n\n\"나도 모르지, 플래스크. 하지만 악마라는 놈은 묘한 작자고, 사악한 놈이야, 내 장담하네. 글쎄, 사람들 말로는 그 악마가 한번은 저 옛날 기함(旗艦)에 어슬렁어슬렁 들어갔다는 거야, 꼬리를 악마답게 아주 능청스럽고 신사처럼 살랑살랑 흔들면서 말이야. 그러고는 늙은 사령관이 댁에 계시느냐고 묻더라는 거지. 그런데 사령관이 마침 계셔서, 악마더러 무슨 일이냐고 물었어. 그러니까 악마가 발굽을 까딱거리며 벌떡 일어나 말하기를, '존을 데려가고 싶소.' '뭐 하러?' 늙은 사령관이 물었지. '그게 당신이 알 바요?' 악마가 화를 내며 말했어.—'그놈을 좀 부려먹을 일이 있소.' '데려가시오.' 사령관이 말했지.—그런데 맹세코, 플래스크, 만약 그 악마가 일을 다 끝내기도 전에 존한테 아시아 콜레라를 안겨 주지 않았다면, 내가 이 고래를 한입에 통째로 먹어 치우겠네. 그건 그렇고 정신 똑바로 차려.—거기 다들 준비됐나? 자, 그럼 앞으로 저어 가서 고래를 뱃전에 갖다 대자고.\"\n\n\"자네가 들려준 것과 비슷한 이야기를 어디선가 본 기억이 나는 것도 같은데,\" 마침내 두 보트가 짐을 끌고 배 쪽으로 천천히 나아가기 시작할 무렵 플래스크가 말했다. \"어디서 봤는지는 도무지 생각이 안 나는군.\"\n\n\"세 스페인 사람들? 그 잔인무도한 군인 세 놈의 모험담 말인가? 자네 그걸 거기서 읽었나, 플래스크? 그런 거지, 아마?\"\n\n\"아니. 그런 책은 본 적도 없어. 얘기는 들어 봤지만. 그런데 말이야, 스터브, 방금 자네가 말한 그 악마가, 지금 페쿼드호에 타고 있다고 한 그 작자와 같은 놈이라고 생각하는 거야?\"\n\n\"내가 이 고래 잡는 걸 도운 바로 그 사람과 같은 사람이냐고? 악마는 영원히 살지 않나? 악마가 죽었다는 소릴 누가 들어 봤어? 자네 어떤 목사가 악마를 위해 상복 입은 걸 본 적 있나? 그리고 악마가 제독실에 드나들 빗장 열쇠를 가졌다면, 그놈이 현창(舷窓)으로 기어들어 오지 못하겠어? 어디 말해 보게, 플래스크 씨?\"\n\n\"페달라가 몇 살쯤 됐다고 생각하나, 스터브?\"\n\n\"저기 저 큰 돛대 보이지?\" 배를 가리키며 그가 말했다. \"자, 저게 숫자 1이라고 치자고. 이제 페쿼드호 선창에 있는 테(箍)를 죄다 가져다가 저 돛대 옆에 한 줄로 죽 늘어놓아서 0을 만들어 봐, 알겠나. 그래도 그건 페달라 나이의 시작도 못 될걸. 천지간의 모든 통장이를 다 모아도 그만큼 많은 0을 만들 만큼 테를 보여 줄 순 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"그런데 잠깐, 스터브, 방금 자네가 좋은 기회만 잡으면 페달라를 바다에 한번 처넣어 주겠다고 좀 으스댄 것 같은데. 그런데 만약 그 작자가 자네 말마따나 그 테들만큼이나 나이를 먹었고, 게다가 영원히 살 거라면, 그놈을 배 밖으로 내던진들 무슨 소용이 있겠어—어디 말해 봐.\"\n\"어쨌든 한번 물맛이나 톡톡히 보여 주는 거지.\"\n\n\"하지만 그놈은 도로 기어 올라올 텐데.\"\n\n\"그럼 또 처박는 거지. 계속 처박아 대는 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 그놈이 도리어 자네를 처박을 생각을 한다면—그래, 그래서 자넬 물에 빠뜨려 죽인다면—그땐 어쩔 건데?\"\n\n\"어디 한번 해 보라지. 내가 그놈 두 눈에 시퍼런 멍을 들여 놔서, 한동안은 제독실에 다시 얼굴도 못 내밀게 만들 테니까. 그놈이 사는 저 맨 아래 화물칸은 말할 것도 없고, 그놈이 그렇게 자주 살금살금 기어 다니는 이 위쪽 갑판 근처에도 말이야. 악마 따위 빌어먹으라지, 플래스크. 그러니까 자넨 내가 악마를 무서워한다고 생각하는 거야? 그놈을 무서워할 자가 누가 있어, 그놈을 잡아다가 마땅히 받아야 할 대로 이중 족쇄를 채울 엄두도 못 내고 그저 사람들이나 납치하도록 내버려 두는 그 늙은 사령관 말고는? 암, 게다가 그놈과 계약서까지 써서, 악마가 납치한 사람은 죄다 자기가 구워 주겠다고 했다지? 그런 게 사령관이라니!\"\n\n\"페달라가 에이해브(Ahab) 선장을 납치하려 든다고 생각하나?\"\n\n\"내가 그렇게 생각하느냐고? 머지않아 자네도 알게 될 거야, 플래스크. 어쨌든 난 지금부터 그놈을 날카롭게 지켜볼 참이야. 그래서 뭔가 아주 수상쩍은 짓을 하는 게 보이면, 그놈 목덜미를 덥석 붙잡고 이렇게 말해 줄 테야.—이봐, 베엘제붑, 그 짓은 안 돼. 그런데도 그놈이 무슨 소란이라도 피우면, 맹세코, 그놈 주머니에 손을 쑥 집어넣어 꼬리를 끄집어내서는 캡스턴에다 감고 어찌나 비틀고 잡아당기는지, 그 꼬리가 밑동에서 댕강 끊어져 버리게 만들 거야—알겠어. 그러고 나면, 그놈도 자기가 그렇게 묘한 꼴로 꼬리가 잘린 걸 알아채고 나면, 가랑이 사이에 꼬리를 끼고 풀이 죽는 그 변변찮은 만족감조차 못 누린 채 슬그머니 꽁무니를 뺄 거라고, 내 짐작엔.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 꼬리를 가지고 뭘 할 건데, 스터브?\"\n\n\"꼬리로 뭘 하느냐고? 집에 돌아가면 소몰이 채찍으로 팔아야지.—그것 말고 뭐가 있겠나?\"\n\n\"이봐, 자네 지금 한 말, 아니 여태껏 줄곧 떠들어 댄 그 말들이 진심이야, 스터브?\"\n\n\"진심이든 아니든, 자, 다 왔네, 배에 도착했어.\"\n\n여기서 보트들을 향해 고래를 좌현 쪽으로 끌고 오라는 외침이 들려왔으니, 그쪽에는 이미 고래를 단단히 매어 둘 꼬리 사슬과 그 밖에 필요한 것들이 마련되어 있었다.\n\n\"내가 그렇게 말하지 않았나?\" 플래스크가 말했다. \"암, 곧 보게 될 거야, 이 참고래 머리가 저 향유고래 머리 맞은편에 매달려 올라가는 걸.\"\n\n머지않아 플래스크의 말이 옳았음이 드러났다. 앞서 페쿼드호는 향유고래 머리 쪽으로 가파르게 기울었으나, 이제 두 머리가 서로 균형을 이룬 덕분에 다시 수평을 되찾았다. 물론 몹시 무리한 부담을 진 채였다는 것은 충분히 짐작할 만한 일이다. 그러니까 한쪽에 로크(Locke)의 머리를 매달아 올리면 그쪽으로 기울지만, 이제 다른 쪽에 칸트(Kant)의 머리를 매달면 다시 제자리로 돌아오는 셈이다. 다만 그 꼴은 영 형편없다. 이렇듯 어떤 정신들은 영영 배의 균형이나 잡으며 살아간다. 오, 어리석은 자들이여! 이 골치 아픈 머리통들을 죄다 배 밖으로 내던져 버려라. 그러면 비로소 가뿐하고 똑바르게 떠다닐 수 있으리니.\n\n참고래의 몸뚱이를 처리하는 일은, 그것을 뱃전에 끌어왔을 때, 향유고래의 경우와 대체로 똑같은 예비 절차를 밟는다. 다만 향유고래의 경우엔 머리를 통째로 잘라 내지만, 참고래의 경우엔 입술과 혀를 따로 떼어 내어, 이른바 크라운피스(crown-piece)에 붙은 그 유명한 검은 뼈(고래수염)와 함께 갑판 위로 끌어올린다. 그러나 이번 경우엔 이런 일은 아무것도 행해지지 않았다. 두 고래의 몸뚱이는 고물 뒤로 늘어뜨려진 채였고, 머리를 잔뜩 짊어진 배는 너무 무거운 짐바구니 한 쌍을 짊어진 노새와 적잖이 닮아 보였다.\n\n그러는 동안 페달라는 참고래의 머리를 차분히 바라보면서, 이따금 거기 깊이 팬 주름살로부터 제 손바닥의 손금으로 시선을 옮기곤 했다. 그리고 에이해브는 우연히도 파르시(Parsee)가 그의 그림자 안에 들어서는 위치에 서 있었으니, 설령 그 파르시의 그림자가 거기 있다 한들 그것은 에이해브의 그림자에 녹아들어 그것을 길게 늘이는 것처럼 보일 따름이었다. 선원들이 부지런히 일하는 동안, 그들 사이에서는 이렇게 스쳐 가는 온갖 일들을 두고 라플란드 사람들이나 할 법한 황당한 억측이 오갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 74. The Sperm Whale’s Head—Contrasted View.\n\n향유고래의 머리—대조적 관찰.\n여기, 지금 두 마리의 거대한 고래가 머리를 맞대고 누워 있다. 우리도 그들과 한데 어울려, 우리 자신의 머리를 함께 맞대 보자.\n\n이절판 리바이어던(Leviathan)이라는 저 웅대한 목(目) 가운데, 향유고래(Sperm Whale)와 참고래(Right Whale)는 단연 가장 주목할 만한 존재다. 이 둘은 인간이 정기적으로 사냥하는 유일한 고래다. 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람에게 이 둘은 알려진 모든 고래 변종 가운데 양극단을 이룬다. 둘 사이의 외형적 차이는 주로 머리에서 관찰되며, 마침 지금 이 순간 그 머리들이 각각 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 뱃전에 매달려 있고, 또한 우리는 갑판을 가로질러 걸음을 옮기는 것만으로 한쪽에서 다른 쪽으로 자유로이 오갈 수 있으니—묻건대, 실용 고래학을 연구하기에 여기보다 더 좋은 기회를 그대는 어디서 얻겠는가?\n\n우선 그대는 이 두 머리 사이의 전반적인 대조에 충격을 받는다. 양쪽 다 충분히 거대하다는 점은 양심에 비추어 봐도 틀림없다. 그러나 향유고래의 머리에는 일종의 수학적 균형미가 있는데, 참고래의 머리에는 안타깝게도 그것이 결여되어 있다. 향유고래의 머리에는 더 많은 개성이 깃들어 있다. 그것을 바라보노라면, 그대는 두루 배어 있는 위엄이라는 점에서 그에게 압도적인 우월함을 저도 모르게 인정하게 된다. 게다가 지금 이 경우에는, 그 위엄이 정수리 부분 머리의 후추와 소금을 뿌린 듯한 빛깔로 한층 돋보이는데, 이는 노년에 이르렀음과 풍부한 경험을 말해 주는 표지다. 요컨대 그는 어부들이 전문 용어로 \"백발 고래\"라 부르는 바로 그것이다.\n\n이제 이 두 머리에서 가장 덜 다른 점—곧 가장 중요한 두 기관인 눈과 귀를 살펴보자. 머리의 옆면 한참 뒤쪽, 아래로 내려가 양쪽 고래의 턱 모서리 근처를 자세히 뒤지면, 마침내 속눈썹 없는 눈 하나를 보게 되는데, 그대는 그것을 어린 망아지의 눈으로 여길지도 모른다. 머리의 거대함에 견주어 그토록 어울리지 않게 작은 까닭이다.\n\n자, 고래 눈의 이 독특한 측면 위치로 미루어 보건대, 그가 정확히 앞에 있는 물체를 결코 볼 수 없으며, 정확히 뒤에 있는 물체 또한 볼 수 없음이 분명하다. 한마디로 고래 눈의 위치는 사람 귀의 위치에 해당하니, 그대 스스로 한번 상상해 보라, 만약 그대가 귀를 통해 물체를 옆으로 둘러본다면 어떤 처지가 될지를. 그대는 똑바른 측면 시선의 앞쪽으로 겨우 삼십 도 남짓, 그리고 그 뒤쪽으로 다시 삼십 도쯤만 볼 수 있음을 알게 될 것이다. 만약 그대의 가장 모진 원수가 대낮에 단검을 치켜든 채 그대를 향해 똑바로 걸어온다 해도, 그대는 그가 등 뒤에서 몰래 다가올 때와 마찬가지로 그를 볼 수 없을 것이다. 한마디로 그대는, 말하자면 두 개의 등을 갖게 되는 셈이다. 그러나 동시에 두 개의 앞면(측면 앞면)도 갖게 된다. 사람의 앞면을 이루는 것이 무엇이겠는가—참으로 그의 눈이 아니고 무엇이겠는가?\n\n더욱이 내가 지금 떠올릴 수 있는 대부분의 다른 동물들은 두 눈이 그 시력(視力)을 알게 모르게 하나로 융합하도록 자리 잡아, 뇌에 두 개가 아닌 하나의 그림을 만들어 낸다. 그러나 고래 눈의 독특한 위치는, 두 골짜기의 호수를 가르는 큰 산처럼 그 사이에 우뚝 솟은 수 세제곱피트에 달하는 단단한 머리로 효과적으로 갈라져 있으니, 이것은 당연히 각각의 독립된 기관이 전하는 인상을 완전히 분리할 수밖에 없다. 따라서 고래는 이쪽에서 하나의 또렷한 그림을 보고, 저쪽에서 또 다른 또렷한 그림을 보아야 하며, 그 사이의 모든 것은 그에게 깊은 어둠이요 허무일 것이다. 사람은 사실상 두 짝이 맞붙은 창문이 달린 보초막에서 세상을 내다본다고 말할 수 있다. 그러나 고래의 경우 이 두 창짝은 따로따로 끼워져 두 개의 별개 창문을 이루지만, 안타깝게도 시야를 망가뜨린다. 고래 눈의 이 특이성은 고래잡이에서 늘 마음에 새겨 두어야 할 것이며, 독자도 뒤에 나올 몇몇 장면에서 기억해 둘 일이다.\n\n이 시각의 문제를 두고 리바이어던과 관련하여 기이하고도 더없이 까다로운 물음 하나를 제기해 볼 수 있다. 그러나 나는 한 가지 암시로 만족해야겠다. 사람의 눈이 빛 속에서 떠 있는 한, 본다는 행위는 비자발적이다. 곧 그는 그때 자기 앞에 있는 어떤 물체든 기계적으로 보지 않을 수 없다. 그럼에도 누구든 자신의 경험으로 이렇게 배우게 된다. 곧 한 번의 눈길로 사물을 분간 없이 휩쓸어 받아들일 수는 있어도, 어떤 두 가지를—그것이 아무리 크든 작든—같은 한순간에 주의 깊게, 그리고 온전히 살피기란 도무지 불가능하다는 것을. 설령 그 둘이 나란히 놓여 서로 맞닿아 있다 해도 마찬가지다. 그러나 이제 그 두 물체를 떼어 놓고, 각각을 깊은 어둠의 고리로 에워싸 보라. 그러면 둘 중 하나를 마음을 기울여 살피려 하는 순간, 다른 하나는 그대의 동시적 의식에서 완전히 배제되고 만다. 그렇다면 고래의 경우는 어떠한가? 과연 그의 두 눈은 그 자체로 동시에 작동해야 한다. 그러나 그의 뇌는 사람의 뇌보다 훨씬 더 포괄적이고, 종합적이며, 정교해서, 같은 한순간에 두 개의 별개 전망을—하나는 한쪽에서, 다른 하나는 정확히 반대 방향에서—주의 깊게 살필 수 있단 말인가? 만약 그럴 수 있다면, 그것은 한 사람이 유클리드(Euclid)의 두 별개 문제 증명을 동시에 해내는 것만큼이나 그에게 경이로운 일이다. 더구나 엄밀히 따져 보면, 이 비유에는 아무런 부조화도 없다.\n\n이는 한낱 부질없는 공상일지도 모르나, 내게는 늘 이렇게 여겨졌다. 곧 어떤 고래들이 서너 척의 보트에 에워싸였을 때 보이는 그 유별난 움직임의 동요, 그런 고래들에게 그토록 흔한 겁먹음과 괴상한 공포에 빠지기 쉬운 성질—이 모든 것이 갈라져 정반대로 향한 그들의 시력이 그들을 빠뜨릴 수밖에 없는 그 무력한 의지의 혼란에서 간접적으로 비롯된다고 나는 생각한다.\n\n그러나 고래의 귀는 눈에 못지않게 기이하다. 만약 그대가 그들 종족에게 전혀 낯선 사람이라면, 이 두 머리를 몇 시간이고 뒤진다 해도 그 기관을 결코 찾아내지 못할 것이다. 귀에는 바깥으로 드러난 귓바퀴가 전혀 없으며, 그 구멍 자체에도 깃펜 하나 끼워 넣기 어려울 만큼, 그토록 놀랍도록 미세하다. 그것은 눈보다 조금 뒤쪽에 자리 잡고 있다. 그들의 귀에 관해서는, 향유고래와 참고래 사이에 다음과 같은 중요한 차이를 눈여겨보아야 한다. 전자의 귀에는 바깥으로 트인 구멍이 있지만, 후자의 귀는 막으로 완전히, 그리고 고르게 덮여 있어, 바깥에서는 도무지 알아챌 수 없다.\n\n기이하지 아니한가, 고래처럼 그토록 거대한 존재가 그토록 작은 눈으로 세상을 보고, 산토끼의 것보다도 작은 귀로 천둥소리를 듣는다는 것이? 그러나 만약 그의 눈이 허셜(Herschel)의 거대한 망원경 렌즈만큼 넓고, 그의 귀가 대성당 현관만큼 널찍하다 한들, 그것이 그를 더 멀리 보게 하거나 더 예민하게 듣게 만들겠는가? 결코 아니다.—그렇다면 그대는 어찌하여 그대의 정신을 \"넓히려\" 애쓰는가? 정신은 정교하게 다듬을 일이다.\n\n이제 손닿는 데 있는 온갖 지레와 증기기관을 써서, 향유고래의 머리를 뒤집어 바닥이 위로 오도록 모로 세워 보자. 그런 다음 사다리를 타고 정수리로 올라가 입속을 들여다보자. 만약 지금 몸뚱이가 그것으로부터 완전히 떨어져 나가지 않았더라면, 등불 하나를 들고 그의 위장이라는 저 거대한 켄터키 매머드 동굴 속으로 내려가 볼 수도 있었으리라. 그러나 여기 이 이빨을 붙잡고 매달려, 우리가 있는 곳 주위를 둘러보자. 참으로 아름답고 정결해 보이는 입이로다! 바닥에서 천장까지, 반짝이는 흰 막으로 안을 댔으니, 아니 차라리 도배했다고 해야겠다, 신부의 새틴처럼 윤이 나는 그 막으로.\n\n그러나 이제 밖으로 나와서, 이 불길해 보이는 아래턱을 보라. 한쪽 옆이 아니라 한쪽 끝에 경첩이 달린, 거대한 코담배갑의 길고 좁은 뚜껑 같지 않은가. 그것을 비집어 들어 올려 머리 위로 들어 올리고 그 이빨 줄을 드러내면, 무시무시한 내리닫이 쇠살문처럼 보인다. 그리고 슬프게도, 그것은 고래잡이에서 수많은 가련한 사내들에게 정녕 그런 것임이 드러나는데, 그 쇠창살 같은 이빨이 그들에게 꿰뚫을 듯한 힘으로 떨어지는 것이다. 그러나 한층 더 끔찍한 광경은, 바닷속 수십 길 아래에서 어떤 시무룩한 고래가 거기 떠서 매달린 채, 십오 피트(약 4.5미터)쯤 되는 그 어마어마한 턱을 제 몸과 직각을 이루며 곧장 아래로 늘어뜨리고 있는 모습을 보는 것이니, 그 모양이 영락없이 배의 지브붐 같다. 이 고래는 죽은 것이 아니다. 다만 기운이 빠졌을 뿐이다. 어쩌면 심기가 불편한 것이리라. 우울증에 걸린 것이리라. 그리고 어찌나 늘어졌는지 턱의 경첩이 풀려 버려, 거기 그렇게 볼썽사나운 꼴로 매달려 있으니, 그 종족 모두에게 수치가 되어, 그들은 필시 그에게 턱이 굳어 버리는 저주를 퍼붓고 있을 것이다.\n\n대개의 경우 이 아래턱은—노련한 솜씨꾼이라면 쉽게 경첩을 풀어내는데—몸에서 떼어 내어 갑판 위로 끌어 올린다. 상아질 이빨을 뽑아내고, 어부들이 지팡이며 우산 자루며 승마용 채찍 손잡이를 비롯한 온갖 진기한 물건을 만드는 데 쓰는 저 단단한 흰 고래뼈를 마련하기 위함이다.\n\n길고도 고된 끌어 올리기 끝에, 턱은 마치 닻이라도 되는 양 배 위로 끌려 올라온다. 그리고 적당한 때가 오면—다른 작업을 끝낸 며칠 뒤—저마다 능숙한 치과의사인 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 다구(Daggoo)와 타슈테고(Tashtego)가 이빨을 뽑는 일에 투입된다. 퀴퀘그가 날카로운 절단용 삽으로 잇몸을 가르면, 그다음 턱을 고리볼트에 단단히 동여매고, 위에서 도르래 장치를 매달아, 마치 미시간의 황소들이 거친 숲의 땅에서 늙은 떡갈나무 그루터기를 뽑아내듯 이 이빨들을 끌어낸다. 이빨은 대개 모두 마흔두 개인데, 늙은 고래의 것은 많이 닳았어도 썩지는 않았고, 우리 인간이 인공적으로 하듯 때워 넣은 것도 아니다. 그 뒤 턱은 톱으로 켜서 판자로 만든 다음, 집 지을 때 쓰는 들보처럼 차곡차곡 쌓아 둔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 75. The Right Whale’s Head—Contrasted View.\n\n갑판을 가로질러 가서, 이제 참고래의 머리를 한참 동안 찬찬히 들여다보기로 하자.\n\n전체적인 형태로 볼 때 저 고귀한 향유고래의 머리가 로마의 전차(특히 폭넓게 둥글려진 앞부분이 그러하다)에 견줄 만하다면, 참고래의 머리는 한 발짝 물러나 넓게 바라볼 때 거대한 뱃머리 모양의 신발과 다소 볼품없는 닮은꼴을 이룬다. 이백 년 전, 어느 늙은 네덜란드 항해자는 그 모양을 구두장이의 골에 빗대었다. 그리고 바로 그 골, 곧 그 신발 속에라면, 자식들이 우글거리던 옛이야기 속의 그 노파가 제 모든 자손과 더불어 아주 편안히 머무를 수 있을 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 거대한 머리에 가까이 다가설수록, 그것은 보는 각도에 따라 저마다 다른 모습을 띠기 시작한다. 그 꼭대기에 올라서서 이 F자 모양의 두 분수공을 들여다본다면, 그대는 그 머리 전체를 거대한 콘트라베이스로, 그리고 이 숨구멍들을 그 공명판에 뚫린 구멍으로 여길 것이다. 또 다시, 만약 그대가 이 덩어리의 꼭대기에 얹힌 저 기묘하고 볏처럼 솟은 빗 모양의 딱지에 — 그린란드 사람들이 참고래의 \"왕관\"이라 부르고 남쪽 어부들이 \"보닛\"이라 부르는, 따개비가 들러붙은 이 푸르스름한 것에 — 오직 시선을 고정한다면, 그대는 그 머리를 가랑이에 새 둥지를 품은 어느 거대한 떡갈나무의 둥치로 여길 것이다. 적어도 이 보닛 위에 둥지를 틀고 사는 저 살아 있는 게들을 지켜본다면, 그러한 생각이 거의 틀림없이 떠오를 것이다. 다만, 그것에게 함께 붙여진 \"왕관\"이라는 전문 용어에 그대의 상상력이 사로잡혀 있지 않다면 말이다. 그럴 경우 그대는, 이 강대한 괴물이 실은 바다의 왕관을 쓴 임금이며, 그의 푸른 왕관이 이토록 경이로운 방식으로 그를 위해 엮여졌다는 사실을 떠올리며 깊은 흥미를 느끼게 될 것이다. 그러나 이 고래가 왕이라면, 왕관을 빛내기에는 몹시 뚱한 얼굴을 한 작자다. 저 축 늘어진 아랫입술을 보라! 거기 어찌나 큰 부루퉁함과 뾰로통함이 서려 있는지! 목수의 자로 재자면 길이 약 이십 피트(약 6미터), 깊이 약 오 피트(약 1.5미터)에 이르는 부루퉁함과 뾰로통함, 그대에게 기름을 오백 갤런이나 그 이상 내어 줄 부루퉁함과 뾰로통함이다.\n\n그런데 이 가련한 고래가 언청이라는 것은 참으로 안타까운 일이다. 그 틈은 폭이 약 일 피트(약 30센티미터)나 된다. 아마도 어미가 어느 중대한 시기에 페루 해안을 따라 헤엄쳐 내려가던 차에, 지진이 일어나 해변이 쩍 벌어졌던 모양이다. 미끄러운 문턱을 넘듯 이 입술을 넘어, 우리는 이제 입속으로 미끄러져 들어간다. 정말이지 내가 매키노에 있었다면, 이곳을 인디언의 천막 안이라고 여겼을 것이다. 맙소사! 이것이 요나(Jonah)가 지나간 그 길이란 말인가? 천장은 높이가 약 십이 피트(약 3.6미터)에 이르고, 마치 거기에 번듯한 마룻대라도 걸린 듯 제법 날카로운 각으로 솟아오른다. 한편 갈빗대처럼 늘어서고 아치형으로 굽었으며 털이 난 이 양쪽 옆면은, 한쪽에 삼백 개쯤 되는 저 경이롭고 거의 수직으로 늘어진 언월도 모양의 고래수염 살들을 우리에게 보여 주는데, 이것들은 머리 윗부분 곧 왕관뼈에서 늘어져 내려, 다른 곳에서 잠깐 언급한 바 있는 저 베네치아식 발(簾)을 이룬다. 이 뼈들의 가장자리에는 털 같은 섬유가 술처럼 달려 있어, 참고래는 이를 통해 물을 걸러 내고, 먹이를 먹을 때 입을 벌린 채 브릿이 가득한 바다를 헤치며 나아갈 적에 그 얽힌 섬유 속에 작은 물고기들을 가두어 둔다. 자연스러운 차례대로 늘어선 한가운데 뼈 발에는 어떤 기묘한 무늬와 곡선, 우묵한 곳과 융기가 새겨져 있는데, 더러 어떤 고래잡이들은 떡갈나무의 나이를 그 둥근 나이테로 헤아리듯, 이로써 이 짐승의 나이를 헤아린다. 이 기준의 확실성이 입증과는 거리가 멀지만, 그래도 유추에 따른 개연성의 맛은 풍긴다. 어쨌든 우리가 이를 받아들인다면, 참고래에게는 첫눈에 그럴듯해 보이는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 나이를 인정해 주어야 할 것이다.\n\n옛날에는 이 발들을 두고 가장 기이한 상상들이 떠돌았던 모양이다. 퍼처스에 실린 어느 항해자는 그것들을 고래 입속의 경이로운 \"구레나룻\"이라 부르고, 다른 이는 \"돼지의 뻣뻣한 털\"이라 부르며, 해클루트에 실린 세 번째 노신사는 다음과 같은 우아한 표현을 쓴다. \"그의 윗턱 양쪽에는 이백오십 개쯤 되는 지느러미가 돋아 있어, 입 양편에서 혀 위로 아치를 이루고 있다.\"\n\n* 이는 우리에게, 참고래가 실제로 일종의 구레나룻, 아니 차라리 콧수염을 지니고 있음을 일깨워 준다. 그것은 아래턱 바깥쪽 끝의 윗부분에 드문드문 난 몇 가닥의 흰 털로 이루어져 있다. 더러 이 털 뭉치는 그 본래 근엄한 얼굴에 다소 산적 같은 표정을 부여하기도 한다.\n\n누구나 알다시피, 바로 이 \"돼지의 뻣뻣한 털\", \"지느러미\", \"구레나룻\", \"발\", 혹은 그대가 무어라 부르든 간에, 그것은 부인네들에게 코르셋 살대를 비롯한 여러 빳빳한 도구를 마련해 준다. 그러나 이 특정한 쓰임에 대한 수요는 오래전부터 줄곧 내리막길을 걸어왔다. 이 뼈가 영광을 누리던 때는 앤 여왕 시절로, 그때는 버팀살을 넣은 치마가 한창 유행이었다. 그리고 그 옛 부인네들이, 말하자면 고래의 턱 안에 든 채로도 흥겹게 거닐었듯이, 오늘날 우리 또한 소나기가 쏟아질 때면 그와 똑같은 무심함으로 바로 그 턱 아래로 몸을 피해 달려 들어간다. 우산이란 바로 그 똑같은 뼈 위로 펼쳐진 천막인 것이다.\n\n그러나 이제 발이며 구레나룻 따위는 잠시 다 잊고, 참고래의 입속에 서서 새삼 주위를 둘러보라. 이토록 정연하게 늘어선 이 모든 뼈의 열주(列柱)들을 보고서, 그대는 자신이 저 거대한 하를럼 오르간 안에 들어와 그 수천 개의 파이프를 올려다보고 있다고 여기지 않겠는가? 오르간에 깔린 양탄자로는, 입바닥에 마치 풀로 붙인 듯 들러붙은 혀가 더없이 부드러운 터키산 융단처럼 깔려 있다. 그것은 몹시 기름지고 연하여, 갑판 위로 끌어올릴 때 갈가리 찢어지기 일쑤다. 지금 우리 앞에 놓인 이 혀로 말하자면, 얼핏 보아도 여섯 통짜리라 하겠다. 곧, 그대에게 그만한 양의 기름을 내어 줄 것이라는 뜻이다.\n\n이쯤 되면, 그대는 내가 처음에 꺼낸 말의 진실을 — 향유고래와 참고래가 거의 완전히 다른 머리를 지녔다는 것을 — 분명히 알아보았을 것이다. 그러니 정리하자면 이렇다. 참고래의 머리에는 향유고래에게 있는 그 거대한 정유(精油)의 샘도 없고, 상아 같은 이빨도 전혀 없으며, 길고 가느다란 아래턱뼈도 없다. 반대로 향유고래에게는 저 뼈로 된 발 따위가 하나도 없고, 거대한 아랫입술도 없으며, 혀라 할 만한 것도 거의 없다. 또 참고래는 바깥으로 드러난 분수공이 두 개인데, 향유고래는 단 하나뿐이다.\n\n이제 이 존엄한, 두건을 쓴 듯한 두 머리가 아직 나란히 놓여 있는 동안, 마지막으로 한 번 더 보아 두라. 머지않아 하나는 기록되지 못한 채 바다 속으로 가라앉을 것이며, 다른 하나도 그 뒤를 따르기까지 그리 오래 걸리지 않을 테니.\n\n저기 향유고래의 표정을 알아볼 수 있겠는가? 그것은 그가 죽을 때 지었던 바로 그 표정이며, 다만 이마의 긴 주름살 몇 가닥이 지금은 흐릿하게 바래 가는 듯하다. 내 생각에 그 너른 이마에는 초원과도 같은 평온함이, 죽음에 대한 사색적 무관심에서 비롯된 평온함이 가득 차 있다. 그러나 다른 머리의 표정을 눈여겨보라. 우연히 배의 옆구리에 짓눌려, 그리하여 턱을 단단히 감싸 안은 저 놀라운 아랫입술을 보라. 이 머리 전체가, 죽음을 마주하는 어떤 거대하고도 실제적인 결의를 말하고 있는 것 같지 않은가? 내가 보기에 이 참고래는 스토아주의자였고, 향유고래는 플라톤주의자였으니, 그는 만년에 스피노자라도 펼쳐 들었을 법한 자였다.\n\n# CHAPTER 76. The Battering-Ram.\n\n당분간 향유고래의 머리를 떠나기에 앞서, 나는 당신이 분별 있는 생리학자로서 그저—특히 그 정면의 모습을, 단단히 응축된 그 모든 짜임새 속에서 눈여겨보아 주기를 바란다. 나는 당신이 지금 그것을 조사하되, 오로지 거기 깃들어 있을지도 모를 충각(衝角)의 힘이 어떤 것이든 그에 관해 과장되지 않은, 분별 있는 평가를 스스로 세우려는 목적만으로 그리해 주기를 바란다. 이것은 매우 긴요한 대목이다. 당신은 이 문제를 스스로 만족스럽게 매듭짓든지, 아니면 어쩌면 기록된 역사 전체를 통틀어 어디서도 찾아보기 힘들, 가장 소름 끼치면서도 그럼에도 못지않게 참된 사건들 가운데 하나에 대해 영영 믿지 못하는 자로 남든지 해야 하기 때문이다.\n\n당신은 향유고래의 보통 헤엄치는 자세에서 그 머리의 정면이 물에 대해 거의 완전히 수직인 평면을 이룬다는 것을 관찰하게 된다. 당신은 그 정면의 아랫부분이 상당히 뒤쪽으로 비스듬히 기울어져, 활대 같은 아래턱을 받아들이는 그 긴 소켓에게 좀 더 후퇴할 공간을 마련해 준다는 것을 관찰하게 된다. 당신은 입이 머리의 완전히 아래쪽에 있다는 것을, 실로 마치 당신 자신의 입이 턱 아래에 통째로 달려 있기라도 한 것처럼 그렇게 놓여 있다는 것을 관찰하게 된다. 게다가 당신은 고래에게 바깥으로 드러난 코가 없다는 것, 그리고 코라 할 만한 것—그 분수공—이 머리 꼭대기에 있다는 것을 관찰하게 된다. 당신은 그 눈과 귀가 머리 양옆에, 정면으로부터 전체 몸길이의 거의 삼분의 일쯤 되는 지점에 있다는 것을 관찰하게 된다. 그러므로 당신은 이제 향유고래 머리의 정면이 그 어떤 기관도, 그 어떤 종류의 연약하게 도드라진 부분도 하나 없는, 죽은 듯 눈먼 벽이라는 것을 깨달았을 것이다. 나아가 당신은 이제 그 머리 정면에서 오직 맨 끝, 가장 아래쪽, 뒤로 비스듬히 기울어진 부분에만 뼈의 흔적이 아주 희미하게 있을 뿐이며, 이마로부터 거의 20피트(약 6미터)쯤 안으로 들어가야 비로소 완전한 두개골의 발달에 이르게 된다는 것을 헤아려야 한다. 그러니 이 거대한 뼈 없는 덩어리 전체가 마치 하나의 솜뭉치 같은 것이다. 끝으로, 곧 밝혀지겠지만, 그 내용물은 일부 더없이 정교한 기름으로 이루어져 있다. 그러나 당신은 이제, 겉으로 보기에 그토록 여리고 부드러운 그 모든 것을 그토록 빈틈없이 감싸고 있는 물질의 성질에 관해 알아 두어야 한다. 앞선 어느 대목에서 나는 껍질이 오렌지를 감싸듯 지방층이 고래의 몸을 감싸는 방식을 당신에게 설명한 바 있다. 머리도 꼭 그러하지만, 다만 이런 차이가 있다. 머리를 감싸는 이 덮개는 그만큼 두껍지는 않으나, 그것을 직접 다루어 본 적 없는 사람으로서는 가늠조차 할 수 없는, 뼈 없는 질긴 단단함을 지녔다. 가장 날카롭게 벼린 작살도, 가장 힘센 사람의 팔이 던진 가장 예리한 창도 거기서 맥없이 튕겨 나온다. 마치 향유고래의 이마가 말발굽으로 포장되어 있기라도 한 것 같다. 나는 거기에 어떤 감각도 도사리고 있으리라고는 생각지 않는다.\n\n또 한 가지를 곰곰이 생각해 보라. 큰 짐을 가득 실은 두 척의 동인도 무역선이 부두에서 어쩌다 서로를 향해 밀려들어 짓누르게 될 때, 선원들은 어떻게 하는가? 그들은 막 맞닿으려는 지점에 쇠나 나무 같은 한낱 단단한 물체를 둘 사이에 끼워 넣지 않는다. 그렇지 않다. 그들은 거기에 가장 두껍고 가장 질긴 쇠가죽으로 감싼, 크고 둥근 뱃밥과 코르크 뭉치를 댄다. 그것이 그들의 떡갈나무 지렛대와 쇠 지렛대를 죄다 부러뜨렸을 그 압착을 늠름하게, 아무 손상 없이 받아 낸다. 이것만으로도 내가 말하려는 명백한 사실은 충분히 잘 드러난다. 그러나 이에 덧붙여, 나에게는 가설로서 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. 곧 보통의 물고기들은 제 안에 이른바 부레라는 것을 지녀 마음대로 그것을 부풀리거나 오므릴 수 있는데, 향유고래는 내가 아는 한 그런 장치를 제 안에 갖고 있지 않다는 것, 게다가 그가 머리를 통째로 수면 아래로 가라앉혔다가도 이내 그것을 물 밖으로 높이 치켜든 채 헤엄치는, 달리는 설명할 길 없는 그 방식을 고려할 때, 그 덮개의 거침없는 탄력성을 고려할 때, 그 머리 내부의 유례없는 구조를 고려할 때—내가 말하건대, 나에게는 가설로서 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. 곧 거기 그 신비로운 폐포 모양의 벌집들이 어쩌면 지금까지 알려지지도, 짐작되지도 않은 어떤 방식으로 바깥 공기와 연결되어, 대기에 의해 부풀고 오므라드는 일이 가능할지도 모른다는 것이다. 만일 그렇다면, 모든 원소 가운데 가장 만질 수 없고 가장 파괴적인 것이 한몫 거드는 그 위력의 거역할 수 없음을 한번 상상해 보라.\n\n자, 잘 보라. 이 죽은 듯하고, 뚫을 수 없으며, 상하지 않는 벽과, 그 안에 깃든 더없이 부력 가득한 것을 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 밀어붙이며, 그 모든 것의 뒤에는 어마어마한 생명의 덩어리가 헤엄치고 있으니, 그것은 쌓아 올린 장작더미를 코드 단위로 가늠하듯 그렇게 헤아릴 수밖에 없는 것이며, 그 모두가 가장 작은 곤충이 그러하듯 하나의 의지에 순순히 따른다. 그러므로 내가 앞으로 이 광대한 괴물 곳곳에 도사린 모든 특수한 점들과 응집된 위력을 당신에게 낱낱이 들려줄 때, 또 그가 머리로 들이받아 부숴 버린, 그중에서도 비교적 사소한 몇몇 위업을 당신에게 보여 줄 때, 나는 당신이 무지한 모든 불신을 버리고 다음의 것을 기꺼이 받아들이게 되리라 믿는다. 곧 설령 향유고래가 다리엔 지협을 들이받아 길을 뚫어 대서양과 태평양을 뒤섞어 놓는다 한들, 당신은 눈썹 한 올조차 치켜올리지 않으리라는 것을. 왜냐하면 당신이 고래를 진정으로 제 것으로 삼지 않는 한, 당신은 진리에 있어 한낱 시골뜨기요 감상가에 지나지 않기 때문이다. 그러나 맑은 진리란 오직 불도마뱀 같은 거인들만이 맞닥뜨릴 수 있는 것이니, 그렇다면 시골뜨기들에게 주어진 기회란 얼마나 보잘것없겠는가? 라이스에서 저 무시무시한 여신의 베일을 들어 올린 그 연약한 젊은이에게 무슨 일이 닥쳤던가?\n* 라이스의 여신: 사이스(이집트)의 여신 이시스를 가리키며, \"내 베일을 들어 올린 인간은 아직 없다\"는 비문이 새겨져 있었다고 전한다. 베일을 들춰 본 젊은이는 그 충격으로 목숨을 잃었다는 전설이 있다.\n\n# CHAPTER 77. The Great Heidelburgh Tun.\n\n이제 케이스(case)에서 기름을 퍼내는 작업이 시작된다. 그러나 이 일을 제대로 이해하려면, 그 작업 대상이 되는 물건의 기묘한 내부 구조에 관해 어느 정도 알아 둘 필요가 있다.\n\n향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 머리를 단단한 직육면체라고 본다면, 그것을 비스듬한 평면을 따라 옆으로 두 개의 쐐기꼴 덩이로 나눌 수 있다.* 그중 아래쪽은 두개골과 턱을 이루는 골격 구조이고, 위쪽은 뼈라고는 전혀 없는 기름진 덩어리인데, 그 넓고 앞으로 나온 끝부분이 바로 고래의 펼쳐진, 수직으로 솟은 듯한 이마를 이룬다. 이 위쪽 쐐기꼴을 이마 한가운데에서 수평으로 다시 나누면, 거의 똑같은 두 부분이 생기는데, 이것들은 본래 두꺼운 힘줄질 물질로 된 내벽에 의해 자연스럽게 나뉘어 있던 것이다.\n\n* 쐐기꼴(quoin)은 유클리드 기하학의 용어가 아니다. 그것은 순수 항해 수학에 속하는 말이다. 이전에 정의된 적이 있는지 나는 알지 못한다. 쐐기꼴이란, 양쪽 면이 함께 뾰족해지면서 날을 이루는 쐐기와는 달리, 한쪽 면의 가파른 경사만으로 그 날카로운 끝이 이루어지는 입체를 말한다.\n\n아래쪽으로 다시 나뉜 부분은 정크(junk)라 불리는데, 그 전체에 걸쳐 질기고 탄력 있는 흰 섬유들이 교차하고 또 교차하며 수만 개의 스며든 작은 방들을 이루어, 거대한 기름의 벌집을 이룬다. 케이스라 불리는 위쪽 부분은 향유고래의 거대한 하이델베르크 술통(Heidelburgh Tun)이라 여길 만하다. 그리고 저 유명한 큰 술통이 앞면에 신비로운 조각으로 새겨져 있듯이, 고래의 광대하게 주름진 이마 또한 그 놀라운 술통을 상징적으로 장식하는 수많은 기묘한 무늬들을 이룬다. 더욱이, 하이델베르크의 술통이 라인 계곡에서 나는 가장 빼어난 포도주로 늘 가득 채워져 있었듯이, 고래의 술통에는 그의 온갖 기름진 양조주 가운데 단연 가장 값진 것이 담겨 있다. 곧 더없이 순수하고 맑으며 향기로운 상태의, 그토록 귀하게 여겨지는 향유(spermaceti)다. 이 귀한 물질은 이 짐승의 다른 어떤 부위에서도 순수한 채로는 발견되지 않는다. 살아 있을 때는 완전히 액체 상태를 유지하지만, 죽은 뒤 공기에 노출되면 곧 굳기 시작하여, 마치 물 위에 처음으로 얇고 섬세한 얼음이 막 얼어붙을 때처럼 아름다운 결정의 싹들을 뻗어 낸다. 큰 고래의 케이스 하나에서는 대개 500갤런쯤의 향유가 나오는데, 다만 어쩔 수 없는 사정으로 적잖은 양이 쏟아지고, 새고, 흘러 떨어지거나, 그렇지 않으면 건질 수 있는 것을 건지려는 그 까다로운 작업 중에 영영 잃어버리고 만다.\n\n하이델베르크 술통이 그 안쪽에 어떤 곱고 값진 재료로 입혀져 있었는지 나는 알지 못하지만, 그 안감이 아무리 화려하다 한들, 고급 모피 외투의 안감처럼 향유고래 케이스의 안쪽 표면을 이루는 저 비단결의 진주빛 막에는 결코 그 풍요로움에 견줄 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n지금까지 본 바로, 향유고래의 하이델베르크 술통은 머리 꼭대기 전체의 길이를 고스란히 차지하고 있음을 알 수 있다. 그리고 — 앞서 다른 곳에서 밝혔듯이 — 머리가 그 짐승 전체 길이의 삼분의 일을 차지하므로, 제법 큰 고래의 길이를 80피트(약 24미터)로 잡는다면, 그 술통을 길이 방향으로 배 옆구리에 세워 매달았을 때 그 깊이는 26피트(약 8미터)가 넘는 셈이다.\n\n고래의 목을 자를 때, 작업자의 연장은 나중에 향유 저장고 안으로 강제로 입구를 뚫게 될 바로 그 지점 가까이에 닿게 된다. 그러므로 그는 부주의하고 때 이른 한 칼질이 그 성소를 침범하여 그 값을 매길 수 없는 내용물을 허비하며 흘려보내지 않도록, 유난히 조심하지 않으면 안 된다. 마침내 물 밖으로 들어 올려져 그 자세로 붙들리게 되는 것도 바로 이 머리의 잘린 끝부분인데, 그것을 붙드는 것은 거대한 절단용 도르래 장치이며, 그 삼밧줄 얽힘이 한쪽에서는 그 일대를 온통 밧줄의 황무지로 만들어 놓는다.\n\n이만큼 말해 두었으니, 이제 부디 주목해 주기를 바란다. 향유고래의 거대한 하이델베르크 술통에 마침내 구멍을 뚫는, 저 경이롭고도 — 바로 이번 경우에는 — 거의 치명적이었던 작업에.\n\n# CHAPTER 78. Cistern and Buckets.\n\n고양이처럼 날렵하게 타슈테고(Tashtego)가 위로 기어오른다. 그는 꼿꼿한 자세를 흩뜨리지 않은 채 그대로 머리 위로 뻗어 나온 큰돛 활대 끝을 따라 똑바로 걸어 나아가, 매달려 올려진 통의 바로 위로 정확히 돌출된 지점에 이른다. 그는 채찍이라 불리는 가벼운 도르래 장치를 가지고 올라갔는데, 이것은 단지 두 가닥으로만 이루어져 외바퀴 활차 하나를 통과하는 것이다. 그는 이 활차가 활대 끝에서 늘어지도록 단단히 고정한 뒤, 밧줄의 한쪽 끝을 흔들어 갑판 위의 누군가가 그것을 붙잡아 단단히 쥐게 한다. 그러고 나서 인디언은 다른 한쪽을 손에서 손으로 잡아 내리며 공중을 타고 내려와, 마침내 솜씨 좋게 고래 머리 꼭대기에 내려선다. 그곳에서 — 여전히 다른 동료들보다 높이 솟아오른 채, 그가 활기차게 소리쳐 부르는 그들을 향해 — 그는 마치 탑 꼭대기에서 선량한 사람들을 기도로 불러 모으는 어느 터키 무에진처럼 보인다. 자루가 짧고 날이 선 삽 하나가 그에게 올려지자, 그는 통을 뚫고 들어가기 시작할 적당한 자리를 부지런히 찾는다. 이 일을 그는 매우 신중하게 진행하는데, 마치 어느 낡은 집에서 보물을 찾는 사람이 금이 벽돌 틈에 박혀 있는 곳을 알아내려고 벽을 두드려 보는 것과도 같다. 이 조심스러운 탐색이 끝날 무렵, 우물에서 쓰는 두레박과 똑 닮은, 쇠테를 두른 튼튼한 양동이가 채찍의 한쪽 끝에 매달리고, 다른 한쪽 끝은 갑판을 가로질러 뻗어 두세 명의 민첩한 일손들이 거머쥐고 있다. 이들이 이제 양동이를 인디언의 손이 닿는 데까지 끌어올리자, 또 다른 사람이 그에게 아주 긴 장대를 올려 건넨다. 타슈테고는 이 장대를 양동이에 끼워 넣고, 양동이를 아래로 통 안으로 몰아넣어 그것이 완전히 사라질 때까지 내린다. 그런 다음 채찍을 잡은 선원들에게 신호를 보내면, 양동이는 갓 짜낸 우유가 담긴 우유 짜는 처녀의 들통처럼 온통 거품을 일으키며 다시 올라온다. 그 높이에서 조심스럽게 내려진, 가득 찬 그릇은 지정된 일손이 받아 들어 재빨리 커다란 통에 비운다. 그러고는 다시 위로 올라가, 깊은 저수조가 더는 아무것도 내어 주지 않을 때까지 똑같은 과정을 되풀이한다. 끝 무렵에 이르면 타슈테고는 자신의 긴 장대를 점점 더 세게, 점점 더 깊이 통 속으로 박아 넣어야 하는데, 마침내는 장대가 스무 자가량이나 들어가 버린다.\n\n이제 피쿼드호(Pequod)의 사람들은 이런 식으로 한동안 퍼내고 있었다. 향기로운 향유로 통 여러 개가 채워졌다. 그런데 별안간 기이한 사고가 일어났다. 저 거친 인디언 타슈테고가 너무 부주의하고 무모한 나머지, 고래 머리를 매달고 있는 굵은 밧줄 도르래에 한 손으로 매달려 있던 것을 한순간 놓아 버린 탓인지, 아니면 그가 서 있던 자리가 그토록 미끄럽고 진득거리는 곳이었던 탓인지, 아니면 악마 그 자신이 — 그 특별한 까닭을 굳이 밝히지는 않은 채 — 그렇게 되기를 바란 탓인지, 정확히 어찌 된 일인지는 이제 와서 말할 도리가 없다. 다만 갑자기, 여든 번째인가 아흔 번째인가의 양동이가 쪽쪽 빨아들이는 소리를 내며 올라오던 그 순간 — 오, 하느님! 가엾은 타슈테고가 — 진짜 우물 속에서 서로 오르내리는 쌍둥이 두레박처럼, 머리부터 거꾸로 이 거대한 하이델베르크의 통 속으로 떨어져, 끔찍하게 기름이 콸콸거리는 소리와 함께 깨끗이 시야에서 사라지고 말았다!\n\n\"사람이 빠졌다!\" 다구(Daggoo)가 외쳤다. 모두가 경악에 빠진 가운데 가장 먼저 정신을 차린 자였다. \"양동이를 이쪽으로 흔들어 보내!\" 그러고는 채찍 자체를 미끄러운 손으로 더 단단히 붙잡기 위해 한 발을 양동이 안에 들여놓자, 끌어올리는 자들은 타슈테고가 머리 속 밑바닥에 채 닿기도 전에 그를 머리 꼭대기까지 높이 끌어올렸다. 그러는 동안 무시무시한 소동이 벌어졌다. 뱃전 너머를 내려다보니, 조금 전까지 생명 없던 고래 머리가 마치 그 순간 어떤 중대한 생각에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯, 바다 표면 바로 아래에서 두근거리며 들썩이고 있었다. 그러나 그것은 다만 가엾은 인디언이 자신이 가라앉은 위험한 깊이를 그 몸부림으로 무심코 드러내고 있는 것에 지나지 않았다.\n\n바로 이 순간, 다구가 머리 꼭대기에서 채찍을 — 어쩌다 거대한 절단용 도르래에 엉켜 버린 그것을 — 풀어내고 있을 때, 날카롭게 우지끈 갈라지는 소리가 들려왔다. 그리고 모두가 이루 말할 수 없는 공포에 휩싸인 가운데, 머리를 매달고 있던 두 개의 거대한 갈고리 중 하나가 뜯겨 나가더니, 어마어마한 진동과 함께 그 거대한 덩어리가 옆으로 휙 흔들렸고, 그 바람에 술 취한 듯 비틀거리던 배가 마치 빙산에 부딪힌 것처럼 휘청거리며 떨었다. 이제 모든 무게가 실리게 된 단 하나 남은 갈고리는, 매 순간 곧 떨어져 나갈 듯 보였다. 머리가 격렬하게 요동치는 탓에 그 가능성은 더욱더 커 보였다.\n\n\"내려와, 내려오라고!\" 선원들이 다구에게 고함쳤다. 그러나 그는 한 손으로 무거운 도르래를 붙든 채여서, 설령 머리가 떨어진다 해도 그대로 매달려 있을 참이었다. 흑인은 엉킨 밧줄을 풀어낸 뒤, 이제 무너져 내린 우물 속으로 양동이를 박아 넣었다. 파묻힌 작살잡이가 그것을 붙잡으면 끌어올릴 수 있으리라는 생각에서였다.\n\n\"제발, 이 사람아,\" 스터브(Stubb)가 소리쳤다. \"거기다 대고 탄약을 쟁여 넣기라도 하는 건가? — 멈춰! 그게 그자에게 무슨 도움이 되겠나, 쇠테 두른 양동이를 그자 머리통 위에다 처박아서 말이야? 그만두라니까!\"\n\n\"도르래에서 비켜!\" 로켓이 터지는 듯한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n거의 같은 순간, 우레 같은 굉음과 함께 그 거대한 덩어리가 바다로 떨어졌다. 나이아가라의 테이블바위가 소용돌이 속으로 떨어지듯이. 갑작스레 짐을 던 선체는 그것에서 멀찍이 굴러나며 번쩍이는 구리판 밑바닥까지 드러냈다. 그리고 모두가 숨을 멈춘 채 바라보니, 절반쯤 흔들리며 — 이제는 선원들 머리 위로, 이제는 물 위로 — 다구가 자욱한 물보라 안개를 뚫고 흔들리는 도르래에 매달려 있는 모습이 어렴풋이 보였고, 한편 가엾은, 산 채로 파묻힌 타슈테고는 끝없이 바다 밑바닥으로 가라앉고 있었다! 그러나 눈을 가리던 수증기가 채 걷히기도 전에, 손에 단검을 든 벌거벗은 형상 하나가 한순간 뱃전 위로 솟구쳐 머무는 모습이 보였다. 다음 순간, 요란한 물소리가 나의 용감한 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 구하러 뛰어들었음을 알렸다. 사람들이 뱃전으로 한 덩어리가 되어 우르르 몰려들었고, 순간이 순간으로 이어지는 동안 모든 눈이 잔물결 하나하나를 헤아렸으나, 가라앉은 자도 뛰어든 자도 그 어떤 자취조차 보이지 않았다. 이제 몇몇 일손이 뱃전에 댄 보트로 뛰어들어 배에서 조금 떨어져 나갔다.\n\n\"하! 하!\" 다구가 별안간 외쳤다. 이제는 머리 위에서 조용히 흔들리는 그의 자리에서였다. 뱃전에서 더 멀리 내다보니, 푸른 물결 사이로 팔 하나가 똑바로 솟아 있는 것이 보였다. 마치 무덤 위 풀밭에서 팔 하나가 불쑥 솟아오른 것을 보는 듯, 기이한 광경이었다.\n\n\"둘 다! 둘 다야! — 둘 다라고!\" 다구가 다시 기쁨에 겨워 환호성을 질렀다. 곧이어, 퀴케그가 한 손으로는 대담하게 물살을 가르고 다른 한 손으로는 인디언의 긴 머리채를 움켜쥐고 있는 모습이 보였다. 기다리고 있던 보트로 끌어 올려진 두 사람은 재빨리 갑판으로 옮겨졌다. 그러나 타슈테고는 한참이 지나서야 정신을 차렸고, 퀴케그도 그리 기운차 보이지는 않았다.\n\n자, 이 갸륵한 구조는 어떻게 이루어졌던가? 다름 아니라, 천천히 가라앉는 머리를 뒤쫓아 잠수한 퀴케그가 날카로운 단검으로 그 밑바닥 가까이를 옆에서 몇 차례 찔러 커다란 구멍 하나를 뚫었던 것이다. 그런 다음 단검을 버리고는 긴 팔을 안쪽으로, 위쪽으로 깊숙이 밀어 넣어, 가엾은 타슈를 머리부터 끌어냈다. 그가 단언하기를, 처음 그를 찾아 손을 밀어 넣었을 때는 다리 한쪽이 먼저 닿았으나, 그것이 마땅한 순서가 아니며 큰 곤란을 일으킬 수 있음을 잘 알고 있었기에 — 다리를 도로 밀어 넣고, 능숙하게 들어 올려 휙 돌리는 솜씨로 인디언의 몸을 한 바퀴 공중제비 돌게 했다. 그리하여 다음번 시도에서는 그가 좋고 오래된 방식대로 — 머리부터 — 나왔다는 것이다. 거대한 머리 그 자체로 말하자면, 그것은 기대할 수 있는 한 가장 좋은 상태였다.\n\n이리하여, 퀴케그의 용기와 산파술에 관한 뛰어난 솜씨를 통해, 타슈테고의 구출, 아니 차라리 그 출산은 더할 나위 없이 불운하고 겉보기에는 가망 없어 보이는 장애를 무릅쓰고도 멋지게 이루어졌다. 이것은 결코 잊어서는 안 될 교훈이다. 산파술도 펜싱과 권투, 승마와 노 젓기와 똑같은 과정 속에서 가르쳐야 마땅하다.\n\n이 게이헤드 인디언의 기묘한 모험담이 어떤 뭍사람들에게는 틀림없이 믿기 어렵게 들리리라는 것을 나는 안다. 비록 그들 자신도 누군가가 뭍의 저수조에 빠진 일을 보았거나 들어 보았을 법한데도 말이다. 그런 사고는 드물지 않게 일어나며, 게다가 향유고래의 우물 가장자리가 그토록 지독히 미끄럽다는 점을 헤아려 보면, 인디언의 경우보다 훨씬 덜한 까닭으로도 벌어지는 일인데도 그렇다.\n\n그러나 어쩌면 누군가는 영리하게 따져 물을지도 모른다. 이게 대체 어찌 된 일인가? 우리는 향유고래의 결결이 짜여 스며든 머리야말로 그 몸에서 가장 가볍고 코르크처럼 잘 뜨는 부분이라고 알고 있었거늘, 그런데도 그대는 그것을 제 무게보다 훨씬 비중이 큰 원소 속으로 가라앉게 만드는가. 자, 이제 그대를 붙잡았노라. 천만의 말씀, 오히려 내가 그대들을 붙잡았노라. 가엾은 타슈가 빠진 그 순간에는 케이스(case) 속에서 가벼운 내용물이 거의 다 비워진 뒤여서, 남은 것이라곤 우물의 빽빽한 힘줄 벽뿐이었기 때문이다. 그것은 앞서 말했듯이 이중으로 용접되고 두들겨 다져진 물질로서, 바닷물보다 훨씬 무거워, 그 한 덩어리는 거의 납처럼 바닷속으로 가라앉는다. 그러나 이 물질이 빠르게 가라앉으려는 경향은, 이번 경우에는 머리의 다른 부분들이 그것에서 떨어져 나가지 않고 그대로 붙어 있던 덕분에 상당히 상쇄되었다. 그리하여 머리는 참으로 아주 천천히, 신중하게 가라앉았고, 그 덕에 퀴케그에게는, 말하자면 달리는 중에 그 날렵한 산파술을 펼칠 그럴듯한 기회가 주어졌던 것이다. 그렇다, 그것은 달리는 도중의 출산이었다, 정말 그러했다.\n\n자, 만일 타슈테고가 그 머리 속에서 목숨을 잃었더라면, 그것은 참으로 진귀한 죽음이었으리라. 더없이 새하얗고 곱디고운 향기로운 경뇌유 속에 질식되어, 고래의 은밀한 내실, 곧 지성소 안에 관처럼 누워, 영구차에 실리고 무덤에 묻히는 셈이었으니. 이보다 더 달콤한 최후로는 단 하나만이 얼른 떠오른다 — 어느 오하이오 꿀 사냥꾼의 감미로운 죽음이다. 속이 빈 나무 가지 틈에서 꿀을 찾던 그는 거기에 어찌나 엄청난 양의 꿀이 쌓여 있던지, 너무 깊이 몸을 기울인 나머지 그 속으로 빨려 들어가, 그렇게 방부 처리된 채 죽고 말았다. 생각해 보라, 얼마나 많은 이들이 그와 마찬가지로 플라톤의 꿀 같은 머릿속에 빠져들어, 그곳에서 달콤하게 스러져 갔겠는가?\n\n# CHAPTER 79. The Prairie.\n\n이 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 얼굴 선을 살피거나 그 머리의 융기를 더듬어 보는 일, 이것은 아직 어떤 관상학자나 골상학자도 손대지 않은 과업이다. 그런 시도는, 라바터가 지브롤터 바위의 주름살을 면밀히 들여다보거나, 갈이 사다리를 타고 올라가 판테온의 둥근 지붕을 만지작거리려 드는 것만큼이나 가망 없어 보일 것이다. 그럼에도 라바터는 그 유명한 저작에서 사람의 갖가지 얼굴뿐 아니라 말과 새와 뱀과 물고기의 얼굴까지도 주의 깊게 연구하고, 그 안에서 분간되는 표정의 변화를 세세히 논했다. 갈과 그의 제자 슈푸르츠하임 또한 인간이 아닌 다른 존재들의 골상학적 특징에 관해 몇 가지 단서를 던지는 일을 게을리하지 않았다. 그러므로 비록 이 두 가지 반쪽짜리 과학을 고래에 적용하는 데 있어 나는 개척자로서는 한참 자격이 모자라지만, 그래도 한번 힘껏 해 보려 한다. 나는 무엇이든 시도한다. 그리고 할 수 있는 만큼은 이루어 낸다.\n\n관상학적으로 본다면 향유고래(Sperm Whale)는 변칙적인 동물이다. 그에게는 제대로 된 코가 없다. 코란 얼굴 생김새 가운데 한복판에 자리 잡은 가장 두드러진 부분이며, 어쩌면 그 모든 생김새가 어우러진 표정을 가장 많이 바꾸고 끝내 좌우하는 것이기에, 외부로 돌출한 부속물로서의 코가 통째로 없다는 사실은 고래의 얼굴 인상에 무척이나 크게 작용할 듯하다. 마치 조경에서 어떤 종류든 첨탑이나 둥근 지붕, 기념비, 혹은 탑이 있어야 비로소 풍경이 완성된다고 여겨지듯이, 코라는 높직하게 트인 종루(鐘樓)가 없으면 어떤 얼굴도 관상학적으로 격을 갖출 수 없는 법이다. 피디아스가 빚어낸 대리석 주피터 상에서 코를 떼어 보라, 남는 것이 얼마나 한심한 꼴이겠는가! 그렇지만 리바이어던은 워낙 위풍당당한 거구인 데다 그 모든 비례가 어찌나 당당한지, 조각된 주피터에게라면 흉측했을 바로 그 결함이 그에게는 조금도 흠이 되지 않는다. 아니, 오히려 더해진 장엄함이다. 고래에게 코란 어울리지 않는 군더더기였으리라. 관상학적 항해에 나서 그대가 거룻배를 타고 그 거대한 머리를 빙 둘러 노 저어 갈 때, 잡아당길 코가 있다는 생각 따위로 그에 대한 고귀한 상념이 더럽혀지는 일은 결코 없다. 옥좌에 앉은 더없이 위엄 있는 왕실 의전관을 바라볼 때조차 곧잘 끼어들겠다고 우겨 대는 그 고약한 망상 말이다.\n\n어떤 점에서 향유고래에 대해 얻을 수 있는 가장 위풍당당한 관상학적 광경은, 아마 그 머리의 정면을 온전히 마주하는 모습일 것이다. 이 모습은 숭고하다.\n\n사색에 잠긴 인간의 멋진 이마는 아침놀로 어수선한 동녘과도 같다. 풀밭에서 한가로이 쉴 때, 황소의 굽이진 이마에는 어딘가 장엄한 기운이 서려 있다. 산골짜기 비탈로 묵직한 대포를 밀어 올리는 코끼리의 이마는 위풍당당하다. 인간의 것이든 짐승의 것이든, 신비로운 이마란 게르만 황제들이 칙령에 찍던 저 거대한 황금 옥새와 같다. 그것이 뜻하는 바는—\"하느님: 오늘 내 손으로 이 일을 행하노라\"이다. 그러나 대부분의 피조물에게, 아니 인간 자신에게도 흔히 이마란 설선(雪線)을 따라 길게 누운 한 자락 알프스 고원에 지나지 않는다. 셰익스피어나 멜란히톤의 이마처럼 그토록 높이 솟았다가 그토록 낮게 내려와, 두 눈 자체가 맑고 영원하며 조수 없는 산정 호수처럼 보이는 이마는 드물다. 그런 이마에서는 그 위쪽 주름살을 따라, 마치 하일랜드의 사냥꾼들이 사슴이 눈밭에 남긴 발자국을 좇듯, 뿔 달린 사념들이 물을 마시러 그곳으로 내려오는 자취를 더듬어 갈 수 있을 듯하다. 그러나 거대한 향유고래에 이르면 이마에 깃든 이 높고도 강대한 신적 위엄이 어찌나 엄청나게 증폭되는지, 그 온전한 정면을 응시할 때 그대는 살아 있는 자연의 다른 어떤 대상을 바라볼 때보다도 신성(神性)과 두려운 권능을 더욱 강렬하게 느끼게 된다. 그도 그럴 것이 그대는 어느 한 점도 또렷이 보지 못하고, 어느 뚜렷한 생김새 하나 드러나지 않으며, 코도 눈도 귀도 입도, 얼굴조차 없으니, 그에게는 제대로 된 얼굴이란 게 없고, 다만 수수께끼로 주름진 그 너른 창공 같은 이마 하나뿐이어서, 보트며 배며 사람들의 파멸을 말없이 굽어보며 잔뜩 찌푸리고 있을 뿐이다. 그렇다고 옆에서 본다 해서 이 경이로운 이마가 작아지는 것도 아니다. 다만 그렇게 보면 그 장엄함이 그대를 그토록 짓누르듯 압도하지는 않는다. 옆에서 보면, 이마 한가운데 가로로 패인 그 반(半)초승달 모양의 함몰이 또렷이 눈에 들어오는데, 그것은 인간에게서라면 라바터가 천재의 표징이라 일컫던 것이다.\n\n그러나 어찌 그러한가? 향유고래에게 천재성이라니? 향유고래가 일찍이 책을 쓴 적이 있었던가, 연설을 한 적이 있었던가? 아니다. 그의 위대한 천재성은 그것을 입증할 만한 별다른 일을 아무것도 하지 않는다는 데서 선언된다. 또한 그것은 그의 피라미드 같은 침묵으로써 선언된다. 그리고 이 대목에서 떠오르는 생각이 있으니, 만일 그 위대한 향유고래가 어린 동방 세계에 알려졌더라면, 그들의 유치한 주술적 사고에 의해 그는 신으로 떠받들어졌으리라는 것이다. 그들은 나일강의 악어를 신격화했는데, 그것은 악어에게 혀가 없기 때문이었다. 그런데 향유고래에게도 혀가 없거나, 적어도 너무나 작아서 내밀 수조차 없을 지경이다. 만일 훗날 어느 고도로 세련되고 시적인 민족이 옛날의 그 흥겨운 오월제(五月祭) 신들을 그들 본래의 자리로 다시 꾀어들이고, 이제는 오만해진 하늘에, 이제는 신령 없는 언덕에 그들을 살아 있는 모습으로 다시 옹립한다면, 그때는 분명 주피터의 드높은 옥좌에 떠받들려 그 위대한 향유고래가 군림하게 되리라.\n\n샹폴리옹은 화강암에 새겨진 주름진 상형문자를 해독했다. 그러나 모든 인간과 모든 존재의 얼굴이라는 이집트를 해독해 줄 샹폴리옹은 어디에도 없다. 관상학도 다른 모든 인간의 학문과 마찬가지로 한때 스쳐 지나가는 우화에 지나지 않는다. 그러니 서른 개 언어를 읽어 냈던 윌리엄 존스 경조차 가장 단순한 농부의 얼굴에 담긴 더 깊고 더 미묘한 뜻을 읽어 내지 못했거늘, 일자무식인 이슈마엘이 어찌 향유고래의 이마에 새겨진 저 무시무시한 칼데아 문자를 읽어 내기를 바랄 수 있겠는가? 나는 다만 그 이마를 그대 앞에 내놓을 뿐이다. 읽을 수 있거든 읽어 보라.\n\n# CHAPTER 80. The Nut.\n\n향유고래가 골상학적으로 스핑크스라면, 골상학자에게 그 뇌는 도저히 정방형으로 만들 수 없는 저 기하학의 원과도 같아 보인다.\n\n다 자란 향유고래의 두개골은 길이가 적어도 20피트(약 6미터)는 된다. 아래턱을 떼어 내고 이 두개골을 옆에서 보면, 평평한 바닥 위에 처음부터 끝까지 얹혀 있는 완만한 경사면의 측면처럼 보인다. 그러나 살아 있을 때는—다른 곳에서 이미 보았듯이—이 경사면이 모가 나게 채워져, 그 위에 얹힌 융크(junk)와 향유의 거대한 덩어리로 인해 거의 정방형을 이룬다. 높은 쪽 끝에서 두개골은 그 덩어리의 일부를 받쳐 주는 분화구를 이루고, 이 분화구의 길쭉한 바닥 아래—길이도 깊이도 좀처럼 10인치(약 25센티미터)를 넘지 않는 또 다른 빈 공간 속에—이 괴물의 뇌가 한 줌 분량으로 자리 잡고 있다. 살아 있을 때 이 뇌는 겉으로 드러난 이마에서 적어도 20피트나 떨어져 있으며, 마치 퀘벡의 광대한 요새 안에 깊숙이 들어앉은 가장 안쪽의 성채처럼, 그 거대한 외벽 뒤에 꼭꼭 숨겨져 있다. 그것이 어찌나 정교한 함처럼 그의 몸속에 감추어져 있는지, 나는 향유고래에게 그 향유 창고를 이루는 수 세제곱야드의 덩어리가 빚어낸 손에 잡히는 뇌의 형상 말고는 어떤 다른 뇌도 없다고 단호히 부정하는 고래잡이들을 몇몇 알고 있다. 기이한 주름과 굽이와 소용돌이를 이루며 누워 있는 그 신비로운 부위야말로, 그들이 보기에는 그의 전반적 위력에 더 잘 어울리는, 곧 그의 지성이 깃든 자리로 여길 만하다는 것이다.\n\n그러니 골상학적으로 보자면 이 리바이어던의 머리는, 살아서 온전한 상태에 있을 때 완전한 착각이라는 것이 분명해진다. 그의 참된 뇌로 말하자면, 그것을 가리키는 어떤 표시도 볼 수 없고 어떤 느낌도 받을 수 없다. 고래는 강대한 모든 것들이 그러하듯, 세상 사람들에게 거짓된 이마를 내보이는 것이다.\n\n그의 두개골에서 향유 더미를 비워 내고 그 뒤쪽 끝, 곧 높은 쪽 끝을 뒤에서 바라보면, 같은 상황에서 같은 시점으로 바라본 인간의 두개골과 너무도 닮아 깜짝 놀라게 될 것이다. 실제로 이 뒤집힌 두개골을(인간 크기로 축소하여) 사람들의 두개골을 늘어놓은 표본 사이에 두면, 자기도 모르게 그것들과 뒤섞어 버릴 것이다. 그리고 그 꼭대기 한 부분이 움푹 꺼진 것을 보고는, 골상학 용어로 이렇게 말할 것이다—이 사람은 자존감도 없고 경외심도 없었다. 그리고 이러한 부정적 특징들을, 그의 어마어마한 부피와 힘이라는 긍정적 사실과 함께 고려해 본다면, 가장 드높은 위력이 무엇인지에 대해 가장 참되되 가장 유쾌하지는 않은 관념을 스스로 가장 잘 그려 낼 수 있을 것이다.\n\n그러나 고래 본연의 뇌가 상대적으로 그토록 작은 것을 보고 그것을 제대로 도면화할 수 없다고 여긴다면, 내게는 또 다른 생각이 있다. 거의 어떤 네발짐승이든 그 척추를 주의 깊게 살펴보면, 그 척추뼈들이 작아진 두개골들을 줄에 꿴 목걸이를 닮았다는 데에 놀라게 될 것인데, 그 하나하나가 본래의 두개골과 초보적인 닮음을 지니고 있다. 척추뼈란 곧 전혀 발달하지 못한 두개골이라는 것이 독일인들의 한 가지 착상이다. 그러나 이 묘한 외형적 닮음을 처음 알아챈 것이 독일인들은 아니었으리라고 나는 생각한다. 언젠가 어느 이방인 친구가 그 점을 내게 일러 준 적이 있는데, 그는 자기가 죽인 원수의 뼈대를 가지고, 그 척추뼈로 카누의 부리 모양 뱃머리를 일종의 얕은 돋을새김(basso-relievo)으로 박아 넣고 있었다. 그러므로 나는 골상학자들이 소뇌에서 척수관을 거쳐 가는 데까지 연구를 밀어붙이지 않은 것이 중요한 점을 빠뜨린 일이라고 본다. 나는 사람의 성품 가운데 많은 부분이 그 등뼈에 나타나 있으리라 믿기 때문이다. 그대가 누구든, 나는 그대의 두개골보다 그대의 척추를 만져 보고 싶다. 빈약한 들보 같은 척추는 충만하고 고귀한 영혼을 떠받쳐 본 적이 없다. 나는 내 척추를 기뻐하노니, 그것은 내가 세상을 향해 반쯤 내걸어 펄럭이는 저 깃발의, 굳세고 담대한 깃대와도 같은 것이다.\n\n골상학의 이 척추 분야를 향유고래에게 적용해 보자. 그의 두개강은 첫 번째 목뼈와 이어져 있고, 그 목뼈에서 척수관 바닥은 폭이 10인치(약 25센티미터), 높이는 8인치(약 20센티미터)에 이르며, 밑변이 아래를 향한 삼각형 모양을 이룬다. 나머지 척추뼈들을 지나면서 이 관은 점차 가늘어지지만, 상당한 거리에 걸쳐 큰 용적을 유지한다. 그런데 물론 이 관은 뇌와 거의 똑같이 기묘하게 섬유질로 된 물질—곧 척수—로 채워져 있고, 뇌와 직접 통해 있다. 게다가 더욱 놀라운 것은, 뇌의 공동을 빠져나온 뒤로도 여러 피트에 걸쳐 척수는 굵기가 줄지 않은 채, 거의 뇌의 굵기에 맞먹는 상태로 남아 있다는 점이다. 이 모든 사정을 고려할 때, 고래의 척추를 골상학적으로 조사하고 도면화하는 일이 과연 터무니없다 하겠는가? 이러한 관점에서 보면, 그의 본래 뇌가 상대적으로 놀랍도록 작다는 사실은, 그의 척수가 상대적으로 놀랍도록 크다는 사실로 충분히 보상되고도 남기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 이 암시는 골상학자들이 알아서 쓰도록 남겨 두고, 나는 다만 향유고래의 혹과 관련하여 잠시 이 척추 이론을 가정해 보고자 한다. 이 위풍당당한 혹은, 내가 틀리지 않았다면, 더 큰 척추뼈들 가운데 하나 위로 솟아오른 것이며, 따라서 어떤 의미에서는 그 척추뼈의 볼록한 바깥 거푸집인 셈이다. 그렇다면 그 상대적 위치로 보아, 나는 이 높다란 혹을 향유고래의 굳건함 또는 불굴의 기상이 깃든 기관이라 부르고 싶다. 그리고 이 거대한 괴물이 굴복할 줄 모르는 존재임을, 그대는 머지않아 알게 될 까닭이 있으리라.\n\n# CHAPTER 81. The Pequod Meets The Virgin.\n\n81장 피쿼드호, 처녀호를 만나다\n운명으로 정해진 그날이 찾아왔고, 우리는 마침내 브레멘 선적의 융프라우(Jungfrau)호를 만났다. 선장은 데리크 데 데어(Derick De Deer)였다.\n\n한때 세계에서 가장 위대한 포경 민족이었던 네덜란드인과 독일인은 이제 가장 보잘것없는 축에 속한다. 그러나 위도와 경도로 멀찍멀찍 떨어진 곳에서나마, 그대는 지금도 이따금 태평양에서 그들의 깃발과 마주치게 된다.\n\n무슨 까닭에서인지 융프라우호는 우리에게 인사를 차리려고 무척 안달이 난 듯했다. 아직 피쿼드(Pequod)호로부터 제법 떨어진 거리에서 그 배는 뱃머리를 돌리더니 보트 한 척을 내렸고, 그 선장은 고물이 아니라 뱃머리에 참을성 없이 버티고 선 채 우리 쪽으로 떠밀려 왔다.\n\n\"저자가 손에 들고 있는 게 뭐지?\" 스타벅(Starbuck)이 독일인이 흔들흔들 들고 있는 무언가를 가리키며 외쳤다. \"설마—램프 기름통이라니!\"\n\n\"그건 아니지.\" 스터브(Stubb)가 말했다. \"아니, 아니라고. 저건 커피포트요, 스타벅 씨. 저 독일 양반이 우리한테 커피를 끓여 주러 오는 거란 말이오. 저 양반 옆구리에 붙은 저 큼지막한 양철통이 안 보이시오?—저게 끓는 물이지. 아, 저 독일 양반, 괜찮은 사람일세.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리들 하는 거야.\" 플래스크(Flask)가 외쳤다. \"저건 램프 기름통이고 기름 깡통이라고. 기름이 다 떨어져서 동냥하러 온 거야.\"\n\n기름배가 고래잡이 어장에서 기름을 빌리러 다닌다는 것이 아무리 기이하게 보일지라도, 또 그것이 뉴캐슬에 석탄을 실어 나른다는 옛 속담을 거꾸로 뒤집는 일처럼 보일지라도, 실제로 이런 일이 더러 벌어진다. 그리고 지금 이 경우에 데리크 데 데어 선장은, 플래스크가 단언한 그대로, 의심할 여지 없이 램프 기름통을 들고 있었다.\n\n데리크가 갑판에 올라서자, 에이해브(Ahab)는 그가 손에 든 것에는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고 불쑥 말을 걸었다. 그러나 독일인은 그 서툰 사투리로 흰 고래(White Whale)에 대해서는 아는 바가 전혀 없음을 곧바로 드러냈고, 즉시 화제를 자기 램프 기름통과 기름 깡통으로 돌리면서, 브레멘 기름의 마지막 한 방울마저 떨어진 데다 부족분을 채워 줄 날치 한 마리조차 아직 잡지 못한 탓에 밤이면 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 해먹에 기어들어야 한다는 둥 몇 마디 늘어놓았다. 그러고는 자기 배가 어업 용어로 이른바 '깨끗한(clean)' 배(다시 말해 텅 빈 배)여서, 융프라우 곧 처녀라는 이름값을 톡톡히 한다는 말로 슬쩍 끝을 맺었다.\n\n필요한 것을 얻은 데리크는 떠나갔다. 그러나 그가 미처 자기 배 옆에 다다르기도 전에, 거의 동시에 두 배의 돛대 꼭대기에서 고래가 떴다는 소리가 올랐다. 데리크는 추격에 어찌나 안달이 났던지, 기름 깡통과 램프 기름통을 배에 올려놓을 새도 없이 보트를 홱 돌려, 그 거대한 리바이어던(Leviathan) 램프 기름통을 뒤쫓았다.\n\n자, 사냥감이 바람 불어가는 쪽에서 떠올랐던 터라, 그와 곧 뒤따른 다른 세 척의 독일 보트는 피쿼드호의 보트들보다 상당히 앞서 출발한 셈이었다. 고래는 모두 여덟 마리로, 평균적인 무리였다. 자기들이 처한 위험을 알아챈 고래들은 한 줄로 나란히 늘어서서 맹렬한 속도로 바람을 정면으로 받으며 곧장 나아갔는데, 마구를 채운 여러 마리의 말들이 그러하듯 서로 옆구리를 바싹 맞댄 채였다. 마치 바다 위로 거대하고 너른 양피지를 끊임없이 펼쳐 나가기라도 하듯, 그들은 크고 넓은 항적을 남겼다.\n\n그 빠른 항적 한복판에서, 여러 길이나 뒤처져, 등이 불룩 솟은 늙은 황소 한 마리가 헤엄치고 있었다. 그 비교적 느린 행보로 보나, 온몸을 뒤덮은 유난스레 누르스름한 딱지로 보나, 황달이나 다른 어떤 병에 시달리는 듯했다. 이 고래가 앞서가는 무리에 속한 것인지는 의심스러워 보였다. 그처럼 늙고 점잖은 리바이어던이 무리와 어울리는 일은 흔치 않기 때문이다. 그럼에도 그는 그들의 항적에 딱 붙어 따라갔는데, 사실 그들이 일으킨 후류(後流)가 그의 속도를 늦추었을 것이 분명했다. 그의 널찍한 주둥이 앞에 이는 흰 물보라가 두 개의 적대적인 해류가 부딪칠 때 생기는 너울처럼 부서지고 있었기 때문이다. 그의 물줄기는 짧고 느리며 힘겨웠다. 컥컥대며 솟구쳐 나오다가는 너덜너덜 찢긴 조각이 되어 흩어졌고, 뒤이어 그의 몸속에서 기이한 지하의 진동 같은 것이 일었는데, 그것은 그의 다른 쪽, 물속에 잠긴 끄트머리로 빠져나가는 듯하여 그의 뒤편 물이 보글보글 끓어오르게 만들었다.\n\n\"누구 진통제 가진 사람 없나?\" 스터브가 말했다. \"저 녀석, 배탈이 난 모양인데. 세상에, 반 에이커짜리 배탈을 한번 생각해 보라고! 역풍이 저놈 뱃속에서 미친 듯이 크리스마스 잔치를 벌이고 있구먼, 친구들. 고물 쪽에서 불어오는 역풍이라니, 내 평생 처음 보는 일일세. 그런데 보게, 고래가 저렇게까지 비틀거리며 가는 걸 누가 본 적 있나? 틀림없이 저놈 키를 잃어버린 게야.\"\n\n짐을 잔뜩 실은 동인도 무역선이 겁에 질린 말들을 갑판 가득 싣고 힌두스탄 해안을 따라 내려가며 기우뚱거리고, 파도에 파묻히고, 좌우로 흔들리고, 뒹굴며 나아가듯, 이 늙은 고래도 늙은 몸뚱이를 들썩이며 나아갔다. 그러다 이따금 그 육중한 갈비뼈 끄트머리 쪽으로 반쯤 몸을 뒤집으면, 그의 갈지자 항적의 원인이 드러났으니, 바로 부자연스럽게 잘려 나간 우현 지느러미였다. 그가 그 지느러미를 싸움에서 잃은 것인지, 아니면 날 때부터 없었던 것인지는 말하기 어려웠다.\n\n\"조금만 기다리게, 영감, 그 다친 팔에 내가 삼각건을 매어 줄 테니.\" 잔인한 플래스크가 곁에 놓인 고래 밧줄을 가리키며 외쳤다.\n\n\"저놈이 그 밧줄로 자네를 옭아매지 않게 조심하게.\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"노를 저어라, 안 그러면 독일인이 저놈을 차지하겠다.\"\n\n서로 경쟁하던 모든 보트가 한마음으로 이 한 마리의 고래를 겨냥했다. 그놈이 가장 클뿐더러 따라서 가장 값진 고래였기 때문만은 아니었다. 그놈이 그들과 가장 가까이 있었던 데다, 더구나 다른 고래들은 어찌나 빠른 속도로 달아나는지 당분간은 추격조차 거의 불가능했기 때문이다. 바로 이 순간 피쿼드호의 보트들은 마지막으로 내려진 세 척의 독일 보트를 앞질러 쏜살같이 나아갔다. 그러나 워낙 앞서 출발한 까닭에 데리크의 보트는 여전히 추격의 선두를 달리고 있었다. 비록 순간순간 외국인 경쟁자들이 바짝 따라붙고는 있었지만 말이다. 그들이 두려워하는 단 한 가지는, 데리크가 이미 사냥감에 너무 가까이 다가가 있어, 그들이 그를 완전히 따라잡고 앞지르기도 전에 작살을 던져 버릴지도 모른다는 점이었다. 데리크로 말하자면, 그는 분명 그렇게 되리라 자신만만한 듯, 이따금 조롱하는 몸짓으로 다른 보트들을 향해 자기 램프 기름통을 흔들어 댔다.\n\n\"저 무례하고 배은망덕한 개 같으니!\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"오 분도 채 안 돼서 내가 채워 준 바로 그 빈민 구호함을 들고 나를 조롱하고 도발하다니!\"—그러고는 예의 그 격렬한 속삭임으로—\"노를 저어라, 사냥개들아! 물고 늘어져!\"\n\n\"내가 말하지, 이보게들\"—스터브가 자기 보트의 선원들에게 외쳤다—\"화를 내는 건 내 신조에 어긋나는 일이지만, 저 악당 같은 독일 놈은 통째로 씹어 먹고 싶군—저어라—안 저을 텐가? 저 불한당한테 질 작정인가? 자네들 브랜디 좋아하지? 그럼 제일 잘하는 자에게 브랜디 한 통을 주겠네. 자, 어째서 너희 중 누구도 혈관 한 가닥 안 터뜨리는 거야? 누가 닻을 바다에 떨어뜨린 거야—우린 한 치도 안 나가잖아—바람 한 점 없이 멈춰 섰다고. 야아, 여기 보트 바닥에 풀이 다 자라네—맙소사, 저기 돛대엔 싹이 트는구먼. 이래선 안 되지, 친구들. 저 독일 놈을 보라고! 한마디로 말해, 이보게들, 자네들 불을 뿜을 텐가 말 텐가?\"\n\n\"오! 저놈이 일으키는 거품 좀 보게!\" 플래스크가 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰며 외쳤다—\"저 혹 좀 봐—오, 제발 힘 좀 더 줘 봐—통나무처럼 가만히 누워 있잖아! 오! 이보게들, 제발 좀 솟구쳐 보게—저녁에 팬케이크랑 대합 요리 먹어야지, 알잖나, 친구들—구운 조개랑 머핀 말이야—오, 제발, 제발 좀 솟구쳐 보라고,—저놈은 백 통짜리야—이제 와서 놓치지 마—놓치지 마, 오, 제발 놓치지 마!—저 독일 놈 좀 봐—오, 자네들 저녁 푸딩을 위해서라도 노를 안 저을 텐가, 이보게들—저렇게 묵직한 놈을! 저렇게 둔한 놈을! 자네들 향유고래(Sperm) 좋아하지 않나? 저기 삼천 달러가 가고 있다고, 친구들!—은행이야!—통째로 은행이라고! 잉글랜드 은행 말일세!—오, 제발, 제발, 제발 좀!—저 독일 놈은 지금 또 뭘 하는 거야?\"\n\n이 순간 데리크는 다가오는 보트들을 향해 자기 램프 기름통을 내던지려는 참이었고, 기름 깡통도 마찬가지였다. 아마도 경쟁자들의 속도를 늦추는 동시에, 뒤로 내던지는 순간적인 반동을 이용해 알뜰하게 제 보트의 속도를 높이려는 일석이조의 속셈이었을 것이다.\n\n\"저 버르장머리 없는 네덜란드 똥배 같으니!\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"자, 저어라, 이보게들, 빨강 머리 악마 오만 명을 실은 전열함처럼 저으란 말이다. 어떤가, 태시테고(Tashtego). 자네는 옛 게이헤드의 명예를 위해서라면 등뼈를 스물두 동강 낼 사내인가? 어떤가?\"\n\n\"내 말하지, 빌어먹게 저어 주겠어.\" 인디언이 외쳤다.\n\n독일인의 조롱에 사납게, 그러나 한결같이 자극받은 피쿼드호의 세 보트는 이제 거의 나란히 늘어서기 시작했고, 그렇게 진을 친 채 순간순간 그에게 다가갔다. 사냥감에 다가설 때면 으레 보이는 작살잡이 우두머리의 그 멋들어지고 느긋하며 기사다운 자세로, 세 항해사는 자랑스레 우뚝 일어섰다. 그러고는 이따금 신명 나는 고함으로 고물 쪽 노잡이의 기운을 북돋웠다. \"저기 미끄러져 간다, 이제! 백색 물푸레나무 바람 만세! 저 독일 놈을 내리눌러라! 저놈을 타고 넘어가라!\"\n\n그러나 데리크의 출발이 워낙 결정적으로 앞서 있었던 터라, 그들의 온갖 용맹에도 불구하고 그가 이 경주의 승자가 되었을 것이다. 만일 그의 중앙 노잡이의 노가 게걸음질에 걸려들어 정의로운 심판이 그에게 내려지지 않았더라면 말이다. 이 굼뜬 멍청이가 자기 물푸레나무 노를 빼내려고 안간힘을 쓰는 동안, 그리고 그 탓에 데리크의 보트가 뒤집힐 지경이 되어 그가 선원들에게 무서운 기세로 천둥같이 호통을 쳐 대는 동안—그것은 스타벅과 스터브와 플래스크에게는 절호의 기회였다. 함성과 함께 그들은 결사적으로 앞으로 치고 나가 비스듬히 독일인의 측면으로 따라붙었다. 다시 한순간, 네 척의 보트가 모두 고래의 바로 뒤 항적 속에 대각선으로 늘어섰고, 그들 양옆으로는 고래가 일으킨 거품 이는 너울이 길게 뻗어 나갔다.\n\n그것은 끔찍하고, 더없이 가엾으며, 미칠 듯한 광경이었다. 이제 고래는 머리를 내밀고 나아가며, 끊임없이 고통에 찬 물줄기를 제 앞으로 내뿜었다. 그러는 동안 그의 가엾은 한쪽 지느러미는 공포의 몸부림으로 옆구리를 두들겨 댔다. 이쪽으로, 또 저쪽으로, 그는 비틀거리는 도주 속에서 갈지자로 흔들렸고, 부서뜨리는 물결마다 발작하듯 바다 속으로 가라앉거나, 그 두들겨 대는 한쪽 지느러미를 하늘 쪽으로 옆으로 굴려 올렸다. 나는 날개가 잘린 새가 허공에서 겁에 질려 끊긴 원을 그리며, 약탈자 매들에게서 벗어나려 헛되이 애쓰는 모습을 본 적이 있다. 그러나 새에게는 목소리가 있어, 애처로운 울음으로 그 두려움을 알린다. 그러나 이 거대하고 말 못 하는 바다 짐승의 두려움은 그의 몸속에 사슬로 묶이고 마법에 걸려 갇혀 있었다. 분수공을 통해 컥컥대는 호흡을 내뱉는 것 말고는 그에게 목소리가 없었고, 그래서 그의 모습은 이루 말할 수 없이 가엾어 보였다. 그러면서도 그의 놀라운 거구와, 성문(城門) 같은 턱과, 전능한 꼬리에는, 그토록 그를 가엾이 여기는 가장 담대한 사내조차 질겁하게 할 만한 것이 충분히 남아 있었다.\n\n이제 몇 순간만 더 지나면 피쿼드호의 보트들에게 유리한 형세가 되리라는 것을 알아챈 데리크는, 이렇게 사냥감을 놓치느니, 마지막 기회가 영영 달아나기 전에 그에게는 유난히 멀게 느껴졌을 것이 분명한 작살 던지기를 한번 감행해 보기로 했다.\n\n그러나 그의 작살잡이가 한 방 던지려고 일어서기가 무섭게, 세 마리 호랑이—퀴케그(Queequeg), 태시테고, 다구—가 본능적으로 벌떡 일어나, 대각선으로 한 줄을 이루어 동시에 미늘을 겨누었다. 그리고 독일인 작살잡이의 머리 너머로, 그들의 낸터킷(Nantucket) 작살 셋이 고래에게 박혀 들어갔다. 눈을 멀게 하는 거품과 흰 불꽃의 안개! 고래가 곤두박질치듯 돌진하는 첫 격분의 순간, 세 보트는 독일인의 보트를 어찌나 세게 들이받았던지, 데리크와 헛물켠 그의 작살잡이는 모두 밖으로 튕겨 나가고, 날아가는 세 척의 보트가 그들 위를 타고 넘어갔다.\n\n\"겁먹지 마라, 내 버터통 친구들아.\" 스터브가 쏜살같이 지나가며 그들에게 흘깃 눈길을 던지고 외쳤다. \"곧 누가 건져 줄 거야—괜찮아—고물 쪽에 상어 몇 마리가 보이더군—세인트버나드 개들 알잖나—조난당한 나그네를 구해 주지. 만세! 이게 바로 항해하는 맛이지. 보트마다 햇살 한 줄기로구나! 만세!—미쳐 날뛰는 쿠거 꼬리에 매달린 양철 주전자 세 개처럼 우리가 간다! 이러니 평원에서 이륜마차를 타고 코끼리에 작살을 박은 일이 생각나는군—그런 식으로 매달리면 바퀴살이 휙휙 날아가지, 친구들. 게다가 언덕을 칠 때면 밖으로 내동댕이쳐질 위험도 있고. 만세! 이게 바로 데이비 존스한테 가는 사내의 기분이라고—끝없는 비탈을 곤두박질쳐 내려가는 거지! 만세! 이 고래가 영원의 우편물을 나르는구나!\"\n\n그러나 그 괴물의 질주는 짧았다. 갑자기 숨을 헐떡이더니, 그는 요란하게 잠수했다. 세 가닥의 밧줄이 삐거덕대며 로것헤드를 휘감고 어찌나 세게 돌아갔던지 거기에 깊은 홈이 패였다. 작살잡이들은 이 빠른 잠수가 곧 밧줄을 다 풀어내 버릴까 봐 어찌나 두려웠던지, 가진 솜씨와 힘을 다해 연거푸 밧줄을 로것헤드에 감아—연기가 날 만큼—붙들고 늘어졌다. 그리하여 마침내—보트의 납을 댄 밧줄받이에서 세 가닥 밧줄이 수직으로 곧장 푸른 바다 속으로 뻗어 내려가며 수직으로 잡아당기는 힘 때문에—뱃머리의 뱃전은 거의 수면과 나란해진 반면, 세 척의 고물은 허공 높이 치켜들렸다. 그러나 고래가 곧 잠수를 멈춘 덕에, 한동안 그들은 그 자세 그대로 머물렀다. 자세가 다소 아슬아슬하기는 했어도, 밧줄을 더 풀어 쓸까 두려웠던 것이다. 그러나 이런 식으로 보트가 끌려 내려가 잃은 일이 있다 해도, 이른바 이 '버티기'야말로, 곧 살아 있는 그의 등살에 날카로운 미늘을 박아 걸어 두는 이것이야말로, 흔히 리바이어던을 고통스럽게 하여 곧 다시 떠올라 적들의 날카로운 창을 마주하게 만드는 것이다. 그러나 그 일의 위험은 차치하더라도, 이 방법이 늘 최선인지는 의문이다. 작살에 찔린 고래가 물속에 오래 머물수록 더 지치리라고 보는 것이 이치에 맞기 때문이다. 그의 표면적이 엄청난 까닭에—다 자란 향유고래라면 2천 제곱피트(약 186제곱미터)에 조금 못 미친다—물의 압력이 어마어마하다. 우리는 우리 자신이 얼마나 놀라운 대기의 무게를 떠받치고 서 있는지 익히 알고 있다. 여기, 땅 위 공기 속에서조차 그러한데, 그렇다면 이백 길(약 366미터)에 달하는 바닷물 기둥을 등에 짊어진 고래의 부담은 얼마나 거대하겠는가! 그것은 적어도 오십 기압의 무게에 맞먹을 것이 분명하다. 어느 고래잡이는 그것을, 대포와 비축품과 승무원을 모두 실은 전열함 스무 척의 무게로 어림했다.\n\n세 척의 보트가 잔잔히 일렁이는 그 바다 위에 떠서, 그 영원한 푸른 정오의 심연을 내려다보고 있을 때, 그리고 그 깊은 곳에서 신음 한 자락, 비명 한 자락은커녕 잔물결 하나, 거품 한 방울조차 올라오지 않을 때, 그 모든 고요와 평온 아래에서 바다의 가장 거대한 괴물이 고통에 몸부림치며 뒤틀고 있으리라고 어느 뭍사람이 생각이나 했겠는가! 뱃머리에는 수직으로 드리운 밧줄이 여덟 인치(약 20센티미터)도 채 보이지 않았다. 그토록 가느다란 세 가닥 실에 저 거대한 리바이어던이 마치 팔일짜리 괘종시계의 묵직한 추처럼 매달려 있다는 것이 믿어지겠는가. 매달려 있다고? 그것도 무엇에? 널빤지 세 조각에. 이것이 한때 그토록 의기양양하게 일컬어진 그 피조물이란 말인가—\"네가 능히 미늘 박힌 작살로 그 가죽을 채우며, 물고기 창으로 그 머리를 채우겠느냐? 그를 치는 자의 칼도 소용없으며, 창이나 투창이나 갑옷도 마찬가지라. 그는 쇠를 지푸라기로 여기고, 화살로도 그를 달아나게 하지 못하며, 투창은 그루터기로 여기고, 창을 휘둘러도 그는 비웃느니라!\" 이것이 그 피조물이란 말인가? 이것이 그란 말인가? 오! 예언이 어찌하여 이렇듯 어긋나야만 하는가. 천 개의 넓적다리에 맞먹는 힘을 꼬리에 지닌 리바이어던이, 피쿼드호의 물고기 창을 피해 제 머리를 바닷속 산더미 아래로 처박았으니 말이다!\n\n그 비스듬히 기우는 오후의 햇살 속에서, 세 보트가 수면 아래로 드리운 그림자는 크세르크세스 군대의 절반을 그늘지게 할 만큼 길고도 넓었을 것이 분명하다. 그토록 거대한 유령들이 제 머리 위로 어른거리는 것이, 상처 입은 고래에게는 얼마나 끔찍하게 느껴졌을지 그 누가 알겠는가!\n\n\"준비해라, 친구들. 저놈이 움직인다.\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. 세 가닥 밧줄이 별안간 물속에서 부르르 떨더니, 마치 자기(磁氣)를 띤 전선처럼 고래의 삶과 죽음의 박동을 그들에게로 또렷이 전해 올렸고, 그리하여 노잡이들은 저마다 자기 자리에서 그것을 느꼈다. 다음 순간, 뱃머리를 끌어내리던 힘이 상당 부분 풀리면서 보트들은 별안간 위로 튀어 올랐다. 마치 빽빽이 모여 있던 흰곰 떼가 겁에 질려 바다로 뛰어들 때 작은 빙원(氷原)이 솟아오르듯이.\n\n\"끌어당겨라! 끌어당겨!\" 스타벅이 다시 외쳤다. \"저놈이 떠오른다.\"\n\n조금 전만 해도 한 뼘조차 당겨 들일 수 없었던 밧줄이, 이제는 길게 휘감기며 물을 뚝뚝 흘린 채 잽싸게 보트 안으로 던져 들여졌다. 그리고 곧 고래가 사냥꾼들로부터 배 길이로 두 척쯤 되는 거리 안에서 물을 가르며 솟아올랐다.\n\n그의 움직임은 그가 극도로 지쳐 있음을 또렷이 드러냈다. 대부분의 육지 동물에게는 여러 혈관에 일종의 판막, 곧 수문(水門)이 있어서, 상처를 입으면 적어도 어느 방향으로는 즉시 어느 정도 피가 막힌다. 그러나 고래는 그렇지 않다. 그의 특이한 점 가운데 하나가 바로 혈관이 통째로 판막이 없는 구조라는 것이어서, 작살처럼 그토록 작은 끝에 찔리기만 해도 그 즉시 온몸의 동맥계 전체로부터 치명적인 출혈이 시작된다. 게다가 수면 아래 한참 깊은 곳에서 받는 어마어마한 물의 압력이 여기에 더해지면, 그의 생명은 끊임없는 줄기가 되어 그에게서 쏟아져 나온다고 할 만하다. 그러나 그의 몸속에는 피의 양이 워낙 방대하고, 그 안의 샘들이 워낙 멀고 또 수없이 많은 까닭에, 그는 이렇게 피를 흘리고 또 흘리면서도 상당히 오랜 기간을 버틴다. 마치 가뭄 속에서도, 멀고 보이지 않는 언덕의 깊은 샘에 그 원천을 둔 강이 끊임없이 흐르듯이. 지금도, 보트들이 이 고래에게 노 저어 다가가, 좌우로 흔들리는 그의 꼬리지느러미 위로 위태롭게 끌어 올라가, 그에게 창을 꽂아 넣었을 때, 새로 낸 상처에서는 줄곧 일정한 핏줄기가 쉴 새 없이 뿜어 나왔다. 반면 그의 머리에 난 본래의 분수공은, 아무리 빠르다 한들 간헐적으로만 그 겁에 질린 물기를 허공으로 내뿜을 뿐이었다. 이 마지막 구멍에서는 아직 피가 나오지 않았으니, 그의 급소가 여태 어느 곳도 찔리지 않은 까닭이었다. 그들이 의미심장하게 일컫는 그의 '생명'은 아직 건드려지지 않았다.\n\n이제 보트들이 그를 더 바싹 둘러싸자, 평소에는 잠겨 있는 부분까지 포함해 그의 몸 윗부분 전체가 또렷이 드러났다. 그의 두 눈이, 아니 차라리 그의 두 눈이 있던 자리가 보였다. 가장 고귀한 떡갈나무라도 쓰러지고 나면 그 옹이구멍에 기이하게 잘못 자란 혹들이 모여들듯, 한때 고래의 두 눈이 차지했던 자리에서도 이제는 멀어 버린 둥근 혹들이 불거져 나와, 차마 볼 수 없을 만큼 가엾었다. 그러나 연민이라고는 없었다. 그 늙은 나이에도, 한쪽 팔뿐인 몸으로도, 멀어 버린 두 눈으로도, 그는 죽음을 맞고 살해당해야만 했다. 인간의 흥겨운 혼례와 그 밖의 온갖 잔치를 환히 밝히기 위해, 또한 너나없이 모두에게 절대적인 무해함을 설교하는 저 엄숙한 교회들을 비추기 위해서. 여전히 제 피 속에서 뒹굴던 그는, 마침내 옆구리 아래쪽에서 부셸 한 말 크기의, 기이하게 변색된 덩어리, 곧 혹 같은 것을 슬며시 드러냈다.\n\n\"좋은 자리로군.\" 플래스크가 외쳤다. \"저기 한 번만 찔러 보게 해 주시오.\"\n\n\"멈춰!\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"그럴 필요 없다!\"\n\n그러나 인정 많은 스타벅의 말은 너무 늦었다. 작살이 꽂히는 순간, 이 잔인한 상처에서 곪은 핏줄기가 솟구쳤고, 거기에 자극받아 참을 수 있는 한계를 넘는 고통에 빠진 고래는, 이제 걸쭉한 피를 내뿜으며 맹렬한 분노로 보트들을 향해 눈먼 채 돌진했다. 그리하여 보트들과 그 위에서 의기양양해하던 선원들을 온통 핏줄기로 흠뻑 적시고, 플래스크의 보트를 뒤집어엎으며 그 뱃머리를 망가뜨렸다. 그것이 그의 죽음의 일격이었다. 이때쯤 그는 피를 너무 많이 흘려 기진맥진한 터라, 제가 만든 잔해로부터 힘없이 뒹굴어 물러났다. 그러고는 옆으로 누워 헐떡이며, 잘린 지느러미를 무력하게 퍼덕이다가, 이지러져 가는 세계처럼 천천히 거듭거듭 돌아 뒤집히더니, 제 배의 하얀 비밀을 위로 뒤집어 드러낸 채, 통나무처럼 누워 죽었다. 그 마지막 숨이 다하는 물줄기는 더없이 가엾었다. 보이지 않는 손에 이끌려 어느 거대한 샘에서 물이 차츰 빠져나가며, 반쯤 막힌 듯한 구슬픈 콸콸 소리와 함께 그 물보라 기둥이 점점 더 낮게 땅으로 가라앉듯—고래의 그 마지막 길게 죽어 가는 물줄기도 그러했다.\n\n이윽고 선원들이 본선이 오기를 기다리는 동안, 그 몸뚱이는 보물을 하나도 약탈당하지 않은 채로 가라앉을 조짐을 보였다. 스타벅의 명에 따라 즉시 여러 지점에 밧줄을 묶었고, 그리하여 머지않아 보트마다 하나의 부표가 되었다. 가라앉은 고래는 밧줄에 매달려 그들 아래 몇 인치쯤 떨어진 곳에 떠 있었다. 본선이 다가오자 매우 신중한 조작 끝에 고래는 본선 옆구리로 옮겨졌고, 가장 튼튼한 꼬리지느러미 사슬로 단단히 묶였다. 인위적으로 떠받치지 않으면 그 몸뚱이가 당장 바닥으로 가라앉으리라는 것이 분명했기 때문이다.\n\n그런데 우연히도, 삽으로 그를 처음 가르기 시작하자마자, 앞서 말한 혹의 아래쪽 살 속에 부식된 작살 한 자루가 통째로 박혀 있는 것이 발견되었다. 그러나 잡힌 고래의 사체에서 작살 토막이 발견되는 일은 흔하고, 그 둘레의 살이 완전히 아물어 그 자리를 가리킬 만한 어떤 돌기도 없는 경우가 보통이다. 그러므로 지금 이 경우에는, 앞서 말한 궤양을 온전히 설명하려면 무언가 다른 알 수 없는 까닭이 있었음에 틀림없다. 그러나 더더욱 기이한 것은, 박혀 있던 그 작살에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 돌로 된 창끝 하나가 그의 몸속에서, 그 둘레의 살이 완전히 단단하게 아문 채로 발견되었다는 사실이다. 누가 그 돌창을 던졌을까? 그리고 언제? 어쩌면 그것은 아메리카가 발견되기 훨씬 전에 어느 북서부 인디언이 던졌는지도 모른다.\n\n이 괴물 같은 진열장에서 또 어떤 경이로운 것들이 들춰져 나왔을지는 알 길이 없다. 그러나 몸뚱이가 가라앉으려는 경향이 어마어마하게 커진 탓에 본선이 전에 없이 옆으로 끌려 바다 쪽으로 기울면서, 더 이상의 발견에는 돌연 제동이 걸렸다. 그러나 일을 지휘하던 스타벅은 끝까지 그것을 붙들고 늘어졌다. 어찌나 단호하게 매달렸던지, 마침내 그 몸뚱이와 팔짱을 낀 채로 계속 버텼다면 본선이 뒤집혔을 지경이었다. 그제야 그것을 끊어 내라는 명령이 떨어졌으나, 꼬리지느러미 사슬과 밧줄이 묶인 횡재(橫材) 머리에 걸린 장력이 어찌나 꿈쩍도 하지 않던지, 그것들을 풀어 던지기란 불가능했다. 그러는 사이 피쿼드호의 모든 것이 기울어 있었다. 갑판 반대편으로 건너가는 것은 가파른 박공지붕을 타고 올라가는 것이나 다름없었다. 배가 신음하고 헐떡였다. 부자연스럽게 어긋난 탓에, 뱃전과 선실에 상아로 박아 넣은 장식들이 여럿 제자리에서 튀어나왔다. 횡재 머리에서 꼬리지느러미 사슬을 비틀어 떼어 내려고 지렛대와 쇠지레를 가져다 댔으나 헛수고였다. 게다가 고래가 이제 너무 낮게 가라앉은 탓에 물에 잠긴 끄트머리에는 손도 댈 수 없었고, 그러는 매 순간 가라앉는 몸뚱이에는 통째로 몇 톤씩의 무게가 더해지는 듯하여, 배는 막 뒤집힐 듯 위태로웠다.\n\n\"붙어 있어라, 붙어 있어, 좀 그래 주겠나?\" 스터브가 그 몸뚱이를 향해 외쳤다. \"그렇게 미친 듯이 서둘러 가라앉지 좀 말라고! 천둥 맞을, 이보게들, 우린 뭐라도 하지 않으면 함께 끝장이야. 거기 지렛대질해 봐야 소용없어. 멈춰, 그 지렛대질 좀 그만두라니까. 그리고 누구든 하나 달려가서 기도서랑 주머니칼을 가져와, 저 큰 사슬을 끊어 버려.\"\n\n\"칼? 알았어, 알았다고.\" 퀴케그가 외치고는, 목수의 묵직한 손도끼를 움켜쥐고 현창 밖으로 몸을 내밀어, 쇠에 강철을 대고 가장 큰 꼬리지느러미 사슬을 후려치기 시작했다. 그러나 불꽃을 튀기며 몇 차례 내리쳤을 뿐인데, 어마어마한 장력이 나머지를 해치웠다. 무시무시한 소리와 함께 모든 결박이 끊겨 나갔고, 배는 똑바로 섰으며, 사체는 가라앉았다.\n\n자, 갓 죽인 향유고래가 이렇게 이따금 어쩔 수 없이 가라앉는 것은 매우 기이한 일이다. 어떤 고래잡이도 아직 그것을 제대로 설명하지 못했다. 보통 죽은 향유고래는 옆구리나 배를 수면 위로 상당히 들어 올린 채 큰 부력으로 떠 있다. 만일 이렇게 가라앉는 고래가 늙고 비쩍 마르고 가슴이 무너진 놈들뿐이어서, 비곗덩이는 줄어들고 뼈는 죄다 무겁고 류머티즘에 걸린 것들뿐이라면, 그런 식으로 가라앉는 물고기에게 부력을 지닌 물질이 없는 탓에 유난히 비중이 높아 가라앉는 것이라고 어느 정도 이치에 맞게 주장할 수 있을 것이다. 그러나 그렇지 않다. 더없이 건강하고 고귀한 포부로 부풀어 오른 젊은 고래들, 생애의 따뜻한 한창때이자 오월 같은 시절에 때 이르게 잘려 나간, 온몸에 팽팽한 비계를 두른 그놈들, 이 건장하고 부력 넘치는 영웅들조차도 더러 가라앉는다.\n\n그러나 향유고래는 다른 어떤 종(種)보다 이런 사고를 당할 가능성이 훨씬 적다는 점은 말해 두어야겠다. 그 종류 하나가 가라앉을 때, 참고래(Right Whale)는 스무 마리가 가라앉는다. 종에 따른 이 차이는 의심할 바 없이 적잖이 참고래의 뼈가 더 많은 데서 비롯된다. 참고래의 베네치아식 발(고래수염) 하나만 해도 때로 일 톤이 넘게 나가는데, 향유고래는 이런 짐에서 완전히 자유롭다. 그러나 여러 시간 혹은 며칠이 지난 뒤에, 가라앉았던 고래가 살아 있을 때보다 더 큰 부력으로 다시 떠오르는 경우도 있다. 그러나 그 까닭은 명백하다. 그의 몸속에서 가스가 생겨나, 그가 엄청난 크기로 부풀어 일종의 동물 풍선이 되는 것이다. 그때는 전열함으로도 그를 좀처럼 물밑에 눌러 두지 못한다. 뉴질랜드의 만(灣)들 사이, 측심줄이 닿는 연안 포경에서는, 참고래가 가라앉을 기미를 보이면 밧줄을 넉넉히 매단 부표를 그에게 묶어 둔다. 그리하여 몸뚱이가 가라앉아도, 그것이 다시 떠오를 때 어디서 찾아야 할지를 알아 두는 것이다.\n\n몸뚱이가 가라앉고 얼마 지나지 않아, 피쿼드호의 돛대 꼭대기에서 외침이 들려왔으니, 융프라우호가 다시 보트들을 내리고 있다는 소식이었다. 비록 눈에 보이는 물줄기라고는 핀백(Fin-Back) 한 마리의 것뿐이었지만 말이다. 핀백은 도무지 믿기 어려운 헤엄 실력 탓에 잡을 수 없는 고래 종류에 속한다. 그럼에도 핀백의 물줄기는 향유고래의 것과 어찌나 비슷한지, 솜씨 없는 고래잡이들은 흔히 그것을 향유고래로 착각한다. 그리하여 데리크와 그의 무리는 이제 이 다가갈 수 없는 짐승을 용감하게 쫓고 있었다. 처녀호는 돛을 잔뜩 펼치고 제 네 척의 어린 보트를 뒤따랐고, 그렇게 그들은 모두 바람 불어가는 쪽 저 멀리로 사라져 갔으니, 여전히 대담하고도 희망에 찬 추격이었다.\n\n오! 핀백은 많고도 많으며, 데리크 또한 많고도 많다네, 나의 벗이여.\n\n# CHAPTER 82. The Honor and Glory of Whaling.\n\n세상에는 세심한 무질서야말로 참된 방법이 되는 일들이 있다.\n\n이 고래잡이라는 문제 속으로 깊이 잠수해 들어가, 그 연구를 바로 그 원천의 샘머리에까지 밀고 올라갈수록, 나는 그 위대한 명예로움과 유구함에 더욱더 깊은 감명을 받는다. 더욱이 온갖 부류의 위대한 반신(半神)과 영웅들, 예언자들이 이런저런 방식으로 이 일에 영예를 더해 주었음을 알게 될 때면, 나 자신도 비록 말단에 지나지 않으나 그토록 찬란히 빛나는 형제단의 일원이라는 생각에 황홀해진다.\n\n용맹한 페르세우스, 곧 유피테르의 아들이야말로 최초의 고래잡이였다. 그리고 우리 천직의 영원한 명예를 위해 말해 두건대, 우리 형제단이 처음으로 공격한 고래는 그 어떤 비루한 욕심으로 죽인 것이 아니었다. 그때는 우리 직업의 기사도적인 시대였으니, 우리는 오로지 곤경에 빠진 이를 구하기 위해서만 무기를 들었을 뿐, 사람들의 등잔 기름통을 채우기 위해서가 아니었다. 페르세우스와 안드로메다의 아름다운 이야기는 누구나 다 안다. 어느 임금의 딸인 사랑스러운 안드로메다가 바닷가 바위에 묶여 있었고, 리바이어던이 막 그녀를 채어 가려는 바로 그 순간, 고래잡이들의 군주 페르세우스가 두려움 없이 나아가 그 괴물에게 작살을 꽂아, 처녀를 구하고 그녀와 혼인했다는 이야기 말이다. 그것은 감탄할 만한 예술적 위업이었으니, 오늘날 최고의 작살잡이들조차 좀처럼 이루지 못하는 일이다. 이 리바이어던은 단 한 번의 첫 투창에 목숨을 잃었기 때문이다. 그러니 이 방주(方舟)의 이야기를 어느 누구도 의심하지 말라. 시리아 해안의 옛 욥바, 곧 지금의 야파에 있는 어느 이교 신전 안에는 오랜 세월 동안 거대한 고래의 뼈대가 서 있었는데, 그 도시의 전설과 모든 주민들은 그것을 페르세우스가 죽인 바로 그 괴물의 뼈라고 주장했다. 로마인들이 욥바를 점령했을 때, 그 똑같은 뼈대는 개선의 전리품으로 이탈리아까지 옮겨졌다. 이 이야기에서 가장 기이하면서도 의미심장하게 중요해 보이는 점은 바로 이것이다. 요나(Jonah)가 배를 타고 떠난 곳이 바로 욥바였다는 사실이다.\n\n페르세우스와 안드로메다의 모험과 닮은—실로 어떤 이들은 그것에서 간접적으로 유래했다고 여기는—이야기가 저 유명한 성 게오르기우스와 용의 전설이다. 그런데 나는 그 용이 바로 고래였다고 주장한다. 수많은 옛 연대기에서 고래와 용은 기이하게 뒤섞여 있고, 흔히 서로를 대신해 등장하기 때문이다. \"너는 물의 사자 같고, 바다의 용 같도다.\" 에스겔이 이렇게 말했으니, 이는 분명 고래를 뜻하는 것이다. 사실 어떤 성경 판본들은 그 단어 자체를 쓰고 있다. 게다가, 성 게오르기우스가 깊은 바다의 거대한 괴물과 싸우는 대신 한낱 땅을 기는 파충류와 마주쳤을 뿐이라면, 그 위업의 영광은 크게 깎이고 말 것이다. 뱀이야 누구라도 죽일 수 있지만, 오직 페르세우스나 성 게오르기우스, 코핀(Coffin) 같은 자만이 고래를 향해 담대히 나아갈 배짱을 지니고 있다.\n\n이 장면을 그린 현대의 그림들에 우리가 속아서는 안 된다. 비록 그 옛날의 용맹한 고래잡이가 마주한 짐승이 그리핀 같은 형상으로 어렴풋이 그려져 있고, 그 싸움이 뭍에서 벌어지며 성인은 말 위에 올라타 있는 모습으로 묘사되어 있긴 하지만, 그 시대의 크나큰 무지를 헤아려 보면—그때는 화가들이 고래의 참된 모습을 알지 못했으니—그리고 페르세우스의 경우처럼 성 게오르기우스의 고래도 바다에서 기어올라 모래밭으로 올라왔을 수 있음을 헤아려 보면, 또한 성 게오르기우스가 올라탄 짐승이 그저 커다란 물범이나 해마였을 수 있음을 헤아려 보면, 이 모든 것을 마음에 새길 때, 이른바 그 용을 다름 아닌 위대한 리바이어던 그 자신으로 여기는 것이 저 성스러운 전설이나 그 장면을 그린 가장 오래된 그림들과 결코 어긋나지는 않을 것이다. 사실, 엄격하고 꿰뚫는 진실 앞에 세워 놓고 보면, 이 이야기 전체는 저 블레셋 사람들의 물고기이자 고기이자 새인 우상, 다곤이라는 이름의 그것과 같은 신세가 될 것이다. 그 다곤은 이스라엘의 방주 앞에 세워지자, 말 머리 같은 머리와 두 손바닥이 그 몸에서 떨어져 나가고, 다만 그루터기, 곧 물고기 같은 부분만이 남았던 것이다. 그러니 우리와 같은 고귀한 본보기를 지닌 한 사람, 바로 고래잡이가 잉글랜드의 수호 성인인 셈이다. 그리고 정당한 권리로 보건대, 우리 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 작살잡이들이야말로 가장 고귀한 성 게오르기우스 기사단에 등록되어야 마땅하다. 그러므로 그 명예로운 기사단의 기사들이여(감히 말하건대, 그들 중 누구도 자기네 위대한 수호 성인처럼 고래를 상대해 본 적이 없으리라), 부디 낸터킷 사람을 결코 경멸의 눈으로 바라보지 말라. 우리는 모직 작업복과 타르 칠한 바지 차림일지언정, 성 게오르기우스의 훈장을 받을 자격이 그들보다 훨씬 더 충분하기 때문이다.\n\n헤라클레스를 우리 무리에 들여야 할지 말지, 이에 관해서 나는 오랫동안 망설였다. 그리스 신화에 따르면 그 고대의 크로켓이자 키트 카슨, 곧 기쁨을 주는 선행을 행하던 그 억센 사나이가 고래에게 삼켜졌다가 다시 토해졌다고는 하지만, 그렇다고 그것이 엄밀히 그를 고래잡이로 만드는지는 논쟁의 여지가 있기 때문이다. 그가 실제로 자기 물고기에게 작살을 꽂았다는 이야기는 어디에도 나오지 않는다, 굳이 따지자면 뱃속에서 꽂은 것이 아니라면 말이다. 그럼에도 그는 일종의 본의 아닌 고래잡이로 여겨질 수 있겠다. 어쨌든 그가 고래를 잡지는 못했어도, 고래가 그를 잡기는 했으니까. 나는 그를 우리 일족의 하나로 주장하는 바이다.\n\n그러나 가장 권위 있되 서로 엇갈리는 견해들에 따르면, 헤라클레스와 고래에 관한 이 그리스 이야기는 그보다 더 오래된 히브리의 요나와 고래 이야기에서 유래했다고 여겨지며, 또 그 반대라고도 한다. 어쨌든 둘은 매우 흡사하다. 내가 그 반신을 우리 사람으로 주장한다면, 어찌 그 예언자라고 못 할 것인가?\n\n영웅과 성인, 반신과 예언자들만이 우리 기사단의 명부 전체를 이루는 것도 아니다. 우리의 대종장(大宗匠)은 아직 거명되지 않았으니, 옛 시대의 왕족 임금들처럼, 우리 형제단의 원류는 다름 아닌 위대한 신들 그 자체에서 비롯되기 때문이다. 이제 저 경이로운 동방의 이야기를 샤스터에서 되새겨 보아야겠다. 그것은 힌두교 신격의 세 위격 중 하나인 두려운 비슈누를 우리에게 전해 주며, 이 신성한 비슈누 그 자신을 우리의 주(主)로 내어 준다. 그 열 차례의 지상 화신 가운데 첫 번째 화신을 통해 고래를 영원히 따로 구별하여 거룩하게 한 비슈누 말이다. 샤스터가 이르기를, 브라흐마, 곧 신들의 신이 주기적인 소멸의 한 시기를 거친 뒤 세계를 다시 창조하기로 결심했을 때, 그 일을 주관하도록 비슈누를 낳았다고 한다. 그런데 베다, 곧 신비의 경전들—비슈누가 창조를 시작하기에 앞서 반드시 통독해야 했던 것으로 보이며, 따라서 젊은 건축가들에게 실용적인 지침 같은 무언가를 담고 있었음이 분명한 그 베다가 물 밑바닥에 가라앉아 있었다. 그리하여 비슈누는 고래의 몸으로 화신하여, 그 안에서 가장 깊은 심연까지 잠수해 내려가, 그 성스러운 경전들을 건져 올렸다. 그렇다면 이 비슈누야말로 고래잡이가 아니었던가? 말을 타는 사람을 마부라 부르듯이 말이다.\n\n페르세우스, 성 게오르기우스, 헤라클레스, 요나, 그리고 비슈누! 이것이야말로 그대들에게 보여 줄 회원 명부다! 고래잡이의 모임 말고 그 어떤 단체가 이렇게 앞장설 수 있단 말인가?\n\n# CHAPTER 83. Jonah Historically Regarded.\n\n앞 장에서 요나(Jonah)와 고래에 관한 역사적 이야기가 언급되었다. 그런데 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들 중에는 요나와 고래에 관한 이 역사적 이야기를 다소 미심쩍게 여기는 이들이 있다. 하지만 옛날에도 회의적인 그리스인과 로마인들이 있었으니, 그들은 당대의 정통 이교도들과 거리를 두면서, 마찬가지로 헤라클레스와 고래 이야기며 아리온과 돌고래 이야기를 의심했다. 그러나 그들이 그러한 전승을 의심했다고 해서, 그 전승들이 조금이라도 덜 사실이 되는 것은 결코 아니었다.\n\n새그하버 출신의 어느 늙은 고래잡이가 그 히브리 이야기를 의심한 주된 이유는 이러했다. 그는 진기하고 비과학적인 도판들로 꾸며진, 그 예스럽고 고풍스러운 성경 중 하나를 가지고 있었는데, 그중 한 도판에는 요나의 고래가 머리에 두 개의 물줄기 구멍을 가진 모습으로 그려져 있었던 것이다. 그런데 이 특징은 오로지 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 한 종류, 곧 참고래와 그 목(目)에 속하는 변종들에게만 들어맞는 것으로, 어부들 사이에서는 이 고래를 두고 \"1페니짜리 빵 한 덩이면 목이 막혀 버린다\"는 말이 떠돈다. 그만큼 그 목구멍이 몹시 작은 것이다. 그러나 이에 대해서는 젭 주교의 선견지명 있는 답변이 마련되어 있다. 주교가 넌지시 이르기를, 우리는 요나가 고래 뱃속에 묻혀 있었다고 여길 것이 아니라, 그 입의 어느 부분에 일시적으로 자리 잡고 있었다고 여기면 된다는 것이다. 이는 선량한 주교에게 충분히 그럴듯하게 들린다. 사실 참고래의 입은 휘스트 노름판 두어 개를 들여놓고도 모든 노름꾼을 편안히 앉힐 만하기 때문이다. 어쩌면 요나는 텅 빈 이빨 속에 몸을 숨겼을 수도 있겠으나, 다시 생각해 보면 참고래에게는 이빨이 없다.\n\n새그하버(그는 그런 이름으로 불렸다)가 이 예언자 문제에 믿음을 갖지 못한 또 다른 이유는, 갇혀 있던 그의 몸과 고래의 위액에 관한 어딘가 모호한 것이었다. 그러나 이 반론 역시 무너지고 마는데, 어느 독일 성서주해가가 추정하기를 요나는 분명 죽은 고래의 떠다니는 몸뚱이 속으로 피신했으리라는 것이다. 러시아 원정에서 프랑스 병사들이 죽은 말을 천막 삼아 그 속으로 기어들어 갔던 것과 마찬가지로 말이다. 게다가 대륙의 다른 주석가들이 헤아려 본 바로는, 요나가 욥바의 배에서 바다로 던져졌을 때 그는 곧장 가까이 있던 다른 배, 곧 고래를 뱃머리 장식으로 단 어떤 배로 피해 갔다는 것이다. 그리고 내가 덧붙이자면, 요즈음 어떤 배들이 \"상어\"니 \"갈매기\"니 \"독수리\"니 하는 이름으로 명명되듯, 어쩌면 그 배는 \"고래\"라고 불렸을지도 모른다. 또한 요나서에 언급된 고래란 그저 구명 도구, 곧 바람을 가득 채운 부대 자루를 뜻할 뿐이며, 위기에 처한 예언자가 헤엄쳐 그것에 다다라 물에 빠져 죽을 운명을 면했다고 주장한 학식 깊은 주해가들도 없지 않았다. 그러므로 가련한 새그하버는 사방에서 패한 듯이 보인다. 그러나 그에게는 믿음을 갖지 못한 또 다른 이유가 남아 있었다. 내 기억이 옳다면 그것은 이러했다. 요나는 지중해에서 고래에게 삼켜졌고, 사흘 뒤에 니네베에서 사흘 걸음 거리쯤 되는 어딘가에 토해졌다는 것이다. 그런데 니네베는 티그리스강 가의 도시로, 지중해 연안의 가장 가까운 지점에서 가로질러 가도 사흘 걸음보다 훨씬 더 먼 곳에 있다. 이를 어찌 설명한단 말인가?\n\n그러나 고래가 그토록 짧은 거리 안에서 예언자를 니네베 가까이 내려놓을 다른 길은 없었던 것일까? 있었다. 고래는 그를 희망봉을 돌아서 데려갔을 수도 있다. 그러나 지중해를 끝에서 끝까지 통과하고, 또다시 페르시아만과 홍해를 거슬러 오르는 항해는 차치하더라도, 그러한 가정은 사흘 안에 아프리카 전체를 완전히 일주하는 일을 수반하게 된다. 그뿐 아니라 니네베의 옛터 가까이 흐르는 티그리스강 물은 어떤 고래도 헤엄칠 수 없을 만큼 얕다는 사실은 말할 것도 없다. 게다가 요나가 그토록 이른 시대에 희망봉을 돌았다는 이 발상은, 그 위대한 곶의 발견이라는 영예를 그곳을 발견했다고 알려진 바르톨로뮤 디아스에게서 빼앗아, 근대 역사를 거짓말쟁이로 만들고 말 것이다.\n\n그러나 늙은 새그하버의 이 모든 어리석은 논변은 다만 이성에 대한 그의 어리석은 오만을 드러낼 뿐이었다. 그가 해와 바다에서 주워 모은 것 말고는 배운 것이 거의 없는 처지였음을 생각하면, 이는 그에게 더욱 비난받아 마땅한 일이었다. 다시 말하노니, 그것은 다만 그의 어리석고 불경한 오만과, 존경받는 성직자들에 대한 가증스럽고 악마 같은 반역을 보여 줄 따름이다. 어느 포르투갈 가톨릭 사제는 요나가 희망봉을 거쳐 니네베로 갔다는 바로 이 발상을 두고, 그 전체 기적을 한층 두드러지게 키워 주는 것이라며 내세웠던 것이다. 과연 그러했다. 게다가 오늘날까지도, 고도로 계몽된 터키인들은 요나의 역사적 이야기를 경건히 믿고 있다. 그리고 약 삼백 년 전, 옛 해리스의 항해기에 실린 어느 영국인 여행자는 요나를 기리어 세운 터키의 한 모스크를 이야기하는데, 그 모스크 안에는 기름 한 방울 없이도 타오르는 기적의 등불이 있었다고 한다.\n\n# CHAPTER 84. Pitchpoling.\n\n창고에 실린 마차의 바퀴축이 쉽고 빠르게 굴러가도록 기름을 칠하듯이, 거의 같은 목적으로 어떤 고래잡이들은 자기 보트에 비슷한 작업을 한다. 보트 바닥에 기름을 발라 매끄럽게 하는 것이다. 그리고 이런 처치가 해로울 게 없는 만큼, 적잖은 이점을 가져다줄 가능성도 부정할 수 없다. 기름과 물은 서로 어긋나는 성질이고, 기름은 미끄러운 물질이며, 노리는 바는 보트가 씩씩하게 미끄러져 나아가게 하는 것이기 때문이다. 퀴케그(Queequeg)는 자기 보트에 기름칠하는 것을 굳게 믿었는데, 독일 배 융프라우호가 사라진 지 얼마 지나지 않은 어느 날 아침, 그 일에 평소보다 더 공을 들였다. 뱃전 너머로 매달린 보트 바닥 밑으로 기어 들어가, 그 매끈한 기름을 문질러 발랐으니, 마치 그 배의 대머리 같은 용골에서 한바탕 털이 돋아나기를 부지런히 바라기라도 하는 듯했다. 그는 어떤 특별한 예감에 따라 움직이는 듯했다. 그리고 그 예감은 뒤이은 사건으로 인해 근거 없는 것이 되지 않았다.\n\n정오 무렵 고래들이 발견되었다. 그러나 배가 그쪽으로 내려가자마자, 고래들은 몸을 돌려 쏜살같이 황급히 달아났으니, 악티움에서 도망친 클레오파트라의 거룻배들처럼 어지러운 도주였다.\n\n그럼에도 보트들은 추격에 나섰고, 스터브(Stubb)의 보트가 선두였다. 엄청난 노력 끝에 마침내 태시테고(Tashtego)가 작살 하나를 박는 데 성공했다. 그러나 작살을 맞은 고래는 잠수할 기색도 없이, 오히려 한층 빨라진 속도로 수평으로 계속 달아났다. 박힌 작살에 이처럼 쉼 없는 압력이 가해지면 머지않아 그것은 어쩔 수 없이 빠져나오고 만다. 달아나는 고래에 창을 박든지, 아니면 놓치기를 감수하든지 둘 중 하나를 선택해야만 했다. 그러나 보트를 그 옆구리까지 끌어붙이는 일은 불가능했으니, 고래가 너무도 빠르고 맹렬하게 헤엄쳤기 때문이다. 그러면 무엇이 남아 있는가?\n\n노련한 고래잡이가 그토록 자주 어쩔 수 없이 동원하게 되는 온갖 경이로운 술책과 솜씨, 손재간과 헤아릴 수 없는 묘기들 가운데, 작살로 부리는 저 절묘한 기교, 곧 피치폴링(pitchpoling)에 견줄 만한 것은 없다. 가는 검이든 넓은 검이든 그 어떤 검술도 이에 비할 것을 자랑하지 못한다. 이것은 끈질기게 달아나는 고래를 상대할 때에만 꼭 필요한 기술이다. 그 위대한 핵심이자 특징은, 극도의 속력으로 거칠게 흔들리고 요동치는 보트에서 긴 창을 정확하게 던져 보내는 그 놀라운 거리에 있다. 강철과 나무를 다 합쳐 그 창 전체의 길이는 열 자 내지 열두 자(약 3미터에서 3.6미터)쯤 되고, 자루는 작살 자루보다 훨씬 가늘며 재질도 더 가벼운 소나무다. 거기에는 워프(warp)라 불리는 가느다란 밧줄이 상당한 길이로 달려 있어, 창을 던진 뒤에도 그 줄을 잡아당겨 손으로 되돌릴 수 있게 되어 있다.\n\n그러나 더 나아가기 전에 여기서 짚어 둘 중요한 사실이 있다. 작살도 창과 같은 방식으로 피치폴링할 수 있기는 하지만, 그런 일은 좀처럼 행해지지 않으며, 행해진다 해도 성공하는 일은 더욱 드물다. 작살은 창에 비해 무게가 더 나가고 길이는 더 짧아서, 사실상 그것이 심각한 약점이 되기 때문이다. 그러므로 일반적으로는 어떤 피치폴링이든 작용하기에 앞서, 먼저 고래에 작살을 박아 단단히 매여 있어야 한다.\n\n이제 스터브를 보라. 더없이 위급한 순간에도 발휘되는 그 유머러스하고 침착한 냉정함과 평정심으로 인해, 그는 피치폴링에 특히나 뛰어날 자격을 갖춘 사람이었다. 그를 보라. 그는 질주하는 보트의 출렁이는 이물에 꼿꼿이 서 있다. 양털 같은 거품에 휩싸인 채, 보트를 끌고 가는 고래는 마흔 자(약 12미터) 앞에 있다. 긴 창을 가볍게 다루며, 그것이 정확히 곧은지 보려고 그 길이를 따라 두세 번 눈길을 굴린 뒤, 스터브는 휘파람을 불며 워프 밧줄 사리를 한 손으로 거두어, 그 자유로운 끝을 손아귀에 단단히 쥐고 나머지는 거치적거리지 않도록 둔다. 그런 다음 창을 허리띠 한가운데 정면에 받쳐 들고 고래를 겨눈다. 창으로 고래를 조준한 채, 손에 쥔 자루 끝을 차분히 내리누르면, 그만큼 창끝이 들려 올라가, 마침내 그 무기가 그의 손바닥 위에 제대로 균형을 잡고 허공 열다섯 자(약 4.5미터) 높이로 곧추선다. 그 모습은 어쩐지 긴 막대를 턱 위에 세워 균형 잡는 곡예사를 떠올리게 한다. 다음 순간, 재빠르고 형언할 수 없는 충동으로, 빛나는 강철이 장엄하고 높은 포물선을 그리며 거품 이는 거리를 가로질러, 고래의 급소에 부르르 떨며 박힌다. 이제 고래는 반짝이는 물 대신 붉은 피를 뿜어 올린다.\n\n\"저놈한테서 마개를 뽑아냈군!\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"오늘은 영원불멸의 칠월 나흘, 독립기념일이다! 오늘은 모든 샘이 포도주를 쏟아 내야 마땅하지! 저게 묵은 올리언스 위스키나 묵은 오하이오 술, 아니면 말로 다 못할 묵은 모논가헬라 위스키라면 얼마나 좋을꼬! 그럼 태시테고, 이 사람아, 자네가 저 물줄기 밑에 깡통을 받쳐 들면, 우리 모두 빙 둘러앉아 한잔씩 들이켤 텐데! 그래, 정말이지, 이 멀쩡히 살아 있는 친구들아, 저놈 숨구멍이 펼쳐 놓은 자리에 최상급 펀치를 빚어, 저 살아 있는 펀치 그릇에서 살아 있는 술을 들이켜 보자고.\"\n\n이런 흥겨운 입담과 더불어 그 능란한 던지기가 거듭되고 또 거듭되니, 창은 솜씨 좋은 끈에 매인 그레이하운드처럼 제 주인에게로 돌아온다. 고통에 몸부림치던 고래가 마지막 발악에 들어간다. 예인줄이 느슨해지고, 피치폴링을 하던 자는 고물 쪽으로 물러나 두 손을 모은 채, 말없이 그 괴물이 죽어 가는 모습을 지켜본다.\n\n# CHAPTER 85. The Fountain.\n\n육천 년 동안 — 그리고 그 이전 몇백만 시대였는지는 아무도 모른다 — 거대한 고래들이 온 바다를 누비며 물을 뿜어 댔고, 마치 수많은 물뿌리개나 물안개통이 그러하듯 심해의 정원에 물을 뿌리고 안개를 드리워 왔다. 그리고 지난 몇 세기 동안 수천 명의 사냥꾼들이 고래의 그 분수 가까이에서 이 흩뿌림과 물뿜음을 지켜보아 왔다. 이 모든 일이 다 있었음에도, 이 복된 순간(서기 1851년 12월 16일 오후 1시 15분 15초)에 이르러서까지도, 이 물뿜음이 과연 정말로 물인지 아니면 그저 수증기에 지나지 않는지가 여전히 풀리지 않은 문제로 남아 있다니 — 이는 분명 주목할 만한 일이다.\n\n그러니 이제 이 문제를, 거기에 딸린 몇 가지 흥미로운 사항들과 더불어 살펴보기로 하자. 누구나 알다시피, 지느러미 달린 종족들은 일반적으로 그들 아가미 특유의 교묘한 구조 덕분에 그들이 헤엄치는 원소 속에 늘 섞여 있는 공기를 호흡한다. 그래서 청어나 대구는 백 년을 살면서도 단 한 번 수면 위로 머리를 내밀지 않을 수 있다. 그러나 고래는 인간의 것과 같은 정식 허파를 갖춘 그 독특한 내부 구조 때문에, 오직 탁 트인 대기 속의 유리된 공기를 들이마셔야만 살 수 있다. 그렇기에 고래는 주기적으로 윗세상을 찾아가야 할 필요가 있는 것이다. 그러나 고래는 결코 입으로는 조금도 숨을 쉴 수 없으니, 평상시 자세에서 향유고래의 입은 적어도 수면 아래 여덟 자(약 2.4미터)에 잠겨 있기 때문이다. 게다가 그 기관(氣管)은 입과 전혀 연결되어 있지 않다. 아니, 고래는 오로지 분수공(噴水孔)으로만 숨을 쉬며, 이것은 그 머리 꼭대기에 있다.\n\n어떤 생물에게든 호흡이란 생명을 유지하는 데 없어서는 안 될 기능에 지나지 않는다고 내가 말한다면 — 호흡이 공기에서 어떤 특정한 성분을 끌어내고, 그것이 나중에 피와 접촉하여 피에 그 생명을 살리는 원리를 부여하기 때문인데 — 나는 틀린 말을 하고 있는 것은 아니라 생각한다. 비록 다소 군더더기 같은 과학 용어를 쓰는 셈일지는 몰라도 말이다. 이를 받아들인다면, 만일 사람의 온몸에 흐르는 피를 단 한 번의 호흡으로 모조리 산소화할 수 있다면, 그는 콧구멍을 막아 버린 채 상당한 시간 동안 다시 숨을 들이쉬지 않아도 되리라는 결론이 따라 나온다. 다시 말해, 그는 숨을 쉬지 않고도 살 수 있다는 것이다. 변칙처럼 보일지 모르나, 이것이 바로 고래의 경우다. 고래는 (바닥에 있을 때) 일정한 간격을 두고 꼬박 한 시간 이상씩을, 단 한 번도 숨을 들이쉬지 않고, 아니 어떤 식으로든 공기 한 점 들이마시지 않고 규칙적으로 살아간다. 기억하라, 고래에게는 아가미가 없다. 어떻게 이런 일이 가능한가? 고래의 갈비뼈 사이와 척추 양옆에는 마치 크레타의 미궁처럼 얽히고설킨, 가느다란 국수 가닥 같은 혈관들이 갖추어져 있는데, 이 혈관들은 고래가 수면을 떠날 때 산소가 가득 실린 피로 완전히 부풀어 오른다. 그리하여 한 시간 남짓, 바다 속 천 길 깊은 곳에서, 고래는 제 몸속에 여분의 생명력을 비축해 다닌다. 마치 물 없는 사막을 건너는 낙타가 훗날 쓰려고 네 개의 보조 위장에 여분의 마실 물을 채워 다니는 것과 똑같이. 이 미궁이 해부학적 사실이라는 점은 반박의 여지가 없다. 그리고 그것을 바탕으로 한 추정이 합리적이고 참되다는 것은, 어부들의 표현대로 저 리바이어던이 \"물뿜기를 다 마치려는\" 그 달리 설명할 길 없는 고집을 내가 곰곰이 생각해 볼 때, 한층 더 설득력 있게 다가온다. 내 말은 이런 뜻이다. 향유고래는 방해받지 않은 채 수면으로 떠오르면, 방해받지 않았던 다른 모든 떠오름과 정확히 똑같은 시간 동안 거기 머무른다. 가령 그가 11분을 머무르며 일흔 번을 뿜는다고, 곧 일흔 번을 호흡한다고 치자. 그러면 다음번에 다시 떠오를 때에도, 그는 어김없이 분(分) 단위까지 정확하게 다시 일흔 번의 호흡을 채우고야 만다. 그런데 만일 그가 몇 번 숨을 들이쉰 뒤에 그대가 놀라게 하여 그가 잠수해 버린다면, 그는 늘 다시 슬쩍 떠올라 제 규칙적인 공기 할당량을 다 채우고야 만다. 그리하여 그 일흔 번의 호흡을 다 셀 때까지는, 결코 마지막으로 내려가 아래에서 제 온전한 시간을 채우려 하지 않는다. 다만 유의할 것은, 개체마다 이 횟수가 서로 다르다는 점이다. 그러나 같은 개체에서는 늘 한결같다. 그렇다면 고래는 영영 내려가기에 앞서 제 공기 저장고를 다시 채우려는 것이 아니라면, 어찌하여 이토록 물뿜기를 다 마치겠다고 고집하겠는가? 또한 고래가 떠올라야 하는 이 필연성이, 그를 추격의 온갖 치명적 위험에 노출시킨다는 사실이 얼마나 명백한가. 햇빛 아래 천 길 깊은 곳을 헤엄칠 때라면, 이 거대한 리바이어던을 그 어떤 갈고리로도 그물로도 잡을 수 없을 테니 말이다. 그러니 오, 사냥꾼이여, 그대에게 승리를 안겨 주는 것은 그대의 기량이라기보다, 저 위대한 필연성인 것이다!\n\n인간에게 호흡은 끊임없이 이어진다 — 한 번의 호흡이 겨우 두세 번의 맥박에만 쓰이니, 깨어 있든 잠들어 있든 어떤 다른 일에 매달려 있든 인간은 반드시 숨을 쉬어야 하며, 그러지 않으면 죽고 만다. 그러나 향유고래는 제 시간의 겨우 칠분의 일, 곧 안식일에 해당하는 동안만 숨을 쉰다.\n\n고래는 오직 분수공으로만 숨을 쉰다고 앞서 말한 바 있다. 만약 거기에 그의 물뿜음이 물과 섞여 있다는 말을 사실대로 덧붙일 수 있다면, 그렇다면 나는 어째서 고래에게 후각이라는 감각이 지워져 버린 듯한지 그 이유를 알게 되리라 본다. 고래에게서 코에 조금이라도 해당하는 유일한 기관이 바로 그 분수공인데, 그것이 두 가지 원소로 그처럼 꽉 막혀 있으니 냄새 맡는 능력을 갖추리라 기대할 수는 없는 노릇이기 때문이다. 그러나 그 물뿜음이 물인지 수증기인지 하는 그 수수께끼 때문에, 이 점에 관해서는 아직 절대적인 확신에 이를 수 없다. 그럼에도 분명한 것은, 향유고래에게는 제대로 된 후각 기관이 없다는 사실이다. 하지만 그것이 고래에게 무슨 소용이 있겠는가? 바다에는 장미도, 제비꽃도, 오드콜로뉴도 없으니 말이다.\n\n더욱이 고래의 기관은 오직 그 물뿜는 관(管)으로만 통하며, 그 기다란 관은 — 저 웅장한 이리 운하처럼 — 공기를 아래로 가두거나 물을 위로 막아 내기 위한 일종의 수문(여닫이로 된)을 갖추고 있다. 그렇기에 고래에게는 목소리가 없다. 다만 고래가 그토록 기이하게 우르릉거릴 때 코로 말을 한다고 하여 그를 모욕하려는 것이 아니라면 말이다. 하지만 그렇다 한들, 고래가 무슨 할 말이 있겠는가? 내가 아는 한, 깊이 있는 존재치고 이 세상에 무언가 할 말이 있는 자는 드물었으니, 다만 입에 풀칠이라도 하려고 더듬더듬 무언가를 억지로 내뱉지 않는 한에는 그러했다. 오! 세상이 이토록 훌륭한 청자(聽者)라니 얼마나 다행한 일인가!\n\n자, 향유고래의 물뿜는 관은 주로 공기를 실어 나르기 위한 것으로, 머리 윗면 바로 아래에 한쪽으로 약간 치우쳐 몇 자 길이로 수평으로 놓여 있다. 이 기묘한 관은 도시의 길 한쪽에 묻어 놓은 가스관과 자못 닮았다. 그러나 다시 의문이 되돌아온다. 이 가스관이 수도관이기도 한가? 다시 말해, 향유고래의 물뿜음은 내쉰 숨의 한낱 수증기일 뿐인가, 아니면 그 내쉰 숨에 입으로 빨아들인 물이 섞여 분수공으로 뿜어 나오는 것인가? 입이 물뿜는 관과 간접적으로 통한다는 것은 분명하다. 그러나 이것이 분수공으로 물을 내보내기 위함이라고는 증명할 수 없다. 왜냐하면 그렇게 할 가장 큰 필요는, 먹이를 먹다가 어쩌다 물을 함께 들이켰을 때일 텐데, 향유고래의 먹이는 수면 한참 아래에 있고, 거기서는 설령 뿜고 싶어도 뿜을 수가 없기 때문이다. 게다가 고래를 아주 면밀히 관찰하며 시계로 시간을 재 보면, 방해받지 않을 때 그 물줄기를 뿜는 간격과 평상시 호흡하는 간격 사이에는 한 치도 어긋나지 않는 운율이 있음을 알게 될 것이다.\n\n그런데 어찌하여 이 주제에 관한 온갖 논증으로 사람을 들볶는단 말인가? 솔직히 말해 보라! 그대는 고래가 물 뿜는 것을 보았으니, 그렇다면 그 물뿜음이 무엇인지 단언해 보라. 물과 공기쯤이야 구별할 수 있지 않은가? 친애하는 선생이여, 이 세상에서 이런 빤한 것들을 가려내기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니라오. 나는 늘 빤한 것이야말로 가장 풀기 어려운 매듭임을 깨닫곤 했소. 그리고 이 고래의 물뿜음으로 말하자면, 그대가 그 안에 거의 들어서 있다 한들, 그것이 정확히 무엇인지는 여전히 가늠하지 못할 것이오.\n\n그 물뿜음의 중심부는 그것을 에워싼 눈처럼 희고 반짝이는 안개 속에 가려져 있다. 그러니 그대가 거기서 정말로 물방울이 떨어지는지 어찌 확실히 가려낼 수 있겠는가. 고래의 물뿜음을 가까이서 볼 만큼 그에게 다가갈 즈음이면, 고래는 늘 어마어마한 소동 속에 있어 물이 그 둘레로 폭포처럼 쏟아지고 있을 테니 말이다. 또한 그런 순간에 그대가 정말로 물뿜음 속에서 물기 어린 방울들을 보았다고 여긴다 한들, 그것들이 한낱 그 수증기에서 응결된 것이 아니라고 어찌 알 수 있겠는가. 또 그것들이, 고래 머리 꼭대기에 파묻혀 들어간 그 분수공 틈새에 표면적으로 고여 있던 바로 그 물방울들이 아니라고 어찌 알 수 있겠는가. 잔잔한 한낮의 바다를 평온히 헤엄치며, 사막의 단봉낙타 등처럼 솟아오른 혹이 햇볕에 바싹 말라 있을 때조차, 고래는 늘 제 머리 위에 작은 물웅덩이 하나를 이고 다닌다. 마치 이글거리는 햇볕 아래 바위에 팬 구멍 하나가 빗물로 가득 차 있는 것을 그대가 이따금 보듯이.\n\n또한 사냥꾼이 고래 물뿜음의 정확한 성질에 관해 지나치게 호기심을 부리는 것은 결코 현명한 짓이 못 된다. 그가 그 속을 들여다보거나 거기에 얼굴을 들이미는 짓은 해서는 안 된다. 그대는 물병을 들고 이 분수로 가서 그것을 채워 떠 올 수가 없다. 흔히 일어나는 일이지만, 그 물줄기의 바깥쪽 자욱한 안개 자락에 조금만 닿아도, 그토록 닿은 물질의 독한 기운 때문에 그대의 살갗은 화끈거리며 쓰라릴 것이기 때문이다. 내가 아는 어떤 이는 그 물뿜음에 한층 더 가까이 닿았는데 — 무슨 과학적 목적이 있었는지 아니면 다른 까닭이었는지는 나도 알 수 없으나 — 그의 뺨과 팔에서 살갗이 벗겨져 버렸다. 그렇기에 고래잡이들 사이에서 그 물뿜음은 독성이 있다고 여겨지며, 그들은 그것을 피하려 애쓴다. 또 한 가지, 그 물줄기가 정통으로 그대의 두 눈에 뿜어지면 눈이 멀 수도 있다는 말을 나는 들은 적이 있는데, 나는 그 말을 그리 의심하지 않는다. 그러니 탐구자가 할 수 있는 가장 현명한 일은, 내 보기에는, 이 치명적인 물뿜음을 그냥 내버려 두는 것이리라.\n\n그래도 우리는 증명하고 확립하지는 못할지언정 가설을 세워 볼 수는 있다. 내 가설은 이러하다. 그 물뿜음은 안개에 지나지 않는다는 것이다. 그리고 다른 여러 이유 외에도, 나는 향유고래에게 깃든 그 크나큰 본연의 존엄과 숭고함에 관한 고려에서 이 결론으로 이끌린다. 나는 그를 흔하고 천박한 존재로 여기지 않으니, 그가 결코 측심(測深) 가능한 얕은 물이나 해안 가까이에서는 발견되지 않는다는 것이 반박의 여지 없는 사실이기 때문이다. 다른 모든 고래들은 더러 그런 곳에 나타나기도 하건만 말이다. 그는 묵직하고도 심오하다. 그리고 나는 확신하건대, 플라톤이든 피론이든 악마든 유피테르든 단테든, 그 밖에 묵직하고 심오한 모든 존재들의 머리에서는, 깊은 사색에 잠겨 있는 동안 늘 어떤 반쯤 보이는 듯한 김이 피어오른다. 「영원에 관하여」라는 작은 논문을 짓는 동안, 나는 호기심에 거울 하나를 내 앞에 놓아 보았다. 그러자 머지않아 내 머리 위 대기 속에서 기묘하게 얽히며 꿈틀거리고 일렁이는 무언가가 거기 비치는 것을 보았다. 팔월 한낮, 얇은 널빤지를 댄 다락방에서 뜨거운 차를 여섯 잔 들이켜고 깊은 사색에 잠겨 있을 때 내 머리카락이 어김없이 축축해지던 것 — 이 또한 앞의 추정을 뒷받침하는 또 하나의 논거인 듯하다.\n\n그리고 저 거대하고 안개 자욱한 괴물이, 잔잔한 열대의 바다를 엄숙히 항해해 가는 모습을 바라볼 때, 그것이 얼마나 고귀하게 우리의 상상을 드높여 주는가. 그의 광대하고 온화한 머리 위로는, 누구에게도 전할 수 없는 그 사색이 빚어낸 수증기의 차양이 드리워 있고, 그 수증기는 — 그대가 이따금 보게 되듯 — 무지개로 영광스럽게 빛나니, 마치 하늘이 친히 그의 생각 위에 옥새를 찍어 놓은 듯하다. 보라, 무지개는 맑은 대기에는 깃들지 않는다. 무지개는 오직 수증기만을 환히 비춰 줄 뿐이다. 그리하여 내 마음속 어슴푸레한 의심의 그 두꺼운 안개를 뚫고, 신성한 직관이 이따금 화살처럼 쏘아 들어와, 내 안개를 천상의 빛줄기로 환히 밝힌다. 이 일로 나는 하느님께 감사드린다. 누구에게나 의심은 있고, 많은 이가 부정하지만, 의심하거나 부정하면서 그와 더불어 직관까지 지닌 이는 드물기 때문이다. 이 땅의 온갖 것들에 대한 의심과, 천상의 어떤 것들에 대한 직관 — 이 둘의 결합은 신자도 불신자도 만들어 내지 않으며, 다만 그 둘을 한결같은 눈으로 바라보는 사람을 만들어 낸다.\n\n# CHAPTER 86. The Tail.\n\n다른 시인들은 영양의 부드러운 눈동자와 결코 내려앉지 않는 새의 사랑스러운 깃털을 노래했지만, 나는 그보다 덜 천상적인 것, 곧 꼬리를 기린다.\n\n가장 큰 향유고래의 꼬리가, 몸통이 가늘어져 사람의 둘레쯤 되는 그 지점에서 시작된다고 본다면, 그 윗면만으로도 적어도 50제곱피트(약 4.6제곱미터)의 넓이를 차지한다. 그 뿌리의 단단하고 둥근 몸체는 넓고 단단하며 평평한 두 개의 손바닥, 곧 갈래꼬리로 펼쳐지면서, 차츰 얕아져 1인치(약 2.5센티미터)도 안 되는 두께까지 가늘어진다. 그 갈래지는 부분, 곧 접합부에서 이 갈래꼬리는 살짝 겹쳐졌다가, 다시 날개처럼 양옆으로 멀어지며 그 사이에 넓은 빈 공간을 남긴다. 살아 있는 그 어떤 것에서도 이 갈래꼬리의 초승달 모양 가장자리만큼 아름다운 선이 정교하게 그려진 것은 없다. 다 자란 고래에서 그 꼬리가 최대로 펼쳐지면, 폭이 20피트(약 6미터)를 훌쩍 넘는다.\n\n이 기관 전체는 용접된 힘줄들이 빽빽하게 짜여 이룬 받침처럼 보이지만, 잘라 보면 세 개의 뚜렷한 층으로 이루어져 있음을 알게 된다 — 상층, 중층, 하층이다. 상층과 하층의 섬유는 길고 수평으로 뻗어 있으며, 중층의 섬유는 아주 짧고 바깥쪽 두 층 사이를 가로질러 달린다. 이 삼위일체의 구조야말로 무엇보다도 꼬리에 힘을 부여한다. 고대 로마의 성벽을 연구하는 이라면, 이 중층이 저 경이로운 고대 유적들에서 언제나 돌과 번갈아 끼워진 얇은 벽돌 층과 묘하게 닮았음을 발견할 것이며, 그 벽돌 층이 석조 구조물의 막대한 견고함에 크게 기여한다는 사실은 의심할 여지가 없다.\n\n그러나 힘줄로 이루어진 꼬리의 이 거대한 국부적 힘만으로는 부족하다는 듯, 리바이어던의 온몸은 근육 섬유와 가닥들의 날실과 씨실로 뜨개질되어 있는데, 이것들이 허리 양옆을 지나 갈래꼬리 속으로 흘러들어, 어느새 그것들과 한데 섞이며 그 위력에 크게 보탬을 준다. 그리하여 꼬리에는 고래 전체의 합류된 가늠할 수 없는 힘이 한 점으로 응축된 듯하다. 물질에 소멸이 일어날 수 있다면, 바로 이것이 그 일을 해낼 물건이리라.\n\n게다가 이 놀라운 힘은 결코 그 움직임의 우아한 굴신을 조금도 손상시키지 않으니, 그 안에서는 어린아이 같은 편안함이 거인적 힘을 헤치며 물결친다. 오히려 그 움직임은 바로 그 힘에서 가장 두려운 아름다움을 얻는다. 참된 힘은 결코 아름다움이나 조화를 해치지 않으며, 도리어 그것을 부여하는 일이 잦다. 그리고 위풍당당하게 아름다운 모든 것에서, 힘은 그 마력과 깊은 관계가 있다. 조각된 헤라클레스 상의 대리석에서 사방으로 터져 나올 듯한 동여매인 힘줄들을 걷어 내 보라, 그러면 그 매력은 사라지고 말 것이다. 경건한 에커만이 괴테(Goethe)의 벌거벗은 주검에서 아마포 천을 걷어 올렸을 때, 그는 로마의 개선문처럼 보이는 그 사내의 육중한 가슴에 압도되고 말았다. 안젤로가 성부 하느님조차 인간의 형상으로 그릴 때, 그 안에 깃든 강건함을 보라. 그리고 그 성자에게 깃든 신성한 사랑을 무엇으로 드러내든, 그분의 관념이 가장 성공적으로 구현된 저 부드럽고 곱슬곱슬하며 자웅동체 같은 이탈리아 그림들은, 근육미라고는 도무지 찾아볼 수 없어, 그 어떤 힘도 암시하지 못하고 다만 순종과 인내라는 소극적이고 여성적인 힘만을 비칠 뿐인데, 누구나 인정하듯 그것이야말로 그분 가르침의 독특한 실천적 미덕을 이룬다.\n\n내가 다루는 이 기관의 미묘한 탄력은 어찌나 절묘한지, 그것이 장난으로 휘둘리든, 진지하게 휘둘리든, 분노로 휘둘리든, 어떤 기분에 놓여 있든, 그 굴신은 한결같이 지극한 우아함으로 빛난다. 그 점에서는 어떤 요정의 팔도 이를 능가하지 못한다.\n\n이 꼬리에는 다섯 가지 큰 움직임이 특유하다. 첫째, 전진을 위한 지느러미로 쓰일 때. 둘째, 전투에서 철퇴로 쓰일 때. 셋째, 후려치기. 넷째, 꼬리치기. 다섯째, 갈래꼬리 곧추세우기다.\n\n첫째. 그 위치가 수평이므로, 리바이어던의 꼬리는 다른 모든 바다 생물의 꼬리와는 다른 방식으로 작동한다. 그것은 결코 꿈틀거리지 않는다. 사람에게서든 물고기에게서든 꿈틀거림은 열등함의 징표다. 고래에게 꼬리는 유일한 추진 수단이다. 두루마리처럼 몸 아래로 앞을 향해 말려 있다가 재빠르게 뒤로 튕겨 나가는 이 동작이, 사납게 헤엄칠 때 그 괴물에게 그토록 독특한 쏜살같고 도약하는 움직임을 부여한다. 옆지느러미는 다만 방향을 잡는 데에만 쓰일 뿐이다.\n\n둘째. 한 향유고래가 다른 향유고래와 싸울 때는 머리와 턱만으로 싸우면서도, 사람과 맞붙을 때는 주로, 그것도 경멸하듯 꼬리를 쓴다는 것은 다소 의미심장하다. 보트를 향해 칠 때, 고래는 갈래꼬리를 잽싸게 보트에서 휘어 빼내며, 그 타격은 오로지 되튕김으로 가해진다. 만약 그 타격이 거침없는 허공에서, 특히 목표를 향해 내리꽂힐 때 이루어진다면, 그 일격은 그야말로 막아 낼 도리가 없다. 사람의 갈비뼈도 보트의 늑재도 그것을 견디지 못한다. 살아남을 유일한 길은 그것을 피하는 것뿐이다. 그러나 그것이 가로막는 물살을 가르며 옆에서 날아올 경우에는, 고래잡이 보트의 가벼운 부력과 그 재료의 탄력 덕분에, 늑재 하나가 금이 가거나 널판 한두 장이 부서지는 정도, 말하자면 옆구리가 살짝 결리는 정도가 대개 가장 심각한 결과다. 이렇게 물속에서 옆으로 받는 타격은 고래잡이에서 워낙 자주 겪는 일이라, 그저 아이들 장난쯤으로 여겨진다. 누군가 작업복 윗도리를 벗어 그 구멍을 틀어막으면 그만이다.\n\n셋째. 증명할 수는 없으나, 내가 보기에 고래는 촉각이 꼬리에 집중되어 있는 듯하다. 이 점에서 그 꼬리에는 오직 코끼리 코의 섬세함에나 견줄 만한 예민함이 있기 때문이다. 이 섬세함은 주로 후려치는 동작에서 드러나는데, 그때 고래는 처녀처럼 부드럽게, 어떤 나긋한 느릿함으로 그 거대한 갈래꼬리를 바다 표면 위에서 이쪽저쪽으로 움직인다. 그러다 선원의 구레나룻 한 올이라도 느끼면, 그 선원에게는 화가 미치리니, 구레나룻이고 뭐고 다 끝장이다. 그 예비적인 접촉에 깃든 부드러움이라니! 이 꼬리에 무언가를 움켜쥐는 능력이 있었다면, 나는 당장 다르모노데스의 코끼리를 떠올렸을 것이다. 그 코끼리는 꽃시장을 어찌나 자주 드나들었던지, 낮게 인사를 건네며 처녀들에게 꽃다발을 바치고는 그들의 허리띠를 어루만지곤 했다. 고래의 꼬리에 이렇게 움켜쥐는 능력이 없다는 것은 여러모로 안타까운 일이다. 또 다른 코끼리에 관해서도 들은 바가 있는데, 그놈은 싸움에서 상처를 입자 코를 둥글게 말아 박힌 투창을 뽑아냈다고 한다.\n\n넷째. 고독한 바다 한가운데서 안전하다 믿고 있는 고래에게 눈치채지 못하게 살그머니 다가가 보면, 그 위엄의 방대한 비만함을 풀어헤친 채 새끼고양이처럼 대양을 마치 난롯가인 양 여기며 노니는 모습을 보게 된다. 그래도 그 장난 속에서 여전히 그 힘이 엿보인다. 그 꼬리의 넓은 손바닥이 허공 높이 휙 치켜올려졌다가, 수면을 내리치면 천둥 같은 충격음이 몇 마일에 걸쳐 울려 퍼진다. 거대한 대포라도 발사된 줄로 알 만하다. 그리고 그 반대쪽 끝의 분수공에서 옅은 김 한 자락이 피어오르는 것을 보았다면, 그것이 점화구에서 나는 연기라고 여겼을 것이다.\n\n다섯째. 리바이어던이 평소 물 위에 떠 있는 자세에서는 갈래꼬리가 등보다 한참 아래에 놓여 수면 밑으로 완전히 자취를 감추지만, 깊은 바닷속으로 곤두박질치려 할 때면 그 갈래꼬리 전체가 적어도 몸의 30피트(약 9미터)와 더불어 허공으로 곧추 솟구쳐 올라, 잠시 떨며 그대로 머물다가 아래로 내리꽂혀 시야에서 사라진다. 어딘가 다른 데서 서술할 저 숭고한 도약을 제외하면, 이 고래의 갈래꼬리 곧추세우기야말로 아마 살아 있는 자연 전체에서 볼 수 있는 가장 장엄한 광경일 것이다. 밑바닥 없는 심연에서 거대한 꼬리가 경련하듯 가장 높은 하늘을 움켜쥐려 드는 듯하다. 꿈속에서 나는 위엄 있는 사탄이 지옥의 불타는 발트해에서 고통에 찬 거대한 발톱을 내뻗는 것을 본 적이 있다. 그러나 그러한 광경을 바라볼 때, 모든 것은 그대가 어떤 기분에 놓여 있느냐에 달려 있다. 단테적인 기분이라면 악마들이 떠오를 것이고, 이사야적인 기분이라면 대천사들이 떠오를 것이다. 하늘과 바다를 진홍으로 물들이던 어느 일출 무렵, 나는 내 배의 돛대 꼭대기에 서 있다가 동쪽에 거대한 고래 떼가 모두 해를 향해 머리를 두르고서, 한순간 갈래꼬리를 곧추세운 채 일제히 떠는 것을 본 적이 있다. 그때 내게는 그토록 장엄하게 구현된 신들에 대한 경배가, 불을 숭배하는 자들의 고향인 페르시아에서조차 일찍이 본 적 없는 광경처럼 느껴졌다. 프톨레마이오스 필로파토르가 아프리카 코끼리에 대해 증언했듯, 나도 그때 고래에 대해 증언하노니, 그를 모든 존재 가운데 가장 경건한 것이라 선언하는 바다. 유바 왕에 따르면, 고대의 전투 코끼리들은 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에서 코를 치켜든 채 아침을 맞이하곤 했다고 하기 때문이다.\n\n이 장에서 우연히 이루어진 고래와 코끼리의 비교는, 한쪽의 꼬리와 다른 쪽의 코의 어떤 측면에 관한 한, 그 두 상반된 기관을 대등하게 놓으려는 뜻이 아니며, 하물며 각각이 속한 두 생물을 동등하게 보려는 것은 더더욱 아니다. 가장 막강한 코끼리조차 리바이어던에게는 한낱 테리어 강아지에 지나지 않듯, 리바이어던의 꼬리에 견주면 코끼리의 코는 백합의 줄기에 불과하기 때문이다. 코끼리의 코가 휘두르는 가장 무시무시한 일격도, 향유고래의 묵직한 갈래꼬리가 가하는 가늠할 수 없는 짓이김과 부서뜨림에 견주면 부채로 장난스레 톡 치는 것에 불과한데, 그 갈래꼬리는 거듭된 사례에서 노와 선원이 다 실린 보트들을 하나씩 잇따라 허공으로 내던졌으니, 그 모습은 인도의 곡예사가 공을 던져 올리는 것과 흡사했다.*\n\n* 고래와 코끼리를 전체적인 부피의 면에서 비교하는 것은 터무니없는 일이다. 그 점에서 코끼리는 고래에 대해 마치 개가 코끼리에 대해 지니는 것과 거의 같은 관계에 놓이기 때문이다. 그럼에도 묘하게 닮은 점이 더러 없지 않은데, 그중 하나가 물줄기를 뿜는 것이다. 코끼리가 흔히 코로 물이나 흙을 빨아올린 다음, 그것을 치켜들어 한 줄기로 내뿜는다는 것은 잘 알려진 사실이다.\n\n이 막강한 꼬리를 곱씹어 볼수록, 나는 그것을 제대로 표현해 내지 못하는 내 무능을 더욱 한탄하게 된다. 때로 그 꼬리에는, 사람의 손에 깃들었다면 능히 우아하게 어울렸을 법한, 그러면서도 도무지 설명할 길 없는 몸짓들이 담겨 있다. 큰 무리 속에서 이 신비로운 몸짓들이 이따금 어찌나 놀라운지, 나는 그것을 프리메이슨의 신호나 상징에 빗대며, 고래가 실로 이러한 방식으로 세상과 지적으로 대화를 나눈다고 단언하는 사냥꾼들의 이야기를 들은 적이 있다. 또한 고래의 몸 전체에는 그 밖에도 온갖 기이함으로 가득 차서, 가장 노련한 공격자조차 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 다른 움직임들이 적지 않다. 그러니 내가 어떻게 그를 해부하든, 나는 다만 살갗 한 꺼풀밖에 들어가지 못한다. 나는 그를 알지 못하며, 앞으로도 결코 알지 못할 것이다. 그러나 이 고래의 꼬리조차 알지 못한다면, 어찌 그 머리를 이해하겠는가? 더구나 그에게 얼굴이라는 것이 없을진대, 어찌 그 얼굴을 헤아리겠는가? 너는 나의 등과 꼬리는 보겠으나, 나의 얼굴은 보지 못하리라, 하고 그는 말하는 듯하다. 그러나 나는 그의 등조차 온전히 알아내지 못하며, 그가 제 얼굴에 대해 무어라 넌지시 비치든, 나는 거듭 말하노니 그에게는 얼굴이 없다.\n\n# CHAPTER 87. The Grand Armada.\n\n말라카의 길고 좁은 반도는 버마(Birmah)의 영토에서 남동쪽으로 뻗어 나가, 아시아 전체에서 가장 남쪽에 자리한 지점을 이룬다. 그 반도로부터 하나의 연속된 선을 그리며 수마트라, 자바, 발리, 티모르의 긴 섬들이 늘어서 있고, 이것들은 다른 수많은 섬들과 더불어 아시아와 오스트레일리아를 길게 잇는 거대한 방파제 혹은 성벽을 이루어, 길고 끊임없는 인도양을 빽빽이 박힌 동방의 군도들로부터 갈라놓는다. 이 성벽에는 배와 고래의 편의를 위한 출격용 통로가 여럿 뚫려 있는데, 그중에서도 눈에 띄는 것이 순다 해협과 말라카 해협이다. 주로 순다 해협을 통하여, 서쪽에서 중국으로 향하는 선박들이 중국해로 빠져나온다.\n\n저 좁은 순다 해협은 수마트라를 자바로부터 갈라놓는다. 그 광대한 섬들의 성벽 한가운데 자리잡고, 뱃사람들에게 자바 헤드라 알려진 저 대담한 초록빛 곶에 떠받쳐져 있는 그 해협은, 어느 광대한 성벽 둘린 제국으로 들어서는 중앙의 관문에 적잖이 들어맞는다. 그리고 저 동방 바다의 수천 섬을 풍요롭게 하는 향료와 비단과 보석과 황금과 상아의 무진장한 부를 헤아려 보면, 그런 보물들이 땅의 생김새 그 자체로 인하여, 비록 헛된 일일지언정, 적어도 모든 것을 움켜쥐려 드는 서구 세계로부터 지켜지는 듯한 외양만이라도 갖추고 있다는 것은 자연의 의미심장한 안배인 듯싶다. 순다 해협의 기슭에는 지중해와 발트해와 프로폰티스의 입구를 지키는 저 위압적인 요새들이 들어서 있지 않다. 덴마크인들과 달리 이 동방인들은, 수 세기 동안 밤낮으로 동방의 가장 값진 화물을 싣고 수마트라와 자바의 섬들 사이를 지나간 그 끝없는 순풍의 선단 행렬을 향하여, 윗돛을 내려 굽실거리는 경의를 표하라고 요구하지 않는다. 그러나 그들은 이런 식의 의례는 선뜻 면제해 주면서도, 더 실속 있는 공물에 대한 요구만큼은 결코 포기하지 않는다.\n\n아득한 옛날부터 말레이인들의 해적선들은 수마트라의 그늘진 낮은 후미와 작은 섬들 사이에 숨어 있다가, 해협을 가로질러 항해하는 선박들 위로 뛰쳐나와 창끝을 들이대며 사납게 공물을 요구해 왔다. 비록 유럽 순양함들의 손에 거듭 피의 응징을 받은 탓에 이 해적들의 만용이 근래 다소 수그러들기는 했으나, 오늘날에도 우리는 이따금 영국과 미국의 선박들이 그 해역에서 무자비하게 나포되고 약탈당했다는 소식을 듣곤 한다.\n\n상쾌하고 알맞은 바람을 받으며 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 이제 그 해협으로 다가가고 있었다. 에이해브(Ahab)는 해협을 지나 자바해로 들어선 다음, 거기서 북쪽으로 항해하며 여기저기 향유고래가 자주 출몰한다고 알려진 바다를 가로질러, 필리핀 제도 연안을 따라 북상하여, 그곳의 대규모 포경 철에 맞추어 멀리 일본 해안에 닿을 작정이었다. 이런 방법으로 세계를 일주하는 피쿼드호는, 태평양의 적도(Line)로 내려가기에 앞서, 세상에 알려진 거의 모든 향유고래 어장을 두루 훑게 될 터였다. 에이해브는 다른 모든 곳에서는 추격이 좌절될지라도, 모비 딕(Moby Dick)이 가장 자주 출몰한다고 알려진 그 바다에서, 또한 그놈이 거기 머물고 있으리라고 가장 합당하게 추정할 수 있는 그 철에, 기어이 그놈과 한판 싸움을 벌이리라고 굳게 믿었다.\n\n그러나 이건 또 어찌 된 일인가? 이렇게 구역을 정해 놓고 벌이는 추적에서 에이해브는 결코 뭍을 밟지 않는단 말인가? 그의 선원들은 공기를 마시고 사는가? 분명 물을 얻으러 어딘가에 들르긴 할 것이다. 아니다. 이미 오래전부터, 곡마장을 돌듯 달리는 태양은 제 불의 고리 안을 질주하면서 제 안에 든 것 외에는 어떤 양식도 필요로 하지 않는다. 에이해브가 그러하다. 포경선에서도 이 점을 눈여겨보라. 다른 배들은 외국의 부두로 옮겨 갈 낯선 물건들로 가득 채워져 무겁게 가라앉지만, 세계를 떠도는 포경선은 자기 자신과 선원들, 그들의 무기와 그들의 필요 외에는 아무 화물도 싣지 않는다. 포경선은 넉넉한 선창 속에 호수 하나만큼의 내용물을 병에 담아 두고 있다. 쓸 데 없는 무쇠 덩이나 짐돌만으로 채워진 것이 아니라, 쓸모 있는 것들로 균형을 잡고 있다. 포경선은 몇 년 치의 물을 그 안에 싣고 있다. 맑고 오래되어 일품인 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 물, 바다에 떠 있은 지 삼 년이 되어도 태평양의 낸터킷 사람은, 어제 막 페루나 인도의 강에서 통에 담아 뗏목으로 실어 온 그 짭짤한 물보다 차라리 그것을 마시고 싶어 한다. 그러기에 다른 배들이 뉴욕(New)에서 중국까지 갔다가 다시 돌아오면서 수십 개의 항구에 들르는 동안, 포경선은 그 모든 기간에 흙 한 톨도 보지 못한 채, 선원들은 자기네와 같이 바다를 떠도는 뱃사람들 말고는 어느 누구도 본 적이 없는 일도 흔하다. 그러니 그들에게 또 한 번의 대홍수가 닥쳐왔다는 소식을 전한다면, 그들은 그저 이렇게 대답할 따름이리라. \"거참, 친구들, 여기 방주가 있잖나!\"\n\n자, 향유고래는 자바 서쪽 해안, 곧 순다 해협의 가까운 인근에서 숱하게 잡혀 왔고, 실로 그 일대의 바다 대부분이 어부들 사이에서 항해하기 좋은 명소로 널리 인정받고 있었던 터라, 피쿼드호가 자바 헤드에 점점 더 가까워질수록 망꾼들은 거듭 호명되며 정신을 바짝 차리고 경계하라는 당부를 받았다. 그러나 초록빛 야자나무 우거진 절벽이 머지않아 우현 뱃머리 쪽에 어렴풋이 떠오르고, 기쁨에 들뜬 콧구멍으로 신선한 계피 향이 공중에서 들이마셔졌건만, 물줄기는 단 한 줄기도 보이지 않았다. 이 부근에서 사냥감을 만나리라는 생각을 거의 단념할 무렵, 배가 어느덧 해협에 들어섰을 때, 돛대 위에서 여느 때처럼 환호하는 외침이 들려왔고, 이윽고 더없이 장엄한 광경이 우리를 맞이했다.\n\n그러나 여기서 미리 일러둘 것이 있다. 근래 향유고래는 네 대양 전역에서 지칠 줄 모르는 활동으로 사냥당해 온 탓에, 옛날처럼 거의 어김없이 작고 흩어진 무리를 지어 다니는 대신, 이제는 흔히 광대한 떼를 이루어 나타나는데, 그 수가 어찌나 많은지 마치 수많은 고래 민족들이 서로 돕고 지켜 주자고 엄숙한 동맹과 맹약을 맺기라도 한 듯 보일 지경이다. 향유고래가 이렇게 거대한 대상(隊商)으로 모여드는 것에, 가장 좋은 어장에서조차 이제는 이따금 몇 주, 몇 달을 내리 항해해도 물줄기 하나 만나지 못하다가, 별안간 때로는 수천 마리에 수천 마리가 더해진 듯한 광경의 환대를 받게 되는 사정을 돌릴 수 있겠다.\n\n양쪽 뱃머리 너머로 활짝, 약 이삼 마일쯤 떨어진 거리에서, 수평선의 절반을 끌어안으며 커다란 반원을 이룬 채, 끊임없이 이어지는 고래 물줄기의 사슬이 한낮의 대기 속에서 뿜어 올라 반짝이고 있었다. 참고래(Right Whale)의 곧추선 한 쌍의 물줄기는 꼭대기에서 둘로 갈라져 마치 버드나무의 갈라져 늘어진 가지처럼 두 갈래로 떨어지지만, 그것과 달리 향유고래의 단 하나뿐인 앞으로 비스듬한 물줄기는 두툼하게 말려 오르는 흰 안개의 덤불을 이루며, 끊임없이 솟았다가는 바람 부는 쪽으로 흩어져 내린다.\n\n그리하여 피쿼드호 갑판에서, 배가 바다의 높은 언덕 위로 솟구쳐 오를 때 바라보면, 저마다 공중으로 말려 올라가는 이 안개 같은 물줄기의 무리는, 푸르스름한 아지랑이가 한데 어우러진 대기를 통해, 향기로운 가을 아침에 어느 높은 곳에 선 말 탄 이의 눈에 비친, 어느 빽빽한 대도시의 수천 개 정겨운 굴뚝처럼 보였다.\n\n산속의 험한 협로로 다가가는 행군 중인 군대가, 그 위험한 길목을 어서 등 뒤에 두고 다시 한번 비교적 안전한 평원에서 대오를 펼치고 싶은 일념에 행군을 재촉하듯이, 바로 그처럼 이 거대한 고래 함대도 이제 해협을 통과하려고 서둘러 앞으로 나아가는 듯 보였다. 반원의 양 날개를 점차 좁혀 가며, 하나의 단단하면서도 여전히 초승달 모양인 중심을 이루어 헤엄쳐 나아갔다.\n\n피쿼드호는 돛을 모조리 펼친 채 그것들을 뒤쫓아 밀고 나아갔다. 작살잡이들은 저마다 무기를 손에 들고, 아직 매달려 있는 보트의 이물에서 소리 높여 환호했다. 바람만 버텨 준다면, 이 순다 해협을 통해 쫓긴 그 거대한 무리가 동방의 바다로 펼쳐져 나가, 제 동족 적잖은 수가 사로잡히는 것을 목격하게 되리라는 점에는 그들도 거의 의심이 없었다. 게다가 그 모여든 대상 가운데, 시암 왕의 대관식 행렬에서 숭배받는 흰 코끼리처럼, 모비 딕 그놈이 잠시 헤엄치고 있을지 누가 알겠는가! 그리하여 보조돛 위에 보조돛을 겹겹이 쌓아 올린 채, 우리는 이 리바이어던들을 앞으로 몰아가며 항해해 나갔다. 그때 별안간 태시테고(Tashtego)의 목소리가 들려와, 우리 배 뒤쪽의 무언가에 주의를 기울이라고 큰 소리로 가리켰다.\n\n우리 앞쪽의 초승달에 상응하여, 우리 뒤쪽에서도 또 하나의 초승달이 보였다. 그것은 흩어진 흰 수증기로 이루어진 듯, 고래의 물줄기처럼 솟았다가 가라앉기를 반복했다. 다만 그것들은 그렇게 완전히 나타났다 사라지지는 않았으니, 끝내 사라지는 법 없이 줄곧 떠 있었다. 망원경을 그 광경에 겨눈 에이해브는 제 의족 구멍 속에서 휙 몸을 돌리며 외쳤다. \"돛대 위로 올라가, 도르래와 양동이를 매어 돛을 적셔라. 말레이인들이오, 선장, 우리 뒤를 쫓고 있소!\"\n\n이 비열한 아시아인들은 피쿼드호가 완전히 해협에 들어설 때까지 곶 뒤에 너무 오래 숨어 있었던 듯, 이제는 그 지나치게 조심스러운 지체를 만회하려는 듯 맹렬히 추격해 왔다. 그러나 날쌘 피쿼드호가 상쾌한 순풍을 받으며 그 자신이 한창 맹렬한 추격을 벌이는 중이었으니, 이 거무튀튀한 박애주의자들이 그 배를 제 스스로 택한 추격으로 더욱 빠르게 몰아 주는 데 일조하다니 얼마나 친절한가. 그들은 그저 배의 채찍이요 박차였을 따름이었다. 망원경을 겨드랑이에 낀 채 에이해브는 갑판을 오락가락 거닐었는데, 앞으로 돌아설 때는 자기가 쫓는 괴물들을 바라보고, 뒤로 돌아설 때는 자기를 쫓는 피에 굶주린 해적들을 바라보았다. 위와 같은 어떤 상념이 그의 마음에 떠올랐던 듯하다. 그리고 그가 배가 그때 항해하던 물의 협로의 초록빛 벽을 흘끗 보고, 저 관문을 지나면 자기 복수의 길이 놓여 있음을 생각하고, 또한 바로 그 관문을 지나며 자기가 지금 쫓는 동시에 제 죽음의 끝을 향해 쫓기고 있음을, 게다가 그뿐 아니라 무자비한 야만의 해적 떼와 인정머리 없는 무신론자 악마들이 저주를 퍼부으며 지옥처럼 자기를 부추기고 있음을 바라보았을 때, 이 모든 상념이 그의 뇌리를 스쳐 지나갔을 때, 에이해브의 이마는 어떤 폭풍의 조수가 갉아먹어도 그 단단한 것을 제자리에서 끌어내지 못한 채 떠나 버린 뒤의 검은 모래 해변처럼, 야위고 골이 패여 남았다.\n\n그러나 이런 상념으로 괴로워한 자는 무모한 선원들 가운데 극히 드물었다. 해적들을 꾸준히 뒤로, 뒤로 떨어뜨린 끝에 마침내 피쿼드호가 수마트라 쪽의 선명한 초록빛 코카투 곶을 쏜살같이 지나, 마침내 그 너머의 드넓은 바다로 빠져나왔을 때, 작살잡이들은 배가 말레이인들을 그토록 승리하듯 따돌렸다는 사실에 기뻐하기보다, 날쌘 고래들이 배보다 앞서가고 있다는 사실에 더 애를 태우는 듯했다. 그러나 여전히 고래들의 자취를 따라 몰아가던 중, 이윽고 고래들이 속도를 늦추는 듯했다. 차츰 배가 그것들에 가까워졌고, 이제 바람이 잦아들자 보트로 뛰어내리라는 명령이 전해졌다. 그러나 향유고래의 어떤 놀라운 본능이 작용했다고 짐작되는바, 그 무리가 자기네 뒤를 쫓는 세 척의 보트를, 비록 아직 한 마일이나 떨어져 있었으되, 알아채자마자 다시 결집하여, 빽빽한 대오와 대대를 이루어 그 물줄기가 모조리 빼곡히 세운 총검의 번쩍이는 줄처럼 보이게 한 채, 갑절의 속도로 나아갔다.\n\n셔츠와 속바지만 걸친 채 우리는 흰 물푸레나무 노를 잡고 뛰어들어, 여러 시간을 저은 끝에 추격을 거의 단념하려던 참이었다. 그때 고래들 사이에 전반적으로 멈칫거리는 동요가 일어, 그것들이 마침내 저 기이한 당혹, 곧 무력한 우유부단의 영향 아래 놓였다는 활기를 돋우는 징표를 보였다. 어부들이 고래에게서 이런 기색을 알아챌 때, 그들은 그놈이 갈리(gallied)되었다고 말한다. 지금껏 빠르고 꾸준히 헤엄쳐 오던 그 빽빽한 전투 대형이 이제는 한없는 패주 속에 흐트러졌다. 그리하여 알렉산드로스와의 인도 전투에서 포루스 왕의 코끼리들처럼, 그것들은 경악에 빠져 미쳐 날뛰는 듯했다. 사방으로 거대한 불규칙 원을 그리며 퍼져 나가, 목적도 없이 이리저리 헤엄치고, 짧고 두툼한 물줄기를 뿜어 댐으로써, 그것들은 공황에 휩싸인 혼란을 여실히 드러냈다. 이는 그것들 가운데 어떤 놈들, 곧 마치 완전히 마비되기라도 한 듯 물에 잠겨 의장이 뜯겨 나간 배처럼 속절없이 바다 위에 떠 있는 놈들에 의해 한층 더 기이하게 입증되었다. 이 리바이어던들이 그저 단순한 양 떼였고, 세 마리 사나운 늑대에게 목초지를 가로질러 쫓겼다 한들, 결코 이토록 지나친 경악을 드러내지는 못했을 것이다. 그러나 이런 이따금의 겁먹음은 거의 모든 무리 짓는 생물의 특성이다. 수만 마리씩 떼를 지으면서도, 서부의 사자 갈기를 두른 들소들은 홀로 말 탄 사람 하나 앞에서 달아나곤 했다. 또한 모든 인간을 보라. 그들이 극장 일층 관람석의 양 우리에 떼 지어 몰려 있을 때, 불이 났다는 아주 작은 경보에도 어떻게 출구를 향해 허둥지둥 몰려들며, 밀치고 짓밟고 끼이고, 무자비하게 서로를 들이받아 죽음에 이르게 하는지를. 그러므로 우리 앞에서 기이하게 갈리된 고래들에 대해 어떤 놀라움도 거두는 편이 낫겠다. 땅 위 짐승들의 그 어떤 어리석음도 인간의 광기에 끝없이 능가당하지 않는 것이 없기 때문이다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 고래 가운데 많은 놈들이 격렬히 움직이고 있었으나, 전체로 보면 그 무리는 앞으로 나아가지도 뒤로 물러나지도 않고 한데 모여 한자리에 머물러 있었음을 눈여겨봐야 한다. 이런 경우의 관례대로, 보트들은 즉시 흩어져 저마다 떼의 변두리에 있는 외톨이 고래 한 마리를 노렸다. 약 삼 분쯤 지났을 때 퀴케그(Queequeg)의 작살이 날아갔다. 작살 맞은 고래는 눈을 멀게 하는 물보라를 우리 얼굴에 끼얹고는, 빛처럼 빠르게 우리를 끌고 달아나며 무리의 한복판을 향해 곧장 키를 잡았다. 이런 상황에서 작살 맞은 고래가 보이는 이런 움직임이 결코 전례 없는 일은 아니고, 실로 거의 언제나 어느 정도는 예상되는 것이긴 하지만, 그래도 그것은 포경의 더 위태로운 변고 가운데 하나를 보여 준다. 그 날쌘 괴물이 미쳐 날뛰는 떼 속으로 그대를 점점 더 깊이 끌고 들어갈수록, 그대는 신중한 삶에 작별을 고하고 오직 정신없는 고동 속에서만 존재하게 되기 때문이다.\n\n눈멀고 귀먹은 채, 마치 순전한 속도의 힘만으로 제 몸에 들러붙은 무쇠 거머리를 떨쳐 내려는 듯 고래가 앞으로 곤두박질치는 동안, 우리가 그렇게 바다에 흰 상처를 째며 나아가는 동안, 사방에서 미쳐 날뛰는 짐승들이 우리 주위를 이리저리 휘몰아치며 우리를 위협했고, 그 한가운데서 포위된 우리 보트는 폭풍 속에서 빙산 떼에 둘러싸여, 언제 갇혀 으스러질지 모르는 채 그 뒤얽힌 수로와 해협을 헤치고 키를 잡으려 애쓰는 배와도 같았다.\n\n그러나 조금도 기죽지 않고 퀴케그는 우리를 사내답게 몰아갔다. 우리 진로 바로 앞을 가로지르는 이 괴물에게서 비켜 가는가 하면, 거대한 꼬리지느러미를 머리 위에 매단 저 괴물에게서 슬쩍 비껴 나기도 했다. 그러는 내내 스타벅(Starbuck)은 이물에 서서 창을 손에 들고, 손이 닿는 고래는 무엇이든 짧게 찔러 우리 길에서 떼어 냈으니, 길게 찌를 틈이 없었기 때문이다. 노잡이들도 마냥 한가하지만은 않았으니, 그들의 본분인 노 젓기는 이제 완전히 면제되었어도 그러했다. 그들은 주로 고함치는 일을 맡았다. \"비키시오, 제독님!\" 한 사람이, 별안간 온몸을 수면 위로 솟구쳐 한순간 우리를 뒤엎을 듯 위협한 거대한 단봉낙타 같은 놈을 향해 외쳤다. \"이봐, 꼬리 좀 세게 내려치라고!\" 또 한 사람이, 우리 뱃전 가까이에서 부채 같은 제 꼬리 끝으로 태연히 제 몸을 식히는 듯한 다른 놈을 향해 외쳤다.\n\n모든 포경 보트는 본래 낸터킷의 인디언(Indian)들이 고안한, 드러그(drugg)라 불리는 기묘한 장치를 몇 개씩 싣고 다닌다. 같은 크기의 두툼한 정사각형 나무 두 개를 단단히 맞물려, 서로의 결이 직각으로 엇갈리게 한다. 그런 다음 이 나무토막의 한가운데에 상당히 긴 밧줄을 매고, 그 밧줄의 다른 끝을 고리로 만들어 두면, 순식간에 작살에 붙들어 맬 수 있다. 이 드러그는 주로 갈리된 고래들 사이에서 쓰인다. 그런 때에는 한 번에 쫓을 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 고래가 그대 주위에 바싹 모여 있기 때문이다. 그런데 향유고래는 날마다 만나는 것이 아니니, 그러므로 만날 수 있을 때 잡을 수 있는 만큼 모조리 잡아야 한다. 그리고 한꺼번에 다 죽일 수 없다면, 그것들을 절름발이로 만들어 두어 나중에 한가할 때 죽일 수 있게 해야 한다. 그러기에 이런 때에 드러그가 요긴해진다. 우리 보트에는 그것이 세 개 갖춰져 있었다. 첫 번째와 두 번째는 성공적으로 던져졌고, 우리는 끌려가는 드러그의 어마어마한 옆쪽 저항에 발이 묶여 비틀거리며 달아나는 고래들을 보았다. 그것들은 쇠사슬과 쇠공에 묶인 죄인들처럼 옴짝달싹 못 했다. 그러나 세 번째 것을 던지면서, 그 투박한 나무토막을 뱃전 밖으로 내던지려던 순간, 그것이 보트의 좌석 하나 밑에 걸려, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 좌석을 뜯어내 끌고 가 버렸고, 좌석이 밑에서 미끄러져 빠지자 노잡이는 보트 바닥에 나동그라졌다. 부서진 널빤지로 양쪽에서 바닷물이 밀려들었으나, 우리는 속바지와 셔츠 두세 벌을 쑤셔 넣어 우선은 새는 곳을 틀어막았다.\n\n이 드러그 작살들을 던지기란, 우리가 떼 속으로 나아갈수록 우리 고래의 속도가 크게 줄어들지 않았더라면, 게다가 우리가 동요의 가장자리에서 점점 더 멀어질수록 그 무시무시한 혼란이 잦아드는 듯하지 않았더라면, 거의 불가능했을 것이다. 그리하여 마침내 홱홱 당기던 작살이 빠지고, 끌고 가던 고래가 옆으로 사라졌을 때, 우리는 그놈이 떨어져 나가며 남긴 점점 가늘어지는 추진력에 실려, 두 마리 고래 사이로 미끄러져 떼의 가장 깊은 한복판으로 들어갔으니, 마치 어느 산골짜기의 급류에서 잔잔한 골짜기 호수로 미끄러져 든 것만 같았다. 여기서는 가장 바깥쪽 고래들 사이의 포효하는 협곡에서 이는 폭풍이 들리기는 해도 느껴지지는 않았다. 이 중앙의 너른 자리에서 바다는 매끄러운 새틴 같은 표면을 드러냈는데, 이를 슬릭(sleek)이라 부르며, 고래가 한결 잔잔한 기분일 때 내뿜는 미묘한 습기로 인해 생겨난다. 그렇다, 우리는 이제 모든 동요의 한복판에 도사리고 있다고들 하는 저 마법에 걸린 고요 속에 들어 있었다. 그러면서도 어지러이 흐트러진 저 멀리서, 우리는 바깥쪽 동심원들의 소란을 바라보았고, 여덟 마리에서 열 마리씩 무리를 이룬 고래 떼들이, 마치 둥근 마장에서 여러 줄로 늘어선 말들처럼 빠르게 빙빙 돌고 또 도는 것을 보았는데, 어깨와 어깨가 어찌나 바싹 붙어 있던지, 거인 같은 곡마사라면 가운데 놈들 위로 가뿐히 다리를 걸쳐 그 등을 타고 빙 돌 수도 있었으리라. 떼의 만(灣)처럼 둘러싸인 축을 한층 가까이 에워싼, 쉬고 있는 고래들이 빽빽이 들어찬 까닭에, 지금으로서는 우리에게 빠져나갈 가망이 전혀 없었다. 우리는 우리를 가둔 살아 있는 벽, 곧 우리를 가두어 두려고만 들여보낸 그 벽에 틈이 생기기를 지켜봐야 했다. 호수의 한가운데에 머무는 동안, 우리는 이따금 작고 온순한 암소들과 새끼들의 방문을 받았으니, 그것들은 이 패주한 무리의 아녀자들이었다.\n\n자, 빙빙 도는 바깥쪽 원들 사이의 이따금씩의 넓은 간격과, 그 원들 가운데 어느 하나에 속한 여러 고래 떼들 사이의 공간까지 모두 합하면, 이 시점에 그 모든 무리가 끌어안은 전체 넓이는 적어도 이삼 제곱마일은 되었을 것이다. 어쨌든, 비록 그런 때에 그런 식의 가늠은 사람을 속일 수도 있겠지만, 우리의 낮은 보트에서 보아도 거의 수평선 가장자리에서부터 솟구쳐 노니는 듯한 물줄기들이 발견되었다. 내가 이런 사정을 언급하는 까닭은, 마치 암소들과 새끼들이 일부러 이 가장 깊숙한 우리 안에 갇혀 있기라도 한 듯, 또 마치 떼의 넓은 펼쳐짐이 지금껏 그것들로 하여금 무리가 멈춘 정확한 까닭을 알아채지 못하게 막아 온 듯, 혹은 어쩌면 너무 어리고, 세상 물정 모르고, 모든 면에서 순진하고 경험 없는 탓이었을지도 모르나, 그것이 어찌 되었든, 이 작은 고래들이, 이따금 호수 가장자리에서 바람 멎어 멈춰 선 우리 보트로 찾아와, 놀라우리만치 두려움 없는 신뢰를, 아니면 도무지 경탄하지 않을 수 없는, 여전히 마법에 걸린 듯한 공황을 드러냈기 때문이다. 그것들은 집에서 기르는 개처럼 우리 주위를 킁킁대며 우리 뱃전까지 바싹 다가와 거기에 몸을 비벼 댔으니, 마치 무슨 주문이 별안간 그것들을 길들이기라도 한 것만 같았다. 퀴케그는 그것들의 이마를 토닥였고, 스타벅은 창으로 그것들의 등을 긁어 주었으나, 뒷일이 두려워 그때만큼은 창을 찔러 넣기를 삼갔다.\n\n그러나 수면 위의 이 경이로운 세계 저 아래 깊은 곳에서, 우리가 뱃전 너머로 응시하자 또 하나의, 한층 더 기이한 세계가 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐졌다. 그 물의 둥근 천장 속에 매달린 채, 새끼에게 젖을 먹이는 어미 고래들과, 그 어마어마한 둘레로 보아 머지않아 어미가 될 듯한 고래들의 형상이 떠 있었던 것이다. 앞서 넌지시 일렀듯이 이 호수는 상당한 깊이까지 더없이 투명했다. 그리고 사람의 갓난아이가 젖을 빨면서도 마치 그 순간 두 개의 다른 삶을 사는 듯, 가슴에서 시선을 돌려 차분하고 골똘하게 먼 곳을 바라보며, 여전히 인간의 양분을 빨아들이면서도 영혼만은 어떤 이 세상 아닌 추억을 누리고 있는 것처럼, 바로 그처럼 이 고래의 새끼들도 우리를 올려다보는 듯했으나, 우리를 보는 것은 아니었으니, 마치 우리가 그들의 갓 태어난 시야 속에서는 한낱 모자반 한 조각에 지나지 않는 것 같았다. 어미들 역시 옆으로 누워 떠 있으면서 우리를 조용히 바라보는 듯했다. 이 작은 새끼들 가운데 하나는 어떤 기묘한 징표로 보아 태어난 지 하루도 채 안 된 듯했는데, 길이가 약 14피트(약 4.3미터)에 둘레가 약 6피트(약 1.8미터)쯤 되었을 것이다. 그놈은 조금 까불댔으나, 그 몸뚱이는 아직 얼마 전까지 머물던, 그 거북한 자세에서 채 회복되지 못한 듯 보였다. 어미의 그물주머니 속에서, 꼬리는 머리 쪽으로 향한 채 마지막 도약을 위한 만반의 채비를 갖추고, 태어나지 않은 고래는 타타르인의 활처럼 구부러져 누워 있는 것이다. 그 섬세한 옆지느러미와 꼬리지느러미의 손바닥은, 멀리 타지에서 갓 도착한 아기의 귀처럼 주름지고 쪼글쪼글한 모습을 여전히 생생히 간직하고 있었다.\n\n\"밧줄! 밧줄!\" 퀴케그가 뱃전 너머를 내려다보며 외쳤다. \"이놈 묶였다! 이놈 묶였어! 누가 이놈 밧줄 걸었나! 누가 작살 박았어? 고래 두 마리, 하나 크고 하나 작아!\"\n\n\"이봐, 자네 무슨 일인가?\" 스타벅이 외쳤다.\n\n\"여기 좀 보쇼.\" 퀴케그가 아래를 가리키며 말했다.\n\n작살 맞은 고래가 통에서 수백 길의 밧줄을 풀어내 휘청거리며 끌고 나가다가, 깊이 잠수한 뒤 다시 떠올라, 느슨하게 말린 밧줄이 둥실 떠올라 공중으로 나선을 그리며 솟아오르는 것을 보여 주듯이, 바로 지금 스타벅은 리바이어던 부인의 탯줄이 길게 사리를 틀고 있는 것을 보았으니, 그것으로 어린 새끼가 아직 제 어미에게 매여 있는 듯했다. 추격의 빠른 변고 속에서, 어미 쪽 끝이 풀린 이 자연의 밧줄이 삼으로 꼰 밧줄과 뒤엉켜, 그로 인해 새끼가 옭매이는 일도 드물지 않다. 바다의 가장 미묘한 비밀 가운데 몇 가지가 이 마법에 걸린 연못 속에서 우리에게 드러나는 듯했다. 우리는 깊은 바닷속에서 어린 리바이어던의 사랑을 보았다.\n\n향유고래는 다른 모든 리바이어던 종(種)이 그러하듯, 그러나 대부분의 다른 물고기와 달리, 계절을 가리지 않고 번식한다. 아마도 아홉 달쯤으로 잡을 수 있는 임신 기간을 거친 뒤, 한 번에 한 마리만 낳는데, 몇몇 알려진 드문 경우에는 에서와 야곱 같은 쌍둥이를 낳기도 한다. 이런 경우에 대비하여 젖을 먹이는 데에는 두 개의 젖꼭지가 마련되어 있으니, 기묘하게도 항문 양쪽에 하나씩 자리잡고 있다. 그러나 젖통 자체는 거기서 위쪽으로 뻗어 있다. 어쩌다 젖 먹이는 고래의 이 귀한 부위가 사냥꾼의 창에 베이면, 어미가 쏟아 내는 젖과 피가 서로 앞다투어 몇 로드에 걸쳐 바다를 물들인다. 그 젖은 매우 달고 진하여, 사람이 맛본 적도 있는데, 딸기와 곁들이면 잘 어울릴 법하다. 고래들은 서로에 대한 존경이 흘러넘칠 때, 인간이 하는 방식대로 사랑을 나눈다.\n\n이렇듯 경악과 공포의 원이 겹겹이 둘러싸고 있었음에도, 한가운데에 있는 이 헤아릴 수 없는 생물들은 거리낌 없이 두려움 없이 온갖 평화로운 일에 빠져들었으니, 그렇다, 더없이 평온하게 사랑의 희롱과 환희를 즐겼다. 그러나 바로 그처럼, 내 존재의 회오리치는 대서양 한가운데서도, 나 자신은 여전히 영원토록 그 중심에서 말 없는 고요 속에 노닐고 있으니, 줄어들 줄 모르는 비통의 묵직한 행성들이 내 주위를 돌고 있는 동안에도, 저 깊은 곳, 저 깊은 내륙에서 나는 여전히 영원한 기쁨의 부드러움 속에 몸을 담그고 있다.\n\n그러는 사이, 우리가 이렇게 황홀경에 잠겨 있는 동안, 멀리서 이따금 별안간 벌어지는 미친 듯한 광경은, 아직도 무리의 변경에서 고래들에게 드러그를 박고 있거나, 혹은 어쩌면 공간이 넉넉하고 편리한 피난처도 몇 군데 마련된 첫 번째 원 안에서 싸움을 벌이고 있을 다른 보트들의 활동을 드러냈다. 그러나 드러그가 박혀 격분한 고래들이 이따금 눈먼 채 원들을 가로질러 이리저리 날뛰는 광경도, 마침내 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐진 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 여느 때보다 유난히 힘세고 민첩한 고래에 작살이 박혔을 때, 말하자면 그 거대한 꼬리 힘줄을 끊거나 상하게 하여 놈의 다리오금을 잘라 버리듯 하는 것이 더러 관례다. 이는 짧은 자루가 달린 절단용 삽을 던져서 하는데, 그 삽에는 다시 끌어당기기 위한 밧줄이 매여 있다. 나중에 우리가 알게 된 바로는, 이 부위에 상처를 입었으나 보아하니 결정적이지는 못했던 어느 고래가, 작살 밧줄의 절반을 끌고 보트에서 떨어져 나갔고, 상처의 엄청난 고통 속에서 이제 사라토가 전투의 외로이 말 탄 무법자 아놀드처럼 빙빙 도는 원들 사이를 휘젓고 다니며, 가는 곳마다 공포를 퍼뜨리고 있었다.\n\n그러나 이 고래의 상처가 아무리 고통스럽고, 어느 모로 보나 충분히 소름 끼치는 광경이긴 했어도, 그놈이 나머지 무리에게 불어넣은 듯한 그 유별난 공포는, 처음엔 그 사이에 가로놓인 거리 때문에 우리에게 가려져 있던 어떤 원인에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그러나 이윽고 우리는 알아챘다. 포경에서나 있을 법한 상상조차 못 할 사고 가운데 하나로, 이 고래가 자기가 끌고 가던 작살 밧줄에 얽혀 버렸던 것이다. 게다가 그놈은 몸에 절단용 삽까지 박은 채 달아나 버렸는데, 그 삽에 매인 밧줄의 자유로운 끝이 꼬리에 감긴 작살 밧줄의 사리 속에 단단히 걸린 채로, 절단용 삽 자체는 살에서 헐거워져 빠져나와 있었다. 그리하여 미칠 지경의 고통에 시달린 그놈은, 이제 물살을 휘저으며 그 유연한 꼬리를 사납게 도리깨질하여, 날카로운 삽을 제 둘레로 마구 휘둘러 대며, 제 동료들을 다치게 하고 죽이고 있었다.\n\n이 무시무시한 광경은 한자리에 멈춰 겁에 질려 있던 무리 전체를 일깨우는 듯했다. 먼저 우리 호수의 가장자리를 이루던 고래들이 조금씩 몰려들어, 마치 멀리서 절반쯤 잦아든 큰 물결에 떠밀리기라도 한 듯 서로를 들이받기 시작했다. 그러더니 호수 자체가 희미하게 일렁이고 부풀어 오르기 시작했다. 물밑의 신방과 보육실은 사라졌다. 더 안쪽의 원에 있던 고래들이 점점 더 좁혀지는 궤도를 그리며 빽빽한 무리로 몰려 헤엄치기 시작했다. 그렇다, 그 긴 고요가 떠나가고 있었다. 이윽고 나직이 다가오는 웅성거림이 들렸다. 그러더니 봄에 큰 강 허드슨이 풀릴 때의 소란스러운 얼음덩이 무더기처럼, 고래 무리 전체가 안쪽 중심을 향해 쏟아져 들어와, 마치 하나의 공통된 산을 이루어 쌓이려는 듯했다. 즉시 스타벅과 퀴케그가 자리를 바꾸었다. 스타벅이 고물을 맡았다.\n\n\"노! 노 저어!\" 그가 키를 움켜쥐며 격렬하게 속삭였다. \"노를 꽉 잡고, 이제 자네들 영혼을 단단히 붙들어! 맙소사, 다들 채비해! 그놈을 밀어내, 퀴케그, 저기 저 고래 말야! 찔러! 쳐! 일어나, 일어나서 그대로 있어! 뛰어, 다들, 저어, 저어! 그놈들 등 따위 상관 말고 긁어 내! 마구 긁어 내!\"\n\n이제 보트는 두 개의 거대한 검은 덩치 사이에 끼이다시피 했으니, 그 긴 몸뚱이들 사이로 좁다란 다르다넬스 해협만이 남아 있었다. 그러나 필사적인 안간힘으로 우리는 마침내 잠시 열린 틈으로 쏜살같이 빠져나갔다. 그러고는 재빨리 노를 저으면서, 동시에 또 다른 출구를 간절히 살폈다. 이와 비슷하게 가까스로 모면한 위기를 숱하게 겪은 끝에, 우리는 마침내 방금 전까지 바깥쪽 원 가운데 하나였던 곳으로 잽싸게 미끄러져 들었는데, 이제는 닥치는 대로 날뛰는 고래들이 모조리 한 중심을 향해 사납게 몰려가며 그곳을 가로지르고 있었다. 이 운 좋은 구원은 퀴케그의 모자를 잃는 값싼 대가로 얻은 것이었으니, 그는 이물에 서서 달아나는 고래들을 찔러 대던 중, 바로 곁에서 한 쌍의 널따란 꼬리지느러미가 별안간 솟구치며 일으킨 소용돌이바람에 모자를 머리에서 말끔히 빼앗기고 말았다.\n\n이제 온 천지의 동요가 아무리 어수선하고 무질서했어도, 그것은 머지않아 어떤 체계적인 움직임으로 보이는 것으로 가닥이 잡혔다. 마침내 하나의 빽빽한 덩어리로 뭉친 고래들은, 이내 더욱 빨라진 속도로 앞을 향한 도주를 재개했기 때문이다. 더 이상의 추격은 소용없었다. 그러나 보트들은 여전히 그 자취에 남아, 뒤에 처져 떨어졌을지 모를 드러그 박힌 고래들을 거두어들이고, 아울러 플라스크(Flask)가 죽여 깃대로 표시해 둔 한 마리를 확보하려 했다. 깃대 표지(waif)란 작은 기를 단 장대로, 보트마다 두세 개씩 싣고 다닌다. 그리고 사냥감을 추가로 잡을 수 있을 때, 죽은 고래의 떠 있는 몸뚱이에 똑바로 꽂아 두어, 바다 위에서 그 자리를 표시하는 동시에, 행여 다른 배의 보트들이 다가올 경우 먼저 차지했다는 표지로 삼는 것이다.\n\n이번 보트 출동의 결과는, 포경에서 전해 오는 저 슬기로운 격언, 곧 고래가 많을수록 잡히는 고래는 적다는 말을 다소나마 잘 보여 주었다. 드러그가 박힌 그 모든 고래 가운데 사로잡은 것은 단 한 마리뿐이었다. 나머지는 그때만큼은 용케 달아났으나, 앞으로 보게 되겠지만, 결국 피쿼드호가 아닌 다른 배에 잡히고 말 운명이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 88. Schools and Schoolmasters.\n\n앞 장에서는 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 거대한 무리, 곧 떼에 관해 이야기했고, 그토록 어마어마한 집결을 불러일으키는 그럴듯한 원인 또한 그때 함께 밝혀 두었다.\n\n그런데 그처럼 거대한 무리가 이따금 마주쳐지기는 하나, 이미 보았다시피 오늘날에도 따로 떨어진 작은 무리가 더러 관찰되곤 하는데, 그 한 무리는 스무 마리에서 쉰 마리에 이르는 개체들을 거느린다. 이런 무리는 스쿨(school)이라 불린다. 그것들은 대체로 두 종류로 나뉜다. 거의 전부가 암컷으로 이루어진 무리와, 젊고 혈기왕성한 수컷, 곧 흔히 황소라 부르는 놈들만으로 짜인 무리다.\n\n암컷 무리를 거느리고 기사처럼 호위하는 자리에는, 다 자란 거구이되 늙지는 않은 수컷 한 마리가 어김없이 보인다. 무슨 위험이라도 닥치면 이 녀석은 맨 뒤로 처져 자기 부인들의 도주를 엄호함으로써 그 용맹을 드러낸다. 사실 이 신사는 호사스러운 오스만 군주여서, 물의 세계를 두루 헤엄쳐 다니되 하렘의 온갖 위안과 애정에 사방으로 둘러싸여 있다. 이 오스만 군주와 그 첩들 사이의 대비는 자못 두드러진다. 군주는 늘 가장 거대한 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 크기를 자랑하지만, 부인들은 다 자란다 한들 평균 크기의 수컷에 비하면 삼분의 일에 지나지 않기 때문이다. 사실 이들은 상대적으로 가냘파서, 감히 말하건대 허리둘레가 여섯 야드를 넘지는 않을 것이다. 그렇지만 전체적으로 보아 그들이 통통한 몸매를 타고날 자격을 지녔다는 사실만은 부인할 수 없다.\n\n이 하렘과 그 주인이 나른하게 어슬렁거리는 모습을 지켜보는 것은 무척 흥미롭다. 멋쟁이들이 그러하듯, 그들은 여유롭게 변화를 좇아 끊임없이 옮겨 다닌다. 그대는 적도의 먹이철이 한창 무르익을 무렵 그들을 적도선(Line) 위에서 만나게 되는데, 아마 그들은 막 북쪽 바다에서 여름을 보내고 돌아온 참이어서, 여름이 안기는 온갖 불쾌한 권태와 더위를 따돌린 셈이다. 적도의 산책로를 한동안 어슬렁어슬렁 오르내리고 나면, 그들은 그곳의 서늘한 계절을 미리 내다보며 동방의 바다로 떠나, 한 해의 또 다른 극단의 기온마저 피해 버린다.\n\n이런 여정 가운데 하나를 평온하게 나아가다가 무언가 낯설고 수상쩍은 광경이 보이면, 우리의 군주 고래는 그 흥미로운 가족에게서 경계의 눈을 떼지 않는다. 만약 분별없이 건방진 젊은 리바이어던이 그쪽으로 다가와 감히 부인들 가운데 하나에게 은밀히 바짝 접근하기라도 하면, 이 파샤가 얼마나 어마어마한 분노로 그놈을 덮쳐 쫓아 버리는지 모른다! 그놈처럼 절개 없는 젊은 난봉꾼이 가정의 행복이라는 신성한 영역을 침범하도록 허락된다면, 그야말로 큰일이 아니겠는가. 그러나 파샤가 무슨 짓을 하든, 가장 악명 높은 호색한을 제 잠자리에서 몰아낼 수는 없으니, 아아! 모든 물고기는 한데 어울려 자기 때문이다. 뭍에서 부인들이 종종 연적들 사이에 더없이 끔찍한 결투를 일으키듯, 고래들 또한 마찬가지여서, 더러는 죽음을 건 싸움에 이르는데 그 모든 것이 사랑 때문이다. 그들은 길쭉한 아래턱으로 칼싸움을 벌이고, 때로는 그것을 서로 맞물어 가며, 뿔을 맞대고 얽혀 다투는 큰사슴처럼 우위를 차지하려 안간힘을 쓴다. 이런 충돌의 깊은 흉터를 지닌 채 붙잡히는 놈도 적지 않으니 — 골이 파인 머리, 부러진 이빨, 가장자리가 너덜너덜한 지느러미, 그리고 더러는 비틀려 빠진 입을 지니고 있다.\n\n그러나 가정의 행복을 침범하려던 자가 하렘 군주의 첫 돌격에 줄행랑을 친다고 가정해 보면, 그때 그 군주를 지켜보는 것이 자못 재미있다. 그는 다시금 그 거대한 몸뚱이를 부인들 사이로 슬며시 들이밀고 한동안 그 속에서 즐기는데, 여전히 젊은 호색한이 애를 태우며 가까이 머무를 만한 거리에 있으니, 마치 경건한 솔로몬이 천 명의 첩들 사이에서 독실하게 예배를 드리는 듯하다. 시야에 다른 고래들이 있다면, 어부들은 좀처럼 이런 대(大) 술탄들을 뒤쫓지 않는다. 이 대 술탄들은 제 힘을 너무 헤프게 써 버려, 그만큼 기름기가 적기 때문이다. 그놈들이 낳은 아들딸로 말하자면, 글쎄, 그 아들딸들은 제 앞가림을 스스로 해야 한다. 적어도 어미의 도움만 받으면서 말이다. 굳이 이름을 댈 수도 있는 다른 잡식성의 떠돌이 정부(情夫)들이 그러하듯, 우리의 군주 고래는 규방(閨房)에는 한껏 취미가 있을지언정 육아실에는 도무지 관심이 없으니, 위대한 여행자인 그는 이름 모를 제 새끼들을 온 세상에 흩뿌려 놓는 셈이고, 그래서 새끼마다 이국의 산물이 된다. 그렇지만 때가 무르익어 젊음의 열정이 시들고, 세월과 우울이 쌓이며, 사색이 그 엄숙한 멈춤을 안기고, 한마디로 물릴 대로 물린 이 술탄에게 전반적인 권태가 찾아들면, 그때는 안락과 덕에 대한 사랑이 처녀들에 대한 사랑을 밀어내고, 우리의 오스만 군주는 무력하고 뉘우치며 훈계나 늘어놓는 인생의 단계로 접어들어, 하렘을 버리고 해산시키며, 본받을 만하되 시무룩한 늙은 영혼이 되어, 자오선과 위선 사이를 홀로 떠돌면서 기도를 올리고, 젊은 리바이어던마다 그 색욕의 과오를 경계하라 타이르며 다니는 것이다.\n\n자, 고래들의 하렘이 어부들 사이에서 스쿨이라 불리듯, 그 스쿨의 주인이자 우두머리는 학술적으로 교장(schoolmaster)이라 일컬어진다. 그러니 그가 제 스스로 학교에 다닌 뒤에, 거기서 배운 것이 아니라 도리어 그 어리석음을 밖으로 나가 가르쳐 댄다는 것은, 감탄스러우리만치 풍자적이긴 해도 그 명칭에 꼭 들어맞지는 않는다. 교장이라는 그 칭호는 하렘 자체에 붙여진 이름에서 자연스레 비롯된 듯이 보이지만, 더러는 이런 오스만 고래에게 처음 그런 칭호를 붙인 자가 비도크(Vidocq)의 회고록을 읽고서, 그 유명한 프랑스인이 젊은 시절 어떤 시골 교장이었는지, 또 그가 일부 제자들에게 은밀히 가르쳤다는 그 수상한 교습이 어떤 성질의 것이었는지 알게 된 것이 틀림없다고 짐작하기도 한다.\n\n교장 고래가 나이 들어 가면서 빠져드는 바로 그 은둔과 고립은, 늙은 향유고래라면 누구에게나 해당하는 진실이다. 거의 예외 없이, 외로운 고래 — 홀로 떠도는 리바이어던을 그렇게 부른다 — 는 늙은 놈으로 드러난다. 이끼 낀 잿빛 수염을 기른 점잖은 대니얼 분처럼, 그는 자연 그 자체 말고는 제 곁에 아무도 두지 않으려 하니, 물의 황야에서 그 자연을 아내로 맞아들인다. 그토록 변덕스러운 비밀을 많이 품고 있긴 해도, 자연이야말로 더없이 좋은 아내인 것이다.\n\n앞서 말한바 젊고 혈기왕성한 수컷들만으로 짜인 무리는 하렘 무리와 뚜렷한 대조를 이룬다. 저 암컷 고래들이 특징적으로 겁이 많은 반면, 젊은 수컷들, 곧 그들이 마흔 배럴짜리 황소라 부르는 놈들은 모든 리바이어던 가운데 단연 가장 호전적이고, 마주치기에 가장 위험하다고 정평이 나 있기 때문이다. 다만 이따금 마주치는 그 경이로운 잿빛 머리에 희끗희끗한 늙은 고래들만은 예외인데, 이놈들은 형벌 같은 통풍에 시달려 약이 오른 사나운 악귀들처럼 그대에게 덤벼들 것이다.\n\n마흔 배럴짜리 황소의 무리는 하렘 무리보다 규모가 더 크다. 젊은 대학생 패거리처럼, 그들은 싸움과 장난과 짓궂음으로 가득 차서, 어찌나 무모하고 떠들썩한 기세로 온 세상을 뒹굴며 돌아다니는지, 분별 있는 보험업자라면 예일이나 하버드의 난봉꾼 녀석을 인수하지 않으려는 것과 마찬가지로 그놈들의 보험을 들어 주려 하지 않을 것이다. 그렇지만 그들은 이내 이런 난폭함을 내려놓고, 거의 사분의 삼쯤 자라면 무리를 흩어, 저마다 정착지를, 다시 말해 하렘을 찾아 떠난다.\n\n수컷 무리와 암컷 무리 사이의 또 다른 차이점은 양성(兩性)의 성질을 한층 더 잘 드러낸다. 그대가 마흔 배럴짜리 황소 한 마리를 작살로 찔렀다고 치자 — 가엾은 녀석! 그 동무들은 모두 그를 버리고 가 버린다. 그러나 하렘 무리의 일원을 찔러 보라, 그 동료들은 온갖 걱정의 기색을 띠고 그녀 주위를 헤엄쳐 돌며, 때로는 너무 가까이, 너무 오래도록 머뭇거리다가 저들 자신마저 먹잇감으로 떨어지고 만다.\n\n# CHAPTER 89. Fast-Fish and Loose-Fish.\n\n바로 앞 장에서 부표(浮標)와 부표 장대를 언급한 일은, 고래잡이의 법과 규정에 관해 어느 정도 설명할 것을 요구하는데, 그 부표야말로 이러한 법규의 위대한 상징이요 표지로 여겨질 만하기 때문이다.\n\n여러 척의 배가 무리를 지어 항해하다 보면, 한 배가 작살을 박은 고래가 달아났다가 결국 다른 배에 죽임을 당해 잡히는 일이 흔히 일어난다. 그리고 여기에는 이 하나의 큰 특징을 공유하는 자잘한 우발적 상황들이 여럿 간접적으로 포함되어 있다. 예컨대, 지치고 위태로운 추격 끝에 고래 한 마리를 잡았으나 사나운 폭풍 탓에 그 몸뚱이가 배에서 풀려나 멀리 바람 아래쪽으로 떠내려갔다가, 두 번째 포경선이 그것을 다시 손에 넣어, 잔잔한 바다에서 목숨도 밧줄도 걸지 않고 느긋하게 뱃전으로 끌고 오는 경우가 있다. 이리하여 모든 경우에 적용되는, 성문이든 불문이든 보편적이고 누구도 이의를 달 수 없는 어떤 법이 없다면, 어부들 사이에서는 가장 성가시고 격렬한 분쟁이 종종 일어나고 말 것이다.\n\n입법 제정에 의해 공인된 유일한 정식 포경 법전은 아마도 네덜란드의 것뿐이었으리라. 그것은 서기 1695년에 네덜란드 연방의회에서 공포되었다. 그러나 다른 어떤 나라도 성문화된 포경법을 가진 적이 없었음에도, 미국 어부들은 이 문제에 있어 스스로 입법자가 되고 법률가가 되어 왔다. 그들은 간결한 포괄성에 있어 유스티니아누스의 학설휘찬(學說彙纂)과 남의 일에 참견하는 짓을 금하기 위한 중국인 협회의 내규(內規)마저 능가하는 체계를 마련해 놓았다. 그렇다, 이 법들은 어찌나 작은지 앤 여왕 시절의 파딩 동전이나 작살의 미늘에 새겨 목에 걸고 다닐 수도 있을 정도다.\n\n하나. 잡힌 고래(Fast-Fish)는 그것을 붙잡고 있는 측에 속한다.\n\n둘. 놓친 고래(Loose-Fish)는 누구든 먼저 잡는 자의 정당한 사냥감이다.\n\n그러나 이 빼어난 법전에 말썽을 일으키는 것은 다름 아닌 그 감탄스러운 간결함이니, 그것을 풀어 설명하자면 방대한 분량의 주해(註解)가 필요해지는 까닭이다.\n\n첫째. 잡힌 고래란 무엇인가? 살아 있든 죽어 있든, 고래는 그것을 점유한 자나 자들이 통제할 수 있는 어떤 매개물—돛대든, 노든, 9인치 굵기의 밧줄이든, 전신줄이든, 거미줄 한 가닥이든 모두 마찬가지다—을 통해 점유 중인 배나 보트와 이어져 있을 때, 전문적인 의미에서 '잡힌' 것이다. 마찬가지로 고래가 부표나 그 밖에 공인된 소유의 표지를 달고 있을 때에도 전문적으로 '잡힌' 것이 되는데, 다만 그것에 부표를 매단 측이 언제든 그것을 뱃전으로 끌고 올 수 있는 능력과 그렇게 하려는 의도를 분명히 드러내는 한에서다.\n\n이것들은 학술적인 주해다. 그러나 고래잡이들 자신의 주해는 때로 거친 말과 더 거친 주먹질로 이루어지니—주먹으로 펴 보이는 코크의 리틀턴 주석서인 셈이다. 사실 더 강직하고 명예로운 고래잡이들 사이에서는, 한 측이 다른 측에 의해 앞서 쫓기거나 죽임을 당한 고래의 소유권을 주장하는 것이 터무니없는 도덕적 불의가 될 만한 특수한 경우에 대해 늘 참작이 이루어진다. 그러나 그만큼 양심적이지 않은 자들도 결코 적지 않다.\n\n오십 년쯤 전 영국에서 고래 횡령에 관한 진기한 소송 한 건이 다투어졌는데, 거기서 원고들은 다음과 같이 진술했다. 즉 북쪽 바다에서 고래 한 마리를 호되게 추격한 끝에, 실제로 그들(원고들)이 그 고래에게 작살을 박는 데 성공하였으나, 마침내 목숨이 위태로워져 밧줄뿐 아니라 보트마저 버릴 수밖에 없었다는 것이다. 그 후 피고들(다른 배의 선원들)이 그 고래를 따라잡아 작살을 박고 죽이고 붙잡아, 마침내 원고들의 바로 눈앞에서 그것을 제 것으로 차지해 버렸다. 그리고 그 피고들이 항의를 받자, 그들의 선장은 원고들의 면전에서 손가락을 튕기며, 자기가 저지른 행위에 대한 송영(頌詠)으로서 이제 붙잡힐 당시 고래에 매달려 있던 그들의 밧줄과 작살과 보트마저 그대로 차지하겠노라고 큰소리쳤다. 그리하여 원고들은 이제 자기네 고래와 밧줄과 작살과 보트의 값을 되찾고자 소송을 제기한 것이었다.\n\n어스킨 씨가 피고들의 변호인이었고, 엘런버러 경이 재판관이었다. 변론을 펴는 과정에서 재치 넘치는 어스킨은 최근의 한 간통(姦通) 소송을 언급하며 자기 주장을 예증해 나갔다. 그 사건에서는 한 신사가 아내의 못된 성정을 다잡으려 헛되이 애쓰다가 결국 인생의 바다 위에 그녀를 버렸는데, 세월이 흐르면서 그 일을 뉘우치고는 그녀에 대한 소유권을 되찾으려 소송을 걸었다는 것이다. 어스킨은 그 사건에서 반대편에 서 있었다. 그러면서 그는 이렇게 말하여 자기 입장을 뒷받침했다. 즉 그 신사가 애초에 그 부인에게 작살을 박아 한때 그녀를 붙잡고 있었으나, 그녀가 격렬하게 날뛰는 못된 성정의 엄청난 압박 때문에 결국 그녀를 버렸다는 것, 그러나 어쨌든 버린 것은 버린 것이므로 그녀는 놓친 고래가 되었고, 따라서 뒤이은 또 다른 신사가 그녀에게 다시 작살을 박았을 때, 그 부인은 그녀의 몸에 박힌 채 발견된 작살이 무엇이든 그것과 함께 뒤이은 그 신사의 소유물이 되었다는 것이다.\n\n그리하여 어스킨은 지금의 이 사건에서 고래의 사례와 부인의 사례가 서로를 예증해 준다고 주장했다.\n\n이러한 변론과 그에 대한 반대 변론을 정식으로 청취한 뒤, 그 매우 학식 깊은 재판관은 다음과 같이 명확한 표현으로 판결을 내렸다. 즉—보트에 관해서는, 원고들이 단지 목숨을 구하고자 그것을 버렸을 뿐이므로 그것을 원고들에게 인도한다. 그러나 다툼의 대상인 고래와 작살과 밧줄에 관해서는, 그것들이 피고들에게 속한다. 고래는 최종적으로 붙잡힐 당시 놓친 고래였기 때문이고, 작살과 밧줄은 그 고래가 그것들을 달고 달아났을 때 그것(고래)이 그 물품들에 대한 소유권을 획득하였으며, 따라서 그 후에 그 고래를 잡은 자라면 누구든 그것들에 대한 권리를 갖기 때문이다. 그런데 피고들이 그 후에 그 고래를 잡았으니, 그러므로 앞서 말한 물품들은 그들의 것이라는 것이었다.\n\n이 매우 학식 깊은 재판관의 판결을 보고 보통 사람이라면 어쩌면 이의를 제기할지도 모른다. 그러나 이 문제의 근본 암반까지 파고들어 보면, 앞서 인용한 한 쌍의 포경법에 정해져 있고 위 사건에서 엘런버러 경이 적용하고 해명한 두 가지 위대한 원리—내가 말하건대, 잡힌 고래와 놓친 고래에 관한 이 두 법은—곰곰이 생각해 보면 모든 인간 법학의 기초임이 드러날 것이다. 왜냐하면 정교하게 조각된 복잡한 무늬에도 불구하고, 법의 신전은 블레셋 사람들의 신전과 마찬가지로 단 두 개의 기둥 위에 서 있을 뿐이기 때문이다.\n\n누구나 입에 달고 사는 말이 있지 않은가, 점유는 법의 절반이라고. 다시 말해 그 물건이 어떻게 점유에 들어왔는지는 상관없다는 것이다. 그러나 점유가 종종 법의 전부일 때도 있다. 러시아 농노와 공화국 노예들의 근육과 영혼이 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이며, 그것에 관한 한 점유가 법의 전부가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 탐욕스러운 지주에게 과부의 마지막 한 푼은 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 저기 발각되지 않은 악당의 대리석 저택이 문패를 부표 삼아 달고 있으니, 그것이 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 중개인 모르데카이가 가련한 파산자 우비곤의 가족을 굶주림에서 구하려는 대출에 대해 그 가련한 우비곤에게서 뜯어내는 그 파멸적인 할인이, 그 파멸적인 할인이 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 영혼을 구원한다는 대주교가 수십만 허리 꺾인 노동자들(모두 영혼구원 대주교의 도움 따위 없이도 천국이 확실한 이들)의 빈약한 빵과 치즈에서 빼앗아 가는 10만 파운드의 수입이, 그 둥글둥글한 10만 파운드가 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 던더 공작이 세습으로 물려받은 마을과 촌락들이 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 저 가공할 작살잡이 존 불에게 가련한 아일랜드는 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 저 사도적인 창잡이 브러더 조너선에게 텍사스는 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 그리고 이 모든 것에 관하여 점유가 법의 전부가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?\n\n그러나 잡힌 고래의 교리가 제법 널리 적용된다면, 그와 한 핏줄인 놓친 고래의 교리는 한층 더 광범위하게 적용된다. 그것은 국제적으로 또 보편적으로 적용되는 것이다.\n\n1492년의 아메리카는 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이었으며, 콜럼버스는 자기 임금과 왕비를 위해 그것에 부표를 매다는 셈으로 거기에 스페인 깃발을 꽂은 것이 아니고 무엇이었겠는가? 차르에게 폴란드는 무엇이었는가? 튀르크에게 그리스는 무엇이었는가? 영국에게 인도는 무엇이었는가? 결국 미합중국에게 멕시코는 무엇이 될 것인가? 모두 놓친 고래다.\n\n인간의 권리와 세계의 자유가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 모든 인간의 정신과 견해가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 그것들 안에 든 종교적 신앙의 원리가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 허세 부리며 말을 밀수하는 자들에게 사상가들의 사유가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 이 거대한 지구 자체가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가? 그리고 독자여, 그대 또한 놓친 고래이자 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?\n\n# CHAPTER 90. Heads or Tails.\n\n“De balena vero sufficit, si rex habeat caput, et regina caudam.”\n\n브랙턴(Bracton), 제3권 제3장.\n이는 잉글랜드 법전에서 따온 라틴어로, 그 맥락과 함께 풀이하면, 그 땅의 해안에서 누군가에게 잡힌 모든 고래에 대해, 명예 대(大)작살잡이인 국왕은 그 머리를 차지해야 하고, 왕비에게는 정중히 그 꼬리를 진상해야 한다는 뜻이다. 고래에 있어 이런 식의 분할은 사과를 반으로 쪼개는 것과 매우 흡사하다. 중간에 남는 부분이라곤 없는 것이다. 그런데 이 법은 일부 수정된 형태로 오늘날까지도 잉글랜드에서 효력을 지니고 있다. 또한 그것은 여러 면에서 잡힌 고래와 놓친 고래(Fast and Loose-Fish)에 관한 일반 법리에 비추어 기묘한 변칙을 이루므로, 여기서는 별도의 장으로 다루기로 한다. 이는 잉글랜드 철도가 왕족의 편의를 위해 특별히 비워 둔 별도 객차의 비용을 기꺼이 부담하게 만드는 것과 똑같은 정중한 원칙에 따른 것이다. 우선, 앞서 말한 법이 여전히 효력을 지니고 있다는 사실을 기묘하게 입증하기 위해, 지난 이태 사이에 일어난 한 가지 사건을 여러분 앞에 펼쳐 보이겠다.\n\n도버나 샌드위치, 혹은 친크 포츠(Cinque Ports) 가운데 어느 항구의 정직한 뱃사람 몇몇이, 처음에 뭍에서 멀리 발견한 멋진 고래 한 마리를 힘겨운 추격 끝에 죽여 해변에 끌어올리는 데 성공했던 모양이다. 그런데 친크 포츠는 부분적으로, 혹은 어떤 식으로든, 로드 워든(Lord Warden)이라 불리는 일종의 경관 또는 관리의 관할 아래 있다. 내가 알기로 이 직책은 왕실로부터 직접 받는 것이어서, 친크 포츠 영토에 딸린 온갖 왕실 수입이 양도를 통해 그의 몫이 된다. 어떤 저술가들은 이 직책을 한직(閑職)이라 부른다. 그러나 그렇지 않다. 로드 워든은 때로 자신의 부수입을 슬쩍 챙기는 일에 바삐 매달려 있기 때문이며, 그 수입이 그의 것이 되는 까닭도 다름 아닌 그 슬쩍 챙기는 행위 덕분인 것이다.\n\n자, 햇볕에 그을린 이 가련한 뱃사람들이 맨발로, 미꾸라지처럼 미끈한 다리 위로 바지를 높이 걷어 올린 채, 그 살진 물고기를 지친 몸으로 높직이 끌어내 물기 없는 곳에 올려놓고서, 귀한 기름과 뼈로 150파운드는 너끈히 벌리라 자신하며, 각자의 몫을 밑천 삼아 아내와 더불어 진귀한 차를 홀짝이고 친구들과 좋은 맥주를 들이켜는 환상에 잠겨 있을 때였다. 그때 매우 학식 깊고 더없이 기독교적이며 자비로운 신사 하나가 블랙스톤(Blackstone) 법서 한 권을 옆구리에 끼고 성큼성큼 다가오더니, 그것을 고래 머리 위에 척 올려놓고는 이렇게 말했다. \"손 떼시오! 여러분, 이 물고기는 잡힌 고기요. 나는 이것을 로드 워든의 소유로 압류하겠소.\" 이에 가련한 뱃사람들은 더없이 잉글랜드인다운, 공손한 경악에 사로잡혀 무어라 말해야 좋을지 몰라, 다들 머리를 벅벅 긁어 대기 시작했다. 그러는 내내 고래에서 그 낯선 사내에게로 침울한 눈길을 옮길 따름이었다. 그러나 그런다고 사정이 나아지거나, 블랙스톤 법서를 든 그 학식 깊은 신사의 굳은 마음이 조금이라도 누그러질 리는 없었다. 마침내 그들 가운데 하나가 한참을 머릿속에서 생각을 더듬은 끝에 용기를 내어 입을 열었다.\n\n\"실례지만 나리, 로드 워든이란 분이 누구신지요?\"\n\n\"공작이시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 공작님은 이 물고기를 잡는 데 아무 관련이 없지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"이건 그분 것이오.\"\n\"우리는 큰 고생과 위험을 무릅쓰고, 적잖은 비용까지 들였는데, 그게 전부 공작님 이득으로 돌아가고, 우리는 고생한 대가로 손에 잡힌 물집밖에 못 얻는단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"이건 그분 것이오.\"\n\n\"공작님이 그토록 가난하셔서 이런 막다른 방식으로 생계를 꾸리셔야 한단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"이건 그분 것이오.\"\n\n\"저는 이 고래에서 제 몫을 나눠 받아 자리보전한 늙은 어머니를 좀 편케 해 드리려 했습니다만.\"\n\n\"이건 그분 것이오.\"\n\n\"공작님께서 사분의 일이나 절반쯤으로 만족하시진 않을까요?\"\n\n\"이건 그분 것이오.\"\n\n한마디로, 고래는 압류되어 팔렸고, 그 돈은 웰링턴 공작 각하의 손에 들어갔다. 어떤 특정한 시각으로 본다면, 이 사건이 형편상 어쩌면 아주 약간은 다소 가혹한 경우로 여겨질 한 가닥 가능성이라도 있지 않을까 생각한 그 고장의 정직한 목사 하나가, 공작 각하께 정중히 서신을 띄워 그 불운한 뱃사람들의 처지를 충분히 헤아려 주시기를 간청했다. 이에 공작 각하는 (두 편지 모두 공개되었다) 요지인즉, 자신은 이미 그 점을 헤아렸으며, 돈도 이미 받았으니, 앞으로 그(목사)께서 남의 일에 참견하기를 삼가 주신다면 고맙겠다고 답했다. 이자가 바로 세 왕국의 모퉁이마다 버티고 서서, 사방으로 거지들에게서 적선을 강요하는, 아직도 호전적인 그 늙은이란 말인가?\n\n이 경우 고래에 대한 공작의 주장된 권리가 군주로부터 위임받은 것임은 쉽게 알 수 있다. 그렇다면 우리는 군주가 본래 무슨 원리에 따라 그 권리를 부여받았는지 마땅히 따져 보아야 한다. 법 자체는 이미 앞에서 밝혔다. 그러나 플라우든(Plowdon)은 그 이유를 우리에게 일러 준다. 플라우든이 말하기를, 그렇게 잡힌 고래는 국왕과 왕비에게 속하는데, \"그 탁월함이 워낙 빼어나기 때문\"이라 한다. 그리고 가장 견실한 주석가들은 이런 문제에서 이것을 줄곧 설득력 있는 논거로 받들어 왔다.\n\n그런데 어찌하여 국왕은 머리를 차지하고, 왕비는 꼬리를 차지하는가? 법률가들이여, 그 까닭을 대 보라!\n\n\"왕비 금(Queen-Gold)\", 곧 왕비 용돈에 관한 논고에서, 윌리엄 프린(William Prynne)이라는 옛 왕좌 법정(King's Bench)의 한 저술가는 이렇게 논한다. \"꼬리는 왕비의 것이니, 이는 왕비의 의상실에 고래수염이 채워지게 하기 위함이다.\" 이 글이 쓰인 때는 그린란드고래, 곧 참고래의 검고 유연한 뼈가 귀부인들의 코르셋에 널리 쓰이던 시절이었다. 그러나 정작 그 뼈는 꼬리에 있는 것이 아니라 머리에 있으니, 프린처럼 명민한 법률가에게는 딱한 착오가 아닐 수 없다. 한데 왕비가 꼬리를 진상받을 인어란 말인가? 어쩌면 여기에 어떤 우의적 의미가 숨어 있는지도 모른다.\n\n잉글랜드 법학 저술가들이 그렇게 부르는 왕실 어류가 둘 있으니, 곧 고래와 철갑상어다. 둘 다 일정한 제한 아래 왕실 재산이며, 명목상 왕실 통상 세입의 열 번째 항목을 채워 준다. 다른 어느 저술가가 이 문제를 넌지시 언급했는지는 나도 알지 못한다. 그러나 미루어 짐작건대, 철갑상어 역시 고래와 똑같은 방식으로 나뉘어, 국왕이 그 물고기 특유의 매우 단단하고 탄력 있는 머리를 차지하게 되는 듯하다. 이를 상징적으로 본다면, 어쩌면 둘 사이에 어떤 기질의 닮음이 있으리라는 가정에 익살스레 근거한 것일지도 모른다. 그러니 만물에는, 심지어 법에조차도, 나름의 이치가 깃들어 있는 모양이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 91. The Pequod Meets The Rose-Bud.\n\n\"이 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 배 속을 헤집어 용연향을 캐려 한들 헛수고였으니, 그 견딜 수 없는 악취가 탐색조차 허락하지 않았기 때문이다.\" —토머스 브라운 경(Sir T. Browne), V.E.\n\n앞서 이야기한 마지막 고래잡이 장면이 있고 한두 주가 지난 어느 무렵이었다. 우리는 졸음에 겨운 듯 안개 자욱한 한낮의 바다 위를 느릿느릿 항해하고 있었는데, 그때 피쿼드호(Pequod) 갑판 위의 수많은 코들이 돛대 꼭대기에 있는 세 쌍의 눈보다 더 예리한 발견자임이 드러났다. 바다에서 야릇하고 그다지 유쾌하지 않은 냄새가 풍겨 온 것이다.\n\n\"내 뭔가 걸고 장담하건대,\" 스터브(Stubb)가 말했다. \"이 근방 어딘가에 며칠 전 우리가 약물 작살로 건드렸던 그 고래들 몇 마리가 있을 거요. 머잖아 배를 까뒤집고 떠오르리라 짐작은 했지.\"\n\n이윽고 앞쪽의 안개가 옆으로 미끄러지듯 갈라졌다. 그러자 멀리 한 척의 배가 누워 있는 것이 보였는데, 둘둘 말려 올라간 돛으로 보아 어떤 고래 한 마리가 그 뱃전에 매여 있는 것이 분명했다. 우리가 점점 가까이 미끄러져 가자, 그 낯선 배는 돛대 꼭대기에 프랑스 국기를 내걸고 있었다. 그리고 그 주위를 빙빙 돌며 맴돌고 내리꽂히는 독수리 같은 바닷새 떼의 소용돌이치는 구름으로 보아, 뱃전에 매인 그 고래는 어부들이 이른바 썩은 고래라 부르는 것이 분명했다. 다시 말해 바다에서 아무에게도 시달리지 않고 죽어, 임자 없는 시체로 떠다니던 고래였다. 그런 덩어리가 얼마나 역겨운 냄새를 내뿜을지는 충분히 짐작할 수 있으니, 산 자들이 죽은 자들을 미처 묻지 못할 만큼 역병에 휩싸인 아시리아의 도시보다도 더 고약하다. 실로 어떤 이들은 이를 도저히 견딜 수 없게 여긴 나머지, 그 어떤 탐욕으로도 그 곁에 배를 댈 마음을 먹게 할 수 없을 정도다. 그럼에도 여전히 그 일을 마다하지 않는 자들이 있으니, 그런 고래에서 얻는 기름이 품질이 매우 떨어지고 결코 장미 향유 같은 것은 아니라는 사실에도 아랑곳하지 않는다.\n\n스러져 가는 미풍을 타고 더욱 가까이 다가가 보니, 그 프랑스 배에는 두 번째 고래가 또 뱃전에 매여 있었다. 그리고 이 두 번째 고래는 첫 번째 것보다 한층 더 꽃다발 같은 냄새를 풍기는 듯했다. 사실, 그것은 일종의 어마어마한 소화 불량, 곧 위장 장애로 바싹 말라 죽어 가는 듯한 저 알 수 없는 고래들 가운데 하나임이 드러났다. 그런 고래는 죽은 몸뚱이에 기름이라 할 만한 것이 거의 한 푼도 남지 않은 빈털터리 신세다. 그럼에도 불구하고, 마땅한 대목에 이르면 우리는 보게 될 것이다. 아무리 노련한 어부라도 썩은 고래 일반은 피한다 할지언정, 이런 고래 앞에서는 결코 코를 돌리지 않는다는 사실을.\n\n피쿼드호는 어느덧 그 낯선 배에 바싹 가까이 다가가 있었기에, 스터브는 자기 고래 절단용 삽자루가 이 고래들 중 한 마리의 꼬리에 둘둘 감긴 밧줄에 얽혀 있는 것을 알아보았노라고 장담했다.\n\n\"저기 보시오, 어여쁜 친구로군,\" 그가 뱃머리에 서서 놀리듯 껄껄 웃었다. \"당신네들에게 보여 줄 자칼이 여기 있소이다! 나는 잘 알지, 이 프랑스 개구리 놈들이 고래잡이에서는 한낱 가련한 얼간이들이라는 걸. 더러는 부서지는 파도를 향유고래 물줄기로 잘못 알고 보트를 내리기도 하고, 또 더러는 얻을 기름이라곤 선장 등잔 심지 적실 만큼도 못 되리라는 걸 뻔히 알면서도, 짐칸에 수지 양초 상자와 심지 가위 궤짝을 가득 싣고 항구를 떠나기도 하지. 그래, 우리 모두 이런 사정을 잘 알고 있잖나. 한데 보시오, 여기 우리가 남긴 찌꺼기로 만족하는 개구리 한 마리가 있구려, 저 약물 먹은 고래 말이오. 그래, 게다가 저쪽에 매단 또 한 마리, 그 귀하신 물고기의 메마른 뼈다귀나 긁어 대는 걸로도 만족한다지. 가련한 얼간이! 내 말하건대, 누가 모자라도 한 바퀴 돌려서 갸륵한 자선의 마음으로 그에게 기름이나 조금 선물해 줍시다. 저 약물 먹은 고래에서 얻을 기름이라곤 감옥에서 태우기에도 부적합하고, 아니, 사형수 감방에서도 못 쓸 물건일 테니. 그리고 저 다른 고래로 말할 것 같으면, 그가 저 뼈 무더기에서 얻을 기름보다, 차라리 우리 배 돛대 셋을 잘게 쪼개 솥에 끓여 짜내는 편이 더 많은 기름을 얻으리라 장담하겠소. 한데 생각해 보니, 그 안에는 기름보다 훨씬 값진 무언가가 들어 있을지도 모르겠군. 그래, 용연향 말이오. 우리 영감님이 그 생각을 해 보셨을까 궁금하군. 한번 시험해 볼 만하지. 그래, 나는 찬성이오.\" 그러고는 이렇게 말하며 그는 뒷갑판을 향해 발길을 옮겼다.\n\n이 무렵 희미하던 바람마저 완전히 잦아들어, 좋든 싫든 피쿼드호는 이제 그 냄새에 완전히 갇혀 버린 꼴이 되었으니, 다시 바람이 일어나 주는 것 말고는 빠져나갈 가망이 없었다. 선실에서 나온 스터브는 이제 자기 보트의 선원들을 불러 모아, 그 낯선 배를 향해 노를 저어 나아갔다. 뱃머리를 가로질러 지나가던 그는, 프랑스 특유의 별난 취향에 걸맞게 그 배 이물 기둥의 윗부분이 거대하게 늘어진 줄기 모양으로 깎여 초록빛으로 칠해져 있고, 가시 대신 여기저기 구리 못이 삐죽삐죽 솟아 있으며, 그 전체가 선명한 붉은빛으로 좌우 대칭을 이루며 오므린 꽃봉오리 모양으로 끝맺어진 것을 보았다. 그 뱃머리 명판에는 커다란 금박 글자로 \"부통 드 로즈(Bouton de Rose)\" — 곧 장미 봉오리, 또는 장미꽃 봉오리라 쓰여 있었으니, 이것이 바로 이 향기로운 배의 낭만적인 이름이었다.\n\n스터브는 그 글귀에서 \"부통(Bouton)\" 부분은 알아듣지 못했으나, \"로즈(rose)\"라는 단어와 그 구근 모양의 뱃머리 장식을 함께 놓고 보니, 전체 뜻이 충분히 헤아려졌다.\n\n\"나무로 깎은 장미 봉오리라, 응?\" 그가 한 손을 코에 갖다 댄 채 외쳤다. \"그것참 그럴듯하군. 한데 어찌 이리도 천지를 뒤덮을 듯한 냄새를 풍기는고!\"\n\n자, 갑판 위의 사람들과 직접 이야기를 나누려면, 그는 뱃머리를 빙 돌아 우현 쪽으로 노를 저어, 그리하여 그 썩은 고래 가까이 다가가서는, 그 고래 너머로 말을 건네야만 했다.\n\n그렇게 그 자리에 다다른 그는, 여전히 한 손을 코에 댄 채 고함쳤다. \"부통 드 로즈호, 어이! 거기 영어 할 줄 아는 부통 드 로즈 양반 없소?\"\n\n\"있소.\" 뱃전에서 건지섬 사내 하나가 대꾸했는데, 알고 보니 그가 일등항해사였다.\n\n\"음, 그렇다면, 내 어여쁜 장미 봉오리님, 흰 고래(White Whale)를 본 적 있소?\"\n\n\"무슨 고래라고?\"\n\n\"흰 고래 말이오 — 향유고래 — 모비 딕(Moby Dick), 그놈을 본 적 있느냔 말이오?\"\n\n\"그런 고래는 들어 본 적도 없소. 카샬로 블랑슈! 흰 고래라 — 본 적 없소.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 그럼. 이만 작별하리다. 곧 다시 부르겠소.\"\n\n그러고는 재빨리 피쿼드호 쪽으로 노를 저어 돌아가니, 에이해브(Ahab)가 뒷갑판 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 그의 보고를 기다리고 있는 것이 보였다. 스터브는 두 손을 모아 나팔처럼 만들고 소리쳤다. \"아닙니다, 선장님! 없답니다!\" 그러자 에이해브는 물러갔고, 스터브는 다시 프랑스 배로 돌아갔다.\n\n이제 보니 그 건지섬 사내는 방금 뱃전 쇠사슬 위로 내려와 고래 절단용 삽을 쓰고 있었는데, 코에는 일종의 자루 같은 것을 둘둘 감아 매달고 있었다.\n\n\"거기 코가 왜 그러시오?\" 스터브가 말했다. \"부러졌소?\"\n\n\"차라리 부러졌더라면, 아니, 아예 코라는 게 없었더라면 좋겠소!\" 자기가 하는 일이 영 마뜩잖은 듯한 건지섬 사내가 대꾸했다. \"한데 댁은 어째서 그쪽 코를 붙잡고 있는 거요?\"\n\n\"오, 아무것도 아니오! 밀랍으로 만든 코라서, 떨어지지 않게 붙잡고 있어야 한다오. 날씨 한번 좋구려, 안 그렇소? 공기가 제법 정원 냄새가 나는데, 한 마디로. 우리한테 꽃다발이나 한 다발 던져 주시겠소, 부통 드 로즈?\"\n\n\"도대체 무슨 빌어먹을 볼일로 여기 왔단 말이오?\" 건지섬 사내가 별안간 발끈 화를 내며 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"오! 진정하시오 — 진정? 그래, 그 말이 딱이군! 그런 고래들 다룰 때 어째서 얼음에 재워 두질 않는 거요? 한데 농담은 그만두고, 장미 봉오리 양반, 당신도 알고 있소? 그런 고래에서 기름을 짜내려는 게 죄다 헛수고라는 걸 말이오? 저쪽 바싹 마른 놈으로 말할 것 같으면, 그 몸뚱이 통째로 기름 한 홉도 안 나올 거요.\"\n\n\"그쯤은 나도 잘 알고 있소. 한데, 보시오, 여기 선장이 도무지 그 말을 믿질 않는단 말이오. 이번이 그의 첫 항해라오. 전에는 쾰른에서 향수 제조나 하던 사람이지. 그러니 배에 올라와 주시오. 혹 내 말은 안 믿어도 댁의 말은 믿을지도 모르니. 그러면 나도 이 더러운 꼴에서 벗어날 수 있겠지.\"\n\n\"당신이 부탁이라면 무엇이든 들어 드리리다, 내 다정하고 유쾌한 친구여.\" 스터브가 대꾸하고는, 그 말과 함께 곧 갑판으로 올라섰다. 거기에는 기이한 광경이 펼쳐져 있었다. 붉은 털실로 짠 술 달린 모자를 쓴 선원들이 고래를 끌어올릴 묵직한 도르래 장비를 채비하고 있었다. 그러나 그들은 일은 영 굼뜨게 하면서 입은 무척 빠르게 놀렸고, 도무지 기분이 좋아 보이지 않았다. 그들의 코는 하나같이 얼굴에서 위쪽으로 삐죽 솟아, 마치 여러 개의 삼각돛 활대 같았다. 이따금 두엇씩 짝을 지어 일손을 놓고는 신선한 공기를 좀 마시려 돛대 꼭대기로 뛰어 올라가곤 했다. 어떤 자들은 역병에 걸릴까 두려워, 뱃밥을 콜타르에 적셔 틈틈이 콧구멍에 갖다 댔다. 또 어떤 자들은 파이프 대를 대통 바로 아래까지 짧게 부러뜨려 놓고는, 담배 연기를 힘차게 뻐끔뻐끔 피워 댔으니, 그리하여 그 연기가 끊임없이 그들의 콧속을 가득 채웠다.\n\n스터브는 고물 쪽 선장실에서 쏟아져 나오는 고함과 저주의 빗발에 흠칫 놀랐다. 그쪽으로 눈길을 돌려 보니, 안에서 빠끔히 열어 둔 문 뒤로 시뻘겋게 달아오른 얼굴 하나가 불쑥 내밀어져 있었다. 그것은 시달릴 대로 시달린 선의(船醫)였으니, 그는 그날의 작업에 헛되이 항의를 거듭하다가, 그 역병을 피하려 선장실(그는 그곳을 \"내실(cabinet)\"이라 불렀다)로 몸을 피해 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그러면서도 이따금 애원과 분노의 고함을 내지르지 않고는 못 배기는 것이었다.\n\n이 모든 것을 눈여겨본 스터브는 자기 계략이 잘 풀리겠다고 헤아리며, 건지섬 사내에게로 돌아서서 잠시 한담을 나누었다. 그러는 동안 그 낯선 배의 항해사는 자기 선장을 향한 혐오를 털어놓았으니, 그는 잘난 척하는 무식쟁이로서 자기들 모두를 이토록 역겹고 수지맞지 않는 곤경에 몰아넣은 자라는 것이었다. 조심스레 그의 속을 떠본 스터브는, 그 건지섬 사내가 용연향에 관해서는 털끝만큼의 낌새도 채지 못하고 있음을 더욱 분명히 알아챘다. 그리하여 그 일에 관해서는 입을 다물었으나, 그 밖의 일에 대해서는 자못 솔직하고 허물없이 대했다. 그렇게 두 사람은 선장을 골탕 먹이는 동시에 비웃어 주려는 작은 계획을 재빨리 꾸며 냈으니, 선장은 그들의 진심을 추호도 의심할 꿈조차 꾸지 못했다. 그들의 이 작은 계획에 따르면, 건지섬 사내는 통역관 노릇이라는 구실 아래 제멋대로 하고 싶은 말을 선장에게 전하되, 그것이 마치 스터브의 입에서 나온 것인 양 꾸미기로 했다. 그리고 스터브로 말하자면, 그 면담 내내 머릿속에 제일 먼저 떠오르는 헛소리는 무엇이든 지껄여 대기로 했다.\n\n이 무렵 그들이 점찍은 제물이 선실에서 나타났다. 그는 자그마하고 거무튀튀한, 그러나 뱃사람치고는 다소 가냘파 보이는 사내였는데, 그래도 큼직한 구레나룻과 콧수염을 기르고 있었다. 그는 붉은 무명 벨벳 조끼를 걸치고 옆구리에는 시곗줄 장식을 늘어뜨리고 있었다. 건지섬 사내는 이제 이 신사에게 스터브를 정중히 소개했는데, 그러면서 곧장 두 사람 사이를 통역하는 듯한 태도를 짐짓 거창하게 차려 보였다.\n\n\"먼저 그에게 뭐라고 말해 줄까요?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄,\" 스터브가 그 벨벳 조끼며 시계와 장식을 훑어보며 말했다. \"우선 이렇게 전해 주시구려, 내 보기에 그가 어쩐지 애송이 같아 보인다고 말이오. 뭐, 내가 그런 걸 가릴 안목이 있다는 건 아니지만.\"\n\n\"그가 이르기를, 무슈,\" 건지섬 사내가 프랑스어로 자기 선장을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"바로 어제 그의 배가 한 선박과 마주쳤는데, 그 배의 선장과 일등항해사가 선원 여섯과 더불어 모두, 뱃전에 끌어다 댄 썩은 고래에서 옮은 열병으로 죽었다고 합니다.\"\n\n이 말에 선장은 흠칫 놀라며 더 자세한 이야기를 간절히 듣고 싶어 했다.\n\n\"이제 뭐라고 할까요?\" 건지섬 사내가 스터브에게 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그가 그토록 태평하게 받아들이니, 이렇게 전하시오. 이제 내가 그를 찬찬히 뜯어보았는데, 그가 고래잡이배를 지휘하기에는 세인트야고의 원숭이만도 못한 위인임을 확신하게 되었다고 말이오. 사실, 내 말이라며 이렇게 전해 주시오, 그가 한 마리 개코원숭이라고.\"\n\n\"그가 맹세코 단언하기를, 무슈,\" 건지섬 사내가 말했다. \"저 다른 고래, 곧 바싹 마른 놈이 썩은 놈보다 훨씬 더 치명적이라고 합니다. 요컨대, 무슈, 그는 우리가 목숨을 아낀다면 이 물고기들에게서 당장 밧줄을 풀어 떼어 내라고 간곡히 권하고 있습니다.\"\n\n선장은 즉시 앞으로 달려 나가, 큰 소리로 선원들에게 명하여 절단용 도르래를 끌어올리는 일을 멈추게 하고, 고래들을 배에 붙들어 매고 있던 밧줄과 쇠사슬을 당장 풀어 던지게 했다.\n\n\"이제 뭐라고 할까요?\" 선장이 그들에게 돌아왔을 때 건지섬 사내가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 어디 보자. 그래, 이제 그에게 이렇게 전해도 좋겠군 — 그러니까 — 사실, 이렇게 전하시오, 내가 그를 감쪽같이 속여먹었다고. 그리고 (혼잣말로 곁들이며) 어쩌면 또 다른 누군가도 말이오.\"\n\n\"그가 이르기를, 무슈, 우리에게 조금이라도 도움이 되었다니 더없이 기쁘다고 합니다.\"\n\n이 말을 듣자 선장은 고마워해야 할 쪽은 오히려 자기들(자신과 항해사를 가리킨 것이었다)이라고 거듭 말하더니, 끝으로 스터브를 자기 선실로 청해 보르도 포도주 한 병을 함께 마시자고 권했다.\n\n\"그가 댁과 함께 포도주 한 잔 들기를 바란답니다.\" 통역관이 말했다.\n\n\"진심으로 고맙다고 전해 주시오. 한데 내가 골탕 먹인 사람과 술을 마시는 건 내 신조에 어긋난다고 전하시구려. 사실, 나는 이만 가 봐야겠다고 전하시오.\"\n\n\"그가 이르기를, 무슈, 자기 신조로 보면 술을 마시는 것은 허락되지 않으나, 만일 무슈가 하루라도 더 살아 술을 마시고 싶거든, 무슈께서는 보트 넷을 모두 내려 이 고래들에게서 배를 끌어내는 편이 상책이라 합니다. 워낙 바람 한 점 없어 배가 저절로 떠밀려 나지 않을 테니 말입니다.\"\n\n이 무렵 스터브는 이미 뱃전을 넘어 자기 보트에 올라타고 있었는데, 건지섬 사내를 향해 이렇게 외쳤다. 자기 보트에 긴 예인 밧줄이 있으니, 두 고래 가운데 가벼운 놈을 그쪽 뱃전에서 끌어내 주는 것으로 힘닿는 데까지 그들을 돕겠다고. 그리하여 그 프랑스 배의 보트들이 배를 한쪽으로 끌어가는 동안, 스터브는 자비롭게도 자기 고래를 반대쪽으로 끌어내며, 유난히도 긴 예인 밧줄을 짐짓 거창하게 풀어 늘어뜨렸다.\n\n이윽고 미풍이 일었다. 스터브는 고래에게서 밧줄을 푸는 시늉을 했다. 프랑스 배는 보트들을 끌어올리고 곧 거리를 벌려 멀어져 갔고, 그사이 피쿼드호가 그 배와 스터브의 고래 사이로 미끄러져 들어왔다. 그러자 스터브는 재빨리 그 떠 있는 시체로 노를 저어 가, 자기 속셈을 알리려 피쿼드호를 향해 소리쳐 두고는, 곧장 자기의 의롭지 못한 잔꾀의 열매를 거두는 일에 착수했다. 날카로운 보트용 삽을 움켜쥐고, 그는 옆지느러미 조금 뒤쪽에서 그 몸뚱이를 파 들어가기 시작했다. 그 모습은 마치 바다 한가운데서 지하실이라도 파는 것처럼 보였다. 그리고 마침내 그의 삽이 앙상한 갈빗대에 부딪쳤을 때, 그것은 마치 기름진 영국 흙 속에 묻힌 옛 로마의 기와와 도기 조각을 파헤쳐 올리는 것과도 같았다. 그의 보트 선원들은 모두 잔뜩 흥분하여 우두머리를 열심히 거들었으니, 그 안달하는 모습이 마치 금을 캐는 자들 같았다.\n\n그러는 내내 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 새들이 그들 주위에서 자맥질하고, 머리를 처박고, 끽끽거리고, 악다구니를 쳐 대며 서로 다투었다. 끔찍한 꽃다발 냄새가 더욱 짙어지자 스터브는 차츰 낙담한 기색을 띠기 시작했는데, 바로 그때 이 역병의 한복판에서 희미한 향내의 가닥이 슬그머니 새어 나왔다. 그것은 한 줄기 강물이 다른 강물 속으로 흘러들었다가 한동안 전혀 섞이지 않은 채 그 강물과 나란히 흘러가듯, 그 고약한 냄새의 물결을 헤치고 흐르면서도 거기에 흡수되지 않았다.\n\n\"찾았다, 찾았어.\" 땅속 깊은 데서 무언가를 삽으로 친 스터브가 기쁨에 겨워 외쳤다. \"지갑이다! 지갑이야!\"\n\n삽을 내던지고 두 손을 쑤셔 넣은 그는, 잘 익은 윈저 비누, 혹은 진하게 얼룩진 묵은 치즈처럼 생긴 무언가를 한 움큼씩 끄집어냈다. 그것은 기름지고도 향기로웠다. 엄지손가락으로 누르면 쉽사리 자국이 남았고, 빛깔은 노란빛과 잿빛 사이를 띠었다. 그리고 이것이야말로, 선량한 벗들이여, 용연향이니, 어느 약제사에게든 한 온스에 금 1기니를 받을 만한 물건이다. 여섯 움큼 남짓 얻어냈으나, 더 많은 양이 어쩔 수 없이 바다에 흩어져 버렸고, 어쩌면 그보다 더 많이 건질 수도 있었으련만, 그만두고 어서 배로 돌아오라는, 그러지 않으면 배가 작별을 고하고 떠나리라는 성마른 에이해브의 우렁찬 명령이 스터브에게 떨어진 탓이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 92. Ambergris.\n\n이 용연향(龍涎香)이라는 것은 참으로 기묘한 물질이며, 교역품으로서 어찌나 중요한지, 1791년에는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 태생의 어느 코핀 선장이 바로 이 문제로 영국 하원의 증언대에 불려 나와 심문을 받기까지 했다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그 무렵까지도, 아니 비교적 최근에 이르기까지도, 용연향의 정확한 기원은 호박(琥珀) 그 자체와 마찬가지로 학자들에게는 풀리지 않는 수수께끼로 남아 있었기 때문이다. 용연향(ambergris)이라는 말은 프랑스어로 회색 호박을 뜻하는 합성어에 지나지 않지만, 사실 이 두 물질은 전혀 별개의 것이다. 호박은 더러 바닷가에서 발견되기도 하나, 바다에서 멀리 떨어진 내륙의 흙 속에서 캐내어지기도 하는 반면, 용연향은 오직 바다에서만 발견될 뿐 그 밖의 곳에서는 결코 찾아볼 수 없기 때문이다. 게다가 호박은 단단하고 투명하며 잘 부서지고 냄새가 없는 물질로서, 파이프의 물부리나 구슬, 장신구 따위를 만드는 데 쓰이지만, 용연향은 부드럽고 밀랍 같으며 어찌나 향기롭고 향내가 짙은지, 향수와 향환(香丸), 값비싼 양초, 머릿가루, 포마드 따위에 널리 쓰인다. 터키인들은 그것을 요리에 쓰기도 하고, 로마의 성 베드로 대성당에 유향을 가져가는 것과 같은 목적으로 메카로 가져가기도 한다. 어떤 포도주 상인들은 향을 내려고 클라레 포도주에 그것을 몇 알 떨어뜨리기도 한다.\n\n그렇다면 그토록 고상한 신사 숙녀들이 병든 고래의 그 추레한 창자 속에서 나온 정수(精髓)로 제 입맛을 돋운다는 것을 누가 짐작이나 하겠는가! 그러나 사실이 그러하다. 어떤 이들은 용연향을 고래가 앓는 소화불량의 원인이라 여기고, 또 어떤 이들은 그 결과라 여긴다. 그런 소화불량을 어떻게 고쳐야 할지는 참으로 말하기 어려운데, 다만 브랜드레스 환약을 보트로 서너 척 분량쯤 먹인 다음, 바위를 폭파하는 일꾼들이 그러하듯 위험을 피해 멀찌감치 달아나는 수밖에는 없을 것이다.\n\n이 용연향 속에서 단단하고 둥근 뼈 같은 판들이 발견되었다는 말을 미처 하지 못했는데, 처음에 스터브(Stubb)는 그것이 선원들 바지 단추가 아닐까 생각했으나, 나중에 밝혀진 바로는 그것은 다름 아니라 그런 식으로 방부 처리된 작은 오징어 뼛조각에 불과했다.\n\n그런데 이토록 향기로운 용연향의 썩지 않음이 바로 그런 부패의 한복판에서 발견된다는 것, 이것이 과연 아무것도 아니란 말인가? 부패와 불멸에 관하여, 우리가 욕된 가운데 뿌려졌으나 영광 가운데 일으킴을 받는다고 한 고린도전서의 성 바울의 그 말씀을 한번 곰곰이 생각해 보라. 그리고 또한 무엇이 가장 좋은 사향을 만드는가에 관한 파라켈수스의 그 말도 떠올려 보라. 또한 온갖 고약한 냄새를 풍기는 것들 가운데서도, 초기 제조 단계의 쾰른 향수가 가장 지독한 냄새를 풍긴다는 그 기묘한 사실도 잊지 마라.\n\n나는 이 장을 위와 같은 호소로 마무리하고 싶지만 그럴 수가 없으니, 고래잡이들에게 흔히 가해지는 한 가지 비난을 반박하고픈 마음이 간절하기 때문이다. 그 비난은, 이미 한쪽으로 기운 어떤 이들의 판단으로는, 저 프랑스인의 두 고래에 관해 앞서 말한 바로 인해 간접적으로 입증되는 것처럼 여겨질 수도 있는 것이다. 이 책의 다른 대목에서 나는, 고래잡이라는 직업이 처음부터 끝까지 칠칠치 못하고 지저분한 일이라는 그 비방을 이미 논박한 바 있다. 그러나 또 한 가지 반박해야 할 것이 있다. 그들은 모든 고래가 늘 고약한 냄새를 풍긴다고 넌지시 비꼰다. 자, 그렇다면 이 가증스러운 낙인은 어디서 비롯된 것일까?\n\n내 생각에 그것은 두 세기도 더 전에 그린란드 고래잡이배들이 런던에 처음 들어왔던 일로 분명히 거슬러 올라갈 수 있다. 왜냐하면 그 고래잡이들은 그때나 지금이나, 남쪽 바다의 배들이 늘 해 온 것처럼 기름을 바다 위에서 정제하지 않고, 신선한 지방을 작은 조각으로 잘라 큰 통의 마개 구멍으로 쑤셔 넣은 다음 그런 식으로 고향까지 싣고 오기 때문이다. 그 얼어붙은 바다에서는 어기(漁期)가 짧고, 또 그들이 맞닥뜨리는 갑작스럽고 사나운 폭풍 탓에 달리 어찌해 볼 도리가 없는 것이다. 그 결과, 그린란드 부두에서 이런 고래 무덤 같은 배의 화물칸을 열어젖히고 짐을 부릴 때면, 마치 산부인과 병원의 기초를 닦으려 오래된 도시의 묘지를 파헤칠 때 피어오르는 것과 어딘가 비슷한 냄새가 풍겨 나온다.\n\n또한 내가 어느 정도 짐작하기로는, 고래잡이들에게 가해진 이 못된 비난은, 옛날 그린란드 해안에 있었던 스흐메렌뷔르흐, 혹은 스메렌베르흐라 불리던 어느 네덜란드 마을의 존재 탓으로도 돌릴 수 있을 듯하다. 이 후자의 이름은 박식한 포고 폰 슬랙이 냄새에 관한 그의 위대한 저작, 곧 그 주제의 교과서에서 쓴 것이다. 그 이름이 뜻하는 바대로(스메르는 기름, 베르흐는 쌓아 두다라는 뜻이다), 이 마을은 네덜란드 고래잡이 선단의 지방을 그 목적으로 네덜란드 본국까지 가져가지 않고 정제할 수 있는 장소를 마련하려고 세워진 것이었다. 그곳은 가마와 기름솥과 기름 창고가 모인 곳이었으니, 작업이 한창 돌아갈 때면 분명 그리 유쾌한 냄새를 풍기지는 않았을 것이다. 그러나 이 모든 것은 남양(南洋)의 향유고래잡이배와는 사뭇 다르다. 향유고래잡이배는 어쩌면 사 년에 걸친 항해 동안 화물칸을 기름으로 가득 채우고 나서도, 기름을 끓여 내는 일에 쉰 날도 채 쓰지 않을 것이며, 게다가 통에 담긴 그 상태의 기름은 거의 냄새가 나지 않는다. 진실을 말하자면, 살아 있든 죽어 있든, 그저 점잖게만 다룬다면 고래라는 종족은 결코 고약한 냄새를 풍기는 짐승이 아니다. 또한 중세 사람들이 일행 가운데 유대인을 코로 가려냈다고 으스대던 식으로 고래잡이를 코로 알아낼 수 있는 것도 아니다. 게다가 고래로 말하자면, 대체로 그토록 더할 나위 없이 건강을 누리고, 운동을 듬뿍 하며, 비록 탁 트인 공기를 쐬는 일은 드물지언정 늘 바깥에서 지내는 처지이니, 향기롭지 않으려야 않을 수가 없다. 단언컨대, 향유고래의 꼬리지느러미가 물 위에서 움직일 때면, 마치 사향 향을 풍기는 부인이 따뜻한 응접실에서 옷자락을 사각이며 스칠 때와 같은 향내가 흩어진다. 그렇다면 그 어마어마한 크기를 헤아릴 때, 나는 향유고래의 향기로움을 무엇에 견주어야 할까? 보석으로 장식한 엄니를 지니고 몰약 향을 물씬 풍기며, 알렉산드로스 대왕에게 경의를 표하고자 어느 인도(Indian) 마을에서 끌려 나왔던 저 이름난 코끼리에 견주어야 마땅하지 않겠는가?\n\n# CHAPTER 93. The Castaway.\n\n프랑스 배와 마주친 지 며칠 지나지 않아, 피쿼드호 선원 가운데 가장 보잘것없는 자에게 가장 의미심장한 사건이 닥쳤다. 더없이 통탄할 만한 사건이었다. 그리고 그 일은, 때로 미친 듯 흥겹고 또 운명 지어진 이 배에, 그녀 자신의 산산조각 난 결말이 무엇이 되든 그것을 살아 숨 쉬며 늘 따라다니는 예언으로 안겨 주는 것으로 끝맺었다.\n\n자, 포경선에서는 누구나 보트에 타는 것이 아니다. 몇몇 일손은 따로 남겨 두어 배지기라 부르는데, 보트들이 고래를 쫓는 동안 본선을 부리는 것이 그들의 소임이다. 대체로 이 배지기들도 보트 선원들 못지않게 단단한 사내들이다. 그러나 배 안에 유달리 호리호리하거나 굼뜨거나 겁 많은 인간이 있다면, 그자는 영락없이 배지기로 정해진다. 피쿼드호에서도 사정은 그러하여, 별명이 피핀이고 줄여서 핍(Pip)이라 불리는 자그마한 흑인이 바로 그러했다. 가엾은 핍! 그대들은 전에도 그의 이야기를 들었으리라. 저 극적이었던 한밤중, 그토록 음울하면서도 흥겹던 때 그가 두드리던 탬버린을 그대들은 분명 기억할 것이다.\n\n겉모습만 보자면 핍과 도우보이는 검은 조랑말과 흰 조랑말이 짝을 이루듯 잘 어울렸으니, 빛깔은 서로 달라도 발육은 엇비슷하여, 한 멍에에 묶여 기묘한 한 쌍을 이룬 격이었다. 그러나 가련한 도우보이가 본래 머리가 둔하고 굼뜬 데 반해, 핍은 지나치게 여린 마음씨이긴 해도 밑바탕은 무척 영민하여, 그 종족 특유의 정답고 너그럽고 흥겨운 총기를 지니고 있었다. 어떤 다른 인종보다도 늘 모든 휴일과 잔치를 더 곱고 더 자유로운 흥취로 즐기는 종족이었다. 흑인들에게라면 한 해의 달력은 365일 내내 독립기념일이요 새해 첫날이어야 마땅하리라. 이 작은 흑인이 빛났노라고 내가 쓴다 해서 그리 웃지는 말라. 검음에도 그 나름의 광채가 있으니, 보라, 임금의 보물함 벽에 장식된 저 윤기 흐르는 흑단을. 그러나 핍은 삶을 사랑했고, 삶이 누리는 온갖 평온한 안락을 사랑했다. 그리하여 그가 어찌 된 영문인지 알 수 없게 휘말려 든 저 공포에 떨게 하는 일은, 그의 총기를 더없이 슬프게 흐려 놓고 말았다. 그러나 머지않아 드러나겠지만, 그렇게 그의 안에서 잠시 짓눌렸던 것은, 마침내 기이하고 거친 불길로 섬뜩하게 환히 밝혀질 운명이었으니, 그 불길은 그를 본래의 광채보다 열 배는 더 밝게 거짓으로 드러내 보였다. 일찍이 그가 고향인 코네티컷의 톨런드 카운티에서 풀밭 위 깡깡이쟁이의 흥겨운 놀이판을 여러 번 돋우어 주던 그 빛, 그리고 가락 흐르는 저물녘이면 그 흥겨운 하하 웃음으로 둥근 지평선을 별이 울리는 하나의 탬버린으로 바꾸어 놓던 그 빛 말이다. 그러니, 비록 환한 대낮의 맑은 공기 속에서 푸른 핏줄 도드라진 목에 걸린 채 맑은 물빛 다이아몬드 한 방울이 건강하게 빛난다 해도, 교활한 보석상이 그대에게 그 다이아몬드의 가장 인상적인 광채를 보여 주려 할 때면, 그는 그것을 음울한 바탕 위에 놓고서, 햇빛이 아니라 어떤 부자연스러운 가스로 불을 밝힌다. 그러면 저 불꽃 같은 광휘가, 지옥처럼 장려하게 솟아오른다. 그러면 사악하게 타오르는 그 다이아몬드는, 한때 수정 같은 하늘의 더없이 신성한 상징이었던 그것은, 지옥의 왕에게서 훔쳐 낸 어떤 왕관의 보석처럼 보이는 것이다. 그러나 이제 이야기로 돌아가자.\n\n일이 이렇게 되었다. 용연향 사건 때 스터브의 뒤쪽 노잡이가 그만 손을 삐어, 한동안 영 못 쓰게 되어 버렸다. 그래서 임시로 핍이 그 자리에 들어가게 되었다.\n\n스터브가 핍을 데리고 처음 보트를 내렸을 때, 핍은 무척이나 안절부절못했다. 그러나 다행히 그때는 고래와 바싹 맞붙는 일을 면했고, 따라서 아주 망신스럽지는 않게 넘어갔다. 다만 그를 지켜보던 스터브는, 나중에 그에게 용기를 한껏 소중히 길러 두라고 일러두었다. 그 용기가 곧잘 필요해질 수도 있는 까닭이었다.\n\n자, 두 번째로 보트를 내렸을 때, 보트는 노를 저어 고래에게 다가갔다. 그리고 그 물고기가 던져진 작살을 맞자, 여느 때처럼 한 차례 후려쳤는데, 하필 이번에는 그것이 가엾은 핍의 자리 바로 밑이었다. 그 순간 저도 모르게 치민 경악에 핍은 노를 손에 든 채 보트 밖으로 펄쩍 뛰어올랐고, 그 바람에 느슨하던 고래 밧줄 한 자락이 그의 가슴에 걸리는 통에, 그것을 가슴으로 밀어내며 함께 뱃전 너머로 떨어졌으니, 마침내 물속으로 풍덩 처박힐 무렵에는 그것에 칭칭 감기고 말았다. 바로 그 찰나 작살 맞은 고래가 사납게 내달리기 시작하면서 밧줄은 순식간에 팽팽해졌고, 보라! 가엾은 핍은 온통 거품을 일으키며 보트의 밧줄걸이까지 끌려 올라왔으니, 그의 가슴과 목을 몇 바퀴 휘감은 그 밧줄에 무자비하게 거기까지 질질 끌려온 것이었다.\n\n태시테고가 뱃머리에 서 있었다. 그는 사냥의 열기로 가득 차 있었다. 그는 핍을 겁쟁이라고 미워했다. 칼집에서 보트 칼을 홱 뽑아 그 날카로운 날을 밧줄 위에 갖다 댄 채, 스터브 쪽으로 돌아서서 묻듯이 외쳤다. \"끊을까요?\" 그러는 사이 핍의 새파랗게 질려 숨 막힌 얼굴은 분명히 이렇게 말하고 있었다. 제발, 어서요! 모든 것이 순식간에 지나갔다. 반 분도 채 안 되는 사이에 이 일 전부가 벌어진 것이었다.\n\n\"빌어먹을 놈, 끊어!\" 스터브가 고함쳤다. 그리하여 고래는 놓치고, 핍은 살아났다.\n\n가까스로 정신을 차리자마자, 가엾고 자그마한 흑인은 선원들의 고함과 저주에 시달렸다. 스터브는 이 어수선한 욕설들이 잦아들도록 잠자코 내버려 두었다가, 이윽고 담담하고 사무적인, 그러나 여전히 반은 익살스러운 투로 핍을 공식적으로 꾸짖었다. 그러고 나서는 비공식적으로 그에게 유익한 충고를 잔뜩 늘어놓았다. 그 알맹이는 이러했다. 핍, 절대 보트에서 뛰어내리지 마라, 다만 — 그러나 나머지는 죄다 두루뭉술했으니, 가장 미더운 충고란 본래 다 그러한 법이다. 자, 대체로 보트에 붙어 있어라가 고래잡이에서 그대의 참된 좌우명이다. 그러나 보트에서 뛰어내려라가 오히려 더 나은 경우도 더러 생기는 법이다. 게다가, 만약 핍에게 한 점 에누리 없이 양심껏 충고를 해 준다면, 앞으로 뛰어내릴 여지를 너무 넓게 남겨 주는 꼴이 되리라는 것을 마침내 깨달은 듯, 스터브는 별안간 모든 충고를 거두고, 단호한 명령으로 끝맺었다. \"보트에 붙어 있어라, 핍. 안 그러면, 맹세코, 네가 뛰어내려도 난 너를 건져 주지 않을 테니, 똑똑히 새겨 둬라. 우린 너 같은 놈 때문에 고래를 놓칠 여유가 없어. 앨라배마에서라면 고래 한 마리가 너보다 서른 배는 값나갈 게다, 핍. 그걸 명심하고, 다시는 뛰어내리지 마라.\" 이로써 스터브는 어쩌면 넌지시 일러 준 셈이었다. 사람이 제 이웃을 사랑한다 해도, 사람은 돈벌이하는 동물이며, 그 천성이 너무도 자주 그의 자비심을 방해한다는 것을.\n\n그러나 우리는 모두 신들의 손안에 있다. 그리고 핍은 또다시 뛰어내렸다. 처음과 아주 비슷한 상황이었으나, 이번에는 밧줄을 가슴으로 밀어내지 못했다. 그래서 고래가 내달리기 시작했을 때, 핍은 서둘러 길 떠난 나그네가 두고 간 가방처럼 바다에 홀로 남겨졌다. 아아! 스터브는 제 말에 너무도 충실했다. 아름답고 풍요로운, 푸른 날이었다. 반짝이는 바다는 잔잔하고 서늘했으며, 사방으로 지평선까지 판판하게 뻗어, 마치 금박장이가 끝까지 두드려 편 가죽 같았다. 그 바다 위에서 오르내리며 까딱이는 핍의 흑단 같은 머리는 정향 한 알처럼 보였다. 그가 그토록 빠르게 고물 뒤로 멀어져 갈 때, 보트 칼은 하나도 들어 올려지지 않았다. 스터브의 가차 없는 등이 그에게로 돌려져 있었다. 그리고 고래는 날개라도 단 듯 내달렸다. 삼 분 만에, 핍과 스터브 사이에는 기슭 없는 바다가 꼬박 일 마일이나 가로놓였다. 바다 한복판에서, 가엾은 핍은 곱슬곱슬 오그라든 검은 머리를 태양 쪽으로 돌렸으니, 또 하나의 외로운 표류자였다, 비록 가장 드높고 가장 빛나는 표류자이긴 했어도.\n\n자, 잔잔한 날씨라면, 너른 바다에서 헤엄치는 일은 숙련된 헤엄꾼에게 뭍에서 용수철 마차를 타는 것만큼이나 수월하다. 그러나 그 무시무시한 외로움은 견딜 수 없는 것이다. 그토록 무정한 광막함 한가운데서 자아가 그토록 강렬하게 응집되는 것이라니, 오 하느님! 누가 그것을 말로 다 할 수 있으랴? 보라, 죽은 듯 잔잔한 바다에서 선원들이 너른 바다에 몸을 담글 때, 그들이 얼마나 제 배에 바싹 매달려 그 뱃전을 따라서만 떠도는지를.\n\n그렇다면 스터브는 정말로 그 가엾고 자그마한 흑인을 운명에 내맡겨 버린 것일까? 아니다. 적어도 그럴 작정은 아니었다. 그의 뒤로 보트 두 척이 따라오고 있었으니, 그는 분명 그 보트들이 당연히 곧 핍에게 다가가 그를 건져 올리리라 여겼던 것이다. 하기야 제 겁약함 탓에 위험에 빠진 노잡이들에 대한 이런 배려가, 비슷한 모든 경우에 사냥꾼들에게서 늘 드러나는 것은 아니다. 그리고 그런 경우는 드물지 않게 벌어진다. 고래잡이에서는 거의 예외 없이, 이른바 겁쟁이라는 자는, 군 해군과 육군 특유의 그 무자비한 혐오로 똑같이 낙인찍히는 법이다.\n\n그런데 일이 이렇게 되고 말았다. 그 보트들은 핍을 보지 못한 채, 별안간 한쪽 가까이에서 고래들을 발견하고는 방향을 틀어 추격에 나섰다. 게다가 스터브의 보트는 이제 너무 멀리 떨어져 있었고, 그와 그의 선원들 모두가 제 고래에 정신이 팔려 있었던 터라, 핍을 둘러싼 둥근 지평선은 그의 주위로 비참하게 넓어져 가기 시작했다. 순전히 우연으로, 마침내 본선이 그를 구해 냈다. 그러나 그 순간부터 그 자그마한 흑인은 백치가 되어 갑판을 돌아다녔다. 적어도 사람들은 그가 그렇다고들 했다. 바다는 비웃듯이 그의 유한한 몸뚱이는 떠받쳐 주었으나, 그의 영혼의 무한함은 빠뜨려 버렸다. 그러나 아주 빠뜨려 죽인 것은 아니었다. 차라리 산 채로 경이로운 심연까지 끌어내려졌으니, 거기서는 뒤틀리지 않은 태곳적 세계의 기이한 형상들이 무심한 그의 눈앞을 이리저리 미끄러져 다녔고, 인색한 인어인 지혜가 제 쌓아 둔 보물 더미를 드러내 보였으며, 그 기쁘고 무정하며 영원토록 앳된 영원들 사이에서, 핍은 무수히 많은, 어디에나 하느님처럼 깃든 산호 벌레들을 보았으니, 그것들은 물의 창공으로부터 저 거대한 천체들을 들어 올리고 있었다. 그는 베틀의 발판을 밟는 하느님의 발을 보았고, 그것을 말했다. 그래서 그의 동료 선원들은 그를 미쳤다고 불렀다. 그러니 사람의 광기란 하늘의 분별이요, 인간은 온갖 필멸의 이치로부터 벗어나 헤매다가 마침내 저 천상의 사념에 다다르는 법인데, 그것은 이치로 보자면 터무니없고 미쳐 날뛰는 것이지만, 그리하여 사람은 화복(禍福)이 어떠하든, 제 하느님처럼 거리낌 없고 초연해지는 것이다.\n\n그 밖의 일은, 스터브를 너무 모질게 탓하지는 말라. 그 고래잡이판에서 흔히 있는 일이다. 그리고 이 이야기가 이어지는 가운데, 그때 가서 나 자신에게도 그와 같은 버림받음이 어떻게 닥쳤는지 드러나게 될 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 94. A Squeeze of the Hand.\n\n스터브가 그토록 비싸게 사들인 그 고래는 마침내 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 뱃전으로 끌어올려졌고, 앞서 자세히 설명했던 절단과 권양 작업이 차례차례 어김없이 이루어졌으며, 마침내 하이델베르크 통, 즉 케이스(case)의 기름을 퍼내는 일에까지 이르렀다.\n\n* 케이스: 향유고래 머리 속의 기름 저장 공간.\n몇몇은 이 마지막 작업에 매달려 있었고, 다른 이들은 향유고래 기름으로 가득 찬 큰 통들을 차례차례 끌어내는 일을 맡았다. 그리고 적당한 때가 이르면 바로 이 기름은 정유 작업장(try-works)으로 보내기에 앞서 세심하게 다루어졌는데, 그 일에 관해서는 곧 말하겠다.\n\n기름은 어느 정도 식어서 결정처럼 굳어 있었기에, 내가 다른 몇 사람과 함께 콘스탄티누스 욕조만 한 큰 통 앞에 앉았을 때, 그것은 묘하게 덩어리져 엉겨 있었고, 여기저기 액체 부분 속을 굴러다니고 있었다. 이 덩어리들을 다시 주물러 액체로 풀어내는 것이 우리의 일이었다. 달콤하고 기름진 일이었다! 옛 시절에 이 기름이 그토록 사랑받는 화장품이었던 것도 당연하다. 이 얼마나 훌륭한 정화제인가! 이 얼마나 훌륭한 향료인가! 이 얼마나 훌륭한 연화제인가! 이 얼마나 황홀한 유연제인가! 단 몇 분간 손을 담그고 있었을 뿐인데, 내 손가락은 뱀장어처럼 느껴졌고, 말하자면 구불구불 뱀처럼 휘감기며 나선을 그리기 시작했다.\n\n힘겨운 권양기 노동을 끝낸 뒤, 푸르고 고요한 하늘 아래, 배는 나른한 돛을 펼친 채 더없이 평온하게 미끄러져 나아가고, 나는 갑판 위에 책상다리를 하고 편안히 앉아 있었다. 그렇게 거의 한 시간 만에 짜인, 스며든 조직의 부드럽고 온화한 알갱이들 속에 손을 담그고 있노라니, 그것들이 내 손가락에 닿아 풍성하게 으깨지면서, 완전히 익은 포도가 제 포도주를 쏟아내듯 그 모든 풍요로움을 내어놓았다. 그리고 그 더럽혀지지 않은 향기를 들이마시니 — 글자 그대로, 참으로 봄 제비꽃 냄새 같았다 — 그대들에게 분명히 말하건대, 그동안 나는 사향 풀밭에 사는 듯한 기분이었다. 나는 우리의 그 끔찍한 맹세를 까맣게 잊어버렸다. 그 말로 다 할 수 없는 기름 속에서, 나는 그 맹세를 손과 마음에서 씻어냈다. 향유고래 기름이 분노의 열기를 가라앉히는 데 진귀한 효험이 있다는 옛 파라켈수스의 미신을 나는 거의 믿을 뻔했다. 그 욕조에 몸을 담그고 있는 동안, 나는 그 어떤 종류의 악의나 짜증이나 원한으로부터도 신성하리만치 자유로워진 기분이었다.\n\n짜라! 짜라! 짜라! 아침 내내, 나는 나 자신마저 거의 그 속에 녹아들 때까지 그 기름을 주물러 짰다. 나는 기묘한 광기가 나를 사로잡을 때까지 그 기름을 주물러 짰다. 그러다 나는 나도 모르게 그 기름 속에서 동료 일꾼들의 손을 짜고 있었으니, 그들의 손을 그 부드러운 알갱이들로 착각했던 것이다. 이 일은 그토록 넘쳐흐르는, 다정하고 우애롭고 사랑스러운 감정을 자아냈기에, 마침내 나는 끊임없이 그들의 손을 쥐어짜며 감상에 젖은 눈으로 그들의 눈을 올려다보았다. 마치 이렇게 말하려는 듯이 — 오! 사랑하는 동료 인간들이여, 어찌하여 우리는 더 이상 사회적인 앙심을 품거나, 조금이라도 언짢음이나 시기심을 알아야 한단 말인가! 자, 다 함께 손을 맞잡고 짜자. 아니, 우리 모두 서로의 속으로 녹아들도록 짜자. 우리 모두 친절이라는 바로 그 젖과 정수 속으로 두루 녹아들도록 짜자.\n\n영원토록 그 기름을 짤 수 있다면 얼마나 좋으랴! 이제 나는, 여러 차례 거듭된 오랜 경험을 통해, 모든 경우에 인간은 결국 자신이 이룰 수 있는 행복에 대한 자만을 낮추거나, 적어도 그 방향을 바꾸어야 함을 깨달았으니 — 그 행복을 지성이나 공상 속 어디엔가 두지 말고, 아내에게, 마음에, 침대에, 식탁에, 안장에, 난롯가에, 고향 땅에 두어야 한다는 것을. 이제 이 모든 것을 깨달았기에, 나는 영원히 케이스의 기름을 짤 준비가 되어 있다. 밤의 환영을 떠올리는 가운데, 나는 천국에서 줄지어 늘어선 천사들을 보았으니, 저마다 향유고래 기름이 든 항아리 속에 손을 담그고 있었다.\n\n이제 향유고래 기름을 이야기한 김에, 정유 작업장에 보낼 향유고래를 손질하는 일에 있어 그것과 비슷한 다른 것들에 관해서도 말하는 것이 마땅하다.\n\n먼저 화이트호스(white-horse)라 불리는 것이 나온다. 이것은 고래의 가늘어지는 부분과 또한 꼬리지느러미의 두툼한 부분에서 얻어진다. 엉겨 붙은 힘줄로 질기며 — 근육 덩어리인데 — 그래도 약간의 기름을 머금고 있다. 고래에서 떼어낸 뒤, 화이트호스는 다지는 기계로 보내기에 앞서 먼저 옮기기 좋은 길쭉한 토막으로 잘린다. 그 모양은 버크셔 대리석 덩어리와 무척 닮았다.\n\n플럼푸딩(plum-pudding)은 고래 살의 어떤 자투리 부분에 붙여진 이름인데, 이것은 비계 외투에 여기저기 들러붙어 있으며, 흔히 그 기름기에 상당한 정도로 함께 젖어 있다. 보기에 더없이 상쾌하고, 흥겹고, 아름다운 물건이다. 그 이름이 말해 주듯, 지극히 풍요로운 얼룩무늬 빛깔을 띠는데, 눈처럼 희고 금빛이 도는 바탕에 줄무늬가 지고, 가장 짙은 진홍과 자줏빛 점들이 점점이 박혀 있다. 마치 레몬빛 그림 속에 박힌 루비 같은 자두들이다. 이성을 거스르고서라도, 그것을 먹지 않고 참기란 어렵다. 고백하건대, 한번은 나도 앞돛대 뒤로 몰래 숨어들어 그것을 맛본 적이 있다. 그 맛은, 뚱보왕 루이의 넓적다리에서 떼어낸 왕의 고기 한 점이 이러했으리라고 내가 짐작할 만한 그런 맛이었다 — 그가 사슴 사냥철이 시작된 첫날에 잡혔고, 그 사슴철이 마침 샹파뉴 포도밭의 유달리 훌륭한 포도주 해와 겹쳤다고 가정한다면 말이다.\n\n이 작업을 하다 보면 마주치게 되는 또 하나의 물질이 있는데, 무척 기이한 것이라 제대로 묘사하기가 몹시 까다롭게 여겨진다. 그것은 슬롭골리언(slobgollion)이라 불린다. 고래잡이들이 만들어낸 독창적인 명칭이며, 그 물질의 성질 또한 그 이름만큼이나 별나다. 그것은 이루 말할 수 없이 끈적끈적하고 실처럼 늘어지는 것으로, 오래 짜낸 뒤 다시 윗물을 따라낸 향유고래 기름 통 속에서 가장 흔히 발견된다. 나는 그것이 케이스의 놀랍도록 얇고 찢어진 막들이 한데 엉겨 붙은 것이라고 생각한다.\n\n거리(gurry)라 불리는 것은 본디 참고래잡이들에게 속한 말이지만, 더러는 향유고래잡이들도 부수적으로 쓰곤 한다. 이것은 그린란드고래, 즉 참고래의 등에서 긁어낸 거무스름하고 끈적이는 물질을 가리키며, 그 천한 리바이어던(Leviathan)을 사냥하는 저열한 영혼들의 갑판은 흔히 이것으로 뒤덮인다.\n\n니퍼(nipper). 엄밀히 말하면 이 단어는 본디 고래잡이의 어휘에 속하지 않는다. 그러나 고래잡이들이 쓰면서 그렇게 되었다. 고래잡이의 니퍼란 리바이어던의 꼬리에서 가늘어지는 부분을 잘라낸, 짧고 단단한 힘줄 조각이다. 두께는 평균 1인치(약 2.5센티미터)쯤 되고, 나머지 크기는 괭이의 쇠붙이 부분만 하다. 모서리를 세워 기름투성이 갑판 위를 밀고 나아가면, 그것은 마치 가죽 물기제거기처럼 작동하며, 마법이라도 부리듯 이름 모를 갖은 아양을 떨어 온갖 불순물을 함께 쓸어 모은다.\n\n그러나 이 오묘한 사항들을 죄다 알고 싶다면, 가장 좋은 방법은 곧장 비계 저장실로 내려가 그곳에 머무는 자들과 오래도록 이야기를 나누는 것이다. 이곳은 앞서, 고래에서 벗겨내어 끌어올린 외투 조각들을 받아두는 곳으로 언급된 바 있다. 그 안에 든 것을 잘라낼 적당한 때가 이르면, 이 방은 모든 풋내기에게, 특히 밤이면 공포의 현장이 된다. 한쪽 켠에는 희미한 등불이 비추는 가운데, 일꾼들을 위해 빈자리가 마련되어 있다. 그들은 대개 둘씩 짝을 지어 들어가는데 — 갈고리창잡이 한 명과 삽잡이 한 명이다. 포경용 창은 같은 이름을 지닌 군함의 백병전 무기와 비슷하다. 갈고리는 보트 갈고리와 얼마간 닮았다. 갈고리잡이는 자신의 갈고리로 비계 한 장을 걸어, 배가 흔들리고 기우뚱거릴 때 그것이 미끄러지지 않도록 붙들려 애쓴다. 그러는 동안 삽잡이는 그 비계 장 위에 올라서서, 수직으로 내리찍어 그것을 옮기기 좋은 말토막으로 잘라낸다. 이 삽은 숫돌이 갈 수 있는 한 날카롭게 갈려 있고, 삽잡이의 발은 맨발이며, 그가 딛고 선 물건은 이따금 썰매처럼 그의 발밑에서 걷잡을 수 없이 미끄러져 달아난다. 그가 제 발가락 하나를, 혹은 조수의 발가락 하나를 잘라낸들, 그대는 그리 크게 놀라겠는가? 비계실의 노련한 일꾼들 사이에서 발가락이란 흔치 않은 물건이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 95. The Cassock.\n\n고래를 이렇게 부검하듯 처리하는 어느 한 시점에 그대가 피쿼드호(Pequod)에 발을 디뎠다고 하자. 그리고 양묘기 근처 앞쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나갔다면, 풍하 쪽 배수구를 따라 길게 누워 있는 매우 기이하고 수수께끼 같은 물건 하나를 적잖은 호기심으로 찬찬히 뜯어보았을 것이 거의 틀림없다. 고래의 거대한 머릿속에 자리한 그 놀라운 저수통도, 빗장이 풀린 듯한 아래턱의 경이로움도, 좌우 대칭을 이룬 꼬리의 기적도 — 이 가운데 어느 것도, 도무지 설명할 길 없는 저 원뿔꼴 물체를 얼핏 반쯤 보는 것만큼 그대를 놀라게 하지는 못할 것이다. 그것은 켄터키 사람의 키보다 더 길고, 밑동의 지름은 거의 한 자에 이르며, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 흑단(黑檀) 우상인 요조처럼 새까맣다. 그리고 그것은 실로 하나의 우상이다. 아니, 옛 시절에는 그것과 똑같이 생긴 것이 우상이었다. 유대 땅 마아가 여왕의 은밀한 숲속에서 발견된 바로 그런 우상이었으니, 그 아들 아사 왕은 어머니가 그것을 섬긴 까닭에 그녀를 폐위하고, 그 우상을 부수어 혐오스러운 물건으로 삼아 기드론 시냇가에서 불태웠다. 이는 열왕기 상 15장에 어둡게 적혀 있는 그대로다.\n\n이제 다가오는, 잘게 써는 자(미서)라 불리는 선원을 보라. 그는 두 명의 조수의 도움을 받아, 뱃사람들이 그란디시무스라 부르는 그것을 묵직하게 등에 짊어지고, 어깨를 잔뜩 구부린 채 비틀거리며 옮겨 간다. 마치 척탄병이 전장에서 죽은 전우를 메고 나오는 듯하다. 그는 그것을 앞갑판 위에 길게 펼쳐 놓고, 이제 아프리카의 사냥꾼이 보아뱀의 가죽을 벗기듯 원통꼴로 그 검은 껍질을 벗겨 낸다. 이 일이 끝나면 그는 바지 가랑이를 뒤집듯 그 껍질을 안팎으로 뒤집고, 단단히 잡아당겨 거의 두 배의 지름이 되도록 늘인 다음, 마침내 잘 펴서 삭구에 매달아 말린다. 이윽고 그것을 끌어 내려, 뾰족한 끝 쪽에서 석 자쯤을 떼어 내고, 다른 쪽 끝에는 팔을 끼울 구멍 두 개를 째어 놓은 뒤, 길이 방향으로 제 몸을 통째로 그 안에 미끄러뜨려 넣는다. 잘게 써는 자는 이제 제 소임에 걸맞은 온전한 제의(祭衣)를 갖춰 입고 그대 앞에 선다. 그 일에 종사하는 모든 이에게 까마득한 옛날부터 전해 온 바, 그가 제 직무의 독특한 기능을 수행하는 동안 오직 이 제복만이 그를 알맞게 지켜 줄 수 있는 것이다.\n\n그 직무란 가마솥에 넣을 지방 덩어리, 곧 말덩이라 불리는 것을 잘게 써는 일이다. 이 작업은 뱃전에 끝을 맞대어 세워 놓은 기묘한 나무 받침대에서 이루어지는데, 그 아래에는 널찍한 통이 놓여 있어, 잘게 썰린 조각들이 황홀경에 빠진 웅변가의 책상에서 종이장이 떨어지듯 빠르게 그 안으로 떨어진다. 단정한 검은 옷을 차려입고, 사람들의 눈길이 쏠리는 설교단을 차지한 채, 성경 책장에 골몰하고 있으니 — 이 잘게 써는 자야말로 얼마나 훌륭한 대주교 후보감이며, 얼마나 어울리는 교황감인가!\n\n* 성경 책장! 성경 책장! 이것은 항해사들이 잘게 써는 자에게 변함없이 외치는 소리다. 이 말은 그에게 조심하여 일감을 되도록 얇은 조각으로 썰라는 명령인데, 그렇게 하면 기름을 끓여 내는 작업이 한결 빨라지고 그 양도 상당히 늘어나며, 어쩌면 품질까지 좋아질 수도 있기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 96. The Try-Works.\n\n보트를 매달아 둔 것 말고도, 미국 포경선을 겉으로 구별 짓는 것은 정유 가마(try-works)다. 포경선은 더없이 단단한 석조 구조물이 참나무와 삼[麻]과 어우러져 완성된 한 척의 배를 이루는, 기묘한 모순을 드러낸다. 마치 너른 들판에서 벽돌 굽는 가마 하나를 통째로 떼어다 갑판 널빤지 위에 옮겨 놓은 듯한 형국이다.\n\n정유 가마는 앞돛대와 큰돛대 사이, 갑판에서 가장 너른 자리에 설치된다. 그 아래의 목재는 유독 튼튼하게 짜여, 가로세로 약 10피트(약 3미터) 곱하기 8피트(약 2.4미터), 높이 5피트(약 1.5미터)에 이르는, 거의 단단한 덩어리나 다름없는 벽돌과 회반죽의 무게를 떠받칠 수 있도록 되어 있다. 그 토대는 갑판을 뚫고 들어가지는 않으나, 석조 구조물은 사방을 떠받쳐 주는 묵직한 무릎쇠[膝鐵]로 표면에 단단히 고정되어 목재에 나사로 단단히 죄어진다. 옆면은 나무로 둘러싸여 있고, 꼭대기는 비스듬히 경사진 널판 덮개 승강구로 완전히 덮여 있다. 이 덮개를 들어내면 커다란 정유솥(try-pots)이 드러나는데, 두 개가 있으며 저마다 몇 배럴의 용량을 지닌다. 쓰지 않을 때는 놀랄 만큼 깨끗하게 관리된다. 더러는 동석[凍石]과 모래로 닦아, 안쪽이 은으로 만든 펀치 그릇처럼 반들반들 빛날 정도다. 야간 당직 동안 냉소적인 늙은 선원들은 그 안으로 기어 들어가 몸을 둘둘 말고 한숨 자기도 한다. 그것을 닦는 일에 매달려 있을 때면 — 솥마다 한 사람씩, 나란히 들어앉아 — 그 무쇠 가장자리 너머로 은밀한 이야기들이 숱하게 오간다. 또한 이곳은 심오한 수학적 명상의 자리이기도 하다. 페쿼드호(Pequod)의 왼쪽 정유솥 안에서, 동석을 부지런히 내 주위로 빙빙 돌리고 있을 때, 나는 처음으로 어떤 주목할 만한 사실에 간접적으로 부딪쳤으니, 곧 기하학에서 사이클로이드 곡선을 따라 미끄러지는 모든 물체는 — 이를테면 내 동석은 — 어느 지점에서 출발하든 정확히 같은 시간에 내려온다는 것이었다.\n\n정유 가마 앞면에서 방화판을 떼어 내면 그쪽의 맨 석조가 드러나는데, 솥 바로 아래에 화로의 무쇠 아가리 두 개가 뚫려 있다. 이 아가리에는 묵직한 무쇠 문이 달려 있다. 불의 맹렬한 열기가 갑판으로 옮아가지 않도록, 가마의 둘러싸인 표면 전체 아래로 뻗은 얕은 저수통이 마련되어 있다. 뒤쪽에 끼워 넣은 관[管]을 통해, 이 저수통은 물이 증발하는 만큼 빠르게 다시 채워진다. 외부로 난 굴뚝은 없으며, 화로는 뒷벽에서 곧장 열린다. 자, 여기서 잠시 이야기를 거슬러 올라가 보자.\n\n페쿼드호의 정유 가마가 이번 항해에서 처음으로 가동된 것은 밤 아홉 시 무렵이었다. 그 일을 감독하는 것은 스터브(Stubb)의 몫이었다.\n\n\"거기 다들 준비됐나? 그럼 덮개를 벗기고 솥을 돌려. 자네, 요리사, 가마에 불을 붙여.\" 이것은 쉬운 일이었으니, 목수가 항해 내내 화로 안에 자기 대팻밥을 쑤셔 넣어 두었기 때문이다. 여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 포경 항해에서 정유 가마의 첫 불은 한동안 장작으로 지펴야 한다. 그 뒤로는 주연료에 빠르게 불을 붙이는 수단으로 쓰는 것 말고는 장작을 쓰지 않는다. 한마디로, 한 번 기름을 짜내고 나면, 바삭하게 쪼그라든 지방 덩어리는 — 이제 찌꺼기 혹은 튀김 부스러기라 불리는데 — 여전히 상당한 기름기를 머금고 있다. 이 부스러기가 불꽃의 먹이가 된다. 피가 너무 많아 타오르는 순교자처럼, 혹은 제 몸을 갉아먹는 인간 혐오자처럼, 한 번 불이 붙으면 고래는 제 연료를 스스로 대고 제 몸으로 타오른다. 차라리 제 연기까지 다 태워 버린다면 좋으련만! 그 연기는 들이마시기에 끔찍하건만, 그래도 들이마실 수밖에 없고, 그것뿐 아니라 한동안 그 속에서 살아야 한다. 그 연기에는 형언할 수 없는, 야만스러운, 힌두 화장터 같은 냄새가 배어 있으니, 장례용 장작더미 근처에 숨어 있을 법한 그런 냄새다. 그것은 최후 심판의 날 왼쪽 날개에서 나는 냄새와 같고, 지옥 구렁이 있음을 입증하는 논거다.\n\n자정 무렵 가마는 한창 돌아가고 있었다. 우리는 고래 사체에서 벗어났고, 돛이 올려졌으며, 바람은 점점 거세지고, 거친 대양의 어둠은 짙고도 깊었다. 그러나 그 어둠은 맹렬한 불길에 핥겨 사라지고 있었으니, 불길은 이따금 그을음 묻은 연도(煙道)에서 갈래갈래 솟구쳐 올라, 저 유명한 그리스의 불처럼 삭구의 높직한 밧줄 하나하나를 환히 비추었다. 불타는 배는 마치 어떤 복수의 과업을 향해 가차 없이 명을 받은 듯 앞으로 내달았다. 그 옛날 담대한 히드라 사람 카나리스의 역청과 유황을 가득 실은 범선들이 한밤의 항구에서 출항하여, 너른 화염의 돛을 펼친 채 터키 군함들을 향해 들이닥쳐 그것들을 화재 속에 휘감아 버렸듯이.\n\n가마 꼭대기에서 벗겨 낸 덮개는 이제 그 앞에 너른 화덕을 마련해 주었다. 그 위에는, 언제나 포경선의 화부 노릇을 하는 이교도 작살잡이들의 지옥 같은 형상이 서 있었다. 그들은 거대한 갈래 장대로 쉭쉭 소리를 내는 지방 덩어리를 펄펄 끓는 솥 안으로 던져 넣거나, 그 아래 불을 휘저었으니, 그러면 뱀 같은 불꽃이 문 밖으로 굽이치며 날름거려 그들의 발을 붙잡으려 들었다. 연기는 음울한 더미를 이루며 굴러갔다. 배가 한 번 출렁일 때마다 끓는 기름도 한 번 출렁였는데, 마치 그들의 얼굴을 향해 뛰어오르려고 안달이 난 듯했다. 가마 아가리 맞은편, 그 너른 나무 화덕 저쪽 편에는 양묘기(揚錨機)가 있었다. 이것은 바다의 소파 노릇을 했다. 여기에 당직들은 달리 할 일이 없을 때면 비스듬히 기대어, 불의 붉은 열기를 들여다보았으니, 마침내 머릿속의 두 눈이 그슬리는 듯 느껴질 지경이었다. 그들의 가무잡잡한 얼굴은 이제 온통 연기와 땀으로 시커멓게 더럽혀졌고, 텁수룩하게 엉킨 수염과, 그와 대조를 이루는 치아의 야만적인 광채, 이 모든 것이 가마의 변덕스러운 불빛 무늬 속에서 기묘하게 드러났다. 그들이 서로에게 자신들의 부정한 모험담을, 그 공포의 이야기를 흥겨운 말투로 들려줄 때, 그들의 미개한 웃음이 화로의 불길처럼 그들에게서 위로 갈래갈래 솟구칠 때, 그들 앞에서 작살잡이들이 거대한 갈래 갈고리와 국자를 들고 이리저리 사납게 손짓을 해 댈 때, 바람이 윙윙거리며 울부짖고, 바다가 펄쩍 뛰어오르고, 배가 신음하며 곤두박질치면서도, 그 붉은 지옥을 바다와 밤의 어둠 속으로 더욱더 굳건히 쏘아 보내며, 입에 문 흰 뼈를 비웃듯 우적우적 씹어 대고, 사방으로 사납게 침을 뱉어 댈 때 — 그때 야만인들을 싣고, 불을 가득 안고, 시체 하나를 태우며, 그 캄캄한 어둠의 심연 속으로 곤두박질쳐 들어가는 저 질주하는 페쿼드호는, 그 편집광 선장의 영혼을 물질로 빚어낸 짝패처럼 보였다.\n\n내가 키를 잡고 서서, 기나긴 시간 동안 말없이 이 바다 위 화염선의 뱃길을 인도하는 동안, 그것은 내게 그렇게 보였다. 그 사이 나 자신은 어둠에 휩싸여 있었기에, 도리어 다른 이들의 붉음과 광기와 끔찍함을 더 잘 볼 수 있었다. 내 앞에서 반쯤은 연기 속에, 반쯤은 불 속에 깡충거리는 그 악마 같은 형상들을 끊임없이 바라보다 보니, 마침내 그것들은 내 영혼 속에도 비슷한 환영을 낳았으니, 한밤중에 키를 잡고 있노라면 늘 나를 덮쳐 오곤 하던 그 까닭 모를 졸음에 내가 굴복하기 시작하자마자 그러했다.\n\n그러나 그날 밤, 유독 그날 밤에, 기이한 (그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧 설명할 수 없는) 일이 내게 일어났다. 잠깐 선 채로 졸다가 화들짝 깨어난 나는, 무언가 치명적으로 잘못되었음을 끔찍하게 의식했다. 턱뼈로 된 키손잡이가 그것에 기대고 있던 내 옆구리를 후려쳤고, 귓가에는 바람에 막 떨리기 시작한 돛의 나직한 웅웅거림이 들려왔다. 나는 두 눈을 뜨고 있다고 여겼고, 손가락을 눈꺼풀에 갖다 대어 그것을 기계적으로 더 벌리고 있다는 것을 어렴풋이 의식하고 있었다. 그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고, 내 앞에는 길을 잡을 나침반이 도무지 보이지 않았다. 한결같은 나침반함 등불이 비추는 가운데 나침반판을 들여다보고 있던 게 불과 일 분 전이었던 것만 같은데도 말이다. 내 앞에는 칠흑 같은 음울함 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았고, 그것은 이따금 붉은 섬광으로 인해 무시무시해질 뿐이었다. 무엇보다 강하게 든 느낌은, 내가 서 있는 그 무엇이든 — 빠르게 내달리는 그것이 — 앞에 있는 어떤 항구에 묶여 있다기보다는, 뒤에 있는 모든 항구로부터 달아나고 있다는 것이었다. 죽음과도 같은, 처절하고 어리둥절한 느낌이 나를 덮쳤다. 발작하듯 두 손으로 키손잡이를 움켜쥐었으나, 그 키손잡이가 어쩐 일인지, 어떤 마법에 걸린 듯이, 거꾸로 뒤집혀 있다는 미친 망상에 사로잡혔다. 맙소사! 내게 무슨 일이 일어난 것인가? 나는 생각했다. 보라! 그 짧은 잠 사이에 나는 몸을 빙 돌려, 뱃머리와 나침반에 등을 진 채, 배의 고물 쪽을 마주 보고 있었던 것이다. 순식간에 나는 다시 뒤로 돌아섰으니, 배가 바람을 안고 곧추서 올라가다가 십중팔구 뒤집히는 것을 가까스로 막을 수 있었다. 한밤중의 이 부자연스러운 환각에서, 그리고 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 밀려날 뻔한 그 치명적인 위기에서 벗어났을 때의 안도가 얼마나 기쁘고 또 얼마나 고마웠던지!\n\n오, 인간이여, 불의 얼굴을 너무 오래 들여다보지 말라! 키에 손을 얹은 채 꿈꾸지 말라! 나침반에 등을 돌리지 말라. 덜컹거리는 키손잡이의 첫 신호를 받아들여라. 인공의 불이 그 붉음으로 만물을 끔찍하게 보이게 할 때, 그 불을 믿지 말라. 내일이면, 자연의 태양 아래, 하늘은 환할 것이다. 갈래진 불길 속에서 악마처럼 이글거리던 자들도, 아침이면 사뭇 다른, 적어도 한결 부드러운 빛 속에 모습을 드러낼 것이다. 영광스럽고, 황금빛이며, 기쁨에 찬 태양, 그 유일한 참된 등불 — 다른 모든 것은 거짓말쟁이일 뿐이다!\n\n그럼에도 태양은 버지니아의 디즈멀 늪을 감추지 못하고, 로마의 저주받은 캄파냐도, 너른 사하라도, 달빛 아래 펼쳐진 수백만 마일에 이르는 사막과 슬픔도 감추지 못한다. 태양은 바다를 감추지 못하니, 바다야말로 이 지구의 어두운 면이요, 이 지구의 삼분의 이를 차지한다. 그러므로, 자기 안에 슬픔보다 기쁨이 더 많은 인간, 그런 인간은 참될 수가 없다 — 참되지 않거나, 아직 덜 여문 것이다. 책 또한 마찬가지다. 모든 사람 가운데 가장 참된 이는 슬픔의 사람이었고, 모든 책 가운데 가장 참된 것은 솔로몬의 책이며, 전도서는 비통함을 곱게 두드려 벼린 강철이다. \"모든 것이 헛되도다.\" 모든 것이. 이 고집스러운 세상은 아직도 기독교적이지 않은 솔로몬의 지혜를 붙들지 못했다. 그러나 병원과 감옥을 슬쩍 피해 다니고, 묘지를 가로지를 때는 걸음을 재촉하며, 지옥보다는 차라리 오페라 이야기를 즐겨 하고, 쿠퍼와 영과 파스칼과 루소를 한낱 병든 자들의 가련한 무리라 부르며, 근심 없는 평생토록 라블레야말로 더없이 현명하기에 흥겨운 자라고 떠받드는 자 — 그런 자는, 묘비 위에 앉아 헤아릴 길 없이 경이로운 솔로몬과 더불어 푸른 곰팡이 낀 축축한 흙을 부수기에는 어울리지 않는다.\n\n그러나 솔로몬조차도 이렇게 말한다. \"명철의 길에서 벗어나 떠도는 자는 (곧, 살아 있으면서도) 죽은 자들의 무리 가운데 머무르리라.\" 그러니 그대 자신을 불에 내맡기지 말라, 한때 그것이 내게 그러했듯, 그것이 그대를 거꾸로 뒤집고 그대를 마비시키지 못하도록. 비통함인 지혜가 있고, 광기인 비통함이 있다. 그리고 어떤 영혼들 속에는 캐츠킬의 독수리가 있어, 가장 캄캄한 협곡 속으로 내리꽂혔다가 거기서 다시 솟아올라 햇살 가득한 공간 속으로 자취를 감출 수 있다. 설령 그 독수리가 영영 협곡 안에서만 날아다닌다 해도, 그 협곡은 산속에 있다. 그리하여 가장 낮게 내리꽂힐 때조차, 산의 독수리는 평원 위의 다른 새들보다 — 그것들이 아무리 높이 솟아오른다 한들 — 여전히 더 높이 있는 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 97. The Lamp.\n\n피쿼드호의 시연(試煉) 화덕에서 비번인 당직 선원들이 잠들어 있는 피쿼드호의 선수루로 내려간다면, 단 한순간이나마 그대는 거의 시성(諡聖)된 왕들과 고문관들을 모신 어느 환히 밝혀진 사당 안에 서 있다고 여겼으리라. 그들은 거기, 삼각형 모양의 떡갈나무 납골당 속에 누워 있었고, 선원 하나하나가 마치 끌로 새겨 낸 침묵의 형상 같았으며, 스무 개 남짓한 등불이 두건처럼 감긴 그들의 눈 위로 빛을 던지고 있었다.\n\n상선에서 선원에게 돌아가는 기름은 여왕의 젖보다도 귀하다. 어둠 속에서 옷을 입고, 어둠 속에서 먹고, 어둠 속을 비틀거리며 자기 짚자리로 가는 것, 이것이 그가 늘 겪는 신세다. 그러나 고래잡이는 빛의 양식을 찾아 나서는 자인 만큼, 빛 속에서 살아간다. 그는 자기 잠자리를 알라딘의 램프로 만들어 그 안에 몸을 누이니, 그리하여 칠흑같이 캄캄한 밤에도 배의 검은 선체는 여전히 환한 빛을 품고 있다.\n\n고래잡이가 얼마나 거리낌 없이 한 움큼의 등불을 — 흔히 낡은 병이나 약병에 지나지 않는 것이긴 하지만 — 시연 화덕의 구리 냉각통으로 가져가, 마치 통에서 에일 잔을 채우듯 거기서 기름을 다시 채우는지 보라. 게다가 그는 가장 순수한 기름을, 가공되지 않은, 그래서 한 점 변질도 없는 상태 그대로 태운다. 그것은 뭍에 있는 태양등이나 달등이나 별등 따위의 어떤 장치도 알지 못하는 액체다. 그 맛은 사월의 갓 돋은 풀밭에서 난 버터처럼 달콤하다. 그는 마치 대초원의 나그네가 제 저녁거리 사냥감을 손수 잡아 오듯, 그 기름의 신선함과 진정성을 확실히 하기 위해 손수 나가 사냥하는 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 98. Stowing Down and Clearing Up.\n\n위대한 리바이어던이 어떻게 멀리 돛대 꼭대기에서 발견되는지, 어떻게 물의 황야를 가로질러 쫓기다가 깊은 바다의 골짜기에서 도살되는지, 어떻게 그러고 나서 뱃전으로 끌려와 목이 잘리는지, 그리고 어떻게 (목이 잘린 자가 입고 죽은 옷이 옛 처형인의 몫이 되던 그 원칙에 따라) 그 거대하고 두툼한 외투가 그 사형 집행인의 소유가 되는지, 어떻게 때가 되면 그것이 가마솥의 형벌을 선고받고, 사드락과 메삭과 아벳느고처럼 그 경뇌와 기름과 뼈가 불길을 다치지 않고 통과하는지—이 모든 것은 이미 이야기한 바 있다. 그러나 이제 이 부분의 묘사에서 마지막 장을 마무리짓는 일이 남았으니, 곧 그 기름을 통 속으로 따라 부어 선창으로 내려보내는, 그리하여 리바이어던이 다시 한번 제 본래의 심연으로 돌아가 예전처럼 수면 아래로 미끄러져 가는—그러나 아아! 다시는 떠올라 물을 뿜는 일이 없을—그 낭만적인 절차를 되짚어 노래하듯, 내가 노래할 수 있다면 노래해 보는 일이다.\n\n기름은 아직 따뜻한 동안, 뜨거운 펀치주처럼 여섯 통들이 큰 통 속으로 받아 담긴다. 그리고 그러는 사이, 어쩌면 한밤중의 바다에서 배가 이리저리 곤두박질치며 흔들리는 동안, 그 거대한 통들은 빙그르르 돌려져 위아래가 뒤집히고, 때로는 미끄러운 갑판을 가로질러 위태롭게 내달리니, 마치 수많은 산사태처럼 굴러가다가 마침내 사람들의 손에 붙들려 제자리에 멈춰 서고, 그 통 둘레의 테에는 두드릴 수 있는 한 많은 망치들이 탁, 탁, 소리를 낸다. 이제 직무상(ex officio) 선원은 누구나 통메장이가 되기 때문이다.\n\n이윽고 마지막 한 파인트까지 통에 담기고 모든 것이 식으면, 큰 승강구들이 봉인이 풀려 열리고, 배의 창자가 활짝 펼쳐지며, 통들이 바다 속 마지막 안식처로 내려간다. 이 일이 끝나면 승강구 뚜껑이 다시 덮이고, 벽을 발라 막은 벽장처럼 완전히 밀폐된다.\n\n향유고래잡이에서 이것은 어쩌면 포경업 전체에서 가장 주목할 만한 사건 가운데 하나다. 어느 날엔 갑판 널빤지가 피와 기름의 봇물로 흥건히 흐르고, 신성한 뒷갑판 위에는 고래 머리의 거대한 덩어리들이 불경스럽게 쌓이며, 양조장 마당처럼 녹슨 큰 통들이 사방에 나뒹굴고, 정제 가마의 연기가 모든 뱃전을 검댕으로 뒤덮으며, 선원들은 온몸에 기름기를 뒤집어쓴 채 돌아다니고, 배 전체가 위대한 리바이어던 그 자신처럼 보이며, 사방에서 들려오는 소음은 귀를 먹먹하게 한다.\n\n그러나 하루나 이틀이 지난 뒤 바로 이 똑같은 배에서 주위를 둘러보고 귀를 곤두세워 보면, 만일 그 속사정을 일러바치는 보트들과 정제 가마가 없었던들, 당신은 더없이 꼼꼼하고 깔끔한 선장이 모는 어느 조용한 상선 갑판을 밟고 있노라고 거의 맹세라도 할 뻔할 것이다. 정제하지 않은 향유고래 기름은 유난히 깨끗하게 닦아 내는 성질을 지녔다. 그들이 이른바 기름 작업이라 부르는 일이 끝난 직후만큼 갑판이 새하얗게 보이는 때가 없는 것은 바로 이 때문이다. 게다가 태워 버린 고래 부스러기의 재로는 손쉽게 강력한 잿물을 만들 수 있어, 고래 등에서 나온 끈적이는 것이 뱃전에 들러붙어 남아 있으면 그 잿물이 재빨리 그것을 없애 버린다. 사람들은 부지런히 뱃전을 따라 오가며 물통과 걸레로 갑판을 더없이 말끔한 상태로 되돌려 놓는다. 아래쪽 삭구의 검댕도 솔질해 털어 낸다. 그동안 쓰였던 그 수많은 도구들도 마찬가지로 충실히 닦여 제자리에 치워진다. 큰 승강구 뚜껑은 박박 문질러 닦여 정제 가마 위에 얹혀 가마솥을 완전히 가리고, 통은 모조리 눈에 띄지 않게 되며, 밧줄 도구들은 모두 보이지 않는 구석에 감겨 놓인다. 그리고 거의 배 전체 선원이 힘을 합쳐 동시에 부지런을 떤 끝에 이 양심적인 임무 전부가 마침내 끝나면, 그제야 선원들 자신도 제 몸을 씻으러 나아가, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 옷을 갈아입고, 마침내 흠 하나 없이 깨끗한 갑판으로 나오니, 마치 더없이 고운 네덜란드산 아마포 침구에서 갓 뛰쳐나온 신랑들처럼 산뜻하고 온통 발그레하게 상기되어 있다.\n\n이제 그들은 의기양양한 걸음으로 둘씩 셋씩 갑판을 거닐며, 응접실이며 소파며 양탄자며 고운 옥양목 따위를 농담 삼아 늘어놓고, 갑판에 돗자리를 깔자고 제안하고, 꼭대기에 차양을 다는 것을 궁리하며, 앞갑판의 베란다에서 달빛 아래 차를 마시는 것도 마다하지 않겠노라 한다. 이렇게 사향 내음을 풍기는 선원들에게 기름이며 뼈며 지방 따위를 입에 올리는 것은 거의 무례에 가까운 일일 것이다. 그들은 당신이 에둘러 넌지시 말하는 그것이 무엇인지조차 모른다. 저리 가서 우리에게 냅킨이나 가져오게!\n\n그러나 보라. 저 위, 세 개의 돛대 꼭대기에는 세 사람이 더 많은 고래를 찾아내려고 골똘히 망을 보며 서 있으니, 만일 그 고래가 잡힌다면 틀림없이 또다시 저 낡은 떡갈나무 가구를 더럽히고, 적어도 어딘가에 자그마한 기름 얼룩 하나쯤은 떨어뜨릴 것이다. 그렇다. 그리고 밤을 모르고 꼬박 아흔여섯 시간을 내리 이어지는 더없이 혹독하고 쉴 틈 없는 노동을 마친 뒤, 적도 부근에서 온종일 노를 저어 손목이 퉁퉁 부은 채 보트에서 갑판으로 올라서자마자—거대한 사슬을 나르고, 무거운 양묘기를 들어 올리고, 자르고 베어 내며, 그래, 그 땀투성이 몸뚱이가 적도의 태양과 적도의 정제 가마라는 두 불길에 다시금 그을리고 데어 가며, 이 모든 일의 끝에 이르러 마침내 기운을 차려 배를 청소하고 그것을 티끌 하나 없는 우유 저장실처럼 만들어 놓는 일이 얼마나 잦았던가. 깨끗한 작업복의 목단추를 막 채우던 그 가련한 친구들이 \"저기 물을 뿜는다!\" 하는 외침에 소스라치게 놀라, 또 다른 고래와 싸우러 날듯이 달려가, 그 지긋지긋한 일을 처음부터 다시 되풀이하는 일이 또 얼마나 잦았던가. 오! 벗들이여, 그러나 이것은 사람 잡는 일이다! 그럼에도 이것이 바로 삶이다. 우리 인간이 오랜 노고 끝에 이 세상의 거대한 몸뚱이에서 그 작지만 값진 정수(精髓)를 가까스로 뽑아내고, 그런 다음 지친 인내로 그 더러움에서 우리 자신을 씻어 내어 영혼의 정결한 성막 안에서 사는 법을 겨우 익히자마자—그 일이 채 끝나기도 전에—저기 물을 뿜는다!—유령이 솟구쳐 오르고, 우리는 또 다른 세상과 싸우러 배를 띄워, 젊은 삶의 그 케케묵은 일상을 다시 한번 되풀이하는 것이다.\n\n오! 윤회여! 오! 피타고라스여, 빛나는 그리스에서, 이천 년 전에, 그토록 선하고, 그토록 지혜롭고, 그토록 온화하게 죽어 간 그대여. 나는 지난 항해에서 그대와 함께 페루 해안을 따라 항해했으니—어리석게도 나는, 풋내기 순박한 소년인 그대에게, 밧줄 잇는 법을 가르쳤더란 말이다!\n\n# CHAPTER 99. The Doubloon.\n\n앞서 에이해브가 자신의 뒷갑판을 거니는 버릇이 있었음을, 그리고 그 양쪽 끝, 곧 나침반 함과 주돛대 사이를 규칙적으로 오가곤 했음을 이야기한 바 있다. 그러나 서술해야 할 다른 여러 가지 일들이 워낙 많았던 탓에, 때때로 이렇게 거니는 동안 가장 깊은 상념에 잠겼을 때면 그가 양쪽 지점에서 차례로 걸음을 멈추고, 그곳에 서서 눈앞의 특정한 대상을 기이하게 응시하곤 했다는 사실은 미처 덧붙이지 못했다. 나침반 함 앞에서 멈춰 서서 그 시선을 나침반 속의 뾰족한 바늘에 못 박았을 때, 그 시선은 그의 목적이 지닌 날카로운 강렬함을 담아 투창처럼 쏘아졌다. 그리고 다시 걸음을 이어 가다가 주돛대 앞에서 또 멈추면, 그 못 박힌 듯한 시선이 거기에 못 박힌 금화에 가서 꽂혔는데, 그때에도 그는 여전히 못으로 박아 놓은 듯한 단호함을 얼굴에 띠었으되, 다만 거기에는 희망이라고까지는 못해도 어떤 사나운 갈망이 한 줄기 섞여 있었다.\n\n그런데 어느 날 아침, 그 금화를 지나치려 몸을 돌리던 그는, 마치 이제 처음으로 그 안에 숨어 있을지 모를 온갖 의미를 자기 나름의 어떤 편집광적인 방식으로 해독하기 시작하기라도 하듯, 거기에 새겨진 기이한 형상과 명문(銘文)에 새삼 이끌리는 듯했다. 그리고 모든 사물 속에는 어떤 의미가 숨어 있는 법이다. 그렇지 않다면 모든 사물은 보잘것없는 것이며, 둥근 이 세계 자체도 한낱 텅 빈 영(零)에 지나지 않으리니, 다만 보스턴 근방의 언덕을 그러하듯 짐수레째 팔아넘겨 은하수 어딘가의 늪을 메우는 데나 쓰일 따름일 것이다.\n\n그런데 이 금화는 더없이 순수한 처녀 금으로, 화려한 언덕의 심장부 어딘가에서 긁어모은 것이었으니, 그곳에서는 동으로 서로 황금빛 모래밭을 가로질러 수많은 팍톨로스강의 원류가 흘러내린다. 그리고 지금은 비록 녹슨 쇠 볼트와 푸른 녹이 슨 구리 못들 한가운데에 못 박혀 있었으나, 그 어떤 더러움도 닿거나 물들이지 못하여, 그것은 여전히 키토의 광휘를 간직하고 있었다. 또한 무자비한 선원들 틈에 놓여 매시간 무자비한 손길이 곁을 스쳐 지나갔고, 길고 긴 밤마다 어떤 좀도둑질의 접근이라도 가려 줄 만한 짙은 어둠에 휩싸여 있었음에도, 매번 해가 떠오를 때마다 그 금화는 해가 질 때 마지막으로 놓아둔 바로 그 자리에 그대로 있었다. 그도 그럴 것이 그것은 따로 떼어져 어떤 경외로운 목적에 봉헌된 것이었기 때문이다. 그래서 선원들은 그 뱃사람다운 방종함이 어떠하든 간에, 너 나 할 것 없이 모두 그것을 흰 고래의 부적으로 떠받들었다. 때로는 밤의 고단한 당직 시간에 그 이야기를 나누며, 그것이 마침내 누구의 것이 될지, 그리고 그가 과연 그것을 쓸 만큼 살아남을지를 궁금해하곤 했다.\n\n그런데 저 고귀한 남아메리카의 금화들은 마치 태양의 메달이요 열대의 표징과도 같다. 거기에는 종려나무와 알파카와 화산들, 태양의 원반과 별들, 황도(黃道)와 풍요의 뿔, 그리고 펄럭이는 화려한 깃발들이 풍성하고도 호화롭게 새겨져 있으니, 그 귀한 금은 그토록 스페인답게 시적인 저 환상적인 조폐소를 거치면서 한층 더한 귀함과 드높은 영광을 얻는 듯 보인다.\n\n마침 피쿼드호의 이 금화는 그러한 것들 가운데서도 더없이 풍성한 본보기였다. 그 둥근 가장자리에는 \"에콰도르 공화국: 키토(REPUBLICA DEL ECUADOR: QUITO)\"라는 글자가 새겨져 있었다. 그러므로 이 빛나는 금화는 세계의 한가운데, 곧 저 위대한 적도 아래에 자리하여 그 이름을 따 명명된 나라에서 온 것이었다. 그것은 안데스 산맥의 중턱, 가을을 모르고 결코 시들지 않는 풍토에서 주조되었다. 그 글자들로 띠를 두른 안쪽에는 안데스 봉우리 셋의 형상이 보였으니, 하나에서는 불꽃이, 다른 하나에는 탑이, 셋째에는 홰를 치며 우는 수탉이 새겨져 있었고, 그 모두를 아치처럼 둘러싼 것은 구획 지어진 황도대(黃道帶)의 한 부분으로, 그 별자리들에는 저마다 흔히 쓰이는 신비로운 기호들이 표시되어 있었으며, 쐐기돌처럼 박힌 태양은 천칭자리에서 추분점으로 들어서고 있었다.\n\n이 적도의 금화 앞에서 에이해브는, 다른 이들의 눈을 피하지 못한 채, 지금 걸음을 멈추고 있었다.\n\n\"산봉우리와 탑, 그리고 다른 모든 웅대하고 드높은 것들에는 언제나 어딘가 자기중심적인 무언가가 깃들어 있다. 보라. 루시퍼처럼 오만한 세 봉우리를. 저 굳건한 탑, 그것이 에이해브다. 저 화산, 그것이 에이해브다. 용감하고 굴하지 않으며 승리에 찬 저 새, 그것 또한 에이해브다. 모두가 에이해브다. 그리고 이 둥근 금은 더 둥근 이 지구의 상(像)에 지나지 않으니, 그것은 마법사의 거울처럼 모든 사람에게 차례로 저마다 자신의 신비로운 자아를 비쳐 줄 뿐이다. 세상더러 자기들을 풀어 달라고 청하는 자들에게는 큰 수고에 작은 소득뿐이다. 세상은 제 자신조차 풀지 못하니까. 이제 보니 이 새겨진 태양은 발그레한 얼굴을 하고 있구나. 그러나 보라! 그렇다, 그가 폭풍의 별자리, 곧 추분점으로 들어서는구나! 그리고 불과 여섯 달 전에는 양자리에서 또 다른 추분을 돌아 나왔거늘! 폭풍에서 폭풍으로! 그렇다면, 그러하라. 진통 속에서 태어났으니, 사람이 고통 속에서 살고 격통 속에서 죽는 것이 마땅하지 않으랴! 그렇다면, 그러하라! 여기 슬픔이 갈아 낼 단단한 재료가 있구나. 그렇다면, 그러하라.\"\n\n\"어떤 요정의 손가락도 이 금을 누른 적은 없으리라. 다만 어제부터 악마의 발톱이 거기에 제 자국을 남겼음에 틀림없다.\" 스타벅이 뱃전에 기댄 채 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"노인은 벨사살의 무서운 글귀를 읽고 있는 듯하군. 나는 한 번도 저 금화를 유심히 들여다본 적이 없었지. 그가 아래로 내려가는구나. 내가 한번 읽어 보자. 하늘에 머무는 세 거대한 봉우리 사이에 놓인 어두운 골짜기라, 어떤 희미한 지상의 상징을 통해 거의 삼위일체처럼 보이는구나. 그러니 이 죽음의 골짜기에서 하느님은 우리를 에워싸시고, 우리의 온갖 어둠 위로 의(義)의 태양은 여전히 등불과 희망으로 빛나신다. 우리가 눈을 내리뜨면 그 어두운 골짜기는 곰팡내 나는 흙을 드러내 보이지만, 우리가 눈을 들어 올리면 저 밝은 태양이 길 중턱까지 마중 나와 우리를 위로한다. 그러나 아, 저 위대한 태양은 붙박이가 아니로다. 한밤중에 우리가 그에게서 어떤 달콤한 위안을 낚아채려 한들, 우리는 헛되이 그를 찾아 헤맬 뿐이다! 이 금화는 내게 지혜롭게, 부드럽게, 진실되게, 그러나 여전히 슬프게 말을 건넨다. 진리가 나를 그릇되게 뒤흔들기 전에, 나는 이것을 떠나리라.\"\n\n\"저기 늙은 몽골 대왕께서 계시는구먼.\" 스터브가 시유(試油) 가마 곁에서 혼잣말을 했다. \"한참을 들여다보셨겠다. 그리고 저기 스타벅도 똑같은 데서 떠나가는데, 둘 다 얼굴이 어찌나 길쭉한지, 내 보기엔 어딘가 아홉 길은 됨직하구나. 이게 다 금붙이 하나를 들여다본 탓이라니. 만약 내가 지금 그걸 니그로 힐이나 콜리어스 훅에서 손에 넣었다면, 오래 들여다볼 새도 없이 써 버렸을 텐데. 흥! 변변찮고 하찮은 내 생각으로는, 이건 참 별나단 말이지. 나도 항해하면서 전에 금화를 본 적이 있다. 옛 스페인의 금화, 페루의 금화, 칠레의 금화, 볼리비아의 금화, 포파얀의 금화 말이다. 게다가 금 모이도르며 피스톨, 조, 반 조, 사분의 일 조도 잔뜩 보았지. 그런데 도대체 이 적도의 금화 속에 뭐가 들었기에 이토록 죽여주게 놀랍단 말인가? 골콘다에 걸고! 어디 한번 읽어 보자. 어럽쇼! 여기 정말 표징과 이적이 있구나! 이건 옹졸한 보디치 영감이 자기 항해 요람에서 황도대라 부르고, 아래에 둔 내 책력에서도 똑같이 부르는 것이렷다. 책력을 가져와야겠다. 다볼의 산수책으로 악마도 불러낼 수 있다는 말을 들은 적이 있으니, 매사추세츠 책력을 들고 이 별난 꼬불꼬불한 무늬에서 무슨 뜻 하나를 불러내 봐야겠다. 여기 책이 있군. 자, 어디 보자. 표징과 이적이라, 그리고 태양, 그놈은 늘 그 사이에 끼어 있단 말이지. 흠, 흠, 흠. 여기들 있구나—여기들 가는구나—다들 살아 펄떡인다. 양자리, 곧 숫양. 황소자리, 곧 황소. 그리고 어럽쇼! 여기 제 모습 그대로 쌍둥이자리, 곧 쌍둥이가 있구나. 자, 태양은 그놈들 사이를 빙빙 돈다. 그래, 여기 금화에서 그놈은 막 둥글게 둘러앉은 열두 개 거실 가운데 두 곳 사이의 문턱을 넘고 있구나. 책아! 너는 거기 누워 있어라. 사실 너희 책이란 것들은 제 분수를 알아야 한단 말이다. 너희는 우리에게 알맹이 없는 말과 사실이나 일러 주면 그뿐, 거기에 생각을 보태는 건 우리 몫이지. 적어도 매사추세츠 책력이며 보디치의 항해서며 다볼의 산수책에 관한 한, 그게 내 변변찮은 경험이다. 표징과 이적이라고? 표징에 놀라울 게 하나도 없고, 이적에 뜻깊을 게 하나도 없다면 그것참 딱한 노릇이지! 어딘가에 실마리가 있다. 잠깐만. 쉿—들어 봐! 제우스에 걸고, 알았다! 자 봐라, 금화야, 여기 네 황도대는 둥근 한 장(章) 속에 담긴 인간의 일생이로구나. 자, 이제 내가 책에서 곧장 읽어 주마. 오너라, 책력아! 자 시작이다. 여기 양자리, 곧 숫양이 있으니—음탕한 놈이 우리를 낳는다. 그다음엔 황소자리, 곧 황소가—맨 처음으로 우리를 들이받는다. 그다음 쌍둥이자리, 곧 쌍둥이는—말하자면 미덕과 악덕이렷다. 우리가 미덕에 닿으려 애쓰는데, 보라! 게자리, 곧 게가 나타나 우리를 도로 끌어당긴다. 그리고 여기, 미덕에서 멀어져 가노라면, 사자자리, 곧 으르렁대는 사자가 길목에 도사리고 있다가—사나운 이빨로 몇 번 물어뜯고 앞발로 심술궂게 후려친다. 우리가 가까스로 벗어나 처녀자리, 곧 처녀에게 인사를 건네니! 그것이 우리의 첫사랑이라. 우리는 혼인하여 영영 행복하리라 여기는데, 불쑥 천칭자리, 곧 저울이 나타나—행복을 달아 보니 모자라더라. 그 일로 우리가 한껏 서글퍼하는 사이, 아이고! 우리가 어찌나 펄쩍 뛰는지, 전갈자리, 곧 전갈이 우리 등을 쏘아 댄다. 그 상처를 치료하고 있노라면, 휙휙 사방에서 화살이 날아든다. 궁수자리, 곧 사수가 제 흥에 겨워 노는 게다. 우리가 그 화살대를 뽑아내는데, 비켜라! 여기 들이받는 숫양, 곧 염소자리, 곧 산양이 전속력으로 돌진해 오니, 우리는 곤두박질치며 나가떨어진다. 그러면 물병자리, 곧 물 긷는 자가 제 홍수를 죄다 쏟아부어 우리를 익사시킨다. 그리고 마지막을 장식하는 물고기자리, 곧 물고기와 더불어 우리는 잠이 든다. 자, 여기 드높은 하늘에 쓰인 한 편의 설교가 있고, 태양은 해마다 그것을 두루 거치면서도 매번 멀쩡히 살아 기운차게 빠져나온다. 저 위에서 그놈은 수고와 시련을 즐겁게 헤쳐 나가지. 그러니 여기 아래에서도 즐거운 스터브가 그렇게 한다. 오, 즐거움이야말로 영원한 한마디로다! 잘 있거라, 금화야! 그런데 가만, 저기 꼬마 킹포스트가 오는구나. 자, 시유 가마 뒤로 슬쩍 비켜서서 저 친구가 무슨 말을 하는지 한번 들어 보자. 옳지, 그가 그 앞에 섰구나. 곧 무슨 말이든 내뱉겠지. 그래, 그래. 시작하는군.\"\n\n\"내 눈엔 여기 금으로 만든 둥근 물건 하나밖에 안 보이는데, 어떤 고래를 잡아내든 그놈을 잡은 자에게 이 둥근 물건이 돌아간다 이거지. 그런데 이 빤히 들여다보는 소동은 다 뭐람? 이게 십육 달러 값어치라는 건 사실이야. 그리고 시가 한 대에 두 푼이라 치면, 구백육십 대로구먼. 나야 스터브처럼 더러운 파이프는 안 피우지만 시가는 좋아하지. 그러니 여기 구백육십 대가 있는 셈이렷다. 그러니 플래스크는 그놈들을 잡아내러 돛대 위로 올라가야겠다.\"\n\n\"저걸 두고 지혜롭다 해야 할지 어리석다 해야 할지. 정말로 지혜로운 거라면 어딘가 어리석어 보이고, 정말로 어리석은 거라면 또 어딘가 그럴듯이 지혜로워 보인단 말이지. 한데 가만 있자, 저기 늙은 맨섬 사람이 오는구나—저 늙은이는 바다로 나오기 전엔 영구차나 몰던 자였을 게 분명해. 저 친구가 금화 앞에서 바람을 거슬러 멈춰 서더니, 어럽쇼, 돛대 반대편으로 돌아가는군. 아, 그쪽엔 편자가 못 박혀 있지. 그러더니 이제 다시 돌아왔구나. 저게 무슨 뜻이람? 들어 봐! 뭔가 중얼거리는데—목소리가 낡아 빠진 커피 분쇄기 같구나. 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 들어 보자!\"\n\n\"흰 고래가 잡혀 나온다면, 그것은 태양이 이 별자리들 가운데 하나에 머무는 한 달하고 하루 안의 일이렷다. 나는 별자리를 공부했고 그 표징을 안다. 마흔 해 전에 코펜하겐의 늙은 마녀에게 배웠지. 자, 그러면 그때 태양은 어느 별자리에 들겠는가? 편자 별자리렷다. 보라, 저기 금화 바로 맞은편에 있지 않은가. 그렇다면 편자 별자리란 무엇인가? 사자가 곧 편자 별자리렷다—으르렁대며 집어삼키는 사자 말이다. 배여, 늙은 배여! 네 생각을 하니 이 늙은 머리가 절로 흔들리는구나.\"\n\n\"또 다른 풀이가 나왔군. 하지만 본문은 여전히 하나지. 보다시피 한 가지 세상 속에 온갖 부류의 사람이 다 있단 말이야. 또 비켜야겠다! 저기 퀴케그가 오는구나—온통 문신투성이라—제 몸 자체가 황도대의 표징처럼 보이는걸. 저 식인종이 뭐라는지 들어 볼까? 살아생전 처음 보는 일이지, 저 친구가 비교를 하고 있구나. 제 넓적다리뼈를 들여다보는걸. 아마 태양이 넓적다리에 들었거나, 종아리에, 아니면 창자 속에 들었거니 여기는 게지. 시골 노파들이 외과 점성술을 떠벌리는 것처럼 말이야. 그리고 제우스에 걸고, 저 친구가 넓적다리께에서 뭔가를 찾아냈구나—내 짐작엔 궁수자리, 곧 사수렷다. 아니야, 저 친구는 금화를 어찌 봐야 할지 모르는걸. 어느 왕의 바지에서 떨어져 나온 낡은 단추쯤으로 여기는 모양이군. 한데 또 옆으로 비키자! 저기 그 유령 같은 악마, 페달라가 오는구나. 여느 때처럼 꼬리는 보이지 않게 사리고, 여느 때처럼 펌프스 발끝에는 뱃밥을 쑤셔 넣었군. 저 친구가 저 눈빛으로 무슨 말을 하나? 아하, 그저 그 표징을 향해 신호를 보내며 절을 하는구나. 금화에는 태양이 있으니—저 친구는 분명 불을 숭배하는 자야. 호! 점점 더하는군. 이쪽으로 핍이 오는구나—가엾은 녀석! 차라리 저 애가 죽었거나, 내가 죽었으면 좋았으련만. 저 애는 어쩐지 절반쯤 끔찍하단 말이야. 저 애도 여태 이 해석자들을—나 자신까지 포함해서—죄다 지켜보고 있었구나. 자, 봐라, 저 세상 것 아닌 얼간이 같은 얼굴을 하고서 읽으러 오는구나. 또 비켜서서 저 애 말을 들어 보자. 들어 봐!\"\n\n\"나는 본다, 너는 본다, 그는 본다. 우리는 본다, 너희는 본다, 그들은 본다.\"\n\n\"내 영혼을 걸고, 저 애가 머레이의 문법책을 공부했구나! 가엾은 녀석이 제 머리를 갈고닦았다니! 그런데 지금 뭐라는 거지—쉿!\"\n\n\"나는 본다, 너는 본다, 그는 본다. 우리는 본다, 너희는 본다, 그들은 본다.\"\n\n\"아니, 저 애가 그걸 외우고 있구나—쉿! 또 한다.\"\n\n\"나는 본다, 너는 본다, 그는 본다. 우리는 본다, 너희는 본다, 그들은 본다.\"\n\n\"흠, 거참 우습군.\"\n\n\"그리고 나와 너와 그, 우리와 너희와 그들은 죄다 박쥐다. 그리고 나는 까마귀로다, 특히 이 소나무 꼭대기에 올라설 때면 말이야. 까악! 까악! 까악! 까악! 까악! 까악! 내가 까마귀가 아니더냐? 그렇다면 허수아비는 어디 있느냐? 저기 서 있구나. 뼈다귀 두 개를 낡은 바지 한 벌에 꽂아 넣고, 또 두 개를 낡은 웃옷 소매에 쑤셔 박았구나.\"\n\n\"저게 날 두고 하는 말인가?—참 고마운 칭찬이로다!—가엾은 녀석!—내 차라리 목이라도 매고 싶구나. 어쨌든 지금은 핍 곁을 떠나야겠다. 다른 자들이야 견딜 만하지, 그들 머리는 멀쩡하니까. 한데 저 애는 너무도 미친 듯 영악해서 멀쩡한 내 정신으로는 못 견디겠다. 자, 그래, 저 애가 중얼거리도록 내버려 두고 떠나자.\"\n\n\"여기 이 금화는 배의 배꼽이렷다. 그런데 다들 그걸 풀어내려고 안달이 났구나. 하지만 네 배꼽을 풀어 보아라. 그럼 무슨 결과가 따르겠느냐? 그렇다고 또 그게 여기 그대로 박혀 있는 것도 흉하긴 매한가지지. 돛대에 무언가가 못 박혀 있다는 건 사정이 절박해진다는 표징이니까. 하하! 늙은 에이해브! 흰 고래가 너를 못 박아 버릴 게다! 이건 소나무로다. 우리 아버지가 옛 톨랜드 카운티에서 한번 소나무를 베었는데, 그 안에 은반지가 살처럼 감싸여 박혀 있는 걸 찾아냈지. 어느 늙은 흑인의 결혼반지였어. 그게 어쩌다 거기 들어갔을까? 그래서 부활의 날에도 그들은 그리 말하겠지. 이 낡은 돛대를 낚아 올렸다가 그 안에 박힌 금화를 찾아내고, 거친 나무껍질엔 굴들이 들러붙어 있는 걸 보고서 말이야. 오, 금이여! 그 귀하고 귀한 금이여! 머지않아 저 푸른 구두쇠가 너를 그러모아 쌓아 두겠지! 쉿! 쉿! 하느님은 세상들 사이를 거니시며 산딸기를 따 모으신다. 요리사! 어이, 요리사! 우리를 요리해 다오! 제니! 어이, 어이, 어이, 어이, 어이, 제니, 제니! 어서 옥수수빵이나 구워 다오!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 100. Leg and Arm.\n\n다리와 팔.\n낸터킷(Nantucket)의 피쿼드(Pequod)호, 런던의 새뮤얼 엔더비(Samuel Enderby)호를 만나다.\n\n\"이봐, 거기 배! 흰 고래(White Whale)를 본 적 있는가?\"\n\n에이해브(Ahab)가 그렇게 외쳤다. 영국 깃발을 단 배 한 척이 고물 쪽으로 바싹 다가오는 것을 보고 또 한 번 부른 것이다. 나팔을 입에 댄 채, 노인은 위로 들어 올린 고물 보트 안에 서 있었으며, 그의 상아 다리가 낯선 선장의 눈에 또렷이 드러났다. 그 선장은 제 보트의 뱃머리에 무심하게 몸을 기대고 있었다. 그는 햇볕에 거뭇하게 그을린, 건장하고 너그러우며 풍채 좋은 예순 안팎의 사내로, 파란 방수천을 휘장처럼 늘어뜨린 헐렁한 짧은 외투를 입고 있었다. 그 웃옷의 한쪽 빈 소매가 마치 경기병 군복의 수놓은 팔처럼 그의 등 뒤로 펄럭였다.\n\n\"흰 고래를 본 적 있는가?\"\n\n\"이걸 보았소?\" 그가 그것을 감추고 있던 옷자락에서 끌어내며, 망치 같은 나무 머리로 끝이 마무리된 향유고래 뼈로 된 새하얀 팔을 치켜들었다.\n\n\"내 보트에 사람을 붙여라!\" 에이해브가 격하게 외치며 곁에 있던 노들을 마구 들썩였다. \"내릴 준비를 하라!\"\n\n채 일 분도 지나지 않아, 그는 제 작은 배에서 내리지 않은 채 선원들과 함께 물 위로 떨어졌고, 곧 낯선 배의 곁에 다다랐다. 그러나 여기서 묘한 난관이 나타났다. 그 순간의 흥분 속에서, 에이해브는 다리를 잃은 이후로 바다에서 제 배가 아닌 어떤 배에도 단 한 번 올라 본 적이 없다는 사실을 잊고 있었다. 게다가 그가 오를 때면 늘 피쿼드호에만 있는 어떤 기발하고 매우 편리한 기계 장치를 썼는데, 그것은 다른 어떤 배에서도 즉석에서 설치하고 갖출 수 있는 물건이 아니었다. 그런데 망망대해에서 보트로부터 뱃전을 기어오르는 일은, 거의 시도 때도 없이 그 일에 익숙한 고래잡이가 아니라면 누구에게나 결코 쉬운 일이 아니다. 거대한 너울이 보트를 뱃전 가까이로 한껏 들어 올렸다가, 다음 순간 곧장 용골(龍骨) 가까이까지 절반쯤 떨어뜨리기 때문이다. 그리하여 한쪽 다리를 잃은 데다, 낯선 배에는 물론 그 친절한 발명품이 전혀 갖춰져 있지 않았으니, 에이해브는 이제 자신이 다시금 비참하게도 서투른 뭍사람 신세로 전락한 것을 깨달았다. 그는 도무지 닿을 가망조차 보이지 않는, 종잡을 수 없이 출렁이는 높이를 절망스레 바라볼 뿐이었다.\n\n앞서 어렴풋이 내비친 바 있을 것이다. 그에게 닥치는 온갖 사소한 거북한 사정, 그리고 그의 불운한 사고에서 간접적으로 비롯된 일은 거의 어김없이 에이해브를 짜증나게 하거나 격분케 했다는 것을. 그리고 지금의 경우에도, 낯선 배의 두 항해사가 못으로 박은 발판으로 된 수직 사다리 옆에서 뱃전 너머로 몸을 기울인 채, 그를 향해 멋스럽게 장식된 한 쌍의 밧줄 손잡이를 흔들어 내미는 광경에 그 모든 짜증이 더욱 도드라졌다. 처음에 그들은 외다리 사내가 그들의 뱃전 난간을 쓰기에는 너무 불편한 몸이라는 것을 미처 생각하지 못한 듯했다. 그러나 그 어색함은 일 분밖에 가지 않았으니, 낯선 선장이 한눈에 사정을 알아채고 이렇게 외친 까닭이었다. \"알겠소, 알겠소! 거기 끌어 올리는 것 멈춰라! 자네들, 어서 뛰어가 절단용 도르래를 휘둘러 넘겨라.\"\n\n운 좋게도, 그들은 하루나 이틀 전에 고래 한 마리를 뱃전에 매어 두었던 터라, 그 큼직한 도르래들이 아직도 높이 매달려 있었고, 이제 깨끗하게 마른 그 묵직한 곡선의 지방 갈고리가 여전히 그 끝에 달려 있었다. 이것이 재빨리 에이해브에게 내려졌고, 그는 단번에 모든 것을 알아차리고는 자신의 외다리 허벅지를 갈고리의 곡선 안으로 밀어 넣었다. 그것은 마치 닻의 갈고리 끝에 앉거나 사과나무의 갈라진 가지에 걸터앉는 것과 같았다. 그러고는 신호를 보내 단단히 몸을 지탱하면서, 동시에 도르래의 움직이는 줄 한 가닥을 두 손으로 번갈아 당겨 제 무게를 끌어 올리는 일을 거들었다. 곧 그는 높은 뱃전 안쪽으로 조심스럽게 흔들려 들어와, 양묘기 머리 위에 사뿐히 내려섰다. 다른 선장은 상아 팔을 환영하듯 거리낌 없이 앞으로 내밀며 다가왔고, 에이해브는 상아 다리를 내밀고 상아 팔을 (마치 두 자루의 황새치 칼날처럼) 맞대며, 그 바다코끼리 같은 투박한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"오, 그래, 반갑소! 우리 뼈끼리 한번 맞잡아 봅시다! 팔 하나와 다리 하나로 말이오! 보다시피 결코 움츠러들 줄 모르는 팔이요, 결코 달아날 줄 모르는 다리요. 어디서 흰 고래를 보았소? 얼마나 오래전이오?\"\n\n\"흰 고래 말이오.\" 영국인이 상아 팔을 동쪽으로 가리키며, 그것이 망원경이라도 되는 양 그 너머로 처량한 시선을 던지며 말했다. \"지난 철에, 적도 부근에서 그놈을 보았소.\"\n\n\"그래서 그놈이 그 팔을 떼어 갔단 말이오?\" 에이해브가 물으며, 양묘기에서 미끄러져 내려와 그러면서 영국인의 어깨에 몸을 기댔다.\n\n\"그렇소, 적어도 그놈이 원인이었지. 저 다리도 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그 이야기를 풀어 보시오.\" 에이해브가 말했다. \"어찌 된 일이었소?\"\n\n\"내 평생 적도 부근을 항해한 것이 그때가 처음이었소.\" 영국인이 입을 열었다. \"그때 나는 흰 고래에 관해 아무것도 몰랐소. 자, 어느 날 우리는 네댓 마리쯤 되는 고래 떼를 보고 보트를 내렸는데, 내 보트가 그중 한 마리에 작살을 박았소. 그놈은 영락없는 곡마단 말처럼, 빙글빙글 돌고 또 돌아 대는 통에, 내 보트 선원들은 모두 엉덩이를 바깥쪽 뱃전에 걸치고 앉아야 겨우 균형을 잡을 수 있었소. 그러자 곧 바다 밑바닥에서 펄쩍 솟구쳐 오르는 커다란 고래 한 마리가 있었는데, 머리와 혹이 젖빛처럼 새하얗고, 온통 까마귀 발자국 같은 주름이 잡혀 있었소.\"\n\n\"그놈이었군, 그놈이었어!\" 에이해브가 참고 있던 숨을 별안간 토해 내며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 그놈 오른쪽 지느러미 근처에 작살들이 박혀 있었소.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그건 내 것이었소, 내 작살이었지!\" 에이해브가 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"어서 계속하시오!\"\n\n\"그럼 좀 말할 틈을 주시오.\" 영국인이 너그러운 투로 말했다. \"자, 이 늙은 증조할아비 같은 놈은, 흰 머리와 혹을 하고서, 온통 거품을 일으키며 고래 떼 속으로 돌진하더니, 내 작살 줄을 사납게 물어뜯기 시작하는 거요!\"\n\n\"그렇지, 알겠소! 줄을 끊어 작살 박힌 고래를 풀어 주려 했군. 그건 오래된 수법이지. 내 그놈을 잘 알아.\"\n\n\"정확히 어찌 된 일인지는,\" 외팔의 선장이 계속했다. \"나도 모르겠소. 다만 줄을 물어뜯는 와중에, 그것이 그놈 이빨에 엉켜 어찌어찌 거기 걸려 버린 거요. 하지만 그때 우리는 그걸 몰랐소. 그래서 우리가 나중에 줄을 당겼을 때, 다른 고래가 아니라 그놈의 혹 위로 그대로 쾅 부딪쳐 버린 거요! 그 다른 고래는 꼬리를 휘저으며 바람 부는 쪽으로 달아나 버렸지. 사정이 어찌 되었는지, 그리고 그것이 얼마나 고귀하고 거대한 고래인지를 보고서 — 선생, 내 평생 본 것 중 가장 고귀하고 가장 큰 놈이었소 — 나는 그놈이 들끓는 분노에 차 있는 듯 보였음에도 그놈을 잡기로 마음먹었소. 그리고 그 위태롭게 걸린 줄이 풀려 버리거나, 그것이 엉킨 이빨이 뽑혀 버릴지도 모른다고 생각했소(내 보트 선원들이 고래 줄을 당기는 데는 지독히 거센 놈들이었으니까). 이 모든 사정을 보고서, 그러니까 말이오, 나는 일등 항해사의 보트로 뛰어들었소 — 여기 마운트톱(Mounttop) 씨의 보트로 말이오(그건 그렇고, 선장 양반 — 이쪽은 마운트톱이오. 마운트톱, 이쪽은 선장님일세). 말한 대로, 나는 마운트톱의 보트로 뛰어들었는데, 보다시피 그때 그 보트는 내 보트와 뱃전을 나란히 맞대고 있었소. 그래서 첫 번째 작살을 움켜쥐고, 이 늙은 증조할아비 같은 놈에게 한 방 먹였소. 그런데, 세상에, 보시오, 선생 — 살아 있는 영혼들이여 — 바로 다음 순간, 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 나는 박쥐처럼 앞을 못 보게 되었소. 두 눈이 다 멀어 버린 거요. 온통 검은 거품에 휩싸여 어찌할 바를 모르게 되었고, 그 속에서 고래 꼬리가 마치 대리석 첨탑처럼 곧추서서 허공으로 솟아오르는 거요. 그러니 모두 뒤로 물러나려 한들 소용이 없었지. 한낮에, 눈부신 햇빛이 온통 왕관 보석처럼 쏟아지는 가운데, 나는 더듬거리며 — 그러니까, 말한 대로, 두 번째 작살을 더듬어 찾아 바다로 던지려 하고 있었는데 — 그때 그 꼬리가 리마의 탑처럼 내리꽂혀, 내 보트를 두 동강 내고, 그 두 쪽을 산산조각 내 버렸소. 그러고는 꼬리부터 들이밀며, 그 하얀 혹이 마치 그것이 죄다 나뭇조각이라도 되는 양 잔해를 헤치고 뒷걸음쳐 빠져나갔소. 우리는 모두 헤엄쳐 빠져나오려 발버둥쳤소. 나는 그놈의 무시무시한 후려침을 피하려고 그놈에게 박혀 있는 내 작살 자루를 붙잡고서, 한순간 빨판상어처럼 거기 매달려 있었소. 그러나 부서지는 파도 한 줄기가 나를 떨쳐 냈고, 바로 그 순간 그 고래가 앞으로 한 번 세게 돌진하더니 번개처럼 물속으로 가라앉았소. 그리고 내 곁에서 끌려가던 그 저주받을 두 번째 작살의 미늘이 바로 여기를 걸어 버린 거요\"(그가 어깨 바로 아래를 손으로 탁 쳤다). \"그렇소, 바로 여기를 걸었단 말이오. 그리고 나를 지옥 불길 속으로 끌어내리는구나, 하고 나는 생각하고 있었소. 그런데, 그런데, 별안간, 자비로우신 하느님(God) 덕분에, 그 미늘이 살을 따라 쭉 찢고 나아가 — 내 팔의 길이를 따라 죄다 — 손목 가까이로 빠져나오면서, 나는 위로 떠올랐소. 그리고 나머지 이야기는 저기 저 신사 양반이 들려줄 거요(그건 그렇고, 선장 양반 — 이쪽은 벙거(Bunger) 박사, 우리 배의 의사라오. 벙거, 이 사람아, 이쪽은 선장님일세). 자, 벙거, 자네 몫의 이야기를 풀어 보게.\"\n\n이렇게 친근하게 가리켜진 그 전문직 신사는, 배에서의 신사다운 지위를 드러낼 만한 뚜렷한 표시 하나 없이, 내내 그들 곁에 서 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 더없이 둥글지만 점잖은 편이었고, 그는 빛바랜 파란 모직 작업복 혹은 셔츠에 기운 바지를 입고 있었다. 그는 지금껏 한 손에 든 밧줄 송곳과 다른 손에 든 알약 갑 사이로 주의를 나누어 두고 있었으며, 이따금 두 불구가 된 선장들의 상아 팔다리에 비평하는 듯한 눈길을 던지곤 했다. 그러나 자기 상관이 그를 에이해브에게 소개하자, 그는 정중하게 고개를 숙이고는 곧바로 선장의 분부를 이행하기 시작했다.\n\n\"끔찍하게 심한 상처였소.\" 그 고래잡이 배의 의사가 입을 열었다. \"그래서 내 충고를 받아들여, 여기 부머(Boomer) 선장은 우리 늙은 새미(Sammy)를 몰아 —\"\n\n\"새뮤얼 엔더비가 내 배 이름이오.\" 외팔의 선장이 에이해브를 향해 말을 가로챘다. \"계속하게, 이 사람아.\"\n\n\"우리 늙은 새미를 북쪽으로 몰아, 적도의 그 타는 듯한 더위에서 벗어나려 했소. 하지만 소용없었지. 나는 할 수 있는 모든 걸 다했소. 밤마다 그의 곁을 지키며 간호했고, 식이요법 문제에 있어서는 그에게 아주 엄격했소 —\"\n\n\"오, 아주 엄격했지!\" 환자 본인이 끼어들었다가, 별안간 목소리를 바꾸어 말했다. \"밤마다 나랑 같이 뜨거운 럼 토디를 마셔 대다가, 붕대를 어떻게 감아야 할지 보이지도 않을 지경이 되어서는, 새벽 세 시쯤 반쯤 취해 비틀거리며 나를 잠자리로 보냈단 말이오. 오, 하늘의 별들이여! 이 사람이 정말로 내 곁을 지키며 간호했고, 내 식단에 아주 엄격했다니. 오! 대단한 간병인에다, 식이요법에 무척이나 엄격한 양반이라, 우리 벙거 박사는 말이오.(벙거, 이 개 같은 사람아, 한번 웃어 보게! 왜 안 웃나? 자네가 끔찍이도 유쾌한 악당이라는 걸 자네도 알면서.) 하지만, 계속하게, 이 사람아, 나는 다른 누구의 손에 목숨을 부지하느니 차라리 자네 손에 죽는 편이 낫겠네.\"\n\n\"선장님은, 존경하는 선생, 진작에 알아채셨겠지만,\" 침착하고 경건해 보이는 벙거가 에이해브에게 가볍게 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"이따금 농을 하시는 분이라오. 우리한테 저런 식의 재치 있는 이야기를 많이 풀어 놓으시지요. 하지만 — 프랑스 사람들 말마따나 지나가는 김에(en passant) — 나 자신은, 그러니까 잭 벙거, 한때 목사 노릇을 하던 이 사람은, 철저한 절대 금주가라는 걸 말해 두는 게 좋겠소. 나는 결코 마시는 법이 없소 —\"\n\n\"물 말이오!\" 선장이 외쳤다. \"이 사람은 절대 물을 안 마시지. 물은 이 사람에게 일종의 발작 같은 거라, 맹물을 마시면 공수병에 걸린단 말이오. 하지만 계속하게 — 그 팔 이야기를 계속하게.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 그러는 게 좋겠소.\" 의사가 차분하게 말했다. \"선생, 부머 선장의 농 섞인 방해가 있기 전에, 내가 말하려던 건, 나의 최선과 가장 엄격한 노력에도 불구하고 상처가 점점 더 나빠지기만 했다는 것이오. 사실 말이지요, 선생, 그것은 의사가 일찍이 본 것 중 가장 흉하게 벌어진 상처였소. 길이가 두 자에다 몇 인치가 더 되었지. 측연줄로 재 보았소. 한마디로, 그것이 까맣게 변했소. 나는 무슨 위험이 닥쳐오는지 알았고, 그래서 팔을 잘라 냈소. 하지만 저 상아 팔을 다는 일에는 내가 손을 댄 게 없소. 저런 건 온갖 규칙에 어긋나는 거요.\" (밧줄 송곳으로 그것을 가리키며) \"저건 선장이 한 일이지, 내가 한 게 아니오. 선장이 목수에게 만들라고 시킨 거요. 끝에다 저 곤봉 망치를 달게 한 건, 짐작건대 누군가의 머리통을 박살 내려는 속셈일 테지요. 한번은 내 머리를 그걸로 쳐 보려 했으니까. 이 양반은 가끔 악마 같은 광분에 빠진다오. 이 움푹 팬 자국이 보이오, 선생?\" (그가 모자를 벗고 머리카락을 헤쳐, 두개골에 사발처럼 움푹 팬 구멍을 드러냈다. 하지만 그것은 흉터의 흔적이라곤 조금도 없었고, 한 번이라도 상처였던 적이 있다는 어떤 표시도 없었다.) \"자, 저기 저 선장이 이게 어찌 생긴 건지 말해 줄 거요. 저 양반이 안다오.\"\n\n\"아니, 난 모르네.\" 선장이 말했다. \"하지만 그의 어미는 알았겠지. 그는 그걸 갖고 태어났으니까. 오, 이 점잖은 척하는 악당아, 이 — 이 벙거 같으니! 이 물의 세상에 또 이런 벙거가 어디 있었겠소? 벙거, 자네가 죽거든, 마땅히 소금물에 절여져 죽어야 하네, 이 개 같은 사람아. 후세를 위해 보존되어야 마땅하단 말일세, 이 악당아.\"\n\n\"흰 고래는 어찌 되었소?\" 이제 에이해브가 외쳤다. 그는 지금껏 두 영국인 사이의 이 곁다리 수작을 안달하며 듣고 있던 참이었다.\n\n\"오!\" 외팔의 선장이 외쳤다. \"오, 그렇지! 자, 그놈이 물속으로 잠수한 뒤로, 우리는 한동안 그놈을 다시 보지 못했소. 사실, 앞서 넌지시 말했듯이, 나는 그때만 해도 내게 그런 장난을 친 게 무슨 고래인지 몰랐소. 그러다 한참 뒤에 다시 적도로 돌아왔을 때, 모비 딕(Moby Dick) — 어떤 이들은 그놈을 그렇게 부르더군 — 에 관한 이야기를 듣고 나서야, 비로소 그게 그놈이었다는 걸 알았소.\"\n\n\"그놈의 지나간 자취를 다시 마주친 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"두 번.\"\n\n\"하지만 작살을 박지는 못했고?\"\n\n\"박아 보려고도 하지 않았소. 팔다리 하나 잃은 걸로 충분하지 않소? 이 나머지 팔마저 없으면 난 어쩌란 말이오? 그리고 내 생각에 모비 딕은 무는 게 아니라 통째로 삼켜 버린단 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 벙거가 끼어들었다. \"오른팔을 되찾으려고 왼팔을 미끼로 내주시구려. 두 분, 아시오?\" (그가 두 선장에게 차례로 더없이 진지하고 수학적인 태도로 고개를 숙이며) \"두 분, 아시오, 고래의 소화 기관이 신의 섭리로 어찌나 헤아릴 수 없게 만들어졌는지, 사람의 팔 하나조차 그놈이 완전히 소화한다는 게 도무지 불가능하다는 걸? 그리고 그놈도 그걸 안단 말이오. 그러니 두 분이 흰 고래의 악의라고 여기는 것은 실은 그놈의 서투름일 뿐이오. 그놈은 결코 팔다리 하나도 삼킬 작정이 아니란 말이오. 다만 으르대는 시늉으로 겁을 주려는 것뿐이지요. 하지만 가끔 그놈은, 예전에 실론(Ceylon)에서 내 환자였던 그 늙은 요술쟁이 같은 짓을 하기도 한다오. 그 사람은 잭나이프를 삼키는 척하다가, 한번은 정말로 하나를 제 속으로 떨어뜨려 버렸는데, 그게 일 년 남짓이나 거기 머물러 있었소. 그래서 내가 토하게 하는 약을 주었더니, 그게 자잘한 조각이 되어 게워져 나오더이다, 보시오. 그놈이 그 잭나이프를 소화시켜 제 몸 전체에 온전히 받아들일 방도란 도무지 없는 거요. 그렇소, 부머 선장, 만일 충분히 재빠르게 일을 처리하고, 한쪽 팔을 점잖게 묻어 줄 특권을 누리기 위해 나머지 한쪽 팔을 저당 잡힐 마음이 있다면, 그렇다면 그 팔은 선장 것이 되는 거요. 다만 머지않아 그 고래에게 선장을 한 번 더 노릴 기회를 주기만 하면 되는 거지, 그게 다요.\"\n\n\"아니, 사양하겠네, 벙거.\" 영국인 선장이 말했다. \"어차피 어쩔 도리도 없었고, 그때는 그놈을 알지도 못했으니, 그놈이 가져간 이 팔이야 그놈에게 줘 버린 셈 치겠소. 하지만 나머지 하나까지는 안 되오. 내겐 더 이상 흰 고래 따위는 없소. 한 번 그놈을 잡으려 보트를 내려 봤고, 그걸로 충분히 만족했소. 그놈을 죽이면 크나큰 영광이 따르리란 걸, 나도 잘 알지요. 그리고 그놈 안에는 배 한 척을 가득 채울 만큼의 귀한 향유가 들어 있소. 하지만, 잘 들으시오, 그놈은 그냥 내버려 두는 게 상책이오. 선장도 그리 생각하지 않소?\" (상아 다리를 흘긋 보면서.)\n\n\"그렇소. 하지만 그래도 그놈은 여전히 쫓길 거요. 그냥 내버려 두는 게 상책인 것이라 해서, 그 저주받은 것이 늘 사람을 가장 덜 끌어당기는 법은 아니지. 그놈은 온통 자석이오! 그놈을 마지막으로 본 게 언제요? 어느 방향으로 가고 있었소?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 그리고 저 더러운 악마의 영혼에 저주를.\" 벙거가 몸을 구부린 채 에이해브 주위를 돌며, 마치 개처럼 기이하게 코를 킁킁대며 외쳤다. \"이 사람 피가 — 체온계를 가져오게! — 끓는점에 이르렀어! 이 사람 맥박이 이 갑판널을 두드리고 있다고! 선생!\" (그가 주머니에서 작은 칼을 꺼내, 에이해브의 팔 가까이로 다가서며.)\n\n\"멈춰!\" 에이해브가 그를 뱃전에 내동댕이치며 호통쳤다. \"보트에 사람을 붙여라! 어느 방향으로 가고 있었소?\"\n\n\"이런 맙소사!\" 그 질문을 받은 영국인 선장이 외쳤다. \"도대체 무슨 일이오? 그놈은 동쪽으로 가고 있었던 것 같소. 당신네 선장이 미친 거요?\" 그가 페달라(Fedallah)에게 속삭였다.\n\n그러나 페달라는 손가락 하나를 입술에 갖다 댄 채 뱃전을 넘어 미끄러져 내려가 보트의 키잡이 노를 잡았고, 에이해브는 절단용 도르래를 제 쪽으로 흔들어 끌어당기며, 그 배의 선원들에게 내릴 준비를 하라고 명령했다.\n\n순식간에 그는 보트의 고물에 서 있었고, 마닐라 사람들은 노를 향해 뛰어들고 있었다. 영국인 선장이 그를 불러 보았으나 헛일이었다. 낯선 배에 등을 돌리고, 제 배를 향해 부싯돌처럼 굳은 얼굴을 한 채, 에이해브는 피쿼드호 곁에 다다를 때까지 꼿꼿이 서 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 101. The Decanter.\n\n그 영국 배가 시야에서 사라지기 전에 여기 적어 두자면, 그 배는 런던에서 출항했으며, 그 도시의 상인이자 명문 포경 상사 엔더비 부자(父子) 상회의 창립자인 고(故) 새뮤얼 엔더비(Samuel Enderby)의 이름을 따서 명명되었다. 이 상회는, 보잘것없는 한 포경꾼인 내 소견으로는, 실제 역사적 흥미라는 점에서 튜더 왕가와 부르봉 왕가를 합친 두 왕실에도 그리 뒤지지 않는다. 우리 주님의 해 1775년 이전에 이 위대한 포경 상회가 얼마나 오래 존속해 왔는지는 내가 모아 둔 수많은 고래 문서들로도 분명히 밝혀지지 않는다. 그러나 바로 그해(1775년)에 이 상회는 향유고래(Sperm Whale)를 정규적으로 사냥한 최초의 영국 배들을 채비해 내보냈다. 물론 그보다 수십 년 전부터(1726년 이래로) 낸터킷(Nantucket)과 비니어드의 용맹한 코핀가(家)와 메이시가(家) 사람들이 대규모 선단을 이루어 그 리바이어던(Leviathan)을 뒤쫓아 왔으나, 그것은 다만 북대서양과 남대서양에서였을 뿐, 다른 곳에서는 아니었다. 여기 분명히 기록해 두건대, 낸터킷 사람들이야말로 문명의 강철 작살로 그 거대한 향유고래를 찔러 잡은 최초의 인류였으며, 반세기 동안 온 지구를 통틀어 그렇게 그 고래를 작살로 잡은 유일한 종족이었다.\n\n1778년, 정예 선박 어밀리아호가 바로 그 목적을 위해, 그리고 정력적인 엔더비 일가의 단독 부담으로 채비를 갖추어 대담하게 혼곶을 돌았으니, 이는 여러 나라 가운데 처음으로 저 광대한 남태평양에 그 어떤 종류의 고래잡이 보트라도 내려뜨린 일이었다. 그 항해는 능란하고도 운 좋은 항해였다. 그리고 귀한 향유로 선창을 가득 채운 채 정박지로 돌아온 어밀리아호의 선례를, 곧 영국과 미국의 다른 배들이 뒤따랐고, 그리하여 태평양의 드넓은 향유고래 어장이 활짝 열렸다. 그러나 이 갸륵한 공적에 만족하지 않고, 지칠 줄 모르는 이 상회는 다시금 분주히 나섰다. 새뮤얼과 그의 아들 모두가 — 그 수가 몇이나 되는지는 그들의 어머니만이 알 터인데 — 그들의 직접적인 후원 아래, 또한 일부는 내 생각에 그들의 비용으로, 영국 정부를 움직여 슬루프형 군함 래틀러호를 남태평양으로의 고래잡이 탐사 항해에 내보내게 했다. 정규 해군 함장의 지휘 아래 래틀러호는 요란한 항해를 펼쳐 얼마간 공을 세웠으나, 그것이 얼마만한 것이었는지는 드러나 있지 않다. 그러나 이것이 전부가 아니다. 1819년에 같은 상회는 자기네 소유의 탐사용 고래잡이배 한 척을 채비해, 일본의 머나먼 바다로 시험 삼아 순항을 떠나보냈다. 그 배는 — \"사이렌\"이라는 그럴듯한 이름이 붙었거니와 — 훌륭한 시험 순항을 해냈으니, 이렇게 하여 그 위대한 일본 고래 어장이 처음으로 널리 알려지게 되었다. 이 유명한 항해에서 사이렌호를 지휘한 것은 낸터킷 출신의 코핀이라는 선장이었다.\n\n그러므로 엔더비 일가에 온갖 영예가 있으라. 그 상회는, 내 생각에, 오늘날까지도 존속하고 있다. 물론 창립자 새뮤얼은 이미 오래전에 닻줄을 풀고 저세상의 저 위대한 남태평양으로 떠났으리라.\n\n그의 이름을 딴 배는 그 영예에 값하는 것이었으니, 매우 빠른 범선이요 모든 면에서 훌륭한 선박이었다. 나는 언젠가 파타고니아 해안 어딘가에서 한밤중에 그 배에 올라, 선수루(船首樓) 아래에서 맛 좋은 플립을 마신 적이 있다. 우리는 멋진 갬을 가졌고, 그들은 하나같이 으뜸가는 사내들이었다 — 배에 탄 영혼 하나하나가 다 그러했다. 그들에게 짧은 삶과 유쾌한 죽음이 있기를. 그리고 내가 가졌던 그 멋진 갬은 — 늙은 에이해브(Ahab)가 상아 발꿈치로 그 갑판 판자를 디딘 지 한참 뒤, 아주 한참 뒤의 일이었거니와 — 그 배의 고결하고 묵직한 색슨식 환대를 떠올리게 한다. 그리고 내가 그 기억을 잊는 날이 온다면, 내 목사가 나를 잊고 악마가 나를 기억하게 되어도 좋다. 플립이라고? 우리가 플립을 마셨다고 했던가? 그렇다, 우리는 시간당 열 갤런의 속도로 그것을 들이켰다. 그리고 돌풍이 불어닥치자(파타고니아 앞바다는 돌풍이 잦으니까), 손님까지 포함한 모든 선원이 윗돛을 줄이라는 호출을 받았는데, 우리는 어찌나 머리 쪽이 무거웠던지 서로를 밧줄 고리에 매달아 위로 끌어올려야 할 지경이었다. 게다가 우리는 멋모르고 재킷 자락을 돛에 함께 말아 넣는 바람에, 울부짖는 강풍 속에 단단히 묶인 채 거기 매달려, 모든 술 취한 뱃사람들에게 경고가 될 본보기가 되고 말았다. 그렇지만 돛대들은 바다로 넘어가지 않았고, 이윽고 우리는 기어 내려왔는데, 어찌나 술이 깨어 멀쩡해졌던지 다시 플립을 돌려야만 했다. 다만 선수루 승강구로 사납게 쏟아져 들어온 짠 물보라가 내 입맛에는 그 플립을 좀 지나치게 묽고 짭짤하게 만들어 놓긴 했다.\n\n쇠고기는 훌륭했으니 — 질겼지만 씹을 만한 실속이 있었다. 어떤 이들은 황소 고기라 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 단봉낙타 고기라 했으나, 그것이 실제로 무엇이었는지 나로서는 확실히 알 수 없다. 만두도 있었는데, 작지만 든든하고, 좌우 대칭으로 둥글고, 도무지 부서지지 않는 만두였다. 내 생각엔 삼키고 난 뒤에도 그것들이 뱃속에서 굴러다니는 것을 느낄 수 있을 것만 같았다. 너무 앞으로 깊이 숙이기라도 하면, 당구공처럼 뱃속에서 튀어나올 위험이 있었다. 빵으로 말하자면 — 하지만 그건 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이었고, 게다가 괴혈병을 막아 주는 것이었다. 요컨대 그 빵은 그들이 가진 유일한 신선한 먹을거리를 담고 있었다. 그러나 선수루는 그리 밝지 않아, 그것을 먹을 때 어두운 구석으로 한 발짝 옮겨 가기가 아주 쉬웠다. 그러나 모든 것을 통틀어, 돛대 꼭대기에서 키까지 그 배를 따져 보고, 요리사의 솥들의 크기를 — 그 자신의 살아 있는 양피지 같은 솥(뱃살)까지 포함하여 — 헤아려 보건대, 이물에서 고물까지, 내 말하건대, 새뮤얼 엔더비호는 유쾌한 배였다. 먹을 것은 좋고도 넉넉했으며, 플립은 훌륭하고도 독했고, 사내들은 죄다 빼어난 친구들이라, 구두 뒤축에서 모자 띠까지 일류였다.\n\n그런데 어찌하여, 그대들은 생각하는가, 새뮤얼 엔더비호와, 내가 아는 몇몇 다른 영국 고래잡이배들이 — 물론 전부는 아니지만 — 그토록 이름난, 그토록 후한 배들이어서, 쇠고기와 빵과 술통과 농담을 돌리고도 먹고 마시고 웃는 일에 좀처럼 지치지 않았던 것일까? 내 그대들에게 일러 주리라. 이 영국 고래잡이배들의 넘쳐 나는 환대는 역사적 탐구의 대상이다. 그리고 나는 필요하다 싶을 때면 역사적 고래 탐구를 결코 아끼지 않았다.\n\n고래잡이업에서 영국인보다 앞선 것은 네덜란드인과 제일란트인과 덴마크인이었으니, 영국인은 그들에게서 오늘날에도 어업에 남아 있는 많은 용어들을 물려받았으며, 그보다 더한 것은, 푸짐하게 먹고 마시는 일에 관한 그들의 기름지고 오래된 풍습까지 물려받았다는 점이다. 대체로 보아 영국 상선은 선원들에게 인색하게 굴지만, 영국 고래잡이배는 그렇지 않기 때문이다. 따라서 영국인에게 이 고래잡이 환대라는 것은 일상적이고 자연스러운 일이 아니라 부수적이고 특수한 일이며, 그러므로 어떤 특별한 기원이 있을 수밖에 없으니, 그것을 여기서 짚어 보고 앞으로 더 밝혀 보고자 한다.\n\n리바이어던에 관한 역사들을 탐구하던 중에, 나는 어느 오래된 네덜란드어 책 한 권을 우연히 만났는데, 거기서 풍기는 곰팡내 나는 고래 냄새로 미루어 그것이 고래잡이에 관한 책임을 단번에 알아차렸다. 제목은 \"단 코프만(Dan Coopman)\"이었으니, 나는 모든 고래잡이배가 통메장이(쿠퍼)를 한 명씩 싣고 다녀야 하는 만큼, 이것이 어업에 종사한 어느 암스테르담 통메장이의 더없이 값진 회고록임에 틀림없다고 결론지었다. 그것이 \"피츠 스왁해머\"라는 자의 저작임을 보고서 이 생각은 한층 굳어졌다. 그러나 내 친구이자 산타클로스·성포트 대학의 저지(低地) 네덜란드어 및 고지(高地) 독일어 교수인, 대단히 박식한 스노드헤드 박사에게 — 수고에 대한 사례로 향유고래 양초 한 상자를 건네며 — 그 책을 번역해 달라고 넘겼더니, 바로 이 스노드헤드 박사가 그 책을 보자마자 \"단 코프만\"은 \"통메장이\"가 아니라 \"상인\"이라는 뜻이라고 확언해 주었다. 요컨대 이 오래되고 학식 깊은 저지 네덜란드어 책은 네덜란드의 상업을 다룬 것이었고, 여러 주제들 가운데 그 나라의 고래잡이업에 관한 대단히 흥미로운 기록을 담고 있었다. 그리고 바로 그 안에, \"스메르(Smeer)\" 곧 \"기름\"이라는 제목이 붙은 장(章)에서, 나는 네덜란드 고래잡이배 180척의 식료품 저장고와 술 창고를 채울 보급품의 길고 상세한 목록을 발견했으니, 스노드헤드 박사가 번역해 준 그 목록에서 다음을 옮겨 적는다.\n\n쇠고기 40만 파운드. 프리슬란트산 돼지고기 6만 파운드. 건어물 15만 파운드. 비스킷 55만 파운드. 부드러운 빵 7만 2천 파운드. 버터 2,800퍼킨. 텍셀산 및 레이던산 치즈 2만 파운드. 치즈 14만 4천 파운드(아마도 질 낮은 것). 진(제네바주) 550앵커. 맥주 1만 800배럴.\n\n대부분의 통계표는 읽노라면 입이 바짝 마르도록 무미건조하지만, 지금 이 경우만은 그렇지 않으니, 여기서는 독자가 통째로 쏟아지는 파이프와 배럴, 쿼트와 질 단위의 좋은 진과 좋은 먹을거리에 흠뻑 잠기게 되는 것이다.\n\n그때 나는 이 모든 맥주와 쇠고기와 빵을 면밀히 소화하는 데 사흘을 바쳤는데, 그동안 초월적이고 플라톤적인 적용이 가능한 여러 심오한 사색들이 부수적으로 내게 떠올랐다. 더 나아가 나는 그 옛 그린란드와 스피츠베르겐 고래잡이업에서 저지 네덜란드인 작살잡이 한 사람이 소비했을 건어물 따위의 짐작 분량에 관하여, 나 자신의 보충 통계표까지 작성했다. 우선, 소비된 버터와 텍셀산·레이던산 치즈의 양은 놀라울 지경으로 보인다. 다만 나는 이를 그들이 타고난 기름진 천성 탓으로 돌리는데, 그 천성은 그들의 직업의 성격으로 인해, 특히 그들이 그 얼어붙은 극지의 바다에서, 곧 사람 좋은 원주민들이 고래기름을 가득 채운 잔으로 서로 축배를 드는 저 에스키모 땅의 바로 그 해안에서 사냥감을 뒤쫓는 까닭에 한층 더 기름지게 되었던 것이다.\n\n맥주의 양 또한 대단히 많으니, 1만 800배럴이다. 그런데 그 극지 어업은 그 기후의 짧은 여름철에만 수행될 수 있었으므로, 이 네덜란드 고래잡이배 한 척의 전체 순항은, 스피츠베르겐 바다를 오가는 짧은 항해까지 포함하더라도 석 달을 그리 넘지 않았다고 하고, 그들의 180척 선단에서 한 척당 30명씩 헤아리면, 모두 합쳐 5,400명의 저지 네덜란드 선원이 있는 셈이다. 그러므로 내 말하건대, 우리는 한 사람당 정확히 맥주 두 배럴씩을, 그것도 진 550앵커 가운데 그가 마땅히 차지할 몫은 빼고서, 열두 주의 배급량으로 갖게 되는 것이다. 그런데 이 진과 맥주에 절은 작살잡이들이, 누구든 짐작할 만큼 곤드레만드레가 되고서도, 보트의 이물에 버티고 서서 날쌔게 달아나는 고래를 제대로 겨냥할 만한 부류의 사내들이었느냐 하면, 그것은 다소 미덥지 않아 보인다. 그러나 그들은 정말로 고래를 겨누었고, 또 명중시키기도 했다. 하지만 이곳은 아주 먼 북쪽이었음을 기억해 두자. 그곳에서는 맥주가 몸에 잘 맞는다. 적도에서, 우리 남방 어업에서라면, 맥주는 작살잡이를 돛대 꼭대기에서 졸게 하고 보트 안에서 곤드레가 되게 하기 십상이어서, 낸터킷과 뉴베드퍼드에 통탄할 손실이 닥칠 수도 있을 것이다.\n\n그러나 더는 말하지 않겠다. 두세 세기 전의 옛 네덜란드 고래잡이들이 호화롭게 먹고 마신 자들이었으며, 영국 고래잡이들이 그토록 훌륭한 본보기를 소홀히 하지 않았다는 것을 보이는 데는 이만하면 충분하다. 그들의 말마따나, 빈 배로 순항할 때 세상에서 더 나은 것을 얻을 수 없다면, 적어도 좋은 저녁 한 끼라도 얻어 내라는 것이다. 그리고 이로써 술병은 비워진다.\n\n# CHAPTER 102. A Bower in the Arsacides.\n\n지금까지 향유고래(Sperm Whale)를 묘사하여 다루면서, 나는 주로 그 바깥 모습의 경이로움에 머물러 있었거나, 혹은 몇 가지 내부 구조의 특징들을 따로따로 자세히 살폈을 뿐이다. 그러나 그를 폭넓고도 철저하게 두루 이해하기 위해서는, 이제 나는 그의 옷고름을 한층 더 풀어헤치고, 그의 양말 매듭을 끄르고, 그의 대님을 풀고, 그 가장 깊숙한 뼈마디를 잇는 갈고리와 고리를 모두 풀어 헤쳐, 마침내 그를 그 최종의 모습으로, 다시 말해 아무 조건 없는 골격 그 자체로 여러분 앞에 세워 놓아야 마땅하다.\n\n그런데 이제 와서 무슨 소린가, 이슈메일? 어찌하여 그대, 고래잡이배의 한낱 노잡이에 불과한 그대가 고래의 땅속 깊은 부분에 관해 무엇이든 안다는 듯이 굴 수 있단 말인가? 박식한 스터브(Stubb)가 그대의 캡스턴 위에 올라서서 고래목(目)의 해부학을 강의하기라도 했단 말인가, 그리고 양묘기의 힘을 빌려 본보기 갈빗대 하나를 들어 올려 전시라도 했단 말인가? 그대 스스로를 해명해 보라, 이슈메일. 요리사가 통구이 새끼돼지를 접시에 담아내듯, 그대는 다 자란 고래를 갑판 위에 올려놓고 살펴볼 수 있단 말인가? 결단코 그럴 수 없으리라. 이슈메일, 그대는 지금까지 진실한 증인이었다. 그러나 오직 요나(Jonah)만이 누렸던 특권을 함부로 움켜쥐려 들지 말라. 곧 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 골조를 이루는 들보와 대들보, 서까래와 마룻대, 침목과 토대에 관하여 논할 특권, 어쩌면 그 뱃속의 기름 가마와 우유 방과 식료품 저장실과 치즈 광에 관하여 논할 특권 말이다.\n\n고백하건대, 요나 이래로 다 자란 고래의 살갗 밑을 아주 깊이 파고든 고래잡이는 드물었다. 그럼에도 나는 그 축소판을 해부해 볼 기회를 다행히도 얻었다. 내가 몸담았던 어느 배에서 한번은 작은 새끼 향유고래 한 마리를 통째로 갑판 위로 끌어올린 적이 있는데, 그 가죽 주머니로 작살의 미늘과 창의 머리를 감쌀 칼집을 만들기 위해서였다. 그런 기회를 내가 그냥 흘려보냈으리라 생각하는가, 보트용 손도끼와 잭나이프를 써서 그 봉인을 뜯고 그 어린 새끼의 속을 낱낱이 읽어 내지 않은 채로?\n\n그리고 거대하게 다 자란 리바이어던의 뼈에 관한 내 정확한 지식으로 말하자면, 그 흔치 않은 지식은 작고한 나의 왕가의 벗 트랑쿠오, 곧 아르사키데스 군도 가운데 하나인 트랑케의 왕에게서 얻은 것이다. 여러 해 전, 무역선 알제(Algiers)의 데이 호에 몸담고 있던 시절 트랑케에 머물 적에, 나는 트랑케의 군주와 더불어 아르사키데스의 명절 일부를 그의 외딴 야자수 별장이 있는 푸펠라에서 보내자는 초대를 받았는데, 그곳은 우리 선원들이 뱀부타운, 곧 대나무 마을이라 부르던 그의 수도에서 그리 멀지 않은 바닷가 골짜기였다.\n\n여러 빼어난 자질 가운데서도, 나의 왕가의 벗 트랑쿠오는 야만적인 골동 취향에 관한 모든 것을 향한 경건한 애정을 타고난 사람이어서, 자기 백성 가운데 더 솜씨 좋은 자들이 빚어낼 수 있는 온갖 진귀한 물건들을 푸펠라에 한데 모아 두었다. 주로 놀라운 도안을 새긴 목각이며, 정교하게 깎은 조개껍데기며, 상감을 박은 창이며, 값비싼 노며, 향내 나는 카누 같은 것들이었다. 그리고 이 모든 것들은, 경이를 가득 싣고 공물을 바치듯 밀려온 파도가 그의 해안에 던져 놓은 온갖 자연의 진기한 것들 사이에 두루 배치되어 있었다.\n\n이 후자의 것들 가운데 으뜸가는 것은 거대한 향유고래였는데, 유난히 오래 휘몰아친 폭풍이 지나간 뒤 죽은 채로 좌초되어 발견된 것으로, 그 머리는 야자나무 한 그루에 기대어 있었고, 깃털처럼 술 모양으로 늘어진 그 나무의 잎사귀들은 마치 고래의 푸른 물줄기처럼 보였다. 그 거대한 몸뚱이가 마침내 한 길 깊이로 두텁게 감싸고 있던 살을 모두 벗겨 내고, 뼈가 햇볕에 먼지처럼 바싹 마르게 되자, 그 골격은 조심스럽게 푸펠라 골짜기 위쪽으로 옮겨졌고, 그곳에서 위풍당당한 야자수들의 웅장한 신전이 이제 그것을 품어 주고 있었다.\n\n갈빗대마다 전리품이 걸렸고, 등뼈마다 기묘한 상형문자로 아르사키데스의 연대기가 새겨졌다. 두개골 속에서는 사제들이 꺼지지 않는 향불을 피워, 그 신비로운 머리가 다시금 안개 같은 물줄기를 뿜어내게 했다. 한편 나뭇가지에 매달린 무시무시한 아래턱은, 다모클레스를 그토록 떨게 했던 머리카락에 매달린 칼처럼, 모든 신도들의 머리 위에서 흔들거렸다.\n\n그것은 경이로운 광경이었다. 숲은 빙곡(氷谷)의 이끼처럼 푸르렀고, 나무들은 제 안에 흐르는 생생한 수액을 느끼며 높고 도도하게 서 있었다. 그 아래 부지런한 대지는 마치 직조공의 베틀과도 같았으니, 그 위에 화려한 양탄자가 깔리고, 땅을 기는 덩굴 줄기가 날줄과 씨줄을 이루며, 살아 있는 꽃들이 무늬를 이루고 있었다. 짐을 가득 진 가지들을 거느린 모든 나무들, 모든 떨기나무와 고사리와 풀들, 소식을 실어 나르는 공기, 이 모든 것들이 쉼 없이 활동하고 있었다. 잎사귀의 얼기설기한 틈 사이로, 위대한 태양은 지치지 않는 푸르름을 짜 내는 나는 북처럼 보였다. 오, 바쁜 직조공이여! 보이지 않는 직조공이여! 멈추라! 한마디만! 그 천은 어디로 흘러가는가? 어떤 궁전을 장식하려는가? 무엇 때문에 이 끝없는 수고를 하는가? 말하라, 직조공이여! 손을 멈추라! 그대와 단 한마디만 나누고 싶구나! 아니, 북은 날아간다. 무늬들은 베틀에서 흘러나와 떠가고, 봇물처럼 쏟아지는 양탄자는 영영 미끄러져 사라진다. 직조공인 신, 그는 천을 짠다. 그리고 그 직조 소리에 귀가 먹어 그는 어떤 인간의 목소리도 듣지 못한다. 그 윙윙거리는 소리에 베틀을 바라보는 우리 또한 귀가 먹는다. 그래서 오직 우리가 거기서 벗어날 때에야 비로소 그 베틀을 통해 말해지는 수천의 목소리를 듣게 되리라. 무릇 모든 물질의 공장에서도 사정은 이와 같다. 날아다니는 방추 사이에서는 들리지 않던 그 말들이, 똑같은 그 말들이 벽 바깥에서는, 열린 들창으로 터져 나오며 또렷이 들리는 법이다. 그리하여 악행이 드러나기도 했다. 아, 죽을 운명의 인간이여! 그러니 조심하라. 이 위대한 세계의 베틀이 내는 온갖 소음 속에서도, 그대의 가장 은밀한 생각들이 멀리서 엿들릴 수 있으니.\n\n자, 그 아르사키데스 숲의 푸르고 생명으로 들끓는 베틀 한가운데, 그 거대하고 새하얗고 숭배받는 골격이 비스듬히 누워 있었다. 거대한 게으름뱅이가! 그러나 끊임없이 짜이는 푸른 날줄과 씨줄이 그를 둘러싸고 뒤섞이며 윙윙거릴 때면, 그 위대한 게으름뱅이는 도리어 교묘한 직조공처럼 보였다. 제 몸 전체가 덩굴로 온통 뒤덮여, 달이 갈 때마다 더 푸르고 더 싱그러운 푸르름을 걸쳤으나, 정작 그 자신은 한낱 골격이었다. 삶이 죽음을 감싸 안았고, 죽음이 삶을 시렁처럼 받쳐 올렸다. 그 음울한 신은 젊은 삶을 아내로 맞아, 곱슬머리의 영광들을 낳았다.\n\n자, 내가 트랑쿠오 왕과 더불어 이 경이로운 고래를 찾아가, 두개골이 제단이 되고 진짜 물줄기가 솟던 자리에서 인공의 연기가 피어오르는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 왕이 한낱 예배당을 골동의 대상으로 여긴다는 데에 놀랐다. 그러자 그는 웃었다. 그러나 사제들이 그 연기 같은 물줄기가 진짜라고 맹세하는 데에 나는 더욱 놀랐다. 나는 이 골격 앞을 이리저리 거닐며, 덩굴을 헤치고, 갈빗대 사이를 뚫고 들어가, 아르사키데스의 실타래 하나를 들고서 그 구불구불하고 그늘진 수많은 회랑과 정자 사이를 한참 헤매고 맴돌았다. 그러나 이내 실이 다 풀려 버렸고, 그 실을 되짚어 따라가다 보니, 나는 처음 들어왔던 그 입구로 다시 나오게 되었다. 안에서는 살아 있는 것이라곤 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그곳에 있는 것이라곤 오직 뼈뿐이었다.\n\n나는 푸른 측량용 장대 하나를 잘라 들고서 다시 한번 골격 안으로 파고들었다. 두개골에 난 화살구멍 같은 틈으로, 사제들이 내가 마지막 갈빗대의 높이를 재는 것을 알아챘다. \"이게 무슨 짓인가!\" 그들이 소리쳤다. \"감히 우리의 신을 측량하다니! 그건 우리가 할 일이다.\" \"그렇소, 사제들이여, 좋소, 그렇다면 그대들은 이놈을 몇 자로 헤아리오?\" 그런데 이 말이 떨어지자, 그들 사이에서 자와 치를 두고 사나운 논쟁이 일어나, 서로의 정수리를 측량 막대로 두들겨 깨뜨렸으니, 그 거대한 두개골이 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸다. 나는 이 절호의 기회를 붙잡아, 재빨리 내 나름의 측량을 마무리 지었다.\n\n이제 나는 이 측량 수치를 여러분 앞에 내놓으려 한다. 그러나 먼저 기록해 두건대, 이 일에서 나는 내키는 대로 아무 상상 속의 수치나 함부로 늘어놓을 처지가 못 된다. 왜냐하면 내 정확성을 시험해 볼 수 있도록 여러분이 참고할 만한 골격 권위자들이 따로 있기 때문이다. 듣자 하니, 영국 헐에 리바이어던 박물관이 있다는데, 그곳은 그 나라의 고래잡이 항구 가운데 하나로, 거기에는 긴수염고래며 그 밖의 고래들의 훌륭한 표본 몇 점이 있다고 한다. 또한 들은 바로는, 뉴햄프셔주 맨체스터의 박물관에는 그 소유주들이 \"미합중국 내 유일한 완벽한 그린란드고래, 곧 강고래 표본\"이라 일컫는 것이 있다고 한다. 게다가 영국 요크셔의 버턴 콘스터블이라는 곳에는, 어느 클리퍼드 콘스터블 경이라는 이가 향유고래 골격 한 구를 소장하고 있는데, 다만 크기가 적당한 정도여서, 결코 나의 벗 트랑쿠오 왕의 것처럼 다 자란 거대한 규모는 아니다.\n\n두 경우 모두, 이 두 골격이 본디 속해 있던 좌초한 고래들은, 그 소유주들이 비슷한 근거를 들어 자기 것이라 주장한 것이었다. 트랑쿠오 왕은 그저 자기가 원했기에 그것을 차지했고, 클리퍼드 경은 자기가 그 지역 영지의 영주였기에 차지했다. 클리퍼드 경의 고래는 골격 전체가 마디마디 연결되어 있어서, 마치 커다란 서랍장처럼, 그 뼈로 된 온갖 빈 공간을 여닫을 수도 있고, 그 갈빗대를 거대한 부채처럼 활짝 펼칠 수도 있으며, 종일토록 그 아래턱에 매달려 흔들흔들할 수도 있다. 그 뚜껑문과 덧문 가운데 몇몇에는 자물쇠를 채워 둘 예정이며, 하인 하나가 허리춤에 열쇠 꾸러미를 차고서 앞으로 찾아올 방문객들을 안내하며 두루 보여 줄 것이다. 클리퍼드 경은 척추 안의 속삭임 회랑을 들여다보는 데 2펜스를, 소뇌의 빈 공간에서 울리는 메아리를 듣는 데 3펜스를, 그 이마에서 바라보는 견줄 데 없는 경관에 6펜스를 받을 작정이라고 한다.\n\n이제 내가 적어 내려갈 골격의 치수는, 내 오른팔에서 한 글자도 틀림없이 베껴 온 것인데, 거기에 그 수치를 문신으로 새겨 두었기 때문이다. 그 무렵 거칠게 떠돌아다니던 처지에서는, 그토록 값진 통계를 보존할 다른 안전한 방도가 달리 없었던 것이다. 그러나 내 몸에 자리가 빠듯했던 데다, 그 무렵 내가 짓고 있던 시 한 편을 위해 내 몸의 다른 부분들은, 적어도 문신이 새겨지지 않은 채로 남아 있을 부분들만이라도 빈 종잇장으로 남겨 두고 싶었기에, 나는 자투리 인치까지는 굳이 신경 쓰지 않았다. 사실 고래를 그 격에 맞게 측량하는 데는, 인치 단위 따위는 애초에 끼어들 자리가 없는 법이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 103. Measurement of The Whale’s Skeleton.\n\n우선 나는 이 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 살아 있는 몸집에 관하여 명확하고 구체적인 설명을 여러분 앞에 펼쳐 보이고자 한다. 그 골격을 우리는 이제 간략히 살펴볼 참이다. 그러한 설명은 여기서 유용하게 쓰일 것이다.\n\n내가 신중하게 계산한 바에 따르면 — 그 일부는 스코어즈비 선장(Captain Scoresby)이 길이 60피트(약 18미터)에 이르는 가장 큰 그린란드 고래의 무게를 70톤으로 추정한 데 근거를 둔 것인데 — 내 그 면밀한 계산에 따르면, 길이가 85피트(약 26미터)에서 90피트(약 27미터) 사이이고 가장 굵은 둘레가 40피트(약 12미터)에 조금 못 미치는 가장 거대한 향유고래(Sperm Whale)는 적어도 90톤은 나갈 것이다. 그러니 한 톤에 열세 사람씩 친다면, 그 한 마리는 주민 1,100명이 사는 마을 전체의 인구를 합친 것보다도 훨씬 더 무거운 셈이다.\n\n그렇다면 이 리바이어던을 뭍사람의 상상 속에서 조금이라도 움직여 보이게 하려면, 멍에를 멘 소처럼 여러 사람의 머리를 한데 묶어 끌게 해야 마땅하지 않겠는가?\n\n이미 나는 여러 방식으로 그 두개골, 분수 구멍, 턱, 이빨, 꼬리, 이마, 지느러미, 그 밖의 갖가지 부위를 여러분 앞에 펼쳐 보였으니, 이제는 다만 그 가림 없이 드러난 뼈의 전체 크기에서 가장 흥미로운 점만을 짚어 보려 한다. 그러나 그 거대한 두개골은 골격 전체에서 워낙 큰 비중을 차지하는 데다, 단연코 가장 복잡한 부분이며, 이 장에서는 그것에 관해 다시 거론하지 않을 터이므로, 우리가 나아가는 동안 여러분은 그것을 머릿속에든 겨드랑이에든 반드시 지니고 있어야 한다. 그러지 않으면 이제 우리가 살펴보려는 전체 구조에 대한 온전한 개념을 얻지 못할 것이다.\n\n트랑케에 있던 향유고래의 골격은 길이가 72피트(약 22미터)였다. 그러니 살아서 살이 온전히 덮이고 몸이 다 펼쳐졌을 때라면 그 길이는 90피트(약 27미터)에 이르렀을 것이다. 고래의 경우, 골격은 살아 있는 몸에 비해 길이가 약 5분의 1쯤 줄어들기 때문이다. 이 72피트 가운데 두개골과 턱이 20피트(약 6미터)쯤을 차지했고, 나머지 50피트(약 15미터) 남짓이 순전한 등뼈였다. 이 등뼈에는 그 길이의 3분의 1에 조금 못 미치는 만큼, 한때 그 내장을 감싸고 있던 거대한 원형의 갈빗대 광주리가 붙어 있었다.\n\n내게는 이 드넓은 상아빛 갈빗대 가슴팍이, 거기서 일직선으로 멀찍이 뻗어 나간 길고 가림 없는 등뼈와 더불어, 마치 선대(船臺) 위에 막 얹어 놓은 큰 배의 선체와 적잖이 닮아 보였다. 헐벗은 뱃머리 늑재 가운데 겨우 스무 개쯤이 끼워 맞춰진 채, 용골은 아직은 그저 길고 이음매 없는 한 토막의 재목에 지나지 않은 그런 배 말이다.\n\n갈빗대는 한쪽에 열 개씩이었다. 목 쪽에서부터 세기 시작하면, 첫 번째 갈빗대는 길이가 거의 6피트(약 1.8미터)였고, 둘째와 셋째와 넷째는 차례차례 점점 길어져, 다섯 번째, 곧 한가운데 갈빗대 가운데 하나의 절정에 이르렀는데, 그것은 길이가 8피트 몇 인치(약 2.5미터)였다. 그 부위부터 나머지 갈빗대들은 점점 줄어들어, 열 번째이자 마지막 갈빗대는 겨우 5피트 몇 인치(약 1.6미터)에 그쳤다. 굵기로 말하자면, 모두가 그 길이에 걸맞게 알맞은 비례를 이루고 있었다. 한가운데 갈빗대들이 가장 활처럼 휘어 있었다. 아르사키데스의 어떤 곳에서는 이 갈빗대를 들보로 삼아 작은 시내 위에 오솔길 다리를 놓는 데 쓴다.\n\n이 갈빗대들을 살펴보면서, 나는 이 책에서 여러 차례 거듭 말해 온 그 사실에 새삼 충격을 받지 않을 수 없었다. 곧 고래의 골격은 결코 그 살이 덮인 형상의 거푸집이 아니라는 사실이다. 트랑케 갈빗대 가운데 가장 큰 것, 곧 한가운데 갈빗대 하나는, 살아 있을 때 깊이가 가장 깊은 부위에 자리하고 있었다. 그런데 이 특정한 고래가 살이 덮였을 때 그 몸의 가장 깊은 곳은 적어도 16피트(약 4.9미터)는 되었을 텐데, 그에 해당하는 갈빗대의 길이는 겨우 8피트(약 2.4미터)를 조금 넘을 뿐이었다. 그러니 이 갈빗대는 그 부위의 살아 있는 크기에 대한 참된 개념의 절반밖에 전해 주지 못하는 셈이다. 게다가 지금 내가 헐벗은 등뼈만을 보고 있는 어느 구간은, 한때 살과 근육과 피와 내장으로 더해진 수 톤의 부피로 휘감겨 있던 자리였다. 더욱이, 그 널찍한 지느러미가 있던 자리에서 나는 여기 흐트러진 몇 개의 관절만을 보았을 뿐이다. 그리고 그 묵직하고도 위풍당당하되 뼈는 없는 꼬리 지느러미 자리에는, 그저 텅 빈 공백뿐이었다!\n\n그렇다면 얼마나 헛되고 어리석은 일인가, 하고 나는 생각했다. 여행이라곤 모르는 겁 많은 사람이 이 경이로운 고래를 제대로 이해해 보겠다고, 이 고요한 숲속에 펼쳐진 채 죽어 메말라 버린 골격만을 들여다보는 일이란. 아니다. 오로지 가장 급박한 위험의 한복판에서만, 오로지 성난 꼬리 지느러미가 일으키는 소용돌이 속에 들어섰을 때에만, 오로지 저 깊고 가없는 바다 위에서만, 살이 온전히 덮인 고래를 참되고도 생생하게 알아낼 수 있는 법이다.\n\n그러나 등뼈는 다르다. 그것을 살펴보는 가장 좋은 방법은 기중기를 써서 그 뼈들을 끝으로 세워 높이 쌓아 올리는 것이다. 결코 빠르게 끝낼 수 있는 일은 아니다. 하지만 이제 다 쌓고 보니, 그것은 영락없이 폼페이우스의 기둥과 비슷한 모양이다.\n\n척추뼈는 모두 마흔 몇 개인데, 골격 상태에서는 서로 맞물려 있지 않다. 그것들은 대개 고딕 첨탑의 커다란 돌출 석재 토막들처럼 놓여, 묵직한 석조물의 단단한 층을 이룬다. 가장 큰 것, 곧 한가운데 척추뼈 하나는 너비가 3피트(약 0.9미터)에 조금 못 미치고, 깊이는 4피트(약 1.2미터)가 넘는다. 가장 작은 것, 곧 등뼈가 가늘어져 꼬리로 사라져 가는 부위의 척추뼈는 너비가 겨우 2인치(약 5센티미터)에 지나지 않아, 마치 하얀 당구공처럼 보인다. 그보다 더 작은 것들도 있었다는 말을 나는 들었으나, 그것들은 사제의 자식들인 어린 식인종 개구쟁이 몇이 구슬치기를 하려고 훔쳐 가는 바람에 잃어버렸다고 했다. 이리하여 우리는 살아 있는 것들 가운데 가장 거대한 것의 등뼈조차, 마침내는 한낱 아이들의 장난거리로 가늘어져 끝나 버리는 모습을 보게 되는 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 104. The Fossil Whale.\n\n그 거대한 몸집 덕분에 고래는 자세히 늘어놓고, 부풀리며, 두루 장광설을 펼치기에 더없이 어울리는 주제가 된다. 설령 그러고 싶다 한들 그대는 그를 압축할 수 없을 것이다. 마땅한 권리로 따지자면 그는 오직 제왕적 2절판 책에서만 다루어져야 한다. 분수공에서 꼬리까지 그 길이가 몇 펄롱(furlong)인지, 허리둘레가 몇 야드인지 다시 늘어놓을 것까지도 없이, 그의 창자가 이루는 거대한 굽이만 떠올려 보라. 그것은 전열함(戰列艦)의 지하 최하갑판에 사려 둔 굵은 케이블과 닻줄처럼 그의 몸속에 사려져 있다.\n\n이 리바이어던(Leviathan)을 직접 다루는 일을 떠맡은 이상, 나는 이 사업에서 전지전능하리만큼 빠짐없이 해내는 모습을 스스로 입증하는 것이 마땅하다. 그의 피 속에 깃든 가장 미세한 씨앗의 싹조차 놓치지 않고, 그의 창자가 사려진 가장 끝 굽이까지 그를 낱낱이 풀어내야 하는 것이다. 나는 이미 그를 현재의 서식과 해부학적 특징의 대부분에 걸쳐 묘사해 두었으니, 이제 남은 일은 고고학적이고 화석학적이며 대홍수 이전의 관점에서 그를 확대해 보는 것이다. 리바이어던이 아닌 다른 어떤 피조물에게—이를테면 개미나 벼룩에게—이처럼 거창한 말들을 갖다 붙인다면 부당하게 과장된 호언장담이라고 여겨도 마땅할 것이다. 그러나 리바이어던이 주제가 될 때는 사정이 달라진다. 나는 사전에 실린 가장 묵직한 낱말들의 무게에 짓눌려 비틀거리면서라도 기꺼이 이 위업에 뛰어들고자 한다. 그리고 여기서 한마디 해 두건대, 이 논고들을 써 내려가는 동안 사전을 펴 볼 일이 생길 때마다 나는 늘 오로지 그 목적을 위해 따로 사 둔 거대한 4절판 존슨 사전을 썼다. 그 유명한 사전 편찬자의 남달리 풍채 좋은 몸집이야말로, 나 같은 고래 작가가 쓸 사전을 엮어 내기에 한결 잘 어울린다고 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n흔히들 작가가 제 주제와 더불어 솟아오르고 부풀어 오른다고 하는데, 그 주제가 한낱 평범한 것일지라도 그렇다는 것이다. 그렇다면 이 리바이어던을 쓰고 있는 나는 어떠하겠는가? 나도 모르는 사이에 내 필체는 벽보에나 쓰일 큰 대문자로 부풀어 오른다. 콘도르의 깃펜을 내게 다오! 베수비오 화산의 분화구를 내게 잉크병으로 다오! 벗들이여, 내 두 팔을 붙들어 다오! 한낱 이 리바이어던에 관한 내 생각을 펜으로 적어 내려가는 것만으로도, 그 생각들은 나를 지치게 하고, 그 휩쓸며 뻗어 나가는 포괄성으로 나를 까무러치게 만들기 때문이다. 마치 온갖 학문의 영역 전체와, 과거와 현재와 미래에 걸친 고래와 인간과 마스토돈의 모든 세대와, 지상에서, 또 그 변두리까지 빠짐없이 온 우주에 걸쳐 펼쳐지는 제국들의 빙글빙글 도는 파노라마 전부를 끌어안으려는 듯이 말이다. 크고 너그러운 주제가 지닌 미덕이란 이러하며, 또 이토록 사람을 키워 놓는 것이다! 우리는 그 거대함에 맞추어 부풀어 오른다. 웅대한 책을 지어내려면 웅대한 주제를 골라야 한다. 벼룩에 관해서는 위대하고 길이 남을 책을 결코 쓸 수 없으니, 비록 그것을 시도한 사람이 숱하게 많았다 한들 그러하다.\n\n화석 고래라는 주제로 들어가기에 앞서, 나는 지질학자로서의 자격을 내보이고자 한다. 곧, 이런저런 일을 하던 시절에 나는 석공 노릇도 했고, 또한 온갖 종류의 도랑과 운하와 우물, 포도주 저장고와 지하실과 물탱크를 파헤치는 데에도 일가견이 있었음을 밝혀 둔다. 마찬가지로 들머리 삼아 독자에게 일러두고 싶은 것은, 더 이른 지질층에서는 이제 거의 완전히 멸종해 버린 괴물들의 화석이 발견되는 반면, 이른바 제3기 지층이라 불리는 곳에서 그 뒤에 발견된 유물들은 그 시대를 거스르는 태곳적 피조물들과, 그 먼 후손이 방주에 올랐다고 전해지는 피조물들 사이를 잇는, 적어도 중간에 끼어 있는 고리처럼 보인다는 점이다. 지금까지 발견된 모든 화석 고래는 제3기에 속하는데, 이는 표층 지층 바로 앞에 오는 마지막 시기다. 그리고 그중 어느 것도 현재의 알려진 어떤 종(種)과 정확히 일치하지는 않지만, 전반적인 면에서는 그것들과 충분히 닮은 점이 있어, 고래목 화석이라는 자리를 차지할 만한 자격은 갖추고 있다.\n\n아담 이전 시대의 고래들이 남긴 떨어져 나간 부서진 화석들, 곧 그 뼈와 골격의 조각들은 지난 삼십 년 동안 여러 차례에 걸쳐 알프스 기슭과 롬바르디아, 프랑스, 잉글랜드, 스코틀랜드, 그리고 루이지애나와 미시시피와 앨라배마 주에서 발견되어 왔다. 그러한 유물 가운데 더욱 진기한 것으로는 1779년에 파리의 도핀 거리에서 파헤쳐진 두개골의 일부가 있는데, 그 거리는 거의 곧장 튀일리 궁을 마주 보고 열려 있는 짧은 골목이다. 또한 나폴레옹 시대에 앤트워프의 거대한 부두를 파내던 중에 발굴된 뼈들도 있다. 퀴비에는 이 조각들이 전혀 알려지지 않은 어떤 리바이어던 종에 속한 것이라고 단언했다.\n\n그러나 모든 고래목 유물 가운데 단연 가장 놀라운 것은 1842년에 앨라배마의 크리그 판사 농장에서 발견된, 거의 온전한 형태의 어느 멸종한 괴물의 거대한 골격이었다. 인근의 두려움에 사로잡힌 잘 믿는 노예들은 그것을 타락한 천사들 가운데 하나의 뼈로 여겼다. 앨라배마의 박사들은 그것을 거대한 파충류라고 선언하며 바실로사우루스라는 이름을 붙였다. 그러나 그 표본 뼈 몇 점이 바다 건너 영국의 해부학자 오언에게 보내지자, 이 파충류라던 것이 실은 멸종한 종에 속하기는 하나 고래였음이 드러났다. 이 책에서 거듭거듭 되풀이된 사실, 곧 고래의 골격이 그의 살로 온전히 둘러싸인 몸의 형태를 알려 주는 단서는 거의 되지 못한다는 사실을 다시금 의미심장하게 보여 주는 예다. 그리하여 오언은 그 괴물을 제우글로돈이라 다시 이름 지었고, 런던 지질학회에서 낭독한 논문에서 그것을 요컨대 지구의 변천이 이 세상에서 지워 버린 가장 비범한 피조물 가운데 하나라고 선언했다.\n\n내가 이 거대한 리바이어던의 골격과 두개골, 엄니와 턱뼈, 갈빗대와 척추뼈 사이에 서 있노라면—그것들은 모두 현존하는 바다 괴물 종족과 부분적으로 닮은 점을 지니면서도, 다른 한편으로는 가늠할 수 없을 만큼 더 오래된 선조인, 절멸한 태곳적 리바이어던들과도 비슷한 친연성을 지니고 있는데—나는 마치 홍수에 휩쓸리듯, 시간 자체가 시작되었다고 말할 수조차 없는 저 경이로운 시대로 떠밀려 간다. 시간은 인간과 더불어 시작되었으니 말이다. 여기서 사투르누스의 잿빛 혼돈이 내 위로 굽이쳐 흐르고, 나는 저 극지의 영원함을 흐릿하고 몸서리치게 엿보게 된다. 쐐기처럼 박힌 얼음의 성채가 지금의 열대 지방인 곳을 짓누르고, 이 세상 둘레 2만 5천 마일 전체에 사람이 깃들 만한 손바닥 너비의 땅조차 보이지 않던 그 시절을. 그때는 온 세상이 고래의 것이었으니, 만물의 왕인 그는 지금의 안데스산맥과 히말라야산맥의 능선을 따라 제 항적을 남겼다. 누가 리바이어던에 견줄 만한 혈통을 내보일 수 있겠는가? 에이해브의 작살은 파라오의 피보다 더 오래된 피를 흘리게 했다. 므두셀라조차 한낱 학동으로 보인다. 나는 셈과 악수라도 나누려고 사방을 둘러본다. 말로 다 할 수 없는 고래의 공포가, 모세 이전 시대의, 그 근원조차 알 수 없는 채로 존재해 왔다는 사실에 나는 소름이 끼친다. 모든 시간에 앞서 존재했던 그것은, 모든 인간의 시대가 끝난 뒤에도 어쩔 수 없이 존재할 것이기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 이 리바이어던은 자연의 연판(鉛版) 위에, 또 석회암과 이회토(泥灰土) 속에 그 아담 이전의 흔적을 남기고 태곳적 흉상을 물려준 것에 그치지 않는다. 그 오래됨이 거의 화석에 가까운 성격을 띤다고 주장할 만한 이집트의 석판 위에서도, 우리는 틀림없는 그 지느러미의 자국을 발견한다. 약 오십 년 전, 덴데라의 거대한 신전 한 방에서 화강암 천장에 새겨지고 채색된 천체도가 발견되었는데, 그것은 켄타우로스와 그리핀과 돌고래로 가득하여, 현대인들의 천구의(天球儀)에 그려진 기괴한 형상들과 비슷했다. 그것들 사이를 미끄러지듯, 늙은 리바이어던은 예전과 다름없이 헤엄치고 있었으니, 솔로몬이 요람에 누이기 수백 년 전부터 그 천체도 속을 헤엄치고 있었던 것이다.\n\n또한 빼놓아서는 안 될 것이, 늙은 바르바리 여행가 존경할 만한 존 레오가 적어 둔, 고래 자신의 뼈로 이루어진 대홍수 이후의 실재를 통해 그 오래됨을 입증하는 또 하나의 기이한 증언이다.\n\n\"바닷가에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 그들은 신전 하나를 두고 있는데, 그 서까래와 들보가 모두 고래 뼈로 만들어져 있다. 괴물처럼 거대한 고래들이 종종 죽은 채 그 해안으로 떠밀려 오기 때문이다. 평민들은 하느님께서 그 신전에 내려 주신 은밀한 힘으로 인하여, 어떤 고래도 그 앞을 지나가면 곧바로 죽지 않고는 못 배긴다고 상상한다. 그러나 그 일의 진상은, 신전 양옆으로 바다를 향해 2마일이나 뻗어 나간 바위들이 있어, 고래들이 그 위에 걸려들면 상처를 입게 된다는 것이다. 그들은 도무지 믿기 어려운 길이의 고래 갈빗대 하나를 기적으로 여겨 간직하고 있는데, 그것은 볼록한 쪽을 위로 하여 땅에 놓여 아치를 이루고 있으며, 그 마루에는 낙타 등에 탄 사람도 손이 닿지 않는다. 이 갈빗대는 (존 레오가 이르기를) 내가 보기 백 년 전부터 그곳에 놓여 있었다고 전해진다. 그들의 사가(史家)들은 마호메트를 예언한 한 예언자가 이 신전에서 나왔다고 단언하며, 더러는 예언자 요나(Jonah)가 이 신전 기슭에서 고래에게 뱉어졌노라고 서슴없이 주장하기도 한다.\"\n\n이 아프리카의 고래 신전에 독자여, 나는 그대를 남겨 두고 떠난다. 그리고 그대가 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람이요 고래잡이라면, 그대는 그곳에서 말없이 경배하리라.\n\n# CHAPTER 105. Does the Whale’s Magnitude Diminish?—Will He Perish?\n\n그렇다면 이 리바이어던이 영원의 원천에서부터 우리에게로 허우적대며 떠내려온 것이라 할진대, 그토록 긴 세대의 흐름 속에서 그가 제 조상들의 본래 거구로부터 퇴화하지는 않았는지를 한번 따져 봄직도 하다.\n\n그러나 조사해 보면 다음과 같은 사실을 알게 된다. 오늘날의 고래들이 제3기 지층(인류 이전의 뚜렷한 한 지질 시대를 아우르는)에서 그 화석 잔해가 발견되는 고래들보다 크기에서 우월할 뿐만 아니라, 그 제3기 지층에서 발견된 고래들 가운데서도 후기 지층에 속한 것들이 초기 지층의 것들을 크기에서 능가한다는 것이다.\n\n지금껏 발굴된 모든 아담 이전 고래들 가운데 단연 가장 큰 것은 앞 장에서 언급한 앨라배마의 고래인데, 그것조차 골격 길이가 70피트(약 21미터)에 못 미쳤다. 반면 우리는 이미 줄자로 재어 보았을 때 큼직한 현대 고래의 골격이 72피트(약 22미터)에 이른다는 사실을 살펴본 바 있다. 그리고 나는 고래잡이들의 권위 있는 증언을 통해, 향유고래(Sperm Whale)가 포획 당시 길이가 거의 100피트(약 30미터)에 달하는 채로 잡힌 적이 있다는 말을 들었다.\n\n그러나 혹시 이런 것은 아닐까. 지금 이 시대의 고래들이 이전의 모든 지질 시대 고래들보다 크기에서 진보했다 하더라도, 아담의 시대 이래로는 도리어 퇴화한 것은 아닐까?\n\n플리니우스(Pliny)나 고대 박물학자들 일반의 기록을 믿어야 한다면, 우리는 분명 그렇게 결론지을 수밖에 없으리라. 플리니우스는 우리에게 살아 있는 거구가 몇 에이커에 달했다는 고래들에 관해 일러 주고, 알드로반두스(Aldrovandus)는 길이가 800피트(약 244미터)에 이르렀다는 또 다른 고래들에 관해 말한다 — 고래라기보다 밧줄 공장이나 템스 터널이라 할 만한 것들이다! 게다가 쿡(Cooke)의 박물학자였던 뱅크스와 솔란데르의 시대에도, 우리는 어느 덴마크 출신 과학원 회원이 어떤 아이슬란드 고래들(레이단시스쿠르, 곧 주름진 배라 불리는 종류)을 120야드, 곧 360피트(약 110미터)로 기록해 둔 것을 본다. 그리고 프랑스 박물학자 라세페드(Lacépède)는 그의 정교한 고래 연구서의 바로 첫머리(3쪽)에서 참고래(Right Whale)를 100미터, 곧 328피트(약 100미터)로 적어 놓았다. 그런데 이 저작은 무려 서기 1825년에야 출간된 것이다.\n\n그러나 어느 고래잡이가 이런 이야기를 믿겠는가? 결코 믿지 않으리라. 오늘날의 고래는 플리니우스 시대의 제 조상들만큼이나 크다. 그리고 만약 내가 언젠가 플리니우스가 있는 곳으로 가게 된다면, 그보다 더한 고래잡이인 나는 대담하게도 그에게 그렇게 일러 주리라. 왜냐하면 나는 도무지 이해할 수가 없기 때문이다. 플리니우스가 태어나기도 수천 년 전에 묻힌 이집트 미라들이 그 관 속에서 양말 신은 현대 켄터키 사람만큼의 키도 되지 못하며, 또한 가장 오래된 이집트와 니네베의 석판에 새겨진 소와 그 밖의 동물들이 그 그려진 상대적 비례로 보아, 혈통 좋고 우리에서 살찌워 상을 받은 스미스필드의 소들이 파라오의 살진 소들 가운데 가장 기름진 것과 견주어도 어깨를 나란히 할 뿐 아니라 크기에서 훨씬 능가한다는 것을 더없이 명백히 입증하거늘 — 이 모든 것을 앞에 두고서, 어찌 모든 동물 가운데 유독 고래만이 퇴화했다고 인정할 수 있겠는가.\n\n그러나 또 하나의 물음이 남아 있으니, 좀 더 사정에 밝은 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들이 종종 떠들어 대는 것이다. 이제는 베링 해협을 뚫고 들어가 세상의 가장 후미진 비밀 서랍과 사물함 속까지 파고드는, 거의 전지(全知)에 가까운 고래잡이배 돛대 꼭대기의 망보기들과, 모든 대륙 연안을 따라 던져지는 수천 자루의 작살과 창 때문에, 리바이어던이 과연 그토록 광범위한 추격과 그토록 무자비한 살육을 오래 견딜 수 있을 것인가 — 마침내 그가 바다에서 절멸당하여, 마지막 고래가 마지막 인간처럼 그 마지막 담뱃대를 피우고 나서 끝내 그 마지막 한 모금과 함께 스스로 증발해 버리지나 않을 것인가. 이것이 바로 논쟁의 핵심이다.\n\n혹 모양으로 등을 솟구친 고래 떼를, 그와 같이 등을 솟구친 들소 떼와 견주어 보자. 들소들은 불과 사십 년 전만 해도 일리노이와 미주리의 대초원을 수만 마리씩 뒤덮으며, 철빛 갈기를 흔들고 천둥처럼 뭉쳐진 미간을 찌푸리며, 사람들로 북적이는 강변 도시들이 들어선 자리를 굽어보았다. 그러나 이제 그 자리에서는 점잖은 중개인이 땅을 1인치에 1달러씩 팔아넘긴다. 이런 비교에서라면, 쫓기는 고래가 이제 머지않은 절멸을 피할 수 없다는 것을 보여 주는 거부할 수 없는 논거가 마련된 듯이 보일 것이다.\n\n그러나 그대들은 이 문제를 온갖 각도에서 들여다보아야 한다. 비록 그토록 짧은 시기 전 — 넉넉한 한평생도 못 되는 — 일리노이의 들소 수효가 지금 런던에 사는 사람의 수효를 넘어섰고, 또 오늘날에는 그 일대 어디에도 그것들의 뿔 하나 발굽 하나 남아 있지 않으며, 게다가 이 놀라운 절멸의 원인이 인간의 창이었다 하더라도, 고래잡이가 지닌 전혀 다른 성격은 리바이어던에게 그토록 영광 없는 종말을 단호히 허락하지 않는다. 한 배에 탄 마흔 명의 사람이 마흔여덟 달 동안 향유고래를 사냥하여, 마침내 고래 마흔 마리의 기름을 싣고 집으로 돌아온다면 그들은 더없이 잘해냈다고 여기며 하느님께 감사를 드린다. 반면 옛 캐나다와 인디언(Indian)의 서부 사냥꾼이자 덫꾼이던 자들의 시절, 곧 머나먼 서부(그 노을 진 곳에서도 해는 여전히 떠오른다)가 황야이자 처녀지였을 무렵에는, 똑같은 수의 모카신을 신은 사내들이 똑같은 수의 달 동안, 배를 타는 대신 말에 올라타고서, 마흔 마리가 아니라 사만 마리도 넘는 들소를 죽였을 것이다. 필요하다면 통계로도 진술할 수 있는 사실이다.\n\n또한, 바르게 따져 보면, 이를테면 향유고래가 점차 절멸해 간다는 주장에 유리한 근거로는 보이지 않는 일이 있다. 곧 지난 세월(이를테면 지난 세기의 후반)에는 이 리바이어던들이 작은 무리를 지어 지금보다 훨씬 자주 마주쳐졌고, 그 결과 항해 기간이 그리 길지 않았으며 또 훨씬 수지가 맞았다는 사실이다. 왜냐하면 다른 데서 이미 짚어 두었듯이, 이 고래들이 무언가 안전을 위한 생각에 이끌려 이제는 거대한 대상(隊商)을 이루어 바다를 헤엄치는 까닭에, 옛날의 흩어진 외톨이며 한 쌍이며 작은 무리며 큰 떼들이 이제는 대체로 거대하되 서로 멀찍이 떨어진, 좀처럼 만나기 어려운 군단으로 결집되었기 때문이다. 그것이 전부다. 그리고 이른바 고래수염 고래들이 지난 세월 그것들로 우글거리던 여러 어장에 더는 출몰하지 않으니 그 종(種) 또한 쇠퇴하는 중이라는 생각도 마찬가지로 그릇된 것이다. 왜냐하면 그것들은 그저 곶에서 곶으로 쫓겨 다닐 뿐이며, 어느 한 연안이 더 이상 그들의 물줄기로 활기를 띠지 못한다면, 분명 또 다른 더 먼 기슭이 바로 얼마 전에 그 낯선 광경에 깜짝 놀랐을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n게다가, 방금 언급한 이 리바이어던들로 말하자면, 그들에게는 인간으로서는 도무지 영원히 함락할 수 없을 것이 거의 분명한 두 개의 견고한 요새가 있다. 그리고 제 골짜기로 침입을 받으면 서리 덮인 스위스 사람들이 저들의 산속으로 물러나듯, 가운데 바다의 너른 초원과 빈터에서 쫓긴 고래수염 고래들도 마침내 그들의 극지(極地) 성채로 피신할 수 있으니, 그곳의 궁극적인 유리 같은 장벽과 벽 아래로 잠수해 들어가 얼음 들판과 부빙(浮氷) 사이로 솟아올라, 영원한 12월의 마법진 안에서 인간의 온갖 추격을 비웃으며 버틸 수 있는 것이다.\n\n그러나 향유고래 한 마리를 잡는 동안 이 고래수염 고래는 어쩌면 쉰 마리쯤 작살에 꿰이는지라, 선원 선실의 어떤 철학자들은 이 명백한 살육이 이미 그들의 군세를 매우 심각하게 줄여 놓았다고 결론지었다. 그러나 비록 얼마 전부터 미국인들만으로도 해마다 1만 3000마리 이상의 이 고래들이 북서 연안에서 도살되어 왔다 하더라도, 이 사정조차 이 문제에서 반대 논거로는 거의, 아니 전혀 무게가 없게 만드는 여러 고려 사항들이 있다.\n\n지구상의 더 거대한 피조물들이 그토록 많다는 데에 다소 미심쩍어하는 것이야 자연스러운 일이지만, 그렇다 한들 고아(Goa)의 역사가 하르토(Harto)에 대해서는 무어라 할 것인가. 그가 우리에게 이르기를, 시암 왕이 한 차례 사냥에서 코끼리 4000마리를 잡았으며, 그 지역에서 코끼리는 온대 지방의 소 떼만큼이나 흔하다고 하니 말이다. 그리고 세미라미스, 포루스, 한니발, 그리고 동방의 역대 모든 군주들에 의해 이제껏 수천 년 동안 사냥당해 온 이 코끼리들이 — 만약 그들이 여전히 거기서 엄청난 수효로 살아남아 있다면, 거대한 고래는 더욱더 모든 사냥을 견뎌 내고도 남으리라는 것을 의심할 이유가 없어 보인다. 왜냐하면 그에게는 마음껏 노닐 목초지가 있는데, 그것은 아시아 전체와 두 아메리카, 유럽과 아프리카, 뉴홀란드(New Holland), 그리고 바다의 모든 섬들을 합친 것보다 정확히 두 배나 넓기 때문이다.\n\n뿐만 아니라 우리는 이런 점도 고려해야 한다. 고래가 매우 장수하리라 추정되는바, 아마도 한 세기 넘는 나이에까지 이르는 까닭에, 어느 한 시기에든 뚜렷이 구별되는 여러 세대의 성체들이 동시대에 함께 살아 있을 수밖에 없다는 것이다. 그것이 어떠한지는, 창조된 만물의 온갖 묘지와 공동묘지와 가족 납골당이 칠십오 년 전에 살아 있던 모든 남자와 여자와 아이들의 산 몸뚱이를 도로 토해 내고, 그 헤아릴 수 없는 무리를 지금 지구상의 인구에 더하는 광경을 상상해 보면 곧 어느 정도 짐작할 수 있으리라.\n\n그러므로 이 모든 것들로 미루어, 우리는 고래가 비록 한 개체로서는 죽을 운명이라 할지라도 그 종(種)에서는 불멸이라 여기는 바이다. 그는 대륙들이 수면 위로 솟아오르기도 전에 바다를 헤엄쳤다. 그는 한때 튀일리 궁과 윈저 성과 크렘린이 들어선 자리 위를 헤엄쳐 다녔다. 노아의 홍수 속에서도 그는 노아의 방주를 업신여겼으며, 만약 언젠가 이 세상이 쥐들을 쓸어내기 위해 네덜란드처럼 또다시 물에 잠긴다 하더라도, 영원한 고래는 그때에도 여전히 살아남아, 적도 위로 차오른 홍수의 가장 높은 마루 위로 몸을 일으켜 세우고는, 거품 이는 제 도전의 물줄기를 하늘을 향해 뿜어 올리리라.\n\n# CHAPTER 106. Ahab’s Leg.\n\n런던의 새뮤얼 엔더비호를 떠날 때 에이해브 선장이 보였던 그 황급한 태도는 그 자신의 몸에 작지 않은 충격을 가하지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 어찌나 거센 기세로 보트의 가로장 위에 내려섰던지, 고래 뼈로 만든 다리가 반쯤 쪼개질 만한 충격을 받고 말았다. 그러고는 자기 배의 갑판에 올라서서 그곳에 마련된 자기만의 다리 받침 구멍에 다리를 박은 뒤, 키잡이에게 다급한 명령을 내리며 그토록 격렬하게 몸을 홱 돌렸을 때(그것은 늘 그렇듯 키를 충분히 흔들림 없이 잡지 못한다는 따위의 꾸지람이었다), 이미 흔들린 고래 뼈 다리는 또 한 번 비틀리고 꺾이는 힘을 받았다. 그리하여 다리가 여전히 온전한 채로 남았고 겉보기에는 멀쩡해 보였으나, 에이해브는 그것을 온전히 믿을 만한 것으로 여기지 않았다.\n\n사실 그가 온통 미친 듯한 무모함에 사로잡혀 있으면서도 때때로 자신이 그 위에 반쯤 의지하고 선 그 죽은 뼈의 상태에 세심한 주의를 기울였다는 것은 그다지 놀랄 만한 일이 아니었다. 피쿼드호가 낸터킷을 떠나기 그리 오래되지 않은 어느 날 밤, 그가 땅바닥에 엎어진 채 의식을 잃고 쓰러져 있는 것이 발견된 적이 있었기 때문이다. 알 수 없고 도무지 설명할 수도, 상상할 수도 없는 어떤 사고로 인해 그 고래 뼈 다리가 어찌나 거칠게 어긋났던지, 그것이 말뚝처럼 사타구니를 찔러 거의 꿰뚫을 뻔했고, 그 고통스러운 상처를 완전히 치유하는 데에도 극심한 어려움이 따랐다.\n\n그리고 그 당시에도, 지금 겪고 있는 그 모든 고통이 다름 아닌 이전의 비탄에서 곧장 비롯된 것이라는 생각이 그의 편집광적인 마음속에 들어오지 않은 것은 아니었다. 그는 또한 너무도 분명하게 깨닫는 듯했으니, 늪지의 가장 독한 파충류가 숲속의 가장 감미로운 명금(鳴禽)만큼이나 어김없이 제 종족을 이어 가듯, 모든 행복과 마찬가지로 모든 비참한 일들 또한 자연스럽게 제 닮은꼴을 낳는다는 것이었다. 아니, 그보다 더하다고 에이해브는 생각했다. 슬픔의 조상과 후손은 기쁨의 조상과 후손보다 더 멀리까지 뻗어 나가기 때문이다. 이런 점은 굳이 들추지 않더라도 말이다. 어떤 경전의 가르침에서 끌어낼 수 있는 추론에 따르면, 이 세상에서 누리는 어떤 자연스러운 즐거움들은 저 세상으로 이어질 자식을 낳지 못하고 도리어 온 지옥의 절망이 안기는 기쁨 없는 무자(無子)의 상태로 이어지지만, 죄로 물든 어떤 인간의 비참들은 여전히 풍요롭게도 무덤 너머까지 영원히 불어나는 슬픔의 자손을 스스로 낳는다는 것이다. 이런 점은 전혀 들추지 않더라도, 이 문제를 더 깊이 파고들면 거기에는 여전히 어떤 불균형이 존재하는 듯하다. 에이해브는 이렇게 생각했다. 이 땅에서 누리는 가장 드높은 행복조차도 그 안에는 어딘가 보잘것없는 하찮음이 도사리고 있는 반면, 그 밑바닥에서 모든 마음의 비통은 신비로운 의미를, 그리고 어떤 사람들에게는 대천사 같은 장엄함을 지니고 있으니, 그것을 부지런히 추적해 들어가도 그 명백한 결론을 뒤집지는 못한다. 이 드높은 인간의 비참들의 계보를 따라가다 보면, 우리는 마침내 신들의 근원 없는 시원(始原)에까지 이르게 된다. 그러므로 즐겁게 건초를 거두게 하는 온갖 태양과, 부드럽게 심벌즈처럼 울리는 둥근 추수의 달들 앞에서도, 우리는 결국 이것을 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 신들 자신조차 영원히 즐겁지만은 않다는 것을. 인간의 이마에 지워지지 않게 새겨진 저 슬픈 모반(母斑)은, 그것을 서명한 자들이 찍어 놓은 비애의 낙인에 지나지 않는다.\n\n여기서 무심결에 한 가지 비밀이 누설되고 말았는데, 그것은 어쩌면 앞서 정해진 자리에서 마땅히 밝혔어야 더 옳았을지도 모른다. 에이해브에 관한 다른 여러 세세한 사정들과 더불어, 어찌하여 그가 피쿼드호가 출항하기 전과 후의 어떤 기간 동안 마치 라마 대승정이라도 된 듯 그토록 사람을 멀리하며 스스로를 숨겨 두었는지, 그리고 그 한동안 마치 죽은 자들의 대리석 원로원에 든 듯 말없는 은둔처를 찾았는지는, 어떤 이들에게 늘 수수께끼로 남아 있었다. 이 일에 대해 펠레그 선장이 떠벌린 이유는 결코 충분해 보이지 않았다. 사실 에이해브의 더 깊은 부분에 닿는 일이라면, 모든 폭로는 설명해 주는 빛이라기보다 의미심장한 어둠을 더 많이 머금고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 결국 모든 것이 드러났다. 적어도 이 한 가지 사정만큼은 그러했다. 그 끔찍한 사고가 그의 일시적 은둔의 밑바닥에 깔려 있었던 것이다. 그리고 이뿐만이 아니었다. 무슨 까닭에서든 그에게 덜 금기시되는 방식으로 다가갈 특권을 지녔던, 뭍에 남은 저 끊임없이 좁아지고 줄어드는 무리에게—그 소심한 무리에게, 앞서 넌지시 비친 그 사고는—에이해브가 우울하게 입을 다문 채 한 번도 그 까닭을 밝히지 않았던 그대로—영혼과 통곡의 나라에서 비롯된 것이 전혀 아니라고는 할 수 없는 공포의 빛깔을 띠게 되었다. 그리하여 그를 향한 열의에서, 그들은 모두 자기들이 할 수 있는 한 이 일에 대한 앎을 다른 이들로부터 감추려고 한통속이 되었다. 그러한 까닭에, 상당한 시일이 흐른 뒤에야 비로소 그 일이 피쿼드호의 갑판 위로 새어 나오게 된 것이다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 것이 어떠하든 간에, 허공의 보이지 않는 모호한 종교회의나, 불의 복수심에 찬 군주들과 권세자들이 이 땅의 에이해브와 관련이 있든 없든, 지금 그의 다리에 관한 이 문제에서만큼은 그는 명백히 실질적인 조치를 취했다. 그는 목수를 불렀다.\n\n그 직무를 맡은 자가 그의 앞에 나타나자, 에이해브는 지체 없이 새 다리를 만드는 일에 착수하라 명했다. 그리고 항해하는 동안 지금껏 모아 둔 향유고래 턱뼈로 만든 받침대와 들보 자재를 모두 그에게 공급하도록 항해사들에게 일렀으니, 그 가운데 가장 튼튼하고 결이 가장 또렷한 자재를 신중하게 골라낼 수 있게 하기 위함이었다. 이 일이 끝나자, 목수는 그날 밤 안으로 다리를 완성하라는 명령과 함께, 지금 쓰고 있는 미덥지 못한 다리에 딸린 부속들과는 별개로 새 다리에 필요한 모든 부품을 마련하라는 명령을 받았다. 게다가 선창 속에서 한동안 놀고 있던 배의 단조 화로를 끌어올리라는 명령이 내려졌고, 일을 서두르기 위해 대장장이는 필요한 온갖 쇠붙이 장치를 지체 없이 벼리기 시작하라는 지시를 받았다.\n\n# CHAPTER 107. The Carpenter.\n\n토성의 여러 위성들 사이에 술탄처럼 자리를 잡고 앉아, 고도로 추상화된 인간 하나만을 따로 떼어 바라보라. 그러면 그는 하나의 경이요, 장엄함이며, 또한 비애로 보일 것이다. 그러나 같은 지점에서 인류를 무리째로 바라보면, 그들은 대체로 불필요한 복제품들의 군중처럼 보일 뿐이니, 그것은 동시대의 복제품이기도 하고 대대로 이어진 복제품이기도 하다. 그러나 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 목수는 비록 더없이 보잘것없는 인물이었고, 저 고매하고 인간적인 추상의 본보기와는 거리가 멀었음에도, 결코 복제품은 아니었다. 그러므로 그는 이제 몸소 이 무대에 등장한다.\n\n바다를 누비는 모든 배의 목수들이 그러하듯, 특히 포경선에 속한 목수들이 더욱 그러하듯, 그는 어느 정도 막힘없는 실용적 범위 안에서 자신의 본업과 인접한 수많은 직종과 일거리에 두루 능숙했다. 목수의 일이라는 것이 본디 나무를 보조 재료로 삼아 이러저러하게 다루는 그 모든 수많은 수공 기술의 오래되고 가지 무성한 줄기이기 때문이다. 그러나 위에서 말한 일반적 언급이 그에게 적용되는 것에 더하여, 이 피쿼드호의 목수는 삼사 년에 걸친 항해 동안 미개하고 머나먼 바다 위에서 큰 배에 끊임없이 닥쳐오는, 이름조차 없는 수천 가지 기계적 위기 상황을 처리하는 데 유난히 유능했다. 부서진 보트를 수리하고, 휘어진 원목을 손보고, 무딘 날의 노 모양을 다시 다듬고, 갑판에 둥근 채광창을 박아 넣고, 뱃전 널빤지에 새 나무못을 끼우는 등, 그의 특수한 본업에 더 직접적으로 속하는 잡다한 일들에서 보여 준 그의 능란함은 말할 것도 없거니와, 그는 게다가 유용한 것이든 변덕스러운 것이든 온갖 상충하는 재주에서도 거침없이 능숙했다.\n\n그가 그토록 다양하고 무수한 배역을 두루 연기하던 하나의 거대한 무대는 그의 바이스 작업대였다. 그것은 길고 거칠고 묵직한 탁자로, 크기가 제각각인 여러 개의 바이스를 갖추었는데, 쇠로 된 것도 있고 나무로 된 것도 있었다. 고래가 뱃전에 붙어 있을 때를 제외하고는 늘, 이 작업대는 정유 가마(Try-works) 뒤편에 가로질러 단단히 동여매여 있었다.\n\n밧줄걸이 핀이 구멍에 쉽게 들어가지 않을 만큼 너무 굵으면, 목수는 그것을 늘 준비되어 있는 바이스 중 하나에 물려 놓고는 곧바로 더 가늘게 줄로 깎아 낸다. 기이한 깃털을 가진 길 잃은 뭍새가 배 안으로 흘러들어 와 사로잡히면, 목수는 깨끗하게 깎아 낸 참고래(Right) 뼈 막대와 향유고래(Sperm) 상아로 만든 가로대를 엮어 탑처럼 생긴 새장을 만들어 준다. 노잡이가 손목을 삐면, 목수는 가라앉히는 연고를 조제해 낸다. 스터브(Stubb)는 자기가 쓰는 노 하나하나의 날에 주홍빛 별을 그려 넣고 싶어 했는데, 목수는 노를 큼직한 나무 바이스에 하나씩 물려 놓고는 좌우 대칭으로 그 별자리를 새겨 넣어 준다. 어느 선원이 상어 뼈로 만든 귀걸이를 차고 싶어 하면, 목수는 그의 귀에 구멍을 뚫어 준다. 또 다른 선원이 치통을 앓으면, 목수는 집게를 꺼내 들고 한 손으로 작업대를 탁 짚으며 거기 앉으라고 명한다. 그러나 가엾은 친구가 채 끝나지도 않은 시술 도중에 어쩔 줄 모르고 움찔거리면, 목수는 나무 바이스의 손잡이를 빙빙 돌리며, 이를 뽑게 하려거든 그 안에 턱을 물리라고 손짓한다.\n\n이리하여 이 목수는 모든 면에서 만반의 준비가 되어 있었고, 그 모든 일에 한결같이 무심하며 아무런 경의도 품지 않았다. 그는 이빨을 상아 조각으로 여겼고, 머리를 그저 도르래 윗부분쯤으로 보았으며, 사람 그 자체를 가볍게 캡스턴 정도로 취급했다. 그러나 그가 이토록 광범위한 영역에서 이처럼 다양하게 숙달되어 있고, 게다가 그토록 활기찬 능란함마저 갖추고 있는 지금, 이 모든 것은 어떤 비범한 지성의 생기를 드러내는 듯 보일 법도 하다. 그러나 꼭 그렇지만은 않았다. 이 사내에게 가장 두드러진 점은 다름 아닌 이를테면 비인격적인 둔중함이었으니, 비인격적이라 함은, 그 둔중함이 주위를 둘러싼 무한한 사물의 세계 속으로 어찌나 아스라이 녹아들어 가는지, 눈에 보이는 온 세계에서 감지되는 저 보편적 둔중함과 하나가 된 듯 보였기 때문이다. 그 보편적 둔중함이란, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 방식으로 쉼 없이 움직이면서도 여전히 영원토록 침묵을 지키며, 그대가 대성당의 주춧돌을 파 내려간다 한들 그대를 모른 체하는 것이다. 그러나 그에게는 이 반쯤은 섬뜩한 둔중함이 깃들어 있었고, 그것은 또한 보기에 모든 곳으로 뻗어 나가는 일종의 냉혹함을 품고 있는 듯했음에도, 이상하게도 때때로 그 둔중함에는 낡은 목발 같고, 노아의 홍수 이전 시대처럼 케케묵고, 쌕쌕거리는 듯한 익살이 섞여 들었으니, 거기에는 이따금 어딘가 희끗희끗 노련한 재치마저 스쳐 지나갔다. 그런 익살이라면 노아의 방주 그 수염 난 앞갑판에서 한밤의 당직 시간을 때우는 데 쓰였을 법도 했다. 이 늙은 목수가 평생을 떠도는 방랑자로 살아온 까닭에, 이리저리 끝없이 굴러다니느라 이끼 한 점 끼지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 더 나아가 본디 그에게 붙어 있었을지 모를 자잘한 겉껍데기마저 죄다 닳아 떨어져 나간 것일까? 그는 발가벗겨진 추상이요, 더 나뉠 수 없는 정수(整數)였으며, 갓 태어난 갓난아기처럼 어디에도 타협하지 않았으니, 이 세상이나 저세상을 미리 염두에 두는 일 없이 살아가는 사람이었다. 그에게 깃든 이 기묘한 비타협성은 어쩌면 일종의 무지성을 품고 있었다고 말해도 좋을 것이다. 그 수많은 일거리에서 그는 이성으로도, 본능으로도, 혹은 단지 그렇게 배웠기 때문에도, 또 이 모든 것이 고르게든 고르지 않게든 뒤섞인 까닭에서도 일하는 것처럼 보이지 않았고, 다만 일종의 귀먹고 벙어리 같은, 즉흥적이고 곧이곧대로의 과정을 통해서만 일하는 듯했기 때문이다. 그는 순전한 손놀림꾼이었으니, 그에게 두뇌라는 것이 애초에 있었다 한들, 그것은 진작에 손가락 근육 속으로 스며들어 가 버렸음에 틀림없었다. 그는 이성을 갖추지 못했으되 여전히 더없이 쓸모 있는, 작은 몸체에 많은 것을 담은(multum in parvo) 셰필드(Sheffield)제 도구 같은 자였다. 겉모습은 ― 조금 부풀어 있기는 해도 ― 흔한 주머니칼처럼 보이지만, 그 안에는 크기가 제각각인 칼날뿐 아니라 나사돌리개, 코르크 따개, 족집게, 송곳, 펜, 자, 손톱줄, 접시머리 따개까지 들어 있는 그런 도구 말이다. 그러니 상관들이 이 목수를 나사돌리개로 쓰고 싶으면, 그의 그 부분을 펼쳐 내기만 하면 나사가 단단히 조여졌고, 족집게로 쓰고 싶으면 두 다리를 잡아 들어 올리기만 하면 거기 족집게가 있었다.\n* 작은 몸체에 많은 것을 담은(multum in parvo): \"작은 것 안에 많은 것\"이라는 뜻의 라틴어 표현.\n\n그러나 앞서 넌지시 비친 바와 같이, 만능 도구를 갖추고 펼쳤다 접었다 하는 이 목수도 따지고 보면 한낱 자동인형 같은 기계는 아니었다. 비록 그에게 평범한 영혼이 깃들어 있지는 않았다 해도, 어딘가 변칙적인 방식으로 제 할 일을 해내는 미묘한 무언가가 그에게 있었다. 그것이 무엇인지, 수은의 정수인지 아니면 사슴뿔 정기(精氣) 몇 방울인지는 알 길이 없다. 그러나 그것은 분명 거기 있었고, 이제 육십 년이 넘도록 거기 머물러 있었다. 그리고 바로 이것이, 그에게 깃든 이 헤아릴 수 없고 교묘한 생명의 원리가, 그로 하여금 많은 시간을 혼잣말로 보내게 한 장본인이었다. 그러나 그것은 다만 이성 없이 윙윙거리며 혼잣말하는 바퀴와도 같았다. 아니, 차라리 그의 몸은 보초막이요, 거기 보초 서며 혼잣말하는 자는 잠들지 않으려고 줄곧 떠들어 대고 있는 것이라 하는 편이 옳겠다.\n\n갑판 — 초저녁 당직.\n\n(목수가 자기 죔쇠 작업대 앞에 서서, 두 개의 등불 빛에 의지해 의족에 쓸 상아 들보를 부지런히 줄질하고 있다. 그 들보는 죔쇠에 단단히 물려 있다. 상아 판자며 가죽끈이며 패드며 나사며 온갖 종류의 갖가지 연장이 작업대 주위에 어지러이 널려 있다. 앞쪽으로는 대장간의 붉은 불꽃이 보이고, 그곳에서 대장장이가 일하고 있다.)\n\n빌어먹을 줄, 빌어먹을 뼈다귀 같으니! 물러야 할 곳은 단단하고, 단단해야 할 곳은 무르구나. 낡은 턱뼈며 정강이뼈를 줄질하는 우리 신세가 다 이렇지. 다른 걸로 해보자. 옳지, 이건 좀 낫군 (재채기를 한다). 어이쿠, 이 뼛가루가 (재채기) — 이거 참 (재채기) — 그래 이게 (재채기) — 맙소사, 말도 못하게 만드는구나! 죽은 목재를 다루다 보면 늙은이가 받는 게 이런 거다. 살아 있는 나무를 톱질해 봐라, 이런 가루는 안 나오지. 살아 있는 뼈를 잘라 봐라, 이런 건 안 나와 (재채기). 자, 자, 이 거기 검댕이 영감, 손 좀 거들어서 그 쇠테와 죔쇠 나사를 가져오게. 곧 그것들이 필요할 테니. 그래도 운이 좋군 (재채기), 무릎 관절을 안 만들어도 되니 말이지. 그건 좀 골치 아팠을 텐데. 단순한 정강이뼈쯤이야 — 이거야 홉 받침대 만드는 것만큼 쉽지. 다만 마무리를 잘 짓고 싶긴 한데. 시간, 시간이 문제야. 시간만 넉넉하다면 응접실에서 숙녀에게 절하며 발을 끄는 (재채기) 그 어떤 다리 못지않게 말끔한 의족을 뽑아낼 수 있으련만. 가게 진열창에서 본 그 사슴가죽 다리며 종아리 따위는 비교도 안 되지. 그것들은 물을 빨아들이거든. 그러니 당연히 류머티즘에 걸리고, 산 다리나 마찬가지로 세척액이며 바르는 약으로 (재채기) 치료를 받아야 한다고. 자, 이제 톱으로 자르기 전에 그 늙은 황제 폐하를 불러서 길이가 맞는지 봐야겠군. 아무래도 조금 짧을 것 같은데. 하! 저게 그 발꿈치 소리로구나. 우리한테 운이 따르는걸. 저기 오는군, 아니면 누군가 딴 사람이거나, 그건 확실하지.\n\n에이해브 (다가오며). (이어지는 장면 내내 목수는 이따금 재채기를 계속한다.)\n\n오, 사람 만드는 자여!\n\n마침 잘 오셨습니다, 선장님. 선장님만 괜찮으시다면 이제 길이를 표시하겠습니다. 좀 재어 보겠습니다, 선장님.\n\n다리를 재게 한단 말이지! 좋다. 뭐, 처음 있는 일도 아니니까. 어서 하게! 자, 그 위에 손가락을 대고 있어라. 이 죔쇠는 단단히도 무는군, 목수. 한번 그 악력을 느껴 보세. 그래, 그래, 제법 꽉 무는구나.\n\n오, 선장님, 그건 뼈도 부러뜨립니다 — 조심하십시오, 조심하세요!\n\n겁날 것 없다. 나는 단단히 무는 게 좋아. 이 미끄러운 세상에서 무언가 단단히 붙잡을 수 있는 것을 느끼는 게 좋단 말이야. 저기 프로메테우스는 뭘 하고 있나? — 대장장이 말이야 — 뭘 하고 있어?\n\n지금쯤 죔쇠 나사를 벼리고 있을 겁니다, 선장님.\n\n옳지. 이건 합작이로군. 그자는 근육 부분을 대는 셈이지. 저기 사나운 붉은 불꽃을 피워 올리는구나!\n\n그렇습니다, 선장님. 이런 정교한 작업에는 백열의 불이 필요하니까요.\n\n음. 그렇겠지. 이제 와 생각하니 참으로 의미심장한 일이로다. 사람들이 말하기를 인간을 만들었다는 저 옛 그리스인 프로메테우스가 대장장이였고, 불로써 인간에게 생명을 불어넣었다니 말이야. 불 속에서 만들어진 것은 마땅히 불에 속하는 법, 그러니 지옥이 있을 법도 하지. 검댕이 어찌나 날리는지! 저게 바로 그 그리스인이 아프리카인을 빚고 남긴 찌꺼기인 게로군. 목수, 그자가 그 죔쇠를 다 만들거든 강철 어깨뼈 한 쌍을 벼리라고 일러 두게. 배에 짓누르는 짐 보따리를 진 행상꾼이 하나 있어서 말이야.\n예, 선장님?\n\n가만, 프로메테우스가 그 일을 하는 김에 내 마음에 드는 본을 따라 완벽한 인간을 하나 주문해야겠다. 첫째로, 양말까지 신어서 키가 오십 피트(약 15미터)일 것. 다음으로 가슴은 템스강 터널을 본떠 만들 것. 다음으로 다리에는 뿌리를 달아서 한자리에 붙박여 있게 할 것. 다음으로 팔은 손목 둘레가 삼 피트(약 90센티미터)일 것. 심장은 아예 없고, 이마는 놋쇠로, 뇌수는 사분의 일 에이커쯤 되는 좋은 것으로. 그리고 가만 보자 — 바깥을 보는 눈을 주문할까? 아니, 그 대신 머리 꼭대기에 채광창을 달아서 안쪽을 비추게 하지. 자, 그 주문을 받아서 어서 가게.\n\n자, 저 양반이 대체 무슨 소리를 하는 거며, 누구한테 하는 말이지? 이거 알 수가 없네. 내가 여기 계속 서 있어야 하나? (혼잣말로).\n\n눈먼 둥근 천장을 짓는 건 어설픈 건축술이지. 여기 하나 있군. 안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼. 등불이 있어야겠다.\n\n호, 호! 그거 말씀이군요, 예? 여기 둘 있습니다, 선장님. 하나면 제 일에 충분하지요.\n\n그 도둑 잡는 등불을 어쩌자고 내 얼굴에 들이미는 거냐, 이 사람아? 들이대는 불빛은 겨눈 권총보다 더 고약하단 말이다.\n\n저는 선장님께서 목수에게 말씀하시는 줄로만 알았습니다.\n\n목수? 그게 무슨 — 아니지 — 자네가 여기서 하는 일은 참으로 깔끔하고, 말하자면 지극히 신사다운 일이로다, 목수여. 아니면 차라리 진흙을 빚는 일을 하고 싶은가?\n\n예, 선장님? — 진흙이요? 진흙이라뇨, 선장님? 그건 진창 아닙니까. 진흙은 도랑 파는 자들에게나 맡기지요, 선장님.\n\n이 친구 불경스럽구나! 무슨 재채기를 그리 하나?\n\n뼈가 좀 먼지가 많아서요, 선장님.\n\n그러면 그 말뜻을 새겨 두게. 그리고 자네가 죽거든 산 사람들 코밑에 절대 묻히지 말게나.\n\n예, 선장님? — 오! 아! — 그렇겠지요 — 예 — 아이고, 저런!\n\n이보게, 목수. 자네는 스스로를 제법 솜씨 좋은 일꾼이라 자부하겠지, 응? 그렇다면 한번 말해 보게. 자네가 만드는 이 다리를 내가 차고 섰을 때, 그럼에도 바로 그 똑같은 자리에서 또 다른 다리가 느껴진다면 — 그러니까 목수, 내 잃어버린 옛 다리, 살과 피로 된 그 다리 말이야 — 그래도 자네 솜씨가 더할 나위 없이 훌륭하다고 할 수 있겠나? 자네는 저 옛 아담을 몰아낼 수 있겠나?\n\n정말이지, 선장님, 이제 좀 알 듯도 합니다. 예, 그 점에 관해서는 저도 묘한 이야기를 들은 적이 있습니다, 선장님. 돛대를 잃은 사람이 그 옛 돛대의 느낌을 결코 완전히 잃지 않고, 이따금 그것이 여전히 쿡쿡 쑤신다는 이야기 말입니다. 황송하오나 정말 그런 것인지 여쭤도 되겠습니까, 선장님?\n\n그렇다네, 이 사람아. 보게, 자네 산 다리를 한때 내 다리가 있던 자리에 놓아 보게. 자, 그래, 눈에는 또렷한 다리가 하나뿐이지만 영혼에는 둘이 있는 셈이지. 자네가 짜릿한 생명을 느끼는 바로 그곳, 바로 정확히 그곳, 한 치도 어긋남 없이 그곳에서 나도 느낀다네. 이게 수수께끼인가?\n\n황송하오나 난제라 하겠습니다, 선장님.\n\n그럼 들어 보게. 어떤 온전한, 살아 있는, 생각하는 존재가 눈에 보이지도 않고 서로 뚫고 지나가지도 않으면서 자네가 지금 서 있는 바로 그 자리에 서 있지 않다고 자네가 어찌 아는가? 그래, 자네가 싫다는데도 거기 버티고 서 있을 수도 있지 않은가? 그렇다면 가장 고독한 시간에 자네는 엿듣는 자들이 두렵지 않은가? 가만, 말하지 말게! 그리고 짓이겨진 내 다리가 이미 그토록 오래전에 스러졌건만 여전히 그 쓰라림이 느껴진다면, 그렇다면 목수, 자네라고 어찌 몸뚱이도 없이 영원토록 지옥의 불타는 고통을 느끼지 못하란 법이 있겠나? 하!\n\n맙소사! 정말이지, 선장님, 일이 그 지경에 이른다면, 저는 처음부터 다시 셈을 해봐야겠습니다. 아무래도 받아올림 하나를 빠뜨린 것 같습니다, 선장님.\n\n이보게, 머리가 푸딩 같은 자들은 절대로 전제를 인정해서는 안 되는 법이야. — 다리가 다 되려면 얼마나 남았나?\n\n아마 한 시간쯤이요, 선장님.\n\n그럼 어서 그것이나 더듬더듬 만들어서 내게 가져오게 (가려고 돌아선다). 오, 인생이여! 나는 여기 그리스 신처럼 당당하게 서 있건만, 그러면서도 딛고 설 뼈다귀 하나 때문에 이 멍청이에게 빚진 신세라니! 장부를 없애 주지 못하는 저 인간들 사이의 상호 부채, 그것에 저주가 있으라. 나는 공기처럼 자유롭고 싶건만, 온 세상의 장부에 빚진 자로 올라 있구나. 나는 어찌나 부유한지, 로마 제국(곧 온 세상)을 경매에 부쳤을 때 가장 부유한 친위대원들과 호가에 호가로 맞붙어 겨룰 수도 있었으련만, 그러면서도 내가 자랑삼아 놀리는 이 혀의 살점 값마저 빚지고 있다니. 하늘에 맹세코! 도가니를 하나 구해다가 그 속에 들어가, 작고 간략한 척추뼈 하나로 나 자신을 녹여 버리리라. 그래.\n\n목수 (다시 일을 시작하며).\n\n거참, 거참, 거참! 스터브가 저분을 누구보다 잘 아는데, 스터브는 늘 저분을 괴짜라고 하지. 그 한마디면 족하다는 듯 괴짜라는 그 짤막한 말 외에는 아무 말도 안 해. 괴짜라니까, 스터브가 그래. 괴짜래 — 괴짜, 괴짜라고. 그러고는 시도 때도 없이 스타벅 씨 귀에다 그 말을 못박듯 박아 넣지 — 괴짜라니까 — 정말이지 — 괴짜, 괴짜, 아주 괴짜라고. 그리고 여기 저분의 다리가 있구나! 그래, 생각해 보니 여기 저분의 잠자리 벗이 있는 셈이지! 고래 턱뼈 막대기 하나를 아내로 삼고 있다니! 그리고 이게 저분의 다리야. 이걸 딛고 서겠지. 아까 그게 무슨 소리였더라, 다리 하나가 세 군데에 서 있고, 그 세 군데가 모두 한 지옥에 서 있다는 — 그게 어떻게 된 거였지? 오! 저분이 날 그리 멸시하듯 쳐다본 것도 무리가 아니지! 사람들이 그러는데 나도 가끔은 묘한 생각을 하는 부류라더군. 하지만 그건 그저 어쩌다 그런 거지. 그러니 나처럼 키 작고 자그마한 늙은이가 키 크고 왜가리처럼 생긴 선장들과 어울려 깊은 물로 첨벙첨벙 걸어 들어가려 해서는 안 되는 거야. 물이 어느새 턱 밑까지 차올라서, 구명보트 내놓으라고 아우성을 치게 되거든. 그리고 여기 그 왜가리 다리가 있군! 길고 가느다란 게 영락없구나! 자, 사람들 대부분은 다리 한 쌍이 평생을 가는데, 그건 다들 다리를 아껴 쓰기 때문이지. 마음씨 고운 노부인이 토실토실한 늙은 마차 말을 살뜰히 부리듯 말이야. 하지만 에이해브는, 오, 그분은 사정없이 몰아붙이는 분이지. 보게나, 한쪽 다리는 죽도록 몰아 부려먹고, 다른 한쪽은 평생 절름발이로 만들더니, 이제는 뼈로 만든 다리를 한 발 한 발 닳아 없애고 있으니. 어이, 거기, 이 검댕이 영감! 그 나사 좀 거들어 줘서 끝장을 보세. 부활의 사도가 나팔을 들고 와서 모든 다리를, 진짜든 가짜든 다 불러내기 전에 말이야. 양조장 사람들이 빈 맥주통을 거두러 다니며 다시 채워 넣듯이 말이지. 이거 참 대단한 다리로군! 영락없이 살아 있는 진짜 다리를 알맹이만 남기고 줄로 깎아낸 것 같지 뭔가. 내일이면 저분이 이걸 딛고 서겠지. 이걸 딛고 태양 고도를 재겠지. 어이쿠! 위도를 계산해 적는 그 자그마한 타원형 석판을, 매끄럽게 다듬은 상아 석판을 깜빡할 뻔했군. 그래, 그래. 자, 이제 끌과 줄과 사포다!\n\n# CHAPTER 109. Ahab and Starbuck in the Cabin.\n\n관례에 따라 그들은 이튿날 아침 배의 펌프질을 하고 있었다. 그런데 보라! 적지 않은 기름이 물과 함께 솟아올라 왔다. 아래에 둔 통들이 심하게 새고 있는 것이 틀림없었다. 큰 우려가 일었다. 그리하여 스타벅(Starbuck)은 이 좋지 않은 일을 알리려고 선실로 내려갔다.*\n\n* 상당한 양의 기름을 실은 향유고래잡이 배에서는, 일주일에 두 번 정기적으로 화물칸 안으로 호스를 끌어들여 통들을 바닷물로 적셔 두는 것이 정해진 일과다. 그 물은 그 뒤 일정치 않은 간격을 두고 배의 펌프로 퍼낸다. 이렇게 함으로써 통들이 축축한 상태로 단단히 밀폐되도록 하는 한편, 퍼낸 물의 성질이 달라진 것을 보고 선원들은 그 귀한 화물에 심각한 누출이 있는지를 쉽게 알아챈다.\n\n이제 페쿼드(Pequod)호는 남쪽과 서쪽에서 포모사와 바시 제도에 가까워지고 있었는데, 그 사이에는 중국 해역에서 태평양으로 빠져나가는 열대의 출구 가운데 하나가 자리하고 있다. 그리하여 스타벅은 동방 군도(群島)의 일반 해도를 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 있는 에이해브(Ahab)를 발견했다. 그 곁에는 일본 열도—니폰, 마쓰마이, 시코케—의 길게 뻗은 동쪽 해안을 그린 또 다른 별도의 해도가 있었다. 눈처럼 새하얀 새 상아 다리를 탁자의 나사못 박힌 다리에 버틴 채, 손에는 가지치기용 낫처럼 길쭉한 잭나이프를 들고서, 그 경이로운 노인은 통로 문에 등을 진 채 이마를 찌푸리며 다시금 자신의 옛 항로를 더듬고 있었다.\n\n\"거기 누구냐?\" 문가에서 발소리를 들었으나, 그쪽으로 돌아보지는 않았다. \"갑판으로! 물러가라!\"\n\n\"에이해브 선장님, 사람을 잘못 보셨습니다. 접니다. 화물칸의 기름이 새고 있습니다, 선장님. 버턴 도르래를 올려 통들을 들어내야 합니다.\"\n\n\"버턴을 올려 들어내자고? 이제 막 일본에 가까워지고 있는데, 낡은 테 몇 개를 손보려고 여기서 일주일이나 멈춰 서 있으란 말인가?\"\n\n\"그렇게 하시든가, 아니면 일 년에 걸쳐 메울 수 있는 양보다 더 많은 기름을 하루에 허비하시든가입니다, 선장님. 이만 마일이나 와서 얻으려는 것이니 지킬 만한 가치가 있습니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 그렇고말고. 손에 넣기만 한다면 말이다.\"\n\n\"저는 화물칸의 기름을 두고 말씀드린 것입니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"그리고 나는 그런 것은 말하지도, 생각하지도 않았다. 물러가라! 새게 내버려 두어라! 나 자신이 온통 새고 있는걸. 그렇다! 새는 것 속에 또 새는 것이라니! 새는 통들로 가득 찼을 뿐만 아니라, 그 새는 통들이 새는 배 안에 들어 있는 것이지. 그건 페쿼드호의 처지보다 훨씬 더 고약한 신세야, 이 사람아. 그래도 나는 내 새는 구멍을 틀어막으려 멈춰 서지 않는다. 짐을 잔뜩 실은 선체 속에서 누가 그 구멍을 찾아낼 수 있겠으며, 설령 찾아낸다 한들 이 삶의 울부짖는 폭풍 속에서 어찌 그것을 틀어막기를 바라겠는가? 스타벅! 버턴을 올리는 일은 없을 것이다.\"\n\n\"선주들이 뭐라고 하겠습니까, 선장님?\"\n\n\"선주들더러 낸터킷(Nantucket) 해변에 서서 태풍보다 더 크게 소리쳐 보라지. 에이해브가 무엇을 신경 쓰겠나? 선주들, 선주들이라? 자네는 늘 그 인색한 선주들 얘기를 내게 지껄여 대는군, 스타벅, 마치 선주들이 내 양심이라도 되는 양 말이야. 하지만 보게, 무엇이든 그것의 유일한 진짜 주인은 그것을 지휘하는 자라네. 그리고 잘 들어 두게, 내 양심은 이 배의 용골 속에 있어.—갑판으로!\"\n\n\"에이해브 선장님.\" 얼굴이 붉어진 일등항해사는 더욱 선실 안으로 들어서며 말했는데, 그 대담함이 어찌나 묘하게 공손하고 조심스러웠던지, 마치 그것이 겉으로 드러나는 일은 아무리 작은 기미라도 온갖 방법으로 피하려 할 뿐만 아니라, 그 속으로도 자기 자신을 절반 넘게 미덥지 않아 하는 듯이 보였다. \"저보다 나은 사람이라면 선장님에게서는 능히 넘겨 버릴 일을, 더 젊은 자에게서라면 곧바로 분개할 것입니다. 그렇습니다, 더 행복한 자에게서라면 말이지요, 에이해브 선장님.\"\n\n\"악마들이로다! 그렇다면 네가 감히 나를 비판적으로 생각하기까지 한단 말이냐?—갑판으로!\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 선장님, 아직은 아닙니다. 간청드립니다. 그리고 저는 감히 청합니다, 선장님—참아 주시기를! 이제껏보다 우리가 서로를 더 잘 이해하면 안 되겠습니까, 에이해브 선장님?\"\n\n에이해브는 (남양 항해선 선실 가구의 대부분을 이루는) 거치대에서 장전된 머스킷 총을 집어 들어 스타벅을 향해 겨누며 외쳤다. \"땅 위를 다스리는 주인이신 하느님이 한 분 계시고, 페쿼드호를 다스리는 주인인 선장이 하나 있을 뿐이다.—갑판으로!\"\n\n한순간 일등항해사의 번뜩이는 눈과 불타는 듯한 뺨을 보면, 그가 정녕 겨누어진 총구의 화염을 받았다고 여길 뻔했다. 그러나 그는 감정을 다스리고 절반쯤 차분하게 몸을 일으켰으며, 선실을 나서면서 잠시 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"선장님은 저를 모욕하신 것이 아니라 격분케 하셨습니다. 하나 그 때문에 저는 선장님께 스타벅을 조심하라 청하지 않겠습니다. 그러시면 한낱 웃어넘기실 테니까요. 다만 에이해브가 에이해브를 조심하기를. 당신 자신을 조심하십시오, 노인장.\"\n\n\"이 친구가 점점 대담해지는군, 그러면서도 복종은 하니, 참으로 신중한 대담함이로다!\" 스타벅이 사라지자 에이해브가 중얼거렸다. \"그가 한 말이 무어더라—에이해브가 에이해브를 조심하라—거기엔 무언가 있어!\" 그러더니 자기도 모르게 머스킷 총을 지팡이 삼아, 쇠처럼 굳은 이마를 한 채 그 비좁은 선실을 이리저리 거닐었다. 그러나 이윽고 그의 이마에 두껍게 잡혔던 주름이 풀렸고, 총을 거치대에 도로 놓고 그는 갑판으로 나갔다.\n\n\"자네는 그저 지나치게 좋은 사람일 뿐이야, 스타벅.\" 그는 일등항해사에게 나직이 말했다. 그러고는 선원들을 향해 목소리를 높였다. \"윗돛을 접고, 앞뒤의 중간 돛은 단단히 졸라매라. 큰 활대를 뒤로 돌려라. 버턴을 올리고, 큰 화물칸을 헐어 내라.\"\n\n스타벅에 관해 에이해브가 어찌하여 이렇게 행동했는지를 정확히 헤아려 보는 것은 아마 부질없는 일이리라. 그것은 그의 내면에서 번뜩인 한 줄기 정직함이었을 수도 있고, 아니면 단순한 신중의 책략—이런 상황에서, 제 배의 중요한 수석 항해사가 비록 한순간일지언정 드러내는 공공연한 불만의 기색만큼은 단호히 금해야 한다는 계산이었을 수도 있다. 어찌 됐든 그의 명령은 실행되었고, 버턴은 올려졌다.\n\n# CHAPTER 110. Queequeg in His Coffin.\n\n창고를 뒤져 보니, 가장 나중에 화물칸에 박아 넣은 큰 통들은 완벽하게 멀쩡했고, 따라서 물이 새는 곳은 더 깊숙한 데 있는 것이 분명했다. 그리하여 날씨가 잔잔했으므로, 그들은 점점 더 깊이 파고들어 가, 맨 밑바닥에 깔린 거대한 통들의 잠을 어지럽혔다. 그 칠흑 같은 한밤중에서 그 거대한 두더지들을 끌어올려 위쪽의 햇빛 속으로 내보낸 셈이었다. 그들이 어찌나 깊이 내려갔던지, 그리고 가장 밑바닥의 큰 통들이 어찌나 오래되고, 부식되고, 해초가 엉겨 붙은 꼴이었던지, 다음에는 노아 선장(Captain Noah)의 동전들과, 어리석은 옛 세상을 향해 홍수를 헛되이 경고하던 벽보 사본들이 담긴 곰팡내 나는 주춧돌 통이라도 나오지 않을까 싶을 지경이었다. 물통과 빵통과 소고기통과 통널 묶음과 철제 테 다발이 한 통씩 차례로 들려 나오니, 마침내 갑판은 쌓인 짐들로 발 디딜 틈조차 어려워졌다. 텅 빈 선체는 발밑에서 메아리쳐, 마치 텅 빈 지하 묘지 위를 걷는 것만 같았고, 공기만 가득 찬 큰 술병처럼 바다 위에서 휘청거리고 굴렀다. 배는 머릿속에 아리스토텔레스 전부를 담고도 저녁을 못 먹은 학생처럼 위쪽만 무거웠다. 그때 태풍이 그들을 찾아오지 않은 것은 그나마 다행이었다.\n\n자, 바로 이 무렵에 나의 가련한 이교도 동무이자 둘도 없는 가슴속 벗인 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 열병에 걸려, 끝없는 종말의 문턱까지 다다르게 되었다.\n\n말해 두거니와, 이 고래잡이라는 직업에는 한가로운 자리란 없다. 위엄과 위험이 손을 맞잡고 나란히 간다. 선장(Captain)이 되기 전까지는, 높이 오를수록 그만큼 더 고되게 일해야 하는 법이다. 가련한 퀴케그도 마찬가지였으니, 작살잡이로서 그는 살아 있는 고래의 온갖 광포함에 맞서야 할 뿐 아니라 — 앞서 다른 곳에서 보았듯이 — 출렁이는 바다 위에서 죽은 고래의 등에 올라타야 했고, 끝내는 화물칸의 어둠 속으로 내려가, 그 땅속 같은 갑갑한 곳에서 온종일 비지땀을 흘리며 가장 다루기 힘든 통들을 억센 힘으로 다루고 그것들을 차곡차곡 쟁여 넣는 일까지 살펴야 했다. 요컨대 고래잡이들 사이에서 작살잡이란 이른바 '적재꾼'인 셈이다.\n\n가련한 퀴케그! 배의 내장이 거의 절반쯤 도려내어졌을 무렵, 당신이 승강구 위로 몸을 굽혀 거기 있는 그를 내려다보았더라면 좋았으련만. 그곳에서, 모직 속바지만 걸친 채 문신을 새긴 그 야만인은 그 축축함과 진창 속을 기어 다니고 있었으니, 마치 우물 밑바닥의 푸른 점박이 도마뱀 같았다. 그리고 그곳은 이 가련한 이교도에게 어쩐 일인지 우물, 아니 얼음 창고나 다름없는 곳이 되고 말았다. 이상한 일이지만, 그토록 비지땀을 흘리는 더위 속에서도 그는 끔찍한 한기에 휩싸였고, 그것이 열병으로 도졌던 것이다. 그리하여 마침내 며칠을 앓고 난 끝에, 그는 그물 침대에 드러눕고 말았으니, 바로 죽음의 문지방 코앞이었다. 그 더디게 늘어진 며칠 사이에 그는 어찌나 야위고 또 야위어 갔던지, 마침내 그에게 남은 것이라곤 뼈대와 문신뿐인 듯했다. 그러나 그의 나머지 모든 것이 여위어 가고 광대뼈가 점점 더 날카로워질수록, 그의 두 눈은 도리어 점점 더 가득 차 오르는 듯했다. 그 눈은 야릇하리만치 부드러운 광채를 띠더니, 병중인 그곳에서 온화하면서도 깊이 당신을 바라보았다 — 죽지도 약해지지도 않을, 그의 안에 깃든 저 불멸의 건강을 증언하는 경이로운 표징이었다. 그리고 물 위의 둥근 파문이 흐려질수록 도리어 넓게 퍼져 나가듯이, 그의 두 눈도 영원의 고리들처럼 점점 더 둥글게 퍼져 가는 듯했다. 이름 붙일 수 없는 외경심이 당신에게 슬며시 스며들었을 것이다 — 이 스러져 가는 야만인의 곁에 앉아, 조로아스터가 죽을 때 곁에 있던 이들이 보았을 법한 그 기이한 무언가를 그의 얼굴에서 보게 된다면 말이다. 인간 속에 깃든 진실로 경이롭고 두려운 것은, 그 무엇도 아직껏 말이나 책으로 담긴 적이 없기 때문이다. 그리고 만인을 똑같이 평등하게 만들고, 만인에게 똑같이 마지막 계시를 새겨 주는 죽음의 다가옴이란, 오직 죽음에서 돌아온 저자만이 온전히 들려줄 수 있는 것이다. 그러니 — 다시 한번 말하건대 — 죽어 가는 어떤 칼데아인이나 그리스인도, 가련한 퀴케그가 흔들리는 그물 침대에 고요히 누워 있을 때 그의 얼굴 위로 기어 다니던 그 신비로운 그늘만큼 높고 거룩한 생각을 품지는 못했으리라. 출렁이는 바다는 그를 마지막 안식으로 부드럽게 흔들어 재우는 듯했고, 대양의 보이지 않는 밀물은 그를 점점 더 높이, 정해진 그의 천국을 향해 들어 올리는 듯했다.\n\n선원 가운데 그를 단념하지 않은 자가 없었다. 그리고 퀴케그 자신으로 말하자면, 그가 제 처지를 어찌 여기는지는 그가 청한 어떤 묘한 부탁에서 똑똑히 드러났다. 그는 날이 막 밝아 오는 잿빛 새벽 당직 시간에 한 사람을 곁으로 불러, 그의 손을 잡고서 말했다. 낸터킷(Nantucket)에 머무는 동안, 그는 우연히 어떤 검은 나무로 만든 작은 카누들을 본 적이 있었는데, 그것이 제 고향 섬의 값진 전쟁용 목재와 흡사했다고 했다. 그래서 물어보니, 낸터킷에서 죽은 고래잡이들은 모두 그 똑같은 검은 카누 안에 누인다는 것을 알게 되었고, 그렇게 누인다는 생각이 그의 마음에 무척 흡족했다고 했다. 그것이 제 종족의 풍습과 별반 다르지 않았기 때문이니, 그들은 죽은 전사의 시신에 향유를 바른 뒤 그를 카누 안에 누여, 별들의 군도(群島)로 떠내려가도록 그대로 띄워 보냈던 것이다. 그들은 별이 곧 섬이라고 믿을 뿐 아니라, 눈에 보이는 모든 수평선 너머 저 멀리, 뭍이라곤 없는 그들 자신의 온화한 바다가 푸른 하늘과 어우러져 흘러들며, 그렇게 은하수의 하얀 물보라를 이룬다고 믿었다. 그는 덧붙이기를, 흔한 바다 풍습대로 제 그물 침대에 싸여 묻혀, 죽음을 게걸스레 삼키는 상어들에게 천한 쓰레기처럼 내던져진다는 생각만 해도 몸서리가 쳐진다고 했다. 아니다. 그는 낸터킷의 그것들과 같은 카누를 원했다. 그것은 고래잡이인 그에게 한결 더 어울리는 것이었으니, 고래잡이 보트처럼 이 관(棺) 카누들에는 용골이 없었기 때문이다. 비록 그 때문에 키잡이가 불안정하고, 흐릿한 세월의 흐름 속에서 옆으로 많이 떠밀려 가게 된다 할지라도 말이다.\n\n자, 이 기이한 사정이 고물 쪽에 알려지자, 목수에게는 즉시 퀴케그의 청을 들어주라는 명령이 떨어졌으니, 그 청에 무엇이 포함되어 있든 상관없었다. 배에는 어떤 이교도풍의, 관 빛깔을 띤 오래된 목재가 실려 있었는데, 그것은 오래전 어느 항해에서 래커데이 제도의 원시림에서 베어 온 것이었다. 그 검은 널빤지들로 관을 만들라는 권유가 내려졌다. 목수는 명령을 전해 듣자마자 자를 들고는, 곧장 제 성격다운 무심하고 민첩한 태도로 선수루로 들어가 퀴케그의 치수를 매우 정확하게 쟀으니, 자를 옮길 때마다 퀴케그의 몸에 일정하게 분필 자국을 그어 가며 했다.\n\n\"아! 가엾은 친구! 이제 죽어야겠구먼.\" 롱아일랜드 출신 선원이 내뱉었다.\n\n목수는 제 바이스 작업대로 가서, 편의와 두루 참고하기 위해, 관이 될 정확한 길이를 그 위에 옮겨 재고는, 그 양 끝에 홈 두 개를 새겨 그 표시를 영구히 남겼다. 이 일을 마치자, 그는 널빤지들과 연장들을 가지런히 늘어놓고 작업에 들어갔다.\n\n마지막 못을 박고, 뚜껑을 알맞게 대패질하여 꼭 맞추고 나자, 그는 그 관을 가볍게 어깨에 둘러메고 앞쪽으로 가면서, 그쪽에서 이제 그것을 받을 준비가 되었는지 물었다.\n\n갑판 위의 사람들이 그 관을 쫓아내려 지르기 시작한, 분개하면서도 반쯤은 익살스러운 외침을 엿들은 퀴케그는, 모두를 경악케 하면서, 그 물건을 당장 자기에게 가져오라고 명령했다. 그의 청을 거절할 도리는 없었다. 모든 인간 가운데 죽어 가는 이들이야말로 더러는 가장 폭군 같은 법이고, 또한 머지않아 그들이 영영 우리를 거의 성가시게 하지 않을 터이니, 그 가련한 친구들의 청을 들어주는 것이 마땅하기 때문이었다.\n\n그물 침대에서 몸을 기울인 채, 퀴케그는 그 관을 한참 동안 주의 깊은 눈으로 바라보았다. 그러더니 자기 작살을 가져오라 하여, 거기서 나무 자루를 뽑아내게 하고는, 쇠로 된 부분을 제 보트의 노 하나와 함께 관 안에 넣게 했다. 또한 모두 그 자신의 요청에 따라, 비스킷이 관 안쪽 옆구리를 빙 둘러 늘어놓였고, 머리맡에는 신선한 물이 담긴 병이 놓였으며, 발치에는 화물칸에서 긁어모은 나무 섞인 흙 한 자루가 놓였다. 그리고 돛천 한 조각을 베개 삼아 둘둘 말아 놓고서, 퀴케그는 이제 자신을 그 마지막 침상으로 들어 올려 달라고 간청했으니, 그것이 안락한 데가 조금이라도 있는지 시험해 보려는 것이었다. 그는 몇 분 동안 꼼짝 않고 누워 있다가, 한 사람더러 제 자루로 가서 자신의 작은 신, 요조(Yojo)를 꺼내 오라고 했다. 그러더니 요조를 사이에 끼우고 두 팔을 가슴 위에 엇갈려 포갠 채, 관 뚜껑을(그는 그것을 승강구 덮개라 불렀다) 제 위에 덮어 달라고 했다. 머리 쪽 부분은 가죽 경첩으로 젖혀졌고, 그렇게 퀴케그는 제 관 안에 누워, 그 차분한 얼굴 외에는 거의 보이지 않게 되었다. \"라르마이\"(괜찮다, 편하다), 마침내 그가 중얼거리고는, 다시 그물 침대로 옮겨 달라는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n그러나 이 일이 이루어지기 전에, 그동안 줄곧 곁을 슬며시 맴돌던 핍(Pip)이 그가 누운 곳으로 다가와, 나직이 흐느끼며 그의 손을 잡았다. 다른 손에는 제 탬버린을 들고 있었다.\n\n\"가엾은 떠돌이여! 이 지긋지긋한 떠돌이 신세를 영영 끝내지 못하려나? 이제 어디로 가나? 하지만 만약 해류가 그대를 저 달콤한 앤틸리스 제도로 데려간다면, 그 바닷가가 오직 수련꽃으로만 찰싹이는 그곳으로 데려간다면, 나를 위해 작은 심부름 하나 해 주려나? 핍이라는 녀석을 찾아 줘, 오래도록 행방불명된 녀석을. 내 생각엔 그가 저 먼 앤틸리스에 있는 것 같아. 만약 그를 찾거든, 위로해 줘. 그는 무척 슬플 테니까. 보라고! 그는 제 탬버린을 두고 갔거든 — 그걸 내가 찾았어. 리그어디그, 디그, 디그! 자, 퀴케그, 죽어. 그럼 내가 그대의 장송곡을 두드려 주지.\"\n\n\"내가 들은 바로는,\" 스타벅(Starbuck)이 승강구를 내려다보며 중얼거렸다. \"심한 열병에 걸리면, 아무것도 모르는 사람들이 고대의 언어로 말을 한다고들 하지. 그리고 그 수수께끼를 캐 보면, 언제나 밝혀지는 것이, 그들이 까맣게 잊어버린 어린 시절에 어떤 고매한 학자들이 그 고대 언어로 말하는 것을 그들이 실제로 들은 적이 있었다는 거야. 그러니 내 애틋한 믿음으로는, 가엾은 핍이 이 미친 상태의 기이한 감미로움 속에서, 우리 모두의 천국 같은 고향을 증명하는 하늘의 증표를 가져오는 것이리라. 그가 저것을 어디서 배웠겠나, 그곳이 아니라면? — 쉿! 그가 또 말을 하는군. 한데 이젠 더 거칠어졌어.\"\n\n\"둘씩 둘씩 줄을 서! 그를 장군으로 삼자고! 호, 그의 작살은 어디 있지? 여기 가로질러 놔. — 리그어디그, 디그, 디그! 만세! 오, 지금 싸움닭 한 마리가 그의 머리 위에 올라앉아 울어 준다면! 퀴케그는 용감하게 죽는다! — 잘들 새겨 둬, 퀴케그는 용감하게 죽어! — 그걸 똑똑히 명심해, 퀴케그는 용감하게 죽는단 말이야! 용감하게, 용감하게, 용감하게! 한데 비천하고 보잘것없는 핍, 그놈은 겁쟁이로 죽었지. 온통 벌벌 떨면서 죽었어 — 핍 따위는 꺼지라지! 들어 봐. 만약 핍을 찾거든, 앤틸리스 전체에 그가 도망자라고 말해 줘. 겁쟁이, 겁쟁이, 겁쟁이라고! 그가 고래잡이 보트에서 뛰어내렸다고 일러 줘! 비천한 핍이 다시 한번 여기서 죽어 간다 한들, 나는 결코 그놈을 위해 탬버린을 두드리며 그를 장군이라 부르지 않을 거야. 안 돼, 안 돼! 모든 겁쟁이에게 수치를 — 그들에게 수치를! 그놈들은 핍처럼 빠져 죽게 내버려 둬, 고래잡이 보트에서 뛰어내린 핍처럼. 수치! 수치다!\"\n\n이러는 내내, 퀴케그는 마치 꿈속에 잠긴 듯 두 눈을 감은 채 누워 있었다. 핍은 이끌려 나갔고, 병든 이는 다시 그물 침대로 옮겨졌다.\n\n그러나 그가 겉보기엔 죽음을 위한 만반의 준비를 다 갖춘 지금, 그의 관이 몸에 꼭 맞는다는 것이 증명된 지금, 퀴케그는 돌연 기운을 차렸다. 머지않아 목수의 그 상자는 필요 없어 보였다. 그리하여 몇몇이 기쁨에 찬 놀라움을 나타내자, 그는 요컨대 이렇게 말했다. 자신이 이토록 갑작스레 회복한 까닭은 이러하다고. — 위중한 순간에, 그는 문득 뭍에서 다 해 두지 못하고 남겨 둔 작은 볼일 하나가 떠올랐고, 그래서 죽음에 대한 마음을 바꾸었다는 것이었다. 아직은 죽을 수 없노라고, 그는 단언했다. 그러자 사람들은 그에게, 그렇다면 살고 죽는 것이 그 자신의 지엄한 의지와 뜻에 달린 문제냐고 물었다. 그는 물론이라고 답했다. 한마디로, 사람이 살기로 마음을 정하면 한낱 병 따위로는 그를 죽일 수 없다는 것이 퀴케그의 지론이었다. 그를 죽일 수 있는 것은 오직 고래나, 거센 폭풍이나, 그런 종류의 사납고 제어할 수 없으며 지각없는 파괴자뿐이라는 것이었다.\n\n자, 야만인과 문명인 사이에는 주목할 만한 차이가 하나 있으니, 그것은 병든 문명인은 대체로 회복에 여섯 달이 걸릴 수도 있는 반면, 병든 야만인은 하루 만에 거의 절반은 나아 버린다는 것이다. 그리하여 마침맞은 때에 나의 퀴케그는 기력을 되찾았다. 그리고 끝내 며칠 동안 권양기 위에 앉아 빈둥거리고 나서(그러면서도 왕성한 식욕으로 먹어 댔다), 그는 돌연 벌떡 일어나 두 팔과 두 다리를 쭉 뻗고는, 시원하게 기지개를 켜고 하품을 조금 하더니, 매달아 둔 제 보트의 이물로 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 작살을 겨눠 들고서, 자신은 싸울 채비가 되었노라고 선언했다.\n\n거친 변덕을 부려, 그는 이제 제 관을 바다 궤짝으로 썼다. 그 안에 제 옷가지가 든 천 자루를 비워 넣고는, 그것들을 그 안에 가지런히 정돈했다. 그는 한가한 시간을 많이 들여, 온갖 기괴한 형상과 그림으로 그 뚜껑에 조각을 새겼다. 그렇게 함으로써 그는 제 투박한 방식으로, 제 몸에 새겨진 비비 꼬인 문신의 일부를 본떠 보려 애쓰는 듯했다. 그리고 이 문신은 그의 섬에서 이미 세상을 떠난 어느 예언자이자 선견자가 새긴 작품이었으니, 그는 그 상형문자 같은 표식들로 퀴케그의 몸에 하늘과 땅에 관한 완전한 이론과, 진리에 이르는 법에 관한 신비로운 논고를 써 놓았던 것이다. 그리하여 퀴케그는 그 자신의 몸뚱이로서 풀어내야 할 하나의 수수께끼였고, 한 권에 담긴 경이로운 저작이었다. 그러나 그 신비를 정작 그 자신조차 읽어 낼 수 없었으니, 비록 그의 살아 있는 심장이 바로 그 신비를 향해 고동치고 있었음에도 그러했다. 그러므로 이 신비들은 끝내, 그것들이 새겨진 살아 있는 양피지와 더불어 썩어 사라져, 마지막까지 풀리지 못할 운명이었다. 그리고 어느 아침 가련한 퀴케그를 바라보다가 등을 돌리며 에이해브(Ahab)가 내뱉었던 저 거친 외침을 떠올리게 한 것도, 바로 이 생각이었음에 틀림없다 — \"오, 신들의 악마 같은 감질나는 희롱이여!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 111. The Pacific.\n\n바시 제도를 미끄러지듯 지나치며 우리는 마침내 저 광대한 남양(南洋)으로 나섰다. 다른 사정만 없었던들 나는 헤아릴 수 없는 감사로 사랑하는 태평양에게 인사를 건넸으리라. 이제 내 젊은 날의 오랜 간구가 응답받았으니, 저 고요한 대양이 내게서 동쪽으로 천 리그에 걸친 푸른 물결을 굽이쳐 펼쳐 내고 있었다.\n\n이 바다에는 무어라 형언할 수 없는 달콤한 신비가 깃들어 있다. 그 잔잔하면서도 두려운 일렁임은 그 아래 숨은 어떤 영혼을 말해 주는 듯하니, 마치 땅에 묻힌 복음서 기자 성 요한의 무덤 위로 전설처럼 물결치던 에페소스의 잔디와도 같다. 그리고 마땅한 일이거니와, 이 바다의 목초지 위로, 사대륙의 너르게 굽이치는 물의 초원이자 모든 옹기장이 밭 위로, 파도가 솟았다 가라앉고 끝없이 밀려왔다 빠져나가는 것이다. 이곳에는 뒤섞인 무수한 그늘과 그림자, 익사한 꿈들, 몽유와 몽상이, 우리가 삶이라 영혼이라 부르는 그 모든 것이 누워 꿈꾸고 있고, 꿈꾸고 있고, 여전히 꿈꾸며, 침대에서 잠든 이들처럼 뒤척이고 있으니, 끊임없이 굽이치는 파도란 다만 그들의 잠 못 이루는 뒤척임이 빚어낸 것일 뿐이다.\n\n* 옹기장이 밭: 마태복음에서 유다가 예수를 판 돈으로 산 나그네 묘지. 이름 없는 자들의 매장지를 뜻한다.\n사색에 잠긴 어떤 동방박사 같은 방랑자에게라도, 이 고요한 태평양은 한 번 바라본 뒤로는 영영 그가 택해 모실 바다가 될 수밖에 없다. 이 바다는 세계의 한복판 물결을 굽이쳐 흐르게 하며, 인도양과 대서양조차 그 양팔에 지나지 않는다. 같은 파도가 이제 막 가장 새로운 인종의 손으로 어제 세워진 캘리포니아 신도시들의 방파제를 적시는가 하면, 아브라함보다도 오래된 아시아 땅의 빛바랬으되 여전히 화려한 옷자락을 씻어 낸다. 그리고 그 사이로는 산호섬들의 은하수와, 낮게 깔린 끝없는 미지의 군도들과, 발 들여놓을 수 없는 일본 열도가 떠 있다. 이렇듯 이 신비롭고 거룩한 태평양은 세계의 온 덩어리를 띠처럼 두르고, 모든 해안을 자신의 한 만(灣)으로 만들며, 마치 조수로 고동치는 지구의 심장처럼 보인다. 저 영원한 너울에 들어 올려질 때, 그대는 이 유혹하는 신에게 고개를 숙여 판(Pan)에게 절하지 않을 수 없으리라.\n\n그러나 에이해브의 머릿속에는 판에 대한 생각이 거의 일지 않았다. 늘 서 있던 자리, 곧 뒷돛대 삭구 곁에서 무쇠 조각상처럼 서서, 한쪽 콧구멍으로는 무심히 바시 제도에서 풍겨 오는 설탕처럼 달콤한 사향 내음(그 향긋한 숲에서는 다정한 연인들이 거닐고 있으리라)을 들이마시고, 다른 콧구멍으로는 새로이 찾아든 바다의 짠 숨결을, 곧 미워 마지않는 흰 고래가 바로 그 순간에도 헤엄치고 있을 그 바다의 숨결을 또렷이 의식하며 들이켰다. 마침내 이 거의 마지막에 다다른 바다 위로 배를 띄워, 일본 어장을 향해 미끄러져 나아가면서, 노인의 결의는 더욱 단단해졌다. 굳게 다문 그의 입술은 죔쇠의 두 턱처럼 맞물렸고, 이마에 솟은 핏줄의 삼각주는 물 넘치는 개울처럼 부풀어 올랐다. 잠결에조차 그의 쩌렁쩌렁한 외침이 둥근 선체 안을 뚫고 울려 퍼졌다. \"전원 후진! 흰 고래가 검붉은 피를 뿜는다!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 112. The Blacksmith.\n\n지금 이 위도에서 펼쳐지는 온화하고 여름처럼 서늘한 날씨를 십분 활용하면서, 또한 머지않아 닥쳐올 유난히 분주한 사냥에 대비하느라, 그을음투성이에 물집투성이인 늙은 대장장이 페스(Perth)는 에이해브(Ahab)의 다리를 만드는 데 거든 일을 마친 뒤에도 휴대용 화로를 다시 선창으로 옮기지 않았고, 여전히 갑판 위에 둔 채 앞돛대 옆의 고리볼트에 단단히 붙들어 매어 두었다. 작살대장과 작살잡이와 뱃머리 노잡이들이 이런저런 소소한 일을 부탁하느라 거의 끊임없이 그를 불러 댔기 때문이었다. 그들은 갖가지 무기와 보트 장비를 손보거나, 고치거나, 새 모양으로 다듬어 달라고 청했다. 그는 종종 차례를 기다리는 사람들의 열성적인 무리에 둘러싸이곤 했는데, 그들은 보트 삽과 창끝과 작살과 투창을 들고서, 그가 땀 흘리며 일하는 동안 검댕투성이의 그 일거수일투족을 샘하듯 지켜보았다. 그럼에도 이 노인의 망치는 참을성 있는 팔에 들린 참을성 있는 망치였다. 그에게서는 어떤 투덜거림도, 어떤 조바심도, 어떤 짜증도 새어 나오지 않았다. 묵묵하고, 느리고, 엄숙하게, 만성적으로 굽은 등을 한층 더 깊이 숙인 채, 그는 마치 노동이 곧 삶 그 자체이고 망치의 무거운 두드림이 곧 제 심장의 무거운 박동이기라도 한 듯이 부지런히 일했다. 그리고 실로 그러했다. 더없이 비참하게도!\n\n이 노인에게는 독특한 걸음걸이가 있었으니, 걸을 때 어딘지 미세하지만 고통스러워 보이는 비틀거림이 항해 초기부터 선원들의 호기심을 자극했다. 끈질기게 거듭되는 그들의 캐물음에 그는 마침내 굴복하고 말았다. 그리하여 이제는 모두가 그 가련한 운명에 얽힌 부끄러운 사연을 알게 되었다.\n\n어느 매서운 겨울날 한밤중, 두 시골 마을 사이로 난 길에서, 늦은 데다 죄 없는 까닭도 아니었던 이 대장장이는 반쯤 멍한 상태로 치명적인 마비가 제 몸을 슬금슬금 덮쳐 오는 것을 느끼고는, 한쪽으로 기울어 다 허물어져 가는 헛간에 몸을 피했다. 그 결과는 두 발 끝부분을 모두 잃은 것이었다. 이 사연에서 한 토막 한 토막씩, 마침내 그의 인생 드라마를 이루는 네 막의 기쁨과, 아직 파국에 이르지 않은 길고 긴 다섯째 막의 슬픔이 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n그는 노인이었으니, 예순에 가까운 나이에 이르러서야 슬픔의 전문 용어로 파멸이라 불리는 그것과 뒤늦게 마주친 사람이었다. 그는 솜씨가 명성 자자한 장인으로, 일거리가 넘쳐 났다. 집과 정원도 소유했고, 젊고 딸처럼 사랑스러운 아내와 발랄하고 혈색 좋은 세 아이를 품에 안았으며, 일요일마다 숲속에 자리한 정겨운 모습의 교회에 다녔다. 그런데 어느 날 밤, 어둠을 틈타, 게다가 더없이 교활한 변장으로 한층 몸을 감춘 채, 한 절박한 강도가 그의 행복한 가정으로 슬며시 기어들어 와 그들에게서 모든 것을 빼앗아 갔다. 더욱 어두운 이야기는, 다름 아닌 대장장이 자신이 알지도 못한 채 이 강도를 제 가족의 심장 한복판으로 끌어들였다는 것이다. 그것은 바로 술병 마술사였다! 그 치명적인 코르크 마개를 따는 순간, 악귀가 튀어나와 그의 가정을 바싹 시들게 했다. 그런데 신중하고 더없이 현명하며 경제적인 이유에서, 대장장이의 작업장은 그가 사는 집의 지하에 있었으되 따로 난 출입구를 갖추고 있었다. 그리하여 젊고 사랑스럽고 건강한 아내는 늘, 어떤 불행한 초조함도 없이 도리어 활기찬 즐거움으로, 젊은 팔뚝을 지닌 늙은 남편이 휘두르는 망치의 우렁찬 울림에 귀를 기울이곤 했다. 마룻바닥과 벽을 지나오며 한결 부드러워진 그 울림은 아기방의 그녀에게로 제법 감미롭게 올라왔다. 그렇게 억센 노동이 들려주는 무쇠 자장가에 맞추어, 대장장이의 어린것들은 잠 속으로 흔들려 갔다.\n\n오, 비탄 위에 또 비탄이! 오, 죽음이여, 그대는 어찌하여 때로는 제때에 찾아오지 못하는가? 완전한 파멸이 이 늙은 대장장이를 덮치기 전에 그대가 그를 데려갔더라면, 젊은 미망인은 달콤한 슬픔을 누렸을 것이요, 그 고아들은 훗날 세월 속에서 꿈꿀 수 있는 참으로 존경스럽고 전설적인 아버지를 가졌을 것이며, 그들 모두 근심을 덜어 줄 넉넉한 살림을 지녔으리라. 그러나 죽음은 어느 덕망 높은 형을 꺾어 버렸으니, 휘파람 불며 날마다 흘리는 그의 노동에 또 다른 한 가정의 책임이 오롯이 걸려 있던 사람이었거니와, 그러면서도 쓸모없는 것만도 못한 이 노인은 그대로 세워 두어, 삶의 끔찍한 썩어 감이 그를 거두기 쉽게 만들 때까지 버려두었다.\n\n그 전부를 굳이 다 이야기할 까닭이 있겠는가? 지하실 망치의 두드림은 날마다 점점 더 띄엄띄엄해졌고, 매번의 두드림은 날마다 지난번보다 더 희미해졌다. 아내는 창가에 얼어붙은 듯 앉아, 눈물조차 마른 눈으로, 우는 아이들의 얼굴을 번득이며 응시했다. 풀무는 늘어졌고, 화로는 잿더미로 막혔으며, 집은 팔렸다. 어머니는 교회 묘지의 긴 풀밭 속으로 잠겨 들었고, 아이들도 두 차례나 그녀를 따라 그곳으로 갔다. 집도 가족도 없는 늙은이는 상장(喪章)을 두른 떠돌이가 되어 비틀거리며 떠나갔다. 그의 비탄은 그 무엇 하나 공경받지 못했고, 그의 희끗한 머리는 노란 곱슬머리들의 비웃음거리가 되었다!\n\n이런 인생에는 죽음만이 유일하게 바람직한 후일담으로 보일 것이다. 그러나 죽음이란 다만 기이한 미지(未知)의 영역으로 배를 띄우는 일에 지나지 않는다. 그것은 광막한 머나먼 곳, 거친 곳, 물의 세계, 기슭 없는 곳이 품은 갖가지 가능성을 향한 첫 인사일 따름이다. 그러므로 죽음을 갈망하면서도 마음속 어딘가에 아직 자살을 꺼리는 양심의 가책이 남아 있는 그런 사람들의 눈앞에, 모든 것을 받아들이고 모든 것이 흘러드는 바다는 그 상상조차 할 수 없는, 사람의 넋을 앗아 가는 공포와 경이로운 새 삶의 모험으로 가득한 너른 들판을 통째로 유혹하듯 펼쳐 보인다. 그리하여 끝없는 태평양들의 가슴속으로부터, 수천의 인어가 그들에게 노래한다. \"이리로 오라, 마음이 부서진 자여. 여기 중간에 끼인 죽음의 죄책감 없이 또 하나의 삶이 있노라. 여기 그것을 위해 죽지 않고도 누릴 초자연의 경이가 있노라. 이리로 오라! 이제 그대가 똑같이 혐오하고 또 그대를 혐오하는 저 뭍의 세계보다 더 망각에 잠긴 삶 속에 그대 자신을 묻으라. 이리로 오라! 그대의 묘비 또한 교회 묘지 안에 세워 두고, 이리로 오라, 우리가 그대를 혼인시켜 줄 때까지!\"\n\n이 목소리들에 귀 기울이며, 동쪽에서 서쪽에서, 이른 해돋이 무렵과 저물녘에, 대장장이의 영혼은 화답했다. 그래, 내가 가마! 그리하여 페스는 고래잡이의 길로 나섰다.\n\n# CHAPTER 113. The Forge.\n\n정오 무렵, 텁수룩한 수염을 하고 빳빳한 상어 가죽 앞치마를 두른 채, 퍼스는 화덕과 모루 사이에 서 있었다. 모루는 철목(鐵木) 토막 위에 놓여 있었고, 그는 한 손으로 작살촉을 숯불 속에 쥐고, 다른 손으로는 화덕의 풀무를 다루고 있었다. 그때 에이해브 선장이 작고 녹슨 듯한 가죽 자루를 손에 들고 다가왔다. 화덕에서 조금 떨어진 곳에서 침울한 에이해브는 걸음을 멈추었다. 마침내 퍼스가 쇠를 불에서 꺼내 모루 위에 두드리기 시작하자 — 벌겋게 달아오른 쇳덩이가 두텁게 떠도는 불꽃을 흩뿌렸고, 그중 몇 점은 에이해브의 곁으로 바짝 날아들었다.\n\n\"이게 자네의 폭풍 바닷새들인가, 퍼스? 늘 자네 자취를 따라 날아다니는군. 길조의 새들이긴 하지만, 모두에게 그런 건 아니지. 보게, 이놈들은 불에 탄다네. 그런데 자네는 — 자네는 그것들 속에서 살면서도 그을음 하나 입지 않는군.\"\n\n\"제가 이미 온몸이 다 그을렸으니 그렇지요, 에이해브 선장님.\" 퍼스가 망치를 짚고 잠시 쉬며 대답했다. \"저는 이미 그을릴 단계를 지났습니다. 흉터를 다시 그을리기란 쉬운 일이 아니지요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 좋아. 그만하게. 자네의 쪼그라든 목소리는 내게 너무도 차분하고, 너무도 멀쩡하게 슬프게 들리는군. 나 스스로도 낙원에 있지 못한 처지인지라, 광기에 미치지 못한 채 그저 비참하기만 한 남의 고통은 도무지 견딜 수가 없네. 자네는 미쳐야 마땅하네, 대장장이. 말해 보게, 어찌하여 자네는 미치지 않는가? 미치지 않고서 어찌 견딘단 말인가? 하늘이 아직도 자네를 미워하기에, 자네가 미칠 수조차 없는 건가? — 거기서 무얼 만들고 있었나?\"\n\n\"낡은 작살촉을 벼리고 있었습니다, 선장님. 이음매며 우그러진 데가 있어서요.\"\n\n\"그러면 그토록 험하게 부려 먹은 것을 다시 매끈하게 만들 수 있겠나, 대장장이?\"\n\n\"그럴 수 있을 것 같습니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 자네는 아무리 단단한 쇠라 한들, 거의 어떤 이음매든 우그러진 데든 매끈하게 펼 수 있겠군그래, 대장장이?\"\n\n\"예, 선장님, 그럴 수 있을 것 같습니다. 단 하나를 빼고는 모든 이음매와 우그러진 데를 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 여기를 보게.\" 에이해브가 격정에 차 다가서며, 두 손으로 퍼스의 두 어깨를 짚고 몸을 기댔다. \"여기를 보게 — 여기 말이야 — 이런 이음매를 자네가 펼 수 있겠나, 대장장이?\" 그러면서 한 손으로 주름진 자기 이마를 쓸어내렸다. \"만일 자네가 그럴 수만 있다면, 대장장이, 나는 기꺼이 이 머리를 자네 모루 위에 얹고, 두 눈 사이로 자네의 가장 묵직한 망치를 받아 보고 싶네. 대답하게! 이 이음매를 펼 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"오! 그것이 바로 그 하나입니다, 선장님! 단 하나만 빼고 모든 이음매와 우그러진 데라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"그래, 대장장이, 그것이 바로 그 하나일세. 그래, 사람아, 그것은 펴지지 않는 것이지. 자네 눈에는 이렇게 내 살갗에만 보이지만, 그것은 내 두개골 뼈 속까지 파고들어 갔네 — 그 속이 온통 주름투성이라네! 하지만 어린애 장난은 그만두지. 오늘은 더 이상 갈고리며 작살은 됐네. 여기를 보게!\" 그는 마치 금화가 가득 든 양 가죽 자루를 짤랑거렸다. \"나도 작살 하나가 필요하네. 천 마리 악마가 멍에를 메고 끌어당겨도 갈라놓지 못할 작살 말일세, 퍼스. 고래의 제 지느러미뼈처럼 그놈 몸에 단단히 박힐 작살 말이야. 재료는 여기 있네.\" 그가 주머니를 모루 위에 내던졌다. \"보게, 대장장이, 이것은 경주마들의 강철 편자에서 모은 못 토막들일세.\"\n\n\"편자 못 토막이라고요, 선장님? 아니, 에이해브 선장님, 그러면 여기 우리 대장장이들이 다뤄 본 것 중에서 가장 좋고 가장 질긴 재료를 가지고 계신 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"나도 아네, 노인장. 이 토막들은 살인자들의 녹은 뼈에서 나온 아교처럼 단단히 엉겨 붙을 걸세. 어서! 내게 작살을 벼려 주게. 우선 그 자루에 들어갈 봉을 열두 개 벼려 주게. 그런 다음 이 열두 개를 예인줄의 가닥과 올처럼 비틀고 꼬아 한데 망치질해 합치게나. 어서! 내가 불을 불어 주겠네.\"\n\n마침내 봉 열두 개가 만들어지자, 에이해브는 그것들을 하나씩 손수 긴 쇠 빗장에 나선처럼 감아 보며 시험했다. \"흠집이 있군!\" 그는 마지막 하나를 물리쳤다. \"이건 다시 손보게, 퍼스.\"\n\n이 일이 끝나자 퍼스는 열두 개를 하나로 용접하려 했으나, 에이해브가 그의 손을 멈춰 세우며 자기 손으로 직접 쇠를 벼리겠다고 말했다. 그리하여 에이해브가 규칙적으로 가쁜 숨을 헐떡이며 모루 위를 두드리고, 퍼스가 벌겋게 달아오른 봉을 하나씩 차례로 그에게 건네고, 세차게 압박된 화덕이 곧고 강렬한 불길을 위로 뿜어 올리는 동안, 파르시(Parsee) 사람이 소리 없이 다가와 불을 향해 머리를 숙이며, 이 노역에 어떤 저주인지 어떤 축복인지를 비는 듯했다. 그러나 에이해브가 고개를 들자 그는 슬며시 옆으로 비켜섰다.\n\n\"저기서 알짱거리는 저 성냥개비 같은 작자는 대체 뭐 하는 거야?\" 앞갑판에서 지켜보던 스터브가 중얼거렸다. \"저 파르시 놈은 도화선처럼 불 냄새를 맡고 다니지. 게다가 제 몸에서도 불 냄새가 나, 마치 달아오른 머스킷 총의 화약 접시처럼 말이야.\"\n\n마침내 하나의 완전한 봉으로 합쳐진 자루가 마지막 가열을 받았다. 퍼스가 그것을 단련하려고 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 옆에 놓인 물통 속에 푹 담그자, 데인 듯 뜨거운 김이 에이해브의 수그린 얼굴로 솟구쳐 올랐다.\n\n\"자네 나를 낙인찍으려는 건가, 퍼스?\" 그는 잠시 통증에 움찔하며 말했다. \"그렇다면 나는 여태 내 낙인 인두를 벼려 온 셈인가?\"\n\n\"하느님께 비옵건대, 부디 그런 일이 아니기를. 그래도 저는 무언가가 두렵습니다, 에이해브 선장님. 이 작살은 흰 고래를 잡으려는 것 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"흰 악마를 잡으려는 것이지! 하지만 이제 미늘 차례일세. 그건 자네가 직접 만들어야 하네, 사람아. 여기 내 면도칼들이 있네 — 최상의 강철이지. 여기, 이걸로 빙해(氷海)의 바늘 같은 진눈깨비처럼 날카롭게 미늘을 만들게.\"\n\n잠시 동안 늙은 대장장이는 차라리 그 면도칼들을 쓰고 싶지 않다는 듯 물끄러미 바라보았다.\n\n\"받게, 사람아, 나는 이제 그것들이 필요 없네. 이제부터 나는 면도도, 식사도, 기도도 하지 않을 테니 — 그때까지는 말이야 — 자, 어서, 일이나 하게!\"\n\n마침내 화살촉 모양으로 빚어진 강철은 퍼스의 손에 의해 자루에 용접되어 곧 그 끝을 뾰족하게 이루었다. 대장장이가 미늘을 단련하기에 앞서 마지막 가열을 하려 할 때, 그는 에이해브에게 물통을 가까이 두라고 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야 — 그것엔 물이 안 되네. 나는 그것이 참된 죽음의 단련을 거치기를 바라네. 어이, 거기! 타슈테고(Tashtego), 퀴퀘그(Queequeg), 다구! 어떤가, 이교도들이여! 이 미늘을 적실 만큼의 피를 내게 주겠나?\" 그는 미늘을 높이 치켜들었다. 검은 얼굴들이 한데 모여 고개를 끄덕여 답했다, 그렇다고. 이교도들의 살에 세 군데 구멍이 뚫렸고, 그리하여 흰 고래의 미늘은 단련되었다.\n\n\"내가 너에게 세례를 주노니, 아버지의 이름이 아니라 악마의 이름으로 하노라!(Ego non baptizo te in nomine patris, sed in nomine diaboli!)\" 악의에 찬 쇠가 세례의 피를 지글지글 집어삼키는 가운데, 에이해브가 광란하듯 울부짖었다.\n\n이제 아래에서 여분의 자루용 장대들을 끌어모은 뒤, 그중 아직 껍질이 그대로 붙어 있는 히커리 나무 하나를 골라, 에이해브는 그 끝을 쇠 미늘의 소켓에 맞추어 끼웠다. 이어 새 예인줄 한 사리를 풀어, 몇 길 정도를 양묘기까지 가져다가 팽팽하게 잡아당겼다. 그 위에 발을 디뎌 밧줄이 하프 줄처럼 윙윙 울릴 때까지 누른 다음, 열심히 몸을 숙여 들여다보고는 가닥이 풀린 데가 없음을 확인하자, 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"좋아! 이제 동여매는 일만 남았군.\"\n\n밧줄의 한쪽 끝을 가닥가닥 풀어 헤친 다음, 따로 펼쳐진 올들을 모두 엮고 짜서 작살의 소켓 둘레에 감았다. 그러고는 장대를 소켓 속으로 단단히 박아 넣었고, 아래쪽 끝에서부터 밧줄을 장대 길이의 절반까지 따라 올려, 노끈을 겹겹이 꼬아 그렇게 단단히 동여맸다. 이 일이 끝나자 장대와 쇠와 밧줄은 — 마치 운명의 세 여신처럼 — 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 하나가 되었고, 에이해브는 그 무기를 들고 침울하게 성큼성큼 걸어갔다. 그의 상아 다리 소리와 히커리 장대 소리가 갑판의 판자마다 텅 빈 울림을 내며 함께 울려 퍼졌다. 그러나 그가 선실로 들어서기 전, 가볍고 부자연스러우며 반쯤은 빈정거리는 듯한, 그러면서도 더없이 가련한 소리가 들려왔다. 오, 핍(Pip)! 너의 비참한 웃음소리, 너의 멍하면서도 쉴 줄 모르는 눈빛. 너의 그 모든 기이한 광대놀음이, 우울한 배의 검은 비극과 의미 없지 않게 뒤섞여, 그것을 조롱하고 있었다!\n\n# CHAPTER 114. The Gilder.\n\n일본 어장의 한복판으로 점점 더 깊숙이 파고들면서, 피쿼드호(Pequod)는 이내 고래잡이로 온통 들끓었다. 온화하고 쾌청한 날씨면 흔히 열두 시간, 열다섯 시간, 열여덟 시간, 스무 시간을 내리 보트에 매달려, 꾸준히 노를 젓거나 돛을 올리거나 노로 물을 헤치며 고래를 뒤쫓았고, 때로는 예순 분이나 일흔 분쯤 막간을 두고 고래가 다시 떠오르기를 차분히 기다리기도 했다. 그러나 그 고생에 비하면 거둔 성과는 보잘것없었다.\n\n그런 때, 한풀 꺾인 햇살 아래 매끄럽고 느리게 너울지는 물결 위에 온종일 떠 있노라면, 자작나무 카누처럼 가벼운 보트에 앉아 부드러운 파도와 어찌나 다정하게 어우러지는지, 그 물결이 난롯가의 고양이처럼 뱃전에 몸을 비비며 가르랑거린다. 이런 때는 꿈결 같은 고요의 시간이어서, 바다 살갗의 잔잔한 아름다움과 광채를 바라보노라면 그 아래에서 헐떡이는 호랑이의 심장을 잊게 되고, 이 벨벳 같은 앞발이 무자비한 송곳니를 감추고 있을 뿐이라는 사실은 기꺼이 떠올리고 싶지 않게 된다.\n\n이런 때면 고래잡이 보트에 탄 떠돌이는 바다를 향해 어딘지 자식 같은, 미덥고 뭍 같은 정을 가만히 느끼게 되어, 바다를 한없이 꽃 핀 대지처럼 여기게 된다. 그리고 돛대 꼭대기만 빠끔히 드러낸 채 저 멀리 보이는 배는 높이 굽이치는 파도가 아니라, 일렁이는 대초원의 키 큰 풀밭을 헤치며 앞으로 나아가려 안간힘을 쓰는 듯이 보인다. 마치 서부로 가는 이주민의 말들이 곤두선 귀끝만 내민 채, 가려진 몸뚱이는 놀랍도록 짙푸른 풀밭 속을 널찍이 헤쳐 나아가듯이.\n\n길게 뻗은 처녀의 골짜기들, 부드러운 푸른 산비탈들. 이 위로 적막과 나직한 웅성거림이 살며시 깃들면, 어느 즐거운 오월에 숲의 꽃들이 꺾이는 무렵, 놀다 지친 아이들이 이 외딴 곳에 누워 잠들어 있다고 거의 단언하고 싶어진다. 그리고 이 모든 것이 그대의 가장 신비로운 심사와 뒤섞여, 사실과 공상이 중간에서 마주쳐 서로 스며들고, 이음매 없는 하나의 전체를 이룬다.\n\n그처럼 마음을 달래 주는 풍경은, 비록 한순간일망정, 에이해브(Ahab)에게도 적어도 그만큼 한순간의 영향을 끼치지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 이 은밀한 황금 열쇠들이 그의 내면에 숨겨진 황금 보물 곳간을 열어젖히는 듯했어도, 거기에 닿는 그의 숨결은 그것을 도리어 흐려 놓을 따름이었다.\n\n오, 풀 우거진 빈터여! 오, 영혼 속에 영원히 봄빛으로 끝없이 펼쳐진 풍경이여. 비록 흙먼지 같은 세상살이의 메마른 가뭄에 오래도록 타들어 갔을지언정, 그대들 속에서 사람들은 아직도, 새 아침 클로버 풀밭의 어린 말들처럼 뒹굴 수 있고, 덧없는 몇 순간이나마 그 몸 위에 영생의 서늘한 이슬을 느낄 수 있으리라. 이 축복받은 평온이 부디 영원하기를. 그러나 뒤섞인 채 뒤섞여 가는 인생의 실들은 날실과 씨실로 짜여 있다. 평온은 폭풍에 가로질리고, 평온 하나마다 폭풍 하나가 따른다. 이 인생에 되돌아가지 않는 한결같은 전진이란 없다. 우리는 정해진 단계를 따라 나아가다가 마지막에 한 번 멈추는 것이 아니다. 곧 유년의 무의식적 황홀, 소년기의 무심한 믿음, 청년기의 회의(만인의 공통된 운명), 그다음 회의주의, 그다음 불신을 거쳐, 마침내 장년기의 '만약(If)'이라는 사색의 안식에 이른다. 그러나 한번 그 길을 다 거치고 나면, 우리는 다시 그 둘레를 따라 돌아, 영원토록 갓난아이가 되고, 소년이 되고, 어른이 되고, '만약'이 된다. 우리가 다시는 닻을 풀지 않을 그 마지막 항구는 어디에 있는가? 가장 지친 자조차 결코 지칠 줄 모르는 그 세계는, 어떤 황홀한 천공을 항해하고 있는가? 버려진 아이의 아버지는 어디에 숨어 있는가? 우리의 영혼은 혼인하지 못한 어미가 그 아이를 낳다가 죽어 버린 저 고아들과 같으니, 우리 혈통의 비밀은 그 무덤 속에 누워 있고, 우리는 그것을 알기 위해 그곳으로 가야만 한다.\n\n그날도 또한, 스타벅(Starbuck)은 보트 뱃전 너머로 그 똑같은 황금빛 바다를 멀리 굽어보며 나직이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"헤아릴 수 없는 사랑스러움이여, 일찍이 어떤 연인이 젊은 신부의 눈동자에서 본 것에 못지않구나! 이빨을 층층이 박은 너의 상어들이며, 사람을 채 가는 식인의 행태일랑 내게 말하지 말아라. 믿음이 사실을 몰아내게 하라, 공상이 기억을 몰아내게 하라. 나는 깊숙이 내려다보며, 진실로 믿노라.\"\n\n그러자 스터브(Stubb)는 물고기처럼 반짝이는 비늘을 번뜩이며 그 똑같은 황금빛 속에서 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다.\n\n\"나는 스터브요, 스터브에게도 제 내력이 있소. 그러나 여기서 스터브는 맹세하노니, 자기는 언제나 유쾌한 사내였노라!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 115. The Pequod Meets The Bachelor.\n\n에이해브의 작살이 벼려진 지 몇 주 지났을 무렵, 바람을 타고 밀려오는 광경과 소리는 참으로 흥겨웠다.\n\n낸터킷(Nantucket)의 배 배철러(Bachelor)호로, 마지막 기름통을 꽉 들어차게 채워 넣고 터질 듯한 승강구를 단단히 닫아 건 참이었다. 이제 흥겨운 축제 차림을 한 채, 다소 우쭐대는 기색이 없지 않았으나 기쁨에 겨워, 고향으로 뱃머리를 돌리기에 앞서 어장 곳곳에 널리 흩어진 배들 사이를 누비며 항해하고 있었다.\n\n돛대 꼭대기의 세 사람은 모자에 좁고 붉은 깃발 천을 긴 띠처럼 늘어뜨리고 있었다. 고물에는 포경 보트 한 척이 바닥을 아래로 한 채 매달려 있었고, 제1사장(斜檣)에는 마지막으로 잡은 고래의 긴 아래턱이 포로처럼 걸려 있었다. 사방으로 온갖 빛깔의 신호기와 깃발과 잭기가 삭구마다 펄럭였다. 바구니 모양으로 짜인 세 개의 돛대 꼭대기 망대 옆구리마다 향유고래(Sperm) 기름통 두 개가 옆으로 묶여 있었고, 그 위 최상부 돛대의 가로대에는 같은 귀한 기름이 담긴 가느다란 작은 통들이 보였으며, 큰 돛대의 우듬지에는 놋쇠 등잔이 못으로 박혀 있었다.\n\n나중에 알게 된 일이지만, 배철러호는 더없이 놀라운 성공을 거두었다. 같은 바다를 항해하면서도 다른 수많은 배들이 단 한 마리도 잡지 못한 채 몇 달을 통째로 흘려보낸 터라, 그 성공은 한층 더 경이로웠다. 훨씬 값진 향유고래 기름을 둘 자리를 마련하느라 소금에 절인 쇠고기와 빵이 든 통들을 내주었을 뿐 아니라, 마주친 배들로부터 빈 통을 더 사들여 보충했으며, 그것들을 갑판을 따라, 또 선장과 항해사들의 선실에 차곡차곡 쟁여 두었다. 심지어 선실 식탁마저 쪼개어 불쏘시개로 만들었고, 선실에서 식사하는 이들은 바닥에 단단히 묶어 식탁 한가운데로 삼은 기름통의 넓은 뚜껑 위에서 끼니를 들었다. 선원들은 앞갑판에서 자기네 궤짝의 틈새를 메우고 역청을 발라 거기에까지 기름을 채워 넣었다. 우스갯소리로 덧붙이자면, 요리사는 가장 큰 솥에 뚜껑을 씌워 그것을 채웠고, 사환은 여분의 커피포트 주둥이를 막아 그것을 채웠으며, 작살잡이들은 작살의 자루 구멍에 마개를 끼워 그것을 채웠다고 했다. 사실 선장의 바지 주머니만 빼고는 무엇이든 향유고래 기름으로 가득 찼는데, 선장은 그 주머니만큼은 두 손을 찔러 넣을 자리로 남겨 두어, 자기만족에 찬 그 손길로 완전한 만족의 증거를 삼고 있었다.\n\n이 행운에 겨운 흥겨운 배가 침울한 피쿼드(Pequod)호를 향해 다가올 때, 그 앞갑판에서 거대한 북을 두드리는 야만스러운 소리가 울려왔다. 더 가까이 다가오니, 한 무리의 선원들이 거대한 정유 솥 둘레에 모여 선 모습이 보였다. 검은 고래의 양피지 같은 위장 가죽을 씌운 그 솥은, 선원들이 움켜쥔 주먹으로 칠 때마다 우렁찬 굉음을 토해 냈다. 뒷갑판에서는 항해사들과 작살잡이들이 폴리네시아 섬들에서 함께 달아나온 올리브빛 피부의 처녀들과 춤을 추고 있었고, 앞돛대와 큰 돛대 사이 높은 곳에 단단히 묶어 둔 화려하게 꾸민 보트 위에서는 롱아일랜드 출신 흑인 세 명이 고래 상아로 만든 번쩍이는 바이올린 활을 들고 그 떠들썩한 춤판을 이끌고 있었다. 그러는 동안 다른 선원들은 거대한 솥을 들어낸 정유로(精油爐)의 벽돌 구조물을 허무느라 요란하게 분주했다. 이제는 쓸모없어진 벽돌과 회반죽을 바다로 내던지며 그들이 어찌나 거친 함성을 질러 대던지, 마치 저주받은 바스티유 감옥이라도 허무는 줄 알았을 것이다.\n\n이 모든 광경을 다스리는 주인이자 지배자로서, 선장은 배의 높다란 뒷갑판 위에 꼿꼿이 서 있었다. 그리하여 이 환희에 찬 한 편의 극이 그의 눈앞에 고스란히 펼쳐졌고, 마치 오로지 그 한 사람의 흥을 돋우려 마련된 듯 보였다.\n\n그리고 에이해브(Ahab), 그 또한 자신의 뒷갑판에 서 있었다. 텁수룩하고 검은 모습으로, 완강한 음울함을 두른 채. 두 배가 서로의 항적을 가로질러 지나갈 때 — 한쪽은 지나간 일들로 온통 환희에 차 있었고, 다른 한쪽은 닥쳐올 일들로 온통 불길한 예감에 잠겨 있었으니 — 두 선장은 그 자신들로써 이 장면의 선명한 대비를 통째로 구현하고 있었다.\n\n\"올라오시오, 어서 올라오시오!\" 흥에 겨운 배철러호의 선장이 잔과 병을 허공에 치켜들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"흰 고래(White Whale)를 보았소?\" 에이해브가 이를 갈며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"아니오, 소문만 들었을 뿐이오. 게다가 그런 게 있다고는 도통 믿지도 않소.\" 상대가 쾌활하게 말했다. \"올라오시오!\"\n\n\"당신은 지나치게 흥겹구려. 가던 길이나 가시오. 사람을 잃은 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"입에 올릴 만큼은 아니오 — 섬사람 둘, 그게 다요. 하지만 어서 올라오시오, 이 정정한 양반, 어서 와요. 그 이마의 검은 그늘쯤 내 금세 걷어 드리리다. 자, 어서 오시구려 (한판 신나게 노는 게 좋잖소). 만선에다 고향길에 오른 배라오.\"\n\n\"어리석은 자란 어찌 이리도 친근한가!\" 에이해브가 중얼거리더니, 이윽고 소리 높여 말했다. \"당신은 만선에다 고향길에 오른 배라고 하는구려. 그렇다면 나를 일러 텅 빈 배에 바깥 바다로 나아가는 배라 부르시오. 그러니 당신은 당신 길을 가시오, 나는 내 길을 갈 테니. 앞쪽, 거기! 돛을 모두 펴고, 바람을 거슬러 배를 몰아라!\"\n\n그리하여 한 배가 산들바람을 받으며 흥겹게 앞으로 나아가는 동안, 다른 한 배는 완강하게 바람에 맞서 싸웠다. 그렇게 두 배는 갈라섰다. 피쿼드호의 선원들은 멀어져 가는 배철러호를 향해 무겁고도 좀처럼 거두지 못하는 눈길을 보냈으나, 배철러호의 선원들은 자기네 흥겨운 잔치판에 빠져 그 눈길에는 아랑곳도 하지 않았다. 에이해브는 고물 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 고향길에 오른 배를 바라보다가, 주머니에서 모래가 담긴 작은 유리병을 꺼냈다. 그러고는 배에서 유리병으로 번갈아 시선을 옮기니, 그로써 멀리 떨어진 두 가지 상념을 하나로 끌어모으는 듯했다. 그 유리병은 낸터킷 앞바다의 모래로 채워져 있었던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 116. The Dying Whale.\n\n이 세상을 살다 보면, 운명의 총아들이 우리 오른편 가까이를 스쳐 지나갈 때, 비록 그 전까지는 모든 것이 축 늘어져 있었더라도, 그 밀려드는 바람의 한 자락을 우리도 붙잡아, 처져 있던 돛이 부풀어 오르는 것을 기쁨에 차 느끼는 일이 적지 않다. 피쿼드(Pequod)호도 그러한 듯했다. 명랑한 배철러(Bachelor)호와 마주친 이튿날, 고래들이 나타나 네 마리가 잡혔고, 그중 한 마리는 에이해브(Ahab)가 잡았던 것이다.\n\n오후도 한참 기운 무렵이었다. 진홍빛 싸움의 작살질이 모두 끝나고, 사랑스러운 저녁놀이 진 바다와 하늘 위로 떠다니며, 해와 고래가 함께 고요히 숨을 거두었을 때, 그토록 달콤하고 그토록 애잔한 정취, 그토록 화환처럼 휘감기는 저녁 기도 소리가 그 장밋빛 대기 속에 피어올라, 마치 저 멀리 마닐라 군도(Manilla isles)의 짙푸른 수도원 골짜기로부터, 멋대로 뱃사람이 되어 버린 스페인의 육지 바람이 이 만과(晚課)의 찬송가들을 싣고 바다로 나선 듯이 보일 정도였다.\n\n다시 마음이 누그러졌으나, 그 누그러짐은 더 깊은 우수(憂愁)로 이어질 뿐이었다. 고래에게서 고물을 돌려 물러나 있던 에이해브는, 이제 잔잔해진 보트 위에서 고래의 마지막 스러짐을 골똘히 지켜보며 앉아 있었다. 죽어 가는 모든 향유고래(Sperm Whale)에게서 볼 수 있는 그 기묘한 광경—머리를 해 쪽으로 돌린 채 그렇게 숨을 거두는 그 기묘한 광경이, 이토록 잔잔한 저녁에 목격되자, 어쩐 일인지 에이해브에게는 일찍이 알지 못하던 경이로움을 전해 주었다.\n\n\"저놈은 해를 향해 몸을 돌리고 또 돌리는구나—얼마나 느리게, 그러나 얼마나 한결같이, 경배를 바치고 기원을 올리는 저 이마를, 죽어 가는 마지막 몸짓으로. 저놈 또한 불을 숭배하는구나. 더없이 충실하고, 드넓고, 영주(領主)에 버금가는 태양의 가신(家臣)이여!—오, 너무도 호의로운 이 눈이 너무도 호의로운 이 광경을 보게 되다니. 보라! 여기, 저 멀리 물에 갇힌 채, 인간의 안녕이나 비탄의 모든 소음에서 벗어나, 이 더없이 솔직하고 공평무사한 바다에서, 어떤 바위도 전설을 새길 석판을 내주지 않는 곳에서, 기나긴 중국의 세월 동안에도 물결이 여전히 말없이, 누구의 말도 듣지 못한 채 굽이쳐 왔던 곳에서, 마치 나이저(Niger)강의 알려지지 않은 발원지 위에 빛나는 별들처럼, 여기서도, 생명은 믿음으로 가득 차 해를 향해 죽어 가는구나. 그러나 보라! 죽자마자 곧, 죽음이 그 주검을 빙그르르 돌려놓아, 그것은 다른 어딘가로 머리를 돌리는구나.\n\n\"오, 자연의 저 어두운 힌두(Hindoo)의 반쪽이여, 물에 빠진 뼈들로 이 푸름 없는 바다의 한복판 어딘가에 너만의 옥좌를 세운 자여. 너는 이교도다, 여왕이여, 그리고 너는 드넓게 살육하는 태풍(Typhoon) 속에서, 또 그 뒤에 찾아드는 적막한 매장과도 같은 고요 속에서 내게 너무도 참되게 말하는구나. 또한 너의 이 고래가 죽어 가는 머리를 해 쪽으로 돌렸다가 다시 빙 돌아 버린 것도, 내게 한 가지 교훈을 남기지 않고서는 그냥 지나가지 않았다.\n\n\"오, 세 겹으로 둘러 매어진 권능의 허리여! 오, 드높이 솟구치는 무지갯빛 물줄기여!—저자는 안간힘을 다해 애쓰고, 이자는 물을 뿜어 올리나, 모두가 헛되도다! 헛되도다, 오 고래여, 너는 저 모든 것을 살아나게 하는 태양과의 중재를 구하지만, 그것은 생명을 불러낼 뿐 다시 돌려주지는 않는구나. 그러나 너는, 더 어두운 반쪽이여, 비록 더 어두운 믿음일지언정 더 자랑스러운 믿음으로 나를 흔들어 어른다. 이름 붙일 수 없는 너의 온갖 뒤섞임이 여기 내 발밑에 떠 있구나. 나는 한때 살아 있던 것들의 숨결에 떠받쳐지고 있으니, 공기로 내뿜어졌으나 이제는 물이 된 그 숨결에.\n\n\"그러니 만세를, 영원토록 만세를 부르노라, 오 바다여, 그 끝없는 일렁임 속에서 들새가 유일한 안식을 찾는 그대여. 대지에서 태어났으되 바다의 젖을 먹고 자랐으니, 비록 언덕과 골짜기가 나를 낳았을지언정, 너희 물결이야말로 나의 젖형제로구나!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 117. The Whale Watch.\n\n그날 저녁에 잡은 네 마리의 고래는 서로 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 죽어 있었다. 한 마리는 바람 불어오는 쪽으로 멀리, 한 마리는 그보다 가까운 바람 불어가는 쪽에, 한 마리는 앞쪽에, 한 마리는 뒤쪽에 있었다. 이 뒤의 세 마리는 해가 지기 전에 배 옆으로 끌어왔으나, 바람 불어오는 쪽의 한 마리는 아침이 되어서야 닿을 수 있었다. 그래서 그것을 잡은 보트는 밤새도록 그 곁에 머물러 있었으니, 그 보트가 바로 에이해브의 것이었다.\n\n표지 막대가 죽은 고래의 분수 구멍에 곧추세워졌고, 그 꼭대기에 매달린 등불이 검고 윤기 흐르는 등판 위로, 그리고 저 멀리 한밤의 물결 위로 어수선하게 일렁이는 불빛을 던졌다. 그 물결은 해변에 부드럽게 부서지는 파도처럼 고래의 넓은 옆구리를 가만히 어루만지고 있었다.\n\n에이해브와 그 보트의 선원들은 모두 잠든 듯했으나, 오직 파시인만은 깨어 있었다. 그는 뱃머리에 웅크리고 앉아, 고래 주위를 유령처럼 맴돌며 꼬리로 가벼운 삼나무 판자를 톡톡 두드리는 상어들을 지켜보았다. 용서받지 못한 고모라의 망령들이 아스팔티테스 호수 위로 떼 지어 신음하는 듯한 소리가, 몸서리치며 허공을 가로질러 흘렀다.\n\n선잠에서 깨어난 에이해브는 얼굴을 마주하고 파시인을 보았다. 밤의 어둠에 둥글게 에워싸인 그 둘은, 물에 잠긴 세상에 남은 마지막 두 사람처럼 보였다. \"또 그 꿈을 꾸었네.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"영구차 꿈 말입니까? 영감, 영구차도 관도 당신 것이 될 수 없다고 내 말하지 않았소?\"\n\n\"그러면 바다에서 죽는 자들은 누가 영구차에 실린단 말인가?\"\n\n\"하지만 영감, 내가 말하지 않았소. 당신이 이 항해에서 죽으려면, 그 전에 반드시 바다 위에서 두 대의 영구차를 보아야 한다고. 그 첫 번째는 인간의 손으로 만든 것이 아니며, 마지막 것의 눈에 보이는 나무는 아메리카에서 자란 것이어야 하오.\"\n\n\"그렇군, 그래! 그건 참으로 기이한 광경이겠구먼, 파시인. 영구차와 그 깃털 장식이 대양 위를 떠다니고, 물결이 관을 메는 사람 노릇을 한다니. 하! 그런 광경은 우리가 쉬이 보지 못할 것이네.\"\n\n\"믿든 안 믿든, 그것을 보기 전에는 당신은 죽을 수 없소, 영감.\"\n\n\"그러면 자네 자신에 관한 그 말은 무엇이었나?\"\n\n\"마지막 순간이 온다 해도, 나는 여전히 당신보다 앞서 당신의 길잡이로 가게 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"그러면 자네가 그렇게 먼저 가 버린 뒤에—만약 그런 일이 정말 일어난다면—내가 뒤따르기 전에, 자네는 여전히 내 앞에 나타나 나를 계속 인도해 주어야 한다는 말이지?—그렇지 않은가? 자, 그렇다면, 오 나의 길잡이여, 자네가 하는 말을 내가 모두 믿는다 해도! 나는 여기 두 가지 약속을 받았으니, 나는 끝내 모비 딕을 죽이고도 살아남으리라는 것일세.\"\n\n\"한 가지 약속을 더 받으시오, 영감.\" 파시인이 말했다. 그의 두 눈은 어둠 속에서 반딧불처럼 빛났다. \"오직 삼(麻)만이 당신을 죽일 수 있소.\"\n\n\"교수대를 두고 하는 말이로군.—그렇다면 나는 뭍에서나 바다에서나 죽지 않는 몸이라는 말 아닌가.\" 에이해브는 비웃는 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"뭍에서나 바다에서나 불멸이라!\"\n\n두 사람은 한 사람처럼 다시 침묵에 빠졌다. 잿빛 새벽이 밝아 오자, 잠들어 있던 선원들이 보트 바닥에서 일어났고, 정오가 되기 전에 죽은 고래는 배 옆으로 끌려왔다.\n\n# 제106장 에이해브의 다리\n\n적도 해역의 철이 마침내 가까워졌다. 그리고 에이해브가 선실에서 나와 눈을 위로 치켜뜰 때마다, 빈틈없는 키잡이는 보란 듯이 키 손잡이를 매만졌고, 잔뜩 들뜬 선원들은 재빨리 아딧줄로 달려가 못 박힌 더블룬 금화에 모든 눈길을 한가운데로 고정시킨 채 그 자리에 서서, 뱃머리를 적도로 향하라는 명령이 떨어지기만을 애타게 기다렸다. 때맞춰 명령이 내려졌다. 거의 정오에 가까운 무렵이었다. 에이해브는 높이 끌어올린 보트의 이물에 앉아, 위도를 가늠하려고 늘 하던 대로 태양 관측을 막 시작하려던 참이었다.\n\n그런데 그 일본 해역에서는 여름날들이 마치 눈부신 광채가 봇물처럼 터지는 것 같았다. 한 점 깜빡임도 없이 선명한 그 일본의 태양은 유리알 같은 대양을 헤아릴 수 없는 화경(火鏡)으로 삼아 그 한가운데로 모든 불길을 모은 듯했다. 하늘은 옻칠을 한 듯하고, 구름이라곤 한 점도 없으며, 수평선은 둥실 떠 있는 듯했다. 그리고 무엇 하나 가리는 것 없는 이 적나라한 광휘는 마치 견딜 수 없는 하느님 옥좌의 찬란함과도 같았다. 다행히 에이해브의 사분의(四分儀)에는 그 태양의 불꽃을 들여다볼 수 있도록 색유리가 끼워져 있었다. 그리하여 그는 배가 흔들리는 대로 앉은 몸을 함께 흔들면서, 점성술 도구처럼 보이는 그 기구를 눈에 갖다 댄 채, 태양이 정확히 자오선에 다다르는 바로 그 순간을 포착하려고 한동안 그 자세를 유지했다. 한편 그가 온 신경을 거기에 쏟고 있는 동안, 파시(Parsee) 교도는 그 아래 갑판에 무릎을 꿇고서 에이해브와 똑같이 얼굴을 위로 젖힌 채 그와 더불어 같은 태양을 바라보고 있었다. 다만 그의 눈은 눈꺼풀이 안구를 반쯤 덮고 있었고, 그 사나운 얼굴은 이 세상 것이 아닌 듯한 무념무상의 평정으로 가라앉아 있었다. 마침내 원하던 관측이 끝났다. 에이해브는 상아 다리에 연필을 대고서, 그 정확한 순간의 자기 위도가 얼마인지 곧 계산해 냈다. 그러고는 잠시 상념에 잠겼다가, 다시 태양을 올려다보며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"그대 바다의 길잡이여! 그대 높고도 권능 있는 도선사여! 그대는 내가 지금 어디에 있는지는 참되이 일러 주지만 — 내가 장차 어디에 있게 될지는 그 작은 실마리 하나라도 던져 줄 수 있는가? 아니면 나 말고 다른 무엇이 지금 이 순간 어디에 살아 있는지 말해 줄 수 있는가? 모비 딕(Moby Dick)은 어디 있는가? 지금 이 순간 그대는 틀림없이 그놈을 바라보고 있으리라. 내 이 두 눈은 지금 바로 그놈을 굽어보고 있는 그 눈을 들여다보고 있다. 그렇다, 그리고 지금 이 순간 그대 너머 알 수 없는 저편의 사물들을 똑같이 굽어보고 있는 그 눈을 들여다보고 있노라, 오 태양이여!\"\n\n그러고 나서 사분의를 응시하며, 그 수많은 신비로운 장치들을 하나하나 매만지다가, 그는 다시금 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 이렇게 중얼거렸다. \"어리석은 장난감! 거만한 제독들과 사령관들과 함장들의 갓난애 노리개로다. 세상은 그대를, 그대의 교묘함과 위력을 자랑한다. 하지만 결국 그대가 할 수 있는 일이라곤, 이 넓은 행성 위에서 그대 자신이 우연히 놓여 있는 위치와 그대를 쥔 손의 위치, 그 가련하고 보잘것없는 지점을 일러 주는 것뿐 아니던가. 아니다! 그 이상은 털끝만큼도 못한다! 그대는 물 한 방울이나 모래 한 알이 내일 정오에 어디에 있을지조차 말하지 못한다. 그러면서도 그 무력함으로 태양을 모욕하는구나! 과학이여! 그대를 저주하노라, 이 헛된 장난감아. 그리고 사람의 눈을 저 하늘로 치켜뜨게 만드는 모든 것에 저주가 있으라. 저 하늘의 생생한 광채는 다만 사람을 그슬릴 뿐이니, 지금 이 늙은 두 눈이 그대의 빛에 그슬리고 있는 것처럼 말이다, 오 태양이여! 본디 사람의 눈길은 이 땅의 수평선과 나란하도록 만들어진 것이지, 하느님이 사람더러 당신의 창공을 우러르라 하신 양 정수리에서 쏘아 올리도록 만들어진 것이 아니다. 그대를 저주하노라, 이 사분의야!\" 그는 그것을 갑판에 내동댕이쳤다. \"더는 그대에게 내 지상의 길을 맡기지 않으리라. 평평한 배의 나침반과, 측정기와 측정줄로 헤아리는 평평한 추측 항법, 바로 이것들이 나를 인도하여 바다 위 내 자리를 일러 주리라. 그렇다.\" 그는 보트에서 갑판으로 뛰어내렸다. \"이렇게 나는 그대를 짓밟노라, 저 높은 곳이나 가리키며 비실거리는 하찮은 물건아. 이렇게 나는 그대를 쪼개어 부숴 버리노라!\"\n\n광란에 빠진 늙은이가 이렇게 말하며 산 발과 죽은 발로 그것을 짓밟는 동안, 에이해브를 향한 듯한 비웃는 승리의 빛과, 자기 자신을 향한 듯한 숙명적 절망의 빛이 — 이 두 가지가 말없이 미동도 없는 파시 교도의 얼굴 위를 스쳐 지나갔다. 아무도 알아채지 못하는 사이에 그는 일어나 슬며시 빠져나갔다. 한편 자기네 지휘관의 모습에 경외감을 느낀 선원들은 앞갑판에 한데 모여 옹기종기 서 있었고, 그러다 에이해브가 어수선하게 갑판을 거닐며 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"아딧줄로! 키를 올려라! 활대를 가로로 돌려라!\"\n\n순식간에 활대들이 빙 돌아갔다. 그리고 배가 발꿈치를 축으로 반쯤 돌아설 때, 길고 갈빗대가 도드라진 선체 위로 든든히 자리 잡고 우아하게 곧추선 세 개의 돛대는 마치 한 마리의 충분한 말 위에서 발끝으로 빙그르르 도는 세 명의 호라티우스 형제처럼 보였다.\n\n스타벅은 나이트헤드 사이에 서서, 요동치며 나아가는 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 항로와, 갑판을 휘청휘청 걸어가는 에이해브의 모습을 함께 지켜보았다.\n\n\"나는 빽빽이 타오르는 석탄불 앞에 앉아, 괴로움에 몸부림치는 그 불꽃의 생명으로 가득 차 온통 시뻘겋게 달아오른 것을 바라본 적이 있다. 그리고 마침내 그것이 사위어 가는 것을, 점점, 점점 내려앉아 더없이 말 없는 재로 돌아가는 것을 보았다. 오 대양의 늙은이여! 그대의 이 모든 불같은 생명에서 끝내 남는 것이라곤 한 줌 작은 잿더미밖에 더 있겠는가!\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"허나 바다 석탄의 재라오 — 그걸 잊지 마시오, 스타벅 씨 — 바다 석탄이지, 흔해 빠진 숯이 아니란 말이오. 자, 자. 나는 에이해브가 이렇게 중얼거리는 걸 들었소. '여기 누군가가 이 늙은 내 손에 이 패를 쥐여 주는구나. 그러고는 내가 반드시 이 패로만 놀아야 하고, 다른 패는 안 된다고 맹세하게 하는구나.' 빌어먹을, 에이해브, 하지만 당신이 옳소. 노름판 속에서 살다가, 그 속에서 죽으시오!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 119. The Candles.\n\n가장 따뜻한 기후가 도리어 가장 잔인한 송곳니를 길러 낸다. 벵골의 호랑이는 끊임없이 푸르른 향료의 숲속에 웅크리고 있다. 가장 눈부신 하늘이 도리어 가장 치명적인 천둥을 품고 있다. 화려한 쿠바는 온순한 북쪽 땅을 결코 휩쓴 적 없는 토네이도를 안다. 그렇듯, 이 찬란한 일본 바다에서도 뱃사람은 모든 폭풍 가운데 가장 무서운 것, 곧 태풍(Typhoon)과 마주친다. 그것은 때로 구름 한 점 없는 하늘에서 갑자기 터져 나오니, 멍하니 잠든 마을 위로 떨어지는 폭탄과도 같다.\n\n그날 저녁 무렵, 피쿼드호(Pequod)는 돛이 갈가리 찢겨 나가, 맨 돛대만 남은 채로 정면에서 들이친 태풍과 맞서 싸워야 했다. 어둠이 내리자 하늘과 바다는 천둥과 더불어 으르렁대며 갈라졌고, 번개로 환히 타올랐으니, 그 빛은 처음 폭풍의 광기가 뒷놀이를 위해 남겨 둔 누더기 조각들을 이리저리 펄럭이는 무력해진 돛대들을 비추어 보여 주었다.\n\n스타벅(Starbuck)은 삭구 줄 하나를 붙든 채 뒷갑판에 서 있었다. 번개가 번쩍일 때마다 위쪽을 올려다보며, 저 뒤엉킨 돛대 장치에 또 어떤 재난이 닥쳤는지를 살폈다. 그동안 스터브(Stubb)와 플래스크(Flask)는 선원들을 지휘하여 보트를 더 높이 끌어 올리고 더 단단히 잡아매도록 했다. 그러나 그들의 온갖 수고도 헛된 듯 보였다. 기중대 맨 꼭대기까지 들어 올렸음에도, 바람받이 쪽 뒷보트(에이해브(Ahab)의 것)는 화를 면치 못했다. 거대한 너울 하나가 비틀거리는 배의 높이 흔들리는 옆구리를 향해 높이 솟구쳐 부딪히더니, 그 보트의 고물 쪽 바닥을 부수어 놓고는 다시 물러갔다. 보트는 체처럼 온통 물을 줄줄 흘리고 있었다.\n\n\"고약한 일이군, 고약한 일이야! 스타벅 씨.\" 스터브가 부서진 보트를 바라보며 말했다. \"허나 바다는 제멋대로 굴기 마련이지요. 적어도 이 스터브는 그것과 싸울 수가 없습니다. 보세요, 스타벅 씨, 파도란 놈은 솟구치기 전에 어마어마하게 긴 도움닫기를 합니다. 온 세상을 한 바퀴 돌고 나서야 비로소 솟구쳐 오르거든요! 하지만 저로 말하자면, 그놈을 맞으러 갈 도움닫기라곤 고작 이 갑판을 가로지르는 게 전부랍니다. 하지만 괘념치 마세요. 다 장난삼아 하는 소립니다. 옛 노래에도 그리 나오잖습니까.\" (노래한다.)\n\n오! 흥겨운 폭풍, 익살꾼 같은 고래, 제 꼬리를 신나게 흔들어 대네, 그렇게나 우습고, 까불대고, 짓궂고, 익살맞고, 농지거리하고, 알쏭달쏭한 녀석, 그게 바로 대양이라네, 오!\n\n흩날리는 물보라, 그건 그저 거품 이는 그놈의 술일 뿐, 양념을 휘저어 넣을 때면, 그렇게나 우습고, 까불대고, 짓궂고, 익살맞고, 농지거리하고, 알쏭달쏭한 녀석, 그게 바로 대양이라네, 오!\n\n천둥이 배를 쪼개도, 그놈은 그저 입맛만 다실 뿐, 이 술을 한 모금 맛보면서, 그렇게나 우습고, 까불대고, 짓궂고, 익살맞고, 농지거리하고, 알쏭달쏭한 녀석, 그게 바로 대양이라네, 오!\n\n\"그만하게, 스터브.\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"태풍더러 노래하게 내버려 두게나. 우리 삭구 위에서 제 하프를 켜라고 말이야. 허나 자네가 용감한 사내라면, 입을 다물고 있을 걸세.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 용감한 사내가 아닙니다. 제가 용감한 사내라고 말한 적도 없고요. 저는 겁쟁이입니다. 그래서 기운을 차리려고 노래하는 겁니다. 그리고 말씀드리지요, 스타벅 씨, 이 세상에서 제 노래를 멈추게 할 방법은 제 목을 베는 것 말고는 없습니다. 그리고 그 일이 끝나고 나면, 십중팔구 저는 마무리로 당신께 영광송을 불러 드릴 겁니다.\"\n\n\"미친 사람 같으니! 네게 네 눈이 없거든, 내 눈을 통해 보아라.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 어떻게 당신은 캄캄한 밤에 남보다 더 잘 본단 말입니까, 그게 아무리 어리석은 소리라 해도요?\"\n\n\"여길 보게!\" 스타벅이 스터브의 어깨를 움켜쥐고 바람받이 뱃머리 쪽으로 손을 가리키며 외쳤다. \"저 폭풍이 동쪽에서 불어오는 게 보이지 않나? 바로 에이해브가 모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 찾아 달려가려는 그 항로 말일세. 바로 그가 오늘 정오에 뱃머리를 돌린 그 항로란 말이야. 자, 저기 그의 보트를 보게. 어디가 부서졌나? 고물 쪽 자리일세, 이 사람아. 그가 늘 서 있곤 하던 자리, 그가 발 딛고 서던 바로 그 자리가 부서졌단 말일세, 이 사람아! 자, 이제 바다로 뛰어들어, 정 그래야겠거든 실컷 노래나 부르게!\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 도통 알아듣지 못하겠군요. 무슨 바람이 부는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 희망봉을 돌아가는 것이 낸터킷(Nantucket)으로 가는 가장 빠른 길이지.\" 스타벅이 스터브의 물음에는 아랑곳없이 불쑥 혼잣말을 했다. \"지금 우리를 부수려고 두들겨 대는 저 폭풍을, 우리는 우리를 집으로 데려다줄 순풍으로 바꿀 수 있어. 저쪽, 바람받이 편은 온통 파멸의 암흑이지만, 바람그늘 편, 곧 고향 쪽은… 저기가 환히 밝아 오는 게 보여. 그러나 번개로 밝아 오는 것은 아니지.\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 번쩍임에 뒤따른 깊은 어둠의 막간 가운데 하나에서 그의 곁에서 어떤 목소리가 들려왔다. 그리고 거의 같은 찰나에 천둥소리 한 무더기가 머리 위에서 우르릉 굴러갔다.\n\n\"거기 누구냐?\"\n\n\"늙은 천둥이오!\" 에이해브가 뱃전을 따라 더듬더듬 제 회전축 구멍 쪽으로 길을 찾아가며 말했다. 그러나 문득 팔꿈치처럼 굽이치는 불의 창들이 그의 길을 환히 밝혀 주는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n자, 뭍에 선 첨탑의 피뢰침이 그 위험한 전류를 흙 속으로 흘려보내려는 것이듯, 바다에서 어떤 배들이 돛대마다 달아 두는 그 비슷한 막대도 그것을 물속으로 끌어 내리려는 것이다. 그러나 이 도체는 그 끝이 선체와 닿는 일이 없도록 상당한 깊이까지 내려가야 하고, 더구나 늘 거기 매달린 채 끌려다니게 되면 여러 사고를 당하기 쉬울뿐더러, 삭구 일부를 적잖이 방해하고 배가 물속을 나아가는 길을 다소간 가로막게 된다. 이런 까닭에 배의 피뢰침 아랫부분은 늘 물속에 잠겨 있지는 않으며, 대개는 길고 가느다란 고리 사슬로 만들어, 필요에 따라 바깥쪽 사슬걸이 위로 더 손쉽게 끌어 올리거나 바다 속으로 던져 내릴 수 있도록 되어 있다.\n\n\"피뢰 사슬을! 피뢰 사슬을!\" 스타벅이 선원들을 향해 외쳤다. 방금 에이해브의 길을 비추려 횃불처럼 번쩍이던 그 생생한 번개에 갑자기 경계심이 일깨워진 것이다. \"그것들이 물속에 내려져 있느냐? 던져 내려라, 이물 쪽도 고물 쪽도. 어서!\"\n\n\"멈춰라!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"우리가 비록 약한 쪽이라 하나, 여기서는 정정당당하게 겨루도록 하자. 그래도 나는 온 세상이 안전해지도록 히말라야와 안데스에 피뢰침 세우는 일에 기꺼이 한몫 거들겠노라. 허나 특혜 따위는 집어치워라! 그것들을 그냥 내버려 두게, 스타벅.\"\n\n\"위를 보십시오!\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"세인트엘모의 불이다! 세인트엘모의 불이야!\"\n\n활대 끝마다 창백한 불이 매달려 있었다. 그리고 세 갈래로 갈라진 피뢰침 끝마다 가늘게 솟구치는 세 줄기 흰 불꽃이 닿아 있어, 세 개의 높은 돛대는 저마다 그 유황 냄새 자욱한 대기 속에서 소리 없이 타오르고 있었으니, 마치 제단 앞에 세워진 세 개의 거대한 밀랍 초와도 같았다.\n\n\"보트는 될 대로 되라지! 내버려 둬!\" 바로 이 순간 스터브가 외쳤다. 사납게 출렁이는 너울이 그 자신의 작은 보트 밑에서 솟구쳐 올라, 그가 잡아매던 줄을 넘기는 사이에 보트 뱃전이 그의 손을 거세게 짓눌렀던 것이다. \"빌어먹을 것!\" 그러나 갑판 위로 미끄러져 뒤로 넘어지면서, 치켜뜬 그의 눈이 그 불꽃들을 사로잡았다. 그러자 곧장 어조를 바꾸어 그가 외쳤다. \"세인트엘모의 불이여, 우리 모두에게 자비를 베푸소서!\"\n\n뱃사람들에게 욕설이란 일상의 말이다. 그들은 잔잔한 황홀경 속에서도 욕을 하고, 폭풍의 이빨 앞에서도 욕을 한다. 끓어오르는 바다 위로 가장 아슬아슬하게 흔들릴 때면 윗돛 활대 끝에서 저주를 퍼붓기도 한다. 그러나 내 모든 항해를 통틀어, 하느님의 불타는 손가락이 배 위에 얹혔을 때, 곧 그분의 \"메네 메네 데겔 우바르신\"이 삭구와 밧줄에 새겨졌을 때만큼은, 흔해 빠진 욕설 한마디조차 좀처럼 들어 본 적이 없다.\n\n이 창백한 불빛이 위쪽에서 타오르는 동안, 마법에 걸린 선원들의 입에서는 거의 아무 말도 새어 나오지 않았다. 그들은 앞갑판 위에 한데 빽빽이 모여 서서, 그 모든 눈이 저 희미한 인광 속에서 빛나고 있었으니, 마치 아득히 먼 별자리와도 같았다. 그 으스스한 빛을 배경으로, 칠흑같이 검은 거구의 흑인 대구(Daggoo)는 본래 키의 세 곱절로 우뚝 솟아올라, 천둥이 쏟아져 나온 검은 구름처럼 보였다. 태시테고(Tashtego)의 벌어진 입은 상어처럼 새하얀 이빨을 드러냈는데, 그 이빨마저 세인트엘모의 불이 끝에 닿은 듯 기이하게 번득였다. 그동안 그 초자연적인 빛을 받은 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 문신은 그의 몸 위에서 사탄의 푸른 불꽃처럼 타올랐다.\n\n이 광경은 마침내 위쪽의 창백한 불빛과 더불어 모두 스러져 갔다. 그리하여 피쿼드호와 갑판 위의 모든 영혼은 다시 한 번 검은 장막에 휩싸였다. 한두 순간이 지났을 때, 스타벅이 앞으로 나아가다가 누군가와 부딪쳤다. 스터브였다. \"이제 어떻게 생각하나, 이 사람아. 자네 외치는 소리를 들었네. 노래 속의 그것과는 달랐어.\"\n\n\"아니지요, 아닙니다, 그건 달랐습니다. 저는 세인트엘모의 불이 우리 모두에게 자비를 베풀어 달라고 했지요. 그리고 지금도 여전히 그들이 그래 주기를 바랍니다. 하지만 그들은 그저 시무룩한 얼굴에만 자비를 베푸는 걸까요? 웃음을 위해서는 도무지 인정이 없는 걸까요? 자, 보세요, 스타벅 씨… 하지만 너무 어두워서 보이질 않는군요. 그럼 들어 보세요. 저는 우리가 본 저 돛대 꼭대기의 불꽃을 행운의 징조로 받아들입니다. 저 돛대들은 곧 향유고래 기름으로 가득 들어찰 선창에 뿌리내리고 있으니까요. 아시겠어요? 그러니 그 모든 향유 기름이 나무의 수액처럼 돛대 위로 빨려 올라갈 겁니다. 그래요, 우리 세 돛대는 머지않아 향유고래 기름 양초 세 자루가 될 겁니다. 그게 바로 우리가 본 좋은 약속입니다.\"\n\n바로 그 순간 스타벅은 스터브의 얼굴이 서서히 어슴푸레 드러나기 시작하는 것을 알아차렸다. 위쪽을 흘긋 올려다보며 그가 외쳤다. \"보게! 저것 좀 보게!\" 그러자 다시 한 번, 높이 가늘게 솟구친 불꽃들이 그 창백함 속에서 갑절로 짙어진 듯한 초자연성을 띤 채 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"세인트엘모의 불이여, 우리 모두에게 자비를 베푸소서.\" 스터브가 다시 외쳤다.\n\n큰돛대 밑동, 곧 금화와 불꽃 바로 아래에서 파르시인(Parsee)이 에이해브 앞에 무릎 꿇고 있었는데, 그 머리는 그에게서 돌려 숙이고 있었다. 한편 그 가까이, 활처럼 휘어 머리 위로 드리운 삭구에서는, 방금 활대 하나를 붙들어 매던 일을 하다가 그 섬광에 멈춰 선 여러 선원들이, 이제 한데 엉겨 붙어 매달려 있었으니, 마치 축 늘어진 과수원 가지에 마비된 채 한 덩어리로 붙은 말벌 떼와도 같았다. 헤르쿨라네움의 서 있거나 발 내딛거나 달리는 해골들처럼, 갖가지 마법에 걸린 자세로 다른 이들은 갑판에 못 박힌 듯 굳어 있었다. 그러나 그들의 눈은 모두 위를 향하고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 제군들!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"저것을 올려다보라. 똑똑히 새겨 두라. 저 흰 불꽃은 다름 아닌 흰 고래(White Whale)에게로 가는 길을 밝혀 주는 것이다! 저기 저 큰돛대 사슬을 내게 다오. 나는 이 맥박을 느껴 보고 싶구나. 그리하여 내 맥박이 그것에 맞서 뛰게 하리라. 피로써 불에 맞서리라! 자, 이렇게.\"\n\n그러고는 몸을 돌려, 마지막 사슬 고리를 왼손에 단단히 쥐고서, 그는 한쪽 발을 파르시인 위에 얹었다. 그리고 위를 향해 눈을 고정한 채, 오른팔을 높이 치켜들고서, 그는 우뚝 솟아 세 갈래로 갈라진 불꽃의 삼위일체 앞에 꼿꼿이 섰다.\n\n\"오! 그대 맑은 불의 맑은 정령이여, 일찍이 나는 이 바다에서 페르시아인으로서 그대를 숭배했더랬다. 그러다 그 성스러운 예식 가운데 그대에게 너무도 심하게 데어, 이 시각까지도 그 흉터를 지니고 있노라. 이제 나는 그대를 안다, 그대 맑은 정령이여. 그리고 이제 나는 그대를 향한 올바른 숭배가 곧 저항임을 안다. 사랑에도 경배에도 그대는 친절하지 않으리라. 그리고 증오에 대해서조차 그대가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 죽이는 것뿐, 그리하여 모두가 죽임을 당한다. 이제 두려움 모르는 그 어떤 바보도 그대 앞에 맞서지 않는다. 나는 그대의 말 없고 자리 없는 권능을 인정한다. 그러나 내 지진 같은 목숨의 마지막 숨이 다할 때까지, 나는 내 안에서 그것이 무조건적이고 완전무결한 지배권을 휘두르는 데 맞서 다투리라. 인격화된 비인격의 한가운데, 여기 하나의 인격이 서 있다. 비록 기껏해야 한낱 점에 지나지 않을지라도, 내가 어디서 왔든, 어디로 가든, 그래도 내가 이 땅에 살아 있는 한, 그 여왕다운 인격이 내 안에 살아 그 왕다운 권리를 느낀다. 그러나 전쟁은 고통이요, 증오는 비애로다. 그대의 가장 낮은 사랑의 모습으로 오라, 그러면 나는 무릎 꿇고 그대에게 입 맞추리라. 그러나 그대의 가장 높은 모습으로, 한낱 천상의 권능으로서 오거든, 그대가 가득 실은 세계들로 이루어진 함대를 띄워 보낸다 한들, 여기 이 안에는 여전히 무심한 채로 남아 있는 무언가가 있노라. 오, 그대 맑은 정령이여, 그대의 불로 그대는 나를 빚었으니, 불의 참된 자식답게, 나는 그것을 그대에게 도로 내뿜노라.\"\n\n[갑작스럽게, 거듭하여 번개가 번쩍인다. 아홉 줄기 불꽃이 길게 솟구쳐 이전 높이의 세 곱절에 이른다. 에이해브는 다른 이들과 함께 눈을 감고, 오른손으로 그 위를 힘껏 누른다.]\n\n\"나는 그대의 말 없고 자리 없는 권능을 인정한다. 내가 그리 말하지 않았던가? 허나 그것은 내게서 억지로 짜낸 말이 아니었다. 그리고 나는 지금 이 사슬을 떨어뜨리지 않는다. 그대는 눈멀게 할 수 있다. 허나 그러면 나는 더듬어 나아가리라. 그대는 태워 없앨 수 있다. 허나 그러면 나는 한 줌 재가 되리라. 이 가련한 두 눈과, 덧창처럼 닫힌 두 손의 경배를 받아라. 나라면 그것을 받지 않으련만. 번개가 내 두개골을 꿰뚫고 번쩍인다. 내 두 눈알이 쑤시고 또 쑤신다. 두들겨 맞은 내 머릿골 전체가 마치 목 잘린 듯, 어떤 아찔한 바닥 위를 데굴데굴 구르는 듯하다. 오, 오! 그래도 눈을 가린 채로, 그래도 나는 그대에게 말하리라. 그대 빛일지언정, 그대는 어둠에서 솟구쳐 나온다. 그러나 나는 빛에서 솟구쳐 나오는 어둠, 그대에게서 솟구쳐 나오는 어둠이다! 창들이 멎는다. 두 눈을 떠라. 보이느냐, 보이지 않느냐? 저기 불꽃이 타오른다! 오, 그대 도량 넓은 이여! 이제 나는 내 혈통을 자랑스러워하노라. 그러나 그대는 한낱 내 불타는 아비일 뿐, 내 다정한 어미를 나는 알지 못한다. 오, 잔인한 이여! 그대는 그녀를 어찌하였는가? 거기에 내 수수께끼가 있다. 허나 그대의 수수께끼가 더 크도다. 그대는 자신이 어찌 생겨났는지 알지 못하니, 그리하여 자신을 낳아진 적 없는 자라 부른다. 분명코 자신의 시작을 알지 못하니, 그리하여 자신을 시작된 적 없는 자라 부른다. 나는 나 자신에 관하여 그대가 그대 자신에 관해 알지 못하는 무언가를 안다, 오, 그대 전능한 이여. 그대 너머에는 스며들지 않는 어떤 것이 있으니, 그대 맑은 정령이여, 그것에게는 그대의 영원함도 한낱 시간에 지나지 않고, 그대의 모든 창조력도 한낱 기계적인 것에 지나지 않는다. 그대를 통하여, 그대의 불타는 자아를 통하여, 내 그을린 두 눈이 그것을 어렴풋이 보노라. 오, 그대 버려진 불꽃이여, 그대 태곳적의 은둔자여, 그대 또한 그대만의 전할 수 없는 수수께끼를, 함께 나눌 수 없는 슬픔을 지니고 있구나. 여기 다시금 오만한 고뇌로써, 나는 내 아비를 읽어 낸다. 솟구쳐라! 솟구쳐 올라, 하늘을 핥아라! 나는 그대와 함께 솟구친다. 나는 그대와 함께 타오른다. 나는 기꺼이 그대와 하나로 녹아 붙고 싶다. 저항하며 나는 그대를 숭배하노라!\"\n\n\"보트! 보트를 보십시오!\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"당신 보트를 보세요, 영감님!\"\n\n퍼스(Perth)의 불에서 벼려진 에이해브의 작살은 그 눈에 띄는 거치대에 단단히 잡아매인 채, 그의 고래잡이 보트 뱃머리 너머로 삐죽 튀어나와 있었다. 그러나 그 보트 바닥을 부수어 놓은 바다가 헐거워진 가죽 칼집을 떨어져 나가게 했고, 이제 그 날카로운 강철 미늘에서 창백하게 갈라진 불의 일직선 화염이 뻗어 나오고 있었다. 그 말 없는 작살이 마치 뱀의 혀처럼 거기서 타오르자, 스타벅이 에이해브의 팔을 움켜잡았다. \"하느님, 하느님이 당신께 맞서고 계십니다, 영감님. 그만두세요! 이건 흉한 항해입니다! 흉하게 시작되어, 흉하게 이어지고 있어요. 아직 그럴 수 있을 때 제가 활대를 바로잡게 해 주세요, 영감님. 그리하여 이 폭풍을 고향으로 향하는 순풍으로 삼아, 이보다 더 나은 항해에 나서게 해 주세요.\"\n\n스타벅의 말을 엿들은 공포에 사로잡힌 선원들은 즉시 아딧줄로 달려갔다. 위에는 돛 한 폭 남아 있지 않았는데도 말이다. 그 순간 경악한 일등항해사의 모든 생각이 곧 그들의 생각이 된 듯했다. 그들은 반쯤 반란을 일으킬 듯한 함성을 질렀다. 그러나 절그렁대는 번개의 사슬을 갑판에 내동댕이치고, 타오르는 작살을 와락 움켜쥔 에이해브는 그것을 그들 사이로 횃불처럼 휘둘렀다. 그러면서 밧줄 한 가닥이라도 풀어 놓는 첫 번째 선원은 그것으로 꿰뚫어 버리겠노라 으름장을 놓았다. 그의 모습에 돌처럼 굳어 버린 데다, 그가 든 그 불타는 화살에 더더욱 움츠러든 선원들은 겁에 질려 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 에이해브는 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"흰 고래를 사냥하겠다는 너희의 모든 맹세는 내 맹세만큼이나 구속력을 지닌다. 그리고 심장도, 영혼도, 육신도, 폐도, 목숨도, 이 늙은 에이해브는 그 맹세에 묶여 있다. 그리고 이 심장이 어떤 가락에 맞추어 뛰는지 너희가 알 수 있도록, 여기를 보라. 나는 이렇듯 마지막 두려움을 불어 꺼 버리노라!\" 그러고는 단숨에 입김을 한 번 불어 그 불꽃을 꺼 버렸다.\n\n벌판을 휩쓰는 태풍 속에서 사람들이 어느 외따로 선 거대한 느릅나무 곁을 피해 달아나듯이 — 바로 그 높이와 힘이 도리어 그 나무를 그만큼 더 위태롭게 만드니, 그만큼 더 벼락의 표적이 되기 때문이다 — 그렇듯 에이해브의 그 마지막 말이 떨어지자 많은 뱃사람들이 경악의 공포에 사로잡혀 그에게서 달아났다.\n\n에이해브가 키 옆에 서 있다. 스타벅이 그에게 다가온다.\n\n\"큰 윗돛 활대를 내려야 합니다, 선장님. 결박이 헐거워지고 있고 바람 아래쪽 받침줄이 반쯤 풀렸습니다. 내릴까요, 선장님?\"\n\n\"아무것도 내리지 마라. 단단히 동여매라. 내게 하늘돛 장대가 있다면 지금이라도 끌어 올렸을 것이다.\"\n\n\"선장님!—제발 하느님의 이름으로!—선장님?\"\n\n\"왜 그러느냐.\"\n\"닻이 흔들리고 있습니다, 선장님. 안으로 끌어들일까요?\"\n\n\"아무것도 내리지 말고, 아무것도 건드리지 마라, 다만 모두 단단히 동여매라. 바람이 거세지고는 있으나, 아직 내 고원 지대까지는 올라오지 못했다. 어서, 가서 처리하라.—돛대와 용골을 두고 맹세하건대! 이자는 나를 무슨 연안을 떠도는 작은 배의 곱사등이 선장쯤으로 보는 모양이구나. 내 큰 윗돛 활대를 내리라니! 어이, 풀단지 같은 자들아! 가장 높은 돛머리는 가장 사나운 바람을 위해 만들어진 것이며, 지금 내 이 머릿속 돛머리는 흩날리는 구름 사이를 항해하고 있다. 그것을 내리라고? 오, 폭풍이 몰아칠 때 제 머릿속 돛머리를 내리는 자는 겁쟁이뿐이다. 저 높은 곳에서 웬 야단법석이란 말이냐! 산통(疝痛)이 요란한 병이라는 것을 내가 모른다면, 저 소리를 숭고하다 여겼을 것이다. 오, 약을 먹어라, 약을 먹어!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 121. Midnight.—The Forecastle Bulwarks.\n\n“아니야, 스터브(Stubb). 거기 그 매듭이야 네 마음껏 두들겨도 좋다만, 방금 네가 한 말만큼은 내 머릿속에 절대 못 박아 넣을걸. 그리고 네가 정반대 소리를 한 게 얼마나 됐다고? 언젠가 네가 이런 말을 안 했나? 에이해브(Ahab)가 어느 배를 타든, 그 배는 보험증권에 웃돈을 좀 더 물어야 한다고, 마치 고물에는 화약통을 싣고 이물에는 성냥갑을 잔뜩 실은 배인 것처럼 말이야. 자, 잠깐만, 네가 그렇게 말하지 않았어?”\n\n“그래, 내가 그랬다고 치자. 그게 뭐 어때서? 그때 이후로 내 살점도 일부는 갈렸는데, 마음이라고 못 바꿀까? 게다가 우리가 정말 고물에 화약통을 싣고 이물에 성냥을 실었다 친들, 이렇게 흠뻑 쏟아지는 물보라 속에서 도대체 그 성냥에 어떻게 불이 붙겠어? 이봐, 꼬마 친구, 자네 머리칼이 제법 빨갛긴 해도, 지금은 거기에 불을 붙일 수가 없을걸. 몸 좀 털어 봐. 자넨 물병자리, 곧 물을 나르는 자야, 플라스크(Flask). 자네 외투 깃에서 물주전자라도 채울 수 있겠어. 그러니 모르겠나, 이런 추가 위험에 대해서는 해상보험회사들이 추가 보증을 받는다는 걸? 여기 소화전이 있다 이거지, 플라스크. 하지만 다시 들어 봐, 그 다른 문제에 대해 답해 줄 테니. 다만 우선 그 다리부터 여기 닻머리에서 치워 봐, 그래야 밧줄을 넘길 수 있으니까. 자, 들어 봐. 폭풍 속에서 돛대의 피뢰침을 붙잡고 있는 것과, 폭풍 속에서 피뢰침이라곤 아예 없는 돛대 바로 옆에 서 있는 것 사이에 무슨 대단한 차이가 있겠어? 모르겠나, 이 멍청아, 돛대가 먼저 벼락을 맞지 않는 한 피뢰침 잡은 사람에게는 아무 해도 없다는 걸? 그럼 자넨 도대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거야? 백 척 중에 한 척도 피뢰침을 달고 다니지 않아, 그러니 에이해브도, 그래, 이 친구야, 우리 모두도, 내 변변찮은 생각으로는 지금 바다를 항해하는 만 척의 배에 탄 모든 선원들보다 조금도 더 위험하지 않았던 거야. 이봐, 이 킹포스트 같은 친구야, 자네는 세상 모든 사람이 민병대 장교의 꼬챙이 꽂은 깃털처럼 모자 모서리에 작은 피뢰침을 세우고, 그 띠처럼 뒤로 질질 끌고 다니길 바라는 모양이지? 좀 분별 있게 굴지그래, 플라스크? 분별 있게 구는 거야 쉬운 일이잖아. 그런데 왜 안 그래? 반쪽 눈만 달린 사람도 분별은 차릴 수 있는데.”\n\n“그건 모르겠는데, 스터브. 자네도 더러는 그게 꽤 어렵다고 느끼잖아.”\n\n“그래, 사람이 흠뻑 젖었을 땐 분별 차리기가 어렵지, 그건 사실이야. 그리고 난 이 물보라에 거의 다 젖어 가는 중이고. 신경 쓰지 마. 거기 한 바퀴 돌려서 넘겨. 내가 보기엔 지금 우리가 이 닻들을 다시는 안 쓸 것처럼 묶어 두고 있는 것 같단 말이야. 여기 이 두 닻을 묶는 게, 플라스크, 사람 손을 등 뒤로 결박하는 것 같단 말이지. 그것도 참 크고 후한 손들이긴 하지만 말이야. 이게 자네의 쇠주먹들인가, 응? 게다가 어찌나 단단히 붙드는지! 궁금하군, 플라스크, 이 세상은 대체 어디엔가 닻을 내리고 있는 걸까. 만약 그렇다면 세상은 유난히 긴 밧줄을 늘어뜨린 채 흔들리고 있는 셈이지. 자, 그 매듭을 두들겨 박아라, 그럼 끝이다. 됐어. 뭍을 밟는 것 다음으로는 갑판에 내려서는 게 제일 흡족하단 말이야. 이봐, 내 웃옷 자락 좀 짜 주겠나? 고맙네. 사람들이 긴 옷자락을 그렇게들 비웃지, 플라스크. 하지만 내가 보기엔, 바다 위 온갖 폭풍 속에서는 늘 긴 꼬리 외투를 입어야 마땅하단 말이야. 그렇게 끝으로 갈수록 가늘어지는 자락이 물을 흘려보내 주거든, 알겠어? 삼각모도 마찬가지지. 그 뾰족한 부분이 박공 처마의 빗물받이 노릇을 한다고, 플라스크. 나는 이제 짧은 작업복 재킷이니 방수포 외투니 하는 건 안 입을 테야. 제비꼬리 연미복을 걸치고, 비버 가죽 모자를 눌러써야겠어, 이렇게. 어이쿠! 휘이! 저기 내 방수포 모자가 바다로 날아가네. 맙소사, 맙소사, 하늘에서 불어오는 바람이 이렇게 무례할 수가 있나! 고약한 밤이야, 이 친구야.”\n\n# CHAPTER 122. Midnight Aloft.—Thunder and Lightning.\n\n『큰 윗돛 활대.—타슈테고(Tashtego)가 새 밧줄을 그 둘레에 감으며.』\n\n“음, 음, 음. 저놈의 천둥 좀 멈춰라! 이 위는 천둥이 너무 많아. 천둥이 무슨 소용이람? 음, 음, 음. 우리는 천둥 따위 바라지 않아. 우리가 바라는 건 럼주야. 럼주 한 잔 다오. 음, 음, 음!”\n\n태풍이 가장 사납게 몰아칠 때, 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 턱뼈 키손잡이를 잡고 선 사람은 그 발작 같은 움직임 탓에 몇 번이나 비틀거리며 갑판으로 내동댕이쳐졌다. 보조 도르래를 키손잡이에 매달아 두긴 했으나 — 줄을 느슨하게 풀어 두었기 때문이었다 — 키손잡이에는 어느 정도의 여유가 반드시 필요했던 것이다.\n\n이처럼 사나운 강풍 속에서, 배가 돌풍에 이리저리 내던져지는 한낱 셔틀콕 신세로 전락할 때면, 나침반의 바늘이 이따금 빙글빙글 도는 광경을 보는 일은 결코 드물지 않다. 피쿼드호의 나침반도 그러했다. 거의 매번 충격이 올 때마다 키잡이는 바늘이 나침반 눈금 위에서 빙빙 도는 그 어지러운 속도를 놓치지 않고 보았다. 그것은 어지간한 사람이라면 어떤 낯선 감정에 사로잡히지 않고는 도저히 바라볼 수 없는 광경이다.\n\n자정이 지나 몇 시간 뒤, 태풍이 어지간히 잦아들자, 스타벅(Starbuck)과 스터브(Stubb)의 — 한 사람은 앞쪽에서, 다른 한 사람은 뒤쪽에서 — 안간힘 어린 노고를 통해, 갈가리 찢긴 삼각돛과 앞돛 그리고 큰 윗돛의 너덜너덜한 잔해가 활대에서 잘려 나가, 폭풍에 시달리던 앨버트로스가 날갯짓을 할 때 더러 바람에 흩날려 떨어지는 그 새의 깃털처럼, 소용돌이치며 바람 부는 아래쪽으로 날아가 버렸다.\n\n이제 거기에 상응하는 새 돛 세 개가 활대에 매달리고 폭을 줄여졌으며, 폭풍용 보조 돛 하나가 더 뒤쪽에 펼쳐졌다. 그리하여 배는 곧 다시금 어느 정도 정확하게 물살을 헤치고 나아갔다. 그리고 그가 잡아야 할 항로 — 당분간은 동남동쪽 — 가, 가능하다면, 다시 한번 키잡이에게 주어졌다. 강풍이 사납게 몰아치는 동안 그는 오직 그 변덕에 맞추어 키를 잡았을 뿐이었기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 그가 나침반을 살피면서 배를 가능한 한 항로 가까이로 끌어가고 있을 때, 보라! 좋은 징조였다! 바람이 고물 쪽으로 돌아오는 듯했다. 그렇다, 고약하던 바람이 순풍으로 바뀐 것이다!\n\n곧바로 활대들이 가로로 반듯하게 맞춰졌으니, “호! 순풍이다! 어이여어, 기운차게, 친구들아!” 하는 흥겨운 노랫소리와 함께였다. 그토록 길조로운 사건이 앞서 닥쳤던 흉조를 이리도 빨리 거짓으로 만들어 준 것이 기뻐, 선원들이 환호하며 노래했다.\n\n언제든, 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 어느 때든, 갑판 일에 뚜렷한 변화가 생기면 즉시 보고하라는 선장의 상시 명령에 따라, 스타벅은 — 비록 마지못해, 또 침울한 심정으로나마 — 활대를 바람에 맞추어 손질해 놓자마자, 기계적으로 아래층으로 내려가 에이해브(Ahab) 선장에게 그 사정을 알리려 했다.\n\n그의 선실 문을 두드리기에 앞서, 그는 자기도 모르게 문 앞에서 잠시 걸음을 멈추었다. 선실 등불은 — 이쪽저쪽으로 길게 흔들리며 — 일렁이듯 타올라, 노인의 빗장 걸린 문 위로 일렁이는 그림자를 드리우고 있었다. 그 문은 얇은 것으로, 위쪽 판자 대신 붙박이 발이 끼워져 있었다. 선실은 외따로 떨어진 지하 동굴 같은 곳이어서, 비록 사방이 천지의 온갖 포효로 에워싸여 있었음에도, 그 안에는 어떤 웅웅대는 정적이 감돌고 있었다. 장전된 머스킷총들이 앞쪽 격벽에 기대어 곧추선 채로 번들거리며 모습을 드러냈다. 스타벅은 정직하고 올곧은 사람이었다. 그러나 그 머스킷총을 본 바로 그 순간, 스타벅의 가슴속에서 이상하게도 사악한 생각 하나가 피어올랐다. 다만 그것은 무심하거나 선한 다른 생각들과 너무도 뒤섞여 있어, 그 찰나에 그는 그것이 무엇인지 좀처럼 알아채지 못했다.\n\n“저 사람은 언젠가 나를 쏘려 했지.” 그가 중얼거렸다. “그래, 바로 저게 그가 내게 겨누었던 그 머스킷총이야. 개머리판에 징이 박힌 저것. 한번 만져 보자 — 들어 보자. 이상하군, 그토록 많은 살벌한 작살을 다뤄 온 내가, 이상하게도, 지금 이렇게 떨고 있다니. 장전됐나? 봐야겠어. 그래, 그래, 약실에 화약도 들어 있군. 좋지 않은 일이야. 쏟아 버리는 게 나을까? 잠깐. 이런 마음일랑 내 스스로 고쳐 보리라. 생각하는 동안 이 총을 대담하게 쥐고 있어야지. 나는 그에게 순풍을 알리러 온 거다. 한데 무슨 순풍이지? 죽음과 파멸을 향한 순풍, 그것이야말로 모비 딕(Moby Dick)에게 순풍이지. 저 저주받은 물고기에게만 순한 바람, 그게 순풍이라니. 그가 내게 겨누었던 바로 그 총신! 바로 그것, 이것 — 내가 여기 쥐고 있는 이것. 그는 지금 내가 손에 든 바로 이것으로 나를 죽이려 했었지. 그래, 그는 제 선원들 모두를 기꺼이 죽이려 들 거야. 그는 어떤 강풍에도 돛대를 내리지 않겠다고 말하지 않았던가? 그 천상의 사분의를 내동댕이치지 않았던가? 게다가 이 위험천만한 바다에서, 오류투성이 측정기의 추측 항법에만 의지해 길을 더듬고 있지 않은가? 또 바로 이 태풍 속에서, 피뢰침 따위는 두지 않겠다고 맹세하지 않았던가? 한데 이 미쳐 버린 노인이 배 한 척의 선원 전부를 제 파멸 속으로 함께 끌고 들어가도록 고분고분 내버려 두어야 한단 말인가? 그래, 만일 이 배가 어떤 치명적인 화를 입게 된다면, 그는 서른 명 넘는 사람들을 고의로 죽인 살인자가 되는 셈이다. 그리고 에이해브가 제멋대로 한다면 이 배가 치명적인 화를 입으리라는 것을, 내 영혼이 맹세컨대 그러하리라. 그렇다면, 만약 그가 바로 이 순간에 — 치워진다면, 그 죄는 그의 것이 되지 않을 텐데. 하! 그가 잠결에 중얼거리고 있나? 그래, 바로 저기, 저 안에서, 그는 잠들어 있다. 잠들어 있다고? 그래, 하지만 여전히 살아 있고, 곧 다시 깨어나겠지. 그렇다면 나는 당신을 당해 낼 수 없소, 노인이여. 이치도, 간언도, 애원도 당신은 귀담아듣지 않을 테고, 그 모든 것을 당신은 비웃을 따름이지. 당신 자신의 단호한 명령에 대한 무조건의 복종, 당신이 숨 쉬듯 바라는 건 오직 그것뿐. 그래, 당신은 사람들이 당신의 맹세를 함께 맹세했다 하고, 우리 모두가 에이해브라 말하지. 위대한 하느님, 그런 일이 없게 하소서! 한데 다른 길은 없는가? 합법적인 길은? 그를 포로로 잡아 고향까지 끌고 갈까? 무어라! 이 노인의 살아 있는 권능을 그의 살아 있는 두 손에서 비틀어 빼앗기를 바란다고? 오직 바보만이 그런 짓을 해 보려 들 테지. 그를 결박했다 치자. 온몸을 밧줄과 닻줄로 칭칭 묶고, 이 선실 바닥의 고리 볼트에 사슬로 붙들어 맸다 치자. 그러면 그는 우리에 갇힌 호랑이보다 더 끔찍해질 거다. 나는 그 꼴을 견뎌 낼 수 없고, 그 울부짖음에서 결코 달아날 수도 없을 거다. 그 참기 어려운 긴 항해 동안 온갖 위안도, 잠 그 자체도, 더없이 값진 이성마저도 나를 떠나 버리리라. 그렇다면, 무엇이 남는가? 육지는 수백 리그나 떨어져 있고, 가장 가까운 곳이라곤 굳게 잠긴 일본뿐이다. 나는 여기 망망대해 위에 홀로 서 있고, 나와 법 사이에는 두 대양과 대륙 하나가 가로놓여 있다. 그래, 그래, 그렇고말고. 하늘이 침대에 누운 살인 미수자에게 벼락을 내리쳐 그 시트와 살갗을 함께 불쏘시개로 만든다면, 하늘은 살인자인가? 그렇다면 나는, 만약—” 그러고는 천천히, 살그머니, 곁눈질로 반쯤 비스듬히 바라보면서, 그는 장전된 머스킷총의 끝을 문에 갖다 댔다.\n\n“이 높이쯤이면, 에이해브의 해먹이 저 안에서 흔들리고 있겠지. 머리가 이쪽을 향하고 있을 테고. 한 번만 건드리면, 스타벅은 살아남아 아내와 자식을 다시 품에 안을 수 있으리라. 오 메리! 메리! 아들아! 아들아! 아들아! 하지만 노인이여, 내가 당신을 죽음으로 깨우지 않는다면, 이 주가 다 가기 전에 스타벅의 몸뚱이가 모든 선원과 함께 그 어떤 가늠할 길 없는 심연으로 가라앉을지, 그 누가 알 수 있겠는가! 위대한 하느님, 당신은 어디 계십니까? 할까? 내가 할까? 바람이 잦아들고 방향을 바꿨습니다, 선장님. 앞돛과 큰 윗돛은 폭을 줄여 펼쳤고, 배는 제 항로로 나아가고 있습니다.”\n\n“전부 후진하라! 오 모비 딕, 내 마침내 네 심장을 움켜쥐었노라!”\n\n이런 소리들이 이제 노인의 괴로움에 시달리는 잠 속에서 튀어나와 부딪쳐 왔으니, 마치 스타벅의 목소리가 그 오래도록 입 다물고 있던 꿈을 말하게 만들기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n여전히 겨눠진 머스킷총이 술 취한 자의 팔처럼 문판자에 대고 떨렸다. 스타벅은 천사와 씨름하는 듯했다. 그러나 문에서 돌아서더니, 그는 그 죽음의 총신을 총가에 도로 꽂아 두고 그곳을 떠났다.\n\n“저분은 너무 깊이 잠드셨네, 스터브 씨. 자네가 내려가서 깨워 드리고 알려 드리게. 나는 여기 갑판을 살펴야 하니. 무슨 말을 해야 할지는 자네가 알 테지.”\n\n# CHAPTER 124. The Needle.\n\n이튿날 아침, 아직 잦아들지 않은 바다가 거대한 부피의 길고 느린 큰 물결로 굽이쳐 와, 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 콸콸거리는 항적 속에서 안간힘을 쓰며, 마치 거인들의 펼친 손바닥처럼 배를 앞으로 밀어 주었다. 강하고 흔들림 없는 바람이 어찌나 가득했던지, 하늘과 대기 전체가 거대하게 부풀어 오른 돛처럼 보였고, 온 세상이 바람결에 둥둥 울리며 나아갔다. 환한 아침 빛에 감싸여 보이지 않는 태양은, 다만 그 자리에 퍼진 강렬한 밝기로써만 그 위치를 알 수 있었으니, 그곳에서는 총검 같은 햇살이 다발을 이루어 뻗어 나가고 있었다. 왕관을 쓴 바빌론의 왕들과 왕비들의 문장(紋章)처럼, 휘황한 빛깔이 만물 위에 군림했다. 바다는 빛과 열을 머금고 부글거리며 솟구치는 녹은 황금의 도가니와도 같았다.\n\n오랫동안 마법에 걸린 듯한 침묵을 지키며, 에이해브(Ahab)는 홀로 떨어져 서 있었다. 그리고 배가 시소처럼 흔들리며 뱃머리 사장(斜檣)을 무겁게 아래로 처박을 때마다, 그는 몸을 돌려 앞쪽으로 뻗어 나가는 밝은 햇살을 바라보았고, 배가 고물 쪽으로 깊이 가라앉을 때면, 뒤로 돌아 태양의 뒤편 자리를 바라보며, 똑같은 노란 햇살이 한 치도 어긋남 없는 항적과 어우러지는 모습을 지켜보았다.\n\n\"하, 하, 내 배여! 너는 지금 태양의 바다 전차(戰車)라 불려도 손색이 없겠구나. 호, 호! 내 뱃머리 앞의 온 나라들이여, 내가 너희에게 태양을 가져다주노라! 저 먼 물결에 멍에를 씌워라. 어이! 두 마리 말을 앞뒤로 이어 매듯, 내가 바다를 몰고 가노라!\"\n\n그러나 어떤 반대되는 생각에 문득 고삐를 당긴 듯, 그는 키 쪽으로 서둘러 다가가, 쉰 목소리로 배가 어느 방향으로 가고 있는지를 다그쳐 물었다.\n\n\"동남동, 동쪽에서 남쪽으로 치우친 방향입니다, 선장님.\" 겁먹은 키잡이가 대답했다.\n\n\"거짓말 마라!\" 그는 움켜쥔 주먹으로 키잡이를 후려쳤다. \"이 아침 시간에 동쪽으로 가는데, 태양이 고물 뒤에 있다고?\"\n\n이 말에 모든 이의 넋이 빠져나간 듯했다. 방금 에이해브가 알아챈 그 현상이 어찌 된 영문인지 다른 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 바로 그 눈멀 만큼 또렷한 명백함이야말로 그 까닭이었으리라.\n\n머리를 나침반 함(函) 속에 절반쯤 들이밀고, 에이해브는 나침반들을 흘끗 보았다. 그러자 치켜들었던 그의 팔이 천천히 떨어졌고, 한순간 그는 거의 비틀거리는 듯 보였다. 그의 뒤에 서 있던 스타벅(Starbuck)이 들여다보니, 아니나 다를까! 두 나침반은 모두 동쪽을 가리키고 있었건만, 피쿼드호는 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 서쪽으로 나아가고 있었다.\n\n그러나 첫 번째 거센 경악이 선원들 사이로 퍼져 나가기도 전에, 노인은 굳은 웃음을 지으며 외쳤다. \"알았다! 전에도 이런 일이 있었다. 스타벅, 간밤의 천둥이 우리 나침반을 돌려놓은 것뿐이다. 자네도 지금껏 이런 일을 들어 본 적이 있을 테지.\"\n\n\"예. 하지만 제게는 한 번도 이런 일이 일어난 적이 없었습니다, 선장님.\" 창백한 일등 항해사가 침울하게 말했다.\n\n여기서 한마디 해 두어야 할 것은, 이와 같은 사고가 한두 번이 아니라 여러 차례, 거센 폭풍 속의 배들에게 일어났다는 사실이다. 뱃사람의 나침반 바늘에서 발현되는 자기(磁氣) 에너지는, 누구나 알다시피 하늘에서 보이는 전기와 본질적으로 하나다. 그러니 이런 일이 일어난다 한들 그리 놀랄 것은 못 된다. 번개가 실제로 배를 내리쳐 활대와 삭구 일부를 부러뜨린 경우에는, 바늘에 미친 영향이 때로 한층 더 치명적이었다. 그 자석으로서의 효력이 송두리째 사라져, 전에는 자성을 띠었던 강철이 늙은 아낙의 뜨개바늘만큼도 쓸모없어진 것이다. 그러나 어느 경우든, 바늘은 그렇게 망가지거나 잃어버린 본래의 효력을 스스로의 힘으로 다시는 되찾지 못한다. 그리고 나침반 함의 나침반들이 영향을 받으면, 똑같은 운명이 배 안에 있을 법한 다른 모든 나침반에까지 미친다. 설령 가장 밑바닥, 용골판(龍骨板) 속에 박혀 있는 것이라 할지라도 그러하다.\n\n나침반 함 앞에 신중하게 서서, 방향이 뒤바뀐 나침반들을 가만히 살피며, 노인은 이제 쭉 뻗은 손의 날을 세워 태양의 정확한 방위를 가늠하더니, 바늘들이 정확히 거꾸로 되어 있음을 확인하고는, 그에 따라 배의 침로를 바꾸라는 명령을 큰 소리로 외쳤다. 활대를 한껏 끌어올렸고, 피쿼드호는 또다시 그 굴함 없는 뱃머리를 맞바람 속으로 들이밀었다. 순풍인 줄 알았던 그 바람이 그저 배를 농락하고 있었을 따름이었으니.\n\n그동안 스타벅은 제 속내가 무엇이든 한마디도 하지 않은 채, 조용히 필요한 명령을 모두 내렸다. 한편 스텁(Stubb)과 플래스크(Flask)는, 그때만큼은 어느 정도 그의 심정을 함께 나누는 듯 보였는데, 마찬가지로 군말 없이 따랐다. 선원들로 말하자면, 몇몇은 낮게 웅성거리기도 했으나, 에이해브에 대한 두려움이 운명에 대한 두려움보다 더 컸다. 그러나 늘 그래 왔듯, 이교도 작살잡이들은 거의 아무런 동요도 보이지 않았다. 설령 동요했다 한들, 그것은 다만 굽힐 줄 모르는 에이해브의 마음에서 그들의 기질에 맞는 가슴속으로 쏘아 들어온 어떤 자력(磁力) 때문이었을 뿐이다.\n\n한동안 노인은 굽이치는 상념에 잠겨 갑판을 거닐었다. 그러다 우연히 상아 의족 뒤축이 미끄러졌을 때, 그는 전날 갑판에 내동댕이쳐 박살 내 버린 사분의(四分儀)의 으스러진 구리 조준관을 보았다.\n\n\"가련하고도 오만하게 하늘을 우러르던 자여, 태양의 안내자여! 어제는 내가 너를 부숴 버렸고, 오늘은 나침반들이 나를 부숴 버리려 들었구나. 그래, 그래. 하지만 에이해브는 아직 저 평평한 자석의 주인이다. 스타벅, 자루 없는 창 한 자루, 윗활대 메, 그리고 돛 만드는 바늘 가운데 가장 작은 것을 가져오게. 어서!\"\n\n지금 그가 막 하려는 일을 부추긴 충동에는, 아마도 어떤 신중한 동기들이 곁들여 있었으리라. 그 동기의 목적인즉, 뒤집힌 나침반처럼 그토록 기묘한 사태를 두고 자신의 정묘한 솜씨를 한 번 펼쳐 보임으로써 선원들의 사기를 되살리려는 것이었을 터다. 게다가 노인은, 방향이 뒤바뀐 바늘로 키를 잡는 일이 비록 서투르게나마 가능하긴 해도, 미신에 사로잡힌 뱃사람들에게는 어떤 전율과 불길한 징조 없이 그냥 넘어갈 만한 일이 아님을 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n\"제군들.\" 일등 항해사가 그가 요구한 물건들을 건네주자, 그는 침착하게 선원들을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"제군들, 천둥이 늙은 에이해브의 바늘들을 돌려놓았다. 하지만 이 강철 토막 하나로 에이해브는 자기만의 바늘을 만들 수 있다. 그것은 그 어떤 바늘 못지않게 정확히 가리킬 것이다.\"\n\n이 말이 떨어지자, 선원들 사이에는 비굴할 만큼 놀라워하면서도 무안해하는 눈길이 오갔다. 그리고 그들은 홀린 듯한 눈으로, 뒤이어 펼쳐질 어떤 마법이든 기다렸다. 그러나 스타벅은 시선을 돌렸다.\n\n에이해브는 윗활대 메를 한 번 내리쳐 창에서 강철 머리를 떼어 내고는, 남은 긴 쇠막대를 일등 항해사에게 건네며 갑판에 닿지 않게 똑바로 세워 잡으라고 일렀다. 그러고 나서 메로 이 쇠막대 윗부분을 거듭 두들긴 뒤, 무뎌진 바늘을 그 꼭대기에 세로로 세워 놓고, 일등 항해사가 여전히 막대를 잡고 있는 채로, 그것을 좀 더 약하게 여러 차례 망치질했다. 그런 다음 그것을 들고 작고 기이한 동작 몇 가지를 했는데, 그것이 강철에 자성을 띠게 하는 데 꼭 필요한 것이었는지, 아니면 그저 선원들의 경외감을 부추기려는 것이었는지는 알 수 없는 일이었다. 이윽고 그는 무명실을 가져오라 하더니, 나침반 함으로 다가가 그 안에 거꾸로 들어 있던 두 바늘을 빼내고, 돛바늘을 그 한가운데에 실을 매어 나침반 눈금판 하나 위에 수평으로 매달았다. 처음에는 강철 바늘이 양 끝을 떨고 진동하며 빙글빙글 돌았으나, 마침내 제자리에 멈추어 섰다. 이 결과를 골똘히 지켜보고 있던 에이해브는, 그러자 거리낌 없이 나침반 함에서 한 걸음 물러서더니, 뻗은 팔로 그것을 가리키며 외쳤다. \"직접들 보아라, 에이해브가 저 평평한 자석의 주인이 아닌지! 태양은 동쪽에 있는데, 저 나침반이 그렇다고 맹세하고 있지 않느냐!\"\n\n하나씩 차례로 그들은 안을 들여다보았다. 그들처럼 무지한 자들을 설득할 수 있는 것은 오직 제 눈뿐이었기 때문이다. 그리고 하나씩 차례로 그들은 슬그머니 물러갔다.\n\n경멸과 승리로 이글거리는 그 두 눈 속에서, 그때 그대들은 그 모든 치명적인 자긍심에 휩싸인 에이해브의 모습을 보았으리라.\n\n# CHAPTER 125. The Log and Line.\n\n운명 지어진 피쿼드(Pequod)호가 이번 항해에서 그토록 오래 바다 위를 떠다니는 동안, 측정줄과 측정기는 좀처럼 쓰인 적이 없었다. 배의 위치를 정하는 다른 수단을 자신만만하게 의지한 탓에, 일부 상선들과 많은 포경선들은, 특히 순항할 때면 측정기를 던지는 일을 아예 소홀히 한다. 그러면서도 동시에, 다른 무엇보다 형식을 갖추기 위한 경우가 많지만, 늘 쓰던 석판 위에다 배가 잡은 항로와 매시간 추정한 평균 진행 속도를 규칙적으로 적어 두기는 한다. 피쿼드호의 사정도 그러했다. 나무로 된 얼레와 거기 매달린 모난 측정기는 오래도록 손길이 닿지 않은 채 고물 쪽 뱃전 난간 바로 아래에 걸려 있었다. 비와 물보라가 그것을 적셨고, 햇볕과 바람이 그것을 뒤틀어 놓았으며, 그렇게 한가로이 매달려 있던 물건을 썩히려고 온갖 자연의 요소들이 한데 힘을 모았다. 그러나 이 모든 것에 아랑곳없이, 자석 소동이 있은 지 몇 시간 지나지 않아 우연히 얼레로 눈길이 갔을 때, 에이해브(Ahab)는 어떤 기분에 사로잡혔고, 자신의 사분의(四分儀)가 이제 없다는 사실을 떠올렸으며, 측정기와 측정줄에 대해 미친 듯이 내뱉었던 맹세를 기억해 냈다. 배는 곤두박질치듯 항해하고 있었고, 고물 뒤로는 파도가 사납게 굽이쳤다.\n\n\"이봐, 거기 앞쪽! 측정기를 던져라!\"\n\n선원 둘이 왔다. 황금빛 살결의 타히티 사람과 백발이 성성한 맨섬 사람이었다.\n\"너희 중 하나가 얼레를 잡아라. 던지는 건 내가 하겠다.\"\n\n그들은 배의 바람 아래쪽, 고물의 맨 끝으로 갔는데, 그곳은 바람의 비스듬한 기세에 밀려 갑판이 이제 거의 옆으로 우유처럼 들이닥치는 바다에 잠길 듯했다.\n\n맨섬 사람이 얼레를 잡고서, 줄을 감은 실패가 돌아가는 굴대의 양쪽 튀어나온 손잡이 끝을 쥐어 높이 치켜들고, 모난 측정기가 아래로 늘어진 채로 그렇게 서서 에이해브가 다가오기를 기다렸다.\n\n에이해브가 그 앞에 서서, 바다로 던지기 전에 미리 한 묶음을 손에 사리려고 서른 바퀴인지 마흔 바퀴인지를 가볍게 풀어내고 있는데, 그와 줄을 골똘히 살펴보던 늙은 맨섬 사람이 용기를 내어 입을 열었다.\n\n\"선장님, 영 미덥지 못합니다. 이 줄은 너무 삭아 보입니다. 오랜 열기와 습기가 못 쓰게 만들어 놓았습니다.\"\n\n\"버텨 줄 걸세, 노인장. 오랜 열기와 습기가 자네는 못 쓰게 만들었던가? 자네는 그래도 버티는 것 같은데. 아니, 어쩌면 더 맞는 말로는, 삶이 자네를 붙들고 있는 거겠지. 자네가 삶을 붙들고 있는 게 아니라.\"\n\n\"저는 실패를 붙들고 있습니다요, 선장님. 하지만 그저 선장님 말씀대로 하지요. 이 허연 머리칼을 이고서 따져 봐야 무슨 소용입니까. 더구나 결코 잘못을 인정하지 않을 윗사람과는 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소린가? 여기 자연 여왕(Queen Nature)의 화강암으로 다져진 대학에 헝겊 기운 교수 하나가 있구나. 그런데 보아하니 지나치게 굽실거리는군. 자넨 어디서 태어났는가?\"\n\n\"작고 바위투성이인 맨섬에서 태어났습니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"훌륭하군! 그 한마디로 자네는 세상의 정곡을 찔렀어.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다만, 저는 거기서 태어났습니다.\"\n\n\"맨섬(Isle of Man)이라고? 음, 거꾸로 보면 그것 참 그럴듯하군. 여기 맨(Man, 사람)에서 온 사람(man)이 있다. 한때 독립국이던 맨에서 태어났으나, 이제는 맨에게서 사내다움(man)을 빼앗긴 사람이라. 그게 무엇한테 빨려 들어간 건가? 얼레를 들어 올려라! 죽어 있는 눈먼 벽은 결국 캐묻는 모든 머리통을 들이받아 버리지. 들어 올려라! 그래.\"\n\n측정기가 던져졌다. 느슨하게 사렸던 줄이 고물 뒤로 길게 끌리며 빠르게 펴졌고, 그러자 곧바로 얼레가 빙글빙글 돌기 시작했다. 굽이치는 파도에 따라 측정기가 끌어당기는 저항이 들쭉날쭉 오르내리면서, 늙은 얼레잡이는 이상하게 비틀거렸다.\n\n\"꽉 붙들어라!\"\n\n탁! 지나치게 팽팽해진 줄이 한 가닥 긴 꽃 장식처럼 축 늘어졌다. 끌려가던 측정기는 사라지고 없었다.\n\n\"내가 사분의를 박살내고, 천둥이 바늘을 뒤집어 놓더니, 이제는 미쳐 날뛰는 바다가 측정줄을 끊어 놓는구나. 그러나 에이해브는 무엇이든 고칠 수 있다. 여기 줄을 끌어들여라, 타히티 사람아. 얼레를 감아라, 맨섬 사람아. 그리고 잘 들어라, 목수더러 측정기를 새로 만들게 하고, 자네는 줄을 손보아라. 잊지 말고.\"\n\n\"저것 보게, 또 가 버리는군. 저 양반한테는 아무 일도 일어나지 않은 게야. 그런데 나한테는 세상 한복판에 박힌 꼬챙이가 헐거워져 빠지는 것만 같으니. 끌어들여라, 끌어들여, 타히티 사람아! 이 줄들은 멀쩡하게 풀려 빙글빙글 돌아 나가더니만, 끊어진 채 느릿느릿 끌려 들어오는구나. 하, 핍(Pip)이냐? 도우러 왔구나, 응, 핍?\"\n\n\"핍? 누구를 핍이라 부르는 거냐? 핍은 포경 보트에서 뛰어내렸다. 핍은 행방불명이야. 어디 보자, 자네가 여기서 그 녀석을 낚아 올린 건 아닌지, 어부 양반. 묵직하게 끌리는군. 아무래도 그 녀석이 매달려 있는 모양이야. 흔들어 떼어 내라, 타히티! 흔들어 떨어뜨려. 여기서는 겁쟁이를 끌어올리지 않는다. 호! 저기 그 녀석 팔이 막 물 위로 솟아오르는군. 손도끼다! 손도끼! 줄을 잘라 버려라 — 여기서는 겁쟁이를 끌어올리지 않으니까. 에이해브 선장! 선장님, 선장님! 여기 핍이 다시 배에 오르려고 하고 있습니다!\"\n\n\"닥쳐라, 이 미친 멍청이야.\" 맨섬 사람이 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 소리쳤다. \"후갑판에서 썩 물러가!\"\n\n\"더 큰 천치가 늘 더 작은 천치를 꾸짖는 법이지.\" 에이해브가 다가서며 중얼거렸다. \"그 신성한 것에서 손을 떼라! 얘야, 핍이 어디 있다고 했느냐?\"\n\n\"저기 고물 쪽이요, 선장님, 고물 쪽! 보세요! 보세요!\"\n\n\"그런데 얘야, 너는 누구냐? 네 눈의 텅 빈 동공 속에 내 모습이 비치지 않는구나. 오 하느님(God)! 사람이 불멸하는 영혼들이 그것을 통해 걸러져야 할 체 같은 것이라니! 너는 누구냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"종지기 소년입니다, 선장님. 배의 외치는 자이지요. 딩, 동, 딩! 핍! 핍! 핍! 핍을 찾는 자에게 진흙 백 파운드 상금! 키는 다섯 자 — 겁쟁이처럼 생겼으니 — 그걸로 단박에 알아본다지! 딩, 동, 딩! 겁쟁이 핍을 본 사람 없소?\"\n\n\"만년설 경계선 위에는 심장이 있을 수 없다. 오, 너희 얼어붙은 하늘이여! 여기 아래를 굽어보아라. 너희가 이 불운한 아이를 낳아 놓고는 버렸구나, 너희 창조의 난봉꾼들아. 이리 오너라, 얘야. 에이해브가 사는 동안, 앞으로 에이해브의 선실이 핍의 집이 될 것이다. 너는 내 가장 깊은 중심을 건드리는구나, 얘야. 너는 내 심장의 현으로 짜인 끈으로 나와 묶여 있다. 자, 내려가자.\"\n\n\"이게 뭐예요? 여기 보드라운 상어 가죽이 있네요.\" 그는 에이해브의 손을 골똘히 들여다보며 만져 보았다. \"아, 이제, 가엾은 핍이 이토록 다정한 것을 한 번이라도 느껴 보았더라면, 어쩌면 영영 잃어버리지 않았을 텐데! 이건 제게는 사람을 붙드는 밧줄 같아 보입니다, 선장님. 약한 영혼들이 붙잡을 수 있는 무언가 말이에요. 오, 선장님, 늙은 퍼스(Perth)를 불러다가 이 두 손을 단단히 박아 붙이게 해 주세요. 검은 손을 흰 손에다 말이에요. 저는 이걸 결코 놓지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 얘야, 나도 너를 놓지 않으마. 그렇게 함으로써 내가 너를 여기보다 더 끔찍한 공포 속으로 끌고 들어가는 게 아니라면 말이다. 그러니 이리 오너라, 내 선실로. 보라! 신들은 온통 선하고 사람은 온통 악하다고 믿는 너희여, 보라! 전지(全知)한 신들이 고통받는 인간을 잊고 있는 것을 보라. 그리고 인간은, 비록 천치이고 제가 무엇을 하는지조차 모르나, 그래도 사랑과 감사의 달콤한 것들로 가득 차 있는 것을 보라. 자! 황제의 손을 잡았을 때보다, 너의 검은 손을 잡고 이끄는 지금이 나는 더 자랑스럽구나!\"\n\n\"저기 얼빠진 자가 둘이나 가는군.\" 늙은 맨섬 사람이 중얼거렸다. \"하나는 힘이 넘쳐 얼이 빠졌고, 다른 하나는 힘이 모자라 얼이 빠졌어. 그건 그렇고, 여기 삭은 줄의 끄트머리로구나 — 그것도 물이 뚝뚝 흐르는. 이걸 손보라고? 내 생각엔 줄을 통째로 새것으로 바꾸는 게 제일 낫겠는데. 스터브(Stubb) 씨한테 가서 말해 봐야겠어.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 126. The Life-Buoy.\n\n앞으로 향한 에이해브(Ahab)의 수평으로 겨눈 강철에 따라 이제 남동쪽으로 진로를 잡고, 그 항진을 오로지 에이해브의 수평 측정용 측정줄과 측정기에만 의지한 채, 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 적도를 향해 나아가는 길을 굳건히 지켰다. 그토록 인적 드문 바다를 그토록 오래 건너며, 단 한 척의 배도 보이지 않았고, 머지않아 변함없는 무역풍에 옆으로 떠밀려 단조롭도록 잔잔한 물결 위를 흘러가니, 이 모든 것은 어떤 격렬하고 절망적인 장면을 예고하는 기이한 고요처럼 보였다.\n\n마침내 배가 적도 어장의 이를테면 변두리에 가까워졌을 때, 새벽에 앞서는 짙은 어둠 속에서 바위투성이 작은 섬 무리 곁을 항해하고 있을 때, 그때 플라스크(Flask)가 이끌던 당직들은 한 울음소리에 소스라쳤다. 그것은 더없이 애처롭고 사납고 이 세상의 것 같지 않은 소리였으니, 헤롯에게 학살당한 무죄한 아기들의 유령이 채 말이 되지 못한 채 흐느끼는 듯한 그 소리에, 너나없이 모두가 몽상에서 화들짝 깨어나, 그 사나운 울음이 들리는 동안 내내, 새겨진 로마 노예상처럼 못 박힌 듯 서서, 혹은 앉아서, 혹은 기댄 채 귀 기울이고 있었다. 선원들 가운데 기독교를 믿거나 문명화된 자들은 그것이 인어라고 하며 몸서리쳤으나, 이교도 작살잡이들은 조금도 겁먹지 않았다. 그런데 백발의 맨섬 사람, 곧 그들 중 가장 늙은 뱃사람은 들려온 그 사납고 오싹한 소리들이 방금 바다에 빠져 죽은 자들의 목소리라고 단언했다.\n\n해먹 속 아래에 있던 에이해브는 잿빛 새벽이 되어 갑판으로 올라오고 나서야 비로소 이 일을 들었다. 그때 플라스크가 그 일을 들려주었는데, 어두운 의미가 은근히 깃들어 있지 않은 것은 아니었다. 에이해브는 공허하게 웃고는, 그 괴이한 일을 이렇게 풀어 설명했다.\n\n배가 지나온 그 바위섬들은 수많은 바다표범이 모여 사는 곳이어서, 어미를 잃은 어린 바다표범 몇 마리, 혹은 새끼를 잃은 어미 몇 마리가 배 가까이 떠올라 배를 따라오며, 사람 비슷한 울음으로 울고 흐느꼈으리라는 것이었다. 그러나 이 설명은 도리어 그들 중 몇몇을 더욱 동요시켰으니, 대부분의 뱃사람은 바다표범에 대해 매우 미신적인 감정을 품고 있기 때문이었다. 그것은 그들이 곤경에 처했을 때 내는 독특한 소리 때문만이 아니라, 둥근 머리와 어딘가 반쯤 영리해 보이는 얼굴이 배 옆 물 위로 빠끔히 솟아오르며 사람 같은 인상을 풍기는 까닭이기도 했다. 어떤 상황에서는 바다에서 바다표범이 사람으로 오인된 일이 한두 번이 아니었다.\n\n그러나 선원들의 불길한 예감은 그날 아침 그들 중 한 사람의 운명으로 가장 그럴듯한 확증을 받을 운명이었다. 해 뜰 무렵 이 사람은 해먹에서 일어나 앞돛대 꼭대기의 망대로 올라갔다. 그가 아직 잠에서 반쯤도 깨지 못한 탓이었는지(뱃사람들은 더러 그런 몽롱한 상태로 돛대 위에 오르기도 하니까), 그가 정말 그러했는지는 이제 알 길이 없다. 그러나 어찌 되었든, 그가 망대에 오른 지 얼마 되지 않았을 때 한 외침이 들렸다. 외침과 함께 무언가 빠르게 떨어지는 소리가 들렸다. 위를 올려다보니 허공에서 떨어지는 유령 같은 형체가 보였고, 아래를 내려다보니 파란 바다 위에 작게 일렁이는 하얀 물거품 더미가 보였다.\n\n구명부표, 곧 길고 가느다란 통 하나가 고물에서 떨어졌다. 그것은 늘 거기 매달려 정교한 용수철에 순순히 매여 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그것을 붙잡으려고 솟아오르는 손은 없었고, 오랫동안 햇볕에 달궈진 그 통은 쪼그라들어 천천히 물이 차올랐으며, 바싹 마른 그 나무는 구멍이란 구멍마다 물을 빨아들였다. 그리하여 쇠테를 두르고 못이 박힌 그 통은 마치 그에게 베개를, 비록 실은 딱딱한 베개에 불과할지언정 베개를 마련해 주려는 듯 그 뱃사람을 따라 바닥으로 가라앉았다.\n\n이리하여 흰 고래(White Whale)를 찾으려고, 그것도 흰 고래만의 독특한 영역에서 돛대에 오른 피쿼드호의 첫 사람이, 그 사람이 깊은 바닷속으로 삼켜졌다. 그러나 그때 그 일을 떠올린 자는 아마 거의 없었으리라. 사실 어떤 의미에서 그들은 이 사건을 슬퍼하지 않았으니, 적어도 하나의 전조로서는 그러했다. 그들은 이를 앞날의 재앙을 예고하는 징조가 아니라, 이미 예고된 재앙이 이루어진 것으로 여겼기 때문이다. 그들은 이제야 간밤에 들었던 그 사나운 비명의 까닭을 알게 되었다고 단언했다. 그러나 다시금 늙은 맨섬 사람은 아니라고 했다.\n\n이제 잃어버린 구명부표를 대신할 것을 마련해야 했다. 스타벅(Starbuck)이 그 일을 맡으라는 지시를 받았다. 그러나 충분히 가벼운 통을 찾을 수 없었고, 항해의 위기가 다가오는 듯한 그 열병 같은 조바심 속에서 모든 선원이, 그 끝이 무엇으로 드러나든 간에, 그 최후의 목적에 직접 연결되지 않은 어떤 수고도 견디지 못하던 터라, 그들은 배 고물에 부표를 마련해 두지 않은 채 내버려 두려 했다. 바로 그때 기묘한 몸짓과 넌지시 던지는 말로, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)가 제 관(棺)에 관한 한 가지 암시를 흘렸다.\n\n\"관으로 만든 구명부표라니!\" 스타벅이 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그것참 좀 별난 일이로군.\" 스터브(Stubb)가 말했다.\n\n\"꽤 쓸 만한 부표가 될 겁니다.\" 플라스크가 말했다. \"여기 목수가 손쉽게 손볼 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"가져오게. 달리 방도가 없네.\" 스타벅이 침울하게 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다. \"그것을 손보게, 목수. 나를 그렇게 쳐다보지 말게 — 관 말일세. 내 말 알아듣겠나? 손보란 말이네.\"\n\n\"그러면 뚜껑에 못을 박을까요, 나리?\" 목수가 망치질을 하듯 손을 움직이며 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"그러고서 이음매를 메울까요, 나리?\" 그가 뱃밥 메우는 인두를 쓰듯 손을 움직이며 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"그러면 그 위에 역청을 칠할까요, 나리?\" 그가 역청 단지를 쓰듯 손을 움직이며 물었다.\n\n\"썩 물러가게! 무엇에 씌어 그러는 겐가? 관으로 구명부표나 만들고, 그 이상은 더 묻지 말게. — 스터브 씨, 플라스크 씨, 나와 함께 앞으로 갑시다.\"\n\n\"성을 내며 가 버리는군. 일 전체는 그가 견딜 수 있으면서, 부분 부분에서는 영 뒤로 빼지. 자, 이건 영 마음에 안 들어. 에이해브 선장을 위해 다리를 만들어 드렸더니 그분은 신사처럼 차고 다니시는데, 퀴퀘그를 위해 상자 하나를 만들어 줬더니 그 친구는 그 안에 머리를 들이밀려 하질 않거든. 저 관에 들인 내 수고가 다 헛것이 되고 마는 건가? 그런데 이제는 그걸 구명부표로 만들라는 분부야. 이건 헌 외투를 뒤집는 것과 같아. 이제 안감을 겉으로 끄집어내자는 격이지. 난 이런 땜장이 짓 같은 일이 싫어 — 도무지 싫단 말이야. 품위가 없어. 내 할 일이 아니라고. 땜장이 새끼들이나 땜장이 일을 하라지. 우리는 그놈들보다 한 수 위니까. 나는 깨끗하고, 처녀처럼 손때 안 묻고, 반듯반듯한 수학 같은 일만 손에 쥐고 싶어. 처음에서 규칙대로 시작해, 중간에 이르면 한가운데에 있고, 결말에 가서 끝나는 그런 일 말이야. 한가운데서 끝나고 끝에서 시작하는 땜장이 일 따위는 싫다고. 땜장이 일을 시키는 건 노파들이나 부리는 수작이야. 원, 노파들은 어찌 그리도 하나같이 땜장이를 좋아하는지! 한번은 예순다섯 먹은 노파가 대머리 젊은 땜장이랑 눈이 맞아 달아난 일을 내 알고 있지. 그래서 나는 바인야드에서 일터를 꾸리던 시절에도, 뭍에서 외로운 과부 노파들 일은 결코 맡으려 하지 않았어. 그 외로운 늙은 머릿속에 나하고 달아날 궁리가 들어찰지도 모르니까. 하지만 에라, 모르겠다! 바다엔 눈으로 덮인 봉우리 말고는 늙은이도 없지. 어디 보자. 뚜껑에 못을 박고, 이음매를 메우고, 그 위에 역청을 칠하고, 단단히 빗장을 질러 막은 다음, 배 고물에 용수철 고리를 달아 매단다. 관을 가지고 이런 짓을 해 본 자가 일찍이 있었던가? 요즘 같으면 미신에 사로잡힌 늙은 목수들은 이런 일을 하느니 차라리 삭구에 제 몸을 묶이는 편을 택할 테지. 하지만 나는 옹이투성이 애루스툭 솔송나무로 빚어진 사람이야. 난 끄떡없어. 관을 엉덩이에 차고 다니다니! 묘지 쟁반을 싣고 항해를 하다니! 하지만 상관없어. 우리 목수들은 관이며 영구차만 만드는 게 아니라 신방 침대며 카드놀이 탁자도 만든다고. 우리는 달삯으로 일하든, 일감 단위로 일하든, 이문을 보고 일하든 하는 것이지, 우리 일의 까닭이며 연유를 따지는 건 우리 몫이 아니야. 너무나도 빌어먹을 땜장이 짓이 아닌 다음에야 말이지. 그럴 땐 할 수만 있으면 슬쩍 치워 버리는 거야. 흠! 이제 그 일을 정성껏 해 주지. 어디 — 가만, 배에 탄 사람이 모두 몇이더라? 아차, 잊어버렸군. 어쨌든 터키 머리 매듭을 단 구명 밧줄을 서른 가닥 따로 마련해서, 저마다 석 자 길이로 관 둘레에 빙 둘러 늘어뜨리자. 그러면 배가 가라앉을 때 관 하나를 두고 서른 명의 팔팔한 녀석들이 서로 다투게 될 테니, 해 아래 그리 자주 보기 힘든 광경이렷다! 자, 망치야, 뱃밥 인두야, 역청 단지야, 밧줄용 송곳아, 다 이리 오너라! 어디 한번 해 보자꾸나.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 127. The Deck.\n\n갑판.\n관은 죔틀 작업대와 열린 승강구 사이, 밧줄통 두 개 위에 놓여 있다. 목수가 그 이음매에 뱃밥을 메워 넣는 중이고, 꼬인 뱃밥 끈은 그의 작업복 가슴팍에 끼워 둔 커다란 뭉치에서 천천히 풀려 나온다. 에이해브가 선실 통로에서 느릿느릿 걸어 나오다가, 핍이 자기를 따라오는 소리를 듣는다.\n\n\"돌아가거라, 얘야. 곧 다시 너와 함께 있어 주마. 가는구나! 저 아이만큼 내 기분을 살갑게 맞춰 주는 이 손은 없으렷다. 어느 교회의 가운뎃길 같구나! 여기 이게 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"구명부표입니다, 선장님. 스타벅 항해사님의 지시랍니다. 아, 보세요, 선장님! 승강구를 조심하세요!\"\n\n\"고맙네, 이 사람아. 자네 관이 무덤 곁에 잘도 놓여 있구먼.\"\n\n\"네? 승강구 말씀입니까? 아! 정말 그렇군요, 선장님, 정말 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"자네가 그 다리 만든 자가 아닌가? 보게, 이 의족이 자네 작업장에서 나온 게 아니더냐?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다, 선장님. 테두리 쇠는 잘 버티고 있는지요?\"\n\n\"그럭저럭 쓸 만하네. 한데 자네는 장의사 노릇도 하는 게 아닌가?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선장님. 제가 여기 이걸 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 관으로 손봐 두었는데, 이제 와서 다른 물건으로 바꾸라 하시는군요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 말해 보게. 자네야말로 손에 잡히는 건 죄다 거머쥐고, 남의 일에 끼어들고, 혼자 다 해 먹는 그 천하의 이교도 늙은 망나니가 아닌가? 하루는 다리를 만들고, 다음 날엔 그 다리를 처넣을 관을 만들고, 또 그다음엔 그 똑같은 관으로 구명부표를 만들어 내니 말이야. 자네는 신들만큼이나 원칙이 없고, 또 신들만큼이나 못 하는 일이 없는 만물박사로구나.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 아무 뜻도 없습니다, 선장님. 그저 시키는 대로 할 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"또 신들 얘기로구먼. 들어 보게, 자네는 관을 손볼 때 노래 한 자락 부르지 않는가? 듣자 하니 티탄들은 화산의 분화구를 깎아 낼 때 곡조를 흥얼거렸다 하고, 연극에 나오는 무덤 파는 자도 삽을 손에 든 채 노래를 부른다네. 자네는 영 안 부르는가?\"\n\n\"노래 말입니까, 선장님? 제가 노래를요? 아, 그런 건 저야 아무래도 상관없습니다, 선장님. 하지만 무덤 파는 자가 가락을 낸 까닭은 분명 제 삽에는 가락이 없었기 때문일 겁니다, 선장님. 한데 이 뱃밥 메우는 망치에는 가락이 그득합니다. 들어 보십시오.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 그건 저 뚜껑이 울림판 노릇을 하기 때문이지. 그리고 무엇이든 울림판을 만드는 건 바로 이것일세 — 그 아래에 아무것도 없다는 것 말이야. 하지만 시신이 든 관도 거의 똑같은 소리를 낸다네, 목수. 자네는 상여를 메는 일을 거든 적이 있어, 관이 교회 묘지의 문에 부딪치며 안으로 들어갈 때 그 소리를 들어 본 적이 있는가?\"\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드려, 선장님, 제가——\"\n\n\"솔직히? 그게 무슨 말인가?\"\n\n\"아니, 그저, 선장님, 그건 그냥 일종의 감탄사 같은 겁니다 — 그게 전부입니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"음, 음. 계속해 보게.\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드리려던 건, 선장님, 그러니까——\"\n\n\"자네는 누에인가? 제 몸에서 제 수의(壽衣)를 자아내는 게야? 자네 가슴팍을 보게! 어서 서둘러! 이 잡동사니들을 눈앞에서 치워 버리게.\"\n\n\"선장님이 고물 쪽으로 가시는군. 갑작스럽기도 하지. 하지만 무더운 위도에선 돌풍도 갑자기 몰아치는 법이지. 듣자 하니 갈라파고스 제도 가운데 하나인 앨버말섬은 적도가 그 한복판을 정확히 가른다더군. 내 보기엔 어떤 종류의 적도가 저 늙은 양반도 그 한복판을 정확히 가르고 있는 듯해. 저이는 늘 적도선 아래 있단 말이야 — 불같이 뜨겁지, 암 그렇고말고! 이쪽을 보고 계시는군 — 자, 뱃밥아, 어서. 다시 시작하자. 이 나무망치는 코르크 마개고, 나는 유리 악기를 다루는 명인이렷다 — 탁, 탁!\"\n\n(에이해브가 혼잣말로.)\n\n\"저런 광경이라니! 저런 소리라니! 저 잿빛 머리 딱따구리가 텅 빈 나무를 쪼아 대고 있구나! 지금이야말로 눈멀고 귀먹은 자가 부럽기 짝이 없다. 보라! 저 물건이 예인줄로 가득 찬 밧줄통 두 개 위에 놓여 있다. 참으로 짓궂은 익살꾼이로구나, 저자는. 따다닥! 저렇게 사람의 초(秒)가 째깍거리는구나! 아! 온갖 물질이란 게 얼마나 덧없는 것인가! 가늠할 수 없는 사념(思念) 말고, 참으로 실재하는 것이 무어란 말인가? 자, 이제 보라, 음산한 죽음의 더없이 무서운 상징이, 한낱 우연에 의해, 가장 위태로운 목숨을 돕고 그 희망을 일러 주는 표지가 되었구나. 관으로 만든 구명부표라니! 이게 더 멀리까지 가는가? 혹여 어떤 영적인 의미에서, 관이란 결국 불멸을 지켜 주는 것에 지나지 않는 게 아닐까! 그 점을 곰곰 생각해 봐야겠다. 아니, 그만두자. 나는 이 땅의 어두운 쪽으로 너무 깊이 와 버려, 그 반대쪽, 곧 이론상의 밝은 쪽은 내게 어슴푸레한 황혼으로밖에 보이지 않는구나. 목수, 자네는 그 저주받은 소리를 영영 멈추지 않을 작정인가? 나는 아래로 내려가네. 내가 다시 돌아왔을 때 저 물건이 여기 있는 꼴은 보지 않게 해 주게. 자, 그럼, 핍아, 우리 이 문제를 함께 이야기해 보자꾸나. 나는 네게서 더없이 놀라운 철학을 빨아들이고 있단다! 알지 못하는 세계에서 흘러나오는 어떤 미지의 물길들이 분명 네 안으로 쏟아져 들고 있는 게야!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 128. The Pequod Meets The Rachel.\n\n이튿날, 큰 배 한 척, 곧 레이철호(Rachel)가 발견되었는데, 그 배는 곧장 피쿼드호(Pequod)를 향해 내려오고 있었고, 활대마다 사람들이 빽빽이 매달려 있었다. 그때 피쿼드호는 물살을 가르며 제법 빠른 속도로 나아가고 있었다. 그러나 바람을 안고 너른 날개를 펼친 그 낯선 배가 가까이 다가들자, 으스대던 돛들이 일제히 터진 오줌보처럼 푹 꺼졌고, 멈춰 선 그 선체에서는 모든 생기가 빠져나갔다.\n\n\"나쁜 소식이야. 저 배가 나쁜 소식을 가져오는군.\" 늙은 맨섬 사람이 중얼거렸다. 그러나 나팔을 입에 댄 채 자기 보트에 일어서 있던 그 배의 선장이 미처 부푼 마음으로 인사를 건네기도 전에, 에이해브(Ahab)의 목소리가 먼저 울렸다.\n\n\"흰 고래(White Whale)를 보았소?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 어제 보았소. 혹시 떠다니는 고래잡이 보트를 보지 못했소?\"\n\n기쁨을 목구멍 안으로 짓눌러 삼키며, 에이해브는 이 뜻밖의 물음에 보지 못했노라고 대답했다. 그러고는 기꺼이 그 낯선 배에 건너가 보려던 참인데, 낯선 배의 선장이 직접 자기 배의 진행을 멈추고서 뱃전을 타고 내려오는 모습이 보였다. 노를 몇 번 세차게 젓자, 그의 보트 갈고리가 어느새 피쿼드호의 주(主)사슬을 단단히 걸어챘고, 그는 갑판 위로 뛰어올랐다. 에이해브는 그를 보자마자 자기가 아는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람임을 알아보았다. 그러나 격식 차린 인사 따위는 오가지 않았다.\n\n\"녀석이 어디 있었소? 죽지는 않았겠지! 죽지는 않았겠지!\" 에이해브는 바싹 다가서며 외쳤다. \"어찌 된 일이오?\"\n\n전해진 사연인즉, 전날 오후 다소 늦은 무렵, 그 낯선 배의 보트 세 척이 고래 떼와 맞붙어 그것들에게 이끌려 배에서 사오 마일이나 떨어진 곳까지 가게 되었는데, 한창 바람 부는 쪽으로 빠르게 추격하던 중에, 모비 딕(Moby Dick)의 흰 혹과 머리가 그리 멀지 않은 바람 아래쪽 바다 위로 불쑥 솟아올랐다고 한다. 그리하여 네 번째로 채비된 보트, 곧 예비로 남겨 두었던 보트가 즉각 내려져 추격에 나섰다. 바람을 등지고 한바탕 날렵하게 내달린 끝에, 모든 배 가운데 가장 빠른 용골을 지닌 이 네 번째 보트는 마침내 고래에 작살을 박아 넣은 듯했다. 적어도 돛대 꼭대기에 있던 사람이 알아볼 수 있는 한으로는 그러했다. 그는 멀리서 점점 작아지는, 점처럼 보이는 보트를 보았고, 이어 부글거리는 흰 물거품이 잠깐 번뜩이는 것을 보았으며, 그 뒤로는 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그리하여 흔히 그러하듯, 작살에 찔린 고래가 자기를 쫓는 자들을 끌고 끝없이 달아나 버린 모양이라고 짐작되었다. 약간의 불안은 있었으나, 아직 분명한 경보가 울린 것은 아니었다. 귀선 신호기가 삭구에 내걸렸고, 이윽고 어둠이 깔렸다. 정반대 방향으로 떨어진 네 번째 보트를 찾으러 가기에 앞서 바람 부는 쪽 멀리 있던 세 척의 보트부터 먼저 거두어들여야 했으므로, 그 배는 부득이 자정 무렵까지 그 보트를 운명에 내맡겨 두어야 했을 뿐만 아니라, 그동안 오히려 그 보트와의 거리를 더 벌릴 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 마침내 나머지 선원들을 무사히 갑판에 거두어들이자, 그 배는 사라진 보트를 뒤쫓아 돛이란 돛은 죄다 펼치고 — 보조돛 위에 또 보조돛을 — 한껏 내달았으며, 봉화로 삼고자 기름 솥에 불을 지피고, 그 밖의 모든 사람을 돛대 위에 올려 망을 보게 했다. 그렇게 항해하여, 마지막으로 목격되었을 때 사라진 자들이 있었으리라 짐작되는 곳까지 넉넉히 다다랐으면서도, 그리고 거기서 잠시 멈춰 예비 보트들을 내려 사방을 샅샅이 뒤졌으면서도, 아무것도 찾지 못해 다시 내달았다가, 또 멈춰 보트를 내리기를 거듭하며 날이 밝을 때까지 그 짓을 계속했으면서도, 사라진 용골의 그림자조차 끝내 보지 못했다.\n\n이야기를 마친 낯선 선장은 곧바로 피쿼드호에 건너온 까닭을 털어놓기 시작했다. 그는 피쿼드호가 자기 배와 합세하여 함께 수색해 주기를, 곧 두 배가 사오 마일 간격을 두고 나란한 항로로 바다를 누비며 말하자면 이중의 수평선을 훑어 주기를 바랐다.\n\n\"내가 지금 무언가 걸고 장담하겠는데,\" 스터브(Stubb)가 플래스크(Flask)에게 속삭였다. \"저 사라진 보트에 탄 누군가가 저 선장의 제일 좋은 외투를 입고 갔든지, 어쩌면 회중시계를 차고 갔든지 한 걸세. 저토록 미친 듯이 되찾으려 안달하는 걸 보면 말이야. 한창 포경철에 점잖은 고래잡이 배 두 척이 사라진 보트 하나를 찾자고 함께 누비고 다닌다는 소리를 누가 들어 봤겠나? 보게, 플래스크, 그저 저 핼쑥한 낯빛 좀 보라고. 눈동자 단추까지 새파랗게 질렸어. 보게나. 외투 따위가 아니었어. 분명 저건 그러니까……\"\n\n\"내 아들, 내 친자식이 그 안에 있소. 제발 부탁이오. 간청하오. 비노니——\" 여기서 낯선 선장이 에이해브를 향해 외쳤다. 에이해브는 그때껏 그의 청을 그저 얼음장같이 받아넘기고 있었다. \"마흔여덟 시간만 당신 배를 빌려 주시오. 기꺼이 값을 치르겠소, 두둑이 치르리다. 달리 방도가 없다면 말이오. 그저 마흔여덟 시간만, 그것만이면 되오. 당신은 반드시, 오, 반드시 그래 줘야만 하오, 또 기어이 그리해 줄 것이오.\"\n\n\"아들이라니!\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"오, 잃어버린 게 바로 제 아들이었구나! 외투니 시계니 했던 말은 죄다 거두겠네. 에이해브는 뭐라 하시려나? 우리는 그 아이를 구해야 해.\"\n\n\"그 애도 어젯밤 나머지 사람들과 함께 물에 빠져 죽었어.\" 그들 뒤에 서 있던 늙은 맨섬 선원이 말했다. \"내 들었거든. 자네들도 그들의 혼령 소리를 들었잖나.\"\n\n그런데 곧 드러난 바이지만, 이 레이철호의 사건을 한층 더 비통하게 만든 것은 다음과 같은 사정이었다. 사라진 보트의 선원들 가운데 선장의 아들 하나가 있었을 뿐만 아니라, 같은 시각 다른 보트들의 선원들 가운데에도, 다만 정반대 편으로, 추격의 어두운 곡절 속에서 배와 떨어져 버린 또 하나의 아들이 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 한동안 가련한 아버지는 더없이 잔혹한 진퇴양난의 밑바닥으로 곤두박질쳤고, 이는 오직 그의 일등항해사가 그런 위급한 경우 고래잡이 배의 통상적인 절차, 곧 위태로운 처지에 빠졌으되 서로 갈라진 보트들 사이에 놓이면 언제나 다수가 탄 쪽부터 먼저 거두어들인다는 원칙을 본능적으로 따른 덕분에 비로소 풀렸다. 그러나 선장은 알 수 없는 천성의 이유로 이 모든 사정을 입에 올리기를 삼가고 있다가, 에이해브의 얼음장 같은 태도에 떠밀려서야 비로소 아직 행방이 묘연한 한 아들 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그 아이는 이제 겨우 열두 살짜리 어린 사내였는데, 그 아버지는 낸터킷 사람 특유의 부정(父情), 곧 간절하면서도 흔들림 없는 강인한 사랑으로, 거의 까마득한 옛날부터 자기 종족 모두의 숙명이 되어 온 그 직업의 위험과 경이 속으로 이토록 일찍부터 아들을 들여보내려 했던 것이다. 그리고 낸터킷의 선장들이 그토록 어린 아들을 자기 곁에서 떠나보내, 자기 배가 아닌 다른 어느 배에 태워 길게는 삼사 년에 이르는 항해에 내보내는 일도 드물지 않다. 이는 고래잡이 직업에 대한 아들의 첫 경험이, 아비의 자연스럽지만 때아닌 편애나 지나친 노심초사가 어쩌다 드러나는 일로 인해 무뎌지지 않도록 하려는 것이다.\n\n그러는 동안에도, 낯선 선장은 여전히 그 가련한 청을 에이해브에게 애원하고 있었다. 그리고 에이해브는 여전히 모루처럼 우뚝 서서, 온갖 충격을 고스란히 받아 내면서도 제 몸은 조금도 떨지 않았다.\n\n\"가지 않겠소.\" 낯선 선장이 말했다. \"당신이 내게 그러겠노라 말해 줄 때까지는. 같은 처지라면 내가 당신에게 해 주었으면 싶은 그대로 나에게 해 주시오. 당신에게도 아들이 있지 않소, 에이해브 선장. 비록 아직 어린아이이고, 지금쯤 집에서 포근히 잠들어 있겠지만 말이오. 게다가 당신 노년에 얻은 자식이 아니오. 그래, 그래, 당신 마음이 누그러지는구려. 내 눈에 보이오. 어서, 어서들, 자, 활대를 가로로 돌릴 채비를 하라!\"\n\n\"멈춰라!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"밧줄 한 가닥도 건드리지 마라.\" 그러고는 한 마디 한 마디를 길게 빚어내는 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"가디너 선장, 나는 그리할 수 없소. 지금 이 순간에도 나는 시간을 허비하고 있소. 잘 가시오, 잘 가시오. 신께서 당신을 축복하시기를, 그리고 나 자신도 나를 용서할 수 있기를. 하지만 나는 가야만 하오. 스타벅(Starbuck) 군, 나침반함의 시계를 보게. 그리고 지금 이 순간으로부터 삼 분 뒤에는 외부인을 모두 내보내게. 그런 뒤 다시 활대를 앞으로 돌려, 배가 전처럼 나아가도록 하게.\"\n\n서둘러 몸을 돌려 얼굴을 외면한 채, 그는 자기 선실로 내려가 버렸고, 그토록 간절한 청을 무조건적으로, 완전히 거절당한 낯선 선장은 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다. 그러나 가디너는 홀린 듯한 상태에서 퍼뜩 깨어나, 말없이 뱃전으로 황급히 다가갔다. 그러고는 자기 보트로 내려갔다기보다 차라리 굴러떨어지듯 몸을 던지더니, 제 배로 돌아갔다.\n\n이윽고 두 배의 항적이 서로 갈라졌다. 그리고 그 낯선 배가 시야에 머무는 내내, 바다 위에 떠 있는 거뭇한 점이라면 아무리 작은 것이라도 하나하나 좇아 이리저리 뱃머리를 비트는 모습이 보였다. 이쪽으로 저쪽으로 활대가 휘둘러졌고, 우현으로 좌현으로 그 배는 끊임없이 침로를 바꾸었다. 이제는 맞바람 치는 바다를 거슬러 올라가다가, 또다시 그 물결에 떠밀려 내려가곤 했다. 그러는 내내, 그 배의 돛대와 활대마다 사람들이 빽빽이 매달려 있었으니, 마치 사내아이들이 가지 사이를 누비며 버찌를 따고 있는 키 큰 벚나무 세 그루 같았다.\n\n그러나 여전히 머뭇거리며 굽이굽이 구슬프게 나아가는 그 항로를 보면, 물보라로 그토록 흐느끼던 이 배가 여태껏 아무런 위로도 얻지 못했음이 또렷이 드러났다. 그 배는 곧 라헬이었다. 자식들을 위해 우는 라헬, 자식들이 이제 더는 세상에 없는 까닭에 우는 라헬이었다.\n* 라헬: 구약 예레미야서의 한 구절에서 따온 것으로, 라헬은 죽은 자식들을 위해 울며 위로받기를 거부하는 어머니로 그려진다.\n\n# CHAPTER 129. The Cabin.\n\n장. 선실.\n(에이해브가 갑판으로 올라가려 움직이는데, 핍이 따라가려고 그의 손을 붙잡는다.)\n\n\"얘야, 얘야, 내 너에게 이르노니 너는 지금 에이해브를 따라와서는 안 된다. 에이해브가 너를 자기에게서 겁주어 쫓아내지는 않으나, 그렇다고 곁에 두지도 않으려는 때가 다가오고 있다. 가엾은 아이야, 네 안에는 내 병을 너무도 잘 낫게 하는 무언가가 있구나. 같은 것이 같은 것을 고치는 법이니, 이 사냥을 위해서는 내 병이 내가 가장 바라는 건강이 되는 것이다. 너는 여기 아래에 머물러라, 마치 네가 선장이라도 된 양 사람들이 너를 섬길 것이다. 그래, 얘야, 너는 여기 내 나사 박힌 의자에 앉으렴. 너 또한 그 의자에 박힌 또 하나의 나사가 되어야 하느니라.\"\n\n\"안 됩니다, 안 돼요, 안 돼요! 선장님은 온전한 몸이 아니시잖아요. 그저 이 가엾은 저를 잃어버린 한쪽 다리 삼아 쓰세요. 그저 저를 밟고 디디기만 하세요, 선장님. 그것밖에 청하지 않을 테니, 그러면 저는 선장님의 일부로 남는 거예요.\"\n\n\"오! 수백만의 악당이 있다 한들, 이 아이는 나로 하여금 인간의 시들지 않는 충심을 맹신하게 만드는구나! 게다가 흑인이고! 게다가 미친 아이가! 허나 같은 것이 같은 것을 고친다는 말은 이 아이에게도 들어맞는 듯하니, 이렇게 다시 제정신을 찾아 가는구나.\"\n\n\"사람들이 제게 말하기를, 선장님, 한번은 스터브가 이 가엾은 어린 핍을 버렸다 하더군요. 살아 있는 살갗은 온통 검은데도, 물에 빠진 그 뼈는 이제 하얗게 드러나는 그 핍을 말이에요. 하지만 저는 결코 스터브가 그랬듯 선장님을 버리지 않을 거예요, 선장님. 선장님, 저는 선장님과 함께 가야만 해요.\"\n\n\"네가 내게 그런 말을 더 늘어놓으면, 에이해브의 결심이 그의 안에서 용골이 뒤집히듯 뒤집히고 만다. 안 된다고 이르지 않느냐. 그리될 수는 없다.\"\n\n\"오, 착하신 주인님, 주인님, 주인님!\"\n\n\"그렇게 울거라, 그러면 내가 너를 죽여 버릴 테다! 조심해라, 에이해브 또한 미쳐 있으니. 귀를 기울이거라, 그러면 내 상아 다리가 갑판을 디디는 소리를 자주 듣게 될 것이고, 그래도 내가 거기 있음을 알게 되리라. 그리고 이제 나는 너를 떠난다. 네 손을!—맞잡았다! 너는 참되구나, 얘야, 원둘레가 그 중심에 충실하듯이. 그러니 하느님께서 영원토록 너를 축복하시기를. 그리고 만일 그 지경에 이른다면,—무슨 일이 닥치든, 하느님께서 영원토록 너를 구원하시기를.\"\n\n(에이해브가 떠난다. 핍이 한 걸음 앞으로 나선다.)\n\n\"여기 그분이 방금 서 계셨지. 나는 그분이 남긴 공기 속에 서 있건만,—나는 혼자구나. 지금 가엾은 핍이라도 여기 있다면 견딜 수 있으련만, 그 애가 보이질 않아. 핍! 핍! 딩, 동, 딩! 누가 핍을 봤나요? 그 애는 여기 위에 있을 거야. 문을 한번 열어 보자. 뭐야? 자물쇠도, 빗장도, 가로대도 없는데, 그런데도 열리질 않네. 이건 분명 주문(呪文) 탓이야. 그분이 내게 여기 머물라 하셨지. 그래, 그리고 이 나사 박힌 의자가 내 것이라 하셨지. 그렇다면 여기, 나는 고물 가로목에 등을 기대고 앉으리라, 배의 한복판에, 그 용골과 세 개의 돛대를 모두 내 앞에 두고서. 우리 늙은 선원들이 말하기를, 그들의 검은 칠십사문 군함에서는 위대한 제독들이 이따금 식탁에 앉아, 줄지어 늘어선 함장들과 부관들 위에 군림한다지. 하! 이게 뭐지? 견장이다! 견장이야! 견장들이 죄다 몰려오는구나! 술병들을 돌려라. 그대들을 만나니 반갑소. 가득 채우시오, 신사 여러분! 검은 소년이 금몰을 단 외투를 걸친 백인들의 주인 노릇을 하다니, 참으로 묘한 기분이로구나!—신사 여러분, 핍이라는 아이를 보셨소?—키가 5피트(약 1.5미터)에, 풀죽은 낯빛을 하고, 겁쟁이인 어린 흑인 소년 말이오! 한번은 포경 보트에서 뛰어내린 적이 있지요.—그 애를 보셨소? 못 보셨다고! 그렇다면 다시 채우시오, 함장들이여, 그리고 모든 겁쟁이들에게 부끄러움이 있으라 건배합시다! 이름은 대지 않겠소. 그들에게 부끄러움이 있으라! 한 발을 식탁 위에 올려놓으시오. 모든 겁쟁이들에게 부끄러움이 있으라.—쉿! 저 위에서, 상아 소리가 들린다—오, 주인님! 주인님! 당신이 제 머리 위를 걸으실 때면 저는 정말 마음이 무겁습니다. 하지만 저는 여기 머물겠어요, 이 고물이 바위에 부딪쳐 깨지고, 그 틈으로 물이 불룩 밀려들어, 굴들이 저와 함께하러 몰려온다 해도.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 130. The Hat.\n\n모자.\n이제 그토록 길고 광범위한 예비 항해를 거친 끝에, 마땅한 때와 장소에 이르러, 에이해브는—다른 모든 고래잡이 해역을 샅샅이 훑은 뒤—적을 어느 대양의 후미진 구석으로 몰아넣어 그곳에서 한층 더 확실하게 죽이려는 듯했다. 이제 그는 자신을 괴롭히던 상처가 새겨졌던 바로 그 위도와 경도의 지척에 와 있음을 깨달았다. 이제 바로 전날 실제로 모비 딕(Moby Dick)과 마주쳤던 한 배와 말을 나눈 터였다. 그리고 이제 여러 배들과 잇따라 만나면서 알게 된 사실들이 대조적으로 한데 모여, 그 흰 고래가 자기를 사냥하는 자들을—그들이 죄를 짓는 쪽이든 죄를 당하는 쪽이든 가리지 않고—찢어발기던 악마 같은 무심함을 드러내 보였다. 이제 노인의 두 눈 속에는 무언가가 도사리고 있었으니, 나약한 영혼들이 차마 견디며 바라보기 힘든 것이었다. 기나긴 북극의 여섯 달 밤 내내 그 꿰뚫는 듯한 한결같은 중심의 시선을 지켜 내는, 결코 지지 않는 북극성처럼, 에이해브의 목적도 이제 음울한 선원들의 변함없는 한밤중을 향해 붙박인 채 빛을 쏟아 내렸다. 그것이 그들 위로 어찌나 군림했던지, 그들의 모든 불길한 예감과 의심과 불안과 두려움은 영혼 밑바닥에 숨어들어, 단 한 개의 창끝이나 잎사귀조차 내밀지 못하고 말았다.\n\n이렇게 무언가를 예고하는 이 막간(幕間)에는 억지로 짜낸 것이든 자연스러운 것이든 모든 익살도 사라졌다. 스터브(Stubb)는 더 이상 웃음을 자아내려 애쓰지 않았고, 스타벅(Starbuck)도 더 이상 그것을 막으려 들지 않았다. 기쁨과 슬픔, 희망과 두려움이 한결같이 더없이 고운 가루로 빻아져, 그때만큼은 에이해브의 무쇠 같은 영혼이라는 단단히 죈 절구 속에서 가루가 되어 버린 듯했다. 기계처럼, 그들은 말없이 갑판 위를 오갔으니, 노인의 폭군 같은 눈이 늘 자신들을 향해 있음을 의식하고 있었다.\n\n그러나 그의 더 은밀하고 사적인 시간을 깊이 들여다보았다면, 곧 그가 단 하나의 시선 외에는 누구의 눈길도 자신에게 닿지 않는다고 여겼던 그 순간을 살펴보았다면, 에이해브의 눈이 선원들의 눈을 그토록 위압했듯이, 그 헤아릴 길 없는 파시교도(Parsee)의 눈길은 에이해브의 눈을 위압했음을, 또는 적어도 어떤 식으로든, 어떤 기이한 방식으로 이따금 그것에 영향을 미쳤음을 보았을 것이다. 이제 그 야윈 페달라(Fedallah)에게는 그처럼 한층 더해진, 미끄러지듯 스며드는 기이함이 깃들기 시작했고, 그처럼 끊임없는 전율이 그를 뒤흔들었기에, 사람들은 그를 미심쩍은 눈으로 바라보았으니, 그가 정말로 죽을 운명을 지닌 살아 있는 존재인지, 아니면 보이지 않는 어떤 존재의 몸뚱이가 갑판 위에 드리운 떨리는 그림자인지를 반쯤 확신하지 못하는 듯했다. 그리고 그 그림자는 늘 그 자리를 맴돌고 있었다. 페달라가 밤이라 한들 잠을 자거나 아래로 내려가는 모습은 단 한 번도 확실하게 목격된 바 없었기 때문이다. 그는 몇 시간이고 가만히 서 있곤 했지만, 결코 앉거나 기대지 않았다. 그의 핏기 없으나 경이로운 두 눈은 분명히 이렇게 말하고 있었다—우리 두 파수꾼은 결코 쉬지 않는다.\n\n또한 어느 때든, 밤이든 낮이든, 에이해브가 그들 앞에 있지 않은 한 선원들은 이제 갑판에 발을 디딜 수 없었으니, 그는 자신의 회전축 구멍에 서 있거나, 큰 돛대와 뒷돛대라는 두 개의 어김없는 한계 사이의 널빤지를 정확히 오가며 거닐었고, 그렇지 않으면 그가 선실 승강구에 서 있는 모습이 보였다. 그의 살아 있는 발은 마치 한 걸음 내디디려는 듯 갑판 위로 내밀어져 있었고, 모자는 두 눈 위로 푹 눌러쓴 채였다. 그래서 그가 아무리 미동도 없이 서 있더라도, 날이 가고 밤이 거듭되어 그가 해먹에 몸을 누인 적이 없을지언정, 그 푹 눌러쓴 모자 밑에 가려진 까닭에, 사람들은 이 모든 와중에 그의 두 눈이 이따금 정말로 감겨 있는지, 아니면 여전히 자신들을 뚫어지게 살피고 있는지를 결코 틀림없이 알아낼 수 없었다. 그가 한 시간 내내 줄곧 승강구에 그렇게 서 있고, 아랑곳하지 않는 밤이슬이 그 돌로 깎아 놓은 듯한 외투와 모자 위에 구슬방울로 맺혀도 그것은 상관없었다. 밤이 적셔 놓은 옷은 이튿날의 햇살이 그의 몸 위에서 말려 주었으니, 그렇게 날이 가고 또 가고, 밤이 가고 또 가도, 그는 더 이상 갑판 널빤지 아래로 내려가지 않았다. 선실에서 무언가가 필요하면 그것을 가져오라고 사람을 시켰다.\n\n그는 같은 바깥 공기 속에서 식사를 했으니, 곧 그가 드는 단 두 끼니인 아침과 저녁이었다. 밤참에는 결코 손대지 않았고, 수염도 깎지 않았다. 그 수염은 마치 바람에 뽑혀 넘어진 나무의 드러난 뿌리가, 윗부분의 푸르름은 시들어 죽었어도 헐벗은 밑동에서 하릴없이 계속 자라나듯, 거무스름하게 온통 뒤엉킨 채 자라났다. 그러나 비록 그의 온 삶이 이제 갑판 위에서의 한 번의 불침번이 되어 버렸고, 또 파시교도의 신비로운 불침번도 그 자신의 것과 마찬가지로 한 치의 끊김도 없었을지언정, 이 두 사람은—한 사람이 다른 사람에게—결코 말을 나누는 법이 없는 듯했으니, 오랜 간격을 두고 지나가는 사소한 일이 그것을 불가피하게 만들 때를 제외하고는 그러했다. 비록 그처럼 강력한 마법이 은밀히 그 둘을 이어 주는 듯했으나, 겉으로 드러나기로는, 그리고 외경에 사로잡힌 선원들에게는, 둘이 양극처럼 멀리 떨어져 있는 듯 보였다. 만일 낮에 그들이 우연히 한마디라도 나눈다면, 밤에는 둘 다 벙어리가 되어, 아주 사소한 말 한 마디 주고받는 일조차 없었다. 이따금 더없이 긴 시간 동안, 단 한 번의 부름도 없이, 그들은 별빛 속에 멀리 떨어져 서 있었으니, 에이해브는 자신의 승강구에, 파시교도는 큰 돛대 곁에 있었으나, 그래도 서로를 붙박인 듯 응시하고 있었다. 마치 에이해브는 파시교도 속에서 자신이 앞으로 드리운 그림자를 보고, 파시교도는 에이해브 속에서 자신이 버린 실체를 보는 듯했다.\n\n그러면서도 어쩐 일인지, 에이해브는—나날이, 매시간, 매 순간 자신의 부하들에게 위엄 있게 드러나는 그 본연의 모습으로—독립된 군주처럼 보였고, 파시교도는 다만 그의 노예처럼 보였다. 그런가 하면 또다시 둘은 한 멍에에 함께 매여 있고, 보이지 않는 폭군이 그들을 몰아가는 듯했으니, 야윈 그림자가 단단한 갈빗대 곁에 붙어 있는 형국이었다. 이 파시교도가 무엇이든 간에, 온통 갈빗대요 용골인 것은 단단한 에이해브였기 때문이다.\n\n새벽의 가장 희미한 첫 어스름이 비칠 무렵이면, 그의 무쇠 같은 목소리가 고물 쪽에서 들려왔다—\"돛대 꼭대기로 올라가라!\" 그리고 온종일, 해가 진 뒤에도, 황혼이 진 뒤에도, 키잡이의 종이 울릴 때마다 매시간 똑같은 목소리가 들려왔다—\"무엇이 보이느냐?—똑똑히! 똑똑히 보라!\"\n\n그러나 아이들을 찾아 헤매는 레이철호와 마주친 뒤로 사나흘이 미끄러지듯 흘러갔는데도 아직 물줄기 하나 보이지 않자, 편집광에 사로잡힌 노인은 선원들의 충실함을, 적어도 이교도 작살잡이들을 제외한 거의 모두의 충실함을 미덥지 않게 여기는 듯했다. 심지어 스터브와 플래스크(Flask)마저도 자기가 찾는 그 광경을 일부러 못 본 체 넘기지나 않을까 의심하는 듯했다. 그러나 이런 의심이 정말로 그의 것이었다 한들, 그의 행동이 그것을 넌지시 내비치는 듯 보일지언정, 그는 슬기롭게도 그것을 말로 드러내기를 삼갔다.\n\n\"고래를 맨 처음 보는 것은 내가 직접 하겠다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그렇고말고! 에이해브가 그 금화를 차지해야지!\" 그러고는 제 손으로 바구니처럼 엮은 밧줄 둥지를 만들었다. 그리고 한 선원을 위로 올려 보내 도르래 하나가 달린 활차를 큰 돛대 꼭대기에 고정하게 한 다음, 아래로 꿰어 내린 밧줄의 두 끝을 받아 들었다. 그러고는 한쪽 끝을 자기 바구니에 매고, 다른 쪽 끝은 난간에 붙들어 매려고 고정 핀을 마련했다. 이 일을 마치고, 그 끝을 아직 손에 쥔 채 핀 곁에 선 그는 선원들을 둘러보며 한 사람에게서 다른 사람에게로 시선을 옮겼고, 다구(Daggoo)와 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 타슈테고(Tashtego)에게는 한참 동안 눈길을 멈추었으나 페달라는 피했다. 그러고는 굳건히 믿음을 거는 눈길을 일등항해사에게 머무르며 말했다—\"이 밧줄을 잡게, 자네—내 그것을 자네 손에 맡기네, 스타벅.\" 그런 다음 바구니 안에 몸을 자리 잡고, 자기를 그 횃대까지 끌어 올리라고 명령을 내리니, 마침내 밧줄을 단단히 붙들어 맨 자는 스타벅이었고, 그 후로 그는 밧줄 곁에 서 있었다. 그리하여 한 손으로 가장 높은 돛대를 부둥켜안은 채, 에이해브는 그토록 높은 곳에서 한눈에 담기는 그 넓게 펼쳐진 원 안의 바다를 몇 마일이고 몇 마일이고 멀리 내다보았다—앞으로, 뒤로, 이쪽으로, 저쪽으로.\n\n선상에서 선원이 삭구(索具)의 어느 높고 거의 외따로 떨어진, 발 디딜 곳이라곤 없는 자리에서 손으로 일을 할 때면, 그는 밧줄에 매달려 그 자리까지 끌어 올려진 채 거기 지탱된다. 이런 경우에 갑판 위에서 그 밧줄을 붙들어 맨 끝은 늘 그것을 특별히 지키는 한 사람에게 엄중히 맡겨진다. 이는 그처럼 어지러이 뒤얽힌 동삭(動索)의 황야에서는, 위쪽에 매여 있는 갖가지 다른 연결 관계를 갑판에서 보이는 것만으로는 늘 어김없이 분간할 수가 없고, 또 이런 밧줄들의 갑판 쪽 끝이 몇 분마다 한 번씩 매듭에서 풀려 내던져지는 까닭에, 만일 변함없는 파수꾼을 두지 않는다면, 끌어 올려진 선원이 선원들의 부주의로 인해 떨어져 나가 바다로 곤두박질쳐 떨어지는 일이 그저 자연스러운 운명이 되고 말 것이기 때문이다. 그러므로 이 일에서 에이해브가 취한 조치는 별스러운 것이 아니었다. 다만 한 가지 기이해 보이는 점이 있었으니, 그것은 일찍이 그에게 조금이라도 결연함에 가까운 무언가로 감히 맞서 본 거의 유일한 사람이자—더구나 망보는 일에 대한 충실함을 그가 다소 의심하는 듯했던 바로 그 사람 가운데 하나인—스타벅을, 그가 바로 자신의 파수꾼으로 골랐다는 점이었다. 그토록 미덥지 않게 여기던 그런 사람의 손에 제 목숨을 통째로 선뜻 맡긴 것이다.\n\n자, 에이해브가 처음으로 높은 곳에 올라앉았을 때, 그가 거기 올라간 지 십 분도 채 되지 않아, 이 위도에서 고래잡이배의 사람 탄 돛대 꼭대기 둘레를 거추장스러울 만큼 가까이 날아다니기 일쑤인 붉은 부리의 사나운 바다매 가운데 한 마리가, 그의 머리 둘레를 종잡을 수 없이 빠른 원을 그리며 어지러이 빙빙 돌면서 끽끽 울어 댔다. 그러더니 그것은 곧장 천 피트나 허공으로 솟구쳐 올랐다가, 다시 나선을 그리며 내려와 그의 머리 둘레를 소용돌이치듯 맴돌았다.\n\n그러나 어슴푸레한 먼 수평선에 시선을 못 박은 채, 에이해브는 이 사나운 새를 알아채지 못하는 듯했다. 사실 다른 어느 누구도 그것을 그다지 눈여겨보지 않았을 터이니, 그것은 드문 일이 아니었기 때문이다. 다만 이제는 거의 그 무엇도 마음에 두지 않는 눈조차도 거의 모든 광경 속에서 어떤 교묘한 의미를 읽어 내는 듯했다.\n\n\"모자, 모자입니다, 선장님!\" 시칠리아 출신 선원이 별안간 외쳤으니, 그는 뒷돛대 꼭대기에 배치되어 에이해브의 바로 뒤쪽에, 다만 그보다는 다소 낮은 자리에 깊은 허공의 골을 사이에 두고 서 있었다.\n\n그러나 이미 그 검은 날개는 노인의 두 눈앞에 와 있었고, 길고 갈고리진 부리는 그의 머리께에 닿아 있었다. 끽끽 한 번 울고는, 그 검은 매가 제 노획물을 채어 쏜살같이 날아가 버렸다.\n\n독수리 한 마리가 타르퀴니우스의 머리 둘레를 세 바퀴 돌며 그의 모자를 벗겼다가 다시 씌워 주었고, 그러자 그의 아내 타나퀼은 타르퀴니우스가 로마의 왕이 되리라 선언했다. 그러나 그 징조가 길조로 여겨진 것은 오로지 모자를 도로 씌워 준 까닭이었다. 에이해브의 모자는 결코 되돌아오지 않았으니, 그 사나운 매는 그것을 물고 자꾸자꾸 날아갔다. 뱃머리보다 훨씬 멀리 앞서 가더니, 마침내 사라져 버렸다. 그리고 그것이 사라진 지점에서, 작디작은 검은 점 하나가 그 까마득한 높이에서 바다로 떨어지는 것이 어렴풋이 보였다.\n\n# CHAPTER 131. The Pequod Meets The Delight.\n\n격렬한 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 계속 나아갔다. 일렁이는 파도와 나날이 지나갔다. 구명부표 겸 관(棺)은 여전히 가볍게 흔들렸고, 또 다른 배 한 척이 시야에 들어왔는데, 더없이 가련하게도 딜라이트(Delight)호라는 어울리지 않는 이름을 단 배였다. 그 배가 가까이 다가오자, 모두의 시선이 시어즈라 불리는 그 넓은 가로대에 쏠렸다. 어떤 포경선에서는 이 가로대가 8피트나 9피트(약 2.4~2.7미터) 높이로 뒷갑판을 가로질러, 예비용으로 비치하거나 삭구를 풀어 둔, 또는 못 쓰게 된 보트들을 얹어 두는 데 쓰인다.\n\n낯선 배의 가로대 위에는 한때 포경 보트였던 것의 산산이 부서진 하얀 갈빗대와, 몇 조각 쪼개진 널빤지가 얹혀 있었다. 그러나 그 잔해 너머로는, 마치 살이 벗겨지고 반쯤 어긋난 채 허옇게 바래 가는 말의 골격을 들여다보듯, 또렷이 속이 들여다보였다.\n\n\"흰고래를 본 적 있소?\"\n\n\"보시오!\" 고물 난간에서 두 볼이 움푹 꺼진 선장이 대답했다. 그러고는 자신의 나팔로 그 잔해를 가리켰다.\n\n\"그놈을 죽였소?\"\n\n\"그 일을 해낼 작살은 아직 벼려지지 않았소.\" 상대편이 대답하며, 갑판 위에 둥글게 묶인 해먹을 슬픈 눈길로 흘긋 보았는데, 그 모아 놓은 양옆을 몇몇 말없는 선원들이 분주히 꿰매고 있었다.\n\n\"벼려지지 않았다고!\" 에이해브(Ahab)는 보트 받침대에서 퍼스(Perth)가 다듬어 놓은 작살을 낚아채더니, 그것을 쑥 내밀며 소리쳤다. \"보시오, 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람이여. 바로 이 손에 그놈의 죽음을 쥐고 있소이다! 피로 담금질하고, 번개로 담금질한 이 미늘들을, 나는 흰고래가 그 저주받은 목숨을 가장 절실히 느끼는 곳, 곧 지느러미 뒤 저 뜨거운 자리에서 세 곱절로 담금질하겠노라 맹세하오!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 하느님께서 그대를 지켜 주시기를, 노인장이여. 저것이 보이시오.\" 선장은 해먹을 가리켰다. \"나는 다섯 명의 건장한 사내 가운데 단 하나만을 묻으려 하오. 바로 어제까지만 해도 살아 있었으나 밤이 되기도 전에 죽어 버린 자들이오. 묻는 것은 오직 저 하나뿐, 나머지는 죽기도 전에 이미 묻혀 버렸소. 그대는 지금 그들의 무덤 위를 항해하고 있는 거요.\" 그러고는 선원들을 돌아보며 말했다. \"거기 준비됐는가? 그렇다면 널빤지를 난간에 걸치고, 시신을 들어 올려라. 자, 그러면—오! 하느님이시여—\" 그는 두 손을 치켜든 채 해먹을 향해 다가갔다. \"부활과 생명이 함께하시기를——\"\n\n\"앞 돛 활대를 돌려라! 키를 올려라!\" 에이해브가 번개처럼 선원들에게 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 갑작스레 움직이기 시작한 피쿼드호도, 곧 시신이 바다에 부딪히며 일으킨 첨벙 소리를 벗어날 만큼 빠르지는 못했다. 그렇다, 그토록 빠르지 못했기에, 흩날린 물거품 몇 방울이 어쩌면 그 유령 같은 세례로 배의 선체를 적셨을지도 모른다.\n\n이제 에이해브가 의기소침한 딜라이트호 곁을 미끄러져 지나갈 때, 피쿼드호의 고물에 매달린 그 기묘한 구명부표가 두드러지게 도드라져 보였다.\n\n\"하! 저기! 저길 보시오, 사람들이여!\" 그 배 뒤로 불길한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"헛되도다, 오, 낯선 이들이여, 그대들은 우리의 슬픈 장례를 피해 달아나지만, 우리에게 그대들의 고물을 돌려 보이는 것은 다만 그대들의 관을 우리에게 내보이는 것일 뿐이로다!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 132. The Symphony.\n\n맑고 강철빛 도는 푸른 날이었다. 하늘과 바다의 창공은 사방에 두루 퍼진 그 쪽빛 속에서 좀처럼 구분되지 않았다. 다만 생각에 잠긴 듯한 대기는 여인의 얼굴빛처럼 투명하리만치 순결하고 부드러웠으며, 사내처럼 건장한 바다는 잠든 삼손의 가슴팍처럼 길고 힘차고 여운 있는 너울로 일렁였다.\n\n높은 곳에서는 작고 얼룩 하나 없는 새들의 눈처럼 흰 날개가 이리저리 미끄러져 갔다. 그것은 여성스러운 대기가 품은 부드러운 상념들이었다. 그러나 깊은 바다 속, 저 밑바닥 없는 푸름 속에서는 거대한 리바이어던과 황새치와 상어들이 이리저리 내달렸다. 그것은 남성스러운 바다가 품은 강하고 어지럽고 살기 어린 상념들이었다.\n\n그러나 안으로는 이렇게 대비를 이루면서도, 겉으로는 그 대비가 다만 빛깔의 농담(濃淡)과 그늘의 차이에 지나지 않았다. 그 둘은 하나처럼 보였으니, 굳이 말하자면 그 둘을 가르는 것은 오직 성(性)뿐이었다.\n\n저 높은 곳에서 황제이자 군왕인 양 태양은 이 부드러운 대기를 저 대담하게 일렁이는 바다에게 내려 주는 듯했으니, 마치 신부를 신랑에게 건네듯 했다. 그리고 수평선의 띠 두른 경계선 위에서는, 적도 부근에서 가장 잘 보이는 그 부드럽고 떨리는 움직임이, 가련한 신부가 제 가슴을 내어 주며 품은 다정하고 두근거리는 신뢰와 사랑에 찬 떨림을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n꽁꽁 묶이고 비틀린 채, 주름으로 옹이 지고 매듭 진 얼굴, 초췌하리만치 굳세고 굽힘 없는 모습, 폐허의 잿더미 속에서도 여태 타오르는 숯덩이처럼 이글거리는 두 눈 — 흔들림 없는 에이해브가 아침의 맑음 속에 우뚝 섰다. 그 깨어진 투구 같은 이마를 저 아리따운 처녀 같은 하늘의 이마를 향해 치켜들고서.\n\n오, 쪽빛 하늘의 불멸하는 유년이여, 그 순결함이여! 우리 주위를 온통 뛰놀며 도는 보이지 않는 날개 달린 피조물들이여! 대기와 하늘의 감미로운 어린 시절이여! 너희는 어찌 그리도 늙은 에이해브의 똬리 튼 슬픔에 무심하던가! 그러나 나는 이런 모습을 본 적이 있다 — 웃음 어린 눈을 한 작은 요정들, 미리암과 마사가 늙은 아비 주위를 아무 근심 없이 뛰놀며, 다 타 버린 그 뇌의 분화구 가장자리에 돋아난 그슬린 머리칼의 둘레를 가지고 장난치는 모습을.\n\n승강구에서 갑판을 천천히 가로질러, 에이해브는 뱃전에 몸을 기대고 물속에 비친 제 그림자가 응시하는 눈길 아래로 점점 더, 점점 더 가라앉는 것을 지켜보았다 — 그가 그 깊이를 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰면 애쓸수록 그림자는 더욱더 깊이 잠겨 들었다. 그러나 그 마법에 걸린 듯한 대기 속의 사랑스러운 향기는 마침내 그의 영혼을 좀먹던 것을 한순간 흩어 버리는 듯했다. 그 기쁘고 행복한 대기, 그 매혹적인 하늘이 마침내 그를 쓰다듬고 어루만졌다. 그토록 오래 잔인하고 — 매정하던 — 계모 같은 세상이 이제 그의 완고한 목덜미에 다정한 팔을 두르고, 아무리 제멋대로이고 그릇된 자식일망정 그래도 마음속 한구석에서 구원하고 축복할 길을 찾아내겠다는 듯, 그를 굽어보며 기쁨에 겨워 흐느끼는 것만 같았다. 푹 눌러쓴 모자 아래로 에이해브는 바다에 눈물 한 방울을 떨어뜨렸다. 태평양을 다 합쳐도 그 작디작은 한 방울만 한 보물은 담겨 있지 않았다.\n\n스타벅이 그 노인을 보았다. 그가 어찌나 무겁게 뱃전에 몸을 기대고 있는지를 보았다. 그리고 그는 주위에 감도는 고요의 한복판에서 새어 나오는 그 가없는 흐느낌을, 제 진실된 가슴속에서 듣는 듯했다. 그를 건드리지 않으려고, 또 그의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 조심하면서도, 그는 그에게로 다가가 곁에 섰다.\n에이해브가 돌아섰다.\n\n\"스타벅!\"\n\n\"예, 선장님.\"\n\n\"오, 스타벅! 온화하구나, 온화한 바람이야, 그리고 온화한 빛깔의 하늘이고. 바로 이런 날에 — 꼭 이만큼이나 감미롭던 날에 — 나는 첫 고래를 잡았지. 열여덟 살 풋내기 작살잡이로 말이야! 사십 — 사십 — 사십 년 전 일이야! 전 일이라고! 사십 년을 쉴 새 없이 고래잡이로 보냈어! 사십 년의 궁핍과 위험과 폭풍의 세월! 사십 년을 저 무자비한 바다 위에서! 사십 년 동안 에이해브는 평화로운 뭍을 저버렸어, 사십 년 동안 깊은 바다의 공포와 전쟁을 벌이려고! 그래, 정말로 그래, 스타벅, 그 사십 년 가운데 뭍에서 보낸 세월은 삼 년도 채 못 돼. 내가 살아온 이 삶을 생각하면, 그것이 얼마나 황량한 고독이었는지, 선장이라는 자리의 그 담장 두른 폐쇄성 — 바깥의 푸른 들녘에서 흘러드는 그 어떤 공감도 좀처럼 들이지 않는 성벽 같은 도시 — 오, 그 피로함이여! 그 무거움이여! 홀로 명령하는 자의 기니 해안 노예살이 같은 삶이여! 이 모든 것을 생각하면 — 전에는 그저 어렴풋이 의심만 했을 뿐 이토록 사무치게 알지는 못했던 것을 — 그리고 사십 년 동안 내가 어떻게 메마른 소금 절임만 먹고 살아왔는지를 — 메마른 내 영혼의 양식에 꼭 어울리는 표상이지! — 가장 가난한 뭍사람조차 날마다 신선한 과일을 손에 쥐고, 세상의 갓 구운 빵을 떼어 먹는 동안, 나는 곰팡이 슨 딱딱한 빵 껍질이나 씹었어 — 저 어린 새색시에게서 멀리, 온 바다만큼이나 멀리 떨어져서 말이야. 쉰을 넘겨 맞아들인 그 색시를, 결혼한 이튿날 케이프 혼을 향해 떠나며, 신혼의 베개에 옴폭 들어간 자국 하나만 남겨 두고 떠나왔지 — 아내라고? 아내? 차라리 남편이 살아 있는 과부라 해야겠지! 그래, 나는 그 가련한 처녀와 혼인하는 그 순간에 그 애를 과부로 만들어 버린 거야, 스타벅. 그러고 나서, 그 광기, 그 광란, 그 끓어오르는 피와 김 서린 이마로, 천 번이나 보트를 내리며 늙은 에이해브는 미친 듯이, 게거품을 물고 제 먹잇감을 뒤쫓았어 — 사람이라기보다 악마에 가까운 꼴로! 그래, 그래! 사십 년을 얼마나 바보로 — 바보로 — 늙은 바보로 살아온 거냐, 이 늙은 에이해브가! 어찌하여 이 추격의 싸움을 벌이느냐? 어찌하여 노와 작살과 창을 쥔 팔을 지치게 하고 마비시키느냐? 지금의 에이해브가 그래서 무엇이 더 부유해지고 더 나아졌단 말이냐? 보라. 오, 스타벅! 가혹하지 않으냐, 이 고단한 짐을 진 채로 그 가련한 한쪽 다리마저 내 몸 밑에서 채여 가 버렸다는 게? 자, 이 늙은 머리칼을 좀 쓸어 넘겨 다오. 눈을 가려서, 마치 내가 우는 것처럼 보이게 하는구나. 이렇게 잿빛이 된 머리칼은 어떤 잿더미에서가 아니고서야 결코 돋아나지 않는 법이지! 그런데 내가 정말 늙어 보이느냐, 아주, 아주 늙어 보이느냐, 스타벅? 죽을 만큼 기운이 빠지고, 등이 굽고 곱사등이 된 듯한 기분이야, 마치 내가 아담이라도 된 양, 낙원 이래로 쌓여 온 세월을 짊어지고 비틀거리는 듯해. 하느님! 하느님! 하느님! 내 심장을 쪼개 주소서! 내 머리를 부숴 주소서! 조롱이로구나! 조롱이야! 잿빛 머리칼의 쓰디쓴, 살을 에는 조롱이여, 내가 너희를 두를 만큼 충분한 기쁨을 누리며 살아왔던가, 이토록 견딜 수 없으리만치 늙어 보이고 또 그렇게 느껴지도록? 가까이! 내게 바싹 다가서라, 스타벅. 사람의 눈을 들여다보게 해 다오. 바다나 하늘을 바라보는 것보다 낫고, 하느님을 우러러보는 것보다 낫구나. 저 푸른 뭍을 두고, 저 환한 화롯가의 돌을 두고 맹세하노니! 이것이야말로 마법의 거울이야, 이 사람아. 네 눈 속에서 나는 내 아내와 내 자식을 본다. 아니, 아니. 배에 남아 있어라, 배에! 내가 보트를 내릴 때 너는 내리지 마라. 낙인찍힌 에이해브가 모비 딕을 뒤쫓을 때 말이다. 그 위험은 네 몫이 되어선 안 돼. 아니, 아니! 그 눈 속에서 내가 보는 저 머나먼 고향을 두고서라도, 안 될 일이야!\"\n\n\"오, 선장님! 선장님! 고귀한 영혼이여! 그래도 역시 위대한 노장의 마음이시여! 어찌하여 누구든 그 가증스러운 물고기를 뒤쫓아야 한단 말입니까! 저와 함께 떠나시지요! 이 죽음의 바다에서 달아납시다! 고향으로 돌아갑시다! 아내와 자식은 이 스타벅에게도 있습니다 — 형제처럼, 누이처럼, 소꿉동무처럼 함께 자란 젊은 날의 아내와 자식이지요. 선장님께도 자애롭고 그리움에 찬 아버지로서의 노년에 둔 아내와 자식이 있는 것처럼 말입니다! 떠납시다! 우리 떠나요! 지금 당장 항로를 바꾸게 해 주십시오! 오, 선장님, 우리가 그리운 낸터킷을 다시 보러 나아가는 길이 얼마나 즐겁고, 얼마나 흥겨울지요! 제 생각엔, 선장님, 낸터킷에도 이렇게 온화하고 푸른 날들이, 바로 오늘 같은 날들이 더러 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"있고말고, 있고말고. 나도 본 적이 있어 — 여름날 아침나절에 말이야. 지금 이맘때쯤이면 — 그래, 지금이 바로 그 아이의 낮잠 시간이겠지 — 그 녀석이 생기 넘치게 깨어나, 잠자리에서 일어나 앉으면, 제 어미가 나에 관해 일러 주겠지, 식인종 같은 이 늙은 나에 관해. 내가 어찌 저 먼 바다에 나가 있는지, 그래도 언젠가는 돌아와 다시 안고 춤춰 줄 거라고 말이야.\"\n\n\"바로 제 메리예요, 제 메리가 틀림없습니다! 제 아내는 약속했지요, 우리 아이를 아침마다 언덕으로 데려가 제 아비의 돛을 맨 먼저 보게 해 주겠다고요! 그래요, 그래요! 더는 안 됩니다! 끝났어요! 낸터킷으로 뱃머리를 돌립시다! 자, 선장님, 항로를 잡으세요, 그리고 떠납시다! 보세요, 보세요! 창문 너머 그 아이의 얼굴이! 언덕 위 그 아이의 손이!\"\n\n그러나 에이해브의 시선은 딴 데로 돌려져 있었다. 그는 시들어 버린 과일나무처럼 몸을 떨며, 마지막으로 남은 잿빛 사과 한 알을 땅으로 떨구었다.\n\n\"이것은 대체 무엇인가, 이름 붙일 수도 없고, 헤아릴 수도 없고, 이 세상 것 같지도 않은 이것은 무엇인가. 어떤 교활하고 숨은 군주이자 주인이, 어떤 잔인하고 무자비한 황제가 나를 호령하기에, 온갖 자연스러운 사랑과 그리움을 거스르고서 나를 이토록 끊임없이 떠밀고, 몰아대고, 짓눌러 가며, 내 본디의 자연스러운 마음으로는 감히 시도조차 못 할 일을 무모하게 해치우도록 채비시키는가? 에이해브는 에이해브인가? 이 팔을 들어 올리는 것은 나인가, 하느님인가, 아니면 누구인가? 그러나 저 거대한 태양조차 스스로 움직이는 게 아니라 하늘의 심부름꾼 소년에 지나지 않고, 단 하나의 별도 어떤 보이지 않는 힘에 의하지 않고서는 돌지 못한다면, 그렇다면 이 작은 심장 하나가 어찌 뛰며, 이 작은 뇌 하나가 어찌 생각을 떠올릴 수 있단 말인가. 하느님이 그 박동을 뛰게 하고, 그 생각을 하게 하고, 그 삶을 살게 하는 것이지, 내가 하는 것이 아니라면 말이야. 하늘에 맹세코, 이 사람아, 우리는 이 세상에서 저기 저 양묘기처럼 빙빙 돌려질 뿐이고, 운명이야말로 그 가로장이지. 그런데 보라, 그 내내 저 미소 짓는 하늘과 이 깊이 모를 바다라니! 보라! 저 다랑어를 보아라! 저 녀석에게 누가 저 날치를 뒤쫓아 물어뜯으라 일러 주었느냐? 살인자들은 어디로 가는가, 이 사람아! 재판관 자신이 피고석으로 끌려 나오는 마당에, 대체 누가 단죄를 한단 말이냐? 그러나 온화하구나, 온화한 바람이야, 그리고 온화한 빛깔의 하늘이고. 게다가 대기에서는 지금, 마치 머나먼 풀밭에서 불어온 듯한 냄새가 나는구나. 누군가 안데스 산비탈 어딘가에서 건초를 만들고 있었나 보다, 스타벅, 그리고 풀 베는 이들은 갓 베어 낸 건초 더미 사이에서 잠들어 있겠지. 잠들었다고? 그래, 우리가 아무리 애써 일한들, 우리 모두 결국엔 들판에 누워 잠들지. 잠든다고? 그래, 그리고 푸름 속에서 녹슬어 가는 거야. 작년에 내던져진 낫들이 반쯤 베다 만 풀밭에 그대로 버려진 것처럼 말이야 — 스타벅!\"\n\n그러나 절망으로 시신의 빛깔이 되도록 핏기가 가신 일등항해사는, 어느새 슬그머니 사라지고 없었다.\n\n에이해브는 갑판을 가로질러 반대편 너머를 바라보았다. 그러나 거기 물속에 비친, 한곳에 고정된 두 눈에 흠칫 놀랐다. 페달라가 같은 난간에 미동도 없이 기대어 서 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 133. The Chase—First Day.\n\n그날 밤, 한밤중 당직 시간에, 노인은 — 이따금 그러하듯 — 자신이 기대어 있던 승강구에서 걸어 나와 자기 회전 구멍 자리로 갔다. 그때 그는 돌연 얼굴을 사납게 내밀고는, 야만의 섬에 다가갈 때 영리한 배 안의 개가 그러하듯 바다 공기를 코로 들이켰다. 그는 고래가 가까이 있는 게 분명하다고 단언했다. 곧 살아 있는 향유고래가 이따금 먼 거리에까지 풍기는 그 특유의 냄새가 당직 선원 모두에게 또렷이 느껴졌다. 그리고 에이해브가 나침반을 살피고, 이어 풍향계를 보고, 그런 다음 가능한 한 정확히 냄새의 방향을 가늠한 뒤 재빨리 배의 항로를 약간 바꾸고 돛을 줄이라 명했을 때, 그 누구도 놀라지 않았다.\n\n이런 동작들을 지시한 예리한 판단은 동틀 무렵에 충분히 입증되었다. 바로 앞바다 위로 길게 뻗은 매끄러운 자국이 보였던 것이다. 그것은 기름처럼 반들거렸고, 그 가장자리를 두른 잔주름진 물결무늬가 마치 깊고 빠른 강 어귀에서 일어나는 거센 조류의, 잘 닦은 금속 같은 자취를 닮아 있었다.\n\n\"돛대 꼭대기로 올라가라! 전원 집합!\"\n\n대거가 곤봉처럼 굵은 손잡이 막대 세 개의 밑동으로 앞갑판을 천둥처럼 두드려 잠든 자들을 깨우니, 그 단호한 타격에 그들은 마치 승강구에서 토해진 듯 옷가지를 손에 든 채 순식간에 나타났다.\n\n\"뭐가 보이느냐?\" 에이해브가 얼굴을 하늘로 바싹 쳐들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아무것도, 아무것도 없습니다!\" 위에서 응답하는 소리가 내려왔다.\n\n\"중간 돛대 윗돛을 펴라! 보조돛도! 아래위로, 양쪽 다!\"\n\n돛이 모두 펴지자, 그는 자신을 주(主) 최상부 돛대 꼭대기로 끌어올리는 데 쓰는 구명줄을 풀어 던졌다. 그러자 잠시 후 그들이 그를 그곳으로 끌어올리기 시작했고, 위로 삼분의 이쯤 올라갔을 무렵, 주 중간돛과 그 윗돛 사이의 수평으로 트인 공간 너머를 응시하던 그가 갈매기 같은 외침을 허공에 띄웠다. \"저기 물을 뿜는다! 저기 뿜는다! 눈 덮인 언덕 같은 혹이다! 모비 딕이다!\"\n\n세 명의 망꾼이 동시에 받아 외치는 듯한 그 고함에 불이 붙은 갑판 위의 선원들은, 그토록 오래 쫓아온 그 이름난 고래를 보려고 삭구로 달려들었다. 에이해브는 이제 마지막 자리, 다른 망꾼들보다 몇 자쯤 더 높은 곳에 이르렀다. 태시테고가 바로 그 아래 최상부 돛대 꼭대기의 캡에 서 있었으므로, 그 인디언의 머리는 에이해브의 발꿈치와 거의 같은 높이였다. 이 높이에서 보니 고래는 이제 한 마일쯤 앞에 있었고, 바다가 굽이칠 때마다 반짝이는 높은 혹을 드러내며 규칙적으로 소리 없는 물줄기를 허공에 뿜어 올렸다. 잘 믿는 선원들에게는 그것이 오래전 달빛 어린 대서양과 인도양에서 보았던 바로 그 소리 없는 물줄기처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n\"너희 중 누구도 그것을 먼저 보지 못했단 말이냐?\" 에이해브가 사방에 올라가 있는 자들을 향해 외쳤다.\n\n\"에이해브 선장님께서 보신 거의 그 순간에 저도 그를 보았고, 소리쳤습니다.\" 태시테고가 말했다.\n\n\"같은 순간이 아니다. 같은 순간이 아니야 — 그래, 그 금화는 내 것이다. 운명이 그 금화를 내 몫으로 남겨 둔 것이다. 오직 나만이! 너희 중 누구도 흰 고래를 먼저 발견할 수 없었다. 저기 물을 뿜는다! 저기 뿜는다! 저기 뿜는다! 또 저기다! 또 저기!\" 그는 길게 끌며, 여운을 남기는 절도 있는 어조로, 고래가 눈에 보이게 점점 길게 뿜어 올리는 물줄기에 맞추어 외쳤다. \"잠수하려 한다! 보조돛을 거둬라! 윗돛을 내려라! 보트 세 척, 대기하라. 스타벅, 명심하게, 자네는 배에 남아 배를 지켜라. 키잡이! 바람 쪽으로 돌려라, 한 점만! 그래, 침착하게, 침착하게! 저기 꼬리지느러미가 간다! 아니, 아니, 그저 검은 물일 뿐이다! 보트들 준비됐나? 대기, 대기하라! 나를 내려라, 스타벅. 내려, 내려라 — 빨리, 더 빨리!\" 그러고는 허공을 미끄러져 갑판으로 내려왔다.\n\n\"녀석은 곧장 바람 아래쪽으로 향하고 있습니다, 선장님.\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"우리에게서 곧장 멀어집니다. 아직 배를 보지 못한 모양입니다.\"\n\n\"입 닥쳐라! 활대 밧줄을 잡아라! 키를 한껏 내려라! 활대를 당겨라! 돛을 떨게 하라! 떨게 하라! 그래, 잘했다! 보트, 보트로!\"\n\n곧 스타벅의 것만 빼고 모든 보트가 내려졌다. 보트의 돛이 모두 펼쳐지고 — 노가 모두 움직이기 시작했다. 잔물결 일으키는 날쌤으로, 바람 아래쪽을 향해 쏜살같이 내달렸다. 에이해브가 그 돌격의 선두에 섰다. 페달라의 푹 꺼진 두 눈에 창백한, 죽음 같은 빛이 어른거렸다. 흉측한 경련이 그의 입을 갉아먹었다.\n\n소리 없는 앵무조개 껍질처럼, 그들의 가벼운 뱃머리가 바다를 가르며 내달렸다. 그러나 적에게 다가가는 속도는 더디기만 했다. 그들이 가까워질수록 바다는 한층 더 잔잔해져, 마치 물결 위에 양탄자를 펼쳐 깔아 놓은 듯했고, 정오의 풀밭처럼 고요하게 펼쳐졌다. 마침내 숨죽인 사냥꾼은, 아무것도 눈치채지 못한 듯한 그 먹잇감에 바싹 다가갔다. 그리하여 눈부신 혹 전체가 또렷이 보였는데, 그것은 마치 외따로 떨어진 물체처럼 바다 위를 미끄러져 갔고, 더없이 곱고 양털 같은 연둣빛 거품의 도는 고리 속에 끊임없이 둘러싸여 있었다. 그는 그 너머로 약간 솟아오른 머리의 거대하고 뒤얽힌 주름들을 보았다. 그 앞으로, 부드러운 터키 양탄자 같은 물 위 저 멀리, 넓고 젖빛 도는 이마에서 비롯된 번쩍이는 흰 그림자가 나아갔고, 음악 같은 잔물결이 그 그림자를 장난스레 뒤따랐다. 그리고 뒤로는 푸른 물이 서로 번갈아 흘러들어 그 한결같은 항적이 이루는 움직이는 골짜기를 채웠으며, 양옆으로는 밝은 거품이 일어나 그 곁에서 춤추었다. 그러나 이 거품들은, 부드럽게 바다를 깃털로 쓰다듬듯 변덕스럽게 날아오르는 수백 마리 흥겨운 물새의 가벼운 발끝에 다시 부서졌다. 그리고 큰 상선의 그림 그려진 선체에서 솟은 깃대처럼, 최근에 박힌 창의 부러진 긴 자루가 흰 고래의 등에서 삐죽 솟아 있었고, 이따금 그 부드러운 발의 물새 떼 중 한 마리가, 고기 위를 차일처럼 이리저리 스치며 맴돌다가, 소리 없이 이 자루에 내려앉아 흔들거렸으니, 그 긴 꽁지깃이 깃발처럼 나부꼈다.\n\n부드러운 환희가 — 날쌤 속에 깃든 위엄 있는 온화한 평정이 미끄러져 가는 고래를 감싸고 있었다. 납치한 에우로페가 그 우아한 뿔에 매달린 채 헤엄쳐 가던 흰 황소 유피테르도 — 처녀를 곁눈질로 응시하는 그 사랑스럽고 음흉한 눈으로, 매끄럽고 매혹적인 날쌤으로 크레타의 혼례 침실을 향해 잔물결 일으키며 곧장 나아가던 그 황소도 — 유피테르도, 저 위대하고 지엄한 최고의 신조차도, 그토록 신성하게 헤엄치는 영광에 싸인 흰 고래를 능가하지 못했다.\n\n그 부드러운 양옆에서 — 한 번 그를 떠나면 그토록 넓게 흘러가 버리는 갈라진 너울과 어울려 — 그 빛나는 양옆에서, 고래는 유혹의 손길을 흩뿌렸다. 이 모든 고요함에 형언할 수 없이 도취되고 홀린 나머지, 감히 그것을 공격하려 들었던 사냥꾼들이 더러 있었던 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그러나 그들은 그 정적이 다름 아닌 폭풍의 겉옷임을 죽음으로써 깨달았던 것이다. 그럼에도 고요하구나, 유혹하는 고요함이여, 오 고래여! 처음으로 너를 보는 모든 이에게, 너는 미끄러져 나아간다 — 네가 그토록 똑같은 방식으로 전에 얼마나 많은 자들을 홀려 파멸시켰든 아랑곳없이.\n\n이리하여, 열대 바다의 잔잔한 고요 속을, 더없는 황홀에 손뼉 치기를 멈춘 물결 사이를, 모비 딕은 나아갔다. 여전히 물에 잠긴 몸통의 온전한 공포를 시야에서 거두어들이고, 그 비틀린 흉측한 턱을 완전히 감춘 채로. 그러나 곧 그의 앞부분이 물에서 천천히 솟아올랐다. 한순간 대리석 같은 몸 전체가 버지니아의 자연 다리처럼 높은 아치를 이루었고, 깃발 같은 꼬리지느러미를 경고하듯 허공에 흔들며, 그 위대한 신은 제 모습을 드러내고는 잠수하여 시야에서 사라졌다. 흰 바닷새들은 맴돌며 멈칫거리다 날개를 담그듯 내려앉아, 그가 남긴 출렁이는 물웅덩이 위를 그리운 듯 서성거렸다.\n\n노를 곧추세우고, 짧은 노를 내리고, 돛줄을 풀어 둔 채, 세 척의 보트는 이제 고요히 떠 있었다. 모비 딕이 다시 나타나기를 기다리며.\n\n\"한 시간이다.\" 에이해브가 자기 보트의 고물에 뿌리박은 듯 서서 말했다. 그러고는 고래가 있던 자리 너머, 바람 아래쪽으로 어슴푸레 푸른 공간들과, 넓게 손짓하는 듯한 텅 빈 곳들을 응시했다. 그것은 한순간일 뿐이었다. 다시 그의 두 눈은 그 물의 원을 휘둘러보면서 머릿속에서 빙글빙글 돌아가는 듯했다. 이제 산들바람이 거세졌고, 바다가 부풀어 오르기 시작했다.\n\n\"새다! 새들이다!\" 태시테고가 외쳤다.\n\n왜가리들이 날아오를 때처럼 길게 인디언 행렬을 이루어, 흰 새들이 이제 모두 에이해브의 보트를 향해 날아오고 있었다. 그리고 몇 야드 안쪽에 이르자 그곳 물 위를 퍼덕이며 빙글빙글 맴돌기 시작했으니, 기쁘고 기대에 찬 울음을 토하며 그러했다. 그들의 시각은 인간의 것보다 예리했다. 에이해브는 바다에서 아무 기척도 찾아낼 수 없었다. 그러나 문득, 그가 그 깊디깊은 곳을 응시하던 차에, 그는 저 깊은 데서 흰 족제비만 한 크기의 살아 있는 흰 점을 어렴풋이 보았다. 그것은 놀라운 속도로 솟아올라, 솟으면서 점점 커지더니, 마침내 몸을 돌리자, 알 수 없는 바닥에서 떠오르는 길고 굽은 두 줄의 희고 번쩍이는 이빨이 또렷이 드러났다. 그것은 모비 딕의 벌린 입과 둘둘 말린 턱이었다. 그 거대하고 그늘진 몸뚱이는 여전히 바다의 푸른빛과 반쯤 뒤섞여 있었다. 번쩍이는 입이 문 열린 대리석 무덤처럼 보트 아래에서 아가리를 벌렸다. 에이해브는 키 잡는 노로 한 번 옆으로 휘저어, 이 어마어마한 환영에서 배를 비켜 돌렸다. 그러고는 페달라에게 자리를 바꾸라 명하고, 앞으로 나아가 뱃머리로 가서, 퍼스의 작살을 움켜쥐고는, 선원들에게 노를 잡고 고물 쪽으로 대기하라 명했다.\n\n이제, 이렇게 때맞추어 보트를 그 축을 중심으로 돌려놓은 까닭에, 그 뱃머리는 미리 헤아린 대로 아직 물속에 있는 고래의 머리를 마주하게 되었다. 그러나 마치 이 술책을 알아챈 듯이, 모비 딕은 그에게 돌려진 그 음흉한 지능으로, 이를테면 순식간에 몸을 옆으로 옮겨, 주름진 머리를 보트 아래로 길게 들이밀었다.\n\n뚫고 또 뚫고, 모든 널빤지와 늑재 하나하나를 관통하며, 한순간 그것은 부르르 떨었다. 고래가 비스듬히 등을 대고 누워, 물어뜯는 상어처럼, 천천히 그리고 느낌을 음미하듯 보트의 뱃머리를 입속에 통째로 받아들이니, 길고 좁다란 둘둘 말린 아래턱이 트인 허공으로 높이 말려 올라가고, 이빨 하나가 노받이에 걸렸다. 턱 안쪽의 푸르스름한 진주빛 흰 면이 에이해브의 머리에서 여섯 인치 거리에 있었고, 그보다 더 높이까지 닿았다. 이런 자세로 흰 고래는, 짐짓 잔인한 고양이가 쥐를 다루듯, 그 가냘픈 삼나무 보트를 흔들었다. 페달라는 놀라움 없는 눈으로 응시하며 팔짱을 꼈다. 그러나 호랑이처럼 누런 선원들은 서로의 머리를 타고 넘으며 고물 맨 끝으로 몰려갔다.\n\n그리고 이제, 탄력 있는 양쪽 뱃전이 안팎으로 튀어 오르는 동안, 고래가 이 악마 같은 방식으로 운명 지어진 배를 희롱하는 동안, 그리고 그의 몸이 보트 아래에 잠겨 있던 탓에 뱃머리에서 그를 향해 작살을 던질 수 없었으니 — 뱃머리가 말하자면 거의 그의 몸속에 들어가 있었기 때문이다 — 다른 보트들이, 도저히 맞설 수 없는 급박한 위기를 앞둔 듯 저도 모르게 멈춰 선 동안, 그때였다, 편집광 에이해브가, 자기 적이 이토록 약 올리듯 가까이 있는 데에 격분하여 — 자신이 그토록 증오하던 바로 그 아가리 안에 산 채로 무력하게 놓이게 되자 — 이 모든 것에 광란하여, 맨손으로 그 긴 뼈를 움켜쥐고, 그것을 그 물린 데서 비틀어 떼어 내려 미친 듯 안간힘을 썼다. 그가 이렇게 헛되이 애쓰는 동안, 턱이 그의 손에서 미끄러졌다. 약한 뱃전이 안으로 휘어지더니, 무너지며 뚝 부러졌고, 두 턱이 거대한 가위처럼 더 뒤쪽으로 미끄러지며 배를 완전히 둘로 물어 끊고는, 두 동강 난 잔해 사이 한가운데서 다시 바다 속에 굳게 맞물렸다. 잔해들은 부러진 끝을 늘어뜨린 채 옆으로 떠내려갔고, 고물 쪽 잔해에 매달린 선원들은 뱃전을 붙들고서, 노를 단단히 붙잡아 가로질러 묶으려 애썼다.\n\n그 서막의 순간, 보트가 아직 부러지기 전, 고래가 교활하게 머리를 치켜드는 것을 보고 — 그 동작에 잠시 물린 힘이 느슨해졌는데 — 그 의도를 맨 먼저 알아챈 에이해브는, 바로 그 순간 손으로 보트를 그 물린 데서 밀어내려 마지막 힘을 다해 보았다. 그러나 보트는 고래의 입속으로 더 깊이 미끄러져 들어갈 뿐이었고, 미끄러지면서 옆으로 기울어져, 그가 밀려고 몸을 기울인 차에, 보트는 그가 턱을 붙잡은 손을 떨쳐 버리고 그를 거기서 쏟아 냈으니, 그리하여 그는 얼굴을 처박은 채 바다 위로 떨어졌다.\n\n먹잇감에게서 잔물결을 일으키며 물러난 모비 딕은, 이제 약간 떨어진 곳에 누워, 길쭉한 흰 머리를 너울 속에서 수직으로 위아래로 내밀었다. 동시에 그 방추형 몸 전체를 천천히 돌렸으니, 그리하여 그의 거대한 주름진 이마가 물 위로 스무 자 남짓 솟아오르자, 이제 부풀어 오르던 너울이, 한데 합류한 온 물결을 거느리고, 눈부시게 그 이마에 부딪쳐 부서지며, 산산이 흩어진 물보라를 앙갚음하듯 더 높이 허공으로 내던졌다. 이리하여, 폭풍 속에서, 절반쯤만 가로막힌 해협의 너울이 에디스톤 등대의 밑동에서 그저 물러났다가, 그 물보라로 의기양양하게 그 꼭대기를 뛰어넘는 것이다.\n\n* 이 동작은 향유고래 특유의 것이다. 이것은 앞서 설명한 피치폴링이라는 동작에서, 고래잡이 창을 던지기에 앞서 위아래로 가누어 균형 잡는 예비 자세에 빗대어 그 이름(피치폴링)을 얻었다. 이 동작으로 고래는 자기를 둘러싼 어떤 대상이든 가장 잘, 그리고 가장 폭넓게 살필 수 있는 것이다.\n\n그러나 곧 수평 자세를 되찾은 모비 딕은, 난파한 선원들 주위를 빙글빙글 빠르게 헤엄쳤다. 복수심에 찬 항적 속에서 옆으로 물을 휘저으니, 마치 또 한 번의, 더욱 치명적인 공격을 위해 스스로를 채찍질하는 듯했다. 산산조각 난 보트를 보자 그는 미쳐 날뛰는 듯했으니, 마치 마카베오기에서 안티오코스의 코끼리들 앞에 뿌려진 포도와 오디의 즙이 그러했듯 했다. 그러는 동안 에이해브는 고래의 오만한 꼬리가 일으킨 거품 속에 반쯤 파묻혀 있었고, 헤엄치기에는 너무도 성치 못한 몸이었으나 — 그래도 그런 소용돌이의 한복판에서조차 여전히 떠 있을 수는 있었다 — 무력한 에이해브의 머리는, 작은 충격에도 터져 버릴 듯 내던져진 거품 방울처럼 보였다. 부서진 보트의 고물 조각에서, 페달라는 무심하고 차분한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 다른 쪽 떠내려가는 끝에 매달린 선원들은 그를 구할 수 없었으니, 제 한 몸 건사하는 것만으로도 벅찼던 것이다. 흰 고래의 모습은 그토록 빙빙 돌며 소름 끼쳤고, 점점 좁혀 드는 그 원은 행성처럼 빨라서, 그가 마치 수평으로 그들을 덮쳐 오는 듯했기 때문이다. 그리고 무사한 다른 보트들이 여전히 바로 곁에서 맴돌고 있었으나, 그래도 감히 소용돌이 안으로 노 저어 들어가 작살을 던지지는 못했으니, 그것이 위태로운 처지의 조난자들, 곧 에이해브와 모두를 그 자리에서 파멸시키는 신호가 될까 두려웠던 것이다. 게다가 그렇게 되면 그들 자신도 빠져나오기를 바랄 수 없었다. 그리하여 그들은 핏발 선 눈으로, 그 무서운 구역의 바깥 가장자리에 머물러 있었으니, 그 중심은 이제 노인의 머리가 되어 있었다.\n\n그러는 동안, 이 모든 일은 처음부터 배의 돛대 꼭대기에서 내려다보이고 있었다. 배는 활대를 가지런히 돌려 그 현장으로 밀고 내려왔고, 이제는 어찌나 가까웠던지, 물속의 에이해브가 배를 향해 외쳐 부를 정도였다. \"고래 위로 배를…….\" 그러나 그 순간 모비 딕에게서 부서진 파도가 그를 덮쳐, 잠시 그를 휩쓸어 버렸다. 그래도 그는 다시 그것을 헤치고 나와, 우연히 우뚝 솟은 물마루 위로 떠오른 차에 외쳤다. \"고래 위로 배를 몰아라! 그를 쫓아내라!\"\n\n피쿼드호의 뱃머리가 겨냥을 정했다. 그리고 마법에 걸린 듯한 그 원을 깨뜨리며, 배는 흰 고래를 그 희생물로부터 효과적으로 갈라놓았다. 고래가 시무룩하게 헤엄쳐 가는 동안, 보트들이 구조하러 날아왔다.\n\n핏발 서 앞이 보이지 않는 눈으로, 주름 사이에 흰 소금이 엉겨 붙은 채, 스터브의 보트로 끌어 올려진 에이해브는, 그토록 오래 팽팽히 당겨졌던 육체의 힘이 끝내 툭 끊어져, 무력하게 제 몸의 운명에 굴복하고 말았다. 한동안 그는 코끼리 떼의 발에 짓밟힌 자처럼, 스터브의 보트 바닥에 짓이겨진 채 누워 있었다. 그에게서 형언할 수 없는 통곡이, 마치 깊은 골짜기에서 새어 나오는 황량한 소리처럼, 저 먼 내륙으로부터 들려오는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 그의 육체가 이토록 격렬하게 무너졌다는 사실은, 도리어 그만큼 더 그것을 짧게 줄였을 뿐이다. 한순간의 폭 안에, 위대한 심장은 이따금 하나의 깊은 격통으로 응축되니, 그것은 더 연약한 인간들의 평생에 걸쳐 어질게 흩뿌려지는 그 얕은 고통들의 총합이다. 그리하여 그런 심장들은, 비록 저마다의 고통에서는 한순간에 그칠지라도, 신들이 그리 정한다면, 그 생애 동안 한 시대 전체에 맞먹는 비애를 그러모으니, 그것은 온통 순간적인 격렬함으로 이루어진 것이다. 그 무딘 중심 속에서조차, 그 고귀한 천성들은 못한 영혼들의 둘레 전체를 품고 있기 때문이다.\n\n\"작살은,\" 에이해브가 반쯤 몸을 일으키며, 굽힌 한쪽 팔에 힘겹게 기대어 말했다. \"무사한가?\"\n\n\"예, 선장님, 던지지 않았으니까요. 여기 있습니다.\" 스터브가 그것을 내보이며 말했다.\n\n\"내 앞에 놓아라. 잃은 사람은 없나?\"\n\n\"하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯 — 노가 다섯이었고, 여기 사람도 다섯입니다.\"\n\n\"좋다. 나를 부축하게. 일어서고 싶다. 그래, 그래, 그가 보인다! 저기! 저기! 여전히 바람 아래쪽으로 가는구나. 저 솟구치는 물줄기 좀 보라! 내게서 손을 떼라! 영원한 수액이 에이해브의 뼛속에 다시 차오른다! 돛을 펴라! 노를 내라! 키를 잡아라!\"\n\n보트가 부서지면, 그 선원들이 다른 보트에 구조되어 그 두 번째 보트를 함께 젓는 일이 흔히 있다. 그리하여 이른바 이중 배치된 노로 추격이 이어지는 것이다. 지금이 바로 그러했다. 그러나 보트에 더해진 힘은 고래에게 더해진 힘에 미치지 못했으니, 고래는 마치 모든 지느러미를 세 배로 늘려 배치한 듯, 빠른 속도로 헤엄쳤기 때문이다. 그 속도는, 만약 이런 상황에서 추격을 계속 밀어붙인다면, 그것이 절망적이지는 않더라도 한없이 길어지리라는 것을 분명히 보여 주었다. 또한 어떤 선원도 그토록 오랜 시간, 그토록 쉼 없이 격렬하게 노에 힘을 쥐어짜는 것을 견뎌 낼 수 없었으니, 그것은 어떤 짧은 한 차례의 위기에서나 겨우 참아 낼 만한 일이었다. 그러므로 이따금 그러하듯, 배 자체가 추격을 따라잡는 가장 유망한 중간 수단이 되어 주었다. 이에 따라 보트들은 이제 배를 향해 나아갔고, 곧 기중기에 끌어 올려졌다 — 부서진 보트의 두 조각은 앞서 배에 매어 둔 터였다. 그런 다음 모든 것을 뱃전으로 끌어 올리고, 돛을 높이 쌓아 올리고, 보조돛으로 옆으로 펼쳤으니, 그것은 마치 신천옹의 두 마디 접힌 날개와도 같았다. 그리하여 피쿼드호는 모비 딕의 바람 아래쪽 항적을 따라 밀고 내려갔다. 잘 알려진 규칙적인 간격마다, 사람을 배치한 돛대 꼭대기에서 고래의 번쩍이는 물줄기가 규칙적으로 알려졌다. 그리고 그가 막 잠수했다고 보고되면, 에이해브는 시간을 재었다. 그러고는 갑판을 거닐며, 나침함 시계를 손에 든 채, 정해진 한 시간의 마지막 초가 지나가자마자 그의 목소리가 들려왔다. \"이제 그 금화는 누구 것이냐? 그가 보이느냐?\" 그리고 만약 응답이 \"아닙니다, 선장님!\" 하는 것이면, 그는 즉시 자기를 망대로 끌어 올리라 명했다. 이런 식으로 하루가 저물어 갔다. 에이해브는 어느 때는 망대 위에서 미동도 없이, 또 어느 때는 쉴 새 없이 갑판을 거닐었다.\n\n이렇게 거니는 동안, 그는 망대 위의 사람들을 부르거나, 돛을 더 높이 올리라 이르거나, 더 넓게 펴라 명하는 것 말고는 아무 소리도 내지 않았다. 이렇게 이리저리 거닐며, 푹 눌러쓴 모자 아래로, 돌아설 때마다 그는 후갑판에 내려놓은 자신의 부서진 보트 곁을 지났으니, 그것은 거기 뒤집힌 채로 — 부러진 뱃머리가 박살 난 고물을 향한 채로 — 놓여 있었다. 마침내 그는 그 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그리고 이미 잔뜩 흐린 하늘 위로 이따금 새로운 구름 떼가 가로질러 흘러가듯, 노인의 얼굴 위로 이제 그런 식의 어떤 어둠이 더하여 슬그머니 깃들었다.\n\n스터브는 그가 멈춰 서는 것을 보았다. 그리고 아마도, 헛되지 않게, 자신의 꺾이지 않은 불굴의 기개를 드러내어 선장의 마음속에서 용감한 자리를 지켜 두려는 뜻이었던지, 그가 다가와 잔해를 바라보며 외쳤다. \"당나귀가 마다한 엉겅퀴군요. 그놈의 입을 너무 따끔하게 찔렀으니까요, 선장님. 하하! 하하!\"\n\n\"난파선 앞에서 웃다니, 이 영혼 없는 물건은 대체 뭐란 말이냐? 이 사람아, 이 사람아! 내가 자네를 두려움 모르는 불처럼 용감하다고 (또 그처럼 기계적이라고) 알지 못했더라면, 자네를 비겁한 겁쟁이라 단언했을 것이다. 난파선 앞에서는 신음도 웃음도 들려서는 안 되는 법이다.\"\n\n\"예, 선장님,\" 스타벅이 다가오며 말했다. \"이건 엄숙한 광경입니다. 하나의 징조, 그것도 불길한 징조지요.\"\n\n\"징조? 징조라고? 그딴 건 사전에나 있는 말이다! 신들이 인간에게 대놓고 말하려 한다면, 그들은 떳떳이 대놓고 말할 것이다. 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 노파들의 어두운 암시 따위나 주지는 않는다. 물러가라! 너희 둘은 한 가지 것의 정반대 양극이다. 스타벅은 뒤집힌 스터브요, 스터브는 스타벅이다. 그리고 너희 둘이 곧 온 인류다. 그리고 에이해브는 이 사람 사는 땅 위 수백만 가운데 홀로 서 있으니, 신도 인간도 그의 이웃이 아니다! 춥다, 추워 — 몸이 떨리는구나! 어찌 된 일이냐? 거기 망대 위! 그가 보이느냐? 물줄기를 뿜을 때마다 외쳐라, 그가 일 초에 열 번을 뿜더라도!\"\n\n하루가 거의 저물어, 다만 그 황금빛 옷자락만이 바스락거릴 뿐이었다. 곧 거의 어두워졌으나, 망꾼들은 여전히 자리에서 내려오지 않았다.\n\n\"이제 물줄기가 보이지 않습니다, 선장님 — 너무 어둡습니다.\" 허공에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"마지막으로 보았을 때 어느 쪽으로 향하고 있었나?\"\n\n\"전과 같습니다, 선장님 — 곧장 바람 아래쪽입니다.\"\n\n\"좋다! 밤이 되었으니 이제 더 느리게 갈 것이다. 최상부 돛과 윗돛의 보조돛을 내려라, 스타벅. 아침이 되기 전에 그를 지나쳐 버려서는 안 된다. 그는 지금 한 항로를 가는 중이니, 잠시 멈춰 표류할지도 모른다. 키잡이! 바람을 가득 안고 곧장 나아가게 하라! 망대 위! 내려오라! 스터브, 앞돛대 꼭대기에 교대 인원을 보내, 아침까지 사람을 배치해 두게.\" 그러고는 큰 돛대의 금화를 향해 다가서며 말했다. \"제군들, 이 금은 내 것이다, 내가 그것을 벌었으니까. 그러나 흰 고래가 죽을 때까지 나는 그것을 여기 그대로 두겠다. 그런 다음, 그가 죽임당하는 그날에, 너희 중 누구든 그를 가장 먼저 발견하는 자, 그 사람의 것이 이 금이다. 그리고 만약 그날 내가 다시 그를 발견하게 되면, 그때는 이 금의 열 배에 해당하는 액수를 너희 모두에게 나누어 주겠다! 자, 이제 가라! 갑판은 자네 것일세, 선장!\"\n\n이렇게 말하고서, 그는 승강구 안에 반쯤 몸을 들이고, 모자를 푹 눌러쓴 채, 동틀 녘까지 거기 서 있었다. 다만 이따금 몸을 일으켜 밤이 어떻게 깊어 가는지 살필 때만 빼고는.\n\n# CHAPTER 134. The Chase—Second Day.\n\n날이 밝자, 세 군데 돛대 꼭대기에는 어김없이 다시 망보는 이들이 배치되었다.\n\n\"그놈이 보이느냐?\" 에이해브는 빛이 퍼질 약간의 여유를 준 뒤 외쳤다.\n\n\"아무것도 안 보입니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"전원 갑판으로 불러내고 돛을 올려라! 그놈은 내 짐작보다 더 빨리 움직인다. 윗돛을 올려라! 그래, 밤새 그 돛을 펴 두었어야 했어. 하지만 상관없다. 한 번 내달리려고 잠시 쉬는 것일 뿐이니.\"\n\n여기서 한 가지 말해 둘 것은, 어느 특정한 고래 한 마리를 이처럼 집요하게 쫓아 낮에서 밤으로, 다시 밤에서 낮으로 추격을 이어 가는 일이 남양 포경업에서 결코 전례 없는 일이 아니라는 점이다. 낸터킷의 어떤 위대한 타고난 천재 선장들은 놀라운 기량과 경험에서 우러난 예지, 그리고 무엇으로도 꺾을 수 없는 자신감을 갖추고 있어서, 고래가 마지막으로 목격되었을 때의 단순한 관찰만으로도, 특정한 주어진 상황에서라면, 그 고래가 시야에서 사라진 동안 한동안 헤엄쳐 갈 방향뿐만 아니라 그 기간에 나아갈 대략의 속도까지도 꽤 정확히 예측해 낸다. 그리고 이런 경우, 그것은 어느 정도 이런 도선사(導船士)와도 같다. 도선사는 그 대체적인 방향을 잘 알고 있고 머지않아 다시, 다만 좀 더 멀리 떨어진 어느 지점에서 돌아가고자 하는 해안이 시야에서 사라지려 할 때, 자기 나침반 곁에 서서 지금 보이는 곶의 정확한 방위를 잰다. 이는 결국 다시 찾아가게 될, 멀리 떨어져 보이지 않는 갑(岬)을 더욱 확실히 제대로 맞히기 위함이다. 어부도 자기 나침반 앞에서 고래를 두고 꼭 그렇게 한다. 여러 시간 환한 대낮에 고래를 쫓으며 부지런히 그 자취를 표시해 둔 뒤라면, 밤이 그 고래를 어둠 속에 가려 버려도, 어둠을 뚫고 나아갈 그 짐승의 앞으로의 항적은 도선사에게 해안이 그러하듯, 영민한 사냥꾼의 마음속에 거의 확정된 것이나 다름없다. 그리하여 이 사냥꾼의 경탄할 만한 기량 앞에서는, 물 위에 쓴 글씨처럼 덧없다는 속담 속의 그 항적조차도, 바라는 모든 목적에 비추어 보면 흔들림 없는 육지만큼이나 거의 믿을 만한 것이 된다. 또한 현대 철도의 강력한 무쇠 리바이어던은 그 한 걸음 한 걸음까지 워낙 친숙하게 알려져 있어서, 사람들이 손에 시계를 들고 마치 의사가 아기의 맥박을 재듯 그 속도를 재며, 상행 열차나 하행 열차가 몇 시쯤이면 어느 지점에 닿을 것이라고 가볍게 말하는데, 거의 그와 같이, 이 낸터킷 사람들도 관찰된 그 속도의 기질에 따라 저 깊은 바다의 또 다른 리바이어던의 시간을 재며, 지금부터 몇 시간 뒤면 이 고래가 200마일을 갔을 것이고, 이러저러한 위도나 경도에 거의 다다라 있을 것이라고 혼잣말하는 경우가 있는 것이다. 그러나 이 예리함이 끝내 조금이라도 성공을 거두려면 바람과 바다가 포경자의 동맹이 되어 주어야 한다. 자기 항구로부터 정확히 93과 4분의 1리그 떨어져 있음을 일러 주는 기량이, 바람이 잦아들었거나 역풍에 묶인 뱃사람에게 지금 당장 무슨 소용이 있겠는가? 이러한 진술들로부터 고래 추격과 관련된 미묘한 부수적 사항들이 여럿 추론될 수 있다.\n\n배는 맹렬히 내달렸다. 잘못 쏘아진 포탄이 보습이 되어 평평한 들판을 갈아엎듯, 바다에 깊은 고랑을 남기며 나아갔다.\n\n\"소금과 삼대에 걸고!\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"이 갑판의 빠른 움직임이 다리를 타고 기어올라 심장을 짜릿하게 하는구나. 이 배와 나는 둘 다 용감한 친구들이렷다! 하, 하! 누가 날 들어다가, 등뼈를 아래로 해서 바다에 내던져 다오. 떡갈나무에 걸고! 내 등뼈가 곧 용골이니. 하, 하! 우리는 뒤에 먼지 한 톨 안 남기는 걸음으로 나아간다!\"\n\n\"저기 물을 뿜는다, 뿜어! 뿜는다! 바로 앞쪽이다!\" 이제 돛대 꼭대기에서 외침이 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"옳지, 옳지!\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"내 그럴 줄 알았다. 넌 빠져나갈 수 없어. 실컷 뿜어 대라, 네 분수 구멍이 터지도록 뿜어 대라, 오 고래여! 미친 악마 그 자신이 네 뒤를 쫓고 있다! 나팔을 불어라, 폐가 부르트도록 불어라! 에이해브가 네 피를 막아 버릴 테다, 방앗간 주인이 물길에 수문을 닫아걸듯이!\"\n\n스터브는 사실상 그 선원 전부의 심정을 대신 말한 것일 뿐이었다. 추격의 광기가 이때쯤이면 그들을 부글부글 끓어오르게 했으니, 마치 오래된 포도주가 새로이 발효되는 것과도 같았다. 그들 중 더러가 전에 느꼈을지 모를 창백한 두려움과 불길한 예감, 그것들은 이제 점점 자라나는 에이해브에 대한 외경(畏敬) 속에 보이지 않게 묻혀 버렸을 뿐만 아니라, 산산이 부서져 사방으로 쫓겨 달아났으니, 마치 껑충대며 달려드는 들소 앞에서 흩어지는 겁 많은 초원의 토끼들과도 같았다. 운명의 손이 그들의 영혼을 모조리 낚아채 갔다. 그리고 전날의 들끓는 위험, 지난밤의 긴장이 안겨 준 고문 같은 시간, 그들의 거친 배가 날아가는 표적을 향해 곤두박질치며 나아가던 그 고정되고, 두려움 없고, 눈멀고, 무모한 방식, 이 모든 것에 의해 그들의 마음은 굴러가듯 휩쓸려 갔다. 그들의 돛을 큰 배(腹)처럼 부풀려 놓고, 보이지 않으면서도 거역할 수 없는 팔로 배를 떠밀어 내달리게 한 바람, 이것은 그들을 그토록 추격에 옭아맨 저 보이지 않는 힘의 상징인 듯했다.\n\n그들은 서른 명이 아니라 한 사람이었다. 그들 모두를 담은 한 척의 배가, 비록 떡갈나무와 단풍나무와 소나무, 쇠와 역청과 삼대처럼 온갖 대조되는 것들로 짜맞추어졌으되, 이 모든 것이 하나의 단단한 선체 안으로 녹아들어, 길게 뻗은 중앙 용골에 의해 균형 잡히고 방향이 잡힌 채 제 길로 내달렸듯이, 꼭 그렇게 선원들의 모든 개별성, 이 사람의 용맹과 저 사람의 두려움, 죄와 죄책감, 그 온갖 다양함이 하나로 용접되어, 그들의 유일한 군주이자 용골인 에이해브가 가리키는 저 치명적인 목표를 향해 모조리 겨누어져 있었다.\n\n삭구(索具)가 살아 움직였다. 돛대 꼭대기는 키 큰 야자나무 우듬지처럼, 팔과 다리가 사방으로 술처럼 무성하게 돋아나 있었다. 어떤 이들은 한 손으로 활대를 붙든 채 다른 한 손을 내뻗어 조바심치며 흔들어 댔고, 또 어떤 이들은 눈부신 햇빛을 손으로 가리며 흔들리는 활대 위 멀찍이 앉아 있었다. 모든 활대마다 인간들을 가득 매단 채, 그들 자신의 운명을 향해 준비되고 무르익어 있었다. 아! 그들은 저 무한한 푸르름을 뚫고 자신들을 파멸시킬지도 모를 그것을 찾아내려 어찌도 그리 안간힘을 쓰고 있었던가!\n\n\"그놈이 보이거든 어찌하여 소리쳐 알리지 않느냐?\" 첫 외침이 있은 뒤 몇 분이 지나도록 더는 아무 소리도 들리지 않자, 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"나를 위로 끌어올려라, 제군들. 너희는 속은 것이다. 모비 딕은 저런 식으로 물줄기 한 번 어설피 뿜고 나서 사라지는 놈이 아니다.\"\n\n과연 그러했다. 앞뒤 가리지 않는 열망에 휩싸여, 선원들은 다른 무언가를 고래의 물줄기로 잘못 본 것이었고, 이는 곧 사건 자체로 증명되었다. 에이해브가 미처 망대에 다다르기도 전에, 밧줄이 갑판의 고정 핀에 채 매이기도 전에, 그가 한 관현악단을 향해 으뜸음을 내질러, 마치 소총들을 한꺼번에 발사한 듯한 소리로 공기를 진동시켰던 것이다. 사슴 가죽처럼 질긴 서른 개의 폐에서 터져 나온 의기양양한 함성이 울려 퍼졌으니, 상상 속 물줄기가 있던 자리보다 배에 훨씬 더 가까운 곳, 1마일도 채 안 되는 앞쪽에서 모비 딕이 그 거대한 몸뚱이를 통째로 드러내며 솟구쳐 올랐던 것이다! 흰 고래는 이번에는 고요하고 나른한 물뿜기로도, 제 머리에 달린 저 신비로운 샘에서 평화로이 솟구치는 물줄기로도 제 위치를 드러낸 것이 아니라, 훨씬 더 경이로운 현상인 도약(跳躍)으로 그리한 것이었다. 향유고래는 가장 깊은 심연으로부터 최대 속도로 솟구쳐 올라, 이렇듯 제 온몸을 순수한 공기의 원소 속으로 쏘아 올리고, 눈부신 거품의 산을 쌓아 올리며, 7마일이 넘는 먼 거리에까지 제 자리를 알린다. 그 순간, 그가 떨쳐 내는 찢기고 성난 파도는 그의 갈기처럼 보이며, 어떤 경우에는 이 도약이 그의 도전 행위가 되기도 한다.\n\n\"저기 솟구친다! 저기 솟구친다!\" 흰 고래가 그 헤아릴 수 없는 허세 속에서 연어처럼 하늘을 향해 몸을 내던지자, 외침이 터져 나왔다. 푸른 바다 평원 위에 그토록 돌연히 나타나, 한층 더 푸른 하늘 가장자리를 배경으로 도드라진 채, 그가 일으킨 물보라는 그 순간 견딜 수 없으리만치 빙하처럼 반짝이며 번득였고, 그 자리에 머물러 처음의 그 눈부신 강렬함으로부터 차츰차츰 옅어지고 또 옅어지다가, 마침내 골짜기로 밀려드는 소나기의 어슴푸레한 안개처럼 스러져 갔다.\n\n\"옳지, 해를 향해 마지막 도약을 해 보아라, 모비 딕!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"네 시간과 네게 박힐 작살이 지척에 있다! 내려와라! 모두 내려와라, 다만 앞돛대에 한 사람만 남기고. 보트를 내려라! 준비하라!\"\n\n선원들은 삭구의 더딘 밧줄 사다리는 거들떠보지도 않고, 따로 떨어진 뒷버팀줄과 도르래 줄을 타고 별똥별처럼 갑판으로 미끄러져 내려왔다. 에이해브도 그처럼 쏜살같지는 않았으나 여전히 빠르게 망대에서 내려졌다.\n\n\"보트를 내려라.\" 그는 자기 보트에 닿자마자 외쳤다. 그것은 전날 오후에 의장(艤裝)을 갖춘 예비 보트였다. \"스타벅 씨, 배는 자네 것일세. 보트들에서 떨어져 있되, 가까이 머물게. 모두 내려라!\"\n\n마치 단번에 그들에게 공포를 심어 주려는 듯, 이번에는 모비 딕 자신이 먼저 공격하는 자가 되어, 몸을 돌려 이제 세 보트의 선원들을 향해 다가오고 있었다. 에이해브의 보트가 한가운데 있었다. 그는 부하들을 격려하며, 자기는 고래를 정면으로, 곧 그 이마를 향해 곧장 노 저어 가겠노라고 일렀다. 이는 그리 드문 일이 아니었으니, 일정한 거리 안에 들어가기만 하면 그런 항로는 고래의 옆쪽 시야에 다가오는 습격이 드러나지 않게 해 주기 때문이었다. 그러나 그 바싹 좁혀진 거리에 이르기도 전에, 아직 세 보트가 모두 그의 눈에 배의 세 돛대처럼 또렷이 보이는 동안, 흰 고래는 제 몸을 휘저어 맹렬한 속도를 내더니, 거의 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 입을 떡 벌리고 꼬리를 채찍처럼 후려치며 보트들 사이로 돌진해 들어와 사방으로 소름 끼치는 싸움을 걸어왔다. 그리고 보트마다 자기를 향해 던지는 작살에는 아랑곳없이, 오로지 그 보트들을 이루는 널빤지 한 장 한 장을 산산이 부숴 버리는 데만 골몰한 듯했다. 그러나 보트들은 능숙하게 조종되어, 들판에서 잘 훈련된 군마처럼 쉴 새 없이 빙빙 돌며, 한동안 그를 피해 다녔다. 비록 때로는 그 간격이 널빤지 한 장 너비에 지나지 않았으나, 그러는 내내 에이해브의 섬뜩한 함성은 그의 것을 제외한 다른 모든 외침을 갈가리 찢어 버렸다.\n\n그러나 마침내 흰 고래는 그 종잡을 수 없는 움직임 속에서 이리저리 거듭 교차하며, 이제 제 몸에 단단히 박힌 세 가닥 밧줄의 느슨한 부분을 천 갈래로 뒤엉키게 만들었고, 그리하여 그 줄들이 짧아지면서 저절로 그 충실한 보트들을 그의 몸에 박힌 작살 쪽으로 끌어당겼다. 그러는가 싶더니 한순간 고래는 마치 한층 더 무시무시한 돌격을 위해 기운을 추스르려는 듯 약간 옆으로 물러났다. 그 기회를 붙잡아, 에이해브는 먼저 밧줄을 더 풀어 주었다가, 다시 그것을 빠르게 끌어당기고 홱홱 잡아채기 시작했으니, 그런 식으로 몇 군데 엉킨 매듭을 풀어 보려는 심산이었다. 그런데 보라! 상어의 이빨이 줄지어 늘어선 것보다 더 흉포한 광경이!\n\n밧줄의 미로 속에 걸리고 비틀려 나사처럼 휘감긴, 풀려난 작살과 창들이, 그 곤두선 미늘과 날카로운 끝을 모조리 드러낸 채, 번득이며 물방울을 뚝뚝 떨구며 에이해브 보트 뱃머리의 받침대까지 솟구쳐 올라왔다. 할 수 있는 일은 단 하나뿐이었다. 그는 보트 칼을 움켜쥐고, 위태롭게도 강철 날들의 안쪽으로, 그 사이로, 그러고는 바깥쪽으로 손을 뻗어, 그 너머의 밧줄을 끌어당겨 뱃전 안쪽으로 넘겨 이물잡이에게 건넨 뒤, 받침대 가까이에서 밧줄을 두 번 끊어, 가로채인 강철 다발을 바다로 떨어뜨렸다. 그리하여 모든 것이 다시 단단히 묶였다. 바로 그 순간, 흰 고래는 다른 밧줄들의 남은 엉킨 매듭 사이로 돌연 돌진했고, 그렇게 함으로써 더 깊이 얽혀든 스터브와 플래스크의 보트들을 거역할 수 없이 제 꼬리지느러미 쪽으로 끌어당겨, 파도에 시달리는 해변에서 굴러다니는 두 개의 깍지처럼 서로 맞부딪쳐 박살 내고는, 바닷속으로 잠겨 들며 부글거리는 소용돌이 속으로 사라졌다. 그 소용돌이 속에서, 한동안 난파선 잔해의 향기로운 삼나무 부스러기들이, 빠르게 휘저은 펀치 사발 속의 갈아 놓은 육두구처럼 빙글빙글 맴돌았다.\n\n두 보트의 선원들이 아직 물속에서 빙빙 돌며, 떠다니는 밧줄 통과 노, 그 밖에 둥둥 뜬 세간을 붙잡으려 손을 뻗는 동안, 비스듬히 기울어진 자세의 작달막한 플래스크가 빈 약병처럼 까닥까닥 떠올랐다 가라앉았다 하며 상어의 무시무시한 아가리를 피하려 두 다리를 위로 버둥거리는 동안, 스터브가 누구든 자기를 건져 올려 달라고 목청껏 외쳐 대는 동안, 그리고 이제 끊어져 가는 노인의 밧줄이 그에게 건질 수 있는 자를 구하러 그 우윳빛 웅덩이 속으로 노 저어 들어갈 여유를 허락하는 동안, 천 가지 응결된 위험들이 한꺼번에 몰아치는 그 거친 동시성 속에서, 에이해브의 아직 부서지지 않은 보트가 보이지 않는 줄에 의해 하늘로 끌어올려지는 듯했다. 흰 고래가 화살처럼 바다에서 수직으로 솟구쳐 올라, 그 넓은 이마를 보트 밑바닥에 들이받아, 보트를 빙글빙글 뒤집으며 공중으로 날려 보냈던 것이다. 마침내 보트가 다시 떨어져 뱃전이 아래를 향한 채 처박혔고, 에이해브와 그의 부하들은 마치 바닷가 동굴에서 빠져나오는 물범처럼 그 밑에서 허우적거리며 기어 나왔다.\n\n고래가 처음 솟구쳐 오른 그 추진력은, 수면을 들이받으면서 방향이 바뀌어, 본의 아니게 그를 수면을 따라 떠밀어, 그가 만들어 낸 파괴의 한가운데서 조금 떨어진 곳으로 데려갔다. 그리고 그곳을 등진 채, 그는 한순간 꼬리지느러미로 이쪽저쪽을 천천히 더듬으며 누워 있었다. 그러다 떠도는 노 한 자루나 널빤지 조각, 보트의 아주 작은 부스러기나 조각 하나라도 제 살갗에 닿을라치면, 그 꼬리는 잽싸게 뒤로 물러났다가 옆으로 휘둘러 바다를 후려쳤다. 그러나 곧, 이번 일은 다 끝났다고 흡족해진 듯, 그는 주름진 이마로 대양을 밀어 헤치며, 뒤엉킨 밧줄들을 제 뒤에 끌고서, 나그네의 한결같은 걸음걸이로 바람 부는 쪽을 향한 길을 계속 나아갔다.\n\n전과 마찬가지로, 그 모든 싸움을 지켜본 주의 깊은 배가 다시금 구조하러 다가와, 보트 한 척을 내려, 물에 떠 있는 선원들과 밧줄 통, 노, 그 밖에 붙잡을 수 있는 것은 무엇이든 건져 올려, 무사히 갑판 위로 올려놓았다. 어깨와 손목과 발목을 삔 자들, 검푸르게 멍든 타박상, 뒤틀린 작살과 창, 풀 길 없이 뒤엉킨 밧줄, 산산이 부서진 노와 널빤지, 이 모든 것이 거기 있었으나, 누구에게도 치명적이거나 중한 화는 미치지 않은 듯했다. 전날의 페달라가 그러했듯, 에이해브도 이번에는 비교적 쉽게 떠 있게 해 준 자기 보트의 부러진 반쪽에 음울하게 매달려 있는 것이 발견되었다. 그리고 그것은 전날의 사고만큼 그를 기진맥진하게 하지는 않았다.\n\n그러나 그가 부축을 받아 갑판으로 올라오자, 모든 눈길이 그에게 쏠렸다. 그가 혼자 서지 못하고, 여태껏 누구보다 앞장서서 그를 부축해 온 스타벅의 어깨에 여전히 반쯤 기대고 있었기 때문이다. 그의 상아 다리는 부러져 나가, 짧고 날카로운 파편 하나만이 남아 있었다.\n\n\"옳지, 옳지, 스타벅, 때로는 기대는 것도 달콤한 법이지, 기댈 상대가 누구든 말이야. 늙은 에이해브가 여태보다 더 자주 기대었더라면 좋았을 것을.\"\n\n\"테가 버텨 주지 못했군요, 선장님.\" 이제 다가온 목수가 말했다. \"그 다리에는 좋은 솜씨를 들였는데 말입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 뼈는 부러지지 않으셨겠지요, 선장님.\" 스터브가 진심으로 걱정하며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다! 게다가 산산조각이 났지, 스터브! 자네도 보지 않는가. 허나 뼈가 부러졌어도 늙은 에이해브는 멀쩡하다. 나는 이 몸에 살아 붙은 뼈 하나가, 잃어버린 이 죽은 뼈보다 조금이라도 더 나라고 여기지 않는다. 흰 고래도, 사람도, 악마도, 제 본연의, 다가갈 수 없는 존재 속에 있는 늙은 에이해브를 털끝만큼도 스치지 못한다. 그 어떤 측연(測鉛)이 저 바닥에 닿을 수 있으며, 그 어떤 돛대가 저 천장을 긁을 수 있겠는가? 거기 위에! 어느 쪽이냐?\"\n\n\"바람 불어가는 쪽으로 곧장입니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 키를 올려라. 다시 돛을 잔뜩 펴라, 배 지키는 자들아! 나머지 예비 보트들을 내려 의장을 갖춰라. 스타벅 씨는 가서 보트 선원들을 소집하게.\"\n\n\"먼저 뱃전 쪽으로 부축해 드리겠습니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"오, 오, 오! 이 파편이 지금 나를 어찌나 쑤셔 대는지! 저주받은 운명이여! 정복할 수 없는 영혼의 선장에게 어찌 이리도 겁 많은 부관이 붙어 있단 말인가!\"\n\n\"선장님?\"\n\n\"내 몸뚱이를 말한 것이다, 이 사람아, 자네가 아니라. 지팡이 삼을 무언가를 다오. 옳지, 저 부러진 창이면 되겠다. 사람들을 소집하라. 분명 내 그를 아직 보지 못했다. 맙소사, 그럴 리가 없다! 보이지 않는다고? 어서! 모두 불러 모아라.\"\n\n노인이 넌지시 떠올린 생각은 사실이었다. 선원들을 소집해 보니, 파시교도가 거기 없었다.\n\n\"파시교도!\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"그는 분명 휩쓸린 게 틀림없——\"\n\n\"검은 토사물이 너를 비틀어 짜 버리기를! 모두 위로, 아래로, 선실로, 선수루로 뛰어가라. 그를 찾아내라. 사라진 게 아니다, 사라진 게 아니야!\"\n\n그러나 그들은 곧 파시교도를 어디서도 찾을 수 없다는 소식을 들고 그에게 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선장님.\" 스터브가 말했다. \"선장님의 밧줄이 엉킨 곳에 휘말렸어요. 그가 끌려 들어가는 걸 본 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"내 밧줄이라고? 내 밧줄? 사라졌다고? 사라져? 그 짧은 말이 무슨 뜻이란 말이냐? 그 속에 무슨 조종(弔鐘)이 울리기에, 늙은 에이해브가 마치 제가 종루라도 된 양 이리 떨린단 말인가. 작살도! 거기 잡동사니를 뒤집어 보아라. 보이느냐? 그 벼려 낸 무쇠, 제군들, 흰 고래의 그것이——아니, 아니, 아니다, 부어터질 멍청이 같으니! 이 손이 그걸 던졌지! 그건 고래 몸속에 있다! 거기 위에! 그놈에게서 눈을 떼지 마라. 어서! 전원 보트 의장에 매달려라. 노를 모아라. 작살잡이들아! 작살을, 작살을! 윗돛을 더 높이 올려라. 모든 돛 줄을 잡아당겨라! 거기 키잡이! 침착하게, 목숨을 걸고 침착하게! 나는 이 헤아릴 수 없는 지구를 열 바퀴라도 두르겠다. 아니, 그것을 곧장 꿰뚫고 잠수해서라도, 기어이 그놈을 죽이고 말리라!\"\n\n\"위대하신 하느님이여! 단 한순간만이라도 모습을 보여 주소서,\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"선장님은 결코, 결코 그놈을 잡지 못하실 겁니다, 노인장. 예수님의 이름으로 비노니, 이런 짓은 그만두십시오. 이건 악마의 광기보다도 더한 것입니다. 이틀을 쫓았고, 두 번이나 산산조각으로 부서졌으며, 선장님의 다리마저 또 한 번 몸 아래에서 뜯겨 나갔습니다. 선장님의 사악한 그림자도 사라졌습니다. 온갖 선한 천사들이 떼 지어 경고로 선장님을 둘러싸고 있습니다. 더 무엇을 바라십니까? 이 살인마 같은 물고기가 마지막 한 사람까지 물에 빠뜨려 버릴 때까지 계속 쫓아야 합니까? 그놈에게 끌려 바다 밑바닥까지 가야 합니까? 그놈에게 이끌려 지옥의 세계로 끌려가야 합니까? 오, 오, 그놈을 더 사냥하는 것은 불경이요 신성모독입니다!\"\n\n\"스타벅, 요즈음 나는 자네에게 이상하게 마음이 끌렸다네. 우리 둘이 서로의 눈 속에서, 자네도 알다시피 그것을 본 그 순간 이래로 말이야. 허나 이 고래의 일에서만큼은, 자네 얼굴의 앞면을 내게 이 손바닥처럼 만들어 다오. 입술도 없고 이목구비도 없는 텅 빈 백지로 말이야. 에이해브는 영원히 에이해브다, 이 사람아. 이 모든 막은 바꿀 수 없이 정해져 있다. 이 대양이 굽이치기 십억 년 전에 자네와 내가 이미 연습한 것이다. 어리석은 자여! 나는 운명의 부관이다. 나는 명령에 따라 움직인다. 보아라, 아랫것이여! 자네가 내 명령에 복종하듯이 말이다. 내 둘레에 모여라, 제군들. 너희는 한 늙은이가 그루터기만 남도록 베인 모습을 본다. 부러진 창에 기대어, 외로운 한 발로 버티고 선 모습을. 이것이 에이해브다. 그의 몸뚱이의 부분이지. 허나 에이해브의 영혼은 백 개의 다리로 움직이는 지네다. 나는 긴장하여 반쯤 좌초된 느낌이다. 돌풍 속에서 돛대 잃은 군함을 끌고 가는 밧줄처럼. 내 꼴이 그리 보일지도 모르지. 허나 내가 끊어지기 전에, 너희는 내가 우지끈 부러지는 소리를 들으리라. 그리고 그 소리를 듣기 전까지는, 에이해브의 닻줄이 아직도 제 목적을 끌고 간다는 것을 알아 두어라. 제군들아, 너희는 징조라 불리는 것들을 믿느냐? 그렇다면 소리 내어 웃어라, 그리고 한 번 더 외쳐라! 물에 빠져 죽기 전에, 빠져 가는 것들은 두 번 수면 위로 떠오르고, 그러고는 다시 한 번 떠올랐다가, 영영 가라앉는 법이니. 모비 딕도 그러하다. 이틀을 떠올라 있었으니, 내일이 사흘째가 되리라. 그렇다, 제군들아, 그놈은 한 번 더 떠오를 것이다. 허나 그것은 다만 마지막 물줄기를 뿜기 위함이리라! 너희는 용감하냐, 제군들아, 용감하냐?\"\n\n\"두려움 없는 불길처럼이요.\" 스터브가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 기계처럼이지.\" 에이해브가 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 사람들이 앞쪽으로 가자, 그는 계속 중얼거렸다. \"징조라 불리는 것들! 그리고 어제 나는 저기 스타벅에게 부서진 내 보트를 두고 똑같은 말을 했지. 오! 내 안에 그토록 단단히 박혀 있는 것을, 다른 이들의 마음에서는 어찌도 그리 용감하게 몰아내려 애쓰는가! 파시교도가, 파시교도가 사라졌다고, 사라졌어? 게다가 그는 나보다 먼저 가기로 되어 있었지. 허나 내가 죽기 전에 다시 한번 보이기로 되어 있었거늘. 그게 어찌 된 일인가? 여기 한 수수께끼가 있구나, 모든 변호사들이 역대 판사들의 망령을 죄다 등에 업고 덤벼도 풀지 못할 수수께끼가. 매의 부리처럼 그것이 내 뇌를 쪼아 댄다. 허나 내가, 내가 기어이 풀고야 말리라!\"\n\n황혼이 내려앉을 무렵, 고래는 여전히 바람 불어가는 쪽에 보였다.\n\n그리하여 다시 한번 돛이 줄여졌고, 모든 것이 전날 밤과 거의 똑같이 흘러갔다. 다만, 망치 소리와 숫돌 가는 소리가 날이 거의 밝을 때까지 들렸으니, 선원들이 등불을 밝히고 예비 보트들의 의장을 완벽하고 꼼꼼하게 갖추며 내일을 위해 새 무기들을 벼리느라 고되게 일했기 때문이다. 그러는 동안, 목수는 에이해브의 부서진 보트의 부러진 용골로 그에게 새 다리를 만들어 주었다. 한편 에이해브는 전날 밤과 다름없이, 구부정한 자세로 자기 승강구 안에 붙박인 듯 서 있었다. 감추어진, 해바라기 같은 그의 시선은 미리 제 해시계 위를 거슬러 돌아가, 가장 이른 해를 맞으려 동쪽을 향한 채로 자리 잡고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 135. The Chase.—Third Day.\n\n셋째 날 아침이 맑고 신선하게 밝아 왔다. 앞돛대 꼭대기에서 홀로 밤을 지새우던 망보기 선원은 다시 한 번, 낮의 망꾼 무리에게 자리를 넘겨주었다. 그들은 모든 돛대와 거의 모든 활대마다 점점이 들어찼다.\n\n\"놈이 보이느냐?\" 에이해브가 외쳤다. 그러나 고래는 아직 시야에 없었다.\n\n\"그래도 놈이 남긴 그 틀림없는 항적을 따라가면 된다. 그 항적만 좇아라, 그뿐이다. 키잡이! 그대로 똑바로, 지금껏 가던 대로 침착하게 가라. 오늘도 또 이리 사랑스러운 날이로구나! 이 세상이 갓 빚어진 새 세상이라 하여 천사들을 위한 여름 별장으로 지어졌다 한들, 그리고 오늘 아침이 그들에게 처음으로 그 문을 활짝 열어 주는 날이라 한들, 이보다 더 아름다운 날은 그 세상에 밝아 오지 못하리라. 여기 곱씹어 볼 거리가 있구나, 에이해브에게 사색할 겨를이 있다면 말이다. 하지만 에이해브는 결코 사색하지 않는다. 다만 느낄 뿐, 느끼고, 느낄 뿐이다. 그것만으로도 죽어야 할 인간에게는 충분히 짜릿하다! 사색이란 만용이다. 오직 하느님만이 그 권리와 특권을 지닌다. 사색이란 차가움이자 고요함이거나, 마땅히 그래야 하는 것이다. 그러나 우리의 가련한 심장은 너무 거세게 뛰고, 우리의 가련한 머릿속은 너무 요란하게 박동한다. 그렇지만 나는 때로 내 머릿속이 무척 차분하다고, 얼어붙은 듯 차분하다고 여겨 왔다. 이 늙은 두개골이 이렇게 갈라지는 것을 보라, 마치 그 속의 내용물이 얼음으로 변해 잔을 산산이 쪼개 버리는 유리잔처럼. 그런데도 이 머리카락은 지금도 자라고 있다. 이 순간에도 자라고 있으니, 열기가 그것을 키워 내야 마땅하건만, 아니다, 이것은 어디서든 자라나는 흔한 풀과 같아서, 그린란드 얼음의 흙 갈라진 틈새든 베수비오의 용암 위든 가리지 않는다. 거센 바람이 어찌나 이 머리칼을 휘날리는지, 마치 찢긴 돛 조각들이 그것이 매달린 흔들리는 배를 후려치듯, 머리칼이 내 주위로 채찍질을 해 댄다. 비열한 바람이로구나, 의심할 바 없이 이전에 감옥의 복도와 감방을, 그리고 병원의 병동을 헤집고 불어 그곳들을 환기시키고는, 이제 양털처럼 천진하게 이리로 불어오니. 꺼져 버려라! 더럽혀진 바람이다. 내가 바람이라면, 이토록 사악하고 비참한 세상에는 다시는 불지 않으리라. 어딘가 동굴로 기어들어 가 거기 숨어 버리리라. 그렇지만 바람이란 고귀하고 영웅적인 것이로다! 일찍이 누가 그것을 정복한 적이 있던가? 어떤 싸움에서든 마지막의, 그리고 가장 쓰라린 일격은 바람의 몫이다. 창을 겨누고 달려들어 보라, 그저 그것을 꿰뚫고 지나갈 뿐이리라. 하! 발가벗은 사내들을 후려치면서도 단 한 번의 반격조차 받아 내려 버티지 않는, 겁쟁이 바람이로다. 에이해브조차 그보다는 더 용감한 존재요, 더 고귀한 존재다. 이제 바람에게 몸뚱이라도 있다면 좋으련만. 그러나 죽어야 할 인간을 가장 분노케 하고 격노케 하는 모든 것들은 죄다 형체가 없으니, 다만 객체로서 형체가 없을 뿐 행위자로서 형체가 없는 것은 아니다. 거기에 더없이 특별하고, 더없이 교활하며, 오, 더없이 악의에 찬 차이가 있도다! 그렇지만 다시 말하노니, 지금 맹세컨대, 바람에는 영광스럽고 자애로운 무언가가 깃들어 있다. 적어도 이 따스한 무역풍은, 맑은 하늘에서 굳세고 한결같게, 활기찬 부드러움으로 곧장 불어오며, 바다의 저급한 해류들이 아무리 방향을 틀고 돌아간들, 뭍의 가장 거대한 미시시피 강들이 아무리 빠르게 비틀거리며 끝내 어디로 가야 할지 갈피를 못 잡은들, 결코 제 목표에서 벗어나지 않는다. 그리고 영원한 양극을 두고 맹세하노니! 내 좋은 배를 이토록 곧게 밀어 주는 이 무역풍, 이 무역풍이, 혹은 그와 비슷한 무언가가, 그토록 변함없고 그만큼 굳센 무언가가, 용골을 단 내 영혼을 앞으로 밀어 가는도다! 자, 가자! 위에 있는 자들! 무엇이 보이느냐?\"\n\"아무것도 없습니다, 선장님.\"\n\n\"아무것도 없다! 정오가 코앞인데! 금화가 임자 없이 구걸하는 신세로구나! 해를 보라! 그래, 그래, 그래야만 하리라. 내가 놈을 지나쳐 와 버렸구나. 어쩌다 놈에게 앞길을 내주었단 말이냐? 그래, 이제는 놈이 나를 쫓고 있는 게로구나. 내가 놈을 쫓는 게 아니라. 그건 나쁜 징조다. 진작 알았어야 했거늘. 멍청이! 밧줄들이며, 놈이 끌고 다니는 작살들을 떠올렸어야지. 그래, 그래, 간밤에 내가 놈을 지나쳐 와 버린 거다. 돌려라! 돌려! 모두 내려오너라, 정규 망꾼들만 빼고! 아딧줄 당기는 자리에 붙어라!\"\n\n지금껏 가던 대로 항해할 때는 바람이 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 고물 쪽 비스듬한 방향에서 불어오고 있었으나, 이제 뱃머리를 정반대로 돌리자, 아딧줄을 팽팽히 당긴 배는 제가 남긴 새하얀 항적 속의 거품을 다시 휘저으며 바람을 거슬러 힘겹게 나아갔다.\n\n\"이제 바람을 거슬러 떡 벌린 아가리를 향해 키를 잡는구나.\" 스타벅(Starbuck)은 새로 끌어당긴 큰돛 아딧줄을 난간에 감으며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"하느님, 우리를 지켜 주소서. 하나 벌써부터 내 뼛속이 축축이 젖어 들고, 그 안에서부터 살까지 적셔 오는구나. 저 사람을 따름으로써 내 하느님을 거역하는 것은 아닐까, 나는 의심스럽다!\"\n\n\"나를 끌어올릴 채비를 하라!\" 에이해브가 삼노끈으로 짠 바구니로 다가서며 외쳤다. \"머지않아 놈과 마주치게 될 것이다.\"\n\n\"예, 예, 선장님.\" 스타벅은 곧장 에이해브의 명을 따랐고, 에이해브는 다시 한 번 높은 곳으로 매달려 올라갔다.\n\n이제 꼬박 한 시간이 흘렀다. 영겁의 세월처럼 얇게 두드려 펴진 시간이었다. 이제 시간 그 자체가 날카로운 긴장 속에 긴 숨을 멈추고 있었다. 그러나 마침내, 바람을 받는 뱃머리에서 세 방위쯤 떨어진 곳에서 에이해브는 다시 물줄기를 발견했고, 그 순간 세 돛대 꼭대기에서 마치 불의 혀들이 그것을 외치기라도 한 듯 세 줄기 비명이 솟구쳐 올랐다.\n\n\"이마와 이마를 맞대고 너와 마주하노라, 이 세 번째로구나, 모비 딕(Moby Dick)! 갑판에 있는 자들아! 아딧줄을 더 바짝 당겨라, 배를 바람의 눈 속으로 몰아넣어라. 놈은 아직 보트를 내리기엔 너무 멀리 있다, 스타벅 씨. 돛이 떨리는구나! 저 키잡이 곁에 망치를 들고 버티고 서라! 그래, 그래. 놈은 빠르게 나아가고, 나는 내려가야 한다. 하나 여기 위에서 바다를 한 번만 더 잘 둘러보게 해 다오. 그럴 시간은 있다. 오래되고 또 오래된 광경이건만, 어쩐지 이리도 젊구나. 그래, 내가 소년 시절 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 모래언덕에서 처음 보았던 그때와 눈 한 번 깜박할 만큼도 변하지 않았구나! 한결같다! 한결같아! 노아에게나 내게나 한결같도다. 바람 불어가는 쪽에 부드러운 소나기가 내린다. 어쩌면 저리도 사랑스러운 바람 아랫녘인가! 저것들은 분명 어딘가로, 흔한 뭍과는 다른 어딘가로, 종려나무보다 더 종려나무다운 곳으로 이어지리라. 바람 아래쪽! 흰고래는 저쪽으로 가는구나. 그러니 바람 부는 쪽을 보아라. 더 쓰라린 쪽일수록 더 좋으니. 그러나 잘 있거라, 잘 있거라, 늙은 돛대 꼭대기여! 이게 무엇이냐? 초록빛인가? 그래, 이 뒤틀린 갈라진 틈마다 자그마한 이끼가 끼었구나. 에이해브의 머리에는 이런 초록빛 풍상의 얼룩이 없도다! 인간의 노년과 물질의 노년 사이에는 바로 이런 차이가 있구나. 하나 그래, 늙은 돛대여, 우리는 함께 늙어 가는구나. 그래도 우리는 선체가 튼튼하지 않으냐, 내 배여? 그래, 다리 하나가 모자랄 뿐, 그뿐이다. 맹세컨대 이 죽은 나무가 어느 모로 보나 내 살아 있는 살보다 낫구나. 나는 그것에 견줄 수가 없도다. 나는 죽은 나무로 만든 배가, 생기 넘치는 아비에게서 가장 생기 넘치는 재료로 빚어진 인간들의 목숨보다 더 오래 살아남는 것을 본 적이 있다. 그가 무어라 했더라? 그는 여전히 내 앞을 먼저 가리라고, 내 길잡이가 되리라고 했지. 그런데도 다시 모습을 보이리라고? 하나 어디서? 끝없는 계단을 내려가 바다 밑바닥에 닿는다 한들, 내게 그것을 볼 눈이 남아 있겠는가? 게다가 나는 밤새도록 놈이 가라앉은 그곳이 어디든 거기서 멀어지는 쪽으로 항해해 왔거늘. 그래, 그래, 다른 수많은 일들처럼 너 자신에 관해서도 너는 무시무시한 진실을 말했구나, 오 파시(Parsee)여. 하나 에이해브여, 그 점에서는 네 화살이 빗나가 짧게 떨어졌도다. 잘 있거라, 돛대 꼭대기여, 내가 자리를 비운 동안 고래를 잘 지켜보아라. 내일 다시 이야기하자, 아니, 오늘 밤이다, 흰고래가 머리와 꼬리를 묶인 채 저 아래 누워 있을 그때.\"\n\n그는 명령을 내렸다. 그러고는 여전히 주위를 둘러보면서, 갈라진 푸른 대기를 가르며 천천히 갑판으로 내려졌다.\n\n때가 되어 보트들이 내려졌다. 그러나 작은 보트 고물에 서서 에이해브가 막 내려가려는 찰나에 멈칫하며, 그는 갑판에서 도르래 밧줄 하나를 붙들고 있던 일등항해사에게 손짓하며 잠시 멈추라고 일렀다.\n\"스타벅!\"\n\n\"네, 선장님?\"\n\n\"내 영혼의 배가 이 항해를 시작하는 것이 세 번째로구나, 스타벅.\"\n\n\"예, 선장님, 선장님께서 그리하시겠다니.\"\n\n\"어떤 배들은 제 항구에서 출항한 뒤로 영영 행방을 알 수 없게 되지, 스타벅!\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 선장님. 더없이 슬픈 진실이지요.\"\n\n\"어떤 사람들은 썰물에 죽고, 어떤 이들은 물이 낮을 때 죽으며, 어떤 이들은 만조에 죽는다. 그런데 나는 지금, 온통 하나의 마루로 솟구쳐 부서지는 큰 물결처럼 느껴지는구나, 스타벅. 나는 늙었다. 손을 다오, 친구여.\"\n\n두 사람의 손이 마주 잡혔고, 두 사람의 눈이 서로에게 못 박혔다. 스타벅의 눈물이 그 둘을 붙드는 아교가 되었다.\n\n\"오, 선장님, 선장님! 고귀한 마음이시여, 가지 마십시오, 가지 마십시오! 보십시오, 우는 것은 용감한 사람입니다. 그러니 만류하려는 이 고뇌가 얼마나 크겠습니까!\"\n\n\"내려라!\" 에이해브는 일등항해사의 팔을 자신에게서 뿌리치며 외쳤다. \"선원들 곁을 지켜라!\"\n\n순식간에 보트는 고물 바로 아래쪽으로 빙 돌아 노를 저었다.\n\n\"상어다! 상어다!\" 저 아래 선실 창에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"오 주인님, 제 주인님, 돌아오세요!\"\n\n그러나 에이해브는 아무것도 듣지 못했다. 그때 그 자신의 목소리가 드높이 솟구쳐 있었기 때문이다. 보트는 앞으로 튀어 나갔다.\n\n그러나 그 목소리는 참된 것을 말한 셈이었다. 그가 배에서 막 밀려 나오자마자, 선체 아래 검은 물속에서 솟아오른 듯한 수많은 상어들이 노가 물에 잠길 때마다 악의에 차서 노깃을 향해 덥석덥석 물어뜯으며, 그렇게 물어뜯음으로 보트를 따라붙었던 것이다. 이는 그처럼 상어가 우글거리는 바다에서 고래잡이 보트에 그리 드물지 않게 일어나는 일이었다. 상어들은 때로, 동방에서 행군하는 연대의 깃발 위를 독수리들이 맴도는 것과 똑같이 앞일을 내다보는 듯한 방식으로 보트를 뒤따른다. 그러나 이놈들은 흰고래가 처음 발견된 이래 피쿼드호가 목격한 첫 상어들이었다. 그리고 에이해브의 선원들이 죄다 그처럼 호랑이 빛 누런 야만인들이라 그 살점이 상어의 감각에 더 사향내처럼 풍긴 까닭인지, 그것이 때로 상어들에게 영향을 미친다는 것은 익히 알려진 사실이거니와, 어쨌든 그 까닭이 무엇이든, 상어들은 다른 보트들은 건드리지 않고 오로지 그 한 척의 보트만 따라붙는 듯했다.\n\n\"단련된 강철의 심장이여!\" 스타벅은 뱃전 너머를 응시하며, 멀어져 가는 보트를 눈으로 좇으며 중얼거렸다. \"그대는 저런 광경 앞에서도 여전히 용감하게 종소리를 울릴 수 있단 말인가? 게걸스러운 상어들 사이로 용골을 내리고, 입을 떡 벌린 채 추격에 나선 그놈들에게 뒤쫓기면서, 그것도 운명을 가르는 셋째 날에? 사흘이 하나의 끊임없고 격렬한 추격으로 흘러갈 때면, 분명 첫째 날은 아침이요, 둘째 날은 정오이며, 셋째 날은 저녁, 곧 그 일의 끝이리라. 그 끝이 무엇이든 간에. 오! 하느님! 내 안을 꿰뚫고 지나가며, 나를 이토록 죽은 듯 고요하면서도 무언가를 기다리게 하고, 전율의 정점에 못 박아 두는 이것은 대체 무엇입니까! 미래의 일들이 내 앞을 헤엄쳐 지나가니, 텅 빈 윤곽과 뼈대처럼 떠오릅니다. 지나간 모든 것은 어쩐지 흐릿해졌습니다. 메리, 내 아내여! 그대는 창백한 영광 속에 내 뒤로 희미하게 사라져 가는구나. 아들아! 네 눈동자만이 놀랍도록 푸르러진 채 보이는 듯하구나. 삶의 가장 기이한 수수께끼들이 풀려 나가는 듯하건만, 구름이 그 사이를 휩쓸고 지나간다. 내 여정의 끝이 다가오는가? 다리에 힘이 빠지는구나. 온종일 걸어 다닌 사람의 다리처럼. 네 심장을 짚어 보라. 아직도 뛰는가? 정신 차려라, 스타벅! 떨쳐 버려라, 움직여라, 움직여! 큰 소리로 외쳐라! 돛대 꼭대기에 있는 자여! 언덕 위에 내 아들의 손이 보이느냐? 미쳤구나. 위에 있는 자여! 가장 날카로운 눈으로 보트들을 지켜보아라. 고래를 잘 살펴라! 호! 또구나! 저 매를 쫓아 버려라! 보라! 놈이 쪼아 댄다, 깃발을 찢어 놓는다.\" 그는 큰돛대 꼭대기에 나부끼는 붉은 깃발을 가리켰다. \"하! 놈이 그것을 채어 높이 솟구쳐 날아가는구나! 그 노인은 지금 어디 있는가? 오 에이해브, 그대는 저 광경이 보이는가! 떨어라, 떨어라!\"\n\n보트들이 그리 멀리 나아가지 않았을 때, 돛대 꼭대기에서 보낸 신호로, 곧 아래쪽을 가리키는 팔을 보고서 에이해브는 고래가 잠수했음을 알았다. 그러나 놈이 다음에 떠오를 때 가까이 있으려는 뜻으로, 그는 배에서 약간 비껴난 방향으로 길을 이어 갔다. 정면으로 부딪쳐 오는 파도가 맞은편 뱃머리를 거듭거듭 두드려 대는 동안, 마법에 걸린 듯한 선원들은 더없이 깊은 침묵을 지켰다.\n\n\"못을 박아라, 못을 박아라, 오 너희 파도들이여! 못대가리 끝까지 깊이 박아 넣어라! 그래 봐야 너희는 뚜껑 없는 것을 두드릴 뿐이다. 내게는 어떤 관도, 어떤 영구차도 없으리라. 그리고 오직 삼노끈만이 나를 죽일 수 있으리라! 하! 하!\"\n\n별안간 그들 주위의 물이 넓은 원을 그리며 천천히 부풀어 올랐다. 그러더니 마치 물에 잠긴 빙산에서 옆으로 미끄러져 나오듯 빠르게 솟구쳐 올라, 수면을 향해 날쌔게 떠올랐다. 낮게 우르릉거리는 소리가 들렸다. 땅속에서 울리는 듯한 웅웅거림이었다. 그러자 모두가 숨을 죽였다. 끌려오는 밧줄들과 작살들과 창들에 칭칭 휘감긴 채, 거대한 형체 하나가 바다에서 비스듬히, 그러나 몸을 길게 뻗으며 솟구쳐 나왔다. 얇게 드리운 안개의 면사포에 싸인 채, 그것은 무지개 빛으로 물든 대기 속에 한순간 떠 있다가, 다시 깊은 바다 속으로 곤두박질쳐 잠겨 들었다. 삼십 피트(약 9미터) 높이로 부서져 솟구친 물줄기는 한순간 분수 무더기처럼 번쩍이다가, 이내 자잘한 물보라가 되어 부서져 내렸다. 그리고 빙 둘러진 수면에는 고래의 대리석 같은 몸통을 에워싼 채 갓 짠 우유처럼 거품이 일었다.\n\n\"저어라!\" 에이해브가 노잡이들에게 외쳤고, 보트들은 공격을 향해 쏜살같이 앞으로 내달았다. 그러나 어제 새로 박힌 작살들이 제 몸속에서 곪아 들어 광란에 빠진 모비 딕은, 하늘에서 떨어진 모든 천사들에게 한꺼번에 사로잡힌 듯했다. 그 넓고 흰 이마를 뒤덮은, 투명한 살갗 아래 용접된 듯 단단한 힘줄의 너른 층층이 한데 얽혀 있는 것처럼 보였다. 놈은 정면으로 돌진하며 보트들 사이로 꼬리를 휘저어 댔고, 다시 한 번 그것들을 후려쳐 흩어 놓았다. 두 항해사의 보트에서 작살과 창들을 쏟아 버리고, 그 뱃머리 윗부분 한쪽을 쳐서 부수었으나, 에이해브의 보트만은 거의 상처 하나 입지 않은 채로 남겨 두었다.\n\n대구(Daggoo)와 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 휜 널빤지의 틈을 틀어막고 있을 때, 그리고 고래가 그들에게서 헤엄쳐 빠져나가 몸을 돌려, 다시 그들 곁을 스쳐 지나며 옆구리 한쪽을 통째로 드러냈을 때, 바로 그 순간 다급한 외침이 솟구쳤다. 물고기의 등에 칭칭 휘감겨, 지난밤 고래가 제 몸 둘레로 밧줄의 사리를 돌리고 또 돌려 감던 그 겹겹의 휘감김 속에 단단히 결박된 채로, 반쯤 찢긴 파시의 몸뚱이가 모습을 드러냈다. 그의 검은 옷자락은 너덜너덜 해어져 있었고, 부릅뜬 두 눈은 늙은 에이해브를 정면으로 향하고 있었다.\n\n작살이 그의 손에서 떨어졌다.\n\n\"속았구나, 속았어!\" 그는 길고 야윈 숨을 들이쉬었다. \"그래, 파시여! 다시 너를 보는구나. 그래, 너는 앞서가는구나. 그리고 이것이, 바로 이것이 네가 약속했던 그 영구차로구나. 하나 나는 네 말의 마지막 글자 한 자까지 붙들겠다. 두 번째 영구차는 어디 있느냐? 가라, 항해사들이여, 배로 가라! 저 보트들은 이제 쓸모가 없다. 시간이 되거든 고칠 수 있으면 고쳐서 내게 돌아오라. 만일 그러지 못한다면, 죽는 데는 에이해브 하나로 족하다. 앉아라, 사람들아! 내가 타고 선 이 보트에서 뛰어내리려 드는 첫 번째 놈이 있다면, 그놈을 내가 작살로 꿰어 버리겠다. 너희는 다른 사람들이 아니라 내 팔이요 내 다리다. 그러니 내게 복종하라. 고래는 어디 있느냐? 또 가라앉아 버렸느냐?\"\n\n그러나 그는 보트와 너무 가까운 곳만 살피고 있었다. 마치 제가 짊어진 시신을 데리고 달아나기로 작정한 듯, 그리고 마치 마지막 격돌이 벌어진 그 장소가 다만 제 바람 아랫녘 항해의 한 단계에 지나지 않았다는 듯, 모비 딕은 이제 다시 꾸준히 앞으로 헤엄쳐 나아가고 있었다. 놈은 이미 배를 거의 다 지나친 참이었다. 배는 지금껏 놈과 정반대 방향으로 항해해 왔으나, 당장은 그 전진이 멈춰 있던 터였다. 놈은 온 힘을 다해 헤엄치는 듯했고, 이제는 오로지 바다에서 제 곧은 길을 좇는 데에만 골몰해 있었다.\n\n\"오! 에이해브 선장님,\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"지금이라도, 이 셋째 날에라도, 그만두기에 너무 늦은 것은 아닙니다. 보십시오! 모비 딕은 선장님을 찾지 않습니다. 미친 듯이 놈을 찾는 것은 바로 선장님, 선장님이십니다!\"\n\n솟구쳐 오르는 바람에 돛을 펼친 채, 외로운 보트는 노와 돛 양쪽의 힘으로 바람 아랫녘을 향해 빠르게 밀려 나아갔다. 그리고 마침내 에이해브가 배 곁을 미끄러져 지나며, 난간 너머로 몸을 기울인 스타벅의 얼굴을 또렷이 알아볼 만큼 가까워졌을 때, 그는 스타벅에게 배를 돌려, 너무 빠르지 않게 적당한 간격을 두고 자신을 뒤따르라고 소리쳐 일렀다. 위를 흘끗 올려다보니, 태시테고(Tashtego)와 퀴케그와 대구가 세 돛대 꼭대기로 부지런히 올라가고 있었고, 노잡이들은 방금 막 뱃전으로 끌어올려진, 부서진 두 척의 보트 안에서 흔들거리며 그것들을 고치느라 분주히 일하고 있었다. 그가 빠르게 지나가는 동안, 그는 또 현창들을 통해 하나하나, 스텁(Stubb)과 플래스크(Flask)가 갑판에서 새 작살과 창 다발들 사이에서 분주히 움직이는 모습을 스치듯 보았다. 이 모든 것을 보았을 때, 부서진 보트들에서 망치질 소리를 들었을 때, 전혀 다른 망치들이 그의 심장에 못을 박아 넣는 듯했다. 그러나 그는 마음을 다잡았다. 그리고 이제 큰돛대 꼭대기에서 풍향기, 곧 깃발이 사라진 것을 알아채고, 그는 막 그 횃대에 오른 태시테고에게 다시 내려가 다른 깃발과 망치와 못을 가져다가 돛대에 단단히 박으라고 외쳤다.\n\n사흘에 걸친 끊임없는 추격에 지친 탓인지, 아니면 제가 짊어진 매듭진 짐이 헤엄을 가로막는 데 저항하느라 지쳐서인지, 그것도 아니면 놈 안에 숨은 어떤 기만과 악의 때문인지, 무엇이 참이든 간에, 흰고래의 나아가는 속도가 이제 누그러지기 시작하는 듯했다. 다시 한 번 그토록 빠르게 다가오는 보트로부터 멀어지려는 듯하면서도. 사실 고래가 마지막으로 내달은 거리는 앞서만큼 길지 않았다. 그리고 에이해브가 파도 위를 미끄러져 가는 내내, 무자비한 상어들이 그를 따라붙었다. 그놈들은 어찌나 끈질기게 보트에 들러붙고, 어찌나 줄곧 부지런히 젓는 노를 물어뜯었던지, 노깃이 들쭉날쭉 깨물려 부서지고, 거의 노를 담글 때마다 자잘한 나뭇조각이 바다에 떨어져 나갔다.\n\n\"저놈들을 신경 쓰지 마라! 저 이빨들은 너희 노에 새 노받이를 달아 줄 뿐이다. 저어라! 무르기만 한 물보다는 상어의 턱이 차라리 더 든든한 받침이니.\"\n\n\"하지만 선장님, 물어뜯길 때마다 가느다란 노깃이 자꾸자꾸 작아집니다!\"\n\n\"오래 버틸 만큼은 버틸 게다! 저어라! 하나 누가 알겠느냐,\" 그는 중얼거렸다. \"이 상어들이 고래의 잔치를 위해 헤엄쳐 오는지, 아니면 에이해브의 잔치를 위해 오는지를? 하나 저어라! 그래, 이제 모두 기운을 내라, 놈에게 다가가고 있다. 키! 키를 잡게 해 다오! 비켜라.\" 이렇게 말하며, 노잡이 둘이 그를 부축해 여전히 내달리는 보트의 뱃머리로 데려갔다.\n\n마침내 보트가 한쪽으로 비껴 던져지듯 돌아, 흰고래의 옆구리와 나란히 줄지어 달리게 되었을 때, 놈은 이상하리만치 그 다가옴을 알아채지 못하는 듯했다. 고래가 이따금 그러하듯이. 그리고 에이해브는 어느새 그 자욱한 산안개 속에 완전히 들어가 있었다. 그것은 고래의 물줄기에서 뿜어져 나와, 모내드녹 산봉우리 같은 놈의 거대한 혹 둘레로 감돌고 있었다. 그는 그렇게나 놈에게 바싹 다가가 있었다. 그때, 몸을 뒤로 한껏 젖히고 두 팔을 길게 높이 치켜들어 겨눈 채로, 그는 그 사나운 작살을, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 더 사나운 저주를 미운 고래에게 던져 박았다. 강철도 저주도 마치 늪 속으로 빨려들 듯 박힌 자리 끝까지 가라앉자, 모비 딕은 옆구리를 비틀며 발작하듯 가까운 쪽 옆구리를 뱃머리에 들이박았다. 그리고 보트에 구멍 하나 내지 않은 채, 어찌나 갑작스레 보트를 기울여 엎었던지, 그 순간 에이해브가 매달려 있던 뱃전의 높직한 부분이 아니었다면, 그는 또 한 번 바다로 내동댕이쳐졌으리라. 실제로 노잡이 셋이 작살이 던져지는 정확한 순간을 미리 알지 못해 그 여파에 대비하지 못한 채 밖으로 튕겨 나갔다. 그러나 떨어지는 모양새가 그러하여, 그중 둘은 순식간에 다시 뱃전을 움켜쥐었고, 부서지는 물결을 타고 그 높이까지 솟아올라 제 몸을 통째로 다시 보트 안으로 던져 넣었다. 세 번째 사내는 속절없이 고물 쪽으로 떨어졌으나, 여전히 물 위에 떠서 헤엄치고 있었다.\n\n거의 동시에, 단계 없이 즉각적인 날쌤을 향한 거대한 의지로, 흰고래는 들끓는 바다를 가르며 쏜살같이 내달았다. 그러나 에이해브가 키잡이에게 밧줄을 새로 감아 단단히 붙들고 있으라고 소리치고, 선원들에게는 자리에서 몸을 돌려 보트를 목표 지점까지 끌어당기라고 명령한 순간, 그 믿을 수 없는 밧줄은 그 이중의 팽팽함과 당김을 느끼기가 무섭게, 텅 빈 허공에서 툭 끊어져 버렸다!\n\n\"내 안에서 무엇이 끊어졌는가? 어떤 힘줄이 갈라진다! 다시 멀쩡해졌구나. 노! 노를 저어라! 놈을 향해 덮쳐라!\"\n\n바다를 부수며 내달려 오는 보트의 어마어마한 돌진 소리를 듣고, 고래는 빙 몸을 돌려 그 텅 빈 이마를 들이대며 맞설 태세를 취했다. 그러나 그렇게 몸을 돌리던 중, 다가오는 배의 검은 선체가 눈에 들어오자, 그 안에서 제가 받은 모든 박해의 근원을 본 듯, 그리고 어쩌면 그것을 더 크고 더 고귀한 적으로 여긴 듯, 별안간 놈은 그 다가오는 뱃머리를 향해 덮쳐 들며, 불꽃 같은 거품의 소나기 속에서 제 턱을 부딪쳐 댔다.\n\n에이해브가 비틀거렸다. 그의 손이 제 이마를 쳤다. \"눈이 머는구나. 사람들아! 내가 더듬어서라도 길을 찾을 수 있도록 내 앞으로 손을 뻗어 다오. 밤인가?\"\n\n\"고래입니다! 배입니다!\" 움츠러든 노잡이들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"노! 노를 저어라! 오 바다여, 그대 깊은 곳을 향해 비스듬히 기울어라, 영영 너무 늦어 버리기 전에, 에이해브가 이 마지막, 마지막으로 제 표적을 향해 미끄러져 갈 수 있도록! 보인다, 배가! 배다! 내달려라, 내 사람들아! 너희는 내 배를 구하지 않겠느냐?\"\n\n그러나 노잡이들이 큰 망치로 두드려 대는 듯한 바다를 헤치며 보트를 거세게 몰아가던 그때, 앞서 고래에게 들이받힌 널빤지 두 장의 뱃머리 끝이 터져 부서졌고, 거의 순식간에 일시적으로 못 쓰게 된 보트는 파도와 거의 같은 높이로 가라앉았다. 반쯤 물에 잠긴 채 물을 튀기는 선원들은 그 갈라진 틈을 막고 쏟아져 들어오는 물을 퍼내려 안간힘을 썼다.\n\n한편, 그 한순간 바라보는 동안, 태시테고의 돛대 꼭대기에서 망치를 든 손은 허공에 멈춰 있었다. 그리고 붉은 깃발은 격자무늬 천처럼 그의 몸을 반쯤 감쌌다가, 마치 앞으로 내달리는 그 자신의 심장처럼, 그에게서 곧장 뻗어 나부꼈다. 그 아래 제1사장 위에 서 있던 스타벅과 스텁은 그가 본 바로 그 순간, 내리덮쳐 오는 괴물을 보았다.\n\n\"고래다, 고래! 키를 올려라, 키를 올려! 오, 그대 대기의 모든 정다운 힘들이여, 이제 나를 꼭 안아 다오! 스타벅이 죽어야 한다면, 여인의 기절 발작 속에서 죽게 하지는 마소서. 키를 올리라니까, 이 멍청이들아, 아가리! 아가리다! 이것이 터져 나오던 내 모든 기도의 끝이란 말인가? 평생토록 지켜 온 내 모든 충성의 끝이란 말인가? 오, 에이해브, 에이해브, 보라, 그대가 한 일을. 침착하라! 키잡이여, 침착하라. 아니, 아니다! 다시 키를 올려라! 놈이 우리를 맞으러 몸을 돌린다! 오, 놈의 달랠 길 없는 이마가 한 사람을 향해 돌진해 오는구나, 제 의무가 떠날 수 없다고 일러 주는 한 사람을. 하느님, 이제 제 곁에 서 주소서!\"\n\n\"내 곁에 서지 말고, 내 아래에 서라, 그대가 누구든 이제 스텁을 도우려는 자라면. 스텁 또한 여기 들러붙어 있으니. 나는 너를 보고 씩 웃노라, 이 씩 웃는 고래야! 일찍이 스텁의 깜박이지 않는 제 눈 말고 그 누가 스텁을 도왔으며, 그 누가 스텁을 깨어 있게 했더란 말이냐? 그리고 이제 가련한 스텁은 너무나 푹신한 매트리스 위에 잠자리에 드는구나. 차라리 잔솔가지로 채워졌으면 좋으련만! 나는 너를 보고 씩 웃노라, 이 씩 웃는 고래야! 보라, 해여, 달이여, 그리고 별들이여! 나는 너희를 일찍이 제 혼을 뿜어 올린 그 어떤 사내 못지않게 훌륭한 친구의 살인자라 부르노라. 그럼에도 너희가 잔을 건네 주기만 한다면, 나는 너희와 잔을 맞대고 짠 소리를 울리고 싶구나! 오, 오! 오, 오! 이 씩 웃는 고래야, 하나 머잖아 실컷 꿀꺽거릴 일이 넘쳐 나겠구나! 어찌하여 달아나지 않는가, 오 에이해브여! 나로 말하자면, 신발과 웃옷을 벗어 던지리라. 스텁은 속바지 차림으로 죽게 하라! 그래도 곰팡내 나고 소금기 지나친 죽음이로다. 버찌! 버찌! 버찌! 오, 플래스크, 죽기 전에 빨간 버찌 한 알이라도!\"\n\n\"버찌? 나는 다만 우리가 그것이 자라는 곳에 있었으면 할 따름이다. 오, 스텁, 나는 우리 가엾은 어머니가 지금쯤 내 봉급 일부를 타 가셨기를 바란다. 그러지 못했다면 이제 어머니께 돌아갈 동전 몇 푼도 변변치 않으리라, 항해가 끝장났으니.\"\n\n배의 뱃머리에서, 이제 거의 모든 선원이 손을 놓은 채 매달려 있었다. 망치며, 널빤지 조각이며, 창이며, 작살이 저마다의 일거리에서 막 던지듯 멈춰 선 그대로, 그들의 손에 기계적으로 쥐어진 채였다. 마법에 걸린 그들의 눈은 모조리 고래를 향해 못 박혀 있었다. 놈은 운명을 예정하는 그 머리를 좌우로 기이하게 진동시키며, 돌진하는 제 앞으로 반원을 그리며 너르게 퍼지는 거품의 띠를 보내고 있었다. 응징과 신속한 복수와 영원한 악의가 놈의 온 모습에 깃들어 있었고, 죽어야 할 인간이 무엇을 할 수 있든 그 모든 것을 비웃듯, 놈의 이마의 단단한 흰 버팀벽이 배의 우현 뱃머리를 들이받았다. 그 충격에 사람들도 목재들도 휘청거렸다. 더러는 얼굴을 박고 납작하게 쓰러졌다. 제자리에서 빠져나온 도르래처럼, 높은 곳의 작살잡이들의 머리가 황소 같은 목 위에서 흔들렸다. 그 갈라진 틈으로, 그들은 산골짜기의 급류가 도랑을 따라 쏟아지듯 물이 밀려드는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"배다! 영구차로구나! 두 번째 영구차로구나!\" 에이해브가 보트에서 외쳤다. \"그 나무가 미국산이 아닐 리 없으니!\"\n\n가라앉는 배 밑으로 자맥질하며, 고래는 그 용골을 따라 부르르 떨며 내달렸다. 그러나 물속에서 몸을 돌려, 다시 빠르게 수면으로 솟구쳐 올랐다. 반대편 뱃머리에서 멀찍이, 그러나 에이해브의 보트에서 몇 야드 안 되는 곳이었다. 거기서 놈은 한동안 잠잠히 누워 있었다.\n\n\"나는 해를 등지고 몸을 돌린다. 이봐, 태시테고! 네 망치 소리를 들려 다오. 오! 그대 항복하지 않은 나의 세 첨탑이여, 갈라지지 않은 용골이여, 오직 신에게만 들이받힌 선체여, 그대 굳건한 갑판과 도도한 키와 북극을 가리키는 뱃머리여, 죽음으로 영광스러운 배여! 그렇다면 그대는 나도 없이 스러져야 한단 말인가? 나는 가장 보잘것없는 난파선 선장들에게 마지막으로 허락된 그 정겨운 자랑마저 빼앗긴 것인가? 오, 외로운 삶 위의 외로운 죽음이여! 오, 이제 나는 깨닫는다, 내 가장 높은 위대함이 내 가장 깊은 비탄 속에 있음을. 호, 호! 너희 가장 먼 끝에서부터, 이제 밀려들어라, 내 지나온 온 생애의 그대 담대한 큰 물결들이여, 그리고 내 죽음의 이 한 겹 쌓인 마루를 그 위에 얹어 다오! 너를 향해 나는 굴러간다, 모든 것을 파괴하지만 정복당하지는 않는 고래여. 마지막까지 나는 너와 맞붙어 싸운다. 지옥의 심장으로부터 나는 너를 찌른다. 증오를 위하여 나는 너를 향해 내 마지막 숨을 뱉노라. 모든 관과 모든 영구차를 하나의 공동 웅덩이에 가라앉혀 버려라! 그리고 그 어느 것도 내 것이 될 수 없으니, 나로 하여금 너를 뒤쫓으며 갈가리 찢기게 하라, 너에게 묶인 채로라도, 이 저주받은 고래야! 이렇게, 나는 작살을 내던진다!\"\n\n작살이 던져졌다. 작살에 맞은 고래는 앞으로 내달았다. 불붙듯 빠른 속도로 밧줄이 홈을 따라 풀려 나갔다. 그러다 얽혀 버렸다. 에이해브가 그것을 풀려고 몸을 굽혔다. 그는 정말로 풀어냈다. 그러나 날아오르던 밧줄 고리가 그의 목을 휘감았고, 터키의 벙어리 형리들이 제 희생자의 목을 활시위로 조르듯 소리 없이, 그는 선원들이 그가 사라진 것을 알아채기도 전에 보트 밖으로 튕겨 나갔다. 다음 순간, 밧줄의 맨 끝에 무겁게 이어 붙인 고리매듭이 텅 빈 통에서 휙 튀어나와 노잡이 하나를 쓰러뜨리고는, 바다를 후려치며 그 깊은 곳으로 사라져 버렸다.\n\n한순간, 넋이 나간 보트의 선원들은 꼼짝없이 서 있었다. 그러더니 몸을 돌렸다. \"배는? 위대한 하느님이시여, 배는 어디 있습니까?\" 머잖아 그들은 흐릿하고 어지러운 매질 너머로, 가스 같은 신기루 속에서처럼 비스듬히 스러져 가는 배의 환영을 보았다. 가장 높은 돛대들만이 물 밖으로 솟아 있을 뿐이었다. 한때 드높았던 제 횃대에 미혹 때문인지, 충성 때문인지, 운명 때문인지 못 박힌 채, 이교도 작살잡이들은 여전히 가라앉아 가는 망보기 자리를 바다 위에 지키고 있었다. 그리고 이제, 동심원들이 그 외로운 보트 그 자체를, 그리고 그 모든 선원을, 떠 있는 노 하나하나를, 창대 하나하나를 움켜쥐었다. 그러고는 산 것이든 죽은 것이든 모조리 빙글빙글 돌리며, 하나의 소용돌이 속에서 빙빙 돌려, 피쿼드호의 가장 작은 나뭇조각 하나까지 시야 밖으로 휩쓸어 가 버렸다.\n\n그러나 마지막으로 덮쳐드는 물결들이 한데 뒤섞여, 큰돛대에 매달린 인디언(Indian)의 가라앉은 머리 위로 쏟아져 내려, 곧추선 활대의 몇 인치와 함께 길게 나부끼는 몇 야드의 깃발자락만 남겨 두었을 때, 그 깃발이 거의 닿을 듯한 그 파괴의 큰 물결들 위로 비웃듯 우연한 박자로 잔잔히 너울거리고 있을 때, 바로 그 순간, 붉은 팔 하나와 망치 하나가 깃발을 가라앉아 가는 활대에 더 빠르게, 또 더욱더 빠르게 박으려는 동작으로, 탁 트인 허공 속에 뒤로 젖혀진 채 치켜올려져 있었다. 별들 사이의 제 본향에서부터 큰돛대 꼭대기를 따라 비웃듯 내려와 깃발을 쪼아 대며 거기 태시테고를 괴롭히던 하늘의 매 한 마리가, 마침 이 순간 그 너른 퍼덕이는 날개를 망치와 나무 사이에 끼워 넣었다. 그리고 그 천상의 전율을 느끼는 것과 동시에, 그 아래 물에 잠긴 야만인은 죽음의 헐떡임 속에서 제 망치를 그 자리에 얼어붙은 듯 멈춰 둔 채 있었다. 그리하여 그 하늘의 새는, 대천사 같은 비명을 지르며, 제왕다운 부리를 위로 치켜든 채, 사로잡힌 온몸이 에이해브의 깃발에 휘감긴 채, 제 배와 함께 가라앉아 갔다. 그 배는, 사탄이 그러하듯, 살아 있는 하늘의 한 자락을 함께 끌고 내려가 그것으로 제 머리에 투구를 씌우기 전에는 결코 지옥으로 가라앉으려 하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n이제 작은 새들이 아직 입을 벌리고 있는 심연 위로 비명을 지르며 날아다녔다. 음울한 흰 파도가 그 가파른 옆구리를 두드렸다. 그러고는 모든 것이 무너져 내렸고, 바다의 거대한 수의는 오천 년 전에 굽이쳤던 그대로 다시 굽이쳐 흘렀다.\n\n맺음말\n\n\"나만 홀로 피하였으므로 당신에게 알리려고 왔나이다.\" 욥기.\n\n연극은 끝났다. 그렇다면 어찌하여 여기 누군가가 앞으로 나선단 말인가? 한 사람이 그 난파에서 살아남았기 때문이다.\n마침 파시가 사라진 뒤로, 그 뱃머리잡이가 비어 있던 자리를 맡게 되었을 때, 에이해브의 뱃머리잡이 자리를 대신하도록 운명의 여신들이 정한 자가 바로 나였다. 마지막 날에 세 사람이 흔들리는 보트에서 튕겨 나갔을 때, 고물 쪽으로 떨어졌던 바로 그 사람이다. 그리하여 이어지는 광경의 가장자리에 떠서, 그것을 온전히 눈앞에 둔 채로, 가라앉은 배가 반쯤 다하여 빨아들이는 힘이 내게 미쳐 왔을 때, 나는 그제야, 그러나 천천히, 닫혀 가는 소용돌이를 향해 끌려들었다. 내가 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그것은 이미 우윳빛 웅덩이로 잦아들어 있었다. 그리하여 나는 빙글빙글, 점점 더 그 천천히 도는 원의 축에 자리한 단추처럼 검은 거품을 향해 좁혀 들며, 또 하나의 익시온처럼 돌고 또 돌았다. 마침내 그 생명의 중심에 다다랐을 때, 검은 거품이 위로 터져 올랐다. 그리고 이제, 그 교묘한 용수철 장치 덕분에 풀려나, 그 엄청난 부력으로 인해 거센 힘으로 솟구쳐 오른 관 모양의 구명부표가 몸을 길게 뻗으며 바다에서 튀어 올라, 옆으로 넘어지더니 내 곁에 둥실 떠올랐다. 그 관에 떠받쳐진 채로, 거의 꼬박 하루 낮과 밤 동안, 나는 부드럽고 만가(輓歌) 같은 바다 위를 떠다녔다. 해를 끼치지 않는 상어들은 마치 제 아가리에 자물쇠라도 채운 듯 곁을 미끄러져 지나갔다. 사나운 바다매들은 부리를 칼집에 넣은 듯 날아다녔다. 둘째 날에, 돛배 하나가 가까이, 더 가까이 다가오더니, 마침내 나를 건져 올렸다. 그것은 이리저리 떠돌며 수색하던 레이철호였으니, 잃어버린 제 자식들을 되짚어 찾아 헤매던 끝에, 또 다른 고아 하나만을 찾아낸 것이었다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-2701"]=[[["This text is a combination of etexts, one from the now-defunct ERIS project at Virginia Tech and one from Project Gutenberg’s archives.", "이 텍스트는 두 개의 전자 텍스트를 결합한 것으로, 하나는 지금은 폐지된 버지니아 공대의 ERIS 프로젝트에서, 다른 하나는 프로젝트 구텐베르크의 자료실에서 가져온 것이다."], ["The proofreaders of this version are indebted to The University of Adelaide Library for preserving the Virginia Tech version.", "이 판본의 교정자들은 버지니아 공대 판본을 보존해 준 애들레이드 대학교 도서관에 감사를 표한다."], ["The resulting etext was compared with a public domain hard copy version of the text.", "그렇게 만들어진 전자 텍스트는 퍼블릭 도메인의 인쇄본과 대조 검토되었다."]], [["(Supplied by a Late Consumptive Usher to a Grammar School.)", "어원. (어느 문법학교의 작고한 폐병 걸린 보조교사가 제공함.)"]], [["The pale Usher—threadbare in coat, heart, body, and brain; I see him now.", "창백한 보조교사 — 외투도, 마음도, 몸도, 머릿속도 모두 닳아 해진 그 사람이 지금도 눈앞에 선하다."], ["He was ever dusting his old lexicons and grammars, with a queer handkerchief, mockingly embellished with all the gay flags of all the known nations of the world.", "그는 늘 낡은 사전과 문법책의 먼지를 떨어내곤 했는데, 세상에 알려진 모든 나라의 화려한 깃발들로 우습게 장식된 기묘한 손수건을 썼다. 그는 낡은 문법책의 먼지 떠는 일을 좋아했다."], ["He loved to dust his old grammars; it somehow mildly reminded him of his mortality.", "어쩐지 그 일이 그에게 자신의 죽을 운명을 은근히 일깨워 주었던 것이다."]], [["“While you take in hand to school others, and to teach them by what name a whale-fish is to be called in our tongue, leaving out, through ignorance, the letter H, which almost alone maketh up the signification of the word, you deliver that which is not true.”", "\"그대가 다른 이들을 가르치려 손을 들고, 우리 말로 고래라는 물고기를 무어라 불러야 하는지 일러주면서, 무지로 인해 H라는 글자를 빠뜨린다면 — 그 글자야말로 그 단어의 의미를 거의 홀로 이루어 내는 것이거늘 — 그대는 진실 아닌 것을 전하는 셈이다.\""], ["—Hackluyt.", "—해클루트(Hackluyt)."]], [["“WHALE. * * * Sw.", "\"고래(WHALE)."], ["and Dan. hval.", "* * * 스웨덴어와 덴마크어의 hval."], ["This animal is named from roundness or rolling; for in Dan.", "이 동물은 둥글거나 구르는 모양에서 이름이 붙여졌다. 덴마크어의 hvalt는 아치형 또는 둥근 천장 모양을 뜻하기 때문이다.\""], ["hvalt is arched or vaulted.” —Webster’s Dictionary.", "—웹스터 사전(Webster's Dictionary)."]], [["“WHALE. * * * It is more immediately from the Dut.", "\"고래(WHALE)."], ["and Ger. Wallen; A.S.", "* * * 더 직접적으로는 네덜란드어와 독일어의 Wallen, 그리고 앵글로색슨어의 Walw-ian에서 왔으며, 구르다, 뒹굴다라는 뜻이다.\""], ["Walw-ian, to roll, to wallow.” —Richardson’s Dictionary.", "—리처드슨 사전(Richardson's Dictionary)."]], [["‏חו‎, Hebrew.", "חו, 히브리어."], ["ϰητος, Greek.", "ϰητος, 그리스어."], ["CETUS, Latin.", "CETUS, 라틴어."], ["WHŒL, Anglo-Saxon.", "WHŒL, 앵글로색슨어."], ["HVALT, Danish.", "HVALT, 덴마크어."], ["WAL, Dutch.", "WAL, 네덜란드어."], ["HWAL, Swedish.", "HWAL, 스웨덴어."], ["HVALUR, Icelandic.", "HVALUR, 아이슬란드어."], ["WHALE, English.", "WHALE, 영어."], ["BALEINE, French.", "BALEINE, 프랑스어."], ["BALLENA, Spanish.", "BALLENA, 스페인어."], ["PEKEE-NUEE-NUEE, Fegee.", "PEKEE-NUEE-NUEE, 피지어."], ["PEHEE-NUEE-NUEE, Erromangoan.", "PEHEE-NUEE-NUEE, 에로망고어."]], [["EXTRACTS.", "발췌록."], ["(Supplied by a Sub-Sub-Librarian).", "(어느 보조 사서가 제공함.)"]], [["It will be seen that this mere painstaking burrower and grub-worm of a poor devil of a Sub-Sub appears to have gone through the long Vaticans and street-stalls of the earth, picking up whatever random allusions to whales he could anyways find in any book whatsoever, sacred or profane.", "보다시피 가련한 이 보조 사서, 부지런히 땅을 파헤치는 굼벵이 같은 이 불쌍한 작자는 신성한 것이든 세속적인 것이든 그 어떤 책에서든 닥치는 대로 찾아낼 수 있는 고래에 관한 온갖 잡다한 언급을 주워 모으느라, 세상의 기나긴 바티칸 도서관들과 거리의 헌책 가판대들을 두루 헤맨 듯하다."], ["Therefore you must not, in every case at least, take the higgledy-piggledy whale statements, however authentic, in these extracts, for veritable gospel cetology.", "그러므로 적어도 모든 경우에 있어, 이 발췌록 안에 담긴 뒤죽박죽 고래 진술들이 아무리 진본이라 한들 그것을 참된 고래학의 복음으로 여겨서는 안 된다. 결코 그래서는 안 된다."], ["Far from it. As touching the ancient authors generally, as well as the poets here appearing, these extracts are solely valuable or entertaining, as affording a glancing bird’s eye view of what has been promiscuously said, thought, fancied, and sung of Leviathan, by many nations and generations, including our own.", "여기 등장하는 시인들은 물론 일반적으로 고대의 저자들에 관해서도, 이 발췌록들은 오로지 우리 자신을 포함한 여러 민족과 세대가 리바이어던(Leviathan)에 관해 두서없이 말하고, 생각하고, 상상하고, 노래해 온 바를 새가 하늘에서 슬쩍 굽어보듯 일별하게 해 준다는 점에서만 가치가 있거나 흥미로운 것이다."]], [["So fare thee well, poor devil of a Sub-Sub, whose commentator I am.", "그러니 잘 가시게, 가련한 보조 사서여, 나는 그대의 주석가일 뿐이다."], ["Thou belongest to that hopeless, sallow tribe which no wine of this world will ever warm; and for whom even Pale Sherry would be too rosy-strong; but with whom one sometimes loves to sit, and feel poor-devilish, too; and grow convivial upon tears; and say to them bluntly, with full eyes and empty glasses, and in not altogether unpleasant sadness—Give it up, Sub-Subs!", "그대는 이 세상 그 어떤 술로도 결코 데워지지 않을 저 희망 없고 누렇게 뜬 부족(部族)에 속해 있다. 그들에게는 창백한 셰리주조차 너무 붉고 독할 터이지만, 그럼에도 더러는 그들과 더불어 앉아 함께 가련해지고 싶어지기도 하는 법, 눈물로 흥에 겨워, 그렁그렁한 눈과 빈 잔을 들고서, 아주 불쾌하지만은 않은 슬픔에 잠겨 그들에게 무뚝뚝하게 말하고 싶어지는 것이다 — 그만두게, 보조 사서들이여!"], ["For by how much the more pains ye take to please the world, by so much the more shall ye for ever go thankless!", "그대들이 세상을 기쁘게 하려 애를 쓰면 쓸수록, 그만큼 더 영영 감사 한마디 받지 못하리니!"], ["Would that I could clear out Hampton Court and the Tuileries for ye!", "그대들을 위해 햄프턴 궁과 튀일리 궁을 통째로 비워 줄 수 있다면 얼마나 좋으련만!"], ["But gulp down your tears and hie aloft to the royal-mast with your hearts; for your friends who have gone before are clearing out the seven-storied heavens, and making refugees of long-pampered Gabriel, Michael, and Raphael, against your coming.", "그러나 눈물일랑 꿀꺽 삼키고, 마음만은 최상부 돛대 꼭대기로 날아오르게나. 그대들보다 먼저 떠나간 벗들이 일곱 층 하늘을 비워 두고, 오래도록 호강하던 가브리엘과 미카엘과 라파엘을 그대들의 도래에 대비해 피난민으로 내몰고 있으니."], ["Here ye strike but splintered hearts together—there, ye shall strike unsplinterable glasses!", "이 세상에서 그대들은 깨어진 마음끼리 부딪칠 뿐이지만 — 저 세상에서는 결코 깨지지 않을 잔들을 마주쳐 울리게 되리라! 발췌록."]], [["“And God created great whales.”", "\"하느님께서 큰 고래들을 창조하셨다.\""], ["—Genesis.", "—창세기."]], [["“Leviathan maketh a path to shine after him; One would think the deep to be hoary.”", "\"리바이어던은 자기가 지나간 뒤에 빛나는 길을 남기니, 깊은 바다가 백발이 된 듯하다.\""], ["—Job.", "—욥기."]], [["“Now the Lord had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah.”", "\"이제 주께서 큰 물고기를 예비하사 요나(Jonah)를 삼키게 하셨더라.\""], ["—Jonah.", "—요나서."]], [["“There go the ships; there is that Leviathan whom thou hast made to play therein.”", "\"저기 배들이 다니고, 주께서 거기서 놀라고 지으신 저 리바이어던이 있나이다.\""], ["—Psalms.", "—시편."]], [["“In that day, the Lord with his sore, and great, and strong sword, shall punish Leviathan the piercing serpent, even Leviathan that crooked serpent; and he shall slay the dragon that is in the sea.”", "\"그날에 주께서 그의 매섭고 크고 강한 칼로 꿰뚫는 뱀 리바이어던, 곧 꼬불꼬불한 뱀 리바이어던을 벌하시며, 바다에 있는 용을 죽이시리라.\""], ["—Isaiah.", "—이사야서."]], [["“And what thing soever besides cometh within the chaos of this monster’s mouth, be it beast, boat, or stone, down it goes all incontinently that foul great swallow of his, and perisheth in the bottomless gulf of his paunch.”", "\"그 밖에 무엇이든 이 괴물의 입이 이루는 혼돈 속으로 들어오는 것은, 짐승이든 배든 돌이든, 그 더럽고 거대한 목구멍 속으로 거침없이 곤두박질쳐 들어가, 밑바닥 없는 그 위장의 심연 속에서 스러지고 만다.\""], ["—Holland’s Plutarch’s Morals.", "—홀랜드 역(譯) 플루타르코스의 모랄리아."]], [["“The Indian Sea breedeth the most and the biggest fishes that are: among which the Whales and Whirlpooles called Balaene, take up as much in length as four acres or arpens of land.”", "\"인도양은 가장 많고 가장 큰 물고기들을 길러 내는데, 그중에서도 발라이네(Balaene)라 불리는 고래와 소용돌이 고래는 그 길이가 네 에이커, 곧 네 아르팡의 땅에 맞먹는다.\""], ["—Holland’s Pliny.", "—홀랜드 역 플리니우스."]], [["“Scarcely had we proceeded two days on the sea, when about sunrise a great many Whales and other monsters of the sea, appeared.", "\"바다로 나선 지 겨우 이틀이 지났을 무렵, 해 뜰 즈음에 수많은 고래들과 다른 바다 괴물들이 나타났다."], ["Among the former, one was of a most monstrous size....", "그중 한 마리는 더없이 괴물 같은 크기였다...."], ["This came towards us, open-mouthed, raising the waves on all sides, and beating the sea before him into a foam.”", "그것이 입을 벌린 채 사방으로 파도를 일으키며, 앞바다를 거품으로 휘몰아치며 우리 쪽으로 다가왔다.\""], ["—Tooke’s Lucian.", "—투크 역 루키아노스."], ["“The True History.”", "\"참된 역사.\""]], [["“He visited this country also with a view of catching horse-whales, which had bones of very great value for their teeth, of which he brought some to the king....", "\"그는 또한 이 나라를 찾아왔는데, 이는 이빨로 인해 매우 값진 뼈를 지닌 바다코끼리를 잡으려는 뜻에서였으며, 그 이빨 몇 개를 왕에게 가져다 바쳤다...."], ["The best whales were catched in his own country, of which some were forty-eight, some fifty yards long.", "가장 좋은 고래들은 그의 고향에서 잡혔는데, 그중 어떤 것은 마흔여덟 야드, 어떤 것은 쉰 야드 길이였다."], ["He said that he was one of six who had killed sixty in two days.”", "그는 자신이 여섯 사람 중 하나로서 이틀 동안 예순 마리를 잡았노라고 말했다.\""], ["—Other or Other’s verbal narrative taken down from his mouth by King Alfred, A.D. 890.", "—890년에 앨프레드 대왕이 오테르(혹은 오테르)의 입에서 직접 받아 적은 구술 이야기."]], [["“And whereas all the other things, whether beast or vessel, that enter into the dreadful gulf of this monster’s (whale’s) mouth, are immediately lost and swallowed up, the sea-gudgeon retires into it in great security, and there sleeps.”", "\"그리고 다른 모든 것들은, 짐승이든 배든, 이 괴물(고래)의 입이 이루는 무시무시한 심연 속으로 들어가면 곧바로 사라지고 삼켜지지만, 바다 모샘치는 그 안으로 아주 안전하게 들어가 거기서 잠을 잔다.\""], ["—MONTAIGNE.", "—몽테뉴."], ["—Apology for Raimond Sebond.", "레몽 스봉을 위한 변호."]], [["“Let us fly, let us fly!", "\"달아나자, 달아나자!"], ["Old Nick take me if it is not Leviathan described by the noble prophet Moses in the life of patient Job.”", "인내심 깊은 욥의 생애 속에서 고귀한 예언자 모세가 묘사한 그 리바이어던이 아니라면 악마가 날 잡아가도 좋다.\""], ["—Rabelais.", "—라블레."]], [["“This whale’s liver was two cartloads.”", "\"이 고래의 간은 짐수레 두 대 분량이었다.\""], ["—Stowe’s Annals.", "—스토우의 연대기."]], [["“The great Leviathan that maketh the seas to seethe like boiling pan.”", "\"바다를 끓는 냄비처럼 들끓게 하는 위대한 리바이어던.\""], ["—Lord Bacon’s Version of the Psalms.", "—베이컨 경의 시편 번역."]], [["“Touching that monstrous bulk of the whale or ork we have received nothing certain.", "\"고래 혹은 오르크라는 저 괴물 같은 거구에 관해서는 우리가 확실히 받아들인 바가 없다."], ["They grow exceeding fat, insomuch that an incredible quantity of oil will be extracted out of one whale.”", "그것들은 엄청나게 살이 쪄서, 고래 한 마리에서 믿기 어려운 양의 기름이 추출된다.\" —같은 책."], ["—Ibid. “History of Life and Death.”", "\"삶과 죽음의 역사.\""]], [["“The sovereignest thing on earth is parmacetti for an inward bruise.”", "\"속의 멍에는 향유고래 기름이 이 세상에서 으뜸가는 약이다.\""], ["—King Henry.", "—헨리 왕."]], [["“Very like a whale.”", "\"꼭 고래 같구나.\""], ["—Hamlet.", "—햄릿."]], [["“Which to secure, no skill of leach’s art Mote him availle, but to returne againe To his wound’s worker, that with lowly dart, Dinting his breast, had bred his restless paine, Like as the wounded whale to shore flies thro’ the maine.”", "\"그것을 막아 보려 한들 의원의 어떤 솜씨도 그에게 소용없으니, 다만 제 상처를 낸 자에게로 되돌아갈 뿐, 낮은 작살이 그 가슴을 찔러 그칠 줄 모르는 고통을 낳았기에, 상처 입은 고래가 망망대해를 가르며 뭍으로 달아나듯이.\""], ["—The Fairie Queen.", "—요정 여왕."]], [["“Immense as whales, the motion of whose vast bodies can in a peaceful calm trouble the ocean till it boil.”", "\"고래처럼 거대하여, 그 거대한 몸뚱이의 움직임이 고요한 정적 속에서도 바다를 들끓을 만큼 휘저어 놓는다.\""], ["—Sir William Davenant.", "—윌리엄 데버넌트 경."], ["Preface to Gondibert.", "곤디버트 서문."]], [["“What spermacetti is, men might justly doubt, since the learned Hosmannus in his work of thirty years, saith plainly, Nescio quid sit.”", "\"향유고래 기름이 무엇인지는 사람들이 마땅히 의심할 만하니, 학식 깊은 호스만누스조차 삼십 년에 걸친 저작에서 분명히 말하기를, '그것이 무엇인지 나는 모른다(Nescio quid sit)'고 하였다.\""], ["—Sir T. Browne.", "—토머스 브라운 경."], ["Of Sperma Ceti and the Sperma Ceti Whale.", "향유고래 기름과 향유고래에 관하여."], ["Vide his V.", "그의 V. E."], ["E.", "참조."]], [["“Like Spencer’s Talus with his modern flail He threatens ruin with his ponderous tail. ...", "\"스펜서의 탈루스가 그 현대적 도리깨를 휘두르듯, 그 묵직한 꼬리로 파멸을 위협한다."], ["Their fixed jav’lins in his side he wears, And on his back a grove of pikes appears.”", "... 그 옆구리에는 박힌 투창들을 지니고, 그 등에는 창들의 숲이 솟아 있다.\""], ["—Waller’s Battle of the Summer Islands.", "—월러의 여름 섬 전투."]], [["“By art is created that great Leviathan, called a Commonwealth or State—(in Latin, Civitas) which is but an artificial man.”", "\"기예로써 저 위대한 리바이어던, 곧 코먼웰스 또는 국가(라틴어로 Civitas)라 불리는 것이 창조되는데, 그것은 다만 인공의 인간일 따름이다.\""], ["—Opening sentence of Hobbes’s Leviathan.", "—홉스의 리바이어던 첫 문장."]], [["“Silly Mansoul swallowed it without chewing, as if it had been a sprat in the mouth of a whale.”", "\"어리석은 맨소울은 그것을 씹지도 않고 삼켰으니, 마치 고래 입 속의 청어 새끼와도 같았다.\""], ["—Pilgrim’s Progress.", "—천로역정."]], [["“That sea beast Leviathan, which God of all his works Created hugest that swim the ocean stream.”", "\"하느님께서 당신의 모든 작품 가운데 바다의 물줄기를 헤엄치는 것 중 가장 거대하게 지으신 저 바다 짐승 리바이어던.\""], ["—Paradise Lost.", "—실낙원."]], [["—“There Leviathan, Hugest of living creatures, in the deep Stretched like a promontory sleeps or swims, And seems a moving land; and at his gills Draws in, and at his breath spouts out a sea.”", "—\"저기 리바이어던, 살아 있는 피조물 가운데 가장 거대한 것이, 깊은 바다 속에 곶처럼 길게 뻗어 잠들거나 헤엄치며, 움직이는 땅덩어리처럼 보이고, 아가미로는 물을 빨아들이고 숨결로는 바다를 뿜어낸다.\""], ["—Ibid.", "—같은 책."]], [["“The mighty whales which swim in a sea of water, and have a sea of oil swimming in them.”", "\"물의 바다 속을 헤엄치며, 제 몸속에는 기름의 바다를 헤엄치게 하는 저 거대한 고래들.\""], ["—Fuller’s Profane and Holy State.", "—풀러의 세속과 성스러운 나라."]], [["“So close behind some promontory lie The huge Leviathan to attend their prey, And give no chance, but swallow in the fry, Which through their gaping jaws mistake the way.”", "\"어느 곶 뒤에 바싹 붙어, 거대한 리바이어던이 먹이를 노리며 도사리고서, 한 치의 기회도 주지 않은 채, 그 떡 벌린 아가리 속으로 길을 잘못 든 작은 물고기 떼를 통째로 삼켜 버린다.\""], ["—Dryden’s Annus Mirabilis.", "—드라이든의 기적의 해."]], [["“While the whale is floating at the stern of the ship, they cut off his head, and tow it with a boat as near the shore as it will come; but it will be aground in twelve or thirteen feet water.”", "\"고래가 배의 고물 쪽에 떠 있는 동안, 그들은 그 머리를 잘라 내어 보트로 뭍에 닿을 수 있는 데까지 최대한 가까이 끌고 가는데, 다만 물 깊이 열두 자나 열세 자쯤 되는 곳에서 바닥에 닿고 만다.\""], ["—Thomas Edge’s Ten Voyages to Spitzbergen, in Purchas.", "—토머스 에지의 스피츠베르겐 열 차례 항해, 퍼처스 수록."]], [["“In their way they saw many whales sporting in the ocean, and in wantonness fuzzing up the water through their pipes and vents, which nature has placed on their shoulders.”", "\"그들은 가는 길에 수많은 고래들이 바다에서 노니는 것을 보았는데, 그것들은 멋대로 장난을 치며 자연이 그 어깨 위에 마련해 준 관과 구멍으로 물을 뿜어 올리고 있었다.\""], ["—Sir T. Herbert’s Voyages into Asia and Africa.", "—토머스 허버트 경의 아시아 아프리카 항해기."], ["Harris Coll.", "해리스 총서."]], [["“Here they saw such huge troops of whales, that they were forced to proceed with a great deal of caution for fear they should run their ship upon them.”", "\"여기서 그들은 어찌나 거대한 고래 떼를 보았던지, 행여 배가 그것들 위로 올라타지나 않을까 두려워 몹시 조심하며 나아갈 수밖에 없었다.\""], ["—Schouten’s Sixth Circumnavigation.", "—스호우텐의 여섯 번째 세계 일주."]], [["“We set sail from the Elbe, wind N.E. in the ship called The Jonas-in-the-Whale....", "\"우리는 북동풍을 받으며 '고래 뱃속의 요나'라는 이름의 배를 타고 엘베강에서 출항했다...."], ["Some say the whale can’t open his mouth, but that is a fable....", "어떤 이들은 고래가 입을 벌리지 못한다고 하나, 그것은 꾸며 낸 이야기다...."], ["They frequently climb up the masts to see whether they can see a whale, for the first discoverer has a ducat for his pains....", "그들은 고래가 보이는지 살피러 자주 돛대 위로 기어오르는데, 처음 발견한 자에게는 그 수고의 대가로 두카트 금화 한 닢이 주어지기 때문이다...."], ["I was told of a whale taken near Shetland, that had above a barrel of herrings in his belly....", "셰틀랜드 근처에서 잡힌 어느 고래는 뱃속에 청어가 한 통이 넘게 들어 있었다는 이야기를 들었다...."], ["One of our harpooneers told me that he caught once a whale in Spitzbergen that was white all over.”", "우리 작살잡이 중 하나가 내게 말하기를, 자기가 한번은 스피츠베르겐에서 온몸이 새하얀 고래를 잡은 적이 있다고 했다.\""], ["—A Voyage to Greenland, A.D. 1671.", "—그린란드 항해기, 1671년."], ["Harris Coll.", "해리스 총서."]], [["“Several whales have come in upon this coast (Fife) Anno 1652, one eighty feet in length of the whale-bone kind came in, which (as I was informed), besides a vast quantity of oil, did afford 500 weight of baleen.", "\"1652년에 이 해안(파이프)으로 고래 몇 마리가 떠밀려 왔는데, 그중 길이 여든 자나 되는 고래수염 종류 한 마리가 들어왔다. (내가 들은 바로는) 이놈은 막대한 양의 기름 외에도 고래수염 500웨이트를 내주었다고 한다."], ["The jaws of it stand for a gate in the garden of Pitferren.”", "그 턱뼈는 핏페런 정원에서 문 노릇을 하고 있다.\""], ["—Sibbald’s Fife and Kinross.", "—시발드의 파이프와 킨로스."]], [["“Myself have agreed to try whether I can master and kill this Sperma-ceti whale, for I could never hear of any of that sort that was killed by any man, such is his fierceness and swiftness.” —Richard Strafford’s Letter from the Bermudas.", "\"나 자신이 이 향유고래를 제압하여 죽일 수 있는지 시험해 보기로 했으니, 이는 그토록 사납고 빠른 까닭에 그런 종류를 어느 누가 죽였다는 소리를 내 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없기 때문이다.\""], ["Phil. Trans.", "—리처드 스트래퍼드가 버뮤다에서 보낸 편지."], ["A.D. 1668.", "철학회보, 1668년."]], [["“Whales in the sea God’s voice obey.” —N.", "\"바다의 고래들은 하느님의 목소리에 복종하느니라.\""], ["E. Primer.", "—뉴잉글랜드 입문서."]], [["“We saw also abundance of large whales, there being more in those southern seas, as I may say, by a hundred to one; than we have to the northward of us.”", "\"우리는 또한 큰 고래들을 무수히 보았는데, 말하자면 우리 북쪽 바다보다 그 남쪽 바다에 백 배는 더 많았다.\""], ["—Captain Cowley’s Voyage round the Globe, A.D. 1729.", "—카울리 선장의 세계 일주 항해기, 1729년."]], [["“...", "\"..."], ["and the breath of the whale is frequently attended with such an insupportable smell, as to bring on a disorder of the brain.”", "그리고 고래의 숨결에는 흔히 견딜 수 없는 악취가 따라붙어, 뇌에 이상을 일으킬 정도다.\""], ["—Ulloa’s South America.", "—우요아의 남아메리카."]], [["“To fifty chosen sylphs of special note, We trust the important charge, the petticoat.", "\"특별히 이름난 쉰 명의 정예 실프에게 우리는 그 중대한 임무, 곧 페티코트를 맡기노라."], ["Oft have we known that seven-fold fence to fail, Tho’ stuffed with hoops and armed with ribs of whale.”", "후프로 채워 넣고 고래 갈빗대로 무장한들, 그 일곱 겹 울타리가 무너지는 일을 우리는 종종 보아 왔으니.\""], ["—Rape of the Lock.", "—머리타래의 강탈."]], [["“If we compare land animals in respect to magnitude, with those that take up their abode in the deep, we shall find they will appear contemptible in the comparison.", "\"육지의 동물을 그 크기로 깊은 바다에 깃들어 사는 것들과 견주어 보면, 그 비교에서 육지 동물은 보잘것없어 보일 것이다."], ["The whale is doubtless the largest animal in creation.”", "고래는 의심할 바 없이 만물 가운데 가장 큰 동물이다.\""], ["—Goldsmith, Nat. Hist.", "—골드스미스, 박물지."]], [["“If you should write a fable for little fishes, you would make them speak like great whales.”", "\"작은 물고기들을 위한 우화를 쓴다면, 자네는 그것들이 큰 고래처럼 말하게 만들 걸세.\""], ["—Goldsmith to Johnson.", "—골드스미스가 존슨에게."]], [["“In the afternoon we saw what was supposed to be a rock, but it was found to be a dead whale, which some Asiatics had killed, and were then towing ashore.", "\"오후에 우리는 바위로 짐작되는 것을 보았으나, 그것이 죽은 고래임이 드러났다. 어떤 아시아인들이 그것을 죽여 뭍으로 끌고 가던 중이었다."], ["They seemed to endeavor to conceal themselves behind the whale, in order to avoid being seen by us.”", "그들은 우리 눈에 띄지 않으려고 고래 뒤에 몸을 숨기려 애쓰는 듯했다.\""], ["—Cook’s Voyages.", "—쿡의 항해기."]], [["“The larger whales, they seldom venture to attack.", "\"더 큰 고래들은 그들이 좀처럼 공격하려 들지 않는다."], ["They stand in so great dread of some of them, that when out at sea they are afraid to mention even their names, and carry dung, lime-stone, juniper-wood, and some other articles of the same nature in their boats, in order to terrify and prevent their too near approach.”", "그중 어떤 것들은 어찌나 무서워하는지, 바다에 나가면 그 이름조차 입에 올리기를 꺼리며, 그것들이 너무 가까이 다가오는 것을 겁주어 막으려고 똥과 석회석과 향나무, 그 밖에 같은 종류의 물건들을 보트에 싣고 다닌다.\""], ["—Uno Von Troil’s Letters on Banks’s and Solander’s Voyage to Iceland in 1772.", "—우노 폰 트로일의 편지, 1772년 뱅크스와 솔란데르의 아이슬란드 항해에 관하여."]], [["“The Spermacetti Whale found by the Nantuckois, is an active, fierce animal, and requires vast address and boldness in the fishermen.”", "\"낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들이 발견한 향유고래는 활기차고 사나운 동물이어서, 어부들에게 막대한 기량과 담력을 요한다.\""], ["—Thomas Jefferson’s Whale Memorial to the French minister in 1778.", "—토머스 제퍼슨이 1778년 프랑스 대신에게 보낸 고래 각서."]], [["“And pray, sir, what in the world is equal to it?”", "\"그런데 선생, 도대체 세상에 무엇이 그것에 견줄 수 있단 말이오?\""], ["—Edmund Burke’s reference in Parliament to the Nantucket Whale-Fishery.", "—에드먼드 버크가 의회에서 낸터킷 고래잡이를 언급하며."]], [["“Spain—a great whale stranded on the shores of Europe.”", "\"스페인 — 유럽의 해안에 좌초한 거대한 고래.\""], ["—Edmund Burke.", "—에드먼드 버크."], ["(somewhere.)", "(어딘가에서.)"]], [["“A tenth branch of the king’s ordinary revenue, said to be grounded on the consideration of his guarding and protecting the seas from pirates and robbers, is the right to royal fish, which are whale and sturgeon.", "\"국왕의 통상 세입 가운데 열 번째 항목은, 바다를 해적과 도적으로부터 지키고 보호하는 데 대한 고려에서 비롯되었다고 하는데, 바로 '왕실' 어류에 대한 권리로서, 이는 고래와 철갑상어다."], ["And these, when either thrown ashore or caught near the coast, are the property of the king.”", "그리고 이것들이 뭍으로 떠밀려 오거나 해안 가까이에서 잡히면 국왕의 소유가 된다.\""], ["—Blackstone.", "—블랙스톤."]], [["“Soon to the sport of death the crews repair: Rodmond unerring o’er his head suspends The barbed steel, and every turn attends.”", "\"머지않아 선원들은 죽음의 놀이를 향해 모여드니, 로드먼드는 빗나감 없이 미늘 박힌 강철을 머리 위로 치켜들고, 매 순간을 노린다.\""], ["—Falconer’s Shipwreck.", "—팰코너의 난파선."]], [["“Bright shone the roofs, the domes, the spires, And rockets blew self driven, To hang their momentary fire Around the vault of heaven.", "\"지붕도, 둥근 천장도, 첨탑도 환히 빛나고, 로켓들은 스스로 솟구쳐 올라, 그 찰나의 불꽃을 하늘 궁창 둘레에 매달았네."]], [["“So fire with water to compare, The ocean serves on high, Up-spouted by a whale in air, To express unwieldy joy.”", "그리하여 불을 물에 견주노라면, 바다가 저 높은 곳에서 한몫을 하니, 고래가 허공으로 뿜어 올리는 그 물줄기로, 주체할 수 없는 기쁨을 드러내는도다.\""], ["—Cowper, on the Queen’s Visit to London.", "—쿠퍼, 여왕의 런던 방문에 부쳐."]], [["“Ten or fifteen gallons of blood are thrown out of the heart at a stroke, with immense velocity.”", "\"한 번 박동할 때마다 심장에서 열 갤런 내지 열다섯 갤런의 피가 어마어마한 속도로 뿜어져 나온다.\""], ["—John Hunter’s account of the dissection of a whale.", "—존 헌터의 고래 해부 기록."], ["(A small sized one.)", "(작은 크기의 것.)"]], [["“The aorta of a whale is larger in the bore than the main pipe of the water-works at London Bridge, and the water roaring in its passage through that pipe is inferior in impetus and velocity to the blood gushing from the whale’s heart.”", "\"고래의 대동맥은 그 구멍이 런던 다리 상수도의 본관보다 더 굵으며, 그 관을 통해 우렁차게 흐르는 물조차 고래 심장에서 솟구치는 피의 기세와 속도에는 미치지 못한다.\""], ["—Paley’s Theology.", "—페일리의 신학."]], [["“The whale is a mammiferous animal without hind feet.”", "\"고래는 뒷다리가 없는 포유동물이다.\""], ["—Baron Cuvier.", "—퀴비에 남작."]], [["“In 40 degrees south, we saw Spermacetti Whales, but did not take any till the first of May, the sea being then covered with them.”", "\"남위 40도에서 우리는 향유고래들을 보았으나, 5월 1일에 이르러서야 비로소 한 마리를 잡았는데, 그때는 바다가 온통 그것들로 뒤덮여 있었다.\""], ["—Colnett’s Voyage for the Purpose of Extending the Spermaceti Whale Fishery.", "—콜넷의 향유고래잡이 확장을 위한 항해."]], [["“In the free element beneath me swam, Floundered and dived, in play, in chace, in battle, Fishes of every colour, form, and kind; Which language cannot paint, and mariner Had never seen; from dread Leviathan To insect millions peopling every wave: Gather’d in shoals immense, like floating islands, Led by mysterious instincts through that waste And trackless region, though on every side Assaulted by voracious enemies, Whales, sharks, and monsters, arm’d in front or jaw, With swords, saws, spiral horns, or hooked fangs.”", "\"내 발밑의 자유로운 원소 속에서, 온갖 빛깔과 모양과 종류의 물고기들이 헤엄치고, 허우적대며 자맥질하니, 놀이로, 추격으로, 싸움으로 — 그 모습은 언어로도 그려 낼 수 없고 어떤 뱃사람도 본 적 없는 것이라. 무시무시한 리바이어던에서부터 물결마다 가득 채운 수백만의 곤충에 이르기까지, 떠다니는 섬처럼 거대한 무리를 이루어 모여들어, 신비로운 본능에 이끌려 저 황량하고 길 없는 영역을 가로지르며, 사방에서 게걸스러운 적들에게 — 고래와 상어와 괴물들, 그 앞머리나 턱에 칼과 톱과 나선 뿔과 갈고리 송곳니로 무장한 것들에게 공격받으면서도.\""], ["—Montgomery’s World before the Flood.", "—몽고메리의 대홍수 이전의 세계."]], [["“Io!", "\"이오!"], ["Paean! Io!", "파이안!"], ["sing.", "이오!"], ["To the finny people’s king.", "노래하라. 지느러미 백성의 왕에게."], ["Not a mightier whale than this In the vast Atlantic is; Not a fatter fish than he, Flounders round the Polar Sea.”", "이놈보다 더 힘센 고래는 저 광대한 대서양에도 없으며, 이놈보다 더 살진 물고기는 극지의 바다를 헤매어도 없으리라.\""], ["—Charles Lamb’s Triumph of the Whale.", "—찰스 램의 고래의 개선."]], [["“In the year 1690 some persons were on a high hill observing the whales spouting and sporting with each other, when one observed: there—pointing to the sea—is a green pasture where our children’s grand-children will go for bread.”", "\"1690년에 어떤 이들이 높은 언덕 위에서 고래들이 물을 뿜고 서로 어울려 노니는 것을 바라보고 있었는데, 그중 한 사람이 바다를 가리키며 이렇게 말했다. 저기가 — 우리 자손의 자손이 빵을 구하러 갈 푸른 초원이라네.\""], ["—Obed Macy’s History of Nantucket.", "—오베드 메이시의 낸터킷 역사."]], [["“I built a cottage for Susan and myself and made a gateway in the form of a Gothic Arch, by setting up a whale’s jaw bones.”", "\"나는 수전과 나를 위해 오두막 한 채를 지었고, 고래 턱뼈를 세워 고딕식 아치 모양의 출입문을 만들었다.\""], ["—Hawthorne’s Twice Told Tales.", "—호손의 두 번 들려준 이야기."]], [["“She came to bespeak a monument for her first love, who had been killed by a whale in the Pacific ocean, no less than forty years ago.”", "\"그녀는 자신의 첫사랑을 위한 비석을 부탁하러 왔는데, 그 사람은 무려 사십 년 전에 태평양에서 고래에게 목숨을 잃었다고 했다.\""], ["—Ibid.", "—같은 책."]], [["“No, Sir, ’tis a Right Whale,”", "\"아니올시다, 나리, 저건 참고래입니다.\" 톰이 대답했다."], ["answered Tom; “I saw his sprout; he threw up a pair of as pretty rainbows as a Christian would wish to look at.", "\"물줄기를 봤습죠. 기독교인이라면 누구나 보고 싶어 할 만큼 어여쁜 무지개를 한 쌍이나 뿜어 올렸거든요."], ["He’s a raal oil-butt, that fellow!”", "저놈은 진짜 기름통이올시다, 저 녀석은!\""], ["—Cooper’s Pilot.", "—쿠퍼의 도선사."]], [["“The papers were brought in, and we saw in the Berlin Gazette that whales had been introduced on the stage there.”", "\"신문이 들어왔고, 우리는 베를린 가제트에서 그곳 무대에 고래가 등장했다는 기사를 보았다.\""], ["—Eckermann’s Conversations with Goethe.", "—에커만의 괴테와의 대화."]], [["“My God! Mr.", "\"맙소사!"], ["Chace, what is the matter?”", "체이스 씨, 무슨 일이오?\""], ["I answered, “we have been stove by a whale.”", "나는 대답했다. \"고래에게 들이받혔습니다.\""], ["—“Narrative of the Shipwreck of the Whale Ship Essex of Nantucket, which was attacked and finally destroyed by a large Sperm Whale in the Pacific Ocean.”", "—\"태평양에서 거대한 향유고래에게 공격받아 끝내 파괴된 낸터킷 포경선 에식스호의 난파에 관한 이야기.\""], ["By Owen Chace of Nantucket, first mate of said vessel.", "그 배의 일등 항해사였던 낸터킷의 오언 체이스 지음."], ["New York, 1821.", "뉴욕, 1821년."]], [["“A mariner sat in the shrouds one night, The wind was piping free; Now bright, now dimmed, was the moonlight pale, And the phospher gleamed in the wake of the whale, As it floundered in the sea.”", "\"어느 밤 한 뱃사람이 삭구 사이에 앉아 있었네, 바람은 거침없이 휘파람을 불었고, 창백한 달빛은 밝아졌다 흐려졌다 하였으며, 고래가 바다에서 허우적댈 때 그 항적에는 인광이 번득였네.\""], ["—Elizabeth Oakes Smith.", "—엘리자베스 오크스 스미스."]], [["“The quantity of line withdrawn from the boats engaged in the capture of this one whale, amounted altogether to 10,440 yards or nearly six English miles....", "\"이 고래 한 마리를 잡는 데 가담한 보트들에서 풀려나간 밧줄의 길이는 통틀어 1만 440야드, 곧 거의 영국 거리로 6마일에 달했다...."]], [["“Sometimes the whale shakes its tremendous tail in the air, which, cracking like a whip, resounds to the distance of three or four miles.”", "\"때로 고래는 그 무시무시한 꼬리를 허공에 흔들어 대는데, 그것이 채찍처럼 철썩 소리를 내며 삼사 마일 거리까지 울려 퍼진다.\""], ["—Scoresby.", "—스코어스비."]], [["“Mad with the agonies he endures from these fresh attacks, the infuriated Sperm Whale rolls over and over; he rears his enormous head, and with wide expanded jaws snaps at everything around him; he rushes at the boats with his head; they are propelled before him with vast swiftness, and sometimes utterly destroyed....", "\"이 새로운 공격으로 겪는 고통에 미쳐 날뛰는 성난 향유고래는 몇 번이고 몸을 뒤집으며, 그 거대한 머리를 쳐들고, 떡 벌린 아가리로 주위의 모든 것을 물어뜯는다. 그것은 머리로 보트들을 향해 돌진하니, 보트들은 그 앞에서 엄청난 속도로 떠밀려 나가고, 때로는 완전히 박살 나고 만다...."], ["It is a matter of great astonishment that the consideration of the habits of so interesting, and, in a commercial point of view, so important an animal (as the Sperm Whale) should have been so entirely neglected, or should have excited so little curiosity among the numerous, and many of them competent observers, that of late years, must have possessed the most abundant and the most convenient opportunities of witnessing their habitudes.”", "참으로 놀라운 일은, 이토록 흥미로우면서도 상업적 관점에서 그토록 중요한 동물(향유고래)의 습성에 대한 고찰이 그처럼 철저히 등한시되어 왔다는 점, 또는 근래에 그 습성을 관찰할 가장 풍부하고 가장 편리한 기회를 분명히 누렸을 수많은 관찰자들, 그것도 그중 다수가 유능한 관찰자들이었음에도 그토록 적은 호기심밖에 불러일으키지 못했다는 점이다.\""], ["—Thomas Beale’s History of the Sperm Whale, 1839.", "—토머스 빌의 향유고래의 역사, 1839년."]], [["“The Cachalot” (Sperm Whale) “is not only better armed than the True Whale”", "\"카샬로트('향유고래')는 참고래('그린란드 고래'"], ["(Greenland or Right Whale) “in possessing a formidable weapon at either extremity of its body, but also more frequently displays a disposition to employ these weapons offensively and in manner at once so artful, bold, and mischievous, as to lead to its being regarded as the most dangerous to attack of all the known species of the whale tribe.”", "또는 '참고래')보다 더 잘 무장되어 있을 뿐 아니라 — 몸의 양쪽 끝에 가공할 무기를 지니고 있기 때문이거니와 — 그 무기들을 공격적으로, 그것도 더없이 교묘하고 대담하며 짓궂은 방식으로 즐겨 휘두르는 기질을 더 자주 드러내는 까닭에, 알려진 모든 고래 종족 가운데 공격하기에 가장 위험한 것으로 여겨지기에 이른다.\""], ["—Frederick Debell Bennett’s Whaling Voyage Round the Globe, 1840.", "—프레더릭 데벨 베닛의 세계 일주 포경 항해, 1840년."]], [["October 13.", "10월 13일."], ["“There she blows,”", "\"저기 물 뿜는다!\""], ["was sung out from the mast-head.", "돛대 꼭대기에서 외치는 소리가 울려 퍼졌다. \"어디냐?\""], ["“Where away?”", "선장이 물었다."], ["demanded the captain. “Three points off the lee bow, sir.”", "\"바람 불어 가는 쪽 이물에서 세 점 떨어진 곳입니다, 나리.\""], ["“Raise up your wheel.", "\"키를 돌려라. 침착하게!\""], ["Steady!” “Steady, sir.”", "\"침착하게, 나리.\""], ["“Mast-head ahoy!", "\"돛대 꼭대기!"], ["Do you see that whale now?”", "이제 그 고래가 보이느냐?\""], ["“Ay ay, sir!", "\"예, 예, 나리!"], ["A shoal of Sperm Whales!", "향유고래 떼입니다!"], ["There she blows!", "저기 물 뿜는다!"], ["There she breaches!”", "저기 솟구쳐 오른다!\""], ["“Sing out!", "\"외쳐라!"], ["sing out every time!”", "보일 때마다 외쳐!\""], ["“Ay Ay, sir!", "\"예, 예, 나리!"], ["There she blows!", "저기 물 뿜는다!"], ["there—there—thar she blows—bowes—bo-o-os!”", "저기 — 저기 — 저어기 물 뿜는다 — 뿜는다 — 뿌우운다!\""], ["“How far off?”", "\"거리는?\""], ["“Two miles and a half.”", "\"이 마일 반입니다.\""], ["“Thunder and lightning!", "\"천둥 번개로다! 그토록 가깝다니!"], ["so near! Call all hands.”", "전원 집합시켜라.\""], ["—J.", "—J."], ["Ross Browne’s Etchings of a Whaling Cruize.", "로스 브라운의 포경 항해 스케치."], ["1846.", "1846년."]], [["“The Whale-ship Globe, on board of which vessel occurred the horrid transactions we are about to relate, belonged to the island of Nantucket.”", "\"이제부터 우리가 이야기하려는 그 끔찍한 사건들이 벌어진 포경선 글로브호는 낸터킷섬 소속이었다.\""], ["—“Narrative of the Globe Mutiny,”", "—\"글로브호 반란 이야기.\""], ["by Lay and Hussey survivors.", "생존자 레이와 허시 지음."], ["A.D. 1828.", "1828년."]], [["Being once pursued by a whale which he had wounded, he parried the assault for some time with a lance; but the furious monster at length rushed on the boat; himself and comrades only being preserved by leaping into the water when they saw the onset was inevitable.”", "\"그는 자기가 부상을 입힌 고래에게 한번 쫓기던 차에, 한동안 창으로 그 공격을 막아 냈다. 그러나 성난 괴물이 끝내 보트로 돌진해 왔고, 그와 동료들은 그 돌격이 피할 수 없음을 깨닫고 물속으로 뛰어든 덕분에 가까스로 목숨을 건졌다.\""], ["—Missionary Journal of Tyerman and Bennett.", "—타이어먼과 베닛의 선교 일지."]], [["“Nantucket itself,” said Mr.", "\"낸터킷 그 자체가,\""], ["Webster, “is a very striking and peculiar portion of the National interest.", "웹스터 씨가 말했다, \"국가적 이익에서 매우 두드러지고 특이한 부분입니다."], ["There is a population of eight or nine thousand persons living here in the sea, adding largely every year to the National wealth by the boldest and most persevering industry.”", "여기 바다 위에 팔구천 명의 인구가 살면서, 가장 대담하고 가장 끈질긴 근면으로 해마다 국부에 크게 보태고 있습니다.\""], ["—Report of Daniel Webster’s Speech in the U. S.", "—낸터킷에 방파제를 세우자는 청원에 관한, 미국 상원에서 행한 대니얼 웹스터의 연설 보고서."], ["Senate, on the application for the Erection of a Breakwater at Nantucket. 1828.", "1828년."]], [["“The whale fell directly over him, and probably killed him in a moment.”", "\"고래가 그의 바로 위로 떨어졌고, 아마도 한순간에 그를 죽였을 것이다.\""], ["—“The Whale and his Captors, or The Whaleman’s Adventures and the Whale’s Biography, gathered on the Homeward Cruise of the Commodore Preble.”", "—\"고래와 그 포획자들, 혹은 포경꾼의 모험과 고래의 전기, 커모도어 프레블호의 귀항 항해에서 모은 이야기.\""], ["By Rev.", "헨리 T."], ["Henry T. Cheever.", "치버 목사 지음."]], [["“If you make the least damn bit of noise,”", "\"네가 손톱만큼이라도 소리를 낸다면,\""], ["replied Samuel, “I will send you to hell.”", "새뮤얼이 대답했다, \"널 지옥으로 보내 버리겠다.\""], ["—Life of Samuel Comstock (the mutineer), by his brother, William Comstock.", "—새뮤얼 콤스톡(그 반란자)의 생애, 그의 동생 윌리엄 콤스톡 지음."], ["Another Version of the whale-ship Globe narrative.", "포경선 글로브호 이야기의 또 다른 판본."]], [["“The voyages of the Dutch and English to the Northern Ocean, in order, if possible, to discover a passage through it to India, though they failed of their main object, laid-open the haunts of the whale.”", "\"네덜란드인과 영국인이 가능하다면 인도로 통하는 항로를 발견하려고 북극해로 떠난 항해들은, 비록 그 주된 목적은 이루지 못했으나, 고래의 서식처를 활짝 열어 보였다.\""], ["—McCulloch’s Commercial Dictionary.", "—매컬럭의 상업 사전."]], [["“These things are reciprocal; the ball rebounds, only to bound forward again; for now in laying open the haunts of the whale, the whalemen seem to have indirectly hit upon new clews to that same mystic North-West Passage.”", "\"이런 일들은 서로 주고받는 것이다. 공은 다시 앞으로 튀어 나가려 되튀어 오르는 법, 이제 고래의 서식처를 열어 보이는 가운데, 포경꾼들은 다름 아닌 저 신비로운 북서 항로로 향하는 새로운 실마리를 우연히 짚어 낸 듯하다.\""], ["—From “Something” unpublished.", "—미간행 \"어떤 글\"에서."]], [["“It is impossible to meet a whale-ship on the ocean without being struck by her near appearance.", "\"바다에서 포경선과 마주치고서 그 가까운 모습에 강한 인상을 받지 않기란 불가능하다."], ["The vessel under short sail, with look-outs at the mast-heads, eagerly scanning the wide expanse around them, has a totally different air from those engaged in regular voyage.”", "돛을 줄이고, 돛대 꼭대기마다 망꾼을 세워 둔 채 주위의 광활한 바다를 열심히 살피는 그 배는, 통상적인 항해에 나선 배들과는 전혀 다른 분위기를 풍긴다.\""], ["—Currents and Whaling. U.S.", "—해류와 포경."], ["Ex. Ex.", "미국 탐사 원정대."]], [["“Pedestrians in the vicinity of London and elsewhere may recollect having seen large curved bones set upright in the earth, either to form arches over gateways, or entrances to alcoves, and they may perhaps have been told that these were the ribs of whales.”", "\"런던 근교와 그 밖의 여러 곳을 거니는 사람들이라면, 땅 위에 곧추세워진 커다랗게 휘어진 뼈들을 본 기억이 있을 것이다. 그것들은 문 위에 아치를 이루거나 정자의 입구를 이루고 있었는데, 어쩌면 그들은 그것이 고래의 갈빗대라는 이야기를 들었을지도 모른다.\""], ["—Tales of a Whale Voyager to the Arctic Ocean.", "—북극해로 떠난 어느 고래 항해자의 이야기."]], [["“It was not till the boats returned from the pursuit of these whales, that the whites saw their ship in bloody possession of the savages enrolled among the crew.”", "\"바로 이 고래들을 쫓아 떠났던 보트들이 돌아오고 나서야, 백인들은 자기네 배가, 선원으로 받아들인 야만인들에게 피투성이로 점령당한 것을 보았다.\""], ["—Newspaper Account of the Taking and Retaking of the Whale-Ship Hobomack.", "—포경선 호보맥호의 탈취와 탈환에 관한 신문 기사."]], [["“It is generally well known that out of the crews of Whaling vessels (American) few ever return in the ships on board of which they departed.”", "\"(미국) 포경선의 선원들 가운데 자기가 떠날 때 탔던 그 배를 타고 돌아오는 자가 거의 없다는 것은 일반적으로 잘 알려진 사실이다.\""], ["—Cruise in a Whale Boat.", "—포경 보트에서의 항해."]], [["“Suddenly a mighty mass emerged from the water, and shot up perpendicularly into the air.", "\"별안간 거대한 덩어리가 물속에서 솟아올라, 허공으로 수직으로 치솟았다."], ["It was the whale.”", "그것은 고래였다.\""], ["—Miriam Coffin or the Whale Fisherman.", "—미리엄 코핀, 혹은 고래잡이 어부."]], [["“The Whale is harpooned to be sure; but bethink you, how you would manage a powerful unbroken colt, with the mere appliance of a rope tied to the root of his tail.”", "\"고래는 물론 작살에 찔린다. 그러나 한번 생각해 보라, 그저 꼬리 밑동에 밧줄 한 가닥을 묶었을 뿐인데 힘세고 길들지 않은 망아지를 어떻게 다스리겠는가.\""], ["—A Chapter on Whaling in Ribs and Trucks.", "—갈빗대와 수레바퀴 속 포경에 관한 한 장(章)."]], [["“On one occasion I saw two of these monsters (whales) probably male and female, slowly swimming, one after the other, within less than a stone’s throw of the shore”", "\"한번은 이 괴물(고래) 두 마리, 아마도 수컷과 암컷인 듯한 것들이 돌 던지면 닿을 만한 거리도 안 되는 해안 가까이에서, 한 마리가 다른 한 마리를 뒤따르며 천천히 헤엄치는 것을 보았다(티에라델푸에고)."], ["(Terra Del Fuego), “over which the beech tree extended its branches.”", "그 위로는 너도밤나무가 가지를 드리우고 있었다.\""], ["—Darwin’s Voyage of a Naturalist.", "—다윈의 어느 박물학자의 항해."]], [["“‘Stern all!’ exclaimed the mate, as upon turning his head, he saw the distended jaws of a large Sperm Whale close to the head of the boat, threatening it with instant destruction;—‘Stern all, for your lives!’”", "\"'전원 뒤로 저어라!' 항해사가 고개를 돌리다가, 거대한 향유고래가 떡 벌린 아가리를 보트 머리 바로 곁에 들이대고 당장이라도 박살 낼 듯 위협하는 것을 보고 외쳤다 — '전원 뒤로 저어라, 목숨이 아깝거든!'\""], ["—Wharton the Whale Killer.", "—고래 잡이 와튼."]], [["“So be cheery, my lads, let your hearts never fail, While the bold harpooneer is striking the whale!”", "\"그러니 기운을 내게, 친구들이여, 대담한 작살잡이가 고래를 찔러 대는 동안, 그대들의 마음일랑 결코 꺾이지 말기를!\""], ["—Nantucket Song.", "—낸터킷 노래."]], [["“Oh, the rare old Whale, mid storm and gale In his ocean home will be A giant in might, where might is right, And King of the boundless sea.”", "\"오, 진귀하고 늙은 고래여, 폭풍과 강풍 한가운데 그 바다 보금자리에서, 힘이 곧 정의인 곳의 강자가 되어, 가없는 바다의 왕이 되리라.\""], ["—Whale Song.", "—고래의 노래."]], [["# CHAPTER 1. Loomings.", "# CHAPTER 1. Loomings."]], [["Call me Ishmael.", "나를 이슈메일이라 불러 다오."], ["Some years ago—never mind how long precisely—having little or no money in my purse, and nothing particular to interest me on shore, I thought I would sail about a little and see the watery part of the world.", "몇 해 전 — 정확히 얼마나 됐는지는 아무래도 좋다 — 지갑에는 돈이 거의 혹은 전혀 없었고, 뭍에서는 딱히 흥미를 끄는 일도 없었기에, 나는 잠시 배를 타고 떠돌며 이 세상의 바닷물 진 부분이나 구경해 볼까 생각했다."], ["It is a way I have of driving off the spleen and regulating the circulation.", "그것은 내가 우울을 떨쳐 내고 피의 순환을 다스리는 방식이다."], ["Whenever I find myself growing grim about the mouth; whenever it is a damp, drizzly November in my soul; whenever I find myself involuntarily pausing before coffin warehouses, and bringing up the rear of every funeral I meet; and especially whenever my hypos get such an upper hand of me, that it requires a strong moral principle to prevent me from deliberately stepping into the street, and methodically knocking people’s hats off—then, I account it high time to get to sea as soon as I can.", "입가가 험상궂게 일그러지는 것을 느낄 때마다, 내 영혼에 축축하고 보슬비 내리는 11월이 깃들 때마다, 나도 모르게 관(棺) 파는 가게 앞에서 발을 멈추고 마주치는 장례 행렬마다 그 꽁무니를 따라가게 될 때마다, 그리고 특히 내 우울증이 어찌나 나를 짓누르는지 거리로 성큼 걸어 나가 사람들의 모자를 차례차례 후려쳐 떨어뜨리지 못하도록 막으려면 굳건한 도덕적 원칙이 필요해질 때마다 — 그럴 때면 나는 되도록 빨리 바다로 나가야 할 때가 되었다고 여긴다."], ["This is my substitute for pistol and ball.", "이것이 권총과 총알을 대신하는 나의 방편이다."], ["With a philosophical flourish Cato throws himself upon his sword; I quietly take to the ship.", "카토는 철학자다운 멋을 부리며 제 칼 위로 몸을 던졌지만, 나는 조용히 배에 오른다."], ["There is nothing surprising in this.", "여기에 놀랄 것은 하나도 없다."], ["If they but knew it, almost all men in their degree, some time or other, cherish very nearly the same feelings towards the ocean with me.", "알고 보면, 거의 모든 사람이 저마다의 정도에 따라 언젠가 한 번쯤은 바다를 향해 나와 거의 똑같은 감정을 품는 법이다."]], [["There now is your insular city of the Manhattoes, belted round by wharves as Indian isles by coral reefs—commerce surrounds it with her surf.", "자, 여기 섬처럼 떠 있는 그대들의 도시 맨해튼이 있다. 산호초가 인도양의 섬들을 두르듯 부두가 그 둘레를 띠처럼 감싸고 있고, 상업이 제 물결로 도시를 에워싸고 있다."], ["Right and left, the streets take you waterward.", "좌우로 뻗은 거리들은 그대를 물가로 데려간다."], ["Its extreme downtown is the battery, where that noble mole is washed by waves, and cooled by breezes, which a few hours previous were out of sight of land.", "도시의 맨 끝, 가장 남쪽에는 배터리 공원이 있어, 그 고귀한 방파제가 파도에 씻기고, 몇 시간 전만 해도 뭍이 보이지 않는 먼바다에 있던 산들바람에 식혀진다."], ["Look at the crowds of water-gazers there.", "거기 바다를 바라보는 사람들의 무리를 보라."]], [["Circumambulate the city of a dreamy Sabbath afternoon.", "꿈결 같은 안식일 오후에 도시를 한 바퀴 거닐어 보라."], ["Go from Corlears Hook to Coenties Slip, and from thence, by Whitehall, northward.", "콜리어스 훅에서 코엔티스 슬립까지, 거기서 다시 화이트홀을 지나 북쪽으로 가 보라."], ["What do you see?—Posted like silent sentinels all around the town, stand thousands upon thousands of mortal men fixed in ocean reveries.", "무엇이 보이는가? 말없는 보초처럼 도시 곳곳에 늘어서서, 바다의 몽상에 붙박인 수천수만의 인간들이 서 있다."], ["Some leaning against the spiles; some seated upon the pier-heads; some looking over the bulwarks of ships from China; some high aloft in the rigging, as if striving to get a still better seaward peep.", "어떤 이는 말뚝에 기대어 있고, 어떤 이는 부두 끝머리에 앉아 있으며, 어떤 이는 중국에서 온 배의 뱃전 너머를 굽어보고, 어떤 이는 더 나은 바다 풍경을 엿보려는 듯 삭구 높은 곳에 올라가 있다. 그러나 이들은 모두 뭍사람이다."], ["But these are all landsmen; of week days pent up in lath and plaster—tied to counters, nailed to benches, clinched to desks.", "평일에는 욋가지와 회벽 안에 갇혀 — 계산대에 묶이고, 의자에 못 박히고, 책상에 들러붙어 지내는 자들이다. 그렇다면 이게 어찌 된 일인가?"], ["How then is this?", "푸른 들판은 다 사라졌단 말인가?"], ["Are the green fields gone? What do they here?", "이들은 여기서 무엇을 하고 있는가?"]], [["But look!", "그런데 보라!"], ["here come more crowds, pacing straight for the water, and seemingly bound for a dive.", "더 많은 무리가 곧장 물가를 향해 걸어오는데, 마치 물에 뛰어들기라도 할 작정인 듯하다."], ["Strange!", "이상한 일이다!"], ["Nothing will content them but the extremest limit of the land; loitering under the shady lee of yonder warehouses will not suffice.", "뭍의 가장 끝자락이 아니고서는 그 무엇도 이들을 만족시키지 못한다. 저 창고들의 그늘진 바람막이 아래서 어슬렁거리는 것만으로는 충분치 않은 것이다."], ["No.", "안 된다."], ["They must get just as nigh the water as they possibly can without falling in.", "이들은 물에 빠지지 않을 한도 내에서 되도록 물에 가까이 다가서야만 한다."], ["And there they stand—miles of them—leagues.", "그리하여 거기 서 있다 — 몇 마일에 걸쳐, 몇 리그에 걸쳐."], ["Inlanders all, they come from lanes and alleys, streets and avenues—north, east, south, and west.", "모두 내륙 사람들이다. 골목과 샛길에서, 거리와 큰길에서 — 북쪽에서, 동쪽에서, 남쪽에서, 서쪽에서 몰려온다."], ["Yet here they all unite.", "그런데도 여기서는 모두 하나로 모인다."], ["Tell me, does the magnetic virtue of the needles of the compasses of all those ships attract them thither?", "말해 보라, 저 모든 배에 달린 나침반 바늘의 자력이 이들을 그리로 끌어당기기라도 한단 말인가?"]], [["Once more.", "다시 한번."], ["Say you are in the country; in some high land of lakes.", "그대가 시골에, 호수가 많은 어느 고지대에 있다고 하자."], ["Take almost any path you please, and ten to one it carries you down in a dale, and leaves you there by a pool in the stream.", "마음 내키는 대로 거의 아무 길이나 골라 잡아 보라. 십중팔구 그 길은 그대를 골짜기로 내려보내, 시냇물 곁 웅덩이 옆에 두고 갈 것이다."], ["There is magic in it.", "거기에는 마법이 깃들어 있다."], ["Let the most absent-minded of men be plunged in his deepest reveries—stand that man on his legs, set his feet a-going, and he will infallibly lead you to water, if water there be in all that region.", "세상에서 가장 정신이 멍한 사람을 가장 깊은 몽상에 빠뜨려 보라 — 그자를 두 발로 세워 발걸음을 떼게 하면, 그 일대에 물이라는 게 있기만 하다면 그는 어김없이 그대를 물가로 이끌 것이다."], ["Should you ever be athirst in the great American desert, try this experiment, if your caravan happen to be supplied with a metaphysical professor.", "혹시라도 광활한 아메리카 사막에서 목이 마르거든, 그대의 대상(隊商)에 형이상학 교수가 한 명 끼어 있을 경우 이 실험을 해 보라."], ["Yes, as every one knows, meditation and water are wedded for ever.", "그렇다, 누구나 알다시피, 명상과 물은 영원히 맺어진 부부 사이다."]], [["But here is an artist.", "그런데 여기 한 화가가 있다."], ["He desires to paint you the dreamiest, shadiest, quietest, most enchanting bit of romantic landscape in all the valley of the Saco.", "그는 사코 계곡을 통틀어 가장 꿈결 같고, 가장 그늘지고, 가장 고요하며, 가장 매혹적인 낭만적 풍경 한 자락을 그려 그대에게 보여 주고자 한다."], ["What is the chief element he employs?", "그가 쓰는 으뜸가는 요소는 무엇인가?"], ["There stand his trees, each with a hollow trunk, as if a hermit and a crucifix were within; and here sleeps his meadow, and there sleep his cattle; and up from yonder cottage goes a sleepy smoke.", "저기 그의 나무들이 서 있는데, 저마다 줄기 속이 텅 비어 마치 그 안에 은자와 십자가상이라도 들어 있는 듯하다. 그리고 여기 그의 풀밭이 잠들어 있고, 저기 그의 가축들이 잠들어 있으며, 저 멀리 오두막에서는 나른한 연기가 피어오른다."], ["Deep into distant woodlands winds a mazy way, reaching to overlapping spurs of mountains bathed in their hill-side blue.", "멀리 깊은 숲속으로는 구불구불한 길이 미로처럼 휘감겨 들어가, 산허리의 푸른 빛에 잠긴 채 겹겹이 포개진 산자락에 가닿는다."], ["But though the picture lies thus tranced, and though this pine-tree shakes down its sighs like leaves upon this shepherd’s head, yet all were vain, unless the shepherd’s eye were fixed upon the magic stream before him.", "그러나 그림이 이처럼 황홀경에 잠겨 있고, 이 소나무가 이 목동의 머리 위로 잎사귀 같은 한숨을 떨군다 한들, 그 목동의 눈길이 제 앞의 마법 같은 시냇물에 붙박여 있지 않다면 이 모든 것은 헛될 따름이다. 6월의 대초원을 찾아가 보라."], ["Go visit the Prairies in June, when for scores on scores of miles you wade knee-deep among Tiger-lilies—what is the one charm wanting?—Water—there is not a drop of water there!", "그곳에서 수십, 수백 마일에 걸쳐 무릎까지 차오르는 참나리꽃 사이를 헤치고 나아갈 때 — 모자라는 단 하나의 매력은 무엇인가? 물이다 — 거기엔 물 한 방울 없는 것이다!"], ["Were Niagara but a cataract of sand, would you travel your thousand miles to see it?", "나이아가라가 모래 폭포에 지나지 않는다면, 그대는 그것을 보겠다고 천 마일을 여행하겠는가?"], ["Why did the poor poet of Tennessee, upon suddenly receiving two handfuls of silver, deliberate whether to buy him a coat, which he sadly needed, or invest his money in a pedestrian trip to Rockaway Beach?", "테네시의 가난한 시인은 어쩌다 은화 두 줌을 손에 쥐었을 때, 절실히 필요했던 외투를 사 입을지 아니면 그 돈을 록어웨이 해변으로 가는 도보 여행에 쓸지를 두고 어찌하여 망설였는가?"], ["Why is almost every robust healthy boy with a robust healthy soul in him, at some time or other crazy to go to sea?", "어찌하여 건강하고 다부진 영혼을 지닌 건장한 사내아이라면 거의 누구나 언젠가 한 번쯤은 바다로 나가고 싶어 안달이 나는가?"], ["Why upon your first voyage as a passenger, did you yourself feel such a mystical vibration, when first told that you and your ship were now out of sight of land?", "어찌하여 그대 자신도 승객으로서 첫 항해에 나섰을 때, 이제 그대와 그대의 배가 뭍에서 보이지 않는 곳에 이르렀다는 말을 처음 들었을 때, 그토록 신비로운 떨림을 느꼈는가?"], ["Why did the old Persians hold the sea holy?", "어찌하여 옛 페르시아인들은 바다를 신성하게 여겼는가?"], ["Why did the Greeks give it a separate deity, and own brother of Jove?", "어찌하여 그리스인들은 바다에 따로 신격을 부여하여 제우스의 친형제로 삼았는가?"], ["Surely all this is not without meaning.", "진실로 이 모든 것에는 뜻이 없지 않다."], ["And still deeper the meaning of that story of Narcissus, who because he could not grasp the tormenting, mild image he saw in the fountain, plunged into it and was drowned.", "그리고 더더욱 깊은 뜻이 나르키소스 이야기 속에 담겨 있으니, 그는 샘물에 비친, 괴롭도록 부드러운 그 영상을 붙잡을 수 없어 그 속으로 뛰어들었다가 빠져 죽고 말았다."], ["But that same image, we ourselves see in all rivers and oceans.", "그러나 바로 그 영상을, 우리 자신도 모든 강과 바다 속에서 보고 있는 것이다."], ["It is the image of the ungraspable phantom of life; and this is the key to it all.", "그것은 붙잡을 수 없는 생명의 환영이 빚어낸 영상이며, 이것이야말로 그 모든 것을 푸는 열쇠다."]], [["Now, when I say that I am in the habit of going to sea whenever I begin to grow hazy about the eyes, and begin to be over conscious of my lungs, I do not mean to have it inferred that I ever go to sea as a passenger.", "자, 내가 눈앞이 흐릿해지고 폐가 지나치게 의식되기 시작할 때마다 바다로 나가는 버릇이 있다고 말한다고 해서, 내가 승객으로 바다에 나간다는 뜻으로 받아들여서는 안 된다. 승객으로 가려면 모름지기 지갑이 있어야 하는데, 지갑이란 그 안에 든 것이 없으면 한낱 헝겊 조각에 지나지 않기 때문이다."], ["For to go as a passenger you must needs have a purse, and a purse is but a rag unless you have something in it.", "게다가 승객은 뱃멀미를 한다 — 신경질을 부리고 — 밤에 잠을 이루지 못하며 — 대체로 그다지 즐거운 시간을 보내지 못한다."], ["Besides, passengers get sea-sick—grow quarrelsome—don’t sleep of nights—do not enjoy themselves much, as a general thing;—no, I never go as a passenger; nor, though I am something of a salt, do I ever go to sea as a Commodore, or a Captain, or a Cook.", "그러니 안 된다, 나는 결코 승객으로는 가지 않는다. 또한 내가 제법 뱃사람티가 난다고 해서, 제독이나 선장이나 요리사로서 바다에 나가는 일도 결코 없다."], ["I abandon the glory and distinction of such offices to those who like them.", "그런 직위의 영예와 위엄일랑, 그것을 좋아하는 자들에게 넘겨준다."], ["For my part, I abominate all honorable respectable toils, trials, and tribulations of every kind whatsoever.", "나로 말하자면, 명예롭고 점잖다는 온갖 종류의 고역과 시련과 고난을 죄다 질색한다."], ["It is quite as much as I can do to take care of myself, without taking care of ships, barques, brigs, schooners, and what not.", "배든, 바크든, 브리그든, 스쿠너든 그 무엇이든 돌볼 것 없이, 나 자신 하나 건사하는 것만으로도 내게는 벅찬 일이다."], ["And as for going as cook,—though I confess there is considerable glory in that, a cook being a sort of officer on ship-board—yet, somehow, I never fancied broiling fowls;—though once broiled, judiciously buttered, and judgmatically salted and peppered, there is no one who will speak more respectfully, not to say reverentially, of a broiled fowl than I will.", "그리고 요리사로 가는 것으로 말하자면 — 거기에 상당한 영예가 따른다는 것은 인정한다, 요리사란 배 위에서 일종의 사관(士官)이니까 — 그래도 어쩐지 나는 가금류를 굽는 일이 영 내키지 않았다. 물론 일단 잘 구워서, 적절히 버터를 바르고, 사리에 맞게 소금과 후추로 간을 한 구운 새고기라면, 나만큼 그것을 정중하게, 아니 경건하다 할 만큼 떠받들어 말할 사람도 없을 테지만 말이다."], ["It is out of the idolatrous dotings of the old Egyptians upon broiled ibis and roasted river horse, that you see the mummies of those creatures in their huge bake-houses the pyramids.", "옛 이집트인들이 구운 따오기와 통째로 구운 하마를 우상처럼 떠받들며 애지중지했던 까닭에, 그 거대한 빵 굽는 가마와도 같은 피라미드 속에서 그 짐승들의 미라를 보게 되는 것이다."]], [["No, when I go to sea, I go as a simple sailor, right before the mast, plumb down into the forecastle, aloft there to the royal mast-head.", "아니다, 나는 바다에 나갈 때 그저 평범한 선원으로, 돛대 바로 앞에 서서, 곧장 앞갑판 선실로 내려갔다가, 다시 저 높은 곳 최상부 돛대 꼭대기로 올라간다."], ["True, they rather order me about some, and make me jump from spar to spar, like a grasshopper in a May meadow.", "사실 그들은 나를 꽤나 이리저리 부려 먹고, 5월 풀밭의 메뚜기처럼 이 활대에서 저 활대로 뛰어다니게 만든다."], ["And at first, this sort of thing is unpleasant enough.", "처음에는 이런 일이 영 불쾌하다."], ["It touches one’s sense of honor, particularly if you come of an old established family in the land, the Van Rensselaers, or Randolphs, or Hardicanutes.", "특히 그대가 이 땅의 유서 깊은 명문가 출신, 이를테면 판 렌셀러가, 랜돌프가, 혹은 하르디크누트가 출신이라면, 그것은 자존심을 건드린다."], ["And more than all, if just previous to putting your hand into the tar-pot, you have been lording it as a country schoolmaster, making the tallest boys stand in awe of you.", "그리고 무엇보다도, 타르 통에 손을 담그기 바로 전까지 그대가 시골 학교 선생으로 군림하며 키 큰 학생들조차 그대를 두려워하게 만들었던 처지라면 더욱 그러하다."], ["The transition is a keen one, I assure you, from a schoolmaster to a sailor, and requires a strong decoction of Seneca and the Stoics to enable you to grin and bear it.", "장담하건대, 학교 선생에서 선원으로 옮겨 가는 그 전환은 뼈저린 것이어서, 그것을 이 악물고 견뎌 내려면 세네카와 스토아 철학자들의 진한 탕약이 필요하다."], ["But even this wears off in time.", "그러나 이마저도 시간이 지나면 차츰 가신다."]], [["What of it, if some old hunks of a sea-captain orders me to get a broom and sweep down the decks?", "심술궂은 늙은 선장이 내게 빗자루를 가져다 갑판을 쓸라고 명령한들 그게 무슨 대수란 말인가?"], ["What does that indignity amount to, weighed, I mean, in the scales of the New Testament?", "신약성경의 저울에 달아 본다면, 그런 모욕이 대체 얼마나 나간단 말인가?"], ["Do you think the archangel Gabriel thinks anything the less of me, because I promptly and respectfully obey that old hunks in that particular instance?", "내가 그 특정한 경우에 그 심술궂은 늙은이에게 선뜻 공손하게 복종한다고 해서, 대천사 가브리엘이 나를 조금이라도 낮추어 본다고 생각하는가? 노예 아닌 자가 누가 있는가?"], ["Who ain’t a slave? Tell me that.", "그것을 내게 말해 보라."], ["Well, then, however the old sea-captains may order me about—however they may thump and punch me about, I have the satisfaction of knowing that it is all right; that everybody else is one way or other served in much the same way—either in a physical or metaphysical point of view, that is; and so the universal thump is passed round, and all hands should rub each other’s shoulder-blades, and be content.", "자, 그러니, 늙은 선장들이 나를 아무리 이리저리 부려 먹든 — 나를 아무리 쥐어박고 두들기든, 나는 그것이 다 괜찮다는 것을, 다른 모든 사람도 이런저런 방식으로 거의 똑같은 대접을 받고 있다는 것을 — 그러니까 육체적 관점에서든 형이상학적 관점에서든 말이다 — 아는 데서 오는 만족을 누린다. 그렇게 보편적인 쥐어박음이 차례로 돌고 도는 것이니, 모두가 서로의 어깻죽지를 문질러 주며 만족할 일이다."]], [["Again, I always go to sea as a sailor, because they make a point of paying me for my trouble, whereas they never pay passengers a single penny that I ever heard of.", "또한 나는 늘 선원으로 바다에 나가는데, 이는 그들이 내 수고에 대해 어김없이 삯을 치러 주기 때문이다. 반면에 승객에게는 단 한 푼이라도 치러 준다는 말을 나는 들어 본 적이 없다."], ["On the contrary, passengers themselves must pay.", "오히려 승객은 제 손으로 돈을 내야 한다."], ["And there is all the difference in the world between paying and being paid.", "그리고 돈을 내는 것과 돈을 받는 것 사이에는 천양지차가 있다."], ["The act of paying is perhaps the most uncomfortable infliction that the two orchard thieves entailed upon us.", "돈을 내는 행위란, 그 두 과수원 도둑이 우리에게 물려준 가장 거북한 형벌일 것이다."], ["But being paid,—what will compare with it?", "그러나 돈을 받는 것은 — 무엇이 이에 견줄 수 있겠는가?"], ["The urbane activity with which a man receives money is really marvellous, considering that we so earnestly believe money to be the root of all earthly ills, and that on no account can a monied man enter heaven.", "사람이 돈을 받을 때 보이는 그 점잖은 활기란 참으로 놀라운 것인데, 우리가 돈이야말로 이 세상 모든 악의 뿌리라고 그토록 진지하게 믿고, 돈 많은 자는 결코 천국에 들어갈 수 없다고 믿는 것을 생각하면 더욱 그렇다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["how cheerfully we consign ourselves to perdition!", "우리는 어쩌면 그토록 즐거이 우리 자신을 파멸에 내맡기는가!"]], [["Finally, I always go to sea as a sailor, because of the wholesome exercise and pure air of the fore-castle deck.", "끝으로, 나는 늘 선원으로 바다에 나가는데, 이는 앞갑판에서 누리는 건강한 운동과 맑은 공기 때문이다."], ["For as in this world, head winds are far more prevalent than winds from astern (that is, if you never violate the Pythagorean maxim), so for the most part the Commodore on the quarter-deck gets his atmosphere at second hand from the sailors on the forecastle.", "이 세상에서는 고물에서 불어오는 바람보다 맞바람이 훨씬 잦은 법이므로 (다시 말해, 그대가 피타고라스의 금언을 결코 어기지 않는다면 말이다), 후갑판의 제독은 대개 앞갑판 선원들에게서 한 번 걸러진 공기를 얻어 마시는 셈이다."], ["He thinks he breathes it first; but not so.", "그는 자기가 그 공기를 맨 먼저 들이마신다고 여기지만, 실은 그렇지 않다."], ["In much the same way do the commonalty lead their leaders in many other things, at the same time that the leaders little suspect it.", "이와 거의 같은 방식으로, 평민들은 다른 여러 일에서도 자기네 지도자를 이끌고 있건만, 정작 지도자들은 그것을 좀처럼 눈치채지 못한다."], ["But wherefore it was that after having repeatedly smelt the sea as a merchant sailor, I should now take it into my head to go on a whaling voyage; this the invisible police officer of the Fates, who has the constant surveillance of me, and secretly dogs me, and influences me in some unaccountable way—he can better answer than any one else.", "그러나 어찌하여, 상선 선원으로 여러 차례 바다 냄새를 맡고 난 내가 이제 와서 고래잡이 항해에 나설 생각을 머릿속에 품게 되었는가 — 이것은 늘 나를 감시하고, 은밀히 내 뒤를 밟으며, 무어라 설명할 수 없는 방식으로 나를 좌우하는 저 운명의 보이지 않는 경찰관이라야 그 누구보다도 잘 답할 수 있을 것이다."], ["And, doubtless, my going on this whaling voyage, formed part of the grand programme of Providence that was drawn up a long time ago.", "그리고 의심할 바 없이, 내가 이 고래잡이 항해에 나선 것은 아주 오래전에 짜인 저 섭리의 거창한 공연 차례표 가운데 한 부분이었다."], ["It came in as a sort of brief interlude and solo between more extensive performances.", "그것은 더 방대한 공연들 사이에 끼인 일종의 짧은 막간극이자 독주(獨奏)로서 끼어든 셈이다."], ["I take it that this part of the bill must have run something like this:", "짐작건대 그 공연 차례표의 이 대목은 대략 다음과 같이 적혀 있었으리라."]], [["“Grand Contested Election for the Presidency of the United States.", "“미합중국 대통령직을 둘러싼 거대한 경합 선거."], ["“WHALING VOYAGE BY ONE ISHMAEL.", "“어느 이슈메일이라는 자의 고래잡이 항해."], ["“BLOODY BATTLE IN AFFGHANISTAN.”", "“아프가니스탄에서의 피비린내 나는 전투.”"]], [["Though I cannot tell why it was exactly that those stage managers, the Fates, put me down for this shabby part of a whaling voyage, when others were set down for magnificent parts in high tragedies, and short and easy parts in genteel comedies, and jolly parts in farces—though I cannot tell why this was exactly; yet, now that I recall all the circumstances, I think I can see a little into the springs and motives which being cunningly presented to me under various disguises, induced me to set about performing the part I did, besides cajoling me into the delusion that it was a choice resulting from my own unbiased freewill and discriminating judgment.", "저 무대 감독들, 곧 운명의 여신들이 어찌하여 다른 이들에게는 장엄한 비극의 화려한 배역을, 점잖은 희극의 짧고 수월한 배역을, 익살극의 흥겨운 배역을 맡겨 두고서, 하필 내게는 이 초라한 고래잡이 항해 배역을 정해 주었는지 — 그 까닭을 나는 정확히 말할 수 없다. 그렇긴 해도, 이제 모든 정황을 돌이켜 보면, 나는 그 동기와 동력의 속내를 조금쯤은 들여다볼 수 있을 듯하다. 그것들은 갖가지 변장을 하고 교묘하게 내 앞에 나타나, 내가 그 배역을 맡아 연기하도록 부추겼고, 게다가 그것이 어떠한 치우침도 없는 나 자신의 자유의지와 분별 있는 판단에서 비롯된 선택이라는 착각에 빠지도록 나를 구슬렸던 것이다."]], [["Chief among these motives was the overwhelming idea of the great whale himself.", "이 동기들 가운데 으뜸가는 것은 저 거대한 고래 자체에 대한 압도적인 상념이었다."], ["Such a portentous and mysterious monster roused all my curiosity.", "그토록 불길하고 신비로운 괴물이 내 모든 호기심을 들쑤셨다."], ["Then the wild and distant seas where he rolled his island bulk; the undeliverable, nameless perils of the whale; these, with all the attending marvels of a thousand Patagonian sights and sounds, helped to sway me to my wish.", "그리고 그놈이 섬덩이 같은 제 몸을 굴리며 노니는 거칠고 머나먼 바다들, 도무지 벗어날 길 없는 이름 모를 고래의 위험들 — 이 모든 것이, 천 가지 파타고니아의 풍경과 소리가 자아내는 온갖 경이로움과 더불어, 내 소망 쪽으로 나를 기울게 했다."], ["With other men, perhaps, such things would not have been inducements; but as for me, I am tormented with an everlasting itch for things remote.", "다른 사람들에게라면 어쩌면 그런 것들이 유인이 되지 못했을지도 모른다. 그러나 나로 말하자면, 머나먼 것들을 향한 그칠 줄 모르는 갈망에 시달리는 사람이다."], ["I love to sail forbidden seas, and land on barbarous coasts.", "나는 금단의 바다를 항해하고, 야만의 해안에 상륙하기를 좋아한다."], ["Not ignoring what is good, I am quick to perceive a horror, and could still be social with it—would they let me—since it is but well to be on friendly terms with all the inmates of the place one lodges in.", "좋은 것을 외면하지는 않으면서도, 나는 공포를 재빨리 알아차리며, 또한 그것과 더불어 어울려 지낼 수도 있다 — 그것이 허락만 해 준다면 말이다 — 자기가 묵는 곳의 모든 식구와 사이좋게 지내는 것이야말로 좋은 일이니까."]], [["By reason of these things, then, the whaling voyage was welcome; the great flood-gates of the wonder-world swung open, and in the wild conceits that swayed me to my purpose, two and two there floated into my inmost soul, endless processions of the whale, and, mid most of them all, one grand hooded phantom, like a snow hill in the air.", "그리하여 이런 까닭들로 해서, 고래잡이 항해는 반가운 것이었다. 경이로운 세계의 거대한 수문이 활짝 열렸고, 나를 그 뜻으로 기울게 한 거친 공상들 속에서, 끝없이 이어지는 고래들의 행렬이 둘씩 둘씩 짝지어 내 영혼 가장 깊은 곳으로 떠밀려 들어왔으며, 그 모든 것의 한복판에는 허공에 솟은 눈 덮인 언덕과도 같은, 두건을 쓴 거대한 환영 하나가 떠 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 2. The Carpet-Bag.", "# 제3권(12절판) 제2장(알제리 돌고래). 해적이다."]], [["I stuffed a shirt or two into my old carpet-bag, tucked it under my arm, and started for Cape Horn and the Pacific.", "셔츠 한두 장을 낡은 융단 가방에 쑤셔 넣고는 그것을 옆구리에 낀 채, 나는 케이프 혼과 태평양을 향해 길을 나섰다."], ["Quitting the good city of old Manhatto, I duly arrived in New Bedford.", "유서 깊은 옛 도시 맨해튼을 떠나, 나는 무사히 뉴베드퍼드(New Bedford)에 닿았다."], ["It was a Saturday night in December.", "때는 십이월의 어느 토요일 밤이었다."], ["Much was I disappointed upon learning that the little packet for Nantucket had already sailed, and that no way of reaching that place would offer, till the following Monday.", "낸터킷(Nantucket)으로 가는 작은 우편선이 이미 떠나 버렸고, 다음 월요일이 되기 전에는 그곳에 닿을 방도가 전혀 없다는 사실을 알게 되었을 때, 나는 적잖이 실망했다."]], [["As most young candidates for the pains and penalties of whaling stop at this same New Bedford, thence to embark on their voyage, it may as well be related that I, for one, had no idea of so doing.", "고래잡이의 고통과 형벌을 자청하는 젊은 지원자들 대부분이 바로 이 뉴베드퍼드에 머물렀다가 그곳에서 항해를 떠나는 까닭에, 나로 말하자면 그럴 생각이 전혀 없었음을 여기서 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠다."], ["For my mind was made up to sail in no other than a Nantucket craft, because there was a fine, boisterous something about everything connected with that famous old island, which amazingly pleased me.", "내 마음은 오로지 낸터킷의 배를 타고 떠나리라는 쪽으로 굳어 있었으니, 그 유명한 옛 섬에 얽힌 모든 것에는 어딘가 멋지고 떠들썩한 무언가가 있어 나를 놀랍도록 흡족하게 했기 때문이다."], ["Besides though New Bedford has of late been gradually monopolising the business of whaling, and though in this matter poor old Nantucket is now much behind her, yet Nantucket was her great original—the Tyre of this Carthage;—the place where the first dead American whale was stranded.", "게다가 뉴베드퍼드가 근래에 들어 고래잡이 사업을 차츰 독차지하고 있고, 이 일에 있어 가련한 옛 낸터킷이 이제 그 도시에 한참 뒤처져 있다 한들, 낸터킷은 그 도시의 위대한 원조였다 — 이 카르타고의 티레였던 것이다. 곧 최초의 죽은 아메리카 고래가 떠밀려 와 좌초한 곳이 바로 낸터킷이었다."], ["Where else but from Nantucket did those aboriginal whalemen, the Red-Men, first sally out in canoes to give chase to the Leviathan?", "저 토착 고래잡이들, 곧 붉은 피부의 사람들이 카누를 타고 처음으로 리바이어던(Leviathan)을 쫓아 나선 곳이 낸터킷 아니고 또 어디였단 말인가?"], ["And where but from Nantucket, too, did that first adventurous little sloop put forth, partly laden with imported cobblestones—so goes the story—to throw at the whales, in order to discover when they were nigh enough to risk a harpoon from the bowsprit?", "그리고 — 이야기에 따르면 — 고래가 뱃머리 작살을 던질 만큼 가까이 왔는지 알아보려고 던질 요량으로 수입한 자갈을 절반쯤 싣고서 처음으로 모험에 나선 그 작은 외돛배가 출항한 곳 또한 낸터킷 아니고 어디였겠는가?"]], [["Now having a night, a day, and still another night following before me in New Bedford, ere I could embark for my destined port, it became a matter of concernment where I was to eat and sleep meanwhile.", "이제 정해진 항구를 향해 떠날 수 있기까지 뉴베드퍼드에서 하룻밤과 하루, 그리고 또 하룻밤을 앞에 두고 보니, 그동안 어디서 먹고 잘 것인가 하는 문제가 신경 쓰이기 시작했다."], ["It was a very dubious-looking, nay, a very dark and dismal night, bitingly cold and cheerless.", "매우 미덥지 못해 보이는, 아니 매우 어둡고 음산한 밤이었으며, 살을 에듯 춥고 쓸쓸했다."], ["I knew no one in the place.", "나는 그곳에 아는 사람이 하나도 없었다."], ["With anxious grapnels I had sounded my pocket, and only brought up a few pieces of silver,—So, wherever you go, Ishmael, said I to myself, as I stood in the middle of a dreary street shouldering my bag, and comparing the gloom towards the north with the darkness towards the south—wherever in your wisdom you may conclude to lodge for the night, my dear Ishmael, be sure to inquire the price, and don’t be too particular.", "불안한 갈고리를 드리워 내 호주머니의 깊이를 재어 보았으나, 건져 올린 것이라곤 은화 몇 닢뿐이었다 — 그러니, 이스마엘, 어디로 가든, 황량한 거리 한복판에 서서 가방을 어깨에 둘러멘 채 북쪽의 어둠과 남쪽의 어둠을 견주어 보며 나는 스스로에게 말했다 — 사랑하는 이스마엘이여, 그대의 지혜로 어디서 하룻밤 묵기로 결정하든, 부디 값부터 물어보고, 너무 까다롭게 굴지는 말지어다."]], [["With halting steps I paced the streets, and passed the sign of “The Crossed Harpoons”—but it looked too expensive and jolly there.", "머뭇거리는 걸음으로 거리를 거닐다가 \"엇갈린 작살\" 간판을 지났는데 — 그곳은 너무 값비싸고 흥겨워 보였다."], ["Further on, from the bright red windows of the “Sword-Fish Inn,”", "좀 더 가니 \"황새치 여관\"의 환한 붉은 창문에서 어찌나 뜨거운 빛줄기가 쏟아져 나왔던지, 집 앞에 다져진 눈과 얼음마저 녹여 버린 듯했다."], ["there came such fervent rays, that it seemed to have melted the packed snow and ice from before the house, for everywhere else the congealed frost lay ten inches thick in a hard, asphaltic pavement,—rather weary for me, when I struck my foot against the flinty projections, because from hard, remorseless service the soles of my boots were in a most miserable plight.", "다른 곳은 어디나 얼어붙은 서리가 단단한 아스팔트 포장처럼 열 인치 두께로 깔려 있었으니 — 내겐 다소 고된 노릇이었다. 부싯돌처럼 솟은 모서리에 발을 부딪칠 때마다, 모질고 가차 없는 혹사 탓에 내 장화 밑창이 더없이 비참한 꼴이 되어 있었던 까닭이다."], ["Too expensive and jolly, again thought I, pausing one moment to watch the broad glare in the street, and hear the sounds of the tinkling glasses within.", "또 너무 값비싸고 흥겹군, 나는 다시 생각하며 잠시 걸음을 멈추고 거리에 넘실대는 환한 불빛을 바라보고 안에서 잔이 부딪치며 쟁그랑거리는 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 하지만 가자, 이스마엘, 나는 마침내 말했다."], ["But go on, Ishmael, said I at last; don’t you hear?", "들리지 않느냐? 문 앞에서 비켜라."], ["get away from before the door; your patched boots are stopping the way.", "그대의 누덕누덕한 장화가 길을 막고 있다. 그래서 나는 다시 걸음을 옮겼다."], ["So on I went. I now by instinct followed the streets that took me waterward, for there, doubtless, were the cheapest, if not the cheeriest inns.", "이제 나는 본능에 이끌려 바다 쪽으로 나를 데려가는 거리를 따라갔으니, 가장 흥겨운 여관은 아닐지언정 가장 값싼 여관이 그곳에 있으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Such dreary streets!", "이 얼마나 황량한 거리인가!"], ["blocks of blackness, not houses, on either hand, and here and there a candle, like a candle moving about in a tomb.", "양옆으로는 집이 아니라 시커먼 어둠의 덩어리뿐이고, 여기저기 촛불 하나가, 마치 무덤 속을 떠도는 촛불처럼 보였다."], ["At this hour of the night, of the last day of the week, that quarter of the town proved all but deserted.", "한 주의 마지막 날, 이 야심한 시각에 그 동네는 거의 인적이 끊겨 있었다."], ["But presently I came to a smoky light proceeding from a low, wide building, the door of which stood invitingly open.", "그러나 이윽고 나는 나지막하고 널찍한 건물에서 새어 나오는 자욱한 불빛에 이르렀는데, 그 문은 어서 들어오라는 듯 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["It had a careless look, as if it were meant for the uses of the public; so, entering, the first thing I did was to stumble over an ash-box in the porch.", "아무렇게나 내버려 둔 듯한 모양새가 마치 누구든 드나들라고 마련된 곳 같았다. 그래서 안으로 들어서며 내가 맨 처음 한 일은 현관에 놓인 잿더미 상자에 걸려 비틀거린 것이었다."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["thought I, ha, as the flying particles almost choked me, are these ashes from that destroyed city, Gomorrah?", "나는 생각했다, 하, 흩날리는 재가 거의 숨통을 막을 지경이 되자, 이것은 저 멸망한 도시 고모라의 재란 말인가?"], ["But “The Crossed Harpoons,” and “The Sword-Fish?”—this, then must needs be the sign of “The Trap.”", "그러나 \"엇갈린 작살\"과 \"황새치\"라니 — 그렇다면 이곳은 분명 \"덫\"이라는 간판을 단 곳이렷다."], ["However, I picked myself up and hearing a loud voice within, pushed on and opened a second, interior door.", "그래도 나는 몸을 추스르고는 안에서 우렁찬 목소리가 들려오기에, 안쪽으로 밀고 들어가 두 번째 안문을 열었다."]], [["It seemed the great Black Parliament sitting in Tophet.", "마치 토페트에 자리한 위대한 흑인 의회 같았다."], ["A hundred black faces turned round in their rows to peer; and beyond, a black Angel of Doom was beating a book in a pulpit.", "백 개의 검은 얼굴이 줄지어 앉은 채 일제히 고개를 돌려 빤히 쳐다보았고, 그 너머에서는 검은 운명의 천사가 강단에서 책을 두드리고 있었다."], ["It was a negro church; and the preacher’s text was about the blackness of darkness, and the weeping and wailing and teeth-gnashing there.", "그곳은 흑인 교회였으니, 설교자의 본문은 어둠의 캄캄함과, 그곳에서의 울부짖음과 통곡과 이 가는 소리에 관한 것이었다."], ["Ha, Ishmael, muttered I, backing out, Wretched entertainment at the sign of ‘The Trap!’", "하, 이스마엘, 나는 뒷걸음질로 빠져나오며 중얼거렸다. \"덫\"이라는 간판에 걸맞은 참담한 환대로구나!"]], [["Moving on, I at last came to a dim sort of light not far from the docks, and heard a forlorn creaking in the air; and looking up, saw a swinging sign over the door with a white painting upon it, faintly representing a tall straight jet of misty spray, and these words underneath—“The Spouter Inn:—Peter Coffin.”", "다시 걸음을 옮겨, 나는 마침내 부두에서 멀지 않은 곳에서 희미한 빛 같은 것을 만났고, 허공에서 처량하게 삐걱대는 소리를 들었다. 위를 올려다보니 문 위에 흔들리는 간판이 걸려 있었는데, 그 위에는 흰 그림이 그려져 있어 자욱한 물보라가 길고 곧게 솟구치는 모양을 어렴풋이 나타내고 있었고, 그 아래에는 이런 글귀가 있었다 — \"물뿜이 여관: 피터 코핀.\""]], [["Coffin?—Spouter?—Rather ominous in that particular connexion, thought I.", "코핀(Coffin)? — 물뿜이라고?"], ["But it is a common name in Nantucket, they say, and I suppose this Peter here is an emigrant from there.", "— 이런 특정한 맥락에서는 다소 불길하군, 나는 생각했다."], ["As the light looked so dim, and the place, for the time, looked quiet enough, and the dilapidated little wooden house itself looked as if it might have been carted here from the ruins of some burnt district, and as the swinging sign had a poverty-stricken sort of creak to it, I thought that here was the very spot for cheap lodgings, and the best of pea coffee.", "하지만 그것이 낸터킷에서는 흔한 이름이라고들 하니, 여기 이 피터라는 자도 그곳에서 온 이주민이려니 싶었다. 불빛이 어찌나 흐릿한지, 그리고 그곳이 적어도 그때만큼은 제법 조용해 보였고, 그 다 허물어져 가는 작은 목조 집 자체가 마치 어느 불탄 동네의 잔해에서 수레로 실어다 놓은 듯한 데다, 흔들리는 간판이 가난에 찌든 듯 삐걱대고 있었기에, 나는 여기야말로 값싼 숙소와 둘도 없는 완두콩 커피를 얻을 바로 그 자리라고 생각했다."]], [["It was a queer sort of place—a gable-ended old house, one side palsied as it were, and leaning over sadly.", "그곳은 기묘한 곳이었다 — 박공지붕을 인 낡은 집으로, 한쪽 면이 마치 중풍에 걸린 듯 슬프게 기울어져 있었다."], ["It stood on a sharp bleak corner, where that tempestuous wind Euroclydon kept up a worse howling than ever it did about poor Paul’s tossed craft.", "그것은 매섭고 황량한 모퉁이에 서 있었는데, 그곳에서는 저 사나운 바람 유로클리돈이, 일찍이 가련한 바울의 요동치던 배를 둘러싸고 울부짖던 때보다 더 거세게 울부짖고 있었다."], ["Euroclydon, nevertheless, is a mighty pleasant zephyr to any one in-doors, with his feet on the hob quietly toasting for bed.", "그렇지만 유로클리돈도 집 안에서 두 발을 난로 선반에 올려놓고 잠자리에 들 채비로 한가로이 몸을 덥히는 사람에게는 더없이 기분 좋은 산들바람이다."], ["“In judging of that tempestuous wind called Euroclydon,”", "\"유로클리돈이라 불리는 저 사나운 바람을 헤아릴진대\""], ["says an old writer—of whose works I possess the only copy extant—“it maketh a marvellous difference, whether thou lookest out at it from a glass window where the frost is all on the outside, or whether thou observest it from that sashless window, where the frost is on both sides, and of which the wight Death is the only glazier.”", "하고 어느 옛 저술가는 — 현존하는 그의 저작이라곤 내가 가진 단 한 부뿐이거니와 — 이렇게 말한다. \"그것은 참으로 놀라운 차이를 빚어내나니, 그대가 바깥에만 온통 서리가 낀 유리창에서 그것을 내다보느냐, 아니면 양쪽 다 서리가 끼고 죽음이라는 자가 유일한 유리장이 노릇을 하는 저 창틀 없는 창에서 그것을 바라보느냐에 따라 그러하니라.\""], ["True enough, thought I, as this passage occurred to my mind—old black-letter, thou reasonest well.", "참으로 옳도다, 이 구절이 내 머릿속에 떠오를 때 나는 생각했다 — 늙은 고딕 활자여, 그대의 논변이 훌륭하구나."], ["Yes, these eyes are windows, and this body of mine is the house.", "그렇다, 이 두 눈은 창이요, 이 내 몸뚱이는 집이로다."], ["What a pity they didn’t stop up the chinks and the crannies though, and thrust in a little lint here and there.", "그래도 그들이 틈새와 구멍을 막지 않고, 여기저기 솜털 한 줌이라도 쑤셔 넣지 않은 것은 참으로 안타까운 노릇이다."], ["But it’s too late to make any improvements now.", "하지만 이제 와서 무엇을 고치기란 너무 늦었다."], ["The universe is finished; the copestone is on, and the chips were carted off a million years ago.", "우주는 이미 완성되었으니, 갓돌은 얹혔고 부스러기들은 백만 년 전에 수레로 실려 나가 버렸다."], ["Poor Lazarus there, chattering his teeth against the curbstone for his pillow, and shaking off his tatters with his shiverings, he might plug up both ears with rags, and put a corn-cob into his mouth, and yet that would not keep out the tempestuous Euroclydon.", "저기 가련한 나사로는, 베개 삼은 연석에 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 떨고, 떨리는 몸으로 누더기를 들썩이고 있으니, 그는 두 귀를 헝겊으로 틀어막고 옥수수 속대를 입에 물어 보아도, 그 사나운 유로클리돈을 막아 내지는 못하리라."], ["Euroclydon!", "유로클리돈이라!"], ["says old Dives, in his red silken wrapper—(he had a redder one afterwards) pooh, pooh!", "붉은 비단 가운을 걸친 늙은 디베스가 말한다 — (훗날 그는 더 붉은 가운을 걸쳤다) 쳇, 쳇!"], ["What a fine frosty night; how Orion glitters; what northern lights!", "어찌나 멋진 서리 내린 밤인가. 오리온은 어찌나 반짝이며, 저 북극광은 또 어떠한가!"], ["Let them talk of their oriental summer climes of everlasting conservatories; give me the privilege of making my own summer with my own coals.", "영원토록 온실로 가득한 동방의 여름 기후를 떠들고 싶거든 떠들라지. 내게는 내 석탄으로 내 여름을 손수 빚어내는 특권을 다오."]], [["But what thinks Lazarus?", "그러나 나사로는 무엇을 생각할까?"], ["Can he warm his blue hands by holding them up to the grand northern lights?", "그가 푸르뎅뎅한 두 손을 저 장엄한 북극광에 치켜든다고 그 손이 따뜻해질까?"], ["Would not Lazarus rather be in Sumatra than here?", "나사로는 여기보다 차라리 수마트라에 있고 싶지 않을까?"], ["Would he not far rather lay him down lengthwise along the line of the equator; yea, ye gods!", "그는 차라리 적도의 선을 따라 몸을 죽 뻗고 눕고 싶지 않을까. 그렇다, 오 신들이시여!"], ["go down to the fiery pit itself, in order to keep out this frost?", "이 서리를 막아 내기 위해서라면 차라리 불구덩이 그 자체로 내려가고 싶지 않을까?"]], [["Now, that Lazarus should lie stranded there on the curbstone before the door of Dives, this is more wonderful than that an iceberg should be moored to one of the Moluccas.", "그런데 나사로가 디베스의 문 앞 연석 위에 좌초한 듯 누워 있다는 것은, 빙산 하나가 몰루카 제도의 어느 섬에 닻을 내리고 매여 있다는 것보다 더 놀라운 일이다."], ["Yet Dives himself, he too lives like a Czar in an ice palace made of frozen sighs, and being a president of a temperance society, he only drinks the tepid tears of orphans.", "그렇지만 디베스 그 자신 또한 얼어붙은 한숨으로 지어 올린 얼음 궁전 안에서 차르처럼 살고 있으며, 금주회의 회장인 까닭에 그가 마시는 것이라곤 고아들의 미적지근한 눈물뿐이다."]], [["But no more of this blubbering now, we are going a-whaling, and there is plenty of that yet to come.", "하지만 이런 눈물 짜는 이야기는 이제 그만하자, 우리는 고래잡이를 떠나려는 참이고, 그런 이야기는 앞으로도 얼마든지 나올 테니까."], ["Let us scrape the ice from our frosted feet, and see what sort of a place this “Spouter” may be.", "서리 낀 우리 발에서 얼음이나 긁어내고, 이 \"물뿜이\"라는 곳이 도대체 어떤 곳인지나 살펴보자."]], [["# CHAPTER 3. The Spouter-Inn.", "# CHAPTER 3. The Spouter-Inn."]], [["Entering that gable-ended Spouter-Inn, you found yourself in a wide, low, straggling entry with old-fashioned wainscots, reminding one of the bulwarks of some condemned old craft.", "그 박공지붕의 물보라 여관(Spouter-Inn)으로 들어서면, 폭이 넓고 천장이 낮은 데다 어수선하게 뻗은 현관이 나타났는데, 구식 징두리 판벽으로 둘러져 있어 어딘가 폐선 선고를 받은 낡은 배의 뱃전이 떠오르게 했다."], ["On one side hung a very large oilpainting so thoroughly besmoked, and every way defaced, that in the unequal crosslights by which you viewed it, it was only by diligent study and a series of systematic visits to it, and careful inquiry of the neighbors, that you could any way arrive at an understanding of its purpose.", "한쪽 벽에는 매우 커다란 유화 한 점이 걸려 있었는데, 어찌나 그을음으로 뒤덮이고 사방이 훼손되었던지, 고르지 못한 빛이 엇갈려 비치는 가운데서 그것을 들여다보자면, 부지런히 살펴보고 체계적으로 여러 차례 찾아가 보며 이웃들에게 꼼꼼히 물어본 뒤에야 비로소 그 그림이 무엇을 그린 것인지 어렴풋이나마 이해에 다다를 수 있었다."], ["Such unaccountable masses of shades and shadows, that at first you almost thought some ambitious young artist, in the time of the New England hags, had endeavored to delineate chaos bewitched.", "도무지 설명할 수 없는 음영과 그림자 덩어리들이 뒤엉켜 있어, 처음에는 뉴잉글랜드(New England)의 마녀 시대에 어느 야심 찬 젊은 화가가 마법에 걸린 혼돈을 그려 보려 애쓴 것이 아닐까 하는 생각마저 들 정도였다."], ["But by dint of much and earnest contemplation, and oft repeated ponderings, and especially by throwing open the little window towards the back of the entry, you at last come to the conclusion that such an idea, however wild, might not be altogether unwarranted.", "그러나 거듭 진지하게 응시하고 몇 번이고 곰곰이 헤아린 끝에, 특히 현관 안쪽으로 난 작은 창을 활짝 열어젖힌 뒤에는, 그런 발상이 아무리 황당하다 한들 전혀 근거 없는 것만은 아닐지도 모른다는 결론에 이르게 되었다."]], [["But what most puzzled and confounded you was a long, limber, portentous, black mass of something hovering in the centre of the picture over three blue, dim, perpendicular lines floating in a nameless yeast.", "그러나 무엇보다도 사람을 어리둥절하게 하고 혼란에 빠뜨린 것은, 그림 한가운데에 어른거리는 길고 흐느적거리며 불길한 검은 덩어리였으니, 그것은 이름 모를 효모 같은 거품 속에 떠 있는 푸르고 흐릿한 세 개의 수직선 위로 드리워져 있었다."], ["A boggy, soggy, squitchy picture truly, enough to drive a nervous man distracted.", "그것은 참으로 질척하고 축축하며 흐물거리는 그림이어서, 신경이 예민한 사람이라면 정신을 놓을 만했다."], ["Yet was there a sort of indefinite, half-attained, unimaginable sublimity about it that fairly froze you to it, till you involuntarily took an oath with yourself to find out what that marvellous painting meant.", "그럼에도 거기에는 어딘가 막연하고 절반쯤만 잡힐 듯한, 상상조차 할 수 없는 숭고함이 깃들어 있어, 그것을 보는 이를 그 자리에 얼어붙게 만들었고, 마침내 저 경이로운 그림이 대체 무엇을 뜻하는지 기어이 알아내고야 말겠다고 자신도 모르게 맹세하게 만들었다. 이따금 밝은, 그러나 아아, 허망하게도 빗나간 생각이 번개처럼 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["Ever and anon a bright, but, alas, deceptive idea would dart you through.—It’s the Black Sea in a midnight gale.—It’s the unnatural combat of the four primal elements.—It’s a blasted heath.—It’s a Hyperborean winter scene.—It’s the breaking-up of the icebound stream of Time.", "— 한밤중 폭풍 속의 흑해(Black Sea)로구나. — 네 가지 원초적 원소들의 부자연스러운 싸움이로구나."], ["But at last all these fancies yielded to that one portentous something in the picture’s midst.", "— 벼락 맞은 황야로구나. — 극북(極北)의 겨울 풍경이로구나."], ["That once found out, and all the rest were plain.", "— 얼음에 갇혀 있던 시간의 강이 풀리며 깨어지는 광경이로구나. 그러나 마침내 이 모든 공상은 그림 한복판의 그 불길한 무언가에 자리를 내주고 말았다."], ["But stop; does it not bear a faint resemblance to a gigantic fish?", "일단 그것의 정체를 알아내고 나면 나머지는 모두 분명해질 터였다. 그러나 잠깐 — 저것은 거대한 물고기와 어렴풋이 닮지 않았는가?"], ["even the great leviathan himself?", "다름 아닌 저 위대한 리바이어던(Leviathan) 그 자체와?"]], [["In fact, the artist’s design seemed this: a final theory of my own, partly based upon the aggregated opinions of many aged persons with whom I conversed upon the subject. The picture represents a Cape-Horner in a great hurricane; the half-foundered ship weltering there with its three dismantled masts alone visible; and an exasperated whale, purposing to spring clean over the craft, is in the enormous act of impaling himself upon the three mast-heads.", "사실 그 화가의 의도는 이러했던 것 같다. 이는 내가 이 주제를 두고 이야기를 나눈 여러 나이 든 사람들의 의견을 한데 모아, 부분적으로 거기에 근거를 두고 세운 나만의 최종적인 견해다. 그 그림은 거대한 허리케인을 만난 케이프 혼(Cape Horn) 항해선을 그린 것이었다. 반쯤 가라앉은 배가 그 자리에서 뒤척이며, 장비를 다 잃은 세 개의 돛대만이 겨우 보일 뿐이었다. 그리고 격분한 고래(Whale) 한 마리가 그 배를 단숨에 뛰어넘으려다가, 세 돛대 꼭대기에 제 몸을 꿰뚫는 그 어마어마한 동작의 한가운데에 있었다."]], [["The opposite wall of this entry was hung all over with a heathenish array of monstrous clubs and spears.", "이 현관의 맞은편 벽에는 괴물 같은 곤봉과 창들이 이교적인 모양새로 빼곡히 걸려 있었다."], ["Some were thickly set with glittering teeth resembling ivory saws; others were tufted with knots of human hair; and one was sickle-shaped, with a vast handle sweeping round like the segment made in the new-mown grass by a long-armed mower.", "어떤 것들은 상아 톱을 닮은 반짝이는 이빨들이 촘촘히 박혀 있었고, 어떤 것들은 사람의 머리카락 다발이 술처럼 매달려 있었으며, 또 하나는 낫 모양으로, 팔이 긴 풀 베는 사람이 갓 벤 풀밭에 그려 내는 호선(弧線)처럼 빙 돌아가는 커다란 자루가 달려 있었다."], ["You shuddered as you gazed, and wondered what monstrous cannibal and savage could ever have gone a death-harvesting with such a hacking, horrifying implement.", "그것을 응시하노라면 몸서리가 쳐졌고, 대체 어떤 괴물 같은 식인종과 야만인이 이토록 끔찍하게 베어 대는 무시무시한 도구로 죽음의 수확에 나섰을까 궁금해졌다."], ["Mixed with these were rusty old whaling lances and harpoons all broken and deformed.", "이것들 사이에는 녹슨 옛 포경용 작은창과 작살들이 죄다 부러지고 일그러진 채 섞여 있었다."], ["Some were storied weapons.", "그중 어떤 것들은 사연이 깃든 무기였다."], ["With this once long lance, now wildly elbowed, fifty years ago did Nathan Swain kill fifteen whales between a sunrise and a sunset.", "지금은 마구 꺾인 이 한때 길었던 작은창으로, 오십 년 전에 네이선 스웨인이 해돋이부터 해넘이 사이에 고래 열다섯 마리를 잡았다."], ["And that harpoon—so like a corkscrew now—was flung in Javan seas, and run away with by a whale, years afterwards slain off the Cape of Blanco.", "그리고 저 작살은 — 지금은 코르크 마개뽑이를 닮았지만 — 자바해(Java seas)에서 던져졌다가 어느 고래에게 박힌 채 끌려갔고, 여러 해 뒤에 블랑코곶(Cape of Blanco) 앞바다에서 그 고래가 잡혔다."], ["The original iron entered nigh the tail, and, like a restless needle sojourning in the body of a man, travelled full forty feet, and at last was found imbedded in the hump.", "처음에 작살이 꽂힌 곳은 꼬리 근처였는데, 마치 사람의 몸속을 쉬지 않고 떠도는 바늘처럼 꼬박 사십 피트(약 12미터)를 옮겨 다니다가, 마침내 등의 혹 속에 박혀 있는 것이 발견되었다."]], [["Crossing this dusky entry, and on through yon low-arched way—cut through what in old times must have been a great central chimney with fireplaces all round—you enter the public room.", "이 어둑한 현관을 가로질러, 저 낮은 아치형 통로 — 옛날에는 둘레마다 벽난로가 딸린 거대한 중앙 굴뚝이었을 곳을 뚫어 만든 길 — 를 지나면 공용실로 들어서게 된다."], ["A still duskier place is this, with such low ponderous beams above, and such old wrinkled planks beneath, that you would almost fancy you trod some old craft’s cockpits, especially of such a howling night, when this corner-anchored old ark rocked so furiously.", "이곳은 한층 더 어둑한 곳으로, 위로는 묵직하고 낮은 들보들이, 아래로는 오래되어 주름진 널빤지들이 깔려 있어, 어느 낡은 배의 조타실을 밟고 있는 듯한 착각이 들 지경이었으니, 특히나 이렇게 바람이 울부짖는 밤, 한 귀퉁이에 닻을 내린 이 낡은 방주가 그토록 사납게 흔들릴 때면 더욱 그러했다."], ["On one side stood a long, low, shelf-like table covered with cracked glass cases, filled with dusty rarities gathered from this wide world’s remotest nooks.", "한쪽에는 길고 낮은 선반 같은 탁자가 놓여 있었는데, 그 위에는 금이 간 유리 진열장들이 덮여 있었고, 그 안에는 이 넓은 세상의 가장 외진 구석구석에서 모아 온 먼지투성이 진귀품들이 가득 차 있었다."], ["Projecting from the further angle of the room stands a dark-looking den—the bar—a rude attempt at a right whale’s head.", "방의 저쪽 모퉁이에서 튀어나온 곳에는 어둑한 굴 같은 곳 — 바로 술청이 — 서 있었는데, 참고래의 머리를 어설프게 흉내 낸 것이었다."], ["Be that how it may, there stands the vast arched bone of the whale’s jaw, so wide, a coach might almost drive beneath it.", "어찌 됐든 거기에는 고래 턱뼈의 거대한 아치가 서 있었으니, 어찌나 넓던지 마차 한 대가 그 밑으로 지나갈 수 있을 듯했다."], ["Within are shabby shelves, ranged round with old decanters, bottles, flasks; and in those jaws of swift destruction, like another cursed Jonah (by which name indeed they called him), bustles a little withered old man, who, for their money, dearly sells the sailors deliriums and death.", "그 안에는 허름한 선반들이 있어, 둘레로 낡은 유리병과 병들과 술병들이 늘어서 있었다. 그리고 그 신속한 파멸의 턱 속에서, 또 다른 저주받은 요나(Jonah)처럼 (실제로 사람들은 그를 그 이름으로 불렀다), 자그마하고 쪼글쪼글한 늙은이가 부산을 떨며, 선원들에게서 돈을 받고 광란과 죽음을 비싸게 팔아넘기고 있었다."]], [["Abominable are the tumblers into which he pours his poison.", "그가 독약을 따라 붓는 술잔들이란 가증스럽기 짝이 없었다."], ["Though true cylinders without—within, the villanous green goggling glasses deceitfully tapered downwards to a cheating bottom.", "겉으로는 영락없는 원통 모양이지만 — 안쪽으로는, 그 음흉한 초록빛 둥근 유리잔들이 교활하게도 아래로 갈수록 좁아져 사람을 속이는 바닥으로 이어졌다."], ["Parallel meridians rudely pecked into the glass, surround these footpads’ goblets.", "유리잔에는 자오선처럼 가지런한 눈금들이 거칠게 새겨져 이 노상강도들의 술잔을 빙 둘러쌌다."], ["Fill to this mark, and your charge is but a penny; to this a penny more; and so on to the full glass—the Cape Horn measure, which you may gulp down for a shilling.", "이 눈금까지 채우면 값은 고작 한 푼, 저 눈금까지면 한 푼 더, 그렇게 가다가 잔을 가득 채우면 — 그것이 바로 케이프 혼 잔으로, 한 실링이면 단숨에 들이켤 수 있었다."]], [["Upon entering the place I found a number of young seamen gathered about a table, examining by a dim light divers specimens of skrimshander.", "그곳에 들어서니 젊은 선원 여럿이 탁자 둘레에 모여 어슴푸레한 불빛 아래 여러 가지 고래 뼈 세공품(skrimshander)을 들여다보고 있었다."], ["I sought the landlord, and telling him I desired to be accommodated with a room, received for answer that his house was full—not a bed unoccupied.", "나는 여관 주인을 찾아 방 하나를 묵게 해 달라고 청했더니, 집이 꽉 차서 빈 침대가 하나도 없다는 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["“But avast,”", "\"하지만 잠깐만,\""], ["he added, tapping his forehead, “you haint no objections to sharing a harpooneer’s blanket, have ye?", "그가 이마를 톡톡 두드리며 덧붙였다. \"작살잡이하고 담요를 나눠 쓰는 건 마다하지 않으시겠죠, 안 그렇소?"], ["I s’pose you are goin’ a-whalin’, so you’d better get used to that sort of thing.”", "보아하니 고래잡이를 나서실 모양인데, 그런 일에 익숙해지는 게 좋을 거요.\""]], [["I told him that I never liked to sleep two in a bed; that if I should ever do so, it would depend upon who the harpooneer might be, and that if he (the landlord) really had no other place for me, and the harpooneer was not decidedly objectionable, why rather than wander further about a strange town on so bitter a night, I would put up with the half of any decent man’s blanket.", "나는 결코 한 침대에서 둘이 자는 것을 좋아한 적이 없으며, 설령 그런 일을 하게 된다 해도 그건 그 작살잡이가 어떤 사람이냐에 달린 일이고, 만약 그(여관 주인)에게 정말로 나를 위한 다른 자리가 없고, 그 작살잡이가 딱히 거슬리는 자가 아니라면, 이토록 매서운 밤에 낯선 도시를 더 헤매고 다니느니 차라리 멀쩡한 사람의 담요 절반을 나눠 쓰는 편을 택하겠노라고 말했다."]], [["“I thought so.", "\"그럴 줄 알았소."], ["All right; take a seat.", "좋소. 자리에 앉으시오."], ["Supper?—you want supper?", "저녁? — 저녁을 드시겠소?"], ["Supper’ll be ready directly.”", "저녁은 곧 준비될 거요.\""]], [["I sat down on an old wooden settle, carved all over like a bench on the Battery.", "나는 배터리 공원의 벤치처럼 온통 조각이 새겨진 낡은 나무 의자에 앉았다."], ["At one end a ruminating tar was still further adorning it with his jack-knife, stooping over and diligently working away at the space between his legs.", "한쪽 끝에서는 무언가 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 늙은 뱃사람 하나가 주머니칼로 그 의자를 한층 더 꾸미고 있었는데, 몸을 잔뜩 수그린 채 두 다리 사이의 공간을 부지런히 깎아 내고 있었다."], ["He was trying his hand at a ship under full sail, but he didn’t make much headway, I thought.", "그는 돛을 한껏 펼친 배 한 척을 새겨 보려 애쓰는 중이었지만, 내가 보기에 별 진척은 없는 듯했다."]], [["At last some four or five of us were summoned to our meal in an adjoining room.", "마침내 우리 중 너덧 명이 옆방으로 식사를 하라는 부름을 받았다."], ["It was cold as Iceland—no fire at all—the landlord said he couldn’t afford it.", "그곳은 아이슬란드처럼 추웠고 — 불이라곤 전혀 없었다 — 여관 주인은 그럴 형편이 못 된다고 했다."], ["Nothing but two dismal tallow candles, each in a winding sheet.", "음울한 수지 양초 두 자루가 각기 수의(壽衣) 같은 촛농에 싸여 있을 뿐이었다."], ["We were fain to button up our monkey jackets, and hold to our lips cups of scalding tea with our half frozen fingers.", "우리는 하는 수 없이 두꺼운 선원용 재킷의 단추를 잠그고, 반쯤 얼어붙은 손가락으로 펄펄 끓는 차 잔을 입술에 가져다 댔다."], ["But the fare was of the most substantial kind—not only meat and potatoes, but dumplings; good heavens!", "그러나 음식만큼은 더없이 푸짐한 종류였으니 — 고기와 감자뿐 아니라 경단까지 있었다. 맙소사!"], ["dumplings for supper!", "저녁상에 경단이라니!"], ["One young fellow in a green box coat, addressed himself to these dumplings in a most direful manner.", "초록빛 박스코트를 걸친 한 젊은이가 그 경단을 더없이 무시무시한 기세로 공략하기 시작했다."]], [["“My boy,”", "\"이보게, 젊은이,\""], ["said the landlord, “you’ll have the nightmare to a dead sartainty.”", "여관 주인이 말했다. \"자넨 틀림없이 가위에 눌릴 걸세, 그건 확실하네.\""]], [["“Landlord,”", "\"주인장,\""], ["I whispered, “that aint the harpooneer is it?”", "내가 속삭였다. \"저 친구가 그 작살잡이는 아니죠?\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"아, 아니오,\""], ["said he, looking a sort of diabolically funny, “the harpooneer is a dark complexioned chap.", "그가 어딘가 악마처럼 우스꽝스러운 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"그 작살잡이는 피부가 가무잡잡한 친구요."], ["He never eats dumplings, he don’t—he eats nothing but steaks, and he likes ’em rare.”", "그자는 경단 따위는 절대 먹지 않소. 절대로 — 그자는 오로지 스테이크만 먹고, 그것도 설익은 걸 좋아하지.\""]], [["“The devil he does,”", "\"그것 참 별난 친구로군,\""], ["says I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Where is that harpooneer?", "\"그 작살잡이는 어디 있소?"], ["Is he here?”", "여기 있소?\""]], [["“He’ll be here afore long,”", "\"머지않아 올 거요,\""], ["was the answer.", "하는 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["I could not help it, but I began to feel suspicious of this “dark complexioned”", "나로서는 어쩔 수 없이, 이 \"가무잡잡한\" 작살잡이에 대해 점점 의심이 들기 시작했다."], ["harpooneer. At any rate, I made up my mind that if it so turned out that we should sleep together, he must undress and get into bed before I did.", "어찌 됐든, 만약 우리가 함께 자게 되는 상황이 벌어진다면 그자가 나보다 먼저 옷을 벗고 침대에 들어가야 한다고 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["Supper over, the company went back to the bar-room, when, knowing not what else to do with myself, I resolved to spend the rest of the evening as a looker on.", "저녁 식사가 끝나자 사람들은 술청으로 돌아갔고, 나는 달리 무엇을 해야 할지 몰라 그날 저녁의 나머지를 구경꾼으로 보내기로 작정했다."]], [["Presently a rioting noise was heard without.", "이윽고 밖에서 떠들썩한 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Starting up, the landlord cried, “That’s the Grampus’s crew.", "여관 주인은 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"저건 그램퍼스호 선원들이로군."], ["I seed her reported in the offing this morning; a three years’ voyage, and a full ship.", "오늘 아침에 앞바다에 그 배가 들어왔다는 소식을 들었지. 삼 년짜리 항해에다 배가 가득 찼다더군."], ["Hurrah, boys; now we’ll have the latest news from the Feegees.”", "야, 이보게들, 이제 피지(Feegees)의 최신 소식을 들을 수 있겠어.\""]], [["A tramping of sea boots was heard in the entry; the door was flung open, and in rolled a wild set of mariners enough.", "현관에서 바다 장화 소리가 쿵쿵 울렸다. 문이 벌컥 열리더니, 더없이 거친 한 무리의 뱃사람들이 우르르 굴러들어 왔다."], ["Enveloped in their shaggy watch coats, and with their heads muffled in woollen comforters, all bedarned and ragged, and their beards stiff with icicles, they seemed an eruption of bears from Labrador.", "텁수룩한 당직용 외투를 두르고, 머리에는 온통 기워지고 너덜너덜한 모직 목도리를 칭칭 감았으며, 수염은 고드름으로 뻣뻣하게 굳은 그들은, 마치 래브라도에서 쏟아져 나온 곰 떼 같았다."], ["They had just landed from their boat, and this was the first house they entered.", "그들은 방금 보트에서 내린 참이었고, 이곳이 그들이 처음 들어선 집이었다."], ["No wonder, then, that they made a straight wake for the whale’s mouth—the bar—when the wrinkled little old Jonah, there officiating, soon poured them out brimmers all round.", "그러니 그들이 곧장 고래의 입 — 술청 — 을 향해 항로를 잡은 것도 놀랄 일이 아니었으니, 거기서 일을 맡아보던 쪼글쪼글한 작은 늙은이 요나가 이내 그들 모두에게 잔이 넘치도록 술을 따라 주었다."], ["One complained of a bad cold in his head, upon which Jonah mixed him a pitch-like potion of gin and molasses, which he swore was a sovereign cure for all colds and catarrhs whatsoever, never mind of how long standing, or whether caught off the coast of Labrador, or on the weather side of an ice-island.", "한 사람이 머리에 지독한 감기가 들었다고 투덜대자, 요나는 그에게 진과 당밀을 섞은 역청 같은 물약을 만들어 주면서, 그것이 아무리 오래된 것이든, 래브라도 해안에서 걸렸든 빙산의 바람받이 쪽에서 걸렸든, 온갖 감기와 카타르에 듣는 만병통치약이라고 장담했다."]], [["The liquor soon mounted into their heads, as it generally does even with the arrantest topers newly landed from sea, and they began capering about most obstreperously.", "술은 곧 그들의 머리로 올라갔으니, 바다에서 갓 내린 지독한 술꾼들조차 대개 그러하듯이, 그들은 더없이 요란스럽게 까불며 날뛰기 시작했다."]], [["I observed, however, that one of them held somewhat aloof, and though he seemed desirous not to spoil the hilarity of his shipmates by his own sober face, yet upon the whole he refrained from making as much noise as the rest.", "그러나 나는 그들 가운데 한 사람이 어쩐지 떨어져 있는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그는 제 진지한 얼굴 때문에 동료들의 흥을 깨고 싶지 않은 듯했지만, 전체적으로는 나머지 사람들만큼 떠들썩하게 굴기를 삼가고 있었다."], ["This man interested me at once; and since the sea-gods had ordained that he should soon become my shipmate (though but a sleeping-partner one, so far as this narrative is concerned), I will here venture upon a little description of him.", "이 사람은 단번에 내 흥미를 끌었다. 그리고 바다의 신들이 그가 곧 나의 뱃동무가 되도록 정해 놓았으므로 (이 이야기에 관한 한 그저 잠든 동업자에 지나지 않지만), 나는 여기서 그를 조금 묘사해 보고자 한다."], ["He stood full six feet in height, with noble shoulders, and a chest like a coffer-dam.", "그는 키가 족히 육 피트(약 1.8미터)는 되었고, 어깨가 떡 벌어졌으며, 가슴은 방죽처럼 단단했다."], ["I have seldom seen such brawn in a man.", "나는 그토록 우람한 근육을 가진 사람을 좀처럼 본 적이 없었다."], ["His face was deeply brown and burnt, making his white teeth dazzling by the contrast; while in the deep shadows of his eyes floated some reminiscences that did not seem to give him much joy.", "그의 얼굴은 깊이 그을려 거뭇했는데, 그 대비로 하얀 이가 눈부시게 빛났다. 한편 그의 눈 깊은 그림자 속에는 그에게 그다지 기쁨을 주지 않는 듯한 어떤 추억들이 떠다니고 있었다."], ["His voice at once announced that he was a Southerner, and from his fine stature, I thought he must be one of those tall mountaineers from the Alleghanian Ridge in Virginia.", "그의 목소리는 단번에 그가 남부 사람임을 알려 주었고, 그 훤칠한 체격으로 미루어 나는 그가 버지니아의 앨러게니 산맥에서 온 키 큰 산사람들 가운데 하나임에 틀림없다고 생각했다."], ["When the revelry of his companions had mounted to its height, this man slipped away unobserved, and I saw no more of him till he became my comrade on the sea.", "동료들의 흥청거림이 절정에 달했을 때, 이 사람은 아무도 모르게 슬며시 빠져나갔고, 나는 그가 바다에서 나의 동료가 되기 전까지는 그를 다시 보지 못했다."], ["In a few minutes, however, he was missed by his shipmates, and being, it seems, for some reason a huge favourite with them, they raised a cry of “Bulkington!", "그러나 몇 분 지나지 않아 동료들은 그가 사라진 것을 알아챘고, 무슨 까닭에선지 그를 무척이나 좋아했던 모양인지, \"벌킹턴!"], ["Bulkington!", "벌킹턴!"], ["where’s Bulkington?”", "벌킹턴은 어디 갔어?\""], ["and darted out of the house in pursuit of him.", "하고 외쳐 대더니 그를 찾아 집 밖으로 우르르 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["It was now about nine o’clock, and the room seeming almost supernaturally quiet after these orgies, I began to congratulate myself upon a little plan that had occurred to me just previous to the entrance of the seamen.", "이제 시각은 아홉 시쯤 되었고, 그 흥청거림이 지나간 뒤 방 안은 거의 초자연적일 만큼 고요해진 듯했다. 나는 선원들이 들어오기 바로 직전에 떠올랐던 자그마한 계획을 두고 스스로 흐뭇해하기 시작했다."]], [["No man prefers to sleep two in a bed.", "한 침대에서 둘이 자는 것을 좋아할 사람은 아무도 없다."], ["In fact, you would a good deal rather not sleep with your own brother.", "사실 친형제와도 함께 자지 않는 편을 훨씬 더 바랄 것이다."], ["I don’t know how it is, but people like to be private when they are sleeping.", "어찌 된 영문인지는 모르겠으나, 사람들은 잠을 잘 때만큼은 혼자이고 싶어 한다."], ["And when it comes to sleeping with an unknown stranger, in a strange inn, in a strange town, and that stranger a harpooneer, then your objections indefinitely multiply.", "더구나 낯선 여관, 낯선 도시에서, 알지도 못하는 낯선 자와 함께 자야 한다고 하면, 게다가 그 낯선 자가 작살잡이라면, 거부감은 한도 끝도 없이 불어난다."], ["Nor was there any earthly reason why I as a sailor should sleep two in a bed, more than anybody else; for sailors no more sleep two in a bed at sea, than bachelor Kings do ashore.", "게다가 선원인 나라고 해서 다른 누구보다 더 한 침대에서 둘이 자야 할 까닭은 이 세상 어디에도 없었다. 바다에서 선원들이 한 침대에서 둘이 자는 일은, 뭍에서 독신 왕들이 그러는 것만큼이나 없는 일이니까."], ["To be sure they all sleep together in one apartment, but you have your own hammock, and cover yourself with your own blanket, and sleep in your own skin.", "물론 그들은 모두 한 선실에서 함께 잠을 자지만, 저마다 제 해먹을 갖고 있고, 제 담요로 몸을 덮고, 제 살갗 속에서 잠을 잔다."]], [["The more I pondered over this harpooneer, the more I abominated the thought of sleeping with him.", "그 작살잡이에 대해 곰곰이 생각하면 할수록, 그자와 함께 잔다는 생각이 점점 더 끔찍하게 여겨졌다."], ["It was fair to presume that being a harpooneer, his linen or woollen, as the case might be, would not be of the tidiest, certainly none of the finest.", "작살잡이인 만큼, 그의 속옷이 무명이든 모직이든 간에 그것이 가장 깨끗하지는 않으리라고, 하물며 가장 고급은 결코 아니리라고 짐작하는 것이 타당했다."], ["I began to twitch all over.", "나는 온몸이 근질거리기 시작했다."], ["Besides, it was getting late, and my decent harpooneer ought to be home and going bedwards.", "게다가 시간도 점점 늦어졌으니, 멀쩡한 작살잡이라면 마땅히 집에 돌아와 잠자리에 들 시각이었다."], ["Suppose now, he should tumble in upon me at midnight—how could I tell from what vile hole he had been coming?", "그런데 만약 그자가 한밤중에 내 위로 굴러들어 온다면 — 그자가 대체 어떤 더러운 소굴에서 오는 길인지 내가 무슨 수로 알겠는가?"]], [["“Landlord!", "\"주인장!"], ["I’ve changed my mind about that harpooneer.—I shan’t sleep with him.", "그 작살잡이에 대해 마음을 바꿨소. — 그자하고는 자지 않겠소."], ["I’ll try the bench here.”", "여기 이 벤치에서 해 보겠소.\""]], [["“Just as you please; I’m sorry I can’t spare ye a tablecloth for a mattress, and it’s a plaguy rough board here”—feeling of the knots and notches.", "\"좋으실 대로 하시오. 매트리스 삼을 식탁보 한 장 내드리지 못해 미안하구려."], ["“But wait a bit, Skrimshander; I’ve got a carpenter’s plane there in the bar—wait, I say, and I’ll make ye snug enough.”", "그리고 여기 이 판자는 지긋지긋하게 거칠다오.\" 그러면서 그는 옹이와 흠집들을 더듬어 보았다."], ["So saying he procured the plane; and with his old silk handkerchief first dusting the bench, vigorously set to planing away at my bed, the while grinning like an ape.", "\"하지만 잠깐만 기다려 보쇼, 뼈 세공쟁이 양반. 술청에 목수용 대패가 하나 있으니 — 기다리라니까, 내 아주 편안하게 만들어 드리리다.\""], ["The shavings flew right and left; till at last the plane-iron came bump against an indestructible knot.", "이렇게 말하며 그는 대패를 가져왔다. 그러고는 낡은 비단 손수건으로 먼저 벤치의 먼지를 턴 다음, 원숭이처럼 히죽거리며 내 잠자리를 힘차게 대패질하기 시작했다."], ["The landlord was near spraining his wrist, and I told him for heaven’s sake to quit—the bed was soft enough to suit me, and I did not know how all the planing in the world could make eider down of a pine plank.", "대팻밥이 좌우로 날렸고, 마침내 대팻날이 도무지 깎이지 않는 옹이에 쾅 부딪혔다. 여관 주인이 손목을 삘 지경이 되자, 나는 제발 그만두라고, 침대는 내게 충분히 부드러우며, 세상의 그 어떤 대패질로도 소나무 널빤지가 오리털이 될 리는 없지 않느냐고 말했다."], ["So gathering up the shavings with another grin, and throwing them into the great stove in the middle of the room, he went about his business, and left me in a brown study.", "그러자 그는 또 한 번 히죽거리며 대팻밥을 그러모아 방 한가운데의 커다란 난로에 던져 넣고는, 제 일을 보러 가 버려 나를 깊은 상념에 잠기게 했다."]], [["I now took the measure of the bench, and found that it was a foot too short; but that could be mended with a chair.", "나는 이제 벤치의 치수를 재 보았는데, 한 발쯤 짧다는 것을 알았다. 그래도 그건 의자 하나로 메울 수 있었다."], ["But it was a foot too narrow, and the other bench in the room was about four inches higher than the planed one—so there was no yoking them.", "그러나 폭이 한 발쯤 좁았고, 방 안의 다른 벤치는 대패질한 것보다 네 인치쯤 높아서 — 둘을 짝지을 수가 없었다."], ["I then placed the first bench lengthwise along the only clear space against the wall, leaving a little interval between, for my back to settle down in.", "그래서 나는 첫 번째 벤치를 벽에 붙은 유일하게 빈 자리에 길게 세로로 놓고, 등을 누일 수 있도록 약간의 틈을 남겨 두었다."], ["But I soon found that there came such a draught of cold air over me from under the sill of the window, that this plan would never do at all, especially as another current from the rickety door met the one from the window, and both together formed a series of small whirlwinds in the immediate vicinity of the spot where I had thought to spend the night.", "그러나 곧 창틀 밑에서 차가운 바람이 어찌나 들이치는지, 이 계획은 도저히 통하지 않으리라는 것을 깨달았다. 더구나 삐걱거리는 문틈에서 들어오는 또 다른 바람이 창에서 오는 바람과 만나, 둘이 합쳐져 내가 밤을 보내려던 바로 그 자리 근처에 자그마한 회오리바람들을 줄지어 일으켰기 때문이었다."]], [["The devil fetch that harpooneer, thought I, but stop, couldn’t I steal a march on him—bolt his door inside, and jump into his bed, not to be wakened by the most violent knockings?", "악마나 그 작살잡이를 잡아가라지, 하고 나는 생각했다. 그러나 잠깐, 그자보다 한발 앞서 선수를 칠 수는 없을까 — 안에서 그자의 방문을 걸어 잠그고, 그자의 침대로 뛰어들어, 아무리 거세게 두드려도 깨지 않으면 어떨까?"], ["It seemed no bad idea; but upon second thoughts I dismissed it.", "나쁘지 않은 생각 같았다. 그러나 다시 곰곰이 따져 보고는 그 생각을 접었다."], ["For who could tell but what the next morning, so soon as I popped out of the room, the harpooneer might be standing in the entry, all ready to knock me down!", "이튿날 아침, 내가 방에서 막 튀어나오자마자 그 작살잡이가 현관에 떡 버티고 서서 나를 한 대 후려칠 채비를 갖추고 있지 않으리라고 누가 장담하겠는가!"]], [["Still, looking round me again, and seeing no possible chance of spending a sufferable night unless in some other person’s bed, I began to think that after all I might be cherishing unwarrantable prejudices against this unknown harpooneer.", "그래도 다시 한번 주위를 둘러보고, 다른 누군가의 침대가 아니고서는 그럭저럭 견딜 만한 밤을 보낼 가능성이 도무지 없음을 깨닫자, 나는 어쩌면 결국 이 알지도 못하는 작살잡이에 대해 근거 없는 편견을 품고 있는 것은 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["Thinks I, I’ll wait awhile; he must be dropping in before long.", "잠시 기다려 보자, 하고 나는 생각했다. 그자도 머지않아 들어올 테니."], ["I’ll have a good look at him then, and perhaps we may become jolly good bedfellows after all—there’s no telling.", "그때 그자를 잘 살펴보면, 어쩌면 우리가 결국 더없이 즐거운 잠자리 동무가 될지도 모를 일이다 — 누가 알겠는가."]], [["But though the other boarders kept coming in by ones, twos, and threes, and going to bed, yet no sign of my harpooneer.", "그러나 다른 투숙객들이 한 명, 두 명, 세 명씩 들어와 잠자리에 드는 동안에도, 내 작살잡이의 기척은 도무지 없었다."]], [["“Landlord!”", "\"주인장!\""], ["said I, “what sort of a chap is he—does he always keep such late hours?”", "내가 말했다. \"그 친구는 대체 어떤 사람이오 — 늘 이렇게 늦게까지 다니오?\""], ["It was now hard upon twelve o’clock.", "시각은 이제 거의 열두 시가 다 되어 가고 있었다."]], [["The landlord chuckled again with his lean chuckle, and seemed to be mightily tickled at something beyond my comprehension.", "여관 주인은 또다시 그 메마른 웃음으로 킬킬거렸는데, 내가 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 무언가가 어지간히 우스운 모양이었다."], ["“No,”", "\"아니오,\""], ["he answered, “generally he’s an early bird—airley to bed and airley to rise—yes, he’s the bird what catches the worm.", "그가 대답했다. \"보통은 일찍 일어나는 새지 — 일찍 자고 일찍 일어나거든 — 그래, 그자야말로 벌레를 잡는 새라오."], ["But to-night he went out a peddling, you see, and I don’t see what on airth keeps him so late, unless, may be, he can’t sell his head.”", "한데 오늘 밤엔 행상을 나갔단 말이지, 알겠소. 그러니 대체 무슨 영문으로 이리 늦는지 나도 모르겠소, 혹시 머리를 못 팔고 있는 게 아니라면 말이오.\""]], [["“Can’t sell his head?—What sort of a bamboozingly story is this you are telling me?”", "\"머리를 못 팔아? — 대체 무슨 사람 홀리는 소릴 지껄이는 거요?\""], ["getting into a towering rage.", "나는 치솟는 분노에 휩싸이며 말했다."], ["“Do you pretend to say, landlord, that this harpooneer is actually engaged this blessed Saturday night, or rather Sunday morning, in peddling his head around this town?”", "\"주인장, 그래 이 작살잡이가 이 거룩한 토요일 밤, 아니 차라리 일요일 새벽에, 이 도시를 돌아다니며 제 머리를 팔고 있다고 말하려는 거요?\""]], [["“That’s precisely it,”", "\"바로 그거요,\""], ["said the landlord, “and I told him he couldn’t sell it here, the market’s overstocked.”", "여관 주인이 말했다. \"그래서 내가 그자한테 여기선 못 판다고 했지, 시장에 물건이 넘쳐 난다고 말이오.\""]], [["“With what?”", "\"무엇이 넘쳐 난단 말이오?\""], ["shouted I.", "내가 소리쳤다."]], [["“With heads to be sure; ain’t there too many heads in the world?”", "\"머리지, 당연히. 세상에 머리가 너무 많지 않소?\""]], [["“I tell you what it is, landlord,”", "\"내 말해 두는데, 주인장,\""], ["said I quite calmly, “you’d better stop spinning that yarn to me—I’m not green.”", "나는 자못 차분하게 말했다. \"그런 허튼소리는 그만두는 게 좋을 거요 — 나는 풋내기가 아니란 말이오.\""]], [["“May be not,”", "\"그럴지도 모르지,\""], ["taking out a stick and whittling a toothpick, “but I rayther guess you’ll be done brown if that ere harpooneer hears you a slanderin’ his head.”", "그가 막대기를 꺼내어 이쑤시개를 깎으며 말했다. \"하지만 저 작살잡이가 자기 머리를 헐뜯는 소릴 듣게 되면, 당신이야말로 아주 노릇노릇하게 구워질 것 같은데.\""]], [["“I’ll break it for him,”", "\"내가 그자 머리를 박살 내 주지,\""], ["said I, now flying into a passion again at this unaccountable farrago of the landlord’s.", "나는 여관 주인의 이 도무지 알 수 없는 횡설수설에 또다시 발끈하며 말했다."]], [["“Broke,”", "\"그건 이미 박살 나 있소,\""], ["said I—“broke, do you mean?”", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Sartain, and that’s the very reason he can’t sell it, I guess.”", "\"박살이 나,\" 내가 말했다. \"박살이 났다니, 그게 무슨 소리요?\""]], [["“Landlord,” said I, going up to him as cool as Mt.", "\"틀림없소, 바로 그게 그자가 그걸 못 파는 까닭이지, 내 짐작엔 말이오.\""], ["Hecla in a snow-storm—“landlord, stop whittling.", "\"주인장,\" 나는 눈보라 속의 헤클라산처럼 차갑게 그에게 다가서며 말했다."], ["You and I must understand one another, and that too without delay.", "\"주인장, 그 이쑤시개 깎는 것 좀 그만두시오. 당신과 나는 서로 이해를 해 둬야겠소, 그것도 지체 없이 말이오."], ["I come to your house and want a bed; you tell me you can only give me half a one; that the other half belongs to a certain harpooneer.", "나는 당신 집에 와서 침대를 원했소. 그런데 당신은 내게 침대 절반밖에 못 준다고, 나머지 절반은 어떤 작살잡이의 것이라고 했소."], ["And about this harpooneer, whom I have not yet seen, you persist in telling me the most mystifying and exasperating stories tending to beget in me an uncomfortable feeling towards the man whom you design for my bedfellow—a sort of connexion, landlord, which is an intimate and confidential one in the highest degree.", "그리고 아직 본 적도 없는 이 작살잡이에 관해, 당신은 더없이 사람을 홀리고 약 올리는 이야기들을 끈질기게 늘어놓아, 당신이 내 잠자리 동무로 정해 놓은 사람에 대해 내 마음속에 불편한 감정을 심어 놓고 있소 — 주인장, 그건 더없이 친밀하고 은밀한 관계란 말이오. 이제 나는 당신에게 분명히 말할 것을 요구하오."], ["I now demand of you to speak out and tell me who and what this harpooneer is, and whether I shall be in all respects safe to spend the night with him.", "이 작살잡이가 누구이며 무엇 하는 자인지, 그리고 내가 모든 면에서 안전하게 그자와 하룻밤을 보낼 수 있을지 말이오. 그리고 무엇보다 먼저, 그자가 머리를 판다는 그 이야기를 부디 거두어 주시오."], ["And in the first place, you will be so good as to unsay that story about selling his head, which if true I take to be good evidence that this harpooneer is stark mad, and I’ve no idea of sleeping with a madman; and you, sir, you I mean, landlord, you, sir, by trying to induce me to do so knowingly, would thereby render yourself liable to a criminal prosecution.”", "만약 그게 사실이라면, 나는 그것을 이 작살잡이가 완전히 미쳤다는 확실한 증거로 받아들일 텐데, 미친 자와 함께 잘 생각은 추호도 없소. 그리고 당신, 당신 말이오, 주인장, 바로 당신이오, 그런 줄 알면서도 나더러 그렇게 하도록 꾀어내려 든다면, 당신은 그로써 형사 소추를 받게 될 거란 말이오.\""]], [["“Wall,” said the landlord, fetching a long breath, “that’s a purty long sarmon for a chap that rips a little now and then. But be easy, be easy, this here harpooneer I have been tellin’ you of has just arrived from the south seas, where he bought up a lot of ’balmed New Zealand heads (great curios, you know), and he’s sold all on ’em but one, and that one he’s trying to sell to-night, cause to-morrow’s Sunday, and it would not do to be sellin’ human heads about the streets when folks is goin’ to churches. He wanted to, last Sunday, but I stopped him just as he was goin’ out of the door with four heads strung on a string, for all the airth like a string of inions.”", "\"흠,\" 여관 주인이 긴 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"이따금 욕설 좀 내뱉는 양반치곤 꽤나 긴 설교로구려. 하지만 진정하쇼, 진정하라니까. 내가 당신한테 얘기해 온 이 작살잡이는 방금 남쪽 바다에서 돌아온 참이라오. 거기서 방부 처리한 뉴질랜드(New Zealand) 머리를 잔뜩 사들였는데 (대단한 골동품이지, 알겠소), 그걸 다 팔고 딱 하나가 남았소. 그래서 그걸 오늘 밤에 팔아 보려는 거요. 내일이 일요일이라, 사람들이 교회에 가는데 거리에서 사람 머리를 팔고 다니는 건 좋지 않을 테니까. 지난 일요일에도 그러려고 했지만, 내가 막 문을 나서려는 그자를 붙들었지. 글쎄 머리 네 개를 줄에 꿰어 들고 있었는데, 영락없이 양파 한 두름 같더라니까.\""]], [["This account cleared up the otherwise unaccountable mystery, and showed that the landlord, after all, had had no idea of fooling me—but at the same time what could I think of a harpooneer who stayed out of a Saturday night clean into the holy Sabbath, engaged in such a cannibal business as selling the heads of dead idolators?", "이 설명은 그러지 않았다면 도무지 풀리지 않았을 수수께끼를 명백히 해 주었고, 여관 주인이 결국은 나를 놀려 먹을 생각이 전혀 없었음을 보여 주었다. 그러나 동시에, 죽은 우상숭배자들의 머리를 파는 그런 식인종 같은 일에 매달려, 토요일 밤부터 거룩한 안식일까지 내내 밖으로 나돌아 다니는 작살잡이에 대해, 내가 무슨 생각을 할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["“Depend upon it, landlord, that harpooneer is a dangerous man.”", "\"틀림없소, 주인장, 그 작살잡이는 위험한 자요.\""]], [["“He pays reg’lar,”", "\"돈은 꼬박꼬박 내지,\""], ["was the rejoinder.", "하는 대꾸가 돌아왔다."], ["“But come, it’s getting dreadful late, you had better be turning flukes—it’s a nice bed; Sal and me slept in that ere bed the night we were spliced.", "\"하지만 자, 시간이 끔찍하게 늦어졌으니, 당신도 어서 꼬리를 틀고 자리에 드는 게 좋을 거요 — 좋은 침대라니까. 나랑 샐이 혼례를 올린 날 밤에 바로 그 침대에서 잤지."], ["There’s plenty of room for two to kick about in that bed; it’s an almighty big bed that.", "둘이 발버둥을 쳐도 남아돌 만큼 자리가 넉넉하다오. 그건 엄청나게 큰 침대거든."], ["Why, afore we give it up, Sal used to put our Sam and little Johnny in the foot of it.", "아, 그 침대를 내주기 전엔, 샐이 우리 샘이랑 어린 조니를 그 발치에 재우곤 했지."], ["But I got a dreaming and sprawling about one night, and somehow, Sam got pitched on the floor, and came near breaking his arm.", "그런데 어느 날 밤 내가 꿈을 꾸면서 마구 버둥대는 통에, 어쩌다 샘이 바닥으로 굴러떨어져 하마터면 팔이 부러질 뻔했다오. 그 뒤로 샐이 안 되겠다고 하더군."], ["Arter that, Sal said it wouldn’t do.", "자, 이리 오쇼, 내 금세 불을 밝혀 드리리다.\" 이렇게 말하며 그는 촛불을 켜서 내게 들이밀며 앞장서겠다고 했다."], ["Come along here, I’ll give ye a glim in a jiffy;”", "그러나 나는 망설이며 서 있었다. 그러자 그는 구석의 시계를 보더니 외쳤다."], ["and so saying he lighted a candle and held it towards me, offering to lead the way.", "\"이런, 벌써 일요일이로군 — 오늘 밤엔 그 작살잡이를 못 볼 거요. 그자는 어딘가에 닻을 내렸겠지 — 그러니 어서 오쇼."], ["But I stood irresolute; when looking at a clock in the corner, he exclaimed “I vum it’s Sunday—you won’t see that harpooneer to-night; he’s come to anchor somewhere—come along then; do come; won’t ye come?”", "자, 오라니까. 안 오겠다는 거요?\""]], [["I considered the matter a moment, and then up stairs we went, and I was ushered into a small room, cold as a clam, and furnished, sure enough, with a prodigious bed, almost big enough indeed for any four harpooneers to sleep abreast.", "나는 잠시 그 일을 곱씹어 보고는, 이윽고 우리는 위층으로 올라갔고, 나는 조개처럼 차가운 작은 방으로 안내받았는데, 과연 거기에는 어마어마하게 큰 침대가 갖춰져 있어, 실로 작살잡이 넷이 나란히 누워 잘 수 있을 만큼 컸다."]], [["“There,”", "\"자,\""], ["said the landlord, placing the candle on a crazy old sea chest that did double duty as a wash-stand and centre table; “there, make yourself comfortable now, and good night to ye.”", "여관 주인이 세면대와 가운데 탁자를 겸하는 다 낡은 선원용 궤짝 위에 촛불을 내려놓으며 말했다. \"자, 이제 편히 쉬쇼."], ["I turned round from eyeing the bed, but he had disappeared.", "그럼 잘 자시오.\" 나는 침대를 살펴보다가 몸을 돌렸는데, 그는 이미 사라지고 없었다."]], [["Folding back the counterpane, I stooped over the bed.", "침대보를 젖히고, 나는 침대 위로 몸을 수그렸다."], ["Though none of the most elegant, it yet stood the scrutiny tolerably well.", "가장 우아한 것은 못 되었지만, 그래도 자세히 살펴보니 그런대로 견딜 만했다."], ["I then glanced round the room; and besides the bedstead and centre table, could see no other furniture belonging to the place, but a rude shelf, the four walls, and a papered fireboard representing a man striking a whale.", "그런 다음 방 안을 둘러보았다. 침대 틀과 가운데 탁자 말고는, 이 방에 딸린 다른 가구라고는 보이지 않았고, 그저 거친 선반 하나, 사방의 벽들, 그리고 한 남자가 고래를 작살로 찌르는 그림이 그려진 벽지 바른 난로 가림판이 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Of things not properly belonging to the room, there was a hammock lashed up, and thrown upon the floor in one corner; also a large seaman’s bag, containing the harpooneer’s wardrobe, no doubt in lieu of a land trunk.", "본래 이 방에 속하지 않은 물건으로는, 단단히 묶여 한구석 바닥에 던져진 해먹 하나가 있었고, 또 뭍에서 쓰는 여행 가방을 대신하는 것이 분명한, 작살잡이의 옷가지가 든 커다란 선원용 자루도 있었다."], ["Likewise, there was a parcel of outlandish bone fish hooks on the shelf over the fire-place, and a tall harpoon standing at the head of the bed.", "마찬가지로, 벽난로 위 선반에는 기괴한 뼈로 만든 낚싯바늘 한 꾸러미가 있었고, 침대 머리맡에는 키 큰 작살 하나가 세워져 있었다."]], [["But what is this on the chest?", "그런데 궤짝 위에 놓인 이건 대체 무엇일까?"], ["I took it up, and held it close to the light, and felt it, and smelt it, and tried every way possible to arrive at some satisfactory conclusion concerning it.", "나는 그것을 집어 들어 불빛에 가까이 대고, 만져 보고, 냄새도 맡아 보며, 그것에 관해 어떤 그럴듯한 결론에 이르고자 가능한 모든 방법을 다 동원해 보았다."], ["I can compare it to nothing but a large door mat, ornamented at the edges with little tinkling tags something like the stained porcupine quills round an Indian moccasin.", "나로서는 그것을 커다란 현관 매트 말고는 달리 비할 데가 없었으니, 그 가장자리에는 인디언(Indian) 모카신 둘레의 물들인 호저 가시 같은, 짤랑거리는 자그마한 술 장식들이 달려 있었다."], ["There was a hole or slit in the middle of this mat, as you see the same in South American ponchos.", "이 매트 한가운데에는 구멍, 곧 트인 자국이 하나 있었는데, 남아메리카의 판초에서 볼 수 있는 것과 똑같았다."], ["But could it be possible that any sober harpooneer would get into a door mat, and parade the streets of any Christian town in that sort of guise?", "그러나 멀쩡한 정신의 작살잡이가 이런 현관 매트를 뒤집어쓰고, 그런 차림새로 어느 기독교 도시의 거리를 활보한다는 것이 과연 있을 법한 일일까?"], ["I put it on, to try it, and it weighed me down like a hamper, being uncommonly shaggy and thick, and I thought a little damp, as though this mysterious harpooneer had been wearing it of a rainy day.", "나는 그것을 한번 걸쳐 보았는데, 보기 드물게 텁수룩하고 두꺼운 데다, 어딘가 조금 축축하기까지 해서 — 마치 이 수수께끼 같은 작살잡이가 비 오는 날 그것을 걸치고 다녔던 것처럼 — 그것은 빨래 광주리처럼 나를 짓눌렀다."], ["I went up in it to a bit of glass stuck against the wall, and I never saw such a sight in my life.", "나는 그것을 걸친 채 벽에 붙은 작은 거울 조각 앞으로 다가가 보았는데, 내 평생 그런 꼴은 본 적이 없었다."], ["I tore myself out of it in such a hurry that I gave myself a kink in the neck.", "어찌나 황급히 그것을 벗어 던졌던지, 그 바람에 목을 삐끗하고 말았다."]], [["I sat down on the side of the bed, and commenced thinking about this head-peddling harpooneer, and his door mat.", "나는 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉아, 이 머리를 행상하는 작살잡이와 그의 현관 매트에 관해 생각하기 시작했다."], ["After thinking some time on the bed-side, I got up and took off my monkey jacket, and then stood in the middle of the room thinking.", "침대 옆에서 한동안 생각에 잠겨 있다가, 나는 일어나 선원용 재킷을 벗고, 방 한가운데에 서서 생각했다."], ["I then took off my coat, and thought a little more in my shirt sleeves.", "그런 다음 윗옷을 벗고, 셔츠 바람으로 조금 더 생각했다."], ["But beginning to feel very cold now, half undressed as I was, and remembering what the landlord said about the harpooneer’s not coming home at all that night, it being so very late, I made no more ado, but jumped out of my pantaloons and boots, and then blowing out the light tumbled into bed, and commended myself to the care of heaven.", "그러나 이렇게 반쯤 벗은 채로 있자니 이제 몹시 추워지기 시작했고, 시간이 너무 늦어 그 작살잡이가 그날 밤에는 아예 집에 돌아오지 않을 거라던 여관 주인의 말이 떠올라, 나는 더 이상 망설이지 않고 바지와 장화를 훌훌 벗어 던지고는, 불을 끄고 침대 속으로 굴러들어 가, 하늘의 보살핌에 나를 맡겼다."]], [["Whether that mattress was stuffed with corn-cobs or broken crockery, there is no telling, but I rolled about a good deal, and could not sleep for a long time.", "그 매트리스가 옥수수 속대로 채워졌는지 깨진 사기 조각으로 채워졌는지는 알 길이 없으나, 나는 한참을 이리저리 뒤척이며 오래도록 잠을 이루지 못했다."], ["At last I slid off into a light doze, and had pretty nearly made a good offing towards the land of Nod, when I heard a heavy footfall in the passage, and saw a glimmer of light come into the room from under the door.", "마침내 어렴풋한 선잠 속으로 미끄러져 들어가, 거의 잠의 나라를 향해 좋은 항로를 잡아 가던 참이었는데, 복도에서 묵직한 발소리가 들려왔고, 문틈 아래로 한 줄기 빛이 방 안으로 새어 드는 것이 보였다."]], [["Lord save me, thinks I, that must be the harpooneer, the infernal head-peddler.", "하느님 맙소사, 하고 나는 생각했다. 저건 틀림없이 그 작살잡이, 그 지긋지긋한 머리 행상꾼이로구나."], ["But I lay perfectly still, and resolved not to say a word till spoken to.", "그러나 나는 꼼짝도 하지 않고 가만히 누워, 말을 걸어오기 전에는 한마디도 하지 않으리라 마음먹었다."], ["Holding a light in one hand, and that identical New Zealand head in the other, the stranger entered the room, and without looking towards the bed, placed his candle a good way off from me on the floor in one corner, and then began working away at the knotted cords of the large bag I before spoke of as being in the room.", "한 손에는 불을 들고, 다른 손에는 바로 그 뉴질랜드 머리를 든 채, 그 낯선 자가 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 침대 쪽은 보지도 않고, 촛불을 나에게서 멀찍이 떨어진 한구석 바닥에 내려놓더니, 앞서 이 방에 있다고 말했던 그 커다란 자루의 매듭진 끈을 풀기 시작했다."], ["I was all eagerness to see his face, but he kept it averted for some time while employed in unlacing the bag’s mouth.", "나는 그의 얼굴을 보고 싶어 안달이 났지만, 그는 자루 입구의 끈을 푸는 동안 한참이나 얼굴을 돌리고 있었다."], ["This accomplished, however, he turned round—when, good heavens!", "그러나 마침내 그 일을 끝내고 그가 몸을 돌렸을 때 — 맙소사!"], ["what a sight!", "그 광경이라니!"], ["Such a face!", "그런 얼굴이라니!"], ["It was of a dark, purplish, yellow colour, here and there stuck over with large blackish looking squares.", "그것은 검붉고 누르스름한 빛깔이었고, 군데군데 거뭇거뭇한 커다란 네모꼴들이 박혀 있었다."], ["Yes, it’s just as I thought, he’s a terrible bedfellow; he’s been in a fight, got dreadfully cut, and here he is, just from the surgeon.", "그래, 내 생각이 옳았어, 저자는 끔찍한 잠자리 동무로구나. 싸움질을 하다가 지독하게 베인 채로, 이제 막 의사한테서 돌아온 모양이로구나."], ["But at that moment he chanced to turn his face so towards the light, that I plainly saw they could not be sticking-plasters at all, those black squares on his cheeks.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 그가 우연히 얼굴을 불빛 쪽으로 돌리는 바람에, 나는 그의 뺨에 박힌 그 검은 네모꼴들이 결코 반창고 따위가 아님을 똑똑히 보게 되었다."], ["They were stains of some sort or other.", "그것은 무언가의 얼룩이었다."], ["At first I knew not what to make of this; but soon an inkling of the truth occurred to me.", "처음에는 그것을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 몰랐으나, 곧 진실의 실마리가 떠올랐다."], ["I remembered a story of a white man—a whaleman too—who, falling among the cannibals, had been tattooed by them.", "나는 어느 백인 — 그것도 고래잡이 — 이 식인종들 사이에 떨어져 그들에게 문신을 새겨졌다는 이야기를 떠올렸다."], ["I concluded that this harpooneer, in the course of his distant voyages, must have met with a similar adventure.", "나는 이 작살잡이도 먼 항해를 다니던 중에 비슷한 일을 겪었음에 틀림없다고 결론지었다. 그래, 결국 그게 무슨 대수겠는가, 하고 나는 생각했다!"], ["And what is it, thought I, after all! It’s only his outside; a man can be honest in any sort of skin.", "그건 그저 그자의 겉모습일 뿐, 사람은 어떤 살갗을 지녔든 정직할 수 있는 법이니까."], ["But then, what to make of his unearthly complexion, that part of it, I mean, lying round about, and completely independent of the squares of tattooing.", "그러나 그렇다면, 그의 그 섬뜩한 안색은 또 어떻게 받아들여야 할까. 내 말은, 문신의 네모꼴들과는 완전히 별개로, 그 둘레에 퍼져 있는 그 빛깔 말이다."], ["To be sure, it might be nothing but a good coat of tropical tanning; but I never heard of a hot sun’s tanning a white man into a purplish yellow one.", "물론, 그것이 그저 열대 지방에서 잘 그을린 빛에 지나지 않을 수도 있었다. 그러나 뜨거운 햇볕이 백인을 검붉고 누런색으로 그을렸다는 소리는 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었다."], ["However, I had never been in the South Seas; and perhaps the sun there produced these extraordinary effects upon the skin.", "하기야 나는 남쪽 바다에 가 본 적이 없으니, 어쩌면 그곳의 햇볕은 살갗에 이런 기이한 효과를 내는지도 모를 일이었다."], ["Now, while all these ideas were passing through me like lightning, this harpooneer never noticed me at all.", "한데 이 모든 생각이 번개처럼 내 머릿속을 스쳐 지나가는 동안, 이 작살잡이는 나를 전혀 알아채지 못했다."], ["But, after some difficulty having opened his bag, he commenced fumbling in it, and presently pulled out a sort of tomahawk, and a seal-skin wallet with the hair on.", "그러다 한참 애를 쓴 끝에 자루를 열고는, 그 안을 더듬거리더니, 이윽고 일종의 토마호크 같은 것과, 털이 그대로 붙은 물범 가죽 지갑을 꺼냈다."], ["Placing these on the old chest in the middle of the room, he then took the New Zealand head—a ghastly thing enough—and crammed it down into the bag.", "이것들을 방 한가운데의 낡은 궤짝 위에 올려놓고, 그는 그 뉴질랜드 머리 — 충분히 소름 끼치는 물건이었다 — 를 집어 들어 자루 속으로 쑤셔 넣었다."], ["He now took off his hat—a new beaver hat—when I came nigh singing out with fresh surprise.", "그는 이제 모자 — 새 비버 가죽 모자 — 를 벗었는데, 나는 새삼스러운 놀라움에 하마터면 소리를 지를 뻔했다."], ["There was no hair on his head—none to speak of at least—nothing but a small scalp-knot twisted up on his forehead.", "그의 머리에는 머리카락이 없었다 — 적어도 머리카락이라고 할 만한 것은 없었다 — 그저 이마 위로 비비 꼬아 올린 자그마한 정수리 매듭 하나뿐이었다."], ["His bald purplish head now looked for all the world like a mildewed skull.", "그 벗어진 검붉은 머리통은 이제 영락없이 곰팡이 핀 해골처럼 보였다."], ["Had not the stranger stood between me and the door, I would have bolted out of it quicker than ever I bolted a dinner.", "그 낯선 자가 나와 문 사이를 가로막고 서 있지만 않았더라면, 나는 일찍이 저녁을 후딱 해치우던 것보다도 더 빨리 그 문으로 줄행랑을 쳤을 것이다."]], [["Even as it was, I thought something of slipping out of the window, but it was the second floor back.", "설령 그렇다 해도, 나는 창문으로 빠져나갈까도 잠시 생각했지만, 그곳은 뒤쪽 이층이었다."], ["I am no coward, but what to make of this head-peddling purple rascal altogether passed my comprehension.", "나는 겁쟁이가 아니지만, 이 머리 행상하는 검붉은 악당을 통째로 어떻게 받아들여야 할지는 도무지 내 이해의 범위를 벗어나는 일이었다."], ["Ignorance is the parent of fear, and being completely nonplussed and confounded about the stranger, I confess I was now as much afraid of him as if it was the devil himself who had thus broken into my room at the dead of night.", "무지는 두려움의 어버이이니, 이 낯선 자에 대해 완전히 어찌할 바를 모르고 혼란에 빠진 나는, 솔직히 말해 이제 그자를, 마치 악마 그 자체가 이렇게 한밤중에 내 방에 쳐들어오기라도 한 양 두려워하고 있었다."], ["In fact, I was so afraid of him that I was not game enough just then to address him, and demand a satisfactory answer concerning what seemed inexplicable in him.", "사실 나는 그자가 어찌나 무서웠던지, 그 순간에는 그에게 말을 걸어, 그에게서 도무지 알 수 없게만 여겨지던 것에 관해 만족스러운 답을 요구할 만한 배짱조차 없었다."]], [["Meanwhile, he continued the business of undressing, and at last showed his chest and arms.", "그러는 사이, 그는 계속 옷을 벗어 갔고, 마침내 가슴과 팔을 드러냈다."], ["As I live, these covered parts of him were checkered with the same squares as his face; his back, too, was all over the same dark squares; he seemed to have been in a Thirty Years’ War, and just escaped from it with a sticking-plaster shirt.", "내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 옷에 가려져 있던 그 부분들도 얼굴과 똑같은 네모꼴들로 바둑판처럼 뒤덮여 있었다. 등도 마찬가지로 온통 그 똑같은 거뭇한 네모꼴들투성이였다."], ["Still more, his very legs were marked, as if a parcel of dark green frogs were running up the trunks of young palms.", "그는 마치 삼십 년 전쟁이라도 치르고는, 반창고로 만든 셔츠만 걸친 채 거기서 막 빠져나온 사람 같았다."], ["It was now quite plain that he must be some abominable savage or other shipped aboard of a whaleman in the South Seas, and so landed in this Christian country.", "게다가, 다리마저도 표시가 새겨져 있었으니, 마치 짙푸른 개구리 한 무리가 어린 야자나무 줄기를 타고 기어오르는 것 같았다. 이제 그가 남쪽 바다에서 어느 고래잡이배에 실려 와, 이렇게 이 기독교 나라에 내려진, 어떤 가증스러운 야만인임에 틀림없다는 것이 아주 명백해졌다."], ["I quaked to think of it.", "나는 그 생각에 몸을 떨었다."], ["A peddler of heads too—perhaps the heads of his own brothers.", "게다가 머리 행상꾼이라니 — 어쩌면 제 형제들의 머리일지도 모르는 것을."], ["He might take a fancy to mine—heavens!", "그자가 내 머리에 욕심을 낼 수도 있는 일 아닌가 — 맙소사!"], ["look at that tomahawk!", "저 토마호크 좀 보라지!"]], [["But there was no time for shuddering, for now the savage went about something that completely fascinated my attention, and convinced me that he must indeed be a heathen.", "그러나 몸서리칠 겨를도 없었으니, 이제 그 야만인이 내 주의를 완전히 사로잡고, 그가 정녕 이교도임에 틀림없음을 내게 확신시켜 준 무언가에 착수했기 때문이다."], ["Going to his heavy grego, or wrapall, or dreadnaught, which he had previously hung on a chair, he fumbled in the pockets, and produced at length a curious little deformed image with a hunch on its back, and exactly the colour of a three days’ old Congo baby.", "그는 앞서 의자에 걸어 두었던 묵직한 그레고, 곧 외투, 또는 두꺼운 방한복으로 다가가 주머니를 뒤지더니, 이윽고 등에 혹이 달리고 빛깔은 영락없이 사흘 된 콩고 아기 같은, 기이하고 자그마한 일그러진 형상 하나를 꺼냈다."], ["Remembering the embalmed head, at first I almost thought that this black manikin was a real baby preserved in some similar manner.", "방부 처리된 머리가 떠올라, 처음에는 이 검은 인형이 비슷한 방식으로 보존된 진짜 아기가 아닐까 하는 생각마저 들 뻔했다."], ["But seeing that it was not at all limber, and that it glistened a good deal like polished ebony, I concluded that it must be nothing but a wooden idol, which indeed it proved to be.", "그러나 그것이 전혀 물렁거리지 않고, 광을 낸 흑단처럼 꽤나 반들거리는 것을 보고는, 그저 나무로 만든 우상에 지나지 않으리라고 결론지었는데, 실제로 그것은 그러했다."], ["For now the savage goes up to the empty fire-place, and removing the papered fire-board, sets up this little hunch-backed image, like a tenpin, between the andirons.", "이제 그 야만인이 빈 벽난로로 다가가, 벽지 바른 난로 가림판을 치우더니, 이 자그마한 곱사등이 형상을 볼링 핀처럼 장작 받침쇠 사이에 세워 놓았다."], ["The chimney jambs and all the bricks inside were very sooty, so that I thought this fire-place made a very appropriate little shrine or chapel for his Congo idol.", "굴뚝 옆벽과 그 안의 벽돌들이 죄다 그을음투성이여서, 나는 이 벽난로가 그의 콩고 우상을 모실 아주 알맞은 자그마한 사당, 곧 예배소가 되겠구나 하고 생각했다."]], [["I now screwed my eyes hard towards the half hidden image, feeling but ill at ease meantime—to see what was next to follow.", "나는 이제 반쯤 가려진 그 형상을 향해 눈을 잔뜩 모아 떴는데, 그러는 내내 영 불안한 심정으로 — 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어질지 지켜보았다."], ["First he takes about a double handful of shavings out of his grego pocket, and places them carefully before the idol; then laying a bit of ship biscuit on top and applying the flame from the lamp, he kindled the shavings into a sacrificial blaze.", "먼저 그는 그레고 주머니에서 대팻밥을 두 줌가량 꺼내어 우상 앞에 조심스레 놓았다. 그런 다음 그 위에 배에서 쓰는 비스킷 한 조각을 얹고, 램프의 불꽃을 갖다 대어 대팻밥을 제물의 불길로 지폈다."], ["Presently, after many hasty snatches into the fire, and still hastier withdrawals of his fingers (whereby he seemed to be scorching them badly), he at last succeeded in drawing out the biscuit; then blowing off the heat and ashes a little, he made a polite offer of it to the little negro.", "이윽고 황급히 불 속으로 손을 여러 번 집어넣었다가 그보다 더 황급히 손가락을 빼내기를 거듭한 끝에 (그 바람에 손가락을 심하게 데이는 듯했다), 그는 마침내 그 비스킷을 꺼내는 데 성공했다. 그러고는 열기와 재를 조금 불어 낸 다음, 그것을 그 자그마한 흑인에게 정중히 권했다."], ["But the little devil did not seem to fancy such dry sort of fare at all; he never moved his lips.", "그러나 그 자그마한 악마는 그런 메마른 음식 따위는 도무지 마음에 들지 않는 모양이었으니, 입술 하나 까딱하지 않았다."], ["All these strange antics were accompanied by still stranger guttural noises from the devotee, who seemed to be praying in a sing-song or else singing some pagan psalmody or other, during which his face twitched about in the most unnatural manner.", "이 모든 기이한 행동에는, 그 신자가 내는, 더욱더 기이한 목구멍 소리가 곁들여졌는데, 그는 가락을 붙여 기도를 하거나, 아니면 무슨 이교의 찬송가를 부르고 있는 듯했고, 그러는 동안 그의 얼굴은 더없이 부자연스러운 모양으로 씰룩거렸다."], ["At last extinguishing the fire, he took the idol up very unceremoniously, and bagged it again in his grego pocket as carelessly as if he were a sportsman bagging a dead woodcock.", "마침내 그는 불을 끄고는, 그 우상을 아주 무람없이 집어 들어, 마치 사냥꾼이 죽은 멧도요를 자루에 챙기듯 무심하게 다시 제 그레고 주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다."]], [["All these queer proceedings increased my uncomfortableness, and seeing him now exhibiting strong symptoms of concluding his business operations, and jumping into bed with me, I thought it was high time, now or never, before the light was put out, to break the spell in which I had so long been bound.", "이 모든 괴이한 행위는 나의 불편함을 더욱 키웠고, 이제 그가 일을 마무리 짓고 나와 함께 침대에 뛰어들 강한 기미를 보이는 것을 알아채자, 나는 불이 꺼지기 전에 지금이야말로, 이때가 아니면 영영 기회가 없으리라 여기고, 그토록 오래 나를 옭아매고 있던 그 주술을 깨야겠다고 생각했다."]], [["But the interval I spent in deliberating what to say, was a fatal one.", "그러나 무슨 말을 해야 할지 궁리하느라 보낸 그 짬은 치명적인 것이었다."], ["Taking up his tomahawk from the table, he examined the head of it for an instant, and then holding it to the light, with his mouth at the handle, he puffed out great clouds of tobacco smoke.", "그는 탁자에서 제 토마호크를 집어 들어, 잠시 그 머리 부분을 살펴보더니, 불빛에 비춰 든 채 자루에 입을 대고 담배 연기를 뭉게뭉게 뿜어 댔다."], ["The next moment the light was extinguished, and this wild cannibal, tomahawk between his teeth, sprang into bed with me.", "다음 순간 불이 꺼졌고, 이 사나운 식인종은, 토마호크를 이 사이에 문 채, 나와 함께 침대로 뛰어들었다."], ["I sang out, I could not help it now; and giving a sudden grunt of astonishment he began feeling me.", "나는 비명을 질렀다, 이제는 도저히 어쩔 수가 없었다. 그러자 그는 화들짝 놀라 켁 하는 소리를 내더니 나를 더듬기 시작했다."]], [["Stammering out something, I knew not what, I rolled away from him against the wall, and then conjured him, whoever or whatever he might be, to keep quiet, and let me get up and light the lamp again.", "나는 무슨 말인지도 모를 말을 더듬더듬 내뱉으며, 그에게서 굴러 벽 쪽으로 몸을 피했다. 그러고는 그가 누구든 무엇이든, 제발 조용히 좀 있어 달라고, 그래서 내가 일어나 다시 램프에 불을 켤 수 있게 해 달라고 그에게 애원했다."], ["But his guttural responses satisfied me at once that he but ill comprehended my meaning.", "그러나 그의 목구멍에서 나오는 대꾸는, 그가 내 말뜻을 거의 알아듣지 못한다는 것을 단번에 내게 확인시켜 주었다."]], [["“Who-e debel you?”—he at last said—“you no speak-e, dam-me, I kill-e.”", "\"누구냐, 이 악마 같은 놈?\" 그가 마침내 입을 열었다."], ["And so saying the lighted tomahawk began flourishing about me in the dark.", "\"말 안 해, 빌어먹을, 죽여 버린다.\" 이렇게 말하며 그 불붙은 토마호크가 어둠 속에서 내 주위로 마구 휘둘러지기 시작했다."]], [["“Landlord, for God’s sake, Peter Coffin!”", "\"주인장, 제발, 피터 코핀!\" 나는 소리쳤다."], ["shouted I.", "\"주인장!"], ["“Landlord!", "경비! 코핀!"], ["Watch! Coffin!", "천사들이여!"], ["Angels! save me!”", "날 좀 살려 줘요!\""]], [["“Speak-e!", "\"말해!"], ["tell-ee me who-ee be, or dam-me, I kill-e!”", "누군지 말하란 말이야, 안 그러면, 빌어먹을, 죽여 버린다!\""], ["again growled the cannibal, while his horrid flourishings of the tomahawk scattered the hot tobacco ashes about me till I thought my linen would get on fire.", "식인종이 다시 으르렁거렸다. 그러는 동안 그가 토마호크를 끔찍하게 휘둘러 대는 통에 뜨거운 담뱃재가 내 주위로 흩날렸고, 나는 내 속옷에 불이 붙는 줄로만 알았다."], ["But thank heaven, at that moment the landlord came into the room light in hand, and leaping from the bed I ran up to him.", "그러나 고맙게도 바로 그 순간 여관 주인이 손에 불을 들고 방 안으로 들어왔고, 나는 침대에서 펄쩍 뛰어내려 그에게로 달려갔다."]], [["“Don’t be afraid now,”", "\"이제 겁낼 거 없소,\""], ["said he, grinning again, “Queequeg here wouldn’t harm a hair of your head.”", "그가 또다시 히죽거리며 말했다. \"여기 이 퀴케그(Queequeg)는 당신 머리카락 한 올도 해치지 않을 거요.\""]], [["“Stop your grinning,”", "\"그 히죽거리는 것 좀 그만두쇼,\""], ["shouted I, “and why didn’t you tell me that that infernal harpooneer was a cannibal?”", "나는 소리쳤다. \"그리고 그 지긋지긋한 작살잡이가 식인종이라는 걸 왜 진작 말해 주지 않았소?\""]], [["“I thought ye know’d it;—didn’t I tell ye, he was a peddlin’ heads around town?—but turn flukes again and go to sleep.", "\"당신이 아는 줄 알았지 — 내가 말하지 않았소, 그자가 시내를 돌며 머리를 행상하고 다닌다고? — 자, 그러니 다시 꼬리를 틀고 자리에 들어 잠이나 자쇼."], ["Queequeg, look here—you sabbee me, I sabbee—you this man sleepe you—you sabbee?”", "퀴케그, 이리 보게 — 자네 내 말 알아듣지(sabbee), 나도 알아 — 자네, 이 사람, 자네하고 잔다(sleepe), 알겠나(sabbee)?\""]], [["“Me sabbee plenty”—grunted Queequeg, puffing away at his pipe and sitting up in bed.", "\"나 많이 알아들어(sabbee)\" 하고 퀴케그가 침대에서 일어나 앉아 제 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 으르렁거렸다."]], [["“You gettee in,”", "\"자네 들어와,\""], ["he added, motioning to me with his tomahawk, and throwing the clothes to one side.", "그가 토마호크로 나에게 손짓하고 옷가지들을 한쪽으로 치우며 덧붙였다."], ["He really did this in not only a civil but a really kind and charitable way.", "그는 정말로 이 일을 정중할 뿐 아니라 참으로 친절하고 자비로운 태도로 행했다."], ["I stood looking at him a moment.", "나는 잠시 그를 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["For all his tattooings he was on the whole a clean, comely looking cannibal.", "그 온갖 문신에도 불구하고, 그는 전체적으로 깨끗하고 보기 좋은 식인종이었다."], ["What’s all this fuss I have been making about, thought I to myself—the man’s a human being just as I am: he has just as much reason to fear me, as I have to be afraid of him.", "내가 그동안 대체 무엇 때문에 이 야단법석을 떨었던가, 하고 나는 혼자 생각했다 — 이 사람도 나와 똑같은 인간 아닌가. 그가 나를 두려워할 까닭이나, 내가 그를 두려워할 까닭이나 매한가지이지 않은가."], ["Better sleep with a sober cannibal than a drunken Christian.", "술 취한 기독교인하고 자느니 술 안 마신 식인종하고 자는 편이 낫지."]], [["“Landlord,”", "\"주인장,\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “tell him to stash his tomahawk there, or pipe, or whatever you call it; tell him to stop smoking, in short, and I will turn in with him.", "\"그자한테 그 토마호크인지, 파이프인지, 뭐라고 부르든 간에 그걸 좀 치우라고 하시오. 한마디로 담배 좀 그만 피우라고 말이오."], ["But I don’t fancy having a man smoking in bed with me.", "그러면 그자와 함께 자리에 들겠소. 하지만 나는 나와 함께 침대에서 담배 피우는 사람은 질색이오."], ["It’s dangerous.", "그건 위험하다고."], ["Besides, I ain’t insured.”", "게다가 나는 보험도 안 들었거든.\""]], [["This being told to Queequeg, he at once complied, and again politely motioned me to get into bed—rolling over to one side as much as to say—“I won’t touch a leg of ye.”", "이 말이 퀴케그에게 전해지자, 그는 즉시 따랐고, 다시 한번 정중하게 내게 침대로 들어오라는 손짓을 했다 — 한쪽으로 잔뜩 굴러가서는, 마치 \"자네 다리 하나 건드리지 않을게\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯이."]], [["“Good night, landlord,”", "\"잘 자시오, 주인장,\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “you may go.”", "\"이제 가 봐도 되오.\""]], [["I turned in, and never slept better in my life.", "나는 자리에 들었고, 내 평생 그날 밤보다 더 잘 잔 적이 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 4. The Counterpane.", "# CHAPTER 4. The Counterpane."]], [["Upon waking next morning about daylight, I found Queequeg’s arm thrown over me in the most loving and affectionate manner.", "이튿날 동틀 무렵 잠에서 깨어 보니, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 팔이 더없이 다정하고 애정 어린 모양새로 내 몸을 감싸고 있었다. 누가 보았다면 내가 그의 아내라도 되는 줄 알았으리라."], ["You had almost thought I had been his wife.", "침대 덮개는 색색의 작고 기묘한 네모와 세모 조각들이 가득한 조각보였다. 그리고 그의 이 팔은 끝없이 이어지는 크레타의 미궁 같은 무늬가 온통 새겨져 있었는데, 그 어느 두 부분도 정확히 같은 빛깔이 아니었다."], ["The counterpane was of patchwork, full of odd little parti-coloured squares and triangles; and this arm of his tattooed all over with an interminable Cretan labyrinth of a figure, no two parts of which were of one precise shade—owing I suppose to his keeping his arm at sea unmethodically in sun and shade, his shirt sleeves irregularly rolled up at various times—this same arm of his, I say, looked for all the world like a strip of that same patchwork quilt.", "짐작건대 바다에서 그가 햇볕과 그늘을 가리지 않고 멋대로 팔을 드러내 두었던 데다, 셔츠 소매를 그때그때 제멋대로 걷어 올렸던 탓이리라. 어쨌든 이 똑같은 팔이 영락없이 저 조각보 이불의 한 자락처럼 보였던 것이다."], ["Indeed, partly lying on it as the arm did when I first awoke, I could hardly tell it from the quilt, they so blended their hues together; and it was only by the sense of weight and pressure that I could tell that Queequeg was hugging me.", "사실 처음 잠에서 깼을 때 그의 팔이 이불 위에 절반쯤 걸쳐져 있는 모양이, 그 빛깔이 너무도 자연스레 어우러진 나머지 나는 그것을 이불과 거의 구별할 수 없을 지경이었다. 다만 그 무게와 짓누르는 감촉으로써만 나는 퀴퀘그가 나를 끌어안고 있음을 알 수 있었다."]], [["My sensations were strange.", "내 느낌은 기묘했다."], ["Let me try to explain them.", "그것을 어떻게든 설명해 보겠다."], ["When I was a child, I well remember a somewhat similar circumstance that befell me; whether it was a reality or a dream, I never could entirely settle.", "어린 시절, 나는 이와 다소 비슷한 일을 겪은 기억이 또렷이 남아 있다. 그것이 실제였는지 꿈이었는지는 끝내 온전히 가려내지 못했다."], ["The circumstance was this.", "사정은 이러했다. 나는 무슨 장난이든 한바탕 벌이고 있었는데 — 며칠 전에 어린 굴뚝 청소부가 하는 것을 본 터라, 아마 굴뚝을 기어오르려 했던 것 같다."], ["I had been cutting up some caper or other—I think it was trying to crawl up the chimney, as I had seen a little sweep do a few days previous; and my stepmother who, somehow or other, was all the time whipping me, or sending me to bed supperless,—my mother dragged me by the legs out of the chimney and packed me off to bed, though it was only two o’clock in the afternoon of the 21st June, the longest day in the year in our hemisphere.", "그러자 무슨 까닭에선지 늘 나를 매질하거나 저녁도 안 주고 잠자리로 내쫓던 새어머니가, 내 다리를 붙잡고 굴뚝에서 끌어내려 침대로 처박아 버렸다. 그때는 6월 21일 오후 두 시밖에 안 된 시각이었으니, 우리 반구(半球)에서 일 년 중 해가 가장 긴 날이었다."], ["I felt dreadfully.", "나는 끔찍한 기분이었다."], ["But there was no help for it, so up stairs I went to my little room in the third floor, undressed myself as slowly as possible so as to kill time, and with a bitter sigh got between the sheets.", "그러나 어쩔 도리가 없었기에, 나는 삼층의 내 작은 방으로 올라가, 시간을 죽이려고 되도록 천천히 옷을 벗고는, 쓰디쓴 한숨과 함께 이불 속으로 기어들었다."]], [["I lay there dismally calculating that sixteen entire hours must elapse before I could hope for a resurrection.", "나는 그곳에 누운 채, 부활을 바랄 수 있기까지 꼬박 열여섯 시간이 흘러야 한다는 것을 음울하게 헤아렸다."], ["Sixteen hours in bed!", "침대에서 열여섯 시간이라니!"], ["the small of my back ached to think of it.", "그 생각만으로도 등허리가 쑤셔 왔다."], ["And it was so light too; the sun shining in at the window, and a great rattling of coaches in the streets, and the sound of gay voices all over the house.", "게다가 바깥은 어찌나 환한지, 창으로 햇살이 비쳐 들고, 거리에서는 마차들이 요란하게 덜컹거렸으며, 집 안 곳곳에서 흥겨운 목소리들이 울려 퍼졌다. 나는 점점 더 견딜 수 없어졌다."], ["I felt worse and worse—at last I got up, dressed, and softly going down in my stockinged feet, sought out my stepmother, and suddenly threw myself at her feet, beseeching her as a particular favour to give me a good slippering for my misbehaviour; anything indeed but condemning me to lie abed such an unendurable length of time.", "마침내 나는 일어나 옷을 입고, 양말만 신은 발로 살금살금 아래층으로 내려가 새어머니를 찾아냈다. 그러고는 느닷없이 그분의 발치에 몸을 던지며, 내 잘못을 슬리퍼로 호되게 때려 주십사 특별한 은혜로 간청했다 — 그토록 견딜 수 없이 긴 시간을 침대에 누워 있으라는 형벌만 아니라면 무엇이든 좋다고."], ["But she was the best and most conscientious of stepmothers, and back I had to go to my room.", "그러나 그분은 더없이 훌륭하고 양심적인 새어머니였기에, 나는 도로 내 방으로 돌아가야만 했다."], ["For several hours I lay there broad awake, feeling a great deal worse than I have ever done since, even from the greatest subsequent misfortunes.", "나는 몇 시간이고 말똥말똥 깬 채 누워 있었는데, 그 뒤로 겪은 가장 큰 불행들에 견주어도 그때만큼 비참했던 적은 없었다. 마침내 나는 어수선한 악몽 같은 선잠에 빠져들었던 모양이다."], ["At last I must have fallen into a troubled nightmare of a doze; and slowly waking from it—half steeped in dreams—I opened my eyes, and the before sun-lit room was now wrapped in outer darkness.", "그리고 그 잠에서 천천히 깨어나며 — 꿈에 반쯤 잠긴 채로 — 눈을 떠 보니, 조금 전까지 햇살이 가득하던 방이 이제는 바깥의 어둠에 휩싸여 있었다. 순간 나는 온몸을 관통하는 충격을 느꼈다."], ["Instantly I felt a shock running through all my frame; nothing was to be seen, and nothing was to be heard; but a supernatural hand seemed placed in mine.", "아무것도 보이지 않고, 아무것도 들리지 않았다. 그런데 초자연적인 손 하나가 내 손 안에 놓여 있는 것만 같았다."], ["My arm hung over the counterpane, and the nameless, unimaginable, silent form or phantom, to which the hand belonged, seemed closely seated by my bed-side.", "내 팔은 침대 덮개 너머로 늘어져 있었고, 그 손의 주인인 이름 없고 상상조차 할 수 없는 고요한 형체 혹은 환영이 내 침대 곁에 바짝 다가앉아 있는 듯했다."], ["For what seemed ages piled on ages, I lay there, frozen with the most awful fears, not daring to drag away my hand; yet ever thinking that if I could but stir it one single inch, the horrid spell would be broken.", "헤아릴 수 없는 세월이 켜켜이 쌓인 듯한 시간 동안, 나는 더없이 끔찍한 공포에 얼어붙어 그곳에 누운 채, 감히 내 손을 빼낼 엄두도 내지 못했다. 그러면서도 단 한 치라도 손을 움직일 수만 있다면 그 무시무시한 주술이 풀리리라는 생각만은 줄곧 했다."], ["I knew not how this consciousness at last glided away from me; but waking in the morning, I shudderingly remembered it all, and for days and weeks and months afterwards I lost myself in confounding attempts to explain the mystery.", "이 의식이 마침내 어떻게 나에게서 스르르 사라져 갔는지는 알지 못한다. 그러나 아침에 깨어나 그 모든 일을 몸서리치며 떠올렸고, 그 뒤로 며칠이고 몇 주고 몇 달이고, 나는 그 수수께끼를 풀어 보려는 갈피 잡을 수 없는 시도 속에서 헤맸다."], ["Nay, to this very hour, I often puzzle myself with it.", "아니, 바로 이 순간까지도 나는 종종 그 일로 골머리를 앓는다."]], [["Now, take away the awful fear, and my sensations at feeling the supernatural hand in mine were very similar, in their strangeness, to those which I experienced on waking up and seeing Queequeg’s pagan arm thrown round me.", "자, 그 끔찍한 공포만 걷어 낸다면, 초자연적인 손이 내 손 안에 있음을 느꼈을 때의 그 감각은, 그 기묘함에 있어 잠에서 깨어나 퀴퀘그의 이교도 팔이 나를 휘감고 있는 것을 보았을 때 겪은 감각과 매우 흡사했다. 그러나 마침내 지난밤의 모든 일들이 하나하나 차분하게, 확고한 현실로 되살아났고, 그제야 나는 이 우스꽝스러운 곤경만을 또렷이 의식하게 되었다."], ["But at length all the past night’s events soberly recurred, one by one, in fixed reality, and then I lay only alive to the comical predicament.", "그의 팔을 치워 보려고 — 그 신랑 같은 포옹을 풀어 보려고 — 애써 보았으나, 잠들어 있으면서도 그는 마치 죽음 말고는 우리 둘을 갈라놓을 것이 없다는 듯 나를 꼭 끌어안고 있었다. 나는 이제 그를 깨워 보려 애썼다."], ["For though I tried to move his arm—unlock his bridegroom clasp—yet, sleeping as he was, he still hugged me tightly, as though naught but death should part us twain.", "\"퀴퀘그!\" 그러나 그의 대답이라곤 코 고는 소리뿐이었다."], ["I now strove to rouse him—“Queequeg!”—but his only answer was a snore.", "그래서 나는 몸을 돌렸는데, 목이 마치 말의 멍에에라도 끼인 것 같았다. 그러다 문득 살짝 긁히는 느낌이 들었다."], ["I then rolled over, my neck feeling as if it were in a horse-collar; and suddenly felt a slight scratch.", "침대 덮개를 젖혀 보니, 미개인의 옆구리에 토마호크가 잠들어 있었다. 마치 도끼 얼굴을 한 아기라도 되는 듯이."], ["Throwing aside the counterpane, there lay the tomahawk sleeping by the savage’s side, as if it were a hatchet-faced baby.", "참으로 한심한 꼴이로구나, 하고 나는 생각했다. 대낮에 낯선 집 침대에 누워, 식인종과 토마호크를 곁에 두고 있다니!"], ["A pretty pickle, truly, thought I; abed here in a strange house in the broad day, with a cannibal and a tomahawk!", "\"퀴퀘그! — 제발 좀, 퀴퀘그, 일어나라고!\""], ["“Queequeg!—in the name of goodness, Queequeg, wake!”", "마침내 나는 한참을 버둥거리고, 사내끼리 그런 부부 같은 꼴로 끌어안는 것이 얼마나 꼴사나운 짓인지 크고 끊임없이 항의한 끝에, 가까스로 그에게서 끙 하는 소리를 끌어냈다. 이윽고 그는 팔을 거두어들이더니, 물에서 막 나온 뉴펀들랜드 개처럼 온몸을 부르르 떨고는, 침대에 꼿꼿이 앉았다."], ["At length, by dint of much wriggling, and loud and incessant expostulations upon the unbecomingness of his hugging a fellow male in that matrimonial sort of style, I succeeded in extracting a grunt; and presently, he drew back his arm, shook himself all over like a Newfoundland dog just from the water, and sat up in bed, stiff as a pike-staff, looking at me, and rubbing his eyes as if he did not altogether remember how I came to be there, though a dim consciousness of knowing something about me seemed slowly dawning over him.", "작살자루처럼 빳빳하게 앉아 나를 바라보며, 내가 어쩌다 거기 있게 되었는지 도무지 기억나지 않는다는 듯 두 눈을 비벼 댔다. 그러면서도 나에 관한 무언가를 알고 있다는 어렴풋한 의식이 그에게 서서히 동터 오는 듯했다."], ["Meanwhile, I lay quietly eyeing him, having no serious misgivings now, and bent upon narrowly observing so curious a creature.", "그동안 나는 조용히 누운 채 그를 살펴보았다. 이제는 별다른 불안도 없었기에, 이토록 기묘한 피조물을 찬찬히 관찰하는 데 온 마음을 기울였다."], ["When, at last, his mind seemed made up touching the character of his bedfellow, and he became, as it were, reconciled to the fact; he jumped out upon the floor, and by certain signs and sounds gave me to understand that, if it pleased me, he would dress first and then leave me to dress afterwards, leaving the whole apartment to myself.", "마침내 그가 제 잠자리 동무의 정체에 관해 마음을 정한 듯, 말하자면 그 사실과 화해하게 되었을 때, 그는 바닥으로 풀쩍 뛰어내리더니 어떤 몸짓과 소리로 내게 이렇게 알렸다. 내가 괜찮다면 자기가 먼저 옷을 입고 나가서 방을 통째로 내게 내어 줄 테니, 나는 그다음에 옷을 입으라는 것이었다."], ["Thinks I, Queequeg, under the circumstances, this is a very civilized overture; but, the truth is, these savages have an innate sense of delicacy, say what you will; it is marvellous how essentially polite they are.", "이런 상황에서 보니, 퀴퀘그, 이거야말로 참으로 점잖은 제안이로구나, 하고 나는 생각했다. 하지만 사실 이 미개인들에게는, 뭐라고들 하든 간에, 타고난 섬세함이 있다."], ["I pay this particular compliment to Queequeg, because he treated me with so much civility and consideration, while I was guilty of great rudeness; staring at him from the bed, and watching all his toilette motions; for the time my curiosity getting the better of my breeding.", "그들이 본질적으로 얼마나 예의 바른지는 참으로 놀라울 정도다. 내가 퀴퀘그에게 이런 각별한 찬사를 보내는 것은, 내가 큰 무례를 저지르고 있는 와중에도 그는 나를 그토록 정중하고 사려 깊게 대해 주었기 때문이다."], ["Nevertheless, a man like Queequeg you don’t see every day, he and his ways were well worth unusual regarding.", "나는 침대에서 그를 빤히 쳐다보며 그의 몸단장하는 동작을 일일이 지켜보고 있었으니, 그동안만큼은 호기심이 예의를 이겨 버린 셈이었다. 그렇기는 해도, 퀴퀘그 같은 사내는 날마다 볼 수 있는 것이 아니었으니, 그와 그의 행동거지는 유난스레 눈여겨볼 만한 가치가 충분했다."]], [["He commenced dressing at top by donning his beaver hat, a very tall one, by the by, and then—still minus his trowsers—he hunted up his boots.", "그는 머리부터 옷을 입기 시작했는데, 우선 비버 가죽 모자를 썼다. 그건 그렇고, 그것은 아주 높다란 모자였다."], ["What under the heavens he did it for, I cannot tell, but his next movement was to crush himself—boots in hand, and hat on—under the bed; when, from sundry violent gaspings and strainings, I inferred he was hard at work booting himself; though by no law of propriety that I ever heard of, is any man required to be private when putting on his boots.", "그러고는 — 여전히 바지는 입지 않은 채로 — 자기 장화를 찾아냈다. 도대체 무슨 영문으로 그랬는지는 알 수 없으나, 그의 다음 동작은 — 장화를 손에 들고 모자를 쓴 채로 — 침대 밑으로 제 몸을 욱여넣는 것이었다."], ["But Queequeg, do you see, was a creature in the transition stage—neither caterpillar nor butterfly.", "그러고는 격렬하게 헐떡이고 끙끙대는 소리로 미루어, 나는 그가 장화를 신느라 안간힘을 쓰고 있음을 짐작했다. 하지만 내가 일찍이 들어 본 그 어떤 예법으로도, 장화를 신을 때 남의 눈을 피해야 한다고 정한 법은 없다."], ["He was just enough civilized to show off his outlandishness in the strangest possible manners.", "그러나 보다시피 퀴퀘그는 과도기 단계의 피조물이었다 — 애벌레도 아니고 나비도 아닌. 그는 더없이 기묘한 방식으로 제 야성을 드러낼 만큼만 딱 적당히 문명화되어 있었다."], ["His education was not yet completed.", "그의 교육은 아직 완성되지 않았다."], ["He was an undergraduate.", "그는 학부생이었던 셈이다."], ["If he had not been a small degree civilized, he very probably would not have troubled himself with boots at all; but then, if he had not been still a savage, he never would have dreamt of getting under the bed to put them on.", "만약 그가 조금이라도 문명화되지 않았더라면, 그는 십중팔구 장화 따위로 골머리를 앓지도 않았을 것이다. 하지만 또 그가 여전히 미개인이 아니었더라면, 그것을 신겠다고 침대 밑으로 기어들 생각은 결코 꿈에도 하지 않았으리라."], ["At last, he emerged with his hat very much dented and crushed down over his eyes, and began creaking and limping about the room, as if, not being much accustomed to boots, his pair of damp, wrinkled cowhide ones—probably not made to order either—rather pinched and tormented him at the first go off of a bitter cold morning.", "마침내 그는 모자가 잔뜩 찌그러진 채 눈까지 푹 눌러쓴 모습으로 기어 나오더니, 삐걱대고 절뚝거리며 방 안을 돌아다니기 시작했다. 장화에 그리 익숙하지 않은 데다, 축축하고 주름진 그의 쇠가죽 장화 한 켤레가 — 아마 맞춤으로 지은 것도 아니었으리라 — 살을 에는 추운 아침의 첫걸음부터 어지간히 그를 죄어 들고 괴롭히는 모양이었다."]], [["Seeing, now, that there were no curtains to the window, and that the street being very narrow, the house opposite commanded a plain view into the room, and observing more and more the indecorous figure that Queequeg made, staving about with little else but his hat and boots on; I begged him as well as I could, to accelerate his toilet somewhat, and particularly to get into his pantaloons as soon as possible.", "이제 보니 창에는 커튼이 없었고, 거리가 몹시 좁은 탓에 맞은편 집에서 방 안이 훤히 들여다보였다. 게다가 퀴퀘그가 모자와 장화 말고는 거의 아무것도 걸치지 않은 채 쿵쾅거리며 돌아다니는 꼴이 점점 더 꼴사나워 보이기에, 나는 되도록 좋은 말로 그에게 몸단장을 좀 서둘러 달라고, 특히 바지부터 한시바삐 입어 달라고 청했다."], ["He complied, and then proceeded to wash himself.", "그는 순순히 따랐고, 그런 다음 세수를 하기 시작했다."], ["At that time in the morning any Christian would have washed his face; but Queequeg, to my amazement, contented himself with restricting his ablutions to his chest, arms, and hands.", "그 시각이면 어떤 기독교도라도 얼굴을 씻었으련만, 놀랍게도 퀴퀘그는 가슴과 팔과 손을 씻는 것으로 제 세정을 끝내는 데 만족했다."], ["He then donned his waistcoat, and taking up a piece of hard soap on the wash-stand centre table, dipped it into water and commenced lathering his face.", "그러고는 조끼를 걸치더니, 세면대 가운데 탁자에 놓인 굳은 비누 한 조각을 집어 들어 물에 적시고는 얼굴에 거품을 내기 시작했다."], ["I was watching to see where he kept his razor, when lo and behold, he takes the harpoon from the bed corner, slips out the long wooden stock, unsheathes the head, whets it a little on his boot, and striding up to the bit of mirror against the wall, begins a vigorous scraping, or rather harpooning of his cheeks.", "나는 그가 면도칼을 어디에 두는지 보려고 지켜보고 있었는데, 그런데 이게 웬일인가, 그는 침대 모서리에서 작살을 집어 들더니, 긴 나무 자루를 빼내고 날의 칼집을 벗긴 뒤, 장화에 슬쩍 갈고는, 벽에 걸린 작은 거울 앞으로 성큼성큼 다가가, 제 두 뺨을 힘차게 긁어 대기 시작했다. 아니, 작살로 면도를 한다고나 할까."], ["Thinks I, Queequeg, this is using Rogers’s best cutlery with a vengeance.", "퀴퀘그, 이건 로저스의 최고급 칼붙이를 작정하고 함부로 쓰는 격이로구나, 하고 나는 생각했다."], ["Afterwards I wondered the less at this operation when I came to know of what fine steel the head of a harpoon is made, and how exceedingly sharp the long straight edges are always kept.", "나중에 작살의 날이 얼마나 좋은 강철로 만들어지는지, 그리고 그 길고 곧은 날이 늘 얼마나 지독히도 날카롭게 갈려 있는지를 알게 되자, 나는 이 광경에 그리 놀라지 않게 되었다."]], [["The rest of his toilet was soon achieved, and he proudly marched out of the room, wrapped up in his great pilot monkey jacket, and sporting his harpoon like a marshal’s baton.", "그의 나머지 몸단장은 이내 마무리되었고, 그는 커다란 도선사용 두툼한 외투를 두르고는, 작살을 마치 원수(元帥)의 지휘봉처럼 멋들어지게 흔들며 의기양양하게 방을 나섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER 5. Breakfast.", "# CHAPTER 5. Breakfast."]], [["I quickly followed suit, and descending into the bar-room accosted the grinning landlord very pleasantly.", "나는 곧바로 그를 따라 일어나 술집으로 내려가서, 싱글거리는 주인장에게 아주 유쾌하게 말을 건넸다."], ["I cherished no malice towards him, though he had been skylarking with me not a little in the matter of my bedfellow.", "그가 내 잠자리 동무 문제로 나를 적잖이 놀려 댔지만, 나는 그에게 아무런 악감정도 품지 않았다."]], [["However, a good laugh is a mighty good thing, and rather too scarce a good thing; the more’s the pity.", "어쨌든 한바탕 웃음이란 참으로 좋은 것이며, 그러기에는 너무 드문 좋은 것이기도 하다."], ["So, if any one man, in his own proper person, afford stuff for a good joke to anybody, let him not be backward, but let him cheerfully allow himself to spend and be spent in that way.", "더욱 안타까운 일이다. 그러니 어떤 사람이 제 한 몸으로 누구에게든 좋은 농담거리를 줄 수 있다면, 망설이지 말고 기꺼이 그 방면으로 제 몸을 내주어 마음껏 쓰이게 할 일이다."], ["And the man that has anything bountifully laughable about him, be sure there is more in that man than you perhaps think for.", "그리고 제 안에 어딘가 넉넉히 웃음을 자아내는 데가 있는 사람이라면, 그 사람 안에는 그대가 짐작하는 것보다 더 많은 것이 들어 있음을 믿어도 좋다."]], [["The bar-room was now full of the boarders who had been dropping in the night previous, and whom I had not as yet had a good look at. They were nearly all whalemen; chief mates, and second mates, and third mates, and sea carpenters, and sea coopers, and sea blacksmiths, and harpooneers, and ship keepers; a brown and brawny company, with bosky beards; an unshorn, shaggy set, all wearing monkey jackets for morning gowns.", "술집은 이제 전날 밤 하나둘 흘러들어 온 하숙객들로 가득 찼는데, 나는 아직 그들을 제대로 살펴보지 못한 터였다. 그들은 거의 모두가 고래잡이였다. 일등 항해사, 이등 항해사, 삼등 항해사, 배 목수, 배 통장이, 배 대장장이, 작살잡이, 그리고 배 지킴이들이었다. 햇볕에 그을려 거무스레하고 억센 무리로, 덥수룩한 수염을 길렀다. 깎지 않은 텁수룩한 패거리가 하나같이 짧은 두꺼운 윗도리를 잠옷 가운 삼아 걸치고 있었다."]], [["You could pretty plainly tell how long each one had been ashore.", "저마다 뭍에 올라온 지 얼마나 되었는지는 제법 또렷이 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["This young fellow’s healthy cheek is like a sun-toasted pear in hue, and would seem to smell almost as musky; he cannot have been three days landed from his Indian voyage.", "이 젊은 친구의 혈색 좋은 뺨은 햇볕에 익은 배(梨)의 빛깔을 띠어, 거의 그만큼이나 사향 냄새를 풍길 듯했다. 인도양 항해에서 뭍에 내린 지 사흘도 채 되지 않았을 것이다."], ["That man next him looks a few shades lighter; you might say a touch of satin wood is in him.", "그 옆에 있는 자는 몇 단계쯤 더 옅어 보였으니, 새틴우드 결이 살짝 비친다고나 할까."], ["In the complexion of a third still lingers a tropic tawn, but slightly bleached withal; he doubtless has tarried whole weeks ashore.", "세 번째 사람의 안색에는 아직 열대의 거무튀튀한 기운이 남아 있었으나, 그래도 조금은 바래 있었다. 이 사람은 분명 몇 주씩이나 뭍에 머물렀을 것이다."], ["But who could show a cheek like Queequeg?", "그러나 누가 퀴퀘그(Queequeg) 같은 뺨을 내보일 수 있겠는가?"], ["which, barred with various tints, seemed like the Andes’ western slope, to show forth in one array, contrasting climates, zone by zone.", "갖가지 색조로 줄무늬가 진 그 뺨은, 마치 안데스산맥의 서쪽 비탈처럼, 서로 다른 기후를 한 화폭 위에 지대별로 나란히 펼쳐 보이는 듯했다."]], [["“Grub, ho!”", "\"밥 나왔소!\""], ["now cried the landlord, flinging open a door, and in we went to breakfast.", "이윽고 주인장이 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 외쳤고, 우리는 안으로 들어가 아침을 먹었다."]], [["They say that men who have seen the world, thereby become quite at ease in manner, quite self-possessed in company.", "세상을 두루 본 사람은 그로 인해 태도가 아주 느긋해지고, 무리 가운데서도 침착해진다고들 한다. 그러나 늘 그런 것은 아니다."], ["Not always, though: Ledyard, the great New England traveller, and Mungo Park, the Scotch one; of all men, they possessed the least assurance in the parlor.", "위대한 뉴잉글랜드의 여행가 레디어드(Ledyard)와 스코틀랜드의 여행가 멍고 파크(Mungo Park), 이 두 사람은 누구보다도 응접실에서 자신감이 가장 없는 사람들이었다."], ["But perhaps the mere crossing of Siberia in a sledge drawn by dogs as Ledyard did, or the taking a long solitary walk on an empty stomach, in the negro heart of Africa, which was the sum of poor Mungo’s performances—this kind of travel, I say, may not be the very best mode of attaining a high social polish.", "그러나 어쩌면 레디어드가 했던 것처럼 개들이 끄는 썰매를 타고 시베리아를 가로지르는 일, 또는 가련한 멍고가 이룬 일의 전부였던, 굶주린 배를 안고 아프리카의 검은 심장부를 홀로 오래도록 걷는 일 — 말하건대 이런 종류의 여행은 높은 사교적 세련됨을 얻기에는 그리 좋은 방편이 아닐지도 모른다."], ["Still, for the most part, that sort of thing is to be had anywhere.", "그래도 대체로 그런 종류의 것이라면 어디서든 얻을 수 있는 법이다."]], [["These reflections just here are occasioned by the circumstance that after we were all seated at the table, and I was preparing to hear some good stories about whaling; to my no small surprise, nearly every man maintained a profound silence.", "마침 여기서 이런 생각이 떠오른 것은, 우리가 모두 식탁에 둘러앉아 내가 고래잡이에 관한 그럴듯한 이야기를 들으려 채비하고 있을 때, 적잖이 놀랍게도 거의 모든 사람이 깊은 침묵을 지켰기 때문이다."], ["And not only that, but they looked embarrassed.", "그뿐만 아니라 그들은 어색해 보이기까지 했다."], ["Yes, here were a set of sea-dogs, many of whom without the slightest bashfulness had boarded great whales on the high seas—entire strangers to them—and duelled them dead without winking; and yet, here they sat at a social breakfast table—all of the same calling, all of kindred tastes—looking round as sheepishly at each other as though they had never been out of sight of some sheepfold among the Green Mountains.", "그렇다, 여기 모인 이 바다의 늙은 개들 가운데 많은 이들은 일찍이 한 점 부끄럼도 없이 망망대해에서 거대한 고래들 — 그들에게는 생판 낯선 상대들 — 의 등에 올라타 눈 하나 깜짝하지 않고 결투를 벌여 숨통을 끊어 놓은 자들이었다. 그런데도 지금 이 화기로운 아침 식탁에 둘러앉아서는 — 모두가 같은 일을 하는, 같은 취향을 지닌 사람들이건만 — 마치 그린마운틴 어느 양 우리를 한 번도 벗어나 본 적 없는 양 서로를 멋쩍게 두리번거리고 있었다."], ["A curious sight; these bashful bears, these timid warrior whalemen!", "참으로 진기한 광경이었다. 이 수줍은 곰들, 이 겁 많은 전사 같은 고래잡이들이라니!"]], [["But as for Queequeg—why, Queequeg sat there among them—at the head of the table, too, it so chanced; as cool as an icicle.", "그러나 퀴퀘그로 말하자면 — 아니, 퀴퀘그는 그들 가운데 앉아 있었으니, 마침 식탁의 상석에 자리 잡고서, 고드름처럼 태연자약했다."], ["To be sure I cannot say much for his breeding.", "물론 그의 몸가짐에 대해서야 내가 좋게 말할 거리가 별로 없다."], ["His greatest admirer could not have cordially justified his bringing his harpoon into breakfast with him, and using it there without ceremony; reaching over the table with it, to the imminent jeopardy of many heads, and grappling the beefsteaks towards him.", "그를 가장 흠모하는 사람이라 해도, 그가 아침 식탁에까지 작살을 들고 와 거리낌 없이 그것을 휘둘러 댄 일, 곧 식탁 너머로 작살을 쭉 뻗어 여러 사람의 머리를 위태롭게 하면서 비프스테이크를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기던 짓을 진심으로 두둔하지는 못했을 것이다."], ["But that was certainly very coolly done by him, and every one knows that in most people’s estimation, to do anything coolly is to do it genteelly.", "그러나 그것을 그는 확실히 아주 침착하게 해냈으니, 누구나 알다시피 대다수 사람들이 보기에 무슨 일이든 침착하게 해낸다는 것은 곧 점잖게 해낸다는 뜻이다."]], [["We will not speak of all Queequeg’s peculiarities here; how he eschewed coffee and hot rolls, and applied his undivided attention to beefsteaks, done rare.", "퀴퀘그의 별난 점을 여기서 다 늘어놓지는 않겠다. 그가 어떻게 커피와 따끈한 롤빵을 마다하고 오로지 설익힌 비프스테이크에만 온 정신을 쏟았는지 따위는."], ["Enough, that when breakfast was over he withdrew like the rest into the public room, lighted his tomahawk-pipe, and was sitting there quietly digesting and smoking with his inseparable hat on, when I sallied out for a stroll.", "다만 아침 식사가 끝나자 그도 나머지 사람들처럼 공용실로 물러나 토마호크 담뱃대에 불을 붙이고, 한시도 떼어 놓지 않는 모자를 쓴 채 그 자리에 앉아 조용히 소화를 시키며 담배를 피우고 있었고, 그동안 나는 산책을 하러 밖으로 나섰다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하다."]], [["# CHAPTER 6. The Street.", "# CHAPTER 6. The Street."]], [["If I had been astonished at first catching a glimpse of so outlandish an individual as Queequeg circulating among the polite society of a civilized town, that astonishment soon departed upon taking my first daylight stroll through the streets of New Bedford.", "처음에 퀴케그(Queequeg)처럼 그토록 기이한 인물이 문명화된 도시의 점잖은 사교계 속을 누비는 모습을 흘끗 보았을 때 나는 적잖이 놀랐지만, 그 놀라움은 뉴베드퍼드(New Bedford)의 거리를 처음으로 대낮에 산책하면서 곧 사라져 버렸다."]], [["In thoroughfares nigh the docks, any considerable seaport will frequently offer to view the queerest looking nondescripts from foreign parts.", "부두 가까운 큰길에서는, 어지간한 규모의 항구 도시라면 흔히 이국에서 온 더없이 괴상한 모습의 정체불명 인간들을 눈앞에 펼쳐 보이게 마련이다."], ["Even in Broadway and Chestnut streets, Mediterranean mariners will sometimes jostle the affrighted ladies.", "브로드웨이나 체스트넛 거리에서조차 지중해의 뱃사람들이 겁에 질린 부인네들과 어깨를 부딪치는 일이 더러 있다."], ["Regent Street is not unknown to Lascars and Malays; and at Bombay, in the Apollo Green, live Yankees have often scared the natives.", "리젠트 거리도 라스카르인과 말레이인에게 낯설지 않으며, 봄베이의 아폴로 그린에서는 살아 있는 양키들이 곧잘 원주민들을 겁먹게 했다."], ["But New Bedford beats all Water Street and Wapping.", "그러나 뉴베드퍼드는 워터 거리와 워핑을 죄다 능가한다."], ["In these last-mentioned haunts you see only sailors; but in New Bedford, actual cannibals stand chatting at street corners; savages outright; many of whom yet carry on their bones unholy flesh.", "방금 언급한 그런 소굴들에서는 그저 선원들만 보일 뿐이지만, 뉴베드퍼드에서는 진짜 식인종들이 길모퉁이에 서서 잡담을 나누고 있다. 영락없는 야만인들이며, 그중 다수는 아직도 제 뼈 위에 부정한 살점을 붙이고 다닌다."], ["It makes a stranger stare.", "이방인이라면 빤히 쳐다보지 않을 수 없는 광경이다."]], [["But, besides the Feegeeans, Tongatobooarrs, Erromanggoans, Pannangians, and Brighggians, and, besides the wild specimens of the whaling-craft which unheeded reel about the streets, you will see other sights still more curious, certainly more comical.", "그러나 피지인, 통가타부인, 에로망고인, 파낭인, 브리기인 외에도, 그리고 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 가운데 거리를 비틀거리며 돌아다니는 고래잡이 무리의 야성적인 표본들 외에도, 그대는 그보다 더 진기하고, 확실히 더 우스꽝스러운 광경들을 보게 될 것이다."], ["There weekly arrive in this town scores of green Vermonters and New Hampshire men, all athirst for gain and glory in the fishery.", "이 도시에는 매주 수십 명의 풋내기 버몬트 사람과 뉴햄프셔 사람이 몰려드는데, 하나같이 포경업에서 돈과 영예를 얻으려 목말라하는 자들이다."], ["They are mostly young, of stalwart frames; fellows who have felled forests, and now seek to drop the axe and snatch the whale-lance.", "대부분 젊고 건장한 체구의 사내들로, 숲을 베어 넘기던 자들이 이제는 도끼를 내던지고 고래 작살을 거머쥐려는 것이다."], ["Many are as green as the Green Mountains whence they came.", "다수는 자신들이 떠나온 그린마운틴만큼이나 풋풋하다."], ["In some things you would think them but a few hours old.", "어떤 면에서는 갓 태어난 지 몇 시간밖에 안 된 것처럼 보일 정도다."], ["Look there!", "저기를 보라!"], ["that chap strutting round the corner.", "모퉁이를 으스대며 돌아 나오는 저 친구를."], ["He wears a beaver hat and swallow-tailed coat, girdled with a sailor-belt and sheath-knife.", "비버 가죽 모자에 제비꼬리 외투를 걸치고, 선원용 허리띠에 칼집 단검을 차고 있다. 여기 또 한 명이 온다."], ["Here comes another with a sou’-wester and a bombazine cloak.", "사우웨스터 방수모를 쓰고 봄바진 천 망토를 두른 사내다."]], [["No town-bred dandy will compare with a country-bred one—I mean a downright bumpkin dandy—a fellow that, in the dog-days, will mow his two acres in buckskin gloves for fear of tanning his hands.", "도회에서 자란 멋쟁이는 시골에서 자란 멋쟁이를 결코 따를 수 없다. 내가 말하는 것은 영락없는 촌뜨기 멋쟁이로, 삼복더위에도 제 손이 햇볕에 그을릴까 두려워 사슴 가죽 장갑을 끼고 자기 밭 두 에이커를 베어 넘기는 그런 작자다."], ["Now when a country dandy like this takes it into his head to make a distinguished reputation, and joins the great whale-fishery, you should see the comical things he does upon reaching the seaport.", "그런데 이런 시골 멋쟁이가 명성 한번 떨쳐 보겠다고 마음먹고 위대한 포경업에 뛰어들면, 항구에 닿은 뒤 그가 벌이는 우스꽝스러운 짓거리를 그대도 꼭 보아야 한다."], ["In bespeaking his sea-outfit, he orders bell-buttons to his waistcoats; straps to his canvas trowsers.", "바다용 장비를 맞추면서 그는 조끼에 종 모양 단추를 달아 달라고 주문하고, 캔버스 천 바지에는 끈을 달아 달라 한다."], ["Ah, poor Hay-Seed!", "아, 가련한 풋내기여!"], ["how bitterly will burst those straps in the first howling gale, when thou art driven, straps, buttons, and all, down the throat of the tempest.", "첫 광풍이 울부짖을 때 그 끈들이 얼마나 비참하게 터져 나갈 것인가. 그때 그대는 끈이고 단추고 죄다 함께 폭풍의 목구멍 속으로 처박히게 될 테니."]], [["But think not that this famous town has only harpooneers, cannibals, and bumpkins to show her visitors.", "그러나 이 이름난 도시가 방문객에게 작살잡이와 식인종과 촌뜨기들만 보여 준다고는 생각지 마라."], ["Not at all.", "결코 그렇지 않다."], ["Still New Bedford is a queer place.", "그래도 뉴베드퍼드는 묘한 곳이다."], ["Had it not been for us whalemen, that tract of land would this day perhaps have been in as howling condition as the coast of Labrador.", "만약 우리 고래잡이들이 없었더라면, 저 땅뙈기는 오늘날 아마 래브라도 해안만큼이나 황량한 처지에 놓여 있었을지도 모른다."], ["As it is, parts of her back country are enough to frighten one, they look so bony.", "그렇기는 해도, 그 내륙 일부는 어찌나 앙상해 보이는지 사람을 겁먹게 하기에 충분하다."], ["The town itself is perhaps the dearest place to live in, in all New England.", "도시 자체는 아마 온 뉴잉글랜드(New England)를 통틀어 가장 살기 비싼 곳일 것이다."], ["It is a land of oil, true enough: but not like Canaan; a land, also, of corn and wine.", "과연 기름의 땅이기는 하다. 그러나 가나안과 같지는 않으니, 가나안은 또한 곡식과 포도주의 땅이었다."], ["The streets do not run with milk; nor in the spring-time do they pave them with fresh eggs.", "이곳 거리에는 젖이 흐르지 않으며, 봄철이라 해서 신선한 달걀로 길을 포장하지도 않는다."], ["Yet, in spite of this, nowhere in all America will you find more patrician-like houses; parks and gardens more opulent, than in New Bedford.", "그럼에도 불구하고, 온 아메리카를 통틀어 뉴베드퍼드보다 더 귀족적인 저택들과 더 호화로운 공원과 정원을 찾을 수 있는 곳은 어디에도 없다."], ["Whence came they?", "그것들은 어디서 왔는가?"], ["how planted upon this once scraggy scoria of a country?", "한때 이 거칠고 메마른 화산암 같은 땅 위에 어떻게 심어졌단 말인가?"]], [["Go and gaze upon the iron emblematical harpoons round yonder lofty mansion, and your question will be answered.", "저 높이 솟은 저택 둘레에 상징처럼 둘러진 무쇠 작살들을 가서 한번 바라보라. 그러면 그대의 물음에 답이 나오리라."], ["Yes; all these brave houses and flowery gardens came from the Atlantic, Pacific, and Indian oceans.", "그렇다. 이 모든 당당한 저택과 꽃 만발한 정원은 대서양과 태평양과 인도양에서 온 것이다."], ["One and all, they were harpooned and dragged up hither from the bottom of the sea.", "하나도 빠짐없이, 그것들은 작살에 꿰여 바다 밑바닥에서 이곳까지 끌어올려졌다."], ["Can Herr Alexander perform a feat like that?", "알렉산더 선생이 이런 묘기를 부릴 수 있겠는가?"]], [["In New Bedford, fathers, they say, give whales for dowers to their daughters, and portion off their nieces with a few porpoises a-piece.", "뉴베드퍼드에서는, 사람들 말이, 아버지들이 딸에게 지참금으로 고래를 주고, 조카딸들에게는 한 사람당 돌고래 몇 마리씩을 떼어 준다고 한다."], ["You must go to New Bedford to see a brilliant wedding; for, they say, they have reservoirs of oil in every house, and every night recklessly burn their lengths in spermaceti candles.", "화려한 결혼식을 보려거든 뉴베드퍼드에 가 봐야 한다. 사람들 말로는, 집집마다 기름 저장고가 있어서, 매일 밤 향유고래 기름 양초를 그 길이만큼 아낌없이 태운다고 하기 때문이다."]], [["In summer time, the town is sweet to see; full of fine maples—long avenues of green and gold.", "여름철이면 이 도시는 보기에 감미롭다. 아름다운 단풍나무로 가득하여, 초록빛과 황금빛이 어우러진 긴 가로수 길을 이룬다."], ["And in August, high in air, the beautiful and bountiful horse-chestnuts, candelabra-wise, proffer the passer-by their tapering upright cones of congregated blossoms.", "그리고 팔월이면, 높다란 허공으로 아름답고 풍성한 마로니에가 촛대처럼 가지를 뻗어, 무리 지어 핀 꽃송이가 뾰족하게 곧추선 원뿔 모양으로 지나가는 행인에게 그 자태를 내민다."], ["So omnipotent is art; which in many a district of New Bedford has superinduced bright terraces of flowers upon the barren refuse rocks thrown aside at creation’s final day.", "예술의 힘이란 이토록 전능하니, 뉴베드퍼드의 여러 구역에서 그것은 천지창조의 마지막 날에 한쪽으로 버려졌던 황량한 폐석 위에 환한 꽃의 계단을 일구어 놓았다."]], [["And the women of New Bedford, they bloom like their own red roses.", "그리고 뉴베드퍼드의 여인들, 그들은 제 고장의 붉은 장미처럼 피어난다."], ["But roses only bloom in summer; whereas the fine carnation of their cheeks is perennial as sunlight in the seventh heavens.", "그러나 장미는 여름에만 피지만, 그들의 뺨에 어린 고운 카네이션 빛깔은 일곱째 하늘의 햇살처럼 사철 변함이 없다."], ["Elsewhere match that bloom of theirs, ye cannot, save in Salem, where they tell me the young girls breathe such musk, their sailor sweethearts smell them miles off shore, as though they were drawing nigh the odorous Moluccas instead of the Puritanic sands.", "그들의 그 꽃다움에 견줄 만한 것은 다른 어디서도 찾을 수 없으니, 다만 세일럼만은 예외다. 사람들이 내게 일러 주기를, 그곳의 젊은 처녀들은 어찌나 향긋한 사향 내음을 풍기는지, 뱃사람 연인들이 바다에서 몇 마일 떨어진 데서도 그 향기를 맡아, 마치 청교도의 모래밭이 아니라 향기로운 몰루카 제도에 다가서는 듯한 기분이 든다고 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 7. The Chapel.", "# CHAPTER 7. The Chapel."]], [["In this same New Bedford there stands a Whaleman’s Chapel, and few are the moody fishermen, shortly bound for the Indian Ocean or Pacific, who fail to make a Sunday visit to the spot.", "이 같은 뉴베드퍼드에는 고래잡이 예배당이 하나 서 있는데, 머지않아 인도양이나 태평양으로 떠날 침울한 어부들 가운데 일요일에 이곳을 찾지 않는 이는 드물다."], ["I am sure that I did not.", "나 역시 빠뜨리지 않았음을 분명히 밝혀 둔다."]], [["Returning from my first morning stroll, I again sallied out upon this special errand.", "첫 아침 산책에서 돌아온 나는, 바로 그 특별한 볼일을 위해 다시 길을 나섰다."], ["The sky had changed from clear, sunny cold, to driving sleet and mist.", "하늘은 맑고 햇살 비치는 추위에서, 휘몰아치는 진눈깨비와 안개로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["Wrapping myself in my shaggy jacket of the cloth called bearskin, I fought my way against the stubborn storm.", "곰가죽이라 불리는 천으로 지은 텁수룩한 외투에 몸을 감싸고, 나는 끈질긴 폭풍을 헤치며 길을 뚫고 나아갔다."], ["Entering, I found a small scattered congregation of sailors, and sailors’ wives and widows.", "안으로 들어서자 선원들과, 선원의 아내들, 그리고 과부들이 띄엄띄엄 모여 앉아 이루는 작은 회중이 보였다."], ["A muffled silence reigned, only broken at times by the shrieks of the storm.", "숨죽인 정적이 자리를 지배했고, 이따금 폭풍의 비명만이 그것을 깨뜨릴 뿐이었다."], ["Each silent worshipper seemed purposely sitting apart from the other, as if each silent grief were insular and incommunicable.", "말 없는 예배자들은 저마다 일부러 떨어져 앉아 있는 듯했으니, 마치 그 말 없는 슬픔이 저마다 외딴섬처럼 동떨어져 서로 통할 수 없는 것인 양했다."], ["The chaplain had not yet arrived; and there these silent islands of men and women sat steadfastly eyeing several marble tablets, with black borders, masoned into the wall on either side the pulpit.", "목사는 아직 도착하지 않았고, 그곳에서 이 말 없는 남녀의 섬들은 설교단 양옆 벽에 박혀 있는, 검은 테두리를 두른 여러 대리석 명판을 한결같이 응시하고 있었다."], ["Three of them ran something like the following, but I do not pretend to quote:—", "그중 세 개는 대략 다음과 같았으나, 정확히 옮긴다고 장담하지는 않겠다."]], [["SACRED TO THE MEMORY OF JOHN TALBOT, Who, at the age of eighteen, was lost overboard, Near the Isle of Desolation, off Patagonia, November 1st, 1836.", "존 탤벗(JOHN TALBOT)을 기리며. 그는 열여덟의 나이에, 1836년 11월 1일, 파타고니아 앞바다 황량의 섬 부근에서 바다에 휩쓸려 목숨을 잃었다."], ["THIS TABLET Is erected to his Memory BY HIS SISTER.", "이 명판은 그의 누이가 그를 기리며 세운 것이다."]], [["SACRED TO THE MEMORY OF ROBERT LONG, WILLIS ELLERY, NATHAN COLEMAN, WALTER CANNY, SETH MACY, AND SAMUEL GLEIG, Forming one of the boats’ crews OF THE SHIP ELIZA Who were towed out of sight by a Whale, On the Off-shore Ground in the PACIFIC, December 31st, 1839.", "로버트 롱(ROBERT LONG), 윌리스 엘러리(WILLIS ELLERY), 네이선 콜먼(NATHAN COLEMAN), 월터 캐니(WALTER CANNY), 세스 메이시(SETH MACY), 그리고 새뮤얼 글레이그(SAMUEL GLEIG)를 기리며. 이들은 일라이자(ELIZA)호의 보트 한 척에 탄 선원들로서, 1839년 12월 31일 태평양의 먼바다 어장에서 고래에게 끌려가 시야에서 사라졌다."], ["THIS MARBLE Is here placed by their surviving SHIPMATES.", "이 대리석은 살아남은 동료 선원들이 이곳에 세운 것이다."]], [["SACRED TO THE MEMORY OF The late CAPTAIN EZEKIEL HARDY, Who in the bows of his boat was killed by a Sperm Whale on the coast of Japan, August 3d, 1833.", "작고한 이지키엘 하디(EZEKIEL HARDY) 선장을 기리며. 그는 1833년 8월 3일, 일본 연안에서 자기 보트의 뱃머리에 서 있다가 향유고래에게 목숨을 잃었다."], ["THIS TABLET Is erected to his Memory BY HIS WIDOW.", "이 명판은 그의 미망인이 그를 기리며 세운 것이다."]], [["Shaking off the sleet from my ice-glazed hat and jacket, I seated myself near the door, and turning sideways was surprised to see Queequeg near me.", "얼음으로 번들거리는 모자와 외투에서 진눈깨비를 털어 내고, 나는 문 가까이에 자리를 잡았는데, 옆으로 고개를 돌리다가 가까이에 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)가 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["Affected by the solemnity of the scene, there was a wondering gaze of incredulous curiosity in his countenance.", "장면의 엄숙함에 사로잡힌 그의 얼굴에는, 믿기지 않는다는 듯한 호기심의 의아한 응시가 어려 있었다. 이 야만인은 내가 들어온 것을 알아챈 듯 보이는 유일한 사람이었다."], ["This savage was the only person present who seemed to notice my entrance; because he was the only one who could not read, and, therefore, was not reading those frigid inscriptions on the wall.", "글을 읽지 못하는 유일한 사람이었기에, 벽에 새겨진 저 싸늘한 비문들을 읽고 있지 않았던 까닭이다. 그곳에 이름이 오른 선원들의 친지 가운데 누군가가 지금 이 회중 속에 있는지 나는 알 수 없었다."], ["Whether any of the relatives of the seamen whose names appeared there were now among the congregation, I knew not; but so many are the unrecorded accidents in the fishery, and so plainly did several women present wear the countenance if not the trappings of some unceasing grief, that I feel sure that here before me were assembled those, in whose unhealing hearts the sight of those bleak tablets sympathetically caused the old wounds to bleed afresh.", "그러나 포경업에는 기록되지 않은 사고가 워낙 많고, 또 그 자리의 여러 여인들이 끊이지 않는 슬픔의 장식까지는 아니더라도 그 표정만은 너무도 또렷이 지니고 있었기에, 나는 분명히 느꼈다. 내 앞에 모여 있는 이들이야말로, 그 아물지 않는 가슴속에서 저 황량한 명판들을 바라보는 것만으로 옛 상처가 새삼 피 흘리고 마는 사람들임을."]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["ye whose dead lie buried beneath the green grass; who standing among flowers can say—here, here lies my beloved; ye know not the desolation that broods in bosoms like these.", "그대들, 죽은 이가 푸른 풀밭 아래 묻혀 있는 자들이여. 꽃들 가운데 서서 여기, 바로 여기 내 사랑하는 이가 누워 있노라고 말할 수 있는 그대들이여."], ["What bitter blanks in those black-bordered marbles which cover no ashes!", "그대들은 이런 가슴속에 도사린 황폐함을 알지 못한다. 재 한 줌 덮지 못한 저 검은 테두리의 대리석 속에 담긴 그 쓰라린 공백이라니!"], ["What despair in those immovable inscriptions!", "저 꿈쩍도 않는 비문 속에 담긴 그 절망이라니!"], ["What deadly voids and unbidden infidelities in the lines that seem to gnaw upon all Faith, and refuse resurrections to the beings who have placelessly perished without a grave.", "모든 믿음(Faith)을 갉아먹는 듯하며, 무덤도 없이 어디서랄 것도 없이 스러진 존재들에게 부활을 허락하지 않는 그 글줄 속에 담긴, 죽음 같은 공허와 청하지도 않은 불신이라니."], ["As well might those tablets stand in the cave of Elephanta as here.", "저 명판들은 차라리 이곳보다 엘레판타 동굴 안에 서 있는 편이 나았으리라."]], [["In what census of living creatures, the dead of mankind are included; why it is that a universal proverb says of them, that they tell no tales, though containing more secrets than the Goodwin Sands; how it is that to his name who yesterday departed for the other world, we prefix so significant and infidel a word, and yet do not thus entitle him, if he but embarks for the remotest Indies of this living earth; why the Life Insurance Companies pay death-forfeitures upon immortals; in what eternal, unstirring paralysis, and deadly, hopeless trance, yet lies antique Adam who died sixty round centuries ago; how it is that we still refuse to be comforted for those who we nevertheless maintain are dwelling in unspeakable bliss; why all the living so strive to hush all the dead; wherefore but the rumor of a knocking in a tomb will terrify a whole city. All these things are not without their meanings.", "살아 있는 피조물의 그 어떤 인구 조사에 인류의 죽은 자들이 포함되는가. 그들에 관해 어찌하여 만국 공통의 속담은, 그들이 굿윈 모래톱보다 더 많은 비밀을 품고 있으면서도 아무 이야기도 들려주지 않는다고 말하는가. 어제 저세상으로 떠난 이의 이름 앞에는 그토록 의미심장하고 불신에 찬 말을 우리가 붙이면서, 정작 그가 이 살아 있는 땅의 가장 먼 인도(Indies)로 떠나간 것이라면 어찌하여 그리 부르지 않는가. 어찌하여 생명 보험 회사들은 불멸의 존재들에게 사망 보험금을 지급하는가. 예순 세기를 헤아리는 둥근 세월 전에 죽은 저 옛 아담은 어떤 영원하고 미동도 없는 마비 속에, 어떤 죽음 같고 희망 없는 혼수상태 속에 여전히 누워 있는가. 우리가 형언할 수 없는 지복(至福) 속에 머물고 있다고 끝내 주장하면서도, 어찌하여 우리는 그들을 위해 위로받기를 여전히 거부하는가. 어찌하여 살아 있는 모든 자들은 죽은 모든 자들을 그토록 잠재우려 애쓰는가. 무덤 안에서 무언가 두드린다는 소문 하나만으로도 어찌하여 도시 하나가 통째로 공포에 떠는가. 이 모든 것에는 다 그럴 만한 의미가 있는 것이다."]], [["But Faith, like a jackal, feeds among the tombs, and even from these dead doubts she gathers her most vital hope.", "그러나 믿음은 자칼처럼 무덤 사이를 누비며 먹이를 구하고, 이런 죽은 의심들에서조차 가장 생생한 희망을 거두어들인다."]], [["It needs scarcely to be told, with what feelings, on the eve of a Nantucket voyage, I regarded those marble tablets, and by the murky light of that darkened, doleful day read the fate of the whalemen who had gone before me.", "낸터킷(Nantucket) 항해를 앞둔 전날 밤, 어떤 심정으로 내가 저 대리석 명판들을 바라보았는지, 그리고 어둡고 음울한 그날의 침침한 빛 속에서 나보다 앞서 떠나간 고래잡이들의 운명을 읽어 내렸는지는 굳이 말할 필요도 없으리라."], ["Yes, Ishmael, the same fate may be thine.", "그래, 이슈메일, 그 같은 운명이 네 것이 될 수도 있으리라."], ["But somehow I grew merry again.", "그러나 어쩐 일인지 나는 다시 유쾌해졌다."], ["Delightful inducements to embark, fine chance for promotion, it seems—aye, a stove boat will make me an immortal by brevet.", "출항을 부추기는 즐거운 유인책에다, 승진의 더없이 좋은 기회까지 있는 듯하니 말이다."], ["Yes, there is death in this business of whaling—a speechlessly quick chaotic bundling of a man into Eternity.", "그렇다, 박살 난 보트 한 척이 나를 약식 임명으로 불멸의 존재로 만들어 주리라. 그렇다, 이 고래잡이라는 일에는 죽음이 도사리고 있다."], ["But what then? Methinks we have hugely mistaken this matter of Life and Death.", "한 인간을 말 한마디 못 할 만큼 순식간에 영원(Eternity) 속으로 혼란스레 처박아 넣는 죽음이."], ["Methinks that what they call my shadow here on earth is my true substance.", "그러나 그래서 어떻단 말인가? 생각건대 우리는 이 삶과 죽음의 문제를 크게 잘못 짚어 온 것이다."], ["Methinks that in looking at things spiritual, we are too much like oysters observing the sun through the water, and thinking that thick water the thinnest of air.", "생각건대 이 땅 위에서 사람들이 내 그림자라 부르는 것이야말로 나의 참된 실체다. 생각건대 영적인 것들을 바라볼 때 우리는, 물을 통해 해를 관찰하면서 그 두꺼운 물이 가장 엷은 공기라 여기는 굴(牡蠣)과 너무도 닮았다."], ["Methinks my body is but the lees of my better being.", "생각건대 내 육신은 더 나은 내 존재가 가라앉고 남은 찌꺼기일 따름이다."], ["In fact take my body who will, take it I say, it is not me.", "사실 내 육신은 누구든 가져가고 싶은 자가 가져가라, 가져가라니까, 그것은 내가 아니다."], ["And therefore three cheers for Nantucket; and come a stove boat and stove body when they will, for stave my soul, Jove himself cannot.", "그러므로 낸터킷을 위하여 만세 삼창을. 그리고 박살 난 보트든 박살 난 육신이든 올 테면 언제든 오라, 내 영혼만은 제우스(Jove)라 한들 결코 박살 내지 못할 터이니."]], [["# CHAPTER 8. The Pulpit.", "# CHAPTER 8. The Pulpit."]], [["I had not been seated very long ere a man of a certain venerable robustness entered; immediately as the storm-pelted door flew back upon admitting him, a quick regardful eyeing of him by all the congregation, sufficiently attested that this fine old man was the chaplain.", "내가 자리에 앉은 지 얼마 지나지 않아, 어딘가 위엄 있는 강건함을 지닌 한 남자가 들어왔다. 폭풍에 두들겨 맞던 문이 그를 맞아들이며 활짝 젖혀지자마자, 회중 전체가 그를 향해 던지는 재빠르고 주의 깊은 눈길이, 이 훌륭한 노인이 바로 그 목사임을 충분히 증명해 주었다."], ["Yes, it was the famous Father Mapple, so called by the whalemen, among whom he was a very great favourite.", "그렇다, 그는 고래잡이들이 매플 신부(Father Mapple)라 부르는 그 유명한 인물이었으며, 그들 사이에서 대단한 인기를 누리고 있었다."], ["He had been a sailor and a harpooneer in his youth, but for many years past had dedicated his life to the ministry.", "그는 젊은 시절 선원이자 작살잡이였으나, 지난 여러 해 동안은 자신의 삶을 성직에 바쳐 왔다."], ["At the time I now write of, Father Mapple was in the hardy winter of a healthy old age; that sort of old age which seems merging into a second flowering youth, for among all the fissures of his wrinkles, there shone certain mild gleams of a newly developing bloom—the spring verdure peeping forth even beneath February’s snow.", "지금 내가 쓰고 있는 이 시절에, 매플 신부는 건강한 노년의 강인한 겨울을 맞고 있었다. 그것은 두 번째로 꽃피는 청춘 속으로 녹아드는 듯한 그런 노년이었으니, 그의 주름 사이 골마다 새로이 피어나는 꽃의 은은한 광채가 어른거렸기 때문이다 — 이월의 눈 아래에서조차 살며시 고개를 내미는 봄의 푸르름 같았다."], ["No one having previously heard his history, could for the first time behold Father Mapple without the utmost interest, because there were certain engrafted clerical peculiarities about him, imputable to that adventurous maritime life he had led.", "그의 내력을 미리 들어 본 적 없는 이라면, 누구도 처음 매플 신부를 보고서 더없는 흥미를 느끼지 않을 수 없었으니, 그가 살아온 그 모험적인 바다 생활에서 비롯된, 성직자다움에 접붙여진 어떤 독특함이 그에게 깃들어 있었던 까닭이다."], ["When he entered I observed that he carried no umbrella, and certainly had not come in his carriage, for his tarpaulin hat ran down with melting sleet, and his great pilot cloth jacket seemed almost to drag him to the floor with the weight of the water it had absorbed.", "그가 들어왔을 때 나는 그가 우산을 들지 않았음을, 그리고 분명 마차를 타고 오지도 않았음을 알아차렸다. 그의 방수모에서는 녹아내리는 진눈깨비가 줄줄 흘러내렸고, 두꺼운 항해용 천으로 지은 큼직한 외투는 빨아들인 물의 무게로 그를 바닥으로 끌어내릴 듯했기 때문이다."], ["However, hat and coat and overshoes were one by one removed, and hung up in a little space in an adjacent corner; when, arrayed in a decent suit, he quietly approached the pulpit.", "그러나 모자와 외투와 덧신은 하나씩 벗겨져, 옆 모퉁이의 작은 공간에 걸렸다. 그렇게 단정한 차림이 된 그는, 조용히 설교단으로 다가갔다."]], [["Like most old fashioned pulpits, it was a very lofty one, and since a regular stairs to such a height would, by its long angle with the floor, seriously contract the already small area of the chapel, the architect, it seemed, had acted upon the hint of Father Mapple, and finished the pulpit without a stairs, substituting a perpendicular side ladder, like those used in mounting a ship from a boat at sea.", "대부분의 옛 양식 설교단이 그러하듯, 그것은 매우 높이 솟은 것이었으며, 그만한 높이까지 정규 계단을 놓는다면 바닥과 이루는 긴 경사 탓에 가뜩이나 비좁은 예배당 공간을 한층 옹색하게 만들 터였으므로, 건축가는 매플 신부의 의견을 따른 듯, 계단 없이 설교단을 완성하고, 그 대신 바다에서 보트를 타고 배에 오를 때 쓰는 것 같은 수직 측면 사다리를 놓았다."], ["The wife of a whaling captain had provided the chapel with a handsome pair of red worsted man-ropes for this ladder, which, being itself nicely headed, and stained with a mahogany colour, the whole contrivance, considering what manner of chapel it was, seemed by no means in bad taste.", "어느 포경선 선장의 아내가 이 사다리에 쓰라고 붉은 털실로 짠 멋진 손잡이 밧줄 한 쌍을 예배당에 마련해 주었는데, 그 밧줄은 끝이 곱게 매듭지어지고 마호가니 빛깔로 물들여져 있어, 이 예배당이 어떤 곳인가를 헤아려 보면, 그 전체 장치가 결코 격에 맞지 않는다고는 할 수 없었다."], ["Halting for an instant at the foot of the ladder, and with both hands grasping the ornamental knobs of the man-ropes, Father Mapple cast a look upwards, and then with a truly sailor-like but still reverential dexterity, hand over hand, mounted the steps as if ascending the main-top of his vessel.", "매플 신부는 사다리 발치에서 잠시 멈추어, 두 손으로 손잡이 밧줄의 장식 매듭을 움켜쥐고 위를 올려다보더니, 이윽고 참으로 선원다우면서도 경건함을 잃지 않은 능숙한 솜씨로, 한 손 한 손 번갈아 짚으며, 마치 제 배의 큰돛대 꼭대기에 오르듯 계단을 올라갔다."]], [["The perpendicular parts of this side ladder, as is usually the case with swinging ones, were of cloth-covered rope, only the rounds were of wood, so that at every step there was a joint.", "이 측면 사다리의 수직 부분은, 흔들리는 사다리들이 으레 그러하듯 천으로 감싼 밧줄로 되어 있었고, 다만 디딤대만 나무였으므로, 한 단마다 이음매가 있었다."], ["At my first glimpse of the pulpit, it had not escaped me that however convenient for a ship, these joints in the present instance seemed unnecessary.", "설교단을 처음 흘끗 보았을 때, 나는 이 이음매들이 배에서는 아무리 편리하다 한들 지금 이 경우에는 불필요해 보인다는 점을 놓치지 않았다."], ["For I was not prepared to see Father Mapple after gaining the height, slowly turn round, and stooping over the pulpit, deliberately drag up the ladder step by step, till the whole was deposited within, leaving him impregnable in his little Quebec.", "매플 신부가 그 높이에 오른 뒤, 천천히 몸을 돌려 설교단 너머로 허리를 굽히고는, 한 단 한 단 신중하게 사다리를 끌어올려 마침내 그 전체를 안쪽에 들여놓고, 자신만의 작은 퀘벡 요새 안에 난공불락으로 들어앉으리라고는 미처 예상하지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["I pondered some time without fully comprehending the reason for this.", "나는 한동안 곰곰이 생각해 보았으나, 그 까닭을 온전히 헤아릴 수 없었다."], ["Father Mapple enjoyed such a wide reputation for sincerity and sanctity, that I could not suspect him of courting notoriety by any mere tricks of the stage.", "매플 신부는 그 진실함과 거룩함으로 워낙 널리 명성을 떨치던 인물이라, 한낱 무대 위 속임수 따위로 세간의 이목을 끌려 한다고는 의심할 수 없었다."], ["No, thought I, there must be some sober reason for this thing; furthermore, it must symbolize something unseen.", "아니다, 하고 나는 생각했다, 여기에는 분명 어떤 진지한 이유가 있을 터이며, 더 나아가 이는 눈에 보이지 않는 무언가를 상징하는 것이 틀림없다."], ["Can it be, then, that by that act of physical isolation, he signifies his spiritual withdrawal for the time, from all outward worldly ties and connexions?", "그렇다면 혹시, 그 육체적 격리의 행위를 통해, 그가 한동안 모든 바깥세상의 끈과 인연으로부터 자신의 영혼이 물러섬을 나타내려는 것은 아닐까?"], ["Yes, for replenished with the meat and wine of the word, to the faithful man of God, this pulpit, I see, is a self-containing stronghold—a lofty Ehrenbreitstein, with a perennial well of water within the walls.", "그렇다, 말씀의 고기와 포도주로 그득히 채워진 하느님의 신실한 사람에게, 이 설교단은, 내가 보기에, 그 자체로 완결된 요새이니 — 성벽 안에 마르지 않는 샘물을 품은 드높은 에렌브라이트슈타인이다."]], [["But the side ladder was not the only strange feature of the place, borrowed from the chaplain’s former sea-farings.", "그러나 측면 사다리만이 이곳에서 목사의 옛 항해 생활에서 빌려 온 유일한 기이한 특징은 아니었다."], ["Between the marble cenotaphs on either hand of the pulpit, the wall which formed its back was adorned with a large painting representing a gallant ship beating against a terrible storm off a lee coast of black rocks and snowy breakers.", "설교단 양옆의 대리석 기념비 사이로, 그 뒤편을 이루는 벽에는 커다란 그림 한 폭이 걸려 있었는데, 검은 바위와 새하얀 파도로 이루어진 바람 받는 해안을 끼고 무시무시한 폭풍과 맞서 싸우는 늠름한 배 한 척을 그린 것이었다."], ["But high above the flying scud and dark-rolling clouds, there floated a little isle of sunlight, from which beamed forth an angel’s face; and this bright face shed a distinct spot of radiance upon the ship’s tossed deck, something like that silver plate now inserted into the Victory’s plank where Nelson fell.", "그러나 흩날리는 물보라와 검게 뒤채는 구름 위 높은 곳에는, 햇살로 이루어진 작은 섬 하나가 떠 있었고, 그곳에서 한 천사의 얼굴이 환히 빛을 발하고 있었다. 그리고 이 밝은 얼굴은 요동치는 배의 갑판 위로 또렷한 빛의 점 하나를 드리웠으니, 넬슨이 쓰러진 자리에 지금도 박혀 있는 빅토리호 갑판 널의 저 은판과도 같았다."], ["“Ah, noble ship,”", "\"아, 고귀한 배여,\""], ["the angel seemed to say, “beat on, beat on, thou noble ship, and bear a hardy helm; for lo!", "천사는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다, \"맞서 나아가라, 맞서 나아가라, 그대 고귀한 배여, 키를 굳세게 잡으라. 보라!"], ["the sun is breaking through; the clouds are rolling off—serenest azure is at hand.”", "해가 뚫고 나오고 있으니, 구름이 걷히고 있으니 — 더없이 맑은 창공이 곧 다가오리라.\""]], [["Nor was the pulpit itself without a trace of the same sea-taste that had achieved the ladder and the picture.", "설교단 자체에도, 사다리와 그림을 빚어낸 그 똑같은 바다의 취향이 깃든 흔적이 없지 않았다."], ["Its panelled front was in the likeness of a ship’s bluff bows, and the Holy Bible rested on a projecting piece of scroll work, fashioned after a ship’s fiddle-headed beak.", "널판으로 짠 그 정면은 배의 불룩한 이물을 닮아 있었고, 성경은 배의 바이올린머리 모양 뱃부리를 본떠 만든, 앞으로 튀어나온 두루마리 장식 위에 놓여 있었다."]], [["What could be more full of meaning?—for the pulpit is ever this earth’s foremost part; all the rest comes in its rear; the pulpit leads the world.", "이보다 더 의미로 가득 찬 것이 또 있겠는가? — 설교단이야말로 늘 이 땅의 가장 앞선 부분이며, 나머지 모든 것은 그 뒤를 따라온다."], ["From thence it is the storm of God’s quick wrath is first descried, and the bow must bear the earliest brunt.", "설교단은 세상을 이끈다. 그곳에서 하느님의 날쌘 진노의 폭풍이 가장 먼저 발견되고, 이물이 그 최초의 맹공을 견뎌 내야 한다."], ["From thence it is the God of breezes fair or foul is first invoked for favourable winds.", "그곳에서 순풍이든 역풍이든 바람을 다스리는 하느님께 순조로운 바람을 비는 기도가 가장 먼저 올려진다."], ["Yes, the world’s a ship on its passage out, and not a voyage complete; and the pulpit is its prow.", "그렇다, 세상은 떠나가는 항해 중인 한 척의 배이지 아직 완수된 항해가 아니며, 설교단은 그 뱃머리다."]], [["# CHAPTER 9. The Sermon.", "# CHAPTER 9. The Sermon."]], [["Father Mapple rose, and in a mild voice of unassuming authority ordered the scattered people to condense.", "매플 신부가 자리에서 일어나, 거드름 없는 권위가 담긴 부드러운 목소리로 흩어져 앉은 사람들에게 한곳으로 모이라고 일렀다."], ["“Starboard gangway, there!", "\"우현 통로에 앉은 분들, 거기!"], ["side away to larboard—larboard gangway to starboard!", "좌현 쪽으로 옮기시오 — 좌현 통로 분들은 우현으로! 한가운데로!"], ["Midships! midships!”", "한가운데로 모이시오!\""]], [["There was a low rumbling of heavy sea-boots among the benches, and a still slighter shuffling of women’s shoes, and all was quiet again, and every eye on the preacher.", "벤치들 사이로 묵직한 바다용 장화들이 낮게 우르릉거리는 소리가 일었고, 그보다 더 가벼운 여인네들의 신발 끄는 소리가 뒤따르더니, 다시금 사방이 고요해졌고, 모든 눈길이 설교자에게로 쏠렸다."]], [["He paused a little; then kneeling in the pulpit’s bows, folded his large brown hands across his chest, uplifted his closed eyes, and offered a prayer so deeply devout that he seemed kneeling and praying at the bottom of the sea.", "그는 잠시 멈추었다. 그러고는 설교단의 뱃머리 쪽에 무릎을 꿇고, 크고 갈색인 두 손을 가슴 위로 포개고, 감은 눈을 위로 들어 올린 채, 어찌나 깊고 경건한 기도를 올리는지, 마치 바다 밑바닥에 무릎 꿇고 기도하는 사람처럼 보였다."]], [["This ended, in prolonged solemn tones, like the continual tolling of a bell in a ship that is foundering at sea in a fog—in such tones he commenced reading the following hymn; but changing his manner towards the concluding stanzas, burst forth with a pealing exultation and joy—", "이 기도가 끝나자, 안개 속에서 바다에 침몰해 가는 배 안에서 끊임없이 울리는 종소리처럼 길게 늘어진 엄숙한 어조로 — 바로 그런 어조로 그는 다음의 찬송가를 읽기 시작했다. 그러나 마지막 연들에 이르러 그 태도를 바꾸어, 종소리 울려 퍼지듯 환희와 기쁨을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“The ribs and terrors in the whale, Arched over me a dismal gloom, While all God’s sun-lit waves rolled by, And lift me deepening down to doom.", "\"고래의 갈빗대와 공포가, 음울한 어둠으로 나를 둘러쳤고, 하느님의 햇살 어린 물결이 모두 곁을 지나 굴러가는 동안, 나를 깊디깊은 멸망으로 끌어내렸네."]], [["“I saw the opening maw of hell, With endless pains and sorrows there; Which none but they that feel can tell— Oh, I was plunging to despair.", "\"나는 지옥의 벌어진 아가리를 보았네, 그 안에 끝없는 고통과 슬픔이 있었으니, 겪어 본 자만이 말할 수 있는 것 — 오, 나는 절망 속으로 곤두박질치고 있었네."]], [["“In black distress, I called my God, When I could scarce believe him mine, He bowed his ear to my complaints— No more the whale did me confine.", "\"검은 고난 속에서 나는 내 하느님을 불렀네, 그분이 내 것이라 차마 믿기 어려운 때에, 그분은 내 탄식에 귀를 기울이셨고 — 더는 고래가 나를 가두지 못했네."]], [["“With speed he flew to my relief, As on a radiant dolphin borne; Awful, yet bright, as lightning shone The face of my Deliverer God.", "\"그분은 빛나는 돌고래에 실린 듯, 나를 구하러 쏜살같이 날아오셨네. 두려우면서도, 번개처럼 밝게 빛났네, 나를 건지신 하느님의 얼굴이."]], [["“My song for ever shall record That terrible, that joyful hour; I give the glory to my God, His all the mercy and the power.”", "\"내 노래는 영원토록 기록하리라, 그 무서웠던, 그 기뻤던 순간을. 나는 내 하느님께 영광을 돌리노니, 모든 자비와 권능이 그분의 것일지라.\""]], [["Nearly all joined in singing this hymn, which swelled high above the howling of the storm.", "거의 모든 사람이 이 찬송에 함께 목소리를 보탰고, 그 소리는 폭풍의 울부짖음을 넘어 드높이 솟아올랐다. 짧은 침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["A brief pause ensued; the preacher slowly turned over the leaves of the Bible, and at last, folding his hand down upon the proper page, said: “Beloved shipmates, clinch the last verse of the first chapter of Jonah—‘And God had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah.’”", "설교자는 성경의 책장을 천천히 넘기더니, 마침내 알맞은 쪽에 손을 펴 짚으며 말했다. \"사랑하는 선원 여러분, 요나서 1장의 마지막 구절을 단단히 새기시오 — '하느님께서 큰 물고기를 예비하사 요나를 삼키게 하셨더라.'\""]], [["“Shipmates, this book, containing only four chapters—four yarns—is one of the smallest strands in the mighty cable of the Scriptures.", "\"선원 여러분, 단 네 장 — 네 가닥의 이야기 — 으로 이루어진 이 책은 성경이라는 그 거대한 밧줄 가운데 가장 가느다란 가닥 중 하나입니다."], ["Yet what depths of the soul does Jonah’s deep sealine sound!", "그러나 요나의 깊은 측심줄이 영혼의 그 얼마나 깊은 곳을 재어 내는지요!"], ["what a pregnant lesson to us is this prophet!", "이 예언자가 우리에게 주는 가르침은 또 얼마나 깊은 뜻을 품고 있는지요!"], ["What a noble thing is that canticle in the fish’s belly!", "물고기 뱃속에서 부른 저 노래는 얼마나 고귀한 것입니까!"], ["How billow-like and boisterously grand!", "얼마나 물결처럼 일렁이며 거세고도 장엄합니까!"], ["We feel the floods surging over us; we sound with him to the kelpy bottom of the waters; sea-weed and all the slime of the sea is about us!", "우리는 홍수가 우리 머리 위로 밀어닥치는 것을 느낍니다. 우리는 그와 함께 해초 가득한 바다 밑바닥까지 측심줄을 내려 봅니다."], ["But what is this lesson that the book of Jonah teaches?", "해초와 바다의 온갖 진흙이 우리를 둘러쌉니다! 그런데 요나서가 가르치는 이 교훈이란 대체 무엇입니까?"], ["Shipmates, it is a two-stranded lesson; a lesson to us all as sinful men, and a lesson to me as a pilot of the living God.", "선원 여러분, 그것은 두 가닥으로 꼬인 교훈입니다. 죄지은 인간으로서 우리 모두에게 주는 교훈이요, 살아 계신 하느님의 키잡이로서 나에게 주는 교훈입니다."], ["As sinful men, it is a lesson to us all, because it is a story of the sin, hard-heartedness, suddenly awakened fears, the swift punishment, repentance, prayers, and finally the deliverance and joy of Jonah.", "죄지은 인간으로서, 이것은 우리 모두에게 주는 교훈이니, 그것은 곧 죄와 완고함과 갑작스레 깨어난 두려움과 신속한 징벌과 회개와 기도, 그리고 마침내 요나가 얻은 구원과 기쁨에 관한 이야기이기 때문입니다."], ["As with all sinners among men, the sin of this son of Amittai was in his wilful disobedience of the command of God—never mind now what that command was, or how conveyed—which he found a hard command.", "사람들 가운데 모든 죄인이 그러하듯, 아미타이의 이 아들이 지은 죄는 하느님의 명령을 제멋대로 거역한 데 있었으니 — 그 명령이 무엇이었는지, 또 어떻게 전해졌는지는 지금 따지지 맙시다 — 그는 그것을 따르기 힘든 명령이라 여겼습니다."], ["But all the things that God would have us do are hard for us to do—remember that—and hence, he oftener commands us than endeavors to persuade.", "그러나 하느님께서 우리에게 행하라 하시는 모든 일은 우리가 행하기에 힘든 법입니다 — 이것을 기억하십시오 — 그러므로 그분은 우리를 설득하려 애쓰기보다 명령을 내리시는 일이 더 잦은 것입니다."], ["And if we obey God, we must disobey ourselves; and it is in this disobeying ourselves, wherein the hardness of obeying God consists.", "그리고 우리가 하느님께 순종하려면, 우리 자신에게는 불복해야 합니다. 그리고 바로 이 자기 자신에게 불복하는 일 가운데, 하느님께 순종하는 일의 어려움이 깃들어 있는 것입니다."]], [["“With this sin of disobedience in him, Jonah still further flouts at God, by seeking to flee from Him.", "\"이 불복종의 죄를 품은 채, 요나는 더 나아가 하느님으로부터 달아나려 함으로써 그분을 조롱합니다."], ["He thinks that a ship made by men will carry him into countries where God does not reign, but only the Captains of this earth.", "그는 사람이 만든 배가 자기를 하느님이 다스리지 않는 나라들, 오직 이 땅의 선장들만이 다스리는 곳으로 데려다 주리라 생각합니다."], ["He skulks about the wharves of Joppa, and seeks a ship that’s bound for Tarshish.", "그는 욥바의 부두 언저리를 몰래 어슬렁거리며, 다시스로 가는 배를 찾습니다."], ["There lurks, perhaps, a hitherto unheeded meaning here.", "어쩌면 여기에 지금껏 누구도 주목하지 않은 어떤 뜻이 숨어 있는지도 모릅니다."], ["By all accounts Tarshish could have been no other city than the modern Cadiz.", "모든 정황으로 보아 다시스는 오늘날의 카디스 말고는 다른 도시일 수 없었습니다."], ["That’s the opinion of learned men.", "그것이 학자들의 견해입니다."], ["And where is Cadiz, shipmates?", "그런데 선원 여러분, 카디스는 어디에 있습니까?"], ["Cadiz is in Spain; as far by water, from Joppa, as Jonah could possibly have sailed in those ancient days, when the Atlantic was an almost unknown sea.", "카디스는 스페인에 있으니, 욥바에서 바닷길로 가자면, 대서양이 아직 거의 알려지지 않은 바다이던 저 옛 시절에 요나가 항해할 수 있었을 가장 먼 거리에 있습니다."], ["Because Joppa, the modern Jaffa, shipmates, is on the most easterly coast of the Mediterranean, the Syrian; and Tarshish or Cadiz more than two thousand miles to the westward from that, just outside the Straits of Gibraltar.", "선원 여러분, 욥바 곧 오늘날의 야파는 지중해에서 가장 동쪽 해안인 시리아 쪽에 있고, 다시스 곧 카디스는 그곳에서 서쪽으로 이천 마일도 넘게 떨어진, 지브롤터 해협 바로 바깥에 있기 때문입니다."], ["See ye not then, shipmates, that Jonah sought to flee world-wide from God?", "그러니 선원 여러분, 요나가 세상 끝까지 하느님으로부터 달아나려 했음을 알지 못하겠습니까?"], ["Miserable man!", "가련한 인간이여!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["most contemptible and worthy of all scorn; with slouched hat and guilty eye, skulking from his God; prowling among the shipping like a vile burglar hastening to cross the seas.", "더없이 경멸스럽고 온갖 조롱을 받아 마땅한 자, 모자를 푹 눌러쓰고 죄지은 눈빛으로 자기 하느님을 피해 몰래 다니며, 바다를 건너려 서두르는 비열한 강도처럼 선박들 사이를 살금살금 헤매는 자입니다."], ["So disordered, self-condemning is his look, that had there been policemen in those days, Jonah, on the mere suspicion of something wrong, had been arrested ere he touched a deck.", "그 행색이 어찌나 어수선하고 스스로를 단죄하는 듯하던지, 그 시절에 순경이 있었다면, 요나는 무언가 잘못이 있으리라는 의심만으로도 갑판에 발을 들여놓기도 전에 체포되었을 것입니다."], ["How plainly he’s a fugitive!", "그가 도망자임이 얼마나 빤히 드러나는지요!"], ["no baggage, not a hat-box, valise, or carpet-bag,—no friends accompany him to the wharf with their adieux.", "짐 보따리도, 모자 상자도, 여행 가방도, 융단 가방도 없고 — 부두까지 작별 인사를 건네며 그를 배웅하는 벗 하나 없습니다."], ["At last, after much dodging search, he finds the Tarshish ship receiving the last items of her cargo; and as he steps on board to see its Captain in the cabin, all the sailors for the moment desist from hoisting in the goods, to mark the stranger’s evil eye.", "마침내 한참을 이리저리 피해 다니며 찾은 끝에, 그는 마지막 화물을 실어 들이는 다시스행 배를 발견합니다. 그리고 그가 선실에서 선장을 만나려고 배에 오르자, 선원들은 모두 잠시 짐 싣는 일을 멈추고 그 낯선 자의 사악한 눈빛을 살핍니다."], ["Jonah sees this; but in vain he tries to look all ease and confidence; in vain essays his wretched smile.", "요나도 이를 알아챕니다. 그러나 아무리 태연하고 자신만만한 척하려 애써도 헛일이요, 그 비참한 미소를 지어 보려 해도 소용이 없습니다."], ["Strong intuitions of the man assure the mariners he can be no innocent.", "뱃사람들은 강렬한 직감으로, 저자가 결코 무고한 자일 리 없다고 확신합니다."], ["In their gamesome but still serious way, one whispers to the other—“Jack, he’s robbed a widow;”", "그들은 장난스러우면서도 자못 진지한 투로 서로 귓속말을 주고받습니다 — '잭, 저자는 과부 재산을 털었어.'"], ["or, “Joe, do you mark him; he’s a bigamist;”", "혹은 '조, 저자를 잘 봐 둬. 중혼한 놈이야.'"], ["or, “Harry lad, I guess he’s the adulterer that broke jail in old Gomorrah, or belike, one of the missing murderers from Sodom.”", "혹은 '해리, 내 짐작엔 저자가 옛 고모라에서 감옥을 깨고 나온 간음한 자거나, 아니면 소돔에서 도망친 살인자들 중 하나일걸.'"], ["Another runs to read the bill that’s stuck against the spile upon the wharf to which the ship is moored, offering five hundred gold coins for the apprehension of a parricide, and containing a description of his person.", "또 다른 한 사람은 배가 매여 있는 부두의 말뚝에 붙은 방을 읽으러 달려가는데, 거기에는 어떤 친부 살해범을 붙잡는 자에게 금화 오백 닢을 준다는 내용과 그 자의 인상착의가 적혀 있습니다."], ["He reads, and looks from Jonah to the bill; while all his sympathetic shipmates now crowd round Jonah, prepared to lay their hands upon him.", "그는 그것을 읽고는, 요나와 방을 번갈아 봅니다. 그러는 사이 동정심 어린 그의 동료들은 이제 요나를 에워싸고, 그에게 손을 대려 합니다."], ["Frighted Jonah trembles, and summoning all his boldness to his face, only looks so much the more a coward.", "겁에 질린 요나는 부들부들 떨며, 온 담력을 얼굴에 끌어모으지만, 그럴수록 더욱더 겁쟁이로만 보일 뿐입니다."], ["He will not confess himself suspected; but that itself is strong suspicion.", "그는 자기가 의심받고 있음을 시인하려 하지 않습니다. 그러나 그것 자체가 더욱 강한 의심을 삽니다."], ["So he makes the best of it; and when the sailors find him not to be the man that is advertised, they let him pass, and he descends into the cabin.", "그래서 그는 어떻게든 그 상황을 모면해 보려 애쓰고, 마침내 뱃사람들이 그가 방에 적힌 그 사람이 아님을 알아내자, 그를 그냥 지나가게 두니, 그는 선실로 내려갑니다."]], [["“‘Who’s there?’ cries the Captain at his busy desk, hurriedly making out his papers for the Customs—‘Who’s there?’ Oh!", "\"'거기 누구요?' 분주한 책상에서 세관에 낼 서류를 서둘러 작성하던 선장이 외칩니다 — '거기 누구요?'"], ["how that harmless question mangles Jonah!", "오! 그 악의 없는 물음이 요나를 얼마나 갈가리 찢어 놓는지요!"], ["For the instant he almost turns to flee again.", "그 순간 그는 다시 달아나려고 거의 몸을 돌릴 뻔합니다. 그러나 마음을 다잡습니다."], ["But he rallies.", "'이 배로 다시스까지 가는 뱃길을 청합니다. 언제 떠나시오?'"], ["‘I seek a passage in this ship to Tarshish; how soon sail ye, sir?’ Thus far the busy Captain had not looked up to Jonah, though the man now stands before him; but no sooner does he hear that hollow voice, than he darts a scrutinizing glance.", "그때까지 분주한 선장은 그 사람이 이제 자기 앞에 서 있는데도 요나를 올려다보지 않았으나, 그 공허한 목소리를 듣자마자 날카로운 눈초리를 던집니다. '다음 밀물 때 떠나오.'"], ["‘We sail with the next coming tide,’ at last he slowly answered, still intently eyeing him.", "마침내 그가 여전히 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 느릿느릿 대답합니다. '그보다 더 빨리는 안 되오, 선장?'"], ["‘No sooner, sir?’—‘Soon enough for any honest man that goes a passenger.’ Ha!", "— '뱃삯 내고 떠나는 점잖은 사람이라면 그쯤이면 충분히 빠르지요.' 하!"], ["Jonah, that’s another stab.", "요나여, 그것이 또 한 번의 비수로구나. 그러나 그는 재빨리 선장의 그 추궁의 냄새를 다른 데로 돌립니다."], ["But he swiftly calls away the Captain from that scent.", "'같이 떠나겠소' — 그가 말합니다 — '뱃삯은 얼마요?"], ["‘I’ll sail with ye,’—he says,—‘the passage money how much is that?—I’ll pay now.’ For it is particularly written, shipmates, as if it were a thing not to be overlooked in this history, ‘that he paid the fare thereof’ ere the craft did sail.", "지금 치르겠소.' 선원 여러분, 이 이야기에서 결코 흘려보아서는 안 될 일이라는 듯이, '그가 그 삯을 치렀다'고 배가 떠나기에 앞서 특별히 기록되어 있기 때문입니다."], ["And taken with the context, this is full of meaning.", "그리고 앞뒤 맥락과 더불어 보면, 이 한 구절은 깊은 뜻으로 가득 차 있습니다."]], [["“Now Jonah’s Captain, shipmates, was one whose discernment detects crime in any, but whose cupidity exposes it only in the penniless.", "\"자, 선원 여러분, 요나의 선장은 누구에게서든 죄를 알아채는 분별력을 지녔으되, 그 탐욕 탓에 한 푼 없는 자에게서만 그 죄를 들춰내는 자였습니다. 선원 여러분, 이 세상에서는 제 길값을 치르는 죄는 통행증도 없이 자유로이 다닐 수 있으나, 미덕은 가난한 자라면 모든 국경에서 가로막힙니다."], ["In this world, shipmates, sin that pays its way can travel freely, and without a passport; whereas Virtue, if a pauper, is stopped at all frontiers.", "그래서 요나의 선장은 그를 드러내 놓고 판단하기에 앞서, 먼저 요나의 지갑 길이를 시험해 보려 합니다. 그는 평소의 세 배에 달하는 삯을 요구하고, 요나는 이에 응합니다."], ["So Jonah’s Captain prepares to test the length of Jonah’s purse, ere he judge him openly.", "그러자 선장은 요나가 도망자임을 알아챕니다. 그러나 동시에, 그 뒤를 황금으로 깔아 주는 도주라면 도와주기로 마음먹습니다."], ["He charges him thrice the usual sum; and it’s assented to.", "그런데도 요나가 막상 지갑을 꺼내자, 신중한 의심이 여전히 선장을 괴롭힙니다."], ["Then the Captain knows that Jonah is a fugitive; but at the same time resolves to help a flight that paves its rear with gold.", "그는 위조 화폐가 섞였나 싶어 동전을 하나하나 울려 봅니다. '어쨌든 위조범은 아니로군'"], ["Yet when Jonah fairly takes out his purse, prudent suspicions still molest the Captain.", "하고 그가 중얼거리고는, 요나의 뱃삯이 치러진 것으로 처리됩니다. '내 선실을 일러 주시오, 선장'"], ["He rings every coin to find a counterfeit.", "하고 이제 요나가 말합니다. '여행에 지쳤소."], ["Not a forger, any way, he mutters; and Jonah is put down for his passage.", "잠을 좀 자야겠소.' '그래 보이는구려'"], ["‘Point out my state-room, Sir,’ says Jonah now, ‘I’m travel-weary; I need sleep.’ ‘Thou lookest like it,’ says the Captain, ‘there’s thy room.’ Jonah enters, and would lock the door, but the lock contains no key.", "하고 선장이 말합니다. '저기가 당신 방이오.'"], ["Hearing him foolishly fumbling there, the Captain laughs lowly to himself, and mutters something about the doors of convicts’ cells being never allowed to be locked within.", "요나가 들어가서 문을 잠그려 하지만, 자물쇠에 열쇠가 없습니다. 그가 그곳에서 어리석게 더듬거리는 소리를 듣고는, 선장이 혼자 나직이 웃으며, 죄수들의 감방 문은 결코 안에서 잠그도록 허락되지 않는다는 식으로 무어라 중얼거립니다."], ["All dressed and dusty as he is, Jonah throws himself into his berth, and finds the little state-room ceiling almost resting on his forehead.", "옷도 다 입은 채, 먼지투성이 그대로, 요나는 자기 침상에 몸을 던지고는, 그 작은 선실 천장이 거의 자기 이마에 닿을 듯한 것을 알아챕니다."], ["The air is close, and Jonah gasps.", "공기는 답답하고, 요나는 숨을 헐떡입니다."], ["Then, in that contracted hole, sunk, too, beneath the ship’s water-line, Jonah feels the heralding presentiment of that stifling hour, when the whale shall hold him in the smallest of his bowels’ wards.", "그러더니 그 비좁은 구멍 속에서, 그것도 배의 흘수선 아래로 가라앉은 그곳에서, 요나는 고래가 그 내장의 가장 작은 방 안에 자기를 가둘 그 숨 막히는 순간을 미리 알리는 전조를 느낍니다."]], [["“Screwed at its axis against the side, a swinging lamp slightly oscillates in Jonah’s room; and the ship, heeling over towards the wharf with the weight of the last bales received, the lamp, flame and all, though in slight motion, still maintains a permanent obliquity with reference to the room; though, in truth, infallibly straight itself, it but made obvious the false, lying levels among which it hung.", "\"제 축을 따라 뱃전에 고정된 채, 흔들리는 등불 하나가 요나의 방에서 가볍게 흔들립니다. 그리고 마지막으로 실어 들인 짐짝들의 무게로 배가 부두 쪽으로 기울자, 등불은 불꽃까지 통째로, 비록 미세하게 흔들리기는 하나, 방을 기준으로 보면 한결같이 비스듬한 각도를 유지합니다."], ["The lamp alarms and frightens Jonah; as lying in his berth his tormented eyes roll round the place, and this thus far successful fugitive finds no refuge for his restless glance.", "사실 등불 자체는 한 치의 오차도 없이 곧건만, 다만 그것이 매달린 그 거짓되고 기만적인 수평면들을 도리어 또렷이 드러내 보일 뿐입니다. 그 등불이 요나를 놀라게 하고 겁먹게 합니다."], ["But that contradiction in the lamp more and more appals him.", "침상에 누운 채 그의 괴로운 눈이 방 안을 굴러다니지만, 여기까지 용케 달아나 온 이 도망자는 그 불안한 시선을 둘 안식처를 찾지 못합니다. 그러나 그 등불의 저 모순이 점점 더 그를 소름 끼치게 합니다."], ["The floor, the ceiling, and the side, are all awry.", "바닥도, 천장도, 뱃전도, 모두가 비뚜름합니다. '오!"], ["‘Oh!", "내 양심이 내 안에 이렇게 매달려 있구나!'"], ["so my conscience hangs in me!’ he groans, ‘straight upwards, so it burns; but the chambers of my soul are all in crookedness!’", "하고 그가 신음합니다. '곧장 위를 향하여, 그렇게 타오르건만, 내 영혼의 방들은 모조리 비뚤어져 있구나!'"]], [["“Like one who after a night of drunken revelry hies to his bed, still reeling, but with conscience yet pricking him, as the plungings of the Roman race-horse but so much the more strike his steel tags into him; as one who in that miserable plight still turns and turns in giddy anguish, praying God for annihilation until the fit be passed; and at last amid the whirl of woe he feels, a deep stupor steals over him, as over the man who bleeds to death, for conscience is the wound, and there’s naught to staunch it; so, after sore wrestlings in his berth, Jonah’s prodigy of ponderous misery drags him drowning down to sleep.", "\"밤새도록 술 취해 흥청거린 끝에, 여전히 비틀거리면서도 양심이 그를 콕콕 찌르는 채로 잠자리로 기어드는 사람처럼 — 로마 경주마가 날뛸수록 그 강철 미늘이 더욱더 살을 파고드는 것처럼 — 그 비참한 처지에서 발작이 지나가기만을 빌며, 하느님께 차라리 자기를 없애 달라 기도하면서 어지러운 고뇌 속에 자꾸만 몸을 뒤척이는 사람처럼, 그러다 마침내 그 비탄의 소용돌이 한가운데서 깊은 혼곤함이 그를 덮쳐 오는 것을 느끼는 사람처럼 — 피를 흘리며 죽어 가는 사람을 덮치는 그 혼곤함처럼, 양심이야말로 그 상처요, 그것을 멎게 할 무엇도 없으니 — 그렇게, 요나는 침상에서 모진 몸부림 끝에, 그 엄청난 비참함의 무게에 짓눌려, 익사하듯 잠 속으로 끌려 내려갑니다."]], [["“And now the time of tide has come; the ship casts off her cables; and from the deserted wharf the uncheered ship for Tarshish, all careening, glides to sea.", "\"그리고 이제 밀물 때가 왔습니다. 배는 닻줄을 풀고, 인적 없는 부두로부터 그 다시스행 배는 환송하는 이 하나 없이, 온통 기우뚱거리며 바다로 미끄러져 나갑니다."], ["That ship, my friends, was the first of recorded smugglers!", "벗들이여, 그 배야말로 기록에 남은 최초의 밀항선이었습니다!"], ["the contraband was Jonah.", "그 밀수품은 바로 요나였지요."], ["But the sea rebels; he will not bear the wicked burden.", "그러나 바다가 반항합니다. 바다는 그 사악한 짐을 견디려 하지 않습니다."], ["A dreadful storm comes on, the ship is like to break.", "무시무시한 폭풍이 닥쳐오고, 배는 곧 부서질 듯합니다."], ["But now when the boatswain calls all hands to lighten her; when boxes, bales, and jars are clattering overboard; when the wind is shrieking, and the men are yelling, and every plank thunders with trampling feet right over Jonah’s head; in all this raging tumult, Jonah sleeps his hideous sleep.", "그러나 이제 갑판장이 배를 가볍게 하려고 모든 선원을 불러 모을 때, 상자며 짐짝이며 항아리가 뱃전 너머로 요란하게 던져질 때, 바람이 비명을 지르고 사람들이 고함을 치며, 요나의 머리 바로 위로 갑판 널빤지마다 짓밟는 발길에 천둥처럼 울려 댈 때 — 이 모든 미쳐 날뛰는 아수라장 속에서도, 요나는 그 끔찍한 잠을 자고 있습니다."], ["He sees no black sky and raging sea, feels not the reeling timbers, and little hears he or heeds he the far rush of the mighty whale, which even now with open mouth is cleaving the seas after him.", "그는 검은 하늘도 미쳐 날뛰는 바다도 보지 못하고, 휘청거리는 선재도 느끼지 못하며, 바로 지금 입을 떡 벌린 채 그를 쫓아 바다를 가르며 다가오는 저 거대한 고래의 먼 돌진 소리도 거의 듣지도, 아랑곳하지도 않습니다."], ["Aye, shipmates, Jonah was gone down into the sides of the ship—a berth in the cabin as I have taken it, and was fast asleep.", "그렇습니다, 선원 여러분, 요나는 배의 옆구리 안쪽으로 — 내가 받아들이기로는 선실 안의 침상으로 — 내려가, 깊이 잠들어 있었던 것입니다."], ["But the frightened master comes to him, and shrieks in his dead ear, ‘What meanest thou, O, sleeper!", "그러나 겁에 질린 선장이 그에게 다가와, 송장처럼 잠든 그의 귀에 대고 소리칩니다. '오, 잠든 자여, 어찌하여 이러고 있느냐!"], ["arise!’ Startled from his lethargy by that direful cry, Jonah staggers to his feet, and stumbling to the deck, grasps a shroud, to look out upon the sea.", "일어나라!' 그 무시무시한 외침에 혼수에서 화들짝 깨어난 요나는 비틀비틀 일어나, 휘청거리며 갑판으로 나가 돛 받침줄 하나를 움켜쥐고 바다를 내다봅니다."], ["But at that moment he is sprung upon by a panther billow leaping over the bulwarks.", "그러나 바로 그 순간, 뱃전 위로 뛰어오른 표범 같은 큰 물결이 그를 덮칩니다."], ["Wave after wave thus leaps into the ship, and finding no speedy vent runs roaring fore and aft, till the mariners come nigh to drowning while yet afloat.", "이렇게 파도가 잇따라 배 안으로 뛰어들지만, 빠져나갈 길을 찾지 못해 뱃머리부터 고물까지 으르렁대며 내달리니, 뱃사람들은 아직 물 위에 떠 있는 채로도 익사할 지경에 이릅니다."], ["And ever, as the white moon shows her affrighted face from the steep gullies in the blackness overhead, aghast Jonah sees the rearing bowsprit pointing high upward, but soon beat downward again towards the tormented deep.", "그리고 그럴 때마다, 머리 위 어둠의 가파른 골짜기에서 새하얀 달이 그 겁먹은 얼굴을 내밀면, 넋이 나간 요나는 솟구쳐 오른 제1사장(이물 돛대)이 하늘 높이 곧추 솟았다가도 이내 다시 저 고통스러운 심연을 향해 곤두박질치는 것을 봅니다."]], [["“Terrors upon terrors run shouting through his soul.", "\"공포 위에 또 공포가 그의 영혼을 가로질러 소리치며 내달립니다."], ["In all his cringing attitudes, the God-fugitive is now too plainly known.", "그 온갖 움츠린 꼴로 인해, 하느님으로부터 도망친 자임이 이제 너무도 빤히 드러납니다."], ["The sailors mark him; more and more certain grow their suspicions of him, and at last, fully to test the truth, by referring the whole matter to high Heaven, they fall to casting lots, to see for whose cause this great tempest was upon them.", "뱃사람들은 그를 눈여겨봅니다. 그를 향한 의심은 점점 더 짙어지고, 마침내 진상을 온전히 가려내고자, 그 모든 일을 드높은 하늘에 맡겨, 이 거대한 폭풍이 누구 탓으로 그들에게 닥쳤는지 알아보려고 제비를 뽑기 시작합니다."], ["The lot is Jonah’s; that discovered, then how furiously they mob him with their questions.", "제비는 요나에게 떨어집니다. 그것이 밝혀지자, 그들은 얼마나 사납게 그를 에워싸고 질문을 퍼붓는지요."], ["‘What is thine occupation?", "'네 직업이 무엇이냐?"], ["Whence comest thou?", "어디서 왔느냐?"], ["Thy country?", "네 나라는?"], ["What people?", "어느 백성이냐?'"], ["But mark now, my shipmates, the behavior of poor Jonah.", "그런데 선원 여러분, 이제 가련한 요나의 행동을 눈여겨보십시오."], ["The eager mariners but ask him who he is, and where from; whereas, they not only receive an answer to those questions, but likewise another answer to a question not put by them, but the unsolicited answer is forced from Jonah by the hard hand of God that is upon him.", "다급한 뱃사람들은 다만 그가 누구이며 어디서 왔는지를 물을 뿐인데, 그들은 그 물음에 대한 답을 받을 뿐 아니라, 그들이 묻지도 않은 또 다른 물음에 대한 답까지 받게 됩니다. 그러나 그 청하지도 않은 답은, 그를 짓누르는 하느님의 무거운 손에 떠밀려 요나에게서 억지로 터져 나온 것입니다."]], [["“‘I am a Hebrew,’ he cries—and then—‘I fear the Lord the God of Heaven who hath made the sea and the dry land!’ Fear him, O Jonah?", "\"'나는 히브리 사람이오' 하고 그가 외칩니다 — 그러고는 — '나는 바다와 마른 땅을 지으신 하늘의 주 하느님을 경외하오!'"], ["Aye, well mightest thou fear the Lord God then!", "요나여, 그분을 경외한다고? 그렇다, 그때야말로 너는 주 하느님을 경외함이 마땅하렷다!"], ["Straightway, he now goes on to make a full confession; whereupon the mariners became more and more appalled, but still are pitiful.", "곧바로 그는 이제 온전한 고백을 이어 갑니다. 그러자 뱃사람들은 점점 더 소름이 돋지만, 그래도 여전히 가엾이 여깁니다."], ["For when Jonah, not yet supplicating God for mercy, since he but too well knew the darkness of his deserts,—when wretched Jonah cries out to them to take him and cast him forth into the sea, for he knew that for his sake this great tempest was upon them; they mercifully turn from him, and seek by other means to save the ship.", "요나가 — 자기가 받아 마땅한 어둠을 너무도 잘 알았기에 아직 하느님께 자비를 빌지도 못한 채 — 가련한 요나가, 자기를 들어 바다에 던지라고, 이 거대한 폭풍이 바로 자기 탓으로 그들에게 닥친 것임을 알기에 그렇게 그들에게 외칠 때, 그들은 자비롭게도 그에게서 돌아서서, 다른 방도로 배를 구하려 애씁니다. 그러나 모두 헛일입니다."], ["But all in vain; the indignant gale howls louder; then, with one hand raised invokingly to God, with the other they not unreluctantly lay hold of Jonah.", "분노한 강풍이 더욱 거세게 울부짖습니다. 그러자 그들은 한 손을 하느님께 간구하듯 치켜들고, 다른 손으로는 마지못한 것만도 아닌 손길로 요나를 붙잡습니다."]], [["“And now behold Jonah taken up as an anchor and dropped into the sea; when instantly an oily calmness floats out from the east, and the sea is still, as Jonah carries down the gale with him, leaving smooth water behind.", "\"그리고 이제 보십시오, 요나가 닻처럼 들려 올려져 바다에 던져지는 것을. 그러자 곧바로 동쪽으로부터 기름처럼 매끄러운 고요가 떠 와, 요나가 그 강풍을 안고 가라앉아 뒤에 잔잔한 물을 남기자, 바다가 잠잠해집니다."], ["He goes down in the whirling heart of such a masterless commotion that he scarce heeds the moment when he drops seething into the yawning jaws awaiting him; and the whale shoots-to all his ivory teeth, like so many white bolts, upon his prison.", "그는 그 누구도 다스리지 못할 격동의 소용돌이치는 한복판으로 내려가니, 자기를 기다리며 떡 벌린 아가리 속으로 부글부글 끓으며 떨어져 드는 그 순간조차 거의 알아채지 못합니다. 그리고 고래는 그 모든 상아 같은 이빨을, 마치 수많은 흰 빗장처럼, 자기 감옥 위로 탁 다물어 버립니다."], ["Then Jonah prayed unto the Lord out of the fish’s belly.", "그러자 요나는 물고기 뱃속에서 주께 기도했습니다."], ["But observe his prayer, and learn a weighty lesson.", "그러나 그 기도를 눈여겨보고, 묵직한 교훈을 배우십시오."], ["For sinful as he is, Jonah does not weep and wail for direct deliverance.", "죄인이건만, 요나는 곧장 구원해 달라고 울며 통곡하지 않습니다."], ["He feels that his dreadful punishment is just.", "그는 자기가 받는 그 무서운 징벌이 마땅한 것임을 느낍니다."], ["He leaves all his deliverance to God, contenting himself with this, that spite of all his pains and pangs, he will still look towards His holy temple.", "그는 자기 구원을 온전히 하느님께 맡긴 채, 다만 이것으로 만족합니다. 곧, 그 온갖 아픔과 고통에도 불구하고, 그래도 여전히 주의 거룩한 성전을 향해 바라보겠노라는 것입니다."], ["And here, shipmates, is true and faithful repentance; not clamorous for pardon, but grateful for punishment.", "그리고 선원 여러분, 바로 여기에 참되고 신실한 회개가 있습니다. 용서를 구하느라 떠들썩한 것이 아니라, 징벌을 감사히 받아들이는 것입니다."], ["And how pleasing to God was this conduct in Jonah, is shown in the eventual deliverance of him from the sea and the whale.", "요나의 이러한 처신이 하느님께 얼마나 흡족했는지는, 그가 끝내 바다와 고래로부터 구원받은 일에서 드러납니다."], ["Shipmates, I do not place Jonah before you to be copied for his sin but I do place him before you as a model for repentance.", "선원 여러분, 나는 요나를 그 죄를 본받으라고 여러분 앞에 세우는 것이 아니라, 회개의 본보기로서 여러분 앞에 세우는 것입니다. 죄짓지 마십시오."], ["Sin not; but if you do, take heed to repent of it like Jonah.”", "그러나 만일 죄를 짓거든, 요나처럼 그것을 회개하도록 마음 쓰십시오.\""]], [["While he was speaking these words, the howling of the shrieking, slanting storm without seemed to add new power to the preacher, who, when describing Jonah’s sea-storm, seemed tossed by a storm himself.", "그가 이 말들을 하고 있는 동안, 바깥에서 비스듬히 몰아치며 비명을 지르는 폭풍의 울부짖음이 이 설교자에게 새로운 힘을 더해 주는 듯했으니, 요나의 바다 폭풍을 그려 낼 때면 그 자신이 폭풍에 내던져진 듯 보였다."], ["His deep chest heaved as with a ground-swell; his tossed arms seemed the warring elements at work; and the thunders that rolled away from off his swarthy brow, and the light leaping from his eye, made all his simple hearers look on him with a quick fear that was strange to them.", "그의 깊은 가슴팍은 너울이 일렁이듯 들썩였고, 내던지듯 휘젓는 두 팔은 서로 다투는 자연의 원소들이 일하는 것만 같았으며, 거무스름한 이마에서 굴러 나오는 천둥과 눈에서 튀어 오르는 빛은, 소박한 청중 모두로 하여금 그들에게는 생소한 어떤 빠른 두려움을 품고 그를 바라보게 했다."]], [["There now came a lull in his look, as he silently turned over the leaves of the Book once more; and, at last, standing motionless, with closed eyes, for the moment, seemed communing with God and himself.", "이제 그의 표정에 잠시 잔잔함이 깃들었으니, 그가 말없이 다시 한번 성경의 책장을 넘기더니, 마침내 눈을 감은 채 잠시 미동도 없이 서서, 하느님과 자기 자신과 더불어 교감하는 듯 보였다."]], [["But again he leaned over towards the people, and bowing his head lowly, with an aspect of the deepest yet manliest humility, he spake these words:", "그러나 그는 다시금 사람들 쪽으로 몸을 기울이더니, 머리를 깊이 숙이고, 더없이 깊으면서도 가장 사내다운 겸손의 빛을 띤 채, 이런 말을 했다."]], [["“Shipmates, God has laid but one hand upon you; both his hands press upon me.", "\"선원 여러분, 하느님께서는 여러분에게는 한 손만을 얹으셨으나, 나에게는 두 손을 다 누르고 계십니다."], ["I have read ye by what murky light may be mine the lesson that Jonah teaches to all sinners; and therefore to ye, and still more to me, for I am a greater sinner than ye.", "나는 나에게 허락된 그 흐릿한 빛으로나마, 요나가 모든 죄인에게 가르치는 그 교훈을 여러분에게 읽어 드렸습니다. 그러므로 여러분에게도, 그러나 나에게는 더더욱 그러하니, 나야말로 여러분보다 더 큰 죄인이기 때문입니다."], ["And now how gladly would I come down from this mast-head and sit on the hatches there where you sit, and listen as you listen, while some one of you reads me that other and more awful lesson which Jonah teaches to me, as a pilot of the living God.", "그러니 이제 나는 이 돛대 꼭대기에서 기꺼이 내려와, 여러분이 앉은 저 승강구 위에 함께 앉아, 여러분이 귀 기울이듯 귀를 기울이며, 여러분 가운데 누군가가 나에게 저 또 하나의, 한층 더 무서운 교훈을 읽어 주기를 얼마나 바라는지 모릅니다 — 살아 계신 하느님의 키잡이인 나에게 요나가 가르치는 그 교훈을 말입니다."], ["How being an anointed pilot-prophet, or speaker of true things, and bidden by the Lord to sound those unwelcome truths in the ears of a wicked Nineveh, Jonah, appalled at the hostility he should raise, fled from his mission, and sought to escape his duty and his God by taking ship at Joppa.", "기름 부음 받은 키잡이 예언자, 곧 참된 것을 전하는 자로서, 주께 명을 받아 그 달갑잖은 진리를 사악한 니네베의 귀에 울려 퍼뜨려야 했건만, 요나는 자기가 불러일으킬 적의에 겁을 먹고, 제 사명에서 달아나, 욥바에서 배를 탐으로써 자기 의무와 자기 하느님을 피하려 했던 것입니다. 그러나 하느님은 어디에나 계십니다."], ["But God is everywhere; Tarshish he never reached.", "그는 다시스에 끝내 닿지 못했습니다."], ["As we have seen, God came upon him in the whale, and swallowed him down to living gulfs of doom, and with swift slantings tore him along ‘into the midst of the seas,’ where the eddying depths sucked him ten thousand fathoms down, and ‘the weeds were wrapped about his head,’ and all the watery world of woe bowled over him.", "우리가 보았듯이, 하느님은 고래의 모습으로 그에게 닥쳐와 그를 살아 있는 멸망의 심연으로 삼켜 버리시고, 재빠른 비스듬한 움직임으로 그를 '바다 한가운데로' 끌고 가셨으니, 거기서 소용돌이치는 심연이 그를 만 길 아래로 빨아들였고, '바닷풀이 그의 머리를 휘감았으며', 비탄으로 가득 찬 온 물의 세계가 그의 위로 굴러 넘쳤습니다."], ["Yet even then beyond the reach of any plummet—‘out of the belly of hell’—when the whale grounded upon the ocean’s utmost bones, even then, God heard the engulphed, repenting prophet when he cried.", "그러나 그때에도, 어떤 측심추도 닿지 못할 곳에서 — '지옥의 뱃속에서' — 고래가 대양의 가장 깊은 뼈대에 닿았을 그때조차도, 하느님은 그 삼켜진 채 회개하는 예언자가 부르짖을 때 그 소리를 들으셨습니다."], ["Then God spake unto the fish; and from the shuddering cold and blackness of the sea, the whale came breeching up towards the warm and pleasant sun, and all the delights of air and earth; and ‘vomited out Jonah upon the dry land;’ when the word of the Lord came a second time; and Jonah, bruised and beaten—his ears, like two sea-shells, still multitudinously murmuring of the ocean—Jonah did the Almighty’s bidding.", "그러자 하느님이 물고기에게 말씀하셨고, 바다의 몸서리치는 차가움과 어둠으로부터, 고래가 따뜻하고 정겨운 햇빛을 향해, 그리고 공기와 땅의 온갖 기쁨을 향해 솟구쳐 올라, '요나를 마른 땅에 토해 냈습니다.' 그때 주의 말씀이 두 번째로 임했고, 멍들고 두들겨 맞은 요나는 — 그 귀가 두 개의 소라껍데기처럼 여전히 무수히 바다를 웅얼대는 가운데 — 전능하신 분의 분부를 행했습니다."], ["And what was that, shipmates?", "그런데 그것이 무엇이었습니까, 선원 여러분?"], ["To preach the Truth to the face of Falsehood!", "거짓의 면전에 대고 진리를 설교하는 것!"], ["That was it!", "바로 그것이었습니다!"]], [["“This, shipmates, this is that other lesson; and woe to that pilot of the living God who slights it.", "\"선원 여러분, 이것이, 이것이 바로 저 또 하나의 교훈입니다. 그리고 살아 계신 하느님의 키잡이로서 이를 가벼이 여기는 자에게 화가 있을지어다."], ["Woe to him whom this world charms from Gospel duty!", "이 세상이 복음의 의무로부터 꾀어내는 그 자에게 화가 있을지어다!"], ["Woe to him who seeks to pour oil upon the waters when God has brewed them into a gale!", "하느님께서 물을 휘저어 강풍으로 끓이셨는데도 그 물 위에 기름을 부으려 하는 자에게 화가 있을지어다!"], ["Woe to him who seeks to please rather than to appal!", "소름 끼치게 하기보다 비위를 맞추려 드는 자에게 화가 있을지어다!"], ["Woe to him whose good name is more to him than goodness!", "선함보다 제 명성을 더 소중히 여기는 자에게 화가 있을지어다!"], ["Woe to him who, in this world, courts not dishonor!", "이 세상에서 불명예를 자청하지 않는 자에게 화가 있을지어다!"], ["Woe to him who would not be true, even though to be false were salvation!", "거짓됨이 곧 구원이 되는 한이 있더라도, 참되려 하지 않는 자에게 화가 있을지어다!"], ["Yea, woe to him who, as the great Pilot Paul has it, while preaching to others is himself a castaway!”", "그렇습니다, 위대한 키잡이 바울이 말했듯이, 남들에게 설교하면서 정작 자기 자신은 버림받은 자가 되는 그 자에게 화가 있을지어다!\""]], [["He dropped and fell away from himself for a moment; then lifting his face to them again, showed a deep joy in his eyes, as he cried out with a heavenly enthusiasm,—“But oh!", "그는 잠시 풀썩 주저앉아 제정신을 놓은 듯하더니, 다시 그들에게 얼굴을 들어, 두 눈에 깊은 기쁨을 내보이며, 천상의 열정으로 외쳤다 — \"그러나 오!"], ["shipmates!", "선원 여러분!"], ["on the starboard hand of every woe, there is a sure delight; and higher the top of that delight, than the bottom of the woe is deep.", "모든 화의 우현 쪽에는, 확실한 기쁨이 하나 있습니다. 그리고 그 기쁨의 꼭대기는 그 화의 밑바닥이 깊은 것보다 더 높습니다."], ["Is not the main-truck higher than the kelson is low?", "대장 돛대 꼭대기가 용골 받침대의 낮음보다 더 높지 않습니까?"], ["Delight is to him—a far, far upward, and inward delight—who against the proud gods and commodores of this earth, ever stands forth his own inexorable self.", "기쁨은 그에게 있습니다 — 저 멀고도 드높은, 그리고 안으로 깊은 기쁨은 — 이 땅의 오만한 신들과 사령관들에 맞서, 언제나 굽힐 줄 모르는 제 자신으로 우뚝 서는 그에게 있습니다."], ["Delight is to him whose strong arms yet support him, when the ship of this base treacherous world has gone down beneath him.", "기쁨은, 이 비열하고 음흉한 세상이라는 배가 그의 발밑에서 가라앉아 버렸을 때에도, 여전히 그를 떠받쳐 주는 튼튼한 두 팔을 지닌 그에게 있습니다."], ["Delight is to him, who gives no quarter in the truth, and kills, burns, and destroys all sin though he pluck it out from under the robes of Senators and Judges.", "기쁨은, 진리에 있어서는 한 치도 봐주지 않으며, 비록 원로원 의원들과 재판관들의 법복 자락 밑에서 그것을 끄집어내야 할지라도 모든 죄를 죽이고 불사르고 부수어 버리는 그에게 있습니다."], ["Delight,—top-gallant delight is to him, who acknowledges no law or lord, but the Lord his God, and is only a patriot to heaven.", "기쁨은, 곧 가장 높은 돛대 꼭대기의 기쁨은, 주 하느님 그분 말고는 그 어떤 법도 주인도 인정하지 않으며, 오직 하늘에만 충성하는 그에게 있습니다."], ["Delight is to him, whom all the waves of the billows of the seas of the boisterous mob can never shake from this sure Keel of the Ages.", "기쁨은, 거세게 날뛰는 군중이라는 바다의 그 큰 물결의 온갖 파도로도 결코 흔들어 놓을 수 없는, 만세반석이라는 저 든든한 용골 위에 굳건한 그에게 있습니다."], ["And eternal delight and deliciousness will be his, who coming to lay him down, can say with his final breath—O Father!—chiefly known to me by Thy rod—mortal or immortal, here I die.", "그리고 영원한 기쁨과 감미로움은, 마침내 누우러 와서, 마지막 숨결로 이렇게 말할 수 있는 그의 것이 되리니 — 오 아버지여! — 무엇보다 당신의 채찍으로써 제가 알게 된 분이여 — 죽을 몸이든 죽지 않을 몸이든, 저 여기서 죽나이다."], ["I have striven to be Thine, more than to be this world’s, or mine own.", "저는 이 세상의 것이 되기보다, 또 제 자신의 것이 되기보다, 당신의 것이 되고자 애써 왔나이다."], ["Yet this is nothing: I leave eternity to Thee; for what is man that he should live out the lifetime of his God?”", "그러나 이것조차 아무것도 아니니, 영원은 당신께 맡기나이다. 한낱 인간이 무엇이기에 제 하느님의 생애만큼 길이 살기를 바라겠나이까?\""]], [["He said no more, but slowly waving a benediction, covered his face with his hands, and so remained kneeling, till all the people had departed, and he was left alone in the place.", "그는 더는 말하지 않고, 다만 천천히 손을 저어 축복을 내린 뒤, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고, 그렇게 무릎 꿇은 채로 머물러 있었으니, 사람들이 모두 떠나가고 그 자리에 그 혼자만 남을 때까지 그러했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 10. A Bosom Friend.", "# CHAPTER 10. A Bosom Friend."]], [["Returning to the Spouter-Inn from the Chapel, I found Queequeg there quite alone; he having left the Chapel before the benediction some time.", "예배당에서 스파우터 여관으로 돌아와 보니 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 혼자 있었다. 그는 축도가 끝나기도 전에 한참 일찍 예배당을 떠나온 것이었다."], ["He was sitting on a bench before the fire, with his feet on the stove hearth, and in one hand was holding close up to his face that little negro idol of his; peering hard into its face, and with a jack-knife gently whittling away at its nose, meanwhile humming to himself in his heathenish way.", "그는 난로 앞 벤치에 앉아 두 발을 화덕 위에 올려놓고, 한 손으로는 그 작은 흑인 우상을 얼굴 가까이 바싹 들이댄 채, 그 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 잭나이프로 코를 살살 깎아 내고 있었다. 그러면서 그는 이교도 특유의 곡조를 혼자 흥얼거렸다."]], [["But being now interrupted, he put up the image; and pretty soon, going to the table, took up a large book there, and placing it on his lap began counting the pages with deliberate regularity; at every fiftieth page—as I fancied—stopping a moment, looking vacantly around him, and giving utterance to a long-drawn gurgling whistle of astonishment. He would then begin again at the next fifty; seeming to commence at number one each time, as though he could not count more than fifty, and it was only by such a large number of fifties being found together, that his astonishment at the multitude of pages was excited.", "그러나 이제 방해를 받자 그는 그 상을 치워 두었다. 곧이어 그는 탁자로 가서 거기 놓인 커다란 책 한 권을 집어 들고, 그것을 무릎 위에 올려놓더니 신중하고 규칙적인 손놀림으로 책장을 세기 시작했다. 쉰 장째에 이를 때마다—내가 보기엔 그랬다—잠시 멈추고는 멍하니 사방을 둘러보며, 길게 끌어 올리는 듯한 경탄의 휘파람을 그르렁그르렁 내뱉었다. 그러고는 다시 다음 쉰 장을 세기 시작하는데, 매번 처음 하나부터 다시 세는 듯했다. 마치 쉰까지밖에는 셀 줄 모르며, 그렇게 많은 쉰 묶음이 한데 모여 있는 것을 발견했을 때라야 비로소 책장의 엄청난 수에 대한 그의 경탄이 솟아나는 것 같았다."]], [["With much interest I sat watching him.", "나는 자못 흥미를 느끼며 앉아서 그를 지켜보았다."], ["Savage though he was, and hideously marred about the face—at least to my taste—his countenance yet had a something in it which was by no means disagreeable.", "비록 그가 야만인이었고, 얼굴은—적어도 내 취향에는—흉측하게 일그러져 있었지만, 그 용모에는 결코 거슬리지 않는 무언가가 깃들어 있었다."], ["You cannot hide the soul.", "영혼은 감출 수가 없는 법이다."], ["Through all his unearthly tattooings, I thought I saw the traces of a simple honest heart; and in his large, deep eyes, fiery black and bold, there seemed tokens of a spirit that would dare a thousand devils.", "그 모든 기괴한 문신 너머로, 나는 소박하고 정직한 마음의 자취를 본 듯했다. 그리고 그 크고 깊은 눈, 불길처럼 검고 담대한 그 눈에는 천 마리 악마라도 맞설 만한 정신의 징표가 어려 있는 듯했다."], ["And besides all this, there was a certain lofty bearing about the Pagan, which even his uncouthness could not altogether maim.", "게다가 이 모든 것 위에, 그 이교도에게는 어떤 고결한 기품이 있어서, 그의 거친 외양으로도 그것을 온전히 망가뜨릴 수는 없었다."], ["He looked like a man who had never cringed and never had had a creditor.", "그는 한 번도 굽실거려 본 적 없고, 빚쟁이를 두어 본 적도 없는 사람처럼 보였다."], ["Whether it was, too, that his head being shaved, his forehead was drawn out in freer and brighter relief, and looked more expansive than it otherwise would, this I will not venture to decide; but certain it was his head was phrenologically an excellent one.", "또한 그의 머리가 빡빡 깎인 까닭에 이마가 한결 시원하고 환하게 드러나, 본래보다 더 넓어 보이는 것인지, 그것은 내가 감히 단정하지 않겠다. 그러나 그의 머리가 골상학적으로 빼어난 두상임은 분명했다."], ["It may seem ridiculous, but it reminded me of General Washington’s head, as seen in the popular busts of him.", "우습게 들릴지 모르나, 그것은 흔히 보는 흉상 속 워싱턴(Washington) 장군의 머리를 떠올리게 했다."], ["It had the same long regularly graded retreating slope from above the brows, which were likewise very projecting, like two long promontories thickly wooded on top.", "똑같이 눈썹 위에서부터 길고 규칙적으로 단계를 이루며 뒤로 물러나는 경사를 지녔는데, 그 눈썹 역시 매우 도드라져, 마치 꼭대기에 나무가 빽빽이 우거진 두 개의 긴 곶 같았다."], ["Queequeg was George Washington cannibalistically developed.", "퀴케그는 식인종 식으로 발현된 조지 워싱턴(George Washington)이었다."]], [["Whilst I was thus closely scanning him, half-pretending meanwhile to be looking out at the storm from the casement, he never heeded my presence, never troubled himself with so much as a single glance; but appeared wholly occupied with counting the pages of the marvellous book.", "내가 이렇게 그를 면밀히 살피며, 그러는 동안 한편으로는 창문 너머로 폭풍우를 내다보는 척하고 있었으나, 그는 내 존재를 전혀 개의치 않았으며, 단 한 번 곁눈질조차 하느라 수고하지 않았다. 다만 그 신기한 책의 책장을 세는 일에 온전히 몰두한 듯 보였다."], ["Considering how sociably we had been sleeping together the night previous, and especially considering the affectionate arm I had found thrown over me upon waking in the morning, I thought this indifference of his very strange.", "우리가 전날 밤 그토록 다정하게 함께 잤다는 것을 생각하면, 더구나 아침에 깨어 보니 그의 애정 어린 팔이 내 몸 위에 걸쳐져 있던 것을 생각하면, 나는 이런 그의 무관심이 무척 이상하게 느껴졌다."], ["But savages are strange beings; at times you do not know exactly how to take them.", "그러나 야만인이란 기이한 존재인지라, 때로는 그들을 어떻게 대해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없는 법이다."], ["At first they are overawing; their calm self-collectedness of simplicity seems a Socratic wisdom.", "처음에는 그들이 위압적으로 다가온다. 그 소박함에서 우러나는 차분한 자기 절제는 마치 소크라테스적 지혜처럼 보인다."], ["I had noticed also that Queequeg never consorted at all, or but very little, with the other seamen in the inn.", "나는 또한 퀴케그가 여관의 다른 선원들과는 전혀, 혹은 거의 어울리지 않는다는 것을 눈여겨보았다."], ["He made no advances whatever; appeared to have no desire to enlarge the circle of his acquaintances.", "그는 어떤 식으로든 먼저 다가가는 일이 없었고, 자기 교제의 범위를 넓히려는 욕심도 없어 보였다."], ["All this struck me as mighty singular; yet, upon second thoughts, there was something almost sublime in it.", "이 모든 것이 내게는 몹시 별나게 비쳤다. 하지만 다시 생각해 보니, 거기에는 어딘가 거의 숭고하다 할 무언가가 있었다."], ["Here was a man some twenty thousand miles from home, by the way of Cape Horn, that is—which was the only way he could get there—thrown among people as strange to him as though he were in the planet Jupiter; and yet he seemed entirely at his ease; preserving the utmost serenity; content with his own companionship; always equal to himself.", "여기 한 사나이가 있으니, 케이프 혼(Cape Horn)을 돌아 고향에서 이만 마일이나 떨어진 곳에—그것이 그가 이리로 올 수 있는 유일한 길이었으니—말하자면 목성에라도 와 있는 듯 자신에게 낯선 사람들 한복판에 내던져졌건만, 그럼에도 그는 더없이 편안해 보였으며, 지극한 평정을 유지한 채 제 자신과의 동무로 만족하며, 언제나 자기 자신과 한결같았다."], ["Surely this was a touch of fine philosophy; though no doubt he had never heard there was such a thing as that.", "이는 분명 훌륭한 철학의 한 자락이었다. 비록 그런 것이 세상에 있다는 말조차 그는 들어 본 적이 없었겠지만 말이다."], ["But, perhaps, to be true philosophers, we mortals should not be conscious of so living or so striving.", "그러나 어쩌면, 참된 철학자가 되려면 우리네 인간은 그렇게 살고 있다거나 그렇게 애쓰고 있다는 것을 의식하지 말아야 하는지도 모른다."], ["So soon as I hear that such or such a man gives himself out for a philosopher, I conclude that, like the dyspeptic old woman, he must have “broken his digester.”", "누군가가 스스로를 철학자라 내세운다는 말을 듣자마자, 나는 그가 소화불량에 걸린 노파처럼 \"소화기를 망가뜨린\" 사람임에 틀림없다고 결론짓는다."]], [["As I sat there in that now lonely room; the fire burning low, in that mild stage when, after its first intensity has warmed the air, it then only glows to be looked at; the evening shades and phantoms gathering round the casements, and peering in upon us silent, solitary twain; the storm booming without in solemn swells; I began to be sensible of strange feelings.", "이제 적막해진 그 방에 앉아 있노라니, 불은 약하게 타들어 가, 처음의 맹렬한 기세가 공기를 데우고 나면 그저 바라보기 좋게 발갛게 빛나기만 하는 그 온화한 단계에 이르러 있었다. 저녁 그림자와 유령들이 창문 둘레로 모여들어, 말없이 외로이 앉은 우리 두 사람을 안으로 들여다보았다."], ["I felt a melting in me.", "밖에서는 폭풍우가 장엄한 너울로 우르릉거렸다."], ["No more my splintered heart and maddened hand were turned against the wolfish world.", "나는 야릇한 감정이 일기 시작하는 것을 느꼈다."], ["This soothing savage had redeemed it.", "내 안에서 무언가가 녹아내리는 듯했다."], ["There he sat, his very indifference speaking a nature in which there lurked no civilized hypocrisies and bland deceits.", "산산이 부서진 내 마음도, 미쳐 날뛰던 내 손도, 더는 이리 떼 같은 세상을 향해 곤두서 있지 않았다. 이 위안을 주는 야만인이 그것을 구원해 준 것이다."], ["Wild he was; a very sight of sights to see; yet I began to feel myself mysteriously drawn towards him.", "그가 거기 앉아 있었고, 그 무관심 자체가 곧 문명화된 위선이나 매끄러운 기만 따위가 도사리지 않은 천성을 말해 주고 있었다. 그는 거칠었다."], ["And those same things that would have repelled most others, they were the very magnets that thus drew me.", "보고 또 보아도 진풍경이었다. 그럼에도 나는 신비롭게 그에게 이끌리는 나 자신을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["I’ll try a pagan friend, thought I, since Christian kindness has proved but hollow courtesy.", "그리고 다른 대부분의 사람이라면 질색했을 바로 그 점들이, 이토록 나를 끌어당기는 자석이었다."], ["I drew my bench near him, and made some friendly signs and hints, doing my best to talk with him meanwhile.", "기독교의 친절이라는 것이 결국 속 빈 예의에 지나지 않음이 드러났으니, 어디 이교도 친구를 한번 사귀어 보자, 나는 생각했다."], ["At first he little noticed these advances; but presently, upon my referring to his last night’s hospitalities, he made out to ask me whether we were again to be bedfellows.", "나는 벤치를 그의 곁으로 끌어다 놓고, 그러는 동안 그와 이야기를 나누어 보려 안간힘을 쓰며 우호적인 몸짓과 신호를 몇 가지 보냈다. 처음에 그는 이런 다가옴에 별 반응이 없었으나, 곧 내가 어젯밤의 환대를 입에 올리자, 우리가 다시 한자리에서 잘 거냐고 그럭저럭 물어 왔다."], ["I told him yes; whereat I thought he looked pleased, perhaps a little complimented.", "나는 그렇다고 답했고, 그러자 그는 기뻐하는 듯, 어쩌면 약간 우쭐해하는 듯 보였다."]], [["We then turned over the book together, and I endeavored to explain to him the purpose of the printing, and the meaning of the few pictures that were in it.", "그러고 나서 우리는 함께 그 책을 넘겼고, 나는 인쇄라는 것의 목적과, 그 안에 든 몇 안 되는 그림의 뜻을 그에게 설명해 보려 애썼다."], ["Thus I soon engaged his interest; and from that we went to jabbering the best we could about the various outer sights to be seen in this famous town.", "그리하여 나는 곧 그의 흥미를 끌어냈다. 그러고는 이 유명한 도시에서 볼 수 있는 갖가지 바깥 풍경들에 관해, 우리는 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 두서없이 떠들어 댔다."], ["Soon I proposed a social smoke; and, producing his pouch and tomahawk, he quietly offered me a puff.", "이윽고 나는 친목 삼아 한 대 피우자고 제안했고, 그는 자기 쌈지와 토마호크 담뱃대를 꺼내 조용히 내게 한 모금 권했다."], ["And then we sat exchanging puffs from that wild pipe of his, and keeping it regularly passing between us.", "그리하여 우리는 그 야성적인 담뱃대로 번갈아 연기를 주고받으며, 그것을 규칙적으로 우리 사이에 돌려 가며 앉아 있었다."]], [["If there yet lurked any ice of indifference towards me in the Pagan’s breast, this pleasant, genial smoke we had, soon thawed it out, and left us cronies.", "설령 그 이교도의 가슴속에 나를 향한 무관심의 얼음이 아직 얼마쯤 도사리고 있었다 한들, 우리가 함께 나눈 이 흐뭇하고 정겨운 한 대의 담배가 그것을 이내 녹여 버려, 우리를 막역한 벗으로 만들어 놓았다. 그가 내게 끌리는 것은 내가 그에게 끌리는 것만큼이나 자연스럽고 스스럼없어 보였다."], ["He seemed to take to me quite as naturally and unbiddenly as I to him; and when our smoke was over, he pressed his forehead against mine, clasped me round the waist, and said that henceforth we were married; meaning, in his country’s phrase, that we were bosom friends; he would gladly die for me, if need should be.", "담배를 다 피우고 나자 그는 자기 이마를 내 이마에 맞대고, 내 허리를 두 팔로 끌어안으며, 이제부터 우리는 부부가 된 거라고 말했다. 그것은 자기 나라 말로 우리가 가슴을 터놓는 벗이 되었다는 뜻이었으니, 필요하다면 그는 기꺼이 나를 위해 죽으리라는 것이었다."], ["In a countryman, this sudden flame of friendship would have seemed far too premature, a thing to be much distrusted; but in this simple savage those old rules would not apply.", "같은 나라 사람끼리였다면, 이렇게 갑작스레 타오르는 우정의 불꽃은 너무 성급해 크게 미덥지 못한 것으로 비쳤겠지만, 이 소박한 야만인에게 그 케케묵은 규칙들은 들어맞지 않았다."]], [["After supper, and another social chat and smoke, we went to our room together.", "저녁 식사를 마치고, 또 한 차례 정겨운 한담과 담배를 나눈 뒤, 우리는 함께 우리 방으로 갔다. 그는 자기가 방부 처리한 머리를 내게 선물로 주었다."], ["He made me a present of his embalmed head; took out his enormous tobacco wallet, and groping under the tobacco, drew out some thirty dollars in silver; then spreading them on the table, and mechanically dividing them into two equal portions, pushed one of them towards me, and said it was mine.", "그러고는 그 어마어마하게 큰 담배 지갑을 꺼내, 담배 밑을 더듬어 은화로 삼십 달러쯤을 끄집어냈다. 그러더니 그것을 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓고, 기계적으로 똑같이 두 몫으로 나누어, 그중 한 몫을 내 쪽으로 밀며 그것이 내 것이라고 말했다."], ["I was going to remonstrate; but he silenced me by pouring them into my trowsers’ pockets.", "나는 한사코 마다하려 했으나, 그는 그것을 내 바지 주머니에 쏟아 넣어 내 입을 막아 버렸다."], ["I let them stay.", "나는 그냥 그대로 두었다."], ["He then went about his evening prayers, took out his idol, and removed the paper fireboard.", "그러고서 그는 저녁 기도를 올리려고 자기 우상을 꺼내고, 종이로 된 난로 가리개를 치웠다."], ["By certain signs and symptoms, I thought he seemed anxious for me to join him; but well knowing what was to follow, I deliberated a moment whether, in case he invited me, I would comply or otherwise.", "어떤 몸짓과 기색으로 보아, 그는 내가 함께하기를 바라는 듯했다. 그러나 무슨 일이 뒤따를지를 잘 아는지라, 나는 만약 그가 나를 청한다면 응할 것인지 말 것인지 한순간 곰곰이 따져 보았다."]], [["I was a good Christian; born and bred in the bosom of the infallible Presbyterian Church.", "나는 어엿한 기독교인이었으니, 오류 없는 장로교회의 품 안에서 나고 자란 사람이었다."], ["How then could I unite with this wild idolator in worshipping his piece of wood?", "그러니 어찌 내가 이 야성의 우상숭배자와 한패가 되어 그의 나뭇조각을 섬길 수 있단 말인가?"], ["But what is worship? thought I.", "그러나 도대체 예배란 무엇인가?"], ["Do you suppose now, Ishmael, that the magnanimous God of heaven and earth—pagans and all included—can possibly be jealous of an insignificant bit of black wood?", "하고 나는 생각했다. 이스마엘, 너는 정녕 천지의 너그러우신 하느님께서—이교도까지 모두 아우르시는 그분께서—하찮은 검은 나뭇조각 따위를 시샘하실 수 있다고 여기느냐?"], ["Impossible!", "있을 수 없는 일이다!"], ["But what is worship?—to do the will of God—that is worship.", "그렇다면 예배란 무엇인가?—하느님의 뜻을 행하는 것, 바로 그것이 예배다."], ["And what is the will of God?—to do to my fellow man what I would have my fellow man to do to me—that is the will of God.", "그러면 하느님의 뜻이란 무엇인가?—내 이웃 사람이 내게 해 주기를 바라는 바를 내가 그 이웃에게 해 주는 것, 바로 그것이 하느님의 뜻이다."], ["Now, Queequeg is my fellow man.", "그런데 퀴케그는 내 이웃 사람이다."], ["And what do I wish that this Queequeg would do to me?", "그러면 나는 이 퀴케그가 내게 무엇을 해 주기를 바라는가?"], ["Why, unite with me in my particular Presbyterian form of worship.", "그야 물론, 나만의 장로교식 예배에 그가 함께해 주기를 바라는 것이다."], ["Consequently, I must then unite with him in his; ergo, I must turn idolator.", "따라서 그렇다면 나도 그의 예배에 함께해야 마땅하다. 고로 나는 우상숭배자가 되어야 한다."], ["So I kindled the shavings; helped prop up the innocent little idol; offered him burnt biscuit with Queequeg; salamed before him twice or thrice; kissed his nose; and that done, we undressed and went to bed, at peace with our own consciences and all the world.", "그리하여 나는 대팻밥에 불을 댕기고, 그 천진한 작은 우상을 받쳐 세우는 것을 거들었으며, 퀴케그와 함께 그 우상에게 탄 비스킷을 바치고, 그 앞에서 두세 번 절을 올렸으며, 그 코에 입을 맞추었다. 그 일을 마치고 우리는 옷을 벗고 잠자리에 들었으니, 우리 각자의 양심과도, 온 세상과도 화평했다."], ["But we did not go to sleep without some little chat.", "그러나 우리는 자그마한 한담을 나누지 않고는 잠들지 않았다."]], [["How it is I know not; but there is no place like a bed for confidential disclosures between friends.", "어찌 된 영문인지 나는 모르겠으나, 벗끼리 흉금을 털어놓는 데에는 잠자리만 한 곳이 없다."], ["Man and wife, they say, there open the very bottom of their souls to each other; and some old couples often lie and chat over old times till nearly morning.", "부부는, 사람들 말로는, 거기서 서로에게 영혼의 맨 밑바닥까지 열어 보인다고 하며, 어떤 늙은 부부는 곧잘 누워서 새벽녘이 다 되도록 옛 시절 이야기로 도란도란 한담을 나눈다고 한다."], ["Thus, then, in our hearts’ honeymoon, lay I and Queequeg—a cosy, loving pair.", "그리하여 마음의 신혼을 맞은 우리, 나와 퀴케그는—아늑하고 정다운 한 쌍으로—그렇게 누워 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 11. Nightgown.", "# CHAPTER 11. Nightgown."]], [["We had lain thus in bed, chatting and napping at short intervals, and Queequeg now and then affectionately throwing his brown tattooed legs over mine, and then drawing them back; so entirely sociable and free and easy were we; when, at last, by reason of our confabulations, what little nappishness remained in us altogether departed, and we felt like getting up again, though day-break was yet some way down the future.", "우리는 그렇게 침대에 누워 짧은 간격으로 이야기를 나누다 깜빡 졸기를 거듭했고, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)는 이따금 문신 새겨진 갈색 다리를 다정하게 내 다리 위로 척 걸쳐 놓았다가 다시 거두어들이곤 했다. 우리는 그토록 허물없고 자유롭고 편안한 사이가 되어 있었다. 그러다 마침내 끝없는 잡담 탓에 우리 안에 남아 있던 약간의 졸음마저 깡그리 달아나 버렸고, 동이 트려면 아직 한참 남았는데도 다시 일어나고 싶은 기분이 들었다."]], [["Yes, we became very wakeful; so much so that our recumbent position began to grow wearisome, and by little and little we found ourselves sitting up; the clothes well tucked around us, leaning against the head-board with our four knees drawn up close together, and our two noses bending over them, as if our kneepans were warming-pans.", "그렇다, 우리는 완전히 말똥말똥해졌다. 어찌나 그러했던지 누워 있는 자세가 점점 지겨워지기 시작했고, 우리는 조금씩 몸을 일으켜 어느새 앉아 있었다."], ["We felt very nice and snug, the more so since it was so chilly out of doors; indeed out of bed-clothes too, seeing that there was no fire in the room.", "이불을 몸에 단단히 두르고 침대 머리판에 기댄 채, 네 무릎을 바싹 모아 세우고 두 콧등을 그 위로 숙였으니, 마치 무릎이 발 데우는 화로라도 되는 듯했다."], ["The more so, I say, because truly to enjoy bodily warmth, some small part of you must be cold, for there is no quality in this world that is not what it is merely by contrast.", "우리는 무척 아늑하고 포근한 기분이었는데, 바깥이 그토록 쌀쌀했기에 더욱 그러했다. 사실 방 안에 불기운이 전혀 없었던 터라 이불 밖 또한 차가웠으니 말이다."], ["Nothing exists in itself.", "다시 말하거니와 그래서 더욱 그러했던 것이다."], ["If you flatter yourself that you are all over comfortable, and have been so a long time, then you cannot be said to be comfortable any more.", "진정으로 몸의 온기를 누리려면 몸의 어느 작은 부분만큼은 차가워야 하기 때문이다. 이 세상에 대비(對比)에 의하지 않고서 본래 그것 자체인 성질이란 없으니, 그 어떤 것도 홀로 존재하지는 않는 법이다."], ["But if, like Queequeg and me in the bed, the tip of your nose or the crown of your head be slightly chilled, why then, indeed, in the general consciousness you feel most delightfully and unmistakably warm.", "만약 그대가 온몸이 빈틈없이 편안하다고, 그것도 오랫동안 그러했다고 으스댄다면, 그대는 더 이상 편안하다고 말할 수 없다. 그러나 침대 속의 퀴퀘그와 나처럼 콧등 끝이나 정수리가 살짝 시리다면, 바로 그때야말로 의식 전체가 더없이 즐겁고도 틀림없는 따뜻함을 느끼게 된다."], ["For this reason a sleeping apartment should never be furnished with a fire, which is one of the luxurious discomforts of the rich.", "이런 까닭에 침실에는 결코 불을 들여놓아서는 안 되는 것인데, 그것이야말로 부자들이 누리는 사치스러운 불편 가운데 하나다."], ["For the height of this sort of deliciousness is to have nothing but the blanket between you and your snugness and the cold of the outer air.", "이런 종류의 감미로움이 절정에 이르려면, 그대와 그대의 아늑함, 그리고 바깥의 찬 공기 사이에 담요 한 장 외에는 아무것도 없어야 하기 때문이다."], ["Then there you lie like the one warm spark in the heart of an arctic crystal.", "그러면 그대는 북극의 수정 한가운데 깃든 하나의 따뜻한 불씨처럼 거기 누워 있게 된다."]], [["We had been sitting in this crouching manner for some time, when all at once I thought I would open my eyes; for when between sheets, whether by day or by night, and whether asleep or awake, I have a way of always keeping my eyes shut, in order the more to concentrate the snugness of being in bed.", "우리는 그렇게 몸을 웅크리고 한동안 앉아 있었는데, 문득 나는 눈을 떠 보아야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 이부자리 사이에 들면, 낮이든 밤이든, 잠들었든 깨어 있든, 나는 침대 속의 아늑함을 더욱 한데 모으려고 늘 눈을 감아 두는 버릇이 있기 때문이다."], ["Because no man can ever feel his own identity aright except his eyes be closed; as if darkness were indeed the proper element of our essences, though light be more congenial to our clayey part.", "빛이 우리의 흙으로 빚어진 부분에는 더 어울릴지언정, 어둠이야말로 정녕 우리 본질의 본래 거처라는 듯이, 눈을 감지 않고서는 누구도 제 자신의 정체를 제대로 느낄 수 없는 법이다."], ["Upon opening my eyes then, and coming out of my own pleasant and self-created darkness into the imposed and coarse outer gloom of the unilluminated twelve-o’clock-at-night, I experienced a disagreeable revulsion.", "그리하여 눈을 뜨고, 내 스스로 만들어 낸 즐거운 어둠에서 빠져나와 불 밝히지 않은 자정의 강요된 거친 바깥 어둠 속으로 나오자, 나는 불쾌한 거부감을 맛보았다."], ["Nor did I at all object to the hint from Queequeg that perhaps it were best to strike a light, seeing that we were so wide awake; and besides he felt a strong desire to have a few quiet puffs from his Tomahawk.", "우리가 이토록 말똥말똥 깨어 있으니 차라리 불을 켜는 편이 낫겠다는 퀴퀘그의 제안에도 나는 조금도 반대하지 않았다. 게다가 그는 자신의 토마호크로 조용히 몇 모금 빨고 싶은 강한 욕구를 느끼고 있었던 것이다."], ["Be it said, that though I had felt such a strong repugnance to his smoking in the bed the night before, yet see how elastic our stiff prejudices grow when love once comes to bend them.", "분명히 말해 두거니와, 전날 밤 나는 그가 침대에서 담배를 피우는 것에 그토록 강한 혐오를 느꼈건만, 일단 애정이 끼어들어 그것을 구부려 놓으면 우리의 뻣뻣한 편견이 얼마나 잘 휘어지는지를 보라."], ["For now I liked nothing better than to have Queequeg smoking by me, even in bed, because he seemed to be full of such serene household joy then.", "이제 나는 퀴퀘그가, 그것도 침대 속에서, 내 곁에서 담배 피우는 것보다 더 좋은 일이 없었으니, 그때 그가 그토록 평온한 가정적 기쁨으로 가득 차 보였기 때문이다."], ["I no more felt unduly concerned for the landlord’s policy of insurance.", "나는 더 이상 주인장의 보험 정책 따위를 지나치게 염려하지 않았다."], ["I was only alive to the condensed confidential comfortableness of sharing a pipe and a blanket with a real friend.", "다만 진정한 벗과 더불어 파이프 하나와 담요 한 장을 나누는, 응축되고 은밀한 안락함에만 온통 마음이 살아 있었다."], ["With our shaggy jackets drawn about our shoulders, we now passed the Tomahawk from one to the other, till slowly there grew over us a blue hanging tester of smoke, illuminated by the flame of the new-lit lamp.", "우리는 텁수룩한 웃옷을 어깨에 두른 채 이제 토마호크를 서로 주고받았고, 마침내 우리 머리 위로 갓 켠 등불의 불꽃에 비친 푸른 연기의 천개(天蓋)가 서서히 드리워졌다."]], [["Whether it was that this undulating tester rolled the savage away to far distant scenes, I know not, but he now spoke of his native island; and, eager to hear his history, I begged him to go on and tell it.", "이 일렁이는 천개가 그 야만인을 머나먼 풍경 속으로 실어 보낸 것인지는 나도 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그는 이제 자신의 고향 섬에 관해 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그의 내력을 듣고 싶었던 나는 그에게 어서 계속해서 들려 달라고 청했다."], ["He gladly complied.", "그는 기꺼이 응했다."], ["Though at the time I but ill comprehended not a few of his words, yet subsequent disclosures, when I had become more familiar with his broken phraseology, now enable me to present the whole story such as it may prove in the mere skeleton I give.", "그때는 그의 말을 적잖이 제대로 알아듣지 못했으나, 훗날 그의 서툰 말투에 더 익숙해진 뒤에 알게 된 사실들 덕분에, 이제 나는 여기 뼈대만 추려 내놓는 정도로나마 그 이야기 전체를 들려줄 수 있게 되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 12. Biographical.", "# CHAPTER 12. Biographical."]], [["Queequeg was a native of Rokovoko, an island far away to the West and South.", "퀴케그는 머나먼 서쪽, 그리고 남쪽에 자리한 로코보코 섬 태생이었다."], ["It is not down in any map; true places never are.", "그 섬은 어떤 지도에도 나와 있지 않다. 참된 장소란 본래 그런 법이다."]], [["When a new-hatched savage running wild about his native woodlands in a grass clout, followed by the nibbling goats, as if he were a green sapling; even then, in Queequeg’s ambitious soul, lurked a strong desire to see something more of Christendom than a specimen whaler or two.", "풀로 엮은 허리가리개 하나만 두른 채 고향의 숲을 마구 헤집고 뛰어다니던 갓 태어난 야만인 시절, 마치 그를 어린 묘목으로 여기는 듯 새끼 염소들이 그 뒤를 졸졸 따라다니며 풀을 뜯던 그 시절에도, 퀴케그의 야심 찬 영혼 속에는 포경선 한두 척 표본 따위로는 만족할 수 없는, 기독교 세계를 좀 더 보고 싶다는 강한 열망이 도사리고 있었다."], ["His father was a High Chief, a King; his uncle a High Priest; and on the maternal side he boasted aunts who were the wives of unconquerable warriors.", "그의 아버지는 대추장이자 왕이었고, 숙부는 대제사장이었다. 또한 외가 쪽으로는 천하무적의 전사들을 남편으로 둔 고모들을 자랑할 만했다."], ["There was excellent blood in his veins—royal stuff; though sadly vitiated, I fear, by the cannibal propensity he nourished in his untutored youth.", "그의 핏줄에는 더없이 고귀한 피 — 왕족의 피가 흐르고 있었다. 다만 안타깝게도, 가르침 받지 못한 어린 시절에 그가 키워 온 식인 성향으로 인해 그 피가 적잖이 더럽혀졌으리라 짐작될 뿐이다."]], [["A Sag Harbor ship visited his father’s bay, and Queequeg sought a passage to Christian lands.", "새그하버에서 온 배 한 척이 그의 아버지가 다스리는 만(灣)을 찾아왔고, 퀴케그는 기독교 세계로 가는 뱃길을 청했다."], ["But the ship, having her full complement of seamen, spurned his suit; and not all the King his father’s influence could prevail.", "그러나 그 배는 이미 선원을 가득 채우고 있던 터라 그의 청을 물리쳤다. 그의 아버지인 왕의 영향력을 모두 동원해도 소용없었다."], ["But Queequeg vowed a vow.", "그러자 퀴케그는 한 가지 맹세를 세웠다."], ["Alone in his canoe, he paddled off to a distant strait, which he knew the ship must pass through when she quitted the island.", "그는 홀로 카누를 몰아, 배가 섬을 떠날 때 반드시 지나가야 하는 줄을 알고 있던 머나먼 해협으로 노 저어 나아갔다."], ["On one side was a coral reef; on the other a low tongue of land, covered with mangrove thickets that grew out into the water.", "한쪽에는 산호초가 있었고, 다른 한쪽에는 물속까지 뻗어 나온 맹그로브 덤불로 뒤덮인 나지막한 곶이 있었다."], ["Hiding his canoe, still afloat, among these thickets, with its prow seaward, he sat down in the stern, paddle low in hand; and when the ship was gliding by, like a flash he darted out; gained her side; with one backward dash of his foot capsized and sank his canoe; climbed up the chains; and throwing himself at full length upon the deck, grappled a ring-bolt there, and swore not to let it go, though hacked in pieces.", "그는 여전히 물 위에 떠 있는 카누를 그 덤불 사이에 뱃머리를 바다 쪽으로 향한 채 숨겨 두고는, 고물에 앉아 노를 낮게 쥐었다. 그리고 배가 미끄러지듯 곁을 지나갈 때, 번개처럼 튀어나가 뱃전에 다다랐고, 한 발을 뒤로 차 카누를 뒤집어 가라앉힌 뒤, 사슬을 타고 기어올라 갑판 위에 온몸을 길게 내던지고는, 거기 박힌 고리 볼트를 움켜쥐고서, 산산조각이 나는 한이 있어도 결코 놓지 않겠노라 맹세했다."]], [["In vain the captain threatened to throw him overboard; suspended a cutlass over his naked wrists; Queequeg was the son of a King, and Queequeg budged not.", "선장은 그를 바다에 던져 버리겠다 으름장을 놓고, 드러난 그의 손목 위로 단검을 들이댔지만 모두 헛수고였다. 퀴케그는 왕의 아들이었고, 그는 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다."], ["Struck by his desperate dauntlessness, and his wild desire to visit Christendom, the captain at last relented, and told him he might make himself at home.", "그 필사적인 담대함과 기독교 세계를 보고 싶어 하는 거친 열망에 마음이 움직인 선장은 마침내 누그러져, 마음대로 지내도 좋다고 말했다."], ["But this fine young savage—this sea Prince of Wales, never saw the Captain’s cabin.", "그러나 이 늠름한 젊은 야만인 — 이 바다의 웨일스 왕자는 선장실 구경 한 번 못 했다."], ["They put him down among the sailors, and made a whaleman of him.", "사람들은 그를 선원들 틈에 처박아 두고 고래잡이로 만들어 버렸다."], ["But like Czar Peter content to toil in the shipyards of foreign cities, Queequeg disdained no seeming ignominy, if thereby he might happily gain the power of enlightening his untutored countrymen.", "그러나 표트르 황제가 이국 도시의 조선소에서 기꺼이 막일을 마다하지 않았듯, 퀴케그도 그로 인해 가르침 받지 못한 제 동족을 일깨울 힘을 얻을 수만 있다면 겉보기의 그 어떤 굴욕도 마다하지 않았다."], ["For at bottom—so he told me—he was actuated by a profound desire to learn among the Christians, the arts whereby to make his people still happier than they were; and more than that, still better than they were.", "그가 내게 말하기를, 그의 마음 밑바닥에는 기독교인들 사이에서 제 백성을 지금보다 더 행복하게 — 그뿐 아니라 지금보다 더 선하게 만들 기술을 배우고자 하는 깊은 열망이 자리 잡고 있었던 것이다."], ["But, alas!", "그러나, 아!"], ["the practices of whalemen soon convinced him that even Christians could be both miserable and wicked; infinitely more so, than all his father’s heathens.", "고래잡이들의 행실은 곧 그에게, 기독교인이라 한들 비참하고 사악할 수 있으며, 그것도 그의 아버지가 다스리던 모든 이교도들보다 한없이 더 심할 수 있음을 깨닫게 해 주었다."], ["Arrived at last in old Sag Harbor; and seeing what the sailors did there; and then going on to Nantucket, and seeing how they spent their wages in that place also, poor Queequeg gave it up for lost.", "마침내 오래된 새그하버에 다다라 그곳 선원들이 무슨 짓을 하는지 보았고, 이어 낸터킷으로 건너가 거기서도 그들이 품삯을 어떻게 탕진하는지를 본 가련한 퀴케그는, 모든 것을 단념하고 말았다. 그는 생각했다."], ["Thought he, it’s a wicked world in all meridians; I’ll die a pagan.", "어느 자오선 아래든 세상이란 사악한 곳이로구나, 나는 이교도로 죽으리라."]], [["And thus an old idolator at heart, he yet lived among these Christians, wore their clothes, and tried to talk their gibberish.", "그리하여 마음속으로는 여전히 오래된 우상숭배자이면서도, 그는 이 기독교인들 틈에서 살아가며 그들의 옷을 입고, 그들의 알아들을 수 없는 말을 따라 해 보려 애썼다."], ["Hence the queer ways about him, though now some time from home.", "고향을 떠나온 지 어느덧 세월이 흘렀건만 그에게서 풍기는 기이한 구석은 바로 거기서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["By hints, I asked him whether he did not propose going back, and having a coronation; since he might now consider his father dead and gone, he being very old and feeble at the last accounts.", "나는 넌지시 그에게, 고향으로 돌아가 즉위식을 치를 생각은 없는지 물었다. 마지막으로 들은 소식으로는 그의 아버지가 무척 늙고 쇠약해졌다니, 이제는 세상을 떠났다고 여겨도 좋을 터였다."], ["He answered no, not yet; and added that he was fearful Christianity, or rather Christians, had unfitted him for ascending the pure and undefiled throne of thirty pagan Kings before him.", "그는 아니라고, 아직은 아니라고 답했다. 그러고는 기독교, 아니 차라리 기독교인들이 자신을 더럽혀, 자기 앞서 서른 명의 이교도 왕이 앉았던 순수하고 흠 없는 왕좌에 오르기에는 합당치 않게 만들어 버린 것이 두렵다고 덧붙였다."], ["But by and by, he said, he would return,—as soon as he felt himself baptized again.", "하지만 머지않아, 다시 세례받았다는 느낌이 들기만 하면 돌아가겠노라고 그는 말했다."], ["For the nonce, however, he proposed to sail about, and sow his wild oats in all four oceans.", "그러나 당장은 사방을 두루 항해하며 네 대양 모두에 젊은 날의 객기를 뿌려 둘 작정이라고 했다."], ["They had made a harpooneer of him, and that barbed iron was in lieu of a sceptre now.", "사람들은 그를 작살잡이로 만들어 놓았으니, 이제는 그 미늘 박힌 작살이 왕홀(王笏)을 대신하고 있었다."]], [["I asked him what might be his immediate purpose, touching his future movements.", "나는 그에게 앞으로의 행보와 관련하여 당장의 목적이 무엇인지 물었다."], ["He answered, to go to sea again, in his old vocation.", "그는 옛 직업 그대로 다시 바다로 나가는 것이라 답했다."], ["Upon this, I told him that whaling was my own design, and informed him of my intention to sail out of Nantucket, as being the most promising port for an adventurous whaleman to embark from.", "그 말에 나는 고래잡이야말로 내가 품은 계획이라 일러 주었고, 모험을 좇는 고래잡이가 출항하기에 가장 유망한 항구인 낸터킷에서 배를 타고 나갈 작정임을 알려 주었다."], ["He at once resolved to accompany me to that island, ship aboard the same vessel, get into the same watch, the same boat, the same mess with me, in short to share my every hap; with both my hands in his, boldly dip into the Potluck of both worlds.", "그는 곧장 나와 함께 그 섬으로 가서, 같은 배를 타고, 같은 당직조에 들고, 같은 보트를 타며, 같은 식탁에 앉아 — 한마디로 내 모든 운명을 함께 나누기로 결심했다. 두 손을 맞잡고서, 두 세계 모두의 운수 자루 속으로 대담하게 손을 담그자는 것이었다."], ["To all this I joyously assented; for besides the affection I now felt for Queequeg, he was an experienced harpooneer, and as such, could not fail to be of great usefulness to one, who, like me, was wholly ignorant of the mysteries of whaling, though well acquainted with the sea, as known to merchant seamen.", "나는 이 모든 것에 기쁘게 동의했다. 이제 퀴케그에게 느끼는 애정은 차치하더라도, 그는 노련한 작살잡이였으니, 상선 선원들이 아는 만큼은 바다에 익숙했어도 고래잡이의 신비에 대해서는 전혀 무지했던 나 같은 사람에게는 더없이 큰 도움이 될 것이 분명했기 때문이다."]], [["His story being ended with his pipe’s last dying puff, Queequeg embraced me, pressed his forehead against mine, and blowing out the light, we rolled over from each other, this way and that, and very soon were sleeping.", "그의 파이프에서 마지막 연기가 사그라지며 이야기가 끝나자, 퀴케그는 나를 끌어안고 제 이마를 내 이마에 맞댄 뒤, 불을 끄고는, 우리는 이리저리 서로에게서 몸을 굴려 떨어졌고, 이내 잠들고 말았다."]], [["# CHAPTER 13. Wheelbarrow.", "# CHAPTER 13. Wheelbarrow."]], [["Next morning, Monday, after disposing of the embalmed head to a barber, for a block, I settled my own and comrade’s bill; using, however, my comrade’s money.", "이튿날 아침, 월요일에, 방부 처리된 머리를 어느 이발사에게 가발 받침으로 넘긴 뒤, 나는 내 몫과 동료의 셈을 치렀다. 다만 그 돈은 동료의 것을 썼다."], ["The grinning landlord, as well as the boarders, seemed amazingly tickled at the sudden friendship which had sprung up between me and Queequeg—especially as Peter Coffin’s cock and bull stories about him had previously so much alarmed me concerning the very person whom I now companied with.", "능글맞게 웃는 여관 주인이며 다른 하숙인들이며 모두, 나와 퀴케그 사이에 갑작스레 싹튼 우정에 놀라우리만치 재미있어하는 눈치였다. 더구나 피터 코핀이 그자에 대해 늘어놓았던 허무맹랑한 이야기들이 일찍이 나를 그토록 겁먹게 했던 바로 그 인물과 내가 지금 함께 다니고 있으니 그럴 만도 했다."]], [["We borrowed a wheelbarrow, and embarking our things, including my own poor carpet-bag, and Queequeg’s canvas sack and hammock, away we went down to “the Moss,”", "우리는 손수레 하나를 빌려, 내 초라한 융단 가방과 퀴케그의 범포 자루와 해먹까지 짐을 모두 싣고서, 부두에 매여 있는 작은 낸터킷 정기 연락선인 스쿠너, \"모스\"호를 향해 내려갔다."], ["the little Nantucket packet schooner moored at the wharf.", "우리가 길을 걸어가는 동안 사람들이 빤히 쳐다보았다. 퀴케그를 보고 그러는 것은 아니었다."], ["As we were going along the people stared; not at Queequeg so much—for they were used to seeing cannibals like him in their streets,—but at seeing him and me upon such confidential terms.", "그들은 거리에서 그 같은 식인종을 보는 데 익숙했으니까. 그보다는 그와 내가 그토록 허물없는 사이로 지내는 모습을 보고 놀란 것이었다."], ["But we heeded them not, going along wheeling the barrow by turns, and Queequeg now and then stopping to adjust the sheath on his harpoon barbs.", "그러나 우리는 그들을 개의치 않고, 번갈아 손수레를 밀며 나아갔으며, 퀴케그는 이따금 멈춰 서서 작살 미늘에 씌운 칼집을 매만지곤 했다."], ["I asked him why he carried such a troublesome thing with him ashore, and whether all whaling ships did not find their own harpoons.", "나는 그에게 어째서 그토록 성가신 물건을 뭍에까지 들고 다니느냐고, 어느 포경선이든 제 작살은 다 마련해 두지 않느냐고 물었다. 이에 그는 대략 이렇게 대답했다."], ["To this, in substance, he replied, that though what I hinted was true enough, yet he had a particular affection for his own harpoon, because it was of assured stuff, well tried in many a mortal combat, and deeply intimate with the hearts of whales.", "내 말이 틀림없는 사실이긴 하나, 그래도 자기는 제 작살에 각별한 애착이 있다는 것이었다. 그것은 믿을 만한 재질로 만들어졌고, 숱한 목숨 건 싸움에서 단단히 검증되었으며, 고래들의 심장과 깊이 친밀한 사이이기 때문이라고 했다."], ["In short, like many inland reapers and mowers, who go into the farmers’ meadows armed with their own scythes—though in no wise obliged to furnish them—even so, Queequeg, for his own private reasons, preferred his own harpoon.", "요컨대, 굳이 제 것을 마련할 의무가 없는데도 제 낫을 들고 농부들의 풀밭으로 들어가는 내륙의 많은 추수꾼과 풀 베는 일꾼들처럼, 퀴케그 역시 제 나름의 사사로운 이유로 제 작살을 더 좋아하는 것이었다."]], [["Shifting the barrow from my hand to his, he told me a funny story about the first wheelbarrow he had ever seen.", "내 손에서 제 손으로 손수레를 넘겨받으며, 그는 난생처음 손수레를 보았던 때의 우스운 이야기 하나를 들려주었다."], ["It was in Sag Harbor.", "그것은 새그 하버에서의 일이었다."], ["The owners of his ship, it seems, had lent him one, in which to carry his heavy chest to his boarding house.", "듣자 하니 그의 배 선주들이 무거운 궤짝을 하숙집까지 나르라고 손수레 하나를 빌려준 모양이었다."], ["Not to seem ignorant about the thing—though in truth he was entirely so, concerning the precise way in which to manage the barrow—Queequeg puts his chest upon it; lashes it fast; and then shoulders the barrow and marches up the wharf.", "손수레를 다루는 정확한 방법에 대해 사실은 전혀 무지했으면서도, 그에 대해 모르는 티를 내지 않으려고, 퀴케그는 제 궤짝을 손수레 위에 올려놓고 단단히 동여맨 다음, 그 손수레를 어깨에 들쳐 메고 부두를 따라 행진해 올라갔던 것이다."], ["“Why,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said I, “Queequeg, you might have known better than that, one would think.", "하고 내가 말했다. \"퀴케그, 그쯤은 알 만도 했을 텐데."], ["Didn’t the people laugh?”", "사람들이 웃지 않던가?\""]], [["Upon this, he told me another story.", "이 말에 그는 또 다른 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["The people of his island of Rokovoko, it seems, at their wedding feasts express the fragrant water of young cocoanuts into a large stained calabash like a punchbowl; and this punchbowl always forms the great central ornament on the braided mat where the feast is held.", "듣자 하니, 그의 고향 섬 로코보코의 사람들은 혼인 잔치 때 어린 야자열매의 향긋한 물을 짜내어 펀치 그릇처럼 큼직한, 얼룩진 호리병박 속에 담아 둔다고 했다. 그리고 이 펀치 그릇은 잔치가 벌어지는 엮은 돗자리 위에서 언제나 한가운데를 차지하는 큰 장식이 된다는 것이었다."], ["Now a certain grand merchant ship once touched at Rokovoko, and its commander—from all accounts, a very stately punctilious gentleman, at least for a sea captain—this commander was invited to the wedding feast of Queequeg’s sister, a pretty young princess just turned of ten.", "그런데 언젠가 어느 거대한 상선 한 척이 로코보코에 들렀고, 그 선장은 — 전해지는 바로는 적어도 바다 선장치고는 매우 위엄 있고 격식을 차리는 신사였다는데 — 그 선장이 갓 열 살을 넘긴 어여쁜 어린 공주, 곧 퀴케그의 누이의 혼인 잔치에 초대되었다. 그런데 모든 혼인 손님들이 신부의 대나무 오두막에 모였을 때, 이 선장이 안으로 들어와 상석에 앉게 되었으니, 펀치 그릇을 마주한 자리, 곧 대제사장과 임금 폐하, 다시 말해 퀴케그의 아버지 사이에 자리를 잡은 것이다."], ["Well; when all the wedding guests were assembled at the bride’s bamboo cottage, this Captain marches in, and being assigned the post of honor, placed himself over against the punchbowl, and between the High Priest and his majesty the King, Queequeg’s father.", "식전 기도가 올려졌는데 — 그 사람들에게도 우리처럼 식전 기도가 있었으니 — 다만 퀴케그가 내게 말하기를, 그런 때에 제 접시를 내려다보는 우리와는 달리, 그들은 오히려 오리를 흉내 내어 모든 잔치를 베푸시는 위대한 분을 우러러본다고 했다. 아무튼 식전 기도가 올려지자, 대제사장은 그 섬의 까마득한 옛 의식으로 잔치의 막을 열었다."], ["Grace being said,—for those people have their grace as well as we—though Queequeg told me that unlike us, who at such times look downwards to our platters, they, on the contrary, copying the ducks, glance upwards to the great Giver of all feasts—Grace, I say, being said, the High Priest opens the banquet by the immemorial ceremony of the island; that is, dipping his consecrated and consecrating fingers into the bowl before the blessed beverage circulates.", "그것은 곧, 그 복된 음료가 돌려지기 전에 자신의 신성하고 또 신성하게 하는 손가락을 그릇 속에 담그는 일이었다. 자신이 제사장 옆자리에 앉은 것을 보고, 또 그 의식을 눈여겨보며, 스스로를 — 배의 선장인 자신이 — 한낱 섬나라 임금보다, 더구나 임금 자신의 집에서야말로 마땅히 윗자리에 있다고 여긴 선장은, 태연히 그 펀치 그릇에 손을 씻기 시작했다."], ["Seeing himself placed next the Priest, and noting the ceremony, and thinking himself—being Captain of a ship—as having plain precedence over a mere island King, especially in the King’s own house—the Captain coolly proceeds to wash his hands in the punchbowl;—taking it I suppose for a huge finger-glass.", "짐작건대 그것을 커다란 핑거볼쯤으로 여긴 모양이었다. \"자,\""], ["“Now,”", "하고 퀴케그가 말했다."], ["said Queequeg, “what you tink now?—Didn’t our people laugh?”", "\"이제 자네는 어떻게 생각하나? — 우리 사람들이 웃지 않았겠나?\""]], [["At last, passage paid, and luggage safe, we stood on board the schooner.", "마침내 뱃삯을 치르고 짐도 무사히 실은 채, 우리는 스쿠너 갑판에 올라섰다."], ["Hoisting sail, it glided down the Acushnet river.", "돛을 올리자 배는 아쿠시넷 강을 미끄러져 내려갔다."], ["On one side, New Bedford rose in terraces of streets, their ice-covered trees all glittering in the clear, cold air.", "한쪽 편에서는 뉴베드퍼드가 층층이 이어진 거리들로 솟아올랐고, 얼음에 덮인 나무들이 맑고 차가운 공기 속에서 온통 반짝거렸다. 부두마다 술통 위에 술통을 쌓아 올린 거대한 언덕과 산들이 쌓여 있었고, 그 곁에는 온 세계를 떠돌던 포경선들이 마침내 말없이 안전하게 매여 잠잠히 누워 있었다."], ["Huge hills and mountains of casks on casks were piled upon her wharves, and side by side the world-wandering whale ships lay silent and safely moored at last; while from others came a sound of carpenters and coopers, with blended noises of fires and forges to melt the pitch, all betokening that new cruises were on the start; that one most perilous and long voyage ended, only begins a second; and a second ended, only begins a third, and so on, for ever and for aye.", "한편 다른 배들에서는 목수와 통장이들의 소리가 들려왔고, 역청을 녹이는 불과 풀무 소리가 뒤섞여 들려오니, 이 모든 것이 새로운 항해가 막 시작되려 함을 알리고 있었다. 더없이 위험하고 기나긴 항해 하나가 끝나면 그저 또 다른 항해가 시작될 뿐이며, 두 번째 항해가 끝나면 그저 세 번째가 시작될 뿐, 그렇게 영영, 끝없이 이어지는 것이다."], ["Such is the endlessness, yea, the intolerableness of all earthly effort.", "모든 세속적 수고란 이토록 끝이 없으며, 그렇다, 견디기 힘든 것이다."]], [["Gaining the more open water, the bracing breeze waxed fresh; the little Moss tossed the quick foam from her bows, as a young colt his snortings. How I snuffed that Tartar air!—how I spurned that turnpike earth!—that common highway all over dented with the marks of slavish heels and hoofs; and turned me to admire the magnanimity of the sea which will permit no records.", "좀 더 탁 트인 바다에 이르자 상쾌한 바닷바람이 한결 싱그러워졌다. 작은 모스호는 어린 망아지가 콧김을 내뿜듯 뱃머리에서 잽싼 물거품을 흩뿌렸다. 아, 나는 그 거친 타타르의 공기를 얼마나 들이켰던가! 아, 나는 그 통행세 받는 땅덩이를 얼마나 발로 차 버렸던가! 노예 같은 발뒤꿈치와 말발굽 자국으로 온통 패인 그 흔한 큰길을 떨쳐 버리고, 어떠한 자취도 남기지 않는 바다의 너그러움을 우러러보았다."]], [["At the same foam-fountain, Queequeg seemed to drink and reel with me.", "바로 그 물거품의 샘에서, 퀴케그도 나와 함께 들이켜며 비틀거리는 듯했다."], ["His dusky nostrils swelled apart; he showed his filed and pointed teeth.", "거무스름한 그의 콧구멍이 벌름거리며 부풀어 올랐고, 그는 줄로 갈아 뾰족하게 만든 이를 드러내 보였다."], ["On, on we flew; and our offing gained, the Moss did homage to the blast; ducked and dived her bows as a slave before the Sultan.", "우리는 자꾸자꾸 내달렸다. 그리고 먼바다에 이르자 모스호는 돌풍에 경의를 표하듯, 술탄 앞의 노예처럼 뱃머리를 숙이고 자맥질했다."], ["Sideways leaning, we sideways darted; every ropeyarn tingling like a wire; the two tall masts buckling like Indian canes in land tornadoes.", "옆으로 기울며 우리도 옆으로 쏜살같이 나아갔고, 밧줄 가닥마다 철사처럼 짜릿하게 울렸으며, 두 개의 높은 돛대는 육지의 회오리바람 속 인도산 등나무 줄기처럼 휘어졌다."], ["So full of this reeling scene were we, as we stood by the plunging bowsprit, that for some time we did not notice the jeering glances of the passengers, a lubber-like assembly, who marvelled that two fellow beings should be so companionable; as though a white man were anything more dignified than a whitewashed negro.", "곤두박질치는 제1사장 곁에 서 있던 우리는 이 비틀거리는 광경에 어찌나 빠져 있었던지, 한동안 우리는 승객들의 비웃는 눈길을 알아채지도 못했다. 풋내기 뱃사람 같은 그 무리는, 마치 백인이 회칠한 흑인보다 더 품위 있기라도 하다는 듯, 두 인간이 그토록 가까이 어울려 다니는 것을 신기해했다."], ["But there were some boobies and bumpkins there, who, by their intense greenness, must have come from the heart and centre of all verdure.", "그러나 그중에는 멍청이며 촌뜨기도 몇 있었는데, 그 지독한 풋내로 보아 온갖 풋것의 한복판, 그 핵심에서 막 나온 게 틀림없었다."], ["Queequeg caught one of these young saplings mimicking him behind his back.", "퀴케그는 그 어린 햇가지 같은 자들 중 하나가 등 뒤에서 자신을 흉내 내는 것을 알아챘다."], ["I thought the bumpkin’s hour of doom was come.", "나는 그 촌뜨기에게 최후의 순간이 닥쳤구나 싶었다."], ["Dropping his harpoon, the brawny savage caught him in his arms, and by an almost miraculous dexterity and strength, sent him high up bodily into the air; then slightly tapping his stern in mid-somerset, the fellow landed with bursting lungs upon his feet, while Queequeg, turning his back upon him, lighted his tomahawk pipe and passed it to me for a puff.", "작살을 내던진 우람한 야만인은 두 팔로 그를 거머잡더니, 거의 기적과도 같은 날렵함과 힘으로 그를 몸째 공중 높이 날려 버렸다. 그러고는 공중제비 한가운데서 그자의 엉덩이를 가볍게 톡 쳐 주니, 그 사내는 터질 듯한 폐를 안고 두 발로 착지했고, 그동안 퀴케그는 그에게 등을 돌린 채 토마호크 담뱃대에 불을 붙여 내게 한 모금 빨라며 건네주었다."]], [["“Capting!", "\"선장님!"], ["Capting!”", "선장님!\""], ["yelled the bumpkin, running towards that officer; “Capting, Capting, here’s the devil.”", "하고 그 촌뜨기가 선장을 향해 달려가며 소리쳤다. \"선장님, 선장님, 여기 악마가 있어요.\""]], [["“Hallo, you sir,”", "\"이봐, 자네 말이야,\""], ["cried the Captain, a gaunt rib of the sea, stalking up to Queequeg, “what in thunder do you mean by that?", "하고 선장이 외쳤다. 그는 비쩍 마른 바다의 갈빗대 같은 사내였는데, 성큼성큼 퀴케그에게 다가서며 말했다."], ["Don’t you know you might have killed that chap?”", "\"도대체 그게 무슨 짓이야? 저 친구를 죽일 뻔했다는 걸 모르나?\""]], [["“What him say?”", "\"저 사람 뭐라 그래?\""], ["said Queequeg, as he mildly turned to me.", "하고 퀴케그가 순순히 내게 돌아서며 물었다."]], [["“He say,”", "\"저 사람 말이,\""], ["said I, “that you came near kill-e that man there,”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"자네가 저기 저 사람을 죽일 뻔했다는 거야.\""], ["pointing to the still shivering greenhorn.", "하며 나는 아직도 벌벌 떨고 있는 풋내기를 가리켰다."]], [["“Kill-e,” cried Queequeg, twisting his tattooed face into an unearthly expression of disdain, “ah! him bevy small-e fish-e; Queequeg no kill-e so small-e fish-e; Queequeg kill-e big whale!”", "\"죽이다니,\" 하고 퀴케그가 외쳤다. 그는 문신 새긴 얼굴을 비틀어 이 세상 것 같지 않은 경멸의 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"허! 저놈은 쪼끄만 물고기야. 퀴케그는 그런 쪼끄만 물고기 안 죽여. 퀴케그는 큰 고래 죽여!\""]], [["“Look you,”", "\"이봐,\" 하고 선장이 으르렁댔다."], ["roared the Captain, “I’ll kill-e you, you cannibal, if you try any more of your tricks aboard here; so mind your eye.”", "\"이 식인종아, 이 배 위에서 또 그따위 수작을 부렸다간 내가 너를 죽여 버릴 테다. 그러니 눈 똑바로 뜨고 조심해.\""]], [["But it so happened just then, that it was high time for the Captain to mind his own eye.", "그런데 바로 그때, 마침 선장 자신이야말로 제 눈을 똑바로 떠야 할 판이었다."], ["The prodigious strain upon the main-sail had parted the weather-sheet, and the tremendous boom was now flying from side to side, completely sweeping the entire after part of the deck.", "주돛에 걸린 엄청난 장력이 바람 쪽 돛줄을 끊어 버려, 그 거대한 활대가 좌우로 마구 흔들리며 갑판의 뒷부분을 통째로 휩쓸고 있었던 것이다."], ["The poor fellow whom Queequeg had handled so roughly, was swept overboard; all hands were in a panic; and to attempt snatching at the boom to stay it, seemed madness.", "퀴케그가 그토록 거칠게 다루었던 그 가엾은 친구는 바다로 휩쓸려 떨어지고 말았다. 모든 선원이 공황에 빠졌고, 활대를 붙잡아 멈춰 보려는 것은 미친 짓이나 다름없어 보였다."], ["It flew from right to left, and back again, almost in one ticking of a watch, and every instant seemed on the point of snapping into splinters.", "활대는 시계가 한 번 째깍하는 사이에 거의 오른쪽에서 왼쪽으로, 다시 되돌아오기를 거듭했고, 매 순간 산산조각으로 부러질 듯이 보였다."], ["Nothing was done, and nothing seemed capable of being done; those on deck rushed towards the bows, and stood eyeing the boom as if it were the lower jaw of an exasperated whale.", "아무것도 손쓸 수 없었고, 또 무엇도 할 수 있을 것 같지 않았다. 갑판 위의 사람들은 뱃머리 쪽으로 우르르 몰려가, 마치 그 활대가 성난 고래의 아래턱이라도 되는 양 빤히 노려보며 서 있었다."], ["In the midst of this consternation, Queequeg dropped deftly to his knees, and crawling under the path of the boom, whipped hold of a rope, secured one end to the bulwarks, and then flinging the other like a lasso, caught it round the boom as it swept over his head, and at the next jerk, the spar was that way trapped, and all was safe.", "이 아수라장 한복판에서, 퀴케그는 날쌔게 무릎을 꿇더니 활대가 지나는 길 아래로 기어들어 밧줄 하나를 낚아채, 그 한쪽 끝을 뱃전에 단단히 매고는, 다른 한쪽 끝을 올가미처럼 휙 던져 머리 위로 휩쓸려 지나가는 활대에 걸었다. 그리고 다음번에 활대가 홱 당겨지는 순간, 그 가로대는 그렇게 옭매여 모든 것이 안전해졌다."], ["The schooner was run into the wind, and while the hands were clearing away the stern boat, Queequeg, stripped to the waist, darted from the side with a long living arc of a leap.", "스쿠너는 바람 쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌렸고, 선원들이 고물의 보트를 풀어 내리는 동안, 퀴케그는 허리까지 옷을 벗어젖히고 길고 살아 있는 활처럼 도약하여 뱃전에서 뛰어들었다."], ["For three minutes or more he was seen swimming like a dog, throwing his long arms straight out before him, and by turns revealing his brawny shoulders through the freezing foam.", "삼 분 남짓 동안, 그가 개처럼 헤엄치며 긴 두 팔을 앞으로 곧게 내뻗고, 번갈아 그 우람한 어깨를 얼어붙을 듯한 물거품 사이로 드러내는 것이 보였다."], ["I looked at the grand and glorious fellow, but saw no one to be saved.", "나는 그 장대하고 영광스러운 사내를 바라보았으나, 구해야 할 사람은 보이지 않았다."], ["The greenhorn had gone down.", "풋내기는 이미 가라앉아 버린 것이다."], ["Shooting himself perpendicularly from the water, Queequeg, now took an instant’s glance around him, and seeming to see just how matters were, dived down and disappeared.", "퀴케그는 물에서 몸을 수직으로 솟구쳐 올려, 한순간 주위를 둘러보고는, 형편이 어떠한지 곧바로 알아챈 듯 물속으로 자맥질해 사라졌다."], ["A few minutes more, and he rose again, one arm still striking out, and with the other dragging a lifeless form.", "다시 몇 분이 지나자 그는 한쪽 팔로는 여전히 물살을 가르며, 다른 팔로는 생기 없는 몸뚱이를 끌고서 떠올랐다."], ["The boat soon picked them up.", "보트가 곧 그들을 끌어올렸다."], ["The poor bumpkin was restored.", "가엾은 촌뜨기는 되살아났다."], ["All hands voted Queequeg a noble trump; the captain begged his pardon.", "모든 선원이 퀴케그를 고결한 호걸이라 입을 모았고, 선장은 그에게 용서를 빌었다."], ["From that hour I clove to Queequeg like a barnacle; yea, till poor Queequeg took his last long dive.", "그 시각부터 나는 따개비처럼 퀴케그에게 들러붙었다. 그렇다, 가엾은 퀴케그가 마지막 긴 자맥질을 할 그때까지도."]], [["Was there ever such unconsciousness?", "이토록 자기 행동에 무심한 사람이 또 있었을까?"], ["He did not seem to think that he at all deserved a medal from the Humane and Magnanimous Societies.", "그는 자신이 인도주의 협회나 자선 협회로부터 훈장을 받을 만하다고는 조금도 생각하지 않는 듯했다. 그는 그저 물을 — 마실 물을 — 소금기를 닦아 낼 무언가를 청할 뿐이었다."], ["He only asked for water—fresh water—something to wipe the brine off; that done, he put on dry clothes, lighted his pipe, and leaning against the bulwarks, and mildly eyeing those around him, seemed to be saying to himself—“It’s a mutual, joint-stock world, in all meridians.", "그러고 나서 그는 마른 옷으로 갈아입고 담뱃대에 불을 붙인 뒤, 뱃전에 기대어 주위 사람들을 온화하게 바라보며, 마치 혼잣말로 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"이 세상은 어느 자오선에서나 서로 돕는 합자(合資)의 세상이지."], ["We cannibals must help these Christians.”", "우리 식인종들도 이 기독교인들을 도와야 하는 법이고.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 14. Nantucket.", "# CHAPTER 14. Nantucket."]], [["Nothing more happened on the passage worthy the mentioning; so, after a fine run, we safely arrived in Nantucket.", "항해 중에 더 언급할 만한 일은 일어나지 않았다. 그리하여 우리는 순조롭게 바다를 달려 낸터킷(Nantucket)에 무사히 도착했다."]], [["Nantucket!", "낸터킷!"], ["Take out your map and look at it.", "지도를 펼쳐 그곳을 보라. 그것이 세상의 얼마나 외진 한구석을 차지하고 있는지를 보라."], ["See what a real corner of the world it occupies; how it stands there, away off shore, more lonely than the Eddystone lighthouse.", "어떻게 그렇게 뭍에서 멀찍이 떨어져, 에디스톤 등대보다도 더 쓸쓸하게 서 있는지를 보라. 보라, 그저 작은 언덕 하나, 모래로 굽은 팔꿈치 하나일 뿐이다."], ["Look at it—a mere hillock, and elbow of sand; all beach, without a background.", "온통 해변일 뿐 배경이라 할 만한 것이 없다. 거기에는 압지(壓紙) 대용으로 이십 년을 써도 남을 만큼의 모래가 있다."], ["There is more sand there than you would use in twenty years as a substitute for blotting paper.", "짓궂은 익살꾼들은 이렇게 말할 것이다. 그곳에서는 잡초마저 심어야 하니 저절로는 자라지 않으며, 캐나다엉겅퀴를 수입해 와야 하고, 기름통의 새는 구멍을 막을 마개 하나를 구하려고 바다 건너로 사람을 보내야 한다고."], ["Some gamesome wights will tell you that they have to plant weeds there, they don’t grow naturally; that they import Canada thistles; that they have to send beyond seas for a spile to stop a leak in an oil cask; that pieces of wood in Nantucket are carried about like bits of the true cross in Rome; that people there plant toadstools before their houses, to get under the shade in summer time; that one blade of grass makes an oasis, three blades in a day’s walk a prairie; that they wear quicksand shoes, something like Laplander snow-shoes; that they are so shut up, belted about, every way inclosed, surrounded, and made an utter island of by the ocean, that to their very chairs and tables small clams will sometimes be found adhering, as to the backs of sea turtles.", "또 낸터킷에서는 나뭇조각이 로마에서 진짜 십자가 조각처럼 떠받들려 다니며, 사람들은 여름철 그늘을 얻으려고 집 앞에 독버섯을 심고, 풀 한 포기가 오아시스를 이루고 하루를 걸어도 세 포기면 대초원을 이루며, 라플란드 사람의 눈신 비슷한 모래신을 신는다고. 또 그곳 사람들은 바다에 의해 사방이 갇히고, 띠처럼 둘러싸이고, 온통 에워싸여 완전한 섬으로 만들어진 까닭에, 그들의 의자와 탁자에까지도 마치 바다거북의 등딱지에 붙듯 작은 대합조개가 들러붙어 있곤 한다고."], ["But these extravaganzas only show that Nantucket is no Illinois.", "그러나 이런 허풍은 다만 낸터킷이 일리노이 같은 곳이 아님을 보여 줄 뿐이다."]], [["Look now at the wondrous traditional story of how this island was settled by the red-men.", "이제 이 섬이 어떻게 인디언들에 의해 정착되었는지를 전하는 그 경이로운 옛이야기를 보라."], ["Thus goes the legend.", "전설은 이러하다."], ["In olden times an eagle swooped down upon the New England coast, and carried off an infant Indian in his talons.", "옛적에 독수리 한 마리가 뉴잉글랜드 해안으로 내리꽂혀 인디언 갓난아기 하나를 발톱으로 채어 갔다."], ["With loud lament the parents saw their child borne out of sight over the wide waters.", "부모는 크게 슬피 울며 제 아이가 드넓은 바다 너머로 실려 가 시야에서 사라지는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["They resolved to follow in the same direction.", "그들은 같은 방향을 좇기로 결심했다."], ["Setting out in their canoes, after a perilous passage they discovered the island, and there they found an empty ivory casket,—the poor little Indian’s skeleton.", "카누를 타고 나선 그들은 위태로운 항해 끝에 그 섬을 발견했고, 거기서 텅 빈 상아 함 하나를 찾아냈으니 — 바로 그 가엾은 어린 인디언의 백골이었다."]], [["What wonder, then, that these Nantucketers, born on a beach, should take to the sea for a livelihood!", "그러니 해변에서 태어난 이 낸터킷 사람들이 바다로 나아가 생계를 꾸린다 한들 무엇이 놀랍겠는가! 그들은 처음에는 모래밭에서 게와 대합을 잡았다."], ["They first caught crabs and quohogs in the sand; grown bolder, they waded out with nets for mackerel; more experienced, they pushed off in boats and captured cod; and at last, launching a navy of great ships on the sea, explored this watery world; put an incessant belt of circumnavigations round it; peeped in at Behring’s Straits; and in all seasons and all oceans declared everlasting war with the mightiest animated mass that has survived the flood; most monstrous and most mountainous!", "더 대담해지자 그물을 들고 물속으로 걸어 들어가 고등어를 잡았다. 더 노련해지자 보트를 밀어내어 대구를 잡았다."], ["That Himmalehan, salt-sea Mastodon, clothed with such portentousness of unconscious power, that his very panics are more to be dreaded than his most fearless and malicious assaults!", "그리고 마침내 거대한 함대를 바다에 띄워 이 물의 세계를 탐험했고, 끊임없이 이어지는 세계 일주의 띠를 그 둘레에 둘렀으며, 베링 해협을 들여다보았고, 모든 계절과 모든 대양에서 대홍수를 견디고 살아남은 가장 강력한 생물 덩어리, 가장 괴물 같고 가장 산더미 같은 그것을 향해 영원한 전쟁을 선포했다! 히말라야 같은 그 소금바다의 마스토돈, 무의식적인 힘의 그토록 무시무시한 위용으로 둘러싸여, 그 가장 두려움 없고 악의에 찬 공격보다도 그것이 겁에 질려 날뛰는 순간이 오히려 더 두려운 그 짐승을!"]], [["And thus have these naked Nantucketers, these sea hermits, issuing from their ant-hill in the sea, overrun and conquered the watery world like so many Alexanders; parcelling out among them the Atlantic, Pacific, and Indian oceans, as the three pirate powers did Poland.", "그리하여 이 헐벗은 낸터킷 사람들, 이 바다의 은둔자들은 바닷속 제 개미집에서 쏟아져 나와, 마치 수많은 알렉산드로스처럼 물의 세계를 휩쓸고 정복했다. 세 해적 강국이 폴란드를 나누어 가졌듯이, 그들끼리 대서양과 태평양과 인도양을 나누어 가진 것이다."], ["Let America add Mexico to Texas, and pile Cuba upon Canada; let the English overswarm all India, and hang out their blazing banner from the sun; two thirds of this terraqueous globe are the Nantucketer’s.", "아메리카가 텍사스에 멕시코를 더하고 캐나다 위에 쿠바를 얹는다 해도, 영국인들이 인도 전역을 새카맣게 뒤덮고 태양으로부터 그 이글거리는 깃발을 내건다 해도, 이 물과 뭍으로 이루어진 지구의 삼분의 이는 낸터킷 사람의 것이다. 바다는 그의 것이기 때문이다."], ["For the sea is his; he owns it, as Emperors own empires; other seamen having but a right of way through it.", "황제가 제국을 소유하듯 그는 바다를 소유한다. 다른 뱃사람들은 그저 그곳을 지나갈 통행권을 가질 뿐이다."], ["Merchant ships are but extension bridges; armed ones but floating forts; even pirates and privateers, though following the sea as highwaymen the road, they but plunder other ships, other fragments of the land like themselves, without seeking to draw their living from the bottomless deep itself.", "상선이란 그저 늘어뜨린 다리에 지나지 않고, 군함이란 떠다니는 요새에 지나지 않는다. 해적과 사략선조차도 노상강도가 길을 따라 다니듯 바다를 따라 다니지만, 그들은 다만 다른 배들, 곧 자기들과 같은 뭍의 부스러기들을 약탈할 뿐, 밑바닥 없는 깊은 바다 그 자체에서 생계를 끌어내려 하지는 않는다."], ["The Nantucketer, he alone resides and riots on the sea; he alone, in Bible language, goes down to it in ships; to and fro ploughing it as his own special plantation.", "낸터킷 사람, 오직 그만이 바다에 거주하며 바다 위에서 마음껏 누린다. 성경의 표현을 빌리자면, 오직 그만이 배를 타고 바다로 내려가, 그것을 제 전용 농장처럼 이리저리 갈아엎는다."], ["There is his home; there lies his business, which a Noah’s flood would not interrupt, though it overwhelmed all the millions in China.", "그곳에 그의 집이 있고, 그곳에 그의 생업이 놓여 있으니, 노아의 홍수가 중국의 수백만 백성을 모조리 삼킨다 한들 그의 일은 멈추지 않을 것이다."], ["He lives on the sea, as prairie cocks in the prairie; he hides among the waves, he climbs them as chamois hunters climb the Alps.", "대초원의 멧닭이 대초원에 살듯 그는 바다에 산다. 그는 물결 사이에 몸을 숨기고, 샤무아 사냥꾼이 알프스를 오르듯 물결을 타고 오른다."], ["For years he knows not the land; so that when he comes to it at last, it smells like another world, more strangely than the moon would to an Earthsman.", "여러 해 동안 그는 뭍을 모르고 지내니, 마침내 뭍에 닿으면 그것은 또 다른 세계처럼, 뭍사람에게 달이 풍기는 것보다도 더 낯설게 풍겨 온다."], ["With the landless gull, that at sunset folds her wings and is rocked to sleep between billows; so at nightfall, the Nantucketer, out of sight of land, furls his sails, and lays him to his rest, while under his very pillow rush herds of walruses and whales.", "해 질 녘이면 날개를 접고 큰 물결 사이에서 흔들리며 잠드는 저 뭍 없는 갈매기처럼, 낸터킷 사람도 밤이 내리면 뭍이 보이지 않는 곳에서 돛을 거두고 쉼에 든다. 바로 그 베개 밑으로 바다코끼리와 고래의 무리가 떼 지어 내달리는 동안."]], [["# CHAPTER 15. Chowder.", "# CHAPTER 15. Chowder."]], [["It was quite late in the evening when the little Moss came snugly to anchor, and Queequeg and I went ashore; so we could attend to no business that day, at least none but a supper and a bed.", "작은 모스호가 아늑하게 닻을 내렸을 때는 저녁도 꽤 늦은 시각이었고, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 나는 뭍으로 올라갔다. 그래서 그날은 아무 볼일도 볼 수 없었으니, 적어도 저녁 식사와 잠자리 외에는 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["The landlord of the Spouter-Inn had recommended us to his cousin Hosea Hussey of the Try Pots, whom he asserted to be the proprietor of one of the best kept hotels in all Nantucket, and moreover he had assured us that Cousin Hosea, as he called him, was famous for his chowders.", "물보라 여관의 주인장은 우리에게 자기 사촌인 트라이 포츠의 호세아 허시를 추천해 주었는데, 그가 낸터킷(Nantucket) 전역에서 가장 잘 관리되는 호텔 가운데 하나의 주인이라고 장담했다. 게다가 그는 자기가 호세아 사촌이라 부르는 그 사람이 차우더 솜씨로 유명하다고 우리에게 보증했다."], ["In short, he plainly hinted that we could not possibly do better than try pot-luck at the Try Pots.", "한마디로, 그는 우리가 트라이 포츠에서 차우더를 맛보는 것보다 더 나은 선택은 결코 없으리라고 노골적으로 넌지시 일러 준 셈이었다. 그러나 그가 우리에게 일러 준 방향이라는 것이 문제였다."], ["But the directions he had given us about keeping a yellow warehouse on our starboard hand till we opened a white church to the larboard, and then keeping that on the larboard hand till we made a corner three points to the starboard, and that done, then ask the first man we met where the place was: these crooked directions of his very much puzzled us at first, especially as, at the outset, Queequeg insisted that the yellow warehouse—our first point of departure—must be left on the larboard hand, whereas I had understood Peter Coffin to say it was on the starboard.", "노란 창고를 오른쪽에 끼고 가다가 왼쪽으로 하얀 교회가 보이는 곳까지 간 다음, 그 교회를 왼쪽에 끼고 가다가 오른쪽으로 세 점쯤 트인 모퉁이에 이르거든, 그제야 처음 만나는 사람에게 그곳이 어디인지 물어보라는 것이었다. 이 꼬불꼬불한 방향 안내는 처음에 우리를 무척이나 헷갈리게 했는데, 특히 출발점에서부터 퀴퀘그가 우리의 첫 기점인 노란 창고를 왼쪽에 두고 가야 한다고 우기는 통에 더욱 그러했다."], ["However, by dint of beating about a little in the dark, and now and then knocking up a peaceable inhabitant to inquire the way, we at last came to something which there was no mistaking.", "나는 피터 코핀이 그것을 오른쪽에 둔다고 말한 것으로 알아들었던 것이다. 그렇지만 어둠 속을 조금 헤매고, 이따금 평화로이 잠든 주민을 깨워 길을 묻고 한 끝에, 우리는 마침내 도저히 잘못 볼 수 없는 무언가에 다다랐다."]], [["Two enormous wooden pots painted black, and suspended by asses’ ears, swung from the cross-trees of an old top-mast, planted in front of an old doorway.", "검게 칠한 거대한 나무 솥 두 개가 당나귀 귀 모양의 고리에 매달려, 낡은 출입구 앞에 박아 세운 오래된 중간 돛대의 가로대에 흔들리고 있었다."], ["The horns of the cross-trees were sawed off on the other side, so that this old top-mast looked not a little like a gallows.", "가로대의 한쪽 끝은 톱으로 잘려 나가 있어서, 이 낡은 중간 돛대는 영락없이 교수대처럼 보였다. 어쩌면 그때의 나는 그런 인상에 지나치게 예민했는지도 모르겠다."], ["Perhaps I was over sensitive to such impressions at the time, but I could not help staring at this gallows with a vague misgiving.", "그러나 나는 어렴풋한 불안감을 품은 채 이 교수대를 빤히 바라보지 않을 수 없었다. 남아 있는 두 개의 가로대 끝을 올려다보노라니 목덜미가 뻐근하게 결렸다."], ["A sort of crick was in my neck as I gazed up to the two remaining horns; yes, two of them, one for Queequeg, and one for me.", "그렇다, 두 개였다. 하나는 퀴퀘그의 몫, 하나는 내 몫."], ["It’s ominous, thinks I.", "불길하다, 하고 나는 생각했다."], ["A Coffin my Innkeeper upon landing in my first whaling port; tombstones staring at me in the whalemen’s chapel; and here a gallows!", "첫 포경 항구에 발을 디디자마자 코핀(관)이라는 자가 내 여관 주인이었고, 고래잡이들의 예배당에서는 묘비들이 나를 노려보았으며, 이제는 여기 교수대까지 있다!"], ["and a pair of prodigious black pots too!", "게다가 어마어마한 검은 솥 한 쌍도!"], ["Are these last throwing out oblique hints touching Tophet?", "이 마지막 것들은 지옥의 불구덩이에 관해 슬며시 암시를 던지고 있는 것일까?"]], [["I was called from these reflections by the sight of a freckled woman with yellow hair and a yellow gown, standing in the porch of the inn, under a dull red lamp swinging there, that looked much like an injured eye, and carrying on a brisk scolding with a man in a purple woollen shirt.", "이런 상념에서 나를 불러낸 것은, 주근깨가 박힌 한 여인의 모습이었다. 노란 머리에 노란 가운을 걸친 그 여인은 여관 현관에 서 있었고, 그 위에는 상처 입은 눈알처럼 보이는 흐릿한 붉은 등불이 흔들리고 있었으며, 그녀는 자줏빛 양모 셔츠를 걸친 한 남자에게 기세 좋게 잔소리를 퍼붓고 있었다."]], [["“Get along with ye,”", "\"썩 꺼지지 못해,\""], ["said she to the man, “or I’ll be combing ye!”", "그녀가 그 남자에게 말했다. \"안 그러면 머리채를 잡고 빗어 줄 테다!\""]], [["“Come on, Queequeg,”", "\"이리 오게, 퀴퀘그,\""], ["said I, “all right.", "내가 말했다. \"괜찮아."], ["There’s Mrs. Hussey.”", "저기 허시 부인이 있군.\""]], [["And so it turned out; Mr.", "그리고 정말 그러했다."], ["Hosea Hussey being from home, but leaving Mrs.", "호세아 허시 씨는 집을 비웠으나, 허시 부인이 그의 모든 일을 도맡아 처리할 만큼 완벽히 능숙했다."], ["Hussey entirely competent to attend to all his affairs.", "우리가 저녁 식사와 잠자리를 원한다고 알리자, 허시 부인은 잔소리를 잠시 미뤄 두고 우리를 자그마한 방으로 안내했다."], ["Upon making known our desires for a supper and a bed, Mrs.", "그러고는 방금 끝난 식사의 잔해가 널린 식탁에 우리를 앉히더니, 우리 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다."], ["Hussey, postponing further scolding for the present, ushered us into a little room, and seating us at a table spread with the relics of a recently concluded repast, turned round to us and said—“Clam or Cod?”", "\"조개? 대구?\""]], [["“What’s that about Cods, ma’am?”", "\"대구라니 무슨 말씀이신지요, 부인?\""], ["said I, with much politeness.", "나는 무척 공손하게 물었다."]], [["“Clam or Cod?”", "\"조개? 대구?\""], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되풀이했다."]], [["“A clam for supper?", "\"저녁으로 조개라고요?"], ["a cold clam; is that what you mean, Mrs.", "차가운 조개 말입니까. 그게 부인의 뜻인가요?\""], ["Hussey?”", "내가 말했다."], ["says I, “but that’s a rather cold and clammy reception in the winter time, ain’t it, Mrs. Hussey?”", "\"그렇지만 겨울철에 그건 좀 차갑고 끈적한 대접이 아닌가요, 허시 부인?\""]], [["But being in a great hurry to resume scolding the man in the purple Shirt, who was waiting for it in the entry, and seeming to hear nothing but the word “clam,” Mrs.", "그러나 허시 부인은 현관에서 잔소리를 기다리고 있는 자줏빛 셔츠의 남자에게 어서 다시 호통을 치고 싶어 안달이 난 데다, \"조개\"라는 말 외에는 아무것도 못 들은 모양이었다."], ["Hussey hurried towards an open door leading to the kitchen, and bawling out “clam for two,”", "그녀는 부엌으로 통하는 열린 문 쪽으로 서둘러 가더니 \"둘 다 조개로!\""], ["disappeared.", "하고 크게 외치고는 사라져 버렸다."]], [["“Queequeg,”", "\"퀴퀘그,\""], ["said I, “do you think that we can make out a supper for us both on one clam?”", "내가 말했다. \"자네 생각엔 우리 둘이 조개 하나로 저녁을 때울 수 있을 것 같나?\""]], [["However, a warm savory steam from the kitchen served to belie the apparently cheerless prospect before us.", "그러나 부엌에서 풍겨 오는 따스하고 구수한 김이, 우리 앞에 놓인 그 음울해 보이던 전망을 거짓으로 만들어 주었다."], ["But when that smoking chowder came in, the mystery was delightfully explained.", "그리고 김이 모락모락 나는 차우더가 들어오자, 그 수수께끼는 더없이 흐뭇하게 풀렸다."], ["Oh, sweet friends!", "오, 다정한 벗들이여!"], ["hearken to me.", "내 말을 들어 보라."], ["It was made of small juicy clams, scarcely bigger than hazel nuts, mixed with pounded ship biscuit, and salted pork cut up into little flakes; the whole enriched with butter, and plentifully seasoned with pepper and salt.", "그것은 개암나무 열매보다 겨우 클까 말까 한 작고 즙 많은 조개에다, 빻은 선박용 비스킷과 잘게 부순 소금 절인 돼지고기를 섞고, 전체에 버터를 듬뿍 넣어 풍미를 더하고 후추와 소금으로 넉넉히 간을 한 것이었다."], ["Our appetites being sharpened by the frosty voyage, and in particular, Queequeg seeing his favourite fishing food before him, and the chowder being surpassingly excellent, we despatched it with great expedition: when leaning back a moment and bethinking me of Mrs.", "서리 내린 항해로 식욕이 한껏 날카로워진 데다, 특히 퀴퀘그는 자기가 좋아하는 생선 음식을 눈앞에 두고 있었고, 차우더는 더없이 훌륭했으므로, 우리는 그것을 잽싸게 해치웠다. 그러고는 잠시 등을 기대고 앉아 허시 부인의 그 조개니 대구니 하던 말을 곰곰이 떠올리다가, 나는 작은 실험을 한번 해 봐야겠다고 생각했다."], ["Hussey’s clam and cod announcement, I thought I would try a little experiment.", "부엌문 쪽으로 다가가 \"대구\"라는 단어를 아주 힘주어 내뱉고는 다시 자리로 돌아왔다."], ["Stepping to the kitchen door, I uttered the word “cod” with great emphasis, and resumed my seat.", "잠시 후 구수한 김이 또다시 피어올랐는데, 이번에는 풍미가 달랐다."], ["In a few moments the savoury steam came forth again, but with a different flavor, and in good time a fine cod-chowder was placed before us.", "그리고 알맞은 때에 근사한 대구 차우더가 우리 앞에 놓였다."]], [["We resumed business; and while plying our spoons in the bowl, thinks I to myself, I wonder now if this here has any effect on the head?", "우리는 다시 식사에 들어갔다. 그릇 속에 숟가락을 부지런히 놀리는 동안, 나는 혼자 생각했다."], ["What’s that stultifying saying about chowder-headed people?", "자, 이게 머리에 무슨 영향이라도 미치는 게 아닐까? 차우더 머리(차우더처럼 멍청한 머리)라는 그 멍청한 속담은 또 뭐란 말인가?"], ["“But look, Queequeg, ain’t that a live eel in your bowl?", "\"그런데 이보게, 퀴퀘그, 자네 그릇 속에 든 게 살아 있는 뱀장어 아닌가?"], ["Where’s your harpoon?”", "작살은 어디다 두었나?\""]], [["Fishiest of all fishy places was the Try Pots, which well deserved its name; for the pots there were always boiling chowders.", "생선 비린내 나는 온갖 곳들 가운데에서도 가장 비린 곳이 트라이 포츠였으니, 그 이름값을 톡톡히 하는 곳이었다. 그곳의 솥들은 언제나 차우더를 펄펄 끓이고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Chowder for breakfast, and chowder for dinner, and chowder for supper, till you began to look for fish-bones coming through your clothes.", "아침에도 차우더, 점심에도 차우더, 저녁에도 차우더라, 급기야 자기 옷을 뚫고 생선 가시가 삐져나오지 않나 살피게 될 지경이었다."], ["The area before the house was paved with clam-shells. Mrs.", "집 앞마당은 조개껍데기로 포장되어 있었다."], ["Hussey wore a polished necklace of codfish vertebra; and Hosea Hussey had his account books bound in superior old shark-skin.", "허시 부인은 대구 척추뼈를 반들반들 갈아 만든 목걸이를 걸고 다녔으며, 호세아 허시는 회계장부를 고급 묵은 상어 가죽으로 장정해 두었다. 우유에서도 비린내가 났는데, 도무지 그 까닭을 알 수 없었다."], ["There was a fishy flavor to the milk, too, which I could not at all account for, till one morning happening to take a stroll along the beach among some fishermen’s boats, I saw Hosea’s brindled cow feeding on fish remnants, and marching along the sand with each foot in a cod’s decapitated head, looking very slip-shod, I assure ye.", "그러다 어느 아침, 어부들의 보트 사이로 해변을 거닐다가, 호세아네 얼룩소가 생선 찌꺼기를 뜯어 먹으며 모래밭을 걷는 모습을 보고서야 알게 되었다. 그놈은 두 발 모두 목 잘린 대구 머리에 푹 박은 채 걸어 다니고 있었는데, 단언컨대 그 꼴이 어찌나 칠칠치 못해 보이던지."]], [["Supper concluded, we received a lamp, and directions from Mrs.", "저녁 식사를 마치자, 우리는 허시 부인에게서 등불 하나와 침실로 가는 가장 가까운 길에 관한 안내를 받았다."], ["Hussey concerning the nearest way to bed; but, as Queequeg was about to precede me up the stairs, the lady reached forth her arm, and demanded his harpoon; she allowed no harpoon in her chambers.", "그러나 퀴퀘그가 막 나를 앞질러 계단을 오르려 할 때, 그 부인은 팔을 쭉 뻗어 그의 작살을 내놓으라고 했다. 그녀는 자기 객실에 작살을 들이는 것을 허락하지 않았다."], ["“Why not?”", "\"왜 안 됩니까?\""], ["said I; “every true whaleman sleeps with his harpoon—but why not?”", "내가 말했다. \"진정한 고래잡이라면 누구나 작살을 끼고 자는데, 어째서 안 된다는 거죠?\""], ["“Because it’s dangerous,”", "\"위험하니까요.\""], ["says she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Ever since young Stiggs coming from that unfort’nt v’y’ge of his, when he was gone four years and a half, with only three barrels of ile, was found dead in my first floor back, with his harpoon in his side; ever since then I allow no boarders to take sich dangerous weepons in their rooms at night.", "\"그 운수 사나운 항해에서 돌아온 젊은 스틱스 이후로 말이에요. 그 애가 사 년 반이나 나가 있다가 겨우 기름 세 통만 가지고 돌아와서는, 옆구리에 자기 작살을 박은 채 우리 일 층 뒤쪽 방에서 죽은 채로 발견됐거든요."], ["So, Mr. Queequeg”", "그 일이 있은 뒤로는, 어떤 숙박객도 밤에 그런 위험한 흉기를 방에 들이는 걸 허락하지 않아요."], ["(for she had learned his name), “I will just take this here iron, and keep it for you till morning.", "그러니, 퀴퀘그 씨\" (그녀는 그새 그의 이름을 알아 둔 것이다) \"이 작살은 내가 받아 두었다가 아침까지 잘 간수해 드리지요."], ["But the chowder; clam or cod to-morrow for breakfast, men?”", "그런데 차우더 말인데요. 내일 아침 식사는 조개로 할까요, 대구로 할까요, 손님들?\""]], [["“Both,”", "\"둘 다요,\""], ["says I; “and let’s have a couple of smoked herring by way of variety.”", "내가 말했다. \"그리고 색다른 맛을 보게 훈제 청어도 두어 마리 곁들여 주시지요.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 16. The Ship.", "# CHAPTER 16. The Ship."]], [["In bed we concocted our plans for the morrow. But to my surprise and no small concern, Queequeg now gave me to understand, that he had been diligently consulting Yojo—the name of his black little god—and Yojo had told him two or three times over, and strongly insisted upon it everyway, that instead of our going together among the whaling-fleet in harbor, and in concert selecting our craft; instead of this, I say, Yojo earnestly enjoined that the selection of the ship should rest wholly with me, inasmuch as Yojo purposed befriending us; and, in order to do so, had already pitched upon a vessel, which, if left to myself, I, Ishmael, should infallibly light upon, for all the world as though it had turned out by chance; and in that vessel I must immediately ship myself, for the present irrespective of Queequeg.", "침대에 누워 우리는 다음 날의 계획을 짰다. 그런데 놀랍게도, 또 적잖이 걱정스럽게도, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)는 이제 내게 이렇게 일러 주었다. 자신이 요조—그의 작고 검은 신의 이름이었다—와 부지런히 상의해 왔는데, 요조가 두세 번에 걸쳐, 그리고 온갖 방식으로 강력히 주장하기를, 우리가 함께 항구의 포경선단 사이를 누비며 한마음으로 우리의 배를 고르는 대신에, 그러니까 그렇게 하는 대신에, 배를 고르는 일은 전적으로 내게 맡겨져야 한다고 요조가 간곡히 명했다는 것이다. 요조는 우리를 도우려는 뜻을 품고 있었고, 그렇게 하기 위해 이미 어떤 배 한 척을 점찍어 두었는데, 내게 맡겨 두기만 하면 나, 이슈메일은 마치 우연히 그렇게 된 것처럼 틀림없이 그 배를 발견하게 되리라는 것이었다. 그리고 그 배에 나는 당장 올라타야 하며, 당분간은 퀴퀘그와는 상관없이 그래야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["I have forgotten to mention that, in many things, Queequeg placed great confidence in the excellence of Yojo’s judgment and surprising forecast of things; and cherished Yojo with considerable esteem, as a rather good sort of god, who perhaps meant well enough upon the whole, but in all cases did not succeed in his benevolent designs.", "내가 미처 말하지 못한 것이 있는데, 여러 가지 일에서 퀴퀘그는 요조의 판단력이 뛰어나고 앞일을 내다보는 능력이 놀랍다고 크게 신뢰했으며, 요조를 상당한 경의로써 떠받들었다. 요조는 대체로 보아 제법 괜찮은 종류의 신으로, 아마도 전반적으로는 좋은 뜻을 품고 있으나, 늘 그 자비로운 계획을 성공시키지는 못하는 신이라고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Now, this plan of Queequeg’s, or rather Yojo’s, touching the selection of our craft; I did not like that plan at all.", "그런데 우리의 배를 고르는 일에 관한 이 퀴퀘그의 계획, 아니 차라리 요조의 계획이 나는 도무지 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["I had not a little relied upon Queequeg’s sagacity to point out the whaler best fitted to carry us and our fortunes securely.", "나는 우리와 우리의 운명을 안전하게 실어 줄 가장 알맞은 포경선을 가려내는 데 퀴퀘그의 명민함에 적잖이 기대를 걸고 있었던 것이다."], ["But as all my remonstrances produced no effect upon Queequeg, I was obliged to acquiesce; and accordingly prepared to set about this business with a determined rushing sort of energy and vigor, that should quickly settle that trifling little affair.", "그러나 나의 온갖 항변도 퀴퀘그에게는 아무런 효과가 없었으므로, 나는 묵묵히 따를 수밖에 없었다. 그리하여 이 사소한 일을 얼른 처리해 버리려고, 단호하고 거침없는 일종의 기력과 활력으로 이 일에 착수할 준비를 했다."], ["Next morning early, leaving Queequeg shut up with Yojo in our little bedroom—for it seemed that it was some sort of Lent or Ramadan, or day of fasting, humiliation, and prayer with Queequeg and Yojo that day; how it was I never could find out, for, though I applied myself to it several times, I never could master his liturgies and XXXIX Articles—leaving Queequeg, then, fasting on his tomahawk pipe, and Yojo warming himself at his sacrificial fire of shavings, I sallied out among the shipping.", "이튿날 이른 아침, 퀴퀘그를 요조와 함께 우리의 작은 침실에 틀어박히게 둔 채—그날은 퀴퀘그와 요조에게 일종의 사순절이나 라마단, 곧 단식과 참회와 기도의 날인 듯했기 때문이다. 그것이 어떤 것인지 나는 끝내 알아낼 수 없었으니, 몇 차례나 알아보려 애썼건만 그의 예배 의식과 신앙 조문 서른아홉 개를 도무지 통달할 수 없었던 것이다—그렇게 퀴퀘그를 그의 토마호크 파이프 위에서 단식하게 두고, 요조를 대팻밥 제물의 불에 몸을 데우게 둔 채, 나는 배들 사이로 나섰다."], ["After much prolonged sauntering and many random inquiries, I learnt that there were three ships up for three-years’ voyages—The Devil-dam, the Tit-bit, and the Pequod.", "한참을 어슬렁거리고 닥치는 대로 이것저것 물어본 끝에, 나는 삼 년짜리 항해에 나설 배가 세 척 있다는 것을 알아냈다. 데블댐호와 티트빗호, 그리고 피쿼드(Pequod)호였다."], ["Devil-Dam, I do not know the origin of; Tit-bit is obvious; Pequod, you will no doubt remember, was the name of a celebrated tribe of Massachusetts Indians; now extinct as the ancient Medes.", "데블댐이라는 이름의 유래는 나도 모른다. 티트빗은 뻔하다."], ["I peered and pryed about the Devil-dam; from her, hopped over to the Tit-bit; and finally, going on board the Pequod, looked around her for a moment, and then decided that this was the very ship for us.", "피쿼드는, 여러분도 분명 기억하겠지만, 매사추세츠 인디언(Indian)의 어느 이름난 부족의 이름으로, 지금은 고대 메디아인들처럼 멸종해 버렸다. 나는 데블댐호를 이리저리 들여다보고 살펴보았고, 거기서 폴짝 건너뛰어 티트빗호로 갔으며, 마침내 피쿼드호에 올라 잠시 둘러본 뒤, 바로 이것이 우리에게 꼭 맞는 배라고 결정했다."]], [["You may have seen many a quaint craft in your day, for aught I know;—square-toed luggers; mountainous Japanese junks; butter-box galliots, and what not; but take my word for it, you never saw such a rare old craft as this same rare old Pequod.", "여러분도 한평생 살아오면서 기묘한 배를 숱하게 보았을 것이다, 내가 어찌 알겠는가마는. 코가 네모진 러거선, 산더미 같은 일본 정크선, 버터 상자처럼 생긴 갤리엇선이며 그 밖에 이런저런 것들을."], ["She was a ship of the old school, rather small if anything; with an old-fashioned claw-footed look about her.", "그러나 내 말을 믿어 두기 바란다, 여러분은 이 진귀하고 오래된 피쿼드호만큼 진귀하고 오래된 배는 결코 본 적이 없을 것이다."], ["Long seasoned and weather-stained in the typhoons and calms of all four oceans, her old hull’s complexion was darkened like a French grenadier’s, who has alike fought in Egypt and Siberia.", "그것은 옛 학파의 배로, 굳이 따지자면 좀 작은 편이었고, 발톱처럼 휘어진 옛날식 다리를 단 듯한 분위기를 풍겼다. 사대양의 태풍과 무풍을 두루 겪으며 오래 묵고 비바람에 물든 그 낡은 선체의 빛깔은, 이집트와 시베리아에서 똑같이 싸워 온 프랑스 척탄병의 얼굴처럼 거뭇하게 그을려 있었다."], ["Her venerable bows looked bearded.", "그 유서 깊은 이물은 수염이 난 듯 보였다."], ["Her masts—cut somewhere on the coast of Japan, where her original ones were lost overboard in a gale—her masts stood stiffly up like the spines of the three old kings of Cologne.", "그 돛대들은—원래의 것은 강풍에 바다로 잃어버려 일본 해안 어딘가에서 베어 온 것이었다—쾰른의 세 늙은 왕들의 등뼈처럼 빳빳이 솟아 있었다."], ["Her ancient decks were worn and wrinkled, like the pilgrim-worshipped flag-stone in Canterbury Cathedral where Becket bled.", "그 오래된 갑판은 닳고 주름져, 베킷이 피를 흘린 캔터베리 대성당의, 순례자들이 우러르며 밟은 포석 같았다."], ["But to all these her old antiquities, were added new and marvellous features, pertaining to the wild business that for more than half a century she had followed.", "그러나 이 모든 낡은 골동품 같은 면면에 더하여, 반세기 넘게 그 배가 좇아온 거친 일에 걸맞은 새롭고 경이로운 특징들이 덧붙여져 있었다."], ["Old Captain Peleg, many years her chief-mate, before he commanded another vessel of his own, and now a retired seaman, and one of the principal owners of the Pequod,—this old Peleg, during the term of his chief-mateship, had built upon her original grotesqueness, and inlaid it, all over, with a quaintness both of material and device, unmatched by anything except it be Thorkill-Hake’s carved buckler or bedstead.", "늙은 펠레그(Peleg) 선장은 자신의 배를 따로 지휘하기 전 여러 해 동안 이 배의 일등항해사였고, 지금은 은퇴한 뱃사람이자 피쿼드호의 주요 선주 가운데 하나였는데—이 늙은 펠레그가 일등항해사로 있던 동안 그 본래의 기괴함 위에 무언가를 보태고, 그것을 온통 박아 넣어, 재료에서나 고안에서나 진기하기 그지없게 꾸며 놓았으니, 토르킬하케의 새겨진 방패나 침상을 빼고는 무엇과도 견줄 수 없을 정도였다."], ["She was apparelled like any barbaric Ethiopian emperor, his neck heavy with pendants of polished ivory.", "그것은 마치 연마된 상아 장신구로 목을 묵직하게 두른 어느 야만적인 에티오피아 황제처럼 치장되어 있었다."], ["She was a thing of trophies.", "그것은 전리품으로 이루어진 물건이었다."], ["A cannibal of a craft, tricking herself forth in the chased bones of her enemies.", "적의 깎아 낸 뼈로 제 몸을 꾸며 낸, 식인종 같은 배였다."], ["All round, her unpanelled, open bulwarks were garnished like one continuous jaw, with the long sharp teeth of the sperm whale, inserted there for pins, to fasten her old hempen thews and tendons to.", "사방으로, 판자를 대지 않은 채 열려 있는 뱃전은 마치 하나로 이어진 턱주가리처럼, 향유고래(Sperm)의 길고 날카로운 이빨들로 장식되어 있었고, 그 이빨들은 못으로 박혀 있어 낡은 삼줄 힘줄과 밧줄을 단단히 매어 두는 데 쓰였다."], ["Those thews ran not through base blocks of land wood, but deftly travelled over sheaves of sea-ivory.", "그 힘줄들은 뭍에서 난 목재로 만든 천한 도르래를 거치지 않고, 바다 상아로 깎은 활차 위로 솜씨 좋게 미끄러져 다녔다."], ["Scorning a turnstile wheel at her reverend helm, she sported there a tiller; and that tiller was in one mass, curiously carved from the long narrow lower jaw of her hereditary foe.", "그 위엄 있는 키에 회전식 바퀴 따위는 거들떠보지도 않고, 그 배는 그 자리에 키손잡이를 자랑스레 달고 있었으니, 그 키손잡이는 한 덩어리로, 대대로 내려온 원수의 길고 좁은 아래턱뼈에서 기묘하게 깎아 낸 것이었다."], ["The helmsman who steered by that tiller in a tempest, felt like the Tartar, when he holds back his fiery steed by clutching its jaw.", "폭풍 속에서 그 키손잡이로 배를 모는 키잡이는, 사나운 준마의 턱을 움켜쥐어 그 기세를 누르는 타타르인이 된 듯한 기분이 들 터였다."], ["A noble craft, but somehow a most melancholy!", "고귀한 배였으나, 어쩐지 더없이 우수에 잠긴 배였다!"], ["All noble things are touched with that.", "모든 고귀한 것에는 그런 빛이 어려 있는 법이다."]], [["Now when I looked about the quarter-deck, for some one having authority, in order to propose myself as a candidate for the voyage, at first I saw nobody; but I could not well overlook a strange sort of tent, or rather wigwam, pitched a little behind the main-mast.", "그런데 갑판 위를 둘러보며, 이 항해의 지원자로 나서 보려고 권한을 지닌 누군가를 찾으려 했을 때, 처음에는 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 큰 돛대 조금 뒤편에 쳐 놓은 기묘한 천막, 아니 차라리 위그웜이라 할 만한 것은 도무지 못 본 척할 수가 없었다."], ["It seemed only a temporary erection used in port.", "그것은 항구에 있을 때 쓰는 일종의 임시 가설물인 듯했다."], ["It was of a conical shape, some ten feet high; consisting of the long, huge slabs of limber black bone taken from the middle and highest part of the jaws of the right-whale.", "그것은 원뿔 모양으로, 높이가 한 십 피트(약 3미터)쯤 되었고, 참고래의 턱뼈 한가운데와 가장 높은 부분에서 떼어 낸 길고 거대한, 휘어지는 검은 뼈판들로 이루어져 있었다."], ["Planted with their broad ends on the deck, a circle of these slabs laced together, mutually sloped towards each other, and at the apex united in a tufted point, where the loose hairy fibres waved to and fro like the top-knot on some old Pottowottamie Sachem’s head.", "그 넓은 끝을 갑판에 박아 놓고, 이 뼈판들을 둥글게 둘러 서로 엮어 비스듬히 맞대어 세웠으며, 꼭대기에서는 한데 모여 술이 달린 뾰족한 점을 이루었는데, 거기서 풀린 털 같은 섬유들이 어느 늙은 포타와토미 추장 머리 위의 상투처럼 이리저리 나부꼈다."], ["A triangular opening faced towards the bows of the ship, so that the insider commanded a complete view forward.", "삼각형 입구가 뱃머리 쪽을 향하고 있어, 그 안에 든 자는 앞쪽을 온전히 내다볼 수 있었다."]], [["And half concealed in this queer tenement, I at length found one who by his aspect seemed to have authority; and who, it being noon, and the ship’s work suspended, was now enjoying respite from the burden of command.", "그리고 이 기묘한 거처 속에 반쯤 몸을 숨긴 채, 나는 마침내 그 풍모로 보아 권한을 지닌 듯한 사람 하나를 찾아냈다. 정오라 배의 일은 멈춰 있었고, 그는 이제 지휘의 짐에서 벗어나 한숨 돌리며 쉬고 있었다."], ["He was seated on an old-fashioned oaken chair, wriggling all over with curious carving; and the bottom of which was formed of a stout interlacing of the same elastic stuff of which the wigwam was constructed.", "그는 온통 진기한 조각으로 꿈틀거리는 듯한 옛날식 떡갈나무 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 그 바닥은 위그웜을 짓는 데 쓰인 것과 똑같은 탄력 있는 재료를 튼튼하게 얽어 만든 것이었다."]], [["There was nothing so very particular, perhaps, about the appearance of the elderly man I saw; he was brown and brawny, like most old seamen, and heavily rolled up in blue pilot-cloth, cut in the Quaker style; only there was a fine and almost microscopic net-work of the minutest wrinkles interlacing round his eyes, which must have arisen from his continual sailings in many hard gales, and always looking to windward;—for this causes the muscles about the eyes to become pursed together. Such eye-wrinkles are very effectual in a scowl.", "내가 본 그 노인의 겉모습에는 그리 특별한 데가 없었는지도 모른다. 그는 여느 늙은 뱃사람들처럼 까무잡잡하고 다부졌으며, 퀘이커식으로 재단한 푸른 파일럿 천을 두툼하게 둘러 입고 있었다. 다만 그의 두 눈 둘레에는 더없이 잘디잔 주름이 곱게, 거의 현미경으로나 볼 만큼 촘촘히 그물처럼 엮여 있었는데, 이는 숱한 거센 강풍 속을 끊임없이 항해하며 늘 바람 부는 쪽을 바라본 데서 비롯된 것이 분명했다. 그렇게 하면 눈가의 근육이 한데 오므라들기 때문이다. 그런 눈가 주름은 노려보는 표정에 아주 효과적이다."]], [["“Is this the Captain of the Pequod?”", "\"이 배가 피쿼드호의 선장이신가요?\""], ["said I, advancing to the door of the tent.", "나는 천막 입구로 다가서며 말했다."]], [["“Supposing it be the captain of the Pequod, what dost thou want of him?”", "\"이 사람이 피쿼드호 선장이라 치자, 그래서 그한테 무얼 바라는 게냐?\""], ["he demanded.", "그가 따져 물었다."]], [["“I was thinking of shipping.”", "\"배를 타 볼까 하고요.\""]], [["“Thou wast, wast thou?", "\"그래, 그렇단 말이지?"], ["I see thou art no Nantucketer—ever been in a stove boat?”", "보아하니 자네는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람은 아니로군—깨진 보트에 타 본 적은 있나?\""]], [["“No, Sir, I never have.”", "\"아니요, 선생님, 한 번도 없습니다.\""]], [["“Dost know nothing at all about whaling, I dare say—eh?", "\"포경에 대해서는 아무것도 모르겠지, 안 그래—응?\""]], [["“Nothing, Sir; but I have no doubt I shall soon learn.", "\"전혀 모릅니다, 선생님. 그래도 곧 배우게 되리라는 건 의심치 않습니다."], ["I’ve been several voyages in the merchant service, and I think that—”", "상선 일로 항해를 여러 번 해 봤고, 그래서 제 생각엔—\""]], [["“Merchant service be damned.", "\"상선이고 나발이고 다 집어치워."], ["Talk not that lingo to me.", "그딴 소리는 내게 늘어놓지 마라."], ["Dost see that leg?—I’ll take that leg away from thy stern, if ever thou talkest of the marchant service to me again.", "이 다리가 보이느냐?—한 번만 더 내게 상선 일 얘기를 꺼내면, 자네 꽁무니에서 이 다리를 떼어 내 줄 테다."], ["Marchant service indeed!", "상선 일은 무슨!"], ["I suppose now ye feel considerable proud of having served in those marchant ships.", "이제 보니 자네는 그런 상선들에서 일했다는 게 어지간히 자랑스러운 모양이로군. 한데 고래꼬리에 맹세코!"], ["But flukes! man, what makes thee want to go a whaling, eh?—it looks a little suspicious, don’t it, eh?—Hast not been a pirate, hast thou?—Didst not rob thy last Captain, didst thou?—Dost not think of murdering the officers when thou gettest to sea?”", "이 사람아, 도대체 자네가 무엇 때문에 포경에 나서고 싶다는 거냐, 응?—좀 수상쩍지 않나, 그렇지 않아, 응?—해적질을 한 건 아니겠지, 안 그러냐?—마지막으로 탔던 선장을 털어먹은 건 아니겠지, 그렇지?—바다에 나가면 사관들을 죽일 생각을 품고 있는 건 아니렷다?\""]], [["I protested my innocence of these things.", "나는 그런 일들에 대해 결백하다고 항변했다."], ["I saw that under the mask of these half humorous innuendoes, this old seaman, as an insulated Quakerish Nantucketer, was full of his insular prejudices, and rather distrustful of all aliens, unless they hailed from Cape Cod or the Vineyard.", "나는 이 반쯤 익살스러운 빈정거림의 가면 뒤에서, 외딴섬에 갇힌 퀘이커교도 낸터킷 사람인 이 늙은 뱃사람이 섬사람 특유의 편견으로 가득 차, 케이프코드나 비니어드 출신이 아닌 한 모든 외지인을 어지간히 미더워하지 않고 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["“But what takes thee a-whaling?", "\"한데 자네는 무엇 때문에 포경에 나서겠다는 거냐?"], ["I want to know that before I think of shipping ye.”", "자네를 배에 태울지 생각하기 전에 그것부터 알아야겠다.\""]], [["“Well, sir, I want to see what whaling is.", "\"글쎄요, 선생님, 저는 포경이 어떤 것인지 보고 싶습니다."], ["I want to see the world.”", "세상을 보고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“Want to see what whaling is, eh?", "\"포경이 어떤 것인지 보고 싶다고, 응?"], ["Have ye clapped eye on Captain Ahab?”", "자네 에이해브(Ahab) 선장을 본 적이 있나?\""]], [["“Who is Captain Ahab, sir?”", "\"에이해브 선장이 누구신데요, 선생님?\""]], [["“Aye, aye, I thought so.", "\"그래, 그래, 그럴 줄 알았다."], ["Captain Ahab is the Captain of this ship.”", "에이해브 선장이 바로 이 배의 선장이야.\""]], [["“I am mistaken then.", "\"그렇다면 제가 잘못 알았군요."], ["I thought I was speaking to the Captain himself.”", "저는 선장님과 직접 이야기하고 있는 줄 알았습니다.\""]], [["“Thou art speaking to Captain Peleg—that’s who ye are speaking to, young man.", "\"자네는 지금 펠레그 선장과 이야기하고 있는 거다—그게 자네가 지금 말 상대하고 있는 사람이지, 젊은이."], ["It belongs to me and Captain Bildad to see the Pequod fitted out for the voyage, and supplied with all her needs, including crew.", "피쿼드호를 항해에 맞게 채비시키고, 선원을 포함해 필요한 모든 것을 갖추어 주는 일은 나와 빌대드(Bildad) 선장의 몫이야."], ["We are part owners and agents.", "우리는 공동 선주이자 대리인이지."], ["But as I was going to say, if thou wantest to know what whaling is, as thou tellest ye do, I can put ye in a way of finding it out before ye bind yourself to it, past backing out.", "한데 내가 말하려던 것처럼, 자네가 말하듯 정말로 포경이 어떤 것인지 알고 싶다면, 발 빼지 못할 만큼 단단히 매이기 전에 미리 그걸 알아낼 길을 일러 줄 수 있다."], ["Clap eye on Captain Ahab, young man, and thou wilt find that he has only one leg.”", "에이해브 선장을 한번 똑똑히 보아라, 젊은이. 그러면 그에게 다리가 하나밖에 없다는 걸 알게 될 테니.\""]], [["“What do you mean, sir?", "\"무슨 말씀이신가요, 선생님?"], ["Was the other one lost by a whale?”", "나머지 한쪽은 고래한테 잃은 건가요?\""]], [["“Lost by a whale!", "\"고래한테 잃었느냐고!"], ["Young man, come nearer to me: it was devoured, chewed up, crunched by the monstrousest parmacetty that ever chipped a boat!—ah, ah!”", "젊은이, 내게 더 가까이 와 보아라. 그건 일찍이 보트를 박살 낸 것 중에서도 가장 괴물 같은 향유고래한테 통째로 먹히고, 씹히고, 으스러진 거다!—아, 아!\""]], [["I was a little alarmed by his energy, perhaps also a little touched at the hearty grief in his concluding exclamation, but said as calmly as I could, “What you say is no doubt true enough, sir; but how could I know there was any peculiar ferocity in that particular whale, though indeed I might have inferred as much from the simple fact of the accident.”", "나는 그의 격한 기세에 약간 놀랐고, 어쩌면 그 마지막 외침에 담긴 진심 어린 슬픔에 조금 마음이 동하기도 했지만, 되도록 차분하게 말했다. \"선생님 말씀이 틀림없이 사실이겠지요. 하지만 비록 그 사고라는 단순한 사실만으로도 그쯤은 짐작할 수 있었을지 모르나, 하필 그 고래에게 무슨 유별난 흉포함이 있었는지를 제가 어찌 알 수 있었겠습니까.\""]], [["“Look ye now, young man, thy lungs are a sort of soft, d’ye see; thou dost not talk shark a bit.", "\"이봐라, 젊은이, 자네 허파는 어딘가 물러 터졌어, 알겠나. 자네는 상어처럼 거칠게 말할 줄을 도무지 모른단 말이야."], ["Sure, ye’ve been to sea before now; sure of that?”", "자네, 분명히 전에 바다에 나가 본 적은 있겠지, 그건 확실하냐?\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["said I, “I thought I told you that I had been four voyages in the merchant—”", "내가 말했다. \"제가 상선에서 네 번 항해했다고 말씀드린 줄 알았는데요—\""]], [["“Hard down out of that!", "\"그딴 소리 당장 거두지 못해!"], ["Mind what I said about the marchant service—don’t aggravate me—I won’t have it.", "상선 일에 대해 내가 한 말을 명심하라고—날 더 약 올리지 마—그런 건 못 참아."], ["But let us understand each other.", "그러나 우리 서로 분명히 해 두자."], ["I have given thee a hint about what whaling is; do ye yet feel inclined for it?”", "나는 자네에게 포경이 어떤 것인지 귀띔을 해 줬어. 그래도 여전히 그게 하고 싶은 마음이 드느냐?\""]], [["“I do, sir.”", "\"듭니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Very good.", "\"아주 좋아."], ["Now, art thou the man to pitch a harpoon down a live whale’s throat, and then jump after it?", "그렇다면, 자네는 살아 있는 고래의 목구멍에 작살을 내리꽂고, 그러고 나서 그 뒤를 따라 뛰어들 만한 사내인가?"], ["Answer, quick!”", "대답해, 어서!\""]], [["“I am, sir, if it should be positively indispensable to do so; not to be got rid of, that is; which I don’t take to be the fact.”", "\"그렇습니다, 선생님. 그게 정말로 없어서는 안 될 일이라면, 그러니까 도저히 피할 수 없는 일이라면 말입니다. 하지만 저는 그게 실제로 그런 일이라고는 생각지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Good again.", "\"또 좋아."], ["Now then, thou not only wantest to go a-whaling, to find out by experience what whaling is, but ye also want to go in order to see the world?", "자, 그렇다면 자네는 포경에 나서서 직접 겪어 봄으로써 포경이 어떤 것인지 알아내고 싶을 뿐 아니라, 세상을 보고 싶어서 나서겠다는 거지?"], ["Was not that what ye said?", "그렇게 말하지 않았나?"], ["I thought so.", "그런 줄 알았다."], ["Well then, just step forward there, and take a peep over the weather-bow, and then back to me and tell me what ye see there.”", "그럼 저기 앞으로 좀 나가서, 바람 부는 쪽 뱃머리 너머를 한번 슬쩍 내다보고, 그러고는 내게 돌아와서 거기 무엇이 보이는지 말해 보아라.\""]], [["For a moment I stood a little puzzled by this curious request, not knowing exactly how to take it, whether humorously or in earnest.", "잠시 나는 이 기묘한 요청에 좀 어리둥절해서, 그것을 익살로 받아들여야 할지 진심으로 받아들여야 할지 도무지 알 수 없어 멈칫 서 있었다."], ["But concentrating all his crow’s feet into one scowl, Captain Peleg started me on the errand.", "그러나 펠레그 선장은 그의 까마귀 발 같은 눈가 주름을 죄다 한데 모아 노려보며 나를 그 심부름길로 떠밀었다."]], [["Going forward and glancing over the weather bow, I perceived that the ship swinging to her anchor with the flood-tide, was now obliquely pointing towards the open ocean.", "앞으로 나아가 바람 부는 쪽 뱃머리 너머를 흘끗 내다보니, 밀물에 닻줄을 따라 흔들리던 배가 이제 비스듬히 망망대해 쪽을 가리키고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["The prospect was unlimited, but exceedingly monotonous and forbidding; not the slightest variety that I could see.", "전망은 끝이 없었으나, 더없이 단조롭고 황량했다. 내 눈에는 조금의 변화도 보이지 않았다."]], [["“Well, what’s the report?”", "\"자, 보고는 어떻게 됐나?\""], ["said Peleg when I came back; “what did ye see?”", "내가 돌아오자 펠레그가 말했다. \"무얼 보았느냐?\""]], [["“Not much,”", "\"별것 없었습니다.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["I replied—“nothing but water; considerable horizon though, and there’s a squall coming up, I think.”", "\"물뿐이었어요. 수평선은 꽤 넓었고요, 그리고 돌풍이 몰려오고 있는 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Well, what does thou think then of seeing the world?", "\"음, 그럼 세상을 본다는 것에 대해서는 어떻게 생각하나?"], ["Do ye wish to go round Cape Horn to see any more of it, eh?", "그걸 좀 더 보겠다고 케이프 혼을 빙 돌고 싶은 거냐, 응?"], ["Can’t ye see the world where you stand?”", "자네가 서 있는 그 자리에서도 세상이 보이지 않더냐?\""]], [["I was a little staggered, but go a-whaling I must, and I would; and the Pequod was as good a ship as any—I thought the best—and all this I now repeated to Peleg.", "나는 좀 말문이 막혔으나, 어쨌든 나는 포경에 나서야 했고, 또 나설 작정이었다. 게다가 피쿼드호는 어느 배 못지않게 좋은 배였다—내 생각엔 가장 좋은 배였다—그래서 나는 이 모든 것을 이제 펠레그에게 거듭 말했다."], ["Seeing me so determined, he expressed his willingness to ship me.", "내가 그토록 단호한 것을 보고, 그는 나를 기꺼이 배에 태우겠노라고 밝혔다."]], [["“And thou mayest as well sign the papers right off,”", "\"그럼 자네는 곧장 서류에 서명하는 게 좋겠군.\""], ["he added—“come along with ye.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"이리 따라오게나.\""], ["And so saying, he led the way below deck into the cabin.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 앞장서서 갑판 아래 선실로 내려갔다."]], [["Seated on the transom was what seemed to me a most uncommon and surprising figure.", "고물 가로판에 앉아 있는 것은 내게는 더없이 흔치 않고 놀라운 인물처럼 보였다."], ["It turned out to be Captain Bildad, who along with Captain Peleg was one of the largest owners of the vessel; the other shares, as is sometimes the case in these ports, being held by a crowd of old annuitants; widows, fatherless children, and chancery wards; each owning about the value of a timber head, or a foot of plank, or a nail or two in the ship.", "그가 바로 빌대드 선장이었으니, 그는 펠레그 선장과 더불어 이 배의 최대 선주 가운데 하나였다. 다른 지분들은, 이런 항구에서 더러 그러하듯, 한 무리의 늙은 연금 수령자들—과부들, 아비 없는 아이들, 그리고 형평법원의 피후견인들—이 나누어 쥐고 있었는데, 저마다 배의 갑목 머리 하나쯤, 또는 널빤지 한 자쯤, 또는 못 한두 개쯤의 값어치를 소유하고 있었다."], ["People in Nantucket invest their money in whaling vessels, the same way that you do yours in approved state stocks bringing in good interest.", "낸터킷 사람들은 여러분이 짭짤한 이자가 붙는 검증된 국공채에 돈을 넣듯이, 포경선에 돈을 투자한다."]], [["Now, Bildad, like Peleg, and indeed many other Nantucketers, was a Quaker, the island having been originally settled by that sect; and to this day its inhabitants in general retain in an uncommon measure the peculiarities of the Quaker, only variously and anomalously modified by things altogether alien and heterogeneous.", "그런데 빌대드는, 펠레그와 마찬가지로, 또 실은 다른 많은 낸터킷 사람들과 마찬가지로 퀘이커교도였다. 이 섬은 본래 그 종파가 정착해 세운 곳이었고, 오늘날까지도 그 주민들은 대체로 퀘이커교도 특유의 기질을 유난히 짙게 지니고 있으되, 다만 전혀 이질적이고 동떨어진 것들에 의해 가지각색으로, 또 야릇하게 변형되어 있을 따름이다."], ["For some of these same Quakers are the most sanguinary of all sailors and whale-hunters.", "바로 이 똑같은 퀘이커교도들 가운데 더러는 모든 뱃사람과 고래잡이를 통틀어 가장 잔혹하기 그지없는 자들이기 때문이다."], ["They are fighting Quakers; they are Quakers with a vengeance.", "그들은 싸우는 퀘이커교도들이요, 앙심 품은 퀘이커교도들이다."]], [["So that there are instances among them of men, who, named with Scripture names—a singularly common fashion on the island—and in childhood naturally imbibing the stately dramatic thee and thou of the Quaker idiom; still, from the audacious, daring, and boundless adventure of their subsequent lives, strangely blend with these unoutgrown peculiarities, a thousand bold dashes of character, not unworthy a Scandinavian sea-king, or a poetical Pagan Roman.", "그리하여 그들 가운데에는 이런 사람들의 예가 있다. 성경의 이름을 따서 이름 지어지고—이는 그 섬에서 유난히 흔한 풍습이었다—어린 시절에 자연스레 퀘이커 말투의 위엄 있고 극적인 그대와 너를 몸에 익혔으되, 그 뒤의 삶에서 대담하고 거침없으며 가없는 모험을 거치는 동안, 미처 벗어 버리지 못한 그 기질에 천 가지 대담한 기상의 획을 야릇하게 뒤섞어 놓은 자들이니, 그것은 스칸디나비아의 어느 바다 왕이나 시적인 이교도 로마인에게도 부끄럽지 않을 만한 것이었다."], ["And when these things unite in a man of greatly superior natural force, with a globular brain and a ponderous heart; who has also by the stillness and seclusion of many long night-watches in the remotest waters, and beneath constellations never seen here at the north, been led to think untraditionally and independently; receiving all nature’s sweet or savage impressions fresh from her own virgin voluntary and confiding breast, and thereby chiefly, but with some help from accidental advantages, to learn a bold and nervous lofty language—that man makes one in a whole nation’s census—a mighty pageant creature, formed for noble tragedies.", "그리고 이런 것들이, 둥근 머리통과 묵직한 심장을 지닌, 천성적으로 월등히 강한 기력을 타고난 사람 안에서 하나로 어우러질 때—더욱이 그 사람이 가장 외딴 바다에서, 이 북녘에서는 결코 볼 수 없는 별자리들 아래서, 숱한 긴 밤 당직의 정적과 고독을 통해 전통에 얽매이지 않고 독자적으로 사고하게 되고, 자연이 그 처녀같이 자발적이고 거리낌 없는 가슴으로부터 직접 건네는 달콤하거나 사나운 온갖 인상을 생생하게 받아들이며, 주로 그것을 통해, 그러나 우연한 이점들의 도움도 얼마간 받아, 대담하고 신경질적이며 드높은 언어를 익히게 될 때—그런 사람은 한 나라의 인구 전체를 통틀어 단 하나뿐인 존재가 되니, 고귀한 비극을 위해 빚어진 위풍당당한 장관(壯觀)의 피조물이다."], ["Nor will it at all detract from him, dramatically regarded, if either by birth or other circumstances, he have what seems a half wilful overruling morbidness at the bottom of his nature.", "또한 극적으로 보아, 만일 그가 타고난 천성이나 다른 형편으로 말미암아 그 본성 밑바닥에 반쯤은 고의로 모든 것을 짓누르는 듯한 병적인 기질을 지니고 있다 한들, 그것이 결코 그의 품위를 깎아내리지는 않을 것이다."], ["For all men tragically great are made so through a certain morbidness.", "비극적으로 위대한 모든 사람은 어떤 병적인 기질을 통해 그렇게 되는 법이기 때문이다."], ["Be sure of this, O young ambition, all mortal greatness is but disease.", "이것만은 분명히 알아 두라, 오 젊은 야망이여, 인간의 모든 위대함이란 한낱 병에 지나지 않는 것임을."], ["But, as yet we have not to do with such an one, but with quite another; and still a man, who, if indeed peculiar, it only results again from another phase of the Quaker, modified by individual circumstances.", "그러나 아직 우리가 다룰 상대는 그런 인물이 아니라, 자못 다른 사람이다. 그래도 역시 한 인간이니, 만일 그가 정말로 별난 데가 있다면, 그것은 다시금 퀘이커교도의 또 다른 면모가 개인적 형편에 의해 변형되어 나온 것일 뿐이다."]], [["Like Captain Peleg, Captain Bildad was a well-to-do, retired whaleman.", "펠레그 선장과 마찬가지로, 빌대드 선장도 살림이 넉넉한, 은퇴한 고래잡이였다."], ["But unlike Captain Peleg—who cared not a rush for what are called serious things, and indeed deemed those self-same serious things the veriest of all trifles—Captain Bildad had not only been originally educated according to the strictest sect of Nantucket Quakerism, but all his subsequent ocean life, and the sight of many unclad, lovely island creatures, round the Horn—all that had not moved this native born Quaker one single jot, had not so much as altered one angle of his vest.", "그러나 펠레그 선장과는 달리—이른바 진지한 것들 따위에는 손톱만큼도 마음을 쓰지 않았고, 실로 바로 그 진지하다는 것들을 세상에서 가장 하찮은 것으로 여겼던 펠레그와는 달리—빌대드 선장은 본래 낸터킷 퀘이커교 중에서도 가장 엄격한 종파의 가르침을 따라 자랐을 뿐 아니라, 그 뒤의 온 바다 생활도, 케이프 혼을 돌며 마주친 헐벗고 사랑스러운 숱한 섬 처녀들의 자태도—그 모든 것도 이 토박이 퀘이커교도를 털끝만큼도 움직이지 못했고, 그의 조끼 한 귀퉁이조차 바꿔 놓지 못했다."], ["Still, for all this immutableness, was there some lack of common consistency about worthy Captain Bildad.", "그래도 이 모든 흔들림 없음에도 불구하고, 이 훌륭한 빌대드 선장에게는 어딘가 흔한 일관성이 결여된 데가 있었다."], ["Though refusing, from conscientious scruples, to bear arms against land invaders, yet himself had illimitably invaded the Atlantic and Pacific; and though a sworn foe to human bloodshed, yet had he in his straight-bodied coat, spilled tuns upon tuns of leviathan gore.", "양심상의 거리낌 때문에 뭍을 침범하는 자들에 맞서 무기를 드는 일은 거부하면서도, 정작 자신은 대서양과 태평양을 한없이 침범해 왔고, 사람의 피 흘리는 일이라면 맹세코 원수처럼 여기면서도, 정작 그 곧게 빠진 외투를 걸친 채 리바이어던의 피를 몇 통이고 몇 통이고 쏟아 왔던 것이다."], ["How now in the contemplative evening of his days, the pious Bildad reconciled these things in the reminiscence, I do not know; but it did not seem to concern him much, and very probably he had long since come to the sage and sensible conclusion that a man’s religion is one thing, and this practical world quite another.", "이제 사색에 잠긴 황혼의 나날에, 경건한 빌대드가 이런 일들을 회상 속에서 어떻게 화해시키는지 나는 모른다. 그러나 그것이 그를 그리 괴롭히는 것 같지는 않았고, 십중팔구 그는 이미 오래전에 현명하고 분별 있는 결론에 다다랐을 것이다—사람의 종교는 별개의 것이요, 이 현실 세계는 또 전혀 별개의 것이라는."], ["This world pays dividends.", "이 세계는 배당금을 지급한다."], ["Rising from a little cabin-boy in short clothes of the drabbest drab, to a harpooneer in a broad shad-bellied waistcoat; from that becoming boat-header, chief-mate, and captain, and finally a ship owner; Bildad, as I hinted before, had concluded his adventurous career by wholly retiring from active life at the goodly age of sixty, and dedicating his remaining days to the quiet receiving of his well-earned income.", "가장 칙칙한 칙칙한 빛깔의 짧은 옷을 입은 작은 사환에서 출발하여, 넓은 청어 배처럼 불룩한 조끼를 입은 작살잡이로, 거기서 보트 지휘자로, 일등항해사로, 그리고 선장으로, 마침내 선주로 올라선 빌대드는, 앞서 내가 넌지시 비쳤듯이, 예순이라는 좋은 나이에 현역 생활에서 완전히 물러나 그 모험으로 가득한 인생을 마무리 짓고, 남은 날들을 마땅히 벌어들인 수입을 조용히 거두어들이는 데 바쳤다."]], [["Now, Bildad, I am sorry to say, had the reputation of being an incorrigible old hunks, and in his sea-going days, a bitter, hard task-master.", "그런데 빌대드는, 말하기 안됐지만, 구제 불능의 늙은 구두쇠라는 평판이 나 있었고, 바다를 누비던 시절에는 모질고 가혹한 감독자였다."], ["They told me in Nantucket, though it certainly seems a curious story, that when he sailed the old Categut whaleman, his crew, upon arriving home, were mostly all carried ashore to the hospital, sore exhausted and worn out.", "낸터킷에서 사람들이 내게 일러 준 바로는—분명 야릇한 이야기로 들리긴 하지만—그가 옛 카테것호를 몰던 시절, 선원들이 고향에 닿았을 때 거의 모두가 녹초가 되고 기진맥진하여 뭍으로 들려 나가 병원으로 실려 갔다고 한다."], ["For a pious man, especially for a Quaker, he was certainly rather hard-hearted, to say the least.", "경건한 사람치고는, 더구나 퀘이커교도치고는, 그는 적어도 어지간히 모진 마음을 지닌 자였던 셈이다."], ["He never used to swear, though, at his men, they said; but somehow he got an inordinate quantity of cruel, unmitigated hard work out of them.", "다만 그는 부하들에게 욕설을 퍼붓는 일은 결코 없었다고들 했다. 그러나 어찌 된 영문인지 그는 그들에게서 잔혹하고 가차 없는 고된 일을 터무니없이 많이 짜냈다."], ["When Bildad was a chief-mate, to have his drab-coloured eye intently looking at you, made you feel completely nervous, till you could clutch something—a hammer or a marling-spike, and go to work like mad, at something or other, never mind what.", "빌대드가 일등항해사였던 시절, 그 칙칙한 빛깔의 눈이 자네를 뚫어지게 바라보면, 자네는 무언가를—망치나 밧줄 꿰는 송곳 같은 것을—움켜쥐고서 무엇이든, 그게 무엇이든 상관없이, 미친 듯이 일에 매달리지 않고는 못 배길 만큼 완전히 안절부절못하게 되고 말았다."], ["Indolence and idleness perished before him.", "게으름과 빈둥거림은 그의 앞에서 스러져 버렸다."], ["His own person was the exact embodiment of his utilitarian character.", "그 자신의 인품이야말로 그 실리적인 성격을 그대로 빚어낸 화신이었다."], ["On his long, gaunt body, he carried no spare flesh, no superfluous beard, his chin having a soft, economical nap to it, like the worn nap of his broad-brimmed hat.", "길쭉하고 비쩍 마른 그 몸뚱이에는 군더더기 살 한 점, 군더더기 수염 한 올 없었고, 그 턱에는 챙 넓은 모자의 닳은 보풀처럼 부드럽고 알뜰한 보풀이 돋아 있었다."]], [["Such, then, was the person that I saw seated on the transom when I followed Captain Peleg down into the cabin.", "펠레그 선장을 따라 선실로 내려갔을 때 고물 가로판에 앉아 있던 사람이 바로 그런 인물이었다. 갑판 사이의 공간은 비좁았다."], ["The space between the decks was small; and there, bolt-upright, sat old Bildad, who always sat so, and never leaned, and this to save his coat tails.", "그리고 거기, 늙은 빌대드가 꼿꼿이 앉아 있었다. 그는 늘 그렇게 앉았으며 결코 등을 기대지 않았으니, 이는 외투 자락을 아끼기 위해서였다."], ["His broad-brim was placed beside him; his legs were stiffly crossed; his drab vesture was buttoned up to his chin; and spectacles on nose, he seemed absorbed in reading from a ponderous volume.", "그의 챙 넓은 모자는 곁에 놓여 있었고, 두 다리는 빳빳이 꼰 채였으며, 그 칙칙한 빛깔의 옷은 턱밑까지 단추가 채워져 있었다. 그리고 콧등에 안경을 걸친 채, 그는 묵직한 책 한 권을 읽는 데 골몰해 있는 듯했다."]], [["“Bildad,”", "\"빌대드,\""], ["cried Captain Peleg, “at it again, Bildad, eh?", "펠레그 선장이 외쳤다. \"또 그러고 있나, 빌대드, 응?"], ["Ye have been studying those Scriptures, now, for the last thirty years, to my certain knowledge.", "자네는 지난 삼십 년 동안 줄곧 그 성경을 들여다보고 있는 게야, 내가 똑똑히 아는 바로는."], ["How far ye got, Bildad?”", "어디까지 읽었나, 빌대드?\""]], [["As if long habituated to such profane talk from his old shipmate, Bildad, without noticing his present irreverence, quietly looked up, and seeing me, glanced again inquiringly towards Peleg.", "오랜 뱃동무에게서 그런 불경스러운 소리를 듣는 데 이골이 난 듯, 빌대드는 그의 이번 불경함도 아랑곳하지 않고 조용히 눈을 들어, 나를 보더니, 다시 펠레그 쪽을 향해 묻는 듯한 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["“He says he’s our man, Bildad,”", "\"이 친구가 자기가 우리 사람이라는군, 빌대드,\""], ["said Peleg, “he wants to ship.”", "펠레그가 말했다. \"배를 타고 싶다는 거야.\""]], [["“Dost thee?”", "\"자네가 그러는가?\""], ["said Bildad, in a hollow tone, and turning round to me.", "빌대드가 공허한 어조로 말하며 내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“I dost,”", "\"제가 그러옵니다.\""], ["said I unconsciously, he was so intense a Quaker.", "나도 모르게 그렇게 말했다. 그가 어찌나 철저한 퀘이커교도였던지."]], [["“What do ye think of him, Bildad?”", "\"이 친구를 어떻게 보나, 빌대드?\""], ["said Peleg.", "펠레그가 말했다."]], [["“He’ll do,”", "\"쓸 만하겠어.\""], ["said Bildad, eyeing me, and then went on spelling away at his book in a mumbling tone quite audible.", "빌대드가 나를 찬찬히 보며 말하더니, 다시 또렷이 들리는 웅얼거리는 어조로 책을 한 자 한 자 짚어 나가기 시작했다."]], [["I thought him the queerest old Quaker I ever saw, especially as Peleg, his friend and old shipmate, seemed such a blusterer.", "나는 그가 내가 본 중에서 가장 별난 늙은 퀘이커교도라고 생각했다. 더구나 그의 벗이자 오랜 뱃동무인 펠레그가 그토록 떠벌이는 자였으니 더욱 그러했다."], ["But I said nothing, only looking round me sharply.", "그러나 나는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 그저 주위를 날카롭게 둘러보기만 했다."], ["Peleg now threw open a chest, and drawing forth the ship’s articles, placed pen and ink before him, and seated himself at a little table.", "펠레그는 이제 궤짝 하나를 열어젖히고, 배의 계약 서류를 꺼내어, 펜과 잉크를 자기 앞에 놓더니, 작은 탁자 앞에 앉았다."], ["I began to think it was high time to settle with myself at what terms I would be willing to engage for the voyage.", "나는 이제 이 항해에 어떤 조건으로 응할 마음이 있는지 스스로 정해 둘 때가 되었다고 생각하기 시작했다."], ["I was already aware that in the whaling business they paid no wages; but all hands, including the captain, received certain shares of the profits called lays, and that these lays were proportioned to the degree of importance pertaining to the respective duties of the ship’s company.", "나는 포경업에서는 삯을 지불하지 않는다는 것을 이미 알고 있었다. 다만 선장을 포함한 모든 선원이 레이(lay)라 불리는 이익의 일정한 몫을 받았으며, 이 레이는 배에 탄 사람들 각자의 임무가 지니는 중요도에 비례한다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["I was also aware that being a green hand at whaling, my own lay would not be very large; but considering that I was used to the sea, could steer a ship, splice a rope, and all that, I made no doubt that from all I had heard I should be offered at least the 275th lay—that is, the 275th part of the clear net proceeds of the voyage, whatever that might eventually amount to.", "나는 또한 포경에 풋내기인 만큼 내 몫의 레이는 그리 크지 않으리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러나 내가 바다에 익숙하고, 배를 몰 줄 알며, 밧줄을 이을 줄도 알고 그 밖에도 이런저런 것을 할 줄 안다는 점을 헤아릴 때, 내가 들은 바로 미루어 적어도 275번째 레이는 제안받으리라는 것을—다시 말해 항해의 순이익이 결국 얼마가 되든 그 275분의 1은 받으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않았다."], ["And though the 275th lay was what they call a rather long lay, yet it was better than nothing; and if we had a lucky voyage, might pretty nearly pay for the clothing I would wear out on it, not to speak of my three years’ beef and board, for which I would not have to pay one stiver.", "그리고 275번째 레이라는 것이 이른바 다소 박한 레이이긴 했으나, 그래도 없는 것보다는 나았다. 게다가 운 좋게 항해를 마친다면, 그 항해 동안 닳아 없앨 옷값쯤은 거의 메울 수 있을 만했고, 한 푼도 치를 필요 없는 삼 년치 쇠고기와 숙식은 더 말할 것도 없었다."]], [["It might be thought that this was a poor way to accumulate a princely fortune—and so it was, a very poor way indeed.", "이것이 왕후 같은 재산을 모으기에는 보잘것없는 방도라고 여겨질지도 모른다—과연 그러했으니, 참으로 보잘것없는 방도였다."], ["But I am one of those that never take on about princely fortunes, and am quite content if the world is ready to board and lodge me, while I am putting up at this grim sign of the Thunder Cloud.", "그러나 나는 왕후 같은 재산 따위로 안달복달하는 법이 없는 부류의 사람이라, 이 음산한 천둥 구름 간판 아래 묵고 있는 동안 세상이 내게 숙식만 베풀어 줄 채비가 되어 있다면 그것으로 흡족하다."], ["Upon the whole, I thought that the 275th lay would be about the fair thing, but would not have been surprised had I been offered the 200th, considering I was of a broad-shouldered make.", "대체로 보아, 나는 275번째 레이가 적당한 셈이리라고 생각했으나, 어깨가 떡 벌어진 체격임을 헤아려 200번째를 제안받았다 한들 놀라지는 않았을 것이다."]], [["But one thing, nevertheless, that made me a little distrustful about receiving a generous share of the profits was this: Ashore, I had heard something of both Captain Peleg and his unaccountable old crony Bildad; how that they being the principal proprietors of the Pequod, therefore the other and more inconsiderable and scattered owners, left nearly the whole management of the ship’s affairs to these two. And I did not know but what the stingy old Bildad might have a mighty deal to say about shipping hands, especially as I now found him on board the Pequod, quite at home there in the cabin, and reading his Bible as if at his own fireside. Now while Peleg was vainly trying to mend a pen with his jack-knife, old Bildad, to my no small surprise, considering that he was such an interested party in these proceedings; Bildad never heeded us, but went on mumbling to himself out of his book, “Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth, where moth—”", "그렇지만, 그래도 한 가지, 내가 후한 몫의 이익을 받는 일에 대해 좀 미덥지 않게 여기게 만든 것이 있었으니 바로 이것이었다. 뭍에 있을 때, 나는 펠레그 선장과 그의 까닭 모를 늙은 단짝 빌대드에 관해 얼마간 이야기를 들었다. 그들이 피쿼드호의 주요 소유주인 까닭에, 나머지 더 보잘것없고 흩어진 선주들이 배의 사무 거의 전부를 이 두 사람에게 맡겨 두고 있다는 것이었다. 그리고 나로서는 그 인색한 늙은 빌대드가 선원 고용에 관해 막강한 발언권을 쥐고 있지나 않을까 알 수 없었으니, 더구나 이제 그가 피쿼드호 선상에서, 선실 안에 마치 제 집 난롯가에 있는 양 편안히 자리 잡고 앉아 성경을 읽고 있는 것을 보았으니 더욱 그러했다. 그런데 펠레그가 잭나이프로 펜을 다듬어 보려고 헛되이 애쓰는 동안, 늙은 빌대드는, 그가 이 일에 그토록 이해관계가 걸린 당사자임을 헤아릴 때 나로서는 적잖이 놀랍게도, 우리에게는 도무지 아랑곳하지 않고, 자기 책에서 혼자 웅얼웅얼 읽어 내려갈 따름이었다. \"너희를 위하여 보물을 땅에 쌓아 두지 마라. 거기는 좀이—\""]], [["“Well, Captain Bildad,”", "\"이봐요, 빌대드 선장,\""], ["interrupted Peleg, “what d’ye say, what lay shall we give this young man?”", "펠레그가 끼어들었다. \"어떻게 하겠나, 이 젊은 친구한테 어떤 레이를 줄까?\""]], [["“Thou knowest best,”", "\"자네가 가장 잘 알겠지.\""], ["was the sepulchral reply, “the seven hundred and seventy-seventh wouldn’t be too much, would it?—‘where moth and rust do corrupt, but lay—’”", "무덤 속에서 나오는 듯한 대답이었다. \"칠백칠십칠 번째라면 너무 과하지는 않겠지, 안 그런가?—'거기는 좀과 동록이 해하나, 너희를 위하여—'\""]], [["Lay, indeed, thought I, and such a lay!", "레이라니, 참, 나는 생각했다, 그것도 그런 레이라니!"], ["the seven hundred and seventy-seventh!", "칠백칠십칠 번째라!"], ["Well, old Bildad, you are determined that I, for one, shall not lay up many lays here below, where moth and rust do corrupt.", "그래, 늙은 빌대드 양반, 당신은 적어도 나 하나만큼은, 좀과 동록이 해하는 이 땅 아래에 레이를 많이 쌓아 두지 못하게 할 작정인 게로군. 그것은 참으로 더없이 박한 레이였다."], ["It was an exceedingly long lay that, indeed; and though from the magnitude of the figure it might at first deceive a landsman, yet the slightest consideration will show that though seven hundred and seventy-seven is a pretty large number, yet, when you come to make a teenth of it, you will then see, I say, that the seven hundred and seventy-seventh part of a farthing is a good deal less than seven hundred and seventy-seven gold doubloons; and so I thought at the time.", "그리고 그 숫자의 크기 때문에 뭍사람이라면 처음엔 속아 넘어갈지도 모르나, 조금만 헤아려 보면 알 수 있으니, 칠백칠십칠은 제법 큰 수이지만, 막상 그것의 몇 분의 일을 따지기 시작하면, 다시 말하건대, 1파딩의 칠백칠십칠 분의 일이 칠백칠십칠 닢의 금화 더블룬보다는 한참 적다는 것이 드러나는 것이다. 그래서 나는 그때 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["“Why, blast your eyes, Bildad,”", "\"이런, 눈깔이 멀었나, 빌대드,\""], ["cried Peleg, “thou dost not want to swindle this young man!", "펠레그가 외쳤다. \"자네 이 젊은 친구를 등쳐 먹을 셈은 아니겠지!"], ["he must have more than that.”", "이 친구는 그보다는 더 받아야 해.\""]], [["“Seven hundred and seventy-seventh,”", "\"칠백칠십칠 번째.\""], ["again said Bildad, without lifting his eyes; and then went on mumbling—“for where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.”", "빌대드가 눈도 들지 않고 다시 말하더니, 이어 웅얼웅얼 읊어 나갔다. \"네 보물 있는 그곳에는 네 마음도 있으리라.\""]], [["“I am going to put him down for the three hundredth,”", "\"나는 이 친구를 삼백 번째로 적어 넣을 작정이야.\""], ["said Peleg, “do ye hear that, Bildad!", "펠레그가 말했다. \"그 말 들었나, 빌대드!"], ["The three hundredth lay, I say.”", "삼백 번째 레이라고, 분명히 말하네.\""]], [["Bildad laid down his book, and turning solemnly towards him said, “Captain Peleg, thou hast a generous heart; but thou must consider the duty thou owest to the other owners of this ship—widows and orphans, many of them—and that if we too abundantly reward the labors of this young man, we may be taking the bread from those widows and those orphans. The seven hundred and seventy-seventh lay, Captain Peleg.”", "빌대드는 책을 내려놓고, 엄숙하게 그를 향해 돌아앉으며 말했다. \"펠레그 선장, 자네는 마음이 후한 사람일세. 하지만 이 배의 다른 선주들—그 가운데에는 과부와 고아도 많지—에게 진 자네의 의무를 헤아려야 하네. 만일 우리가 이 젊은이의 노고에 너무 후한 보답을 한다면, 우리는 그 과부들과 그 고아들의 입에서 빵을 앗아 가는 셈이 될지도 모르거든. 칠백칠십칠 번째 레이일세, 펠레그 선장.\""]], [["“Thou Bildad!”", "\"이런 빌대드 같으니라고!\""], ["roared Peleg, starting up and clattering about the cabin.", "펠레그가 벌떡 일어나 선실 안을 쿵쾅거리며 돌아다니며 고함쳤다."], ["“Blast ye, Captain Bildad, if I had followed thy advice in these matters, I would afore now had a conscience to lug about that would be heavy enough to founder the largest ship that ever sailed round Cape Horn.”", "\"젠장맞을, 빌대드 선장, 이런 일에서 내가 자네 충고를 따랐더라면, 나는 지금쯤 케이프 혼을 돌아 항해한 그 어떤 큰 배라도 가라앉힐 만큼 묵직한 양심 덩어리를 짊어지고 다녀야 했을 게야.\""]], [["“Captain Peleg,”", "\"펠레그 선장,\" 빌대드가 차분하게 말했다."], ["said Bildad steadily, “thy conscience may be drawing ten inches of water, or ten fathoms, I can’t tell; but as thou art still an impenitent man, Captain Peleg, I greatly fear lest thy conscience be but a leaky one; and will in the end sink thee foundering down to the fiery pit, Captain Peleg.”", "\"자네 양심이 물에 열 인치쯤 잠겨 있을지, 열 길쯤 잠겨 있을지, 나야 알 수 없네. 하지만 자네가 여전히 회개를 모르는 사람인지라, 펠레그 선장, 나는 자네 양심이 그저 물이 새는 양심에 지나지 않아, 끝내는 자네를 불구덩이 속으로 가라앉혀 버리지나 않을까 몹시 두렵다네, 펠레그 선장.\""]], [["“Fiery pit!", "\"불구덩이!"], ["fiery pit!", "불구덩이라니!"], ["ye insult me, man; past all natural bearing, ye insult me.", "자네 지금 나를 모욕하는 게야, 이 사람아. 인간이 도저히 참을 수 없을 만큼 나를 모욕하고 있어."], ["It’s an all-fired outrage to tell any human creature that he’s bound to hell.", "사람을 두고 지옥에 떨어지게 정해져 있다고 말하는 건 천하에 괘씸한 망발이야."], ["Flukes and flames!", "고래꼬리에 불벼락 맞을!"], ["Bildad, say that again to me, and start my soul-bolts, but I’ll—I’ll—yes, I’ll swallow a live goat with all his hair and horns on.", "빌대드, 그 말을 내게 한 번만 더 해 봐, 내 영혼의 빗장이 튀어나가게, 그러면 나는—나는—그래, 나는 산 염소를 털이며 뿔이며 통째로 삼켜 버릴 테다."], ["Out of the cabin, ye canting, drab-coloured son of a wooden gun—a straight wake with ye!”", "선실에서 나가게, 이 위선이나 떠는 칙칙한 빛깔의, 나무 대포 같은 작자야—곧장 침로를 잡고 꺼지라고!\""]], [["As he thundered out this he made a rush at Bildad, but with a marvellous oblique, sliding celerity, Bildad for that time eluded him.", "이렇게 우레같이 내지르며 그는 빌대드에게 달려들었으나, 빌대드는 놀랍도록 비스듬히, 미끄러지듯 잽싸게 그때만큼은 그를 피해 버렸다."]], [["Alarmed at this terrible outburst between the two principal and responsible owners of the ship, and feeling half a mind to give up all idea of sailing in a vessel so questionably owned and temporarily commanded, I stepped aside from the door to give egress to Bildad, who, I made no doubt, was all eagerness to vanish from before the awakened wrath of Peleg.", "이 배의 가장 주요하고 책임 있는 두 선주 사이에서 벌어진 이 무시무시한 폭발에 깜짝 놀라, 또 이토록 미심쩍게 소유되고 임시로 지휘되는 배에 오를 생각을 죄다 접어 버릴까 하는 마음이 반쯤 들어, 나는 문간에서 비켜서서 빌대드가 나가도록 길을 터 주었다. 그가 깨어난 펠레그의 분노 앞에서 어서 사라지려 안달이 나 있으리라는 것을 나는 추호도 의심치 않았던 것이다."], ["But to my astonishment, he sat down again on the transom very quietly, and seemed to have not the slightest intention of withdrawing.", "그러나 놀랍게도, 그는 다시 고물 가로판에 아주 조용히 앉더니, 물러날 생각이라고는 털끝만큼도 없는 듯했다."], ["He seemed quite used to impenitent Peleg and his ways.", "그는 회개를 모르는 펠레그와 그의 행태에 자못 이골이 난 모양이었다."], ["As for Peleg, after letting off his rage as he had, there seemed no more left in him, and he, too, sat down like a lamb, though he twitched a little as if still nervously agitated.", "펠레그로 말하자면, 그렇게 분풀이를 다 하고 나니, 안에 더 남은 것이 없는 듯, 그 역시 어린양처럼 얌전히 앉았다. 다만 여전히 신경이 곤두선 듯 이따금 움찔거리기는 했다."], ["“Whew!”", "\"휘유!\""], ["he whistled at last—“the squall’s gone off to leeward, I think.", "마침내 그가 휘파람을 불었다—\"돌풍이 바람 불어 가는 쪽으로 물러간 모양이야, 내 생각엔."], ["Bildad, thou used to be good at sharpening a lance, mend that pen, will ye.", "빌대드, 자네 예전에 창날 가는 솜씨가 좋았지, 이 펜 좀 다듬어 주게나."], ["My jack-knife here needs the grindstone.", "내 잭나이프는 여기 숫돌이 필요하군."], ["That’s he; thank ye, Bildad.", "옳지, 그거야. 고맙네, 빌대드."], ["Now then, my young man, Ishmael’s thy name, didn’t ye say?", "자, 그럼, 젊은이, 자네 이름이 이슈메일이라 했던가?"], ["Well then, down ye go here, Ishmael, for the three hundredth lay.”", "좋아, 그렇다면 여기 적어 넣게나, 이슈메일, 삼백 번째 레이로.\""]], [["“Captain Peleg,”", "\"펠레그 선장,\""], ["said I, “I have a friend with me who wants to ship too—shall I bring him down to-morrow?”", "내가 말했다. \"저와 함께 온 친구가 하나 있는데, 그 친구도 배를 타고 싶어 합니다—내일 데려와도 될까요?\""]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"물론이지.\""], ["said Peleg.", "펠레그가 말했다."], ["“Fetch him along, and we’ll look at him.”", "\"데려오게, 그러면 우리가 한번 보지.\""]], [["“What lay does he want?”", "\"그 친구는 어떤 레이를 원하나?\""], ["groaned Bildad, glancing up from the book in which he had again been burying himself.", "다시 책 속에 파묻혀 있던 빌대드가 눈을 들며 끙 하고 물었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["never thee mind about that, Bildad,”", "그건 자네가 신경 쓸 일이 아닐세, 빌대드.\""], ["said Peleg.", "펠레그가 말했다."], ["“Has he ever whaled it any?”", "\"그 친구는 포경을 해 본 적이 있나?\""], ["turning to me.", "그가 내게로 돌아서며 물었다."]], [["“Killed more whales than I can count, Captain Peleg.”", "\"제가 셀 수 없을 만큼 많은 고래를 잡았습니다, 펠레그 선장.\""]], [["“Well, bring him along then.”", "\"좋아, 그럼 데려오게나.\""]], [["And, after signing the papers, off I went; nothing doubting but that I had done a good morning’s work, and that the Pequod was the identical ship that Yojo had provided to carry Queequeg and me round the Cape.", "그리하여 서류에 서명을 마치고 나는 떠났다. 내가 오전을 알차게 잘 보냈다는 것, 그리고 피쿼드호야말로 요조가 퀴퀘그와 나를 케이프 혼 너머로 실어 보내려고 마련해 둔 바로 그 배라는 것을 추호도 의심치 않으면서."]], [["But I had not proceeded far, when I began to bethink me that the Captain with whom I was to sail yet remained unseen by me; though, indeed, in many cases, a whale-ship will be completely fitted out, and receive all her crew on board, ere the captain makes himself visible by arriving to take command; for sometimes these voyages are so prolonged, and the shore intervals at home so exceedingly brief, that if the captain have a family, or any absorbing concernment of that sort, he does not trouble himself much about his ship in port, but leaves her to the owners till all is ready for sea.", "그러나 얼마 가지 않아, 나는 정작 함께 항해할 그 선장을 아직 한 번도 보지 못했다는 데 생각이 미치기 시작했다. 하기야, 많은 경우에 포경선은 선장이 지휘를 맡으러 나타나 모습을 드러내기 전에 채비를 완전히 갖추고 선원들을 죄다 배에 태우기도 한다."], ["However, it is always as well to have a look at him before irrevocably committing yourself into his hands.", "이런 항해는 때로 너무도 길게 이어지고, 고향에서 뭍에 머무는 기간은 더없이 짧은 까닭에, 선장이 가정을 두었거나 그런 종류의 마음 쓰이는 일이 있으면, 항구에 있는 배에는 그리 마음을 쓰지 않고, 출항 채비가 다 갖춰질 때까지 그것을 선주들에게 맡겨 두기 때문이다. 그러나 자신을 돌이킬 수 없이 그의 손에 맡기기 전에, 늘 한번 그를 보아 두는 편이 나은 법이다."], ["Turning back I accosted Captain Peleg, inquiring where Captain Ahab was to be found.", "발길을 돌려 나는 펠레그 선장에게 다가가, 에이해브 선장을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 물었다."]], [["“And what dost thou want of Captain Ahab?", "\"한데 에이해브 선장한테 무슨 볼일이 있느냐?"], ["It’s all right enough; thou art shipped.”", "다 끝난 일이야. 자네는 배에 오르기로 된 몸이라고.\""]], [["“Yes, but I should like to see him.”", "\"네, 하지만 그분을 좀 뵙고 싶어서요.\""]], [["“But I don’t think thou wilt be able to at present.", "\"하지만 지금은 자네가 그러지 못할 것 같군. 나도 그에게 정확히 무슨 일이 있는 건지는 모르겠으나, 그는 집 안에만 틀어박혀 있어."], ["I don’t know exactly what’s the matter with him; but he keeps close inside the house; a sort of sick, and yet he don’t look so.", "일종의 병자 같으면서도, 또 그렇게 보이지도 않거든. 사실 그는 아픈 게 아니야."], ["In fact, he ain’t sick; but no, he isn’t well either.", "하지만, 그렇다고 또 멀쩡한 것도 아니지. 어쨌든, 젊은이, 그는 나조차도 늘 만나 주지는 않으니, 자네라고 만나 줄 성싶지가 않군."], ["Any how, young man, he won’t always see me, so I don’t suppose he will thee.", "그는 별난 사람이야, 에이해브 선장은—더러는 그렇게들 여기지—하지만 좋은 사람이지. 오, 자네도 그를 충분히 좋아하게 될 거야."], ["He’s a queer man, Captain Ahab—so some think—but a good one.", "걱정 말게, 걱정 마. 그는 위대하고, 불경하면서도, 신과 같은 사람이야, 에이해브 선장은."], ["Oh, thou’lt like him well enough; no fear, no fear.", "말수는 적지만, 일단 입을 열면, 그때는 자네 귀를 기울이는 게 좋을 거야. 명심하게, 미리 일러 두는데, 에이해브는 여느 사람을 넘어선 인물이야."], ["He’s a grand, ungodly, god-like man, Captain Ahab; doesn’t speak much; but, when he does speak, then you may well listen.", "에이해브는 대학에도 다녔고, 식인종 사이에서도 살아 봤어. 파도보다 더 깊은 경이를 겪어 왔고, 고래보다 더 강하고 더 기이한 적들에게 그 불타는 창을 꽂아 왔지."], ["Mark ye, be forewarned; Ahab’s above the common; Ahab’s been in colleges, as well as ’mong the cannibals; been used to deeper wonders than the waves; fixed his fiery lance in mightier, stranger foes than whales.", "그의 창이라니! 그래, 우리 섬을 통틀어 가장 날카롭고 가장 어김없는 창이지!"], ["His lance!", "오! 그는 빌대드 선장이 아니야."], ["aye, the keenest and the surest that out of all our isle!", "그렇고말고, 펠레그 선장도 아니지. 그는 에이해브야, 이 사람아."], ["Oh! he ain’t Captain Bildad; no, and he ain’t Captain Peleg; he’s Ahab, boy; and Ahab of old, thou knowest, was a crowned king!”", "그리고 옛적의 에이해브는, 자네도 알다시피, 왕관을 쓴 왕이었지!\""]], [["“And a very vile one.", "\"게다가 아주 사악한 왕이었지요."], ["When that wicked king was slain, the dogs, did they not lick his blood?”", "그 못된 왕이 죽임을 당했을 때, 개들이 그의 피를 핥지 않았던가요?\""]], [["“Come hither to me—hither, hither,”", "\"이리 가까이 와 보게나—이리로, 이리로.\""], ["said Peleg, with a significance in his eye that almost startled me.", "펠레그가 거의 나를 흠칫하게 할 만큼 의미심장한 눈빛으로 말했다."], ["“Look ye, lad; never say that on board the Pequod.", "\"이봐라, 젊은이. 피쿼드호 위에서는 결코 그런 말을 하지 마라."], ["Never say it anywhere.", "어디서든 절대 하지 마."], ["Captain Ahab did not name himself.", "에이해브 선장이 스스로 제 이름을 지은 게 아니야."], ["’Twas a foolish, ignorant whim of his crazy, widowed mother, who died when he was only a twelvemonth old.", "그건 그의 미친, 과부가 된 어머니의 어리석고 무지한 변덕이었어. 그 어머니는 그가 겨우 한 살밖에 안 됐을 때 죽었지."], ["And yet the old squaw Tistig, at Gayhead, said that the name would somehow prove prophetic.", "그런데도 게이헤드의 늙은 무녀 티스티그는 그 이름이 어쩐지 예언처럼 들어맞으리라고 했어. 그리고 어쩌면 그 여자 같은 다른 바보들도 자네한테 똑같은 소리를 할지 모르지."], ["And, perhaps, other fools like her may tell thee the same.", "나는 자네에게 미리 일러 두고 싶네. 그건 거짓말이야."], ["I wish to warn thee. It’s a lie.", "나는 에이해브 선장을 잘 알아."], ["I know Captain Ahab well; I’ve sailed with him as mate years ago; I know what he is—a good man—not a pious, good man, like Bildad, but a swearing good man—something like me—only there’s a good deal more of him.", "몇 해 전에 그와 항해사로서 함께 항해했거든. 나는 그가 어떤 사람인지 알아—좋은 사람이지—빌대드처럼 경건한 좋은 사람이 아니라, 욕설을 입에 달고 사는 좋은 사람이야—나하고 좀 비슷한 부류지—다만 그가 나보다 훨씬 더 큰 그릇일 뿐이야."], ["Aye, aye, I know that he was never very jolly; and I know that on the passage home, he was a little out of his mind for a spell; but it was the sharp shooting pains in his bleeding stump that brought that about, as any one might see.", "그래, 그래, 그가 한 번도 그리 유쾌한 사람이 아니었던 건 나도 알아. 그리고 고향으로 돌아오는 항해에서 한동안 그가 좀 제정신이 아니었던 것도 알지."], ["I know, too, that ever since he lost his leg last voyage by that accursed whale, he’s been a kind of moody—desperate moody, and savage sometimes; but that will all pass off.", "하지만 그건 그 피 흘리는 다리 토막에서 솟구치는 날카로운 통증 때문에 그리된 거였어, 누구라도 알 만한 일이지. 또 나는 알아, 지난 항해에서 그 저주받은 고래한테 다리를 잃은 뒤로, 그가 줄곧 일종의 침울한—지독하게 침울하고, 때로는 사나운 사람이 되어 버린 것을."], ["And once for all, let me tell thee and assure thee, young man, it’s better to sail with a moody good captain than a laughing bad one.", "하지만 그것도 다 지나갈 거야. 그리고 마지막으로 단단히 일러 두는데, 젊은이, 잘 새겨 두게나, 웃음 헤픈 나쁜 선장과 함께 항해하느니 침울한 좋은 선장과 함께 항해하는 편이 나은 법이야."], ["So good-bye to thee—and wrong not Captain Ahab, because he happens to have a wicked name.", "그러니 잘 가게나—그리고 에이해브 선장이 마침 사악한 이름을 지녔다 하여 그를 함부로 깎아내리지 말게."], ["Besides, my boy, he has a wife—not three voyages wedded—a sweet, resigned girl.", "게다가, 이 사람아, 그에게는 아내가 있어—혼인한 지 세 번의 항해도 채 안 된, 다정하고 순종적인 처녀라네. 생각해 보게나."], ["Think of that; by that sweet girl that old man has a child: hold ye then there can be any utter, hopeless harm in Ahab?", "그 다정한 처녀를 통해 그 노인에게는 자식도 하나 있어. 그러니 자네, 에이해브에게 어떤 철저하고 가망 없는 악이 깃들어 있을 수 있겠나?"], ["No, no, my lad; stricken, blasted, if he be, Ahab has his humanities!”", "아닐세, 아니야, 이 사람아. 비록 그가 얻어맞고 짓밟혀 망가졌다 한들, 에이해브에게도 인간다운 따스함은 있다네!\""]], [["As I walked away, I was full of thoughtfulness; what had been incidentally revealed to me of Captain Ahab, filled me with a certain wild vagueness of painfulness concerning him.", "발걸음을 옮기는 동안, 나는 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 에이해브 선장에 관해 우연히 내게 드러난 것들이, 그에 대한 어떤 거칠고 막연한 고통스러움으로 나를 가득 채웠다."], ["And somehow, at the time, I felt a sympathy and a sorrow for him, but for I don’t know what, unless it was the cruel loss of his leg.", "그리고 어쩐지, 그때 나는 그에게 동정과 슬픔을 느꼈는데, 그것이 무엇 때문이었는지는 모르겠다, 그의 잔혹한 다리 상실 때문이 아니라면 말이다. 그러면서도 나는 그에게 야릇한 경외감 또한 느꼈다."], ["And yet I also felt a strange awe of him; but that sort of awe, which I cannot at all describe, was not exactly awe; I do not know what it was.", "그러나 그 경외감이라는 것은, 내가 도무지 설명할 수 없는 종류의 것이어서, 정확히 말하면 경외감도 아니었다. 그것이 무엇이었는지 나는 모른다."], ["But I felt it; and it did not disincline me towards him; though I felt impatience at what seemed like mystery in him, so imperfectly as he was known to me then.", "그러나 나는 그것을 느꼈다. 그리고 그것이 나를 그에게서 멀어지게 하지는 않았다."], ["However, my thoughts were at length carried in other directions, so that for the present dark Ahab slipped my mind.", "다만 그때 그가 내게 그토록 어렴풋이만 알려진 까닭에, 그에게 깃든 듯한 그 수수께끼 같은 것에 나는 조바심을 느꼈다. 그러나 내 생각은 마침내 다른 방향으로 흘러갔고, 그리하여 당분간 어두운 에이해브는 내 마음에서 미끄러져 나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 17. The Ramadan.", "# CHAPTER 17. The Ramadan."]], [["As Queequeg’s Ramadan, or Fasting and Humiliation, was to continue all day, I did not choose to disturb him till towards night-fall; for I cherish the greatest respect towards everybody’s religious obligations, never mind how comical, and could not find it in my heart to undervalue even a congregation of ants worshipping a toad-stool; or those other creatures in certain parts of our earth, who with a degree of footmanism quite unprecedented in other planets, bow down before the torso of a deceased landed proprietor merely on account of the inordinate possessions yet owned and rented in his name.", "퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 라마단, 곧 단식과 고행은 온종일 이어질 예정이었으므로, 나는 해 질 무렵까지 그를 방해하지 않기로 했다. 나는 그것이 아무리 우스꽝스럽더라도 누구의 종교적 의무든 더없이 존중하는 사람이라, 두꺼비버섯을 숭배하는 개미 떼라 한들 그 모임을 함부로 깔보는 마음을 도무지 품을 수 없는 까닭이다. 또한 우리 지구의 어떤 지역에 사는 또 다른 피조물들, 다른 행성에서는 결코 볼 수 없는 정도의 노예근성으로, 어느 죽은 지주의 몸뚱이 앞에 머리를 조아리는 자들 역시 마찬가지다. 그것도 다만 그자의 이름으로 여전히 소유되고 임대되는 터무니없이 막대한 재산 때문에 그러는 것일 뿐인데도 말이다."]], [["I say, we good Presbyterian Christians should be charitable in these things, and not fancy ourselves so vastly superior to other mortals, pagans and what not, because of their half-crazy conceits on these subjects.", "내 말은, 우리 선량한 장로교 기독교인들은 이런 문제에 있어 너그러워야 하며, 이교도든 무엇이든 다른 인간들이 이런 주제에 관해 반쯤 미친 망상을 품고 있다 해서 우리가 그들보다 그토록 까마득히 우월하다고 자만해서는 안 된다는 것이다. 자, 여기 퀴퀘그가 있었다."], ["There was Queequeg, now, certainly entertaining the most absurd notions about Yojo and his Ramadan;—but what of that?", "그는 분명 요조(Yojo)와 자기 라마단에 관해 더없이 터무니없는 생각들을 품고 있었다 — 그러나 그게 무슨 상관인가? 퀴퀘그는 자기가 무얼 하는지 안다고 여기는 듯했고, 그것으로 만족하는 듯했다."], ["Queequeg thought he knew what he was about, I suppose; he seemed to be content; and there let him rest.", "그러니 그냥 내버려 두자. 우리가 아무리 그와 논쟁한들 아무 소용이 없을 터, 그저 내버려 두자는 것이다."], ["All our arguing with him would not avail; let him be, I say: and Heaven have mercy on us all—Presbyterians and Pagans alike—for we are all somehow dreadfully cracked about the head, and sadly need mending.", "그리고 하늘이여, 우리 모두에게 — 장로교도든 이교도든 똑같이 — 자비를 베푸소서. 왜냐하면 우리는 모두 어쩐지 머릿속이 지독히도 금이 가 있고, 안타깝게도 손질이 절실히 필요한 자들이니."]], [["Towards evening, when I felt assured that all his performances and rituals must be over, I went up to his room and knocked at the door; but no answer.", "저녁 무렵, 그의 모든 의식과 예배가 틀림없이 끝났으리라 확신이 들었을 때, 나는 그의 방으로 올라가 문을 두드렸다. 그러나 대답이 없었다."], ["I tried to open it, but it was fastened inside.", "문을 열어 보려 했으나 안에서 잠겨 있었다."], ["“Queequeg,”", "\"퀴퀘그.\""], ["said I softly through the key-hole:—all silent.", "나는 열쇠 구멍에 대고 나직이 불렀다. 사방이 고요했다."], ["“I say, Queequeg!", "\"이봐, 퀴퀘그!"], ["why don’t you speak?", "왜 말을 안 해?"], ["It’s I—Ishmael.”", "날세 — 이슈메일이야.\""], ["But all remained still as before.", "그러나 여전히 아까처럼 잠잠했다."], ["I began to grow alarmed.", "나는 슬슬 겁이 나기 시작했다."], ["I had allowed him such abundant time; I thought he might have had an apoplectic fit.", "그에게 그토록 넉넉한 시간을 주었으니, 혹시 뇌졸중이라도 일으킨 게 아닐까 싶었다."], ["I looked through the key-hole; but the door opening into an odd corner of the room, the key-hole prospect was but a crooked and sinister one.", "열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보았으나, 문이 방의 묘한 구석으로 열리게 되어 있어 열쇠 구멍으로 보이는 광경은 비뚜름하고 불길하기만 했다. 침대 발치 판자의 일부와 벽의 한 줄기만 보일 뿐, 그 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["I could only see part of the foot-board of the bed and a line of the wall, but nothing more.", "그런데 벽에 기대 세워진 퀴퀘그의 작살 나무 자루가 눈에 들어와 나는 놀랐다."], ["I was surprised to behold resting against the wall the wooden shaft of Queequeg’s harpoon, which the landlady the evening previous had taken from him, before our mounting to the chamber.", "그것은 전날 저녁 우리가 방으로 올라가기 전에 안주인이 그에게서 빼앗아 두었던 것이었다. 이상한걸, 하고 나는 생각했다."], ["That’s strange, thought I; but at any rate, since the harpoon stands yonder, and he seldom or never goes abroad without it, therefore he must be inside here, and no possible mistake.", "그러나 어쨌든 작살이 저기 서 있고, 그는 그것 없이는 좀처럼, 아니 결코 밖에 나가는 법이 없으니, 그렇다면 그는 틀림없이 이 안에 있는 것이다. 추호도 틀릴 리 없었다."]], [["“Queequeg!—Queequeg!”—all still.", "\"퀴퀘그! — 퀴퀘그!\""], ["Something must have happened.", "— 여전히 고요했다. 무슨 일이 벌어진 게 분명했다."], ["Apoplexy!", "뇌졸중이다!"], ["I tried to burst open the door; but it stubbornly resisted.", "나는 문을 부수어 열려고 했으나, 문은 완강히 버텼다."], ["Running down stairs, I quickly stated my suspicions to the first person I met—the chamber-maid.", "아래층으로 달려 내려가 처음 마주친 사람 — 객실 하녀 — 에게 재빨리 내 의심을 털어놓았다. \"어머!"], ["“La! la!”", "어머나!\""], ["she cried, “I thought something must be the matter.", "그녀가 소리쳤다. \"뭔가 일이 났구나 했어요."], ["I went to make the bed after breakfast, and the door was locked; and not a mouse to be heard; and it’s been just so silent ever since.", "아침 식사 후에 침대를 정돈하러 갔더니 문이 잠겨 있고, 쥐새끼 한 마리 소리도 안 나는 거예요. 그 뒤로 줄곧 그렇게 조용했죠."], ["But I thought, may be, you had both gone off and locked your baggage in for safe keeping.", "하지만 전 두 분이 함께 어디 나가시면서 짐을 안전하게 두려고 안에 가둬 잠가 두신 줄로만 알았어요."], ["La!", "어머!"], ["la, ma’am!—Mistress!", "어머나, 마님! — 마님!"], ["murder!", "살인이에요!"], ["Mrs. Hussey!", "허시 부인!"], ["apoplexy!”—and with these cries, she ran towards the kitchen, I following.", "뇌졸중이라고요!\" — 그러고는 이런 외침과 함께 부엌으로 달려갔고, 나도 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["Mrs.", "허시 부인이 곧 나타났는데, 한 손에는 겨자 단지를, 다른 손에는 식초병을 들고 있었다."], ["Hussey soon appeared, with a mustard-pot in one hand and a vinegar-cruet in the other, having just broken away from the occupation of attending to the castors, and scolding her little black boy meantime.", "마침 양념병 받침대를 손보는 일에서 막 손을 떼고, 그새 자기네 흑인 꼬마를 야단치던 참이었다."]], [["“Wood-house!”", "\"장작 헛간!\""], ["cried I, “which way to it?", "내가 소리쳤다. \"그게 어느 쪽이오?"], ["Run for God’s sake, and fetch something to pry open the door—the axe!—the axe!", "제발 빨리 달려가서 문 비틀어 열 만한 걸 가져와요 — 도끼! — 도끼 말이오!"], ["he’s had a stroke; depend upon it!”—and so saying I was unmethodically rushing up stairs again empty-handed, when Mrs.", "그가 졸도했단 말이오. 틀림없소!\""], ["Hussey interposed the mustard-pot and vinegar-cruet, and the entire castor of her countenance.", "— 그렇게 외치며 나는 빈손으로 다시 정신없이 위층으로 돌진하려 했는데, 허시 부인이 겨자 단지와 식초병을, 그리고 자기 얼굴이라는 양념병 받침대 통째로 내 앞을 가로막았다."]], [["“What’s the matter with you, young man?”", "\"젊은이, 대체 무슨 일이오?\""]], [["“Get the axe!", "\"도끼를 가져와요!"], ["For God’s sake, run for the doctor, some one, while I pry it open!”", "제발 누구든 의사를 부르러 달려가요, 내가 문을 비틀어 여는 동안!\""]], [["“Look here,”", "\"여보쇼.\""], ["said the landlady, quickly putting down the vinegar-cruet, so as to have one hand free; “look here; are you talking about prying open any of my doors?”—and with that she seized my arm.", "안주인은 한 손을 자유롭게 하려고 재빨리 식초병을 내려놓으며 말했다. \"여보쇼, 지금 내 집 문짝을 비틀어 연다는 거요?\""], ["“What’s the matter with you?", "— 그러더니 내 팔을 붙들었다."], ["What’s the matter with you, shipmate?”", "\"젊은이, 대체 무슨 일이오? 무슨 일이냐니까, 뱃사람 양반?\""]], [["In as calm, but rapid a manner as possible, I gave her to understand the whole case.", "나는 되도록 차분하면서도 빠른 어조로 사정의 전말을 그녀에게 알아듣게 설명했다."], ["Unconsciously clapping the vinegar-cruet to one side of her nose, she ruminated for an instant; then exclaimed—“No!", "그녀는 무심결에 식초병을 콧등 한쪽에 갖다 댄 채 잠시 곰곰이 생각하더니, 이윽고 외쳤다. \"아니!"], ["I haven’t seen it since I put it there.”", "내가 거기 두고 나선 그걸 본 적이 없는데.\""], ["Running to a little closet under the landing of the stairs, she glanced in, and returning, told me that Queequeg’s harpoon was missing.", "그녀는 계단 층계참 아래의 작은 벽장으로 달려가 안을 흘긋 들여다보더니, 돌아와서는 퀴퀘그의 작살이 없어졌다고 내게 말했다."], ["“He’s killed himself,”", "\"그 사람이 자살했어.\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“It’s unfort’nate Stiggs done over again—there goes another counterpane—God pity his poor mother!—it will be the ruin of my house.", "\"불운한 스티그스 사건이 또 되풀이되는구먼 — 침대보 한 장 또 날아가게 생겼네 — 하느님, 그 가엾은 어미를 불쌍히 여기소서! — 우리 집안 망조가 들겠어."], ["Has the poor lad a sister?", "그 가엾은 젊은이한테 누이가 있나? 그 계집애 어디 갔어?"], ["Where’s that girl?—there, Betty, go to Snarles the Painter, and tell him to paint me a sign, with—“no suicides permitted here, and no smoking in the parlor;”—might as well kill both birds at once.", "— 거기, 베티, 화가 스내얼스한테 가서 간판 하나 그려 달라고 해라, 이렇게 — '이곳에서는 자살 금지, 응접실 흡연 금지'라고. 이왕이면 한꺼번에 두 마리 새를 잡는 게 낫지."], ["Kill?", "죽인다고?"], ["The Lord be merciful to his ghost!", "주여, 그 영혼에 자비를 베푸소서!"], ["What’s that noise there?", "저기 저 소리는 뭐야?"], ["You, young man, avast there!”", "이봐, 젊은이, 거기 멈춰!\""]], [["And running up after me, she caught me as I was again trying to force open the door.", "그러고는 내 뒤를 쫓아 올라와, 내가 다시 문을 억지로 열려고 하는 참에 나를 붙잡았다."]], [["“I don’t allow it; I won’t have my premises spoiled.", "\"안 돼, 그건 허락 못 해. 내 집을 망가뜨리게 둘 순 없지."], ["Go for the locksmith, there’s one about a mile from here.", "자물쇠장수한테 가요, 여기서 한 마일쯤 가면 하나 있으니까."], ["But avast!”", "아니, 잠깐!\""], ["putting her hand in her side-pocket, “here’s a key that’ll fit, I guess; let’s see.”", "그녀는 옆 주머니에 손을 넣으며 말했다. \"여기 맞을 만한 열쇠가 있어, 아마."], ["And with that, she turned it in the lock; but, alas!", "어디 보자.\" 그러면서 그녀는 자물쇠에 열쇠를 넣고 돌렸다."], ["Queequeg’s supplemental bolt remained unwithdrawn within.", "그러나 아아! 퀴퀘그가 안에서 따로 질러 둔 빗장은 풀리지 않은 채였다."]], [["“Have to burst it open,”", "\"부수어 열 수밖에 없겠소.\" 나는 이렇게 말하고, 제대로 도움닫기를 하려고 복도를 조금 뒤로 달려 내려갔다."], ["said I, and was running down the entry a little, for a good start, when the landlady caught at me, again vowing I should not break down her premises; but I tore from her, and with a sudden bodily rush dashed myself full against the mark.", "그러자 안주인이 다시 내 집을 부수지 못하게 하겠노라 다짐하며 나를 붙들었다. 그러나 나는 그녀에게서 몸을 떼어 내고, 온몸을 돌연 내던지듯 표적을 향해 정면으로 부딪쳐 갔다."]], [["With a prodigious noise the door flew open, and the knob slamming against the wall, sent the plaster to the ceiling; and there, good heavens!", "엄청난 소리와 함께 문이 활짝 열렸고, 문손잡이가 벽에 쾅 부딪치며 회반죽이 천장까지 튀어 올랐다. 그리고 거기에 — 맙소사!"], ["there sat Queequeg, altogether cool and self-collected; right in the middle of the room; squatting on his hams, and holding Yojo on top of his head.", "— 거기에 퀴퀘그가 앉아 있었다. 더없이 침착하고 평온한 모습으로, 방 한복판에 떡하니, 엉덩이를 깔고 쪼그려 앉아, 요조를 자기 머리 꼭대기에 얹은 채로."], ["He looked neither one way nor the other way, but sat like a carved image with scarce a sign of active life.", "그는 이쪽도 저쪽도 보지 않고, 살아 움직이는 기색이라곤 거의 없이, 마치 조각상처럼 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Queequeg,”", "\"퀴퀘그.\""], ["said I, going up to him, “Queequeg, what’s the matter with you?”", "나는 그에게 다가가며 말했다. \"퀴퀘그, 자네 대체 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["“He hain’t been a sittin’ so all day, has he?”", "\"이 양반 온종일 저러고 앉아 있었던 거 아니우?\""], ["said the landlady.", "안주인이 말했다."]], [["But all we said, not a word could we drag out of him; I almost felt like pushing him over, so as to change his position, for it was almost intolerable, it seemed so painfully and unnaturally constrained; especially, as in all probability he had been sitting so for upwards of eight or ten hours, going too without his regular meals.", "그러나 우리가 무슨 말을 하든, 그에게서는 한마디도 끌어낼 수 없었다. 나는 그의 자세를 바꿔 주려고 그를 밀어 넘어뜨리고 싶은 심정마저 들었다. 그 모습이 거의 견딜 수 없을 만큼, 너무도 고통스럽고 부자연스럽게 옥죄여 보였기 때문이다. 더구나 그는 정해진 끼니마저 거른 채 십중팔구 여덟 시간이고 열 시간이고 그렇게 앉아 있었을 터였다."]], [["“Mrs.", "\"허시 부인.\""], ["Hussey,”", "나는 말했다. \"어쨌든 이 사람은 살아 있소."], ["said I, “he’s alive at all events; so leave us, if you please, and I will see to this strange affair myself.”", "그러니 괜찮으시다면 우리를 좀 놔두시오. 이 기묘한 일은 내가 직접 처리하리다.\""]], [["Closing the door upon the landlady, I endeavored to prevail upon Queequeg to take a chair; but in vain.", "안주인을 내보내고 문을 닫은 뒤, 나는 퀴퀘그에게 의자에 앉으라고 설득해 보려 애썼다. 그러나 헛수고였다."], ["There he sat; and all he could do—for all my polite arts and blandishments—he would not move a peg, nor say a single word, nor even look at me, nor notice my presence in the slightest way.", "그는 그대로 앉아 있었다. 내가 아무리 정중한 재간과 살가운 말로 구슬려도 — 그는 손톱만큼도 움직이려 들지 않았고, 단 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 나를 쳐다보지도, 내 존재를 조금도 알은체하지도 않았다."]], [["I wonder, thought I, if this can possibly be a part of his Ramadan; do they fast on their hams that way in his native island. It must be so; yes, it’s part of his creed, I suppose; well, then, let him rest; he’ll get up sooner or later, no doubt. It can’t last for ever, thank God, and his Ramadan only comes once a year; and I don’t believe it’s very punctual then.", "이것도 어쩌면 그의 라마단의 일부일까, 하고 나는 생각했다. 그의 고향 섬에서는 저렇게 엉덩이를 깔고 단식을 하는 걸까. 그렇겠지. 그래, 이것도 그의 신조의 일부인가 보다. 그렇다면 그냥 내버려 두자. 그도 머잖아 일어날 게 틀림없다. 영원히 갈 수야 없으니 다행이고, 게다가 그의 라마단은 일 년에 한 번뿐이지 않은가. 더구나 그때도 그리 정확하게 지켜지는 것 같지는 않으니."]], [["I went down to supper. After sitting a long time listening to the long stories of some sailors who had just come from a plum-pudding voyage, as they called it (that is, a short whaling-voyage in a schooner or brig, confined to the north of the line, in the Atlantic Ocean only); after listening to these plum-puddingers till nearly eleven o’clock, I went up stairs to go to bed, feeling quite sure by this time Queequeg must certainly have brought his Ramadan to a termination. But no; there he was just where I had left him; he had not stirred an inch. I began to grow vexed with him; it seemed so downright senseless and insane to be sitting there all day and half the night on his hams in a cold room, holding a piece of wood on his head.", "나는 저녁을 먹으러 내려갔다. 자기들 말로 '건포도 푸딩 항해'라 부르는 항해에서 막 돌아온 몇몇 선원들의 긴 이야기를 한참 듣고 난 뒤였다(그것은 대서양에서만, 적도 이북에 한정해 스쿠너나 브리그선으로 다녀오는 짧은 고래잡이 항해를 일컫는 말이다). 이 건포도 푸딩 선원들의 이야기를 거의 열한 시까지 듣고 나서, 나는 잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갔다. 이쯤이면 퀴퀘그도 분명 자기 라마단을 끝마쳤으리라 굳게 믿으면서. 그러나 아니었다. 그는 내가 두고 간 바로 그 자리에 그대로 있었다. 한 치도 움직이지 않았다. 나는 슬슬 그가 짜증스러워지기 시작했다. 추운 방 안에서 머리에 나무 조각을 얹은 채, 온종일 그리고 한밤중까지 엉덩이를 깔고 앉아 있다니, 너무도 어처구니없고 제정신이 아닌 짓으로만 보였다."]], [["“For heaven’s sake, Queequeg, get up and shake yourself; get up and have some supper.", "\"제발 좀, 퀴퀘그, 일어나서 몸 좀 추슬러. 일어나서 저녁이라도 먹게."], ["You’ll starve; you’ll kill yourself, Queequeg.”", "굶어 죽겠어. 자네 스스로 목숨을 끊는 셈이야, 퀴퀘그.\""], ["But not a word did he reply.", "그러나 그는 한마디도 대답하지 않았다."]], [["Despairing of him, therefore, I determined to go to bed and to sleep; and no doubt, before a great while, he would follow me.", "그리하여 나는 그에게 단념하고, 잠자리에 들어 자기로 마음먹었다. 머잖아 그도 분명 나를 따라 잘 터였다."], ["But previous to turning in, I took my heavy bearskin jacket, and threw it over him, as it promised to be a very cold night; and he had nothing but his ordinary round jacket on.", "그러나 누워 들기 전에, 나는 내 묵직한 곰가죽 외투를 가져다 그에게 덮어 주었다. 밤이 몹시 추워질 듯한데, 그는 평소의 둥근 짧은 외투 하나만 걸치고 있었기 때문이다."], ["For some time, do all I would, I could not get into the faintest doze.", "한동안 나는 무슨 짓을 해도 도무지 어렴풋한 졸음조차 들지 못했다."], ["I had blown out the candle; and the mere thought of Queequeg—not four feet off—sitting there in that uneasy position, stark alone in the cold and dark; this made me really wretched.", "촛불은 꺼 둔 터였다. 그런데 퀴퀘그가 — 네 자도 채 떨어지지 않은 곳에서 — 그 불편한 자세로, 춥고 어두운 가운데 홀로 외따로 앉아 있다는 생각만으로도, 나는 정말이지 비참한 심정이 되었다."], ["Think of it; sleeping all night in the same room with a wide awake pagan on his hams in this dreary, unaccountable Ramadan!", "생각해 보라. 이 음울하고 알 수 없는 라마단을 치르느라 멀쩡히 깨어 엉덩이를 깔고 앉아 있는 이교도와 한방에서 밤새 자야 한다니!"]], [["But somehow I dropped off at last, and knew nothing more till break of day; when, looking over the bedside, there squatted Queequeg, as if he had been screwed down to the floor. But as soon as the first glimpse of sun entered the window, up he got, with stiff and grating joints, but with a cheerful look; limped towards me where I lay; pressed his forehead again against mine; and said his Ramadan was over.", "그러나 어찌어찌 나는 마침내 잠이 들었고, 동틀 녘까지 아무것도 알지 못했다. 그러다 침대맡 너머로 내려다보니, 퀴퀘그가 거기 쪼그려 앉아 있었다. 마치 바닥에 나사못으로 박혀 고정된 듯이. 그러나 햇살의 첫 자락이 창으로 들어오자마자 그는 벌떡 일어났다. 뻣뻣하게 굳어 삐걱대는 관절을 하고서도 명랑한 얼굴로, 내가 누운 쪽으로 절뚝거리며 다가와, 다시 자기 이마를 내 이마에 갖다 대고는, 자기 라마단이 끝났노라고 말했다."]], [["Now, as I before hinted, I have no objection to any person’s religion, be it what it may, so long as that person does not kill or insult any other person, because that other person don’t believe it also.", "자, 앞서 넌지시 말했듯이, 나는 그 어떤 사람의 종교든, 그것이 무엇이든 간에 이의가 없다. 다만 그 사람이 다른 사람이 그 종교를 함께 믿지 않는다는 이유로 그 사람을 죽이거나 모욕하지 않는 한에서다."], ["But when a man’s religion becomes really frantic; when it is a positive torment to him; and, in fine, makes this earth of ours an uncomfortable inn to lodge in; then I think it high time to take that individual aside and argue the point with him.", "그러나 한 사람의 종교가 정말로 광적으로 치닫고, 그것이 그에게 명백한 고문이 되며, 끝내는 우리의 이 땅을 머물기 거북한 여인숙으로 만들어 버린다면 — 그때야말로 그 개인을 한쪽으로 데려가 그 점을 두고 따져 볼 때라고 나는 생각한다."]], [["And just so I now did with Queequeg.", "그리고 바로 그렇게, 나는 이제 퀴퀘그에게 그리했다."], ["“Queequeg,”", "\"퀴퀘그.\""], ["said I, “get into bed now, and lie and listen to me.”", "나는 말했다. \"이제 침대로 들어가서 누워 내 말을 좀 들어 보게.\""], ["I then went on, beginning with the rise and progress of the primitive religions, and coming down to the various religions of the present time, during which time I labored to show Queequeg that all these Lents, Ramadans, and prolonged ham-squattings in cold, cheerless rooms were stark nonsense; bad for the health; useless for the soul; opposed, in short, to the obvious laws of Hygiene and common sense.", "그러고는 원시 종교의 발생과 발전에서 시작해 오늘날의 여러 종교에 이르기까지 이야기를 풀어 나갔다. 그러는 동안 나는 이 모든 사순절이며 라마단이며, 춥고 음산한 방에서 엉덩이를 깔고 오래도록 쪼그려 앉는 짓들이 새빨간 헛소리이며, 건강에 해롭고, 영혼에 무익하며, 한마디로 명백한 위생의 법칙과 상식에 어긋나는 것임을 퀴퀘그에게 보여 주려 애썼다."], ["I told him, too, that he being in other things such an extremely sensible and sagacious savage, it pained me, very badly pained me, to see him now so deplorably foolish about this ridiculous Ramadan of his.", "또한 나는 그에게, 다른 일에 있어서는 그토록 더없이 분별 있고 명민한 야만인인 그가, 지금 이 우스꽝스러운 라마단에 관해서는 이토록 한심하리만치 어리석은 꼴을 보이니 내 마음이 아프다고, 몹시도 아프다고 일러 주었다."], ["Besides, argued I, fasting makes the body cave in; hence the spirit caves in; and all thoughts born of a fast must necessarily be half-starved.", "게다가 단식은 몸을 움푹 꺼지게 하고, 따라서 정신도 움푹 꺼지게 하며, 단식에서 태어난 온갖 생각은 필연코 반쯤 굶주린 것일 수밖에 없다고 나는 따져 말했다."], ["This is the reason why most dyspeptic religionists cherish such melancholy notions about their hereafters.", "바로 이런 까닭에 소화불량에 걸린 신앙가들 대다수가 자기네 내세에 관해 그토록 우울한 관념을 품는 것이다."], ["In one word, Queequeg, said I, rather digressively; hell is an idea first born on an undigested apple-dumpling; and since then perpetuated through the hereditary dyspepsias nurtured by Ramadans.", "한마디로 말해, 퀴퀘그, 하고 나는 다소 옆길로 새며 말했다. 지옥이란 소화되지 않은 사과 경단에서 처음 생겨난 관념이며, 그 뒤로 라마단이 길러 낸 대물림 소화불량을 통해 줄곧 이어져 온 것이라고."]], [["I then asked Queequeg whether he himself was ever troubled with dyspepsia; expressing the idea very plainly, so that he could take it in.", "이어서 나는 퀴퀘그에게 그 자신이 소화불량으로 고생한 적이 있는지 물었다. 그가 알아들을 수 있도록 아주 쉽게 풀어서 표현했다."], ["He said no; only upon one memorable occasion.", "그는 없다고 대답했다. 다만 한 번 잊지 못할 일이 있었다고 했다."], ["It was after a great feast given by his father the king, on the gaining of a great battle wherein fifty of the enemy had been killed by about two o’clock in the afternoon, and all cooked and eaten that very evening.", "그것은 그의 아버지인 왕이 큰 전투에서 승리한 것을 기념해 베푼 성대한 잔치 뒤의 일이었는데, 그 전투에서는 오후 두 시쯤까지 적군 쉰 명이 죽임을 당했고, 바로 그날 저녁에 그 모두를 요리해 먹어 치웠다는 것이었다."]], [["“No more, Queequeg,”", "\"그만하게, 퀴퀘그.\""], ["said I, shuddering; “that will do;”", "나는 몸서리치며 말했다. \"그쯤이면 됐어.\""], ["for I knew the inferences without his further hinting them.", "그가 더 넌지시 일러 주지 않아도 나는 그 결말을 이미 알고 있었기 때문이다. 나는 바로 그 섬을 다녀온 어느 선원을 본 적이 있는데, 그가 내게 말하기를, 거기서는 큰 전투에서 이기면 죽은 자들을 모조리 승리자의 마당이나 정원에서 통째로 구워 내는 풍습이 있다고 했다."], ["I had seen a sailor who had visited that very island, and he told me that it was the custom, when a great battle had been gained there, to barbecue all the slain in the yard or garden of the victor; and then, one by one, they were placed in great wooden trenchers, and garnished round like a pilau, with breadfruit and cocoanuts; and with some parsley in their mouths, were sent round with the victor’s compliments to all his friends, just as though these presents were so many Christmas turkeys.", "그런 다음 하나씩 커다란 나무 쟁반에 담아, 필라우 요리처럼 빵나무 열매와 야자열매로 둘레를 곁들이고, 입에는 파슬리를 물려, 승리자의 인사와 함께 그의 모든 친구들에게 돌렸다는 것이다. 마치 그 선물들이 크리스마스 칠면조라도 되는 양 말이다."]], [["After all, I do not think that my remarks about religion made much impression upon Queequeg.", "결국, 종교에 관한 내 이야기가 퀴퀘그에게 그리 큰 인상을 남긴 것 같지는 않다. 왜냐하면, 첫째로 그는 그 중대한 주제에 관해서는 어쩐지 귀가 어두운 듯했기 때문이다 — 그것이 자기 자신의 관점에서 다뤄지지 않는 한에는."], ["Because, in the first place, he somehow seemed dull of hearing on that important subject, unless considered from his own point of view; and, in the second place, he did not more than one third understand me, couch my ideas simply as I would; and, finally, he no doubt thought he knew a good deal more about the true religion than I did.", "둘째로, 내가 아무리 내 생각을 단순하게 풀어 놓아도 그는 그것을 삼분의 일도 채 이해하지 못했다. 그리고 끝으로, 그는 의심할 바 없이 참된 종교에 관해서라면 나보다 자기가 훨씬 더 많이 안다고 여겼다."], ["He looked at me with a sort of condescending concern and compassion, as though he thought it a great pity that such a sensible young man should be so hopelessly lost to evangelical pagan piety.", "그는 일종의 너그러운 염려와 측은함이 어린 눈길로 나를 바라보았다. 마치 이토록 분별 있는 젊은이가 복음에 충실한 이교도의 경건함에서 이렇게나 가망 없이 벗어나 있다니 참으로 딱하다고 여기는 듯이."]], [["At last we rose and dressed; and Queequeg, taking a prodigiously hearty breakfast of chowders of all sorts, so that the landlady should not make much profit by reason of his Ramadan, we sallied out to board the Pequod, sauntering along, and picking our teeth with halibut bones.", "마침내 우리는 일어나 옷을 입었다. 그리고 퀴퀘그는 온갖 종류의 차우더로 엄청나게 푸짐한 아침 식사를 했는데 — 자기 라마단 탓에 안주인이 큰 이득을 보지 못하게 하려는 셈이었다 — 우리는 페쿼드호(Pequod)에 오르러 길을 나섰다. 어슬렁어슬렁 거닐면서, 넙치 뼈로 이를 쑤시며."]], [["# CHAPTER 18. His Mark.", "# CHAPTER 18. His Mark."]], [["As we were walking down the end of the wharf towards the ship, Queequeg carrying his harpoon, Captain Peleg in his gruff voice loudly hailed us from his wigwam, saying he had not suspected my friend was a cannibal, and furthermore announcing that he let no cannibals on board that craft, unless they previously produced their papers.", "우리가 부두 끝을 따라 배 쪽으로 걸어 내려갈 때, 퀴케그(Queequeg)는 작살을 메고 있었고, 펠레그(Peleg) 선장은 자기 천막 안에서 그 무뚝뚝한 목소리로 우리를 향해 큰 소리로 불러 댔다. 내 친구가 식인종일 줄은 미처 몰랐다며, 게다가 미리 서류를 내보이지 않는 한 그 어떤 식인종도 저 배에 태우지 않겠다고 선언하는 것이었다."]], [["“What do you mean by that, Captain Peleg?”", "\"그게 무슨 말씀입니까, 펠레그 선장님?\""], ["said I, now jumping on the bulwarks, and leaving my comrade standing on the wharf.", "나는 이렇게 말하며 뱃전 위로 펄쩍 뛰어올랐고, 내 동료는 부두에 그대로 세워 둔 채였다."]], [["“I mean,”", "\"내 말은,\""], ["he replied, “he must show his papers.”", "그가 대답했다. \"저자가 서류를 내보여야 한다는 거다.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇지,\""], ["said Captain Bildad in his hollow voice, sticking his head from behind Peleg’s, out of the wigwam.", "빌대드(Bildad) 선장이 그 공허한 목소리로 펠레그의 등 뒤, 천막 안에서 고개를 쑥 내밀며 말했다."], ["“He must show that he’s converted.", "\"저자가 개종했다는 걸 증명해야지."], ["Son of darkness,”", "어둠의 자식아,\""], ["he added, turning to Queequeg, “art thou at present in communion with any Christian church?”", "그는 퀴케그를 향해 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"그대는 지금 어느 기독교 교회와 교통(交通)하고 있는가?\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"왜요,\""], ["said I, “he’s a member of the first Congregational Church.”", "내가 말했다. \"이 친구는 제일회중교회의 신자입니다.\""], ["Here be it said, that many tattooed savages sailing in Nantucket ships at last come to be converted into the churches.", "여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 낸터킷(Nantucket) 배를 타는 문신 새긴 야만인들 가운데 많은 수가 끝내 교회로 개종해 들어오게 된다."]], [["“First Congregational Church,”", "\"제일회중교회,\""], ["cried Bildad, “what!", "빌대드가 외쳤다."], ["that worships in Deacon Deuteronomy Coleman’s meeting-house?”", "\"뭐라고! 듀터로노미 콜먼 집사의 예배당에서 예배 보는 그 교회 말이냐?\""], ["and so saying, taking out his spectacles, he rubbed them with his great yellow bandana handkerchief, and putting them on very carefully, came out of the wigwam, and leaning stiffly over the bulwarks, took a good long look at Queequeg.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 안경을 꺼내 그 커다란 노란색 반다나 손수건으로 닦은 다음, 아주 조심스레 쓰고는 천막 밖으로 나와, 뱃전 위로 뻣뻣하게 몸을 기울인 채 퀴케그를 한참 동안 찬찬히 뜯어보았다."]], [["“How long hath he been a member?”", "\"신자가 된 지는 얼마나 되었느냐?\""], ["he then said, turning to me; “not very long, I rather guess, young man.”", "이윽고 그가 내게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"그리 오래되지는 않았겠지, 내 짐작에는, 젊은이.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니지,\""], ["said Peleg, “and he hasn’t been baptized right either, or it would have washed some of that devil’s blue off his face.”", "펠레그가 말했다. \"그리고 세례도 제대로 받지 못했어, 아니었으면 그 얼굴에서 악마의 푸른 물이 얼마쯤은 씻겨 나갔을 텐데 말이야.\""]], [["“Do tell, now,”", "\"어디 말해 보게나,\""], ["cried Bildad, “is this Philistine a regular member of Deacon Deuteronomy’s meeting?", "빌대드가 외쳤다. \"이 필리스티아 사람이 듀터로노미 집사의 예배당에 정식으로 다니는 신자란 말인가?"], ["I never saw him going there, and I pass it every Lord’s day.”", "나는 저 사람이 거기 가는 걸 한 번도 본 적이 없네, 나는 주일마다 그 앞을 지나가는데도 말이야.\""]], [["“I don’t know anything about Deacon Deuteronomy or his meeting,”", "\"저는 듀터로노미 집사나 그 예배당에 대해서는 아무것도 모릅니다.\""], ["said I; “all I know is, that Queequeg here is a born member of the First Congregational Church.", "내가 말했다. \"제가 아는 거라곤 여기 이 퀴케그가 제일회중교회의 타고난 신자라는 것뿐입니다."], ["He is a deacon himself, Queequeg is.”", "퀴케그는 그 자신이 집사예요.\""]], [["“Young man,”", "\"젊은이,\""], ["said Bildad sternly, “thou art skylarking with me—explain thyself, thou young Hittite.", "빌대드가 엄하게 말했다. \"그대는 지금 나를 놀리고 있구나 — 똑똑히 밝혀 보아라, 이 젊은 히타이트 사람아."], ["What church dost thee mean?", "그대가 말하는 교회가 대체 어떤 교회냐?"], ["answer me.”", "내게 대답하라.\""]], [["Finding myself thus hard pushed, I replied. “I mean, sir, the same ancient Catholic Church to which you and I, and Captain Peleg there, and Queequeg here, and all of us, and every mother’s son and soul of us belong; the great and everlasting First Congregation of this whole worshipping world; we all belong to that; only some of us cherish some queer crotchets no ways touching the grand belief; in that we all join hands.”", "이렇게 궁지에 몰린 나는 이렇게 대답했다. \"제 말은요, 선장님, 선장님과 저, 그리고 저기 펠레그 선장님, 또 여기 퀴케그, 우리 모두와, 어머니 배에서 난 모든 아들과 영혼이 다 함께 속해 있는 바로 그 오래된 보편 교회 말입니다. 예배 드리는 이 온 세상의 위대하고 영원한 제일회중 말입니다. 우리는 모두 거기에 속해 있어요. 다만 우리 중 더러는 그 위대한 믿음과는 조금도 상관없는 별난 고집을 품고 있을 뿐, 바로 그 믿음 안에서 우리는 모두 손을 맞잡는 거죠.\""]], [["“Splice, thou mean’st splice hands,”", "\"맞이음이지, 그대가 말하려는 건 손을 맞이음하는 거야.\""], ["cried Peleg, drawing nearer.", "펠레그가 더 가까이 다가오며 외쳤다."], ["“Young man, you’d better ship for a missionary, instead of a fore-mast hand; I never heard a better sermon.", "\"젊은이, 자네는 앞돛대 갑판원보다 선교사로 배를 타는 편이 낫겠어. 그렇게 훌륭한 설교는 들어 본 적이 없거든."], ["Deacon Deuteronomy—why Father Mapple himself couldn’t beat it, and he’s reckoned something.", "듀터로노미 집사라니 — 아니, 매플(Mapple) 신부님조차도 그걸 당해 내지 못할걸세, 그분도 대단한 분으로 쳐주는데 말이야."], ["Come aboard, come aboard; never mind about the papers.", "자, 배에 오르게, 어서 오르게나. 서류 따위는 신경 쓸 것 없어."], ["I say, tell Quohog there—what’s that you call him?", "그러니까, 저기 저 쿼호그에게 일러 주게나 — 저 친구를 뭐라고 부른다고 했지?"], ["tell Quohog to step along.", "쿼호그에게 어서 오라고 일러 주게."], ["By the great anchor, what a harpoon he’s got there!", "저 거대한 닻에 두고 맹세컨대, 정말이지 굉장한 작살을 들고 있군!"], ["looks like good stuff that; and he handles it about right.", "좋은 물건 같은데, 다루는 솜씨도 제법이야."], ["I say, Quohog, or whatever your name is, did you ever stand in the head of a whale-boat?", "이봐, 쿼호그, 아니 자네 이름이 뭐든 간에, 자네 고래잡이 보트의 뱃머리에 서 본 적이 있나?"], ["did you ever strike a fish?”", "물고기를 작살로 찔러 본 적은 있고?\""]], [["Without saying a word, Queequeg, in his wild sort of way, jumped upon the bulwarks, from thence into the bows of one of the whale-boats hanging to the side; and then bracing his left knee, and poising his harpoon, cried out in some such way as this:—", "퀴케그는 한마디도 하지 않은 채, 그 특유의 거친 동작으로 뱃전 위로 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다가, 거기서 다시 뱃전에 매달린 고래잡이 보트 가운데 하나의 뱃머리로 뛰어내렸다. 그러고는 왼쪽 무릎을 굽혀 버틴 채 작살을 겨누고서 이런 식으로 외쳤다."]], [["“Cap’ain, you see him small drop tar on water dere?", "\"선장님, 저기 물 위에 타르 작은 방울 떨어진 거 보이지요?"], ["You see him?", "보여요?"], ["well, spose him one whale eye, well, den!”", "좋아요, 저게 고래 눈깔이라 칩시다, 자, 그럼!\""], ["and taking sharp aim at it, he darted the iron right over old Bildad’s broad brim, clean across the ship’s decks, and struck the glistening tar spot out of sight.", "그러고는 거기에 날카롭게 겨눠, 늙은 빌대드의 챙 넓은 모자 바로 위로, 배의 갑판을 가로질러 작살을 던졌고, 반짝이던 타르 자국을 흔적도 없이 날려 버렸다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Queequeg, quietly hauling in the line, “spos-ee him whale-e eye; why, dad whale dead.”", "퀴케그가 줄을 조용히 끌어당기며 말했다. \"저게 고래 눈깔이라 치면, 그럼, 그 고래 죽은 거지요.\""]], [["“Quick, Bildad,”", "\"빨리, 빌대드,\""], ["said Peleg, his partner, who, aghast at the close vicinity of the flying harpoon, had retreated towards the cabin gangway.", "그의 동업자 펠레그가 말했다. 날아간 작살이 너무 바로 곁을 스쳐 지나간 통에 기겁한 그는 선실 통로 쪽으로 물러나 있었다."], ["“Quick, I say, you Bildad, and get the ship’s papers.", "\"어서, 이보게 빌대드, 빨리 배의 서류를 가져오게."], ["We must have Hedgehog there, I mean Quohog, in one of our boats.", "저기 저 헤지호그를, 아니 쿼호그를 우리 보트 가운데 하나에 꼭 태워야겠어."], ["Look ye, Quohog, we’ll give ye the ninetieth lay, and that’s more than ever was given a harpooneer yet out of Nantucket.”", "이보게 쿼호그, 자네에게 90분의 1 몫을 주겠네. 낸터킷에서 어떤 작살잡이에게도 일찍이 주어진 적 없는 후한 몫이지.\""]], [["So down we went into the cabin, and to my great joy Queequeg was soon enrolled among the same ship’s company to which I myself belonged.", "그래서 우리는 선실로 내려갔고, 더없이 기쁘게도 퀴케그는 곧 나 자신이 속한 바로 그 배의 선원 명부에 올랐다."]], [["When all preliminaries were over and Peleg had got everything ready for signing, he turned to me and said, “I guess, Quohog there don’t know how to write, does he?", "모든 사전 절차가 끝나고 펠레그가 서명에 필요한 모든 준비를 마치자, 그는 내게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"내 생각에 저기 저 쿼호그는 글씨를 쓸 줄 모르겠지, 안 그런가?"], ["I say, Quohog, blast ye!", "이보게 쿼호그, 빌어먹을!"], ["dost thou sign thy name or make thy mark?”", "자네 이름을 직접 서명하겠나, 아니면 표시를 그리겠나?\""]], [["But at this question, Queequeg, who had twice or thrice before taken part in similar ceremonies, looked no ways abashed; but taking the offered pen, copied upon the paper, in the proper place, an exact counterpart of a queer round figure which was tattooed upon his arm; so that through Captain Peleg’s obstinate mistake touching his appellative, it stood something like this:—", "그러나 이 질문에, 전에도 두세 차례 비슷한 의식에 참여해 본 적이 있는 퀴케그는 조금도 당황하는 기색 없이, 건네받은 펜을 들고 종이의 적절한 자리에다, 자기 팔에 새겨진 기묘한 둥근 무늬와 똑같은 모양을 정확히 베껴 그렸다. 그리하여 그의 이름에 관한 펠레그 선장의 고집스러운 착오 덕분에, 그것은 대략 이런 모양이 되었다."]], [["Quohog.", "쿼호그."], ["his X mark.", "그의 X 표시."]], [["Meanwhile Captain Bildad sat earnestly and steadfastly eyeing Queequeg, and at last rising solemnly and fumbling in the huge pockets of his broad-skirted drab coat, took out a bundle of tracts, and selecting one entitled “The Latter Day Coming; or No Time to Lose,” placed it in Queequeg’s hands, and then grasping them and the book with both his, looked earnestly into his eyes, and said, “Son of darkness, I must do my duty by thee; I am part owner of this ship, and feel concerned for the souls of all its crew; if thou still clingest to thy Pagan ways, which I sadly fear, I beseech thee, remain not for aye a Belial bondsman. Spurn the idol Bell, and the hideous dragon; turn from the wrath to come; mind thine eye, I say; oh! goodness gracious! steer clear of the fiery pit!”", "그동안 빌대드 선장은 퀴케그를 진지하고도 흔들림 없이 응시하고 앉아 있다가, 마침내 엄숙하게 일어나 자신의 폭 넓은 칙칙한 갈색 외투의 커다란 주머니를 더듬어, 소책자 한 묶음을 꺼냈다. 그러고는 그중 \"다가오는 마지막 날, 곧 지체할 시간이 없도다\"라는 제목이 붙은 것을 골라 퀴케그의 손에 쥐여 주고는, 두 손으로 그의 손과 그 책을 한꺼번에 움켜잡은 채, 그의 두 눈을 진지하게 들여다보며 말했다. \"어둠의 자식아, 나는 그대에게 내 의무를 다해야겠다. 나는 이 배의 공동 선주이며, 그 모든 선원의 영혼을 염려하는 마음이 있다. 만일 그대가 여전히 그 이교의 방식에 매달려 있다면 — 슬프게도 나는 그것이 두렵구나 — 부디 간청하노니, 영영 벨리알의 종으로 남지 말라. 우상 벨을, 그리고 그 끔찍한 용을 내쳐 버려라. 다가올 진노에서 돌아서라. 정신을 똑바로 차려라, 내 말 잘 들어라. 오! 자비로우신 하느님이시여! 저 불타는 구렁텅이를 피하라!\""]], [["Something of the salt sea yet lingered in old Bildad’s language, heterogeneously mixed with Scriptural and domestic phrases.", "늙은 빌대드의 말투에는 짠 바닷내가 아직도 얼마간 배어 있어, 성경 구절과 일상의 표현이 잡다하게 뒤섞여 있었다."]], [["“Avast there, avast there, Bildad, avast now spoiling our harpooneer,”", "\"멈춰, 거기 멈추라고, 빌대드, 우리 작살잡이를 망쳐 놓는 짓은 이제 그만두게.\""], ["cried Peleg.", "펠레그가 외쳤다."], ["“Pious harpooneers never make good voyagers—it takes the shark out of ’em; no harpooneer is worth a straw who aint pretty sharkish.", "\"경건한 작살잡이는 결코 좋은 항해자가 못 돼 — 경건함은 그자들에게서 상어 기질을 빼내 버리거든. 어지간히 상어 같지 않은 작살잡이는 지푸라기만큼의 값어치도 없는 거야."], ["There was young Nat Swaine, once the bravest boat-header out of all Nantucket and the Vineyard; he joined the meeting, and never came to good.", "옛날에 냇 스웨인이라는 젊은이가 있었지, 한때는 낸터킷과 빈야드 통틀어 가장 용감한 보트 지휘자였어. 그런데 그자가 예배당에 다니기 시작하더니, 영 신통치 못하게 되어 버렸지."], ["He got so frightened about his plaguy soul, that he shrinked and sheered away from whales, for fear of after-claps, in case he got stove and went to Davy Jones.”", "자기의 그 빌어먹을 영혼이 어찌나 무서웠던지, 보트가 부서져 데이비 존스의 바닷속으로 떨어지기라도 하면 뒤탈이 있을까 봐 겁이 나서, 고래만 보면 움츠러들고 슬슬 피해 다녔다네.\""]], [["“Peleg!", "\"펠레그!"], ["Peleg!”", "펠레그!\""], ["said Bildad, lifting his eyes and hands, “thou thyself, as I myself, hast seen many a perilous time; thou knowest, Peleg, what it is to have the fear of death; how, then, can’st thou prate in this ungodly guise.", "빌대드가 두 눈과 두 손을 치켜들며 말했다. \"그대 자신도, 나 자신과 마찬가지로, 숱한 위험한 순간을 겪어 보지 않았는가."], ["Thou beliest thine own heart, Peleg.", "펠레그, 그대도 죽음의 공포를 안다는 것이 어떤 것인지 알 터인데, 어찌 그토록 불경한 모습으로 함부로 지껄일 수 있단 말인가."], ["Tell me, when this same Pequod here had her three masts overboard in that typhoon on Japan, that same voyage when thou went mate with Captain Ahab, did’st thou not think of Death and the Judgment then?”", "그대는 자기 마음을 속이고 있어, 펠레그. 어디 말해 보게, 일본 앞바다의 그 태풍 속에서 여기 이 피쿼드(Pequod)호가 돛대 세 개를 모조리 바다로 날려 버렸을 때, 그러니까 그대가 에이해브(Ahab) 선장 밑에서 일등항해사로 갔던 바로 그 항해 때, 그대는 죽음과 심판을 떠올리지 않았던가?\""]], [["“Hear him, hear him now,”", "\"저 사람 말 좀 들어 보게, 지금 저 사람 말 좀 들어 보라고.\""], ["cried Peleg, marching across the cabin, and thrusting his hands far down into his pockets,—“hear him, all of ye.", "펠레그가 선실을 가로질러 성큼성큼 걸으며 두 손을 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣은 채 외쳤다. \"저 사람 말 좀 들어 보게나, 다들."], ["Think of that!", "생각해 보라고!"], ["When every moment we thought the ship would sink!", "순간순간마다 배가 가라앉으리라 여겼던 그때를!"], ["Death and the Judgment then?", "그때 죽음과 심판?"], ["What?", "뭐라고?"], ["With all three masts making such an everlasting thundering against the side; and every sea breaking over us, fore and aft.", "돛대 세 개가 모조리 뱃전을 향해 그토록 끝없이 우레처럼 부딪쳐 대고, 바닷물은 앞에서 뒤에서 우리 머리 위를 덮쳐 오는데."], ["Think of Death and the Judgment then?", "그런 때에 죽음과 심판을 생각해? 천만에!"], ["No! no time to think about Death then.", "그때는 죽음 따위를 생각할 겨를이 없었어."], ["Life was what Captain Ahab and I was thinking of; and how to save all hands—how to rig jury-masts—how to get into the nearest port; that was what I was thinking of.”", "에이해브 선장과 내가 생각한 건 오직 삶이었지. 어떻게 모두를 살릴까 — 어떻게 임시 돛대를 세울까 — 어떻게 가장 가까운 항구로 들어갈까, 내가 생각한 건 바로 그런 것들이었어.\""]], [["Bildad said no more, but buttoning up his coat, stalked on deck, where we followed him.", "빌대드는 더는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 외투 단추를 채운 채 갑판으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔고, 우리도 그를 따라 나갔다."], ["There he stood, very quietly overlooking some sailmakers who were mending a top-sail in the waist.", "거기서 그는 배 중간께에서 윗돛을 깁고 있는 돛 만드는 일꾼 몇몇을 아주 조용히 내려다보며 서 있었다."], ["Now and then he stooped to pick up a patch, or save an end of tarred twine, which otherwise might have been wasted.", "이따금 그는 몸을 굽혀 천 조각을 주워 들거나, 그러지 않았으면 버려졌을 타르 묻은 노끈 끄트머리를 챙겨 두곤 했다."]], [["“Shipmates, have ye shipped in that ship?”", "「선원 양반들, 저 배에 승선 계약을 했소?」"]], [["Queequeg and I had just left the Pequod, and were sauntering away from the water, for the moment each occupied with his own thoughts, when the above words were put to us by a stranger, who, pausing before us, levelled his massive forefinger at the vessel in question.", "퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 나는 막 피쿼드호(Pequod)를 떠나 물가에서 멀어지며 한가로이 걷고 있던 참이었고, 저마다 제 생각에 잠겨 있었는데, 그때 낯선 사내 하나가 우리 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 서더니 그 육중한 집게손가락을 문제의 그 배 쪽으로 겨누며 위와 같은 말을 던졌다."], ["He was but shabbily apparelled in faded jacket and patched trowsers; a rag of a black handkerchief investing his neck.", "그는 빛바랜 웃옷에 기운 바지를 걸친, 초라하기 짝이 없는 행색이었다. 목에는 검은 손수건 누더기 한 조각을 둘러매고 있었다."], ["A confluent small-pox had in all directions flowed over his face, and left it like the complicated ribbed bed of a torrent, when the rushing waters have been dried up.", "천연두 자국이 한데 엉겨 그의 얼굴을 사방으로 뒤덮어, 거센 물살이 말라붙은 뒤에 남은 격류의 골 진 바닥처럼 복잡하게 패어 있었다."]], [["“Have ye shipped in her?”", "「저 배에 계약을 했냐 말이오?」"], ["he repeated.", "그가 거듭 물었다."]], [["“You mean the ship Pequod, I suppose,”", "「피쿼드호를 말하는 거겠지요.」"], ["said I, trying to gain a little more time for an uninterrupted look at him.", "나는 그를 방해받지 않고 좀 더 찬찬히 뜯어볼 시간을 벌려고 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“Aye, the Pequod—that ship there,”", "「그렇소, 피쿼드호 말이오—저기 저 배.」"], ["he said, drawing back his whole arm, and then rapidly shoving it straight out from him, with the fixed bayonet of his pointed finger darted full at the object.", "그는 팔 전체를 뒤로 당겼다가 다시 재빨리 앞으로 쭉 내뻗으며, 뾰족한 손가락이라는 꽂힌 총검을 그 대상에 정통으로 겨누었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "「그렇소,」"], ["said I, “we have just signed the articles.”", "내가 말했다. 「우린 막 계약서에 서명한 참이오.」"]], [["“Anything down there about your souls?”", "「거기 당신들 영혼에 관한 조항도 있던가?」"]], [["“About what?”", "「뭐에 관한 거라고요?」"]], [["“Oh, perhaps you hav’n’t got any,”", "「오, 어쩌면 당신들한텐 영혼이란 게 없는지도 모르지.」 그가 잽싸게 말했다."], ["he said quickly.", "「뭐, 그래도 상관없소."], ["“No matter though, I know many chaps that hav’n’t got any,—good luck to ’em; and they are all the better off for it.", "나는 영혼 없는 친구들을 숱하게 알고 있으니까—그자들에게 복이 있기를. 그들은 그 덕에 도리어 형편이 더 낫지."], ["A soul’s a sort of a fifth wheel to a wagon.”", "영혼이란 짐수레에 달린 다섯 번째 바퀴 같은 거니까.」"]], [["“What are you jabbering about, shipmate?”", "「대체 무슨 헛소리를 지껄이는 거요, 선원 양반?」"], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“He’s got enough, though, to make up for all deficiencies of that sort in other chaps,”", "「하지만 그 사람은 다른 친구들의 그런 모자람을 죄다 채우고도 남을 만큼 충분히 가졌지.」"], ["abruptly said the stranger, placing a nervous emphasis upon the word he.", "낯선 사내가 불쑥 말하며, 그 사람이라는 말에 신경질적으로 힘을 주었다."]], [["“Queequeg,” said I, “let’s go; this fellow has broken loose from somewhere; he’s talking about something and somebody we don’t know.”", "「퀴퀘그,」 내가 말했다. 「가자. 이 작자는 어디선가 풀려나온 모양이야. 우리가 알지도 못하는 무언가와 누군가에 대해 떠들어 대고 있어.」"]], [["“Stop!”", "「잠깐!」"], ["cried the stranger.", "낯선 사내가 외쳤다."], ["“Ye said true—ye hav’n’t seen Old Thunder yet, have ye?”", "「당신 말이 옳소—아직 늙은 천둥(Old Thunder)을 본 적이 없지, 안 그렇소?」"]], [["“Who’s Old Thunder?”", "「늙은 천둥이 누구요?」"], ["said I, again riveted with the insane earnestness of his manner.", "나는 그 미친 듯한 진지함에 또다시 못 박힌 듯 멈춰 서며 물었다."]], [["“Captain Ahab.”", "「에이해브(Ahab) 선장 말이오.」"]], [["“What!", "「뭐라고!"], ["the captain of our ship, the Pequod?”", "우리 배 피쿼드호의 선장 말이오?」"]], [["“Aye, among some of us old sailor chaps, he goes by that name.", "「그렇소, 우리 같은 늙은 뱃사람 몇몇은 그분을 그 이름으로 부른다오."], ["Ye hav’n’t seen him yet, have ye?”", "아직 그분을 본 적이 없지, 안 그렇소?」"]], [["“No, we hav’n’t.", "「그렇소, 아직 못 봤소."], ["He’s sick they say, but is getting better, and will be all right again before long.”", "듣자 하니 편찮으시다는데, 차도가 있어서 머지않아 다시 멀쩡해지실 거라더군요.」"]], [["“All right again before long!”", "「머지않아 다시 멀쩡해진다!」"], ["laughed the stranger, with a solemnly derisive sort of laugh.", "낯선 사내가 엄숙하면서도 비웃는 듯한 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 「잘 들으시오."], ["“Look ye; when Captain Ahab is all right, then this left arm of mine will be all right; not before.”", "에이해브 선장이 멀쩡해질 때면, 그때는 내 이 왼팔도 멀쩡해질 거요. 그전엔 어림없지.」"]], [["“What do you know about him?”", "「그 사람에 대해 뭘 안다는 거요?」"]], [["“What did they tell you about him?", "「그 사람에 대해 그자들이 당신한테 뭐라고 일러주던가?"], ["Say that!”", "그걸 말해 보시오!」"]], [["“They didn’t tell much of anything about him; only I’ve heard that he’s a good whale-hunter, and a good captain to his crew.”", "「그 사람에 대해 별 얘기는 없었소. 다만 고래잡이 솜씨가 뛰어나고, 선원들에게 좋은 선장이라는 말은 들었지.」"]], [["“That’s true, that’s true—yes, both true enough.", "「그건 사실이지, 사실이고말고—그렇소, 둘 다 틀림없는 사실이오."], ["But you must jump when he gives an order.", "하지만 그분이 명령을 내리면 당신은 펄쩍 뛰어 움직여야 할 거요."], ["Step and growl; growl and go—that’s the word with Captain Ahab.", "걸으면서 으르렁대고, 으르렁대면서 가라—그게 에이해브 선장과 함께하는 법칙이오."], ["But nothing about that thing that happened to him off Cape Horn, long ago, when he lay like dead for three days and nights; nothing about that deadly skrimmage with the Spaniard afore the altar in Santa?—heard nothing about that, eh?", "하지만 오래전 케이프 혼(Cape Horn) 앞바다에서 그분에게 일어난 그 일, 사흘 밤낮을 죽은 듯이 누워 있던 그 일에 대해선 아무 말도 없었지. 산타(Santa)에서 제단 앞에서 스페인 사람과 벌인 그 목숨을 건 난투극에 대해서도—그것에 대해선 아무것도 못 들었지, 응?"], ["Nothing about the silver calabash he spat into?", "그분이 침을 뱉었던 은 표주박에 대해서도 아무 말 없었고?"], ["And nothing about his losing his leg last voyage, according to the prophecy.", "그리고 예언대로 지난 항해에서 다리를 잃은 일에 대해서도 아무것도."], ["Didn’t ye hear a word about them matters and something more, eh?", "그런 일들에 대해, 또 그 밖의 일들에 대해 단 한마디도 못 들었지, 응?"], ["No, I don’t think ye did; how could ye?", "아니, 못 들었을 거요. 어떻게 들을 수 있었겠소?"], ["Who knows it?", "그걸 누가 알겠소?"], ["Not all Nantucket, I guess.", "낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들 전부가 아는 것도 아닐 테니."], ["But hows’ever, mayhap, ye’ve heard tell about the leg, and how he lost it; aye, ye have heard of that, I dare say.", "하지만 어쩌면 그 다리에 대해선, 그분이 그걸 어떻게 잃었는지에 대해선 들어 봤을지도 모르겠군. 그렇소, 그건 들었을 거요, 틀림없이."], ["Oh yes, that every one knows a’most—I mean they know he’s only one leg; and that a parmacetti took the other off.”", "오, 그렇지, 그건 거의 누구나 다 아는 일이오—내 말은, 그분이 다리가 하나뿐이고, 향유고래 한 마리가 다른 한쪽을 떼어 갔다는 걸 안다는 말이오.」"]], [["“My friend,”", "「이보시오,」"], ["said I, “what all this gibberish of yours is about, I don’t know, and I don’t much care; for it seems to me that you must be a little damaged in the head.", "내가 말했다. 「당신이 지껄이는 이 모든 헛소리가 대체 무엇에 관한 것인지 나는 모르겠고, 별로 알고 싶지도 않소."], ["But if you are speaking of Captain Ahab, of that ship there, the Pequod, then let me tell you, that I know all about the loss of his leg.”", "내가 보기엔 당신 머리가 좀 어떻게 된 것 같으니 말이오. 하지만 저기 저 배, 피쿼드호의 에이해브 선장 얘기를 하는 거라면, 분명히 말해 두는데, 나는 그분이 다리를 잃은 일에 대해 죄다 알고 있소.」"]], [["“All about it, eh—sure you do?—all?”", "「죄다 안다고, 응—정말 그렇소?—죄다?」"]], [["“Pretty sure.”", "「거의 틀림없소.」"]], [["With finger pointed and eye levelled at the Pequod, the beggar-like stranger stood a moment, as if in a troubled reverie; then starting a little, turned and said:—“Ye’ve shipped, have ye?", "거지꼴의 낯선 사내는 손가락을 겨눈 채 피쿼드호에 눈길을 고정하고서, 괴로운 몽상에 잠긴 듯 한순간 가만히 서 있었다. 그러더니 흠칫 놀란 듯 몸을 돌려 말했다.—「계약을 했단 말이지, 응?"], ["Names down on the papers?", "이름을 서류에 적었고? 그래, 그래, 서명한 건 서명한 거고, 될 일은 될 테고, 또 어쩌면, 결국 안 될지도 모르지."], ["Well, well, what’s signed, is signed; and what’s to be, will be; and then again, perhaps it won’t be, after all.", "어쨌든 그건 이미 죄다 정해지고 짜여 있는 일이오. 그리고 어떤 선원이든 누군가는 그분과 함께 가야 할 테지."], ["Anyhow, it’s all fixed and arranged a’ready; and some sailors or other must go with him, I suppose; as well these as any other men, God pity ’em!", "이 사람들이든 다른 누구든 마찬가지일 테고, 하느님(God)이 그들을 가엾이 여기시기를! 잘 가시오, 선원 양반들, 잘 가시오."], ["Morning to ye, shipmates, morning; the ineffable heavens bless ye; I’m sorry I stopped ye.”", "형언할 수 없는 하늘이 당신들에게 복을 내리시기를. 붙잡아 세워서 미안하오.」"]], [["“Look here, friend,” said I, “if you have anything important to tell us, out with it; but if you are only trying to bamboozle us, you are mistaken in your game; that’s all I have to say.”", "「이보시오, 친구,」 내가 말했다. 「우리한테 들려줄 중요한 얘기가 있거든 어서 털어놓으시오. 하지만 그저 우리를 어리둥절하게 만들려는 수작이라면, 당신은 헛다리를 짚은 거요. 내가 할 말은 그게 다요.」"]], [["“And it’s said very well, and I like to hear a chap talk up that way; you are just the man for him—the likes of ye.", "「그것참 잘 말했소, 그리고 사내가 그렇게 당차게 받아치는 걸 듣는 게 나는 좋소. 당신이야말로 그분에게 꼭 맞는 사람이오—당신 같은 사람 말이오."], ["Morning to ye, shipmates, morning!", "잘 가시오, 선원 양반들, 잘 가시오!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["when ye get there, tell ’em I’ve concluded not to make one of ’em.”", "거기 가거든, 그분들에게 전해 주시오. 나는 그들 중 하나가 되지 않기로 결심했다고.」"]], [["“Ah, my dear fellow, you can’t fool us that way—you can’t fool us.", "「아, 이보시오, 그런 식으로는 우리를 속일 수 없소—우릴 속일 순 없지."], ["It is the easiest thing in the world for a man to look as if he had a great secret in him.”", "자기 속에 무슨 대단한 비밀이라도 품은 듯이 굴기는 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일이니까.」"]], [["“Morning to ye, shipmates, morning.”", "「잘 가시오, 선원 양반들, 잘 가시오.」"]], [["“Morning it is,” said I.", "「잘 가라니, 그러지.」"], ["“Come along, Queequeg, let’s leave this crazy man.", "내가 말했다. 「가자, 퀴퀘그, 이 미친 작자는 내버려 두고."], ["But stop, tell me your name, will you?” “Elijah.”", "그런데 잠깐, 당신 이름이나 좀 말해 주겠소?」"]], [["Elijah! thought I, and we walked away, both commenting, after each other’s fashion, upon this ragged old sailor; and agreed that he was nothing but a humbug, trying to be a bugbear.", "「일라이저(Elijah)요.」"], ["But we had not gone perhaps above a hundred yards, when chancing to turn a corner, and looking back as I did so, who should be seen but Elijah following us, though at a distance.", "일라이저라! 나는 속으로 생각했고, 우리는 저마다 제 식대로 이 누더기 차림의 늙은 뱃사람에 대해 한마디씩 주고받으며 걸어갔다."], ["Somehow, the sight of him struck me so, that I said nothing to Queequeg of his being behind, but passed on with my comrade, anxious to see whether the stranger would turn the same corner that we did.", "그리고 그가 사람을 놀래키려 드는 허풍선이에 지나지 않는다는 데 의견을 모았다. 그러나 백 야드도 채 가지 못했을 무렵, 우연히 모퉁이를 돌면서 돌아보니, 놀랍게도 거기엔 일라이저가, 비록 멀찍이 떨어져서지만 우리를 뒤따라오고 있는 것이 아닌가."], ["He did; and then it seemed to me that he was dogging us, but with what intent I could not for the life of me imagine.", "어쩐지 그 모습이 나를 어찌나 충격에 빠뜨렸던지, 나는 그가 뒤따라온다는 것을 퀴퀘그에게는 한마디도 하지 않고, 그저 동무와 함께 걸음을 옮기며, 저 낯선 사내가 우리가 돈 바로 그 모퉁이를 돌아오는지 어떤지 지켜보려고 애를 태웠다. 과연 그는 그렇게 했다."], ["This circumstance, coupled with his ambiguous, half-hinting, half-revealing, shrouded sort of talk, now begat in me all kinds of vague wonderments and half-apprehensions, and all connected with the Pequod; and Captain Ahab; and the leg he had lost; and the Cape Horn fit; and the silver calabash; and what Captain Peleg had said of him, when I left the ship the day previous; and the prediction of the squaw Tistig; and the voyage we had bound ourselves to sail; and a hundred other shadowy things.", "그러자 내게는 그가 우리를 미행하고 있는 것처럼 여겨졌으나, 도대체 무슨 속셈인지는 죽었다 깨어나도 짐작할 수 없었다. 이 정황은, 그 모호하고, 반쯤 넌지시 비추고 반쯤 드러내는, 안개에 싸인 듯한 말투와 한데 어우러져, 이제 내 안에 온갖 막연한 의혹과 어렴풋한 불안을 자아냈고, 그 모든 것이 피쿼드호와, 에이해브 선장과, 그가 잃은 다리와, 케이프 혼에서의 발작과, 은 표주박과, 전날 내가 배를 떠날 때 펠레그(Peleg) 선장이 그에 대해 했던 말과, 인디언 여인 티스티그(Tistig)의 예언과, 우리가 항해하기로 스스로 묶어 둔 그 항해와, 그 밖에 그림자처럼 어른거리는 수백 가지 일들과 얽혀 있었다."]], [["I was resolved to satisfy myself whether this ragged Elijah was really dogging us or not, and with that intent crossed the way with Queequeg, and on that side of it retraced our steps.", "나는 이 누더기 차림의 일라이저가 정말로 우리를 미행하고 있는지 아닌지 내 눈으로 확인하기로 마음먹고, 그 속셈으로 퀴퀘그와 함께 길을 건너, 그쪽 길을 따라 왔던 걸음을 되짚어 갔다."], ["But Elijah passed on, without seeming to notice us.", "그러나 일라이저는 우리를 알아채는 기색도 없이 그냥 지나쳐 갔다."], ["This relieved me; and once more, and finally as it seemed to me, I pronounced him in my heart, a humbug.", "그것이 나를 안심시켰다. 그리하여 나는 다시 한번, 그리고 내게는 이번이 마지막인 듯이, 그를 마음속으로 허풍선이라 단정 지었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 20. All Astir.", "# CHAPTER 20. All Astir."]], [["A day or two passed, and there was great activity aboard the Pequod.", "하루 이틀이 지나는 동안 피쿼드(Pequod)호 선상은 분주하기 그지없었다."], ["Not only were the old sails being mended, but new sails were coming on board, and bolts of canvas, and coils of rigging; in short, everything betokened that the ship’s preparations were hurrying to a close.", "낡은 돛을 손보는 일은 물론이고, 새 돛이 배에 실려 올라왔으며, 두루마리 캔버스 천과 둘둘 감은 삭구 뭉치도 들어왔다. 한마디로 모든 것이 배의 출항 준비가 막바지로 치닫고 있음을 알려 주고 있었다."], ["Captain Peleg seldom or never went ashore, but sat in his wigwam keeping a sharp look-out upon the hands: Bildad did all the purchasing and providing at the stores; and the men employed in the hold and on the rigging were working till long after night-fall.", "펠레그(Peleg) 선장은 좀처럼, 아니 거의 뭍에 오르는 법이 없이 자기 천막 안에 앉아 일꾼들을 매섭게 감독했고, 빌대드(Bildad)는 상점들을 돌며 물품을 사들이고 조달하는 일을 도맡았다. 그리고 선창과 삭구에서 일하는 사람들은 밤이 깊도록 한참을 더 일했다."]], [["On the day following Queequeg’s signing the articles, word was given at all the inns where the ship’s company were stopping, that their chests must be on board before night, for there was no telling how soon the vessel might be sailing.", "퀴케그(Queequeg)가 계약서에 서명한 다음 날, 선원들이 묵고 있던 모든 여관에 통지가 내려졌다. 배가 얼마나 빨리 떠나게 될지 알 수 없으니, 밤이 되기 전에 각자의 궤짝을 배에 실어 두라는 것이었다."], ["So Queequeg and I got down our traps, resolving, however, to sleep ashore till the last.", "그래서 퀴케그와 나는 짐을 챙겨 내리면서도, 마지막 순간까지는 뭍에서 자기로 마음먹었다."], ["But it seems they always give very long notice in these cases, and the ship did not sail for several days.", "그러나 이런 경우에는 으레 미리 길게 통지를 하는 모양이어서, 배는 며칠이 지나도록 출항하지 않았다."], ["But no wonder; there was a good deal to be done, and there is no telling how many things to be thought of, before the Pequod was fully equipped.", "하기야 놀랄 일도 아니었다. 해야 할 일이 산더미였고, 피쿼드호를 온전히 갖추기까지 챙겨야 할 것이 얼마나 많은지 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었으니까."]], [["Every one knows what a multitude of things—beds, sauce-pans, knives and forks, shovels and tongs, napkins, nut-crackers, and what not, are indispensable to the business of housekeeping.", "살림살이라는 것이 침대며 냄비며 칼과 포크, 부삽과 부집게, 냅킨, 호두까기 따위에 이르기까지 얼마나 많은 물건을 없어서는 안 될 필수품으로 요구하는지는 누구나 알고 있다."], ["Just so with whaling, which necessitates a three-years’ housekeeping upon the wide ocean, far from all grocers, costermongers, doctors, bakers, and bankers.", "고래잡이도 꼭 그러한데, 이는 모든 식료품상과 행상인, 의사와 제빵사와 은행가로부터 멀리 떨어진 망망대해 위에서 삼 년에 걸친 살림을 꾸려야 하는 일이기 때문이다."], ["And though this also holds true of merchant vessels, yet not by any means to the same extent as with whalemen.", "물론 이것은 상선에도 해당되는 이야기지만, 그래도 고래잡이배만큼은 결코 아니다."], ["For besides the great length of the whaling voyage, the numerous articles peculiar to the prosecution of the fishery, and the impossibility of replacing them at the remote harbors usually frequented, it must be remembered, that of all ships, whaling vessels are the most exposed to accidents of all kinds, and especially to the destruction and loss of the very things upon which the success of the voyage most depends.", "고래잡이 항해의 어마어마한 길이는 차치하고라도, 어업을 수행하는 데 필요한 수많은 특수 물품들과, 흔히 드나드는 외딴 항구에서는 그것들을 다시 마련할 수 없다는 점, 그리고 무엇보다도 모든 배 가운데 고래잡이배야말로 온갖 종류의 사고에, 특히 항해의 성패가 가장 크게 달려 있는 바로 그 물건들의 파손과 유실에 가장 많이 노출되어 있다는 사실을 잊어서는 안 된다."], ["Hence, the spare boats, spare spars, and spare lines and harpoons, and spare everythings, almost, but a spare Captain and duplicate ship.", "그런 까닭에 예비 보트, 예비 원목, 예비 밧줄과 작살, 그리고 거의 모든 것의 예비품이 갖춰진다. 다만 예비 선장과 똑같은 배 한 척만은 예외였다."]], [["At the period of our arrival at the Island, the heaviest storage of the Pequod had been almost completed; comprising her beef, bread, water, fuel, and iron hoops and staves.", "우리가 이 섬에 닿았을 무렵, 피쿼드호에 실을 가장 묵직한 화물은 이미 거의 다 실린 상태였다. 쇠고기와 빵, 물과 연료, 그리고 쇠 테와 통널 따위가 그것이었다."], ["But, as before hinted, for some time there was a continual fetching and carrying on board of divers odds and ends of things, both large and small.", "그러나 앞서 넌지시 말했듯이, 한동안은 크고 작은 온갖 자질구레한 물건들을 끊임없이 배로 가져오고 또 실어 나르는 일이 계속되었다."]], [["Chief among those who did this fetching and carrying was Captain Bildad’s sister, a lean old lady of a most determined and indefatigable spirit, but withal very kindhearted, who seemed resolved that, if she could help it, nothing should be found wanting in the Pequod, after once fairly getting to sea.", "이렇게 물건을 가져오고 실어 나르는 사람들 가운데 으뜸가는 이는 빌대드 선장의 누이였다. 더없이 굳센 의지와 지칠 줄 모르는 정신을 지닌, 그러면서도 마음씨가 무척 고운 깡마른 노부인이었는데, 일단 배가 바다로 제대로 나가고 나면, 자기 힘으로 막을 수만 있다면 피쿼드호에 부족한 것이라곤 단 하나도 없게 하리라 단단히 작정한 듯했다."], ["At one time she would come on board with a jar of pickles for the steward’s pantry; another time with a bunch of quills for the chief mate’s desk, where he kept his log; a third time with a roll of flannel for the small of some one’s rheumatic back.", "한 번은 사환의 식료품 저장실에 둘 피클 단지를 들고 배에 올랐고, 또 한 번은 일등항해사가 항해일지를 적어 두는 책상에 둘 깃펜 한 묶음을 들고 왔으며, 세 번째에는 누군가의 류머티즘 앓는 허리께에 댈 플란넬 천 한 두루마리를 들고 나타났다."], ["Never did any woman better deserve her name, which was Charity—Aunt Charity, as everybody called her.", "그 어떤 여인도 자신의 이름을 이보다 더 마땅히 누린 적은 없었으니, 그 이름은 채러티(Charity), 곧 자비였다."], ["And like a sister of charity did this charitable Aunt Charity bustle about hither and thither, ready to turn her hand and heart to anything that promised to yield safety, comfort, and consolation to all on board a ship in which her beloved brother Bildad was concerned, and in which she herself owned a score or two of well-saved dollars.", "모두가 그녀를 채러티 아주머니라 불렀다. 그리고 자비의 수녀처럼, 이 자비로운 채러티 아주머니는 이리저리 부산을 떨며, 사랑하는 동생 빌대드가 관여하고 또 자기 자신도 알뜰히 모은 몇 십 달러의 몫을 지닌 그 배 위의 모든 이에게 안전과 안락과 위안을 가져다줄 만한 일이라면 무엇에든 기꺼이 손과 마음을 내어 줄 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["But it was startling to see this excellent hearted Quakeress coming on board, as she did the last day, with a long oil-ladle in one hand, and a still longer whaling lance in the other.", "* 채러티(Charity): 자비, 자선을 뜻하는 영어 이름. 그러나 마음씨 고운 이 퀘이커 교도 부인이, 마지막 날에 그러했듯, 한 손에는 기다란 기름 국자를, 다른 손에는 그보다 더 기다란 고래잡이 창을 들고 배에 오르는 광경은 보기에 자못 놀라웠다."], ["Nor was Bildad himself nor Captain Peleg at all backward.", "빌대드 자신도, 펠레그 선장도 결코 뒤지지 않았다."], ["As for Bildad, he carried about with him a long list of the articles needed, and at every fresh arrival, down went his mark opposite that article upon the paper.", "빌대드로 말하자면, 필요한 물품을 적은 기다란 목록을 늘 지니고 다니다가, 새로운 물건이 도착할 때마다 종이 위의 그 항목 옆에 표시를 해 나갔다."], ["Every once in a while Peleg came hobbling out of his whalebone den, roaring at the men down the hatchways, roaring up to the riggers at the mast-head, and then concluded by roaring back into his wigwam.", "그리고 펠레그는 이따금 고래뼈로 꾸민 자기 굴에서 절름거리며 나와, 승강구 아래의 일꾼들을 향해 고함을 치고, 돛대 꼭대기의 삭구공들을 향해 고함을 질러 대다가, 끝내는 다시 천막 안으로 고함을 치며 돌아가곤 했다."]], [["During these days of preparation, Queequeg and I often visited the craft, and as often I asked about Captain Ahab, and how he was, and when he was going to come on board his ship.", "이 준비의 나날 동안, 퀴케그와 나는 자주 그 배를 찾았고, 그때마다 나는 에이해브(Ahab) 선장에 관해, 그의 상태가 어떤지, 그리고 언제 배에 오를 것인지를 물었다."], ["To these questions they would answer, that he was getting better and better, and was expected aboard every day; meantime, the two captains, Peleg and Bildad, could attend to everything necessary to fit the vessel for the voyage.", "그러면 그들은 그가 점점 나아지고 있으며 날마다 배에 오를 것으로 기대하고 있다고, 그동안에는 두 선장 펠레그와 빌대드가 항해를 위해 배를 채비하는 데 필요한 모든 일을 처리할 수 있다고 대답하곤 했다."], ["If I had been downright honest with myself, I would have seen very plainly in my heart that I did but half fancy being committed this way to so long a voyage, without once laying my eyes on the man who was to be the absolute dictator of it, so soon as the ship sailed out upon the open sea.", "만일 내가 나 자신에게 철저히 솔직했더라면, 배가 망망대해로 나서는 순간부터 이 항해의 절대적 지배자가 될 그 사람을 단 한 번도 직접 보지 못한 채 이토록 긴 항해에 몸을 맡기는 일이 도무지 마음에 차지 않는다는 사실을 내 마음속에서 똑똑히 보았으리라."], ["But when a man suspects any wrong, it sometimes happens that if he be already involved in the matter, he insensibly strives to cover up his suspicions even from himself.", "그러나 사람이 무언가 잘못된 것을 의심할 때, 이미 그 일에 발을 들여놓은 처지라면, 자기 자신에게마저 그 의심을 덮어 감추려 부지중에 애쓰는 일이 더러 일어난다. 나도 꼭 그러했다."], ["And much this way it was with me. I said nothing, and tried to think nothing.", "나는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 아무 생각도 하지 않으려 애썼다."]], [["At last it was given out that some time next day the ship would certainly sail.", "마침내 다음 날 어느 때엔가 배가 틀림없이 출항하리라는 통지가 내려졌다."], ["So next morning, Queequeg and I took a very early start.", "그래서 이튿날 아침, 퀴케그와 나는 아주 이른 시각에 길을 나섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER 21. Going Aboard.", "# CHAPTER 21. Going Aboard."]], [["It was nearly six o’clock, but only grey imperfect misty dawn, when we drew nigh the wharf.", "째 무렵, 우리가 부두에 다가섰을 때는 아직 여섯 시가 채 안 되었고, 잿빛으로 흐릿하게 안개 낀 미명만이 깔려 있었다."]], [["“There are some sailors running ahead there, if I see right,”", "\"저기 앞쪽에 선원 몇 명이 달려가고 있어, 내가 제대로 봤다면 말이야.\" 내가 퀴케그(Queequeg)에게 말했다."], ["said I to Queequeg, “it can’t be shadows; she’s off by sunrise, I guess; come on!”", "\"그림자일 리는 없고, 배는 해 뜰 무렵에 떠나는 모양이야. 자, 어서 가자!\""]], [["“Avast!”", "\"멈추게!\""], ["cried a voice, whose owner at the same time coming close behind us, laid a hand upon both our shoulders, and then insinuating himself between us, stood stooping forward a little, in the uncertain twilight, strangely peering from Queequeg to me.", "어떤 목소리가 외쳤다. 그 목소리의 주인이 동시에 우리 등 뒤로 바짝 다가와 두 손을 우리 두 사람의 어깨에 얹고는, 우리 사이를 비집고 들어와 흐릿한 어스름 속에서 몸을 약간 앞으로 구부린 채 서서, 퀴케그에게서 나에게로 기이하게 눈길을 던졌다."], ["It was Elijah.", "일라이저(Elijah)였다."]], [["“Going aboard?”", "\"배에 타려는 건가?\""]], [["“Hands off, will you,”", "\"손 좀 떼지 그래.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Lookee here,”", "\"이봐.\""], ["said Queequeg, shaking himself, “go ’way!”", "퀴케그가 몸을 흔들며 말했다. \"저리 가!\""]], [["“Ain’t going aboard, then?”", "\"그럼 배에 안 타는 건가?\""]], [["“Yes, we are,”", "\"아니, 탈 거요.\""], ["said I, “but what business is that of yours?", "내가 말했다. \"그런데 그게 당신과 무슨 상관이오?"], ["Do you know, Mr. Elijah, that I consider you a little impertinent?”", "일라이저 씨, 내가 당신을 좀 무례한 사람이라고 여기고 있다는 걸 아시오?\""]], [["“No, no, no; I wasn’t aware of that,”", "\"아니, 아니, 아니지. 그건 내 미처 몰랐군.\""], ["said Elijah, slowly and wonderingly looking from me to Queequeg, with the most unaccountable glances.", "일라이저가 나에게서 퀴케그에게로 천천히, 의아한 듯, 도무지 알 수 없는 눈빛으로 시선을 옮기며 말했다."]], [["“Elijah,”", "\"일라이저.\""], ["said I, “you will oblige my friend and me by withdrawing.", "내가 말했다. \"내 친구와 나를 위해 물러나 주면 고맙겠소."], ["We are going to the Indian and Pacific Oceans, and would prefer not to be detained.”", "우리는 인도양과 태평양으로 가는 길이고, 붙잡혀 있고 싶지 않소.\""]], [["“Ye be, be ye?", "\"그렇다, 그런 거지?"], ["Coming back afore breakfast?”", "아침 먹기 전에 돌아올 텐가?\""]], [["“He’s cracked, Queequeg,”", "\"이 사람 머리가 좀 돌았어, 퀴케그.\""], ["said I, “come on.”", "내가 말했다. \"어서 가자.\""]], [["“Holloa!”", "\"여보게!\""], ["cried stationary Elijah, hailing us when we had removed a few paces.", "그 자리에 가만히 선 일라이저가 우리가 몇 걸음 멀어졌을 때 우리를 불렀다."]], [["“Never mind him,”", "\"신경 쓰지 마.\""], ["said I, “Queequeg, come on.”", "내가 말했다. \"퀴케그, 어서 가자.\""]], [["But he stole up to us again, and suddenly clapping his hand on my shoulder, said—“Did ye see anything looking like men going towards that ship a while ago?”", "그러나 그는 다시 슬그머니 우리에게 다가와, 느닷없이 내 어깨에 손을 짚으며 말했다. \"조금 전에 저 배 쪽으로 사람처럼 보이는 무언가가 가는 걸 보지 않았나?\""]], [["Struck by this plain matter-of-fact question, I answered, saying, “Yes, I thought I did see four or five men; but it was too dim to be sure.”", "이 평범하고 사실적인 물음에 흠칫한 나는 대답했다. \"그래요, 네다섯 사람을 본 것 같소. 하지만 너무 어두워 확실하지는 않았소.\""]], [["“Very dim, very dim,”", "\"아주 어둡지, 아주 어두워.\""], ["said Elijah.", "일라이저가 말했다."], ["“Morning to ye.”", "\"잘들 가시게.\""]], [["Once more we quitted him; but once more he came softly after us; and touching my shoulder again, said, “See if you can find ’em now, will ye?", "우리는 또 한 번 그를 떠났다. 그러나 그는 또다시 슬며시 우리 뒤를 따라와, 다시 내 어깨를 건드리며 말했다."], ["“Find who?”", "\"이제 그자들을 찾을 수 있는지 한번 보게, 그래 줄 텐가?\""]], [["“Morning to ye!", "\"누구를 찾으란 말이오?\""], ["morning to ye!”", "\"잘들 가시게!"], ["he rejoined, again moving off.", "잘들 가시게!\" 그는 다시 떠나가며 대꾸했다."], ["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I was going to warn ye against—but never mind, never mind—it’s all one, all in the family too;—sharp frost this morning, ain’t it?", "자네들에게 경고하려던 참이었는데 — 하지만 됐네, 됐어 — 다 마찬가지지, 다 한 식구이기도 하고. 오늘 아침 서리가 매섭군, 그렇지 않나?"], ["Good-bye to ye.", "잘들 가시게."], ["Shan’t see ye again very soon, I guess; unless it’s before the Grand Jury.”", "곧 다시 보지는 못할 걸세, 내 짐작에는. 대배심 앞에서가 아니라면 말이야.\""], ["And with these cracked words he finally departed, leaving me, for the moment, in no small wonderment at his frantic impudence.", "이 정신 나간 말들을 남기고 그는 마침내 떠나갔고, 나는 그 순간 그의 광적인 뻔뻔함에 적잖이 어리둥절했다."]], [["At last, stepping on board the Pequod, we found everything in profound quiet, not a soul moving.", "마침내 페쿼드(Pequod)호에 올라 보니 모든 것이 깊은 정적에 잠겨 있었고, 움직이는 사람 하나 없었다."], ["The cabin entrance was locked within; the hatches were all on, and lumbered with coils of rigging.", "선실 입구는 안에서 잠겨 있었고, 승강구는 모두 닫힌 채 둘둘 감긴 삭구 더미로 막혀 있었다."], ["Going forward to the forecastle, we found the slide of the scuttle open.", "앞쪽 선수루로 가 보니 갑판 승강구의 미닫이가 열려 있었다."], ["Seeing a light, we went down, and found only an old rigger there, wrapped in a tattered pea-jacket.", "불빛이 보여 우리는 아래로 내려갔는데, 거기에는 너덜너덜한 두꺼운 모직 외투를 걸친 늙은 삭구장이 한 사람만 있었다."], ["He was thrown at whole length upon two chests, his face downwards and inclosed in his folded arms.", "그는 궤짝 두 개 위에 온몸을 길게 뻗고, 얼굴은 아래로 향한 채 포갠 두 팔에 파묻혀 있었다."], ["The profoundest slumber slept upon him.", "더없이 깊은 잠이 그를 덮고 있었다."]], [["“Those sailors we saw, Queequeg, where can they have gone to?”", "\"우리가 본 그 선원들 말이야, 퀴케그, 어디로 갔을까?\""], ["said I, looking dubiously at the sleeper.", "잠든 사람을 미심쩍게 바라보며 내가 말했다."], ["But it seemed that, when on the wharf, Queequeg had not at all noticed what I now alluded to; hence I would have thought myself to have been optically deceived in that matter, were it not for Elijah’s otherwise inexplicable question.", "그러나 부두에서 퀴케그는 내가 지금 말한 것을 전혀 알아차리지 못한 듯했다. 그래서 일라이저의 달리 설명할 길 없는 물음만 아니었다면, 나는 그 일에 관해 내 눈이 착각을 일으켰다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["But I beat the thing down; and again marking the sleeper, jocularly hinted to Queequeg that perhaps we had best sit up with the body; telling him to establish himself accordingly.", "하지만 나는 그 생각을 눌러 버렸다. 그리고 다시 잠든 사람을 보며, 어쩌면 우리가 시신을 지키며 밤을 새우는 게 좋겠다고 퀴케그에게 농담조로 넌지시 말하고는, 그에 맞게 자리를 잡으라고 일렀다."], ["He put his hand upon the sleeper’s rear, as though feeling if it was soft enough; and then, without more ado, sat quietly down there.", "그는 그것이 충분히 부드러운지 확인하듯 잠든 사람의 엉덩이에 손을 얹어 보더니, 더 따질 것도 없이 그 위에 조용히 주저앉았다."]], [["“Gracious!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Queequeg, don’t sit there,”", "퀴케그, 거기 앉지 마.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["perry dood seat,”", "아주 좋은 자리야.\" 퀴케그가 말했다."], ["said Queequeg, “my country way; won’t hurt him face.”", "\"우리 나라 방식이지. 이 사람 얼굴 안 다쳐.\""]], [["“Face!”", "\"얼굴이라고!\""], ["said I, “call that his face?", "내가 말했다. \"그걸 얼굴이라 부르는 거야?"], ["very benevolent countenance then; but how hard he breathes, he’s heaving himself; get off, Queequeg, you are heavy, it’s grinding the face of the poor.", "그렇다면 참 자비로운 낯짝이군. 그런데 숨을 어찌나 가쁘게 쉬는지, 몸을 들썩이고 있잖아."], ["Get off, Queequeg!", "내려와, 퀴케그, 너 무거워."], ["Look, he’ll twitch you off soon.", "이러다 가난한 자의 얼굴을 짓이기겠다. 내려와, 퀴케그!"], ["I wonder he don’t wake.”", "봐, 곧 너를 떨쳐 낼 거야. 어째 안 깨는지 신기하군.\""]], [["Queequeg removed himself to just beyond the head of the sleeper, and lighted his tomahawk pipe.", "퀴케그는 잠든 사람의 머리맡 바로 너머로 자리를 옮기고, 도끼 파이프에 불을 붙였다."], ["I sat at the feet.", "나는 발치에 앉았다."], ["We kept the pipe passing over the sleeper, from one to the other.", "우리는 잠든 사람을 사이에 두고 파이프를 서로 주거니 받거니 했다."], ["Meanwhile, upon questioning him in his broken fashion, Queequeg gave me to understand that, in his land, owing to the absence of settees and sofas of all sorts, the king, chiefs, and great people generally, were in the custom of fattening some of the lower orders for ottomans; and to furnish a house comfortably in that respect, you had only to buy up eight or ten lazy fellows, and lay them round in the piers and alcoves.", "그러는 동안, 그의 더듬거리는 말투로 물어보니, 퀴케그가 내게 일러 준 바로는, 그의 고향에서는 온갖 종류의 긴 의자와 소파가 없는 까닭에, 왕과 추장과 대체로 지체 높은 사람들이 낮은 신분의 몇몇을 살찌워 발걸상으로 쓰는 관습이 있다고 했다. 그래서 그 점에서 집을 안락하게 꾸미려면, 그저 게으른 녀석 여덟이나 열쯤 사들여 마루와 벽감 둘레에 죽 늘어놓기만 하면 된다는 것이었다."], ["Besides, it was very convenient on an excursion; much better than those garden-chairs which are convertible into walking-sticks; upon occasion, a chief calling his attendant, and desiring him to make a settee of himself under a spreading tree, perhaps in some damp marshy place.", "게다가 나들이를 갈 때도 무척 편리해서, 지팡이로 바꿀 수 있는 그 정원용 의자보다 훨씬 낫다고 했다. 이를테면 추장이 시종을 불러, 가지를 넓게 드리운 나무 아래, 어쩌면 축축하고 질척한 곳에서, 제 몸을 긴 의자로 만들라고 명할 수 있으니 말이다."]], [["While narrating these things, every time Queequeg received the tomahawk from me, he flourished the hatchet-side of it over the sleeper’s head.", "이런 이야기를 늘어놓는 동안, 퀴케그는 내게서 도끼 파이프를 건네받을 때마다 잠든 사람의 머리 위로 도끼날 쪽을 휘둘러 댔다."]], [["“What’s that for, Queequeg?”", "\"그건 왜 그러는 거야, 퀴케그?\""]], [["“Perry easy, kill-e; oh!", "\"아주 쉽게, 죽여. 오!"], ["perry easy!”", "아주 쉽게!\""]], [["He was going on with some wild reminiscences about his tomahawk-pipe, which, it seemed, had in its two uses both brained his foes and soothed his soul, when we were directly attracted to the sleeping rigger.", "그는 자신의 도끼 파이프에 얽힌 거친 추억담을 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 보아하니 그것은 두 가지 쓰임새로 적의 골통을 깨부수기도 하고 제 영혼을 달래기도 했던 모양이다. 그때 우리의 눈길은 곧장 잠든 삭구장에게로 쏠렸다."], ["The strong vapor now completely filling the contracted hole, it began to tell upon him.", "이제 짙은 연기가 그 비좁은 구멍을 완전히 채우자, 그것이 그에게 영향을 미치기 시작했다."], ["He breathed with a sort of muffledness; then seemed troubled in the nose; then revolved over once or twice; then sat up and rubbed his eyes.", "그는 무언가 막힌 듯이 숨을 쉬더니, 코가 거북한 모양이었고, 그러더니 한두 번 몸을 뒤척이고는, 일어나 앉아 눈을 비볐다."]], [["“Holloa!”", "\"여보게!\""], ["he breathed at last, “who be ye smokers?”", "그가 마침내 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"담배 피우는 자네들은 누군가?\""]], [["“Shipped men,”", "\"승선 계약을 한 사람들이오.\""], ["answered I, “when does she sail?”", "내가 대답했다. \"이 배는 언제 떠나오?\""]], [["“Aye, aye, ye are going in her, be ye?", "\"그래, 그래, 자네들도 이 배를 타고 가는 거로군, 그렇지?"], ["She sails to-day.", "배는 오늘 떠나네."], ["The Captain came aboard last night.”", "선장이 어젯밤에 배에 올랐지.\""]], [["“What Captain?—Ahab?”", "\"무슨 선장 말이오?"], ["“Who but him indeed?”", "에이해브(Ahab) 말이오?\""]], [["I was going to ask him some further questions concerning Ahab, when we heard a noise on deck.", "\"그분 말고 또 누가 있겠나?\" 나는 에이해브에 관해 그에게 몇 가지를 더 물어보려던 참에, 갑판에서 무슨 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Holloa!", "\"여보게!"], ["Starbuck’s astir,”", "스타벅(Starbuck)이 일어났군.\""], ["said the rigger.", "삭구장이 말했다."], ["“He’s a lively chief mate, that; good man, and a pious; but all alive now, I must turn to.”", "\"저 양반은 활기찬 일등 항해사야. 좋은 사람이고, 신앙심도 깊지."], ["And so saying he went on deck, and we followed.", "그런데 이제 다들 움직이는군, 나도 일하러 가야겠어.\" 그렇게 말하며 그는 갑판으로 올라갔고, 우리도 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["It was now clear sunrise.", "이제 완연히 해가 떠올랐다."], ["Soon the crew came on board in twos and threes; the riggers bestirred themselves; the mates were actively engaged; and several of the shore people were busy in bringing various last things on board.", "곧 선원들이 두셋씩 짝지어 배에 올랐고, 삭구장들은 분주히 움직였으며, 항해사들도 활발히 일에 매달렸다. 또 뭍에서 온 사람 몇몇은 갖가지 마지막 물건들을 배에 실어 나르느라 바빴다."], ["Meanwhile Captain Ahab remained invisibly enshrined within his cabin.", "그러는 동안 에이해브 선장은 자신의 선실 안에 모습을 감춘 채 신전에 모셔진 듯 머물러 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 22. Merry Christmas.", "# CHAPTER 22. Merry Christmas."]], [["At length, towards noon, upon the final dismissal of the ship’s riggers, and after the Pequod had been hauled out from the wharf, and after the ever-thoughtful Charity had come off in a whale-boat, with her last gift—a night-cap for Stubb, the second mate, her brother-in-law, and a spare Bible for the steward—after all this, the two Captains, Peleg and Bildad, issued from the cabin, and turning to the chief mate, Peleg said:", "마침내 정오 무렵, 배의 의장공(艤裝工)들이 모두 해산되고, 페쿼드(Pequod)호가 부두에서 끌려 나온 뒤, 또한 늘 사려 깊은 채리티가 고래잡이 보트를 타고 마지막 선물을 들고 찾아온 뒤에 — 그 선물이란 이등 항해사이자 그녀의 시동생인 스터브(Stubb)에게 줄 수면모자 하나와, 사환에게 줄 여분의 성경책 한 권이었다 — 이 모든 일이 끝난 다음에, 두 선장 펠레그(Peleg)와 빌다드(Bildad)가 선실에서 나와, 일등 항해사를 돌아보며 펠레그가 말했다."]], [["“Now, Mr. Starbuck, are you sure everything is right?", "\"자, 스타벅(Starbuck) 군, 모든 게 다 제대로 되어 있는 게 확실한가?"], ["Captain Ahab is all ready—just spoke to him—nothing more to be got from shore, eh?", "에이해브(Ahab) 선장도 준비를 다 마쳤네 — 방금 이야기를 나눴어 — 뭍에서 더 가져올 것은 없겠지, 응?"], ["Well, call all hands, then.", "좋아, 그럼 전원을 불러 모으게."], ["Muster ’em aft here—blast ’em!”", "죄다 여기 고물 쪽으로 집합시켜 — 빌어먹을 놈들!\""]], [["“No need of profane words, however great the hurry, Peleg,”", "\"아무리 급하기로서니 불경한 말을 입에 담을 필요는 없네, 펠레그.\""], ["said Bildad, “but away with thee, friend Starbuck, and do our bidding.”", "빌다드가 말했다. \"어서 가게나, 친구 스타벅, 우리가 시킨 대로 하게.\""]], [["How now!", "이게 무슨 일인가!"], ["Here upon the very point of starting for the voyage, Captain Peleg and Captain Bildad were going it with a high hand on the quarter-deck, just as if they were to be joint-commanders at sea, as well as to all appearances in port.", "막 항해를 떠나려는 바로 이 순간에, 펠레그 선장과 빌다드 선장은 마치 항구에서뿐만 아니라 바다에서도 공동 지휘관 노릇을 할 사람들인 양, 뒷갑판에서 거리낌 없이 위세를 부리고 있었다."], ["And, as for Captain Ahab, no sign of him was yet to be seen; only, they said he was in the cabin.", "그리고 에이해브 선장으로 말하자면, 아직 그의 모습은 어디에도 보이지 않았다. 다만 선실에 있다는 말뿐이었다."], ["But then, the idea was, that his presence was by no means necessary in getting the ship under weigh, and steering her well out to sea.", "그러나 생각해 보면, 배를 출항시키고 능숙하게 먼바다로 몰아 나가는 데 그가 굳이 모습을 드러낼 필요는 전혀 없었다."], ["Indeed, as that was not at all his proper business, but the pilot’s; and as he was not yet completely recovered—so they said—therefore, Captain Ahab stayed below.", "사실 그것은 그의 본래 임무가 아니라 도선사의 일이었고, 또 그가 아직 완전히 회복되지 않은 탓에 — 사람들은 그렇게 말했다 — 에이해브 선장은 아래에 머물러 있었다."], ["And all this seemed natural enough; especially as in the merchant service many captains never show themselves on deck for a considerable time after heaving up the anchor, but remain over the cabin table, having a farewell merry-making with their shore friends, before they quit the ship for good with the pilot.", "이 모든 것이 충분히 자연스러워 보였다. 더욱이 상선에서는 닻을 올린 뒤에도 상당 시간 동안 갑판에 모습을 드러내지 않고, 도선사와 함께 배를 영영 떠나기 전에 선실 탁자에 둘러앉아 뭍의 친구들과 작별의 흥겨운 자리를 갖는 선장이 적지 않았으니 말이다."]], [["But there was not much chance to think over the matter, for Captain Peleg was now all alive.", "그러나 이 일을 곰곰이 따져 볼 겨를은 없었으니, 펠레그 선장이 이제 펄펄 살아 움직이고 있었기 때문이다."], ["He seemed to do most of the talking and commanding, and not Bildad.", "말하고 지휘하는 일은 대부분 빌다드가 아니라 그의 몫인 듯했다."]], [["“Aft here, ye sons of bachelors,”", "\"고물로 모이라니까, 이 홀아비 자식들아.\""], ["he cried, as the sailors lingered at the main-mast.", "선원들이 큰 돛대 곁에서 우물쭈물하자 그가 소리쳤다."], ["“Mr. Starbuck, drive ’em aft.”", "\"스타벅 군, 저놈들을 고물로 몰아붙이게.\""]], [["“Strike the tent there!”—was the next order.", "\"거기 천막을 거두어라!\" 다음 명령이 떨어졌다."], ["As I hinted before, this whalebone marquee was never pitched except in port; and on board the Pequod, for thirty years, the order to strike the tent was well known to be the next thing to heaving up the anchor.", "앞에서 넌지시 일렀듯이, 이 고래수염 천막은 항구에 있을 때 말고는 결코 치지 않았다. 그리고 페쿼드호에서는 삼십 년 동안 천막을 거두라는 명령이 곧 닻을 올릴 차례임을 알리는 신호로 널리 통했다."]], [["“Man the capstan!", "\"양묘기(揚錨機)를 잡아라! 천둥벼락이 칠 노릇이다!"], ["Blood and thunder!—jump!”—was the next command, and the crew sprang for the handspikes.", "— 뛰어!\" 다음 명령이 떨어지자, 선원들은 양묘봉을 향해 달려들었다."]], [["Now in getting under weigh, the station generally occupied by the pilot is the forward part of the ship.", "자, 배를 출항시킬 때 도선사가 통상 자리 잡는 곳은 배의 앞부분이다. 그리고 바로 여기서 빌다드의 모습이 보였다."], ["And here Bildad, who, with Peleg, be it known, in addition to his other officers, was one of the licensed pilots of the port—he being suspected to have got himself made a pilot in order to save the Nantucket pilot-fee to all the ships he was concerned in, for he never piloted any other craft—Bildad, I say, might now be seen actively engaged in looking over the bows for the approaching anchor, and at intervals singing what seemed a dismal stave of psalmody, to cheer the hands at the windlass, who roared forth some sort of a chorus about the girls in Booble Alley, with hearty good will.", "미리 밝혀 두자면, 빌다드는 펠레그와 더불어 다른 직책 외에도 이 항구의 면허받은 도선사 가운데 한 사람이었는데 — 사람들은 그가 자신이 관여하는 모든 배에 들어가는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 도선료를 아끼려고 일부러 도선사 자격을 얻은 게 아니냐고 의심했으니, 그가 다른 어떤 배도 인도한 적이 없었기 때문이다 — 그 빌다드가, 말하건대, 이제 뱃머리 너머로 다가오는 닻을 살피느라 부지런히 매달려 있었고, 이따금 양묘기에서 일하는 선원들을 북돋우려는 듯 음울한 찬송가 한 소절 같은 것을 흥얼거렸으니, 그 선원들은 한껏 흥에 겨워 부블 골목의 처녀들에 관한 후렴 같은 것을 목청껏 부르고 있었다."], ["Nevertheless, not three days previous, Bildad had told them that no profane songs would be allowed on board the Pequod, particularly in getting under weigh; and Charity, his sister, had placed a small choice copy of Watts in each seaman’s berth.", "그러나 빌다드는 불과 사흘 전에, 페쿼드호 위에서는, 특히 출항할 때는 불경한 노래를 일절 허용하지 않겠다고 그들에게 일러두었었다. 게다가 그의 누이 채리티는 선원 각자의 침상마다 와츠 찬송가 선집의 자그마한 정선본을 한 권씩 놓아 두었던 터였다."]], [["Meantime, overseeing the other part of the ship, Captain Peleg ripped and swore astern in the most frightful manner.", "한편, 펠레그 선장은 배의 다른 쪽을 감독하느라 고물 쪽에서 더없이 무시무시하게 욕설을 퍼붓고 고함을 질렀다."], ["I almost thought he would sink the ship before the anchor could be got up; involuntarily I paused on my handspike, and told Queequeg to do the same, thinking of the perils we both ran, in starting on the voyage with such a devil for a pilot.", "나는 닻이 올라오기도 전에 그가 배를 가라앉혀 버리지 않을까 싶을 정도였다. 나도 모르게 양묘봉을 잡은 채 멈춰 서서 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)에게도 그렇게 하라 일렀으니, 저런 악마 같은 자를 도선사로 삼고 항해를 떠나는 우리 둘이 무릅쓸 위험들을 떠올렸기 때문이다."], ["I was comforting myself, however, with the thought that in pious Bildad might be found some salvation, spite of his seven hundred and seventy-seventh lay; when I felt a sudden sharp poke in my rear, and turning round, was horrified at the apparition of Captain Peleg in the act of withdrawing his leg from my immediate vicinity.", "그래도 나는 경건한 빌다드에게서 일말의 구원을 찾을 수 있으리라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로하고 있었다 — 그가 777분의 1이라는 배당 몫을 매겼음에도 불구하고 말이다. 그때 별안간 엉덩이에 날카로운 쿡 찌름이 느껴졌고, 돌아보니 펠레그 선장이 바로 내 곁에서 다리를 거두어들이는 모습이 눈에 들어와 소스라치게 놀랐다."], ["That was my first kick.", "그것이 내가 처음 걷어차인 발길질이었다."]], [["“Is that the way they heave in the marchant service?”", "\"상선에서는 그딴 식으로 닻을 끌어 올리더냐?\""], ["he roared.", "그가 고함쳤다."], ["“Spring, thou sheep-head; spring, and break thy backbone!", "\"용을 써, 이 양대가리야. 용을 써, 등뼈가 부러지도록!"], ["Why don’t ye spring, I say, all of ye—spring!", "왜 용을 안 쓰는 거냐, 이 말이다, 죄다 — 용을 써!"], ["Quohog!", "코호그!"], ["spring, thou chap with the red whiskers; spring there, Scotch-cap; spring, thou green pants.", "용을 써, 거기 붉은 구레나룻 사내."], ["Spring, I say, all of ye, and spring your eyes out!”", "용을 써, 스코틀랜드 모자. 용을 써, 거기 초록 바지."], ["And so saying, he moved along the windlass, here and there using his leg very freely, while imperturbable Bildad kept leading off with his psalmody.", "용을 쓰라니까, 죄다, 눈알이 튀어나오도록 용을 써!\" 그러고는 양묘기를 따라 이리저리 옮겨 다니며 다리를 거리낌 없이 마구 휘둘렀고, 그동안 태연자약한 빌다드는 줄곧 찬송가를 선창하고 있었다."], ["Thinks I, Captain Peleg must have been drinking something to-day.", "나는 속으로 생각했다, 펠레그 선장이 오늘 무언가 술을 좀 걸친 게 틀림없다고."]], [["At last the anchor was up, the sails were set, and off we glided.", "마침내 닻이 올라오고, 돛이 펼쳐졌으며, 우리는 미끄러지듯 나아갔다."], ["It was a short, cold Christmas; and as the short northern day merged into night, we found ourselves almost broad upon the wintry ocean, whose freezing spray cased us in ice, as in polished armor.", "짧고 추운 성탄절이었다. 그리고 짧은 북녘의 낮이 밤 속으로 잦아들 무렵, 우리는 어느덧 겨울 바다 한가운데로 거의 다 나와 있었으니, 그 얼어붙는 물보라가 윤이 나는 갑옷처럼 우리를 얼음으로 감쌌다."], ["The long rows of teeth on the bulwarks glistened in the moonlight; and like the white ivory tusks of some huge elephant, vast curving icicles depended from the bows.", "뱃전을 따라 길게 늘어선 이빨들이 달빛 아래 반짝였고, 거대한 코끼리의 새하얀 상아 엄니처럼, 한껏 굽은 거대한 고드름들이 뱃머리에 매달려 있었다."]], [["Lank Bildad, as pilot, headed the first watch, and ever and anon, as the old craft deep dived into the green seas, and sent the shivering frost all over her, and the winds howled, and the cordage rang, his steady notes were heard,—", "깡마른 빌다드는 도선사로서 첫 당직을 이끌었다. 그리고 그 낡은 배가 검푸른 바다 속으로 깊이 곤두박질치며 오슬오슬한 서리를 온몸에 뒤집어쓰고, 바람이 울부짖고 삭구가 윙윙 울릴 때마다, 그의 흔들림 없는 가락이 들려왔다."]], [["“Sweet fields beyond the swelling flood, Stand dressed in living green.", "\"넘실대는 강물 너머 정다운 들판이, 살아 숨 쉬는 초록빛 옷을 입고 서 있네."], ["So to the Jews old Canaan stood, While Jordan rolled between.”", "그렇게 옛 유대인들 앞에 가나안이 서 있었으니, 그 사이로 요단강이 굽이쳐 흐를 적에.\""]], [["Never did those sweet words sound more sweetly to me than then.", "그 정다운 노랫말이 그때만큼 내게 더없이 정답게 울린 적은 없었다."], ["They were full of hope and fruition.", "그 가락은 희망과 결실로 가득 차 있었다."], ["Spite of this frigid winter night in the boisterous Atlantic, spite of my wet feet and wetter jacket, there was yet, it then seemed to me, many a pleasant haven in store; and meads and glades so eternally vernal, that the grass shot up by the spring, untrodden, unwilted, remains at midsummer.", "사나운 대서양의 이 매서운 겨울밤에도, 젖은 발과 더 흠뻑 젖은 외투에도 불구하고, 그때 내게는 앞으로 다다를 기분 좋은 항구가 수없이 마련되어 있을 것만 같았다. 그리고 영원히 봄빛을 띤 초원과 숲속 빈터들이 있어, 봄에 돋아난 풀이 밟히지도 시들지도 않은 채 한여름까지 그대로 남아 있을 것만 같았다."]], [["At last we gained such an offing, that the two pilots were needed no longer.", "마침내 우리는 두 도선사가 더는 필요 없을 만큼 먼바다로 나아갔다."], ["The stout sail-boat that had accompanied us began ranging alongside.", "우리를 뒤따라온 튼튼한 돛단배가 옆으로 바싹 붙어 오기 시작했다."]], [["It was curious and not unpleasing, how Peleg and Bildad were affected at this juncture, especially Captain Bildad. For loath to depart, yet; very loath to leave, for good, a ship bound on so long and perilous a voyage—beyond both stormy Capes; a ship in which some thousands of his hard earned dollars were invested; a ship, in which an old shipmate sailed as captain; a man almost as old as he, once more starting to encounter all the terrors of the pitiless jaw; loath to say good-bye to a thing so every way brimful of every interest to him,—poor old Bildad lingered long; paced the deck with anxious strides; ran down into the cabin to speak another farewell word there; again came on deck, and looked to windward; looked towards the wide and endless waters, only bounded by the far-off unseen Eastern Continents; looked towards the land; looked aloft; looked right and left; looked everywhere and nowhere; and at last, mechanically coiling a rope upon its pin, convulsively grasped stout Peleg by the hand, and holding up a lantern, for a moment stood gazing heroically in his face, as much as to say, “Nevertheless, friend Peleg, I can stand it; yes, I can.”", "이 갈림길에서 펠레그와 빌다드가, 특히 빌다드 선장이 어떤 심정에 휩싸였는지는 묘하면서도 보기 싫지 않은 광경이었다. 떠나기가 못내 아쉬웠던 것이다. 그토록 길고 위태로운 항해를 — 폭풍 몰아치는 두 곶을 모두 넘어가야 할 항해를 — 떠나는 배와 영영 작별하기가 못내 아쉬웠던 것이다. 자신이 애써 번 수천 달러를 투자한 배, 한때의 옛 동료가 선장으로 타고 있는 배, 자신과 거의 나이가 엇비슷한 그 사내가 또다시 저 무자비한 아가리의 온갖 공포와 맞서러 떠나는 배, 그에게는 모든 면에서 온갖 관심거리로 넘쳐 나는 그 무엇과 작별 인사를 나누기가 못내 아쉬웠던 것이다 — 가련한 늙은 빌다드는 오래도록 머뭇거렸다. 불안한 걸음으로 갑판을 서성였고, 또 한 번 작별의 말을 건네러 선실로 뛰어 내려갔다가, 다시 갑판으로 올라와 바람 부는 쪽을 바라보았다. 저 멀리 보이지 않는 동방의 대륙들로만 경계 지어진, 넓고 끝없는 바다를 바라보았다. 뭍 쪽을 바라보았다. 위쪽을 올려다보았다. 좌우를 둘러보았다. 사방을 보면서도 아무 데도 보지 않았다. 그러다 마침내, 기계적으로 밧줄을 고정 핀에 둘둘 감고는, 발작하듯 우람한 펠레그의 손을 움켜쥐었고, 등불을 치켜든 채 잠시 그의 얼굴을 영웅적으로 응시하며 서 있었으니, 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"그래도 말일세, 친구 펠레그, 나는 견딜 수 있네. 그래, 나는 견딜 수 있어.\""]], [["As for Peleg himself, he took it more like a philosopher; but for all his philosophy, there was a tear twinkling in his eye, when the lantern came too near.", "펠레그로 말하자면, 그는 이를 좀 더 철학자답게 받아들였다. 그러나 그 모든 철학에도 불구하고, 등불이 너무 가까이 다가오자 그의 눈에는 눈물 한 방울이 반짝이고 있었다."], ["And he, too, did not a little run from cabin to deck—now a word below, and now a word with Starbuck, the chief mate.", "그 역시 선실과 갑판 사이를 적잖이 오르내렸다 — 아래에서 한마디 건네고, 또 일등 항해사 스타벅과 한마디 나누곤 했다."]], [["But, at last, he turned to his comrade, with a final sort of look about him,—“Captain Bildad—come, old shipmate, we must go.", "그러나 마침내 그는, 마지막을 고하는 듯한 눈빛으로 주위를 둘러보며 동료를 돌아보았다. \"빌다드 선장 — 자, 옛 동료여, 우리는 가야 하네."], ["Back the main-yard there!", "거기 큰 활대를 뒤로 돌려라!"], ["Boat ahoy!", "보트, 어이!"], ["Stand by to come close alongside, now!", "자, 옆으로 바싹 붙일 준비를 해라!"], ["Careful, careful!—come, Bildad, boy—say your last.", "조심해, 조심하라고!"], ["Luck to ye, Starbuck—luck to ye, Mr.", "— 자, 빌다드, 이 사람아 — 마지막 인사를 하게."], ["Stubb—luck to ye, Mr. Flask—good-bye and good luck to ye all—and this day three years I’ll have a hot supper smoking for ye in old Nantucket.", "행운을 비네, 스타벅 — 행운을 비네, 스터브 군 — 행운을 비네, 플래스크(Flask) 군 — 모두들 잘 가게, 행운을 비네 — 그리고 오늘로부터 삼 년 뒤에는 옛 낸터킷에서 김이 모락모락 나는 뜨거운 저녁을 자네들에게 차려 두고 기다리겠네."], ["Hurrah and away!”", "만세, 자, 떠나라!\""]], [["“God bless ye, and have ye in His holy keeping, men,”", "\"신의 가호가 함께하길, 신의 거룩한 보살핌 속에 머무르길 비네, 사람들아.\""], ["murmured old Bildad, almost incoherently.", "늙은 빌다드가 거의 두서없이 중얼거렸다."], ["“I hope ye’ll have fine weather now, so that Captain Ahab may soon be moving among ye—a pleasant sun is all he needs, and ye’ll have plenty of them in the tropic voyage ye go.", "\"이제 날씨가 좋아져서 에이해브 선장이 어서 자네들 사이를 거닐 수 있게 되길 바라네 — 그분에게 필요한 건 따사로운 햇볕뿐인데, 자네들이 떠나는 열대 항해에서는 그런 날이 넘쳐 날 테지."], ["Be careful in the hunt, ye mates.", "사냥할 때 조심들 하게, 항해사들."], ["Don’t stave the boats needlessly, ye harpooneers; good white cedar plank is raised full three per cent.", "보트를 공연히 부수지 말게, 작살잡이들. 좋은 흰 삼나무 판자가 올 한 해에만 값이 꼬박 3퍼센트나 올랐네."], ["within the year. Don’t forget your prayers, either.", "기도하는 것도 잊지 말고."], ["Mr. Starbuck, mind that cooper don’t waste the spare staves.", "스타벅 군, 통장이가 여분의 통널을 낭비하지 않도록 살피게."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["the sail-needles are in the green locker!", "돛 꿰매는 바늘은 초록색 사물함 속에 있네!"], ["Don’t whale it too much a’ Lord’s days, men; but don’t miss a fair chance either, that’s rejecting Heaven’s good gifts.", "주님의 날에는 너무 고래잡이에 열을 올리지 말게, 사람들아. 하지만 좋은 기회를 놓치지도 말게, 그건 하늘이 주신 좋은 선물을 마다하는 짓이니."], ["Have an eye to the molasses tierce, Mr.", "당밀 큰 통을 잘 살피게, 스터브 군."], ["Stubb; it was a little leaky, I thought.", "좀 새는 데가 있던 것 같으니."], ["If ye touch at the islands, Mr. Flask, beware of fornication.", "섬에 들르거든, 플래스크 군, 음행을 조심하게."], ["Good-bye, good-bye!", "잘 가게, 잘 가!"], ["Don’t keep that cheese too long down in the hold, Mr.", "그 치즈를 선창에 너무 오래 두지 말게, 스타벅 군."], ["Starbuck; it’ll spoil.", "상하고 말 테니."], ["Be careful with the butter—twenty cents the pound it was, and mind ye, if—”", "버터도 조심하고 — 파운드에 20센트나 주고 산 것이니, 명심하게나, 혹시라도 —\""]], [["“Come, come, Captain Bildad; stop palavering,—away!”", "\"자, 자, 빌다드 선장. 잔소리는 그만두고 — 어서 가세!\""], ["and with that, Peleg hurried him over the side, and both dropt into the boat.", "그 말과 함께 펠레그는 그를 서둘러 뱃전 너머로 내몰았고, 두 사람은 보트 안으로 내려섰다."]], [["Ship and boat diverged; the cold, damp night breeze blew between; a screaming gull flew overhead; the two hulls wildly rolled; we gave three heavy-hearted cheers, and blindly plunged like fate into the lone Atlantic.", "배와 보트는 서로 멀어져 갔다. 차고 축축한 밤바람이 그 사이로 불었다. 끼룩끼룩 우는 갈매기 한 마리가 머리 위로 날아갔다. 두 선체가 사납게 일렁였다. 우리는 무거운 마음으로 세 번 환호성을 올린 뒤, 운명처럼 눈먼 채 외로운 대서양 속으로 곤두박질쳐 들어갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 23. The Lee Shore.", "# CHAPTER 23. The Lee Shore."]], [["Some chapters back, one Bulkington was spoken of, a tall, newlanded mariner, encountered in New Bedford at the inn.", "몇 장 앞에서 벌킹턴이라는 사람이 언급된 적이 있다. 키가 크고, 갓 뭍에 오른 선원으로, 뉴베드퍼드의 여인숙에서 마주쳤던 인물이다."]], [["When on that shivering winter’s night, the Pequod thrust her vindictive bows into the cold malicious waves, who should I see standing at her helm but Bulkington! I looked with sympathetic awe and fearfulness upon the man, who in mid-winter just landed from a four years’ dangerous voyage, could so unrestingly push off again for still another tempestuous term. The land seemed scorching to his feet. Wonderfullest things are ever the unmentionable; deep memories yield no epitaphs; this six-inch chapter is the stoneless grave of Bulkington. Let me only say that it fared with him as with the storm-tossed ship, that miserably drives along the leeward land. The port would fain give succor; the port is pitiful; in the port is safety, comfort, hearthstone, supper, warm blankets, friends, all that’s kind to our mortalities. But in that gale, the port, the land, is that ship’s direst jeopardy; she must fly all hospitality; one touch of land, though it but graze the keel, would make her shudder through and through. With all her might she crowds all sail off shore; in so doing, fights ’gainst the very winds that fain would blow her homeward; seeks all the lashed sea’s landlessness again; for refuge’s sake forlornly rushing into peril; her only friend her bitterest foe!", "그 살을 에는 겨울밤, 피쿼드(Pequod)호가 복수심에 찬 뱃머리를 차갑고 악의에 찬 파도 속으로 들이밀었을 때, 그 키를 잡고 서 있는 사람이 다름 아닌 벌킹턴이 아니겠는가! 나는 연민 어린 경외와 두려움으로 그 사내를 바라보았다. 한겨울에, 사 년에 걸친 위험천만한 항해에서 막 돌아온 그가, 또다시 폭풍우 같은 한 차례의 항해를 향해 그토록 쉴 새 없이 다시 떠밀려 나갈 수 있다니. 뭍은 그의 발을 태워 버릴 듯한 모양이었다. 가장 경이로운 것은 언제나 입에 담을 수 없는 법이니, 깊은 기억은 어떤 묘비명도 내놓지 않는다. 이 여섯 치짜리 장(章)이야말로 벌킹턴의, 비석 없는 무덤이다. 다만 이것만은 말해 두자. 그에게 닥친 일은 폭풍에 시달리는 배의 처지와도 같아서, 그 배는 비참하게도 바람 불어가는 쪽의 뭍을 따라 떠밀려 간다. 항구는 기꺼이 구원의 손길을 내밀려 하고, 항구는 가엾이 여기는 마음으로 가득하며, 항구 안에는 안전과 안락과 화롯가와 저녁 식사와 따뜻한 담요와 벗들, 곧 죽을 운명을 타고난 우리에게 다정한 모든 것이 있다. 그러나 그 폭풍 속에서는 항구가, 그 뭍이 곧 그 배에게 가장 무서운 위험인 것이다. 배는 모든 환대를 피해 달아나야만 한다. 한 번의 뭍과의 닿음은, 설령 그것이 용골을 스치기만 할지라도, 배를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부르르 떨게 만들 것이다. 배는 온 힘을 다해 돛이란 돛을 모조리 펼쳐 뭍에서 멀어진다. 그리하여 그 와중에, 자신을 고향으로 불어 보내고픈 바로 그 바람들과 맞서 싸운다. 채찍질당하는 바다의 뭍 없음을 다시금 찾아 나선다. 피난처를 얻기 위해 쓸쓸히 위험 속으로 돌진해 들어간다. 배의 유일한 벗이 곧 가장 쓰라린 적인 것이다!"]], [["Know ye now, Bulkington?", "이제 알겠는가, 벌킹턴이여?"], ["Glimpses do ye seem to see of that mortally intolerable truth; that all deep, earnest thinking is but the intrepid effort of the soul to keep the open independence of her sea; while the wildest winds of heaven and earth conspire to cast her on the treacherous, slavish shore?", "그대는 저 죽도록 견딜 수 없는 진실의 단편을 언뜻언뜻 보는 듯하다. 곧 모든 깊고 진지한 사색이란, 하늘과 땅의 가장 사나운 바람들이 한데 모의하여 영혼을 저 위험천만하고 비굴한 뭍으로 내동댕이치려 할 때, 그 바다의 탁 트인 독립을 지켜 내려는 영혼의 두려움 없는 분투에 지나지 않는다는 진실을?"]], [["But as in landlessness alone resides highest truth, shoreless, indefinite as God—so, better is it to perish in that howling infinite, than be ingloriously dashed upon the lee, even if that were safety!", "그러나 가장 드높은 진실이 오직 뭍 없음 속에만 깃들어, 하느님처럼 물가도 없고 끝도 없는 것이라면 — 그렇다면, 비록 그것이 안전이라 할지라도, 명예롭지 못하게 바람 불어가는 쪽에 부서져 버리느니, 차라리 저 울부짖는 무한 속에서 스러지는 편이 낫다!"], ["For worm-like, then, oh!", "그러할진대, 아아!"], ["who would craven crawl to land!", "누가 벌레처럼 비겁하게 뭍을 향해 기어가려 하겠는가!"], ["Terrors of the terrible!", "무시무시한 것 중에서도 무시무시한 것이여!"], ["is all this agony so vain?", "이 모든 고뇌가 그토록 헛된 것이란 말인가?"], ["Take heart, take heart, O Bulkington!", "용기를 내라, 용기를 내라, 오 벌킹턴이여!"], ["Bear thee grimly, demigod!", "의연히 견뎌 내라, 반신반인(半神半人)이여!"], ["Up from the spray of thy ocean-perishing—straight up, leaps thy apotheosis!", "그대를 삼키는 대양의 물보라 속에서 — 곧장 위로, 그대의 신격화가 솟구쳐 오르나니!"]], [["# CHAPTER 24. The Advocate.", "# CHAPTER 24. The Advocate."]], [["As Queequeg and I are now fairly embarked in this business of whaling; and as this business of whaling has somehow come to be regarded among landsmen as a rather unpoetical and disreputable pursuit; therefore, I am all anxiety to convince ye, ye landsmen, of the injustice hereby done to us hunters of whales.", "퀴퀘그와 나는 이제 어엿이 이 포경업이라는 일에 발을 들여놓았다. 그런데 이 포경업이라는 것이 어찌 된 영문인지 뭍사람들 사이에서는 다소 시적이지 못하고 평판 나쁜 생업으로 여겨지게 되었다. 그러니 나는 그대들, 뭍사람들에게 우리 고래 사냥꾼들이 이로 인해 받고 있는 부당함을 꼭 납득시키고 싶어 안달이 난 것이다."]], [["In the first place, it may be deemed almost superfluous to establish the fact, that among people at large, the business of whaling is not accounted on a level with what are called the liberal professions.", "우선, 일반 대중들 사이에서 포경업이 이른바 자유전문직과 동등한 수준으로 평가받지 못한다는 사실을 입증하는 일은 거의 군더더기로 여겨질지도 모른다."], ["If a stranger were introduced into any miscellaneous metropolitan society, it would but slightly advance the general opinion of his merits, were he presented to the company as a harpooneer, say; and if in emulation of the naval officers he should append the initials S.W.F. (Sperm Whale Fishery) to his visiting card, such a procedure would be deemed pre-eminently presuming and ridiculous.", "어느 잡다한 대도시 사교계에 낯선 사람 하나가 소개된다고 했을 때, 그가 이를테면 작살잡이로서 그 모임에 소개된다 한들 그의 인물됨에 대한 세간의 평이 조금이라도 올라갈 리 없으며, 만일 그가 해군 장교들을 흉내 내어 자기 명함에 S.W.F.(향유고래 어업)라는 머리글자를 덧붙인다면, 그런 짓은 더없이 주제넘고 가소로운 일로 여겨질 것이다."]], [["Doubtless one leading reason why the world declines honoring us whalemen, is this: they think that, at best, our vocation amounts to a butchering sort of business; and that when actively engaged therein, we are surrounded by all manner of defilements.", "세상이 우리 고래잡이들에게 영예를 돌리기를 마다하는 한 가지 주된 이유는 분명 이것이다. 그들은 우리 직업이 기껏해야 도살에 가까운 부류의 일이며, 그 일에 한창 매달려 있을 때 우리는 온갖 더러움에 둘러싸여 있다고 여기는 것이다."], ["Butchers we are, that is true.", "우리가 도살꾼인 것은 사실이다."], ["But butchers, also, and butchers of the bloodiest badge have been all Martial Commanders whom the world invariably delights to honor.", "그러나 도살꾼이라면, 그것도 가장 피비린내 나는 표지를 단 도살꾼이라면, 세상이 한결같이 기꺼이 떠받드는 저 모든 군사 지휘관들 또한 그러했다."], ["And as for the matter of the alleged uncleanliness of our business, ye shall soon be initiated into certain facts hitherto pretty generally unknown, and which, upon the whole, will triumphantly plant the sperm whale-ship at least among the cleanliest things of this tidy earth.", "그리고 우리 일이 불결하다는 그 비난에 관해서라면, 그대들은 머지않아 지금껏 거의 널리 알려지지 않았던 어떤 사실들을 깨우치게 될 것이며, 그 사실들은 대체로 보아 향유고래잡이 배를 적어도 이 단정한 지구상에서 가장 깨끗한 것들 가운데 당당히 세워 놓을 것이다."], ["But even granting the charge in question to be true; what disordered slippery decks of a whale-ship are comparable to the unspeakable carrion of those battle-fields from which so many soldiers return to drink in all ladies’ plaudits?", "그러나 설령 문제의 그 비난을 사실로 인정한다 한들, 고래잡이 배의 어질러지고 미끄러운 갑판이 그토록 많은 병사들이 돌아와 온갖 숙녀들의 갈채를 들이켜는 저 전장의 형언할 수 없는 시체 더미에 비할 만한 것이겠는가?"], ["And if the idea of peril so much enhances the popular conceit of the soldier’s profession; let me assure ye that many a veteran who has freely marched up to a battery, would quickly recoil at the apparition of the sperm whale’s vast tail, fanning into eddies the air over his head.", "그리고 위험이라는 관념이 병사라는 직업에 대한 세간의 자부심을 그토록 드높여 준다면, 단언컨대 포대를 향해 거침없이 진격해 본 적 있는 숱한 노병들도 향유고래의 거대한 꼬리가 머리 위 허공을 소용돌이로 부채질하는 그 환영 앞에서는 순식간에 뒷걸음질 칠 것이다."], ["For what are the comprehensible terrors of man compared with the interlinked terrors and wonders of God!", "인간이 헤아릴 수 있는 공포 따위가 신의 그 서로 얽힌 공포와 경이에 어찌 비할 수 있겠는가!"]], [["But, though the world scouts at us whale hunters, yet does it unwittingly pay us the profoundest homage; yea, an all-abounding adoration!", "그러나 세상이 우리 고래 사냥꾼들을 비웃을지언정, 그것은 자기도 모르게 우리에게 가장 깊은 경의를 — 그렇다, 넘치고 넘치는 흠모를 바치고 있는 것이다!"], ["for almost all the tapers, lamps, and candles that burn round the globe, burn, as before so many shrines, to our glory!", "지구 곳곳에서 타오르는 거의 모든 양초와 등불과 촛불이, 마치 수많은 신전 앞에서 타오르듯, 우리의 영광을 위해 타오르고 있으니!"]], [["But look at this matter in other lights; weigh it in all sorts of scales; see what we whalemen are, and have been.", "그러나 이 문제를 다른 빛으로 비추어 보고, 온갖 저울에 달아 보라. 우리 고래잡이들이 무엇이며, 또 무엇이었는지를 보라."]], [["Why did the Dutch in De Witt’s time have admirals of their whaling fleets? Why did Louis XVI.", "어찌하여 드 비트 시대의 네덜란드인들은 그들의 포경 선단에 제독을 두었는가?"], ["of France, at his own personal expense, fit out whaling ships from Dunkirk, and politely invite to that town some score or two of families from our own island of Nantucket?", "어찌하여 프랑스의 루이 16세는 사비를 털어 됭케르크에서 포경선을 의장하고, 우리 고향 낸터킷 섬에서 스무남은 가구를 그 도시로 정중히 초청했는가?"], ["Why did Britain between the years 1750 and 1788 pay to her whalemen in bounties upwards of £1,000,000?", "어찌하여 영국은 1750년에서 1788년 사이에 자국 고래잡이들에게 100만 파운드가 넘는 장려금을 지불했는가?"], ["And lastly, how comes it that we whalemen of America now outnumber all the rest of the banded whalemen in the world; sail a navy of upwards of seven hundred vessels; manned by eighteen thousand men; yearly consuming 4,000,000 of dollars; the ships worth, at the time of sailing, $20,000,000! and every year importing into our harbors a well reaped harvest of $7,000,000.", "그리고 끝으로, 어찌하여 우리 아메리카의 고래잡이들이 이제 세계의 나머지 모든 고래잡이 무리를 합친 수를 능가하며, 700척이 넘는 함대를 띄우고, 1만 8천 명의 사람을 태우며, 해마다 400만 달러를 쓰고, 출항 당시 그 배들의 값어치가 2천만 달러에 이르며, 또 해마다 우리 항구로 700만 달러라는 알찬 수확을 거두어들이게 되었는가?"], ["How comes all this, if there be not something puissant in whaling?", "포경업에 무언가 강력한 힘이 깃들어 있지 않다면, 이 모든 일이 어찌하여 일어난단 말인가?"]], [["But this is not the half; look again.", "그러나 이것은 절반에도 미치지 못한다. 다시 보라."]], [["I freely assert, that the cosmopolite philosopher cannot, for his life, point out one single peaceful influence, which within the last sixty years has operated more potentially upon the whole broad world, taken in one aggregate, than the high and mighty business of whaling.", "나는 거리낌 없이 단언하건대, 저 세계주의 철학자라 한들 목숨을 걸고서라도 지난 60년 동안 온 넓은 세상에 한데 묶어 통틀어 보아 저 높고도 강력한 포경업보다 더 강력하게 작용한 평화로운 영향력 단 하나조차 짚어 낼 수 없을 것이다."], ["One way and another, it has begotten events so remarkable in themselves, and so continuously momentous in their sequential issues, that whaling may well be regarded as that Egyptian mother, who bore offspring themselves pregnant from her womb.", "이러구러 포경업은 그 자체로 너무도 주목할 만하고, 그 잇따른 결과에 있어 끊임없이 중대한 사건들을 낳아 왔으니, 포경업은 가히 저 이집트의 어미에 견줄 만하다. 제 태에서 낳은 자식들이 다시 제 안에 아이를 밴 채로 태어나게 한 그 어미 말이다."], ["It would be a hopeless, endless task to catalogue all these things.", "이 모든 것을 일일이 목록으로 정리하는 일은 가망 없고 끝없는 작업이 될 것이다."], ["Let a handful suffice.", "한 줌만으로 족하다."], ["For many years past the whale-ship has been the pioneer in ferreting out the remotest and least known parts of the earth.", "지난 여러 해 동안 고래잡이 배는 지구상에서 가장 멀고 가장 덜 알려진 지역들을 들추어 찾아내는 선구자였다."], ["She has explored seas and archipelagoes which had no chart, where no Cook or Vancouver had ever sailed.", "그 배는 해도(海圖)에도 없고, 쿡(Cook)이나 밴쿠버조차 한 번도 항해한 적 없는 바다와 군도들을 탐험했다."], ["If American and European men-of-war now peacefully ride in once savage harbors, let them fire salutes to the honor and glory of the whale-ship, which originally showed them the way, and first interpreted between them and the savages.", "만일 아메리카와 유럽의 군함들이 이제 한때 야만스럽던 항구에 평화로이 정박해 있다면, 그들로 하여금 애초에 그 길을 보여 주고 그들과 야만인들 사이에서 처음으로 통역해 준 고래잡이 배의 명예와 영광을 위해 예포를 쏘게 하라."], ["They may celebrate as they will the heroes of Exploring Expeditions, your Cooks, your Krusensterns; but I say that scores of anonymous Captains have sailed out of Nantucket, that were as great, and greater than your Cook and your Krusenstern.", "그들이 탐험 원정대의 영웅들, 그대들의 쿡이며 그대들의 크루젠슈테른을 마음껏 기린들, 나는 말하노니 낸터킷에서 출항한 이름 없는 선장 수십 명이 그대들의 쿡이나 크루젠슈테른만큼, 아니 그보다 더 위대했다."], ["For in their succourless empty-handedness, they, in the heathenish sharked waters, and by the beaches of unrecorded, javelin islands, battled with virgin wonders and terrors that Cook with all his marines and muskets would not willingly have dared.", "그들은 도움도 없이 빈손인 채로, 상어가 들끓는 저 이교의 바다에서, 기록에도 없고 투창이나 날아드는 섬들의 해변에서, 쿡이 그 모든 해병과 머스킷 총을 거느리고도 선뜻 무릅쓰려 하지 않았을 처녀지의 경이와 공포에 맞서 싸웠던 것이다."], ["All that is made such a flourish of in the old South Sea Voyages, those things were but the life-time commonplaces of our heroic Nantucketers.", "저 옛 남양 항해기에서 그토록 요란하게 떠벌리는 그 모든 것들이, 우리 영웅적인 낸터킷 사람들에게는 한평생의 예사로운 일에 지나지 않았다."], ["Often, adventures which Vancouver dedicates three chapters to, these men accounted unworthy of being set down in the ship’s common log.", "밴쿠버가 세 장(章)을 통째로 바쳐 적은 모험들을, 이 사람들은 흔히 배의 일지에 적어 넣을 가치조차 없는 것으로 쳤다."], ["Ah, the world!", "아, 세상이여!"], ["Oh, the world!", "오, 세상이여!"]], [["Until the whale fishery rounded Cape Horn, no commerce but colonial, scarcely any intercourse but colonial, was carried on between Europe and the long line of the opulent Spanish provinces on the Pacific coast.", "고래잡이 어업이 케이프 혼을 돌아가기 전까지, 유럽과 태평양 연안에 늘어선 저 부유한 스페인 식민지들 사이에는 식민지 무역 외의 어떤 통상도, 식민지 교류 외의 어떤 왕래도 거의 이루어지지 않았다."], ["It was the whaleman who first broke through the jealous policy of the Spanish crown, touching those colonies; and, if space permitted, it might be distinctly shown how from those whalemen at last eventuated the liberation of Peru, Chili, and Bolivia from the yoke of Old Spain, and the establishment of the eternal democracy in those parts.", "그 식민지들에 관한 스페인 왕실의 시샘 어린 정책을 처음으로 깨뜨린 이가 바로 고래잡이였다. 그리고 지면이 허락한다면, 어찌하여 그 고래잡이들로부터 마침내 페루와 칠레와 볼리비아가 옛 스페인의 멍에에서 해방되고, 그 지역에 영원한 민주정이 수립되기에 이르렀는지를 또렷이 보여 줄 수도 있으리라."]], [["That great America on the other side of the sphere, Australia, was given to the enlightened world by the whaleman.", "지구 저편의 저 또 다른 위대한 아메리카, 곧 오스트레일리아를 깨우친 세상에 안겨 준 것도 고래잡이였다."], ["After its first blunder-born discovery by a Dutchman, all other ships long shunned those shores as pestiferously barbarous; but the whale-ship touched there.", "어느 네덜란드인이 어쩌다 헛디뎌 그곳을 발견한 뒤로, 다른 모든 배들은 그 해안을 역병처럼 야만스러운 곳으로 여겨 오래도록 피해 다녔으나, 고래잡이 배만은 그곳에 닿았다."], ["The whale-ship is the true mother of that now mighty colony.", "고래잡이 배야말로 이제 강성해진 그 식민지의 참된 어머니다."], ["Moreover, in the infancy of the first Australian settlement, the emigrants were several times saved from starvation by the benevolent biscuit of the whale-ship luckily dropping an anchor in their waters.", "게다가 최초의 오스트레일리아 정착지가 갓 생겨났을 무렵, 이주민들은 여러 차례 굶주림에서 구원받았으니, 다행히 그 바다에 닻을 내린 고래잡이 배가 베푼 자비로운 비스킷 덕분이었다."], ["The uncounted isles of all Polynesia confess the same truth, and do commercial homage to the whale-ship, that cleared the way for the missionary and the merchant, and in many cases carried the primitive missionaries to their first destinations.", "폴리네시아 전역의 헤아릴 수 없는 섬들도 같은 진실을 고백하며, 선교사와 상인을 위해 길을 터 주고, 많은 경우 초기 선교사들을 그들의 첫 목적지까지 실어 나른 고래잡이 배에 통상의 경의를 바친다."], ["If that double-bolted land, Japan, is ever to become hospitable, it is the whale-ship alone to whom the credit will be due; for already she is on the threshold.", "만일 저 이중으로 빗장을 지른 땅, 일본이 언젠가 손님을 맞아들이게 된다면, 그 공로는 오로지 고래잡이 배에게 돌아갈 것이다. 그 배는 이미 그 문턱에 와 있으니 말이다."]], [["But if, in the face of all this, you still declare that whaling has no æsthetically noble associations connected with it, then am I ready to shiver fifty lances with you there, and unhorse you with a split helmet every time.", "그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 그대가 여전히 포경업에는 미학적으로 고귀하다 할 만한 연상이 조금도 없다고 단언한다면, 그렇다면 나는 그 자리에서 그대와 창 쉰 자루를 부딪칠 각오가 되어 있으며, 매번 그대의 투구를 쪼개어 그대를 말에서 떨어뜨릴 준비가 되어 있다."]], [["The whale has no famous author, and whaling no famous chronicler, you will say.", "고래에게는 이름난 작가가 없고, 포경업에는 이름난 연대기 작가가 없다고 그대는 말할 것이다."]], [["The whale no famous author, and whaling no famous chronicler?", "고래에게 이름난 작가가 없고, 포경업에 이름난 연대기 작가가 없다고?"], ["Who wrote the first account of our Leviathan?", "우리의 리바이어던에 관한 최초의 기록을 쓴 이가 누구인가?"], ["Who but mighty Job!", "다름 아닌 저 위대한 욥이 아니던가!"], ["And who composed the first narrative of a whaling-voyage?", "그리고 포경 항해에 관한 최초의 이야기를 지은 이가 누구인가?"], ["Who, but no less a prince than Alfred the Great, who, with his own royal pen, took down the words from Other, the Norwegian whale-hunter of those times!", "다름 아닌 앨프레드 대왕 그 못지않은 왕이니, 그는 친히 왕의 펜으로 그 시대의 노르웨이 고래 사냥꾼 오테르의 입에서 나온 말을 받아 적었다!"], ["And who pronounced our glowing eulogy in Parliament?", "그리고 의회에서 우리에게 그 빛나는 찬사를 바친 이가 누구인가?"], ["Who, but Edmund Burke!", "다름 아닌 에드먼드 버크가 아니던가!"]], [["True enough, but then whalemen themselves are poor devils; they have no good blood in their veins.", "과연 옳은 말이다, 그러나 고래잡이들 자신은 가련한 작자들이며, 그들의 핏줄에는 고귀한 피가 흐르지 않는다고 할 것이다."]], [["No good blood in their veins?", "그들의 핏줄에 고귀한 피가 흐르지 않는다고?"], ["They have something better than royal blood there.", "거기에는 왕족의 피보다 더 나은 무언가가 흐른다."], ["The grandmother of Benjamin Franklin was Mary Morrel; afterwards, by marriage, Mary Folger, one of the old settlers of Nantucket, and the ancestress to a long line of Folgers and harpooneers—all kith and kin to noble Benjamin—this day darting the barbed iron from one side of the world to the other.", "벤저민 프랭클린의 할머니는 메리 모렐이었으니, 훗날 혼인으로 메리 폴저가 되어 낸터킷의 옛 정착민 가운데 한 사람이자, 길게 이어지는 폴저 가문과 작살잡이들의 여조상이 되었다. 모두가 고귀한 벤저민의 일가친척이며, 오늘날에도 미늘 박힌 작살을 세계의 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝으로 내던지고 있다."]], [["Good again; but then all confess that somehow whaling is not respectable.", "또 좋은 말이다, 그러나 어쨌든 포경업이 점잖지 못하다는 것은 모두가 인정하지 않느냐고 할 것이다."]], [["Whaling not respectable?", "포경업이 점잖지 못하다고?"], ["Whaling is imperial!", "포경업은 제왕의 것이다!"], ["By old English statutory law, the whale is declared “a royal fish.” *", "옛 영국 성문법에 따르면 고래는 \"왕실 어류\"로 선포되어 있다."]], [["Oh, that’s only nominal!", "오, 그것은 그저 이름뿐인 것이다!"], ["The whale himself has never figured in any grand imposing way.", "고래 그 자체가 대단하고 위엄 있는 방식으로 등장한 적은 한 번도 없다고 할 것이다."]], [["The whale never figured in any grand imposing way?", "고래가 대단하고 위엄 있는 방식으로 등장한 적이 한 번도 없다고?"], ["In one of the mighty triumphs given to a Roman general upon his entering the world’s capital, the bones of a whale, brought all the way from the Syrian coast, were the most conspicuous object in the cymballed procession.*", "어느 로마 장군이 세계의 수도에 입성할 때 베풀어진 저 장엄한 개선식 가운데 하나에서, 멀리 시리아 해안에서부터 가져온 고래의 뼈가 심벌즈 울리는 행렬 속에서 가장 눈에 띄는 대상이었다."]], [["*See subsequent chapters for something more on this head.", "* 이 점에 관한 더 자세한 내용은 이후의 장들을 보라."]], [["Grant it, since you cite it; but, say what you will, there is no real dignity in whaling.", "그대가 그것을 인용하니 인정하겠다, 그러나 그대가 무어라 하든 포경업에는 진정한 위엄이 없다고 할 것이다."]], [["No dignity in whaling?", "포경업에 위엄이 없다고?"], ["The dignity of our calling the very heavens attest.", "우리 직업의 위엄은 저 하늘이 친히 증언한다."], ["Cetus is a constellation in the South! No more!", "고래자리(Cetus)는 남쪽 하늘의 별자리가 아닌가!"], ["Drive down your hat in presence of the Czar, and take it off to Queequeg! No more!", "더 말할 것도 없다!"], ["I know a man that, in his lifetime, has taken three hundred and fifty whales.", "차르 앞에서는 모자를 푹 눌러쓰되, 퀴퀘그 앞에서는 모자를 벗으라! 더 말할 것도 없다!"], ["I account that man more honorable than that great captain of antiquity who boasted of taking as many walled towns.", "나는 한평생 고래를 350마리나 잡은 사람을 알고 있다. 나는 그 사람을, 그만큼 많은 성곽 도시를 함락했노라 뽐내던 저 고대의 위대한 장군보다 더 명예로운 자로 친다."]], [["And, as for me, if, by any possibility, there be any as yet undiscovered prime thing in me; if I shall ever deserve any real repute in that small but high hushed world which I might not be unreasonably ambitious of; if hereafter I shall do anything that, upon the whole, a man might rather have done than to have left undone; if, at my death, my executors, or more properly my creditors, find any precious MSS.", "그리고 나 자신으로 말하자면, 만일 어떤 식으로든 내 안에 아직 발견되지 않은 으뜸가는 무언가가 있다면, 만일 내가 언젠가 그 작지만 고고하게 침묵하는 세계 — 내가 부당하다 할 수 없을 만큼 야심을 품어 봄 직한 그 세계 — 에서 진정한 명성을 얻을 자격이 생긴다면, 만일 내가 훗날 대체로 보아 하지 않고 내버려 두느니 차라리 해 두는 편이 나을 어떤 일을 해낸다면, 만일 내가 죽을 때 나의 유언 집행인들이, 아니 더 정확히 말해 나의 채권자들이 내 책상에서 무언가 값진 원고를 발견하게 된다면, 그렇다면 나는 여기서 미리 그 모든 명예와 영광을 포경업에 돌리는 바다."], ["in my desk, then here I prospectively ascribe all the honor and the glory to whaling; for a whale-ship was my Yale College and my Harvard.", "고래잡이 배야말로 나의 예일 대학이요 나의 하버드였으니."]], [["# CHAPTER 25. Postscript.", "# CHAPTER 25. Postscript."]], [["In behalf of the dignity of whaling, I would fain advance naught but substantiated facts.", "포경업의 존엄을 위하여, 나는 입증된 사실만을 내세우고 싶다."], ["But after embattling his facts, an advocate who should wholly suppress a not unreasonable surmise, which might tell eloquently upon his cause—such an advocate, would he not be blameworthy?", "그러나 자신의 사실들을 단단히 진열해 놓고서도, 자기 주장에 웅변적으로 보탬이 될 법한 그리 무리하지 않은 추측 하나를 통째로 억눌러 버리는 변호인이 있다면 — 그런 변호인이라면 비난받아 마땅하지 않겠는가?"]], [["It is well known that at the coronation of kings and queens, even modern ones, a certain curious process of seasoning them for their functions is gone through.", "널리 알려진 바와 같이, 왕과 왕비의 대관식에서는, 심지어 근대의 군주들조차도, 그들을 자기 직무에 맞게 간 맞추는 어떤 기묘한 절차를 거친다."], ["There is a saltcellar of state, so called, and there may be a castor of state.", "이른바 국가 의례용 소금그릇이라는 것이 있고, 국가 의례용 양념병도 있을 법하다."], ["How they use the salt, precisely—who knows?", "그들이 그 소금을 정확히 어떻게 쓰는지 — 누가 알겠는가?"], ["Certain I am, however, that a king’s head is solemnly oiled at his coronation, even as a head of salad.", "그러나 내가 확실히 아는 것은, 왕의 머리가 대관식에서 엄숙하게 기름 부음을 받는다는 사실이다, 마치 샐러드 한 접시에 기름을 두르듯이."], ["Can it be, though, that they anoint it with a view of making its interior run well, as they anoint machinery?", "하지만 혹시 그들이, 기계에 기름칠을 하듯, 그 머릿속이 잘 돌아가게 하려고 기름을 바르는 것은 아닐까?"], ["Much might be ruminated here, concerning the essential dignity of this regal process, because in common life we esteem but meanly and contemptibly a fellow who anoints his hair, and palpably smells of that anointing.", "이 왕가의 절차가 지닌 본질적 존엄에 관하여는 여기서 곰곰이 되새겨 볼 만한 것이 많다. 일상생활에서 우리는 머리에 기름을 바르고 그 기름 냄새를 노골적으로 풍기는 사내를 천박하고 멸시할 만한 자로 여기기 때문이다."], ["In truth, a mature man who uses hair-oil, unless medicinally, that man has probably got a quoggy spot in him somewhere.", "사실, 다 자란 성인 남자가 약용이 아닌 이상 머릿기름을 쓴다면, 그자는 어딘가에 물렁하고 미심쩍은 구석이 있을 공산이 크다."], ["As a general rule, he can’t amount to much in his totality.", "일반적으로 말해, 그런 자는 그 전체를 따져 보아도 변변치 못한 인물일 수밖에 없다."]], [["But the only thing to be considered here, is this—what kind of oil is used at coronations?", "그러나 여기서 따져 볼 단 한 가지는 바로 이것이다 — 대관식에서는 어떤 종류의 기름을 쓰는가?"], ["Certainly it cannot be olive oil, nor macassar oil, nor castor oil, nor bear’s oil, nor train oil, nor cod-liver oil.", "분명 올리브유는 아닐 것이고, 마카사르유도, 피마자유도, 곰 기름도, 고래 기름도, 대구 간유도 아닐 것이다."], ["What then can it possibly be, but sperm oil in its unmanufactured, unpolluted state, the sweetest of all oils?", "그렇다면 그것이, 정제도 오염도 거치지 않은 본디 그대로의, 모든 기름 가운데 가장 향기로운 향유고래 기름이 아니고서야 대체 무엇일 수 있겠는가?"]], [["Think of that, ye loyal Britons!", "이를 한번 생각해 보라, 충성스러운 영국인들이여!"], ["we whalemen supply your kings and queens with coronation stuff!", "그대들의 왕과 왕비에게 대관식에 쓸 물건을 대 주는 것이 바로 우리 고래잡이들이다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 26. Knights and Squires.", "# CHAPTER 26. Knights and Squires."]], [["The chief mate of the Pequod was Starbuck, a native of Nantucket, and a Quaker by descent.", "피쿼드호의 일등항해사는 스타벅으로, 낸터킷 태생이며 가계로 보면 퀘이커 교도였다."], ["He was a long, earnest man, and though born on an icy coast, seemed well adapted to endure hot latitudes, his flesh being hard as twice-baked biscuit.", "그는 키가 크고 진지한 사람이었으며, 비록 얼음으로 뒤덮인 해안에서 태어났으나 더운 위도를 견디기에 잘 맞는 듯했으니, 그의 살은 두 번 구운 비스킷처럼 단단했다."], ["Transported to the Indies, his live blood would not spoil like bottled ale.", "서인도제도로 옮겨 놓는다 해도 그의 살아 있는 피는 병에 담긴 맥주처럼 상하지 않을 터였다."], ["He must have been born in some time of general drought and famine, or upon one of those fast days for which his state is famous.", "그는 분명 어느 가뭄과 기근이 만연한 시절에, 아니면 그의 고향 주(州)가 유명한 그 금식일 가운데 어느 날에 태어났을 것이다."], ["Only some thirty arid summers had he seen; those summers had dried up all his physical superfluousness.", "그가 본 메마른 여름이라곤 고작 서른 번 남짓이었다. 그 여름들이 그의 육신에서 군더더기를 모조리 말려 버린 것이다."], ["But this, his thinness, so to speak, seemed no more the token of wasting anxieties and cares, than it seemed the indication of any bodily blight.", "그러나 이 야윈 몸은, 말하자면, 그를 갉아먹는 근심과 걱정의 표시도, 어떤 육체적 쇠락의 징후도 아닌 듯했다."], ["It was merely the condensation of the man.", "그것은 다만 그 사람을 응축해 놓은 것일 뿐이었다."], ["He was by no means ill-looking; quite the contrary.", "그는 결코 못난 인물이 아니었다. 오히려 그 반대였다."], ["His pure tight skin was an excellent fit; and closely wrapped up in it, and embalmed with inner health and strength, like a revivified Egyptian, this Starbuck seemed prepared to endure for long ages to come, and to endure always, as now; for be it Polar snow or torrid sun, like a patent chronometer, his interior vitality was warranted to do well in all climates.", "깨끗하고 탱탱한 피부는 그의 몸에 더없이 잘 맞았으며, 그 안에 단단히 감싸인 채 내면의 건강과 힘으로 방부 처리된 듯한 이 스타벅은, 마치 되살아난 이집트인처럼 앞으로 오랜 세월을 견디고, 지금처럼 언제까지나 견뎌 낼 채비가 된 듯했다. 극지의 눈이든 작열하는 태양이든, 정밀한 크로노미터처럼 그의 내면의 활력은 어떤 기후에서도 잘 작동하리라 보증된 것이었다."], ["Looking into his eyes, you seemed to see there the yet lingering images of those thousand-fold perils he had calmly confronted through life.", "그의 눈을 들여다보면, 거기에 그가 평생 동안 침착하게 맞서 온 그 수천 겹의 위험들이 아직 어른거리는 모습을 보는 듯했다."], ["A staid, steadfast man, whose life for the most part was a telling pantomime of action, and not a tame chapter of sounds.", "진중하고 한결같은 사람, 그의 삶은 대부분 소리로 이루어진 시시한 한 장(章)이 아니라, 행동으로 말하는 무언극이었다."], ["Yet, for all his hardy sobriety and fortitude, there were certain qualities in him which at times affected, and in some cases seemed well nigh to overbalance all the rest.", "그러나 그 모든 강인한 절제와 불굴의 정신에도 불구하고, 그에게는 때때로 마음을 흔들고, 어떤 경우에는 그 나머지 모든 것을 거의 압도하다시피 하는 어떤 자질들이 있었다."], ["Uncommonly conscientious for a seaman, and endued with a deep natural reverence, the wild watery loneliness of his life did therefore strongly incline him to superstition; but to that sort of superstition, which in some organizations seems rather to spring, somehow, from intelligence than from ignorance.", "뱃사람치고는 보기 드물게 양심적이었고, 깊고 천성적인 경외심을 타고난 그였기에, 그의 삶이 지닌 거칠고 물에 잠긴 고독은 그를 강하게 미신 쪽으로 기울게 했다. 그러나 그것은 어떤 기질에서는 어쩐 일인지 무지가 아니라 오히려 지성에서 솟아나는 듯한 그런 종류의 미신이었다."], ["Outward portents and inward presentiments were his.", "바깥의 전조와 안의 예감이 그의 것이었다."], ["And if at times these things bent the welded iron of his soul, much more did his far-away domestic memories of his young Cape wife and child, tend to bend him still more from the original ruggedness of his nature, and open him still further to those latent influences which, in some honest-hearted men, restrain the gush of dare-devil daring, so often evinced by others in the more perilous vicissitudes of the fishery.", "그리고 때때로 이런 것들이 그의 영혼의 단련된 쇠를 휘어 놓았다면, 멀리 떨어진 고향의 추억들 — 케이프코드의 젊은 아내와 아이에 대한 기억은 그보다 훨씬 더, 본래 거칠던 그의 천성을 휘어 놓았고, 어떤 정직한 마음을 지닌 사람들에게서 무모한 만용의 분출을 억제하는 — 다른 이들이 포경업의 더 위험한 격변 속에서 그토록 자주 드러내는 그 만용을 억누르는 잠재적 영향력에 그를 한층 더 활짝 열어 놓았다."], ["“I will have no man in my boat,” said Starbuck, “who is not afraid of a whale.”", "\"나는 고래를 두려워하지 않는 자는 내 보트에 태우지 않겠다.\""], ["By this, he seemed to mean, not only that the most reliable and useful courage was that which arises from the fair estimation of the encountered peril, but that an utterly fearless man is a far more dangerous comrade than a coward.", "스타벅은 이렇게 말했다. 이 말로써 그가 뜻한 바는, 가장 믿음직하고 쓸모 있는 용기란 마주친 위험을 공정하게 가늠하는 데서 솟아나는 용기라는 것뿐만 아니라, 전혀 두려움을 모르는 사람이야말로 겁쟁이보다 훨씬 더 위험한 동료라는 것인 듯했다."]], [["“Aye, aye,”", "\"옳소, 옳소.\""], ["said Stubb, the second mate, “Starbuck, there, is as careful a man as you’ll find anywhere in this fishery.”", "이등항해사 스터브가 말했다. \"저기 저 스타벅은 이 포경업 어디를 가도 찾아보기 힘들 만큼 신중한 사람이지.\""], ["But we shall ere long see what that word “careful” precisely means when used by a man like Stubb, or almost any other whale hunter.", "그러나 스터브 같은 사람이나, 거의 모든 다른 고래잡이가 쓸 때 그 \"신중한\"이라는 말이 정확히 무엇을 뜻하는지는 머지않아 알게 될 것이다."]], [["Starbuck was no crusader after perils; in him courage was not a sentiment; but a thing simply useful to him, and always at hand upon all mortally practical occasions.", "스타벅은 위험을 찾아 나서는 십자군 같은 사람이 아니었다. 그에게 용기란 하나의 감정이 아니라, 그저 쓸모 있는 것이었으며, 목숨이 걸린 온갖 실제적 상황에서 늘 곁에 두는 것이었다."], ["Besides, he thought, perhaps, that in this business of whaling, courage was one of the great staple outfits of the ship, like her beef and her bread, and not to be foolishly wasted.", "게다가 그는 아마도, 이 포경이라는 일에서 용기란 쇠고기나 빵처럼 배가 갖추어야 할 중요한 비품 가운데 하나이므로 어리석게 낭비해서는 안 된다고 여겼던 것이다."], ["Wherefore he had no fancy for lowering for whales after sun-down; nor for persisting in fighting a fish that too much persisted in fighting him.", "그래서 그는 해가 진 뒤에 고래를 잡으러 보트를 내리는 것을 달가워하지 않았고, 자기에게 너무 끈질기게 맞서 싸우는 고래와 끝까지 싸우는 것도 좋아하지 않았다. 스타벅은 이렇게 생각했던 것이다."], ["For, thought Starbuck, I am here in this critical ocean to kill whales for my living, and not to be killed by them for theirs; and that hundreds of men had been so killed Starbuck well knew.", "나는 이 험난한 바다에서 내 밥벌이를 위해 고래를 죽이러 온 것이지, 고래의 밥벌이를 위해 그들에게 죽임을 당하러 온 것이 아니다. 그리고 그렇게 죽임을 당한 사람이 수백 명에 이른다는 것을 스타벅은 잘 알고 있었다."], ["What doom was his own father’s?", "그 자신의 아버지의 운명은 어떠했던가?"], ["Where, in the bottomless deeps, could he find the torn limbs of his brother?", "저 밑바닥 없는 심연 어디에서 그는 형제의 찢긴 사지를 찾을 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["With memories like these in him, and, moreover, given to a certain superstitiousness, as has been said; the courage of this Starbuck which could, nevertheless, still flourish, must indeed have been extreme.", "이런 기억들을 품은 데다, 앞서 말했듯 어떤 미신적 기질까지 지닌 그였으니, 그럼에도 여전히 꽃피울 수 있었던 이 스타벅의 용기란 참으로 대단한 것이었음이 틀림없다."], ["But it was not in reasonable nature that a man so organized, and with such terrible experiences and remembrances as he had; it was not in nature that these things should fail in latently engendering an element in him, which, under suitable circumstances, would break out from its confinement, and burn all his courage up.", "그러나 그처럼 기질이 형성되고, 그토록 끔찍한 경험과 기억을 지닌 사람에게서 이런 것들이, 적절한 상황이 닥치면 그 갇혀 있던 데서 터져 나와 그의 모든 용기를 불살라 버릴 어떤 요소를 은밀히 잉태하지 않으리라는 것은 이치에 맞는 자연의 섭리가 아니었다."], ["And brave as he might be, it was that sort of bravery chiefly, visible in some intrepid men, which, while generally abiding firm in the conflict with seas, or winds, or whales, or any of the ordinary irrational horrors of the world, yet cannot withstand those more terrific, because more spiritual terrors, which sometimes menace you from the concentrating brow of an enraged and mighty man.", "그리고 그가 아무리 용감하다 한들, 그것은 주로 어떤 대담한 사람들에게서 보이는 그런 종류의 용기였으니, 바다나 바람이나 고래와 맞서거나, 세상의 여느 비이성적인 공포와 맞설 때는 대체로 굳건히 버티면서도, 때때로 격노한 강대한 사람의 응집된 이맛살에서 그대를 위협해 오는, 더 무시무시한 — 더욱 영적인 까닭에 무시무시한 — 공포에는 끝내 견뎌 내지 못하는 그런 용기였다."]], [["But were the coming narrative to reveal in any instance, the complete abasement of poor Starbuck’s fortitude, scarce might I have the heart to write it; for it is a thing most sorrowful, nay shocking, to expose the fall of valour in the soul.", "그러나 만일 앞으로 펼쳐질 이야기가 어느 대목에선가 가련한 스타벅의 불굴의 정신이 완전히 무너져 내리는 모습을 드러낸다면, 나는 그것을 차마 쓸 마음이 좀처럼 들지 않을 것이다. 영혼 속에서 용맹이 추락하는 모습을 드러내 보이는 일은 더없이 슬픈, 아니, 충격적인 일이기 때문이다."], ["Men may seem detestable as joint stock-companies and nations; knaves, fools, and murderers there may be; men may have mean and meagre faces; but man, in the ideal, is so noble and so sparkling, such a grand and glowing creature, that over any ignominious blemish in him all his fellows should run to throw their costliest robes.", "사람들은 합자회사나 국가처럼 가증스러워 보일 수도 있다. 악당과 바보와 살인자가 있을 수도 있다."], ["That immaculate manliness we feel within ourselves, so far within us, that it remains intact though all the outer character seem gone; bleeds with keenest anguish at the undraped spectacle of a valor-ruined man.", "사람들은 비열하고 빈약한 얼굴을 하고 있을 수도 있다. 그러나 인간은, 그 이상(理想)에서 보자면 너무나 고귀하고 너무나 찬란하며, 너무나 위대하고 빛나는 피조물이어서, 그에게 있는 어떤 치욕스러운 흠이라도 모든 동료들이 달려와 자신들의 가장 값진 예복을 던져 덮어 주어야 마땅한 것이다."], ["Nor can piety itself, at such a shameful sight, completely stifle her upbraidings against the permitting stars.", "우리가 우리 자신 안에서, 우리 깊은 곳에서 느끼는 그 티 없는 인간다움은, 바깥의 모든 인격이 사라진 듯 보일지라도 고스란히 남아 있어, 용맹이 무너진 사람의 헐벗은 광경 앞에서 더없이 날카로운 고통으로 피 흘린다."], ["But this august dignity I treat of, is not the dignity of kings and robes, but that abounding dignity which has no robed investiture.", "그처럼 부끄러운 광경 앞에서는 경건함조차도, 그것을 허락한 별들을 향한 책망을 온전히 억누를 수 없다. 그러나 내가 다루는 이 장엄한 존엄은 왕과 예복의 존엄이 아니라, 예복을 걸치지 않은 그 넘쳐흐르는 존엄이다."], ["Thou shalt see it shining in the arm that wields a pick or drives a spike; that democratic dignity which, on all hands, radiates without end from God; Himself!", "그대는 그것이 곡괭이를 휘두르거나 못을 박는 팔에서 빛나는 것을 보게 될 것이다. 그 민주적 존엄은 사방으로, 끝없이 하느님으로부터 뻗어 나오는 것이니, 하느님 바로 그분에게서!"], ["The great God absolute!", "절대자이신 위대한 하느님!"], ["The centre and circumference of all democracy!", "모든 민주주의의 중심이자 둘레!"], ["His omnipresence, our divine equality!", "그분의 편재(遍在)야말로 우리의 신성한 평등이다!"]], [["If, then, to meanest mariners, and renegades and castaways, I shall hereafter ascribe high qualities, though dark; weave round them tragic graces; if even the most mournful, perchance the most abased, among them all, shall at times lift himself to the exalted mounts; if I shall touch that workman’s arm with some ethereal light; if I shall spread a rainbow over his disastrous set of sun; then against all mortal critics bear me out in it, thou just Spirit of Equality, which hast spread one royal mantle of humanity over all my kind!", "그러므로 만일 내가 앞으로 가장 미천한 뱃사람들과 배교자들과 표류자들에게, 비록 어두울지언정 고귀한 자질을 부여하고, 그들 둘레에 비극적인 기품을 엮어 넣는다면, 만일 그들 모두 가운데 가장 비통하고, 어쩌면 가장 천하게 추락한 자조차도 때로는 스스로를 드높은 봉우리로 들어 올린다면, 만일 내가 저 노동자의 팔에 어떤 천상의 빛을 입힌다면, 만일 내가 그의 재앙으로 저무는 해 위에 무지개를 펼쳐 놓는다면, 그때는 온 세상의 비판자들에 맞서 나를 끝까지 옹호해 다오, 그대 공정한 평등의 정신이여, 내 동족 모두에게 인류라는 하나의 왕다운 망토를 펼쳐 덮어 준 그대여!"], ["Bear me out in it, thou great democratic God!", "나를 끝까지 옹호해 다오, 그대 위대한 민주의 하느님이여!"], ["who didst not refuse to the swart convict, Bunyan, the pale, poetic pearl; Thou who didst clothe with doubly hammered leaves of finest gold, the stumped and paupered arm of old Cervantes; Thou who didst pick up Andrew Jackson from the pebbles; who didst hurl him upon a war-horse; who didst thunder him higher than a throne!", "거무튀튀한 죄수 버니언에게 창백하고 시적인 진주를 마다하지 않으셨던 그대여, 늙은 세르반테스의 잘리고 가난해진 팔을 더없이 고운 황금을 두 번 두드려 편 잎사귀로 입히셨던 그대여, 앤드루 잭슨을 자갈밭에서 주워 올려, 군마(軍馬) 위로 내던지시고, 옥좌보다 더 높이 천둥처럼 끌어올리셨던 그대여!"], ["Thou who, in all Thy mighty, earthly marchings, ever cullest Thy selectest champions from the kingly commons; bear me out in it, O God!", "그대의 모든 위대한 지상의 행진 속에서, 늘 왕다운 평민들 가운데서 가장 빼어난 투사들을 골라내시는 그대여, 나를 끝까지 옹호해 다오, 오 하느님이여!"]], [["# CHAPTER 27. Knights and Squires.", "# CHAPTER 27. Knights and Squires."]], [["Stubb was the second mate.", "스터브(Stubb)는 이등항해사였다."], ["He was a native of Cape Cod; and hence, according to local usage, was called a Cape-Cod-man.", "그는 케이프코드 태생이었고, 그래서 그곳 관습에 따라 케이프코드 사람이라 불렸다. 천하태평한 사내였다."], ["A happy-go-lucky; neither craven nor valiant; taking perils as they came with an indifferent air; and while engaged in the most imminent crisis of the chase, toiling away, calm and collected as a journeyman joiner engaged for the year.", "겁쟁이도 아니고 용맹한 자도 아니어서, 위험이 닥쳐오면 그저 무심한 태도로 받아들였다. 추격이 가장 위급한 절정에 이른 와중에도 그는 일 년 계약으로 고용된 목수가 일하듯 차분하고 침착하게 묵묵히 제 일을 해냈다."], ["Good-humored, easy, and careless, he presided over his whale-boat as if the most deadly encounter were but a dinner, and his crew all invited guests.", "성품이 쾌활하고 느긋하고 무던하여, 가장 치명적인 충돌의 순간조차 한 끼 만찬쯤으로, 그의 선원들을 모두 초대받은 손님쯤으로 여기는 양 자신의 포경 보트를 다스렸다."], ["He was as particular about the comfortable arrangement of his part of the boat, as an old stage-driver is about the snugness of his box.", "그는 보트에서 제 몫의 자리를 편안하게 꾸미는 일에 어찌나 까다로웠던지, 마치 늙은 역마차 마부가 제 마부석의 아늑함에 신경 쓰는 것과 같았다."], ["When close to the whale, in the very death-lock of the fight, he handled his unpitying lance coolly and off-handedly, as a whistling tinker his hammer.", "고래에 바싹 다가가 사투의 한복판에 들어섰을 때에도, 그는 휘파람 부는 땜장이가 망치를 다루듯 그 무자비한 작살창을 태연하고 거침없이 휘둘렀다."], ["He would hum over his old rigadig tunes while flank and flank with the most exasperated monster.", "가장 약이 오른 괴물과 옆구리를 맞댄 채로도 그는 케케묵은 옛 뱃노래 가락을 흥얼거리곤 했다."], ["Long usage had, for this Stubb, converted the jaws of death into an easy chair.", "오랜 세월의 단련은 이 스터브에게 죽음의 아가리를 안락의자로 바꾸어 놓았다. 그가 죽음 그 자체를 어떻게 생각했는지는 알 길이 없다."], ["What he thought of death itself, there is no telling.", "도대체 그것을 생각이나 해 보았는지조차 의문일지 모른다."], ["Whether he ever thought of it at all, might be a question; but, if he ever did chance to cast his mind that way after a comfortable dinner, no doubt, like a good sailor, he took it to be a sort of call of the watch to tumble aloft, and bestir themselves there, about something which he would find out when he obeyed the order, and not sooner.", "그러나 혹시라도 푸짐한 만찬을 마친 뒤 마음을 그쪽으로 돌리는 일이 있었다면, 틀림없이 그는 훌륭한 선원답게 그것을 일종의 당직 호출쯤으로, 곧 돛대 위로 후딱 올라가 그곳에서 무언가를 두고 부산을 떨라는 신호쯤으로 여겼을 것이다. 그 무언가가 무엇인지는 명령에 따라 올라가 본 뒤에야 알게 될 일이지, 그 전에는 알 수 없는 노릇이라고 말이다."]], [["What, perhaps, with other things, made Stubb such an easy-going, unfearing man, so cheerily trudging off with the burden of life in a world full of grave pedlars, all bowed to the ground with their packs; what helped to bring about that almost impious good-humor of his; that thing must have been his pipe.", "다른 여러 이유와 더불어, 짐 보따리에 짓눌려 땅바닥에 허리를 구부린 음울한 행상꾼들로 가득한 세상에서 스터브를 그토록 느긋하고 두려움 없는 사내로, 삶의 짐을 짊어진 채 그토록 명랑하게 터덜터덜 걸어가는 사내로 만든 것이 무엇이었는가. 그 거의 불경하다 할 만한 쾌활함을 빚어내는 데 한몫한 것이 무엇이었는가."], ["For, like his nose, his short, black little pipe was one of the regular features of his face.", "그것은 틀림없이 그의 파이프였을 것이다. 그의 코와 마찬가지로, 짤막한 검은 그 파이프는 그의 얼굴을 이루는 변함없는 한 부분이었다."], ["You would almost as soon have expected him to turn out of his bunk without his nose as without his pipe.", "그가 파이프 없이 침상에서 일어나리라 기대하느니 차라리 코 없이 일어나리라 기대하는 편이 나았을 정도다."], ["He kept a whole row of pipes there ready loaded, stuck in a rack, within easy reach of his hand; and, whenever he turned in, he smoked them all out in succession, lighting one from the other to the end of the chapter; then loading them again to be in readiness anew.", "그는 미리 담배를 채워 둔 파이프를 한 줄로 가지런히 걸이에 꽂아 손닿는 자리에 두었다. 그래서 잠자리에 들 때마다 하나에 불을 붙여 그것으로 다음 것에 옮겨 붙이는 식으로 끝까지 차례차례 다 피웠고, 그러고는 다시 담배를 채워 새로 채비를 해 두었다."], ["For, when Stubb dressed, instead of first putting his legs into his trowsers, he put his pipe into his mouth.", "스터브는 옷을 입을 때 바지에 먼저 다리를 꿰는 대신 입에 파이프부터 물었으니 말이다."]], [["I say this continual smoking must have been one cause, at least, of his peculiar disposition; for every one knows that this earthly air, whether ashore or afloat, is terribly infected with the nameless miseries of the numberless mortals who have died exhaling it; and as in time of the cholera, some people go about with a camphorated handkerchief to their mouths; so, likewise, against all mortal tribulations, Stubb’s tobacco smoke might have operated as a sort of disinfecting agent.", "내 말하건대, 이 끊임없는 흡연이야말로 적어도 그의 독특한 기질을 만든 한 가지 원인이었음이 분명하다. 누구나 알다시피 이 지상의 공기는, 뭍에 있든 물 위에 있든, 그것을 내뿜으며 죽어 간 수많은 인간들의 이름 없는 비참함에 끔찍하게 오염되어 있기 때문이다. 콜레라가 도는 때에 어떤 사람들이 입에 장뇌 적신 손수건을 대고 다니듯, 그와 마찬가지로 죽을 운명의 온갖 환난에 맞서 스터브의 담배 연기는 일종의 소독제 노릇을 했는지도 모른다."]], [["The third mate was Flask, a native of Tisbury, in Martha’s Vineyard.", "삼등항해사는 플래스크(Flask)로, 마서스 비니어드의 티스버리 태생이었다. 키가 작고 다부지며 혈색 좋은 젊은 친구였는데, 고래에 관한 한 무척이나 호전적이어서, 어쩐 일인지 저 거대한 리바이어던(Leviathan)들이 자기를 개인적으로, 또 대대로 모욕해 왔다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["A short, stout, ruddy young fellow, very pugnacious concerning whales, who somehow seemed to think that the great leviathans had personally and hereditarily affronted him; and therefore it was a sort of point of honor with him, to destroy them whenever encountered.", "그래서 마주칠 때마다 그것들을 없애 버리는 일이 그에게는 일종의 명예가 걸린 문제였다. 그는 고래의 장엄한 거구와 신비로운 습성이 자아내는 수많은 경이에 대한 경외감을 깡그리 잃은 사람이었다."], ["So utterly lost was he to all sense of reverence for the many marvels of their majestic bulk and mystic ways; and so dead to anything like an apprehension of any possible danger from encountering them; that in his poor opinion, the wondrous whale was but a species of magnified mouse, or at least water-rat, requiring only a little circumvention and some small application of time and trouble in order to kill and boil.", "그것들과 마주치는 데서 비롯될지 모를 위험에 대한 두려움 따위도 아예 죽어 버린 사람이었다. 그의 어리석은 소견으로는, 저 경이로운 고래란 그저 몸집을 부풀린 생쥐, 적어도 물쥐 정도의 종류에 지나지 않아서, 죽여서 삶아 내자면 약간의 술수와 자그마한 수고와 품만 들이면 그만인 짐승이었다."], ["This ignorant, unconscious fearlessness of his made him a little waggish in the matter of whales; he followed these fish for the fun of it; and a three years’ voyage round Cape Horn was only a jolly joke that lasted that length of time.", "이 무지하고 무의식적인 두려움 없음 탓에 그는 고래에 관해서라면 다소 익살스러운 데가 있었다. 그는 재미 삼아 이 물고기들을 쫓아다녔다."], ["As a carpenter’s nails are divided into wrought nails and cut nails; so mankind may be similarly divided.", "케이프혼을 도는 삼 년의 항해도 그에게는 그 기간만큼 이어지는 한바탕 유쾌한 농담일 뿐이었다. 목수의 못이 단조한 못과 잘라 만든 못으로 나뉘듯, 인류 또한 그렇게 나뉠 수 있을 것이다."], ["Little Flask was one of the wrought ones; made to clinch tight and last long.", "작은 플래스크는 단조한 못 가운데 하나였으니, 단단히 박혀 오래 버티도록 만들어진 못이었다. 페쿼드(Pequod)호 선상에서 사람들은 그를 킹포스트라 불렀다."], ["They called him King-Post on board of the Pequod; because, in form, he could be well likened to the short, square timber known by that name in Arctic whalers; and which by the means of many radiating side timbers inserted into it, serves to brace the ship against the icy concussions of those battering seas.", "생김새로 보아 그가 북극 포경선에서 그 이름으로 불리는, 짧고 네모진 기둥에 빗댈 만했기 때문이다. 그 기둥은 사방으로 뻗어 박힌 여러 곁기둥의 도움으로, 사정없이 들이치는 저 빙해의 충격에 맞서 배를 떠받치는 역할을 한다."]], [["Now these three mates—Starbuck, Stubb, and Flask, were momentous men.", "자, 이 세 항해사, 곧 스타벅(Starbuck)과 스터브와 플래스크는 막중한 인물들이었다."], ["They it was who by universal prescription commanded three of the Pequod’s boats as headsmen.", "보편적인 관례에 따라 페쿼드호의 보트 세 척을 선두잡이로서 지휘하는 자들이 바로 이들이었다."], ["In that grand order of battle in which Captain Ahab would probably marshal his forces to descend on the whales, these three headsmen were as captains of companies.", "에이해브(Ahab) 선장이 고래들에게 덮쳐들기 위해 자신의 병력을 배치할 저 웅대한 전투 대형에서, 이 세 선두잡이는 중대를 거느린 대장과도 같았다."], ["Or, being armed with their long keen whaling spears, they were as a picked trio of lancers; even as the harpooneers were flingers of javelins.", "또한 길고 날카로운 포경 창으로 무장한 그들은 선발된 세 창기병과도 같았으니, 마치 작살잡이들이 투창을 던지는 자들이었던 것과 같았다."]], [["And since in this famous fishery, each mate or headsman, like a Gothic Knight of old, is always accompanied by his boat-steerer or harpooneer, who in certain conjunctures provides him with a fresh lance, when the former one has been badly twisted, or elbowed in the assault; and moreover, as there generally subsists between the two, a close intimacy and friendliness; it is therefore but meet, that in this place we set down who the Pequod’s harpooneers were, and to what headsman each of them belonged.", "그리고 이 이름난 포경업에서는, 각 항해사 또는 선두잡이가 옛 고딕 기사처럼 언제나 자신의 키잡이, 곧 작살잡이를 동반한다. 그 작살잡이는 어떤 상황에서 앞서 쓰던 창이 공격 도중에 심하게 휘거나 비틀려 버렸을 때 새 창을 그에게 건네주는 역할을 맡는다. 게다가 이 둘 사이에는 대개 긴밀한 친밀함과 우정이 자리 잡고 있다. 그러므로 이 자리에서 페쿼드호의 작살잡이들이 누구였는지, 또 그들 각자가 어느 선두잡이에게 속해 있었는지를 적어 두는 것이 마땅하겠다."]], [["First of all was Queequeg, whom Starbuck, the chief mate, had selected for his squire.", "무엇보다 먼저 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 있었으니, 일등항해사 스타벅이 자신의 종자로 택한 사람이었다."], ["But Queequeg is already known.", "그러나 퀴케그는 이미 알려진 인물이다."]], [["Next was Tashtego, an unmixed Indian from Gay Head, the most westerly promontory of Martha’s Vineyard, where there still exists the last remnant of a village of red men, which has long supplied the neighboring island of Nantucket with many of her most daring harpooneers.", "다음은 태시테고(Tashtego)였다. 마서스 비니어드의 가장 서쪽 곶인 게이헤드 출신의 순수 혈통 인디언이었다."], ["In the fishery, they usually go by the generic name of Gay-Headers.", "그곳에는 아직도 붉은 피부 사람들이 살던 마을의 마지막 자취가 남아 있는데, 그 마을은 오래도록 이웃 섬 낸터킷(Nantucket)에 가장 대담한 작살잡이들을 숱하게 공급해 왔다."], ["Tashtego’s long, lean, sable hair, his high cheek bones, and black rounding eyes—for an Indian, Oriental in their largeness, but Antarctic in their glittering expression—all this sufficiently proclaimed him an inheritor of the unvitiated blood of those proud warrior hunters, who, in quest of the great New England moose, had scoured, bow in hand, the aboriginal forests of the main.", "포경업에서 그들은 보통 게이헤더라는 통칭으로 불린다. 태시테고의 길고 야윈 칠흑 같은 머리카락, 높이 솟은 광대뼈, 둥글고 검은 두 눈은 — 인디언치고는 동양인처럼 컸으나 그 반짝이는 표정만은 남극처럼 차가웠으니 — 이 모든 것이, 그가 일찍이 활을 들고 저 거대한 뉴잉글랜드 큰사슴을 좇아 본토의 원시림을 누비던 자랑스러운 사냥 전사들의 순수한 피를 물려받은 후예임을 충분히 드러내고 있었다."], ["But no longer snuffing in the trail of the wild beasts of the woodland, Tashtego now hunted in the wake of the great whales of the sea; the unerring harpoon of the son fitly replacing the infallible arrow of the sires.", "그러나 이제 더는 숲속 야수의 발자취를 좇아 코를 킁킁대지 않고, 태시테고는 바다의 거대한 고래들이 남긴 항적을 쫓아 사냥했다. 아들의 빗나감 없는 작살이 선조들의 어김없는 화살을 알맞게 대신한 셈이었다."], ["To look at the tawny brawn of his lithe snaky limbs, you would almost have credited the superstitions of some of the earlier Puritans, and half-believed this wild Indian to be a son of the Prince of the Powers of the Air.", "뱀처럼 유연한 그의 사지에 어린 황갈색 근육을 바라보고 있노라면, 초기 청교도들의 미신을 거의 곧이듣게 되어, 이 거친 인디언이야말로 공중 권세의 군왕의 아들이 아닐까 반쯤 믿게 될 지경이었다."], ["Tashtego was Stubb the second mate’s squire.", "태시테고는 이등항해사 스터브의 종자였다."]], [["Third among the harpooneers was Daggoo, a gigantic, coal-black negro-savage, with a lion-like tread—an Ahasuerus to behold.", "작살잡이 가운데 셋째는 대구(Daggoo)였다. 거대한 몸집에 석탄처럼 새까만 흑인 야만인으로, 사자처럼 당당히 걸음을 옮기는 — 보기에 아하수에로 같은 사내였다."], ["Suspended from his ears were two golden hoops, so large that the sailors called them ring-bolts, and would talk of securing the top-sail halyards to them.", "그의 귀에는 황금 고리 두 개가 매달려 있었는데 어찌나 컸던지 선원들은 그것을 고리볼트라 부르며 거기에 윗돛 마룻줄을 묶어 두자고 농을 던지곤 했다."], ["In his youth Daggoo had voluntarily shipped on board of a whaler, lying in a lonely bay on his native coast.", "젊은 시절 대구는 제 고향 해안의 외딴 만에 정박해 있던 어느 포경선에 스스로 올라탔다."], ["And never having been anywhere in the world but in Africa, Nantucket, and the pagan harbors most frequented by whalemen; and having now led for many years the bold life of the fishery in the ships of owners uncommonly heedful of what manner of men they shipped; Daggoo retained all his barbaric virtues, and erect as a giraffe, moved about the decks in all the pomp of six feet five in his socks.", "그리고 아프리카와 낸터킷, 그리고 고래잡이들이 가장 자주 드나드는 이교도 항구들 말고는 세상 어디에도 가 본 적이 없었으며, 이제는 여러 해 동안, 어떤 부류의 사람을 태우는지에 유달리 신경 쓰는 선주들의 배에서 포경업의 대담한 삶을 이끌어 왔던 터라, 대구는 자신의 야만적 미덕을 고스란히 간직하고 있었다. 그리하여 그는 기린처럼 꼿꼿하게, 양말만 신은 채로도 6피트 5인치(약 1.95미터)에 이르는 위풍당당함을 뽐내며 갑판을 누비고 다녔다."], ["There was a corporeal humility in looking up at him; and a white man standing before him seemed a white flag come to beg truce of a fortress.", "그를 올려다보노라면 절로 몸이 겸손해졌다. 그의 앞에 선 백인은 마치 요새에 휴전을 구걸하러 온 백기처럼 보였다."], ["Curious to tell, this imperial negro, Ahasuerus Daggoo, was the Squire of little Flask, who looked like a chess-man beside him.", "신기하게도 이 제왕 같은 흑인, 아하수에로 같은 대구는 그의 곁에 서면 장기짝처럼 보이는 작은 플래스크의 종자였다."], ["As for the residue of the Pequod’s company, be it said, that at the present day not one in two of the many thousand men before the mast employed in the American whale fishery, are Americans born, though pretty nearly all the officers are.", "페쿼드호 선원의 나머지에 관해 말하자면, 오늘날 미국 포경업에 종사하는 수천 명의 평선원 가운데 미국 태생은 둘에 하나꼴도 되지 않는다는 점을 짚어 두어야겠다. 다만 사관들은 거의 모두가 미국 태생이다."], ["Herein it is the same with the American whale fishery as with the American army and military and merchant navies, and the engineering forces employed in the construction of the American Canals and Railroads.", "이 점에서 미국 포경업은 미국 육군과 군함대와 상선대, 그리고 미국의 운하와 철도 건설에 동원된 공병대와 매한가지다. 매한가지라고 말하는 까닭은, 이 모든 경우에 미국 태생 사람들이 두뇌를 넉넉히 대고 나머지 세계가 그에 못지않게 후하게 근육을 대 주기 때문이다."], ["The same, I say, because in all these cases the native American liberally provides the brains, the rest of the world as generously supplying the muscles.", "이 포경 선원들 가운데 적지 않은 수가 아조레스 제도 출신인데, 그곳은 출항하는 낸터킷 포경선들이 그 바위 해안의 억센 농민들로 선원을 보충하려고 자주 들르는 곳이다."], ["No small number of these whaling seamen belong to the Azores, where the outward bound Nantucket whalers frequently touch to augment their crews from the hardy peasants of those rocky shores.", "마찬가지로 헐이나 런던에서 출항하는 그린란드 포경선들은 셰틀랜드 제도에 들러 선원 정원을 가득 채운다. 귀항하는 길에는 그들을 다시 그곳에 내려놓는다."], ["In like manner, the Greenland whalers sailing out of Hull or London, put in at the Shetland Islands, to receive the full complement of their crew.", "어찌 된 영문인지 알 수는 없으나, 섬사람들이 가장 뛰어난 고래잡이가 되는 듯하다. 페쿼드호의 선원도 거의 다 섬사람이었다."], ["Upon the passage homewards, they drop them there again. How it is, there is no telling, but Islanders seem to make the best whalemen.", "나는 그런 자들을 고립자라 부르는데, 인류 공동의 대륙을 인정하지 않고 저마다 자신만의 별개의 대륙에서 살아가는 까닭이다."], ["They were nearly all Islanders in the Pequod, Isolatoes too, I call such, not acknowledging the common continent of men, but each Isolato living on a separate continent of his own.", "그러나 이제 하나의 용골을 따라 연방을 이룬 이 고립자들은 어떤 무리였던가! 바다의 모든 섬과 땅의 모든 끝에서 모여든 아나카르시스 클로츠의 사절단이라 할 만했으니, 늙은 에이해브를 따라 페쿼드호에 올라, 그들 가운데 그리 많은 수가 다시는 돌아오지 못할 저 심판대 앞에 세상의 원한을 호소하러 가는 길이었다."], ["Yet now, federated along one keel, what a set these Isolatoes were!", "검은 꼬마 핍(Pip) — 그는 끝내 돌아오지 못했다. 오, 아니지!"], ["An Anacharsis Clootz deputation from all the isles of the sea, and all the ends of the earth, accompanying Old Ahab in the Pequod to lay the world’s grievances before that bar from which not very many of them ever come back.", "그는 먼저 떠나갔다."], ["Black Little Pip—he never did—oh, no!", "가엾은 앨라배마 소년이여!"], ["he went before. Poor Alabama boy!", "음산한 페쿼드호의 앞갑판에서 그대들은 머지않아 그가 탬버린을 두드리는 모습을 보게 되리라."], ["On the grim Pequod’s forecastle, ye shall ere long see him, beating his tambourine; prelusive of the eternal time, when sent for, to the great quarter-deck on high, he was bid strike in with angels, and beat his tambourine in glory; called a coward here, hailed a hero there!", "그것은 영원의 시간을 알리는 전주곡이었으니, 부름을 받아 저 높은 곳 거대한 뒷갑판으로 불려 가, 천사들과 한데 어울려 영광 속에 탬버린을 두드리라는 명을 받을 그날의 전조였다. 이곳에서는 겁쟁이라 불렸으나, 저곳에서는 영웅이라 환호받으리라!"]], [["# CHAPTER 28. Ahab.", "# CHAPTER 28. Ahab."]], [["For several days after leaving Nantucket, nothing above hatches was seen of Captain Ahab.", "낸터킷(Nantucket)을 떠난 뒤로 여러 날 동안, 갑판 위에서는 에이해브(Ahab) 선장의 모습을 좀처럼 볼 수 없었다."], ["The mates regularly relieved each other at the watches, and for aught that could be seen to the contrary, they seemed to be the only commanders of the ship; only they sometimes issued from the cabin with orders so sudden and peremptory, that after all it was plain they but commanded vicariously.", "항해사들은 정해진 차례에 따라 번갈아 당직을 섰고, 겉으로 드러난 것만 보아서는 그들이 이 배의 유일한 지휘관인 것처럼 보였다. 다만 이따금 그들은 너무도 갑작스럽고 단호한 명령을 들고 선실에서 나오곤 했는데, 결국 그들이 누군가를 대신하여 명령을 내리고 있을 뿐이라는 사실이 분명히 드러나곤 했다."], ["Yes, their supreme lord and dictator was there, though hitherto unseen by any eyes not permitted to penetrate into the now sacred retreat of the cabin.", "그렇다, 그들의 지고한 주군이자 독재자가 거기 있었던 것이다. 다만 지금껏 그 신성한 은둔처가 된 선실 안을 들여다볼 허락을 받지 못한 어떤 눈에도 그는 보이지 않았을 뿐이다."]], [["Every time I ascended to the deck from my watches below, I instantly gazed aft to mark if any strange face were visible; for my first vague disquietude touching the unknown captain, now in the seclusion of the sea, became almost a perturbation.", "아래에서 당직을 마치고 갑판으로 올라올 때마다, 나는 곧장 고물 쪽을 바라보며 낯선 얼굴이 보이는지 살피곤 했다. 이제 망망대해의 고립 속에 들어앉은 미지의 선장에 대해 처음에는 막연하던 내 불안이 거의 동요라 할 만한 것으로 자라났기 때문이다."], ["This was strangely heightened at times by the ragged Elijah’s diabolical incoherences uninvitedly recurring to me, with a subtle energy I could not have before conceived of.", "게다가 이 불안은, 누더기를 걸친 일라이저(Elijah)의 그 악마 같은 횡설수설이 청하지도 않았는데 자꾸만 내 머릿속에 되살아남으로써 이따금 기이하게 더해졌으니, 그것은 내가 전에는 상상조차 할 수 없었던 미묘한 힘으로 다가왔다."], ["But poorly could I withstand them, much as in other moods I was almost ready to smile at the solemn whimsicalities of that outlandish prophet of the wharves.", "그러나 나는 그것들을 거의 견뎌 내지 못했는데, 다른 기분일 때에는 그 부두의 괴상한 예언자가 늘어놓던 엄숙한 변덕에 거의 미소를 지을 뻔하기도 했건만 말이다."], ["But whatever it was of apprehensiveness or uneasiness—to call it so—which I felt, yet whenever I came to look about me in the ship, it seemed against all warrantry to cherish such emotions.", "그러나 내가 느낀 것이 무엇이었든 — 두려움이라 하든 불안이라 하든 — 막상 배 안을 둘러볼 때면, 그런 감정을 품는 것이 온갖 이치에 어긋나는 것처럼 여겨졌다."], ["For though the harpooneers, with the great body of the crew, were a far more barbaric, heathenish, and motley set than any of the tame merchant-ship companies which my previous experiences had made me acquainted with, still I ascribed this—and rightly ascribed it—to the fierce uniqueness of the very nature of that wild Scandinavian vocation in which I had so abandonedly embarked.", "작살잡이들은 물론 선원 대다수가, 내가 전에 겪어 익히 알던 그 어떤 점잖은 상선의 무리보다 훨씬 더 야만스럽고 이교도적이며 잡다한 패거리였지만, 나는 그것을 — 그리고 마땅히 그렇게 보았듯이 — 내가 그토록 무모하게 몸을 내던진 저 거친 스칸디나비아식 직업의 본성 자체가 지닌 사나운 독특함 탓으로 돌렸던 것이다."], ["But it was especially the aspect of the three chief officers of the ship, the mates, which was most forcibly calculated to allay these colourless misgivings, and induce confidence and cheerfulness in every presentment of the voyage.", "그러나 이 빛바랜 의심들을 가장 강력하게 가라앉히고, 이번 항해의 온갖 정경에 대해 신뢰와 명랑함을 불러일으킨 것은 무엇보다도 이 배의 세 수석 사관, 곧 항해사들의 면모였다."], ["Three better, more likely sea-officers and men, each in his own different way, could not readily be found, and they were every one of them Americans; a Nantucketer, a Vineyarder, a Cape man.", "이들보다 더 낫고, 더 미더운 바다의 사관이자 사내를 각기 제 나름의 방식대로 그리 쉽게 찾아내기는 어려웠을 것이다. 그리고 그들은 하나같이 미국인이었으니, 낸터킷 사람과 비니어드 사람과 케이프 사람이었다."], ["Now, it being Christmas when the ship shot from out her harbor, for a space we had biting Polar weather, though all the time running away from it to the southward; and by every degree and minute of latitude which we sailed, gradually leaving that merciless winter, and all its intolerable weather behind us.", "그런데 배가 항구를 박차고 나선 때가 마침 크리스마스 무렵이었던지라, 한동안 우리는 살을 에는 극지의 날씨를 겪었다. 다만 내내 그것을 피해 남쪽으로 달아나는 중이었으니, 우리가 항해한 위도의 한 도, 한 분마다 그 무자비한 겨울과 그 견딜 수 없는 날씨를 차츰 뒤로 떨쳐 내고 있었다."], ["It was one of those less lowering, but still grey and gloomy enough mornings of the transition, when with a fair wind the ship was rushing through the water with a vindictive sort of leaping and melancholy rapidity, that as I mounted to the deck at the call of the forenoon watch, so soon as I levelled my glance towards the taffrail, foreboding shivers ran over me.", "그날은 그 전환기의 아침들 가운데 하나로, 음울함이 조금 덜하긴 했으나 여전히 잿빛이고 어둑한 아침이었다. 순풍을 받은 배가 어딘가 복수심에 찬 듯한 도약과 우울한 속도로 물살을 헤치며 내달리고 있을 때, 오전 당직을 부르는 소리에 갑판으로 올라가 고물 난간 쪽으로 시선을 던지는 순간, 불길한 전율이 온몸을 훑고 지나갔다."], ["Reality outran apprehension; Captain Ahab stood upon his quarter-deck.", "현실이 예감을 앞질렀던 것이다. 에이해브 선장이 후갑판 위에 서 있었다."]], [["There seemed no sign of common bodily illness about him, nor of the recovery from any.", "그에게서는 흔한 육체의 병색도, 그런 병에서 회복된 흔적도 보이지 않았다."], ["He looked like a man cut away from the stake, when the fire has overrunningly wasted all the limbs without consuming them, or taking away one particle from their compacted aged robustness.", "그는 화형대에서 풀려난 사람처럼 보였다. 불길이 사지를 모조리 태워 버리지는 못한 채, 빈틈없이 다져진 그 노년의 강건함에서 단 한 톨도 앗아 가지 못한 채, 그저 다 핥아 버리고 만 그런 사람 말이다."], ["His whole high, broad form, seemed made of solid bronze, and shaped in an unalterable mould, like Cellini’s cast Perseus.", "그의 크고 떡 벌어진 온몸은 단단한 청동으로 빚어, 결코 바꿀 수 없는 거푸집에 부어 만든 것 같았으니, 마치 첼리니가 주조한 페르세우스상 같았다."], ["Threading its way out from among his grey hairs, and continuing right down one side of his tawny scorched face and neck, till it disappeared in his clothing, you saw a slender rod-like mark, lividly whitish.", "그의 잿빛 머리카락 사이로 길을 내며 빠져나와, 햇볕에 그을린 누런 얼굴과 목의 한쪽을 따라 곧장 내려가다 옷 속으로 사라지는, 가느다란 막대 모양의 자국 하나가 보였는데, 그것은 납빛이 도는 희끄무레한 색이었다."], ["It resembled that perpendicular seam sometimes made in the straight, lofty trunk of a great tree, when the upper lightning tearingly darts down it, and without wrenching a single twig, peels and grooves out the bark from top to bottom, ere running off into the soil, leaving the tree still greenly alive, but branded.", "그것은 거대한 나무의 곧고 우뚝한 줄기에 이따금 생기는 그 수직의 흠과 닮아 있었다. 위에서 번개가 내리꽂혀 갈가리 찢으며 내달릴 때, 잔가지 하나 비틀지 않으면서도 꼭대기에서 밑동까지 껍질을 벗기고 홈을 파낸 뒤 땅속으로 빠져나가, 나무는 여전히 푸르게 살아 있되 낙인이 찍힌 채로 남게 되는, 바로 그런 흠 말이다."], ["Whether that mark was born with him, or whether it was the scar left by some desperate wound, no one could certainly say.", "그 자국이 태어날 때부터 그와 함께한 것인지, 아니면 어떤 끔찍한 상처가 남긴 흉터인지는 아무도 확실히 말할 수 없었다."], ["By some tacit consent, throughout the voyage little or no allusion was made to it, especially by the mates.", "어떤 무언의 합의에 따라, 항해 내내 그것에 관해서는 거의 또는 전혀 언급이 없었으니, 특히 항해사들이 그러했다."], ["But once Tashtego’s senior, an old Gay-Head Indian among the crew, superstitiously asserted that not till he was full forty years old did Ahab become that way branded, and then it came upon him, not in the fury of any mortal fray, but in an elemental strife at sea.", "그러나 한번은 태시테고(Tashtego)의 손윗사람으로 선원 중에 끼어 있던 늙은 게이헤드 인디언이, 에이해브가 그렇게 낙인찍히게 된 것은 그가 마흔이 다 차고 나서야 일어난 일이며, 그것도 어떤 목숨을 건 싸움의 광란 속에서가 아니라 바다에서 벌어진 원초적 격투 가운데 그에게 닥친 것이라고 미신적으로 단언했다."], ["Yet, this wild hint seemed inferentially negatived, by what a grey Manxman insinuated, an old sepulchral man, who, having never before sailed out of Nantucket, had never ere this laid eye upon wild Ahab.", "그러나 이 거친 추측은, 잿빛의 늙은 맨섬 사람이 넌지시 흘린 말로 미루어 부정되는 듯했다. 그는 무덤처럼 음침한 늙은이로, 전에 한 번도 낸터킷을 벗어나 항해해 본 적이 없었던 까닭에 이번이 있기 전까지는 거친 에이해브를 본 적이 없었다."], ["Nevertheless, the old sea-traditions, the immemorial credulities, popularly invested this old Manxman with preternatural powers of discernment.", "그럼에도 오래된 바다의 전설들, 아득한 옛날부터 내려온 속신들은 이 늙은 맨섬 사람에게 초자연적인 통찰의 힘이 깃들어 있다고 널리 믿게 만들었다."], ["So that no white sailor seriously contradicted him when he said that if ever Captain Ahab should be tranquilly laid out—which might hardly come to pass, so he muttered—then, whoever should do that last office for the dead, would find a birth-mark on him from crown to sole.", "그래서 그가 말하기를, 만약 언젠가 에이해브 선장이 고요히 염습되어 눕는 날이 온다면 — 그런 일이 좀처럼 일어나지는 않으리라고 그는 중얼거렸지만 — 그때 죽은 자를 위해 그 마지막 의식을 행하는 자가 누구이든, 그의 몸에서 정수리부터 발바닥까지 이어진 태생의 점을 발견하게 될 것이라고 했을 때, 어떤 백인 선원도 그 말에 진지하게 반박하지 못했다."]], [["So powerfully did the whole grim aspect of Ahab affect me, and the livid brand which streaked it, that for the first few moments I hardly noted that not a little of this overbearing grimness was owing to the barbaric white leg upon which he partly stood.", "에이해브의 그 음산한 면모 전체와 그것을 가로지른 납빛 낙인이 어찌나 강렬하게 나를 사로잡았던지, 처음 얼마 동안 나는 그 위압적인 음산함의 적지 않은 부분이 그가 한쪽으로 기대어 선 야만스러운 흰 다리에서 비롯된 것임을 거의 알아채지 못했다."], ["It had previously come to me that this ivory leg had at sea been fashioned from the polished bone of the sperm whale’s jaw.", "이 상아 다리가 바다에서 향유고래 턱뼈를 매끄럽게 갈아 만든 것이라는 사실은 전에 이미 내 귀에 들어와 있었다."], ["“Aye, he was dismasted off Japan,”", "\"그래, 그는 일본 앞바다에서 돛대를 잃었지.\""], ["said the old Gay-Head Indian once; “but like his dismasted craft, he shipped another mast without coming home for it.", "언젠가 그 늙은 게이헤드 인디언이 말했다. \"하지만 돛대를 잃은 자기 배처럼, 그는 고향에 돌아가지 않고도 새 돛대를 갖춰 달았어."], ["He has a quiver of ’em.”", "그런 걸 한 통 가득 갖고 있거든.\""]], [["I was struck with the singular posture he maintained.", "나는 그가 취하고 있던 그 기이한 자세에 깊은 인상을 받았다."], ["Upon each side of the Pequod’s quarter deck, and pretty close to the mizzen shrouds, there was an auger hole, bored about half an inch or so, into the plank.", "피쿼드호(Pequod)의 후갑판 양편, 뒷돛대 지삭에 꽤 가까운 자리에, 송곳으로 뚫은 구멍이 하나씩 있었는데, 널빤지 속으로 반 인치쯤 파인 것이었다."], ["His bone leg steadied in that hole; one arm elevated, and holding by a shroud; Captain Ahab stood erect, looking straight out beyond the ship’s ever-pitching prow.", "그의 뼈다귀 다리가 그 구멍에 단단히 박혀 있었고, 한쪽 팔은 위로 치켜올려 지삭을 붙들고 있었다. 에이해브 선장은 곧추선 채로, 끊임없이 출렁이는 뱃머리 너머 저 먼 곳을 똑바로 응시하고 있었다."], ["There was an infinity of firmest fortitude, a determinate, unsurrenderable wilfulness, in the fixed and fearless, forward dedication of that glance.", "흔들림 없이 두려움 없는, 앞을 향한 그 시선의 헌신 속에는 더없이 굳건한 불굴의 의지가, 단호하고 결코 굴복하지 않을 고집이 한없이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Not a word he spoke; nor did his officers say aught to him; though by all their minutest gestures and expressions, they plainly showed the uneasy, if not painful, consciousness of being under a troubled master-eye.", "그는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 그의 사관들도 그에게 아무 말도 건네지 않았다. 다만 그들의 더없이 사소한 몸짓과 표정 하나하나로, 번민에 찬 주인의 눈 아래 놓여 있다는, 고통스럽다고까지는 못해도 불안한 의식을 분명히 드러내 보이고 있었다."], ["And not only that, but moody stricken Ahab stood before them with a crucifixion in his face; in all the nameless regal overbearing dignity of some mighty woe.", "그뿐 아니라, 우울에 짓눌린 에이해브는 얼굴에 십자가형의 고난을 띤 채, 어떤 거대한 비통이 자아내는 이름 붙일 수 없는 제왕적이고 위압적인 위엄을 온통 두른 채로 그들 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["Ere long, from his first visit in the air, he withdrew into his cabin. But after that morning, he was every day visible to the crew; either standing in his pivot-hole, or seated upon an ivory stool he had; or heavily walking the deck. As the sky grew less gloomy; indeed, began to grow a little genial, he became still less and less a recluse; as if, when the ship had sailed from home, nothing but the dead wintry bleakness of the sea had then kept him so secluded. And, by and by, it came to pass, that he was almost continually in the air; but, as yet, for all that he said, or perceptibly did, on the at last sunny deck, he seemed as unnecessary there as another mast. But the Pequod was only making a passage now; not regularly cruising; nearly all whaling preparatives needing supervision the mates were fully competent to, so that there was little or nothing, out of himself, to employ or excite Ahab, now; and thus chase away, for that one interval, the clouds that layer upon layer were piled upon his brow, as ever all clouds choose the loftiest peaks to pile themselves upon.", "이윽고 그는 처음으로 바깥 공기를 쐬고 나서 다시 선실로 물러났다. 그러나 그날 아침 이후로 그는 날마다 선원들 앞에 모습을 드러냈다. 그 구멍에 다리를 박고 서 있거나, 가지고 있던 상아 의자에 앉아 있거나, 무거운 걸음으로 갑판을 거닐거나 했다. 하늘이 덜 음울해지고, 실로 조금씩 온화해지기 시작하자, 그도 점점 덜 은둔하게 되었다. 마치 배가 고향을 떠났을 때 그를 그토록 칩거하게 만든 것이 오직 죽은 듯 황량한 겨울 바다의 적막뿐이었던 것처럼 말이다. 그리고 차츰, 그는 거의 끊임없이 바깥 공기 속에 머무르게 되었다. 그러나 아직까지는, 마침내 햇살이 비치는 갑판 위에서 그가 한 말이나 눈에 띄게 행한 모든 것을 보더라도, 그곳에서 그는 또 하나의 돛대만큼이나 쓸모없는 존재처럼 보였다. 하지만 지금 피쿼드호는 그저 항로를 지나가고 있을 뿐, 본격적으로 순항하는 것은 아니었다. 거의 모든 고래잡이 준비는 감독이 필요했으되 항해사들이 충분히 감당할 수 있는 일이었으니, 이제 에이해브 자신을 제외하고는 그를 부리거나 자극할 거리가 거의, 아니 전혀 없었다. 그리하여 그 한동안만이라도, 겹겹이 쌓여 그의 이마에 얹혀 있는 구름을 쫓아낼 길이 없었던 것이다. 마치 모든 구름이 가장 높은 봉우리를 골라 그 위에 쌓이기를 택하듯이."]], [["Nevertheless, ere long, the warm, warbling persuasiveness of the pleasant, holiday weather we came to, seemed gradually to charm him from his mood.", "그럼에도 머지않아, 우리가 들어선 그 화창한 휴일 같은 날씨의 따뜻하고 지저귀는 듯한 설득력이 차츰 그를 그 침울한 기분에서 끌어내는 듯했다."], ["For, as when the red-cheeked, dancing girls, April and May, trip home to the wintry, misanthropic woods; even the barest, ruggedest, most thunder-cloven old oak will at least send forth some few green sprouts, to welcome such glad-hearted visitants; so Ahab did, in the end, a little respond to the playful allurings of that girlish air.", "볼이 발그레한 채 춤추는 처녀들, 곧 사월과 오월이 겨울의 인간 혐오에 잠긴 숲으로 사뿐히 돌아올 때, 헐벗고 거칠며 벼락에 갈라진 가장 늙은 떡갈나무조차 그 기쁨에 찬 손님들을 맞이하려 몇 줄기 푸른 새싹을 적어도 내미는 법이듯이, 에이해브도 마침내는 그 소녀 같은 대기의 장난스러운 유혹에 조금은 응답했던 것이다."], ["More than once did he put forth the faint blossom of a look, which, in any other man, would have soon flowered out in a smile.", "그는 한 번 이상, 어떤 표정의 희미한 꽃봉오리를 피워 보였는데, 다른 사람 같았으면 그것은 이내 미소로 활짝 피어났을 그런 표정이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 29. Enter Ahab; to Him, Stubb.", "# CHAPTER 29. Enter Ahab; to Him, Stubb."]], [["Some days elapsed, and ice and icebergs all astern, the Pequod now went rolling through the bright Quito spring, which, at sea, almost perpetually reigns on the threshold of the eternal August of the Tropic.", "며칠이 흘렀고, 얼음과 빙산도 모두 고물 뒤로 멀어졌다. 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 이제 밝은 키토의 봄을 가르며 일렁일렁 나아갔다."], ["The warmly cool, clear, ringing, perfumed, overflowing, redundant days, were as crystal goblets of Persian sherbet, heaped up—flaked up, with rose-water snow.", "바다에서는 그 봄이 거의 영구히 열대의 영원한 팔월 문턱을 다스리고 있었다. 따스하면서도 서늘하고, 맑고, 청량하게 울리며, 향기롭고, 넘쳐흐르며, 풍요로운 그 나날들은, 장미수 눈을 켜켜이 쌓아 올린 — 송이송이 얹은 페르시아 셔벗을 담은 수정 잔과도 같았다."], ["The starred and stately nights seemed haughty dames in jewelled velvets, nursing at home in lonely pride, the memory of their absent conquering Earls, the golden helmeted suns!", "별이 총총하고 위풍당당한 밤들은, 보석 박힌 벨벳을 두른 채 고독한 자긍심 속에 홀로 집을 지키며, 멀리 떠난 정복자 백작들, 곧 황금 투구를 쓴 태양들의 기억을 품어 기르는 도도한 귀부인들 같았다!"], ["For sleeping man, ’twas hard to choose between such winsome days and such seducing nights.", "잠을 자려는 이에게는 이토록 매혹적인 낮과 이토록 유혹적인 밤 사이에서 어느 하나를 고르기란 쉽지 않았다."], ["But all the witcheries of that unwaning weather did not merely lend new spells and potencies to the outward world.", "그러나 그 시들 줄 모르는 날씨의 온갖 마법은 단지 바깥 세계에 새로운 주문과 위력을 더해 주는 데 그치지 않았다."], ["Inward they turned upon the soul, especially when the still mild hours of eve came on; then, memory shot her crystals as the clear ice most forms of noiseless twilights.", "그것들은 안으로 돌아 영혼을 파고들었으니, 특히 저녁의 고요하고 부드러운 시간이 다가올 때 그러했다. 그러면 소리 없는 황혼이 가장 또렷한 얼음을 빚어내듯, 기억은 제 수정들을 쏘아 올렸다."], ["And all these subtle agencies, more and more they wrought on Ahab’s texture.", "그리고 이 모든 미묘한 작용들은, 점점 더, 에이해브(Ahab)의 결을 파고들어 갔다."]], [["Old age is always wakeful; as if, the longer linked with life, the less man has to do with aught that looks like death.", "노년은 늘 잠이 없는 법이다. 마치 삶과 더 오래 이어질수록, 인간은 죽음을 닮은 그 무엇과도 덜 엮이려는 듯이."], ["Among sea-commanders, the old greybeards will oftenest leave their berths to visit the night-cloaked deck.", "바다의 지휘관들 가운데서도 늙고 흰 수염을 기른 자들이 가장 자주 잠자리를 떠나 밤의 장막에 덮인 갑판을 찾는다. 에이해브도 그러했다."], ["It was so with Ahab; only that now, of late, he seemed so much to live in the open air, that truly speaking, his visits were more to the cabin, than from the cabin to the planks.", "다만 요즈음 들어, 그는 어찌나 바깥 공기 속에 머무는 듯 살았던지, 사실대로 말하자면 그의 발걸음은 선실에서 갑판으로 나오는 일보다 갑판에서 선실로 들어가는 일이 더 많았다. \"나 같은 늙은 선장이 이 좁은 승강구로 내려가, 무덤처럼 파인 잠자리로 향하는 건\""], ["“It feels like going down into one’s tomb,”—he would mutter to himself—“for an old captain like me to be descending this narrow scuttle, to go to my grave-dug berth.”", "하고 그는 혼자 중얼거리곤 했다. \"꼭 제 무덤 속으로 내려가는 기분이로군.\""]], [["So, almost every twenty-four hours, when the watches of the night were set, and the band on deck sentinelled the slumbers of the band below; and when if a rope was to be hauled upon the forecastle, the sailors flung it not rudely down, as by day, but with some cautiousness dropt it to its place for fear of disturbing their slumbering shipmates; when this sort of steady quietude would begin to prevail, habitually, the silent steersman would watch the cabin-scuttle; and ere long the old man would emerge, gripping at the iron banister, to help his crippled way.", "그리하여 거의 스물네 시간마다, 밤의 당직이 정해지고, 갑판 위의 무리가 아래에서 잠든 무리의 단잠을 지키며 보초를 설 무렵, 그리고 앞갑판에서 밧줄을 끌어당겨야 할 때면 선원들이 낮처럼 거칠게 그것을 내던지지 않고, 잠든 동료들을 깨울까 두려워 조심스레 제자리에 내려놓을 무렵, 이런 한결같은 정적이 으레 깃들기 시작하면, 말없는 키잡이는 선실 승강구를 지켜보았다. 그리고 머지않아 그 노인이, 불편한 걸음을 거들려고 쇠 난간을 움켜쥔 채 모습을 드러내곤 했다."], ["Some considering touch of humanity was in him; for at times like these, he usually abstained from patrolling the quarter-deck; because to his wearied mates, seeking repose within six inches of his ivory heel, such would have been the reverberating crack and din of that bony step, that their dreams would have been on the crunching teeth of sharks.", "그에게도 남을 헤아리는 인정의 손길이 어느 정도 남아 있었으니, 이런 때면 그는 대개 후갑판을 거니는 일을 삼갔다. 그의 상아 발꿈치에서 여섯 인치도 안 되는 곳에서 안식을 구하는 지친 항해사들에게, 그 뼈로 된 걸음이 울려대는 우지끈 소리와 소란이라니, 그들의 꿈이 온통 상어가 뼈를 으드득 씹는 소리로 가득 찰 지경이었기 때문이다."], ["But once, the mood was on him too deep for common regardings; and as with heavy, lumber-like pace he was measuring the ship from taffrail to mainmast, Stubb, the old second mate, came up from below, with a certain unassured, deprecating humorousness, hinted that if Captain Ahab was pleased to walk the planks, then, no one could say nay; but there might be some way of muffling the noise; hinting something indistinctly and hesitatingly about a globe of tow, and the insertion into it, of the ivory heel.", "그러나 한번은, 그가 빠져든 기분이 그런 사소한 헤아림에는 미치지 못할 만큼 깊었다. 그가 무겁고 통나무 같은 걸음으로 고물 난간에서 큰돛대까지 배의 길이를 재며 거닐고 있을 때, 늙은 이등 항해사 스터브(Stubb)가 아래에서 올라와, 어딘가 자신 없고 미안해하는 듯한 익살을 띤 채, 만약 에이해브 선장이 갑판을 거니는 게 흡족하시다면야 아무도 안 된다 할 수 없겠으나, 그래도 그 소리를 죽일 무슨 방도가 있지 않겠느냐고 넌지시 말했다."], ["Ah!", "그러고는 뱃밥 뭉치 한 덩이에다 그 상아 발꿈치를 끼워 넣는 일에 관해 무언가를 어물어물 망설이며 흘렸다."], ["Stubb, thou didst not know Ahab then.", "아! 스터브, 그대는 그때 에이해브를 알지 못했다."]], [["“Am I a cannon-ball, Stubb,”", "\"내가 포탄이라도 된단 말이냐, 스터브\" 하고 에이해브가 말했다."], ["said Ahab, “that thou wouldst wad me that fashion?", "\"그런 식으로 나를 메워 막으려 하다니? 허나 가던 길이나 가거라."], ["But go thy ways; I had forgot.", "내가 깜빡했군. 네 밤의 무덤으로 내려가라."], ["Below to thy nightly grave; where such as ye sleep between shrouds, to use ye to the filling one at last.—Down, dog, and kennel!”", "너희 같은 자들이 수의(壽衣) 사이에서 잠을 자며, 끝내 그 안을 채우는 데 익숙해지는 그곳으로. — 내려가라, 개야, 개집으로!\""]], [["Starting at the unforseen concluding exclamation of the so suddenly scornful old man, Stubb was speechless a moment; then said excitedly, “I am not used to be spoken to that way, sir; I do but less than half like it, sir.”", "그토록 갑작스레 경멸을 내뱉는 노인의, 미처 예상치 못한 끝맺음의 외침에 흠칫 놀란 스터브는 한순간 말을 잃었다. 그러더니 격앙되어 말했다. \"저는 그런 식으로 말을 듣는 데 익숙하지 않습니다, 선장님. 절반도 채 마음에 들지 않는군요, 선장님.\""]], [["“Avast!", "\"닥쳐!\""], ["gritted Ahab between his set teeth, and violently moving away, as if to avoid some passionate temptation.", "에이해브가 앙다문 이 사이로 으드득 내뱉으며, 무슨 격정 어린 유혹이라도 피하려는 듯 거세게 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“No, sir; not yet,”", "\"아닙니다, 선장님. 아직은 아닙니다\""], ["said Stubb, emboldened, “I will not tamely be called a dog, sir.”", "하고 스터브가 대담해져서 말했다. \"저는 순순히 개라 불릴 생각은 없습니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“Then be called ten times a donkey, and a mule, and an ass, and begone, or I’ll clear the world of thee!”", "\"그렇다면 당나귀라고 열 번, 노새라고, 멍청이라고 불러 주마. 그러니 썩 꺼져라, 안 그러면 이 세상에서 너를 쓸어버리겠다!\""]], [["As he said this, Ahab advanced upon him with such overbearing terrors in his aspect, that Stubb involuntarily retreated.", "이렇게 말하면서 에이해브가 그 표정에 어찌나 압도적인 위세를 띠고 다가들었던지, 스터브는 저도 모르게 물러서고 말았다."]], [["“I was never served so before without giving a hard blow for it,”", "\"이렇게 당하고도 호되게 한 방 갚아 주지 못한 건 난생처음이군\""], ["muttered Stubb, as he found himself descending the cabin-scuttle.", "하고 스터브는 어느새 선실 승강구를 내려가는 자신을 발견하고는 중얼거렸다. \"참 묘하단 말이야."], ["“It’s very queer.", "가만, 스터브."], ["Stop, Stubb; somehow, now, I don’t well know whether to go back and strike him, or—what’s that?—down here on my knees and pray for him?", "어쩐 일인지 지금 나는 다시 올라가서 그를 한 대 갈겨야 할지, 아니면 — 저게 뭐람? — 여기 무릎을 꿇고 그를 위해 기도라도 해야 할지 잘 모르겠단 말이지."], ["Yes, that was the thought coming up in me; but it would be the first time I ever did pray.", "그래, 그런 생각이 내 안에서 떠오르고 있었어. 하지만 그건 내가 기도라는 걸 다 해 보는 첫 번째 일이 될 거야."], ["It’s queer; very queer; and he’s queer too; aye, take him fore and aft, he’s about the queerest old man Stubb ever sailed with.", "묘해, 아주 묘하단 말이지. 그리고 저 양반도 묘해."], ["How he flashed at me!—his eyes like powder-pans!", "그래, 앞에서 보나 뒤에서 보나, 스터브가 함께 항해해 본 늙은이 중에 가장 묘한 양반이야. 어떻게 나한테 그렇게 번뜩였담!"], ["is he mad?", "— 그 눈이 화약 접시 같았어!"], ["Anyway there’s something on his mind, as sure as there must be something on a deck when it cracks.", "미친 건가? 어쨌든 그의 마음속에 뭔가 걸린 게 있어, 갑판이 우지끈할 때 그 위에 분명 뭔가가 얹혀 있는 것만큼이나 확실하게."], ["He aint in his bed now, either, more than three hours out of the twenty-four; and he don’t sleep then.", "그리고 그 양반은 이제 하루 스물네 시간 중에 세 시간도 침상에 누워 있지 않아. 게다가 그때조차 잠을 안 자."], ["Didn’t that Dough-Boy, the steward, tell me that of a morning he always finds the old man’s hammock clothes all rumpled and tumbled, and the sheets down at the foot, and the coverlid almost tied into knots, and the pillow a sort of frightful hot, as though a baked brick had been on it?", "저 도-보이라는 급사가 나한테 그러지 않았나, 아침마다 보면 늘 그 노인의 해먹 침구가 온통 구겨지고 헝클어져 있고, 시트는 발치까지 흘러내려 있고, 덮개는 거의 매듭이 지도록 비틀려 있고, 베개는 마치 구운 벽돌이라도 얹혀 있었던 것처럼 무섭게 뜨끈하다고 말이야."], ["A hot old man!", "뜨거운 늙은이야!"], ["I guess he’s got what some folks ashore call a conscience; it’s a kind of Tic-Dolly-row they say—worse nor a toothache.", "내 짐작엔 저 양반, 뭍에 사는 어떤 사람들이 양심이라 부르는 걸 지니고 있는 것 같아. 그게 일종의 안면 신경통이라던데 — 치통보다 더 고약한 거라잖아."], ["Well, well; I don’t know what it is, but the Lord keep me from catching it.", "그래, 그래, 그게 뭔지는 모르겠지만, 주여, 제가 그것에 옮지 않게 지켜 주소서. 그 양반은 온통 수수께끼투성이야."], ["He’s full of riddles; I wonder what he goes into the after hold for, every night, as Dough-Boy tells me he suspects; what’s that for, I should like to know?", "도-보이가 의심한다고 나한테 일러 준 대로, 밤마다 도대체 무엇 하러 뒤쪽 선창으로 들어가는 걸까. 그게 무엇 때문인지 좀 알고 싶군그래."], ["Who’s made appointments with him in the hold?", "누가 선창에서 그 양반과 약속이라도 잡아 놨나?"], ["Ain’t that queer, now?", "자, 이거 묘하지 않은가?"], ["But there’s no telling, it’s the old game—Here goes for a snooze.", "하지만 알 길이 없지, 늘 그렇고 그런 노릇이니까. — 자, 이제 한숨 자러 가자."], ["Damn me, it’s worth a fellow’s while to be born into the world, if only to fall right asleep.", "빌어먹을, 사람이 이 세상에 태어나는 보람이 있다면, 그저 곤히 잠들 수 있다는 것 하나만으로도 그럴 만하지."], ["And now that I think of it, that’s about the first thing babies do, and that’s a sort of queer, too.", "그러고 보니 그게 아기들이 하는 거의 첫 번째 일이기도 하군. 그것도 일종의 묘한 일이지."], ["Damn me, but all things are queer, come to think of ’em.", "빌어먹을, 따지고 보면 세상만사가 다 묘하단 말이야."], ["But that’s against my principles.", "하지만 그건 내 신조에 어긋나지."], ["Think not, is my eleventh commandment; and sleep when you can, is my twelfth—So here goes again.", "생각하지 말 것, 이게 내 열한 번째 계명이고, 잘 수 있을 때 자 둘 것, 이게 내 열두 번째 계명이거든. — 그러니 자, 다시 가 보자고."], ["But how’s that?", "그런데 저게 어찌 된 거지?"], ["didn’t he call me a dog?", "그가 나를 개라고 부르지 않았나?"], ["blazes!", "제기랄!"], ["he called me ten times a donkey, and piled a lot of jackasses on top of that!", "나를 당나귀라고 열 번이나 부르고, 그 위에다 멍청이를 한 무더기 쌓아 올렸어!"], ["He might as well have kicked me, and done with it.", "차라리 나를 걷어차고 끝내 버리는 편이 나을 뻔했군."], ["Maybe he did kick me, and I didn’t observe it, I was so taken all aback with his brow, somehow.", "어쩌면 정말로 나를 걷어찼는데, 내가 미처 못 알아챘는지도 몰라, 어쩐지 그 양반 이마에 그만 완전히 넋이 나가 버렸으니까. 그게 바랜 뼈처럼 번뜩였단 말이지."], ["It flashed like a bleached bone.", "도대체 나한테 무슨 일이 일어난 거야?"], ["What the devil’s the matter with me?", "두 다리로 똑바로 서 있지를 못하겠어. 저 늙은이와 부딪힌 게 나를 어쩐지 뒤집어 까 놓은 모양이야."], ["I don’t stand right on my legs.", "맙소사, 내가 꿈이라도 꾸고 있었던 게 분명해. — 어떻게?"], ["Coming afoul of that old man has a sort of turned me wrong side out.", "어떻게? 어떻게?"], ["By the Lord, I must have been dreaming, though—How? how?", "— 하지만 유일한 길은 이걸 다 치워 두는 거지."], ["how?—but the only way’s to stash it; so here goes to hammock again; and in the morning, I’ll see how this plaguey juggling thinks over by daylight.”", "그러니 자, 다시 해먹으로 가자. 그리고 아침이 되면, 이 골치 아픈 요술이 환한 대낮에 어떻게 비치는지 한번 따져 봐야겠어.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 30. The Pipe.", "# CHAPTER 30. The Pipe."]], [["When Stubb had departed, Ahab stood for a while leaning over the bulwarks; and then, as had been usual with him of late, calling a sailor of the watch, he sent him below for his ivory stool, and also his pipe.", "스터브가 물러간 뒤, 에이해브는 한동안 뱃전 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 서 있었다. 그러다가 요즈음 들어 버릇처럼 된 대로, 당직 선원 하나를 불러 아래로 내려보내 자신의 상아 의자와 파이프를 가져오게 했다."], ["Lighting the pipe at the binnacle lamp and planting the stool on the weather side of the deck, he sat and smoked.", "그는 나침반함 등불에 파이프를 붙여 불을 댕긴 다음, 갑판의 바람받이 쪽에 의자를 놓고 앉아서 담배를 피웠다."]], [["In old Norse times, the thrones of the sea-loving Danish kings were fabricated, saith tradition, of the tusks of the narwhale.", "전해 오는 이야기로는, 옛 노르드 시대에 바다를 사랑하던 덴마크 왕들의 옥좌는 일각고래의 엄니로 만들어졌다고 한다."], ["How could one look at Ahab then, seated on that tripod of bones, without bethinking him of the royalty it symbolized?", "그렇다면 그 뼈로 된 삼각 의자 위에 앉은 에이해브를 바라보면서, 그것이 상징하는 왕권을 떠올리지 않을 수 있겠는가?"], ["For a Khan of the plank, and a king of the sea, and a great lord of Leviathans was Ahab.", "에이해브야말로 갑판의 칸(汗)이요, 바다의 왕이요, 리바이어던의 위대한 군주였으니 말이다."]], [["Some moments passed, during which the thick vapor came from his mouth in quick and constant puffs, which blew back again into his face.", "얼마간 시간이 흘렀고, 그동안 짙은 연기가 그의 입에서 빠르고도 끊임없는 뭉치로 뿜어져 나왔다가 도로 그의 얼굴로 불려 돌아왔다."], ["“How now,”", "\"이게 어찌 된 일인가.\""], ["he soliloquized at last, withdrawing the tube, “this smoking no longer soothes.", "마침내 그가 파이프 대롱을 입에서 떼며 혼잣말을 했다. \"이 담배가 더는 마음을 달래 주지 않는구나."], ["Oh, my pipe!", "오, 내 파이프여!"], ["hard must it go with me if thy charm be gone!", "네 마법마저 사라졌다면 나는 참으로 견디기 힘들 터인데!"], ["Here have I been unconsciously toiling, not pleasuring—aye, and ignorantly smoking to windward all the while; to windward, and with such nervous whiffs, as if, like the dying whale, my final jets were the strongest and fullest of trouble.", "여기서 나는 즐기지도 못한 채 나도 모르게 고역만 치르고 있었구나 — 그래, 게다가 줄곧 어리석게도 바람받이 쪽으로 담배를 피우고 있었으니, 바람 부는 쪽으로, 그것도 그토록 신경질적인 연기를 뿜어 대며, 마치 죽어 가는 고래가 가장 거센, 가장 고통으로 가득 찬 마지막 물줄기를 뿜어 올리듯이 말이다."], ["What business have I with this pipe?", "내가 이 파이프와 무슨 상관이 있단 말인가?"], ["This thing that is meant for sereneness, to send up mild white vapors among mild white hairs, not among torn iron-grey locks like mine.", "이 물건은 본디 평온을 위한 것, 부드러운 흰 머리칼 사이로 부드러운 흰 연기를 피워 올리라고 있는 것이지, 나처럼 헝클어진 쇳빛 잿빛 머리채 사이로 피우라고 있는 것이 아니거늘."], ["I’ll smoke no more—”", "나는 더 이상 담배를 피우지 않으리라—\""]], [["He tossed the still lighted pipe into the sea.", "그는 아직도 불이 붙은 파이프를 바다로 던져 버렸다."], ["The fire hissed in the waves; the same instant the ship shot by the bubble the sinking pipe made.", "불이 물결 속에서 쉭 소리를 냈고, 바로 그 순간 배는 가라앉는 파이프가 만든 거품 곁을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["With slouched hat, Ahab lurchingly paced the planks.", "모자를 푹 눌러쓴 채, 에이해브는 비틀거리며 갑판을 거닐었다."]], [["Next morning Stubb accosted Flask.", "이튿날 아침, 스터브가 플라스크에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“Such a queer dream, King-Post, I never had.", "\"이렇게 괴상한 꿈은 처음 꿔 봤네, 킹포스트. 영감님의 그 상아 다리 알지?"], ["You know the old man’s ivory leg, well I dreamed he kicked me with it; and when I tried to kick back, upon my soul, my little man, I kicked my leg right off!", "글쎄 꿈속에서 그 다리로 날 걷어차지 뭔가. 그래서 나도 되받아 차려고 했더니, 맙소사, 이 사람아, 내가 내 다리를 통째로 차서 떼어 내 버렸지 뭔가!"], ["And then, presto!", "그러더니 순식간에 에이해브가 피라미드처럼 변하고, 나는 미친 멍청이마냥 그걸 줄곧 걷어차고 있었네. 한데 더 이상한 건 말이야, 플라스크 — 꿈이란 게 다 얼마나 묘한지 자네도 알겠지만 — 그렇게 화가 머리끝까지 치밀어 오른 와중에도, 어쩐지 속으로 이런 생각이 드는 거야."], ["Ahab seemed a pyramid, and I, like a blazing fool, kept kicking at it.", "따지고 보면 에이해브한테 차인 게 그리 대단한 모욕은 아니라고 말일세. '아니,'"], ["But what was still more curious, Flask—you know how curious all dreams are—through all this rage that I was in, I somehow seemed to be thinking to myself, that after all, it was not much of an insult, that kick from Ahab.", "하고 나는 생각했지, '뭐가 문제야? 진짜 다리도 아니고, 그저 가짜 다리잖아.'"], ["‘Why,’ thinks I, ‘what’s the row?", "살아 있는 것한테 맞는 것과 죽은 것한테 맞는 것 사이엔 엄청난 차이가 있는 법이거든. 그래서, 플라스크, 손으로 맞는 게 지팡이로 맞는 것보다 쉰 배는 더 사납게 느껴지는 거라네."], ["It’s not a real leg, only a false leg.’ And there’s a mighty difference between a living thump and a dead thump.", "살아 있는 신체 — 그게 바로 살아 있는 모욕을 만드는 거야, 이 사람아. 그리고 명심하게, 내가 그 빌어먹을 피라미드에다 멍청한 발가락을 줄곧 짓찧고 있는 내내 속으로 이렇게 생각하고 있었단 말일세 — 그게 또 어찌나 앞뒤가 안 맞던지 — 어쨌든 내내 속으로 이런 생각을 하고 있었어."], ["That’s what makes a blow from the hand, Flask, fifty times more savage to bear than a blow from a cane.", "'저 영감 다리가 지금 지팡이지 뭐야 — 고래 뼈로 만든 지팡이 말이야. 그래,'"], ["The living member—that makes the living insult, my little man.", "하고 나는 생각했지, '저건 그저 장난삼아 한 대 두들긴 것 — 사실 고래 뼈로 살짝 건드린 것뿐이지 — 비열한 발길질이 아니야. 게다가,'"], ["And thinks I to myself all the while, mind, while I was stubbing my silly toes against that cursed pyramid—so confoundedly contradictory was it all, all the while, I say, I was thinking to myself, ‘what’s his leg now, but a cane—a whalebone cane.", "하고 나는 생각했네, '한번 보라고. 그 끝부분 — 발끝 쪽 말이야 — 얼마나 자그마한 끝인가."], ["Yes,’ thinks I, ‘it was only a playful cudgelling—in fact, only a whaleboning that he gave me—not a base kick.", "만약 발 넓적한 농부가 날 걷어찼다면, 그건 정말로 지독하게 넓은 모욕일 테지. 한데 이 모욕은 그저 한 점으로 깎여 내려간 거 아닌가.'"], ["Besides,’ thinks I, ‘look at it once; why, the end of it—the foot part—what a small sort of end it is; whereas, if a broad footed farmer kicked me, there’s a devilish broad insult.", "그런데 이제 그 꿈에서 제일 우스운 대목이 나오네, 플라스크. 내가 그 피라미드를 한창 두들겨 패고 있는데, 등에 혹이 달리고 오소리 털 같은 머리를 한 늙은 인어 사내 하나가 내 어깨를 붙잡더니 휙 돌려세우지 뭔가."], ["But this insult is whittled down to a point only.’ But now comes the greatest joke of the dream, Flask.", "'뭐 하는 거야?' 하고 그자가 묻더군."], ["While I was battering away at the pyramid, a sort of badger-haired old merman, with a hump on his back, takes me by the shoulders, and slews me round.", "이런! 이 사람아, 나는 깜짝 놀랐다네."], ["‘What are you ’bout?’ says he.", "그 상판대기라니! 그런데 어찌 된 일인지 다음 순간엔 놀란 게 가시더라고."], ["Slid!", "'내가 뭘 하느냐고?'"], ["man, but I was frightened.", "마침내 내가 말했지. '그게 당신하고 무슨 상관인지 좀 알았으면 좋겠는데, 꼽추 양반?"], ["Such a phiz!", "당신도 한 대 걷어차여 보고 싶나?' 맙소사, 플라스크, 내가 그 말을 내뱉기가 무섭게 그자가 등짝을 내 쪽으로 돌리더니 허리를 굽혀, 가리개 삼아 두르고 있던 해초 뭉치를 끌어 올리는 거야 — 그래서 내가 뭘 봤겠나?"], ["But, somehow, next moment I was over the fright.", "— 세상에 이런 벼락 맞을, 이 사람아, 그자 등짝에 밧줄 송곳이 끝을 바깥으로 향한 채 잔뜩 박혀 있지 뭔가. 나는 다시 생각해 보고 말했지, '아무래도 당신은 안 차는 게 낫겠소, 영감.'"], ["‘What am I about?’ says I at last.", "'현명한 스터브로군,' 하고 그자가 말하더니, '현명한 스터브야'를 줄곧 중얼거리는데, 마치 굴뚝 마녀가 제 잇몸을 우물거리듯 하더군."], ["‘And what business is that of yours, I should like to know, Mr.", "그자가 '현명한 스터브, 현명한 스터브' 하는 소리를 멈출 기색이 없기에, 나는 다시 그 피라미드를 걷어차는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했네."], ["Humpback?", "그런데 막 발을 들어 올린 참인데, 그자가 버럭 소리치는 거야, '그 발길질 멈춰!' '아니,'"], ["Do you want a kick?’ By the lord, Flask, I had no sooner said that, than he turned round his stern to me, bent over, and dragging up a lot of seaweed he had for a clout—what do you think, I saw?—why thunder alive, man, his stern was stuck full of marlinspikes, with the points out.", "하고 내가 말했지, '이번엔 또 뭐가 문제요, 영감?' '여기 좀 보게,'"], ["Says I, on second thoughts, ‘I guess I won’t kick you, old fellow.’ ‘Wise Stubb,’ said he, ‘wise Stubb;’ and kept muttering it all the time, a sort of eating of his own gums like a chimney hag.", "하고 그자가 말하더군. '그 모욕에 대해 한번 따져 보세."], ["Seeing he wasn’t going to stop saying over his ‘wise Stubb, wise Stubb,’ I thought I might as well fall to kicking the pyramid again.", "에이해브 선장이 자넬 걷어찼지, 안 그런가?' '그래, 그랬소,'"], ["But I had only just lifted my foot for it, when he roared out, ‘Stop that kicking!’ ‘Halloa,’ says I, ‘what’s the matter now, old fellow?’ ‘Look ye here,’ says he; ‘let’s argue the insult.", "하고 내가 말했네 — '바로 여기를 걷어찼지.' '아주 좋아,'"], ["Captain Ahab kicked ye, didn’t he?’ ‘Yes, he did,’ says I—‘right here it was.’ ‘Very good,’ says he—‘he used his ivory leg, didn’t he?’ ‘Yes, he did,’ says I.", "하고 그자가 말했지 — '그가 상아 다리를 썼지, 안 그래?' '그래, 그랬소,'"], ["‘Well then,’ says he, ‘wise Stubb, what have you to complain of?", "하고 내가 말했네. '그렇다면,'"], ["Didn’t he kick with right good will?", "하고 그자가 말하더군, '현명한 스터브, 자네가 불평할 게 뭐가 있나? 그가 기꺼운 마음으로 걷어찬 거 아닌가?"], ["it wasn’t a common pitch pine leg he kicked with, was it?", "그가 흔해 빠진 소나무 다리로 찬 게 아니잖아, 그렇지? 아니지, 자넨 위대한 사람한테, 그것도 아름다운 상아 다리로 걷어차인 거야, 스터브."], ["No, you were kicked by a great man, and with a beautiful ivory leg, Stubb.", "그건 영광일세. 나는 그걸 영광이라고 여기네."], ["It’s an honor; I consider it an honor.", "들어 보게, 현명한 스터브. 옛 잉글랜드에서는 가장 위대한 영주들이 여왕한테 한 대 얻어맞아 가터 기사로 책봉되는 걸 큰 영예로 여긴다네."], ["Listen, wise Stubb.", "한데 자네는, 스터브, 늙은 에이해브한테 걷어차여 현명한 사람이 되었노라고 자랑하게나. 내 말 명심하게."], ["In old England the greatest lords think it great glory to be slapped by a queen, and made garter-knights of; but, be your boast, Stubb, that ye were kicked by old Ahab, and made a wise man of.", "그분한테 차이게나. 그분의 발길질을 영광으로 여기게."], ["Remember what I say; be kicked by him; account his kicks honors; and on no account kick back; for you can’t help yourself, wise Stubb.", "그리고 절대로 되받아 차지 말게. 어차피 자네 힘으로는 어쩔 수 없으니, 현명한 스터브."], ["Don’t you see that pyramid?’ With that, he all of a sudden seemed somehow, in some queer fashion, to swim off into the air.", "저 피라미드가 보이지 않나?' 그러더니 그자가 갑자기 어쩐 일인지 묘한 모양새로 허공으로 헤엄쳐 사라지는 거야."], ["I snored; rolled over; and there I was in my hammock!", "나는 코를 골았지. 몸을 뒤척였지."], ["Now, what do you think of that dream, Flask?”", "그러고 보니 내 해먹 안이지 뭔가! 자, 그 꿈을 어떻게 생각하나, 플라스크?\""]], [["“I don’t know; it seems a sort of foolish to me, tho.’”", "\"모르겠네. 한데 나한테는 좀 어리석은 소리처럼 들리는걸.\""]], [["“May be; may be.", "\"그럴지도, 그럴지도 모르지."], ["But it’s made a wise man of me, Flask.", "하지만 그 꿈이 날 현명한 사람으로 만들었다네, 플라스크."], ["D’ye see Ahab standing there, sideways looking over the stern?", "저기 에이해브가 옆으로 서서 고물 너머를 바라보고 있는 거 보이나?"], ["Well, the best thing you can do, Flask, is to let the old man alone; never speak to him, whatever he says.", "그래, 자네가 할 수 있는 최선은 말일세, 플라스크, 저 영감을 가만 내버려 두는 거야. 무슨 말을 하든 절대 말을 걸지 말게."], ["Halloa!", "이런!"], ["What’s that he shouts?", "저 양반이 뭐라고 외치는 거지?"], ["Hark!”", "들어 보게!\""]], [["“Mast-head, there!", "\"돛대 꼭대기, 거기!"], ["Look sharp, all of ye!", "다들 눈 똑바로 떠라!"], ["There are whales hereabouts!", "이 근처에 고래가 있다!"]], [["“If ye see a white one, split your lungs for him!", "\"하얀 놈이 보이거든, 폐가 터지도록 소리쳐라!\""]], [["“What do you think of that now, Flask?", "\"이번엔 그걸 어떻게 생각하나, 플라스크?"], ["ain’t there a small drop of something queer about that, eh?", "거기엔 뭔가 묘한 구석이 한 방울쯤 들어 있지 않나, 응?"], ["A white whale—did ye mark that, man?", "하얀 고래라 — 자네도 그 말을 새겨들었나, 이 사람아?"], ["Look ye—there’s something special in the wind.", "잘 보게 — 바람결에 뭔가 심상찮은 게 실려 있어."], ["Stand by for it, Flask.", "단단히 대비하게, 플라스크."], ["Ahab has that that’s bloody on his mind.", "에이해브는 마음속에 뭔가 피비린내 나는 걸 품고 있어."], ["But, mum; he comes this way.”", "한데, 쉿. 이쪽으로 오고 있네.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 32. Cetology.", "# CHAPTER 32. Cetology."]], [["Already we are boldly launched upon the deep; but soon we shall be lost in its unshored, harbourless immensities.", "세상 이미 우리는 대담하게도 깊은 바다 위로 나아갔다. 그러나 머지않아 우리는 그 기슭도 항구도 없는 광막함 속에서 길을 잃고 말 것이다."], ["Ere that come to pass; ere the Pequod’s weedy hull rolls side by side with the barnacled hulls of the leviathan; at the outset it is but well to attend to a matter almost indispensable to a thorough appreciative understanding of the more special leviathanic revelations and allusions of all sorts which are to follow.", "그런 일이 닥치기 전에, 곧 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 해초 낀 선체가 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 따개비 달라붙은 거구와 나란히 뒹굴기 전에, 출항의 첫머리인 지금, 앞으로 이어질 온갖 종류의 더욱 특별한 고래 이야기와 암시들을 철저하고 깊이 있게 이해하는 데 거의 없어서는 안 될 한 가지 문제에 주의를 기울여 두는 것이 좋겠다."]], [["It is some systematized exhibition of the whale in his broad genera, that I would now fain put before you.", "내가 지금 그대들 앞에 기꺼이 펼쳐 보이고자 하는 것은, 고래를 그 폭넓은 종속(種屬)에 따라 어떤 식으로든 체계적으로 정리해 보여 주는 일이다."], ["Yet is it no easy task.", "그러나 그것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아니다."], ["The classification of the constituents of a chaos, nothing less is here essayed.", "여기서 시도하려는 것은 다름 아닌 혼돈을 이루는 구성 요소들의 분류 작업이다."], ["Listen to what the best and latest authorities have laid down.", "가장 뛰어나고 가장 최근의 권위자들이 무어라 정해 놓았는지 들어 보라."]], [["“No branch of Zoology is so much involved as that which is entitled Cetology,”", "\"동물학의 어떤 분과도 고래학(Cetology)이라는 이름이 붙은 분야만큼 복잡한 것은 없다.\""], ["says Captain Scoresby, A.D. 1820.", "스코어스비 선장이 서기 1820년에 한 말이다."]], [["“It is not my intention, were it in my power, to enter into the inquiry as to the true method of dividing the cetacea into groups and families.", "\"향유고래(Sperm)를 무리와 과(科)로 나누는 참된 방법에 관한 탐구에 들어가는 일은, 설령 내게 그럴 능력이 있다 하더라도 나의 의도가 아니다."], ["* * * Utter confusion exists among the historians of this animal”", "* * * 이 동물(향유고래)을 다룬 사가(史家)들 사이에는 완전한 혼란이 존재한다.\""], ["(sperm whale), says Surgeon Beale, A.D. 1839.", "외과의 빌이 서기 1839년에 한 말이다."]], [["“Unfitness to pursue our research in the unfathomable waters.”", "\"헤아릴 수 없는 물속에서 우리의 연구를 밀고 나가기에는 부적합하다.\""], ["“Impenetrable veil covering our knowledge of the cetacea.”", "\"고래에 관한 우리의 지식을 가린 뚫을 수 없는 장막.\""], ["“A field strewn with thorns.”", "\"가시가 흩뿌려진 들판.\""], ["“All these incomplete indications but serve to torture us naturalists.”", "\"이 모든 불완전한 단서들은 그저 우리 박물학자들을 괴롭히는 데 쓰일 뿐이다.\""]], [["Thus speak of the whale, the great Cuvier, and John Hunter, and Lesson, those lights of zoology and anatomy.", "고래에 관해 이렇게 말한 이들은 저 위대한 퀴비에와 존 헌터와 레송이니, 동물학과 해부학의 등불 같은 인물들이다."], ["Nevertheless, though of real knowledge there be little, yet of books there are a plenty; and so in some small degree, with cetology, or the science of whales.", "그럼에도 불구하고, 참된 지식은 거의 없을지언정 책만큼은 차고 넘친다. 그러니 고래학, 곧 고래에 관한 학문도 어느 정도까지는 사정이 같다."], ["Many are the men, small and great, old and new, landsmen and seamen, who have at large or in little, written of the whale.", "작든 크든, 옛사람이든 요즘 사람이든, 뭍사람이든 뱃사람이든, 길게든 짧게든 고래에 관해 글을 쓴 이는 한둘이 아니다."], ["Run over a few:—The Authors of the Bible; Aristotle; Pliny; Aldrovandi; Sir Thomas Browne; Gesner; Ray; Linnæus; Rondeletius; Willoughby; Green; Artedi; Sibbald; Brisson; Marten; Lacépède; Bonneterre; Desmarest; Baron Cuvier; Frederick Cuvier; John Hunter; Owen; Scoresby; Beale; Bennett; J.", "그 몇몇을 훑어보자. 성경의 저자들, 아리스토텔레스, 플리니우스, 알드로반디, 토머스 브라운 경, 게스너, 레이, 린나이우스, 론델레티우스, 윌러비, 그린, 아르테디, 시발드, 브리송, 마르텐, 라세페드, 보네테르, 데마레, 퀴비에 남작, 프레데리크 퀴비에, 존 헌터, 오언, 스코어스비, 빌, 베넷, J."], ["Ross Browne; the Author of Miriam Coffin; Olmstead; and the Rev. T.", "로스 브라운, 『미리엄 코핀』의 저자, 옴스테드, 그리고 목사 T."], ["Cheever.", "치버."], ["But to what ultimate generalizing purpose all these have written, the above cited extracts will show.", "그러나 이들 모두가 결국 어떤 일반화의 목적을 두고 글을 썼는지는 위에 인용한 발췌문들이 잘 보여 줄 것이다."]], [["Of the names in this list of whale authors, only those following Owen ever saw living whales; and but one of them was a real professional harpooneer and whaleman.", "이 고래 저술가 명단에 오른 이름들 가운데, 오언 이후에 오는 자들만이 살아 있는 고래를 직접 본 적이 있으며, 그들 가운데 진짜 직업적인 작살잡이이자 고래잡이였던 이는 단 한 사람뿐이다."], ["I mean Captain Scoresby.", "스코어스비 선장을 두고 하는 말이다."], ["On the separate subject of the Greenland or right-whale, he is the best existing authority.", "그린란드(Greenland)고래, 곧 참고래(Right Whale)라는 별개의 주제에 관해서라면 그는 현존하는 최고의 권위자다."], ["But Scoresby knew nothing and says nothing of the great sperm whale, compared with which the Greenland whale is almost unworthy mentioning.", "그러나 스코어스비는 저 위대한 향유고래에 관해서는 아무것도 알지 못했고 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 향유고래에 견주면 그린란드고래는 거의 입에 올릴 가치조차 없을 정도다."], ["And here be it said, that the Greenland whale is an usurper upon the throne of the seas.", "그리고 여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 그린란드고래는 바다의 옥좌를 찬탈한 자다."], ["He is not even by any means the largest of the whales.", "그놈은 결코 고래들 가운데 가장 큰 놈조차도 아니다."], ["Yet, owing to the long priority of his claims, and the profound ignorance which, till some seventy years back, invested the then fabulous or utterly unknown sperm-whale, and which ignorance to this present day still reigns in all but some few scientific retreats and whale-ports; this usurpation has been every way complete.", "그럼에도 불구하고, 그놈이 옛적부터 오래도록 그 자리를 주장해 온 까닭에, 또한 칠십 년쯤 전까지만 해도 그때까지 전설적이거나 전혀 알려지지 않았던 향유고래를 둘러싸고 있던 깊은 무지, 그리고 오늘날까지도 몇몇 과학적 은신처와 고래잡이 항구를 빼면 여전히 군림하고 있는 그 무지 탓에, 이 찬탈은 어느 모로 보나 완전하게 이루어졌다."], ["Reference to nearly all the leviathanic allusions in the great poets of past days, will satisfy you that the Greenland whale, without one rival, was to them the monarch of the seas.", "지나간 시대의 위대한 시인들이 남긴 거의 모든 고래 관련 언급을 살펴보면, 그들에게는 그린란드고래가 단 하나의 경쟁자도 없는 바다의 군주였음을 그대들도 수긍하게 될 것이다."], ["But the time has at last come for a new proclamation.", "그러나 마침내 새로운 포고를 내릴 때가 왔다. 여기는 채링 크로스다."], ["This is Charing Cross; hear ye!", "모든 백성이여, 들으라!"], ["good people all,—the Greenland whale is deposed,—the great sperm whale now reigneth!", "그린란드고래는 폐위되었노라. 이제는 저 위대한 향유고래가 군림하노라!"]], [["There are only two books in being which at all pretend to put the living sperm whale before you, and at the same time, in the remotest degree succeed in the attempt.", "살아 있는 향유고래를 그대들 앞에 펼쳐 보이겠노라고 조금이라도 자처하면서, 동시에 그 시도에 아주 미미한 정도나마 성공을 거둔 책은 세상에 단 두 권뿐이다."], ["Those books are Beale’s and Bennett’s; both in their time surgeons to English South-Sea whale-ships, and both exact and reliable men.", "그 두 책은 빌의 책과 베넷의 책이다. 둘 다 한 시절 영국 남양(南洋) 포경선의 외과의였으며, 둘 다 정확하고 믿을 만한 사람들이다."], ["The original matter touching the sperm whale to be found in their volumes is necessarily small; but so far as it goes, it is of excellent quality, though mostly confined to scientific description.", "그들의 책에서 찾아볼 수 있는, 향유고래를 다룬 독창적인 내용은 필연적으로 적을 수밖에 없다. 그러나 그 분량이 닿는 데까지는 그 질이 빼어나니, 비록 대부분이 과학적 서술에 한정되어 있긴 하다."], ["As yet, however, the sperm whale, scientific or poetic, lives not complete in any literature.", "그러나 아직까지도 향유고래는, 과학적으로든 시적으로든, 그 어떤 문헌 속에서도 온전하게 살아 있지 못하다."], ["Far above all other hunted whales, his is an unwritten life.", "사냥당하는 다른 모든 고래들을 훨씬 능가하는 이 고래의 생애는, 아직 쓰이지 않은 생애인 것이다."]], [["Now the various species of whales need some sort of popular comprehensive classification, if only an easy outline one for the present, hereafter to be filled in all its departments by subsequent laborers.", "자, 그렇다면 고래의 여러 종(種)은 어떤 식으로든 일반인이 두루 이해할 만한 분류가 필요하다. 비록 지금으로서는 손쉬운 개요에 지나지 않을지라도, 나중에 뒤따르는 일꾼들이 그 모든 갈래를 빠짐없이 채워 넣을 수 있도록 하기 위함이다."], ["As no better man advances to take this matter in hand, I hereupon offer my own poor endeavors.", "나보다 나은 사람이 나서서 이 일을 떠맡지 않으니, 나는 이에 나 자신의 보잘것없는 노력을 바치고자 한다."], ["I promise nothing complete; because any human thing supposed to be complete, must for that very reason infallibly be faulty.", "나는 무엇 하나 완전하리라고 약속하지 않는다. 무릇 완전하다고 여겨지는 인간의 모든 것은, 바로 그 이유 때문에 어김없이 결함을 지니게 마련인 까닭이다."], ["I shall not pretend to a minute anatomical description of the various species, or—in this place at least—to much of any description.", "나는 여러 종에 대한 세밀한 해부학적 서술을 꾀하지도 않을 것이며, 적어도 이 자리에서만큼은 그 어떤 자세한 서술도 그리 많이 늘어놓지 않을 것이다."], ["My object here is simply to project the draught of a systematization of cetology.", "여기서 내 목적은 그저 고래학을 체계화하는 설계도의 초안을 그려 보이는 데 있다."], ["I am the architect, not the builder.", "나는 건축가이지 시공자가 아니다."]], [["But it is a ponderous task; no ordinary letter-sorter in the Post-Office is equal to it.", "그러나 이는 묵직한 과업이다. 우체국의 어떤 평범한 우편물 분류원도 이 일을 감당하지는 못한다."], ["To grope down into the bottom of the sea after them; to have one’s hands among the unspeakable foundations, ribs, and very pelvis of the world; this is a fearful thing.", "그놈들을 찾아 바다 밑바닥까지 더듬어 내려가는 일, 이 세상의 형언할 수 없는 토대와 갈빗대와 바로 그 골반에 손을 대는 일, 이것은 두려운 일이다. 도대체 내가 무엇이라고 이 리바이어던의 코에 갈고리를 꿰려 든단 말인가!"], ["What am I that I should essay to hook the nose of this leviathan!", "욥기에 나오는 저 무시무시한 조롱이 능히 나를 질리게 할 만하다."], ["The awful tauntings in Job might well appal me.", "그(리바이어던)가 너와 언약을 맺겠느냐? 보라, 그를 잡으려는 희망은 헛되도다!"], ["Will he (the leviathan) make a covenant with thee?", "그러나 나는 도서관들을 헤엄쳐 건넜고 대양들을 항해해 건넜다."], ["Behold the hope of him is vain!", "나는 눈에 보이는 이 두 손으로 고래들을 직접 다루어 왔다."], ["But I have swam through libraries and sailed through oceans; I have had to do with whales with these visible hands; I am in earnest; and I will try.", "나는 진지하다. 그러니 한번 해 보겠다."], ["There are some preliminaries to settle.", "먼저 정리해 두어야 할 몇 가지 예비 사항이 있다."]], [["First: The uncertain, unsettled condition of this science of Cetology is in the very vestibule attested by the fact, that in some quarters it still remains a moot point whether a whale be a fish.", "첫째, 이 고래학이라는 학문이 얼마나 불확실하고 정립되지 못한 상태에 있는지는, 어떤 부류에서는 고래가 과연 물고기인가 하는 것이 아직도 논란거리로 남아 있다는 사실만으로도 그 입구에서부터 입증된다."], ["In his System of Nature, A.D. 1776, Linnæus declares, “I hereby separate the whales from the fish.”", "린나이우스는 서기 1776년에 펴낸 『자연의 체계』에서 \"나는 이로써 고래를 물고기로부터 분리하노라\"라고 선언했다."], ["But of my own knowledge, I know that down to the year 1850, sharks and shad, alewives and herring, against Linnæus’s express edict, were still found dividing the possession of the same seas with the Leviathan.", "그러나 내가 직접 아는 바로는, 1850년에 이르기까지도 상어와 청어 무리, 그리고 앨와이프와 청어가 린나이우스의 분명한 칙령을 거슬러 여전히 리바이어던과 같은 바다를 나누어 차지하고 있는 것이 발견되었다."]], [["The grounds upon which Linnæus would fain have banished the whales from the waters, he states as follows: “On account of their warm bilocular heart, their lungs, their movable eyelids, their hollow ears, penem intrantem feminam mammis lactantem,”", "린나이우스가 고래를 물속에서 기어이 추방하고자 했던 근거를 그는 다음과 같이 진술한다. \"그것들이 따뜻하고 두 칸으로 나뉜 심장을 지녔고, 폐를 지녔으며, 움직이는 눈꺼풀과 속이 빈 귀를 지녔고, 페넴 인트란템 페미남 맘미스 락탄템(암컷에게 삽입하는 음경과 젖으로 새끼를 먹임)인 까닭에서다.\""], ["and finally, “ex lege naturæ jure meritoque.”", "그리고 끝으로 \"엑스 레게 나투라이 유레 메리토케(자연의 법에 따라 정당하고도 마땅하게)\"라고 했다."], ["I submitted all this to my friends Simeon Macey and Charley Coffin, of Nantucket, both messmates of mine in a certain voyage, and they united in the opinion that the reasons set forth were altogether insufficient.", "나는 이 모든 것을 내 친구인 낸터킷(Nantucket) 출신의 시므온 메이시와 찰리 코핀에게 내놓았는데, 두 사람 다 어느 항해에서 나와 한솥밥을 먹던 동료였다. 그들은 한목소리로 거기에 제시된 이유들이 도무지 충분치 않다는 의견을 내놓았다."], ["Charley profanely hinted they were humbug.", "찰리는 불경스럽게도 그것들이 다 헛소리라고 넌지시 비꼬았다."]], [["Be it known that, waiving all argument, I take the good old fashioned ground that the whale is a fish, and call upon holy Jonah to back me.", "분명히 밝혀 두지만, 나는 온갖 논쟁을 다 제쳐 두고서, 고래는 물고기라는 저 정겨운 옛 방식의 입장을 취하며, 거룩한 요나(Jonah)를 불러내어 나를 거들게 하겠다."], ["This fundamental thing settled, the next point is, in what internal respect does the whale differ from other fish.", "이 근본적인 문제가 정해졌으니, 다음으로 따져 볼 점은 고래가 내적으로 어떤 면에서 다른 물고기들과 다른가 하는 것이다. 앞서 린나이우스가 그 항목들을 그대들에게 일러 주었다."], ["Above, Linnæus has given you those items.", "그러나 간략히 말하자면 그것은 이러하다."], ["But in brief, they are these: lungs and warm blood; whereas, all other fish are lungless and cold blooded.", "폐와 따뜻한 피다. 반면 다른 모든 물고기는 폐가 없고 차가운 피를 지녔다."]], [["Next: how shall we define the whale, by his obvious externals, so as conspicuously to label him for all time to come?", "다음으로, 우리는 고래를 그 한눈에 드러나는 겉모습으로 어떻게 정의하여, 앞으로 영원토록 또렷이 그 이름표를 붙여 줄 것인가?"], ["To be short, then, a whale is a spouting fish with a horizontal tail.", "그렇다면 짧게 말해, 고래란 수평으로 누운 꼬리를 지니고 물을 뿜는 물고기다."], ["There you have him.", "자, 이것이 고래다."], ["However contracted, that definition is the result of expanded meditation.", "아무리 압축된 것이라 한들, 그 정의는 한껏 부풀려진 사색의 결실이다."], ["A walrus spouts much like a whale, but the walrus is not a fish, because he is amphibious.", "바다코끼리도 고래와 꽤 비슷하게 물을 뿜지만, 바다코끼리는 물고기가 아니니 양서(兩棲)하는 까닭이다."], ["But the last term of the definition is still more cogent, as coupled with the first.", "그러나 정의의 마지막 항목은 첫 항목과 짝을 이룰 때 한층 더 설득력을 지닌다."], ["Almost any one must have noticed that all the fish familiar to landsmen have not a flat, but a vertical, or up-and-down tail.", "뭍사람들에게 익숙한 모든 물고기가 납작한 꼬리가 아니라 수직으로 선, 곧 위아래로 놓인 꼬리를 지녔다는 것은 거의 누구나 눈여겨보았을 것이다."], ["Whereas, among spouting fish the tail, though it may be similarly shaped, invariably assumes a horizontal position.", "반면 물을 뿜는 물고기들 사이에서는, 그 꼬리가 설령 비슷한 모양일지라도 어김없이 수평으로 놓인 자세를 취한다."]], [["By the above definition of what a whale is, I do by no means exclude from the leviathanic brotherhood any sea creature hitherto identified with the whale by the best informed Nantucketers; nor, on the other hand, link with it any fish hitherto authoritatively regarded as alien.* Hence, all the smaller, spouting, and horizontal tailed fish must be included in this ground-plan of Cetology.", "고래가 무엇인지에 관한 위의 정의로써, 나는 지금껏 가장 정통한 낸터킷 사람들이 고래와 동일하게 여겨 온 그 어떤 바다 생물도 결코 리바이어던 형제단에서 배제하지 않으며, 또한 반대로 지금껏 권위 있게 이방의 것으로 간주되어 온 그 어떤 물고기도 거기에 결부시키지 않는다.* 그러므로 물을 뿜고 수평으로 누운 꼬리를 지닌 더 작은 물고기들도 모두 이 고래학의 기본 설계도 안에 포함되어야 한다."], ["Now, then, come the grand divisions of the entire whale host.", "자, 그렇다면 이제 고래 무리 전체의 큰 갈래로 들어가 보자."]], [["*I am aware that down to the present time, the fish styled Lamatins and Dugongs (Pig-fish and Sow-fish of the Coffins of Nantucket) are included by many naturalists among the whales.", "* 나는 오늘날에 이르기까지도 라마틴과 듀공(낸터킷 코핀 집안 사람들이 부르는 돼지물고기와 암퇘지물고기)이라 불리는 물고기들이 많은 박물학자들에 의해 고래에 포함되고 있음을 알고 있다."], ["But as these pig-fish are a noisy, contemptible set, mostly lurking in the mouths of rivers, and feeding on wet hay, and especially as they do not spout, I deny their credentials as whales; and have presented them with their passports to quit the Kingdom of Cetology.", "그러나 이 돼지물고기들은 시끄럽고 하찮은 무리로, 대개 강어귀에 숨어 젖은 건초를 뜯어 먹으며, 무엇보다도 물을 뿜지 않는 까닭에, 나는 그것들이 고래라는 자격을 인정하지 않으며, 그것들에게 고래학의 왕국을 떠나라는 통행증을 발급하였다."]], [["First: According to magnitude I divide the whales into three primary BOOKS (subdivisible into CHAPTERS), and these shall comprehend them all, both small and large.", "첫째, 나는 크기에 따라 고래를 세 권의 으뜸가는 책(다시 장으로 나뉠 수 있다)으로 나누는데, 이 책들이 작은 것이든 큰 것이든 그 모두를 아우르게 될 것이다."]], [["I.", "I."], ["THE FOLIO WHALE; II.", "2절판(folio) 고래. II."], ["the OCTAVO WHALE; III.", "8절판(octavo) 고래. III."], ["the DUODECIMO WHALE.", "12절판(duodecimo) 고래."]], [["As the type of the FOLIO I present the Sperm Whale; of the OCTAVO, the Grampus; of the DUODECIMO, the Porpoise.", "2절판의 대표로 나는 향유고래를 내놓고, 8절판의 대표로는 그램퍼스를, 12절판의 대표로는 돌고래를 내놓는다."]], [["FOLIOS.", "2절판."], ["Among these I here include the following chapters:—I.", "이 항목 아래 나는 다음과 같은 장들을 포함시킨다."], ["The Sperm Whale; II.", "I. 향유고래."], ["the Right Whale; III.", "II. 참고래."], ["the Fin-Back Whale; IV.", "III. 긴수염고래."], ["the Hump-backed Whale; V.", "IV. 혹등고래."], ["the Razor Back Whale; VI.", "V. 면도날등고래."], ["the Sulphur Bottom Whale.", "VI. 흰배고래."]], [["BOOK I. (Folio), CHAPTER I.", "제1책(2절판), 제1장(향유고래)."], ["(Sperm Whale).—This whale, among the English of old vaguely known as the Trumpa whale, and the Physeter whale, and the Anvil Headed whale, is the present Cachalot of the French, and the Pottsfich of the Germans, and the Macrocephalus of the Long Words.", "이 고래는 옛 영국인들 사이에서 트럼파 고래, 피세터 고래, 모루머리 고래라고 막연히 알려졌던 것으로, 오늘날 프랑스인들이 부르는 카샬로(Cachalot), 독일인들의 포츠피슈(Pottsfich), 그리고 장황한 학명을 좋아하는 이들의 마크로케팔루스(Macrocephalus)와 같은 것이다."], ["He is, without doubt, the largest inhabitant of the globe; the most formidable of all whales to encounter; the most majestic in aspect; and lastly, by far the most valuable in commerce; he being the only creature from which that valuable substance, spermaceti, is obtained.", "그것은 의심할 바 없이 지구상에서 가장 거대한 거주자이며, 모든 고래 가운데 맞닥뜨리기에 가장 무시무시하고, 그 모습이 가장 위풍당당하며, 끝으로 상업적으로도 단연 가장 값진 존재이니, 향유(spermaceti)라는 저 귀한 물질을 얻을 수 있는 유일한 피조물이기 때문이다."], ["All his peculiarities will, in many other places, be enlarged upon.", "그 모든 특이점들은 다른 여러 대목에서 자세히 다루어질 것이다."], ["It is chiefly with his name that I now have to do.", "지금 내가 다루어야 할 것은 주로 그 이름이다."], ["Philologically considered, it is absurd.", "어원학적으로 따져 보면 그것은 터무니없는 이름이다."], ["Some centuries ago, when the Sperm whale was almost wholly unknown in his own proper individuality, and when his oil was only accidentally obtained from the stranded fish; in those days spermaceti, it would seem, was popularly supposed to be derived from a creature identical with the one then known in England as the Greenland or Right Whale.", "몇 세기 전, 향유고래가 그 본래의 개별적 실체로는 거의 전혀 알려지지 않았고, 그 기름이 오로지 뭍에 떠밀려 온 물고기에서 우연히 얻어지던 시절에는, 향유라는 것이 당시 영국에서 그린란드 고래 혹은 참고래라 불리던 것과 동일한 피조물에서 나온다고 흔히들 여겼던 모양이다."], ["It was the idea also, that this same spermaceti was that quickening humor of the Greenland Whale which the first syllable of the word literally expresses.", "또한 이 향유가 그 단어의 첫 음절이 글자 그대로 나타내는 바, 곧 그린란드 고래의 생명을 북돋우는 정액이라는 생각도 있었다."], ["In those times, also, spermaceti was exceedingly scarce, not being used for light, but only as an ointment and medicament.", "그 시절에는 향유가 몹시 귀해서 등불용으로 쓰이지 않고 오로지 연고나 약재로만 쓰였다."], ["It was only to be had from the druggists as you nowadays buy an ounce of rhubarb.", "오늘날 대황 한 온스를 사듯이, 그것은 오로지 약제상에게서만 구할 수 있었다."], ["When, as I opine, in the course of time, the true nature of spermaceti became known, its original name was still retained by the dealers; no doubt to enhance its value by a notion so strangely significant of its scarcity.", "내 짐작으로는, 세월이 흐르며 향유의 참된 본성이 밝혀진 뒤에도 상인들은 그 본래의 이름을 그대로 간직했으니, 이는 분명 그 희소성을 그토록 묘하게 암시하는 이름을 통해 값어치를 한층 높이려는 속셈이었을 것이다."], ["And so the appellation must at last have come to be bestowed upon the whale from which this spermaceti was really derived.", "그리하여 마침내 그 명칭은 이 향유가 실제로 나오는 고래에게 붙여지기에 이르렀을 것이다."]], [["BOOK I. (Folio), CHAPTER II.", "제1책 (이절판), 제2장 (참고래)."], ["(Right Whale).—In one respect this is the most venerable of the leviathans, being the one first regularly hunted by man.", "한 가지 점에서 이것은 리바이어던 가운데 가장 유서 깊은 종류이니, 인간이 처음으로 정식 사냥을 시작한 대상이기 때문이다."], ["It yields the article commonly known as whalebone or baleen; and the oil specially known as “whale oil,” an inferior article in commerce.", "이놈에게서는 흔히 고래수염 혹은 발린(baleen)이라 불리는 물건이 나오며, 또한 특별히 \"고래 기름\"이라 알려진, 상업적으로는 질이 떨어지는 기름이 나온다."], ["Among the fishermen, he is indiscriminately designated by all the following titles: The Whale; the Greenland Whale; the Black Whale; the Great Whale; the True Whale; the Right Whale.", "어부들 사이에서 이놈은 다음과 같은 온갖 이름으로 두루 불린다. 고래, 그린란드고래, 검은고래, 큰고래, 참고래(True Whale), 참고래(Right Whale)."], ["There is a deal of obscurity concerning the identity of the species thus multitudinously baptised.", "이처럼 무수한 이름으로 세례를 받은 이 종(種)의 정체에 관해서는 모호함이 많다."], ["What then is the whale, which I include in the second species of my Folios?", "그렇다면 내가 이절판의 두 번째 종으로 포함시킨 이 고래는 대체 무엇인가?"], ["It is the Great Mysticetus of the English naturalists; the Greenland Whale of the English whalemen; the Baleine Ordinaire of the French whalemen; the Growlands Walfish of the Swedes.", "그것은 영국 박물학자들이 말하는 큰미스티케투스(Great Mysticetus)요, 영국 고래잡이들이 말하는 그린란드고래요, 프랑스 고래잡이들이 말하는 발렌 오르디네르(Baleine Ordinaire)요, 스웨덴 사람들이 말하는 그뢴란드발피슈(Growlands Walfish)다."], ["It is the whale which for more than two centuries past has been hunted by the Dutch and English in the Arctic seas; it is the whale which the American fishermen have long pursued in the Indian ocean, on the Brazil Banks, on the Nor’ West Coast, and various other parts of the world, designated by them Right Whale Cruising Grounds.", "그것은 지난 두 세기가 넘도록 네덜란드인과 영국인이 북극해에서 사냥해 온 고래요, 미국 어부들이 인도양에서, 브라질 어장에서, 북서 해안에서, 그리고 세계의 여러 다른 곳에서 오랫동안 뒤쫓아 온 고래로서, 그들은 그곳들을 참고래 조업 어장이라 부른다."]], [["Some pretend to see a difference between the Greenland whale of the English and the right whale of the Americans.", "어떤 이들은 영국인의 그린란드고래와 미국인의 참고래 사이에 차이가 있다고 우긴다."], ["But they precisely agree in all their grand features; nor has there yet been presented a single determinate fact upon which to ground a radical distinction.", "그러나 그 둘은 모든 중요한 특징에서 정확히 일치하며, 근본적인 구별의 근거가 될 만한 단 하나의 확정적 사실조차 아직 제시된 바 없다."], ["It is by endless subdivisions based upon the most inconclusive differences, that some departments of natural history become so repellingly intricate.", "박물학의 어떤 분야들이 그토록 진저리 나게 복잡해지는 것은, 가장 결론 없는 차이에 기댄 끝없는 세분화 때문이다."], ["The right whale will be elsewhere treated of at some length, with reference to elucidating the sperm whale.", "참고래는 다른 곳에서 향유고래를 해설하는 데 견주어 가며 어느 정도 길게 다루어질 것이다."]], [["BOOK I.", "제1책."], ["(Folio), CHAPTER III.", "(이절판), 제3장."], ["(Fin-Back).—Under this head I reckon a monster which, by the various names of Fin-Back, Tall-Spout, and Long-John, has been seen almost in every sea and is commonly the whale whose distant jet is so often descried by passengers crossing the Atlantic, in the New York packet-tracks.", "(긴수염고래).—이 항목 아래에 나는 한 마리 괴물을 분류해 넣으니, 긴수염고래, 높은물줄기고래, 긴존이라는 여러 이름으로 거의 모든 바다에서 목격되어 왔으며, 뉴욕 정기선 항로를 따라 대서양을 건너는 승객들이 그 멀리서 솟는 물줄기를 흔히 발견하게 되는 바로 그 고래다."], ["In the length he attains, and in his baleen, the Fin-back resembles the right whale, but is of a less portly girth, and a lighter colour, approaching to olive.", "도달하는 몸길이와 고래수염에 있어서 긴수염고래는 참고래를 닮았으나, 둘레는 그만큼 우람하지 않고, 빛깔은 더 옅어 올리브색에 가깝다."], ["His great lips present a cable-like aspect, formed by the intertwisting, slanting folds of large wrinkles.", "그 거대한 입술은, 큼직한 주름들이 비스듬히 겹쳐 서로 얽히면서 빚어내는 밧줄 같은 모양을 띤다."], ["His grand distinguishing feature, the fin, from which he derives his name, is often a conspicuous object.", "그 이름의 유래가 된 위대한 식별 특징인 지느러미는 흔히 눈에 잘 띄는 대상이다."], ["This fin is some three or four feet long, growing vertically from the hinder part of the back, of an angular shape, and with a very sharp pointed end.", "이 지느러미는 길이가 서너 자쯤 되며, 등의 뒤쪽에서 수직으로 솟아 모난 형태를 이루고, 끝이 매우 날카롭게 뾰족하다."], ["Even if not the slightest other part of the creature be visible, this isolated fin will, at times, be seen plainly projecting from the surface.", "설령 이 짐승의 다른 부분이 조금도 보이지 않을 때라도, 이 외따로 떨어진 지느러미만은 이따금 수면 위로 또렷이 솟아오른 것이 목격되곤 한다."], ["When the sea is moderately calm, and slightly marked with spherical ripples, and this gnomon-like fin stands up and casts shadows upon the wrinkled surface, it may well be supposed that the watery circle surrounding it somewhat resembles a dial, with its style and wavy hour-lines graved on it.", "바다가 어지간히 잔잔하고 둥근 잔물결이 잘게 일렁이는 가운데 이 해시계 바늘 같은 지느러미가 곧추서서 주름진 수면 위에 그림자를 드리울 때면, 그것을 에워싼 물의 둥근 테두리가 마치 바늘과 물결무늬 시각선이 새겨진 해시계와 얼마간 닮았다고 여길 만도 하다."], ["On that Ahaz-dial the shadow often goes back.", "그 아하스의 해시계 위에서 그림자는 흔히 뒤로 물러난다."], ["The Fin-Back is not gregarious.", "긴수염고래는 무리를 짓지 않는다."], ["He seems a whale-hater, as some men are man-haters.", "어떤 사람들이 인간 혐오자이듯, 이놈은 고래 혐오자처럼 보인다."], ["Very shy; always going solitary; unexpectedly rising to the surface in the remotest and most sullen waters; his straight and single lofty jet rising like a tall misanthropic spear upon a barren plain; gifted with such wondrous power and velocity in swimming, as to defy all present pursuit from man; this leviathan seems the banished and unconquerable Cain of his race, bearing for his mark that style upon his back.", "몹시 수줍어서 늘 홀로 다니며, 가장 멀고 가장 음울한 바다에서 느닷없이 수면 위로 떠오른다. 그 곧고 외줄기로 높이 솟는 물줄기는 황량한 들판 위에 우뚝 선 키 큰 염세가의 창처럼 치솟고, 헤엄에 있어 어찌나 놀라운 힘과 속도를 타고났는지 인간의 그 어떤 추격도 당장에 비웃어 버린다."], ["From having the baleen in his mouth, the Fin-Back is sometimes included with the right whale, among a theoretic species denominated Whalebone whales, that is, whales with baleen.", "이 리바이어던은 제 종족 가운데 추방된, 결코 정복되지 않는 카인처럼 보이며, 그 표징으로 등에 저 바늘을 지고 다닌다. 입속에 고래수염을 지닌 까닭에, 긴수염고래는 때로 참고래와 더불어 고래수염고래라 불리는 이론상의 종(種), 곧 고래수염을 지닌 고래들 무리에 포함된다."], ["Of these so called Whalebone whales, there would seem to be several varieties, most of which, however, are little known.", "이른바 고래수염고래라 일컫는 이것들에는 여러 변종이 있는 듯하나, 그 대부분은 거의 알려져 있지 않다."], ["Broad-nosed whales and beaked whales; pike-headed whales; bunched whales; under-jawed whales and rostrated whales, are the fishermen’s names for a few sorts.", "넓적코고래와 부리고래, 창머리고래, 혹고래, 아래턱고래와 주둥이고래 따위가 몇몇 종류에 대한 어부들의 이름이다."]], [["In connection with this appellative of “Whalebone whales,” it is of great importance to mention, that however such a nomenclature may be convenient in facilitating allusions to some kind of whales, yet it is in vain to attempt a clear classification of the Leviathan, founded upon either his baleen, or hump, or fin, or teeth; notwithstanding that those marked parts or features very obviously seem better adapted to afford the basis for a regular system of Cetology than any other detached bodily distinctions, which the whale, in his kinds, presents.", "이 “고래수염고래”라는 호칭과 관련하여 매우 중요하게 언급해 둘 것이 있으니, 그러한 명명법이 어떤 종류의 고래들을 가리키는 일을 수월하게 하는 데에는 편리할지 몰라도, 그 고래수염이든 혹이든 지느러미든 이빨이든 그 어느 것을 근거로 삼아 리바이어던을 명확히 분류하려는 시도는 헛되다는 점이다."], ["How then? The baleen, hump, back-fin, and teeth; these are things whose peculiarities are indiscriminately dispersed among all sorts of whales, without any regard to what may be the nature of their structure in other and more essential particulars.", "그런 두드러진 부위나 특징들이, 고래가 그 여러 종류에 걸쳐 드러내는 다른 어떤 떨어진 신체적 구별점들보다도 정연한 고래학 체계의 기초를 제공하기에 더 알맞아 보이는 것이 너무도 명백한데도 그러하다."], ["Thus, the sperm whale and the humpbacked whale, each has a hump; but there the similitude ceases.", "그러면 어찌 된 일인가? 고래수염, 혹, 등지느러미, 이빨—이런 것들은 그 구조가 다른 더 본질적인 세부에서 어떤 성질을 띠는지와는 아랑곳없이, 그 특이성이 온갖 종류의 고래들 사이에 마구잡이로 흩어져 있는 것들이다."], ["Then, this same humpbacked whale and the Greenland whale, each of these has baleen; but there again the similitude ceases.", "그리하여 향유고래와 혹등고래는 저마다 혹을 지녔으되, 닮음은 거기서 그친다. 그런가 하면 이 같은 혹등고래와 그린란드고래는 저마다 고래수염을 지녔으되, 또다시 닮음은 거기서 그친다."], ["And it is just the same with the other parts above mentioned.", "그리고 앞서 말한 다른 부위들도 사정은 매한가지다."], ["In various sorts of whales, they form such irregular combinations; or, in the case of any one of them detached, such an irregular isolation; as utterly to defy all general methodization formed upon such a basis.", "여러 종류의 고래들에서 그것들은 그토록 불규칙한 조합을 이루거나, 혹은 그중 어느 하나만 떼어 놓고 보면 그토록 불규칙하게 고립되어 있어서, 그러한 기초 위에 세운 그 어떤 일반적 체계화도 완전히 무색하게 만든다."], ["On this rock every one of the whale-naturalists has split.", "고래 박물학자라면 누구나 이 바위에 부딪쳐 좌초하고 말았다."]], [["But it may possibly be conceived that, in the internal parts of the whale, in his anatomy—there, at least, we shall be able to hit the right classification.", "그러나 어쩌면 고래의 내부, 곧 그 해부 구조 안에서라면—적어도 거기서는 올바른 분류에 다다를 수 있으리라 생각할 수도 있다."], ["Nay; what thing, for example, is there in the Greenland whale’s anatomy more striking than his baleen?", "천만에. 이를테면 그린란드고래의 해부 구조에서 그 고래수염보다 더 두드러진 것이 무엇이 있겠는가?"], ["Yet we have seen that by his baleen it is impossible correctly to classify the Greenland whale.", "그런데도 우리는 그 고래수염으로는 그린란드고래를 정확히 분류하기가 불가능하다는 것을 이미 보았다."], ["And if you descend into the bowels of the various leviathans, why there you will not find distinctions a fiftieth part as available to the systematizer as those external ones already enumerated.", "또한 여러 리바이어던의 내장 속으로 내려가 본들, 거기서는 이미 열거한 저 외부의 특징들에 견주어 오십분의 일만큼도 분류학자에게 쓸모 있는 구별점을 찾아내지 못할 것이다."], ["What then remains?", "그러면 무엇이 남는가?"], ["nothing but to take hold of the whales bodily, in their entire liberal volume, and boldly sort them that way.", "고래들을 통째로, 그 너그러운 부피 전체로 움켜쥐고서 대담하게 그런 식으로 분류하는 길밖에 없다."], ["And this is the Bibliographical system here adopted; and it is the only one that can possibly succeed, for it alone is practicable.", "그리고 이것이야말로 여기서 채택한 서지학적 분류 체계이며, 성공할 가망이 있는 유일한 방법이니, 오직 그것만이 실행 가능하기 때문이다."], ["To proceed.", "그럼 계속하자."]], [["BOOK I.", "제1권."], ["(Folio) CHAPTER IV.", "(이절판) 제4장."], ["(Hump Back).—This whale is often seen on the northern American coast.", "(혹등고래.)―이 고래는 북아메리카 연안에서 자주 눈에 띈다."], ["He has been frequently captured there, and towed into harbor.", "그곳에서 빈번히 잡혀 항구로 끌려오곤 했다."], ["He has a great pack on him like a peddler; or you might call him the Elephant and Castle whale.", "등에는 행상인처럼 커다란 짐 보따리를 짊어진 듯한 혹이 있어, 코끼리와 성(城) 고래라 불러도 무방할 것이다."], ["At any rate, the popular name for him does not sufficiently distinguish him, since the sperm whale also has a hump though a smaller one.", "어쨌거나 이 통속적인 이름만으로는 그를 충분히 구별해 내지 못하는데, 향유고래(Sperm Whale) 역시 비록 더 작긴 하나 혹을 지니고 있기 때문이다."], ["His oil is not very valuable.", "그 기름은 그다지 값나가지 않는다."], ["He has baleen.", "고래수염을 지녔다."], ["He is the most gamesome and light-hearted of all the whales, making more gay foam and white water generally than any other of them.", "모든 고래 가운데 가장 장난기 많고 쾌활한 녀석으로, 대체로 다른 어떤 고래보다도 더 흥겨운 거품과 새하얀 물보라를 일으킨다."]], [["BOOK I. (Folio), CHAPTER V.", "제1책(이절판), 제5장(레이저 백).—이 고래에 관해서는 그 이름 말고는 알려진 바가 거의 없다."], ["(Razor Back).—Of this whale little is known but his name. I have seen him at a distance off Cape Horn.", "나는 케이프 혼 앞바다에서 멀찍이 그를 본 적이 있다."], ["Of a retiring nature, he eludes both hunters and philosophers.", "천성이 은둔을 즐기는지라, 그는 사냥꾼도 철학자도 모두 따돌린다."], ["Though no coward, he has never yet shown any part of him but his back, which rises in a long sharp ridge.", "비겁자는 아니지만, 그는 아직껏 길고 날카로운 등마루처럼 솟아오르는 등 말고는 제 몸의 어느 한 부분도 드러내 보인 적이 없다."], ["Let him go.", "가게 내버려 두자."], ["I know little more of him, nor does anybody else.", "나도 그에 관해서는 그 이상 아는 바가 없으며, 다른 누구도 마찬가지다."], ["BOOK I. (Folio), CHAPTER VI.", "제1책(이절판), 제6장(설퍼 보텀).—또 한 명의 은둔자다."], ["(Sulphur Bottom).—Another retiring gentleman, with a brimstone belly, doubtless got by scraping along the Tartarian tiles in some of his profounder divings.", "유황빛 배를 지녔는데, 이는 필시 더 깊은 잠수를 하던 어느 때에 저 지옥의 바닥 타일을 긁으며 다닌 탓에 얻은 것이리라. 그는 좀처럼 모습을 보이지 않는다."], ["He is seldom seen; at least I have never seen him except in the remoter southern seas, and then always at too great a distance to study his countenance.", "적어도 나는 저 멀고 외진 남쪽 바다에서가 아니고는 그를 본 적이 없으며, 그나마도 늘 그 얼굴을 살피기에는 너무 먼 거리에서였다. 그는 결코 추격당하지 않는다."], ["He is never chased; he would run away with rope-walks of line.", "추격하다가는 밧줄 공장 하나 분량의 작살 줄을 통째로 끌고 달아나 버릴 테니."], ["Prodigies are told of him.", "그에 관해서는 온갖 기이한 이야기가 전해진다."], ["Adieu, Sulphur Bottom!", "잘 가게, 설퍼 보텀이여!"], ["I can say nothing more that is true of ye, nor can the oldest Nantucketer.", "그대에 관해 더는 참된 것을 한마디도 할 수 없으니, 가장 나이 든 낸터킷 사람이라 한들 마찬가지다."]], [["Thus ends BOOK I. (Folio), and now begins BOOK II. (Octavo).", "이로써 제1책(이절판)이 끝나고, 이제 제2책(팔절판)이 시작된다."]], [["OCTAVOES.*—These embrace the whales of middling magnitude, among which present may be numbered:—I., the Grampus; II., the Black Fish; III., the Narwhale; IV., the Thrasher; V., the Killer.", "팔절판류.*—여기에는 중간 크기의 고래들이 속하는데, 현재로서는 다음과 같은 것들을 꼽을 수 있다.—제1, 그램퍼스. 제2, 블랙 피시. 제3, 일각고래. 제4, 스래셔. 제5, 킬러."]], [["*Why this book of whales is not denominated the Quarto is very plain.", "*고래에 관한 이 책을 어째서 사절판이라 부르지 않는지는 매우 분명하다."], ["Because, while the whales of this order, though smaller than those of the former order, nevertheless retain a proportionate likeness to them in figure, yet the bookbinder’s Quarto volume in its dimensioned form does not preserve the shape of the Folio volume, but the Octavo volume does.", "이 부류의 고래들이 비록 앞 부류의 고래들보다 작기는 하지만 그래도 그 형태에서 비례적인 닮음을 지니고 있는데, 제본업자의 사절판 책은 그 규격화된 형태에서 이절판 책의 모양을 보존하지 못하는 반면, 팔절판 책은 그것을 보존하기 때문이다."]], [["BOOK II.", "제2서."], ["(Octavo), CHAPTER I.", "(8절판), 제1장 (그램퍼스)."], ["(Grampus).—Though this fish, whose loud sonorous breathing, or rather blowing, has furnished a proverb to landsmen, is so well known a denizen of the deep, yet is he not popularly classed among whales.", "— 그 우렁차고 낭랑한 숨결, 아니 차라리 물뿜기로써 뭍사람들에게 속담 하나를 마련해 준 이 물고기는, 심해에 사는 친숙한 주민으로 잘 알려져 있으면서도 흔히 고래의 무리에 끼이지는 않는다."], ["But possessing all the grand distinctive features of the leviathan, most naturalists have recognised him for one.", "그러나 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 위대한 특징들을 모두 갖추고 있는 까닭에, 대부분의 박물학자들은 이놈을 고래의 일종으로 인정해 왔다."], ["He is of moderate octavo size, varying from fifteen to twenty-five feet in length, and of corresponding dimensions round the waist.", "이놈은 적당한 8절판 크기로, 길이는 15피트(약 4.6미터)에서 25피트(약 7.6미터)까지 다양하며, 허리둘레도 그에 걸맞은 치수를 지닌다."], ["He swims in herds; he is never regularly hunted, though his oil is considerable in quantity, and pretty good for light.", "떼를 지어 헤엄치고, 비록 그 기름이 양으로 보아 제법 상당하고 등불용으로도 꽤 쓸 만하지만 정식으로 사냥당하는 일은 결코 없다."], ["By some fishermen his approach is regarded as premonitory of the advance of the great sperm whale.", "어떤 어부들은 이놈이 다가오는 것을 보고 거대한 향유고래(Sperm Whale)가 곧 나타날 전조로 여기기도 한다."]], [["BOOK II. (Octavo), CHAPTER II.", "제2서 (8절판), 제2장 (흑어).—나는 이 모든 물고기에 대해 어부들이 흔히 쓰는 이름을 그대로 옮긴다."], ["(Black Fish).—I give the popular fishermen’s names for all these fish, for generally they are the best.", "대체로 그것이 가장 나은 이름이기 때문이다."], ["Where any name happens to be vague or inexpressive, I shall say so, and suggest another.", "어떤 이름이 모호하거나 그 특징을 제대로 드러내지 못할 경우에는 그렇다고 말하고 다른 이름을 제안하겠다."], ["I do so now, touching the Black Fish, so-called, because blackness is the rule among almost all whales.", "흔히 흑어(黑魚)라 불리는 이 물고기를 두고 지금 바로 그렇게 하려 한다. 검은빛이라는 것은 거의 모든 고래에게 공통된 특징이기 때문이다."], ["So, call him the Hyena Whale, if you please.", "그러니 원한다면 이놈을 하이에나고래라 부르기로 하자."], ["His voracity is well known, and from the circumstance that the inner angles of his lips are curved upwards, he carries an everlasting Mephistophelean grin on his face.", "그 게걸스러움은 익히 알려져 있고, 입술의 안쪽 끝이 위로 휘어 올라간 까닭에 그 얼굴에는 영원히 메피스토펠레스 같은 능글맞은 웃음이 걸려 있다."], ["This whale averages some sixteen or eighteen feet in length.", "이 고래는 평균 16피트에서 18피트(약 5미터에서 5.5미터) 정도의 길이다."], ["He is found in almost all latitudes.", "거의 모든 위도에서 발견된다."], ["He has a peculiar way of showing his dorsal hooked fin in swimming, which looks something like a Roman nose.", "헤엄칠 때 등의 갈고리 모양 지느러미를 드러내는 독특한 방식이 있는데, 그 모습이 어쩐지 로마식 매부리코를 닮았다."], ["When not more profitably employed, the sperm whale hunters sometimes capture the Hyena whale, to keep up the supply of cheap oil for domestic employment—as some frugal housekeepers, in the absence of company, and quite alone by themselves, burn unsavory tallow instead of odorous wax.", "더 수지맞는 일에 매여 있지 않을 때면, 향유고래 사냥꾼들은 가끔 이 하이에나고래를 잡아 가정용으로 쓸 값싼 기름의 공급을 이어 가곤 한다 — 마치 알뜰한 살림꾼이 손님도 없이 혼자 지낼 때면 향기로운 밀랍 대신 냄새 고약한 짐승 기름을 태우는 것과 마찬가지다."], ["Though their blubber is very thin, some of these whales will yield you upwards of thirty gallons of oil.", "비록 이 고래들의 지방층은 매우 얇지만, 그래도 그중 어떤 놈은 30갤런(약 114리터)이 넘는 기름을 내주기도 할 것이다."]], [["BOOK II. (Octavo), CHAPTER III.", "제2권(8절판), 제3장(외뿔고래), 곧 콧구멍 고래다."], ["(Narwhale), that is, Nostril whale.—Another instance of a curiously named whale, so named I suppose from his peculiar horn being originally mistaken for a peaked nose.", "기묘하게 이름 붙은 고래의 또 하나의 사례로, 짐작건대 그 독특한 뿔이 본래 뾰족한 코로 잘못 여겨졌던 데서 그런 이름이 붙은 듯하다."], ["The creature is some sixteen feet in length, while its horn averages five feet, though some exceed ten, and even attain to fifteen feet.", "이 동물은 길이가 약 16피트(약 4.9미터)인데, 그 뿔은 평균 5피트(약 1.5미터)에 이르며, 더러는 10피트(약 3미터)를 넘기고, 심지어 15피트(약 4.6미터)에까지 닿는 것도 있다."], ["Strictly speaking, this horn is but a lengthened tusk, growing out from the jaw in a line a little depressed from the horizontal.", "엄밀히 말하면 이 뿔은 길게 자라난 엄니에 지나지 않으니, 수평에서 약간 아래로 기운 선을 따라 턱에서 뻗어 나온다."], ["But it is only found on the sinister side, which has an ill effect, giving its owner something analogous to the aspect of a clumsy left-handed man.", "그런데 그것은 왼쪽에만 나 있어 보기에 좋지 않은 인상을 주는데, 그 주인을 어딘지 서툰 왼손잡이 사내와 비슷한 모습으로 만들어 버린다."], ["What precise purpose this ivory horn or lance answers, it would be hard to say.", "이 상아 뿔, 곧 창이 정확히 어떤 쓸모를 지니는지는 말하기 어렵다."], ["It does not seem to be used like the blade of the sword-fish and bill-fish; though some sailors tell me that the Narwhale employs it for a rake in turning over the bottom of the sea for food.", "그것은 황새치나 부리고래의 칼날처럼 쓰이는 것 같지는 않다. 그래도 어떤 선원들은 외뿔고래가 그것을 갈퀴 삼아 바다 밑바닥을 헤집어 먹이를 찾는 데 쓴다고 내게 말해 준다."], ["Charley Coffin said it was used for an ice-piercer; for the Narwhale, rising to the surface of the Polar Sea, and finding it sheeted with ice, thrusts his horn up, and so breaks through.", "찰리 코핀(Charley Coffin)은 그것이 얼음을 뚫는 데 쓰인다고 말했다. 외뿔고래가 북극해의 수면으로 떠오르다 그곳이 온통 얼음으로 덮여 있는 것을 보면, 제 뿔을 위로 치밀어 그렇게 얼음을 깨고 나온다는 것이다."], ["But you cannot prove either of these surmises to be correct.", "그러나 이 추측들 중 어느 하나도 옳다고 증명할 수는 없다."], ["My own opinion is, that however this one-sided horn may really be used by the Narwhale—however that may be—it would certainly be very convenient to him for a folder in reading pamphlets.", "내 개인적인 견해로는, 외뿔고래가 이 한쪽으로만 난 뿔을 실제로 어떻게 쓰든 간에 — 그것이 어떻든 — 소책자를 읽을 때 책장을 누르는 종이칼로는 분명 무척 요긴할 것이라는 점이다."], ["The Narwhale I have heard called the Tusked whale, the Horned whale, and the Unicorn whale.", "외뿔고래는 엄니고래, 뿔고래, 일각수고래라고 불리는 것을 나는 들어 왔다."], ["He is certainly a curious example of the Unicornism to be found in almost every kingdom of animated nature.", "그것은 살아 있는 자연의 거의 모든 왕국에서 찾아볼 수 있는 일각수성(一角獸性)의 기묘한 한 본보기임에 틀림없다."], ["From certain cloistered old authors I have gathered that this same sea-unicorn’s horn was in ancient days regarded as the great antidote against poison, and as such, preparations of it brought immense prices.", "어느 수도원에 틀어박혔던 옛 저자들로부터 나는, 이 바다 일각수의 뿔이 옛날에는 독에 대항하는 위대한 해독제로 여겨졌으며, 그런 까닭에 그것으로 만든 조제약이 어마어마한 값에 팔렸다는 사실을 알게 되었다."], ["It was also distilled to a volatile salts for fainting ladies, the same way that the horns of the male deer are manufactured into hartshorn.", "그것은 또 정신을 잃은 귀부인들을 위한 휘발성 소금으로 증류되기도 했으니, 수사슴의 뿔을 녹용정(鹿茸精)으로 만들어 내는 것과 같은 방식이었다."], ["Originally it was in itself accounted an object of great curiosity.", "본래 그것은 그 자체로 커다란 호기심의 대상으로 여겨졌다."], ["Black Letter tells me that Sir Martin Frobisher on his return from that voyage, when Queen Bess did gallantly wave her jewelled hand to him from a window of Greenwich Palace, as his bold ship sailed down the Thames; “when Sir Martin returned from that voyage,”", "흑체활자본(黑體活字本)이 내게 전하는 바로는, 마틴 프로비셔 경이 그 항해에서 돌아왔을 때 — 그의 대담한 배가 템스강을 따라 내려갈 적에 엘리자베스 여왕이 그리니치 궁전의 창가에서 보석 박힌 손을 늠름하게 흔들어 보였다는 그 항해에서 돌아왔을 때 — \"마틴 경이 그 항해에서 돌아왔을 때,\""], ["saith Black", "흑체활자본은 이르기를,"]], [["Letter, “on bended knees he presented to her highness a prodigious long horn of the Narwhale, which for a long period after hung in the castle at Windsor.”", "무릎을 꿇은 채 \"그는 외뿔고래의 어마어마하게 긴 뿔 하나를 폐하께 바쳤는데, 그것은 그 뒤로도 오랫동안 윈저 성에 걸려 있었다.\""], ["An Irish author avers that the Earl of Leicester, on bended knees, did likewise present to her highness another horn, pertaining to a land beast of the unicorn nature.", "어느 아일랜드 작가는 레스터 백작이 무릎을 꿇은 채, 외뿔 짐승의 부류에 속하는 또 다른 뿔, 곧 육지 짐승의 뿔을 폐하께 마찬가지로 바쳤다고 주장한다."]], [["The Narwhale has a very picturesque, leopard-like look, being of a milk-white ground colour, dotted with round and oblong spots of black.", "외뿔고래는 매우 그림 같은, 표범을 닮은 모습을 하고 있는데, 우윳빛 바탕에 검은 둥근 점과 길쭉한 점들이 흩뿌려져 있기 때문이다."], ["His oil is very superior, clear and fine; but there is little of it, and he is seldom hunted.", "그 기름은 더없이 우수하여 맑고 고우나, 그 양이 적고, 좀처럼 사냥당하지 않는다."], ["He is mostly found in the circumpolar seas.", "이놈은 대개 극지방을 둘러싼 바다에서 발견된다."]], [["BOOK II. (Octavo), CHAPTER IV.", "제2부(8절판), 제4장(킬러)."], ["(Killer).—Of this whale little is precisely known to the Nantucketer, and nothing at all to the professed naturalist.", "이 고래에 관해서는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람조차 정확히 아는 바가 거의 없고, 자칭 박물학자에 이르러서는 그야말로 아무것도 알지 못한다."], ["From what I have seen of him at a distance, I should say that he was about the bigness of a grampus.", "멀리서 본 바에 따르면, 나로서는 그 크기가 큰돌고래만 하다고 말할 수 있겠다."], ["He is very savage—a sort of Feegee fish.", "이놈은 매우 사나운데, 일종의 피지(Feegee)산 물고기라 할 만하다."], ["He sometimes takes the great Folio whales by the lip, and hangs there like a leech, till the mighty brute is worried to death.", "이놈은 이따금 거대한 2절판 고래의 입술을 물고는, 거머리처럼 거기 매달려 있다가, 그 힘센 짐승이 시달리다 못해 죽음에 이르게 만든다."], ["The Killer is never hunted.", "킬러는 결코 사냥의 대상이 되지 않는다."], ["I never heard what sort of oil he has.", "이놈이 어떤 종류의 기름을 지녔는지 나는 들어 본 적이 없다."], ["Exception might be taken to the name bestowed upon this whale, on the ground of its indistinctness.", "이 고래에 붙은 이름에 대해서는, 그 모호함을 이유로 이의를 제기할 만하다."], ["For we are all killers, on land and on sea; Bonapartes and Sharks included.", "왜냐하면 우리 모두가, 뭍에서든 바다에서든 살인자들이기 때문이다. 보나파르트도 상어도 거기에 포함된다."]], [["BOOK II. (Octavo), CHAPTER V.", "제2서(8절판), 제5장(타작고래)."], ["(Thrasher).—This gentleman is famous for his tail, which he uses for a ferule in thrashing his foes.", "— 이 신사가 이름을 떨치는 것은 그 꼬리 덕분인데, 그는 그것을 적을 후려치는 회초리로 쓴다."], ["He mounts the Folio whale’s back, and as he swims, he works his passage by flogging him; as some schoolmasters get along in the world by a similar process.", "그는 2절판 고래의 등에 올라타, 헤엄을 치면서 상대를 채찍질하여 제 갈 길을 헤쳐 나간다. 마치 어떤 학교 선생들이 비슷한 방식으로 세상을 살아가는 것처럼 말이다."], ["Still less is known of the Thrasher than of the Killer.", "타작고래에 관해서는 범고래보다도 더 알려진 바가 적다."], ["Both are outlaws, even in the lawless seas.", "둘 다 무법자이니, 무법천지인 바다에서조차 그러하다."]], [["Thus ends BOOK II. (Octavo), and begins BOOK III. (Duodecimo).", "이로써 제2서(8절판)가 끝나고, 제3서(12절판)가 시작된다."]], [["DUODECIMOES.—These include the smaller whales.", "12절판. — 여기에는 비교적 작은 고래들이 포함된다."], ["I. The Huzza Porpoise.", "첫째, 만세돌고래."], ["II. The Algerine Porpoise.", "둘째, 알제리돌고래."], ["III. The Mealy-mouthed Porpoise.", "셋째, 입조심돌고래."]], [["To those who have not chanced specially to study the subject, it may possibly seem strange, that fishes not commonly exceeding four or five feet should be marshalled among WHALES—a word, which, in the popular sense, always conveys an idea of hugeness.", "이 주제를 특별히 연구해 본 적이 없는 이들에게는, 흔히 길이가 네다섯 자를 넘지 않는 물고기들이 고래들 사이에 줄지어 놓이는 것이 어쩌면 이상해 보일지도 모른다. 고래라는 말은 통속적인 의미에서 언제나 거대함의 관념을 전하기 때문이다."], ["But the creatures set down above as Duodecimoes are infallibly whales, by the terms of my definition of what a whale is—i.e.", "그러나 위에서 12절판으로 분류해 둔 이 생물들은, 고래란 무엇인가에 대한 나의 정의에 따르면 틀림없는 고래다."], ["a spouting fish, with a horizontal tail.", "곧, 수평으로 누운 꼬리를 지니고 물을 뿜는 물고기인 것이다."]], [["BOOK III.", "제3권."], ["(Duodecimo), CHAPTER 1.", "(12절판), 제1장."], ["(Huzza Porpoise).—This is the common porpoise found almost all over the globe.", "(만세돌고래).—이것은 거의 전 세계에 걸쳐 발견되는 흔한 돌고래다."], ["The name is of my own bestowal; for there are more than one sort of porpoises, and something must be done to distinguish them.", "이 이름은 내가 직접 붙인 것인데, 돌고래에도 여러 종류가 있어 그것들을 구별할 무언가가 필요하기 때문이다."], ["I call him thus, because he always swims in hilarious shoals, which upon the broad sea keep tossing themselves to heaven like caps in a Fourth-of-July crowd.", "내가 이놈을 이렇게 부르는 까닭은, 이놈이 언제나 흥에 겨운 무리를 지어 헤엄치며, 드넓은 바다 위에서 독립기념일 군중 속의 모자들처럼 하늘을 향해 제 몸을 끊임없이 솟구쳐 올리기 때문이다."], ["Their appearance is generally hailed with delight by the mariner.", "이놈들의 모습은 대체로 뱃사람들에게 기쁨으로 환영받는다."], ["Full of fine spirits, they invariably come from the breezy billows to windward.", "활기가 넘치는 이놈들은 어김없이 바람 부는 쪽의 산들거리는 물결에서 다가온다."], ["They are the lads that always live before the wind.", "이놈들은 늘 바람 앞에 사는 녀석들이다."], ["They are accounted a lucky omen.", "행운의 징조로 여겨지기도 한다."], ["If you yourself can withstand three cheers at beholding these vivacious fish, then heaven help ye; the spirit of godly gamesomeness is not in ye.", "만약 당신이 이 생기발랄한 물고기를 보고도 만세 삼창을 참아 낼 수 있다면, 부디 하늘이 당신을 도우시기를. 당신에게는 경건한 유희의 정신이 깃들어 있지 않은 것이다."], ["A well-fed, plump Huzza Porpoise will yield you one good gallon of good oil.", "잘 먹어 토실토실한 만세돌고래 한 마리는 질 좋은 기름 한 갤런을 내준다."], ["But the fine and delicate fluid extracted from his jaws is exceedingly valuable.", "그러나 그 턱에서 추출되는 곱고 섬세한 액체는 더없이 귀하다."], ["It is in request among jewellers and watchmakers.", "보석 세공인과 시계공 사이에서 수요가 있다."], ["Sailors put it on their hones.", "선원들은 그것을 숫돌에 바른다."], ["Porpoise meat is good eating, you know.", "돌고래 고기는 먹을 만하다고들 한다."], ["It may never have occurred to you that a porpoise spouts.", "돌고래가 물을 뿜는다는 사실은 어쩌면 당신이 미처 생각해 본 적 없을지도 모른다."], ["Indeed, his spout is so small that it is not very readily discernible.", "사실 이놈의 물줄기는 너무 작아서 좀처럼 쉽게 알아보기 어렵다."], ["But the next time you have a chance, watch him; and you will then see the great Sperm whale himself in miniature.", "그러나 다음에 기회가 닿거든 이놈을 잘 지켜보라. 그러면 저 위대한 향유고래(Sperm whale)를 그대로 축소해 놓은 모습을 보게 될 것이다."]], [["BOOK III.", "매우 사납다."], ["(Duodecimo), CHAPTER II. (Algerine Porpoise).—A pirate.", "내 생각에 이놈은 오로지 태평양에서만 발견된다."], ["Very savage. He is only found, I think, in the Pacific.", "환호 돌고래보다 다소 크지만, 전체적인 생김새는 거의 같다."], ["He is somewhat larger than the Huzza Porpoise, but much of the same general make.", "이놈을 화나게 하면 상어에게도 덤벼들 것이다."], ["Provoke him, and he will buckle to a shark. I have lowered for him many times, but never yet saw him captured.", "나는 이놈을 잡으려고 여러 번 보트를 내렸지만, 아직껏 한 번도 포획되는 것을 본 적이 없다."], ["BOOK III. (Duodecimo), CHAPTER III.", "제3권(12절판) 제3장(가루입 돌고래)."], ["(Mealy-mouthed Porpoise).—The largest kind of Porpoise; and only found in the Pacific, so far as it is known.", "돌고래 중에서도 가장 큰 종류이며, 알려진 한에서는 오로지 태평양에서만 발견된다."], ["The only English name, by which he has hitherto been designated, is that of the fishers—Right-Whale Porpoise, from the circumstance that he is chiefly found in the vicinity of that Folio.", "지금까지 이놈에게 붙여진 유일한 영어 이름은 어부들이 부르는 명칭, 곧 참고래 돌고래인데, 이는 이놈이 주로 그 2절판 고래의 근처에서 발견되기 때문이다."], ["In shape, he differs in some degree from the Huzza Porpoise, being of a less rotund and jolly girth; indeed, he is of quite a neat and gentleman-like figure.", "생김새로 보면 환호 돌고래와는 어느 정도 다른데, 덜 둥글고 덜 풍채 좋은 몸통을 지녔다. 실로 이놈은 제법 단정하고 신사다운 자태를 갖추었다."], ["He has no fins on his back (most other porpoises have), he has a lovely tail, and sentimental Indian eyes of a hazel hue.", "등에는 지느러미가 없고(다른 대부분의 돌고래에게는 있다), 사랑스러운 꼬리와 개암빛 도는 감상적인 인디언의 눈을 지녔다."], ["But his mealy-mouth spoils all.", "그러나 그 가루입이 모든 것을 망쳐 놓는다."], ["Though his entire back down to his side fins is of a deep sable, yet a boundary line, distinct as the mark in a ship’s hull, called the “bright waist,” that line streaks him from stem to stern, with two separate colours, black above and white below.", "이놈의 등 전체는 옆구리 지느러미까지 짙은 흑색이지만, 배의 선체에 그어진 \"밝은 허리\"라 불리는 표시처럼 또렷한 경계선이 이놈을 뱃머리에서 고물까지 두 빛깔로 가르니, 위는 검고 아래는 희다."], ["The white comprises part of his head, and the whole of his mouth, which makes him look as if he had just escaped from a felonious visit to a meal-bag.", "흰 부분은 머리의 일부와 입 전체를 차지하는데, 그 때문에 이놈은 마치 밀가루 자루에 흉악하게 들이닥쳤다가 막 빠져나온 것처럼 보인다."], ["A most mean and mealy aspect!", "더없이 비루하고 가루투성이인 몰골이다!"], ["His oil is much like that of the common porpoise.", "이놈의 기름은 보통 돌고래의 것과 거의 같다."]], [["Beyond the DUODECIMO, this system does not proceed, inasmuch as the Porpoise is the smallest of the whales.", "12절판을 넘어서면 이 분류 체계는 더 나아가지 않으니, 돌고래야말로 고래 가운데 가장 작은 것이기 때문이다."], ["Above, you have all the Leviathans of note.", "앞에서 그대들은 이름난 리바이어던들을 모두 보았다."], ["But there are a rabble of uncertain, fugitive, half-fabulous whales, which, as an American whaleman, I know by reputation, but not personally.", "그러나 그 밖에도 불확실하고, 종잡을 수 없으며, 반쯤은 전설에 가까운 고래들의 무리가 있는데, 미국 고래잡이로서 나는 이것들을 소문으로는 알지만 직접 본 적은 없다."], ["I shall enumerate them by their fore-castle appellations; for possibly such a list may be valuable to future investigators, who may complete what I have here but begun.", "나는 이놈들을 선원들이 앞갑판에서 부르는 이름으로 열거하려 한다. 어쩌면 이러한 목록이, 내가 여기서 다만 시작만 해 놓은 것을 완성해 줄 후세의 연구자들에게 값진 것이 될지도 모르기 때문이다."], ["If any of the following whales, shall hereafter be caught and marked, then he can readily be incorporated into this System, according to his Folio, Octavo, or Duodecimo magnitude:—The Bottle-Nose Whale; the Junk Whale; the Pudding-Headed Whale; the Cape Whale; the Leading Whale; the Cannon Whale; the Scragg Whale; the Coppered Whale; the Elephant Whale; the Iceberg Whale; the Quog Whale; the Blue Whale; etc.", "만약 다음에 열거하는 고래들 가운데 어느 것이라도 훗날 잡혀서 표시가 매겨진다면, 그때는 그 크기에 따라 2절판이든 8절판이든 12절판이든 이 체계 속에 손쉽게 편입될 수 있을 것이다. 곧 병코고래, 정크고래, 푸딩머리고래, 케이프고래, 길잡이고래, 대포고래, 깡마른고래, 구리고래, 코끼리고래, 빙산고래, 쿽고래, 푸른고래 따위다."], ["From Icelandic, Dutch, and old English authorities, there might be quoted other lists of uncertain whales, blessed with all manner of uncouth names.", "아이슬란드어와 네덜란드어, 그리고 고대 영어 전거에서라면, 온갖 투박한 이름을 단 또 다른 불확실한 고래들의 목록을 끌어올 수도 있을 것이다."], ["But I omit them as altogether obsolete; and can hardly help suspecting them for mere sounds, full of Leviathanism, but signifying nothing.", "그러나 나는 그것들을 모조리 한물간 것으로 보아 생략하거니와, 그것들이 리바이어던다움으로 가득 차 있되 실은 아무것도 뜻하지 않는 한낱 소리에 지나지 않으리라는 의심을 도무지 떨칠 수가 없다."]], [["Finally: It was stated at the outset, that this system would not be here, and at once, perfected.", "끝으로, 처음에 밝혔듯이 이 체계는 이 자리에서 단숨에 완성되지는 않을 것이라고 했다."], ["You cannot but plainly see that I have kept my word.", "그대들은 내가 그 약속을 지켰음을 분명히 보지 않을 수 없으리라."], ["But I now leave my cetological System standing thus unfinished, even as the great Cathedral of Cologne was left, with the crane still standing upon the top of the uncompleted tower.", "그러나 나는 이제 나의 고래학 체계를 이렇게 미완인 채로 남겨 두니, 마치 저 위대한 쾰른 대성당이 미완성의 탑 꼭대기에 기중기를 그대로 세워 둔 채로 남겨졌던 것과도 같다."], ["For small erections may be finished by their first architects; grand ones, true ones, ever leave the copestone to posterity.", "작은 건축물이야 그것을 처음 세운 건축가가 마무리할 수 있겠지만, 웅대한 것, 참된 것은 언제나 그 마지막 돌을 후세에 넘겨주는 법이다."], ["God keep me from ever completing anything.", "하느님이여, 내가 그 무엇이든 결코 완성하지 않게 하소서."], ["This whole book is but a draught—nay, but the draught of a draught.", "이 책 전체는 한낱 초고에 지나지 않는다. 아니, 초고의 초고일 따름이다."], ["Oh, Time, Strength, Cash, and Patience!", "오, 시간이여, 힘이여, 돈이여, 그리고 인내여!"]], [["# CHAPTER 33. The Specksnyder.", "# CHAPTER 33. The Specksnyder."]], [["Concerning the officers of the whale-craft, this seems as good a place as any to set down a little domestic peculiarity on ship-board, arising from the existence of the harpooneer class of officers, a class unknown of course in any other marine than the whale-fleet.", "포경선의 사관들에 관해 말하자면, 작살잡이라는 사관 계급의 존재에서 비롯된, 배 안에서의 사소한 살림살이상의 특수성을 적어 두기에 이만한 자리도 없을 듯하다. 그것은 물론 포경 선단(船團) 말고는 그 어떤 바다 세계에서도 찾아볼 수 없는 계급이다."]], [["The large importance attached to the harpooneer’s vocation is evinced by the fact, that originally in the old Dutch Fishery, two centuries and more ago, the command of a whale ship was not wholly lodged in the person now called the captain, but was divided between him and an officer called the Specksnyder.", "작살잡이라는 직분에 부여된 막중한 중요성은, 본래 두 세기도 더 거슬러 올라가는 옛 네덜란드 포경업에서, 포경선의 지휘권이 오늘날 선장이라 불리는 인물 한 사람에게 온전히 맡겨진 것이 아니라, 그와 슈펙스니데르(Specksnyder)라 불리는 사관 사이에 나뉘어 있었다는 사실로써 입증된다."], ["Literally this word means Fat-Cutter; usage, however, in time made it equivalent to Chief Harpooneer.", "이 단어를 글자 그대로 옮기면 기름 베는 자라는 뜻이지만, 세월이 흐르며 관습은 그것을 수석 작살잡이와 같은 말로 만들어 놓았다."], ["In those days, the captain’s authority was restricted to the navigation and general management of the vessel; while over the whale-hunting department and all its concerns, the Specksnyder or Chief Harpooneer reigned supreme.", "그 시절 선장의 권한은 배의 항해와 전반적인 운영에만 한정되어 있었던 반면, 고래 사냥 부문과 그에 딸린 모든 일에 대해서는 슈펙스니데르, 곧 수석 작살잡이가 최고의 권위로 군림했다."], ["In the British Greenland Fishery, under the corrupted title of Specksioneer, this old Dutch official is still retained, but his former dignity is sadly abridged.", "영국의 그린란드 포경업에서는 이 옛 네덜란드 직책이 슈펙시오니어(Specksioneer)라는 변형된 이름으로 아직 남아 있으나, 예전의 위엄은 안타깝게도 크게 깎여 버렸다."], ["At present he ranks simply as senior Harpooneer; and as such, is but one of the captain’s more inferior subalterns.", "오늘날 그는 그저 선임 작살잡이로 자리매김할 뿐이며, 그런 자격으로는 선장 휘하의 비교적 낮은 부하 하나에 지나지 않는다."], ["Nevertheless, as upon the good conduct of the harpooneers the success of a whaling voyage largely depends, and since in the American Fishery he is not only an important officer in the boat, but under certain circumstances (night watches on a whaling ground) the command of the ship’s deck is also his; therefore the grand political maxim of the sea demands, that he should nominally live apart from the men before the mast, and be in some way distinguished as their professional superior; though always, by them, familiarly regarded as their social equal.", "그럼에도 작살잡이들의 처신 여하에 포경 항해의 성패가 크게 달려 있고, 또 미국 포경업에서 그는 보트에서 중요한 사관일 뿐 아니라 특정한 상황(고래잡이 어장에서의 야간 당직)에서는 갑판의 지휘권마저 그의 몫이 되는 까닭에, 바다의 저 위대한 통치 원칙은 그가 명목상으로나마 앞돛대 앞의 일반 선원들과 떨어져 살며, 어떤 식으로든 그들의 직무상 상급자로 구별되어야 한다고 요구한다. 다만 선원들은 언제나 그를 사회적으로는 자기네와 대등한 동료로 허물없이 여기기는 한다."]], [["Now, the grand distinction drawn between officer and man at sea, is this—the first lives aft, the last forward.", "자, 바다에서 사관과 일반 선원 사이에 그어지는 그 큰 구별이란 바로 이것이다 — 전자는 고물 쪽에 거하고, 후자는 이물 쪽에 거한다."], ["Hence, in whale-ships and merchantmen alike, the mates have their quarters with the captain; and so, too, in most of the American whalers the harpooneers are lodged in the after part of the ship.", "그리하여 포경선과 상선을 막론하고 항해사들은 선장과 함께 거처를 두며, 마찬가지로 미국 포경선 대부분에서도 작살잡이들은 배의 고물 쪽에 머문다."], ["That is to say, they take their meals in the captain’s cabin, and sleep in a place indirectly communicating with it.", "다시 말해 그들은 선장의 선실에서 식사를 하고, 그 선실과 간접적으로 통하는 곳에서 잠을 잔다."]], [["Though the long period of a Southern whaling voyage (by far the longest of all voyages now or ever made by man), the peculiar perils of it, and the community of interest prevailing among a company, all of whom, high or low, depend for their profits, not upon fixed wages, but upon their common luck, together with their common vigilance, intrepidity, and hard work; though all these things do in some cases tend to beget a less rigorous discipline than in merchantmen generally; yet, never mind how much like an old Mesopotamian family these whalemen may, in some primitive instances, live together; for all that, the punctilious externals, at least, of the quarter-deck are seldom materially relaxed, and in no instance done away.", "남빙양 포경 항해는 그 기간이 길고(지금이든 예전이든 인간이 행한 모든 항해 가운데 단연 가장 길다), 거기에 따르는 특유의 위험이 있으며, 또 높든 낮든 모든 선원이 고정된 임금이 아니라 다 함께 누리는 운수와 더불어 다 함께 기울이는 경계심과 담대함과 고된 노동에 그 이익을 의지하는 이해 공동체를 이루는 까닭에, 이 모든 사정이 어떤 경우에는 일반 상선보다 덜 엄격한 규율을 낳는 쪽으로 작용하기도 한다. 그렇기는 해도, 이 고래잡이들이 더러 원시적인 사례에서 옛 메소포타미아의 한 가족처럼 어우러져 산다 한들, 그럼에도 불구하고 적어도 후갑판의 격식 차린 외양만큼은 실질적으로 느슨해지는 일이 좀처럼 없으며, 아예 폐기되는 일은 결코 없다."], ["Indeed, many are the Nantucket ships in which you will see the skipper parading his quarter-deck with an elated grandeur not surpassed in any military navy; nay, extorting almost as much outward homage as if he wore the imperial purple, and not the shabbiest of pilot-cloth.", "실로 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 배들 가운데는, 선장이 그 어떤 군함 해군에도 뒤지지 않는 의기양양한 위풍을 떨치며 후갑판을 거니는 광경을 볼 수 있는 배가 한둘이 아니다. 아니, 그가 누더기 같은 수로 안내인용 천이 아니라 황제의 자줏빛 망토라도 두른 듯, 겉으로 바치는 경의를 거의 그만큼이나 강요하기까지 한다."]], [["And though of all men the moody captain of the Pequod was the least given to that sort of shallowest assumption; and though the only homage he ever exacted, was implicit, instantaneous obedience; though he required no man to remove the shoes from his feet ere stepping upon the quarter-deck; and though there were times when, owing to peculiar circumstances connected with events hereafter to be detailed, he addressed them in unusual terms, whether of condescension or in terrorem, or otherwise; yet even Captain Ahab was by no means unobservant of the paramount forms and usages of the sea.", "그리고 페쿼드(Pequod)호의 침울한 선장은 모든 사람 가운데서도 그런 천박하기 짝이 없는 허세와는 가장 거리가 먼 사람이었고, 그가 여태껏 요구한 유일한 경의라고는 두말없는, 즉각적인 복종뿐이었으며, 후갑판에 발을 올리기 전에 신발을 벗으라고 누구에게도 시킨 적이 없었고, 또 앞으로 상세히 풀어 놓을 사건들과 얽힌 특수한 사정으로 인해 때로는 짐짓 겸손한 어조로든 위협을 담은 어조로든 그 밖의 어조로든 평소와 다른 말투로 그들에게 말을 건넨 적이 있기는 했으나, 그런 에이해브(Ahab) 선장조차도 바다의 으뜸가는 격식과 관습을 결코 소홀히 여기지는 않았다."]], [["Nor, perhaps, will it fail to be eventually perceived, that behind those forms and usages, as it were, he sometimes masked himself; incidentally making use of them for other and more private ends than they were legitimately intended to subserve.", "또한 어쩌면 종국에는 이런 사실이 드러나지 않을 수 없을 것이다 — 그가 때때로 그러한 격식과 관습 뒤에, 말하자면 자신을 가면처럼 가리었고, 그것들이 본디 정당하게 떠받들도록 마련된 목적이 아니라 다른, 더 사사로운 목적을 위해 부수적으로 이용했다는 사실 말이다."], ["That certain sultanism of his brain, which had otherwise in a good degree remained unmanifested; through those forms that same sultanism became incarnate in an irresistible dictatorship.", "그의 두뇌에 깃든 어떤 술탄적 전제(專制)의 기질은 본래대로라면 상당 부분 겉으로 드러나지 않은 채 머물렀을 터인데, 바로 그러한 격식들을 통하여 그 술탄적 기질은 저항할 수 없는 독재로 화신(化身)했다."], ["For be a man’s intellectual superiority what it will, it can never assume the practical, available supremacy over other men, without the aid of some sort of external arts and entrenchments, always, in themselves, more or less paltry and base.", "한 인간의 지적 우월함이 제아무리 대단할지라도, 그것은 그 자체로 다소간 하찮고 비루한 어떤 외적 술책과 방벽의 도움 없이는, 다른 사람들에 대한 실질적이고 행사 가능한 지배력으로 결코 굳어질 수 없는 법이다."], ["This it is, that for ever keeps God’s true princes of the Empire from the world’s hustings; and leaves the highest honors that this air can give, to those men who become famous more through their infinite inferiority to the choice hidden handful of the Divine Inert, than through their undoubted superiority over the dead level of the mass.", "바로 이것이 하느님께서 세우신 진정한 제국의 군주들을 이 세상의 선거 연단에서 영영 멀리 떼어 놓으며, 이 세속의 대기가 베풀 수 있는 최고의 영예를, 무리라는 죽은 듯한 평면을 넘어서는 의심할 바 없는 우월함보다는, 오히려 신성한 무위(無爲) 속에 숨겨진 선택받은 소수에 대한 그들 자신의 한없는 열등함을 통해 명성을 얻는 자들에게 넘겨주는 것이다."], ["Such large virtue lurks in these small things when extreme political superstitions invest them, that in some royal instances even to idiot imbecility they have imparted potency.", "극단적인 정치적 미신이 이런 사소한 것들에 옷을 입혀 줄 때면 그 속에는 그토록 큰 위력이 도사리는지라, 어떤 왕가의 사례에서는 백치 같은 천치에게조차 그것들이 권능을 부여하기도 했다."], ["But when, as in the case of Nicholas the Czar, the ringed crown of geographical empire encircles an imperial brain; then, the plebeian herds crouch abased before the tremendous centralization.", "그러나 차르 니콜라이의 경우처럼, 지리적 제국의 둥근 왕관이 한 제왕적 두뇌를 휘감을 때면, 그때는 평민의 무리가 그 가공할 중앙집권 앞에 비굴하게 웅크리고 만다."], ["Nor, will the tragic dramatist who would depict mortal indomitableness in its fullest sweep and direct swing, ever forget a hint, incidentally so important in his art, as the one now alluded to.", "그리고 인간의 불굴함을 그 가장 폭넓은 진폭과 가장 곧은 휘두름으로 그려 내고자 하는 비극 작가라면, 자신의 예술에서 부수적으로나마 그토록 중요한, 지금 여기서 넌지시 가리킨 그 암시를 결코 잊지 않을 것이다."]], [["But Ahab, my Captain, still moves before me in all his Nantucket grimness and shagginess; and in this episode touching Emperors and Kings, I must not conceal that I have only to do with a poor old whale-hunter like him; and, therefore, all outward majestical trappings and housings are denied me.", "그러나 나의 선장 에이해브는 지금도 그 모든 낸터킷식 험상궂음과 텁수룩함을 지닌 채 내 눈앞을 거닐고 있다. 그리고 황제와 왕을 다루는 이 대목에서 나는 숨기지 말아야겠다 — 내가 다루는 것은 다만 그와 같은 가난하고 늙은 고래잡이 한 사람일 뿐이며, 따라서 겉으로 드러난 온갖 제왕적 장식과 의례용 천막은 내게 허락되지 않았다는 사실을."], ["Oh, Ahab!", "오, 에이해브여!"], ["what shall be grand in thee, it must needs be plucked at from the skies, and dived for in the deep, and featured in the unbodied air!", "그대 안에서 무엇이 위대하든, 그것은 모름지기 하늘에서 끌어 내려야 하고, 깊은 바닷속으로 자맥질하여 건져 올려야 하며, 형체 없는 대기 속에서 그려 내야 하리라!"]], [["# CHAPTER 34. The Cabin-Table.", "# CHAPTER 34. The Cabin-Table."]], [["It is noon; and Dough-Boy, the steward, thrusting his pale loaf-of-bread face from the cabin-scuttle, announces dinner to his lord and master; who, sitting in the lee quarter-boat, has just been taking an observation of the sun; and is now mutely reckoning the latitude on the smooth, medallion-shaped tablet, reserved for that daily purpose on the upper part of his ivory leg.", "정오다. 식당 사환인 도우보이가 선실 승강구로 빵 덩어리처럼 창백한 얼굴을 내밀고, 자기 주인이자 상전에게 식사를 알린다."], ["From his complete inattention to the tidings, you would think that moody Ahab had not heard his menial.", "그 상전은 바람 아래쪽 사분정에 앉아 막 태양을 관측하던 참이었고, 지금은 그 일과를 위해 마련해 둔, 상아 다리 윗부분의 매끈하고 메달처럼 둥근 판에 위도를 말없이 계산해 적고 있다."], ["But presently, catching hold of the mizen shrouds, he swings himself to the deck, and in an even, unexhilarated voice, saying, “Dinner, Mr.", "그가 그 전갈에 도무지 주의를 기울이지 않는 모습을 보면, 우울한 에이해브가 하인의 말을 듣지 못했다고 여길 법하다."], ["Starbuck,” disappears into the cabin.", "그러나 곧 그는 뒷돛대의 삭구를 붙잡고 갑판으로 몸을 휙 내려, 무덤덤하고 들뜸 없는 목소리로 \"식사하지, 스타벅 군\"이라 말한 뒤 선실 안으로 사라진다."]], [["When the last echo of his sultan’s step has died away, and Starbuck, the first Emir, has every reason to suppose that he is seated, then Starbuck rouses from his quietude, takes a few turns along the planks, and, after a grave peep into the binnacle, says, with some touch of pleasantness, “Dinner, Mr. Stubb,” and descends the scuttle. The second Emir lounges about the rigging awhile, and then slightly shaking the main brace, to see whether it will be all right with that important rope, he likewise takes up the old burden, and with a rapid “Dinner, Mr. Flask,” follows after his predecessors.", "그 술탄 같은 발걸음의 마지막 메아리가 잦아들고, 첫째 에미르인 스타벅이 그가 자리에 앉았으리라 충분히 짐작할 만해지면, 그제야 스타벅은 고요함에서 깨어나 갑판 위를 몇 차례 거닐고, 나침반함을 진지하게 한 번 들여다본 다음, 다소 유쾌한 기색을 담아 \"식사하게, 스텁 군\"이라 말하고 승강구를 내려간다. 둘째 에미르는 잠시 삭구 주변을 어슬렁거리다가, 메인브레이스를 가볍게 흔들어 그 중요한 밧줄이 멀쩡한지 확인한 뒤, 역시 그 오래된 임무를 떠맡아 빠르게 \"식사하게, 플라스크 군\"이라 던지고는 앞선 이들의 뒤를 따른다."]], [["But the third Emir, now seeing himself all alone on the quarter-deck, seems to feel relieved from some curious restraint; for, tipping all sorts of knowing winks in all sorts of directions, and kicking off his shoes, he strikes into a sharp but noiseless squall of a hornpipe right over the Grand Turk’s head; and then, by a dexterous sleight, pitching his cap up into the mizentop for a shelf, he goes down rollicking so far at least as he remains visible from the deck, reversing all other processions, by bringing up the rear with music. But ere stepping into the cabin doorway below, he pauses, ships a new face altogether, and, then, independent, hilarious little Flask enters King Ahab’s presence, in the character of Abjectus, or the Slave.", "그러나 셋째 에미르는 이제 뒷갑판에 자기 혼자 남은 것을 깨닫고는, 어떤 기묘한 속박에서 풀려난 듯한 기분이 드는 모양이다. 사방으로 온갖 의미심장한 윙크를 날리고 신발을 걷어차 벗어 던지더니, 그는 저 대투르크의 머리 바로 위에서 날카롭되 소리 없는 혼파이프 춤을 한바탕 추어 댄다. 그러고는 능숙한 솜씨로 모자를 뒷돛대 망루로 휙 던져 선반 삼아 걸쳐 놓고, 적어도 갑판에서 보이는 데까지는 흥겹게 까불며 내려간다. 다른 모든 행렬을 거꾸로 뒤집어, 음악과 함께 맨 뒤를 장식하면서 말이다. 그러나 아래쪽 선실 문턱으로 들어서기 직전, 그는 잠시 멈춰 서서 완전히 새로운 얼굴로 갈아입고, 그런 다음 독립적이고 유쾌한 작은 사내 플라스크는 비천한 자, 곧 노예의 역할로 에이해브 왕 앞에 들어선다."]], [["It is not the least among the strange things bred by the intense artificialness of sea-usages, that while in the open air of the deck some officers will, upon provocation, bear themselves boldly and defyingly enough towards their commander; yet, ten to one, let those very officers the next moment go down to their customary dinner in that same commander’s cabin, and straightway their inoffensive, not to say deprecatory and humble air towards him, as he sits at the head of the table; this is marvellous, sometimes most comical.", "바다 관습의 그 지독한 인위성이 길러 낸 기묘한 일들 가운데서도 결코 사소하다 할 수 없는 것은 이런 것이다. 갑판 위 탁 트인 곳에서는 더러 어떤 사관들이 자극을 받으면 자기 지휘관에게 제법 대담하고 반항적으로 굴기도 하지만, 십중팔구 바로 그 사관들이 다음 순간 그 똑같은 지휘관의 선실로 늘 그렇듯 식사하러 내려가면, 곧장 그가 식탁 상석에 앉아 있는 동안 그에게 거슬리지 않는, 아니 굽실대고 겸손하다 할 만한 태도를 보인다는 점이다."], ["Wherefore this difference?", "이는 참으로 놀랍고, 때로는 더없이 우스꽝스럽다. 어째서 이런 차이가 생기는가?"], ["A problem? Perhaps not.", "풀기 어려운 문제인가?"], ["To have been Belshazzar, King of Babylon; and to have been Belshazzar, not haughtily but courteously, therein certainly must have been some touch of mundane grandeur.", "아마 아닐 것이다. 바빌론의 왕 벨사살이 되었다는 것, 그것도 거만하게가 아니라 정중하게 벨사살이 되었다는 것에는, 분명 세속적 위엄의 어떤 기운이 깃들어 있었을 것이다."], ["But he who in the rightly regal and intelligent spirit presides over his own private dinner-table of invited guests, that man’s unchallenged power and dominion of individual influence for the time; that man’s royalty of state transcends Belshazzar’s, for Belshazzar was not the greatest.", "그러나 진실로 제왕다운, 지혜로운 정신으로 자기 사사로운 식탁에 초대한 손님들을 주재하는 사람, 그 사람이 한동안 누리는 도전받지 않는 권력과 개인적 영향력의 지배, 그 사람의 제왕다운 위세는 벨사살의 것을 능가한다. 벨사살은 가장 위대한 자가 아니었기 때문이다."], ["Who has but once dined his friends, has tasted what it is to be Cæsar.", "단 한 번이라도 벗들에게 식사를 베풀어 본 자는 카이사르가 된다는 것이 무엇인지 맛본 셈이다."], ["It is a witchery of social czarship which there is no withstanding.", "그것은 사교적 황제 노릇이라는 마법이니, 도무지 거역할 수 없는 것이다."], ["Now, if to this consideration you superadd the official supremacy of a ship-master, then, by inference, you will derive the cause of that peculiarity of sea-life just mentioned.", "자, 이런 고려에 더하여 선장의 공식적 절대 권위를 덧붙인다면, 그로부터 방금 언급한 바다 생활의 그 특이한 점이 어디서 비롯되는지 추론해 낼 수 있을 것이다."]], [["Over his ivory-inlaid table, Ahab presided like a mute, maned sea-lion on the white coral beach, surrounded by his warlike but still deferential cubs.", "상아를 박아 넣은 식탁을 두고, 에이해브는 하얀 산호 모래밭 위의 말 없는, 갈기 달린 바다사자처럼, 호전적이지만 여전히 공손한 새끼들에게 둘러싸여 좌중을 주재했다."], ["In his own proper turn, each officer waited to be served.", "각 사관은 저마다 제 차례를 기다려 음식을 받았다."], ["They were as little children before Ahab; and yet, in Ahab, there seemed not to lurk the smallest social arrogance.", "그들은 에이해브 앞에서 어린아이와 다름없었다. 그럼에도 에이해브 안에는 사교적 오만이라곤 티끌만큼도 도사리고 있지 않은 듯했다."], ["With one mind, their intent eyes all fastened upon the old man’s knife, as he carved the chief dish before him.", "그가 자기 앞의 주된 요리를 썰 때면, 그들은 한마음으로 골똘한 눈길을 모두 노인의 칼에 붙박았다."], ["I do not suppose that for the world they would have profaned that moment with the slightest observation, even upon so neutral a topic as the weather.", "나는 그들이 무슨 일이 있어도 그 순간을, 설령 날씨처럼 무난하기 짝이 없는 화제로라도 사소한 한마디로 더럽히는 일은 없었으리라고 생각한다. 결코 없었다!"], ["No! And when reaching out his knife and fork, between which the slice of beef was locked, Ahab thereby motioned Starbuck’s plate towards him, the mate received his meat as though receiving alms; and cut it tenderly; and a little started if, perchance, the knife grazed against the plate; and chewed it noiselessly; and swallowed it, not without circumspection.", "그리고 에이해브가 칼과 포크를 내밀어 그 사이에 고기 한 점을 끼운 채 스타벅의 접시를 제 쪽으로 가져오라는 시늉을 하면, 일등항해사는 마치 적선을 받듯 제 몫의 고기를 받았다."], ["For, like the Coronation banquet at Frankfort, where the German Emperor profoundly dines with the seven Imperial Electors, so these cabin meals were somehow solemn meals, eaten in awful silence; and yet at table old Ahab forbade not conversation; only he himself was dumb.", "그리고 조심스레 그것을 썰었다. 혹여 칼이 접시에 스치기라도 하면 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["What a relief it was to choking Stubb, when a rat made a sudden racket in the hold below.", "소리 없이 씹었다. 신중을 기하지 않고는 삼키지도 않았다."], ["And poor little Flask, he was the youngest son, and little boy of this weary family party.", "독일 황제가 일곱 선제후와 더불어 장엄하게 식사하는 프랑크푸르트의 대관식 연회처럼, 이 선실의 식사 또한 어쩐지 엄숙한 식사여서, 무서운 침묵 속에 이루어졌다. 그러면서도 늙은 에이해브는 식탁에서 대화를 금하지는 않았으니, 다만 그 자신이 입을 다물 뿐이었다."], ["His were the shinbones of the saline beef; his would have been the drumsticks.", "숨이 막힐 듯한 스텁에게는, 아래쪽 짐칸에서 쥐 한 마리가 갑자기 소동을 일으켰을 때 그것이 얼마나 큰 위안이 되었던가."], ["For Flask to have presumed to help himself, this must have seemed to him tantamount to larceny in the first degree.", "그리고 가련한 작은 플라스크, 그는 이 지친 가족 모임의 막내아들이자 어린 사내아이였다. 소금에 절인 쇠고기의 정강이뼈가 그의 몫이었고, 닭으로 치면 북채 자리가 그의 차지였을 것이다."], ["Had he helped himself at that table, doubtless, never more would he have been able to hold his head up in this honest world; nevertheless, strange to say, Ahab never forbade him.", "플라스크가 감히 제 손으로 음식을 덜어 먹는다는 것은, 그에게는 일급 절도죄나 다름없게 여겨졌으리라. 그가 그 식탁에서 제 손으로 음식을 덜어 먹었다면, 틀림없이 이 정직한 세상에서 다시는 고개를 들고 다닐 수 없었을 것이다."], ["And had Flask helped himself, the chances were Ahab had never so much as noticed it.", "그럼에도, 이상한 말이지만, 에이해브는 결코 그것을 금한 적이 없었다. 게다가 플라스크가 제 손으로 덜어 먹었더라도, 십중팔구 에이해브는 그것을 알아채지조차 못했을 것이다."], ["Least of all, did Flask presume to help himself to butter.", "무엇보다도, 플라스크는 감히 버터를 제 손으로 떠 먹을 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["Whether he thought the owners of the ship denied it to him, on account of its clotting his clear, sunny complexion; or whether he deemed that, on so long a voyage in such marketless waters, butter was at a premium, and therefore was not for him, a subaltern; however it was, Flask, alas! was a butterless man!", "배의 선주들이 버터가 그의 맑고 햇살 같은 혈색을 엉기게 한다는 이유로 그에게 버터를 금했다고 여겼던 것인지, 아니면 이렇게 시장이라곤 없는 바다에서 이토록 긴 항해를 하는 동안 버터는 귀한 물건이라 일개 하급자인 자기 몫은 아니라고 생각했던 것인지, 어쨌든 가련한 플라스크는 버터 없는 사내였다!"]], [["Another thing.", "또 한 가지 있다."], ["Flask was the last person down at the dinner, and Flask is the first man up.", "플라스크는 식사 자리에 맨 마지막으로 내려오는 사람이었고, 또 맨 먼저 일어나는 사람이었다."], ["Consider!", "생각해 보라!"], ["For hereby Flask’s dinner was badly jammed in point of time.", "이 때문에 플라스크의 식사 시간은 양쪽으로 고약하게 짓눌렸다."], ["Starbuck and Stubb both had the start of him; and yet they also have the privilege of lounging in the rear.", "스타벅과 스텁은 둘 다 그보다 먼저 시작했고, 게다가 뒤에 느긋이 남아 있을 특권까지 누렸다."], ["If Stubb even, who is but a peg higher than Flask, happens to have but a small appetite, and soon shows symptoms of concluding his repast, then Flask must bestir himself, he will not get more than three mouthfuls that day; for it is against holy usage for Stubb to precede Flask to the deck.", "플라스크보다 겨우 한 단계 위일 뿐인 스텁조차 어쩌다 입맛이 없어 곧 식사를 마칠 기색을 보이면, 플라스크는 서둘러야 했으니, 그날은 세 입 이상 먹지 못할 판이었다. 스텁이 플라스크보다 먼저 갑판으로 올라가는 것은 신성한 관습에 어긋나는 일이었기 때문이다."], ["Therefore it was that Flask once admitted in private, that ever since he had arisen to the dignity of an officer, from that moment he had never known what it was to be otherwise than hungry, more or less.", "그래서 플라스크는 언젠가 사석에서 이렇게 털어놓은 적이 있었다. 자기가 사관의 지위에 오른 그 순간부터, 정도의 차이는 있을지언정 늘 배고프지 않은 상태라는 게 어떤 것인지 도무지 알지 못하게 되었노라고."], ["For what he ate did not so much relieve his hunger, as keep it immortal in him.", "그가 먹는 것은 허기를 달래 준다기보다, 오히려 그 허기를 그의 안에 영원히 살아 있게 할 뿐이었기 때문이다. 평화와 만족은 내 위장에서 영영 떠나 버렸구나, 하고 플라스크는 생각했다."], ["Peace and satisfaction, thought Flask, have for ever departed from my stomach.", "나는 사관이지만, 돛대 앞 평선원이던 시절 그랬던 것처럼 앞갑판 선원실에서 옛날식 쇠고기 한 점이라도 낚아 올릴 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까."], ["I am an officer; but, how I wish I could fish a bit of old-fashioned beef in the forecastle, as I used to when I was before the mast.", "이게 바로 승진의 열매라는 것이다. 이게 바로 영광의 헛됨이다."], ["There’s the fruits of promotion now; there’s the vanity of glory: there’s the insanity of life!", "이게 바로 삶의 광기다! 게다가 만일 페쿼드호의 어떤 일개 선원이 사관으로서의 플라스크에게 앙심을 품었다 치자."], ["Besides, if it were so that any mere sailor of the Pequod had a grudge against Flask in Flask’s official capacity, all that sailor had to do, in order to obtain ample vengeance, was to go aft at dinner-time, and get a peep at Flask through the cabin sky-light, sitting silly and dumfoundered before awful Ahab.", "그가 충분한 복수를 손에 넣으려면 그저 식사 시간에 고물 쪽으로 가, 선실 천창 너머로 플라스크를 한 번 훔쳐보기만 하면 그만이었다. 무시무시한 에이해브 앞에서 바보같이, 얼이 빠진 채 앉아 있는 그 모습을 말이다."]], [["Now, Ahab and his three mates formed what may be called the first table in the Pequod’s cabin.", "자, 에이해브와 그의 세 항해사는 페쿼드호 선실에서 이른바 첫째 식탁을 이루었다."], ["After their departure, taking place in inverted order to their arrival, the canvas cloth was cleared, or rather was restored to some hurried order by the pallid steward.", "그들이 들어온 순서와 정반대로 자리를 뜨고 나면, 식탁보가 치워지거나, 아니 차라리 창백한 사환의 손에 의해 다소 황급히 어느 정도 정돈되었다."], ["And then the three harpooneers were bidden to the feast, they being its residuary legatees.", "그런 다음 세 작살잡이가 그 잔치에 불려 들어왔으니, 그들이야말로 그 잔여 유산의 상속자였던 셈이다."], ["They made a sort of temporary servants’ hall of the high and mighty cabin.", "그들은 높고 위엄 있는 선실을 일종의 임시 하인 방쯤으로 만들어 버렸다."]], [["In strange contrast to the hardly tolerable constraint and nameless invisible domineerings of the captain’s table, was the entire care-free license and ease, the almost frantic democracy of those inferior fellows the harpooneers.", "선장의 식탁이 자아내던, 견디기 힘든 그 억압과 이름조차 붙일 수 없는 보이지 않는 위세와는 기묘한 대조를 이루며, 작살잡이라는 저 하급자들은 완전히 근심 없는 방종과 편안함, 거의 광적이다 싶은 민주주의를 누렸다."], ["While their masters, the mates, seemed afraid of the sound of the hinges of their own jaws, the harpooneers chewed their food with such a relish that there was a report to it.", "그들의 상전인 항해사들이 제 턱 경첩 소리조차 두려워하는 듯했던 반면, 작살잡이들은 어찌나 맛있게 음식을 씹어 대던지 소리가 다 날 지경이었다."], ["They dined like lords; they filled their bellies like Indian ships all day loading with spices.", "그들은 영주처럼 식사했다. 온종일 향신료를 싣는 인도행 배들처럼 제 배를 채웠다."], ["Such portentous appetites had Queequeg and Tashtego, that to fill out the vacancies made by the previous repast, often the pale Dough-Boy was fain to bring on a great baron of salt-junk, seemingly quarried out of the solid ox.", "퀴케그와 타슈테고의 식욕이 어찌나 불길할 만큼 대단했던지, 앞서 먹어 치운 식사가 남긴 빈자리를 메우려고, 창백한 도우보이는 통째로 깎아 낸 소처럼 보이는 소금에 절인 쇠고기 한 덩이를 큼지막하게 가져와야 할 때가 많았다."], ["And if he were not lively about it, if he did not go with a nimble hop-skip-and-jump, then Tashtego had an ungentlemanly way of accelerating him by darting a fork at his back, harpoon-wise.", "그가 굼뜨게 굴거나 날렵하게 깡충깡충 뛰어다니지 않으면, 타슈테고는 작살 던지듯 포크를 그의 등에 휙 꽂아 그를 재촉하는 신사답지 못한 버릇이 있었다."], ["And once Daggoo, seized with a sudden humor, assisted Dough-Boy’s memory by snatching him up bodily, and thrusting his head into a great empty wooden trencher, while Tashtego, knife in hand, began laying out the circle preliminary to scalping him.", "한번은 대구가 갑작스러운 장난기에 사로잡혀, 도우보이의 기억을 일깨워 주겠다며 그를 통째로 번쩍 들어 올려 그 머리를 텅 빈 커다란 나무 접시에 처박았고, 그러는 동안 타슈테고는 칼을 손에 쥐고 그의 머릿가죽을 벗기기에 앞서 빙 둘러 금을 긋기 시작했다."], ["He was naturally a very nervous, shuddering sort of little fellow, this bread-faced steward; the progeny of a bankrupt baker and a hospital nurse.", "이 빵 덩어리 얼굴을 한 사환은 천성적으로 몹시 신경질적이고 벌벌 떠는 작은 사내였으니, 파산한 빵집 주인과 병원 간호사 사이에서 난 자식이었다."], ["And what with the standing spectacle of the black terrific Ahab, and the periodical tumultuous visitations of these three savages, Dough-Boy’s whole life was one continual lip-quiver.", "그리고 시커멓고 무시무시한 에이해브가 늘 버티고 있는 광경과 이 세 야만인의 주기적이고 소란스러운 행패가 겹치니, 도우보이의 삶은 온통 끊임없이 입술을 떠는 나날이었다."], ["Commonly, after seeing the harpooneers furnished with all things they demanded, he would escape from their clutches into his little pantry adjoining, and fearfully peep out at them through the blinds of its door, till all was over.", "대개 그는 작살잡이들이 요구한 것을 모두 갖춰 준 뒤에는, 그들의 손아귀를 벗어나 바로 곁에 딸린 작은 식료품 저장실로 도망쳐, 그 문의 블라인드 틈으로 모든 일이 끝날 때까지 겁에 질려 그들을 엿보곤 했다."]], [["It was a sight to see Queequeg seated over against Tashtego, opposing his filed teeth to the Indian’s: crosswise to them, Daggoo seated on the floor, for a bench would have brought his hearse-plumed head to the low carlines; at every motion of his colossal limbs, making the low cabin framework to shake, as when an African elephant goes passenger in a ship.", "퀴케그가 타슈테고를 마주 보고 앉아, 자기 줄로 간 뾰족한 이빨을 인디언의 이빨에 맞세우고 있는 광경은 볼만했다. 그 둘과 직각을 이루어 대구는 바닥에 앉아 있었으니, 의자에 앉았다가는 영구차 깃털 장식 같은 그의 머리가 낮은 천장 들보에 닿을 판이었기 때문이다."], ["But for all this, the great negro was wonderfully abstemious, not to say dainty.", "그의 거대한 팔다리가 움직일 때마다, 마치 아프리카코끼리가 배에 승객으로 올라탄 듯, 낮은 선실 골조가 흔들렸다."], ["It seemed hardly possible that by such comparatively small mouthfuls he could keep up the vitality diffused through so broad, baronial, and superb a person.", "그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 그 거대한 흑인은 놀랍도록 절제할 줄 알았으니, 까다롭다 할 만큼 정갈하게 먹었다. 저토록 작은 양을 입에 넣으면서, 그토록 넓고 영주답고 당당한 몸뚱이에 두루 퍼진 생기를 어떻게 지탱할 수 있는지 도무지 믿기 어려울 정도였다."], ["But, doubtless, this noble savage fed strong and drank deep of the abounding element of air; and through his dilated nostrils snuffed in the sublime life of the worlds.", "그러나 의심할 바 없이, 이 고귀한 야만인은 사방에 넘쳐 나는 공기라는 원소를 든든히 먹고 깊이 마셨으니, 한껏 벌어진 콧구멍으로 세상의 숭고한 생명을 들이마셨던 것이다."], ["Not by beef or by bread, are giants made or nourished.", "거인은 쇠고기나 빵으로 만들어지거나 길러지는 것이 아니다."], ["But Queequeg, he had a mortal, barbaric smack of the lip in eating—an ugly sound enough—so much so, that the trembling Dough-Boy almost looked to see whether any marks of teeth lurked in his own lean arms.", "그러나 퀴케그는 음식을 먹을 때 입맛 다시는 소리가 죽을 듯이 야만스러웠으니, 그 흉한 소리가 어찌나 컸던지, 벌벌 떠는 도우보이는 행여 제 깡마른 팔뚝에 이빨 자국이 남지나 않았나 거의 들여다볼 지경이었다."], ["And when he would hear Tashtego singing out for him to produce himself, that his bones might be picked, the simple-witted steward all but shattered the crockery hanging round him in the pantry, by his sudden fits of the palsy.", "그리고 타슈테고가 그를 향해 어서 와서 제 뼈를 발라 가라고 외치는 소리를 들을 때면, 이 단순한 사환은 갑작스러운 경련에 사로잡혀, 식료품 저장실 둘레에 걸린 사기그릇을 죄다 박살 낼 뻔했다."], ["Nor did the whetstone which the harpooneers carried in their pockets, for their lances and other weapons; and with which whetstones, at dinner, they would ostentatiously sharpen their knives; that grating sound did not at all tend to tranquillize poor Dough-Boy.", "작살잡이들이 자기네 창과 다른 무기를 갈려고 주머니에 넣고 다니던 숫돌, 그리고 식사 자리에서 보란 듯이 칼을 갈아 대던 그 숫돌, 그 갈리는 소리 또한 가련한 도우보이를 진정시키는 데는 조금도 도움이 되지 않았다."], ["How could he forget that in his Island days, Queequeg, for one, must certainly have been guilty of some murderous, convivial indiscretions.", "고향 섬에서 지내던 시절, 적어도 퀴케그만큼은 분명 사람을 죽이고 흥겹게 즐기는 어떤 무분별한 짓을 저질렀으리라는 것을, 그가 어찌 잊을 수 있었겠는가."], ["Alas! Dough-Boy!", "아아, 도우보이여!"], ["hard fares the white waiter who waits upon cannibals.", "식인종의 시중을 드는 백인 급사의 신세란 참으로 고달프구나."], ["Not a napkin should he carry on his arm, but a buckler.", "그는 팔에 냅킨이 아니라 방패를 걸치고 다녀야 마땅하리라."], ["In good time, though, to his great delight, the three salt-sea warriors would rise and depart; to his credulous, fable-mongering ears, all their martial bones jingling in them at every step, like Moorish scimetars in scabbards.", "그러나 때가 되면, 그가 더없이 기뻐하게도 세 바닷소금 전사들은 일어나 떠나갔다. 잘 속아 넘어가고 허무맹랑한 이야기에 솔깃해하는 그의 귀에는, 그들이 걸음을 옮길 때마다 그 몸속의 호전적인 뼈들이 모조리 쩔렁거리는 소리가, 마치 칼집 속의 무어인 언월도 소리처럼 들렸다."]], [["But, though these barbarians dined in the cabin, and nominally lived there; still, being anything but sedentary in their habits, they were scarcely ever in it except at mealtimes, and just before sleeping-time, when they passed through it to their own peculiar quarters.", "그러나 이 야만인들이 선실에서 식사하고, 명목상으로는 거기서 산다고는 해도, 그들의 습성은 도무지 한자리에 붙어 있는 법이 없어서, 식사 때와 잠자기 직전, 곧 그곳을 지나 제 고유한 처소로 갈 때를 빼고는 좀처럼 선실 안에 있는 일이 없었다."]], [["In this one matter, Ahab seemed no exception to most American whale captains, who, as a set, rather incline to the opinion that by rights the ship’s cabin belongs to them; and that it is by courtesy alone that anybody else is, at any time, permitted there.", "이 한 가지 점에서, 에이해브는 대다수 미국 고래잡이 선장들과 다를 바 없어 보였다. 그들은 대체로 배의 선실이란 본디 자기네 것이며, 그 누구든 언제든 거기 들어오도록 허락받는 것은 오로지 호의 덕분이라는 견해 쪽으로 기우는 부류였다."], ["So that, in real truth, the mates and harpooneers of the Pequod might more properly be said to have lived out of the cabin than in it.", "그러므로 사실대로 말하자면, 페쿼드호의 항해사들과 작살잡이들은 선실 안에서 살았다기보다 선실 바깥에서 살았다고 하는 편이 더 옳을 것이다."], ["For when they did enter it, it was something as a street-door enters a house; turning inwards for a moment, only to be turned out the next; and, as a permanent thing, residing in the open air.", "그들이 선실에 들어설 때면, 그것은 마치 거리로 난 현관문이 집 안으로 들어서는 것과도 같았으니, 한순간 안으로 향했다가 다음 순간 곧장 밖으로 향했고, 항구적으로는 탁 트인 바깥에 머물러 있었기 때문이다."], ["Nor did they lose much hereby; in the cabin was no companionship; socially, Ahab was inaccessible.", "그렇다고 그들이 이로써 크게 잃은 것도 없었다. 선실에는 동료애란 것이 없었고, 사교적으로 에이해브는 다가설 수 없는 사람이었다."], ["Though nominally included in the census of Christendom, he was still an alien to it.", "명목상으로는 기독교 세계의 인구에 포함되어 있었으나, 그는 여전히 거기에 이방인이었다."], ["He lived in the world, as the last of the Grisly Bears lived in settled Missouri.", "그는 이 세상을 살아가되, 마치 정착민이 들어찬 미주리에서 마지막 남은 회색곰처럼 살아갔다."], ["And as when Spring and Summer had departed, that wild Logan of the woods, burying himself in the hollow of a tree, lived out the winter there, sucking his own paws; so, in his inclement, howling old age, Ahab’s soul, shut up in the caved trunk of his body, there fed upon the sullen paws of its gloom!", "그리고 봄과 여름이 떠나가고 나면, 저 숲속의 거친 로건이 나무 구멍 속에 제 몸을 파묻고 거기서 제 발바닥을 빨며 겨울을 나듯이, 에이해브의 영혼 또한 그 매섭고 울부짖는 노년에 제 몸뚱이라는 동굴 같은 줄기 속에 갇혀, 거기서 제 음울한 발바닥을 빨며 우울함을 양식으로 삼았던 것이다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 35. The Mast-Head.", "# CHAPTER 35. The Mast-Head."]], [["It was during the more pleasant weather, that in due rotation with the other seamen my first mast-head came round.", "더없이 쾌적한 날씨가 이어지던 무렵, 다른 선원들과 정해진 차례를 따라 돌아가다가 마침내 내 첫 돛대 꼭대기 망보기 차례가 돌아왔다."]], [["In most American whalemen the mast-heads are manned almost simultaneously with the vessel’s leaving her port; even though she may have fifteen thousand miles, and more, to sail ere reaching her proper cruising ground.", "미국의 포경선 대부분에서는, 배가 항구를 떠나는 것과 거의 동시에 돛대 꼭대기에 사람이 배치된다. 비록 제대로 된 어장에 닿기까지 1만 5천 마일, 혹은 그 이상을 항해해야 하는 경우라도 그렇다."], ["And if, after a three, four, or five years’ voyage she is drawing nigh home with anything empty in her—say, an empty vial even—then, her mast-heads are kept manned to the last; and not till her skysail-poles sail in among the spires of the port, does she altogether relinquish the hope of capturing one whale more.", "그리고 만약 삼 년, 사 년, 혹은 오 년에 걸친 항해 끝에 배 안에 무언가 빈 것이 있는 채로—이를테면 빈 약병 하나라도 있는 채로—고향에 가까워지고 있다면, 그때에도 그 돛대 꼭대기에는 마지막까지 사람이 배치된다. 그래서 그 배의 스카이세일 돛대 끝이 항구의 첨탑들 사이로 미끄러져 들어갈 때에야 비로소, 배는 고래를 한 마리 더 잡으리라는 희망을 완전히 내려놓는 것이다."]], [["Now, as the business of standing mast-heads, ashore or afloat, is a very ancient and interesting one, let us in some measure expatiate here.", "자, 뭍에서든 물 위에서든 돛대 꼭대기에 서서 망을 보는 일이란 매우 오래되고 흥미로운 것이니, 여기서 어느 정도 그 이야기를 풀어 보기로 하자. 내가 보기에 가장 이른 시기에 돛대 꼭대기에 선 자들은 옛 이집트인들이었던 듯하다."], ["I take it, that the earliest standers of mast-heads were the old Egyptians; because, in all my researches, I find none prior to them.", "내가 아무리 살펴보아도 그들보다 앞선 자들을 찾을 수 없기 때문이다. 물론 그들의 선조인 바벨탑을 세운 자들도, 그 탑으로 아시아든 아프리카든 통틀어 가장 높다란 돛대 꼭대기를 세우려 했음이 분명하다."], ["For though their progenitors, the builders of Babel, must doubtless, by their tower, have intended to rear the loftiest mast-head in all Asia, or Africa either; yet (ere the final truck was put to it) as that great stone mast of theirs may be said to have gone by the board, in the dread gale of God’s wrath; therefore, we cannot give these Babel builders priority over the Egyptians.", "그러나 (그 꼭대기에 마지막 장식이 얹히기도 전에) 그 거대한 돌 돛대는 하느님의 진노라는 무서운 폭풍 속에서 배 밖으로 떨어져 나갔다고 할 수 있으니, 따라서 우리는 이 바벨탑 건설자들에게 이집트인들보다 앞선 자리를 내줄 수 없다. 그리고 이집트인들이 돛대 꼭대기에 선 자들의 민족이었다는 주장은, 최초의 피라미드들이 천문학적 목적으로 세워졌다는 고고학자들 사이의 일반적인 믿음에 근거를 둔다."], ["And that the Egyptians were a nation of mast-head standers, is an assertion based upon the general belief among archæologists, that the first pyramids were founded for astronomical purposes: a theory singularly supported by the peculiar stair-like formation of all four sides of those edifices; whereby, with prodigious long upliftings of their legs, those old astronomers were wont to mount to the apex, and sing out for new stars; even as the look-outs of a modern ship sing out for a sail, or a whale just bearing in sight.", "이 학설은 그 건축물들의 네 면이 모두 계단 모양으로 독특하게 이루어져 있다는 사실로 유달리 뒷받침되는데, 그 계단을 따라 옛 천문학자들은 다리를 엄청나게 높이 들어 올리며 꼭대기까지 기어올라 새로운 별을 발견하면 큰 소리로 외치곤 했던 것이다. 마치 현대 선박의 망꾼들이 돛이나 막 시야에 들어온 고래를 보고 큰 소리로 외치듯이 말이다."], ["In Saint Stylites, the famous Christian hermit of old times, who built him a lofty stone pillar in the desert and spent the whole latter portion of his life on its summit, hoisting his food from the ground with a tackle; in him we have a remarkable instance of a dauntless stander-of-mast-heads; who was not to be driven from his place by fogs or frosts, rain, hail, or sleet; but valiantly facing everything out to the last, literally died at his post.", "옛 시대의 이름난 기독교 은수자 성(聖) 스틸리테스의 경우, 그는 사막에 높다란 돌기둥을 세우고 그 꼭대기에서 생애의 후반부를 통째로 보냈으며, 도르래로 땅에서 음식을 끌어 올리곤 했다. 그에게서 우리는 두려움을 모르는 돛대 꼭대기 망꾼의 놀라운 본보기를 본다."], ["Of modern standers-of-mast-heads we have but a lifeless set; mere stone, iron, and bronze men; who, though well capable of facing out a stiff gale, are still entirely incompetent to the business of singing out upon discovering any strange sight.", "그는 안개에도, 서리에도, 비에도, 우박에도, 진눈깨비에도 제자리에서 쫓겨나지 않고, 끝까지 모든 것을 용감히 견뎌 내며, 말 그대로 제 임무의 자리에서 죽었다. 현대의 돛대 꼭대기 망꾼들로는 우리에게 생명 없는 무리뿐이니, 한낱 돌과 쇠와 청동으로 만든 사람들이다."], ["There is Napoleon; who, upon the top of the column of Vendome, stands with arms folded, some one hundred and fifty feet in the air; careless, now, who rules the decks below; whether Louis Philippe, Louis Blanc, or Louis the Devil.", "그들은 거센 폭풍을 견뎌 낼 능력은 충분하지만, 무언가 기이한 광경을 발견하고 큰 소리로 외치는 일에는 전혀 무능하다. 저기 나폴레옹이 있다."], ["Great Washington, too, stands high aloft on his towering main-mast in Baltimore, and like one of Hercules’ pillars, his column marks that point of human grandeur beyond which few mortals will go.", "그는 방돔 광장 기둥 꼭대기에서 팔짱을 낀 채, 허공 백오십 피트(약 45미터) 높이에 서 있으되, 이제는 저 아래 갑판을 누가 다스리든—루이 필리프든, 루이 블랑이든, 악마 루이든—아랑곳하지 않는다. 위대한 워싱턴 역시 볼티모어에서 우뚝 솟은 큰 돛대 꼭대기 높은 곳에 서 있으니, 헤라클레스의 기둥 가운데 하나처럼, 그의 기둥은 인간의 위대함이 다다를 수 있는 그 한계점을 표시하는바, 그 너머로 나아갈 인간은 거의 없을 것이다."], ["Admiral Nelson, also, on a capstan of gun-metal, stands his mast-head in Trafalgar Square; and ever when most obscured by that London smoke, token is yet given that a hidden hero is there; for where there is smoke, must be fire.", "넬슨 제독 또한 청동포 금속으로 만든 캡스턴 위에서 트라팔가 광장의 돛대 꼭대기에 서 있으니, 저 런던의 매연에 가장 짙게 가려 있을 때에도 그곳에 숨은 영웅이 있다는 표징은 여전히 주어진다. 연기가 있는 곳에는 반드시 불이 있는 법이니까."], ["But neither great Washington, nor Napoleon, nor Nelson, will answer a single hail from below, however madly invoked to befriend by their counsels the distracted decks upon which they gaze; however it may be surmised, that their spirits penetrate through the thick haze of the future, and descry what shoals and what rocks must be shunned.", "그러나 위대한 워싱턴도, 나폴레옹도, 넬슨도, 저 아래에서 아무리 미친 듯이 불러내어 그들이 굽어보는 어지러운 갑판을 그 지혜로 도와 달라 간청한들, 단 한 번의 외침에도 응답하지 않을 것이다. 비록 그들의 영혼이 미래의 자욱한 안개를 꿰뚫어, 어떤 모래톱과 어떤 암초를 피해야 하는지를 알아본다고 짐작될지라도 말이다."]], [["It may seem unwarrantable to couple in any respect the mast-head standers of the land with those of the sea; but that in truth it is not so, is plainly evinced by an item for which Obed Macy, the sole historian of Nantucket, stands accountable.", "뭍에서 돛대 꼭대기에 선 자들을 바다에서 그렇게 한 자들과 어떤 식으로든 짝지어 놓는 것이 부당해 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 사실은 그렇지 않다는 것은, 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 유일한 역사가인 오베드 메이시가 책임지고 전하는 한 항목으로 분명히 입증된다."], ["The worthy Obed tells us, that in the early times of the whale fishery, ere ships were regularly launched in pursuit of the game, the people of that island erected lofty spars along the sea-coast, to which the look-outs ascended by means of nailed cleats, something as fowls go upstairs in a hen-house.", "갸륵한 오베드는 우리에게 이렇게 일러 준다. 고래잡이가 시작되던 초창기, 아직 배들이 정기적으로 사냥감을 좇아 출항하기 전에, 그 섬 사람들은 바닷가를 따라 높다란 기둥들을 세웠고, 망꾼들은 거기에 못으로 박은 디딤대를 밟고 올라갔는데, 그 모습이 마치 닭들이 닭장에서 횃대 위로 올라가는 것과 비슷했다고 한다."], ["A few years ago this same plan was adopted by the Bay whalemen of New Zealand, who, upon descrying the game, gave notice to the ready-manned boats nigh the beach.", "몇 해 전에는 뉴질랜드의 만(灣) 포경꾼들도 이와 똑같은 방식을 채택하여, 사냥감을 발견하면 해변 가까이에 사람을 다 태워 둔 보트들에게 신호를 보냈다."], ["But this custom has now become obsolete; turn we then to the one proper mast-head, that of a whale-ship at sea.", "그러나 이 관습은 이제 한물갔으니, 그렇다면 본래의 제대로 된 돛대 꼭대기, 곧 바다 위 포경선의 돛대 꼭대기로 눈을 돌려 보자."], ["The three mast-heads are kept manned from sun-rise to sun-set; the seamen taking their regular turns (as at the helm), and relieving each other every two hours.", "세 개의 돛대 꼭대기에는 해 뜰 때부터 해 질 때까지 사람이 배치되며, 선원들은 (키를 잡을 때처럼) 정해진 차례를 따라 두 시간마다 서로 교대한다."], ["In the serene weather of the tropics it is exceedingly pleasant the mast-head; nay, to a dreamy meditative man it is delightful.", "열대 지방의 고요한 날씨 속에서 돛대 꼭대기란 더없이 쾌적한 곳이다. 아니, 몽상에 잠겨 사색하기를 좋아하는 사람에게는 그야말로 즐거운 곳이다."], ["There you stand, a hundred feet above the silent decks, striding along the deep, as if the masts were gigantic stilts, while beneath you and between your legs, as it were, swim the hugest monsters of the sea, even as ships once sailed between the boots of the famous Colossus at old Rhodes.", "그곳에서 그대는 고요한 갑판으로부터 백 피트(약 30미터) 높이에 서서, 마치 돛대가 거대한 죽마라도 되는 듯 깊은 바다 위를 성큼성큼 누비는데, 그러는 동안 그대의 발밑에서, 말하자면 그대의 두 다리 사이로, 바다에서 가장 거대한 괴물들이 헤엄쳐 가니, 마치 옛 로도스의 그 이름난 거상(巨像)의 두 장화 사이로 배들이 한때 항해했던 것과도 같다. 그곳에서 그대는 바다의 무한한 연속 속에 자신을 잃은 채 서 있고, 물결 외에는 흐트러지는 것이라곤 없다."], ["There you stand, lost in the infinite series of the sea, with nothing ruffled but the waves.", "황홀경에 빠진 배는 나른하게 흔들리고, 졸음 오는 무역풍이 불어오니, 모든 것이 그대를 나른함 속으로 녹여 든다."], ["The tranced ship indolently rolls; the drowsy trade winds blow; everything resolves you into languor.", "이 열대 포경 생활에서는 대체로, 숭고하리만치 아무 일도 일어나지 않는 평온이 그대를 감싼다. 그대는 어떤 소식도 듣지 못하고, 어떤 신문도 읽지 못하며, 평범한 일들을 깜짝 놀랄 만한 이야기로 부풀린 호외(號外)가 그대를 쓸데없는 흥분으로 꾀어내는 일도 결코 없다."], ["For the most part, in this tropic whaling life, a sublime uneventfulness invests you; you hear no news; read no gazettes; extras with startling accounts of commonplaces never delude you into unnecessary excitements; you hear of no domestic afflictions; bankrupt securities; fall of stocks; are never troubled with the thought of what you shall have for dinner—for all your meals for three years and more are snugly stowed in casks, and your bill of fare is immutable.", "집안의 우환이나, 부도난 증권이나, 주가 폭락에 관한 소식도 듣지 못하며, 저녁으로 무엇을 먹을까 하는 생각으로 시달리는 일도 결코 없다. 삼 년 넘는 동안 먹을 끼니가 모조리 통 속에 차곡차곡 채워져 있고, 그대의 식단표는 바뀔 줄을 모르기 때문이다."]], [["In one of those southern whalesmen, on a long three or four years’ voyage, as often happens, the sum of the various hours you spend at the mast-head would amount to several entire months.", "이러한 남방 포경선들 가운데 하나에서, 흔히 그러하듯 삼 년이나 사 년에 걸친 긴 항해를 한다면, 그대가 돛대 꼭대기에서 보내는 여러 시간을 모두 합치면 몇 달이나 될 것이다."], ["And it is much to be deplored that the place to which you devote so considerable a portion of the whole term of your natural life, should be so sadly destitute of anything approaching to a cosy inhabitiveness, or adapted to breed a comfortable localness of feeling, such as pertains to a bed, a hammock, a hearse, a sentry box, a pulpit, a coach, or any other of those small and snug contrivances in which men temporarily isolate themselves.", "그리고 참으로 한탄스러운 일은, 그대가 타고난 인생의 전체 기간 가운데 그토록 상당한 부분을 바치는 그 자리가, 아늑한 거주성 비슷한 것이라곤 어이없을 만큼 결여되어 있다는 점이다. 침대나, 해먹이나, 영구차나, 보초막이나, 설교단이나, 마차나, 그 밖에 사람들이 잠시나마 스스로를 고립시키는 자그맣고 아늑한 장치들에 깃든, 편안한 장소감 같은 것을 길러 내기에 알맞은 구석이라곤 없는 것이다."], ["Your most usual point of perch is the head of the t’ gallant-mast, where you stand upon two thin parallel sticks (almost peculiar to whalemen) called the t’ gallant cross-trees.", "그대가 가장 흔히 자리 잡는 횃대는 톱갈란트 돛대의 꼭대기인데, 거기서 그대는 톱갈란트 가로대라 불리는 (거의 포경꾼들에게만 특유한) 두 개의 가느다란 평행 막대 위에 선다. 바다에 휘둘려 이리저리 흔들리는 이곳에서, 초심자가 느끼는 아늑함이란 황소의 뿔 위에 선 것과 비슷한 정도다."], ["Here, tossed about by the sea, the beginner feels about as cosy as he would standing on a bull’s horns.", "물론 추운 날씨에는 당직용 외투의 형태로 제 집을 위로 가지고 올라갈 수도 있다."], ["To be sure, in cold weather you may carry your house aloft with you, in the shape of a watch-coat; but properly speaking the thickest watch-coat is no more of a house than the unclad body; for as the soul is glued inside of its fleshy tabernacle, and cannot freely move about in it, nor even move out of it, without running great risk of perishing (like an ignorant pilgrim crossing the snowy Alps in winter); so a watch-coat is not so much of a house as it is a mere envelope, or additional skin encasing you.", "그러나 엄밀히 말하면, 아무리 두꺼운 당직용 외투라도 옷 한 점 걸치지 않은 몸뚱이 이상으로 집이 되어 주지는 못한다. 영혼이 제 살로 된 장막 안에 들러붙어, 그 안에서 자유로이 움직이지도 못하고, 멸망의 큰 위험을 무릅쓰지 않고서는 (마치 겨울에 눈 덮인 알프스를 건너는 무지한 순례자처럼) 거기서 빠져나오지도 못하는 것처럼, 당직용 외투란 집이라기보다는 한낱 봉투, 곧 그대를 감싸는 또 하나의 살갗에 지나지 않는다."], ["You cannot put a shelf or chest of drawers in your body, and no more can you make a convenient closet of your watch-coat.", "그대는 제 몸뚱이 안에 선반이나 서랍장을 들여놓을 수 없고, 마찬가지로 당직용 외투를 편리한 벽장으로 만들 수도 없다."]], [["Concerning all this, it is much to be deplored that the mast-heads of a southern whale ship are unprovided with those enviable little tents or pulpits, called crow’s-nests, in which the look-outs of a Greenland whaler are protected from the inclement weather of the frozen seas.", "이 모든 것과 관련하여 참으로 한탄스러운 것은, 남방 포경선의 돛대 꼭대기에는 까마귀 둥지(crow's-nest)라 불리는, 저 부러운 작은 천막 혹은 설교단이 갖추어져 있지 않다는 점이다. 그린란드 포경선의 망꾼들은 그 안에서 얼어붙은 바다의 혹독한 날씨로부터 보호받는다."], ["In the fireside narrative of Captain Sleet, entitled “A Voyage among the Icebergs, in quest of the Greenland Whale, and incidentally for the re-discovery of the Lost Icelandic Colonies of Old Greenland;” in this admirable volume, all standers of mast-heads are furnished with a charmingly circumstantial account of the then recently invented crow’s-nest of the Glacier, which was the name of Captain Sleet’s good craft.", "슬릿 선장의 노변(爐邊) 이야기, 곧 \"그린란드 고래를 찾아, 그리고 부수적으로는 옛 그린란드의 사라진 아이슬란드 식민지를 다시 발견하기 위한, 빙산 사이의 항해\"라는 제목의 책에서, 이 훌륭한 책에서, 돛대 꼭대기에 서는 모든 자들은 글레이셔호의 당시 갓 발명된 까마귀 둥지에 관한 매혹적이고 상세한 설명을 제공받는다."], ["He called it the Sleet’s crow’s-nest, in honor of himself; he being the original inventor and patentee, and free from all ridiculous false delicacy, and holding that if we call our own children after our own names (we fathers being the original inventors and patentees), so likewise should we denominate after ourselves any other apparatus we may beget.", "글레이셔호란 슬릿 선장의 멋진 배의 이름이었다. 그는 그것을 스스로의 영예를 기리어 \"슬릿의 까마귀 둥지\"라 불렀으니, 그가 본래의 발명가이자 특허권자였기 때문이요, 또한 그가 온갖 우스꽝스러운 거짓 겸양에서 자유로운 사람으로서, 우리가 우리 자신의 자식을 우리 자신의 이름을 따라 부른다면 (우리 아비들이야말로 본래의 발명가이자 특허권자이니), 마찬가지로 우리가 낳은 다른 어떤 장치든 우리 자신을 따라 이름 붙여야 마땅하다고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["In shape, the Sleet’s crow’s-nest is something like a large tierce or pipe; it is open above, however, where it is furnished with a movable side-screen to keep to windward of your head in a hard gale.", "모양으로 보자면, 슬릿의 까마귀 둥지는 커다란 술통이나 큰 통과 비슷한 무엇이다. 다만 위쪽이 열려 있는데, 그곳에는 거센 폭풍 속에서 머리를 바람으로부터 막아 주도록 움직일 수 있는 측면 가림막이 갖추어져 있다."], ["Being fixed on the summit of the mast, you ascend into it through a little trap-hatch in the bottom.", "돛대 꼭대기에 고정되어 있어, 그 바닥의 작은 뚜껑 문을 통해 안으로 올라간다."], ["On the after side, or side next the stern of the ship, is a comfortable seat, with a locker underneath for umbrellas, comforters, and coats.", "뒤쪽, 곧 배의 고물에 가까운 쪽에는 편안한 자리가 있고, 그 아래에는 우산과 목도리와 외투를 넣어 두는 사물함이 있다."], ["In front is a leather rack, in which to keep your speaking trumpet, pipe, telescope, and other nautical conveniences.", "앞쪽에는 가죽으로 된 걸이가 있어, 거기에 그대의 나팔형 확성기와 담뱃대와 망원경과 그 밖의 항해용 편의 물품들을 보관한다."], ["When Captain Sleet in person stood his mast-head in this crow’s-nest of his, he tells us that he always had a rifle with him (also fixed in the rack), together with a powder flask and shot, for the purpose of popping off the stray narwhales, or vagrant sea unicorns infesting those waters; for you cannot successfully shoot at them from the deck owing to the resistance of the water, but to shoot down upon them is a very different thing.", "슬릿 선장이 몸소 이 자신의 까마귀 둥지에서 돛대 꼭대기 망을 설 때면, 그는 늘 소총 한 자루를 (역시 걸이에 고정해서) 화약통과 탄알과 함께 가지고 다녔다고 우리에게 일러 준다."], ["Now, it was plainly a labor of love for Captain Sleet to describe, as he does, all the little detailed conveniences of his crow’s-nest; but though he so enlarges upon many of these, and though he treats us to a very scientific account of his experiments in this crow’s-nest, with a small compass he kept there for the purpose of counteracting the errors resulting from what is called the “local attraction”", "그 물에 들끓는 길 잃은 일각고래, 곧 떠도는 바다 유니콘을 쏘아 잡기 위함이었으니, 갑판에서는 물의 저항 때문에 그것들을 명중시킬 수 없으나, 위에서 아래로 내리쏘는 것은 전혀 다른 문제이기 때문이다. 자, 슬릿 선장이 그렇게 자신의 까마귀 둥지에 딸린 자잘한 편의 시설들을 빠짐없이 묘사하는 것은 분명 애정에서 우러난 수고였다."], ["of all binnacle magnets; an error ascribable to the horizontal vicinity of the iron in the ship’s planks, and in the Glacier’s case, perhaps, to there having been so many broken-down blacksmiths among her crew; I say, that though the Captain is very discreet and scientific here, yet, for all his learned “binnacle deviations,” “azimuth compass observations,”", "그러나 그가 이런 것들 가운데 많은 것을 그토록 자세히 늘어놓으면서, 또 그가 그 까마귀 둥지 안에서 행한 실험에 관한 매우 과학적인 설명을 우리에게 베풀면서—그곳에 작은 나침반을 하나 두고서, 모든 나침반함 자석의 이른바 \"국지적 인력\"에서 비롯되는 오차를 상쇄하려 했으니, 이 오차는 배의 널빤지 속에 든 쇠붙이가 수평으로 가까이 있는 데서, 그리고 글레이셔호의 경우에는 어쩌면 그 선원들 가운데 몰락한 대장장이가 그토록 많았던 데서 비롯된 것이라 할 만하다—내가 말하건대, 비록 슬릿 선장이 이 대목에서 매우 신중하고 과학적이긴 하나, 그 온갖 박식한 \"나침반함 편차\"와 \"방위 나침반 관측\"과 \"근사 오차\"에도 불구하고, 그는 잘 알고 있다, 슬릿 선장은."], ["and “approximate errors,” he knows very well, Captain Sleet, that he was not so much immersed in those profound magnetic meditations, as to fail being attracted occasionally towards that well replenished little case-bottle, so nicely tucked in on one side of his crow’s nest, within easy reach of his hand.", "자신이 그 심오한 자기(磁氣) 명상에 그토록 깊이 빠져 있던 나머지, 까마귀 둥지 한쪽에 그토록 산뜻하게 끼워져 손만 뻗으면 닿을 곳에 놓인, 가득 채워진 그 작은 휴대용 술병 쪽으로 이따금 마음이 끌리지 않을 수 없었다는 것을."], ["Though, upon the whole, I greatly admire and even love the brave, the honest, and learned Captain; yet I take it very ill of him that he should so utterly ignore that case-bottle, seeing what a faithful friend and comforter it must have been, while with mittened fingers and hooded head he was studying the mathematics aloft there in that bird’s nest within three or four perches of the pole.", "비록 전체적으로 보아 나는 이 용감하고 정직하며 학식 깊은 선장을 크게 흠모하고 심지어 사랑하기까지 하지만, 그가 그 휴대용 술병을 그토록 철저히 묵살한 것은 그에게 몹시 못마땅하다. 장갑 낀 손가락과 두건 쓴 머리로, 극점에서 서너 길도 떨어지지 않은 저 위의 새 둥지 안에서 수학을 연구하던 동안, 그 술병이 그에게 얼마나 충실한 벗이요 위로가 되어 주었을 것인가를 생각하면 더욱 그러하다."]], [["But if we Southern whale-fishers are not so snugly housed aloft as Captain Sleet and his Greenlandmen were; yet that disadvantage is greatly counter-balanced by the widely contrasting serenity of those seductive seas in which we South fishers mostly float.", "그러나 우리 남방 고래잡이들이 슬릿 선장과 그의 그린란드 사람들만큼 위에서 아늑하게 거처를 누리지는 못한다 해도, 그 불리함은 우리 남방 어부들이 주로 떠다니는 그 매혹적인 바다의 사뭇 대조적인 고요함으로 크게 상쇄된다."], ["For one, I used to lounge up the rigging very leisurely, resting in the top to have a chat with Queequeg, or any one else off duty whom I might find there; then ascending a little way further, and throwing a lazy leg over the top-sail yard, take a preliminary view of the watery pastures, and so at last mount to my ultimate destination.", "한 예로, 나는 아주 느긋하게 삭구를 타고 올라가, 망루에 자리를 잡고 쉬면서 퀴케그(Queequeg)나, 혹은 거기서 마주치는 비번인 다른 누군가와 이야기를 나누곤 했다. 그런 다음 조금 더 위로 올라가, 윗돛 활대 위로 게으른 다리 하나를 척 걸치고서 물의 목초지를 미리 한번 둘러본 뒤에, 마침내 나의 최종 목적지까지 기어올랐다."]], [["Let me make a clean breast of it here, and frankly admit that I kept but sorry guard.", "여기서 솔직히 털어놓고, 내가 형편없이 망을 보았다는 것을 거리낌 없이 인정하겠다."], ["With the problem of the universe revolving in me, how could I—being left completely to myself at such a thought-engendering altitude—how could I but lightly hold my obligations to observe all whale-ships’ standing orders, “Keep your weather eye open, and sing out every time.”", "우주의 문제가 내 안에서 빙빙 도는 마당에—그토록 사색을 자아내는 높이에서 완전히 혼자 내버려진 채로—내가 어찌 모든 포경선의 상비 명령, 곧 \"바람 부는 쪽 눈을 똑바로 뜨고, 무언가 보일 때마다 큰 소리로 외쳐라\"라는 의무를 가볍게 여기지 않을 수 있었겠는가."]], [["And let me in this place movingly admonish you, ye ship-owners of Nantucket!", "그리고 이 자리에서 그대들에게 간곡히 충고하노라, 낸터킷의 선주들이여!"], ["Beware of enlisting in your vigilant fisheries any lad with lean brow and hollow eye; given to unseasonable meditativeness; and who offers to ship with the Phædon instead of Bowditch in his head.", "그대들의 빈틈없는 어업에, 이마가 야위고 눈이 움푹 꺼진, 철 모르는 사색에 빠져들기 일쑤이며, 머릿속에 보디치 대신 파이돈을 담고 승선하겠노라는 젊은이를 결코 끌어들이지 말라. 내가 말하노니, 그런 자를 경계하라."], ["Beware of such an one, I say; your whales must be seen before they can be killed; and this sunken-eyed young Platonist will tow you ten wakes round the world, and never make you one pint of sperm the richer.", "그대들의 고래는 잡히기에 앞서 먼저 눈에 띄어야 하거늘, 이 눈 꺼진 젊은 플라톤주의자는 그대들을 세상을 열 바퀴 돌도록 헛되이 끌고 다니면서도, 향유고래 기름 한 파인트도 더 채워 주지 못할 것이다."], ["Nor are these monitions at all unneeded.", "또한 이런 경고가 전혀 필요 없는 것도 아니다."], ["For nowadays, the whale-fishery furnishes an asylum for many romantic, melancholy, and absent-minded young men, disgusted with the carking cares of earth, and seeking sentiment in tar and blubber.", "요즘 들어 고래잡이는, 세상의 좀먹는 근심에 진저리가 나서 타르와 고래 비계 속에서 감상(感傷)을 찾는, 낭만적이고 우울하며 정신이 딴 데 가 있는 수많은 젊은이들에게 피난처를 제공하기 때문이다."], ["Childe Harold not unfrequently perches himself upon the mast-head of some luckless disappointed whale-ship, and in moody phrase ejaculates:—", "차일드 해럴드는 운수 사나운, 실망에 찬 어느 포경선의 돛대 꼭대기에 적잖이 자주 올라앉아, 침울한 어조로 이렇게 내뱉는다."]], [["“Roll on, thou deep and dark blue ocean, roll!", "\"굽이쳐라, 그대 깊고 검푸른 대양이여, 굽이쳐라!"], ["Ten thousand blubber-hunters sweep over thee in vain.”", "일만 명의 비계 사냥꾼들이 그대 위를 휩쓸어도 헛되도다.\""]], [["Very often do the captains of such ships take those absent-minded young philosophers to task, upbraiding them with not feeling sufficient “interest”", "그런 배의 선장들은 정신이 딴 데 가 있는 이 젊은 철학자들을 곧잘 꾸짖으며, 항해에 충분한 \"관심\"을 느끼지 않는다고 나무란다."], ["in the voyage; half-hinting that they are so hopelessly lost to all honorable ambition, as that in their secret souls they would rather not see whales than otherwise.", "그러면서 그들이 온갖 명예로운 야망을 가망 없이 잃어버린 나머지, 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 차라리 고래를 보지 않는 편을 더 바란다고 은근히 빗대기도 한다. 그러나 모두 헛수고다."], ["But all in vain; those young Platonists have a notion that their vision is imperfect; they are short-sighted; what use, then, to strain the visual nerve?", "이 젊은 플라톤주의자들은 자신들의 시력이 불완전하다는 생각을 품고 있다. 자기들은 근시라는 것이다."], ["They have left their opera-glasses at home.", "그렇다면 무엇 하러 시신경을 혹사하겠는가? 그들은 오페라용 쌍안경을 집에 두고 온 것이다."]], [["“Why, thou monkey,” said a harpooneer to one of these lads, “we’ve been cruising now hard upon three years, and thou hast not raised a whale yet. Whales are scarce as hen’s teeth whenever thou art up here.” Perhaps they were; or perhaps there might have been shoals of them in the far horizon; but lulled into such an opium-like listlessness of vacant, unconscious reverie is this absent-minded youth by the blending cadence of waves with thoughts, that at last he loses his identity; takes the mystic ocean at his feet for the visible image of that deep, blue, bottomless soul, pervading mankind and nature; and every strange, half-seen, gliding, beautiful thing that eludes him; every dimly-discovered, uprising fin of some undiscernible form, seems to him the embodiment of those elusive thoughts that only people the soul by continually flitting through it. In this enchanted mood, thy spirit ebbs away to whence it came; becomes diffused through time and space; like Cranmer’s sprinkled Pantheistic ashes, forming at last a part of every shore the round globe over.", "\"이놈, 이 원숭이 같은 녀석아.\" 어느 작살잡이가 이런 젊은이 가운데 하나에게 말했다. \"우리가 거의 삼 년이 다 되도록 바다를 누비고 다녔건만, 너는 아직 고래 한 마리도 발견하지 못했다. 네가 저 위에 올라가 있을 때면 고래가 닭의 이빨만큼이나 드물어지는구나.\" 어쩌면 정말 그러했을지도 모른다. 아니면 어쩌면 저 먼 수평선에 고래 떼가 있었을지도 모른다. 그러나 이 정신이 딴 데 가 있는 젊은이는, 물결의 율동과 자신의 상념이 어우러진 그 가락에 아편에라도 취한 듯 멍하니 무의식적인 몽상의 나른함에 잠겨 든 나머지, 마침내 자신의 정체성을 잃고 만다. 그는 제 발밑의 신비로운 바다를, 인류와 자연을 두루 적시는 저 깊고 푸르며 밑바닥 없는 영혼의 눈에 보이는 형상으로 여긴다. 그리고 그를 슬며시 비껴가는 온갖 기이하고, 어렴풋이 보이며, 미끄러지듯 흐르는 아름다운 것들, 분간할 수 없는 어떤 형체에서 흐릿하게 발견되어 솟아오르는 모든 지느러미가, 그에게는 끊임없이 영혼을 가로질러 스치며 그곳에 깃들 뿐인 저 붙잡을 수 없는 상념들의 화신으로 보인다. 이 홀린 듯한 기분 속에서, 그대의 영혼은 그것이 비롯된 곳으로 썰물처럼 빠져나가, 시간과 공간을 가로질러 흩어진다. 크랜머의 뿌려진 범신론적 재처럼, 마침내 이 둥근 지구 위 모든 해안의 일부가 되는 것이다."]], [["There is no life in thee, now, except that rocking life imparted by a gently rolling ship; by her, borrowed from the sea; by the sea, from the inscrutable tides of God.", "이제 그대 안에는 생명이라곤 없으니, 다만 부드럽게 흔들리는 배가 전해 주는 그 일렁이는 생명뿐이다. 배는 그것을 바다에서 빌려 왔고, 바다는 그것을 헤아릴 길 없는 하느님의 조수(潮水)에서 빌려 왔다."], ["But while this sleep, this dream is on ye, move your foot or hand an inch; slip your hold at all; and your identity comes back in horror.", "그러나 이 잠이, 이 꿈이 그대 위에 드리워 있는 동안, 발이나 손을 한 치라도 움직여 보라. 잡고 있던 손을 조금이라도 놓쳐 보라."], ["Over Descartian vortices you hover.", "그러면 그대의 정체성이 공포와 더불어 되돌아온다."], ["And perhaps, at mid-day, in the fairest weather, with one half-throttled shriek you drop through that transparent air into the summer sea, no more to rise for ever.", "그대는 데카르트의 소용돌이 위를 떠돈다. 그리고 어쩌면, 한낮에, 더없이 화창한 날씨 속에서, 반쯤 목 졸린 듯한 외마디 비명과 함께, 그대는 그 투명한 대기를 뚫고 한여름의 바다 속으로 떨어져, 다시는 영영 솟아오르지 못하리라."], ["Heed it well, ye Pantheists!", "이를 명심하라, 그대 범신론자들이여!"]], [["(Enter Ahab: Then, all.)", "(에이해브 등장. 이어서 모두.)"]], [["It was not a great while after the affair of the pipe, that one morning shortly after breakfast, Ahab, as was his wont, ascended the cabin-gangway to the deck.", "파이프 사건이 있은 지 그리 오래지 않은 어느 날 아침, 식사를 마친 지 얼마 안 되어 에이해브(Ahab)는 여느 때처럼 선실 통로를 거쳐 갑판으로 올라왔다."], ["There most sea-captains usually walk at that hour, as country gentlemen, after the same meal, take a few turns in the garden.", "시골 신사가 같은 식사를 마치고 정원을 몇 바퀴 거니는 것처럼, 바다의 선장들은 대개 그 시각에 그렇게 갑판을 거닐곤 한다."]], [["Soon his steady, ivory stride was heard, as to and fro he paced his old rounds, upon planks so familiar to his tread, that they were all over dented, like geological stones, with the peculiar mark of his walk.", "곧 그의 한결같은 상아 다리 발걸음 소리가 들려왔다. 그가 오랜 산책길을 오가며 거닐던 그 널빤지는 그의 발길에 너무도 익숙해진 나머지, 마치 지질학적 암석처럼 그 독특한 걸음의 흔적으로 온통 움푹움푹 패어 있었다."], ["Did you fixedly gaze, too, upon that ribbed and dented brow; there also, you would see still stranger foot-prints—the foot-prints of his one unsleeping, ever-pacing thought.", "그 골이 파이고 자국이 새겨진 이마를 그대도 가만히 들여다보았더라면, 거기서도 한층 더 기이한 발자국을 보았으리라 — 잠들지 않고 끊임없이 서성이는 그의 한 가지 생각이 남긴 발자국을."]], [["But on the occasion in question, those dents looked deeper, even as his nervous step that morning left a deeper mark.", "그러나 지금 문제의 그날 아침에는, 그 자국들이 한결 깊어 보였다. 그날따라 그의 초조한 발걸음이 한층 깊은 흔적을 남겼던 것처럼."], ["And, so full of his thought was Ahab, that at every uniform turn that he made, now at the main-mast and now at the binnacle, you could almost see that thought turn in him as he turned, and pace in him as he paced; so completely possessing him, indeed, that it all but seemed the inward mould of every outer movement.", "그리고 에이해브는 자신의 생각에 어찌나 골똘히 잠겨 있었던지, 그가 큰 돛대 앞에서, 또 나침반함 앞에서 한결같은 걸음으로 돌아설 때마다, 그가 돌아서면 그 생각도 그 안에서 함께 돌아서고, 그가 거닐면 그 생각도 그 안에서 함께 거니는 것이 거의 눈에 보일 듯했다. 그 생각이 어찌나 완전히 그를 사로잡고 있었던지, 그것은 마치 바깥으로 드러나는 모든 동작을 안에서 빚어내는 거푸집인 듯싶었다."]], [["“D’ye mark him, Flask?”", "\"저 양반을 좀 보게, 플라스크(Flask).\""], ["whispered Stubb; “the chick that’s in him pecks the shell.", "스터브(Stubb)가 속삭였다. \"저 안에 든 병아리가 껍데기를 쪼고 있어."], ["’Twill soon be out.”", "곧 튀어나올 걸세.\""]], [["The hours wore on;—Ahab now shut up within his cabin; anon, pacing the deck, with the same intense bigotry of purpose in his aspect.", "시간이 흘러갔다. 에이해브는 이제 선실 안에 틀어박혔다가, 이윽고 다시 갑판을 거닐었는데, 그 얼굴에는 여전히 무서울 만큼 한곳에 집착하는 광기가 어려 있었다."]], [["It drew near the close of day.", "하루해가 저물어 가고 있었다."], ["Suddenly he came to a halt by the bulwarks, and inserting his bone leg into the auger-hole there, and with one hand grasping a shroud, he ordered Starbuck to send everybody aft.", "갑자기 그는 뱃전 곁에 멈춰 서더니, 거기 뚫린 송곳 구멍에 뼈다리를 꽂아 넣고, 한 손으로는 돛줄을 움켜쥐고서, 스타벅(Starbuck)에게 모두를 고물 쪽으로 불러 모으라고 명령했다."]], [["“Sir!”", "\"선장님!\""], ["said the mate, astonished at an order seldom or never given on ship-board except in some extraordinary case.", "일등항해사가 말했다. 무언가 예사롭지 않은 경우가 아니고서는 배 위에서 좀처럼, 아니 결코 내려지지 않는 명령에 그는 놀란 것이었다."]], [["“Send everybody aft,”", "\"모두를 고물로 불러 모아라.\""], ["repeated Ahab.", "에이해브가 거듭 명했다."], ["“Mast-heads, there!", "\"돛대 꼭대기에 있는 자들!"], ["come down!”", "내려와!\""]], [["When the entire ship’s company were assembled, and with curious and not wholly unapprehensive faces, were eyeing him, for he looked not unlike the weather horizon when a storm is coming up, Ahab, after rapidly glancing over the bulwarks, and then darting his eyes among the crew, started from his standpoint; and as though not a soul were nigh him resumed his heavy turns upon the deck.", "배의 선원이 모두 모여, 호기심 어린, 그러면서도 약간의 불안을 감추지 못한 얼굴로 그를 바라보았다. 폭풍이 다가올 때의 수평선 같은 그의 모습 때문이었다."], ["With bent head and half-slouched hat he continued to pace, unmindful of the wondering whispering among the men; till Stubb cautiously whispered to Flask, that Ahab must have summoned them there for the purpose of witnessing a pedestrian feat.", "에이해브는 뱃전 너머로 재빨리 한 번 눈길을 던지고는, 다시 선원들 사이로 시선을 쏘아 보내더니, 서 있던 자리에서 다시 걸음을 옮겼다. 그리고 곁에 아무도 없다는 듯이, 묵직한 발걸음으로 갑판을 도는 일을 다시 시작했다."], ["But this did not last long.", "고개를 숙이고 모자를 반쯤 눌러쓴 채, 그는 선원들 사이에 도는 의아한 속삭임에는 아랑곳없이 계속 거닐었다. 마침내 스터브가 플라스크에게 조심스레 속삭이기를, 에이해브가 사람들을 여기 불러 모은 것은 보행 묘기라도 구경시키려는 셈인 모양이라고 했다."], ["Vehemently pausing, he cried:—", "그러나 그 일은 오래가지 않았다. 그는 거칠게 걸음을 멈추더니 외쳤다."]], [["“What do ye do when ye see a whale, men?”", "\"제군들, 고래를 보면 어떻게 하나?\""]], [["“Sing out for him!”", "\"소리쳐 알립니다!\""], ["was the impulsive rejoinder from a score of clubbed voices.", "스무 명도 넘는 목소리가 한데 뭉쳐 충동적으로 답했다."]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋다!\""], ["cried Ahab, with a wild approval in his tones; observing the hearty animation into which his unexpected question had so magnetically thrown them.", "에이해브가 사나운 칭찬이 담긴 목소리로 외쳤다. 자신의 뜻밖의 물음이 그들을 그토록 자석처럼 끌어당겨 일으킨 그 진심 어린 활기를 지켜보면서."]], [["“And what do ye next, men?”", "\"그다음엔 어떻게 하나, 제군들?\""]], [["“Lower away, and after him!”", "\"보트를 내려서 쫓아갑니다!\""]], [["“And what tune is it ye pull to, men?”", "\"그러면 어떤 가락에 맞춰 노를 젓나, 제군들?\""]], [["“A dead whale or a stove boat!”", "\"죽은 고래가 아니면 부서진 보트다!\""]], [["More and more strangely and fiercely glad and approving, grew the countenance of the old man at every shout; while the mariners began to gaze curiously at each other, as if marvelling how it was that they themselves became so excited at such seemingly purposeless questions.", "외침이 터질 때마다 노인의 얼굴은 점점 더 기이하고 사납게 기뻐하며 흡족해졌다. 한편 선원들은 호기심 어린 눈으로 서로를 바라보기 시작했다. 도무지 무슨 뜻인지도 모를 그런 물음에 자기들이 어쩌면 이토록 흥분해 버렸는지 의아해하는 듯한 모습이었다."]], [["But, they were all eagerness again, as Ahab, now half-revolving in his pivot-hole, with one hand reaching high up a shroud, and tightly, almost convulsively grasping it, addressed them thus:—", "그러나 그들은 다시 온통 열기에 휩싸였다. 에이해브가 이제 다리를 꽂은 구멍 속에서 반쯤 몸을 돌리고, 한 손으로 돛줄 높은 데를 붙잡아 거의 발작하듯 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 그들에게 이렇게 말한 것이다."]], [["“All ye mast-headers have before now heard me give orders about a white whale.", "\"돛대 꼭대기에 올라가는 자들은 모두 전에 내가 흰 고래에 관해 명령을 내리는 것을 들었을 것이다."], ["Look ye!", "자, 보라!"], ["d’ye see this Spanish ounce of gold?”—holding up a broad bright coin to the sun—“it is a sixteen dollar piece, men.", "이 스페인 금화 한 닢이 보이나?\" 그는 넓적하고 번쩍이는 동전 한 닢을 햇빛에 치켜들었다."], ["D’ye see it? Mr.", "\"16달러짜리다, 제군들."], ["Starbuck, hand me yon top-maul.”", "보이나? 스타벅 군, 저 큰 망치를 좀 건네주게.\""]], [["While the mate was getting the hammer, Ahab, without speaking, was slowly rubbing the gold piece against the skirts of his jacket, as if to heighten its lustre, and without using any words was meanwhile lowly humming to himself, producing a sound so strangely muffled and inarticulate that it seemed the mechanical humming of the wheels of his vitality in him.", "일등항해사가 망치를 가지러 간 사이, 에이해브는 아무 말 없이 그 금화를 윗옷 자락에 천천히 문지르고 있었다. 마치 그 광택을 더하려는 듯이. 그러면서도 아무 말 없이 그동안 낮게 콧노래를 흥얼거리고 있었는데, 그 소리가 어찌나 기이하게 먹먹하고 또렷하지 않았던지, 마치 그의 몸속에서 생명력의 톱니바퀴가 기계처럼 돌아가며 내는 소리 같았다."]], [["Receiving the top-maul from Starbuck, he advanced towards the main-mast with the hammer uplifted in one hand, exhibiting the gold with the other, and with a high raised voice exclaiming: “Whosoever of ye raises me a white-headed whale with a wrinkled brow and a crooked jaw; whosoever of ye raises me that white-headed whale, with three holes punctured in his starboard fluke—look ye, whosoever of ye raises me that same white whale, he shall have this gold ounce, my boys!”", "스타벅에게서 큰 망치를 받아 든 그는, 한 손에는 망치를 치켜들고 다른 손으로는 금화를 내보이며 큰 돛대 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그리고 한껏 높인 목소리로 외쳤다. \"그대들 가운데 누구든 주름진 이마와 비뚤어진 턱을 가진 흰머리 고래를 내게 발견해 주는 자, 그대들 가운데 누구든 오른쪽 꼬리지느러미에 구멍 세 개가 뚫린 그 흰머리 고래를 내게 발견해 주는 자 — 보라, 그대들 가운데 누구든 그 흰 고래를 내게 발견해 주는 자, 그자는 이 금화 한 닢을 차지하리라, 제군들!\""]], [["“Huzza!", "\"만세!"], ["huzza!”", "만세!\""], ["cried the seamen, as with swinging tarpaulins they hailed the act of nailing the gold to the mast.", "선원들이 외치며, 방수모를 흔들어 금화를 돛대에 박는 그 행동에 환호를 보냈다."]], [["“It’s a white whale, I say,”", "\"흰 고래다, 똑똑히 말하노라.\" 에이해브가 큰 망치를 내던지며 말을 이었다."], ["resumed Ahab, as he threw down the topmaul: “a white whale.", "\"흰 고래다. 눈에 불을 켜고 그놈을 찾아라, 제군들."], ["Skin your eyes for him, men; look sharp for white water; if ye see but a bubble, sing out.”", "흰 물보라를 날카롭게 살펴라. 거품 하나라도 보이거든 소리쳐 알려라.\""]], [["All this while Tashtego, Daggoo, and Queequeg had looked on with even more intense interest and surprise than the rest, and at the mention of the wrinkled brow and crooked jaw they had started as if each was separately touched by some specific recollection.", "이러는 내내 태시테고(Tashtego)와 대구(Daggoo), 그리고 퀴케그(Queequeg)는 다른 누구보다도 더 골똘한 관심과 놀라움으로 지켜보고 있었다. 주름진 이마와 비뚤어진 턱이라는 말이 나오자, 세 사람은 저마다 어떤 특정한 기억에 따로따로 건드려진 듯 움찔했다."]], [["“Captain Ahab,”", "\"에이해브 선장님,\""], ["said Tashtego, “that white whale must be the same that some call Moby Dick.”", "태시테고가 말했다. \"그 흰 고래는 어떤 이들이 모비 딕(Moby Dick)이라 부르는 바로 그놈이 틀림없습니다.\""]], [["“Moby Dick?”", "\"모비 딕이라고?\""], ["shouted Ahab.", "에이해브가 소리쳤다."], ["“Do ye know the white whale then, Tash?”", "\"그럼 자네는 그 흰 고래를 아나, 태시?\""]], [["“Does he fan-tail a little curious, sir, before he goes down?”", "\"녀석은 물속으로 가라앉기 전에 꼬리를 좀 묘하게 부채처럼 펼치지 않습니까, 선장님?\""], ["said the Gay-Header deliberately.", "게이헤더 출신 태시테고가 신중하게 말했다."]], [["“And has he a curious spout, too,”", "\"게다가 물 뿜는 모양도 묘하지 않습니까,\""], ["said Daggoo, “very bushy, even for a parmacetty, and mighty quick, Captain Ahab?”", "대구가 말했다. \"향유고래치고도 유난히 무성하고, 또 엄청나게 빠르지요, 에이해브 선장님?\""]], [["“And he have one, two, three—oh!", "\"그리고 녀석 가죽에 한 개, 두 개, 세 개 — 오!"], ["good many iron in him hide, too, Captain,”", "작살이 아주 많이 박혀 있어요, 선장님.\""], ["cried Queequeg disjointedly, “all twiske-tee be-twisk, like him—him—”", "퀴케그가 띄엄띄엄 외쳤다. \"전부 비비 꼬여서 비틀려 있는데, 꼭 이놈 — 이놈 —\""], ["faltering hard for a word, and screwing his hand round and round as though uncorking a bottle—“like him—him—”", "그는 적당한 말을 찾느라 몹시 애를 먹으며, 병마개를 따듯 손을 빙빙 돌렸다. \"꼭 이놈 — 이놈 —\""]], [["“Corkscrew!”", "\"타래송곳!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Ahab, “aye, Queequeg, the harpoons lie all twisted and wrenched in him; aye, Daggoo, his spout is a big one, like a whole shock of wheat, and white as a pile of our Nantucket wool after the great annual sheep-shearing; aye, Tashtego, and he fan-tails like a split jib in a squall.", "\"그래, 퀴케그, 작살들이 녀석 몸에 온통 비틀리고 뒤틀린 채 박혀 있지. 그래, 대구, 녀석의 물줄기는 큼직해서, 마치 밀단을 통째로 쌓은 것 같고, 해마다 큰 양털 깎기를 마친 뒤의 우리 낸터킷(Nantucket) 양털 무더기처럼 새하얗지."], ["Death and devils!", "그래, 태시테고, 녀석은 돌풍에 찢긴 삼각돛처럼 꼬리를 부채꼴로 펼치지."], ["men, it is Moby Dick ye have seen—Moby Dick—Moby Dick!”", "죽음과 악마여! 제군들, 그대들이 본 것은 모비 딕이다 — 모비 딕 — 모비 딕이란 말이다!\""]], [["“Captain Ahab,”", "\"에이해브 선장님,\""], ["said Starbuck, who, with Stubb and Flask, had thus far been eyeing his superior with increasing surprise, but at last seemed struck with a thought which somewhat explained all the wonder.", "스타벅이 말했다. 그는 스터브와 플라스크와 더불어 여태껏 점점 커지는 놀라움으로 상관을 지켜보고 있었으나, 마침내 그 모든 의아함을 어느 정도 풀어 주는 어떤 생각에 부딪힌 듯했다."], ["“Captain Ahab, I have heard of Moby Dick—but it was not Moby Dick that took off thy leg?”", "\"에이해브 선장님, 저도 모비 딕에 관해 들은 바가 있습니다 — 그러나 선장님의 다리를 앗아간 것이 모비 딕은 아니었지요?\""]], [["“Who told thee that?”", "\"누가 자네한테 그런 소리를 했나?\" 에이해브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Ahab; then pausing, “Aye, Starbuck; aye, my hearties all round; it was Moby Dick that dismasted me; Moby Dick that brought me to this dead stump I stand on now. Aye, aye,”", "그러고는 잠시 말을 멈췄다가, \"그래, 스타벅."], ["he shouted with a terrific, loud, animal sob, like that of a heart-stricken moose; “Aye, aye!", "그래, 사방에 둘러선 나의 친구들이여. 나를 돛대 잃은 배처럼 만든 것이 바로 모비 딕이었다."], ["it was that accursed white whale that razeed me; made a poor pegging lubber of me for ever and a day!”", "지금 내가 디디고 선 이 죽은 그루터기로 나를 끌어내린 것이 바로 모비 딕이었어. 그래, 그래.\""], ["Then tossing both arms, with measureless imprecations he shouted out: “Aye, aye!", "그는 가슴을 얻어맞은 큰 사슴의 울음 같은, 무시무시하고 우렁찬 짐승의 흐느낌으로 소리쳤다. \"그래, 그렇다!"], ["and I’ll chase him round Good Hope, and round the Horn, and round the Norway Maelstrom, and round perdition’s flames before I give him up.", "나를 깡그리 허물어뜨린 것이 그 저주받은 흰 고래였어. 나를 영원토록 의족이나 끼고 비틀거리는 가련한 병신으로 만든 것이!\""], ["And this is what ye have shipped for, men!", "그러더니 그는 두 팔을 내던지며, 한없는 저주를 퍼부으며 외쳤다. \"그래, 그렇다!"], ["to chase that white whale on both sides of land, and over all sides of earth, till he spouts black blood and rolls fin out.", "나는 녀석을 희망봉을 돌아서도, 혼곶을 돌아서도, 노르웨이 큰 소용돌이를 돌아서도, 지옥의 불길을 돌아서라도 쫓아갈 테다. 녀석을 포기하느니 차라리."], ["What say ye, men, will ye splice hands on it, now?", "그리고 이것이 바로 그대들이 배에 오른 까닭이다, 제군들! 그 흰 고래를 육지의 이쪽저쪽에서, 또 이 땅의 사방팔방에서 쫓아가, 녀석이 검은 피를 뿜으며 지느러미를 위로 한 채 뒤집힐 때까지 쫓는 것이다."], ["I think ye do look brave.”", "어떤가, 제군들, 이 일에 손을 맞잡고 함께하겠나? 보아하니 그대들은 용감해 보이는군.\""]], [["“Aye, aye!”", "\"그렇습니다, 그렇습니다!\" 작살잡이들과 선원들이 외치며, 흥분한 노인 가까이로 바싹 다가섰다."], ["shouted the harpooneers and seamen, running closer to the excited old man: “A sharp eye for the white whale; a sharp lance for Moby Dick!”", "\"흰 고래를 향한 날카로운 눈! 모비 딕을 향한 날카로운 창!\""]], [["“God bless ye,”", "\"하느님께서 그대들을 축복하시길.\""], ["he seemed to half sob and half shout.", "그가 반쯤 흐느끼며, 반쯤 외치는 듯이 말했다."], ["“God bless ye, men. Steward!", "\"하느님께서 그대들을 축복하시길, 제군들."], ["go draw the great measure of grog.", "급사! 가서 큰 통의 그로그주를 따라 오너라."], ["But what’s this long face about, Mr.", "한데 그 긴 얼굴은 무슨 까닭인가, 스타벅 군."], ["Starbuck; wilt thou not chase the white whale?", "자네는 그 흰 고래를 쫓지 않겠다는 건가?"], ["art not game for Moby Dick?”", "모비 딕을 상대할 배짱이 없는 건가?\""]], [["“I am game for his crooked jaw, and for the jaws of Death too, Captain Ahab, if it fairly comes in the way of the business we follow; but I came here to hunt whales, not my commander’s vengeance.", "\"녀석의 비뚤어진 턱이라도, 또 죽음의 아가리라도 저는 마다하지 않습니다, 에이해브 선장님. 그것이 우리가 좇는 일의 떳떳한 길목에 들어선다면 말입니다."], ["How many barrels will thy vengeance yield thee even if thou gettest it, Captain Ahab?", "그러나 저는 고래를 사냥하러 여기 왔지, 선장님의 복수를 위해 온 것이 아닙니다. 설령 그 복수를 이룬다 한들, 선장님의 복수가 기름을 몇 통이나 내주겠습니까, 에이해브 선장님?"], ["it will not fetch thee much in our Nantucket market.”", "우리 낸터킷 시장에서는 그것으로 큰돈을 받지 못할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Nantucket market!", "\"낸터킷 시장이라고!"], ["Hoot!", "흥!"], ["But come closer, Starbuck; thou requirest a little lower layer.", "한데 더 가까이 와 보게, 스타벅. 자네에게는 좀 더 밑바닥에 깔린 속내가 필요하군."], ["If money’s to be the measurer, man, and the accountants have computed their great counting-house the globe, by girdling it with guineas, one to every three parts of an inch; then, let me tell thee, that my vengeance will fetch a great premium here!”", "돈이 척도가 되어야 한다면, 그리고 회계사들이 이 지구라는 거대한 회계소의 셈을 매기되, 4분의 1인치마다 기니 금화 한 닢씩으로 그 둘레를 빙 둘러 계산한다면, 내 자네에게 말하지, 나의 복수는 바로 이곳에서 막대한 웃돈을 받아 낼 걸세!\" 그는 자신의 가슴을 쳤다."]], [["“He smites his chest,”", "\"가슴을 치는군.\""], ["whispered Stubb, “what’s that for?", "스터브가 속삭였다. \"저건 무슨 뜻이지?"], ["methinks it rings most vast, but hollow.”", "내 보기엔 어마어마하게 울려 퍼지긴 하는데, 텅 빈 소리야.\""]], [["“Vengeance on a dumb brute!”", "\"말 못 하는 짐승에게 복수라니!\""], ["cried Starbuck, “that simply smote thee from blindest instinct!", "스타벅이 외쳤다. \"녀석은 그저 가장 눈먼 본능에 따라 선장님을 들이받았을 뿐입니다!"], ["Madness!", "미친 짓입니다!"], ["To be enraged with a dumb thing, Captain Ahab, seems blasphemous.”", "말 못 하는 것에게 분노한다는 것은, 에이해브 선장님, 신성모독으로 여겨집니다.\""]], [["“Hark ye yet again—the little lower layer.", "\"다시 한번 내 말을 들어 보게 — 그 좀 더 밑바닥에 깔린 속내를."], ["All visible objects, man, are but as pasteboard masks.", "눈에 보이는 모든 사물이란, 이 사람아, 한낱 두꺼운 종이 가면에 지나지 않아."], ["But in each event—in the living act, the undoubted deed—there, some unknown but still reasoning thing puts forth the mouldings of its features from behind the unreasoning mask.", "그러나 모든 사건 속에서 — 살아 있는 행위 속에서, 의심할 바 없는 그 행동 속에서 — 거기, 알 수 없으나 여전히 이성을 지닌 무언가가, 이성 없는 가면 뒤에서 제 모습의 윤곽을 내밀고 있단 말이지."], ["If man will strike, strike through the mask!", "사람이 내리쳐야 한다면, 그 가면을 꿰뚫어 내리쳐야 해!"], ["How can the prisoner reach outside except by thrusting through the wall?", "갇힌 자가 벽을 뚫고 나아가지 않고서야 어떻게 바깥에 닿을 수 있겠나?"], ["To me, the white whale is that wall, shoved near to me.", "내게는 그 흰 고래가 바로 그 벽이야, 내 코앞에 바싹 들이밀어진."], ["Sometimes I think there’s naught beyond.", "때로는 그 너머에 아무것도 없으리라는 생각도 드네."], ["But ’tis enough.", "그러나 그것으로 족해."], ["He tasks me; he heaps me; I see in him outrageous strength, with an inscrutable malice sinewing it.", "녀석은 나를 못살게 굴고, 나를 짓눌러. 나는 녀석에게서 터무니없는 힘을 보고, 그것을 떠받치는 헤아릴 수 없는 악의를 보네."], ["That inscrutable thing is chiefly what I hate; and be the white whale agent, or be the white whale principal, I will wreak that hate upon him.", "내가 무엇보다도 미워하는 것은 바로 그 헤아릴 수 없는 것이야. 그러니 그 흰 고래가 앞잡이든, 아니면 그 흰 고래가 장본인이든, 나는 그 미움을 녀석에게 퍼부어 줄 테다."], ["Talk not to me of blasphemy, man; I’d strike the sun if it insulted me.", "내게 신성모독이라는 말일랑 꺼내지 말게, 이 사람아. 나를 모욕한다면 나는 태양이라도 내리칠 걸세."], ["For could the sun do that, then could I do the other; since there is ever a sort of fair play herein, jealousy presiding over all creations.", "태양이 그럴 수 있다면, 나도 그 반대로 할 수 있는 것이니. 여기에는 늘 일종의 공정한 승부라는 것이 있어서, 질투가 모든 창조물을 다스리고 있으니 말이야."], ["But not my master, man, is even that fair play.", "그러나 이 사람아, 그 공정한 승부조차 내 주인은 아니야."], ["Who’s over me?", "나의 위에 누가 있단 말인가?"], ["Truth hath no confines.", "진리에는 경계가 없네."], ["Take off thine eye!", "그 눈을 치우게!"], ["more intolerable than fiends’ glarings is a doltish stare!", "악마의 노려봄보다 더 견디기 힘든 것이 바로 멍청한 응시야! 그래, 그래."], ["So, so; thou reddenest and palest; my heat has melted thee to anger-glow.", "자네가 붉어졌다 창백해졌다 하는군. 내 열기가 자네를 녹여 분노의 불빛으로 만들었어."], ["But look ye, Starbuck, what is said in heat, that thing unsays itself.", "그러나 보게, 스타벅, 열에 들떠 한 말은 그 말 스스로가 도로 거두어들이는 법이야."], ["There are men from whom warm words are small indignity.", "더운 말 한마디쯤은 작은 모욕도 못 되는 사람들이 있지."], ["I meant not to incense thee.", "자네를 화나게 하려던 것은 아니었네."], ["Let it go. Look!", "그 일은 그냥 흘려보내게."], ["see yonder Turkish cheeks of spotted tawn—living, breathing pictures painted by the sun.", "보게! 저기 얼룩진 황갈색의 튀르크인 같은 뺨을 보게 — 태양이 그려 놓은, 살아 숨 쉬는 그림이지."], ["The Pagan leopards—the unrecking and unworshipping things, that live; and seek, and give no reasons for the torrid life they feel!", "이교도의 표범들 — 헤아릴 줄도, 경배할 줄도 모르면서 살아가는 것들, 자기가 느끼는 그 뜨거운 삶에 대해 아무런 까닭도 대지 않고 구하며 살아가는 것들 말이야!"], ["The crew, man, the crew!", "선원들이야, 이 사람아, 저 선원들!"], ["Are they not one and all with Ahab, in this matter of the whale?", "저들은 이 고래의 일에서 하나같이 에이해브와 한마음이 아니던가?"], ["See Stubb!", "스터브를 보게!"], ["he laughs!", "저자는 웃고 있어!"], ["See yonder Chilian!", "저기 저 칠레 사람을 보게!"], ["he snorts to think of it.", "그 생각만으로도 콧김을 내뿜고 있지 않나."], ["Stand up amid the general hurricane, thy one tost sapling cannot, Starbuck!", "스타벅, 온 천지를 휩쓰는 이 태풍 속에서 자네의 외로이 흔들리는 어린나무 한 그루로는 버텨 설 수가 없네!"], ["And what is it?", "한데 그게 무어란 말인가?"], ["Reckon it.", "한번 헤아려 보게."], ["’Tis but to help strike a fin; no wondrous feat for Starbuck.", "그저 지느러미 하나 찌르는 일을 돕는 것일 뿐이야. 스타벅에게야 무슨 놀랄 만한 일도 아니지."], ["What is it more?", "그게 그 이상 무어란 말인가?"], ["From this one poor hunt, then, the best lance out of all Nantucket, surely he will not hang back, when every foremast-hand has clutched a whetstone?", "그렇다면 이 한 번의 변변찮은 사냥에서, 낸터킷을 통틀어 으뜸가는 창잡이가, 앞돛대의 일꾼들이 저마다 숫돌을 거머쥐고 있는 마당에, 설마 뒤로 물러나지는 않겠지?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["constrainings seize thee; I see!", "어떤 속박이 자네를 옭아매는군, 보여!"], ["the billow lifts thee!", "큰 물결이 자네를 들어 올리고 있어!"], ["Speak, but speak!—Aye, aye!", "말하게, 어서 말하게! — 그래, 그렇다!"], ["thy silence, then, that voices thee.", "그렇다면 자네의 침묵, 그것이 자네를 대변하는군."], ["(Aside) Something shot from my dilated nostrils, he has inhaled it in his lungs.", "(혼잣말로) 부풀어 오른 내 콧구멍에서 무언가가 뿜어져 나왔고, 저자가 그것을 제 폐 속으로 들이마셨어."], ["Starbuck now is mine; cannot oppose me now, without rebellion.”", "스타벅은 이제 내 것이야. 이제는 반역하지 않고서는 나를 거스를 수 없지.\""]], [["“God keep me!—keep us all!”", "\"하느님, 저를 지켜 주소서! — 저희 모두를 지켜 주소서!\""], ["murmured Starbuck, lowly.", "스타벅이 나직이 중얼거렸다."]], [["But in his joy at the enchanted, tacit acquiescence of the mate, Ahab did not hear his foreboding invocation; nor yet the low laugh from the hold; nor yet the presaging vibrations of the winds in the cordage; nor yet the hollow flap of the sails against the masts, as for a moment their hearts sank in.", "그러나 일등항해사의, 마치 홀린 듯한 그 말 없는 묵종에 기뻐한 나머지 에이해브는 그 불길한 기도 소리를 듣지 못했다. 선창에서 새어 나오는 나직한 웃음소리도, 삭구를 스치는 바람의 불길한 떨림도, 한순간 모두의 가슴이 철렁 내려앉던 그때 돛이 돛대를 때리는 텅 빈 펄럭임 소리도 듣지 못했다."], ["For again Starbuck’s downcast eyes lighted up with the stubbornness of life; the subterranean laugh died away; the winds blew on; the sails filled out; the ship heaved and rolled as before.", "다시금 스타벅의 내리깐 눈에 삶의 완강함이 불을 밝혔고, 땅 밑에서 울리던 웃음은 잦아들었으며, 바람은 계속 불었고, 돛은 다시 부풀어 올랐고, 배는 전과 다름없이 출렁이며 흔들렸으니 말이다."], ["Ah, ye admonitions and warnings!", "아, 그대들 훈계와 경고여!"], ["why stay ye not when ye come?", "어찌하여 그대들은 찾아왔다가도 머물지 않는가?"], ["But rather are ye predictions than warnings, ye shadows!", "그대들 그림자들이여, 그러나 그대들은 경고라기보다는 차라리 예언이로다!"], ["Yet not so much predictions from without, as verifications of the foregoing things within.", "그러나 그것도 바깥에서 오는 예언이라기보다는, 이미 안에 깃들어 있던 것들에 대한 확증이라 하겠다."], ["For with little external to constrain us, the innermost necessities in our being, these still drive us on.", "우리를 옭아맬 외부의 힘이 거의 없을 때라도, 우리 존재의 가장 깊은 속에 자리한 필연들, 바로 그것들이 여전히 우리를 몰아가는 까닭이다."]], [["“The measure!", "\"술이다!"], ["the measure!”", "술을 가져와라!\""], ["cried Ahab.", "에이해브가 외쳤다."]], [["Receiving the brimming pewter, and turning to the harpooneers, he ordered them to produce their weapons.", "찰랑찰랑 가득 찬 백랍 잔을 받아 든 그는, 작살잡이들 쪽으로 돌아서서 그들에게 무기를 가져오라고 명했다."], ["Then ranging them before him near the capstan, with their harpoons in their hands, while his three mates stood at his side with their lances, and the rest of the ship’s company formed a circle round the group; he stood for an instant searchingly eyeing every man of his crew.", "그러고는 작살을 손에 든 그들을 양묘기 가까이 자기 앞에 늘어세우고, 세 명의 항해사는 창을 든 채 그의 곁에 서게 했으며, 나머지 선원들은 그 무리 둘레로 둥글게 원을 이루게 했다. 그는 한순간 선원 한 사람 한 사람을 샅샅이 훑어보며 서 있었다."], ["But those wild eyes met his, as the bloodshot eyes of the prairie wolves meet the eye of their leader, ere he rushes on at their head in the trail of the bison; but, alas!", "그러나 그 사나운 눈들이 그의 눈과 마주친 모습은, 우두머리가 들소의 자취를 좇아 앞장서서 내달리기 직전, 핏발 선 눈의 초원 이리들이 그 우두머리의 눈과 마주치는 광경과도 같았다 — 그러나, 아아!"], ["only to fall into the hidden snare of the Indian.", "그저 인디언이 숨겨 둔 덫에 걸려들고 마는 신세일 뿐이건만."]], [["“Drink and pass!”", "\"마시고 돌려라!\""], ["he cried, handing the heavy charged flagon to the nearest seaman.", "그는 가장 가까이 선 선원에게 묵직하게 가득 찬 술병을 건네며 외쳤다."], ["“The crew alone now drink.", "\"이제 선원들만 마신다."], ["Round with it, round!", "빙 둘러라, 빙!"], ["Short draughts—long swallows, men; ’tis hot as Satan’s hoof.", "짧게 들이켜고 — 길게 삼켜라, 제군들. 사탄의 발굽처럼 뜨거운 놈이지."], ["So, so; it goes round excellently.", "그래, 그래. 아주 잘도 돌아가는구나."], ["It spiralizes in ye; forks out at the serpent-snapping eye.", "그것이 그대들 속에서 소용돌이치고, 뱀처럼 번뜩이는 눈으로 갈래져 솟구치는군."], ["Well done; almost drained.", "잘했다. 거의 비워졌어."], ["That way it went, this way it comes.", "저쪽으로 갔다가 이쪽으로 돌아오는구나."], ["Hand it me—here’s a hollow!", "그것을 내게 다오 — 이건 빈 통이로군!"], ["Men, ye seem the years; so brimming life is gulped and gone.", "제군들, 그대들은 마치 세월 같구나. 이토록 가득 차오른 생명이 단숨에 들이켜져 사라지니."], ["Steward, refill!", "급사, 다시 채워라!"]], [["“Attend now, my braves.", "\"이제 잘 들어라, 나의 용사들이여. 내가 그대들 모두를 이 양묘기 둘레로 불러 모았다."], ["I have mustered ye all round this capstan; and ye mates, flank me with your lances; and ye harpooneers, stand there with your irons; and ye, stout mariners, ring me in, that I may in some sort revive a noble custom of my fisherman fathers before me.", "항해사들이여, 그대들의 창으로 내 양옆을 지켜라. 작살잡이들이여, 그대들의 작살을 들고 거기 서라."], ["O men, you will yet see that—Ha!", "그리고 그대들, 굳센 선원들이여, 나를 빙 둘러싸라. 나보다 앞서 살았던 어부 조상들의 고귀한 관습을 내 이렇게나마 되살려 보려는 것이니."], ["boy, come back?", "오, 제군들, 그대들은 머잖아 보게 될 것이다 — 하!"], ["bad pennies come not sooner.", "이놈, 벌써 돌아왔느냐? 못된 동전도 이보다 빨리 굴러오진 않겠다."], ["Hand it me.", "그것을 내게 다오."], ["Why, now, this pewter had run brimming again, wer’t not thou St. Vitus’ imp—away, thou ague!", "한데 이런, 이 백랍 잔이 다시 넘치도록 채워졌겠구나, 네놈이 그 성 비투스 무도병에 걸린 도깨비만 아니었던들 — 썩 물러가라, 이 학질 같은 놈아!"]], [["“Advance, ye mates!", "\"앞으로 나오라, 항해사들이여!"], ["Cross your lances full before me.", "그대들의 창을 내 앞에 가득히 엇갈리게 하라."], ["Well done!", "잘했다!"], ["Let me touch the axis.”", "그 축을 내 만져 보게 하라.\""], ["So saying, with extended arm, he grasped the three level, radiating lances at their crossed centre; while so doing, suddenly and nervously twitched them; meanwhile, glancing intently from Starbuck to Stubb; from Stubb to Flask.", "그렇게 말하며 그는 팔을 뻗어, 수평으로 사방을 향해 뻗은 세 자루의 창을 그 엇갈린 중심에서 움켜쥐었다. 그러면서 별안간 신경질적으로 그것들을 홱홱 잡아당겼다."], ["It seemed as though, by some nameless, interior volition, he would fain have shocked into them the same fiery emotion accumulated within the Leyden jar of his own magnetic life.", "그러는 동안 그는 스타벅에게서 스터브에게로, 스터브에게서 플라스크에게로 골똘히 눈길을 던졌다. 마치 어떤 이름 붙일 수 없는 내면의 의지로, 자신의 자성 어린 생명력이 깃든 라이덴병 속에 쌓인 그 똑같은 불같은 격정을 그들 속으로 충격을 가하듯 흘려보내고 싶어 하는 듯했다."], ["The three mates quailed before his strong, sustained, and mystic aspect.", "세 항해사는 그의 강렬하고 흔들림 없는, 신비로운 모습 앞에서 움츠러들었다."], ["Stubb and Flask looked sideways from him; the honest eye of Starbuck fell downright.", "스터브와 플라스크는 그에게서 곁눈질로 눈을 돌렸고, 스타벅의 정직한 눈은 곧장 아래로 떨어졌다."]], [["“In vain!”", "\"헛수고로구나!\""], ["cried Ahab; “but, maybe, ’tis well.", "에이해브가 외쳤다. \"그러나, 어쩌면, 그편이 나으리라."], ["For did ye three but once take the full-forced shock, then mine own electric thing, that had perhaps expired from out me.", "그대들 셋이 단 한 번이라도 그 충격을 온전히 받았더라면, 그때 내 안의 그 전기 같은 것이, 어쩌면 내게서 다 빠져나가 버렸을지도 모르니."], ["Perchance, too, it would have dropped ye dead.", "어쩌면 그것이 그대들을 그 자리에서 죽게 만들었을지도 모르지."], ["Perchance ye need it not.", "어쩌면 그대들에게는 그것이 필요 없을지도."], ["Down lances!", "창을 내려라!"], ["And now, ye mates, I do appoint ye three cupbearers to my three pagan kinsmen there—yon three most honorable gentlemen and noblemen, my valiant harpooneers.", "자, 이제 항해사들이여, 내 그대들 셋을 저기 저 나의 이교도 형제 셋의 술잔지기로 임명하노라 — 저기 저 더없이 존귀한 신사이자 귀족인 세 사람, 나의 용맹한 작살잡이들 말이다."], ["Disdain the task?", "그 일이 하찮다 여기는가?"], ["What, when the great Pope washes the feet of beggars, using his tiara for ewer?", "무어라, 위대한 교황께서도 거지들의 발을 씻겨 주시며, 당신의 삼중관을 물그릇 삼아 쓰시거늘?"], ["Oh, my sweet cardinals!", "오, 나의 어여쁜 추기경들이여!"], ["your own condescension, that shall bend ye to it.", "바로 그대들 자신의 겸양이, 그것이 그대들을 그 일에 굽히게 하리라."], ["I do not order ye; ye will it.", "내 그대들에게 명하는 것이 아니다."], ["Cut your seizings and draw the poles, ye harpooneers!”", "그대들이 스스로 그리 원하는 것이지. 묶음줄을 끊고 자루를 뽑아라, 작살잡이들이여!\""]], [["Silently obeying the order, the three harpooneers now stood with the detached iron part of their harpoons, some three feet long, held, barbs up, before him.", "말없이 그 명령에 따라, 세 작살잡이는 이제 자루에서 떼어 낸, 길이 석 자쯤 되는 작살의 쇠 부분을, 미늘을 위로 한 채 그의 앞에 들고 섰다."]], [["“Stab me not with that keen steel!", "\"그 날카로운 강철로 나를 찌르지는 마라!"], ["Cant them; cant them over!", "그것을 기울여라, 거꾸로 기울이란 말이다!"], ["know ye not the goblet end?", "잔으로 쓸 끝이 어느 쪽인지 모르느냐?"], ["Turn up the socket!", "그 자루 끼우는 구멍을 위로 향하게 하라!"], ["So, so; now, ye cup-bearers, advance.", "그래, 그래. 자, 술잔지기들이여, 앞으로 나오라."], ["The irons!", "그 작살들을!"], ["take them; hold them while I fill!”", "그것을 받아 들어라. 내가 채우는 동안 단단히 붙들고 있어라!\""], ["Forthwith, slowly going from one officer to the other, he brimmed the harpoon sockets with the fiery waters from the pewter.", "그러고는 곧바로, 한 장교에게서 다른 장교에게로 천천히 옮겨 다니며, 백랍 잔에 든 불같은 물로 작살의 자루 구멍을 찰랑찰랑 채웠다."]], [["“Now, three to three, ye stand.", "\"이제 셋 대 셋으로 그대들이 마주 섰구나."], ["Commend the murderous chalices!", "이 살인의 성배를 받들어라!"], ["Bestow them, ye who are now made parties to this indissoluble league.", "그것을 건네라, 이제 이 풀 수 없는 맹약의 당사자가 된 그대들이여."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Starbuck!", "스타벅!"], ["but the deed is done!", "그러나 일은 이미 이루어졌도다!"], ["Yon ratifying sun now waits to sit upon it.", "저기 저 비준하는 태양이 이제 그 위에 앉으려 기다리고 있으니."], ["Drink, ye harpooneers!", "마셔라, 작살잡이들이여!"], ["drink and swear, ye men that man the deathful whaleboat’s bow—Death to Moby Dick!", "마시고 맹세하라, 죽음을 부르는 고래잡이 보트의 뱃머리를 책임지는 그대들이여 — 모비 딕에게 죽음을!"], ["God hunt us all, if we do not hunt Moby Dick to his death!”", "우리가 모비 딕을 죽음으로 몰아 사냥하지 못한다면, 하느님께서 우리 모두를 사냥하시기를!\""], ["The long, barbed steel goblets were lifted; and to cries and maledictions against the white whale, the spirits were simultaneously quaffed down with a hiss.", "길고 미늘 박힌 강철 잔들이 치켜올려졌다. 그리고 흰 고래를 향한 외침과 저주와 더불어, 그 독한 술이 일제히 쉭 소리를 내며 단숨에 들이켜졌다."], ["Starbuck paled, and turned, and shivered.", "스타벅은 창백해지더니, 돌아서서 몸을 떨었다."], ["Once more, and finally, the replenished pewter went the rounds among the frantic crew; when, waving his free hand to them, they all dispersed; and Ahab retired within his cabin.", "다시 한번, 그리고 마지막으로, 새로 채워진 백랍 잔이 광란에 빠진 선원들 사이를 돌았다. 이윽고 에이해브가 한 손을 들어 그들에게 흔들어 보이자, 모두가 흩어졌고, 그는 자신의 선실로 물러갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 37. Sunset.", "# CHAPTER 37. Sunset."]], [["The cabin; by the stern windows; Ahab sitting alone, and gazing out.", "선실, 고물 쪽 창가. 에이해브가 홀로 앉아 바깥을 응시하고 있다."]], [["I leave a white and turbid wake; pale waters, paler cheeks, where’er I sail. The envious billows sidelong swell to whelm my track; let them; but first I pass.", "나는 희고 탁한 항적을 남긴다. 창백한 물결, 더 창백한 뺨, 내가 어디로 항해하든 그러하다. 시기에 찬 큰 물결이 내 지나간 자리를 삼키려 비스듬히 부풀어 오른다. 그러라지. 하지만 먼저 내가 지나가고 나서다."]], [["Yonder, by ever-brimming goblet’s rim, the warm waves blush like wine.", "저기, 늘 넘칠 듯 가득 찬 술잔의 가장자리에서, 따뜻한 물결이 포도주처럼 붉게 물든다."], ["The gold brow plumbs the blue.", "황금빛 이마가 푸른 바다를 가늠하며 잠겨 든다."], ["The diver sun—slow dived from noon—goes down; my soul mounts up!", "잠수부 같은 태양이 — 정오부터 천천히 자맥질해 내려온 그 태양이 — 가라앉는다."], ["she wearies with her endless hill.", "한데 내 영혼은 솟아오른다! 영혼은 그 끝없는 언덕에 지쳐 있다."], ["Is, then, the crown too heavy that I wear?", "그렇다면 내가 쓰고 있는 이 관(冠)이 너무 무거운 것인가? 이 롬바르디아의 철관(鐵冠)이."], ["this Iron Crown of Lombardy.", "그래도 그것은 수많은 보석으로 빛난다."], ["Yet is it bright with many a gem; I the wearer, see not its far flashings; but darkly feel that I wear that, that dazzlingly confounds.", "그것을 쓰고 있는 나는 그 머나먼 광채를 보지 못하지만, 눈부시게 사람을 어지럽히는 그 무언가를 쓰고 있음을 어렴풋이 느낀다. 그것은 쇠다 — 그것을 나는 안다 — 금이 아니다."], ["’Tis iron—that I know—not gold.", "또한 그것은 쪼개져 있다 — 그것을 나는 느낀다. 들쭉날쭉한 모서리가 어찌나 나를 쓰리게 하는지, 내 뇌가 그 단단한 쇠를 향해 쿵쿵 부딪치는 듯하다."], ["’Tis split, too—that I feel; the jagged edge galls me so, my brain seems to beat against the solid metal; aye, steel skull, mine; the sort that needs no helmet in the most brain-battering fight!", "그렇다, 강철 두개골, 내 것이다. 뇌가 으스러지는 가장 격렬한 싸움에서도 투구 따위 필요 없는 그런 두개골 말이다!"]], [["Dry heat upon my brow?", "내 이마에 메마른 열기가 도는가?"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["time was, when as the sunrise nobly spurred me, so the sunset soothed. No more.", "일찍이 한때는, 해돋이가 나를 고귀하게 분기시켰듯, 해넘이는 나를 달래 주던 시절이 있었다."], ["This lovely light, it lights not me; all loveliness is anguish to me, since I can ne’er enjoy.", "이제는 그렇지 않다. 이 사랑스러운 빛은, 나를 비추지 않는다."], ["Gifted with the high perception, I lack the low, enjoying power; damned, most subtly and most malignantly!", "온갖 아름다움이 내게는 고통이니, 나는 결코 그것을 누릴 수 없는 까닭이다. 높은 통찰력은 타고났으나, 나는 낮은 것, 곧 누리는 능력이 없다."], ["damned in the midst of Paradise!", "저주받았다, 더없이 교묘하고 더없이 악의적으로!"], ["Good night—good night!", "낙원 한복판에서 저주받았다!"], ["(waving his hand, he moves from the window.)", "잘 자라 — 잘 자라! (손을 흔들며 창가에서 물러난다.)"]], [["’Twas not so hard a task.", "그리 어려운 일은 아니었다."], ["I thought to find one stubborn, at the least; but my one cogged circle fits into all their various wheels, and they revolve.", "적어도 한 놈쯤은 완고한 자가 있으리라 여겼건만, 톱니 박힌 내 둥근 바퀴 하나가 그들 각양각색의 바퀴 모두에 꼭 들어맞으니, 그들은 따라 돈다."], ["Or, if you will, like so many ant-hills of powder, they all stand before me; and I their match. Oh, hard!", "아니면 이렇게 말해도 좋다, 그들은 화약 더미를 쌓은 개미집처럼 모조리 내 앞에 늘어서 있고, 나는 그것들에 불을 댕길 성냥이다."], ["that to fire others, the match itself must needs be wasting!", "오, 모진 일이로다! 남들에게 불을 붙이려면, 성냥 그 자신이 다 타 없어져야만 한다니!"], ["What I’ve dared, I’ve willed; and what I’ve willed, I’ll do!", "내가 감행한 것을 나는 의지했고, 내가 의지한 것을 나는 행하리라!"], ["They think me mad—Starbuck does; but I’m demoniac, I am madness maddened!", "그들은 나를 미쳤다 여긴다 — 스타벅(Starbuck)이 그렇다. 하지만 나는 악귀에 들렸다, 나는 광기가 더욱 미쳐 날뛰는 것이다!"], ["That wild madness that’s only calm to comprehend itself!", "오직 제 자신을 깨닫기 위해서만 잔잔해지는 그 사나운 광기 말이다!"], ["The prophecy was that I should be dismembered; and—Aye!", "예언인즉 내가 사지를 잃으리라 하였으니 — 그렇다!"], ["I lost this leg.", "나는 이 다리를 잃었다."], ["I now prophesy that I will dismember my dismemberer.", "이제 나는 예언하노라, 내가 나를 토막 낸 그놈을 토막 내리라고."], ["Now, then, be the prophet and the fulfiller one.", "자, 그렇다면 예언하는 자와 이루는 자가 하나가 되게 하라."], ["That’s more than ye, ye great gods, ever were.", "그것은 그대들, 위대한 신들이여, 일찍이 그대들이 이루었던 것보다 더한 것이다."], ["I laugh and hoot at ye, ye cricket-players, ye pugilists, ye deaf Burkes and blinded Bendigoes!", "나는 그대들을 비웃고 야유한다, 그대들 크리켓 선수들이여, 그대들 권투꾼들이여, 그대들 귀먹은 버크와 눈먼 벤디고들이여!"], ["I will not say as schoolboys do to bullies—Take some one of your own size; don’t pommel me!", "나는 학생들이 골목대장에게 하듯 말하지 않으리라 — 네 덩치에 맞는 놈이나 골라라, 나를 두들기지 마!"], ["No, ye’ve knocked me down, and I am up again; but ye have run and hidden.", "아니다, 그대들은 나를 쓰러뜨렸으나, 나는 다시 일어섰다. 한데 그대들은 도망쳐 몸을 숨겼다."], ["Come forth from behind your cotton bags!", "그 솜뭉치 자루 뒤에서 썩 나오라! 나는 그대들에게 닿을 장총이 없으니."], ["I have no long gun to reach ye.", "오라, 에이해브의 안부를 그대들에게 전하노라."], ["Come, Ahab’s compliments to ye; come and see if ye can swerve me.", "와서 그대들이 나를 빗나가게 할 수 있는지 보라. 나를 빗나가게 한다고?"], ["Swerve me? ye cannot swerve me, else ye swerve yourselves!", "그대들은 나를 빗나가게 할 수 없으니, 그러려다간 그대들 자신을 빗나가게 할 뿐이다!"], ["man has ye there. Swerve me?", "거기서는 인간이 그대들을 이긴다."], ["The path to my fixed purpose is laid with iron rails, whereon my soul is grooved to run.", "나를 빗나가게 한다고? 내 확고한 목적으로 가는 길에는 무쇠 궤도가 놓여 있고, 내 영혼은 그 위를 달리도록 홈이 파여 있다."], ["Over unsounded gorges, through the rifled hearts of mountains, under torrents’ beds, unerringly I rush!", "깊이를 알 수 없는 협곡을 넘어, 산들의 갈라진 심장을 꿰뚫고, 격류의 바닥 밑을 지나, 나는 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 내달린다!"], ["Naught’s an obstacle, naught’s an angle to the iron way!", "그 무쇠 길에는 그 무엇도 장애가 아니며, 그 무엇도 굽이가 아니다!"]], [["By the Mainmast; Starbuck leaning against it.", "내 영혼은 적수를 넘어선 무엇과 맞붙었다. 압도당했다."], ["My soul is more than matched; she’s overmanned; and by a madman!", "그것도 미치광이에게!"], ["Insufferable sting, that sanity should ground arms on such a field!", "견딜 수 없는 가시 같은 일이로다, 멀쩡한 정신이 이런 싸움터에서 무기를 내려놓아야 하다니!"], ["But he drilled deep down, and blasted all my reason out of me!", "하지만 그는 깊숙이 파고들어, 내 안의 이성을 모조리 폭파해 버렸다!"], ["I think I see his impious end; but feel that I must help him to it.", "나는 그의 불경한 최후가 어렴풋이 보이는 듯하다. 그러나 내가 그를 그 길로 도와야 하리라는 것을 느낀다."], ["Will I, nill I, the ineffable thing has tied me to him; tows me with a cable I have no knife to cut.", "원하든 원치 않든, 그 형언할 수 없는 무엇이 나를 그에게 동여매었다. 끊을 칼조차 없는 밧줄로 나를 끌어당긴다."], ["Horrible old man!", "끔찍한 늙은이여!"], ["Who’s over him, he cries;—aye, he would be a democrat to all above; look, how he lords it over all below!", "누가 자기 위에 있느냐고 그는 외친다. 그렇다, 그는 자기 위에 있는 모든 것에는 평등을 외치는 민주주의자가 되려 하면서, 보라, 자기 아래에 있는 모든 것 위에는 어찌 그리 군림하는가!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["I plainly see my miserable office,—to obey, rebelling; and worse yet, to hate with touch of pity!", "나는 내 비참한 직분을 똑똑히 본다. 반항하면서도 복종하는 일, 더 나쁘게는 연민이 섞인 채로 미워하는 일!"], ["For in his eyes I read some lurid woe would shrivel me up, had I it.", "그의 눈 속에서 나는 어떤 음울한 비애를 읽으니, 내가 그것을 지녔더라면 나를 바싹 오그라들게 했을 것이다."], ["Yet is there hope.", "그래도 희망은 있다."], ["Time and tide flow wide.", "세월과 조수는 넓게 흐른다."], ["The hated whale has the round watery world to swim in, as the small gold-fish has its glassy globe.", "저 가증스러운 고래에게는 작은 금붕어가 유리 어항을 가지듯, 헤엄칠 둥근 바다 세계가 통째로 있다."], ["His heaven-insulting purpose, God may wedge aside.", "하늘을 모욕하는 그의 목적을, 하느님이 옆으로 밀쳐 버리실지도 모른다."], ["I would up heart, were it not like lead.", "마음이 납덩이만 같지 않다면 나는 기운을 차려 보련만."], ["But my whole clock’s run down; my heart the all-controlling weight, I have no key to lift again.", "그러나 내 시계는 통째로 다 풀려 멈춰 버렸다. 온 시계를 다스리는 추인 내 마음을, 다시 감아올릴 열쇠가 내게는 없다."]], [["[A burst of revelry from the forecastle.]", "[앞갑판에서 흥청거리는 떠들썩한 소리가 터져 나온다.]"]], [["Oh, God!", "오, 하느님!"], ["to sail with such a heathen crew that have small touch of human mothers in them!", "인간 어미의 정이라곤 거의 깃들지 않은 저런 이교도 선원들과 더불어 항해를 하다니!"], ["Whelped somewhere by the sharkish sea.", "어디선가 상어 같은 바다가 낳아 놓은 것들이로구나."], ["The white whale is their demigorgon.", "흰고래는 저들의 반신반귀(半神半鬼)다. 들어 보라!"], ["Hark! the infernal orgies!", "저 지옥 같은 광란을!"], ["that revelry is forward!", "저 흥청거림은 앞쪽에 있다!"], ["mark the unfaltering silence aft!", "고물 쪽의 흔들림 없는 침묵을 보라!"], ["Methinks it pictures life.", "내게는 그것이 인생을 그려 보이는 듯하다."], ["Foremost through the sparkling sea shoots on the gay, embattled, bantering bow, but only to drag dark Ahab after it, where he broods within his sternward cabin, builded over the dead water of the wake, and further on, hunted by its wolfish gurglings.", "반짝이는 바다를 가르며 가장 앞장서 나아가는 것은, 흥겹고 무장한 듯 위풍당당하며 빈정대는 뱃머리지만, 그것은 다만 어두운 에이해브를 뒤에 끌고 갈 뿐이니, 그는 항적의 죽은 물 위에 지어진 고물 쪽 선실 안에 틀어박혀 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있고, 더 멀리 뒤로는 늑대처럼 부글거리는 물소리에 쫓기고 있다."], ["The long howl thrills me through! Peace!", "저 길게 우짖는 소리가 나를 온통 전율케 한다!"], ["ye revellers, and set the watch!", "그만들 하라! 흥청대는 자들이여, 당직을 세워라!"], ["Oh, life!", "오, 인생이여!"], ["’tis in an hour like this, with soul beat down and held to knowledge,—as wild, untutored things are forced to feed—Oh, life!", "영혼이 짓눌린 채 깨달음에 붙들리는 — 마치 야생의 길들지 않은 짐승들이 억지로 먹이를 받아먹도록 내몰리듯 하는 — 바로 이런 순간이로다, 오, 인생이여!"], ["’tis now that I do feel the latent horror in thee!", "너 속에 잠재한 공포를 내가 느끼는 것은 바로 지금이다!"], ["but ’tis not me!", "그러나 그것은 내가 아니다!"], ["that horror’s out of me!", "그 공포는 내 바깥에 있다!"], ["and with the soft feeling of the human in me, yet will I try to fight ye, ye grim, phantom futures!", "그리고 내 안에 깃든 인간의 부드러운 감정을 안고서, 그래도 나는 너희와 맞서 싸워 보리라, 너희 음산한 환영 같은 미래들이여!"], ["Stand by me, hold me, bind me, O ye blessed influences!", "내 곁에 서라, 나를 붙들라, 나를 동여매라, 오, 너희 축복받은 힘들이여!"]], [["(Stubb solus, and mending a brace.)\nHa!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["hem!", "흠!"], ["clear my throat!—I’ve been thinking over it ever since, and that ha, ha’s the final consequence.", "목청 좀 가다듬자!—그때부터 줄곧 그 일을 곱씹어 왔는데, 결국 도달한 결말이 그 하, 하는 웃음이다."], ["Why so?", "왜냐고?"], ["Because a laugh’s the wisest, easiest answer to all that’s queer; and come what will, one comfort’s always left—that unfailing comfort is, it’s all predestinated.", "웃음이야말로 기이한 모든 것에 대한 가장 현명하고 손쉬운 대답이니까. 게다가 무슨 일이 닥치든 위안 하나는 늘 남는 법이다—그 변치 않는 위안이란, 모든 게 다 예정되어 있다는 것이다."], ["I heard not all his talk with Starbuck; but to my poor eye Starbuck then looked something as I the other evening felt.", "그가 스타벅(Starbuck)과 나눈 이야기를 다 듣지는 못했다. 하지만 내 보잘것없는 눈에 그때 스타벅의 표정은 며칠 전 저녁의 내 심정과 어딘가 닮아 보였다."], ["Be sure the old Mogul has fixed him, too.", "틀림없이 저 늙은 무굴 황제가 그도 단단히 옭아맸을 것이다."], ["I twigged it, knew it; had had the gift, might readily have prophesied it—for when I clapped my eye upon his skull I saw it.", "나는 그걸 알아챘다, 알고 있었다. 그런 재주가 내게 있었다면 쉽사리 예언이라도 했을 것이다—그의 두개골에 눈길이 꽂히던 순간 나는 그걸 보았으니까."], ["Well, Stubb, wise Stubb—that’s my title—well, Stubb, what of it, Stubb?", "그래, 스텁(Stubb), 현명한 스텁—이게 내 칭호지—그래, 스텁, 그게 어쨌다는 거냐, 스텁?"], ["Here’s a carcase.", "여기 송장 하나가 있다."], ["I know not all that may be coming, but be it what it will, I’ll go to it laughing.", "앞으로 닥칠 모든 일을 다 알지는 못하지만, 그것이 무엇이든 나는 웃으며 맞이하러 가련다."], ["Such a waggish leering as lurks in all your horribles!", "너희들의 그 온갖 끔찍한 것들 속에 숨어 있는 장난스러운 곁눈질이라니!"], ["I feel funny.", "어쩐지 우습다."], ["Fa, la!", "파, 라!"], ["lirra, skirra!", "리라, 스키라!"], ["What’s my juicy little pear at home doing now?", "고향에 두고 온 내 탱탱하고 작은 배(梨) 같은 그 애는 지금 무얼 하고 있을까?"], ["Crying its eyes out?—Giving a party to the last arrived harpooneers, I dare say, gay as a frigate’s pennant, and so am I—fa, la!", "눈이 퉁퉁 붓도록 울고 있으려나?—갓 들어온 작살잡이들에게 잔치라도 베풀고 있겠지, 군함의 깃발처럼 흥겹게, 나도 그러하니—파, 라!"], ["lirra, skirra!", "리라, 스키라!"], ["Oh—", "오—"]], [["We’ll drink to-night with hearts as light, To love, as gay and fleeting As bubbles that swim, on the beaker’s brim, And break on the lips while meeting.", "오늘 밤 우리는 가벼운 마음으로 마시리, 사랑을 위하여, 잔 가장자리를 헤엄치다가 입술이 닿는 순간 터져 버리는 거품처럼 흥겹고 덧없이."]], [["A brave stave that—who calls? Mr.", "멋들어진 한 가락이다—누가 부르나?"], ["Starbuck?", "스타벅 씨?"], ["Aye, aye, sir—(Aside) he’s my superior, he has his too, if I’m not mistaken.—Aye, aye, sir, just through with this job—coming.", "예, 예, 알겠습니다—(혼잣말로) 저 사람은 내 상관이지, 헌데 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면 그에게도 제 상관이 따로 있지.—예, 예, 알겠습니다, 이 일만 마저 끝내고—갑니다."]], [["# HARPOONEERS AND SAILORS.", "# HARPOONEERS AND SAILORS."]], [["(Foresail rises and discovers the watch standing, lounging, leaning, and lying in various attitudes, all singing in chorus.)", "작살잡이들과 선원들. (앞돛이 솟아오르며, 당직 선원들이 서 있고, 기대어 있고, 비스듬히 누워 있고, 가지각색 자세로 모두 합창하는 모습이 드러난다.)"]], [["Farewell and adieu to you, Spanish ladies!", "안녕히, 잘 가시오, 스페인 아가씨들이여!"], ["Farewell and adieu to you, ladies of Spain!", "안녕히, 잘 가시오,\n스페인의 아가씨들이여!"], ["Our captain’s commanded.—", "우리 선장이 명을 내리셨으니—"]], [["1ST NANTUCKET SAILOR.", "낸터킷 선원 1. 야, 친구들, 너무 감상에 젖지 마라."], ["Oh, boys, don’t be sentimental; it’s bad for the digestion!", "소화에 안 좋아! 강장제 한잔이다, 날 따라 해!"], ["Take a tonic, follow me!", "(노래하자, 모두 따라 부른다.)"]], [["(Sings, and all follow.)", "우리 선장이 갑판 위에 서서, 손에 망원경을 들고, 물줄기마다"]], [["Our captain stood upon the deck, A spy-glass in his hand, A viewing of those gallant whales That blew at every strand. Oh, your tubs in your boats, my boys, And by your braces stand, And we’ll have one of those fine whales, Hand, boys, over hand! So, be cheery, my lads! may your hearts never fail! While the bold harpooner is striking the whale!", "물을 뿜어 올리는 저 늠름한 고래들을 살펴보았네. 오, 보트의 통을\n챙겨라, 얘들아, 아딧줄 곁에 서라, 그러면 저 멋진 고래들 가운데"]], [["MATE’S VOICE FROM THE QUARTER-DECK.", "한 놈을 잡으리니, 손에 손을 맞잡고, 얘들아! 그러니, 기운을 내라,\n녀석들아!"], ["Eight bells there, forward!", "그대들의 마음이 결코 꺾이지 않기를! 용맹한 작살잡이가"]], [["2ND NANTUCKET SAILOR. Avast the chorus! Eight bells there! d’ye hear, bell-boy? Strike the bell eight, thou Pip! thou blackling! and let me call the watch. I’ve the sort of mouth for that—the hogshead mouth. So, so, (thrusts his head down the scuttle,) Star-bo-l-e-e-n-s, a-h-o-y! Eight bells there below! Tumble up!", "고래를 내리꽂는 동안! 뒷갑판에서 들려오는 항해사의 목소리. 거기 앞쪽, 여덟 점 종이다!"]], [["DUTCH SAILOR. Grand snoozing to-night, maty; fat night for that. I mark this in our old Mogul’s wine; it’s quite as deadening to some as filliping to others. We sing; they sleep—aye, lie down there, like ground-tier butts. At ’em again! There, take this copper-pump, and hail ’em through it. Tell ’em to avast dreaming of their lasses. Tell ’em it’s the resurrection; they must kiss their last, and come to judgment. That’s the way—that’s it; thy throat ain’t spoiled with eating Amsterdam butter.", "낸터킷 선원 2. 합창을 멈춰라! 여덟 점 종이야! 들리느냐, 종지기 소년아? 종을 여덟 번 쳐라, 핍 너 말이다! 이 까만 녀석아! 그리고 내가 당직을 부르게 해 다오. 나야말로 그 일에 알맞은 입을 가졌지—큰 술통 같은 입을 말이야. 자, 자, (승강구 아래로 머리를 들이밀며) 좌현 당직들, 어이! 거기 아래, 여덟 점 종이다! 굴러 나와라! 네덜란드 선원. 오늘 밤 한숨 푹 자기 딱 좋겠는걸, 친구. 그러기 살진 밤이야. 우리 늙은 모굴의 포도주 탓이라고 봐. 그건 어떤 이에게는 사람 죽이는 약이고, 어떤 이에게는 손가락 튕기듯 자극이 되거든. 우리는 노래하고, 저들은 잠들지—그래, 저기 바닥에 드러누웠군, 맨 아래층에 쟁여 둔 술통처럼 말이야. 다시 깨워 보자고! 자, 이 구리 펌프를 받아라, 이걸 통해 외쳐 봐. 제 계집 꿈일랑 그만 꾸라고 일러. 부활의 날이라고, 마지막 입맞춤을 하고 심판대 앞에 나오라고 일러. 그렇지—바로 그거야. 네 목청은 암스테르담 버터를 먹어 망가진 데가 없구나."]], [["FRENCH SAILOR.", "프랑스 선원."], ["Hist, boys!", "쉿, 얘들아!"], ["let’s have a jig or two before we ride to anchor in Blanket Bay.", "담요 만(灣)에 닻을 내리고 잠들기 전에 지그춤이나 한두 번 추자."], ["What say ye?", "어때?"], ["There comes the other watch.", "저기 다른 당직이 오는군."], ["Stand by all legs!", "다들 다리를 준비해라!"], ["Pip!", "핍!"], ["little Pip!", "꼬마 핍!"], ["hurrah with your tambourine!", "탬버린을 들고 한번 신나게 놀아 보자!"]], [["PIP.", "핍."], ["(Sulky and sleepy.) Don’t know where it is.", "(뚱하니 졸린 채로.) 그게 어디 있는지 모르겠어요."]], [["FRENCH SAILOR.", "프랑스 선원."], ["Beat thy belly, then, and wag thy ears.", "그럼 네 배라도 두드리고, 귀라도 흔들어라."], ["Jig it, men, I say; merry’s the word; hurrah!", "추라니까, 다들. 흥겨운 게 제일이다, 만세!"], ["Damn me, won’t you dance?", "빌어먹을, 춤 안 출 거야?"], ["Form, now, Indian-file, and gallop into the double-shuffle?", "자, 이제, 인디언처럼 한 줄로 늘어서서, 더블 셔플로 신나게 내달려 보자!"], ["Throw yourselves!", "몸을 던져라!"], ["Legs!", "다리!"], ["legs!", "다리!"]], [["ICELAND SAILOR.", "아이슬란드 선원."], ["I don’t like your floor, maty; it’s too springy to my taste.", "자네 마룻바닥이 영 마음에 안 드는군, 친구. 내 취향엔 너무 물렁물렁해."], ["I’m used to ice-floors.", "난 얼음 바닥에 익숙하거든."], ["I’m sorry to throw cold water on the subject; but excuse me.", "이런 일에 찬물을 끼얹어 미안하네만, 양해해 주게."]], [["MALTESE SAILOR.", "몰타 선원. 나도 그래."], ["Me too; where’s your girls?", "자네들 계집애들은 어딨나?"], ["Who but a fool would take his left hand by his right, and say to himself, how d’ye do?", "바보가 아니고서야 제 오른손으로 제 왼손을 잡고는 혼잣말로 '안녕하세요?' 하고 인사하겠나?"], ["Partners!", "짝이다!"], ["I must have partners!", "난 짝이 있어야겠어!"]], [["SICILIAN SAILOR.", "시칠리아 선원."], ["Aye; girls and a green!—then I’ll hop with ye; yea, turn grasshopper!", "그래, 계집애들과 풀밭이 있어야지!—그럼 자네들과 한바탕 뛰어 보겠네. 그래, 메뚜기로 변신이라도 하지!"]], [["LONG-ISLAND SAILOR.", "롱아일랜드 선원."], ["Well, well, ye sulkies, there’s plenty more of us.", "자, 자, 이 뚱한 녀석들아, 우리는 얼마든지 더 있어."], ["Hoe corn when you may, say I.", "옥수수밭은 김맬 수 있을 때 매라, 나는 그렇게 말한다네."], ["All legs go to harvest soon.", "머지않아 모든 다리들이 추수하러 나갈 테니까."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["here comes the music; now for it!", "저기 음악이 오는군. 자, 이제 시작이다!"]], [["AZORE SAILOR.", "아조레스 선원."], ["(Ascending, and pitching the tambourine up the scuttle.) Here you are, Pip; and there’s the windlass-bitts; up you mount!", "(올라오면서, 탬버린을 승강구 위로 휙 던져 올리며.) 자, 여기 있다, 핍. 그리고 저기 양묘기 기둥이 있으니, 거기 올라타라!"], ["Now, boys!", "자, 얘들아!"], ["(The half of them dance to the tambourine; some go below; some sleep or lie among the coils of rigging.", "(그들 중 절반이 탬버린에 맞춰 춤춘다. 더러는 아래로 내려가고, 더러는 잠들거나 밧줄 사리 사이에 드러눕는다."], ["Oaths a-plenty.)", "욕설이 푸짐하다.)"]], [["AZORE SAILOR.", "아조레스 선원."], ["(Dancing) Go it, Pip!", "(춤추며) 가자, 핍!"], ["Bang it, bell-boy!", "두드려라, 종지기 소년아!"], ["Rig it, dig it, stig it, quig it, bell-boy!", "흔들고, 쑤시고, 찌르고, 까불어라, 종지기 소년아!"], ["Make fire-flies; break the jinglers!", "반딧불을 만들어라, 그 딸랑이들을 깨부숴라!"]], [["PIP.", "핍."], ["Jinglers, you say?—there goes another, dropped off; I pound it so.", "딸랑이라고요?—저기 또 하나 떨어져 나가네요, 내가 어찌나 세게 두드리는지."]], [["CHINA SAILOR.", "중국 선원."], ["Rattle thy teeth, then, and pound away; make a pagoda of thyself.", "그럼 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 마구 두드려라. 네 몸을 탑(塔) 하나로 만들어 봐."]], [["FRENCH SAILOR.", "프랑스 선원."], ["Merry-mad!", "신나게 미쳤구나!"], ["Hold up thy hoop, Pip, till I jump through it!", "핍, 네 굴렁쇠를 높이 들어라, 내가 그걸 통과해 뛰어넘게!"], ["Split jibs!", "삼각돛을 찢어라!"], ["tear yourselves!", "몸을 박살 내듯 뛰어라!"]], [["TASHTEGO.", "태시테고."], ["(Quietly smoking.) That’s a white man; he calls that fun: humph!", "(조용히 담배를 피우며.) 저게 백인이지. 저런 걸 재미라고 부르는군."], ["I save my sweat.", "흥! 나는 내 땀이나 아껴 두겠어."]], [["OLD MANX SAILOR.", "늙은 맨섬 선원."], ["I wonder whether those jolly lads bethink them of what they are dancing over.", "저 흥겨운 녀석들이 자기네가 무엇 위에서 춤추고 있는지 한 번이라도 생각이나 해 봤을까."], ["I’ll dance over your grave, I will—that’s the bitterest threat of your night-women, that beat head-winds round corners. O Christ!", "내가 네 무덤 위에서 춤추겠다, 그렇고말고—그게 모퉁이를 돌 때마다 역풍처럼 들이치는 너희 밤 계집들의 가장 모진 저주가 아니던가."], ["to think of the green navies and the green-skulled crews!", "오, 그리스도여! 저 푸른 함대와 푸른 해골이 된 선원들을 생각하면!"], ["Well, well; belike the whole world’s a ball, as you scholars have it; and so ’tis right to make one ballroom of it.", "흠, 흠. 아마 온 세상이 너희 학자들 말마따나 하나의 공일 테고, 그러니 그것을 하나의 무도장으로 삼는 게 옳기도 하겠지."], ["Dance on, lads, you’re young; I was once.", "계속 춤춰라, 얘들아, 너희는 젊으니. 나도 한때는 그랬다."]], [["3D NANTUCKET SAILOR.", "낸터킷 선원 3."], ["Spell oh!—whew!", "잠깐 쉬자!—휴!"], ["this is worse than pulling after whales in a calm—give us a whiff, Tash.", "이건 잔잔한 바다에서 고래 뒤를 노 저어 쫓는 것보다 더 힘들군—한 모금만 빨게 해 주게, 태시."]], [["(They cease dancing, and gather in clusters.", "(그들이 춤을 멈추고 무리 지어 모인다."], ["Meantime the sky darkens—the wind rises.)", "그 사이 하늘이 어두워지고—바람이 인다.)"]], [["LASCAR SAILOR.", "라스카르 선원."], ["By Brahma!", "브라마 신이시여!"], ["boys, it’ll be douse sail soon.", "얘들아, 곧 돛을 내려야겠어."], ["The sky-born, high-tide Ganges turned to wind!", "하늘에서 태어난, 만조의 갠지스강이 바람으로 변했구나!"], ["Thou showest thy black brow, Seeva!", "그대의 검은 이마를 드러내시는군요, 시바 신이여!"]], [["MALTESE SAILOR.", "몰타 선원."], ["(Reclining and shaking his cap.) It’s the waves—the snow’s caps turn to jig it now.", "(비스듬히 누워 모자를 흔들며.) 저건 파도야—눈 같은 물마루들이 이제 춤을 추려고 하는군."], ["They’ll shake their tassels soon.", "저것들도 곧 술 장식을 흔들어 댈 거야."], ["Now would all the waves were women, then I’d go drown, and chassee with them evermore!", "지금 저 모든 파도가 여자라면, 나는 기꺼이 빠져 죽으며 그들과 영원토록 춤춰 나가련만!"], ["There’s naught so sweet on earth—heaven may not match it!—as those swift glances of warm, wild bosoms in the dance, when the over-arboring arms hide such ripe, bursting grapes.", "이 땅에 그토록 달콤한 것은 없어—천국조차 견줄 수 없으리라!—춤출 때 따뜻하고 거친 가슴이 던지는 저 날렵한 눈길만큼은, 머리 위로 뻗은 두 팔이 그 무르익어 터질 듯한 포도송이를 가릴 때 말이야."]], [["SICILIAN SAILOR.", "시칠리아 선원."], ["(Reclining.) Tell me not of it!", "(비스듬히 누워.) 그 얘긴 꺼내지도 마!"], ["Hark ye, lad—fleet interlacings of the limbs—lithe swayings—coyings—flutterings!", "들어 봐, 친구—날래게 뒤엉키는 팔다리—나긋나긋한 흔들림—수줍은 몸짓—파르르 떨림! 입술!"], ["lip!", "가슴!"], ["heart!", "엉덩이!"], ["hip! all graze: unceasing touch and go!", "모두 스칠 듯 말 듯: 끊임없이 닿았다 떨어지는 것!"], ["not taste, observe ye, else come satiety.", "맛보지는 마, 알겠나, 그러면 곧 물려 버리니까. 그렇지, 이교도 양반?"], ["Eh, Pagan? (Nudging.)", "(옆구리를 쿡 찌르며.)"]], [["TAHITAN SAILOR.", "타히티 선원."], ["(Reclining on a mat.) Hail, holy nakedness of our dancing girls!—the Heeva-Heeva!", "(돗자리에 비스듬히 누워.) 만세, 우리 무희들의 성스러운 알몸이여!—헤에바-헤에바!"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["low veiled, high palmed Tahiti!", "낮게 드리워지고 높이 야자수가 솟은 타히티여!"], ["I still rest me on thy mat, but the soft soil has slid!", "나는 아직도 네 돗자리 위에서 쉬건만, 부드러운 흙은 이미 미끄러져 떠나 버렸구나!"], ["I saw thee woven in the wood, my mat!", "숲에서 너를 엮는 모습을 내가 보았지, 내 돗자리야!"], ["green the first day I brought ye thence; now worn and wilted quite.", "너를 거기서 가져온 첫날에는 푸르렀건만, 이제는 닳고 시들어 버렸구나."], ["Ah me!—not thou nor I can bear the change!", "아, 나여!—너도 나도 이 변화를 견딜 수 없으니!"], ["How then, if so be transplanted to yon sky?", "그렇다면 저 하늘로 옮겨 심긴다면 어떠하랴?"], ["Hear I the roaring streams from Pirohitee’s peak of spears, when they leap down the crags and drown the villages?—The blast!", "피로히티 봉우리의 창날 같은 정상에서 우렁차게 쏟아지는 물줄기 소리가 들리는가, 그것들이 벼랑을 뛰어내려 마을을 잠기게 할 때?—돌풍이다!"], ["the blast!", "돌풍이야!"], ["Up, spine, and meet it!", "일어서라, 등뼈여, 맞서라!"], ["(Leaps to his feet.)", "(벌떡 일어선다.)"]], [["PORTUGUESE SAILOR.", "포르투갈 선원."], ["How the sea rolls swashing ’gainst the side!", "바다가 어찌나 출렁이며 뱃전에 부딪쳐 오는지!"], ["Stand by for reefing, hearties!", "돛을 줄일 준비를 해라, 친구들아!"], ["the winds are just crossing swords, pell-mell they’ll go lunging presently.", "바람이 막 칼을 맞부딪치고 있으니, 머지않아 마구 뒤엉켜 찔러 댈 것이다."]], [["DANISH SAILOR.", "덴마크 선원."], ["Crack, crack, old ship!", "삐걱, 삐걱, 늙은 배여!"], ["so long as thou crackest, thou holdest!", "네가 삐걱대는 한, 너는 버티는 것이다!"], ["Well done!", "잘한다!"], ["The mate there holds ye to it stiffly.", "저기 항해사가 너를 단단히 붙들고 있구나."], ["He’s no more afraid than the isle fort at Cattegat, put there to fight the Baltic with storm-lashed guns, on which the sea-salt cakes!", "그는 카테가트 해협의 섬 요새보다도 겁이 없어, 폭풍에 두들겨 맞은 대포로 발트해와 맞서 싸우라고 거기 세워진, 바닷소금이 켜켜이 들러붙은 그 요새 말이야!"]], [["4TH NANTUCKET SAILOR.", "낸터킷 선원 4."], ["He has his orders, mind ye that.", "그는 명령을 받았어, 그 점을 명심해라."], ["I heard old Ahab tell him he must always kill a squall, something as they burst a waterspout with a pistol—fire your ship right into it!", "늙은 에이해브가 그에게 이르는 걸 내가 들었지, 언제나 돌풍을 죽여야 한다고, 마치 권총으로 용오름을 터뜨리듯이 말이야—네 배를 곧장 그 속으로 쏘아 넣으라는 거지!"]], [["ENGLISH SAILOR.", "영국 선원."], ["Blood!", "빌어먹을!"], ["but that old man’s a grand old cove!", "그 늙은이는 정말 대단한 양반이야!"], ["We are the lads to hunt him up his whale!", "우리야말로 그를 위해 그의 고래를 사냥해 줄 녀석들이지!"]], [["ALL.", "모두."], ["Aye!", "옳소!"], ["aye!", "옳소!"]], [["OLD MANX SAILOR.", "늙은 맨섬 선원."], ["How the three pines shake!", "저 세 그루 소나무가 어찌나 흔들리는지!"], ["Pines are the hardest sort of tree to live when shifted to any other soil, and here there’s none but the crew’s cursed clay.", "소나무는 다른 흙으로 옮겨지면 살아남기 가장 어려운 나무인데, 여기엔 선원들의 저주받은 진흙밖에 없으니."], ["Steady, helmsman!", "침착해라, 키잡이!"], ["steady.", "침착해."], ["This is the sort of weather when brave hearts snap ashore, and keeled hulls split at sea.", "이런 날씨야말로 용감한 마음이 뭍에서 뚝 꺾이고, 용골 박힌 선체가 바다에서 쪼개지는 그런 날씨다."], ["Our captain has his birthmark; look yonder, boys, there’s another in the sky—lurid-like, ye see, all else pitch black.", "우리 선장에게는 그의 모반(母斑)이 있지. 저기 좀 봐라, 얘들아, 하늘에도 또 하나가 있으니—거 봐, 핏빛처럼 음산하고, 그 밖에는 온통 칠흑같이 검구나."]], [["DAGGOO.", "대구."], ["What of that?", "그게 뭐 어쨌다고?"], ["Who’s afraid of black’s afraid of me!", "검은 것을 두려워하는 자는 나를 두려워하는 거다!"], ["I’m quarried out of it!", "나는 검은 것에서 깎여 나온 몸이니까!"]], [["SPANISH SAILOR.", "스페인 선원."], ["(Aside.) He wants to bully, ah!—the old grudge makes me touchy (Advancing.) Aye, harpooneer, thy race is the undeniable dark side of mankind—devilish dark at that.", "(방백.) 저자가 으스대려는군, 아!—오래된 원한 때문에 신경이 곤두서는걸 (앞으로 나서며.) 그래, 작살잡이여, 자네 종족은 부정할 수 없는 인류의 어두운 면이지—그것도 악마처럼 시커먼 면이고."], ["No offence.", "기분 상해하진 말게."]], [["DAGGOO (grimly).", "대구 (험악하게)."], ["None.", "상할 것 없어."]], [["ST. JAGO’S SAILOR.", "세인트야고 선원."], ["That Spaniard’s mad or drunk.", "저 스페인 사람이 미쳤거나 취했군."], ["But that can’t be, or else in his one case our old Mogul’s fire-waters are somewhat long in working.", "하지만 그럴 리 없지, 그게 아니라면 저 한 사람에게만은 우리 늙은 모굴의 독주가 효력을 내는 데 좀 오래 걸리는 셈이니까."]], [["5TH NANTUCKET SAILOR.", "낸터킷 선원 5."], ["What’s that I saw—lightning?", "내가 본 게 뭐지—번개인가?"], ["Yes.", "그래."]], [["SPANISH SAILOR.", "스페인 선원. 아니."], ["No; Daggoo showing his teeth.", "대구가 이빨을 드러낸 거야."]], [["DAGGOO (springing).", "대구 (벌떡 일어서며)."], ["Swallow thine, mannikin!", "네 이빨이나 삼켜라, 이 난쟁이야!"], ["White skin, white liver!", "흰 살갗에, 흰 간이로구나!"]], [["SPANISH SAILOR (meeting him).", "스페인 선원 (그와 맞서며)."], ["Knife thee heartily!", "네놈을 신나게 칼로 찔러 주마!"], ["big frame, small spirit!", "덩치만 크고, 배짱은 쥐꼬리만 하구나!"]], [["ALL.", "모두."], ["A row!", "싸움이다!"], ["a row!", "싸움이야!"], ["a row!", "싸움이다!"]], [["TASHTEGO (with a whiff).", "태시테고 (한 모금 빨며)."], ["A row a’low, and a row aloft—Gods and men—both brawlers!", "아래도 싸움, 위도 싸움—신들도 인간들도—다 싸움질이로군!"], ["Humph!", "흥!"]], [["BELFAST SAILOR.", "벨파스트 선원."], ["A row!", "싸움이다!"], ["arrah a row!", "어이쿠, 싸움이야!"], ["The Virgin be blessed, a row!", "성모께 축복 있으라, 싸움이다!"], ["Plunge in with ye!", "다들 뛰어들어라!"]], [["ENGLISH SAILOR.", "영국 선원."], ["Fair play!", "정정당당하게!"], ["Snatch the Spaniard’s knife!", "스페인 놈의 칼을 빼앗아라!"], ["A ring, a ring!", "둥글게, 둥글게 둘러서라!"]], [["OLD MANX SAILOR.", "늙은 맨섬 선원."], ["Ready formed. There!", "벌써 둥글게 만들어졌군."], ["the ringed horizon.", "저기! 둥근 수평선이로다."], ["In that ring Cain struck Abel.", "저 둥근 테 안에서 카인이 아벨을 쳤지."], ["Sweet work, right work!", "달콤한 짓이고, 옳은 짓이야! 아니라고?"], ["No? Why then, God, mad’st thou the ring?", "그럼 어째서, 하느님이시여, 당신은 저 둥근 테를 만드셨나이까?"]], [["MATE’S VOICE FROM THE QUARTER-DECK.", "뒷갑판에서 들려오는 항해사의 목소리."], ["Hands by the halyards!", "모두 활대 밧줄을 잡아라!"], ["in top-gallant sails!", "윗돛을 거둬라!"], ["Stand by to reef topsails!", "큰돛을 줄일 준비를 해라!"]], [["ALL.", "모두."], ["The squall!", "돌풍이다!"], ["the squall!", "돌풍이야!"], ["jump, my jollies!", "뛰어라, 흥겨운 친구들아!"], ["(They scatter.)", "(그들이 흩어진다.)"]], [["PIP (shrinking under the windlass).", "핍 (양묘기 밑으로 움츠러들며). 흥겨운 친구들?"], ["Jollies? Lord help such jollies!", "주여, 저런 흥겨운 친구들을 도우소서!"], ["Crish, crash!", "우지끈, 와장창!"], ["there goes the jib-stay!", "저기 삼각돛 버팀줄이 끊어지네!"], ["Blang-whang!", "쾅-쾅! 하느님!"], ["God! Duck lower, Pip, here comes the royal yard!", "더 낮게 숙여라, 핍, 저기 윗활대가 내려온다!"], ["It’s worse than being in the whirled woods, the last day of the year!", "한 해의 마지막 날에 빙빙 도는 숲속에 있는 것보다 더 끔찍해!"], ["Who’d go climbing after chestnuts now?", "이런 판에 누가 밤톨을 따러 올라가겠어?"], ["But there they go, all cursing, and here I don’t.", "그런데 저들은 가는군, 모두 욕설을 퍼부으며, 나는 안 가지만."], ["Fine prospects to ’em; they’re on the road to heaven.", "저들에게는 좋은 앞날이 있지. 천국으로 가는 길에 올랐으니까."], ["Hold on hard!", "단단히 붙들어라!"], ["Jimmini, what a squall!", "맙소사, 무슨 이런 돌풍이!"], ["But those chaps there are worse yet—they are your white squalls, they.", "하지만 저기 저 녀석들은 더 고약해—저것들은 흰 돌풍이야, 정말."], ["White squalls?", "흰 돌풍이라고?"], ["white whale, shirr!", "흰 고래, 휙!"], ["shirr!", "휙!"], ["Here have I heard all their chat just now, and the white whale—shirr!", "방금 저들이 떠드는 소리를 다 들었는데, 흰 고래는—휙!"], ["shirr!—but spoken of once!", "휙!—딱 한 번 입에 올렸을 뿐이지!"], ["and only this evening—it makes me jingle all over like my tambourine—that anaconda of an old man swore ’em in to hunt him!", "그것도 바로 오늘 저녁—그 생각만 하면 온몸이 내 탬버린처럼 딸랑딸랑 떨려 온다—저 아나콘다 같은 늙은이가 그놈을 사냥하라고 저들에게 맹세를 시켰어!"], ["Oh, thou big white God aloft there somewhere in yon darkness, have mercy on this small black boy down here; preserve him from all men that have no bowels to feel fear!", "오, 저 어둠 속 어딘가 높은 곳에 계신 위대한 흰 신이시여, 이 아래에 있는 이 작은 검둥이 소년에게 자비를 베푸소서. 두려움을 느낄 창자조차 없는 모든 인간들로부터 그를 지켜 주소서!"]], [["# CHAPTER 41. Moby Dick.", "# CHAPTER 41. Moby Dick."]], [["I, Ishmael, was one of that crew; my shouts had gone up with the rest; my oath had been welded with theirs; and stronger I shouted, and more did I hammer and clinch my oath, because of the dread in my soul.", "나, 이슈메일도 그 선원들 가운데 하나였다. 내 함성도 다른 이들의 함성과 함께 솟아올랐고, 내 맹세도 그들의 맹세와 한데 용접되었다."], ["A wild, mystical, sympathetical feeling was in me; Ahab’s quenchless feud seemed mine.", "그리고 나는 영혼 속에 깃든 두려움 때문에 더욱 크게 외쳤으며, 더욱 힘껏 맹세를 두드려 박고 단단히 다졌다. 거칠고 신비롭고 동조하는 감정이 내 안에 있었다."], ["With greedy ears I learned the history of that murderous monster against whom I and all the others had taken our oaths of violence and revenge.", "에이해브의 꺼지지 않는 원한이 마치 나 자신의 것인 양 느껴졌다. 나는 탐욕스러운 귀로, 나와 다른 모든 이들이 폭력과 복수의 맹세를 바친 그 살인적인 괴물의 내력을 들었다."]], [["For some time past, though at intervals only, the unaccompanied, secluded White Whale had haunted those uncivilized seas mostly frequented by the Sperm Whale fishermen.", "지난 한동안, 비록 띄엄띄엄이긴 했으나, 홀로 외떨어진 흰 고래는 향유고래잡이 어부들이 주로 드나드는 저 미개한 바다를 떠돌고 있었다."], ["But not all of them knew of his existence; only a few of them, comparatively, had knowingly seen him; while the number who as yet had actually and knowingly given battle to him, was small indeed.", "그러나 그들 모두가 그 존재를 알고 있었던 것은 아니다. 그를 직접 보고 알아본 자는 비교적 소수에 지나지 않았고, 실제로 그를 알아보고 맞서 싸운 자의 수에 이르러서는 참으로 적었다."], ["For, owing to the large number of whale-cruisers; the disorderly way they were sprinkled over the entire watery circumference, many of them adventurously pushing their quest along solitary latitudes, so as seldom or never for a whole twelvemonth or more on a stretch, to encounter a single news-telling sail of any sort; the inordinate length of each separate voyage; the irregularity of the times of sailing from home; all these, with other circumstances, direct and indirect, long obstructed the spread through the whole world-wide whaling-fleet of the special individualizing tidings concerning Moby Dick.", "그도 그럴 것이, 고래잡이배의 수가 워낙 많고, 그것들이 광대한 바다의 둘레 전체에 어수선하게 흩어져 있는 데다, 그 가운데 다수가 외딴 위도를 따라 모험적으로 사냥을 밀고 나아가서, 한 해 남짓을 내리 항해해도 소식을 전해 줄 돛 한 척조차 좀처럼, 아니 결코 마주치지 못하는 일이 흔했기 때문이다. 게다가 항해 하나하나가 터무니없이 길고, 고향을 떠나는 출항 시기 또한 들쭉날쭉했으니, 이런 사정들이 직간접의 다른 정황들과 더불어, 모비 딕에 관한 특별하고도 구체적인 소식이 전 세계 고래잡이 선단에 두루 퍼지는 일을 오랫동안 가로막았다."], ["It was hardly to be doubted, that several vessels reported to have encountered, at such or such a time, or on such or such a meridian, a Sperm Whale of uncommon magnitude and malignity, which whale, after doing great mischief to his assailants, had completely escaped them; to some minds it was not an unfair presumption, I say, that the whale in question must have been no other than Moby Dick.", "어느 때, 혹은 어느 자오선 위에서 유달리 거대하고 흉포한 향유고래를 만났으며, 그 고래가 공격자들에게 큰 화를 입힌 뒤 완전히 빠져나갔다고 보고한 배가 여럿 있었음은 거의 의심할 여지가 없었다. 내 말하건대, 어떤 이들에게는 그 문제의 고래가 다름 아닌 모비 딕이었으리라는 짐작이 결코 부당한 것이 아니었다."], ["Yet as of late the Sperm Whale fishery had been marked by various and not unfrequent instances of great ferocity, cunning, and malice in the monster attacked; therefore it was, that those who by accident ignorantly gave battle to Moby Dick; such hunters, perhaps, for the most part, were content to ascribe the peculiar terror he bred, more, as it were, to the perils of the Sperm Whale fishery at large, than to the individual cause.", "그러나 근래 들어 향유고래잡이에는 공격받은 괴물이 보인 엄청난 흉포함과 교활함과 악의의 사례들이 다양하게, 그것도 드물지 않게 나타났기에, 우연히 영문도 모른 채 모비 딕과 맞붙은 자들, 즉 그런 사냥꾼들은 대개 그가 빚어내는 그 유별난 공포를, 말하자면 그 개별적인 원인보다는 향유고래잡이 전반의 위험에 돌리는 것으로 만족했다."], ["In that way, mostly, the disastrous encounter between Ahab and the whale had hitherto been popularly regarded.", "에이해브와 그 고래 사이의 참혹한 조우 또한 지금까지는 대체로 그런 식으로 사람들에게 받아들여져 왔다."]], [["And as for those who, previously hearing of the White Whale, by chance caught sight of him; in the beginning of the thing they had every one of them, almost, as boldly and fearlessly lowered for him, as for any other whale of that species.", "그리고 흰 고래에 관해 미리 들어 두었다가 우연히 그를 목격하게 된 자들로 말하자면, 일이 처음 벌어졌을 때 그들은 거의 하나같이 그 종(種)의 다른 어떤 고래를 잡을 때와 다름없이 대담하고 거침없이 그를 향해 보트를 내렸다."], ["But at length, such calamities did ensue in these assaults—not restricted to sprained wrists and ankles, broken limbs, or devouring amputations—but fatal to the last degree of fatality; those repeated disastrous repulses, all accumulating and piling their terrors upon Moby Dick; those things had gone far to shake the fortitude of many brave hunters, to whom the story of the White Whale had eventually come.", "그러나 마침내 이 공격들에는 온갖 재앙이 뒤따르고 말았으니 — 손목이나 발목이 삐고, 사지가 부러지고, 끔찍한 절단을 당하는 정도에 그치지 않고 — 가장 극심한 죽음에까지 이르는 일이 거듭되었다. 그렇게 거듭된 참혹한 격퇴가, 모비 딕에게 그 모든 공포를 차곡차곡 쌓아 올렸으니, 이런 일들은 흰 고래의 이야기가 끝내 흘러든 수많은 용맹한 사냥꾼들의 굳센 기개마저 크게 뒤흔들어 놓았다."]], [["Nor did wild rumors of all sorts fail to exaggerate, and still the more horrify the true histories of these deadly encounters.", "또한 온갖 거친 소문들도 이 치명적인 조우의 참된 내력을 부풀리고 한층 더 끔찍하게 만드는 데 빠지지 않았다."], ["For not only do fabulous rumors naturally grow out of the very body of all surprising terrible events,—as the smitten tree gives birth to its fungi; but, in maritime life, far more than in that of terra firma, wild rumors abound, wherever there is any adequate reality for them to cling to.", "그도 그럴 것이, 벼락 맞은 나무가 거기서 버섯을 피워 내듯, 놀랍고 무시무시한 사건의 바로 그 몸뚱이에서 황당한 소문이 절로 자라나는 것은 당연하거니와, 게다가 뭍의 삶에서보다 바다의 삶에서, 그 소문이 매달릴 만한 그럴듯한 실체가 조금이라도 있는 곳이면 어디든 거친 소문이 넘쳐 나기 때문이다."], ["And as the sea surpasses the land in this matter, so the whale fishery surpasses every other sort of maritime life, in the wonderfulness and fearfulness of the rumors which sometimes circulate there.", "그리고 이 점에서 바다가 뭍을 능가하듯이, 고래잡이는 거기서 이따금 떠도는 소문들의 경이로움과 무시무시함에 있어 다른 모든 종류의 바다 생활을 능가한다."], ["For not only are whalemen as a body unexempt from that ignorance and superstitiousness hereditary to all sailors; but of all sailors, they are by all odds the most directly brought into contact with whatever is appallingly astonishing in the sea; face to face they not only eye its greatest marvels, but, hand to jaw, give battle to them.", "그도 그럴 것이, 고래잡이들이 한 무리로서 모든 뱃사람에게 대물림된 그 무지와 미신에서 벗어나 있지 못할 뿐만 아니라, 모든 뱃사람 가운데 그들이야말로 단연 바다에서 가장 소름 끼치도록 놀라운 것들과 가장 직접 맞닿아 살아가는 자들이기 때문이다. 그들은 얼굴을 맞대고 바다의 가장 위대한 경이를 두 눈으로 볼 뿐 아니라, 손과 턱을 맞대고 그것들과 싸운다."], ["Alone, in such remotest waters, that though you sailed a thousand miles, and passed a thousand shores, you would not come to any chiseled hearth-stone, or aught hospitable beneath that part of the sun; in such latitudes and longitudes, pursuing too such a calling as he does, the whaleman is wrapped by influences all tending to make his fancy pregnant with many a mighty birth.", "천 마일을 항해하고 천 개의 해안을 지나도 정으로 다듬은 화롯돌 하나, 저 햇빛 아래 사람을 맞아 줄 것 하나 만나지 못할 만큼 외딴 바다, 그토록 먼 바다에서 홀로, 그것도 그가 하는 것과 같은 일을 좇으며 살아가니, 고래잡이는 그 상상력을 숱한 거대한 산물로 잉태시키는 온갖 영향력에 휩싸여 있는 것이다."]], [["No wonder, then, that ever gathering volume from the mere transit over the widest watery spaces, the outblown rumors of the White Whale did in the end incorporate with themselves all manner of morbid hints, and half-formed fœtal suggestions of supernatural agencies, which eventually invested Moby Dick with new terrors unborrowed from anything that visibly appears.", "그러니, 가장 드넓은 바다를 가로질러 흐르는 동안 부피만 점점 불려 나간 끝에, 흰 고래에 관해 퍼져 나간 소문이 마침내 온갖 병적인 암시와, 초자연적 작용을 어렴풋이 빚어내는 태아 같은 추측들까지 제 속에 끌어안게 된 것도 놀랄 일이 아니다. 그것들은 결국 눈에 보이는 그 무엇에서도 빌려 오지 않은 새로운 공포를 모비 딕에게 입혔다."], ["So that in many cases such a panic did he finally strike, that few who by those rumors, at least, had heard of the White Whale, few of those hunters were willing to encounter the perils of his jaw.", "그리하여 많은 경우 그가 마침내 불러일으킨 공황이 얼마나 컸던지, 적어도 그런 소문을 통해 흰 고래에 관해 들어 본 자들 가운데 그의 턱이 안기는 위험과 기꺼이 맞서려는 사냥꾼은 거의 없었다."]], [["But there were still other and more vital practical influences at work.", "그러나 여기에는 또 다른, 더 본질적이고 실질적인 영향력들이 작용하고 있었다."], ["Not even at the present day has the original prestige of the Sperm Whale, as fearfully distinguished from all other species of the leviathan, died out of the minds of the whalemen as a body.", "오늘날에 이르러서도, 리바이어던의 다른 모든 종과 무섭도록 구별되는 향유고래 본연의 위엄은 고래잡이라는 무리의 마음에서 사그라지지 않았다. 오늘날 그들 가운데에는, 그린란드고래나 참고래에게 싸움을 거는 데는 충분히 영리하고 용감하면서도, 직업적 경험 부족이거나 무능이거나 소심함 때문에 향유고래와의 대결은 마다할 자들이 있을 것이다."], ["There are those this day among them, who, though intelligent and courageous enough in offering battle to the Greenland or Right whale, would perhaps—either from professional inexperience, or incompetency, or timidity, decline a contest with the Sperm Whale; at any rate, there are plenty of whalemen, especially among those whaling nations not sailing under the American flag, who have never hostilely encountered the Sperm Whale, but whose sole knowledge of the leviathan is restricted to the ignoble monster primitively pursued in the North; seated on their hatches, these men will hearken with a childish fireside interest and awe, to the wild, strange tales of Southern whaling.", "어쨌거나, 특히 미국 깃발 아래 항해하지 않는 고래잡이 나라들 사이에는, 향유고래와 적대적으로 맞붙어 본 적이 한 번도 없고, 리바이어던에 관한 지식이라고는 처음부터 북쪽에서 쫓던 그 변변찮은 괴물에 국한된 고래잡이가 수두룩하다. 이런 자들은 승강구에 걸터앉아, 어린아이가 화롯가에서 듣듯 호기심과 경외에 차서 남쪽 고래잡이의 거칠고 기이한 이야기에 귀를 기울일 것이다."], ["Nor is the pre-eminent tremendousness of the great Sperm Whale anywhere more feelingly comprehended, than on board of those prows which stem him.", "그리고 위대한 향유고래의 그 압도적인 무시무시함을, 그를 뱃머리로 헤치며 나아가는 그 배들 위에서보다 더 절실히 헤아리는 곳은 어디에도 없다."]], [["And as if the now tested reality of his might had in former legendary times thrown its shadow before it; we find some book naturalists—Olassen and Povelson—declaring the Sperm Whale not only to be a consternation to every other creature in the sea, but also to be so incredibly ferocious as continually to be athirst for human blood.", "그리고 마치 이제 입증된 그 힘의 실체가 옛 전설의 시대에 미리 그 그림자를 드리워 두기라도 한 듯, 우리는 어떤 책상물림 박물학자들 — 올라센과 포벨손 — 이 향유고래를 두고 바다의 다른 모든 피조물에게 공포의 대상일 뿐 아니라, 끊임없이 사람의 피에 목말라할 만큼 믿을 수 없으리만치 흉포하다고 단언한 것을 본다."], ["Nor even down to so late a time as Cuvier’s, were these or almost similar impressions effaced.", "그리고 퀴비에의 시대만큼 늦은 무렵까지도 이러한, 혹은 거의 비슷한 인상은 지워지지 않았다."], ["For in his Natural History, the Baron himself affirms that at sight of the Sperm Whale, all fish (sharks included) are “struck with the most lively terrors,”", "그도 그럴 것이, 남작 자신은 그의 박물지에서, 향유고래를 보면 모든 물고기가(상어까지 포함하여) \"더없이 생생한 공포에 사로잡히며\""], ["and “often in the precipitancy of their flight dash themselves against the rocks with such violence as to cause instantaneous death.”", "또 \"달아나려는 황급함 속에서 어찌나 사납게 바위에 제 몸을 부딪치는지 그 자리에서 즉사할 정도\"라고 단언했기 때문이다."], ["And however the general experiences in the fishery may amend such reports as these; yet in their full terribleness, even to the bloodthirsty item of Povelson, the superstitious belief in them is, in some vicissitudes of their vocation, revived in the minds of the hunters.", "그리고 고래잡이의 일반적 경험이 이러한 보고들을 아무리 바로잡는다 한들, 그것들이 지닌 온전한 무시무시함, 심지어 포벨손이 말한 그 피에 굶주린 대목에 이르기까지, 그것들에 대한 미신적 믿음은 그 직업의 어떤 굴곡진 순간에 사냥꾼들의 마음속에서 되살아나는 것이다."]], [["So that overawed by the rumors and portents concerning him, not a few of the fishermen recalled, in reference to Moby Dick, the earlier days of the Sperm Whale fishery, when it was oftentimes hard to induce long practised Right whalemen to embark in the perils of this new and daring warfare; such men protesting that although other leviathans might be hopefully pursued, yet to chase and point lance at such an apparition as the Sperm Whale was not for mortal man.", "그리하여 그에 관한 소문과 흉조에 압도된 적지 않은 어부들이, 모비 딕과 관련하여 향유고래잡이의 초기 시절을 떠올렸다. 그때는 오랜 경험을 쌓은 참고래잡이들을 이 새롭고 대담한 싸움의 위험 속으로 끌어들이기가 흔히 어려웠다."], ["That to attempt it, would be inevitably to be torn into a quick eternity.", "그런 자들은 항변하기를, 다른 리바이어던이라면 희망을 품고 쫓을 만하나, 향유고래 같은 환영을 뒤쫓아 작살을 겨누는 일은 인간의 몫이 아니라고 했다. 그런 짓을 시도하는 것은 곧 산 채로 갈가리 찢겨 순식간에 영원 속으로 떨어지는 일이라는 것이었다."], ["On this head, there are some remarkable documents that may be consulted.", "이 점에 관해서는 참고할 만한 주목할 기록들이 더러 남아 있다."]], [["Nevertheless, some there were, who even in the face of these things were ready to give chase to Moby Dick; and a still greater number who, chancing only to hear of him distantly and vaguely, without the specific details of any certain calamity, and without superstitious accompaniments, were sufficiently hardy not to flee from the battle if offered.", "그럼에도, 이런 일들 앞에서조차 모비 딕을 기꺼이 추격하려는 자들이 더러 있었다. 그리고 그를 멀리서 어렴풋이 들어 보았을 뿐, 어떤 확실한 재앙의 구체적 정황도, 미신적인 꾸밈도 없이 알게 된 까닭에, 싸움이 닥쳐와도 달아나지 않을 만큼 충분히 담대한 자들은 그보다 훨씬 더 많았다."]], [["One of the wild suggestions referred to, as at last coming to be linked with the White Whale in the minds of the superstitiously inclined, was the unearthly conceit that Moby Dick was ubiquitous; that he had actually been encountered in opposite latitudes at one and the same instant of time.", "미신에 기울어진 자들의 마음속에서 끝내 흰 고래와 결부되기에 이른, 앞서 언급한 그 거친 추측들 가운데 하나는, 모비 딕이 어디에나 동시에 존재한다는 섬뜩한 망상이었다. 다시 말해, 그가 같은 시각 정반대의 위도에서 실제로 목격되었다는 것이었다."]], [["Nor, credulous as such minds must have been, was this conceit altogether without some faint show of superstitious probability.", "그러나 그런 마음들이 아무리 잘 속아 넘어가는 것이었다 해도, 이 망상이 미신적 개연성의 어렴풋한 자취조차 전혀 없는 것은 아니었다."], ["For as the secrets of the currents in the seas have never yet been divulged, even to the most erudite research; so the hidden ways of the Sperm Whale when beneath the surface remain, in great part, unaccountable to his pursuers; and from time to time have originated the most curious and contradictory speculations regarding them, especially concerning the mystic modes whereby, after sounding to a great depth, he transports himself with such vast swiftness to the most widely distant points.", "그도 그럴 것이, 바다 해류의 비밀이 아직껏 가장 박식한 연구에조차 밝혀진 바 없듯이, 향유고래가 수면 아래로 잠겼을 때 다니는 그 숨은 길도 대부분은 추격자들로서는 헤아릴 수 없는 것으로 남아 있다. 그리하여 그것들에 관해, 특히 그가 깊은 바닥까지 잠수한 뒤에 그토록 엄청난 속도로 더없이 멀리 떨어진 지점으로 제 몸을 옮겨 가는 그 신비로운 방식에 관해, 더없이 기이하고 모순된 억측들이 이따금 생겨나곤 했다."]], [["It is a thing well known to both American and English whale-ships, and as well a thing placed upon authoritative record years ago by Scoresby, that some whales have been captured far north in the Pacific, in whose bodies have been found the barbs of harpoons darted in the Greenland seas.", "어떤 고래들이 태평양 멀리 북쪽에서 잡혔는데, 그 몸뚱이 안에서 그린란드 바다에서 던진 작살의 미늘이 발견되었다는 것은, 미국과 영국 고래잡이배 양쪽에 두루 잘 알려진 사실이며, 또한 여러 해 전에 스코어즈비가 권위 있는 기록으로 남겨 둔 사실이기도 하다."], ["Nor is it to be gainsaid, that in some of these instances it has been declared that the interval of time between the two assaults could not have exceeded very many days.", "그리고 이런 사례들 가운데 더러는 두 차례 공격 사이의 시간 간격이 며칠을 그리 넘지 않았으리라고 단언된 바 있음 또한 부인할 수 없다."], ["Hence, by inference, it has been believed by some whalemen, that the Nor’ West Passage, so long a problem to man, was never a problem to the whale.", "그리하여 어떤 고래잡이들은 미루어 짐작하기를, 그토록 오랫동안 인간에게 풀리지 않는 숙제였던 북서 항로가 고래에게는 애초에 숙제였던 적이 없다고 믿었다."], ["So that here, in the real living experience of living men, the prodigies related in old times of the inland Strello mountain in Portugal (near whose top there was said to be a lake in which the wrecks of ships floated up to the surface); and that still more wonderful story of the Arethusa fountain near Syracuse (whose waters were believed to have come from the Holy Land by an underground passage); these fabulous narrations are almost fully equalled by the realities of the whalemen.", "그리하여 여기, 살아 있는 사람들의 실제로 겪은 경험 속에서, 포르투갈 내륙의 스트렐로산에 관해 옛날부터 전해 오는 신기한 이야기(그 꼭대기 가까이에 호수가 있어 그 안으로 난파선의 잔해가 수면 위로 떠올랐다고 한다)와, 그보다 한층 더 놀라운 시라쿠사 근처 아레투사 샘에 관한 이야기(그 샘물이 지하 통로를 거쳐 성지에서 흘러왔다고 믿어졌다) — 이런 황당한 전설들이 고래잡이들의 현실에 거의 고스란히 맞먹게 되는 것이다."]], [["Forced into familiarity, then, with such prodigies as these; and knowing that after repeated, intrepid assaults, the White Whale had escaped alive; it cannot be much matter of surprise that some whalemen should go still further in their superstitions; declaring Moby Dick not only ubiquitous, but immortal (for immortality is but ubiquity in time); that though groves of spears should be planted in his flanks, he would still swim away unharmed; or if indeed he should ever be made to spout thick blood, such a sight would be but a ghastly deception; for again in unensanguined billows hundreds of leagues away, his unsullied jet would once more be seen.", "그렇다면, 이런 신기한 일들에 익히 길들여진 데다, 거듭된 용맹한 공격 끝에도 흰 고래가 산 채로 빠져나갔음을 아는 터이니, 어떤 고래잡이들이 그 미신을 한층 더 멀리까지 밀고 나아간다 한들 그리 놀랄 일은 아니다. 그들은 모비 딕이 어디에나 존재할 뿐 아니라 죽지 않는다고 단언했다(불멸이란 곧 시간 속의 편재일 뿐이므로). 그의 옆구리에 창의 숲이 빽빽이 박힌다 해도 그는 여전히 아무 상처 없이 헤엄쳐 달아날 것이며, 설령 정말로 그가 검붉은 피를 뿜게 되는 일이 있다 해도, 그런 광경은 그저 소름 끼치는 속임수에 지나지 않으리니, 수백 리그 떨어진 피 한 점 섞이지 않은 물결 속에서 그의 티 없이 맑은 물줄기가 다시 한 번 보이리라는 것이었다."]], [["But even stripped of these supernatural surmisings, there was enough in the earthly make and incontestable character of the monster to strike the imagination with unwonted power.", "그러나 이러한 초자연적 억측들을 모두 벗겨 낸다 해도, 그 괴물의 지상적 생김새와 반박할 수 없는 본성 속에는 상상력을 유례없는 힘으로 후려치는 무언가가 충분히 있었다."], ["For, it was not so much his uncommon bulk that so much distinguished him from other sperm whales, but, as was elsewhere thrown out—a peculiar snow-white wrinkled forehead, and a high, pyramidical white hump.", "그도 그럴 것이, 그를 다른 향유고래들과 그토록 두드러지게 갈라놓는 것은 비범한 그 거구라기보다는 — 다른 데서 이미 언급했듯이 — 유달리 새하얗게 주름진 이마와, 높고 피라미드처럼 솟은 흰 혹이었다."], ["These were his prominent features; the tokens whereby, even in the limitless, uncharted seas, he revealed his identity, at a long distance, to those who knew him.", "이것들이 그의 두드러진 특징이었으니, 끝없이 펼쳐진 해도에도 없는 바다에서조차, 그를 아는 자들에게 먼 거리에서도 제 정체를 드러내 보이는 표지였다."]], [["The rest of his body was so streaked, and spotted, and marbled with the same shrouded hue, that, in the end, he had gained his distinctive appellation of the White Whale; a name, indeed, literally justified by his vivid aspect, when seen gliding at high noon through a dark blue sea, leaving a milky-way wake of creamy foam, all spangled with golden gleamings.", "그의 몸뚱이 나머지 부분도 그 똑같이 수의 같은 빛깔로 줄무늬가 지고, 점이 박히고, 대리석처럼 얼룩져 있어서, 끝내 그는 흰 고래라는 그 독특한 별칭을 얻기에 이르렀다. 그것은 참으로, 한낮에 짙푸른 바다를 미끄러지듯 헤치며, 황금빛 광채가 점점이 반짝이는 크림 같은 거품의 은하수 같은 항적을 남기는 그의 선명한 자태를 보면, 글자 그대로 정당화되는 이름이었다."]], [["Nor was it his unwonted magnitude, nor his remarkable hue, nor yet his deformed lower jaw, that so much invested the whale with natural terror, as that unexampled, intelligent malignity which, according to specific accounts, he had over and over again evinced in his assaults.", "그러나 그 고래에게 천부적인 공포를 그토록 짙게 입힌 것은, 비범한 그 크기도, 두드러진 그 빛깔도, 일그러진 그 아래턱조차도 아니었으니, 바로 구체적인 증언들에 따르면 그가 공격할 때 거듭거듭 드러냈다는, 유례없이 지능적인 악의가 그것이었다."], ["More than all, his treacherous retreats struck more of dismay than perhaps aught else.", "무엇보다도 그의 음흉한 후퇴가 어쩌면 다른 그 무엇보다 더 큰 경악을 안겼다."], ["For, when swimming before his exulting pursuers, with every apparent symptom of alarm, he had several times been known to turn round suddenly, and, bearing down upon them, either stave their boats to splinters, or drive them back in consternation to their ship.", "그도 그럴 것이, 의기양양한 추격자들 앞에서 온갖 겁먹은 기색을 다 보이며 헤엄쳐 달아나다가, 그가 불쑥 몸을 돌려 그들을 덮쳐서는 보트를 산산조각으로 부숴 버리거나, 그들을 혼비백산하여 제 배로 도로 쫓아 버린 일이 여러 차례 알려져 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Already several fatalities had attended his chase.", "이미 그를 쫓는 일에는 여러 차례 죽음이 따랐다."], ["But though similar disasters, however little bruited ashore, were by no means unusual in the fishery; yet, in most instances, such seemed the White Whale’s infernal aforethought of ferocity, that every dismembering or death that he caused, was not wholly regarded as having been inflicted by an unintelligent agent.", "그러나 그와 비슷한 재앙들이, 뭍에서는 별로 소문나지 않았을지언정, 고래잡이에서는 결코 드문 일이 아니었음에도, 대부분의 경우 흰 고래가 보인 그 흉포함이 어찌나 지옥처럼 미리 계산된 것 같았던지, 그가 일으킨 모든 절단과 죽음이 지능 없는 행위자에 의해 가해진 것이라고만은 결코 여겨지지 않았다."]], [["Judge, then, to what pitches of inflamed, distracted fury the minds of his more desperate hunters were impelled, when amid the chips of chewed boats, and the sinking limbs of torn comrades, they swam out of the white curds of the whale’s direful wrath into the serene, exasperating sunlight, that smiled on, as if at a birth or a bridal.", "그러니 헤아려 보라, 더 필사적인 사냥꾼들의 마음이 얼마나 활활 타오르고 갈피를 잃은 분노의 절정으로 몰렸을지를. 그들이 씹어 으깨진 보트 조각들과, 찢긴 동료들의 가라앉는 사지 한가운데서, 고래의 무시무시한 진노가 빚어낸 흰 거품을 헤치고 빠져나와, 마치 탄생이나 혼례를 보고 웃기라도 하는 듯 미소 지으며 사람의 속을 뒤집어 놓는 저 고요한 햇살 속으로 나아갔을 때 말이다."]], [["His three boats stove around him, and oars and men both whirling in the eddies; one captain, seizing the line-knife from his broken prow, had dashed at the whale, as an Arkansas duellist at his foe, blindly seeking with a six inch blade to reach the fathom-deep life of the whale.", "그를 둘러싸고 세 척의 보트가 박살이 나고, 노와 사람이 모두 소용돌이 속에서 빙빙 도는 가운데, 한 선장이 부서진 뱃머리에서 밧줄칼을 움켜쥐고는, 아칸소의 결투자가 제 원수에게 달려들듯 고래에게 돌진하여, 여섯 인치짜리 칼날로 한 길 깊이에 자리한 고래의 생명을 맹목적으로 더듬어 찾으려 했다."], ["That captain was Ahab.", "그 선장이 바로 에이해브였다."], ["And then it was, that suddenly sweeping his sickle-shaped lower jaw beneath him, Moby Dick had reaped away Ahab’s leg, as a mower a blade of grass in the field.", "그리고 바로 그때, 모비 딕이 낫 모양의 아래턱을 그의 발밑으로 휙 쓸어 올려, 풀 베는 이가 들판의 풀잎을 베어 내듯 에이해브의 다리를 거두어 가 버렸다."], ["No turbaned Turk, no hired Venetian or Malay, could have smote him with more seeming malice.", "터번을 두른 어떤 튀르크인도, 돈에 팔린 어떤 베네치아인이나 말레이인도, 그보다 더 악의에 차 보이는 일격을 가하지는 못했으리라."], ["Small reason was there to doubt, then, that ever since that almost fatal encounter, Ahab had cherished a wild vindictiveness against the whale, all the more fell for that in his frantic morbidness he at last came to identify with him, not only all his bodily woes, but all his intellectual and spiritual exasperations.", "그렇다면 그 거의 치명적이었던 조우 이후로, 에이해브가 그 고래에 대해 거친 복수심을 품어 왔음을 의심할 까닭은 거의 없었으니, 그 복수심이 한층 더 사나워진 것은, 그가 광적인 병증 속에서 마침내 자신의 모든 육체적 고통뿐 아니라 지적이고 영적인 격분까지 모두 그 고래와 동일시하기에 이르렀기 때문이었다."], ["The White Whale swam before him as the monomaniac incarnation of all those malicious agencies which some deep men feel eating in them, till they are left living on with half a heart and half a lung.", "흰 고래는, 어떤 깊은 사람들이 제 안에서 갉아먹히는 것을 느끼다가 끝내 반쪽짜리 심장과 반쪽짜리 폐로 연명하게 만드는 그 모든 악의에 찬 작용의, 편집광적 화신(化身)으로서 그의 앞을 헤엄쳐 다녔다."], ["That intangible malignity which has been from the beginning; to whose dominion even the modern Christians ascribe one-half of the worlds; which the ancient Ophites of the east reverenced in their statue devil;—Ahab did not fall down and worship it like them; but deliriously transferring its idea to the abhorred white whale, he pitted himself, all mutilated, against it.", "태초부터 있어 온 그 손에 잡히지 않는 악의, 현대의 기독교인들조차 그 지배에 세상의 절반을 돌리고, 동방의 옛 오피스교도들이 그들의 악마 신상으로 떠받든 그 악의 — 에이해브는 그들처럼 그 앞에 엎드려 숭배하지는 않았으나, 그 관념을 가증스러운 흰 고래에게 미친 듯이 옮겨 놓고는, 사지가 잘린 몸으로 그것에 맞섰다."], ["All that most maddens and torments; all that stirs up the lees of things; all truth with malice in it; all that cracks the sinews and cakes the brain; all the subtle demonisms of life and thought; all evil, to crazy Ahab, were visibly personified, and made practically assailable in Moby Dick.", "사람을 가장 미치게 하고 괴롭히는 모든 것, 사물의 앙금을 휘저어 올리는 모든 것, 악의가 깃든 모든 진실, 힘줄을 끊고 뇌를 굳혀 버리는 모든 것, 삶과 사유의 온갖 교묘한 악마성, 모든 악이, 미쳐 버린 에이해브에게는 모비 딕 안에 눈에 보이게 인격화되어, 실제로 공격할 수 있는 것이 되었다."], ["He piled upon the whale’s white hump the sum of all the general rage and hate felt by his whole race from Adam down; and then, as if his chest had been a mortar, he burst his hot heart’s shell upon it.", "그는 아담 이래로 자기 종족 전체가 느껴 온 일반적 분노와 증오의 총합을 그 고래의 흰 혹 위에 쌓아 올렸다. 그러고는 마치 제 가슴이 박격포라도 되는 양, 그 위로 뜨거운 심장의 탄피를 터뜨렸다."]], [["It is not probable that this monomania in him took its instant rise at the precise time of his bodily dismemberment.", "그의 이 편집광이 정확히 그가 육체를 절단당한 그 순간에 곧바로 솟아났을 성싶지는 않다."], ["Then, in darting at the monster, knife in hand, he had but given loose to a sudden, passionate, corporal animosity; and when he received the stroke that tore him, he probably but felt the agonizing bodily laceration, but nothing more.", "그때, 칼을 손에 쥐고 그 괴물에게 돌진했을 때 그는 그저 갑작스럽고 격정적인 육체적 적의를 분출했을 뿐이며, 그를 찢어 놓은 그 일격을 받았을 때도 그는 아마 살을 에는 육체의 찢김만을 느꼈을 뿐, 그 이상의 것은 아니었으리라."], ["Yet, when by this collision forced to turn towards home, and for long months of days and weeks, Ahab and anguish lay stretched together in one hammock, rounding in mid winter that dreary, howling Patagonian Cape; then it was, that his torn body and gashed soul bled into one another; and so interfusing, made him mad.", "그러나 이 충돌로 말미암아 어쩔 수 없이 고향을 향해 뱃머리를 돌리고, 여러 달에 걸친 나날과 주(週)들 동안, 한겨울 저 황량하고 울부짖는 파타고니아의 곶을 돌아 나오면서 에이해브와 고뇌가 한 해먹 속에 나란히 누워 있었을 때, 바로 그때, 그의 찢긴 몸과 베인 영혼이 서로의 안으로 피를 흘려 보내며, 그렇게 한데 뒤섞여 그를 미치게 만들었다."], ["That it was only then, on the homeward voyage, after the encounter, that the final monomania seized him, seems all but certain from the fact that, at intervals during the passage, he was a raving lunatic; and, though unlimbed of a leg, yet such vital strength yet lurked in his Egyptian chest, and was moreover intensified by his delirium, that his mates were forced to lace him fast, even there, as he sailed, raving in his hammock.", "그 마지막 편집광이 그를 사로잡은 것이 오직 그때, 그 조우 이후 귀향 항해에서였다는 것은 거의 확실해 보인다. 항해 도중 띄엄띄엄 그는 발광하는 미치광이였으며, 비록 다리 하나를 잃었음에도 그의 이집트인 같은 가슴팍에는 여전히 무서운 활력이 도사리고 있었고, 게다가 그 활력이 광란으로 한층 거세진 까닭에, 항해하는 동안 해먹 속에서 미쳐 날뛰는 그를 항해사들이 거기다 단단히 동여매지 않을 수 없을 정도였다는 사실에서 그러하다."], ["In a strait-jacket, he swung to the mad rockings of the gales.", "구속복에 묶인 채 그는 광풍의 미친 흔들림에 따라 흔들렸다."], ["And, when running into more sufferable latitudes, the ship, with mild stun’sails spread, floated across the tranquil tropics, and, to all appearances, the old man’s delirium seemed left behind him with the Cape Horn swells, and he came forth from his dark den into the blessed light and air; even then, when he bore that firm, collected front, however pale, and issued his calm orders once again; and his mates thanked God the direful madness was now gone; even then, Ahab, in his hidden self, raved on.", "그러다 한결 견딜 만한 위도로 접어들어, 배가 부드러운 보조돛을 펼치고 고요한 열대 바다를 가로질러 떠가고, 겉보기에 노인의 광란은 케이프 혼의 너울과 함께 뒤에 남겨 두고 온 듯하여, 그가 어두운 굴에서 나와 축복받은 빛과 공기 속으로 걸어 나왔을 때 — 그때조차, 비록 창백하나마 그가 그 굳건하고 차분한 표정을 짓고 다시금 침착한 명령을 내렸을 때, 그리고 항해사들이 그 무시무시한 광기가 이제 사라졌다고 하느님께 감사드렸을 때 — 그때조차, 에이해브는 그 감추어진 자아 속에서 여전히 미쳐 날뛰고 있었다."], ["Human madness is oftentimes a cunning and most feline thing.", "인간의 광기란 흔히 교활하고 더없이 고양이 같은 것이다."], ["When you think it fled, it may have but become transfigured into some still subtler form.", "그것이 달아났다고 여길 때, 그것은 그저 한층 더 교묘한 형태로 모습을 바꾸었을 뿐일지도 모른다."], ["Ahab’s full lunacy subsided not, but deepeningly contracted; like the unabated Hudson, when that noble Northman flows narrowly, but unfathomably through the Highland gorge.", "에이해브의 온전한 광기는 가라앉은 것이 아니라, 깊어지며 좁혀들었으니, 마치 줄지 않고 흐르는 허드슨강이 저 고결한 북방의 물줄기가 되어 하이랜드 협곡을 비좁게, 그러나 헤아릴 수 없이 깊게 흐르는 것과 같았다."], ["But, as in his narrow-flowing monomania, not one jot of Ahab’s broad madness had been left behind; so in that broad madness, not one jot of his great natural intellect had perished.", "그러나 그 비좁게 흐르는 편집광 속에 에이해브의 드넓은 광기가 한 톨도 남김없이 담겨 있었듯이, 그 드넓은 광기 속에는 그의 위대한 천부적 지성이 한 톨도 사라지지 않고 그대로였다."], ["That before living agent, now became the living instrument.", "전에는 살아 있는 행위자였던 그것이, 이제는 살아 있는 도구가 되었다."], ["If such a furious trope may stand, his special lunacy stormed his general sanity, and carried it, and turned all its concentred cannon upon its own mad mark; so that far from having lost his strength, Ahab, to that one end, did now possess a thousand fold more potency than ever he had sanely brought to bear upon any one reasonable object.", "이런 격렬한 비유가 허락된다면, 그의 특수한 광기가 그의 일반적인 온전한 정신을 폭풍처럼 휩쓸어 점령하고는, 그 모든 응집된 대포를 제 자신의 미친 표적에 겨누어 발사했다고 하겠다. 그리하여 에이해브는 그 힘을 잃기는커녕, 오로지 그 한 가지 목적을 위하여, 온전한 정신으로 그 어떤 분별 있는 대상에 쏟아부었던 것보다 천 배는 더한 위력을 이제 갖추게 된 것이다."]], [["This is much; yet Ahab’s larger, darker, deeper part remains unhinted.", "이것만 해도 대단하다. 그러나 에이해브의 더 크고, 더 어둡고, 더 깊은 부분은 아직 암시조차 되지 않은 채로 남아 있다."], ["But vain to popularize profundities, and all truth is profound.", "그러나 심오한 것을 대중에게 쉽게 풀어 보이려는 것은 헛된 일이며, 모든 진실은 심오한 법이다. 우리가 지금 서 있는 이 첨탑 솟은 클뤼니 호텔의 바로 그 심장부에서 — 그것이 아무리 웅장하고 경이롭더라도, 이제 그곳을 떠나, 멀리 아래로 굽이굽이 내려가라."], ["Winding far down from within the very heart of this spiked Hotel de Cluny where we here stand—however grand and wonderful, now quit it;—and take your way, ye nobler, sadder souls, to those vast Roman halls of Thermes; where far beneath the fantastic towers of man’s upper earth, his root of grandeur, his whole awful essence sits in bearded state; an antique buried beneath antiquities, and throned on torsoes!", "그대들, 더 고귀하고 더 슬픈 영혼들이여, 저 광대한 로마의 테르메 대욕장으로 발길을 옮기라. 그곳, 인간의 위쪽 대지에 솟은 환상적인 탑들 저 깊은 아래, 그의 위대함의 뿌리, 그의 온전한 무시무시한 본질이 수염을 기른 위엄 속에 앉아 있나니, 옛것들 아래 묻힌 옛것이요, 토르소들 위에 옥좌를 차지하고 앉은 존재로다!"], ["So with a broken throne, the great gods mock that captive king; so like a Caryatid, he patient sits, upholding on his frozen brow the piled entablatures of ages.", "그렇게, 부서진 옥좌와 함께, 위대한 신들은 그 사로잡힌 왕을 조롱한다. 그렇게 카리아티드처럼, 그는 인내하며 앉아, 그 얼어붙은 이마 위에 쌓인 시대의 들보들을 떠받치고 있다."], ["Wind ye down there, ye prouder, sadder souls!", "그곳으로 굽이굽이 내려가라, 그대들 더 자랑스럽고 더 슬픈 영혼들이여!"], ["question that proud, sad king!", "저 자랑스럽고 슬픈 왕에게 물으라!"], ["A family likeness!", "가문의 닮은꼴이라!"], ["aye, he did beget ye, ye young exiled royalties; and from your grim sire only will the old State-secret come.", "그렇다, 그가 그대들을 낳았다, 그대들 추방당한 어린 왕족들이여. 그리고 그대들의 그 험상궂은 아비에게서만, 저 오래된 국가의 비밀이 흘러나오리라."]], [["Now, in his heart, Ahab had some glimpse of this, namely: all my means are sane, my motive and my object mad.", "자, 에이해브는 마음속으로 이것을 어렴풋이 엿보고 있었으니, 곧 나의 모든 수단은 온전하나, 나의 동기와 나의 목적은 미쳐 있다는 것이었다."], ["Yet without power to kill, or change, or shun the fact; he likewise knew that to mankind he did long dissemble; in some sort, did still.", "그러나 그 사실을 죽이거나, 바꾸거나, 피할 힘이 없었으니, 그는 또한 자신이 인간들에게 오래도록 거짓을 꾸며 왔으며, 어떤 면에서는 지금도 여전히 그러하다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["But that thing of his dissembling was only subject to his perceptibility, not to his will determinate.", "그러나 그가 꾸며 내는 그 가식은 다만 그의 자각에만 매여 있었을 뿐, 그의 결연한 의지에 매여 있는 것은 아니었다."], ["Nevertheless, so well did he succeed in that dissembling, that when with ivory leg he stepped ashore at last, no Nantucketer thought him otherwise than but naturally grieved, and that to the quick, with the terrible casualty which had overtaken him.", "그럼에도 그는 그 가식에 어찌나 능했던지, 마침내 상아 다리로 뭍에 발을 디뎠을 때, 어떤 낸터킷 사람도 그를, 자신을 덮친 그 끔찍한 재난으로 자연스레, 그것도 뼈저리게 슬퍼하는 사람으로밖에는 달리 여기지 않았다."]], [["The report of his undeniable delirium at sea was likewise popularly ascribed to a kindred cause.", "바다에서 그가 부정할 수 없이 광란했다는 소문 또한 사람들에게는 그와 같은 부류의 원인으로 돌려졌다."], ["And so too, all the added moodiness which always afterwards, to the very day of sailing in the Pequod on the present voyage, sat brooding on his brow.", "그리고 그 후로 줄곧, 이번 항해를 위해 피쿼드호로 출항하던 바로 그날까지 그의 이마에 깊이 자리 잡고 있던 그 한층 더해진 침울함 또한 마찬가지였다."], ["Nor is it so very unlikely, that far from distrusting his fitness for another whaling voyage, on account of such dark symptoms, the calculating people of that prudent isle were inclined to harbor the conceit, that for those very reasons he was all the better qualified and set on edge, for a pursuit so full of rage and wildness as the bloody hunt of whales.", "또한, 그토록 어두운 징후들 때문에 그가 또 한 번의 고래잡이 항해에 적합한지를 미심쩍게 여기기는커녕, 그 신중한 섬의 타산적인 사람들이 오히려 이런 생각을 품기 시작했다는 것도 그리 있을 법하지 않은 일은 아니다. 즉, 바로 그런 이유들 때문에 그가 피비린내 나는 고래 사냥처럼 분노와 광기로 가득한 추격에 한층 더 적합하고 한껏 날이 서 있다는 생각 말이다."], ["Gnawed within and scorched without, with the infixed, unrelenting fangs of some incurable idea; such an one, could he be found, would seem the very man to dart his iron and lift his lance against the most appalling of all brutes.", "어떤 불치의 관념이 박아 넣은 그 가차 없는 송곳니에 안으로는 갉아먹히고 밖으로는 그을린 자, 만약 그런 자를 찾아낼 수 있다면, 그야말로 온갖 짐승 가운데 가장 소름 끼치는 놈에게 작살을 던지고 창을 치켜들 적임자로 보일 것이었다."], ["Or, if for any reason thought to be corporeally incapacitated for that, yet such an one would seem superlatively competent to cheer and howl on his underlings to the attack.", "혹은 어떤 이유로든 그가 육체적으로 그 일에 부적합하다고 여겨진다 해도, 그런 자야말로 부하들을 격려하고 함성으로 부추겨 공격에 나서게 하는 데는 더없이 뛰어난 적임자로 보일 것이었다."], ["But be all this as it may, certain it is, that with the mad secret of his unabated rage bolted up and keyed in him, Ahab had purposely sailed upon the present voyage with the one only and all-engrossing object of hunting the White Whale.", "그러나 이 모든 것이 어찌 되었든, 분명한 것은, 사그라지지 않은 그 분노의 미친 비밀을 제 안에 빗장 질러 단단히 잠가 둔 채로, 에이해브가 오직 흰 고래를 사냥한다는 그 단 하나의, 모든 것을 집어삼키는 목적을 품고 이번 항해에 일부러 나섰다는 사실이다."], ["Had any one of his old acquaintances on shore but half dreamed of what was lurking in him then, how soon would their aghast and righteous souls have wrenched the ship from such a fiendish man!", "뭍에 있는 그의 옛 지인들 가운데 누구라도 그 안에 도사리고 있던 것을 절반이나마 어렴풋이 꿈꿔 보았더라면, 그들의 경악하고 의로운 영혼이 얼마나 재빨리 그토록 악마 같은 사내의 손에서 그 배를 비틀어 빼앗았겠는가!"], ["They were bent on profitable cruises, the profit to be counted down in dollars from the mint.", "그들은 돈벌이가 되는 항해에 마음을 두고 있었으니, 그 이문은 조폐국에서 찍어 낸 달러로 헤아릴 수 있는 것이었다."], ["He was intent on an audacious, immitigable, and supernatural revenge.", "그러나 그는 대담하고, 누그러뜨릴 수 없으며, 초자연적인 복수에 골몰해 있었다."]], [["Here, then, was this grey-headed, ungodly old man, chasing with curses a Job’s whale round the world, at the head of a crew, too, chiefly made up of mongrel renegades, and castaways, and cannibals—morally enfeebled also, by the incompetence of mere unaided virtue or right-mindedness in Starbuck, the invulnerable jollity of indifference and recklessness in Stubb, and the pervading mediocrity in Flask.", "그러니 여기, 머리가 희끗희끗한 이 불경한 노인이, 욥의 고래를 저주를 퍼부으며 온 세상을 돌며 쫓고 있었으니, 게다가 그가 거느린 선원들이라는 것도 대개는 잡종 변절자와 표류자와 식인종들로 이루어진 무리였다. 도덕적으로도 약화된 무리였으니, 스타벅에게는 그저 도움받지 못한 미덕이나 올바른 마음만으로는 어쩔 수 없는 무능이 있었고, 스터브에게는 무관심과 무모함이 빚어내는 그 어떤 것에도 끄떡없는 쾌활함이 있었으며, 플래스크에게는 사방에 배어든 범용함이 있었다."], ["Such a crew, so officered, seemed specially picked and packed by some infernal fatality to help him to his monomaniac revenge.", "그렇게 짜인 선원들과 그렇게 임명된 항해사들은, 마치 어떤 지옥의 운명이 그의 편집광적 복수를 거들도록 특별히 골라 한데 묶어 놓은 것만 같았다."], ["How it was that they so aboundingly responded to the old man’s ire—by what evil magic their souls were possessed, that at times his hate seemed almost theirs; the White Whale as much their insufferable foe as his; how all this came to be—what the White Whale was to them, or how to their unconscious understandings, also, in some dim, unsuspected way, he might have seemed the gliding great demon of the seas of life,—all this to explain, would be to dive deeper than Ishmael can go.", "그들이 어찌하여 그토록 넘치게 노인의 분노에 호응했는지 — 어떤 사악한 마법에 그들의 영혼이 사로잡혔기에, 때로 그의 증오가 거의 그들 자신의 것처럼 보였고, 흰 고래가 그에게만큼이나 그들에게도 견딜 수 없는 원수처럼 보였는지, 이 모든 것이 어떻게 그리되었는지 — 흰 고래가 그들에게 무엇이었는지, 혹은 그들의 무의식적인 이해 속에서도, 어떤 어렴풋하고 미처 짐작지 못한 방식으로, 그가 삶의 바다를 미끄러져 다니는 거대한 악마처럼 보였을지도 모른다는 것을, 이 모든 것을 설명하려는 것은 이슈메일이 잠수할 수 있는 것보다 더 깊이 잠수하는 일일 터이다."], ["The subterranean miner that works in us all, how can one tell whither leads his shaft by the ever shifting, muffled sound of his pick?", "우리 모두의 안에서 일하는 저 지하의 광부, 끊임없이 자리를 옮기는 그 곡괭이의 둔탁한 소리만으로 그의 갱도가 어디로 이어지는지를 누가 알 수 있겠는가?"], ["Who does not feel the irresistible arm drag?", "그 거역할 수 없는 팔의 끌어당김을 느끼지 못할 자가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["What skiff in tow of a seventy-four can stand still?", "칠십사 문(門) 군함에 끌려가는 작은 거룻배가 어떻게 가만히 멈춰 있을 수 있겠는가?"], ["For one, I gave myself up to the abandonment of the time and the place; but while yet all a-rush to encounter the whale, could see naught in that brute but the deadliest ill.", "나로 말하자면, 나는 그 시간과 장소의 방기(放棄) 속에 나 자신을 내맡겨 버렸다. 그러나 온통 고래와 맞붙으러 내달리면서도, 그 짐승에게서 더없이 치명적인 악(惡) 말고는 아무것도 볼 수가 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 42. The Whiteness of the Whale.", "# CHAPTER 42. The Whiteness of the Whale."]], [["What the white whale was to Ahab, has been hinted; what, at times, he was to me, as yet remains unsaid.", "흰 고래가 에이해브(Ahab)에게 무엇이었는지는 이미 넌지시 비쳐 두었다. 하지만 그것이 때때로 나에게 무엇이었는지는 아직 말하지 않은 채로 남아 있다."]], [["Aside from those more obvious considerations touching Moby Dick, which could not but occasionally awaken in any man’s soul some alarm, there was another thought, or rather vague, nameless horror concerning him, which at times by its intensity completely overpowered all the rest; and yet so mystical and well nigh ineffable was it, that I almost despair of putting it in a comprehensible form.", "모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 둘러싼, 누구의 영혼에든 이따금 어떤 경계심을 불러일으키지 않을 수 없는 저 보다 명백한 사정들은 차치하더라도, 그 고래와 관련하여 또 하나의 생각이, 아니 그보다는 막연하고 이름조차 붙일 수 없는 공포가 있었으니, 그것은 때때로 그 강렬함으로 다른 모든 것을 완전히 압도해 버리곤 했다. 그러면서도 그것은 너무도 신비롭고 거의 형언할 수 없는 것이어서, 나는 그것을 이해할 만한 형태로 옮겨 놓는 일에 거의 절망하고 만다."], ["It was the whiteness of the whale that above all things appalled me.", "무엇보다도 나를 소름 끼치게 한 것은 그 고래의 흰빛이었다. 그러나 여기서 내가 어떻게 나 자신을 설명할 수 있기를 바랄 수 있겠는가."], ["But how can I hope to explain myself here; and yet, in some dim, random way, explain myself I must, else all these chapters might be naught.", "그럼에도 어슴푸레하고 두서없는 방식으로나마, 나는 기어이 나 자신을 설명해 내야만 한다. 그러지 않는다면 이 모든 장(章)이 한낱 헛것이 되고 말 테니까."]], [["Though in many natural objects, whiteness refiningly enhances beauty, as if imparting some special virtue of its own, as in marbles, japonicas, and pearls; and though various nations have in some way recognised a certain royal preeminence in this hue; even the barbaric, grand old kings of Pegu placing the title “Lord of the White Elephants” above all their other magniloquent ascriptions of dominion; and the modern kings of Siam unfurling the same snow-white quadruped in the royal standard; and the Hanoverian flag bearing the one figure of a snow-white charger; and the great Austrian Empire, Cæsarian, heir to overlording Rome, having for the imperial colour the same imperial hue; and though this pre-eminence in it applies to the human race itself, giving the white man ideal mastership over every dusky tribe; and though, besides, all this, whiteness has been even made significant of gladness, for among the Romans a white stone marked a joyful day; and though in other mortal sympathies and symbolizings, this same hue is made the emblem of many touching, noble things—the innocence of brides, the benignity of age; though among the Red Men of America the giving of the white belt of wampum was the deepest pledge of honor; though in many climes, whiteness typifies the majesty of Justice in the ermine of the Judge, and contributes to the daily state of kings and queens drawn by milk-white steeds; though even in the higher mysteries of the most august religions it has been made the symbol of the divine spotlessness and power; by the Persian fire worshippers, the white forked flame being held the holiest on the altar; and in the Greek mythologies, Great Jove himself being made incarnate in a snow-white bull; and though to the noble Iroquois, the midwinter sacrifice of the sacred White Dog was by far the holiest festival of their theology, that spotless, faithful creature being held the purest envoy they could send to the Great Spirit with the annual tidings of their own fidelity; and though directly from the Latin word for white, all Christian priests derive the name of one part of their sacred vesture, the alb or tunic, worn beneath the cassock; and though among the holy pomps of the Romish faith, white is specially employed in the celebration of the Passion of our Lord; though in the Vision of St. John, white robes are given to the redeemed, and the four-and-twenty elders stand clothed in white before the great white throne, and the Holy One that sitteth there white like wool; yet for all these accumulated associations, with whatever is sweet, and honorable, and sublime, there yet lurks an elusive something in the innermost idea of this hue, which strikes more of panic to the soul than that redness which affrights in blood.", "비록 수많은 자연의 사물들에서 흰빛이 마치 제 고유의 어떤 특별한 미덕을 부여하기라도 하듯 아름다움을 정련하여 한층 돋보이게 하는 것이 사실이고, 대리석과 동백꽃과 진주가 그러하며, 또한 여러 민족이 어떤 식으로든 이 빛깔 속에서 일종의 제왕적 탁월함을 인정해 온 것도 사실이어서, 저 야만스럽고 위대한 페구의 옛 왕들조차 \"흰 코끼리의 군주\"라는 칭호를 자신들의 온갖 호언장담하는 지배의 칭호들 위에 두었고, 현대 시암의 왕들 또한 그 눈처럼 흰 네발짐승을 왕실의 깃발에 펼쳐 내걸었으며, 하노버의 깃발에도 눈처럼 흰 군마(軍馬) 한 마리가 그려져 있고, 군림하던 로마의 상속자인 저 위대한 오스트리아 제국, 카이사르의 후예인 그 제국 역시 황제의 빛깔로 바로 그 제왕의 색조를 삼았으며, 또한 이 색의 탁월함은 인류 자신에게도 적용되어 백인에게 모든 거무스름한 종족 위에 군림하는 이상적 지배권을 부여하는가 하면, 이 모든 것에 더하여 흰빛은 심지어 기쁨의 표상이 되기도 하여 로마인들 사이에서는 흰 돌이 즐거운 날을 표시했고, 또한 인간의 다른 여러 정서와 상징 속에서도 이 같은 빛깔이 수많은 애틋하고 고결한 것들의 표상으로 — 신부의 순결과 노년의 자애로움의 표상으로 — 쓰였으며, 아메리카의 붉은 사람들 사이에서는 흰 조가비 띠를 건네는 것이 가장 깊은 명예의 서약이었고, 또한 많은 풍토에서 흰빛은 재판관의 흰담비 모피 속에 깃든 정의의 위엄을 상징하며 젖빛처럼 흰 준마들이 끄는 왕과 왕비의 나날의 위의(威儀)에 한몫을 보태고, 심지어 가장 존엄한 종교들의 더 높은 신비 속에서도 그것은 신성한 무결함과 권능의 상징이 되어, 페르시아의 불 숭배자들에게는 흰빛으로 갈라진 불꽃이 제단 위에서 가장 거룩한 것으로 여겨졌으며, 그리스 신화에서는 위대한 제우스 신마저 눈처럼 흰 황소로 화신(化身)했고, 또한 고결한 이로쿼이 족에게는 한겨울에 신성한 흰 개를 제물로 바치는 의식이 그들 신학에서 단연 가장 거룩한 축제였으니, 그 티 없고 충직한 짐승이야말로 자신들의 충성을 알리는 해마다의 소식과 더불어 위대한 정령에게 보낼 수 있는 가장 순결한 사자(使者)로 여겨졌으며, 또한 모든 기독교 사제는 흰빛을 뜻하는 라틴어 단어에서 직접 그들 성스러운 제의의 한 부분, 곧 검은 사제복 아래 입는 흰 장백의(長白衣)의 이름을 따왔고, 로마 가톨릭 신앙의 거룩한 장엄함 속에서도 흰빛은 특별히 우리 주님의 수난을 기리는 데 쓰이며, 성 요한의 묵시 속에서는 구원받은 자들에게 흰 옷이 주어지고 스물네 명의 장로가 위대한 흰 보좌 앞에 흰 옷을 입고 서 있으며 거기 앉으신 거룩한 분은 양털처럼 희다고 하였으되 — 이 모든 누적된 연상에도, 곧 달콤하고 명예롭고 숭고한 그 무엇과 결부되어 있음에도, 이 빛깔의 가장 깊숙한 관념 속에는 여전히 붙잡기 힘든 그 무엇이 도사리고 있어, 피 속의 그 붉음이 자아내는 공포보다도 더한 공황(恐慌)을 영혼에 들이친다."]], [["This elusive quality it is, which causes the thought of whiteness, when divorced from more kindly associations, and coupled with any object terrible in itself, to heighten that terror to the furthest bounds.", "바로 이 붙잡기 힘든 성질이야말로, 흰빛에 대한 생각이 보다 다정한 연상들에서 떨어져 나와 그 자체로 끔찍한 어떤 대상과 결합될 때, 그 공포를 극한의 경계까지 치솟게 하는 원인이다."], ["Witness the white bear of the poles, and the white shark of the tropics; what but their smooth, flaky whiteness makes them the transcendent horrors they are?", "극지의 흰곰과 열대의 흰 상어를 보라. 그 매끄럽고 비늘 같은 흰빛이 아니라면 무엇이 그것들을 그토록 초월적인 공포로 만들겠는가?"], ["That ghastly whiteness it is which imparts such an abhorrent mildness, even more loathsome than terrific, to the dumb gloating of their aspect.", "그 소름 끼치는 흰빛이야말로, 무언의 음흉한 표정에 그토록 혐오스러운 온화함을 — 무시무시함보다도 더 역겨운 그 온화함을 — 부여하는 것이다."], ["So that not the fierce-fanged tiger in his heraldic coat can so stagger courage as the white-shrouded bear or shark.*", "그리하여 문장(紋章)의 외피를 두른 사나운 송곳니의 호랑이조차도, 흰 수의를 두른 곰이나 상어만큼 용기를 휘청거리게 만들지는 못한다.*"]], [["*With reference to the Polar bear, it may possibly be urged by him who would fain go still deeper into this matter, that it is not the whiteness, separately regarded, which heightens the intolerable hideousness of that brute; for, analysed, that heightened hideousness, it might be said, only rises from the circumstance, that the irresponsible ferociousness of the creature stands invested in the fleece of celestial innocence and love; and hence, by bringing together two such opposite emotions in our minds, the Polar bear frightens us with so unnatural a contrast. But even assuming all this to be true; yet, were it not for the whiteness, you would not have that intensified terror.", "* 북극곰에 관하여, 이 문제를 더 깊이 파고들고자 하는 이는 어쩌면 이렇게 주장할지도 모른다. 곧, 그 짐승의 견딜 수 없는 흉측함을 한층 더 돋우는 것은 따로 떼어 본 흰빛 그 자체가 아니라는 것이다. 분석해 보면, 그 가중된 흉측함은 다만 다음과 같은 사정에서 비롯될 뿐이라고 말할 수 있으리라. 곧 그 짐승의 책임 없는 흉포함이 천상의 무구함과 사랑의 양털을 두르고 있다는 사정, 그리하여 우리 마음속에 그토록 상반된 두 감정을 한데 끌어모음으로써 북극곰이 그토록 부자연스러운 대비로 우리를 두렵게 한다는 것이다. 그러나 이 모든 것이 사실이라 치더라도, 흰빛이 아니었던들 그 강화된 공포는 결코 없었을 것이다."]], [["As for the white shark, the white gliding ghostliness of repose in that creature, when beheld in his ordinary moods, strangely tallies with the same quality in the Polar quadruped.", "흰 상어로 말하자면, 그 짐승이 평소의 기분으로 있을 때 보이는 그 희고 미끄러지는 유령 같은 정적은 묘하게도 극지의 네발짐승이 지닌 그 같은 성질과 들어맞는다."], ["This peculiarity is most vividly hit by the French in the name they bestow upon that fish.", "이 독특함을 가장 생생하게 짚어 낸 것은 프랑스인들이 그 물고기에게 붙인 이름이다."], ["The Romish mass for the dead begins with “Requiem eternam” (eternal rest), whence Requiem denominating the mass itself, and any other funeral music.", "로마 가톨릭의 죽은 자를 위한 미사는 \"레퀴엠 에테르남(영원한 안식)\"으로 시작되며, 거기서 레퀴엠이라는 말이 미사 자체와 온갖 장례 음악을 가리키게 되었다."], ["Now, in allusion to the white, silent stillness of death in this shark, and the mild deadliness of his habits, the French call him Requin.", "그런데 이 상어가 지닌 죽음의 희고 고요한 정적과 그 온화한 치명성을 빗대어, 프랑스인들은 그것을 르캥(Requin)이라 부른다."]], [["Bethink thee of the albatross, whence come those clouds of spiritual wonderment and pale dread, in which that white phantom sails in all imaginations?", "신천옹을 떠올려 보라. 모든 상상 속에서 그 흰 유령이 헤쳐 나아가는 저 영적 경이와 창백한 두려움의 구름은 어디서 오는 것인가?"], ["Not Coleridge first threw that spell; but God’s great, unflattering laureate, Nature.*", "그 주문을 처음 던진 것은 콜리지가 아니다. 그것은 하느님의 위대하고 아첨할 줄 모르는 계관시인, 곧 자연이었다.*"]], [["*I remember the first albatross I ever saw.", "* 내가 난생처음 본 신천옹을 나는 기억한다."], ["It was during a prolonged gale, in waters hard upon the Antarctic seas.", "그것은 남극해에 바싹 닿은 바다에서, 한차례 오래 이어진 폭풍이 부는 동안의 일이었다."], ["From my forenoon watch below, I ascended to the overclouded deck; and there, dashed upon the main hatches, I saw a regal, feathery thing of unspotted whiteness, and with a hooked, Roman bill sublime.", "오전 비번을 마치고 아래에서 올라와 구름이 잔뜩 낀 갑판에 발을 디뎠는데, 거기 주 승강구 위에 내동댕이쳐진 채로, 티 한 점 없는 흰빛의 제왕다운, 깃털 달린 존재가 보였다. 그것은 갈고리처럼 굽은, 숭고한 로마식 부리를 지니고 있었다."], ["At intervals, it arched forth its vast archangel wings, as if to embrace some holy ark.", "이따금 그것은 마치 어떤 거룩한 방주를 끌어안기라도 하려는 듯, 그 광대한 대천사의 날개를 활짝 펼쳤다."], ["Wondrous flutterings and throbbings shook it.", "경이로운 파닥임과 떨림이 그것을 뒤흔들었다."], ["Though bodily unharmed, it uttered cries, as some king’s ghost in supernatural distress.", "몸은 다친 데 없었으나, 그것은 초자연적 고뇌에 빠진 어느 왕의 유령처럼 울음소리를 냈다."], ["Through its inexpressible, strange eyes, methought I peeped to secrets which took hold of God.", "그 형언할 수 없는 기이한 눈을 통해, 나는 하느님을 사로잡은 비밀들을 엿보는 듯한 느낌을 받았다."], ["As Abraham before the angels, I bowed myself; the white thing was so white, its wings so wide, and in those for ever exiled waters, I had lost the miserable warping memories of traditions and of towns.", "천사들 앞에 선 아브라함처럼, 나는 머리를 조아렸다. 그 흰 것은 너무도 희었고, 그 날개는 너무도 넓었으며, 영영 추방된 그 바다 위에서 나는 전통과 도시들에 얽힌 저 비참하게 뒤틀린 기억들을 잃어버렸다."], ["Long I gazed at that prodigy of plumage.", "나는 오래도록 그 깃털의 경이를 바라보았다."], ["I cannot tell, can only hint, the things that darted through me then.", "그때 내 안을 꿰뚫고 지나간 것들을 나는 말로 다 할 수 없으며, 다만 넌지시 비칠 수 있을 뿐이다."], ["But at last I awoke; and turning, asked a sailor what bird was this.", "그러나 마침내 나는 정신을 차렸고, 돌아서서 한 선원에게 이것이 무슨 새냐고 물었다."], ["A goney, he replied.", "고니(goney)요, 하고 그가 대답했다."], ["Goney!", "고니라니!"], ["never had heard that name before; is it conceivable that this glorious thing is utterly unknown to men ashore!", "그런 이름은 그때껏 들어 본 적이 없었다. 이토록 영광스러운 존재가 뭍의 사람들에게 전혀 알려져 있지 않다는 것이 과연 있을 수 있는 일인가!"], ["never! But some time after, I learned that goney was some seaman’s name for albatross.", "결코 그럴 수 없다!"], ["So that by no possibility could Coleridge’s wild Rhyme have had aught to do with those mystical impressions which were mine, when I saw that bird upon our deck.", "그러나 얼마 뒤에야 나는 고니가 신천옹을 가리키는 어느 뱃사람의 이름임을 알게 되었다. 그러니 우리 갑판 위에서 그 새를 보았을 때 내가 느낀 저 신비로운 인상들과 콜리지의 격렬한 운율시가 무슨 관계가 있었을 리는 도저히 없는 것이다."], ["For neither had I then read the Rhyme, nor knew the bird to be an albatross.", "그때 나는 그 시를 읽은 적도 없었고, 그 새가 신천옹임을 알지도 못했으니까."], ["Yet, in saying this, I do but indirectly burnish a little brighter the noble merit of the poem and the poet.", "그러나 이렇게 말함으로써 나는 다만 그 시와 그 시인의 고귀한 공적을 에둘러 조금 더 환히 빛내 줄 따름이다."]], [["I assert, then, that in the wondrous bodily whiteness of the bird chiefly lurks the secret of the spell; a truth the more evinced in this, that by a solecism of terms there are birds called grey albatrosses; and these I have frequently seen, but never with such emotions as when I beheld the Antarctic fowl.", "그렇다면 나는 단언하노니, 그 주문의 비밀은 무엇보다도 그 새의 경이로운 육신의 흰빛 속에 도사리고 있다. 이 진실은, 어법의 모순으로 회색 신천옹이라 불리는 새들이 있다는 점에서 한층 더 분명하게 드러난다. 나는 이런 새들을 여러 번 보았으나, 저 남극의 새를 보았을 때와 같은 감정을 느낀 적은 한 번도 없었다."]], [["But how had the mystic thing been caught?", "그런데 그 신비로운 것은 어떻게 잡혔던가?"], ["Whisper it not, and I will tell; with a treacherous hook and line, as the fowl floated on the sea.", "소리 죽여 말할 테니 누구에게도 발설하지 말라. 그것은 새가 바다 위에 떠 있을 때 음흉한 낚싯바늘과 낚싯줄로 잡혔다."], ["At last the Captain made a postman of it; tying a lettered, leathern tally round its neck, with the ship’s time and place; and then letting it escape.", "마침내 선장은 그것을 우편배달부로 삼아, 가죽에 글자를 새긴 표찰에 배의 날짜와 위치를 적어 그 목에 매달고는 놓아주었다. 그러나 나는 의심하지 않는다."], ["But I doubt not, that leathern tally, meant for man, was taken off in Heaven, when the white fowl flew to join the wing-folding, the invoking, and adoring cherubim!", "사람을 위한 그 가죽 표찰은, 그 흰 새가 날개를 접고 기도하고 흠숭하는 천사들 무리에 합류하러 날아갔을 때, 천국에서 떼어졌으리라는 것을!"]], [["Most famous in our Western annals and Indian traditions is that of the White Steed of the Prairies; a magnificent milk-white charger, large-eyed, small-headed, bluff-chested, and with the dignity of a thousand monarchs in his lofty, overscorning carriage.", "우리 서부의 연대기와 인디언 전설 가운데 가장 유명한 것은 대초원의 백마(白馬) 이야기다. 젖빛처럼 흰 그 장려한 군마는 눈이 크고 머리는 작으며 가슴은 떡 벌어졌고, 그 드높고 모든 것을 깔보는 듯한 거동에는 천 명의 군주가 지닌 위엄이 서려 있었다."], ["He was the elected Xerxes of vast herds of wild horses, whose pastures in those days were only fenced by the Rocky Mountains and the Alleghanies.", "그것은 광대한 야생마 무리의 선택된 크세르크세스였으니, 그 시절 그 무리의 목초지는 로키 산맥과 앨러게이니 산맥만이 울타리를 쳐 줄 뿐이었다."], ["At their flaming head he westward trooped it like that chosen star which every evening leads on the hosts of light.", "그것은 그 불타는 듯한 무리의 선두에 서서, 매일 저녁 빛의 군대를 이끄는 저 선택된 별처럼 서쪽으로 무리 지어 나아갔다."], ["The flashing cascade of his mane, the curving comet of his tail, invested him with housings more resplendent than gold and silver-beaters could have furnished him.", "번뜩이며 쏟아지는 폭포 같은 갈기와 곡선을 그리는 혜성 같은 꼬리는, 금은 세공장이가 마련해 줄 수 있는 그 어떤 마구보다도 더 찬란한 장식을 그것에 둘러 주었다."], ["A most imperial and archangelical apparition of that unfallen, western world, which to the eyes of the old trappers and hunters revived the glories of those primeval times when Adam walked majestic as a god, bluff-browed and fearless as this mighty steed.", "타락하지 않은 그 서부 세계의, 더없이 제왕적이고 대천사 같은 환영(幻影)이었으니, 늙은 덫 사냥꾼들과 수렵꾼들의 눈에는 아담이 신처럼 위엄 있게, 이 강대한 군마처럼 떡 벌어진 이마에 두려움 없이 거닐던 저 태곳적의 영광을 되살려 주는 것이었다."], ["Whether marching amid his aides and marshals in the van of countless cohorts that endlessly streamed it over the plains, like an Ohio; or whether with his circumambient subjects browsing all around at the horizon, the White Steed gallopingly reviewed them with warm nostrils reddening through his cool milkiness; in whatever aspect he presented himself, always to the bravest Indians he was the object of trembling reverence and awe.", "오하이오 강처럼 끝없이 평원 위로 흘러내리는 무수한 부대의 선봉에서 부관들과 원수들 사이를 행진하든, 혹은 지평선 둘레에서 풀을 뜯는 신민들에게 에워싸인 채 백마가 그 서늘한 젖빛을 뚫고 발그레해지는 따뜻한 콧김을 내뿜으며 그들을 질주하듯 사열하든, 어떤 모습으로 나타나든 간에 그것은 언제나 가장 용감한 인디언들에게조차 떨리는 경외와 외경의 대상이었다."], ["Nor can it be questioned from what stands on legendary record of this noble horse, that it was his spiritual whiteness chiefly, which so clothed him with divineness; and that this divineness had that in it which, though commanding worship, at the same time enforced a certain nameless terror.", "그리고 이 고귀한 말에 관해 전설로 전해 내려오는 기록을 보건대, 그것에 신성함을 그토록 입혀 준 것이 무엇보다도 그 영적인 흰빛이었으며, 또한 이 신성함 속에는 흠숭을 자아내는 동시에 어떤 이름 없는 공포를 강요하는 그 무엇이 깃들어 있었음은 의심할 여지가 없다."]], [["But there are other instances where this whiteness loses all that accessory and strange glory which invests it in the White Steed and Albatross.", "그러나 이 흰빛이, 백마와 신천옹을 감싸던 저 부수적이고 기이한 영광을 모조리 잃어버리는 다른 사례들도 있다."]], [["What is it that in the Albino man so peculiarly repels and often shocks the eye, as that sometimes he is loathed by his own kith and kin!", "백색증 환자에게서 흰빛이 그토록 유난히 눈을 거슬리게 하고 종종 충격을 주어, 때로는 제 살붙이와 핏줄에게마저 혐오를 사게 하는 까닭은 무엇인가!"], ["It is that whiteness which invests him, a thing expressed by the name he bears.", "그것은 그를 감싸고 있는 저 흰빛이니, 그가 지닌 이름 자체가 그것을 말해 준다."], ["The Albino is as well made as other men—has no substantive deformity—and yet this mere aspect of all-pervading whiteness makes him more strangely hideous than the ugliest abortion.", "백색증 환자도 여느 사람만큼 잘 빚어진 몸을 지녔고 — 실질적인 기형이라곤 없는데도 — 그럼에도 온몸에 퍼진 흰빛이라는 이 단순한 외양 하나가 가장 추한 기형아보다도 그를 더 기이하게 흉측해 보이게 만든다."], ["Why should this be so?", "어찌하여 그러한가?"]], [["Nor, in quite other aspects, does Nature in her least palpable but not the less malicious agencies, fail to enlist among her forces this crowning attribute of the terrible.", "또한 전혀 다른 측면에서, 자연은 그 가장 손에 잡히지 않으면서도 결코 덜 악의적이지는 않은 작용들 속에서, 이 무시무시함의 으뜸가는 속성을 제 군대에 끌어들이기를 빠뜨리지 않는다."], ["From its snowy aspect, the gauntleted ghost of the Southern Seas has been denominated the White Squall.", "그 눈처럼 흰 모습 때문에, 남쪽 바다의 철갑을 두른 유령은 '하얀 돌풍'이라 이름 붙여졌다."], ["Nor, in some historic instances, has the art of human malice omitted so potent an auxiliary.", "또한 몇몇 역사적 사례에서, 인간 악의의 술책 역시 이토록 강력한 조력자를 빠뜨리지 않았다."], ["How wildly it heightens the effect of that passage in Froissart, when, masked in the snowy symbol of their faction, the desperate White Hoods of Ghent murder their bailiff in the market-place!", "프루아사르의 그 대목, 곧 헨트의 절망에 찬 '하얀 두건단'이 자기네 파벌의 눈처럼 흰 상징으로 얼굴을 가린 채 시장 한복판에서 그들의 행정관을 살해하는 그 장면이 그 효과를 얼마나 격렬하게 드높이는가!"]], [["Nor, in some things, does the common, hereditary experience of all mankind fail to bear witness to the supernaturalism of this hue.", "또한 어떤 것들에서는, 온 인류가 공통으로 물려받은 경험 역시 이 빛깔의 초자연성을 증언하기를 빠뜨리지 않는다."], ["It cannot well be doubted, that the one visible quality in the aspect of the dead which most appals the gazer, is the marble pallor lingering there; as if indeed that pallor were as much like the badge of consternation in the other world, as of mortal trepidation here.", "죽은 자의 모습에서 보는 이를 가장 소름 끼치게 하는 단 하나의 눈에 보이는 성질이 거기 머무는 대리석 같은 창백함이라는 것은 좀처럼 의심할 수 없으니, 마치 그 창백함이 이 세상에서의 죽을 운명의 전율을 나타내는 만큼이나 저세상에서의 경악의 표지(標識)이기라도 한 듯하다."], ["And from that pallor of the dead, we borrow the expressive hue of the shroud in which we wrap them.", "그리고 죽은 자의 그 창백함에서, 우리는 그들을 감싸는 수의의 저 표현력 짙은 빛깔을 빌려 온다."], ["Nor even in our superstitions do we fail to throw the same snowy mantle round our phantoms; all ghosts rising in a milk-white fog—Yea, while these terrors seize us, let us add, that even the king of terrors, when personified by the evangelist, rides on his pallid horse.", "심지어 우리의 미신 속에서도 우리는 우리의 유령들에 그 같은 눈빛의 망토를 둘러 주기를 빠뜨리지 않으니, 모든 망령은 젖빛처럼 흰 안개 속에서 일어선다 — 그렇다, 이 공포들이 우리를 사로잡는 동안 한마디 덧붙이자면, 복음 기록자에 의해 인격화된 공포의 왕마저도 그 창백한 말을 타고 달리는 것이다."]], [["Therefore, in his other moods, symbolize whatever grand or gracious thing he will by whiteness, no man can deny that in its profoundest idealized significance it calls up a peculiar apparition to the soul.", "그러므로 다른 기분에 잠겼을 때, 그가 흰빛으로 그 어떤 장엄하거나 우아한 것을 상징하든 간에, 그것이 그 가장 깊고 이상화된 의미에서 영혼에 어떤 독특한 환영을 불러일으킨다는 것을 부정할 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없다."]], [["But though without dissent this point be fixed, how is mortal man to account for it?", "그러나 이 점이 이론(異論) 없이 확정된다 하더라도, 죽을 운명의 인간이 그것을 어떻게 설명할 수 있겠는가?"], ["To analyse it, would seem impossible.", "그것을 분석한다는 것은 불가능해 보인다."], ["Can we, then, by the citation of some of those instances wherein this thing of whiteness—though for the time either wholly or in great part stripped of all direct associations calculated to impart to it aught fearful, but nevertheless, is found to exert over us the same sorcery, however modified;—can we thus hope to light upon some chance clue to conduct us to the hidden cause we seek?", "그렇다면 우리는, 이 흰빛이라는 것이 — 비록 한동안은 두려움을 안겨 주도록 짜인 모든 직접적 연상에서 전적으로 혹은 대체로 벗어나 있을지라도, 그럼에도 형태야 달라졌을망정 우리에게 똑같은 마법을 행사하고 있음이 드러나는 — 그런 몇몇 사례를 인용함으로써, 우리가 찾는 저 숨은 원인으로 우리를 이끌어 줄 우연한 실마리 하나를 더듬어 만나기를 바랄 수 있을까?"]], [["Let us try.", "한번 시도해 보자."], ["But in a matter like this, subtlety appeals to subtlety, and without imagination no man can follow another into these halls.", "그러나 이런 문제에서는 미묘함이 미묘함에 호소하는 법이어서, 상상력 없이는 그 누구도 다른 이를 따라 이 회랑 속으로 들어설 수 없다."], ["And though, doubtless, some at least of the imaginative impressions about to be presented may have been shared by most men, yet few perhaps were entirely conscious of them at the time, and therefore may not be able to recall them now.", "그리고 이제 제시하려는 상상적 인상들 가운데 적어도 일부는 의심할 바 없이 대다수 사람이 함께 느껴 본 것이겠으나, 그것을 그 당시에 온전히 의식했던 사람은 아마 거의 없을 터이니, 따라서 지금 그것을 떠올리지 못할지도 모른다."]], [["Why to the man of untutored ideality, who happens to be but loosely acquainted with the peculiar character of the day, does the bare mention of Whitsuntide marshal in the fancy such long, dreary, speechless processions of slow-pacing pilgrims, down-cast and hooded with new-fallen snow?", "성령강림절이라는 날의 독특한 성격을 그저 어렴풋이밖에 알지 못하는, 가르침받지 못한 관념의 소유자에게, 그저 그 절기의 이름을 입에 올리기만 해도 어찌하여 그토록 길고 음울하며 말 없는 행렬이 — 고개를 떨군 채 갓 내린 눈을 두건처럼 뒤집어쓰고 느릿느릿 걸어가는 순례자들의 행렬이 — 환상 속에 줄지어 늘어서는가?"], ["Or, to the unread, unsophisticated Protestant of the Middle American States, why does the passing mention of a White Friar or a White Nun, evoke such an eyeless statue in the soul?", "혹은 중부 아메리카 여러 주의 책 안 읽고 세상 물정 모르는 프로테스탄트에게, 백의(白衣)의 수사나 백의의 수녀를 지나가는 말로 언급하기만 해도 어찌하여 그토록 눈먼 석상(石像)이 영혼 속에 떠오르는가?"]], [["Or what is there apart from the traditions of dungeoned warriors and kings (which will not wholly account for it) that makes the White Tower of London tell so much more strongly on the imagination of an untravelled American, than those other storied structures, its neighbors—the Byward Tower, or even the Bloody?", "혹은, 지하 감옥에 갇힌 전사들과 왕들의 전설을 (그것만으로는 온전히 설명되지 않거니와) 떼어 놓고 보았을 때, 무엇이 있기에 런던의 '하얀 탑'이, 그 이웃에 있는 다른 유서 깊은 건축물들 — 곧 바이워드 탑이나 심지어 '피의 탑'보다도 — 여행해 본 적 없는 아메리카인의 상상에 그토록 더 강하게 작용하는가?"], ["And those sublimer towers, the White Mountains of New Hampshire, whence, in peculiar moods, comes that gigantic ghostliness over the soul at the bare mention of that name, while the thought of Virginia’s Blue Ridge is full of a soft, dewy, distant dreaminess?", "또한 저 한층 더 숭고한 탑들, 곧 뉴햄프셔의 화이트 산맥은 어떠한가. 그 이름을 입에 올리기만 해도 독특한 기분에 잠긴 영혼 위로 그 거대한 유령다움이 덮쳐 오는 반면, 버지니아의 블루리지 산맥을 떠올리면 부드럽고 이슬 맺힌 아득한 몽환으로 가득 차는 것은 어찌 된 일인가?"], ["Or why, irrespective of all latitudes and longitudes, does the name of the White Sea exert such a spectralness over the fancy, while that of the Yellow Sea lulls us with mortal thoughts of long lacquered mild afternoons on the waves, followed by the gaudiest and yet sleepiest of sunsets?", "혹은 위도와 경도를 모두 떠나서, 어찌하여 백해(白海)라는 이름은 환상 위에 그토록 유령 같은 기운을 드리우는 반면, 황해라는 이름은 옻칠한 듯 잔잔한 긴 오후를 물결 위에서 보내고 그 뒤에 더없이 현란하면서도 더없이 졸음 겨운 일몰이 이어지는, 죽을 운명의 인간다운 생각으로 우리를 달래는가?"], ["Or, to choose a wholly unsubstantial instance, purely addressed to the fancy, why, in reading the old fairy tales of Central Europe, does “the tall pale man” of the Hartz forests, whose changeless pallor unrustlingly glides through the green of the groves—why is this phantom more terrible than all the whooping imps of the Blocksburg?", "혹은, 순전히 환상에만 호소하는 전혀 실체 없는 사례를 하나 고르자면, 중부 유럽의 옛 동화들을 읽을 때, 그 변치 않는 창백함으로 숲의 푸름 사이를 바스락 소리 하나 없이 미끄러져 다니는 하르츠 숲의 \"키 큰 창백한 사내\"는 — 어찌하여 이 유령이 블록스부르크의 온갖 고래고래 소리 지르는 작은 마귀들보다 더 무서운가?"]], [["Nor is it, altogether, the remembrance of her cathedral-toppling earthquakes; nor the stampedoes of her frantic seas; nor the tearlessness of arid skies that never rain; nor the sight of her wide field of leaning spires, wrenched cope-stones, and crosses all adroop (like canted yards of anchored fleets); and her suburban avenues of house-walls lying over upon each other, as a tossed pack of cards;—it is not these things alone which make tearless Lima, the strangest, saddest city thou can’st see.", "또한 리마를 두고 보건대, 그토록 기이하고 슬픈 도시로 만드는 것은, 전적으로 그 대성당을 무너뜨린 지진들에 대한 기억도, 그 미친 듯 날뛰던 바다의 아우성도, 결코 비를 뿌리지 않는 메마른 하늘의 눈물 없음도, 기울어진 첨탑들과 비틀린 갓돌들과 (닻 내린 함대의 기운 활대들처럼) 죄다 늘어진 십자가들이 드넓게 펼쳐진 광경도, 던져 놓은 카드 한 벌처럼 서로의 위로 쓰러진 집 벽들이 늘어선 교외의 거리들도 — 이런 것들만이 눈물 없는 리마를 네가 볼 수 있는 가장 기이하고 가장 슬픈 도시로 만드는 것은 아니다."], ["For Lima has taken the white veil; and there is a higher horror in this whiteness of her woe.", "리마는 흰 너울을 썼기 때문이니, 그 슬픔의 흰빛 속에는 한층 더 높은 공포가 깃들어 있다."], ["Old as Pizarro, this whiteness keeps her ruins for ever new; admits not the cheerful greenness of complete decay; spreads over her broken ramparts the rigid pallor of an apoplexy that fixes its own distortions.", "피사로만큼이나 오래된 이 흰빛은 그 폐허를 영원히 새것인 채로 유지하여, 완전한 부패가 띠는 명랑한 푸르름을 허락하지 않으며, 그 무너진 성벽 위에 제 스스로의 일그러짐을 고정시켜 버리는 뇌졸중의 그 뻣뻣한 창백함을 펼쳐 놓는다."]], [["I know that, to the common apprehension, this phenomenon of whiteness is not confessed to be the prime agent in exaggerating the terror of objects otherwise terrible; nor to the unimaginative mind is there aught of terror in those appearances whose awfulness to another mind almost solely consists in this one phenomenon, especially when exhibited under any form at all approaching to muteness or universality.", "나는 안다. 흔한 통념으로는, 이 흰빛이라는 현상이 다른 면에서 끔찍한 사물들의 공포를 부풀리는 으뜸가는 작인(作因)으로 인정받지 못한다는 것을."], ["What I mean by these two statements may perhaps be respectively elucidated by the following examples.", "또한 상상력 없는 정신에게는, 다른 정신에게는 그 무서움이 거의 오로지 이 한 가지 현상에만 — 특히 그것이 무언(無言)이나 보편성에 조금이라도 다가가는 어떤 형태로든 나타날 때 — 있는 그런 외양들 속에서, 아무런 공포도 느껴지지 않는다는 것을. 이 두 가지 진술로 내가 뜻하는 바는, 아마 다음의 두 예로 각각 밝혀질 수 있을 것이다."]], [["First: The mariner, when drawing nigh the coasts of foreign lands, if by night he hear the roar of breakers, starts to vigilance, and feels just enough of trepidation to sharpen all his faculties; but under precisely similar circumstances, let him be called from his hammock to view his ship sailing through a midnight sea of milky whiteness—as if from encircling headlands shoals of combed white bears were swimming round him, then he feels a silent, superstitious dread; the shrouded phantom of the whitened waters is horrible to him as a real ghost; in vain the lead assures him he is still off soundings; heart and helm they both go down; he never rests till blue water is under him again. Yet where is the mariner who will tell thee, “Sir, it was not so much the fear of striking hidden rocks, as the fear of that hideous whiteness that so stirred me?”", "첫째. 뱃사람은 낯선 나라의 해안에 다가갈 때, 밤중에 부서지는 파도의 굉음을 들으면 정신이 번쩍 들어 경계 태세에 들어가며, 모든 감각을 날카롭게 벼리기에 꼭 알맞을 만큼의 전율을 느낀다. 그러나 그와 똑같은 상황에서, 그를 해먹에서 불러내어 자기 배가 젖빛처럼 흰 한밤의 바다를 가르며 나아가는 모습을 보게 한다면 — 마치 에워싼 곶들로부터 빗질한 듯한 흰곰 떼가 그의 주위로 헤엄쳐 오기라도 하는 듯한 그 광경을 보게 한다면, 그제야 그는 말 없는 미신적 공포를 느낀다. 흰빛으로 물든 바다의 수의(壽衣)에 싸인 유령은 진짜 망령처럼 그에게 끔찍하니, 측연(測鉛)이 아직 수심이 깊은 곳에 있다고 아무리 안심시킨들 소용없이, 그의 심장과 키는 둘 다 가라앉고 만다. 다시 발밑에 푸른 바다가 펼쳐지기 전까지 그는 결코 마음을 놓지 못한다. 그러나 너에게 이렇게 말해 줄 뱃사람이 어디 있겠는가. \"선생, 나를 그토록 동요시킨 것은 숨은 바위에 부딪칠까 하는 두려움이라기보다, 저 흉측한 흰빛에 대한 두려움이었소\"라고?"]], [["Second: To the native Indian of Peru, the continual sight of the snow-howdahed Andes conveys naught of dread, except, perhaps, in the mere fancying of the eternal frosted desolateness reigning at such vast altitudes, and the natural conceit of what a fearfulness it would be to lose oneself in such inhuman solitudes.", "둘째. 페루 원주민 인디언에게는, 눈으로 가마를 씌운 듯한 안데스 산맥을 늘 바라보는 것이 아무런 두려움도 안겨 주지 않는다."], ["Much the same is it with the backwoodsman of the West, who with comparative indifference views an unbounded prairie sheeted with driven snow, no shadow of tree or twig to break the fixed trance of whiteness.", "다만 어쩌면 그토록 광막한 고도에 군림하는 영원히 얼어붙은 황량함을 그저 상상해 볼 때, 그리고 그토록 비인간적인 적막 속에서 길을 잃는다면 그 얼마나 무서운 일일까 하는 자연스러운 공상에서나 약간의 두려움이 일 뿐이다."], ["Not so the sailor, beholding the scenery of the Antarctic seas; where at times, by some infernal trick of legerdemain in the powers of frost and air, he, shivering and half shipwrecked, instead of rainbows speaking hope and solace to his misery, views what seems a boundless churchyard grinning upon him with its lean ice monuments and splintered crosses.", "서부의 오지 사냥꾼도 거의 마찬가지여서, 흩날린 눈으로 뒤덮여 나무 한 그루 잔가지 하나의 그림자조차 그 고정된 흰빛의 황홀경을 깨뜨리지 않는, 끝없는 대초원을 비교적 무심하게 바라본다. 그러나 남극해의 풍광을 마주한 뱃사람은 그렇지 않으니, 그곳에서는 때때로 서리와 대기의 힘이 부리는 어떤 지옥 같은 요술 솜씨로 말미암아, 추위에 떨며 반쯤 난파된 그가 자신의 비참함에 희망과 위안을 건네는 무지개 대신, 비쩍 마른 얼음 기념비들과 쪼개진 십자가들을 곁에 거느린 채 그를 향해 히죽 웃는 끝없는 묘지처럼 보이는 것을 바라보게 된다."]], [["But thou sayest, methinks that white-lead chapter about whiteness is but a white flag hung out from a craven soul; thou surrenderest to a hypo, Ishmael.", "그러나 너는 말하겠지. 내 생각엔, 흰빛에 관한 저 백연(白鉛) 같은 장(章)은 겁쟁이 영혼이 내건 한낱 흰 깃발에 지나지 않으며, 이슈마엘, 너는 우울증에 항복하는 것이라고."]], [["Tell me, why this strong young colt, foaled in some peaceful valley of Vermont, far removed from all beasts of prey—why is it that upon the sunniest day, if you but shake a fresh buffalo robe behind him, so that he cannot even see it, but only smells its wild animal muskiness—why will he start, snort, and with bursting eyes paw the ground in phrensies of affright?", "말해 보라. 어찌하여 이 힘찬 어린 망아지는, 온갖 맹수로부터 멀리 떨어진 버몬트의 어느 평화로운 골짜기에서 태어났는데도, 더없이 화창한 날에 갓 벗긴 들소 가죽을 그 등 뒤에서 흔들어 — 그것을 보지조차 못하고 그저 그 야생 짐승의 사향 내음만을 맡을 뿐인데도 — 어찌하여 그것은 펄쩍 뛰며 콧김을 내뿜고, 눈을 부릅뜬 채 미친 듯한 공포에 사로잡혀 땅을 긁어 대는가?"], ["There is no remembrance in him of any gorings of wild creatures in his green northern home, so that the strange muskiness he smells cannot recall to him anything associated with the experience of former perils; for what knows he, this New England colt, of the black bisons of distant Oregon?", "그 안에는 푸른 북쪽 고향에서 야생 짐승에게 들이받힌 어떤 기억도 없으니, 그것이 맡는 그 낯선 사향 내음이 지난날의 위험에 얽힌 그 무엇도 떠올려 줄 리가 없다. 이 뉴잉글랜드 망아지가, 머나먼 오리건의 검은 들소에 대해 무엇을 알겠는가?"]], [["No: but here thou beholdest even in a dumb brute, the instinct of the knowledge of the demonism in the world.", "아니다. 그러나 여기서 너는 말 못 하는 짐승에게서조차 세상에 깃든 악마성을 아는 본능을 목격하는 것이다."], ["Though thousands of miles from Oregon, still when he smells that savage musk, the rending, goring bison herds are as present as to the deserted wild foal of the prairies, which this instant they may be trampling into dust.", "오리건에서 수천 마일 떨어져 있을지언정, 그 야만스러운 사향 내음을 맡는 순간, 그 갈가리 찢고 들이받는 들소 떼는, 바로 이 순간 그것들에게 짓밟혀 먼지가 되고 있을지도 모를 대초원의 버려진 야생 망아지에게만큼이나 생생하게 거기 있는 것이다."]], [["Thus, then, the muffled rollings of a milky sea; the bleak rustlings of the festooned frosts of mountains; the desolate shiftings of the windrowed snows of prairies; all these, to Ishmael, are as the shaking of that buffalo robe to the frightened colt!", "그러므로, 젖빛 바다의 그 둔탁한 일렁임도, 산을 화환처럼 두른 서리의 그 음산한 바스락거림도, 대초원에 줄지어 쌓인 눈의 그 황량한 일렁임도 — 이 모든 것은, 이슈마엘에게는 겁먹은 망아지에게 흔들어 보이는 그 들소 가죽과도 같은 것이다!"]], [["Though neither knows where lie the nameless things of which the mystic sign gives forth such hints; yet with me, as with the colt, somewhere those things must exist.", "비록 그 신비로운 표징이 그토록 넌지시 비치는 저 이름 없는 것들이 어디에 있는지는 둘 다 알지 못하나, 그럼에도 그 망아지와 마찬가지로 나에게도, 그것들은 어딘가에 분명히 존재할 수밖에 없다."], ["Though in many of its aspects this visible world seems formed in love, the invisible spheres were formed in fright.", "이 눈에 보이는 세계는 여러 모습에서 사랑으로 빚어진 듯 보이지만, 눈에 보이지 않는 영역들은 두려움으로 빚어졌던 것이다."]], [["But not yet have we solved the incantation of this whiteness, and learned why it appeals with such power to the soul; and more strange and far more portentous—why, as we have seen, it is at once the most meaning symbol of spiritual things, nay, the very veil of the Christian’s Deity; and yet should be as it is, the intensifying agent in things the most appalling to mankind.", "그러나 우리는 아직 이 흰빛의 주술을 풀지 못했고, 어찌하여 그것이 그토록 강력한 힘으로 영혼에 호소하는지를 알아내지 못했다. 그리고 한층 더 기이하고 한층 더 불길한 것은 — 어찌하여, 우리가 이미 보았듯, 그것이 동시에 영적인 것들의 가장 의미심장한 상징이며, 아니 기독교도의 신을 가리는 바로 그 너울이면서도, 또한 지금 그러하듯 인류에게 가장 소름 끼치는 것들을 한층 더 강화하는 작인이기도 한가 하는 점이다."]], [["Is it that by its indefiniteness it shadows forth the heartless voids and immensities of the universe, and thus stabs us from behind with the thought of annihilation, when beholding the white depths of the milky way?", "그것은 그 막연함으로 말미암아 이 우주의 무정한 공허와 광막함을 어렴풋이 드러내고, 그리하여 우리가 은하수의 흰 심연을 바라볼 때 소멸이라는 생각으로 우리를 등 뒤에서 찌르기 때문인가?"], ["Or is it, that as in essence whiteness is not so much a colour as the visible absence of colour; and at the same time the concrete of all colours; is it for these reasons that there is such a dumb blankness, full of meaning, in a wide landscape of snows—a colourless, all-colour of atheism from which we shrink?", "혹은 그것은, 본질에 있어 흰빛이란 하나의 빛깔이라기보다 빛깔의 눈에 보이는 부재(不在)인 동시에 모든 빛깔의 응결체이기 때문인가? 바로 이런 까닭으로, 드넓은 설경 속에는 그토록 말 없는, 의미로 가득 찬 공백이 — 우리가 움츠러들게 되는, 무신론의 무색(無色)이면서 만색(萬色)인 그 빛깔이 — 깃들어 있는 것인가?"], ["And when we consider that other theory of the natural philosophers, that all other earthly hues—every stately or lovely emblazoning—the sweet tinges of sunset skies and woods; yea, and the gilded velvets of butterflies, and the butterfly cheeks of young girls; all these are but subtile deceits, not actually inherent in substances, but only laid on from without; so that all deified Nature absolutely paints like the harlot, whose allurements cover nothing but the charnel-house within; and when we proceed further, and consider that the mystical cosmetic which produces every one of her hues, the great principle of light, for ever remains white or colorless in itself, and if operating without medium upon matter, would touch all objects, even tulips and roses, with its own blank tinge—pondering all this, the palsied universe lies before us a leper; and like wilful travellers in Lapland, who refuse to wear coloured and colouring glasses upon their eyes, so the wretched infidel gazes himself blind at the monumental white shroud that wraps all the prospect around him.", "또한 자연철학자들의 저 다른 이론, 곧 다른 모든 지상의 빛깔들이 — 모든 장엄하거나 사랑스러운 채색이, 일몰 무렵 하늘과 숲의 그 달콤한 색조가, 그렇다, 나비의 금박을 입힌 듯한 보드라운 빛깔과 어린 소녀들의 나비 같은 두 볼이 — 이 모든 것이 실은 사물 자체에 내재한 것이 아니라 다만 바깥에서 입혀진, 미묘한 기만에 지나지 않는다는 그 이론을 우리가 고려할 때, 그리하여 신격화된 자연 전체가 실은 창부(娼婦)처럼 분칠을 하고 있을 뿐이며 그 유혹이 가리는 것은 그 안의 납골당밖에 없다는 것을 — 그리고 더 나아가, 그녀의 빛깔을 하나하나 빚어내는 그 신비로운 화장품, 곧 빛이라는 저 위대한 원리가 그 자체로는 영원히 희거나 무색으로 남아 있어, 만일 매질(媒質) 없이 물질에 직접 작용한다면 튤립이든 장미든 모든 사물을 제 고유의 공백한 색조로 물들이고 말리라는 것을 우리가 헤아릴 때 — 이 모든 것을 곰곰이 따져 보면, 마비된 우주가 한 사람의 나병 환자처럼 우리 앞에 누워 있고, 색을 입히는 색안경을 눈에 쓰기를 한사코 거부하는 저 라플란드의 고집스러운 여행자들처럼, 가련한 불신자는 제 둘레의 온 전경(全景)을 감싼 그 기념비적인 흰 수의를 노려보다가 끝내 눈이 멀고 마는 것이다."], ["And of all these things the Albino whale was the symbol.", "그리고 이 모든 것의 상징이 바로 그 백색증의 고래였다."], ["Wonder ye then at the fiery hunt?", "그러니 그대들은 그 불타는 듯한 추격을 이상하게 여기겠는가?"]], [["“HIST!", "\"쉿!"], ["Did you hear that noise, Cabaco?”", "카바코, 자네 저 소리 들었나?\""]], [["It was the middle-watch: a fair moonlight; the seamen were standing in a cordon, extending from one of the fresh-water butts in the waist, to the scuttle-butt near the taffrail.", "자정 당직 시간이었다. 달빛이 맑게 흐르고 있었다."], ["In this manner, they passed the buckets to fill the scuttle-butt.", "선원들은 갑판 중앙의 담수통 하나에서 고물 난간 근처의 식수통까지 한 줄로 늘어서 있었다."], ["Standing, for the most part, on the hallowed precincts of the quarter-deck, they were careful not to speak or rustle their feet.", "그렇게 그들은 물통을 차례로 건네며 식수통을 채웠다. 대부분은 신성하게 여겨지는 뒷갑판 구역에 서 있었으므로, 말을 하거나 발을 끌어 부스럭거리지 않으려고 조심했다."], ["From hand to hand, the buckets went in the deepest silence, only broken by the occasional flap of a sail, and the steady hum of the unceasingly advancing keel.", "손에서 손으로, 물통은 깊디깊은 정적 속을 오갔다. 그 정적을 깨뜨리는 것이라곤 이따금 펄럭이는 돛 소리와, 끊임없이 나아가는 용골이 내는 한결같은 웅웅거림뿐이었다."]], [["It was in the midst of this repose, that Archy, one of the cordon, whose post was near the after-hatches, whispered to his neighbor, a Cholo, the words above.", "바로 이 고요의 한가운데서, 줄지어 선 사람들 중 하나로 뒤쪽 승강구 근처를 맡고 있던 아치가 옆 사람인 촐로 사내에게 위의 말을 속삭였던 것이다."]], [["“Hist!", "\"쉿!"], ["did you hear that noise, Cabaco?”", "카바코, 자네 저 소리 들었나?\""]], [["“Take the bucket, will ye, Archy?", "\"물통이나 받게, 아치, 응?"], ["what noise d’ye mean?”", "무슨 소리 말인가?\""]], [["“There it is again—under the hatches—don’t you hear it—a cough—it sounded like a cough.”", "\"또 들린다네 — 승강구 아래에서 — 안 들리나 — 기침 소리야 — 꼭 기침 같았어.\""]], [["“Cough be damned!", "\"기침은 무슨 기침!"], ["Pass along that return bucket.”", "빈 물통이나 어서 넘기게.\""]], [["“There again—there it is!—it sounds like two or three sleepers turning over, now!”", "\"또야 — 또 들려! — 이번엔 누가 두셋쯤 잠결에 몸을 뒤척이는 소리 같다니까!\""]], [["“Caramba!", "\"카람바!"], ["have done, shipmate, will ye?", "그만하게, 여보게, 응?"], ["It’s the three soaked biscuits ye eat for supper turning over inside of ye—nothing else.", "저녁에 자네가 먹은 그 물에 불린 비스킷 세 조각이 뱃속에서 뒤집히는 소리야 — 그뿐이라고."], ["Look to the bucket!”", "물통이나 보게!\""]], [["“Say what ye will, shipmate; I’ve sharp ears.”", "\"자네야 뭐라 하든 마음대로 하게, 여보게. 난 귀가 밝거든.\""]], [["“Aye, you are the chap, ain’t ye, that heard the hum of the old Quakeress’s knitting-needles fifty miles at sea from Nantucket; you’re the chap.”", "\"그래, 자네가 바로 그 사내지, 안 그런가? 낸터킷에서 바다로 오십 마일이나 나와서도 늙은 퀘이커 부인의 뜨개바늘 소리를 들었다는 그 사내 말이야. 자네가 그 작자야.\" \"실컷 비웃게나. 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 두고 보자고. 잘 듣게, 카바코. 뒤쪽 선창 아래에 아직 갑판에서 한 번도 모습을 보이지 않은 누군가가 있어. 그리고 우리 늙은 무굴 영감도 그걸 뭔가 알고 있는 것 같단 말이지. 새벽 당직 때 스텁이 플라스크한테 그런 낌새가 있다고 말하는 걸 내가 들었거든.\""]], [["“Grin away; we’ll see what turns up.", "\"쳇! 물통이나!\""], ["Hark ye, Cabaco, there is somebody down in the after-hold that has not yet been seen on deck; and I suspect our old Mogul knows something of it too.", "* 무굴 영감: 에이해브 선장을 가리키는 선원들 사이의 별칭."], ["I heard Stubb tell Flask, one morning watch, that there was something of that sort in the wind.”", "무굴은 인도를 호령하던 절대 군주를 뜻한다."]], [["# CHAPTER 44. The Chart.", "# CHAPTER 44. The Chart."]], [["Had you followed Captain Ahab down into his cabin after the squall that took place on the night succeeding that wild ratification of his purpose with his crew, you would have seen him go to a locker in the transom, and bringing out a large wrinkled roll of yellowish sea charts, spread them before him on his screwed-down table.", "그날 밤, 에이해브가 선원들 앞에서 자신의 목적을 거칠게 다짐받은 그 광란의 의식이 있은 다음 날 밤, 한바탕 돌풍이 지나간 뒤에 그를 따라 선실로 내려가 보았더라면, 당신은 그가 고물 쪽 사물함으로 가서 누렇게 바랜 해도(海圖) 한 무더기를 둘둘 만 채로 꺼내, 나사로 고정된 탁자 위에 펼쳐 놓는 광경을 보았을 것이다."], ["Then seating himself before it, you would have seen him intently study the various lines and shadings which there met his eye; and with slow but steady pencil trace additional courses over spaces that before were blank.", "그러고는 그 앞에 앉아, 눈앞에 펼쳐진 갖가지 선과 음영을 골똘히 들여다보며, 느리지만 흔들림 없는 연필 끝으로 전에는 비어 있던 공간 위에 새로운 항로를 그려 넣는 모습을 보았을 것이다."], ["At intervals, he would refer to piles of old log-books beside him, wherein were set down the seasons and places in which, on various former voyages of various ships, sperm whales had been captured or seen.", "이따금 그는 곁에 쌓아 둔 낡은 항해일지 더미를 들춰 보았는데, 거기에는 여러 배의 갖가지 지난 항해에서 향유고래를 잡았거나 목격한 계절과 장소가 적혀 있었다."]], [["While thus employed, the heavy pewter lamp suspended in chains over his head, continually rocked with the motion of the ship, and for ever threw shifting gleams and shadows of lines upon his wrinkled brow, till it almost seemed that while he himself was marking out lines and courses on the wrinkled charts, some invisible pencil was also tracing lines and courses upon the deeply marked chart of his forehead.", "이렇게 일에 몰두하는 동안, 머리 위 쇠사슬에 매달린 묵직한 백랍 등잔은 배의 흔들림을 따라 끊임없이 좌우로 흔들리며, 그의 주름진 이마 위로 끊임없이 변하는 빛과 선의 그림자를 던졌다. 마침내 그것은 마치, 그 자신이 주름진 해도 위에 선과 항로를 그려 넣는 동안, 어떤 보이지 않는 연필이 그의 이마라는 깊이 새겨진 해도 위에도 선과 항로를 그려 넣고 있는 것처럼 보일 지경이었다."]], [["But it was not this night in particular that, in the solitude of his cabin, Ahab thus pondered over his charts.", "그러나 에이해브가 선실의 고독 속에서 이처럼 해도를 들여다본 것은 비단 이날 밤만의 일이 아니었다."], ["Almost every night they were brought out; almost every night some pencil marks were effaced, and others were substituted.", "거의 매일 밤 해도가 펼쳐졌고, 거의 매일 밤 어떤 연필 자국은 지워지고 다른 자국이 그 자리를 대신했다."], ["For with the charts of all four oceans before him, Ahab was threading a maze of currents and eddies, with a view to the more certain accomplishment of that monomaniac thought of his soul.", "사방 네 대양의 해도를 앞에 놓고서, 에이해브는 자기 영혼의 그 편집광적인 생각을 더욱 확실히 이루려는 일념으로 해류와 소용돌이의 미로를 더듬어 가고 있었던 것이다."]], [["Now, to any one not fully acquainted with the ways of the leviathans, it might seem an absurdly hopeless task thus to seek out one solitary creature in the unhooped oceans of this planet.", "리바이어던의 생태에 완전히 정통하지 못한 사람이라면, 이 행성의 테두리 없는 대양에서 단 한 마리의 고독한 짐승을 이렇게 찾아내려는 일이 터무니없이 가망 없는 짓으로 보일지도 모른다."], ["But not so did it seem to Ahab, who knew the sets of all tides and currents; and thereby calculating the driftings of the sperm whale’s food; and, also, calling to mind the regular, ascertained seasons for hunting him in particular latitudes; could arrive at reasonable surmises, almost approaching to certainties, concerning the timeliest day to be upon this or that ground in search of his prey.", "그러나 에이해브에게는 그렇지 않았다. 그는 모든 조수와 해류의 흐름을 알았고, 그로써 향유고래의 먹이가 떠도는 경로를 계산해 냈으며, 또한 특정 위도에서 그놈을 사냥하기에 일정하게 검증된 계절을 떠올려, 이러저러한 어장에서 먹잇감을 찾기에 가장 알맞은 날에 관하여, 거의 확신에 가까운 합당한 추정에 이를 수 있었던 것이다."]], [["So assured, indeed, is the fact concerning the periodicalness of the sperm whale’s resorting to given waters, that many hunters believe that, could he be closely observed and studied throughout the world; were the logs for one voyage of the entire whale fleet carefully collated, then the migrations of the sperm whale would be found to correspond in invariability to those of the herring-shoals or the flights of swallows.", "실제로 향유고래가 일정한 해역을 주기적으로 찾아든다는 사실은 워낙 확실해서, 많은 사냥꾼들은 만약 그놈을 전 세계에 걸쳐 면밀히 관찰하고 연구할 수 있다면, 또 전체 포경 선단의 한 차례 항해 일지를 빠짐없이 대조할 수 있다면, 향유고래의 회유(回遊)가 청어 떼나 제비의 비행만큼이나 변함없는 규칙성을 보이리라고 믿는다."], ["On this hint, attempts have been made to construct elaborate migratory charts of the sperm whale.*", "이러한 암시에 힘입어, 향유고래의 정교한 회유 해도를 작성하려는 시도가 이루어져 왔다.*"]], [["*Since the above was written, the statement is happily borne out by an official circular, issued by Lieutenant Maury, of the National Observatory, Washington, April 16th, 1851.", "* 위 글을 쓴 이후로, 1851년 4월 16일 워싱턴 국립천문대의 모리 중위가 발표한 공식 회람에 의해 이 진술이 다행히도 뒷받침되었다."], ["By that circular, it appears that precisely such a chart is in course of completion; and portions of it are presented in the circular.", "그 회람에 따르면, 바로 그러한 해도가 완성 단계에 있으며, 그 일부가 회람에 실려 있다."], ["“This chart divides the ocean into districts of five degrees of latitude by five degrees of longitude; perpendicularly through each of which districts are twelve columns for the twelve months; and horizontally through each of which districts are three lines; one to show the number of days that have been spent in each month in every district, and the two others to show the number of days in which whales, sperm or right, have been seen.”", "\"이 해도는 대양을 위도 5도, 경도 5도의 구역으로 나누고, 각 구역을 세로로 가로질러 열두 달을 나타내는 열두 개의 칸을 두며, 가로로 가로질러서는 세 개의 선을 두는데, 하나는 각 구역에서 매달 보낸 날수를 나타내고, 나머지 둘은 향유고래든 참고래든 고래가 목격된 날수를 나타낸다.\""]], [["Besides, when making a passage from one feeding-ground to another, the sperm whales, guided by some infallible instinct—say, rather, secret intelligence from the Deity—mostly swim in veins, as they are called; continuing their way along a given ocean-line with such undeviating exactitude, that no ship ever sailed her course, by any chart, with one tithe of such marvellous precision.", "게다가, 한 먹이터에서 다른 먹이터로 옮겨 갈 때, 향유고래는 어떤 틀림없는 본능에 — 아니, 차라리 신으로부터의 은밀한 계시라고 해야 하리라 — 이끌려 대개 이른바 '맥(脈, vein)'을 따라 헤엄친다. 정해진 대양의 한 선을 따라 어찌나 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 정확하게 나아가는지, 그 어떤 배도 그 어떤 해도를 가지고도 그처럼 경이로운 정확성의 십분의 일도 따라가지 못한다."], ["Though, in these cases, the direction taken by any one whale be straight as a surveyor’s parallel, and though the line of advance be strictly confined to its own unavoidable, straight wake, yet the arbitrary vein in which at these times he is said to swim, generally embraces some few miles in width (more or less, as the vein is presumed to expand or contract); but never exceeds the visual sweep from the whale-ship’s mast-heads, when circumspectly gliding along this magic zone.", "비록 이런 경우에 어느 한 마리의 고래가 잡는 방향은 측량사가 그은 평행선처럼 곧고, 그 전진의 선은 피할 수 없는 제 자신의 곧은 항적에 엄격히 한정되지만, 이때 그놈이 헤엄친다고 일컬어지는 그 임의의 '맥'은 대체로 폭이 몇 마일에 이른다(맥이 넓어지거나 좁아지리라 짐작되는 데 따라 더하기도 덜하기도 한다). 그러나 그 폭은, 이 마법의 띠를 따라 신중하게 미끄러져 갈 때 포경선의 돛대 꼭대기에서 둘러볼 수 있는 시야의 범위를 결코 넘지 않는다."], ["The sum is, that at particular seasons within that breadth and along that path, migrating whales may with great confidence be looked for.", "요컨대 특정한 계절에 그 폭 안에서, 그 길을 따라, 회유하는 고래를 큰 확신을 가지고 기다릴 수 있다는 것이다."]], [["And hence not only at substantiated times, upon well known separate feeding-grounds, could Ahab hope to encounter his prey; but in crossing the widest expanses of water between those grounds he could, by his art, so place and time himself on his way, as even then not to be wholly without prospect of a meeting.", "따라서 에이해브는 잘 알려진 별개의 먹이터에서 검증된 시기에 먹잇감을 만나기를 바랄 수 있었을 뿐만 아니라, 그 먹이터들 사이의 가장 넓은 바다를 가로지를 때에도, 자기 기량을 발휘하여 길 위의 자기 위치와 시간을 적절히 안배함으로써, 그때조차 마주칠 가망이 아주 없지는 않게끔 할 수 있었다."]], [["There was a circumstance which at first sight seemed to entangle his delirious but still methodical scheme.", "처음 보기에 그의 광기 어린, 그러나 여전히 체계적인 계획을 얽어맬 듯한 사정이 하나 있었다."], ["But not so in the reality, perhaps.", "그러나 실제로는 어쩌면 그렇지 않았다."], ["Though the gregarious sperm whales have their regular seasons for particular grounds, yet in general you cannot conclude that the herds which haunted such and such a latitude or longitude this year, say, will turn out to be identically the same with those that were found there the preceding season; though there are peculiar and unquestionable instances where the contrary of this has proved true.", "무리 짓는 향유고래가 특정 어장을 찾는 일정한 계절을 가지고 있긴 하지만, 일반적으로 올해 이러저러한 위도나 경도에 출몰한 무리가 지난 계절에 그곳에서 발견된 바로 그 무리와 동일하리라고 단정할 수는 없다 — 비록 그 반대가 사실임이 입증된 독특하고 의심할 바 없는 사례들이 있긴 하지만."], ["In general, the same remark, only within a less wide limit, applies to the solitaries and hermits among the matured, aged sperm whales.", "일반적으로 같은 이야기가, 다만 그보다 좁은 한계 안에서, 다 자란 늙은 향유고래 가운데 외톨이와 은둔자들에게도 적용된다."], ["So that though Moby Dick had in a former year been seen, for example, on what is called the Seychelle ground in the Indian ocean, or Volcano Bay on the Japanese Coast; yet it did not follow, that were the Pequod to visit either of those spots at any subsequent corresponding season, she would infallibly encounter him there.", "그러므로 가령 모비 딕이 지난 어느 해에 인도양의 이른바 세이셸 어장이나 일본 연안의 화산만(火山灣)에서 목격되었다 하더라도, 피쿼드호가 그 어느 곳이든 그 이후의 상응하는 계절에 찾아간다고 해서 그곳에서 틀림없이 그놈을 마주치리라는 결론이 따라 나오는 것은 아니었다."], ["So, too, with some other feeding grounds, where he had at times revealed himself.", "그놈이 때때로 모습을 드러낸 다른 몇몇 먹이터에 대해서도 마찬가지였다."], ["But all these seemed only his casual stopping-places and ocean-inns, so to speak, not his places of prolonged abode.", "그러나 이 모든 곳은 말하자면 그놈이 우연히 들르는 정거장이요 바다의 여인숙일 뿐, 오래 머무는 거처는 아닌 듯했다."], ["And where Ahab’s chances of accomplishing his object have hitherto been spoken of, allusion has only been made to whatever way-side, antecedent, extra prospects were his, ere a particular set time or place were attained, when all possibilities would become probabilities, and, as Ahab fondly thought, every possibility the next thing to a certainty.", "그리고 지금까지 에이해브가 자기 목적을 이룰 가망에 관해 이야기한 것은, 어떤 특정한 정해진 시기나 장소에 다다르기 전, 길가에서 마주칠 법한 사전적이고 부수적인 가망들에 대한 언급에 불과했다. 그 정해진 시기나 장소에 이르면 모든 가능성이 개연성으로 바뀌고, 에이해브가 흐뭇하게 여겼듯이, 모든 가능성이 확실성에 가장 가까운 것이 되는 것이었다."], ["That particular set time and place were conjoined in the one technical phrase—the Season-on-the-Line.", "그 정해진 특정한 시기와 장소는 하나의 전문적인 표현으로 합쳐져 있었으니 — 바로 '적도선의 계절(Season-on-the-Line)'이다."], ["For there and then, for several consecutive years, Moby Dick had been periodically descried, lingering in those waters for awhile, as the sun, in its annual round, loiters for a predicted interval in any one sign of the Zodiac.", "거기서, 그때, 여러 해 연달아 모비 딕은 주기적으로 발견되었으며, 마치 태양이 한 해의 순환 속에서 황도 십이궁의 어느 한 별자리에 예고된 기간만큼 머무는 것처럼, 한동안 그 해역에 머물렀던 것이다."], ["There it was, too, that most of the deadly encounters with the white whale had taken place; there the waves were storied with his deeds; there also was that tragic spot where the monomaniac old man had found the awful motive to his vengeance.", "또한 그곳은 흰 고래와의 치명적인 충돌이 대부분 벌어진 곳이었고, 그곳의 파도는 그놈의 행적으로 이야깃거리가 되었으며, 그곳은 또한 그 편집광 노인이 자신의 끔찍한 복수의 동기를 얻은 비극의 자리이기도 했다."], ["But in the cautious comprehensiveness and unloitering vigilance with which Ahab threw his brooding soul into this unfaltering hunt, he would not permit himself to rest all his hopes upon the one crowning fact above mentioned, however flattering it might be to those hopes; nor in the sleeplessness of his vow could he so tranquillize his unquiet heart as to postpone all intervening quest.", "그러나 에이해브가 이 흔들림 없는 사냥에 자신의 곱씹는 영혼을 던져 넣을 때의 그 신중한 포괄성과 게으름 없는 경계심을 가지고서, 그는 앞서 말한 그 으뜸가는 사실 하나에만 모든 희망을 걸어 두려 하지 않았다 — 그것이 그 희망에 아무리 솔깃한 것이라 할지라도. 또한 그 맹세의 잠 못 이루는 집념 속에서, 그는 그사이에 끼어들 온갖 탐색을 미뤄 둘 만큼 자신의 불안한 마음을 가라앉힐 수도 없었다."]], [["Now, the Pequod had sailed from Nantucket at the very beginning of the Season-on-the-Line.", "그런데 피쿼드호는 바로 적도선의 계절이 막 시작될 무렵에 낸터킷을 출항했다."], ["No possible endeavor then could enable her commander to make the great passage southwards, double Cape Horn, and then running down sixty degrees of latitude arrive in the equatorial Pacific in time to cruise there.", "그러니 어떤 노력을 기울인다 해도, 그 함장이 남쪽으로 대항해를 감행하여 케이프 혼을 돌고, 그런 뒤 위도 육십 도를 내달려, 적도 부근 태평양에 제때 도착해 그곳을 항해할 수는 없는 노릇이었다."], ["Therefore, he must wait for the next ensuing season.", "그러므로 그는 다음에 이어지는 계절을 기다려야 했다."], ["Yet the premature hour of the Pequod’s sailing had, perhaps, been correctly selected by Ahab, with a view to this very complexion of things.", "그러나 피쿼드호가 이처럼 이른 시각에 출항한 것은, 어쩌면 바로 이러한 사정의 국면을 내다보고 에이해브가 정확하게 택한 것일 수도 있었다."], ["Because, an interval of three hundred and sixty-five days and nights was before him; an interval which, instead of impatiently enduring ashore, he would spend in a miscellaneous hunt; if by chance the White Whale, spending his vacation in seas far remote from his periodical feeding-grounds, should turn up his wrinkled brow off the Persian Gulf, or in the Bengal Bay, or China Seas, or in any other waters haunted by his race.", "왜냐하면 그의 앞에는 삼백예순닷새의 낮과 밤이라는 기간이 놓여 있었고, 그는 그 기간을 뭍에서 안달하며 견디는 대신 잡다한 사냥으로 보내려 했기 때문이다. 만약 어쩌다 흰 고래가 주기적으로 찾는 먹이터에서 멀리 떨어진 바다에서 휴가를 보내다가, 페르시아 만이나 벵골 만이나 중국해, 혹은 제 동족이 출몰하는 그 밖의 어느 해역에서 그 주름진 이마를 불쑥 쳐들 수도 있을 테니까."], ["So that Monsoons, Pampas, Nor’-Westers, Harmattans, Trades; any wind but the Levanter and Simoon, might blow Moby Dick into the devious zig-zag world-circle of the Pequod’s circumnavigating wake.", "그리하여 계절풍이든, 팜파스의 바람이든, 북서풍이든, 하르마탄이든, 무역풍이든 — 레반테르와 시문을 제외한 어떤 바람이든 — 모비 딕을 피쿼드호가 세계를 일주하는 항적의 그 구불구불한 갈지자 원궤 속으로 몰아넣을 수도 있었던 것이다."]], [["But granting all this; yet, regarded discreetly and coolly, seems it not but a mad idea, this; that in the broad boundless ocean, one solitary whale, even if encountered, should be thought capable of individual recognition from his hunter, even as a white-bearded Mufti in the thronged thoroughfares of Constantinople?", "그러나 이 모든 것을 인정한다 하더라도, 신중하고 냉정하게 따져 보면, 이것은 한낱 미친 생각에 지나지 않는 것이 아닐까? 넓고 끝없는 대양에서, 단 한 마리의 고독한 고래를, 설령 마주친다 한들, 마치 콘스탄티노플의 붐비는 큰길에서 흰 수염의 무프티를 알아보듯, 사냥꾼이 그놈만을 따로 알아볼 수 있으리라고 여기는 것이?"], ["Yes.", "그렇다."], ["For the peculiar snow-white brow of Moby Dick, and his snow-white hump, could not but be unmistakable.", "알아볼 수 있다. 모비 딕의 그 독특한 눈처럼 흰 이마와 눈처럼 흰 혹은 결코 못 알아볼 수 없는 것이기 때문이다."], ["And have I not tallied the whale, Ahab would mutter to himself, as after poring over his charts till long after midnight he would throw himself back in reveries—tallied him, and shall he escape?", "게다가 내가 그 고래를 점찍어 두지 않았던가, 하고 에이해브는 자정이 한참 지나도록 해도를 들여다보다가 몸을 뒤로 젖히고 공상에 잠긴 채 혼잣말로 중얼거리곤 했다 — 그놈을 점찍어 두었거늘, 그놈이 빠져나갈 수 있겠는가?"], ["His broad fins are bored, and scalloped out like a lost sheep’s ear!", "그 널찍한 지느러미는 잃어버린 양의 귀처럼 구멍이 뚫리고 가장자리가 부채꼴로 패어 있지 않은가!"], ["And here, his mad mind would run on in a breathless race; till a weariness and faintness of pondering came over him; and in the open air of the deck he would seek to recover his strength.", "그러다 그의 광기 어린 정신은 숨 가쁜 질주를 이어 가곤 했고, 마침내 곱씹기에 지친 노곤함과 아득함이 그를 덮쳐 왔다. 그러면 그는 갑판의 트인 바람을 쐬며 기력을 되찾으려 했다."], ["Ah, God!", "아, 하느님이시여!"], ["what trances of torments does that man endure who is consumed with one unachieved revengeful desire.", "이루지 못한 복수의 욕망 하나에 사로잡혀 타들어 가는 자가 견뎌 내는 그 고통의 황홀경이란 어떤 것이란 말입니까."], ["He sleeps with clenched hands; and wakes with his own bloody nails in his palms.", "그는 두 주먹을 꽉 쥔 채 잠들었다가, 제 손바닥에 제 피투성이 손톱이 박힌 채로 깨어나곤 했다."]], [["Often, when forced from his hammock by exhausting and intolerably vivid dreams of the night, which, resuming his own intense thoughts through the day, carried them on amid a clashing of phrensies, and whirled them round and round and round in his blazing brain, till the very throbbing of his life-spot became insufferable anguish; and when, as was sometimes the case, these spiritual throes in him heaved his being up from its base, and a chasm seemed opening in him, from which forked flames and lightnings shot up, and accursed fiends beckoned him to leap down among them; when this hell in himself yawned beneath him, a wild cry would be heard through the ship; and with glaring eyes Ahab would burst from his state room, as though escaping from a bed that was on fire.", "밤이면 진을 빼는, 견딜 수 없이 생생한 꿈에 시달려 해먹에서 뛰쳐나오는 일이 잦았다. 그 꿈은 낮 동안의 격렬한 생각을 다시 이어받아, 광란들이 서로 부딪치는 가운데 그것들을 끌고 다니며, 그의 불타는 머릿속에서 빙글빙글 돌리고 또 돌려, 마침내 그의 생명이 깃든 자리의 고동 자체가 견딜 수 없는 고통이 될 지경이었다."], ["Yet these, perhaps, instead of being the unsuppressable symptoms of some latent weakness, or fright at his own resolve, were but the plainest tokens of its intensity.", "그리고 때때로 그렇듯, 이 영적인 진통이 그의 존재를 그 근저에서부터 들어 올릴 때면, 그의 안에서 심연이 입을 벌리는 듯했고, 그 심연으로부터 갈래진 불꽃과 번개가 솟구쳐 오르며, 저주받은 악귀들이 그 한가운데로 뛰어내리라 그를 손짓해 불렀다. 이렇듯 자기 안의 지옥이 그의 발밑에서 입을 벌릴 때면, 배 안에는 사나운 비명이 울려 퍼지곤 했고, 에이해브는 불붙은 침대에서 달아나기라도 하듯, 이글거리는 눈으로 자기 선실에서 튀어나오는 것이었다."], ["For, at such times, crazy Ahab, the scheming, unappeasedly steadfast hunter of the white whale; this Ahab that had gone to his hammock, was not the agent that so caused him to burst from it in horror again.", "그러나 이것은 어쩌면 어떤 잠재된 약함이나 자기 결심에 대한 두려움의 억누를 수 없는 징후가 아니라, 도리어 그 결심이 그만큼 강렬하다는 가장 명백한 표시였는지도 모른다. 왜냐하면 그런 순간에, 흰 고래를 노리는 음모 꾸미는, 달랠 길 없이 한결같은 사냥꾼인 미친 에이해브, 해먹에 든 바로 그 에이해브는, 그를 다시금 공포에 질려 거기서 튀어나오게 만든 그 주체가 아니었기 때문이다."], ["The latter was the eternal, living principle or soul in him; and in sleep, being for the time dissociated from the characterizing mind, which at other times employed it for its outer vehicle or agent, it spontaneously sought escape from the scorching contiguity of the frantic thing, of which, for the time, it was no longer an integral.", "후자는 그의 안에 깃든 영원하고 살아 있는 원리, 곧 영혼이었다. 그리고 잠 속에서 그것은 한동안, 평소에는 그것을 바깥세상에 내보내는 수레나 대리자로 부려 먹는 그 규정하는 정신과 떨어져 나와 있었기에, 그 미쳐 날뛰는 것 — 한동안 더 이상 그것의 한 부분이 아니게 된 그것 — 의 살을 태우는 가까움에서 저절로 달아나려 한 것이었다."], ["But as the mind does not exist unless leagued with the soul, therefore it must have been that, in Ahab’s case, yielding up all his thoughts and fancies to his one supreme purpose; that purpose, by its own sheer inveteracy of will, forced itself against gods and devils into a kind of self-assumed, independent being of its own.", "그러나 정신이란 영혼과 결합되지 않으면 존재하지 못하는 까닭에, 에이해브의 경우에는 이러했음이 틀림없다. 곧, 자신의 모든 생각과 공상을 그 하나의 지고한 목적에 내맡겨 버린 결과, 그 목적이 제 의지의 순전한 뿌리 깊음으로 말미암아, 신들과 악마들을 거스르면서까지 스스로 떠맡은 일종의 독립된 존재로 자기 자신을 밀어붙였던 것이다."], ["Nay, could grimly live and burn, while the common vitality to which it was conjoined, fled horror-stricken from the unbidden and unfathered birth.", "아니, 그것은 자신이 결합되어 있던 그 공통의 생명력이 부르지도 않았고 아비도 없는 그 탄생에 질겁하여 달아나는 동안에도, 음산하게 살아남아 불타오를 수 있었던 것이다."], ["Therefore, the tormented spirit that glared out of bodily eyes, when what seemed Ahab rushed from his room, was for the time but a vacated thing, a formless somnambulistic being, a ray of living light, to be sure, but without an object to colour, and therefore a blankness in itself.", "그러므로 에이해브로 보이는 것이 제 선실에서 뛰쳐나올 때, 육신의 두 눈에서 이글거리며 내다보던 그 고통받는 정신은, 한동안 텅 비워진 것, 곧 형체 없는 몽유병적 존재요, 분명 살아 있는 빛의 한 줄기이긴 하되, 물들일 대상이 없는, 그리하여 그 자체로 하나의 공백이었던 것이다. 신이여 그대를 도우소서, 노인이여, 그대의 생각이 그대 안에 한 피조물을 만들어 냈으니."], ["God help thee, old man, thy thoughts have created a creature in thee; and he whose intense thinking thus makes him a Prometheus; a vulture feeds upon that heart for ever; that vulture the very creature he creates.", "강렬한 생각으로 자신을 이렇게 프로메테우스로 만든 자, 그자의 그 심장을 한 마리 독수리가 영원히 파먹는다. 그리고 그 독수리는 바로 그자가 만들어 낸 그 피조물이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 45. The Affidavit.", "# CHAPTER 45. The Affidavit."]], [["So far as what there may be of a narrative in this book; and, indeed, as indirectly touching one or two very interesting and curious particulars in the habits of sperm whales, the foregoing chapter, in its earlier part, is as important a one as will be found in this volume; but the leading matter of it requires to be still further and more familiarly enlarged upon, in order to be adequately understood, and moreover to take away any incredulity which a profound ignorance of the entire subject may induce in some minds, as to the natural verity of the main points of this affair.", "이 책에 담긴 이야기다운 이야기에 관한 한, 그리고 사실 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 습성에 관한 한두 가지 매우 흥미롭고 진기한 세부 사항을 간접적으로 다룬다는 점에서, 앞 장(章)의 앞부분은 이 책 전체에서 찾아볼 수 있는 가장 중요한 대목 가운데 하나다. 그러나 그 핵심 내용은 제대로 이해되기 위해, 그리고 나아가 이 사안의 주요 요점이 자연 그대로의 진실임을 두고 그 주제 전반에 대한 깊은 무지가 어떤 이들의 마음속에 불러일으킬지 모를 온갖 의심을 걷어 내기 위해, 여전히 더 자세하고 더 친근하게 풀어 놓을 필요가 있다."]], [["I care not to perform this part of my task methodically; but shall be content to produce the desired impression by separate citations of items, practically or reliably known to me as a whaleman; and from these citations, I take it—the conclusion aimed at will naturally follow of itself.", "나는 이 과업의 이 부분을 체계적으로 수행할 마음이 없다. 다만 고래잡이로서 내가 실제로, 또는 믿을 만하게 알고 있는 여러 사례를 따로따로 인용함으로써 원하는 인상을 만들어 내는 것으로 만족하려 한다. 그리고 이러한 인용들로부터 — 내가 보기에 — 목표로 삼은 결론이 자연스럽게 절로 따라 나올 것이다."]], [["First: I have personally known three instances where a whale, after receiving a harpoon, has effected a complete escape; and, after an interval (in one instance of three years), has been again struck by the same hand, and slain; when the two irons, both marked by the same private cypher, have been taken from the body.", "첫째: 나는 고래 한 마리가 작살을 맞고도 완벽하게 달아났다가, 일정한 시간이 지난 뒤(한 경우에는 삼 년이 흘렀다) 다시 같은 사람의 손에 작살을 맞고 죽임을 당해, 그 몸에서 동일한 개인 표식이 새겨진 두 개의 작살이 빠져나온 사례를 세 차례나 직접 알고 있다."], ["In the instance where three years intervened between the flinging of the two harpoons; and I think it may have been something more than that; the man who darted them happening, in the interval, to go in a trading ship on a voyage to Africa, went ashore there, joined a discovery party, and penetrated far into the interior, where he travelled for a period of nearly two years, often endangered by serpents, savages, tigers, poisonous miasmas, with all the other common perils incident to wandering in the heart of unknown regions.", "두 작살을 던진 사이에 삼 년이 흐른 경우에는 — 어쩌면 그보다 좀 더 되었을지도 모른다 — 작살을 던진 그 사람이 그 사이에 어느 무역선을 타고 아프리카로 항해를 떠났다가 그곳에 상륙하여 어느 탐험대에 합류했고, 내륙 깊숙이 파고들어 거의 이 년에 걸쳐 떠돌았는데, 그동안 그는 뱀과 야만인과 호랑이, 독기 어린 장기(瘴氣), 그리고 미지의 땅 한복판을 헤매는 데 따르는 온갖 흔한 위험에 거듭 목숨을 위협받았다."], ["Meanwhile, the whale he had struck must also have been on its travels; no doubt it had thrice circumnavigated the globe, brushing with its flanks all the coasts of Africa; but to no purpose.", "한편 그가 작살로 찔렀던 고래 또한 제 나름의 여행을 하고 있었을 것이 분명하니, 의심할 바 없이 그놈은 지구를 세 번이나 일주하며 그 옆구리로 아프리카의 모든 해안을 스치고 다녔을 것이다. 그러나 다 부질없는 일이었다."], ["This man and this whale again came together, and the one vanquished the other.", "이 사람과 이 고래가 다시 만났고, 한쪽이 다른 한쪽을 제압했다."], ["I say I, myself, have known three instances similar to this; that is in two of them I saw the whales struck; and, upon the second attack, saw the two irons with the respective marks cut in them, afterwards taken from the dead fish.", "거듭 말하지만, 나 자신이 이와 비슷한 사례를 세 차례 알고 있다. 즉 그중 두 경우에서 나는 고래가 작살에 찔리는 것을 보았고, 두 번째 공격이 이루어진 뒤 죽은 고래 몸에서 각각의 표식이 새겨진 두 개의 작살이 빠져나오는 것을 보았다."], ["In the three-year instance, it so fell out that I was in the boat both times, first and last, and the last time distinctly recognised a peculiar sort of huge mole under the whale’s eye, which I had observed there three years previous.", "삼 년의 시간이 흐른 그 경우에는, 마침 내가 처음과 마지막 두 번 다 보트에 타고 있었는데, 마지막 번에 나는 고래의 눈 밑에 난 어떤 특이한 큼직한 사마귀를 또렷이 알아보았으니, 그것은 삼 년 전에 내가 거기서 눈여겨봐 두었던 것이었다."], ["I say three years, but I am pretty sure it was more than that.", "삼 년이라고 말하지만, 사실 나는 그보다 더 되었으리라고 거의 확신한다."], ["Here are three instances, then, which I personally know the truth of; but I have heard of many other instances from persons whose veracity in the matter there is no good ground to impeach.", "그러니 여기 내가 직접 그 진실을 아는 세 가지 사례가 있다. 그러나 나는 이 문제에 관한 진실성을 의심할 만한 근거가 전혀 없는 사람들에게서 그 밖에도 많은 사례를 전해 들었다."]], [["Secondly: It is well known in the Sperm Whale Fishery, however ignorant the world ashore may be of it, that there have been several memorable historical instances where a particular whale in the ocean has been at distant times and places popularly cognisable.", "둘째: 뭍의 세상 사람들이야 아무리 모를지언정, 향유고래잡이 업계에서는 망망대해의 어떤 특정한 고래가 멀리 떨어진 시기와 장소에서도 널리 알아볼 수 있는 존재였던, 기억할 만한 역사적 사례가 여럿 있었다는 것이 잘 알려져 있다. 그런 고래가 그처럼 이름이 붙게 된 까닭이 처음부터 전적으로 다른 고래들과 구별되는 그 몸의 특이함 때문만은 아니었다."], ["Why such a whale became thus marked was not altogether and originally owing to his bodily peculiarities as distinguished from other whales; for however peculiar in that respect any chance whale may be, they soon put an end to his peculiarities by killing him, and boiling him down into a peculiarly valuable oil.", "우연히 마주친 어떤 고래가 그 점에서 아무리 특이하다 한들, 사람들은 그놈을 죽여서 끓여 특별히 값진 기름으로 만들어 버림으로써 그 특이함에 곧장 종지부를 찍어 버리기 때문이다. 아니다."], ["No: the reason was this: that from the fatal experiences of the fishery there hung a terrible prestige of perilousness about such a whale as there did about Rinaldo Rinaldini, insomuch that most fishermen were content to recognise him by merely touching their tarpaulins when he would be discovered lounging by them on the sea, without seeking to cultivate a more intimate acquaintance.", "그 까닭은 이러했다. 고래잡이의 치명적인 경험들로 말미암아, 마치 리날도 리날디니를 둘러싸고 그랬듯, 그런 고래를 둘러싸고 위험함이라는 무시무시한 위세가 감돌았던 것이다."], ["Like some poor devils ashore that happen to know an irascible great man, they make distant unobtrusive salutations to him in the street, lest if they pursued the acquaintance further, they might receive a summary thump for their presumption.", "그리하여 대다수 고래잡이들은 그놈이 바다 위에서 자기들 곁을 어슬렁거리는 것을 발견하더라도 더 친밀한 사귐을 도모하려 들지 않고, 그저 방수모에 손을 갖다 대어 아는 척하는 것으로 만족했다. 마치 뭍에서 어쩌다 성마른 거물 한 사람을 알게 된 가련한 작자들이, 행여 사귐을 더 밀고 나갔다가는 그 주제넘음에 대한 대가로 단번에 한 대 얻어맞을까 봐, 길에서 멀찍이 떨어져 눈에 거슬리지 않게 인사나 건네는 것과 같았다."]], [["But not only did each of these famous whales enjoy great individual celebrity—Nay, you may call it an ocean-wide renown; not only was he famous in life and now is immortal in forecastle stories after death, but he was admitted into all the rights, privileges, and distinctions of a name; had as much a name indeed as Cambyses or Cæsar.", "그러나 이 이름난 고래들은 저마다 대단한 개인적 명성을 누렸을 뿐 아니라 — 아니, 그것은 온 바다에 두루 퍼진 명망이라 불러야 마땅하다. 살아생전에 유명했을 뿐 아니라 죽은 뒤에도 선원실 이야기 속에서 불멸의 존재가 되었으며, 나아가 하나의 이름이 누리는 온갖 권리와 특권과 영예를 인정받았으니, 실로 캄비세스나 카이사르 못지않은 이름을 지녔던 것이다."], ["Was it not so, O Timor Tom!", "그렇지 않은가, 오 티모르 톰(Timor Tom)이여!"], ["thou famed leviathan, scarred like an iceberg, who so long did’st lurk in the Oriental straits of that name, whose spout was oft seen from the palmy beach of Ombay?", "빙산처럼 상처투성이인 그대, 이름난 리바이어던(Leviathan)이여, 그토록 오랫동안 같은 이름의 동방 해협에 숨어 지내며, 옴바이의 야자수 우거진 해변에서 그 물줄기가 자주 목격되던 그대여."], ["Was it not so, O New Zealand Jack!", "그렇지 않은가, 오 뉴질랜드 잭(New Zealand Jack)이여!"], ["thou terror of all cruisers that crossed their wakes in the vicinity of the Tattoo Land?", "문신의 땅 근방에서 제 항적을 가로지르던 모든 순항선들의 공포였던 그대여."], ["Was it not so, O Morquan!", "그렇지 않은가, 오 모르콴(Morquan)이여!"], ["King of Japan, whose lofty jet they say at times assumed the semblance of a snow-white cross against the sky?", "일본의 왕이여, 사람들 말로는 그 드높이 솟구치는 물줄기가 때로 하늘을 배경으로 새하얀 십자가의 형상을 띠곤 했다는 그대여."], ["Was it not so, O Don Miguel!", "그렇지 않은가, 오 돈 미겔(Don Miguel)이여!"], ["thou Chilian whale, marked like an old tortoise with mystic hieroglyphics upon the back!", "등에 신비로운 상형문자가 마치 늙은 거북의 등딱지처럼 새겨진 그대, 칠레의 고래여!"], ["In plain prose, here are four whales as well known to the students of Cetacean History as Marius or Sylla to the classic scholar.", "쉽게 말하자면, 여기 고래학사(史)를 공부하는 이들에게 마치 고전학자들에게 마리우스나 술라가 그러하듯 익히 알려진 네 마리 고래가 있는 셈이다."]], [["But this is not all.", "그러나 이것이 전부가 아니다."], ["New Zealand Tom and Don Miguel, after at various times creating great havoc among the boats of different vessels, were finally gone in quest of, systematically hunted out, chased and killed by valiant whaling captains, who heaved up their anchors with that express object as much in view, as in setting out through the Narragansett Woods, Captain Butler of old had it in his mind to capture that notorious murderous savage Annawon, the headmost warrior of the Indian King Philip.", "뉴질랜드 톰과 돈 미겔은 여러 시기에 걸쳐 갖가지 선박의 보트들 사이에서 큰 참화를 일으킨 끝에, 마침내 용맹한 고래잡이 선장들에게 표적이 되어 조직적으로 수색되고 추격당해 죽임을 당했다. 그 선장들은 오로지 그 분명한 목적을 염두에 두고 닻을 끌어올렸으니, 이는 옛날 버틀러 선장이 내러갠셋 숲을 뚫고 나서며 저 악명 높고 살기등등한 야만인 애너원, 곧 인디언(Indian) 왕 필립의 으뜸가는 전사를 사로잡으리라 마음먹었던 것과 다르지 않았다."]], [["I do not know where I can find a better place than just here, to make mention of one or two other things, which to me seem important, as in printed form establishing in all respects the reasonableness of the whole story of the White Whale, more especially the catastrophe.", "내가 보기에 중요하다고 여겨지는 한두 가지 다른 일들을 언급하기에, 바로 이 자리보다 더 나은 곳을 찾을 수 없을 듯하다. 그것들은 인쇄된 형태로써 흰 고래(White Whale) 이야기 전체의 타당성을, 특히 그 대참사의 타당성을 모든 면에서 확립해 주기 때문이다."], ["For this is one of those disheartening instances where truth requires full as much bolstering as error.", "이는 진실이 오류 못지않게 받침대를 필요로 하는, 저 맥 빠지는 사례들 가운데 하나이기 때문이다."], ["So ignorant are most landsmen of some of the plainest and most palpable wonders of the world, that without some hints touching the plain facts, historical and otherwise, of the fishery, they might scout at Moby Dick as a monstrous fable, or still worse and more detestable, a hideous and intolerable allegory.", "대다수 뭍사람들은 이 세상의 가장 평범하고도 가장 뚜렷한 경이들 가운데 일부에 대해서조차 너무나 무지하여, 고래잡이의 평범한 사실들, 곧 역사적인 것이든 그 밖의 것이든, 그에 관한 약간의 암시라도 없다면 모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 괴물 같은 우화라고, 아니 그보다 더 나쁘고 더 가증스럽게도 흉측하고 견딜 수 없는 알레고리라고 비웃어 넘길지도 모른다."]], [["First: Though most men have some vague flitting ideas of the general perils of the grand fishery, yet they have nothing like a fixed, vivid conception of those perils, and the frequency with which they recur.", "첫째: 대다수 사람들은 이 위대한 고래잡이업의 일반적인 위험들에 관해 어렴풋하고 스치는 관념 정도는 지니고 있으나, 그 위험들에 대해서, 그리고 그 위험들이 되풀이되는 빈도에 대해서 확고하고 생생한 개념 같은 것은 전혀 갖고 있지 못하다."], ["One reason perhaps is, that not one in fifty of the actual disasters and deaths by casualties in the fishery, ever finds a public record at home, however transient and immediately forgotten that record.", "그 한 가지 까닭은 아마도, 고래잡이에서 실제로 일어나는 재난과 우발적 사고로 인한 죽음 가운데 단 쉰에 하나도 본국에서 공적인 기록으로 남는 일이 없기 때문일 것이다 — 설령 남는다 해도 그 기록은 덧없이 곧 잊히고 만다."], ["Do you suppose that that poor fellow there, who this moment perhaps caught by the whale-line off the coast of New Guinea, is being carried down to the bottom of the sea by the sounding leviathan—do you suppose that that poor fellow’s name will appear in the newspaper obituary you will read to-morrow at your breakfast?", "지금 이 순간 어쩌면 뉴기니 앞바다에서 고래 밧줄에 걸려, 깊이 잠수하는 리바이어던에게 끌려 바다 밑바닥으로 내려가고 있을 저 가련한 친구의 이름이, 그대가 내일 아침 식사 자리에서 읽을 신문의 부고란에 실리리라고 그대는 생각하는가?"], ["No: because the mails are very irregular between here and New Guinea.", "아니다. 여기와 뉴기니 사이의 우편이라는 것이 몹시 불규칙하기 때문이다."], ["In fact, did you ever hear what might be called regular news direct or indirect from New Guinea?", "사실 그대는 뉴기니로부터 직접적이든 간접적이든 정규 소식이라 할 만한 것을 들어 본 적이 있는가?"], ["Yet I tell you that upon one particular voyage which I made to the Pacific, among many others we spoke thirty different ships, every one of which had had a death by a whale, some of them more than one, and three that had each lost a boat’s crew.", "그러나 내가 그대에게 말하건대, 내가 태평양으로 떠났던 어느 항해에서, 여러 다른 배들 가운데 우리는 서른 척의 각기 다른 배와 말을 나누었는데, 그 모든 배마다 고래로 인한 죽음을 겪었고, 그중 더러는 한 번이 아니었으며, 세 척은 저마다 보트 한 척의 선원 전원을 잃은 터였다."], ["For God’s sake, be economical with your lamps and candles!", "제발 부탁하노니, 그대의 등잔과 양초를 아껴 쓰라!"], ["not a gallon you burn, but at least one drop of man’s blood was spilled for it.", "그대가 태우는 기름 한 갤런마다, 그것을 위해 적어도 사람의 피 한 방울은 흘려졌으니."]], [["Secondly: People ashore have indeed some indefinite idea that a whale is an enormous creature of enormous power; but I have ever found that when narrating to them some specific example of this two-fold enormousness, they have significantly complimented me upon my facetiousness; when, I declare upon my soul, I had no more idea of being facetious than Moses, when he wrote the history of the plagues of Egypt.", "둘째: 뭍의 사람들은 실로 고래가 어마어마한 힘을 지닌 어마어마한 생물이라는 막연한 관념 정도는 지니고 있다. 그러나 내가 그들에게 이 이중의 어마어마함을 보여 주는 어떤 구체적인 사례를 들려줄 때마다, 그들은 으레 내 익살스러움을 의미심장하게 추켜세우곤 했다. 그러나 내 영혼을 걸고 단언하건대, 나는 모세가 이집트의 재앙들에 관한 역사를 기록했을 때 그러했던 것 못지않게 익살을 부릴 생각이 조금도 없었다."]], [["But fortunately the special point I here seek can be established upon testimony entirely independent of my own.", "그러나 다행스럽게도 내가 여기서 밝히려는 특정한 요점은 전적으로 내 것이 아닌 증언 위에서 확립될 수 있다."], ["That point is this: The Sperm Whale is in some cases sufficiently powerful, knowing, and judiciously malicious, as with direct aforethought to stave in, utterly destroy, and sink a large ship; and what is more, the Sperm Whale has done it.", "그 요점이란 이것이다. 향유고래는 어떤 경우에는 충분히 강력하고, 영리하며, 판단력을 갖춘 악의로써, 직접적인 사전 계획 아래 커다란 배 한 척을 들이받아 완전히 부수고 침몰시킬 만큼 강하다는 것, 그리고 더 나아가, 향유고래가 실제로 그런 일을 해냈다는 것이다."]], [["First: In the year 1820 the ship Essex, Captain Pollard, of Nantucket, was cruising in the Pacific Ocean.", "첫째: 1820년에 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 폴러드 선장이 지휘하는 에식스호가 태평양에서 항해하고 있었다."], ["One day she saw spouts, lowered her boats, and gave chase to a shoal of sperm whales.", "어느 날 그 배는 물줄기를 발견하고 보트들을 내려 향유고래 떼를 추격했다."], ["Ere long, several of the whales were wounded; when, suddenly, a very large whale escaping from the boats, issued from the shoal, and bore directly down upon the ship.", "머지않아 고래 여러 마리가 부상을 입었는데, 그때 별안간 아주 커다란 고래 한 마리가 보트들로부터 빠져나와 무리에서 솟구쳐 나오더니 곧장 배를 향해 돌진해 왔다. 그놈은 자기 이마를 배의 선체에 들이박아 어찌나 심하게 부숴 놓았던지, \"십 분\"도 채 안 되어 배는 가라앉으며 옆으로 쓰러져 버렸다."], ["Dashing his forehead against her hull, he so stove her in, that in less than “ten minutes”", "그 뒤로 그 배의 멀쩡한 널빤지 한 조각도 보인 일이 없었다. 가장 혹독한 노출을 견뎌 낸 끝에 선원 일부가 보트를 타고 뭍에 닿았다."], ["she settled down and fell over. Not a surviving plank of her has been seen since.", "마침내 본국으로 돌아온 폴러드 선장은 또 다른 배를 지휘하여 다시 한번 태평양으로 항해를 떠났으나, 신들은 그를 미지의 암초와 부서지는 파도 위에서 또다시 난파시켰다."], ["After the severest exposure, part of the crew reached the land in their boats.", "두 번째로 그의 배가 완전히 잃어버렸고, 그는 곧장 바다와 인연을 끊겠노라 맹세하고는 그 뒤로 다시는 바다를 넘보지 않았다."], ["Being returned home at last, Captain Pollard once more sailed for the Pacific in command of another ship, but the gods shipwrecked him again upon unknown rocks and breakers; for the second time his ship was utterly lost, and forthwith forswearing the sea, he has never tempted it since.", "오늘날 폴러드 선장은 낸터킷에 거주하고 있다. 나는 그 비극이 일어났을 당시 에식스호의 일등 항해사였던 오언 체이스를 만나 본 적이 있다."], ["At this day Captain Pollard is a resident of Nantucket.", "나는 그의 꾸밈없고 충실한 기록을 읽어 보았다."], ["I have seen Owen Chace, who was chief mate of the Essex at the time of the tragedy; I have read his plain and faithful narrative; I have conversed with his son; and all this within a few miles of the scene of the catastrophe.*", "나는 그의 아들과 이야기를 나누어 보았다. 그리고 이 모든 일이 그 대참사의 현장에서 불과 몇 마일 안쪽에서 이루어졌다.*"]], [["*The following are extracts from Chace’s narrative: “Every fact seemed to warrant me in concluding that it was anything but chance which directed his operations; he made two several attacks upon the ship, at a short interval between them, both of which, according to their direction, were calculated to do us the most injury, by being made ahead, and thereby combining the speed of the two objects for the shock; to effect which, the exact manœuvres which he made were necessary.", "* 다음은 체이스의 기록에서 발췌한 것이다. \"모든 사실이 그놈의 행동을 이끈 것이 결코 우연 따위가 아니라는 결론으로 나를 몰아가는 듯했다."], ["His aspect was most horrible, and such as indicated resentment and fury.", "그놈은 짧은 간격을 두고 배에 두 차례 따로 공격을 가했는데, 그 방향으로 미루어 보건대 두 번 다 정면에서 이루어짐으로써 두 물체의 속도를 충돌에 합치도록 계산된 것이어서 우리에게 가장 큰 손상을 입히도록 되어 있었으니, 그 일을 이루기 위해서는 그놈이 행한 바로 그 정확한 기동이 필요했다."], ["He came directly from the shoal which we had just before entered, and in which we had struck three of his companions, as if fired with revenge for their sufferings.”", "그 형상은 더없이 끔찍했으며, 원한과 분노를 드러내는 듯한 것이었다. 그놈은 우리가 바로 직전에 들어갔던, 그리고 우리가 그놈의 동료 셋을 작살로 찌른 바로 그 무리에서 곧장 나왔으니, 마치 동료들이 겪은 고통에 대한 복수심에 불타오르는 듯했다.\""], ["Again: “At all events, the whole circumstances taken together, all happening before my own eyes, and producing, at the time, impressions in my mind of decided, calculating mischief, on the part of the whale (many of which impressions I cannot now recall), induce me to be satisfied that I am correct in my opinion.”", "또 이렇게 적었다. \"어쨌거나, 모든 정황을 한데 모아 보면, 그 모든 것이 내 눈앞에서 벌어졌고, 또 그 당시 내 마음속에 고래 쪽의 단호하고 계산된 악의(그 인상들 가운데 많은 것을 나는 지금 다 떠올릴 수 없다)라는 인상을 자아냈으니, 그것들이 나로 하여금 내 견해가 옳다고 확신하게 만든다.\""]], [["Here are his reflections some time after quitting the ship, during a black night in an open boat, when almost despairing of reaching any hospitable shore.", "다음은 그가 배를 떠난 뒤 한동안, 어느 환대해 줄 해안에라도 닿을 수 있을지 거의 절망에 빠진 채 칠흑 같은 밤에 지붕도 없는 보트 위에서 떠올린 상념들이다. \"어두운 바다와 부풀어 오르는 물결은 아무것도 아니었다."], ["“The dark ocean and swelling waters were nothing; the fears of being swallowed up by some dreadful tempest, or dashed upon hidden rocks, with all the other ordinary subjects of fearful contemplation, seemed scarcely entitled to a moment’s thought; the dismal looking wreck, and the horrid aspect and revenge of the whale, wholly engrossed my reflections, until day again made its appearance.”", "어떤 무시무시한 폭풍에 삼켜지지나 않을까, 숨은 암초에 부딪혀 부서지지나 않을까 하는 두려움과, 그 밖에 두려움을 자아내는 온갖 평범한 상념들은 한순간의 생각조차 할 가치가 없는 듯했다. 음산해 보이는 난파선의 잔해와, 그 고래의 끔찍한 형상과 복수심이 온통 내 상념을 사로잡았으니, 마침내 다시 날이 밝아 올 때까지 그러했다.\""]], [["In another place—p. 45,—he speaks of “the mysterious and mortal attack of the animal.”", "또 다른 대목에서 — 45쪽에서 — 그는 \"그 짐승의 불가사의하고 치명적인 공격\"을 이야기한다."]], [["Secondly: The ship Union, also of Nantucket, was in the year 1807 totally lost off the Azores by a similar onset, but the authentic particulars of this catastrophe I have never chanced to encounter, though from the whale hunters I have now and then heard casual allusions to it.", "둘째: 역시 낸터킷 소속인 유니언호가 1807년에 비슷한 습격으로 아조레스 앞바다에서 완전히 침몰했는데, 이 대참사의 믿을 만한 세부 사항을 나는 한 번도 우연히 마주친 적이 없다. 다만 고래잡이들로부터 이따금 그에 대한 우발적인 언급을 들은 일은 있다."]], [["Thirdly: Some eighteen or twenty years ago Commodore J——, then commanding an American sloop-of-war of the first class, happened to be dining with a party of whaling captains, on board a Nantucket ship in the harbor of Oahu, Sandwich Islands.", "셋째: 한 십팔 년에서 이십 년쯤 전에, 당시 미국 일급 전투용 슬루프함을 지휘하던 J 준장이 마침 샌드위치 제도 오아후 항구에 정박한 어느 낸터킷 배에서 여러 고래잡이 선장들과 함께 식사를 하게 되었다."], ["Conversation turning upon whales, the Commodore was pleased to be sceptical touching the amazing strength ascribed to them by the professional gentlemen present.", "이야기가 고래에 미치자, 준장은 그 자리에 있던 직업적인 신사들이 고래에 부여하는 그 놀라운 힘에 대해 기꺼이 회의적인 태도를 보였다."], ["He peremptorily denied for example, that any whale could so smite his stout sloop-of-war as to cause her to leak so much as a thimbleful.", "예컨대 그는 어떤 고래가 자신의 튼튼한 슬루프함을 들이받아 골무 한 개분만큼의 물이라도 새게 할 수 있다는 것을 단호히 부정했다. 좋다."], ["Very good; but there is more coming.", "그러나 뒷이야기가 더 남아 있다."], ["Some weeks after, the Commodore set sail in this impregnable craft for Valparaiso.", "몇 주 뒤, 준장은 이 난공불락의 함선을 타고 발파라이소를 향해 출항했다."], ["But he was stopped on the way by a portly sperm whale, that begged a few moments’ confidential business with him.", "그러나 그는 도중에 어느 풍채 좋은 향유고래에게 가로막혔으니, 그놈은 잠깐 동안 그와 은밀한 볼일이 있다고 청해 온 셈이었다."], ["That business consisted in fetching the Commodore’s craft such a thwack, that with all his pumps going he made straight for the nearest port to heave down and repair.", "그 볼일이란 준장의 함선에 어찌나 세찬 일격을 가하는 것이었던지, 준장은 펌프란 펌프를 모조리 가동하면서도 곧장 가장 가까운 항구로 달려가 배를 기울여 수리할 수밖에 없었다."], ["I am not superstitious, but I consider the Commodore’s interview with that whale as providential.", "나는 미신을 믿는 사람이 아니지만, 그 고래와 준장의 면담을 천우신조라고 여긴다."], ["Was not Saul of Tarsus converted from unbelief by a similar fright?", "타르수스의 사울도 비슷한 공포로 인해 불신에서 회심하지 않았던가?"], ["I tell you, the sperm whale will stand no nonsense.", "내가 그대에게 말하노니, 향유고래는 어떤 허튼수작도 가만두지 않는다."]], [["I will now refer you to Langsdorff’s Voyages for a little circumstance in point, peculiarly interesting to the writer hereof.", "이제 나는 이 글을 쓰는 사람에게 특히 흥미로운, 여기에 꼭 들어맞는 자그마한 정황 하나를 위해 그대를 랑스도르프의 항해기로 안내하려 한다."], ["Langsdorff, you must know by the way, was attached to the Russian Admiral Krusenstern’s famous Discovery Expedition in the beginning of the present century.", "그런데 미리 알아 두어야 할 것은, 랑스도르프가 금세기 초에 러시아 제독 크루젠슈테른의 이름난 발견 탐험대에 소속되어 있었다는 사실이다."], ["Captain Langsdorff thus begins his seventeenth chapter:", "랑스도르프 선장은 그 열일곱 번째 장(章)을 이렇게 시작한다."]], [["“By the thirteenth of May our ship was ready to sail, and the next day we were out in the open sea, on our way to Ochotsh.", "\"5월 13일까지 우리 배는 출항할 채비를 마쳤고, 이튿날 우리는 망망대해로 나아가 오호츠크를 향해 가는 길에 올랐다."], ["The weather was very clear and fine, but so intolerably cold that we were obliged to keep on our fur clothing.", "날씨는 매우 맑고 좋았으나, 어찌나 견딜 수 없이 추웠던지 우리는 모피옷을 계속 걸치고 있을 수밖에 없었다."], ["For some days we had very little wind; it was not till the nineteenth that a brisk gale from the northwest sprang up.", "며칠 동안 바람이 거의 없다가, 19일이 되어서야 비로소 북서쪽에서 세찬 강풍이 불어왔다."], ["An uncommon large whale, the body of which was larger than the ship itself, lay almost at the surface of the water, but was not perceived by any one on board till the moment when the ship, which was in full sail, was almost upon him, so that it was impossible to prevent its striking against him.", "몸집이 배 자체보다도 더 큰, 보기 드물게 거대한 고래 한 마리가 수면 바로 아래에 누워 있었으나, 돛을 활짝 편 배가 그놈을 거의 덮칠 지경에 이른 그 순간까지 배에 탄 누구도 그것을 알아차리지 못했으니, 그놈과 부딪치는 것을 막기란 불가능했다."], ["We were thus placed in the most imminent danger, as this gigantic creature, setting up its back, raised the ship three feet at least out of the water.", "이리하여 우리는 더없이 절박한 위험에 처하게 되었으니, 이 거대한 생물이 등을 곧추세우는 바람에 배가 물 밖으로 적어도 삼 피트(약 0.9미터)는 들어 올려졌기 때문이다."], ["The masts reeled, and the sails fell altogether, while we who were below all sprang instantly upon the deck, concluding that we had struck upon some rock; instead of this we saw the monster sailing off with the utmost gravity and solemnity.", "돛대들이 휘청거렸고 돛들이 한꺼번에 내려앉았으며, 아래에 있던 우리는 우리가 어떤 암초에 부딪힌 것이라 단정 짓고 모두 즉시 갑판 위로 뛰쳐 올라갔다. 그러나 그게 아니라, 우리는 그 괴물이 더없는 위엄과 엄숙함을 갖추고 떠나가는 모습을 보았다."], ["Captain D’Wolf applied immediately to the pumps to examine whether or not the vessel had received any damage from the shock, but we found that very happily it had escaped entirely uninjured.”", "더울프 선장은 충돌로 배가 손상을 입었는지 어떤지를 살피려 즉시 펌프를 가동시켰으나, 우리는 매우 다행스럽게도 배가 아무런 손상도 입지 않고 온전히 벗어났음을 알게 되었다.\""]], [["Now, the Captain D’Wolf here alluded to as commanding the ship in question, is a New Englander, who, after a long life of unusual adventures as a sea-captain, this day resides in the village of Dorchester near Boston.", "자, 여기서 문제의 배를 지휘하던 인물로 언급된 더울프 선장은 뉴잉글랜드 사람으로, 바다 선장으로서 보기 드문 모험으로 점철된 긴 생애를 보낸 뒤 오늘날 보스턴 근처 도체스터 마을에 거주하고 있다."], ["I have the honor of being a nephew of his.", "나는 영광스럽게도 그의 조카다."], ["I have particularly questioned him concerning this passage in Langsdorff.", "나는 랑스도르프의 이 대목에 관해 그에게 특별히 물어보았다."], ["He substantiates every word.", "그는 한마디 한마디를 다 사실이라고 확인해 주었다."], ["The ship, however, was by no means a large one: a Russian craft built on the Siberian coast, and purchased by my uncle after bartering away the vessel in which he sailed from home.", "다만 그 배는 결코 큰 배가 아니었으니, 시베리아 해안에서 건조된 러시아 선박으로, 외삼촌이 고향에서 타고 떠났던 배와 맞바꾼 뒤에 사들인 것이었다."]], [["In that up and down manly book of old-fashioned adventure, so full, too, of honest wonders—the voyage of Lionel Wafer, one of ancient Dampier’s old chums—I found a little matter set down so like that just quoted from Langsdorff, that I cannot forbear inserting it here for a corroborative example, if such be needed.", "옛 댐피어의 오랜 벗 가운데 하나인 라이오넬 웨이퍼의 항해기 — 진솔한 경이들로 가득 찬, 거침없고 사내다운 그 구식 모험담 — 에서 나는 방금 랑스도르프에게서 인용한 것과 너무도 흡사한 자그마한 일화 하나를 발견했으니, 혹시라도 그런 것이 필요하다면 보강하는 사례로 삼고자 여기 끼워 넣지 않을 수 없다."]], [["Lionel, it seems, was on his way to “John Ferdinando,” as he calls the modern Juan Fernandes.", "라이오넬은 보아하니 그가 \"존 페르디난도\"라 부르는 곳, 곧 오늘날의 후안 페르난데스로 가는 길이었던 모양이다."], ["“In our way thither,”", "\"그리로 가는 도중에,\""], ["he says, “about four o’clock in the morning, when we were about one hundred and fifty leagues from the Main of America, our ship felt a terrible shock, which put our men in such consternation that they could hardly tell where they were or what to think; but every one began to prepare for death.", "그는 이렇게 말한다, \"새벽 네 시쯤, 우리가 아메리카 본토에서 약 백오십 리그쯤 떨어져 있을 무렵, 우리 배가 끔찍한 충격을 느꼈으니, 그 바람에 우리 선원들은 어찌나 당황했던지 자기들이 어디에 있는지, 무엇을 어찌 생각해야 할지조차 거의 알 수 없을 지경이었다. 그리하여 저마다 죽음을 준비하기 시작했다."], ["And, indeed, the shock was so sudden and violent, that we took it for granted the ship had struck against a rock; but when the amazement was a little over, we cast the lead, and sounded, but found no ground.", "사실 그 충격이 어찌나 갑작스럽고 격렬했던지, 우리는 배가 암초에 부딪혔으려니 하고 당연히 여겼다. 그러나 놀라움이 좀 가시자 우리는 측연을 던져 수심을 재 보았으나, 바닥에 닿지 않았다."], ["* * * * * The suddenness of the shock made the guns leap in their carriages, and several of the men were shaken out of their hammocks.", "* * * * * 그 충격이 어찌나 별안간이었던지 대포들이 포가(砲架) 위에서 펄쩍 뛰어올랐고, 여러 사람이 해먹에서 떨어져 나뒹굴었다."], ["Captain Davis, who lay with his head on a gun, was thrown out of his cabin!”", "머리를 대포에 베고 누워 있던 데이비스 선장은 선실 밖으로 내동댕이쳐졌다!\""], ["Lionel then goes on to impute the shock to an earthquake, and seems to substantiate the imputation by stating that a great earthquake, somewhere about that time, did actually do great mischief along the Spanish land.", "그러고 나서 라이오넬은 그 충격을 지진 탓으로 돌리며, 그 무렵 어디선가 큰 지진이 실제로 일어나 스페인 땅 일대에 큰 피해를 입혔다고 진술함으로써 그 추정을 뒷받침하려는 듯하다."], ["But I should not much wonder if, in the darkness of that early hour of the morning, the shock was after all caused by an unseen whale vertically bumping the hull from beneath.", "그러나 그 이른 새벽의 어둠 속에서, 그 충격이 결국 보이지 않는 고래가 밑에서 선체를 수직으로 들이받아 일어난 것이라 한들, 나로서는 그다지 놀랄 일이 아닐 것이다."]], [["I might proceed with several more examples, one way or another known to me, of the great power and malice at times of the sperm whale.", "나는 이런저런 경로로 내가 알고 있는, 향유고래가 때때로 발휘하는 그 거대한 힘과 악의에 관한 사례를 몇 가지 더 들 수도 있을 것이다."], ["In more than one instance, he has been known, not only to chase the assailing boats back to their ships, but to pursue the ship itself, and long withstand all the lances hurled at him from its decks.", "한 번 이상의 경우에서 그놈은 공격해 오는 보트들을 그 모선까지 쫓아 보내는 데 그치지 않고, 배 자체를 추격하며 갑판에서 자신을 향해 던지는 모든 작살을 오래도록 버텨 낸 것으로 알려져 있다. 영국(English) 배 푸지 홀호가 그에 관한 이야기 한 토막을 들려줄 수 있을 것이다."], ["The English ship Pusie Hall can tell a story on that head; and, as for his strength, let me say, that there have been examples where the lines attached to a running sperm whale have, in a calm, been transferred to the ship, and secured there; the whale towing her great hull through the water, as a horse walks off with a cart.", "또한 그 힘으로 말하자면, 잔잔한 바다에서 달아나는 향유고래에 매인 밧줄이 배로 옮겨져 그곳에 단단히 묶이고는, 그 고래가 마치 말이 수레를 끌고 가듯 그 거대한 선체를 물살을 가르며 끌고 간 사례들이 있었다는 것을 말해 두고자 한다. 또한 한 번 작살에 찔린 향유고래가 다시 기운을 추스를 시간을 얻게 되면, 그놈은 흔히 눈먼 분노로 행동하기보다 추격자들을 파괴하려는 고의적이고 신중한 의도로 행동한다는 사실이 매우 자주 목격된다."], ["Again, it is very often observed that, if the sperm whale, once struck, is allowed time to rally, he then acts, not so often with blind rage, as with wilful, deliberate designs of destruction to his pursuers; nor is it without conveying some eloquent indication of his character, that upon being attacked he will frequently open his mouth, and retain it in that dread expansion for several consecutive minutes.", "또한 그놈이 공격을 받으면 자주 입을 벌리고는 그 무시무시하게 벌어진 상태를 몇 분 동안 잇따라 유지한다는 것도, 그 성정에 관한 어떤 웅변적인 징표를 전해 주지 않는 것이 아니다. 그러나 나는 단 하나의 사례를, 그것도 마무리 짓는 예증을 드는 것으로 만족해야겠다."], ["But I must be content with only one more and a concluding illustration; a remarkable and most significant one, by which you will not fail to see, that not only is the most marvellous event in this book corroborated by plain facts of the present day, but that these marvels (like all marvels) are mere repetitions of the ages; so that for the millionth time we say amen with Solomon—Verily there is nothing new under the sun.", "그것은 주목할 만하고 더없이 의미심장한 사례로서, 그것을 통해 그대는 이 책에서 가장 경이로운 사건이 오늘날의 평범한 사실들로 뒷받침될 뿐 아니라, 이 경이들이 (모든 경이가 그러하듯) 시대를 거듭한 한낱 반복일 따름이라는 것을 어김없이 보게 될 것이다. 그리하여 우리는 백만 번째로 솔로몬과 더불어 아멘이라 말하노니 — 진실로 해 아래 새것이 없도다."]], [["In the sixth Christian century lived Procopius, a Christian magistrate of Constantinople, in the days when Justinian was Emperor and Belisarius general.", "서력 6세기에 프로코피우스라는 사람이 살았으니, 그는 유스티니아누스가 황제요 벨리사리우스가 장군이던 시절, 콘스탄티노플의 그리스도교 행정관이었다."], ["As many know, he wrote the history of his own times, a work every way of uncommon value.", "많은 이들이 알다시피, 그는 자기 시대의 역사를 기록했는데, 그것은 여러모로 보기 드문 가치를 지닌 저작이다."], ["By the best authorities, he has always been considered a most trustworthy and unexaggerating historian, except in some one or two particulars, not at all affecting the matter presently to be mentioned.", "가장 권위 있는 전거들에 따르면, 그는 한두 가지 세부 사항을 제외하고는 — 그것들은 지금 언급하려는 사안에 전혀 영향을 미치지 않는다 — 늘 더없이 믿을 만하고 과장하지 않는 역사가로 여겨져 왔다."]], [["Now, in this history of his, Procopius mentions that, during the term of his prefecture at Constantinople, a great sea-monster was captured in the neighboring Propontis, or Sea of Marmora, after having destroyed vessels at intervals in those waters for a period of more than fifty years.", "자, 그가 쓴 이 역사 속에서 프로코피우스는, 자신이 콘스탄티노플의 행정관으로 재직하던 기간에, 인근 프로폰티스, 곧 마르마라해에서 거대한 바다 괴물 한 마리가 사로잡혔다고 언급한다. 그놈은 그 바다에서 오십 년이 넘는 세월에 걸쳐 이따금 배들을 파괴해 온 끝에 잡힌 것이었다."], ["A fact thus set down in substantial history cannot easily be gainsaid.", "이처럼 견실한 역사 속에 적혀 있는 사실은 쉽사리 부인할 수 없다."], ["Nor is there any reason it should be.", "또 그래야 할 까닭도 없다."], ["Of what precise species this sea-monster was, is not mentioned.", "이 바다 괴물이 정확히 어떤 종류였는지는 언급되어 있지 않다."], ["But as he destroyed ships, as well as for other reasons, he must have been a whale; and I am strongly inclined to think a sperm whale.", "그러나 그놈이 배들을 파괴했다는 점에서, 또 다른 여러 이유에서, 그것은 틀림없이 고래였을 것이며, 나는 그것이 향유고래였으리라고 강하게 기우는 바다."], ["And I will tell you why.", "그 까닭을 말해 주겠다."], ["For a long time I fancied that the sperm whale had been always unknown in the Mediterranean and the deep waters connecting with it.", "오랫동안 나는 향유고래가 지중해와 그에 이어지는 깊은 바다에서는 늘 알려진 바가 없었으리라 짐작했다."], ["Even now I am certain that those seas are not, and perhaps never can be, in the present constitution of things, a place for his habitual gregarious resort.", "지금도 나는 그 바다들이, 현재의 사물 질서 안에서는, 그놈이 습관적으로 무리 지어 즐겨 찾는 곳이 아니며, 어쩌면 결코 그런 곳이 될 수 없으리라고 확신한다."], ["But further investigations have recently proved to me, that in modern times there have been isolated instances of the presence of the sperm whale in the Mediterranean.", "그러나 더 깊은 조사를 통해 최근에 나는, 근래에 들어 지중해에 향유고래가 출현한 고립된 사례들이 있었음을 알게 되었다."], ["I am told, on good authority, that on the Barbary coast, a Commodore Davis of the British navy found the skeleton of a sperm whale.", "나는 믿을 만한 전거에 따라, 바르바리 해안에서 영국 해군의 데이비스 준장이 향유고래의 골격을 발견했다는 이야기를 들었다."], ["Now, as a vessel of war readily passes through the Dardanelles, hence a sperm whale could, by the same route, pass out of the Mediterranean into the Propontis.", "자, 전투용 함선이 다르다넬스 해협을 손쉽게 통과하니, 향유고래 또한 같은 경로를 따라 지중해에서 프로폰티스로 빠져나갈 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["In the Propontis, as far as I can learn, none of that peculiar substance called brit is to be found, the aliment of the right whale.", "내가 알아낼 수 있는 한, 프로폰티스에서는 참고래의 먹이인, 브릿(brit)이라 불리는 그 특이한 물질이 전혀 발견되지 않는다."], ["But I have every reason to believe that the food of the sperm whale—squid or cuttle-fish—lurks at the bottom of that sea, because large creatures, but by no means the largest of that sort, have been found at its surface.", "그러나 나는 향유고래의 먹이인 오징어 또는 갑오징어가 그 바다 밑바닥에 숨어 있으리라고 믿을 만한 온갖 근거를 지니고 있으니, 그것은 그런 종류 가운데 결코 가장 큰 것은 아니지만 커다란 생물들이 그 수면에서 발견된 적이 있기 때문이다."], ["If, then, you properly put these statements together, and reason upon them a bit, you will clearly perceive that, according to all human reasoning, Procopius’s sea-monster, that for half a century stove the ships of a Roman Emperor, must in all probability have been a sperm whale.", "그러니 그대가 이러한 진술들을 제대로 한데 모아 조금만 따져 본다면, 인간의 모든 추론에 비추어, 반세기 동안 어느 로마 황제의 배들을 부수었던 프로코피우스의 그 바다 괴물이 십중팔구 향유고래였음에 틀림없다는 것을 그대는 명확히 깨닫게 될 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 46. Surmises.", "# CHAPTER 46. Surmises."]], [["Though, consumed with the hot fire of his purpose, Ahab in all his thoughts and actions ever had in view the ultimate capture of Moby Dick; though he seemed ready to sacrifice all mortal interests to that one passion; nevertheless it may have been that he was by nature and long habituation far too wedded to a fiery whaleman’s ways, altogether to abandon the collateral prosecution of the voyage.", "비록 에이해브는 자신의 목적이 지핀 뜨거운 불길에 휩싸여, 그 모든 생각과 행동에서 늘 모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 끝내 사로잡는 일을 염두에 두었고, 그 한 가지 열정을 위해서라면 인간으로서의 온갖 이해관계를 기꺼이 희생할 듯이 보였지만, 그럼에도 불구하고 그는 본성과 오랜 습관으로 말미암아 불같은 고래잡이의 방식에 너무도 깊이 매여 있었던 까닭에, 항해의 부수적인 본업을 통째로 저버리지는 못했던 것이리라."], ["Or at least if this were otherwise, there were not wanting other motives much more influential with him.", "혹은 적어도 사정이 그렇지 않았다 하더라도, 그를 훨씬 더 강하게 움직이는 다른 동기들이 없지 않았다."], ["It would be refining too much, perhaps, even considering his monomania, to hint that his vindictiveness towards the White Whale might have possibly extended itself in some degree to all sperm whales, and that the more monsters he slew by so much the more he multiplied the chances that each subsequently encountered whale would prove to be the hated one he hunted.", "그의 편집광적 광기를 감안한다 해도, 어쩌면 그가 흰 고래를 향해 품은 복수심이 어느 정도는 모든 향유고래에게로까지 번져 나갔을지 모른다고, 그래서 더 많은 괴물을 죽일수록 그만큼 더, 이후에 마주치는 고래마다 자기가 쫓는 그 증오스러운 놈으로 드러날 가능성을 늘려 갔던 것이라고 넌지시 말하는 것은 지나치게 세밀한 해석일 터다."], ["But if such an hypothesis be indeed exceptionable, there were still additional considerations which, though not so strictly according with the wildness of his ruling passion, yet were by no means incapable of swaying him.", "그러나 그러한 가설이 설령 받아들이기 어렵다 한들, 비록 그를 지배하는 열정의 광포함과는 그리 엄밀히 맞아떨어지지 않으나 결코 그를 흔들지 못할 것도 아닌, 또 다른 고려 사항들이 여전히 남아 있었다."]], [["To accomplish his object Ahab must use tools; and of all tools used in the shadow of the moon, men are most apt to get out of order.", "자신의 목적을 이루기 위해 에이해브는 도구를 써야만 했다. 그런데 달빛 아래에서 쓰이는 온갖 도구 가운데 가장 탈이 나기 쉬운 것이 바로 사람이다."], ["He knew, for example, that however magnetic his ascendency in some respects was over Starbuck, yet that ascendency did not cover the complete spiritual man any more than mere corporeal superiority involves intellectual mastership; for to the purely spiritual, the intellectual but stand in a sort of corporeal relation.", "이를테면 그는, 어떤 면에서 자기가 스타벅(Starbuck)에게 자석처럼 끌어당기는 우위를 지녔다 하더라도, 그 우위가 오로지 육체적 우월이 곧 지적 지배를 뜻하지 않는 것과 마찬가지로 영적인 인간 전체를 덮지는 못한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 순수하게 영적인 것에 비하면 지적인 것이란 일종의 육체적 관계에 머무를 뿐이기 때문이다."], ["Starbuck’s body and Starbuck’s coerced will were Ahab’s, so long as Ahab kept his magnet at Starbuck’s brain; still he knew that for all this the chief mate, in his soul, abhorred his captain’s quest, and could he, would joyfully disintegrate himself from it, or even frustrate it.", "에이해브가 스타벅의 머릿속에 자석을 들이대고 있는 한, 스타벅의 몸과 억눌린 의지는 에이해브의 것이었다. 그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 일등항해사가 마음속으로는 선장의 추적을 혐오하고 있으며, 할 수만 있다면 거기서 기꺼이 자신을 떼어 내거나 심지어 그 일을 좌절시키려 들리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["It might be that a long interval would elapse ere the White Whale was seen.", "흰 고래가 모습을 드러내기까지 오랜 시간이 흐를 수도 있었다."], ["During that long interval Starbuck would ever be apt to fall into open relapses of rebellion against his captain’s leadership, unless some ordinary, prudential, circumstantial influences were brought to bear upon him.", "그 긴 기간 동안, 평범하고 신중하며 정황에 맞는 어떤 영향력이 그에게 가해지지 않는 한, 스타벅은 언제든 선장의 지휘에 맞서 공공연히 반항의 도로 빠져들기 쉬울 터였다."], ["Not only that, but the subtle insanity of Ahab respecting Moby Dick was noways more significantly manifested than in his superlative sense and shrewdness in foreseeing that, for the present, the hunt should in some way be stripped of that strange imaginative impiousness which naturally invested it; that the full terror of the voyage must be kept withdrawn into the obscure background (for few men’s courage is proof against protracted meditation unrelieved by action); that when they stood their long night watches, his officers and men must have some nearer things to think of than Moby Dick.", "그뿐만 아니라, 모비 딕에 관한 에이해브의 교묘한 광기는, 당분간 이 추적에서 그것을 자연히 휘감고 있던 저 기이하고 환상적인 불경함을 어떻게든 벗겨 내야 한다는 것을 그가 더없이 예민하고 빈틈없는 감각으로 내다보았다는 점에서 가장 뚜렷이 드러났다. 곧 이 항해의 온전한 공포는 어두운 배경 속으로 물러나 있게 해야 했고(행동으로 풀리지 않는 채 오래 이어지는 사색을 견뎌 낼 만큼 굳센 용기를 지닌 사람은 드물기 때문이다), 그들이 긴 밤 당직을 설 때면 그의 부하 장교들과 선원들은 모비 딕보다 더 가까운 무언가를 생각거리로 가져야만 했다."], ["For however eagerly and impetuously the savage crew had hailed the announcement of his quest; yet all sailors of all sorts are more or less capricious and unreliable—they live in the varying outer weather, and they inhale its fickleness—and when retained for any object remote and blank in the pursuit, however promissory of life and passion in the end, it is above all things requisite that temporary interests and employments should intervene and hold them healthily suspended for the final dash.", "아무리 그 야만스러운 선원들이 그의 추적 선언을 열렬하고도 격렬하게 환호로 맞이했다 한들, 온갖 부류의 모든 뱃사람이란 다소간 변덕스럽고 미덥지 못한 법이어서 — 그들은 끊임없이 변하는 바깥 날씨 속에 살며 그 변덕을 들이마시므로 — 끝내 가서는 생명과 열정을 약속하는 일이라 할지라도, 추구하는 동안에는 아득하고 막연한 어떤 목적을 위해 붙들어 둘 때에는, 무엇보다도 임시적인 관심거리와 일거리가 그 사이에 끼어들어 그들을 마지막 돌진을 위해 건강하게 매달려 있도록 유지하는 것이 반드시 필요했다."]], [["Nor was Ahab unmindful of another thing.", "또한 에이해브는 다른 한 가지도 잊지 않고 있었다."], ["In times of strong emotion mankind disdain all base considerations; but such times are evanescent.", "강렬한 감정의 순간에는 인간이 온갖 비천한 셈속을 경멸한다. 그러나 그러한 순간은 덧없이 사라진다."], ["The permanent constitutional condition of the manufactured man, thought Ahab, is sordidness.", "만들어진 인간의 영속적이고 본질적인 상태는 천박함이라고 에이해브는 생각했다."], ["Granting that the White Whale fully incites the hearts of this my savage crew, and playing round their savageness even breeds a certain generous knight-errantism in them, still, while for the love of it they give chase to Moby Dick, they must also have food for their more common, daily appetites.", "흰 고래가 이 야만스러운 내 선원들의 가슴을 온전히 부추기고, 그들의 야만성 둘레를 맴돌며 그들에게 일종의 너그러운 기사도적 모험심마저 길러 낸다 치더라도, 그들이 그것을 사랑하여 모비 딕을 뒤쫓는 동안에도 그들은 더 흔하고 일상적인 욕구를 채울 양식 또한 가져야만 했다."], ["For even the high lifted and chivalric Crusaders of old times were not content to traverse two thousand miles of land to fight for their holy sepulchre, without committing burglaries, picking pockets, and gaining other pious perquisites by the way.", "옛날의 드높고 의로운 십자군조차도, 그 거룩한 성묘를 위해 싸우려고 이천 마일의 땅을 가로지르면서도 가는 길에 도둑질을 하고, 소매치기를 하고, 그 밖의 경건한 부수입을 챙기지 않고서는 만족하지 못했다."], ["Had they been strictly held to their one final and romantic object—that final and romantic object, too many would have turned from in disgust.", "만약 그들이 단 하나의 최종적이고 낭만적인 목적에만 엄격히 매여 있었다면 — 너무도 많은 이들이 그 최종적이고 낭만적인 목적을 혐오스러워하며 등을 돌렸으리라."], ["I will not strip these men, thought Ahab, of all hopes of cash—aye, cash.", "나는 이 사람들에게서 현금에 대한 온갖 희망을 — 그렇다, 현금에 대한 희망을 — 빼앗지 않으리라고 에이해브는 생각했다."], ["They may scorn cash now; but let some months go by, and no perspective promise of it to them, and then this same quiescent cash all at once mutinying in them, this same cash would soon cashier Ahab.", "그들은 지금이야 현금을 비웃을지 모른다. 그러나 몇 달이 흐르고 그들에게 현금에 대한 어떤 전망의 약속도 없다면, 그때는 그 잠잠하던 현금이 한꺼번에 그들 속에서 반란을 일으켜, 바로 그 현금이 머지않아 에이해브를 면직시켜 버릴 것이다."]], [["Nor was there wanting still another precautionary motive more related to Ahab personally.", "게다가 에이해브 자신과 더 직접적으로 관련된, 또 하나의 예방적 동기도 없지 않았다."], ["Having impulsively, it is probable, and perhaps somewhat prematurely revealed the prime but private purpose of the Pequod’s voyage, Ahab was now entirely conscious that, in so doing, he had indirectly laid himself open to the unanswerable charge of usurpation; and with perfect impunity, both moral and legal, his crew if so disposed, and to that end competent, could refuse all further obedience to him, and even violently wrest from him the command.", "아마도 충동적으로, 그리고 어쩌면 다소 성급하게 피쿼드호(Pequod) 항해의 으뜸가는 사적 목적을 드러내 버린 까닭에, 에이해브는 이제 그렇게 함으로써 자신이 권한 찬탈이라는 반박할 수 없는 비난에 간접적으로 스스로를 내맡긴 셈이라는 것을 온전히 의식하고 있었다. 그리하여 만약 그의 선원들이 그럴 마음을 먹고 또 그럴 만한 역량을 갖춘다면, 도덕적으로든 법적으로든 아무런 책임도 지지 않은 채 그에 대한 일체의 복종을 거부할 수도 있고, 심지어 폭력으로 지휘권을 빼앗아 갈 수도 있었다."], ["From even the barely hinted imputation of usurpation, and the possible consequences of such a suppressed impression gaining ground, Ahab must of course have been most anxious to protect himself.", "권한 찬탈이라는, 그저 넌지시 비치기만 한 혐의로부터도, 또 그렇게 억눌려 있던 인상이 점점 힘을 얻어 갈 때 빚어질 수 있는 결과로부터도, 에이해브는 물론 자신을 지키려고 더없이 애를 태웠을 것이 분명하다."], ["That protection could only consist in his own predominating brain and heart and hand, backed by a heedful, closely calculating attention to every minute atmospheric influence which it was possible for his crew to be subjected to.", "그러한 보호는 오로지, 자신의 압도적인 두뇌와 심장과 손으로써, 그리고 그의 선원들이 휩쓸릴 수 있는 온갖 미세한 분위기의 영향력 하나하나에까지 빈틈없고 면밀히 셈하는 주의를 기울이는 것으로써만 이루어질 수 있었다."]], [["For all these reasons then, and others perhaps too analytic to be verbally developed here, Ahab plainly saw that he must still in a good degree continue true to the natural, nominal purpose of the Pequod’s voyage; observe all customary usages; and not only that, but force himself to evince all his well known passionate interest in the general pursuit of his profession.", "그러니 이 모든 이유에서, 그리고 어쩌면 여기서 말로 풀어내기에는 너무 분석적인 다른 이유들에서, 에이해브는 자신이 여전히 상당한 정도로 피쿼드호 항해의 자연스럽고 명목상의 목적에 충실해야 하며, 온갖 관습적인 관행을 지켜야 하고, 그뿐만 아니라 자기 직업의 일반적인 추구에 대해 익히 알려진 자신의 열정적인 관심을 빠짐없이 내보이도록 스스로를 다그쳐야 한다는 것을 똑똑히 깨달았다."]], [["Be all this as it may, his voice was now often heard hailing the three mast-heads and admonishing them to keep a bright look-out, and not omit reporting even a porpoise.", "이 모든 사정이야 어떠하든, 이제 그가 세 개의 돛대 꼭대기를 향해 외치며 환히 망을 보라고, 돌고래 한 마리조차 보고하기를 빠뜨리지 말라고 타이르는 목소리가 자주 들려왔다."], ["This vigilance was not long without reward.", "이러한 경계심은 오래지 않아 보답을 받았다."]], [["# CHAPTER 47. The Mat-Maker.", "# CHAPTER 47. The Mat-Maker."]], [["It was a cloudy, sultry afternoon; the seamen were lazily lounging about the decks, or vacantly gazing over into the lead-coloured waters.", "흐리고 무더운 오후였다. 선원들은 갑판 여기저기에 게으르게 늘어져 있거나, 납빛 바닷물을 멍하니 굽어보고 있었다."], ["Queequeg and I were mildly employed weaving what is called a sword-mat, for an additional lashing to our boat.", "퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 나는 우리 보트에 덧댈 결박줄로 쓸, 이른바 검매트(sword-mat)라는 것을 짜며 한가로이 일하고 있었다."], ["So still and subdued and yet somehow preluding was all the scene, and such an incantation of reverie lurked in the air, that each silent sailor seemed resolved into his own invisible self.", "온 풍경이 어찌나 고요하고 차분하면서도 어딘가 무언가를 예고하는 듯하던지, 또 공기 속에는 그런 몽상의 주문이 깃들어 있어, 말없는 선원들 하나하나가 저마다 보이지 않는 제 자신 속으로 녹아드는 듯했다."]], [["I was the attendant or page of Queequeg, while busy at the mat.", "매트를 짜느라 분주한 동안 나는 퀴퀘그의 조수, 곧 시동(侍童) 노릇을 했다. 내가 날실(warp)을 이루는 기다란 실들 사이로 씨실(woof)이 될 가는 노끈을 손수 북 삼아 가며 거듭 오가게 하는 동안, 퀴퀘그는 옆으로 서서 이따금 묵직한 떡갈나무 검을 실 사이로 밀어 넣으며, 물을 무심히 바라보다가 아무렇게나 생각 없이 실마다 단단히 박아 넣었다."], ["As I kept passing and repassing the filling or woof of marline between the long yarns of the warp, using my own hand for the shuttle, and as Queequeg, standing sideways, ever and anon slid his heavy oaken sword between the threads, and idly looking off upon the water, carelessly and unthinkingly drove home every yarn: I say so strange a dreaminess did there then reign all over the ship and all over the sea, only broken by the intermitting dull sound of the sword, that it seemed as if this were the Loom of Time, and I myself were a shuttle mechanically weaving and weaving away at the Fates.", "그러니까 그때 온 배와 온 바다를 뒤덮은 그 기묘한 몽환은 오직 그 검이 간간이 내는 둔탁한 소리에만 깨어졌고, 그것은 마치 이곳이 시간의 베틀(Loom of Time)이며, 나 자신은 운명을 향해 기계적으로 짜고 또 짜 나가는 한 자루의 북인 것만 같았다. 거기 날실의 고정된 실들은 단 하나의, 늘 되돌아오는, 변함없는 진동에만 내맡겨져 있었고, 그 진동은 그저 다른 실들이 제 실과 가로질러 엮여 들 만큼만의 여지를 줄 뿐이었다."], ["There lay the fixed threads of the warp subject to but one single, ever returning, unchanging vibration, and that vibration merely enough to admit of the crosswise interblending of other threads with its own.", "이 날실은 필연인 듯했다. 그리고 여기서, 나는 생각했다, 나는 내 손으로 내 북을 놀려 이 바꿀 수 없는 실들 속에 내 운명을 짜 넣고 있는 것이다."], ["This warp seemed necessity; and here, thought I, with my own hand I ply my own shuttle and weave my own destiny into these unalterable threads.", "그러는 동안 퀴퀘그의 충동적이고 무심한 검은, 때로는 씨실을 비스듬히, 때로는 비뚤게, 때로는 세게, 때로는 약하게, 그때그때 형편대로 때렸다. 그리고 그 마지막 타격의 차이로 말미암아 완성된 직물의 최종 모습에 그에 상응하는 차이가 빚어졌다."], ["Meantime, Queequeg’s impulsive, indifferent sword, sometimes hitting the woof slantingly, or crookedly, or strongly, or weakly, as the case might be; and by this difference in the concluding blow producing a corresponding contrast in the final aspect of the completed fabric; this savage’s sword, thought I, which thus finally shapes and fashions both warp and woof; this easy, indifferent sword must be chance—aye, chance, free will, and necessity—nowise incompatible—all interweavingly working together.", "이리하여 날실과 씨실 둘 다를 끝내 빚어내고 모양 지우는 이 야만인의 검, 이 거침없고 무심한 검은 곧 우연임에 틀림없다고 나는 생각했다 — 그렇다, 우연과 자유의지와 필연, 이 셋은 결코 서로 어긋나지 않으며, 모두가 서로 엮이며 함께 일하는 것이다. 필연의 곧은 날실은 제 궁극의 행로에서 벗어날 수 없으며, 그 번갈아 오가는 진동 하나하나도 실은 오로지 그 행로를 향할 뿐이다."], ["The straight warp of necessity, not to be swerved from its ultimate course—its every alternating vibration, indeed, only tending to that; free will still free to ply her shuttle between given threads; and chance, though restrained in its play within the right lines of necessity, and sideways in its motions directed by free will, though thus prescribed to by both, chance by turns rules either, and has the last featuring blow at events.", "자유의지는 여전히 주어진 실들 사이로 제 북을 놀릴 자유를 누린다. 그리고 우연은, 비록 필연의 곧은 선들 안에서만 그 놀이가 허락되고, 그 옆걸음치는 움직임은 자유의지의 지시를 받아, 이처럼 양쪽 모두에게 규정을 받으면서도, 번갈아 가며 그 둘을 다스리고, 사건의 마지막 모양새를 결정짓는 타격을 가하는 것이다."]], [["Thus we were weaving and weaving away when I started at a sound so strange, long drawn, and musically wild and unearthly, that the ball of free will dropped from my hand, and I stood gazing up at the clouds whence that voice dropped like a wing.", "이렇게 우리가 짜고 또 짜 나가던 그때, 어찌나 기묘하고 길게 늘어지며, 음악처럼 거칠고 이 세상 것 같지 않은 소리가 들려왔던지, 나는 흠칫 놀라 자유의지의 실뭉치를 손에서 떨어뜨리고는, 그 목소리가 날개처럼 떨어져 내린 구름을 올려다보며 서 있었다."], ["High aloft in the cross-trees was that mad Gay-Header, Tashtego.", "가로돛대 높은 곳에는 저 미치광이 게이헤드 사람, 태시테고(Tashtego)가 있었다."], ["His body was reaching eagerly forward, his hand stretched out like a wand, and at brief sudden intervals he continued his cries.", "그의 몸은 간절하게 앞으로 뻗어 나가 있었고, 손은 지팡이처럼 뻗쳐 있었으며, 짧고 갑작스러운 간격마다 그는 외침을 이어 갔다."], ["To be sure the same sound was that very moment perhaps being heard all over the seas, from hundreds of whalemen’s look-outs perched as high in the air; but from few of those lungs could that accustomed old cry have derived such a marvellous cadence as from Tashtego the Indian’s.", "분명 그 똑같은 소리는 바로 그 순간 온 바다 곳곳에서, 그처럼 높은 허공에 올라앉은 수백 명의 고래잡이 망꾼들에게서 들려오고 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그 익숙한 옛 외침을, 인디언 태시테고에게서 나오는 것만큼 그토록 경이로운 가락으로 빚어낼 수 있는 폐(肺)는 그중에서도 몇 되지 않았다."]], [["As he stood hovering over you half suspended in air, so wildly and eagerly peering towards the horizon, you would have thought him some prophet or seer beholding the shadows of Fate, and by those wild cries announcing their coming.", "그가 허공에 반쯤 매달린 채 그대 머리 위에 떠 있듯 서서, 그처럼 거칠고 간절하게 수평선 쪽을 응시하는 모습을 보았다면, 그대는 그를 운명의 그림자를 바라보며 그 거친 외침으로 그 도래를 알리는 어떤 예언자나 선견자라 여겼을 것이다."]], [["“There she blows!", "\"저기 물을 뿜는다!"], ["there!", "저기!"], ["there!", "저기!"], ["there!", "저기!"], ["she blows!", "물을 뿜는다!"], ["she blows!”", "물을 뿜어!\""]], [["“Where-away?”", "\"어느 쪽이냐?\""]], [["“On the lee-beam, about two miles off!", "\"바람 불어가는 쪽 옆으로, 한 이 마일쯤 떨어져서!"], ["a school of them!”", "떼로 몰려 있어요!\""]], [["Instantly all was commotion.", "순식간에 온통 야단법석이 되었다."]], [["The Sperm Whale blows as a clock ticks, with the same undeviating and reliable uniformity.", "향유고래(Sperm Whale)는 시계가 째깍거리듯, 똑같이 어김없고 믿음직한 규칙으로 물을 뿜는다."], ["And thereby whalemen distinguish this fish from other tribes of his genus.", "고래잡이들은 바로 이로써 이 고래를 같은 속(屬)의 다른 무리들과 구별해 낸다."]], [["“There go flukes!”", "\"꼬리지느러미가 내려간다!\""], ["was now the cry from Tashtego; and the whales disappeared.", "이제 태시테고에게서 이런 외침이 들려왔다. 그리고 고래들은 사라져 버렸다."]], [["“Quick, steward!”", "\"빨리, 급사!\""], ["cried Ahab.", "에이해브(Ahab)가 외쳤다."], ["“Time!", "\"시간을!"], ["time!”", "시간을 재!\""]], [["Dough-Boy hurried below, glanced at the watch, and reported the exact minute to Ahab.", "도우보이가 서둘러 아래로 내려가 시계를 흘끗 보고는, 정확한 분(分)을 에이해브에게 일러바쳤다."]], [["The ship was now kept away from the wind, and she went gently rolling before it.", "이제 배는 바람을 등지도록 돌려졌고, 그 앞으로 부드럽게 흔들리며 나아갔다."], ["Tashtego reporting that the whales had gone down heading to leeward, we confidently looked to see them again directly in advance of our bows.", "고래들이 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 머리를 두고 물속으로 내려갔다는 태시테고의 보고에 따라, 우리는 곧장 뱃머리 바로 앞에서 그것들을 다시 보게 되리라 자신했다."], ["For that singular craft at times evinced by the Sperm Whale when, sounding with his head in one direction, he nevertheless, while concealed beneath the surface, mills round, and swiftly swims off in the opposite quarter—this deceitfulness of his could not now be in action; for there was no reason to suppose that the fish seen by Tashtego had been in any way alarmed, or indeed knew at all of our vicinity.", "향유고래가 이따금 보이는 그 독특한 술수, 곧 머리를 한쪽 방향으로 두고 잠수했다가도 수면 아래 몸을 숨긴 채 빙 돌아 정반대 쪽으로 잽싸게 헤엄쳐 달아나는 그 교활함은 이번에는 발휘될 리가 없었다. 태시테고가 본 그 고래가 어떤 식으로든 놀랐거나, 실로 우리가 가까이 있다는 것을 조금이라도 알아챘다고 볼 까닭이 없었기 때문이다."], ["One of the men selected for shipkeepers—that is, those not appointed to the boats, by this time relieved the Indian at the main-mast head.", "보트에 배정되지 않은, 곧 배지기로 뽑힌 사람들 가운데 하나가 이제 큰돛대 꼭대기에서 인디언과 교대했다. 앞돛대와 뒷돛대에 있던 선원들은 내려왔다."], ["The sailors at the fore and mizzen had come down; the line tubs were fixed in their places; the cranes were thrust out; the mainyard was backed, and the three boats swung over the sea like three samphire baskets over high cliffs.", "밧줄 통들은 제자리에 고정되었고, 크레인들은 바깥으로 내밀어졌으며, 큰돛의 활대는 뒤로 돌려졌고, 세 척의 보트는 높은 절벽 위로 내밀린 세 개의 함초 바구니처럼 바다 위로 흔들려 나갔다."], ["Outside of the bulwarks their eager crews with one hand clung to the rail, while one foot was expectantly poised on the gunwale.", "뱃전 바깥에서 그 열성적인 보트 선원들은 한 손으로는 난간을 붙잡고, 한 발은 기대에 차서 뱃전 위에 올려 둔 채였다."], ["So look the long line of man-of-war’s men about to throw themselves on board an enemy’s ship.", "적함 위로 뛰어들기 직전의 군함 병사들이 길게 늘어선 모습이 꼭 그러할 것이다."]], [["But at this critical instant a sudden exclamation was heard that took every eye from the whale.", "그러나 이 위태로운 찰나에, 모든 이의 눈을 고래에서 떼어 놓는 한 줄기 갑작스러운 외침이 들려왔다."], ["With a start all glared at dark Ahab, who was surrounded by five dusky phantoms that seemed fresh formed out of air.", "모두가 흠칫 놀라 어두운 에이해브를 쏘아보았는데, 그는 마치 막 허공에서 빚어져 나온 듯한 거무스름한 다섯 유령에게 둘러싸여 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 48. The First Lowering.", "# CHAPTER 48. The First Lowering."]], [["The phantoms, for so they then seemed, were flitting on the other side of the deck, and, with a noiseless celerity, were casting loose the tackles and bands of the boat which swung there.", "그때는 유령처럼 보였던 그 형체들이 갑판 반대편에서 어른거리며, 소리 하나 없이 날래게 그곳에 매달려 있던 보트의 도르래와 결박을 풀고 있었다."], ["This boat had always been deemed one of the spare boats, though technically called the captain’s, on account of its hanging from the starboard quarter.", "이 보트는 우현 선미 쪽에 매달려 있는 까닭에 기술적으로는 선장의 보트라 불렸으나, 늘 예비 보트 중 하나로 여겨져 왔다."], ["The figure that now stood by its bows was tall and swart, with one white tooth evilly protruding from its steel-like lips.", "지금 그 뱃머리 곁에 선 형체는 키가 크고 거무튀튀했으며, 강철 같은 입술 사이로 새하얀 이 하나가 사악하게 비죽 튀어나와 있었다."], ["A rumpled Chinese jacket of black cotton funereally invested him, with wide black trowsers of the same dark stuff.", "검은 무명으로 지은 구겨진 중국식 윗옷이 장례식이라도 치르듯 그의 몸을 감쌌고, 같은 검은 천으로 지은 헐렁한 검정 바지가 어우러져 있었다."], ["But strangely crowning this ebonness was a glistening white plaited turban, the living hair braided and coiled round and round upon his head.", "그런데 이 칠흑 같은 검음 위에 기묘하게 얹힌 것은 반짝이는 새하얀 머릿수건이었으니, 살아 있는 머리카락을 땋아 머리 위에 둘둘 감아올린 것이었다."], ["Less swart in aspect, the companions of this figure were of that vivid, tiger-yellow complexion peculiar to some of the aboriginal natives of the Manillas;—a race notorious for a certain diabolism of subtilty, and by some honest white mariners supposed to be the paid spies and secret confidential agents on the water of the devil, their lord, whose counting-room they suppose to be elsewhere.", "이 형체의 동료들은 얼굴빛이 덜 검었으되, 마닐라 원주민 일부에게서만 볼 수 있는 선명한 호랑이 같은 누런 살빛을 띠고 있었다. 그들은 어떤 교묘한 악마성으로 악명이 높은 종족이었고, 정직한 백인 선원들 가운데는 그들을 두고 바다 위에서 자신들의 주인인 악마에게 돈을 받고 일하는 첩자이자 은밀한 심복이며, 그 악마의 회계실은 어딘가 다른 곳에 있으리라 믿는 이들도 있었다."]], [["While yet the wondering ship’s company were gazing upon these strangers, Ahab cried out to the white-turbaned old man at their head, “All ready there, Fedallah?”", "어리둥절해진 선원들이 아직 이 낯선 자들을 응시하고 있을 때, 에이해브가 그들의 우두머리 격인 흰 머릿수건을 쓴 노인을 향해 외쳤다. \"거기 다 됐나, 페달라?\""]], [["“Ready,”", "\"됐습니다.\""], ["was the half-hissed reply.", "반쯤 쉭쉭거리는 듯한 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Lower away then; d’ye hear?”", "\"그럼 내려라. 들었느냐?\""], ["shouting across the deck.", "갑판 너머로 고함이 울렸다."], ["“Lower away there, I say.”", "\"거기 내리라니까.\""]], [["Such was the thunder of his voice, that spite of their amazement the men sprang over the rail; the sheaves whirled round in the blocks; with a wallow, the three boats dropped into the sea; while, with a dexterous, off-handed daring, unknown in any other vocation, the sailors, goat-like, leaped down the rolling ship’s side into the tossed boats below.", "그 목소리가 어찌나 우레와 같았던지, 사람들은 놀라움에도 불구하고 난간을 뛰어넘었다. 도르래 바퀴가 활차 안에서 빙글빙글 돌았고, 세 척의 보트가 첨벙거리며 바다로 떨어져 내렸다. 그러는 동안 선원들은 다른 어떤 직업에서도 볼 수 없는 능란하고 거침없는 대담함으로, 염소처럼 출렁이는 뱃전을 따라 아래에서 까딱이는 보트로 뛰어내렸다."]], [["Hardly had they pulled out from under the ship’s lee, when a fourth keel, coming from the windward side, pulled round under the stern, and showed the five strangers rowing Ahab, who, standing erect in the stern, loudly hailed Starbuck, Stubb, and Flask, to spread themselves widely, so as to cover a large expanse of water.", "그들이 배 그늘 아래에서 막 노 저어 빠져나오자, 바람이 불어오는 쪽에서 네 번째 보트가 나타나 선미를 돌아 다가왔는데, 거기에는 에이해브를 태우고 노 젓는 다섯 명의 낯선 자들이 보였다. 에이해브는 선미에 꼿꼿이 선 채 스타벅과 스터브, 그리고 플래스크에게 큰소리로 외쳐, 넓은 바다를 덮을 수 있도록 멀찍이 흩어지라고 명했다."], ["But with all their eyes again riveted upon the swart Fedallah and his crew, the inmates of the other boats obeyed not the command.", "그러나 모두의 눈길이 다시금 거무튀튀한 페달라와 그 부하들에게 못 박힌 탓에, 다른 보트의 선원들은 그 명령을 따르지 않았다."]], [["“Captain Ahab?—”", "\"에이해브 선장님—\""], ["said Starbuck.", "스타벅이 입을 열었다."]], [["“Spread yourselves,”", "\"흩어져라.\""], ["cried Ahab; “give way, all four boats.", "에이해브가 외쳤다. \"네 척 모두 노를 저어라."], ["Thou, Flask, pull out more to leeward!”", "너, 플래스크, 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 더 빠져나가!\""]], [["“Aye, aye, sir,”", "\"예, 예, 선장님.\""], ["cheerily cried little King-Post, sweeping round his great steering oar.", "작은 킹포스트가 커다란 키잡이 노를 휘돌리며 명랑하게 외쳤다."], ["“Lay back!”", "\"몸을 젖혀라!\""], ["addressing his crew.", "그가 부하들에게 명했다."], ["“There!—there!—there again!", "\"저기다!—저기!—또 저기!"], ["There she blows right ahead, boys!—lay back!”", "바로 앞에서 물을 뿜는다, 친구들!—몸을 젖혀라!\""]], [["“Never heed yonder yellow boys, Archy.”", "\"저기 누런 녀석들 따위 신경 쓰지 마라, 아치.\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t mind ’em, sir,”", "\"오, 신경 안 씁니다, 선장님.\""], ["said Archy; “I knew it all before now.", "아치가 말했다. \"전 진작에 다 알고 있었거든요."], ["Didn’t I hear ’em in the hold?", "선창에서 그놈들 소리를 듣지 않았겠습니까?"], ["And didn’t I tell Cabaco here of it?", "그래서 여기 카바코한테 말해 주지 않았습니까?"], ["What say ye, Cabaco?", "그렇지, 카바코?"], ["They are stowaways, Mr. Flask.”", "저놈들은 밀항자입니다, 플래스크 씨.\""]], [["“Pull, pull, my fine hearts-alive; pull, my children; pull, my little ones,”", "\"저어라, 저어라, 살아 펄떡이는 내 사랑들아. 저어라, 내 새끼들아, 저어라, 내 귀여운 것들아.\""], ["drawlingly and soothingly sighed Stubb to his crew, some of whom still showed signs of uneasiness.", "스터브가 부하들을 향해 느릿느릿 달래듯 한숨처럼 내뱉었는데, 그중 몇몇은 아직도 불안한 기색을 보였다."], ["“Why don’t you break your backbones, my boys?", "\"어째서 등뼈가 부러지도록 젓지 않는 거냐, 얘들아?"], ["What is it you stare at?", "뭘 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보는 거냐?"], ["Those chaps in yonder boat?", "저기 보트에 탄 녀석들?"], ["Tut!", "쳇!"], ["They are only five more hands come to help us—never mind from where—the more the merrier.", "저들은 그저 우리를 도우러 온 일손 다섯에 불과해—어디서 왔든 무슨 상관이냐—사람이 많을수록 즐거운 법이지."], ["Pull, then, do pull; never mind the brimstone—devils are good fellows enough.", "그러니 저어라, 어서 저어라. 유황 따위 신경 쓰지 마라—악마라도 꽤 괜찮은 친구들이니까."], ["So, so; there you are now; that’s the stroke for a thousand pounds; that’s the stroke to sweep the stakes!", "그렇지, 그렇지. 자, 이제 됐다."], ["Hurrah for the gold cup of sperm oil, my heroes!", "천 파운드짜리 노 젓기다. 판돈을 싹쓸이할 노 젓기야!"], ["Three cheers, men—all hearts alive!", "향유고래 기름의 황금 잔을 위하여, 내 영웅들아!"], ["Easy, easy; don’t be in a hurry—don’t be in a hurry.", "세 번 만세를 부르자, 얘들아—모두 살아 펄떡이며!"], ["Why don’t you snap your oars, you rascals?", "천천히, 천천히. 서두르지 마라—서두르지 마."], ["Bite something, you dogs!", "어째서 노를 부러뜨리지 않느냐, 이 악당들아?"], ["So, so, so, then:—softly, softly!", "뭔가를 물어뜯어라, 이 개 같은 놈들아!"], ["That’s it—that’s it!", "그렇지, 그렇지, 그래—부드럽게, 부드럽게!"], ["long and strong.", "바로 그거다—그거야!"], ["Give way there, give way!", "길고 힘차게. 저어라, 저어!"], ["The devil fetch ye, ye ragamuffin rapscallions; ye are all asleep.", "악마가 너희를 잡아가기를, 이 누더기 같은 부랑자 놈들아. 죄다 잠들었구나."], ["Stop snoring, ye sleepers, and pull.", "코 그만 골고 저어라, 이 잠꾸러기들아."], ["Pull, will ye?", "저으라니까?"], ["pull, can’t ye?", "저을 줄 모르느냐?"], ["pull, won’t ye?", "젓기 싫으냐?"], ["Why in the name of gudgeons and ginger-cakes don’t ye pull?—pull and break something!", "모샘치와 생강과자의 이름으로 묻건대 어째서 젓지 않는 거냐?—저어서 뭔가를 부러뜨려라!"], ["pull, and start your eyes out!", "저어서 눈깔이 튀어나오게 해라!"], ["Here!”", "자!\""], ["whipping out the sharp knife from his girdle; “every mother’s son of ye draw his knife, and pull with the blade between his teeth.", "그가 허리띠에서 날카로운 칼을 휙 뽑아 들었다. \"너희 어미가 낳은 자식들 하나하나 모두 칼을 뽑아 들고, 칼날을 이로 문 채 저어라."], ["That’s it—that’s it.", "그거다—그거야."], ["Now ye do something; that looks like it, my steel-bits.", "이제야 뭔가 하는구나. 그래야 그림이 나오지, 내 강철 조각들아."], ["Start her—start her, my silver-spoons!", "출발해라—출발해, 내 은수저들아!"], ["Start her, marling-spikes!”", "출발해라, 밧줄 송곳들아!\""]], [["Stubb’s exordium to his crew is given here at large, because he had rather a peculiar way of talking to them in general, and especially in inculcating the religion of rowing.", "스터브가 부하들에게 늘어놓은 이 서두를 여기 길게 옮긴 것은, 그가 대체로 그들에게 말을 거는 방식이, 특히 노 젓기의 종교를 설파하는 방식이 자못 독특했기 때문이다."], ["But you must not suppose from this specimen of his sermonizings that he ever flew into downright passions with his congregation.", "그러나 이 설교 한 토막을 보고서 그가 자기 신도들에게 진짜로 노발대발했으리라 짐작해서는 안 된다."], ["Not at all; and therein consisted his chief peculiarity.", "결코 그렇지 않았고, 바로 거기에 그의 가장 큰 특이함이 있었다."], ["He would say the most terrific things to his crew, in a tone so strangely compounded of fun and fury, and the fury seemed so calculated merely as a spice to the fun, that no oarsman could hear such queer invocations without pulling for dear life, and yet pulling for the mere joke of the thing.", "그는 부하들에게 더없이 끔찍한 말들을 내뱉으면서도, 그 어조가 익살과 분노가 어찌나 묘하게 뒤섞였던지, 그 분노조차 오로지 익살에 곁들이는 양념처럼 계산된 것으로 보일 정도였다. 그래서 어떤 노잡이도 그토록 기묘한 호령을 들으면 죽을힘을 다해 노를 젓지 않을 수 없었으되, 그러면서도 한낱 농담거리로 여기며 젓는 것이었다."], ["Besides he all the time looked so easy and indolent himself, so loungingly managed his steering-oar, and so broadly gaped—open-mouthed at times—that the mere sight of such a yawning commander, by sheer force of contrast, acted like a charm upon the crew.", "게다가 그는 내내 무척이나 느긋하고 게으른 모습이었고, 키잡이 노도 너무나 빈둥대듯 다루었으며, 어찌나 입을 떡 벌리고—때로는 아예 하품하듯 벌어진 입으로—있었던지, 그렇게 하품하는 지휘관의 모습이 오로지 대조의 힘만으로 부하들에게 마법처럼 작용하는 것이었다."], ["Then again, Stubb was one of those odd sort of humorists, whose jollity is sometimes so curiously ambiguous, as to put all inferiors on their guard in the matter of obeying them.", "그런가 하면 스터브는 그 명랑함이 때로 어찌나 야릇하게 모호한지, 명령에 복종하는 일에 있어 아랫사람을 죄다 경계하게 만드는, 그런 기묘한 부류의 익살꾼 가운데 하나였다."]], [["In obedience to a sign from Ahab, Starbuck was now pulling obliquely across Stubb’s bow; and when for a minute or so the two boats were pretty near to each other, Stubb hailed the mate.", "에이해브의 신호에 따라, 스타벅은 이제 스터브의 뱃머리를 비스듬히 가로질러 노를 젓고 있었다. 그리고 잠깐 동안 두 보트가 제법 가까워지자 스터브가 일등항해사를 불렀다."]], [["“Mr. Starbuck!", "\"스타벅 씨!"], ["larboard boat there, ahoy!", "거기 좌현 보트, 어이!"], ["a word with ye, sir, if ye please!”", "한 말씀 드릴까 하는데요, 선생, 괜찮으시다면요!\""]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"여어!\""], ["returned Starbuck, turning round not a single inch as he spoke; still earnestly but whisperingly urging his crew; his face set like a flint from Stubb’s.", "스타벅이 말하면서도 단 한 치도 고개를 돌리지 않은 채 대답했다. 그는 여전히 진지하면서도 속삭이듯 부하들을 다그치고 있었고, 그 얼굴은 스터브 쪽으로는 부싯돌처럼 굳어 있었다."]], [["“What think ye of those yellow boys, sir!”", "\"저 누런 녀석들을 어찌 생각하십니까, 선생!\""]], [["“Smuggled on board, somehow, before the ship sailed.", "\"배가 출항하기 전에 어떻게든 몰래 실린 거지."], ["(Strong, strong, boys!)”", "(힘차게, 힘차게, 얘들아!)\""], ["in a whisper to his crew, then speaking out loud again: “A sad business, Mr.", "부하들에게 속삭인 뒤, 다시 큰 소리로 말했다. \"딱한 일이오, 스터브 씨!"], ["Stubb! (seethe her, seethe her, my lads!) but never mind, Mr.", "(휘저어라, 휘저어, 얘들아!) 하지만 신경 쓰지 마시오, 스터브 씨, 다 잘되자고 하는 일이니."], ["Stubb, all for the best. Let all your crew pull strong, come what will.", "무슨 일이 닥치든 부하들을 힘차게 젓게 하시오."], ["(Spring, my men, spring!) There’s hogsheads of sperm ahead, Mr. Stubb, and that’s what ye came for.", "(튀어 올라라, 얘들아, 튀어!) 앞에 향유고래가 통째로 가득하오, 스터브 씨, 바로 그걸 잡으러 온 게 아니오."], ["(Pull, my boys!) Sperm, sperm’s the play!", "(저어라, 얘들아!) 향유고래, 향유고래가 관건이지!"], ["This at least is duty; duty and profit hand in hand.”", "적어도 이건 의무요. 의무와 이익이 손을 맞잡았소.\""]], [["“Aye, aye, I thought as much,”", "\"그래, 그래, 그럴 줄 알았지.\""], ["soliloquized Stubb, when the boats diverged, “as soon as I clapt eye on ’em, I thought so.", "보트들이 갈라지자 스터브가 혼잣말을 했다. \"그놈들을 처음 보자마자 그런 줄 알았다니까."], ["Aye, and that’s what he went into the after hold for, so often, as Dough-Boy long suspected.", "그래, 그래서 도우보이가 오래전부터 의심했던 대로, 그자가 그토록 자주 뒤쪽 선창으로 내려갔던 거지."], ["They were hidden down there.", "놈들은 거기 아래 숨어 있었던 거야."], ["The White Whale’s at the bottom of it.", "흰 고래가 이 일의 밑바닥에 깔려 있어."], ["Well, well, so be it!", "자, 자, 그래 좋다!"], ["Can’t be helped!", "어쩔 수 없지!"], ["All right!", "좋아!"], ["Give way, men!", "저어라, 얘들아!"], ["It ain’t the White Whale to-day!", "오늘은 흰 고래가 아니다!"], ["Give way!”", "저어라!\""]], [["Now the advent of these outlandish strangers at such a critical instant as the lowering of the boats from the deck, this had not unreasonably awakened a sort of superstitious amazement in some of the ship’s company; but Archy’s fancied discovery having some time previous got abroad among them, though indeed not credited then, this had in some small measure prepared them for the event.", "자, 갑판에서 보트를 내리는 그토록 위중한 순간에 이 별난 낯선 자들이 등장한 것은, 선원들 가운데 몇몇에게 일종의 미신적인 경악을 불러일으켰던 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그러나 아치가 멋대로 발견했다는 이야기가 얼마 전부터 그들 사이에 퍼져 있던 터라—물론 그때는 아무도 믿지 않았지만—이것이 그나마 조금은 그들로 하여금 이 사태에 대비하게 만들어 주었다."], ["It took off the extreme edge of their wonder; and so what with all this and Stubb’s confident way of accounting for their appearance, they were for the time freed from superstitious surmisings; though the affair still left abundant room for all manner of wild conjectures as to dark Ahab’s precise agency in the matter from the beginning.", "그것이 그들의 놀라움에서 가장 날카로운 모서리를 깎아 냈다. 그리하여 이 모든 것에다 스터브가 그들의 등장을 자신만만하게 설명해 준 덕분에, 그들은 당분간 미신적인 억측에서 벗어났다."], ["For me, I silently recalled the mysterious shadows I had seen creeping on board the Pequod during the dim Nantucket dawn, as well as the enigmatical hintings of the unaccountable Elijah.", "하지만 이 일은 여전히, 애초부터 어두운 에이해브가 이 문제에 정확히 어떻게 관여했는지를 두고 온갖 거친 추측이 일어날 넉넉한 여지를 남겨 두었다. 나로 말하자면, 어슴푸레한 낸터킷의 새벽녘에 피쿼드호로 살그머니 올라타던 그 신비로운 그림자들을, 그리고 도무지 알 수 없던 일라이저의 수수께끼 같은 암시들을 말없이 떠올렸다."]], [["Meantime, Ahab, out of hearing of his officers, having sided the furthest to windward, was still ranging ahead of the other boats; a circumstance bespeaking how potent a crew was pulling him.", "그러는 동안, 부하 항해사들의 목소리가 닿지 않는 곳까지 바람 불어오는 쪽으로 가장 멀리 빠져나간 에이해브는, 여전히 다른 보트들보다 앞서 나아가고 있었으니, 이는 그를 태우고 노 젓는 선원들이 얼마나 강력한지를 말해 주는 정황이었다."], ["Those tiger yellow creatures of his seemed all steel and whalebone; like five trip-hammers they rose and fell with regular strokes of strength, which periodically started the boat along the water like a horizontal burst boiler out of a Mississippi steamer.", "호랑이처럼 누런 그의 부하들은 온통 강철과 고래수염으로 빚은 듯했다. 다섯 개의 증기 해머처럼 그들은 규칙적인 힘의 박자에 맞추어 솟았다 떨어졌고, 그 박자마다 보트는 마치 미시시피 증기선에서 터져 나온 수평으로 누운 보일러처럼 물살을 가르며 튀어 나갔다."], ["As for Fedallah, who was seen pulling the harpooneer oar, he had thrown aside his black jacket, and displayed his naked chest with the whole part of his body above the gunwale, clearly cut against the alternating depressions of the watery horizon; while at the other end of the boat Ahab, with one arm, like a fencer’s, thrown half backward into the air, as if to counterbalance any tendency to trip; Ahab was seen steadily managing his steering oar as in a thousand boat lowerings ere the White Whale had torn him.", "작살잡이의 노를 젓는 모습이 보이던 페달라로 말하자면, 그는 검은 윗옷을 벗어 던지고 맨가슴을 드러낸 채 뱃전 위로 솟은 상반신 전부를, 번갈아 가라앉는 물 수평선을 배경으로 또렷이 새겨 보였다. 한편 보트 반대편 끝에서 에이해브는, 마치 휘청이는 어떤 기미라도 상쇄하려는 듯 검객처럼 한쪽 팔을 반쯤 뒤로 젖혀 허공에 들어 올린 채, 흰 고래가 그의 다리를 찢어 놓기 전 천 번의 보트 내리기 때 그러했던 것처럼 한결같이 키잡이 노를 다루고 있었다."], ["All at once the outstretched arm gave a peculiar motion and then remained fixed, while the boat’s five oars were seen simultaneously peaked.", "별안간 그 뻗은 팔이 기묘하게 움직이더니 그대로 멈추었고, 동시에 보트의 다섯 노가 일제히 곤두선 채 멈춰 들리는 것이 보였다."], ["Boat and crew sat motionless on the sea.", "보트도 선원들도 바다 위에 미동도 없이 앉아 있었다."], ["Instantly the three spread boats in the rear paused on their way.", "그 즉시 뒤쪽에 흩어져 있던 세 척의 보트도 가던 길을 멈추었다."], ["The whales had irregularly settled bodily down into the blue, thus giving no distantly discernible token of the movement, though from his closer vicinity Ahab had observed it.", "고래들은 불규칙하게 몸뚱이를 푸른 물속으로 가라앉혔던 터라, 그 움직임을 멀리서 알아챌 만한 기색은 전혀 남기지 않았으되, 더 가까이 있던 에이해브는 그것을 알아보았던 것이다."]], [["“Every man look out along his oars!”", "\"모두 자기 노를 따라 잘 살펴라!\""], ["cried Starbuck.", "스타벅이 외쳤다."], ["“Thou, Queequeg, stand up!”", "\"너, 퀴케그, 일어서라!\""]], [["Nimbly springing up on the triangular raised box in the bow, the savage stood erect there, and with intensely eager eyes gazed off towards the spot where the chase had last been descried.", "야만인은 뱃머리에 솟은 삼각형 상자 위로 날렵하게 뛰어올라 거기 꼿꼿이 서서는, 사냥감이 마지막으로 보였던 지점을 향해 강렬하고도 간절한 눈으로 응시했다."], ["Likewise upon the extreme stern of the boat where it was also triangularly platformed level with the gunwale, Starbuck himself was seen coolly and adroitly balancing himself to the jerking tossings of his chip of a craft, and silently eyeing the vast blue eye of the sea.", "마찬가지로 보트의 맨 끝 선미에도 뱃전과 같은 높이로 삼각형 발판이 마련되어 있었는데, 그 위에서 스타벅 자신이 나무 조각 같은 자기 배의 들썩이는 출렁임에 침착하고 능란하게 몸의 균형을 잡으며, 말없이 바다의 광대한 푸른 눈을 응시하고 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["Not very far distant Flask’s boat was also lying breathlessly still; its commander recklessly standing upon the top of the loggerhead, a stout sort of post rooted in the keel, and rising some two feet above the level of the stern platform.", "그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 플래스크의 보트 역시 숨죽인 듯 고요히 떠 있었다. 그 지휘관은 무모하게도 로거헤드 꼭대기에 올라서 있었으니, 그것은 용골에 박힌 굵직한 기둥으로 선미 발판 높이보다 두 자(약 60센티미터)쯤 솟아 있었다."], ["It is used for catching turns with the whale line.", "그것은 고래 밧줄을 감아 두는 데 쓰이는 것이었다."], ["Its top is not more spacious than the palm of a man’s hand, and standing upon such a base as that, Flask seemed perched at the mast-head of some ship which had sunk to all but her trucks.", "그 꼭대기는 사람 손바닥보다 넓지 않은데, 플래스크는 그런 받침 위에 올라서 있던 까닭에, 마치 돛대 꼭대기만 남기고 침몰해 버린 어느 배의 그 꼭대기에 걸터앉아 있는 듯 보였다."], ["But little King-Post was small and short, and at the same time little King-Post was full of a large and tall ambition, so that this loggerhead stand-point of his did by no means satisfy King-Post.", "그러나 작은 킹포스트는 키가 작고 땅딸막했고, 동시에 작은 킹포스트는 크고 드높은 야심으로 가득 차 있었던 까닭에, 이 로거헤드 발판 따위로는 결코 만족하지 못했다."]], [["“I can’t see three seas off; tip us up an oar there, and let me on to that.”", "\"파도 세 마루 너머도 안 보이는군. 거기 노를 하나 세워다오, 그 위로 올라가게.\""]], [["Upon this, Daggoo, with either hand upon the gunwale to steady his way, swiftly slid aft, and then erecting himself volunteered his lofty shoulders for a pedestal.", "이에 대구가 가는 길을 안정시키려 양손을 뱃전에 짚은 채 잽싸게 선미로 미끄러져 가더니, 몸을 곧추세우고는 자신의 우뚝한 어깨를 받침대로 자청해 내밀었다."]], [["“Good a mast-head as any, sir.", "\"여느 돛대 꼭대기 못지않습니다, 선생."], ["Will you mount?”", "올라타시겠습니까?\""]], [["“That I will, and thank ye very much, my fine fellow; only I wish you fifty feet taller.”", "\"그러고말고, 정말 고맙다, 멋진 친구야. 다만 네가 쉰 자(약 15미터)는 더 컸으면 싶구나.\""]], [["Whereupon planting his feet firmly against two opposite planks of the boat, the gigantic negro, stooping a little, presented his flat palm to Flask’s foot, and then putting Flask’s hand on his hearse-plumed head and bidding him spring as he himself should toss, with one dexterous fling landed the little man high and dry on his shoulders.", "그러자 그 거대한 흑인은 보트의 마주 보는 두 판자에 두 발을 단단히 디디고 약간 몸을 숙여, 자신의 펼친 손바닥을 플래스크의 발에 받쳐 주고는, 플래스크의 손을 자신의 영구차 깃털 같은 머리에 얹게 한 뒤 자기가 들어 올릴 때 맞춰 뛰어오르라 이르더니, 능란하게 한 번 던져 그 작은 사내를 자기 어깨 위에 높직이 마른 채로 올려놓았다."], ["And here was Flask now standing, Daggoo with one lifted arm furnishing him with a breastband to lean against and steady himself by.", "이렇게 플래스크는 이제 그 위에 서 있었고, 대구는 한쪽 팔을 들어 올려 그가 기대어 몸을 가눌 가슴받이를 만들어 주었다."]], [["At any time it is a strange sight to the tyro to see with what wondrous habitude of unconscious skill the whaleman will maintain an erect posture in his boat, even when pitched about by the most riotously perverse and cross-running seas.", "언제 보아도 풋내기에게는, 더없이 사납고 비뚤어지게 엇갈리며 출렁이는 파도에 마구 흔들리면서도 고래잡이가 제 보트 안에서 꼿꼿한 자세를 유지하는, 그 무의식적인 솜씨의 놀라운 능숙함이 기이한 광경이다."], ["Still more strange to see him giddily perched upon the loggerhead itself, under such circumstances.", "그런 상황에서 로거헤드 그 자체에 아찔하게 올라선 모습을 보는 것은 더욱 기이하다."], ["But the sight of little Flask mounted upon gigantic Daggoo was yet more curious; for sustaining himself with a cool, indifferent, easy, unthought of, barbaric majesty, the noble negro to every roll of the sea harmoniously rolled his fine form.", "그러나 거대한 대구 위에 올라탄 작은 플래스크의 모습은 한층 더 기묘했다. 차분하고 무심하고 느긋하며 아무 생각 없는, 야만적인 위엄으로 몸을 지탱하면서, 그 고귀한 흑인은 바다가 한 번 출렁일 때마다 자신의 멋진 몸을 조화롭게 함께 출렁였던 것이다."], ["On his broad back, flaxen-haired Flask seemed a snow-flake.", "그 널찍한 등판 위에서 아마빛 머리의 플래스크는 한 송이 눈송이처럼 보였다."], ["The bearer looked nobler than the rider.", "짊어진 자가 올라탄 자보다 더 고귀해 보였다."], ["Though truly vivacious, tumultuous, ostentatious little Flask would now and then stamp with impatience; but not one added heave did he thereby give to the negro’s lordly chest.", "참으로 활기차고 부산스럽고 과시적인 작은 플래스크가 이따금 안달이 나서 발을 굴렀으나, 그렇다고 흑인의 당당한 가슴팍을 단 한 번이라도 더 들썩이게 만들지는 못했다."], ["So have I seen Passion and Vanity stamping the living magnanimous earth, but the earth did not alter her tides and her seasons for that.", "그렇게 나는 정념과 허영이 살아 있는 너그러운 대지를 발로 구르는 것을 보아 왔으되, 대지는 그런다고 제 조수와 제 계절을 바꾸지 않았다."]], [["Meanwhile Stubb, the third mate, betrayed no such far-gazing solicitudes.", "그러는 동안 삼등항해사 스터브는 그처럼 멀리 내다보는 노심초사 따위는 조금도 내비치지 않았다."], ["The whales might have made one of their regular soundings, not a temporary dive from mere fright; and if that were the case, Stubb, as his wont in such cases, it seems, was resolved to solace the languishing interval with his pipe.", "고래들은 단순히 겁먹어 잠깐 잠수한 것이 아니라, 늘 하듯 깊이 가라앉은 것인지도 몰랐다. 만일 그렇다면, 그런 경우 그의 버릇대로 스터브는 따분하게 늘어지는 그 막간을 자기 파이프로 달래기로 작정한 모양이었다."], ["He withdrew it from his hatband, where he always wore it aslant like a feather.", "그는 늘 깃털처럼 비스듬히 꽂아 두던 모자 띠에서 파이프를 뽑아냈다. 그것에 담배를 채우고 엄지 끝으로 꾹꾹 눌러 다졌다."], ["He loaded it, and rammed home the loading with his thumb-end; but hardly had he ignited his match across the rough sandpaper of his hand, when Tashtego, his harpooneer, whose eyes had been setting to windward like two fixed stars, suddenly dropped like light from his erect attitude to his seat, crying out in a quick phrensy of hurry, “Down, down all, and give way!—there they are!”", "그러나 거친 사포 같은 자기 손바닥에 성냥을 그어 막 불을 붙이려던 참에, 두 개의 붙박이별처럼 바람 불어오는 쪽을 향해 눈을 박고 있던 그의 작살잡이 태시테고가, 별안간 꼿꼿한 자세에서 빛처럼 휙 제자리에 주저앉으며 다급한 광기에 휩싸여 외쳤다. \"엎드려, 모두 엎드려, 저어라!—저기 있다!\""]], [["To a landsman, no whale, nor any sign of a herring, would have been visible at that moment; nothing but a troubled bit of greenish white water, and thin scattered puffs of vapor hovering over it, and suffusingly blowing off to leeward, like the confused scud from white rolling billows.", "그 순간 뭍사람의 눈에는 고래는커녕 청어 한 마리의 기색조차 보이지 않았으리라. 보이는 것이라곤 어지러이 일렁이는 연녹색의 흰 물결 한 자락과, 그 위를 맴도는 흩어진 옅은 수증기 다발들, 그리고 흰 너울에서 일어나는 어수선한 물보라처럼 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 흩날려 가는 김뿐이었다."], ["The air around suddenly vibrated and tingled, as it were, like the air over intensely heated plates of iron.", "주위의 공기는 별안간 진동하며 따끔거리는 듯했으니, 마치 시뻘겋게 달군 철판 위의 공기와도 같았다."], ["Beneath this atmospheric waving and curling, and partially beneath a thin layer of water, also, the whales were swimming.", "이렇게 너울거리며 휘감기는 대기 아래, 또 일부는 얇은 물의 층 아래에서, 고래들이 헤엄치고 있었다."], ["Seen in advance of all the other indications, the puffs of vapor they spouted, seemed their forerunning couriers and detached flying outriders.", "다른 어떤 징후보다 앞서 보이던, 그들이 뿜어 올린 수증기 다발은 마치 그들의 선발 전령이자 떨어져 나와 날아다니는 척후병들처럼 보였다."]], [["All four boats were now in keen pursuit of that one spot of troubled water and air.", "이제 네 척의 보트는 모두 그 어지러운 물과 공기의 한 지점을 향해 열렬한 추격에 나섰다."], ["But it bade fair to outstrip them; it flew on and on, as a mass of interblending bubbles borne down a rapid stream from the hills.", "그러나 그것은 그들을 따돌릴 기세였다. 그것은 마치 산에서 빠른 물살에 떠밀려 내려오는 거품 덩어리처럼, 자꾸자꾸 앞으로 날아갔다."]], [["“Pull, pull, my good boys,”", "\"저어라, 저어, 착한 녀석들아.\""], ["said Starbuck, in the lowest possible but intensest concentrated whisper to his men; while the sharp fixed glance from his eyes darted straight ahead of the bow, almost seemed as two visible needles in two unerring binnacle compasses.", "스타벅이 부하들에게 가능한 한 가장 낮으면서도 가장 강렬하게 응축된 속삭임으로 말했다. 그러는 동안 그의 두 눈에서 쏘아져 나오는 날카롭게 고정된 시선은 뱃머리 정면으로 곧장 뻗어, 마치 어김없는 두 개의 나침반 위에 놓인 두 개의 눈에 보이는 바늘 같았다."], ["He did not say much to his crew, though, nor did his crew say anything to him.", "그래도 그는 부하들에게 별말을 하지 않았고, 부하들 역시 그에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Only the silence of the boat was at intervals startlingly pierced by one of his peculiar whispers, now harsh with command, now soft with entreaty.", "다만 보트의 침묵을 이따금 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 꿰뚫는 것은 명령조로 거칠어졌다가 애원조로 부드러워지는, 그의 그 독특한 속삭임 하나뿐이었다."]], [["How different the loud little King-Post.", "소리 큰 작은 킹포스트는 이와 얼마나 달랐던가."], ["“Sing out and say something, my hearties.", "\"큰 소리로 외치고 뭐라고 말 좀 해라, 친구들아."], ["Roar and pull, my thunderbolts!", "포효하며 저어라, 내 벼락들아!"], ["Beach me, beach me on their black backs, boys; only do that for me, and I’ll sign over to you my Martha’s Vineyard plantation, boys; including wife and children, boys.", "나를 들이박게 해 다오, 저놈들의 검은 등판에 나를 들이박아 다오, 얘들아. 나한테 그것만 해 주면, 내 마서스비니어드 농장을 너희한테 양도해 주마, 얘들아."], ["Lay me on—lay me on!", "처자식까지 다 포함해서 말이다, 얘들아. 나를 갖다 대 다오—나를 갖다 대 다오!"], ["O Lord, Lord!", "오 주여, 주여!"], ["but I shall go stark, staring mad!", "아무래도 내가 완전히 미쳐 버리고 말 것 같구나!"], ["See!", "봐라!"], ["see that white water!”", "저 흰 물결을 봐라!\""], ["And so shouting, he pulled his hat from his head, and stamped up and down on it; then picking it up, flirted it far off upon the sea; and finally fell to rearing and plunging in the boat’s stern like a crazed colt from the prairie.", "그렇게 외치며 그는 머리에서 모자를 벗어 발로 짓밟더니, 다시 집어 들어 바다 멀리 휙 던져 버리고는, 마침내 대초원에서 온 미친 망아지처럼 보트 선미에서 날뛰며 곤두박질치기 시작했다."]], [["“Look at that chap now,”", "\"저 친구 좀 보게.\""], ["philosophically drawled Stubb, who, with his unlighted short pipe, mechanically retained between his teeth, at a short distance, followed after—“He’s got fits, that Flask has.", "스터브가 짧은 거리를 두고 뒤따르며, 불도 붙이지 않은 짧은 파이프를 기계적으로 입에 문 채 철학자처럼 느릿느릿 말했다. \"발작을 일으켰구먼, 저 플래스크 녀석이."], ["Fits?", "발작이라?"], ["yes, give him fits—that’s the very word—pitch fits into ’em.", "그래, 발작 한번 안겨 주지—그게 딱 맞는 말이야—놈들한테 발작을 처넣어 주는 거야."], ["Merrily, merrily, hearts-alive.", "즐겁게, 즐겁게, 살아 펄떡이는 녀석들아."], ["Pudding for supper, you know;—merry’s the word.", "저녁엔 푸딩이 나온다잖느냐—즐거움이 좌우명이지."], ["Pull, babes—pull, sucklings—pull, all.", "저어라, 갓난것들아—저어라, 젖먹이들아—모두 저어라."], ["But what the devil are you hurrying about?", "그런데 도대체 뭘 그리 서두르는 거냐?"], ["Softly, softly, and steadily, my men.", "부드럽게, 부드럽게, 그리고 한결같이, 얘들아."], ["Only pull, and keep pulling; nothing more.", "그저 젓기만 해라, 계속 젓기만 해. 그뿐이다."], ["Crack all your backbones, and bite your knives in two—that’s all.", "등뼈를 죄다 으스러뜨리고, 칼을 둘로 깨물어 버려라—그게 전부다."], ["Take it easy—why don’t ye take it easy, I say, and burst all your livers and lungs!”", "느긋하게 해라—어째서 느긋하게 안 하는 거냐, 내 말은, 그리고 간이고 폐고 죄다 터뜨려 버려라!\""]], [["But what it was that inscrutable Ahab said to that tiger-yellow crew of his—these were words best omitted here; for you live under the blessed light of the evangelical land.", "그러나 그 헤아릴 길 없는 에이해브가 자기의 호랑이처럼 누런 부하들에게 한 말이 무엇이었는지—그것은 여기서 차라리 빼 두는 편이 나으리라. 그대들은 복음의 빛이 축복처럼 비치는 땅 아래 살고 있으니 말이다."], ["Only the infidel sharks in the audacious seas may give ear to such words, when, with tornado brow, and eyes of red murder, and foam-glued lips, Ahab leaped after his prey.", "회오리바람 같은 이마와 시뻘건 살의의 눈, 그리고 거품으로 굳어 버린 입술로 에이해브가 사냥감을 향해 뛰어들 때, 그런 말에 귀 기울일 수 있는 것은 오로지 저 대담무쌍한 바다의 이교도 상어들뿐이리라."]], [["Meanwhile, all the boats tore on.", "그러는 동안 모든 보트가 맹렬히 내달렸다."], ["The repeated specific allusions of Flask to “that whale,” as he called the fictitious monster which he declared to be incessantly tantalizing his boat’s bow with its tail—these allusions of his were at times so vivid and life-like, that they would cause some one or two of his men to snatch a fearful look over the shoulder.", "플래스크가 거듭 \"저 고래\"를 구체적으로 들먹였는데—그는 그 꼬리로 자기 보트의 뱃머리를 쉼 없이 약 올린다고 우겨 대는 그 가상의 괴물을 그렇게 불렀다—그의 이런 언급들은 때로 어찌나 생생하고 실감 났던지, 부하 한둘이 어깨 너머로 겁에 질린 시선을 던지게 만들 정도였다."], ["But this was against all rule; for the oarsmen must put out their eyes, and ram a skewer through their necks; usage pronouncing that they must have no organs but ears, and no limbs but arms, in these critical moments.", "그러나 이것은 온갖 규칙에 어긋나는 짓이었으니, 노잡이들은 제 눈을 도려내고 목에 꼬챙이를 꿰뚫어야 마땅했다. 관례가 말하기를, 이런 위중한 순간에 그들에게는 귀 말고는 어떤 감각기관도 없어야 하고, 팔 말고는 어떤 사지도 없어야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["It was a sight full of quick wonder and awe!", "그것은 순식간의 경이와 외경으로 가득 찬 광경이었다!"], ["The vast swells of the omnipotent sea; the surging, hollow roar they made, as they rolled along the eight gunwales, like gigantic bowls in a boundless bowling-green; the brief suspended agony of the boat, as it would tip for an instant on the knife-like edge of the sharper waves, that almost seemed threatening to cut it in two; the sudden profound dip into the watery glens and hollows; the keen spurrings and goadings to gain the top of the opposite hill; the headlong, sled-like slide down its other side;—all these, with the cries of the headsmen and harpooneers, and the shuddering gasps of the oarsmen, with the wondrous sight of the ivory Pequod bearing down upon her boats with outstretched sails, like a wild hen after her screaming brood;—all this was thrilling.", "전능한 바다의 거대한 너울들, 끝없는 볼링장의 거대한 공처럼 여덟 개의 뱃전을 따라 굴러가며 일으키는 솟구치고 텅 빈 듯한 그 포효, 더 날카로운 파도가 거의 보트를 둘로 베어 버리겠다고 위협하듯 그 칼날 같은 마루 위에서 한순간 보트가 기우뚱 매달릴 때의 그 짧게 멈추어 선 듯한 고통, 물의 골짜기와 우묵한 곳으로 별안간 깊숙이 곤두박질치는 것, 맞은편 언덕 꼭대기에 오르려고 날카롭게 박차를 가하며 몰아세우는 것, 그 반대편 비탈을 썰매처럼 곤두박질쳐 미끄러져 내려오는 것—이 모든 것이, 우두머리 사냥꾼들과 작살잡이들의 외침, 노잡이들의 부르르 떠는 헐떡임, 그리고 돛을 활짝 펴고 제 보트들을 향해 덮쳐 오는 상아빛 피쿼드호가 마치 비명 지르는 새끼들을 좇는 야생 암탉 같던 그 경이로운 광경과 더불어—이 모든 것이 짜릿하기 그지없었다."]], [["Not the raw recruit, marching from the bosom of his wife into the fever heat of his first battle; not the dead man’s ghost encountering the first unknown phantom in the other world;—neither of these can feel stranger and stronger emotions than that man does, who for the first time finds himself pulling into the charmed, churned circle of the hunted sperm whale.", "아내의 품에서 나와 제 첫 전투의 열기 속으로 행군해 들어가는 풋내기 신병도, 저세상에서 처음 보는 미지의 유령과 마주치는 죽은 이의 혼령도—이 둘 중 어느 쪽도, 사냥당하는 향유고래의 마법에 걸린 채 휘저어진 원 속으로 처음으로 노 저어 들어가는 자가 느끼는 것보다 더 기이하고 더 강렬한 감정을 느끼지는 못하리라."]], [["The dancing white water made by the chase was now becoming more and more visible, owing to the increasing darkness of the dun cloud-shadows flung upon the sea.", "추격이 일으킨 춤추는 흰 물결은, 바다 위로 드리워지는 칙칙한 구름 그림자가 점점 더 짙어지는 탓에 이제 점점 더 또렷이 보였다."], ["The jets of vapor no longer blended, but tilted everywhere to right and left; the whales seemed separating their wakes.", "수증기 줄기들은 더는 한데 섞이지 않고 여기저기 좌우로 기울었다. 고래들은 제각기 자취를 갈라놓는 듯했다."], ["The boats were pulled more apart; Starbuck giving chase to three whales running dead to leeward.", "보트들은 더 멀찍이 떨어져 노를 저었고, 스타벅은 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 곧장 달아나는 고래 세 마리를 쫓고 있었다."], ["Our sail was now set, and, with the still rising wind, we rushed along; the boat going with such madness through the water, that the lee oars could scarcely be worked rapidly enough to escape being torn from the row-locks.", "이제 우리의 돛이 펼쳐졌고, 여전히 거세지는 바람을 받으며 우리는 내달렸다. 보트가 어찌나 미친 듯이 물살을 가르고 나아갔던지, 바람 불어가는 쪽 노들은 노받이에서 뜯겨 나가지 않으려고 빨리 놀려도 도무지 따라잡을 수 없을 지경이었다."]], [["Soon we were running through a suffusing wide veil of mist; neither ship nor boat to be seen.", "이윽고 우리는 넓게 퍼진 안개의 장막을 뚫고 달리게 되었으니, 배도 보트도 보이지 않았다."]], [["“Give way, men,”", "\"저어라, 얘들아.\""], ["whispered Starbuck, drawing still further aft the sheet of his sail; “there is time to kill a fish yet before the squall comes.", "스타벅이 돛의 시트를 더욱 선미 쪽으로 당기며 속삭였다. \"돌풍이 닥치기 전에 아직 고래 한 마리 잡을 시간은 있다."], ["There’s white water again!—close to!", "저기 또 흰 물결이다!—바로 가까이에!"], ["Spring!”", "튀어 올라라!\""]], [["Soon after, two cries in quick succession on each side of us denoted that the other boats had got fast; but hardly were they overheard, when with a lightning-like hurtling whisper Starbuck said: “Stand up!”", "곧이어 우리 양옆에서 잇따라 두 차례 터진 외침은 다른 보트들이 고래를 작살로 꽂았음을 알리는 것이었다. 그러나 그 소리가 채 귓전에 닿기도 전에, 스타벅이 번개처럼 휙 스치는 속삭임으로 말했다."], ["and Queequeg, harpoon in hand, sprang to his feet.", "\"일어서!\" 그러자 퀴케그가 손에 작살을 든 채 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["Though not one of the oarsmen was then facing the life and death peril so close to them ahead, yet with their eyes on the intense countenance of the mate in the stern of the boat, they knew that the imminent instant had come; they heard, too, an enormous wallowing sound as of fifty elephants stirring in their litter.", "그때 노잡이들 가운데 그 누구도 바로 앞에 그토록 가까이 닥친 생사의 위험을 마주하고 있지는 않았으되, 그들은 보트 선미에 선 일등항해사의 긴장된 얼굴에 눈길을 두고서, 절박한 순간이 왔음을 알았다. 또한 그들은 코끼리 쉰 마리가 우리 안에서 뒤척이는 듯한 거대한 출렁임 소리를 들었다."], ["Meanwhile the boat was still booming through the mist, the waves curling and hissing around us like the erected crests of enraged serpents.", "그러는 동안 보트는 여전히 안개를 뚫고 우르르 내달렸고, 파도는 성난 뱀들이 곧추세운 볏처럼 우리 주위에서 곱이치며 쉭쉭거렸다."]], [["“That’s his hump.", "\"저게 그놈의 혹이다."], ["There, there, give it to him!”", "저기, 저기, 안겨 줘라!\""], ["whispered Starbuck.", "스타벅이 속삭였다."]], [["A short rushing sound leaped out of the boat; it was the darted iron of Queequeg.", "보트에서 짧고 휙 하는 소리가 솟아 나왔다. 퀴케그가 던진 작살이었다."], ["Then all in one welded commotion came an invisible push from astern, while forward the boat seemed striking on a ledge; the sail collapsed and exploded; a gush of scalding vapor shot up near by; something rolled and tumbled like an earthquake beneath us.", "그러더니 한 덩어리로 뒤엉킨 소동 속에서, 선미 쪽으로부터 보이지 않는 떠밀림이 들이닥쳤고, 한편 뱃머리는 무슨 암붕에라도 부딪치는 듯했다. 돛이 푹 꺼지며 펑 터졌고, 바로 곁에서 펄펄 끓는 듯한 수증기가 확 솟구쳐 올랐으며, 우리 발밑에서는 지진처럼 무언가가 굴러 뒹굴었다."], ["The whole crew were half suffocated as they were tossed helter-skelter into the white curdling cream of the squall.", "선원 전원은 돌풍이 빚어낸 하얗게 응어리진 거품 속으로 뒤죽박죽 내동댕이쳐지면서 반쯤 숨이 막혔다."], ["Squall, whale, and harpoon had all blended together; and the whale, merely grazed by the iron, escaped.", "돌풍과 고래와 작살이 죄다 한데 뒤섞여 버렸고, 작살에 그저 스치기만 한 고래는 달아나 버렸다."]], [["Though completely swamped, the boat was nearly unharmed.", "보트는 완전히 물에 잠겼으되, 거의 멀쩡했다."], ["Swimming round it we picked up the floating oars, and lashing them across the gunwale, tumbled back to our places.", "우리는 그 둘레를 헤엄치며 떠다니는 노들을 건져 올려 뱃전에 가로질러 동여매고는, 굴러떨어지듯 제자리로 돌아갔다."], ["There we sat up to our knees in the sea, the water covering every rib and plank, so that to our downward gazing eyes the suspended craft seemed a coral boat grown up to us from the bottom of the ocean.", "거기서 우리는 바다에 무릎까지 잠긴 채 앉아 있었으니, 물이 모든 늑재와 판자를 뒤덮어, 아래를 내려다보는 우리 눈에는 그 떠 있는 배가 마치 대양 밑바닥에서 우리를 향해 자라 올라온 산호 보트처럼 보였다."]], [["The wind increased to a howl; the waves dashed their bucklers together; the whole squall roared, forked, and crackled around us like a white fire upon the prairie, in which, unconsumed, we were burning; immortal in these jaws of death!", "바람은 울부짖음으로 거세졌고, 파도는 제 방패들을 서로 맞부딪쳤다. 돌풍 전체가 우리 주위에서 포효하고 갈래갈래 갈라지며 탁탁 튀었으니, 마치 대초원 위의 흰 불길 같았고, 우리는 그 속에서 타지 않은 채 타오르고 있었다."], ["In vain we hailed the other boats; as well roar to the live coals down the chimney of a flaming furnace as hail those boats in that storm.", "이 죽음의 아가리 속에서 불멸하는 존재로! 우리는 헛되이 다른 보트들을 불렀다."], ["Meanwhile the driving scud, rack, and mist, grew darker with the shadows of night; no sign of the ship could be seen.", "그 폭풍 속에서 저 보트들을 부른다는 것은 활활 타오르는 용광로의 굴뚝 아래 시뻘건 숯덩이를 향해 고함치는 것이나 마찬가지였다. 그러는 동안 휘몰아치는 물보라와 찢긴 구름과 안개는 밤의 그림자로 점점 어두워졌고, 배의 자취는 어디서도 보이지 않았다."], ["The rising sea forbade all attempts to bale out the boat.", "솟구치는 바다는 보트의 물을 퍼낼 어떤 시도도 허락하지 않았다."], ["The oars were useless as propellers, performing now the office of life-preservers.", "노는 추진기로서는 쓸모가 없어, 이제 구명 도구의 노릇을 하고 있었다."], ["So, cutting the lashing of the waterproof match keg, after many failures Starbuck contrived to ignite the lamp in the lantern; then stretching it on a waif pole, handed it to Queequeg as the standard-bearer of this forlorn hope.", "그래서 스타벅은 방수 처리된 성냥통의 결박을 끊고, 여러 번 실패한 끝에 간신히 등롱 속 램프에 불을 붙였다. 그러고는 그것을 표지 막대에 매달아, 이 가망 없는 사투의 기수로 삼아 퀴케그에게 건넸다."], ["There, then, he sat, holding up that imbecile candle in the heart of that almighty forlornness.", "거기, 그 자리에서, 그는 그 전능한 절망의 한복판에서 저 무력한 촛불을 치켜든 채 앉아 있었다."], ["There, then, he sat, the sign and symbol of a man without faith, hopelessly holding up hope in the midst of despair.", "거기, 그 자리에서, 그는 믿음 없는 인간의 표지이자 상징으로서, 절망 한가운데서 가망 없이 희망을 치켜든 채 앉아 있었다."]], [["Wet, drenched through, and shivering cold, despairing of ship or boat, we lifted up our eyes as the dawn came on.", "흠뻑 젖어 온몸이 으슬으슬 떨리고, 배도 보트도 만나리란 희망을 잃은 채, 우리는 동이 터 오자 눈을 들어 올렸다."], ["The mist still spread over the sea, the empty lantern lay crushed in the bottom of the boat.", "안개는 여전히 바다 위에 퍼져 있었고, 텅 빈 등롱은 보트 바닥에 짓이겨져 나뒹굴었다."], ["Suddenly Queequeg started to his feet, hollowing his hand to his ear.", "별안간 퀴케그가 벌떡 일어서더니 손을 오므려 귀에 갖다 댔다."], ["We all heard a faint creaking, as of ropes and yards hitherto muffled by the storm.", "우리 모두 폭풍에 가려 그때껏 들리지 않던 밧줄과 활대의 희미한 삐걱거림 같은 소리를 들었다."], ["The sound came nearer and nearer; the thick mists were dimly parted by a huge, vague form.", "그 소리는 점점 가까워졌다. 짙은 안개가 거대하고 흐릿한 형체에 의해 어슴푸레 갈라졌다."], ["Affrighted, we all sprang into the sea as the ship at last loomed into view, bearing right down upon us within a distance of not much more than its length.", "우리는 기겁하여 다 함께 바다로 뛰어들었으니, 마침내 모습을 드러낸 배가 제 배 길이보다 그리 멀지 않은 거리 안으로 곧장 우리를 향해 덮쳐 왔기 때문이었다."]], [["Floating on the waves we saw the abandoned boat, as for one instant it tossed and gaped beneath the ship’s bows like a chip at the base of a cataract; and then the vast hull rolled over it, and it was seen no more till it came up weltering astern.", "파도 위에 떠서 우리는 버려진 보트를 보았는데, 그것은 한순간 폭포 밑바닥의 나무 부스러기처럼 배의 뱃머리 아래에서 까딱이며 입을 떡 벌렸다. 그러더니 그 거대한 선체가 그 위로 굴러 넘어갔고, 보트는 선미 쪽으로 허우적대며 떠오르기 전까지는 다시 보이지 않았다."], ["Again we swam for it, were dashed against it by the seas, and were at last taken up and safely landed on board.", "우리는 다시 그쪽으로 헤엄쳤고, 파도에 떠밀려 그것에 부딪쳤다가, 마침내 끌어 올려져 무사히 배 위로 옮겨졌다."], ["Ere the squall came close to, the other boats had cut loose from their fish and returned to the ship in good time.", "돌풍이 가까이 닥치기 전에 다른 보트들은 제 고래를 끊어 버리고 제때 배로 돌아와 있었다."], ["The ship had given us up, but was still cruising, if haply it might light upon some token of our perishing,—an oar or a lance pole.", "배는 우리를 단념했으되, 혹시라도 우리가 죽었음을 알리는 어떤 표적—노 한 자루나 창 자루 하나라도—마주칠까 하여 여전히 바다를 누비고 있었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 49. The Hyena.", "# CHAPTER 49. The Hyena."]], [["There are certain queer times and occasions in this strange mixed affair we call life when a man takes this whole universe for a vast practical joke, though the wit thereof he but dimly discerns, and more than suspects that the joke is at nobody’s expense but his own.", "우리가 인생이라 부르는 이 기묘하게 뒤섞인 사건 속에는, 한 사내가 이 우주 전체를 거대한 짓궂은 장난으로 여기게 되는 야릇한 순간과 경우가 더러 있다. 비록 그 농담의 묘미를 그는 어렴풋이밖에 알아채지 못하지만, 그 농담이 다른 누구도 아닌 바로 자기 자신을 겨냥한 것임을 어렴풋이 짐작하는 정도를 넘어선다."], ["However, nothing dispirits, and nothing seems worth while disputing.", "그러나 그 무엇도 그의 기를 꺾지 못하고, 그 무엇도 따져 볼 가치가 있어 보이지 않는다."], ["He bolts down all events, all creeds, and beliefs, and persuasions, all hard things visible and invisible, never mind how knobby; as an ostrich of potent digestion gobbles down bullets and gun flints.", "그는 모든 사건과 모든 신조와 믿음과 신념을, 보이는 것이든 보이지 않는 것이든 온갖 딱딱한 것들을 그것이 아무리 울퉁불퉁하든 아랑곳없이 통째로 삼켜 버린다. 마치 소화력이 강한 타조가 총알과 부싯돌을 꿀꺽 삼키듯이."], ["And as for small difficulties and worryings, prospects of sudden disaster, peril of life and limb; all these, and death itself, seem to him only sly, good-natured hits, and jolly punches in the side bestowed by the unseen and unaccountable old joker.", "그리고 사소한 곤경과 근심거리, 갑작스러운 재앙의 전망, 목숨과 사지가 처한 위험, 이 모든 것이, 그리고 죽음 그 자체마저도, 그에게는 보이지 않고 헤아릴 수 없는 저 늙은 익살꾼이 슬쩍 던지는 짓궂으면서도 마음씨 좋은 한 방, 옆구리를 유쾌하게 쿡 찌르는 농에 지나지 않게 보인다."], ["That odd sort of wayward mood I am speaking of, comes over a man only in some time of extreme tribulation; it comes in the very midst of his earnestness, so that what just before might have seemed to him a thing most momentous, now seems but a part of the general joke.", "내가 말하는 그 묘한 변덕스러운 기분은, 오직 극심한 환난의 시기에만 사람을 찾아온다. 그것은 그가 한창 진지한 와중에 찾아오기에, 바로 조금 전까지만 해도 더없이 중대해 보이던 일이 이제는 그저 저 보편적 농담의 한 부분으로만 보이게 된다."], ["There is nothing like the perils of whaling to breed this free and easy sort of genial, desperado philosophy; and with it I now regarded this whole voyage of the Pequod, and the great White Whale its object.", "이런 거리낌 없고 느긋하며 호방한, 무뢰한의 철학을 길러 내는 데에는 고래잡이의 위험만 한 것이 없다. 그리고 나는 이제 그런 마음가짐으로 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 이 항해 전부와, 그 목표인 저 거대한 흰 고래를 바라보았다."]], [["“Queequeg,”", "\"퀴퀘그(Queequeg).\""], ["said I, when they had dragged me, the last man, to the deck, and I was still shaking myself in my jacket to fling off the water; “Queequeg, my fine friend, does this sort of thing often happen?”", "그들이 맨 마지막으로 나를 갑판 위로 끌어올렸고, 내가 아직도 웃옷을 흔들어 물을 털어 내고 있을 때 나는 말했다. \"퀴퀘그, 나의 멋진 친구여, 이런 일이 자주 일어나는 건가?\""], ["Without much emotion, though soaked through just like me, he gave me to understand that such things did often happen.", "나와 꼭 마찬가지로 흠뻑 젖었으면서도 그는 별다른 감정 없이, 이런 일이 자주 일어난다는 뜻을 내게 전했다."]], [["“Mr. Stubb,”", "\"스터브(Stubb) 씨.\""], ["said I, turning to that worthy, who, buttoned up in his oil-jacket, was now calmly smoking his pipe in the rain; “Mr.", "나는 그 훌륭한 사내 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. 그는 기름 먹인 웃옷의 단추를 채운 채, 이제 빗속에서 태연히 파이프를 피우고 있었다."], ["Stubb, I think I have heard you say that of all whalemen you ever met, our chief mate, Mr. Starbuck, is by far the most careful and prudent.", "\"스터브 씨, 당신이 여태껏 만난 모든 고래잡이 중에서 우리 일등항해사 스타벅(Starbuck) 씨가 단연 가장 조심스럽고 신중한 사람이라고 말하는 걸 들은 것 같은데."], ["I suppose then, that going plump on a flying whale with your sail set in a foggy squall is the height of a whaleman’s discretion?”", "그렇다면 안개 낀 돌풍 속에서 돛을 활짝 펴고 달아나는 고래에게 곧장 돌진하는 것이 고래잡이의 신중함의 극치라는 말이오?\""]], [["“Certain.", "\"물론이지."], ["I’ve lowered for whales from a leaking ship in a gale off Cape Horn.”", "나는 케이프 혼(Cape Horn) 앞바다 강풍 속에서, 물이 새는 배에서도 고래 잡으러 보트를 내린 적이 있다네.\""]], [["“Mr. Flask,”", "\"플래스크(Flask) 씨.\""], ["said I, turning to little King-Post, who was standing close by; “you are experienced in these things, and I am not.", "나는 바로 곁에 서 있던 키 작은 킹포스트에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"당신은 이런 일에 경험이 많고 나는 그렇지 못하오."], ["Will you tell me whether it is an unalterable law in this fishery, Mr. Flask, for an oarsman to break his own back pulling himself back-foremost into death’s jaws?”", "플래스크 씨, 이 어업에서 노잡이가 자기 등이 부러지도록 노를 저어 거꾸로 죽음의 아가리 속으로 끌려 들어가는 것이 결코 바뀌지 않는 법칙인지 말해 주겠소?\""]], [["“Can’t you twist that smaller?”", "\"그걸 좀 더 간단히 비틀 순 없나?\""], ["said Flask.", "플래스크가 말했다."], ["“Yes, that’s the law.", "\"그래, 그게 법칙이지."], ["I should like to see a boat’s crew backing water up to a whale face foremost.", "나는 보트의 선원이 고래를 정면으로 마주 보면서 물을 거꾸로 저어 가는 꼴을 한번 보고 싶군."], ["Ha, ha!", "하하!"], ["the whale would give them squint for squint, mind that!”", "그러면 고래가 그들에게 곁눈질에는 곁눈질로 응수해 줄 걸세, 명심하라고!\""]], [["Here then, from three impartial witnesses, I had a deliberate statement of the entire case. Considering, therefore, that squalls and capsizings in the water and consequent bivouacks on the deep, were matters of common occurrence in this kind of life; considering that at the superlatively critical instant of going on to the whale I must resign my life into the hands of him who steered the boat—oftentimes a fellow who at that very moment is in his impetuousness upon the point of scuttling the craft with his own frantic stampings; considering that the particular disaster to our own particular boat was chiefly to be imputed to Starbuck’s driving on to his whale almost in the teeth of a squall, and considering that Starbuck, notwithstanding, was famous for his great heedfulness in the fishery; considering that I belonged to this uncommonly prudent Starbuck’s boat; and finally considering in what a devil’s chase I was implicated, touching the White Whale: taking all things together, I say, I thought I might as well go below and make a rough draft of my will. “Queequeg,” said I, “come along, you shall be my lawyer, executor, and legatee.”", "이렇게 해서 나는 세 사람의 공정한 증인으로부터 이 사건 전체에 관한 신중한 진술을 얻은 셈이었다. 그러니 돌풍과 물속에서의 전복, 그리고 그에 따라 깊은 바다 위에서 야영하듯 떠 있는 일이 이런 종류의 삶에서는 흔히 일어나는 일임을 헤아려 보면. 또한 고래에게 돌진하는 더없이 위태로운 그 순간에, 나는 보트를 모는 자의 손에 내 목숨을 내맡길 수밖에 없으며—그자는 흔히 바로 그 순간 자신의 격렬함에 사로잡혀, 미친 듯이 발을 굴러 제 손으로 배를 침몰시키려는 지경에 이른 사내임을 헤아려 보면. 또한 우리 보트가 당한 특정한 재난이 주로, 거의 돌풍의 정면에 맞서다시피 자신의 고래에게 돌진한 스타벅의 탓으로 돌려야 할 일이었음을 헤아려 보면. 그러면서도 스타벅이 어업에서 더없이 신중하기로 이름난 사람이었음을 헤아려 보면. 또한 내가 이 유달리 사려 깊은 스타벅의 보트에 속해 있었음을 헤아려 보면. 그리고 끝으로, 흰 고래를 두고 내가 얼마나 악마 같은 추격에 얽혀 들었는가를 헤아려 보면. 이 모든 것을 한데 묶어 보건대, 나는 차라리 아래로 내려가 유언장 초안이나 대충 써 두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다. \"퀴퀘그.\" 나는 말했다. \"이리 오게. 자네가 내 변호사이자 유언 집행자이며 유산 상속인이 되어 주게.\""]], [["It may seem strange that of all men sailors should be tinkering at their last wills and testaments, but there are no people in the world more fond of that diversion.", "모든 사람 중에서 하필 뱃사람들이 자기 유언장을 만지작거린다는 것은 이상하게 보일지 모르나, 세상에 그 소일거리를 그들보다 더 좋아하는 사람은 없다."], ["This was the fourth time in my nautical life that I had done the same thing.", "이번 일은 내가 뱃사람으로 살아오면서 같은 짓을 한 네 번째였다."], ["After the ceremony was concluded upon the present occasion, I felt all the easier; a stone was rolled away from my heart.", "이번에도 그 의식을 마치고 나니, 한결 마음이 가벼워졌다. 가슴 위에서 돌덩이 하나가 굴러 떨어진 듯했다."], ["Besides, all the days I should now live would be as good as the days that Lazarus lived after his resurrection; a supplementary clean gain of so many months or weeks as the case might be.", "게다가 이제부터 내가 살아갈 모든 날들은, 부활한 뒤에 나사로가 살았던 날들만큼이나 값진 것이리라. 형편에 따라 몇 달이든 몇 주든, 깨끗이 덤으로 얻은 이득인 셈이었다."], ["I survived myself; my death and burial were locked up in my chest.", "나는 나 자신을 넘어 살아남았다. 내 죽음과 매장은 내 궤짝 속에 단단히 잠겨 있었다."], ["I looked round me tranquilly and contentedly, like a quiet ghost with a clean conscience sitting inside the bars of a snug family vault.", "나는 차분하고 흡족한 마음으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 마치 떳떳한 양심을 지닌 채 아늑한 가족 납골당 창살 안에 앉아 있는 조용한 유령처럼."]], [["Now then, thought I, unconsciously rolling up the sleeves of my frock, here goes for a cool, collected dive at death and destruction, and the devil fetch the hindmost.", "자, 이제, 하고 나는 생각하며 나도 모르게 작업복 소매를 걷어 올렸다. 죽음과 파멸을 향해 냉정하고 침착하게 뛰어들 테니, 뒤처지는 놈은 악마에게나 잡혀가라지."]], [["# CHAPTER 50. Ahab’s Boat and Crew. Fedallah.", "# CHAPTER 50. Ahab’s Boat and Crew. Fedallah."]], [["“Who would have thought it, Flask!”", "\"누가 그런 일을 상상이나 했겠나, 플래스크!\" 스터브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Stubb; “if I had but one leg you would not catch me in a boat, unless maybe to stop the plug-hole with my timber toe.", "\"내가 만약 다리가 하나뿐이었다면, 자네는 결코 나를 보트 안에서 보지 못했을 걸세. 굳이 본다면 내 나무 발가락으로 배수 구멍이나 틀어막을 때쯤이겠지."], ["Oh!", "아아!"], ["he’s a wonderful old man!”", "그분은 정말 대단한 노인이야!\""]], [["“I don’t think it so strange, after all, on that account,”", "\"난 그 점에 관해서라면 그렇게 이상하다고는 생각하지 않네.\""], ["said Flask.", "플래스크가 말했다."], ["“If his leg were off at the hip, now, it would be a different thing.", "\"이제 만약 그분 다리가 엉덩이께에서 잘려 나갔다면, 그건 사정이 다르겠지."], ["That would disable him; but he has one knee, and good part of the other left, you know.”", "그러면 아주 못 쓰게 되는 거니까. 하지만 그분에게는 한쪽 무릎이 멀쩡하고, 다른 쪽도 상당 부분이 남아 있잖나.\""]], [["“I don’t know that, my little man; I never yet saw him kneel.”", "\"그건 나도 모르겠네, 이 작은 친구야. 나는 아직 그분이 무릎 꿇는 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없으니까.\""]], [["Among whale-wise people it has often been argued whether, considering the paramount importance of his life to the success of the voyage, it is right for a whaling captain to jeopardize that life in the active perils of the chase.", "고래에 정통한 사람들 사이에서는 종종 이런 논쟁이 벌어지곤 했다. 선장의 생명이 항해의 성공에 더없이 중대하다는 점을 고려할 때, 고래잡이 선장이 추격의 능동적인 위험 속에 자기 목숨을 내거는 것이 과연 옳은가 하는 문제였다."], ["So Tamerlane’s soldiers often argued with tears in their eyes, whether that invaluable life of his ought to be carried into the thickest of the fight.", "마찬가지로 티무르의 병사들도 눈물을 글썽이며 자주 논쟁을 벌였으니, 그토록 값진 그의 생명을 싸움의 가장 치열한 한복판으로 끌고 들어가야 하는가 하는 것이었다."]], [["But with Ahab the question assumed a modified aspect.", "그러나 에이해브에게 이르러서는 그 문제가 다소 변형된 양상을 띠었다."], ["Considering that with two legs man is but a hobbling wight in all times of danger; considering that the pursuit of whales is always under great and extraordinary difficulties; that every individual moment, indeed, then comprises a peril; under these circumstances is it wise for any maimed man to enter a whale-boat in the hunt?", "인간이란 두 다리가 있어도 위험한 순간이면 모두 절뚝거리는 신세에 지나지 않는다는 점을 고려할 때, 고래 추격이란 언제나 크고 비상한 곤경 아래 이루어진다는 점을, 실로 그때는 매 순간순간이 하나하나 위험을 품고 있다는 점을 고려할 때, 이러한 상황에서 불구의 몸을 한 사람이 사냥에 나선 고래잡이 보트에 오르는 것이 과연 현명한 일인가?"], ["As a general thing, the joint-owners of the Pequod must have plainly thought not.", "대체로 보아, 피쿼드호의 공동 선주들은 분명 그렇지 않다고 여겼을 것이다."]], [["Ahab well knew that although his friends at home would think little of his entering a boat in certain comparatively harmless vicissitudes of the chase, for the sake of being near the scene of action and giving his orders in person, yet for Captain Ahab to have a boat actually apportioned to him as a regular headsman in the hunt—above all for Captain Ahab to be supplied with five extra men, as that same boat’s crew, he well knew that such generous conceits never entered the heads of the owners of the Pequod.", "에이해브는 잘 알고 있었다. 비교적 해롭지 않은 추격의 어떤 우여곡절 속에서, 행동의 현장 가까이에 머물며 몸소 명령을 내리기 위해 그가 보트에 오르는 일이라면, 고향의 친구들은 대수롭지 않게 여길 터였다."], ["Therefore he had not solicited a boat’s crew from them, nor had he in any way hinted his desires on that head.", "그러나 에이해브 선장에게 정식 작살 두목으로서 보트 한 척이 실제로 배정되고 — 무엇보다 에이해브 선장에게 그 보트의 선원으로 다섯 명의 별도 인원이 주어진다는 것은, 그런 후한 발상 따위가 피쿼드호 선주들의 머릿속에는 결코 떠오르지 않으리라는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었다. 그러므로 그는 선주들에게 보트 선원을 청하지도 않았고, 그 문제에 관한 자신의 바람을 어떤 식으로도 내비치지 않았다."], ["Nevertheless he had taken private measures of his own touching all that matter.", "그럼에도 그는 그 모든 일과 관련하여 은밀히 자기 나름의 조치를 취해 두었다. 카바코가 그 사실을 공공연히 폭로하기 전까지, 선원들은 그것을 거의 예측하지 못했다."], ["Until Cabaco’s published discovery, the sailors had little foreseen it, though to be sure when, after being a little while out of port, all hands had concluded the customary business of fitting the whaleboats for service; when some time after this Ahab was now and then found bestirring himself in the matter of making thole-pins with his own hands for what was thought to be one of the spare boats, and even solicitously cutting the small wooden skewers, which when the line is running out are pinned over the groove in the bow: when all this was observed in him, and particularly his solicitude in having an extra coat of sheathing in the bottom of the boat, as if to make it better withstand the pointed pressure of his ivory limb; and also the anxiety he evinced in exactly shaping the thigh board, or clumsy cleat, as it is sometimes called, the horizontal piece in the boat’s bow for bracing the knee against in darting or stabbing at the whale; when it was observed how often he stood up in that boat with his solitary knee fixed in the semi-circular depression in the cleat, and with the carpenter’s chisel gouged out a little here and straightened it a little there; all these things, I say, had awakened much interest and curiosity at the time.", "물론 항구를 떠난 지 얼마쯤 지났을 때, 모든 선원이 고래잡이 보트를 작업에 쓸 수 있도록 정비하는 통상적인 일을 마무리한 뒤, 그로부터 얼마간 시간이 흐른 무렵에 예비 보트 중 하나로 여겨지던 것을 위해 에이해브가 이따금 직접 노받이 못을 만드는 일에 분주히 손을 놀리는 모습이 눈에 띄었고, 심지어 밧줄이 풀려 나갈 때 뱃머리의 홈 위에 꽂아 두는 작은 나무 쐐기까지 정성껏 깎고 있는 것이 목격되기도 했다. 이 모든 것이 그에게서 관찰되었을 때, 특히 그가 보트 바닥에 덧대는 판자를 한 겹 더 깔아 두는 데 보이던 그 정성 — 마치 자신의 상아 다리가 가하는 뾰족한 압력을 더 잘 견디게 하려는 듯한 — 이 눈에 띄었을 때, 또한 허벅지받이판, 혹은 흔히 투박한 받침대라고도 불리는, 고래에게 작살을 던지거나 창을 찌를 때 무릎을 받쳐 버티는 데 쓰는 보트 뱃머리의 그 수평 목재를 정확한 모양으로 다듬는 데 그가 보이던 그 노심초사가 목격되었을 때, 그가 얼마나 자주 그 보트 안에 일어서서 외로운 한쪽 무릎을 그 받침대의 반원형 홈에 끼우고는, 목수의 끌로 여기를 조금 파내고 저기를 조금 곧게 다듬었는지가 관찰되었을 때 — 이 모든 일이, 다시 말하건대, 그 당시에 적지 않은 흥미와 호기심을 불러일으켰던 것이다."], ["But almost everybody supposed that this particular preparative heedfulness in Ahab must only be with a view to the ultimate chase of Moby Dick; for he had already revealed his intention to hunt that mortal monster in person.", "그러나 거의 모든 이가 에이해브의 이 유난스러운 준비성과 세심함이 오로지 모비 딕을 향한 최후의 추격을 염두에 둔 것이라고만 짐작했다. 왜냐하면 그는 이미 그 치명적인 괴물을 몸소 사냥하겠다는 의도를 드러낸 바 있었기 때문이다."], ["But such a supposition did by no means involve the remotest suspicion as to any boat’s crew being assigned to that boat.", "그러나 그러한 짐작에는 그 보트에 어떤 선원이 배정되리라는 데 대한 일말의 의심조차 결코 포함되어 있지 않았다."]], [["Now, with the subordinate phantoms, what wonder remained soon waned away; for in a whaler wonders soon wane. Besides, now and then such unaccountable odds and ends of strange nations come up from the unknown nooks and ash-holes of the earth to man these floating outlaws of whalers; and the ships themselves often pick up such queer castaway creatures found tossing about the open sea on planks, bits of wreck, oars, whaleboats, canoes, blown-off Japanese junks, and what not; that Beelzebub himself might climb up the side and step down into the cabin to chat with the captain, and it would not create any unsubduable excitement in the forecastle.", "자, 그 부속 유령들에 관해서는 남아 있던 의아함마저 곧 사그라들었다. 고래잡이 배에서는 놀라움이란 금세 시드는 법이니까. 게다가 이따금 그런 도무지 설명할 길 없는 갖가지 잡동사니 같은 이방 인종들이 땅의 알려지지 않은 구석과 재 구덩이에서 솟아 나와, 이 떠다니는 고래잡이 무법자 배들에 인원을 채우곤 한다. 또한 배들 자체도 종종 그런 기이하게 표류하던 생명체들을 건져 올리는데, 그들은 망망대해 위에서 널빤지나 난파선 조각, 노, 고래잡이 보트, 카누, 바람에 떠밀려 온 일본 정크선, 그 밖의 온갖 것들에 매달려 까닥거리고 있던 자들이다. 그러니 바알세불 본인이 뱃전을 기어올라 선실로 내려와 선장과 한담을 나눈다 한들, 그것은 선원실에 그 어떤 누를 수 없는 동요도 일으키지 못할 것이다."]], [["But be all this as it may, certain it is that while the subordinate phantoms soon found their place among the crew, though still as it were somehow distinct from them, yet that hair-turbaned Fedallah remained a muffled mystery to the last.", "그러나 그 모든 것이 어떠하든 간에, 한 가지 확실한 것은, 그 부속 유령들은 비록 여전히 어쩐지 다른 선원들과 구별되는 듯하기는 했어도 곧 선원들 사이에서 자기 자리를 찾았으나, 머리에 터번을 두른 저 페달라만은 끝끝내 베일에 싸인 수수께끼로 남았다는 점이다. 이처럼 점잖은 세상에 그가 대체 어디서 왔는지, 어떤 종류의 설명할 수 없는 끈으로 그가 그토록 빨리 에이해브의 기이한 운명과 얽혀 있음을 드러냈는지, 아니, 어느 정도까지는 은근히 암시되는 영향력을 — 하늘만이 알겠지만 어쩌면 그에 대한 권위마저도 — 지니게 되었는지, 이 모든 것을 아는 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["Whence he came in a mannerly world like this, by what sort of unaccountable tie he soon evinced himself to be linked with Ahab’s peculiar fortunes; nay, so far as to have some sort of a half-hinted influence; Heaven knows, but it might have been even authority over him; all this none knew.", "그러나 누구도 페달라에 관해서만은 무관심한 태도를 유지할 수 없었다. 그는 온대 지방의 문명화된 가정적인 사람들이 오직 꿈에서나 볼 법한, 그것도 흐릿하게만 볼 법한 그런 존재였다."], ["But one cannot sustain an indifferent air concerning Fedallah.", "그러나 그와 비슷한 자들은 변함없는 아시아의 공동체들, 특히 대륙 동쪽의 동방 섬들 사이를 이따금 미끄러지듯 오간다."], ["He was such a creature as civilized, domestic people in the temperate zone only see in their dreams, and that but dimly; but the like of whom now and then glide among the unchanging Asiatic communities, especially the Oriental isles to the east of the continent—those insulated, immemorial, unalterable countries, which even in these modern days still preserve much of the ghostly aboriginalness of earth’s primal generations, when the memory of the first man was a distinct recollection, and all men his descendants, unknowing whence he came, eyed each other as real phantoms, and asked of the sun and the moon why they were created and to what end; when though, according to Genesis, the angels indeed consorted with the daughters of men, the devils also, add the uncanonical Rabbins, indulged in mundane amours.", "그 외따로 떨어진, 까마득히 오래된, 결코 변하지 않는 나라들은 이 현대에 이르러서도 여전히 지구의 원초적 세대들이 지녔던 유령 같은 원시성을 상당 부분 간직하고 있으니, 그것은 첫 사람에 대한 기억이 아직 또렷한 회상으로 남아 있던 시절, 그리고 그의 후손인 모든 인간이 자신이 어디서 왔는지도 모른 채 서로를 진짜 유령처럼 바라보며, 해와 달을 향해 어찌하여 그것들이 창조되었으며 무슨 목적을 위함이냐고 묻던 시절의 것이다. 그때는, 창세기에 따르면 천사들이 실로 사람의 딸들과 어울렸고, 또한 외경에 속한 랍비들의 말을 덧붙이자면 악마들조차 이 세상의 사랑놀음에 탐닉했던 시절이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 51. The Spirit-Spout.", "# CHAPTER 51. The Spirit-Spout."]], [["Days, weeks passed, and under easy sail, the ivory Pequod had slowly swept across four several cruising-grounds; that off the Azores; off the Cape de Verdes; on the Plate (so called), being off the mouth of the Rio de la Plata; and the Carrol Ground, an unstaked, watery locality, southerly from St.", "여러 날, 여러 주가 흘러갔다. 상아로 무장한 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 편안한 돛을 펼친 채 네 군데의 서로 다른 어장을 천천히 가로질렀다."], ["Helena.", "아조레스 근해, 카보베르데 근해, 라플라타강 어귀에 있어 흔히 플레이트라 불리는 어장, 그리고 세인트헬레나섬에서 남쪽으로 떨어진, 표지 하나 없는 망망한 바다 캐럴 어장이 그것이었다."]], [["It was while gliding through these latter waters that one serene and moonlight night, when all the waves rolled by like scrolls of silver; and, by their soft, suffusing seethings, made what seemed a silvery silence, not a solitude; on such a silent night a silvery jet was seen far in advance of the white bubbles at the bow.", "이 마지막 바다를 미끄러지듯 지나던 어느 고요하고 달 밝은 밤이었다. 물결이 모조리 은빛 두루마리처럼 굽이쳐 흘러가고, 그 부드럽게 번져 가는 들끓음으로 인해 적막이라기보다는 차라리 은빛 정적이라 할 만한 것이 펼쳐졌다."], ["Lit up by the moon, it looked celestial; seemed some plumed and glittering god uprising from the sea.", "그런 고요한 밤에, 뱃머리의 하얀 거품 떼보다 훨씬 앞쪽에서 은빛 물줄기 하나가 솟구치는 것이 보였다."], ["Fedallah first descried this jet.", "달빛을 받아 그것은 천상의 것처럼 보였다."], ["For of these moonlight nights, it was his wont to mount to the main-mast head, and stand a look-out there, with the same precision as if it had been day.", "마치 깃털을 두르고 번쩍이는 어떤 신이 바다에서 솟아오르는 듯했다. 이 물줄기를 가장 먼저 알아본 것은 페달라였다."], ["And yet, though herds of whales were seen by night, not one whaleman in a hundred would venture a lowering for them.", "이런 달 밝은 밤이면 그는 큰돛대 꼭대기에 올라가, 마치 대낮이기라도 한 듯 똑같이 정확하게 망을 보는 것이 버릇이었기 때문이다."], ["You may think with what emotions, then, the seamen beheld this old Oriental perched aloft at such unusual hours; his turban and the moon, companions in one sky.", "그런데 밤에 고래 떼가 보인다 한들, 백 명 가운데 한 명도 그것을 잡으러 보트를 내리겠다고 나설 고래잡이는 없었다. 그러니 선원들이 이 늙은 동방인이 그토록 야릇한 시각에 높은 곳에 걸터앉아 있는 모습을 어떤 심정으로 바라보았을지 짐작이 갈 것이다."], ["But when, after spending his uniform interval there for several successive nights without uttering a single sound; when, after all this silence, his unearthly voice was heard announcing that silvery, moon-lit jet, every reclining mariner started to his feet as if some winged spirit had lighted in the rigging, and hailed the mortal crew.", "그의 터번과 달이 한 하늘 아래 동무로 떠 있었다. 그러나 그가 며칠 밤을 잇따라 한결같은 간격을 지켜 그곳에 머물면서도 단 한 마디 소리도 내지 않다가, 그 모든 침묵 끝에 마침내 그 은빛 달빛 물줄기를 알리는 기괴한 목소리가 들려왔을 때, 누워 있던 선원들은 마치 날개 돋친 정령이 삭구에 내려앉아 죽을 운명의 선원들을 부르기라도 한 듯 저마다 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["“There she blows!”", "\"저기 물을 뿜는다!\""], ["Had the trump of judgment blown, they could not have quivered more; yet still they felt no terror; rather pleasure.", "최후 심판의 나팔이 울렸다 한들 그들이 그보다 더 떨지는 못했으리라. 그러나 그들은 공포를 느끼지 않았다."], ["For though it was a most unwonted hour, yet so impressive was the cry, and so deliriously exciting, that almost every soul on board instinctively desired a lowering.", "오히려 기쁨을 느꼈다. 비록 더없이 별난 시각이었으나, 그 외침은 너무도 인상적이고 너무도 정신없이 흥분되는 것이어서, 배에 탄 거의 모든 영혼이 본능적으로 보트를 내리고 싶어 했던 것이다."]], [["Walking the deck with quick, side-lunging strides, Ahab commanded the t’gallant sails and royals to be set, and every stunsail spread.", "에이해브(Ahab)는 옆으로 몸을 내던지듯 성큼성큼 빠른 걸음으로 갑판을 거닐며, 윗돛과 맨 윗돛을 올리고 보조돛을 빠짐없이 펼치라 명령했다."], ["The best man in the ship must take the helm.", "배에서 가장 솜씨 좋은 자가 키를 잡아야 했다."], ["Then, with every mast-head manned, the piled-up craft rolled down before the wind.", "그리하여 돛대 꼭대기마다 망꾼이 배치된 채, 돛을 잔뜩 쌓아 올린 배는 바람을 안고 내달렸다."], ["The strange, upheaving, lifting tendency of the taffrail breeze filling the hollows of so many sails, made the buoyant, hovering deck to feel like air beneath the feet; while still she rushed along, as if two antagonistic influences were struggling in her—one to mount direct to heaven, the other to drive yawingly to some horizontal goal.", "고물 쪽에서 불어오는 바람이 그 숱한 돛의 빈 공간을 채우며 야릇하게 솟구쳐 들어 올리는 기세가, 둥실 떠올라 맴도는 듯한 갑판을 발밑에 마치 허공인 양 느끼게 했다. 그러면서도 배는 여전히 앞으로 돌진했으니, 마치 두 개의 상반된 힘이 배 안에서 다투는 듯했다."], ["And had you watched Ahab’s face that night, you would have thought that in him also two different things were warring.", "하나는 곧장 하늘로 솟구치려는 힘이요, 다른 하나는 좌우로 흔들리며 어떤 수평의 목표를 향해 내달리려는 힘이었다. 그날 밤 에이해브의 얼굴을 보았더라면, 그의 안에서도 두 가지 서로 다른 것이 싸우고 있다고 여겼으리라."], ["While his one live leg made lively echoes along the deck, every stroke of his dead limb sounded like a coffin-tap.", "그의 살아 있는 한쪽 다리가 갑판 위에 경쾌한 메아리를 울리는 동안, 죽은 다리가 내딛는 걸음걸음은 관을 두드리는 소리처럼 들렸다."], ["On life and death this old man walked.", "이 늙은이는 삶과 죽음을 딛고 걸었다."], ["But though the ship so swiftly sped, and though from every eye, like arrows, the eager glances shot, yet the silvery jet was no more seen that night.", "그러나 배가 그토록 쏜살같이 내달리고, 모든 눈이 화살처럼 간절한 시선을 쏘아 보냈건만, 그 은빛 물줄기는 그날 밤 다시는 보이지 않았다."], ["Every sailor swore he saw it once, but not a second time.", "선원마다 한 번은 분명히 보았노라 맹세했으나, 두 번째로 본 자는 없었다."]], [["This midnight-spout had almost grown a forgotten thing, when, some days after, lo!", "이 한밤중의 물줄기는 거의 잊힌 것이 되어 가던 차에, 며칠 뒤, 보라! 똑같이 고요한 시각에 그것이 또다시 알려졌다."], ["at the same silent hour, it was again announced: again it was descried by all; but upon making sail to overtake it, once more it disappeared as if it had never been.", "다시 모두가 그것을 알아보았다. 그러나 돛을 올려 뒤쫓으려 들자, 마치 처음부터 있지도 않았던 것처럼 또 한 번 사라져 버렸다."], ["And so it served us night after night, till no one heeded it but to wonder at it.", "그렇게 그것은 밤마다 우리를 농락했고, 마침내 아무도 그것을 마음에 두지 않은 채 그저 의아해할 뿐이었다."], ["Mysteriously jetted into the clear moonlight, or starlight, as the case might be; disappearing again for one whole day, or two days, or three; and somehow seeming at every distinct repetition to be advancing still further and further in our van, this solitary jet seemed for ever alluring us on.", "때로는 맑은 달빛 속으로, 때로는 별빛 속으로 신비롭게 솟구쳐 올랐다가, 다시 하루나 이틀, 사흘씩 자취를 감추곤 했다. 그리고 어쩐 일인지 또렷이 되풀이될 때마다 우리 앞쪽으로 점점 더 멀리, 더 멀리 나아가는 듯이 보이는 이 외로운 물줄기는, 영영 우리를 앞으로 꾀어내는 것만 같았다."]], [["Nor with the immemorial superstition of their race, and in accordance with the preternaturalness, as it seemed, which in many things invested the Pequod, were there wanting some of the seamen who swore that whenever and wherever descried; at however remote times, or in however far apart latitudes and longitudes, that unnearable spout was cast by one self-same whale; and that whale, Moby Dick.", "그들 종족의 태고 적부터 내려온 미신과, 또 여러 면에서 피쿼드호를 휘감은 듯 보이던 그 초자연적 기운에 걸맞게도, 선원들 가운데에는 그것이 언제 어디서 보이든, 아무리 동떨어진 시각이든, 아무리 멀리 떨어진 위도와 경도든, 그 다가갈 수 없는 물줄기는 단 하나의 똑같은 고래가 뿜어 올린 것이며, 그 고래가 바로 모비 딕(Moby Dick)이라고 맹세하는 자들이 없지 않았다."], ["For a time, there reigned, too, a sense of peculiar dread at this flitting apparition, as if it were treacherously beckoning us on and on, in order that the monster might turn round upon us, and rend us at last in the remotest and most savage seas.", "한동안은 이 어른거리는 환영에 대해 묘한 공포가 감돌기도 했으니, 마치 그것이 음흉하게 우리를 앞으로, 또 앞으로 손짓해 불러내어, 그 괴물이 마침내 가장 외지고 가장 사나운 바다에서 우리에게 돌아서서 우리를 갈가리 찢어 놓으려는 것만 같았다."]], [["These temporary apprehensions, so vague but so awful, derived a wondrous potency from the contrasting serenity of the weather, in which, beneath all its blue blandness, some thought there lurked a devilish charm, as for days and days we voyaged along, through seas so wearily, lonesomely mild, that all space, in repugnance to our vengeful errand, seemed vacating itself of life before our urn-like prow.", "이 막연하지만 너무도 끔찍한 일시적 불안은, 대조를 이루는 날씨의 고요함에서 놀라운 힘을 얻었다. 그 푸르른 부드러움 아래 어떤 악마적 마력이 도사리고 있다고 여기는 자도 있었으니, 우리는 며칠이고 며칠이고, 너무도 지겹고 외롭도록 잔잔한 바다를 가로질러 항해했고, 온 공간이 우리의 복수에 찬 사명을 혐오하기라도 하듯 우리의 항아리 같은 뱃머리 앞에서 스스로 생명을 비워 내는 듯했다."]], [["But, at last, when turning to the eastward, the Cape winds began howling around us, and we rose and fell upon the long, troubled seas that are there; when the ivory-tusked Pequod sharply bowed to the blast, and gored the dark waves in her madness, till, like showers of silver chips, the foam-flakes flew over her bulwarks; then all this desolate vacuity of life went away, but gave place to sights more dismal than before.", "그러나 마침내 동쪽으로 뱃머리를 돌리자, 곶의 바람이 우리 둘레에서 울부짖기 시작했고, 우리는 그곳의 길고 거친 바다 위로 솟구쳤다 떨어졌다 했다. 상아 엄니를 단 피쿼드호가 돌풍을 향해 날카롭게 머리를 숙이고, 미친 듯이 검은 물결을 들이받자, 마침내 거품 조각이 은빛 부스러기 소나기처럼 뱃전 너머로 흩날렸다. 그러자 이 황량하고 텅 빈 생명의 공허는 사라졌으나, 그 자리에 이전보다 더 음울한 광경들이 들어섰다."]], [["Close to our bows, strange forms in the water darted hither and thither before us; while thick in our rear flew the inscrutable sea-ravens.", "뱃머리 가까이에서는 물속의 기이한 형체들이 우리 앞에서 이리저리 쏜살같이 내달렸고, 한편 우리 고물 쪽으로는 속을 알 수 없는 바다 까마귀들이 빽빽이 날아들었다."], ["And every morning, perched on our stays, rows of these birds were seen; and spite of our hootings, for a long time obstinately clung to the hemp, as though they deemed our ship some drifting, uninhabited craft; a thing appointed to desolation, and therefore fit roosting-place for their homeless selves.", "그리고 아침마다 우리 지삭(支索)에 걸터앉은 이 새들이 줄지어 보였다. 우리가 아무리 쫓으려 소리를 질러도 오랫동안 그것들은 삼노끈에 끈질기게 매달려 있었으니, 마치 우리 배를 아무도 살지 않은 채 떠다니는 표류선쯤으로, 황폐함에 운명 지어졌기에 집 없는 제 몸들이 깃들기에 알맞은 자리쯤으로 여기는 듯했다."], ["And heaved and heaved, still unrestingly heaved the black sea, as if its vast tides were a conscience; and the great mundane soul were in anguish and remorse for the long sin and suffering it had bred.", "그리고 검은 바다는 들썩이고 또 들썩이며, 쉴 줄 모르고 여전히 들썩였으니, 마치 그 광막한 조수가 하나의 양심이기라도 한 듯, 그리고 이 거대한 세상의 영혼이 제가 길러 낸 오랜 죄악과 고통에 괴로워하고 뉘우치기라도 하는 듯했다."]], [["Cape of Good Hope, do they call ye?", "희망봉이라, 그대들은 그곳을 그리 부르는가?"], ["Rather Cape Tormentoso, as called of yore; for long allured by the perfidious silences that before had attended us, we found ourselves launched into this tormented sea, where guilty beings transformed into those fowls and these fish, seemed condemned to swim on everlastingly without any haven in store, or beat that black air without any horizon.", "차라리 옛적에 불리던 대로 토르멘토소곶, 곧 고난의 곶이라 부르는 편이 옳으리라. 앞서 우리를 따라다니던 그 음흉한 침묵에 오래도록 홀려 끌려온 끝에, 우리는 이 고난의 바다로 내던져진 자신을 발견했으니, 그곳에서는 저 새와 이 물고기로 둔갑한 죄지은 존재들이, 깃들일 항구 하나 없이 영영 헤엄쳐야 하고, 지평선 하나 없는 저 검은 허공을 끝없이 날갯짓해야 할 운명에 처한 듯 보였다."], ["But calm, snow-white, and unvarying; still directing its fountain of feathers to the sky; still beckoning us on from before, the solitary jet would at times be descried.", "그러나 고요하고, 눈처럼 새하얗고, 변함없이, 여전히 깃털 같은 분수를 하늘로 뿜어 올리며, 여전히 앞쪽에서 우리를 손짓해 부르는 그 외로운 물줄기가 때때로 보이곤 했다."]], [["During all this blackness of the elements, Ahab, though assuming for the time the almost continual command of the drenched and dangerous deck, manifested the gloomiest reserve; and more seldom than ever addressed his mates.", "천지자연이 온통 이렇게 캄캄하던 내내, 에이해브는 비록 한동안 물에 흠뻑 젖은 위험한 갑판을 거의 쉴 새 없이 지휘하긴 했으나, 더없이 음울한 침묵을 드러냈고, 그 어느 때보다 드물게 항해사들에게 말을 건넸다."], ["In tempestuous times like these, after everything above and aloft has been secured, nothing more can be done but passively to await the issue of the gale.", "이런 폭풍우의 시절에는, 갑판 위와 높은 곳의 모든 것을 단단히 매어 두고 나면, 그저 폭풍이 어찌 될지를 가만히 기다리는 것 말고는 더 할 수 있는 일이 없다."], ["Then Captain and crew become practical fatalists.", "그러면 선장도 선원도 현실적인 운명론자가 된다."], ["So, with his ivory leg inserted into its accustomed hole, and with one hand firmly grasping a shroud, Ahab for hours and hours would stand gazing dead to windward, while an occasional squall of sleet or snow would all but congeal his very eyelashes together.", "그리하여 상아 다리를 늘 끼우던 구멍에 박아 넣고, 한 손으로는 삭구를 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 에이해브는 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 바람 불어오는 쪽을 죽은 듯 응시하며 서 있었으며, 이따금 진눈깨비나 눈보라가 몰아쳐 그의 속눈썹마저 거의 얼어붙게 했다."], ["Meantime, the crew driven from the forward part of the ship by the perilous seas that burstingly broke over its bows, stood in a line along the bulwarks in the waist; and the better to guard against the leaping waves, each man had slipped himself into a sort of bowline secured to the rail, in which he swung as in a loosened belt.", "그동안 선원들은 뱃머리 위로 부서지듯 덮쳐 오는 위험한 파도에 밀려 배의 앞쪽에서 쫓겨나, 한가운데 뱃전을 따라 한 줄로 늘어서 있었다. 그리고 펄쩍 뛰어오르는 파도를 더 잘 막아 내려고, 저마다 난간에 묶어 둔 일종의 고리줄에 제 몸을 끼워 넣은 채, 헐거운 허리띠에 매달리듯 그 안에서 흔들거렸다."], ["Few or no words were spoken; and the silent ship, as if manned by painted sailors in wax, day after day tore on through all the swift madness and gladness of the demoniac waves.", "오가는 말은 거의, 아니 전혀 없었다. 그리고 마치 밀랍으로 빚어 색칠한 선원들이 부리는 듯한 그 말 없는 배는, 날이면 날마다 악마 같은 파도의 그 거센 광기와 환희를 뚫고 내달렸다."], ["By night the same muteness of humanity before the shrieks of the ocean prevailed; still in silence the men swung in the bowlines; still wordless Ahab stood up to the blast.", "밤이 되어도 대양의 비명 앞에 선 인간의 그 똑같은 침묵이 지배했다. 여전히 침묵 속에서 사람들은 고리줄에 매달려 흔들렸고, 여전히 말없이 에이해브는 돌풍을 마주하고 서 있었다."], ["Even when wearied nature seemed demanding repose he would not seek that repose in his hammock.", "지칠 대로 지친 육신이 휴식을 요구하는 듯할 때조차 그는 해먹에서 그 휴식을 구하려 하지 않았다."], ["Never could Starbuck forget the old man’s aspect, when one night going down into the cabin to mark how the barometer stood, he saw him with closed eyes sitting straight in his floor-screwed chair; the rain and half-melted sleet of the storm from which he had some time before emerged, still slowly dripping from the unremoved hat and coat.", "스타벅(Starbuck)은 그 늙은이의 모습을 결코 잊을 수 없었으니, 어느 밤 기압계가 어떤지 살피러 선실로 내려갔다가, 그가 바닥에 나사로 고정된 의자에 두 눈을 감은 채 꼿꼿이 앉아 있는 것을 보았던 것이다. 얼마 전 그가 빠져나온 폭풍의 비와 반쯤 녹은 진눈깨비가, 여태 벗지 않은 모자와 외투에서 여전히 천천히 떨어지고 있었다."], ["On the table beside him lay unrolled one of those charts of tides and currents which have previously been spoken of.", "그의 곁 탁자 위에는 앞서 이야기한 바 있는 조수와 해류 해도 가운데 하나가 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["His lantern swung from his tightly clenched hand.", "그의 등잔은 꽉 움켜쥔 손에 매달려 흔들렸다."], ["Though the body was erect, the head was thrown back so that the closed eyes were pointed towards the needle of the tell-tale that swung from a beam in the ceiling.*", "몸은 꼿꼿이 세웠으나 머리는 뒤로 젖혀져 있어서, 감긴 두 눈은 천장 들보에 매달려 흔들리는 알림나침반의 바늘을 향하고 있었다."]], [["*The cabin-compass is called the tell-tale, because without going to the compass at the helm, the Captain, while below, can inform himself of the course of the ship.", "* 선실 나침반은 알림나침반이라 불리는데, 이는 선장이 아래에 있으면서도 키의 나침반까지 가지 않고도 배의 항로를 알 수 있기 때문이다."]], [["Terrible old man!", "무서운 늙은이로구나!"], ["thought Starbuck with a shudder, sleeping in this gale, still thou steadfastly eyest thy purpose.", "스타벅은 몸서리치며 생각했다. 이 폭풍 속에 잠들어서도 그대는 여전히 한결같이 그대의 목적을 응시하고 있구나."]], [["# CHAPTER 52. The Albatross.", "# CHAPTER 52. The Albatross."]], [["South-eastward from the Cape, off the distant Crozetts, a good cruising ground for Right Whalemen, a sail loomed ahead, the Goney (Albatross) by name.", "희망봉에서 남동쪽으로, 멀리 떨어진 크로제 제도 부근, 참고래(Right Whale)잡이들에게는 더없이 좋은 조업 해역에서, 한 척의 배가 앞쪽에 어렴풋이 모습을 드러냈다. 고니(앨버트로스)호라는 이름의 배였다."], ["As she slowly drew nigh, from my lofty perch at the fore-mast-head, I had a good view of that sight so remarkable to a tyro in the far ocean fisheries—a whaler at sea, and long absent from home.", "그 배가 천천히 다가올 때, 나는 앞돛대 꼭대기 높은 자리에서 먼바다 어장에 갓 들어선 풋내기에게는 더없이 놀라운 광경을 똑똑히 볼 수 있었다. 오랫동안 고향을 떠나 바다 위를 떠도는 고래잡이배의 모습이었다."]], [["As if the waves had been fullers, this craft was bleached like the skeleton of a stranded walrus.", "마치 파도가 표백공이라도 되는 양, 이 배는 뭍에 좌초된 바다코끼리의 뼈처럼 허옇게 바래 있었다."], ["All down her sides, this spectral appearance was traced with long channels of reddened rust, while all her spars and her rigging were like the thick branches of trees furred over with hoar-frost.", "옆구리를 따라 아래로 죽 늘어선 붉은 녹의 긴 줄기들이 이 유령 같은 모습을 더해 주었고, 활대와 삭구는 온통 흰 서리로 뒤덮인 나무의 굵은 가지들 같았다."], ["Only her lower sails were set.", "펼쳐진 돛은 아래쪽 돛뿐이었다."], ["A wild sight it was to see her long-bearded look-outs at those three mast-heads.", "세 돛대 꼭대기에 자리한 긴 수염의 망꾼들을 바라보는 것은 야릇한 광경이었다. 그들은 짐승의 가죽을 두른 듯 보였으니, 거의 사 년에 걸친 항해를 견뎌 낸 옷가지는 그토록 찢기고 기워져 있었다."], ["They seemed clad in the skins of beasts, so torn and bepatched the raiment that had survived nearly four years of cruising.", "돛대에 못으로 박은 쇠테 안에 선 채로, 그들은 깊이를 알 수 없는 바다 위에서 흔들리고 출렁였다. 배가 천천히 미끄러져 우리 고물 아래로 바싹 다가왔을 때, 공중에 있던 우리 여섯 사내는 서로 어찌나 가까워졌던지 한 배의 돛대 꼭대기에서 다른 배의 돛대 꼭대기로 뛰어 건너갈 수 있을 정도였다."], ["Standing in iron hoops nailed to the mast, they swayed and swung over a fathomless sea; and though, when the ship slowly glided close under our stern, we six men in the air came so nigh to each other that we might almost have leaped from the mast-heads of one ship to those of the other; yet, those forlorn-looking fishermen, mildly eyeing us as they passed, said not one word to our own look-outs, while the quarter-deck hail was being heard from below.", "그럼에도 저 쓸쓸해 보이는 어부들은 스쳐 지나가며 우리를 부드럽게 바라볼 뿐, 우리 망꾼들에게 한마디도 건네지 않았다. 그러는 동안 아래쪽 뒷갑판에서는 외치는 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“Ship ahoy!", "\"어이, 배!"], ["Have ye seen the White Whale?”", "흰 고래(White Whale)를 본 적이 있소?\""]], [["But as the strange captain, leaning over the pallid bulwarks, was in the act of putting his trumpet to his mouth, it somehow fell from his hand into the sea; and the wind now rising amain, he in vain strove to make himself heard without it.", "그러나 그 낯선 선장이 핏기 없는 뱃전 너머로 몸을 기울인 채 나팔을 입에 가져가려는 찰나, 어쩐 일인지 그것이 손에서 빠져 바다로 떨어지고 말았다. 게다가 바람이 세차게 거세지면서 그는 나팔도 없이 자기 목소리를 들리게 하려 애썼으나 헛수고였다."], ["Meantime his ship was still increasing the distance between.", "그사이 그의 배는 둘 사이의 거리를 점점 더 벌리고 있었다."], ["While in various silent ways the seamen of the Pequod were evincing their observance of this ominous incident at the first mere mention of the White Whale’s name to another ship, Ahab for a moment paused; it almost seemed as though he would have lowered a boat to board the stranger, had not the threatening wind forbade.", "다른 배에 흰 고래의 이름을 처음 입에 올린 그 일이 불길한 사건임을 페쿼드(Pequod)호의 선원들이 갖가지 말 없는 방식으로 드러내고 있는 동안, 에이해브(Ahab)는 한순간 멈칫했다. 위협적인 바람이 막지만 않았더라면 그는 보트를 내려 낯선 배에 올라타기라도 할 것처럼 보였다."], ["But taking advantage of his windward position, he again seized his trumpet, and knowing by her aspect that the stranger vessel was a Nantucketer and shortly bound home, he loudly hailed—“Ahoy there!", "그러나 자신이 바람 위쪽에 있는 이점을 살려, 그는 다시 나팔을 거머쥐었고, 배의 생김새로 보아 그 낯선 배가 낸터킷(Nantucket) 배이며 곧 고향으로 돌아가는 길임을 알아채고는 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"어이, 거기!"], ["This is the Pequod, bound round the world!", "이 배는 세계를 일주하는 페쿼드호다!"], ["Tell them to address all future letters to the Pacific ocean!", "앞으로 부치는 편지는 모두 태평양으로 보내라고 전해 주시오!"], ["and this time three years, if I am not at home, tell them to address them to ——”", "그리고 만 삼 년 뒤에도 내가 집에 없거든, 편지를 ——로 부치라고 전해 주시오!\""]], [["At that moment the two wakes were fairly crossed, and instantly, then, in accordance with their singular ways, shoals of small harmless fish, that for some days before had been placidly swimming by our side, darted away with what seemed shuddering fins, and ranged themselves fore and aft with the stranger’s flanks.", "바로 그 순간, 두 배의 물길이 완전히 엇갈렸다. 그러자 그 별난 습성에 따라, 며칠 전부터 우리 곁에서 잔잔히 헤엄치던 작고 해롭지 않은 물고기 떼가 마치 지느러미를 떨듯 쏜살같이 달아나, 낯선 배의 옆구리를 따라 앞뒤로 늘어섰다."], ["Though in the course of his continual voyagings Ahab must often before have noticed a similar sight, yet, to any monomaniac man, the veriest trifles capriciously carry meanings.", "끝없는 항해의 와중에 에이해브도 분명 전에 비슷한 광경을 자주 보았을 터이나, 그럼에도 어떤 편집광에게든 가장 하찮은 것조차 제멋대로 의미를 띠는 법이다."]], [["“Swim away from me, do ye?”", "\"나에게서 헤엄쳐 달아나는구나, 그래?\""], ["murmured Ahab, gazing over into the water.", "에이해브가 물속을 들여다보며 중얼거렸다."], ["There seemed but little in the words, but the tone conveyed more of deep helpless sadness than the insane old man had ever before evinced.", "그 말에는 별것이 담겨 있지 않은 듯했으나, 그 어조는 이 미친 노인이 일찍이 드러낸 그 어떤 것보다도 깊고 어쩔 수 없는 슬픔을 품고 있었다."], ["But turning to the steersman, who thus far had been holding the ship in the wind to diminish her headway, he cried out in his old lion voice,—“Up helm!", "그러나 이제껏 배의 속력을 줄이려고 바람을 향해 뱃머리를 붙들고 있던 키잡이를 돌아보며, 그는 예의 그 사자 같은 목소리로 외쳤다. \"키를 올려라!"], ["Keep her off round the world!”", "세계를 돌아 계속 나아가게 하라!\""]], [["Round the world!", "세계 일주라!"], ["There is much in that sound to inspire proud feelings; but whereto does all that circumnavigation conduct?", "그 울림에는 자랑스러운 감정을 불러일으키는 데가 많다. 그러나 그 모든 항해는 끝내 우리를 어디로 데려가는가?"], ["Only through numberless perils to the very point whence we started, where those that we left behind secure, were all the time before us.", "그저 헤아릴 수 없는 위험들을 거쳐, 우리가 출발했던 바로 그 지점으로 돌아갈 뿐이다. 그곳에는 우리가 안전하게 두고 떠났던 이들이, 줄곧 우리보다 앞서 있었던 것이다."]], [["Were this world an endless plain, and by sailing eastward we could for ever reach new distances, and discover sights more sweet and strange than any Cyclades or Islands of King Solomon, then there were promise in the voyage.", "이 세상이 끝없는 평원이어서 동쪽으로 항해해 가면 영원히 새로운 거리에 가닿고, 그 어떤 키클라데스 제도나 솔로몬 왕의 섬들보다 더 달콤하고 기이한 광경을 발견할 수 있다면, 그 항해에는 약속이 깃들어 있으리라."], ["But in pursuit of those far mysteries we dream of, or in tormented chase of that demon phantom that, some time or other, swims before all human hearts; while chasing such over this round globe, they either lead us on in barren mazes or midway leave us whelmed.", "그러나 우리가 꿈꾸는 저 머나먼 신비를 좇거나, 언젠가 모든 인간의 마음 앞을 헤엄쳐 지나가는 그 악마 같은 환영을 괴로이 추격하면서, 이 둥근 지구 위에서 그런 것을 뒤쫓을 때면, 그것들은 우리를 메마른 미궁 속으로 이끌거나 한복판에서 우리를 집어삼킨 채 버려두고 만다."]], [["# CHAPTER 53. The Gam.", "# CHAPTER 53. The Gam."]], [["The ostensible reason why Ahab did not go on board of the whaler we had spoken was this: the wind and sea betokened storms.", "에이해브가 우리가 인사를 나눈 그 포경선에 오르지 않은 표면적인 이유는 이러했다. 바람과 바다가 폭풍을 예고하고 있었던 것이다."], ["But even had this not been the case, he would not after all, perhaps, have boarded her—judging by his subsequent conduct on similar occasions—if so it had been that, by the process of hailing, he had obtained a negative answer to the question he put.", "그러나 설령 사정이 그렇지 않았다 하더라도, 그는 어쩌면 결국 그 배에 오르지 않았을 것이다. 비슷한 경우에 그가 보인 훗날의 행동으로 미루어 판단하건대, 만약 인사를 주고받는 과정에서 자신이 던진 질문에 부정적인 대답을 얻었더라면 말이다."], ["For, as it eventually turned out, he cared not to consort, even for five minutes, with any stranger captain, except he could contribute some of that information he so absorbingly sought.", "결국 드러난 바로는, 그는 자신이 그토록 골똘히 찾아 헤매는 정보를 얼마간이라도 보태 줄 수 없는 한, 어떤 낯선 선장과도 단 오 분조차 어울리기를 원치 않았던 것이다."], ["But all this might remain inadequately estimated, were not something said here of the peculiar usages of whaling-vessels when meeting each other in foreign seas, and especially on a common cruising-ground.", "그러나 머나먼 바다에서, 특히 공동의 어장에서 포경선들이 서로 마주칠 때의 독특한 관습에 관해 여기서 한마디 해 두지 않는다면, 이 모든 것은 충분히 이해되지 못한 채로 남을 것이다."]], [["If two strangers crossing the Pine Barrens in New York State, or the equally desolate Salisbury Plain in England; if casually encountering each other in such inhospitable wilds, these twain, for the life of them, cannot well avoid a mutual salutation; and stopping for a moment to interchange the news; and, perhaps, sitting down for a while and resting in concert: then, how much more natural that upon the illimitable Pine Barrens and Salisbury Plains of the sea, two whaling vessels descrying each other at the ends of the earth—off lone Fanning’s Island, or the far away King’s Mills; how much more natural, I say, that under such circumstances these ships should not only interchange hails, but come into still closer, more friendly and sociable contact.", "뉴욕주의 소나무 황무지(Pine Barrens)나, 그에 못지않게 황량한 영국의 솔즈베리 평원을 가로지르던 낯선 두 사람이, 그토록 인적 드문 황야에서 우연히 마주친다면, 이 두 사람은 무슨 일이 있어도 서로 인사를 나누지 않을 수 없을 것이며, 잠시 멈춰 서서 소식을 주고받고, 어쩌면 한동안 함께 앉아 쉬기도 할 것이다. 그렇다면 바다라는 가없는 소나무 황무지와 솔즈베리 평원 위에서, 세상의 끝자락에서 — 외딴 패닝섬 앞바다나 머나먼 킹스밀스 앞바다에서 — 서로를 알아본 두 포경선이라면, 이런 상황에서 단지 인사만 주고받는 것이 아니라 더 가깝고, 더 다정하며, 더 사교적인 접촉을 나누는 것이 얼마나 더 자연스러운 일이겠는가."], ["And especially would this seem to be a matter of course, in the case of vessels owned in one seaport, and whose captains, officers, and not a few of the men are personally known to each other; and consequently, have all sorts of dear domestic things to talk about.", "더구나 같은 항구에 선적을 둔 배들, 그리고 그 선장들과 사관들, 적지 않은 선원들이 서로 개인적으로 알고 지내며, 따라서 정겨운 고향 이야깃거리를 온갖 종류로 나눌 수 있는 경우라면, 이는 더더욱 당연한 일로 보일 것이다."]], [["For the long absent ship, the outward-bounder, perhaps, has letters on board; at any rate, she will be sure to let her have some papers of a date a year or two later than the last one on her blurred and thumb-worn files.", "오랫동안 집을 떠나 있던 배에게는, 어쩌면 이제 막 출항한 배가 편지를 싣고 있을지도 모른다. 적어도 그 배는, 흐릿하고 손때 묻은 서류철에 끼워 둔 가장 최근 것보다 한두 해는 더 새로운 신문 몇 부를 틀림없이 건네줄 것이다."], ["And in return for that courtesy, the outward-bound ship would receive the latest whaling intelligence from the cruising-ground to which she may be destined, a thing of the utmost importance to her.", "그리고 그 친절에 대한 보답으로, 출항하는 배는 자신이 향하게 될 어장에 관한 가장 최신의 포경 소식을 얻게 되니, 이는 그 배에게 더없이 중요한 일이다."], ["And in degree, all this will hold true concerning whaling vessels crossing each other’s track on the cruising-ground itself, even though they are equally long absent from home.", "그리고 정도의 차이는 있을지언정, 이 모든 것은 어장 한복판에서 서로의 항로를 가로지르는 포경선들에게도 똑같이 적용되는데, 비록 두 배 모두 집을 떠난 지 오래되었다 해도 그러하다."], ["For one of them may have received a transfer of letters from some third, and now far remote vessel; and some of those letters may be for the people of the ship she now meets.", "그중 한 배가 어떤 제삼의, 이제는 멀리 떨어진 배로부터 편지를 넘겨받았을 수도 있고, 그 편지들 가운데 일부는 지금 마주친 배의 사람들에게 보내는 것일 수도 있기 때문이다."], ["Besides, they would exchange the whaling news, and have an agreeable chat.", "게다가 그들은 포경 소식을 주고받고, 즐거운 잡담을 나누기도 할 것이다."], ["For not only would they meet with all the sympathies of sailors, but likewise with all the peculiar congenialities arising from a common pursuit and mutually shared privations and perils.", "그들은 선원들 사이의 온갖 동질감으로 마주할 뿐만 아니라, 공동의 생업과 함께 나눈 결핍과 위험에서 비롯되는 온갖 독특한 친밀감으로도 마주하기 때문이다."]], [["Nor would difference of country make any very essential difference; that is, so long as both parties speak one language, as is the case with Americans and English.", "나라가 다르다고 해서 본질적으로 큰 차이가 생기는 것도 아니다. 다시 말해, 미국인과 영국인의 경우가 그렇듯, 양쪽이 같은 언어를 쓰는 한 그러하다."], ["Though, to be sure, from the small number of English whalers, such meetings do not very often occur, and when they do occur there is too apt to be a sort of shyness between them; for your Englishman is rather reserved, and your Yankee, he does not fancy that sort of thing in anybody but himself.", "물론 영국 포경선의 수가 워낙 적은 탓에 이런 만남이 그리 자주 일어나지는 않으며, 일어난다 해도 둘 사이에는 일종의 서먹함이 깃들기 십상이다. 영국인은 다소 내성적이고, 양키는 그런 식의 내성적인 태도를 자기 말고 다른 누구에게서도 달가워하지 않기 때문이다."], ["Besides, the English whalers sometimes affect a kind of metropolitan superiority over the American whalers; regarding the long, lean Nantucketer, with his nondescript provincialisms, as a sort of sea-peasant.", "게다가 영국 포경선들은 때때로 미국 포경선들 위에 군림하는 일종의 도회적 우월감을 내비치며, 정체 모를 촌티를 풍기는 길쭉하고 비쩍 마른 낸터킷 사람을 일종의 바다 촌뜨기쯤으로 여기곤 한다."], ["But where this superiority in the English whalemen does really consist, it would be hard to say, seeing that the Yankees in one day, collectively, kill more whales than all the English, collectively, in ten years.", "그러나 양키들이 단 하루에 다 함께 잡는 고래가 영국인 전부가 십 년 동안 다 함께 잡는 것보다 많은 마당에, 영국 포경꾼들의 이 우월감이 대체 어디에 근거하는 것인지는 말하기 어렵다."], ["But this is a harmless little foible in the English whale-hunters, which the Nantucketer does not take much to heart; probably, because he knows that he has a few foibles himself.", "그러나 이것은 영국 고래 사냥꾼들의 해롭지 않은 사소한 결점일 뿐이어서, 낸터킷 사람은 이를 그리 마음에 담아 두지 않는다. 아마도 자기에게도 사소한 결점 몇 가지쯤은 있다는 것을 알기 때문이리라."]], [["So, then, we see that of all ships separately sailing the sea, the whalers have most reason to be sociable—and they are so.", "그러니 바다를 저마다 항해하는 온갖 배들 가운데서도, 포경선이야말로 가장 사교적일 이유를 지녔으며 — 실제로도 그러하다는 것을 우리는 알 수 있다."], ["Whereas, some merchant ships crossing each other’s wake in the mid-Atlantic, will oftentimes pass on without so much as a single word of recognition, mutually cutting each other on the high seas, like a brace of dandies in Broadway; and all the time indulging, perhaps, in finical criticism upon each other’s rig.", "반면에 대서양 한복판에서 서로의 항적을 가로지르는 어떤 상선들은, 알아본다는 말 한마디 없이 그대로 지나쳐 버리는 일이 흔하며, 마치 브로드웨이의 멋쟁이 한 쌍처럼 망망대해에서 서로를 본체만체하면서, 그동안 어쩌면 서로의 삭구를 두고 까다로운 흠이나 잡고 있을 것이다."], ["As for Men-of-War, when they chance to meet at sea, they first go through such a string of silly bowings and scrapings, such a ducking of ensigns, that there does not seem to be much right-down hearty good-will and brotherly love about it at all.", "군함으로 말하자면, 바다에서 우연히 마주치면 먼저 그토록 어리석은 절과 굽실거림을 줄줄이 늘어놓고, 군기를 그토록 까딱까딱 내려 인사하니, 거기에는 진심에서 우러난 정이나 형제애 같은 것이 그다지 깃들어 있는 것 같지가 않다. 노예선들이 마주치는 경우로 말하자면, 그들은 어찌나 엄청나게 서두르는지 되도록 빨리 서로에게서 달아나 버린다."], ["As touching Slave-ships meeting, why, they are in such a prodigious hurry, they run away from each other as soon as possible.", "해적들로 말하자면, 우연히 서로의 해골 깃발이 엇갈리면 첫 인사가 — \"해골이 몇 개냐?\""], ["And as for Pirates, when they chance to cross each other’s cross-bones, the first hail is—“How many skulls?”—the same way that whalers hail—“How many barrels?”", "인데, 이는 포경선들이 — \"기름이 몇 통이냐?\" 하고 인사하는 것과 똑같은 방식이다."], ["And that question once answered, pirates straightway steer apart, for they are infernal villains on both sides, and don’t like to see overmuch of each other’s villanous likenesses.", "그리고 그 질문에 답이 나오면, 해적들은 곧장 서로 갈라져 뱃길을 돌린다. 양쪽 다 지옥에서 온 악당들이라, 서로의 악당 같은 몰골을 너무 많이 보고 싶어 하지 않기 때문이다."]], [["But look at the godly, honest, unostentatious, hospitable, sociable, free-and-easy whaler!", "그러나 저 경건하고, 정직하고, 꾸밈없고, 손님 대접 후하고, 사교적이며, 격의 없는 포경선을 보라!"], ["What does the whaler do when she meets another whaler in any sort of decent weather? She has a “Gam,”", "포경선은 그럭저럭 괜찮은 날씨에 다른 포경선을 만나면 무엇을 하는가?"], ["a thing so utterly unknown to all other ships that they never heard of the name even; and if by chance they should hear of it, they only grin at it, and repeat gamesome stuff about “spouters” and “blubber-boilers,”", "그들은 \"갬(Gam)\"을 갖는데, 이는 다른 모든 배들에게는 너무도 생소하여 그 이름조차 들어 본 적이 없는 것이며, 혹 우연히 그 말을 듣게 된다 해도 그저 비죽 웃으며 \"물 뿜는 놈들\"이니 \"기름 끓이는 놈들\"이니 하는 그런 짓궂은 농지거리나 되풀이할 뿐이다."], ["and such like pretty exclamations. Why it is that all Merchant-seamen, and also all Pirates and Man-of-War’s men, and Slave-ship sailors, cherish such a scornful feeling towards Whale-ships; this is a question it would be hard to answer.", "어찌하여 모든 상선 선원들이, 그리고 모든 해적과 군함 선원들, 노예선 뱃사람들까지도 포경선을 향해 그토록 경멸하는 감정을 품는지, 이는 답하기 어려운 물음이다."], ["Because, in the case of pirates, say, I should like to know whether that profession of theirs has any peculiar glory about it.", "이를테면 해적의 경우, 나는 그들의 그 직업에 무슨 특별한 영광이 깃들어 있는지 알고 싶을 따름이다."], ["It sometimes ends in uncommon elevation, indeed; but only at the gallows.", "과연 그것이 때때로 유별난 드높음으로 끝나기는 하지만, 그것은 오직 교수대 위에서일 뿐이다."], ["And besides, when a man is elevated in that odd fashion, he has no proper foundation for his superior altitude.", "게다가 사람이 그런 묘한 방식으로 높이 들어 올려질 때는, 그 우월한 높이를 떠받칠 변변한 토대가 없는 법이다."], ["Hence, I conclude, that in boasting himself to be high lifted above a whaleman, in that assertion the pirate has no solid basis to stand on.", "그러므로 나는 이렇게 결론짓는다. 포경꾼보다 한참 높이 들어 올려져 있노라고 으스대는 그 주장에서, 해적은 발 디딜 단단한 근거가 없다고."]], [["But what is a Gam?", "그런데 갬이란 무엇인가?"], ["You might wear out your index-finger running up and down the columns of dictionaries, and never find the word.", "사전의 갈피갈피를 오르내리며 집게손가락이 닳도록 뒤져 본들, 그 단어를 결코 찾아내지 못할 것이다."], ["Dr. Johnson never attained to that erudition; Noah Webster’s ark does not hold it.", "존슨 박사조차 그런 박학에 이르지 못했으며, 노아 웹스터의 방주에도 그것은 실려 있지 않다."], ["Nevertheless, this same expressive word has now for many years been in constant use among some fifteen thousand true born Yankees.", "그럼에도 바로 이 함축적인 단어는, 이제 여러 해 동안 한 만오천 명쯤 되는 진짜 토박이 양키들 사이에서 끊임없이 쓰여 왔다."], ["Certainly, it needs a definition, and should be incorporated into the Lexicon.", "분명 그것은 정의를 내릴 필요가 있으며, 마땅히 사전에 편입되어야 한다."], ["With that view, let me learnedly define it.", "그런 취지에서, 내가 학자답게 그것을 정의해 보겠다."]], [["GAM.", "갬."], ["NOUN—A social meeting of two (or more) Whaleships, generally on a cruising-ground; when, after exchanging hails, they exchange visits by boats’ crews: the two captains remaining, for the time, on board of one ship, and the two chief mates on the other.", "명사 — 두 척(또는 그 이상)의 포경선이, 대개 어장에서 갖는 사교적 모임. 이때 그들은 인사를 주고받은 뒤 보트의 선원들을 통해 서로를 방문하는데, 두 선장은 한동안 한 배에 함께 머물고, 두 일등항해사는 다른 배에 머문다."]], [["There is another little item about Gamming which must not be forgotten here.", "여기서 잊지 말아야 할, 갬에 관한 또 하나의 자그마한 사항이 있다."], ["All professions have their own little peculiarities of detail; so has the whale fishery.", "모든 직업에는 저마다 세부적인 자잘한 특색이 있는 법인데, 포경업 또한 그러하다."], ["In a pirate, man-of-war, or slave ship, when the captain is rowed anywhere in his boat, he always sits in the stern sheets on a comfortable, sometimes cushioned seat there, and often steers himself with a pretty little milliner’s tiller decorated with gay cords and ribbons.", "해적선이든 군함이든 노예선이든, 선장이 보트를 타고 어딘가로 노 저어 갈 때면, 그는 늘 고물의 자리에, 더러는 방석까지 깔린 안락한 좌석에 앉아, 종종 화사한 끈과 리본으로 장식한 예쁘장한 모자 가게풍의 작은 키손잡이로 직접 키를 잡는다."], ["But the whale-boat has no seat astern, no sofa of that sort whatever, and no tiller at all.", "그러나 포경 보트에는 고물에 좌석도 없고, 그런 종류의 소파 따위는 전혀 없으며, 키손잡이도 아예 없다."], ["High times indeed, if whaling captains were wheeled about the water on castors like gouty old aldermen in patent chairs.", "포경선 선장들이 통풍에 시달리는 늙은 시의원들처럼 특허받은 의자에 앉아 바퀴를 달고 물 위를 굴러다닌다면, 참으로 호사스러운 시절이 아니겠는가."], ["And as for a tiller, the whale-boat never admits of any such effeminacy; and therefore as in gamming a complete boat’s crew must leave the ship, and hence as the boat steerer or harpooneer is of the number, that subordinate is the steersman upon the occasion, and the captain, having no place to sit in, is pulled off to his visit all standing like a pine tree.", "그리고 키손잡이로 말하자면, 포경 보트는 그런 나약한 물건을 결코 허용하지 않는다. 그러므로 갬을 할 때는 한 보트의 선원 전부가 배를 떠나야 하고, 따라서 키잡이 혹은 작살잡이도 그 인원에 포함되므로, 그 부하가 이 경우의 키잡이가 되며, 앉을 자리가 없는 선장은 소나무처럼 꼿꼿이 선 채로 노 저어져 방문길에 오른다."], ["And often you will notice that being conscious of the eyes of the whole visible world resting on him from the sides of the two ships, this standing captain is all alive to the importance of sustaining his dignity by maintaining his legs.", "그리고 당신은 종종 보게 될 것이다. 두 배의 뱃전에서 눈에 보이는 온 세상의 눈길이 자기에게 쏠려 있음을 의식한 채, 이 선 채로 가는 선장이 두 다리를 굳건히 버텨 자신의 위엄을 지키는 일의 중대함에 온 신경을 곤두세우고 있는 모습을 말이다."], ["Nor is this any very easy matter; for in his rear is the immense projecting steering oar hitting him now and then in the small of his back, the after-oar reciprocating by rapping his knees in front.", "게다가 이것은 그리 만만한 일이 아니다. 그의 뒤에서는 엄청나게 길게 튀어나온 조타용 노가 이따금 그의 등허리를 후려치고, 앞에서는 맨 뒤쪽 노가 맞장구치듯 그의 무릎을 탁탁 친다."], ["He is thus completely wedged before and behind, and can only expand himself sideways by settling down on his stretched legs; but a sudden, violent pitch of the boat will often go far to topple him, because length of foundation is nothing without corresponding breadth.", "그리하여 그는 앞뒤로 완전히 끼인 채, 오로지 쩍 벌린 다리를 낮춰 디딤으로써만 옆으로 몸을 펼 수 있을 뿐이다. 그러나 보트가 갑작스럽고 격렬하게 출렁이면 종종 그를 넘어뜨리고도 남는데, 토대의 길이란 그에 걸맞은 너비가 없으면 아무 소용이 없기 때문이다."], ["Merely make a spread angle of two poles, and you cannot stand them up.", "그저 막대 두 개를 벌려 각을 만들어 보라, 그것들을 똑바로 세울 수는 없을 것이다."], ["Then, again, it would never do in plain sight of the world’s riveted eyes, it would never do, I say, for this straddling captain to be seen steadying himself the slightest particle by catching hold of anything with his hands; indeed, as token of his entire, buoyant self-command, he generally carries his hands in his trowsers’ pockets; but perhaps being generally very large, heavy hands, he carries them there for ballast.", "또한 온 세상의 못 박힌 듯한 눈길이 빤히 지켜보는 앞에서, 이렇게 다리를 벌리고 선 선장이 손으로 무언가를 붙잡고 단 한 치라도 몸을 가누는 모습을 보인다는 것은 결코 있을 수 없는 일이다, 정말이지 결코 있을 수 없는 일이라고 나는 말한다. 실로 자신의 온전하고 거침없는 자제력의 증표로서, 그는 대개 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣고 다닌다."], ["Nevertheless there have occurred instances, well authenticated ones too, where the captain has been known for an uncommonly critical moment or two, in a sudden squall say—to seize hold of the nearest oarsman’s hair, and hold on there like grim death.", "그러나 어쩌면 그 손이 대체로 무척 크고 묵직한 손이라, 그는 그것을 균형추 삼아 거기에 넣어 두는 것일지도 모른다. 그렇지만, 충분히 입증된 사례들도 있는바, 선장이 유난히 위태로운 한두 순간에 — 이를테면 느닷없는 돌풍 속에서 — 가장 가까이 있는 노잡이의 머리카락을 움켜쥐고는 죽기 살기로 거기에 매달려 있었던 경우도 있었다."]], [["(As told at the Golden Inn.)\nThe Cape of Good Hope, and all the watery region round about there, is much like some noted four corners of a great highway, where you meet more travellers than in any other part.", "지브롤터 곶, 곧 희망봉(Cape of Good Hope)과 그 주위를 둘러싼 광대한 바다는 거대한 도로의 이름난 네거리와 무척이나 닮아 있어, 그 어떤 곳보다 더 많은 나그네를 만나게 되는 곳이다."]], [["It was not very long after speaking the Goney that another homeward-bound whaleman, the Town-Ho,* was encountered.", "고니 호(Goney)와 말을 주고받은 지 그리 오래지 않아, 우리는 또 한 척의 귀향길에 오른 포경선 타운호(Town-Ho)*와 마주쳤다."], ["She was manned almost wholly by Polynesians.", "그 배에는 거의 전부가 폴리네시아 사람들로 채워져 있었다."], ["In the short gam that ensued she gave us strong news of Moby Dick.", "뒤이어 벌어진 짧은 가다(gam) 동안, 그 배는 우리에게 모비 딕(Moby Dick)에 관한 강렬한 소식을 전해 주었다."], ["To some the general interest in the White Whale was now wildly heightened by a circumstance of the Town-Ho’s story, which seemed obscurely to involve with the whale a certain wondrous, inverted visitation of one of those so called judgments of God which at times are said to overtake some men.", "어떤 이들에게는 흰 고래(White Whale)에 대한 보편적인 관심이 이제 타운호의 이야기에 담긴 한 가지 사정으로 인해 격렬하게 고조되었으니, 그것은 그 고래와 더불어, 사람들이 흔히 말하는바 때때로 어떤 사람들에게 들이닥친다는 저 하느님의 심판(judgments of God) 가운데 하나가, 기이하고도 거꾸로 뒤집힌 방문의 형태로 어렴풋이 얽혀 있는 듯 보였기 때문이다."], ["This latter circumstance, with its own particular accompaniments, forming what may be called the secret part of the tragedy about to be narrated, never reached the ears of Captain Ahab or his mates.", "이 후자의 사정은, 그 나름의 특별한 부수적인 정황들과 더불어, 이제부터 이야기될 비극의 이른바 비밀스러운 부분을 이루는 것으로서, 결코 에이해브(Ahab) 선장이나 그의 항해사들의 귀에는 닿지 못했다."], ["For that secret part of the story was unknown to the captain of the Town-Ho himself.", "그 이야기의 비밀스러운 부분은 타운호의 선장 자신에게조차 알려져 있지 않았기 때문이다."], ["It was the private property of three confederate white seamen of that ship, one of whom, it seems, communicated it to Tashtego with Romish injunctions of secrecy, but the following night Tashtego rambled in his sleep, and revealed so much of it in that way, that when he was wakened he could not well withhold the rest.", "그것은 그 배의 백인 선원 세 사람이 한패가 되어 간직한 사사로운 소유물이었는데, 그 가운데 하나가 가톨릭식 비밀 엄수의 맹세와 함께 그것을 태시테고(Tashtego)에게 털어놓은 듯하나, 그다음 날 밤 태시테고가 잠결에 헛소리를 하며 그 일을 그렇게 많이 드러내고 말아, 막상 깨어났을 때는 나머지를 차마 감출 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["Nevertheless, so potent an influence did this thing have on those seamen in the Pequod who came to the full knowledge of it, and by such a strange delicacy, to call it so, were they governed in this matter, that they kept the secret among themselves so that it never transpired abaft the Pequod’s main-mast.", "그럼에도 불구하고, 이 일은 그것을 온전히 알게 된 피쿼드호(Pequod)의 선원들에게 어찌나 강력한 영향을 미쳤던지, 그리고 그들은 이 문제에 있어 — 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 — 어떤 기묘한 섬세함에 사로잡혀 있었던지라, 그 비밀을 자기들끼리만 간직하여 그것이 피쿼드호의 큰돛대 뒤편으로는 결코 새어 나가지 않게 했다."], ["Interweaving in its proper place this darker thread with the story as publicly narrated on the ship, the whole of this strange affair I now proceed to put on lasting record.", "배 위에서 공공연하게 이야기된 그 사연 속에, 이 한층 어두운 실가닥을 마땅한 자리에 엮어 넣어, 나는 이제 이 기이한 사건의 전모를 영원한 기록으로 남기고자 한다."]], [["*The ancient whale-cry upon first sighting a whale from the mast-head, still used by whalemen in hunting the famous Gallipagos terrapin.", "* 돛대 꼭대기에서 고래를 처음 발견했을 때 외치던 옛 포경 함성으로, 지금도 포경선원들이 그 유명한 갈라파고스(Gallipagos) 거북을 사냥할 때 쓴다."]], [["For my humor’s sake, I shall preserve the style in which I once narrated it at Lima, to a lounging circle of my Spanish friends, one saint’s eve, smoking upon the thick-gilt tiled piazza of the Golden Inn.", "내 취향에 맞추어, 나는 언젠가 리마(Lima)에서 이 이야기를 풀어놓던 그 방식을 그대로 보존하려 한다. 그날은 어느 성인의 전야제로, 나는 골든 인(Golden Inn)의 두꺼운 금박 타일이 깔린 회랑에서 담배를 피우며 빈둥거리던 스페인 친구들 무리에게 이 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["Of those fine cavaliers, the young Dons, Pedro and Sebastian, were on the closer terms with me; and hence the interluding questions they occasionally put, and which are duly answered at the time.", "그 멋진 기사들, 곧 젊은 돈(Don)들 가운데 페드로와 세바스티안이 나와 더 가까운 사이였는데, 그래서 그들이 이따금 끼워 넣던 질문들, 그리고 그때그때 거기에 답한 내용을 함께 적어 둔다."]], [["“Some two years prior to my first learning the events which I am about rehearsing to you, gentlemen, the Town-Ho, Sperm Whaler of Nantucket, was cruising in your Pacific here, not very many days’ sail eastward from the eaves of this good Golden Inn.", "\"여러분, 내가 지금 여러분께 들려드리려는 이 사건들을 처음 알게 되기 한 이 년쯤 전의 일이오. 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 향유고래잡이 배 타운호가 바로 이곳 여러분의 태평양을 항해하고 있었는데, 이 좋은 골든 인의 처마에서 동쪽으로 며칠 안 되는 뱃길의 거리였소."], ["She was somewhere to the northward of the Line.", "그 배는 적도(Line) 어딘가 북쪽에 있었소. 어느 날 아침, 날마다 하는 관례대로 펌프를 다루던 중에, 그 배의 선창에 평소보다 물이 더 많이 차 있는 것이 발견되었소."], ["One morning upon handling the pumps, according to daily usage, it was observed that she made more water in her hold than common.", "사람들은 황새치가 배를 들이받았으리라 짐작했소, 여러분. 그러나 선장은 그 위도에서 보기 드문 행운이 자기를 기다리고 있다고 믿을 만한 무슨 남다른 이유가 있었던지라, 그 해역을 떠나는 것을 몹시 꺼렸고, 또한 당시로서는 그 누수가 전혀 위험하다고 여겨지지 않았소."], ["They supposed a sword-fish had stabbed her, gentlemen.", "사실 험한 날씨 속에서 선창을 가능한 한 깊은 데까지 뒤져 보고도 누수의 자리를 찾아내지 못하긴 했지만 말이오. 그리하여 배는 계속 항해를 이어 갔고, 선원들은 넉넉하고 여유로운 간격으로 펌프 앞에서 일했소."], ["But the captain, having some unusual reason for believing that rare good luck awaited him in those latitudes; and therefore being very averse to quit them, and the leak not being then considered at all dangerous, though, indeed, they could not find it after searching the hold as low down as was possible in rather heavy weather, the ship still continued her cruisings, the mariners working at the pumps at wide and easy intervals; but no good luck came; more days went by, and not only was the leak yet undiscovered, but it sensibly increased.", "그러나 행운은 오지 않았소. 날이 더 지나도, 누수의 자리는 여전히 찾지 못했을 뿐 아니라 눈에 띄게 더 심해졌소."], ["So much so, that now taking some alarm, the captain, making all sail, stood away for the nearest harbor among the islands, there to have his hull hove out and repaired.", "어찌나 심했던지, 이제 적잖이 놀란 선장은 돛을 모조리 펴고서 섬들 사이의 가장 가까운 항구를 향해 뱃머리를 돌렸소. 거기서 선체를 기울여 끌어 올려 수리할 작정이었소."]], [["“Though no small passage was before her, yet, if the commonest chance favoured, he did not at all fear that his ship would founder by the way, because his pumps were of the best, and being periodically relieved at them, those six-and-thirty men of his could easily keep the ship free; never mind if the leak should double on her. In truth, well nigh the whole of this passage being attended by very prosperous breezes, the Town-Ho had all but certainly arrived in perfect safety at her port without the occurrence of the least fatality, had it not been for the brutal overbearing of Radney, the mate, a Vineyarder, and the bitterly provoked vengeance of Steelkilt, a Lakeman and desperado from Buffalo.", "그 배 앞에 결코 만만찮은 항로가 놓여 있긴 했으나, 그래도 가장 평범한 운만 따라 준다면, 선장은 가는 도중에 배가 침몰하리라고는 조금도 걱정하지 않았소. 그의 펌프가 최상품이었던 데다, 거기에서 번갈아 가며 일을 덜어 가면, 그의 서른여섯 선원들이 능히 배 안을 물 없이 지킬 수 있었기 때문이오. 설령 누수가 곱절로 늘어난다 해도 상관없었소. 사실, 이 항로의 거의 전 구간이 더없이 순조로운 바람을 받았던지라, 타운호는 단 한 차례의 사고도 없이 거의 틀림없이 무사히 항구에 닿았을 것이오. 만일 그 일등항해사인 빈야드(Vineyard) 출신의 래드니(Radney)의 잔인하고 거만한 횡포와, 버펄로(Buffalo) 출신의 호반인(Lakeman)이자 무법자인 스틸킬트(Steelkilt)의 쓰라리게 도발된 복수만 없었더라면 말이오."]], [["“‘Lakeman!—Buffalo!", "'호반인! 버펄로라니!"], ["Pray, what is a Lakeman, and where is Buffalo?’ said Don Sebastian, rising in his swinging mat of grass.", "부디 묻겠소만, 호반인이란 무엇이고, 버펄로는 어디에 있소?' 돈 세바스티안이 풀로 엮은 흔들리는 깔개에서 몸을 일으키며 말했다."]], [["“On the eastern shore of our Lake Erie, Don; but—I crave your courtesy—may be, you shall soon hear further of all that.", "우리 이리(Erie) 호수의 동쪽 기슭이라오, 돈. 하지만 — 너그러이 헤아려 주시오 — 그 모든 사정은 곧 더 듣게 되실 게요."], ["Now, gentlemen, in square-sail brigs and three-masted ships, well-nigh as large and stout as any that ever sailed out of your old Callao to far Manilla; this Lakeman, in the land-locked heart of our America, had yet been nurtured by all those agrarian freebooting impressions popularly connected with the open ocean.", "자, 여러분, 옛 카야오(Callao)에서 머나먼 마닐라(Manilla)까지 항해하던 그 어떤 배 못지않게 크고 튼튼한, 가로돛을 단 쌍돛대 범선과 세 돛대 배를 부리는 일에 있어, 이 호반인은 우리 아메리카(America)의 육지에 둘러싸인 한복판에서 자랐으되, 흔히 망망대해와 결부되곤 하는 그 모든 농경적 약탈자의 기질을 고스란히 길러 받았다오. 왜냐하면 함께 흘러들어 하나로 모인 그 광대한 우리의 담수의 바다들 — 이리와 온타리오와 휴런과 슈피리어와 미시간 — 은 대양과도 같은 광활함을 지녔고, 대양의 가장 고귀한 특질들을 숱하게 갖추었으며, 대양처럼 둘레에 갖가지 인종과 풍토를 거느리고 있기 때문이오."], ["For in their interflowing aggregate, those grand fresh-water seas of ours,—Erie, and Ontario, and Huron, and Superior, and Michigan,—possess an ocean-like expansiveness, with many of the ocean’s noblest traits; with many of its rimmed varieties of races and of climes.", "그것들은 폴리네시아(Polynesia)의 바다가 그러하듯 낭만적인 섬들로 이루어진 둥근 군도(群島)를 품고 있고, 대서양(Atlantic)이 그러하듯 그 상당 부분이 서로 극명히 대비되는 두 위대한 나라에 의해 해안을 이루며, 그 기슭 곳곳에 흩어져 있는 동쪽으로부터의 우리의 수많은 식민 영토로 향하는 긴 해상 통로를 제공하오. 여기저기에서 그 바다들은 포대(砲臺)와, 우뚝 솟은 매키노(Mackinaw)의 산양 같은 험준한 대포들의 위협을 받고, 해전의 승리를 알리는 빠른 천둥소리를 들어 왔으며, 이따금 그 해변을 야만의 미개인들에게 내어 주는데, 그들의 붉게 칠한 얼굴이 모피로 지은 천막에서 번뜩이오."], ["They contain round archipelagoes of romantic isles, even as the Polynesian waters do; in large part, are shored by two great contrasting nations, as the Atlantic is; they furnish long maritime approaches to our numerous territorial colonies from the East, dotted all round their banks; here and there are frowned upon by batteries, and by the goat-like craggy guns of lofty Mackinaw; they have heard the fleet thunderings of naval victories; at intervals, they yield their beaches to wild barbarians, whose red painted faces flash from out their peltry wigwams; for leagues and leagues are flanked by ancient and unentered forests, where the gaunt pines stand like serried lines of kings in Gothic genealogies; those same woods harboring wild Afric beasts of prey, and silken creatures whose exported furs give robes to Tartar Emperors; they mirror the paved capitals of Buffalo and Cleveland, as well as Winnebago villages; they float alike the full-rigged merchant ship, the armed cruiser of the State, the steamer, and the beech canoe; they are swept by Borean and dismasting blasts as direful as any that lash the salted wave; they know what shipwrecks are, for out of sight of land, however inland, they have drowned full many a midnight ship with all its shrieking crew.", "또한 수십 리, 수백 리에 걸쳐 오래되고 사람의 발길이 닿지 않은 숲이 그 옆구리를 지키는데, 거기에는 야윈 소나무들이 고딕식 족보에 늘어선 왕들의 행렬처럼 서 있소. 바로 그 숲들이 아프리카(Afric)의 사나운 맹수와, 그 모피가 수출되어 타타르(Tartar) 황제들의 어의(御衣)가 되는 비단결 같은 짐승들을 품고 있다오."], ["Thus, gentlemen, though an inlander, Steelkilt was wild-ocean born, and wild-ocean nurtured; as much of an audacious mariner as any.", "그 바다들은 위네바고(Winnebago) 부락의 모습뿐 아니라, 버펄로와 클리블랜드(Cleveland)의 포장된 도시들을 거울처럼 비추고, 완전한 삭구를 갖춘 상선과 국가의 무장 순양함과 증기선과 너도밤나무 카누를 한결같이 띄우며, 짠 바닷물을 후려치는 그 어떤 것 못지않게 무시무시하게 돛대를 부러뜨리는 북풍(Borean)의 돌풍에 휩쓸리오."], ["And for Radney, though in his infancy he may have laid him down on the lone Nantucket beach, to nurse at his maternal sea; though in after life he had long followed our austere Atlantic and your contemplative Pacific; yet was he quite as vengeful and full of social quarrel as the backwoods seaman, fresh from the latitudes of buck-horn handled Bowie-knives.", "그 바다들은 난파가 무엇인지 알고 있으니, 아무리 내륙에 있다 한들 뭍이 보이지 않는 그곳에서, 비명을 지르는 선원을 모조리 태운 한밤의 배들을 숱하게 익사시켜 왔기 때문이오. 그러니, 여러분, 비록 내륙 사람이긴 하나, 스틸킬트는 거친 대양에서 태어나 거친 대양에서 길러진 자로서, 그 누구 못지않게 대담한 뱃사람이었소."], ["Yet was this Nantucketer a man with some good-hearted traits; and this Lakeman, a mariner, who though a sort of devil indeed, might yet by inflexible firmness, only tempered by that common decency of human recognition which is the meanest slave’s right; thus treated, this Steelkilt had long been retained harmless and docile.", "그리고 래드니로 말하자면, 비록 갓난아기 때 외로운 낸터킷 해변에 누워 어머니인 바다의 젖을 빨았을지 모르고, 또 훗날의 삶에서 우리의 준엄한 대서양과 여러분의 사색에 잠긴 태평양을 오래도록 따라다녔을지 모르나, 그럼에도 그는 수사슴 뿔 손잡이가 달린 보위 칼(Bowie-knife)의 위도에서 갓 올라온 오지의 뱃사람만큼이나 복수심에 차 있고 시비 걸기를 일삼는 자였소. 그래도 이 낸터킷 사람에게는 몇 가지 마음씨 좋은 구석이 있었고, 또 이 호반인은, 비록 실은 일종의 악마 같은 자이긴 했으나, 그래도 흔들림 없는 단호함으로 다스리되 — 그 단호함을, 가장 천한 노예조차 누릴 권리인 인간으로서의 최소한의 예우라는 평범한 품위로만 누그러뜨리면 — 그렇게만 대해 주면, 이 스틸킬트는 오래도록 해롭지 않고 고분고분하게 부릴 수 있는 자였소."], ["At all events, he had proved so thus far; but Radney was doomed and made mad, and Steelkilt—but, gentlemen, you shall hear.", "적어도 여태까지는 그가 그러함을 입증해 왔소. 그러나 래드니는 파멸할 운명이었고 미쳐 버렸으며, 스틸킬트는 — 하지만, 여러분, 직접 들어 보시오."]], [["“It was not more than a day or two at the furthest after pointing her prow for her island haven, that the Town-Ho’s leak seemed again increasing, but only so as to require an hour or more at the pumps every day.", "타운호가 섬의 피난처를 향해 뱃머리를 돌린 뒤 길어야 하루나 이틀쯤 지났을 무렵, 그 배의 누수가 다시 심해지는 듯했으나, 그래봐야 날마다 한 시간 남짓 펌프질을 해야 하는 정도였소. 여러분은 알아 두셔야 하오."], ["You must know that in a settled and civilized ocean like our Atlantic, for example, some skippers think little of pumping their whole way across it; though of a still, sleepy night, should the officer of the deck happen to forget his duty in that respect, the probability would be that he and his shipmates would never again remember it, on account of all hands gently subsiding to the bottom.", "이를테면 우리 대서양처럼 자리 잡히고 문명화된 바다에서는, 어떤 선장들은 항해 내내 펌프질을 해 가며 건너는 것을 대수롭지 않게 여긴다오. 다만 고요하고 졸음 오는 밤에, 갑판 당직 사관이 어쩌다 그 일을 깜빡 잊기라도 한다면, 십중팔구 그와 그의 동료들은 다시는 그것을 기억해 내지 못하게 될 게요."], ["Nor in the solitary and savage seas far from you to the westward, gentlemen, is it altogether unusual for ships to keep clanging at their pump-handles in full chorus even for a voyage of considerable length; that is, if it lie along a tolerably accessible coast, or if any other reasonable retreat is afforded them.", "모든 사람이 슬그머니 바다 밑바닥으로 가라앉아 버릴 테니 말이오. 또한 여기서 서쪽으로 멀리 떨어진 외롭고 사나운 바다에서도, 여러분, 배들이 상당히 긴 항해 동안에도 펌프 손잡이를 다 함께 한목소리로 절그럭절그럭 울려 대는 일이 아주 드문 것은 아니라오."], ["It is only when a leaky vessel is in some very out of the way part of those waters, some really landless latitude, that her captain begins to feel a little anxious.", "그러니까, 어지간히 접근할 만한 해안을 끼고 있거나, 달리 의지할 만한 합당한 피난처가 주어진다면 말이오. 누수가 있는 배가 그 바다의 외진 곳, 정말로 뭍이라곤 없는 위도에 놓였을 때라야 비로소, 그 선장은 조금 불안을 느끼기 시작하는 것이오."]], [["“Much this way had it been with the Town-Ho; so when her leak was found gaining once more, there was in truth some small concern manifested by several of her company; especially by Radney the mate.", "타운호의 사정도 대체로 이러했소. 그래서 누수가 다시 심해지고 있음이 드러나자, 사실 그 배의 몇몇 선원들은 적잖이 걱정하는 기색을 내비쳤는데, 특히 일등항해사 래드니가 그러했소."], ["He commanded the upper sails to be well hoisted, sheeted home anew, and every way expanded to the breeze.", "그는 윗돛들을 단단히 끌어 올리고, 돛자락을 새로 팽팽히 당겨, 온갖 방법으로 바람을 받도록 활짝 펴라고 명령했소."], ["Now this Radney, I suppose, was as little of a coward, and as little inclined to any sort of nervous apprehensiveness touching his own person as any fearless, unthinking creature on land or on sea that you can conveniently imagine, gentlemen.", "그런데 이 래드니라는 자는, 내 짐작으로는, 여러분이 손쉽게 떠올릴 수 있는 뭍이든 바다든 그 어떤 겁 없고 생각 없는 피조물 못지않게 겁이라곤 거의 없고, 또 자기 한 몸의 안위에 대해 어떤 신경질적인 불안 따위도 거의 느끼지 않는 자였소, 여러분."], ["Therefore when he betrayed this solicitude about the safety of the ship, some of the seamen declared that it was only on account of his being a part owner in her.", "그러므로 그가 배의 안전을 두고 이런 근심을 드러내자, 선원들 가운데 더러는 그것이 다만 그가 그 배의 지분을 가진 공동 선주이기 때문이라고들 했소."], ["So when they were working that evening at the pumps, there was on this head no small gamesomeness slily going on among them, as they stood with their feet continually overflowed by the rippling clear water; clear as any mountain spring, gentlemen—that bubbling from the pumps ran across the deck, and poured itself out in steady spouts at the lee scupper-holes.", "그래서 그날 저녁 펌프 앞에서 일하던 그들 사이에는, 이 문제를 두고 적잖은 짓궂은 농지거리가 슬그머니 오갔는데, 그동안 그들의 발은 잔물결 이는 맑은 물에 끊임없이 잠겨 있었소. 산골 샘물처럼 맑은 물이었다오, 여러분 — 펌프에서 보글거리며 솟아난 그 물이 갑판을 가로질러 흘러, 바람 아래쪽 배수구를 통해 꾸준한 물줄기로 쏟아져 나갔소."]], [["“Now, as you well know, it is not seldom the case in this conventional world of ours—watery or otherwise; that when a person placed in command over his fellow-men finds one of them to be very significantly his superior in general pride of manhood, straightway against that man he conceives an unconquerable dislike and bitterness; and if he have a chance he will pull down and pulverize that subaltern’s tower, and make a little heap of dust of it.", "자, 여러분도 잘 아시다시피, 우리의 이 관습적인 세상에서는 — 물 위든 어디든 — 동료들 위에 명령권자로 앉은 어떤 사람이, 그들 가운데 하나가 사내다운 자존심에 있어 자기보다 현저히 뛰어남을 알아챘을 때, 그 즉시 그 사람을 향해 도무지 누를 수 없는 혐오와 쓰라림을 품게 되는 일이 드물지 않다오. 그리고 기회만 닿으면 그는 그 아랫사람의 탑을 허물어 가루로 만들고, 한 줌 먼지 더미로 빻아 버리려 하지요."], ["Be this conceit of mine as it may, gentlemen, at all events Steelkilt was a tall and noble animal with a head like a Roman, and a flowing golden beard like the tasseled housings of your last viceroy’s snorting charger; and a brain, and a heart, and a soul in him, gentlemen, which had made Steelkilt Charlemagne, had he been born son to Charlemagne’s father.", "이런 내 생각이야 어찌 됐든, 여러분, 어쨌든 스틸킬트는 키가 크고 고귀한 짐승 같은 사내로, 머리는 로마인 같았고, 흘러내리는 황금빛 수염은 여러분네 최근 부왕(副王)의 콧김 뿜는 군마에 두른 술 달린 마의(馬衣) 같았으며, 그의 안에는 두뇌와 심장과 영혼이 깃들어 있었으니, 만일 그가 샤를마뉴의 아버지의 아들로 태어났더라면 스틸킬트를 능히 샤를마뉴로 만들었을 그런 것이었소."], ["But Radney, the mate, was ugly as a mule; yet as hardy, as stubborn, as malicious.", "그러나 일등항해사 래드니는 노새처럼 못생긴 자였소. 그러면서도 노새처럼 단단하고, 고집스럽고, 악의에 차 있었소."], ["He did not love Steelkilt, and Steelkilt knew it.", "그는 스틸킬트를 좋아하지 않았고, 스틸킬트도 그것을 알고 있었소."]], [["“Espying the mate drawing near as he was toiling at the pump with the rest, the Lakeman affected not to notice him, but unawed, went on with his gay banterings.", "다른 이들과 함께 펌프 앞에서 고되게 일하던 호반인은, 일등항해사가 가까이 다가오는 것을 곁눈으로 보고도 못 본 척하며, 조금도 주눅 들지 않고 흥겨운 농담을 이어 갔소."]], [["“‘Aye, aye, my merry lads, it’s a lively leak this; hold a cannikin, one of ye, and let’s have a taste.", "'그래, 그래, 유쾌한 친구들아, 이거 참 활기찬 누수로구나. 누가 깡통 하나 받쳐 봐, 맛 좀 보자고."], ["By the Lord, it’s worth bottling!", "맙소사, 이건 병에 담아 둘 만한 물이야!"], ["I tell ye what, men, old Rad’s investment must go for it!", "이봐, 친구들, 내 말 좀 들어 보게. 늙은 래드 영감의 투자금이 이걸로 날아가게 생겼어!"], ["he had best cut away his part of the hull and tow it home.", "영감은 차라리 제 몫의 선체를 잘라 내서 집으로 끌고 가는 게 낫겠다."], ["The fact is, boys, that sword-fish only began the job; he’s come back again with a gang of ship-carpenters, saw-fish, and file-fish, and what not; and the whole posse of ’em are now hard at work cutting and slashing at the bottom; making improvements, I suppose.", "사실은 말이야, 친구들, 그 황새치 놈은 일을 시작했을 뿐이라네. 이제 그놈이 배 목수 패거리를 데리고 다시 돌아왔거든."], ["If old Rad were here now, I’d tell him to jump overboard and scatter ’em.", "톱상어에 줄상어에 별별 놈들을 다 데리고 말이지. 그 패거리 전부가 지금 배 밑바닥을 깎고 베면서 한창 일하는 중이라네."], ["They’re playing the devil with his estate, I can tell him.", "아마 개량 공사를 하는 모양이지. 만일 늙은 래드 영감이 지금 여기 있다면, 나는 바다로 뛰어들어 그놈들을 흩어 버리라고 일러 줄 텐데."], ["But he’s a simple old soul,—Rad, and a beauty too.", "그놈들이 영감 재산을 가지고 아주 야단법석을 떨고 있다고 말해 줄 테야."], ["Boys, they say the rest of his property is invested in looking-glasses.", "하지만 영감은 단순한 늙은이지 — 래드 말이야, 게다가 미남이기까지 하고. 친구들아, 영감 재산 나머지는 죄다 거울에 처박혀 있다더군."], ["I wonder if he’d give a poor devil like me the model of his nose.’", "나 같은 가련한 놈한테 그 코의 본을 떠 주지 않으려나 모르겠네.'"]], [["“‘Damn your eyes!", "'이 망할 눈깔들!"], ["what’s that pump stopping for?’ roared Radney, pretending not to have heard the sailors’ talk.", "그 펌프가 왜 멈췄어?' 래드니가 선원들의 말을 못 들은 척하며 고함쳤소."], ["‘Thunder away at it!’", "'천둥 치듯 두들겨 대란 말이야!'"]], [["“‘Aye, aye, sir,’ said Steelkilt, merry as a cricket.", "'예, 예, 나리.' 귀뚜라미처럼 흥겹게 스틸킬트가 말했소."], ["‘Lively, boys, lively, now!’ And with that the pump clanged like fifty fire-engines; the men tossed their hats off to it, and ere long that peculiar gasping of the lungs was heard which denotes the fullest tension of life’s utmost energies.", "'활기차게, 친구들, 자, 활기차게!' 그러자 펌프가 쉰 대의 소방 펌프처럼 절그럭거렸고, 사람들은 그 위로 모자를 벗어 던졌으며, 이윽고 폐가 헐떡이는 그 독특한 소리가 들려왔으니, 그것은 삶의 가장 극한의 기력을 한껏 짜내고 있음을 알리는 소리였소."]], [["“Quitting the pump at last, with the rest of his band, the Lakeman went forward all panting, and sat himself down on the windlass; his face fiery red, his eyes bloodshot, and wiping the profuse sweat from his brow.", "마침내 자기 패거리의 나머지와 함께 펌프를 떠난 호반인은 헐떡이며 앞쪽으로 나아가, 양묘기(揚錨機) 위에 주저앉았소. 얼굴은 불처럼 붉고, 두 눈은 핏발이 섰으며, 이마에서 줄줄 흐르는 땀을 닦고 있었소."], ["Now what cozening fiend it was, gentlemen, that possessed Radney to meddle with such a man in that corporeally exasperated state, I know not; but so it happened.", "자, 여러분, 도대체 어떤 사람을 속이는 마귀가 래드니에게 들러붙어, 그토록 육체적으로 지쳐 격앙된 상태에 있는 사내를 건드리게 만들었는지 나는 모르오. 하지만 일은 그렇게 벌어졌소."], ["Intolerably striding along the deck, the mate commanded him to get a broom and sweep down the planks, and also a shovel, and remove some offensive matters consequent upon allowing a pig to run at large.", "견딜 수 없는 거드름을 피우며 갑판을 성큼성큼 가로지른 일등항해사는, 빗자루를 가져와 널빤지를 쓸어 내라고, 또 삽도 가져와서, 돼지를 마음대로 돌아다니게 둔 탓에 생긴 더러운 것들을 치우라고 그에게 명령했소."]], [["“Now, gentlemen, sweeping a ship’s deck at sea is a piece of household work which in all times but raging gales is regularly attended to every evening; it has been known to be done in the case of ships actually foundering at the time.", "자, 여러분, 바다에서 배의 갑판을 쓰는 일은, 사나운 폭풍이 몰아치는 때만 빼고는 언제든 저녁마다 으레 행해지는 일종의 집안일이오. 실제로 침몰하는 와중에 있는 배에서도 그 일이 행해진 적이 있다고 알려져 있소."], ["Such, gentlemen, is the inflexibility of sea-usages and the instinctive love of neatness in seamen; some of whom would not willingly drown without first washing their faces.", "여러분, 바다의 관습이란 그토록 굽힐 줄 모르는 것이고, 또 선원들에게는 그토록 본능적인 청결에 대한 사랑이 있는 것이오. 그들 가운데 어떤 이들은 먼저 얼굴을 씻지 않고서는 기꺼이 익사하려 들지도 않을 게요."], ["But in all vessels this broom business is the prescriptive province of the boys, if boys there be aboard.", "그러나 어느 배에서든 이 빗자루 일은, 만일 배에 소년들이 있다면, 으레 소년들에게 정해진 몫이오."], ["Besides, it was the stronger men in the Town-Ho that had been divided into gangs, taking turns at the pumps; and being the most athletic seaman of them all, Steelkilt had been regularly assigned captain of one of the gangs; consequently he should have been freed from any trivial business not connected with truly nautical duties, such being the case with his comrades.", "게다가 타운호에서는 더 힘센 사내들이 패거리로 나뉘어 번갈아 가며 펌프질을 맡고 있었고, 스틸킬트는 그들 가운데 가장 건장한 뱃사람이었던지라 으레 한 패의 우두머리로 정해져 있었소. 따라서 그는, 그의 동료들이 그러했듯, 참된 선원의 임무와 무관한 어떤 하찮은 일에서도 마땅히 면제되어야 했소."], ["I mention all these particulars so that you may understand exactly how this affair stood between the two men.", "내가 이 모든 세세한 사정을 말하는 것은, 이 일이 그 두 사람 사이에서 정확히 어떤 형국이었는지를 여러분이 똑똑히 이해하시도록 하기 위함이오."]], [["“But there was more than this: the order about the shovel was almost as plainly meant to sting and insult Steelkilt, as though Radney had spat in his face.", "그러나 이게 다가 아니었소. 삽에 관한 그 명령은, 마치 래드니가 그의 얼굴에 침을 뱉기라도 한 것처럼, 거의 노골적으로 스틸킬트를 찌르고 모욕하려는 뜻이었소."], ["Any man who has gone sailor in a whale-ship will understand this; and all this and doubtless much more, the Lakeman fully comprehended when the mate uttered his command.", "포경선에서 뱃사람 노릇을 해 본 사람이라면 누구나 이 점을 이해할 게요. 그리고 일등항해사가 그 명령을 내뱉었을 때, 호반인은 이 모든 것을, 아니 의심할 바 없이 그 이상을 온전히 알아챘소."], ["But as he sat still for a moment, and as he steadfastly looked into the mate’s malignant eye and perceived the stacks of powder-casks heaped up in him and the slow-match silently burning along towards them; as he instinctively saw all this, that strange forbearance and unwillingness to stir up the deeper passionateness in any already ireful being—a repugnance most felt, when felt at all, by really valiant men even when aggrieved—this nameless phantom feeling, gentlemen, stole over Steelkilt.", "그러나 그가 잠시 가만히 앉아, 일등항해사의 악의에 찬 눈을 뚫어지게 들여다보며, 그 안에 쌓여 있는 화약통 더미와, 그것을 향해 소리 없이 타들어 가는 도화선을 알아보았을 때 — 본능적으로 이 모든 것을 알아챘을 때 — 이미 노기에 찬 어떤 존재의 더 깊은 격정을 휘저어 놓기를 꺼리는 저 기묘한 자제심과 망설임이, 곧 참으로 용감한 자들이 부당한 일을 당했을 때조차 느끼곤 하는, 혹여 느낀다면 가장 강하게 느끼는 그 거부감이, 이 이름 없는 유령 같은 감정이, 여러분, 스틸킬트를 슬며시 사로잡았소."]], [["“Therefore, in his ordinary tone, only a little broken by the bodily exhaustion he was temporarily in, he answered him saying that sweeping the deck was not his business, and he would not do it.", "그리하여, 잠시 빠져 있던 육체적 탈진으로 조금 끊기긴 했으나 평소의 어조로, 그는 갑판을 쓰는 것은 자기 일이 아니며 그 일을 하지 않겠다고 그에게 답했소."], ["And then, without at all alluding to the shovel, he pointed to three lads as the customary sweepers; who, not being billeted at the pumps, had done little or nothing all day.", "그러고는 삽에 대해서는 전혀 언급하지 않은 채, 으레 갑판을 쓰는 일을 맡은 세 소년을 가리켰는데, 그들은 펌프에 배치되지 않았던지라 온종일 일을 거의, 아니 전혀 하지 않은 터였소."], ["To this, Radney replied with an oath, in a most domineering and outrageous manner unconditionally reiterating his command; meanwhile advancing upon the still seated Lakeman, with an uplifted cooper’s club hammer which he had snatched from a cask near by.", "이에 래드니는 욕설을 내뱉으며, 더없이 위압적이고 무도한 태도로 자기 명령을 조건 없이 거듭 되풀이했소. 그러는 동안 그는 근처의 통에서 낚아챈 통메장이의 큰 망치를 치켜든 채, 여전히 앉아 있는 호반인을 향해 다가섰소."]], [["“Heated and irritated as he was by his spasmodic toil at the pumps, for all his first nameless feeling of forbearance the sweating Steelkilt could but ill brook this bearing in the mate; but somehow still smothering the conflagration within him, without speaking he remained doggedly rooted to his seat, till at last the incensed Radney shook the hammer within a few inches of his face, furiously commanding him to do his bidding.", "펌프질의 발작적인 노동으로 몸이 달아오르고 약이 오를 대로 오른 데다, 그 처음의 이름 없는 자제심에도 불구하고, 땀에 젖은 스틸킬트는 일등항해사의 이 태도를 좀처럼 견딜 수가 없었소. 그러나 그래도 어떻게든 안에서 타오르는 불길을 억누른 채, 그는 말없이 제 자리에 끈질기게 뿌리내린 듯 앉아 있었소. 그러다 마침내 격분한 래드니가 그의 얼굴에서 몇 인치 안 되는 데까지 망치를 휘두르며, 시키는 대로 하라고 사납게 명령하기에 이르렀소."]], [["“Steelkilt rose, and slowly retreating round the windlass, steadily followed by the mate with his menacing hammer, deliberately repeated his intention not to obey.", "스틸킬트는 일어나, 위협하는 망치를 든 일등항해사가 끈질기게 뒤따르는 가운데, 천천히 양묘기를 돌아 물러나면서, 복종하지 않겠다는 제 뜻을 차분히 거듭 밝혔소."], ["Seeing, however, that his forbearance had not the slightest effect, by an awful and unspeakable intimation with his twisted hand he warned off the foolish and infatuated man; but it was to no purpose.", "그러나 자신의 자제심이 손톱만 한 효과도 없음을 알아채고는, 비틀린 손으로 무섭고 형언할 수 없는 신호를 보내, 어리석고 분별을 잃은 그 사내에게 물러서라고 경고했소. 하지만 그것도 헛수고였소."], ["And in this way the two went once slowly round the windlass; when, resolved at last no longer to retreat, bethinking him that he had now forborne as much as comported with his humor, the Lakeman paused on the hatches and thus spoke to the officer:", "그렇게 두 사람은 양묘기를 천천히 한 바퀴 돌았소. 마침내 더는 물러서지 않기로 작정하고, 자기 성미에 어울리는 만큼은 이제 충분히 참았다고 생각한 호반인은, 승강구 뚜껑 위에 멈춰 서서 그 사관에게 이렇게 말했소."]], [["“‘Mr. Radney, I will not obey you.", "'래드니 씨, 나는 당신 명령을 따르지 않겠소."], ["Take that hammer away, or look to yourself.’ But the predestinated mate coming still closer to him, where the Lakeman stood fixed, now shook the heavy hammer within an inch of his teeth; meanwhile repeating a string of insufferable maledictions.", "그 망치를 치우든지, 아니면 당신 몸이나 조심하시오.' 그러나 운명이 미리 정해 둔 그 일등항해사는 호반인이 우뚝 멈춰 선 자리로 한층 더 바싹 다가서더니, 그 묵직한 망치를 그의 이빨에서 한 치 떨어진 데까지 휘둘러 댔소."], ["Retreating not the thousandth part of an inch; stabbing him in the eye with the unflinching poniard of his glance, Steelkilt, clenching his right hand behind him and creepingly drawing it back, told his persecutor that if the hammer but grazed his cheek he (Steelkilt) would murder him.", "그러면서 차마 견딜 수 없는 저주를 줄줄이 퍼부었소. 스틸킬트는 천분의 일 인치도 물러서지 않고, 흔들림 없는 단도 같은 눈빛으로 상대의 눈을 찌르며, 오른손을 등 뒤로 움켜쥐고 슬그머니 끌어당기면서, 그 망치가 제 뺨을 스치기만 해도 자기가(스틸킬트가) 그를 죽여 버리겠노라고 그 박해자에게 일렀소."], ["But, gentlemen, the fool had been branded for the slaughter by the gods.", "그러나, 여러분, 그 바보는 이미 신들에게 도살의 낙인이 찍힌 자였소."], ["Immediately the hammer touched the cheek; the next instant the lower jaw of the mate was stove in his head; he fell on the hatch spouting blood like a whale.", "그 즉시 망치가 뺨에 닿았고, 다음 순간 일등항해사의 아래턱이 머릿속으로 으스러져 박혔소. 그는 고래처럼 피를 뿜으며 승강구 뚜껑 위로 쓰러졌소."]], [["“Ere the cry could go aft Steelkilt was shaking one of the backstays leading far aloft to where two of his comrades were standing their mastheads.", "그 외침이 고물 쪽으로 미처 닿기도 전에, 스틸킬트는 저 높은 곳으로 이어진 뒷버팀 밧줄(backstay) 하나를 흔들고 있었으니, 거기 돛대 꼭대기에는 그의 동료 둘이 망보고 서 있었소."], ["They were both Canallers.", "두 사람 다 운하 사람(Canaller)이었소."]], [["“‘Canallers!’ cried Don Pedro.", "'운하 사람들이라니!' 돈 페드로가 외쳤다."], ["‘We have seen many whale-ships in our harbours, but never heard of your Canallers.", "'우리 항구에서 포경선이라면 숱하게 보았소만, 운하 사람이란 말은 들어 본 적이 없소."], ["Pardon: who and what are they?’", "미안하지만, 그들은 누구이고 무엇이오?'"]], [["“‘Canallers, Don, are the boatmen belonging to our grand Erie Canal.", "'운하 사람이란, 돈, 우리의 그 위대한 이리 운하(Erie Canal)에 속한 뱃사람들이라오."], ["You must have heard of it.’", "그 운하 이야기는 분명 들어 보셨을 게요.'"]], [["“‘Nay, Senor; hereabouts in this dull, warm, most lazy, and hereditary land, we know but little of your vigorous North.’", "'아니오, 세뇨르. 이 무덥고 나른하며 더없이 게으른, 대대로 물려받은 이 땅에서, 우리는 여러분네 활기찬 북녘에 대해 아는 바가 거의 없다오.'"]], [["“‘Aye?", "'그렇소?"], ["Well then, Don, refill my cup.", "그럼, 돈, 내 잔이나 다시 채워 주시오. 여러분네 치차(chicha)는 참으로 훌륭하구려."], ["Your chicha’s very fine; and ere proceeding further I will tell ye what our Canallers are; for such information may throw side-light upon my story.’", "더 나아가기 전에, 우리 운하 사람들이 어떤 자들인지 일러 드리리다. 그런 정보가 내 이야기에 곁들이는 빛을 던져 줄 수 있을 테니 말이오.'"]], [["“For three hundred and sixty miles, gentlemen, through the entire breadth of the state of New York; through numerous populous cities and most thriving villages; through long, dismal, uninhabited swamps, and affluent, cultivated fields, unrivalled for fertility; by billiard-room and bar-room; through the holy-of-holies of great forests; on Roman arches over Indian rivers; through sun and shade; by happy hearts or broken; through all the wide contrasting scenery of those noble Mohawk counties; and especially, by rows of snow-white chapels, whose spires stand almost like milestones, flows one continual stream of Venetianly corrupt and often lawless life.", "여러분, 삼백육십 마일에 걸쳐, 뉴욕(New York) 주 전체를 가로질러, 인구로 북적이는 숱한 도시들과 더없이 번창하는 마을들을 지나, 길고 음산한 사람 없는 늪지와, 비옥하기로는 견줄 데 없는 풍요로운 경작지를 지나, 당구장과 술집 곁을 지나, 거대한 숲의 지성소(至聖所)를 지나, 인디언(Indian)의 강 위에 걸린 로마식 아치를 건너, 햇살과 그늘 사이를 지나, 행복한 마음과 부서진 마음 곁을 지나, 저 고귀한 모호크(Mohawk) 군(郡)들의 그 넓디넓고 대조적인 온갖 풍경을 가로질러, 그리고 특히, 그 첨탑이 거의 이정표처럼 늘어선 눈처럼 새하얀 예배당들의 줄을 따라, 베네치아처럼 타락하고 흔히 무법한 삶의 끊이지 않는 물줄기 하나가 흐르고 있다오. 바로 거기 여러분네 진짜 아샨티(Ashantee)가 있소, 여러분."], ["There’s your true Ashantee, gentlemen; there howl your pagans; where you ever find them, next door to you; under the long-flung shadow, and the snug patronising lee of churches.", "거기서 여러분네 이교도들이 울부짖고 있소. 여러분이 그들을 발견하는 곳이라면 어디든, 그들은 바로 여러분의 옆집에 있다오."], ["For by some curious fatality, as it is often noted of your metropolitan freebooters that they ever encamp around the halls of justice, so sinners, gentlemen, most abound in holiest vicinities.", "길게 드리워진 그늘 아래, 교회의 아늑하고 너그러운 바람그늘 속에 말이오. 왜냐하면, 여러분네 대도시의 약탈자들이 늘 재판소 주위에 진을 친다고 흔히들 말하듯, 어떤 기묘한 운명의 장난으로, 죄인들은, 여러분, 가장 거룩한 곳 가까이에 가장 많이 들끓는 법이니 말이오."]], [["“‘Is that a friar passing?’ said Don Pedro, looking downwards into the crowded plazza, with humorous concern.", "'저기 지나가는 게 수사(修士)요?' 돈 페드로가 익살스러운 걱정을 담아, 북적이는 광장을 내려다보며 말했다."]], [["“‘Well for our northern friend, Dame Isabella’s Inquisition wanes in Lima,’ laughed Don Sebastian.", "'우리 북녘 친구로서는 다행이지요, 이사벨라 여왕(Dame Isabella)의 종교재판이 리마에서는 시들해졌으니 말이오.' 돈 세바스티안이 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘Proceed, Senor.’", "'계속하시오, 세뇨르.'"]], [["“‘A moment!", "'잠깐만!"], ["Pardon!’ cried another of the company.", "미안하오!' 일행 가운데 또 한 사람이 외쳤다."], ["‘In the name of all us Limeese, I but desire to express to you, sir sailor, that we have by no means overlooked your delicacy in not substituting present Lima for distant Venice in your corrupt comparison.", "'우리 리마 사람들 모두의 이름으로, 나는 다만 뱃사람 양반께 한마디 하고 싶소. 당신이 그 타락의 비유에서, 가까운 리마를 먼 베네치아로 바꿔 넣는 섬세한 배려를 보여 주신 것을 우리가 결코 놓치지 않았다는 것을 말이오."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["do not bow and look surprised; you know the proverb all along this coast—“Corrupt as Lima.”", "고개를 숙이며 놀란 척하지 마시오. 이 해안 일대에 두루 퍼진 그 속담을 당신도 알고 있잖소 — \"리마처럼 타락했다.\""], ["It but bears out your saying, too; churches more plentiful than billiard-tables, and for ever open—and “Corrupt as Lima.”", "그 속담이 당신의 그 말마따나 들어맞소. 교회가 당구대보다 더 흔하고, 또 언제나 문이 열려 있으니 — \"리마처럼 타락했다.\""], ["So, too, Venice; I have been there; the holy city of the blessed evangelist, St.", "베네치아도 마찬가지요. 나도 거기 가 본 적이 있소."], ["Mark!—St.", "복된 복음사가, 성 마르코(St. Mark)의 거룩한 도시 말이오!"], ["Dominic, purge it!", "— 성 도미니쿠스(St. Dominic)여, 그곳을 정화하소서!"], ["Your cup!", "당신 잔을 이리 주시오! 고맙소."], ["Thanks: here I refill; now, you pour out again.’", "자, 채우겠소. 이제 당신이 다시 따라 주시오.'"]], [["“Freely depicted in his own vocation, gentlemen, the Canaller would make a fine dramatic hero, so abundantly and picturesquely wicked is he.", "여러분, 자신의 생업 속에 거리낌 없이 그려 놓고 보면, 운하 사람은 멋들어진 극적 영웅이 될 만하니, 그토록 풍성하고도 그림 같은 악당이기 때문이오."], ["Like Mark Antony, for days and days along his green-turfed, flowery Nile, he indolently floats, openly toying with his red-cheeked Cleopatra, ripening his apricot thigh upon the sunny deck.", "마르쿠스 안토니우스(Mark Antony)처럼, 그는 푸른 잔디와 꽃이 우거진 자기 나일강을 따라 며칠이고 며칠이고 나른하게 떠다니며, 볼이 발그레한 자기 클레오파트라(Cleopatra)와 드러내 놓고 희롱하고, 햇살 비치는 갑판 위에서 살구 같은 허벅지를 무르익히고 있소."], ["But ashore, all this effeminacy is dashed.", "그러나 뭍에 오르면, 이 모든 나약함은 흔적도 없이 사라지오."], ["The brigandish guise which the Canaller so proudly sports; his slouched and gaily-ribboned hat betoken his grand features.", "운하 사람이 그토록 자랑스레 뽐내며 두르는 그 도적 같은 차림새, 그 비뚜름하게 눌러쓴, 화려한 리본이 달린 모자가 그의 위풍당당한 면모를 드러내오. 그가 떠다니며 지나는 마을들의 그 미소 짓는 순박함에는 공포의 대상이며, 그 거무튀튀한 얼굴과 거침없는 활갯짓은 도시에서도 사람들이 피하지 않을 수 없는 것이라오."], ["A terror to the smiling innocence of the villages through which he floats; his swart visage and bold swagger are not unshunned in cities.", "한때 나 자신도 그 운하 위에서 떠돌이 신세였는데, 이런 운하 사람 가운데 하나에게서 좋은 신세를 진 적이 있소. 나는 그에게 진심으로 고마워하며, 은혜를 모르는 자가 되고 싶지 않소."], ["Once a vagabond on his own canal, I have received good turns from one of these Canallers; I thank him heartily; would fain be not ungrateful; but it is often one of the prime redeeming qualities of your man of violence, that at times he has as stiff an arm to back a poor stranger in a strait, as to plunder a wealthy one.", "다만, 여러분네 폭력배의 으뜸가는 미덕 가운데 하나가 흔히 이것이라오. 그가 때때로 부유한 자를 약탈하는 데 못지않게, 곤경에 빠진 가련한 낯선 이를 떠받쳐 주는 데도 그토록 굳센 팔뚝을 가졌다는 것 말이오."], ["In sum, gentlemen, what the wildness of this canal life is, is emphatically evinced by this; that our wild whale-fishery contains so many of its most finished graduates, and that scarce any race of mankind, except Sydney men, are so much distrusted by our whaling captains.", "요컨대, 여러분, 이 운하 생활의 거칢이 어떠한지는 바로 이것으로 분명히 드러나오. 우리의 거친 포경업이 그 운하가 길러 낸 가장 노련한 졸업생들을 그토록 많이 품고 있다는 것, 그리고 시드니(Sydney) 사람들을 빼고는 인류의 그 어떤 족속도 우리 포경선 선장들에게 그토록 불신받는 자들은 없다는 것이 그 증거요."], ["Nor does it at all diminish the curiousness of this matter, that to many thousands of our rural boys and young men born along its line, the probationary life of the Grand Canal furnishes the sole transition between quietly reaping in a Christian corn-field, and recklessly ploughing the waters of the most barbaric seas.", "또한, 그 운하 일대에서 태어난 우리네 수천수만의 시골 소년과 젊은이들에게, 그 거대한 운하의 수습 생활이 그리스도교의 보리밭에서 조용히 곡식을 거두던 삶과, 더없이 야만적인 바다의 물결을 무모하게 헤쳐 가는 삶 사이의 유일한 이행기를 마련해 준다는 사실 또한, 이 문제의 기묘함을 조금도 덜어 주지 않는다오."]], [["“‘I see!", "'알겠소!"], ["I see!’ impetuously exclaimed Don Pedro, spilling his chicha upon his silvery ruffles.", "알겠소!' 돈 페드로가 자기 치차를 은빛 주름 장식에 쏟아 가며 격하게 외쳤다."], ["‘No need to travel!", "'여행할 필요가 없겠구려!"], ["The world’s one Lima.", "세상이 온통 하나의 리마니 말이오."], ["I had thought, now, that at your temperate North the generations were cold and holy as the hills.—But the story.’", "나는 지금껏, 여러분네 온화한 북녘에서는 사람들이 저 언덕들처럼 차갑고 거룩하리라 여겼었소. 하지만 — 그 이야기나 계속하시오.'"]], [["“I left off, gentlemen, where the Lakeman shook the backstay.", "여러분, 호반인이 뒷버팀 밧줄을 흔드는 데서 이야기를 멈췄었소."], ["Hardly had he done so, when he was surrounded by the three junior mates and the four harpooneers, who all crowded him to the deck.", "그가 그렇게 하기가 무섭게, 세 명의 하급 항해사와 네 명의 작살잡이가 그를 에워싸고, 모두가 그를 갑판 위로 밀어붙였소."], ["But sliding down the ropes like baleful comets, the two Canallers rushed into the uproar, and sought to drag their man out of it towards the forecastle.", "그러나 두 운하 사람이 불길한 혜성처럼 밧줄을 타고 미끄러져 내려와 그 소란 속으로 뛰어들더니, 자기네 사내를 거기서 끌어내어 앞갑판 쪽으로 데려가려 했소."], ["Others of the sailors joined with them in this attempt, and a twisted turmoil ensued; while standing out of harm’s way, the valiant captain danced up and down with a whale-pike, calling upon his officers to manhandle that atrocious scoundrel, and smoke him along to the quarter-deck.", "다른 선원들도 이 시도에 가세하자, 뒤엉킨 혼전이 벌어졌소. 그동안 그 용맹한 선장은 위험을 피해 비켜선 채, 고래 작살창을 들고 오르락내리락 날뛰며, 자기 사관들에게 저 흉악한 악당을 거칠게 다루어 뒷갑판으로 몰아오라고 외쳐 댔소."], ["At intervals, he ran close up to the revolving border of the confusion, and prying into the heart of it with his pike, sought to prick out the object of his resentment.", "이따금 그는 빙빙 도는 혼란의 가장자리까지 바싹 다가가, 작살창으로 그 한복판을 쑤시며, 자기 원한의 대상을 찔러 끄집어내려 했소."], ["But Steelkilt and his desperadoes were too much for them all; they succeeded in gaining the forecastle deck, where, hastily slewing about three or four large casks in a line with the windlass, these sea-Parisians entrenched themselves behind the barricade.", "그러나 스틸킬트와 그의 무법자들은 그들 전부에게 벅찬 상대였소. 그들은 앞갑판에 오르는 데 성공했고, 거기서 큼직한 통 서너 개를 양묘기와 일렬로 황급히 돌려놓아, 이 바다의 파리지앵들은 그 바리케이드 뒤에 진을 쳤소."]], [["“‘Come out of that, ye pirates!’ roared the captain, now menacing them with a pistol in each hand, just brought to him by the steward.", "'거기서 나와라, 이 해적 놈들아!' 선장이 이제 양손에 권총을 든 채 그들을 위협하며 고함쳤소."], ["‘Come out of that, ye cut-throats!’", "권총은 방금 사환이 그에게 가져다준 것이었소. '거기서 나오란 말이다, 이 살인자 놈들아!'"]], [["“Steelkilt leaped on the barricade, and striding up and down there, defied the worst the pistols could do; but gave the captain to understand distinctly, that his (Steelkilt’s) death would be the signal for a murderous mutiny on the part of all hands.", "스틸킬트는 바리케이드 위로 뛰어올라, 거기서 오르락내리락 성큼성큼 거닐며, 그 권총들이 할 수 있는 최악의 짓을 비웃었소. 그러나 자신(스틸킬트)의 죽음이 곧 전 선원의 살인적 반란을 알리는 신호가 되리라는 것을 선장에게 똑똑히 깨우쳐 주었소."], ["Fearing in his heart lest this might prove but too true, the captain a little desisted, but still commanded the insurgents instantly to return to their duty.", "이 말이 너무도 사실로 드러날까 마음속으로 두려워한 선장은 조금 물러섰으나, 그래도 반란자들에게 당장 제 임무로 돌아가라고 명령했소."]], [["“‘Will you promise not to touch us, if we do?’ demanded their ringleader.", "'우리가 돌아가면, 우리한테 손대지 않겠다고 약속하겠소?' 그들의 우두머리가 따져 물었소."]], [["“‘Turn to!", "'일이나 해!"], ["turn to!—I make no promise;—to your duty!", "일이나 하란 말이다! 약속은 못 한다."], ["Do you want to sink the ship, by knocking off at a time like this?", "네 임무로 돌아가! 이런 판국에 일손을 놓아 배를 침몰시킬 작정이냐?"], ["Turn to!’ and he once more raised a pistol.", "일이나 해!' 그러면서 그는 다시 한번 권총을 치켜들었소."]], [["“‘Sink the ship?’ cried Steelkilt.", "'배를 침몰시키겠다고?' 스틸킬트가 외쳤소."], ["‘Aye, let her sink.", "'그래, 가라앉히라지."], ["Not a man of us turns to, unless you swear not to raise a rope-yarn against us.", "우리 가운데 단 한 놈도 돌아가지 않을 거다. 당신이 우리한테 밧줄 한 가닥 들이대지 않겠다고 맹세하지 않는 한은 말이야."], ["What say ye, men?’ turning to his comrades.", "어떤가, 친구들?' 그가 동료들을 돌아보며 말했소."], ["A fierce cheer was their response.", "사나운 환호가 그들의 대답이었소."]], [["“The Lakeman now patrolled the barricade, all the while keeping his eye on the Captain, and jerking out such sentences as these:—‘It’s not our fault; we didn’t want it; I told him to take his hammer away; it was boy’s business; he might have known me before this; I told him not to prick the buffalo; I believe I have broken a finger here against his cursed jaw; ain’t those mincing knives down in the forecastle there, men? look to those handspikes, my hearties. Captain, by God, look to yourself; say the word; don’t be a fool; forget it all; we are ready to turn to; treat us decently, and we’re your men; but we won’t be flogged.’", "호반인은 이제 바리케이드 위를 순찰하듯 거닐며, 줄곧 선장에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 이런 말들을 툭툭 내뱉었소. '우리 잘못이 아니다. 우리가 원한 일도 아니야. 나는 그자한테 망치를 치우라고 일렀다. 그건 소년들이나 할 일이었어. 그자는 진작 나를 알아봤어야 했지. 나는 그자한테 들소를 찌르지 말라고 했단 말이다. 여기 그자의 그 빌어먹을 턱에 부딪쳐 손가락 하나가 부러진 것 같군. 저 앞갑판에 다지는 칼들이 있지 않나, 친구들? 저 지렛대들을 챙겨 둬, 내 용감한 친구들아. 선장, 맹세코, 당신 몸이나 조심하시지. 한마디만 하시오. 바보짓 좀 하지 말고. 다 잊어버리란 말이야. 우리는 일하러 돌아갈 준비가 돼 있어. 우리를 점잖게 대해 주면, 우리는 당신 사람들이야. 하지만 채찍질만은 당하지 않겠다.'"]], [["“‘Turn to!", "'일이나 해!"], ["I make no promises, turn to, I say!’", "나는 약속 따위 안 한다, 일이나 하란 말이다!'"]], [["“‘Look ye, now,’ cried the Lakeman, flinging out his arm towards him, ‘there are a few of us here (and I am one of them) who have shipped for the cruise, d’ye see; now as you well know, sir, we can claim our discharge as soon as the anchor is down; so we don’t want a row; it’s not our interest; we want to be peaceable; we are ready to work, but we won’t be flogged.’", "'이봐, 잘 들으시오.' 호반인이 그를 향해 팔을 쭉 뻗으며 외쳤소. '여기 우리 가운데 몇 사람은(나도 그중 하나요) 이번 항해만 하기로 계약하고 탔단 말이오, 알겠소? 그리고 당신도 잘 알다시피, 나리, 우리는 닻이 내려지는 즉시 우리 해고를 요구할 수 있소. 그러니 우리는 소동을 원치 않소. 그건 우리한테도 이로울 게 없으니까. 우리는 평화롭게 지내고 싶소. 우리는 일할 준비가 돼 있소. 하지만 채찍질만은 당하지 않겠다.'"]], [["“‘Turn to!’ roared the Captain.", "'일이나 해!' 선장이 으르렁거렸소."]], [["“Steelkilt glanced round him a moment, and then said:—‘I tell you what it is now, Captain, rather than kill ye, and be hung for such a shabby rascal, we won’t lift a hand against ye unless ye attack us; but till you say the word about not flogging us, we don’t do a hand’s turn.’", "스틸킬트는 잠시 주위를 둘러보더니, 이윽고 말했소. '이제 사정이 이렇소, 선장. 당신을 죽이고서 그런 보잘것없는 악당 노릇으로 교수형을 당하느니, 우리는 당신이 우리를 공격하지 않는 한 당신한테 손 하나 까딱하지 않겠소. 하지만 당신이 우리를 채찍질하지 않겠다고 그 한마디를 할 때까지는, 우리는 손가락 하나 까딱하지 않겠소.'"]], [["“‘Down into the forecastle then, down with ye, I’ll keep ye there till ye’re sick of it.", "'그럼 앞갑판 밑으로 내려가, 내려가란 말이다. 너희가 진절머리 날 때까지 거기 처박아 두겠다."], ["Down ye go.’", "내려가.'"]], [["“‘Shall we?’ cried the ringleader to his men.", "'그럴까?' 우두머리가 자기 사내들에게 외쳤소."], ["Most of them were against it; but at length, in obedience to Steelkilt, they preceded him down into their dark den, growlingly disappearing, like bears into a cave.", "그들 대부분은 그것에 반대했소. 그러나 마침내 스틸킬트의 말에 따라, 그들은 그를 앞세워 자기네의 어두운 굴속으로 으르렁거리며, 동굴로 들어가는 곰들처럼 사라졌소."]], [["“As the Lakeman’s bare head was just level with the planks, the Captain and his posse leaped the barricade, and rapidly drawing over the slide of the scuttle, planted their group of hands upon it, and loudly called for the steward to bring the heavy brass padlock belonging to the companionway.", "호반인의 맨머리가 막 갑판 널빤지와 같은 높이에 이르렀을 때, 선장과 그의 패거리가 바리케이드를 뛰어넘어, 재빨리 승강구의 미닫이 뚜껑을 끌어 닫고, 그 위에 여럿이 손을 짚어 누른 채, 사환에게 큰 소리로 외쳐 그 승강구에 딸린 묵직한 놋쇠 자물쇠를 가져오게 했소."], ["Then opening the slide a little, the Captain whispered something down the crack, closed it, and turned the key upon them—ten in number—leaving on deck some twenty or more, who thus far had remained neutral.", "그러고는 미닫이를 조금 열어, 선장이 그 틈으로 무언가를 속삭여 내려보내더니, 다시 닫고 그들에게 자물쇠를 채웠소 — 열 사람이었소. 그리고 갑판에는 여태껏 중립을 지킨 스무 명 남짓을 남겨 두었소."]], [["“All night a wide-awake watch was kept by all the officers, forward and aft, especially about the forecastle scuttle and fore hatchway; at which last place it was feared the insurgents might emerge, after breaking through the bulkhead below.", "밤새도록 모든 사관이 배의 앞뒤에서 한순간도 졸지 않고 망을 보았는데, 특히 앞갑판 승강구와 앞쪽 승강구를 살폈소. 반란자들이 아래쪽 격벽을 뚫고서 이 마지막 곳으로 튀어나올까 두려웠던 것이오."], ["But the hours of darkness passed in peace; the men who still remained at their duty toiling hard at the pumps, whose clinking and clanking at intervals through the dreary night dismally resounded through the ship.", "그러나 어둠의 시간은 평온하게 지나갔소. 여전히 제 임무에 남아 있던 자들은 펌프 앞에서 고되게 일했고, 그 절그럭절그럭하는 소리가 음산한 밤 내내 이따금 배 안에 음울하게 울려 퍼졌소."]], [["“At sunrise the Captain went forward, and knocking on the deck, summoned the prisoners to work; but with a yell they refused.", "해 뜰 녘에 선장은 앞쪽으로 가서, 갑판을 두드려 죄수들에게 일하러 나오라고 명했소. 그러나 그들은 고함을 지르며 거부했소."], ["Water was then lowered down to them, and a couple of handfuls of biscuit were tossed after it; when again turning the key upon them and pocketing it, the Captain returned to the quarter-deck.", "그러자 물을 그들에게 내려보내고, 그 뒤로 비스킷 두어 줌을 던져 넣었소. 그러고 나서 다시 그들에게 자물쇠를 채워 열쇠를 주머니에 넣은 채, 선장은 뒷갑판으로 돌아갔소."], ["Twice every day for three days this was repeated; but on the fourth morning a confused wrangling, and then a scuffling was heard, as the customary summons was delivered; and suddenly four men burst up from the forecastle, saying they were ready to turn to.", "사흘 동안 하루 두 차례씩 이 일이 되풀이되었소. 그러나 나흘째 아침, 으레 하던 부름을 전하자, 어수선하게 다투는 소리가, 이어서 몸싸움하는 소리가 들리더니, 별안간 네 사람이 앞갑판에서 튀어 올라와, 일하러 돌아갈 준비가 되었다고 말했소."], ["The fetid closeness of the air, and a famishing diet, united perhaps to some fears of ultimate retribution, had constrained them to surrender at discretion.", "견딜 수 없이 답답한 공기와 굶주리게 하는 식사가, 어쩌면 끝내 닥칠 응보에 대한 어떤 두려움과 한데 어우러져, 그들로 하여금 무조건 항복하도록 몰아붙였던 것이오."], ["Emboldened by this, the Captain reiterated his demand to the rest, but Steelkilt shouted up to him a terrific hint to stop his babbling and betake himself where he belonged.", "이에 기세가 오른 선장은 나머지에게 자기 요구를 거듭했으나, 스틸킬트가 그에게 무시무시한 한마디를 외쳐 올려, 그 잔소리를 그만두고 제가 있을 곳으로 꺼지라고 했소."], ["On the fifth morning three others of the mutineers bolted up into the air from the desperate arms below that sought to restrain them.", "닷새째 아침에는 반란자 가운데 또 다른 셋이, 자기들을 붙잡으려는 아래쪽의 필사적인 팔들을 뿌리치고 위로 튀어 올라왔소."], ["Only three were left.", "이제 셋만 남았소."]], [["“‘Better turn to, now?’ said the Captain with a heartless jeer.", "'이제는 일하러 돌아가는 게 낫지 않나?' 선장이 매정하게 비웃으며 말했소."]], [["“‘Shut us up again, will ye!’ cried Steelkilt.", "'우리를 다시 처박아 둬, 그럴 테냐!' 스틸킬트가 외쳤소."]], [["“‘Oh certainly,’ said the Captain, and the key clicked.", "'오, 그러고말고.' 선장이 말했고, 자물쇠가 철컥 잠겼소."]], [["“It was at this point, gentlemen, that enraged by the defection of seven of his former associates, and stung by the mocking voice that had last hailed him, and maddened by his long entombment in a place as black as the bowels of despair; it was then that Steelkilt proposed to the two Canallers, thus far apparently of one mind with him, to burst out of their hole at the next summoning of the garrison; and armed with their keen mincing knives (long, crescentic, heavy implements with a handle at each end) run amuck from the bowsprit to the taffrail; and if by any devilishness of desperation possible, seize the ship.", "여러분, 바로 이 대목에서, 일곱이나 되는 옛 동료의 변절에 격분하고, 마지막으로 자기를 부르던 그 조롱하는 목소리에 찔리고, 절망의 창자처럼 새까만 곳에 오래 갇혀 있던 탓에 미쳐 버린 스틸킬트는, 여태까지는 겉보기에 자기와 한마음인 듯하던 두 운하 사람에게, 그 수비대가 다음번에 부를 때 자기네 굴에서 뛰쳐나가자고 제안했소. 그리고 그들의 예리한 다지는 칼(길고, 초승달 모양에, 양쪽 끝에 손잡이가 달린 묵직한 연장이오)로 무장하고서 이물의 활대(bowsprit)에서 고물 난간(taffrail)까지 닥치는 대로 날뛰며, 만일 어떤 필사적인 악착스러움으로 가능하기만 하다면 배를 빼앗자고 했소."], ["For himself, he would do this, he said, whether they joined him or not.", "자기로 말하자면, 그들이 함께하든 아니든 이 일을 하겠다고 그는 말했소."], ["That was the last night he should spend in that den.", "그날 밤이 그 굴속에서 보내는 마지막 밤이 될 것이라고 했소."], ["But the scheme met with no opposition on the part of the other two; they swore they were ready for that, or for any other mad thing, for anything in short but a surrender.", "그러나 그 계획에 나머지 두 사람도 반대하지 않았소. 그들은 그 일이든, 다른 어떤 미친 일이든, 요컨대 항복만 빼고는 무엇이든 할 준비가 되어 있다고 맹세했소."], ["And what was more, they each insisted upon being the first man on deck, when the time to make the rush should come.", "게다가, 그들은 저마다 돌격할 때가 오면 자기가 갑판에 첫 번째로 올라가는 사람이 되겠다고 우겼소. 그러나 이에 대해서는 그들의 우두머리가 그 우선권을 자기 몫으로 남겨 두며 못지않게 사납게 반대했소."], ["But to this their leader as fiercely objected, reserving that priority for himself; particularly as his two comrades would not yield, the one to the other, in the matter; and both of them could not be first, for the ladder would but admit one man at a time.", "특히 그의 두 동료가 서로 그 일에서 양보하려 들지 않았기 때문이오. 그리고 사다리는 한 번에 한 사람만 받아들이는지라, 둘 다 첫 번째가 될 수는 없었소."], ["And here, gentlemen, the foul play of these miscreants must come out.", "그리고 여기서, 여러분, 이 악당들의 비열한 배반이 드러나게 되오."]], [["“Upon hearing the frantic project of their leader, each in his own separate soul had suddenly lighted, it would seem, upon the same piece of treachery, namely: to be foremost in breaking out, in order to be the first of the three, though the last of the ten, to surrender; and thereby secure whatever small chance of pardon such conduct might merit.", "자기네 우두머리의 미친 계획을 듣고서, 그들 각자는 저마다 따로따로 제 영혼 속에서, 똑같은 한 가지 배신을 별안간 떠올린 듯했으니, 곧 이것이오. 뛰쳐나가는 데 맨 앞장을 서서, 셋 가운데 첫 번째로, 비록 열 사람 가운데서는 마지막이긴 하나, 항복하는 사람이 되고, 그리하여 그런 처신이 받을 수도 있는 자그마한 사면의 기회나마 확보하자는 것이었소."], ["But when Steelkilt made known his determination still to lead them to the last, they in some way, by some subtle chemistry of villany, mixed their before secret treacheries together; and when their leader fell into a doze, verbally opened their souls to each other in three sentences; and bound the sleeper with cords, and gagged him with cords; and shrieked out for the Captain at midnight.", "그러나 스틸킬트가 끝까지 자기가 그들을 이끌겠다는 결심을 밝히자, 그들은 어떤 식으로든, 악당 짓의 어떤 교묘한 화학 작용으로, 여태 서로에게 감춰 두었던 배신을 한데 뒤섞었소. 그리고 그들의 우두머리가 깜빡 잠이 들자, 단 세 마디로 서로에게 제 영혼을 털어놓고, 잠든 그를 밧줄로 묶고, 밧줄로 입에 재갈을 물리고는, 한밤중에 선장을 향해 비명을 질러 댔소."]], [["“Thinking murder at hand, and smelling in the dark for the blood, he and all his armed mates and harpooneers rushed for the forecastle.", "살인이 임박했다고 여기고, 어둠 속에서 피 냄새를 맡으며, 선장은 무장한 항해사들과 작살잡이들을 모두 거느리고 앞갑판으로 달려갔소. 몇 분 만에 승강구가 열렸고, 손발이 묶인 채 여전히 몸부림치는 우두머리가, 신의를 저버린 자기 동료들의 손에 떠밀려 위로 올라왔소."], ["In a few minutes the scuttle was opened, and, bound hand and foot, the still struggling ringleader was shoved up into the air by his perfidious allies, who at once claimed the honor of securing a man who had been fully ripe for murder.", "그들은 곧바로, 살인을 저지를 만큼 충분히 무르익었던 사내 하나를 붙잡은 공로를 내세웠소. 그러나 이들 모두 멱살을 잡혀, 죽은 가축처럼 갑판을 따라 끌려갔소."], ["But all these were collared, and dragged along the deck like dead cattle; and, side by side, were seized up into the mizzen rigging, like three quarters of meat, and there they hung till morning.", "그리고 고기 네 덩이의 사분의 삼처럼, 나란히 뒤쪽 돛대의 삭구에 끌어 올려져, 거기서 아침까지 매달려 있었소. '이런 망할 놈들.'"], ["‘Damn ye,’ cried the Captain, pacing to and fro before them, ‘the vultures would not touch ye, ye villains!’", "선장이 그들 앞에서 오락가락 거닐며 외쳤소. '독수리도 너희한텐 손대지 않을 게다, 이 악당 놈들아!'"]], [["“At sunrise he summoned all hands; and separating those who had rebelled from those who had taken no part in the mutiny, he told the former that he had a good mind to flog them all round—thought, upon the whole, he would do so—he ought to—justice demanded it; but for the present, considering their timely surrender, he would let them go with a reprimand, which he accordingly administered in the vernacular.", "해 뜰 녘에 그는 전 선원을 불러 모았소. 그리고 반란을 일으킨 자들을 그 반란에 가담하지 않은 자들로부터 갈라놓고서, 전자에게 이르기를, 자기는 너희를 모조리 돌아가며 채찍질하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같다고, 곰곰이 생각해 보니 그렇게 하겠다고, 마땅히 그래야 한다고, 정의가 그것을 요구한다고 했소. 그러나 지금으로서는 그들이 때맞추어 항복한 것을 헤아려, 견책 한마디로 보내 주겠다고 했고, 그리하여 그 견책을 토속어로 퍼부어 주었소."]], [["“‘But as for you, ye carrion rogues,’ turning to the three men in the rigging—‘for you, I mean to mince ye up for the try-pots;’ and, seizing a rope, he applied it with all his might to the backs of the two traitors, till they yelled no more, but lifelessly hung their heads sideways, as the two crucified thieves are drawn.", "'하지만 너희로 말하자면, 이 썩은 고기 같은 악당들아.' 그가 삭구에 매달린 세 사람을 돌아보며 말했소. '너희는, 정유 솥(try-pot)에 넣게 잘게 다져 줄 작정이다.' 그러고는 밧줄 하나를 움켜쥐고, 두 배신자의 등에 온 힘을 다해 후려쳤소. 마침내 그들이 더는 비명도 지르지 못하고, 십자가에 못 박힌 두 도둑을 그린 그림처럼, 숨이 끊어진 듯 고개를 옆으로 떨굴 때까지 말이오."]], [["“‘My wrist is sprained with ye!’ he cried, at last; ‘but there is still rope enough left for you, my fine bantam, that wouldn’t give up.", "'네놈들 때문에 내 손목을 삐었구나!' 그가 마침내 외쳤소."], ["Take that gag from his mouth, and let us hear what he can say for himself.’", "'하지만 끝까지 굽히지 않겠다던 내 멋진 싸움닭, 네놈한테 쓸 밧줄은 아직 충분히 남아 있다. 저놈 입에서 그 재갈을 빼내, 제놈이 무슨 변명을 할 수 있는지 들어 보자.'"]], [["“For a moment the exhausted mutineer made a tremulous motion of his cramped jaws, and then painfully twisting round his head, said in a sort of hiss, ‘What I say is this—and mind it well—if you flog me, I murder you!’", "잠시 그 지쳐 버린 반란자는 경련하는 턱을 떨듯이 움직이더니, 이윽고 고통스레 고개를 비틀어 돌리고는, 일종의 쉭쉭거리는 소리로 말했소. '내 할 말은 이것이오 — 그리고 잘 새겨들으시오 — 만일 당신이 나를 채찍질하면, 나는 당신을 죽이겠소!'"]], [["“‘Say ye so?", "'네놈이 그렇게 말한다?"], ["then see how ye frighten me’—and the Captain drew off with the rope to strike.", "그럼 내가 얼마나 무서워하는지 어디 봐라.' 그러면서 선장은 후려치려고 밧줄을 뒤로 빼들었소."]], [["“‘Best not,’ hissed the Lakeman.", "'그러지 않는 게 좋을 거요.' 호반인이 쉭쉭거리며 말했소."]], [["“‘But I must,’—and the rope was once more drawn back for the stroke.", "'하지만 나는 해야겠다.' 그러면서 밧줄은 다시 한번 후려치려고 뒤로 당겨졌소."]], [["“Steelkilt here hissed out something, inaudible to all but the Captain; who, to the amazement of all hands, started back, paced the deck rapidly two or three times, and then suddenly throwing down his rope, said, ‘I won’t do it—let him go—cut him down: d’ye hear?’", "스틸킬트는 여기서 무언가를 쉭쉭거리며 내뱉었는데, 선장 말고는 누구에게도 들리지 않았소. 그러자 선장은, 전 선원이 깜짝 놀라게도, 흠칫 물러서더니, 갑판을 두세 번 빠르게 거닐고는, 별안간 밧줄을 내던지며 말했소. '안 하겠다 — 저놈을 놓아줘라 — 끌어 내려라. 알아들었나?'"]], [["“But as the junior mates were hurrying to execute the order, a pale man, with a bandaged head, arrested them—Radney the chief mate.", "그러나 하급 항해사들이 그 명령을 서둘러 실행하려 할 때, 머리에 붕대를 감은 창백한 사내 하나가 그들을 가로막았으니 — 일등항해사 래드니였소."], ["Ever since the blow, he had lain in his berth; but that morning, hearing the tumult on the deck, he had crept out, and thus far had watched the whole scene.", "그 일격을 당한 이래 그는 줄곧 제 침상에 누워 있었으나, 그날 아침 갑판의 소동을 듣고서 기어 나와, 여태껏 그 전모를 지켜본 터였소."], ["Such was the state of his mouth, that he could hardly speak; but mumbling something about his being willing and able to do what the captain dared not attempt, he snatched the rope and advanced to his pinioned foe.", "그의 입은 그 지경이어서 거의 말도 할 수 없었소. 그러나 선장이 감히 시도하지 못한 일을 자기는 기꺼이, 또 능히 할 수 있다는 식의 무언가를 웅얼거리며, 그는 밧줄을 낚아채어 묶여 있는 제 원수를 향해 나아갔소."]], [["“‘You are a coward!’ hissed the Lakeman.", "'당신은 겁쟁이야!' 호반인이 쉭쉭거렸소."]], [["“‘So I am, but take that.’ The mate was in the very act of striking, when another hiss stayed his uplifted arm.", "'그래, 나는 겁쟁이다. 하지만 이거나 받아라.'"], ["He paused: and then pausing no more, made good his word, spite of Steelkilt’s threat, whatever that might have been.", "일등항해사가 막 후려치려는 바로 그 순간, 또 한 번의 쉭쉭거리는 소리가 그의 치켜든 팔을 멈춰 세웠소. 그는 멈칫했소."], ["The three men were then cut down, all hands were turned to, and, sullenly worked by the moody seamen, the iron pumps clanged as before.", "그러더니 더는 멈칫하지 않고, 스틸킬트의 위협이 무엇이었든 그것을 무릅쓰고, 제 말을 실행에 옮겼소. 그러고 나서 세 사람은 끌어 내려졌고, 전 선원이 일하러 돌아갔으며, 침울한 선원들이 시무룩하게 다루는 가운데, 그 무쇠 펌프가 전처럼 절그럭거렸소."]], [["“Just after dark that day, when one watch had retired below, a clamor was heard in the forecastle; and the two trembling traitors running up, besieged the cabin door, saying they durst not consort with the crew.", "그날 어둠이 막 내린 뒤, 한 당직조가 아래로 물러나 있을 때, 앞갑판에서 소란이 일었소. 그러자 부들부들 떠는 두 배신자가 뛰어 올라와, 차마 선원들과 어울릴 수 없다며 선실 문을 에워쌌소."], ["Entreaties, cuffs, and kicks could not drive them back, so at their own instance they were put down in the ship’s run for salvation.", "애원도, 손찌검도, 발길질도 그들을 돌려보내지 못해, 결국 그들 자신의 청에 따라 그들은 목숨을 부지하라고 배의 후미진 선저(船底)로 내려보내졌소."], ["Still, no sign of mutiny reappeared among the rest.", "그래도 나머지 사이에서는 반란의 기미가 다시 나타나지 않았소."], ["On the contrary, it seemed, that mainly at Steelkilt’s instigation, they had resolved to maintain the strictest peacefulness, obey all orders to the last, and, when the ship reached port, desert her in a body.", "오히려, 주로 스틸킬트의 부추김으로, 그들은 더없이 철저한 평온을 지키고, 끝까지 모든 명령에 복종하며, 배가 항구에 닿으면 다 함께 배를 버리고 달아나기로 결의한 듯했소."], ["But in order to insure the speediest end to the voyage, they all agreed to another thing—namely, not to sing out for whales, in case any should be discovered.", "그러나 항해가 가장 빨리 끝나도록 보장하기 위해, 그들은 또 한 가지에 모두 합의했으니 — 곧, 혹여 고래가 발견되더라도 고래라고 외쳐 알리지 않는다는 것이었소."], ["For, spite of her leak, and spite of all her other perils, the Town-Ho still maintained her mast-heads, and her captain was just as willing to lower for a fish that moment, as on the day his craft first struck the cruising ground; and Radney the mate was quite as ready to change his berth for a boat, and with his bandaged mouth seek to gag in death the vital jaw of the whale.", "왜냐하면, 누수에도 불구하고, 또 다른 온갖 위험에도 불구하고, 타운호는 여전히 돛대 꼭대기에 망꾼을 두고 있었고, 그 선장은 자기 배가 어장에 처음 들어선 날 못지않게 그 순간 당장이라도 고래를 잡으러 보트를 내릴 마음이 있었기 때문이오. 그리고 일등항해사 래드니 또한, 자기 침상을 보트로 기꺼이 바꾸어, 붕대 감은 입으로, 고래의 그 목숨 같은 턱을 죽음 속에 틀어막으려 들 만반의 준비가 되어 있었소."]], [["“But though the Lakeman had induced the seamen to adopt this sort of passiveness in their conduct, he kept his own counsel (at least till all was over) concerning his own proper and private revenge upon the man who had stung him in the ventricles of his heart.", "그러나 호반인은 선원들이 처신에 있어 이런 식의 수동성을 받아들이도록 이끌어 놓긴 했으나, 자기 심장의 심실(心室)을 찌른 그 사내에 대한 자신의 정당하고도 사사로운 복수에 관해서는 (적어도 모든 일이 끝날 때까지는) 제 속내를 혼자 간직했소."], ["He was in Radney the chief mate’s watch; and as if the infatuated man sought to run more than half way to meet his doom, after the scene at the rigging, he insisted, against the express counsel of the captain, upon resuming the head of his watch at night.", "그는 일등항해사 래드니의 당직조에 속해 있었소. 그리고 그 분별을 잃은 사내는 마치 제 운명을 마중하러 절반도 넘게 달려 나가려는 듯이, 삭구에서의 그 소동이 있은 뒤, 선장의 분명한 충고에도 아랑곳없이, 밤에 다시 제 당직조의 우두머리 자리에 서겠다고 우겼소."], ["Upon this, and one or two other circumstances, Steelkilt systematically built the plan of his revenge.", "바로 이 점과, 그 밖의 한두 가지 정황 위에, 스틸킬트는 자기 복수의 계획을 차근차근 쌓아 올렸소."]], [["“During the night, Radney had an unseamanlike way of sitting on the bulwarks of the quarter-deck, and leaning his arm upon the gunwale of the boat which was hoisted up there, a little above the ship’s side.", "밤이면 래드니는 뒷갑판의 뱃전에 앉아, 거기 배의 옆구리에서 조금 위쪽으로 끌어 올려져 있던 보트의 뱃전에 팔을 괴는, 선원답지 못한 버릇이 있었소."], ["In this attitude, it was well known, he sometimes dozed.", "이런 자세로 그가 더러 깜빡 졸곤 한다는 것은 잘 알려진 사실이었소."], ["There was a considerable vacancy between the boat and the ship, and down between this was the sea.", "보트와 배 사이에는 제법 빈틈이 있었고, 그 아래로는 바다였소."], ["Steelkilt calculated his time, and found that his next trick at the helm would come round at two o’clock, in the morning of the third day from that in which he had been betrayed.", "스틸킬트는 자기 시간을 가늠해 보고, 그가 배신당한 그날로부터 사흘째 되는 날 새벽 두 시에 자기가 다음번 키를 잡을 차례가 돌아온다는 것을 알아냈소."], ["At his leisure, he employed the interval in braiding something very carefully in his watches below.", "그는 한가한 틈에, 아래 선실에서 쉬는 동안 무언가를 아주 정성스레 땋는 데 그 짬을 썼소."]], [["“‘What are you making there?’ said a shipmate.", "'거기서 뭘 만들고 있나?' 한 동료 선원이 말했소."]], [["“‘What do you think?", "'뭘 만드는 것 같나?"], ["what does it look like?’", "뭐처럼 보이나?'"]], [["“‘Like a lanyard for your bag; but it’s an odd one, seems to me.’", "'자네 자루에 매다는 끈 같긴 한데, 내가 보기엔 이상한 끈이로군.'"]], [["“‘Yes, rather oddish,’ said the Lakeman, holding it at arm’s length before him; ‘but I think it will answer.", "'그래, 좀 이상한 끈이지.' 호반인이 그것을 팔 길이만큼 앞으로 들어 보이며 말했소."], ["Shipmate, I haven’t enough twine,—have you any?’", "'하지만 쓸모는 있을 것 같네. 이보게, 내가 노끈이 모자라는데 — 자네한테 좀 있나?'"]], [["“But there was none in the forecastle.", "그러나 앞갑판에는 노끈이 없었소."]], [["“‘Then I must get some from old Rad;’ and he rose to go aft.", "'그럼 늙은 래드한테서 좀 얻어 와야겠군.' 그러면서 그는 고물 쪽으로 가려고 일어섰소."]], [["“‘You don’t mean to go a begging to him!’ said a sailor.", "'설마 그자한테 빌어먹으러 가려는 건 아니겠지!' 한 선원이 말했소."]], [["“‘Why not?", "'왜 아니겠나?"], ["Do you think he won’t do me a turn, when it’s to help himself in the end, shipmate?’ and going to the mate, he looked at him quietly, and asked him for some twine to mend his hammock.", "그게 결국엔 제놈을 돕는 일인데, 그자가 나한테 호의 하나 베풀지 않을 것 같나, 이 사람아?' 그러면서 일등항해사에게 가서, 그는 그를 조용히 바라보며, 해먹을 손보게 노끈 좀 달라고 청했소."], ["It was given him—neither twine nor lanyard were seen again; but the next night an iron ball, closely netted, partly rolled from the pocket of the Lakeman’s monkey jacket, as he was tucking the coat into his hammock for a pillow.", "노끈은 주어졌소 — 그 노끈도 끈도 다시는 눈에 띄지 않았소. 그러나 다음 날 밤, 호반인이 외투를 베개 삼아 해먹 속에 쑤셔 넣을 때, 그의 두꺼운 작업복 주머니에서 촘촘히 그물처럼 엮인 무쇠 공 하나가 반쯤 굴러 나왔소."], ["Twenty-four hours after, his trick at the silent helm—nigh to the man who was apt to doze over the grave always ready dug to the seaman’s hand—that fatal hour was then to come; and in the fore-ordaining soul of Steelkilt, the mate was already stark and stretched as a corpse, with his forehead crushed in.", "스물네 시간 뒤, 그가 그 고요한 키를 잡을 차례가 — 늘 선원의 손에 미리 파인 무덤 위로 깜빡 졸곤 하던 그 사내 가까이서 — 그 운명의 시각이 이제 닥쳐오게 되어 있었소. 그리고 미리 운명을 정해 두는 스틸킬트의 영혼 속에서는, 일등항해사가 이미 이마가 으스러진 채 뻣뻣하게 시체처럼 뻗어 있었소."]], [["“But, gentlemen, a fool saved the would-be murderer from the bloody deed he had planned.", "그러나, 여러분, 한 바보가 그 살인자가 될 뻔한 자를 그가 꾸민 그 피의 행위로부터 구해 주었소."], ["Yet complete revenge he had, and without being the avenger.", "그래도 그는 완전한 복수를 거두었으니, 더욱이 자기가 복수의 손이 되지 않고서 그러했소."], ["For by a mysterious fatality, Heaven itself seemed to step in to take out of his hands into its own the damning thing he would have done.", "어떤 신비로운 운명에 의해, 하늘 그 자체가 나서서, 그가 저지르려던 그 저주받을 짓을 그의 손에서 거두어 제 손으로 가져가는 듯했기 때문이오."]], [["“It was just between daybreak and sunrise of the morning of the second day, when they were washing down the decks, that a stupid Teneriffe man, drawing water in the main-chains, all at once shouted out, ‘There she rolls!", "바로 그 이튿날 아침, 동틀 녘과 해 뜰 녘 사이, 그들이 갑판을 씻어 내리고 있을 때였소. 큰 돛대 사슬 쪽에서 물을 긷던 어느 멍청한 테네리페(Teneriffe) 사내가, 별안간 소리쳤소."], ["there she rolls!’ Jesu, what a whale!", "'저기 구르고 있다! 저기 구르고 있어!'"], ["It was Moby Dick.", "맙소사, 그 무슨 고래였던지! 그것은 모비 딕이었소."]], [["“‘Moby Dick!’ cried Don Sebastian; ‘St.", "'모비 딕이라니!' 돈 세바스티안이 외쳤다."], ["Dominic!", "'성 도미니쿠스여!"], ["Sir sailor, but do whales have christenings?", "뱃사람 양반, 그래 고래도 세례명을 받는단 말이오?"], ["Whom call you Moby Dick?’", "누구를 모비 딕이라 부르는 거요?'"]], [["“‘A very white, and famous, and most deadly immortal monster, Don;—but that would be too long a story.’", "'아주 새하얗고, 이름나고, 더없이 치명적이며 죽지 않는 괴물이라오, 돈. 하지만 그 이야기는 너무 길어질 게요.'"]], [["“‘How?", "'어째서요?"], ["how?’ cried all the young Spaniards, crowding.", "어떻게 그렇소?' 젊은 스페인 사람들이 모두 다가서며 외쳤다."]], [["“‘Nay, Dons, Dons—nay, nay!", "'아니, 돈들, 돈들 — 아니, 아니오!"], ["I cannot rehearse that now.", "지금은 그 이야기를 풀어놓을 수가 없소."], ["Let me get more into the air, Sirs.’", "좀 더 바람이나 쐬게 해 주시오, 여러분.'"]], [["“‘The chicha! the chicha!’ cried Don Pedro; ‘our vigorous friend looks faint;—fill up his empty glass!’", "'치차! 치차를!' 돈 페드로가 외쳤다. '우리 기운찬 친구가 어지러워 보이는구려. 빈 잔을 채워 주시오!'"]], [["“No need, gentlemen; one moment, and I proceed.—Now, gentlemen, so suddenly perceiving the snowy whale within fifty yards of the ship—forgetful of the compact among the crew—in the excitement of the moment, the Teneriffe man had instinctively and involuntarily lifted his voice for the monster, though for some little time past it had been plainly beheld from the three sullen mast-heads.", "그럴 것 없소, 여러분."], ["All was now a phrensy.", "잠깐이면 되오, 계속하리다."], ["‘The White Whale—the White Whale!’ was the cry from captain, mates, and harpooneers, who, undeterred by fearful rumours, were all anxious to capture so famous and precious a fish; while the dogged crew eyed askance, and with curses, the appalling beauty of the vast milky mass, that lit up by a horizontal spangling sun, shifted and glistened like a living opal in the blue morning sea.", "자, 여러분, 그 눈처럼 흰 고래가 배에서 쉰 야드도 안 되는 데 있는 것을 그토록 별안간 알아채고서 — 선원들 사이의 그 약속도 잊은 채 — 그 순간의 흥분에 휩싸여, 테네리페 사내는 본능적으로, 또 저도 모르게 그 괴물을 향해 목청을 높였소. 비록 그 괴물은 얼마 전부터 이미 그 침울한 세 돛대 꼭대기에서 똑똑히 보이고 있었지만 말이오."], ["Gentlemen, a strange fatality pervades the whole career of these events, as if verily mapped out before the world itself was charted.", "이제 온통 광란이었소. '흰 고래다 — 흰 고래!'"], ["The mutineer was the bowsman of the mate, and when fast to a fish, it was his duty to sit next him, while Radney stood up with his lance in the prow, and haul in or slacken the line, at the word of command.", "선장과 항해사들과 작살잡이들이 외쳐 댔으니, 그들은 그 무시무시한 소문에도 굴하지 않고, 그토록 이름나고 값진 고래를 잡고 싶어 모두 안달이 나 있었소. 한편 고집스러운 선원들은, 수평으로 반짝이는 햇살을 받아 환히 빛나며 푸른 아침 바다 위에서 살아 있는 오팔처럼 일렁이며 반짝이는, 그 거대한 젖빛 덩어리의 소름 끼치는 아름다움을, 곁눈질로, 또 저주를 퍼부으며 바라보았소."], ["Moreover, when the four boats were lowered, the mate’s got the start; and none howled more fiercely with delight than did Steelkilt, as he strained at his oar.", "여러분, 이 일련의 사건들이 펼쳐지는 그 모든 과정에는, 마치 세상 그 자체가 지도로 그려지기도 전에 진실로 미리 짜여 있기라도 한 듯, 기묘한 운명이 깃들어 있소."], ["After a stiff pull, their harpooneer got fast, and, spear in hand, Radney sprang to the bow.", "그 반란자는 일등항해사의 노잡이였고, 고래에 작살이 박혀 줄이 당겨질 때면, 래드니가 이물에 작살을 들고 일어서는 동안 그 곁에 앉아, 명령 한마디에 줄을 끌어들이거나 늦추는 것이 그의 임무였소."], ["He was always a furious man, it seems, in a boat.", "게다가, 네 척의 보트가 내려졌을 때, 일등항해사의 보트가 앞서 나갔소."], ["And now his bandaged cry was, to beach him on the whale’s topmost back.", "그리고 스틸킬트가 제 노를 힘껏 당기며 기뻐 날뛰는 그 으르렁대는 소리보다 더 사납게 울부짖는 자는 아무도 없었소."], ["Nothing loath, his bowsman hauled him up and up, through a blinding foam that blent two whitenesses together; till of a sudden the boat struck as against a sunken ledge, and keeling over, spilled out the standing mate.", "한바탕 힘껏 노를 저은 끝에, 그들의 작살잡이가 작살을 박았고, 창을 손에 든 래드니가 이물로 뛰어올랐소. 보트에서는 그가 늘 사나운 사내였던 모양이오."], ["That instant, as he fell on the whale’s slippery back, the boat righted, and was dashed aside by the swell, while Radney was tossed over into the sea, on the other flank of the whale.", "그리고 이제 그의 붕대 감은 입에서 나온 외침은, 자기를 고래의 가장 높은 등 위에 올려 달라는 것이었소. 그의 노잡이는 거리낌 없이, 두 가지 흰빛을 한데 뒤섞는 눈먼 듯한 거품을 헤치고, 그를 위로 또 위로 끌어 올렸소."], ["He struck out through the spray, and, for an instant, was dimly seen through that veil, wildly seeking to remove himself from the eye of Moby Dick.", "그러다 별안간 보트가 물밑 암초에 부딪치듯 부딪치며, 옆으로 기울어, 서 있던 일등항해사를 쏟아 냈소. 그 순간, 그가 고래의 미끄러운 등 위로 떨어지자, 보트는 바로 서며 너울에 밀려 옆으로 내동댕이쳐졌고, 래드니는 고래의 반대편 옆구리로 바다에 내던져졌소."], ["But the whale rushed round in a sudden maelstrom; seized the swimmer between his jaws; and rearing high up with him, plunged headlong again, and went down.", "그는 물보라를 헤치고 헤엄쳐 나와, 한순간, 그 장막 너머로 어렴풋이 보였으니, 모비 딕의 눈에서 벗어나려고 미친 듯이 발버둥치고 있었소. 그러나 그 고래는 별안간 소용돌이를 일으키며 빙 돌더니, 그 헤엄치는 자를 두 턱 사이에 움켜잡았고, 그를 물고 높이 솟구쳤다가, 다시 거꾸로 곤두박질치며 물속으로 내려갔소."]], [["“Meantime, at the first tap of the boat’s bottom, the Lakeman had slackened the line, so as to drop astern from the whirlpool; calmly looking on, he thought his own thoughts.", "그동안, 보트 바닥이 처음 부딪친 그 순간, 호반인은 줄을 늦추어, 그 소용돌이에서 고물 쪽으로 빠져나오게 했소. 그는 차분히 지켜보며, 제 생각에 잠겨 있었소."], ["But a sudden, terrific, downward jerking of the boat, quickly brought his knife to the line.", "그러나 별안간 보트가 무섭게 아래로 홱 잡아당겨지자, 그는 재빨리 칼을 줄에 갖다 댔소."], ["He cut it; and the whale was free.", "그가 줄을 끊었고, 고래는 풀려났소."], ["But, at some distance, Moby Dick rose again, with some tatters of Radney’s red woollen shirt, caught in the teeth that had destroyed him.", "그러나 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 모비 딕이 다시 떠올랐는데, 그를 파멸시킨 그 이빨에는 래드니의 붉은 모직 셔츠의 너덜너덜한 조각이 걸려 있었소."], ["All four boats gave chase again; but the whale eluded them, and finally wholly disappeared.", "네 척의 보트가 다시 추격에 나섰소. 그러나 고래는 그들을 따돌렸고, 마침내 완전히 사라져 버렸소."]], [["“In good time, the Town-Ho reached her port—a savage, solitary place—where no civilized creature resided.", "이윽고, 타운호는 그 항구에 닿았소 — 야만적이고 외딴 곳으로, 문명화된 피조물이라곤 살지 않는 데였소."], ["There, headed by the Lakeman, all but five or six of the foremastmen deliberately deserted among the palms; eventually, as it turned out, seizing a large double war-canoe of the savages, and setting sail for some other harbor.", "거기서, 호반인을 우두머리로 하여, 다섯이나 여섯을 뺀 앞돛대 선원 전부가 야자수 사이로 작정하고 달아났소. 끝내 드러난 바로는, 그들은 미개인들의 큼직한 쌍둥이 전투용 카누 한 척을 빼앗아, 어딘가 다른 항구를 향해 돛을 올렸다 하오."]], [["“The ship’s company being reduced to but a handful, the captain called upon the Islanders to assist him in the laborious business of heaving down the ship to stop the leak.", "배의 인원이 한 줌으로 줄어들자, 선장은 섬사람들을 불러, 누수를 막으려 배를 기울여 끌어 내리는 그 고된 일을 거들어 달라고 했소."], ["But to such unresting vigilance over their dangerous allies was this small band of whites necessitated, both by night and by day, and so extreme was the hard work they underwent, that upon the vessel being ready again for sea, they were in such a weakened condition that the captain durst not put off with them in so heavy a vessel.", "그러나 이 한 줌의 백인들은, 위험한 자기네 협력자들을 밤이고 낮이고 한순간도 쉬지 않고 경계해야만 했고, 또 그들이 치른 고역이 어찌나 혹독했던지, 배가 다시 바다로 나갈 채비가 갖추어졌을 무렵, 그들은 너무도 쇠약해진 형편이라, 선장은 그토록 묵직한 배에 그들을 태우고 떠날 엄두를 내지 못했소. 사관들과 의논한 끝에, 그는 배를 가능한 한 해안에서 멀리 떼어 닻을 내리고, 이물의 대포 두 문에 장전하여 밀어 내고, 고물 갑판에 머스킷 총을 쌓아 두었소."], ["After taking counsel with his officers, he anchored the ship as far off shore as possible; loaded and ran out his two cannon from the bows; stacked his muskets on the poop; and warning the Islanders not to approach the ship at their peril, took one man with him, and setting the sail of his best whale-boat, steered straight before the wind for Tahiti, five hundred miles distant, to procure a reinforcement to his crew.", "그러고는 섬사람들에게 목숨이 아깝거든 배에 가까이 오지 말라고 경고한 뒤, 사내 하나를 데리고, 자기의 가장 좋은 포경 보트의 돛을 올려, 바람을 받으며 곧장 오백 마일 떨어진 타히티(Tahiti)를 향해 키를 잡았소. 자기 선원을 보충하기 위해서였소."]], [["“On the fourth day of the sail, a large canoe was descried, which seemed to have touched at a low isle of corals.", "항해 나흘째 되는 날, 큰 카누 한 척이 눈에 띄었는데, 그것은 산호로 이루어진 낮은 섬에 잠시 들렀던 듯했소. 그는 그것을 피해 키를 돌렸소."], ["He steered away from it; but the savage craft bore down on him; and soon the voice of Steelkilt hailed him to heave to, or he would run him under water.", "그러나 그 미개인의 배가 그를 향해 다가왔소. 곧 스틸킬트의 목소리가 그에게 외쳐, 배를 멈추라고, 그러지 않으면 그를 물속에 처박아 버리겠다고 했소."], ["The captain presented a pistol.", "선장은 권총을 들이댔소."], ["With one foot on each prow of the yoked war-canoes, the Lakeman laughed him to scorn; assuring him that if the pistol so much as clicked in the lock, he would bury him in bubbles and foam.", "멍에처럼 한데 묶인 전투용 카누의 두 이물에 한 발씩 디딘 채, 호반인은 그를 비웃으며, 만일 그 권총의 공이가 조금이라도 철컥하기만 하면, 그를 거품과 물보라 속에 파묻어 버리겠노라고 장담했소."]], [["“‘What do you want of me?’ cried the captain.", "'나한테 무얼 원하는 거냐?' 선장이 외쳤소."]], [["“‘Where are you bound?", "'당신은 어디로 가는 거요? 또 무엇 때문에 가는 거요?'"], ["and for what are you bound?’ demanded Steelkilt; ‘no lies.’", "스틸킬트가 따져 물었소. '거짓말은 마시오.'"]], [["“‘I am bound to Tahiti for more men.’", "'나는 사람을 더 구하러 타히티로 가는 길이다.'"]], [["“‘Very good.", "'좋소. 잠깐 당신 배에 올라가게 해 주시오 — 나는 평화롭게 가는 거요.'"], ["Let me board you a moment—I come in peace.’ With that he leaped from the canoe, swam to the boat; and climbing the gunwale, stood face to face with the captain.", "그러면서 그는 카누에서 뛰어내려, 헤엄쳐 보트로 다가갔소. 그리고 뱃전을 기어올라, 선장과 얼굴을 마주하고 섰소."]], [["“‘Cross your arms, sir; throw back your head.", "'두 팔을 가슴에 포개시오, 나리. 고개를 뒤로 젖히고."], ["Now, repeat after me.", "자, 내 말을 따라 하시오."], ["As soon as Steelkilt leaves me, I swear to beach this boat on yonder island, and remain there six days.", "스틸킬트가 나를 떠나는 즉시, 나는 이 보트를 저기 저 섬에 대고 거기서 엿새 동안 머물 것을 맹세한다."], ["If I do not, may lightnings strike me!’", "만일 그러지 않거든, 벼락이 나를 내리쳐도 좋다!'"]], [["“‘A pretty scholar,’ laughed the Lakeman.", "'제법 학자 양반이로군.' 호반인이 웃었소."], ["‘Adios, Senor!’ and leaping into the sea, he swam back to his comrades.", "'잘 가시오, 세뇨르!' 그러면서 그는 바다로 뛰어들어, 헤엄쳐 자기 동료들에게로 돌아갔소."]], [["“Watching the boat till it was fairly beached, and drawn up to the roots of the cocoa-nut trees, Steelkilt made sail again, and in due time arrived at Tahiti, his own place of destination.", "그 보트가 마침내 해변에 닿아, 야자나무 뿌리께까지 끌어 올려지는 것을 지켜본 뒤, 스틸킬트는 다시 돛을 올렸고, 때맞추어 자기 목적지인 타히티에 닿았소. 거기서 운이 그를 도왔소."], ["There, luck befriended him; two ships were about to sail for France, and were providentially in want of precisely that number of men which the sailor headed.", "두 척의 배가 막 프랑스를 향해 떠나려던 참이었는데, 마치 하늘의 섭리인 양 그 뱃사람이 거느린 바로 그만큼의 인원을 마침 필요로 하고 있었소. 그들은 배에 올랐소."], ["They embarked; and so for ever got the start of their former captain, had he been at all minded to work them legal retribution.", "그리하여, 설령 그들의 옛 선장이 그들에게 법대로 응보를 가할 마음을 조금이라도 품었더라 한들, 그들은 영영 그보다 한발 앞서 떠나 버린 것이오."]], [["“Some ten days after the French ships sailed, the whale-boat arrived, and the captain was forced to enlist some of the more civilized Tahitians, who had been somewhat used to the sea.", "프랑스 배들이 떠난 지 열흘쯤 뒤에, 그 포경 보트가 도착했고, 선장은 어쩔 수 없이 바다에 어느 정도 익숙해진, 좀 더 문명화된 타히티 사람 몇을 새로 고용했소."], ["Chartering a small native schooner, he returned with them to his vessel; and finding all right there, again resumed his cruisings.", "작은 토착 스쿠너 한 척을 빌려, 그는 그들을 데리고 제 배로 돌아갔소. 그리고 거기 모든 것이 무사함을 보고서, 다시 항해를 이어 갔소."]], [["“Where Steelkilt now is, gentlemen, none know; but upon the island of Nantucket, the widow of Radney still turns to the sea which refuses to give up its dead; still in dreams sees the awful white whale that destroyed him.", "여러분, 스틸킬트가 지금 어디에 있는지는 아무도 모르오. 그러나 낸터킷 섬에서는, 래드니의 미망인이 지금도 제 죽은 자를 내어 주기를 거부하는 그 바다를 향해 발길을 돌리고 있소."], ["* * * *", "그리고 꿈속에서 지금도, 제 남편을 파멸시킨 그 무서운 흰 고래를 보고 있다오."]], [["“‘Are you through?’ said Don Sebastian, quietly.", "'다 끝났소?' 돈 세바스티안이 조용히 말했다."]], [["“‘I am, Don.’", "'다 끝났소, 돈.'"]], [["“‘Then I entreat you, tell me if to the best of your own convictions, this your story is in substance really true?", "'그럼 부탁이니, 말해 주시오. 당신 자신의 확신에 비추어, 이 당신 이야기가 그 본질에 있어 정말로 참된 것이오?"], ["It is so passing wonderful!", "그토록 지나치게 놀라운 이야기인지라!"], ["Did you get it from an unquestionable source?", "의심할 여지없는 출처에서 들은 것이오?"], ["Bear with me if I seem to press.’", "내가 다그치는 듯 보이거든 너그러이 받아 주시오.'"]], [["“‘Also bear with all of us, sir sailor; for we all join in Don Sebastian’s suit,’ cried the company, with exceeding interest.", "'우리 모두에게도 너그러우시오, 뱃사람 양반. 우리 모두가 돈 세바스티안의 청에 한마음으로 동참하니 말이오.' 일행이 더없이 큰 관심을 담아 외쳤다."]], [["“‘Is there a copy of the Holy Evangelists in the Golden Inn, gentlemen?’", "'골든 인에 거룩한 복음서(Holy Evangelists) 한 권이라도 있겠소, 여러분?'"]], [["“‘Nay,’ said Don Sebastian; ‘but I know a worthy priest near by, who will quickly procure one for me.", "'아니오.' 돈 세바스티안이 말했다."], ["I go for it; but are you well advised?", "'하지만 근처에 훌륭한 사제 한 분을 아는데, 그분이 얼른 한 권 구해다 주실 게요. 내가 가져오리다."], ["this may grow too serious.’", "그런데 당신, 정녕 잘 생각하신 게요? 이게 너무 심각해질 수도 있소.'"]], [["“‘Will you be so good as to bring the priest also, Don?’", "'사제님도 함께 모셔다주시면 고맙겠소, 돈?'"]], [["“‘Though there are no Auto-da-Fés in Lima now,’ said one of the company to another; ‘I fear our sailor friend runs risk of the archiepiscopacy.", "'지금이야 리마에 화형식(Auto-da-Fé) 같은 건 없소만.' 일행 가운데 하나가 다른 이에게 말했다."], ["Let us withdraw more out of the moonlight.", "'나는 우리 뱃사람 친구가 대주교청의 미움을 살 위험이 있을까 걱정되는구려. 달빛에서 좀 더 물러나 있읍시다."], ["I see no need of this.’", "이럴 필요까지는 없을 듯한데.'"]], [["“‘Excuse me for running after you, Don Sebastian; but may I also beg that you will be particular in procuring the largest sized Evangelists you can.’", "'쫓아가서 미안하오, 돈 세바스티안. 하지만 한 가지 더 부탁해도 되겠소? 구할 수 있는 가장 큰 판형의 복음서로 각별히 마련해다 주시오.'"]], [["“‘This is the priest, he brings you the Evangelists,’ said Don Sebastian, gravely, returning with a tall and solemn figure.", "'이분이 그 사제님이오, 당신께 복음서를 가져다주실 게요.' 돈 세바스티안이 키 크고 근엄한 인물과 함께 돌아오며 자못 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“‘Let me remove my hat.", "'내 모자를 벗겠소."], ["Now, venerable priest, further into the light, and hold the Holy Book before me that I may touch it.", "자, 존경하는 사제님, 빛 속으로 좀 더 가까이 오시어, 그 거룩한 책을 제 앞에 들어 주시오, 제가 거기 손을 댈 수 있도록 말이오.'"]], [["“‘So help me Heaven, and on my honor the story I have told ye, gentlemen, is in substance and its great items, true.", "'하늘이여 나를 도우소서, 또 내 명예를 걸고, 여러분, 내가 여러분께 들려드린 이 이야기는 그 본질과 그 큰 줄거리에 있어 참된 것이오. 나는 그것이 참됨을 알고 있소."], ["I know it to be true; it happened on this ball; I trod the ship; I knew the crew; I have seen and talked with Steelkilt since the death of Radney.’”", "그 일은 이 지구 위에서 벌어졌소. 나는 그 배를 디뎌 보았고, 그 선원들을 알았으며, 래드니가 죽은 뒤로 스틸킬트를 만나 그와 이야기를 나누었소.'\""]], [["# CHAPTER 55. Of the Monstrous Pictures of Whales.", "# CHAPTER 55. Of the Monstrous Pictures of Whales."]], [["I shall ere long paint to you as well as one can without canvas, something like the true form of the whale as he actually appears to the eye of the whaleman when in his own absolute body the whale is moored alongside the whale-ship so that he can be fairly stepped upon there.", "머지않아 나는 화폭 없이도 그릴 수 있는 한 최대한 충실하게, 고래가 그 자신의 온전한 몸뚱이 그대로 포경선 곁에 매여 있어 그 위에 정통으로 발을 디딜 수 있을 때 고래잡이의 눈에 실제로 비치는 고래의 참된 형상에 가까운 무언가를 그대들에게 그려 보이고자 한다."], ["It may be worth while, therefore, previously to advert to those curious imaginary portraits of him which even down to the present day confidently challenge the faith of the landsman.", "그러므로 그에 앞서, 오늘날에 이르기까지도 뭍사람의 믿음을 당당히 시험하는 저 기이하고 상상으로 빚어낸 고래 초상화들을 잠시 짚어 보는 것이 가치 있는 일일 것이다."], ["It is time to set the world right in this matter, by proving such pictures of the whale all wrong.", "그러한 고래 그림들이 모조리 틀렸음을 입증함으로써, 이 문제에 관하여 세상을 바로잡을 때가 되었다."]], [["It may be that the primal source of all those pictorial delusions will be found among the oldest Hindoo, Egyptian, and Grecian sculptures.", "이 모든 그림 속 망상의 근원은 어쩌면 가장 오래된 힌두와 이집트와 그리스의 조각들 속에서 찾아질지도 모른다."], ["For ever since those inventive but unscrupulous times when on the marble panellings of temples, the pedestals of statues, and on shields, medallions, cups, and coins, the dolphin was drawn in scales of chain-armor like Saladin’s, and a helmeted head like St. George’s; ever since then has something of the same sort of license prevailed, not only in most popular pictures of the whale, but in many scientific presentations of him.", "사원의 대리석 패널과 조각상의 받침대, 그리고 방패와 메달리온과 잔과 동전 위에, 돌고래가 살라딘의 것과 같은 사슬 갑옷 비늘과 성 게오르기우스의 것과 같은 투구 쓴 머리로 그려지던, 저 창의적이지만 거리낌 없던 시절 이래로, 그와 똑같은 종류의 자유분방함이 고래의 가장 대중적인 그림들뿐 아니라 그에 관한 수많은 과학적 묘사들에까지 줄곧 만연해 온 것이다."]], [["Now, by all odds, the most ancient extant portrait anyways purporting to be the whale’s, is to be found in the famous cavern-pagoda of Elephanta, in India.", "자, 무엇보다도, 어떤 식으로든 고래를 그렸노라 자처하는 현존하는 가장 오래된 초상은 인도의 그 유명한 엘레판타 동굴 사원에서 찾아볼 수 있다."], ["The Brahmins maintain that in the almost endless sculptures of that immemorial pagoda, all the trades and pursuits, every conceivable avocation of man, were prefigured ages before any of them actually came into being.", "브라만들은 그 태곳적 사원의 거의 끝없는 조각들 속에, 인간의 모든 직업과 일거리, 상상할 수 있는 온갖 생업이 그것들이 실제로 생겨나기 수천 년 전에 미리 그려져 있다고 주장한다."], ["No wonder then, that in some sort our noble profession of whaling should have been there shadowed forth.", "그러니 우리의 고귀한 포경업이 어떤 식으로든 거기에 어렴풋이 비쳐 있다 한들 놀랄 일이 아니다."], ["The Hindoo whale referred to, occurs in a separate department of the wall, depicting the incarnation of Vishnu in the form of leviathan, learnedly known as the Matse Avatar.", "그 힌두 고래는 벽의 별도 구획에 등장하는데, 학식 있는 자들 사이에서 마트세 아바타르로 알려진, 리바이어던의 형상을 한 비슈누의 화신을 묘사한 것이다."], ["But though this sculpture is half man and half whale, so as only to give the tail of the latter, yet that small section of him is all wrong.", "그러나 이 조각은 절반은 사람이고 절반은 고래여서 후자의 꼬리만을 보여 주는데, 그나마 그 작은 부분조차 온통 틀렸다."], ["It looks more like the tapering tail of an anaconda, than the broad palms of the true whale’s majestic flukes.", "그것은 참된 고래의 위풍당당한 꼬리지느러미의 널찍한 손바닥 같은 모양보다는, 차라리 아나콘다의 가늘어지는 꼬리에 더 가까워 보인다."]], [["But go to the old Galleries, and look now at a great Christian painter’s portrait of this fish; for he succeeds no better than the antediluvian Hindoo.", "그러나 옛 화랑들로 가서, 이제 어느 위대한 기독교 화가가 그린 이 물고기의 초상을 보라. 그라고 해서 대홍수 이전의 힌두인보다 더 나을 것이 없으니 말이다."], ["It is Guido’s picture of Perseus rescuing Andromeda from the sea-monster or whale.", "그것은 페르세우스가 바다 괴물 혹은 고래로부터 안드로메다를 구하는 장면을 그린 귀도의 그림이다."], ["Where did Guido get the model of such a strange creature as that?", "귀도는 도대체 어디서 그토록 기괴한 피조물의 모델을 얻었단 말인가?"], ["Nor does Hogarth, in painting the same scene in his own “Perseus Descending,” make out one whit better.", "호가스 역시 자신의 작품 \"강림하는 페르세우스\"에서 같은 장면을 그리면서 조금도 나을 바가 없다."], ["The huge corpulence of that Hogarthian monster undulates on the surface, scarcely drawing one inch of water.", "그 호가스적 괴물의 거대한 비만은 수면 위에서 일렁이는데, 물에 잠긴 깊이는 채 한 치도 되지 않는다."], ["It has a sort of howdah on its back, and its distended tusked mouth into which the billows are rolling, might be taken for the Traitors’ Gate leading from the Thames by water into the Tower.", "그것은 등에 일종의 가마 같은 것을 얹고 있고, 파도가 굽이쳐 들어가는 그 떡 벌어진 엄니 달린 입은, 템스강에서 물길을 따라 탑으로 통하는 반역자의 문으로 여겨질 만하다."], ["Then, there are the Prodromus whales of old Scotch Sibbald, and Jonah’s whale, as depicted in the prints of old Bibles and the cuts of old primers.", "그다음으로는, 늙은 스코틀랜드인 시발드의 프로드로무스 고래들이 있고, 낡은 성경의 판화와 낡은 입문서의 삽화에 그려진 요나(Jonah)의 고래가 있다."], ["What shall be said of these?", "이것들에 대해서는 무어라 말해야 할까?"], ["As for the book-binder’s whale winding like a vine-stalk round the stock of a descending anchor—as stamped and gilded on the backs and title-pages of many books both old and new—that is a very picturesque but purely fabulous creature, imitated, I take it, from the like figures on antique vases.", "내려가는 닻의 몸통을 포도 덩굴처럼 휘감고 있는 — 오래된 책이든 새 책이든 수많은 책의 뒤표지와 표제지에 찍히고 도금된 — 제본업자의 고래로 말하자면, 그것은 매우 그림 같지만 순전히 꾸며 낸 피조물로서, 내 생각으로는 고대 항아리에 그려진 비슷한 형상에서 본떠 온 것이다."], ["Though universally denominated a dolphin, I nevertheless call this book-binder’s fish an attempt at a whale; because it was so intended when the device was first introduced.", "비록 어디서나 돌고래라 불리지만, 그럼에도 나는 이 제본업자의 물고기를 고래를 그리려 한 시도라 부르겠다. 그 도안이 처음 도입되었을 때 그렇게 의도되었기 때문이다."], ["It was introduced by an old Italian publisher somewhere about the 15th century, during the Revival of Learning; and in those days, and even down to a comparatively late period, dolphins were popularly supposed to be a species of the Leviathan.", "그것은 학문 부흥기였던 15세기 어름에 어느 늙은 이탈리아 출판업자가 도입한 것인데, 그 시절에는, 아니 비교적 후대에 이르기까지도, 돌고래는 흔히 리바이어던의 일종으로 여겨졌던 것이다."]], [["In the vignettes and other embellishments of some ancient books you will at times meet with very curious touches at the whale, where all manner of spouts, jets d’eau, hot springs and cold, Saratoga and Baden-Baden, come bubbling up from his unexhausted brain.", "어떤 옛 책들의 작은 삽화와 그 밖의 장식들 속에서, 그대는 이따금 고래에 관한 매우 기이한 손길과 마주치게 될 터인데, 거기서는 온갖 종류의 물줄기와 분수와 더운 샘과 찬 샘, 새러토가와 바덴바덴이 그것의 마르지 않는 머릿속에서 부글부글 솟아오른다. \"학문의 진보\""], ["In the title-page of the original edition of the “Advancement of Learning” you will find some curious whales.", "초판본의 표제지에서도 그대는 몇몇 기이한 고래들을 발견하게 될 것이다."]], [["But quitting all these unprofessional attempts, let us glance at those pictures of leviathan purporting to be sober, scientific delineations, by those who know.", "그러나 이 모든 비전문가적 시도들은 접어 두고, 잘 아는 자들이 그렸다고 자처하는, 진중하고 과학적인 묘사를 표방하는 저 리바이어던 그림들을 한번 훑어보자."], ["In old Harris’s collection of voyages there are some plates of whales extracted from a Dutch book of voyages, A.D. 1671, entitled “A Whaling Voyage to Spitzbergen in the ship Jonas in the Whale, Peter Peterson of Friesland, master.”", "늙은 해리스의 항해기 모음집에는 1671년의 어느 네덜란드 항해서에서 발췌한 고래 도판 몇 점이 실려 있는데, 그 책의 제목은 \"고래 뱃속의 요나호를 타고 떠난 스피츠베르겐 포경 항해, 선장 프리슬란트의 페터 페터르손\"이다."], ["In one of those plates the whales, like great rafts of logs, are represented lying among ice-isles, with white bears running over their living backs.", "그 도판들 가운데 하나에서는 통나무 뗏목처럼 거대한 고래들이 얼음섬들 사이에 누워 있고, 흰곰들이 그 살아 있는 등 위를 뛰어다니는 모습으로 그려져 있다."], ["In another plate, the prodigious blunder is made of representing the whale with perpendicular flukes.", "또 다른 도판에서는 고래의 꼬리지느러미를 수직으로 그려 놓는 어처구니없는 실수를 저지르고 있다."]], [["Then again, there is an imposing quarto, written by one Captain Colnett, a Post Captain in the English navy, entitled “A Voyage round Cape Horn into the South Seas, for the purpose of extending the Spermaceti Whale Fisheries.”", "또한, 영국 해군의 함장 콜넷이라는 사람이 쓴, 위풍당당한 사절판 책이 한 권 있는데, 그 제목은 \"향유고래잡이 확장을 위한 케이프 혼을 돌아 남쪽 바다로 가는 항해\"이다."], ["In this book is an outline purporting to be a “Picture of a Physeter or Spermaceti whale, drawn by scale from one killed on the coast of Mexico, August, 1793, and hoisted on deck.”", "이 책에는 \"1793년 8월 멕시코 연안에서 잡혀 갑판으로 끌어올려진 한 마리를 척도에 맞추어 그린 피세테르, 곧 향유고래의 그림\"이라 표방하는 윤곽도가 실려 있다."], ["I doubt not the captain had this veracious picture taken for the benefit of his marines.", "나는 이 함장이 자기 해병들을 위해 이 진실한 그림을 그리게 한 것이라 믿어 의심치 않는다."], ["To mention but one thing about it, let me say that it has an eye which applied, according to the accompanying scale, to a full grown sperm whale, would make the eye of that whale a bow-window some five feet long.", "그것에 관해 한 가지만 짚고 넘어가자면, 거기에는 눈이 하나 그려져 있는데, 그 곁에 붙은 척도에 따라 다 자란 향유고래에 적용해 보면, 그 고래의 눈은 길이가 다섯 자쯤 되는 활 모양 내닫이창이 되고 말 것이다."], ["Ah, my gallant captain, why did ye not give us Jonah looking out of that eye!", "아, 나의 용맹한 함장이여, 어찌하여 그 눈으로 내다보는 요나는 우리에게 그려 주지 않았단 말이오!"]], [["Nor are the most conscientious compilations of Natural History for the benefit of the young and tender, free from the same heinousness of mistake.", "어린이와 여린 마음을 위한, 가장 양심적인 박물지 편찬물조차도 이 가증스러운 오류에서 자유롭지 못하다."], ["Look at that popular work “Goldsmith’s Animated Nature.”", "저 대중적인 저작 \"골드스미스의 생동하는 자연\"을 보라."], ["In the abridged London edition of 1807, there are plates of an alleged “whale” and a “narwhale.”", "1807년 런던 축약판에는 이른바 \"고래\"와 \"외뿔고래\"의 도판이 실려 있다."], ["I do not wish to seem inelegant, but this unsightly whale looks much like an amputated sow; and, as for the narwhale, one glimpse at it is enough to amaze one, that in this nineteenth century such a hippogriff could be palmed for genuine upon any intelligent public of schoolboys.", "무례하게 굴고 싶지는 않으나, 이 흉측한 고래는 다리가 잘린 암퇘지를 영락없이 닮았으며, 외뿔고래로 말하자면, 그것을 한번 흘끗 보기만 해도, 이 십구 세기에 그런 히포그리프가 진품인 양 어느 영민한 학동 무리에게 떠넘겨질 수 있다는 사실에 그저 아연실색하게 된다."]], [["Then, again, in 1825, Bernard Germain, Count de Lacépède, a great naturalist, published a scientific systemized whale book, wherein are several pictures of the different species of the Leviathan.", "또한, 1825년에는 위대한 박물학자 베르나르 제르맹 라세페드 백작이 리바이어던의 여러 종을 그린 그림 몇 점이 담긴, 과학적으로 체계화된 고래 책을 펴냈다."], ["All these are not only incorrect, but the picture of the Mysticetus or Greenland whale (that is to say, the Right whale), even Scoresby, a long experienced man as touching that species, declares not to have its counterpart in nature.", "이것들은 모조리 부정확할뿐더러, 미스티세투스, 곧 그린란드 고래(다시 말해 참고래)의 그림은, 그 종에 관하여 오랜 경험을 쌓은 사람인 스코어즈비조차도 자연계에는 그에 대응하는 것이 존재하지 않는다고 단언하는 것이다."]], [["But the placing of the cap-sheaf to all this blundering business was reserved for the scientific Frederick Cuvier, brother to the famous Baron.", "그러나 이 모든 엉터리 작업에 화룡점정을 찍는 일은, 그 유명한 남작의 동생인 과학자 프레데리크 퀴비에에게 맡겨져 있었다."], ["In 1836, he published a Natural History of Whales, in which he gives what he calls a picture of the Sperm Whale.", "1836년에 그는 고래의 박물지를 펴냈는데, 거기서 그는 자신이 향유고래의 그림이라 일컫는 것을 내놓는다."], ["Before showing that picture to any Nantucketer, you had best provide for your summary retreat from Nantucket.", "그 그림을 어느 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람에게 보여 주기 전에, 그대는 낸터킷에서 신속히 달아날 방도부터 마련해 두는 편이 좋을 것이다."], ["In a word, Frederick Cuvier’s Sperm Whale is not a Sperm Whale, but a squash.", "한마디로, 프레데리크 퀴비에의 향유고래는 향유고래가 아니라 호박덩어리다."], ["Of course, he never had the benefit of a whaling voyage (such men seldom have), but whence he derived that picture, who can tell?", "물론 그는 포경 항해의 혜택을 누려 본 적이 없었지만(그런 사람들은 좀처럼 그런 경험이 없다), 도대체 그가 그 그림을 어디서 끌어왔는지 누가 알겠는가?"], ["Perhaps he got it as his scientific predecessor in the same field, Desmarest, got one of his authentic abortions; that is, from a Chinese drawing.", "어쩌면 그는 같은 분야에서 그보다 앞선 과학자 데마레가 자신의 진품 같지 않은 졸작 하나를 얻은 것과 같은 방식으로, 곧 어느 중국 그림에서 그것을 얻었는지도 모른다."], ["And what sort of lively lads with the pencil those Chinese are, many queer cups and saucers inform us.", "그리고 그 중국인들이 붓을 든 얼마나 활기찬 친구들인지는, 수많은 기묘한 잔과 받침 접시들이 우리에게 일러 준다."]], [["As for the sign-painters’ whales seen in the streets hanging over the shops of oil-dealers, what shall be said of them?", "기름 장수의 가게 위에 매달려 거리에서 볼 수 있는 간판장이의 고래들로 말하자면, 그것들에 대해서는 무어라 말해야 할까?"], ["They are generally Richard III. whales, with dromedary humps, and very savage; breakfasting on three or four sailor tarts, that is whaleboats full of mariners: their deformities floundering in seas of blood and blue paint.", "그것들은 대개 단봉낙타 같은 혹을 단, 매우 사나운 리처드 3세 고래로서, 세네 개의 선원 파이를, 다시 말해 뱃사람들로 가득 찬 포경 보트를 아침거리로 먹어 치우는데, 그 일그러진 몸뚱이들이 피와 푸른 물감의 바다 속에서 허우적거리고 있다."]], [["But these manifold mistakes in depicting the whale are not so very surprising after all. Consider!", "그러나 고래를 묘사함에 있어 이토록 갖가지 실수가 빚어진 것도 따지고 보면 그리 놀라운 일은 아니다."], ["Most of the scientific drawings have been taken from the stranded fish; and these are about as correct as a drawing of a wrecked ship, with broken back, would correctly represent the noble animal itself in all its undashed pride of hull and spars.", "한번 생각해 보라! 과학적 그림들의 대부분은 좌초한 물고기를 보고 그린 것이며, 이는 등이 부러진 난파선을 그린 그림이, 선체와 활대의 한 점 흠 없는 위풍을 온전히 갖춘 그 고귀한 선박 자체를 정확히 그려 낼 수 있는 것과 엇비슷한 정도로만 정확하다."], ["Though elephants have stood for their full-lengths, the living Leviathan has never yet fairly floated himself for his portrait.", "코끼리는 그 전신을 통째로 그림의 모델로 세웠건만, 살아 있는 리바이어던은 아직 단 한 번도 제 초상을 위해 제대로 몸을 떠올려 본 적이 없다."], ["The living whale, in his full majesty and significance, is only to be seen at sea in unfathomable waters; and afloat the vast bulk of him is out of sight, like a launched line-of-battle ship; and out of that element it is a thing eternally impossible for mortal man to hoist him bodily into the air, so as to preserve all his mighty swells and undulations.", "살아 있는 고래는, 그 온전한 위엄과 의미를 갖춘 모습으로는, 오직 바다의 헤아릴 수 없는 깊은 물에서만 볼 수 있다. 그리고 물 위에 떠 있을 때 그 거대한 몸뚱이는, 진수된 전열함처럼 시야에서 벗어나 있다."], ["And, not to speak of the highly presumable difference of contour between a young sucking whale and a full-grown Platonian Leviathan; yet, even in the case of one of those young sucking whales hoisted to a ship’s deck, such is then the outlandish, eel-like, limbered, varying shape of him, that his precise expression the devil himself could not catch.", "또한 그 원소 밖으로 그것을 통째로 공중에 들어 올려, 그 위풍당당한 모든 융기와 굴곡을 고스란히 보존하는 일은, 죽을 운명의 인간에게는 영원히 불가능한 노릇이다. 그리고 젖을 빠는 어린 고래와 다 자란 플라톤적 리바이어던 사이에 충분히 짐작될 만한 윤곽의 차이는 말할 것도 없거니와, 설령 그런 젖먹이 어린 고래 가운데 한 마리를 배의 갑판으로 끌어올린다 한들, 그때 그것의 모습은 어찌나 괴상하고 뱀장어처럼 흐물거리며 시시각각 변하는지, 그 정확한 표정만큼은 악마 자신도 붙잡아 낼 수 없을 것이다."]], [["But it may be fancied, that from the naked skeleton of the stranded whale, accurate hints may be derived touching his true form.", "그러나 좌초한 고래의 앙상한 뼈대에서라면 그 참된 형상에 관한 정확한 단서를 얻을 수 있으리라 짐작할 수도 있겠다."], ["Not at all.", "천만의 말씀이다."], ["For it is one of the more curious things about this Leviathan, that his skeleton gives very little idea of his general shape.", "이 리바이어던에 관한 더욱 기이한 점 하나는, 그 뼈대가 전체적인 형상에 관해 거의 아무런 단서도 주지 않는다는 것이기 때문이다."], ["Though Jeremy Bentham’s skeleton, which hangs for candelabra in the library of one of his executors, correctly conveys the idea of a burly-browed utilitarian old gentleman, with all Jeremy’s other leading personal characteristics; yet nothing of this kind could be inferred from any leviathan’s articulated bones.", "제러미 벤담의 유언 집행인 가운데 하나의 서재에 촛대 노릇으로 걸려 있는 그의 뼈대는 떡 벌어진 이마를 가진 공리주의 노신사의 인상을, 그 밖에 제러미의 다른 주된 개인적 특징들과 함께 정확히 전해 주지만, 그 어떤 리바이어던의 짜 맞춰진 뼈에서도 이런 종류의 것은 추론해 낼 수 없다."], ["In fact, as the great Hunter says, the mere skeleton of the whale bears the same relation to the fully invested and padded animal as the insect does to the chrysalis that so roundingly envelopes it.", "사실, 위대한 헌터가 말하듯, 고래의 뼈대만으로는, 그것이 온전히 살로 둘러싸이고 충전된 동물에 대해 가지는 관계가, 마치 곤충이 그것을 둥글게 감싸고 있는 번데기에 대해 가지는 관계와 같다."], ["This peculiarity is strikingly evinced in the head, as in some part of this book will be incidentally shown.", "이 특이함은 머리에서 두드러지게 드러나는데, 이는 이 책의 어느 부분에서 곁들여 보이게 될 것이다."], ["It is also very curiously displayed in the side fin, the bones of which almost exactly answer to the bones of the human hand, minus only the thumb.", "그것은 또한 옆지느러미에서도 매우 흥미롭게 나타나는데, 그 뼈들은 거의 정확히 사람 손의 뼈에 들어맞으며, 다만 엄지만 빠져 있을 뿐이다."], ["This fin has four regular bone-fingers, the index, middle, ring, and little finger.", "이 지느러미에는 집게손가락, 가운뎃손가락, 약손가락, 새끼손가락에 해당하는 네 개의 가지런한 뼈손가락이 있다."], ["But all these are permanently lodged in their fleshy covering, as the human fingers in an artificial covering.", "그러나 이것들은 모두, 사람의 손가락이 인공의 덮개 속에 들어 있듯, 살로 된 덮개 속에 영영 박혀 있다."], ["“However recklessly the whale may sometimes serve us,”", "\"고래가 때로 우리를 아무리 무지막지하게 대한다 한들,\""], ["said humorous Stubb one day, “he can never be truly said to handle us without mittens.”", "익살꾼 스터브가 어느 날 말했다, \"그놈이 장갑도 끼지 않고 우리를 다룬다고는 결코 말할 수 없지.\""]], [["For all these reasons, then, any way you may look at it, you must needs conclude that the great Leviathan is that one creature in the world which must remain unpainted to the last.", "그러므로 이 모든 이유로, 어느 모로 보든, 그대는 이 위대한 리바이어던이야말로 끝끝내 그려지지 않은 채로 남아야 할, 이 세상의 단 하나뿐인 피조물이라는 결론에 다다를 수밖에 없다."], ["True, one portrait may hit the mark much nearer than another, but none can hit it with any very considerable degree of exactness.", "물론 어떤 초상이 다른 초상보다 과녁에 훨씬 더 가까이 맞을 수는 있겠으나, 어느 것도 그다지 대단한 정확성으로는 그것을 맞힐 수 없다."], ["So there is no earthly way of finding out precisely what the whale really looks like.", "그러니 고래가 정말로 어떻게 생겼는지 정확히 알아낼 길은 이 세상 어디에도 없다."], ["And the only mode in which you can derive even a tolerable idea of his living contour, is by going a whaling yourself; but by so doing, you run no small risk of being eternally stove and sunk by him.", "그리고 그것의 살아 있는 윤곽에 대해 그나마 그런대로 괜찮은 관념이라도 얻을 수 있는 유일한 방법은, 그대 자신이 직접 고래잡이에 나서는 것이다. 그러나 그렇게 함으로써 그대는 그놈에게 영원히 들이받혀 침몰당할 적잖은 위험을 무릅쓰게 된다."], ["Wherefore, it seems to me you had best not be too fastidious in your curiosity touching this Leviathan.", "그러므로, 내 보기에는, 이 리바이어던에 관한 호기심을 두고 그대는 너무 까다롭게 굴지 않는 편이 좋을 듯하다."]], [["CHAPTER 56. Of the Less Erroneous Pictures of Whales, and the True Pictures of Whaling Scenes.", "고래에 관한 저 괴물 같은 그림들과 관련하여, 나는 여기서 그것들에 얽힌 한층 더 괴물 같은 이야기들로 넘어가고 싶은 강한 유혹을 느낀다."], ["In connexion with the monstrous pictures of whales, I am strongly tempted here to enter upon those still more monstrous stories of them which are to be found in certain books, both ancient and modern, especially in Pliny, Purchas, Hackluyt, Harris, Cuvier, etc.", "그런 이야기들은 고대와 근대를 막론하고 어떤 책들 속에서, 특히 플리니우스, 퍼처스, 해클루트, 해리스, 퀴비에 따위의 글 속에서 발견된다."], ["But I pass that matter by.", "그러나 그 문제는 그냥 지나치기로 한다."]], [["I know of only four published outlines of the great Sperm Whale; Colnett’s, Huggins’s, Frederick Cuvier’s, and Beale’s.", "위대한 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 윤곽을 출판으로 그려낸 것 가운데 내가 아는 것은 단 네 가지뿐이다."], ["In the previous chapter Colnett and Cuvier have been referred to.", "콜넷, 허긴스, 프레더릭 퀴비에, 그리고 빌의 것이다. 앞 장에서 콜넷과 퀴비에는 이미 언급했다."], ["Huggins’s is far better than theirs; but, by great odds, Beale’s is the best.", "허긴스의 것은 그들의 것보다 훨씬 낫지만, 단연코 가장 뛰어난 것은 빌의 그림이다."], ["All Beale’s drawings of this whale are good, excepting the middle figure in the picture of three whales in various attitudes, capping his second chapter.", "빌이 그린 이 고래의 그림들은 모두 훌륭한데, 다만 그의 둘째 장 첫머리를 장식한, 여러 자세를 취한 고래 세 마리를 담은 그림 가운데 한가운데 놓인 형상만은 예외다."], ["His frontispiece, boats attacking Sperm Whales, though no doubt calculated to excite the civil scepticism of some parlor men, is admirably correct and life-like in its general effect.", "그의 권두 삽화는 향유고래를 공격하는 보트들을 그린 것인데, 어느 응접실 신사들의 점잖은 회의를 자아내도록 계산된 것이라 한들, 그 전체적인 효과만큼은 감탄할 만큼 정확하고 생생하다. J."], ["Some of the Sperm Whale drawings in J. Ross Browne are pretty correct in contour; but they are wretchedly engraved.", "로스 브라운의 향유고래 그림 가운데 일부는 윤곽이 제법 정확하지만, 판각이 형편없다."], ["That is not his fault though.", "하지만 그것이 그의 잘못은 아니다."]], [["Of the Right Whale, the best outline pictures are in Scoresby; but they are drawn on too small a scale to convey a desirable impression.", "참고래(Right Whale)에 관해서라면 가장 뛰어난 윤곽화는 스코어스비의 것이지만, 너무 작은 축척으로 그려져 있어 바람직한 인상을 전하지 못한다."], ["He has but one picture of whaling scenes, and this is a sad deficiency, because it is by such pictures only, when at all well done, that you can derive anything like a truthful idea of the living whale as seen by his living hunters.", "그에게는 고래잡이 장면을 담은 그림이 단 하나뿐인데, 이는 안타까운 결함이다. 살아 있는 사냥꾼들의 눈에 비친 살아 있는 고래에 관해 그나마 진실에 가까운 관념을 얻을 수 있는 것은, 오직 그런 그림들을 통해서, 그것도 제대로 그려졌을 때에만 가능하기 때문이다."]], [["But, taken for all in all, by far the finest, though in some details not the most correct, presentations of whales and whaling scenes to be anywhere found, are two large French engravings, well executed, and taken from paintings by one Garnery.", "그러나 모든 점을 두루 따져 볼 때, 비록 세부에서는 가장 정확하다고 할 수 없을지언정, 어디서든 찾아볼 수 있는 고래와 고래잡이 장면의 묘사 가운데 단연 가장 빼어난 것은 두 점의 큼직한 프랑스 판화다. 솜씨 좋게 새겨진 이 그림들은 가르네리라는 어떤 이의 회화를 바탕으로 한 것이다."], ["Respectively, they represent attacks on the Sperm and Right Whale.", "두 그림은 각각 향유고래와 참고래에 대한 공격을 묘사한다."], ["In the first engraving a noble Sperm Whale is depicted in full majesty of might, just risen beneath the boat from the profundities of the ocean, and bearing high in the air upon his back the terrific wreck of the stoven planks.", "첫째 판화에는 위풍당당한 향유고래 한 마리가 그 힘의 위엄을 한껏 드러낸 채 그려져 있는데, 방금 대양의 심연에서 보트 밑으로 솟구쳐 올라, 박살 난 널빤지들의 처참한 잔해를 등에 짊어진 채 허공 높이 떠받치고 있다."], ["The prow of the boat is partially unbroken, and is drawn just balancing upon the monster’s spine; and standing in that prow, for that one single incomputable flash of time, you behold an oarsman, half shrouded by the incensed boiling spout of the whale, and in the act of leaping, as if from a precipice.", "보트의 뱃머리는 일부만 부서진 채 그 괴물의 등뼈 위에서 아슬아슬하게 균형을 잡고 있는 모습으로 그려져 있다. 그리고 그 뱃머리에 한 사공이 서 있는데, 셈할 수도 없는 그 단 한 순간의 섬광 같은 찰나에, 고래가 성난 듯 끓어오르며 뿜어내는 물줄기에 반쯤 가려진 채 마치 벼랑에서 뛰어내리려는 듯한 자세를 취하고 있다."], ["The action of the whole thing is wonderfully good and true.", "이 모든 동작이 놀랍도록 훌륭하고 진실하다."], ["The half-emptied line-tub floats on the whitened sea; the wooden poles of the spilled harpoons obliquely bob in it; the heads of the swimming crew are scattered about the whale in contrasting expressions of affright; while in the black stormy distance the ship is bearing down upon the scene.", "반쯤 비어 가는 작살줄 통은 하얗게 부서지는 바다 위에 떠 있고, 쏟아진 작살들의 나무 자루가 그 안에서 비스듬히 까딱거린다. 헤엄치는 선원들의 머리는 고래 주변에 흩어진 채 저마다 다른 공포의 표정을 짓고 있고, 한편 검고 폭풍우 치는 저 먼 곳에서는 배가 그 현장을 향해 내달려 오고 있다."], ["Serious fault might be found with the anatomical details of this whale, but let that pass; since, for the life of me, I could not draw so good a one.", "이 고래의 해부학적 세부에는 자못 심각한 흠을 찾아낼 수도 있겠으나, 그냥 넘어가기로 하자. 나로서는 죽었다 깨어나도 이만큼 잘 그려낼 수 없을 테니 말이다."]], [["In the second engraving, the boat is in the act of drawing alongside the barnacled flank of a large running Right Whale, that rolls his black weedy bulk in the sea like some mossy rock-slide from the Patagonian cliffs.", "둘째 판화에서는 보트가 거대한 참고래의, 따개비로 뒤덮인 옆구리로 막 다가서는 참이다. 이 고래는 검고 해초가 엉긴 거구를 바다 속에서 굴리며 나아가는데, 그 모습이 마치 파타고니아의 절벽에서 이끼 낀 바위가 무너져 내리는 것 같다."], ["His jets are erect, full, and black like soot; so that from so abounding a smoke in the chimney, you would think there must be a brave supper cooking in the great bowels below.", "그것이 뿜어 올리는 물줄기는 곧추서서 가득하고 그을음처럼 검다. 굴뚝에서 그토록 자욱한 연기가 피어오르는 것을 보면, 저 거대한 뱃속 아래에서 진수성찬 같은 만찬이 끓고 있으리라 여길 법도 하다."], ["Sea fowls are pecking at the small crabs, shell-fish, and other sea candies and maccaroni, which the Right Whale sometimes carries on his pestilent back.", "바닷새들은 작은 게며 조개며, 참고래가 이따금 그 역겨운 등에 싣고 다니는 갖가지 바다 사탕과 마카로니 같은 것들을 쪼아 먹고 있다."], ["And all the while the thick-lipped leviathan is rushing through the deep, leaving tons of tumultuous white curds in his wake, and causing the slight boat to rock in the swells like a skiff caught nigh the paddle-wheels of an ocean steamer.", "그러는 내내 입술 두툼한 리바이어던(Leviathan)은 깊은 바다를 가르며 돌진하느라, 뒤로는 몇 톤이나 되는 거센 흰 거품을 남기고, 그 작은 보트를 너울 속에서 흔들어 대니, 마치 외륜 증기선의 물갈퀴 바퀴 가까이에 휘말린 거룻배 같다."], ["Thus, the foreground is all raging commotion; but behind, in admirable artistic contrast, is the glassy level of a sea becalmed, the drooping unstarched sails of the powerless ship, and the inert mass of a dead whale, a conquered fortress, with the flag of capture lazily hanging from the whale-pole inserted into his spout-hole.", "이렇듯 화면 앞쪽은 온통 격렬한 소란이지만, 그 뒤에는 감탄스러운 예술적 대비로 잔잔히 가라앉은 바다의 유리알 같은 수면과, 바람 없이 맥없이 늘어진 배의 풀기 빠진 돛, 그리고 죽은 고래의 미동도 없는 거구가 정복당한 요새처럼 놓여 있고, 그 분수공에 꽂힌 고래 깃대에서는 포획의 깃발이 나른하게 늘어져 있다."]], [["Who Garnery the painter is, or was, I know not.", "화가 가르네리가 누구인지, 혹은 누구였는지 나는 알지 못한다."], ["But my life for it he was either practically conversant with his subject, or else marvellously tutored by some experienced whaleman.", "그러나 내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 그는 자기가 그리는 대상에 실제로 정통한 사람이었거나, 아니면 노련한 고래잡이에게 놀라우리만치 잘 배운 사람이었음이 틀림없다. 동작을 그리는 데는 프랑스인이 제격이다."], ["The French are the lads for painting action.", "유럽의 모든 회화를 두루 살펴보라."], ["Go and gaze upon all the paintings of Europe, and where will you find such a gallery of living and breathing commotion on canvas, as in that triumphal hall at Versailles; where the beholder fights his way, pell-mell, through the consecutive great battles of France; where every sword seems a flash of the Northern Lights, and the successive armed kings and Emperors dash by, like a charge of crowned centaurs?", "베르사유의 저 개선 회랑만큼 살아 숨 쉬는 소란을 화폭에 담아낸 화랑을 어디서 찾을 수 있겠는가. 그곳에서는 관람자가 프랑스의 잇따른 대전투들을 헤치며 우왕좌왕 길을 뚫고 나아가고, 칼이란 칼은 모두 북극광의 섬광처럼 번쩍이며, 무장한 왕들과 황제들이 줄지어 내달려 가는 모습이 마치 왕관 쓴 켄타우로스 무리가 돌격하는 것 같다."], ["Not wholly unworthy of a place in that gallery, are these sea battle-pieces of Garnery.", "가르네리의 이 바다 전투화들은, 저 회랑에 한 자리를 차지하기에 결코 손색이 없다."]], [["The natural aptitude of the French for seizing the picturesqueness of things seems to be peculiarly evinced in what paintings and engravings they have of their whaling scenes.", "사물의 그림 같은 매력을 포착해 내는 프랑스인 특유의 천부적 재능은, 그들이 그린 고래잡이 장면의 회화와 판화에서 유독 두드러지게 드러나는 듯하다."], ["With not one tenth of England’s experience in the fishery, and not the thousandth part of that of the Americans, they have nevertheless furnished both nations with the only finished sketches at all capable of conveying the real spirit of the whale hunt.", "그들은 고래잡이 경험이 영국의 십분의 일에도, 미국의 천분의 일에도 미치지 못함에도 불구하고, 고래 사냥의 참된 정신을 그나마 전할 수 있는 유일하게 완성된 스케치를 두 나라 모두에게 제공해 왔다."], ["For the most part, the English and American whale draughtsmen seem entirely content with presenting the mechanical outline of things, such as the vacant profile of the whale; which, so far as picturesqueness of effect is concerned, is about tantamount to sketching the profile of a pyramid.", "영국과 미국의 고래 화공들은 대개 고래의 텅 빈 옆모습 같은, 사물의 기계적인 윤곽을 제시하는 것으로 완전히 만족하는 듯한데, 그림 같은 효과로 말할 것 같으면 그것은 피라미드의 옆모습을 스케치하는 것과 별반 다를 바 없다."], ["Even Scoresby, the justly renowned Right whaleman, after giving us a stiff full length of the Greenland whale, and three or four delicate miniatures of narwhales and porpoises, treats us to a series of classical engravings of boat hooks, chopping knives, and grapnels; and with the microscopic diligence of a Leuwenhoeck submits to the inspection of a shivering world ninety-six fac-similes of magnified Arctic snow crystals.", "정당하게 명성을 떨친 참고래잡이 스코어스비조차도, 그린란드 고래의 뻣뻣한 전신상을 하나 보여 주고 일각고래와 돌고래의 섬세한 세밀화를 서너 점 곁들인 다음에는, 보트 갈고리며 자르는 칼이며 닻갈고리 따위의 고전적인 판화를 줄줄이 내놓는다. 그리고 레이우엔훅 같은 현미경적 근면함으로, 확대한 북극 눈 결정의 모사도 아흔여섯 점을 추위에 떠는 세상의 검열에 부친다."], ["I mean no disparagement to the excellent voyager (I honor him for a veteran), but in so important a matter it was certainly an oversight not to have procured for every crystal a sworn affidavit taken before a Greenland Justice of the Peace.", "나는 이 훌륭한 항해자를 폄하할 뜻은 조금도 없으며(오히려 노련한 베테랑으로서 존경한다), 다만 그토록 중대한 사안에 있어, 결정 하나하나에 그린란드 치안판사 앞에서 작성한 선서 진술서를 확보해 두지 않은 것은 분명 한 가지 실수였다 하겠다."]], [["In addition to those fine engravings from Garnery, there are two other French engravings worthy of note, by some one who subscribes himself “H.", "가르네리의 저 빼어난 판화들 외에도, 주목할 만한 프랑스 판화가 두 점 더 있는데, 자신을 \"H. 뒤랑\"이라 서명한 어떤 이의 작품이다."], ["Durand.” One of them, though not precisely adapted to our present purpose, nevertheless deserves mention on other accounts.", "그중 하나는 우리의 지금 목적에 꼭 들어맞는 것은 아니지만, 다른 이유에서 언급할 만하다."], ["It is a quiet noon-scene among the isles of the Pacific; a French whaler anchored, inshore, in a calm, and lazily taking water on board; the loosened sails of the ship, and the long leaves of the palms in the background, both drooping together in the breezeless air.", "그것은 태평양 섬들 사이의 고요한 정오 풍경이다. 프랑스 포경선 한 척이 잔잔한 물 위, 해안 가까이에 닻을 내린 채 한가로이 물을 싣고 있고, 풀어 놓은 배의 돛과 배경의 야자수 긴 잎사귀들이 바람 한 점 없는 공기 속에서 함께 늘어져 있다."], ["The effect is very fine, when considered with reference to its presenting the hardy fishermen under one of their few aspects of oriental repose.", "그 효과는 자못 빼어난데, 강인한 고래잡이들을 좀처럼 보기 힘든 동방적 휴식의 한 모습으로 담아냈다는 점을 고려하면 더욱 그렇다. 다른 판화는 사뭇 다른 광경이다."], ["The other engraving is quite a different affair: the ship hove-to upon the open sea, and in the very heart of the Leviathanic life, with a Right Whale alongside; the vessel (in the act of cutting-in) hove over to the monster as if to a quay; and a boat, hurriedly pushing off from this scene of activity, is about giving chase to whales in the distance.", "배는 망망대해 위, 바로 리바이어던적 생명의 한복판에서 닻을 풀고 멈춰 선 채, 옆구리에는 참고래 한 마리를 끼고 있다. 배는 (해체 작업을 하느라) 마치 부두에 대듯 그 괴물 쪽으로 기울어져 있고, 보트 한 척은 이 부산한 현장에서 황급히 밀려 나와 저 멀리 있는 고래들을 향해 막 추격에 나서려는 참이다."], ["The harpoons and lances lie levelled for use; three oarsmen are just setting the mast in its hole; while from a sudden roll of the sea, the little craft stands half-erect out of the water, like a rearing horse.", "작살과 창들은 곧장 쓸 수 있도록 가지런히 누워 있고, 세 사공이 마침 돛대를 그 구멍에 세우고 있는 동안, 갑자기 바다가 너울치는 바람에 그 작은 배는 마치 앞발을 치켜든 말처럼 물 밖으로 반쯤 곧추서 있다."], ["From the ship, the smoke of the torments of the boiling whale is going up like the smoke over a village of smithies; and to windward, a black cloud, rising up with earnest of squalls and rains, seems to quicken the activity of the excited seamen.", "배 쪽에서는 끓는 고래의 고통에서 피어오르는 연기가 마치 대장간 마을 위로 솟는 연기처럼 올라가고 있고, 바람 불어오는 쪽에서는 검은 구름이 돌풍과 비를 예고하며 피어올라, 흥분한 선원들의 분주함을 한층 재촉하는 듯하다."]], [["CHAPTER 57. Of Whales in Paint; in Teeth; in Wood; in Sheet-Iron; in Stone; in Mountains; in Stars.", "런던 부두로 내려가는 길목인 타워힐에서, 자기 앞에 그림 한 폭을 그려 넣은 판자를 받쳐 든 채 서 있는 불구의 거지(선원들 말로는 케저)를 본 적이 있을 것이다."], ["On Tower-hill, as you go down to the London docks, you may have seen a crippled beggar (or kedger, as the sailors say) holding a painted board before him, representing the tragic scene in which he lost his leg.", "그 판자에는 그가 다리를 잃었던 비극적인 장면이 그려져 있다."], ["There are three whales and three boats; and one of the boats (presumed to contain the missing leg in all its original integrity) is being crunched by the jaws of the foremost whale.", "고래 세 마리와 보트 세 척이 있고, 그중 한 척의 보트(그 안에 잃어버린 다리가 본래의 온전한 모습 그대로 들어 있다고 추정되는)가 맨 앞 고래의 턱에 으스러지고 있다."], ["Any time these ten years, they tell me, has that man held up that picture, and exhibited that stump to an incredulous world.", "사람들이 내게 일러 준 바로는, 지난 십 년 동안 줄곧 그 사내는 그 그림을 치켜들고, 믿으려 들지 않는 세상을 향해 그 잘린 다리를 내보여 왔다고 한다."], ["But the time of his justification has now come.", "그러나 이제 그의 정당성이 입증될 때가 왔다."], ["His three whales are as good whales as were ever published in Wapping, at any rate; and his stump as unquestionable a stump as any you will find in the western clearings.", "그의 고래 세 마리는 적어도 워핑(Wapping)에서 그동안 세상에 내놓은 어떤 고래 못지않게 훌륭한 고래이며, 그의 잘린 다리 또한 서부 개척지에서 보게 될 어떤 그루터기 다리만큼이나 의심할 여지 없는 그루터기다."], ["But, though for ever mounted on that stump, never a stump-speech does the poor whaleman make; but, with downcast eyes, stands ruefully contemplating his own amputation.", "그러나, 그 그루터기 위에 영영 올라서 있으면서도, 이 가련한 고래잡이는 결코 그루터기 연설 따위는 하지 않는다. 다만 눈을 내리깐 채, 서글피 제 절단된 다리를 들여다보며 서 있을 뿐이다."]], [["Throughout the Pacific, and also in Nantucket, and New Bedford, and Sag Harbor, you will come across lively sketches of whales and whaling-scenes, graven by the fishermen themselves on Sperm Whale-teeth, or ladies’ busks wrought out of the Right Whale-bone, and other like skrimshander articles, as the whalemen call the numerous little ingenious contrivances they elaborately carve out of the rough material, in their hours of ocean leisure.", "태평양 전역에서, 그리고 낸터킷(Nantucket)과 뉴베드퍼드와 새그하버에서도, 어부들이 손수 향유고래의 이빨에 새겨 넣은 고래와 포경 장면의 생생한 그림들을 마주치게 될 것이다. 또 참고래의 뼈로 깎아 만든 부인용 코르셋 살대며, 그 밖에 비슷한 스크림샌더 세공품들도 보게 되는데, 이는 고래잡이들이 망망대해에서 한가로운 시간을 보낼 적에 거친 재료를 정성껏 깎아 만들어 내는 그 수많은 자그마하고 정교한 물건들을 일컫는 그들 나름의 말이다."], ["Some of them have little boxes of dentistical-looking implements, specially intended for the skrimshandering business.", "그들 중 더러는 치과 기구처럼 생긴 자그마한 연장 상자를 갖추고 있는데, 오로지 이 스크림샌더 일을 위해 마련한 것이다."], ["But, in general, they toil with their jack-knives alone; and, with that almost omnipotent tool of the sailor, they will turn you out anything you please, in the way of a mariner’s fancy.", "그러나 대체로는 잭나이프 하나만 가지고 작업하며, 선원이 지닌 그 거의 만능에 가까운 연장 하나로, 뱃사람의 상상이 닿는 것이라면 그 무엇이든 원하는 대로 척척 만들어 낸다."]], [["Long exile from Christendom and civilization inevitably restores a man to that condition in which God placed him, i.e. what is called savagery.", "기독교 세계와 문명으로부터 오래 떠나 있다 보면, 사람은 어쩔 수 없이 하느님께서 처음 그를 두셨던 그 상태, 곧 야만이라 불리는 상태로 되돌아가게 마련이다."], ["Your true whale-hunter is as much a savage as an Iroquois.", "진정한 고래 사냥꾼은 이로쿼이족 못지않은 야만인이다."], ["I myself am a savage, owning no allegiance but to the King of the Cannibals; and ready at any moment to rebel against him.", "나 자신도 야만인이어서, 식인종의 왕에게밖에는 그 어떤 충성도 바치지 않으며, 언제라도 그에게 반기를 들 채비가 되어 있다."]], [["Now, one of the peculiar characteristics of the savage in his domestic hours, is his wonderful patience of industry.", "그런데 집안에서 시간을 보낼 적에 야만인이 보이는 독특한 특징 가운데 하나는, 그 일에 쏟는 놀라운 인내심이다."], ["An ancient Hawaiian war-club or spear-paddle, in its full multiplicity and elaboration of carving, is as great a trophy of human perseverance as a Latin lexicon.", "고대 하와이의 전투용 곤봉이나 창 모양의 노는, 그 새김의 온갖 복잡함과 정교함을 따져 보면, 라틴어 사전에 견줄 만한 인간 끈기의 위대한 전리품이다."], ["For, with but a bit of broken sea-shell or a shark’s tooth, that miraculous intricacy of wooden net-work has been achieved; and it has cost steady years of steady application.", "왜냐하면 깨진 조개껍데기 한 조각이나 상어 이빨 하나만 가지고, 그 기적 같은 정교한 나무 그물 세공을 이루어 냈으며, 그것은 한결같은 정성을 쏟은 여러 해의 세월을 들인 끝에 얻어진 것이기 때문이다."]], [["As with the Hawaiian savage, so with the white sailor-savage.", "하와이의 야만인이 그러하듯, 백인 선원 야만인 역시 마찬가지다."], ["With the same marvellous patience, and with the same single shark’s tooth, of his one poor jack-knife, he will carve you a bit of bone sculpture, not quite as workmanlike, but as close packed in its maziness of design, as the Greek savage, Achilles’s shield; and full of barbaric spirit and suggestiveness, as the prints of that fine old Dutch savage, Albert Durer.", "똑같은 경이로운 인내심으로, 그리고 자신의 보잘것없는 잭나이프 하나에 달린 똑같은 상어 이빨 한 점으로, 그는 당신을 위해 뼈 조각 한 점을 깎아 낼 텐데, 솜씨야 그만 못할지언정 그 무늬의 어지러운 복잡함만큼은 저 그리스의 야만인 아킬레우스의 방패에 못지않게 빽빽이 들어차 있고, 또 저 노련한 네덜란드의 야만인 알브레히트 뒤러의 판화처럼 야만적인 기백과 암시로 가득 차 있다."]], [["Wooden whales, or whales cut in profile out of the small dark slabs of the noble South Sea war-wood, are frequently met with in the forecastles of American whalers.", "나무로 깎은 고래나, 고귀한 남양산 전투용 목재의 작고 검은 판자를 옆모습으로 깎아 낸 고래는 미국 포경선의 선원실에서 흔히 마주칠 수 있다."], ["Some of them are done with much accuracy.", "그중 더러는 상당히 정확하게 만들어져 있다."]], [["At some old gable-roofed country houses you will see brass whales hung by the tail for knockers to the road-side door.", "박공지붕을 인 어느 오래된 시골집들에서는, 길가로 난 문에 노커로 꼬리를 매달아 둔 놋쇠 고래를 보게 될 것이다."], ["When the porter is sleepy, the anvil-headed whale would be best.", "문지기가 졸음에 겨워할 때라면, 모루 머리를 한 고래가 가장 안성맞춤일 것이다."], ["But these knocking whales are seldom remarkable as faithful essays.", "그러나 이렇게 문을 두드리는 고래들이 충실한 작품으로서 눈에 띌 만한 경우는 드물다."], ["On the spires of some old-fashioned churches you will see sheet-iron whales placed there for weather-cocks; but they are so elevated, and besides that are to all intents and purposes so labelled with “Hands off!” you cannot examine them closely enough to decide upon their merit.", "구식 교회의 몇몇 첨탑에서는 풍향계로 거기 얹어 둔 양철 고래를 보게 될 텐데, 워낙 높이 매달려 있는 데다 게다가 어느 모로 보나 \"손대지 마시오!\"라는 딱지를 붙여 둔 셈이라, 그 솜씨의 우열을 가늠할 만큼 가까이서 살펴볼 수는 없다."]], [["In bony, ribby regions of the earth, where at the base of high broken cliffs masses of rock lie strewn in fantastic groupings upon the plain, you will often discover images as of the petrified forms of the Leviathan partly merged in grass, which of a windy day breaks against them in a surf of green surges.", "뼈가 드러나고 갈빗대 같은 능선이 펼쳐진 땅, 곧 높고 부서진 절벽 기슭에 바위 덩어리들이 기묘한 무리를 이루어 들판 위에 흩어져 있는 곳에서는, 풀밭에 절반쯤 잠긴 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 석화된 형상 같은 모습들을 종종 발견하게 될 것이다. 바람 부는 날이면 풀밭은 초록 너울의 물보라처럼 그 형상들에 부딪쳐 부서진다."]], [["Then, again, in mountainous countries where the traveller is continually girdled by amphitheatrical heights; here and there from some lucky point of view you will catch passing glimpses of the profiles of whales defined along the undulating ridges. But you must be a thorough whaleman, to see these sights; and not only that, but if you wish to return to such a sight again, you must be sure and take the exact intersecting latitude and longitude of your first stand-point, else so chance-like are such observations of the hills, that your precise, previous stand-point would require a laborious re-discovery; like the Soloma Islands, which still remain incognita, though once high-ruffed Mendanna trod them and old Figuera chronicled them.", "그런가 하면 또, 나그네가 원형극장처럼 둘러선 높은 봉우리들에 끊임없이 에워싸이는 산악 지대에서는, 운 좋은 어느 시점에서 그 굽이치는 능선을 따라 윤곽이 잡힌 고래의 옆모습을 언뜻언뜻 스쳐 보게 될 것이다. 그러나 이런 광경을 보려면 당신은 철저한 고래잡이여야만 한다. 그뿐 아니라, 그러한 광경을 다시 보고자 한다면, 처음 섰던 그 자리의 정확한 위도와 경도가 교차하는 지점을 반드시 알아 두어야 한다. 그러지 않으면 이러한 산봉우리 관찰이란 워낙 우연에 좌우되는 것이어서, 정확히 앞서 섰던 그 자리를 다시 찾아내자면 고된 재발견의 수고를 치러야 할 것이기 때문이다. 마치 솔로마 제도가, 한때 높은 주름깃을 단 멘다냐가 그 땅을 밟고 늙은 피게로아가 그 기록을 남겼음에도 여태껏 미지의 섬으로 남아 있는 것과도 같다."]], [["Nor when expandingly lifted by your subject, can you fail to trace out great whales in the starry heavens, and boats in pursuit of them; as when long filled with thoughts of war the Eastern nations saw armies locked in battle among the clouds.", "또한 당신의 주제에 마음이 한껏 부풀어 들려 올라갈 때라면, 별이 총총한 하늘에서 거대한 고래들과 그것들을 뒤쫓는 보트들의 자취를 더듬어 내지 않을 수 없으리라. 마치 오래도록 전쟁 생각에 잠겨 있던 동방의 여러 민족이 구름 사이에서 맞붙어 싸우는 군대를 보았던 것처럼 말이다."], ["Thus at the North have I chased Leviathan round and round the Pole with the revolutions of the bright points that first defined him to me.", "그리하여 나는 북쪽에서, 처음 내게 그 윤곽을 일러 준 밝은 별들의 회전을 좇아, 북극을 돌고 또 돌며 리바이어던을 뒤쫓았다."], ["And beneath the effulgent Antarctic skies I have boarded the Argo-Navis, and joined the chase against the starry Cetus far beyond the utmost stretch of Hydrus and the Flying Fish.", "또 눈부신 남극의 하늘 아래서는 아르고자리에 올라타, 물뱀자리와 날치자리가 닿는 가장 먼 끝조차 한참 넘어선 곳에서 별이 빛나는 고래자리를 뒤쫓는 추격에 가담하였다."]], [["With a frigate’s anchors for my bridle-bitts and fasces of harpoons for spurs, would I could mount that whale and leap the topmost skies, to see whether the fabled heavens with all their countless tents really lie encamped beyond my mortal sight!", "군함의 닻을 재갈로 삼고 작살 묶음을 박차로 삼아, 저 고래에 올라타 가장 높은 하늘로 뛰어오를 수 있다면 얼마나 좋으랴. 그리하여 전설 속의 하늘이, 그 헤아릴 수 없는 천막들을 모조리 거느린 채, 정녕 내 인간의 시야 너머에 진을 치고 있는지 두 눈으로 확인할 수 있다면!"]], [["# CHAPTER 58. Brit.", "# CHAPTER 58. Brit."]], [["Steering north-eastward from the Crozetts, we fell in with vast meadows of brit, the minute, yellow substance, upon which the Right Whale largely feeds.", "크로제트 제도에서 북동쪽으로 키를 잡고 나아가던 우리는, 참고래(Right Whale)가 주로 먹고 사는 미세하고 누런 물질인 브릿(brit)의 광활한 초원과 마주쳤다."], ["For leagues and leagues it undulated round us, so that we seemed to be sailing through boundless fields of ripe and golden wheat.", "그것은 몇 리그를 가도 다시 몇 리그를 가도 우리 주위로 물결치고 있어서, 마치 익어 황금빛으로 물든 끝없는 밀밭 사이를 항해하는 듯했다."]], [["On the second day, numbers of Right Whales were seen, who, secure from the attack of a Sperm Whaler like the Pequod, with open jaws sluggishly swam through the brit, which, adhering to the fringing fibres of that wondrous Venetian blind in their mouths, was in that manner separated from the water that escaped at the lip.", "이튿날 수많은 참고래가 모습을 드러냈는데, 피쿼드(Pequod)호 같은 향유고래잡이배의 공격으로부터 안전한 그놈들은 입을 벌린 채 느릿느릿 브릿 사이를 헤엄쳤다. 브릿은 그 입속에 달린 경이로운 베네치아식 발 같은 술 모양 섬유에 들러붙었고, 그렇게 해서 입술 사이로 빠져나가는 물과 걸러졌다."]], [["As morning mowers, who side by side slowly and seethingly advance their scythes through the long wet grass of marshy meads; even so these monsters swam, making a strange, grassy, cutting sound; and leaving behind them endless swaths of blue upon the yellow sea.*", "마치 아침의 풀 베는 일꾼들이 어깨를 나란히 한 채 늪지 풀밭의 길고 축축한 풀 사이로 천천히, 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 낫을 밀어 나가듯이, 이 괴물들도 그렇게 헤엄치며 기묘하고 풀 베는 듯한 절단음을 냈다. 그러고는 누런 바다 위에 끝없이 푸른 자국을 베어 남겼다.*"]], [["*That part of the sea known among whalemen as the “Brazil Banks” does not bear that name as the Banks of Newfoundland do, because of there being shallows and soundings there, but because of this remarkable meadow-like appearance, caused by the vast drifts of brit continually floating in those latitudes, where the Right Whale is often chased.", "* 고래잡이들 사이에서 \"브라질 둑(Brazil Banks)\"이라 알려진 그 해역은, 뉴펀들랜드 둑처럼 그곳에 얕은 여울과 측심 가능한 바닥이 있어서 그런 이름이 붙은 것이 아니라, 참고래가 자주 쫓기는 그 위도에서 끊임없이 떠다니는 광대한 브릿 무리가 자아내는 이 두드러진 초원 같은 광경 때문에 그렇게 불린다."]], [["But it was only the sound they made as they parted the brit which at all reminded one of mowers.", "그러나 풀 베는 일꾼을 떠올리게 하는 것은 오직 그놈들이 브릿을 가를 때 내는 소리뿐이었다."], ["Seen from the mast-heads, especially when they paused and were stationary for a while, their vast black forms looked more like lifeless masses of rock than anything else.", "돛대 꼭대기에서 내려다보면, 특히 그놈들이 잠시 멈춰 가만히 머물러 있을 때면, 그 거대하고 검은 형체는 그 무엇보다도 생명 없는 바위 덩어리처럼 보였다."], ["And as in the great hunting countries of India, the stranger at a distance will sometimes pass on the plains recumbent elephants without knowing them to be such, taking them for bare, blackened elevations of the soil; even so, often, with him, who for the first time beholds this species of the leviathans of the sea.", "인도의 저 광대한 수렵지에서 멀리서 온 나그네가 들판에 누워 있는 코끼리들을 그것인 줄도 모르고 그저 헐벗고 거뭇한 흙더미로 여기며 지나치곤 하듯이, 이 바다의 리바이어던(Leviathan) 종족을 처음 보는 사람도 흔히 그러하다."], ["And even when recognised at last, their immense magnitude renders it very hard really to believe that such bulky masses of overgrowth can possibly be instinct, in all parts, with the same sort of life that lives in a dog or a horse.", "그리고 마침내 그것이 무엇인지 알아본 뒤에도, 그 어마어마한 크기 때문에 이토록 거대하게 자란 살덩이가 그 온몸 구석구석에 개나 말에게 깃든 것과 같은 생명으로 살아 숨 쉬고 있다는 사실을 정말로 믿기란 무척 어렵다."]], [["Indeed, in other respects, you can hardly regard any creatures of the deep with the same feelings that you do those of the shore.", "실로 다른 점에서도, 깊은 바다의 어떤 생물도 뭍의 생물을 대하는 것과 같은 감정으로 바라보기란 좀처럼 어렵다."], ["For though some old naturalists have maintained that all creatures of the land are of their kind in the sea; and though taking a broad general view of the thing, this may very well be; yet coming to specialties, where, for example, does the ocean furnish any fish that in disposition answers to the sagacious kindness of the dog?", "비록 몇몇 옛 박물학자들이 뭍의 모든 생물에는 바다에 그에 상응하는 종류가 있다고 주장해 왔고, 또 이 문제를 폭넓고 일반적인 관점에서 본다면 충분히 그럴 법도 하지만, 막상 세부로 들어가 보면, 이를테면 바다가 그 기질에 있어 개의 영리한 다정함에 견줄 만한 물고기를 어디 내놓는단 말인가?"], ["The accursed shark alone can in any generic respect be said to bear comparative analogy to him.", "오직 저주받은 상어만이 어떤 일반적인 면에서나마 개에 비교될 만한 유사성을 지녔다고 말할 수 있을 따름이다."]], [["But though, to landsmen in general, the native inhabitants of the seas have ever been regarded with emotions unspeakably unsocial and repelling; though we know the sea to be an everlasting terra incognita, so that Columbus sailed over numberless unknown worlds to discover his one superficial western one; though, by vast odds, the most terrific of all mortal disasters have immemorially and indiscriminately befallen tens and hundreds of thousands of those who have gone upon the waters; though but a moment’s consideration will teach, that however baby man may brag of his science and skill, and however much, in a flattering future, that science and skill may augment; yet for ever and for ever, to the crack of doom, the sea will insult and murder him, and pulverize the stateliest, stiffest frigate he can make; nevertheless, by the continual repetition of these very impressions, man has lost that sense of the full awfulness of the sea which aboriginally belongs to it.", "그러나 비록 일반적으로 뭍사람들에게 바다의 토박이 거주자들이 형언할 수 없으리만치 비사교적이고 거부감을 주는 감정으로 여겨져 왔고, 또 우리가 바다를 영원히 알 수 없는 미지의 땅으로 알고 있어 콜럼버스가 자신의 그 하나의 피상적인 서쪽 세계를 발견하려고 무수한 미지의 세계 위를 항해했으며, 또 헤아릴 수 없이 큰 차이로 모든 인간이 겪는 재난 가운데 가장 무시무시한 것들이 아득한 옛날부터 바다로 나선 수십만 수백만의 사람들에게 가리지 않고 닥쳐왔으며, 또 잠시만 생각해 보아도 어린아이 같은 인간이 제 과학과 기술을 아무리 뽐낸들, 또 흐뭇한 미래에 그 과학과 기술이 아무리 늘어난들, 영원토록, 세상이 끝나는 날까지 바다는 인간을 모욕하고 살해할 것이며 그가 만들 수 있는 가장 위풍당당하고 견고한 군함조차 가루로 빻아 버리리라는 것을 깨닫게 되건만, 그럼에도 불구하고 바로 이런 인상들이 끊임없이 되풀이된 탓에 인간은 본디 바다에 깃들어 있던 그 온전한 두려움의 감각을 잃어버리고 말았다."]], [["The first boat we read of, floated on an ocean, that with Portuguese vengeance had whelmed a whole world without leaving so much as a widow.", "우리가 글에서 읽는 최초의 배는, 포르투갈인의 복수처럼 한 세상 전부를 삼켜 과부 하나조차 남기지 않은 그 대양 위에 떠 있었다."], ["That same ocean rolls now; that same ocean destroyed the wrecked ships of last year.", "그와 똑같은 대양이 지금도 출렁이고 있다. 그와 똑같은 대양이 지난해의 난파선들을 파괴했다."], ["Yea, foolish mortals, Noah’s flood is not yet subsided; two thirds of the fair world it yet covers.", "그렇다, 어리석은 인간들이여, 노아의 홍수는 아직 가라앉지 않았다. 그것은 아직도 이 아름다운 세상의 삼분의 이를 뒤덮고 있다."]], [["Wherein differ the sea and the land, that a miracle upon one is not a miracle upon the other?", "바다와 뭍이 무엇이 다르기에, 한쪽에서 일어난 기적이 다른 쪽에서는 기적이 아니란 말인가?"], ["Preternatural terrors rested upon the Hebrews, when under the feet of Korah and his company the live ground opened and swallowed them up for ever; yet not a modern sun ever sets, but in precisely the same manner the live sea swallows up ships and crews.", "코라(Korah)와 그 무리의 발밑에서 산 땅이 갈라져 그들을 영영 삼켜 버렸을 때, 초자연적인 공포가 히브리인들 위에 내려앉았다. 그러나 오늘날의 해는 단 한 번도 그와 똑같은 방식으로 산 바다가 배와 선원들을 삼켜 버리지 않는 날에 지는 법이 없다."]], [["But not only is the sea such a foe to man who is an alien to it, but it is also a fiend to its own off-spring; worse than the Persian host who murdered his own guests; sparing not the creatures which itself hath spawned.", "그러나 바다는 자신에게 낯선 인간에게만 그런 적인 것이 아니라, 제가 낳은 새끼들에게도 마귀와 같다. 제 손님들을 살해한 페르시아의 군주보다도 더 고약하게, 제 스스로 낳아 놓은 생물들조차 가만두지 않는다."], ["Like a savage tigress that tossing in the jungle overlays her own cubs, so the sea dashes even the mightiest whales against the rocks, and leaves them there side by side with the split wrecks of ships.", "정글에서 몸을 뒤척이다 제 새끼들을 깔아뭉개는 사나운 암호랑이처럼, 바다는 가장 힘센 고래들조차 바위에 내동댕이쳐서 갈라진 난파선 잔해와 나란히 거기 버려둔다."], ["No mercy, no power but its own controls it.", "그 어떤 자비도, 그 자신 말고는 그 어떤 힘도 바다를 다스리지 못한다."], ["Panting and snorting like a mad battle steed that has lost its rider, the masterless ocean overruns the globe.", "제 기수를 잃은 미쳐 날뛰는 군마처럼 헐떡이고 콧김을 내뿜으며, 주인 없는 대양이 온 지구를 짓밟고 다닌다."]], [["Consider the subtleness of the sea; how its most dreaded creatures glide under water, unapparent for the most part, and treacherously hidden beneath the loveliest tints of azure.", "바다의 교활함을 생각해 보라. 그 가장 무서운 생물들이 대개는 보이지도 않게 물속을 미끄러져 다니며, 더없이 아름다운 쪽빛의 빛깔 아래 음흉하게 몸을 숨기고 있음을."], ["Consider also the devilish brilliance and beauty of many of its most remorseless tribes, as the dainty embellished shape of many species of sharks.", "또한 그 가장 무자비한 무리들 가운데 많은 것들이 지닌, 여러 종의 상어가 보여 주는 우아하게 꾸민 자태처럼 악마적인 광채와 아름다움을 생각해 보라."], ["Consider, once more, the universal cannibalism of the sea; all whose creatures prey upon each other, carrying on eternal war since the world began.", "다시 한번, 바다의 보편적인 동족상잔을 생각해 보라. 그 모든 생물이 서로를 잡아먹으며 세상이 시작된 이래로 영원한 전쟁을 이어 오고 있음을."]], [["Consider all this; and then turn to this green, gentle, and most docile earth; consider them both, the sea and the land; and do you not find a strange analogy to something in yourself?", "이 모든 것을 생각해 보라. 그런 다음 이 푸르고 온화하며 더없이 순한 땅으로 눈을 돌려 보라."], ["For as this appalling ocean surrounds the verdant land, so in the soul of man there lies one insular Tahiti, full of peace and joy, but encompassed by all the horrors of the half known life.", "바다와 뭍, 그 둘을 함께 생각해 보라. 그러면 그대 자신 안의 무언가와 닮은 기묘한 유사성을 발견하지 않겠는가?"], ["God keep thee!", "이 소름 끼치는 대양이 푸르른 땅을 에워싸고 있듯이, 인간의 영혼 속에도 평화와 기쁨으로 가득 찬 하나의 외딴 타히티 섬이 자리하고 있되, 그것은 절반밖에 알려지지 않은 삶의 온갖 공포에 둘러싸여 있으니."], ["Push not off from that isle, thou canst never return!", "하느님(God)께서 그대를 지켜 주시기를! 그 섬에서 배를 띄워 떠나지 말라, 그대는 결코 돌아오지 못하리니!"]], [["# CHAPTER 59. Squid.", "# CHAPTER 59. Squid."]], [["Slowly wading through the meadows of brit, the Pequod still held on her way north-eastward towards the island of Java; a gentle air impelling her keel, so that in the surrounding serenity her three tall tapering masts mildly waved to that languid breeze, as three mild palms on a plain.", "천천히 브릿(brit)의 초원을 헤치고 나아가며, 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 여전히 자바섬을 향해 북동쪽으로 항로를 잡고 있었다. 부드러운 바람이 용골을 밀어 주어, 사방의 고요 속에서 가늘게 뻗어 오른 세 개의 높다란 돛대가 그 나른한 미풍에 맞추어 평원에 선 세 그루의 온순한 야자수처럼 잔잔히 흔들렸다."], ["And still, at wide intervals in the silvery night, the lonely, alluring jet would be seen.", "그리고 여전히, 은빛으로 빛나는 밤이면 넓은 간격을 두고서 외롭고도 사람을 홀리는 물줄기가 보이곤 했다."]], [["But one transparent blue morning, when a stillness almost preternatural spread over the sea, however unattended with any stagnant calm; when the long burnished sun-glade on the waters seemed a golden finger laid across them, enjoining some secrecy; when the slippered waves whispered together as they softly ran on; in this profound hush of the visible sphere a strange spectre was seen by Daggoo from the main-mast-head.", "그러나 어느 투명하고 푸른 아침, 거의 초자연적이라 할 만한 정적이 바다 위에 깔렸으나 그렇다고 무거운 무풍의 정체는 아니었던 그때, 수면 위로 길게 늘어선 윤나는 햇살의 띠가 마치 어떤 비밀을 명하듯 바다를 가로질러 놓인 황금 손가락처럼 보였던 그때, 부드럽게 미끄러져 가는 물결들이 사뿐히 흐르며 서로 속삭이던 그때, 눈에 보이는 천지의 이 깊은 침묵 속에서 대구(Daggoo)가 큰 돛대 꼭대기에서 기이한 유령을 보았다."]], [["In the distance, a great white mass lazily rose, and rising higher and higher, and disentangling itself from the azure, at last gleamed before our prow like a snow-slide, new slid from the hills.", "저 멀리, 거대한 흰 덩어리가 나른하게 솟아오르더니, 점점 더 높이 올라와 짙푸른 바다에서 제 몸을 풀어내며, 마침내 우리 뱃머리 앞에서 언덕에서 갓 미끄러져 내린 눈사태처럼 번쩍였다."], ["Thus glistening for a moment, as slowly it subsided, and sank.", "그렇게 한순간 번득이다가, 천천히 가라앉으며 물속으로 잠겨 들었다."], ["Then once more arose, and silently gleamed.", "그러더니 다시 한번 떠올라 소리 없이 빛났다."], ["It seemed not a whale; and yet is this Moby Dick?", "그것은 고래 같지 않았다. 하지만 이것이 모비 딕(Moby Dick)인가?"], ["thought Daggoo.", "대구는 생각했다."], ["Again the phantom went down, but on re-appearing once more, with a stiletto-like cry that startled every man from his nod, the negro yelled out—“There!", "다시금 그 유령은 가라앉았으나, 또 한 번 떠오르자, 단검처럼 날카로운 외침으로 졸고 있던 모든 사람을 화들짝 깨우며 그 흑인은 고함쳤다. \"저기!"], ["there again!", "저기 또!"], ["there she breaches!", "솟구쳐 오른다!"], ["right ahead!", "바로 앞이다!"], ["The White Whale, the White Whale!”", "흰 고래다, 흰 고래야!\""]], [["Upon this, the seamen rushed to the yard-arms, as in swarming-time the bees rush to the boughs.", "이 말에 선원들은, 분봉 철에 벌들이 가지로 몰려들듯, 활대 끝으로 우르르 달려갔다."], ["Bare-headed in the sultry sun, Ahab stood on the bowsprit, and with one hand pushed far behind in readiness to wave his orders to the helmsman, cast his eager glance in the direction indicated aloft by the outstretched motionless arm of Daggoo.", "무더운 햇볕 아래 모자도 쓰지 않은 채, 에이해브(Ahab)는 제1사장(斜檣) 위에 서서, 키잡이에게 명령을 흔들어 전할 채비로 한 손을 등 뒤로 한껏 젖힌 채, 위쪽에서 대구가 쭉 뻗은 미동 없는 팔로 가리킨 방향으로 간절한 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["Whether the flitting attendance of the one still and solitary jet had gradually worked upon Ahab, so that he was now prepared to connect the ideas of mildness and repose with the first sight of the particular whale he pursued; however this was, or whether his eagerness betrayed him; whichever way it might have been, no sooner did he distinctly perceive the white mass, than with a quick intensity he instantly gave orders for lowering.", "홀로 외롭게 떠다니며 따라붙던 그 잔잔한 물줄기가 차츰 에이해브의 마음에 작용을 일으켜, 이제 그가 자신이 쫓는 바로 그 고래를 처음 보는 순간에 온순함과 안식이라는 관념을 결부시킬 채비가 되어 있었던 것인지, 그것이 어떠했든 간에, 혹은 그의 조급함이 그를 속였던 것인지, 그 어느 쪽이었든 간에, 그가 그 흰 덩어리를 또렷이 알아보자마자, 그는 즉시 격렬한 기세로 보트를 내리라는 명령을 내렸다."]], [["The four boats were soon on the water; Ahab’s in advance, and all swiftly pulling towards their prey.", "네 척의 보트가 곧 물 위에 떴다. 에이해브의 것이 앞장섰고, 모두가 잽싸게 먹잇감을 향해 노를 저었다."], ["Soon it went down, and while, with oars suspended, we were awaiting its reappearance, lo!", "곧 그것이 가라앉았고, 우리가 노를 멈춘 채 그것이 다시 나타나기를 기다리고 있는데, 보라!"], ["in the same spot where it sank, once more it slowly rose.", "그것이 잠겼던 바로 그 자리에서, 또 한 번 천천히 솟아올랐다."], ["Almost forgetting for the moment all thoughts of Moby Dick, we now gazed at the most wondrous phenomenon which the secret seas have hitherto revealed to mankind.", "한순간 모비 딕에 관한 모든 생각조차 거의 잊어버린 채, 우리는 이제 비밀의 바다가 여태껏 인류에게 드러내 보인 가장 경이로운 현상을 응시했다."], ["A vast pulpy mass, furlongs in length and breadth, of a glancing cream-colour, lay floating on the water, innumerable long arms radiating from its centre, and curling and twisting like a nest of anacondas, as if blindly to clutch at any hapless object within reach.", "길이와 너비가 몇 펄롱(furlong)에 이르는, 반짝이는 크림빛의 거대한 물컹한 덩어리가 수면 위에 둥둥 떠 있었고, 그 중심에서 수없이 많은 긴 팔들이 사방으로 뻗어 나와, 마치 손에 닿는 어떤 가엾은 것이든 무턱대고 움켜쥐려는 듯, 한 무리의 아나콘다처럼 휘감기고 비틀렸다."], ["No perceptible face or front did it have; no conceivable token of either sensation or instinct; but undulated there on the billows, an unearthly, formless, chance-like apparition of life.", "알아볼 만한 얼굴도 앞면도 없었고, 감각이나 본능이라 할 만한 어떤 짐작 가는 표시도 없었으며, 다만 거기 물결 위에서 일렁이고 있을 뿐이었으니, 이 세상 것 같지 않고 형체 없으며 우연처럼 나타난 생명의 환영이었다."]], [["As with a low sucking sound it slowly disappeared again, Starbuck still gazing at the agitated waters where it had sunk, with a wild voice exclaimed—“Almost rather had I seen Moby Dick and fought him, than to have seen thee, thou white ghost!”", "낮게 빨아들이는 듯한 소리를 내며 그것이 다시금 천천히 사라지자, 스타벅(Starbuck)은 그것이 가라앉은 곳의 일렁이는 물을 여전히 바라보며 거친 목소리로 외쳤다. \"차라리 모비 딕을 보고 그놈과 싸우는 편이, 너를 보는 것보다 나았을 것이다, 이 흰 유령아!\""]], [["“What was it, Sir?”", "\"그게 뭐였습니까, 선장님?\""], ["said Flask.", "플래스크(Flask)가 물었다."]], [["“The great live squid, which, they say, few whale-ships ever beheld, and returned to their ports to tell of it.”", "\"저 거대한 살아 있는 오징어다. 사람들 말로는, 그것을 보고도 항구로 돌아와 그 이야기를 전한 고래잡이배가 몇 척 없다더군.\""]], [["But Ahab said nothing; turning his boat, he sailed back to the vessel; the rest as silently following.", "그러나 에이해브는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 보트를 돌려 그는 다시 본선으로 돌아갔고, 나머지도 마찬가지로 말없이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["Whatever superstitions the sperm whalemen in general have connected with the sight of this object, certain it is, that a glimpse of it being so very unusual, that circumstance has gone far to invest it with portentousness.", "향유고래(Sperm) 잡이들이 일반적으로 이 물체를 본 일에 어떤 미신들을 결부시켜 왔든 간에, 분명한 것은 그것을 언뜻 보는 일조차 너무도 드문지라, 그 사정이 그것을 불길한 전조로 둘러싸는 데 크게 한몫했다는 점이다."], ["So rarely is it beheld, that though one and all of them declare it to be the largest animated thing in the ocean, yet very few of them have any but the most vague ideas concerning its true nature and form; notwithstanding, they believe it to furnish to the sperm whale his only food.", "그것이 워낙 드물게 목격되는 까닭에, 그들 모두가 한결같이 그것을 바다에서 가장 거대한 살아 있는 것이라 단언하면서도, 그것의 참된 본성과 형체에 관해서는 더없이 막연한 관념밖에 지니지 못한 자가 대부분이다. 그럼에도 그들은 그것이 향유고래에게 유일한 먹이를 대 준다고 믿는다."], ["For though other species of whales find their food above water, and may be seen by man in the act of feeding, the spermaceti whale obtains his whole food in unknown zones below the surface; and only by inference is it that any one can tell of what, precisely, that food consists.", "다른 종류의 고래들은 수면 위에서 먹이를 찾으므로 사람이 그것들이 먹이를 먹는 모습을 볼 수 있지만, 향유고래는 그 먹이를 모조리 수면 아래 알려지지 않은 영역에서 구하기에, 그 먹이가 정확히 무엇으로 이루어졌는지는 오로지 추론으로만 누군가 말할 수 있을 따름이다."], ["At times, when closely pursued, he will disgorge what are supposed to be the detached arms of the squid; some of them thus exhibited exceeding twenty and thirty feet in length.", "때때로 바싹 쫓기면 그놈은 오징어의 떨어져 나온 팔로 짐작되는 것들을 토해 내는데, 그렇게 드러난 것들 가운데 더러는 길이가 20피트, 30피트(약 6미터, 9미터)를 넘는 것도 있다."], ["They fancy that the monster to which these arms belonged ordinarily clings by them to the bed of the ocean; and that the sperm whale, unlike other species, is supplied with teeth in order to attack and tear it.", "그들은 이 팔들이 달린 괴물이 평소 그 팔들로 바다 밑바닥에 들러붙어 있으며, 향유고래는 다른 종들과 달리 그것을 공격해 찢어발기기 위해 이빨을 갖추고 있는 것이라 짐작한다."]], [["There seems some ground to imagine that the great Kraken of Bishop Pontoppodan may ultimately resolve itself into Squid.", "폰토피단(Pontoppodan) 주교가 말한 저 거대한 크라켄(Kraken)이 끝내 오징어로 귀결되리라 상상할 만한 어떤 근거가 있는 듯하다."], ["The manner in which the Bishop describes it, as alternately rising and sinking, with some other particulars he narrates, in all this the two correspond.", "주교가 그것을 번갈아 떠올랐다 가라앉았다 하는 것으로 묘사한 방식이며, 그가 서술한 그 밖의 몇몇 세부 사항이며, 이 모든 점에서 둘은 들어맞는다."], ["But much abatement is necessary with respect to the incredible bulk he assigns it.", "다만 그가 그것에 부여한 믿기 어려운 거구에 관해서는 상당한 에누리가 필요하다."]], [["By some naturalists who have vaguely heard rumors of the mysterious creature, here spoken of, it is included among the class of cuttle-fish, to which, indeed, in certain external respects it would seem to belong, but only as the Anak of the tribe.", "여기서 말한 이 신비로운 생물에 관한 소문을 어렴풋이 전해 들은 어떤 박물학자들은 그것을 갑오징어 부류에 포함시키는데, 과연 어떤 외형적 면에서는 거기에 속하는 듯이 보이기는 하나, 그저 그 종족의 아낙(Anak)으로서일 뿐이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 60. The Line.", "# CHAPTER 60. The Line."]], [["With reference to the whaling scene shortly to be described, as well as for the better understanding of all similar scenes elsewhere presented, I have here to speak of the magical, sometimes horrible whale-line.", "곧 묘사하게 될 포경 장면과 관련하여, 또한 다른 곳에서 펼쳐질 그와 비슷한 모든 장면을 더 잘 이해할 수 있도록, 나는 여기서 그 마법 같고 때로는 끔찍한 고래 밧줄에 관해 이야기해 두어야겠다."]], [["The line originally used in the fishery was of the best hemp, slightly vapored with tar, not impregnated with it, as in the case of ordinary ropes; for while tar, as ordinarily used, makes the hemp more pliable to the rope-maker, and also renders the rope itself more convenient to the sailor for common ship use; yet, not only would the ordinary quantity too much stiffen the whale-line for the close coiling to which it must be subjected; but as most seamen are beginning to learn, tar in general by no means adds to the rope’s durability or strength, however much it may give it compactness and gloss.", "포경업에서 본래 쓰이던 밧줄은 최상품 삼(麻)으로 만든 것으로, 보통 밧줄처럼 타르를 흠뻑 먹인 것이 아니라 살짝 김을 쐬듯 발라 둔 정도였다. 타르를 통상적으로 쓰면 밧줄 제작자에게는 삼이 더 잘 휘어지게 해 주고, 또 선원이 배에서 일상적으로 쓰기에도 더 편리하게 해 주지만, 통상적인 분량을 쓰면 고래 밧줄이 너무 뻣뻣해져서, 그것이 반드시 거쳐야 하는 빽빽한 사리기에 부적합해질 뿐 아니라, 대다수 선원들이 차츰 깨닫기 시작하듯이, 타르는 밧줄에 단단함과 윤기를 더해 줄지언정 그 내구성이나 강도를 높여 주는 일은 결코 없기 때문이다."]], [["Of late years the Manilla rope has in the American fishery almost entirely superseded hemp as a material for whale-lines; for, though not so durable as hemp, it is stronger, and far more soft and elastic; and I will add (since there is an æsthetics in all things), is much more handsome and becoming to the boat, than hemp.", "근래에 들어 미국 포경업에서는 마닐라 밧줄이 고래 밧줄의 재료로서 삼을 거의 완전히 대신하게 되었다. 마닐라 밧줄은 삼만큼 오래가지는 못하지만 더 튼튼하고, 훨씬 더 부드럽고 탄력이 있기 때문이다."], ["Hemp is a dusky, dark fellow, a sort of Indian; but Manilla is as a golden-haired Circassian to behold.", "게다가 (모든 것에는 나름의 미학이 깃들어 있으니) 한마디 덧붙이자면, 그것은 삼보다 훨씬 보기 좋고 보트에도 잘 어울린다. 삼은 거무튀튀하고 어두운 친구로 일종의 인디언 같다면, 마닐라는 보기에 금발의 체르케스 여인 같다."]], [["The whale-line is only two-thirds of an inch in thickness.", "고래 밧줄의 굵기는 겨우 3분의 2인치에 지나지 않는다."], ["At first sight, you would not think it so strong as it really is.", "언뜻 보아서는 그것이 실제만큼 그렇게 튼튼하리라 여기지 못할 것이다."], ["By experiment its one and fifty yarns will each suspend a weight of one hundred and twenty pounds; so that the whole rope will bear a strain nearly equal to three tons.", "실험에 따르면, 그것을 이루는 쉰한 가닥의 실은 저마다 120파운드의 무게를 견뎌 내니, 밧줄 전체로는 거의 3톤에 맞먹는 장력을 버틸 수 있다."], ["In length, the common sperm whale-line measures something over two hundred fathoms.", "길이로 말하자면, 보통의 향유고래 밧줄은 200패덤 남짓 된다."], ["Towards the stern of the boat it is spirally coiled away in the tub, not like the worm-pipe of a still though, but so as to form one round, cheese-shaped mass of densely bedded “sheaves,” or layers of concentric spiralizations, without any hollow but the “heart,”", "보트의 고물 쪽에서 그것은 통 속에 나선형으로 사려져 있는데, 증류기의 나선관처럼 감겨 있는 것이 아니라, 빽빽하게 포개진 \"가닥들\", 곧 동심원을 그리며 나선으로 쌓인 층들이 둥근 치즈 모양의 한 덩어리를 이루도록 사려져 있으며, 그 한가운데에는 치즈의 축에 해당하는 자리에 생긴 \"심장\", 곧 작고 수직으로 뚫린 관 외에는 빈 곳이 없다."], ["or minute vertical tube formed at the axis of the cheese. As the least tangle or kink in the coiling would, in running out, infallibly take somebody’s arm, leg, or entire body off, the utmost precaution is used in stowing the line in its tub.", "사려 놓은 밧줄에 조금이라도 엉킴이나 꼬임이 있으면, 밧줄이 풀려 나갈 때 누군가의 팔이나 다리, 혹은 온몸을 영락없이 앗아 가 버릴 것이므로, 통 속에 밧줄을 갈무리할 때는 더없이 세심한 주의를 기울인다."], ["Some harpooneers will consume almost an entire morning in this business, carrying the line high aloft and then reeving it downwards through a block towards the tub, so as in the act of coiling to free it from all possible wrinkles and twists.", "어떤 작살잡이들은 이 일에 거의 한나절을 다 쓰기도 하는데, 밧줄을 높이 들어 올린 다음 도르래를 통해 아래쪽 통을 향해 늘어뜨려, 사리는 과정에서 일어날 수 있는 모든 주름과 뒤틀림을 없애는 것이다."]], [["In the English boats two tubs are used instead of one; the same line being continuously coiled in both tubs.", "영국 보트에서는 통을 하나가 아니라 둘을 쓰는데, 같은 밧줄을 두 통에 연이어 사려 둔다. 여기에는 어느 정도 이점이 있다."], ["There is some advantage in this; because these twin-tubs being so small they fit more readily into the boat, and do not strain it so much; whereas, the American tub, nearly three feet in diameter and of proportionate depth, makes a rather bulky freight for a craft whose planks are but one half-inch in thickness; for the bottom of the whale-boat is like critical ice, which will bear up a considerable distributed weight, but not very much of a concentrated one.", "이 쌍둥이 통은 워낙 작아서 보트에 더 수월하게 들어맞고, 보트에 무리를 덜 주기 때문이다. 반면 미국식 통은 지름이 거의 3피트에 깊이도 그에 걸맞아서, 널빤지 두께가 겨우 반 인치밖에 안 되는 작은 배에는 제법 부피가 큰 짐이 된다."], ["When the painted canvas cover is clapped on the American line-tub, the boat looks as if it were pulling off with a prodigious great wedding-cake to present to the whales.", "고래잡이 보트의 밑바닥은 살얼음과도 같아서, 고루 분산된 무게라면 상당량을 떠받칠 수 있어도 한곳에 집중된 무게는 그리 많이 견디지 못하기 때문이다. 미국식 밧줄 통에 칠한 캔버스 덮개를 씌워 놓으면, 보트는 마치 고래들에게 선사하려고 어마어마하게 큰 결혼식 케이크를 싣고 노 저어 나가는 것처럼 보인다."]], [["Both ends of the line are exposed; the lower end terminating in an eye-splice or loop coming up from the bottom against the side of the tub, and hanging over its edge completely disengaged from everything.", "밧줄의 양쪽 끝은 모두 밖으로 드러나 있다. 아래쪽 끝은 고리매듭, 곧 통 바닥에서부터 옆면을 타고 올라와 가장자리에 걸쳐진 채 그 무엇에도 매이지 않고 늘어진 올가미 모양으로 끝난다."], ["This arrangement of the lower end is necessary on two accounts.", "아래쪽 끝을 이렇게 해 두는 데에는 두 가지 이유가 있다."], ["First: In order to facilitate the fastening to it of an additional line from a neighboring boat, in case the stricken whale should sound so deep as to threaten to carry off the entire line originally attached to the harpoon.", "첫째, 작살 맞은 고래가 너무 깊이 잠수하여, 본래 작살에 매여 있던 밧줄 전체를 끌고 가 버릴 위험이 생길 경우, 이웃한 보트에서 가져온 여분의 밧줄을 거기에 손쉽게 잇기 위함이다."], ["In these instances, the whale of course is shifted like a mug of ale, as it were, from the one boat to the other; though the first boat always hovers at hand to assist its consort.", "이런 경우 고래는, 말하자면 맥주 한 잔이 옮겨 가듯 한 보트에서 다른 보트로 넘겨지는데, 그래도 처음 보트는 늘 곁에 머물며 짝패 보트를 돕는다."], ["Second: This arrangement is indispensable for common safety’s sake; for were the lower end of the line in any way attached to the boat, and were the whale then to run the line out to the end almost in a single, smoking minute as he sometimes does, he would not stop there, for the doomed boat would infallibly be dragged down after him into the profundity of the sea; and in that case no town-crier would ever find her again.", "둘째, 이런 처리는 공동의 안전을 위해 없어서는 안 된다. 밧줄의 아래쪽 끝이 어떤 식으로든 보트에 매여 있다면, 그리고 고래가 이따금 그러하듯 김을 내뿜으며 단 한 순간 만에 밧줄을 끝까지 풀어 내 버린다면, 고래는 거기서 멈추지 않을 것이고, 운명 지어진 그 보트는 영락없이 고래를 따라 바다 깊은 심연 속으로 끌려 내려갈 것이며, 그렇게 되면 그 어떤 마을 포고꾼도 그 보트를 두 번 다시 찾아내지 못할 것이기 때문이다."]], [["Before lowering the boat for the chase, the upper end of the line is taken aft from the tub, and passing round the loggerhead there, is again carried forward the entire length of the boat, resting crosswise upon the loom or handle of every man’s oar, so that it jogs against his wrist in rowing; and also passing between the men, as they alternately sit at the opposite gunwales, to the leaded chocks or grooves in the extreme pointed prow of the boat, where a wooden pin or skewer the size of a common quill, prevents it from slipping out.", "추격을 위해 보트를 내리기 전에, 밧줄의 위쪽 끝을 통에서 빼내어 고물 쪽으로 가져가, 거기 있는 밧줄걸이 기둥을 한 바퀴 돌린 다음, 다시 보트 전체 길이만큼 앞으로 끌어가는데, 모든 사람의 노 손잡이 위에 가로질러 얹혀, 노를 저을 때마다 그의 손목에 부딪치게 된다. 또한 양쪽 뱃전에 번갈아 앉은 사람들 사이를 지나, 보트의 뾰족한 이물 맨 끝에 있는 납을 댄 홈 받침에 이르는데, 거기서는 보통 깃펜만 한 굵기의 나무못이나 꼬챙이가 밧줄이 빠져나가지 못하게 막아 준다."], ["From the chocks it hangs in a slight festoon over the bows, and is then passed inside the boat again; and some ten or twenty fathoms (called box-line) being coiled upon the box in the bows, it continues its way to the gunwale still a little further aft, and is then attached to the short-warp—the rope which is immediately connected with the harpoon; but previous to that connexion, the short-warp goes through sundry mystifications too tedious to detail.", "그 받침에서 밧줄은 이물 위로 살짝 늘어진 곡선을 그리며 걸쳐졌다가 다시 보트 안쪽으로 들어오고, 그중 열 패덤 내지 스무 패덤가량(상자 밧줄이라 불린다)이 이물의 상자 위에 사려진 다음, 조금 더 고물 쪽 뱃전을 향해 이어지다가 거기서 짧은 줄, 곧 작살과 곧바로 연결되는 밧줄에 매인다. 다만 그렇게 연결되기에 앞서, 짧은 줄은 일일이 늘어놓기에는 너무 번거로운 갖가지 복잡한 처리 과정을 거친다."]], [["Thus the whale-line folds the whole boat in its complicated coils, twisting and writhing around it in almost every direction.", "이리하여 고래 밧줄은 그 얽히고설킨 사림으로 보트 전체를 감싸 안고, 거의 모든 방향에서 보트를 휘감으며 뒤틀린다. 모든 노잡이가 그 위태로운 뒤틀림 속에 휘말려 들어, 뭍사람의 겁먹은 눈에는 그들이 마치 가장 치명적인 뱀들을 제 팔다리에 장난스레 늘어뜨린 인디언 곡예사들처럼 보인다."], ["All the oarsmen are involved in its perilous contortions; so that to the timid eye of the landsman, they seem as Indian jugglers, with the deadliest snakes sportively festooning their limbs.", "죽을 운명을 타고난 어떤 여인의 아들이든, 처음으로 그 삼 밧줄의 미궁 한복판에 앉아 노에 온 힘을 쏟는 가운데, 언제 어느 순간에 작살이 던져지고 이 끔찍한 뒤틀림들이 동그라미 친 번개처럼 한꺼번에 작동하기 시작할지 모른다는 생각에 잠겨 보라. 그런 처지에 놓이고서 어찌 몸서리를 치지 않을 수 있겠는가."], ["Nor can any son of mortal woman, for the first time, seat himself amid those hempen intricacies, and while straining his utmost at the oar, bethink him that at any unknown instant the harpoon may be darted, and all these horrible contortions be put in play like ringed lightnings; he cannot be thus circumstanced without a shudder that makes the very marrow in his bones to quiver in him like a shaken jelly.", "그 몸서리는 뼛속 골수마저 흔들린 젤리처럼 떨리게 만든다. 그러나 습관이란 — 참으로 기이한 것이다!"], ["Yet habit—strange thing!", "습관이 이루어 내지 못하는 일이 어디 있으랴!"], ["what cannot habit accomplish?—Gayer sallies, more merry mirth, better jokes, and brighter repartees, you never heard over your mahogany, than you will hear over the half-inch white cedar of the whale-boat, when thus hung in hangman’s nooses; and, like the six burghers of Calais before King Edward, the six men composing the crew pull into the jaws of death, with a halter around every neck, as you may say.", "— 그대가 마호가니 식탁에 둘러앉아 들어 본 그 어떤 것보다도, 이렇게 교수형 올가미를 두른 채 반 인치짜리 흰 삼나무 보트에 매달려 있을 때, 더 흥겨운 농지거리와 더 명랑한 웃음, 더 재미난 농담과 더 재치 있는 응수를 듣게 될 것이다. 그리하여, 에드워드 왕 앞에 선 칼레의 여섯 시민처럼, 보트의 선원을 이루는 여섯 사내는 말하자면 저마다 목에 올가미를 두른 채로 죽음의 아가리 속을 향해 노를 저어 들어가는 것이다."]], [["Perhaps a very little thought will now enable you to account for those repeated whaling disasters—some few of which are casually chronicled—of this man or that man being taken out of the boat by the line, and lost.", "이제 조금만 생각해 보면, 그대는 저 거듭되는 포경 참사들 — 그중 몇몇은 우연히 기록으로 남았는데, 이 사람 혹은 저 사람이 밧줄에 끌려 보트 밖으로 내동댕이쳐져 목숨을 잃었다는 이야기들 — 을 능히 헤아릴 수 있을 것이다."], ["For, when the line is darting out, to be seated then in the boat, is like being seated in the midst of the manifold whizzings of a steam-engine in full play, when every flying beam, and shaft, and wheel, is grazing you.", "밧줄이 쏜살같이 풀려 나가는 동안 보트에 앉아 있다는 것은, 모든 날아다니는 들보와 축과 바퀴가 그대를 스치며 돌아가는, 한창 가동 중인 증기기관의 갖가지 윙윙거림 한복판에 앉아 있는 것과 같다."], ["It is worse; for you cannot sit motionless in the heart of these perils, because the boat is rocking like a cradle, and you are pitched one way and the other, without the slightest warning; and only by a certain self-adjusting buoyancy and simultaneousness of volition and action, can you escape being made a Mazeppa of, and run away with where the all-seeing sun himself could never pierce you out.", "아니, 그보다 더하다. 보트가 요람처럼 흔들리는 통에 그대는 이 위험들의 한가운데에서 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있을 수가 없고, 아무런 예고도 없이 이쪽저쪽으로 내던져지며, 오직 일종의 스스로를 가다듬는 균형 감각과, 의지와 행동의 동시성에 의해서만, 마제파 신세가 되어 그 모든 것을 굽어보는 태양조차 결코 그대를 찾아낼 수 없는 곳으로 끌려가는 일을 면할 수 있을 따름이다."]], [["Again: as the profound calm which only apparently precedes and prophesies of the storm, is perhaps more awful than the storm itself; for, indeed, the calm is but the wrapper and envelope of the storm; and contains it in itself, as the seemingly harmless rifle holds the fatal powder, and the ball, and the explosion; so the graceful repose of the line, as it silently serpentines about the oarsmen before being brought into actual play—this is a thing which carries more of true terror than any other aspect of this dangerous affair.", "또한, 폭풍에 겉으로만 앞서며 그것을 예고하는 듯한 그 깊은 고요가 어쩌면 폭풍 그 자체보다 더 무서운 것이듯 — 실로 그 고요란 폭풍을 감싼 포장지요 봉투에 지나지 않으며, 겉으로 무해해 보이는 소총이 치명적인 화약과 탄알과 폭발을 품고 있듯, 그 고요는 폭풍을 제 안에 품고 있으니 — 그와 마찬가지로, 밧줄이 실제로 작동하기 전에 노잡이들 둘레를 소리 없이 뱀처럼 휘감으며 짓는 그 우아한 정적, 이것이야말로 이 위험천만한 일의 그 어떤 면모보다도 더한 진정한 공포를 품고 있다. 그러나 더 말해 무엇 하랴?"], ["But why say more? All men live enveloped in whale-lines.", "모든 인간은 고래 밧줄에 감싸인 채 살아간다."], ["All are born with halters round their necks; but it is only when caught in the swift, sudden turn of death, that mortals realize the silent, subtle, ever-present perils of life.", "모두가 목에 올가미를 두른 채 태어난다. 다만 죽음의 그 빠르고 갑작스러운 회전에 사로잡힐 때라야, 인간은 비로소 삶의 소리 없고 은밀하며 늘 곁에 있는 위험들을 깨닫는 것이다."], ["And if you be a philosopher, though seated in the whale-boat, you would not at heart feel one whit more of terror, than though seated before your evening fire with a poker, and not a harpoon, by your side.", "그리고 만일 그대가 철학자라면, 고래잡이 보트에 앉아 있다 한들, 그대 곁에 작살이 아니라 부지깽이를 두고 저녁 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있을 때와 견주어, 마음속으로 느끼는 공포가 터럭만큼도 더하지 않을 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 61. Stubb Kills a Whale.", "# CHAPTER 61. Stubb Kills a Whale."]], [["If to Starbuck the apparition of the Squid was a thing of portents, to Queequeg it was quite a different object.", "스타벅에게 오징어의 출현이 흉조였다면, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)에게 그것은 전혀 다른 의미를 지닌 것이었다."]], [["“When you see him ’quid,”", "\"오징어 보이면,\""], ["said the savage, honing his harpoon in the bow of his hoisted boat, “then you quick see him ’parm whale.”", "그 야만인은 끌어 올린 보트 뱃머리에서 작살을 갈며 말했다. \"곧 향유고래 본다, 빨리.\""]], [["The next day was exceedingly still and sultry, and with nothing special to engage them, the Pequod’s crew could hardly resist the spell of sleep induced by such a vacant sea.", "이튿날은 유난히 고요하고 무더웠다. 딱히 할 일이 없었던 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 선원들은 그토록 텅 빈 바다가 자아내는 졸음의 마력을 좀처럼 떨쳐 낼 수 없었다."], ["For this part of the Indian Ocean through which we then were voyaging is not what whalemen call a lively ground; that is, it affords fewer glimpses of porpoises, dolphins, flying-fish, and other vivacious denizens of more stirring waters, than those off the Rio de la Plata, or the in-shore ground off Peru.", "우리가 그때 항해하고 있던 인도양의 이 해역은 고래잡이들이 이른바 활기찬 어장이라 부르는 곳이 아니었다. 다시 말해, 리오데라플라타 앞바다나 페루 연안 어장에 비해, 더 출렁이는 바다에 사는 쇠돌고래나 돌고래, 날치, 그 밖의 활달한 주민들을 볼 기회가 훨씬 드문 곳이었다."]], [["It was my turn to stand at the foremast-head; and with my shoulders leaning against the slackened royal shrouds, to and fro I idly swayed in what seemed an enchanted air. No resolution could withstand it; in that dreamy mood losing all consciousness, at last my soul went out of my body; though my body still continued to sway as a pendulum will, long after the power which first moved it is withdrawn.", "앞돛대 꼭대기에 올라가 망을 볼 차례가 내게 돌아왔다. 나는 느슨하게 풀린 윗돛 받침줄에 어깨를 기댄 채, 마법에 걸린 듯한 대기 속에서 멍하니 앞뒤로 흔들거렸다. 그 어떤 결심으로도 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 없었다. 그 몽롱한 기분 속에서 나는 의식을 모두 잃었고, 마침내 내 영혼이 육체를 빠져나갔다. 하지만 내 육체는, 처음 그것을 움직인 힘이 거두어진 지 한참이 지난 뒤에도 진자처럼 계속 흔들리고 있었다."]], [["Ere forgetfulness altogether came over me, I had noticed that the seamen at the main and mizzen-mast-heads were already drowsy.", "망각이 완전히 나를 덮치기 전에, 나는 큰돛대와 뒷돛대 꼭대기의 선원들이 이미 졸고 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["So that at last all three of us lifelessly swung from the spars, and for every swing that we made there was a nod from below from the slumbering helmsman.", "그리하여 마침내 우리 셋은 모두 활대에 매달려 생기 없이 흔들렸고, 우리가 한 번 흔들릴 때마다 저 아래에서 졸고 있던 키잡이의 고갯짓이 화답했다."], ["The waves, too, nodded their indolent crests; and across the wide trance of the sea, east nodded to west, and the sun over all.", "파도 또한 그 나른한 물마루를 끄덕였고, 바다의 드넓은 혼곤함을 가로질러 동쪽이 서쪽을 향해 끄덕였으며, 그 모든 것 위로 해가 끄덕이고 있었다."]], [["Suddenly bubbles seemed bursting beneath my closed eyes; like vices my hands grasped the shrouds; some invisible, gracious agency preserved me; with a shock I came back to life.", "별안간 감은 두 눈 밑에서 거품이 터지는 듯했다. 두 손이 만력기처럼 받침줄을 움켜쥐었다."], ["And lo!", "보이지 않는 어떤 자비로운 손길이 나를 지켜 주었다."], ["close under our lee, not forty fathoms off, a gigantic Sperm Whale lay rolling in the water like the capsized hull of a frigate, his broad, glossy back, of an Ethiopian hue, glistening in the sun’s rays like a mirror.", "나는 화들짝 놀라며 생기를 되찾았다. 그리고 보라!"], ["But lazily undulating in the trough of the sea, and ever and anon tranquilly spouting his vapory jet, the whale looked like a portly burgher smoking his pipe of a warm afternoon.", "우리 바람 아래쪽, 마흔 길도 채 안 되는 가까운 곳에서, 거대한 향유고래(Sperm Whale) 한 마리가 전복된 프리깃함의 선체처럼 물 위에서 뒹굴고 있었다. 에티오피아인의 피부 같은 빛깔을 띤 그 넓고 윤기 흐르는 등이 햇살을 받아 거울처럼 반짝였다."], ["But that pipe, poor whale, was thy last.", "하지만 바다의 골을 따라 나른하게 일렁이며 이따금 평온하게 수증기 같은 물줄기를 뿜어 올리는 그 고래는, 따스한 오후에 파이프 담배를 피우는 풍채 좋은 시민처럼 보였다. 그러나 가련한 고래여, 그 파이프가 네 마지막 담배였다."], ["As if struck by some enchanter’s wand, the sleepy ship and every sleeper in it all at once started into wakefulness; and more than a score of voices from all parts of the vessel, simultaneously with the three notes from aloft, shouted forth the accustomed cry, as the great fish slowly and regularly spouted the sparkling brine into the air.", "마법사의 지팡이에라도 맞은 듯, 졸고 있던 배와 그 안에서 잠들어 있던 자들이 일제히 화들짝 깨어났다. 그리고 그 거대한 물고기가 천천히, 규칙적으로 반짝이는 짠물을 허공으로 뿜어 올리는 가운데, 돛대 위에서 들려오는 세 가닥의 외침과 동시에 배의 곳곳에서 스무 명이 넘는 목소리가 익숙한 그 함성을 내질렀다."]], [["“Clear away the boats!", "\"보트를 내려라!"], ["Luff!” cried Ahab.", "바람 위로 돌려라!\""], ["And obeying his own order, he dashed the helm down before the helmsman could handle the spokes.", "에이해브(Ahab)가 외쳤다. 그러고는 자신이 내린 명령에 스스로 따르듯, 키잡이가 키 자루를 잡을 새도 없이 키를 내리눌렀다."]], [["The sudden exclamations of the crew must have alarmed the whale; and ere the boats were down, majestically turning, he swam away to the leeward, but with such a steady tranquillity, and making so few ripples as he swam, that thinking after all he might not as yet be alarmed, Ahab gave orders that not an oar should be used, and no man must speak but in whispers.", "선원들의 갑작스러운 외침이 고래를 놀라게 한 것이 분명했다. 보트가 내려지기도 전에, 고래는 위풍당당하게 몸을 틀어 바람 아래쪽으로 헤엄쳐 갔다."], ["So seated like Ontario Indians on the gunwales of the boats, we swiftly but silently paddled along; the calm not admitting of the noiseless sails being set.", "하지만 어찌나 한결같이 평온하게, 헤엄치면서 어찌나 잔물결을 적게 일으켰던지, 결국 아직은 놀라지 않았을지도 모른다고 생각한 에이해브는, 노를 단 한 자루도 쓰지 말고 누구든 속삭이는 소리 외에는 입을 열지 말라고 명령했다. 그리하여 우리는 온타리오 인디언처럼 보트의 뱃전에 걸터앉아, 빠르되 소리 없이 노를 저어 나아갔다."], ["Presently, as we thus glided in chase, the monster perpendicularly flitted his tail forty feet into the air, and then sank out of sight like a tower swallowed up.", "잔잔한 바다라 소리 없는 돛조차 펼 수 없었던 것이다. 이윽고 우리가 그렇게 미끄러지듯 추격하는 동안, 그 괴물이 꼬리를 수직으로 마흔 자 높이까지 휙 치켜올리더니, 이내 삼켜져 버린 탑처럼 시야에서 가라앉았다."]], [["“There go flukes!”", "\"꼬리가 들어간다!\""], ["was the cry, an announcement immediately followed by Stubb’s producing his match and igniting his pipe, for now a respite was granted.", "하는 외침이 터졌고, 이 외침에 곧바로 이어 스텁(Stubb)이 성냥을 꺼내 파이프에 불을 붙였다. 이제 잠시 한숨 돌릴 틈이 주어졌기 때문이다."], ["After the full interval of his sounding had elapsed, the whale rose again, and being now in advance of the smoker’s boat, and much nearer to it than to any of the others, Stubb counted upon the honor of the capture.", "고래가 잠수해 있던 시간이 온전히 흐른 뒤, 고래가 다시 떠올랐다. 이제 그놈은 담배 피우는 자의 보트보다 앞쪽에 있었고, 다른 어떤 보트보다도 그 보트에 훨씬 가까웠으므로, 스텁은 포획의 영예를 자기 것이라 셈했다."], ["It was obvious, now, that the whale had at length become aware of his pursuers.", "이제 고래가 마침내 추격자들의 존재를 알아챘다는 것이 분명해졌다."], ["All silence of cautiousness was therefore no longer of use.", "따라서 조심스레 지키던 온갖 침묵은 더 이상 쓸모가 없었다."], ["Paddles were dropped, and oars came loudly into play.", "노 젓는 노들이 내려지고, 본격적인 노들이 요란하게 움직이기 시작했다."], ["And still puffing at his pipe, Stubb cheered on his crew to the assault.", "스텁은 여전히 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 부하들에게 돌격을 독려했다."]], [["Yes, a mighty change had come over the fish.", "그렇다, 그 물고기에게 엄청난 변화가 일어났다. 자신이 처한 위기를 온몸으로 깨달은 그놈은 \"머리를 내밀고\""], ["All alive to his jeopardy, he was going “head out”; that part obliquely projecting from the mad yeast which he brewed.*", "나아가고 있었다. 다시 말해 자기가 휘저어 일으킨 그 미친 거품 속에서 머리 부분을 비스듬히 내밀고 있었던 것이다.*"]], [["*It will be seen in some other place of what a very light substance the entire interior of the sperm whale’s enormous head consists.", "* 다른 대목에서 보게 되겠지만, 향유고래의 거대한 머리는 그 내부 전체가 매우 가벼운 물질로 이루어져 있다."], ["Though apparently the most massive, it is by far the most buoyant part about him.", "겉보기에는 가장 육중한 부분이지만, 실은 그 몸에서 단연 가장 잘 뜨는 부분이다."], ["So that with ease he elevates it in the air, and invariably does so when going at his utmost speed.", "그래서 고래는 손쉽게 그 머리를 공중으로 치켜올리며, 전속력으로 나아갈 때는 반드시 그렇게 한다."], ["Besides, such is the breadth of the upper part of the front of his head, and such the tapering cut-water formation of the lower part, that by obliquely elevating his head, he thereby may be said to transform himself from a bluff-bowed sluggish galliot into a sharppointed New York pilot-boat.", "게다가 머리 앞부분 위쪽의 폭이 워낙 넓고 아래쪽이 워낙 뾰족하게 깎인 뱃머리 모양을 이루고 있어서, 머리를 비스듬히 치켜올림으로써 그놈은 뭉툭한 뱃머리의 굼뜬 갈리오트선을 날카롭게 뾰족한 뉴욕 수로 안내선으로 바꾸어 놓는다고 할 만하다."]], [["“Start her, start her, my men! Don’t hurry yourselves; take plenty of time—but start her; start her like thunder-claps, that’s all,” cried Stubb, spluttering out the smoke as he spoke. “Start her, now; give ’em the long and strong stroke, Tashtego. Start her, Tash, my boy—start her, all; but keep cool, keep cool—cucumbers is the word—easy, easy—only start her like grim death and grinning devils, and raise the buried dead perpendicular out of their graves, boys—that’s all. Start her!”", "\"저어라, 저어, 이 사람들아! 서두르진 마라. 시간은 넉넉히 잡되, 그래도 저어라. 벼락 치듯 저으란 말이다, 그게 전부다.\" 스텁이 말하면서 연기를 푹푹 내뿜으며 외쳤다. \"자, 저어라. 길고 힘차게 한 번에 당겨라, 태시테고(Tashtego). 저어라, 태시, 이 친구야. 모두 저어라. 하지만 침착하게, 침착하게 해라. 오이처럼 시원하게가 핵심이다. 편하게, 편하게 해라. 다만 무서운 죽음과 이빨 드러낸 악마처럼 저어라, 그리고 묻힌 망자들을 무덤에서 수직으로 일으켜 세워라, 친구들아. 그게 전부다. 저어라!\""]], [["“Woo-hoo!", "\"우후!"], ["Wa-hee!”", "와히!\""], ["screamed the Gay-Header in reply, raising some old war-whoop to the skies; as every oarsman in the strained boat involuntarily bounced forward with the one tremendous leading stroke which the eager Indian gave.", "게이헤드 출신 사내가 화답하며 외쳐, 어떤 오래된 전쟁 함성을 하늘 높이 내질렀다. 그러자 잔뜩 긴장한 보트 안의 노잡이들이 저마다, 그 열성적인 인디언이 내지른 한 번의 어마어마한 선도 동작에 자기도 모르게 앞으로 튕겨 나갔다."]], [["But his wild screams were answered by others quite as wild.", "하지만 그의 거친 비명에는 못지않게 거친 다른 비명들이 응답했다."], ["“Kee-hee!", "\"키히!"], ["Kee-hee!”", "키히!\""], ["yelled Daggoo, straining forwards and backwards on his seat, like a pacing tiger in his cage.", "대구(Daggoo)가 우리 안을 어슬렁대는 호랑이처럼 좌석에서 앞뒤로 몸을 잡아당기며 고함쳤다."]], [["“Ka-la!", "\"칼라!"], ["Koo-loo!”", "쿨루!\""], ["howled Queequeg, as if smacking his lips over a mouthful of Grenadier’s steak.", "퀴퀘그는 척탄병의 스테이크 한 입을 두고 입맛을 다시기라도 하듯 울부짖었다."], ["And thus with oars and yells the keels cut the sea.", "그리하여 노와 함성과 더불어 용골들이 바다를 갈랐다."], ["Meanwhile, Stubb retaining his place in the van, still encouraged his men to the onset, all the while puffing the smoke from his mouth.", "그동안 스텁은 선두 자리를 지키며 줄곧 입에서 연기를 뿜어 대면서 부하들에게 돌격을 독려했다."], ["Like desperadoes they tugged and they strained, till the welcome cry was heard—“Stand up, Tashtego!—give it to him!”", "그들은 무법자들처럼 당기고 또 잡아당겼고, 마침내 반가운 외침이 들려왔다. \"일어서라, 태시테고!"], ["The harpoon was hurled.", "한 방 먹여라!\" 작살이 날아갔다."], ["“Stern all!”", "\"모두 뒤로!\""], ["The oarsmen backed water; the same moment something went hot and hissing along every one of their wrists.", "노잡이들이 노를 거꾸로 저었다. 바로 그 순간 무언가 뜨겁게 쉭쉭거리며 저마다의 손목을 스치고 지나갔다."], ["It was the magical line.", "그 마법의 밧줄이었다."], ["An instant before, Stubb had swiftly caught two additional turns with it round the loggerhead, whence, by reason of its increased rapid circlings, a hempen blue smoke now jetted up and mingled with the steady fumes from his pipe.", "한순간 전, 스텁은 그 밧줄을 잽싸게 밧줄걸이 기둥에 두 바퀴 더 감았는데, 밧줄이 점점 빠르게 휘감겨 도는 까닭에, 이제 그 기둥에서 삼실의 푸른 연기가 솟구쳐 올라 그의 파이프에서 꾸준히 피어오르는 연기와 뒤섞였다."], ["As the line passed round and round the loggerhead; so also, just before reaching that point, it blisteringly passed through and through both of Stubb’s hands, from which the hand-cloths, or squares of quilted canvas sometimes worn at these times, had accidentally dropped.", "밧줄이 그 기둥을 휘휘 감고 도는 사이, 또한 그 지점에 닿기 직전에, 밧줄은 스텁의 두 손을 사정없이 스치고 또 스치며 살을 데었다. 손에서 손싸개가, 즉 이런 때 더러 끼는 누비 캔버스 천 조각이 어쩌다 떨어져 나간 탓이었다."], ["It was like holding an enemy’s sharp two-edged sword by the blade, and that enemy all the time striving to wrest it out of your clutch.", "그것은 마치 적의 날카로운 양날 검을 칼날 쪽으로 움켜쥔 것 같았고, 그러는 내내 그 적은 그것을 당신의 손아귀에서 비틀어 빼내려 안간힘을 쓰고 있는 셈이었다."]], [["“Wet the line!", "\"밧줄을 적셔라!"], ["wet the line!”", "밧줄을 적셔!\""], ["cried Stubb to the tub oarsman (him seated by the tub) who, snatching off his hat, dashed sea-water into it.* More turns were taken, so that the line began holding its place.", "스텁이 통 옆에 앉은 통 담당 노잡이에게 외쳤다. 그러자 그 사내는 모자를 홱 벗어 거기에 바닷물을 퍼 담았다.* 밧줄이 몇 바퀴 더 감기자, 밧줄은 비로소 제자리를 잡기 시작했다."], ["The boat now flew through the boiling water like a shark all fins.", "이제 보트는 온통 지느러미뿐인 상어처럼 끓어오르는 물살을 뚫고 날아갔다."], ["Stubb and Tashtego here changed places—stem for stern—a staggering business truly in that rocking commotion.", "여기서 스텁과 태시테고는 자리를 바꾸었다. 뱃머리와 고물을 맞바꾼 것인데, 그 출렁이는 소란 속에서 그것은 참으로 아슬아슬한 일이었다."]], [["*Partly to show the indispensableness of this act, it may here be stated, that, in the old Dutch fishery, a mop was used to dash the running line with water; in many other ships, a wooden piggin, or bailer, is set apart for that purpose.", "* 이 행위가 얼마나 필수적인지 어느 정도 보여 주기 위해 여기서 밝혀 두자면, 옛 네덜란드 어업에서는 풀려 나가는 밧줄에 물을 끼얹는 데 자루걸레를 썼다. 다른 여러 배에서는 그 목적을 위해 나무 물통, 즉 물 푸는 바가지를 따로 마련해 둔다."], ["Your hat, however, is the most convenient.", "하지만 모자가 가장 편리하다."]], [["From the vibrating line extending the entire length of the upper part of the boat, and from its now being more tight than a harpstring, you would have thought the craft had two keels—one cleaving the water, the other the air—as the boat churned on through both opposing elements at once.", "보트 윗부분의 전체 길이에 걸쳐 팽팽하게 뻗은 그 진동하는 밧줄을 보고, 또 그것이 이제 하프 줄보다도 더 팽팽하게 당겨진 것을 보았다면, 당신은 이 배에 용골이 둘 달렸다고 — 하나는 물을 가르고, 다른 하나는 공기를 가른다고 — 여겼을 것이다. 보트가 서로 맞서는 두 원소를 동시에 휘저으며 나아갔으니 말이다."], ["A continual cascade played at the bows; a ceaseless whirling eddy in her wake; and, at the slightest motion from within, even but of a little finger, the vibrating, cracking craft canted over her spasmodic gunwale into the sea.", "뱃머리에서는 폭포수가 끊임없이 일었고, 그 항적에서는 소용돌이가 쉴 새 없이 휘돌았다. 그리고 안에서 누군가 새끼손가락 하나라도 까딱 움직이면, 진동하며 삐걱대는 그 배는 경련하듯 한쪽 뱃전을 기울이며 당장이라도 바닷속으로 뒤집힐 듯했다."], ["Thus they rushed; each man with might and main clinging to his seat, to prevent being tossed to the foam; and the tall form of Tashtego at the steering oar crouching almost double, in order to bring down his centre of gravity.", "그렇게 그들은 내달렸다. 저마다 거품 속으로 내동댕이쳐지지 않으려 온 힘을 다해 자기 좌석에 매달렸고, 키 잡는 노 앞의 키 큰 태시테고는 무게 중심을 낮추려고 거의 두 겹으로 접히다시피 몸을 웅크렸다."], ["Whole Atlantics and Pacifics seemed passed as they shot on their way, till at length the whale somewhat slackened his flight.", "대서양과 태평양을 통째로 지나친 듯한 기세로 그들은 쏜살같이 나아갔고, 마침내 고래가 그 질주를 다소 늦추었다."]], [["“Haul in—haul in!”", "\"끌어당겨라, 끌어당겨!\""], ["cried Stubb to the bowsman!", "스텁이 뱃머리 사내에게 외쳤다."], ["and, facing round towards the whale, all hands began pulling the boat up to him, while yet the boat was being towed on.", "그리고 모두 고래 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 보트가 아직 끌려가는 와중에도 보트를 고래 쪽으로 끌어당기기 시작했다."], ["Soon ranging up by his flank, Stubb, firmly planting his knee in the clumsy cleat, darted dart after dart into the flying fish; at the word of command, the boat alternately sterning out of the way of the whale’s horrible wallow, and then ranging up for another fling.", "이윽고 고래의 옆구리에 바싹 다가서자, 스텁은 무릎을 그 투박한 받침대에 단단히 박은 채, 달아나는 물고기에게 작살을 연거푸 던져 넣었다. 호령에 따라 보트는 번갈아 가며 고래의 끔찍한 몸부림을 피해 뒤로 물러났다가, 다시 또 한 번 던지기 위해 다가서곤 했다."]], [["The red tide now poured from all sides of the monster like brooks down a hill.", "이제 그 괴물의 사방에서 붉은 물결이, 언덕을 따라 흘러내리는 시냇물처럼 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["His tormented body rolled not in brine but in blood, which bubbled and seethed for furlongs behind in their wake.", "고통에 시달리는 그 몸뚱이는 이제 짠물이 아니라 피 속에서 뒹굴었고, 그 피는 항적 뒤로 몇 펄롱에 걸쳐 부글거리며 끓어올랐다."], ["The slanting sun playing upon this crimson pond in the sea, sent back its reflection into every face, so that they all glowed to each other like red men.", "비스듬히 기운 해가 바다의 이 진홍빛 못 위에서 어른거리며 그 반사광을 모두의 얼굴에 되쏘았고, 그리하여 그들은 서로에게 붉은 인간처럼 이글거려 보였다."], ["And all the while, jet after jet of white smoke was agonizingly shot from the spiracle of the whale, and vehement puff after puff from the mouth of the excited headsman; as at every dart, hauling in upon his crooked lance (by the line attached to it), Stubb straightened it again and again, by a few rapid blows against the gunwale, then again and again sent it into the whale.", "그러는 내내, 고래의 분수공에서는 흰 연기가 한 줄기씩 고통스럽게 뿜어 나왔고, 흥분한 작살잡이의 입에서는 격렬한 연기가 한 모금씩 내뿜어졌다. 작살을 한 번 던질 때마다, 스텁은 (거기에 매인 밧줄로) 자신의 휜 창을 끌어당겨, 뱃전에 몇 번 잽싸게 두드려 그것을 거듭거듭 곧게 펴고는, 또다시 고래에게 박아 넣었던 것이다."]], [["“Pull up—pull up!”", "\"끌어 올려라, 끌어 올려!\""], ["he now cried to the bowsman, as the waning whale relaxed in his wrath.", "그가 이제 뱃머리 사내에게 외쳤다. 점점 기력이 다해 가는 고래가 그 분노를 누그러뜨렸기 때문이다."], ["“Pull up!—close to!”", "\"끌어 올려라! 바싹 붙어라!\""], ["and the boat ranged along the fish’s flank.", "그러자 보트가 고래의 옆구리를 따라 나란히 섰다."], ["When reaching far over the bow, Stubb slowly churned his long sharp lance into the fish, and kept it there, carefully churning and churning, as if cautiously seeking to feel after some gold watch that the whale might have swallowed, and which he was fearful of breaking ere he could hook it out.", "스텁은 뱃머리 너머로 멀찍이 몸을 뻗어, 길고 날카로운 창을 천천히 고래의 몸속에 휘저어 넣고 그대로 둔 채, 조심스레 휘젓고 또 휘저었다. 마치 고래가 삼켰을지 모를 어떤 금시계를 손끝으로 더듬어 찾으면서, 그것을 갈고리로 끌어내기 전에 행여 깨뜨릴까 봐 두려워하는 듯한 손길이었다."], ["But that gold watch he sought was the innermost life of the fish.", "하지만 그가 찾던 그 금시계란 다름 아닌 고래의 가장 깊은 곳에 있는 목숨이었다."], ["And now it is struck; for, starting from his trance into that unspeakable thing called his “flurry,”", "이제 그것이 찔렸다."], ["the monster horribly wallowed in his blood, overwrapped himself in impenetrable, mad, boiling spray, so that the imperilled craft, instantly dropping astern, had much ado blindly to struggle out from that phrensied twilight into the clear air of the day.", "혼곤한 잠에서 깨어나 이른바 그 \"발작\"이라 불리는 형언할 수 없는 상태로 접어든 그 괴물은, 제 피 속에서 끔찍하게 뒹굴며 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 미치광이 같은 끓어오르는 물보라로 제 몸을 휘감았다. 그리하여 위태로워진 그 작은 배는 곧장 뒤로 물러나면서, 그 광란의 어스름에서 벗어나 환한 대낮의 맑은 대기 속으로 더듬더듬 빠져나오느라 진땀을 뺐다."]], [["And now abating in his flurry, the whale once more rolled out into view; surging from side to side; spasmodically dilating and contracting his spout-hole, with sharp, cracking, agonized respirations.", "이제 발작이 잦아들면서, 고래는 다시 한번 모습을 드러내며 떠올랐다. 좌우로 출렁이며, 분수공을 발작하듯 벌렸다 오므렸다 하면서, 날카롭고 갈라지는, 고통에 찬 숨을 몰아쉬었다."], ["At last, gush after gush of clotted red gore, as if it had been the purple lees of red wine, shot into the frighted air; and falling back again, ran dripping down his motionless flanks into the sea.", "마침내, 마치 적포도주의 자줏빛 찌끼라도 되는 양 엉긴 붉은 핏덩이가 한 줄기씩 거듭 겁먹은 허공으로 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그러고는 다시 떨어져 내려, 미동도 없는 그 옆구리를 따라 뚝뚝 흘러 바다로 흘러 들어갔다."], ["His heart had burst!", "그 심장이 터져 버린 것이다!"]], [["“He’s dead, Mr. Stubb,”", "\"놈이 죽었습니다, 스텁 씨.\""], ["said Daggoo.", "대구가 말했다."]], [["“Yes; both pipes smoked out!”", "\"그래, 두 파이프가 다 타 버렸군!\""], ["and withdrawing his own from his mouth, Stubb scattered the dead ashes over the water; and, for a moment, stood thoughtfully eyeing the vast corpse he had made.", "스텁은 자기 입에서 파이프를 빼내며 죽은 재를 물 위에 흩뿌렸다. 그러고는 잠시, 자신이 만들어 낸 그 거대한 시체를 생각에 잠긴 눈으로 바라보며 서 있었다."]], [["A word concerning an incident in the last chapter.", "앞 장에서 일어난 한 가지 사건에 관해 한마디 덧붙여 둔다."]], [["According to the invariable usage of the fishery, the whale-boat pushes off from the ship, with the headsman or whale-killer as temporary steersman, and the harpooneer or whale-fastener pulling the foremost oar, the one known as the harpooneer-oar. Now it needs a strong, nervous arm to strike the first iron into the fish; for often, in what is called a long dart, the heavy implement has to be flung to the distance of twenty or thirty feet. But however prolonged and exhausting the chase, the harpooneer is expected to pull his oar meanwhile to the uttermost; indeed, he is expected to set an example of superhuman activity to the rest, not only by incredible rowing, but by repeated loud and intrepid exclamations; and what it is to keep shouting at the top of one’s compass, while all the other muscles are strained and half started—what that is none know but those who have tried it. For one, I cannot bawl very heartily and work very recklessly at one and the same time. In this straining, bawling state, then, with his back to the fish, all at once the exhausted harpooneer hears the exciting cry—“Stand up, and give it to him!” He now has to drop and secure his oar, turn round on his centre half way, seize his harpoon from the crotch, and with what little strength may remain, he essays to pitch it somehow into the whale. No wonder, taking the whole fleet of whalemen in a body, that out of fifty fair chances for a dart, not five are successful; no wonder that so many hapless harpooneers are madly cursed and disrated; no wonder that some of them actually burst their blood-vessels in the boat; no wonder that some sperm whalemen are absent four years with four barrels; no wonder that to many ship owners, whaling is but a losing concern; for it is the harpooneer that makes the voyage, and if you take the breath out of his body how can you expect to find it there when most wanted!", "포경업의 변함없는 관습에 따르면, 고래잡이 보트는 머리잡이(headsman), 곧 고래를 죽이는 자가 임시 조타수를 맡고, 작살잡이(harpooneer), 곧 고래에 작살을 박는 자가 맨 앞자리의 노 — 이른바 작살잡이 노라 불리는 것 — 를 저으며 본선에서 떨어져 나간다. 그런데 처음으로 작살을 고래 몸에 꽂으려면 강하고 단단한 팔이 필요하다. 흔히 긴 던지기라 불리는 경우에는, 그 묵직한 도구를 20피트나 30피트(약 6미터 내지 9미터) 거리까지 내던져야 하기 때문이다. 그러나 추격이 아무리 길고 진을 빼는 것일지라도, 작살잡이는 그동안 자기 노를 있는 힘껏 저어야 한다. 아니, 그는 믿기 어려운 노젓기뿐 아니라 거듭 터뜨리는 크고 담대한 외침으로써 나머지 사람들에게 초인적인 활약의 본보기를 보여 주어야 한다. 다른 근육이란 근육이 모두 팽팽하게 당겨지고 반쯤 풀려나는 와중에 목청껏 고함을 계속 질러야 한다는 것이 어떤 일인지 — 그것은 해 본 자가 아니면 아무도 모른다. 나로 말하자면, 한순간에 마음껏 고함을 지르면서 동시에 죽을힘을 다해 일하는 것은 도무지 못 하겠다. 그리하여 이렇게 힘을 짜내며 고래에게 등을 돌린 채 고함치는 상태에서, 기진맥진한 작살잡이는 별안간 가슴 뛰는 외침을 듣는다 — \"일어서서, 놈에게 한 방 먹여!\" 이제 그는 노를 놓고 단단히 묶은 뒤, 제자리에서 몸을 반쯤 돌려, 받침대에서 작살을 움켜쥐고, 남아 있는 얼마 안 되는 힘으로 어떻게든 그것을 고래에게 던져 박으려 안간힘을 쓴다. 그러니 고래잡이 선단 전체를 한데 놓고 볼 때, 던질 기회가 쉰 번 있어도 그중 다섯 번도 성공하지 못한다 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 그토록 많은 가련한 작살잡이들이 미친 듯이 욕을 먹고 강등당한다 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 그들 중 더러는 보트 안에서 실제로 혈관이 터지고 만다 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 어떤 향유고래잡이들이 사 년이나 집을 비우고도 기름 네 통밖에 채우지 못한다 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 많은 선주들에게 포경업이 그저 밑지는 장사일 뿐이라 해도 이상할 것이 없다. 항해의 성패를 가르는 것은 바로 작살잡이이니, 그의 몸에서 숨을 다 빼 놓고서야 어찌 가장 절실한 순간에 그 숨이 남아 있기를 바랄 수 있겠는가!"]], [["Again, if the dart be successful, then at the second critical instant, that is, when the whale starts to run, the boatheader and harpooneer likewise start to running fore and aft, to the imminent jeopardy of themselves and every one else.", "또한, 던지기가 성공한다 해도, 두 번째 결정적인 순간, 곧 고래가 내달리기 시작할 때, 머리잡이와 작살잡이도 마찬가지로 앞뒤로 자리를 옮기느라 분주해져, 자기 자신은 물론 다른 모든 이들까지 임박한 위험에 빠뜨린다."], ["It is then they change places; and the headsman, the chief officer of the little craft, takes his proper station in the bows of the boat.", "바로 이때 그들은 자리를 맞바꾸며, 그 작은 배의 우두머리 사관인 머리잡이가 보트 뱃머리의 제자리를 차지하게 된다."]], [["Now, I care not who maintains the contrary, but all this is both foolish and unnecessary.", "그런데 나는 누가 그 반대를 주장하든 개의치 않거니와, 이 모든 것이야말로 어리석고 불필요한 짓이다."], ["The headsman should stay in the bows from first to last; he should both dart the harpoon and the lance, and no rowing whatever should be expected of him, except under circumstances obvious to any fisherman.", "머리잡이는 처음부터 끝까지 뱃머리에 머물러 있어야 한다. 그는 작살도 던지고 창도 던져야 하며, 어떤 어부에게나 빤히 보이는 상황을 제외하고는 그에게 노젓는 일을 기대해서는 안 된다."], ["I know that this would sometimes involve a slight loss of speed in the chase; but long experience in various whalemen of more than one nation has convinced me that in the vast majority of failures in the fishery, it has not by any means been so much the speed of the whale as the before described exhaustion of the harpooneer that has caused them.", "물론 이렇게 하면 때때로 추격 속도가 약간 떨어지는 일도 있으리란 것을 나는 안다. 그러나 한 나라에 그치지 않는 여러 포경선에서 쌓은 오랜 경험을 통해 나는, 포경업에서 일어나는 실패의 압도적 대다수가, 결코 고래의 속도 때문이라기보다는 앞서 말한 작살잡이의 탈진 때문에 빚어진 것임을 확신하게 되었다."]], [["To insure the greatest efficiency in the dart, the harpooneers of this world must start to their feet from out of idleness, and not from out of toil.", "던지기에서 최대의 효율을 보장하려면, 이 세상의 작살잡이들은 고된 일에서가 아니라 한가로움에서 벌떡 일어서야 하는 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 63. The Crotch.", "# CHAPTER 63. The Crotch."]], [["Out of the trunk, the branches grow; out of them, the twigs.", "줄기에서 가지가 자라나고, 그 가지에서 잔가지가 돋는다."], ["So, in productive subjects, grow the chapters.", "그처럼 결실 많은 주제에서는 여러 장(章)이 자라난다."]], [["The crotch alluded to on a previous page deserves independent mention.", "앞 쪽에서 언급한 받침대(crotch)는 따로 떼어 설명할 만하다."], ["It is a notched stick of a peculiar form, some two feet in length, which is perpendicularly inserted into the starboard gunwale near the bow, for the purpose of furnishing a rest for the wooden extremity of the harpoon, whose other naked, barbed end slopingly projects from the prow.", "그것은 독특한 형태로 홈이 파인 막대인데, 길이는 2피트(약 60센티미터)쯤 되며, 뱃머리 가까운 우현 뱃전에 수직으로 꽂아 두어 작살의 나무 자루 끝을 받쳐 두는 데 쓰인다. 작살의 다른 한쪽, 곧 미늘 박힌 맨살의 끝은 뱃머리에서 비스듬히 앞으로 튀어나오게 된다."], ["Thereby the weapon is instantly at hand to its hurler, who snatches it up as readily from its rest as a backwoodsman swings his rifle from the wall.", "그리하여 그 무기는 던지는 자의 손에 즉각 닿게 되어, 마치 변경의 산사람이 벽에 걸린 소총을 낚아채듯 받침대에서 단숨에 집어 들 수 있다."], ["It is customary to have two harpoons reposing in the crotch, respectively called the first and second irons.", "받침대에는 작살 두 자루를 얹어 두는 것이 관례인데, 각각 첫째 작살과 둘째 작살이라 부른다."]], [["But these two harpoons, each by its own cord, are both connected with the line; the object being this: to dart them both, if possible, one instantly after the other into the same whale; so that if, in the coming drag, one should draw out, the other may still retain a hold.", "그러나 이 두 작살은 저마다 제 줄로 본줄에 이어져 있으니, 그 목적은 이러하다. 가능하다면 둘 다, 하나를 던진 직후 곧바로 다른 하나를 같은 고래에 꽂아 넣으려는 것이다."], ["It is a doubling of the chances.", "그리하여 뒤이어 끌려갈 때 만일 하나가 빠져나가더라도 다른 하나가 여전히 박혀 있게 하려는 것이다."], ["But it very often happens that owing to the instantaneous, violent, convulsive running of the whale upon receiving the first iron, it becomes impossible for the harpooneer, however lightning-like in his movements, to pitch the second iron into him.", "이는 가능성을 곱절로 늘리는 일이다. 그러나 첫째 작살을 맞은 고래가 순식간에 격렬하고 발작적으로 내달리는 까닭에, 작살잡이가 번개처럼 몸을 놀린다 해도 둘째 작살을 그놈에게 박아 넣기란 불가능해지는 경우가 매우 잦다."], ["Nevertheless, as the second iron is already connected with the line, and the line is running, hence that weapon must, at all events, be anticipatingly tossed out of the boat, somehow and somewhere; else the most terrible jeopardy would involve all hands.", "그럼에도 둘째 작살은 이미 본줄에 이어져 있고 그 줄은 풀려 나가고 있으니, 그 무기는 어떻게든, 또 어디로든, 어떤 일이 있어도 미리 보트 밖으로 던져 버려야만 한다. 그러지 않으면 더없이 끔찍한 위험이 선원 모두를 덮칠 것이기 때문이다."], ["Tumbled into the water, it accordingly is in such cases; the spare coils of box line (mentioned in a preceding chapter) making this feat, in most instances, prudently practicable.", "그리하여 그런 경우에는 작살이 물속으로 던져지는데, (앞 장에서 언급한) 통에 감겨 있는 여분의 줄 사리가 있어 대개는 이 일을 신중하게 해낼 수 있다."], ["But this critical act is not always unattended with the saddest and most fatal casualties.", "그러나 이 위태로운 행위에는 더없이 슬프고 치명적인 사고가 따르지 않는 법이 없다."]], [["Furthermore: you must know that when the second iron is thrown overboard, it thenceforth becomes a dangling, sharp-edged terror, skittishly curvetting about both boat and whale, entangling the lines, or cutting them, and making a prodigious sensation in all directions.", "게다가 알아 두어야 할 것은, 둘째 작살이 배 밖으로 던져지고 나면 그때부터 그것은 대롱대롱 매달려 날뛰는, 날 선 공포의 물건이 된다는 점이다. 그것은 보트와 고래 둘레를 변덕스레 펄쩍거리며 휘돌아, 줄을 엉키게 하거나 끊어 버리고, 사방에 어마어마한 소동을 일으킨다."], ["Nor, in general, is it possible to secure it again until the whale is fairly captured and a corpse.", "그리고 고래가 완전히 잡혀 송장이 되기 전까지는 대개 그것을 다시 거두어들이는 일이 불가능하다."]], [["Consider, now, how it must be in the case of four boats all engaging one unusually strong, active, and knowing whale; when owing to these qualities in him, as well as to the thousand concurring accidents of such an audacious enterprise, eight or ten loose second irons may be simultaneously dangling about him.", "이제 생각해 보라. 보통보다 훨씬 더 힘세고 날래며 영악한 고래 한 마리를 보트 네 척이 한꺼번에 상대하는 경우가 어떠할지를."], ["For, of course, each boat is supplied with several harpoons to bend on to the line should the first one be ineffectually darted without recovery.", "그놈의 그러한 성질에다, 그토록 대담한 일에 으레 뒤따르는 수천 가지 사고가 겹치는 까닭에, 풀려난 둘째 작살 여덟 자루나 열 자루가 한꺼번에 그놈 둘레에서 대롱거리는 사태가 벌어질 수도 있다."], ["All these particulars are faithfully narrated here, as they will not fail to elucidate several most important, however intricate passages, in scenes hereafter to be painted.", "물론 보트마다 작살이 여러 자루씩 갖춰져 있어, 첫째 작살이 헛되이 던져져 거두지 못하게 되면 본줄에 새로 이어 맬 수 있기 때문이다. 이 모든 자세한 사정을 여기에 충실히 적어 두는 까닭은, 앞으로 그려 낼 장면들 속에서 더없이 중요하면서도 까다로운 여러 대목을 밝히는 데 반드시 도움이 될 것이기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 64. Stubb’s Supper.", "# CHAPTER 64. Stubb’s Supper."]], [["Stubb’s whale had been killed some distance from the ship.", "스터브의 고래는 배에서 제법 떨어진 곳에서 죽어 있었다."], ["It was a calm; so, forming a tandem of three boats, we commenced the slow business of towing the trophy to the Pequod.", "바다는 잔잔했다. 그리하여 우리는 보트 세 척을 한 줄로 잇대어, 그 전리품을 피쿼드(Pequod)호까지 끌고 가는 더딘 일에 착수했다."], ["And now, as we eighteen men with our thirty-six arms, and one hundred and eighty thumbs and fingers, slowly toiled hour after hour upon that inert, sluggish corpse in the sea; and it seemed hardly to budge at all, except at long intervals; good evidence was hereby furnished of the enormousness of the mass we moved.", "그리고 이제 우리 열여덟 명이 서른여섯 개의 팔과 백여든 개의 엄지와 손가락을 놀려, 바다 위의 그 굼뜨고 묵직한 시체를 한 시간 또 한 시간 천천히 끌어당겼으나, 그것은 좀처럼 꿈쩍도 하지 않는 듯했고, 다만 긴 간격을 두고서야 겨우 움직일 뿐이었으니, 우리가 움직이는 그 덩어리가 얼마나 어마어마한지를 여기서 충분히 짐작할 수 있었다."], ["For, upon the great canal of Hang-Ho, or whatever they call it, in China, four or five laborers on the foot-path will draw a bulky freighted junk at the rate of a mile an hour; but this grand argosy we towed heavily forged along, as if laden with pig-lead in bulk.", "중국의 항허(Hang-Ho) 대운하인지 뭐라 부르든 그곳에서는, 길가의 인부 네댓 명이 짐을 가득 실은 거룻배를 시속 1마일로 끌어가지만, 우리가 끌고 가는 이 거대한 배는 마치 무게 단위로 잰 납덩이를 가득 실은 것처럼 무겁게 짓이기며 나아갔다."]], [["Darkness came on; but three lights up and down in the Pequod’s main-rigging dimly guided our way; till drawing nearer we saw Ahab dropping one of several more lanterns over the bulwarks.", "어둠이 내려앉았다. 그러나 피쿼드호의 큰 돛 삭구에 위아래로 매달린 세 개의 불빛이 우리의 길을 어슴푸레 인도해 주었다."], ["Vacantly eyeing the heaving whale for a moment, he issued the usual orders for securing it for the night, and then handing his lantern to a seaman, went his way into the cabin, and did not come forward again until morning.", "점점 가까워지자 에이해브(Ahab)가 뱃전 너머로 등불 몇 개를 더 늘어뜨리는 모습이 보였다. 그는 출렁이는 고래를 잠시 멍하니 바라보더니, 밤새 그것을 단단히 붙잡아 두라는 평소의 지시를 내렸고, 그런 다음 등불을 한 선원에게 건네고 선실로 들어가서는, 아침이 될 때까지 다시는 앞으로 나오지 않았다."]], [["Though, in overseeing the pursuit of this whale, Captain Ahab had evinced his customary activity, to call it so; yet now that the creature was dead, some vague dissatisfaction, or impatience, or despair, seemed working in him; as if the sight of that dead body reminded him that Moby Dick was yet to be slain; and though a thousand other whales were brought to his ship, all that would not one jot advance his grand, monomaniac object.", "이 고래를 추격하는 일을 감독하면서 에이해브 선장(Captain)은 — 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 — 평소의 활력을 보여 주었으나, 이제 그 짐승이 죽고 나자 어떤 막연한 불만이나 초조함이나 절망 같은 것이 그의 안에서 꿈틀대는 듯했다. 마치 그 죽은 몸뚱이를 보는 것이 모비 딕(Moby Dick)은 아직 죽이지 못했음을 그에게 일깨워 주기라도 하는 것처럼, 그리고 천 마리의 다른 고래를 그의 배로 끌어온다 한들, 그 모든 것이 그의 위대한, 편집광적인 목표를 손톱만큼도 진전시키지 못하기라도 하는 것처럼."], ["Very soon you would have thought from the sound on the Pequod’s decks, that all hands were preparing to cast anchor in the deep; for heavy chains are being dragged along the deck, and thrust rattling out of the port-holes.", "머지않아 그대는 피쿼드호 갑판에서 나는 소리만 듣고도, 온 선원이 깊은 바다에 닻을 내릴 채비를 하고 있다고 생각했을 것이다. 묵직한 쇠사슬들이 갑판을 따라 질질 끌려가고, 현문(舷門) 구멍 밖으로 덜컹거리며 밀려 나가고 있었으니 말이다."], ["But by those clanking links, the vast corpse itself, not the ship, is to be moored.", "그러나 저 철컹대는 사슬 고리에 매여 묶이는 것은 배가 아니라 그 거대한 시체 자체였다."], ["Tied by the head to the stern, and by the tail to the bows, the whale now lies with its black hull close to the vessel’s and seen through the darkness of the night, which obscured the spars and rigging aloft, the two—ship and whale, seemed yoked together like colossal bullocks, whereof one reclines while the other remains standing.*", "머리는 고물에, 꼬리는 이물에 묶인 채, 고래는 이제 그 검은 선체를 배의 선체에 바싹 붙인 채 누워 있었고, 위쪽의 활대와 삭구를 가려 버린 밤의 어둠을 통해 바라보면, 그 둘 — 배와 고래는 마치 거대한 황소 한 쌍이 멍에에 함께 묶인 듯, 그중 하나는 누워 있고 다른 하나는 선 채로 있는 것처럼 보였다.*"]], [["*A little item may as well be related here.", "* 여기서 사소한 이야기 하나를 곁들여도 좋겠다."], ["The strongest and most reliable hold which the ship has upon the whale when moored alongside, is by the flukes or tail; and as from its greater density that part is relatively heavier than any other (excepting the side-fins), its flexibility even in death, causes it to sink low beneath the surface; so that with the hand you cannot get at it from the boat, in order to put the chain round it.", "고래를 배 옆에 붙들어 맬 때 배가 고래를 붙잡는 가장 튼튼하고 미더운 고리는 꼬리지느러미, 곧 꼬리다. 그 부위는 밀도가 더 높아 다른 어느 부분(옆지느러미를 제외하고)보다도 상대적으로 무겁기에, 죽은 뒤에도 그 유연함 탓에 수면 아래로 깊이 가라앉는다."], ["But this difficulty is ingeniously overcome: a small, strong line is prepared with a wooden float at its outer end, and a weight in its middle, while the other end is secured to the ship.", "그래서 보트에서는 손을 뻗어도 그것에 닿을 수가 없어, 사슬을 둘러 감을 수가 없다. 그러나 이 난관은 기발하게 극복된다."], ["By adroit management the wooden float is made to rise on the other side of the mass, so that now having girdled the whale, the chain is readily made to follow suit; and being slipped along the body, is at last locked fast round the smallest part of the tail, at the point of junction with its broad flukes or lobes.", "가늘고 튼튼한 밧줄을 마련하여 바깥쪽 끝에는 나무 부표를 달고 가운데에는 추를 매달며, 다른 쪽 끝은 배에 단단히 고정한다. 능숙하게 다루면 나무 부표가 그 덩어리의 반대편으로 떠오르게 되고, 그리하여 이제 고래를 한 바퀴 둘렀으니 사슬도 손쉽게 그 뒤를 따르게 되며, 몸을 따라 미끄러져 내려가다가 마침내 넓은 꼬리지느러미, 곧 갈래와 맞닿는 지점에서 꼬리의 가장 가느다란 부분을 단단히 감아 잠근다."]], [["If moody Ahab was now all quiescence, at least so far as could be known on deck, Stubb, his second mate, flushed with conquest, betrayed an unusual but still good-natured excitement.", "침울한 에이해브가 이제 — 적어도 갑판에서 알 수 있는 한 — 온통 고요에 잠겨 있었다면, 그의 이등 항해사 스터브(Stubb)는 승리에 들떠 평소답지 않으면서도 여전히 사람 좋은 흥분을 드러내고 있었다."], ["Such an unwonted bustle was he in that the staid Starbuck, his official superior, quietly resigned to him for the time the sole management of affairs.", "그가 어찌나 유난스레 부산을 떨었던지, 그의 공식적인 상관인 진중한 스타벅(Starbuck)은 한동안 모든 일의 지휘권을 그에게 조용히 넘겨주었다."], ["One small, helping cause of all this liveliness in Stubb, was soon made strangely manifest.", "스터브의 이 모든 활기에 한몫 거든 작은 까닭 하나가 곧 묘하게 드러났다."], ["Stubb was a high liver; he was somewhat intemperately fond of the whale as a flavorish thing to his palate.", "스터브는 잘 먹고 잘 마시는 자였고, 입맛을 돋우는 별미로서 고래를 다소 절제 없이 좋아하는 편이었다."]], [["“A steak, a steak, ere I sleep!", "\"스테이크다, 스테이크! 잠들기 전에 한 점 먹어야겠다!"], ["You, Daggoo!", "자네, 대구(Daggoo)!"], ["overboard you go, and cut me one from his small!”", "어서 물로 뛰어들어, 저놈의 꼬리 쪽에서 한 덩이 잘라 와!\""]], [["Here be it known, that though these wild fishermen do not, as a general thing, and according to the great military maxim, make the enemy defray the current expenses of the war (at least before realizing the proceeds of the voyage), yet now and then you find some of these Nantucketers who have a genuine relish for that particular part of the Sperm Whale designated by Stubb; comprising the tapering extremity of the body.", "여기서 밝혀 두건대, 이 거친 어부들은 일반적으로, 또 저 위대한 군사 격언에 따라 적에게 전쟁의 당면 비용을 치르게 하는 법이(적어도 항해의 수익을 거두어들이기 전에는) 없지만, 그래도 이따금 이 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들 가운데에는 스터브가 가리킨 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 바로 그 부위, 곧 끝으로 갈수록 가늘어지는 몸뚱이의 끄트머리에 진심으로 입맛을 다시는 자들이 있다."]], [["About midnight that steak was cut and cooked; and lighted by two lanterns of sperm oil, Stubb stoutly stood up to his spermaceti supper at the capstan-head, as if that capstan were a sideboard.", "자정 무렵 그 스테이크는 잘려 익혀졌다. 그리고 향유고래 기름을 채운 등불 두 개의 불빛 아래, 스터브는 캡스턴(capstan) 꼭대기에서 마치 그 캡스턴이 식탁이라도 되는 양 떡 버티고 서서 자신의 향유고래 만찬을 들었다."], ["Nor was Stubb the only banqueter on whale’s flesh that night.", "그날 밤 고래 살로 잔치를 벌인 것은 스터브뿐만이 아니었다."], ["Mingling their mumblings with his own mastications, thousands on thousands of sharks, swarming round the dead leviathan, smackingly feasted on its fatness.", "죽은 리바이어던(Leviathan) 주위로 떼 지어 몰려든 수천수만 마리의 상어들이, 그의 씹는 소리에 저들의 우물거리는 소리를 뒤섞으며 그 기름진 살을 쩝쩝거리며 포식했다."], ["The few sleepers below in their bunks were often startled by the sharp slapping of their tails against the hull, within a few inches of the sleepers’ hearts.", "아래쪽 침상에서 잠을 청하던 몇 안 되는 자들은, 잠든 이의 심장에서 불과 몇 인치 떨어진 곳에서 선체를 후려치는 상어 꼬리의 날카로운 철썩임에 번번이 화들짝 놀라곤 했다."], ["Peering over the side you could just see them (as before you heard them) wallowing in the sullen, black waters, and turning over on their backs as they scooped out huge globular pieces of the whale of the bigness of a human head.", "뱃전 너머로 내려다보면, (앞서 그 소리를 들었듯이) 그놈들이 음침하고 검은 물속에서 뒹굴며, 사람 머리만 한 크기의 거대하고 둥근 고래 살점을 도려내면서 등을 뒤집는 모습을 어렴풋이 볼 수 있었다."], ["This particular feat of the shark seems all but miraculous.", "상어의 이 유별난 재주는 거의 기적과도 같아 보인다."], ["How at such an apparently unassailable surface, they contrive to gouge out such symmetrical mouthfuls, remains a part of the universal problem of all things.", "겉보기에 그토록 비집고 들어갈 데 없어 보이는 표면에서, 어떻게 그처럼 좌우 대칭의 한 입 거리를 후벼 파내는지는 만물에 깃든 보편적 수수께끼의 일부로 남아 있다."], ["The mark they thus leave on the whale, may best be likened to the hollow made by a carpenter in countersinking for a screw.", "그렇게 해서 고래에 남긴 자국은, 목수가 나사를 박으려고 파 놓은 우묵한 구멍에 견주는 것이 가장 적절하리라."]], [["Though amid all the smoking horror and diabolism of a sea-fight, sharks will be seen longingly gazing up to the ship’s decks, like hungry dogs round a table where red meat is being carved, ready to bolt down every killed man that is tossed to them; and though, while the valiant butchers over the deck-table are thus cannibally carving each other’s live meat with carving-knives all gilded and tasselled, the sharks, also, with their jewel-hilted mouths, are quarrelsomely carving away under the table at the dead meat; and though, were you to turn the whole affair upside down, it would still be pretty much the same thing, that is to say, a shocking sharkish business enough for all parties; and though sharks also are the invariable outriders of all slave ships crossing the Atlantic, systematically trotting alongside, to be handy in case a parcel is to be carried anywhere, or a dead slave to be decently buried; and though one or two other like instances might be set down, touching the set terms, places, and occasions, when sharks do most socially congregate, and most hilariously feast; yet is there no conceivable time or occasion when you will find them in such countless numbers, and in gayer or more jovial spirits, than around a dead sperm whale, moored by night to a whaleship at sea.", "해전의 매캐한 공포와 악마성 한가운데서도, 상어들이 마치 붉은 고기를 썰어 내는 식탁 둘레의 굶주린 개들처럼 배의 갑판을 갈망 어린 눈길로 올려다보며, 자기들에게 던져지는 죽은 자라면 누구든 덥석 삼킬 채비를 하고 있는 모습을 보게 되고, 또 갑판이라는 식탁 위의 용맹한 백정들이 황금빛에 술까지 달린 칼로 서로의 살아 있는 살점을 식인종처럼 베어 내는 동안, 보석 박힌 칼자루 같은 입을 가진 상어들 역시 식탁 아래에서 죽은 살점을 서로 다투며 베어 가고 있는 모습을 보게 되며, 또 그 모든 광경을 거꾸로 뒤집어 놓는다 한들 여전히 거의 같은 일, 곧 모든 편에게 충격적이리만치 상어 같은 짓거리일 따름이며, 또 상어들은 대서양을 건너는 모든 노예선의 변함없는 호위병으로서, 어딘가로 짐 꾸러미가 옮겨지거나 죽은 노예가 점잖게 매장되어야 할 경우에 대비해 빈틈없이 곁에서 종종걸음을 치며 따라붙고, 또 상어들이 가장 사교적으로 모여들어 가장 흥겹게 잔치를 벌이는 정해진 조건과 장소와 경우에 관해 이와 비슷한 사례를 한둘 더 적어 둘 수도 있겠으나, 그럼에도 밤중에 바다에서 고래잡이배에 붙들어 맨 죽은 향유고래 둘레만큼 상어들이 그토록 헤아릴 수 없는 수효로, 그토록 들뜨고 흥겨운 기분으로 모여드는 때나 경우는 달리 생각해 낼 수가 없다."], ["If you have never seen that sight, then suspend your decision about the propriety of devil-worship, and the expediency of conciliating the devil.", "그대가 그 광경을 한 번도 본 적이 없다면, 악마 숭배가 과연 온당한지, 또 악마를 달래는 것이 과연 현명한 일인지에 대한 판단일랑 잠시 미뤄 두는 것이 좋으리라."]], [["But, as yet, Stubb heeded not the mumblings of the banquet that was going on so nigh him, no more than the sharks heeded the smacking of his own epicurean lips.", "그러나 아직까지 스터브는, 상어들이 자신의 미식가다운 입술이 쩝쩝대는 소리에 아랑곳하지 않는 것과 마찬가지로, 바로 곁에서 벌어지는 그 잔치의 우물거림에 조금도 신경 쓰지 않았다."]], [["“Cook, cook!—where’s that old Fleece?” he cried at length, widening his legs still further, as if to form a more secure base for his supper; and, at the same time darting his fork into the dish, as if stabbing with his lance; “cook, you cook!—sail this way, cook!”", "\"요리사, 요리사! 그 늙은 플리스(Fleece) 어디 갔나?\" 그는 마침내 소리쳤다. 마치 자기 만찬을 위해 더 든든한 받침대를 만들기라도 하듯 두 다리를 한층 더 벌리면서, 동시에 작살을 찌르듯 포크를 접시에 푹 꽂으며. \"요리사, 이봐 요리사! 이쪽으로 와, 요리사!\""]], [["The old black, not in any very high glee at having been previously roused from his warm hammock at a most unseasonable hour, came shambling along from his galley, for, like many old blacks, there was something the matter with his knee-pans, which he did not keep well scoured like his other pans; this old Fleece, as they called him, came shuffling and limping along, assisting his step with his tongs, which, after a clumsy fashion, were made of straightened iron hoops; this old Ebony floundered along, and in obedience to the word of command, came to a dead stop on the opposite side of Stubb’s sideboard; when, with both hands folded before him, and resting on his two-legged cane, he bowed his arched back still further over, at the same time sideways inclining his head, so as to bring his best ear into play.", "이 늙은 흑인은, 가장 한가롭지 못한 시각에 따뜻한 해먹에서 끌려 나온 것이 그리 신이 날 리 없는 터라, 주방에서 비척비척 걸어 나왔다. 다른 많은 늙은 흑인들처럼 그도 무릎 종지에 무슨 탈이 있었는데, 그것만은 다른 냄비들처럼 반들반들 닦아 두지 않았던 것이다. 사람들이 플리스라 부르던 이 노인은 발을 끌며 절뚝절뚝 다가오면서, 곧게 편 쇠테로 어설프게 만든 부젓가락에 의지해 걸음을 옮겼다. 이 늙은 흑단(黑檀) 같은 사내는 허우적허우적 걸어와, 명령에 따라 스터브의 식탁 맞은편에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그러고는 두 손을 앞으로 모아 두 갈래로 된 지팡이에 얹고서, 굽은 등을 한층 더 깊이 숙이는 동시에 머리를 옆으로 기울여 가장 잘 들리는 쪽 귀를 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Cook,”", "\"요리사,\""], ["said Stubb, rapidly lifting a rather reddish morsel to his mouth, “don’t you think this steak is rather overdone?", "스터브가 불그스름한 살점 한 조각을 재빨리 입으로 가져가며 말했다. \"이 스테이크가 좀 너무 익은 것 같지 않나?"], ["You’ve been beating this steak too much, cook; it’s too tender.", "자네 이 스테이크를 너무 두들겼어, 요리사. 너무 물렁물렁해."], ["Don’t I always say that to be good, a whale-steak must be tough?", "내가 늘 말하지 않나, 고래 스테이크가 맛있으려면 질겨야 한다고?"], ["There are those sharks now over the side, don’t you see they prefer it tough and rare?", "저기 뱃전 너머의 저 상어들 좀 봐, 저놈들이 질기고 설익은 걸 좋아하는 게 안 보여?"], ["What a shindy they are kicking up!", "저놈들 한바탕 소란을 떨어 대는 꼴이라니!"], ["Cook, go and talk to ’em; tell ’em they are welcome to help themselves civilly, and in moderation, but they must keep quiet.", "요리사, 가서 저놈들한테 한마디 하게. 점잖게, 적당히 알아서들 먹는 건 좋다고, 하지만 좀 조용히 해야 한다고 일러 줘."], ["Blast me, if I can hear my own voice.", "빌어먹을, 내 목소리도 안 들릴 지경이야."], ["Away, cook, and deliver my message.", "어서 가, 요리사, 가서 내 말을 전해."], ["Here, take this lantern,”", "자, 이 등불을 들고 가게.\""], ["snatching one from his sideboard; “now then, go and preach to ’em!”", "그러면서 그는 식탁에서 하나를 낚아챘다. \"자 그럼, 가서 저놈들한테 설교를 하라고!\""]], [["Sullenly taking the offered lantern, old Fleece limped across the deck to the bulwarks; and then, with one hand dropping his light low over the sea, so as to get a good view of his congregation, with the other hand he solemnly flourished his tongs, and leaning far over the side in a mumbling voice began addressing the sharks, while Stubb, softly crawling behind, overheard all that was said.", "늙은 플리스는 마지못해 건네받은 등불을 받아 들고, 절뚝거리며 갑판을 가로질러 뱃전으로 갔다. 그러고는 한 손으로 등불을 바다 위로 낮게 늘어뜨려 자기 신도들을 잘 볼 수 있도록 한 다음, 다른 손으로는 부젓가락을 엄숙하게 휘두르며, 뱃전 너머로 몸을 깊이 기울인 채 우물거리는 목소리로 상어들에게 설교를 시작했다. 그동안 스터브는 살그머니 뒤로 기어와 그가 하는 말을 죄다 엿들었다."]], [["“Fellow-critters: I’se ordered here to say dat you must stop dat dam noise dare.", "\"동포 짐승들이여, 내 여기 명을 받고 와서 이르노니, 너희는 저 빌어먹을 소란을 멈춰야 하느니라."], ["You hear?", "듣고 있느냐?"], ["Stop dat dam smackin’ ob de lip!", "저 빌어먹을 입맛 다시는 소리를 멈추란 말이다!"], ["Massa Stubb say dat you can fill your dam bellies up to de hatchings, but by Gor!", "스터브 나리께서 이르시되, 너희 빌어먹을 배때기를 천장까지 그득 채워도 좋다 하셨으나, 맙소사!"], ["you must stop dat dam racket!”", "저 빌어먹을 야단법석만은 멈춰야 하느니라!\""]], [["“Cook,”", "\"요리사,\""], ["here interposed Stubb, accompanying the word with a sudden slap on the shoulder,—“Cook!", "여기서 스터브가 끼어들며, 그 말과 함께 별안간 어깨를 탁 치고는 말했다. \"요리사!"], ["why, damn your eyes, you mustn’t swear that way when you’re preaching.", "아니, 이런 빌어먹을, 설교를 할 때는 그렇게 욕지거리를 해선 안 되지."], ["That’s no way to convert sinners, cook!”", "그렇게 해선 죄인들을 회개시킬 수가 없어, 요리사!\""]], [["“Who dat?", "\"누가 그래요?"], ["Den preach to him yourself,”", "그럼 나리가 직접 설교를 하든가.\""], ["sullenly turning to go.", "그가 시무룩하게 돌아서서 가려 했다."]], [["“No, cook; go on, go on.”", "\"아냐, 요리사. 계속해, 계속하라고.\""]], [["“Well, den, Belubed fellow-critters:”—", "\"그럼 좋소, 사랑하는 동포 짐승들이여—\""]], [["“Right!”", "\"옳지!\""], ["exclaimed Stubb, approvingly, “coax ’em to it; try that,”", "스터브가 흐뭇하게 외쳤다. \"그렇게 어르고 달래야지."], ["and Fleece continued.", "그렇게 해 봐.\" 그러자 플리스가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Do you is all sharks, and by natur wery woracious, yet I zay to you, fellow-critters, dat dat woraciousness—’top dat dam slappin’ ob de tail!", "\"너희가 죄다 상어이고, 천성이 무척이나 게걸스러운 줄은 안다만, 그래도 내 너희에게 이르노니, 동포 짐승들이여, 그 게걸스러움이란—저 빌어먹을 꼬리 철썩이는 짓을 멈추라!"], ["How you tink to hear, spose you keep up such a dam slappin’ and bitin’ dare?”", "너희가 그렇게 빌어먹을 철썩질에 물어뜯기를 계속하면서 어찌 들을 생각을 하느냐?\""]], [["“Cook,”", "\"요리사,\""], ["cried Stubb, collaring him, “I won’t have that swearing.", "스터브가 그의 멱살을 잡으며 외쳤다. \"그 욕지거리는 안 된다니까."], ["Talk to ’em gentlemanly.”", "점잖게 말하란 말이야.\""]], [["Once more the sermon proceeded.", "또다시 설교가 이어졌다."]], [["“Your woraciousness, fellow-critters, I don’t blame ye so much for; dat is natur, and can’t be helped; but to gobern dat wicked natur, dat is de pint.", "\"너희의 게걸스러움은, 동포 짐승들이여, 내 그리 탓하지는 않으마. 그건 천성이고 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이니."], ["You is sharks, sartin; but if you gobern de shark in you, why den you be angel; for all angel is not’ing more dan de shark well goberned.", "허나 그 못된 천성을 다스리는 것, 그것이 바로 요체니라. 너희는 분명 상어다."], ["Now, look here, bred’ren, just try wonst to be cibil, a helping yourselbs from dat whale.", "허나 너희 안의 상어를 다스린다면, 그때 너희는 천사가 되느니라. 무릇 천사란 천성을 잘 다스린 상어에 지나지 않으니."], ["Don’t be tearin’ de blubber out your neighbour’s mout, I say.", "자, 이보게 형제들이여, 단 한 번만이라도 점잖게 굴어 보게, 저 고래에서 제 몫을 챙기되 말일세. 이웃의 입에서 비계를 잡아채지 말란 말이다."], ["Is not one shark dood right as toder to dat whale?", "어느 한 상어가 다른 상어보다 저 고래에 더 큰 권리를 가졌다더냐?"], ["And, by Gor, none on you has de right to dat whale; dat whale belong to some one else.", "게다가 맙소사, 너희 가운데 아무도 저 고래에 권리가 없느니라. 저 고래는 다른 누군가의 것이니."], ["I know some o’ you has berry brig mout, brigger dan oders; but den de brig mouts sometimes has de small bellies; so dat de brigness of de mout is not to swaller wid, but to bit off de blubber for de small fry ob sharks, dat can’t get into de scrouge to help demselves.”", "내 알기로 너희 가운데 몇몇은 주둥이가 무척 커서, 다른 놈들보다 더 크다만, 그러나 주둥이 큰 놈이 때로는 배때기가 작은 법. 그러니 주둥이가 큰 까닭은 삼키라는 것이 아니라, 그 비계를 베어 내어, 비집고 들어와 제 몫을 챙길 수 없는 송사리 같은 작은 상어들에게 나눠 주라는 것이니라.\""]], [["“Well done, old Fleece!”", "\"잘했어, 늙은 플리스!\" 스터브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Stubb, “that’s Christianity; go on.”", "\"그게 바로 기독교 정신이야. 계속해.\""]], [["“No use goin’ on; de dam willains will keep a scougin’ and slappin’ each oder, Massa Stubb; dey don’t hear one word; no use a-preachin’ to such dam g’uttons as you call ’em, till dare bellies is full, and dare bellies is bottomless; and when dey do get ’em full, dey wont hear you den; for den dey sink in de sea, go fast to sleep on de coral, and can’t hear not’ing at all, no more, for eber and eber.”", "\"계속해 봐야 소용없소, 스터브 나리. 저 빌어먹을 악당들은 서로 떠밀고 후려치기만 할 뿐, 한마디도 안 듣소. 나리가 부르는 대로 저런 빌어먹을 식충이들한테는, 배때기가 그득 찰 때까지는 설교해 봐야 소용없는데, 저놈들 배때기는 바닥이 없소. 그리고 배가 정말 그득 차고 나면, 그때는 또 나리 말을 안 듣소. 그땐 바닷속으로 가라앉아 산호 위에서 곯아떨어져, 영영토록 아무것도, 더는 아무것도 못 듣소.\""]], [["“Upon my soul, I am about of the same opinion; so give the benediction, Fleece, and I’ll away to my supper.”", "\"내 영혼을 걸고, 나도 거의 같은 생각이네. 그러니 축도나 해 주게, 플리스. 나는 내 만찬으로 돌아가야겠으니.\""]], [["Upon this, Fleece, holding both hands over the fishy mob, raised his shrill voice, and cried—", "이 말에 플리스는 두 손을 저 물고기 떼 위로 쳐들고, 새된 목소리를 높여 외쳤다—"]], [["“Cussed fellow-critters!", "\"저주받은 동포 짐승들이여!"], ["Kick up de damndest row as ever you can; fill your dam’ bellies ’till dey bust—and den die.”", "너희가 부릴 수 있는 가장 빌어먹을 야단법석이나 실컷 부려라. 너희 빌어먹을 배때기를 터지도록 채우고—그러고는 뒈져 버려라.\""]], [["“Now, cook,”", "\"자, 요리사,\""], ["said Stubb, resuming his supper at the capstan; “stand just where you stood before, there, over against me, and pay particular attention.”", "스터브가 캡스턴에서 만찬을 다시 들며 말했다. \"아까 섰던 바로 그 자리, 거기, 내 맞은편에 서서, 똑똑히 귀를 기울이게.\""]], [["“All dention,”", "\"귀 쫑긋.\""], ["said Fleece, again stooping over upon his tongs in the desired position.", "플리스가 다시 부젓가락에 몸을 기대며 시키는 대로 자세를 잡고 말했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Stubb, helping himself freely meanwhile; “I shall now go back to the subject of this steak.", "스터브가 그러는 동안 마음껏 먹어 대며 말했다. \"이제 이 스테이크 이야기로 돌아가지."], ["In the first place, how old are you, cook?”", "우선 말이야, 자네 나이가 몇인가, 요리사?\""]], [["“What dat do wid de ’teak,”", "\"그게 스테이크하고 무슨 상관이오.\""], ["said the old black, testily.", "늙은 흑인이 퉁명스레 말했다."]], [["“Silence!", "\"닥쳐!"], ["How old are you, cook?”", "나이가 몇이냐고, 요리사?\""]], [["“’Bout ninety, dey say,”", "\"아흔쯤 됐다고들 합디다.\""], ["he gloomily muttered.", "그가 침울하게 중얼거렸다."]], [["“And you have lived in this world hard upon one hundred years, cook, and don’t know yet how to cook a whale-steak?”", "\"이 세상을 백 년 가까이 살아오고도, 아직 고래 스테이크 하나 제대로 구울 줄을 모른단 말인가, 요리사?\""], ["rapidly bolting another mouthful at the last word, so that morsel seemed a continuation of the question.", "마지막 말과 함께 또 한 입을 재빨리 삼키는 통에, 그 살점이 마치 질문의 연장처럼 보였다."], ["“Where were you born, cook?”", "\"자넨 어디서 태어났나, 요리사?\""]], [["“’Hind de hatchway, in ferry-boat, goin’ ober de Roanoke.”", "\"승강구 뒤쪽에서요, 로어노크(Roanoke) 건너던 나룻배에서.\""]], [["“Born in a ferry-boat!", "\"나룻배에서 태어났다고!"], ["That’s queer, too.", "그것 참 별나기도 하구먼."], ["But I want to know what country you were born in, cook!”", "하지만 내가 알고 싶은 건 자네가 어느 나라에서 태어났느냐는 거야, 요리사!\""]], [["“Didn’t I say de Roanoke country?”", "\"로어노크 고장이라고 안 했소?\""], ["he cried sharply.", "그가 날카롭게 외쳤다."]], [["“No, you didn’t, cook; but I’ll tell you what I’m coming to, cook.", "\"아니, 안 했어, 요리사. 하지만 내가 무슨 말을 하려는지 일러 주지, 요리사."], ["You must go home and be born over again; you don’t know how to cook a whale-steak yet.”", "자넨 고향에 돌아가서 다시 태어나야겠어. 아직 고래 스테이크 굽는 법도 모르니 말이야.\""]], [["“Bress my soul, if I cook noder one,”", "\"내 영혼을 걸고, 다시는 안 굽겠소.\""], ["he growled, angrily, turning round to depart.", "그가 성을 내며 으르렁대고는 떠나려고 돌아섰다."]], [["“Come back, cook;—here, hand me those tongs;—now take that bit of steak there, and tell me if you think that steak cooked as it should be?", "\"돌아와, 요리사.—자, 그 부젓가락 이리 주게.—자, 거기 그 스테이크 한 점을 집어서, 그게 제대로 구워졌다고 생각하는지 말해 보게."], ["Take it, I say”—holding the tongs towards him—“take it, and taste it.”", "집으라니까\"—부젓가락을 그에게 내밀며—\"집어서, 맛을 보란 말이야.\""]], [["Faintly smacking his withered lips over it for a moment, the old negro muttered, “Best cooked ’teak I eber taste; joosy, berry joosy.”", "늙은 흑인은 잠시 시든 입술로 그것을 희미하게 쩝쩝거려 보더니 중얼거렸다. \"내 평생 맛본 중 제일 잘 구운 스테이크요. 촉촉하고, 아주 촉촉합니다.\""]], [["“Cook,”", "\"요리사,\""], ["said Stubb, squaring himself once more; “do you belong to the church?”", "스터브가 다시 한번 몸을 떡 버티며 말했다. \"자넨 교회에 다니나?\""]], [["“Passed one once in Cape-Down,”", "\"한번은 케이프타운(Cape-Town)에서 하나 지나친 적이 있소.\""], ["said the old man sullenly.", "늙은이가 시무룩하게 말했다."]], [["“And you have once in your life passed a holy church in Cape-Town, where you doubtless overheard a holy parson addressing his hearers as his beloved fellow-creatures, have you, cook!", "\"그러니까 자넨 평생에 단 한 번 케이프타운에서 거룩한 교회를 지나쳤고, 거기서 틀림없이 거룩한 목사가 자기 청중을 사랑하는 동포 짐승들이라 부르며 설교하는 걸 엿들었다 이거지, 요리사!"], ["And yet you come here, and tell me such a dreadful lie as you did just now, eh?”", "그래 놓고도 자넨 여기 와서, 방금 같은 그런 끔찍한 거짓말을 했다 이 말이지, 응?\""], ["said Stubb.", "스터브가 말했다."], ["“Where do you expect to go to, cook?”", "\"자넨 어디로 갈 작정인가, 요리사?\""]], [["“Go to bed berry soon,”", "\"곧 잠자리로 갈 작정이오.\""], ["he mumbled, half-turning as he spoke.", "그가 말하면서 반쯤 돌아서며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Avast!", "\"멈춰!"], ["heave to!", "정선하라고!"], ["I mean when you die, cook.", "내 말은 자네가 죽으면 말이야, 요리사."], ["It’s an awful question.", "이건 엄숙한 질문이야."], ["Now what’s your answer?”", "자, 자네 대답은 뭔가?\""]], [["“When dis old brack man dies,” said the negro slowly, changing his whole air and demeanor, “he hisself won’t go nowhere; but some bressed angel will come and fetch him.”", "\"이 늙은 흑인이 죽으면 말이오,\" 흑인이 천천히 말했다. 그의 분위기와 태도가 온통 달라졌다. \"그 자신은 아무 데도 안 가오. 다만 어떤 복된 천사가 와서 그를 데려갈 거요.\""]], [["“Fetch him?", "\"데려가?"], ["How?", "어떻게?"], ["In a coach and four, as they fetched Elijah?", "네 마리 말이 끄는 마차로, 엘리야를 데려가듯이?"], ["And fetch him where?”", "그래서 그를 어디로 데려간다는 건가?\""]], [["“Up dere,”", "\"저 위로요.\""], ["said Fleece, holding his tongs straight over his head, and keeping it there very solemnly.", "플리스가 부젓가락을 머리 위로 곧추세워 들고는, 그것을 아주 엄숙하게 그 자리에 그대로 두며 말했다."]], [["“So, then, you expect to go up into our main-top, do you, cook, when you are dead?", "\"그러니까, 자넨 죽으면 우리 큰 돛 꼭대기로 올라갈 작정이다 이거지, 요리사?"], ["But don’t you know the higher you climb, the colder it gets?", "하지만 자네 모르나, 높이 올라갈수록 더 추워진다는 걸?"], ["Main-top, eh?”", "큰 돛 꼭대기라, 응?\""]], [["“Didn’t say dat t’all,”", "\"그런 말 한 적 없소.\""], ["said Fleece, again in the sulks.", "플리스가 다시 부루퉁해져 말했다."]], [["“You said up there, didn’t you?", "\"저 위라고 했잖아, 안 그래?"], ["and now look yourself, and see where your tongs are pointing.", "자, 이제 자네 자신을 좀 봐, 그리고 자네 부젓가락이 어딜 가리키는지 봐."], ["But, perhaps you expect to get into heaven by crawling through the lubber’s hole, cook; but, no, no, cook, you don’t get there, except you go the regular way, round by the rigging.", "하지만 어쩌면 자넨 풋내기 구멍으로 기어 올라 천국에 들어갈 작정인지도 모르지, 요리사. 하지만 안 돼, 안 돼, 요리사, 거긴 정식 길로, 삭구를 빙 돌아가지 않고서는 못 들어가."], ["It’s a ticklish business, but must be done, or else it’s no go.", "까다로운 일이지만, 해내야만 하지, 안 그러면 헛일이야."], ["But none of us are in heaven yet.", "하지만 우리 가운데 아직 천국에 가 있는 사람은 아무도 없어."], ["Drop your tongs, cook, and hear my orders.", "부젓가락 내려놓게, 요리사, 그리고 내 명령을 듣게."], ["Do ye hear?", "듣고 있나?"], ["Hold your hat in one hand, and clap t’other a’top of your heart, when I’m giving my orders, cook.", "내가 명령을 내릴 때는, 한 손에 모자를 들고, 다른 손은 가슴 위에 척 얹게, 요리사."], ["What!", "뭐야!"], ["that your heart, there?—that’s your gizzard!", "그게 자네 가슴이라고, 거기가?—그건 모래주머니잖아!"], ["Aloft!", "위로!"], ["aloft!—that’s it—now you have it.", "위로!—그래, 바로 거기—이제 됐어."], ["Hold it there now, and pay attention.”", "자, 거기에 손을 얹고, 귀를 기울이게.\""]], [["“All ’dention,”", "\"귀 쫑긋.\""], ["said the old black, with both hands placed as desired, vainly wriggling his grizzled head, as if to get both ears in front at one and the same time.", "늙은 흑인이 시키는 대로 두 손을 얹고서, 두 귀를 한꺼번에 앞쪽으로 가져오려는 듯 희끗희끗한 머리를 부질없이 꿈틀거리며 말했다."]], [["“Well then, cook, you see this whale-steak of yours was so very bad, that I have put it out of sight as soon as possible; you see that, don’t you?", "\"자 그럼, 요리사, 자네 이 고래 스테이크가 어찌나 형편없던지, 내가 되도록 빨리 안 보이게 치워 버렸지, 그건 자네도 알지, 안 그래?"], ["Well, for the future, when you cook another whale-steak for my private table here, the capstan, I’ll tell you what to do so as not to spoil it by overdoing.", "자, 앞으로 자네가 여기 내 개인 식탁, 곧 이 캡스턴에 올릴 고래 스테이크를 또 구울 때는, 너무 익혀서 망치지 않도록 어떻게 해야 하는지 내 일러 주지."], ["Hold the steak in one hand, and show a live coal to it with the other; that done, dish it; d’ye hear?", "한 손에 스테이크를 들고, 다른 손으로 시뻘건 숯덩이를 한 번 보여 주게. 그게 끝나면 접시에 담아."], ["And now to-morrow, cook, when we are cutting in the fish, be sure you stand by to get the tips of his fins; have them put in pickle.", "알겠나? 그리고 자, 내일 말이야, 요리사, 우리가 그 물고기를 토막 낼 때, 그 지느러미 끄트머리를 챙길 수 있게 꼭 곁에 붙어 있게."], ["As for the ends of the flukes, have them soused, cook.", "그것들을 식초에 절여 두라고. 그리고 꼬리지느러미 끝은, 양념에 푹 담가 두게, 요리사."], ["There, now ye may go.”", "자, 이제 가도 좋아.\""]], [["But Fleece had hardly got three paces off, when he was recalled.", "그러나 플리스가 채 세 걸음도 떼기 전에, 그는 다시 불려 왔다."]], [["“Cook, give me cutlets for supper to-morrow night in the mid-watch.", "\"요리사, 내일 밤 중간 당직 때 내 야식으로 커틀릿을 만들어 주게."], ["D’ye hear?", "듣고 있나?"], ["away you sail, then.—Halloa!", "그럼 어서 가 봐.—어이!"], ["stop!", "잠깐!"], ["make a bow before you go.—Avast heaving again!", "가기 전에 절을 하고 가야지.—또 멈추라니까!"], ["Whale-balls for breakfast—don’t forget.”", "아침으로는 고래 알을—잊지 말게.\""]], [["“Wish, by gor! whale eat him, ’stead of him eat whale.", "\"맙소사, 차라리 고래가 저 양반을 잡아먹는 게 낫겠네, 저 양반이 고래를 먹느니."], ["I’m bressed if he ain’t more of shark dan Massa Shark hisself,”", "내 영혼을 걸고, 저 양반은 상어 나리 본인보다 더한 상어야.\""], ["muttered the old man, limping away; with which sage ejaculation he went to his hammock.", "늙은이가 절뚝거리며 멀어져 가면서 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 이 현명한 탄식을 끝으로 자기 해먹으로 갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 65. The Whale as a Dish.", "# CHAPTER 65. The Whale as a Dish."]], [["That mortal man should feed upon the creature that feeds his lamp, and, like Stubb, eat him by his own light, as you may say; this seems so outlandish a thing that one must needs go a little into the history and philosophy of it.", "죽어야 할 인간이 제 등불을 살찌우는 그 짐승을 먹어야 한다니, 게다가 스텁(Stubb)처럼 말하자면 제 등불 빛으로 그놈을 먹어야 한다니, 이것은 너무도 기이한 일이어서 그 역사와 철학을 조금쯤 들여다볼 필요가 있다."]], [["It is upon record, that three centuries ago the tongue of the Right Whale was esteemed a great delicacy in France, and commanded large prices there.", "기록에 따르면, 삼백 년 전 프랑스에서는 참고래(Right Whale)의 혀가 진귀한 별미로 여겨져 비싼 값에 거래되었다고 한다."], ["Also, that in Henry VIIIth’s time, a certain cook of the court obtained a handsome reward for inventing an admirable sauce to be eaten with barbacued porpoises, which, you remember, are a species of whale.", "또한 헨리 8세(Henry) 시절에는 궁정의 어느 요리사가 바비큐로 구운 돌고래에 곁들여 먹을 훌륭한 소스를 고안해 낸 덕분에 후한 상을 받았다고 하는데, 기억하다시피 돌고래도 고래의 한 종류다."], ["Porpoises, indeed, are to this day considered fine eating.", "사실 돌고래는 오늘날까지도 훌륭한 식재료로 여겨진다."], ["The meat is made into balls about the size of billiard balls, and being well seasoned and spiced might be taken for turtle-balls or veal balls.", "그 고기는 당구공만 한 크기의 완자로 빚어지는데, 양념과 향신료를 잘 입히면 자라 완자나 송아지 완자로 착각할 만하다."], ["The old monks of Dunfermline were very fond of them.", "던펌린의 늙은 수도사들은 이것을 무척 좋아했다."], ["They had a great porpoise grant from the crown.", "그들은 왕실로부터 돌고래에 관한 큰 하사권을 받고 있었다."]], [["The fact is, that among his hunters at least, the whale would by all hands be considered a noble dish, were there not so much of him; but when you come to sit down before a meat-pie nearly one hundred feet long, it takes away your appetite.", "사실인즉, 적어도 고래를 사냥하는 자들 사이에서라면 고래는 누구나 인정하는 고귀한 요리로 여겨질 만하지만, 다만 그 양이 너무도 많다는 것이 문제다. 그러나 길이가 거의 백 피트(약 30미터)에 이르는 고기 파이를 앞에 두고 앉게 되면, 그만 입맛이 싹 가셔 버린다."], ["Only the most unprejudiced of men like Stubb, nowadays partake of cooked whales; but the Esquimaux are not so fastidious.", "스텁처럼 편견이라곤 없는 극소수의 사람들만이 요즘 들어 조리한 고래 고기를 먹을 뿐이다. 그러나 에스키모인들은 그렇게 까다롭지 않다."], ["We all know how they live upon whales, and have rare old vintages of prime old train oil.", "그들이 고래로 연명하며, 진귀하고 오래 묵은 일등급 고래 기름을 묵은 술처럼 갖추고 있다는 것은 우리 모두가 아는 바다."], ["Zogranda, one of their most famous doctors, recommends strips of blubber for infants, as being exceedingly juicy and nourishing.", "그들의 가장 이름난 의사 가운데 하나인 조그란다는 갓난아이에게 고래 지방 띠를 권하는데, 그것이 더없이 즙이 많고 영양가가 높다는 이유에서다."], ["And this reminds me that certain Englishmen, who long ago were accidentally left in Greenland by a whaling vessel—that these men actually lived for several months on the mouldy scraps of whales which had been left ashore after trying out the blubber.", "그러고 보니 떠오르는 이야기가 있다. 오래전 어느 포경선에 의해 그린란드에 우연히 버려진 영국인들이 있었는데, 이 사람들은 실제로 지방을 끓여 내고 난 뒤 해안에 버려진 곰팡이 핀 고래 찌꺼기로 여러 달을 연명했다고 한다."], ["Among the Dutch whalemen these scraps are called “fritters”; which, indeed, they greatly resemble, being brown and crisp, and smelling something like old Amsterdam housewives’ dough-nuts or oly-cooks, when fresh.", "네덜란드 고래잡이들 사이에서 이 찌꺼기는 \"프리터(fritter)\"라 불리는데, 사실 그 모양이 프리터와 매우 닮았다. 갈색에 바삭하고, 갓 구워 냈을 때는 옛 암스테르담 주부들이 만드는 도넛이나 올리쿡(oly-cook) 같은 냄새가 나기 때문이다."], ["They have such an eatable look that the most self-denying stranger can hardly keep his hands off.", "어찌나 먹음직스러워 보이는지, 아무리 절제심 강한 이방인이라도 좀처럼 거기에 손대지 않고는 못 배긴다."]], [["But what further depreciates the whale as a civilized dish, is his exceeding richness.", "* 올리쿡: 네덜란드식 튀김 과자로, 오늘날 도넛의 기원이 된 음식. 그러나 고래를 문명인의 요리로서 한층 더 격을 떨어뜨리는 것은 그 지나친 기름기다."], ["He is the great prize ox of the sea, too fat to be delicately good.", "고래는 바다의 거대한 우량 황소와도 같아서, 너무 기름져 섬세하게 좋은 맛을 내지 못한다."], ["Look at his hump, which would be as fine eating as the buffalo’s (which is esteemed a rare dish), were it not such a solid pyramid of fat.", "그 혹을 보라. 만약 그것이 그토록 단단한 기름 덩어리의 피라미드가 아니었다면, 들소의 혹(진귀한 요리로 여겨지는 그것)만큼이나 훌륭한 먹거리가 되었으리라."], ["But the spermaceti itself, how bland and creamy that is; like the transparent, half-jellied, white meat of a cocoanut in the third month of its growth, yet far too rich to supply a substitute for butter.", "그러나 향유(spermaceti) 그 자체는 어찌나 부드럽고 크림처럼 매끄러운지, 자라난 지 석 달째 된 코코넛의 투명하고 반쯤 젤리처럼 굳은 흰 과육과도 같으나, 버터의 대용으로 쓰기에는 그 맛이 너무도 진하다."], ["Nevertheless, many whalemen have a method of absorbing it into some other substance, and then partaking of it.", "그럼에도 많은 고래잡이들은 그것을 다른 어떤 재료에 스며들게 한 뒤 먹는 방법을 알고 있다."], ["In the long try watches of the night it is a common thing for the seamen to dip their ship-biscuit into the huge oil-pots and let them fry there awhile.", "밤의 기나긴 기름 짜는 당직을 서는 동안, 선원들이 선상용 비스킷을 커다란 기름 솥에 담가 잠시 튀겨 내는 것은 흔한 일이다."], ["Many a good supper have I thus made.", "나도 이렇게 해서 맛 좋은 저녁을 여러 번 먹었다."]], [["In the case of a small Sperm Whale the brains are accounted a fine dish.", "작은 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 경우에는 골이 별미로 여겨진다. 도끼로 두개골이라는 함을 쪼개어 두 개의 통통하고 희끄무레한 뇌엽(꼭 커다란 푸딩 두 덩이를 닮았다)을 꺼낸 다음, 그것을 밀가루와 섞어 더없이 맛깔스러운 요리로 만들어 내는데, 그 풍미가 송아지 머리 고기와 어느 정도 비슷하다."], ["The casket of the skull is broken into with an axe, and the two plump, whitish lobes being withdrawn (precisely resembling two large puddings), they are then mixed with flour, and cooked into a most delectable mess, in flavor somewhat resembling calves’ head, which is quite a dish among some epicures; and every one knows that some young bucks among the epicures, by continually dining upon calves’ brains, by and by get to have a little brains of their own, so as to be able to tell a calf’s head from their own heads; which, indeed, requires uncommon discrimination.", "송아지 머리 고기로 말하자면 어떤 미식가들 사이에서는 제법 인기 있는 요리다. 누구나 알다시피, 미식가 가운데 어떤 젊은 멋쟁이들은 송아지 골을 줄곧 먹어 댄 끝에 차차 제 머릿속에도 골이 조금 생겨나, 송아지 머리와 제 머리를 구별할 수 있게 되는데, 이는 실로 비범한 분별력을 요하는 일이다."], ["And that is the reason why a young buck with an intelligent looking calf’s head before him, is somehow one of the saddest sights you can see.", "그리고 바로 그런 까닭에, 영리해 보이는 송아지 머리 고기를 앞에 둔 젊은 멋쟁이의 모습은 어쩐지 세상에서 볼 수 있는 가장 서글픈 광경 가운데 하나다. 그 머리는 \"브루투스(Brute), 너마저!\""], ["The head looks a sort of reproachfully at him, with an “Et tu Brute!” expression.", "하는 표정으로 그를 어딘가 책망하듯 바라보는 것이다."]], [["It is not, perhaps, entirely because the whale is so excessively unctuous that landsmen seem to regard the eating of him with abhorrence; that appears to result, in some way, from the consideration before mentioned: i.e.", "뭍사람들이 고래 먹기를 혐오스럽게 여기는 것은, 아마도 고래가 그토록 지나치게 기름지기 때문만은 아닐 것이다. 그것은 어떤 식으로든 앞서 말한 고려에서 비롯되는 듯하다."], ["that a man should eat a newly murdered thing of the sea, and eat it too by its own light.", "곧, 인간이 바다에서 갓 죽인 것을 먹어야 한다는 것, 게다가 그것을 그 자신의 빛으로 먹어야 한다는 것 말이다. 그러나 의심할 바 없이, 최초로 황소를 죽인 자는 살인자로 여겨졌으리라."], ["But no doubt the first man that ever murdered an ox was regarded as a murderer; perhaps he was hung; and if he had been put on his trial by oxen, he certainly would have been; and he certainly deserved it if any murderer does.", "어쩌면 그는 교수형을 당했을지도 모른다. 그리고 만약 그가 황소들 앞에서 재판을 받았더라면 틀림없이 교수형을 당했을 터이고, 또 어떤 살인자가 그래 마땅하다면 그 역시 마땅히 그러했을 것이다."], ["Go to the meat-market of a Saturday night and see the crowds of live bipeds staring up at the long rows of dead quadrupeds.", "토요일 밤 고기 시장에 가서, 길게 늘어선 죽은 네발짐승들을 멍하니 올려다보는 산 두발짐승들의 무리를 보라."], ["Does not that sight take a tooth out of the cannibal’s jaw?", "그 광경이 식인종의 턱에서 이빨 하나쯤 빼놓지 않겠는가? 식인종이라고?"], ["Cannibals? who is not a cannibal?", "도대체 누가 식인종이 아니란 말인가?"], ["I tell you it will be more tolerable for the Fejee that salted down a lean missionary in his cellar against a coming famine; it will be more tolerable for that provident Fejee, I say, in the day of judgment, than for thee, civilized and enlightened gourmand, who nailest geese to the ground and feastest on their bloated livers in thy paté-de-foie-gras.", "내 그대에게 말하노니, 다가올 기근에 대비해 비쩍 마른 선교사를 제 지하실에 소금에 절여 둔 피지(Fejee) 사람이, 곧 그 앞날을 내다본 피지 사람이, 심판의 날에 더 너그러운 처분을 받을 것이다. 거위를 땅에 못 박아 두고 그 부풀어 오른 간을 푸아그라로 즐기는, 문명화되고 계몽된 그대 미식가보다는 말이다."]], [["But Stubb, he eats the whale by its own light, does he?", "그런데 스텁은 고래를 그 자신의 빛으로 먹는다고?"], ["and that is adding insult to injury, is it?", "그러니 그것은 상처에 모욕까지 더하는 짓이라고? 그대의 칼 손잡이를 보라."], ["Look at your knife-handle, there, my civilized and enlightened gourmand dining off that roast beef, what is that handle made of?—what but the bones of the brother of the very ox you are eating?", "거기, 그 구운 쇠고기를 먹고 있는 문명화되고 계몽된 그대 미식가여, 그 손잡이는 무엇으로 만들어졌는가? 바로 그대가 먹고 있는 그 황소의 형제뻘 되는 짐승의 뼈가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["And what do you pick your teeth with, after devouring that fat goose?", "그리고 그 살진 거위를 다 먹어 치운 뒤에 그대는 무엇으로 이를 쑤시는가?"], ["With a feather of the same fowl.", "바로 그 같은 새의 깃털로다."], ["And with what quill did the Secretary of the Society for the Suppression of Cruelty to Ganders formally indite his circulars?", "그리고 거위 학대 방지 협회의 간사는 도대체 어떤 깃펜으로 그 회람을 격식 차려 적어 내려갔단 말인가?"], ["It is only within the last month or two that that society passed a resolution to patronize nothing but steel pens.", "그 협회가 강철 펜 외에는 아무것도 쓰지 않기로 결의한 것은 겨우 한두 달 전의 일이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 66. The Shark Massacre.", "# CHAPTER 66. The Shark Massacre."]], [["When in the Southern Fishery, a captured Sperm Whale, after long and weary toil, is brought alongside late at night, it is not, as a general thing at least, customary to proceed at once to the business of cutting him in.", "남쪽 어장에서 향유고래를 잡은 뒤, 길고 고된 수고 끝에 밤늦게야 배 옆구리에 그것을 끌어다 붙였을 경우, 적어도 일반적인 관례로는 곧바로 해체 작업에 착수하지 않는다."], ["For that business is an exceedingly laborious one; is not very soon completed; and requires all hands to set about it.", "그 작업이란 지극히 고된 일이어서, 좀처럼 빨리 끝나지 않고, 또 선원 전부가 달려들어야 하기 때문이다."], ["Therefore, the common usage is to take in all sail; lash the helm a’lee; and then send every one below to his hammock till daylight, with the reservation that, until that time, anchor-watches shall be kept; that is, two and two for an hour, each couple, the crew in rotation shall mount the deck to see that all goes well.", "그러므로 흔히 따르는 방식은 돛을 모두 거두고, 키를 바람 부는 쪽으로 묶어 둔 다음, 날이 밝을 때까지 모두를 갑판 아래 해먹으로 내려보내는 것이다. 다만 그때까지는 정박 당직을 세운다는 조건이 붙는데, 즉 두 사람씩 한 시간 간격으로 짝을 지어, 선원들이 차례로 갑판에 올라가 모든 일이 무사한지 살피는 것이다."]], [["But sometimes, especially upon the Line in the Pacific, this plan will not answer at all; because such incalculable hosts of sharks gather round the moored carcase, that were he left so for six hours, say, on a stretch, little more than the skeleton would be visible by morning.", "그러나 때로는, 특히 태평양 적도선 부근에서는 이 방식이 전혀 통하지 않는다. 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 상어 떼가 매어 둔 사체 주위로 몰려들기 때문인데, 가령 그것을 여섯 시간쯤 내처 그대로 두었다가는 아침이 되면 뼈대 말고는 거의 아무것도 보이지 않게 될 지경이다."], ["In most other parts of the ocean, however, where these fish do not so largely abound, their wondrous voracity can be at times considerably diminished, by vigorously stirring them up with sharp whaling-spades, a procedure notwithstanding, which, in some instances, only seems to tickle them into still greater activity.", "그렇지만 이 물고기들이 그토록 많지 않은 바다의 대부분 다른 해역에서는, 날카로운 고래잡이 삽으로 그것들을 힘차게 휘저어 쑤셔 대면 그 놀라운 식탐을 때때로 상당히 누그러뜨릴 수 있다. 물론 어떤 경우에는 그런 짓이 도리어 그것들을 자극해 더욱더 기승을 부리게 만드는 것처럼 보이기도 하지만 말이다."], ["But it was not thus in the present case with the Pequod’s sharks; though, to be sure, any man unaccustomed to such sights, to have looked over her side that night, would have almost thought the whole round sea was one huge cheese, and those sharks the maggots in it.", "그러나 이번 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 상어들의 경우는 그렇지 않았다. 하기야 그런 광경에 익숙지 않은 사람이 그날 밤 뱃전 너머를 내려다보았다면, 둥근 바다 전체가 하나의 거대한 치즈 덩어리이고 저 상어들이 그 속에서 들끓는 구더기라고 생각했을 법도 하다."]], [["Nevertheless, upon Stubb setting the anchor-watch after his supper was concluded; and when, accordingly, Queequeg and a forecastle seaman came on deck, no small excitement was created among the sharks; for immediately suspending the cutting stages over the side, and lowering three lanterns, so that they cast long gleams of light over the turbid sea, these two mariners, darting their long whaling-spades, kept up an incessant murdering of the sharks,* by striking the keen steel deep into their skulls, seemingly their only vital part.", "그럼에도 불구하고, 스터브(Stubb)가 저녁 식사를 끝낸 뒤 정박 당직을 세우자, 그리고 그에 따라 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 선수루의 한 선원이 갑판에 올라오자, 상어들 사이에서는 적잖은 소동이 일었다. 두 뱃사람이 즉시 해체 발판을 뱃전 너머로 늘어뜨리고 등불 세 개를 내려, 그 등불이 탁한 바다 위로 긴 빛줄기를 드리우게 한 다음, 자신들의 긴 고래잡이 삽을 내리꽂으며 쉴 새 없이 상어들을 죽여 나갔던 것이다.* 날카로운 강철을 그것들의 두개골 깊숙이 박아 넣었는데, 그곳이야말로 유일한 급소인 듯했다."], ["But in the foamy confusion of their mixed and struggling hosts, the marksmen could not always hit their mark; and this brought about new revelations of the incredible ferocity of the foe.", "그러나 서로 뒤엉켜 발버둥 치는 무리가 일으키는 거품투성이 아수라장 속에서 두 사수는 늘 표적을 명중시킬 수는 없었고, 이로 인해 그 적들의 믿기 어려운 흉포함이 새삼 드러나게 되었다."], ["They viciously snapped, not only at each other’s disembowelments, but like flexible bows, bent round, and bit their own; till those entrails seemed swallowed over and over again by the same mouth, to be oppositely voided by the gaping wound.", "그것들은 서로의 쏟아진 창자에 사납게 달려들 뿐 아니라, 유연한 활처럼 몸을 둥글게 구부려 제 창자를 물어뜯었다. 그리하여 그 내장들은 같은 입으로 몇 번이고 거듭 삼켜졌다가, 떡 벌어진 상처 구멍으로 도로 게워져 나오는 듯했다."], ["Nor was this all.", "이뿐만이 아니었다."], ["It was unsafe to meddle with the corpses and ghosts of these creatures.", "이 짐승들의 시체와 망령을 함부로 건드리는 것은 위험한 일이었다."], ["A sort of generic or Pantheistic vitality seemed to lurk in their very joints and bones, after what might be called the individual life had departed.", "이른바 개체의 생명이라 할 만한 것이 떠나간 뒤에도, 그것들의 관절과 뼈마디 자체에는 일종의 종(種) 전체에 깃든, 혹은 범신론적인 생명력이 도사리고 있는 듯했다."], ["Killed and hoisted on deck for the sake of his skin, one of these sharks almost took poor Queequeg’s hand off, when he tried to shut down the dead lid of his murderous jaw.", "가죽을 벗기려고 죽여서 갑판 위로 끌어 올린 상어 한 마리는, 퀴퀘그가 그 잔혹한 턱의 죽은 뚜껑을 닫아 보려 하자 하마터면 가엾은 퀴퀘그의 손을 잘라 낼 뻔했다."]], [["*The whaling-spade used for cutting-in is made of the very best steel; is about the bigness of a man’s spread hand; and in general shape, corresponds to the garden implement after which it is named; only its sides are perfectly flat, and its upper end considerably narrower than the lower.", "* 해체 작업에 쓰는 고래잡이 삽은 더없이 좋은 강철로 만들어지며, 크기는 사람이 손을 활짝 편 정도이고, 전체적인 모양은 그 이름의 유래가 된 정원용 연장과 비슷하다. 다만 양옆이 완벽하게 평평하고, 위쪽 끝이 아래쪽보다 상당히 좁다."], ["This weapon is always kept as sharp as possible; and when being used is occasionally honed, just like a razor.", "이 무기는 늘 가능한 한 날카롭게 갈아 두며, 쓸 때는 면도칼처럼 이따금 숫돌에 갈기도 한다."], ["In its socket, a stiff pole, from twenty to thirty feet long, is inserted for a handle.", "그 자루 구멍에는 손잡이로 쓸 스무 자에서 서른 자(약 6미터에서 9미터) 길이의 단단한 장대를 끼운다."]], [["“Queequeg no care what god made him shark,”", "\"퀴퀘그, 어떤 신이 상어 만들었든 상관없어.\" 야만인은 괴로운 듯 손을 위아래로 흔들며 말했다."], ["said the savage, agonizingly lifting his hand up and down; “wedder Fejee god or Nantucket god; but de god wat made shark must be one dam Ingin.”", "\"피지의 신이든 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 신이든 상관없어. 하지만 상어 만든 신은 분명 빌어먹을 인디언일 거야.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 67. Cutting In.", "# CHAPTER 67. Cutting In."]], [["It was a Saturday night, and such a Sabbath as followed!", "토요일 밤이었고, 그 뒤로 이어진 안식일이라니!"], ["Ex officio professors of Sabbath breaking are all whalemen.", "안식일을 어기는 일에 관한 한 모든 고래잡이는 직무상의 전문가다."], ["The ivory Pequod was turned into what seemed a shamble; every sailor a butcher.", "상아로 뒤덮인 피쿼드호(Pequod)는 마치 도살장처럼 변해 버렸다. 선원들은 하나같이 백정이 되었다."], ["You would have thought we were offering up ten thousand red oxen to the sea gods.", "우리가 바다의 신들에게 붉은 황소 만 마리를 바치는 중이라 해도 믿을 만했을 것이다."]], [["In the first place, the enormous cutting tackles, among other ponderous things comprising a cluster of blocks generally painted green, and which no single man can possibly lift—this vast bunch of grapes was swayed up to the main-top and firmly lashed to the lower mast-head, the strongest point anywhere above a ship’s deck. The end of the hawser-like rope winding through these intricacies, was then conducted to the windlass, and the huge lower block of the tackles was swung over the whale; to this block the great blubber hook, weighing some one hundred pounds, was attached. And now suspended in stages over the side, Starbuck and Stubb, the mates, armed with their long spades, began cutting a hole in the body for the insertion of the hook just above the nearest of the two side-fins. This done, a broad, semicircular line is cut round the hole, the hook is inserted, and the main body of the crew striking up a wild chorus, now commence heaving in one dense crowd at the windlass. When instantly, the entire ship careens over on her side; every bolt in her starts like the nail-heads of an old house in frosty weather; she trembles, quivers, and nods her frighted mast-heads to the sky. More and more she leans over to the whale, while every gasping heave of the windlass is answered by a helping heave from the billows; till at last, a swift, startling snap is heard; with a great swash the ship rolls upwards and backwards from the whale, and the triumphant tackle rises into sight dragging after it the disengaged semicircular end of the first strip of blubber. Now as the blubber envelopes the whale precisely as the rind does an orange, so is it stripped off from the body precisely as an orange is sometimes stripped by spiralizing it. For the strain constantly kept up by the windlass continually keeps the whale rolling over and over in the water, and as the blubber in one strip uniformly peels off along the line called the “scarf,” simultaneously cut by the spades of Starbuck and Stubb, the mates; and just as fast as it is thus peeled off, and indeed by that very act itself, it is all the time being hoisted higher and higher aloft till its upper end grazes the main-top; the men at the windlass then cease heaving, and for a moment or two the prodigious blood-dripping mass sways to and fro as if let down from the sky, and every one present must take good heed to dodge it when it swings, else it may box his ears and pitch him headlong overboard.", "우선, 그 거대한 절단용 도르래 장치가 등장한다. 그것은 다른 묵직한 물건들 가운데서도 흔히 초록색으로 칠해진 한 무더기의 활차(滑車)로 이루어져 있어, 어떤 장사도 혼자서는 결코 들어 올릴 수 없는 것이다. 이 거대한 포도송이 같은 덩어리가 큰 돛대 꼭대기로 끌어 올려진 다음, 갑판 위에서 가장 튼튼한 지점인 아래쪽 돛대머리에 단단히 결박된다. 이 복잡한 장치 사이를 굽이굽이 통과해 나온 닻줄 같은 밧줄의 끝은 양묘기(揚錨機)로 이어지고, 도르래 장치의 거대한 아래쪽 활차는 고래 위로 흔들려 걸쳐진다. 이 활차에는 무게가 백 파운드(약 45킬로그램)쯤 되는 큼직한 지방 갈고리가 매달린다. 이제 뱃전 너머로 발판에 매달린 채, 일등항해사 스타벅(Starbuck)과 이등항해사 스터브(Stubb)가 각자 긴 삽을 들고서, 두 옆지느러미 중 가까운 쪽 바로 위에 갈고리를 끼워 넣을 구멍을 고래 몸통에 파기 시작한다. 이 일이 끝나면 그 구멍 둘레로 넓고 반원을 그리는 선이 새겨지고, 갈고리가 박힌다. 그러면 선원들 대부분이 거친 합창을 터뜨리며 빽빽한 한 무리를 이루어 양묘기를 감아 당기기 시작한다. 그 순간, 배 전체가 한쪽으로 기울어진다. 배에 박힌 모든 볼트가 서리 내린 날씨에 낡은 집의 못대가리가 튀어나오듯 들썩이고, 배는 떨고, 부르르 진동하며, 겁에 질린 돛대머리를 하늘을 향해 끄덕거린다. 배는 점점 더 고래 쪽으로 기울고, 양묘기가 헐떡이며 한 번씩 당겨 올릴 때마다 물결도 도움을 보태듯 한 번씩 출렁여 답한다. 그러다 마침내 빠르고 소스라치는 우지끈 소리가 들린다. 거대한 물보라와 함께 배는 고래로부터 위로, 뒤로 굴러 일어서고, 의기양양한 도르래 장치가 떨어져 나온 첫 번째 지방 띠의 반원형 끄트머리를 끌어당기며 모습을 드러낸다. 자, 지방이 오렌지 껍질이 오렌지를 감싸듯 고래를 정확히 감싸고 있는 까닭에, 그것을 벗겨 내는 일도 오렌지를 때로 나선형으로 돌려 가며 껍질을 벗기듯 정확히 그렇게 이루어진다. 양묘기가 끊임없이 유지하는 장력이 고래를 물속에서 쉴 새 없이 빙글빙글 돌리고, 그동안 항해사 스타벅과 스터브의 삽이 동시에 베어 나가는 이른바 \"스카프\"라 불리는 선을 따라 지방이 한 가닥 띠를 이루며 고르게 벗겨진다. 그리고 그렇게 벗겨지는 그 속도 그대로, 아니 바로 그 벗겨지는 동작 자체에 의해, 지방 띠는 내내 점점 더 높이 위로 끌어 올려져 마침내 그 위쪽 끝이 큰 돛대 꼭대기에 닿을 듯 스친다. 그러면 양묘기에 매달린 사람들은 감아 당기기를 멈추고, 잠깐 동안 그 엄청난, 피가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 덩어리가 하늘에서 내려진 듯이 이리저리 흔들린다. 그곳에 있는 사람은 누구나 그것이 흔들릴 때 잘 피하도록 단단히 조심해야 한다. 그러지 않으면 그것에 따귀를 얻어맞고 뱃전 너머로 곤두박질쳐 떨어질 수도 있기 때문이다."]], [["One of the attending harpooneers now advances with a long, keen weapon called a boarding-sword, and watching his chance he dexterously slices out a considerable hole in the lower part of the swaying mass.", "이제 곁에서 거들던 작살잡이 하나가 보딩소드(boarding-sword)라 불리는 길고 날카로운 무기를 들고 앞으로 나선다. 그는 기회를 노리다가, 흔들리는 덩어리의 아래쪽에 능숙한 솜씨로 제법 큰 구멍을 베어 낸다."], ["Into this hole, the end of the second alternating great tackle is then hooked so as to retain a hold upon the blubber, in order to prepare for what follows.", "그러고는 다음에 이어질 작업을 준비하기 위해, 지방을 붙들어 두려고 두 번째로 번갈아 쓰는 큰 도르래 장치의 끝을 이 구멍에 건다."], ["Whereupon, this accomplished swordsman, warning all hands to stand off, once more makes a scientific dash at the mass, and with a few sidelong, desperate, lunging slicings, severs it completely in twain; so that while the short lower part is still fast, the long upper strip, called a blanket-piece, swings clear, and is all ready for lowering.", "그런 다음 이 솜씨 좋은 검객은 모두에게 물러서라 경고하고는, 다시 한 번 그 덩어리를 향해 정밀하게 돌진하여, 옆으로 비스듬히 몇 차례 필사적으로 내리찍듯 베어, 그것을 완전히 둘로 갈라 놓는다. 그리하여 짧은 아래쪽 부분은 여전히 매여 있는 채로, 담요 조각(blanket-piece)이라 불리는 길고 위쪽인 띠는 자유로이 흔들리며 내릴 채비를 모두 갖추게 된다."], ["The heavers forward now resume their song, and while the one tackle is peeling and hoisting a second strip from the whale, the other is slowly slackened away, and down goes the first strip through the main hatchway right beneath, into an unfurnished parlor called the blubber-room.", "이제 앞쪽의 감아 올리는 사람들이 다시 노래를 시작하고, 한쪽 도르래 장치가 고래에서 두 번째 지방 띠를 벗겨 끌어 올리는 동안, 다른 한쪽은 천천히 풀려 내려가, 첫 번째 띠가 바로 아래에 있는 큰 승강구를 통해 지방 저장실(blubber-room)이라 불리는 텅 빈 방으로 내려간다."], ["Into this twilight apartment sundry nimble hands keep coiling away the long blanket-piece as if it were a great live mass of plaited serpents.", "이 어스름한 방 안에서는 여러 날렵한 손길들이 그 긴 담요 조각을, 마치 살아 꿈틀대는 거대한 뱀 다발이라도 되는 양 쉴 새 없이 둘둘 사려 놓는다. 그렇게 작업은 이어진다."], ["And thus the work proceeds; the two tackles hoisting and lowering simultaneously; both whale and windlass heaving, the heavers singing, the blubber-room gentlemen coiling, the mates scarfing, the ship straining, and all hands swearing occasionally, by way of assuaging the general friction.", "두 도르래 장치가 동시에 끌어 올리고 내리며, 고래도 양묘기도 들썩이고, 끌어당기는 자들은 노래하고, 지방 저장실의 사내들은 사리고, 항해사들은 띠를 베어 내고, 배는 삐걱대며 버틴다. 그리고 모든 선원들은 이따금 욕설을 내뱉는데, 그것은 이 전반적인 마찰을 누그러뜨리기 위한 나름의 방편인 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 68. The Blanket.", "# CHAPTER 68. The Blanket."]], [["I have given no small attention to that not unvexed subject, the skin of the whale.", "내가 적잖이 마음을 쏟아 온 주제가 하나 있으니, 골치깨나 썩이는 그 문제란 바로 고래의 피부다."], ["I have had controversies about it with experienced whalemen afloat, and learned naturalists ashore.", "나는 이 문제를 두고 바다 위의 노련한 고래잡이들과도, 뭍의 학식 깊은 박물학자들과도 갑론을박을 벌여 왔다."], ["My original opinion remains unchanged; but it is only an opinion.", "처음의 내 견해는 지금도 변함이 없으나, 어디까지나 그것은 하나의 견해일 뿐이다."]], [["The question is, what and where is the skin of the whale?", "문제는 이것이다. 고래의 피부는 무엇이며 어디에 있는가?"], ["Already you know what his blubber is.", "고래의 지방층(blubber)이 무엇인지는 이미 알고 있을 것이다."], ["That blubber is something of the consistence of firm, close-grained beef, but tougher, more elastic and compact, and ranges from eight or ten to twelve and fifteen inches in thickness.", "그 지방층은 단단하고 결이 촘촘한 쇠고기와 비슷한 데가 있으나, 그보다 더 질기고 탄력이 있으며 촘촘하고, 두께는 8인치나 10인치에서 12인치, 15인치(약 38센티미터)에 이르기까지 다양하다."]], [["Now, however preposterous it may at first seem to talk of any creature’s skin as being of that sort of consistence and thickness, yet in point of fact these are no arguments against such a presumption; because you cannot raise any other dense enveloping layer from the whale’s body but that same blubber; and the outermost enveloping layer of any animal, if reasonably dense, what can that be but the skin?", "자, 어떤 짐승의 피부를 두고 그런 정도의 단단함과 두께를 지녔다고 말하는 것이 처음에는 아무리 터무니없게 들릴지라도, 사실 그런 추정에 반박할 만한 근거는 전혀 없다. 고래의 몸에서 그 지방층 말고는 다른 어떤 빽빽한 외피층도 벗겨 낼 수 없기 때문이다."], ["True, from the unmarred dead body of the whale, you may scrape off with your hand an infinitely thin, transparent substance, somewhat resembling the thinnest shreds of isinglass, only it is almost as flexible and soft as satin; that is, previous to being dried, when it not only contracts and thickens, but becomes rather hard and brittle.", "그리고 어느 동물이든 가장 바깥쪽을 감싸는 층이 어지간히 빽빽하다면, 그것을 피부 말고 무엇이라 부를 수 있겠는가? 사실, 흠집 하나 없는 고래의 사체에서라면 손으로 한없이 얇고 투명한 물질을 긁어낼 수 있는데, 그것은 부레풀의 가장 얇은 조각과 다소 비슷하되, 새틴처럼 부드럽고 나긋나긋하다."], ["I have several such dried bits, which I use for marks in my whale-books.", "다만 이는 마르기 전의 이야기이며, 일단 마르고 나면 수축하여 두꺼워질 뿐만 아니라 제법 단단하고 부서지기 쉬워진다."], ["It is transparent, as I said before; and being laid upon the printed page, I have sometimes pleased myself with fancying it exerted a magnifying influence.", "나는 그렇게 말린 조각을 몇 개 가지고 있어, 고래에 관한 내 책들의 갈피표로 쓰고 있다. 앞서 말했듯 그것은 투명해서, 인쇄된 책장 위에 얹어 놓으면 마치 글자를 확대해 주는 듯한 느낌이 들어 혼자 흐뭇해할 때가 있다."], ["At any rate, it is pleasant to read about whales through their own spectacles, as you may say.", "어찌 됐든, 말하자면 고래 자신의 안경을 통해 고래에 관한 글을 읽는 것은 즐거운 일이다."], ["But what I am driving at here is this.", "그러나 여기서 내가 말하려는 요지는 이것이다."], ["That same infinitely thin, isinglass substance, which, I admit, invests the entire body of the whale, is not so much to be regarded as the skin of the creature, as the skin of the skin, so to speak; for it were simply ridiculous to say, that the proper skin of the tremendous whale is thinner and more tender than the skin of a new-born child.", "내가 인정하건대 고래의 온몸을 감싸고 있는 그 한없이 얇은 부레풀 같은 물질은, 고래의 피부라기보다는 차라리 피부의 피부쯤으로 보아야 한다는 것이다. 그토록 어마어마한 고래의 진짜 피부가 갓난아기의 피부보다 더 얇고 여리다고 말하는 것은 그야말로 우스꽝스러운 노릇이기 때문이다."], ["But no more of this.", "하지만 이 이야기는 이쯤 해 두자."]], [["Assuming the blubber to be the skin of the whale; then, when this skin, as in the case of a very large Sperm Whale, will yield the bulk of one hundred barrels of oil; and, when it is considered that, in quantity, or rather weight, that oil, in its expressed state, is only three fourths, and not the entire substance of the coat; some idea may hence be had of the enormousness of that animated mass, a mere part of whose mere integument yields such a lake of liquid as that.", "지방층을 고래의 피부라고 가정하자. 그렇다면 이 피부는, 매우 큰 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 경우, 기름 백 배럴에 맞먹는 양을 내준다."], ["Reckoning ten barrels to the ton, you have ten tons for the net weight of only three quarters of the stuff of the whale’s skin.", "게다가 그 짜낸 기름이 양으로, 아니 무게로 따져 외피 전체 물질의 4분의 3에 불과하고 전부가 아님을 헤아려 본다면, 한낱 외피의 한낱 일부분이 그토록 호수만 한 액체를 내놓는 그 살아 있는 거구가 얼마나 어마어마한지 어렴풋이나마 짐작할 수 있으리라. 1톤을 10배럴로 잡으면, 고래 피부 물질의 4분의 3에 해당하는 것만으로도 순중량 10톤이 나오는 셈이다."]], [["In life, the visible surface of the Sperm Whale is not the least among the many marvels he presents.", "살아 있을 때, 향유고래의 눈에 보이는 표면은 그것이 보여 주는 숱한 경이 가운데서도 결코 작은 것이 아니다."], ["Almost invariably it is all over obliquely crossed and re-crossed with numberless straight marks in thick array, something like those in the finest Italian line engravings.", "그 표면은 거의 어김없이 온통, 무수한 곧은 자국들이 빽빽이 늘어서서 비스듬히 가로지르고 또 가로지르는데, 그 모습이 이탈리아의 가장 정교한 선 동판화의 선들과 어딘가 닮았다."], ["But these marks do not seem to be impressed upon the isinglass substance above mentioned, but seem to be seen through it, as if they were engraved upon the body itself.", "그러나 이 자국들은 앞서 말한 부레풀 같은 물질 위에 새겨진 것이 아니라, 마치 몸뚱이 자체에 새겨진 듯, 그 물질을 투과해 보이는 듯하다."], ["Nor is this all.", "그뿐이 아니다."], ["In some instances, to the quick, observant eye, those linear marks, as in a veritable engraving, but afford the ground for far other delineations.", "어떤 경우에는, 재빠르고 눈썰미 있는 이의 눈에는 그 선 자국들이 진짜 동판화에서처럼 다른 갖가지 그림을 그려 낼 바탕이 되어 준다."], ["These are hieroglyphical; that is, if you call those mysterious cyphers on the walls of pyramids hieroglyphics, then that is the proper word to use in the present connexion.", "이 그림들은 상형문자와도 같으니, 다시 말해 피라미드 벽면의 저 신비로운 부호들을 상형문자라 부른다면, 지금 이 경우에도 그 말을 쓰는 것이 마땅하다."], ["By my retentive memory of the hieroglyphics upon one Sperm Whale in particular, I was much struck with a plate representing the old Indian characters chiselled on the famous hieroglyphic palisades on the banks of the Upper Mississippi.", "나는 특히 어느 향유고래 한 마리에 새겨진 상형문자를 또렷이 기억하는데, 그 덕분에 어퍼미시시피 강가의 이름난 상형문자 절벽에 새겨진 옛 인디언(Indian) 글자를 그려 놓은 도판을 보고 몹시 놀랐다."], ["Like those mystic rocks, too, the mystic-marked whale remains undecipherable.", "그 신비로운 바위들과 마찬가지로, 신비로운 무늬가 새겨진 고래 역시 끝내 해독할 수 없는 채로 남아 있다."], ["This allusion to the Indian rocks reminds me of another thing.", "인디언의 바위 이야기가 나온 김에 또 한 가지가 떠오른다."], ["Besides all the other phenomena which the exterior of the Sperm Whale presents, he not seldom displays the back, and more especially his flanks, effaced in great part of the regular linear appearance, by reason of numerous rude scratches, altogether of an irregular, random aspect.", "향유고래의 외양이 보여 주는 다른 온갖 현상 말고도, 이 고래는 등, 그중에서도 특히 옆구리가, 제멋대로 아무렇게나 난 거친 흠집들이 무수해서 규칙적인 선 무늬가 상당 부분 지워진 모습을 드물지 않게 드러낸다."], ["I should say that those New England rocks on the sea-coast, which Agassiz imagines to bear the marks of violent scraping contact with vast floating icebergs—I should say, that those rocks must not a little resemble the Sperm Whale in this particular.", "거대한 부빙(浮氷)들과 격렬하게 긁히며 부딪친 자국을 지니고 있으리라고 아가시가 상상한 저 뉴잉글랜드(New England) 해안의 바위들이야말로, 바로 이 점에서 향유고래와 적잖이 닮았으리라고 나는 말하고 싶다."], ["It also seems to me that such scratches in the whale are probably made by hostile contact with other whales; for I have most remarked them in the large, full-grown bulls of the species.", "또 내게는, 고래에 난 그런 흠집들이 아마도 다른 고래들과 적대적으로 부딪쳐 생긴 것이 아닐까 싶다. 그런 자국을 나는 이 종의 크고 다 자란 수컷들에게서 가장 많이 보아 왔기 때문이다."]], [["A word or two more concerning this matter of the skin or blubber of the whale.", "고래의 피부, 곧 지방층이라는 이 문제에 관해 한두 마디 더 보태고자 한다."], ["It has already been said, that it is stript from him in long pieces, called blanket-pieces.", "앞서 말했듯, 그것은 고래의 몸에서 긴 조각으로 벗겨 내며, 이를 담요 조각(blanket-piece)이라 부른다."], ["Like most sea-terms, this one is very happy and significant.", "대개의 항해 용어가 그렇듯, 이 말 또한 더없이 절묘하고 함축적이다."], ["For the whale is indeed wrapt up in his blubber as in a real blanket or counterpane; or, still better, an Indian poncho slipt over his head, and skirting his extremity.", "고래는 실제로 마치 진짜 담요나 침대보를 두른 듯이 제 지방층에 둘둘 감싸여 있기 때문이다. 아니, 그보다 더 적절히 말하자면, 머리 위로 푹 뒤집어쓰고 발끝까지 자락이 늘어진 인디언의 판초를 걸친 격이다."], ["It is by reason of this cosy blanketing of his body, that the whale is enabled to keep himself comfortable in all weathers, in all seas, times, and tides.", "바로 이렇게 제 몸을 아늑하게 감싸 덮은 덕분에, 고래는 어떤 날씨에도, 어떤 바다와 어떤 시간과 어떤 물때에도 제 몸을 편안하게 지킬 수 있다. 가령 그린란드고래가, 그 아늑한 외투를 두르지 못한 채 저 몸서리치도록 차디찬 북녘 바다에 있다면 어떻게 되겠는가?"], ["What would become of a Greenland whale, say, in those shuddering, icy seas of the North, if unsupplied with his cosy surtout?", "하기야, 저 극북의 바닷물 속에서도 더없이 활기차게 사는 다른 물고기들이 있긴 하다. 그러나 유념할 것은, 이들이 냉혈에 허파도 없는 물고기여서, 제 배때기 자체가 냉장고나 다름없는 부류라는 점이다."], ["True, other fish are found exceedingly brisk in those Hyperborean waters; but these, be it observed, are your cold-blooded, lungless fish, whose very bellies are refrigerators; creatures, that warm themselves under the lee of an iceberg, as a traveller in winter would bask before an inn fire; whereas, like man, the whale has lungs and warm blood.", "이런 것들은 겨울 나그네가 여관 난롯불 앞에서 몸을 녹이듯, 빙산의 바람을 막아 주는 그늘에서 제 몸을 덥힌다. 반면에 고래는 사람과 마찬가지로 허파가 있고 더운 피가 흐른다."], ["Freeze his blood, and he dies.", "그 피가 얼면, 고래는 죽는다."], ["How wonderful is it then—except after explanation—that this great monster, to whom corporeal warmth is as indispensable as it is to man; how wonderful that he should be found at home, immersed to his lips for life in those Arctic waters!", "그러니 — 설명을 듣기 전이라면 — 사람에게 그렇듯 몸의 온기가 없어서는 안 될 이 거대한 괴물이, 입술까지 저 북극 바다에 잠긴 채로 평생을 제집처럼 지낸다는 것이 얼마나 놀라운 일인가!"], ["where, when seamen fall overboard, they are sometimes found, months afterwards, perpendicularly frozen into the hearts of fields of ice, as a fly is found glued in amber.", "그 바다에서는 선원들이 떨어지면 더러는 몇 달 뒤에야 발견되는데, 마치 호박(琥珀) 속에 들러붙은 파리처럼, 얼음판의 한복판에 똑바로 선 채 얼어붙은 모습으로 나오기도 한다."], ["But more surprising is it to know, as has been proved by experiment, that the blood of a Polar whale is warmer than that of a Borneo negro in summer.", "그러나 더욱 놀라운 것은, 실험으로 증명된 바와 같이, 북극고래의 피가 한여름 보르네오 흑인의 피보다 더 따뜻하다는 사실이다."]], [["It does seem to me, that herein we see the rare virtue of a strong individual vitality, and the rare virtue of thick walls, and the rare virtue of interior spaciousness. Oh, man!", "내게는, 바로 여기서 우리가 강인한 개체적 생명력이라는 보기 드문 미덕과, 두꺼운 벽이라는 보기 드문 미덕과, 내부의 드넓음이라는 보기 드문 미덕을 보게 되는 듯하다."], ["admire and model thyself after the whale!", "오, 인간이여! 고래에 감탄하고 고래를 본받으라!"], ["Do thou, too, remain warm among ice.", "그대 또한 얼음 속에서도 따뜻함을 잃지 말라."], ["Do thou, too, live in this world without being of it.", "그대 또한 이 세상에 살되 이 세상에 속하지는 말라."], ["Be cool at the equator; keep thy blood fluid at the Pole.", "적도에서는 서늘하라. 극지에서는 그대의 피를 흐르게 하라."], ["Like the great dome of St. Peter’s, and like the great whale, retain, O man!", "성베드로 대성당의 저 거대한 둥근 지붕처럼, 또 저 거대한 고래처럼, 오, 인간이여!"], ["in all seasons a temperature of thine own.", "어느 계절에나 그대 자신만의 체온을 지키라."]], [["But how easy and how hopeless to teach these fine things! Of erections, how few are domed like St.", "그러나 이런 멋진 이치를 가르치기란 얼마나 쉽고, 또 얼마나 가망 없는 일인가!"], ["Peter’s! of creatures, how few vast as the whale!", "사람이 세운 건축물 중에 성베드로 대성당처럼 둥근 지붕을 인 것이 몇이나 되며, 살아 있는 것들 중에 고래만큼 거대한 것이 몇이나 되겠는가!"]], [["“Haul in the chains!", "\"쇠사슬을 끌어들여라!"], ["Let the carcase go astern!”", "시체는 고물 쪽으로 흘려보내라!\""]], [["The vast tackles have now done their duty.", "거대한 도르래 장치들은 이제 제 소임을 다했다."], ["The peeled white body of the beheaded whale flashes like a marble sepulchre; though changed in hue, it has not perceptibly lost anything in bulk.", "머리가 잘려 나가고 껍질이 벗겨진 고래의 하얀 몸뚱이는 대리석 무덤처럼 번쩍인다. 빛깔은 달라졌으나, 부피만큼은 눈에 띄게 줄어든 데가 없다."], ["It is still colossal.", "여전히 거대하다."], ["Slowly it floats more and more away, the water round it torn and splashed by the insatiate sharks, and the air above vexed with rapacious flights of screaming fowls, whose beaks are like so many insulting poniards in the whale.", "그것은 천천히 점점 더 멀리 떠내려가는데, 그 주위의 물은 만족을 모르는 상어들에게 갈가리 찢기고 튀겨 오르며, 그 위의 공중은 비명을 질러대는 게걸스러운 새 떼의 비행으로 어지러운데, 그 부리들은 고래에게 꽂히는 수많은 모욕의 단검과도 같다."], ["The vast white headless phantom floats further and further from the ship, and every rod that it so floats, what seem square roods of sharks and cubic roods of fowls, augment the murderous din.", "머리 없는 그 거대한 흰 유령은 배에서 점점 더 멀리 떠가고, 그것이 그렇게 떠가는 한 걸음 한 걸음마다, 마치 평방 단위로 펼쳐진 상어 떼와 입방 단위로 들끓는 새 떼가 살기 어린 소란을 더욱 키워 가는 듯하다."], ["For hours and hours from the almost stationary ship that hideous sight is seen.", "거의 멈춰 선 배에서 그 끔찍한 광경이 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 내내 보인다."], ["Beneath the unclouded and mild azure sky, upon the fair face of the pleasant sea, wafted by the joyous breezes, that great mass of death floats on and on, till lost in infinite perspectives.", "구름 한 점 없이 부드러운 쪽빛 하늘 아래, 즐거운 산들바람에 실려, 잔잔한 바다의 고운 얼굴 위로, 저 거대한 죽음의 덩어리는 끝없는 원경 속으로 사라질 때까지 하염없이 떠가고 또 떠간다."]], [["There’s a most doleful and most mocking funeral!", "세상에 이보다 더 구슬프고 더 조롱 어린 장례식은 없으리라!"], ["The sea-vultures all in pious mourning, the air-sharks all punctiliously in black or speckled.", "바다의 독수리들은 모두 경건한 상복을 차려입었고, 하늘의 상어들은 모조리 격식을 갖춰 검정이나 얼룩무늬 옷을 둘렀다."], ["In life but few of them would have helped the whale, I ween, if peradventure he had needed it; but upon the banquet of his funeral they most piously do pounce.", "살아생전 그 고래가 혹여 도움을 필요로 했더라도, 짐작건대 이들 가운데 고래를 도울 자는 거의 없었으리라. 그러나 고래의 장례 연회 앞에서는 더없이 경건하게 달려든다."], ["Oh, horrible vultureism of earth!", "오, 이 땅의 끔찍한 독수리 근성이여!"], ["from which not the mightiest whale is free.", "가장 힘센 고래조차 거기서 자유롭지 못하구나."]], [["Nor is this the end.", "그러나 이것이 끝은 아니다."], ["Desecrated as the body is, a vengeful ghost survives and hovers over it to scare.", "시신은 그토록 능욕당했어도, 복수심에 찬 유령이 살아남아 그 위를 맴돌며 두려움을 자아낸다."], ["Espied by some timid man-of-war or blundering discovery-vessel from afar, when the distance obscuring the swarming fowls, nevertheless still shows the white mass floating in the sun, and the white spray heaving high against it; straightway the whale’s unharming corpse, with trembling fingers is set down in the log—shoals, rocks, and breakers hereabouts: beware!", "멀리서 어느 겁 많은 군함이나 길을 헤매는 탐사선이 그것을 발견하는데, 거리가 들끓는 새 떼는 가려 주지만 그래도 햇빛 속에 떠 있는 흰 덩어리와 그 위로 높이 솟구치는 흰 물보라만은 여전히 보일 때, 누구도 해치지 않는 그 고래의 시체는 곧장 떨리는 손가락에 의해 항해일지에 기록되는 것이다. 이 부근에 모래톱과 암초와 부서지는 파도 있음."], ["And for years afterwards, perhaps, ships shun the place; leaping over it as silly sheep leap over a vacuum, because their leader originally leaped there when a stick was held.", "주의할 것! 그리하여 그 뒤로 여러 해 동안, 어쩌면 배들은 그곳을 피해 다닐 터인데, 이는 어리석은 양들이 텅 빈 허공을 뛰어넘는 격이니, 애초에 우두머리가 막대기를 들이댔을 때 거기서 뛰어넘었기 때문이다."], ["There’s your law of precedents; there’s your utility of traditions; there’s the story of your obstinate survival of old beliefs never bottomed on the earth, and now not even hovering in the air!", "바로 그것이 그대들의 선례라는 법칙이요, 그것이 그대들의 전통이라는 효용이며, 땅에 뿌리내린 적도 없고 이제는 공중에서 맴돌지조차 않는 낡은 믿음이 끈질기게 살아남는 이야기다! 그것이 바로 정통 교리라는 것이다!"], ["There’s orthodoxy!", "이렇듯, 살아 있을 때 거대한 고래의 몸뚱이는 그 적들에게 실로 공포의 대상이었을지 모르나, 죽고 나면 그 유령이 온 세상을 향해 아무 힘도 없는 공황(恐慌)이 되고 마는 것이다."]], [["Thus, while in life the great whale’s body may have been a real terror to his foes, in his death his ghost becomes a powerless panic to a world.", "그대는 유령을 믿는 사람인가, 벗이여? 콕레인의 유령 말고도 다른 유령들이 있으며, 그것들을 믿는 자들 중에는 존슨 박사보다 훨씬 더 깊이 있는 사람들도 있다."]], [["Are you a believer in ghosts, my friend? There are other ghosts than the Cock-Lane one, and far deeper men than Doctor Johnson who believe in them.", "* 콕레인의 유령: 18세기 런던 콕레인에서 일어난 유명한 유령 소동으로, 존슨 박사가 진상 조사에 참여했으나 결국 사기극으로 밝혀졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER 70. The Sphynx.", "# CHAPTER 70. The Sphynx."]], [["It should not have been omitted that previous to completely stripping the body of the leviathan, he was beheaded.", "리바이어던의 몸뚱이를 완전히 벗겨 내기에 앞서 먼저 머리를 잘라 냈다는 사실을 빠뜨려서는 안 될 것이다."], ["Now, the beheading of the Sperm Whale is a scientific anatomical feat, upon which experienced whale surgeons very much pride themselves: and not without reason.", "그런데 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 머리를 자르는 일은 과학적이고 해부학적인 솜씨를 요하는 작업으로, 노련한 고래 외과의들이 자못 자랑스러워하는 일이다. 그럴 만한 까닭이 없지 않다."]], [["Consider that the whale has nothing that can properly be called a neck; on the contrary, where his head and body seem to join, there, in that very place, is the thickest part of him.", "생각해 보라. 고래에게는 제대로 목이라 부를 만한 부위가 전혀 없다."], ["Remember, also, that the surgeon must operate from above, some eight or ten feet intervening between him and his subject, and that subject almost hidden in a discoloured, rolling, and oftentimes tumultuous and bursting sea.", "오히려 머리와 몸이 맞닿은 듯이 보이는 바로 그 자리가 그놈의 몸에서 가장 두꺼운 부분이다. 또한 외과의는 위쪽에서 작업을 해야 하며, 그와 그가 다루는 대상 사이에는 여덟 자에서 열 자(약 2.4미터에서 3미터)나 되는 간격이 가로놓여 있고, 그 대상은 빛깔이 탁하고 출렁이며 때로는 사납게 들끓고 부서지는 바다 속에 거의 가려져 있다는 점을 기억하라."], ["Bear in mind, too, that under these untoward circumstances he has to cut many feet deep in the flesh; and in that subterraneous manner, without so much as getting one single peep into the ever-contracting gash thus made, he must skilfully steer clear of all adjacent, interdicted parts, and exactly divide the spine at a critical point hard by its insertion into the skull.", "또 이런 고약한 여건 속에서 그는 살을 여러 자나 깊숙이 베어 들어가야 한다는 것도 명심하라. 그리고 그렇게 땅속을 파듯 작업하면서, 그렇게 낸 칼자국 속을 단 한 번도 들여다보지 못한 채 — 그 틈은 끊임없이 오므라들거니와 — 인접한 모든 금지 부위들을 능숙하게 피해 가면서, 척추가 두개골에 박혀 드는 바로 그 결정적인 지점에서 정확히 등뼈를 갈라내야 한다."], ["Do you not marvel, then, at Stubb’s boast, that he demanded but ten minutes to behead a sperm whale?", "그러니 향유고래의 머리를 자르는 데 십 분이면 충분하다던 스터브(Stubb)의 호언장담에 그대는 경탄하지 않을 수 있겠는가?"]], [["When first severed, the head is dropped astern and held there by a cable till the body is stripped.", "머리를 처음 잘라 내면 그것을 고물 쪽으로 떨구어 밧줄로 매어 두었다가, 몸뚱이를 다 벗길 때까지 그대로 둔다."], ["That done, if it belong to a small whale it is hoisted on deck to be deliberately disposed of.", "그 일이 끝났을 때, 만일 그것이 작은 고래의 것이라면 갑판으로 끌어올려 천천히 처리한다."], ["But, with a full grown leviathan this is impossible; for the sperm whale’s head embraces nearly one third of his entire bulk, and completely to suspend such a burden as that, even by the immense tackles of a whaler, this were as vain a thing as to attempt weighing a Dutch barn in jewellers’ scales.", "그러나 다 자란 리바이어던의 경우에는 이것이 불가능하다. 향유고래의 머리는 그 몸 전체의 거의 삼분의 일을 차지하는 까닭에, 포경선의 그 거대한 도르래 장치로도 그런 짐덩이를 통째로 매달아 올리려는 것은, 네덜란드의 헛간을 보석상의 저울로 달아 보려는 것만큼이나 헛된 일이기 때문이다."]], [["The Pequod’s whale being decapitated and the body stripped, the head was hoisted against the ship’s side—about half way out of the sea, so that it might yet in great part be buoyed up by its native element.", "피쿼드(Pequod)호가 잡은 고래의 목을 자르고 몸뚱이를 벗겨 낸 뒤, 머리는 뱃전에 매달아 올렸다 — 바다 밖으로 절반쯤 나오게 하여, 여전히 대부분이 제 본래의 원소에 떠받쳐지도록 한 것이다."], ["And there with the strained craft steeply leaning over to it, by reason of the enormous downward drag from the lower mast-head, and every yard-arm on that side projecting like a crane over the waves; there, that blood-dripping head hung to the Pequod’s waist like the giant Holofernes’s from the girdle of Judith.", "그리하여 아래쪽 돛대 꼭대기에서 내리누르는 엄청난 견인력 때문에 무리하게 짐을 진 배가 그쪽으로 가파르게 기울어지고, 그쪽 활대 끝이 죄다 파도 위로 기중기처럼 뻗어 나간 가운데, 피가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 그 머리는 마치 유디트의 허리띠에 매달린 거인 홀로페르네스의 머리처럼 피쿼드호의 허리께에 매달려 있었다."]], [["When this last task was accomplished it was noon, and the seamen went below to their dinner.", "이 마지막 작업이 끝났을 때는 정오였고, 선원들은 식사를 하러 아래로 내려갔다."], ["Silence reigned over the before tumultuous but now deserted deck.", "조금 전까지 소란하던, 그러나 이제는 텅 비어 버린 갑판 위로 정적이 감돌았다."], ["An intense copper calm, like a universal yellow lotus, was more and more unfolding its noiseless measureless leaves upon the sea.", "온 세상을 뒤덮은 한 송이 노란 연꽃 같은, 짙은 구릿빛 적막이 소리 없이 헤아릴 수 없는 잎새를 바다 위에 점점 더 활짝 펼쳐 가고 있었다."]], [["A short space elapsed, and up into this noiselessness came Ahab alone from his cabin.", "잠시 시간이 흐른 뒤, 이 소리 없는 적막 속으로 에이해브(Ahab)가 홀로 선실에서 올라왔다."], ["Taking a few turns on the quarter-deck, he paused to gaze over the side, then slowly getting into the main-chains he took Stubb’s long spade—still remaining there after the whale’s decapitation—and striking it into the lower part of the half-suspended mass, placed its other end crutch-wise under one arm, and so stood leaning over with eyes attentively fixed on this head.", "뒷갑판을 몇 바퀴 돌던 그는 걸음을 멈추고 뱃전 너머를 바라보았다. 그러고는 천천히 큰 돛대의 쇠사슬로 다가가, 고래의 목을 잘라 낸 뒤로 여태 거기 남아 있던 스터브의 긴 삽을 집어 들더니, 그것을 절반쯤 매달린 그 덩어리의 아래쪽에 박아 넣고 다른 쪽 끝을 목발처럼 한쪽 겨드랑이에 괴고서, 그 머리에 두 눈을 골똘히 못 박은 채 몸을 기울이고 서 있었다."]], [["It was a black and hooded head; and hanging there in the midst of so intense a calm, it seemed the Sphynx’s in the desert.", "그것은 검고 두건을 뒤집어쓴 듯한 머리였다. 그토록 깊은 적막의 한복판에 매달려 있는 그것은 마치 사막의 스핑크스 같았다."], ["“Speak, thou vast and venerable head,”", "\"말해 보라, 그대 거대하고 존엄한 머리여.\""], ["muttered Ahab, “which, though ungarnished with a beard, yet here and there lookest hoary with mosses; speak, mighty head, and tell us the secret thing that is in thee.", "에이해브가 중얼거렸다. \"비록 수염으로 꾸며지지는 않았으나 여기저기 이끼가 끼어 백발이 성성한 듯 보이는 그대여, 말하라, 위대한 머리여, 그대 속에 든 비밀을 우리에게 일러 다오."], ["Of all divers, thou hast dived the deepest.", "모든 잠수부 가운데 그대는 가장 깊은 곳까지 잠겨 보았으리라."], ["That head upon which the upper sun now gleams, has moved amid this world’s foundations.", "지금 머리 위의 해가 그 위에 어른거리는 그 머리는, 일찍이 이 세상의 토대 사이를 누비고 다녔다."], ["Where unrecorded names and navies rust, and untold hopes and anchors rot; where in her murderous hold this frigate earth is ballasted with bones of millions of the drowned; there, in that awful water-land, there was thy most familiar home.", "기록되지 못한 이름들과 함대들이 녹슬어 가고, 알려지지 않은 희망과 닻들이 썩어 가는 곳, 살육의 선창 속에서 이 군함 같은 지구가 익사한 수백만의 뼈를 바닥짐으로 싣고 있는 그곳 — 그 무시무시한 물의 나라야말로 그대에게는 가장 정든 보금자리였으리라."], ["Thou hast been where bell or diver never went; hast slept by many a sailor’s side, where sleepless mothers would give their lives to lay them down.", "그대는 종(鐘)도 잠수부도 결코 가 닿지 못한 곳까지 다녀왔다. 잠 못 이루는 어머니들이 자기 목숨을 내주고서라도 눕고 싶어 했을 그 자리에서, 수많은 뱃사람들 곁에 나란히 잠들기도 했으리라."], ["Thou saw’st the locked lovers when leaping from their flaming ship; heart to heart they sank beneath the exulting wave; true to each other, when heaven seemed false to them.", "그대는 불타는 배에서 함께 뛰어내리는, 서로 얽힌 연인들을 보았다. 가슴과 가슴을 맞댄 채 그들은 환호하는 파도 아래로 가라앉았으니, 하늘이 그들을 저버린 듯 보이던 그 순간에도 서로에게만은 한결같았다."], ["Thou saw’st the murdered mate when tossed by pirates from the midnight deck; for hours he fell into the deeper midnight of the insatiate maw; and his murderers still sailed on unharmed—while swift lightnings shivered the neighboring ship that would have borne a righteous husband to outstretched, longing arms.", "그대는 한밤의 갑판에서 해적들에게 내던져진, 살해당한 항해사를 보았다. 그는 몇 시간이고 저 채울 줄 모르는 아가리의 더 깊은 한밤 속으로 떨어져 내렸으되, 그를 죽인 자들은 여전히 멀쩡히 항해를 이어 갔으니 — 그사이 잽싼 번개가, 의로운 남편을 활짝 벌린 채 애타게 기다리는 두 팔로 데려다주었을 이웃의 배를 산산이 부수어 놓았다."], ["O head!", "오, 머리여!"], ["thou hast seen enough to split the planets and make an infidel of Abraham, and not one syllable is thine!”", "그대는 행성을 쪼개 버리고 아브라함마저 불신자로 만들 만큼 많은 것을 보았건만, 그대에게서는 단 한 마디도 흘러나오지 않는구나!\""]], [["“Sail ho!”", "\"돛이다, 어이!\""], ["cried a triumphant voice from the main-mast-head.", "큰 돛대 꼭대기에서 의기양양한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["“Aye?", "\"그래?"], ["Well, now, that’s cheering,”", "그것 참, 반가운 소리로구나.\""], ["cried Ahab, suddenly erecting himself, while whole thunder-clouds swept aside from his brow.", "에이해브가 별안간 몸을 곧추세우며 외쳤다. 그러는 사이 먹구름 떼가 그의 이마에서 죄다 걷혀 나갔다."], ["“That lively cry upon this deadly calm might almost convert a better man.—Where away?”", "\"이 죽음 같은 적막 속에서 들려오는 저 생기 넘치는 외침이라면, 나보다 나은 사람조차 거의 회심시킬 만하구나. — 어느 쪽이냐?\""]], [["“Three points on the starboard bow, sir, and bringing down her breeze to us!", "\"우현 이물 쪽으로 세 포인트입니다, 선장님. 게다가 그쪽 바람을 우리 쪽으로 몰고 오고 있습니다!\""]], [["“Better and better, man.", "\"갈수록 좋군그래, 이 사람아."], ["Would now St. Paul would come along that way, and to my breezelessness bring his breeze!", "이참에 성 바울이 그 길로 와서, 바람 한 점 없는 내게 그의 바람을 좀 가져다주었으면 좋으련만!"], ["O Nature, and O soul of man!", "오, 자연이여, 그리고 오, 인간의 영혼이여!"], ["how far beyond all utterance are your linked analogies!", "그대들의 그 얽히고설킨 닮은꼴은 그 어떤 말로도 다 담아낼 수 없도다!"], ["not the smallest atom stirs or lives on matter, but has its cunning duplicate in mind.”", "아무리 작은 원자 하나라도 물질 위에서 움직이거나 살아 숨 쉬는 것치고, 마음속에 그 교묘한 분신을 지니지 않은 것이 없으니.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 71. The Jeroboam’s Story.", "# CHAPTER 71. The Jeroboam’s Story."]], [["Hand in hand, ship and breeze blew on; but the breeze came faster than the ship, and soon the Pequod began to rock.", "손에 손을 맞잡듯 배와 산들바람이 함께 나아갔다. 그러나 바람이 배보다 빨랐기에, 이내 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 흔들리기 시작했다."]], [["By and by, through the glass the stranger’s boats and manned mast-heads proved her a whale-ship.", "이윽고 망원경 너머로 그 낯선 배의 보트들과 사람이 올라가 있는 돛대 꼭대기가 보였으니, 그 배가 고래잡이배임이 드러났다."], ["But as she was so far to windward, and shooting by, apparently making a passage to some other ground, the Pequod could not hope to reach her.", "그러나 그 배가 바람 불어오는 쪽으로 너무 멀리 떨어진 채 빠르게 스쳐 지나가며, 보아하니 어느 다른 어장으로 가는 길인 듯하여, 피쿼드호로서는 그 배에 닿기를 바랄 수 없었다."], ["So the signal was set to see what response would be made.", "그리하여 어떤 응답이 올지 보려고 신호기를 내걸었다."]], [["Here be it said, that like the vessels of military marines, the ships of the American Whale Fleet have each a private signal; all which signals being collected in a book with the names of the respective vessels attached, every captain is provided with it.", "여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 군함과 마찬가지로 미국 포경 선단의 배들도 저마다 고유의 신호를 지니고 있으며, 그 모든 신호가 해당 선박들의 이름과 함께 한 권의 책으로 묶여 모든 선장에게 비치되어 있다."], ["Thereby, the whale commanders are enabled to recognise each other upon the ocean, even at considerable distances and with no small facility.", "그 덕분에 고래잡이 선장들은 상당한 거리에서도 적잖이 수월하게 바다 위에서 서로를 알아볼 수 있다."]], [["The Pequod’s signal was at last responded to by the stranger’s setting her own; which proved the ship to be the Jeroboam of Nantucket.", "피쿼드호의 신호에 마침내 그 낯선 배가 제 신호를 내걸어 응답하니, 그 배가 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 제로보암(Jeroboam)호임이 드러났다."], ["Squaring her yards, she bore down, ranged abeam under the Pequod’s lee, and lowered a boat; it soon drew nigh; but, as the side-ladder was being rigged by Starbuck’s order to accommodate the visiting captain, the stranger in question waved his hand from his boat’s stern in token of that proceeding being entirely unnecessary.", "활대를 직각으로 돌린 그 배는 바람을 받으며 다가와 피쿼드호의 바람 아래쪽 옆구리에 나란히 자리를 잡고 보트 한 척을 내렸다. 보트는 곧 가까이 다가왔다."], ["It turned out that the Jeroboam had a malignant epidemic on board, and that Mayhew, her captain, was fearful of infecting the Pequod’s company.", "그러나 스타벅(Starbuck)의 명에 따라 찾아오는 선장을 맞으려고 뱃전 사다리가 내려지는 동안, 문제의 그 낯선 선장은 보트 고물에서 손을 내저어 그런 절차가 전혀 필요 없음을 알렸다."], ["For, though himself and boat’s crew remained untainted, and though his ship was half a rifle-shot off, and an incorruptible sea and air rolling and flowing between; yet conscientiously adhering to the timid quarantine of the land, he peremptorily refused to come into direct contact with the Pequod.", "알고 보니 제로보암호에는 악성 전염병이 돌고 있었고, 그 배의 선장 메이휴(Mayhew)는 피쿼드호의 선원들에게 병을 옮길까 두려워하고 있었던 것이다. 비록 그 자신과 보트의 선원들은 아직 병에 걸리지 않았고, 그의 배가 소총 한 발 거리의 절반쯤 떨어져 있으며, 그 사이로 더럽혀질 수 없는 바다와 공기가 굽이쳐 흐르고 있었음에도, 그는 양심에 따라 뭍에서 행하는 소심한 격리 관습을 고집하며 피쿼드호와 직접 닿는 것을 단호히 거부했다."]], [["But this did by no means prevent all communications.", "그러나 이것이 모든 소통을 가로막은 것은 결코 아니었다."], ["Preserving an interval of some few yards between itself and the ship, the Jeroboam’s boat by the occasional use of its oars contrived to keep parallel to the Pequod, as she heavily forged through the sea (for by this time it blew very fresh), with her main-topsail aback; though, indeed, at times by the sudden onset of a large rolling wave, the boat would be pushed some way ahead; but would be soon skilfully brought to her proper bearings again.", "제 배와 피쿼드호 사이에 몇 야드의 간격을 유지하면서, 제로보암호의 보트는 이따금 노를 저어 가며, 큰 돛대의 윗돛을 뒤로 젖힌 채 무겁게 바다를 헤치며 나아가는 피쿼드호와(이 무렵에는 바람이 자못 거세게 불고 있었다) 나란히 가려고 애썼다. 물론 더러는 큰 너울이 갑작스레 들이치는 통에 보트가 한참 앞으로 떠밀려 가기도 했으나, 곧 능숙하게 제자리로 돌아오곤 했다."], ["Subject to this, and other the like interruptions now and then, a conversation was sustained between the two parties; but at intervals not without still another interruption of a very different sort.", "이런 일과 그 밖에 비슷한 방해가 이따금 끼어드는 가운데서도 두 진영 사이에서는 대화가 이어졌다. 그러나 그 간간이 또 다른, 사뭇 다른 종류의 방해가 끼어드는 일도 없지 않았다."]], [["Pulling an oar in the Jeroboam’s boat, was a man of a singular appearance, even in that wild whaling life where individual notabilities make up all totalities.", "제로보암호의 보트에서 노를 젓고 있는 자 가운데, 개개인의 별난 인물됨이 전부를 이루는 저 거친 포경 생활에서조차 유독 기이한 모습을 한 사내가 하나 있었다."], ["He was a small, short, youngish man, sprinkled all over his face with freckles, and wearing redundant yellow hair.", "그는 키 작고 땅딸막한 데다 아직 젊은 사내로, 얼굴 가득 주근깨가 뿌려져 있고 노란 머리칼이 더부룩했다."], ["A long-skirted, cabalistically-cut coat of a faded walnut tinge enveloped him; the overlapping sleeves of which were rolled up on his wrists.", "빛바랜 호두색의, 자락이 긴 데다 신비주의적으로 재단된 외투가 그를 감싸고 있었으며, 그 겹쳐진 소매는 손목께까지 걷어 올려져 있었다."], ["A deep, settled, fanatic delirium was in his eyes.", "그의 두 눈에는 깊이 자리 잡은 광신적 광기가 서려 있었다."]], [["So soon as this figure had been first descried, Stubb had exclaimed—“That’s he!", "이 인물이 처음 눈에 띄자마자 스터브(Stubb)는 외쳤다. \"저놈이다!"], ["that’s he!—the long-togged scaramouch the Town-Ho’s company told us of!”", "저놈이야! 타운호(Town-Ho)호 선원들이 우리한테 말해 준 그 긴 옷 차림의 어릿광대 놈!\""], ["Stubb here alluded to a strange story told of the Jeroboam, and a certain man among her crew, some time previous when the Pequod spoke the Town-Ho.", "스터브가 여기서 빗대어 말한 것은, 얼마 전 피쿼드호가 타운호호와 마주쳤을 때 들었던 제로보암호와 그 선원 중 한 사람에 관한 기이한 이야기였다."], ["According to this account and what was subsequently learned, it seemed that the scaramouch in question had gained a wonderful ascendency over almost everybody in the Jeroboam.", "그 이야기와 나중에 알게 된 바에 따르면, 문제의 그 어릿광대는 제로보암호의 거의 모든 이들 위에 놀라운 영향력을 떨치게 된 모양이었다."], ["His story was this:", "그의 내력은 이러했다."]], [["He had been originally nurtured among the crazy society of Neskyeuna Shakers, where he had been a great prophet; in their cracked, secret meetings having several times descended from heaven by the way of a trap-door, announcing the speedy opening of the seventh vial, which he carried in his vest-pocket; but, which, instead of containing gunpowder, was supposed to be charged with laudanum.", "그는 본디 네스큐나의 광신적인 셰이커교도 무리 속에서 자라났으며, 그곳에서 위대한 예언자 노릇을 했다. 그 미치광이 같은 비밀 집회에서 그는 여러 차례 들창문을 통해 하늘에서 내려오는 시늉을 하며, 자신이 조끼 주머니에 지니고 있다는 일곱째 대접을 곧 열겠노라 선포하곤 했는데, 그 대접에는 화약이 아니라 아편 팅크가 담겨 있으리라고들 여겨졌다."], ["A strange, apostolic whim having seized him, he had left Neskyeuna for Nantucket, where, with that cunning peculiar to craziness, he assumed a steady, common-sense exterior, and offered himself as a green-hand candidate for the Jeroboam’s whaling voyage.", "어떤 기이한 사도적 변덕에 사로잡힌 그는 네스큐나를 떠나 낸터킷으로 왔고, 그곳에서 광기 특유의 교활함을 발휘하여 침착하고 분별 있는 겉모습을 꾸미고는, 제로보암호의 포경 항해에 풋내기 지원자로 제 몸을 내놓았다."], ["They engaged him; but straightway upon the ship’s getting out of sight of land, his insanity broke out in a freshet.", "사람들은 그를 채용했다. 그러나 배가 뭍을 벗어나 보이지 않게 되자마자 그의 광기는 봇물 터지듯 솟구쳤다."], ["He announced himself as the archangel Gabriel, and commanded the captain to jump overboard.", "그는 자신을 대천사 가브리엘이라 선언하며 선장에게 바다로 뛰어들라고 명했다."], ["He published his manifesto, whereby he set himself forth as the deliverer of the isles of the sea and vicar-general of all Oceanica.", "그는 선언문을 발표하여 자신을 바다 섬들의 구원자요 온 오세아니아의 총대리(總代理)로 내세웠다."], ["The unflinching earnestness with which he declared these things;—the dark, daring play of his sleepless, excited imagination, and all the preternatural terrors of real delirium, united to invest this Gabriel in the minds of the majority of the ignorant crew, with an atmosphere of sacredness.", "이런 것들을 선포할 때의 그 흔들림 없는 진지함, 잠들 줄 모르는 흥분된 상상력의 어둡고 대담한 활약, 그리고 진짜 광기가 빚어내는 온갖 초자연적 공포가 한데 어우러져, 이 가브리엘을 무지한 선원 대다수의 마음속에 신성의 분위기로 감싸 놓았다."], ["Moreover, they were afraid of him.", "더구나 그들은 그를 두려워했다."], ["As such a man, however, was not of much practical use in the ship, especially as he refused to work except when he pleased, the incredulous captain would fain have been rid of him; but apprised that that individual’s intention was to land him in the first convenient port, the archangel forthwith opened all his seals and vials—devoting the ship and all hands to unconditional perdition, in case this intention was carried out.", "그러나 그런 사내는 배에서 그다지 실질적인 쓸모가 없었고, 더욱이 제 내키는 때가 아니면 일하기를 거부했으므로, 그를 믿지 않는 선장은 그를 떨쳐 버리고 싶어 했다. 그러나 선장이 처음 닿는 적당한 항구에 자기를 내려놓을 작정임을 눈치챈 그 대천사는 즉시 제 모든 봉인과 대접을 열어, 만약 그 계획이 실행된다면 배와 선원 전부를 무조건 멸망에 바치겠노라 저주했다."], ["So strongly did he work upon his disciples among the crew, that at last in a body they went to the captain and told him if Gabriel was sent from the ship, not a man of them would remain.", "그가 선원들 가운데 제 제자들에게 어찌나 강하게 작용했던지, 마침내 그들은 떼를 지어 선장에게 가서, 가브리엘을 배에서 내쫓으면 자기들 중 한 사람도 남지 않겠다고 말했다."], ["He was therefore forced to relinquish his plan.", "그리하여 선장은 제 계획을 단념할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Nor would they permit Gabriel to be any way maltreated, say or do what he would; so that it came to pass that Gabriel had the complete freedom of the ship.", "또한 그들은 가브리엘이 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 짓을 하든 그가 조금이라도 학대당하는 것을 용납하지 않았다. 그리하여 가브리엘은 배 안에서 완전한 자유를 누리게 되었다."], ["The consequence of all this was, that the archangel cared little or nothing for the captain and mates; and since the epidemic had broken out, he carried a higher hand than ever; declaring that the plague, as he called it, was at his sole command; nor should it be stayed but according to his good pleasure.", "이 모든 일의 결과로, 그 대천사는 선장이나 항해사들 따위는 거의, 아니 전혀 안중에도 두지 않게 되었다. 그리고 전염병이 돌기 시작한 뒤로 그는 그 어느 때보다 더 큰 권세를 휘둘렀으니, 그가 역병이라 부르는 그것이 오로지 자신의 명령 아래 있으며, 자신의 뜻에 따르지 않고는 결코 그칠 수 없다고 선언했다."], ["The sailors, mostly poor devils, cringed, and some of them fawned before him; in obedience to his instructions, sometimes rendering him personal homage, as to a god.", "대개 가련한 처지의 선원들은 그 앞에서 굽실거렸고, 더러는 아첨까지 했으며, 그의 지시에 복종하여 때로는 신을 대하듯 그에게 몸소 경배를 바치기도 했다."], ["Such things may seem incredible; but, however wondrous, they are true.", "이런 일들이 믿기 어렵게 여겨질지도 모른다. 그러나 아무리 놀랍다 한들 그것은 사실이다."], ["Nor is the history of fanatics half so striking in respect to the measureless self-deception of the fanatic himself, as his measureless power of deceiving and bedevilling so many others.", "광신자들의 역사에서, 광신자 자신의 헤아릴 수 없는 자기기만보다 더 놀라운 것은, 그토록 많은 다른 이들을 속이고 홀려 내는 그의 헤아릴 수 없는 능력이다."], ["But it is time to return to the Pequod.", "그러나 이제 피쿼드호로 돌아갈 때다."]], [["“I fear not thy epidemic, man,”", "\"나는 그대의 전염병 따위 두렵지 않소, 이보시오.\""], ["said Ahab from the bulwarks, to Captain Mayhew, who stood in the boat’s stern; “come on board.”", "에이해브(Ahab)가 뱃전에서 보트 고물에 서 있는 메이휴 선장에게 말했다. \"올라오시오.\""]], [["But now Gabriel started to his feet.", "그러나 이때 가브리엘이 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“Think, think of the fevers, yellow and bilious!", "\"생각하시오, 열병을 생각해 보란 말이오, 누렇게 뜨고 담즙이 끓는 열병을!"], ["Beware of the horrible plague!”", "저 끔찍한 역병을 조심하시오!\""]], [["“Gabriel!", "\"가브리엘!"], ["Gabriel!”", "가브리엘!\""], ["cried Captain Mayhew; “thou must either—”", "메이휴 선장이 외쳤다. \"너는 어느 쪽이든—\""], ["But that instant a headlong wave shot the boat far ahead, and its seethings drowned all speech.", "그러나 그 순간 곤두박질치는 파도가 보트를 멀찍이 앞으로 내쏘았고, 그 들끓는 물소리가 모든 말을 삼켜 버렸다."]], [["“Hast thou seen the White Whale?”", "\"흰 고래를 본 적이 있소?\""], ["demanded Ahab, when the boat drifted back.", "보트가 다시 떠밀려 돌아오자 에이해브가 물었다."]], [["“Think, think of thy whale-boat, stoven and sunk!", "\"생각하시오, 그대의 고래잡이 보트를 생각해 보란 말이오, 부서지고 가라앉은 그것을!"], ["Beware of the horrible tail!”", "저 끔찍한 꼬리를 조심하시오!\""]], [["“I tell thee again, Gabriel, that—”", "\"내 다시 말하건대, 가브리엘, 그—\""], ["But again the boat tore ahead as if dragged by fiends.", "그러나 다시금 보트는 마치 마귀들에게 끌려가듯 앞으로 내달았다."], ["Nothing was said for some moments, while a succession of riotous waves rolled by, which by one of those occasional caprices of the seas were tumbling, not heaving it.", "한동안 아무 말도 오가지 못했으니, 사납게 날뛰는 파도가 잇따라 굽이쳐 지나갔기 때문이다. 그것은 바다가 이따금 부리는 변덕 가운데 하나로, 너울이 솟구치는 것이 아니라 마구 뒹굴며 굴러가는 모양이었다."], ["Meantime, the hoisted sperm whale’s head jogged about very violently, and Gabriel was seen eyeing it with rather more apprehensiveness than his archangel nature seemed to warrant.", "그러는 사이 끌어올려 둔 향유고래의 머리가 몹시 격렬하게 흔들거렸고, 가브리엘이 자신의 대천사다운 천성에 어울릴 법한 것보다 사뭇 더 불안한 눈길로 그것을 바라보고 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["When this interlude was over, Captain Mayhew began a dark story concerning Moby Dick; not, however, without frequent interruptions from Gabriel, whenever his name was mentioned, and the crazy sea that seemed leagued with him.", "이 막간이 끝나자 메이휴 선장은 모비 딕(Moby Dick)에 관한 어두운 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그러나 그 이름이 입에 오를 때마다, 그리고 그와 한패가 된 듯한 저 미친 바다 탓에, 가브리엘의 잦은 방해가 끼어드는 것을 피할 수는 없었다."]], [["It seemed that the Jeroboam had not long left home, when upon speaking a whale-ship, her people were reliably apprised of the existence of Moby Dick, and the havoc he had made.", "이야기인즉슨, 제로보암호가 고향을 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않았을 무렵, 어느 고래잡이배와 마주쳐 이야기를 나누던 중 그 선원들이 모비 딕의 존재와 그가 저질러 온 참화를 확실히 전해 들었다는 것이다."], ["Greedily sucking in this intelligence, Gabriel solemnly warned the captain against attacking the White Whale, in case the monster should be seen; in his gibbering insanity, pronouncing the White Whale to be no less a being than the Shaker God incarnated; the Shakers receiving the Bible.", "이 소식을 게걸스레 빨아들인 가브리엘은, 혹시라도 그 괴물이 눈에 띄거든 흰 고래를 공격하지 말라고 선장에게 엄숙히 경고했다. 그는 횡설수설하는 광기 속에서, 흰 고래가 다름 아닌 화신(化身)한 셰이커교의 신이라고 선언했으니, 셰이커교도들도 성경을 받드는 까닭이었다."], ["But when, some year or two afterwards, Moby Dick was fairly sighted from the mast-heads, Macey, the chief mate, burned with ardour to encounter him; and the captain himself being not unwilling to let him have the opportunity, despite all the archangel’s denunciations and forewarnings, Macey succeeded in persuading five men to man his boat.", "그러나 그로부터 한두 해 뒤, 마침내 돛대 꼭대기에서 모비 딕이 또렷이 목격되자, 일등항해사 메이시(Macey)는 그놈과 맞붙고 싶은 열의로 불타올랐다. 그리고 선장 또한 대천사의 온갖 비난과 사전 경고에도 불구하고 그에게 기회를 주는 것을 마다하지 않았으므로, 메이시는 다섯 사람을 설득하여 제 보트에 태우는 데 성공했다."], ["With them he pushed off; and, after much weary pulling, and many perilous, unsuccessful onsets, he at last succeeded in getting one iron fast.", "그는 그들과 함께 배를 밀어 나갔고, 지칠 만큼 노를 젓고 위태롭게 여러 차례 헛되이 달려든 끝에, 마침내 작살 하나를 단단히 박아 넣는 데 성공했다."], ["Meantime, Gabriel, ascending to the main-royal mast-head, was tossing one arm in frantic gestures, and hurling forth prophecies of speedy doom to the sacrilegious assailants of his divinity.", "그러는 사이 가브리엘은 큰 돛대 맨 꼭대기로 기어올라 한쪽 팔을 미친 듯이 휘저으며, 자신의 신성을 모독하는 불경한 공격자들에게 곧 닥칠 파멸의 예언을 마구 쏟아 내고 있었다."], ["Now, while Macey, the mate, was standing up in his boat’s bow, and with all the reckless energy of his tribe was venting his wild exclamations upon the whale, and essaying to get a fair chance for his poised lance, lo!", "그런데 항해사 메이시가 보트 이물에 우뚝 선 채, 제 동족 특유의 무모한 기세로 고래를 향해 거친 고함을 내지르며 겨눈 창을 던질 좋은 기회를 노리고 있던 바로 그때, 보라!"], ["a broad white shadow rose from the sea; by its quick, fanning motion, temporarily taking the breath out of the bodies of the oarsmen.", "넓고 흰 그림자 하나가 바다에서 솟아오르더니, 그 빠르고 부채질하는 듯한 움직임으로 노잡이들의 몸에서 한순간 숨을 앗아 갔다."], ["Next instant, the luckless mate, so full of furious life, was smitten bodily into the air, and making a long arc in his descent, fell into the sea at the distance of about fifty yards.", "다음 순간, 그토록 격렬한 생기로 가득 차 있던 가련한 항해사는 통째로 허공으로 내쳐졌고, 떨어지면서 긴 호를 그리다가 약 오십 야드쯤 떨어진 바다에 떨어졌다."], ["Not a chip of the boat was harmed, nor a hair of any oarsman’s head; but the mate for ever sank.", "보트는 나뭇조각 하나 상하지 않았고 노잡이 누구의 머리카락 한 올 다치지 않았으나, 그 항해사는 영영 가라앉고 말았다."]], [["It is well to parenthesize here, that of the fatal accidents in the Sperm-Whale Fishery, this kind is perhaps almost as frequent as any.", "여기서 한마디 덧붙여 두는 것이 좋겠다. 향유고래잡이에서 일어나는 치명적 사고 가운데, 이런 종류는 아마도 어느 것 못지않게 잦은 편이다."], ["Sometimes, nothing is injured but the man who is thus annihilated; oftener the boat’s bow is knocked off, or the thigh-board, in which the headsman stands, is torn from its place and accompanies the body.", "때로는 이렇게 흔적도 없이 사라지는 사람 외에는 아무것도 상하지 않는다. 더 흔하게는 보트의 이물이 부서져 나가거나, 작살잡이가 발을 딛고 서는 넓적다리 받침판이 제자리에서 뜯겨 나가 그 몸과 함께 사라진다."], ["But strangest of all is the circumstance, that in more instances than one, when the body has been recovered, not a single mark of violence is discernible; the man being stark dead.", "그러나 무엇보다 기이한 것은, 한두 번이 아니라 여러 차례 그 시신을 건져 냈을 때, 폭력의 흔적이라고는 단 하나도 찾아볼 수 없는데도 그 사람이 완전히 죽어 있더라는 사실이다."]], [["The whole calamity, with the falling form of Macey, was plainly descried from the ship.", "메이시가 떨어지는 모습과 함께 이 모든 참극이 배에서 또렷이 목격되었다."], ["Raising a piercing shriek—“The vial! the vial!”", "가브리엘은 찢어지는 듯한 비명을 지르며—\"대접이다!"], ["Gabriel called off the terror-stricken crew from the further hunting of the whale.", "대접이다!\"—공포에 질린 선원들을 불러들여 더는 그 고래를 쫓지 못하게 했다."], ["This terrible event clothed the archangel with added influence; because his credulous disciples believed that he had specifically fore-announced it, instead of only making a general prophecy, which any one might have done, and so have chanced to hit one of many marks in the wide margin allowed.", "이 무서운 사건은 그 대천사에게 한층 더한 영향력을 입혀 주었다. 잘 속아 넘어가는 그의 제자들은, 그가 그저 누구나 할 수 있을 법한 막연한 예언을 하여 넓게 허용된 여백 속에서 여러 표적 가운데 하나를 우연히 맞힌 것이 아니라, 바로 이 일을 콕 집어 미리 예고했었다고 믿었기 때문이다."], ["He became a nameless terror to the ship.", "그는 배에서 이름조차 입에 올릴 수 없는 공포의 존재가 되었다."]], [["Mayhew having concluded his narration, Ahab put such questions to him, that the stranger captain could not forbear inquiring whether he intended to hunt the White Whale, if opportunity should offer.", "메이휴가 이야기를 마치자 에이해브가 그에게 이런저런 물음을 던졌고, 그 낯선 선장은 끝내 묻지 않을 수 없어, 만약 기회가 온다면 흰 고래를 사냥할 작정이냐고 되물었다. 이에 에이해브는 \"그렇소\"라고 대답했다."], ["To which Ahab answered—“Aye.”", "그러자 곧바로 가브리엘이 다시 한번 벌떡 일어나 노인을 노려보더니, 손가락을 아래로 가리키며 격렬하게 외쳤다."], ["Straightway, then, Gabriel once more started to his feet, glaring upon the old man, and vehemently exclaimed, with downward pointed finger—“Think, think of the blasphemer—dead, and down there!—beware of the blasphemer’s end!”", "\"생각하라, 저 신성모독자를 생각하라—죽어서 저 아래 가라앉은 그자를! 신성모독자의 최후를 조심하라!\""]], [["Ahab stolidly turned aside; then said to Mayhew, “Captain, I have just bethought me of my letter-bag; there is a letter for one of thy officers, if I mistake not.", "에이해브는 무덤덤하게 고개를 돌리더니, 메이휴에게 말했다. \"선장, 마침 내 편지 자루가 떠올랐소."], ["Starbuck, look over the bag.”", "내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 그대의 사관 중 한 사람에게 온 편지가 있소. 스타벅, 자루를 뒤져 보게.\""]], [["Every whale-ship takes out a goodly number of letters for various ships, whose delivery to the persons to whom they may be addressed, depends upon the mere chance of encountering them in the four oceans.", "고래잡이배는 저마다 여러 배 앞으로 온 적잖은 편지를 싣고 다니는데, 그 편지를 수신인에게 전하는 일은 오로지 네 대양에서 그들과 마주치는 우연에 달려 있다."], ["Thus, most letters never reach their mark; and many are only received after attaining an age of two or three years or more.", "그리하여 대부분의 편지는 끝내 제 목적지에 닿지 못하며, 더러는 두세 해, 혹은 그 이상의 세월을 묵힌 뒤에야 비로소 받아 보게 된다."]], [["Soon Starbuck returned with a letter in his hand.", "이윽고 스타벅이 편지 한 통을 손에 들고 돌아왔다."], ["It was sorely tumbled, damp, and covered with a dull, spotted, green mould, in consequence of being kept in a dark locker of the cabin.", "선실의 어두운 사물함 속에 보관되어 있던 탓에, 그 편지는 잔뜩 구겨지고 눅눅하며 칙칙한 얼룩무늬의 푸른 곰팡이로 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["Of such a letter, Death himself might well have been the post-boy.", "그런 편지라면 죽음 그 자체가 우편배달부 노릇을 했다 해도 능히 어울렸으리라."]], [["“Can’st not read it?”", "\"못 읽겠소?\" 에이해브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Ahab. “Give it me, man.", "\"이리 주시오, 이보게."], ["Aye, aye, it’s but a dim scrawl;—what’s this?”", "그래그래, 흐릿하게 갈겨쓴 글씨로군—이건 뭐지?\""], ["As he was studying it out, Starbuck took a long cutting-spade pole, and with his knife slightly split the end, to insert the letter there, and in that way, hand it to the boat, without its coming any closer to the ship.", "그가 그것을 들여다보며 풀어 가는 동안, 스타벅은 긴 고래 자르는 삽 자루를 가져다 칼로 그 끝을 살짝 쪼개어 편지를 끼워 넣었으니, 그렇게 하면 보트가 배에 더 가까이 다가오지 않고도 편지를 건넬 수 있었다."]], [["Meantime, Ahab holding the letter, muttered, “Mr. Har—yes, Mr.", "그러는 사이 에이해브는 편지를 들고 중얼거렸다."], ["Harry—(a woman’s pinny hand,—the man’s wife, I’ll wager)—Aye—Mr. Harry Macey, Ship Jeroboam;—why it’s Macey, and he’s dead!”", "\"해리—그래, 해리 씨—(여자의 가냘픈 필체로군—이 사내의 아내가 분명해, 내기를 걸어도 좋아)—그래—해리 메이시 씨, 제로보암호—아니, 이건 메이시잖나, 그런데 그자는 죽었거늘!\""]], [["“Poor fellow!", "\"가련한 사람!"], ["poor fellow!", "가련한 사람!"], ["and from his wife,”", "게다가 그 아내가 보낸 것이라니.\""], ["sighed Mayhew; “but let me have it.”", "메이휴가 한숨지었다. \"하지만 내게 주시오.\""]], [["“Nay, keep it thyself,”", "\"아니, 네가 가지고 있어라.\""], ["cried Gabriel to Ahab; “thou art soon going that way.”", "가브리엘이 에이해브에게 외쳤다. \"너도 머지않아 그 길로 가게 될 테니.\""]], [["“Curses throttle thee!”", "\"저주가 네 목을 졸라 버리기를!\""], ["yelled Ahab.", "에이해브가 고함쳤다."], ["“Captain Mayhew, stand by now to receive it”; and taking the fatal missive from Starbuck’s hands, he caught it in the slit of the pole, and reached it over towards the boat.", "\"메이휴 선장, 자, 받을 채비를 하시오.\" 그러고는 스타벅의 손에서 그 불길한 편지를 받아 자루 끝의 쪼개진 틈에 끼우고 보트 쪽으로 내밀었다."], ["But as he did so, the oarsmen expectantly desisted from rowing; the boat drifted a little towards the ship’s stern; so that, as if by magic, the letter suddenly ranged along with Gabriel’s eager hand.", "그러나 그가 그리하는 사이, 노잡이들이 무언가를 기대하며 노 젓기를 멈추는 바람에, 보트가 배의 고물 쪽으로 조금 떠밀렸다. 그리하여 마치 마법에라도 걸린 듯, 편지가 별안간 가브리엘의 잽싼 손과 나란히 놓이게 되었다."], ["He clutched it in an instant, seized the boat-knife, and impaling the letter on it, sent it thus loaded back into the ship.", "그는 순식간에 그것을 움켜쥐더니 보트용 칼을 집어 그 위에 편지를 꿰어, 그렇게 얹은 채로 도로 배 안으로 내던졌다."], ["It fell at Ahab’s feet.", "편지는 에이해브의 발치에 떨어졌다."], ["Then Gabriel shrieked out to his comrades to give way with their oars, and in that manner the mutinous boat rapidly shot away from the Pequod.", "그러자 가브리엘은 동료들에게 노를 저으라고 비명을 질렀고, 그렇게 하여 그 반항적인 보트는 순식간에 피쿼드호로부터 멀어져 갔다."]], [["As, after this interlude, the seamen resumed their work upon the jacket of the whale, many strange things were hinted in reference to this wild affair.", "이 막간이 지난 뒤 선원들이 다시 고래의 껍질을 벗기는 일에 손을 대는 동안, 이 거친 사건을 두고 갖가지 기이한 이야기들이 넌지시 오갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 72. The Monkey-Rope.", "# CHAPTER 72. The Monkey-Rope."]], [["In the tumultuous business of cutting-in and attending to a whale, there is much running backwards and forwards among the crew.", "고래를 해체하고 돌보는 그 소란스러운 작업 중에는, 선원들 사이에서 앞뒤로 뛰어다니는 일이 무척 많다."], ["Now hands are wanted here, and then again hands are wanted there.", "어느 순간에는 이곳에 일손이 필요하고, 또 어느 순간에는 저곳에 일손이 필요하다."], ["There is no staying in any one place; for at one and the same time everything has to be done everywhere.", "한자리에 머물러 있을 수가 없다. 같은 순간에 모든 일이 사방에서 처리되어야 하기 때문이다."], ["It is much the same with him who endeavors the description of the scene.", "이 장면을 묘사하려 애쓰는 사람도 사정은 매한가지다."], ["We must now retrace our way a little.", "우리는 이제 왔던 길을 조금 되짚어 가야 한다."], ["It was mentioned that upon first breaking ground in the whale’s back, the blubber-hook was inserted into the original hole there cut by the spades of the mates.", "앞서 고래의 등에 처음 작업을 시작할 때, 항해사들의 작업삽으로 잘라 낸 그 첫 구멍에 지방 갈고리를 박아 넣었다는 이야기를 했다."], ["But how did so clumsy and weighty a mass as that same hook get fixed in that hole?", "그런데 그토록 둔중하고 묵직한 덩어리인 그 갈고리가 어떻게 그 구멍에 단단히 박힐 수 있었던 것일까?"], ["It was inserted there by my particular friend Queequeg, whose duty it was, as harpooneer, to descend upon the monster’s back for the special purpose referred to.", "그것을 거기에 박아 넣은 사람은 다름 아닌 나의 각별한 친구 퀴케그(Queequeg)였으니, 작살잡이로서 앞서 말한 그 특별한 목적을 위해 괴물의 등으로 내려가는 것이 그의 임무였다."], ["But in very many cases, circumstances require that the harpooneer shall remain on the whale till the whole flensing or stripping operation is concluded.", "그러나 매우 많은 경우, 사정상 작살잡이는 해체 혹은 껍질 벗기는 작업이 모두 끝날 때까지 고래 위에 남아 있어야 한다."], ["The whale, be it observed, lies almost entirely submerged, excepting the immediate parts operated upon.", "눈여겨볼 것은, 고래가 작업이 이루어지는 바로 그 부분만을 제외하고는 거의 전부 물속에 잠긴 채로 떠 있다는 점이다."], ["So down there, some ten feet below the level of the deck, the poor harpooneer flounders about, half on the whale and half in the water, as the vast mass revolves like a tread-mill beneath him.", "그래서 갑판 높이보다 약 열 자(약 3미터) 아래 그 자리에서, 가련한 작살잡이는 몸의 절반은 고래 위에, 절반은 물속에 둔 채로 허우적거린다. 그러는 동안 그 거대한 덩어리는 발밑에서 디딜방아처럼 빙글빙글 돌아간다."], ["On the occasion in question, Queequeg figured in the Highland costume—a shirt and socks—in which to my eyes, at least, he appeared to uncommon advantage; and no one had a better chance to observe him, as will presently be seen.", "지금 말하는 그 경우에, 퀴케그는 하일랜드식 복장—셔츠와 양말—을 하고 있었는데, 적어도 내 눈에는 그 차림이 그를 유달리 돋보이게 했다. 그리고 곧 보게 되겠지만, 그를 관찰하기에 나보다 더 좋은 위치에 있는 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["Being the savage’s bowsman, that is, the person who pulled the bow-oar in his boat (the second one from forward), it was my cheerful duty to attend upon him while taking that hard-scrabble scramble upon the dead whale’s back.", "나는 그 야만인의 이물 노잡이, 다시 말해 그의 보트(앞에서 두 번째 보트)에서 이물 노를 젓는 사람이었으므로, 죽은 고래의 등 위에서 그가 그 고된 발버둥질을 하는 동안 그를 보살피는 것이 나의 즐거운 임무였다."], ["You have seen Italian organ-boys holding a dancing-ape by a long cord.", "당신들도 긴 줄로 춤추는 원숭이를 붙들고 있는 이탈리아 풍금 소년을 본 적이 있을 것이다."], ["Just so, from the ship’s steep side, did I hold Queequeg down there in the sea, by what is technically called in the fishery a monkey-rope, attached to a strong strip of canvas belted round his waist.", "꼭 그와 같이, 나는 배의 가파른 옆구리에서, 포경업계에서 전문적으로 원숭이 밧줄(monkey-rope)이라 부르는 것으로 저 아래 바닷속의 퀴케그를 붙들고 있었으니, 그 밧줄은 그의 허리에 두른 튼튼한 범포 띠에 매여 있었다."]], [["It was a humorously perilous business for both of us.", "그것은 우리 둘 모두에게 우스꽝스러우면서도 위태로운 일이었다."], ["For, before we proceed further, it must be said that the monkey-rope was fast at both ends; fast to Queequeg’s broad canvas belt, and fast to my narrow leather one.", "더 나아가기 전에 반드시 말해 두어야 할 것은, 이 원숭이 밧줄이 양쪽 끝이 모두 단단히 매여 있었다는 점이다. 한쪽 끝은 퀴케그의 넓은 범포 띠에, 다른 한쪽 끝은 나의 좁은 가죽 띠에 묶여 있었다."], ["So that for better or for worse, we two, for the time, were wedded; and should poor Queequeg sink to rise no more, then both usage and honor demanded, that instead of cutting the cord, it should drag me down in his wake.", "그리하여 좋든 나쁘든, 우리 둘은 그 순간만큼은 혼인한 사이였다. 만약 가련한 퀴케그가 가라앉아 다시는 떠오르지 못하게 된다면, 관습으로 보나 명예로 보나, 그 밧줄을 끊어 버리는 대신 그것이 그를 따라 나를 함께 끌고 내려가야 마땅했다."], ["So, then, an elongated Siamese ligature united us.", "그러니 길게 늘어진 샴쌍둥이의 끈이 우리를 하나로 잇고 있었던 셈이다."], ["Queequeg was my own inseparable twin brother; nor could I any way get rid of the dangerous liabilities which the hempen bond entailed.", "퀴케그는 나의 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 쌍둥이 형제였으며, 그 삼줄의 인연이 안겨 준 위험한 책임에서 나는 결코 어떤 식으로도 벗어날 수 없었다."]], [["So strongly and metaphysically did I conceive of my situation then, that while earnestly watching his motions, I seemed distinctly to perceive that my own individuality was now merged in a joint stock company of two; that my free will had received a mortal wound; and that another’s mistake or misfortune might plunge innocent me into unmerited disaster and death.", "그때 나는 내 처지를 어찌나 강렬하게, 또 형이상학적으로 헤아렸던지, 그의 움직임을 골똘히 지켜보면서 분명히 깨닫는 듯했다. 내 자신의 개별성이 이제 둘이 합쳐진 합자회사(合資會社) 속에 녹아들었다는 것을, 내 자유의지가 치명적인 상처를 입었다는 것을, 그리고 남의 실수나 불운이 죄 없는 나를 마땅하지 않은 재앙과 죽음 속으로 빠뜨릴 수도 있다는 것을."], ["Therefore, I saw that here was a sort of interregnum in Providence; for its even-handed equity never could have so gross an injustice.", "그러므로 나는 여기에 일종의 섭리의 공백기가 있음을 알았다. 섭리의 공정한 형평이라면 결코 이토록 엄청난 불의를 허용할 리 없기 때문이다."], ["And yet still further pondering—while I jerked him now and then from between the whale and ship, which would threaten to jam him—still further pondering, I say, I saw that this situation of mine was the precise situation of every mortal that breathes; only, in most cases, he, one way or other, has this Siamese connexion with a plurality of other mortals.", "그럼에도 더 깊이 생각을 거듭하면서—이따금 그를 고래와 배 사이에서 홱 끌어올려 주면서, 그렇지 않으면 그 틈에 끼일 위험이 있었기에—더더욱 깊이 생각을 거듭한 끝에, 나는 깨달았다. 이 나의 처지야말로 숨 쉬는 모든 인간의 정확한 처지라는 것을."], ["If your banker breaks, you snap; if your apothecary by mistake sends you poison in your pills, you die.", "다만 대부분의 경우, 인간은 이런저런 방식으로 다른 여러 인간과 이런 샴쌍둥이식 연결을 맺고 있다는 점이 다를 뿐이다."], ["True, you may say that, by exceeding caution, you may possibly escape these and the multitudinous other evil chances of life.", "당신의 은행가가 파산하면 당신도 끊어지고, 당신의 약제사가 실수로 알약 속에 독을 담아 보내면 당신은 죽는다. 물론 당신은 지나칠 만큼 조심하면 이런 일들과, 그 밖의 무수한 삶의 악운들을 피할 수 있을지도 모른다고 말할 수 있다."], ["But handle Queequeg’s monkey-rope heedfully as I would, sometimes he jerked it so, that I came very near sliding overboard.", "그러나 내가 아무리 퀴케그의 원숭이 밧줄을 조심스레 다루어도, 때로는 그가 어찌나 세게 그것을 홱 잡아당기던지, 나는 하마터면 뱃전 너머로 미끄러져 떨어질 뻔했다."], ["Nor could I possibly forget that, do what I would, I only had the management of one end of it.*", "게다가 내가 무슨 수를 쓴들 그 밧줄의 한쪽 끝만 관리할 수 있을 뿐이라는 사실을 결코 잊을 수 없었다.*"]], [["*The monkey-rope is found in all whalers; but it was only in the Pequod that the monkey and his holder were ever tied together.", "* 원숭이 밧줄은 모든 포경선에서 볼 수 있다. 그러나 원숭이와 그를 붙드는 자가 서로 묶여 있었던 것은 오직 피쿼드(Pequod)호뿐이었다."], ["This improvement upon the original usage was introduced by no less a man than Stubb, in order to afford the imperilled harpooneer the strongest possible guarantee for the faithfulness and vigilance of his monkey-rope holder.", "본래의 관습에 이런 개량을 도입한 사람은 다름 아닌 스터브(Stubb)였으니, 위험에 처한 작살잡이에게 그를 붙드는 원숭이 밧줄잡이의 성실함과 경계심에 대한 가능한 한 가장 강력한 보증을 마련해 주기 위함이었다."]], [["I have hinted that I would often jerk poor Queequeg from between the whale and the ship—where he would occasionally fall, from the incessant rolling and swaying of both.", "나는 가련한 퀴케그를 고래와 배 사이에서 이따금 홱 끌어올려 주곤 했다는 것을 넌지시 말한 바 있다—그는 양쪽이 끊임없이 흔들리고 출렁이는 통에 때때로 그 틈에 빠지곤 했던 것이다."], ["But this was not the only jamming jeopardy he was exposed to.", "그러나 그가 노출된 위험은 그렇게 끼이는 것만이 아니었다."], ["Unappalled by the massacre made upon them during the night, the sharks now freshly and more keenly allured by the before pent blood which began to flow from the carcass—the rabid creatures swarmed round it like bees in a beehive.", "간밤에 자기들에게 가해진 대량 학살에도 전혀 겁먹지 않은 채, 이제 상어들은 사체에서 흘러나오기 시작한, 그동안 갇혀 있던 피에 새로이, 그리고 더욱 날카롭게 이끌려—그 미쳐 날뛰는 짐승들이 벌집 속의 벌 떼처럼 사체 주위로 우글거리며 몰려들었다."]], [["And right in among those sharks was Queequeg; who often pushed them aside with his floundering feet.", "그리고 바로 그 상어들 한복판에 퀴케그가 있었으니, 그는 허우적거리는 발로 자주 그것들을 밀어내곤 했다."], ["A thing altogether incredible were it not that attracted by such prey as a dead whale, the otherwise miscellaneously carnivorous shark will seldom touch a man.", "죽은 고래 같은 먹이에 이끌리면 평소에는 닥치는 대로 무엇이든 먹어 치우는 상어도 좀처럼 사람은 건드리지 않는다는 사실이 아니었다면, 이는 도무지 믿을 수 없는 일이었을 것이다."]], [["Nevertheless, it may well be believed that since they have such a ravenous finger in the pie, it is deemed but wise to look sharp to them.", "그럼에도 상어들이 그토록 게걸스럽게 그 잔치에 손을 들이밀고 있는 만큼, 그것들을 예리하게 경계하는 것이 현명한 일로 여겨지는 것은 충분히 수긍할 만하다."], ["Accordingly, besides the monkey-rope, with which I now and then jerked the poor fellow from too close a vicinity to the maw of what seemed a peculiarly ferocious shark—he was provided with still another protection.", "그리하여 원숭이 밧줄 외에도—나는 그 밧줄로 이따금 가련한 친구를 유난히 사나워 보이는 어느 상어의 아가리에 너무 가까이 다가가지 않도록 홱 끌어당겨 주었다—그에게는 또 다른 보호 장치가 마련되어 있었다."], ["Suspended over the side in one of the stages, Tashtego and Daggoo continually flourished over his head a couple of keen whale-spades, wherewith they slaughtered as many sharks as they could reach.", "뱃전 너머로 늘어뜨린 발판 가운데 하나에 매달린 채, 태시테고(Tashtego)와 대구(Daggoo)가 그의 머리 위에서 끊임없이 날카로운 고래삽 두 자루를 휘둘러, 손이 닿는 데까지 닥치는 대로 상어를 도살했다."], ["This procedure of theirs, to be sure, was very disinterested and benevolent of them.", "물론 그들의 이런 행동은 무척이나 사심 없고 자비로운 것이었다."], ["They meant Queequeg’s best happiness, I admit; but in their hasty zeal to befriend him, and from the circumstance that both he and the sharks were at times half hidden by the blood-muddled water, those indiscreet spades of theirs would come nearer amputating a leg than a tail.", "그들이 퀴케그의 더없는 행복을 바랐다는 것은 나도 인정한다. 그러나 그를 도우려는 성급한 열의 탓에, 또 그와 상어가 때때로 피로 흐려진 물에 절반쯤 가려져 있던 사정 탓에, 그들의 그 경솔한 삽은 꼬리보다는 다리를 잘라 낼 뻔하기 일쑤였다."], ["But poor Queequeg, I suppose, straining and gasping there with that great iron hook—poor Queequeg, I suppose, only prayed to his Yojo, and gave up his life into the hands of his gods.", "하지만 가련한 퀴케그는, 내 짐작에, 그 커다란 쇠갈고리를 붙들고 안간힘을 쓰며 헐떡이던 그 자리에서—가련한 퀴케그는, 내 짐작에, 그저 자기 요조(Yojo)에게 기도하며 제 목숨을 신들의 손에 맡겼을 따름이다."]], [["Well, well, my dear comrade and twin-brother, thought I, as I drew in and then slacked off the rope to every swell of the sea—what matters it, after all?", "자, 자, 나의 소중한 동지이자 쌍둥이 형제여, 나는 바다의 너울에 따라 밧줄을 끌어당겼다가 다시 늦추어 주면서 생각했다—결국 그게 다 무슨 상관이란 말인가?"], ["Are you not the precious image of each and all of us men in this whaling world?", "그대야말로 이 포경 세계 속 우리 모든 인간 하나하나의 소중한 표상이 아니던가?"], ["That unsounded ocean you gasp in, is Life; those sharks, your foes; those spades, your friends; and what between sharks and spades you are in a sad pickle and peril, poor lad.", "그대가 그 속에서 헐떡이는 저 깊이를 알 수 없는 대양은 삶이요, 저 상어들은 그대의 적이며, 저 삽들은 그대의 벗이로다. 그리고 상어와 삽 사이에 끼인 그대는, 가련한 친구여, 슬픈 곤경과 위험에 처해 있구나."]], [["But courage!", "그러나 용기를 내라!"], ["there is good cheer in store for you, Queequeg.", "그대에게도 좋은 위안이 마련되어 있다네, 퀴케그여."], ["For now, as with blue lips and blood-shot eyes the exhausted savage at last climbs up the chains and stands all dripping and involuntarily trembling over the side; the steward advances, and with a benevolent, consolatory glance hands him—what?", "이제, 새파래진 입술과 핏발 선 눈으로 기진맥진한 그 야만인이 마침내 사슬을 타고 기어올라 온몸에서 물을 뚝뚝 흘리며 저도 모르게 부들부들 떨면서 뱃전에 서자, 사환이 다가와 자애롭고도 위로 어린 눈길로 그에게 무언가를 건넨다—무엇을?"], ["Some hot Cognac?", "뜨거운 코냑이라도? 천만에!"], ["No! hands him, ye gods!", "그가 건넨 것은, 오 신들이여!"], ["hands him a cup of tepid ginger and water!", "그가 건넨 것은 미지근한 생강 물 한 잔이었다!"]], [["“Ginger?", "\"생강?"], ["Do I smell ginger?”", "내가 지금 생강 냄새를 맡은 건가?\""], ["suspiciously asked Stubb, coming near.", "스터브가 다가오며 미심쩍게 물었다."], ["“Yes, this must be ginger,”", "\"그래, 이건 분명 생강이야.\""], ["peering into the as yet untasted cup.", "그는 아직 아무도 입을 대지 않은 잔을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["Then standing as if incredulous for a while, he calmly walked towards the astonished steward slowly saying, “Ginger?", "그러고는 한동안 도무지 믿기지 않는다는 듯이 서 있다가, 어안이 벙벙해진 사환을 향해 차분히 걸어가며 천천히 말했다. \"생강?"], ["ginger?", "생강이라?"], ["and will you have the goodness to tell me, Mr. Dough-Boy, where lies the virtue of ginger?", "도우보이(Dough-Boy)씨, 부디 좀 알려 주시겠나, 생강의 효능이 도대체 어디에 있는지 말일세?"], ["Ginger!", "생강이라!"], ["is ginger the sort of fuel you use, Dough-boy, to kindle a fire in this shivering cannibal?", "도우보이, 자네는 이 벌벌 떠는 식인종 몸뚱이에 불을 지피겠다고 생강 따위를 연료로 쓰는 건가?"], ["Ginger!—what the devil is ginger?", "생강이라니!—도대체 생강이 무엇이냔 말이야?"], ["Sea-coal?", "석탄인가?"], ["firewood?—lucifer matches?—tinder?—gunpowder?—what the devil is ginger, I say, that you offer this cup to our poor Queequeg here.”", "장작인가?—성냥개비인가?—부싯깃인가?—화약인가?—내 다시 묻건대, 도대체 생강이란 게 무엇이기에 자네는 여기 우리 가련한 퀴케그에게 이런 잔을 내미는 건가.\""]], [["“There is some sneaking Temperance Society movement about this business,”", "\"이 일에는 뭔가 음흉한 금주협회(禁酒協會)의 책동이 끼어 있구먼.\""], ["he suddenly added, now approaching Starbuck, who had just come from forward.", "그는 갑자기 이렇게 덧붙이며, 이제 막 앞쪽에서 온 스타벅(Starbuck)에게 다가갔다."], ["“Will you look at that kannakin, sir: smell of it, if you please.”", "\"이 깡통 좀 보십시오, 항해사님. 괜찮으시다면 냄새 한번 맡아 보시지요.\""], ["Then watching the mate’s countenance, he added, “The steward, Mr.", "그러고는 항해사의 표정을 살피며 덧붙였다."], ["Starbuck, had the face to offer that calomel and jalap to Queequeg, there, this instant off the whale.", "\"스타벅 씨, 저 사환 놈이 글쎄 방금 고래에서 막 올라온 저기 퀴케그에게 이런 감홍(甘汞)이며 약쑥 따위를 내밀 낯짝이 있더란 말입니다."], ["Is the steward an apothecary, sir?", "항해사님, 저 사환이 약제사라도 됩니까?"], ["and may I ask whether this is the sort of bitters by which he blows back the life into a half-drowned man?”", "그리고 묻고 싶습니다만, 반쯤 익사한 사람에게 다시 숨을 불어넣겠다는 게 고작 이런 쓴 약 따위입니까?\""]], [["“I trust not,”", "\"그건 아니길 바라네.\""], ["said Starbuck, “it is poor stuff enough.”", "스타벅이 말했다. \"정말 형편없는 물건이군.\""]], [["“Aye, aye, steward,”", "\"옳소, 옳소, 사환.\" 스터브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Stubb, “we’ll teach you to drug a harpooneer; none of your apothecary’s medicine here; you want to poison us, do ye?", "\"작살잡이한테 약 먹이는 짓이 어떤 건지 우리가 똑똑히 가르쳐 주지. 여기서는 자네 약제사 약 따위는 통하지 않아."], ["You have got out insurances on our lives and want to murder us all, and pocket the proceeds, do ye?”", "우리를 독살하려는 게냐, 응? 우리 목숨에 보험이라도 들어 놓고 우리 모두를 죽인 다음에 그 돈을 챙기려는 게지, 안 그래?\""]], [["“It was not me,”", "\"제가 한 게 아닙니다.\" 도우보이가 외쳤다."], ["cried Dough-Boy, “it was Aunt Charity that brought the ginger on board; and bade me never give the harpooneers any spirits, but only this ginger-jub—so she called it.”", "\"그 생강은 채러티(Charity) 아주머니가 배에 실어 놓으신 겁니다. 그러면서 작살잡이들한테는 절대 독한 술을 주지 말고 오직 이 생강 음료만 주라고 분부하셨어요—아주머니가 그렇게 부르시더군요.\""]], [["“Ginger-jub!", "\"생강 음료라니!"], ["you gingerly rascal! take that!", "이 생강 같은 비실비실한 녀석아!"], ["and run along with ye to the lockers, and get something better.", "이거나 받아라! 그리고 어서 저장고로 달려가서 더 나은 걸 가져와."], ["I hope I do no wrong, Mr. Starbuck.", "스타벅 씨, 제가 잘못하는 건 아니겠지요."], ["It is the captain’s orders—grog for the harpooneer on a whale.”", "이건 선장의 명령입니다—고래 위에 오른 작살잡이에게는 그로그주를 주라는.\""]], [["“Enough,”", "\"됐네.\""], ["replied Starbuck, “only don’t hit him again, but—”", "스타벅이 대답했다. \"다만 저 친구를 다시 때리지는 말게, 그저—\""]], [["“Oh, I never hurt when I hit, except when I hit a whale or something of that sort; and this fellow’s a weazel.", "\"아, 저는 때린다고 사람을 다치게 한 적이 없습니다요, 고래나 그 비슷한 걸 칠 때 말고는요. 게다가 이 녀석은 족제비 같은 놈인걸요."], ["What were you about saying, sir?”", "그런데 무슨 말씀을 하시려던 참이었습니까, 항해사님?\""]], [["“Only this: go down with him, and get what thou wantest thyself.”", "\"그저 이것뿐일세. 저 친구와 함께 내려가서, 자네가 원하는 걸 직접 가져오게.\""]], [["When Stubb reappeared, he came with a dark flask in one hand, and a sort of tea-caddy in the other.", "스터브가 다시 나타났을 때, 그는 한 손에는 거무스름한 술병을, 다른 손에는 차 보관통 비슷한 것을 들고 있었다."], ["The first contained strong spirits, and was handed to Queequeg; the second was Aunt Charity’s gift, and that was freely given to the waves.", "첫 번째 것에는 독한 술이 들어 있었고 그것은 퀴케그에게 건네졌다. 두 번째 것은 채러티 아주머니의 선물이었는데, 그것은 아낌없이 파도에게 내던져졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER 73. Stubb and Flask kill a Right Whale; and Then Have a Talk over Him.", "# CHAPTER 73. Stubb and Flask kill a Right Whale; and Then Have a Talk over Him."]], [["It must be borne in mind that all this time we have a Sperm Whale’s prodigious head hanging to the Pequod’s side.", "이 모든 시간 동안 우리에게는 페쿼드(Pequod)호의 뱃전에 매달려 있는 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 거대한 머리가 있었다는 사실을 잊지 말아야 한다."], ["But we must let it continue hanging there a while till we can get a chance to attend to it.", "그러나 그 머리를 손볼 기회가 생길 때까지는 잠시 그대로 매달려 있게 두는 수밖에 없다."], ["For the present other matters press, and the best we can do now for the head, is to pray heaven the tackles may hold.", "지금은 다른 일들이 더 급하게 밀려들고 있으니, 그 머리를 위해 우리가 지금 할 수 있는 최선이란 다만 도르래 장치가 버텨 주기를 하늘에 비는 것뿐이다."]], [["Now, during the past night and forenoon, the Pequod had gradually drifted into a sea, which, by its occasional patches of yellow brit, gave unusual tokens of the vicinity of Right Whales, a species of the Leviathan that but few supposed to be at this particular time lurking anywhere near.", "자, 지난밤과 그날 오전 동안 페쿼드호는 차츰차츰 어떤 바다로 흘러들어 갔는데, 그곳은 군데군데 떠 있는 누런 브릿(brit) 무리로 미루어 참고래(Right Whale)가 가까이 있음을 알리는 심상찮은 징조를 드러내고 있었다. 참고래는 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 한 종류로, 바로 이 무렵 이 근처 어딘가에 숨어 있으리라고는 거의 아무도 짐작하지 못한 놈이었다."], ["And though all hands commonly disdained the capture of those inferior creatures; and though the Pequod was not commissioned to cruise for them at all, and though she had passed numbers of them near the Crozetts without lowering a boat; yet now that a Sperm Whale had been brought alongside and beheaded, to the surprise of all, the announcement was made that a Right Whale should be captured that day, if opportunity offered.", "비록 선원들 모두가 이런 하등한 짐승을 잡는 일을 흔히 업신여겼고, 페쿼드호 역시 애초에 그것을 좇으라는 명을 받은 바가 전혀 없었으며, 크로제 제도 근처에서 수많은 참고래를 지나치면서도 보트 한 척 내리지 않았건만, 이제 향유고래 한 마리를 뱃전으로 끌어와 목을 베고 보니, 모두가 놀랍게도 기회만 닿는다면 그날 참고래 한 마리를 잡자는 선언이 내려졌다. * 브릿(brit): 고래가 먹이로 삼는 작은 바다 생물 무리."]], [["Nor was this long wanting.", "그리고 그 기회는 오래 기다릴 것도 없이 찾아왔다."], ["Tall spouts were seen to leeward; and two boats, Stubb’s and Flask’s, were detached in pursuit.", "바람 불어가는 쪽에서 높이 솟구치는 물줄기가 보였고, 스터브(Stubb)와 플래스크(Flask)의 보트 두 척이 추격에 나섰다."], ["Pulling further and further away, they at last became almost invisible to the men at the mast-head.", "점점 더 멀리 노를 저어 나아가는 통에, 그들은 마침내 돛대 꼭대기에 있는 사람들의 눈에도 거의 보이지 않게 되었다."], ["But suddenly in the distance, they saw a great heap of tumultuous white water, and soon after news came from aloft that one or both the boats must be fast.", "그러나 갑자기 저 멀리서, 그들은 어지러이 들끓는 새하얀 물보라가 크게 솟구치는 것을 보았고, 곧이어 두 보트 중 하나, 혹은 둘 다 고래에 작살을 박아 끌려가고 있다는 소식이 돛대 위에서 들려왔다."], ["An interval passed and the boats were in plain sight, in the act of being dragged right towards the ship by the towing whale.", "한참 지나자 보트들이 또렷이 보였는데, 끌고 가는 고래에 의해 곧장 배 쪽으로 끌려오는 중이었다."], ["So close did the monster come to the hull, that at first it seemed as if he meant it malice; but suddenly going down in a maelstrom, within three rods of the planks, he wholly disappeared from view, as if diving under the keel.", "그 괴물이 어찌나 선체 가까이 다가왔던지, 처음에는 마치 배에 앙심을 품은 것처럼 보였다. 그러나 별안간 뱃전 널빤지에서 세 길도 채 안 되는 거리에서 소용돌이 속으로 가라앉더니, 마치 용골 밑으로 잠수해 들어가기라도 하듯 시야에서 완전히 사라져 버렸다."], ["“Cut, cut!”", "\"줄을 끊어, 끊으라고!\""], ["was the cry from the ship to the boats, which, for one instant, seemed on the point of being brought with a deadly dash against the vessel’s side.", "배에서 보트들을 향해 외치는 소리가 터져 나왔다. 한순간 보트들이 배 옆구리에 치명적으로 부딪칠 것만 같았기 때문이다."], ["But having plenty of line yet in the tubs, and the whale not sounding very rapidly, they paid out abundance of rope, and at the same time pulled with all their might so as to get ahead of the ship.", "그러나 줄통에는 아직 밧줄이 넉넉히 남아 있었고, 고래 또한 그리 빠르게 잠수하지는 않았으므로, 그들은 밧줄을 넉넉히 풀어 주는 동시에 온 힘을 다해 노를 저어 배보다 앞서 나가려 했다."], ["For a few minutes the struggle was intensely critical; for while they still slacked out the tightened line in one direction, and still plied their oars in another, the contending strain threatened to take them under.", "몇 분 동안 그 싸움은 극도로 아슬아슬했다. 한쪽으로는 팽팽해진 줄을 계속 늦춰 주면서, 다른 쪽으로는 여전히 노를 저어 댔으니, 서로 맞서는 그 힘이 그들을 물속으로 끌어들일 듯 위협했던 것이다."], ["But it was only a few feet advance they sought to gain.", "그러나 그들이 얻으려던 것은 겨우 몇 자(尺)의 전진뿐이었다."], ["And they stuck to it till they did gain it; when instantly, a swift tremor was felt running like lightning along the keel, as the strained line, scraping beneath the ship, suddenly rose to view under her bows, snapping and quivering; and so flinging off its drippings, that the drops fell like bits of broken glass on the water, while the whale beyond also rose to sight, and once more the boats were free to fly.", "그리고 그들은 그것을 얻을 때까지 끝끝내 버텼다. 그러자 곧장, 번개처럼 빠른 떨림이 용골을 타고 달리는 것이 느껴졌으니, 배 밑을 긁고 지나가던 팽팽한 줄이 갑자기 뱃머리 아래로 떠오르며 탁탁 튀고 부르르 떨면서, 묻은 물방울을 사방으로 털어 냈는데, 그 물방울들이 마치 깨진 유리 조각처럼 수면 위로 떨어졌고, 그 너머에서 고래 또한 모습을 드러냈으니, 이제 보트들은 다시 자유로이 내달릴 수 있게 되었다."], ["But the fagged whale abated his speed, and blindly altering his course, went round the stern of the ship towing the two boats after him, so that they performed a complete circuit.", "그러나 지친 고래는 속도를 늦추더니, 무작정 방향을 틀어 배의 고물 쪽을 돌아 나가면서 두 보트를 뒤에 매단 채 끌고 가는 통에, 그들은 배 주위를 완전히 한 바퀴 돌게 되었다."]], [["Meantime, they hauled more and more upon their lines, till close flanking him on both sides, Stubb answered Flask with lance for lance; and thus round and round the Pequod the battle went, while the multitudes of sharks that had before swum round the Sperm Whale’s body, rushed to the fresh blood that was spilled, thirstily drinking at every new gash, as the eager Israelites did at the new bursting fountains that poured from the smitten rock.", "그러는 동안 그들은 점점 더 줄을 끌어당겨, 마침내 고래의 양 옆구리에 바짝 붙어서서, 스터브가 작살을 던지면 플래스크가 작살로 화답하는 식이 되었다. 그렇게 페쿼드호 둘레를 돌고 또 돌며 싸움이 이어지는 동안, 앞서 향유고래의 몸뚱이 둘레를 헤엄치던 무수한 상어 떼가 새로이 흘러나온 피로 몰려들어, 새로 베일 때마다 갈증 난 듯 그 피를 들이켰으니, 그 모습은 마치 바위를 쳐서 솟구쳐 나온 새 샘물에 목마른 이스라엘 백성이 달려들던 것과도 같았다."]], [["At last his spout grew thick, and with a frightful roll and vomit, he turned upon his back a corpse.", "마침내 그 물줄기가 걸쭉해지더니, 끔찍하게 한 번 뒤척이며 토악질을 하고는, 고래는 죽은 몸뚱이가 되어 벌렁 나자빠졌다."]], [["While the two headsmen were engaged in making fast cords to his flukes, and in other ways getting the mass in readiness for towing, some conversation ensued between them.", "두 작살잡이 우두머리가 고래 꼬리에 밧줄을 매고, 그 거대한 덩어리를 끌고 갈 채비를 이런저런 방법으로 갖추는 동안, 둘 사이에 이런 대화가 오갔다."]], [["“I wonder what the old man wants with this lump of foul lard,”", "\"영감님이 이런 고약한 비계 덩어리를 가지고 대체 뭘 하시려는 건지 모르겠군.\""], ["said Stubb, not without some disgust at the thought of having to do with so ignoble a leviathan.", "이렇게도 변변찮은 리바이어던을 상대해야 한다는 생각에 다소 역겨움을 느끼며 스터브가 말했다."]], [["“Wants with it?” said Flask, coiling some spare line in the boat’s bow, “did you never hear that the ship which but once has a Sperm Whale’s head hoisted on her starboard side, and at the same time a Right Whale’s on the larboard; did you never hear, Stubb, that that ship can never afterwards capsize?”", "\"이걸 가지고 뭘 하느냐고?\" 플래스크가 보트 뱃머리에 남는 밧줄을 사리며 말했다. \"자네 그런 얘기 한 번도 못 들어 봤나, 한 배가 우현에 향유고래 머리를 매단 동시에 좌현에 참고래 머리를 매달기만 하면, 그 배는 그 뒤로 절대 뒤집히는 일이 없다는 말? 그런 말 못 들어 봤어, 스터브?\" \"왜 안 뒤집힌다는 거야?\""]], [["“Why not?", "\"나도 모르지."], ["“I don’t know, but I heard that gamboge ghost of a Fedallah saying so, and he seems to know all about ships’ charms.", "하지만 저 누리끼리한 유령 같은 페달라(Fedallah)가 그렇게 말하는 걸 들었거든. 그 작자는 배에 관한 부적 따위는 죄다 꿰고 있는 것 같아."], ["But I sometimes think he’ll charm the ship to no good at last.", "그런데 난 가끔 그 작자가 결국엔 이 배에 좋지 못한 주술을 걸 거라는 생각이 들어."], ["I don’t half like that chap, Stubb.", "난 저 친구가 영 마음에 안 들어, 스터브."], ["Did you ever notice how that tusk of his is a sort of carved into a snake’s head, Stubb?”", "자네 그 작자 송곳니가 일종의 뱀 대가리 모양으로 깎여 있는 걸 본 적 있나, 스터브?\""]], [["“Sink him!", "\"그 작자야 물속에 처박혀 버리라지!"], ["I never look at him at all; but if ever I get a chance of a dark night, and he standing hard by the bulwarks, and no one by; look down there, Flask”—pointing into the sea with a peculiar motion of both hands—“Aye, will I!", "난 아예 쳐다보지도 않아. 하지만 언젠가 캄캄한 밤에 기회가 닿아서, 그 작자가 뱃전 난간 바로 옆에 서 있고 곁에 아무도 없으면, 저기 저 아래를 좀 봐, 플래스크.\""], ["Flask, I take that Fedallah to be the devil in disguise.", "두 손으로 묘한 동작을 지으며 바다를 가리켰다. \"암, 그렇게 하고말고!"], ["Do you believe that cock and bull story about his having been stowed away on board ship?", "플래스크, 난 그 페달라가 변장한 악마라고 생각해. 그 작자가 배에 몰래 숨어들었다는 그 황당무계한 이야기를 자네는 믿나?"], ["He’s the devil, I say.", "난 그자가 악마라고 단언하네."], ["The reason why you don’t see his tail, is because he tucks it up out of sight; he carries it coiled away in his pocket, I guess.", "자네가 그 작자 꼬리를 못 보는 이유는, 그 작자가 그걸 안 보이게 말아 올려 두기 때문이야. 내 짐작엔 그걸 둘둘 사려서 주머니에 넣고 다니는 거지."], ["Blast him!", "빌어먹을 놈!"], ["now that I think of it, he’s always wanting oakum to stuff into the toes of his boots.”", "이제 생각해 보니, 그 작자는 늘 부츠 발끝에 쑤셔 넣을 뱃밥을 달라고 하거든.\""]], [["“He sleeps in his boots, don’t he?", "\"그 작자는 부츠를 신은 채로 잔다지, 안 그래?"], ["He hasn’t got any hammock; but I’ve seen him lay of nights in a coil of rigging.”", "해먹도 없잖아. 난 그자가 밤이면 밧줄 사린 무더기 속에 드러눕는 걸 본 적이 있어.\""]], [["“No doubt, and it’s because of his cursed tail; he coils it down, do ye see, in the eye of the rigging.”", "\"틀림없어. 그게 다 그 빌어먹을 꼬리 때문이지. 알겠나, 그 작자는 밧줄 사린 그 구멍 속에 꼬리를 둘둘 사려 넣는 거야.\""]], [["“What’s the old man have so much to do with him for?”", "\"영감님은 대체 무슨 일로 그 작자랑 그렇게 자주 어울리는 거야?\""]], [["“Striking up a swap or a bargain, I suppose.”", "\"무슨 거래나 흥정을 트는 거겠지, 내 짐작엔.\""]], [["“Bargain?—about what?”", "\"흥정?—뭘 가지고?\""]], [["“Why, do ye see, the old man is hard bent after that White Whale, and the devil there is trying to come round him, and get him to swap away his silver watch, or his soul, or something of that sort, and then he’ll surrender Moby Dick.”", "\"그게 말이지, 알겠나, 영감님은 저 흰 고래(White Whale)를 잡겠다고 단단히 작정하셨잖아. 그러니까 저 악마가 영감님한테 접근해서, 은시계든 영혼이든 뭐 그런 걸 넘기게 꾀어내려는 거지. 그러면 그 대가로 자기가 모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 넘겨주겠다는 거야.\""]], [["“Pooh!", "\"흥!"], ["Stubb, you are skylarking; how can Fedallah do that?”", "스터브, 자네 지금 장난치는 거지. 페달라가 어떻게 그런 일을 한단 말이야?\""]], [["“I don’t know, Flask, but the devil is a curious chap, and a wicked one, I tell ye.", "\"나도 모르지, 플래스크. 하지만 악마라는 놈은 묘한 작자고, 사악한 놈이야, 내 장담하네."], ["Why, they say as how he went a sauntering into the old flag-ship once, switching his tail about devilish easy and gentlemanlike, and inquiring if the old governor was at home.", "글쎄, 사람들 말로는 그 악마가 한번은 저 옛날 기함(旗艦)에 어슬렁어슬렁 들어갔다는 거야, 꼬리를 악마답게 아주 능청스럽고 신사처럼 살랑살랑 흔들면서 말이야. 그러고는 늙은 사령관이 댁에 계시느냐고 묻더라는 거지."], ["Well, he was at home, and asked the devil what he wanted.", "그런데 사령관이 마침 계셔서, 악마더러 무슨 일이냐고 물었어. 그러니까 악마가 발굽을 까딱거리며 벌떡 일어나 말하기를, '존을 데려가고 싶소.'"], ["The devil, switching his hoofs, up and says, ‘I want John.’ ‘What for?’ says the old governor.", "'뭐 하러?' 늙은 사령관이 물었지."], ["‘What business is that of yours,’ says the devil, getting mad,—‘I want to use him.’ ‘Take him,’ says the governor—and by the Lord, Flask, if the devil didn’t give John the Asiatic cholera before he got through with him, I’ll eat this whale in one mouthful.", "'그게 당신이 알 바요?' 악마가 화를 내며 말했어.—'그놈을 좀 부려먹을 일이 있소.'"], ["But look sharp—ain’t you all ready there?", "'데려가시오.' 사령관이 말했지.—그런데 맹세코, 플래스크, 만약 그 악마가 일을 다 끝내기도 전에 존한테 아시아 콜레라를 안겨 주지 않았다면, 내가 이 고래를 한입에 통째로 먹어 치우겠네."], ["Well, then, pull ahead, and let’s get the whale alongside.”", "그건 그렇고 정신 똑바로 차려.—거기 다들 준비됐나? 자, 그럼 앞으로 저어 가서 고래를 뱃전에 갖다 대자고.\""]], [["“I think I remember some such story as you were telling,”", "\"자네가 들려준 것과 비슷한 이야기를 어디선가 본 기억이 나는 것도 같은데,\""], ["said Flask, when at last the two boats were slowly advancing with their burden towards the ship, “but I can’t remember where.”", "마침내 두 보트가 짐을 끌고 배 쪽으로 천천히 나아가기 시작할 무렵 플래스크가 말했다. \"어디서 봤는지는 도무지 생각이 안 나는군.\""]], [["“Three Spaniards?", "\"세 스페인 사람들?"], ["Adventures of those three bloody-minded soldadoes?", "그 잔인무도한 군인 세 놈의 모험담 말인가?"], ["Did ye read it there, Flask?", "자네 그걸 거기서 읽었나, 플래스크?"], ["I guess ye did?”", "그런 거지, 아마?\""]], [["“No: never saw such a book; heard of it, though.", "\"아니. 그런 책은 본 적도 없어."], ["But now, tell me, Stubb, do you suppose that that devil you was speaking of just now, was the same you say is now on board the Pequod?”", "얘기는 들어 봤지만. 그런데 말이야, 스터브, 방금 자네가 말한 그 악마가, 지금 페쿼드호에 타고 있다고 한 그 작자와 같은 놈이라고 생각하는 거야?\""]], [["“Am I the same man that helped kill this whale?", "\"내가 이 고래 잡는 걸 도운 바로 그 사람과 같은 사람이냐고?"], ["Doesn’t the devil live for ever; who ever heard that the devil was dead?", "악마는 영원히 살지 않나? 악마가 죽었다는 소릴 누가 들어 봤어?"], ["Did you ever see any parson a wearing mourning for the devil?", "자네 어떤 목사가 악마를 위해 상복 입은 걸 본 적 있나?"], ["And if the devil has a latch-key to get into the admiral’s cabin, don’t you suppose he can crawl into a porthole?", "그리고 악마가 제독실에 드나들 빗장 열쇠를 가졌다면, 그놈이 현창(舷窓)으로 기어들어 오지 못하겠어?"], ["Tell me that, Mr. Flask?”", "어디 말해 보게, 플래스크 씨?\""]], [["“How old do you suppose Fedallah is, Stubb?”", "\"페달라가 몇 살쯤 됐다고 생각하나, 스터브?\""]], [["“Do you see that mainmast there?”", "\"저기 저 큰 돛대 보이지?\" 배를 가리키며 그가 말했다."], ["pointing to the ship; “well, that’s the figure one; now take all the hoops in the Pequod’s hold, and string along in a row with that mast, for oughts, do you see; well, that wouldn’t begin to be Fedallah’s age.", "\"자, 저게 숫자 1이라고 치자고. 이제 페쿼드호 선창에 있는 테(箍)를 죄다 가져다가 저 돛대 옆에 한 줄로 죽 늘어놓아서 0을 만들어 봐, 알겠나."], ["Nor all the coopers in creation couldn’t show hoops enough to make oughts enough.”", "그래도 그건 페달라 나이의 시작도 못 될걸. 천지간의 모든 통장이를 다 모아도 그만큼 많은 0을 만들 만큼 테를 보여 줄 순 없을 거야.\""]], [["“But see here, Stubb, I thought you a little boasted just now, that you meant to give Fedallah a sea-toss, if you got a good chance.", "\"그런데 잠깐, 스터브, 방금 자네가 좋은 기회만 잡으면 페달라를 바다에 한번 처넣어 주겠다고 좀 으스댄 것 같은데."], ["Now, if he’s so old as all those hoops of yours come to, and if he is going to live for ever, what good will it do to pitch him overboard—tell me that?", "그런데 만약 그 작자가 자네 말마따나 그 테들만큼이나 나이를 먹었고, 게다가 영원히 살 거라면, 그놈을 배 밖으로 내던진들 무슨 소용이 있겠어—어디 말해 봐.\" \"어쨌든 한번 물맛이나 톡톡히 보여 주는 거지.\""]], [["“Give him a good ducking, anyhow.”", "\"하지만 그놈은 도로 기어 올라올 텐데.\""]], [["“But he’d crawl back.”", "\"그럼 또 처박는 거지."], ["“Duck him again; and keep ducking him.”", "계속 처박아 대는 거야.\""]], [["“Suppose he should take it into his head to duck you, though—yes, and drown you—what then?”", "\"하지만 그놈이 도리어 자네를 처박을 생각을 한다면—그래, 그래서 자넬 물에 빠뜨려 죽인다면—그땐 어쩔 건데?\""]], [["“I should like to see him try it; I’d give him such a pair of black eyes that he wouldn’t dare to show his face in the admiral’s cabin again for a long while, let alone down in the orlop there, where he lives, and hereabouts on the upper decks where he sneaks so much.", "\"어디 한번 해 보라지. 내가 그놈 두 눈에 시퍼런 멍을 들여 놔서, 한동안은 제독실에 다시 얼굴도 못 내밀게 만들 테니까."], ["Damn the devil, Flask; so you suppose I’m afraid of the devil?", "그놈이 사는 저 맨 아래 화물칸은 말할 것도 없고, 그놈이 그렇게 자주 살금살금 기어 다니는 이 위쪽 갑판 근처에도 말이야. 악마 따위 빌어먹으라지, 플래스크."], ["Who’s afraid of him, except the old governor who daresn’t catch him and put him in double-darbies, as he deserves, but lets him go about kidnapping people; aye, and signed a bond with him, that all the people the devil kidnapped, he’d roast for him?", "그러니까 자넨 내가 악마를 무서워한다고 생각하는 거야? 그놈을 무서워할 자가 누가 있어, 그놈을 잡아다가 마땅히 받아야 할 대로 이중 족쇄를 채울 엄두도 못 내고 그저 사람들이나 납치하도록 내버려 두는 그 늙은 사령관 말고는?"], ["There’s a governor!”", "암, 게다가 그놈과 계약서까지 써서, 악마가 납치한 사람은 죄다 자기가 구워 주겠다고 했다지? 그런 게 사령관이라니!\""]], [["“Do you suppose Fedallah wants to kidnap Captain Ahab?”", "\"페달라가 에이해브(Ahab) 선장을 납치하려 든다고 생각하나?\""]], [["“Do I suppose it?", "\"내가 그렇게 생각하느냐고? 머지않아 자네도 알게 될 거야, 플래스크."], ["You’ll know it before long, Flask.", "어쨌든 난 지금부터 그놈을 날카롭게 지켜볼 참이야. 그래서 뭔가 아주 수상쩍은 짓을 하는 게 보이면, 그놈 목덜미를 덥석 붙잡고 이렇게 말해 줄 테야.—이봐, 베엘제붑, 그 짓은 안 돼."], ["But I am going now to keep a sharp look-out on him; and if I see anything very suspicious going on, I’ll just take him by the nape of his neck, and say—Look here, Beelzebub, you don’t do it; and if he makes any fuss, by the Lord I’ll make a grab into his pocket for his tail, take it to the capstan, and give him such a wrenching and heaving, that his tail will come short off at the stump—do you see; and then, I rather guess when he finds himself docked in that queer fashion, he’ll sneak off without the poor satisfaction of feeling his tail between his legs.”", "그런데도 그놈이 무슨 소란이라도 피우면, 맹세코, 그놈 주머니에 손을 쑥 집어넣어 꼬리를 끄집어내서는 캡스턴에다 감고 어찌나 비틀고 잡아당기는지, 그 꼬리가 밑동에서 댕강 끊어져 버리게 만들 거야—알겠어. 그러고 나면, 그놈도 자기가 그렇게 묘한 꼴로 꼬리가 잘린 걸 알아채고 나면, 가랑이 사이에 꼬리를 끼고 풀이 죽는 그 변변찮은 만족감조차 못 누린 채 슬그머니 꽁무니를 뺄 거라고, 내 짐작엔.\""]], [["“And what will you do with the tail, Stubb?”", "\"그럼 그 꼬리를 가지고 뭘 할 건데, 스터브?\""]], [["“Do with it?", "\"꼬리로 뭘 하느냐고?"], ["Sell it for an ox whip when we get home;—what else?”", "집에 돌아가면 소몰이 채찍으로 팔아야지.—그것 말고 뭐가 있겠나?\""]], [["“Now, do you mean what you say, and have been saying all along, Stubb?”", "\"이봐, 자네 지금 한 말, 아니 여태껏 줄곧 떠들어 댄 그 말들이 진심이야, 스터브?\""]], [["“Mean or not mean, here we are at the ship.”", "\"진심이든 아니든, 자, 다 왔네, 배에 도착했어.\""]], [["The boats were here hailed, to tow the whale on the larboard side, where fluke chains and other necessaries were already prepared for securing him.", "여기서 보트들을 향해 고래를 좌현 쪽으로 끌고 오라는 외침이 들려왔으니, 그쪽에는 이미 고래를 단단히 매어 둘 꼬리 사슬과 그 밖에 필요한 것들이 마련되어 있었다."]], [["“Didn’t I tell you so?”", "\"내가 그렇게 말하지 않았나?\""], ["said Flask; “yes, you’ll soon see this right whale’s head hoisted up opposite that parmacetti’s.”", "플래스크가 말했다. \"암, 곧 보게 될 거야, 이 참고래 머리가 저 향유고래 머리 맞은편에 매달려 올라가는 걸.\""]], [["In good time, Flask’s saying proved true.", "머지않아 플래스크의 말이 옳았음이 드러났다."], ["As before, the Pequod steeply leaned over towards the sperm whale’s head, now, by the counterpoise of both heads, she regained her even keel; though sorely strained, you may well believe.", "앞서 페쿼드호는 향유고래 머리 쪽으로 가파르게 기울었으나, 이제 두 머리가 서로 균형을 이룬 덕분에 다시 수평을 되찾았다. 물론 몹시 무리한 부담을 진 채였다는 것은 충분히 짐작할 만한 일이다."], ["So, when on one side you hoist in Locke’s head, you go over that way; but now, on the other side, hoist in Kant’s and you come back again; but in very poor plight.", "그러니까 한쪽에 로크(Locke)의 머리를 매달아 올리면 그쪽으로 기울지만, 이제 다른 쪽에 칸트(Kant)의 머리를 매달면 다시 제자리로 돌아오는 셈이다. 다만 그 꼴은 영 형편없다."], ["Thus, some minds for ever keep trimming boat.", "이렇듯 어떤 정신들은 영영 배의 균형이나 잡으며 살아간다."], ["Oh, ye foolish!", "오, 어리석은 자들이여!"], ["throw all these thunder-heads overboard, and then you will float light and right.", "이 골치 아픈 머리통들을 죄다 배 밖으로 내던져 버려라. 그러면 비로소 가뿐하고 똑바르게 떠다닐 수 있으리니."]], [["In disposing of the body of a right whale, when brought alongside the ship, the same preliminary proceedings commonly take place as in the case of a sperm whale; only, in the latter instance, the head is cut off whole, but in the former the lips and tongue are separately removed and hoisted on deck, with all the well known black bone attached to what is called the crown-piece.", "참고래의 몸뚱이를 처리하는 일은, 그것을 뱃전에 끌어왔을 때, 향유고래의 경우와 대체로 똑같은 예비 절차를 밟는다. 다만 향유고래의 경우엔 머리를 통째로 잘라 내지만, 참고래의 경우엔 입술과 혀를 따로 떼어 내어, 이른바 크라운피스(crown-piece)에 붙은 그 유명한 검은 뼈(고래수염)와 함께 갑판 위로 끌어올린다."], ["But nothing like this, in the present case, had been done.", "그러나 이번 경우엔 이런 일은 아무것도 행해지지 않았다."], ["The carcases of both whales had dropped astern; and the head-laden ship not a little resembled a mule carrying a pair of overburdening panniers.", "두 고래의 몸뚱이는 고물 뒤로 늘어뜨려진 채였고, 머리를 잔뜩 짊어진 배는 너무 무거운 짐바구니 한 쌍을 짊어진 노새와 적잖이 닮아 보였다."]], [["Meantime, Fedallah was calmly eyeing the right whale’s head, and ever and anon glancing from the deep wrinkles there to the lines in his own hand.", "그러는 동안 페달라는 참고래의 머리를 차분히 바라보면서, 이따금 거기 깊이 팬 주름살로부터 제 손바닥의 손금으로 시선을 옮기곤 했다."], ["And Ahab chanced so to stand, that the Parsee occupied his shadow; while, if the Parsee’s shadow was there at all it seemed only to blend with, and lengthen Ahab’s.", "그리고 에이해브는 우연히도 파르시(Parsee)가 그의 그림자 안에 들어서는 위치에 서 있었으니, 설령 그 파르시의 그림자가 거기 있다 한들 그것은 에이해브의 그림자에 녹아들어 그것을 길게 늘이는 것처럼 보일 따름이었다."], ["As the crew toiled on, Laplandish speculations were bandied among them, concerning all these passing things.", "선원들이 부지런히 일하는 동안, 그들 사이에서는 이렇게 스쳐 가는 온갖 일들을 두고 라플란드 사람들이나 할 법한 황당한 억측이 오갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 74. The Sperm Whale’s Head—Contrasted View.", "# CHAPTER 74. The Sperm Whale’s Head—Contrasted View."]], [["Here, now, are two great whales, laying their heads together; let us join them, and lay together our own.", "향유고래의 머리—대조적 관찰. 여기, 지금 두 마리의 거대한 고래가 머리를 맞대고 누워 있다. 우리도 그들과 한데 어울려, 우리 자신의 머리를 함께 맞대 보자."]], [["Of the grand order of folio leviathans, the Sperm Whale and the Right Whale are by far the most noteworthy.", "이절판 리바이어던(Leviathan)이라는 저 웅대한 목(目) 가운데, 향유고래(Sperm Whale)와 참고래(Right Whale)는 단연 가장 주목할 만한 존재다."], ["They are the only whales regularly hunted by man.", "이 둘은 인간이 정기적으로 사냥하는 유일한 고래다."], ["To the Nantucketer, they present the two extremes of all the known varieties of the whale.", "낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람에게 이 둘은 알려진 모든 고래 변종 가운데 양극단을 이룬다."], ["As the external difference between them is mainly observable in their heads; and as a head of each is this moment hanging from the Pequod’s side; and as we may freely go from one to the other, by merely stepping across the deck:—where, I should like to know, will you obtain a better chance to study practical cetology than here?", "둘 사이의 외형적 차이는 주로 머리에서 관찰되며, 마침 지금 이 순간 그 머리들이 각각 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 뱃전에 매달려 있고, 또한 우리는 갑판을 가로질러 걸음을 옮기는 것만으로 한쪽에서 다른 쪽으로 자유로이 오갈 수 있으니—묻건대, 실용 고래학을 연구하기에 여기보다 더 좋은 기회를 그대는 어디서 얻겠는가?"]], [["In the first place, you are struck by the general contrast between these heads.", "우선 그대는 이 두 머리 사이의 전반적인 대조에 충격을 받는다."], ["Both are massive enough in all conscience; but there is a certain mathematical symmetry in the Sperm Whale’s which the Right Whale’s sadly lacks.", "양쪽 다 충분히 거대하다는 점은 양심에 비추어 봐도 틀림없다. 그러나 향유고래의 머리에는 일종의 수학적 균형미가 있는데, 참고래의 머리에는 안타깝게도 그것이 결여되어 있다."], ["There is more character in the Sperm Whale’s head.", "향유고래의 머리에는 더 많은 개성이 깃들어 있다."], ["As you behold it, you involuntarily yield the immense superiority to him, in point of pervading dignity.", "그것을 바라보노라면, 그대는 두루 배어 있는 위엄이라는 점에서 그에게 압도적인 우월함을 저도 모르게 인정하게 된다."], ["In the present instance, too, this dignity is heightened by the pepper and salt colour of his head at the summit, giving token of advanced age and large experience.", "게다가 지금 이 경우에는, 그 위엄이 정수리 부분 머리의 후추와 소금을 뿌린 듯한 빛깔로 한층 돋보이는데, 이는 노년에 이르렀음과 풍부한 경험을 말해 주는 표지다."], ["In short, he is what the fishermen technically call a “grey-headed whale.”", "요컨대 그는 어부들이 전문 용어로 \"백발 고래\"라 부르는 바로 그것이다."]], [["Let us now note what is least dissimilar in these heads—namely, the two most important organs, the eye and the ear.", "이제 이 두 머리에서 가장 덜 다른 점—곧 가장 중요한 두 기관인 눈과 귀를 살펴보자."], ["Far back on the side of the head, and low down, near the angle of either whale’s jaw, if you narrowly search, you will at last see a lashless eye, which you would fancy to be a young colt’s eye; so out of all proportion is it to the magnitude of the head.", "머리의 옆면 한참 뒤쪽, 아래로 내려가 양쪽 고래의 턱 모서리 근처를 자세히 뒤지면, 마침내 속눈썹 없는 눈 하나를 보게 되는데, 그대는 그것을 어린 망아지의 눈으로 여길지도 모른다. 머리의 거대함에 견주어 그토록 어울리지 않게 작은 까닭이다."]], [["Now, from this peculiar sideway position of the whale’s eyes, it is plain that he can never see an object which is exactly ahead, no more than he can one exactly astern.", "자, 고래 눈의 이 독특한 측면 위치로 미루어 보건대, 그가 정확히 앞에 있는 물체를 결코 볼 수 없으며, 정확히 뒤에 있는 물체 또한 볼 수 없음이 분명하다."], ["In a word, the position of the whale’s eyes corresponds to that of a man’s ears; and you may fancy, for yourself, how it would fare with you, did you sideways survey objects through your ears.", "한마디로 고래 눈의 위치는 사람 귀의 위치에 해당하니, 그대 스스로 한번 상상해 보라, 만약 그대가 귀를 통해 물체를 옆으로 둘러본다면 어떤 처지가 될지를."], ["You would find that you could only command some thirty degrees of vision in advance of the straight side-line of sight; and about thirty more behind it.", "그대는 똑바른 측면 시선의 앞쪽으로 겨우 삼십 도 남짓, 그리고 그 뒤쪽으로 다시 삼십 도쯤만 볼 수 있음을 알게 될 것이다."], ["If your bitterest foe were walking straight towards you, with dagger uplifted in broad day, you would not be able to see him, any more than if he were stealing upon you from behind.", "만약 그대의 가장 모진 원수가 대낮에 단검을 치켜든 채 그대를 향해 똑바로 걸어온다 해도, 그대는 그가 등 뒤에서 몰래 다가올 때와 마찬가지로 그를 볼 수 없을 것이다. 한마디로 그대는, 말하자면 두 개의 등을 갖게 되는 셈이다."], ["In a word, you would have two backs, so to speak; but, at the same time, also, two fronts (side fronts): for what is it that makes the front of a man—what, indeed, but his eyes?", "그러나 동시에 두 개의 앞면(측면 앞면)도 갖게 된다. 사람의 앞면을 이루는 것이 무엇이겠는가—참으로 그의 눈이 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"]], [["Moreover, while in most other animals that I can now think of, the eyes are so planted as imperceptibly to blend their visual power, so as to produce one picture and not two to the brain; the peculiar position of the whale’s eyes, effectually divided as they are by many cubic feet of solid head, which towers between them like a great mountain separating two lakes in valleys; this, of course, must wholly separate the impressions which each independent organ imparts.", "더욱이 내가 지금 떠올릴 수 있는 대부분의 다른 동물들은 두 눈이 그 시력(視力)을 알게 모르게 하나로 융합하도록 자리 잡아, 뇌에 두 개가 아닌 하나의 그림을 만들어 낸다. 그러나 고래 눈의 독특한 위치는, 두 골짜기의 호수를 가르는 큰 산처럼 그 사이에 우뚝 솟은 수 세제곱피트에 달하는 단단한 머리로 효과적으로 갈라져 있으니, 이것은 당연히 각각의 독립된 기관이 전하는 인상을 완전히 분리할 수밖에 없다."], ["The whale, therefore, must see one distinct picture on this side, and another distinct picture on that side; while all between must be profound darkness and nothingness to him.", "따라서 고래는 이쪽에서 하나의 또렷한 그림을 보고, 저쪽에서 또 다른 또렷한 그림을 보아야 하며, 그 사이의 모든 것은 그에게 깊은 어둠이요 허무일 것이다."], ["Man may, in effect, be said to look out on the world from a sentry-box with two joined sashes for his window.", "사람은 사실상 두 짝이 맞붙은 창문이 달린 보초막에서 세상을 내다본다고 말할 수 있다."], ["But with the whale, these two sashes are separately inserted, making two distinct windows, but sadly impairing the view.", "그러나 고래의 경우 이 두 창짝은 따로따로 끼워져 두 개의 별개 창문을 이루지만, 안타깝게도 시야를 망가뜨린다."], ["This peculiarity of the whale’s eyes is a thing always to be borne in mind in the fishery; and to be remembered by the reader in some subsequent scenes.", "고래 눈의 이 특이성은 고래잡이에서 늘 마음에 새겨 두어야 할 것이며, 독자도 뒤에 나올 몇몇 장면에서 기억해 둘 일이다."]], [["A curious and most puzzling question might be started concerning this visual matter as touching the Leviathan.", "이 시각의 문제를 두고 리바이어던과 관련하여 기이하고도 더없이 까다로운 물음 하나를 제기해 볼 수 있다. 그러나 나는 한 가지 암시로 만족해야겠다."], ["But I must be content with a hint.", "사람의 눈이 빛 속에서 떠 있는 한, 본다는 행위는 비자발적이다."], ["So long as a man’s eyes are open in the light, the act of seeing is involuntary; that is, he cannot then help mechanically seeing whatever objects are before him.", "곧 그는 그때 자기 앞에 있는 어떤 물체든 기계적으로 보지 않을 수 없다. 그럼에도 누구든 자신의 경험으로 이렇게 배우게 된다."], ["Nevertheless, any one’s experience will teach him, that though he can take in an undiscriminating sweep of things at one glance, it is quite impossible for him, attentively, and completely, to examine any two things—however large or however small—at one and the same instant of time; never mind if they lie side by side and touch each other.", "곧 한 번의 눈길로 사물을 분간 없이 휩쓸어 받아들일 수는 있어도, 어떤 두 가지를—그것이 아무리 크든 작든—같은 한순간에 주의 깊게, 그리고 온전히 살피기란 도무지 불가능하다는 것을. 설령 그 둘이 나란히 놓여 서로 맞닿아 있다 해도 마찬가지다."], ["But if you now come to separate these two objects, and surround each by a circle of profound darkness; then, in order to see one of them, in such a manner as to bring your mind to bear on it, the other will be utterly excluded from your contemporary consciousness.", "그러나 이제 그 두 물체를 떼어 놓고, 각각을 깊은 어둠의 고리로 에워싸 보라. 그러면 둘 중 하나를 마음을 기울여 살피려 하는 순간, 다른 하나는 그대의 동시적 의식에서 완전히 배제되고 만다."], ["How is it, then, with the whale?", "그렇다면 고래의 경우는 어떠한가?"], ["True, both his eyes, in themselves, must simultaneously act; but is his brain so much more comprehensive, combining, and subtle than man’s, that he can at the same moment of time attentively examine two distinct prospects, one on one side of him, and the other in an exactly opposite direction?", "과연 그의 두 눈은 그 자체로 동시에 작동해야 한다. 그러나 그의 뇌는 사람의 뇌보다 훨씬 더 포괄적이고, 종합적이며, 정교해서, 같은 한순간에 두 개의 별개 전망을—하나는 한쪽에서, 다른 하나는 정확히 반대 방향에서—주의 깊게 살필 수 있단 말인가?"], ["If he can, then is it as marvellous a thing in him, as if a man were able simultaneously to go through the demonstrations of two distinct problems in Euclid.", "만약 그럴 수 있다면, 그것은 한 사람이 유클리드(Euclid)의 두 별개 문제 증명을 동시에 해내는 것만큼이나 그에게 경이로운 일이다."], ["Nor, strictly investigated, is there any incongruity in this comparison.", "더구나 엄밀히 따져 보면, 이 비유에는 아무런 부조화도 없다."]], [["It may be but an idle whim, but it has always seemed to me, that the extraordinary vacillations of movement displayed by some whales when beset by three or four boats; the timidity and liability to queer frights, so common to such whales; I think that all this indirectly proceeds from the helpless perplexity of volition, in which their divided and diametrically opposite powers of vision must involve them.", "이는 한낱 부질없는 공상일지도 모르나, 내게는 늘 이렇게 여겨졌다. 곧 어떤 고래들이 서너 척의 보트에 에워싸였을 때 보이는 그 유별난 움직임의 동요, 그런 고래들에게 그토록 흔한 겁먹음과 괴상한 공포에 빠지기 쉬운 성질—이 모든 것이 갈라져 정반대로 향한 그들의 시력이 그들을 빠뜨릴 수밖에 없는 그 무력한 의지의 혼란에서 간접적으로 비롯된다고 나는 생각한다."]], [["But the ear of the whale is full as curious as the eye.", "그러나 고래의 귀는 눈에 못지않게 기이하다."], ["If you are an entire stranger to their race, you might hunt over these two heads for hours, and never discover that organ.", "만약 그대가 그들 종족에게 전혀 낯선 사람이라면, 이 두 머리를 몇 시간이고 뒤진다 해도 그 기관을 결코 찾아내지 못할 것이다."], ["The ear has no external leaf whatever; and into the hole itself you can hardly insert a quill, so wondrously minute is it.", "귀에는 바깥으로 드러난 귓바퀴가 전혀 없으며, 그 구멍 자체에도 깃펜 하나 끼워 넣기 어려울 만큼, 그토록 놀랍도록 미세하다."], ["It is lodged a little behind the eye.", "그것은 눈보다 조금 뒤쪽에 자리 잡고 있다."], ["With respect to their ears, this important difference is to be observed between the sperm whale and the right.", "그들의 귀에 관해서는, 향유고래와 참고래 사이에 다음과 같은 중요한 차이를 눈여겨보아야 한다."], ["While the ear of the former has an external opening, that of the latter is entirely and evenly covered over with a membrane, so as to be quite imperceptible from without.", "전자의 귀에는 바깥으로 트인 구멍이 있지만, 후자의 귀는 막으로 완전히, 그리고 고르게 덮여 있어, 바깥에서는 도무지 알아챌 수 없다."]], [["Is it not curious, that so vast a being as the whale should see the world through so small an eye, and hear the thunder through an ear which is smaller than a hare’s?", "기이하지 아니한가, 고래처럼 그토록 거대한 존재가 그토록 작은 눈으로 세상을 보고, 산토끼의 것보다도 작은 귀로 천둥소리를 듣는다는 것이?"], ["But if his eyes were broad as the lens of Herschel’s great telescope; and his ears capacious as the porches of cathedrals; would that make him any longer of sight, or sharper of hearing?", "그러나 만약 그의 눈이 허셜(Herschel)의 거대한 망원경 렌즈만큼 넓고, 그의 귀가 대성당 현관만큼 널찍하다 한들, 그것이 그를 더 멀리 보게 하거나 더 예민하게 듣게 만들겠는가?"], ["Not at all.—Why then do you try to “enlarge”", "결코 아니다.—그렇다면 그대는 어찌하여 그대의 정신을 \"넓히려\" 애쓰는가?"], ["your mind? Subtilize it.", "정신은 정교하게 다듬을 일이다."]], [["Let us now with whatever levers and steam-engines we have at hand, cant over the sperm whale’s head, that it may lie bottom up; then, ascending by a ladder to the summit, have a peep down the mouth; and were it not that the body is now completely separated from it, with a lantern we might descend into the great Kentucky Mammoth Cave of his stomach.", "이제 손닿는 데 있는 온갖 지레와 증기기관을 써서, 향유고래의 머리를 뒤집어 바닥이 위로 오도록 모로 세워 보자. 그런 다음 사다리를 타고 정수리로 올라가 입속을 들여다보자."], ["But let us hold on here by this tooth, and look about us where we are.", "만약 지금 몸뚱이가 그것으로부터 완전히 떨어져 나가지 않았더라면, 등불 하나를 들고 그의 위장이라는 저 거대한 켄터키 매머드 동굴 속으로 내려가 볼 수도 있었으리라."], ["What a really beautiful and chaste-looking mouth!", "그러나 여기 이 이빨을 붙잡고 매달려, 우리가 있는 곳 주위를 둘러보자."], ["from floor to ceiling, lined, or rather papered with a glistening white membrane, glossy as bridal satins.", "참으로 아름답고 정결해 보이는 입이로다! 바닥에서 천장까지, 반짝이는 흰 막으로 안을 댔으니, 아니 차라리 도배했다고 해야겠다, 신부의 새틴처럼 윤이 나는 그 막으로."]], [["But come out now, and look at this portentous lower jaw, which seems like the long narrow lid of an immense snuff-box, with the hinge at one end, instead of one side.", "그러나 이제 밖으로 나와서, 이 불길해 보이는 아래턱을 보라. 한쪽 옆이 아니라 한쪽 끝에 경첩이 달린, 거대한 코담배갑의 길고 좁은 뚜껑 같지 않은가."], ["If you pry it up, so as to get it overhead, and expose its rows of teeth, it seems a terrific portcullis; and such, alas!", "그것을 비집어 들어 올려 머리 위로 들어 올리고 그 이빨 줄을 드러내면, 무시무시한 내리닫이 쇠살문처럼 보인다. 그리고 슬프게도, 그것은 고래잡이에서 수많은 가련한 사내들에게 정녕 그런 것임이 드러나는데, 그 쇠창살 같은 이빨이 그들에게 꿰뚫을 듯한 힘으로 떨어지는 것이다."], ["it proves to many a poor wight in the fishery, upon whom these spikes fall with impaling force.", "그러나 한층 더 끔찍한 광경은, 바닷속 수십 길 아래에서 어떤 시무룩한 고래가 거기 떠서 매달린 채, 십오 피트(약 4.5미터)쯤 되는 그 어마어마한 턱을 제 몸과 직각을 이루며 곧장 아래로 늘어뜨리고 있는 모습을 보는 것이니, 그 모양이 영락없이 배의 지브붐 같다. 이 고래는 죽은 것이 아니다."], ["But far more terrible is it to behold, when fathoms down in the sea, you see some sulky whale, floating there suspended, with his prodigious jaw, some fifteen feet long, hanging straight down at right-angles with his body, for all the world like a ship’s jib-boom.", "다만 기운이 빠졌을 뿐이다. 어쩌면 심기가 불편한 것이리라."], ["This whale is not dead; he is only dispirited; out of sorts, perhaps; hypochondriac; and so supine, that the hinges of his jaw have relaxed, leaving him there in that ungainly sort of plight, a reproach to all his tribe, who must, no doubt, imprecate lock-jaws upon him.", "우울증에 걸린 것이리라. 그리고 어찌나 늘어졌는지 턱의 경첩이 풀려 버려, 거기 그렇게 볼썽사나운 꼴로 매달려 있으니, 그 종족 모두에게 수치가 되어, 그들은 필시 그에게 턱이 굳어 버리는 저주를 퍼붓고 있을 것이다."]], [["In most cases this lower jaw—being easily unhinged by a practised artist—is disengaged and hoisted on deck for the purpose of extracting the ivory teeth, and furnishing a supply of that hard white whalebone with which the fishermen fashion all sorts of curious articles, including canes, umbrella-stocks, and handles to riding-whips.", "대개의 경우 이 아래턱은—노련한 솜씨꾼이라면 쉽게 경첩을 풀어내는데—몸에서 떼어 내어 갑판 위로 끌어 올린다. 상아질 이빨을 뽑아내고, 어부들이 지팡이며 우산 자루며 승마용 채찍 손잡이를 비롯한 온갖 진기한 물건을 만드는 데 쓰는 저 단단한 흰 고래뼈를 마련하기 위함이다."]], [["With a long, weary hoist the jaw is dragged on board, as if it were an anchor; and when the proper time comes—some few days after the other work—Queequeg, Daggoo, and Tashtego, being all accomplished dentists, are set to drawing teeth.", "길고도 고된 끌어 올리기 끝에, 턱은 마치 닻이라도 되는 양 배 위로 끌려 올라온다. 그리고 적당한 때가 오면—다른 작업을 끝낸 며칠 뒤—저마다 능숙한 치과의사인 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 다구(Daggoo)와 타슈테고(Tashtego)가 이빨을 뽑는 일에 투입된다."], ["With a keen cutting-spade, Queequeg lances the gums; then the jaw is lashed down to ringbolts, and a tackle being rigged from aloft, they drag out these teeth, as Michigan oxen drag stumps of old oaks out of wild wood lands.", "퀴퀘그가 날카로운 절단용 삽으로 잇몸을 가르면, 그다음 턱을 고리볼트에 단단히 동여매고, 위에서 도르래 장치를 매달아, 마치 미시간의 황소들이 거친 숲의 땅에서 늙은 떡갈나무 그루터기를 뽑아내듯 이 이빨들을 끌어낸다."], ["There are generally forty-two teeth in all; in old whales, much worn down, but undecayed; nor filled after our artificial fashion.", "이빨은 대개 모두 마흔두 개인데, 늙은 고래의 것은 많이 닳았어도 썩지는 않았고, 우리 인간이 인공적으로 하듯 때워 넣은 것도 아니다."], ["The jaw is afterwards sawn into slabs, and piled away like joists for building houses.", "그 뒤 턱은 톱으로 켜서 판자로 만든 다음, 집 지을 때 쓰는 들보처럼 차곡차곡 쌓아 둔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 75. The Right Whale’s Head—Contrasted View.", "# CHAPTER 75. The Right Whale’s Head—Contrasted View."]], [["Crossing the deck, let us now have a good long look at the Right Whale’s head.", "갑판을 가로질러 가서, 이제 참고래의 머리를 한참 동안 찬찬히 들여다보기로 하자."]], [["As in general shape the noble Sperm Whale’s head may be compared to a Roman war-chariot (especially in front, where it is so broadly rounded); so, at a broad view, the Right Whale’s head bears a rather inelegant resemblance to a gigantic galliot-toed shoe.", "전체적인 형태로 볼 때 저 고귀한 향유고래의 머리가 로마의 전차(특히 폭넓게 둥글려진 앞부분이 그러하다)에 견줄 만하다면, 참고래의 머리는 한 발짝 물러나 넓게 바라볼 때 거대한 뱃머리 모양의 신발과 다소 볼품없는 닮은꼴을 이룬다."], ["Two hundred years ago an old Dutch voyager likened its shape to that of a shoemaker’s last.", "이백 년 전, 어느 늙은 네덜란드 항해자는 그 모양을 구두장이의 골에 빗대었다."], ["And in this same last or shoe, that old woman of the nursery tale, with the swarming brood, might very comfortably be lodged, she and all her progeny.", "그리고 바로 그 골, 곧 그 신발 속에라면, 자식들이 우글거리던 옛이야기 속의 그 노파가 제 모든 자손과 더불어 아주 편안히 머무를 수 있을 것이다."]], [["But as you come nearer to this great head it begins to assume different aspects, according to your point of view.", "그러나 이 거대한 머리에 가까이 다가설수록, 그것은 보는 각도에 따라 저마다 다른 모습을 띠기 시작한다."], ["If you stand on its summit and look at these two F-shaped spoutholes, you would take the whole head for an enormous bass-viol, and these spiracles, the apertures in its sounding-board.", "그 꼭대기에 올라서서 이 F자 모양의 두 분수공을 들여다본다면, 그대는 그 머리 전체를 거대한 콘트라베이스로, 그리고 이 숨구멍들을 그 공명판에 뚫린 구멍으로 여길 것이다."], ["Then, again, if you fix your eye upon this strange, crested, comb-like incrustation on the top of the mass—this green, barnacled thing, which the Greenlanders call the “crown,” and the Southern fishers the “bonnet”", "또 다시, 만약 그대가 이 덩어리의 꼭대기에 얹힌 저 기묘하고 볏처럼 솟은 빗 모양의 딱지에 — 그린란드 사람들이 참고래의 \"왕관\"이라 부르고 남쪽 어부들이 \"보닛\"이라 부르는, 따개비가 들러붙은 이 푸르스름한 것에 — 오직 시선을 고정한다면, 그대는 그 머리를 가랑이에 새 둥지를 품은 어느 거대한 떡갈나무의 둥치로 여길 것이다."], ["of the Right Whale; fixing your eyes solely on this, you would take the head for the trunk of some huge oak, with a bird’s nest in its crotch.", "적어도 이 보닛 위에 둥지를 틀고 사는 저 살아 있는 게들을 지켜본다면, 그러한 생각이 거의 틀림없이 떠오를 것이다."], ["At any rate, when you watch those live crabs that nestle here on this bonnet, such an idea will be almost sure to occur to you; unless, indeed, your fancy has been fixed by the technical term “crown”", "다만, 그것에게 함께 붙여진 \"왕관\"이라는 전문 용어에 그대의 상상력이 사로잡혀 있지 않다면 말이다."], ["also bestowed upon it; in which case you will take great interest in thinking how this mighty monster is actually a diademed king of the sea, whose green crown has been put together for him in this marvellous manner.", "그럴 경우 그대는, 이 강대한 괴물이 실은 바다의 왕관을 쓴 임금이며, 그의 푸른 왕관이 이토록 경이로운 방식으로 그를 위해 엮여졌다는 사실을 떠올리며 깊은 흥미를 느끼게 될 것이다."], ["But if this whale be a king, he is a very sulky looking fellow to grace a diadem.", "그러나 이 고래가 왕이라면, 왕관을 빛내기에는 몹시 뚱한 얼굴을 한 작자다."], ["Look at that hanging lower lip!", "저 축 늘어진 아랫입술을 보라!"], ["what a huge sulk and pout is there!", "거기 어찌나 큰 부루퉁함과 뾰로통함이 서려 있는지!"], ["a sulk and pout, by carpenter’s measurement, about twenty feet long and five feet deep; a sulk and pout that will yield you some 500 gallons of oil and more.", "목수의 자로 재자면 길이 약 이십 피트(약 6미터), 깊이 약 오 피트(약 1.5미터)에 이르는 부루퉁함과 뾰로통함, 그대에게 기름을 오백 갤런이나 그 이상 내어 줄 부루퉁함과 뾰로통함이다."]], [["A great pity, now, that this unfortunate whale should be hare-lipped.", "그런데 이 가련한 고래가 언청이라는 것은 참으로 안타까운 일이다."], ["The fissure is about a foot across.", "그 틈은 폭이 약 일 피트(약 30센티미터)나 된다."], ["Probably the mother during an important interval was sailing down the Peruvian coast, when earthquakes caused the beach to gape.", "아마도 어미가 어느 중대한 시기에 페루 해안을 따라 헤엄쳐 내려가던 차에, 지진이 일어나 해변이 쩍 벌어졌던 모양이다."], ["Over this lip, as over a slippery threshold, we now slide into the mouth.", "미끄러운 문턱을 넘듯 이 입술을 넘어, 우리는 이제 입속으로 미끄러져 들어간다."], ["Upon my word were I at Mackinaw, I should take this to be the inside of an Indian wigwam.", "정말이지 내가 매키노에 있었다면, 이곳을 인디언의 천막 안이라고 여겼을 것이다. 맙소사!"], ["Good Lord! is this the road that Jonah went?", "이것이 요나(Jonah)가 지나간 그 길이란 말인가?"], ["The roof is about twelve feet high, and runs to a pretty sharp angle, as if there were a regular ridge-pole there; while these ribbed, arched, hairy sides, present us with those wondrous, half vertical, scimetar-shaped slats of whalebone, say three hundred on a side, which depending from the upper part of the head or crown bone, form those Venetian blinds which have elsewhere been cursorily mentioned.", "천장은 높이가 약 십이 피트(약 3.6미터)에 이르고, 마치 거기에 번듯한 마룻대라도 걸린 듯 제법 날카로운 각으로 솟아오른다. 한편 갈빗대처럼 늘어서고 아치형으로 굽었으며 털이 난 이 양쪽 옆면은, 한쪽에 삼백 개쯤 되는 저 경이롭고 거의 수직으로 늘어진 언월도 모양의 고래수염 살들을 우리에게 보여 주는데, 이것들은 머리 윗부분 곧 왕관뼈에서 늘어져 내려, 다른 곳에서 잠깐 언급한 바 있는 저 베네치아식 발(簾)을 이룬다."], ["The edges of these bones are fringed with hairy fibres, through which the Right Whale strains the water, and in whose intricacies he retains the small fish, when openmouthed he goes through the seas of brit in feeding time.", "이 뼈들의 가장자리에는 털 같은 섬유가 술처럼 달려 있어, 참고래는 이를 통해 물을 걸러 내고, 먹이를 먹을 때 입을 벌린 채 브릿이 가득한 바다를 헤치며 나아갈 적에 그 얽힌 섬유 속에 작은 물고기들을 가두어 둔다."], ["In the central blinds of bone, as they stand in their natural order, there are certain curious marks, curves, hollows, and ridges, whereby some whalemen calculate the creature’s age, as the age of an oak by its circular rings.", "자연스러운 차례대로 늘어선 한가운데 뼈 발에는 어떤 기묘한 무늬와 곡선, 우묵한 곳과 융기가 새겨져 있는데, 더러 어떤 고래잡이들은 떡갈나무의 나이를 그 둥근 나이테로 헤아리듯, 이로써 이 짐승의 나이를 헤아린다."], ["Though the certainty of this criterion is far from demonstrable, yet it has the savor of analogical probability.", "이 기준의 확실성이 입증과는 거리가 멀지만, 그래도 유추에 따른 개연성의 맛은 풍긴다."], ["At any rate, if we yield to it, we must grant a far greater age to the Right Whale than at first glance will seem reasonable.", "어쨌든 우리가 이를 받아들인다면, 참고래에게는 첫눈에 그럴듯해 보이는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 나이를 인정해 주어야 할 것이다."]], [["In old times, there seem to have prevailed the most curious fancies concerning these blinds. One voyager in Purchas calls them the wondrous “whiskers”", "옛날에는 이 발들을 두고 가장 기이한 상상들이 떠돌았던 모양이다."], ["inside of the whale’s mouth;* another, “hogs’ bristles”; a third old gentleman in Hackluyt uses the following elegant language: “There are about two hundred and fifty fins growing on each side of his upper chop, which arch over his tongue on each side of his mouth.”", "퍼처스에 실린 어느 항해자는 그것들을 고래 입속의 경이로운 \"구레나룻\"이라 부르고, 다른 이는 \"돼지의 뻣뻣한 털\"이라 부르며, 해클루트에 실린 세 번째 노신사는 다음과 같은 우아한 표현을 쓴다. \"그의 윗턱 양쪽에는 이백오십 개쯤 되는 지느러미가 돋아 있어, 입 양편에서 혀 위로 아치를 이루고 있다.\""]], [["*This reminds us that the Right Whale really has a sort of whisker, or rather a moustache, consisting of a few scattered white hairs on the upper part of the outer end of the lower jaw.", "* 이는 우리에게, 참고래가 실제로 일종의 구레나룻, 아니 차라리 콧수염을 지니고 있음을 일깨워 준다. 그것은 아래턱 바깥쪽 끝의 윗부분에 드문드문 난 몇 가닥의 흰 털로 이루어져 있다."], ["Sometimes these tufts impart a rather brigandish expression to his otherwise solemn countenance.", "더러 이 털 뭉치는 그 본래 근엄한 얼굴에 다소 산적 같은 표정을 부여하기도 한다."]], [["As every one knows, these same “hogs’ bristles,” “fins,”", "누구나 알다시피, 바로 이 \"돼지의 뻣뻣한 털\", \"지느러미\", \"구레나룻\", \"발\", 혹은 그대가 무어라 부르든 간에, 그것은 부인네들에게 코르셋 살대를 비롯한 여러 빳빳한 도구를 마련해 준다."], ["“whiskers,” “blinds,”", "그러나 이 특정한 쓰임에 대한 수요는 오래전부터 줄곧 내리막길을 걸어왔다."], ["or whatever you please, furnish to the ladies their busks and other stiffening contrivances.", "이 뼈가 영광을 누리던 때는 앤 여왕 시절로, 그때는 버팀살을 넣은 치마가 한창 유행이었다."], ["But in this particular, the demand has long been on the decline. It was in Queen Anne’s time that the bone was in its glory, the farthingale being then all the fashion.", "그리고 그 옛 부인네들이, 말하자면 고래의 턱 안에 든 채로도 흥겹게 거닐었듯이, 오늘날 우리 또한 소나기가 쏟아질 때면 그와 똑같은 무심함으로 바로 그 턱 아래로 몸을 피해 달려 들어간다."], ["And as those ancient dames moved about gaily, though in the jaws of the whale, as you may say; even so, in a shower, with the like thoughtlessness, do we nowadays fly under the same jaws for protection; the umbrella being a tent spread over the same bone.", "우산이란 바로 그 똑같은 뼈 위로 펼쳐진 천막인 것이다."]], [["But now forget all about blinds and whiskers for a moment, and, standing in the Right Whale’s mouth, look around you afresh.", "그러나 이제 발이며 구레나룻 따위는 잠시 다 잊고, 참고래의 입속에 서서 새삼 주위를 둘러보라."], ["Seeing all these colonnades of bone so methodically ranged about, would you not think you were inside of the great Haarlem organ, and gazing upon its thousand pipes?", "이토록 정연하게 늘어선 이 모든 뼈의 열주(列柱)들을 보고서, 그대는 자신이 저 거대한 하를럼 오르간 안에 들어와 그 수천 개의 파이프를 올려다보고 있다고 여기지 않겠는가?"], ["For a carpet to the organ we have a rug of the softest Turkey—the tongue, which is glued, as it were, to the floor of the mouth.", "오르간에 깔린 양탄자로는, 입바닥에 마치 풀로 붙인 듯 들러붙은 혀가 더없이 부드러운 터키산 융단처럼 깔려 있다."], ["It is very fat and tender, and apt to tear in pieces in hoisting it on deck.", "그것은 몹시 기름지고 연하여, 갑판 위로 끌어올릴 때 갈가리 찢어지기 일쑤다."], ["This particular tongue now before us; at a passing glance I should say it was a six-barreler; that is, it will yield you about that amount of oil.", "지금 우리 앞에 놓인 이 혀로 말하자면, 얼핏 보아도 여섯 통짜리라 하겠다. 곧, 그대에게 그만한 양의 기름을 내어 줄 것이라는 뜻이다."]], [["Ere this, you must have plainly seen the truth of what I started with—that the Sperm Whale and the Right Whale have almost entirely different heads.", "이쯤 되면, 그대는 내가 처음에 꺼낸 말의 진실을 — 향유고래와 참고래가 거의 완전히 다른 머리를 지녔다는 것을 — 분명히 알아보았을 것이다."], ["To sum up, then: in the Right Whale’s there is no great well of sperm; no ivory teeth at all; no long, slender mandible of a lower jaw, like the Sperm Whale’s.", "그러니 정리하자면 이렇다. 참고래의 머리에는 향유고래에게 있는 그 거대한 정유(精油)의 샘도 없고, 상아 같은 이빨도 전혀 없으며, 길고 가느다란 아래턱뼈도 없다."], ["Nor in the Sperm Whale are there any of those blinds of bone; no huge lower lip; and scarcely anything of a tongue.", "반대로 향유고래에게는 저 뼈로 된 발 따위가 하나도 없고, 거대한 아랫입술도 없으며, 혀라 할 만한 것도 거의 없다."], ["Again, the Right Whale has two external spout-holes, the Sperm Whale only one.", "또 참고래는 바깥으로 드러난 분수공이 두 개인데, 향유고래는 단 하나뿐이다."]], [["Look your last, now, on these venerable hooded heads, while they yet lie together; for one will soon sink, unrecorded, in the sea; the other will not be very long in following.", "이제 이 존엄한, 두건을 쓴 듯한 두 머리가 아직 나란히 놓여 있는 동안, 마지막으로 한 번 더 보아 두라. 머지않아 하나는 기록되지 못한 채 바다 속으로 가라앉을 것이며, 다른 하나도 그 뒤를 따르기까지 그리 오래 걸리지 않을 테니."]], [["Can you catch the expression of the Sperm Whale’s there?", "저기 향유고래의 표정을 알아볼 수 있겠는가?"], ["It is the same he died with, only some of the longer wrinkles in the forehead seem now faded away.", "그것은 그가 죽을 때 지었던 바로 그 표정이며, 다만 이마의 긴 주름살 몇 가닥이 지금은 흐릿하게 바래 가는 듯하다."], ["I think his broad brow to be full of a prairie-like placidity, born of a speculative indifference as to death.", "내 생각에 그 너른 이마에는 초원과도 같은 평온함이, 죽음에 대한 사색적 무관심에서 비롯된 평온함이 가득 차 있다."], ["But mark the other head’s expression.", "그러나 다른 머리의 표정을 눈여겨보라."], ["See that amazing lower lip, pressed by accident against the vessel’s side, so as firmly to embrace the jaw.", "우연히 배의 옆구리에 짓눌려, 그리하여 턱을 단단히 감싸 안은 저 놀라운 아랫입술을 보라."], ["Does not this whole head seem to speak of an enormous practical resolution in facing death?", "이 머리 전체가, 죽음을 마주하는 어떤 거대하고도 실제적인 결의를 말하고 있는 것 같지 않은가?"], ["This Right Whale I take to have been a Stoic; the Sperm Whale, a Platonian, who might have taken up Spinoza in his latter years.", "내가 보기에 이 참고래는 스토아주의자였고, 향유고래는 플라톤주의자였으니, 그는 만년에 스피노자라도 펼쳐 들었을 법한 자였다."]], [["# CHAPTER 76. The Battering-Ram.", "# CHAPTER 76. The Battering-Ram."]], [["Ere quitting, for the nonce, the Sperm Whale’s head, I would have you, as a sensible physiologist, simply—particularly remark its front aspect, in all its compacted collectedness.", "당분간 향유고래의 머리를 떠나기에 앞서, 나는 당신이 분별 있는 생리학자로서 그저—특히 그 정면의 모습을, 단단히 응축된 그 모든 짜임새 속에서 눈여겨보아 주기를 바란다."], ["I would have you investigate it now with the sole view of forming to yourself some unexaggerated, intelligent estimate of whatever battering-ram power may be lodged there.", "나는 당신이 지금 그것을 조사하되, 오로지 거기 깃들어 있을지도 모를 충각(衝角)의 힘이 어떤 것이든 그에 관해 과장되지 않은, 분별 있는 평가를 스스로 세우려는 목적만으로 그리해 주기를 바란다."], ["Here is a vital point; for you must either satisfactorily settle this matter with yourself, or for ever remain an infidel as to one of the most appalling, but not the less true events, perhaps anywhere to be found in all recorded history.", "이것은 매우 긴요한 대목이다. 당신은 이 문제를 스스로 만족스럽게 매듭짓든지, 아니면 어쩌면 기록된 역사 전체를 통틀어 어디서도 찾아보기 힘들, 가장 소름 끼치면서도 그럼에도 못지않게 참된 사건들 가운데 하나에 대해 영영 믿지 못하는 자로 남든지 해야 하기 때문이다."]], [["You observe that in the ordinary swimming position of the Sperm Whale, the front of his head presents an almost wholly vertical plane to the water; you observe that the lower part of that front slopes considerably backwards, so as to furnish more of a retreat for the long socket which receives the boom-like lower jaw; you observe that the mouth is entirely under the head, much in the same way, indeed, as though your own mouth were entirely under your chin.", "당신은 향유고래의 보통 헤엄치는 자세에서 그 머리의 정면이 물에 대해 거의 완전히 수직인 평면을 이룬다는 것을 관찰하게 된다. 당신은 그 정면의 아랫부분이 상당히 뒤쪽으로 비스듬히 기울어져, 활대 같은 아래턱을 받아들이는 그 긴 소켓에게 좀 더 후퇴할 공간을 마련해 준다는 것을 관찰하게 된다."], ["Moreover you observe that the whale has no external nose; and that what nose he has—his spout hole—is on the top of his head; you observe that his eyes and ears are at the sides of his head, nearly one third of his entire length from the front.", "당신은 입이 머리의 완전히 아래쪽에 있다는 것을, 실로 마치 당신 자신의 입이 턱 아래에 통째로 달려 있기라도 한 것처럼 그렇게 놓여 있다는 것을 관찰하게 된다. 게다가 당신은 고래에게 바깥으로 드러난 코가 없다는 것, 그리고 코라 할 만한 것—그 분수공—이 머리 꼭대기에 있다는 것을 관찰하게 된다."], ["Wherefore, you must now have perceived that the front of the Sperm Whale’s head is a dead, blind wall, without a single organ or tender prominence of any sort whatsoever.", "당신은 그 눈과 귀가 머리 양옆에, 정면으로부터 전체 몸길이의 거의 삼분의 일쯤 되는 지점에 있다는 것을 관찰하게 된다. 그러므로 당신은 이제 향유고래 머리의 정면이 그 어떤 기관도, 그 어떤 종류의 연약하게 도드라진 부분도 하나 없는, 죽은 듯 눈먼 벽이라는 것을 깨달았을 것이다."], ["Furthermore, you are now to consider that only in the extreme, lower, backward sloping part of the front of the head, is there the slightest vestige of bone; and not till you get near twenty feet from the forehead do you come to the full cranial development.", "나아가 당신은 이제 그 머리 정면에서 오직 맨 끝, 가장 아래쪽, 뒤로 비스듬히 기울어진 부분에만 뼈의 흔적이 아주 희미하게 있을 뿐이며, 이마로부터 거의 20피트(약 6미터)쯤 안으로 들어가야 비로소 완전한 두개골의 발달에 이르게 된다는 것을 헤아려야 한다."], ["So that this whole enormous boneless mass is as one wad.", "그러니 이 거대한 뼈 없는 덩어리 전체가 마치 하나의 솜뭉치 같은 것이다."], ["Finally, though, as will soon be revealed, its contents partly comprise the most delicate oil; yet, you are now to be apprised of the nature of the substance which so impregnably invests all that apparent effeminacy.", "끝으로, 곧 밝혀지겠지만, 그 내용물은 일부 더없이 정교한 기름으로 이루어져 있다. 그러나 당신은 이제, 겉으로 보기에 그토록 여리고 부드러운 그 모든 것을 그토록 빈틈없이 감싸고 있는 물질의 성질에 관해 알아 두어야 한다."], ["In some previous place I have described to you how the blubber wraps the body of the whale, as the rind wraps an orange.", "앞선 어느 대목에서 나는 껍질이 오렌지를 감싸듯 지방층이 고래의 몸을 감싸는 방식을 당신에게 설명한 바 있다."], ["Just so with the head; but with this difference: about the head this envelope, though not so thick, is of a boneless toughness, inestimable by any man who has not handled it.", "머리도 꼭 그러하지만, 다만 이런 차이가 있다. 머리를 감싸는 이 덮개는 그만큼 두껍지는 않으나, 그것을 직접 다루어 본 적 없는 사람으로서는 가늠조차 할 수 없는, 뼈 없는 질긴 단단함을 지녔다."], ["The severest pointed harpoon, the sharpest lance darted by the strongest human arm, impotently rebounds from it.", "가장 날카롭게 벼린 작살도, 가장 힘센 사람의 팔이 던진 가장 예리한 창도 거기서 맥없이 튕겨 나온다."], ["It is as though the forehead of the Sperm Whale were paved with horses’ hoofs.", "마치 향유고래의 이마가 말발굽으로 포장되어 있기라도 한 것 같다."], ["I do not think that any sensation lurks in it.", "나는 거기에 어떤 감각도 도사리고 있으리라고는 생각지 않는다."]], [["Bethink yourself also of another thing.", "또 한 가지를 곰곰이 생각해 보라."], ["When two large, loaded Indiamen chance to crowd and crush towards each other in the docks, what do the sailors do?", "큰 짐을 가득 실은 두 척의 동인도 무역선이 부두에서 어쩌다 서로를 향해 밀려들어 짓누르게 될 때, 선원들은 어떻게 하는가?"], ["They do not suspend between them, at the point of coming contact, any merely hard substance, like iron or wood.", "그들은 막 맞닿으려는 지점에 쇠나 나무 같은 한낱 단단한 물체를 둘 사이에 끼워 넣지 않는다. 그렇지 않다."], ["No, they hold there a large, round wad of tow and cork, enveloped in the thickest and toughest of ox-hide.", "그들은 거기에 가장 두껍고 가장 질긴 쇠가죽으로 감싼, 크고 둥근 뱃밥과 코르크 뭉치를 댄다."], ["That bravely and uninjured takes the jam which would have snapped all their oaken handspikes and iron crow-bars.", "그것이 그들의 떡갈나무 지렛대와 쇠 지렛대를 죄다 부러뜨렸을 그 압착을 늠름하게, 아무 손상 없이 받아 낸다. 이것만으로도 내가 말하려는 명백한 사실은 충분히 잘 드러난다."], ["By itself this sufficiently illustrates the obvious fact I drive at.", "그러나 이에 덧붙여, 나에게는 가설로서 이런 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["But supplementary to this, it has hypothetically occurred to me, that as ordinary fish possess what is called a swimming bladder in them, capable, at will, of distension or contraction; and as the Sperm Whale, as far as I know, has no such provision in him; considering, too, the otherwise inexplicable manner in which he now depresses his head altogether beneath the surface, and anon swims with it high elevated out of the water; considering the unobstructed elasticity of its envelope; considering the unique interior of his head; it has hypothetically occurred to me, I say, that those mystical lung-celled honeycombs there may possibly have some hitherto unknown and unsuspected connexion with the outer air, so as to be susceptible to atmospheric distension and contraction.", "곧 보통의 물고기들은 제 안에 이른바 부레라는 것을 지녀 마음대로 그것을 부풀리거나 오므릴 수 있는데, 향유고래는 내가 아는 한 그런 장치를 제 안에 갖고 있지 않다는 것, 게다가 그가 머리를 통째로 수면 아래로 가라앉혔다가도 이내 그것을 물 밖으로 높이 치켜든 채 헤엄치는, 달리는 설명할 길 없는 그 방식을 고려할 때, 그 덮개의 거침없는 탄력성을 고려할 때, 그 머리 내부의 유례없는 구조를 고려할 때—내가 말하건대, 나에게는 가설로서 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. 곧 거기 그 신비로운 폐포 모양의 벌집들이 어쩌면 지금까지 알려지지도, 짐작되지도 않은 어떤 방식으로 바깥 공기와 연결되어, 대기에 의해 부풀고 오므라드는 일이 가능할지도 모른다는 것이다."], ["If this be so, fancy the irresistibleness of that might, to which the most impalpable and destructive of all elements contributes.", "만일 그렇다면, 모든 원소 가운데 가장 만질 수 없고 가장 파괴적인 것이 한몫 거드는 그 위력의 거역할 수 없음을 한번 상상해 보라."]], [["Now, mark.", "자, 잘 보라."], ["Unerringly impelling this dead, impregnable, uninjurable wall, and this most buoyant thing within; there swims behind it all a mass of tremendous life, only to be adequately estimated as piled wood is—by the cord; and all obedient to one volition, as the smallest insect.", "이 죽은 듯하고, 뚫을 수 없으며, 상하지 않는 벽과, 그 안에 깃든 더없이 부력 가득한 것을 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 밀어붙이며, 그 모든 것의 뒤에는 어마어마한 생명의 덩어리가 헤엄치고 있으니, 그것은 쌓아 올린 장작더미를 코드 단위로 가늠하듯 그렇게 헤아릴 수밖에 없는 것이며, 그 모두가 가장 작은 곤충이 그러하듯 하나의 의지에 순순히 따른다."], ["So that when I shall hereafter detail to you all the specialities and concentrations of potency everywhere lurking in this expansive monster; when I shall show you some of his more inconsiderable braining feats; I trust you will have renounced all ignorant incredulity, and be ready to abide by this; that though the Sperm Whale stove a passage through the Isthmus of Darien, and mixed the Atlantic with the Pacific, you would not elevate one hair of your eye-brow.", "그러므로 내가 앞으로 이 광대한 괴물 곳곳에 도사린 모든 특수한 점들과 응집된 위력을 당신에게 낱낱이 들려줄 때, 또 그가 머리로 들이받아 부숴 버린, 그중에서도 비교적 사소한 몇몇 위업을 당신에게 보여 줄 때, 나는 당신이 무지한 모든 불신을 버리고 다음의 것을 기꺼이 받아들이게 되리라 믿는다. 곧 설령 향유고래가 다리엔 지협을 들이받아 길을 뚫어 대서양과 태평양을 뒤섞어 놓는다 한들, 당신은 눈썹 한 올조차 치켜올리지 않으리라는 것을."], ["For unless you own the whale, you are but a provincial and sentimentalist in Truth.", "왜냐하면 당신이 고래를 진정으로 제 것으로 삼지 않는 한, 당신은 진리에 있어 한낱 시골뜨기요 감상가에 지나지 않기 때문이다."], ["But clear Truth is a thing for salamander giants only to encounter; how small the chances for the provincials then?", "그러나 맑은 진리란 오직 불도마뱀 같은 거인들만이 맞닥뜨릴 수 있는 것이니, 그렇다면 시골뜨기들에게 주어진 기회란 얼마나 보잘것없겠는가? 라이스에서 저 무시무시한 여신의 베일을 들어 올린 그 연약한 젊은이에게 무슨 일이 닥쳤던가?"], ["What befell the weakling youth lifting the dread goddess’s veil at Lais?", "* 라이스의 여신: 사이스(이집트)의 여신 이시스를 가리키며, \"내 베일을 들어 올린 인간은 아직 없다\"는 비문이 새겨져 있었다고 전한다. 베일을 들춰 본 젊은이는 그 충격으로 목숨을 잃었다는 전설이 있다."]], [["# CHAPTER 77. The Great Heidelburgh Tun.", "# CHAPTER 77. The Great Heidelburgh Tun."]], [["Now comes the Baling of the Case.", "이제 케이스(case)에서 기름을 퍼내는 작업이 시작된다."], ["But to comprehend it aright, you must know something of the curious internal structure of the thing operated upon.", "그러나 이 일을 제대로 이해하려면, 그 작업 대상이 되는 물건의 기묘한 내부 구조에 관해 어느 정도 알아 둘 필요가 있다."]], [["Regarding the Sperm Whale’s head as a solid oblong, you may, on an inclined plane, sideways divide it into two quoins,* whereof the lower is the bony structure, forming the cranium and jaws, and the upper an unctuous mass wholly free from bones; its broad forward end forming the expanded vertical apparent forehead of the whale.", "향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 머리를 단단한 직육면체라고 본다면, 그것을 비스듬한 평면을 따라 옆으로 두 개의 쐐기꼴 덩이로 나눌 수 있다.* 그중 아래쪽은 두개골과 턱을 이루는 골격 구조이고, 위쪽은 뼈라고는 전혀 없는 기름진 덩어리인데, 그 넓고 앞으로 나온 끝부분이 바로 고래의 펼쳐진, 수직으로 솟은 듯한 이마를 이룬다."], ["At the middle of the forehead horizontally subdivide this upper quoin, and then you have two almost equal parts, which before were naturally divided by an internal wall of a thick tendinous substance.", "이 위쪽 쐐기꼴을 이마 한가운데에서 수평으로 다시 나누면, 거의 똑같은 두 부분이 생기는데, 이것들은 본래 두꺼운 힘줄질 물질로 된 내벽에 의해 자연스럽게 나뉘어 있던 것이다."]], [["*Quoin is not a Euclidean term.", "* 쐐기꼴(quoin)은 유클리드 기하학의 용어가 아니다."], ["It belongs to the pure nautical mathematics.", "그것은 순수 항해 수학에 속하는 말이다."], ["I know not that it has been defined before.", "이전에 정의된 적이 있는지 나는 알지 못한다."], ["A quoin is a solid which differs from a wedge in having its sharp end formed by the steep inclination of one side, instead of the mutual tapering of both sides.", "쐐기꼴이란, 양쪽 면이 함께 뾰족해지면서 날을 이루는 쐐기와는 달리, 한쪽 면의 가파른 경사만으로 그 날카로운 끝이 이루어지는 입체를 말한다."]], [["The lower subdivided part, called the junk, is one immense honeycomb of oil, formed by the crossing and recrossing, into ten thousand infiltrated cells, of tough elastic white fibres throughout its whole extent.", "아래쪽으로 다시 나뉜 부분은 정크(junk)라 불리는데, 그 전체에 걸쳐 질기고 탄력 있는 흰 섬유들이 교차하고 또 교차하며 수만 개의 스며든 작은 방들을 이루어, 거대한 기름의 벌집을 이룬다."], ["The upper part, known as the Case, may be regarded as the great Heidelburgh Tun of the Sperm Whale.", "케이스라 불리는 위쪽 부분은 향유고래의 거대한 하이델베르크 술통(Heidelburgh Tun)이라 여길 만하다."], ["And as that famous great tierce is mystically carved in front, so the whale’s vast plaited forehead forms innumerable strange devices for the emblematical adornment of his wondrous tun.", "그리고 저 유명한 큰 술통이 앞면에 신비로운 조각으로 새겨져 있듯이, 고래의 광대하게 주름진 이마 또한 그 놀라운 술통을 상징적으로 장식하는 수많은 기묘한 무늬들을 이룬다."], ["Moreover, as that of Heidelburgh was always replenished with the most excellent of the wines of the Rhenish valleys, so the tun of the whale contains by far the most precious of all his oily vintages; namely, the highly-prized spermaceti, in its absolutely pure, limpid, and odoriferous state.", "더욱이, 하이델베르크의 술통이 라인 계곡에서 나는 가장 빼어난 포도주로 늘 가득 채워져 있었듯이, 고래의 술통에는 그의 온갖 기름진 양조주 가운데 단연 가장 값진 것이 담겨 있다. 곧 더없이 순수하고 맑으며 향기로운 상태의, 그토록 귀하게 여겨지는 향유(spermaceti)다."], ["Nor is this precious substance found unalloyed in any other part of the creature.", "이 귀한 물질은 이 짐승의 다른 어떤 부위에서도 순수한 채로는 발견되지 않는다."], ["Though in life it remains perfectly fluid, yet, upon exposure to the air, after death, it soon begins to concrete; sending forth beautiful crystalline shoots, as when the first thin delicate ice is just forming in water.", "살아 있을 때는 완전히 액체 상태를 유지하지만, 죽은 뒤 공기에 노출되면 곧 굳기 시작하여, 마치 물 위에 처음으로 얇고 섬세한 얼음이 막 얼어붙을 때처럼 아름다운 결정의 싹들을 뻗어 낸다."], ["A large whale’s case generally yields about five hundred gallons of sperm, though from unavoidable circumstances, considerable of it is spilled, leaks, and dribbles away, or is otherwise irrevocably lost in the ticklish business of securing what you can.", "큰 고래의 케이스 하나에서는 대개 500갤런쯤의 향유가 나오는데, 다만 어쩔 수 없는 사정으로 적잖은 양이 쏟아지고, 새고, 흘러 떨어지거나, 그렇지 않으면 건질 수 있는 것을 건지려는 그 까다로운 작업 중에 영영 잃어버리고 만다."]], [["I know not with what fine and costly material the Heidelburgh Tun was coated within, but in superlative richness that coating could not possibly have compared with the silken pearl-coloured membrane, like the lining of a fine pelisse, forming the inner surface of the Sperm Whale’s case.", "하이델베르크 술통이 그 안쪽에 어떤 곱고 값진 재료로 입혀져 있었는지 나는 알지 못하지만, 그 안감이 아무리 화려하다 한들, 고급 모피 외투의 안감처럼 향유고래 케이스의 안쪽 표면을 이루는 저 비단결의 진주빛 막에는 결코 그 풍요로움에 견줄 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["It will have been seen that the Heidelburgh Tun of the Sperm Whale embraces the entire length of the entire top of the head; and since—as has been elsewhere set forth—the head embraces one third of the whole length of the creature, then setting that length down at eighty feet for a good sized whale, you have more than twenty-six feet for the depth of the tun, when it is lengthwise hoisted up and down against a ship’s side.", "지금까지 본 바로, 향유고래의 하이델베르크 술통은 머리 꼭대기 전체의 길이를 고스란히 차지하고 있음을 알 수 있다. 그리고 — 앞서 다른 곳에서 밝혔듯이 — 머리가 그 짐승 전체 길이의 삼분의 일을 차지하므로, 제법 큰 고래의 길이를 80피트(약 24미터)로 잡는다면, 그 술통을 길이 방향으로 배 옆구리에 세워 매달았을 때 그 깊이는 26피트(약 8미터)가 넘는 셈이다."]], [["As in decapitating the whale, the operator’s instrument is brought close to the spot where an entrance is subsequently forced into the spermaceti magazine; he has, therefore, to be uncommonly heedful, lest a careless, untimely stroke should invade the sanctuary and wastingly let out its invaluable contents.", "고래의 목을 자를 때, 작업자의 연장은 나중에 향유 저장고 안으로 강제로 입구를 뚫게 될 바로 그 지점 가까이에 닿게 된다. 그러므로 그는 부주의하고 때 이른 한 칼질이 그 성소를 침범하여 그 값을 매길 수 없는 내용물을 허비하며 흘려보내지 않도록, 유난히 조심하지 않으면 안 된다."], ["It is this decapitated end of the head, also, which is at last elevated out of the water, and retained in that position by the enormous cutting tackles, whose hempen combinations, on one side, make quite a wilderness of ropes in that quarter.", "마침내 물 밖으로 들어 올려져 그 자세로 붙들리게 되는 것도 바로 이 머리의 잘린 끝부분인데, 그것을 붙드는 것은 거대한 절단용 도르래 장치이며, 그 삼밧줄 얽힘이 한쪽에서는 그 일대를 온통 밧줄의 황무지로 만들어 놓는다."]], [["Thus much being said, attend now, I pray you, to that marvellous and—in this particular instance—almost fatal operation whereby the Sperm Whale’s great Heidelburgh Tun is tapped.", "이만큼 말해 두었으니, 이제 부디 주목해 주기를 바란다. 향유고래의 거대한 하이델베르크 술통에 마침내 구멍을 뚫는, 저 경이롭고도 — 바로 이번 경우에는 — 거의 치명적이었던 작업에."]], [["# CHAPTER 78. Cistern and Buckets.", "# CHAPTER 78. Cistern and Buckets."]], [["Nimble as a cat, Tashtego mounts aloft; and without altering his erect posture, runs straight out upon the overhanging mainyard-arm, to the part where it exactly projects over the hoisted Tun.", "고양이처럼 날렵하게 타슈테고(Tashtego)가 위로 기어오른다. 그는 꼿꼿한 자세를 흩뜨리지 않은 채 그대로 머리 위로 뻗어 나온 큰돛 활대 끝을 따라 똑바로 걸어 나아가, 매달려 올려진 통의 바로 위로 정확히 돌출된 지점에 이른다."], ["He has carried with him a light tackle called a whip, consisting of only two parts, travelling through a single-sheaved block.", "그는 채찍이라 불리는 가벼운 도르래 장치를 가지고 올라갔는데, 이것은 단지 두 가닥으로만 이루어져 외바퀴 활차 하나를 통과하는 것이다."], ["Securing this block, so that it hangs down from the yard-arm, he swings one end of the rope, till it is caught and firmly held by a hand on deck.", "그는 이 활차가 활대 끝에서 늘어지도록 단단히 고정한 뒤, 밧줄의 한쪽 끝을 흔들어 갑판 위의 누군가가 그것을 붙잡아 단단히 쥐게 한다."], ["Then, hand-over-hand, down the other part, the Indian drops through the air, till dexterously he lands on the summit of the head.", "그러고 나서 인디언은 다른 한쪽을 손에서 손으로 잡아 내리며 공중을 타고 내려와, 마침내 솜씨 좋게 고래 머리 꼭대기에 내려선다."], ["There—still high elevated above the rest of the company, to whom he vivaciously cries—he seems some Turkish Muezzin calling the good people to prayers from the top of a tower.", "그곳에서 — 여전히 다른 동료들보다 높이 솟아오른 채, 그가 활기차게 소리쳐 부르는 그들을 향해 — 그는 마치 탑 꼭대기에서 선량한 사람들을 기도로 불러 모으는 어느 터키 무에진처럼 보인다."], ["A short-handled sharp spade being sent up to him, he diligently searches for the proper place to begin breaking into the Tun.", "자루가 짧고 날이 선 삽 하나가 그에게 올려지자, 그는 통을 뚫고 들어가기 시작할 적당한 자리를 부지런히 찾는다."], ["In this business he proceeds very heedfully, like a treasure-hunter in some old house, sounding the walls to find where the gold is masoned in.", "이 일을 그는 매우 신중하게 진행하는데, 마치 어느 낡은 집에서 보물을 찾는 사람이 금이 벽돌 틈에 박혀 있는 곳을 알아내려고 벽을 두드려 보는 것과도 같다."], ["By the time this cautious search is over, a stout iron-bound bucket, precisely like a well-bucket, has been attached to one end of the whip; while the other end, being stretched across the deck, is there held by two or three alert hands.", "이 조심스러운 탐색이 끝날 무렵, 우물에서 쓰는 두레박과 똑 닮은, 쇠테를 두른 튼튼한 양동이가 채찍의 한쪽 끝에 매달리고, 다른 한쪽 끝은 갑판을 가로질러 뻗어 두세 명의 민첩한 일손들이 거머쥐고 있다."], ["These last now hoist the bucket within grasp of the Indian, to whom another person has reached up a very long pole.", "이들이 이제 양동이를 인디언의 손이 닿는 데까지 끌어올리자, 또 다른 사람이 그에게 아주 긴 장대를 올려 건넨다."], ["Inserting this pole into the bucket, Tashtego downward guides the bucket into the Tun, till it entirely disappears; then giving the word to the seamen at the whip, up comes the bucket again, all bubbling like a dairy-maid’s pail of new milk.", "타슈테고는 이 장대를 양동이에 끼워 넣고, 양동이를 아래로 통 안으로 몰아넣어 그것이 완전히 사라질 때까지 내린다. 그런 다음 채찍을 잡은 선원들에게 신호를 보내면, 양동이는 갓 짜낸 우유가 담긴 우유 짜는 처녀의 들통처럼 온통 거품을 일으키며 다시 올라온다."], ["Carefully lowered from its height, the full-freighted vessel is caught by an appointed hand, and quickly emptied into a large tub.", "그 높이에서 조심스럽게 내려진, 가득 찬 그릇은 지정된 일손이 받아 들어 재빨리 커다란 통에 비운다."], ["Then remounting aloft, it again goes through the same round until the deep cistern will yield no more.", "그러고는 다시 위로 올라가, 깊은 저수조가 더는 아무것도 내어 주지 않을 때까지 똑같은 과정을 되풀이한다."], ["Towards the end, Tashtego has to ram his long pole harder and harder, and deeper and deeper into the Tun, until some twenty feet of the pole have gone down.", "끝 무렵에 이르면 타슈테고는 자신의 긴 장대를 점점 더 세게, 점점 더 깊이 통 속으로 박아 넣어야 하는데, 마침내는 장대가 스무 자가량이나 들어가 버린다."]], [["Now, the people of the Pequod had been baling some time in this way; several tubs had been filled with the fragrant sperm; when all at once a queer accident happened.", "이제 피쿼드호(Pequod)의 사람들은 이런 식으로 한동안 퍼내고 있었다. 향기로운 향유로 통 여러 개가 채워졌다."], ["Whether it was that Tashtego, that wild Indian, was so heedless and reckless as to let go for a moment his one-handed hold on the great cabled tackles suspending the head; or whether the place where he stood was so treacherous and oozy; or whether the Evil One himself would have it to fall out so, without stating his particular reasons; how it was exactly, there is no telling now; but, on a sudden, as the eightieth or ninetieth bucket came suckingly up—my God!", "그런데 별안간 기이한 사고가 일어났다. 저 거친 인디언 타슈테고가 너무 부주의하고 무모한 나머지, 고래 머리를 매달고 있는 굵은 밧줄 도르래에 한 손으로 매달려 있던 것을 한순간 놓아 버린 탓인지, 아니면 그가 서 있던 자리가 그토록 미끄럽고 진득거리는 곳이었던 탓인지, 아니면 악마 그 자신이 — 그 특별한 까닭을 굳이 밝히지는 않은 채 — 그렇게 되기를 바란 탓인지, 정확히 어찌 된 일인지는 이제 와서 말할 도리가 없다."], ["poor Tashtego—like the twin reciprocating bucket in a veritable well, dropped head-foremost down into this great Tun of Heidelburgh, and with a horrible oily gurgling, went clean out of sight!", "다만 갑자기, 여든 번째인가 아흔 번째인가의 양동이가 쪽쪽 빨아들이는 소리를 내며 올라오던 그 순간 — 오, 하느님! 가엾은 타슈테고가 — 진짜 우물 속에서 서로 오르내리는 쌍둥이 두레박처럼, 머리부터 거꾸로 이 거대한 하이델베르크의 통 속으로 떨어져, 끔찍하게 기름이 콸콸거리는 소리와 함께 깨끗이 시야에서 사라지고 말았다!"]], [["“Man overboard!”", "\"사람이 빠졌다!\""], ["cried Daggoo, who amid the general consternation first came to his senses.", "다구(Daggoo)가 외쳤다. 모두가 경악에 빠진 가운데 가장 먼저 정신을 차린 자였다."], ["“Swing the bucket this way!”", "\"양동이를 이쪽으로 흔들어 보내!\""], ["and putting one foot into it, so as the better to secure his slippery hand-hold on the whip itself, the hoisters ran him high up to the top of the head, almost before Tashtego could have reached its interior bottom.", "그러고는 채찍 자체를 미끄러운 손으로 더 단단히 붙잡기 위해 한 발을 양동이 안에 들여놓자, 끌어올리는 자들은 타슈테고가 머리 속 밑바닥에 채 닿기도 전에 그를 머리 꼭대기까지 높이 끌어올렸다."], ["Meantime, there was a terrible tumult.", "그러는 동안 무시무시한 소동이 벌어졌다."], ["Looking over the side, they saw the before lifeless head throbbing and heaving just below the surface of the sea, as if that moment seized with some momentous idea; whereas it was only the poor Indian unconsciously revealing by those struggles the perilous depth to which he had sunk.", "뱃전 너머를 내려다보니, 조금 전까지 생명 없던 고래 머리가 마치 그 순간 어떤 중대한 생각에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯, 바다 표면 바로 아래에서 두근거리며 들썩이고 있었다. 그러나 그것은 다만 가엾은 인디언이 자신이 가라앉은 위험한 깊이를 그 몸부림으로 무심코 드러내고 있는 것에 지나지 않았다."]], [["At this instant, while Daggoo, on the summit of the head, was clearing the whip—which had somehow got foul of the great cutting tackles—a sharp cracking noise was heard; and to the unspeakable horror of all, one of the two enormous hooks suspending the head tore out, and with a vast vibration the enormous mass sideways swung, till the drunk ship reeled and shook as if smitten by an iceberg.", "바로 이 순간, 다구가 머리 꼭대기에서 채찍을 — 어쩌다 거대한 절단용 도르래에 엉켜 버린 그것을 — 풀어내고 있을 때, 날카롭게 우지끈 갈라지는 소리가 들려왔다. 그리고 모두가 이루 말할 수 없는 공포에 휩싸인 가운데, 머리를 매달고 있던 두 개의 거대한 갈고리 중 하나가 뜯겨 나가더니, 어마어마한 진동과 함께 그 거대한 덩어리가 옆으로 휙 흔들렸고, 그 바람에 술 취한 듯 비틀거리던 배가 마치 빙산에 부딪힌 것처럼 휘청거리며 떨었다."], ["The one remaining hook, upon which the entire strain now depended, seemed every instant to be on the point of giving way; an event still more likely from the violent motions of the head.", "이제 모든 무게가 실리게 된 단 하나 남은 갈고리는, 매 순간 곧 떨어져 나갈 듯 보였다. 머리가 격렬하게 요동치는 탓에 그 가능성은 더욱더 커 보였다."]], [["“Come down, come down!” yelled the seamen to Daggoo, but with one hand holding on to the heavy tackles, so that if the head should drop, he would still remain suspended; the negro having cleared the foul line, rammed down the bucket into the now collapsed well, meaning that the buried harpooneer should grasp it, and so be hoisted out.", "\"내려와, 내려오라고!\" 선원들이 다구에게 고함쳤다. 그러나 그는 한 손으로 무거운 도르래를 붙든 채여서, 설령 머리가 떨어진다 해도 그대로 매달려 있을 참이었다. 흑인은 엉킨 밧줄을 풀어낸 뒤, 이제 무너져 내린 우물 속으로 양동이를 박아 넣었다. 파묻힌 작살잡이가 그것을 붙잡으면 끌어올릴 수 있으리라는 생각에서였다."]], [["“In heaven’s name, man,”", "\"제발, 이 사람아,\""], ["cried Stubb, “are you ramming home a cartridge there?—Avast!", "스터브(Stubb)가 소리쳤다. \"거기다 대고 탄약을 쟁여 넣기라도 하는 건가?"], ["How will that help him; jamming that iron-bound bucket on top of his head?", "— 멈춰! 그게 그자에게 무슨 도움이 되겠나, 쇠테 두른 양동이를 그자 머리통 위에다 처박아서 말이야?"], ["Avast, will ye!”", "그만두라니까!\""]], [["“Stand clear of the tackle!”", "\"도르래에서 비켜!\""], ["cried a voice like the bursting of a rocket.", "로켓이 터지는 듯한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["Almost in the same instant, with a thunder-boom, the enormous mass dropped into the sea, like Niagara’s Table-Rock into the whirlpool; the suddenly relieved hull rolled away from it, to far down her glittering copper; and all caught their breath, as half swinging—now over the sailors’ heads, and now over the water—Daggoo, through a thick mist of spray, was dimly beheld clinging to the pendulous tackles, while poor, buried-alive Tashtego was sinking utterly down to the bottom of the sea!", "거의 같은 순간, 우레 같은 굉음과 함께 그 거대한 덩어리가 바다로 떨어졌다. 나이아가라의 테이블바위가 소용돌이 속으로 떨어지듯이."], ["But hardly had the blinding vapor cleared away, when a naked figure with a boarding-sword in his hand, was for one swift moment seen hovering over the bulwarks.", "갑작스레 짐을 던 선체는 그것에서 멀찍이 굴러나며 번쩍이는 구리판 밑바닥까지 드러냈다. 그리고 모두가 숨을 멈춘 채 바라보니, 절반쯤 흔들리며 — 이제는 선원들 머리 위로, 이제는 물 위로 — 다구가 자욱한 물보라 안개를 뚫고 흔들리는 도르래에 매달려 있는 모습이 어렴풋이 보였고, 한편 가엾은, 산 채로 파묻힌 타슈테고는 끝없이 바다 밑바닥으로 가라앉고 있었다!"], ["The next, a loud splash announced that my brave Queequeg had dived to the rescue.", "그러나 눈을 가리던 수증기가 채 걷히기도 전에, 손에 단검을 든 벌거벗은 형상 하나가 한순간 뱃전 위로 솟구쳐 머무는 모습이 보였다."], ["One packed rush was made to the side, and every eye counted every ripple, as moment followed moment, and no sign of either the sinker or the diver could be seen.", "다음 순간, 요란한 물소리가 나의 용감한 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 구하러 뛰어들었음을 알렸다. 사람들이 뱃전으로 한 덩어리가 되어 우르르 몰려들었고, 순간이 순간으로 이어지는 동안 모든 눈이 잔물결 하나하나를 헤아렸으나, 가라앉은 자도 뛰어든 자도 그 어떤 자취조차 보이지 않았다."], ["Some hands now jumped into a boat alongside, and pushed a little off from the ship.", "이제 몇몇 일손이 뱃전에 댄 보트로 뛰어들어 배에서 조금 떨어져 나갔다."]], [["“Ha!", "\"하! 하!\""], ["ha!”", "다구가 별안간 외쳤다. 이제는 머리 위에서 조용히 흔들리는 그의 자리에서였다."], ["cried Daggoo, all at once, from his now quiet, swinging perch overhead; and looking further off from the side, we saw an arm thrust upright from the blue waves; a sight strange to see, as an arm thrust forth from the grass over a grave.", "뱃전에서 더 멀리 내다보니, 푸른 물결 사이로 팔 하나가 똑바로 솟아 있는 것이 보였다. 마치 무덤 위 풀밭에서 팔 하나가 불쑥 솟아오른 것을 보는 듯, 기이한 광경이었다."]], [["“Both! both!—it is both!”—cried Daggoo again with a joyful shout; and soon after, Queequeg was seen boldly striking out with one hand, and with the other clutching the long hair of the Indian. Drawn into the waiting boat, they were quickly brought to the deck; but Tashtego was long in coming to, and Queequeg did not look very brisk.", "\"둘 다! 둘 다야! — 둘 다라고!\" 다구가 다시 기쁨에 겨워 환호성을 질렀다. 곧이어, 퀴케그가 한 손으로는 대담하게 물살을 가르고 다른 한 손으로는 인디언의 긴 머리채를 움켜쥐고 있는 모습이 보였다. 기다리고 있던 보트로 끌어 올려진 두 사람은 재빨리 갑판으로 옮겨졌다. 그러나 타슈테고는 한참이 지나서야 정신을 차렸고, 퀴케그도 그리 기운차 보이지는 않았다."]], [["Now, how had this noble rescue been accomplished?", "자, 이 갸륵한 구조는 어떻게 이루어졌던가?"], ["Why, diving after the slowly descending head, Queequeg with his keen sword had made side lunges near its bottom, so as to scuttle a large hole there; then dropping his sword, had thrust his long arm far inwards and upwards, and so hauled out poor Tash by the head.", "다름 아니라, 천천히 가라앉는 머리를 뒤쫓아 잠수한 퀴케그가 날카로운 단검으로 그 밑바닥 가까이를 옆에서 몇 차례 찔러 커다란 구멍 하나를 뚫었던 것이다. 그런 다음 단검을 버리고는 긴 팔을 안쪽으로, 위쪽으로 깊숙이 밀어 넣어, 가엾은 타슈를 머리부터 끌어냈다."], ["He averred, that upon first thrusting in for him, a leg was presented; but well knowing that that was not as it ought to be, and might occasion great trouble;—he had thrust back the leg, and by a dexterous heave and toss, had wrought a somerset upon the Indian; so that with the next trial, he came forth in the good old way—head foremost.", "그가 단언하기를, 처음 그를 찾아 손을 밀어 넣었을 때는 다리 한쪽이 먼저 닿았으나, 그것이 마땅한 순서가 아니며 큰 곤란을 일으킬 수 있음을 잘 알고 있었기에 — 다리를 도로 밀어 넣고, 능숙하게 들어 올려 휙 돌리는 솜씨로 인디언의 몸을 한 바퀴 공중제비 돌게 했다. 그리하여 다음번 시도에서는 그가 좋고 오래된 방식대로 — 머리부터 — 나왔다는 것이다."], ["As for the great head itself, that was doing as well as could be expected.", "거대한 머리 그 자체로 말하자면, 그것은 기대할 수 있는 한 가장 좋은 상태였다."]], [["And thus, through the courage and great skill in obstetrics of Queequeg, the deliverance, or rather, delivery of Tashtego, was successfully accomplished, in the teeth, too, of the most untoward and apparently hopeless impediments; which is a lesson by no means to be forgotten.", "이리하여, 퀴케그의 용기와 산파술에 관한 뛰어난 솜씨를 통해, 타슈테고의 구출, 아니 차라리 그 출산은 더할 나위 없이 불운하고 겉보기에는 가망 없어 보이는 장애를 무릅쓰고도 멋지게 이루어졌다. 이것은 결코 잊어서는 안 될 교훈이다."], ["Midwifery should be taught in the same course with fencing and boxing, riding and rowing.", "산파술도 펜싱과 권투, 승마와 노 젓기와 똑같은 과정 속에서 가르쳐야 마땅하다."]], [["I know that this queer adventure of the Gay-Header’s will be sure to seem incredible to some landsmen, though they themselves may have either seen or heard of some one’s falling into a cistern ashore; an accident which not seldom happens, and with much less reason too than the Indian’s, considering the exceeding slipperiness of the curb of the Sperm Whale’s well.", "이 게이헤드 인디언의 기묘한 모험담이 어떤 뭍사람들에게는 틀림없이 믿기 어렵게 들리리라는 것을 나는 안다. 비록 그들 자신도 누군가가 뭍의 저수조에 빠진 일을 보았거나 들어 보았을 법한데도 말이다. 그런 사고는 드물지 않게 일어나며, 게다가 향유고래의 우물 가장자리가 그토록 지독히 미끄럽다는 점을 헤아려 보면, 인디언의 경우보다 훨씬 덜한 까닭으로도 벌어지는 일인데도 그렇다."]], [["But, peradventure, it may be sagaciously urged, how is this?", "그러나 어쩌면 누군가는 영리하게 따져 물을지도 모른다. 이게 대체 어찌 된 일인가?"], ["We thought the tissued, infiltrated head of the Sperm Whale, was the lightest and most corky part about him; and yet thou makest it sink in an element of a far greater specific gravity than itself.", "우리는 향유고래의 결결이 짜여 스며든 머리야말로 그 몸에서 가장 가볍고 코르크처럼 잘 뜨는 부분이라고 알고 있었거늘, 그런데도 그대는 그것을 제 무게보다 훨씬 비중이 큰 원소 속으로 가라앉게 만드는가. 자, 이제 그대를 붙잡았노라."], ["We have thee there.", "천만의 말씀, 오히려 내가 그대들을 붙잡았노라."], ["Not at all, but I have ye; for at the time poor Tash fell in, the case had been nearly emptied of its lighter contents, leaving little but the dense tendinous wall of the well—a double welded, hammered substance, as I have before said, much heavier than the sea water, and a lump of which sinks in it like lead almost.", "가엾은 타슈가 빠진 그 순간에는 케이스(case) 속에서 가벼운 내용물이 거의 다 비워진 뒤여서, 남은 것이라곤 우물의 빽빽한 힘줄 벽뿐이었기 때문이다. 그것은 앞서 말했듯이 이중으로 용접되고 두들겨 다져진 물질로서, 바닷물보다 훨씬 무거워, 그 한 덩어리는 거의 납처럼 바닷속으로 가라앉는다."], ["But the tendency to rapid sinking in this substance was in the present instance materially counteracted by the other parts of the head remaining undetached from it, so that it sank very slowly and deliberately indeed, affording Queequeg a fair chance for performing his agile obstetrics on the run, as you may say.", "그러나 이 물질이 빠르게 가라앉으려는 경향은, 이번 경우에는 머리의 다른 부분들이 그것에서 떨어져 나가지 않고 그대로 붙어 있던 덕분에 상당히 상쇄되었다. 그리하여 머리는 참으로 아주 천천히, 신중하게 가라앉았고, 그 덕에 퀴케그에게는, 말하자면 달리는 중에 그 날렵한 산파술을 펼칠 그럴듯한 기회가 주어졌던 것이다."], ["Yes, it was a running delivery, so it was.", "그렇다, 그것은 달리는 도중의 출산이었다, 정말 그러했다."]], [["Now, had Tashtego perished in that head, it had been a very precious perishing; smothered in the very whitest and daintiest of fragrant spermaceti; coffined, hearsed, and tombed in the secret inner chamber and sanctum sanctorum of the whale.", "자, 만일 타슈테고가 그 머리 속에서 목숨을 잃었더라면, 그것은 참으로 진귀한 죽음이었으리라. 더없이 새하얗고 곱디고운 향기로운 경뇌유 속에 질식되어, 고래의 은밀한 내실, 곧 지성소 안에 관처럼 누워, 영구차에 실리고 무덤에 묻히는 셈이었으니."], ["Only one sweeter end can readily be recalled—the delicious death of an Ohio honey-hunter, who seeking honey in the crotch of a hollow tree, found such exceeding store of it, that leaning too far over, it sucked him in, so that he died embalmed.", "이보다 더 달콤한 최후로는 단 하나만이 얼른 떠오른다 — 어느 오하이오 꿀 사냥꾼의 감미로운 죽음이다. 속이 빈 나무 가지 틈에서 꿀을 찾던 그는 거기에 어찌나 엄청난 양의 꿀이 쌓여 있던지, 너무 깊이 몸을 기울인 나머지 그 속으로 빨려 들어가, 그렇게 방부 처리된 채 죽고 말았다."], ["How many, think ye, have likewise fallen into Plato’s honey head, and sweetly perished there?", "생각해 보라, 얼마나 많은 이들이 그와 마찬가지로 플라톤의 꿀 같은 머릿속에 빠져들어, 그곳에서 달콤하게 스러져 갔겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER 79. The Prairie.", "# CHAPTER 79. The Prairie."]], [["To scan the lines of his face, or feel the bumps on the head of this Leviathan; this is a thing which no Physiognomist or Phrenologist has as yet undertaken.", "이 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 얼굴 선을 살피거나 그 머리의 융기를 더듬어 보는 일, 이것은 아직 어떤 관상학자나 골상학자도 손대지 않은 과업이다."], ["Such an enterprise would seem almost as hopeful as for Lavater to have scrutinized the wrinkles on the Rock of Gibraltar, or for Gall to have mounted a ladder and manipulated the Dome of the Pantheon.", "그런 시도는, 라바터가 지브롤터 바위의 주름살을 면밀히 들여다보거나, 갈이 사다리를 타고 올라가 판테온의 둥근 지붕을 만지작거리려 드는 것만큼이나 가망 없어 보일 것이다."], ["Still, in that famous work of his, Lavater not only treats of the various faces of men, but also attentively studies the faces of horses, birds, serpents, and fish; and dwells in detail upon the modifications of expression discernible therein.", "그럼에도 라바터는 그 유명한 저작에서 사람의 갖가지 얼굴뿐 아니라 말과 새와 뱀과 물고기의 얼굴까지도 주의 깊게 연구하고, 그 안에서 분간되는 표정의 변화를 세세히 논했다."], ["Nor have Gall and his disciple Spurzheim failed to throw out some hints touching the phrenological characteristics of other beings than man.", "갈과 그의 제자 슈푸르츠하임 또한 인간이 아닌 다른 존재들의 골상학적 특징에 관해 몇 가지 단서를 던지는 일을 게을리하지 않았다."], ["Therefore, though I am but ill qualified for a pioneer, in the application of these two semi-sciences to the whale, I will do my endeavor.", "그러므로 비록 이 두 가지 반쪽짜리 과학을 고래에 적용하는 데 있어 나는 개척자로서는 한참 자격이 모자라지만, 그래도 한번 힘껏 해 보려 한다. 나는 무엇이든 시도한다."], ["I try all things; I achieve what I can.", "그리고 할 수 있는 만큼은 이루어 낸다."]], [["Physiognomically regarded, the Sperm Whale is an anomalous creature.", "관상학적으로 본다면 향유고래(Sperm Whale)는 변칙적인 동물이다."], ["He has no proper nose.", "그에게는 제대로 된 코가 없다."], ["And since the nose is the central and most conspicuous of the features; and since it perhaps most modifies and finally controls their combined expression; hence it would seem that its entire absence, as an external appendage, must very largely affect the countenance of the whale.", "코란 얼굴 생김새 가운데 한복판에 자리 잡은 가장 두드러진 부분이며, 어쩌면 그 모든 생김새가 어우러진 표정을 가장 많이 바꾸고 끝내 좌우하는 것이기에, 외부로 돌출한 부속물로서의 코가 통째로 없다는 사실은 고래의 얼굴 인상에 무척이나 크게 작용할 듯하다."], ["For as in landscape gardening, a spire, cupola, monument, or tower of some sort, is deemed almost indispensable to the completion of the scene; so no face can be physiognomically in keeping without the elevated open-work belfry of the nose.", "마치 조경에서 어떤 종류든 첨탑이나 둥근 지붕, 기념비, 혹은 탑이 있어야 비로소 풍경이 완성된다고 여겨지듯이, 코라는 높직하게 트인 종루(鐘樓)가 없으면 어떤 얼굴도 관상학적으로 격을 갖출 수 없는 법이다."], ["Dash the nose from Phidias’s marble Jove, and what a sorry remainder!", "피디아스가 빚어낸 대리석 주피터 상에서 코를 떼어 보라, 남는 것이 얼마나 한심한 꼴이겠는가!"], ["Nevertheless, Leviathan is of so mighty a magnitude, all his proportions are so stately, that the same deficiency which in the sculptured Jove were hideous, in him is no blemish at all.", "그렇지만 리바이어던은 워낙 위풍당당한 거구인 데다 그 모든 비례가 어찌나 당당한지, 조각된 주피터에게라면 흉측했을 바로 그 결함이 그에게는 조금도 흠이 되지 않는다."], ["Nay, it is an added grandeur.", "아니, 오히려 더해진 장엄함이다."], ["A nose to the whale would have been impertinent.", "고래에게 코란 어울리지 않는 군더더기였으리라."], ["As on your physiognomical voyage you sail round his vast head in your jolly-boat, your noble conceptions of him are never insulted by the reflection that he has a nose to be pulled.", "관상학적 항해에 나서 그대가 거룻배를 타고 그 거대한 머리를 빙 둘러 노 저어 갈 때, 잡아당길 코가 있다는 생각 따위로 그에 대한 고귀한 상념이 더럽혀지는 일은 결코 없다."], ["A pestilent conceit, which so often will insist upon obtruding even when beholding the mightiest royal beadle on his throne.", "옥좌에 앉은 더없이 위엄 있는 왕실 의전관을 바라볼 때조차 곧잘 끼어들겠다고 우겨 대는 그 고약한 망상 말이다."]], [["In some particulars, perhaps the most imposing physiognomical view to be had of the Sperm Whale, is that of the full front of his head.", "어떤 점에서 향유고래에 대해 얻을 수 있는 가장 위풍당당한 관상학적 광경은, 아마 그 머리의 정면을 온전히 마주하는 모습일 것이다."], ["This aspect is sublime.", "이 모습은 숭고하다."]], [["In thought, a fine human brow is like the East when troubled with the morning.", "사색에 잠긴 인간의 멋진 이마는 아침놀로 어수선한 동녘과도 같다."], ["In the repose of the pasture, the curled brow of the bull has a touch of the grand in it.", "풀밭에서 한가로이 쉴 때, 황소의 굽이진 이마에는 어딘가 장엄한 기운이 서려 있다."], ["Pushing heavy cannon up mountain defiles, the elephant’s brow is majestic.", "산골짜기 비탈로 묵직한 대포를 밀어 올리는 코끼리의 이마는 위풍당당하다."], ["Human or animal, the mystical brow is as that great golden seal affixed by the German emperors to their decrees.", "인간의 것이든 짐승의 것이든, 신비로운 이마란 게르만 황제들이 칙령에 찍던 저 거대한 황금 옥새와 같다."], ["It signifies—“God: done this day by my hand.”", "그것이 뜻하는 바는—\"하느님: 오늘 내 손으로 이 일을 행하노라\"이다."], ["But in most creatures, nay in man himself, very often the brow is but a mere strip of alpine land lying along the snow line.", "그러나 대부분의 피조물에게, 아니 인간 자신에게도 흔히 이마란 설선(雪線)을 따라 길게 누운 한 자락 알프스 고원에 지나지 않는다."], ["Few are the foreheads which like Shakespeare’s or Melancthon’s rise so high, and descend so low, that the eyes themselves seem clear, eternal, tideless mountain lakes; and all above them in the forehead’s wrinkles, you seem to track the antlered thoughts descending there to drink, as the Highland hunters track the snow prints of the deer.", "셰익스피어나 멜란히톤의 이마처럼 그토록 높이 솟았다가 그토록 낮게 내려와, 두 눈 자체가 맑고 영원하며 조수 없는 산정 호수처럼 보이는 이마는 드물다. 그런 이마에서는 그 위쪽 주름살을 따라, 마치 하일랜드의 사냥꾼들이 사슴이 눈밭에 남긴 발자국을 좇듯, 뿔 달린 사념들이 물을 마시러 그곳으로 내려오는 자취를 더듬어 갈 수 있을 듯하다."], ["But in the great Sperm Whale, this high and mighty god-like dignity inherent in the brow is so immensely amplified, that gazing on it, in that full front view, you feel the Deity and the dread powers more forcibly than in beholding any other object in living nature.", "그러나 거대한 향유고래에 이르면 이마에 깃든 이 높고도 강대한 신적 위엄이 어찌나 엄청나게 증폭되는지, 그 온전한 정면을 응시할 때 그대는 살아 있는 자연의 다른 어떤 대상을 바라볼 때보다도 신성(神性)과 두려운 권능을 더욱 강렬하게 느끼게 된다."], ["For you see no one point precisely; not one distinct feature is revealed; no nose, eyes, ears, or mouth; no face; he has none, proper; nothing but that one broad firmament of a forehead, pleated with riddles; dumbly lowering with the doom of boats, and ships, and men.", "그도 그럴 것이 그대는 어느 한 점도 또렷이 보지 못하고, 어느 뚜렷한 생김새 하나 드러나지 않으며, 코도 눈도 귀도 입도, 얼굴조차 없으니, 그에게는 제대로 된 얼굴이란 게 없고, 다만 수수께끼로 주름진 그 너른 창공 같은 이마 하나뿐이어서, 보트며 배며 사람들의 파멸을 말없이 굽어보며 잔뜩 찌푸리고 있을 뿐이다."], ["Nor, in profile, does this wondrous brow diminish; though that way viewed its grandeur does not domineer upon you so.", "그렇다고 옆에서 본다 해서 이 경이로운 이마가 작아지는 것도 아니다. 다만 그렇게 보면 그 장엄함이 그대를 그토록 짓누르듯 압도하지는 않는다."], ["In profile, you plainly perceive that horizontal, semi-crescentic depression in the forehead’s middle, which, in man, is Lavater’s mark of genius.", "옆에서 보면, 이마 한가운데 가로로 패인 그 반(半)초승달 모양의 함몰이 또렷이 눈에 들어오는데, 그것은 인간에게서라면 라바터가 천재의 표징이라 일컫던 것이다."]], [["But how? Genius in the Sperm Whale?", "그러나 어찌 그러한가?"], ["Has the Sperm Whale ever written a book, spoken a speech?", "향유고래에게 천재성이라니? 향유고래가 일찍이 책을 쓴 적이 있었던가, 연설을 한 적이 있었던가?"], ["No, his great genius is declared in his doing nothing particular to prove it.", "아니다. 그의 위대한 천재성은 그것을 입증할 만한 별다른 일을 아무것도 하지 않는다는 데서 선언된다."], ["It is moreover declared in his pyramidical silence.", "또한 그것은 그의 피라미드 같은 침묵으로써 선언된다."], ["And this reminds me that had the great Sperm Whale been known to the young Orient World, he would have been deified by their child-magian thoughts.", "그리고 이 대목에서 떠오르는 생각이 있으니, 만일 그 위대한 향유고래가 어린 동방 세계에 알려졌더라면, 그들의 유치한 주술적 사고에 의해 그는 신으로 떠받들어졌으리라는 것이다."], ["They deified the crocodile of the Nile, because the crocodile is tongueless; and the Sperm Whale has no tongue, or at least it is so exceedingly small, as to be incapable of protrusion.", "그들은 나일강의 악어를 신격화했는데, 그것은 악어에게 혀가 없기 때문이었다. 그런데 향유고래에게도 혀가 없거나, 적어도 너무나 작아서 내밀 수조차 없을 지경이다."], ["If hereafter any highly cultured, poetical nation shall lure back to their birth-right, the merry May-day gods of old; and livingly enthrone them again in the now egotistical sky; in the now unhaunted hill; then be sure, exalted to Jove’s high seat, the great Sperm Whale shall lord it.", "만일 훗날 어느 고도로 세련되고 시적인 민족이 옛날의 그 흥겨운 오월제(五月祭) 신들을 그들 본래의 자리로 다시 꾀어들이고, 이제는 오만해진 하늘에, 이제는 신령 없는 언덕에 그들을 살아 있는 모습으로 다시 옹립한다면, 그때는 분명 주피터의 드높은 옥좌에 떠받들려 그 위대한 향유고래가 군림하게 되리라."]], [["Champollion deciphered the wrinkled granite hieroglyphics.", "샹폴리옹은 화강암에 새겨진 주름진 상형문자를 해독했다."], ["But there is no Champollion to decipher the Egypt of every man’s and every being’s face.", "그러나 모든 인간과 모든 존재의 얼굴이라는 이집트를 해독해 줄 샹폴리옹은 어디에도 없다."], ["Physiognomy, like every other human science, is but a passing fable.", "관상학도 다른 모든 인간의 학문과 마찬가지로 한때 스쳐 지나가는 우화에 지나지 않는다."], ["If then, Sir William Jones, who read in thirty languages, could not read the simplest peasant’s face in its profounder and more subtle meanings, how may unlettered Ishmael hope to read the awful Chaldee of the Sperm Whale’s brow?", "그러니 서른 개 언어를 읽어 냈던 윌리엄 존스 경조차 가장 단순한 농부의 얼굴에 담긴 더 깊고 더 미묘한 뜻을 읽어 내지 못했거늘, 일자무식인 이슈마엘이 어찌 향유고래의 이마에 새겨진 저 무시무시한 칼데아 문자를 읽어 내기를 바랄 수 있겠는가?"], ["I but put that brow before you.", "나는 다만 그 이마를 그대 앞에 내놓을 뿐이다."], ["Read it if you can.", "읽을 수 있거든 읽어 보라."]], [["# CHAPTER 80. The Nut.", "# CHAPTER 80. The Nut."]], [["If the Sperm Whale be physiognomically a Sphinx, to the phrenologist his brain seems that geometrical circle which it is impossible to square.", "향유고래가 골상학적으로 스핑크스라면, 골상학자에게 그 뇌는 도저히 정방형으로 만들 수 없는 저 기하학의 원과도 같아 보인다."]], [["In the full-grown creature the skull will measure at least twenty feet in length.", "다 자란 향유고래의 두개골은 길이가 적어도 20피트(약 6미터)는 된다."], ["Unhinge the lower jaw, and the side view of this skull is as the side of a moderately inclined plane resting throughout on a level base.", "아래턱을 떼어 내고 이 두개골을 옆에서 보면, 평평한 바닥 위에 처음부터 끝까지 얹혀 있는 완만한 경사면의 측면처럼 보인다."], ["But in life—as we have elsewhere seen—this inclined plane is angularly filled up, and almost squared by the enormous superincumbent mass of the junk and sperm.", "그러나 살아 있을 때는—다른 곳에서 이미 보았듯이—이 경사면이 모가 나게 채워져, 그 위에 얹힌 융크(junk)와 향유의 거대한 덩어리로 인해 거의 정방형을 이룬다."], ["At the high end the skull forms a crater to bed that part of the mass; while under the long floor of this crater—in another cavity seldom exceeding ten inches in length and as many in depth—reposes the mere handful of this monster’s brain.", "높은 쪽 끝에서 두개골은 그 덩어리의 일부를 받쳐 주는 분화구를 이루고, 이 분화구의 길쭉한 바닥 아래—길이도 깊이도 좀처럼 10인치(약 25센티미터)를 넘지 않는 또 다른 빈 공간 속에—이 괴물의 뇌가 한 줌 분량으로 자리 잡고 있다."], ["The brain is at least twenty feet from his apparent forehead in life; it is hidden away behind its vast outworks, like the innermost citadel within the amplified fortifications of Quebec.", "살아 있을 때 이 뇌는 겉으로 드러난 이마에서 적어도 20피트나 떨어져 있으며, 마치 퀘벡의 광대한 요새 안에 깊숙이 들어앉은 가장 안쪽의 성채처럼, 그 거대한 외벽 뒤에 꼭꼭 숨겨져 있다."], ["So like a choice casket is it secreted in him, that I have known some whalemen who peremptorily deny that the Sperm Whale has any other brain than that palpable semblance of one formed by the cubic-yards of his sperm magazine.", "그것이 어찌나 정교한 함처럼 그의 몸속에 감추어져 있는지, 나는 향유고래에게 그 향유 창고를 이루는 수 세제곱야드의 덩어리가 빚어낸 손에 잡히는 뇌의 형상 말고는 어떤 다른 뇌도 없다고 단호히 부정하는 고래잡이들을 몇몇 알고 있다."], ["Lying in strange folds, courses, and convolutions, to their apprehensions, it seems more in keeping with the idea of his general might to regard that mystic part of him as the seat of his intelligence.", "기이한 주름과 굽이와 소용돌이를 이루며 누워 있는 그 신비로운 부위야말로, 그들이 보기에는 그의 전반적 위력에 더 잘 어울리는, 곧 그의 지성이 깃든 자리로 여길 만하다는 것이다."]], [["It is plain, then, that phrenologically the head of this Leviathan, in the creature’s living intact state, is an entire delusion.", "그러니 골상학적으로 보자면 이 리바이어던의 머리는, 살아서 온전한 상태에 있을 때 완전한 착각이라는 것이 분명해진다."], ["As for his true brain, you can then see no indications of it, nor feel any.", "그의 참된 뇌로 말하자면, 그것을 가리키는 어떤 표시도 볼 수 없고 어떤 느낌도 받을 수 없다."], ["The whale, like all things that are mighty, wears a false brow to the common world.", "고래는 강대한 모든 것들이 그러하듯, 세상 사람들에게 거짓된 이마를 내보이는 것이다."]], [["If you unload his skull of its spermy heaps and then take a rear view of its rear end, which is the high end, you will be struck by its resemblance to the human skull, beheld in the same situation, and from the same point of view.", "그의 두개골에서 향유 더미를 비워 내고 그 뒤쪽 끝, 곧 높은 쪽 끝을 뒤에서 바라보면, 같은 상황에서 같은 시점으로 바라본 인간의 두개골과 너무도 닮아 깜짝 놀라게 될 것이다."], ["Indeed, place this reversed skull (scaled down to the human magnitude) among a plate of men’s skulls, and you would involuntarily confound it with them; and remarking the depressions on one part of its summit, in phrenological phrase you would say—This man had no self-esteem, and no veneration.", "실제로 이 뒤집힌 두개골을(인간 크기로 축소하여) 사람들의 두개골을 늘어놓은 표본 사이에 두면, 자기도 모르게 그것들과 뒤섞어 버릴 것이다. 그리고 그 꼭대기 한 부분이 움푹 꺼진 것을 보고는, 골상학 용어로 이렇게 말할 것이다—이 사람은 자존감도 없고 경외심도 없었다."], ["And by those negations, considered along with the affirmative fact of his prodigious bulk and power, you can best form to yourself the truest, though not the most exhilarating conception of what the most exalted potency is.", "그리고 이러한 부정적 특징들을, 그의 어마어마한 부피와 힘이라는 긍정적 사실과 함께 고려해 본다면, 가장 드높은 위력이 무엇인지에 대해 가장 참되되 가장 유쾌하지는 않은 관념을 스스로 가장 잘 그려 낼 수 있을 것이다."]], [["But if from the comparative dimensions of the whale’s proper brain, you deem it incapable of being adequately charted, then I have another idea for you.", "그러나 고래 본연의 뇌가 상대적으로 그토록 작은 것을 보고 그것을 제대로 도면화할 수 없다고 여긴다면, 내게는 또 다른 생각이 있다."], ["If you attentively regard almost any quadruped’s spine, you will be struck with the resemblance of its vertebræ to a strung necklace of dwarfed skulls, all bearing rudimental resemblance to the skull proper.", "거의 어떤 네발짐승이든 그 척추를 주의 깊게 살펴보면, 그 척추뼈들이 작아진 두개골들을 줄에 꿴 목걸이를 닮았다는 데에 놀라게 될 것인데, 그 하나하나가 본래의 두개골과 초보적인 닮음을 지니고 있다."], ["It is a German conceit, that the vertebræ are absolutely undeveloped skulls.", "척추뼈란 곧 전혀 발달하지 못한 두개골이라는 것이 독일인들의 한 가지 착상이다."], ["But the curious external resemblance, I take it the Germans were not the first men to perceive.", "그러나 이 묘한 외형적 닮음을 처음 알아챈 것이 독일인들은 아니었으리라고 나는 생각한다."], ["A foreign friend once pointed it out to me, in the skeleton of a foe he had slain, and with the vertebræ of which he was inlaying, in a sort of basso-relievo, the beaked prow of his canoe.", "언젠가 어느 이방인 친구가 그 점을 내게 일러 준 적이 있는데, 그는 자기가 죽인 원수의 뼈대를 가지고, 그 척추뼈로 카누의 부리 모양 뱃머리를 일종의 얕은 돋을새김(basso-relievo)으로 박아 넣고 있었다."], ["Now, I consider that the phrenologists have omitted an important thing in not pushing their investigations from the cerebellum through the spinal canal.", "그러므로 나는 골상학자들이 소뇌에서 척수관을 거쳐 가는 데까지 연구를 밀어붙이지 않은 것이 중요한 점을 빠뜨린 일이라고 본다."], ["For I believe that much of a man’s character will be found betokened in his backbone.", "나는 사람의 성품 가운데 많은 부분이 그 등뼈에 나타나 있으리라 믿기 때문이다."], ["I would rather feel your spine than your skull, whoever you are.", "그대가 누구든, 나는 그대의 두개골보다 그대의 척추를 만져 보고 싶다."], ["A thin joist of a spine never yet upheld a full and noble soul.", "빈약한 들보 같은 척추는 충만하고 고귀한 영혼을 떠받쳐 본 적이 없다."], ["I rejoice in my spine, as in the firm audacious staff of that flag which I fling half out to the world.", "나는 내 척추를 기뻐하노니, 그것은 내가 세상을 향해 반쯤 내걸어 펄럭이는 저 깃발의, 굳세고 담대한 깃대와도 같은 것이다."]], [["Apply this spinal branch of phrenology to the Sperm Whale.", "골상학의 이 척추 분야를 향유고래에게 적용해 보자."], ["His cranial cavity is continuous with the first neck-vertebra; and in that vertebra the bottom of the spinal canal will measure ten inches across, being eight in height, and of a triangular figure with the base downwards.", "그의 두개강은 첫 번째 목뼈와 이어져 있고, 그 목뼈에서 척수관 바닥은 폭이 10인치(약 25센티미터), 높이는 8인치(약 20센티미터)에 이르며, 밑변이 아래를 향한 삼각형 모양을 이룬다."], ["As it passes through the remaining vertebræ the canal tapers in size, but for a considerable distance remains of large capacity.", "나머지 척추뼈들을 지나면서 이 관은 점차 가늘어지지만, 상당한 거리에 걸쳐 큰 용적을 유지한다."], ["Now, of course, this canal is filled with much the same strangely fibrous substance—the spinal cord—as the brain; and directly communicates with the brain.", "그런데 물론 이 관은 뇌와 거의 똑같이 기묘하게 섬유질로 된 물질—곧 척수—로 채워져 있고, 뇌와 직접 통해 있다."], ["And what is still more, for many feet after emerging from the brain’s cavity, the spinal cord remains of an undecreasing girth, almost equal to that of the brain.", "게다가 더욱 놀라운 것은, 뇌의 공동을 빠져나온 뒤로도 여러 피트에 걸쳐 척수는 굵기가 줄지 않은 채, 거의 뇌의 굵기에 맞먹는 상태로 남아 있다는 점이다."], ["Under all these circumstances, would it be unreasonable to survey and map out the whale’s spine phrenologically?", "이 모든 사정을 고려할 때, 고래의 척추를 골상학적으로 조사하고 도면화하는 일이 과연 터무니없다 하겠는가?"], ["For, viewed in this light, the wonderful comparative smallness of his brain proper is more than compensated by the wonderful comparative magnitude of his spinal cord.", "이러한 관점에서 보면, 그의 본래 뇌가 상대적으로 놀랍도록 작다는 사실은, 그의 척수가 상대적으로 놀랍도록 크다는 사실로 충분히 보상되고도 남기 때문이다."]], [["But leaving this hint to operate as it may with the phrenologists, I would merely assume the spinal theory for a moment, in reference to the Sperm Whale’s hump.", "그러나 이 암시는 골상학자들이 알아서 쓰도록 남겨 두고, 나는 다만 향유고래의 혹과 관련하여 잠시 이 척추 이론을 가정해 보고자 한다."], ["This august hump, if I mistake not, rises over one of the larger vertebræ, and is, therefore, in some sort, the outer convex mould of it.", "이 위풍당당한 혹은, 내가 틀리지 않았다면, 더 큰 척추뼈들 가운데 하나 위로 솟아오른 것이며, 따라서 어떤 의미에서는 그 척추뼈의 볼록한 바깥 거푸집인 셈이다."], ["From its relative situation then, I should call this high hump the organ of firmness or indomitableness in the Sperm Whale.", "그렇다면 그 상대적 위치로 보아, 나는 이 높다란 혹을 향유고래의 굳건함 또는 불굴의 기상이 깃든 기관이라 부르고 싶다."], ["And that the great monster is indomitable, you will yet have reason to know.", "그리고 이 거대한 괴물이 굴복할 줄 모르는 존재임을, 그대는 머지않아 알게 될 까닭이 있으리라."]], [["# CHAPTER 81. The Pequod Meets The Virgin.", "# CHAPTER 81. The Pequod Meets The Virgin."]], [["The predestinated day arrived, and we duly met the ship Jungfrau, Derick De Deer, master, of Bremen.", "81장 피쿼드호, 처녀호를 만나다\n운명으로 정해진 그날이 찾아왔고, 우리는 마침내 브레멘 선적의 융프라우(Jungfrau)호를 만났다. 선장은 데리크 데 데어(Derick De Deer)였다."]], [["At one time the greatest whaling people in the world, the Dutch and Germans are now among the least; but here and there at very wide intervals of latitude and longitude, you still occasionally meet with their flag in the Pacific.", "한때 세계에서 가장 위대한 포경 민족이었던 네덜란드인과 독일인은 이제 가장 보잘것없는 축에 속한다. 그러나 위도와 경도로 멀찍멀찍 떨어진 곳에서나마, 그대는 지금도 이따금 태평양에서 그들의 깃발과 마주치게 된다."]], [["For some reason, the Jungfrau seemed quite eager to pay her respects.", "무슨 까닭에서인지 융프라우호는 우리에게 인사를 차리려고 무척 안달이 난 듯했다."], ["While yet some distance from the Pequod, she rounded to, and dropping a boat, her captain was impelled towards us, impatiently standing in the bows instead of the stern.", "아직 피쿼드(Pequod)호로부터 제법 떨어진 거리에서 그 배는 뱃머리를 돌리더니 보트 한 척을 내렸고, 그 선장은 고물이 아니라 뱃머리에 참을성 없이 버티고 선 채 우리 쪽으로 떠밀려 왔다."]], [["“What has he in his hand there?”", "\"저자가 손에 들고 있는 게 뭐지?\""], ["cried Starbuck, pointing to something wavingly held by the German.", "스타벅(Starbuck)이 독일인이 흔들흔들 들고 있는 무언가를 가리키며 외쳤다."], ["“Impossible!—a lamp-feeder!”", "\"설마—램프 기름통이라니!\""]], [["“Not that,”", "\"그건 아니지.\" 스터브(Stubb)가 말했다."], ["said Stubb, “no, no, it’s a coffee-pot, Mr.", "\"아니, 아니라고. 저건 커피포트요, 스타벅 씨."], ["Starbuck; he’s coming off to make us our coffee, is the Yarman; don’t you see that big tin can there alongside of him?—that’s his boiling water.", "저 독일 양반이 우리한테 커피를 끓여 주러 오는 거란 말이오. 저 양반 옆구리에 붙은 저 큼지막한 양철통이 안 보이시오?—저게 끓는 물이지."], ["Oh! he’s all right, is the Yarman.”", "아, 저 독일 양반, 괜찮은 사람일세.\""]], [["“Go along with you,”", "\"무슨 소리들 하는 거야.\""], ["cried Flask, “it’s a lamp-feeder and an oil-can.", "플래스크(Flask)가 외쳤다. \"저건 램프 기름통이고 기름 깡통이라고."], ["He’s out of oil, and has come a-begging.”", "기름이 다 떨어져서 동냥하러 온 거야.\""]], [["However curious it may seem for an oil-ship to be borrowing oil on the whale-ground, and however much it may invertedly contradict the old proverb about carrying coals to Newcastle, yet sometimes such a thing really happens; and in the present case Captain Derick De Deer did indubitably conduct a lamp-feeder as Flask did declare.", "기름배가 고래잡이 어장에서 기름을 빌리러 다닌다는 것이 아무리 기이하게 보일지라도, 또 그것이 뉴캐슬에 석탄을 실어 나른다는 옛 속담을 거꾸로 뒤집는 일처럼 보일지라도, 실제로 이런 일이 더러 벌어진다. 그리고 지금 이 경우에 데리크 데 데어 선장은, 플래스크가 단언한 그대로, 의심할 여지 없이 램프 기름통을 들고 있었다."]], [["As he mounted the deck, Ahab abruptly accosted him, without at all heeding what he had in his hand; but in his broken lingo, the German soon evinced his complete ignorance of the White Whale; immediately turning the conversation to his lamp-feeder and oil can, with some remarks touching his having to turn into his hammock at night in profound darkness—his last drop of Bremen oil being gone, and not a single flying-fish yet captured to supply the deficiency; concluding by hinting that his ship was indeed what in the Fishery is technically called a clean one (that is, an empty one), well deserving the name of Jungfrau or the Virgin.", "데리크가 갑판에 올라서자, 에이해브(Ahab)는 그가 손에 든 것에는 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고 불쑥 말을 걸었다. 그러나 독일인은 그 서툰 사투리로 흰 고래(White Whale)에 대해서는 아는 바가 전혀 없음을 곧바로 드러냈고, 즉시 화제를 자기 램프 기름통과 기름 깡통으로 돌리면서, 브레멘 기름의 마지막 한 방울마저 떨어진 데다 부족분을 채워 줄 날치 한 마리조차 아직 잡지 못한 탓에 밤이면 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 해먹에 기어들어야 한다는 둥 몇 마디 늘어놓았다. 그러고는 자기 배가 어업 용어로 이른바 '깨끗한(clean)' 배(다시 말해 텅 빈 배)여서, 융프라우 곧 처녀라는 이름값을 톡톡히 한다는 말로 슬쩍 끝을 맺었다."]], [["His necessities supplied, Derick departed; but he had not gained his ship’s side, when whales were almost simultaneously raised from the mast-heads of both vessels; and so eager for the chase was Derick, that without pausing to put his oil-can and lamp-feeder aboard, he slewed round his boat and made after the leviathan lamp-feeders.", "필요한 것을 얻은 데리크는 떠나갔다. 그러나 그가 미처 자기 배 옆에 다다르기도 전에, 거의 동시에 두 배의 돛대 꼭대기에서 고래가 떴다는 소리가 올랐다. 데리크는 추격에 어찌나 안달이 났던지, 기름 깡통과 램프 기름통을 배에 올려놓을 새도 없이 보트를 홱 돌려, 그 거대한 리바이어던(Leviathan) 램프 기름통을 뒤쫓았다."]], [["Now, the game having risen to leeward, he and the other three German boats that soon followed him, had considerably the start of the Pequod’s keels.", "자, 사냥감이 바람 불어가는 쪽에서 떠올랐던 터라, 그와 곧 뒤따른 다른 세 척의 독일 보트는 피쿼드호의 보트들보다 상당히 앞서 출발한 셈이었다."], ["There were eight whales, an average pod.", "고래는 모두 여덟 마리로, 평균적인 무리였다."], ["Aware of their danger, they were going all abreast with great speed straight before the wind, rubbing their flanks as closely as so many spans of horses in harness.", "자기들이 처한 위험을 알아챈 고래들은 한 줄로 나란히 늘어서서 맹렬한 속도로 바람을 정면으로 받으며 곧장 나아갔는데, 마구를 채운 여러 마리의 말들이 그러하듯 서로 옆구리를 바싹 맞댄 채였다."], ["They left a great, wide wake, as though continually unrolling a great wide parchment upon the sea.", "마치 바다 위로 거대하고 너른 양피지를 끊임없이 펼쳐 나가기라도 하듯, 그들은 크고 넓은 항적을 남겼다."]], [["Full in this rapid wake, and many fathoms in the rear, swam a huge, humped old bull, which by his comparatively slow progress, as well as by the unusual yellowish incrustations overgrowing him, seemed afflicted with the jaundice, or some other infirmity.", "그 빠른 항적 한복판에서, 여러 길이나 뒤처져, 등이 불룩 솟은 늙은 황소 한 마리가 헤엄치고 있었다. 그 비교적 느린 행보로 보나, 온몸을 뒤덮은 유난스레 누르스름한 딱지로 보나, 황달이나 다른 어떤 병에 시달리는 듯했다."], ["Whether this whale belonged to the pod in advance, seemed questionable; for it is not customary for such venerable leviathans to be at all social.", "이 고래가 앞서가는 무리에 속한 것인지는 의심스러워 보였다. 그처럼 늙고 점잖은 리바이어던이 무리와 어울리는 일은 흔치 않기 때문이다."], ["Nevertheless, he stuck to their wake, though indeed their back water must have retarded him, because the white-bone or swell at his broad muzzle was a dashed one, like the swell formed when two hostile currents meet.", "그럼에도 그는 그들의 항적에 딱 붙어 따라갔는데, 사실 그들이 일으킨 후류(後流)가 그의 속도를 늦추었을 것이 분명했다. 그의 널찍한 주둥이 앞에 이는 흰 물보라가 두 개의 적대적인 해류가 부딪칠 때 생기는 너울처럼 부서지고 있었기 때문이다."], ["His spout was short, slow, and laborious; coming forth with a choking sort of gush, and spending itself in torn shreds, followed by strange subterranean commotions in him, which seemed to have egress at his other buried extremity, causing the waters behind him to upbubble.", "그의 물줄기는 짧고 느리며 힘겨웠다. 컥컥대며 솟구쳐 나오다가는 너덜너덜 찢긴 조각이 되어 흩어졌고, 뒤이어 그의 몸속에서 기이한 지하의 진동 같은 것이 일었는데, 그것은 그의 다른 쪽, 물속에 잠긴 끄트머리로 빠져나가는 듯하여 그의 뒤편 물이 보글보글 끓어오르게 만들었다."]], [["“Who’s got some paregoric?”", "\"누구 진통제 가진 사람 없나?\""], ["said Stubb, “he has the stomach-ache, I’m afraid.", "스터브가 말했다. \"저 녀석, 배탈이 난 모양인데."], ["Lord, think of having half an acre of stomach-ache!", "세상에, 반 에이커짜리 배탈을 한번 생각해 보라고!"], ["Adverse winds are holding mad Christmas in him, boys.", "역풍이 저놈 뱃속에서 미친 듯이 크리스마스 잔치를 벌이고 있구먼, 친구들."], ["It’s the first foul wind I ever knew to blow from astern; but look, did ever whale yaw so before?", "고물 쪽에서 불어오는 역풍이라니, 내 평생 처음 보는 일일세. 그런데 보게, 고래가 저렇게까지 비틀거리며 가는 걸 누가 본 적 있나?"], ["it must be, he’s lost his tiller.”", "틀림없이 저놈 키를 잃어버린 게야.\""]], [["As an overladen Indiaman bearing down the Hindostan coast with a deck load of frightened horses, careens, buries, rolls, and wallows on her way; so did this old whale heave his aged bulk, and now and then partly turning over on his cumbrous rib-ends, expose the cause of his devious wake in the unnatural stump of his starboard fin.", "짐을 잔뜩 실은 동인도 무역선이 겁에 질린 말들을 갑판 가득 싣고 힌두스탄 해안을 따라 내려가며 기우뚱거리고, 파도에 파묻히고, 좌우로 흔들리고, 뒹굴며 나아가듯, 이 늙은 고래도 늙은 몸뚱이를 들썩이며 나아갔다. 그러다 이따금 그 육중한 갈비뼈 끄트머리 쪽으로 반쯤 몸을 뒤집으면, 그의 갈지자 항적의 원인이 드러났으니, 바로 부자연스럽게 잘려 나간 우현 지느러미였다."], ["Whether he had lost that fin in battle, or had been born without it, it were hard to say.", "그가 그 지느러미를 싸움에서 잃은 것인지, 아니면 날 때부터 없었던 것인지는 말하기 어려웠다."]], [["“Only wait a bit, old chap, and I’ll give ye a sling for that wounded arm,”", "\"조금만 기다리게, 영감, 그 다친 팔에 내가 삼각건을 매어 줄 테니.\""], ["cried cruel Flask, pointing to the whale-line near him.", "잔인한 플래스크가 곁에 놓인 고래 밧줄을 가리키며 외쳤다."]], [["“Mind he don’t sling thee with it,”", "\"저놈이 그 밧줄로 자네를 옭아매지 않게 조심하게.\""], ["cried Starbuck.", "스타벅이 외쳤다."], ["“Give way, or the German will have him.”", "\"노를 저어라, 안 그러면 독일인이 저놈을 차지하겠다.\""]], [["With one intent all the combined rival boats were pointed for this one fish, because not only was he the largest, and therefore the most valuable whale, but he was nearest to them, and the other whales were going with such great velocity, moreover, as almost to defy pursuit for the time.", "서로 경쟁하던 모든 보트가 한마음으로 이 한 마리의 고래를 겨냥했다. 그놈이 가장 클뿐더러 따라서 가장 값진 고래였기 때문만은 아니었다."], ["At this juncture the Pequod’s keels had shot by the three German boats last lowered; but from the great start he had had, Derick’s boat still led the chase, though every moment neared by his foreign rivals.", "그놈이 그들과 가장 가까이 있었던 데다, 더구나 다른 고래들은 어찌나 빠른 속도로 달아나는지 당분간은 추격조차 거의 불가능했기 때문이다. 바로 이 순간 피쿼드호의 보트들은 마지막으로 내려진 세 척의 독일 보트를 앞질러 쏜살같이 나아갔다."], ["The only thing they feared, was, that from being already so nigh to his mark, he would be enabled to dart his iron before they could completely overtake and pass him.", "그러나 워낙 앞서 출발한 까닭에 데리크의 보트는 여전히 추격의 선두를 달리고 있었다. 비록 순간순간 외국인 경쟁자들이 바짝 따라붙고는 있었지만 말이다."], ["As for Derick, he seemed quite confident that this would be the case, and occasionally with a deriding gesture shook his lamp-feeder at the other boats.", "그들이 두려워하는 단 한 가지는, 데리크가 이미 사냥감에 너무 가까이 다가가 있어, 그들이 그를 완전히 따라잡고 앞지르기도 전에 작살을 던져 버릴지도 모른다는 점이었다. 데리크로 말하자면, 그는 분명 그렇게 되리라 자신만만한 듯, 이따금 조롱하는 몸짓으로 다른 보트들을 향해 자기 램프 기름통을 흔들어 댔다."]], [["“The ungracious and ungrateful dog!”", "\"저 무례하고 배은망덕한 개 같으니!\""], ["cried Starbuck; “he mocks and dares me with the very poor-box I filled for him not five minutes ago!”—then in his old intense whisper—“Give way, greyhounds!", "스타벅이 외쳤다. \"오 분도 채 안 돼서 내가 채워 준 바로 그 빈민 구호함을 들고 나를 조롱하고 도발하다니!\"—그러고는 예의 그 격렬한 속삭임으로—\"노를 저어라, 사냥개들아!"], ["Dog to it!”", "물고 늘어져!\""]], [["“I tell ye what it is, men”—cried Stubb to his crew—“it’s against my religion to get mad; but I’d like to eat that villainous Yarman—Pull—won’t ye?", "\"내가 말하지, 이보게들\"—스터브가 자기 보트의 선원들에게 외쳤다—\"화를 내는 건 내 신조에 어긋나는 일이지만, 저 악당 같은 독일 놈은 통째로 씹어 먹고 싶군—저어라—안 저을 텐가?"], ["Are ye going to let that rascal beat ye?", "저 불한당한테 질 작정인가?"], ["Do ye love brandy?", "자네들 브랜디 좋아하지?"], ["A hogshead of brandy, then, to the best man.", "그럼 제일 잘하는 자에게 브랜디 한 통을 주겠네."], ["Come, why don’t some of ye burst a blood-vessel?", "자, 어째서 너희 중 누구도 혈관 한 가닥 안 터뜨리는 거야?"], ["Who’s that been dropping an anchor overboard—we don’t budge an inch—we’re becalmed.", "누가 닻을 바다에 떨어뜨린 거야—우린 한 치도 안 나가잖아—바람 한 점 없이 멈춰 섰다고."], ["Halloo, here’s grass growing in the boat’s bottom—and by the Lord, the mast there’s budding.", "야아, 여기 보트 바닥에 풀이 다 자라네—맙소사, 저기 돛대엔 싹이 트는구먼."], ["This won’t do, boys.", "이래선 안 되지, 친구들."], ["Look at that Yarman!", "저 독일 놈을 보라고!"], ["The short and long of it is, men, will ye spit fire or not?”", "한마디로 말해, 이보게들, 자네들 불을 뿜을 텐가 말 텐가?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["see the suds he makes!”", "저놈이 일으키는 거품 좀 보게!\""], ["cried Flask, dancing up and down—“What a hump—Oh, do pile on the beef—lays like a log!", "플래스크가 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰며 외쳤다—\"저 혹 좀 봐—오, 제발 힘 좀 더 줘 봐—통나무처럼 가만히 누워 있잖아!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["my lads, do spring—slap-jacks and quahogs for supper, you know, my lads—baked clams and muffins—oh, do, do, spring,—he’s a hundred barreller—don’t lose him now—don’t oh, don’t!—see that Yarman—Oh, won’t ye pull for your duff, my lads—such a sog!", "이보게들, 제발 좀 솟구쳐 보게—저녁에 팬케이크랑 대합 요리 먹어야지, 알잖나, 친구들—구운 조개랑 머핀 말이야—오, 제발, 제발 좀 솟구쳐 보라고,—저놈은 백 통짜리야—이제 와서 놓치지 마—놓치지 마, 오, 제발 놓치지 마!—저 독일 놈 좀 봐—오, 자네들 저녁 푸딩을 위해서라도 노를 안 저을 텐가, 이보게들—저렇게 묵직한 놈을!"], ["such a sogger!", "저렇게 둔한 놈을!"], ["Don’t ye love sperm?", "자네들 향유고래(Sperm) 좋아하지 않나?"], ["There goes three thousand dollars, men!—a bank!—a whole bank!", "저기 삼천 달러가 가고 있다고, 친구들!—은행이야!—통째로 은행이라고!"], ["The bank of England!—Oh, do, do, do!—What’s that Yarman about now?”", "잉글랜드 은행 말일세!—오, 제발, 제발, 제발 좀!—저 독일 놈은 지금 또 뭘 하는 거야?\""]], [["At this moment Derick was in the act of pitching his lamp-feeder at the advancing boats, and also his oil-can; perhaps with the double view of retarding his rivals’ way, and at the same time economically accelerating his own by the momentary impetus of the backward toss.", "이 순간 데리크는 다가오는 보트들을 향해 자기 램프 기름통을 내던지려는 참이었고, 기름 깡통도 마찬가지였다. 아마도 경쟁자들의 속도를 늦추는 동시에, 뒤로 내던지는 순간적인 반동을 이용해 알뜰하게 제 보트의 속도를 높이려는 일석이조의 속셈이었을 것이다."]], [["“The unmannerly Dutch dogger!”", "\"저 버르장머리 없는 네덜란드 똥배 같으니!\""], ["cried Stubb.", "스터브가 외쳤다."], ["“Pull now, men, like fifty thousand line-of-battle-ship loads of red-haired devils.", "\"자, 저어라, 이보게들, 빨강 머리 악마 오만 명을 실은 전열함처럼 저으란 말이다."], ["What d’ye say, Tashtego; are you the man to snap your spine in two-and-twenty pieces for the honor of old Gayhead?", "어떤가, 태시테고(Tashtego). 자네는 옛 게이헤드의 명예를 위해서라면 등뼈를 스물두 동강 낼 사내인가?"], ["What d’ye say?”", "어떤가?\""]], [["“I say, pull like god-dam,”—cried the Indian.", "\"내 말하지, 빌어먹게 저어 주겠어.\" 인디언이 외쳤다."]], [["Fiercely, but evenly incited by the taunts of the German, the Pequod’s three boats now began ranging almost abreast; and, so disposed, momentarily neared him.", "독일인의 조롱에 사납게, 그러나 한결같이 자극받은 피쿼드호의 세 보트는 이제 거의 나란히 늘어서기 시작했고, 그렇게 진을 친 채 순간순간 그에게 다가갔다."], ["In that fine, loose, chivalrous attitude of the headsman when drawing near to his prey, the three mates stood up proudly, occasionally backing the after oarsman with an exhilarating cry of, “There she slides, now!", "사냥감에 다가설 때면 으레 보이는 작살잡이 우두머리의 그 멋들어지고 느긋하며 기사다운 자세로, 세 항해사는 자랑스레 우뚝 일어섰다. 그러고는 이따금 신명 나는 고함으로 고물 쪽 노잡이의 기운을 북돋웠다."], ["Hurrah for the white-ash breeze!", "\"저기 미끄러져 간다, 이제! 백색 물푸레나무 바람 만세!"], ["Down with the Yarman!", "저 독일 놈을 내리눌러라!"], ["Sail over him!”", "저놈을 타고 넘어가라!\""]], [["But so decided an original start had Derick had, that spite of all their gallantry, he would have proved the victor in this race, had not a righteous judgment descended upon him in a crab which caught the blade of his midship oarsman.", "그러나 데리크의 출발이 워낙 결정적으로 앞서 있었던 터라, 그들의 온갖 용맹에도 불구하고 그가 이 경주의 승자가 되었을 것이다. 만일 그의 중앙 노잡이의 노가 게걸음질에 걸려들어 정의로운 심판이 그에게 내려지지 않았더라면 말이다."], ["While this clumsy lubber was striving to free his white-ash, and while, in consequence, Derick’s boat was nigh to capsizing, and he thundering away at his men in a mighty rage;—that was a good time for Starbuck, Stubb, and Flask.", "이 굼뜬 멍청이가 자기 물푸레나무 노를 빼내려고 안간힘을 쓰는 동안, 그리고 그 탓에 데리크의 보트가 뒤집힐 지경이 되어 그가 선원들에게 무서운 기세로 천둥같이 호통을 쳐 대는 동안—그것은 스타벅과 스터브와 플래스크에게는 절호의 기회였다."], ["With a shout, they took a mortal start forwards, and slantingly ranged up on the German’s quarter.", "함성과 함께 그들은 결사적으로 앞으로 치고 나가 비스듬히 독일인의 측면으로 따라붙었다."], ["An instant more, and all four boats were diagonically in the whale’s immediate wake, while stretching from them, on both sides, was the foaming swell that he made.", "다시 한순간, 네 척의 보트가 모두 고래의 바로 뒤 항적 속에 대각선으로 늘어섰고, 그들 양옆으로는 고래가 일으킨 거품 이는 너울이 길게 뻗어 나갔다."]], [["It was a terrific, most pitiable, and maddening sight.", "그것은 끔찍하고, 더없이 가엾으며, 미칠 듯한 광경이었다."], ["The whale was now going head out, and sending his spout before him in a continual tormented jet; while his one poor fin beat his side in an agony of fright.", "이제 고래는 머리를 내밀고 나아가며, 끊임없이 고통에 찬 물줄기를 제 앞으로 내뿜었다. 그러는 동안 그의 가엾은 한쪽 지느러미는 공포의 몸부림으로 옆구리를 두들겨 댔다."], ["Now to this hand, now to that, he yawed in his faltering flight, and still at every billow that he broke, he spasmodically sank in the sea, or sideways rolled towards the sky his one beating fin.", "이쪽으로, 또 저쪽으로, 그는 비틀거리는 도주 속에서 갈지자로 흔들렸고, 부서뜨리는 물결마다 발작하듯 바다 속으로 가라앉거나, 그 두들겨 대는 한쪽 지느러미를 하늘 쪽으로 옆으로 굴려 올렸다. 나는 날개가 잘린 새가 허공에서 겁에 질려 끊긴 원을 그리며, 약탈자 매들에게서 벗어나려 헛되이 애쓰는 모습을 본 적이 있다."], ["So have I seen a bird with clipped wing making affrighted broken circles in the air, vainly striving to escape the piratical hawks.", "그러나 새에게는 목소리가 있어, 애처로운 울음으로 그 두려움을 알린다. 그러나 이 거대하고 말 못 하는 바다 짐승의 두려움은 그의 몸속에 사슬로 묶이고 마법에 걸려 갇혀 있었다."], ["But the bird has a voice, and with plaintive cries will make known her fear; but the fear of this vast dumb brute of the sea, was chained up and enchanted in him; he had no voice, save that choking respiration through his spiracle, and this made the sight of him unspeakably pitiable; while still, in his amazing bulk, portcullis jaw, and omnipotent tail, there was enough to appal the stoutest man who so pitied.", "분수공을 통해 컥컥대는 호흡을 내뱉는 것 말고는 그에게 목소리가 없었고, 그래서 그의 모습은 이루 말할 수 없이 가엾어 보였다. 그러면서도 그의 놀라운 거구와, 성문(城門) 같은 턱과, 전능한 꼬리에는, 그토록 그를 가엾이 여기는 가장 담대한 사내조차 질겁하게 할 만한 것이 충분히 남아 있었다."]], [["Seeing now that but a very few moments more would give the Pequod’s boats the advantage, and rather than be thus foiled of his game, Derick chose to hazard what to him must have seemed a most unusually long dart, ere the last chance would for ever escape.", "이제 몇 순간만 더 지나면 피쿼드호의 보트들에게 유리한 형세가 되리라는 것을 알아챈 데리크는, 이렇게 사냥감을 놓치느니, 마지막 기회가 영영 달아나기 전에 그에게는 유난히 멀게 느껴졌을 것이 분명한 작살 던지기를 한번 감행해 보기로 했다."]], [["But no sooner did his harpooneer stand up for the stroke, than all three tigers—Queequeg, Tashtego, Daggoo—instinctively sprang to their feet, and standing in a diagonal row, simultaneously pointed their barbs; and darted over the head of the German harpooneer, their three Nantucket irons entered the whale.", "그러나 그의 작살잡이가 한 방 던지려고 일어서기가 무섭게, 세 마리 호랑이—퀴케그(Queequeg), 태시테고, 다구—가 본능적으로 벌떡 일어나, 대각선으로 한 줄을 이루어 동시에 미늘을 겨누었다. 그리고 독일인 작살잡이의 머리 너머로, 그들의 낸터킷(Nantucket) 작살 셋이 고래에게 박혀 들어갔다."], ["Blinding vapors of foam and white-fire!", "눈을 멀게 하는 거품과 흰 불꽃의 안개!"], ["The three boats, in the first fury of the whale’s headlong rush, bumped the German’s aside with such force, that both Derick and his baffled harpooneer were spilled out, and sailed over by the three flying keels.", "고래가 곤두박질치듯 돌진하는 첫 격분의 순간, 세 보트는 독일인의 보트를 어찌나 세게 들이받았던지, 데리크와 헛물켠 그의 작살잡이는 모두 밖으로 튕겨 나가고, 날아가는 세 척의 보트가 그들 위를 타고 넘어갔다."]], [["“Don’t be afraid, my butter-boxes,”", "\"겁먹지 마라, 내 버터통 친구들아.\""], ["cried Stubb, casting a passing glance upon them as he shot by; “ye’ll be picked up presently—all right—I saw some sharks astern—St.", "스터브가 쏜살같이 지나가며 그들에게 흘깃 눈길을 던지고 외쳤다. \"곧 누가 건져 줄 거야—괜찮아—고물 쪽에 상어 몇 마리가 보이더군—세인트버나드 개들 알잖나—조난당한 나그네를 구해 주지."], ["Bernard’s dogs, you know—relieve distressed travellers.", "만세! 이게 바로 항해하는 맛이지."], ["Hurrah! this is the way to sail now.", "보트마다 햇살 한 줄기로구나!"], ["Every keel a sunbeam! Hurrah!—Here we go like three tin kettles at the tail of a mad cougar!", "만세!—미쳐 날뛰는 쿠거 꼬리에 매달린 양철 주전자 세 개처럼 우리가 간다!"], ["This puts me in mind of fastening to an elephant in a tilbury on a plain—makes the wheel-spokes fly, boys, when you fasten to him that way; and there’s danger of being pitched out too, when you strike a hill.", "이러니 평원에서 이륜마차를 타고 코끼리에 작살을 박은 일이 생각나는군—그런 식으로 매달리면 바퀴살이 휙휙 날아가지, 친구들. 게다가 언덕을 칠 때면 밖으로 내동댕이쳐질 위험도 있고."], ["Hurrah!", "만세!"], ["this is the way a fellow feels when he’s going to Davy Jones—all a rush down an endless inclined plane!", "이게 바로 데이비 존스한테 가는 사내의 기분이라고—끝없는 비탈을 곤두박질쳐 내려가는 거지!"], ["Hurrah!", "만세!"], ["this whale carries the everlasting mail!”", "이 고래가 영원의 우편물을 나르는구나!\""]], [["But the monster’s run was a brief one.", "그러나 그 괴물의 질주는 짧았다."], ["Giving a sudden gasp, he tumultuously sounded.", "갑자기 숨을 헐떡이더니, 그는 요란하게 잠수했다."], ["With a grating rush, the three lines flew round the loggerheads with such a force as to gouge deep grooves in them; while so fearful were the harpooneers that this rapid sounding would soon exhaust the lines, that using all their dexterous might, they caught repeated smoking turns with the rope to hold on; till at last—owing to the perpendicular strain from the lead-lined chocks of the boats, whence the three ropes went straight down into the blue—the gunwales of the bows were almost even with the water, while the three sterns tilted high in the air.", "세 가닥의 밧줄이 삐거덕대며 로것헤드를 휘감고 어찌나 세게 돌아갔던지 거기에 깊은 홈이 패였다. 작살잡이들은 이 빠른 잠수가 곧 밧줄을 다 풀어내 버릴까 봐 어찌나 두려웠던지, 가진 솜씨와 힘을 다해 연거푸 밧줄을 로것헤드에 감아—연기가 날 만큼—붙들고 늘어졌다."], ["And the whale soon ceasing to sound, for some time they remained in that attitude, fearful of expending more line, though the position was a little ticklish.", "그리하여 마침내—보트의 납을 댄 밧줄받이에서 세 가닥 밧줄이 수직으로 곧장 푸른 바다 속으로 뻗어 내려가며 수직으로 잡아당기는 힘 때문에—뱃머리의 뱃전은 거의 수면과 나란해진 반면, 세 척의 고물은 허공 높이 치켜들렸다."], ["But though boats have been taken down and lost in this way, yet it is this “holding on,”", "그러나 고래가 곧 잠수를 멈춘 덕에, 한동안 그들은 그 자세 그대로 머물렀다."], ["as it is called; this hooking up by the sharp barbs of his live flesh from the back; this it is that often torments the Leviathan into soon rising again to meet the sharp lance of his foes.", "자세가 다소 아슬아슬하기는 했어도, 밧줄을 더 풀어 쓸까 두려웠던 것이다. 그러나 이런 식으로 보트가 끌려 내려가 잃은 일이 있다 해도, 이른바 이 '버티기'야말로, 곧 살아 있는 그의 등살에 날카로운 미늘을 박아 걸어 두는 이것이야말로, 흔히 리바이어던을 고통스럽게 하여 곧 다시 떠올라 적들의 날카로운 창을 마주하게 만드는 것이다."], ["Yet not to speak of the peril of the thing, it is to be doubted whether this course is always the best; for it is but reasonable to presume, that the longer the stricken whale stays under water, the more he is exhausted.", "그러나 그 일의 위험은 차치하더라도, 이 방법이 늘 최선인지는 의문이다. 작살에 찔린 고래가 물속에 오래 머물수록 더 지치리라고 보는 것이 이치에 맞기 때문이다."], ["Because, owing to the enormous surface of him—in a full grown sperm whale something less than 2000 square feet—the pressure of the water is immense.", "그의 표면적이 엄청난 까닭에—다 자란 향유고래라면 2천 제곱피트(약 186제곱미터)에 조금 못 미친다—물의 압력이 어마어마하다."], ["We all know what an astonishing atmospheric weight we ourselves stand up under; even here, above-ground, in the air; how vast, then, the burden of a whale, bearing on his back a column of two hundred fathoms of ocean!", "우리는 우리 자신이 얼마나 놀라운 대기의 무게를 떠받치고 서 있는지 익히 알고 있다. 여기, 땅 위 공기 속에서조차 그러한데, 그렇다면 이백 길(약 366미터)에 달하는 바닷물 기둥을 등에 짊어진 고래의 부담은 얼마나 거대하겠는가!"], ["It must at least equal the weight of fifty atmospheres.", "그것은 적어도 오십 기압의 무게에 맞먹을 것이 분명하다."], ["One whaleman has estimated it at the weight of twenty line-of-battle ships, with all their guns, and stores, and men on board.", "어느 고래잡이는 그것을, 대포와 비축품과 승무원을 모두 실은 전열함 스무 척의 무게로 어림했다."]], [["As the three boats lay there on that gently rolling sea, gazing down into its eternal blue noon; and as not a single groan or cry of any sort, nay, not so much as a ripple or a bubble came up from its depths; what landsman would have thought, that beneath all that silence and placidity, the utmost monster of the seas was writhing and wrenching in agony!", "세 척의 보트가 잔잔히 일렁이는 그 바다 위에 떠서, 그 영원한 푸른 정오의 심연을 내려다보고 있을 때, 그리고 그 깊은 곳에서 신음 한 자락, 비명 한 자락은커녕 잔물결 하나, 거품 한 방울조차 올라오지 않을 때, 그 모든 고요와 평온 아래에서 바다의 가장 거대한 괴물이 고통에 몸부림치며 뒤틀고 있으리라고 어느 뭍사람이 생각이나 했겠는가!"], ["Not eight inches of perpendicular rope were visible at the bows.", "뱃머리에는 수직으로 드리운 밧줄이 여덟 인치(약 20센티미터)도 채 보이지 않았다."], ["Seems it credible that by three such thin threads the great Leviathan was suspended like the big weight to an eight day clock.", "그토록 가느다란 세 가닥 실에 저 거대한 리바이어던이 마치 팔일짜리 괘종시계의 묵직한 추처럼 매달려 있다는 것이 믿어지겠는가."], ["Suspended?", "매달려 있다고?"], ["and to what?", "그것도 무엇에?"], ["To three bits of board.", "널빤지 세 조각에."], ["Is this the creature of whom it was once so triumphantly said—“Canst thou fill his skin with barbed irons? or his head with fish-spears?", "이것이 한때 그토록 의기양양하게 일컬어진 그 피조물이란 말인가—\"네가 능히 미늘 박힌 작살로 그 가죽을 채우며, 물고기 창으로 그 머리를 채우겠느냐?"], ["The sword of him that layeth at him cannot hold, the spear, the dart, nor the habergeon: he esteemeth iron as straw; the arrow cannot make him flee; darts are counted as stubble; he laugheth at the shaking of a spear!”", "그를 치는 자의 칼도 소용없으며, 창이나 투창이나 갑옷도 마찬가지라. 그는 쇠를 지푸라기로 여기고, 화살로도 그를 달아나게 하지 못하며, 투창은 그루터기로 여기고, 창을 휘둘러도 그는 비웃느니라!\""], ["This the creature?", "이것이 그 피조물이란 말인가?"], ["this he? Oh!", "이것이 그란 말인가?"], ["that unfulfilments should follow the prophets.", "오! 예언이 어찌하여 이렇듯 어긋나야만 하는가."], ["For with the strength of a thousand thighs in his tail, Leviathan had run his head under the mountains of the sea, to hide him from the Pequod’s fish-spears!", "천 개의 넓적다리에 맞먹는 힘을 꼬리에 지닌 리바이어던이, 피쿼드호의 물고기 창을 피해 제 머리를 바닷속 산더미 아래로 처박았으니 말이다!"]], [["In that sloping afternoon sunlight, the shadows that the three boats sent down beneath the surface, must have been long enough and broad enough to shade half Xerxes’ army.", "그 비스듬히 기우는 오후의 햇살 속에서, 세 보트가 수면 아래로 드리운 그림자는 크세르크세스 군대의 절반을 그늘지게 할 만큼 길고도 넓었을 것이 분명하다."], ["Who can tell how appalling to the wounded whale must have been such huge phantoms flitting over his head!", "그토록 거대한 유령들이 제 머리 위로 어른거리는 것이, 상처 입은 고래에게는 얼마나 끔찍하게 느껴졌을지 그 누가 알겠는가!"]], [["“Stand by, men; he stirs,”", "\"준비해라, 친구들. 저놈이 움직인다.\""], ["cried Starbuck, as the three lines suddenly vibrated in the water, distinctly conducting upwards to them, as by magnetic wires, the life and death throbs of the whale, so that every oarsman felt them in his seat.", "스타벅이 외쳤다. 세 가닥 밧줄이 별안간 물속에서 부르르 떨더니, 마치 자기(磁氣)를 띤 전선처럼 고래의 삶과 죽음의 박동을 그들에게로 또렷이 전해 올렸고, 그리하여 노잡이들은 저마다 자기 자리에서 그것을 느꼈다."], ["The next moment, relieved in great part from the downward strain at the bows, the boats gave a sudden bounce upwards, as a small icefield will, when a dense herd of white bears are scared from it into the sea.", "다음 순간, 뱃머리를 끌어내리던 힘이 상당 부분 풀리면서 보트들은 별안간 위로 튀어 올랐다. 마치 빽빽이 모여 있던 흰곰 떼가 겁에 질려 바다로 뛰어들 때 작은 빙원(氷原)이 솟아오르듯이."]], [["“Haul in!", "\"끌어당겨라!"], ["Haul in!”", "끌어당겨!\""], ["cried Starbuck again; “he’s rising.”", "스타벅이 다시 외쳤다. \"저놈이 떠오른다.\""]], [["The lines, of which, hardly an instant before, not one hand’s breadth could have been gained, were now in long quick coils flung back all dripping into the boats, and soon the whale broke water within two ship’s lengths of the hunters.", "조금 전만 해도 한 뼘조차 당겨 들일 수 없었던 밧줄이, 이제는 길게 휘감기며 물을 뚝뚝 흘린 채 잽싸게 보트 안으로 던져 들여졌다. 그리고 곧 고래가 사냥꾼들로부터 배 길이로 두 척쯤 되는 거리 안에서 물을 가르며 솟아올랐다."]], [["His motions plainly denoted his extreme exhaustion.", "그의 움직임은 그가 극도로 지쳐 있음을 또렷이 드러냈다."], ["In most land animals there are certain valves or flood-gates in many of their veins, whereby when wounded, the blood is in some degree at least instantly shut off in certain directions.", "대부분의 육지 동물에게는 여러 혈관에 일종의 판막, 곧 수문(水門)이 있어서, 상처를 입으면 적어도 어느 방향으로는 즉시 어느 정도 피가 막힌다. 그러나 고래는 그렇지 않다."], ["Not so with the whale; one of whose peculiarities it is to have an entire non-valvular structure of the blood-vessels, so that when pierced even by so small a point as a harpoon, a deadly drain is at once begun upon his whole arterial system; and when this is heightened by the extraordinary pressure of water at a great distance below the surface, his life may be said to pour from him in incessant streams.", "그의 특이한 점 가운데 하나가 바로 혈관이 통째로 판막이 없는 구조라는 것이어서, 작살처럼 그토록 작은 끝에 찔리기만 해도 그 즉시 온몸의 동맥계 전체로부터 치명적인 출혈이 시작된다. 게다가 수면 아래 한참 깊은 곳에서 받는 어마어마한 물의 압력이 여기에 더해지면, 그의 생명은 끊임없는 줄기가 되어 그에게서 쏟아져 나온다고 할 만하다."], ["Yet so vast is the quantity of blood in him, and so distant and numerous its interior fountains, that he will keep thus bleeding and bleeding for a considerable period; even as in a drought a river will flow, whose source is in the well-springs of far-off and undiscernible hills.", "그러나 그의 몸속에는 피의 양이 워낙 방대하고, 그 안의 샘들이 워낙 멀고 또 수없이 많은 까닭에, 그는 이렇게 피를 흘리고 또 흘리면서도 상당히 오랜 기간을 버틴다. 마치 가뭄 속에서도, 멀고 보이지 않는 언덕의 깊은 샘에 그 원천을 둔 강이 끊임없이 흐르듯이."], ["Even now, when the boats pulled upon this whale, and perilously drew over his swaying flukes, and the lances were darted into him, they were followed by steady jets from the new made wound, which kept continually playing, while the natural spout-hole in his head was only at intervals, however rapid, sending its affrighted moisture into the air.", "지금도, 보트들이 이 고래에게 노 저어 다가가, 좌우로 흔들리는 그의 꼬리지느러미 위로 위태롭게 끌어 올라가, 그에게 창을 꽂아 넣었을 때, 새로 낸 상처에서는 줄곧 일정한 핏줄기가 쉴 새 없이 뿜어 나왔다. 반면 그의 머리에 난 본래의 분수공은, 아무리 빠르다 한들 간헐적으로만 그 겁에 질린 물기를 허공으로 내뿜을 뿐이었다."], ["From this last vent no blood yet came, because no vital part of him had thus far been struck.", "이 마지막 구멍에서는 아직 피가 나오지 않았으니, 그의 급소가 여태 어느 곳도 찔리지 않은 까닭이었다."], ["His life, as they significantly call it, was untouched.", "그들이 의미심장하게 일컫는 그의 '생명'은 아직 건드려지지 않았다."]], [["As the boats now more closely surrounded him, the whole upper part of his form, with much of it that is ordinarily submerged, was plainly revealed.", "이제 보트들이 그를 더 바싹 둘러싸자, 평소에는 잠겨 있는 부분까지 포함해 그의 몸 윗부분 전체가 또렷이 드러났다."], ["His eyes, or rather the places where his eyes had been, were beheld.", "그의 두 눈이, 아니 차라리 그의 두 눈이 있던 자리가 보였다."], ["As strange misgrown masses gather in the knot-holes of the noblest oaks when prostrate, so from the points which the whale’s eyes had once occupied, now protruded blind bulbs, horribly pitiable to see.", "가장 고귀한 떡갈나무라도 쓰러지고 나면 그 옹이구멍에 기이하게 잘못 자란 혹들이 모여들듯, 한때 고래의 두 눈이 차지했던 자리에서도 이제는 멀어 버린 둥근 혹들이 불거져 나와, 차마 볼 수 없을 만큼 가엾었다."], ["But pity there was none.", "그러나 연민이라고는 없었다."], ["For all his old age, and his one arm, and his blind eyes, he must die the death and be murdered, in order to light the gay bridals and other merry-makings of men, and also to illuminate the solemn churches that preach unconditional inoffensiveness by all to all.", "그 늙은 나이에도, 한쪽 팔뿐인 몸으로도, 멀어 버린 두 눈으로도, 그는 죽음을 맞고 살해당해야만 했다. 인간의 흥겨운 혼례와 그 밖의 온갖 잔치를 환히 밝히기 위해, 또한 너나없이 모두에게 절대적인 무해함을 설교하는 저 엄숙한 교회들을 비추기 위해서."], ["Still rolling in his blood, at last he partially disclosed a strangely discoloured bunch or protuberance, the size of a bushel, low down on the flank.", "여전히 제 피 속에서 뒹굴던 그는, 마침내 옆구리 아래쪽에서 부셸 한 말 크기의, 기이하게 변색된 덩어리, 곧 혹 같은 것을 슬며시 드러냈다."]], [["“A nice spot,”", "\"좋은 자리로군.\""], ["cried Flask; “just let me prick him there once.”", "플래스크가 외쳤다. \"저기 한 번만 찔러 보게 해 주시오.\""]], [["“Avast!”", "\"멈춰!\""], ["cried Starbuck, “there’s no need of that!”", "스타벅이 외쳤다. \"그럴 필요 없다!\""]], [["But humane Starbuck was too late.", "그러나 인정 많은 스타벅의 말은 너무 늦었다."], ["At the instant of the dart an ulcerous jet shot from this cruel wound, and goaded by it into more than sufferable anguish, the whale now spouting thick blood, with swift fury blindly darted at the craft, bespattering them and their glorying crews all over with showers of gore, capsizing Flask’s boat and marring the bows.", "작살이 꽂히는 순간, 이 잔인한 상처에서 곪은 핏줄기가 솟구쳤고, 거기에 자극받아 참을 수 있는 한계를 넘는 고통에 빠진 고래는, 이제 걸쭉한 피를 내뿜으며 맹렬한 분노로 보트들을 향해 눈먼 채 돌진했다. 그리하여 보트들과 그 위에서 의기양양해하던 선원들을 온통 핏줄기로 흠뻑 적시고, 플래스크의 보트를 뒤집어엎으며 그 뱃머리를 망가뜨렸다."], ["It was his death stroke.", "그것이 그의 죽음의 일격이었다."], ["For, by this time, so spent was he by loss of blood, that he helplessly rolled away from the wreck he had made; lay panting on his side, impotently flapped with his stumped fin, then over and over slowly revolved like a waning world; turned up the white secrets of his belly; lay like a log, and died.", "이때쯤 그는 피를 너무 많이 흘려 기진맥진한 터라, 제가 만든 잔해로부터 힘없이 뒹굴어 물러났다. 그러고는 옆으로 누워 헐떡이며, 잘린 지느러미를 무력하게 퍼덕이다가, 이지러져 가는 세계처럼 천천히 거듭거듭 돌아 뒤집히더니, 제 배의 하얀 비밀을 위로 뒤집어 드러낸 채, 통나무처럼 누워 죽었다."], ["It was most piteous, that last expiring spout.", "그 마지막 숨이 다하는 물줄기는 더없이 가엾었다."], ["As when by unseen hands the water is gradually drawn off from some mighty fountain, and with half-stifled melancholy gurglings the spray-column lowers and lowers to the ground—so the last long dying spout of the whale.", "보이지 않는 손에 이끌려 어느 거대한 샘에서 물이 차츰 빠져나가며, 반쯤 막힌 듯한 구슬픈 콸콸 소리와 함께 그 물보라 기둥이 점점 더 낮게 땅으로 가라앉듯—고래의 그 마지막 길게 죽어 가는 물줄기도 그러했다."]], [["Soon, while the crews were awaiting the arrival of the ship, the body showed symptoms of sinking with all its treasures unrifled.", "이윽고 선원들이 본선이 오기를 기다리는 동안, 그 몸뚱이는 보물을 하나도 약탈당하지 않은 채로 가라앉을 조짐을 보였다."], ["Immediately, by Starbuck’s orders, lines were secured to it at different points, so that ere long every boat was a buoy; the sunken whale being suspended a few inches beneath them by the cords.", "스타벅의 명에 따라 즉시 여러 지점에 밧줄을 묶었고, 그리하여 머지않아 보트마다 하나의 부표가 되었다. 가라앉은 고래는 밧줄에 매달려 그들 아래 몇 인치쯤 떨어진 곳에 떠 있었다."], ["By very heedful management, when the ship drew nigh, the whale was transferred to her side, and was strongly secured there by the stiffest fluke-chains, for it was plain that unless artificially upheld, the body would at once sink to the bottom.", "본선이 다가오자 매우 신중한 조작 끝에 고래는 본선 옆구리로 옮겨졌고, 가장 튼튼한 꼬리지느러미 사슬로 단단히 묶였다. 인위적으로 떠받치지 않으면 그 몸뚱이가 당장 바닥으로 가라앉으리라는 것이 분명했기 때문이다."]], [["It so chanced that almost upon first cutting into him with the spade, the entire length of a corroded harpoon was found imbedded in his flesh, on the lower part of the bunch before described.", "그런데 우연히도, 삽으로 그를 처음 가르기 시작하자마자, 앞서 말한 혹의 아래쪽 살 속에 부식된 작살 한 자루가 통째로 박혀 있는 것이 발견되었다."], ["But as the stumps of harpoons are frequently found in the dead bodies of captured whales, with the flesh perfectly healed around them, and no prominence of any kind to denote their place; therefore, there must needs have been some other unknown reason in the present case fully to account for the ulceration alluded to.", "그러나 잡힌 고래의 사체에서 작살 토막이 발견되는 일은 흔하고, 그 둘레의 살이 완전히 아물어 그 자리를 가리킬 만한 어떤 돌기도 없는 경우가 보통이다. 그러므로 지금 이 경우에는, 앞서 말한 궤양을 온전히 설명하려면 무언가 다른 알 수 없는 까닭이 있었음에 틀림없다."], ["But still more curious was the fact of a lance-head of stone being found in him, not far from the buried iron, the flesh perfectly firm about it.", "그러나 더더욱 기이한 것은, 박혀 있던 그 작살에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 돌로 된 창끝 하나가 그의 몸속에서, 그 둘레의 살이 완전히 단단하게 아문 채로 발견되었다는 사실이다."], ["Who had darted that stone lance?", "누가 그 돌창을 던졌을까?"], ["And when?", "그리고 언제?"], ["It might have been darted by some Nor’ West Indian long before America was discovered.", "어쩌면 그것은 아메리카가 발견되기 훨씬 전에 어느 북서부 인디언이 던졌는지도 모른다."]], [["What other marvels might have been rummaged out of this monstrous cabinet there is no telling.", "이 괴물 같은 진열장에서 또 어떤 경이로운 것들이 들춰져 나왔을지는 알 길이 없다."], ["But a sudden stop was put to further discoveries, by the ship’s being unprecedentedly dragged over sideways to the sea, owing to the body’s immensely increasing tendency to sink.", "그러나 몸뚱이가 가라앉으려는 경향이 어마어마하게 커진 탓에 본선이 전에 없이 옆으로 끌려 바다 쪽으로 기울면서, 더 이상의 발견에는 돌연 제동이 걸렸다. 그러나 일을 지휘하던 스타벅은 끝까지 그것을 붙들고 늘어졌다."], ["However, Starbuck, who had the ordering of affairs, hung on to it to the last; hung on to it so resolutely, indeed, that when at length the ship would have been capsized, if still persisting in locking arms with the body; then, when the command was given to break clear from it, such was the immovable strain upon the timber-heads to which the fluke-chains and cables were fastened, that it was impossible to cast them off.", "어찌나 단호하게 매달렸던지, 마침내 그 몸뚱이와 팔짱을 낀 채로 계속 버텼다면 본선이 뒤집혔을 지경이었다. 그제야 그것을 끊어 내라는 명령이 떨어졌으나, 꼬리지느러미 사슬과 밧줄이 묶인 횡재(橫材) 머리에 걸린 장력이 어찌나 꿈쩍도 하지 않던지, 그것들을 풀어 던지기란 불가능했다."], ["Meantime everything in the Pequod was aslant.", "그러는 사이 피쿼드호의 모든 것이 기울어 있었다."], ["To cross to the other side of the deck was like walking up the steep gabled roof of a house.", "갑판 반대편으로 건너가는 것은 가파른 박공지붕을 타고 올라가는 것이나 다름없었다."], ["The ship groaned and gasped.", "배가 신음하고 헐떡였다."], ["Many of the ivory inlayings of her bulwarks and cabins were started from their places, by the unnatural dislocation.", "부자연스럽게 어긋난 탓에, 뱃전과 선실에 상아로 박아 넣은 장식들이 여럿 제자리에서 튀어나왔다."], ["In vain handspikes and crows were brought to bear upon the immovable fluke-chains, to pry them adrift from the timberheads; and so low had the whale now settled that the submerged ends could not be at all approached, while every moment whole tons of ponderosity seemed added to the sinking bulk, and the ship seemed on the point of going over.", "횡재 머리에서 꼬리지느러미 사슬을 비틀어 떼어 내려고 지렛대와 쇠지레를 가져다 댔으나 헛수고였다. 게다가 고래가 이제 너무 낮게 가라앉은 탓에 물에 잠긴 끄트머리에는 손도 댈 수 없었고, 그러는 매 순간 가라앉는 몸뚱이에는 통째로 몇 톤씩의 무게가 더해지는 듯하여, 배는 막 뒤집힐 듯 위태로웠다."]], [["“Hold on, hold on, won’t ye?”", "\"붙어 있어라, 붙어 있어, 좀 그래 주겠나?\""], ["cried Stubb to the body, “don’t be in such a devil of a hurry to sink!", "스터브가 그 몸뚱이를 향해 외쳤다. \"그렇게 미친 듯이 서둘러 가라앉지 좀 말라고!"], ["By thunder, men, we must do something or go for it.", "천둥 맞을, 이보게들, 우린 뭐라도 하지 않으면 함께 끝장이야. 거기 지렛대질해 봐야 소용없어."], ["No use prying there; avast, I say with your handspikes, and run one of ye for a prayer book and a pen-knife, and cut the big chains.”", "멈춰, 그 지렛대질 좀 그만두라니까. 그리고 누구든 하나 달려가서 기도서랑 주머니칼을 가져와, 저 큰 사슬을 끊어 버려.\""]], [["“Knife?", "\"칼?"], ["Aye, aye,”", "알았어, 알았다고.\""], ["cried Queequeg, and seizing the carpenter’s heavy hatchet, he leaned out of a porthole, and steel to iron, began slashing at the largest fluke-chains.", "퀴케그가 외치고는, 목수의 묵직한 손도끼를 움켜쥐고 현창 밖으로 몸을 내밀어, 쇠에 강철을 대고 가장 큰 꼬리지느러미 사슬을 후려치기 시작했다."], ["But a few strokes, full of sparks, were given, when the exceeding strain effected the rest.", "그러나 불꽃을 튀기며 몇 차례 내리쳤을 뿐인데, 어마어마한 장력이 나머지를 해치웠다."], ["With a terrific snap, every fastening went adrift; the ship righted, the carcase sank.", "무시무시한 소리와 함께 모든 결박이 끊겨 나갔고, 배는 똑바로 섰으며, 사체는 가라앉았다."]], [["Now, this occasional inevitable sinking of the recently killed Sperm Whale is a very curious thing; nor has any fisherman yet adequately accounted for it.", "자, 갓 죽인 향유고래가 이렇게 이따금 어쩔 수 없이 가라앉는 것은 매우 기이한 일이다. 어떤 고래잡이도 아직 그것을 제대로 설명하지 못했다."], ["Usually the dead Sperm Whale floats with great buoyancy, with its side or belly considerably elevated above the surface.", "보통 죽은 향유고래는 옆구리나 배를 수면 위로 상당히 들어 올린 채 큰 부력으로 떠 있다."], ["If the only whales that thus sank were old, meagre, and broken-hearted creatures, their pads of lard diminished and all their bones heavy and rheumatic; then you might with some reason assert that this sinking is caused by an uncommon specific gravity in the fish so sinking, consequent upon this absence of buoyant matter in him.", "만일 이렇게 가라앉는 고래가 늙고 비쩍 마르고 가슴이 무너진 놈들뿐이어서, 비곗덩이는 줄어들고 뼈는 죄다 무겁고 류머티즘에 걸린 것들뿐이라면, 그런 식으로 가라앉는 물고기에게 부력을 지닌 물질이 없는 탓에 유난히 비중이 높아 가라앉는 것이라고 어느 정도 이치에 맞게 주장할 수 있을 것이다."], ["But it is not so.", "그러나 그렇지 않다."], ["For young whales, in the highest health, and swelling with noble aspirations, prematurely cut off in the warm flush and May of life, with all their panting lard about them; even these brawny, buoyant heroes do sometimes sink.", "더없이 건강하고 고귀한 포부로 부풀어 오른 젊은 고래들, 생애의 따뜻한 한창때이자 오월 같은 시절에 때 이르게 잘려 나간, 온몸에 팽팽한 비계를 두른 그놈들, 이 건장하고 부력 넘치는 영웅들조차도 더러 가라앉는다."]], [["Be it said, however, that the Sperm Whale is far less liable to this accident than any other species.", "그러나 향유고래는 다른 어떤 종(種)보다 이런 사고를 당할 가능성이 훨씬 적다는 점은 말해 두어야겠다."], ["Where one of that sort go down, twenty Right Whales do.", "그 종류 하나가 가라앉을 때, 참고래(Right Whale)는 스무 마리가 가라앉는다."], ["This difference in the species is no doubt imputable in no small degree to the greater quantity of bone in the Right Whale; his Venetian blinds alone sometimes weighing more than a ton; from this incumbrance the Sperm Whale is wholly free.", "종에 따른 이 차이는 의심할 바 없이 적잖이 참고래의 뼈가 더 많은 데서 비롯된다. 참고래의 베네치아식 발(고래수염) 하나만 해도 때로 일 톤이 넘게 나가는데, 향유고래는 이런 짐에서 완전히 자유롭다."], ["But there are instances where, after the lapse of many hours or several days, the sunken whale again rises, more buoyant than in life.", "그러나 여러 시간 혹은 며칠이 지난 뒤에, 가라앉았던 고래가 살아 있을 때보다 더 큰 부력으로 다시 떠오르는 경우도 있다."], ["But the reason of this is obvious.", "그러나 그 까닭은 명백하다."], ["Gases are generated in him; he swells to a prodigious magnitude; becomes a sort of animal balloon.", "그의 몸속에서 가스가 생겨나, 그가 엄청난 크기로 부풀어 일종의 동물 풍선이 되는 것이다."], ["A line-of-battle ship could hardly keep him under then.", "그때는 전열함으로도 그를 좀처럼 물밑에 눌러 두지 못한다."], ["In the Shore Whaling, on soundings, among the Bays of New Zealand, when a Right Whale gives token of sinking, they fasten buoys to him, with plenty of rope; so that when the body has gone down, they know where to look for it when it shall have ascended again.", "뉴질랜드의 만(灣)들 사이, 측심줄이 닿는 연안 포경에서는, 참고래가 가라앉을 기미를 보이면 밧줄을 넉넉히 매단 부표를 그에게 묶어 둔다. 그리하여 몸뚱이가 가라앉아도, 그것이 다시 떠오를 때 어디서 찾아야 할지를 알아 두는 것이다."]], [["It was not long after the sinking of the body that a cry was heard from the Pequod’s mast-heads, announcing that the Jungfrau was again lowering her boats; though the only spout in sight was that of a Fin-Back, belonging to the species of uncapturable whales, because of its incredible power of swimming.", "몸뚱이가 가라앉고 얼마 지나지 않아, 피쿼드호의 돛대 꼭대기에서 외침이 들려왔으니, 융프라우호가 다시 보트들을 내리고 있다는 소식이었다. 비록 눈에 보이는 물줄기라고는 핀백(Fin-Back) 한 마리의 것뿐이었지만 말이다."], ["Nevertheless, the Fin-Back’s spout is so similar to the Sperm Whale’s, that by unskilful fishermen it is often mistaken for it.", "핀백은 도무지 믿기 어려운 헤엄 실력 탓에 잡을 수 없는 고래 종류에 속한다. 그럼에도 핀백의 물줄기는 향유고래의 것과 어찌나 비슷한지, 솜씨 없는 고래잡이들은 흔히 그것을 향유고래로 착각한다."], ["And consequently Derick and all his host were now in valiant chase of this unnearable brute.", "그리하여 데리크와 그의 무리는 이제 이 다가갈 수 없는 짐승을 용감하게 쫓고 있었다."], ["The Virgin crowding all sail, made after her four young keels, and thus they all disappeared far to leeward, still in bold, hopeful chase.", "처녀호는 돛을 잔뜩 펼치고 제 네 척의 어린 보트를 뒤따랐고, 그렇게 그들은 모두 바람 불어가는 쪽 저 멀리로 사라져 갔으니, 여전히 대담하고도 희망에 찬 추격이었다."]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["many are the Fin-Backs, and many are the Dericks, my friend.", "핀백은 많고도 많으며, 데리크 또한 많고도 많다네, 나의 벗이여."]], [["# CHAPTER 82. The Honor and Glory of Whaling.", "# CHAPTER 82. The Honor and Glory of Whaling."]], [["There are some enterprises in which a careful disorderliness is the true method.", "세상에는 세심한 무질서야말로 참된 방법이 되는 일들이 있다."]], [["The more I dive into this matter of whaling, and push my researches up to the very spring-head of it so much the more am I impressed with its great honorableness and antiquity; and especially when I find so many great demi-gods and heroes, prophets of all sorts, who one way or other have shed distinction upon it, I am transported with the reflection that I myself belong, though but subordinately, to so emblazoned a fraternity.", "이 고래잡이라는 문제 속으로 깊이 잠수해 들어가, 그 연구를 바로 그 원천의 샘머리에까지 밀고 올라갈수록, 나는 그 위대한 명예로움과 유구함에 더욱더 깊은 감명을 받는다. 더욱이 온갖 부류의 위대한 반신(半神)과 영웅들, 예언자들이 이런저런 방식으로 이 일에 영예를 더해 주었음을 알게 될 때면, 나 자신도 비록 말단에 지나지 않으나 그토록 찬란히 빛나는 형제단의 일원이라는 생각에 황홀해진다."]], [["The gallant Perseus, a son of Jupiter, was the first whaleman; and to the eternal honor of our calling be it said, that the first whale attacked by our brotherhood was not killed with any sordid intent.", "용맹한 페르세우스, 곧 유피테르의 아들이야말로 최초의 고래잡이였다. 그리고 우리 천직의 영원한 명예를 위해 말해 두건대, 우리 형제단이 처음으로 공격한 고래는 그 어떤 비루한 욕심으로 죽인 것이 아니었다."], ["Those were the knightly days of our profession, when we only bore arms to succor the distressed, and not to fill men’s lamp-feeders.", "그때는 우리 직업의 기사도적인 시대였으니, 우리는 오로지 곤경에 빠진 이를 구하기 위해서만 무기를 들었을 뿐, 사람들의 등잔 기름통을 채우기 위해서가 아니었다."], ["Every one knows the fine story of Perseus and Andromeda; how the lovely Andromeda, the daughter of a king, was tied to a rock on the sea-coast, and as Leviathan was in the very act of carrying her off, Perseus, the prince of whalemen, intrepidly advancing, harpooned the monster, and delivered and married the maid.", "페르세우스와 안드로메다의 아름다운 이야기는 누구나 다 안다. 어느 임금의 딸인 사랑스러운 안드로메다가 바닷가 바위에 묶여 있었고, 리바이어던이 막 그녀를 채어 가려는 바로 그 순간, 고래잡이들의 군주 페르세우스가 두려움 없이 나아가 그 괴물에게 작살을 꽂아, 처녀를 구하고 그녀와 혼인했다는 이야기 말이다."], ["It was an admirable artistic exploit, rarely achieved by the best harpooneers of the present day; inasmuch as this Leviathan was slain at the very first dart.", "그것은 감탄할 만한 예술적 위업이었으니, 오늘날 최고의 작살잡이들조차 좀처럼 이루지 못하는 일이다. 이 리바이어던은 단 한 번의 첫 투창에 목숨을 잃었기 때문이다."], ["And let no man doubt this Arkite story; for in the ancient Joppa, now Jaffa, on the Syrian coast, in one of the Pagan temples, there stood for many ages the vast skeleton of a whale, which the city’s legends and all the inhabitants asserted to be the identical bones of the monster that Perseus slew.", "그러니 이 방주(方舟)의 이야기를 어느 누구도 의심하지 말라. 시리아 해안의 옛 욥바, 곧 지금의 야파에 있는 어느 이교 신전 안에는 오랜 세월 동안 거대한 고래의 뼈대가 서 있었는데, 그 도시의 전설과 모든 주민들은 그것을 페르세우스가 죽인 바로 그 괴물의 뼈라고 주장했다."], ["When the Romans took Joppa, the same skeleton was carried to Italy in triumph.", "로마인들이 욥바를 점령했을 때, 그 똑같은 뼈대는 개선의 전리품으로 이탈리아까지 옮겨졌다."], ["What seems most singular and suggestively important in this story, is this: it was from Joppa that Jonah set sail.", "이 이야기에서 가장 기이하면서도 의미심장하게 중요해 보이는 점은 바로 이것이다. 요나(Jonah)가 배를 타고 떠난 곳이 바로 욥바였다는 사실이다."]], [["Akin to the adventure of Perseus and Andromeda—indeed, by some supposed to be indirectly derived from it—is that famous story of St.", "페르세우스와 안드로메다의 모험과 닮은—실로 어떤 이들은 그것에서 간접적으로 유래했다고 여기는—이야기가 저 유명한 성 게오르기우스와 용의 전설이다."], ["George and the Dragon; which dragon I maintain to have been a whale; for in many old chronicles whales and dragons are strangely jumbled together, and often stand for each other.", "그런데 나는 그 용이 바로 고래였다고 주장한다. 수많은 옛 연대기에서 고래와 용은 기이하게 뒤섞여 있고, 흔히 서로를 대신해 등장하기 때문이다."], ["“Thou art as a lion of the waters, and as a dragon of the sea,”", "\"너는 물의 사자 같고, 바다의 용 같도다.\""], ["saith Ezekiel; hereby, plainly meaning a whale; in truth, some versions of the Bible use that word itself.", "에스겔이 이렇게 말했으니, 이는 분명 고래를 뜻하는 것이다. 사실 어떤 성경 판본들은 그 단어 자체를 쓰고 있다."], ["Besides, it would much subtract from the glory of the exploit had St. George but encountered a crawling reptile of the land, instead of doing battle with the great monster of the deep.", "게다가, 성 게오르기우스가 깊은 바다의 거대한 괴물과 싸우는 대신 한낱 땅을 기는 파충류와 마주쳤을 뿐이라면, 그 위업의 영광은 크게 깎이고 말 것이다."], ["Any man may kill a snake, but only a Perseus, a St. George, a Coffin, have the heart in them to march boldly up to a whale.", "뱀이야 누구라도 죽일 수 있지만, 오직 페르세우스나 성 게오르기우스, 코핀(Coffin) 같은 자만이 고래를 향해 담대히 나아갈 배짱을 지니고 있다."]], [["Let not the modern paintings of this scene mislead us; for though the creature encountered by that valiant whaleman of old is vaguely represented of a griffin-like shape, and though the battle is depicted on land and the saint on horseback, yet considering the great ignorance of those times, when the true form of the whale was unknown to artists; and considering that as in Perseus’ case, St.", "이 장면을 그린 현대의 그림들에 우리가 속아서는 안 된다. 비록 그 옛날의 용맹한 고래잡이가 마주한 짐승이 그리핀 같은 형상으로 어렴풋이 그려져 있고, 그 싸움이 뭍에서 벌어지며 성인은 말 위에 올라타 있는 모습으로 묘사되어 있긴 하지만, 그 시대의 크나큰 무지를 헤아려 보면—그때는 화가들이 고래의 참된 모습을 알지 못했으니—그리고 페르세우스의 경우처럼 성 게오르기우스의 고래도 바다에서 기어올라 모래밭으로 올라왔을 수 있음을 헤아려 보면, 또한 성 게오르기우스가 올라탄 짐승이 그저 커다란 물범이나 해마였을 수 있음을 헤아려 보면, 이 모든 것을 마음에 새길 때, 이른바 그 용을 다름 아닌 위대한 리바이어던 그 자신으로 여기는 것이 저 성스러운 전설이나 그 장면을 그린 가장 오래된 그림들과 결코 어긋나지는 않을 것이다."], ["George’s whale might have crawled up out of the sea on the beach; and considering that the animal ridden by St.", "사실, 엄격하고 꿰뚫는 진실 앞에 세워 놓고 보면, 이 이야기 전체는 저 블레셋 사람들의 물고기이자 고기이자 새인 우상, 다곤이라는 이름의 그것과 같은 신세가 될 것이다."], ["George might have been only a large seal, or sea-horse; bearing all this in mind, it will not appear altogether incompatible with the sacred legend and the ancientest draughts of the scene, to hold this so-called dragon no other than the great Leviathan himself.", "그 다곤은 이스라엘의 방주 앞에 세워지자, 말 머리 같은 머리와 두 손바닥이 그 몸에서 떨어져 나가고, 다만 그루터기, 곧 물고기 같은 부분만이 남았던 것이다. 그러니 우리와 같은 고귀한 본보기를 지닌 한 사람, 바로 고래잡이가 잉글랜드의 수호 성인인 셈이다."], ["In fact, placed before the strict and piercing truth, this whole story will fare like that fish, flesh, and fowl idol of the Philistines, Dagon by name; who being planted before the ark of Israel, his horse’s head and both the palms of his hands fell off from him, and only the stump or fishy part of him remained.", "그리고 정당한 권리로 보건대, 우리 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 작살잡이들이야말로 가장 고귀한 성 게오르기우스 기사단에 등록되어야 마땅하다."], ["Thus, then, one of our own noble stamp, even a whaleman, is the tutelary guardian of England; and by good rights, we harpooneers of Nantucket should be enrolled in the most noble order of St. George.", "그러므로 그 명예로운 기사단의 기사들이여(감히 말하건대, 그들 중 누구도 자기네 위대한 수호 성인처럼 고래를 상대해 본 적이 없으리라), 부디 낸터킷 사람을 결코 경멸의 눈으로 바라보지 말라."], ["And therefore, let not the knights of that honorable company (none of whom, I venture to say, have ever had to do with a whale like their great patron), let them never eye a Nantucketer with disdain, since even in our woollen frocks and tarred trowsers we are much better entitled to St. George’s decoration than they.", "우리는 모직 작업복과 타르 칠한 바지 차림일지언정, 성 게오르기우스의 훈장을 받을 자격이 그들보다 훨씬 더 충분하기 때문이다."]], [["Whether to admit Hercules among us or not, concerning this I long remained dubious: for though according to the Greek mythologies, that antique Crockett and Kit Carson—that brawny doer of rejoicing good deeds, was swallowed down and thrown up by a whale; still, whether that strictly makes a whaleman of him, that might be mooted.", "헤라클레스를 우리 무리에 들여야 할지 말지, 이에 관해서 나는 오랫동안 망설였다. 그리스 신화에 따르면 그 고대의 크로켓이자 키트 카슨, 곧 기쁨을 주는 선행을 행하던 그 억센 사나이가 고래에게 삼켜졌다가 다시 토해졌다고는 하지만, 그렇다고 그것이 엄밀히 그를 고래잡이로 만드는지는 논쟁의 여지가 있기 때문이다."], ["It nowhere appears that he ever actually harpooned his fish, unless, indeed, from the inside.", "그가 실제로 자기 물고기에게 작살을 꽂았다는 이야기는 어디에도 나오지 않는다, 굳이 따지자면 뱃속에서 꽂은 것이 아니라면 말이다."], ["Nevertheless, he may be deemed a sort of involuntary whaleman; at any rate the whale caught him, if he did not the whale.", "그럼에도 그는 일종의 본의 아닌 고래잡이로 여겨질 수 있겠다. 어쨌든 그가 고래를 잡지는 못했어도, 고래가 그를 잡기는 했으니까."], ["I claim him for one of our clan.", "나는 그를 우리 일족의 하나로 주장하는 바이다."]], [["But, by the best contradictory authorities, this Grecian story of Hercules and the whale is considered to be derived from the still more ancient Hebrew story of Jonah and the whale; and vice versâ; certainly they are very similar.", "그러나 가장 권위 있되 서로 엇갈리는 견해들에 따르면, 헤라클레스와 고래에 관한 이 그리스 이야기는 그보다 더 오래된 히브리의 요나와 고래 이야기에서 유래했다고 여겨지며, 또 그 반대라고도 한다. 어쨌든 둘은 매우 흡사하다."], ["If I claim the demi-god then, why not the prophet?", "내가 그 반신을 우리 사람으로 주장한다면, 어찌 그 예언자라고 못 할 것인가?"]], [["Nor do heroes, saints, demigods, and prophets alone comprise the whole roll of our order.", "영웅과 성인, 반신과 예언자들만이 우리 기사단의 명부 전체를 이루는 것도 아니다. 우리의 대종장(大宗匠)은 아직 거명되지 않았으니, 옛 시대의 왕족 임금들처럼, 우리 형제단의 원류는 다름 아닌 위대한 신들 그 자체에서 비롯되기 때문이다."], ["Our grand master is still to be named; for like royal kings of old times, we find the head waters of our fraternity in nothing short of the great gods themselves.", "이제 저 경이로운 동방의 이야기를 샤스터에서 되새겨 보아야겠다. 그것은 힌두교 신격의 세 위격 중 하나인 두려운 비슈누를 우리에게 전해 주며, 이 신성한 비슈누 그 자신을 우리의 주(主)로 내어 준다."], ["That wondrous oriental story is now to be rehearsed from the Shaster, which gives us the dread Vishnoo, one of the three persons in the godhead of the Hindoos; gives us this divine Vishnoo himself for our Lord;—Vishnoo, who, by the first of his ten earthly incarnations, has for ever set apart and sanctified the whale.", "그 열 차례의 지상 화신 가운데 첫 번째 화신을 통해 고래를 영원히 따로 구별하여 거룩하게 한 비슈누 말이다. 샤스터가 이르기를, 브라흐마, 곧 신들의 신이 주기적인 소멸의 한 시기를 거친 뒤 세계를 다시 창조하기로 결심했을 때, 그 일을 주관하도록 비슈누를 낳았다고 한다."], ["When Brahma, or the God of Gods, saith the Shaster, resolved to recreate the world after one of its periodical dissolutions, he gave birth to Vishnoo, to preside over the work; but the Vedas, or mystical books, whose perusal would seem to have been indispensable to Vishnoo before beginning the creation, and which therefore must have contained something in the shape of practical hints to young architects, these Vedas were lying at the bottom of the waters; so Vishnoo became incarnate in a whale, and sounding down in him to the uttermost depths, rescued the sacred volumes.", "그런데 베다, 곧 신비의 경전들—비슈누가 창조를 시작하기에 앞서 반드시 통독해야 했던 것으로 보이며, 따라서 젊은 건축가들에게 실용적인 지침 같은 무언가를 담고 있었음이 분명한 그 베다가 물 밑바닥에 가라앉아 있었다. 그리하여 비슈누는 고래의 몸으로 화신하여, 그 안에서 가장 깊은 심연까지 잠수해 내려가, 그 성스러운 경전들을 건져 올렸다."], ["Was not this Vishnoo a whaleman, then?", "그렇다면 이 비슈누야말로 고래잡이가 아니었던가?"], ["even as a man who rides a horse is called a horseman?", "말을 타는 사람을 마부라 부르듯이 말이다."]], [["Perseus, St. George, Hercules, Jonah, and Vishnoo!", "페르세우스, 성 게오르기우스, 헤라클레스, 요나, 그리고 비슈누!"], ["there’s a member-roll for you!", "이것이야말로 그대들에게 보여 줄 회원 명부다!"], ["What club but the whaleman’s can head off like that?", "고래잡이의 모임 말고 그 어떤 단체가 이렇게 앞장설 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["# CHAPTER 83. Jonah Historically Regarded.", "# CHAPTER 83. Jonah Historically Regarded."]], [["Reference was made to the historical story of Jonah and the whale in the preceding chapter.", "앞 장에서 요나(Jonah)와 고래에 관한 역사적 이야기가 언급되었다."], ["Now some Nantucketers rather distrust this historical story of Jonah and the whale.", "그런데 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들 중에는 요나와 고래에 관한 이 역사적 이야기를 다소 미심쩍게 여기는 이들이 있다."], ["But then there were some sceptical Greeks and Romans, who, standing out from the orthodox pagans of their times, equally doubted the story of Hercules and the whale, and Arion and the dolphin; and yet their doubting those traditions did not make those traditions one whit the less facts, for all that.", "하지만 옛날에도 회의적인 그리스인과 로마인들이 있었으니, 그들은 당대의 정통 이교도들과 거리를 두면서, 마찬가지로 헤라클레스와 고래 이야기며 아리온과 돌고래 이야기를 의심했다. 그러나 그들이 그러한 전승을 의심했다고 해서, 그 전승들이 조금이라도 덜 사실이 되는 것은 결코 아니었다."]], [["One old Sag-Harbor whaleman’s chief reason for questioning the Hebrew story was this:—He had one of those quaint old-fashioned Bibles, embellished with curious, unscientific plates; one of which represented Jonah’s whale with two spouts in his head—a peculiarity only true with respect to a species of the Leviathan (the Right Whale, and the varieties of that order), concerning which the fishermen have this saying, “A penny roll would choke him”; his swallow is so very small.", "새그하버 출신의 어느 늙은 고래잡이가 그 히브리 이야기를 의심한 주된 이유는 이러했다. 그는 진기하고 비과학적인 도판들로 꾸며진, 그 예스럽고 고풍스러운 성경 중 하나를 가지고 있었는데, 그중 한 도판에는 요나의 고래가 머리에 두 개의 물줄기 구멍을 가진 모습으로 그려져 있었던 것이다."], ["But, to this, Bishop Jebb’s anticipative answer is ready. It is not necessary, hints the Bishop, that we consider Jonah as tombed in the whale’s belly, but as temporarily lodged in some part of his mouth.", "그런데 이 특징은 오로지 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 한 종류, 곧 참고래와 그 목(目)에 속하는 변종들에게만 들어맞는 것으로, 어부들 사이에서는 이 고래를 두고 \"1페니짜리 빵 한 덩이면 목이 막혀 버린다\"는 말이 떠돈다."], ["And this seems reasonable enough in the good Bishop.", "그만큼 그 목구멍이 몹시 작은 것이다. 그러나 이에 대해서는 젭 주교의 선견지명 있는 답변이 마련되어 있다."], ["For truly, the Right Whale’s mouth would accommodate a couple of whist-tables, and comfortably seat all the players.", "주교가 넌지시 이르기를, 우리는 요나가 고래 뱃속에 묻혀 있었다고 여길 것이 아니라, 그 입의 어느 부분에 일시적으로 자리 잡고 있었다고 여기면 된다는 것이다. 이는 선량한 주교에게 충분히 그럴듯하게 들린다."], ["Possibly, too, Jonah might have ensconced himself in a hollow tooth; but, on second thoughts, the Right Whale is toothless.", "사실 참고래의 입은 휘스트 노름판 두어 개를 들여놓고도 모든 노름꾼을 편안히 앉힐 만하기 때문이다. 어쩌면 요나는 텅 빈 이빨 속에 몸을 숨겼을 수도 있겠으나, 다시 생각해 보면 참고래에게는 이빨이 없다."]], [["Another reason which Sag-Harbor (he went by that name) urged for his want of faith in this matter of the prophet, was something obscurely in reference to his incarcerated body and the whale’s gastric juices.", "새그하버(그는 그런 이름으로 불렸다)가 이 예언자 문제에 믿음을 갖지 못한 또 다른 이유는, 갇혀 있던 그의 몸과 고래의 위액에 관한 어딘가 모호한 것이었다. 그러나 이 반론 역시 무너지고 마는데, 어느 독일 성서주해가가 추정하기를 요나는 분명 죽은 고래의 떠다니는 몸뚱이 속으로 피신했으리라는 것이다."], ["But this objection likewise falls to the ground, because a German exegetist supposes that Jonah must have taken refuge in the floating body of a dead whale—even as the French soldiers in the Russian campaign turned their dead horses into tents, and crawled into them.", "러시아 원정에서 프랑스 병사들이 죽은 말을 천막 삼아 그 속으로 기어들어 갔던 것과 마찬가지로 말이다. 게다가 대륙의 다른 주석가들이 헤아려 본 바로는, 요나가 욥바의 배에서 바다로 던져졌을 때 그는 곧장 가까이 있던 다른 배, 곧 고래를 뱃머리 장식으로 단 어떤 배로 피해 갔다는 것이다."], ["Besides, it has been divined by other continental commentators, that when Jonah was thrown overboard from the Joppa ship, he straightway effected his escape to another vessel near by, some vessel with a whale for a figure-head; and, I would add, possibly called “The Whale,”", "그리고 내가 덧붙이자면, 요즈음 어떤 배들이 \"상어\"니 \"갈매기\"니 \"독수리\"니 하는 이름으로 명명되듯, 어쩌면 그 배는 \"고래\"라고 불렸을지도 모른다. 또한 요나서에 언급된 고래란 그저 구명 도구, 곧 바람을 가득 채운 부대 자루를 뜻할 뿐이며, 위기에 처한 예언자가 헤엄쳐 그것에 다다라 물에 빠져 죽을 운명을 면했다고 주장한 학식 깊은 주해가들도 없지 않았다."], ["as some craft are nowadays christened the “Shark,” the “Gull,”", "그러므로 가련한 새그하버는 사방에서 패한 듯이 보인다."], ["the “Eagle.” Nor have there been wanting learned exegetists who have opined that the whale mentioned in the book of Jonah merely meant a life-preserver—an inflated bag of wind—which the endangered prophet swam to, and so was saved from a watery doom.", "그러나 그에게는 믿음을 갖지 못한 또 다른 이유가 남아 있었다."], ["Poor Sag-Harbor, therefore, seems worsted all round. But he had still another reason for his want of faith.", "내 기억이 옳다면 그것은 이러했다."], ["It was this, if I remember right: Jonah was swallowed by the whale in the Mediterranean Sea, and after three days he was vomited up somewhere within three days’ journey of Nineveh, a city on the Tigris, very much more than three days’ journey across from the nearest point of the Mediterranean coast.", "요나는 지중해에서 고래에게 삼켜졌고, 사흘 뒤에 니네베에서 사흘 걸음 거리쯤 되는 어딘가에 토해졌다는 것이다. 그런데 니네베는 티그리스강 가의 도시로, 지중해 연안의 가장 가까운 지점에서 가로질러 가도 사흘 걸음보다 훨씬 더 먼 곳에 있다."], ["How is that?", "이를 어찌 설명한단 말인가?"]], [["But was there no other way for the whale to land the prophet within that short distance of Nineveh? Yes.", "그러나 고래가 그토록 짧은 거리 안에서 예언자를 니네베 가까이 내려놓을 다른 길은 없었던 것일까?"], ["He might have carried him round by the way of the Cape of Good Hope.", "있었다. 고래는 그를 희망봉을 돌아서 데려갔을 수도 있다."], ["But not to speak of the passage through the whole length of the Mediterranean, and another passage up the Persian Gulf and Red Sea, such a supposition would involve the complete circumnavigation of all Africa in three days, not to speak of the Tigris waters, near the site of Nineveh, being too shallow for any whale to swim in.", "그러나 지중해를 끝에서 끝까지 통과하고, 또다시 페르시아만과 홍해를 거슬러 오르는 항해는 차치하더라도, 그러한 가정은 사흘 안에 아프리카 전체를 완전히 일주하는 일을 수반하게 된다. 그뿐 아니라 니네베의 옛터 가까이 흐르는 티그리스강 물은 어떤 고래도 헤엄칠 수 없을 만큼 얕다는 사실은 말할 것도 없다."], ["Besides, this idea of Jonah’s weathering the Cape of Good Hope at so early a day would wrest the honor of the discovery of that great headland from Bartholomew Diaz, its reputed discoverer, and so make modern history a liar.", "게다가 요나가 그토록 이른 시대에 희망봉을 돌았다는 이 발상은, 그 위대한 곶의 발견이라는 영예를 그곳을 발견했다고 알려진 바르톨로뮤 디아스에게서 빼앗아, 근대 역사를 거짓말쟁이로 만들고 말 것이다."]], [["But all these foolish arguments of old Sag-Harbor only evinced his foolish pride of reason—a thing still more reprehensible in him, seeing that he had but little learning except what he had picked up from the sun and the sea.", "그러나 늙은 새그하버의 이 모든 어리석은 논변은 다만 이성에 대한 그의 어리석은 오만을 드러낼 뿐이었다. 그가 해와 바다에서 주워 모은 것 말고는 배운 것이 거의 없는 처지였음을 생각하면, 이는 그에게 더욱 비난받아 마땅한 일이었다."], ["I say it only shows his foolish, impious pride, and abominable, devilish rebellion against the reverend clergy.", "다시 말하노니, 그것은 다만 그의 어리석고 불경한 오만과, 존경받는 성직자들에 대한 가증스럽고 악마 같은 반역을 보여 줄 따름이다."], ["For by a Portuguese Catholic priest, this very idea of Jonah’s going to Nineveh via the Cape of Good Hope was advanced as a signal magnification of the general miracle.", "어느 포르투갈 가톨릭 사제는 요나가 희망봉을 거쳐 니네베로 갔다는 바로 이 발상을 두고, 그 전체 기적을 한층 두드러지게 키워 주는 것이라며 내세웠던 것이다."], ["And so it was.", "과연 그러했다."], ["Besides, to this day, the highly enlightened Turks devoutly believe in the historical story of Jonah.", "게다가 오늘날까지도, 고도로 계몽된 터키인들은 요나의 역사적 이야기를 경건히 믿고 있다."], ["And some three centuries ago, an English traveller in old Harris’s Voyages, speaks of a Turkish Mosque built in honor of Jonah, in which Mosque was a miraculous lamp that burnt without any oil.", "그리고 약 삼백 년 전, 옛 해리스의 항해기에 실린 어느 영국인 여행자는 요나를 기리어 세운 터키의 한 모스크를 이야기하는데, 그 모스크 안에는 기름 한 방울 없이도 타오르는 기적의 등불이 있었다고 한다."]], [["# CHAPTER 84. Pitchpoling.", "# CHAPTER 84. Pitchpoling."]], [["To make them run easily and swiftly, the axles of carriages are anointed; and for much the same purpose, some whalers perform an analogous operation upon their boat; they grease the bottom.", "창고에 실린 마차의 바퀴축이 쉽고 빠르게 굴러가도록 기름을 칠하듯이, 거의 같은 목적으로 어떤 고래잡이들은 자기 보트에 비슷한 작업을 한다. 보트 바닥에 기름을 발라 매끄럽게 하는 것이다."], ["Nor is it to be doubted that as such a procedure can do no harm, it may possibly be of no contemptible advantage; considering that oil and water are hostile; that oil is a sliding thing, and that the object in view is to make the boat slide bravely.", "그리고 이런 처치가 해로울 게 없는 만큼, 적잖은 이점을 가져다줄 가능성도 부정할 수 없다. 기름과 물은 서로 어긋나는 성질이고, 기름은 미끄러운 물질이며, 노리는 바는 보트가 씩씩하게 미끄러져 나아가게 하는 것이기 때문이다."], ["Queequeg believed strongly in anointing his boat, and one morning not long after the German ship Jungfrau disappeared, took more than customary pains in that occupation; crawling under its bottom, where it hung over the side, and rubbing in the unctuousness as though diligently seeking to insure a crop of hair from the craft’s bald keel.", "퀴케그(Queequeg)는 자기 보트에 기름칠하는 것을 굳게 믿었는데, 독일 배 융프라우호가 사라진 지 얼마 지나지 않은 어느 날 아침, 그 일에 평소보다 더 공을 들였다. 뱃전 너머로 매달린 보트 바닥 밑으로 기어 들어가, 그 매끈한 기름을 문질러 발랐으니, 마치 그 배의 대머리 같은 용골에서 한바탕 털이 돋아나기를 부지런히 바라기라도 하는 듯했다."], ["He seemed to be working in obedience to some particular presentiment.", "그는 어떤 특별한 예감에 따라 움직이는 듯했다."], ["Nor did it remain unwarranted by the event.", "그리고 그 예감은 뒤이은 사건으로 인해 근거 없는 것이 되지 않았다."]], [["Towards noon whales were raised; but so soon as the ship sailed down to them, they turned and fled with swift precipitancy; a disordered flight, as of Cleopatra’s barges from Actium.", "정오 무렵 고래들이 발견되었다. 그러나 배가 그쪽으로 내려가자마자, 고래들은 몸을 돌려 쏜살같이 황급히 달아났으니, 악티움에서 도망친 클레오파트라의 거룻배들처럼 어지러운 도주였다."]], [["Nevertheless, the boats pursued, and Stubb’s was foremost.", "그럼에도 보트들은 추격에 나섰고, 스터브(Stubb)의 보트가 선두였다."], ["By great exertion, Tashtego at last succeeded in planting one iron; but the stricken whale, without at all sounding, still continued his horizontal flight, with added fleetness.", "엄청난 노력 끝에 마침내 태시테고(Tashtego)가 작살 하나를 박는 데 성공했다. 그러나 작살을 맞은 고래는 잠수할 기색도 없이, 오히려 한층 빨라진 속도로 수평으로 계속 달아났다."], ["Such unintermitted strainings upon the planted iron must sooner or later inevitably extract it.", "박힌 작살에 이처럼 쉼 없는 압력이 가해지면 머지않아 그것은 어쩔 수 없이 빠져나오고 만다."], ["It became imperative to lance the flying whale, or be content to lose him.", "달아나는 고래에 창을 박든지, 아니면 놓치기를 감수하든지 둘 중 하나를 선택해야만 했다."], ["But to haul the boat up to his flank was impossible, he swam so fast and furious.", "그러나 보트를 그 옆구리까지 끌어붙이는 일은 불가능했으니, 고래가 너무도 빠르고 맹렬하게 헤엄쳤기 때문이다."], ["What then remained?", "그러면 무엇이 남아 있는가?"]], [["Of all the wondrous devices and dexterities, the sleights of hand and countless subtleties, to which the veteran whaleman is so often forced, none exceed that fine manœuvre with the lance called pitchpoling.", "노련한 고래잡이가 그토록 자주 어쩔 수 없이 동원하게 되는 온갖 경이로운 술책과 솜씨, 손재간과 헤아릴 수 없는 묘기들 가운데, 작살로 부리는 저 절묘한 기교, 곧 피치폴링(pitchpoling)에 견줄 만한 것은 없다."], ["Small sword, or broad sword, in all its exercises boasts nothing like it.", "가는 검이든 넓은 검이든 그 어떤 검술도 이에 비할 것을 자랑하지 못한다."], ["It is only indispensable with an inveterate running whale; its grand fact and feature is the wonderful distance to which the long lance is accurately darted from a violently rocking, jerking boat, under extreme headway.", "이것은 끈질기게 달아나는 고래를 상대할 때에만 꼭 필요한 기술이다. 그 위대한 핵심이자 특징은, 극도의 속력으로 거칠게 흔들리고 요동치는 보트에서 긴 창을 정확하게 던져 보내는 그 놀라운 거리에 있다."], ["Steel and wood included, the entire spear is some ten or twelve feet in length; the staff is much slighter than that of the harpoon, and also of a lighter material—pine.", "강철과 나무를 다 합쳐 그 창 전체의 길이는 열 자 내지 열두 자(약 3미터에서 3.6미터)쯤 되고, 자루는 작살 자루보다 훨씬 가늘며 재질도 더 가벼운 소나무다."], ["It is furnished with a small rope called a warp, of considerable length, by which it can be hauled back to the hand after darting.", "거기에는 워프(warp)라 불리는 가느다란 밧줄이 상당한 길이로 달려 있어, 창을 던진 뒤에도 그 줄을 잡아당겨 손으로 되돌릴 수 있게 되어 있다."]], [["But before going further, it is important to mention here, that though the harpoon may be pitchpoled in the same way with the lance, yet it is seldom done; and when done, is still less frequently successful, on account of the greater weight and inferior length of the harpoon as compared with the lance, which in effect become serious drawbacks.", "그러나 더 나아가기 전에 여기서 짚어 둘 중요한 사실이 있다. 작살도 창과 같은 방식으로 피치폴링할 수 있기는 하지만, 그런 일은 좀처럼 행해지지 않으며, 행해진다 해도 성공하는 일은 더욱 드물다."], ["As a general thing, therefore, you must first get fast to a whale, before any pitchpoling comes into play.", "작살은 창에 비해 무게가 더 나가고 길이는 더 짧아서, 사실상 그것이 심각한 약점이 되기 때문이다. 그러므로 일반적으로는 어떤 피치폴링이든 작용하기에 앞서, 먼저 고래에 작살을 박아 단단히 매여 있어야 한다."]], [["Look now at Stubb; a man who from his humorous, deliberate coolness and equanimity in the direst emergencies, was specially qualified to excel in pitchpoling.", "이제 스터브를 보라. 더없이 위급한 순간에도 발휘되는 그 유머러스하고 침착한 냉정함과 평정심으로 인해, 그는 피치폴링에 특히나 뛰어날 자격을 갖춘 사람이었다."], ["Look at him; he stands upright in the tossed bow of the flying boat; wrapt in fleecy foam, the towing whale is forty feet ahead.", "그를 보라. 그는 질주하는 보트의 출렁이는 이물에 꼿꼿이 서 있다."], ["Handling the long lance lightly, glancing twice or thrice along its length to see if it be exactly straight, Stubb whistlingly gathers up the coil of the warp in one hand, so as to secure its free end in his grasp, leaving the rest unobstructed.", "양털 같은 거품에 휩싸인 채, 보트를 끌고 가는 고래는 마흔 자(약 12미터) 앞에 있다. 긴 창을 가볍게 다루며, 그것이 정확히 곧은지 보려고 그 길이를 따라 두세 번 눈길을 굴린 뒤, 스터브는 휘파람을 불며 워프 밧줄 사리를 한 손으로 거두어, 그 자유로운 끝을 손아귀에 단단히 쥐고 나머지는 거치적거리지 않도록 둔다."], ["Then holding the lance full before his waistband’s middle, he levels it at the whale; when, covering him with it, he steadily depresses the butt-end in his hand, thereby elevating the point till the weapon stands fairly balanced upon his palm, fifteen feet in the air.", "그런 다음 창을 허리띠 한가운데 정면에 받쳐 들고 고래를 겨눈다. 창으로 고래를 조준한 채, 손에 쥔 자루 끝을 차분히 내리누르면, 그만큼 창끝이 들려 올라가, 마침내 그 무기가 그의 손바닥 위에 제대로 균형을 잡고 허공 열다섯 자(약 4.5미터) 높이로 곧추선다."], ["He minds you somewhat of a juggler, balancing a long staff on his chin.", "그 모습은 어쩐지 긴 막대를 턱 위에 세워 균형 잡는 곡예사를 떠올리게 한다."], ["Next moment with a rapid, nameless impulse, in a superb lofty arch the bright steel spans the foaming distance, and quivers in the life spot of the whale.", "다음 순간, 재빠르고 형언할 수 없는 충동으로, 빛나는 강철이 장엄하고 높은 포물선을 그리며 거품 이는 거리를 가로질러, 고래의 급소에 부르르 떨며 박힌다."], ["Instead of sparkling water, he now spouts red blood.", "이제 고래는 반짝이는 물 대신 붉은 피를 뿜어 올린다."]], [["“That drove the spigot out of him!”", "\"저놈한테서 마개를 뽑아냈군!\""], ["cried Stubb.", "스터브가 외쳤다."], ["“’Tis July’s immortal Fourth; all fountains must run wine today!", "\"오늘은 영원불멸의 칠월 나흘, 독립기념일이다! 오늘은 모든 샘이 포도주를 쏟아 내야 마땅하지!"], ["Would now, it were old Orleans whiskey, or old Ohio, or unspeakable old Monongahela!", "저게 묵은 올리언스 위스키나 묵은 오하이오 술, 아니면 말로 다 못할 묵은 모논가헬라 위스키라면 얼마나 좋을꼬!"], ["Then, Tashtego, lad, I’d have ye hold a canakin to the jet, and we’d drink round it!", "그럼 태시테고, 이 사람아, 자네가 저 물줄기 밑에 깡통을 받쳐 들면, 우리 모두 빙 둘러앉아 한잔씩 들이켤 텐데!"], ["Yea, verily, hearts alive, we’d brew choice punch in the spread of his spout-hole there, and from that live punch-bowl quaff the living stuff.”", "그래, 정말이지, 이 멀쩡히 살아 있는 친구들아, 저놈 숨구멍이 펼쳐 놓은 자리에 최상급 펀치를 빚어, 저 살아 있는 펀치 그릇에서 살아 있는 술을 들이켜 보자고.\""]], [["Again and again to such gamesome talk, the dexterous dart is repeated, the spear returning to its master like a greyhound held in skilful leash.", "이런 흥겨운 입담과 더불어 그 능란한 던지기가 거듭되고 또 거듭되니, 창은 솜씨 좋은 끈에 매인 그레이하운드처럼 제 주인에게로 돌아온다."], ["The agonized whale goes into his flurry; the tow-line is slackened, and the pitchpoler dropping astern, folds his hands, and mutely watches the monster die.", "고통에 몸부림치던 고래가 마지막 발악에 들어간다. 예인줄이 느슨해지고, 피치폴링을 하던 자는 고물 쪽으로 물러나 두 손을 모은 채, 말없이 그 괴물이 죽어 가는 모습을 지켜본다."]], [["# CHAPTER 85. The Fountain.", "# CHAPTER 85. The Fountain."]], [["That for six thousand years—and no one knows how many millions of ages before—the great whales should have been spouting all over the sea, and sprinkling and mistifying the gardens of the deep, as with so many sprinkling or mistifying pots; and that for some centuries back, thousands of hunters should have been close by the fountain of the whale, watching these sprinklings and spoutings—that all this should be, and yet, that down to this blessed minute (fifteen and a quarter minutes past one o’clock P.M.", "육천 년 동안 — 그리고 그 이전 몇백만 시대였는지는 아무도 모른다 — 거대한 고래들이 온 바다를 누비며 물을 뿜어 댔고, 마치 수많은 물뿌리개나 물안개통이 그러하듯 심해의 정원에 물을 뿌리고 안개를 드리워 왔다. 그리고 지난 몇 세기 동안 수천 명의 사냥꾼들이 고래의 그 분수 가까이에서 이 흩뿌림과 물뿜음을 지켜보아 왔다."], ["of this sixteenth day of December, A.D. 1851), it should still remain a problem, whether these spoutings are, after all, really water, or nothing but vapor—this is surely a noteworthy thing.", "이 모든 일이 다 있었음에도, 이 복된 순간(서기 1851년 12월 16일 오후 1시 15분 15초)에 이르러서까지도, 이 물뿜음이 과연 정말로 물인지 아니면 그저 수증기에 지나지 않는지가 여전히 풀리지 않은 문제로 남아 있다니 — 이는 분명 주목할 만한 일이다."]], [["Let us, then, look at this matter, along with some interesting items contingent.", "그러니 이제 이 문제를, 거기에 딸린 몇 가지 흥미로운 사항들과 더불어 살펴보기로 하자."], ["Every one knows that by the peculiar cunning of their gills, the finny tribes in general breathe the air which at all times is combined with the element in which they swim; hence, a herring or a cod might live a century, and never once raise its head above the surface.", "누구나 알다시피, 지느러미 달린 종족들은 일반적으로 그들 아가미 특유의 교묘한 구조 덕분에 그들이 헤엄치는 원소 속에 늘 섞여 있는 공기를 호흡한다. 그래서 청어나 대구는 백 년을 살면서도 단 한 번 수면 위로 머리를 내밀지 않을 수 있다."], ["But owing to his marked internal structure which gives him regular lungs, like a human being’s, the whale can only live by inhaling the disengaged air in the open atmosphere.", "그러나 고래는 인간의 것과 같은 정식 허파를 갖춘 그 독특한 내부 구조 때문에, 오직 탁 트인 대기 속의 유리된 공기를 들이마셔야만 살 수 있다."], ["Wherefore the necessity for his periodical visits to the upper world.", "그렇기에 고래는 주기적으로 윗세상을 찾아가야 할 필요가 있는 것이다."], ["But he cannot in any degree breathe through his mouth, for, in his ordinary attitude, the Sperm Whale’s mouth is buried at least eight feet beneath the surface; and what is still more, his windpipe has no connexion with his mouth.", "그러나 고래는 결코 입으로는 조금도 숨을 쉴 수 없으니, 평상시 자세에서 향유고래의 입은 적어도 수면 아래 여덟 자(약 2.4미터)에 잠겨 있기 때문이다. 게다가 그 기관(氣管)은 입과 전혀 연결되어 있지 않다."], ["No, he breathes through his spiracle alone; and this is on the top of his head.", "아니, 고래는 오로지 분수공(噴水孔)으로만 숨을 쉬며, 이것은 그 머리 꼭대기에 있다."]], [["If I say, that in any creature breathing is only a function indispensable to vitality, inasmuch as it withdraws from the air a certain element, which being subsequently brought into contact with the blood imparts to the blood its vivifying principle, I do not think I shall err; though I may possibly use some superfluous scientific words.", "어떤 생물에게든 호흡이란 생명을 유지하는 데 없어서는 안 될 기능에 지나지 않는다고 내가 말한다면 — 호흡이 공기에서 어떤 특정한 성분을 끌어내고, 그것이 나중에 피와 접촉하여 피에 그 생명을 살리는 원리를 부여하기 때문인데 — 나는 틀린 말을 하고 있는 것은 아니라 생각한다. 비록 다소 군더더기 같은 과학 용어를 쓰는 셈일지는 몰라도 말이다."], ["Assume it, and it follows that if all the blood in a man could be aerated with one breath, he might then seal up his nostrils and not fetch another for a considerable time.", "이를 받아들인다면, 만일 사람의 온몸에 흐르는 피를 단 한 번의 호흡으로 모조리 산소화할 수 있다면, 그는 콧구멍을 막아 버린 채 상당한 시간 동안 다시 숨을 들이쉬지 않아도 되리라는 결론이 따라 나온다. 다시 말해, 그는 숨을 쉬지 않고도 살 수 있다는 것이다."], ["That is to say, he would then live without breathing.", "변칙처럼 보일지 모르나, 이것이 바로 고래의 경우다."], ["Anomalous as it may seem, this is precisely the case with the whale, who systematically lives, by intervals, his full hour and more (when at the bottom) without drawing a single breath, or so much as in any way inhaling a particle of air; for, remember, he has no gills.", "고래는 (바닥에 있을 때) 일정한 간격을 두고 꼬박 한 시간 이상씩을, 단 한 번도 숨을 들이쉬지 않고, 아니 어떤 식으로든 공기 한 점 들이마시지 않고 규칙적으로 살아간다. 기억하라, 고래에게는 아가미가 없다."], ["How is this?", "어떻게 이런 일이 가능한가?"], ["Between his ribs and on each side of his spine he is supplied with a remarkable involved Cretan labyrinth of vermicelli-like vessels, which vessels, when he quits the surface, are completely distended with oxygenated blood.", "고래의 갈비뼈 사이와 척추 양옆에는 마치 크레타의 미궁처럼 얽히고설킨, 가느다란 국수 가닥 같은 혈관들이 갖추어져 있는데, 이 혈관들은 고래가 수면을 떠날 때 산소가 가득 실린 피로 완전히 부풀어 오른다. 그리하여 한 시간 남짓, 바다 속 천 길 깊은 곳에서, 고래는 제 몸속에 여분의 생명력을 비축해 다닌다."], ["So that for an hour or more, a thousand fathoms in the sea, he carries a surplus stock of vitality in him, just as the camel crossing the waterless desert carries a surplus supply of drink for future use in its four supplementary stomachs.", "마치 물 없는 사막을 건너는 낙타가 훗날 쓰려고 네 개의 보조 위장에 여분의 마실 물을 채워 다니는 것과 똑같이. 이 미궁이 해부학적 사실이라는 점은 반박의 여지가 없다."], ["The anatomical fact of this labyrinth is indisputable; and that the supposition founded upon it is reasonable and true, seems the more cogent to me, when I consider the otherwise inexplicable obstinacy of that leviathan in having his spoutings out, as the fishermen phrase it.", "그리고 그것을 바탕으로 한 추정이 합리적이고 참되다는 것은, 어부들의 표현대로 저 리바이어던이 \"물뿜기를 다 마치려는\" 그 달리 설명할 길 없는 고집을 내가 곰곰이 생각해 볼 때, 한층 더 설득력 있게 다가온다."], ["This is what I mean.", "내 말은 이런 뜻이다."], ["If unmolested, upon rising to the surface, the Sperm Whale will continue there for a period of time exactly uniform with all his other unmolested risings.", "향유고래는 방해받지 않은 채 수면으로 떠오르면, 방해받지 않았던 다른 모든 떠오름과 정확히 똑같은 시간 동안 거기 머무른다."], ["Say he stays eleven minutes, and jets seventy times, that is, respires seventy breaths; then whenever he rises again, he will be sure to have his seventy breaths over again, to a minute.", "가령 그가 11분을 머무르며 일흔 번을 뿜는다고, 곧 일흔 번을 호흡한다고 치자. 그러면 다음번에 다시 떠오를 때에도, 그는 어김없이 분(分) 단위까지 정확하게 다시 일흔 번의 호흡을 채우고야 만다."], ["Now, if after he fetches a few breaths you alarm him, so that he sounds, he will be always dodging up again to make good his regular allowance of air.", "그런데 만일 그가 몇 번 숨을 들이쉰 뒤에 그대가 놀라게 하여 그가 잠수해 버린다면, 그는 늘 다시 슬쩍 떠올라 제 규칙적인 공기 할당량을 다 채우고야 만다."], ["And not till those seventy breaths are told, will he finally go down to stay out his full term below.", "그리하여 그 일흔 번의 호흡을 다 셀 때까지는, 결코 마지막으로 내려가 아래에서 제 온전한 시간을 채우려 하지 않는다."], ["Remark, however, that in different individuals these rates are different; but in any one they are alike.", "다만 유의할 것은, 개체마다 이 횟수가 서로 다르다는 점이다. 그러나 같은 개체에서는 늘 한결같다."], ["Now, why should the whale thus insist upon having his spoutings out, unless it be to replenish his reservoir of air, ere descending for good?", "그렇다면 고래는 영영 내려가기에 앞서 제 공기 저장고를 다시 채우려는 것이 아니라면, 어찌하여 이토록 물뿜기를 다 마치겠다고 고집하겠는가?"], ["How obvious is it, too, that this necessity for the whale’s rising exposes him to all the fatal hazards of the chase.", "또한 고래가 떠올라야 하는 이 필연성이, 그를 추격의 온갖 치명적 위험에 노출시킨다는 사실이 얼마나 명백한가."], ["For not by hook or by net could this vast leviathan be caught, when sailing a thousand fathoms beneath the sunlight.", "햇빛 아래 천 길 깊은 곳을 헤엄칠 때라면, 이 거대한 리바이어던을 그 어떤 갈고리로도 그물로도 잡을 수 없을 테니 말이다."], ["Not so much thy skill, then, O hunter, as the great necessities that strike the victory to thee!", "그러니 오, 사냥꾼이여, 그대에게 승리를 안겨 주는 것은 그대의 기량이라기보다, 저 위대한 필연성인 것이다!"]], [["In man, breathing is incessantly going on—one breath only serving for two or three pulsations; so that whatever other business he has to attend to, waking or sleeping, breathe he must, or die he will.", "인간에게 호흡은 끊임없이 이어진다 — 한 번의 호흡이 겨우 두세 번의 맥박에만 쓰이니, 깨어 있든 잠들어 있든 어떤 다른 일에 매달려 있든 인간은 반드시 숨을 쉬어야 하며, 그러지 않으면 죽고 만다."], ["But the Sperm Whale only breathes about one seventh or Sunday of his time.", "그러나 향유고래는 제 시간의 겨우 칠분의 일, 곧 안식일에 해당하는 동안만 숨을 쉰다."]], [["It has been said that the whale only breathes through his spout-hole; if it could truthfully be added that his spouts are mixed with water, then I opine we should be furnished with the reason why his sense of smell seems obliterated in him; for the only thing about him that at all answers to his nose is that identical spout-hole; and being so clogged with two elements, it could not be expected to have the power of smelling.", "고래는 오직 분수공으로만 숨을 쉰다고 앞서 말한 바 있다. 만약 거기에 그의 물뿜음이 물과 섞여 있다는 말을 사실대로 덧붙일 수 있다면, 그렇다면 나는 어째서 고래에게 후각이라는 감각이 지워져 버린 듯한지 그 이유를 알게 되리라 본다."], ["But owing to the mystery of the spout—whether it be water or whether it be vapor—no absolute certainty can as yet be arrived at on this head.", "고래에게서 코에 조금이라도 해당하는 유일한 기관이 바로 그 분수공인데, 그것이 두 가지 원소로 그처럼 꽉 막혀 있으니 냄새 맡는 능력을 갖추리라 기대할 수는 없는 노릇이기 때문이다. 그러나 그 물뿜음이 물인지 수증기인지 하는 그 수수께끼 때문에, 이 점에 관해서는 아직 절대적인 확신에 이를 수 없다."], ["Sure it is, nevertheless, that the Sperm Whale has no proper olfactories.", "그럼에도 분명한 것은, 향유고래에게는 제대로 된 후각 기관이 없다는 사실이다."], ["But what does he want of them?", "하지만 그것이 고래에게 무슨 소용이 있겠는가?"], ["No roses, no violets, no Cologne-water in the sea.", "바다에는 장미도, 제비꽃도, 오드콜로뉴도 없으니 말이다."]], [["Furthermore, as his windpipe solely opens into the tube of his spouting canal, and as that long canal—like the grand Erie Canal—is furnished with a sort of locks (that open and shut) for the downward retention of air or the upward exclusion of water, therefore the whale has no voice; unless you insult him by saying, that when he so strangely rumbles, he talks through his nose.", "더욱이 고래의 기관은 오직 그 물뿜는 관(管)으로만 통하며, 그 기다란 관은 — 저 웅장한 이리 운하처럼 — 공기를 아래로 가두거나 물을 위로 막아 내기 위한 일종의 수문(여닫이로 된)을 갖추고 있다. 그렇기에 고래에게는 목소리가 없다."], ["But then again, what has the whale to say?", "다만 고래가 그토록 기이하게 우르릉거릴 때 코로 말을 한다고 하여 그를 모욕하려는 것이 아니라면 말이다."], ["Seldom have I known any profound being that had anything to say to this world, unless forced to stammer out something by way of getting a living.", "하지만 그렇다 한들, 고래가 무슨 할 말이 있겠는가? 내가 아는 한, 깊이 있는 존재치고 이 세상에 무언가 할 말이 있는 자는 드물었으니, 다만 입에 풀칠이라도 하려고 더듬더듬 무언가를 억지로 내뱉지 않는 한에는 그러했다."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["happy that the world is such an excellent listener!", "세상이 이토록 훌륭한 청자(聽者)라니 얼마나 다행한 일인가!"]], [["Now, the spouting canal of the Sperm Whale, chiefly intended as it is for the conveyance of air, and for several feet laid along, horizontally, just beneath the upper surface of his head, and a little to one side; this curious canal is very much like a gas-pipe laid down in a city on one side of a street.", "자, 향유고래의 물뿜는 관은 주로 공기를 실어 나르기 위한 것으로, 머리 윗면 바로 아래에 한쪽으로 약간 치우쳐 몇 자 길이로 수평으로 놓여 있다. 이 기묘한 관은 도시의 길 한쪽에 묻어 놓은 가스관과 자못 닮았다."], ["But the question returns whether this gas-pipe is also a water-pipe; in other words, whether the spout of the Sperm Whale is the mere vapor of the exhaled breath, or whether that exhaled breath is mixed with water taken in at the mouth, and discharged through the spiracle.", "그러나 다시 의문이 되돌아온다. 이 가스관이 수도관이기도 한가?"], ["It is certain that the mouth indirectly communicates with the spouting canal; but it cannot be proved that this is for the purpose of discharging water through the spiracle.", "다시 말해, 향유고래의 물뿜음은 내쉰 숨의 한낱 수증기일 뿐인가, 아니면 그 내쉰 숨에 입으로 빨아들인 물이 섞여 분수공으로 뿜어 나오는 것인가?"], ["Because the greatest necessity for so doing would seem to be, when in feeding he accidentally takes in water.", "입이 물뿜는 관과 간접적으로 통한다는 것은 분명하다."], ["But the Sperm Whale’s food is far beneath the surface, and there he cannot spout even if he would.", "그러나 이것이 분수공으로 물을 내보내기 위함이라고는 증명할 수 없다."], ["Besides, if you regard him very closely, and time him with your watch, you will find that when unmolested, there is an undeviating rhyme between the periods of his jets and the ordinary periods of respiration.", "왜냐하면 그렇게 할 가장 큰 필요는, 먹이를 먹다가 어쩌다 물을 함께 들이켰을 때일 텐데, 향유고래의 먹이는 수면 한참 아래에 있고, 거기서는 설령 뿜고 싶어도 뿜을 수가 없기 때문이다. 게다가 고래를 아주 면밀히 관찰하며 시계로 시간을 재 보면, 방해받지 않을 때 그 물줄기를 뿜는 간격과 평상시 호흡하는 간격 사이에는 한 치도 어긋나지 않는 운율이 있음을 알게 될 것이다."]], [["But why pester one with all this reasoning on the subject?", "그런데 어찌하여 이 주제에 관한 온갖 논증으로 사람을 들볶는단 말인가?"], ["Speak out!", "솔직히 말해 보라!"], ["You have seen him spout; then declare what the spout is; can you not tell water from air?", "그대는 고래가 물 뿜는 것을 보았으니, 그렇다면 그 물뿜음이 무엇인지 단언해 보라. 물과 공기쯤이야 구별할 수 있지 않은가?"], ["My dear sir, in this world it is not so easy to settle these plain things.", "친애하는 선생이여, 이 세상에서 이런 빤한 것들을 가려내기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니라오."], ["I have ever found your plain things the knottiest of all.", "나는 늘 빤한 것이야말로 가장 풀기 어려운 매듭임을 깨닫곤 했소."], ["And as for this whale spout, you might almost stand in it, and yet be undecided as to what it is precisely.", "그리고 이 고래의 물뿜음으로 말하자면, 그대가 그 안에 거의 들어서 있다 한들, 그것이 정확히 무엇인지는 여전히 가늠하지 못할 것이오."]], [["The central body of it is hidden in the snowy sparkling mist enveloping it; and how can you certainly tell whether any water falls from it, when, always, when you are close enough to a whale to get a close view of his spout, he is in a prodigious commotion, the water cascading all around him. And if at such times you should think that you really perceived drops of moisture in the spout, how do you know that they are not merely condensed from its vapor; or how do you know that they are not those identical drops superficially lodged in the spout-hole fissure, which is countersunk into the summit of the whale’s head? For even when tranquilly swimming through the mid-day sea in a calm, with his elevated hump sun-dried as a dromedary’s in the desert; even then, the whale always carries a small basin of water on his head, as under a blazing sun you will sometimes see a cavity in a rock filled up with rain.", "그 물뿜음의 중심부는 그것을 에워싼 눈처럼 희고 반짝이는 안개 속에 가려져 있다. 그러니 그대가 거기서 정말로 물방울이 떨어지는지 어찌 확실히 가려낼 수 있겠는가. 고래의 물뿜음을 가까이서 볼 만큼 그에게 다가갈 즈음이면, 고래는 늘 어마어마한 소동 속에 있어 물이 그 둘레로 폭포처럼 쏟아지고 있을 테니 말이다. 또한 그런 순간에 그대가 정말로 물뿜음 속에서 물기 어린 방울들을 보았다고 여긴다 한들, 그것들이 한낱 그 수증기에서 응결된 것이 아니라고 어찌 알 수 있겠는가. 또 그것들이, 고래 머리 꼭대기에 파묻혀 들어간 그 분수공 틈새에 표면적으로 고여 있던 바로 그 물방울들이 아니라고 어찌 알 수 있겠는가. 잔잔한 한낮의 바다를 평온히 헤엄치며, 사막의 단봉낙타 등처럼 솟아오른 혹이 햇볕에 바싹 말라 있을 때조차, 고래는 늘 제 머리 위에 작은 물웅덩이 하나를 이고 다닌다. 마치 이글거리는 햇볕 아래 바위에 팬 구멍 하나가 빗물로 가득 차 있는 것을 그대가 이따금 보듯이."]], [["Nor is it at all prudent for the hunter to be over curious touching the precise nature of the whale spout.", "또한 사냥꾼이 고래 물뿜음의 정확한 성질에 관해 지나치게 호기심을 부리는 것은 결코 현명한 짓이 못 된다."], ["It will not do for him to be peering into it, and putting his face in it.", "그가 그 속을 들여다보거나 거기에 얼굴을 들이미는 짓은 해서는 안 된다."], ["You cannot go with your pitcher to this fountain and fill it, and bring it away.", "그대는 물병을 들고 이 분수로 가서 그것을 채워 떠 올 수가 없다."], ["For even when coming into slight contact with the outer, vapory shreds of the jet, which will often happen, your skin will feverishly smart, from the acridness of the thing so touching it.", "흔히 일어나는 일이지만, 그 물줄기의 바깥쪽 자욱한 안개 자락에 조금만 닿아도, 그토록 닿은 물질의 독한 기운 때문에 그대의 살갗은 화끈거리며 쓰라릴 것이기 때문이다."], ["And I know one, who coming into still closer contact with the spout, whether with some scientific object in view, or otherwise, I cannot say, the skin peeled off from his cheek and arm.", "내가 아는 어떤 이는 그 물뿜음에 한층 더 가까이 닿았는데 — 무슨 과학적 목적이 있었는지 아니면 다른 까닭이었는지는 나도 알 수 없으나 — 그의 뺨과 팔에서 살갗이 벗겨져 버렸다."], ["Wherefore, among whalemen, the spout is deemed poisonous; they try to evade it.", "그렇기에 고래잡이들 사이에서 그 물뿜음은 독성이 있다고 여겨지며, 그들은 그것을 피하려 애쓴다."], ["Another thing; I have heard it said, and I do not much doubt it, that if the jet is fairly spouted into your eyes, it will blind you.", "또 한 가지, 그 물줄기가 정통으로 그대의 두 눈에 뿜어지면 눈이 멀 수도 있다는 말을 나는 들은 적이 있는데, 나는 그 말을 그리 의심하지 않는다."], ["The wisest thing the investigator can do then, it seems to me, is to let this deadly spout alone.", "그러니 탐구자가 할 수 있는 가장 현명한 일은, 내 보기에는, 이 치명적인 물뿜음을 그냥 내버려 두는 것이리라."]], [["Still, we can hypothesize, even if we cannot prove and establish.", "그래도 우리는 증명하고 확립하지는 못할지언정 가설을 세워 볼 수는 있다."], ["My hypothesis is this: that the spout is nothing but mist.", "내 가설은 이러하다. 그 물뿜음은 안개에 지나지 않는다는 것이다."], ["And besides other reasons, to this conclusion I am impelled, by considerations touching the great inherent dignity and sublimity of the Sperm Whale; I account him no common, shallow being, inasmuch as it is an undisputed fact that he is never found on soundings, or near shores; all other whales sometimes are.", "그리고 다른 여러 이유 외에도, 나는 향유고래에게 깃든 그 크나큰 본연의 존엄과 숭고함에 관한 고려에서 이 결론으로 이끌린다. 나는 그를 흔하고 천박한 존재로 여기지 않으니, 그가 결코 측심(測深) 가능한 얕은 물이나 해안 가까이에서는 발견되지 않는다는 것이 반박의 여지 없는 사실이기 때문이다."], ["He is both ponderous and profound.", "다른 모든 고래들은 더러 그런 곳에 나타나기도 하건만 말이다."], ["And I am convinced that from the heads of all ponderous profound beings, such as Plato, Pyrrho, the Devil, Jupiter, Dante, and so on, there always goes up a certain semi-visible steam, while in the act of thinking deep thoughts.", "그는 묵직하고도 심오하다. 그리고 나는 확신하건대, 플라톤이든 피론이든 악마든 유피테르든 단테든, 그 밖에 묵직하고 심오한 모든 존재들의 머리에서는, 깊은 사색에 잠겨 있는 동안 늘 어떤 반쯤 보이는 듯한 김이 피어오른다."], ["While composing a little treatise on Eternity, I had the curiosity to place a mirror before me; and ere long saw reflected there, a curious involved worming and undulation in the atmosphere over my head.", "「영원에 관하여」라는 작은 논문을 짓는 동안, 나는 호기심에 거울 하나를 내 앞에 놓아 보았다. 그러자 머지않아 내 머리 위 대기 속에서 기묘하게 얽히며 꿈틀거리고 일렁이는 무언가가 거기 비치는 것을 보았다."], ["The invariable moisture of my hair, while plunged in deep thought, after six cups of hot tea in my thin shingled attic, of an August noon; this seems an additional argument for the above supposition.", "팔월 한낮, 얇은 널빤지를 댄 다락방에서 뜨거운 차를 여섯 잔 들이켜고 깊은 사색에 잠겨 있을 때 내 머리카락이 어김없이 축축해지던 것 — 이 또한 앞의 추정을 뒷받침하는 또 하나의 논거인 듯하다."]], [["And how nobly it raises our conceit of the mighty, misty monster, to behold him solemnly sailing through a calm tropical sea; his vast, mild head overhung by a canopy of vapor, engendered by his incommunicable contemplations, and that vapor—as you will sometimes see it—glorified by a rainbow, as if Heaven itself had put its seal upon his thoughts.", "그리고 저 거대하고 안개 자욱한 괴물이, 잔잔한 열대의 바다를 엄숙히 항해해 가는 모습을 바라볼 때, 그것이 얼마나 고귀하게 우리의 상상을 드높여 주는가. 그의 광대하고 온화한 머리 위로는, 누구에게도 전할 수 없는 그 사색이 빚어낸 수증기의 차양이 드리워 있고, 그 수증기는 — 그대가 이따금 보게 되듯 — 무지개로 영광스럽게 빛나니, 마치 하늘이 친히 그의 생각 위에 옥새를 찍어 놓은 듯하다."], ["For, d’ye see, rainbows do not visit the clear air; they only irradiate vapor.", "보라, 무지개는 맑은 대기에는 깃들지 않는다. 무지개는 오직 수증기만을 환히 비춰 줄 뿐이다."], ["And so, through all the thick mists of the dim doubts in my mind, divine intuitions now and then shoot, enkindling my fog with a heavenly ray.", "그리하여 내 마음속 어슴푸레한 의심의 그 두꺼운 안개를 뚫고, 신성한 직관이 이따금 화살처럼 쏘아 들어와, 내 안개를 천상의 빛줄기로 환히 밝힌다."], ["And for this I thank God; for all have doubts; many deny; but doubts or denials, few along with them, have intuitions.", "이 일로 나는 하느님께 감사드린다. 누구에게나 의심은 있고, 많은 이가 부정하지만, 의심하거나 부정하면서 그와 더불어 직관까지 지닌 이는 드물기 때문이다."], ["Doubts of all things earthly, and intuitions of some things heavenly; this combination makes neither believer nor infidel, but makes a man who regards them both with equal eye.", "이 땅의 온갖 것들에 대한 의심과, 천상의 어떤 것들에 대한 직관 — 이 둘의 결합은 신자도 불신자도 만들어 내지 않으며, 다만 그 둘을 한결같은 눈으로 바라보는 사람을 만들어 낸다."]], [["# CHAPTER 86. The Tail.", "# CHAPTER 86. The Tail."]], [["Other poets have warbled the praises of the soft eye of the antelope, and the lovely plumage of the bird that never alights; less celestial, I celebrate a tail.", "다른 시인들은 영양의 부드러운 눈동자와 결코 내려앉지 않는 새의 사랑스러운 깃털을 노래했지만, 나는 그보다 덜 천상적인 것, 곧 꼬리를 기린다."]], [["Reckoning the largest sized Sperm Whale’s tail to begin at that point of the trunk where it tapers to about the girth of a man, it comprises upon its upper surface alone, an area of at least fifty square feet.", "가장 큰 향유고래의 꼬리가, 몸통이 가늘어져 사람의 둘레쯤 되는 그 지점에서 시작된다고 본다면, 그 윗면만으로도 적어도 50제곱피트(약 4.6제곱미터)의 넓이를 차지한다."], ["The compact round body of its root expands into two broad, firm, flat palms or flukes, gradually shoaling away to less than an inch in thickness.", "그 뿌리의 단단하고 둥근 몸체는 넓고 단단하며 평평한 두 개의 손바닥, 곧 갈래꼬리로 펼쳐지면서, 차츰 얕아져 1인치(약 2.5센티미터)도 안 되는 두께까지 가늘어진다."], ["At the crotch or junction, these flukes slightly overlap, then sideways recede from each other like wings, leaving a wide vacancy between.", "그 갈래지는 부분, 곧 접합부에서 이 갈래꼬리는 살짝 겹쳐졌다가, 다시 날개처럼 양옆으로 멀어지며 그 사이에 넓은 빈 공간을 남긴다."], ["In no living thing are the lines of beauty more exquisitely defined than in the crescentic borders of these flukes.", "살아 있는 그 어떤 것에서도 이 갈래꼬리의 초승달 모양 가장자리만큼 아름다운 선이 정교하게 그려진 것은 없다."], ["At its utmost expansion in the full grown whale, the tail will considerably exceed twenty feet across.", "다 자란 고래에서 그 꼬리가 최대로 펼쳐지면, 폭이 20피트(약 6미터)를 훌쩍 넘는다."]], [["The entire member seems a dense webbed bed of welded sinews; but cut into it, and you find that three distinct strata compose it:—upper, middle, and lower.", "이 기관 전체는 용접된 힘줄들이 빽빽하게 짜여 이룬 받침처럼 보이지만, 잘라 보면 세 개의 뚜렷한 층으로 이루어져 있음을 알게 된다 — 상층, 중층, 하층이다."], ["The fibres in the upper and lower layers, are long and horizontal; those of the middle one, very short, and running crosswise between the outside layers.", "상층과 하층의 섬유는 길고 수평으로 뻗어 있으며, 중층의 섬유는 아주 짧고 바깥쪽 두 층 사이를 가로질러 달린다."], ["This triune structure, as much as anything else, imparts power to the tail.", "이 삼위일체의 구조야말로 무엇보다도 꼬리에 힘을 부여한다."], ["To the student of old Roman walls, the middle layer will furnish a curious parallel to the thin course of tiles always alternating with the stone in those wonderful relics of the antique, and which undoubtedly contribute so much to the great strength of the masonry.", "고대 로마의 성벽을 연구하는 이라면, 이 중층이 저 경이로운 고대 유적들에서 언제나 돌과 번갈아 끼워진 얇은 벽돌 층과 묘하게 닮았음을 발견할 것이며, 그 벽돌 층이 석조 구조물의 막대한 견고함에 크게 기여한다는 사실은 의심할 여지가 없다."]], [["But as if this vast local power in the tendinous tail were not enough, the whole bulk of the leviathan is knit over with a warp and woof of muscular fibres and filaments, which passing on either side the loins and running down into the flukes, insensibly blend with them, and largely contribute to their might; so that in the tail the confluent measureless force of the whole whale seems concentrated to a point.", "그러나 힘줄로 이루어진 꼬리의 이 거대한 국부적 힘만으로는 부족하다는 듯, 리바이어던의 온몸은 근육 섬유와 가닥들의 날실과 씨실로 뜨개질되어 있는데, 이것들이 허리 양옆을 지나 갈래꼬리 속으로 흘러들어, 어느새 그것들과 한데 섞이며 그 위력에 크게 보탬을 준다. 그리하여 꼬리에는 고래 전체의 합류된 가늠할 수 없는 힘이 한 점으로 응축된 듯하다."], ["Could annihilation occur to matter, this were the thing to do it.", "물질에 소멸이 일어날 수 있다면, 바로 이것이 그 일을 해낼 물건이리라."]], [["Nor does this—its amazing strength, at all tend to cripple the graceful flexion of its motions; where infantileness of ease undulates through a Titanism of power.", "게다가 이 놀라운 힘은 결코 그 움직임의 우아한 굴신을 조금도 손상시키지 않으니, 그 안에서는 어린아이 같은 편안함이 거인적 힘을 헤치며 물결친다."], ["On the contrary, those motions derive their most appalling beauty from it.", "오히려 그 움직임은 바로 그 힘에서 가장 두려운 아름다움을 얻는다."], ["Real strength never impairs beauty or harmony, but it often bestows it; and in everything imposingly beautiful, strength has much to do with the magic.", "참된 힘은 결코 아름다움이나 조화를 해치지 않으며, 도리어 그것을 부여하는 일이 잦다. 그리고 위풍당당하게 아름다운 모든 것에서, 힘은 그 마력과 깊은 관계가 있다."], ["Take away the tied tendons that all over seem bursting from the marble in the carved Hercules, and its charm would be gone.", "조각된 헤라클레스 상의 대리석에서 사방으로 터져 나올 듯한 동여매인 힘줄들을 걷어 내 보라, 그러면 그 매력은 사라지고 말 것이다."], ["As devout Eckerman lifted the linen sheet from the naked corpse of Goethe, he was overwhelmed with the massive chest of the man, that seemed as a Roman triumphal arch.", "경건한 에커만이 괴테(Goethe)의 벌거벗은 주검에서 아마포 천을 걷어 올렸을 때, 그는 로마의 개선문처럼 보이는 그 사내의 육중한 가슴에 압도되고 말았다."], ["When Angelo paints even God the Father in human form, mark what robustness is there.", "안젤로가 성부 하느님조차 인간의 형상으로 그릴 때, 그 안에 깃든 강건함을 보라."], ["And whatever they may reveal of the divine love in the Son, the soft, curled, hermaphroditical Italian pictures, in which his idea has been most successfully embodied; these pictures, so destitute as they are of all brawniness, hint nothing of any power, but the mere negative, feminine one of submission and endurance, which on all hands it is conceded, form the peculiar practical virtues of his teachings.", "그리고 그 성자에게 깃든 신성한 사랑을 무엇으로 드러내든, 그분의 관념이 가장 성공적으로 구현된 저 부드럽고 곱슬곱슬하며 자웅동체 같은 이탈리아 그림들은, 근육미라고는 도무지 찾아볼 수 없어, 그 어떤 힘도 암시하지 못하고 다만 순종과 인내라는 소극적이고 여성적인 힘만을 비칠 뿐인데, 누구나 인정하듯 그것이야말로 그분 가르침의 독특한 실천적 미덕을 이룬다."]], [["Such is the subtle elasticity of the organ I treat of, that whether wielded in sport, or in earnest, or in anger, whatever be the mood it be in, its flexions are invariably marked by exceeding grace.", "내가 다루는 이 기관의 미묘한 탄력은 어찌나 절묘한지, 그것이 장난으로 휘둘리든, 진지하게 휘둘리든, 분노로 휘둘리든, 어떤 기분에 놓여 있든, 그 굴신은 한결같이 지극한 우아함으로 빛난다."], ["Therein no fairy’s arm can transcend it.", "그 점에서는 어떤 요정의 팔도 이를 능가하지 못한다."]], [["Five great motions are peculiar to it. First, when used as a fin for progression; Second, when used as a mace in battle; Third, in sweeping; Fourth, in lobtailing; Fifth, in peaking flukes.", "이 꼬리에는 다섯 가지 큰 움직임이 특유하다. 첫째, 전진을 위한 지느러미로 쓰일 때. 둘째, 전투에서 철퇴로 쓰일 때. 셋째, 후려치기. 넷째, 꼬리치기. 다섯째, 갈래꼬리 곧추세우기다."]], [["First: Being horizontal in its position, the Leviathan’s tail acts in a different manner from the tails of all other sea creatures.", "첫째. 그 위치가 수평이므로, 리바이어던의 꼬리는 다른 모든 바다 생물의 꼬리와는 다른 방식으로 작동한다."], ["It never wriggles.", "그것은 결코 꿈틀거리지 않는다."], ["In man or fish, wriggling is a sign of inferiority.", "사람에게서든 물고기에게서든 꿈틀거림은 열등함의 징표다."], ["To the whale, his tail is the sole means of propulsion.", "고래에게 꼬리는 유일한 추진 수단이다."], ["Scroll-wise coiled forwards beneath the body, and then rapidly sprung backwards, it is this which gives that singular darting, leaping motion to the monster when furiously swimming.", "두루마리처럼 몸 아래로 앞을 향해 말려 있다가 재빠르게 뒤로 튕겨 나가는 이 동작이, 사납게 헤엄칠 때 그 괴물에게 그토록 독특한 쏜살같고 도약하는 움직임을 부여한다."], ["His side-fins only serve to steer by.", "옆지느러미는 다만 방향을 잡는 데에만 쓰일 뿐이다."]], [["Second: It is a little significant, that while one sperm whale only fights another sperm whale with his head and jaw, nevertheless, in his conflicts with man, he chiefly and contemptuously uses his tail.", "둘째. 한 향유고래가 다른 향유고래와 싸울 때는 머리와 턱만으로 싸우면서도, 사람과 맞붙을 때는 주로, 그것도 경멸하듯 꼬리를 쓴다는 것은 다소 의미심장하다."], ["In striking at a boat, he swiftly curves away his flukes from it, and the blow is only inflicted by the recoil.", "보트를 향해 칠 때, 고래는 갈래꼬리를 잽싸게 보트에서 휘어 빼내며, 그 타격은 오로지 되튕김으로 가해진다."], ["If it be made in the unobstructed air, especially if it descend to its mark, the stroke is then simply irresistible.", "만약 그 타격이 거침없는 허공에서, 특히 목표를 향해 내리꽂힐 때 이루어진다면, 그 일격은 그야말로 막아 낼 도리가 없다."], ["No ribs of man or boat can withstand it.", "사람의 갈비뼈도 보트의 늑재도 그것을 견디지 못한다."], ["Your only salvation lies in eluding it; but if it comes sideways through the opposing water, then partly owing to the light buoyancy of the whale-boat, and the elasticity of its materials, a cracked rib or a dashed plank or two, a sort of stitch in the side, is generally the most serious result.", "살아남을 유일한 길은 그것을 피하는 것뿐이다. 그러나 그것이 가로막는 물살을 가르며 옆에서 날아올 경우에는, 고래잡이 보트의 가벼운 부력과 그 재료의 탄력 덕분에, 늑재 하나가 금이 가거나 널판 한두 장이 부서지는 정도, 말하자면 옆구리가 살짝 결리는 정도가 대개 가장 심각한 결과다."], ["These submerged side blows are so often received in the fishery, that they are accounted mere child’s play.", "이렇게 물속에서 옆으로 받는 타격은 고래잡이에서 워낙 자주 겪는 일이라, 그저 아이들 장난쯤으로 여겨진다."], ["Some one strips off a frock, and the hole is stopped.", "누군가 작업복 윗도리를 벗어 그 구멍을 틀어막으면 그만이다."]], [["Third: I cannot demonstrate it, but it seems to me, that in the whale the sense of touch is concentrated in the tail; for in this respect there is a delicacy in it only equalled by the daintiness of the elephant’s trunk.", "셋째. 증명할 수는 없으나, 내가 보기에 고래는 촉각이 꼬리에 집중되어 있는 듯하다."], ["This delicacy is chiefly evinced in the action of sweeping, when in maidenly gentleness the whale with a certain soft slowness moves his immense flukes from side to side upon the surface of the sea; and if he feel but a sailor’s whisker, woe to that sailor, whiskers and all.", "이 점에서 그 꼬리에는 오직 코끼리 코의 섬세함에나 견줄 만한 예민함이 있기 때문이다. 이 섬세함은 주로 후려치는 동작에서 드러나는데, 그때 고래는 처녀처럼 부드럽게, 어떤 나긋한 느릿함으로 그 거대한 갈래꼬리를 바다 표면 위에서 이쪽저쪽으로 움직인다."], ["What tenderness there is in that preliminary touch!", "그러다 선원의 구레나룻 한 올이라도 느끼면, 그 선원에게는 화가 미치리니, 구레나룻이고 뭐고 다 끝장이다. 그 예비적인 접촉에 깃든 부드러움이라니!"], ["Had this tail any prehensile power, I should straightway bethink me of Darmonodes’ elephant that so frequented the flower-market, and with low salutations presented nosegays to damsels, and then caressed their zones.", "이 꼬리에 무언가를 움켜쥐는 능력이 있었다면, 나는 당장 다르모노데스의 코끼리를 떠올렸을 것이다. 그 코끼리는 꽃시장을 어찌나 자주 드나들었던지, 낮게 인사를 건네며 처녀들에게 꽃다발을 바치고는 그들의 허리띠를 어루만지곤 했다."], ["On more accounts than one, a pity it is that the whale does not possess this prehensile virtue in his tail; for I have heard of yet another elephant, that when wounded in the fight, curved round his trunk and extracted the dart.", "고래의 꼬리에 이렇게 움켜쥐는 능력이 없다는 것은 여러모로 안타까운 일이다. 또 다른 코끼리에 관해서도 들은 바가 있는데, 그놈은 싸움에서 상처를 입자 코를 둥글게 말아 박힌 투창을 뽑아냈다고 한다."]], [["Fourth: Stealing unawares upon the whale in the fancied security of the middle of solitary seas, you find him unbent from the vast corpulence of his dignity, and kitten-like, he plays on the ocean as if it were a hearth.", "넷째. 고독한 바다 한가운데서 안전하다 믿고 있는 고래에게 눈치채지 못하게 살그머니 다가가 보면, 그 위엄의 방대한 비만함을 풀어헤친 채 새끼고양이처럼 대양을 마치 난롯가인 양 여기며 노니는 모습을 보게 된다."], ["But still you see his power in his play.", "그래도 그 장난 속에서 여전히 그 힘이 엿보인다."], ["The broad palms of his tail are flirted high into the air; then smiting the surface, the thunderous concussion resounds for miles.", "그 꼬리의 넓은 손바닥이 허공 높이 휙 치켜올려졌다가, 수면을 내리치면 천둥 같은 충격음이 몇 마일에 걸쳐 울려 퍼진다."], ["You would almost think a great gun had been discharged; and if you noticed the light wreath of vapor from the spiracle at his other extremity, you would think that that was the smoke from the touch-hole.", "거대한 대포라도 발사된 줄로 알 만하다. 그리고 그 반대쪽 끝의 분수공에서 옅은 김 한 자락이 피어오르는 것을 보았다면, 그것이 점화구에서 나는 연기라고 여겼을 것이다."]], [["Fifth: As in the ordinary floating posture of the leviathan the flukes lie considerably below the level of his back, they are then completely out of sight beneath the surface; but when he is about to plunge into the deeps, his entire flukes with at least thirty feet of his body are tossed erect in the air, and so remain vibrating a moment, till they downwards shoot out of view.", "다섯째. 리바이어던이 평소 물 위에 떠 있는 자세에서는 갈래꼬리가 등보다 한참 아래에 놓여 수면 밑으로 완전히 자취를 감추지만, 깊은 바닷속으로 곤두박질치려 할 때면 그 갈래꼬리 전체가 적어도 몸의 30피트(약 9미터)와 더불어 허공으로 곧추 솟구쳐 올라, 잠시 떨며 그대로 머물다가 아래로 내리꽂혀 시야에서 사라진다."], ["Excepting the sublime breach—somewhere else to be described—this peaking of the whale’s flukes is perhaps the grandest sight to be seen in all animated nature.", "어딘가 다른 데서 서술할 저 숭고한 도약을 제외하면, 이 고래의 갈래꼬리 곧추세우기야말로 아마 살아 있는 자연 전체에서 볼 수 있는 가장 장엄한 광경일 것이다."], ["Out of the bottomless profundities the gigantic tail seems spasmodically snatching at the highest heaven.", "밑바닥 없는 심연에서 거대한 꼬리가 경련하듯 가장 높은 하늘을 움켜쥐려 드는 듯하다."], ["So in dreams, have I seen majestic Satan thrusting forth his tormented colossal claw from the flame Baltic of Hell.", "꿈속에서 나는 위엄 있는 사탄이 지옥의 불타는 발트해에서 고통에 찬 거대한 발톱을 내뻗는 것을 본 적이 있다."], ["But in gazing at such scenes, it is all in all what mood you are in; if in the Dantean, the devils will occur to you; if in that of Isaiah, the archangels.", "그러나 그러한 광경을 바라볼 때, 모든 것은 그대가 어떤 기분에 놓여 있느냐에 달려 있다. 단테적인 기분이라면 악마들이 떠오를 것이고, 이사야적인 기분이라면 대천사들이 떠오를 것이다."], ["Standing at the mast-head of my ship during a sunrise that crimsoned sky and sea, I once saw a large herd of whales in the east, all heading towards the sun, and for a moment vibrating in concert with peaked flukes.", "하늘과 바다를 진홍으로 물들이던 어느 일출 무렵, 나는 내 배의 돛대 꼭대기에 서 있다가 동쪽에 거대한 고래 떼가 모두 해를 향해 머리를 두르고서, 한순간 갈래꼬리를 곧추세운 채 일제히 떠는 것을 본 적이 있다."], ["As it seemed to me at the time, such a grand embodiment of adoration of the gods was never beheld, even in Persia, the home of the fire worshippers.", "그때 내게는 그토록 장엄하게 구현된 신들에 대한 경배가, 불을 숭배하는 자들의 고향인 페르시아에서조차 일찍이 본 적 없는 광경처럼 느껴졌다."], ["As Ptolemy Philopater testified of the African elephant, I then testified of the whale, pronouncing him the most devout of all beings.", "프톨레마이오스 필로파토르가 아프리카 코끼리에 대해 증언했듯, 나도 그때 고래에 대해 증언하노니, 그를 모든 존재 가운데 가장 경건한 것이라 선언하는 바다."], ["For according to King Juba, the military elephants of antiquity often hailed the morning with their trunks uplifted in the profoundest silence.", "유바 왕에 따르면, 고대의 전투 코끼리들은 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에서 코를 치켜든 채 아침을 맞이하곤 했다고 하기 때문이다."]], [["The chance comparison in this chapter, between the whale and the elephant, so far as some aspects of the tail of the one and the trunk of the other are concerned, should not tend to place those two opposite organs on an equality, much less the creatures to which they respectively belong.", "이 장에서 우연히 이루어진 고래와 코끼리의 비교는, 한쪽의 꼬리와 다른 쪽의 코의 어떤 측면에 관한 한, 그 두 상반된 기관을 대등하게 놓으려는 뜻이 아니며, 하물며 각각이 속한 두 생물을 동등하게 보려는 것은 더더욱 아니다."], ["For as the mightiest elephant is but a terrier to Leviathan, so, compared with Leviathan’s tail, his trunk is but the stalk of a lily.", "가장 막강한 코끼리조차 리바이어던에게는 한낱 테리어 강아지에 지나지 않듯, 리바이어던의 꼬리에 견주면 코끼리의 코는 백합의 줄기에 불과하기 때문이다."], ["The most direful blow from the elephant’s trunk were as the playful tap of a fan, compared with the measureless crush and crash of the sperm whale’s ponderous flukes, which in repeated instances have one after the other hurled entire boats with all their oars and crews into the air, very much as an Indian juggler tosses his balls.*", "코끼리의 코가 휘두르는 가장 무시무시한 일격도, 향유고래의 묵직한 갈래꼬리가 가하는 가늠할 수 없는 짓이김과 부서뜨림에 견주면 부채로 장난스레 톡 치는 것에 불과한데, 그 갈래꼬리는 거듭된 사례에서 노와 선원이 다 실린 보트들을 하나씩 잇따라 허공으로 내던졌으니, 그 모습은 인도의 곡예사가 공을 던져 올리는 것과 흡사했다.*"]], [["*Though all comparison in the way of general bulk between the whale and the elephant is preposterous, inasmuch as in that particular the elephant stands in much the same respect to the whale that a dog does to the elephant; nevertheless, there are not wanting some points of curious similitude; among these is the spout.", "* 고래와 코끼리를 전체적인 부피의 면에서 비교하는 것은 터무니없는 일이다. 그 점에서 코끼리는 고래에 대해 마치 개가 코끼리에 대해 지니는 것과 거의 같은 관계에 놓이기 때문이다."], ["It is well known that the elephant will often draw up water or dust in his trunk, and then elevating it, jet it forth in a stream.", "그럼에도 묘하게 닮은 점이 더러 없지 않은데, 그중 하나가 물줄기를 뿜는 것이다. 코끼리가 흔히 코로 물이나 흙을 빨아올린 다음, 그것을 치켜들어 한 줄기로 내뿜는다는 것은 잘 알려진 사실이다."]], [["The more I consider this mighty tail, the more do I deplore my inability to express it.", "이 막강한 꼬리를 곱씹어 볼수록, 나는 그것을 제대로 표현해 내지 못하는 내 무능을 더욱 한탄하게 된다."], ["At times there are gestures in it, which, though they would well grace the hand of man, remain wholly inexplicable.", "때로 그 꼬리에는, 사람의 손에 깃들었다면 능히 우아하게 어울렸을 법한, 그러면서도 도무지 설명할 길 없는 몸짓들이 담겨 있다."], ["In an extensive herd, so remarkable, occasionally, are these mystic gestures, that I have heard hunters who have declared them akin to Free-Mason signs and symbols; that the whale, indeed, by these methods intelligently conversed with the world.", "큰 무리 속에서 이 신비로운 몸짓들이 이따금 어찌나 놀라운지, 나는 그것을 프리메이슨의 신호나 상징에 빗대며, 고래가 실로 이러한 방식으로 세상과 지적으로 대화를 나눈다고 단언하는 사냥꾼들의 이야기를 들은 적이 있다."], ["Nor are there wanting other motions of the whale in his general body, full of strangeness, and unaccountable to his most experienced assailant.", "또한 고래의 몸 전체에는 그 밖에도 온갖 기이함으로 가득 차서, 가장 노련한 공격자조차 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 다른 움직임들이 적지 않다."], ["Dissect him how I may, then, I but go skin deep; I know him not, and never will.", "그러니 내가 어떻게 그를 해부하든, 나는 다만 살갗 한 꺼풀밖에 들어가지 못한다. 나는 그를 알지 못하며, 앞으로도 결코 알지 못할 것이다."], ["But if I know not even the tail of this whale, how understand his head?", "그러나 이 고래의 꼬리조차 알지 못한다면, 어찌 그 머리를 이해하겠는가?"], ["much more, how comprehend his face, when face he has none?", "더구나 그에게 얼굴이라는 것이 없을진대, 어찌 그 얼굴을 헤아리겠는가?"], ["Thou shalt see my back parts, my tail, he seems to say, but my face shall not be seen.", "너는 나의 등과 꼬리는 보겠으나, 나의 얼굴은 보지 못하리라, 하고 그는 말하는 듯하다."], ["But I cannot completely make out his back parts; and hint what he will about his face, I say again he has no face.", "그러나 나는 그의 등조차 온전히 알아내지 못하며, 그가 제 얼굴에 대해 무어라 넌지시 비치든, 나는 거듭 말하노니 그에게는 얼굴이 없다."]], [["# CHAPTER 87. The Grand Armada.", "# CHAPTER 87. The Grand Armada."]], [["The long and narrow peninsula of Malacca, extending south-eastward from the territories of Birmah, forms the most southerly point of all Asia.", "말라카의 길고 좁은 반도는 버마(Birmah)의 영토에서 남동쪽으로 뻗어 나가, 아시아 전체에서 가장 남쪽에 자리한 지점을 이룬다."], ["In a continuous line from that peninsula stretch the long islands of Sumatra, Java, Bally, and Timor; which, with many others, form a vast mole, or rampart, lengthwise connecting Asia with Australia, and dividing the long unbroken Indian ocean from the thickly studded oriental archipelagoes.", "그 반도로부터 하나의 연속된 선을 그리며 수마트라, 자바, 발리, 티모르의 긴 섬들이 늘어서 있고, 이것들은 다른 수많은 섬들과 더불어 아시아와 오스트레일리아를 길게 잇는 거대한 방파제 혹은 성벽을 이루어, 길고 끊임없는 인도양을 빽빽이 박힌 동방의 군도들로부터 갈라놓는다."], ["This rampart is pierced by several sally-ports for the convenience of ships and whales; conspicuous among which are the straits of Sunda and Malacca.", "이 성벽에는 배와 고래의 편의를 위한 출격용 통로가 여럿 뚫려 있는데, 그중에서도 눈에 띄는 것이 순다 해협과 말라카 해협이다."], ["By the straits of Sunda, chiefly, vessels bound to China from the west, emerge into the China seas.", "주로 순다 해협을 통하여, 서쪽에서 중국으로 향하는 선박들이 중국해로 빠져나온다."]], [["Those narrow straits of Sunda divide Sumatra from Java; and standing midway in that vast rampart of islands, buttressed by that bold green promontory, known to seamen as Java Head; they not a little correspond to the central gateway opening into some vast walled empire: and considering the inexhaustible wealth of spices, and silks, and jewels, and gold, and ivory, with which the thousand islands of that oriental sea are enriched, it seems a significant provision of nature, that such treasures, by the very formation of the land, should at least bear the appearance, however ineffectual, of being guarded from the all-grasping western world.", "저 좁은 순다 해협은 수마트라를 자바로부터 갈라놓는다. 그 광대한 섬들의 성벽 한가운데 자리잡고, 뱃사람들에게 자바 헤드라 알려진 저 대담한 초록빛 곶에 떠받쳐져 있는 그 해협은, 어느 광대한 성벽 둘린 제국으로 들어서는 중앙의 관문에 적잖이 들어맞는다."], ["The shores of the Straits of Sunda are unsupplied with those domineering fortresses which guard the entrances to the Mediterranean, the Baltic, and the Propontis.", "그리고 저 동방 바다의 수천 섬을 풍요롭게 하는 향료와 비단과 보석과 황금과 상아의 무진장한 부를 헤아려 보면, 그런 보물들이 땅의 생김새 그 자체로 인하여, 비록 헛된 일일지언정, 적어도 모든 것을 움켜쥐려 드는 서구 세계로부터 지켜지는 듯한 외양만이라도 갖추고 있다는 것은 자연의 의미심장한 안배인 듯싶다."], ["Unlike the Danes, these Orientals do not demand the obsequious homage of lowered top-sails from the endless procession of ships before the wind, which for centuries past, by night and by day, have passed between the islands of Sumatra and Java, freighted with the costliest cargoes of the east.", "순다 해협의 기슭에는 지중해와 발트해와 프로폰티스의 입구를 지키는 저 위압적인 요새들이 들어서 있지 않다. 덴마크인들과 달리 이 동방인들은, 수 세기 동안 밤낮으로 동방의 가장 값진 화물을 싣고 수마트라와 자바의 섬들 사이를 지나간 그 끝없는 순풍의 선단 행렬을 향하여, 윗돛을 내려 굽실거리는 경의를 표하라고 요구하지 않는다."], ["But while they freely waive a ceremonial like this, they do by no means renounce their claim to more solid tribute.", "그러나 그들은 이런 식의 의례는 선뜻 면제해 주면서도, 더 실속 있는 공물에 대한 요구만큼은 결코 포기하지 않는다."]], [["Time out of mind the piratical proas of the Malays, lurking among the low shaded coves and islets of Sumatra, have sallied out upon the vessels sailing through the straits, fiercely demanding tribute at the point of their spears.", "아득한 옛날부터 말레이인들의 해적선들은 수마트라의 그늘진 낮은 후미와 작은 섬들 사이에 숨어 있다가, 해협을 가로질러 항해하는 선박들 위로 뛰쳐나와 창끝을 들이대며 사납게 공물을 요구해 왔다."], ["Though by the repeated bloody chastisements they have received at the hands of European cruisers, the audacity of these corsairs has of late been somewhat repressed; yet, even at the present day, we occasionally hear of English and American vessels, which, in those waters, have been remorselessly boarded and pillaged.", "비록 유럽 순양함들의 손에 거듭 피의 응징을 받은 탓에 이 해적들의 만용이 근래 다소 수그러들기는 했으나, 오늘날에도 우리는 이따금 영국과 미국의 선박들이 그 해역에서 무자비하게 나포되고 약탈당했다는 소식을 듣곤 한다."]], [["With a fair, fresh wind, the Pequod was now drawing nigh to these straits; Ahab purposing to pass through them into the Javan sea, and thence, cruising northwards, over waters known to be frequented here and there by the Sperm Whale, sweep inshore by the Philippine Islands, and gain the far coast of Japan, in time for the great whaling season there.", "상쾌하고 알맞은 바람을 받으며 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 이제 그 해협으로 다가가고 있었다. 에이해브(Ahab)는 해협을 지나 자바해로 들어선 다음, 거기서 북쪽으로 항해하며 여기저기 향유고래가 자주 출몰한다고 알려진 바다를 가로질러, 필리핀 제도 연안을 따라 북상하여, 그곳의 대규모 포경 철에 맞추어 멀리 일본 해안에 닿을 작정이었다."], ["By these means, the circumnavigating Pequod would sweep almost all the known Sperm Whale cruising grounds of the world, previous to descending upon the Line in the Pacific; where Ahab, though everywhere else foiled in his pursuit, firmly counted upon giving battle to Moby Dick, in the sea he was most known to frequent; and at a season when he might most reasonably be presumed to be haunting it.", "이런 방법으로 세계를 일주하는 피쿼드호는, 태평양의 적도(Line)로 내려가기에 앞서, 세상에 알려진 거의 모든 향유고래 어장을 두루 훑게 될 터였다. 에이해브는 다른 모든 곳에서는 추격이 좌절될지라도, 모비 딕(Moby Dick)이 가장 자주 출몰한다고 알려진 그 바다에서, 또한 그놈이 거기 머물고 있으리라고 가장 합당하게 추정할 수 있는 그 철에, 기어이 그놈과 한판 싸움을 벌이리라고 굳게 믿었다."]], [["But how now?", "그러나 이건 또 어찌 된 일인가?"], ["in this zoned quest, does Ahab touch no land?", "이렇게 구역을 정해 놓고 벌이는 추적에서 에이해브는 결코 뭍을 밟지 않는단 말인가?"], ["does his crew drink air?", "그의 선원들은 공기를 마시고 사는가?"], ["Surely, he will stop for water. Nay.", "분명 물을 얻으러 어딘가에 들르긴 할 것이다."], ["For a long time, now, the circus-running sun has raced within his fiery ring, and needs no sustenance but what’s in himself.", "아니다. 이미 오래전부터, 곡마장을 돌듯 달리는 태양은 제 불의 고리 안을 질주하면서 제 안에 든 것 외에는 어떤 양식도 필요로 하지 않는다."], ["So Ahab. Mark this, too, in the whaler.", "에이해브가 그러하다."], ["While other hulls are loaded down with alien stuff, to be transferred to foreign wharves; the world-wandering whale-ship carries no cargo but herself and crew, their weapons and their wants.", "포경선에서도 이 점을 눈여겨보라. 다른 배들은 외국의 부두로 옮겨 갈 낯선 물건들로 가득 채워져 무겁게 가라앉지만, 세계를 떠도는 포경선은 자기 자신과 선원들, 그들의 무기와 그들의 필요 외에는 아무 화물도 싣지 않는다."], ["She has a whole lake’s contents bottled in her ample hold.", "포경선은 넉넉한 선창 속에 호수 하나만큼의 내용물을 병에 담아 두고 있다."], ["She is ballasted with utilities; not altogether with unusable pig-lead and kentledge.", "쓸 데 없는 무쇠 덩이나 짐돌만으로 채워진 것이 아니라, 쓸모 있는 것들로 균형을 잡고 있다."], ["She carries years’ water in her.", "포경선은 몇 년 치의 물을 그 안에 싣고 있다."], ["Clear old prime Nantucket water; which, when three years afloat, the Nantucketer, in the Pacific, prefers to drink before the brackish fluid, but yesterday rafted off in casks, from the Peruvian or Indian streams.", "맑고 오래되어 일품인 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 물, 바다에 떠 있은 지 삼 년이 되어도 태평양의 낸터킷 사람은, 어제 막 페루나 인도의 강에서 통에 담아 뗏목으로 실어 온 그 짭짤한 물보다 차라리 그것을 마시고 싶어 한다."], ["Hence it is, that, while other ships may have gone to China from New York, and back again, touching at a score of ports, the whale-ship, in all that interval, may not have sighted one grain of soil; her crew having seen no man but floating seamen like themselves.", "그러기에 다른 배들이 뉴욕(New)에서 중국까지 갔다가 다시 돌아오면서 수십 개의 항구에 들르는 동안, 포경선은 그 모든 기간에 흙 한 톨도 보지 못한 채, 선원들은 자기네와 같이 바다를 떠도는 뱃사람들 말고는 어느 누구도 본 적이 없는 일도 흔하다."], ["So that did you carry them the news that another flood had come; they would only answer—“Well, boys, here’s the ark!”", "그러니 그들에게 또 한 번의 대홍수가 닥쳐왔다는 소식을 전한다면, 그들은 그저 이렇게 대답할 따름이리라. \"거참, 친구들, 여기 방주가 있잖나!\""]], [["Now, as many Sperm Whales had been captured off the western coast of Java, in the near vicinity of the Straits of Sunda; indeed, as most of the ground, roundabout, was generally recognised by the fishermen as an excellent spot for cruising; therefore, as the Pequod gained more and more upon Java Head, the look-outs were repeatedly hailed, and admonished to keep wide awake.", "자, 향유고래는 자바 서쪽 해안, 곧 순다 해협의 가까운 인근에서 숱하게 잡혀 왔고, 실로 그 일대의 바다 대부분이 어부들 사이에서 항해하기 좋은 명소로 널리 인정받고 있었던 터라, 피쿼드호가 자바 헤드에 점점 더 가까워질수록 망꾼들은 거듭 호명되며 정신을 바짝 차리고 경계하라는 당부를 받았다."], ["But though the green palmy cliffs of the land soon loomed on the starboard bow, and with delighted nostrils the fresh cinnamon was snuffed in the air, yet not a single jet was descried.", "그러나 초록빛 야자나무 우거진 절벽이 머지않아 우현 뱃머리 쪽에 어렴풋이 떠오르고, 기쁨에 들뜬 콧구멍으로 신선한 계피 향이 공중에서 들이마셔졌건만, 물줄기는 단 한 줄기도 보이지 않았다."], ["Almost renouncing all thought of falling in with any game hereabouts, the ship had well nigh entered the straits, when the customary cheering cry was heard from aloft, and ere long a spectacle of singular magnificence saluted us.", "이 부근에서 사냥감을 만나리라는 생각을 거의 단념할 무렵, 배가 어느덧 해협에 들어섰을 때, 돛대 위에서 여느 때처럼 환호하는 외침이 들려왔고, 이윽고 더없이 장엄한 광경이 우리를 맞이했다."]], [["But here be it premised, that owing to the unwearied activity with which of late they have been hunted over all four oceans, the Sperm Whales, instead of almost invariably sailing in small detached companies, as in former times, are now frequently met with in extensive herds, sometimes embracing so great a multitude, that it would almost seem as if numerous nations of them had sworn solemn league and covenant for mutual assistance and protection.", "그러나 여기서 미리 일러둘 것이 있다. 근래 향유고래는 네 대양 전역에서 지칠 줄 모르는 활동으로 사냥당해 온 탓에, 옛날처럼 거의 어김없이 작고 흩어진 무리를 지어 다니는 대신, 이제는 흔히 광대한 떼를 이루어 나타나는데, 그 수가 어찌나 많은지 마치 수많은 고래 민족들이 서로 돕고 지켜 주자고 엄숙한 동맹과 맹약을 맺기라도 한 듯 보일 지경이다."], ["To this aggregation of the Sperm Whale into such immense caravans, may be imputed the circumstance that even in the best cruising grounds, you may now sometimes sail for weeks and months together, without being greeted by a single spout; and then be suddenly saluted by what sometimes seems thousands on thousands.", "향유고래가 이렇게 거대한 대상(隊商)으로 모여드는 것에, 가장 좋은 어장에서조차 이제는 이따금 몇 주, 몇 달을 내리 항해해도 물줄기 하나 만나지 못하다가, 별안간 때로는 수천 마리에 수천 마리가 더해진 듯한 광경의 환대를 받게 되는 사정을 돌릴 수 있겠다."]], [["Broad on both bows, at the distance of some two or three miles, and forming a great semicircle, embracing one half of the level horizon, a continuous chain of whale-jets were up-playing and sparkling in the noon-day air.", "양쪽 뱃머리 너머로 활짝, 약 이삼 마일쯤 떨어진 거리에서, 수평선의 절반을 끌어안으며 커다란 반원을 이룬 채, 끊임없이 이어지는 고래 물줄기의 사슬이 한낮의 대기 속에서 뿜어 올라 반짝이고 있었다."], ["Unlike the straight perpendicular twin-jets of the Right Whale, which, dividing at top, fall over in two branches, like the cleft drooping boughs of a willow, the single forward-slanting spout of the Sperm Whale presents a thick curled bush of white mist, continually rising and falling away to leeward.", "참고래(Right Whale)의 곧추선 한 쌍의 물줄기는 꼭대기에서 둘로 갈라져 마치 버드나무의 갈라져 늘어진 가지처럼 두 갈래로 떨어지지만, 그것과 달리 향유고래의 단 하나뿐인 앞으로 비스듬한 물줄기는 두툼하게 말려 오르는 흰 안개의 덤불을 이루며, 끊임없이 솟았다가는 바람 부는 쪽으로 흩어져 내린다."]], [["Seen from the Pequod’s deck, then, as she would rise on a high hill of the sea, this host of vapory spouts, individually curling up into the air, and beheld through a blending atmosphere of bluish haze, showed like the thousand cheerful chimneys of some dense metropolis, descried of a balmy autumnal morning, by some horseman on a height.", "그리하여 피쿼드호 갑판에서, 배가 바다의 높은 언덕 위로 솟구쳐 오를 때 바라보면, 저마다 공중으로 말려 올라가는 이 안개 같은 물줄기의 무리는, 푸르스름한 아지랑이가 한데 어우러진 대기를 통해, 향기로운 가을 아침에 어느 높은 곳에 선 말 탄 이의 눈에 비친, 어느 빽빽한 대도시의 수천 개 정겨운 굴뚝처럼 보였다."]], [["As marching armies approaching an unfriendly defile in the mountains, accelerate their march, all eagerness to place that perilous passage in their rear, and once more expand in comparative security upon the plain; even so did this vast fleet of whales now seem hurrying forward through the straits; gradually contracting the wings of their semicircle, and swimming on, in one solid, but still crescentic centre.", "산속의 험한 협로로 다가가는 행군 중인 군대가, 그 위험한 길목을 어서 등 뒤에 두고 다시 한번 비교적 안전한 평원에서 대오를 펼치고 싶은 일념에 행군을 재촉하듯이, 바로 그처럼 이 거대한 고래 함대도 이제 해협을 통과하려고 서둘러 앞으로 나아가는 듯 보였다. 반원의 양 날개를 점차 좁혀 가며, 하나의 단단하면서도 여전히 초승달 모양인 중심을 이루어 헤엄쳐 나아갔다."]], [["Crowding all sail the Pequod pressed after them; the harpooneers handling their weapons, and loudly cheering from the heads of their yet suspended boats.", "피쿼드호는 돛을 모조리 펼친 채 그것들을 뒤쫓아 밀고 나아갔다. 작살잡이들은 저마다 무기를 손에 들고, 아직 매달려 있는 보트의 이물에서 소리 높여 환호했다."], ["If the wind only held, little doubt had they, that chased through these Straits of Sunda, the vast host would only deploy into the Oriental seas to witness the capture of not a few of their number.", "바람만 버텨 준다면, 이 순다 해협을 통해 쫓긴 그 거대한 무리가 동방의 바다로 펼쳐져 나가, 제 동족 적잖은 수가 사로잡히는 것을 목격하게 되리라는 점에는 그들도 거의 의심이 없었다."], ["And who could tell whether, in that congregated caravan, Moby Dick himself might not temporarily be swimming, like the worshipped white-elephant in the coronation procession of the Siamese!", "게다가 그 모여든 대상 가운데, 시암 왕의 대관식 행렬에서 숭배받는 흰 코끼리처럼, 모비 딕 그놈이 잠시 헤엄치고 있을지 누가 알겠는가!"], ["So with stun-sail piled on stun-sail, we sailed along, driving these leviathans before us; when, of a sudden, the voice of Tashtego was heard, loudly directing attention to something in our wake.", "그리하여 보조돛 위에 보조돛을 겹겹이 쌓아 올린 채, 우리는 이 리바이어던들을 앞으로 몰아가며 항해해 나갔다. 그때 별안간 태시테고(Tashtego)의 목소리가 들려와, 우리 배 뒤쪽의 무언가에 주의를 기울이라고 큰 소리로 가리켰다."]], [["Corresponding to the crescent in our van, we beheld another in our rear.", "우리 앞쪽의 초승달에 상응하여, 우리 뒤쪽에서도 또 하나의 초승달이 보였다. 그것은 흩어진 흰 수증기로 이루어진 듯, 고래의 물줄기처럼 솟았다가 가라앉기를 반복했다."], ["It seemed formed of detached white vapors, rising and falling something like the spouts of the whales; only they did not so completely come and go; for they constantly hovered, without finally disappearing.", "다만 그것들은 그렇게 완전히 나타났다 사라지지는 않았으니, 끝내 사라지는 법 없이 줄곧 떠 있었다. 망원경을 그 광경에 겨눈 에이해브는 제 의족 구멍 속에서 휙 몸을 돌리며 외쳤다."], ["Levelling his glass at this sight, Ahab quickly revolved in his pivot-hole, crying, “Aloft there, and rig whips and buckets to wet the sails;—Malays, sir, and after us!”", "\"돛대 위로 올라가, 도르래와 양동이를 매어 돛을 적셔라. 말레이인들이오, 선장, 우리 뒤를 쫓고 있소!\""]], [["As if too long lurking behind the headlands, till the Pequod should fairly have entered the straits, these rascally Asiatics were now in hot pursuit, to make up for their over-cautious delay.", "이 비열한 아시아인들은 피쿼드호가 완전히 해협에 들어설 때까지 곶 뒤에 너무 오래 숨어 있었던 듯, 이제는 그 지나치게 조심스러운 지체를 만회하려는 듯 맹렬히 추격해 왔다."], ["But when the swift Pequod, with a fresh leading wind, was herself in hot chase; how very kind of these tawny philanthropists to assist in speeding her on to her own chosen pursuit,—mere riding-whips and rowels to her, that they were.", "그러나 날쌘 피쿼드호가 상쾌한 순풍을 받으며 그 자신이 한창 맹렬한 추격을 벌이는 중이었으니, 이 거무튀튀한 박애주의자들이 그 배를 제 스스로 택한 추격으로 더욱 빠르게 몰아 주는 데 일조하다니 얼마나 친절한가."], ["As with glass under arm, Ahab to-and-fro paced the deck; in his forward turn beholding the monsters he chased, and in the after one the bloodthirsty pirates chasing him; some such fancy as the above seemed his.", "그들은 그저 배의 채찍이요 박차였을 따름이었다. 망원경을 겨드랑이에 낀 채 에이해브는 갑판을 오락가락 거닐었는데, 앞으로 돌아설 때는 자기가 쫓는 괴물들을 바라보고, 뒤로 돌아설 때는 자기를 쫓는 피에 굶주린 해적들을 바라보았다."], ["And when he glanced upon the green walls of the watery defile in which the ship was then sailing, and bethought him that through that gate lay the route to his vengeance, and beheld, how that through that same gate he was now both chasing and being chased to his deadly end; and not only that, but a herd of remorseless wild pirates and inhuman atheistical devils were infernally cheering him on with their curses;—when all these conceits had passed through his brain, Ahab’s brow was left gaunt and ribbed, like the black sand beach after some stormy tide has been gnawing it, without being able to drag the firm thing from its place.", "위와 같은 어떤 상념이 그의 마음에 떠올랐던 듯하다. 그리고 그가 배가 그때 항해하던 물의 협로의 초록빛 벽을 흘끗 보고, 저 관문을 지나면 자기 복수의 길이 놓여 있음을 생각하고, 또한 바로 그 관문을 지나며 자기가 지금 쫓는 동시에 제 죽음의 끝을 향해 쫓기고 있음을, 게다가 그뿐 아니라 무자비한 야만의 해적 떼와 인정머리 없는 무신론자 악마들이 저주를 퍼부으며 지옥처럼 자기를 부추기고 있음을 바라보았을 때, 이 모든 상념이 그의 뇌리를 스쳐 지나갔을 때, 에이해브의 이마는 어떤 폭풍의 조수가 갉아먹어도 그 단단한 것을 제자리에서 끌어내지 못한 채 떠나 버린 뒤의 검은 모래 해변처럼, 야위고 골이 패여 남았다."]], [["But thoughts like these troubled very few of the reckless crew; and when, after steadily dropping and dropping the pirates astern, the Pequod at last shot by the vivid green Cockatoo Point on the Sumatra side, emerging at last upon the broad waters beyond; then, the harpooneers seemed more to grieve that the swift whales had been gaining upon the ship, than to rejoice that the ship had so victoriously gained upon the Malays.", "그러나 이런 상념으로 괴로워한 자는 무모한 선원들 가운데 극히 드물었다. 해적들을 꾸준히 뒤로, 뒤로 떨어뜨린 끝에 마침내 피쿼드호가 수마트라 쪽의 선명한 초록빛 코카투 곶을 쏜살같이 지나, 마침내 그 너머의 드넓은 바다로 빠져나왔을 때, 작살잡이들은 배가 말레이인들을 그토록 승리하듯 따돌렸다는 사실에 기뻐하기보다, 날쌘 고래들이 배보다 앞서가고 있다는 사실에 더 애를 태우는 듯했다."], ["But still driving on in the wake of the whales, at length they seemed abating their speed; gradually the ship neared them; and the wind now dying away, word was passed to spring to the boats.", "그러나 여전히 고래들의 자취를 따라 몰아가던 중, 이윽고 고래들이 속도를 늦추는 듯했다. 차츰 배가 그것들에 가까워졌고, 이제 바람이 잦아들자 보트로 뛰어내리라는 명령이 전해졌다."], ["But no sooner did the herd, by some presumed wonderful instinct of the Sperm Whale, become notified of the three keels that were after them,—though as yet a mile in their rear,—than they rallied again, and forming in close ranks and battalions, so that their spouts all looked like flashing lines of stacked bayonets, moved on with redoubled velocity.", "그러나 향유고래의 어떤 놀라운 본능이 작용했다고 짐작되는바, 그 무리가 자기네 뒤를 쫓는 세 척의 보트를, 비록 아직 한 마일이나 떨어져 있었으되, 알아채자마자 다시 결집하여, 빽빽한 대오와 대대를 이루어 그 물줄기가 모조리 빼곡히 세운 총검의 번쩍이는 줄처럼 보이게 한 채, 갑절의 속도로 나아갔다."]], [["Stripped to our shirts and drawers, we sprang to the white-ash, and after several hours’ pulling were almost disposed to renounce the chase, when a general pausing commotion among the whales gave animating token that they were now at last under the influence of that strange perplexity of inert irresolution, which, when the fishermen perceive it in the whale, they say he is gallied.", "셔츠와 속바지만 걸친 채 우리는 흰 물푸레나무 노를 잡고 뛰어들어, 여러 시간을 저은 끝에 추격을 거의 단념하려던 참이었다. 그때 고래들 사이에 전반적으로 멈칫거리는 동요가 일어, 그것들이 마침내 저 기이한 당혹, 곧 무력한 우유부단의 영향 아래 놓였다는 활기를 돋우는 징표를 보였다."], ["The compact martial columns in which they had been hitherto rapidly and steadily swimming, were now broken up in one measureless rout; and like King Porus’ elephants in the Indian battle with Alexander, they seemed going mad with consternation.", "어부들이 고래에게서 이런 기색을 알아챌 때, 그들은 그놈이 갈리(gallied)되었다고 말한다. 지금껏 빠르고 꾸준히 헤엄쳐 오던 그 빽빽한 전투 대형이 이제는 한없는 패주 속에 흐트러졌다."], ["In all directions expanding in vast irregular circles, and aimlessly swimming hither and thither, by their short thick spoutings, they plainly betrayed their distraction of panic.", "그리하여 알렉산드로스와의 인도 전투에서 포루스 왕의 코끼리들처럼, 그것들은 경악에 빠져 미쳐 날뛰는 듯했다. 사방으로 거대한 불규칙 원을 그리며 퍼져 나가, 목적도 없이 이리저리 헤엄치고, 짧고 두툼한 물줄기를 뿜어 댐으로써, 그것들은 공황에 휩싸인 혼란을 여실히 드러냈다."], ["This was still more strangely evinced by those of their number, who, completely paralysed as it were, helplessly floated like water-logged dismantled ships on the sea.", "이는 그것들 가운데 어떤 놈들, 곧 마치 완전히 마비되기라도 한 듯 물에 잠겨 의장이 뜯겨 나간 배처럼 속절없이 바다 위에 떠 있는 놈들에 의해 한층 더 기이하게 입증되었다."], ["Had these Leviathans been but a flock of simple sheep, pursued over the pasture by three fierce wolves, they could not possibly have evinced such excessive dismay.", "이 리바이어던들이 그저 단순한 양 떼였고, 세 마리 사나운 늑대에게 목초지를 가로질러 쫓겼다 한들, 결코 이토록 지나친 경악을 드러내지는 못했을 것이다."], ["But this occasional timidity is characteristic of almost all herding creatures.", "그러나 이런 이따금의 겁먹음은 거의 모든 무리 짓는 생물의 특성이다."], ["Though banding together in tens of thousands, the lion-maned buffaloes of the West have fled before a solitary horseman.", "수만 마리씩 떼를 지으면서도, 서부의 사자 갈기를 두른 들소들은 홀로 말 탄 사람 하나 앞에서 달아나곤 했다."], ["Witness, too, all human beings, how when herded together in the sheepfold of a theatre’s pit, they will, at the slightest alarm of fire, rush helter-skelter for the outlets, crowding, trampling, jamming, and remorselessly dashing each other to death.", "또한 모든 인간을 보라. 그들이 극장 일층 관람석의 양 우리에 떼 지어 몰려 있을 때, 불이 났다는 아주 작은 경보에도 어떻게 출구를 향해 허둥지둥 몰려들며, 밀치고 짓밟고 끼이고, 무자비하게 서로를 들이받아 죽음에 이르게 하는지를."], ["Best, therefore, withhold any amazement at the strangely gallied whales before us, for there is no folly of the beasts of the earth which is not infinitely outdone by the madness of men.", "그러므로 우리 앞에서 기이하게 갈리된 고래들에 대해 어떤 놀라움도 거두는 편이 낫겠다. 땅 위 짐승들의 그 어떤 어리석음도 인간의 광기에 끝없이 능가당하지 않는 것이 없기 때문이다."]], [["Though many of the whales, as has been said, were in violent motion, yet it is to be observed that as a whole the herd neither advanced nor retreated, but collectively remained in one place.", "앞서 말했듯이 고래 가운데 많은 놈들이 격렬히 움직이고 있었으나, 전체로 보면 그 무리는 앞으로 나아가지도 뒤로 물러나지도 않고 한데 모여 한자리에 머물러 있었음을 눈여겨봐야 한다."], ["As is customary in those cases, the boats at once separated, each making for some one lone whale on the outskirts of the shoal.", "이런 경우의 관례대로, 보트들은 즉시 흩어져 저마다 떼의 변두리에 있는 외톨이 고래 한 마리를 노렸다."], ["In about three minutes’ time, Queequeg’s harpoon was flung; the stricken fish darted blinding spray in our faces, and then running away with us like light, steered straight for the heart of the herd.", "약 삼 분쯤 지났을 때 퀴케그(Queequeg)의 작살이 날아갔다. 작살 맞은 고래는 눈을 멀게 하는 물보라를 우리 얼굴에 끼얹고는, 빛처럼 빠르게 우리를 끌고 달아나며 무리의 한복판을 향해 곧장 키를 잡았다."], ["Though such a movement on the part of the whale struck under such circumstances, is in no wise unprecedented; and indeed is almost always more or less anticipated; yet does it present one of the more perilous vicissitudes of the fishery.", "이런 상황에서 작살 맞은 고래가 보이는 이런 움직임이 결코 전례 없는 일은 아니고, 실로 거의 언제나 어느 정도는 예상되는 것이긴 하지만, 그래도 그것은 포경의 더 위태로운 변고 가운데 하나를 보여 준다."], ["For as the swift monster drags you deeper and deeper into the frantic shoal, you bid adieu to circumspect life and only exist in a delirious throb.", "그 날쌘 괴물이 미쳐 날뛰는 떼 속으로 그대를 점점 더 깊이 끌고 들어갈수록, 그대는 신중한 삶에 작별을 고하고 오직 정신없는 고동 속에서만 존재하게 되기 때문이다."]], [["As, blind and deaf, the whale plunged forward, as if by sheer power of speed to rid himself of the iron leech that had fastened to him; as we thus tore a white gash in the sea, on all sides menaced as we flew, by the crazed creatures to and fro rushing about us; our beset boat was like a ship mobbed by ice-isles in a tempest, and striving to steer through their complicated channels and straits, knowing not at what moment it may be locked in and crushed.", "눈멀고 귀먹은 채, 마치 순전한 속도의 힘만으로 제 몸에 들러붙은 무쇠 거머리를 떨쳐 내려는 듯 고래가 앞으로 곤두박질치는 동안, 우리가 그렇게 바다에 흰 상처를 째며 나아가는 동안, 사방에서 미쳐 날뛰는 짐승들이 우리 주위를 이리저리 휘몰아치며 우리를 위협했고, 그 한가운데서 포위된 우리 보트는 폭풍 속에서 빙산 떼에 둘러싸여, 언제 갇혀 으스러질지 모르는 채 그 뒤얽힌 수로와 해협을 헤치고 키를 잡으려 애쓰는 배와도 같았다."]], [["But not a bit daunted, Queequeg steered us manfully; now sheering off from this monster directly across our route in advance; now edging away from that, whose colossal flukes were suspended overhead, while all the time, Starbuck stood up in the bows, lance in hand, pricking out of our way whatever whales he could reach by short darts, for there was no time to make long ones.", "그러나 조금도 기죽지 않고 퀴케그는 우리를 사내답게 몰아갔다. 우리 진로 바로 앞을 가로지르는 이 괴물에게서 비켜 가는가 하면, 거대한 꼬리지느러미를 머리 위에 매단 저 괴물에게서 슬쩍 비껴 나기도 했다."], ["Nor were the oarsmen quite idle, though their wonted duty was now altogether dispensed with.", "그러는 내내 스타벅(Starbuck)은 이물에 서서 창을 손에 들고, 손이 닿는 고래는 무엇이든 짧게 찔러 우리 길에서 떼어 냈으니, 길게 찌를 틈이 없었기 때문이다."], ["They chiefly attended to the shouting part of the business.", "노잡이들도 마냥 한가하지만은 않았으니, 그들의 본분인 노 젓기는 이제 완전히 면제되었어도 그러했다."], ["“Out of the way, Commodore!”", "그들은 주로 고함치는 일을 맡았다."], ["cried one, to a great dromedary that of a sudden rose bodily to the surface, and for an instant threatened to swamp us.", "\"비키시오, 제독님!\" 한 사람이, 별안간 온몸을 수면 위로 솟구쳐 한순간 우리를 뒤엎을 듯 위협한 거대한 단봉낙타 같은 놈을 향해 외쳤다."], ["“Hard down with your tail, there!”", "\"이봐, 꼬리 좀 세게 내려치라고!\""], ["cried a second to another, which, close to our gunwale, seemed calmly cooling himself with his own fan-like extremity.", "또 한 사람이, 우리 뱃전 가까이에서 부채 같은 제 꼬리 끝으로 태연히 제 몸을 식히는 듯한 다른 놈을 향해 외쳤다."]], [["All whaleboats carry certain curious contrivances, originally invented by the Nantucket Indians, called druggs.", "모든 포경 보트는 본래 낸터킷의 인디언(Indian)들이 고안한, 드러그(drugg)라 불리는 기묘한 장치를 몇 개씩 싣고 다닌다."], ["Two thick squares of wood of equal size are stoutly clenched together, so that they cross each other’s grain at right angles; a line of considerable length is then attached to the middle of this block, and the other end of the line being looped, it can in a moment be fastened to a harpoon.", "같은 크기의 두툼한 정사각형 나무 두 개를 단단히 맞물려, 서로의 결이 직각으로 엇갈리게 한다. 그런 다음 이 나무토막의 한가운데에 상당히 긴 밧줄을 매고, 그 밧줄의 다른 끝을 고리로 만들어 두면, 순식간에 작살에 붙들어 맬 수 있다."], ["It is chiefly among gallied whales that this drugg is used.", "이 드러그는 주로 갈리된 고래들 사이에서 쓰인다."], ["For then, more whales are close round you than you can possibly chase at one time.", "그런 때에는 한 번에 쫓을 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 고래가 그대 주위에 바싹 모여 있기 때문이다."], ["But sperm whales are not every day encountered; while you may, then, you must kill all you can.", "그런데 향유고래는 날마다 만나는 것이 아니니, 그러므로 만날 수 있을 때 잡을 수 있는 만큼 모조리 잡아야 한다."], ["And if you cannot kill them all at once, you must wing them, so that they can be afterwards killed at your leisure.", "그리고 한꺼번에 다 죽일 수 없다면, 그것들을 절름발이로 만들어 두어 나중에 한가할 때 죽일 수 있게 해야 한다."], ["Hence it is, that at times like these the drugg, comes into requisition.", "그러기에 이런 때에 드러그가 요긴해진다."], ["Our boat was furnished with three of them.", "우리 보트에는 그것이 세 개 갖춰져 있었다."], ["The first and second were successfully darted, and we saw the whales staggeringly running off, fettered by the enormous sidelong resistance of the towing drugg.", "첫 번째와 두 번째는 성공적으로 던져졌고, 우리는 끌려가는 드러그의 어마어마한 옆쪽 저항에 발이 묶여 비틀거리며 달아나는 고래들을 보았다."], ["They were cramped like malefactors with the chain and ball.", "그것들은 쇠사슬과 쇠공에 묶인 죄인들처럼 옴짝달싹 못 했다."], ["But upon flinging the third, in the act of tossing overboard the clumsy wooden block, it caught under one of the seats of the boat, and in an instant tore it out and carried it away, dropping the oarsman in the boat’s bottom as the seat slid from under him.", "그러나 세 번째 것을 던지면서, 그 투박한 나무토막을 뱃전 밖으로 내던지려던 순간, 그것이 보트의 좌석 하나 밑에 걸려, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 좌석을 뜯어내 끌고 가 버렸고, 좌석이 밑에서 미끄러져 빠지자 노잡이는 보트 바닥에 나동그라졌다."], ["On both sides the sea came in at the wounded planks, but we stuffed two or three drawers and shirts in, and so stopped the leaks for the time.", "부서진 널빤지로 양쪽에서 바닷물이 밀려들었으나, 우리는 속바지와 셔츠 두세 벌을 쑤셔 넣어 우선은 새는 곳을 틀어막았다."]], [["It had been next to impossible to dart these drugged-harpoons, were it not that as we advanced into the herd, our whale’s way greatly diminished; moreover, that as we went still further and further from the circumference of commotion, the direful disorders seemed waning.", "이 드러그 작살들을 던지기란, 우리가 떼 속으로 나아갈수록 우리 고래의 속도가 크게 줄어들지 않았더라면, 게다가 우리가 동요의 가장자리에서 점점 더 멀어질수록 그 무시무시한 혼란이 잦아드는 듯하지 않았더라면, 거의 불가능했을 것이다."], ["So that when at last the jerking harpoon drew out, and the towing whale sideways vanished; then, with the tapering force of his parting momentum, we glided between two whales into the innermost heart of the shoal, as if from some mountain torrent we had slid into a serene valley lake.", "그리하여 마침내 홱홱 당기던 작살이 빠지고, 끌고 가던 고래가 옆으로 사라졌을 때, 우리는 그놈이 떨어져 나가며 남긴 점점 가늘어지는 추진력에 실려, 두 마리 고래 사이로 미끄러져 떼의 가장 깊은 한복판으로 들어갔으니, 마치 어느 산골짜기의 급류에서 잔잔한 골짜기 호수로 미끄러져 든 것만 같았다."], ["Here the storms in the roaring glens between the outermost whales, were heard but not felt.", "여기서는 가장 바깥쪽 고래들 사이의 포효하는 협곡에서 이는 폭풍이 들리기는 해도 느껴지지는 않았다."], ["In this central expanse the sea presented that smooth satin-like surface, called a sleek, produced by the subtle moisture thrown off by the whale in his more quiet moods.", "이 중앙의 너른 자리에서 바다는 매끄러운 새틴 같은 표면을 드러냈는데, 이를 슬릭(sleek)이라 부르며, 고래가 한결 잔잔한 기분일 때 내뿜는 미묘한 습기로 인해 생겨난다."], ["Yes, we were now in that enchanted calm which they say lurks at the heart of every commotion.", "그렇다, 우리는 이제 모든 동요의 한복판에 도사리고 있다고들 하는 저 마법에 걸린 고요 속에 들어 있었다."], ["And still in the distracted distance we beheld the tumults of the outer concentric circles, and saw successive pods of whales, eight or ten in each, swiftly going round and round, like multiplied spans of horses in a ring; and so closely shoulder to shoulder, that a Titanic circus-rider might easily have over-arched the middle ones, and so have gone round on their backs.", "그러면서도 어지러이 흐트러진 저 멀리서, 우리는 바깥쪽 동심원들의 소란을 바라보았고, 여덟 마리에서 열 마리씩 무리를 이룬 고래 떼들이, 마치 둥근 마장에서 여러 줄로 늘어선 말들처럼 빠르게 빙빙 돌고 또 도는 것을 보았는데, 어깨와 어깨가 어찌나 바싹 붙어 있던지, 거인 같은 곡마사라면 가운데 놈들 위로 가뿐히 다리를 걸쳐 그 등을 타고 빙 돌 수도 있었으리라."], ["Owing to the density of the crowd of reposing whales, more immediately surrounding the embayed axis of the herd, no possible chance of escape was at present afforded us.", "떼의 만(灣)처럼 둘러싸인 축을 한층 가까이 에워싼, 쉬고 있는 고래들이 빽빽이 들어찬 까닭에, 지금으로서는 우리에게 빠져나갈 가망이 전혀 없었다."], ["We must watch for a breach in the living wall that hemmed us in; the wall that had only admitted us in order to shut us up.", "우리는 우리를 가둔 살아 있는 벽, 곧 우리를 가두어 두려고만 들여보낸 그 벽에 틈이 생기기를 지켜봐야 했다."], ["Keeping at the centre of the lake, we were occasionally visited by small tame cows and calves; the women and children of this routed host.", "호수의 한가운데에 머무는 동안, 우리는 이따금 작고 온순한 암소들과 새끼들의 방문을 받았으니, 그것들은 이 패주한 무리의 아녀자들이었다."]], [["Now, inclusive of the occasional wide intervals between the revolving outer circles, and inclusive of the spaces between the various pods in any one of those circles, the entire area at this juncture, embraced by the whole multitude, must have contained at least two or three square miles.", "자, 빙빙 도는 바깥쪽 원들 사이의 이따금씩의 넓은 간격과, 그 원들 가운데 어느 하나에 속한 여러 고래 떼들 사이의 공간까지 모두 합하면, 이 시점에 그 모든 무리가 끌어안은 전체 넓이는 적어도 이삼 제곱마일은 되었을 것이다."], ["At any rate—though indeed such a test at such a time might be deceptive—spoutings might be discovered from our low boat that seemed playing up almost from the rim of the horizon.", "어쨌든, 비록 그런 때에 그런 식의 가늠은 사람을 속일 수도 있겠지만, 우리의 낮은 보트에서 보아도 거의 수평선 가장자리에서부터 솟구쳐 노니는 듯한 물줄기들이 발견되었다."], ["I mention this circumstance, because, as if the cows and calves had been purposely locked up in this innermost fold; and as if the wide extent of the herd had hitherto prevented them from learning the precise cause of its stopping; or, possibly, being so young, unsophisticated, and every way innocent and inexperienced; however it may have been, these smaller whales—now and then visiting our becalmed boat from the margin of the lake—evinced a wondrous fearlessness and confidence, or else a still becharmed panic which it was impossible not to marvel at.", "내가 이런 사정을 언급하는 까닭은, 마치 암소들과 새끼들이 일부러 이 가장 깊숙한 우리 안에 갇혀 있기라도 한 듯, 또 마치 떼의 넓은 펼쳐짐이 지금껏 그것들로 하여금 무리가 멈춘 정확한 까닭을 알아채지 못하게 막아 온 듯, 혹은 어쩌면 너무 어리고, 세상 물정 모르고, 모든 면에서 순진하고 경험 없는 탓이었을지도 모르나, 그것이 어찌 되었든, 이 작은 고래들이, 이따금 호수 가장자리에서 바람 멎어 멈춰 선 우리 보트로 찾아와, 놀라우리만치 두려움 없는 신뢰를, 아니면 도무지 경탄하지 않을 수 없는, 여전히 마법에 걸린 듯한 공황을 드러냈기 때문이다."], ["Like household dogs they came snuffling round us, right up to our gunwales, and touching them; till it almost seemed that some spell had suddenly domesticated them.", "그것들은 집에서 기르는 개처럼 우리 주위를 킁킁대며 우리 뱃전까지 바싹 다가와 거기에 몸을 비벼 댔으니, 마치 무슨 주문이 별안간 그것들을 길들이기라도 한 것만 같았다."], ["Queequeg patted their foreheads; Starbuck scratched their backs with his lance; but fearful of the consequences, for the time refrained from darting it.", "퀴케그는 그것들의 이마를 토닥였고, 스타벅은 창으로 그것들의 등을 긁어 주었으나, 뒷일이 두려워 그때만큼은 창을 찔러 넣기를 삼갔다."]], [["But far beneath this wondrous world upon the surface, another and still stranger world met our eyes as we gazed over the side.", "그러나 수면 위의 이 경이로운 세계 저 아래 깊은 곳에서, 우리가 뱃전 너머로 응시하자 또 하나의, 한층 더 기이한 세계가 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐졌다."], ["For, suspended in those watery vaults, floated the forms of the nursing mothers of the whales, and those that by their enormous girth seemed shortly to become mothers.", "그 물의 둥근 천장 속에 매달린 채, 새끼에게 젖을 먹이는 어미 고래들과, 그 어마어마한 둘레로 보아 머지않아 어미가 될 듯한 고래들의 형상이 떠 있었던 것이다."], ["The lake, as I have hinted, was to a considerable depth exceedingly transparent; and as human infants while suckling will calmly and fixedly gaze away from the breast, as if leading two different lives at the time; and while yet drawing mortal nourishment, be still spiritually feasting upon some unearthly reminiscence;—even so did the young of these whales seem looking up towards us, but not at us, as if we were but a bit of Gulfweed in their new-born sight.", "앞서 넌지시 일렀듯이 이 호수는 상당한 깊이까지 더없이 투명했다. 그리고 사람의 갓난아이가 젖을 빨면서도 마치 그 순간 두 개의 다른 삶을 사는 듯, 가슴에서 시선을 돌려 차분하고 골똘하게 먼 곳을 바라보며, 여전히 인간의 양분을 빨아들이면서도 영혼만은 어떤 이 세상 아닌 추억을 누리고 있는 것처럼, 바로 그처럼 이 고래의 새끼들도 우리를 올려다보는 듯했으나, 우리를 보는 것은 아니었으니, 마치 우리가 그들의 갓 태어난 시야 속에서는 한낱 모자반 한 조각에 지나지 않는 것 같았다."], ["Floating on their sides, the mothers also seemed quietly eyeing us.", "어미들 역시 옆으로 누워 떠 있으면서 우리를 조용히 바라보는 듯했다."], ["One of these little infants, that from certain queer tokens seemed hardly a day old, might have measured some fourteen feet in length, and some six feet in girth.", "이 작은 새끼들 가운데 하나는 어떤 기묘한 징표로 보아 태어난 지 하루도 채 안 된 듯했는데, 길이가 약 14피트(약 4.3미터)에 둘레가 약 6피트(약 1.8미터)쯤 되었을 것이다."], ["He was a little frisky; though as yet his body seemed scarce yet recovered from that irksome position it had so lately occupied in the maternal reticule; where, tail to head, and all ready for the final spring, the unborn whale lies bent like a Tartar’s bow.", "그놈은 조금 까불댔으나, 그 몸뚱이는 아직 얼마 전까지 머물던, 그 거북한 자세에서 채 회복되지 못한 듯 보였다. 어미의 그물주머니 속에서, 꼬리는 머리 쪽으로 향한 채 마지막 도약을 위한 만반의 채비를 갖추고, 태어나지 않은 고래는 타타르인의 활처럼 구부러져 누워 있는 것이다."], ["The delicate side-fins, and the palms of his flukes, still freshly retained the plaited crumpled appearance of a baby’s ears newly arrived from foreign parts.", "그 섬세한 옆지느러미와 꼬리지느러미의 손바닥은, 멀리 타지에서 갓 도착한 아기의 귀처럼 주름지고 쪼글쪼글한 모습을 여전히 생생히 간직하고 있었다."]], [["“Line!", "\"밧줄!"], ["line!”", "밧줄!\""], ["cried Queequeg, looking over the gunwale; “him fast!", "퀴케그가 뱃전 너머를 내려다보며 외쳤다. \"이놈 묶였다!"], ["him fast!—Who line him!", "이놈 묶였어! 누가 이놈 밧줄 걸었나!"], ["Who struck?—Two whale; one big, one little!”", "누가 작살 박았어? 고래 두 마리, 하나 크고 하나 작아!\""]], [["“What ails ye, man?”", "\"이봐, 자네 무슨 일인가?\""], ["cried Starbuck.", "스타벅이 외쳤다."]], [["“Look-e here,”", "\"여기 좀 보쇼.\""], ["said Queequeg, pointing down.", "퀴케그가 아래를 가리키며 말했다."]], [["As when the stricken whale, that from the tub has reeled out hundreds of fathoms of rope; as, after deep sounding, he floats up again, and shows the slackened curling line buoyantly rising and spiralling towards the air; so now, Starbuck saw long coils of the umbilical cord of Madame Leviathan, by which the young cub seemed still tethered to its dam.", "작살 맞은 고래가 통에서 수백 길의 밧줄을 풀어내 휘청거리며 끌고 나가다가, 깊이 잠수한 뒤 다시 떠올라, 느슨하게 말린 밧줄이 둥실 떠올라 공중으로 나선을 그리며 솟아오르는 것을 보여 주듯이, 바로 지금 스타벅은 리바이어던 부인의 탯줄이 길게 사리를 틀고 있는 것을 보았으니, 그것으로 어린 새끼가 아직 제 어미에게 매여 있는 듯했다."], ["Not seldom in the rapid vicissitudes of the chase, this natural line, with the maternal end loose, becomes entangled with the hempen one, so that the cub is thereby trapped.", "추격의 빠른 변고 속에서, 어미 쪽 끝이 풀린 이 자연의 밧줄이 삼으로 꼰 밧줄과 뒤엉켜, 그로 인해 새끼가 옭매이는 일도 드물지 않다."], ["Some of the subtlest secrets of the seas seemed divulged to us in this enchanted pond.", "바다의 가장 미묘한 비밀 가운데 몇 가지가 이 마법에 걸린 연못 속에서 우리에게 드러나는 듯했다."], ["We saw young Leviathan amours in the deep.*", "우리는 깊은 바닷속에서 어린 리바이어던의 사랑을 보았다."]], [["*The sperm whale, as with all other species of the Leviathan, but unlike most other fish, breeds indifferently at all seasons; after a gestation which may probably be set down at nine months, producing but one at a time; though in some few known instances giving birth to an Esau and Jacob:—a contingency provided for in suckling by two teats, curiously situated, one on each side of the anus; but the breasts themselves extend upwards from that.", "향유고래는 다른 모든 리바이어던 종(種)이 그러하듯, 그러나 대부분의 다른 물고기와 달리, 계절을 가리지 않고 번식한다. 아마도 아홉 달쯤으로 잡을 수 있는 임신 기간을 거친 뒤, 한 번에 한 마리만 낳는데, 몇몇 알려진 드문 경우에는 에서와 야곱 같은 쌍둥이를 낳기도 한다."], ["When by chance these precious parts in a nursing whale are cut by the hunter’s lance, the mother’s pouring milk and blood rivallingly discolour the sea for rods.", "이런 경우에 대비하여 젖을 먹이는 데에는 두 개의 젖꼭지가 마련되어 있으니, 기묘하게도 항문 양쪽에 하나씩 자리잡고 있다. 그러나 젖통 자체는 거기서 위쪽으로 뻗어 있다."], ["The milk is very sweet and rich; it has been tasted by man; it might do well with strawberries.", "어쩌다 젖 먹이는 고래의 이 귀한 부위가 사냥꾼의 창에 베이면, 어미가 쏟아 내는 젖과 피가 서로 앞다투어 몇 로드에 걸쳐 바다를 물들인다. 그 젖은 매우 달고 진하여, 사람이 맛본 적도 있는데, 딸기와 곁들이면 잘 어울릴 법하다."], ["When overflowing with mutual esteem, the whales salute more hominum.", "고래들은 서로에 대한 존경이 흘러넘칠 때, 인간이 하는 방식대로 사랑을 나눈다."]], [["And thus, though surrounded by circle upon circle of consternations and affrights, did these inscrutable creatures at the centre freely and fearlessly indulge in all peaceful concernments; yea, serenely revelled in dalliance and delight.", "이렇듯 경악과 공포의 원이 겹겹이 둘러싸고 있었음에도, 한가운데에 있는 이 헤아릴 수 없는 생물들은 거리낌 없이 두려움 없이 온갖 평화로운 일에 빠져들었으니, 그렇다, 더없이 평온하게 사랑의 희롱과 환희를 즐겼다."], ["But even so, amid the tornadoed Atlantic of my being, do I myself still for ever centrally disport in mute calm; and while ponderous planets of unwaning woe revolve round me, deep down and deep inland there I still bathe me in eternal mildness of joy.", "그러나 바로 그처럼, 내 존재의 회오리치는 대서양 한가운데서도, 나 자신은 여전히 영원토록 그 중심에서 말 없는 고요 속에 노닐고 있으니, 줄어들 줄 모르는 비통의 묵직한 행성들이 내 주위를 돌고 있는 동안에도, 저 깊은 곳, 저 깊은 내륙에서 나는 여전히 영원한 기쁨의 부드러움 속에 몸을 담그고 있다."]], [["Meanwhile, as we thus lay entranced, the occasional sudden frantic spectacles in the distance evinced the activity of the other boats, still engaged in drugging the whales on the frontier of the host; or possibly carrying on the war within the first circle, where abundance of room and some convenient retreats were afforded them.", "그러는 사이, 우리가 이렇게 황홀경에 잠겨 있는 동안, 멀리서 이따금 별안간 벌어지는 미친 듯한 광경은, 아직도 무리의 변경에서 고래들에게 드러그를 박고 있거나, 혹은 어쩌면 공간이 넉넉하고 편리한 피난처도 몇 군데 마련된 첫 번째 원 안에서 싸움을 벌이고 있을 다른 보트들의 활동을 드러냈다."], ["But the sight of the enraged drugged whales now and then blindly darting to and fro across the circles, was nothing to what at last met our eyes.", "그러나 드러그가 박혀 격분한 고래들이 이따금 눈먼 채 원들을 가로질러 이리저리 날뛰는 광경도, 마침내 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐진 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다."], ["It is sometimes the custom when fast to a whale more than commonly powerful and alert, to seek to hamstring him, as it were, by sundering or maiming his gigantic tail-tendon.", "여느 때보다 유난히 힘세고 민첩한 고래에 작살이 박혔을 때, 말하자면 그 거대한 꼬리 힘줄을 끊거나 상하게 하여 놈의 다리오금을 잘라 버리듯 하는 것이 더러 관례다."], ["It is done by darting a short-handled cutting-spade, to which is attached a rope for hauling it back again.", "이는 짧은 자루가 달린 절단용 삽을 던져서 하는데, 그 삽에는 다시 끌어당기기 위한 밧줄이 매여 있다."], ["A whale wounded (as we afterwards learned) in this part, but not effectually, as it seemed, had broken away from the boat, carrying along with him half of the harpoon line; and in the extraordinary agony of the wound, he was now dashing among the revolving circles like the lone mounted desperado Arnold, at the battle of Saratoga, carrying dismay wherever he went.", "나중에 우리가 알게 된 바로는, 이 부위에 상처를 입었으나 보아하니 결정적이지는 못했던 어느 고래가, 작살 밧줄의 절반을 끌고 보트에서 떨어져 나갔고, 상처의 엄청난 고통 속에서 이제 사라토가 전투의 외로이 말 탄 무법자 아놀드처럼 빙빙 도는 원들 사이를 휘젓고 다니며, 가는 곳마다 공포를 퍼뜨리고 있었다."]], [["But agonizing as was the wound of this whale, and an appalling spectacle enough, any way; yet the peculiar horror with which he seemed to inspire the rest of the herd, was owing to a cause which at first the intervening distance obscured from us.", "그러나 이 고래의 상처가 아무리 고통스럽고, 어느 모로 보나 충분히 소름 끼치는 광경이긴 했어도, 그놈이 나머지 무리에게 불어넣은 듯한 그 유별난 공포는, 처음엔 그 사이에 가로놓인 거리 때문에 우리에게 가려져 있던 어떤 원인에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그러나 이윽고 우리는 알아챘다."], ["But at length we perceived that by one of the unimaginable accidents of the fishery, this whale had become entangled in the harpoon-line that he towed; he had also run away with the cutting-spade in him; and while the free end of the rope attached to that weapon, had permanently caught in the coils of the harpoon-line round his tail, the cutting-spade itself had worked loose from his flesh.", "포경에서나 있을 법한 상상조차 못 할 사고 가운데 하나로, 이 고래가 자기가 끌고 가던 작살 밧줄에 얽혀 버렸던 것이다. 게다가 그놈은 몸에 절단용 삽까지 박은 채 달아나 버렸는데, 그 삽에 매인 밧줄의 자유로운 끝이 꼬리에 감긴 작살 밧줄의 사리 속에 단단히 걸린 채로, 절단용 삽 자체는 살에서 헐거워져 빠져나와 있었다."], ["So that tormented to madness, he was now churning through the water, violently flailing with his flexible tail, and tossing the keen spade about him, wounding and murdering his own comrades.", "그리하여 미칠 지경의 고통에 시달린 그놈은, 이제 물살을 휘저으며 그 유연한 꼬리를 사납게 도리깨질하여, 날카로운 삽을 제 둘레로 마구 휘둘러 대며, 제 동료들을 다치게 하고 죽이고 있었다."]], [["This terrific object seemed to recall the whole herd from their stationary fright.", "이 무시무시한 광경은 한자리에 멈춰 겁에 질려 있던 무리 전체를 일깨우는 듯했다. 먼저 우리 호수의 가장자리를 이루던 고래들이 조금씩 몰려들어, 마치 멀리서 절반쯤 잦아든 큰 물결에 떠밀리기라도 한 듯 서로를 들이받기 시작했다."], ["First, the whales forming the margin of our lake began to crowd a little, and tumble against each other, as if lifted by half spent billows from afar; then the lake itself began faintly to heave and swell; the submarine bridal-chambers and nurseries vanished; in more and more contracting orbits the whales in the more central circles began to swim in thickening clusters.", "그러더니 호수 자체가 희미하게 일렁이고 부풀어 오르기 시작했다. 물밑의 신방과 보육실은 사라졌다."], ["Yes, the long calm was departing.", "더 안쪽의 원에 있던 고래들이 점점 더 좁혀지는 궤도를 그리며 빽빽한 무리로 몰려 헤엄치기 시작했다. 그렇다, 그 긴 고요가 떠나가고 있었다."], ["A low advancing hum was soon heard; and then like to the tumultuous masses of block-ice when the great river Hudson breaks up in Spring, the entire host of whales came tumbling upon their inner centre, as if to pile themselves up in one common mountain.", "이윽고 나직이 다가오는 웅성거림이 들렸다. 그러더니 봄에 큰 강 허드슨이 풀릴 때의 소란스러운 얼음덩이 무더기처럼, 고래 무리 전체가 안쪽 중심을 향해 쏟아져 들어와, 마치 하나의 공통된 산을 이루어 쌓이려는 듯했다."], ["Instantly Starbuck and Queequeg changed places; Starbuck taking the stern.", "즉시 스타벅과 퀴케그가 자리를 바꾸었다. 스타벅이 고물을 맡았다."]], [["“Oars!", "\"노!"], ["Oars!”", "노 저어!\""], ["he intensely whispered, seizing the helm—“gripe your oars, and clutch your souls, now!", "그가 키를 움켜쥐며 격렬하게 속삭였다. \"노를 꽉 잡고, 이제 자네들 영혼을 단단히 붙들어!"], ["My God, men, stand by!", "맙소사, 다들 채비해! 그놈을 밀어내, 퀴케그, 저기 저 고래 말야!"], ["Shove him off, you Queequeg—the whale there!—prick him!—hit him!", "찔러! 쳐!"], ["Stand up—stand up, and stay so!", "일어나, 일어나서 그대로 있어! 뛰어, 다들, 저어, 저어!"], ["Spring, men—pull, men; never mind their backs—scrape them!—scrape away!”", "그놈들 등 따위 상관 말고 긁어 내! 마구 긁어 내!\""]], [["The boat was now all but jammed between two vast black bulks, leaving a narrow Dardanelles between their long lengths.", "이제 보트는 두 개의 거대한 검은 덩치 사이에 끼이다시피 했으니, 그 긴 몸뚱이들 사이로 좁다란 다르다넬스 해협만이 남아 있었다."], ["But by desperate endeavor we at last shot into a temporary opening; then giving way rapidly, and at the same time earnestly watching for another outlet.", "그러나 필사적인 안간힘으로 우리는 마침내 잠시 열린 틈으로 쏜살같이 빠져나갔다. 그러고는 재빨리 노를 저으면서, 동시에 또 다른 출구를 간절히 살폈다."], ["After many similar hair-breadth escapes, we at last swiftly glided into what had just been one of the outer circles, but now crossed by random whales, all violently making for one centre.", "이와 비슷하게 가까스로 모면한 위기를 숱하게 겪은 끝에, 우리는 마침내 방금 전까지 바깥쪽 원 가운데 하나였던 곳으로 잽싸게 미끄러져 들었는데, 이제는 닥치는 대로 날뛰는 고래들이 모조리 한 중심을 향해 사납게 몰려가며 그곳을 가로지르고 있었다."], ["This lucky salvation was cheaply purchased by the loss of Queequeg’s hat, who, while standing in the bows to prick the fugitive whales, had his hat taken clean from his head by the air-eddy made by the sudden tossing of a pair of broad flukes close by.", "이 운 좋은 구원은 퀴케그의 모자를 잃는 값싼 대가로 얻은 것이었으니, 그는 이물에 서서 달아나는 고래들을 찔러 대던 중, 바로 곁에서 한 쌍의 널따란 꼬리지느러미가 별안간 솟구치며 일으킨 소용돌이바람에 모자를 머리에서 말끔히 빼앗기고 말았다."]], [["Riotous and disordered as the universal commotion now was, it soon resolved itself into what seemed a systematic movement; for having clumped together at last in one dense body, they then renewed their onward flight with augmented fleetness.", "이제 온 천지의 동요가 아무리 어수선하고 무질서했어도, 그것은 머지않아 어떤 체계적인 움직임으로 보이는 것으로 가닥이 잡혔다. 마침내 하나의 빽빽한 덩어리로 뭉친 고래들은, 이내 더욱 빨라진 속도로 앞을 향한 도주를 재개했기 때문이다."], ["Further pursuit was useless; but the boats still lingered in their wake to pick up what drugged whales might be dropped astern, and likewise to secure one which Flask had killed and waifed.", "더 이상의 추격은 소용없었다. 그러나 보트들은 여전히 그 자취에 남아, 뒤에 처져 떨어졌을지 모를 드러그 박힌 고래들을 거두어들이고, 아울러 플라스크(Flask)가 죽여 깃대로 표시해 둔 한 마리를 확보하려 했다."], ["The waif is a pennoned pole, two or three of which are carried by every boat; and which, when additional game is at hand, are inserted upright into the floating body of a dead whale, both to mark its place on the sea, and also as token of prior possession, should the boats of any other ship draw near.", "깃대 표지(waif)란 작은 기를 단 장대로, 보트마다 두세 개씩 싣고 다닌다. 그리고 사냥감을 추가로 잡을 수 있을 때, 죽은 고래의 떠 있는 몸뚱이에 똑바로 꽂아 두어, 바다 위에서 그 자리를 표시하는 동시에, 행여 다른 배의 보트들이 다가올 경우 먼저 차지했다는 표지로 삼는 것이다."]], [["The result of this lowering was somewhat illustrative of that sagacious saying in the Fishery,—the more whales the less fish.", "이번 보트 출동의 결과는, 포경에서 전해 오는 저 슬기로운 격언, 곧 고래가 많을수록 잡히는 고래는 적다는 말을 다소나마 잘 보여 주었다."], ["Of all the drugged whales only one was captured.", "드러그가 박힌 그 모든 고래 가운데 사로잡은 것은 단 한 마리뿐이었다."], ["The rest contrived to escape for the time, but only to be taken, as will hereafter be seen, by some other craft than the Pequod.", "나머지는 그때만큼은 용케 달아났으나, 앞으로 보게 되겠지만, 결국 피쿼드호가 아닌 다른 배에 잡히고 말 운명이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 88. Schools and Schoolmasters.", "# CHAPTER 88. Schools and Schoolmasters."]], [["The previous chapter gave account of an immense body or herd of Sperm Whales, and there was also then given the probable cause inducing those vast aggregations.", "앞 장에서는 향유고래(Sperm Whale)의 거대한 무리, 곧 떼에 관해 이야기했고, 그토록 어마어마한 집결을 불러일으키는 그럴듯한 원인 또한 그때 함께 밝혀 두었다."]], [["Now, though such great bodies are at times encountered, yet, as must have been seen, even at the present day, small detached bands are occasionally observed, embracing from twenty to fifty individuals each.", "그런데 그처럼 거대한 무리가 이따금 마주쳐지기는 하나, 이미 보았다시피 오늘날에도 따로 떨어진 작은 무리가 더러 관찰되곤 하는데, 그 한 무리는 스무 마리에서 쉰 마리에 이르는 개체들을 거느린다."], ["Such bands are known as schools.", "이런 무리는 스쿨(school)이라 불린다."], ["They generally are of two sorts; those composed almost entirely of females, and those mustering none but young vigorous males, or bulls, as they are familiarly designated.", "그것들은 대체로 두 종류로 나뉜다. 거의 전부가 암컷으로 이루어진 무리와, 젊고 혈기왕성한 수컷, 곧 흔히 황소라 부르는 놈들만으로 짜인 무리다."]], [["In cavalier attendance upon the school of females, you invariably see a male of full grown magnitude, but not old; who, upon any alarm, evinces his gallantry by falling in the rear and covering the flight of his ladies.", "암컷 무리를 거느리고 기사처럼 호위하는 자리에는, 다 자란 거구이되 늙지는 않은 수컷 한 마리가 어김없이 보인다. 무슨 위험이라도 닥치면 이 녀석은 맨 뒤로 처져 자기 부인들의 도주를 엄호함으로써 그 용맹을 드러낸다."], ["In truth, this gentleman is a luxurious Ottoman, swimming about over the watery world, surroundingly accompanied by all the solaces and endearments of the harem.", "사실 이 신사는 호사스러운 오스만 군주여서, 물의 세계를 두루 헤엄쳐 다니되 하렘의 온갖 위안과 애정에 사방으로 둘러싸여 있다."], ["The contrast between this Ottoman and his concubines is striking; because, while he is always of the largest leviathanic proportions, the ladies, even at full growth, are not more than one-third of the bulk of an average-sized male.", "이 오스만 군주와 그 첩들 사이의 대비는 자못 두드러진다. 군주는 늘 가장 거대한 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 크기를 자랑하지만, 부인들은 다 자란다 한들 평균 크기의 수컷에 비하면 삼분의 일에 지나지 않기 때문이다."], ["They are comparatively delicate, indeed; I dare say, not to exceed half a dozen yards round the waist.", "사실 이들은 상대적으로 가냘파서, 감히 말하건대 허리둘레가 여섯 야드를 넘지는 않을 것이다."], ["Nevertheless, it cannot be denied, that upon the whole they are hereditarily entitled to en bon point.", "그렇지만 전체적으로 보아 그들이 통통한 몸매를 타고날 자격을 지녔다는 사실만은 부인할 수 없다."]], [["It is very curious to watch this harem and its lord in their indolent ramblings.", "이 하렘과 그 주인이 나른하게 어슬렁거리는 모습을 지켜보는 것은 무척 흥미롭다."], ["Like fashionables, they are for ever on the move in leisurely search of variety.", "멋쟁이들이 그러하듯, 그들은 여유롭게 변화를 좇아 끊임없이 옮겨 다닌다."], ["You meet them on the Line in time for the full flower of the Equatorial feeding season, having just returned, perhaps, from spending the summer in the Northern seas, and so cheating summer of all unpleasant weariness and warmth.", "그대는 적도의 먹이철이 한창 무르익을 무렵 그들을 적도선(Line) 위에서 만나게 되는데, 아마 그들은 막 북쪽 바다에서 여름을 보내고 돌아온 참이어서, 여름이 안기는 온갖 불쾌한 권태와 더위를 따돌린 셈이다."], ["By the time they have lounged up and down the promenade of the Equator awhile, they start for the Oriental waters in anticipation of the cool season there, and so evade the other excessive temperature of the year.", "적도의 산책로를 한동안 어슬렁어슬렁 오르내리고 나면, 그들은 그곳의 서늘한 계절을 미리 내다보며 동방의 바다로 떠나, 한 해의 또 다른 극단의 기온마저 피해 버린다."]], [["When serenely advancing on one of these journeys, if any strange suspicious sights are seen, my lord whale keeps a wary eye on his interesting family.", "이런 여정 가운데 하나를 평온하게 나아가다가 무언가 낯설고 수상쩍은 광경이 보이면, 우리의 군주 고래는 그 흥미로운 가족에게서 경계의 눈을 떼지 않는다."], ["Should any unwarrantably pert young Leviathan coming that way, presume to draw confidentially close to one of the ladies, with what prodigious fury the Bashaw assails him, and chases him away!", "만약 분별없이 건방진 젊은 리바이어던이 그쪽으로 다가와 감히 부인들 가운데 하나에게 은밀히 바짝 접근하기라도 하면, 이 파샤가 얼마나 어마어마한 분노로 그놈을 덮쳐 쫓아 버리는지 모른다!"], ["High times, indeed, if unprincipled young rakes like him are to be permitted to invade the sanctity of domestic bliss; though do what the Bashaw will, he cannot keep the most notorious Lothario out of his bed; for, alas!", "그놈처럼 절개 없는 젊은 난봉꾼이 가정의 행복이라는 신성한 영역을 침범하도록 허락된다면, 그야말로 큰일이 아니겠는가. 그러나 파샤가 무슨 짓을 하든, 가장 악명 높은 호색한을 제 잠자리에서 몰아낼 수는 없으니, 아아!"], ["all fish bed in common.", "모든 물고기는 한데 어울려 자기 때문이다."], ["As ashore, the ladies often cause the most terrible duels among their rival admirers; just so with the whales, who sometimes come to deadly battle, and all for love.", "뭍에서 부인들이 종종 연적들 사이에 더없이 끔찍한 결투를 일으키듯, 고래들 또한 마찬가지여서, 더러는 죽음을 건 싸움에 이르는데 그 모든 것이 사랑 때문이다."], ["They fence with their long lower jaws, sometimes locking them together, and so striving for the supremacy like elks that warringly interweave their antlers.", "그들은 길쭉한 아래턱으로 칼싸움을 벌이고, 때로는 그것을 서로 맞물어 가며, 뿔을 맞대고 얽혀 다투는 큰사슴처럼 우위를 차지하려 안간힘을 쓴다."], ["Not a few are captured having the deep scars of these encounters,—furrowed heads, broken teeth, scolloped fins; and in some instances, wrenched and dislocated mouths.", "이런 충돌의 깊은 흉터를 지닌 채 붙잡히는 놈도 적지 않으니 — 골이 파인 머리, 부러진 이빨, 가장자리가 너덜너덜한 지느러미, 그리고 더러는 비틀려 빠진 입을 지니고 있다."]], [["But supposing the invader of domestic bliss to betake himself away at the first rush of the harem’s lord, then is it very diverting to watch that lord.", "그러나 가정의 행복을 침범하려던 자가 하렘 군주의 첫 돌격에 줄행랑을 친다고 가정해 보면, 그때 그 군주를 지켜보는 것이 자못 재미있다."], ["Gently he insinuates his vast bulk among them again and revels there awhile, still in tantalizing vicinity to young Lothario, like pious Solomon devoutly worshipping among his thousand concubines.", "그는 다시금 그 거대한 몸뚱이를 부인들 사이로 슬며시 들이밀고 한동안 그 속에서 즐기는데, 여전히 젊은 호색한이 애를 태우며 가까이 머무를 만한 거리에 있으니, 마치 경건한 솔로몬이 천 명의 첩들 사이에서 독실하게 예배를 드리는 듯하다."], ["Granting other whales to be in sight, the fishermen will seldom give chase to one of these Grand Turks; for these Grand Turks are too lavish of their strength, and hence their unctuousness is small.", "시야에 다른 고래들이 있다면, 어부들은 좀처럼 이런 대(大) 술탄들을 뒤쫓지 않는다. 이 대 술탄들은 제 힘을 너무 헤프게 써 버려, 그만큼 기름기가 적기 때문이다."], ["As for the sons and the daughters they beget, why, those sons and daughters must take care of themselves; at least, with only the maternal help.", "그놈들이 낳은 아들딸로 말하자면, 글쎄, 그 아들딸들은 제 앞가림을 스스로 해야 한다. 적어도 어미의 도움만 받으면서 말이다."], ["For like certain other omnivorous roving lovers that might be named, my Lord Whale has no taste for the nursery, however much for the bower; and so, being a great traveller, he leaves his anonymous babies all over the world; every baby an exotic.", "굳이 이름을 댈 수도 있는 다른 잡식성의 떠돌이 정부(情夫)들이 그러하듯, 우리의 군주 고래는 규방(閨房)에는 한껏 취미가 있을지언정 육아실에는 도무지 관심이 없으니, 위대한 여행자인 그는 이름 모를 제 새끼들을 온 세상에 흩뿌려 놓는 셈이고, 그래서 새끼마다 이국의 산물이 된다."], ["In good time, nevertheless, as the ardour of youth declines; as years and dumps increase; as reflection lends her solemn pauses; in short, as a general lassitude overtakes the sated Turk; then a love of ease and virtue supplants the love for maidens; our Ottoman enters upon the impotent, repentant, admonitory stage of life, forswears, disbands the harem, and grown to an exemplary, sulky old soul, goes about all alone among the meridians and parallels saying his prayers, and warning each young Leviathan from his amorous errors.", "그렇지만 때가 무르익어 젊음의 열정이 시들고, 세월과 우울이 쌓이며, 사색이 그 엄숙한 멈춤을 안기고, 한마디로 물릴 대로 물린 이 술탄에게 전반적인 권태가 찾아들면, 그때는 안락과 덕에 대한 사랑이 처녀들에 대한 사랑을 밀어내고, 우리의 오스만 군주는 무력하고 뉘우치며 훈계나 늘어놓는 인생의 단계로 접어들어, 하렘을 버리고 해산시키며, 본받을 만하되 시무룩한 늙은 영혼이 되어, 자오선과 위선 사이를 홀로 떠돌면서 기도를 올리고, 젊은 리바이어던마다 그 색욕의 과오를 경계하라 타이르며 다니는 것이다."]], [["Now, as the harem of whales is called by the fishermen a school, so is the lord and master of that school technically known as the schoolmaster.", "자, 고래들의 하렘이 어부들 사이에서 스쿨이라 불리듯, 그 스쿨의 주인이자 우두머리는 학술적으로 교장(schoolmaster)이라 일컬어진다."], ["It is therefore not in strict character, however admirably satirical, that after going to school himself, he should then go abroad inculcating not what he learned there, but the folly of it.", "그러니 그가 제 스스로 학교에 다닌 뒤에, 거기서 배운 것이 아니라 도리어 그 어리석음을 밖으로 나가 가르쳐 댄다는 것은, 감탄스러우리만치 풍자적이긴 해도 그 명칭에 꼭 들어맞지는 않는다."], ["His title, schoolmaster, would very naturally seem derived from the name bestowed upon the harem itself, but some have surmised that the man who first thus entitled this sort of Ottoman whale, must have read the memoirs of Vidocq, and informed himself what sort of a country-schoolmaster that famous Frenchman was in his younger days, and what was the nature of those occult lessons he inculcated into some of his pupils.", "교장이라는 그 칭호는 하렘 자체에 붙여진 이름에서 자연스레 비롯된 듯이 보이지만, 더러는 이런 오스만 고래에게 처음 그런 칭호를 붙인 자가 비도크(Vidocq)의 회고록을 읽고서, 그 유명한 프랑스인이 젊은 시절 어떤 시골 교장이었는지, 또 그가 일부 제자들에게 은밀히 가르쳤다는 그 수상한 교습이 어떤 성질의 것이었는지 알게 된 것이 틀림없다고 짐작하기도 한다."]], [["The same secludedness and isolation to which the schoolmaster whale betakes himself in his advancing years, is true of all aged Sperm Whales.", "교장 고래가 나이 들어 가면서 빠져드는 바로 그 은둔과 고립은, 늙은 향유고래라면 누구에게나 해당하는 진실이다."], ["Almost universally, a lone whale—as a solitary Leviathan is called—proves an ancient one.", "거의 예외 없이, 외로운 고래 — 홀로 떠도는 리바이어던을 그렇게 부른다 — 는 늙은 놈으로 드러난다."], ["Like venerable moss-bearded Daniel Boone, he will have no one near him but Nature herself; and her he takes to wife in the wilderness of waters, and the best of wives she is, though she keeps so many moody secrets.", "이끼 낀 잿빛 수염을 기른 점잖은 대니얼 분처럼, 그는 자연 그 자체 말고는 제 곁에 아무도 두지 않으려 하니, 물의 황야에서 그 자연을 아내로 맞아들인다. 그토록 변덕스러운 비밀을 많이 품고 있긴 해도, 자연이야말로 더없이 좋은 아내인 것이다."]], [["The schools composing none but young and vigorous males, previously mentioned, offer a strong contrast to the harem schools.", "앞서 말한바 젊고 혈기왕성한 수컷들만으로 짜인 무리는 하렘 무리와 뚜렷한 대조를 이룬다."], ["For while those female whales are characteristically timid, the young males, or forty-barrel-bulls, as they call them, are by far the most pugnacious of all Leviathans, and proverbially the most dangerous to encounter; excepting those wondrous grey-headed, grizzled whales, sometimes met, and these will fight you like grim fiends exasperated by a penal gout.", "저 암컷 고래들이 특징적으로 겁이 많은 반면, 젊은 수컷들, 곧 그들이 마흔 배럴짜리 황소라 부르는 놈들은 모든 리바이어던 가운데 단연 가장 호전적이고, 마주치기에 가장 위험하다고 정평이 나 있기 때문이다. 다만 이따금 마주치는 그 경이로운 잿빛 머리에 희끗희끗한 늙은 고래들만은 예외인데, 이놈들은 형벌 같은 통풍에 시달려 약이 오른 사나운 악귀들처럼 그대에게 덤벼들 것이다."]], [["The Forty-barrel-bull schools are larger than the harem schools.", "마흔 배럴짜리 황소의 무리는 하렘 무리보다 규모가 더 크다."], ["Like a mob of young collegians, they are full of fight, fun, and wickedness, tumbling round the world at such a reckless, rollicking rate, that no prudent underwriter would insure them any more than he would a riotous lad at Yale or Harvard.", "젊은 대학생 패거리처럼, 그들은 싸움과 장난과 짓궂음으로 가득 차서, 어찌나 무모하고 떠들썩한 기세로 온 세상을 뒹굴며 돌아다니는지, 분별 있는 보험업자라면 예일이나 하버드의 난봉꾼 녀석을 인수하지 않으려는 것과 마찬가지로 그놈들의 보험을 들어 주려 하지 않을 것이다."], ["They soon relinquish this turbulence though, and when about three-fourths grown, break up, and separately go about in quest of settlements, that is, harems.", "그렇지만 그들은 이내 이런 난폭함을 내려놓고, 거의 사분의 삼쯤 자라면 무리를 흩어, 저마다 정착지를, 다시 말해 하렘을 찾아 떠난다."]], [["Another point of difference between the male and female schools is still more characteristic of the sexes.", "수컷 무리와 암컷 무리 사이의 또 다른 차이점은 양성(兩性)의 성질을 한층 더 잘 드러낸다."], ["Say you strike a Forty-barrel-bull—poor devil! all his comrades quit him.", "그대가 마흔 배럴짜리 황소 한 마리를 작살로 찔렀다고 치자 — 가엾은 녀석!"], ["But strike a member of the harem school, and her companions swim around her with every token of concern, sometimes lingering so near her and so long, as themselves to fall a prey.", "그 동무들은 모두 그를 버리고 가 버린다. 그러나 하렘 무리의 일원을 찔러 보라, 그 동료들은 온갖 걱정의 기색을 띠고 그녀 주위를 헤엄쳐 돌며, 때로는 너무 가까이, 너무 오래도록 머뭇거리다가 저들 자신마저 먹잇감으로 떨어지고 만다."]], [["# CHAPTER 89. Fast-Fish and Loose-Fish.", "# CHAPTER 89. Fast-Fish and Loose-Fish."]], [["The allusion to the waif and waif-poles in the last chapter but one, necessitates some account of the laws and regulations of the whale fishery, of which the waif may be deemed the grand symbol and badge.", "바로 앞 장에서 부표(浮標)와 부표 장대를 언급한 일은, 고래잡이의 법과 규정에 관해 어느 정도 설명할 것을 요구하는데, 그 부표야말로 이러한 법규의 위대한 상징이요 표지로 여겨질 만하기 때문이다."]], [["It frequently happens that when several ships are cruising in company, a whale may be struck by one vessel, then escape, and be finally killed and captured by another vessel; and herein are indirectly comprised many minor contingencies, all partaking of this one grand feature.", "여러 척의 배가 무리를 지어 항해하다 보면, 한 배가 작살을 박은 고래가 달아났다가 결국 다른 배에 죽임을 당해 잡히는 일이 흔히 일어난다. 그리고 여기에는 이 하나의 큰 특징을 공유하는 자잘한 우발적 상황들이 여럿 간접적으로 포함되어 있다."], ["For example,—after a weary and perilous chase and capture of a whale, the body may get loose from the ship by reason of a violent storm; and drifting far away to leeward, be retaken by a second whaler, who, in a calm, snugly tows it alongside, without risk of life or line.", "예컨대, 지치고 위태로운 추격 끝에 고래 한 마리를 잡았으나 사나운 폭풍 탓에 그 몸뚱이가 배에서 풀려나 멀리 바람 아래쪽으로 떠내려갔다가, 두 번째 포경선이 그것을 다시 손에 넣어, 잔잔한 바다에서 목숨도 밧줄도 걸지 않고 느긋하게 뱃전으로 끌고 오는 경우가 있다."], ["Thus the most vexatious and violent disputes would often arise between the fishermen, were there not some written or unwritten, universal, undisputed law applicable to all cases.", "이리하여 모든 경우에 적용되는, 성문이든 불문이든 보편적이고 누구도 이의를 달 수 없는 어떤 법이 없다면, 어부들 사이에서는 가장 성가시고 격렬한 분쟁이 종종 일어나고 말 것이다."]], [["Perhaps the only formal whaling code authorized by legislative enactment, was that of Holland.", "입법 제정에 의해 공인된 유일한 정식 포경 법전은 아마도 네덜란드의 것뿐이었으리라."], ["It was decreed by the States-General in A.D. 1695.", "그것은 서기 1695년에 네덜란드 연방의회에서 공포되었다."], ["But though no other nation has ever had any written whaling law, yet the American fishermen have been their own legislators and lawyers in this matter.", "그러나 다른 어떤 나라도 성문화된 포경법을 가진 적이 없었음에도, 미국 어부들은 이 문제에 있어 스스로 입법자가 되고 법률가가 되어 왔다."], ["They have provided a system which for terse comprehensiveness surpasses Justinian’s Pandects and the By-laws of the Chinese Society for the Suppression of Meddling with other People’s Business.", "그들은 간결한 포괄성에 있어 유스티니아누스의 학설휘찬(學說彙纂)과 남의 일에 참견하는 짓을 금하기 위한 중국인 협회의 내규(內規)마저 능가하는 체계를 마련해 놓았다."], ["Yes; these laws might be engraven on a Queen Anne’s farthing, or the barb of a harpoon, and worn round the neck, so small are they.", "그렇다, 이 법들은 어찌나 작은지 앤 여왕 시절의 파딩 동전이나 작살의 미늘에 새겨 목에 걸고 다닐 수도 있을 정도다."]], [["I.", "하나."], ["A Fast-Fish belongs to the party fast to it.", "잡힌 고래(Fast-Fish)는 그것을 붙잡고 있는 측에 속한다."]], [["II.", "둘."], ["A Loose-Fish is fair game for anybody who can soonest catch it.", "놓친 고래(Loose-Fish)는 누구든 먼저 잡는 자의 정당한 사냥감이다."]], [["But what plays the mischief with this masterly code is the admirable brevity of it, which necessitates a vast volume of commentaries to expound it.", "그러나 이 빼어난 법전에 말썽을 일으키는 것은 다름 아닌 그 감탄스러운 간결함이니, 그것을 풀어 설명하자면 방대한 분량의 주해(註解)가 필요해지는 까닭이다."]], [["First: What is a Fast-Fish?", "첫째. 잡힌 고래란 무엇인가?"], ["Alive or dead a fish is technically fast, when it is connected with an occupied ship or boat, by any medium at all controllable by the occupant or occupants,—a mast, an oar, a nine-inch cable, a telegraph wire, or a strand of cobweb, it is all the same.", "살아 있든 죽어 있든, 고래는 그것을 점유한 자나 자들이 통제할 수 있는 어떤 매개물—돛대든, 노든, 9인치 굵기의 밧줄이든, 전신줄이든, 거미줄 한 가닥이든 모두 마찬가지다—을 통해 점유 중인 배나 보트와 이어져 있을 때, 전문적인 의미에서 '잡힌' 것이다."], ["Likewise a fish is technically fast when it bears a waif, or any other recognised symbol of possession; so long as the party waifing it plainly evince their ability at any time to take it alongside, as well as their intention so to do.", "마찬가지로 고래가 부표나 그 밖에 공인된 소유의 표지를 달고 있을 때에도 전문적으로 '잡힌' 것이 되는데, 다만 그것에 부표를 매단 측이 언제든 그것을 뱃전으로 끌고 올 수 있는 능력과 그렇게 하려는 의도를 분명히 드러내는 한에서다."]], [["These are scientific commentaries; but the commentaries of the whalemen themselves sometimes consist in hard words and harder knocks—the Coke-upon-Littleton of the fist.", "이것들은 학술적인 주해다. 그러나 고래잡이들 자신의 주해는 때로 거친 말과 더 거친 주먹질로 이루어지니—주먹으로 펴 보이는 코크의 리틀턴 주석서인 셈이다."], ["True, among the more upright and honorable whalemen allowances are always made for peculiar cases, where it would be an outrageous moral injustice for one party to claim possession of a whale previously chased or killed by another party.", "사실 더 강직하고 명예로운 고래잡이들 사이에서는, 한 측이 다른 측에 의해 앞서 쫓기거나 죽임을 당한 고래의 소유권을 주장하는 것이 터무니없는 도덕적 불의가 될 만한 특수한 경우에 대해 늘 참작이 이루어진다."], ["But others are by no means so scrupulous.", "그러나 그만큼 양심적이지 않은 자들도 결코 적지 않다."]], [["Some fifty years ago there was a curious case of whale-trover litigated in England, wherein the plaintiffs set forth that after a hard chase of a whale in the Northern seas; and when indeed they (the plaintiffs) had succeeded in harpooning the fish; they were at last, through peril of their lives, obliged to forsake not only their lines, but their boat itself.", "오십 년쯤 전 영국에서 고래 횡령에 관한 진기한 소송 한 건이 다투어졌는데, 거기서 원고들은 다음과 같이 진술했다. 즉 북쪽 바다에서 고래 한 마리를 호되게 추격한 끝에, 실제로 그들(원고들)이 그 고래에게 작살을 박는 데 성공하였으나, 마침내 목숨이 위태로워져 밧줄뿐 아니라 보트마저 버릴 수밖에 없었다는 것이다."], ["Ultimately the defendants (the crew of another ship) came up with the whale, struck, killed, seized, and finally appropriated it before the very eyes of the plaintiffs.", "그 후 피고들(다른 배의 선원들)이 그 고래를 따라잡아 작살을 박고 죽이고 붙잡아, 마침내 원고들의 바로 눈앞에서 그것을 제 것으로 차지해 버렸다."], ["And when those defendants were remonstrated with, their captain snapped his fingers in the plaintiffs’ teeth, and assured them that by way of doxology to the deed he had done, he would now retain their line, harpoons, and boat, which had remained attached to the whale at the time of the seizure.", "그리고 그 피고들이 항의를 받자, 그들의 선장은 원고들의 면전에서 손가락을 튕기며, 자기가 저지른 행위에 대한 송영(頌詠)으로서 이제 붙잡힐 당시 고래에 매달려 있던 그들의 밧줄과 작살과 보트마저 그대로 차지하겠노라고 큰소리쳤다."], ["Wherefore the plaintiffs now sued for the recovery of the value of their whale, line, harpoons, and boat.", "그리하여 원고들은 이제 자기네 고래와 밧줄과 작살과 보트의 값을 되찾고자 소송을 제기한 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Erskine was counsel for the defendants; Lord Ellenborough was the judge.", "어스킨 씨가 피고들의 변호인이었고, 엘런버러 경이 재판관이었다."], ["In the course of the defence, the witty Erskine went on to illustrate his position, by alluding to a recent crim. con.", "변론을 펴는 과정에서 재치 넘치는 어스킨은 최근의 한 간통(姦通) 소송을 언급하며 자기 주장을 예증해 나갔다."], ["case, wherein a gentleman, after in vain trying to bridle his wife’s viciousness, had at last abandoned her upon the seas of life; but in the course of years, repenting of that step, he instituted an action to recover possession of her.", "그 사건에서는 한 신사가 아내의 못된 성정을 다잡으려 헛되이 애쓰다가 결국 인생의 바다 위에 그녀를 버렸는데, 세월이 흐르면서 그 일을 뉘우치고는 그녀에 대한 소유권을 되찾으려 소송을 걸었다는 것이다. 어스킨은 그 사건에서 반대편에 서 있었다."], ["Erskine was on the other side; and he then supported it by saying, that though the gentleman had originally harpooned the lady, and had once had her fast, and only by reason of the great stress of her plunging viciousness, had at last abandoned her; yet abandon her he did, so that she became a loose-fish; and therefore when a subsequent gentleman re-harpooned her, the lady then became that subsequent gentleman’s property, along with whatever harpoon might have been found sticking in her.", "그러면서 그는 이렇게 말하여 자기 입장을 뒷받침했다. 즉 그 신사가 애초에 그 부인에게 작살을 박아 한때 그녀를 붙잡고 있었으나, 그녀가 격렬하게 날뛰는 못된 성정의 엄청난 압박 때문에 결국 그녀를 버렸다는 것, 그러나 어쨌든 버린 것은 버린 것이므로 그녀는 놓친 고래가 되었고, 따라서 뒤이은 또 다른 신사가 그녀에게 다시 작살을 박았을 때, 그 부인은 그녀의 몸에 박힌 채 발견된 작살이 무엇이든 그것과 함께 뒤이은 그 신사의 소유물이 되었다는 것이다."]], [["Now in the present case Erskine contended that the examples of the whale and the lady were reciprocally illustrative of each other.", "그리하여 어스킨은 지금의 이 사건에서 고래의 사례와 부인의 사례가 서로를 예증해 준다고 주장했다."]], [["These pleadings, and the counter pleadings, being duly heard, the very learned judge in set terms decided, to wit,—That as for the boat, he awarded it to the plaintiffs, because they had merely abandoned it to save their lives; but that with regard to the controverted whale, harpoons, and line, they belonged to the defendants; the whale, because it was a Loose-Fish at the time of the final capture; and the harpoons and line because when the fish made off with them, it (the fish) acquired a property in those articles; and hence anybody who afterwards took the fish had a right to them. Now the defendants afterwards took the fish; ergo, the aforesaid articles were theirs.", "이러한 변론과 그에 대한 반대 변론을 정식으로 청취한 뒤, 그 매우 학식 깊은 재판관은 다음과 같이 명확한 표현으로 판결을 내렸다. 즉—보트에 관해서는, 원고들이 단지 목숨을 구하고자 그것을 버렸을 뿐이므로 그것을 원고들에게 인도한다. 그러나 다툼의 대상인 고래와 작살과 밧줄에 관해서는, 그것들이 피고들에게 속한다. 고래는 최종적으로 붙잡힐 당시 놓친 고래였기 때문이고, 작살과 밧줄은 그 고래가 그것들을 달고 달아났을 때 그것(고래)이 그 물품들에 대한 소유권을 획득하였으며, 따라서 그 후에 그 고래를 잡은 자라면 누구든 그것들에 대한 권리를 갖기 때문이다. 그런데 피고들이 그 후에 그 고래를 잡았으니, 그러므로 앞서 말한 물품들은 그들의 것이라는 것이었다."]], [["A common man looking at this decision of the very learned Judge, might possibly object to it.", "이 매우 학식 깊은 재판관의 판결을 보고 보통 사람이라면 어쩌면 이의를 제기할지도 모른다."], ["But ploughed up to the primary rock of the matter, the two great principles laid down in the twin whaling laws previously quoted, and applied and elucidated by Lord Ellenborough in the above cited case; these two laws touching Fast-Fish and Loose-Fish, I say, will, on reflection, be found the fundamentals of all human jurisprudence; for notwithstanding its complicated tracery of sculpture, the Temple of the Law, like the Temple of the Philistines, has but two props to stand on.", "그러나 이 문제의 근본 암반까지 파고들어 보면, 앞서 인용한 한 쌍의 포경법에 정해져 있고 위 사건에서 엘런버러 경이 적용하고 해명한 두 가지 위대한 원리—내가 말하건대, 잡힌 고래와 놓친 고래에 관한 이 두 법은—곰곰이 생각해 보면 모든 인간 법학의 기초임이 드러날 것이다. 왜냐하면 정교하게 조각된 복잡한 무늬에도 불구하고, 법의 신전은 블레셋 사람들의 신전과 마찬가지로 단 두 개의 기둥 위에 서 있을 뿐이기 때문이다."]], [["Is it not a saying in every one’s mouth, Possession is half of the law: that is, regardless of how the thing came into possession?", "누구나 입에 달고 사는 말이 있지 않은가, 점유는 법의 절반이라고. 다시 말해 그 물건이 어떻게 점유에 들어왔는지는 상관없다는 것이다."], ["But often possession is the whole of the law.", "그러나 점유가 종종 법의 전부일 때도 있다."], ["What are the sinews and souls of Russian serfs and Republican slaves but Fast-Fish, whereof possession is the whole of the law?", "러시아 농노와 공화국 노예들의 근육과 영혼이 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이며, 그것에 관한 한 점유가 법의 전부가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What to the rapacious landlord is the widow’s last mite but a Fast-Fish?", "탐욕스러운 지주에게 과부의 마지막 한 푼은 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What is yonder undetected villain’s marble mansion with a door-plate for a waif; what is that but a Fast-Fish?", "저기 발각되지 않은 악당의 대리석 저택이 문패를 부표 삼아 달고 있으니, 그것이 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What is the ruinous discount which Mordecai, the broker, gets from poor Woebegone, the bankrupt, on a loan to keep Woebegone’s family from starvation; what is that ruinous discount but a Fast-Fish?", "중개인 모르데카이가 가련한 파산자 우비곤의 가족을 굶주림에서 구하려는 대출에 대해 그 가련한 우비곤에게서 뜯어내는 그 파멸적인 할인이, 그 파멸적인 할인이 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What is the Archbishop of Savesoul’s income of £100,000 seized from the scant bread and cheese of hundreds of thousands of broken-backed laborers (all sure of heaven without any of Savesoul’s help) what is that globular £100,000 but a Fast-Fish?", "영혼을 구원한다는 대주교가 수십만 허리 꺾인 노동자들(모두 영혼구원 대주교의 도움 따위 없이도 천국이 확실한 이들)의 빈약한 빵과 치즈에서 빼앗아 가는 10만 파운드의 수입이, 그 둥글둥글한 10만 파운드가 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What are the Duke of Dunder’s hereditary towns and hamlets but Fast-Fish?", "던더 공작이 세습으로 물려받은 마을과 촌락들이 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What to that redoubted harpooneer, John Bull, is poor Ireland, but a Fast-Fish?", "저 가공할 작살잡이 존 불에게 가련한 아일랜드는 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What to that apostolic lancer, Brother Jonathan, is Texas but a Fast-Fish?", "저 사도적인 창잡이 브러더 조너선에게 텍사스는 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["And concerning all these, is not Possession the whole of the law?", "그리고 이 모든 것에 관하여 점유가 법의 전부가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"]], [["But if the doctrine of Fast-Fish be pretty generally applicable, the kindred doctrine of Loose-Fish is still more widely so.", "그러나 잡힌 고래의 교리가 제법 널리 적용된다면, 그와 한 핏줄인 놓친 고래의 교리는 한층 더 광범위하게 적용된다."], ["That is internationally and universally applicable.", "그것은 국제적으로 또 보편적으로 적용되는 것이다."]], [["What was America in 1492 but a Loose-Fish, in which Columbus struck the Spanish standard by way of waifing it for his royal master and mistress?", "1492년의 아메리카는 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이었으며, 콜럼버스는 자기 임금과 왕비를 위해 그것에 부표를 매다는 셈으로 거기에 스페인 깃발을 꽂은 것이 아니고 무엇이었겠는가?"], ["What was Poland to the Czar?", "차르에게 폴란드는 무엇이었는가?"], ["What Greece to the Turk?", "튀르크에게 그리스는 무엇이었는가?"], ["What India to England?", "영국에게 인도는 무엇이었는가?"], ["What at last will Mexico be to the United States?", "결국 미합중국에게 멕시코는 무엇이 될 것인가?"], ["All Loose-Fish.", "모두 놓친 고래다."]], [["What are the Rights of Man and the Liberties of the World but Loose-Fish?", "인간의 권리와 세계의 자유가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What all men’s minds and opinions but Loose-Fish?", "모든 인간의 정신과 견해가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What is the principle of religious belief in them but a Loose-Fish?", "그것들 안에 든 종교적 신앙의 원리가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What to the ostentatious smuggling verbalists are the thoughts of thinkers but Loose-Fish?", "허세 부리며 말을 밀수하는 자들에게 사상가들의 사유가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["What is the great globe itself but a Loose-Fish?", "이 거대한 지구 자체가 놓친 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"], ["And what are you, reader, but a Loose-Fish and a Fast-Fish, too?", "그리고 독자여, 그대 또한 놓친 고래이자 잡힌 고래가 아니고 무엇이겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER 90. Heads or Tails.", "# CHAPTER 90. Heads or Tails."]], [["“De balena vero sufficit, si rex habeat caput, et regina caudam.” Bracton, l. 3, c. 3.", "“De balena vero sufficit, si rex habeat caput, et regina caudam.”"]], [["Latin from the books of the Laws of England, which taken along with the context, means, that of all whales captured by anybody on the coast of that land, the King, as Honorary Grand Harpooneer, must have the head, and the Queen be respectfully presented with the tail.", "브랙턴(Bracton), 제3권 제3장. 이는 잉글랜드 법전에서 따온 라틴어로, 그 맥락과 함께 풀이하면, 그 땅의 해안에서 누군가에게 잡힌 모든 고래에 대해, 명예 대(大)작살잡이인 국왕은 그 머리를 차지해야 하고, 왕비에게는 정중히 그 꼬리를 진상해야 한다는 뜻이다."], ["A division which, in the whale, is much like halving an apple; there is no intermediate remainder.", "고래에 있어 이런 식의 분할은 사과를 반으로 쪼개는 것과 매우 흡사하다. 중간에 남는 부분이라곤 없는 것이다."], ["Now as this law, under a modified form, is to this day in force in England; and as it offers in various respects a strange anomaly touching the general law of Fast and Loose-Fish, it is here treated of in a separate chapter, on the same courteous principle that prompts the English railways to be at the expense of a separate car, specially reserved for the accommodation of royalty.", "그런데 이 법은 일부 수정된 형태로 오늘날까지도 잉글랜드에서 효력을 지니고 있다. 또한 그것은 여러 면에서 잡힌 고래와 놓친 고래(Fast and Loose-Fish)에 관한 일반 법리에 비추어 기묘한 변칙을 이루므로, 여기서는 별도의 장으로 다루기로 한다."], ["In the first place, in curious proof of the fact that the above-mentioned law is still in force, I proceed to lay before you a circumstance that happened within the last two years.", "이는 잉글랜드 철도가 왕족의 편의를 위해 특별히 비워 둔 별도 객차의 비용을 기꺼이 부담하게 만드는 것과 똑같은 정중한 원칙에 따른 것이다. 우선, 앞서 말한 법이 여전히 효력을 지니고 있다는 사실을 기묘하게 입증하기 위해, 지난 이태 사이에 일어난 한 가지 사건을 여러분 앞에 펼쳐 보이겠다."]], [["It seems that some honest mariners of Dover, or Sandwich, or some one of the Cinque Ports, had after a hard chase succeeded in killing and beaching a fine whale which they had originally descried afar off from the shore.", "도버나 샌드위치, 혹은 친크 포츠(Cinque Ports) 가운데 어느 항구의 정직한 뱃사람 몇몇이, 처음에 뭍에서 멀리 발견한 멋진 고래 한 마리를 힘겨운 추격 끝에 죽여 해변에 끌어올리는 데 성공했던 모양이다."], ["Now the Cinque Ports are partially or somehow under the jurisdiction of a sort of policeman or beadle, called a Lord Warden.", "그런데 친크 포츠는 부분적으로, 혹은 어떤 식으로든, 로드 워든(Lord Warden)이라 불리는 일종의 경관 또는 관리의 관할 아래 있다."], ["Holding the office directly from the crown, I believe, all the royal emoluments incident to the Cinque Port territories become by assignment his.", "내가 알기로 이 직책은 왕실로부터 직접 받는 것이어서, 친크 포츠 영토에 딸린 온갖 왕실 수입이 양도를 통해 그의 몫이 된다."], ["By some writers this office is called a sinecure.", "어떤 저술가들은 이 직책을 한직(閑職)이라 부른다."], ["But not so.", "그러나 그렇지 않다."], ["Because the Lord Warden is busily employed at times in fobbing his perquisites; which are his chiefly by virtue of that same fobbing of them.", "로드 워든은 때로 자신의 부수입을 슬쩍 챙기는 일에 바삐 매달려 있기 때문이며, 그 수입이 그의 것이 되는 까닭도 다름 아닌 그 슬쩍 챙기는 행위 덕분인 것이다."]], [["Now when these poor sun-burnt mariners, bare-footed, and with their trowsers rolled high up on their eely legs, had wearily hauled their fat fish high and dry, promising themselves a good £150 from the precious oil and bone; and in fantasy sipping rare tea with their wives, and good ale with their cronies, upon the strength of their respective shares; up steps a very learned and most Christian and charitable gentleman, with a copy of Blackstone under his arm; and laying it upon the whale’s head, he says—“Hands off!", "자, 햇볕에 그을린 이 가련한 뱃사람들이 맨발로, 미꾸라지처럼 미끈한 다리 위로 바지를 높이 걷어 올린 채, 그 살진 물고기를 지친 몸으로 높직이 끌어내 물기 없는 곳에 올려놓고서, 귀한 기름과 뼈로 150파운드는 너끈히 벌리라 자신하며, 각자의 몫을 밑천 삼아 아내와 더불어 진귀한 차를 홀짝이고 친구들과 좋은 맥주를 들이켜는 환상에 잠겨 있을 때였다. 그때 매우 학식 깊고 더없이 기독교적이며 자비로운 신사 하나가 블랙스톤(Blackstone) 법서 한 권을 옆구리에 끼고 성큼성큼 다가오더니, 그것을 고래 머리 위에 척 올려놓고는 이렇게 말했다."], ["this fish, my masters, is a Fast-Fish.", "\"손 떼시오! 여러분, 이 물고기는 잡힌 고기요."], ["I seize it as the Lord Warden’s.”", "나는 이것을 로드 워든의 소유로 압류하겠소.\""], ["Upon this the poor mariners in their respectful consternation—so truly English—knowing not what to say, fall to vigorously scratching their heads all round; meanwhile ruefully glancing from the whale to the stranger.", "이에 가련한 뱃사람들은 더없이 잉글랜드인다운, 공손한 경악에 사로잡혀 무어라 말해야 좋을지 몰라, 다들 머리를 벅벅 긁어 대기 시작했다. 그러는 내내 고래에서 그 낯선 사내에게로 침울한 눈길을 옮길 따름이었다."], ["But that did in nowise mend the matter, or at all soften the hard heart of the learned gentleman with the copy of Blackstone.", "그러나 그런다고 사정이 나아지거나, 블랙스톤 법서를 든 그 학식 깊은 신사의 굳은 마음이 조금이라도 누그러질 리는 없었다."], ["At length one of them, after long scratching about for his ideas, made bold to speak,", "마침내 그들 가운데 하나가 한참을 머릿속에서 생각을 더듬은 끝에 용기를 내어 입을 열었다."]], [["“Please, sir, who is the Lord Warden?”", "\"실례지만 나리, 로드 워든이란 분이 누구신지요?\""]], [["“The Duke.”", "\"공작이시오.\""]], [["“But the duke had nothing to do with taking this fish?” “It is his.”", "\"하지만 그 공작님은 이 물고기를 잡는 데 아무 관련이 없지 않습니까?\""]], [["“We have been at great trouble, and peril, and some expense, and is all that to go to the Duke’s benefit; we getting nothing at all for our pains but our blisters?”", "\"이건 그분 것이오.\" \"우리는 큰 고생과 위험을 무릅쓰고, 적잖은 비용까지 들였는데, 그게 전부 공작님 이득으로 돌아가고, 우리는 고생한 대가로 손에 잡힌 물집밖에 못 얻는단 말입니까?\""]], [["“It is his.”", "\"이건 그분 것이오.\""]], [["“Is the Duke so very poor as to be forced to this desperate mode of getting a livelihood?”", "\"공작님이 그토록 가난하셔서 이런 막다른 방식으로 생계를 꾸리셔야 한단 말입니까?\""]], [["“It is his.”", "\"이건 그분 것이오.\""]], [["“I thought to relieve my old bed-ridden mother by part of my share of this whale.”", "\"저는 이 고래에서 제 몫을 나눠 받아 자리보전한 늙은 어머니를 좀 편케 해 드리려 했습니다만.\""]], [["“It is his.”", "\"이건 그분 것이오.\""]], [["“Won’t the Duke be content with a quarter or a half?”", "\"공작님께서 사분의 일이나 절반쯤으로 만족하시진 않을까요?\""]], [["“It is his.”", "\"이건 그분 것이오.\""]], [["In a word, the whale was seized and sold, and his Grace the Duke of Wellington received the money.", "한마디로, 고래는 압류되어 팔렸고, 그 돈은 웰링턴 공작 각하의 손에 들어갔다."], ["Thinking that viewed in some particular lights, the case might by a bare possibility in some small degree be deemed, under the circumstances, a rather hard one, an honest clergyman of the town respectfully addressed a note to his Grace, begging him to take the case of those unfortunate mariners into full consideration.", "어떤 특정한 시각으로 본다면, 이 사건이 형편상 어쩌면 아주 약간은 다소 가혹한 경우로 여겨질 한 가닥 가능성이라도 있지 않을까 생각한 그 고장의 정직한 목사 하나가, 공작 각하께 정중히 서신을 띄워 그 불운한 뱃사람들의 처지를 충분히 헤아려 주시기를 간청했다."], ["To which my Lord Duke in substance replied (both letters were published) that he had already done so, and received the money, and would be obliged to the reverend gentleman if for the future he (the reverend gentleman) would decline meddling with other people’s business.", "이에 공작 각하는 (두 편지 모두 공개되었다) 요지인즉, 자신은 이미 그 점을 헤아렸으며, 돈도 이미 받았으니, 앞으로 그(목사)께서 남의 일에 참견하기를 삼가 주신다면 고맙겠다고 답했다."], ["Is this the still militant old man, standing at the corners of the three kingdoms, on all hands coercing alms of beggars?", "이자가 바로 세 왕국의 모퉁이마다 버티고 서서, 사방으로 거지들에게서 적선을 강요하는, 아직도 호전적인 그 늙은이란 말인가?"]], [["It will readily be seen that in this case the alleged right of the Duke to the whale was a delegated one from the Sovereign.", "이 경우 고래에 대한 공작의 주장된 권리가 군주로부터 위임받은 것임은 쉽게 알 수 있다."], ["We must needs inquire then on what principle the Sovereign is originally invested with that right.", "그렇다면 우리는 군주가 본래 무슨 원리에 따라 그 권리를 부여받았는지 마땅히 따져 보아야 한다."], ["The law itself has already been set forth.", "법 자체는 이미 앞에서 밝혔다."], ["But Plowdon gives us the reason for it.", "그러나 플라우든(Plowdon)은 그 이유를 우리에게 일러 준다."], ["Says Plowdon, the whale so caught belongs to the King and Queen, “because of its superior excellence.”", "플라우든이 말하기를, 그렇게 잡힌 고래는 국왕과 왕비에게 속하는데, \"그 탁월함이 워낙 빼어나기 때문\"이라 한다."], ["And by the soundest commentators this has ever been held a cogent argument in such matters.", "그리고 가장 견실한 주석가들은 이런 문제에서 이것을 줄곧 설득력 있는 논거로 받들어 왔다."]], [["But why should the King have the head, and the Queen the tail?", "그런데 어찌하여 국왕은 머리를 차지하고, 왕비는 꼬리를 차지하는가?"], ["A reason for that, ye lawyers!", "법률가들이여, 그 까닭을 대 보라!"]], [["In his treatise on “Queen-Gold,” or Queen-pinmoney, an old King’s Bench author, one William Prynne, thus discourseth: “Ye tail is ye Queen’s, that ye Queen’s wardrobe may be supplied with ye whalebone.”", "\"왕비 금(Queen-Gold)\", 곧 왕비 용돈에 관한 논고에서, 윌리엄 프린(William Prynne)이라는 옛 왕좌 법정(King's Bench)의 한 저술가는 이렇게 논한다."], ["Now this was written at a time when the black limber bone of the Greenland or Right whale was largely used in ladies’ bodices.", "\"꼬리는 왕비의 것이니, 이는 왕비의 의상실에 고래수염이 채워지게 하기 위함이다.\" 이 글이 쓰인 때는 그린란드고래, 곧 참고래의 검고 유연한 뼈가 귀부인들의 코르셋에 널리 쓰이던 시절이었다."], ["But this same bone is not in the tail; it is in the head, which is a sad mistake for a sagacious lawyer like Prynne.", "그러나 정작 그 뼈는 꼬리에 있는 것이 아니라 머리에 있으니, 프린처럼 명민한 법률가에게는 딱한 착오가 아닐 수 없다."], ["But is the Queen a mermaid, to be presented with a tail?", "한데 왕비가 꼬리를 진상받을 인어란 말인가?"], ["An allegorical meaning may lurk here.", "어쩌면 여기에 어떤 우의적 의미가 숨어 있는지도 모른다."]], [["There are two royal fish so styled by the English law writers—the whale and the sturgeon; both royal property under certain limitations, and nominally supplying the tenth branch of the crown’s ordinary revenue.", "잉글랜드 법학 저술가들이 그렇게 부르는 왕실 어류가 둘 있으니, 곧 고래와 철갑상어다. 둘 다 일정한 제한 아래 왕실 재산이며, 명목상 왕실 통상 세입의 열 번째 항목을 채워 준다."], ["I know not that any other author has hinted of the matter; but by inference it seems to me that the sturgeon must be divided in the same way as the whale, the King receiving the highly dense and elastic head peculiar to that fish, which, symbolically regarded, may possibly be humorously grounded upon some presumed congeniality.", "다른 어느 저술가가 이 문제를 넌지시 언급했는지는 나도 알지 못한다. 그러나 미루어 짐작건대, 철갑상어 역시 고래와 똑같은 방식으로 나뉘어, 국왕이 그 물고기 특유의 매우 단단하고 탄력 있는 머리를 차지하게 되는 듯하다."], ["And thus there seems a reason in all things, even in law.", "이를 상징적으로 본다면, 어쩌면 둘 사이에 어떤 기질의 닮음이 있으리라는 가정에 익살스레 근거한 것일지도 모른다. 그러니 만물에는, 심지어 법에조차도, 나름의 이치가 깃들어 있는 모양이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 91. The Pequod Meets The Rose-Bud.", "# CHAPTER 91. The Pequod Meets The Rose-Bud."]], [["“In vain it was to rake for Ambergriese in the paunch of this Leviathan, insufferable fetor denying not inquiry.”", "\"이 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 배 속을 헤집어 용연향을 캐려 한들 헛수고였으니, 그 견딜 수 없는 악취가 탐색조차 허락하지 않았기 때문이다.\" —토머스 브라운 경(Sir T."], ["Sir T. Browne, V.E.", "Browne), V.E."]], [["It was a week or two after the last whaling scene recounted, and when we were slowly sailing over a sleepy, vapory, mid-day sea, that the many noses on the Pequod’s deck proved more vigilant discoverers than the three pairs of eyes aloft.", "앞서 이야기한 마지막 고래잡이 장면이 있고 한두 주가 지난 어느 무렵이었다. 우리는 졸음에 겨운 듯 안개 자욱한 한낮의 바다 위를 느릿느릿 항해하고 있었는데, 그때 피쿼드호(Pequod) 갑판 위의 수많은 코들이 돛대 꼭대기에 있는 세 쌍의 눈보다 더 예리한 발견자임이 드러났다."], ["A peculiar and not very pleasant smell was smelt in the sea.", "바다에서 야릇하고 그다지 유쾌하지 않은 냄새가 풍겨 온 것이다."]], [["“I will bet something now,”", "\"내 뭔가 걸고 장담하건대,\""], ["said Stubb, “that somewhere hereabouts are some of those drugged whales we tickled the other day.", "스터브(Stubb)가 말했다. \"이 근방 어딘가에 며칠 전 우리가 약물 작살로 건드렸던 그 고래들 몇 마리가 있을 거요."], ["I thought they would keel up before long.”", "머잖아 배를 까뒤집고 떠오르리라 짐작은 했지.\""]], [["Presently, the vapors in advance slid aside; and there in the distance lay a ship, whose furled sails betokened that some sort of whale must be alongside.", "이윽고 앞쪽의 안개가 옆으로 미끄러지듯 갈라졌다. 그러자 멀리 한 척의 배가 누워 있는 것이 보였는데, 둘둘 말려 올라간 돛으로 보아 어떤 고래 한 마리가 그 뱃전에 매여 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["As we glided nearer, the stranger showed French colours from his peak; and by the eddying cloud of vulture sea-fowl that circled, and hovered, and swooped around him, it was plain that the whale alongside must be what the fishermen call a blasted whale, that is, a whale that has died unmolested on the sea, and so floated an unappropriated corpse.", "우리가 점점 가까이 미끄러져 가자, 그 낯선 배는 돛대 꼭대기에 프랑스 국기를 내걸고 있었다. 그리고 그 주위를 빙빙 돌며 맴돌고 내리꽂히는 독수리 같은 바닷새 떼의 소용돌이치는 구름으로 보아, 뱃전에 매인 그 고래는 어부들이 이른바 썩은 고래라 부르는 것이 분명했다."], ["It may well be conceived, what an unsavory odor such a mass must exhale; worse than an Assyrian city in the plague, when the living are incompetent to bury the departed.", "다시 말해 바다에서 아무에게도 시달리지 않고 죽어, 임자 없는 시체로 떠다니던 고래였다. 그런 덩어리가 얼마나 역겨운 냄새를 내뿜을지는 충분히 짐작할 수 있으니, 산 자들이 죽은 자들을 미처 묻지 못할 만큼 역병에 휩싸인 아시리아의 도시보다도 더 고약하다."], ["So intolerable indeed is it regarded by some, that no cupidity could persuade them to moor alongside of it.", "실로 어떤 이들은 이를 도저히 견딜 수 없게 여긴 나머지, 그 어떤 탐욕으로도 그 곁에 배를 댈 마음을 먹게 할 수 없을 정도다."], ["Yet are there those who will still do it; notwithstanding the fact that the oil obtained from such subjects is of a very inferior quality, and by no means of the nature of attar-of-rose.", "그럼에도 여전히 그 일을 마다하지 않는 자들이 있으니, 그런 고래에서 얻는 기름이 품질이 매우 떨어지고 결코 장미 향유 같은 것은 아니라는 사실에도 아랑곳하지 않는다."]], [["Coming still nearer with the expiring breeze, we saw that the Frenchman had a second whale alongside; and this second whale seemed even more of a nosegay than the first.", "스러져 가는 미풍을 타고 더욱 가까이 다가가 보니, 그 프랑스 배에는 두 번째 고래가 또 뱃전에 매여 있었다. 그리고 이 두 번째 고래는 첫 번째 것보다 한층 더 꽃다발 같은 냄새를 풍기는 듯했다."], ["In truth, it turned out to be one of those problematical whales that seem to dry up and die with a sort of prodigious dyspepsia, or indigestion; leaving their defunct bodies almost entirely bankrupt of anything like oil.", "사실, 그것은 일종의 어마어마한 소화 불량, 곧 위장 장애로 바싹 말라 죽어 가는 듯한 저 알 수 없는 고래들 가운데 하나임이 드러났다. 그런 고래는 죽은 몸뚱이에 기름이라 할 만한 것이 거의 한 푼도 남지 않은 빈털터리 신세다."], ["Nevertheless, in the proper place we shall see that no knowing fisherman will ever turn up his nose at such a whale as this, however much he may shun blasted whales in general.", "그럼에도 불구하고, 마땅한 대목에 이르면 우리는 보게 될 것이다. 아무리 노련한 어부라도 썩은 고래 일반은 피한다 할지언정, 이런 고래 앞에서는 결코 코를 돌리지 않는다는 사실을."]], [["The Pequod had now swept so nigh to the stranger, that Stubb vowed he recognised his cutting spade-pole entangled in the lines that were knotted round the tail of one of these whales.", "피쿼드호는 어느덧 그 낯선 배에 바싹 가까이 다가가 있었기에, 스터브는 자기 고래 절단용 삽자루가 이 고래들 중 한 마리의 꼬리에 둘둘 감긴 밧줄에 얽혀 있는 것을 알아보았노라고 장담했다."]], [["“There’s a pretty fellow, now,”", "\"저기 보시오, 어여쁜 친구로군,\""], ["he banteringly laughed, standing in the ship’s bows, “there’s a jackal for ye!", "그가 뱃머리에 서서 놀리듯 껄껄 웃었다. \"당신네들에게 보여 줄 자칼이 여기 있소이다!"], ["I well know that these Crappoes of Frenchmen are but poor devils in the fishery; sometimes lowering their boats for breakers, mistaking them for Sperm Whale spouts; yes, and sometimes sailing from their port with their hold full of boxes of tallow candles, and cases of snuffers, foreseeing that all the oil they will get won’t be enough to dip the Captain’s wick into; aye, we all know these things; but look ye, here’s a Crappo that is content with our leavings, the drugged whale there, I mean; aye, and is content too with scraping the dry bones of that other precious fish he has there.", "나는 잘 알지, 이 프랑스 개구리 놈들이 고래잡이에서는 한낱 가련한 얼간이들이라는 걸. 더러는 부서지는 파도를 향유고래 물줄기로 잘못 알고 보트를 내리기도 하고, 또 더러는 얻을 기름이라곤 선장 등잔 심지 적실 만큼도 못 되리라는 걸 뻔히 알면서도, 짐칸에 수지 양초 상자와 심지 가위 궤짝을 가득 싣고 항구를 떠나기도 하지."], ["Poor devil!", "그래, 우리 모두 이런 사정을 잘 알고 있잖나."], ["I say, pass round a hat, some one, and let’s make him a present of a little oil for dear charity’s sake.", "한데 보시오, 여기 우리가 남긴 찌꺼기로 만족하는 개구리 한 마리가 있구려, 저 약물 먹은 고래 말이오. 그래, 게다가 저쪽에 매단 또 한 마리, 그 귀하신 물고기의 메마른 뼈다귀나 긁어 대는 걸로도 만족한다지."], ["For what oil he’ll get from that drugged whale there, wouldn’t be fit to burn in a jail; no, not in a condemned cell.", "가련한 얼간이! 내 말하건대, 누가 모자라도 한 바퀴 돌려서 갸륵한 자선의 마음으로 그에게 기름이나 조금 선물해 줍시다."], ["And as for the other whale, why, I’ll agree to get more oil by chopping up and trying out these three masts of ours, than he’ll get from that bundle of bones; though, now that I think of it, it may contain something worth a good deal more than oil; yes, ambergris.", "저 약물 먹은 고래에서 얻을 기름이라곤 감옥에서 태우기에도 부적합하고, 아니, 사형수 감방에서도 못 쓸 물건일 테니. 그리고 저 다른 고래로 말할 것 같으면, 그가 저 뼈 무더기에서 얻을 기름보다, 차라리 우리 배 돛대 셋을 잘게 쪼개 솥에 끓여 짜내는 편이 더 많은 기름을 얻으리라 장담하겠소."], ["I wonder now if our old man has thought of that.", "한데 생각해 보니, 그 안에는 기름보다 훨씬 값진 무언가가 들어 있을지도 모르겠군. 그래, 용연향 말이오."], ["It’s worth trying.", "우리 영감님이 그 생각을 해 보셨을까 궁금하군."], ["Yes, I’m for it;”", "한번 시험해 볼 만하지."], ["and so saying he started for the quarter-deck.", "그래, 나는 찬성이오.\" 그러고는 이렇게 말하며 그는 뒷갑판을 향해 발길을 옮겼다."]], [["By this time the faint air had become a complete calm; so that whether or no, the Pequod was now fairly entrapped in the smell, with no hope of escaping except by its breezing up again.", "이 무렵 희미하던 바람마저 완전히 잦아들어, 좋든 싫든 피쿼드호는 이제 그 냄새에 완전히 갇혀 버린 꼴이 되었으니, 다시 바람이 일어나 주는 것 말고는 빠져나갈 가망이 없었다."], ["Issuing from the cabin, Stubb now called his boat’s crew, and pulled off for the stranger.", "선실에서 나온 스터브는 이제 자기 보트의 선원들을 불러 모아, 그 낯선 배를 향해 노를 저어 나아갔다."], ["Drawing across her bow, he perceived that in accordance with the fanciful French taste, the upper part of her stem-piece was carved in the likeness of a huge drooping stalk, was painted green, and for thorns had copper spikes projecting from it here and there; the whole terminating in a symmetrical folded bulb of a bright red colour.", "뱃머리를 가로질러 지나가던 그는, 프랑스 특유의 별난 취향에 걸맞게 그 배 이물 기둥의 윗부분이 거대하게 늘어진 줄기 모양으로 깎여 초록빛으로 칠해져 있고, 가시 대신 여기저기 구리 못이 삐죽삐죽 솟아 있으며, 그 전체가 선명한 붉은빛으로 좌우 대칭을 이루며 오므린 꽃봉오리 모양으로 끝맺어진 것을 보았다."], ["Upon her head boards, in large gilt letters, he read “Bouton de Rose,”—Rose-button, or Rose-bud; and this was the romantic name of this aromatic ship.", "그 뱃머리 명판에는 커다란 금박 글자로 \"부통 드 로즈(Bouton de Rose)\" — 곧 장미 봉오리, 또는 장미꽃 봉오리라 쓰여 있었으니, 이것이 바로 이 향기로운 배의 낭만적인 이름이었다."]], [["Though Stubb did not understand the Bouton part of the inscription, yet the word rose, and the bulbous figure-head put together, sufficiently explained the whole to him.", "스터브는 그 글귀에서 \"부통(Bouton)\" 부분은 알아듣지 못했으나, \"로즈(rose)\"라는 단어와 그 구근 모양의 뱃머리 장식을 함께 놓고 보니, 전체 뜻이 충분히 헤아려졌다."]], [["“A wooden rose-bud, eh?”", "\"나무로 깎은 장미 봉오리라, 응?\" 그가 한 손을 코에 갖다 댄 채 외쳤다."], ["he cried with his hand to his nose, “that will do very well; but how like all creation it smells!”", "\"그것참 그럴듯하군. 한데 어찌 이리도 천지를 뒤덮을 듯한 냄새를 풍기는고!\""]], [["Now in order to hold direct communication with the people on deck, he had to pull round the bows to the starboard side, and thus come close to the blasted whale; and so talk over it.", "자, 갑판 위의 사람들과 직접 이야기를 나누려면, 그는 뱃머리를 빙 돌아 우현 쪽으로 노를 저어, 그리하여 그 썩은 고래 가까이 다가가서는, 그 고래 너머로 말을 건네야만 했다."]], [["Arrived then at this spot, with one hand still to his nose, he bawled—“Bouton-de-Rose, ahoy!", "그렇게 그 자리에 다다른 그는, 여전히 한 손을 코에 댄 채 고함쳤다. \"부통 드 로즈호, 어이!"], ["are there any of you Bouton-de-Roses that speak English?”", "거기 영어 할 줄 아는 부통 드 로즈 양반 없소?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"있소.\""], ["rejoined a Guernsey-man from the bulwarks, who turned out to be the chief-mate.", "뱃전에서 건지섬 사내 하나가 대꾸했는데, 알고 보니 그가 일등항해사였다."]], [["“Well, then, my Bouton-de-Rose-bud, have you seen the White Whale?”", "\"음, 그렇다면, 내 어여쁜 장미 봉오리님, 흰 고래(White Whale)를 본 적 있소?\""]], [["“What whale?”", "\"무슨 고래라고?\""]], [["“The White Whale—a Sperm Whale—Moby Dick, have ye seen him?", "\"흰 고래 말이오 — 향유고래 — 모비 딕(Moby Dick), 그놈을 본 적 있느냔 말이오?\""]], [["“Never heard of such a whale.", "\"그런 고래는 들어 본 적도 없소."], ["Cachalot Blanche!", "카샬로 블랑슈!"], ["White Whale—no.”", "흰 고래라 — 본 적 없소.\""]], [["“Very good, then; good bye now, and I’ll call again in a minute.”", "\"좋아요, 그럼. 이만 작별하리다. 곧 다시 부르겠소.\""]], [["Then rapidly pulling back towards the Pequod, and seeing Ahab leaning over the quarter-deck rail awaiting his report, he moulded his two hands into a trumpet and shouted—“No, Sir!", "그러고는 재빨리 피쿼드호 쪽으로 노를 저어 돌아가니, 에이해브(Ahab)가 뒷갑판 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 그의 보고를 기다리고 있는 것이 보였다. 스터브는 두 손을 모아 나팔처럼 만들고 소리쳤다."], ["No!”", "\"아닙니다, 선장님!"], ["Upon which Ahab retired, and Stubb returned to the Frenchman.", "없답니다!\" 그러자 에이해브는 물러갔고, 스터브는 다시 프랑스 배로 돌아갔다."]], [["He now perceived that the Guernsey-man, who had just got into the chains, and was using a cutting-spade, had slung his nose in a sort of bag.", "이제 보니 그 건지섬 사내는 방금 뱃전 쇠사슬 위로 내려와 고래 절단용 삽을 쓰고 있었는데, 코에는 일종의 자루 같은 것을 둘둘 감아 매달고 있었다."]], [["“What’s the matter with your nose, there?”", "\"거기 코가 왜 그러시오?\" 스터브가 말했다."], ["said Stubb. “Broke it?”", "\"부러졌소?\""]], [["“I wish it was broken, or that I didn’t have any nose at all!”", "\"차라리 부러졌더라면, 아니, 아예 코라는 게 없었더라면 좋겠소!\""], ["answered the Guernsey-man, who did not seem to relish the job he was at very much.", "자기가 하는 일이 영 마뜩잖은 듯한 건지섬 사내가 대꾸했다."], ["“But what are you holding yours for?”", "\"한데 댁은 어째서 그쪽 코를 붙잡고 있는 거요?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing!", "\"오, 아무것도 아니오!"], ["It’s a wax nose; I have to hold it on.", "밀랍으로 만든 코라서, 떨어지지 않게 붙잡고 있어야 한다오."], ["Fine day, ain’t it?", "날씨 한번 좋구려, 안 그렇소?"], ["Air rather gardenny, I should say; throw us a bunch of posies, will ye, Bouton-de-Rose?”", "공기가 제법 정원 냄새가 나는데, 한 마디로. 우리한테 꽃다발이나 한 다발 던져 주시겠소, 부통 드 로즈?\""]], [["“What in the devil’s name do you want here?”", "\"도대체 무슨 빌어먹을 볼일로 여기 왔단 말이오?\""], ["roared the Guernseyman, flying into a sudden passion.", "건지섬 사내가 별안간 발끈 화를 내며 으르렁거렸다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["keep cool—cool?", "진정하시오 — 진정?"], ["yes, that’s the word!", "그래, 그 말이 딱이군!"], ["why don’t you pack those whales in ice while you’re working at ’em?", "그런 고래들 다룰 때 어째서 얼음에 재워 두질 않는 거요?"], ["But joking aside, though; do you know, Rose-bud, that it’s all nonsense trying to get any oil out of such whales?", "한데 농담은 그만두고, 장미 봉오리 양반, 당신도 알고 있소? 그런 고래에서 기름을 짜내려는 게 죄다 헛수고라는 걸 말이오?"], ["As for that dried up one, there, he hasn’t a gill in his whole carcase.”", "저쪽 바싹 마른 놈으로 말할 것 같으면, 그 몸뚱이 통째로 기름 한 홉도 안 나올 거요.\""]], [["“I know that well enough; but, d’ye see, the Captain here won’t believe it; this is his first voyage; he was a Cologne manufacturer before. But come aboard, and mayhap he’ll believe you, if he won’t me; and so I’ll get out of this dirty scrape.”", "\"그쯤은 나도 잘 알고 있소. 한데, 보시오, 여기 선장이 도무지 그 말을 믿질 않는단 말이오. 이번이 그의 첫 항해라오. 전에는 쾰른에서 향수 제조나 하던 사람이지. 그러니 배에 올라와 주시오. 혹 내 말은 안 믿어도 댁의 말은 믿을지도 모르니. 그러면 나도 이 더러운 꼴에서 벗어날 수 있겠지.\""]], [["“Anything to oblige ye, my sweet and pleasant fellow,”", "\"당신이 부탁이라면 무엇이든 들어 드리리다, 내 다정하고 유쾌한 친구여.\""], ["rejoined Stubb, and with that he soon mounted to the deck.", "스터브가 대꾸하고는, 그 말과 함께 곧 갑판으로 올라섰다."], ["There a queer scene presented itself.", "거기에는 기이한 광경이 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["The sailors, in tasselled caps of red worsted, were getting the heavy tackles in readiness for the whales.", "붉은 털실로 짠 술 달린 모자를 쓴 선원들이 고래를 끌어올릴 묵직한 도르래 장비를 채비하고 있었다."], ["But they worked rather slow and talked very fast, and seemed in anything but a good humor.", "그러나 그들은 일은 영 굼뜨게 하면서 입은 무척 빠르게 놀렸고, 도무지 기분이 좋아 보이지 않았다."], ["All their noses upwardly projected from their faces like so many jib-booms.", "그들의 코는 하나같이 얼굴에서 위쪽으로 삐죽 솟아, 마치 여러 개의 삼각돛 활대 같았다."], ["Now and then pairs of them would drop their work, and run up to the mast-head to get some fresh air.", "이따금 두엇씩 짝을 지어 일손을 놓고는 신선한 공기를 좀 마시려 돛대 꼭대기로 뛰어 올라가곤 했다."], ["Some thinking they would catch the plague, dipped oakum in coal-tar, and at intervals held it to their nostrils.", "어떤 자들은 역병에 걸릴까 두려워, 뱃밥을 콜타르에 적셔 틈틈이 콧구멍에 갖다 댔다."], ["Others having broken the stems of their pipes almost short off at the bowl, were vigorously puffing tobacco-smoke, so that it constantly filled their olfactories.", "또 어떤 자들은 파이프 대를 대통 바로 아래까지 짧게 부러뜨려 놓고는, 담배 연기를 힘차게 뻐끔뻐끔 피워 댔으니, 그리하여 그 연기가 끊임없이 그들의 콧속을 가득 채웠다."]], [["Stubb was struck by a shower of outcries and anathemas proceeding from the Captain’s round-house abaft; and looking in that direction saw a fiery face thrust from behind the door, which was held ajar from within.", "스터브는 고물 쪽 선장실에서 쏟아져 나오는 고함과 저주의 빗발에 흠칫 놀랐다. 그쪽으로 눈길을 돌려 보니, 안에서 빠끔히 열어 둔 문 뒤로 시뻘겋게 달아오른 얼굴 하나가 불쑥 내밀어져 있었다."], ["This was the tormented surgeon, who, after in vain remonstrating against the proceedings of the day, had betaken himself to the Captain’s round-house (cabinet he called it) to avoid the pest; but still, could not help yelling out his entreaties and indignations at times.", "그것은 시달릴 대로 시달린 선의(船醫)였으니, 그는 그날의 작업에 헛되이 항의를 거듭하다가, 그 역병을 피하려 선장실(그는 그곳을 \"내실(cabinet)\"이라 불렀다)로 몸을 피해 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그러면서도 이따금 애원과 분노의 고함을 내지르지 않고는 못 배기는 것이었다."]], [["Marking all this, Stubb argued well for his scheme, and turning to the Guernsey-man had a little chat with him, during which the stranger mate expressed his detestation of his Captain as a conceited ignoramus, who had brought them all into so unsavory and unprofitable a pickle.", "이 모든 것을 눈여겨본 스터브는 자기 계략이 잘 풀리겠다고 헤아리며, 건지섬 사내에게로 돌아서서 잠시 한담을 나누었다. 그러는 동안 그 낯선 배의 항해사는 자기 선장을 향한 혐오를 털어놓았으니, 그는 잘난 척하는 무식쟁이로서 자기들 모두를 이토록 역겹고 수지맞지 않는 곤경에 몰아넣은 자라는 것이었다."], ["Sounding him carefully, Stubb further perceived that the Guernsey-man had not the slightest suspicion concerning the ambergris.", "조심스레 그의 속을 떠본 스터브는, 그 건지섬 사내가 용연향에 관해서는 털끝만큼의 낌새도 채지 못하고 있음을 더욱 분명히 알아챘다."], ["He therefore held his peace on that head, but otherwise was quite frank and confidential with him, so that the two quickly concocted a little plan for both circumventing and satirizing the Captain, without his at all dreaming of distrusting their sincerity.", "그리하여 그 일에 관해서는 입을 다물었으나, 그 밖의 일에 대해서는 자못 솔직하고 허물없이 대했다. 그렇게 두 사람은 선장을 골탕 먹이는 동시에 비웃어 주려는 작은 계획을 재빨리 꾸며 냈으니, 선장은 그들의 진심을 추호도 의심할 꿈조차 꾸지 못했다."], ["According to this little plan of theirs, the Guernsey-man, under cover of an interpreter’s office, was to tell the Captain what he pleased, but as coming from Stubb; and as for Stubb, he was to utter any nonsense that should come uppermost in him during the interview.", "그들의 이 작은 계획에 따르면, 건지섬 사내는 통역관 노릇이라는 구실 아래 제멋대로 하고 싶은 말을 선장에게 전하되, 그것이 마치 스터브의 입에서 나온 것인 양 꾸미기로 했다. 그리고 스터브로 말하자면, 그 면담 내내 머릿속에 제일 먼저 떠오르는 헛소리는 무엇이든 지껄여 대기로 했다."]], [["By this time their destined victim appeared from his cabin.", "이 무렵 그들이 점찍은 제물이 선실에서 나타났다."], ["He was a small and dark, but rather delicate looking man for a sea-captain, with large whiskers and moustache, however; and wore a red cotton velvet vest with watch-seals at his side.", "그는 자그마하고 거무튀튀한, 그러나 뱃사람치고는 다소 가냘파 보이는 사내였는데, 그래도 큼직한 구레나룻과 콧수염을 기르고 있었다. 그는 붉은 무명 벨벳 조끼를 걸치고 옆구리에는 시곗줄 장식을 늘어뜨리고 있었다."], ["To this gentleman, Stubb was now politely introduced by the Guernsey-man, who at once ostentatiously put on the aspect of interpreting between them.", "건지섬 사내는 이제 이 신사에게 스터브를 정중히 소개했는데, 그러면서 곧장 두 사람 사이를 통역하는 듯한 태도를 짐짓 거창하게 차려 보였다."]], [["“What shall I say to him first?”", "\"먼저 그에게 뭐라고 말해 줄까요?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"글쎄,\" 스터브가 그 벨벳 조끼며 시계와 장식을 훑어보며 말했다."], ["said Stubb, eyeing the velvet vest and the watch and seals, “you may as well begin by telling him that he looks a sort of babyish to me, though I don’t pretend to be a judge.”", "\"우선 이렇게 전해 주시구려, 내 보기에 그가 어쩐지 애송이 같아 보인다고 말이오. 뭐, 내가 그런 걸 가릴 안목이 있다는 건 아니지만.\""]], [["“He says, Monsieur,”", "\"그가 이르기를, 무슈,\""], ["said the Guernsey-man, in French, turning to his captain, “that only yesterday his ship spoke a vessel, whose captain and chief-mate, with six sailors, had all died of a fever caught from a blasted whale they had brought alongside.”", "건지섬 사내가 프랑스어로 자기 선장을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"바로 어제 그의 배가 한 선박과 마주쳤는데, 그 배의 선장과 일등항해사가 선원 여섯과 더불어 모두, 뱃전에 끌어다 댄 썩은 고래에서 옮은 열병으로 죽었다고 합니다.\""]], [["Upon this the captain started, and eagerly desired to know more.", "이 말에 선장은 흠칫 놀라며 더 자세한 이야기를 간절히 듣고 싶어 했다."]], [["“What now?”", "\"이제 뭐라고 할까요?\""], ["said the Guernsey-man to Stubb.", "건지섬 사내가 스터브에게 물었다."]], [["“Why, since he takes it so easy, tell him that now I have eyed him carefully, I’m quite certain that he’s no more fit to command a whale-ship than a St.", "\"그래, 그가 그토록 태평하게 받아들이니, 이렇게 전하시오. 이제 내가 그를 찬찬히 뜯어보았는데, 그가 고래잡이배를 지휘하기에는 세인트야고의 원숭이만도 못한 위인임을 확신하게 되었다고 말이오."], ["Jago monkey. In fact, tell him from me he’s a baboon.”", "사실, 내 말이라며 이렇게 전해 주시오, 그가 한 마리 개코원숭이라고.\""]], [["“He vows and declares, Monsieur, that the other whale, the dried one, is far more deadly than the blasted one; in fine, Monsieur, he conjures us, as we value our lives, to cut loose from these fish.”", "\"그가 맹세코 단언하기를, 무슈,\" 건지섬 사내가 말했다. \"저 다른 고래, 곧 바싹 마른 놈이 썩은 놈보다 훨씬 더 치명적이라고 합니다. 요컨대, 무슈, 그는 우리가 목숨을 아낀다면 이 물고기들에게서 당장 밧줄을 풀어 떼어 내라고 간곡히 권하고 있습니다.\""]], [["Instantly the captain ran forward, and in a loud voice commanded his crew to desist from hoisting the cutting-tackles, and at once cast loose the cables and chains confining the whales to the ship.", "선장은 즉시 앞으로 달려 나가, 큰 소리로 선원들에게 명하여 절단용 도르래를 끌어올리는 일을 멈추게 하고, 고래들을 배에 붙들어 매고 있던 밧줄과 쇠사슬을 당장 풀어 던지게 했다."]], [["“What now?”", "\"이제 뭐라고 할까요?\""], ["said the Guernsey-man, when the Captain had returned to them.", "선장이 그들에게 돌아왔을 때 건지섬 사내가 물었다."]], [["“Why, let me see; yes, you may as well tell him now that—that—in fact, tell him I’ve diddled him, and (aside to himself) perhaps somebody else.”", "\"그래, 어디 보자. 그래, 이제 그에게 이렇게 전해도 좋겠군 — 그러니까 — 사실, 이렇게 전하시오, 내가 그를 감쪽같이 속여먹었다고. 그리고 (혼잣말로 곁들이며) 어쩌면 또 다른 누군가도 말이오.\""]], [["“He says, Monsieur, that he’s very happy to have been of any service to us.”", "\"그가 이르기를, 무슈, 우리에게 조금이라도 도움이 되었다니 더없이 기쁘다고 합니다.\""]], [["Hearing this, the captain vowed that they were the grateful parties (meaning himself and mate) and concluded by inviting Stubb down into his cabin to drink a bottle of Bordeaux.", "이 말을 듣자 선장은 고마워해야 할 쪽은 오히려 자기들(자신과 항해사를 가리킨 것이었다)이라고 거듭 말하더니, 끝으로 스터브를 자기 선실로 청해 보르도 포도주 한 병을 함께 마시자고 권했다."]], [["“He wants you to take a glass of wine with him,”", "\"그가 댁과 함께 포도주 한 잔 들기를 바란답니다.\""], ["said the interpreter.", "통역관이 말했다."]], [["“Thank him heartily; but tell him it’s against my principles to drink with the man I’ve diddled.", "\"진심으로 고맙다고 전해 주시오. 한데 내가 골탕 먹인 사람과 술을 마시는 건 내 신조에 어긋난다고 전하시구려."], ["In fact, tell him I must go.”", "사실, 나는 이만 가 봐야겠다고 전하시오.\""]], [["“He says, Monsieur, that his principles won’t admit of his drinking; but that if Monsieur wants to live another day to drink, then Monsieur had best drop all four boats, and pull the ship away from these whales, for it’s so calm they won’t drift.”", "\"그가 이르기를, 무슈, 자기 신조로 보면 술을 마시는 것은 허락되지 않으나, 만일 무슈가 하루라도 더 살아 술을 마시고 싶거든, 무슈께서는 보트 넷을 모두 내려 이 고래들에게서 배를 끌어내는 편이 상책이라 합니다. 워낙 바람 한 점 없어 배가 저절로 떠밀려 나지 않을 테니 말입니다.\""]], [["By this time Stubb was over the side, and getting into his boat, hailed the Guernsey-man to this effect,—that having a long tow-line in his boat, he would do what he could to help them, by pulling out the lighter whale of the two from the ship’s side.", "이 무렵 스터브는 이미 뱃전을 넘어 자기 보트에 올라타고 있었는데, 건지섬 사내를 향해 이렇게 외쳤다. 자기 보트에 긴 예인 밧줄이 있으니, 두 고래 가운데 가벼운 놈을 그쪽 뱃전에서 끌어내 주는 것으로 힘닿는 데까지 그들을 돕겠다고."], ["While the Frenchman’s boats, then, were engaged in towing the ship one way, Stubb benevolently towed away at his whale the other way, ostentatiously slacking out a most unusually long tow-line.", "그리하여 그 프랑스 배의 보트들이 배를 한쪽으로 끌어가는 동안, 스터브는 자비롭게도 자기 고래를 반대쪽으로 끌어내며, 유난히도 긴 예인 밧줄을 짐짓 거창하게 풀어 늘어뜨렸다."]], [["Presently a breeze sprang up; Stubb feigned to cast off from the whale; hoisting his boats, the Frenchman soon increased his distance, while the Pequod slid in between him and Stubb’s whale.", "이윽고 미풍이 일었다. 스터브는 고래에게서 밧줄을 푸는 시늉을 했다."], ["Whereupon Stubb quickly pulled to the floating body, and hailing the Pequod to give notice of his intentions, at once proceeded to reap the fruit of his unrighteous cunning.", "프랑스 배는 보트들을 끌어올리고 곧 거리를 벌려 멀어져 갔고, 그사이 피쿼드호가 그 배와 스터브의 고래 사이로 미끄러져 들어왔다. 그러자 스터브는 재빨리 그 떠 있는 시체로 노를 저어 가, 자기 속셈을 알리려 피쿼드호를 향해 소리쳐 두고는, 곧장 자기의 의롭지 못한 잔꾀의 열매를 거두는 일에 착수했다."], ["Seizing his sharp boat-spade, he commenced an excavation in the body, a little behind the side fin.", "날카로운 보트용 삽을 움켜쥐고, 그는 옆지느러미 조금 뒤쪽에서 그 몸뚱이를 파 들어가기 시작했다."], ["You would almost have thought he was digging a cellar there in the sea; and when at length his spade struck against the gaunt ribs, it was like turning up old Roman tiles and pottery buried in fat English loam.", "그 모습은 마치 바다 한가운데서 지하실이라도 파는 것처럼 보였다. 그리고 마침내 그의 삽이 앙상한 갈빗대에 부딪쳤을 때, 그것은 마치 기름진 영국 흙 속에 묻힌 옛 로마의 기와와 도기 조각을 파헤쳐 올리는 것과도 같았다."], ["His boat’s crew were all in high excitement, eagerly helping their chief, and looking as anxious as gold-hunters.", "그의 보트 선원들은 모두 잔뜩 흥분하여 우두머리를 열심히 거들었으니, 그 안달하는 모습이 마치 금을 캐는 자들 같았다."]], [["And all the time numberless fowls were diving, and ducking, and screaming, and yelling, and fighting around them.", "그러는 내내 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 새들이 그들 주위에서 자맥질하고, 머리를 처박고, 끽끽거리고, 악다구니를 쳐 대며 서로 다투었다."], ["Stubb was beginning to look disappointed, especially as the horrible nosegay increased, when suddenly from out the very heart of this plague, there stole a faint stream of perfume, which flowed through the tide of bad smells without being absorbed by it, as one river will flow into and then along with another, without at all blending with it for a time.", "끔찍한 꽃다발 냄새가 더욱 짙어지자 스터브는 차츰 낙담한 기색을 띠기 시작했는데, 바로 그때 이 역병의 한복판에서 희미한 향내의 가닥이 슬그머니 새어 나왔다. 그것은 한 줄기 강물이 다른 강물 속으로 흘러들었다가 한동안 전혀 섞이지 않은 채 그 강물과 나란히 흘러가듯, 그 고약한 냄새의 물결을 헤치고 흐르면서도 거기에 흡수되지 않았다."]], [["“I have it, I have it,”", "\"찾았다, 찾았어.\""], ["cried Stubb, with delight, striking something in the subterranean regions, “a purse!", "땅속 깊은 데서 무언가를 삽으로 친 스터브가 기쁨에 겨워 외쳤다. \"지갑이다!"], ["a purse!”", "지갑이야!\""]], [["Dropping his spade, he thrust both hands in, and drew out handfuls of something that looked like ripe Windsor soap, or rich mottled old cheese; very unctuous and savory withal.", "삽을 내던지고 두 손을 쑤셔 넣은 그는, 잘 익은 윈저 비누, 혹은 진하게 얼룩진 묵은 치즈처럼 생긴 무언가를 한 움큼씩 끄집어냈다. 그것은 기름지고도 향기로웠다."], ["You might easily dent it with your thumb; it is of a hue between yellow and ash colour.", "엄지손가락으로 누르면 쉽사리 자국이 남았고, 빛깔은 노란빛과 잿빛 사이를 띠었다."], ["And this, good friends, is ambergris, worth a gold guinea an ounce to any druggist.", "그리고 이것이야말로, 선량한 벗들이여, 용연향이니, 어느 약제사에게든 한 온스에 금 1기니를 받을 만한 물건이다."], ["Some six handfuls were obtained; but more was unavoidably lost in the sea, and still more, perhaps, might have been secured were it not for impatient Ahab’s loud command to Stubb to desist, and come on board, else the ship would bid them good bye.", "여섯 움큼 남짓 얻어냈으나, 더 많은 양이 어쩔 수 없이 바다에 흩어져 버렸고, 어쩌면 그보다 더 많이 건질 수도 있었으련만, 그만두고 어서 배로 돌아오라는, 그러지 않으면 배가 작별을 고하고 떠나리라는 성마른 에이해브의 우렁찬 명령이 스터브에게 떨어진 탓이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 92. Ambergris.", "# CHAPTER 92. Ambergris."]], [["Now this ambergris is a very curious substance, and so important as an article of commerce, that in 1791 a certain Nantucket-born Captain Coffin was examined at the bar of the English House of Commons on that subject.", "이 용연향(龍涎香)이라는 것은 참으로 기묘한 물질이며, 교역품으로서 어찌나 중요한지, 1791년에는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 태생의 어느 코핀 선장이 바로 이 문제로 영국 하원의 증언대에 불려 나와 심문을 받기까지 했다."], ["For at that time, and indeed until a comparatively late day, the precise origin of ambergris remained, like amber itself, a problem to the learned.", "그도 그럴 것이, 그 무렵까지도, 아니 비교적 최근에 이르기까지도, 용연향의 정확한 기원은 호박(琥珀) 그 자체와 마찬가지로 학자들에게는 풀리지 않는 수수께끼로 남아 있었기 때문이다."], ["Though the word ambergris is but the French compound for grey amber, yet the two substances are quite distinct.", "용연향(ambergris)이라는 말은 프랑스어로 회색 호박을 뜻하는 합성어에 지나지 않지만, 사실 이 두 물질은 전혀 별개의 것이다."], ["For amber, though at times found on the sea-coast, is also dug up in some far inland soils, whereas ambergris is never found except upon the sea.", "호박은 더러 바닷가에서 발견되기도 하나, 바다에서 멀리 떨어진 내륙의 흙 속에서 캐내어지기도 하는 반면, 용연향은 오직 바다에서만 발견될 뿐 그 밖의 곳에서는 결코 찾아볼 수 없기 때문이다."], ["Besides, amber is a hard, transparent, brittle, odorless substance, used for mouth-pieces to pipes, for beads and ornaments; but ambergris is soft, waxy, and so highly fragrant and spicy, that it is largely used in perfumery, in pastiles, precious candles, hair-powders, and pomatum.", "게다가 호박은 단단하고 투명하며 잘 부서지고 냄새가 없는 물질로서, 파이프의 물부리나 구슬, 장신구 따위를 만드는 데 쓰이지만, 용연향은 부드럽고 밀랍 같으며 어찌나 향기롭고 향내가 짙은지, 향수와 향환(香丸), 값비싼 양초, 머릿가루, 포마드 따위에 널리 쓰인다."], ["The Turks use it in cooking, and also carry it to Mecca, for the same purpose that frankincense is carried to St.", "터키인들은 그것을 요리에 쓰기도 하고, 로마의 성 베드로 대성당에 유향을 가져가는 것과 같은 목적으로 메카로 가져가기도 한다."], ["Peter’s in Rome. Some wine merchants drop a few grains into claret, to flavor it.", "어떤 포도주 상인들은 향을 내려고 클라레 포도주에 그것을 몇 알 떨어뜨리기도 한다."]], [["Who would think, then, that such fine ladies and gentlemen should regale themselves with an essence found in the inglorious bowels of a sick whale! Yet so it is.", "그렇다면 그토록 고상한 신사 숙녀들이 병든 고래의 그 추레한 창자 속에서 나온 정수(精髓)로 제 입맛을 돋운다는 것을 누가 짐작이나 하겠는가!"], ["By some, ambergris is supposed to be the cause, and by others the effect, of the dyspepsia in the whale.", "그러나 사실이 그러하다. 어떤 이들은 용연향을 고래가 앓는 소화불량의 원인이라 여기고, 또 어떤 이들은 그 결과라 여긴다."], ["How to cure such a dyspepsia it were hard to say, unless by administering three or four boat loads of Brandreth’s pills, and then running out of harm’s way, as laborers do in blasting rocks.", "그런 소화불량을 어떻게 고쳐야 할지는 참으로 말하기 어려운데, 다만 브랜드레스 환약을 보트로 서너 척 분량쯤 먹인 다음, 바위를 폭파하는 일꾼들이 그러하듯 위험을 피해 멀찌감치 달아나는 수밖에는 없을 것이다."]], [["I have forgotten to say that there were found in this ambergris, certain hard, round, bony plates, which at first Stubb thought might be sailors’ trowsers buttons; but it afterwards turned out that they were nothing more than pieces of small squid bones embalmed in that manner.", "이 용연향 속에서 단단하고 둥근 뼈 같은 판들이 발견되었다는 말을 미처 하지 못했는데, 처음에 스터브(Stubb)는 그것이 선원들 바지 단추가 아닐까 생각했으나, 나중에 밝혀진 바로는 그것은 다름 아니라 그런 식으로 방부 처리된 작은 오징어 뼛조각에 불과했다."]], [["Now that the incorruption of this most fragrant ambergris should be found in the heart of such decay; is this nothing?", "그런데 이토록 향기로운 용연향의 썩지 않음이 바로 그런 부패의 한복판에서 발견된다는 것, 이것이 과연 아무것도 아니란 말인가?"], ["Bethink thee of that saying of St. Paul in Corinthians, about corruption and incorruption; how that we are sown in dishonor, but raised in glory.", "부패와 불멸에 관하여, 우리가 욕된 가운데 뿌려졌으나 영광 가운데 일으킴을 받는다고 한 고린도전서의 성 바울의 그 말씀을 한번 곰곰이 생각해 보라."], ["And likewise call to mind that saying of Paracelsus about what it is that maketh the best musk.", "그리고 또한 무엇이 가장 좋은 사향을 만드는가에 관한 파라켈수스의 그 말도 떠올려 보라."], ["Also forget not the strange fact that of all things of ill-savor, Cologne-water, in its rudimental manufacturing stages, is the worst.", "또한 온갖 고약한 냄새를 풍기는 것들 가운데서도, 초기 제조 단계의 쾰른 향수가 가장 지독한 냄새를 풍긴다는 그 기묘한 사실도 잊지 마라."]], [["I should like to conclude the chapter with the above appeal, but cannot, owing to my anxiety to repel a charge often made against whalemen, and which, in the estimation of some already biased minds, might be considered as indirectly substantiated by what has been said of the Frenchman’s two whales.", "나는 이 장을 위와 같은 호소로 마무리하고 싶지만 그럴 수가 없으니, 고래잡이들에게 흔히 가해지는 한 가지 비난을 반박하고픈 마음이 간절하기 때문이다. 그 비난은, 이미 한쪽으로 기운 어떤 이들의 판단으로는, 저 프랑스인의 두 고래에 관해 앞서 말한 바로 인해 간접적으로 입증되는 것처럼 여겨질 수도 있는 것이다."], ["Elsewhere in this volume the slanderous aspersion has been disproved, that the vocation of whaling is throughout a slatternly, untidy business.", "이 책의 다른 대목에서 나는, 고래잡이라는 직업이 처음부터 끝까지 칠칠치 못하고 지저분한 일이라는 그 비방을 이미 논박한 바 있다."], ["But there is another thing to rebut.", "그러나 또 한 가지 반박해야 할 것이 있다."], ["They hint that all whales always smell bad.", "그들은 모든 고래가 늘 고약한 냄새를 풍긴다고 넌지시 비꼰다."], ["Now how did this odious stigma originate?", "자, 그렇다면 이 가증스러운 낙인은 어디서 비롯된 것일까?"]], [["I opine, that it is plainly traceable to the first arrival of the Greenland whaling ships in London, more than two centuries ago.", "내 생각에 그것은 두 세기도 더 전에 그린란드 고래잡이배들이 런던에 처음 들어왔던 일로 분명히 거슬러 올라갈 수 있다."], ["Because those whalemen did not then, and do not now, try out their oil at sea as the Southern ships have always done; but cutting up the fresh blubber in small bits, thrust it through the bung holes of large casks, and carry it home in that manner; the shortness of the season in those Icy Seas, and the sudden and violent storms to which they are exposed, forbidding any other course.", "왜냐하면 그 고래잡이들은 그때나 지금이나, 남쪽 바다의 배들이 늘 해 온 것처럼 기름을 바다 위에서 정제하지 않고, 신선한 지방을 작은 조각으로 잘라 큰 통의 마개 구멍으로 쑤셔 넣은 다음 그런 식으로 고향까지 싣고 오기 때문이다. 그 얼어붙은 바다에서는 어기(漁期)가 짧고, 또 그들이 맞닥뜨리는 갑작스럽고 사나운 폭풍 탓에 달리 어찌해 볼 도리가 없는 것이다."], ["The consequence is, that upon breaking into the hold, and unloading one of these whale cemeteries, in the Greenland dock, a savor is given forth somewhat similar to that arising from excavating an old city grave-yard, for the foundations of a Lying-in Hospital.", "그 결과, 그린란드 부두에서 이런 고래 무덤 같은 배의 화물칸을 열어젖히고 짐을 부릴 때면, 마치 산부인과 병원의 기초를 닦으려 오래된 도시의 묘지를 파헤칠 때 피어오르는 것과 어딘가 비슷한 냄새가 풍겨 나온다."]], [["I partly surmise also, that this wicked charge against whalers may be likewise imputed to the existence on the coast of Greenland, in former times, of a Dutch village called Schmerenburgh or Smeerenberg, which latter name is the one used by the learned Fogo Von Slack, in his great work on Smells, a text-book on that subject.", "또한 내가 어느 정도 짐작하기로는, 고래잡이들에게 가해진 이 못된 비난은, 옛날 그린란드 해안에 있었던 스흐메렌뷔르흐, 혹은 스메렌베르흐라 불리던 어느 네덜란드 마을의 존재 탓으로도 돌릴 수 있을 듯하다. 이 후자의 이름은 박식한 포고 폰 슬랙이 냄새에 관한 그의 위대한 저작, 곧 그 주제의 교과서에서 쓴 것이다."], ["As its name imports (smeer, fat; berg, to put up), this village was founded in order to afford a place for the blubber of the Dutch whale fleet to be tried out, without being taken home to Holland for that purpose.", "그 이름이 뜻하는 바대로(스메르는 기름, 베르흐는 쌓아 두다라는 뜻이다), 이 마을은 네덜란드 고래잡이 선단의 지방을 그 목적으로 네덜란드 본국까지 가져가지 않고 정제할 수 있는 장소를 마련하려고 세워진 것이었다."], ["It was a collection of furnaces, fat-kettles, and oil sheds; and when the works were in full operation certainly gave forth no very pleasant savor.", "그곳은 가마와 기름솥과 기름 창고가 모인 곳이었으니, 작업이 한창 돌아갈 때면 분명 그리 유쾌한 냄새를 풍기지는 않았을 것이다."], ["But all this is quite different with a South Sea Sperm Whaler; which in a voyage of four years perhaps, after completely filling her hold with oil, does not, perhaps, consume fifty days in the business of boiling out; and in the state that it is casked, the oil is nearly scentless.", "그러나 이 모든 것은 남양(南洋)의 향유고래잡이배와는 사뭇 다르다. 향유고래잡이배는 어쩌면 사 년에 걸친 항해 동안 화물칸을 기름으로 가득 채우고 나서도, 기름을 끓여 내는 일에 쉰 날도 채 쓰지 않을 것이며, 게다가 통에 담긴 그 상태의 기름은 거의 냄새가 나지 않는다."], ["The truth is, that living or dead, if but decently treated, whales as a species are by no means creatures of ill odor; nor can whalemen be recognised, as the people of the middle ages affected to detect a Jew in the company, by the nose.", "진실을 말하자면, 살아 있든 죽어 있든, 그저 점잖게만 다룬다면 고래라는 종족은 결코 고약한 냄새를 풍기는 짐승이 아니다. 또한 중세 사람들이 일행 가운데 유대인을 코로 가려냈다고 으스대던 식으로 고래잡이를 코로 알아낼 수 있는 것도 아니다."], ["Nor indeed can the whale possibly be otherwise than fragrant, when, as a general thing, he enjoys such high health; taking abundance of exercise; always out of doors; though, it is true, seldom in the open air.", "게다가 고래로 말하자면, 대체로 그토록 더할 나위 없이 건강을 누리고, 운동을 듬뿍 하며, 비록 탁 트인 공기를 쐬는 일은 드물지언정 늘 바깥에서 지내는 처지이니, 향기롭지 않으려야 않을 수가 없다."], ["I say, that the motion of a Sperm Whale’s flukes above water dispenses a perfume, as when a musk-scented lady rustles her dress in a warm parlor.", "단언컨대, 향유고래의 꼬리지느러미가 물 위에서 움직일 때면, 마치 사향 향을 풍기는 부인이 따뜻한 응접실에서 옷자락을 사각이며 스칠 때와 같은 향내가 흩어진다."], ["What then shall I liken the Sperm Whale to for fragrance, considering his magnitude?", "그렇다면 그 어마어마한 크기를 헤아릴 때, 나는 향유고래의 향기로움을 무엇에 견주어야 할까?"], ["Must it not be to that famous elephant, with jewelled tusks, and redolent with myrrh, which was led out of an Indian town to do honor to Alexander the Great?", "보석으로 장식한 엄니를 지니고 몰약 향을 물씬 풍기며, 알렉산드로스 대왕에게 경의를 표하고자 어느 인도(Indian) 마을에서 끌려 나왔던 저 이름난 코끼리에 견주어야 마땅하지 않겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER 93. The Castaway.", "# CHAPTER 93. The Castaway."]], [["It was but some few days after encountering the Frenchman, that a most significant event befell the most insignificant of the Pequod’s crew; an event most lamentable; and which ended in providing the sometimes madly merry and predestinated craft with a living and ever accompanying prophecy of whatever shattered sequel might prove her own.", "프랑스 배와 마주친 지 며칠 지나지 않아, 피쿼드호 선원 가운데 가장 보잘것없는 자에게 가장 의미심장한 사건이 닥쳤다. 더없이 통탄할 만한 사건이었다. 그리고 그 일은, 때로 미친 듯 흥겹고 또 운명 지어진 이 배에, 그녀 자신의 산산조각 난 결말이 무엇이 되든 그것을 살아 숨 쉬며 늘 따라다니는 예언으로 안겨 주는 것으로 끝맺었다."]], [["Now, in the whale ship, it is not every one that goes in the boats.", "자, 포경선에서는 누구나 보트에 타는 것이 아니다."], ["Some few hands are reserved called ship-keepers, whose province it is to work the vessel while the boats are pursuing the whale.", "몇몇 일손은 따로 남겨 두어 배지기라 부르는데, 보트들이 고래를 쫓는 동안 본선을 부리는 것이 그들의 소임이다. 대체로 이 배지기들도 보트 선원들 못지않게 단단한 사내들이다."], ["As a general thing, these ship-keepers are as hardy fellows as the men comprising the boats’ crews.", "그러나 배 안에 유달리 호리호리하거나 굼뜨거나 겁 많은 인간이 있다면, 그자는 영락없이 배지기로 정해진다."], ["But if there happen to be an unduly slender, clumsy, or timorous wight in the ship, that wight is certain to be made a ship-keeper.", "피쿼드호에서도 사정은 그러하여, 별명이 피핀이고 줄여서 핍(Pip)이라 불리는 자그마한 흑인이 바로 그러했다."], ["It was so in the Pequod with the little negro Pippin by nick-name, Pip by abbreviation.", "가엾은 핍! 그대들은 전에도 그의 이야기를 들었으리라."], ["Poor Pip! ye have heard of him before; ye must remember his tambourine on that dramatic midnight, so gloomy-jolly.", "저 극적이었던 한밤중, 그토록 음울하면서도 흥겹던 때 그가 두드리던 탬버린을 그대들은 분명 기억할 것이다."]], [["In outer aspect, Pip and Dough-Boy made a match, like a black pony and a white one, of equal developments, though of dissimilar colour, driven in one eccentric span.", "겉모습만 보자면 핍과 도우보이는 검은 조랑말과 흰 조랑말이 짝을 이루듯 잘 어울렸으니, 빛깔은 서로 달라도 발육은 엇비슷하여, 한 멍에에 묶여 기묘한 한 쌍을 이룬 격이었다. 그러나 가련한 도우보이가 본래 머리가 둔하고 굼뜬 데 반해, 핍은 지나치게 여린 마음씨이긴 해도 밑바탕은 무척 영민하여, 그 종족 특유의 정답고 너그럽고 흥겨운 총기를 지니고 있었다."], ["But while hapless Dough-Boy was by nature dull and torpid in his intellects, Pip, though over tender-hearted, was at bottom very bright, with that pleasant, genial, jolly brightness peculiar to his tribe; a tribe, which ever enjoy all holidays and festivities with finer, freer relish than any other race.", "어떤 다른 인종보다도 늘 모든 휴일과 잔치를 더 곱고 더 자유로운 흥취로 즐기는 종족이었다. 흑인들에게라면 한 해의 달력은 365일 내내 독립기념일이요 새해 첫날이어야 마땅하리라."], ["For blacks, the year’s calendar should show naught but three hundred and sixty-five Fourth of Julys and New Year’s Days.", "이 작은 흑인이 빛났노라고 내가 쓴다 해서 그리 웃지는 말라. 검음에도 그 나름의 광채가 있으니, 보라, 임금의 보물함 벽에 장식된 저 윤기 흐르는 흑단을."], ["Nor smile so, while I write that this little black was brilliant, for even blackness has its brilliancy; behold yon lustrous ebony, panelled in king’s cabinets.", "그러나 핍은 삶을 사랑했고, 삶이 누리는 온갖 평온한 안락을 사랑했다. 그리하여 그가 어찌 된 영문인지 알 수 없게 휘말려 든 저 공포에 떨게 하는 일은, 그의 총기를 더없이 슬프게 흐려 놓고 말았다."], ["But Pip loved life, and all life’s peaceable securities; so that the panic-striking business in which he had somehow unaccountably become entrapped, had most sadly blurred his brightness; though, as ere long will be seen, what was thus temporarily subdued in him, in the end was destined to be luridly illumined by strange wild fires, that fictitiously showed him off to ten times the natural lustre with which in his native Tolland County in Connecticut, he had once enlivened many a fiddler’s frolic on the green; and at melodious even-tide, with his gay ha-ha!", "그러나 머지않아 드러나겠지만, 그렇게 그의 안에서 잠시 짓눌렸던 것은, 마침내 기이하고 거친 불길로 섬뜩하게 환히 밝혀질 운명이었으니, 그 불길은 그를 본래의 광채보다 열 배는 더 밝게 거짓으로 드러내 보였다. 일찍이 그가 고향인 코네티컷의 톨런드 카운티에서 풀밭 위 깡깡이쟁이의 흥겨운 놀이판을 여러 번 돋우어 주던 그 빛, 그리고 가락 흐르는 저물녘이면 그 흥겨운 하하 웃음으로 둥근 지평선을 별이 울리는 하나의 탬버린으로 바꾸어 놓던 그 빛 말이다."], ["had turned the round horizon into one star-belled tambourine. So, though in the clear air of day, suspended against a blue-veined neck, the pure-watered diamond drop will healthful glow; yet, when the cunning jeweller would show you the diamond in its most impressive lustre, he lays it against a gloomy ground, and then lights it up, not by the sun, but by some unnatural gases.", "그러니, 비록 환한 대낮의 맑은 공기 속에서 푸른 핏줄 도드라진 목에 걸린 채 맑은 물빛 다이아몬드 한 방울이 건강하게 빛난다 해도, 교활한 보석상이 그대에게 그 다이아몬드의 가장 인상적인 광채를 보여 주려 할 때면, 그는 그것을 음울한 바탕 위에 놓고서, 햇빛이 아니라 어떤 부자연스러운 가스로 불을 밝힌다."], ["Then come out those fiery effulgences, infernally superb; then the evil-blazing diamond, once the divinest symbol of the crystal skies, looks like some crown-jewel stolen from the King of Hell.", "그러면 저 불꽃 같은 광휘가, 지옥처럼 장려하게 솟아오른다. 그러면 사악하게 타오르는 그 다이아몬드는, 한때 수정 같은 하늘의 더없이 신성한 상징이었던 그것은, 지옥의 왕에게서 훔쳐 낸 어떤 왕관의 보석처럼 보이는 것이다."], ["But let us to the story.", "그러나 이제 이야기로 돌아가자."]], [["It came to pass, that in the ambergris affair Stubb’s after-oarsman chanced so to sprain his hand, as for a time to become quite maimed; and, temporarily, Pip was put into his place.", "일이 이렇게 되었다. 용연향 사건 때 스터브의 뒤쪽 노잡이가 그만 손을 삐어, 한동안 영 못 쓰게 되어 버렸다. 그래서 임시로 핍이 그 자리에 들어가게 되었다."]], [["The first time Stubb lowered with him, Pip evinced much nervousness; but happily, for that time, escaped close contact with the whale; and therefore came off not altogether discreditably; though Stubb observing him, took care, afterwards, to exhort him to cherish his courageousness to the utmost, for he might often find it needful.", "스터브가 핍을 데리고 처음 보트를 내렸을 때, 핍은 무척이나 안절부절못했다. 그러나 다행히 그때는 고래와 바싹 맞붙는 일을 면했고, 따라서 아주 망신스럽지는 않게 넘어갔다. 다만 그를 지켜보던 스터브는, 나중에 그에게 용기를 한껏 소중히 길러 두라고 일러두었다. 그 용기가 곧잘 필요해질 수도 있는 까닭이었다."]], [["Now upon the second lowering, the boat paddled upon the whale; and as the fish received the darted iron, it gave its customary rap, which happened, in this instance, to be right under poor Pip’s seat.", "자, 두 번째로 보트를 내렸을 때, 보트는 노를 저어 고래에게 다가갔다. 그리고 그 물고기가 던져진 작살을 맞자, 여느 때처럼 한 차례 후려쳤는데, 하필 이번에는 그것이 가엾은 핍의 자리 바로 밑이었다."], ["The involuntary consternation of the moment caused him to leap, paddle in hand, out of the boat; and in such a way, that part of the slack whale line coming against his chest, he breasted it overboard with him, so as to become entangled in it, when at last plumping into the water.", "그 순간 저도 모르게 치민 경악에 핍은 노를 손에 든 채 보트 밖으로 펄쩍 뛰어올랐고, 그 바람에 느슨하던 고래 밧줄 한 자락이 그의 가슴에 걸리는 통에, 그것을 가슴으로 밀어내며 함께 뱃전 너머로 떨어졌으니, 마침내 물속으로 풍덩 처박힐 무렵에는 그것에 칭칭 감기고 말았다."], ["That instant the stricken whale started on a fierce run, the line swiftly straightened; and presto!", "바로 그 찰나 작살 맞은 고래가 사납게 내달리기 시작하면서 밧줄은 순식간에 팽팽해졌고, 보라!"], ["poor Pip came all foaming up to the chocks of the boat, remorselessly dragged there by the line, which had taken several turns around his chest and neck.", "가엾은 핍은 온통 거품을 일으키며 보트의 밧줄걸이까지 끌려 올라왔으니, 그의 가슴과 목을 몇 바퀴 휘감은 그 밧줄에 무자비하게 거기까지 질질 끌려온 것이었다."]], [["Tashtego stood in the bows.", "태시테고가 뱃머리에 서 있었다."], ["He was full of the fire of the hunt.", "그는 사냥의 열기로 가득 차 있었다."], ["He hated Pip for a poltroon.", "그는 핍을 겁쟁이라고 미워했다."], ["Snatching the boat-knife from its sheath, he suspended its sharp edge over the line, and turning towards Stubb, exclaimed interrogatively, “Cut?”", "칼집에서 보트 칼을 홱 뽑아 그 날카로운 날을 밧줄 위에 갖다 댄 채, 스터브 쪽으로 돌아서서 묻듯이 외쳤다. \"끊을까요?\""], ["Meantime Pip’s blue, choked face plainly looked, Do, for God’s sake!", "그러는 사이 핍의 새파랗게 질려 숨 막힌 얼굴은 분명히 이렇게 말하고 있었다. 제발, 어서요!"], ["All passed in a flash.", "모든 것이 순식간에 지나갔다."], ["In less than half a minute, this entire thing happened.", "반 분도 채 안 되는 사이에 이 일 전부가 벌어진 것이었다."]], [["“Damn him, cut!”", "\"빌어먹을 놈, 끊어!\""], ["roared Stubb; and so the whale was lost and Pip was saved.", "스터브가 고함쳤다. 그리하여 고래는 놓치고, 핍은 살아났다."]], [["So soon as he recovered himself, the poor little negro was assailed by yells and execrations from the crew.", "가까스로 정신을 차리자마자, 가엾고 자그마한 흑인은 선원들의 고함과 저주에 시달렸다."], ["Tranquilly permitting these irregular cursings to evaporate, Stubb then in a plain, business-like, but still half humorous manner, cursed Pip officially; and that done, unofficially gave him much wholesome advice.", "스터브는 이 어수선한 욕설들이 잦아들도록 잠자코 내버려 두었다가, 이윽고 담담하고 사무적인, 그러나 여전히 반은 익살스러운 투로 핍을 공식적으로 꾸짖었다. 그러고 나서는 비공식적으로 그에게 유익한 충고를 잔뜩 늘어놓았다."], ["The substance was, Never jump from a boat, Pip, except—but all the rest was indefinite, as the soundest advice ever is.", "그 알맹이는 이러했다. 핍, 절대 보트에서 뛰어내리지 마라, 다만 — 그러나 나머지는 죄다 두루뭉술했으니, 가장 미더운 충고란 본래 다 그러한 법이다."], ["Now, in general, Stick to the boat, is your true motto in whaling; but cases will sometimes happen when Leap from the boat, is still better.", "자, 대체로 보트에 붙어 있어라가 고래잡이에서 그대의 참된 좌우명이다. 그러나 보트에서 뛰어내려라가 오히려 더 나은 경우도 더러 생기는 법이다."], ["Moreover, as if perceiving at last that if he should give undiluted conscientious advice to Pip, he would be leaving him too wide a margin to jump in for the future; Stubb suddenly dropped all advice, and concluded with a peremptory command, “Stick to the boat, Pip, or by the Lord, I won’t pick you up if you jump; mind that.", "게다가, 만약 핍에게 한 점 에누리 없이 양심껏 충고를 해 준다면, 앞으로 뛰어내릴 여지를 너무 넓게 남겨 주는 꼴이 되리라는 것을 마침내 깨달은 듯, 스터브는 별안간 모든 충고를 거두고, 단호한 명령으로 끝맺었다. \"보트에 붙어 있어라, 핍."], ["We can’t afford to lose whales by the likes of you; a whale would sell for thirty times what you would, Pip, in Alabama.", "안 그러면, 맹세코, 네가 뛰어내려도 난 너를 건져 주지 않을 테니, 똑똑히 새겨 둬라. 우린 너 같은 놈 때문에 고래를 놓칠 여유가 없어."], ["Bear that in mind, and don’t jump any more.”", "앨라배마에서라면 고래 한 마리가 너보다 서른 배는 값나갈 게다, 핍. 그걸 명심하고, 다시는 뛰어내리지 마라.\""], ["Hereby perhaps Stubb indirectly hinted, that though man loved his fellow, yet man is a money-making animal, which propensity too often interferes with his benevolence.", "이로써 스터브는 어쩌면 넌지시 일러 준 셈이었다. 사람이 제 이웃을 사랑한다 해도, 사람은 돈벌이하는 동물이며, 그 천성이 너무도 자주 그의 자비심을 방해한다는 것을."]], [["But we are all in the hands of the Gods; and Pip jumped again.", "그러나 우리는 모두 신들의 손안에 있다. 그리고 핍은 또다시 뛰어내렸다."], ["It was under very similar circumstances to the first performance; but this time he did not breast out the line; and hence, when the whale started to run, Pip was left behind on the sea, like a hurried traveller’s trunk.", "처음과 아주 비슷한 상황이었으나, 이번에는 밧줄을 가슴으로 밀어내지 못했다. 그래서 고래가 내달리기 시작했을 때, 핍은 서둘러 길 떠난 나그네가 두고 간 가방처럼 바다에 홀로 남겨졌다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["Stubb was but too true to his word.", "스터브는 제 말에 너무도 충실했다."], ["It was a beautiful, bounteous, blue day; the spangled sea calm and cool, and flatly stretching away, all round, to the horizon, like gold-beater’s skin hammered out to the extremest.", "아름답고 풍요로운, 푸른 날이었다. 반짝이는 바다는 잔잔하고 서늘했으며, 사방으로 지평선까지 판판하게 뻗어, 마치 금박장이가 끝까지 두드려 편 가죽 같았다."], ["Bobbing up and down in that sea, Pip’s ebon head showed like a head of cloves.", "그 바다 위에서 오르내리며 까딱이는 핍의 흑단 같은 머리는 정향 한 알처럼 보였다."], ["No boat-knife was lifted when he fell so rapidly astern.", "그가 그토록 빠르게 고물 뒤로 멀어져 갈 때, 보트 칼은 하나도 들어 올려지지 않았다."], ["Stubb’s inexorable back was turned upon him; and the whale was winged.", "스터브의 가차 없는 등이 그에게로 돌려져 있었다. 그리고 고래는 날개라도 단 듯 내달렸다."], ["In three minutes, a whole mile of shoreless ocean was between Pip and Stubb.", "삼 분 만에, 핍과 스터브 사이에는 기슭 없는 바다가 꼬박 일 마일이나 가로놓였다."], ["Out from the centre of the sea, poor Pip turned his crisp, curling, black head to the sun, another lonely castaway, though the loftiest and the brightest.", "바다 한복판에서, 가엾은 핍은 곱슬곱슬 오그라든 검은 머리를 태양 쪽으로 돌렸으니, 또 하나의 외로운 표류자였다, 비록 가장 드높고 가장 빛나는 표류자이긴 했어도."]], [["Now, in calm weather, to swim in the open ocean is as easy to the practised swimmer as to ride in a spring-carriage ashore.", "자, 잔잔한 날씨라면, 너른 바다에서 헤엄치는 일은 숙련된 헤엄꾼에게 뭍에서 용수철 마차를 타는 것만큼이나 수월하다."], ["But the awful lonesomeness is intolerable.", "그러나 그 무시무시한 외로움은 견딜 수 없는 것이다."], ["The intense concentration of self in the middle of such a heartless immensity, my God!", "그토록 무정한 광막함 한가운데서 자아가 그토록 강렬하게 응집되는 것이라니, 오 하느님! 누가 그것을 말로 다 할 수 있으랴?"], ["who can tell it? Mark, how when sailors in a dead calm bathe in the open sea—mark how closely they hug their ship and only coast along her sides.", "보라, 죽은 듯 잔잔한 바다에서 선원들이 너른 바다에 몸을 담글 때, 그들이 얼마나 제 배에 바싹 매달려 그 뱃전을 따라서만 떠도는지를."]], [["But had Stubb really abandoned the poor little negro to his fate? No; he did not mean to, at least. Because there were two boats in his wake, and he supposed, no doubt, that they would of course come up to Pip very quickly, and pick him up; though, indeed, such considerations towards oarsmen jeopardized through their own timidity, is not always manifested by the hunters in all similar instances; and such instances not unfrequently occur; almost invariably in the fishery, a coward, so called, is marked with the same ruthless detestation peculiar to military navies and armies.", "그렇다면 스터브는 정말로 그 가엾고 자그마한 흑인을 운명에 내맡겨 버린 것일까? 아니다. 적어도 그럴 작정은 아니었다. 그의 뒤로 보트 두 척이 따라오고 있었으니, 그는 분명 그 보트들이 당연히 곧 핍에게 다가가 그를 건져 올리리라 여겼던 것이다. 하기야 제 겁약함 탓에 위험에 빠진 노잡이들에 대한 이런 배려가, 비슷한 모든 경우에 사냥꾼들에게서 늘 드러나는 것은 아니다. 그리고 그런 경우는 드물지 않게 벌어진다. 고래잡이에서는 거의 예외 없이, 이른바 겁쟁이라는 자는, 군 해군과 육군 특유의 그 무자비한 혐오로 똑같이 낙인찍히는 법이다."]], [["But it so happened, that those boats, without seeing Pip, suddenly spying whales close to them on one side, turned, and gave chase; and Stubb’s boat was now so far away, and he and all his crew so intent upon his fish, that Pip’s ringed horizon began to expand around him miserably.", "그런데 일이 이렇게 되고 말았다. 그 보트들은 핍을 보지 못한 채, 별안간 한쪽 가까이에서 고래들을 발견하고는 방향을 틀어 추격에 나섰다."], ["By the merest chance the ship itself at last rescued him; but from that hour the little negro went about the deck an idiot; such, at least, they said he was.", "게다가 스터브의 보트는 이제 너무 멀리 떨어져 있었고, 그와 그의 선원들 모두가 제 고래에 정신이 팔려 있었던 터라, 핍을 둘러싼 둥근 지평선은 그의 주위로 비참하게 넓어져 가기 시작했다. 순전히 우연으로, 마침내 본선이 그를 구해 냈다."], ["The sea had jeeringly kept his finite body up, but drowned the infinite of his soul.", "그러나 그 순간부터 그 자그마한 흑인은 백치가 되어 갑판을 돌아다녔다. 적어도 사람들은 그가 그렇다고들 했다."], ["Not drowned entirely, though.", "바다는 비웃듯이 그의 유한한 몸뚱이는 떠받쳐 주었으나, 그의 영혼의 무한함은 빠뜨려 버렸다."], ["Rather carried down alive to wondrous depths, where strange shapes of the unwarped primal world glided to and fro before his passive eyes; and the miser-merman, Wisdom, revealed his hoarded heaps; and among the joyous, heartless, ever-juvenile eternities, Pip saw the multitudinous, God-omnipresent, coral insects, that out of the firmament of waters heaved the colossal orbs.", "그러나 아주 빠뜨려 죽인 것은 아니었다. 차라리 산 채로 경이로운 심연까지 끌어내려졌으니, 거기서는 뒤틀리지 않은 태곳적 세계의 기이한 형상들이 무심한 그의 눈앞을 이리저리 미끄러져 다녔고, 인색한 인어인 지혜가 제 쌓아 둔 보물 더미를 드러내 보였으며, 그 기쁘고 무정하며 영원토록 앳된 영원들 사이에서, 핍은 무수히 많은, 어디에나 하느님처럼 깃든 산호 벌레들을 보았으니, 그것들은 물의 창공으로부터 저 거대한 천체들을 들어 올리고 있었다."], ["He saw God’s foot upon the treadle of the loom, and spoke it; and therefore his shipmates called him mad.", "그는 베틀의 발판을 밟는 하느님의 발을 보았고, 그것을 말했다. 그래서 그의 동료 선원들은 그를 미쳤다고 불렀다."], ["So man’s insanity is heaven’s sense; and wandering from all mortal reason, man comes at last to that celestial thought, which, to reason, is absurd and frantic; and weal or woe, feels then uncompromised, indifferent as his God.", "그러니 사람의 광기란 하늘의 분별이요, 인간은 온갖 필멸의 이치로부터 벗어나 헤매다가 마침내 저 천상의 사념에 다다르는 법인데, 그것은 이치로 보자면 터무니없고 미쳐 날뛰는 것이지만, 그리하여 사람은 화복(禍福)이 어떠하든, 제 하느님처럼 거리낌 없고 초연해지는 것이다."]], [["For the rest, blame not Stubb too hardly.", "그 밖의 일은, 스터브를 너무 모질게 탓하지는 말라."], ["The thing is common in that fishery; and in the sequel of the narrative, it will then be seen what like abandonment befell myself.", "그 고래잡이판에서 흔히 있는 일이다. 그리고 이 이야기가 이어지는 가운데, 그때 가서 나 자신에게도 그와 같은 버림받음이 어떻게 닥쳤는지 드러나게 될 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 94. A Squeeze of the Hand.", "# CHAPTER 94. A Squeeze of the Hand."]], [["That whale of Stubb’s, so dearly purchased, was duly brought to the Pequod’s side, where all those cutting and hoisting operations previously detailed, were regularly gone through, even to the baling of the Heidelburgh Tun, or Case.", "스터브가 그토록 비싸게 사들인 그 고래는 마침내 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 뱃전으로 끌어올려졌고, 앞서 자세히 설명했던 절단과 권양 작업이 차례차례 어김없이 이루어졌으며, 마침내 하이델베르크 통, 즉 케이스(case)의 기름을 퍼내는 일에까지 이르렀다."]], [["While some were occupied with this latter duty, others were employed in dragging away the larger tubs, so soon as filled with the sperm; and when the proper time arrived, this same sperm was carefully manipulated ere going to the try-works, of which anon.", "* 케이스: 향유고래 머리 속의 기름 저장 공간. 몇몇은 이 마지막 작업에 매달려 있었고, 다른 이들은 향유고래 기름으로 가득 찬 큰 통들을 차례차례 끌어내는 일을 맡았다. 그리고 적당한 때가 이르면 바로 이 기름은 정유 작업장(try-works)으로 보내기에 앞서 세심하게 다루어졌는데, 그 일에 관해서는 곧 말하겠다."]], [["It had cooled and crystallized to such a degree, that when, with several others, I sat down before a large Constantine’s bath of it, I found it strangely concreted into lumps, here and there rolling about in the liquid part.", "기름은 어느 정도 식어서 결정처럼 굳어 있었기에, 내가 다른 몇 사람과 함께 콘스탄티누스 욕조만 한 큰 통 앞에 앉았을 때, 그것은 묘하게 덩어리져 엉겨 있었고, 여기저기 액체 부분 속을 굴러다니고 있었다."], ["It was our business to squeeze these lumps back into fluid.", "이 덩어리들을 다시 주물러 액체로 풀어내는 것이 우리의 일이었다."], ["A sweet and unctuous duty!", "달콤하고 기름진 일이었다!"], ["No wonder that in old times this sperm was such a favourite cosmetic.", "옛 시절에 이 기름이 그토록 사랑받는 화장품이었던 것도 당연하다."], ["Such a clearer! such a sweetener!", "이 얼마나 훌륭한 정화제인가!"], ["such a softener!", "이 얼마나 훌륭한 향료인가!"], ["such a delicious molifier!", "이 얼마나 훌륭한 연화제인가!"], ["After having my hands in it for only a few minutes, my fingers felt like eels, and began, as it were, to serpentine and spiralise.", "이 얼마나 황홀한 유연제인가! 단 몇 분간 손을 담그고 있었을 뿐인데, 내 손가락은 뱀장어처럼 느껴졌고, 말하자면 구불구불 뱀처럼 휘감기며 나선을 그리기 시작했다."]], [["As I sat there at my ease, cross-legged on the deck; after the bitter exertion at the windlass; under a blue tranquil sky; the ship under indolent sail, and gliding so serenely along; as I bathed my hands among those soft, gentle globules of infiltrated tissues, woven almost within the hour; as they richly broke to my fingers, and discharged all their opulence, like fully ripe grapes their wine; as I snuffed up that uncontaminated aroma,—literally and truly, like the smell of spring violets; I declare to you, that for the time I lived as in a musky meadow; I forgot all about our horrible oath; in that inexpressible sperm, I washed my hands and my heart of it; I almost began to credit the old Paracelsan superstition that sperm is of rare virtue in allaying the heat of anger; while bathing in that bath, I felt divinely free from all ill-will, or petulance, or malice, of any sort whatsoever.", "힘겨운 권양기 노동을 끝낸 뒤, 푸르고 고요한 하늘 아래, 배는 나른한 돛을 펼친 채 더없이 평온하게 미끄러져 나아가고, 나는 갑판 위에 책상다리를 하고 편안히 앉아 있었다. 그렇게 거의 한 시간 만에 짜인, 스며든 조직의 부드럽고 온화한 알갱이들 속에 손을 담그고 있노라니, 그것들이 내 손가락에 닿아 풍성하게 으깨지면서, 완전히 익은 포도가 제 포도주를 쏟아내듯 그 모든 풍요로움을 내어놓았다. 그리고 그 더럽혀지지 않은 향기를 들이마시니 — 글자 그대로, 참으로 봄 제비꽃 냄새 같았다 — 그대들에게 분명히 말하건대, 그동안 나는 사향 풀밭에 사는 듯한 기분이었다. 나는 우리의 그 끔찍한 맹세를 까맣게 잊어버렸다. 그 말로 다 할 수 없는 기름 속에서, 나는 그 맹세를 손과 마음에서 씻어냈다. 향유고래 기름이 분노의 열기를 가라앉히는 데 진귀한 효험이 있다는 옛 파라켈수스의 미신을 나는 거의 믿을 뻔했다. 그 욕조에 몸을 담그고 있는 동안, 나는 그 어떤 종류의 악의나 짜증이나 원한으로부터도 신성하리만치 자유로워진 기분이었다."]], [["Squeeze!", "짜라!"], ["squeeze!", "짜라! 짜라!"], ["squeeze!", "아침 내내, 나는 나 자신마저 거의 그 속에 녹아들 때까지 그 기름을 주물러 짰다."], ["all the morning long; I squeezed that sperm till I myself almost melted into it; I squeezed that sperm till a strange sort of insanity came over me; and I found myself unwittingly squeezing my co-laborers’ hands in it, mistaking their hands for the gentle globules.", "나는 기묘한 광기가 나를 사로잡을 때까지 그 기름을 주물러 짰다. 그러다 나는 나도 모르게 그 기름 속에서 동료 일꾼들의 손을 짜고 있었으니, 그들의 손을 그 부드러운 알갱이들로 착각했던 것이다."], ["Such an abounding, affectionate, friendly, loving feeling did this avocation beget; that at last I was continually squeezing their hands, and looking up into their eyes sentimentally; as much as to say,—Oh!", "이 일은 그토록 넘쳐흐르는, 다정하고 우애롭고 사랑스러운 감정을 자아냈기에, 마침내 나는 끊임없이 그들의 손을 쥐어짜며 감상에 젖은 눈으로 그들의 눈을 올려다보았다. 마치 이렇게 말하려는 듯이 — 오!"], ["my dear fellow beings, why should we longer cherish any social acerbities, or know the slightest ill-humor or envy!", "사랑하는 동료 인간들이여, 어찌하여 우리는 더 이상 사회적인 앙심을 품거나, 조금이라도 언짢음이나 시기심을 알아야 한단 말인가! 자, 다 함께 손을 맞잡고 짜자."], ["Come; let us squeeze hands all round; nay, let us all squeeze ourselves into each other; let us squeeze ourselves universally into the very milk and sperm of kindness.", "아니, 우리 모두 서로의 속으로 녹아들도록 짜자. 우리 모두 친절이라는 바로 그 젖과 정수 속으로 두루 녹아들도록 짜자."]], [["Would that I could keep squeezing that sperm for ever!", "영원토록 그 기름을 짤 수 있다면 얼마나 좋으랴!"], ["For now, since by many prolonged, repeated experiences, I have perceived that in all cases man must eventually lower, or at least shift, his conceit of attainable felicity; not placing it anywhere in the intellect or the fancy; but in the wife, the heart, the bed, the table, the saddle, the fireside, the country; now that I have perceived all this, I am ready to squeeze case eternally.", "이제 나는, 여러 차례 거듭된 오랜 경험을 통해, 모든 경우에 인간은 결국 자신이 이룰 수 있는 행복에 대한 자만을 낮추거나, 적어도 그 방향을 바꾸어야 함을 깨달았으니 — 그 행복을 지성이나 공상 속 어디엔가 두지 말고, 아내에게, 마음에, 침대에, 식탁에, 안장에, 난롯가에, 고향 땅에 두어야 한다는 것을. 이제 이 모든 것을 깨달았기에, 나는 영원히 케이스의 기름을 짤 준비가 되어 있다."], ["In thoughts of the visions of the night, I saw long rows of angels in paradise, each with his hands in a jar of spermaceti.", "밤의 환영을 떠올리는 가운데, 나는 천국에서 줄지어 늘어선 천사들을 보았으니, 저마다 향유고래 기름이 든 항아리 속에 손을 담그고 있었다."]], [["Now, while discoursing of sperm, it behooves to speak of other things akin to it, in the business of preparing the sperm whale for the try-works.", "이제 향유고래 기름을 이야기한 김에, 정유 작업장에 보낼 향유고래를 손질하는 일에 있어 그것과 비슷한 다른 것들에 관해서도 말하는 것이 마땅하다."]], [["First comes white-horse, so called, which is obtained from the tapering part of the fish, and also from the thicker portions of his flukes.", "먼저 화이트호스(white-horse)라 불리는 것이 나온다. 이것은 고래의 가늘어지는 부분과 또한 꼬리지느러미의 두툼한 부분에서 얻어진다."], ["It is tough with congealed tendons—a wad of muscle—but still contains some oil.", "엉겨 붙은 힘줄로 질기며 — 근육 덩어리인데 — 그래도 약간의 기름을 머금고 있다."], ["After being severed from the whale, the white-horse is first cut into portable oblongs ere going to the mincer.", "고래에서 떼어낸 뒤, 화이트호스는 다지는 기계로 보내기에 앞서 먼저 옮기기 좋은 길쭉한 토막으로 잘린다."], ["They look much like blocks of Berkshire marble.", "그 모양은 버크셔 대리석 덩어리와 무척 닮았다."]], [["Plum-pudding is the term bestowed upon certain fragmentary parts of the whale’s flesh, here and there adhering to the blanket of blubber, and often participating to a considerable degree in its unctuousness.", "플럼푸딩(plum-pudding)은 고래 살의 어떤 자투리 부분에 붙여진 이름인데, 이것은 비계 외투에 여기저기 들러붙어 있으며, 흔히 그 기름기에 상당한 정도로 함께 젖어 있다."], ["It is a most refreshing, convivial, beautiful object to behold.", "보기에 더없이 상쾌하고, 흥겹고, 아름다운 물건이다."], ["As its name imports, it is of an exceedingly rich, mottled tint, with a bestreaked snowy and golden ground, dotted with spots of the deepest crimson and purple.", "그 이름이 말해 주듯, 지극히 풍요로운 얼룩무늬 빛깔을 띠는데, 눈처럼 희고 금빛이 도는 바탕에 줄무늬가 지고, 가장 짙은 진홍과 자줏빛 점들이 점점이 박혀 있다."], ["It is plums of rubies, in pictures of citron.", "마치 레몬빛 그림 속에 박힌 루비 같은 자두들이다."], ["Spite of reason, it is hard to keep yourself from eating it.", "이성을 거스르고서라도, 그것을 먹지 않고 참기란 어렵다."], ["I confess, that once I stole behind the foremast to try it.", "고백하건대, 한번은 나도 앞돛대 뒤로 몰래 숨어들어 그것을 맛본 적이 있다."], ["It tasted something as I should conceive a royal cutlet from the thigh of Louis le Gros might have tasted, supposing him to have been killed the first day after the venison season, and that particular venison season contemporary with an unusually fine vintage of the vineyards of Champagne.", "그 맛은, 뚱보왕 루이의 넓적다리에서 떼어낸 왕의 고기 한 점이 이러했으리라고 내가 짐작할 만한 그런 맛이었다 — 그가 사슴 사냥철이 시작된 첫날에 잡혔고, 그 사슴철이 마침 샹파뉴 포도밭의 유달리 훌륭한 포도주 해와 겹쳤다고 가정한다면 말이다."]], [["There is another substance, and a very singular one, which turns up in the course of this business, but which I feel it to be very puzzling adequately to describe.", "이 작업을 하다 보면 마주치게 되는 또 하나의 물질이 있는데, 무척 기이한 것이라 제대로 묘사하기가 몹시 까다롭게 여겨진다. 그것은 슬롭골리언(slobgollion)이라 불린다."], ["It is called slobgollion; an appellation original with the whalemen, and even so is the nature of the substance.", "고래잡이들이 만들어낸 독창적인 명칭이며, 그 물질의 성질 또한 그 이름만큼이나 별나다."], ["It is an ineffably oozy, stringy affair, most frequently found in the tubs of sperm, after a prolonged squeezing, and subsequent decanting.", "그것은 이루 말할 수 없이 끈적끈적하고 실처럼 늘어지는 것으로, 오래 짜낸 뒤 다시 윗물을 따라낸 향유고래 기름 통 속에서 가장 흔히 발견된다."], ["I hold it to be the wondrously thin, ruptured membranes of the case, coalescing.", "나는 그것이 케이스의 놀랍도록 얇고 찢어진 막들이 한데 엉겨 붙은 것이라고 생각한다."]], [["Gurry, so called, is a term properly belonging to right whalemen, but sometimes incidentally used by the sperm fishermen.", "거리(gurry)라 불리는 것은 본디 참고래잡이들에게 속한 말이지만, 더러는 향유고래잡이들도 부수적으로 쓰곤 한다."], ["It designates the dark, glutinous substance which is scraped off the back of the Greenland or right whale, and much of which covers the decks of those inferior souls who hunt that ignoble Leviathan.", "이것은 그린란드고래, 즉 참고래의 등에서 긁어낸 거무스름하고 끈적이는 물질을 가리키며, 그 천한 리바이어던(Leviathan)을 사냥하는 저열한 영혼들의 갑판은 흔히 이것으로 뒤덮인다."]], [["Nippers.", "니퍼(nipper)."], ["Strictly this word is not indigenous to the whale’s vocabulary.", "엄밀히 말하면 이 단어는 본디 고래잡이의 어휘에 속하지 않는다."], ["But as applied by whalemen, it becomes so.", "그러나 고래잡이들이 쓰면서 그렇게 되었다."], ["A whaleman’s nipper is a short firm strip of tendinous stuff cut from the tapering part of Leviathan’s tail: it averages an inch in thickness, and for the rest, is about the size of the iron part of a hoe.", "고래잡이의 니퍼란 리바이어던의 꼬리에서 가늘어지는 부분을 잘라낸, 짧고 단단한 힘줄 조각이다. 두께는 평균 1인치(약 2.5센티미터)쯤 되고, 나머지 크기는 괭이의 쇠붙이 부분만 하다."], ["Edgewise moved along the oily deck, it operates like a leathern squilgee; and by nameless blandishments, as of magic, allures along with it all impurities.", "모서리를 세워 기름투성이 갑판 위를 밀고 나아가면, 그것은 마치 가죽 물기제거기처럼 작동하며, 마법이라도 부리듯 이름 모를 갖은 아양을 떨어 온갖 불순물을 함께 쓸어 모은다."]], [["But to learn all about these recondite matters, your best way is at once to descend into the blubber-room, and have a long talk with its inmates.", "그러나 이 오묘한 사항들을 죄다 알고 싶다면, 가장 좋은 방법은 곧장 비계 저장실로 내려가 그곳에 머무는 자들과 오래도록 이야기를 나누는 것이다."], ["This place has previously been mentioned as the receptacle for the blanket-pieces, when stript and hoisted from the whale.", "이곳은 앞서, 고래에서 벗겨내어 끌어올린 외투 조각들을 받아두는 곳으로 언급된 바 있다."], ["When the proper time arrives for cutting up its contents, this apartment is a scene of terror to all tyros, especially by night.", "그 안에 든 것을 잘라낼 적당한 때가 이르면, 이 방은 모든 풋내기에게, 특히 밤이면 공포의 현장이 된다."], ["On one side, lit by a dull lantern, a space has been left clear for the workmen.", "한쪽 켠에는 희미한 등불이 비추는 가운데, 일꾼들을 위해 빈자리가 마련되어 있다."], ["They generally go in pairs,—a pike-and-gaffman and a spade-man.", "그들은 대개 둘씩 짝을 지어 들어가는데 — 갈고리창잡이 한 명과 삽잡이 한 명이다."], ["The whaling-pike is similar to a frigate’s boarding-weapon of the same name.", "포경용 창은 같은 이름을 지닌 군함의 백병전 무기와 비슷하다."], ["The gaff is something like a boat-hook.", "갈고리는 보트 갈고리와 얼마간 닮았다."], ["With his gaff, the gaffman hooks on to a sheet of blubber, and strives to hold it from slipping, as the ship pitches and lurches about.", "갈고리잡이는 자신의 갈고리로 비계 한 장을 걸어, 배가 흔들리고 기우뚱거릴 때 그것이 미끄러지지 않도록 붙들려 애쓴다."], ["Meanwhile, the spade-man stands on the sheet itself, perpendicularly chopping it into the portable horse-pieces.", "그러는 동안 삽잡이는 그 비계 장 위에 올라서서, 수직으로 내리찍어 그것을 옮기기 좋은 말토막으로 잘라낸다."], ["This spade is sharp as hone can make it; the spademan’s feet are shoeless; the thing he stands on will sometimes irresistibly slide away from him, like a sledge.", "이 삽은 숫돌이 갈 수 있는 한 날카롭게 갈려 있고, 삽잡이의 발은 맨발이며, 그가 딛고 선 물건은 이따금 썰매처럼 그의 발밑에서 걷잡을 수 없이 미끄러져 달아난다."], ["If he cuts off one of his own toes, or one of his assistants’, would you be very much astonished?", "그가 제 발가락 하나를, 혹은 조수의 발가락 하나를 잘라낸들, 그대는 그리 크게 놀라겠는가?"], ["Toes are scarce among veteran blubber-room men.", "비계실의 노련한 일꾼들 사이에서 발가락이란 흔치 않은 물건이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 95. The Cassock.", "# CHAPTER 95. The Cassock."]], [["Had you stepped on board the Pequod at a certain juncture of this post-mortemizing of the whale; and had you strolled forward nigh the windlass, pretty sure am I that you would have scanned with no small curiosity a very strange, enigmatical object, which you would have seen there, lying along lengthwise in the lee scuppers.", "고래를 이렇게 부검하듯 처리하는 어느 한 시점에 그대가 피쿼드호(Pequod)에 발을 디뎠다고 하자. 그리고 양묘기 근처 앞쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나갔다면, 풍하 쪽 배수구를 따라 길게 누워 있는 매우 기이하고 수수께끼 같은 물건 하나를 적잖은 호기심으로 찬찬히 뜯어보았을 것이 거의 틀림없다."], ["Not the wondrous cistern in the whale’s huge head; not the prodigy of his unhinged lower jaw; not the miracle of his symmetrical tail; none of these would so surprise you, as half a glimpse of that unaccountable cone,—longer than a Kentuckian is tall, nigh a foot in diameter at the base, and jet-black as Yojo, the ebony idol of Queequeg.", "고래의 거대한 머릿속에 자리한 그 놀라운 저수통도, 빗장이 풀린 듯한 아래턱의 경이로움도, 좌우 대칭을 이룬 꼬리의 기적도 — 이 가운데 어느 것도, 도무지 설명할 길 없는 저 원뿔꼴 물체를 얼핏 반쯤 보는 것만큼 그대를 놀라게 하지는 못할 것이다. 그것은 켄터키 사람의 키보다 더 길고, 밑동의 지름은 거의 한 자에 이르며, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 흑단(黑檀) 우상인 요조처럼 새까맣다."], ["And an idol, indeed, it is; or, rather, in old times, its likeness was.", "그리고 그것은 실로 하나의 우상이다. 아니, 옛 시절에는 그것과 똑같이 생긴 것이 우상이었다."], ["Such an idol as that found in the secret groves of Queen Maachah in Judea; and for worshipping which, King Asa, her son, did depose her, and destroyed the idol, and burnt it for an abomination at the brook Kedron, as darkly set forth in the 15th chapter of the First Book of Kings.", "유대 땅 마아가 여왕의 은밀한 숲속에서 발견된 바로 그런 우상이었으니, 그 아들 아사 왕은 어머니가 그것을 섬긴 까닭에 그녀를 폐위하고, 그 우상을 부수어 혐오스러운 물건으로 삼아 기드론 시냇가에서 불태웠다. 이는 열왕기 상 15장에 어둡게 적혀 있는 그대로다."]], [["Look at the sailor, called the mincer, who now comes along, and assisted by two allies, heavily backs the grandissimus, as the mariners call it, and with bowed shoulders, staggers off with it as if he were a grenadier carrying a dead comrade from the field.", "이제 다가오는, 잘게 써는 자(미서)라 불리는 선원을 보라. 그는 두 명의 조수의 도움을 받아, 뱃사람들이 그란디시무스라 부르는 그것을 묵직하게 등에 짊어지고, 어깨를 잔뜩 구부린 채 비틀거리며 옮겨 간다."], ["Extending it upon the forecastle deck, he now proceeds cylindrically to remove its dark pelt, as an African hunter the pelt of a boa.", "마치 척탄병이 전장에서 죽은 전우를 메고 나오는 듯하다. 그는 그것을 앞갑판 위에 길게 펼쳐 놓고, 이제 아프리카의 사냥꾼이 보아뱀의 가죽을 벗기듯 원통꼴로 그 검은 껍질을 벗겨 낸다."], ["This done he turns the pelt inside out, like a pantaloon leg; gives it a good stretching, so as almost to double its diameter; and at last hangs it, well spread, in the rigging, to dry.", "이 일이 끝나면 그는 바지 가랑이를 뒤집듯 그 껍질을 안팎으로 뒤집고, 단단히 잡아당겨 거의 두 배의 지름이 되도록 늘인 다음, 마침내 잘 펴서 삭구에 매달아 말린다."], ["Ere long, it is taken down; when removing some three feet of it, towards the pointed extremity, and then cutting two slits for arm-holes at the other end, he lengthwise slips himself bodily into it.", "이윽고 그것을 끌어 내려, 뾰족한 끝 쪽에서 석 자쯤을 떼어 내고, 다른 쪽 끝에는 팔을 끼울 구멍 두 개를 째어 놓은 뒤, 길이 방향으로 제 몸을 통째로 그 안에 미끄러뜨려 넣는다."], ["The mincer now stands before you invested in the full canonicals of his calling.", "잘게 써는 자는 이제 제 소임에 걸맞은 온전한 제의(祭衣)를 갖춰 입고 그대 앞에 선다."], ["Immemorial to all his order, this investiture alone will adequately protect him, while employed in the peculiar functions of his office.", "그 일에 종사하는 모든 이에게 까마득한 옛날부터 전해 온 바, 그가 제 직무의 독특한 기능을 수행하는 동안 오직 이 제복만이 그를 알맞게 지켜 줄 수 있는 것이다."]], [["That office consists in mincing the horse-pieces of blubber for the pots; an operation which is conducted at a curious wooden horse, planted endwise against the bulwarks, and with a capacious tub beneath it, into which the minced pieces drop, fast as the sheets from a rapt orator’s desk.", "그 직무란 가마솥에 넣을 지방 덩어리, 곧 말덩이라 불리는 것을 잘게 써는 일이다. 이 작업은 뱃전에 끝을 맞대어 세워 놓은 기묘한 나무 받침대에서 이루어지는데, 그 아래에는 널찍한 통이 놓여 있어, 잘게 썰린 조각들이 황홀경에 빠진 웅변가의 책상에서 종이장이 떨어지듯 빠르게 그 안으로 떨어진다."], ["Arrayed in decent black; occupying a conspicuous pulpit; intent on bible leaves; what a candidate for an archbishopric, what a lad for a Pope were this mincer!*", "단정한 검은 옷을 차려입고, 사람들의 눈길이 쏠리는 설교단을 차지한 채, 성경 책장에 골몰하고 있으니 — 이 잘게 써는 자야말로 얼마나 훌륭한 대주교 후보감이며, 얼마나 어울리는 교황감인가!"]], [["*Bible leaves!", "* 성경 책장!"], ["Bible leaves!", "성경 책장!"], ["This is the invariable cry from the mates to the mincer.", "이것은 항해사들이 잘게 써는 자에게 변함없이 외치는 소리다."], ["It enjoins him to be careful, and cut his work into as thin slices as possible, inasmuch as by so doing the business of boiling out the oil is much accelerated, and its quantity considerably increased, besides perhaps improving it in quality.", "이 말은 그에게 조심하여 일감을 되도록 얇은 조각으로 썰라는 명령인데, 그렇게 하면 기름을 끓여 내는 작업이 한결 빨라지고 그 양도 상당히 늘어나며, 어쩌면 품질까지 좋아질 수도 있기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 96. The Try-Works.", "# CHAPTER 96. The Try-Works."]], [["Besides her hoisted boats, an American whaler is outwardly distinguished by her try-works.", "보트를 매달아 둔 것 말고도, 미국 포경선을 겉으로 구별 짓는 것은 정유 가마(try-works)다."], ["She presents the curious anomaly of the most solid masonry joining with oak and hemp in constituting the completed ship.", "포경선은 더없이 단단한 석조 구조물이 참나무와 삼[麻]과 어우러져 완성된 한 척의 배를 이루는, 기묘한 모순을 드러낸다."], ["It is as if from the open field a brick-kiln were transported to her planks.", "마치 너른 들판에서 벽돌 굽는 가마 하나를 통째로 떼어다 갑판 널빤지 위에 옮겨 놓은 듯한 형국이다."]], [["The try-works are planted between the foremast and mainmast, the most roomy part of the deck.", "정유 가마는 앞돛대와 큰돛대 사이, 갑판에서 가장 너른 자리에 설치된다."], ["The timbers beneath are of a peculiar strength, fitted to sustain the weight of an almost solid mass of brick and mortar, some ten feet by eight square, and five in height.", "그 아래의 목재는 유독 튼튼하게 짜여, 가로세로 약 10피트(약 3미터) 곱하기 8피트(약 2.4미터), 높이 5피트(약 1.5미터)에 이르는, 거의 단단한 덩어리나 다름없는 벽돌과 회반죽의 무게를 떠받칠 수 있도록 되어 있다."], ["The foundation does not penetrate the deck, but the masonry is firmly secured to the surface by ponderous knees of iron bracing it on all sides, and screwing it down to the timbers.", "그 토대는 갑판을 뚫고 들어가지는 않으나, 석조 구조물은 사방을 떠받쳐 주는 묵직한 무릎쇠[膝鐵]로 표면에 단단히 고정되어 목재에 나사로 단단히 죄어진다."], ["On the flanks it is cased with wood, and at top completely covered by a large, sloping, battened hatchway.", "옆면은 나무로 둘러싸여 있고, 꼭대기는 비스듬히 경사진 널판 덮개 승강구로 완전히 덮여 있다."], ["Removing this hatch we expose the great try-pots, two in number, and each of several barrels’ capacity.", "이 덮개를 들어내면 커다란 정유솥(try-pots)이 드러나는데, 두 개가 있으며 저마다 몇 배럴의 용량을 지닌다."], ["When not in use, they are kept remarkably clean.", "쓰지 않을 때는 놀랄 만큼 깨끗하게 관리된다."], ["Sometimes they are polished with soapstone and sand, till they shine within like silver punch-bowls.", "더러는 동석[凍石]과 모래로 닦아, 안쪽이 은으로 만든 펀치 그릇처럼 반들반들 빛날 정도다."], ["During the night-watches some cynical old sailors will crawl into them and coil themselves away there for a nap.", "야간 당직 동안 냉소적인 늙은 선원들은 그 안으로 기어 들어가 몸을 둘둘 말고 한숨 자기도 한다."], ["While employed in polishing them—one man in each pot, side by side—many confidential communications are carried on, over the iron lips.", "그것을 닦는 일에 매달려 있을 때면 — 솥마다 한 사람씩, 나란히 들어앉아 — 그 무쇠 가장자리 너머로 은밀한 이야기들이 숱하게 오간다."], ["It is a place also for profound mathematical meditation.", "또한 이곳은 심오한 수학적 명상의 자리이기도 하다."], ["It was in the left hand try-pot of the Pequod, with the soapstone diligently circling round me, that I was first indirectly struck by the remarkable fact, that in geometry all bodies gliding along the cycloid, my soapstone for example, will descend from any point in precisely the same time.", "페쿼드호(Pequod)의 왼쪽 정유솥 안에서, 동석을 부지런히 내 주위로 빙빙 돌리고 있을 때, 나는 처음으로 어떤 주목할 만한 사실에 간접적으로 부딪쳤으니, 곧 기하학에서 사이클로이드 곡선을 따라 미끄러지는 모든 물체는 — 이를테면 내 동석은 — 어느 지점에서 출발하든 정확히 같은 시간에 내려온다는 것이었다."]], [["Removing the fire-board from the front of the try-works, the bare masonry of that side is exposed, penetrated by the two iron mouths of the furnaces, directly underneath the pots.", "정유 가마 앞면에서 방화판을 떼어 내면 그쪽의 맨 석조가 드러나는데, 솥 바로 아래에 화로의 무쇠 아가리 두 개가 뚫려 있다."], ["These mouths are fitted with heavy doors of iron.", "이 아가리에는 묵직한 무쇠 문이 달려 있다."], ["The intense heat of the fire is prevented from communicating itself to the deck, by means of a shallow reservoir extending under the entire inclosed surface of the works.", "불의 맹렬한 열기가 갑판으로 옮아가지 않도록, 가마의 둘러싸인 표면 전체 아래로 뻗은 얕은 저수통이 마련되어 있다."], ["By a tunnel inserted at the rear, this reservoir is kept replenished with water as fast as it evaporates.", "뒤쪽에 끼워 넣은 관[管]을 통해, 이 저수통은 물이 증발하는 만큼 빠르게 다시 채워진다."], ["There are no external chimneys; they open direct from the rear wall.", "외부로 난 굴뚝은 없으며, 화로는 뒷벽에서 곧장 열린다."], ["And here let us go back for a moment.", "자, 여기서 잠시 이야기를 거슬러 올라가 보자."]], [["It was about nine o’clock at night that the Pequod’s try-works were first started on this present voyage.", "페쿼드호의 정유 가마가 이번 항해에서 처음으로 가동된 것은 밤 아홉 시 무렵이었다."], ["It belonged to Stubb to oversee the business.", "그 일을 감독하는 것은 스터브(Stubb)의 몫이었다."]], [["“All ready there?", "\"거기 다들 준비됐나?"], ["Off hatch, then, and start her.", "그럼 덮개를 벗기고 솥을 돌려."], ["You cook, fire the works.”", "자네, 요리사, 가마에 불을 붙여.\""], ["This was an easy thing, for the carpenter had been thrusting his shavings into the furnace throughout the passage.", "이것은 쉬운 일이었으니, 목수가 항해 내내 화로 안에 자기 대팻밥을 쑤셔 넣어 두었기 때문이다."], ["Here be it said that in a whaling voyage the first fire in the try-works has to be fed for a time with wood.", "여기서 한마디 해 두자면, 포경 항해에서 정유 가마의 첫 불은 한동안 장작으로 지펴야 한다."], ["After that no wood is used, except as a means of quick ignition to the staple fuel.", "그 뒤로는 주연료에 빠르게 불을 붙이는 수단으로 쓰는 것 말고는 장작을 쓰지 않는다."], ["In a word, after being tried out, the crisp, shrivelled blubber, now called scraps or fritters, still contains considerable of its unctuous properties.", "한마디로, 한 번 기름을 짜내고 나면, 바삭하게 쪼그라든 지방 덩어리는 — 이제 찌꺼기 혹은 튀김 부스러기라 불리는데 — 여전히 상당한 기름기를 머금고 있다."], ["These fritters feed the flames.", "이 부스러기가 불꽃의 먹이가 된다."], ["Like a plethoric burning martyr, or a self-consuming misanthrope, once ignited, the whale supplies his own fuel and burns by his own body.", "피가 너무 많아 타오르는 순교자처럼, 혹은 제 몸을 갉아먹는 인간 혐오자처럼, 한 번 불이 붙으면 고래는 제 연료를 스스로 대고 제 몸으로 타오른다."], ["Would that he consumed his own smoke!", "차라리 제 연기까지 다 태워 버린다면 좋으련만!"], ["for his smoke is horrible to inhale, and inhale it you must, and not only that, but you must live in it for the time.", "그 연기는 들이마시기에 끔찍하건만, 그래도 들이마실 수밖에 없고, 그것뿐 아니라 한동안 그 속에서 살아야 한다."], ["It has an unspeakable, wild, Hindoo odor about it, such as may lurk in the vicinity of funereal pyres.", "그 연기에는 형언할 수 없는, 야만스러운, 힌두 화장터 같은 냄새가 배어 있으니, 장례용 장작더미 근처에 숨어 있을 법한 그런 냄새다."], ["It smells like the left wing of the day of judgment; it is an argument for the pit.", "그것은 최후 심판의 날 왼쪽 날개에서 나는 냄새와 같고, 지옥 구렁이 있음을 입증하는 논거다."]], [["By midnight the works were in full operation.", "자정 무렵 가마는 한창 돌아가고 있었다."], ["We were clear from the carcase; sail had been made; the wind was freshening; the wild ocean darkness was intense.", "우리는 고래 사체에서 벗어났고, 돛이 올려졌으며, 바람은 점점 거세지고, 거친 대양의 어둠은 짙고도 깊었다."], ["But that darkness was licked up by the fierce flames, which at intervals forked forth from the sooty flues, and illuminated every lofty rope in the rigging, as with the famed Greek fire.", "그러나 그 어둠은 맹렬한 불길에 핥겨 사라지고 있었으니, 불길은 이따금 그을음 묻은 연도(煙道)에서 갈래갈래 솟구쳐 올라, 저 유명한 그리스의 불처럼 삭구의 높직한 밧줄 하나하나를 환히 비추었다."], ["The burning ship drove on, as if remorselessly commissioned to some vengeful deed.", "불타는 배는 마치 어떤 복수의 과업을 향해 가차 없이 명을 받은 듯 앞으로 내달았다."], ["So the pitch and sulphur-freighted brigs of the bold Hydriote, Canaris, issuing from their midnight harbors, with broad sheets of flame for sails, bore down upon the Turkish frigates, and folded them in conflagrations.", "그 옛날 담대한 히드라 사람 카나리스의 역청과 유황을 가득 실은 범선들이 한밤의 항구에서 출항하여, 너른 화염의 돛을 펼친 채 터키 군함들을 향해 들이닥쳐 그것들을 화재 속에 휘감아 버렸듯이."]], [["The hatch, removed from the top of the works, now afforded a wide hearth in front of them.", "가마 꼭대기에서 벗겨 낸 덮개는 이제 그 앞에 너른 화덕을 마련해 주었다."], ["Standing on this were the Tartarean shapes of the pagan harpooneers, always the whale-ship’s stokers.", "그 위에는, 언제나 포경선의 화부 노릇을 하는 이교도 작살잡이들의 지옥 같은 형상이 서 있었다."], ["With huge pronged poles they pitched hissing masses of blubber into the scalding pots, or stirred up the fires beneath, till the snaky flames darted, curling, out of the doors to catch them by the feet.", "그들은 거대한 갈래 장대로 쉭쉭 소리를 내는 지방 덩어리를 펄펄 끓는 솥 안으로 던져 넣거나, 그 아래 불을 휘저었으니, 그러면 뱀 같은 불꽃이 문 밖으로 굽이치며 날름거려 그들의 발을 붙잡으려 들었다."], ["The smoke rolled away in sullen heaps.", "연기는 음울한 더미를 이루며 굴러갔다."], ["To every pitch of the ship there was a pitch of the boiling oil, which seemed all eagerness to leap into their faces.", "배가 한 번 출렁일 때마다 끓는 기름도 한 번 출렁였는데, 마치 그들의 얼굴을 향해 뛰어오르려고 안달이 난 듯했다."], ["Opposite the mouth of the works, on the further side of the wide wooden hearth, was the windlass.", "가마 아가리 맞은편, 그 너른 나무 화덕 저쪽 편에는 양묘기(揚錨機)가 있었다."], ["This served for a sea-sofa.", "이것은 바다의 소파 노릇을 했다."], ["Here lounged the watch, when not otherwise employed, looking into the red heat of the fire, till their eyes felt scorched in their heads.", "여기에 당직들은 달리 할 일이 없을 때면 비스듬히 기대어, 불의 붉은 열기를 들여다보았으니, 마침내 머릿속의 두 눈이 그슬리는 듯 느껴질 지경이었다."], ["Their tawny features, now all begrimed with smoke and sweat, their matted beards, and the contrasting barbaric brilliancy of their teeth, all these were strangely revealed in the capricious emblazonings of the works.", "그들의 가무잡잡한 얼굴은 이제 온통 연기와 땀으로 시커멓게 더럽혀졌고, 텁수룩하게 엉킨 수염과, 그와 대조를 이루는 치아의 야만적인 광채, 이 모든 것이 가마의 변덕스러운 불빛 무늬 속에서 기묘하게 드러났다."], ["As they narrated to each other their unholy adventures, their tales of terror told in words of mirth; as their uncivilized laughter forked upwards out of them, like the flames from the furnace; as to and fro, in their front, the harpooneers wildly gesticulated with their huge pronged forks and dippers; as the wind howled on, and the sea leaped, and the ship groaned and dived, and yet steadfastly shot her red hell further and further into the blackness of the sea and the night, and scornfully champed the white bone in her mouth, and viciously spat round her on all sides; then the rushing Pequod, freighted with savages, and laden with fire, and burning a corpse, and plunging into that blackness of darkness, seemed the material counterpart of her monomaniac commander’s soul.", "그들이 서로에게 자신들의 부정한 모험담을, 그 공포의 이야기를 흥겨운 말투로 들려줄 때, 그들의 미개한 웃음이 화로의 불길처럼 그들에게서 위로 갈래갈래 솟구칠 때, 그들 앞에서 작살잡이들이 거대한 갈래 갈고리와 국자를 들고 이리저리 사납게 손짓을 해 댈 때, 바람이 윙윙거리며 울부짖고, 바다가 펄쩍 뛰어오르고, 배가 신음하며 곤두박질치면서도, 그 붉은 지옥을 바다와 밤의 어둠 속으로 더욱더 굳건히 쏘아 보내며, 입에 문 흰 뼈를 비웃듯 우적우적 씹어 대고, 사방으로 사납게 침을 뱉어 댈 때 — 그때 야만인들을 싣고, 불을 가득 안고, 시체 하나를 태우며, 그 캄캄한 어둠의 심연 속으로 곤두박질쳐 들어가는 저 질주하는 페쿼드호는, 그 편집광 선장의 영혼을 물질로 빚어낸 짝패처럼 보였다."]], [["So seemed it to me, as I stood at her helm, and for long hours silently guided the way of this fire-ship on the sea.", "내가 키를 잡고 서서, 기나긴 시간 동안 말없이 이 바다 위 화염선의 뱃길을 인도하는 동안, 그것은 내게 그렇게 보였다."], ["Wrapped, for that interval, in darkness myself, I but the better saw the redness, the madness, the ghastliness of others.", "그 사이 나 자신은 어둠에 휩싸여 있었기에, 도리어 다른 이들의 붉음과 광기와 끔찍함을 더 잘 볼 수 있었다."], ["The continual sight of the fiend shapes before me, capering half in smoke and half in fire, these at last begat kindred visions in my soul, so soon as I began to yield to that unaccountable drowsiness which ever would come over me at a midnight helm.", "내 앞에서 반쯤은 연기 속에, 반쯤은 불 속에 깡충거리는 그 악마 같은 형상들을 끊임없이 바라보다 보니, 마침내 그것들은 내 영혼 속에도 비슷한 환영을 낳았으니, 한밤중에 키를 잡고 있노라면 늘 나를 덮쳐 오곤 하던 그 까닭 모를 졸음에 내가 굴복하기 시작하자마자 그러했다."]], [["But that night, in particular, a strange (and ever since inexplicable) thing occurred to me.", "그러나 그날 밤, 유독 그날 밤에, 기이한 (그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧 설명할 수 없는) 일이 내게 일어났다."], ["Starting from a brief standing sleep, I was horribly conscious of something fatally wrong.", "잠깐 선 채로 졸다가 화들짝 깨어난 나는, 무언가 치명적으로 잘못되었음을 끔찍하게 의식했다."], ["The jaw-bone tiller smote my side, which leaned against it; in my ears was the low hum of sails, just beginning to shake in the wind; I thought my eyes were open; I was half conscious of putting my fingers to the lids and mechanically stretching them still further apart.", "턱뼈로 된 키손잡이가 그것에 기대고 있던 내 옆구리를 후려쳤고, 귓가에는 바람에 막 떨리기 시작한 돛의 나직한 웅웅거림이 들려왔다. 나는 두 눈을 뜨고 있다고 여겼고, 손가락을 눈꺼풀에 갖다 대어 그것을 기계적으로 더 벌리고 있다는 것을 어렴풋이 의식하고 있었다."], ["But, spite of all this, I could see no compass before me to steer by; though it seemed but a minute since I had been watching the card, by the steady binnacle lamp illuminating it.", "그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고, 내 앞에는 길을 잡을 나침반이 도무지 보이지 않았다. 한결같은 나침반함 등불이 비추는 가운데 나침반판을 들여다보고 있던 게 불과 일 분 전이었던 것만 같은데도 말이다."], ["Nothing seemed before me but a jet gloom, now and then made ghastly by flashes of redness.", "내 앞에는 칠흑 같은 음울함 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았고, 그것은 이따금 붉은 섬광으로 인해 무시무시해질 뿐이었다."], ["Uppermost was the impression, that whatever swift, rushing thing I stood on was not so much bound to any haven ahead as rushing from all havens astern.", "무엇보다 강하게 든 느낌은, 내가 서 있는 그 무엇이든 — 빠르게 내달리는 그것이 — 앞에 있는 어떤 항구에 묶여 있다기보다는, 뒤에 있는 모든 항구로부터 달아나고 있다는 것이었다."], ["A stark, bewildered feeling, as of death, came over me.", "죽음과도 같은, 처절하고 어리둥절한 느낌이 나를 덮쳤다."], ["Convulsively my hands grasped the tiller, but with the crazy conceit that the tiller was, somehow, in some enchanted way, inverted.", "발작하듯 두 손으로 키손잡이를 움켜쥐었으나, 그 키손잡이가 어쩐 일인지, 어떤 마법에 걸린 듯이, 거꾸로 뒤집혀 있다는 미친 망상에 사로잡혔다."], ["My God!", "맙소사!"], ["what is the matter with me?", "내게 무슨 일이 일어난 것인가?"], ["thought I. Lo!", "나는 생각했다."], ["in my brief sleep I had turned myself about, and was fronting the ship’s stern, with my back to her prow and the compass.", "보라! 그 짧은 잠 사이에 나는 몸을 빙 돌려, 뱃머리와 나침반에 등을 진 채, 배의 고물 쪽을 마주 보고 있었던 것이다."], ["In an instant I faced back, just in time to prevent the vessel from flying up into the wind, and very probably capsizing her.", "순식간에 나는 다시 뒤로 돌아섰으니, 배가 바람을 안고 곧추서 올라가다가 십중팔구 뒤집히는 것을 가까스로 막을 수 있었다."], ["How glad and how grateful the relief from this unnatural hallucination of the night, and the fatal contingency of being brought by the lee!", "한밤중의 이 부자연스러운 환각에서, 그리고 바람 불어가는 쪽으로 밀려날 뻔한 그 치명적인 위기에서 벗어났을 때의 안도가 얼마나 기쁘고 또 얼마나 고마웠던지!"]], [["Look not too long in the face of the fire, O man!", "오, 인간이여, 불의 얼굴을 너무 오래 들여다보지 말라! 키에 손을 얹은 채 꿈꾸지 말라!"], ["Never dream with thy hand on the helm!", "나침반에 등을 돌리지 말라. 덜컹거리는 키손잡이의 첫 신호를 받아들여라."], ["Turn not thy back to the compass; accept the first hint of the hitching tiller; believe not the artificial fire, when its redness makes all things look ghastly.", "인공의 불이 그 붉음으로 만물을 끔찍하게 보이게 할 때, 그 불을 믿지 말라. 내일이면, 자연의 태양 아래, 하늘은 환할 것이다."], ["To-morrow, in the natural sun, the skies will be bright; those who glared like devils in the forking flames, the morn will show in far other, at least gentler, relief; the glorious, golden, glad sun, the only true lamp—all others but liars!", "갈래진 불길 속에서 악마처럼 이글거리던 자들도, 아침이면 사뭇 다른, 적어도 한결 부드러운 빛 속에 모습을 드러낼 것이다. 영광스럽고, 황금빛이며, 기쁨에 찬 태양, 그 유일한 참된 등불 — 다른 모든 것은 거짓말쟁이일 뿐이다!"]], [["Nevertheless the sun hides not Virginia’s Dismal Swamp, nor Rome’s accursed Campagna, nor wide Sahara, nor all the millions of miles of deserts and of griefs beneath the moon.", "그럼에도 태양은 버지니아의 디즈멀 늪을 감추지 못하고, 로마의 저주받은 캄파냐도, 너른 사하라도, 달빛 아래 펼쳐진 수백만 마일에 이르는 사막과 슬픔도 감추지 못한다."], ["The sun hides not the ocean, which is the dark side of this earth, and which is two thirds of this earth.", "태양은 바다를 감추지 못하니, 바다야말로 이 지구의 어두운 면이요, 이 지구의 삼분의 이를 차지한다."], ["So, therefore, that mortal man who hath more of joy than sorrow in him, that mortal man cannot be true—not true, or undeveloped.", "그러므로, 자기 안에 슬픔보다 기쁨이 더 많은 인간, 그런 인간은 참될 수가 없다 — 참되지 않거나, 아직 덜 여문 것이다."], ["With books the same.", "책 또한 마찬가지다."], ["The truest of all men was the Man of Sorrows, and the truest of all books is Solomon’s, and Ecclesiastes is the fine hammered steel of woe.", "모든 사람 가운데 가장 참된 이는 슬픔의 사람이었고, 모든 책 가운데 가장 참된 것은 솔로몬의 책이며, 전도서는 비통함을 곱게 두드려 벼린 강철이다."], ["“All is vanity.” ALL.", "\"모든 것이 헛되도다.\""], ["This wilful world hath not got hold of unchristian Solomon’s wisdom yet.", "모든 것이. 이 고집스러운 세상은 아직도 기독교적이지 않은 솔로몬의 지혜를 붙들지 못했다."], ["But he who dodges hospitals and jails, and walks fast crossing graveyards, and would rather talk of operas than hell; calls Cowper, Young, Pascal, Rousseau, poor devils all of sick men; and throughout a care-free lifetime swears by Rabelais as passing wise, and therefore jolly;—not that man is fitted to sit down on tomb-stones, and break the green damp mould with unfathomably wondrous Solomon.", "그러나 병원과 감옥을 슬쩍 피해 다니고, 묘지를 가로지를 때는 걸음을 재촉하며, 지옥보다는 차라리 오페라 이야기를 즐겨 하고, 쿠퍼와 영과 파스칼과 루소를 한낱 병든 자들의 가련한 무리라 부르며, 근심 없는 평생토록 라블레야말로 더없이 현명하기에 흥겨운 자라고 떠받드는 자 — 그런 자는, 묘비 위에 앉아 헤아릴 길 없이 경이로운 솔로몬과 더불어 푸른 곰팡이 낀 축축한 흙을 부수기에는 어울리지 않는다."]], [["But even Solomon, he says, “the man that wandereth out of the way of understanding shall remain”", "그러나 솔로몬조차도 이렇게 말한다. \"명철의 길에서 벗어나 떠도는 자는 (곧, 살아 있으면서도) 죽은 자들의 무리 가운데 머무르리라.\""], ["(i.e., even while living) “in the congregation of the dead.” Give not thyself up, then, to fire, lest it invert thee, deaden thee; as for the time it did me.", "그러니 그대 자신을 불에 내맡기지 말라, 한때 그것이 내게 그러했듯, 그것이 그대를 거꾸로 뒤집고 그대를 마비시키지 못하도록."], ["There is a wisdom that is woe; but there is a woe that is madness.", "비통함인 지혜가 있고, 광기인 비통함이 있다."], ["And there is a Catskill eagle in some souls that can alike dive down into the blackest gorges, and soar out of them again and become invisible in the sunny spaces.", "그리고 어떤 영혼들 속에는 캐츠킬의 독수리가 있어, 가장 캄캄한 협곡 속으로 내리꽂혔다가 거기서 다시 솟아올라 햇살 가득한 공간 속으로 자취를 감출 수 있다."], ["And even if he for ever flies within the gorge, that gorge is in the mountains; so that even in his lowest swoop the mountain eagle is still higher than other birds upon the plain, even though they soar.", "설령 그 독수리가 영영 협곡 안에서만 날아다닌다 해도, 그 협곡은 산속에 있다. 그리하여 가장 낮게 내리꽂힐 때조차, 산의 독수리는 평원 위의 다른 새들보다 — 그것들이 아무리 높이 솟아오른다 한들 — 여전히 더 높이 있는 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 97. The Lamp.", "# CHAPTER 97. The Lamp."]], [["Had you descended from the Pequod’s try-works to the Pequod’s forecastle, where the off duty watch were sleeping, for one single moment you would have almost thought you were standing in some illuminated shrine of canonized kings and counsellors.", "피쿼드호의 시연(試煉) 화덕에서 비번인 당직 선원들이 잠들어 있는 피쿼드호의 선수루로 내려간다면, 단 한순간이나마 그대는 거의 시성(諡聖)된 왕들과 고문관들을 모신 어느 환히 밝혀진 사당 안에 서 있다고 여겼으리라."], ["There they lay in their triangular oaken vaults, each mariner a chiselled muteness; a score of lamps flashing upon his hooded eyes.", "그들은 거기, 삼각형 모양의 떡갈나무 납골당 속에 누워 있었고, 선원 하나하나가 마치 끌로 새겨 낸 침묵의 형상 같았으며, 스무 개 남짓한 등불이 두건처럼 감긴 그들의 눈 위로 빛을 던지고 있었다."]], [["In merchantmen, oil for the sailor is more scarce than the milk of queens.", "상선에서 선원에게 돌아가는 기름은 여왕의 젖보다도 귀하다."], ["To dress in the dark, and eat in the dark, and stumble in darkness to his pallet, this is his usual lot.", "어둠 속에서 옷을 입고, 어둠 속에서 먹고, 어둠 속을 비틀거리며 자기 짚자리로 가는 것, 이것이 그가 늘 겪는 신세다."], ["But the whaleman, as he seeks the food of light, so he lives in light.", "그러나 고래잡이는 빛의 양식을 찾아 나서는 자인 만큼, 빛 속에서 살아간다."], ["He makes his berth an Aladdin’s lamp, and lays him down in it; so that in the pitchiest night the ship’s black hull still houses an illumination.", "그는 자기 잠자리를 알라딘의 램프로 만들어 그 안에 몸을 누이니, 그리하여 칠흑같이 캄캄한 밤에도 배의 검은 선체는 여전히 환한 빛을 품고 있다."]], [["See with what entire freedom the whaleman takes his handful of lamps—often but old bottles and vials, though—to the copper cooler at the try-works, and replenishes them there, as mugs of ale at a vat.", "고래잡이가 얼마나 거리낌 없이 한 움큼의 등불을 — 흔히 낡은 병이나 약병에 지나지 않는 것이긴 하지만 — 시연 화덕의 구리 냉각통으로 가져가, 마치 통에서 에일 잔을 채우듯 거기서 기름을 다시 채우는지 보라."], ["He burns, too, the purest of oil, in its unmanufactured, and, therefore, unvitiated state; a fluid unknown to solar, lunar, or astral contrivances ashore.", "게다가 그는 가장 순수한 기름을, 가공되지 않은, 그래서 한 점 변질도 없는 상태 그대로 태운다. 그것은 뭍에 있는 태양등이나 달등이나 별등 따위의 어떤 장치도 알지 못하는 액체다."], ["It is sweet as early grass butter in April.", "그 맛은 사월의 갓 돋은 풀밭에서 난 버터처럼 달콤하다."], ["He goes and hunts for his oil, so as to be sure of its freshness and genuineness, even as the traveller on the prairie hunts up his own supper of game.", "그는 마치 대초원의 나그네가 제 저녁거리 사냥감을 손수 잡아 오듯, 그 기름의 신선함과 진정성을 확실히 하기 위해 손수 나가 사냥하는 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 98. Stowing Down and Clearing Up.", "# CHAPTER 98. Stowing Down and Clearing Up."]], [["Already has it been related how the great leviathan is afar off descried from the mast-head; how he is chased over the watery moors, and slaughtered in the valleys of the deep; how he is then towed alongside and beheaded; and how (on the principle which entitled the headsman of old to the garments in which the beheaded was killed) his great padded surtout becomes the property of his executioner; how, in due time, he is condemned to the pots, and, like Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, his spermaceti, oil, and bone pass unscathed through the fire;—but now it remains to conclude the last chapter of this part of the description by rehearsing—singing, if I may—the romantic proceeding of decanting off his oil into the casks and striking them down into the hold, where once again leviathan returns to his native profundities, sliding along beneath the surface as before; but, alas!", "위대한 리바이어던이 어떻게 멀리 돛대 꼭대기에서 발견되는지, 어떻게 물의 황야를 가로질러 쫓기다가 깊은 바다의 골짜기에서 도살되는지, 어떻게 그러고 나서 뱃전으로 끌려와 목이 잘리는지, 그리고 어떻게 (목이 잘린 자가 입고 죽은 옷이 옛 처형인의 몫이 되던 그 원칙에 따라) 그 거대하고 두툼한 외투가 그 사형 집행인의 소유가 되는지, 어떻게 때가 되면 그것이 가마솥의 형벌을 선고받고, 사드락과 메삭과 아벳느고처럼 그 경뇌와 기름과 뼈가 불길을 다치지 않고 통과하는지—이 모든 것은 이미 이야기한 바 있다. 그러나 이제 이 부분의 묘사에서 마지막 장을 마무리짓는 일이 남았으니, 곧 그 기름을 통 속으로 따라 부어 선창으로 내려보내는, 그리하여 리바이어던이 다시 한번 제 본래의 심연으로 돌아가 예전처럼 수면 아래로 미끄러져 가는—그러나 아아!"], ["never more to rise and blow.", "다시는 떠올라 물을 뿜는 일이 없을—그 낭만적인 절차를 되짚어 노래하듯, 내가 노래할 수 있다면 노래해 보는 일이다."]], [["While still warm, the oil, like hot punch, is received into the six-barrel casks; and while, perhaps, the ship is pitching and rolling this way and that in the midnight sea, the enormous casks are slewed round and headed over, end for end, and sometimes perilously scoot across the slippery deck, like so many land slides, till at last man-handled and stayed in their course; and all round the hoops, rap, rap, go as many hammers as can play upon them, for now, ex officio, every sailor is a cooper.", "기름은 아직 따뜻한 동안, 뜨거운 펀치주처럼 여섯 통들이 큰 통 속으로 받아 담긴다. 그리고 그러는 사이, 어쩌면 한밤중의 바다에서 배가 이리저리 곤두박질치며 흔들리는 동안, 그 거대한 통들은 빙그르르 돌려져 위아래가 뒤집히고, 때로는 미끄러운 갑판을 가로질러 위태롭게 내달리니, 마치 수많은 산사태처럼 굴러가다가 마침내 사람들의 손에 붙들려 제자리에 멈춰 서고, 그 통 둘레의 테에는 두드릴 수 있는 한 많은 망치들이 탁, 탁, 소리를 낸다. 이제 직무상(ex officio) 선원은 누구나 통메장이가 되기 때문이다."]], [["At length, when the last pint is casked, and all is cool, then the great hatchways are unsealed, the bowels of the ship are thrown open, and down go the casks to their final rest in the sea.", "이윽고 마지막 한 파인트까지 통에 담기고 모든 것이 식으면, 큰 승강구들이 봉인이 풀려 열리고, 배의 창자가 활짝 펼쳐지며, 통들이 바다 속 마지막 안식처로 내려간다."], ["This done, the hatches are replaced, and hermetically closed, like a closet walled up.", "이 일이 끝나면 승강구 뚜껑이 다시 덮이고, 벽을 발라 막은 벽장처럼 완전히 밀폐된다."]], [["In the sperm fishery, this is perhaps one of the most remarkable incidents in all the business of whaling.", "향유고래잡이에서 이것은 어쩌면 포경업 전체에서 가장 주목할 만한 사건 가운데 하나다."], ["One day the planks stream with freshets of blood and oil; on the sacred quarter-deck enormous masses of the whale’s head are profanely piled; great rusty casks lie about, as in a brewery yard; the smoke from the try-works has besooted all the bulwarks; the mariners go about suffused with unctuousness; the entire ship seems great leviathan himself; while on all hands the din is deafening.", "어느 날엔 갑판 널빤지가 피와 기름의 봇물로 흥건히 흐르고, 신성한 뒷갑판 위에는 고래 머리의 거대한 덩어리들이 불경스럽게 쌓이며, 양조장 마당처럼 녹슨 큰 통들이 사방에 나뒹굴고, 정제 가마의 연기가 모든 뱃전을 검댕으로 뒤덮으며, 선원들은 온몸에 기름기를 뒤집어쓴 채 돌아다니고, 배 전체가 위대한 리바이어던 그 자신처럼 보이며, 사방에서 들려오는 소음은 귀를 먹먹하게 한다."]], [["But a day or two after, you look about you, and prick your ears in this self-same ship; and were it not for the tell-tale boats and try-works, you would all but swear you trod some silent merchant vessel, with a most scrupulously neat commander.", "그러나 하루나 이틀이 지난 뒤 바로 이 똑같은 배에서 주위를 둘러보고 귀를 곤두세워 보면, 만일 그 속사정을 일러바치는 보트들과 정제 가마가 없었던들, 당신은 더없이 꼼꼼하고 깔끔한 선장이 모는 어느 조용한 상선 갑판을 밟고 있노라고 거의 맹세라도 할 뻔할 것이다."], ["The unmanufactured sperm oil possesses a singularly cleansing virtue.", "정제하지 않은 향유고래 기름은 유난히 깨끗하게 닦아 내는 성질을 지녔다."], ["This is the reason why the decks never look so white as just after what they call an affair of oil.", "그들이 이른바 기름 작업이라 부르는 일이 끝난 직후만큼 갑판이 새하얗게 보이는 때가 없는 것은 바로 이 때문이다."], ["Besides, from the ashes of the burned scraps of the whale, a potent lye is readily made; and whenever any adhesiveness from the back of the whale remains clinging to the side, that lye quickly exterminates it.", "게다가 태워 버린 고래 부스러기의 재로는 손쉽게 강력한 잿물을 만들 수 있어, 고래 등에서 나온 끈적이는 것이 뱃전에 들러붙어 남아 있으면 그 잿물이 재빨리 그것을 없애 버린다."], ["Hands go diligently along the bulwarks, and with buckets of water and rags restore them to their full tidiness.", "사람들은 부지런히 뱃전을 따라 오가며 물통과 걸레로 갑판을 더없이 말끔한 상태로 되돌려 놓는다."], ["The soot is brushed from the lower rigging.", "아래쪽 삭구의 검댕도 솔질해 털어 낸다."], ["All the numerous implements which have been in use are likewise faithfully cleansed and put away.", "그동안 쓰였던 그 수많은 도구들도 마찬가지로 충실히 닦여 제자리에 치워진다."], ["The great hatch is scrubbed and placed upon the try-works, completely hiding the pots; every cask is out of sight; all tackles are coiled in unseen nooks; and when by the combined and simultaneous industry of almost the entire ship’s company, the whole of this conscientious duty is at last concluded, then the crew themselves proceed to their own ablutions; shift themselves from top to toe; and finally issue to the immaculate deck, fresh and all aglow, as bridegrooms new-leaped from out the daintiest Holland.", "큰 승강구 뚜껑은 박박 문질러 닦여 정제 가마 위에 얹혀 가마솥을 완전히 가리고, 통은 모조리 눈에 띄지 않게 되며, 밧줄 도구들은 모두 보이지 않는 구석에 감겨 놓인다. 그리고 거의 배 전체 선원이 힘을 합쳐 동시에 부지런을 떤 끝에 이 양심적인 임무 전부가 마침내 끝나면, 그제야 선원들 자신도 제 몸을 씻으러 나아가, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 옷을 갈아입고, 마침내 흠 하나 없이 깨끗한 갑판으로 나오니, 마치 더없이 고운 네덜란드산 아마포 침구에서 갓 뛰쳐나온 신랑들처럼 산뜻하고 온통 발그레하게 상기되어 있다."]], [["Now, with elated step, they pace the planks in twos and threes, and humorously discourse of parlors, sofas, carpets, and fine cambrics; propose to mat the deck; think of having hanging to the top; object not to taking tea by moonlight on the piazza of the forecastle.", "이제 그들은 의기양양한 걸음으로 둘씩 셋씩 갑판을 거닐며, 응접실이며 소파며 양탄자며 고운 옥양목 따위를 농담 삼아 늘어놓고, 갑판에 돗자리를 깔자고 제안하고, 꼭대기에 차양을 다는 것을 궁리하며, 앞갑판의 베란다에서 달빛 아래 차를 마시는 것도 마다하지 않겠노라 한다."], ["To hint to such musked mariners of oil, and bone, and blubber, were little short of audacity.", "이렇게 사향 내음을 풍기는 선원들에게 기름이며 뼈며 지방 따위를 입에 올리는 것은 거의 무례에 가까운 일일 것이다."], ["They know not the thing you distantly allude to.", "그들은 당신이 에둘러 넌지시 말하는 그것이 무엇인지조차 모른다."], ["Away, and bring us napkins!", "저리 가서 우리에게 냅킨이나 가져오게!"]], [["But mark: aloft there, at the three mast heads, stand three men intent on spying out more whales, which, if caught, infallibly will again soil the old oaken furniture, and drop at least one small grease-spot somewhere.", "그러나 보라. 저 위, 세 개의 돛대 꼭대기에는 세 사람이 더 많은 고래를 찾아내려고 골똘히 망을 보며 서 있으니, 만일 그 고래가 잡힌다면 틀림없이 또다시 저 낡은 떡갈나무 가구를 더럽히고, 적어도 어딘가에 자그마한 기름 얼룩 하나쯤은 떨어뜨릴 것이다."], ["Yes; and many is the time, when, after the severest uninterrupted labors, which know no night; continuing straight through for ninety-six hours; when from the boat, where they have swelled their wrists with all day rowing on the Line,—they only step to the deck to carry vast chains, and heave the heavy windlass, and cut and slash, yea, and in their very sweatings to be smoked and burned anew by the combined fires of the equatorial sun and the equatorial try-works; when, on the heel of all this, they have finally bestirred themselves to cleanse the ship, and make a spotless dairy room of it; many is the time the poor fellows, just buttoning the necks of their clean frocks, are startled by the cry of “There she blows!”", "그렇다. 그리고 밤을 모르고 꼬박 아흔여섯 시간을 내리 이어지는 더없이 혹독하고 쉴 틈 없는 노동을 마친 뒤, 적도 부근에서 온종일 노를 저어 손목이 퉁퉁 부은 채 보트에서 갑판으로 올라서자마자—거대한 사슬을 나르고, 무거운 양묘기를 들어 올리고, 자르고 베어 내며, 그래, 그 땀투성이 몸뚱이가 적도의 태양과 적도의 정제 가마라는 두 불길에 다시금 그을리고 데어 가며, 이 모든 일의 끝에 이르러 마침내 기운을 차려 배를 청소하고 그것을 티끌 하나 없는 우유 저장실처럼 만들어 놓는 일이 얼마나 잦았던가."], ["and away they fly to fight another whale, and go through the whole weary thing again.", "깨끗한 작업복의 목단추를 막 채우던 그 가련한 친구들이 \"저기 물을 뿜는다!\" 하는 외침에 소스라치게 놀라, 또 다른 고래와 싸우러 날듯이 달려가, 그 지긋지긋한 일을 처음부터 다시 되풀이하는 일이 또 얼마나 잦았던가."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["my friends, but this is man-killing! Yet this is life.", "벗들이여, 그러나 이것은 사람 잡는 일이다!"], ["For hardly have we mortals by long toilings extracted from this world’s vast bulk its small but valuable sperm; and then, with weary patience, cleansed ourselves from its defilements, and learned to live here in clean tabernacles of the soul; hardly is this done, when—There she blows!—the ghost is spouted up, and away we sail to fight some other world, and go through young life’s old routine again.", "그럼에도 이것이 바로 삶이다. 우리 인간이 오랜 노고 끝에 이 세상의 거대한 몸뚱이에서 그 작지만 값진 정수(精髓)를 가까스로 뽑아내고, 그런 다음 지친 인내로 그 더러움에서 우리 자신을 씻어 내어 영혼의 정결한 성막 안에서 사는 법을 겨우 익히자마자—그 일이 채 끝나기도 전에—저기 물을 뿜는다!—유령이 솟구쳐 오르고, 우리는 또 다른 세상과 싸우러 배를 띄워, 젊은 삶의 그 케케묵은 일상을 다시 한번 되풀이하는 것이다."]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["the metempsychosis!", "윤회여!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["Pythagoras, that in bright Greece, two thousand years ago, did die, so good, so wise, so mild; I sailed with thee along the Peruvian coast last voyage—and, foolish as I am, taught thee, a green simple boy, how to splice a rope!", "피타고라스여, 빛나는 그리스에서, 이천 년 전에, 그토록 선하고, 그토록 지혜롭고, 그토록 온화하게 죽어 간 그대여. 나는 지난 항해에서 그대와 함께 페루 해안을 따라 항해했으니—어리석게도 나는, 풋내기 순박한 소년인 그대에게, 밧줄 잇는 법을 가르쳤더란 말이다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 99. The Doubloon.", "# CHAPTER 99. The Doubloon."]], [["Ere now it has been related how Ahab was wont to pace his quarter-deck, taking regular turns at either limit, the binnacle and mainmast; but in the multiplicity of other things requiring narration it has not been added how that sometimes in these walks, when most plunged in his mood, he was wont to pause in turn at each spot, and stand there strangely eyeing the particular object before him.", "앞서 에이해브가 자신의 뒷갑판을 거니는 버릇이 있었음을, 그리고 그 양쪽 끝, 곧 나침반 함과 주돛대 사이를 규칙적으로 오가곤 했음을 이야기한 바 있다. 그러나 서술해야 할 다른 여러 가지 일들이 워낙 많았던 탓에, 때때로 이렇게 거니는 동안 가장 깊은 상념에 잠겼을 때면 그가 양쪽 지점에서 차례로 걸음을 멈추고, 그곳에 서서 눈앞의 특정한 대상을 기이하게 응시하곤 했다는 사실은 미처 덧붙이지 못했다."], ["When he halted before the binnacle, with his glance fastened on the pointed needle in the compass, that glance shot like a javelin with the pointed intensity of his purpose; and when resuming his walk he again paused before the mainmast, then, as the same riveted glance fastened upon the riveted gold coin there, he still wore the same aspect of nailed firmness, only dashed with a certain wild longing, if not hopefulness.", "나침반 함 앞에서 멈춰 서서 그 시선을 나침반 속의 뾰족한 바늘에 못 박았을 때, 그 시선은 그의 목적이 지닌 날카로운 강렬함을 담아 투창처럼 쏘아졌다. 그리고 다시 걸음을 이어 가다가 주돛대 앞에서 또 멈추면, 그 못 박힌 듯한 시선이 거기에 못 박힌 금화에 가서 꽂혔는데, 그때에도 그는 여전히 못으로 박아 놓은 듯한 단호함을 얼굴에 띠었으되, 다만 거기에는 희망이라고까지는 못해도 어떤 사나운 갈망이 한 줄기 섞여 있었다."]], [["But one morning, turning to pass the doubloon, he seemed to be newly attracted by the strange figures and inscriptions stamped on it, as though now for the first time beginning to interpret for himself in some monomaniac way whatever significance might lurk in them.", "그런데 어느 날 아침, 그 금화를 지나치려 몸을 돌리던 그는, 마치 이제 처음으로 그 안에 숨어 있을지 모를 온갖 의미를 자기 나름의 어떤 편집광적인 방식으로 해독하기 시작하기라도 하듯, 거기에 새겨진 기이한 형상과 명문(銘文)에 새삼 이끌리는 듯했다."], ["And some certain significance lurks in all things, else all things are little worth, and the round world itself but an empty cipher, except to sell by the cartload, as they do hills about Boston, to fill up some morass in the Milky Way.", "그리고 모든 사물 속에는 어떤 의미가 숨어 있는 법이다. 그렇지 않다면 모든 사물은 보잘것없는 것이며, 둥근 이 세계 자체도 한낱 텅 빈 영(零)에 지나지 않으리니, 다만 보스턴 근방의 언덕을 그러하듯 짐수레째 팔아넘겨 은하수 어딘가의 늪을 메우는 데나 쓰일 따름일 것이다."]], [["Now this doubloon was of purest, virgin gold, raked somewhere out of the heart of gorgeous hills, whence, east and west, over golden sands, the head-waters of many a Pactolus flows.", "그런데 이 금화는 더없이 순수한 처녀 금으로, 화려한 언덕의 심장부 어딘가에서 긁어모은 것이었으니, 그곳에서는 동으로 서로 황금빛 모래밭을 가로질러 수많은 팍톨로스강의 원류가 흘러내린다."], ["And though now nailed amidst all the rustiness of iron bolts and the verdigris of copper spikes, yet, untouchable and immaculate to any foulness, it still preserved its Quito glow.", "그리고 지금은 비록 녹슨 쇠 볼트와 푸른 녹이 슨 구리 못들 한가운데에 못 박혀 있었으나, 그 어떤 더러움도 닿거나 물들이지 못하여, 그것은 여전히 키토의 광휘를 간직하고 있었다."], ["Nor, though placed amongst a ruthless crew and every hour passed by ruthless hands, and through the livelong nights shrouded with thick darkness which might cover any pilfering approach, nevertheless every sunrise found the doubloon where the sunset left it last.", "또한 무자비한 선원들 틈에 놓여 매시간 무자비한 손길이 곁을 스쳐 지나갔고, 길고 긴 밤마다 어떤 좀도둑질의 접근이라도 가려 줄 만한 짙은 어둠에 휩싸여 있었음에도, 매번 해가 떠오를 때마다 그 금화는 해가 질 때 마지막으로 놓아둔 바로 그 자리에 그대로 있었다."], ["For it was set apart and sanctified to one awe-striking end; and however wanton in their sailor ways, one and all, the mariners revered it as the white whale’s talisman.", "그도 그럴 것이 그것은 따로 떼어져 어떤 경외로운 목적에 봉헌된 것이었기 때문이다. 그래서 선원들은 그 뱃사람다운 방종함이 어떠하든 간에, 너 나 할 것 없이 모두 그것을 흰 고래의 부적으로 떠받들었다."], ["Sometimes they talked it over in the weary watch by night, wondering whose it was to be at last, and whether he would ever live to spend it.", "때로는 밤의 고단한 당직 시간에 그 이야기를 나누며, 그것이 마침내 누구의 것이 될지, 그리고 그가 과연 그것을 쓸 만큼 살아남을지를 궁금해하곤 했다."]], [["Now those noble golden coins of South America are as medals of the sun and tropic token-pieces.", "그런데 저 고귀한 남아메리카의 금화들은 마치 태양의 메달이요 열대의 표징과도 같다."], ["Here palms, alpacas, and volcanoes; sun’s disks and stars; ecliptics, horns-of-plenty, and rich banners waving, are in luxuriant profusion stamped; so that the precious gold seems almost to derive an added preciousness and enhancing glories, by passing through those fancy mints, so Spanishly poetic.", "거기에는 종려나무와 알파카와 화산들, 태양의 원반과 별들, 황도(黃道)와 풍요의 뿔, 그리고 펄럭이는 화려한 깃발들이 풍성하고도 호화롭게 새겨져 있으니, 그 귀한 금은 그토록 스페인답게 시적인 저 환상적인 조폐소를 거치면서 한층 더한 귀함과 드높은 영광을 얻는 듯 보인다."]], [["It so chanced that the doubloon of the Pequod was a most wealthy example of these things.", "마침 피쿼드호의 이 금화는 그러한 것들 가운데서도 더없이 풍성한 본보기였다."], ["On its round border it bore the letters, REPUBLICA DEL ECUADOR: QUITO.", "그 둥근 가장자리에는 \"에콰도르 공화국: 키토(REPUBLICA DEL ECUADOR: QUITO)\"라는 글자가 새겨져 있었다."], ["So this bright coin came from a country planted in the middle of the world, and beneath the great equator, and named after it; and it had been cast midway up the Andes, in the unwaning clime that knows no autumn.", "그러므로 이 빛나는 금화는 세계의 한가운데, 곧 저 위대한 적도 아래에 자리하여 그 이름을 따 명명된 나라에서 온 것이었다. 그것은 안데스 산맥의 중턱, 가을을 모르고 결코 시들지 않는 풍토에서 주조되었다."], ["Zoned by those letters you saw the likeness of three Andes’ summits; from one a flame; a tower on another; on the third a crowing cock; while arching over all was a segment of the partitioned zodiac, the signs all marked with their usual cabalistics, and the keystone sun entering the equinoctial point at Libra.", "그 글자들로 띠를 두른 안쪽에는 안데스 봉우리 셋의 형상이 보였으니, 하나에서는 불꽃이, 다른 하나에는 탑이, 셋째에는 홰를 치며 우는 수탉이 새겨져 있었고, 그 모두를 아치처럼 둘러싼 것은 구획 지어진 황도대(黃道帶)의 한 부분으로, 그 별자리들에는 저마다 흔히 쓰이는 신비로운 기호들이 표시되어 있었으며, 쐐기돌처럼 박힌 태양은 천칭자리에서 추분점으로 들어서고 있었다."]], [["Before this equatorial coin, Ahab, not unobserved by others, was now pausing.", "이 적도의 금화 앞에서 에이해브는, 다른 이들의 눈을 피하지 못한 채, 지금 걸음을 멈추고 있었다."]], [["“There’s something ever egotistical in mountain-tops and towers, and all other grand and lofty things; look here,—three peaks as proud as Lucifer.", "\"산봉우리와 탑, 그리고 다른 모든 웅대하고 드높은 것들에는 언제나 어딘가 자기중심적인 무언가가 깃들어 있다. 보라."], ["The firm tower, that is Ahab; the volcano, that is Ahab; the courageous, the undaunted, and victorious fowl, that, too, is Ahab; all are Ahab; and this round gold is but the image of the rounder globe, which, like a magician’s glass, to each and every man in turn but mirrors back his own mysterious self.", "루시퍼처럼 오만한 세 봉우리를. 저 굳건한 탑, 그것이 에이해브다."], ["Great pains, small gains for those who ask the world to solve them; it cannot solve itself.", "저 화산, 그것이 에이해브다. 용감하고 굴하지 않으며 승리에 찬 저 새, 그것 또한 에이해브다."], ["Methinks now this coined sun wears a ruddy face; but see!", "모두가 에이해브다. 그리고 이 둥근 금은 더 둥근 이 지구의 상(像)에 지나지 않으니, 그것은 마법사의 거울처럼 모든 사람에게 차례로 저마다 자신의 신비로운 자아를 비쳐 줄 뿐이다."], ["aye, he enters the sign of storms, the equinox!", "세상더러 자기들을 풀어 달라고 청하는 자들에게는 큰 수고에 작은 소득뿐이다. 세상은 제 자신조차 풀지 못하니까."], ["and but six months before he wheeled out of a former equinox at Aries!", "이제 보니 이 새겨진 태양은 발그레한 얼굴을 하고 있구나. 그러나 보라!"], ["From storm to storm! So be it, then.", "그렇다, 그가 폭풍의 별자리, 곧 추분점으로 들어서는구나!"], ["Born in throes, ’tis fit that man should live in pains and die in pangs!", "그리고 불과 여섯 달 전에는 양자리에서 또 다른 추분을 돌아 나왔거늘! 폭풍에서 폭풍으로!"], ["So be it, then!", "그렇다면, 그러하라."], ["Here’s stout stuff for woe to work on.", "진통 속에서 태어났으니, 사람이 고통 속에서 살고 격통 속에서 죽는 것이 마땅하지 않으랴! 그렇다면, 그러하라!"], ["So be it, then.”", "여기 슬픔이 갈아 낼 단단한 재료가 있구나. 그렇다면, 그러하라.\""]], [["“No fairy fingers can have pressed the gold, but devil’s claws must have left their mouldings there since yesterday,”", "\"어떤 요정의 손가락도 이 금을 누른 적은 없으리라. 다만 어제부터 악마의 발톱이 거기에 제 자국을 남겼음에 틀림없다.\""], ["murmured Starbuck to himself, leaning against the bulwarks.", "스타벅이 뱃전에 기댄 채 혼자 중얼거렸다."], ["“The old man seems to read Belshazzar’s awful writing.", "\"노인은 벨사살의 무서운 글귀를 읽고 있는 듯하군."], ["I have never marked the coin inspectingly.", "나는 한 번도 저 금화를 유심히 들여다본 적이 없었지."], ["He goes below; let me read.", "그가 아래로 내려가는구나."], ["A dark valley between three mighty, heaven-abiding peaks, that almost seem the Trinity, in some faint earthly symbol.", "내가 한번 읽어 보자. 하늘에 머무는 세 거대한 봉우리 사이에 놓인 어두운 골짜기라, 어떤 희미한 지상의 상징을 통해 거의 삼위일체처럼 보이는구나."], ["So in this vale of Death, God girds us round; and over all our gloom, the sun of Righteousness still shines a beacon and a hope.", "그러니 이 죽음의 골짜기에서 하느님은 우리를 에워싸시고, 우리의 온갖 어둠 위로 의(義)의 태양은 여전히 등불과 희망으로 빛나신다."], ["If we bend down our eyes, the dark vale shows her mouldy soil; but if we lift them, the bright sun meets our glance half way, to cheer.", "우리가 눈을 내리뜨면 그 어두운 골짜기는 곰팡내 나는 흙을 드러내 보이지만, 우리가 눈을 들어 올리면 저 밝은 태양이 길 중턱까지 마중 나와 우리를 위로한다."], ["Yet, oh, the great sun is no fixture; and if, at midnight, we would fain snatch some sweet solace from him, we gaze for him in vain!", "그러나 아, 저 위대한 태양은 붙박이가 아니로다. 한밤중에 우리가 그에게서 어떤 달콤한 위안을 낚아채려 한들, 우리는 헛되이 그를 찾아 헤맬 뿐이다!"], ["This coin speaks wisely, mildly, truly, but still sadly to me.", "이 금화는 내게 지혜롭게, 부드럽게, 진실되게, 그러나 여전히 슬프게 말을 건넨다."], ["I will quit it, lest Truth shake me falsely.”", "진리가 나를 그릇되게 뒤흔들기 전에, 나는 이것을 떠나리라.\""]], [["“There now’s the old Mogul,”", "\"저기 늙은 몽골 대왕께서 계시는구먼.\" 스터브가 시유(試油) 가마 곁에서 혼잣말을 했다."], ["soliloquized Stubb by the try-works, “he’s been twigging it; and there goes Starbuck from the same, and both with faces which I should say might be somewhere within nine fathoms long.", "\"한참을 들여다보셨겠다. 그리고 저기 스타벅도 똑같은 데서 떠나가는데, 둘 다 얼굴이 어찌나 길쭉한지, 내 보기엔 어딘가 아홉 길은 됨직하구나."], ["And all from looking at a piece of gold, which did I have it now on Negro Hill or in Corlaer’s Hook, I’d not look at it very long ere spending it.", "이게 다 금붙이 하나를 들여다본 탓이라니. 만약 내가 지금 그걸 니그로 힐이나 콜리어스 훅에서 손에 넣었다면, 오래 들여다볼 새도 없이 써 버렸을 텐데."], ["Humph!", "흥!"], ["in my poor, insignificant opinion, I regard this as queer.", "변변찮고 하찮은 내 생각으로는, 이건 참 별나단 말이지. 나도 항해하면서 전에 금화를 본 적이 있다."], ["I have seen doubloons before now in my voyagings; your doubloons of old Spain, your doubloons of Peru, your doubloons of Chili, your doubloons of Bolivia, your doubloons of Popayan; with plenty of gold moidores and pistoles, and joes, and half joes, and quarter joes.", "옛 스페인의 금화, 페루의 금화, 칠레의 금화, 볼리비아의 금화, 포파얀의 금화 말이다. 게다가 금 모이도르며 피스톨, 조, 반 조, 사분의 일 조도 잔뜩 보았지."], ["What then should there be in this doubloon of the Equator that is so killing wonderful?", "그런데 도대체 이 적도의 금화 속에 뭐가 들었기에 이토록 죽여주게 놀랍단 말인가?"], ["By Golconda!", "골콘다에 걸고!"], ["let me read it once.", "어디 한번 읽어 보자."], ["Halloa!", "어럽쇼!"], ["here’s signs and wonders truly!", "여기 정말 표징과 이적이 있구나!"], ["That, now, is what old Bowditch in his Epitome calls the zodiac, and what my almanac below calls ditto.", "이건 옹졸한 보디치 영감이 자기 항해 요람에서 황도대라 부르고, 아래에 둔 내 책력에서도 똑같이 부르는 것이렷다."], ["I’ll get the almanac and as I have heard devils can be raised with Daboll’s arithmetic, I’ll try my hand at raising a meaning out of these queer curvicues here with the Massachusetts calendar.", "책력을 가져와야겠다. 다볼의 산수책으로 악마도 불러낼 수 있다는 말을 들은 적이 있으니, 매사추세츠 책력을 들고 이 별난 꼬불꼬불한 무늬에서 무슨 뜻 하나를 불러내 봐야겠다."], ["Here’s the book.", "여기 책이 있군."], ["Let’s see now.", "자, 어디 보자."], ["Signs and wonders; and the sun, he’s always among ’em.", "표징과 이적이라, 그리고 태양, 그놈은 늘 그 사이에 끼어 있단 말이지. 흠, 흠, 흠."], ["Hem, hem, hem; here they are—here they go—all alive:—Aries, or the Ram; Taurus, or the Bull and Jimimi!", "여기들 있구나—여기들 가는구나—다들 살아 펄떡인다. 양자리, 곧 숫양."], ["here’s Gemini himself, or the Twins.", "황소자리, 곧 황소. 그리고 어럽쇼!"], ["Well; the sun he wheels among ’em.", "여기 제 모습 그대로 쌍둥이자리, 곧 쌍둥이가 있구나."], ["Aye, here on the coin he’s just crossing the threshold between two of twelve sitting-rooms all in a ring.", "자, 태양은 그놈들 사이를 빙빙 돈다. 그래, 여기 금화에서 그놈은 막 둥글게 둘러앉은 열두 개 거실 가운데 두 곳 사이의 문턱을 넘고 있구나."], ["Book!", "책아!"], ["you lie there; the fact is, you books must know your places.", "너는 거기 누워 있어라. 사실 너희 책이란 것들은 제 분수를 알아야 한단 말이다."], ["You’ll do to give us the bare words and facts, but we come in to supply the thoughts.", "너희는 우리에게 알맹이 없는 말과 사실이나 일러 주면 그뿐, 거기에 생각을 보태는 건 우리 몫이지. 적어도 매사추세츠 책력이며 보디치의 항해서며 다볼의 산수책에 관한 한, 그게 내 변변찮은 경험이다."], ["That’s my small experience, so far as the Massachusetts calendar, and Bowditch’s navigator, and Daboll’s arithmetic go.", "표징과 이적이라고? 표징에 놀라울 게 하나도 없고, 이적에 뜻깊을 게 하나도 없다면 그것참 딱한 노릇이지!"], ["Signs and wonders, eh?", "어딘가에 실마리가 있다. 잠깐만."], ["Pity if there is nothing wonderful in signs, and significant in wonders!", "쉿—들어 봐! 제우스에 걸고, 알았다!"], ["There’s a clue somewhere; wait a bit; hist—hark!", "자 봐라, 금화야, 여기 네 황도대는 둥근 한 장(章) 속에 담긴 인간의 일생이로구나. 자, 이제 내가 책에서 곧장 읽어 주마."], ["By Jove, I have it!", "오너라, 책력아! 자 시작이다."], ["Look you, Doubloon, your zodiac here is the life of man in one round chapter; and now I’ll read it off, straight out of the book.", "여기 양자리, 곧 숫양이 있으니—음탕한 놈이 우리를 낳는다. 그다음엔 황소자리, 곧 황소가—맨 처음으로 우리를 들이받는다."], ["Come, Almanack!", "그다음 쌍둥이자리, 곧 쌍둥이는—말하자면 미덕과 악덕이렷다. 우리가 미덕에 닿으려 애쓰는데, 보라!"], ["To begin: there’s Aries, or the Ram—lecherous dog, he begets us; then, Taurus, or the Bull—he bumps us the first thing; then Gemini, or the Twins—that is, Virtue and Vice; we try to reach Virtue, when lo!", "게자리, 곧 게가 나타나 우리를 도로 끌어당긴다. 그리고 여기, 미덕에서 멀어져 가노라면, 사자자리, 곧 으르렁대는 사자가 길목에 도사리고 있다가—사나운 이빨로 몇 번 물어뜯고 앞발로 심술궂게 후려친다."], ["comes Cancer the Crab, and drags us back; and here, going from Virtue, Leo, a roaring Lion, lies in the path—he gives a few fierce bites and surly dabs with his paw; we escape, and hail Virgo, the Virgin!", "우리가 가까스로 벗어나 처녀자리, 곧 처녀에게 인사를 건네니! 그것이 우리의 첫사랑이라."], ["that’s our first love; we marry and think to be happy for aye, when pop comes Libra, or the Scales—happiness weighed and found wanting; and while we are very sad about that, Lord!", "우리는 혼인하여 영영 행복하리라 여기는데, 불쑥 천칭자리, 곧 저울이 나타나—행복을 달아 보니 모자라더라. 그 일로 우리가 한껏 서글퍼하는 사이, 아이고!"], ["how we suddenly jump, as Scorpio, or the Scorpion, stings us in the rear; we are curing the wound, when whang come the arrows all round; Sagittarius, or the Archer, is amusing himself.", "우리가 어찌나 펄쩍 뛰는지, 전갈자리, 곧 전갈이 우리 등을 쏘아 댄다. 그 상처를 치료하고 있노라면, 휙휙 사방에서 화살이 날아든다."], ["As we pluck out the shafts, stand aside!", "궁수자리, 곧 사수가 제 흥에 겨워 노는 게다. 우리가 그 화살대를 뽑아내는데, 비켜라!"], ["here’s the battering-ram, Capricornus, or the Goat; full tilt, he comes rushing, and headlong we are tossed; when Aquarius, or the Water-bearer, pours out his whole deluge and drowns us; and to wind up with Pisces, or the Fishes, we sleep.", "여기 들이받는 숫양, 곧 염소자리, 곧 산양이 전속력으로 돌진해 오니, 우리는 곤두박질치며 나가떨어진다. 그러면 물병자리, 곧 물 긷는 자가 제 홍수를 죄다 쏟아부어 우리를 익사시킨다."], ["There’s a sermon now, writ in high heaven, and the sun goes through it every year, and yet comes out of it all alive and hearty.", "그리고 마지막을 장식하는 물고기자리, 곧 물고기와 더불어 우리는 잠이 든다. 자, 여기 드높은 하늘에 쓰인 한 편의 설교가 있고, 태양은 해마다 그것을 두루 거치면서도 매번 멀쩡히 살아 기운차게 빠져나온다."], ["Jollily he, aloft there, wheels through toil and trouble; and so, alow here, does jolly Stubb.", "저 위에서 그놈은 수고와 시련을 즐겁게 헤쳐 나가지. 그러니 여기 아래에서도 즐거운 스터브가 그렇게 한다."], ["Oh, jolly’s the word for aye!", "오, 즐거움이야말로 영원한 한마디로다!"], ["Adieu, Doubloon!", "잘 있거라, 금화야!"], ["But stop; here comes little King-Post; dodge round the try-works, now, and let’s hear what he’ll have to say.", "그런데 가만, 저기 꼬마 킹포스트가 오는구나. 자, 시유 가마 뒤로 슬쩍 비켜서서 저 친구가 무슨 말을 하는지 한번 들어 보자."], ["There; he’s before it; he’ll out with something presently.", "옳지, 그가 그 앞에 섰구나. 곧 무슨 말이든 내뱉겠지."], ["So, so; he’s beginning.”", "그래, 그래. 시작하는군.\""]], [["“I see nothing here, but a round thing made of gold, and whoever raises a certain whale, this round thing belongs to him.", "\"내 눈엔 여기 금으로 만든 둥근 물건 하나밖에 안 보이는데, 어떤 고래를 잡아내든 그놈을 잡은 자에게 이 둥근 물건이 돌아간다 이거지. 그런데 이 빤히 들여다보는 소동은 다 뭐람?"], ["So, what’s all this staring been about?", "이게 십육 달러 값어치라는 건 사실이야."], ["It is worth sixteen dollars, that’s true; and at two cents the cigar, that’s nine hundred and sixty cigars.", "그리고 시가 한 대에 두 푼이라 치면, 구백육십 대로구먼. 나야 스터브처럼 더러운 파이프는 안 피우지만 시가는 좋아하지."], ["I won’t smoke dirty pipes like Stubb, but I like cigars, and here’s nine hundred and sixty of them; so here goes Flask aloft to spy ’em out.”", "그러니 여기 구백육십 대가 있는 셈이렷다. 그러니 플래스크는 그놈들을 잡아내러 돛대 위로 올라가야겠다.\""]], [["“Shall I call that wise or foolish, now; if it be really wise it has a foolish look to it; yet, if it be really foolish, then has it a sort of wiseish look to it.", "\"저걸 두고 지혜롭다 해야 할지 어리석다 해야 할지. 정말로 지혜로운 거라면 어딘가 어리석어 보이고, 정말로 어리석은 거라면 또 어딘가 그럴듯이 지혜로워 보인단 말이지."], ["But, avast; here comes our old Manxman—the old hearse-driver, he must have been, that is, before he took to the sea.", "한데 가만 있자, 저기 늙은 맨섬 사람이 오는구나—저 늙은이는 바다로 나오기 전엔 영구차나 몰던 자였을 게 분명해. 저 친구가 금화 앞에서 바람을 거슬러 멈춰 서더니, 어럽쇼, 돛대 반대편으로 돌아가는군."], ["He luffs up before the doubloon; halloa, and goes round on the other side of the mast; why, there’s a horse-shoe nailed on that side; and now he’s back again; what does that mean?", "아, 그쪽엔 편자가 못 박혀 있지. 그러더니 이제 다시 돌아왔구나."], ["Hark!", "저게 무슨 뜻이람?"], ["he’s muttering—voice like an old worn-out coffee-mill.", "들어 봐! 뭔가 중얼거리는데—목소리가 낡아 빠진 커피 분쇄기 같구나."], ["Prick ears, and listen!”", "귀를 쫑긋 세우고 들어 보자!\""]], [["“If the White Whale be raised, it must be in a month and a day, when the sun stands in some one of these signs.", "\"흰 고래가 잡혀 나온다면, 그것은 태양이 이 별자리들 가운데 하나에 머무는 한 달하고 하루 안의 일이렷다."], ["I’ve studied signs, and know their marks; they were taught me two score years ago, by the old witch in Copenhagen.", "나는 별자리를 공부했고 그 표징을 안다. 마흔 해 전에 코펜하겐의 늙은 마녀에게 배웠지."], ["Now, in what sign will the sun then be?", "자, 그러면 그때 태양은 어느 별자리에 들겠는가?"], ["The horse-shoe sign; for there it is, right opposite the gold.", "편자 별자리렷다. 보라, 저기 금화 바로 맞은편에 있지 않은가."], ["And what’s the horse-shoe sign?", "그렇다면 편자 별자리란 무엇인가?"], ["The lion is the horse-shoe sign—the roaring and devouring lion.", "사자가 곧 편자 별자리렷다—으르렁대며 집어삼키는 사자 말이다."], ["Ship, old ship!", "배여, 늙은 배여!"], ["my old head shakes to think of thee.”", "네 생각을 하니 이 늙은 머리가 절로 흔들리는구나.\""]], [["“There’s another rendering now; but still one text.", "\"또 다른 풀이가 나왔군. 하지만 본문은 여전히 하나지."], ["All sorts of men in one kind of world, you see.", "보다시피 한 가지 세상 속에 온갖 부류의 사람이 다 있단 말이야."], ["Dodge again!", "또 비켜야겠다!"], ["here comes Queequeg—all tattooing—looks like the signs of the Zodiac himself.", "저기 퀴케그가 오는구나—온통 문신투성이라—제 몸 자체가 황도대의 표징처럼 보이는걸. 저 식인종이 뭐라는지 들어 볼까?"], ["What says the Cannibal?", "살아생전 처음 보는 일이지, 저 친구가 비교를 하고 있구나."], ["As I live he’s comparing notes; looking at his thigh bone; thinks the sun is in the thigh, or in the calf, or in the bowels, I suppose, as the old women talk Surgeon’s Astronomy in the back country.", "제 넓적다리뼈를 들여다보는걸. 아마 태양이 넓적다리에 들었거나, 종아리에, 아니면 창자 속에 들었거니 여기는 게지."], ["And by Jove, he’s found something there in the vicinity of his thigh—I guess it’s Sagittarius, or the Archer.", "시골 노파들이 외과 점성술을 떠벌리는 것처럼 말이야. 그리고 제우스에 걸고, 저 친구가 넓적다리께에서 뭔가를 찾아냈구나—내 짐작엔 궁수자리, 곧 사수렷다."], ["No: he don’t know what to make of the doubloon; he takes it for an old button off some king’s trowsers.", "아니야, 저 친구는 금화를 어찌 봐야 할지 모르는걸. 어느 왕의 바지에서 떨어져 나온 낡은 단추쯤으로 여기는 모양이군."], ["But, aside again!", "한데 또 옆으로 비키자!"], ["here comes that ghost-devil, Fedallah; tail coiled out of sight as usual, oakum in the toes of his pumps as usual.", "저기 그 유령 같은 악마, 페달라가 오는구나. 여느 때처럼 꼬리는 보이지 않게 사리고, 여느 때처럼 펌프스 발끝에는 뱃밥을 쑤셔 넣었군."], ["What does he say, with that look of his?", "저 친구가 저 눈빛으로 무슨 말을 하나?"], ["Ah, only makes a sign to the sign and bows himself; there is a sun on the coin—fire worshipper, depend upon it.", "아하, 그저 그 표징을 향해 신호를 보내며 절을 하는구나. 금화에는 태양이 있으니—저 친구는 분명 불을 숭배하는 자야."], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["more and more.", "점점 더하는군."], ["This way comes Pip—poor boy!", "이쪽으로 핍이 오는구나—가엾은 녀석!"], ["would he had died, or I; he’s half horrible to me.", "차라리 저 애가 죽었거나, 내가 죽었으면 좋았으련만. 저 애는 어쩐지 절반쯤 끔찍하단 말이야."], ["He too has been watching all of these interpreters—myself included—and look now, he comes to read, with that unearthly idiot face.", "저 애도 여태 이 해석자들을—나 자신까지 포함해서—죄다 지켜보고 있었구나. 자, 봐라, 저 세상 것 아닌 얼간이 같은 얼굴을 하고서 읽으러 오는구나."], ["Stand away again and hear him.", "또 비켜서서 저 애 말을 들어 보자."], ["Hark!”", "들어 봐!\""]], [["“I look, you look, he looks; we look, ye look, they look.”", "\"나는 본다, 너는 본다, 그는 본다. 우리는 본다, 너희는 본다, 그들은 본다.\""]], [["“Upon my soul, he’s been studying Murray’s Grammar!", "\"내 영혼을 걸고, 저 애가 머레이의 문법책을 공부했구나!"], ["Improving his mind, poor fellow!", "가엾은 녀석이 제 머리를 갈고닦았다니!"], ["But what’s that he says now—hist!”", "그런데 지금 뭐라는 거지—쉿!\""]], [["“I look, you look, he looks; we look, ye look, they look.”", "\"나는 본다, 너는 본다, 그는 본다. 우리는 본다, 너희는 본다, 그들은 본다.\""]], [["“Why, he’s getting it by heart—hist!", "\"아니, 저 애가 그걸 외우고 있구나—쉿!"], ["again.”", "또 한다.\""]], [["“I look, you look, he looks; we look, ye look, they look.”", "\"나는 본다, 너는 본다, 그는 본다. 우리는 본다, 너희는 본다, 그들은 본다.\""]], [["“Well, that’s funny.”", "\"흠, 거참 우습군.\""]], [["“And I, you, and he; and we, ye, and they, are all bats; and I’m a crow, especially when I stand a’top of this pine tree here.", "\"그리고 나와 너와 그, 우리와 너희와 그들은 죄다 박쥐다. 그리고 나는 까마귀로다, 특히 이 소나무 꼭대기에 올라설 때면 말이야."], ["Caw!", "까악!"], ["caw!", "까악!"], ["caw!", "까악!"], ["caw!", "까악!"], ["caw!", "까악!"], ["caw!", "까악!"], ["Ain’t I a crow?", "내가 까마귀가 아니더냐?"], ["And where’s the scare-crow?", "그렇다면 허수아비는 어디 있느냐?"], ["There he stands; two bones stuck into a pair of old trowsers, and two more poked into the sleeves of an old jacket.”", "저기 서 있구나. 뼈다귀 두 개를 낡은 바지 한 벌에 꽂아 넣고, 또 두 개를 낡은 웃옷 소매에 쑤셔 박았구나.\""]], [["“Wonder if he means me?—complimentary!—poor lad!—I could go hang myself.", "\"저게 날 두고 하는 말인가?—참 고마운 칭찬이로다!—가엾은 녀석!—내 차라리 목이라도 매고 싶구나."], ["Any way, for the present, I’ll quit Pip’s vicinity.", "어쨌든 지금은 핍 곁을 떠나야겠다."], ["I can stand the rest, for they have plain wits; but he’s too crazy-witty for my sanity.", "다른 자들이야 견딜 만하지, 그들 머리는 멀쩡하니까. 한데 저 애는 너무도 미친 듯 영악해서 멀쩡한 내 정신으로는 못 견디겠다."], ["So, so, I leave him muttering.”", "자, 그래, 저 애가 중얼거리도록 내버려 두고 떠나자.\""]], [["“Here’s the ship’s navel, this doubloon here, and they are all on fire to unscrew it.", "\"여기 이 금화는 배의 배꼽이렷다. 그런데 다들 그걸 풀어내려고 안달이 났구나."], ["But, unscrew your navel, and what’s the consequence?", "하지만 네 배꼽을 풀어 보아라. 그럼 무슨 결과가 따르겠느냐?"], ["Then again, if it stays here, that is ugly, too, for when aught’s nailed to the mast it’s a sign that things grow desperate.", "그렇다고 또 그게 여기 그대로 박혀 있는 것도 흉하긴 매한가지지. 돛대에 무언가가 못 박혀 있다는 건 사정이 절박해진다는 표징이니까."], ["Ha, ha!", "하하!"], ["old Ahab!", "늙은 에이해브!"], ["the White Whale; he’ll nail ye!", "흰 고래가 너를 못 박아 버릴 게다!"], ["This is a pine tree.", "이건 소나무로다."], ["My father, in old Tolland county, cut down a pine tree once, and found a silver ring grown over in it; some old darkey’s wedding ring.", "우리 아버지가 옛 톨랜드 카운티에서 한번 소나무를 베었는데, 그 안에 은반지가 살처럼 감싸여 박혀 있는 걸 찾아냈지. 어느 늙은 흑인의 결혼반지였어."], ["How did it get there?", "그게 어쩌다 거기 들어갔을까?"], ["And so they’ll say in the resurrection, when they come to fish up this old mast, and find a doubloon lodged in it, with bedded oysters for the shaggy bark.", "그래서 부활의 날에도 그들은 그리 말하겠지. 이 낡은 돛대를 낚아 올렸다가 그 안에 박힌 금화를 찾아내고, 거친 나무껍질엔 굴들이 들러붙어 있는 걸 보고서 말이야."], ["Oh, the gold!", "오, 금이여!"], ["the precious, precious, gold!", "그 귀하고 귀한 금이여!"], ["the green miser’ll hoard ye soon!", "머지않아 저 푸른 구두쇠가 너를 그러모아 쌓아 두겠지!"], ["Hish!", "쉿!"], ["hish!", "쉿!"], ["God goes ’mong the worlds blackberrying.", "하느님은 세상들 사이를 거니시며 산딸기를 따 모으신다."], ["Cook!", "요리사!"], ["ho, cook!", "어이, 요리사!"], ["and cook us!", "우리를 요리해 다오!"], ["Jenny!", "제니!"], ["hey, hey, hey, hey, hey, Jenny, Jenny!", "어이, 어이, 어이, 어이, 어이, 제니, 제니!"], ["and get your hoe-cake done!”", "어서 옥수수빵이나 구워 다오!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 100. Leg and Arm.", "# CHAPTER 100. Leg and Arm."]], [["The Pequod, of Nantucket, Meets the Samuel Enderby, of London.", "다리와 팔. 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 피쿼드(Pequod)호, 런던의 새뮤얼 엔더비(Samuel Enderby)호를 만나다."]], [["“Ship, ahoy!", "\"이봐, 거기 배!"], ["Hast seen the White Whale?”", "흰 고래(White Whale)를 본 적 있는가?\""]], [["So cried Ahab, once more hailing a ship showing English colours, bearing down under the stern.", "에이해브(Ahab)가 그렇게 외쳤다. 영국 깃발을 단 배 한 척이 고물 쪽으로 바싹 다가오는 것을 보고 또 한 번 부른 것이다."], ["Trumpet to mouth, the old man was standing in his hoisted quarter-boat, his ivory leg plainly revealed to the stranger captain, who was carelessly reclining in his own boat’s bow.", "나팔을 입에 댄 채, 노인은 위로 들어 올린 고물 보트 안에 서 있었으며, 그의 상아 다리가 낯선 선장의 눈에 또렷이 드러났다. 그 선장은 제 보트의 뱃머리에 무심하게 몸을 기대고 있었다."], ["He was a darkly-tanned, burly, good-natured, fine-looking man, of sixty or thereabouts, dressed in a spacious roundabout, that hung round him in festoons of blue pilot-cloth; and one empty arm of this jacket streamed behind him like the broidered arm of a hussar’s surcoat.", "그는 햇볕에 거뭇하게 그을린, 건장하고 너그러우며 풍채 좋은 예순 안팎의 사내로, 파란 방수천을 휘장처럼 늘어뜨린 헐렁한 짧은 외투를 입고 있었다. 그 웃옷의 한쪽 빈 소매가 마치 경기병 군복의 수놓은 팔처럼 그의 등 뒤로 펄럭였다."]], [["“Hast seen the White Whale?”", "\"흰 고래를 본 적 있는가?\""]], [["“See you this?”", "\"이걸 보았소?\""], ["and withdrawing it from the folds that had hidden it, he held up a white arm of sperm whale bone, terminating in a wooden head like a mallet.", "그가 그것을 감추고 있던 옷자락에서 끌어내며, 망치 같은 나무 머리로 끝이 마무리된 향유고래 뼈로 된 새하얀 팔을 치켜들었다."]], [["“Man my boat!”", "\"내 보트에 사람을 붙여라!\""], ["cried Ahab, impetuously, and tossing about the oars near him—“Stand by to lower!”", "에이해브가 격하게 외치며 곁에 있던 노들을 마구 들썩였다. \"내릴 준비를 하라!\""]], [["In less than a minute, without quitting his little craft, he and his crew were dropped to the water, and were soon alongside of the stranger.", "채 일 분도 지나지 않아, 그는 제 작은 배에서 내리지 않은 채 선원들과 함께 물 위로 떨어졌고, 곧 낯선 배의 곁에 다다랐다."], ["But here a curious difficulty presented itself.", "그러나 여기서 묘한 난관이 나타났다."], ["In the excitement of the moment, Ahab had forgotten that since the loss of his leg he had never once stepped on board of any vessel at sea but his own, and then it was always by an ingenious and very handy mechanical contrivance peculiar to the Pequod, and a thing not to be rigged and shipped in any other vessel at a moment’s warning.", "그 순간의 흥분 속에서, 에이해브는 다리를 잃은 이후로 바다에서 제 배가 아닌 어떤 배에도 단 한 번 올라 본 적이 없다는 사실을 잊고 있었다. 게다가 그가 오를 때면 늘 피쿼드호에만 있는 어떤 기발하고 매우 편리한 기계 장치를 썼는데, 그것은 다른 어떤 배에서도 즉석에서 설치하고 갖출 수 있는 물건이 아니었다."], ["Now, it is no very easy matter for anybody—except those who are almost hourly used to it, like whalemen—to clamber up a ship’s side from a boat on the open sea; for the great swells now lift the boat high up towards the bulwarks, and then instantaneously drop it half way down to the kelson.", "그런데 망망대해에서 보트로부터 뱃전을 기어오르는 일은, 거의 시도 때도 없이 그 일에 익숙한 고래잡이가 아니라면 누구에게나 결코 쉬운 일이 아니다. 거대한 너울이 보트를 뱃전 가까이로 한껏 들어 올렸다가, 다음 순간 곧장 용골(龍骨) 가까이까지 절반쯤 떨어뜨리기 때문이다."], ["So, deprived of one leg, and the strange ship of course being altogether unsupplied with the kindly invention, Ahab now found himself abjectly reduced to a clumsy landsman again; hopelessly eyeing the uncertain changeful height he could hardly hope to attain.", "그리하여 한쪽 다리를 잃은 데다, 낯선 배에는 물론 그 친절한 발명품이 전혀 갖춰져 있지 않았으니, 에이해브는 이제 자신이 다시금 비참하게도 서투른 뭍사람 신세로 전락한 것을 깨달았다. 그는 도무지 닿을 가망조차 보이지 않는, 종잡을 수 없이 출렁이는 높이를 절망스레 바라볼 뿐이었다."]], [["It has before been hinted, perhaps, that every little untoward circumstance that befell him, and which indirectly sprang from his luckless mishap, almost invariably irritated or exasperated Ahab.", "앞서 어렴풋이 내비친 바 있을 것이다. 그에게 닥치는 온갖 사소한 거북한 사정, 그리고 그의 불운한 사고에서 간접적으로 비롯된 일은 거의 어김없이 에이해브를 짜증나게 하거나 격분케 했다는 것을."], ["And in the present instance, all this was heightened by the sight of the two officers of the strange ship, leaning over the side, by the perpendicular ladder of nailed cleets there, and swinging towards him a pair of tastefully-ornamented man-ropes; for at first they did not seem to bethink them that a one-legged man must be too much of a cripple to use their sea bannisters.", "그리고 지금의 경우에도, 낯선 배의 두 항해사가 못으로 박은 발판으로 된 수직 사다리 옆에서 뱃전 너머로 몸을 기울인 채, 그를 향해 멋스럽게 장식된 한 쌍의 밧줄 손잡이를 흔들어 내미는 광경에 그 모든 짜증이 더욱 도드라졌다. 처음에 그들은 외다리 사내가 그들의 뱃전 난간을 쓰기에는 너무 불편한 몸이라는 것을 미처 생각하지 못한 듯했다."], ["But this awkwardness only lasted a minute, because the strange captain, observing at a glance how affairs stood, cried out, “I see, I see!—avast heaving there!", "그러나 그 어색함은 일 분밖에 가지 않았으니, 낯선 선장이 한눈에 사정을 알아채고 이렇게 외친 까닭이었다. \"알겠소, 알겠소!"], ["Jump, boys, and swing over the cutting-tackle.”", "거기 끌어 올리는 것 멈춰라! 자네들, 어서 뛰어가 절단용 도르래를 휘둘러 넘겨라.\""]], [["As good luck would have it, they had had a whale alongside a day or two previous, and the great tackles were still aloft, and the massive curved blubber-hook, now clean and dry, was still attached to the end.", "운 좋게도, 그들은 하루나 이틀 전에 고래 한 마리를 뱃전에 매어 두었던 터라, 그 큼직한 도르래들이 아직도 높이 매달려 있었고, 이제 깨끗하게 마른 그 묵직한 곡선의 지방 갈고리가 여전히 그 끝에 달려 있었다. 이것이 재빨리 에이해브에게 내려졌고, 그는 단번에 모든 것을 알아차리고는 자신의 외다리 허벅지를 갈고리의 곡선 안으로 밀어 넣었다."], ["This was quickly lowered to Ahab, who at once comprehending it all, slid his solitary thigh into the curve of the hook (it was like sitting in the fluke of an anchor, or the crotch of an apple tree), and then giving the word, held himself fast, and at the same time also helped to hoist his own weight, by pulling hand-over-hand upon one of the running parts of the tackle.", "그것은 마치 닻의 갈고리 끝에 앉거나 사과나무의 갈라진 가지에 걸터앉는 것과 같았다. 그러고는 신호를 보내 단단히 몸을 지탱하면서, 동시에 도르래의 움직이는 줄 한 가닥을 두 손으로 번갈아 당겨 제 무게를 끌어 올리는 일을 거들었다."], ["Soon he was carefully swung inside the high bulwarks, and gently landed upon the capstan head.", "곧 그는 높은 뱃전 안쪽으로 조심스럽게 흔들려 들어와, 양묘기 머리 위에 사뿐히 내려섰다. 다른 선장은 상아 팔을 환영하듯 거리낌 없이 앞으로 내밀며 다가왔고, 에이해브는 상아 다리를 내밀고 상아 팔을 (마치 두 자루의 황새치 칼날처럼) 맞대며, 그 바다코끼리 같은 투박한 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["With his ivory arm frankly thrust forth in welcome, the other captain advanced, and Ahab, putting out his ivory leg, and crossing the ivory arm (like two sword-fish blades) cried out in his walrus way, “Aye, aye, hearty!", "\"오, 그래, 반갑소! 우리 뼈끼리 한번 맞잡아 봅시다!"], ["let us shake bones together!—an arm and a leg!—an arm that never can shrink, d’ye see; and a leg that never can run.", "팔 하나와 다리 하나로 말이오! 보다시피 결코 움츠러들 줄 모르는 팔이요, 결코 달아날 줄 모르는 다리요."], ["Where did’st thou see the White Whale?—how long ago?”", "어디서 흰 고래를 보았소? 얼마나 오래전이오?\""]], [["“The White Whale,”", "\"흰 고래 말이오.\""], ["said the Englishman, pointing his ivory arm towards the East, and taking a rueful sight along it, as if it had been a telescope; “there I saw him, on the Line, last season.”", "영국인이 상아 팔을 동쪽으로 가리키며, 그것이 망원경이라도 되는 양 그 너머로 처량한 시선을 던지며 말했다. \"지난 철에, 적도 부근에서 그놈을 보았소.\""]], [["“And he took that arm off, did he?”", "\"그래서 그놈이 그 팔을 떼어 갔단 말이오?\""], ["asked Ahab, now sliding down from the capstan, and resting on the Englishman’s shoulder, as he did so.", "에이해브가 물으며, 양묘기에서 미끄러져 내려와 그러면서 영국인의 어깨에 몸을 기댔다."]], [["“Aye, he was the cause of it, at least; and that leg, too?”", "\"그렇소, 적어도 그놈이 원인이었지. 저 다리도 그렇소?\""]], [["“Spin me the yarn,”", "\"그 이야기를 풀어 보시오.\""], ["said Ahab; “how was it?”", "에이해브가 말했다. \"어찌 된 일이었소?\""]], [["“It was the first time in my life that I ever cruised on the Line,”", "\"내 평생 적도 부근을 항해한 것이 그때가 처음이었소.\""], ["began the Englishman.", "영국인이 입을 열었다."], ["“I was ignorant of the White Whale at that time.", "\"그때 나는 흰 고래에 관해 아무것도 몰랐소."], ["Well, one day we lowered for a pod of four or five whales, and my boat fastened to one of them; a regular circus horse he was, too, that went milling and milling round so, that my boat’s crew could only trim dish, by sitting all their sterns on the outer gunwale.", "자, 어느 날 우리는 네댓 마리쯤 되는 고래 떼를 보고 보트를 내렸는데, 내 보트가 그중 한 마리에 작살을 박았소. 그놈은 영락없는 곡마단 말처럼, 빙글빙글 돌고 또 돌아 대는 통에, 내 보트 선원들은 모두 엉덩이를 바깥쪽 뱃전에 걸치고 앉아야 겨우 균형을 잡을 수 있었소."], ["Presently up breaches from the bottom of the sea a bouncing great whale, with a milky-white head and hump, all crows’ feet and wrinkles.”", "그러자 곧 바다 밑바닥에서 펄쩍 솟구쳐 오르는 커다란 고래 한 마리가 있었는데, 머리와 혹이 젖빛처럼 새하얗고, 온통 까마귀 발자국 같은 주름이 잡혀 있었소.\""]], [["“It was he, it was he!”", "\"그놈이었군, 그놈이었어!\""], ["cried Ahab, suddenly letting out his suspended breath.", "에이해브가 참고 있던 숨을 별안간 토해 내며 외쳤다."]], [["“And harpoons sticking in near his starboard fin.”", "\"그리고 그놈 오른쪽 지느러미 근처에 작살들이 박혀 있었소.\""]], [["“Aye, aye—they were mine—my irons,”", "\"그래, 그래, 그건 내 것이었소, 내 작살이었지!\""], ["cried Ahab, exultingly—“but on!”", "에이해브가 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"어서 계속하시오!\""]], [["“Give me a chance, then,”", "\"그럼 좀 말할 틈을 주시오.\""], ["said the Englishman, good-humoredly.", "영국인이 너그러운 투로 말했다."], ["“Well, this old great-grandfather, with the white head and hump, runs all afoam into the pod, and goes to snapping furiously at my fast-line!", "\"자, 이 늙은 증조할아비 같은 놈은, 흰 머리와 혹을 하고서, 온통 거품을 일으키며 고래 떼 속으로 돌진하더니, 내 작살 줄을 사납게 물어뜯기 시작하는 거요!\""]], [["“Aye, I see!—wanted to part it; free the fast-fish—an old trick—I know him.”", "\"그렇지, 알겠소! 줄을 끊어 작살 박힌 고래를 풀어 주려 했군. 그건 오래된 수법이지. 내 그놈을 잘 알아.\""]], [["“How it was exactly,” continued the one-armed commander, “I do not know; but in biting the line, it got foul of his teeth, caught there somehow; but we didn’t know it then; so that when we afterwards pulled on the line, bounce we came plump on to his hump! instead of the other whale’s; that went off to windward, all fluking. Seeing how matters stood, and what a noble great whale it was—the noblest and biggest I ever saw, sir, in my life—I resolved to capture him, spite of the boiling rage he seemed to be in. And thinking the hap-hazard line would get loose, or the tooth it was tangled to might draw (for I have a devil of a boat’s crew for a pull on a whale-line); seeing all this, I say, I jumped into my first mate’s boat—Mr. Mounttop’s here (by the way, Captain—Mounttop; Mounttop—the captain);—as I was saying, I jumped into Mounttop’s boat, which, d’ye see, was gunwale and gunwale with mine, then; and snatching the first harpoon, let this old great-grandfather have it. But, Lord, look you, sir—hearts and souls alive, man—the next instant, in a jiff, I was blind as a bat—both eyes out—all befogged and bedeadened with black foam—the whale’s tail looming straight up out of it, perpendicular in the air, like a marble steeple. No use sterning all, then; but as I was groping at midday, with a blinding sun, all crown-jewels; as I was groping, I say, after the second iron, to toss it overboard—down comes the tail like a Lima tower, cutting my boat in two, leaving each half in splinters; and, flukes first, the white hump backed through the wreck, as though it was all chips. We all struck out. To escape his terrible flailings, I seized hold of my harpoon-pole sticking in him, and for a moment clung to that like a sucking fish. But a combing sea dashed me off, and at the same instant, the fish, taking one good dart forwards, went down like a flash; and the barb of that cursed second iron towing along near me caught me here” (clapping his hand just below his shoulder); “yes, caught me just here, I say, and bore me down to Hell’s flames, I was thinking; when, when, all of a sudden, thank the good God, the barb ript its way along the flesh—clear along the whole length of my arm—came out nigh my wrist, and up I floated;—and that gentleman there will tell you the rest (by the way, captain—Dr. Bunger, ship’s surgeon: Bunger, my lad,—the captain). Now, Bunger boy, spin your part of the yarn.”", "\"정확히 어찌 된 일인지는,\" 외팔의 선장이 계속했다. \"나도 모르겠소. 다만 줄을 물어뜯는 와중에, 그것이 그놈 이빨에 엉켜 어찌어찌 거기 걸려 버린 거요. 하지만 그때 우리는 그걸 몰랐소. 그래서 우리가 나중에 줄을 당겼을 때, 다른 고래가 아니라 그놈의 혹 위로 그대로 쾅 부딪쳐 버린 거요! 그 다른 고래는 꼬리를 휘저으며 바람 부는 쪽으로 달아나 버렸지. 사정이 어찌 되었는지, 그리고 그것이 얼마나 고귀하고 거대한 고래인지를 보고서 — 선생, 내 평생 본 것 중 가장 고귀하고 가장 큰 놈이었소 — 나는 그놈이 들끓는 분노에 차 있는 듯 보였음에도 그놈을 잡기로 마음먹었소. 그리고 그 위태롭게 걸린 줄이 풀려 버리거나, 그것이 엉킨 이빨이 뽑혀 버릴지도 모른다고 생각했소(내 보트 선원들이 고래 줄을 당기는 데는 지독히 거센 놈들이었으니까). 이 모든 사정을 보고서, 그러니까 말이오, 나는 일등 항해사의 보트로 뛰어들었소 — 여기 마운트톱(Mounttop) 씨의 보트로 말이오(그건 그렇고, 선장 양반 — 이쪽은 마운트톱이오. 마운트톱, 이쪽은 선장님일세). 말한 대로, 나는 마운트톱의 보트로 뛰어들었는데, 보다시피 그때 그 보트는 내 보트와 뱃전을 나란히 맞대고 있었소. 그래서 첫 번째 작살을 움켜쥐고, 이 늙은 증조할아비 같은 놈에게 한 방 먹였소. 그런데, 세상에, 보시오, 선생 — 살아 있는 영혼들이여 — 바로 다음 순간, 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 나는 박쥐처럼 앞을 못 보게 되었소. 두 눈이 다 멀어 버린 거요. 온통 검은 거품에 휩싸여 어찌할 바를 모르게 되었고, 그 속에서 고래 꼬리가 마치 대리석 첨탑처럼 곧추서서 허공으로 솟아오르는 거요. 그러니 모두 뒤로 물러나려 한들 소용이 없었지. 한낮에, 눈부신 햇빛이 온통 왕관 보석처럼 쏟아지는 가운데, 나는 더듬거리며 — 그러니까, 말한 대로, 두 번째 작살을 더듬어 찾아 바다로 던지려 하고 있었는데 — 그때 그 꼬리가 리마의 탑처럼 내리꽂혀, 내 보트를 두 동강 내고, 그 두 쪽을 산산조각 내 버렸소. 그러고는 꼬리부터 들이밀며, 그 하얀 혹이 마치 그것이 죄다 나뭇조각이라도 되는 양 잔해를 헤치고 뒷걸음쳐 빠져나갔소. 우리는 모두 헤엄쳐 빠져나오려 발버둥쳤소. 나는 그놈의 무시무시한 후려침을 피하려고 그놈에게 박혀 있는 내 작살 자루를 붙잡고서, 한순간 빨판상어처럼 거기 매달려 있었소. 그러나 부서지는 파도 한 줄기가 나를 떨쳐 냈고, 바로 그 순간 그 고래가 앞으로 한 번 세게 돌진하더니 번개처럼 물속으로 가라앉았소. 그리고 내 곁에서 끌려가던 그 저주받을 두 번째 작살의 미늘이 바로 여기를 걸어 버린 거요\"(그가 어깨 바로 아래를 손으로 탁 쳤다). \"그렇소, 바로 여기를 걸었단 말이오. 그리고 나를 지옥 불길 속으로 끌어내리는구나, 하고 나는 생각하고 있었소. 그런데, 그런데, 별안간, 자비로우신 하느님(God) 덕분에, 그 미늘이 살을 따라 쭉 찢고 나아가 — 내 팔의 길이를 따라 죄다 — 손목 가까이로 빠져나오면서, 나는 위로 떠올랐소. 그리고 나머지 이야기는 저기 저 신사 양반이 들려줄 거요(그건 그렇고, 선장 양반 — 이쪽은 벙거(Bunger) 박사, 우리 배의 의사라오. 벙거, 이 사람아, 이쪽은 선장님일세). 자, 벙거, 자네 몫의 이야기를 풀어 보게.\""]], [["The professional gentleman thus familiarly pointed out, had been all the time standing near them, with nothing specific visible, to denote his gentlemanly rank on board.", "이렇게 친근하게 가리켜진 그 전문직 신사는, 배에서의 신사다운 지위를 드러낼 만한 뚜렷한 표시 하나 없이, 내내 그들 곁에 서 있었다."], ["His face was an exceedingly round but sober one; he was dressed in a faded blue woollen frock or shirt, and patched trowsers; and had thus far been dividing his attention between a marlingspike he held in one hand, and a pill-box held in the other, occasionally casting a critical glance at the ivory limbs of the two crippled captains.", "그의 얼굴은 더없이 둥글지만 점잖은 편이었고, 그는 빛바랜 파란 모직 작업복 혹은 셔츠에 기운 바지를 입고 있었다. 그는 지금껏 한 손에 든 밧줄 송곳과 다른 손에 든 알약 갑 사이로 주의를 나누어 두고 있었으며, 이따금 두 불구가 된 선장들의 상아 팔다리에 비평하는 듯한 눈길을 던지곤 했다."], ["But, at his superior’s introduction of him to Ahab, he politely bowed, and straightway went on to do his captain’s bidding.", "그러나 자기 상관이 그를 에이해브에게 소개하자, 그는 정중하게 고개를 숙이고는 곧바로 선장의 분부를 이행하기 시작했다."]], [["“It was a shocking bad wound,”", "\"끔찍하게 심한 상처였소.\""], ["began the whale-surgeon; “and, taking my advice, Captain Boomer here, stood our old Sammy—”", "그 고래잡이 배의 의사가 입을 열었다. \"그래서 내 충고를 받아들여, 여기 부머(Boomer) 선장은 우리 늙은 새미(Sammy)를 몰아 —\""]], [["“Samuel Enderby is the name of my ship,”", "\"새뮤얼 엔더비가 내 배 이름이오.\""], ["interrupted the one-armed captain, addressing Ahab; “go on, boy.”", "외팔의 선장이 에이해브를 향해 말을 가로챘다. \"계속하게, 이 사람아.\""]], [["“Stood our old Sammy off to the northward, to get out of the blazing hot weather there on the Line.", "\"우리 늙은 새미를 북쪽으로 몰아, 적도의 그 타는 듯한 더위에서 벗어나려 했소. 하지만 소용없었지."], ["But it was no use—I did all I could; sat up with him nights; was very severe with him in the matter of diet—”", "나는 할 수 있는 모든 걸 다했소. 밤마다 그의 곁을 지키며 간호했고, 식이요법 문제에 있어서는 그에게 아주 엄격했소 —\""]], [["“Oh, very severe!”", "\"오, 아주 엄격했지!\""], ["chimed in the patient himself; then suddenly altering his voice, “Drinking hot rum toddies with me every night, till he couldn’t see to put on the bandages; and sending me to bed, half seas over, about three o’clock in the morning.", "환자 본인이 끼어들었다가, 별안간 목소리를 바꾸어 말했다. \"밤마다 나랑 같이 뜨거운 럼 토디를 마셔 대다가, 붕대를 어떻게 감아야 할지 보이지도 않을 지경이 되어서는, 새벽 세 시쯤 반쯤 취해 비틀거리며 나를 잠자리로 보냈단 말이오."], ["Oh, ye stars!", "오, 하늘의 별들이여!"], ["he sat up with me indeed, and was very severe in my diet.", "이 사람이 정말로 내 곁을 지키며 간호했고, 내 식단에 아주 엄격했다니."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["a great watcher, and very dietetically severe, is Dr. Bunger.", "대단한 간병인에다, 식이요법에 무척이나 엄격한 양반이라, 우리 벙거 박사는 말이오.(벙거, 이 개 같은 사람아, 한번 웃어 보게!"], ["(Bunger, you dog, laugh out!", "왜 안 웃나?"], ["why don’t ye? You know you’re a precious jolly rascal.) But, heave ahead, boy, I’d rather be killed by you than kept alive by any other man.”", "자네가 끔찍이도 유쾌한 악당이라는 걸 자네도 알면서.) 하지만, 계속하게, 이 사람아, 나는 다른 누구의 손에 목숨을 부지하느니 차라리 자네 손에 죽는 편이 낫겠네.\""]], [["“My captain, you must have ere this perceived, respected sir”—said the imperturbable godly-looking Bunger, slightly bowing to Ahab—“is apt to be facetious at times; he spins us many clever things of that sort. But I may as well say—en passant, as the French remark—that I myself—that is to say, Jack Bunger, late of the reverend clergy—am a strict total abstinence man; I never drink—”", "\"선장님은, 존경하는 선생, 진작에 알아채셨겠지만,\" 침착하고 경건해 보이는 벙거가 에이해브에게 가볍게 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"이따금 농을 하시는 분이라오. 우리한테 저런 식의 재치 있는 이야기를 많이 풀어 놓으시지요. 하지만 — 프랑스 사람들 말마따나 지나가는 김에(en passant) — 나 자신은, 그러니까 잭 벙거, 한때 목사 노릇을 하던 이 사람은, 철저한 절대 금주가라는 걸 말해 두는 게 좋겠소. 나는 결코 마시는 법이 없소 —\""]], [["“Water!” cried the captain; “he never drinks it; it’s a sort of fits to him; fresh water throws him into the hydrophobia; but go on—go on with the arm story.”", "\"물 말이오!\" 선장이 외쳤다. \"이 사람은 절대 물을 안 마시지. 물은 이 사람에게 일종의 발작 같은 거라, 맹물을 마시면 공수병에 걸린단 말이오. 하지만 계속하게 — 그 팔 이야기를 계속하게.\""]], [["“Yes, I may as well,” said the surgeon, coolly. “I was about observing, sir, before Captain Boomer’s facetious interruption, that spite of my best and severest endeavors, the wound kept getting worse and worse; the truth was, sir, it was as ugly gaping wound as surgeon ever saw; more than two feet and several inches long. I measured it with the lead line. In short, it grew black; I knew what was threatened, and off it came. But I had no hand in shipping that ivory arm there; that thing is against all rule”—pointing at it with the marlingspike—“that is the captain’s work, not mine; he ordered the carpenter to make it; he had that club-hammer there put to the end, to knock some one’s brains out with, I suppose, as he tried mine once. He flies into diabolical passions sometimes. Do ye see this dent, sir”—removing his hat, and brushing aside his hair, and exposing a bowl-like cavity in his skull, but which bore not the slightest scarry trace, or any token of ever having been a wound—“Well, the captain there will tell you how that came here; he knows.”", "\"그렇지요, 그러는 게 좋겠소.\" 의사가 차분하게 말했다. \"선생, 부머 선장의 농 섞인 방해가 있기 전에, 내가 말하려던 건, 나의 최선과 가장 엄격한 노력에도 불구하고 상처가 점점 더 나빠지기만 했다는 것이오. 사실 말이지요, 선생, 그것은 의사가 일찍이 본 것 중 가장 흉하게 벌어진 상처였소. 길이가 두 자에다 몇 인치가 더 되었지. 측연줄로 재 보았소. 한마디로, 그것이 까맣게 변했소. 나는 무슨 위험이 닥쳐오는지 알았고, 그래서 팔을 잘라 냈소. 하지만 저 상아 팔을 다는 일에는 내가 손을 댄 게 없소. 저런 건 온갖 규칙에 어긋나는 거요.\" (밧줄 송곳으로 그것을 가리키며) \"저건 선장이 한 일이지, 내가 한 게 아니오. 선장이 목수에게 만들라고 시킨 거요. 끝에다 저 곤봉 망치를 달게 한 건, 짐작건대 누군가의 머리통을 박살 내려는 속셈일 테지요. 한번은 내 머리를 그걸로 쳐 보려 했으니까. 이 양반은 가끔 악마 같은 광분에 빠진다오. 이 움푹 팬 자국이 보이오, 선생?\" (그가 모자를 벗고 머리카락을 헤쳐, 두개골에 사발처럼 움푹 팬 구멍을 드러냈다. 하지만 그것은 흉터의 흔적이라곤 조금도 없었고, 한 번이라도 상처였던 적이 있다는 어떤 표시도 없었다.) \"자, 저기 저 선장이 이게 어찌 생긴 건지 말해 줄 거요. 저 양반이 안다오.\""]], [["“No, I don’t,”", "\"아니, 난 모르네.\""], ["said the captain, “but his mother did; he was born with it.", "선장이 말했다. \"하지만 그의 어미는 알았겠지."], ["Oh, you solemn rogue, you—you Bunger!", "그는 그걸 갖고 태어났으니까."], ["was there ever such another Bunger in the watery world?", "오, 이 점잖은 척하는 악당아, 이 — 이 벙거 같으니! 이 물의 세상에 또 이런 벙거가 어디 있었겠소?"], ["Bunger, when you die, you ought to die in pickle, you dog; you should be preserved to future ages, you rascal.”", "벙거, 자네가 죽거든, 마땅히 소금물에 절여져 죽어야 하네, 이 개 같은 사람아. 후세를 위해 보존되어야 마땅하단 말일세, 이 악당아.\""]], [["“What became of the White Whale?”", "\"흰 고래는 어찌 되었소?\""], ["now cried Ahab, who thus far had been impatiently listening to this by-play between the two Englishmen.", "이제 에이해브가 외쳤다. 그는 지금껏 두 영국인 사이의 이 곁다리 수작을 안달하며 듣고 있던 참이었다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\" 외팔의 선장이 외쳤다."], ["cried the one-armed captain, “oh, yes!", "\"오, 그렇지! 자, 그놈이 물속으로 잠수한 뒤로, 우리는 한동안 그놈을 다시 보지 못했소."], ["Well; after he sounded, we didn’t see him again for some time; in fact, as I before hinted, I didn’t then know what whale it was that had served me such a trick, till some time afterwards, when coming back to the Line, we heard about Moby Dick—as some call him—and then I knew it was he.”", "사실, 앞서 넌지시 말했듯이, 나는 그때만 해도 내게 그런 장난을 친 게 무슨 고래인지 몰랐소. 그러다 한참 뒤에 다시 적도로 돌아왔을 때, 모비 딕(Moby Dick) — 어떤 이들은 그놈을 그렇게 부르더군 — 에 관한 이야기를 듣고 나서야, 비로소 그게 그놈이었다는 걸 알았소.\""]], [["“Did’st thou cross his wake again?”", "\"그놈의 지나간 자취를 다시 마주친 적이 있소?\""]], [["“Twice.”", "\"두 번.\""]], [["“But could not fasten?”", "\"하지만 작살을 박지는 못했고?\""]], [["“Didn’t want to try to: ain’t one limb enough?", "\"박아 보려고도 하지 않았소. 팔다리 하나 잃은 걸로 충분하지 않소?"], ["What should I do without this other arm?", "이 나머지 팔마저 없으면 난 어쩌란 말이오?"], ["And I’m thinking Moby Dick doesn’t bite so much as he swallows.”", "그리고 내 생각에 모비 딕은 무는 게 아니라 통째로 삼켜 버린단 말이오.\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["interrupted Bunger, “give him your left arm for bait to get the right.", "벙거가 끼어들었다. \"오른팔을 되찾으려고 왼팔을 미끼로 내주시구려."], ["Do you know, gentlemen”—very gravely and mathematically bowing to each Captain in succession—“Do you know, gentlemen, that the digestive organs of the whale are so inscrutably constructed by Divine Providence, that it is quite impossible for him to completely digest even a man’s arm?", "두 분, 아시오?\" (그가 두 선장에게 차례로 더없이 진지하고 수학적인 태도로 고개를 숙이며) \"두 분, 아시오, 고래의 소화 기관이 신의 섭리로 어찌나 헤아릴 수 없게 만들어졌는지, 사람의 팔 하나조차 그놈이 완전히 소화한다는 게 도무지 불가능하다는 걸?"], ["And he knows it too.", "그리고 그놈도 그걸 안단 말이오."], ["So that what you take for the White Whale’s malice is only his awkwardness.", "그러니 두 분이 흰 고래의 악의라고 여기는 것은 실은 그놈의 서투름일 뿐이오."], ["For he never means to swallow a single limb; he only thinks to terrify by feints.", "그놈은 결코 팔다리 하나도 삼킬 작정이 아니란 말이오. 다만 으르대는 시늉으로 겁을 주려는 것뿐이지요."], ["But sometimes he is like the old juggling fellow, formerly a patient of mine in Ceylon, that making believe swallow jack-knives, once upon a time let one drop into him in good earnest, and there it stayed for a twelvemonth or more; when I gave him an emetic, and he heaved it up in small tacks, d’ye see.", "하지만 가끔 그놈은, 예전에 실론(Ceylon)에서 내 환자였던 그 늙은 요술쟁이 같은 짓을 하기도 한다오. 그 사람은 잭나이프를 삼키는 척하다가, 한번은 정말로 하나를 제 속으로 떨어뜨려 버렸는데, 그게 일 년 남짓이나 거기 머물러 있었소."], ["No possible way for him to digest that jack-knife, and fully incorporate it into his general bodily system.", "그래서 내가 토하게 하는 약을 주었더니, 그게 자잘한 조각이 되어 게워져 나오더이다, 보시오. 그놈이 그 잭나이프를 소화시켜 제 몸 전체에 온전히 받아들일 방도란 도무지 없는 거요."], ["Yes, Captain Boomer, if you are quick enough about it, and have a mind to pawn one arm for the sake of the privilege of giving decent burial to the other, why in that case the arm is yours; only let the whale have another chance at you shortly, that’s all.”", "그렇소, 부머 선장, 만일 충분히 재빠르게 일을 처리하고, 한쪽 팔을 점잖게 묻어 줄 특권을 누리기 위해 나머지 한쪽 팔을 저당 잡힐 마음이 있다면, 그렇다면 그 팔은 선장 것이 되는 거요. 다만 머지않아 그 고래에게 선장을 한 번 더 노릴 기회를 주기만 하면 되는 거지, 그게 다요.\""]], [["“No, thank ye, Bunger,” said the English Captain, “he’s welcome to the arm he has, since I can’t help it, and didn’t know him then; but not to another one. No more White Whales for me; I’ve lowered for him once, and that has satisfied me. There would be great glory in killing him, I know that; and there is a ship-load of precious sperm in him, but, hark ye, he’s best let alone; don’t you think so, Captain?”—glancing at the ivory leg.", "\"아니, 사양하겠네, 벙거.\" 영국인 선장이 말했다. \"어차피 어쩔 도리도 없었고, 그때는 그놈을 알지도 못했으니, 그놈이 가져간 이 팔이야 그놈에게 줘 버린 셈 치겠소. 하지만 나머지 하나까지는 안 되오. 내겐 더 이상 흰 고래 따위는 없소. 한 번 그놈을 잡으려 보트를 내려 봤고, 그걸로 충분히 만족했소. 그놈을 죽이면 크나큰 영광이 따르리란 걸, 나도 잘 알지요. 그리고 그놈 안에는 배 한 척을 가득 채울 만큼의 귀한 향유가 들어 있소. 하지만, 잘 들으시오, 그놈은 그냥 내버려 두는 게 상책이오. 선장도 그리 생각하지 않소?\" (상아 다리를 흘긋 보면서.)"]], [["“He is.", "\"그렇소."], ["But he will still be hunted, for all that.", "하지만 그래도 그놈은 여전히 쫓길 거요."], ["What is best let alone, that accursed thing is not always what least allures.", "그냥 내버려 두는 게 상책인 것이라 해서, 그 저주받은 것이 늘 사람을 가장 덜 끌어당기는 법은 아니지."], ["He’s all a magnet!", "그놈은 온통 자석이오!"], ["How long since thou saw’st him last?", "그놈을 마지막으로 본 게 언제요?"], ["Which way heading?”", "어느 방향으로 가고 있었소?\""]], [["“Bless my soul, and curse the foul fiend’s,” cried Bunger, stoopingly walking round Ahab, and like a dog, strangely snuffing; “this man’s blood—bring the thermometer!—it’s at the boiling point!—his pulse makes these planks beat!—sir!”—taking a lancet from his pocket, and drawing near to Ahab’s arm.", "\"맙소사, 그리고 저 더러운 악마의 영혼에 저주를.\" 벙거가 몸을 구부린 채 에이해브 주위를 돌며, 마치 개처럼 기이하게 코를 킁킁대며 외쳤다. \"이 사람 피가 — 체온계를 가져오게! — 끓는점에 이르렀어! 이 사람 맥박이 이 갑판널을 두드리고 있다고! 선생!\" (그가 주머니에서 작은 칼을 꺼내, 에이해브의 팔 가까이로 다가서며.)"]], [["“Avast!”", "\"멈춰!\""], ["roared Ahab, dashing him against the bulwarks—“Man the boat!", "에이해브가 그를 뱃전에 내동댕이치며 호통쳤다. \"보트에 사람을 붙여라!"], ["Which way heading?”", "어느 방향으로 가고 있었소?\""]], [["“Good God!”", "\"이런 맙소사!\""], ["cried the English Captain, to whom the question was put.", "그 질문을 받은 영국인 선장이 외쳤다."], ["“What’s the matter?", "\"도대체 무슨 일이오?"], ["He was heading east, I think.—Is your Captain crazy?”", "그놈은 동쪽으로 가고 있었던 것 같소. 당신네 선장이 미친 거요?\""], ["whispering Fedallah.", "그가 페달라(Fedallah)에게 속삭였다."]], [["But Fedallah, putting a finger on his lip, slid over the bulwarks to take the boat’s steering oar, and Ahab, swinging the cutting-tackle towards him, commanded the ship’s sailors to stand by to lower.", "그러나 페달라는 손가락 하나를 입술에 갖다 댄 채 뱃전을 넘어 미끄러져 내려가 보트의 키잡이 노를 잡았고, 에이해브는 절단용 도르래를 제 쪽으로 흔들어 끌어당기며, 그 배의 선원들에게 내릴 준비를 하라고 명령했다."]], [["In a moment he was standing in the boat’s stern, and the Manilla men were springing to their oars.", "순식간에 그는 보트의 고물에 서 있었고, 마닐라 사람들은 노를 향해 뛰어들고 있었다."], ["In vain the English Captain hailed him.", "영국인 선장이 그를 불러 보았으나 헛일이었다."], ["With back to the stranger ship, and face set like a flint to his own, Ahab stood upright till alongside of the Pequod.", "낯선 배에 등을 돌리고, 제 배를 향해 부싯돌처럼 굳은 얼굴을 한 채, 에이해브는 피쿼드호 곁에 다다를 때까지 꼿꼿이 서 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 101. The Decanter.", "# CHAPTER 101. The Decanter."]], [["Ere the English ship fades from sight, be it set down here, that she hailed from London, and was named after the late Samuel Enderby, merchant of that city, the original of the famous whaling house of Enderby & Sons; a house which in my poor whaleman’s opinion, comes not far behind the united royal houses of the Tudors and Bourbons, in point of real historical interest.", "그 영국 배가 시야에서 사라지기 전에 여기 적어 두자면, 그 배는 런던에서 출항했으며, 그 도시의 상인이자 명문 포경 상사 엔더비 부자(父子) 상회의 창립자인 고(故) 새뮤얼 엔더비(Samuel Enderby)의 이름을 따서 명명되었다. 이 상회는, 보잘것없는 한 포경꾼인 내 소견으로는, 실제 역사적 흥미라는 점에서 튜더 왕가와 부르봉 왕가를 합친 두 왕실에도 그리 뒤지지 않는다."], ["How long, prior to the year of our Lord 1775, this great whaling house was in existence, my numerous fish-documents do not make plain; but in that year (1775) it fitted out the first English ships that ever regularly hunted the Sperm Whale; though for some score of years previous (ever since 1726) our valiant Coffins and Maceys of Nantucket and the Vineyard had in large fleets pursued that Leviathan, but only in the North and South Atlantic: not elsewhere.", "우리 주님의 해 1775년 이전에 이 위대한 포경 상회가 얼마나 오래 존속해 왔는지는 내가 모아 둔 수많은 고래 문서들로도 분명히 밝혀지지 않는다. 그러나 바로 그해(1775년)에 이 상회는 향유고래(Sperm Whale)를 정규적으로 사냥한 최초의 영국 배들을 채비해 내보냈다."], ["Be it distinctly recorded here, that the Nantucketers were the first among mankind to harpoon with civilized steel the great Sperm Whale; and that for half a century they were the only people of the whole globe who so harpooned him.", "물론 그보다 수십 년 전부터(1726년 이래로) 낸터킷(Nantucket)과 비니어드의 용맹한 코핀가(家)와 메이시가(家) 사람들이 대규모 선단을 이루어 그 리바이어던(Leviathan)을 뒤쫓아 왔으나, 그것은 다만 북대서양과 남대서양에서였을 뿐, 다른 곳에서는 아니었다. 여기 분명히 기록해 두건대, 낸터킷 사람들이야말로 문명의 강철 작살로 그 거대한 향유고래를 찔러 잡은 최초의 인류였으며, 반세기 동안 온 지구를 통틀어 그렇게 그 고래를 작살로 잡은 유일한 종족이었다."]], [["In 1778, a fine ship, the Amelia, fitted out for the express purpose, and at the sole charge of the vigorous Enderbys, boldly rounded Cape Horn, and was the first among the nations to lower a whale-boat of any sort in the great South Sea.", "1778년, 정예 선박 어밀리아호가 바로 그 목적을 위해, 그리고 정력적인 엔더비 일가의 단독 부담으로 채비를 갖추어 대담하게 혼곶을 돌았으니, 이는 여러 나라 가운데 처음으로 저 광대한 남태평양에 그 어떤 종류의 고래잡이 보트라도 내려뜨린 일이었다."], ["The voyage was a skilful and lucky one; and returning to her berth with her hold full of the precious sperm, the Amelia’s example was soon followed by other ships, English and American, and thus the vast Sperm Whale grounds of the Pacific were thrown open.", "그 항해는 능란하고도 운 좋은 항해였다. 그리고 귀한 향유로 선창을 가득 채운 채 정박지로 돌아온 어밀리아호의 선례를, 곧 영국과 미국의 다른 배들이 뒤따랐고, 그리하여 태평양의 드넓은 향유고래 어장이 활짝 열렸다."], ["But not content with this good deed, the indefatigable house again bestirred itself: Samuel and all his Sons—how many, their mother only knows—and under their immediate auspices, and partly, I think, at their expense, the British government was induced to send the sloop-of-war Rattler on a whaling voyage of discovery into the South Sea.", "그러나 이 갸륵한 공적에 만족하지 않고, 지칠 줄 모르는 이 상회는 다시금 분주히 나섰다. 새뮤얼과 그의 아들 모두가 — 그 수가 몇이나 되는지는 그들의 어머니만이 알 터인데 — 그들의 직접적인 후원 아래, 또한 일부는 내 생각에 그들의 비용으로, 영국 정부를 움직여 슬루프형 군함 래틀러호를 남태평양으로의 고래잡이 탐사 항해에 내보내게 했다."], ["Commanded by a naval Post-Captain, the Rattler made a rattling voyage of it, and did some service; how much does not appear.", "정규 해군 함장의 지휘 아래 래틀러호는 요란한 항해를 펼쳐 얼마간 공을 세웠으나, 그것이 얼마만한 것이었는지는 드러나 있지 않다."], ["But this is not all.", "그러나 이것이 전부가 아니다."], ["In 1819, the same house fitted out a discovery whale ship of their own, to go on a tasting cruise to the remote waters of Japan.", "1819년에 같은 상회는 자기네 소유의 탐사용 고래잡이배 한 척을 채비해, 일본의 머나먼 바다로 시험 삼아 순항을 떠나보냈다."], ["That ship—well called the “Syren”—made a noble experimental cruise; and it was thus that the great Japanese Whaling Ground first became generally known.", "그 배는 — \"사이렌\"이라는 그럴듯한 이름이 붙었거니와 — 훌륭한 시험 순항을 해냈으니, 이렇게 하여 그 위대한 일본 고래 어장이 처음으로 널리 알려지게 되었다."], ["The Syren in this famous voyage was commanded by a Captain Coffin, a Nantucketer.", "이 유명한 항해에서 사이렌호를 지휘한 것은 낸터킷 출신의 코핀이라는 선장이었다."]], [["All honor to the Enderbies, therefore, whose house, I think, exists to the present day; though doubtless the original Samuel must long ago have slipped his cable for the great South Sea of the other world.", "그러므로 엔더비 일가에 온갖 영예가 있으라. 그 상회는, 내 생각에, 오늘날까지도 존속하고 있다. 물론 창립자 새뮤얼은 이미 오래전에 닻줄을 풀고 저세상의 저 위대한 남태평양으로 떠났으리라."]], [["The ship named after him was worthy of the honor, being a very fast sailer and a noble craft every way.", "그의 이름을 딴 배는 그 영예에 값하는 것이었으니, 매우 빠른 범선이요 모든 면에서 훌륭한 선박이었다."], ["I boarded her once at midnight somewhere off the Patagonian coast, and drank good flip down in the forecastle.", "나는 언젠가 파타고니아 해안 어딘가에서 한밤중에 그 배에 올라, 선수루(船首樓) 아래에서 맛 좋은 플립을 마신 적이 있다."], ["It was a fine gam we had, and they were all trumps—every soul on board.", "우리는 멋진 갬을 가졌고, 그들은 하나같이 으뜸가는 사내들이었다 — 배에 탄 영혼 하나하나가 다 그러했다."], ["A short life to them, and a jolly death.", "그들에게 짧은 삶과 유쾌한 죽음이 있기를."], ["And that fine gam I had—long, very long after old Ahab touched her planks with his ivory heel—it minds me of the noble, solid, Saxon hospitality of that ship; and may my parson forget me, and the devil remember me, if I ever lose sight of it.", "그리고 내가 가졌던 그 멋진 갬은 — 늙은 에이해브(Ahab)가 상아 발꿈치로 그 갑판 판자를 디딘 지 한참 뒤, 아주 한참 뒤의 일이었거니와 — 그 배의 고결하고 묵직한 색슨식 환대를 떠올리게 한다. 그리고 내가 그 기억을 잊는 날이 온다면, 내 목사가 나를 잊고 악마가 나를 기억하게 되어도 좋다."], ["Flip?", "플립이라고?"], ["Did I say we had flip?", "우리가 플립을 마셨다고 했던가? 그렇다, 우리는 시간당 열 갤런의 속도로 그것을 들이켰다."], ["Yes, and we flipped it at the rate of ten gallons the hour; and when the squall came (for it’s squally off there by Patagonia), and all hands—visitors and all—were called to reef topsails, we were so top-heavy that we had to swing each other aloft in bowlines; and we ignorantly furled the skirts of our jackets into the sails, so that we hung there, reefed fast in the howling gale, a warning example to all drunken tars.", "그리고 돌풍이 불어닥치자(파타고니아 앞바다는 돌풍이 잦으니까), 손님까지 포함한 모든 선원이 윗돛을 줄이라는 호출을 받았는데, 우리는 어찌나 머리 쪽이 무거웠던지 서로를 밧줄 고리에 매달아 위로 끌어올려야 할 지경이었다. 게다가 우리는 멋모르고 재킷 자락을 돛에 함께 말아 넣는 바람에, 울부짖는 강풍 속에 단단히 묶인 채 거기 매달려, 모든 술 취한 뱃사람들에게 경고가 될 본보기가 되고 말았다."], ["However, the masts did not go overboard; and by and by we scrambled down, so sober, that we had to pass the flip again, though the savage salt spray bursting down the forecastle scuttle, rather too much diluted and pickled it to my taste.", "그렇지만 돛대들은 바다로 넘어가지 않았고, 이윽고 우리는 기어 내려왔는데, 어찌나 술이 깨어 멀쩡해졌던지 다시 플립을 돌려야만 했다. 다만 선수루 승강구로 사납게 쏟아져 들어온 짠 물보라가 내 입맛에는 그 플립을 좀 지나치게 묽고 짭짤하게 만들어 놓긴 했다."]], [["The beef was fine—tough, but with body in it.", "쇠고기는 훌륭했으니 — 질겼지만 씹을 만한 실속이 있었다."], ["They said it was bull-beef; others, that it was dromedary beef; but I do not know, for certain, how that was.", "어떤 이들은 황소 고기라 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 단봉낙타 고기라 했으나, 그것이 실제로 무엇이었는지 나로서는 확실히 알 수 없다."], ["They had dumplings too; small, but substantial, symmetrically globular, and indestructible dumplings.", "만두도 있었는데, 작지만 든든하고, 좌우 대칭으로 둥글고, 도무지 부서지지 않는 만두였다."], ["I fancied that you could feel them, and roll them about in you after they were swallowed.", "내 생각엔 삼키고 난 뒤에도 그것들이 뱃속에서 굴러다니는 것을 느낄 수 있을 것만 같았다."], ["If you stooped over too far forward, you risked their pitching out of you like billiard-balls.", "너무 앞으로 깊이 숙이기라도 하면, 당구공처럼 뱃속에서 튀어나올 위험이 있었다."], ["The bread—but that couldn’t be helped; besides, it was an anti-scorbutic; in short, the bread contained the only fresh fare they had.", "빵으로 말하자면 — 하지만 그건 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이었고, 게다가 괴혈병을 막아 주는 것이었다. 요컨대 그 빵은 그들이 가진 유일한 신선한 먹을거리를 담고 있었다."], ["But the forecastle was not very light, and it was very easy to step over into a dark corner when you ate it.", "그러나 선수루는 그리 밝지 않아, 그것을 먹을 때 어두운 구석으로 한 발짝 옮겨 가기가 아주 쉬웠다."], ["But all in all, taking her from truck to helm, considering the dimensions of the cook’s boilers, including his own live parchment boilers; fore and aft, I say, the Samuel Enderby was a jolly ship; of good fare and plenty; fine flip and strong; crack fellows all, and capital from boot heels to hat-band.", "그러나 모든 것을 통틀어, 돛대 꼭대기에서 키까지 그 배를 따져 보고, 요리사의 솥들의 크기를 — 그 자신의 살아 있는 양피지 같은 솥(뱃살)까지 포함하여 — 헤아려 보건대, 이물에서 고물까지, 내 말하건대, 새뮤얼 엔더비호는 유쾌한 배였다. 먹을 것은 좋고도 넉넉했으며, 플립은 훌륭하고도 독했고, 사내들은 죄다 빼어난 친구들이라, 구두 뒤축에서 모자 띠까지 일류였다."]], [["But why was it, think ye, that the Samuel Enderby, and some other English whalers I know of—not all though—were such famous, hospitable ships; that passed round the beef, and the bread, and the can, and the joke; and were not soon weary of eating, and drinking, and laughing? I will tell you.", "그런데 어찌하여, 그대들은 생각하는가, 새뮤얼 엔더비호와, 내가 아는 몇몇 다른 영국 고래잡이배들이 — 물론 전부는 아니지만 — 그토록 이름난, 그토록 후한 배들이어서, 쇠고기와 빵과 술통과 농담을 돌리고도 먹고 마시고 웃는 일에 좀처럼 지치지 않았던 것일까?"], ["The abounding good cheer of these English whalers is matter for historical research.", "내 그대들에게 일러 주리라. 이 영국 고래잡이배들의 넘쳐 나는 환대는 역사적 탐구의 대상이다."], ["Nor have I been at all sparing of historical whale research, when it has seemed needed.", "그리고 나는 필요하다 싶을 때면 역사적 고래 탐구를 결코 아끼지 않았다."]], [["The English were preceded in the whale fishery by the Hollanders, Zealanders, and Danes; from whom they derived many terms still extant in the fishery; and what is yet more, their fat old fashions, touching plenty to eat and drink.", "고래잡이업에서 영국인보다 앞선 것은 네덜란드인과 제일란트인과 덴마크인이었으니, 영국인은 그들에게서 오늘날에도 어업에 남아 있는 많은 용어들을 물려받았으며, 그보다 더한 것은, 푸짐하게 먹고 마시는 일에 관한 그들의 기름지고 오래된 풍습까지 물려받았다는 점이다."], ["For, as a general thing, the English merchant-ship scrimps her crew; but not so the English whaler.", "대체로 보아 영국 상선은 선원들에게 인색하게 굴지만, 영국 고래잡이배는 그렇지 않기 때문이다."], ["Hence, in the English, this thing of whaling good cheer is not normal and natural, but incidental and particular; and, therefore, must have some special origin, which is here pointed out, and will be still further elucidated.", "따라서 영국인에게 이 고래잡이 환대라는 것은 일상적이고 자연스러운 일이 아니라 부수적이고 특수한 일이며, 그러므로 어떤 특별한 기원이 있을 수밖에 없으니, 그것을 여기서 짚어 보고 앞으로 더 밝혀 보고자 한다."]], [["During my researches in the Leviathanic histories, I stumbled upon an ancient Dutch volume, which, by the musty whaling smell of it, I knew must be about whalers. The title was, “Dan Coopman,” wherefore I concluded that this must be the invaluable memoirs of some Amsterdam cooper in the fishery, as every whale ship must carry its cooper. I was reinforced in this opinion by seeing that it was the production of one “Fitz Swackhammer.” But my friend Dr. Snodhead, a very learned man, professor of Low Dutch and High German in the college of Santa Claus and St. Pott’s, to whom I handed the work for translation, giving him a box of sperm candles for his trouble—this same Dr. Snodhead, so soon as he spied the book, assured me that “Dan Coopman” did not mean “The Cooper,” but “The Merchant.” In short, this ancient and learned Low Dutch book treated of the commerce of Holland; and, among other subjects, contained a very interesting account of its whale fishery. And in this chapter it was, headed, “Smeer,” or “Fat,” that I found a long detailed list of the outfits for the larders and cellars of 180 sail of Dutch whalemen; from which list, as translated by Dr. Snodhead, I transcribe the following:", "리바이어던에 관한 역사들을 탐구하던 중에, 나는 어느 오래된 네덜란드어 책 한 권을 우연히 만났는데, 거기서 풍기는 곰팡내 나는 고래 냄새로 미루어 그것이 고래잡이에 관한 책임을 단번에 알아차렸다. 제목은 \"단 코프만(Dan Coopman)\"이었으니, 나는 모든 고래잡이배가 통메장이(쿠퍼)를 한 명씩 싣고 다녀야 하는 만큼, 이것이 어업에 종사한 어느 암스테르담 통메장이의 더없이 값진 회고록임에 틀림없다고 결론지었다. 그것이 \"피츠 스왁해머\"라는 자의 저작임을 보고서 이 생각은 한층 굳어졌다. 그러나 내 친구이자 산타클로스·성포트 대학의 저지(低地) 네덜란드어 및 고지(高地) 독일어 교수인, 대단히 박식한 스노드헤드 박사에게 — 수고에 대한 사례로 향유고래 양초 한 상자를 건네며 — 그 책을 번역해 달라고 넘겼더니, 바로 이 스노드헤드 박사가 그 책을 보자마자 \"단 코프만\"은 \"통메장이\"가 아니라 \"상인\"이라는 뜻이라고 확언해 주었다. 요컨대 이 오래되고 학식 깊은 저지 네덜란드어 책은 네덜란드의 상업을 다룬 것이었고, 여러 주제들 가운데 그 나라의 고래잡이업에 관한 대단히 흥미로운 기록을 담고 있었다. 그리고 바로 그 안에, \"스메르(Smeer)\" 곧 \"기름\"이라는 제목이 붙은 장(章)에서, 나는 네덜란드 고래잡이배 180척의 식료품 저장고와 술 창고를 채울 보급품의 길고 상세한 목록을 발견했으니, 스노드헤드 박사가 번역해 준 그 목록에서 다음을 옮겨 적는다."]], [["400,000 lbs. of beef.", "쇠고기 40만 파운드."], ["60,000 lbs. Friesland pork.", "프리슬란트산 돼지고기 6만 파운드."], ["150,000 lbs. of stock fish.", "건어물 15만 파운드."], ["550,000 lbs. of biscuit.", "비스킷 55만 파운드."], ["72,000 lbs. of soft bread.", "부드러운 빵 7만 2천 파운드."], ["2,800 firkins of butter.", "버터 2,800퍼킨."], ["20,000 lbs. Texel & Leyden cheese.", "텍셀산 및 레이던산 치즈 2만 파운드."], ["144,000 lbs. cheese (probably an inferior article).", "치즈 14만 4천 파운드(아마도 질 낮은 것)."], ["550 ankers of Geneva.", "진(제네바주) 550앵커."], ["10,800 barrels of beer.", "맥주 1만 800배럴."]], [["Most statistical tables are parchingly dry in the reading; not so in the present case, however, where the reader is flooded with whole pipes, barrels, quarts, and gills of good gin and good cheer.", "대부분의 통계표는 읽노라면 입이 바짝 마르도록 무미건조하지만, 지금 이 경우만은 그렇지 않으니, 여기서는 독자가 통째로 쏟아지는 파이프와 배럴, 쿼트와 질 단위의 좋은 진과 좋은 먹을거리에 흠뻑 잠기게 되는 것이다."]], [["At the time, I devoted three days to the studious digesting of all this beer, beef, and bread, during which many profound thoughts were incidentally suggested to me, capable of a transcendental and Platonic application; and, furthermore, I compiled supplementary tables of my own, touching the probable quantity of stock-fish, etc., consumed by every Low Dutch harpooneer in that ancient Greenland and Spitzbergen whale fishery.", "그때 나는 이 모든 맥주와 쇠고기와 빵을 면밀히 소화하는 데 사흘을 바쳤는데, 그동안 초월적이고 플라톤적인 적용이 가능한 여러 심오한 사색들이 부수적으로 내게 떠올랐다. 더 나아가 나는 그 옛 그린란드와 스피츠베르겐 고래잡이업에서 저지 네덜란드인 작살잡이 한 사람이 소비했을 건어물 따위의 짐작 분량에 관하여, 나 자신의 보충 통계표까지 작성했다."], ["In the first place, the amount of butter, and Texel and Leyden cheese consumed, seems amazing.", "우선, 소비된 버터와 텍셀산·레이던산 치즈의 양은 놀라울 지경으로 보인다."], ["I impute it, though, to their naturally unctuous natures, being rendered still more unctuous by the nature of their vocation, and especially by their pursuing their game in those frigid Polar Seas, on the very coasts of that Esquimaux country where the convivial natives pledge each other in bumpers of train oil.", "다만 나는 이를 그들이 타고난 기름진 천성 탓으로 돌리는데, 그 천성은 그들의 직업의 성격으로 인해, 특히 그들이 그 얼어붙은 극지의 바다에서, 곧 사람 좋은 원주민들이 고래기름을 가득 채운 잔으로 서로 축배를 드는 저 에스키모 땅의 바로 그 해안에서 사냥감을 뒤쫓는 까닭에 한층 더 기름지게 되었던 것이다."]], [["The quantity of beer, too, is very large, 10,800 barrels.", "맥주의 양 또한 대단히 많으니, 1만 800배럴이다."], ["Now, as those polar fisheries could only be prosecuted in the short summer of that climate, so that the whole cruise of one of these Dutch whalemen, including the short voyage to and from the Spitzbergen sea, did not much exceed three months, say, and reckoning 30 men to each of their fleet of 180 sail, we have 5,400 Low Dutch seamen in all; therefore, I say, we have precisely two barrels of beer per man, for a twelve weeks’ allowance, exclusive of his fair proportion of that 550 ankers of gin.", "그런데 그 극지 어업은 그 기후의 짧은 여름철에만 수행될 수 있었으므로, 이 네덜란드 고래잡이배 한 척의 전체 순항은, 스피츠베르겐 바다를 오가는 짧은 항해까지 포함하더라도 석 달을 그리 넘지 않았다고 하고, 그들의 180척 선단에서 한 척당 30명씩 헤아리면, 모두 합쳐 5,400명의 저지 네덜란드 선원이 있는 셈이다. 그러므로 내 말하건대, 우리는 한 사람당 정확히 맥주 두 배럴씩을, 그것도 진 550앵커 가운데 그가 마땅히 차지할 몫은 빼고서, 열두 주의 배급량으로 갖게 되는 것이다."], ["Now, whether these gin and beer harpooneers, so fuddled as one might fancy them to have been, were the right sort of men to stand up in a boat’s head, and take good aim at flying whales; this would seem somewhat improbable.", "그런데 이 진과 맥주에 절은 작살잡이들이, 누구든 짐작할 만큼 곤드레만드레가 되고서도, 보트의 이물에 버티고 서서 날쌔게 달아나는 고래를 제대로 겨냥할 만한 부류의 사내들이었느냐 하면, 그것은 다소 미덥지 않아 보인다. 그러나 그들은 정말로 고래를 겨누었고, 또 명중시키기도 했다."], ["Yet they did aim at them, and hit them too.", "하지만 이곳은 아주 먼 북쪽이었음을 기억해 두자."], ["But this was very far North, be it remembered, where beer agrees well with the constitution; upon the Equator, in our southern fishery, beer would be apt to make the harpooneer sleepy at the mast-head and boozy in his boat; and grievous loss might ensue to Nantucket and New Bedford.", "그곳에서는 맥주가 몸에 잘 맞는다. 적도에서, 우리 남방 어업에서라면, 맥주는 작살잡이를 돛대 꼭대기에서 졸게 하고 보트 안에서 곤드레가 되게 하기 십상이어서, 낸터킷과 뉴베드퍼드에 통탄할 손실이 닥칠 수도 있을 것이다."]], [["But no more; enough has been said to show that the old Dutch whalers of two or three centuries ago were high livers; and that the English whalers have not neglected so excellent an example.", "그러나 더는 말하지 않겠다. 두세 세기 전의 옛 네덜란드 고래잡이들이 호화롭게 먹고 마신 자들이었으며, 영국 고래잡이들이 그토록 훌륭한 본보기를 소홀히 하지 않았다는 것을 보이는 데는 이만하면 충분하다."], ["For, say they, when cruising in an empty ship, if you can get nothing better out of the world, get a good dinner out of it, at least.", "그들의 말마따나, 빈 배로 순항할 때 세상에서 더 나은 것을 얻을 수 없다면, 적어도 좋은 저녁 한 끼라도 얻어 내라는 것이다."], ["And this empties the decanter.", "그리고 이로써 술병은 비워진다."]], [["# CHAPTER 102. A Bower in the Arsacides.", "# CHAPTER 102. A Bower in the Arsacides."]], [["Hitherto, in descriptively treating of the Sperm Whale, I have chiefly dwelt upon the marvels of his outer aspect; or separately and in detail upon some few interior structural features.", "지금까지 향유고래(Sperm Whale)를 묘사하여 다루면서, 나는 주로 그 바깥 모습의 경이로움에 머물러 있었거나, 혹은 몇 가지 내부 구조의 특징들을 따로따로 자세히 살폈을 뿐이다."], ["But to a large and thorough sweeping comprehension of him, it behooves me now to unbutton him still further, and untagging the points of his hose, unbuckling his garters, and casting loose the hooks and the eyes of the joints of his innermost bones, set him before you in his ultimatum; that is to say, in his unconditional skeleton.", "그러나 그를 폭넓고도 철저하게 두루 이해하기 위해서는, 이제 나는 그의 옷고름을 한층 더 풀어헤치고, 그의 양말 매듭을 끄르고, 그의 대님을 풀고, 그 가장 깊숙한 뼈마디를 잇는 갈고리와 고리를 모두 풀어 헤쳐, 마침내 그를 그 최종의 모습으로, 다시 말해 아무 조건 없는 골격 그 자체로 여러분 앞에 세워 놓아야 마땅하다."]], [["But how now, Ishmael?", "그런데 이제 와서 무슨 소린가, 이슈메일?"], ["How is it, that you, a mere oarsman in the fishery, pretend to know aught about the subterranean parts of the whale?", "어찌하여 그대, 고래잡이배의 한낱 노잡이에 불과한 그대가 고래의 땅속 깊은 부분에 관해 무엇이든 안다는 듯이 굴 수 있단 말인가? 박식한 스터브(Stubb)가 그대의 캡스턴 위에 올라서서 고래목(目)의 해부학을 강의하기라도 했단 말인가, 그리고 양묘기의 힘을 빌려 본보기 갈빗대 하나를 들어 올려 전시라도 했단 말인가?"], ["Did erudite Stubb, mounted upon your capstan, deliver lectures on the anatomy of the Cetacea; and by help of the windlass, hold up a specimen rib for exhibition?", "그대 스스로를 해명해 보라, 이슈메일. 요리사가 통구이 새끼돼지를 접시에 담아내듯, 그대는 다 자란 고래를 갑판 위에 올려놓고 살펴볼 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Explain thyself, Ishmael.", "결단코 그럴 수 없으리라."], ["Can you land a full-grown whale on your deck for examination, as a cook dishes a roast-pig?", "이슈메일, 그대는 지금까지 진실한 증인이었다. 그러나 오직 요나(Jonah)만이 누렸던 특권을 함부로 움켜쥐려 들지 말라."], ["Surely not. A veritable witness have you hitherto been, Ishmael; but have a care how you seize the privilege of Jonah alone; the privilege of discoursing upon the joists and beams; the rafters, ridge-pole, sleepers, and under-pinnings, making up the frame-work of leviathan; and belike of the tallow-vats, dairy-rooms, butteries, and cheeseries in his bowels.", "곧 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 골조를 이루는 들보와 대들보, 서까래와 마룻대, 침목과 토대에 관하여 논할 특권, 어쩌면 그 뱃속의 기름 가마와 우유 방과 식료품 저장실과 치즈 광에 관하여 논할 특권 말이다."]], [["I confess, that since Jonah, few whalemen have penetrated very far beneath the skin of the adult whale; nevertheless, I have been blessed with an opportunity to dissect him in miniature.", "고백하건대, 요나 이래로 다 자란 고래의 살갗 밑을 아주 깊이 파고든 고래잡이는 드물었다. 그럼에도 나는 그 축소판을 해부해 볼 기회를 다행히도 얻었다."], ["In a ship I belonged to, a small cub Sperm Whale was once bodily hoisted to the deck for his poke or bag, to make sheaths for the barbs of the harpoons, and for the heads of the lances.", "내가 몸담았던 어느 배에서 한번은 작은 새끼 향유고래 한 마리를 통째로 갑판 위로 끌어올린 적이 있는데, 그 가죽 주머니로 작살의 미늘과 창의 머리를 감쌀 칼집을 만들기 위해서였다."], ["Think you I let that chance go, without using my boat-hatchet and jack-knife, and breaking the seal and reading all the contents of that young cub?", "그런 기회를 내가 그냥 흘려보냈으리라 생각하는가, 보트용 손도끼와 잭나이프를 써서 그 봉인을 뜯고 그 어린 새끼의 속을 낱낱이 읽어 내지 않은 채로?"]], [["And as for my exact knowledge of the bones of the leviathan in their gigantic, full grown development, for that rare knowledge I am indebted to my late royal friend Tranquo, king of Tranque, one of the Arsacides.", "그리고 거대하게 다 자란 리바이어던의 뼈에 관한 내 정확한 지식으로 말하자면, 그 흔치 않은 지식은 작고한 나의 왕가의 벗 트랑쿠오, 곧 아르사키데스 군도 가운데 하나인 트랑케의 왕에게서 얻은 것이다."], ["For being at Tranque, years ago, when attached to the trading-ship Dey of Algiers, I was invited to spend part of the Arsacidean holidays with the lord of Tranque, at his retired palm villa at Pupella; a sea-side glen not very far distant from what our sailors called Bamboo-Town, his capital.", "여러 해 전, 무역선 알제(Algiers)의 데이 호에 몸담고 있던 시절 트랑케에 머물 적에, 나는 트랑케의 군주와 더불어 아르사키데스의 명절 일부를 그의 외딴 야자수 별장이 있는 푸펠라에서 보내자는 초대를 받았는데, 그곳은 우리 선원들이 뱀부타운, 곧 대나무 마을이라 부르던 그의 수도에서 그리 멀지 않은 바닷가 골짜기였다."]], [["Among many other fine qualities, my royal friend Tranquo, being gifted with a devout love for all matters of barbaric vertu, had brought together in Pupella whatever rare things the more ingenious of his people could invent; chiefly carved woods of wonderful devices, chiselled shells, inlaid spears, costly paddles, aromatic canoes; and all these distributed among whatever natural wonders, the wonder-freighted, tribute-rendering waves had cast upon his shores.", "여러 빼어난 자질 가운데서도, 나의 왕가의 벗 트랑쿠오는 야만적인 골동 취향에 관한 모든 것을 향한 경건한 애정을 타고난 사람이어서, 자기 백성 가운데 더 솜씨 좋은 자들이 빚어낼 수 있는 온갖 진귀한 물건들을 푸펠라에 한데 모아 두었다. 주로 놀라운 도안을 새긴 목각이며, 정교하게 깎은 조개껍데기며, 상감을 박은 창이며, 값비싼 노며, 향내 나는 카누 같은 것들이었다. 그리고 이 모든 것들은, 경이를 가득 싣고 공물을 바치듯 밀려온 파도가 그의 해안에 던져 놓은 온갖 자연의 진기한 것들 사이에 두루 배치되어 있었다."]], [["Chief among these latter was a great Sperm Whale, which, after an unusually long raging gale, had been found dead and stranded, with his head against a cocoa-nut tree, whose plumage-like, tufted droopings seemed his verdant jet.", "이 후자의 것들 가운데 으뜸가는 것은 거대한 향유고래였는데, 유난히 오래 휘몰아친 폭풍이 지나간 뒤 죽은 채로 좌초되어 발견된 것으로, 그 머리는 야자나무 한 그루에 기대어 있었고, 깃털처럼 술 모양으로 늘어진 그 나무의 잎사귀들은 마치 고래의 푸른 물줄기처럼 보였다."], ["When the vast body had at last been stripped of its fathom-deep enfoldings, and the bones become dust dry in the sun, then the skeleton was carefully transported up the Pupella glen, where a grand temple of lordly palms now sheltered it.", "그 거대한 몸뚱이가 마침내 한 길 깊이로 두텁게 감싸고 있던 살을 모두 벗겨 내고, 뼈가 햇볕에 먼지처럼 바싹 마르게 되자, 그 골격은 조심스럽게 푸펠라 골짜기 위쪽으로 옮겨졌고, 그곳에서 위풍당당한 야자수들의 웅장한 신전이 이제 그것을 품어 주고 있었다."]], [["The ribs were hung with trophies; the vertebræ were carved with Arsacidean annals, in strange hieroglyphics; in the skull, the priests kept up an unextinguished aromatic flame, so that the mystic head again sent forth its vapory spout; while, suspended from a bough, the terrific lower jaw vibrated over all the devotees, like the hair-hung sword that so affrighted Damocles.", "갈빗대마다 전리품이 걸렸고, 등뼈마다 기묘한 상형문자로 아르사키데스의 연대기가 새겨졌다. 두개골 속에서는 사제들이 꺼지지 않는 향불을 피워, 그 신비로운 머리가 다시금 안개 같은 물줄기를 뿜어내게 했다. 한편 나뭇가지에 매달린 무시무시한 아래턱은, 다모클레스를 그토록 떨게 했던 머리카락에 매달린 칼처럼, 모든 신도들의 머리 위에서 흔들거렸다."]], [["It was a wondrous sight.", "그것은 경이로운 광경이었다."], ["The wood was green as mosses of the Icy Glen; the trees stood high and haughty, feeling their living sap; the industrious earth beneath was as a weaver’s loom, with a gorgeous carpet on it, whereof the ground-vine tendrils formed the warp and woof, and the living flowers the figures.", "숲은 빙곡(氷谷)의 이끼처럼 푸르렀고, 나무들은 제 안에 흐르는 생생한 수액을 느끼며 높고 도도하게 서 있었다. 그 아래 부지런한 대지는 마치 직조공의 베틀과도 같았으니, 그 위에 화려한 양탄자가 깔리고, 땅을 기는 덩굴 줄기가 날줄과 씨줄을 이루며, 살아 있는 꽃들이 무늬를 이루고 있었다."], ["All the trees, with all their laden branches; all the shrubs, and ferns, and grasses; the message-carrying air; all these unceasingly were active.", "짐을 가득 진 가지들을 거느린 모든 나무들, 모든 떨기나무와 고사리와 풀들, 소식을 실어 나르는 공기, 이 모든 것들이 쉼 없이 활동하고 있었다. 잎사귀의 얼기설기한 틈 사이로, 위대한 태양은 지치지 않는 푸르름을 짜 내는 나는 북처럼 보였다."], ["Through the lacings of the leaves, the great sun seemed a flying shuttle weaving the unwearied verdure.", "오, 바쁜 직조공이여! 보이지 않는 직조공이여!"], ["Oh, busy weaver!", "멈추라! 한마디만!"], ["unseen weaver!—pause!—one word!—whither flows the fabric?", "그 천은 어디로 흘러가는가? 어떤 궁전을 장식하려는가?"], ["what palace may it deck?", "무엇 때문에 이 끝없는 수고를 하는가? 말하라, 직조공이여!"], ["wherefore all these ceaseless toilings?", "손을 멈추라! 그대와 단 한마디만 나누고 싶구나!"], ["Speak, weaver!—stay thy hand!—but one single word with thee!", "아니, 북은 날아간다. 무늬들은 베틀에서 흘러나와 떠가고, 봇물처럼 쏟아지는 양탄자는 영영 미끄러져 사라진다."], ["Nay—the shuttle flies—the figures float from forth the loom; the freshet-rushing carpet for ever slides away.", "직조공인 신, 그는 천을 짠다. 그리고 그 직조 소리에 귀가 먹어 그는 어떤 인간의 목소리도 듣지 못한다."], ["The weaver-god, he weaves; and by that weaving is he deafened, that he hears no mortal voice; and by that humming, we, too, who look on the loom are deafened; and only when we escape it shall we hear the thousand voices that speak through it.", "그 윙윙거리는 소리에 베틀을 바라보는 우리 또한 귀가 먹는다. 그래서 오직 우리가 거기서 벗어날 때에야 비로소 그 베틀을 통해 말해지는 수천의 목소리를 듣게 되리라."], ["For even so it is in all material factories.", "무릇 모든 물질의 공장에서도 사정은 이와 같다."], ["The spoken words that are inaudible among the flying spindles; those same words are plainly heard without the walls, bursting from the opened casements.", "날아다니는 방추 사이에서는 들리지 않던 그 말들이, 똑같은 그 말들이 벽 바깥에서는, 열린 들창으로 터져 나오며 또렷이 들리는 법이다. 그리하여 악행이 드러나기도 했다."], ["Thereby have villainies been detected.", "아, 죽을 운명의 인간이여!"], ["Ah, mortal!", "그러니 조심하라."], ["then, be heedful; for so, in all this din of the great world’s loom, thy subtlest thinkings may be overheard afar.", "이 위대한 세계의 베틀이 내는 온갖 소음 속에서도, 그대의 가장 은밀한 생각들이 멀리서 엿들릴 수 있으니."]], [["Now, amid the green, life-restless loom of that Arsacidean wood, the great, white, worshipped skeleton lay lounging—a gigantic idler!", "자, 그 아르사키데스 숲의 푸르고 생명으로 들끓는 베틀 한가운데, 그 거대하고 새하얗고 숭배받는 골격이 비스듬히 누워 있었다. 거대한 게으름뱅이가!"], ["Yet, as the ever-woven verdant warp and woof intermixed and hummed around him, the mighty idler seemed the cunning weaver; himself all woven over with the vines; every month assuming greener, fresher verdure; but himself a skeleton.", "그러나 끊임없이 짜이는 푸른 날줄과 씨줄이 그를 둘러싸고 뒤섞이며 윙윙거릴 때면, 그 위대한 게으름뱅이는 도리어 교묘한 직조공처럼 보였다. 제 몸 전체가 덩굴로 온통 뒤덮여, 달이 갈 때마다 더 푸르고 더 싱그러운 푸르름을 걸쳤으나, 정작 그 자신은 한낱 골격이었다."], ["Life folded Death; Death trellised Life; the grim god wived with youthful Life, and begat him curly-headed glories.", "삶이 죽음을 감싸 안았고, 죽음이 삶을 시렁처럼 받쳐 올렸다. 그 음울한 신은 젊은 삶을 아내로 맞아, 곱슬머리의 영광들을 낳았다."]], [["Now, when with royal Tranquo I visited this wondrous whale, and saw the skull an altar, and the artificial smoke ascending from where the real jet had issued, I marvelled that the king should regard a chapel as an object of vertu.", "자, 내가 트랑쿠오 왕과 더불어 이 경이로운 고래를 찾아가, 두개골이 제단이 되고 진짜 물줄기가 솟던 자리에서 인공의 연기가 피어오르는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 왕이 한낱 예배당을 골동의 대상으로 여긴다는 데에 놀랐다. 그러자 그는 웃었다."], ["He laughed. But more I marvelled that the priests should swear that smoky jet of his was genuine.", "그러나 사제들이 그 연기 같은 물줄기가 진짜라고 맹세하는 데에 나는 더욱 놀랐다."], ["To and fro I paced before this skeleton—brushed the vines aside—broke through the ribs—and with a ball of Arsacidean twine, wandered, eddied long amid its many winding, shaded colonnades and arbours.", "나는 이 골격 앞을 이리저리 거닐며, 덩굴을 헤치고, 갈빗대 사이를 뚫고 들어가, 아르사키데스의 실타래 하나를 들고서 그 구불구불하고 그늘진 수많은 회랑과 정자 사이를 한참 헤매고 맴돌았다."], ["But soon my line was out; and following it back, I emerged from the opening where I entered.", "그러나 이내 실이 다 풀려 버렸고, 그 실을 되짚어 따라가다 보니, 나는 처음 들어왔던 그 입구로 다시 나오게 되었다."], ["I saw no living thing within; naught was there but bones.", "안에서는 살아 있는 것이라곤 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그곳에 있는 것이라곤 오직 뼈뿐이었다."]], [["Cutting me a green measuring-rod, I once more dived within the skeleton.", "나는 푸른 측량용 장대 하나를 잘라 들고서 다시 한번 골격 안으로 파고들었다."], ["From their arrow-slit in the skull, the priests perceived me taking the altitude of the final rib, “How now!”", "두개골에 난 화살구멍 같은 틈으로, 사제들이 내가 마지막 갈빗대의 높이를 재는 것을 알아챘다. \"이게 무슨 짓인가!\""], ["they shouted; “Dar’st thou measure this our god!", "그들이 소리쳤다. \"감히 우리의 신을 측량하다니!"], ["That’s for us.”", "그건 우리가 할 일이다.\""], ["“Aye, priests—well, how long do ye make him, then?”", "\"그렇소, 사제들이여, 좋소, 그렇다면 그대들은 이놈을 몇 자로 헤아리오?\""], ["But hereupon a fierce contest rose among them, concerning feet and inches; they cracked each other’s sconces with their yard-sticks—the great skull echoed—and seizing that lucky chance, I quickly concluded my own admeasurements.", "그런데 이 말이 떨어지자, 그들 사이에서 자와 치를 두고 사나운 논쟁이 일어나, 서로의 정수리를 측량 막대로 두들겨 깨뜨렸으니, 그 거대한 두개골이 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸다. 나는 이 절호의 기회를 붙잡아, 재빨리 내 나름의 측량을 마무리 지었다."]], [["These admeasurements I now propose to set before you.", "이제 나는 이 측량 수치를 여러분 앞에 내놓으려 한다."], ["But first, be it recorded, that, in this matter, I am not free to utter any fancied measurement I please.", "그러나 먼저 기록해 두건대, 이 일에서 나는 내키는 대로 아무 상상 속의 수치나 함부로 늘어놓을 처지가 못 된다."], ["Because there are skeleton authorities you can refer to, to test my accuracy.", "왜냐하면 내 정확성을 시험해 볼 수 있도록 여러분이 참고할 만한 골격 권위자들이 따로 있기 때문이다."], ["There is a Leviathanic Museum, they tell me, in Hull, England, one of the whaling ports of that country, where they have some fine specimens of fin-backs and other whales.", "듣자 하니, 영국 헐에 리바이어던 박물관이 있다는데, 그곳은 그 나라의 고래잡이 항구 가운데 하나로, 거기에는 긴수염고래며 그 밖의 고래들의 훌륭한 표본 몇 점이 있다고 한다."], ["Likewise, I have heard that in the museum of Manchester, in New Hampshire, they have what the proprietors call “the only perfect specimen of a Greenland or River Whale in the United States.”", "또한 들은 바로는, 뉴햄프셔주 맨체스터의 박물관에는 그 소유주들이 \"미합중국 내 유일한 완벽한 그린란드고래, 곧 강고래 표본\"이라 일컫는 것이 있다고 한다."], ["Moreover, at a place in Yorkshire, England, Burton Constable by name, a certain Sir Clifford Constable has in his possession the skeleton of a Sperm Whale, but of moderate size, by no means of the full-grown magnitude of my friend King Tranquo’s.", "게다가 영국 요크셔의 버턴 콘스터블이라는 곳에는, 어느 클리퍼드 콘스터블 경이라는 이가 향유고래 골격 한 구를 소장하고 있는데, 다만 크기가 적당한 정도여서, 결코 나의 벗 트랑쿠오 왕의 것처럼 다 자란 거대한 규모는 아니다."]], [["In both cases, the stranded whales to which these two skeletons belonged, were originally claimed by their proprietors upon similar grounds.", "두 경우 모두, 이 두 골격이 본디 속해 있던 좌초한 고래들은, 그 소유주들이 비슷한 근거를 들어 자기 것이라 주장한 것이었다."], ["King Tranquo seizing his because he wanted it; and Sir Clifford, because he was lord of the seignories of those parts.", "트랑쿠오 왕은 그저 자기가 원했기에 그것을 차지했고, 클리퍼드 경은 자기가 그 지역 영지의 영주였기에 차지했다."], ["Sir Clifford’s whale has been articulated throughout; so that, like a great chest of drawers, you can open and shut him, in all his bony cavities—spread out his ribs like a gigantic fan—and swing all day upon his lower jaw.", "클리퍼드 경의 고래는 골격 전체가 마디마디 연결되어 있어서, 마치 커다란 서랍장처럼, 그 뼈로 된 온갖 빈 공간을 여닫을 수도 있고, 그 갈빗대를 거대한 부채처럼 활짝 펼칠 수도 있으며, 종일토록 그 아래턱에 매달려 흔들흔들할 수도 있다."], ["Locks are to be put upon some of his trap-doors and shutters; and a footman will show round future visitors with a bunch of keys at his side.", "그 뚜껑문과 덧문 가운데 몇몇에는 자물쇠를 채워 둘 예정이며, 하인 하나가 허리춤에 열쇠 꾸러미를 차고서 앞으로 찾아올 방문객들을 안내하며 두루 보여 줄 것이다."], ["Sir Clifford thinks of charging twopence for a peep at the whispering gallery in the spinal column; threepence to hear the echo in the hollow of his cerebellum; and sixpence for the unrivalled view from his forehead.", "클리퍼드 경은 척추 안의 속삭임 회랑을 들여다보는 데 2펜스를, 소뇌의 빈 공간에서 울리는 메아리를 듣는 데 3펜스를, 그 이마에서 바라보는 견줄 데 없는 경관에 6펜스를 받을 작정이라고 한다."]], [["The skeleton dimensions I shall now proceed to set down are copied verbatim from my right arm, where I had them tattooed; as in my wild wanderings at that period, there was no other secure way of preserving such valuable statistics.", "이제 내가 적어 내려갈 골격의 치수는, 내 오른팔에서 한 글자도 틀림없이 베껴 온 것인데, 거기에 그 수치를 문신으로 새겨 두었기 때문이다. 그 무렵 거칠게 떠돌아다니던 처지에서는, 그토록 값진 통계를 보존할 다른 안전한 방도가 달리 없었던 것이다."], ["But as I was crowded for space, and wished the other parts of my body to remain a blank page for a poem I was then composing—at least, what untattooed parts might remain—I did not trouble myself with the odd inches; nor, indeed, should inches at all enter into a congenial admeasurement of the whale.", "그러나 내 몸에 자리가 빠듯했던 데다, 그 무렵 내가 짓고 있던 시 한 편을 위해 내 몸의 다른 부분들은, 적어도 문신이 새겨지지 않은 채로 남아 있을 부분들만이라도 빈 종잇장으로 남겨 두고 싶었기에, 나는 자투리 인치까지는 굳이 신경 쓰지 않았다. 사실 고래를 그 격에 맞게 측량하는 데는, 인치 단위 따위는 애초에 끼어들 자리가 없는 법이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 103. Measurement of The Whale’s Skeleton.", "# CHAPTER 103. Measurement of The Whale’s Skeleton."]], [["In the first place, I wish to lay before you a particular, plain statement, touching the living bulk of this leviathan, whose skeleton we are briefly to exhibit.", "우선 나는 이 리바이어던(Leviathan)의 살아 있는 몸집에 관하여 명확하고 구체적인 설명을 여러분 앞에 펼쳐 보이고자 한다. 그 골격을 우리는 이제 간략히 살펴볼 참이다."], ["Such a statement may prove useful here.", "그러한 설명은 여기서 유용하게 쓰일 것이다."]], [["According to a careful calculation I have made, and which I partly base upon Captain Scoresby’s estimate, of seventy tons for the largest sized Greenland whale of sixty feet in length; according to my careful calculation, I say, a Sperm Whale of the largest magnitude, between eighty-five and ninety feet in length, and something less than forty feet in its fullest circumference, such a whale will weigh at least ninety tons; so that, reckoning thirteen men to a ton, he would considerably outweigh the combined population of a whole village of one thousand one hundred inhabitants.", "내가 신중하게 계산한 바에 따르면 — 그 일부는 스코어즈비 선장(Captain Scoresby)이 길이 60피트(약 18미터)에 이르는 가장 큰 그린란드 고래의 무게를 70톤으로 추정한 데 근거를 둔 것인데 — 내 그 면밀한 계산에 따르면, 길이가 85피트(약 26미터)에서 90피트(약 27미터) 사이이고 가장 굵은 둘레가 40피트(약 12미터)에 조금 못 미치는 가장 거대한 향유고래(Sperm Whale)는 적어도 90톤은 나갈 것이다. 그러니 한 톤에 열세 사람씩 친다면, 그 한 마리는 주민 1,100명이 사는 마을 전체의 인구를 합친 것보다도 훨씬 더 무거운 셈이다."]], [["Think you not then that brains, like yoked cattle, should be put to this leviathan, to make him at all budge to any landsman’s imagination?", "그렇다면 이 리바이어던을 뭍사람의 상상 속에서 조금이라도 움직여 보이게 하려면, 멍에를 멘 소처럼 여러 사람의 머리를 한데 묶어 끌게 해야 마땅하지 않겠는가?"]], [["Having already in various ways put before you his skull, spout-hole, jaw, teeth, tail, forehead, fins, and divers other parts, I shall now simply point out what is most interesting in the general bulk of his unobstructed bones.", "이미 나는 여러 방식으로 그 두개골, 분수 구멍, 턱, 이빨, 꼬리, 이마, 지느러미, 그 밖의 갖가지 부위를 여러분 앞에 펼쳐 보였으니, 이제는 다만 그 가림 없이 드러난 뼈의 전체 크기에서 가장 흥미로운 점만을 짚어 보려 한다."], ["But as the colossal skull embraces so very large a proportion of the entire extent of the skeleton; as it is by far the most complicated part; and as nothing is to be repeated concerning it in this chapter, you must not fail to carry it in your mind, or under your arm, as we proceed, otherwise you will not gain a complete notion of the general structure we are about to view.", "그러나 그 거대한 두개골은 골격 전체에서 워낙 큰 비중을 차지하는 데다, 단연코 가장 복잡한 부분이며, 이 장에서는 그것에 관해 다시 거론하지 않을 터이므로, 우리가 나아가는 동안 여러분은 그것을 머릿속에든 겨드랑이에든 반드시 지니고 있어야 한다. 그러지 않으면 이제 우리가 살펴보려는 전체 구조에 대한 온전한 개념을 얻지 못할 것이다."]], [["In length, the Sperm Whale’s skeleton at Tranque measured seventy-two feet; so that when fully invested and extended in life, he must have been ninety feet long; for in the whale, the skeleton loses about one fifth in length compared with the living body.", "트랑케에 있던 향유고래의 골격은 길이가 72피트(약 22미터)였다. 그러니 살아서 살이 온전히 덮이고 몸이 다 펼쳐졌을 때라면 그 길이는 90피트(약 27미터)에 이르렀을 것이다."], ["Of this seventy-two feet, his skull and jaw comprised some twenty feet, leaving some fifty feet of plain back-bone.", "고래의 경우, 골격은 살아 있는 몸에 비해 길이가 약 5분의 1쯤 줄어들기 때문이다. 이 72피트 가운데 두개골과 턱이 20피트(약 6미터)쯤을 차지했고, 나머지 50피트(약 15미터) 남짓이 순전한 등뼈였다."], ["Attached to this back-bone, for something less than a third of its length, was the mighty circular basket of ribs which once enclosed his vitals.", "이 등뼈에는 그 길이의 3분의 1에 조금 못 미치는 만큼, 한때 그 내장을 감싸고 있던 거대한 원형의 갈빗대 광주리가 붙어 있었다."]], [["To me this vast ivory-ribbed chest, with the long, unrelieved spine, extending far away from it in a straight line, not a little resembled the hull of a great ship new-laid upon the stocks, when only some twenty of her naked bow-ribs are inserted, and the keel is otherwise, for the time, but a long, disconnected timber.", "내게는 이 드넓은 상아빛 갈빗대 가슴팍이, 거기서 일직선으로 멀찍이 뻗어 나간 길고 가림 없는 등뼈와 더불어, 마치 선대(船臺) 위에 막 얹어 놓은 큰 배의 선체와 적잖이 닮아 보였다. 헐벗은 뱃머리 늑재 가운데 겨우 스무 개쯤이 끼워 맞춰진 채, 용골은 아직은 그저 길고 이음매 없는 한 토막의 재목에 지나지 않은 그런 배 말이다."]], [["The ribs were ten on a side.", "갈빗대는 한쪽에 열 개씩이었다."], ["The first, to begin from the neck, was nearly six feet long; the second, third, and fourth were each successively longer, till you came to the climax of the fifth, or one of the middle ribs, which measured eight feet and some inches.", "목 쪽에서부터 세기 시작하면, 첫 번째 갈빗대는 길이가 거의 6피트(약 1.8미터)였고, 둘째와 셋째와 넷째는 차례차례 점점 길어져, 다섯 번째, 곧 한가운데 갈빗대 가운데 하나의 절정에 이르렀는데, 그것은 길이가 8피트 몇 인치(약 2.5미터)였다."], ["From that part, the remaining ribs diminished, till the tenth and last only spanned five feet and some inches.", "그 부위부터 나머지 갈빗대들은 점점 줄어들어, 열 번째이자 마지막 갈빗대는 겨우 5피트 몇 인치(약 1.6미터)에 그쳤다."], ["In general thickness, they all bore a seemly correspondence to their length.", "굵기로 말하자면, 모두가 그 길이에 걸맞게 알맞은 비례를 이루고 있었다."], ["The middle ribs were the most arched.", "한가운데 갈빗대들이 가장 활처럼 휘어 있었다."], ["In some of the Arsacides they are used for beams whereon to lay footpath bridges over small streams.", "아르사키데스의 어떤 곳에서는 이 갈빗대를 들보로 삼아 작은 시내 위에 오솔길 다리를 놓는 데 쓴다."]], [["In considering these ribs, I could not but be struck anew with the circumstance, so variously repeated in this book, that the skeleton of the whale is by no means the mould of his invested form.", "이 갈빗대들을 살펴보면서, 나는 이 책에서 여러 차례 거듭 말해 온 그 사실에 새삼 충격을 받지 않을 수 없었다. 곧 고래의 골격은 결코 그 살이 덮인 형상의 거푸집이 아니라는 사실이다."], ["The largest of the Tranque ribs, one of the middle ones, occupied that part of the fish which, in life, is greatest in depth.", "트랑케 갈빗대 가운데 가장 큰 것, 곧 한가운데 갈빗대 하나는, 살아 있을 때 깊이가 가장 깊은 부위에 자리하고 있었다."], ["Now, the greatest depth of the invested body of this particular whale must have been at least sixteen feet; whereas, the corresponding rib measured but little more than eight feet.", "그런데 이 특정한 고래가 살이 덮였을 때 그 몸의 가장 깊은 곳은 적어도 16피트(약 4.9미터)는 되었을 텐데, 그에 해당하는 갈빗대의 길이는 겨우 8피트(약 2.4미터)를 조금 넘을 뿐이었다."], ["So that this rib only conveyed half of the true notion of the living magnitude of that part.", "그러니 이 갈빗대는 그 부위의 살아 있는 크기에 대한 참된 개념의 절반밖에 전해 주지 못하는 셈이다."], ["Besides, for some way, where I now saw but a naked spine, all that had been once wrapped round with tons of added bulk in flesh, muscle, blood, and bowels.", "게다가 지금 내가 헐벗은 등뼈만을 보고 있는 어느 구간은, 한때 살과 근육과 피와 내장으로 더해진 수 톤의 부피로 휘감겨 있던 자리였다."], ["Still more, for the ample fins, I here saw but a few disordered joints; and in place of the weighty and majestic, but boneless flukes, an utter blank!", "더욱이, 그 널찍한 지느러미가 있던 자리에서 나는 여기 흐트러진 몇 개의 관절만을 보았을 뿐이다. 그리고 그 묵직하고도 위풍당당하되 뼈는 없는 꼬리 지느러미 자리에는, 그저 텅 빈 공백뿐이었다!"]], [["How vain and foolish, then, thought I, for timid untravelled man to try to comprehend aright this wondrous whale, by merely poring over his dead attenuated skeleton, stretched in this peaceful wood.", "그렇다면 얼마나 헛되고 어리석은 일인가, 하고 나는 생각했다. 여행이라곤 모르는 겁 많은 사람이 이 경이로운 고래를 제대로 이해해 보겠다고, 이 고요한 숲속에 펼쳐진 채 죽어 메말라 버린 골격만을 들여다보는 일이란."], ["No.", "아니다."], ["Only in the heart of quickest perils; only when within the eddyings of his angry flukes; only on the profound unbounded sea, can the fully invested whale be truly and livingly found out.", "오로지 가장 급박한 위험의 한복판에서만, 오로지 성난 꼬리 지느러미가 일으키는 소용돌이 속에 들어섰을 때에만, 오로지 저 깊고 가없는 바다 위에서만, 살이 온전히 덮인 고래를 참되고도 생생하게 알아낼 수 있는 법이다."]], [["But the spine.", "그러나 등뼈는 다르다."], ["For that, the best way we can consider it is, with a crane, to pile its bones high up on end.", "그것을 살펴보는 가장 좋은 방법은 기중기를 써서 그 뼈들을 끝으로 세워 높이 쌓아 올리는 것이다."], ["No speedy enterprise.", "결코 빠르게 끝낼 수 있는 일은 아니다."], ["But now it’s done, it looks much like Pompey’s Pillar.", "하지만 이제 다 쌓고 보니, 그것은 영락없이 폼페이우스의 기둥과 비슷한 모양이다."]], [["There are forty and odd vertebræ in all, which in the skeleton are not locked together.", "척추뼈는 모두 마흔 몇 개인데, 골격 상태에서는 서로 맞물려 있지 않다."], ["They mostly lie like the great knobbed blocks on a Gothic spire, forming solid courses of heavy masonry.", "그것들은 대개 고딕 첨탑의 커다란 돌출 석재 토막들처럼 놓여, 묵직한 석조물의 단단한 층을 이룬다."], ["The largest, a middle one, is in width something less than three feet, and in depth more than four.", "가장 큰 것, 곧 한가운데 척추뼈 하나는 너비가 3피트(약 0.9미터)에 조금 못 미치고, 깊이는 4피트(약 1.2미터)가 넘는다."], ["The smallest, where the spine tapers away into the tail, is only two inches in width, and looks something like a white billiard-ball.", "가장 작은 것, 곧 등뼈가 가늘어져 꼬리로 사라져 가는 부위의 척추뼈는 너비가 겨우 2인치(약 5센티미터)에 지나지 않아, 마치 하얀 당구공처럼 보인다."], ["I was told that there were still smaller ones, but they had been lost by some little cannibal urchins, the priest’s children, who had stolen them to play marbles with.", "그보다 더 작은 것들도 있었다는 말을 나는 들었으나, 그것들은 사제의 자식들인 어린 식인종 개구쟁이 몇이 구슬치기를 하려고 훔쳐 가는 바람에 잃어버렸다고 했다."], ["Thus we see how that the spine of even the hugest of living things tapers off at last into simple child’s play.", "이리하여 우리는 살아 있는 것들 가운데 가장 거대한 것의 등뼈조차, 마침내는 한낱 아이들의 장난거리로 가늘어져 끝나 버리는 모습을 보게 되는 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 104. The Fossil Whale.", "# CHAPTER 104. The Fossil Whale."]], [["From his mighty bulk the whale affords a most congenial theme whereon to enlarge, amplify, and generally expatiate.", "그 거대한 몸집 덕분에 고래는 자세히 늘어놓고, 부풀리며, 두루 장광설을 펼치기에 더없이 어울리는 주제가 된다."], ["Would you, you could not compress him.", "설령 그러고 싶다 한들 그대는 그를 압축할 수 없을 것이다."], ["By good rights he should only be treated of in imperial folio.", "마땅한 권리로 따지자면 그는 오직 제왕적 2절판 책에서만 다루어져야 한다."], ["Not to tell over again his furlongs from spiracle to tail, and the yards he measures about the waist; only think of the gigantic involutions of his intestines, where they lie in him like great cables and hawsers coiled away in the subterranean orlop-deck of a line-of-battle-ship.", "분수공에서 꼬리까지 그 길이가 몇 펄롱(furlong)인지, 허리둘레가 몇 야드인지 다시 늘어놓을 것까지도 없이, 그의 창자가 이루는 거대한 굽이만 떠올려 보라. 그것은 전열함(戰列艦)의 지하 최하갑판에 사려 둔 굵은 케이블과 닻줄처럼 그의 몸속에 사려져 있다."]], [["Since I have undertaken to manhandle this Leviathan, it behooves me to approve myself omnisciently exhaustive in the enterprise; not overlooking the minutest seminal germs of his blood, and spinning him out to the uttermost coil of his bowels.", "이 리바이어던(Leviathan)을 직접 다루는 일을 떠맡은 이상, 나는 이 사업에서 전지전능하리만큼 빠짐없이 해내는 모습을 스스로 입증하는 것이 마땅하다. 그의 피 속에 깃든 가장 미세한 씨앗의 싹조차 놓치지 않고, 그의 창자가 사려진 가장 끝 굽이까지 그를 낱낱이 풀어내야 하는 것이다."], ["Having already described him in most of his present habitatory and anatomical peculiarities, it now remains to magnify him in an archæological, fossiliferous, and antediluvian point of view.", "나는 이미 그를 현재의 서식과 해부학적 특징의 대부분에 걸쳐 묘사해 두었으니, 이제 남은 일은 고고학적이고 화석학적이며 대홍수 이전의 관점에서 그를 확대해 보는 것이다."], ["Applied to any other creature than the Leviathan—to an ant or a flea—such portly terms might justly be deemed unwarrantably grandiloquent.", "리바이어던이 아닌 다른 어떤 피조물에게—이를테면 개미나 벼룩에게—이처럼 거창한 말들을 갖다 붙인다면 부당하게 과장된 호언장담이라고 여겨도 마땅할 것이다."], ["But when Leviathan is the text, the case is altered.", "그러나 리바이어던이 주제가 될 때는 사정이 달라진다."], ["Fain am I to stagger to this emprise under the weightiest words of the dictionary.", "나는 사전에 실린 가장 묵직한 낱말들의 무게에 짓눌려 비틀거리면서라도 기꺼이 이 위업에 뛰어들고자 한다."], ["And here be it said, that whenever it has been convenient to consult one in the course of these dissertations, I have invariably used a huge quarto edition of Johnson, expressly purchased for that purpose; because that famous lexicographer’s uncommon personal bulk more fitted him to compile a lexicon to be used by a whale author like me.", "그리고 여기서 한마디 해 두건대, 이 논고들을 써 내려가는 동안 사전을 펴 볼 일이 생길 때마다 나는 늘 오로지 그 목적을 위해 따로 사 둔 거대한 4절판 존슨 사전을 썼다. 그 유명한 사전 편찬자의 남달리 풍채 좋은 몸집이야말로, 나 같은 고래 작가가 쓸 사전을 엮어 내기에 한결 잘 어울린다고 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["One often hears of writers that rise and swell with their subject, though it may seem but an ordinary one.", "흔히들 작가가 제 주제와 더불어 솟아오르고 부풀어 오른다고 하는데, 그 주제가 한낱 평범한 것일지라도 그렇다는 것이다."], ["How, then, with me, writing of this Leviathan?", "그렇다면 이 리바이어던을 쓰고 있는 나는 어떠하겠는가?"], ["Unconsciously my chirography expands into placard capitals.", "나도 모르는 사이에 내 필체는 벽보에나 쓰일 큰 대문자로 부풀어 오른다."], ["Give me a condor’s quill!", "콘도르의 깃펜을 내게 다오!"], ["Give me Vesuvius’ crater for an inkstand!", "베수비오 화산의 분화구를 내게 잉크병으로 다오!"], ["Friends, hold my arms!", "벗들이여, 내 두 팔을 붙들어 다오!"], ["For in the mere act of penning my thoughts of this Leviathan, they weary me, and make me faint with their outreaching comprehensiveness of sweep, as if to include the whole circle of the sciences, and all the generations of whales, and men, and mastodons, past, present, and to come, with all the revolving panoramas of empire on earth, and throughout the whole universe, not excluding its suburbs.", "한낱 이 리바이어던에 관한 내 생각을 펜으로 적어 내려가는 것만으로도, 그 생각들은 나를 지치게 하고, 그 휩쓸며 뻗어 나가는 포괄성으로 나를 까무러치게 만들기 때문이다. 마치 온갖 학문의 영역 전체와, 과거와 현재와 미래에 걸친 고래와 인간과 마스토돈의 모든 세대와, 지상에서, 또 그 변두리까지 빠짐없이 온 우주에 걸쳐 펼쳐지는 제국들의 빙글빙글 도는 파노라마 전부를 끌어안으려는 듯이 말이다."], ["Such, and so magnifying, is the virtue of a large and liberal theme!", "크고 너그러운 주제가 지닌 미덕이란 이러하며, 또 이토록 사람을 키워 놓는 것이다!"], ["We expand to its bulk.", "우리는 그 거대함에 맞추어 부풀어 오른다."], ["To produce a mighty book, you must choose a mighty theme.", "웅대한 책을 지어내려면 웅대한 주제를 골라야 한다."], ["No great and enduring volume can ever be written on the flea, though many there be who have tried it.", "벼룩에 관해서는 위대하고 길이 남을 책을 결코 쓸 수 없으니, 비록 그것을 시도한 사람이 숱하게 많았다 한들 그러하다."]], [["Ere entering upon the subject of Fossil Whales, I present my credentials as a geologist, by stating that in my miscellaneous time I have been a stone-mason, and also a great digger of ditches, canals and wells, wine-vaults, cellars, and cisterns of all sorts.", "화석 고래라는 주제로 들어가기에 앞서, 나는 지질학자로서의 자격을 내보이고자 한다. 곧, 이런저런 일을 하던 시절에 나는 석공 노릇도 했고, 또한 온갖 종류의 도랑과 운하와 우물, 포도주 저장고와 지하실과 물탱크를 파헤치는 데에도 일가견이 있었음을 밝혀 둔다."], ["Likewise, by way of preliminary, I desire to remind the reader, that while in the earlier geological strata there are found the fossils of monsters now almost completely extinct; the subsequent relics discovered in what are called the Tertiary formations seem the connecting, or at any rate intercepted links, between the antichronical creatures, and those whose remote posterity are said to have entered the Ark; all the Fossil Whales hitherto discovered belong to the Tertiary period, which is the last preceding the superficial formations.", "마찬가지로 들머리 삼아 독자에게 일러두고 싶은 것은, 더 이른 지질층에서는 이제 거의 완전히 멸종해 버린 괴물들의 화석이 발견되는 반면, 이른바 제3기 지층이라 불리는 곳에서 그 뒤에 발견된 유물들은 그 시대를 거스르는 태곳적 피조물들과, 그 먼 후손이 방주에 올랐다고 전해지는 피조물들 사이를 잇는, 적어도 중간에 끼어 있는 고리처럼 보인다는 점이다. 지금까지 발견된 모든 화석 고래는 제3기에 속하는데, 이는 표층 지층 바로 앞에 오는 마지막 시기다."], ["And though none of them precisely answer to any known species of the present time, they are yet sufficiently akin to them in general respects, to justify their taking rank as Cetacean fossils.", "그리고 그중 어느 것도 현재의 알려진 어떤 종(種)과 정확히 일치하지는 않지만, 전반적인 면에서는 그것들과 충분히 닮은 점이 있어, 고래목 화석이라는 자리를 차지할 만한 자격은 갖추고 있다."]], [["Detached broken fossils of pre-adamite whales, fragments of their bones and skeletons, have within thirty years past, at various intervals, been found at the base of the Alps, in Lombardy, in France, in England, in Scotland, and in the States of Louisiana, Mississippi, and Alabama.", "아담 이전 시대의 고래들이 남긴 떨어져 나간 부서진 화석들, 곧 그 뼈와 골격의 조각들은 지난 삼십 년 동안 여러 차례에 걸쳐 알프스 기슭과 롬바르디아, 프랑스, 잉글랜드, 스코틀랜드, 그리고 루이지애나와 미시시피와 앨라배마 주에서 발견되어 왔다."], ["Among the more curious of such remains is part of a skull, which in the year 1779 was disinterred in the Rue Dauphine in Paris, a short street opening almost directly upon the palace of the Tuileries; and bones disinterred in excavating the great docks of Antwerp, in Napoleon’s time.", "그러한 유물 가운데 더욱 진기한 것으로는 1779년에 파리의 도핀 거리에서 파헤쳐진 두개골의 일부가 있는데, 그 거리는 거의 곧장 튀일리 궁을 마주 보고 열려 있는 짧은 골목이다. 또한 나폴레옹 시대에 앤트워프의 거대한 부두를 파내던 중에 발굴된 뼈들도 있다."], ["Cuvier pronounced these fragments to have belonged to some utterly unknown Leviathanic species.", "퀴비에는 이 조각들이 전혀 알려지지 않은 어떤 리바이어던 종에 속한 것이라고 단언했다."]], [["But by far the most wonderful of all Cetacean relics was the almost complete vast skeleton of an extinct monster, found in the year 1842, on the plantation of Judge Creagh, in Alabama.", "그러나 모든 고래목 유물 가운데 단연 가장 놀라운 것은 1842년에 앨라배마의 크리그 판사 농장에서 발견된, 거의 온전한 형태의 어느 멸종한 괴물의 거대한 골격이었다."], ["The awe-stricken credulous slaves in the vicinity took it for the bones of one of the fallen angels.", "인근의 두려움에 사로잡힌 잘 믿는 노예들은 그것을 타락한 천사들 가운데 하나의 뼈로 여겼다."], ["The Alabama doctors declared it a huge reptile, and bestowed upon it the name of Basilosaurus.", "앨라배마의 박사들은 그것을 거대한 파충류라고 선언하며 바실로사우루스라는 이름을 붙였다."], ["But some specimen bones of it being taken across the sea to Owen, the English Anatomist, it turned out that this alleged reptile was a whale, though of a departed species.", "그러나 그 표본 뼈 몇 점이 바다 건너 영국의 해부학자 오언에게 보내지자, 이 파충류라던 것이 실은 멸종한 종에 속하기는 하나 고래였음이 드러났다."], ["A significant illustration of the fact, again and again repeated in this book, that the skeleton of the whale furnishes but little clue to the shape of his fully invested body.", "이 책에서 거듭거듭 되풀이된 사실, 곧 고래의 골격이 그의 살로 온전히 둘러싸인 몸의 형태를 알려 주는 단서는 거의 되지 못한다는 사실을 다시금 의미심장하게 보여 주는 예다."], ["So Owen rechristened the monster Zeuglodon; and in his paper read before the London Geological Society, pronounced it, in substance, one of the most extraordinary creatures which the mutations of the globe have blotted out of existence.", "그리하여 오언은 그 괴물을 제우글로돈이라 다시 이름 지었고, 런던 지질학회에서 낭독한 논문에서 그것을 요컨대 지구의 변천이 이 세상에서 지워 버린 가장 비범한 피조물 가운데 하나라고 선언했다."]], [["When I stand among these mighty Leviathan skeletons, skulls, tusks, jaws, ribs, and vertebræ, all characterized by partial resemblances to the existing breeds of sea-monsters; but at the same time bearing on the other hand similar affinities to the annihilated antichronical Leviathans, their incalculable seniors; I am, by a flood, borne back to that wondrous period, ere time itself can be said to have begun; for time began with man.", "내가 이 거대한 리바이어던의 골격과 두개골, 엄니와 턱뼈, 갈빗대와 척추뼈 사이에 서 있노라면—그것들은 모두 현존하는 바다 괴물 종족과 부분적으로 닮은 점을 지니면서도, 다른 한편으로는 가늠할 수 없을 만큼 더 오래된 선조인, 절멸한 태곳적 리바이어던들과도 비슷한 친연성을 지니고 있는데—나는 마치 홍수에 휩쓸리듯, 시간 자체가 시작되었다고 말할 수조차 없는 저 경이로운 시대로 떠밀려 간다. 시간은 인간과 더불어 시작되었으니 말이다."], ["Here Saturn’s grey chaos rolls over me, and I obtain dim, shuddering glimpses into those Polar eternities; when wedged bastions of ice pressed hard upon what are now the Tropics; and in all the 25,000 miles of this world’s circumference, not an inhabitable hand’s breadth of land was visible.", "여기서 사투르누스의 잿빛 혼돈이 내 위로 굽이쳐 흐르고, 나는 저 극지의 영원함을 흐릿하고 몸서리치게 엿보게 된다. 쐐기처럼 박힌 얼음의 성채가 지금의 열대 지방인 곳을 짓누르고, 이 세상 둘레 2만 5천 마일 전체에 사람이 깃들 만한 손바닥 너비의 땅조차 보이지 않던 그 시절을."], ["Then the whole world was the whale’s; and, king of creation, he left his wake along the present lines of the Andes and the Himmalehs.", "그때는 온 세상이 고래의 것이었으니, 만물의 왕인 그는 지금의 안데스산맥과 히말라야산맥의 능선을 따라 제 항적을 남겼다."], ["Who can show a pedigree like Leviathan?", "누가 리바이어던에 견줄 만한 혈통을 내보일 수 있겠는가?"], ["Ahab’s harpoon had shed older blood than the Pharaoh’s.", "에이해브의 작살은 파라오의 피보다 더 오래된 피를 흘리게 했다."], ["Methuselah seems a school-boy.", "므두셀라조차 한낱 학동으로 보인다."], ["I look round to shake hands with Shem.", "나는 셈과 악수라도 나누려고 사방을 둘러본다."], ["I am horror-struck at this antemosaic, unsourced existence of the unspeakable terrors of the whale, which, having been before all time, must needs exist after all humane ages are over.", "말로 다 할 수 없는 고래의 공포가, 모세 이전 시대의, 그 근원조차 알 수 없는 채로 존재해 왔다는 사실에 나는 소름이 끼친다. 모든 시간에 앞서 존재했던 그것은, 모든 인간의 시대가 끝난 뒤에도 어쩔 수 없이 존재할 것이기 때문이다."]], [["But not alone has this Leviathan left his pre-adamite traces in the stereotype plates of nature, and in limestone and marl bequeathed his ancient bust; but upon Egyptian tablets, whose antiquity seems to claim for them an almost fossiliferous character, we find the unmistakable print of his fin.", "그러나 이 리바이어던은 자연의 연판(鉛版) 위에, 또 석회암과 이회토(泥灰土) 속에 그 아담 이전의 흔적을 남기고 태곳적 흉상을 물려준 것에 그치지 않는다. 그 오래됨이 거의 화석에 가까운 성격을 띤다고 주장할 만한 이집트의 석판 위에서도, 우리는 틀림없는 그 지느러미의 자국을 발견한다."], ["In an apartment of the great temple of Denderah, some fifty years ago, there was discovered upon the granite ceiling a sculptured and painted planisphere, abounding in centaurs, griffins, and dolphins, similar to the grotesque figures on the celestial globe of the moderns.", "약 오십 년 전, 덴데라의 거대한 신전 한 방에서 화강암 천장에 새겨지고 채색된 천체도가 발견되었는데, 그것은 켄타우로스와 그리핀과 돌고래로 가득하여, 현대인들의 천구의(天球儀)에 그려진 기괴한 형상들과 비슷했다."], ["Gliding among them, old Leviathan swam as of yore; was there swimming in that planisphere, centuries before Solomon was cradled.", "그것들 사이를 미끄러지듯, 늙은 리바이어던은 예전과 다름없이 헤엄치고 있었으니, 솔로몬이 요람에 누이기 수백 년 전부터 그 천체도 속을 헤엄치고 있었던 것이다."]], [["Nor must there be omitted another strange attestation of the antiquity of the whale, in his own osseous post-diluvian reality, as set down by the venerable John Leo, the old Barbary traveller.", "또한 빼놓아서는 안 될 것이, 늙은 바르바리 여행가 존경할 만한 존 레오가 적어 둔, 고래 자신의 뼈로 이루어진 대홍수 이후의 실재를 통해 그 오래됨을 입증하는 또 하나의 기이한 증언이다."]], [["“Not far from the Sea-side, they have a Temple, the Rafters and Beams of which are made of Whale-Bones; for Whales of a monstrous size are oftentimes cast up dead upon that shore.", "\"바닷가에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 그들은 신전 하나를 두고 있는데, 그 서까래와 들보가 모두 고래 뼈로 만들어져 있다. 괴물처럼 거대한 고래들이 종종 죽은 채 그 해안으로 떠밀려 오기 때문이다."], ["The Common People imagine, that by a secret Power bestowed by God upon the Temple, no Whale can pass it without immediate death.", "평민들은 하느님께서 그 신전에 내려 주신 은밀한 힘으로 인하여, 어떤 고래도 그 앞을 지나가면 곧바로 죽지 않고는 못 배긴다고 상상한다."], ["But the truth of the Matter is, that on either side of the Temple, there are Rocks that shoot two Miles into the Sea, and wound the Whales when they light upon ’em.", "그러나 그 일의 진상은, 신전 양옆으로 바다를 향해 2마일이나 뻗어 나간 바위들이 있어, 고래들이 그 위에 걸려들면 상처를 입게 된다는 것이다."], ["They keep a Whale’s Rib of an incredible length for a Miracle, which lying upon the Ground with its convex part uppermost, makes an Arch, the Head of which cannot be reached by a Man upon a Camel’s Back.", "그들은 도무지 믿기 어려운 길이의 고래 갈빗대 하나를 기적으로 여겨 간직하고 있는데, 그것은 볼록한 쪽을 위로 하여 땅에 놓여 아치를 이루고 있으며, 그 마루에는 낙타 등에 탄 사람도 손이 닿지 않는다."], ["This Rib (says John Leo) is said to have layn there a hundred Years before I saw it.", "이 갈빗대는 (존 레오가 이르기를) 내가 보기 백 년 전부터 그곳에 놓여 있었다고 전해진다."], ["Their Historians affirm, that a Prophet who prophesy’d of Mahomet, came from this Temple, and some do not stand to assert, that the Prophet Jonas was cast forth by the Whale at the Base of the Temple.”", "그들의 사가(史家)들은 마호메트를 예언한 한 예언자가 이 신전에서 나왔다고 단언하며, 더러는 예언자 요나(Jonah)가 이 신전 기슭에서 고래에게 뱉어졌노라고 서슴없이 주장하기도 한다.\""]], [["In this Afric Temple of the Whale I leave you, reader, and if you be a Nantucketer, and a whaleman, you will silently worship there.", "이 아프리카의 고래 신전에 독자여, 나는 그대를 남겨 두고 떠난다. 그리고 그대가 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람이요 고래잡이라면, 그대는 그곳에서 말없이 경배하리라."]], [["# CHAPTER 105. Does the Whale’s Magnitude Diminish?—Will He Perish?", "# CHAPTER 105. Does the Whale’s Magnitude Diminish?—Will He Perish?"]], [["Inasmuch, then, as this Leviathan comes floundering down upon us from the head-waters of the Eternities, it may be fitly inquired, whether, in the long course of his generations, he has not degenerated from the original bulk of his sires.", "그렇다면 이 리바이어던이 영원의 원천에서부터 우리에게로 허우적대며 떠내려온 것이라 할진대, 그토록 긴 세대의 흐름 속에서 그가 제 조상들의 본래 거구로부터 퇴화하지는 않았는지를 한번 따져 봄직도 하다."]], [["But upon investigation we find, that not only are the whales of the present day superior in magnitude to those whose fossil remains are found in the Tertiary system (embracing a distinct geological period prior to man), but of the whales found in that Tertiary system, those belonging to its latter formations exceed in size those of its earlier ones.", "그러나 조사해 보면 다음과 같은 사실을 알게 된다. 오늘날의 고래들이 제3기 지층(인류 이전의 뚜렷한 한 지질 시대를 아우르는)에서 그 화석 잔해가 발견되는 고래들보다 크기에서 우월할 뿐만 아니라, 그 제3기 지층에서 발견된 고래들 가운데서도 후기 지층에 속한 것들이 초기 지층의 것들을 크기에서 능가한다는 것이다."]], [["Of all the pre-adamite whales yet exhumed, by far the largest is the Alabama one mentioned in the last chapter, and that was less than seventy feet in length in the skeleton.", "지금껏 발굴된 모든 아담 이전 고래들 가운데 단연 가장 큰 것은 앞 장에서 언급한 앨라배마의 고래인데, 그것조차 골격 길이가 70피트(약 21미터)에 못 미쳤다."], ["Whereas, we have already seen, that the tape-measure gives seventy-two feet for the skeleton of a large sized modern whale.", "반면 우리는 이미 줄자로 재어 보았을 때 큼직한 현대 고래의 골격이 72피트(약 22미터)에 이른다는 사실을 살펴본 바 있다."], ["And I have heard, on whalemen’s authority, that Sperm Whales have been captured near a hundred feet long at the time of capture.", "그리고 나는 고래잡이들의 권위 있는 증언을 통해, 향유고래(Sperm Whale)가 포획 당시 길이가 거의 100피트(약 30미터)에 달하는 채로 잡힌 적이 있다는 말을 들었다."]], [["But may it not be, that while the whales of the present hour are an advance in magnitude upon those of all previous geological periods; may it not be, that since Adam’s time they have degenerated?", "그러나 혹시 이런 것은 아닐까. 지금 이 시대의 고래들이 이전의 모든 지질 시대 고래들보다 크기에서 진보했다 하더라도, 아담의 시대 이래로는 도리어 퇴화한 것은 아닐까?"]], [["Assuredly, we must conclude so, if we are to credit the accounts of such gentlemen as Pliny, and the ancient naturalists generally.", "플리니우스(Pliny)나 고대 박물학자들 일반의 기록을 믿어야 한다면, 우리는 분명 그렇게 결론지을 수밖에 없으리라."], ["For Pliny tells us of whales that embraced acres of living bulk, and Aldrovandus of others which measured eight hundred feet in length—Rope Walks and Thames Tunnels of Whales!", "플리니우스는 우리에게 살아 있는 거구가 몇 에이커에 달했다는 고래들에 관해 일러 주고, 알드로반두스(Aldrovandus)는 길이가 800피트(약 244미터)에 이르렀다는 또 다른 고래들에 관해 말한다 — 고래라기보다 밧줄 공장이나 템스 터널이라 할 만한 것들이다!"], ["And even in the days of Banks and Solander, Cooke’s naturalists, we find a Danish member of the Academy of Sciences setting down certain Iceland Whales (reydan-siskur, or Wrinkled Bellies) at one hundred and twenty yards; that is, three hundred and sixty feet.", "게다가 쿡(Cooke)의 박물학자였던 뱅크스와 솔란데르의 시대에도, 우리는 어느 덴마크 출신 과학원 회원이 어떤 아이슬란드 고래들(레이단시스쿠르, 곧 주름진 배라 불리는 종류)을 120야드, 곧 360피트(약 110미터)로 기록해 둔 것을 본다."], ["And Lacépède, the French naturalist, in his elaborate history of whales, in the very beginning of his work (page 3), sets down the Right Whale at one hundred metres, three hundred and twenty-eight feet.", "그리고 프랑스 박물학자 라세페드(Lacépède)는 그의 정교한 고래 연구서의 바로 첫머리(3쪽)에서 참고래(Right Whale)를 100미터, 곧 328피트(약 100미터)로 적어 놓았다."], ["And this work was published so late as A.D. 1825.", "그런데 이 저작은 무려 서기 1825년에야 출간된 것이다."]], [["But will any whaleman believe these stories? No.", "그러나 어느 고래잡이가 이런 이야기를 믿겠는가?"], ["The whale of to-day is as big as his ancestors in Pliny’s time.", "결코 믿지 않으리라. 오늘날의 고래는 플리니우스 시대의 제 조상들만큼이나 크다."], ["And if ever I go where Pliny is, I, a whaleman (more than he was), will make bold to tell him so.", "그리고 만약 내가 언젠가 플리니우스가 있는 곳으로 가게 된다면, 그보다 더한 고래잡이인 나는 대담하게도 그에게 그렇게 일러 주리라."], ["Because I cannot understand how it is, that while the Egyptian mummies that were buried thousands of years before even Pliny was born, do not measure so much in their coffins as a modern Kentuckian in his socks; and while the cattle and other animals sculptured on the oldest Egyptian and Nineveh tablets, by the relative proportions in which they are drawn, just as plainly prove that the high-bred, stall-fed, prize cattle of Smithfield, not only equal, but far exceed in magnitude the fattest of Pharaoh’s fat kine; in the face of all this, I will not admit that of all animals the whale alone should have degenerated.", "왜냐하면 나는 도무지 이해할 수가 없기 때문이다. 플리니우스가 태어나기도 수천 년 전에 묻힌 이집트 미라들이 그 관 속에서 양말 신은 현대 켄터키 사람만큼의 키도 되지 못하며, 또한 가장 오래된 이집트와 니네베의 석판에 새겨진 소와 그 밖의 동물들이 그 그려진 상대적 비례로 보아, 혈통 좋고 우리에서 살찌워 상을 받은 스미스필드의 소들이 파라오의 살진 소들 가운데 가장 기름진 것과 견주어도 어깨를 나란히 할 뿐 아니라 크기에서 훨씬 능가한다는 것을 더없이 명백히 입증하거늘 — 이 모든 것을 앞에 두고서, 어찌 모든 동물 가운데 유독 고래만이 퇴화했다고 인정할 수 있겠는가."]], [["But still another inquiry remains; one often agitated by the more recondite Nantucketers.", "그러나 또 하나의 물음이 남아 있으니, 좀 더 사정에 밝은 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람들이 종종 떠들어 대는 것이다."], ["Whether owing to the almost omniscient look-outs at the mast-heads of the whale-ships, now penetrating even through Behring’s straits, and into the remotest secret drawers and lockers of the world; and the thousand harpoons and lances darted along all continental coasts; the moot point is, whether Leviathan can long endure so wide a chase, and so remorseless a havoc; whether he must not at last be exterminated from the waters, and the last whale, like the last man, smoke his last pipe, and then himself evaporate in the final puff.", "이제는 베링 해협을 뚫고 들어가 세상의 가장 후미진 비밀 서랍과 사물함 속까지 파고드는, 거의 전지(全知)에 가까운 고래잡이배 돛대 꼭대기의 망보기들과, 모든 대륙 연안을 따라 던져지는 수천 자루의 작살과 창 때문에, 리바이어던이 과연 그토록 광범위한 추격과 그토록 무자비한 살육을 오래 견딜 수 있을 것인가 — 마침내 그가 바다에서 절멸당하여, 마지막 고래가 마지막 인간처럼 그 마지막 담뱃대를 피우고 나서 끝내 그 마지막 한 모금과 함께 스스로 증발해 버리지나 않을 것인가. 이것이 바로 논쟁의 핵심이다."]], [["Comparing the humped herds of whales with the humped herds of buffalo, which, not forty years ago, overspread by tens of thousands the prairies of Illinois and Missouri, and shook their iron manes and scowled with their thunder-clotted brows upon the sites of populous river-capitals, where now the polite broker sells you land at a dollar an inch; in such a comparison an irresistible argument would seem furnished, to show that the hunted whale cannot now escape speedy extinction.", "혹 모양으로 등을 솟구친 고래 떼를, 그와 같이 등을 솟구친 들소 떼와 견주어 보자. 들소들은 불과 사십 년 전만 해도 일리노이와 미주리의 대초원을 수만 마리씩 뒤덮으며, 철빛 갈기를 흔들고 천둥처럼 뭉쳐진 미간을 찌푸리며, 사람들로 북적이는 강변 도시들이 들어선 자리를 굽어보았다. 그러나 이제 그 자리에서는 점잖은 중개인이 땅을 1인치에 1달러씩 팔아넘긴다. 이런 비교에서라면, 쫓기는 고래가 이제 머지않은 절멸을 피할 수 없다는 것을 보여 주는 거부할 수 없는 논거가 마련된 듯이 보일 것이다."]], [["But you must look at this matter in every light.", "그러나 그대들은 이 문제를 온갖 각도에서 들여다보아야 한다."], ["Though so short a period ago—not a good lifetime—the census of the buffalo in Illinois exceeded the census of men now in London, and though at the present day not one horn or hoof of them remains in all that region; and though the cause of this wondrous extermination was the spear of man; yet the far different nature of the whale-hunt peremptorily forbids so inglorious an end to the Leviathan.", "비록 그토록 짧은 시기 전 — 넉넉한 한평생도 못 되는 — 일리노이의 들소 수효가 지금 런던에 사는 사람의 수효를 넘어섰고, 또 오늘날에는 그 일대 어디에도 그것들의 뿔 하나 발굽 하나 남아 있지 않으며, 게다가 이 놀라운 절멸의 원인이 인간의 창이었다 하더라도, 고래잡이가 지닌 전혀 다른 성격은 리바이어던에게 그토록 영광 없는 종말을 단호히 허락하지 않는다."], ["Forty men in one ship hunting the Sperm Whales for forty-eight months think they have done extremely well, and thank God, if at last they carry home the oil of forty fish.", "한 배에 탄 마흔 명의 사람이 마흔여덟 달 동안 향유고래를 사냥하여, 마침내 고래 마흔 마리의 기름을 싣고 집으로 돌아온다면 그들은 더없이 잘해냈다고 여기며 하느님께 감사를 드린다."], ["Whereas, in the days of the old Canadian and Indian hunters and trappers of the West, when the far west (in whose sunset suns still rise) was a wilderness and a virgin, the same number of moccasined men, for the same number of months, mounted on horse instead of sailing in ships, would have slain not forty, but forty thousand and more buffaloes; a fact that, if need were, could be statistically stated.", "반면 옛 캐나다와 인디언(Indian)의 서부 사냥꾼이자 덫꾼이던 자들의 시절, 곧 머나먼 서부(그 노을 진 곳에서도 해는 여전히 떠오른다)가 황야이자 처녀지였을 무렵에는, 똑같은 수의 모카신을 신은 사내들이 똑같은 수의 달 동안, 배를 타는 대신 말에 올라타고서, 마흔 마리가 아니라 사만 마리도 넘는 들소를 죽였을 것이다. 필요하다면 통계로도 진술할 수 있는 사실이다."]], [["Nor, considered aright, does it seem any argument in favour of the gradual extinction of the Sperm Whale, for example, that in former years (the latter part of the last century, say) these Leviathans, in small pods, were encountered much oftener than at present, and, in consequence, the voyages were not so prolonged, and were also much more remunerative.", "또한, 바르게 따져 보면, 이를테면 향유고래가 점차 절멸해 간다는 주장에 유리한 근거로는 보이지 않는 일이 있다. 곧 지난 세월(이를테면 지난 세기의 후반)에는 이 리바이어던들이 작은 무리를 지어 지금보다 훨씬 자주 마주쳐졌고, 그 결과 항해 기간이 그리 길지 않았으며 또 훨씬 수지가 맞았다는 사실이다."], ["Because, as has been elsewhere noticed, those whales, influenced by some views to safety, now swim the seas in immense caravans, so that to a large degree the scattered solitaries, yokes, and pods, and schools of other days are now aggregated into vast but widely separated, unfrequent armies.", "왜냐하면 다른 데서 이미 짚어 두었듯이, 이 고래들이 무언가 안전을 위한 생각에 이끌려 이제는 거대한 대상(隊商)을 이루어 바다를 헤엄치는 까닭에, 옛날의 흩어진 외톨이며 한 쌍이며 작은 무리며 큰 떼들이 이제는 대체로 거대하되 서로 멀찍이 떨어진, 좀처럼 만나기 어려운 군단으로 결집되었기 때문이다."], ["That is all.", "그것이 전부다."], ["And equally fallacious seems the conceit, that because the so-called whale-bone whales no longer haunt many grounds in former years abounding with them, hence that species also is declining.", "그리고 이른바 고래수염 고래들이 지난 세월 그것들로 우글거리던 여러 어장에 더는 출몰하지 않으니 그 종(種) 또한 쇠퇴하는 중이라는 생각도 마찬가지로 그릇된 것이다."], ["For they are only being driven from promontory to cape; and if one coast is no longer enlivened with their jets, then, be sure, some other and remoter strand has been very recently startled by the unfamiliar spectacle.", "왜냐하면 그것들은 그저 곶에서 곶으로 쫓겨 다닐 뿐이며, 어느 한 연안이 더 이상 그들의 물줄기로 활기를 띠지 못한다면, 분명 또 다른 더 먼 기슭이 바로 얼마 전에 그 낯선 광경에 깜짝 놀랐을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["Furthermore: concerning these last mentioned Leviathans, they have two firm fortresses, which, in all human probability, will for ever remain impregnable.", "게다가, 방금 언급한 이 리바이어던들로 말하자면, 그들에게는 인간으로서는 도무지 영원히 함락할 수 없을 것이 거의 분명한 두 개의 견고한 요새가 있다."], ["And as upon the invasion of their valleys, the frosty Swiss have retreated to their mountains; so, hunted from the savannas and glades of the middle seas, the whale-bone whales can at last resort to their Polar citadels, and diving under the ultimate glassy barriers and walls there, come up among icy fields and floes; and in a charmed circle of everlasting December, bid defiance to all pursuit from man.", "그리고 제 골짜기로 침입을 받으면 서리 덮인 스위스 사람들이 저들의 산속으로 물러나듯, 가운데 바다의 너른 초원과 빈터에서 쫓긴 고래수염 고래들도 마침내 그들의 극지(極地) 성채로 피신할 수 있으니, 그곳의 궁극적인 유리 같은 장벽과 벽 아래로 잠수해 들어가 얼음 들판과 부빙(浮氷) 사이로 솟아올라, 영원한 12월의 마법진 안에서 인간의 온갖 추격을 비웃으며 버틸 수 있는 것이다."]], [["But as perhaps fifty of these whale-bone whales are harpooned for one cachalot, some philosophers of the forecastle have concluded that this positive havoc has already very seriously diminished their battalions.", "그러나 향유고래 한 마리를 잡는 동안 이 고래수염 고래는 어쩌면 쉰 마리쯤 작살에 꿰이는지라, 선원 선실의 어떤 철학자들은 이 명백한 살육이 이미 그들의 군세를 매우 심각하게 줄여 놓았다고 결론지었다."], ["But though for some time past a number of these whales, not less than 13,000, have been annually slain on the nor’ west coast by the Americans alone; yet there are considerations which render even this circumstance of little or no account as an opposing argument in this matter.", "그러나 비록 얼마 전부터 미국인들만으로도 해마다 1만 3000마리 이상의 이 고래들이 북서 연안에서 도살되어 왔다 하더라도, 이 사정조차 이 문제에서 반대 논거로는 거의, 아니 전혀 무게가 없게 만드는 여러 고려 사항들이 있다."]], [["Natural as it is to be somewhat incredulous concerning the populousness of the more enormous creatures of the globe, yet what shall we say to Harto, the historian of Goa, when he tells us that at one hunting the King of Siam took 4,000 elephants; that in those regions elephants are numerous as droves of cattle in the temperate climes.", "지구상의 더 거대한 피조물들이 그토록 많다는 데에 다소 미심쩍어하는 것이야 자연스러운 일이지만, 그렇다 한들 고아(Goa)의 역사가 하르토(Harto)에 대해서는 무어라 할 것인가. 그가 우리에게 이르기를, 시암 왕이 한 차례 사냥에서 코끼리 4000마리를 잡았으며, 그 지역에서 코끼리는 온대 지방의 소 떼만큼이나 흔하다고 하니 말이다."], ["And there seems no reason to doubt that if these elephants, which have now been hunted for thousands of years, by Semiramis, by Porus, by Hannibal, and by all the successive monarchs of the East—if they still survive there in great numbers, much more may the great whale outlast all hunting, since he has a pasture to expatiate in, which is precisely twice as large as all Asia, both Americas, Europe and Africa, New Holland, and all the Isles of the sea combined.", "그리고 세미라미스, 포루스, 한니발, 그리고 동방의 역대 모든 군주들에 의해 이제껏 수천 년 동안 사냥당해 온 이 코끼리들이 — 만약 그들이 여전히 거기서 엄청난 수효로 살아남아 있다면, 거대한 고래는 더욱더 모든 사냥을 견뎌 내고도 남으리라는 것을 의심할 이유가 없어 보인다. 왜냐하면 그에게는 마음껏 노닐 목초지가 있는데, 그것은 아시아 전체와 두 아메리카, 유럽과 아프리카, 뉴홀란드(New Holland), 그리고 바다의 모든 섬들을 합친 것보다 정확히 두 배나 넓기 때문이다."]], [["Moreover: we are to consider, that from the presumed great longevity of whales, their probably attaining the age of a century and more, therefore at any one period of time, several distinct adult generations must be contemporary.", "뿐만 아니라 우리는 이런 점도 고려해야 한다. 고래가 매우 장수하리라 추정되는바, 아마도 한 세기 넘는 나이에까지 이르는 까닭에, 어느 한 시기에든 뚜렷이 구별되는 여러 세대의 성체들이 동시대에 함께 살아 있을 수밖에 없다는 것이다."], ["And what that is, we may soon gain some idea of, by imagining all the grave-yards, cemeteries, and family vaults of creation yielding up the live bodies of all the men, women, and children who were alive seventy-five years ago; and adding this countless host to the present human population of the globe.", "그것이 어떠한지는, 창조된 만물의 온갖 묘지와 공동묘지와 가족 납골당이 칠십오 년 전에 살아 있던 모든 남자와 여자와 아이들의 산 몸뚱이를 도로 토해 내고, 그 헤아릴 수 없는 무리를 지금 지구상의 인구에 더하는 광경을 상상해 보면 곧 어느 정도 짐작할 수 있으리라."]], [["Wherefore, for all these things, we account the whale immortal in his species, however perishable in his individuality.", "그러므로 이 모든 것들로 미루어, 우리는 고래가 비록 한 개체로서는 죽을 운명이라 할지라도 그 종(種)에서는 불멸이라 여기는 바이다."], ["He swam the seas before the continents broke water; he once swam over the site of the Tuileries, and Windsor Castle, and the Kremlin.", "그는 대륙들이 수면 위로 솟아오르기도 전에 바다를 헤엄쳤다. 그는 한때 튀일리 궁과 윈저 성과 크렘린이 들어선 자리 위를 헤엄쳐 다녔다."], ["In Noah’s flood he despised Noah’s Ark; and if ever the world is to be again flooded, like the Netherlands, to kill off its rats, then the eternal whale will still survive, and rearing upon the topmost crest of the equatorial flood, spout his frothed defiance to the skies.", "노아의 홍수 속에서도 그는 노아의 방주를 업신여겼으며, 만약 언젠가 이 세상이 쥐들을 쓸어내기 위해 네덜란드처럼 또다시 물에 잠긴다 하더라도, 영원한 고래는 그때에도 여전히 살아남아, 적도 위로 차오른 홍수의 가장 높은 마루 위로 몸을 일으켜 세우고는, 거품 이는 제 도전의 물줄기를 하늘을 향해 뿜어 올리리라."]], [["# CHAPTER 106. Ahab’s Leg.", "# CHAPTER 106. Ahab’s Leg."]], [["The precipitating manner in which Captain Ahab had quitted the Samuel Enderby of London, had not been unattended with some small violence to his own person.", "런던의 새뮤얼 엔더비호를 떠날 때 에이해브 선장이 보였던 그 황급한 태도는 그 자신의 몸에 작지 않은 충격을 가하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He had lighted with such energy upon a thwart of his boat that his ivory leg had received a half-splintering shock.", "그는 어찌나 거센 기세로 보트의 가로장 위에 내려섰던지, 고래 뼈로 만든 다리가 반쯤 쪼개질 만한 충격을 받고 말았다."], ["And when after gaining his own deck, and his own pivot-hole there, he so vehemently wheeled round with an urgent command to the steersman (it was, as ever, something about his not steering inflexibly enough); then, the already shaken ivory received such an additional twist and wrench, that though it still remained entire, and to all appearances lusty, yet Ahab did not deem it entirely trustworthy.", "그러고는 자기 배의 갑판에 올라서서 그곳에 마련된 자기만의 다리 받침 구멍에 다리를 박은 뒤, 키잡이에게 다급한 명령을 내리며 그토록 격렬하게 몸을 홱 돌렸을 때(그것은 늘 그렇듯 키를 충분히 흔들림 없이 잡지 못한다는 따위의 꾸지람이었다), 이미 흔들린 고래 뼈 다리는 또 한 번 비틀리고 꺾이는 힘을 받았다. 그리하여 다리가 여전히 온전한 채로 남았고 겉보기에는 멀쩡해 보였으나, 에이해브는 그것을 온전히 믿을 만한 것으로 여기지 않았다."]], [["And, indeed, it seemed small matter for wonder, that for all his pervading, mad recklessness, Ahab did at times give careful heed to the condition of that dead bone upon which he partly stood.", "사실 그가 온통 미친 듯한 무모함에 사로잡혀 있으면서도 때때로 자신이 그 위에 반쯤 의지하고 선 그 죽은 뼈의 상태에 세심한 주의를 기울였다는 것은 그다지 놀랄 만한 일이 아니었다."], ["For it had not been very long prior to the Pequod’s sailing from Nantucket, that he had been found one night lying prone upon the ground, and insensible; by some unknown, and seemingly inexplicable, unimaginable casualty, his ivory limb having been so violently displaced, that it had stake-wise smitten, and all but pierced his groin; nor was it without extreme difficulty that the agonizing wound was entirely cured.", "피쿼드호가 낸터킷을 떠나기 그리 오래되지 않은 어느 날 밤, 그가 땅바닥에 엎어진 채 의식을 잃고 쓰러져 있는 것이 발견된 적이 있었기 때문이다. 알 수 없고 도무지 설명할 수도, 상상할 수도 없는 어떤 사고로 인해 그 고래 뼈 다리가 어찌나 거칠게 어긋났던지, 그것이 말뚝처럼 사타구니를 찔러 거의 꿰뚫을 뻔했고, 그 고통스러운 상처를 완전히 치유하는 데에도 극심한 어려움이 따랐다."]], [["Nor, at the time, had it failed to enter his monomaniac mind, that all the anguish of that then present suffering was but the direct issue of a former woe; and he too plainly seemed to see, that as the most poisonous reptile of the marsh perpetuates his kind as inevitably as the sweetest songster of the grove; so, equally with every felicity, all miserable events do naturally beget their like. Yea, more than equally, thought Ahab; since both the ancestry and posterity of Grief go further than the ancestry and posterity of Joy. For, not to hint of this: that it is an inference from certain canonic teachings, that while some natural enjoyments here shall have no children born to them for the other world, but, on the contrary, shall be followed by the joy-childlessness of all hell’s despair; whereas, some guilty mortal miseries shall still fertilely beget to themselves an eternally progressive progeny of griefs beyond the grave; not at all to hint of this, there still seems an inequality in the deeper analysis of the thing. For, thought Ahab, while even the highest earthly felicities ever have a certain unsignifying pettiness lurking in them, but, at bottom, all heartwoes, a mystic significance, and, in some men, an archangelic grandeur; so do their diligent tracings-out not belie the obvious deduction. To trail the genealogies of these high mortal miseries, carries us at last among the sourceless primogenitures of the gods; so that, in the face of all the glad, hay-making suns, and soft cymballing, round harvest-moons, we must needs give in to this: that the gods themselves are not for ever glad. The ineffaceable, sad birth-mark in the brow of man, is but the stamp of sorrow in the signers.", "그리고 그 당시에도, 지금 겪고 있는 그 모든 고통이 다름 아닌 이전의 비탄에서 곧장 비롯된 것이라는 생각이 그의 편집광적인 마음속에 들어오지 않은 것은 아니었다. 그는 또한 너무도 분명하게 깨닫는 듯했으니, 늪지의 가장 독한 파충류가 숲속의 가장 감미로운 명금(鳴禽)만큼이나 어김없이 제 종족을 이어 가듯, 모든 행복과 마찬가지로 모든 비참한 일들 또한 자연스럽게 제 닮은꼴을 낳는다는 것이었다. 아니, 그보다 더하다고 에이해브는 생각했다. 슬픔의 조상과 후손은 기쁨의 조상과 후손보다 더 멀리까지 뻗어 나가기 때문이다. 이런 점은 굳이 들추지 않더라도 말이다. 어떤 경전의 가르침에서 끌어낼 수 있는 추론에 따르면, 이 세상에서 누리는 어떤 자연스러운 즐거움들은 저 세상으로 이어질 자식을 낳지 못하고 도리어 온 지옥의 절망이 안기는 기쁨 없는 무자(無子)의 상태로 이어지지만, 죄로 물든 어떤 인간의 비참들은 여전히 풍요롭게도 무덤 너머까지 영원히 불어나는 슬픔의 자손을 스스로 낳는다는 것이다. 이런 점은 전혀 들추지 않더라도, 이 문제를 더 깊이 파고들면 거기에는 여전히 어떤 불균형이 존재하는 듯하다. 에이해브는 이렇게 생각했다. 이 땅에서 누리는 가장 드높은 행복조차도 그 안에는 어딘가 보잘것없는 하찮음이 도사리고 있는 반면, 그 밑바닥에서 모든 마음의 비통은 신비로운 의미를, 그리고 어떤 사람들에게는 대천사 같은 장엄함을 지니고 있으니, 그것을 부지런히 추적해 들어가도 그 명백한 결론을 뒤집지는 못한다. 이 드높은 인간의 비참들의 계보를 따라가다 보면, 우리는 마침내 신들의 근원 없는 시원(始原)에까지 이르게 된다. 그러므로 즐겁게 건초를 거두게 하는 온갖 태양과, 부드럽게 심벌즈처럼 울리는 둥근 추수의 달들 앞에서도, 우리는 결국 이것을 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 신들 자신조차 영원히 즐겁지만은 않다는 것을. 인간의 이마에 지워지지 않게 새겨진 저 슬픈 모반(母斑)은, 그것을 서명한 자들이 찍어 놓은 비애의 낙인에 지나지 않는다."]], [["Unwittingly here a secret has been divulged, which perhaps might more properly, in set way, have been disclosed before.", "여기서 무심결에 한 가지 비밀이 누설되고 말았는데, 그것은 어쩌면 앞서 정해진 자리에서 마땅히 밝혔어야 더 옳았을지도 모른다."], ["With many other particulars concerning Ahab, always had it remained a mystery to some, why it was, that for a certain period, both before and after the sailing of the Pequod, he had hidden himself away with such Grand-Lama-like exclusiveness; and, for that one interval, sought speechless refuge, as it were, among the marble senate of the dead.", "에이해브에 관한 다른 여러 세세한 사정들과 더불어, 어찌하여 그가 피쿼드호가 출항하기 전과 후의 어떤 기간 동안 마치 라마 대승정이라도 된 듯 그토록 사람을 멀리하며 스스로를 숨겨 두었는지, 그리고 그 한동안 마치 죽은 자들의 대리석 원로원에 든 듯 말없는 은둔처를 찾았는지는, 어떤 이들에게 늘 수수께끼로 남아 있었다."], ["Captain Peleg’s bruited reason for this thing appeared by no means adequate; though, indeed, as touching all Ahab’s deeper part, every revelation partook more of significant darkness than of explanatory light.", "이 일에 대해 펠레그 선장이 떠벌린 이유는 결코 충분해 보이지 않았다. 사실 에이해브의 더 깊은 부분에 닿는 일이라면, 모든 폭로는 설명해 주는 빛이라기보다 의미심장한 어둠을 더 많이 머금고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But, in the end, it all came out; this one matter did, at least.", "그러나 결국 모든 것이 드러났다. 적어도 이 한 가지 사정만큼은 그러했다."], ["That direful mishap was at the bottom of his temporary recluseness.", "그 끔찍한 사고가 그의 일시적 은둔의 밑바닥에 깔려 있었던 것이다."], ["And not only this, but to that ever-contracting, dropping circle ashore, who, for any reason, possessed the privilege of a less banned approach to him; to that timid circle the above hinted casualty—remaining, as it did, moodily unaccounted for by Ahab—invested itself with terrors, not entirely underived from the land of spirits and of wails.", "그리고 이뿐만이 아니었다. 무슨 까닭에서든 그에게 덜 금기시되는 방식으로 다가갈 특권을 지녔던, 뭍에 남은 저 끊임없이 좁아지고 줄어드는 무리에게—그 소심한 무리에게, 앞서 넌지시 비친 그 사고는—에이해브가 우울하게 입을 다문 채 한 번도 그 까닭을 밝히지 않았던 그대로—영혼과 통곡의 나라에서 비롯된 것이 전혀 아니라고는 할 수 없는 공포의 빛깔을 띠게 되었다."], ["So that, through their zeal for him, they had all conspired, so far as in them lay, to muffle up the knowledge of this thing from others; and hence it was, that not till a considerable interval had elapsed, did it transpire upon the Pequod’s decks.", "그리하여 그를 향한 열의에서, 그들은 모두 자기들이 할 수 있는 한 이 일에 대한 앎을 다른 이들로부터 감추려고 한통속이 되었다. 그러한 까닭에, 상당한 시일이 흐른 뒤에야 비로소 그 일이 피쿼드호의 갑판 위로 새어 나오게 된 것이다."]], [["But be all this as it may; let the unseen, ambiguous synod in the air, or the vindictive princes and potentates of fire, have to do or not with earthly Ahab, yet, in this present matter of his leg, he took plain practical procedures;—he called the carpenter.", "그러나 이 모든 것이 어떠하든 간에, 허공의 보이지 않는 모호한 종교회의나, 불의 복수심에 찬 군주들과 권세자들이 이 땅의 에이해브와 관련이 있든 없든, 지금 그의 다리에 관한 이 문제에서만큼은 그는 명백히 실질적인 조치를 취했다. 그는 목수를 불렀다."]], [["And when that functionary appeared before him, he bade him without delay set about making a new leg, and directed the mates to see him supplied with all the studs and joists of jaw-ivory (Sperm Whale) which had thus far been accumulated on the voyage, in order that a careful selection of the stoutest, clearest-grained stuff might be secured.", "그 직무를 맡은 자가 그의 앞에 나타나자, 에이해브는 지체 없이 새 다리를 만드는 일에 착수하라 명했다. 그리고 항해하는 동안 지금껏 모아 둔 향유고래 턱뼈로 만든 받침대와 들보 자재를 모두 그에게 공급하도록 항해사들에게 일렀으니, 그 가운데 가장 튼튼하고 결이 가장 또렷한 자재를 신중하게 골라낼 수 있게 하기 위함이었다."], ["This done, the carpenter received orders to have the leg completed that night; and to provide all the fittings for it, independent of those pertaining to the distrusted one in use.", "이 일이 끝나자, 목수는 그날 밤 안으로 다리를 완성하라는 명령과 함께, 지금 쓰고 있는 미덥지 못한 다리에 딸린 부속들과는 별개로 새 다리에 필요한 모든 부품을 마련하라는 명령을 받았다."], ["Moreover, the ship’s forge was ordered to be hoisted out of its temporary idleness in the hold; and, to accelerate the affair, the blacksmith was commanded to proceed at once to the forging of whatever iron contrivances might be needed.", "게다가 선창 속에서 한동안 놀고 있던 배의 단조 화로를 끌어올리라는 명령이 내려졌고, 일을 서두르기 위해 대장장이는 필요한 온갖 쇠붙이 장치를 지체 없이 벼리기 시작하라는 지시를 받았다."]], [["# CHAPTER 107. The Carpenter.", "# CHAPTER 107. The Carpenter."]], [["Seat thyself sultanically among the moons of Saturn, and take high abstracted man alone; and he seems a wonder, a grandeur, and a woe.", "토성의 여러 위성들 사이에 술탄처럼 자리를 잡고 앉아, 고도로 추상화된 인간 하나만을 따로 떼어 바라보라. 그러면 그는 하나의 경이요, 장엄함이며, 또한 비애로 보일 것이다."], ["But from the same point, take mankind in mass, and for the most part, they seem a mob of unnecessary duplicates, both contemporary and hereditary.", "그러나 같은 지점에서 인류를 무리째로 바라보면, 그들은 대체로 불필요한 복제품들의 군중처럼 보일 뿐이니, 그것은 동시대의 복제품이기도 하고 대대로 이어진 복제품이기도 하다."], ["But most humble though he was, and far from furnishing an example of the high, humane abstraction; the Pequod’s carpenter was no duplicate; hence, he now comes in person on this stage.", "그러나 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 목수는 비록 더없이 보잘것없는 인물이었고, 저 고매하고 인간적인 추상의 본보기와는 거리가 멀었음에도, 결코 복제품은 아니었다. 그러므로 그는 이제 몸소 이 무대에 등장한다."]], [["Like all sea-going ship carpenters, and more especially those belonging to whaling vessels, he was, to a certain off-handed, practical extent, alike experienced in numerous trades and callings collateral to his own; the carpenter’s pursuit being the ancient and outbranching trunk of all those numerous handicrafts which more or less have to do with wood as an auxiliary material.", "바다를 누비는 모든 배의 목수들이 그러하듯, 특히 포경선에 속한 목수들이 더욱 그러하듯, 그는 어느 정도 막힘없는 실용적 범위 안에서 자신의 본업과 인접한 수많은 직종과 일거리에 두루 능숙했다. 목수의 일이라는 것이 본디 나무를 보조 재료로 삼아 이러저러하게 다루는 그 모든 수많은 수공 기술의 오래되고 가지 무성한 줄기이기 때문이다."], ["But, besides the application to him of the generic remark above, this carpenter of the Pequod was singularly efficient in those thousand nameless mechanical emergencies continually recurring in a large ship, upon a three or four years’ voyage, in uncivilized and far-distant seas.", "그러나 위에서 말한 일반적 언급이 그에게 적용되는 것에 더하여, 이 피쿼드호의 목수는 삼사 년에 걸친 항해 동안 미개하고 머나먼 바다 위에서 큰 배에 끊임없이 닥쳐오는, 이름조차 없는 수천 가지 기계적 위기 상황을 처리하는 데 유난히 유능했다."], ["For not to speak of his readiness in ordinary duties:—repairing stove boats, sprung spars, reforming the shape of clumsy-bladed oars, inserting bull’s eyes in the deck, or new tree-nails in the side planks, and other miscellaneous matters more directly pertaining to his special business; he was moreover unhesitatingly expert in all manner of conflicting aptitudes, both useful and capricious.", "부서진 보트를 수리하고, 휘어진 원목을 손보고, 무딘 날의 노 모양을 다시 다듬고, 갑판에 둥근 채광창을 박아 넣고, 뱃전 널빤지에 새 나무못을 끼우는 등, 그의 특수한 본업에 더 직접적으로 속하는 잡다한 일들에서 보여 준 그의 능란함은 말할 것도 없거니와, 그는 게다가 유용한 것이든 변덕스러운 것이든 온갖 상충하는 재주에서도 거침없이 능숙했다."]], [["The one grand stage where he enacted all his various parts so manifold, was his vice-bench; a long rude ponderous table furnished with several vices, of different sizes, and both of iron and of wood.", "그가 그토록 다양하고 무수한 배역을 두루 연기하던 하나의 거대한 무대는 그의 바이스 작업대였다. 그것은 길고 거칠고 묵직한 탁자로, 크기가 제각각인 여러 개의 바이스를 갖추었는데, 쇠로 된 것도 있고 나무로 된 것도 있었다."], ["At all times except when whales were alongside, this bench was securely lashed athwartships against the rear of the Try-works.", "고래가 뱃전에 붙어 있을 때를 제외하고는 늘, 이 작업대는 정유 가마(Try-works) 뒤편에 가로질러 단단히 동여매여 있었다."]], [["A belaying pin is found too large to be easily inserted into its hole: the carpenter claps it into one of his ever-ready vices, and straightway files it smaller.", "밧줄걸이 핀이 구멍에 쉽게 들어가지 않을 만큼 너무 굵으면, 목수는 그것을 늘 준비되어 있는 바이스 중 하나에 물려 놓고는 곧바로 더 가늘게 줄로 깎아 낸다."], ["A lost land-bird of strange plumage strays on board, and is made a captive: out of clean shaved rods of right-whale bone, and cross-beams of sperm whale ivory, the carpenter makes a pagoda-looking cage for it.", "기이한 깃털을 가진 길 잃은 뭍새가 배 안으로 흘러들어 와 사로잡히면, 목수는 깨끗하게 깎아 낸 참고래(Right) 뼈 막대와 향유고래(Sperm) 상아로 만든 가로대를 엮어 탑처럼 생긴 새장을 만들어 준다."], ["An oarsman sprains his wrist: the carpenter concocts a soothing lotion.", "노잡이가 손목을 삐면, 목수는 가라앉히는 연고를 조제해 낸다."], ["Stubb longed for vermillion stars to be painted upon the blade of his every oar; screwing each oar in his big vice of wood, the carpenter symmetrically supplies the constellation.", "스터브(Stubb)는 자기가 쓰는 노 하나하나의 날에 주홍빛 별을 그려 넣고 싶어 했는데, 목수는 노를 큼직한 나무 바이스에 하나씩 물려 놓고는 좌우 대칭으로 그 별자리를 새겨 넣어 준다."], ["A sailor takes a fancy to wear shark-bone ear-rings: the carpenter drills his ears.", "어느 선원이 상어 뼈로 만든 귀걸이를 차고 싶어 하면, 목수는 그의 귀에 구멍을 뚫어 준다."], ["Another has the toothache: the carpenter out pincers, and clapping one hand upon his bench bids him be seated there; but the poor fellow unmanageably winces under the unconcluded operation; whirling round the handle of his wooden vice, the carpenter signs him to clap his jaw in that, if he would have him draw the tooth.", "또 다른 선원이 치통을 앓으면, 목수는 집게를 꺼내 들고 한 손으로 작업대를 탁 짚으며 거기 앉으라고 명한다. 그러나 가엾은 친구가 채 끝나지도 않은 시술 도중에 어쩔 줄 모르고 움찔거리면, 목수는 나무 바이스의 손잡이를 빙빙 돌리며, 이를 뽑게 하려거든 그 안에 턱을 물리라고 손짓한다."]], [["Thus, this carpenter was prepared at all points, and alike indifferent and without respect in all.", "이리하여 이 목수는 모든 면에서 만반의 준비가 되어 있었고, 그 모든 일에 한결같이 무심하며 아무런 경의도 품지 않았다."], ["Teeth he accounted bits of ivory; heads he deemed but top-blocks; men themselves he lightly held for capstans.", "그는 이빨을 상아 조각으로 여겼고, 머리를 그저 도르래 윗부분쯤으로 보았으며, 사람 그 자체를 가볍게 캡스턴 정도로 취급했다."], ["But while now upon so wide a field thus variously accomplished and with such liveliness of expertness in him, too; all this would seem to argue some uncommon vivacity of intelligence.", "그러나 그가 이토록 광범위한 영역에서 이처럼 다양하게 숙달되어 있고, 게다가 그토록 활기찬 능란함마저 갖추고 있는 지금, 이 모든 것은 어떤 비범한 지성의 생기를 드러내는 듯 보일 법도 하다."], ["But not precisely so.", "그러나 꼭 그렇지만은 않았다."], ["For nothing was this man more remarkable, than for a certain impersonal stolidity as it were; impersonal, I say; for it so shaded off into the surrounding infinite of things, that it seemed one with the general stolidity discernible in the whole visible world; which while pauselessly active in uncounted modes, still eternally holds its peace, and ignores you, though you dig foundations for cathedrals.", "이 사내에게 가장 두드러진 점은 다름 아닌 이를테면 비인격적인 둔중함이었으니, 비인격적이라 함은, 그 둔중함이 주위를 둘러싼 무한한 사물의 세계 속으로 어찌나 아스라이 녹아들어 가는지, 눈에 보이는 온 세계에서 감지되는 저 보편적 둔중함과 하나가 된 듯 보였기 때문이다. 그 보편적 둔중함이란, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 방식으로 쉼 없이 움직이면서도 여전히 영원토록 침묵을 지키며, 그대가 대성당의 주춧돌을 파 내려간다 한들 그대를 모른 체하는 것이다."], ["Yet was this half-horrible stolidity in him, involving, too, as it appeared, an all-ramifying heartlessness;—yet was it oddly dashed at times, with an old, crutch-like, antediluvian, wheezing humorousness, not unstreaked now and then with a certain grizzled wittiness; such as might have served to pass the time during the midnight watch on the bearded forecastle of Noah’s ark.", "그러나 그에게는 이 반쯤은 섬뜩한 둔중함이 깃들어 있었고, 그것은 또한 보기에 모든 곳으로 뻗어 나가는 일종의 냉혹함을 품고 있는 듯했음에도, 이상하게도 때때로 그 둔중함에는 낡은 목발 같고, 노아의 홍수 이전 시대처럼 케케묵고, 쌕쌕거리는 듯한 익살이 섞여 들었으니, 거기에는 이따금 어딘가 희끗희끗 노련한 재치마저 스쳐 지나갔다. 그런 익살이라면 노아의 방주 그 수염 난 앞갑판에서 한밤의 당직 시간을 때우는 데 쓰였을 법도 했다."], ["Was it that this old carpenter had been a life-long wanderer, whose much rolling, to and fro, not only had gathered no moss; but what is more, had rubbed off whatever small outward clingings might have originally pertained to him?", "이 늙은 목수가 평생을 떠도는 방랑자로 살아온 까닭에, 이리저리 끝없이 굴러다니느라 이끼 한 점 끼지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 더 나아가 본디 그에게 붙어 있었을지 모를 자잘한 겉껍데기마저 죄다 닳아 떨어져 나간 것일까?"], ["He was a stript abstract; an unfractioned integral; uncompromised as a new-born babe; living without premeditated reference to this world or the next.", "그는 발가벗겨진 추상이요, 더 나뉠 수 없는 정수(整數)였으며, 갓 태어난 갓난아기처럼 어디에도 타협하지 않았으니, 이 세상이나 저세상을 미리 염두에 두는 일 없이 살아가는 사람이었다."], ["You might almost say, that this strange uncompromisedness in him involved a sort of unintelligence; for in his numerous trades, he did not seem to work so much by reason or by instinct, or simply because he had been tutored to it, or by any intermixture of all these, even or uneven; but merely by a kind of deaf and dumb, spontaneous literal process.", "그에게 깃든 이 기묘한 비타협성은 어쩌면 일종의 무지성을 품고 있었다고 말해도 좋을 것이다. 그 수많은 일거리에서 그는 이성으로도, 본능으로도, 혹은 단지 그렇게 배웠기 때문에도, 또 이 모든 것이 고르게든 고르지 않게든 뒤섞인 까닭에서도 일하는 것처럼 보이지 않았고, 다만 일종의 귀먹고 벙어리 같은, 즉흥적이고 곧이곧대로의 과정을 통해서만 일하는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["He was a pure manipulator; his brain, if he had ever had one, must have early oozed along into the muscles of his fingers.", "그는 순전한 손놀림꾼이었으니, 그에게 두뇌라는 것이 애초에 있었다 한들, 그것은 진작에 손가락 근육 속으로 스며들어 가 버렸음에 틀림없었다."], ["He was like one of those unreasoning but still highly useful, multum in parvo, Sheffield contrivances, assuming the exterior—though a little swelled—of a common pocket knife; but containing, not only blades of various sizes, but also screw-drivers, cork-screws, tweezers, awls, pens, rulers, nail-filers, countersinkers.", "그는 이성을 갖추지 못했으되 여전히 더없이 쓸모 있는, 작은 몸체에 많은 것을 담은(multum in parvo) 셰필드(Sheffield)제 도구 같은 자였다. 겉모습은 ― 조금 부풀어 있기는 해도 ― 흔한 주머니칼처럼 보이지만, 그 안에는 크기가 제각각인 칼날뿐 아니라 나사돌리개, 코르크 따개, 족집게, 송곳, 펜, 자, 손톱줄, 접시머리 따개까지 들어 있는 그런 도구 말이다."], ["So, if his superiors wanted to use the carpenter for a screw-driver, all they had to do was to open that part of him, and the screw was fast: or if for tweezers, take him up by the legs, and there they were.", "그러니 상관들이 이 목수를 나사돌리개로 쓰고 싶으면, 그의 그 부분을 펼쳐 내기만 하면 나사가 단단히 조여졌고, 족집게로 쓰고 싶으면 두 다리를 잡아 들어 올리기만 하면 거기 족집게가 있었다. * 작은 몸체에 많은 것을 담은(multum in parvo): \"작은 것 안에 많은 것\"이라는 뜻의 라틴어 표현."]], [["Yet, as previously hinted, this omnitooled, open-and-shut carpenter, was, after all, no mere machine of an automaton.", "그러나 앞서 넌지시 비친 바와 같이, 만능 도구를 갖추고 펼쳤다 접었다 하는 이 목수도 따지고 보면 한낱 자동인형 같은 기계는 아니었다."], ["If he did not have a common soul in him, he had a subtle something that somehow anomalously did its duty.", "비록 그에게 평범한 영혼이 깃들어 있지는 않았다 해도, 어딘가 변칙적인 방식으로 제 할 일을 해내는 미묘한 무언가가 그에게 있었다."], ["What that was, whether essence of quicksilver, or a few drops of hartshorn, there is no telling.", "그것이 무엇인지, 수은의 정수인지 아니면 사슴뿔 정기(精氣) 몇 방울인지는 알 길이 없다. 그러나 그것은 분명 거기 있었고, 이제 육십 년이 넘도록 거기 머물러 있었다."], ["But there it was; and there it had abided for now some sixty years or more.", "그리고 바로 이것이, 그에게 깃든 이 헤아릴 수 없고 교묘한 생명의 원리가, 그로 하여금 많은 시간을 혼잣말로 보내게 한 장본인이었다."], ["And this it was, this same unaccountable, cunning life-principle in him; this it was, that kept him a great part of the time soliloquizing; but only like an unreasoning wheel, which also hummingly soliloquizes; or rather, his body was a sentry-box and this soliloquizer on guard there, and talking all the time to keep himself awake.", "그러나 그것은 다만 이성 없이 윙윙거리며 혼잣말하는 바퀴와도 같았다. 아니, 차라리 그의 몸은 보초막이요, 거기 보초 서며 혼잣말하는 자는 잠들지 않으려고 줄곧 떠들어 대고 있는 것이라 하는 편이 옳겠다."]], [["The Deck—First Night Watch.", "갑판 — 초저녁 당직."]], [["(Carpenter standing before his vice-bench, and by the light of two lanterns busily filing the ivory joist for the leg, which joist is firmly fixed in the vice.", "(목수가 자기 죔쇠 작업대 앞에 서서, 두 개의 등불 빛에 의지해 의족에 쓸 상아 들보를 부지런히 줄질하고 있다. 그 들보는 죔쇠에 단단히 물려 있다."], ["Slabs of ivory, leather straps, pads, screws, and various tools of all sorts lying about the bench.", "상아 판자며 가죽끈이며 패드며 나사며 온갖 종류의 갖가지 연장이 작업대 주위에 어지러이 널려 있다."], ["Forward, the red flame of the forge is seen, where the blacksmith is at work.)", "앞쪽으로는 대장간의 붉은 불꽃이 보이고, 그곳에서 대장장이가 일하고 있다.)"]], [["Drat the file, and drat the bone!", "빌어먹을 줄, 빌어먹을 뼈다귀 같으니! 물러야 할 곳은 단단하고, 단단해야 할 곳은 무르구나."], ["That is hard which should be soft, and that is soft which should be hard.", "낡은 턱뼈며 정강이뼈를 줄질하는 우리 신세가 다 이렇지. 다른 걸로 해보자."], ["So we go, who file old jaws and shinbones.", "옳지, 이건 좀 낫군 (재채기를 한다). 어이쿠, 이 뼛가루가 (재채기) — 이거 참 (재채기) — 그래 이게 (재채기) — 맙소사, 말도 못하게 만드는구나!"], ["Let’s try another. Aye, now, this works better (sneezes).", "죽은 목재를 다루다 보면 늙은이가 받는 게 이런 거다."], ["Halloa, this bone dust is (sneezes)—why it’s (sneezes)—yes it’s (sneezes)—bless my soul, it won’t let me speak!", "살아 있는 나무를 톱질해 봐라, 이런 가루는 안 나오지. 살아 있는 뼈를 잘라 봐라, 이런 건 안 나와 (재채기)."], ["This is what an old fellow gets now for working in dead lumber.", "자, 자, 이 거기 검댕이 영감, 손 좀 거들어서 그 쇠테와 죔쇠 나사를 가져오게. 곧 그것들이 필요할 테니."], ["Saw a live tree, and you don’t get this dust; amputate a live bone, and you don’t get it (sneezes).", "그래도 운이 좋군 (재채기), 무릎 관절을 안 만들어도 되니 말이지. 그건 좀 골치 아팠을 텐데."], ["Come, come, you old Smut, there, bear a hand, and let’s have that ferule and buckle-screw; I’ll be ready for them presently.", "단순한 정강이뼈쯤이야 — 이거야 홉 받침대 만드는 것만큼 쉽지. 다만 마무리를 잘 짓고 싶긴 한데."], ["Lucky now (sneezes) there’s no knee-joint to make; that might puzzle a little; but a mere shinbone—why it’s easy as making hop-poles; only I should like to put a good finish on.", "시간, 시간이 문제야. 시간만 넉넉하다면 응접실에서 숙녀에게 절하며 발을 끄는 (재채기) 그 어떤 다리 못지않게 말끔한 의족을 뽑아낼 수 있으련만."], ["Time, time; if I but only had the time, I could turn him out as neat a leg now as ever (sneezes) scraped to a lady in a parlor.", "가게 진열창에서 본 그 사슴가죽 다리며 종아리 따위는 비교도 안 되지. 그것들은 물을 빨아들이거든."], ["Those buckskin legs and calves of legs I’ve seen in shop windows wouldn’t compare at all.", "그러니 당연히 류머티즘에 걸리고, 산 다리나 마찬가지로 세척액이며 바르는 약으로 (재채기) 치료를 받아야 한다고. 자, 이제 톱으로 자르기 전에 그 늙은 황제 폐하를 불러서 길이가 맞는지 봐야겠군."], ["They soak water, they do; and of course get rheumatic, and have to be doctored (sneezes) with washes and lotions, just like live legs.", "아무래도 조금 짧을 것 같은데. 하!"], ["There; before I saw it off, now, I must call his old Mogulship, and see whether the length will be all right; too short, if anything, I guess. Ha!", "저게 그 발꿈치 소리로구나."], ["that’s the heel; we are in luck; here he comes, or it’s somebody else, that’s certain.", "우리한테 운이 따르는걸. 저기 오는군, 아니면 누군가 딴 사람이거나, 그건 확실하지."]], [["AHAB (advancing).", "에이해브 (다가오며)."], ["(During the ensuing scene, the carpenter continues sneezing at times.)", "(이어지는 장면 내내 목수는 이따금 재채기를 계속한다.)"]], [["Well, manmaker!", "오, 사람 만드는 자여!"]], [["Just in time, sir.", "마침 잘 오셨습니다, 선장님."], ["If the captain pleases, I will now mark the length.", "선장님만 괜찮으시다면 이제 길이를 표시하겠습니다."], ["Let me measure, sir.", "좀 재어 보겠습니다, 선장님."]], [["Measured for a leg!", "다리를 재게 한단 말이지!"], ["good.", "좋다."], ["Well, it’s not the first time.", "뭐, 처음 있는 일도 아니니까."], ["About it!", "어서 하게!"], ["There; keep thy finger on it.", "자, 그 위에 손가락을 대고 있어라."], ["This is a cogent vice thou hast here, carpenter; let me feel its grip once.", "이 죔쇠는 단단히도 무는군, 목수. 한번 그 악력을 느껴 보세."], ["So, so; it does pinch some.", "그래, 그래, 제법 꽉 무는구나."]], [["Oh, sir, it will break bones—beware, beware!", "오, 선장님, 그건 뼈도 부러뜨립니다 — 조심하십시오, 조심하세요!"]], [["No fear; I like a good grip; I like to feel something in this slippery world that can hold, man. What’s Prometheus about there?—the blacksmith, I mean—what’s he about?", "겁날 것 없다. 나는 단단히 무는 게 좋아. 이 미끄러운 세상에서 무언가 단단히 붙잡을 수 있는 것을 느끼는 게 좋단 말이야. 저기 프로메테우스는 뭘 하고 있나? — 대장장이 말이야 — 뭘 하고 있어?"]], [["He must be forging the buckle-screw, sir, now.", "지금쯤 죔쇠 나사를 벼리고 있을 겁니다, 선장님."]], [["Right.", "옳지."], ["It’s a partnership; he supplies the muscle part.", "이건 합작이로군. 그자는 근육 부분을 대는 셈이지."], ["He makes a fierce red flame there!", "저기 사나운 붉은 불꽃을 피워 올리는구나!"]], [["Aye, sir; he must have the white heat for this kind of fine work.", "그렇습니다, 선장님. 이런 정교한 작업에는 백열의 불이 필요하니까요."]], [["Um-m.", "음."], ["So he must.", "그렇겠지. 이제 와 생각하니 참으로 의미심장한 일이로다."], ["I do deem it now a most meaning thing, that that old Greek, Prometheus, who made men, they say, should have been a blacksmith, and animated them with fire; for what’s made in fire must properly belong to fire; and so hell’s probable.", "사람들이 말하기를 인간을 만들었다는 저 옛 그리스인 프로메테우스가 대장장이였고, 불로써 인간에게 생명을 불어넣었다니 말이야. 불 속에서 만들어진 것은 마땅히 불에 속하는 법, 그러니 지옥이 있을 법도 하지."], ["How the soot flies!", "검댕이 어찌나 날리는지!"], ["This must be the remainder the Greek made the Africans of.", "저게 바로 그 그리스인이 아프리카인을 빚고 남긴 찌꺼기인 게로군. 목수, 그자가 그 죔쇠를 다 만들거든 강철 어깨뼈 한 쌍을 벼리라고 일러 두게."], ["Carpenter, when he’s through with that buckle, tell him to forge a pair of steel shoulder-blades; there’s a pedlar aboard with a crushing pack.", "배에 짓누르는 짐 보따리를 진 행상꾼이 하나 있어서 말이야. 예, 선장님?"]], [["Sir?", "가만, 프로메테우스가 그 일을 하는 김에 내 마음에 드는 본을 따라 완벽한 인간을 하나 주문해야겠다."], ["Hold; while Prometheus is about it, I’ll order a complete man after a desirable pattern.", "첫째로, 양말까지 신어서 키가 오십 피트(약 15미터)일 것. 다음으로 가슴은 템스강 터널을 본떠 만들 것."], ["Imprimis, fifty feet high in his socks; then, chest modelled after the Thames Tunnel; then, legs with roots to ’em, to stay in one place; then, arms three feet through the wrist; no heart at all, brass forehead, and about a quarter of an acre of fine brains; and let me see—shall I order eyes to see outwards?", "다음으로 다리에는 뿌리를 달아서 한자리에 붙박여 있게 할 것. 다음으로 팔은 손목 둘레가 삼 피트(약 90센티미터)일 것."], ["No, but put a sky-light on top of his head to illuminate inwards.", "심장은 아예 없고, 이마는 놋쇠로, 뇌수는 사분의 일 에이커쯤 되는 좋은 것으로. 그리고 가만 보자 — 바깥을 보는 눈을 주문할까?"], ["There, take the order, and away.", "아니, 그 대신 머리 꼭대기에 채광창을 달아서 안쪽을 비추게 하지. 자, 그 주문을 받아서 어서 가게."]], [["Now, what’s he speaking about, and who’s he speaking to, I should like to know?", "자, 저 양반이 대체 무슨 소리를 하는 거며, 누구한테 하는 말이지? 이거 알 수가 없네."], ["Shall I keep standing here?", "내가 여기 계속 서 있어야 하나?"], ["(aside).", "(혼잣말로)."]], [["’Tis but indifferent architecture to make a blind dome; here’s one.", "눈먼 둥근 천장을 짓는 건 어설픈 건축술이지. 여기 하나 있군."], ["No, no, no; I must have a lantern.", "안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼. 등불이 있어야겠다."]], [["Ho, ho!", "호, 호!"], ["That’s it, hey?", "그거 말씀이군요, 예?"], ["Here are two, sir; one will serve my turn.", "여기 둘 있습니다, 선장님. 하나면 제 일에 충분하지요."]], [["What art thou thrusting that thief-catcher into my face for, man?", "그 도둑 잡는 등불을 어쩌자고 내 얼굴에 들이미는 거냐, 이 사람아?"], ["Thrusted light is worse than presented pistols.", "들이대는 불빛은 겨눈 권총보다 더 고약하단 말이다."]], [["I thought, sir, that you spoke to carpenter.", "저는 선장님께서 목수에게 말씀하시는 줄로만 알았습니다."]], [["Carpenter?", "목수?"], ["why that’s—but no;—a very tidy, and, I may say, an extremely gentlemanlike sort of business thou art in here, carpenter;—or would’st thou rather work in clay?", "그게 무슨 — 아니지 — 자네가 여기서 하는 일은 참으로 깔끔하고, 말하자면 지극히 신사다운 일이로다, 목수여. 아니면 차라리 진흙을 빚는 일을 하고 싶은가?"]], [["Sir?—Clay?", "예, 선장님? — 진흙이요?"], ["clay, sir?", "진흙이라뇨, 선장님?"], ["That’s mud; we leave clay to ditchers, sir.", "그건 진창 아닙니까. 진흙은 도랑 파는 자들에게나 맡기지요, 선장님."]], [["The fellow’s impious!", "이 친구 불경스럽구나!"], ["What art thou sneezing about?", "무슨 재채기를 그리 하나?"]], [["Bone is rather dusty, sir.", "뼈가 좀 먼지가 많아서요, 선장님."]], [["Take the hint, then; and when thou art dead, never bury thyself under living people’s noses.", "그러면 그 말뜻을 새겨 두게. 그리고 자네가 죽거든 산 사람들 코밑에 절대 묻히지 말게나."]], [["Sir?—oh!", "예, 선장님? — 오!"], ["ah!—I guess so;—yes—oh, dear!", "아! — 그렇겠지요 — 예 — 아이고, 저런!"]], [["Look ye, carpenter, I dare say thou callest thyself a right good workmanlike workman, eh?", "이보게, 목수. 자네는 스스로를 제법 솜씨 좋은 일꾼이라 자부하겠지, 응?"], ["Well, then, will it speak thoroughly well for thy work, if, when I come to mount this leg thou makest, I shall nevertheless feel another leg in the same identical place with it; that is, carpenter, my old lost leg; the flesh and blood one, I mean.", "그렇다면 한번 말해 보게. 자네가 만드는 이 다리를 내가 차고 섰을 때, 그럼에도 바로 그 똑같은 자리에서 또 다른 다리가 느껴진다면 — 그러니까 목수, 내 잃어버린 옛 다리, 살과 피로 된 그 다리 말이야 — 그래도 자네 솜씨가 더할 나위 없이 훌륭하다고 할 수 있겠나?"], ["Canst thou not drive that old Adam away?", "자네는 저 옛 아담을 몰아낼 수 있겠나?"]], [["Truly, sir, I begin to understand somewhat now.", "정말이지, 선장님, 이제 좀 알 듯도 합니다."], ["Yes, I have heard something curious on that score, sir; how that a dismasted man never entirely loses the feeling of his old spar, but it will be still pricking him at times.", "예, 그 점에 관해서는 저도 묘한 이야기를 들은 적이 있습니다, 선장님. 돛대를 잃은 사람이 그 옛 돛대의 느낌을 결코 완전히 잃지 않고, 이따금 그것이 여전히 쿡쿡 쑤신다는 이야기 말입니다."], ["May I humbly ask if it be really so, sir?", "황송하오나 정말 그런 것인지 여쭤도 되겠습니까, 선장님?"]], [["It is, man.", "그렇다네, 이 사람아."], ["Look, put thy live leg here in the place where mine once was; so, now, here is only one distinct leg to the eye, yet two to the soul.", "보게, 자네 산 다리를 한때 내 다리가 있던 자리에 놓아 보게. 자, 그래, 눈에는 또렷한 다리가 하나뿐이지만 영혼에는 둘이 있는 셈이지."], ["Where thou feelest tingling life; there, exactly there, there to a hair, do I.", "자네가 짜릿한 생명을 느끼는 바로 그곳, 바로 정확히 그곳, 한 치도 어긋남 없이 그곳에서 나도 느낀다네."], ["Is’t a riddle?", "이게 수수께끼인가?"]], [["I should humbly call it a poser, sir.", "황송하오나 난제라 하겠습니다, 선장님."]], [["Hist, then.", "그럼 들어 보게."], ["How dost thou know that some entire, living, thinking thing may not be invisibly and uninterpenetratingly standing precisely where thou now standest; aye, and standing there in thy spite?", "어떤 온전한, 살아 있는, 생각하는 존재가 눈에 보이지도 않고 서로 뚫고 지나가지도 않으면서 자네가 지금 서 있는 바로 그 자리에 서 있지 않다고 자네가 어찌 아는가? 그래, 자네가 싫다는데도 거기 버티고 서 있을 수도 있지 않은가?"], ["In thy most solitary hours, then, dost thou not fear eavesdroppers?", "그렇다면 가장 고독한 시간에 자네는 엿듣는 자들이 두렵지 않은가?"], ["Hold, don’t speak!", "가만, 말하지 말게!"], ["And if I still feel the smart of my crushed leg, though it be now so long dissolved; then, why mayst not thou, carpenter, feel the fiery pains of hell for ever, and without a body?", "그리고 짓이겨진 내 다리가 이미 그토록 오래전에 스러졌건만 여전히 그 쓰라림이 느껴진다면, 그렇다면 목수, 자네라고 어찌 몸뚱이도 없이 영원토록 지옥의 불타는 고통을 느끼지 못하란 법이 있겠나?"], ["Hah!", "하!"]], [["Good Lord!", "맙소사!"], ["Truly, sir, if it comes to that, I must calculate over again; I think I didn’t carry a small figure, sir.", "정말이지, 선장님, 일이 그 지경에 이른다면, 저는 처음부터 다시 셈을 해봐야겠습니다. 아무래도 받아올림 하나를 빠뜨린 것 같습니다, 선장님."]], [["Look ye, pudding-heads should never grant premises.—How long before the leg is done?", "이보게, 머리가 푸딩 같은 자들은 절대로 전제를 인정해서는 안 되는 법이야. — 다리가 다 되려면 얼마나 남았나?"]], [["Perhaps an hour, sir.", "아마 한 시간쯤이요, 선장님."]], [["Bungle away at it then, and bring it to me (turns to go).", "그럼 어서 그것이나 더듬더듬 만들어서 내게 가져오게 (가려고 돌아선다)."], ["Oh, Life!", "오, 인생이여!"], ["Here I am, proud as Greek god, and yet standing debtor to this blockhead for a bone to stand on!", "나는 여기 그리스 신처럼 당당하게 서 있건만, 그러면서도 딛고 설 뼈다귀 하나 때문에 이 멍청이에게 빚진 신세라니!"], ["Cursed be that mortal inter-indebtedness which will not do away with ledgers.", "장부를 없애 주지 못하는 저 인간들 사이의 상호 부채, 그것에 저주가 있으라."], ["I would be free as air; and I’m down in the whole world’s books.", "나는 공기처럼 자유롭고 싶건만, 온 세상의 장부에 빚진 자로 올라 있구나."], ["I am so rich, I could have given bid for bid with the wealthiest Prætorians at the auction of the Roman empire (which was the world’s); and yet I owe for the flesh in the tongue I brag with.", "나는 어찌나 부유한지, 로마 제국(곧 온 세상)을 경매에 부쳤을 때 가장 부유한 친위대원들과 호가에 호가로 맞붙어 겨룰 수도 있었으련만, 그러면서도 내가 자랑삼아 놀리는 이 혀의 살점 값마저 빚지고 있다니."], ["By heavens!", "하늘에 맹세코!"], ["I’ll get a crucible, and into it, and dissolve myself down to one small, compendious vertebra.", "도가니를 하나 구해다가 그 속에 들어가, 작고 간략한 척추뼈 하나로 나 자신을 녹여 버리리라."], ["So.", "그래."]], [["CARPENTER (resuming his work).", "목수 (다시 일을 시작하며)."]], [["Well, well, well!", "거참, 거참, 거참!"], ["Stubb knows him best of all, and Stubb always says he’s queer; says nothing but that one sufficient little word queer; he’s queer, says Stubb; he’s queer—queer, queer; and keeps dinning it into Mr.", "스터브가 저분을 누구보다 잘 아는데, 스터브는 늘 저분을 괴짜라고 하지. 그 한마디면 족하다는 듯 괴짜라는 그 짤막한 말 외에는 아무 말도 안 해."], ["Starbuck all the time—queer—sir—queer, queer, very queer.", "괴짜라니까, 스터브가 그래. 괴짜래 — 괴짜, 괴짜라고."], ["And here’s his leg! Yes, now that I think of it, here’s his bedfellow!", "그러고는 시도 때도 없이 스타벅 씨 귀에다 그 말을 못박듯 박아 넣지 — 괴짜라니까 — 정말이지 — 괴짜, 괴짜, 아주 괴짜라고."], ["has a stick of whale’s jaw-bone for a wife!", "그리고 여기 저분의 다리가 있구나! 그래, 생각해 보니 여기 저분의 잠자리 벗이 있는 셈이지!"], ["And this is his leg; he’ll stand on this.", "고래 턱뼈 막대기 하나를 아내로 삼고 있다니! 그리고 이게 저분의 다리야."], ["What was that now about one leg standing in three places, and all three places standing in one hell—how was that?", "이걸 딛고 서겠지. 아까 그게 무슨 소리였더라, 다리 하나가 세 군데에 서 있고, 그 세 군데가 모두 한 지옥에 서 있다는 — 그게 어떻게 된 거였지?"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["I don’t wonder he looked so scornful at me!", "저분이 날 그리 멸시하듯 쳐다본 것도 무리가 아니지!"], ["I’m a sort of strange-thoughted sometimes, they say; but that’s only haphazard-like.", "사람들이 그러는데 나도 가끔은 묘한 생각을 하는 부류라더군. 하지만 그건 그저 어쩌다 그런 거지."], ["Then, a short, little old body like me, should never undertake to wade out into deep waters with tall, heron-built captains; the water chucks you under the chin pretty quick, and there’s a great cry for life-boats.", "그러니 나처럼 키 작고 자그마한 늙은이가 키 크고 왜가리처럼 생긴 선장들과 어울려 깊은 물로 첨벙첨벙 걸어 들어가려 해서는 안 되는 거야. 물이 어느새 턱 밑까지 차올라서, 구명보트 내놓으라고 아우성을 치게 되거든."], ["And here’s the heron’s leg!", "그리고 여기 그 왜가리 다리가 있군!"], ["long and slim, sure enough!", "길고 가느다란 게 영락없구나!"], ["Now, for most folks one pair of legs lasts a lifetime, and that must be because they use them mercifully, as a tender-hearted old lady uses her roly-poly old coach-horses.", "자, 사람들 대부분은 다리 한 쌍이 평생을 가는데, 그건 다들 다리를 아껴 쓰기 때문이지. 마음씨 고운 노부인이 토실토실한 늙은 마차 말을 살뜰히 부리듯 말이야."], ["But Ahab; oh he’s a hard driver.", "하지만 에이해브는, 오, 그분은 사정없이 몰아붙이는 분이지."], ["Look, driven one leg to death, and spavined the other for life, and now wears out bone legs by the cord.", "보게나, 한쪽 다리는 죽도록 몰아 부려먹고, 다른 한쪽은 평생 절름발이로 만들더니, 이제는 뼈로 만든 다리를 한 발 한 발 닳아 없애고 있으니. 어이, 거기, 이 검댕이 영감!"], ["Halloa, there, you Smut!", "그 나사 좀 거들어 줘서 끝장을 보세."], ["bear a hand there with those screws, and let’s finish it before the resurrection fellow comes a-calling with his horn for all legs, true or false, as brewery-men go round collecting old beer barrels, to fill ’em up again.", "부활의 사도가 나팔을 들고 와서 모든 다리를, 진짜든 가짜든 다 불러내기 전에 말이야. 양조장 사람들이 빈 맥주통을 거두러 다니며 다시 채워 넣듯이 말이지."], ["What a leg this is!", "이거 참 대단한 다리로군!"], ["It looks like a real live leg, filed down to nothing but the core; he’ll be standing on this to-morrow; he’ll be taking altitudes on it.", "영락없이 살아 있는 진짜 다리를 알맹이만 남기고 줄로 깎아낸 것 같지 뭔가. 내일이면 저분이 이걸 딛고 서겠지."], ["Halloa!", "이걸 딛고 태양 고도를 재겠지."], ["I almost forgot the little oval slate, smoothed ivory, where he figures up the latitude.", "어이쿠! 위도를 계산해 적는 그 자그마한 타원형 석판을, 매끄럽게 다듬은 상아 석판을 깜빡할 뻔했군."], ["So, so; chisel, file, and sand-paper, now!", "그래, 그래. 자, 이제 끌과 줄과 사포다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 109. Ahab and Starbuck in the Cabin.", "# CHAPTER 109. Ahab and Starbuck in the Cabin."]], [["According to usage they were pumping the ship next morning; and lo!", "관례에 따라 그들은 이튿날 아침 배의 펌프질을 하고 있었다. 그런데 보라!"], ["no inconsiderable oil came up with the water; the casks below must have sprung a bad leak.", "적지 않은 기름이 물과 함께 솟아올라 왔다. 아래에 둔 통들이 심하게 새고 있는 것이 틀림없었다."], ["Much concern was shown; and Starbuck went down into the cabin to report this unfavourable affair.*", "큰 우려가 일었다. 그리하여 스타벅(Starbuck)은 이 좋지 않은 일을 알리려고 선실로 내려갔다.*"]], [["*In Sperm-whalemen with any considerable quantity of oil on board, it is a regular semi-weekly duty to conduct a hose into the hold, and drench the casks with sea-water; which afterwards, at varying intervals, is removed by the ship’s pumps.", "* 상당한 양의 기름을 실은 향유고래잡이 배에서는, 일주일에 두 번 정기적으로 화물칸 안으로 호스를 끌어들여 통들을 바닷물로 적셔 두는 것이 정해진 일과다. 그 물은 그 뒤 일정치 않은 간격을 두고 배의 펌프로 퍼낸다."], ["Hereby the casks are sought to be kept damply tight; while by the changed character of the withdrawn water, the mariners readily detect any serious leakage in the precious cargo.", "이렇게 함으로써 통들이 축축한 상태로 단단히 밀폐되도록 하는 한편, 퍼낸 물의 성질이 달라진 것을 보고 선원들은 그 귀한 화물에 심각한 누출이 있는지를 쉽게 알아챈다."]], [["Now, from the South and West the Pequod was drawing nigh to Formosa and the Bashee Isles, between which lies one of the tropical outlets from the China waters into the Pacific.", "이제 페쿼드(Pequod)호는 남쪽과 서쪽에서 포모사와 바시 제도에 가까워지고 있었는데, 그 사이에는 중국 해역에서 태평양으로 빠져나가는 열대의 출구 가운데 하나가 자리하고 있다."], ["And so Starbuck found Ahab with a general chart of the oriental archipelagoes spread before him; and another separate one representing the long eastern coasts of the Japanese islands—Niphon, Matsmai, and Sikoke.", "그리하여 스타벅은 동방 군도(群島)의 일반 해도를 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 있는 에이해브(Ahab)를 발견했다. 그 곁에는 일본 열도—니폰, 마쓰마이, 시코케—의 길게 뻗은 동쪽 해안을 그린 또 다른 별도의 해도가 있었다."], ["With his snow-white new ivory leg braced against the screwed leg of his table, and with a long pruning-hook of a jack-knife in his hand, the wondrous old man, with his back to the gangway door, was wrinkling his brow, and tracing his old courses again.", "눈처럼 새하얀 새 상아 다리를 탁자의 나사못 박힌 다리에 버틴 채, 손에는 가지치기용 낫처럼 길쭉한 잭나이프를 들고서, 그 경이로운 노인은 통로 문에 등을 진 채 이마를 찌푸리며 다시금 자신의 옛 항로를 더듬고 있었다."]], [["“Who’s there?”", "\"거기 누구냐?\""], ["hearing the footstep at the door, but not turning round to it.", "문가에서 발소리를 들었으나, 그쪽으로 돌아보지는 않았다."], ["“On deck!", "\"갑판으로!"], ["Begone!”", "물러가라!\""]], [["“Captain Ahab mistakes; it is I.", "\"에이해브 선장님, 사람을 잘못 보셨습니다."], ["The oil in the hold is leaking, sir.", "접니다. 화물칸의 기름이 새고 있습니다, 선장님."], ["We must up Burtons and break out.”", "버턴 도르래를 올려 통들을 들어내야 합니다.\""]], [["“Up Burtons and break out?", "\"버턴을 올려 들어내자고?"], ["Now that we are nearing Japan; heave-to here for a week to tinker a parcel of old hoops?”", "이제 막 일본에 가까워지고 있는데, 낡은 테 몇 개를 손보려고 여기서 일주일이나 멈춰 서 있으란 말인가?\""]], [["“Either do that, sir, or waste in one day more oil than we may make good in a year.", "\"그렇게 하시든가, 아니면 일 년에 걸쳐 메울 수 있는 양보다 더 많은 기름을 하루에 허비하시든가입니다, 선장님."], ["What we come twenty thousand miles to get is worth saving, sir.”", "이만 마일이나 와서 얻으려는 것이니 지킬 만한 가치가 있습니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“So it is, so it is; if we get it.”", "\"그렇지, 그렇고말고. 손에 넣기만 한다면 말이다.\""]], [["“I was speaking of the oil in the hold, sir.”", "\"저는 화물칸의 기름을 두고 말씀드린 것입니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“And I was not speaking or thinking of that at all.", "\"그리고 나는 그런 것은 말하지도, 생각하지도 않았다."], ["Begone!", "물러가라!"], ["Let it leak!", "새게 내버려 두어라!"], ["I’m all aleak myself.", "나 자신이 온통 새고 있는걸."], ["Aye!", "그렇다!"], ["leaks in leaks!", "새는 것 속에 또 새는 것이라니!"], ["not only full of leaky casks, but those leaky casks are in a leaky ship; and that’s a far worse plight than the Pequod’s, man.", "새는 통들로 가득 찼을 뿐만 아니라, 그 새는 통들이 새는 배 안에 들어 있는 것이지. 그건 페쿼드호의 처지보다 훨씬 더 고약한 신세야, 이 사람아."], ["Yet I don’t stop to plug my leak; for who can find it in the deep-loaded hull; or how hope to plug it, even if found, in this life’s howling gale?", "그래도 나는 내 새는 구멍을 틀어막으려 멈춰 서지 않는다. 짐을 잔뜩 실은 선체 속에서 누가 그 구멍을 찾아낼 수 있겠으며, 설령 찾아낸다 한들 이 삶의 울부짖는 폭풍 속에서 어찌 그것을 틀어막기를 바라겠는가?"], ["Starbuck!", "스타벅!"], ["I’ll not have the Burtons hoisted.”", "버턴을 올리는 일은 없을 것이다.\""]], [["“What will the owners say, sir?”", "\"선주들이 뭐라고 하겠습니까, 선장님?\""]], [["“Let the owners stand on Nantucket beach and outyell the Typhoons.", "\"선주들더러 낸터킷(Nantucket) 해변에 서서 태풍보다 더 크게 소리쳐 보라지."], ["What cares Ahab?", "에이해브가 무엇을 신경 쓰겠나?"], ["Owners, owners?", "선주들, 선주들이라?"], ["Thou art always prating to me, Starbuck, about those miserly owners, as if the owners were my conscience.", "자네는 늘 그 인색한 선주들 얘기를 내게 지껄여 대는군, 스타벅, 마치 선주들이 내 양심이라도 되는 양 말이야."], ["But look ye, the only real owner of anything is its commander; and hark ye, my conscience is in this ship’s keel.—On deck!”", "하지만 보게, 무엇이든 그것의 유일한 진짜 주인은 그것을 지휘하는 자라네. 그리고 잘 들어 두게, 내 양심은 이 배의 용골 속에 있어.—갑판으로!\""]], [["“Captain Ahab,”", "\"에이해브 선장님.\" 얼굴이 붉어진 일등항해사는 더욱 선실 안으로 들어서며 말했는데, 그 대담함이 어찌나 묘하게 공손하고 조심스러웠던지, 마치 그것이 겉으로 드러나는 일은 아무리 작은 기미라도 온갖 방법으로 피하려 할 뿐만 아니라, 그 속으로도 자기 자신을 절반 넘게 미덥지 않아 하는 듯이 보였다."], ["said the reddening mate, moving further into the cabin, with a daring so strangely respectful and cautious that it almost seemed not only every way seeking to avoid the slightest outward manifestation of itself, but within also seemed more than half distrustful of itself; “A better man than I might well pass over in thee what he would quickly enough resent in a younger man; aye, and in a happier, Captain Ahab.”", "\"저보다 나은 사람이라면 선장님에게서는 능히 넘겨 버릴 일을, 더 젊은 자에게서라면 곧바로 분개할 것입니다. 그렇습니다, 더 행복한 자에게서라면 말이지요, 에이해브 선장님.\""]], [["“Devils!", "\"악마들이로다!"], ["Dost thou then so much as dare to critically think of me?—On deck!”", "그렇다면 네가 감히 나를 비판적으로 생각하기까지 한단 말이냐?—갑판으로!\""]], [["“Nay, sir, not yet; I do entreat.", "\"아닙니다, 선장님, 아직은 아닙니다. 간청드립니다."], ["And I do dare, sir—to be forbearing!", "그리고 저는 감히 청합니다, 선장님—참아 주시기를!"], ["Shall we not understand each other better than hitherto, Captain Ahab?”", "이제껏보다 우리가 서로를 더 잘 이해하면 안 되겠습니까, 에이해브 선장님?\""]], [["Ahab seized a loaded musket from the rack (forming part of most South-Sea-men’s cabin furniture), and pointing it towards Starbuck, exclaimed: “There is one God that is Lord over the earth, and one Captain that is lord over the Pequod.—On deck!”", "에이해브는 (남양 항해선 선실 가구의 대부분을 이루는) 거치대에서 장전된 머스킷 총을 집어 들어 스타벅을 향해 겨누며 외쳤다. \"땅 위를 다스리는 주인이신 하느님이 한 분 계시고, 페쿼드호를 다스리는 주인인 선장이 하나 있을 뿐이다.—갑판으로!\""]], [["For an instant in the flashing eyes of the mate, and his fiery cheeks, you would have almost thought that he had really received the blaze of the levelled tube. But, mastering his emotion, he half calmly rose, and as he quitted the cabin, paused for an instant and said: “Thou hast outraged, not insulted me, sir; but for that I ask thee not to beware of Starbuck; thou wouldst but laugh; but let Ahab beware of Ahab; beware of thyself, old man.”", "한순간 일등항해사의 번뜩이는 눈과 불타는 듯한 뺨을 보면, 그가 정녕 겨누어진 총구의 화염을 받았다고 여길 뻔했다. 그러나 그는 감정을 다스리고 절반쯤 차분하게 몸을 일으켰으며, 선실을 나서면서 잠시 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"선장님은 저를 모욕하신 것이 아니라 격분케 하셨습니다. 하나 그 때문에 저는 선장님께 스타벅을 조심하라 청하지 않겠습니다. 그러시면 한낱 웃어넘기실 테니까요. 다만 에이해브가 에이해브를 조심하기를. 당신 자신을 조심하십시오, 노인장.\""]], [["“He waxes brave, but nevertheless obeys; most careful bravery that!”", "\"이 친구가 점점 대담해지는군, 그러면서도 복종은 하니, 참으로 신중한 대담함이로다!\""], ["murmured Ahab, as Starbuck disappeared.", "스타벅이 사라지자 에이해브가 중얼거렸다."], ["“What’s that he said—Ahab beware of Ahab—there’s something there!”", "\"그가 한 말이 무어더라—에이해브가 에이해브를 조심하라—거기엔 무언가 있어!\""], ["Then unconsciously using the musket for a staff, with an iron brow he paced to and fro in the little cabin; but presently the thick plaits of his forehead relaxed, and returning the gun to the rack, he went to the deck.", "그러더니 자기도 모르게 머스킷 총을 지팡이 삼아, 쇠처럼 굳은 이마를 한 채 그 비좁은 선실을 이리저리 거닐었다. 그러나 이윽고 그의 이마에 두껍게 잡혔던 주름이 풀렸고, 총을 거치대에 도로 놓고 그는 갑판으로 나갔다."]], [["“Thou art but too good a fellow, Starbuck,” he said lowly to the mate; then raising his voice to the crew: “Furl the t’gallant-sails, and close-reef the top-sails, fore and aft; back the main-yard; up Burton, and break out in the main-hold.”", "\"자네는 그저 지나치게 좋은 사람일 뿐이야, 스타벅.\" 그는 일등항해사에게 나직이 말했다. 그러고는 선원들을 향해 목소리를 높였다. \"윗돛을 접고, 앞뒤의 중간 돛은 단단히 졸라매라. 큰 활대를 뒤로 돌려라. 버턴을 올리고, 큰 화물칸을 헐어 내라.\""]], [["It were perhaps vain to surmise exactly why it was, that as respecting Starbuck, Ahab thus acted.", "스타벅에 관해 에이해브가 어찌하여 이렇게 행동했는지를 정확히 헤아려 보는 것은 아마 부질없는 일이리라."], ["It may have been a flash of honesty in him; or mere prudential policy which, under the circumstance, imperiously forbade the slightest symptom of open disaffection, however transient, in the important chief officer of his ship.", "그것은 그의 내면에서 번뜩인 한 줄기 정직함이었을 수도 있고, 아니면 단순한 신중의 책략—이런 상황에서, 제 배의 중요한 수석 항해사가 비록 한순간일지언정 드러내는 공공연한 불만의 기색만큼은 단호히 금해야 한다는 계산이었을 수도 있다."], ["However it was, his orders were executed; and the Burtons were hoisted.", "어찌 됐든 그의 명령은 실행되었고, 버턴은 올려졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER 110. Queequeg in His Coffin.", "# CHAPTER 110. Queequeg in His Coffin."]], [["Upon searching, it was found that the casks last struck into the hold were perfectly sound, and that the leak must be further off.", "창고를 뒤져 보니, 가장 나중에 화물칸에 박아 넣은 큰 통들은 완벽하게 멀쩡했고, 따라서 물이 새는 곳은 더 깊숙한 데 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["So, it being calm weather, they broke out deeper and deeper, disturbing the slumbers of the huge ground-tier butts; and from that black midnight sending those gigantic moles into the daylight above.", "그리하여 날씨가 잔잔했으므로, 그들은 점점 더 깊이 파고들어 가, 맨 밑바닥에 깔린 거대한 통들의 잠을 어지럽혔다. 그 칠흑 같은 한밤중에서 그 거대한 두더지들을 끌어올려 위쪽의 햇빛 속으로 내보낸 셈이었다."], ["So deep did they go; and so ancient, and corroded, and weedy the aspect of the lowermost puncheons, that you almost looked next for some mouldy corner-stone cask containing coins of Captain Noah, with copies of the posted placards, vainly warning the infatuated old world from the flood.", "그들이 어찌나 깊이 내려갔던지, 그리고 가장 밑바닥의 큰 통들이 어찌나 오래되고, 부식되고, 해초가 엉겨 붙은 꼴이었던지, 다음에는 노아 선장(Captain Noah)의 동전들과, 어리석은 옛 세상을 향해 홍수를 헛되이 경고하던 벽보 사본들이 담긴 곰팡내 나는 주춧돌 통이라도 나오지 않을까 싶을 지경이었다."], ["Tierce after tierce, too, of water, and bread, and beef, and shooks of staves, and iron bundles of hoops, were hoisted out, till at last the piled decks were hard to get about; and the hollow hull echoed under foot, as if you were treading over empty catacombs, and reeled and rolled in the sea like an air-freighted demijohn.", "물통과 빵통과 소고기통과 통널 묶음과 철제 테 다발이 한 통씩 차례로 들려 나오니, 마침내 갑판은 쌓인 짐들로 발 디딜 틈조차 어려워졌다. 텅 빈 선체는 발밑에서 메아리쳐, 마치 텅 빈 지하 묘지 위를 걷는 것만 같았고, 공기만 가득 찬 큰 술병처럼 바다 위에서 휘청거리고 굴렀다."], ["Top-heavy was the ship as a dinnerless student with all Aristotle in his head.", "배는 머릿속에 아리스토텔레스 전부를 담고도 저녁을 못 먹은 학생처럼 위쪽만 무거웠다."], ["Well was it that the Typhoons did not visit them then.", "그때 태풍이 그들을 찾아오지 않은 것은 그나마 다행이었다."]], [["Now, at this time it was that my poor pagan companion, and fast bosom-friend, Queequeg, was seized with a fever, which brought him nigh to his endless end.", "자, 바로 이 무렵에 나의 가련한 이교도 동무이자 둘도 없는 가슴속 벗인 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 열병에 걸려, 끝없는 종말의 문턱까지 다다르게 되었다."]], [["Be it said, that in this vocation of whaling, sinecures are unknown; dignity and danger go hand in hand; till you get to be Captain, the higher you rise the harder you toil.", "말해 두거니와, 이 고래잡이라는 직업에는 한가로운 자리란 없다. 위엄과 위험이 손을 맞잡고 나란히 간다."], ["So with poor Queequeg, who, as harpooneer, must not only face all the rage of the living whale, but—as we have elsewhere seen—mount his dead back in a rolling sea; and finally descend into the gloom of the hold, and bitterly sweating all day in that subterraneous confinement, resolutely manhandle the clumsiest casks and see to their stowage.", "선장(Captain)이 되기 전까지는, 높이 오를수록 그만큼 더 고되게 일해야 하는 법이다. 가련한 퀴케그도 마찬가지였으니, 작살잡이로서 그는 살아 있는 고래의 온갖 광포함에 맞서야 할 뿐 아니라 — 앞서 다른 곳에서 보았듯이 — 출렁이는 바다 위에서 죽은 고래의 등에 올라타야 했고, 끝내는 화물칸의 어둠 속으로 내려가, 그 땅속 같은 갑갑한 곳에서 온종일 비지땀을 흘리며 가장 다루기 힘든 통들을 억센 힘으로 다루고 그것들을 차곡차곡 쟁여 넣는 일까지 살펴야 했다."], ["To be short, among whalemen, the harpooneers are the holders, so called.", "요컨대 고래잡이들 사이에서 작살잡이란 이른바 '적재꾼'인 셈이다."]], [["Poor Queequeg!", "가련한 퀴케그!"], ["when the ship was about half disembowelled, you should have stooped over the hatchway, and peered down upon him there; where, stripped to his woollen drawers, the tattooed savage was crawling about amid that dampness and slime, like a green spotted lizard at the bottom of a well.", "배의 내장이 거의 절반쯤 도려내어졌을 무렵, 당신이 승강구 위로 몸을 굽혀 거기 있는 그를 내려다보았더라면 좋았으련만. 그곳에서, 모직 속바지만 걸친 채 문신을 새긴 그 야만인은 그 축축함과 진창 속을 기어 다니고 있었으니, 마치 우물 밑바닥의 푸른 점박이 도마뱀 같았다."], ["And a well, or an ice-house, it somehow proved to him, poor pagan; where, strange to say, for all the heat of his sweatings, he caught a terrible chill which lapsed into a fever; and at last, after some days’ suffering, laid him in his hammock, close to the very sill of the door of death.", "그리고 그곳은 이 가련한 이교도에게 어쩐 일인지 우물, 아니 얼음 창고나 다름없는 곳이 되고 말았다. 이상한 일이지만, 그토록 비지땀을 흘리는 더위 속에서도 그는 끔찍한 한기에 휩싸였고, 그것이 열병으로 도졌던 것이다."], ["How he wasted and wasted away in those few long-lingering days, till there seemed but little left of him but his frame and tattooing.", "그리하여 마침내 며칠을 앓고 난 끝에, 그는 그물 침대에 드러눕고 말았으니, 바로 죽음의 문지방 코앞이었다. 그 더디게 늘어진 며칠 사이에 그는 어찌나 야위고 또 야위어 갔던지, 마침내 그에게 남은 것이라곤 뼈대와 문신뿐인 듯했다."], ["But as all else in him thinned, and his cheek-bones grew sharper, his eyes, nevertheless, seemed growing fuller and fuller; they became of a strange softness of lustre; and mildly but deeply looked out at you there from his sickness, a wondrous testimony to that immortal health in him which could not die, or be weakened.", "그러나 그의 나머지 모든 것이 여위어 가고 광대뼈가 점점 더 날카로워질수록, 그의 두 눈은 도리어 점점 더 가득 차 오르는 듯했다. 그 눈은 야릇하리만치 부드러운 광채를 띠더니, 병중인 그곳에서 온화하면서도 깊이 당신을 바라보았다 — 죽지도 약해지지도 않을, 그의 안에 깃든 저 불멸의 건강을 증언하는 경이로운 표징이었다."], ["And like circles on the water, which, as they grow fainter, expand; so his eyes seemed rounding and rounding, like the rings of Eternity.", "그리고 물 위의 둥근 파문이 흐려질수록 도리어 넓게 퍼져 나가듯이, 그의 두 눈도 영원의 고리들처럼 점점 더 둥글게 퍼져 가는 듯했다."], ["An awe that cannot be named would steal over you as you sat by the side of this waning savage, and saw as strange things in his face, as any beheld who were bystanders when Zoroaster died.", "이름 붙일 수 없는 외경심이 당신에게 슬며시 스며들었을 것이다 — 이 스러져 가는 야만인의 곁에 앉아, 조로아스터가 죽을 때 곁에 있던 이들이 보았을 법한 그 기이한 무언가를 그의 얼굴에서 보게 된다면 말이다."], ["For whatever is truly wondrous and fearful in man, never yet was put into words or books.", "인간 속에 깃든 진실로 경이롭고 두려운 것은, 그 무엇도 아직껏 말이나 책으로 담긴 적이 없기 때문이다."], ["And the drawing near of Death, which alike levels all, alike impresses all with a last revelation, which only an author from the dead could adequately tell.", "그리고 만인을 똑같이 평등하게 만들고, 만인에게 똑같이 마지막 계시를 새겨 주는 죽음의 다가옴이란, 오직 죽음에서 돌아온 저자만이 온전히 들려줄 수 있는 것이다."], ["So that—let us say it again—no dying Chaldee or Greek had higher and holier thoughts than those, whose mysterious shades you saw creeping over the face of poor Queequeg, as he quietly lay in his swaying hammock, and the rolling sea seemed gently rocking him to his final rest, and the ocean’s invisible flood-tide lifted him higher and higher towards his destined heaven.", "그러니 — 다시 한번 말하건대 — 죽어 가는 어떤 칼데아인이나 그리스인도, 가련한 퀴케그가 흔들리는 그물 침대에 고요히 누워 있을 때 그의 얼굴 위로 기어 다니던 그 신비로운 그늘만큼 높고 거룩한 생각을 품지는 못했으리라. 출렁이는 바다는 그를 마지막 안식으로 부드럽게 흔들어 재우는 듯했고, 대양의 보이지 않는 밀물은 그를 점점 더 높이, 정해진 그의 천국을 향해 들어 올리는 듯했다."]], [["Not a man of the crew but gave him up; and, as for Queequeg himself, what he thought of his case was forcibly shown by a curious favour he asked. He called one to him in the grey morning watch, when the day was just breaking, and taking his hand, said that while in Nantucket he had chanced to see certain little canoes of dark wood, like the rich war-wood of his native isle; and upon inquiry, he had learned that all whalemen who died in Nantucket, were laid in those same dark canoes, and that the fancy of being so laid had much pleased him; for it was not unlike the custom of his own race, who, after embalming a dead warrior, stretched him out in his canoe, and so left him to be floated away to the starry archipelagoes; for not only do they believe that the stars are isles, but that far beyond all visible horizons, their own mild, uncontinented seas, interflow with the blue heavens; and so form the white breakers of the milky way. He added, that he shuddered at the thought of being buried in his hammock, according to the usual sea-custom, tossed like something vile to the death-devouring sharks. No: he desired a canoe like those of Nantucket, all the more congenial to him, being a whaleman, that like a whale-boat these coffin-canoes were without a keel; though that involved but uncertain steering, and much lee-way adown the dim ages.", "선원 가운데 그를 단념하지 않은 자가 없었다. 그리고 퀴케그 자신으로 말하자면, 그가 제 처지를 어찌 여기는지는 그가 청한 어떤 묘한 부탁에서 똑똑히 드러났다. 그는 날이 막 밝아 오는 잿빛 새벽 당직 시간에 한 사람을 곁으로 불러, 그의 손을 잡고서 말했다. 낸터킷(Nantucket)에 머무는 동안, 그는 우연히 어떤 검은 나무로 만든 작은 카누들을 본 적이 있었는데, 그것이 제 고향 섬의 값진 전쟁용 목재와 흡사했다고 했다. 그래서 물어보니, 낸터킷에서 죽은 고래잡이들은 모두 그 똑같은 검은 카누 안에 누인다는 것을 알게 되었고, 그렇게 누인다는 생각이 그의 마음에 무척 흡족했다고 했다. 그것이 제 종족의 풍습과 별반 다르지 않았기 때문이니, 그들은 죽은 전사의 시신에 향유를 바른 뒤 그를 카누 안에 누여, 별들의 군도(群島)로 떠내려가도록 그대로 띄워 보냈던 것이다. 그들은 별이 곧 섬이라고 믿을 뿐 아니라, 눈에 보이는 모든 수평선 너머 저 멀리, 뭍이라곤 없는 그들 자신의 온화한 바다가 푸른 하늘과 어우러져 흘러들며, 그렇게 은하수의 하얀 물보라를 이룬다고 믿었다. 그는 덧붙이기를, 흔한 바다 풍습대로 제 그물 침대에 싸여 묻혀, 죽음을 게걸스레 삼키는 상어들에게 천한 쓰레기처럼 내던져진다는 생각만 해도 몸서리가 쳐진다고 했다. 아니다. 그는 낸터킷의 그것들과 같은 카누를 원했다. 그것은 고래잡이인 그에게 한결 더 어울리는 것이었으니, 고래잡이 보트처럼 이 관(棺) 카누들에는 용골이 없었기 때문이다. 비록 그 때문에 키잡이가 불안정하고, 흐릿한 세월의 흐름 속에서 옆으로 많이 떠밀려 가게 된다 할지라도 말이다."]], [["Now, when this strange circumstance was made known aft, the carpenter was at once commanded to do Queequeg’s bidding, whatever it might include.", "자, 이 기이한 사정이 고물 쪽에 알려지자, 목수에게는 즉시 퀴케그의 청을 들어주라는 명령이 떨어졌으니, 그 청에 무엇이 포함되어 있든 상관없었다."], ["There was some heathenish, coffin-coloured old lumber aboard, which, upon a long previous voyage, had been cut from the aboriginal groves of the Lackaday islands, and from these dark planks the coffin was recommended to be made.", "배에는 어떤 이교도풍의, 관 빛깔을 띤 오래된 목재가 실려 있었는데, 그것은 오래전 어느 항해에서 래커데이 제도의 원시림에서 베어 온 것이었다. 그 검은 널빤지들로 관을 만들라는 권유가 내려졌다."], ["No sooner was the carpenter apprised of the order, than taking his rule, he forthwith with all the indifferent promptitude of his character, proceeded into the forecastle and took Queequeg’s measure with great accuracy, regularly chalking Queequeg’s person as he shifted the rule.", "목수는 명령을 전해 듣자마자 자를 들고는, 곧장 제 성격다운 무심하고 민첩한 태도로 선수루로 들어가 퀴케그의 치수를 매우 정확하게 쟀으니, 자를 옮길 때마다 퀴케그의 몸에 일정하게 분필 자국을 그어 가며 했다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["poor fellow!", "가엾은 친구!"], ["he’ll have to die now,”", "이제 죽어야겠구먼.\""], ["ejaculated the Long Island sailor.", "롱아일랜드 출신 선원이 내뱉었다."]], [["Going to his vice-bench, the carpenter for convenience sake and general reference, now transferringly measured on it the exact length the coffin was to be, and then made the transfer permanent by cutting two notches at its extremities.", "목수는 제 바이스 작업대로 가서, 편의와 두루 참고하기 위해, 관이 될 정확한 길이를 그 위에 옮겨 재고는, 그 양 끝에 홈 두 개를 새겨 그 표시를 영구히 남겼다."], ["This done, he marshalled the planks and his tools, and to work.", "이 일을 마치자, 그는 널빤지들과 연장들을 가지런히 늘어놓고 작업에 들어갔다."]], [["When the last nail was driven, and the lid duly planed and fitted, he lightly shouldered the coffin and went forward with it, inquiring whether they were ready for it yet in that direction.", "마지막 못을 박고, 뚜껑을 알맞게 대패질하여 꼭 맞추고 나자, 그는 그 관을 가볍게 어깨에 둘러메고 앞쪽으로 가면서, 그쪽에서 이제 그것을 받을 준비가 되었는지 물었다."]], [["Overhearing the indignant but half-humorous cries with which the people on deck began to drive the coffin away, Queequeg, to every one’s consternation, commanded that the thing should be instantly brought to him, nor was there any denying him; seeing that, of all mortals, some dying men are the most tyrannical; and certainly, since they will shortly trouble us so little for evermore, the poor fellows ought to be indulged.", "갑판 위의 사람들이 그 관을 쫓아내려 지르기 시작한, 분개하면서도 반쯤은 익살스러운 외침을 엿들은 퀴케그는, 모두를 경악케 하면서, 그 물건을 당장 자기에게 가져오라고 명령했다. 그의 청을 거절할 도리는 없었다. 모든 인간 가운데 죽어 가는 이들이야말로 더러는 가장 폭군 같은 법이고, 또한 머지않아 그들이 영영 우리를 거의 성가시게 하지 않을 터이니, 그 가련한 친구들의 청을 들어주는 것이 마땅하기 때문이었다."]], [["Leaning over in his hammock, Queequeg long regarded the coffin with an attentive eye.", "그물 침대에서 몸을 기울인 채, 퀴케그는 그 관을 한참 동안 주의 깊은 눈으로 바라보았다."], ["He then called for his harpoon, had the wooden stock drawn from it, and then had the iron part placed in the coffin along with one of the paddles of his boat.", "그러더니 자기 작살을 가져오라 하여, 거기서 나무 자루를 뽑아내게 하고는, 쇠로 된 부분을 제 보트의 노 하나와 함께 관 안에 넣게 했다."], ["All by his own request, also, biscuits were then ranged round the sides within: a flask of fresh water was placed at the head, and a small bag of woody earth scraped up in the hold at the foot; and a piece of sail-cloth being rolled up for a pillow, Queequeg now entreated to be lifted into his final bed, that he might make trial of its comforts, if any it had.", "또한 모두 그 자신의 요청에 따라, 비스킷이 관 안쪽 옆구리를 빙 둘러 늘어놓였고, 머리맡에는 신선한 물이 담긴 병이 놓였으며, 발치에는 화물칸에서 긁어모은 나무 섞인 흙 한 자루가 놓였다. 그리고 돛천 한 조각을 베개 삼아 둘둘 말아 놓고서, 퀴케그는 이제 자신을 그 마지막 침상으로 들어 올려 달라고 간청했으니, 그것이 안락한 데가 조금이라도 있는지 시험해 보려는 것이었다."], ["He lay without moving a few minutes, then told one to go to his bag and bring out his little god, Yojo.", "그는 몇 분 동안 꼼짝 않고 누워 있다가, 한 사람더러 제 자루로 가서 자신의 작은 신, 요조(Yojo)를 꺼내 오라고 했다."], ["Then crossing his arms on his breast with Yojo between, he called for the coffin lid (hatch he called it) to be placed over him.", "그러더니 요조를 사이에 끼우고 두 팔을 가슴 위에 엇갈려 포갠 채, 관 뚜껑을(그는 그것을 승강구 덮개라 불렀다) 제 위에 덮어 달라고 했다."], ["The head part turned over with a leather hinge, and there lay Queequeg in his coffin with little but his composed countenance in view.", "머리 쪽 부분은 가죽 경첩으로 젖혀졌고, 그렇게 퀴케그는 제 관 안에 누워, 그 차분한 얼굴 외에는 거의 보이지 않게 되었다."], ["“Rarmai” (it will do; it is easy), he murmured at last, and signed to be replaced in his hammock.", "\"라르마이\"(괜찮다, 편하다), 마침내 그가 중얼거리고는, 다시 그물 침대로 옮겨 달라는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["But ere this was done, Pip, who had been slily hovering near by all this while, drew nigh to him where he lay, and with soft sobbings, took him by the hand; in the other, holding his tambourine.", "그러나 이 일이 이루어지기 전에, 그동안 줄곧 곁을 슬며시 맴돌던 핍(Pip)이 그가 누운 곳으로 다가와, 나직이 흐느끼며 그의 손을 잡았다. 다른 손에는 제 탬버린을 들고 있었다."]], [["“Poor rover!", "\"가엾은 떠돌이여!"], ["will ye never have done with all this weary roving?", "이 지긋지긋한 떠돌이 신세를 영영 끝내지 못하려나?"], ["where go ye now?", "이제 어디로 가나?"], ["But if the currents carry ye to those sweet Antilles where the beaches are only beat with water-lilies, will ye do one little errand for me?", "하지만 만약 해류가 그대를 저 달콤한 앤틸리스 제도로 데려간다면, 그 바닷가가 오직 수련꽃으로만 찰싹이는 그곳으로 데려간다면, 나를 위해 작은 심부름 하나 해 주려나?"], ["Seek out one Pip, who’s now been missing long: I think he’s in those far Antilles.", "핍이라는 녀석을 찾아 줘, 오래도록 행방불명된 녀석을. 내 생각엔 그가 저 먼 앤틸리스에 있는 것 같아."], ["If ye find him, then comfort him; for he must be very sad; for look!", "만약 그를 찾거든, 위로해 줘. 그는 무척 슬플 테니까."], ["he’s left his tambourine behind;—I found it.", "보라고! 그는 제 탬버린을 두고 갔거든 — 그걸 내가 찾았어."], ["Rig-a-dig, dig, dig!", "리그어디그, 디그, 디그!"], ["Now, Queequeg, die; and I’ll beat ye your dying march.”", "자, 퀴케그, 죽어. 그럼 내가 그대의 장송곡을 두드려 주지.\""]], [["“I have heard,”", "\"내가 들은 바로는,\""], ["murmured Starbuck, gazing down the scuttle, “that in violent fevers, men, all ignorance, have talked in ancient tongues; and that when the mystery is probed, it turns out always that in their wholly forgotten childhood those ancient tongues had been really spoken in their hearing by some lofty scholars.", "스타벅(Starbuck)이 승강구를 내려다보며 중얼거렸다. \"심한 열병에 걸리면, 아무것도 모르는 사람들이 고대의 언어로 말을 한다고들 하지."], ["So, to my fond faith, poor Pip, in this strange sweetness of his lunacy, brings heavenly vouchers of all our heavenly homes.", "그리고 그 수수께끼를 캐 보면, 언제나 밝혀지는 것이, 그들이 까맣게 잊어버린 어린 시절에 어떤 고매한 학자들이 그 고대 언어로 말하는 것을 그들이 실제로 들은 적이 있었다는 거야. 그러니 내 애틋한 믿음으로는, 가엾은 핍이 이 미친 상태의 기이한 감미로움 속에서, 우리 모두의 천국 같은 고향을 증명하는 하늘의 증표를 가져오는 것이리라."], ["Where learned he that, but there?—Hark!", "그가 저것을 어디서 배웠겠나, 그곳이 아니라면? — 쉿!"], ["he speaks again: but more wildly now.”", "그가 또 말을 하는군. 한데 이젠 더 거칠어졌어.\""]], [["“Form two and two!", "\"둘씩 둘씩 줄을 서!"], ["Let’s make a General of him!", "그를 장군으로 삼자고!"], ["Ho, where’s his harpoon?", "호, 그의 작살은 어디 있지?"], ["Lay it across here.—Rig-a-dig, dig, dig!", "여기 가로질러 놔. — 리그어디그, 디그, 디그!"], ["huzza!", "만세!"], ["Oh for a game cock now to sit upon his head and crow!", "오, 지금 싸움닭 한 마리가 그의 머리 위에 올라앉아 울어 준다면! 퀴케그는 용감하게 죽는다!"], ["Queequeg dies game!—mind ye that; Queequeg dies game!—take ye good heed of that; Queequeg dies game!", "— 잘들 새겨 둬, 퀴케그는 용감하게 죽어! — 그걸 똑똑히 명심해, 퀴케그는 용감하게 죽는단 말이야!"], ["I say; game, game, game!", "용감하게, 용감하게, 용감하게!"], ["but base little Pip, he died a coward; died all a’shiver;—out upon Pip!", "한데 비천하고 보잘것없는 핍, 그놈은 겁쟁이로 죽었지. 온통 벌벌 떨면서 죽었어 — 핍 따위는 꺼지라지!"], ["Hark ye; if ye find Pip, tell all the Antilles he’s a runaway; a coward, a coward, a coward!", "들어 봐. 만약 핍을 찾거든, 앤틸리스 전체에 그가 도망자라고 말해 줘."], ["Tell them he jumped from a whale-boat!", "겁쟁이, 겁쟁이, 겁쟁이라고! 그가 고래잡이 보트에서 뛰어내렸다고 일러 줘!"], ["I’d never beat my tambourine over base Pip, and hail him General, if he were once more dying here. No, no!", "비천한 핍이 다시 한번 여기서 죽어 간다 한들, 나는 결코 그놈을 위해 탬버린을 두드리며 그를 장군이라 부르지 않을 거야."], ["shame upon all cowards—shame upon them!", "안 돼, 안 돼! 모든 겁쟁이에게 수치를 — 그들에게 수치를!"], ["Let ’em go drown like Pip, that jumped from a whale-boat.", "그놈들은 핍처럼 빠져 죽게 내버려 둬, 고래잡이 보트에서 뛰어내린 핍처럼."], ["Shame!", "수치!"], ["shame!”", "수치다!\""]], [["During all this, Queequeg lay with closed eyes, as if in a dream.", "이러는 내내, 퀴케그는 마치 꿈속에 잠긴 듯 두 눈을 감은 채 누워 있었다."], ["Pip was led away, and the sick man was replaced in his hammock.", "핍은 이끌려 나갔고, 병든 이는 다시 그물 침대로 옮겨졌다."]], [["But now that he had apparently made every preparation for death; now that his coffin was proved a good fit, Queequeg suddenly rallied; soon there seemed no need of the carpenter’s box: and thereupon, when some expressed their delighted surprise, he, in substance, said, that the cause of his sudden convalescence was this;—at a critical moment, he had just recalled a little duty ashore, which he was leaving undone; and therefore had changed his mind about dying: he could not die yet, he averred. They asked him, then, whether to live or die was a matter of his own sovereign will and pleasure. He answered, certainly. In a word, it was Queequeg’s conceit, that if a man made up his mind to live, mere sickness could not kill him: nothing but a whale, or a gale, or some violent, ungovernable, unintelligent destroyer of that sort.", "그러나 그가 겉보기엔 죽음을 위한 만반의 준비를 다 갖춘 지금, 그의 관이 몸에 꼭 맞는다는 것이 증명된 지금, 퀴케그는 돌연 기운을 차렸다. 머지않아 목수의 그 상자는 필요 없어 보였다. 그리하여 몇몇이 기쁨에 찬 놀라움을 나타내자, 그는 요컨대 이렇게 말했다. 자신이 이토록 갑작스레 회복한 까닭은 이러하다고. — 위중한 순간에, 그는 문득 뭍에서 다 해 두지 못하고 남겨 둔 작은 볼일 하나가 떠올랐고, 그래서 죽음에 대한 마음을 바꾸었다는 것이었다. 아직은 죽을 수 없노라고, 그는 단언했다. 그러자 사람들은 그에게, 그렇다면 살고 죽는 것이 그 자신의 지엄한 의지와 뜻에 달린 문제냐고 물었다. 그는 물론이라고 답했다. 한마디로, 사람이 살기로 마음을 정하면 한낱 병 따위로는 그를 죽일 수 없다는 것이 퀴케그의 지론이었다. 그를 죽일 수 있는 것은 오직 고래나, 거센 폭풍이나, 그런 종류의 사납고 제어할 수 없으며 지각없는 파괴자뿐이라는 것이었다."]], [["Now, there is this noteworthy difference between savage and civilized; that while a sick, civilized man may be six months convalescing, generally speaking, a sick savage is almost half-well again in a day.", "자, 야만인과 문명인 사이에는 주목할 만한 차이가 하나 있으니, 그것은 병든 문명인은 대체로 회복에 여섯 달이 걸릴 수도 있는 반면, 병든 야만인은 하루 만에 거의 절반은 나아 버린다는 것이다."], ["So, in good time my Queequeg gained strength; and at length after sitting on the windlass for a few indolent days (but eating with a vigorous appetite) he suddenly leaped to his feet, threw out his arms and legs, gave himself a good stretching, yawned a little bit, and then springing into the head of his hoisted boat, and poising a harpoon, pronounced himself fit for a fight.", "그리하여 마침맞은 때에 나의 퀴케그는 기력을 되찾았다. 그리고 끝내 며칠 동안 권양기 위에 앉아 빈둥거리고 나서(그러면서도 왕성한 식욕으로 먹어 댔다), 그는 돌연 벌떡 일어나 두 팔과 두 다리를 쭉 뻗고는, 시원하게 기지개를 켜고 하품을 조금 하더니, 매달아 둔 제 보트의 이물로 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 작살을 겨눠 들고서, 자신은 싸울 채비가 되었노라고 선언했다."]], [["With a wild whimsiness, he now used his coffin for a sea-chest; and emptying into it his canvas bag of clothes, set them in order there. Many spare hours he spent, in carving the lid with all manner of grotesque figures and drawings; and it seemed that hereby he was striving, in his rude way, to copy parts of the twisted tattooing on his body. And this tattooing had been the work of a departed prophet and seer of his island, who, by those hieroglyphic marks, had written out on his body a complete theory of the heavens and the earth, and a mystical treatise on the art of attaining truth; so that Queequeg in his own proper person was a riddle to unfold; a wondrous work in one volume; but whose mysteries not even himself could read, though his own live heart beat against them; and these mysteries were therefore destined in the end to moulder away with the living parchment whereon they were inscribed, and so be unsolved to the last. And this thought it must have been which suggested to Ahab that wild exclamation of his, when one morning turning away from surveying poor Queequeg—“Oh, devilish tantalization of the gods!”", "거친 변덕을 부려, 그는 이제 제 관을 바다 궤짝으로 썼다. 그 안에 제 옷가지가 든 천 자루를 비워 넣고는, 그것들을 그 안에 가지런히 정돈했다. 그는 한가한 시간을 많이 들여, 온갖 기괴한 형상과 그림으로 그 뚜껑에 조각을 새겼다. 그렇게 함으로써 그는 제 투박한 방식으로, 제 몸에 새겨진 비비 꼬인 문신의 일부를 본떠 보려 애쓰는 듯했다. 그리고 이 문신은 그의 섬에서 이미 세상을 떠난 어느 예언자이자 선견자가 새긴 작품이었으니, 그는 그 상형문자 같은 표식들로 퀴케그의 몸에 하늘과 땅에 관한 완전한 이론과, 진리에 이르는 법에 관한 신비로운 논고를 써 놓았던 것이다. 그리하여 퀴케그는 그 자신의 몸뚱이로서 풀어내야 할 하나의 수수께끼였고, 한 권에 담긴 경이로운 저작이었다. 그러나 그 신비를 정작 그 자신조차 읽어 낼 수 없었으니, 비록 그의 살아 있는 심장이 바로 그 신비를 향해 고동치고 있었음에도 그러했다. 그러므로 이 신비들은 끝내, 그것들이 새겨진 살아 있는 양피지와 더불어 썩어 사라져, 마지막까지 풀리지 못할 운명이었다. 그리고 어느 아침 가련한 퀴케그를 바라보다가 등을 돌리며 에이해브(Ahab)가 내뱉었던 저 거친 외침을 떠올리게 한 것도, 바로 이 생각이었음에 틀림없다 — \"오, 신들의 악마 같은 감질나는 희롱이여!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 111. The Pacific.", "# CHAPTER 111. The Pacific."]], [["When gliding by the Bashee isles we emerged at last upon the great South Sea; were it not for other things, I could have greeted my dear Pacific with uncounted thanks, for now the long supplication of my youth was answered; that serene ocean rolled eastwards from me a thousand leagues of blue.", "바시 제도를 미끄러지듯 지나치며 우리는 마침내 저 광대한 남양(南洋)으로 나섰다. 다른 사정만 없었던들 나는 헤아릴 수 없는 감사로 사랑하는 태평양에게 인사를 건넸으리라. 이제 내 젊은 날의 오랜 간구가 응답받았으니, 저 고요한 대양이 내게서 동쪽으로 천 리그에 걸친 푸른 물결을 굽이쳐 펼쳐 내고 있었다."]], [["There is, one knows not what sweet mystery about this sea, whose gently awful stirrings seem to speak of some hidden soul beneath; like those fabled undulations of the Ephesian sod over the buried Evangelist St.", "이 바다에는 무어라 형언할 수 없는 달콤한 신비가 깃들어 있다."], ["John.", "그 잔잔하면서도 두려운 일렁임은 그 아래 숨은 어떤 영혼을 말해 주는 듯하니, 마치 땅에 묻힌 복음서 기자 성 요한의 무덤 위로 전설처럼 물결치던 에페소스의 잔디와도 같다."], ["And meet it is, that over these sea-pastures, wide-rolling watery prairies and Potters’ Fields of all four continents, the waves should rise and fall, and ebb and flow unceasingly; for here, millions of mixed shades and shadows, drowned dreams, somnambulisms, reveries; all that we call lives and souls, lie dreaming, dreaming, still; tossing like slumberers in their beds; the ever-rolling waves but made so by their restlessness.", "그리고 마땅한 일이거니와, 이 바다의 목초지 위로, 사대륙의 너르게 굽이치는 물의 초원이자 모든 옹기장이 밭 위로, 파도가 솟았다 가라앉고 끝없이 밀려왔다 빠져나가는 것이다. 이곳에는 뒤섞인 무수한 그늘과 그림자, 익사한 꿈들, 몽유와 몽상이, 우리가 삶이라 영혼이라 부르는 그 모든 것이 누워 꿈꾸고 있고, 꿈꾸고 있고, 여전히 꿈꾸며, 침대에서 잠든 이들처럼 뒤척이고 있으니, 끊임없이 굽이치는 파도란 다만 그들의 잠 못 이루는 뒤척임이 빚어낸 것일 뿐이다."]], [["To any meditative Magian rover, this serene Pacific, once beheld, must ever after be the sea of his adoption.", "* 옹기장이 밭: 마태복음에서 유다가 예수를 판 돈으로 산 나그네 묘지. 이름 없는 자들의 매장지를 뜻한다."], ["It rolls the midmost waters of the world, the Indian ocean and Atlantic being but its arms.", "사색에 잠긴 어떤 동방박사 같은 방랑자에게라도, 이 고요한 태평양은 한 번 바라본 뒤로는 영영 그가 택해 모실 바다가 될 수밖에 없다."], ["The same waves wash the moles of the new-built Californian towns, but yesterday planted by the recentest race of men, and lave the faded but still gorgeous skirts of Asiatic lands, older than Abraham; while all between float milky-ways of coral isles, and low-lying, endless, unknown Archipelagoes, and impenetrable Japans.", "이 바다는 세계의 한복판 물결을 굽이쳐 흐르게 하며, 인도양과 대서양조차 그 양팔에 지나지 않는다. 같은 파도가 이제 막 가장 새로운 인종의 손으로 어제 세워진 캘리포니아 신도시들의 방파제를 적시는가 하면, 아브라함보다도 오래된 아시아 땅의 빛바랬으되 여전히 화려한 옷자락을 씻어 낸다."], ["Thus this mysterious, divine Pacific zones the world’s whole bulk about; makes all coasts one bay to it; seems the tide-beating heart of earth.", "그리고 그 사이로는 산호섬들의 은하수와, 낮게 깔린 끝없는 미지의 군도들과, 발 들여놓을 수 없는 일본 열도가 떠 있다. 이렇듯 이 신비롭고 거룩한 태평양은 세계의 온 덩어리를 띠처럼 두르고, 모든 해안을 자신의 한 만(灣)으로 만들며, 마치 조수로 고동치는 지구의 심장처럼 보인다."], ["Lifted by those eternal swells, you needs must own the seductive god, bowing your head to Pan.", "저 영원한 너울에 들어 올려질 때, 그대는 이 유혹하는 신에게 고개를 숙여 판(Pan)에게 절하지 않을 수 없으리라."]], [["But few thoughts of Pan stirred Ahab’s brain, as standing like an iron statue at his accustomed place beside the mizen rigging, with one nostril he unthinkingly snuffed the sugary musk from the Bashee isles (in whose sweet woods mild lovers must be walking), and with the other consciously inhaled the salt breath of the new found sea; that sea in which the hated White Whale must even then be swimming.", "그러나 에이해브의 머릿속에는 판에 대한 생각이 거의 일지 않았다. 늘 서 있던 자리, 곧 뒷돛대 삭구 곁에서 무쇠 조각상처럼 서서, 한쪽 콧구멍으로는 무심히 바시 제도에서 풍겨 오는 설탕처럼 달콤한 사향 내음(그 향긋한 숲에서는 다정한 연인들이 거닐고 있으리라)을 들이마시고, 다른 콧구멍으로는 새로이 찾아든 바다의 짠 숨결을, 곧 미워 마지않는 흰 고래가 바로 그 순간에도 헤엄치고 있을 그 바다의 숨결을 또렷이 의식하며 들이켰다."], ["Launched at length upon these almost final waters, and gliding towards the Japanese cruising-ground, the old man’s purpose intensified itself.", "마침내 이 거의 마지막에 다다른 바다 위로 배를 띄워, 일본 어장을 향해 미끄러져 나아가면서, 노인의 결의는 더욱 단단해졌다."], ["His firm lips met like the lips of a vice; the Delta of his forehead’s veins swelled like overladen brooks; in his very sleep, his ringing cry ran through the vaulted hull, “Stern all!", "굳게 다문 그의 입술은 죔쇠의 두 턱처럼 맞물렸고, 이마에 솟은 핏줄의 삼각주는 물 넘치는 개울처럼 부풀어 올랐다. 잠결에조차 그의 쩌렁쩌렁한 외침이 둥근 선체 안을 뚫고 울려 퍼졌다."], ["the White Whale spouts thick blood!”", "\"전원 후진! 흰 고래가 검붉은 피를 뿜는다!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 112. The Blacksmith.", "# CHAPTER 112. The Blacksmith."]], [["Availing himself of the mild, summer-cool weather that now reigned in these latitudes, and in preparation for the peculiarly active pursuits shortly to be anticipated, Perth, the begrimed, blistered old blacksmith, had not removed his portable forge to the hold again, after concluding his contributory work for Ahab’s leg, but still retained it on deck, fast lashed to ringbolts by the foremast; being now almost incessantly invoked by the headsmen, and harpooneers, and bowsmen to do some little job for them; altering, or repairing, or new shaping their various weapons and boat furniture.", "지금 이 위도에서 펼쳐지는 온화하고 여름처럼 서늘한 날씨를 십분 활용하면서, 또한 머지않아 닥쳐올 유난히 분주한 사냥에 대비하느라, 그을음투성이에 물집투성이인 늙은 대장장이 페스(Perth)는 에이해브(Ahab)의 다리를 만드는 데 거든 일을 마친 뒤에도 휴대용 화로를 다시 선창으로 옮기지 않았고, 여전히 갑판 위에 둔 채 앞돛대 옆의 고리볼트에 단단히 붙들어 매어 두었다. 작살대장과 작살잡이와 뱃머리 노잡이들이 이런저런 소소한 일을 부탁하느라 거의 끊임없이 그를 불러 댔기 때문이었다."], ["Often he would be surrounded by an eager circle, all waiting to be served; holding boat-spades, pike-heads, harpoons, and lances, and jealously watching his every sooty movement, as he toiled.", "그들은 갖가지 무기와 보트 장비를 손보거나, 고치거나, 새 모양으로 다듬어 달라고 청했다. 그는 종종 차례를 기다리는 사람들의 열성적인 무리에 둘러싸이곤 했는데, 그들은 보트 삽과 창끝과 작살과 투창을 들고서, 그가 땀 흘리며 일하는 동안 검댕투성이의 그 일거수일투족을 샘하듯 지켜보았다."], ["Nevertheless, this old man’s was a patient hammer wielded by a patient arm.", "그럼에도 이 노인의 망치는 참을성 있는 팔에 들린 참을성 있는 망치였다."], ["No murmur, no impatience, no petulance did come from him.", "그에게서는 어떤 투덜거림도, 어떤 조바심도, 어떤 짜증도 새어 나오지 않았다."], ["Silent, slow, and solemn; bowing over still further his chronically broken back, he toiled away, as if toil were life itself, and the heavy beating of his hammer the heavy beating of his heart.", "묵묵하고, 느리고, 엄숙하게, 만성적으로 굽은 등을 한층 더 깊이 숙인 채, 그는 마치 노동이 곧 삶 그 자체이고 망치의 무거운 두드림이 곧 제 심장의 무거운 박동이기라도 한 듯이 부지런히 일했다."], ["And so it was.—Most miserable!", "그리고 실로 그러했다. 더없이 비참하게도!"]], [["A peculiar walk in this old man, a certain slight but painful appearing yawing in his gait, had at an early period of the voyage excited the curiosity of the mariners.", "이 노인에게는 독특한 걸음걸이가 있었으니, 걸을 때 어딘지 미세하지만 고통스러워 보이는 비틀거림이 항해 초기부터 선원들의 호기심을 자극했다."], ["And to the importunity of their persisted questionings he had finally given in; and so it came to pass that every one now knew the shameful story of his wretched fate.", "끈질기게 거듭되는 그들의 캐물음에 그는 마침내 굴복하고 말았다. 그리하여 이제는 모두가 그 가련한 운명에 얽힌 부끄러운 사연을 알게 되었다."]], [["Belated, and not innocently, one bitter winter’s midnight, on the road running between two country towns, the blacksmith half-stupidly felt the deadly numbness stealing over him, and sought refuge in a leaning, dilapidated barn.", "어느 매서운 겨울날 한밤중, 두 시골 마을 사이로 난 길에서, 늦은 데다 죄 없는 까닭도 아니었던 이 대장장이는 반쯤 멍한 상태로 치명적인 마비가 제 몸을 슬금슬금 덮쳐 오는 것을 느끼고는, 한쪽으로 기울어 다 허물어져 가는 헛간에 몸을 피했다."], ["The issue was, the loss of the extremities of both feet.", "그 결과는 두 발 끝부분을 모두 잃은 것이었다."], ["Out of this revelation, part by part, at last came out the four acts of the gladness, and the one long, and as yet uncatastrophied fifth act of the grief of his life’s drama.", "이 사연에서 한 토막 한 토막씩, 마침내 그의 인생 드라마를 이루는 네 막의 기쁨과, 아직 파국에 이르지 않은 길고 긴 다섯째 막의 슬픔이 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["He was an old man, who, at the age of nearly sixty, had postponedly encountered that thing in sorrow’s technicals called ruin.", "그는 노인이었으니, 예순에 가까운 나이에 이르러서야 슬픔의 전문 용어로 파멸이라 불리는 그것과 뒤늦게 마주친 사람이었다. 그는 솜씨가 명성 자자한 장인으로, 일거리가 넘쳐 났다."], ["He had been an artisan of famed excellence, and with plenty to do; owned a house and garden; embraced a youthful, daughter-like, loving wife, and three blithe, ruddy children; every Sunday went to a cheerful-looking church, planted in a grove.", "집과 정원도 소유했고, 젊고 딸처럼 사랑스러운 아내와 발랄하고 혈색 좋은 세 아이를 품에 안았으며, 일요일마다 숲속에 자리한 정겨운 모습의 교회에 다녔다. 그런데 어느 날 밤, 어둠을 틈타, 게다가 더없이 교활한 변장으로 한층 몸을 감춘 채, 한 절박한 강도가 그의 행복한 가정으로 슬며시 기어들어 와 그들에게서 모든 것을 빼앗아 갔다."], ["But one night, under cover of darkness, and further concealed in a most cunning disguisement, a desperate burglar slid into his happy home, and robbed them all of everything.", "더욱 어두운 이야기는, 다름 아닌 대장장이 자신이 알지도 못한 채 이 강도를 제 가족의 심장 한복판으로 끌어들였다는 것이다. 그것은 바로 술병 마술사였다!"], ["And darker yet to tell, the blacksmith himself did ignorantly conduct this burglar into his family’s heart.", "그 치명적인 코르크 마개를 따는 순간, 악귀가 튀어나와 그의 가정을 바싹 시들게 했다. 그런데 신중하고 더없이 현명하며 경제적인 이유에서, 대장장이의 작업장은 그가 사는 집의 지하에 있었으되 따로 난 출입구를 갖추고 있었다."], ["It was the Bottle Conjuror! Upon the opening of that fatal cork, forth flew the fiend, and shrivelled up his home.", "그리하여 젊고 사랑스럽고 건강한 아내는 늘, 어떤 불행한 초조함도 없이 도리어 활기찬 즐거움으로, 젊은 팔뚝을 지닌 늙은 남편이 휘두르는 망치의 우렁찬 울림에 귀를 기울이곤 했다."], ["Now, for prudent, most wise, and economic reasons, the blacksmith’s shop was in the basement of his dwelling, but with a separate entrance to it; so that always had the young and loving healthy wife listened with no unhappy nervousness, but with vigorous pleasure, to the stout ringing of her young-armed old husband’s hammer; whose reverberations, muffled by passing through the floors and walls, came up to her, not unsweetly, in her nursery; and so, to stout Labor’s iron lullaby, the blacksmith’s infants were rocked to slumber.", "마룻바닥과 벽을 지나오며 한결 부드러워진 그 울림은 아기방의 그녀에게로 제법 감미롭게 올라왔다. 그렇게 억센 노동이 들려주는 무쇠 자장가에 맞추어, 대장장이의 어린것들은 잠 속으로 흔들려 갔다."]], [["Oh, woe on woe!", "오, 비탄 위에 또 비탄이!"], ["Oh, Death, why canst thou not sometimes be timely?", "오, 죽음이여, 그대는 어찌하여 때로는 제때에 찾아오지 못하는가?"], ["Hadst thou taken this old blacksmith to thyself ere his full ruin came upon him, then had the young widow had a delicious grief, and her orphans a truly venerable, legendary sire to dream of in their after years; and all of them a care-killing competency.", "완전한 파멸이 이 늙은 대장장이를 덮치기 전에 그대가 그를 데려갔더라면, 젊은 미망인은 달콤한 슬픔을 누렸을 것이요, 그 고아들은 훗날 세월 속에서 꿈꿀 수 있는 참으로 존경스럽고 전설적인 아버지를 가졌을 것이며, 그들 모두 근심을 덜어 줄 넉넉한 살림을 지녔으리라."], ["But Death plucked down some virtuous elder brother, on whose whistling daily toil solely hung the responsibilities of some other family, and left the worse than useless old man standing, till the hideous rot of life should make him easier to harvest.", "그러나 죽음은 어느 덕망 높은 형을 꺾어 버렸으니, 휘파람 불며 날마다 흘리는 그의 노동에 또 다른 한 가정의 책임이 오롯이 걸려 있던 사람이었거니와, 그러면서도 쓸모없는 것만도 못한 이 노인은 그대로 세워 두어, 삶의 끔찍한 썩어 감이 그를 거두기 쉽게 만들 때까지 버려두었다."]], [["Why tell the whole? The blows of the basement hammer every day grew more and more between; and each blow every day grew fainter than the last; the wife sat frozen at the window, with tearless eyes, glitteringly gazing into the weeping faces of her children; the bellows fell; the forge choked up with cinders; the house was sold; the mother dived down into the long church-yard grass; her children twice followed her thither; and the houseless, familyless old man staggered off a vagabond in crape; his every woe unreverenced; his grey head a scorn to flaxen curls!", "그 전부를 굳이 다 이야기할 까닭이 있겠는가? 지하실 망치의 두드림은 날마다 점점 더 띄엄띄엄해졌고, 매번의 두드림은 날마다 지난번보다 더 희미해졌다. 아내는 창가에 얼어붙은 듯 앉아, 눈물조차 마른 눈으로, 우는 아이들의 얼굴을 번득이며 응시했다. 풀무는 늘어졌고, 화로는 잿더미로 막혔으며, 집은 팔렸다. 어머니는 교회 묘지의 긴 풀밭 속으로 잠겨 들었고, 아이들도 두 차례나 그녀를 따라 그곳으로 갔다. 집도 가족도 없는 늙은이는 상장(喪章)을 두른 떠돌이가 되어 비틀거리며 떠나갔다. 그의 비탄은 그 무엇 하나 공경받지 못했고, 그의 희끗한 머리는 노란 곱슬머리들의 비웃음거리가 되었다!"]], [["Death seems the only desirable sequel for a career like this; but Death is only a launching into the region of the strange Untried; it is but the first salutation to the possibilities of the immense Remote, the Wild, the Watery, the Unshored; therefore, to the death-longing eyes of such men, who still have left in them some interior compunctions against suicide, does the all-contributed and all-receptive ocean alluringly spread forth his whole plain of unimaginable, taking terrors, and wonderful, new-life adventures; and from the hearts of infinite Pacifics, the thousand mermaids sing to them—“Come hither, broken-hearted; here is another life without the guilt of intermediate death; here are wonders supernatural, without dying for them. Come hither! bury thyself in a life which, to your now equally abhorred and abhorring, landed world, is more oblivious than death. Come hither! put up thy gravestone, too, within the churchyard, and come hither, till we marry thee!”", "이런 인생에는 죽음만이 유일하게 바람직한 후일담으로 보일 것이다. 그러나 죽음이란 다만 기이한 미지(未知)의 영역으로 배를 띄우는 일에 지나지 않는다. 그것은 광막한 머나먼 곳, 거친 곳, 물의 세계, 기슭 없는 곳이 품은 갖가지 가능성을 향한 첫 인사일 따름이다. 그러므로 죽음을 갈망하면서도 마음속 어딘가에 아직 자살을 꺼리는 양심의 가책이 남아 있는 그런 사람들의 눈앞에, 모든 것을 받아들이고 모든 것이 흘러드는 바다는 그 상상조차 할 수 없는, 사람의 넋을 앗아 가는 공포와 경이로운 새 삶의 모험으로 가득한 너른 들판을 통째로 유혹하듯 펼쳐 보인다. 그리하여 끝없는 태평양들의 가슴속으로부터, 수천의 인어가 그들에게 노래한다. \"이리로 오라, 마음이 부서진 자여. 여기 중간에 끼인 죽음의 죄책감 없이 또 하나의 삶이 있노라. 여기 그것을 위해 죽지 않고도 누릴 초자연의 경이가 있노라. 이리로 오라! 이제 그대가 똑같이 혐오하고 또 그대를 혐오하는 저 뭍의 세계보다 더 망각에 잠긴 삶 속에 그대 자신을 묻으라. 이리로 오라! 그대의 묘비 또한 교회 묘지 안에 세워 두고, 이리로 오라, 우리가 그대를 혼인시켜 줄 때까지!\""]], [["Hearkening to these voices, East and West, by early sunrise, and by fall of eve, the blacksmith’s soul responded, Aye, I come!", "이 목소리들에 귀 기울이며, 동쪽에서 서쪽에서, 이른 해돋이 무렵과 저물녘에, 대장장이의 영혼은 화답했다. 그래, 내가 가마!"], ["And so Perth went a-whaling.", "그리하여 페스는 고래잡이의 길로 나섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER 113. The Forge.", "# CHAPTER 113. The Forge."]], [["With matted beard, and swathed in a bristling shark-skin apron, about mid-day, Perth was standing between his forge and anvil, the latter placed upon an iron-wood log, with one hand holding a pike-head in the coals, and with the other at his forge’s lungs, when Captain Ahab came along, carrying in his hand a small rusty-looking leathern bag. While yet a little distance from the forge, moody Ahab paused; till at last, Perth, withdrawing his iron from the fire, began hammering it upon the anvil—the red mass sending off the sparks in thick hovering flights, some of which flew close to Ahab.", "정오 무렵, 텁수룩한 수염을 하고 빳빳한 상어 가죽 앞치마를 두른 채, 퍼스는 화덕과 모루 사이에 서 있었다. 모루는 철목(鐵木) 토막 위에 놓여 있었고, 그는 한 손으로 작살촉을 숯불 속에 쥐고, 다른 손으로는 화덕의 풀무를 다루고 있었다. 그때 에이해브 선장이 작고 녹슨 듯한 가죽 자루를 손에 들고 다가왔다. 화덕에서 조금 떨어진 곳에서 침울한 에이해브는 걸음을 멈추었다. 마침내 퍼스가 쇠를 불에서 꺼내 모루 위에 두드리기 시작하자 — 벌겋게 달아오른 쇳덩이가 두텁게 떠도는 불꽃을 흩뿌렸고, 그중 몇 점은 에이해브의 곁으로 바짝 날아들었다."]], [["“Are these thy Mother Carey’s chickens, Perth? they are always flying in thy wake; birds of good omen, too, but not to all;—look here, they burn; but thou—thou liv’st among them without a scorch.”", "\"이게 자네의 폭풍 바닷새들인가, 퍼스? 늘 자네 자취를 따라 날아다니는군. 길조의 새들이긴 하지만, 모두에게 그런 건 아니지. 보게, 이놈들은 불에 탄다네. 그런데 자네는 — 자네는 그것들 속에서 살면서도 그을음 하나 입지 않는군.\""]], [["“Because I am scorched all over, Captain Ahab,”", "\"제가 이미 온몸이 다 그을렸으니 그렇지요, 에이해브 선장님.\" 퍼스가 망치를 짚고 잠시 쉬며 대답했다."], ["answered Perth, resting for a moment on his hammer; “I am past scorching; not easily can’st thou scorch a scar.”", "\"저는 이미 그을릴 단계를 지났습니다. 흉터를 다시 그을리기란 쉬운 일이 아니지요.\""]], [["“Well, well; no more.", "\"좋아, 좋아. 그만하게."], ["Thy shrunk voice sounds too calmly, sanely woeful to me.", "자네의 쪼그라든 목소리는 내게 너무도 차분하고, 너무도 멀쩡하게 슬프게 들리는군."], ["In no Paradise myself, I am impatient of all misery in others that is not mad.", "나 스스로도 낙원에 있지 못한 처지인지라, 광기에 미치지 못한 채 그저 비참하기만 한 남의 고통은 도무지 견딜 수가 없네."], ["Thou should’st go mad, blacksmith; say, why dost thou not go mad?", "자네는 미쳐야 마땅하네, 대장장이. 말해 보게, 어찌하여 자네는 미치지 않는가?"], ["How can’st thou endure without being mad?", "미치지 않고서 어찌 견딘단 말인가?"], ["Do the heavens yet hate thee, that thou can’st not go mad?—What wert thou making there?”", "하늘이 아직도 자네를 미워하기에, 자네가 미칠 수조차 없는 건가? — 거기서 무얼 만들고 있었나?\""]], [["“Welding an old pike-head, sir; there were seams and dents in it.”", "\"낡은 작살촉을 벼리고 있었습니다, 선장님. 이음매며 우그러진 데가 있어서요.\""]], [["“And can’st thou make it all smooth again, blacksmith, after such hard usage as it had?”", "\"그러면 그토록 험하게 부려 먹은 것을 다시 매끈하게 만들 수 있겠나, 대장장이?\""]], [["“I think so, sir.”", "\"그럴 수 있을 것 같습니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“And I suppose thou can’st smoothe almost any seams and dents; never mind how hard the metal, blacksmith?”", "\"그렇다면 자네는 아무리 단단한 쇠라 한들, 거의 어떤 이음매든 우그러진 데든 매끈하게 펼 수 있겠군그래, 대장장이?\""]], [["“Aye, sir, I think I can; all seams and dents but one.”", "\"예, 선장님, 그럴 수 있을 것 같습니다. 단 하나를 빼고는 모든 이음매와 우그러진 데를 말이지요.\""]], [["“Look ye here, then,”", "\"그렇다면 여기를 보게.\""], ["cried Ahab, passionately advancing, and leaning with both hands on Perth’s shoulders; “look ye here—here—can ye smoothe out a seam like this, blacksmith,”", "에이해브가 격정에 차 다가서며, 두 손으로 퍼스의 두 어깨를 짚고 몸을 기댔다. \"여기를 보게 — 여기 말이야 — 이런 이음매를 자네가 펼 수 있겠나, 대장장이?\""], ["sweeping one hand across his ribbed brow; “if thou could’st, blacksmith, glad enough would I lay my head upon thy anvil, and feel thy heaviest hammer between my eyes.", "그러면서 한 손으로 주름진 자기 이마를 쓸어내렸다. \"만일 자네가 그럴 수만 있다면, 대장장이, 나는 기꺼이 이 머리를 자네 모루 위에 얹고, 두 눈 사이로 자네의 가장 묵직한 망치를 받아 보고 싶네."], ["Answer!", "대답하게!"], ["Can’st thou smoothe this seam?”", "이 이음매를 펼 수 있겠나?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["that is the one, sir!", "그것이 바로 그 하나입니다, 선장님!"], ["Said I not all seams and dents but one?”", "단 하나만 빼고 모든 이음매와 우그러진 데라고 말씀드리지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“Aye, blacksmith, it is the one; aye, man, it is unsmoothable; for though thou only see’st it here in my flesh, it has worked down into the bone of my skull—that is all wrinkles!", "\"그래, 대장장이, 그것이 바로 그 하나일세. 그래, 사람아, 그것은 펴지지 않는 것이지."], ["But, away with child’s play; no more gaffs and pikes to-day.", "자네 눈에는 이렇게 내 살갗에만 보이지만, 그것은 내 두개골 뼈 속까지 파고들어 갔네 — 그 속이 온통 주름투성이라네! 하지만 어린애 장난은 그만두지."], ["Look ye here!”", "오늘은 더 이상 갈고리며 작살은 됐네. 여기를 보게!\""], ["jingling the leathern bag, as if it were full of gold coins.", "그는 마치 금화가 가득 든 양 가죽 자루를 짤랑거렸다. \"나도 작살 하나가 필요하네."], ["“I, too, want a harpoon made; one that a thousand yoke of fiends could not part, Perth; something that will stick in a whale like his own fin-bone.", "천 마리 악마가 멍에를 메고 끌어당겨도 갈라놓지 못할 작살 말일세, 퍼스. 고래의 제 지느러미뼈처럼 그놈 몸에 단단히 박힐 작살 말이야."], ["There’s the stuff,”", "재료는 여기 있네.\""], ["flinging the pouch upon the anvil.", "그가 주머니를 모루 위에 내던졌다."], ["“Look ye, blacksmith, these are the gathered nail-stubbs of the steel shoes of racing horses.”", "\"보게, 대장장이, 이것은 경주마들의 강철 편자에서 모은 못 토막들일세.\""]], [["“Horse-shoe stubbs, sir?", "\"편자 못 토막이라고요, 선장님?"], ["Why, Captain Ahab, thou hast here, then, the best and stubbornest stuff we blacksmiths ever work.”", "아니, 에이해브 선장님, 그러면 여기 우리 대장장이들이 다뤄 본 것 중에서 가장 좋고 가장 질긴 재료를 가지고 계신 셈입니다.\""]], [["“I know it, old man; these stubbs will weld together like glue from the melted bones of murderers.", "\"나도 아네, 노인장. 이 토막들은 살인자들의 녹은 뼈에서 나온 아교처럼 단단히 엉겨 붙을 걸세."], ["Quick!", "어서!"], ["forge me the harpoon.", "내게 작살을 벼려 주게."], ["And forge me first, twelve rods for its shank; then wind, and twist, and hammer these twelve together like the yarns and strands of a tow-line.", "우선 그 자루에 들어갈 봉을 열두 개 벼려 주게. 그런 다음 이 열두 개를 예인줄의 가닥과 올처럼 비틀고 꼬아 한데 망치질해 합치게나."], ["Quick!", "어서!"], ["I’ll blow the fire.”", "내가 불을 불어 주겠네.\""]], [["When at last the twelve rods were made, Ahab tried them, one by one, by spiralling them, with his own hand, round a long, heavy iron bolt.", "마침내 봉 열두 개가 만들어지자, 에이해브는 그것들을 하나씩 손수 긴 쇠 빗장에 나선처럼 감아 보며 시험했다. \"흠집이 있군!\""], ["“A flaw!” rejecting the last one.", "그는 마지막 하나를 물리쳤다."], ["“Work that over again, Perth.”", "\"이건 다시 손보게, 퍼스.\""]], [["This done, Perth was about to begin welding the twelve into one, when Ahab stayed his hand, and said he would weld his own iron.", "이 일이 끝나자 퍼스는 열두 개를 하나로 용접하려 했으나, 에이해브가 그의 손을 멈춰 세우며 자기 손으로 직접 쇠를 벼리겠다고 말했다."], ["As, then, with regular, gasping hems, he hammered on the anvil, Perth passing to him the glowing rods, one after the other, and the hard pressed forge shooting up its intense straight flame, the Parsee passed silently, and bowing over his head towards the fire, seemed invoking some curse or some blessing on the toil.", "그리하여 에이해브가 규칙적으로 가쁜 숨을 헐떡이며 모루 위를 두드리고, 퍼스가 벌겋게 달아오른 봉을 하나씩 차례로 그에게 건네고, 세차게 압박된 화덕이 곧고 강렬한 불길을 위로 뿜어 올리는 동안, 파르시(Parsee) 사람이 소리 없이 다가와 불을 향해 머리를 숙이며, 이 노역에 어떤 저주인지 어떤 축복인지를 비는 듯했다."], ["But, as Ahab looked up, he slid aside.", "그러나 에이해브가 고개를 들자 그는 슬며시 옆으로 비켜섰다."]], [["“What’s that bunch of lucifers dodging about there for?”", "\"저기서 알짱거리는 저 성냥개비 같은 작자는 대체 뭐 하는 거야?\""], ["muttered Stubb, looking on from the forecastle.", "앞갑판에서 지켜보던 스터브가 중얼거렸다."], ["“That Parsee smells fire like a fusee; and smells of it himself, like a hot musket’s powder-pan.”", "\"저 파르시 놈은 도화선처럼 불 냄새를 맡고 다니지. 게다가 제 몸에서도 불 냄새가 나, 마치 달아오른 머스킷 총의 화약 접시처럼 말이야.\""]], [["At last the shank, in one complete rod, received its final heat; and as Perth, to temper it, plunged it all hissing into the cask of water near by, the scalding steam shot up into Ahab’s bent face.", "마침내 하나의 완전한 봉으로 합쳐진 자루가 마지막 가열을 받았다. 퍼스가 그것을 단련하려고 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 옆에 놓인 물통 속에 푹 담그자, 데인 듯 뜨거운 김이 에이해브의 수그린 얼굴로 솟구쳐 올랐다."]], [["“Would’st thou brand me, Perth?”", "\"자네 나를 낙인찍으려는 건가, 퍼스?\""], ["wincing for a moment with the pain; “have I been but forging my own branding-iron, then?”", "그는 잠시 통증에 움찔하며 말했다. \"그렇다면 나는 여태 내 낙인 인두를 벼려 온 셈인가?\""]], [["“Pray God, not that; yet I fear something, Captain Ahab.", "\"하느님께 비옵건대, 부디 그런 일이 아니기를. 그래도 저는 무언가가 두렵습니다, 에이해브 선장님."], ["Is not this harpoon for the White Whale?”", "이 작살은 흰 고래를 잡으려는 것 아닙니까?\""]], [["“For the white fiend!", "\"흰 악마를 잡으려는 것이지!"], ["But now for the barbs; thou must make them thyself, man.", "하지만 이제 미늘 차례일세. 그건 자네가 직접 만들어야 하네, 사람아."], ["Here are my razors—the best of steel; here, and make the barbs sharp as the needle-sleet of the Icy Sea.”", "여기 내 면도칼들이 있네 — 최상의 강철이지. 여기, 이걸로 빙해(氷海)의 바늘 같은 진눈깨비처럼 날카롭게 미늘을 만들게.\""]], [["For a moment, the old blacksmith eyed the razors as though he would fain not use them.", "잠시 동안 늙은 대장장이는 차라리 그 면도칼들을 쓰고 싶지 않다는 듯 물끄러미 바라보았다."]], [["“Take them, man, I have no need for them; for I now neither shave, sup, nor pray till—but here—to work!”", "\"받게, 사람아, 나는 이제 그것들이 필요 없네. 이제부터 나는 면도도, 식사도, 기도도 하지 않을 테니 — 그때까지는 말이야 — 자, 어서, 일이나 하게!\""]], [["Fashioned at last into an arrowy shape, and welded by Perth to the shank, the steel soon pointed the end of the iron; and as the blacksmith was about giving the barbs their final heat, prior to tempering them, he cried to Ahab to place the water-cask near.", "마침내 화살촉 모양으로 빚어진 강철은 퍼스의 손에 의해 자루에 용접되어 곧 그 끝을 뾰족하게 이루었다. 대장장이가 미늘을 단련하기에 앞서 마지막 가열을 하려 할 때, 그는 에이해브에게 물통을 가까이 두라고 외쳤다."]], [["“No, no—no water for that; I want it of the true death-temper.", "\"아니, 아니야 — 그것엔 물이 안 되네. 나는 그것이 참된 죽음의 단련을 거치기를 바라네."], ["Ahoy, there!", "어이, 거기!"], ["Tashtego, Queequeg, Daggoo! What say ye, pagans!", "타슈테고(Tashtego), 퀴퀘그(Queequeg), 다구!"], ["Will ye give me as much blood as will cover this barb?”", "어떤가, 이교도들이여! 이 미늘을 적실 만큼의 피를 내게 주겠나?\""], ["holding it high up.", "그는 미늘을 높이 치켜들었다."], ["A cluster of dark nods replied, Yes.", "검은 얼굴들이 한데 모여 고개를 끄덕여 답했다, 그렇다고."], ["Three punctures were made in the heathen flesh, and the White Whale’s barbs were then tempered.", "이교도들의 살에 세 군데 구멍이 뚫렸고, 그리하여 흰 고래의 미늘은 단련되었다."]], [["“Ego non baptizo te in nomine patris, sed in nomine diaboli!”", "\"내가 너에게 세례를 주노니, 아버지의 이름이 아니라 악마의 이름으로 하노라!(Ego non baptizo te in nomine patris, sed in nomine diaboli!)\""], ["deliriously howled Ahab, as the malignant iron scorchingly devoured the baptismal blood.", "악의에 찬 쇠가 세례의 피를 지글지글 집어삼키는 가운데, 에이해브가 광란하듯 울부짖었다."]], [["Now, mustering the spare poles from below, and selecting one of hickory, with the bark still investing it, Ahab fitted the end to the socket of the iron.", "이제 아래에서 여분의 자루용 장대들을 끌어모은 뒤, 그중 아직 껍질이 그대로 붙어 있는 히커리 나무 하나를 골라, 에이해브는 그 끝을 쇠 미늘의 소켓에 맞추어 끼웠다."], ["A coil of new tow-line was then unwound, and some fathoms of it taken to the windlass, and stretched to a great tension.", "이어 새 예인줄 한 사리를 풀어, 몇 길 정도를 양묘기까지 가져다가 팽팽하게 잡아당겼다."], ["Pressing his foot upon it, till the rope hummed like a harp-string, then eagerly bending over it, and seeing no strandings, Ahab exclaimed, “Good!", "그 위에 발을 디뎌 밧줄이 하프 줄처럼 윙윙 울릴 때까지 누른 다음, 열심히 몸을 숙여 들여다보고는 가닥이 풀린 데가 없음을 확인하자, 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"좋아!"], ["and now for the seizings.”", "이제 동여매는 일만 남았군.\""]], [["At one extremity the rope was unstranded, and the separate spread yarns were all braided and woven round the socket of the harpoon; the pole was then driven hard up into the socket; from the lower end the rope was traced half-way along the pole’s length, and firmly secured so, with intertwistings of twine.", "밧줄의 한쪽 끝을 가닥가닥 풀어 헤친 다음, 따로 펼쳐진 올들을 모두 엮고 짜서 작살의 소켓 둘레에 감았다. 그러고는 장대를 소켓 속으로 단단히 박아 넣었고, 아래쪽 끝에서부터 밧줄을 장대 길이의 절반까지 따라 올려, 노끈을 겹겹이 꼬아 그렇게 단단히 동여맸다."], ["This done, pole, iron, and rope—like the Three Fates—remained inseparable, and Ahab moodily stalked away with the weapon; the sound of his ivory leg, and the sound of the hickory pole, both hollowly ringing along every plank.", "이 일이 끝나자 장대와 쇠와 밧줄은 — 마치 운명의 세 여신처럼 — 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 하나가 되었고, 에이해브는 그 무기를 들고 침울하게 성큼성큼 걸어갔다. 그의 상아 다리 소리와 히커리 장대 소리가 갑판의 판자마다 텅 빈 울림을 내며 함께 울려 퍼졌다."], ["But ere he entered his cabin, light, unnatural, half-bantering, yet most piteous sound was heard.", "그러나 그가 선실로 들어서기 전, 가볍고 부자연스러우며 반쯤은 빈정거리는 듯한, 그러면서도 더없이 가련한 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Oh, Pip!", "오, 핍(Pip)!"], ["thy wretched laugh, thy idle but unresting eye; all thy strange mummeries not unmeaningly blended with the black tragedy of the melancholy ship, and mocked it!", "너의 비참한 웃음소리, 너의 멍하면서도 쉴 줄 모르는 눈빛. 너의 그 모든 기이한 광대놀음이, 우울한 배의 검은 비극과 의미 없지 않게 뒤섞여, 그것을 조롱하고 있었다!"]], [["# CHAPTER 114. The Gilder.", "# CHAPTER 114. The Gilder."]], [["Penetrating further and further into the heart of the Japanese cruising ground, the Pequod was soon all astir in the fishery.", "일본 어장의 한복판으로 점점 더 깊숙이 파고들면서, 피쿼드호(Pequod)는 이내 고래잡이로 온통 들끓었다."], ["Often, in mild, pleasant weather, for twelve, fifteen, eighteen, and twenty hours on the stretch, they were engaged in the boats, steadily pulling, or sailing, or paddling after the whales, or for an interlude of sixty or seventy minutes calmly awaiting their uprising; though with but small success for their pains.", "온화하고 쾌청한 날씨면 흔히 열두 시간, 열다섯 시간, 열여덟 시간, 스무 시간을 내리 보트에 매달려, 꾸준히 노를 젓거나 돛을 올리거나 노로 물을 헤치며 고래를 뒤쫓았고, 때로는 예순 분이나 일흔 분쯤 막간을 두고 고래가 다시 떠오르기를 차분히 기다리기도 했다. 그러나 그 고생에 비하면 거둔 성과는 보잘것없었다."]], [["At such times, under an abated sun; afloat all day upon smooth, slow heaving swells; seated in his boat, light as a birch canoe; and so sociably mixing with the soft waves themselves, that like hearth-stone cats they purr against the gunwale; these are the times of dreamy quietude, when beholding the tranquil beauty and brilliancy of the ocean’s skin, one forgets the tiger heart that pants beneath it; and would not willingly remember, that this velvet paw but conceals a remorseless fang.", "그런 때, 한풀 꺾인 햇살 아래 매끄럽고 느리게 너울지는 물결 위에 온종일 떠 있노라면, 자작나무 카누처럼 가벼운 보트에 앉아 부드러운 파도와 어찌나 다정하게 어우러지는지, 그 물결이 난롯가의 고양이처럼 뱃전에 몸을 비비며 가르랑거린다. 이런 때는 꿈결 같은 고요의 시간이어서, 바다 살갗의 잔잔한 아름다움과 광채를 바라보노라면 그 아래에서 헐떡이는 호랑이의 심장을 잊게 되고, 이 벨벳 같은 앞발이 무자비한 송곳니를 감추고 있을 뿐이라는 사실은 기꺼이 떠올리고 싶지 않게 된다."]], [["These are the times, when in his whale-boat the rover softly feels a certain filial, confident, land-like feeling towards the sea; that he regards it as so much flowery earth; and the distant ship revealing only the tops of her masts, seems struggling forward, not through high rolling waves, but through the tall grass of a rolling prairie: as when the western emigrants’ horses only show their erected ears, while their hidden bodies widely wade through the amazing verdure.", "이런 때면 고래잡이 보트에 탄 떠돌이는 바다를 향해 어딘지 자식 같은, 미덥고 뭍 같은 정을 가만히 느끼게 되어, 바다를 한없이 꽃 핀 대지처럼 여기게 된다. 그리고 돛대 꼭대기만 빠끔히 드러낸 채 저 멀리 보이는 배는 높이 굽이치는 파도가 아니라, 일렁이는 대초원의 키 큰 풀밭을 헤치며 앞으로 나아가려 안간힘을 쓰는 듯이 보인다. 마치 서부로 가는 이주민의 말들이 곤두선 귀끝만 내민 채, 가려진 몸뚱이는 놀랍도록 짙푸른 풀밭 속을 널찍이 헤쳐 나아가듯이."]], [["The long-drawn virgin vales; the mild blue hill-sides; as over these there steals the hush, the hum; you almost swear that play-wearied children lie sleeping in these solitudes, in some glad May-time, when the flowers of the woods are plucked.", "길게 뻗은 처녀의 골짜기들, 부드러운 푸른 산비탈들. 이 위로 적막과 나직한 웅성거림이 살며시 깃들면, 어느 즐거운 오월에 숲의 꽃들이 꺾이는 무렵, 놀다 지친 아이들이 이 외딴 곳에 누워 잠들어 있다고 거의 단언하고 싶어진다."], ["And all this mixes with your most mystic mood; so that fact and fancy, half-way meeting, interpenetrate, and form one seamless whole.", "그리고 이 모든 것이 그대의 가장 신비로운 심사와 뒤섞여, 사실과 공상이 중간에서 마주쳐 서로 스며들고, 이음매 없는 하나의 전체를 이룬다."]], [["Nor did such soothing scenes, however temporary, fail of at least as temporary an effect on Ahab.", "그처럼 마음을 달래 주는 풍경은, 비록 한순간일망정, 에이해브(Ahab)에게도 적어도 그만큼 한순간의 영향을 끼치지 않을 수 없었다."], ["But if these secret golden keys did seem to open in him his own secret golden treasuries, yet did his breath upon them prove but tarnishing.", "그러나 이 은밀한 황금 열쇠들이 그의 내면에 숨겨진 황금 보물 곳간을 열어젖히는 듯했어도, 거기에 닿는 그의 숨결은 그것을 도리어 흐려 놓을 따름이었다."]], [["Oh, grassy glades!", "오, 풀 우거진 빈터여!"], ["oh, ever vernal endless landscapes in the soul; in ye,—though long parched by the dead drought of the earthy life,—in ye, men yet may roll, like young horses in new morning clover; and for some few fleeting moments, feel the cool dew of the life immortal on them.", "오, 영혼 속에 영원히 봄빛으로 끝없이 펼쳐진 풍경이여. 비록 흙먼지 같은 세상살이의 메마른 가뭄에 오래도록 타들어 갔을지언정, 그대들 속에서 사람들은 아직도, 새 아침 클로버 풀밭의 어린 말들처럼 뒹굴 수 있고, 덧없는 몇 순간이나마 그 몸 위에 영생의 서늘한 이슬을 느낄 수 있으리라."], ["Would to God these blessed calms would last.", "이 축복받은 평온이 부디 영원하기를. 그러나 뒤섞인 채 뒤섞여 가는 인생의 실들은 날실과 씨실로 짜여 있다."], ["But the mingled, mingling threads of life are woven by warp and woof: calms crossed by storms, a storm for every calm.", "평온은 폭풍에 가로질리고, 평온 하나마다 폭풍 하나가 따른다. 이 인생에 되돌아가지 않는 한결같은 전진이란 없다."], ["There is no steady unretracing progress in this life; we do not advance through fixed gradations, and at the last one pause:—through infancy’s unconscious spell, boyhood’s thoughtless faith, adolescence’ doubt (the common doom), then scepticism, then disbelief, resting at last in manhood’s pondering repose of If.", "우리는 정해진 단계를 따라 나아가다가 마지막에 한 번 멈추는 것이 아니다. 곧 유년의 무의식적 황홀, 소년기의 무심한 믿음, 청년기의 회의(만인의 공통된 운명), 그다음 회의주의, 그다음 불신을 거쳐, 마침내 장년기의 '만약(If)'이라는 사색의 안식에 이른다."], ["But once gone through, we trace the round again; and are infants, boys, and men, and Ifs eternally.", "그러나 한번 그 길을 다 거치고 나면, 우리는 다시 그 둘레를 따라 돌아, 영원토록 갓난아이가 되고, 소년이 되고, 어른이 되고, '만약'이 된다."], ["Where lies the final harbor, whence we unmoor no more?", "우리가 다시는 닻을 풀지 않을 그 마지막 항구는 어디에 있는가?"], ["In what rapt ether sails the world, of which the weariest will never weary?", "가장 지친 자조차 결코 지칠 줄 모르는 그 세계는, 어떤 황홀한 천공을 항해하고 있는가?"], ["Where is the foundling’s father hidden?", "버려진 아이의 아버지는 어디에 숨어 있는가?"], ["Our souls are like those orphans whose unwedded mothers die in bearing them: the secret of our paternity lies in their grave, and we must there to learn it.", "우리의 영혼은 혼인하지 못한 어미가 그 아이를 낳다가 죽어 버린 저 고아들과 같으니, 우리 혈통의 비밀은 그 무덤 속에 누워 있고, 우리는 그것을 알기 위해 그곳으로 가야만 한다."]], [["And that same day, too, gazing far down from his boat’s side into that same golden sea, Starbuck lowly murmured:—", "그날도 또한, 스타벅(Starbuck)은 보트 뱃전 너머로 그 똑같은 황금빛 바다를 멀리 굽어보며 나직이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Loveliness unfathomable, as ever lover saw in his young bride’s eye!—Tell me not of thy teeth-tiered sharks, and thy kidnapping cannibal ways.", "\"헤아릴 수 없는 사랑스러움이여, 일찍이 어떤 연인이 젊은 신부의 눈동자에서 본 것에 못지않구나! 이빨을 층층이 박은 너의 상어들이며, 사람을 채 가는 식인의 행태일랑 내게 말하지 말아라."], ["Let faith oust fact; let fancy oust memory; I look deep down and do believe.”", "믿음이 사실을 몰아내게 하라, 공상이 기억을 몰아내게 하라. 나는 깊숙이 내려다보며, 진실로 믿노라.\""]], [["And Stubb, fish-like, with sparkling scales, leaped up in that same golden light:—", "그러자 스터브(Stubb)는 물고기처럼 반짝이는 비늘을 번뜩이며 그 똑같은 황금빛 속에서 펄쩍 뛰어올랐다."]], [["“I am Stubb, and Stubb has his history; but here Stubb takes oaths that he has always been jolly!”", "\"나는 스터브요, 스터브에게도 제 내력이 있소. 그러나 여기서 스터브는 맹세하노니, 자기는 언제나 유쾌한 사내였노라!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 115. The Pequod Meets The Bachelor.", "# CHAPTER 115. The Pequod Meets The Bachelor."]], [["And jolly enough were the sights and the sounds that came bearing down before the wind, some few weeks after Ahab’s harpoon had been welded.", "에이해브의 작살이 벼려진 지 몇 주 지났을 무렵, 바람을 타고 밀려오는 광경과 소리는 참으로 흥겨웠다."]], [["It was a Nantucket ship, the Bachelor, which had just wedged in her last cask of oil, and bolted down her bursting hatches; and now, in glad holiday apparel, was joyously, though somewhat vain-gloriously, sailing round among the widely-separated ships on the ground, previous to pointing her prow for home.", "낸터킷(Nantucket)의 배 배철러(Bachelor)호로, 마지막 기름통을 꽉 들어차게 채워 넣고 터질 듯한 승강구를 단단히 닫아 건 참이었다. 이제 흥겨운 축제 차림을 한 채, 다소 우쭐대는 기색이 없지 않았으나 기쁨에 겨워, 고향으로 뱃머리를 돌리기에 앞서 어장 곳곳에 널리 흩어진 배들 사이를 누비며 항해하고 있었다."]], [["The three men at her mast-head wore long streamers of narrow red bunting at their hats; from the stern, a whale-boat was suspended, bottom down; and hanging captive from the bowsprit was seen the long lower jaw of the last whale they had slain.", "돛대 꼭대기의 세 사람은 모자에 좁고 붉은 깃발 천을 긴 띠처럼 늘어뜨리고 있었다. 고물에는 포경 보트 한 척이 바닥을 아래로 한 채 매달려 있었고, 제1사장(斜檣)에는 마지막으로 잡은 고래의 긴 아래턱이 포로처럼 걸려 있었다."], ["Signals, ensigns, and jacks of all colours were flying from her rigging, on every side.", "사방으로 온갖 빛깔의 신호기와 깃발과 잭기가 삭구마다 펄럭였다."], ["Sideways lashed in each of her three basketed tops were two barrels of sperm; above which, in her top-mast cross-trees, you saw slender breakers of the same precious fluid; and nailed to her main truck was a brazen lamp.", "바구니 모양으로 짜인 세 개의 돛대 꼭대기 망대 옆구리마다 향유고래(Sperm) 기름통 두 개가 옆으로 묶여 있었고, 그 위 최상부 돛대의 가로대에는 같은 귀한 기름이 담긴 가느다란 작은 통들이 보였으며, 큰 돛대의 우듬지에는 놋쇠 등잔이 못으로 박혀 있었다."]], [["As was afterwards learned, the Bachelor had met with the most surprising success; all the more wonderful, for that while cruising in the same seas numerous other vessels had gone entire months without securing a single fish.", "나중에 알게 된 일이지만, 배철러호는 더없이 놀라운 성공을 거두었다. 같은 바다를 항해하면서도 다른 수많은 배들이 단 한 마리도 잡지 못한 채 몇 달을 통째로 흘려보낸 터라, 그 성공은 한층 더 경이로웠다."], ["Not only had barrels of beef and bread been given away to make room for the far more valuable sperm, but additional supplemental casks had been bartered for, from the ships she had met; and these were stowed along the deck, and in the captain’s and officers’ state-rooms.", "훨씬 값진 향유고래 기름을 둘 자리를 마련하느라 소금에 절인 쇠고기와 빵이 든 통들을 내주었을 뿐 아니라, 마주친 배들로부터 빈 통을 더 사들여 보충했으며, 그것들을 갑판을 따라, 또 선장과 항해사들의 선실에 차곡차곡 쟁여 두었다."], ["Even the cabin table itself had been knocked into kindling-wood; and the cabin mess dined off the broad head of an oil-butt, lashed down to the floor for a centrepiece.", "심지어 선실 식탁마저 쪼개어 불쏘시개로 만들었고, 선실에서 식사하는 이들은 바닥에 단단히 묶어 식탁 한가운데로 삼은 기름통의 넓은 뚜껑 위에서 끼니를 들었다. 선원들은 앞갑판에서 자기네 궤짝의 틈새를 메우고 역청을 발라 거기에까지 기름을 채워 넣었다."], ["In the forecastle, the sailors had actually caulked and pitched their chests, and filled them; it was humorously added, that the cook had clapped a head on his largest boiler, and filled it; that the steward had plugged his spare coffee-pot and filled it; that the harpooneers had headed the sockets of their irons and filled them; that indeed everything was filled with sperm, except the captain’s pantaloons pockets, and those he reserved to thrust his hands into, in self-complacent testimony of his entire satisfaction.", "우스갯소리로 덧붙이자면, 요리사는 가장 큰 솥에 뚜껑을 씌워 그것을 채웠고, 사환은 여분의 커피포트 주둥이를 막아 그것을 채웠으며, 작살잡이들은 작살의 자루 구멍에 마개를 끼워 그것을 채웠다고 했다. 사실 선장의 바지 주머니만 빼고는 무엇이든 향유고래 기름으로 가득 찼는데, 선장은 그 주머니만큼은 두 손을 찔러 넣을 자리로 남겨 두어, 자기만족에 찬 그 손길로 완전한 만족의 증거를 삼고 있었다."]], [["As this glad ship of good luck bore down upon the moody Pequod, the barbarian sound of enormous drums came from her forecastle; and drawing still nearer, a crowd of her men were seen standing round her huge try-pots, which, covered with the parchment-like poke or stomach skin of the black fish, gave forth a loud roar to every stroke of the clenched hands of the crew.", "이 행운에 겨운 흥겨운 배가 침울한 피쿼드(Pequod)호를 향해 다가올 때, 그 앞갑판에서 거대한 북을 두드리는 야만스러운 소리가 울려왔다. 더 가까이 다가오니, 한 무리의 선원들이 거대한 정유 솥 둘레에 모여 선 모습이 보였다."], ["On the quarter-deck, the mates and harpooneers were dancing with the olive-hued girls who had eloped with them from the Polynesian Isles; while suspended in an ornamented boat, firmly secured aloft between the foremast and mainmast, three Long Island negroes, with glittering fiddle-bows of whale ivory, were presiding over the hilarious jig.", "검은 고래의 양피지 같은 위장 가죽을 씌운 그 솥은, 선원들이 움켜쥔 주먹으로 칠 때마다 우렁찬 굉음을 토해 냈다. 뒷갑판에서는 항해사들과 작살잡이들이 폴리네시아 섬들에서 함께 달아나온 올리브빛 피부의 처녀들과 춤을 추고 있었고, 앞돛대와 큰 돛대 사이 높은 곳에 단단히 묶어 둔 화려하게 꾸민 보트 위에서는 롱아일랜드 출신 흑인 세 명이 고래 상아로 만든 번쩍이는 바이올린 활을 들고 그 떠들썩한 춤판을 이끌고 있었다."], ["Meanwhile, others of the ship’s company were tumultuously busy at the masonry of the try-works, from which the huge pots had been removed.", "그러는 동안 다른 선원들은 거대한 솥을 들어낸 정유로(精油爐)의 벽돌 구조물을 허무느라 요란하게 분주했다."], ["You would have almost thought they were pulling down the cursed Bastille, such wild cries they raised, as the now useless brick and mortar were being hurled into the sea.", "이제는 쓸모없어진 벽돌과 회반죽을 바다로 내던지며 그들이 어찌나 거친 함성을 질러 대던지, 마치 저주받은 바스티유 감옥이라도 허무는 줄 알았을 것이다."]], [["Lord and master over all this scene, the captain stood erect on the ship’s elevated quarter-deck, so that the whole rejoicing drama was full before him, and seemed merely contrived for his own individual diversion.", "이 모든 광경을 다스리는 주인이자 지배자로서, 선장은 배의 높다란 뒷갑판 위에 꼿꼿이 서 있었다. 그리하여 이 환희에 찬 한 편의 극이 그의 눈앞에 고스란히 펼쳐졌고, 마치 오로지 그 한 사람의 흥을 돋우려 마련된 듯 보였다."]], [["And Ahab, he too was standing on his quarter-deck, shaggy and black, with a stubborn gloom; and as the two ships crossed each other’s wakes—one all jubilations for things passed, the other all forebodings as to things to come—their two captains in themselves impersonated the whole striking contrast of the scene.", "그리고 에이해브(Ahab), 그 또한 자신의 뒷갑판에 서 있었다. 텁수룩하고 검은 모습으로, 완강한 음울함을 두른 채. 두 배가 서로의 항적을 가로질러 지나갈 때 — 한쪽은 지나간 일들로 온통 환희에 차 있었고, 다른 한쪽은 닥쳐올 일들로 온통 불길한 예감에 잠겨 있었으니 — 두 선장은 그 자신들로써 이 장면의 선명한 대비를 통째로 구현하고 있었다."]], [["“Come aboard, come aboard!”", "\"올라오시오, 어서 올라오시오!\""], ["cried the gay Bachelor’s commander, lifting a glass and a bottle in the air.", "흥에 겨운 배철러호의 선장이 잔과 병을 허공에 치켜들며 외쳤다."]], [["“Hast seen the White Whale?”", "\"흰 고래(White Whale)를 보았소?\""], ["gritted Ahab in reply.", "에이해브가 이를 갈며 대꾸했다."]], [["“No; only heard of him; but don’t believe in him at all,”", "\"아니오, 소문만 들었을 뿐이오. 게다가 그런 게 있다고는 도통 믿지도 않소.\""], ["said the other good-humoredly.", "상대가 쾌활하게 말했다."], ["“Come aboard!”", "\"올라오시오!\""]], [["“Thou art too damned jolly.", "\"당신은 지나치게 흥겹구려."], ["Sail on.", "가던 길이나 가시오."], ["Hast lost any men?”", "사람을 잃은 적이 있소?\""]], [["“Not enough to speak of—two islanders, that’s all;—but come aboard, old hearty, come along.", "\"입에 올릴 만큼은 아니오 — 섬사람 둘, 그게 다요. 하지만 어서 올라오시오, 이 정정한 양반, 어서 와요."], ["I’ll soon take that black from your brow.", "그 이마의 검은 그늘쯤 내 금세 걷어 드리리다."], ["Come along, will ye (merry’s the play); a full ship and homeward-bound.”", "자, 어서 오시구려 (한판 신나게 노는 게 좋잖소). 만선에다 고향길에 오른 배라오.\""]], [["“How wondrous familiar is a fool!”", "\"어리석은 자란 어찌 이리도 친근한가!\" 에이해브가 중얼거리더니, 이윽고 소리 높여 말했다."], ["muttered Ahab; then aloud, “Thou art a full ship and homeward bound, thou sayst; well, then, call me an empty ship, and outward-bound.", "\"당신은 만선에다 고향길에 오른 배라고 하는구려. 그렇다면 나를 일러 텅 빈 배에 바깥 바다로 나아가는 배라 부르시오."], ["So go thy ways, and I will mine. Forward there!", "그러니 당신은 당신 길을 가시오, 나는 내 길을 갈 테니."], ["Set all sail, and keep her to the wind!”", "앞쪽, 거기! 돛을 모두 펴고, 바람을 거슬러 배를 몰아라!\""]], [["And thus, while the one ship went cheerily before the breeze, the other stubbornly fought against it; and so the two vessels parted; the crew of the Pequod looking with grave, lingering glances towards the receding Bachelor; but the Bachelor’s men never heeding their gaze for the lively revelry they were in. And as Ahab, leaning over the taffrail, eyed the homeward-bound craft, he took from his pocket a small vial of sand, and then looking from the ship to the vial, seemed thereby bringing two remote associations together, for that vial was filled with Nantucket soundings.", "그리하여 한 배가 산들바람을 받으며 흥겹게 앞으로 나아가는 동안, 다른 한 배는 완강하게 바람에 맞서 싸웠다. 그렇게 두 배는 갈라섰다. 피쿼드호의 선원들은 멀어져 가는 배철러호를 향해 무겁고도 좀처럼 거두지 못하는 눈길을 보냈으나, 배철러호의 선원들은 자기네 흥겨운 잔치판에 빠져 그 눈길에는 아랑곳도 하지 않았다. 에이해브는 고물 난간에 몸을 기댄 채 고향길에 오른 배를 바라보다가, 주머니에서 모래가 담긴 작은 유리병을 꺼냈다. 그러고는 배에서 유리병으로 번갈아 시선을 옮기니, 그로써 멀리 떨어진 두 가지 상념을 하나로 끌어모으는 듯했다. 그 유리병은 낸터킷 앞바다의 모래로 채워져 있었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 116. The Dying Whale.", "# CHAPTER 116. The Dying Whale."]], [["Not seldom in this life, when, on the right side, fortune’s favourites sail close by us, we, though all adroop before, catch somewhat of the rushing breeze, and joyfully feel our bagging sails fill out.", "이 세상을 살다 보면, 운명의 총아들이 우리 오른편 가까이를 스쳐 지나갈 때, 비록 그 전까지는 모든 것이 축 늘어져 있었더라도, 그 밀려드는 바람의 한 자락을 우리도 붙잡아, 처져 있던 돛이 부풀어 오르는 것을 기쁨에 차 느끼는 일이 적지 않다."], ["So seemed it with the Pequod.", "피쿼드(Pequod)호도 그러한 듯했다."], ["For next day after encountering the gay Bachelor, whales were seen and four were slain; and one of them by Ahab.", "명랑한 배철러(Bachelor)호와 마주친 이튿날, 고래들이 나타나 네 마리가 잡혔고, 그중 한 마리는 에이해브(Ahab)가 잡았던 것이다."]], [["It was far down the afternoon; and when all the spearings of the crimson fight were done: and floating in the lovely sunset sea and sky, sun and whale both stilly died together; then, such a sweetness and such plaintiveness, such inwreathing orisons curled up in that rosy air, that it almost seemed as if far over from the deep green convent valleys of the Manilla isles, the Spanish land-breeze, wantonly turned sailor, had gone to sea, freighted with these vesper hymns.", "오후도 한참 기운 무렵이었다. 진홍빛 싸움의 작살질이 모두 끝나고, 사랑스러운 저녁놀이 진 바다와 하늘 위로 떠다니며, 해와 고래가 함께 고요히 숨을 거두었을 때, 그토록 달콤하고 그토록 애잔한 정취, 그토록 화환처럼 휘감기는 저녁 기도 소리가 그 장밋빛 대기 속에 피어올라, 마치 저 멀리 마닐라 군도(Manilla isles)의 짙푸른 수도원 골짜기로부터, 멋대로 뱃사람이 되어 버린 스페인의 육지 바람이 이 만과(晚課)의 찬송가들을 싣고 바다로 나선 듯이 보일 정도였다."]], [["Soothed again, but only soothed to deeper gloom, Ahab, who had sterned off from the whale, sat intently watching his final wanings from the now tranquil boat.", "다시 마음이 누그러졌으나, 그 누그러짐은 더 깊은 우수(憂愁)로 이어질 뿐이었다. 고래에게서 고물을 돌려 물러나 있던 에이해브는, 이제 잔잔해진 보트 위에서 고래의 마지막 스러짐을 골똘히 지켜보며 앉아 있었다."], ["For that strange spectacle observable in all sperm whales dying—the turning sunwards of the head, and so expiring—that strange spectacle, beheld of such a placid evening, somehow to Ahab conveyed a wondrousness unknown before.", "죽어 가는 모든 향유고래(Sperm Whale)에게서 볼 수 있는 그 기묘한 광경—머리를 해 쪽으로 돌린 채 그렇게 숨을 거두는 그 기묘한 광경이, 이토록 잔잔한 저녁에 목격되자, 어쩐 일인지 에이해브에게는 일찍이 알지 못하던 경이로움을 전해 주었다."]], [["“He turns and turns him to it,—how slowly, but how steadfastly, his homage-rendering and invoking brow, with his last dying motions.", "\"저놈은 해를 향해 몸을 돌리고 또 돌리는구나—얼마나 느리게, 그러나 얼마나 한결같이, 경배를 바치고 기원을 올리는 저 이마를, 죽어 가는 마지막 몸짓으로."], ["He too worships fire; most faithful, broad, baronial vassal of the sun!—Oh that these too-favouring eyes should see these too-favouring sights.", "저놈 또한 불을 숭배하는구나. 더없이 충실하고, 드넓고, 영주(領主)에 버금가는 태양의 가신(家臣)이여!—오, 너무도 호의로운 이 눈이 너무도 호의로운 이 광경을 보게 되다니."], ["Look!", "보라!"], ["here, far water-locked; beyond all hum of human weal or woe; in these most candid and impartial seas; where to traditions no rocks furnish tablets; where for long Chinese ages, the billows have still rolled on speechless and unspoken to, as stars that shine upon the Niger’s unknown source; here, too, life dies sunwards full of faith; but see!", "여기, 저 멀리 물에 갇힌 채, 인간의 안녕이나 비탄의 모든 소음에서 벗어나, 이 더없이 솔직하고 공평무사한 바다에서, 어떤 바위도 전설을 새길 석판을 내주지 않는 곳에서, 기나긴 중국의 세월 동안에도 물결이 여전히 말없이, 누구의 말도 듣지 못한 채 굽이쳐 왔던 곳에서, 마치 나이저(Niger)강의 알려지지 않은 발원지 위에 빛나는 별들처럼, 여기서도, 생명은 믿음으로 가득 차 해를 향해 죽어 가는구나. 그러나 보라!"], ["no sooner dead, than death whirls round the corpse, and it heads some other way.", "죽자마자 곧, 죽음이 그 주검을 빙그르르 돌려놓아, 그것은 다른 어딘가로 머리를 돌리는구나."]], [["“Oh, thou dark Hindoo half of nature, who of drowned bones hast builded thy separate throne somewhere in the heart of these unverdured seas; thou art an infidel, thou queen, and too truly speakest to me in the wide-slaughtering Typhoon, and the hushed burial of its after calm.", "\"오, 자연의 저 어두운 힌두(Hindoo)의 반쪽이여, 물에 빠진 뼈들로 이 푸름 없는 바다의 한복판 어딘가에 너만의 옥좌를 세운 자여. 너는 이교도다, 여왕이여, 그리고 너는 드넓게 살육하는 태풍(Typhoon) 속에서, 또 그 뒤에 찾아드는 적막한 매장과도 같은 고요 속에서 내게 너무도 참되게 말하는구나."], ["Nor has this thy whale sunwards turned his dying head, and then gone round again, without a lesson to me.", "또한 너의 이 고래가 죽어 가는 머리를 해 쪽으로 돌렸다가 다시 빙 돌아 버린 것도, 내게 한 가지 교훈을 남기지 않고서는 그냥 지나가지 않았다."]], [["“Oh, trebly hooped and welded hip of power!", "\"오, 세 겹으로 둘러 매어진 권능의 허리여!"], ["Oh, high aspiring, rainbowed jet!—that one strivest, this one jettest all in vain!", "오, 드높이 솟구치는 무지갯빛 물줄기여!—저자는 안간힘을 다해 애쓰고, 이자는 물을 뿜어 올리나, 모두가 헛되도다!"], ["In vain, oh whale, dost thou seek intercedings with yon all-quickening sun, that only calls forth life, but gives it not again.", "헛되도다, 오 고래여, 너는 저 모든 것을 살아나게 하는 태양과의 중재를 구하지만, 그것은 생명을 불러낼 뿐 다시 돌려주지는 않는구나."], ["Yet dost thou, darker half, rock me with a prouder, if a darker faith.", "그러나 너는, 더 어두운 반쪽이여, 비록 더 어두운 믿음일지언정 더 자랑스러운 믿음으로 나를 흔들어 어른다."], ["All thy unnamable imminglings float beneath me here; I am buoyed by breaths of once living things, exhaled as air, but water now.", "이름 붙일 수 없는 너의 온갖 뒤섞임이 여기 내 발밑에 떠 있구나. 나는 한때 살아 있던 것들의 숨결에 떠받쳐지고 있으니, 공기로 내뿜어졌으나 이제는 물이 된 그 숨결에."]], [["“Then hail, for ever hail, O sea, in whose eternal tossings the wild fowl finds his only rest.", "\"그러니 만세를, 영원토록 만세를 부르노라, 오 바다여, 그 끝없는 일렁임 속에서 들새가 유일한 안식을 찾는 그대여."], ["Born of earth, yet suckled by the sea; though hill and valley mothered me, ye billows are my foster-brothers!”", "대지에서 태어났으되 바다의 젖을 먹고 자랐으니, 비록 언덕과 골짜기가 나를 낳았을지언정, 너희 물결이야말로 나의 젖형제로구나!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 117. The Whale Watch.", "# CHAPTER 117. The Whale Watch."]], [["The four whales slain that evening had died wide apart; one, far to windward; one, less distant, to leeward; one ahead; one astern.", "그날 저녁에 잡은 네 마리의 고래는 서로 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 죽어 있었다. 한 마리는 바람 불어오는 쪽으로 멀리, 한 마리는 그보다 가까운 바람 불어가는 쪽에, 한 마리는 앞쪽에, 한 마리는 뒤쪽에 있었다."], ["These last three were brought alongside ere nightfall; but the windward one could not be reached till morning; and the boat that had killed it lay by its side all night; and that boat was Ahab’s.", "이 뒤의 세 마리는 해가 지기 전에 배 옆으로 끌어왔으나, 바람 불어오는 쪽의 한 마리는 아침이 되어서야 닿을 수 있었다. 그래서 그것을 잡은 보트는 밤새도록 그 곁에 머물러 있었으니, 그 보트가 바로 에이해브의 것이었다."]], [["The waif-pole was thrust upright into the dead whale’s spout-hole; and the lantern hanging from its top, cast a troubled flickering glare upon the black, glossy back, and far out upon the midnight waves, which gently chafed the whale’s broad flank, like soft surf upon a beach.", "표지 막대가 죽은 고래의 분수 구멍에 곧추세워졌고, 그 꼭대기에 매달린 등불이 검고 윤기 흐르는 등판 위로, 그리고 저 멀리 한밤의 물결 위로 어수선하게 일렁이는 불빛을 던졌다. 그 물결은 해변에 부드럽게 부서지는 파도처럼 고래의 넓은 옆구리를 가만히 어루만지고 있었다."]], [["Ahab and all his boat’s crew seemed asleep but the Parsee; who crouching in the bow, sat watching the sharks, that spectrally played round the whale, and tapped the light cedar planks with their tails.", "에이해브와 그 보트의 선원들은 모두 잠든 듯했으나, 오직 파시인만은 깨어 있었다. 그는 뱃머리에 웅크리고 앉아, 고래 주위를 유령처럼 맴돌며 꼬리로 가벼운 삼나무 판자를 톡톡 두드리는 상어들을 지켜보았다."], ["A sound like the moaning in squadrons over Asphaltites of unforgiven ghosts of Gomorrah, ran shuddering through the air.", "용서받지 못한 고모라의 망령들이 아스팔티테스 호수 위로 떼 지어 신음하는 듯한 소리가, 몸서리치며 허공을 가로질러 흘렀다."]], [["Started from his slumbers, Ahab, face to face, saw the Parsee; and hooped round by the gloom of the night they seemed the last men in a flooded world.", "선잠에서 깨어난 에이해브는 얼굴을 마주하고 파시인을 보았다. 밤의 어둠에 둥글게 에워싸인 그 둘은, 물에 잠긴 세상에 남은 마지막 두 사람처럼 보였다."], ["“I have dreamed it again,”", "\"또 그 꿈을 꾸었네.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Of the hearses?", "\"영구차 꿈 말입니까?"], ["Have I not said, old man, that neither hearse nor coffin can be thine?”", "영감, 영구차도 관도 당신 것이 될 수 없다고 내 말하지 않았소?\""]], [["“And who are hearsed that die on the sea?”", "\"그러면 바다에서 죽는 자들은 누가 영구차에 실린단 말인가?\""]], [["“But I said, old man, that ere thou couldst die on this voyage, two hearses must verily be seen by thee on the sea; the first not made by mortal hands; and the visible wood of the last one must be grown in America.”", "\"하지만 영감, 내가 말하지 않았소. 당신이 이 항해에서 죽으려면, 그 전에 반드시 바다 위에서 두 대의 영구차를 보아야 한다고. 그 첫 번째는 인간의 손으로 만든 것이 아니며, 마지막 것의 눈에 보이는 나무는 아메리카에서 자란 것이어야 하오.\""]], [["“Aye, aye!", "\"그렇군, 그래!"], ["a strange sight that, Parsee:—a hearse and its plumes floating over the ocean with the waves for the pall-bearers.", "그건 참으로 기이한 광경이겠구먼, 파시인. 영구차와 그 깃털 장식이 대양 위를 떠다니고, 물결이 관을 메는 사람 노릇을 한다니."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Such a sight we shall not soon see.”", "그런 광경은 우리가 쉬이 보지 못할 것이네.\""]], [["“Believe it or not, thou canst not die till it be seen, old man.”", "\"믿든 안 믿든, 그것을 보기 전에는 당신은 죽을 수 없소, 영감.\""]], [["“And what was that saying about thyself?”", "\"그러면 자네 자신에 관한 그 말은 무엇이었나?\""]], [["“Though it come to the last, I shall still go before thee thy pilot.”", "\"마지막 순간이 온다 해도, 나는 여전히 당신보다 앞서 당신의 길잡이로 가게 될 것이오.\""]], [["“And when thou art so gone before—if that ever befall—then ere I can follow, thou must still appear to me, to pilot me still?—Was it not so?", "\"그러면 자네가 그렇게 먼저 가 버린 뒤에—만약 그런 일이 정말 일어난다면—내가 뒤따르기 전에, 자네는 여전히 내 앞에 나타나 나를 계속 인도해 주어야 한다는 말이지?—그렇지 않은가?"], ["Well, then, did I believe all ye say, oh my pilot!", "자, 그렇다면, 오 나의 길잡이여, 자네가 하는 말을 내가 모두 믿는다 해도!"], ["I have here two pledges that I shall yet slay Moby Dick and survive it.”", "나는 여기 두 가지 약속을 받았으니, 나는 끝내 모비 딕을 죽이고도 살아남으리라는 것일세.\""]], [["“Take another pledge, old man,”", "\"한 가지 약속을 더 받으시오, 영감.\" 파시인이 말했다."], ["said the Parsee, as his eyes lighted up like fire-flies in the gloom—“Hemp only can kill thee.”", "그의 두 눈은 어둠 속에서 반딧불처럼 빛났다. \"오직 삼(麻)만이 당신을 죽일 수 있소.\""]], [["“The gallows, ye mean.—I am immortal then, on land and on sea,”", "\"교수대를 두고 하는 말이로군.—그렇다면 나는 뭍에서나 바다에서나 죽지 않는 몸이라는 말 아닌가.\""], ["cried Ahab, with a laugh of derision;—“Immortal on land and on sea!”", "에이해브는 비웃는 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"뭍에서나 바다에서나 불멸이라!\""]], [["Both were silent again, as one man.", "두 사람은 한 사람처럼 다시 침묵에 빠졌다."], ["The grey dawn came on, and the slumbering crew arose from the boat’s bottom, and ere noon the dead whale was brought to the ship.", "잿빛 새벽이 밝아 오자, 잠들어 있던 선원들이 보트 바닥에서 일어났고, 정오가 되기 전에 죽은 고래는 배 옆으로 끌려왔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 118. The Quadrant.", "# 제106장 에이해브의 다리"]], [["The season for the Line at length drew near; and every day when Ahab, coming from his cabin, cast his eyes aloft, the vigilant helmsman would ostentatiously handle his spokes, and the eager mariners quickly run to the braces, and would stand there with all their eyes centrally fixed on the nailed doubloon; impatient for the order to point the ship’s prow for the equator.", "적도 해역의 철이 마침내 가까워졌다. 그리고 에이해브가 선실에서 나와 눈을 위로 치켜뜰 때마다, 빈틈없는 키잡이는 보란 듯이 키 손잡이를 매만졌고, 잔뜩 들뜬 선원들은 재빨리 아딧줄로 달려가 못 박힌 더블룬 금화에 모든 눈길을 한가운데로 고정시킨 채 그 자리에 서서, 뱃머리를 적도로 향하라는 명령이 떨어지기만을 애타게 기다렸다."], ["In good time the order came.", "때맞춰 명령이 내려졌다."], ["It was hard upon high noon; and Ahab, seated in the bows of his high-hoisted boat, was about taking his wonted daily observation of the sun to determine his latitude.", "거의 정오에 가까운 무렵이었다. 에이해브는 높이 끌어올린 보트의 이물에 앉아, 위도를 가늠하려고 늘 하던 대로 태양 관측을 막 시작하려던 참이었다."]], [["Now, in that Japanese sea, the days in summer are as freshets of effulgences.", "그런데 그 일본 해역에서는 여름날들이 마치 눈부신 광채가 봇물처럼 터지는 것 같았다."], ["That unblinkingly vivid Japanese sun seems the blazing focus of the glassy ocean’s immeasurable burning-glass.", "한 점 깜빡임도 없이 선명한 그 일본의 태양은 유리알 같은 대양을 헤아릴 수 없는 화경(火鏡)으로 삼아 그 한가운데로 모든 불길을 모은 듯했다."], ["The sky looks lacquered; clouds there are none; the horizon floats; and this nakedness of unrelieved radiance is as the insufferable splendors of God’s throne.", "하늘은 옻칠을 한 듯하고, 구름이라곤 한 점도 없으며, 수평선은 둥실 떠 있는 듯했다. 그리고 무엇 하나 가리는 것 없는 이 적나라한 광휘는 마치 견딜 수 없는 하느님 옥좌의 찬란함과도 같았다."], ["Well that Ahab’s quadrant was furnished with coloured glasses, through which to take sight of that solar fire.", "다행히 에이해브의 사분의(四分儀)에는 그 태양의 불꽃을 들여다볼 수 있도록 색유리가 끼워져 있었다."], ["So, swinging his seated form to the roll of the ship, and with his astrological-looking instrument placed to his eye, he remained in that posture for some moments to catch the precise instant when the sun should gain its precise meridian.", "그리하여 그는 배가 흔들리는 대로 앉은 몸을 함께 흔들면서, 점성술 도구처럼 보이는 그 기구를 눈에 갖다 댄 채, 태양이 정확히 자오선에 다다르는 바로 그 순간을 포착하려고 한동안 그 자세를 유지했다."], ["Meantime while his whole attention was absorbed, the Parsee was kneeling beneath him on the ship’s deck, and with face thrown up like Ahab’s, was eyeing the same sun with him; only the lids of his eyes half hooded their orbs, and his wild face was subdued to an earthly passionlessness.", "한편 그가 온 신경을 거기에 쏟고 있는 동안, 파시(Parsee) 교도는 그 아래 갑판에 무릎을 꿇고서 에이해브와 똑같이 얼굴을 위로 젖힌 채 그와 더불어 같은 태양을 바라보고 있었다. 다만 그의 눈은 눈꺼풀이 안구를 반쯤 덮고 있었고, 그 사나운 얼굴은 이 세상 것이 아닌 듯한 무념무상의 평정으로 가라앉아 있었다."], ["At length the desired observation was taken; and with his pencil upon his ivory leg, Ahab soon calculated what his latitude must be at that precise instant.", "마침내 원하던 관측이 끝났다. 에이해브는 상아 다리에 연필을 대고서, 그 정확한 순간의 자기 위도가 얼마인지 곧 계산해 냈다."], ["Then falling into a moment’s revery, he again looked up towards the sun and murmured to himself: “Thou sea-mark!", "그러고는 잠시 상념에 잠겼다가, 다시 태양을 올려다보며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"그대 바다의 길잡이여!"], ["thou high and mighty Pilot!", "그대 높고도 권능 있는 도선사여!"], ["thou tellest me truly where I am—but canst thou cast the least hint where I shall be?", "그대는 내가 지금 어디에 있는지는 참되이 일러 주지만 — 내가 장차 어디에 있게 될지는 그 작은 실마리 하나라도 던져 줄 수 있는가?"], ["Or canst thou tell where some other thing besides me is this moment living?", "아니면 나 말고 다른 무엇이 지금 이 순간 어디에 살아 있는지 말해 줄 수 있는가? 모비 딕(Moby Dick)은 어디 있는가?"], ["Where is Moby Dick? This instant thou must be eyeing him.", "지금 이 순간 그대는 틀림없이 그놈을 바라보고 있으리라."], ["These eyes of mine look into the very eye that is even now beholding him; aye, and into the eye that is even now equally beholding the objects on the unknown, thither side of thee, thou sun!”", "내 이 두 눈은 지금 바로 그놈을 굽어보고 있는 그 눈을 들여다보고 있다. 그렇다, 그리고 지금 이 순간 그대 너머 알 수 없는 저편의 사물들을 똑같이 굽어보고 있는 그 눈을 들여다보고 있노라, 오 태양이여!\""]], [["Then gazing at his quadrant, and handling, one after the other, its numerous cabalistical contrivances, he pondered again, and muttered: “Foolish toy!", "그러고 나서 사분의를 응시하며, 그 수많은 신비로운 장치들을 하나하나 매만지다가, 그는 다시금 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 이렇게 중얼거렸다. \"어리석은 장난감!"], ["babies’ plaything of haughty Admirals, and Commodores, and Captains; the world brags of thee, of thy cunning and might; but what after all canst thou do, but tell the poor, pitiful point, where thou thyself happenest to be on this wide planet, and the hand that holds thee: no!", "거만한 제독들과 사령관들과 함장들의 갓난애 노리개로다. 세상은 그대를, 그대의 교묘함과 위력을 자랑한다."], ["not one jot more!", "하지만 결국 그대가 할 수 있는 일이라곤, 이 넓은 행성 위에서 그대 자신이 우연히 놓여 있는 위치와 그대를 쥔 손의 위치, 그 가련하고 보잘것없는 지점을 일러 주는 것뿐 아니던가. 아니다!"], ["Thou canst not tell where one drop of water or one grain of sand will be to-morrow noon; and yet with thy impotence thou insultest the sun!", "그 이상은 털끝만큼도 못한다! 그대는 물 한 방울이나 모래 한 알이 내일 정오에 어디에 있을지조차 말하지 못한다."], ["Science!", "그러면서도 그 무력함으로 태양을 모욕하는구나! 과학이여!"], ["Curse thee, thou vain toy; and cursed be all the things that cast man’s eyes aloft to that heaven, whose live vividness but scorches him, as these old eyes are even now scorched with thy light, O sun!", "그대를 저주하노라, 이 헛된 장난감아. 그리고 사람의 눈을 저 하늘로 치켜뜨게 만드는 모든 것에 저주가 있으라."], ["Level by nature to this earth’s horizon are the glances of man’s eyes; not shot from the crown of his head, as if God had meant him to gaze on his firmament.", "저 하늘의 생생한 광채는 다만 사람을 그슬릴 뿐이니, 지금 이 늙은 두 눈이 그대의 빛에 그슬리고 있는 것처럼 말이다, 오 태양이여! 본디 사람의 눈길은 이 땅의 수평선과 나란하도록 만들어진 것이지, 하느님이 사람더러 당신의 창공을 우러르라 하신 양 정수리에서 쏘아 올리도록 만들어진 것이 아니다."], ["Curse thee, thou quadrant!”", "그대를 저주하노라, 이 사분의야!\" 그는 그것을 갑판에 내동댕이쳤다."], ["dashing it to the deck, “no longer will I guide my earthly way by thee; the level ship’s compass, and the level dead-reckoning, by log and by line; these shall conduct me, and show me my place on the sea.", "\"더는 그대에게 내 지상의 길을 맡기지 않으리라. 평평한 배의 나침반과, 측정기와 측정줄로 헤아리는 평평한 추측 항법, 바로 이것들이 나를 인도하여 바다 위 내 자리를 일러 주리라."], ["Aye,”", "그렇다.\" 그는 보트에서 갑판으로 뛰어내렸다."], ["lighting from the boat to the deck, “thus I trample on thee, thou paltry thing that feebly pointest on high; thus I split and destroy thee!”", "\"이렇게 나는 그대를 짓밟노라, 저 높은 곳이나 가리키며 비실거리는 하찮은 물건아. 이렇게 나는 그대를 쪼개어 부숴 버리노라!\""]], [["As the frantic old man thus spoke and thus trampled with his live and dead feet, a sneering triumph that seemed meant for Ahab, and a fatalistic despair that seemed meant for himself—these passed over the mute, motionless Parsee’s face.", "광란에 빠진 늙은이가 이렇게 말하며 산 발과 죽은 발로 그것을 짓밟는 동안, 에이해브를 향한 듯한 비웃는 승리의 빛과, 자기 자신을 향한 듯한 숙명적 절망의 빛이 — 이 두 가지가 말없이 미동도 없는 파시 교도의 얼굴 위를 스쳐 지나갔다. 아무도 알아채지 못하는 사이에 그는 일어나 슬며시 빠져나갔다."], ["Unobserved he rose and glided away; while, awestruck by the aspect of their commander, the seamen clustered together on the forecastle, till Ahab, troubledly pacing the deck, shouted out—“To the braces!", "한편 자기네 지휘관의 모습에 경외감을 느낀 선원들은 앞갑판에 한데 모여 옹기종기 서 있었고, 그러다 에이해브가 어수선하게 갑판을 거닐며 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"아딧줄로!"], ["Up helm!—square in!”", "키를 올려라! 활대를 가로로 돌려라!\""]], [["In an instant the yards swung round; and as the ship half-wheeled upon her heel, her three firm-seated graceful masts erectly poised upon her long, ribbed hull, seemed as the three Horatii pirouetting on one sufficient steed.", "순식간에 활대들이 빙 돌아갔다. 그리고 배가 발꿈치를 축으로 반쯤 돌아설 때, 길고 갈빗대가 도드라진 선체 위로 든든히 자리 잡고 우아하게 곧추선 세 개의 돛대는 마치 한 마리의 충분한 말 위에서 발끝으로 빙그르르 도는 세 명의 호라티우스 형제처럼 보였다."]], [["Standing between the knight-heads, Starbuck watched the Pequod’s tumultuous way, and Ahab’s also, as he went lurching along the deck.", "스타벅은 나이트헤드 사이에 서서, 요동치며 나아가는 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 항로와, 갑판을 휘청휘청 걸어가는 에이해브의 모습을 함께 지켜보았다."]], [["“I have sat before the dense coal fire and watched it all aglow, full of its tormented flaming life; and I have seen it wane at last, down, down, to dumbest dust.", "\"나는 빽빽이 타오르는 석탄불 앞에 앉아, 괴로움에 몸부림치는 그 불꽃의 생명으로 가득 차 온통 시뻘겋게 달아오른 것을 바라본 적이 있다. 그리고 마침내 그것이 사위어 가는 것을, 점점, 점점 내려앉아 더없이 말 없는 재로 돌아가는 것을 보았다."], ["Old man of oceans!", "오 대양의 늙은이여!"], ["of all this fiery life of thine, what will at length remain but one little heap of ashes!”", "그대의 이 모든 불같은 생명에서 끝내 남는 것이라곤 한 줌 작은 잿더미밖에 더 있겠는가!\""]], [["“Aye,” cried Stubb, “but sea-coal ashes—mind ye that, Mr. Starbuck—sea-coal, not your common charcoal. Well, well; I heard Ahab mutter, ‘Here some one thrusts these cards into these old hands of mine; swears that I must play them, and no others.’ And damn me, Ahab, but thou actest right; live in the game, and die in it!”", "\"그렇소.\" 스터브가 외쳤다. \"허나 바다 석탄의 재라오 — 그걸 잊지 마시오, 스타벅 씨 — 바다 석탄이지, 흔해 빠진 숯이 아니란 말이오. 자, 자. 나는 에이해브가 이렇게 중얼거리는 걸 들었소. '여기 누군가가 이 늙은 내 손에 이 패를 쥐여 주는구나. 그러고는 내가 반드시 이 패로만 놀아야 하고, 다른 패는 안 된다고 맹세하게 하는구나.' 빌어먹을, 에이해브, 하지만 당신이 옳소. 노름판 속에서 살다가, 그 속에서 죽으시오!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 119. The Candles.", "# CHAPTER 119. The Candles."]], [["Warmest climes but nurse the cruellest fangs: the tiger of Bengal crouches in spiced groves of ceaseless verdure.", "가장 따뜻한 기후가 도리어 가장 잔인한 송곳니를 길러 낸다. 벵골의 호랑이는 끊임없이 푸르른 향료의 숲속에 웅크리고 있다."], ["Skies the most effulgent but basket the deadliest thunders: gorgeous Cuba knows tornadoes that never swept tame northern lands.", "가장 눈부신 하늘이 도리어 가장 치명적인 천둥을 품고 있다. 화려한 쿠바는 온순한 북쪽 땅을 결코 휩쓴 적 없는 토네이도를 안다."], ["So, too, it is, that in these resplendent Japanese seas the mariner encounters the direst of all storms, the Typhoon.", "그렇듯, 이 찬란한 일본 바다에서도 뱃사람은 모든 폭풍 가운데 가장 무서운 것, 곧 태풍(Typhoon)과 마주친다."], ["It will sometimes burst from out that cloudless sky, like an exploding bomb upon a dazed and sleepy town.", "그것은 때로 구름 한 점 없는 하늘에서 갑자기 터져 나오니, 멍하니 잠든 마을 위로 떨어지는 폭탄과도 같다."]], [["Towards evening of that day, the Pequod was torn of her canvas, and bare-poled was left to fight a Typhoon which had struck her directly ahead.", "그날 저녁 무렵, 피쿼드호(Pequod)는 돛이 갈가리 찢겨 나가, 맨 돛대만 남은 채로 정면에서 들이친 태풍과 맞서 싸워야 했다."], ["When darkness came on, sky and sea roared and split with the thunder, and blazed with the lightning, that showed the disabled masts fluttering here and there with the rags which the first fury of the tempest had left for its after sport.", "어둠이 내리자 하늘과 바다는 천둥과 더불어 으르렁대며 갈라졌고, 번개로 환히 타올랐으니, 그 빛은 처음 폭풍의 광기가 뒷놀이를 위해 남겨 둔 누더기 조각들을 이리저리 펄럭이는 무력해진 돛대들을 비추어 보여 주었다."]], [["Holding by a shroud, Starbuck was standing on the quarter-deck; at every flash of the lightning glancing aloft, to see what additional disaster might have befallen the intricate hamper there; while Stubb and Flask were directing the men in the higher hoisting and firmer lashing of the boats.", "스타벅(Starbuck)은 삭구 줄 하나를 붙든 채 뒷갑판에 서 있었다. 번개가 번쩍일 때마다 위쪽을 올려다보며, 저 뒤엉킨 돛대 장치에 또 어떤 재난이 닥쳤는지를 살폈다."], ["But all their pains seemed naught.", "그동안 스터브(Stubb)와 플래스크(Flask)는 선원들을 지휘하여 보트를 더 높이 끌어 올리고 더 단단히 잡아매도록 했다."], ["Though lifted to the very top of the cranes, the windward quarter boat (Ahab’s) did not escape.", "그러나 그들의 온갖 수고도 헛된 듯 보였다. 기중대 맨 꼭대기까지 들어 올렸음에도, 바람받이 쪽 뒷보트(에이해브(Ahab)의 것)는 화를 면치 못했다."], ["A great rolling sea, dashing high up against the reeling ship’s high teetering side, stove in the boat’s bottom at the stern, and left it again, all dripping through like a sieve.", "거대한 너울 하나가 비틀거리는 배의 높이 흔들리는 옆구리를 향해 높이 솟구쳐 부딪히더니, 그 보트의 고물 쪽 바닥을 부수어 놓고는 다시 물러갔다. 보트는 체처럼 온통 물을 줄줄 흘리고 있었다."]], [["“Bad work, bad work!", "\"고약한 일이군, 고약한 일이야! 스타벅 씨.\""], ["Mr. Starbuck,”", "스터브가 부서진 보트를 바라보며 말했다."], ["said Stubb, regarding the wreck, “but the sea will have its way.", "\"허나 바다는 제멋대로 굴기 마련이지요. 적어도 이 스터브는 그것과 싸울 수가 없습니다."], ["Stubb, for one, can’t fight it. You see, Mr.", "보세요, 스타벅 씨, 파도란 놈은 솟구치기 전에 어마어마하게 긴 도움닫기를 합니다."], ["Starbuck, a wave has such a great long start before it leaps, all round the world it runs, and then comes the spring!", "온 세상을 한 바퀴 돌고 나서야 비로소 솟구쳐 오르거든요! 하지만 저로 말하자면, 그놈을 맞으러 갈 도움닫기라곤 고작 이 갑판을 가로지르는 게 전부랍니다."], ["But as for me, all the start I have to meet it, is just across the deck here.", "하지만 괘념치 마세요. 다 장난삼아 하는 소립니다."], ["But never mind; it’s all in fun: so the old song says;”—(sings.)", "옛 노래에도 그리 나오잖습니까.\" (노래한다.)"]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["jolly is the gale, And a joker is the whale, A’ flourishin’ his tail,— Such a funny, sporty, gamy, jesty, joky, hoky-poky lad, is the Ocean, oh!", "흥겨운 폭풍, 익살꾼 같은 고래, 제 꼬리를 신나게 흔들어 대네, 그렇게나 우습고, 까불대고, 짓궂고, 익살맞고, 농지거리하고, 알쏭달쏭한 녀석, 그게 바로 대양이라네, 오!"]], [["The scud all a flyin’, That’s his flip only foamin’; When he stirs in the spicin’,— Such a funny, sporty, gamy, jesty, joky, hoky-poky lad, is the Ocean, oh!", "흩날리는 물보라, 그건 그저 거품 이는 그놈의 술일 뿐, 양념을 휘저어 넣을 때면, 그렇게나 우습고, 까불대고, 짓궂고, 익살맞고, 농지거리하고, 알쏭달쏭한 녀석, 그게 바로 대양이라네, 오!"]], [["Thunder splits the ships, But he only smacks his lips, A tastin’ of this flip,— Such a funny, sporty, gamy, jesty, joky, hoky-poky lad, is the Ocean, oh!", "천둥이 배를 쪼개도, 그놈은 그저 입맛만 다실 뿐, 이 술을 한 모금 맛보면서, 그렇게나 우습고, 까불대고, 짓궂고, 익살맞고, 농지거리하고, 알쏭달쏭한 녀석, 그게 바로 대양이라네, 오!"]], [["“Avast Stubb,” cried Starbuck, “let the Typhoon sing, and strike his harp here in our rigging; but if thou art a brave man thou wilt hold thy peace.”", "\"그만하게, 스터브.\" 스타벅이 외쳤다. \"태풍더러 노래하게 내버려 두게나. 우리 삭구 위에서 제 하프를 켜라고 말이야. 허나 자네가 용감한 사내라면, 입을 다물고 있을 걸세.\""]], [["“But I am not a brave man; never said I was a brave man; I am a coward; and I sing to keep up my spirits.", "\"하지만 저는 용감한 사내가 아닙니다. 제가 용감한 사내라고 말한 적도 없고요."], ["And I tell you what it is, Mr.", "저는 겁쟁이입니다. 그래서 기운을 차리려고 노래하는 겁니다."], ["Starbuck, there’s no way to stop my singing in this world but to cut my throat.", "그리고 말씀드리지요, 스타벅 씨, 이 세상에서 제 노래를 멈추게 할 방법은 제 목을 베는 것 말고는 없습니다."], ["And when that’s done, ten to one I sing ye the doxology for a wind-up.”", "그리고 그 일이 끝나고 나면, 십중팔구 저는 마무리로 당신께 영광송을 불러 드릴 겁니다.\""]], [["“Madman!", "\"미친 사람 같으니!"], ["look through my eyes if thou hast none of thine own.”", "네게 네 눈이 없거든, 내 눈을 통해 보아라.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["how can you see better of a dark night than anybody else, never mind how foolish?”", "어떻게 당신은 캄캄한 밤에 남보다 더 잘 본단 말입니까, 그게 아무리 어리석은 소리라 해도요?\""]], [["“Here!”", "\"여길 보게!\""], ["cried Starbuck, seizing Stubb by the shoulder, and pointing his hand towards the weather bow, “markest thou not that the gale comes from the eastward, the very course Ahab is to run for Moby Dick?", "스타벅이 스터브의 어깨를 움켜쥐고 바람받이 뱃머리 쪽으로 손을 가리키며 외쳤다. \"저 폭풍이 동쪽에서 불어오는 게 보이지 않나?"], ["the very course he swung to this day noon?", "바로 에이해브가 모비 딕(Moby Dick)을 찾아 달려가려는 그 항로 말일세. 바로 그가 오늘 정오에 뱃머리를 돌린 그 항로란 말이야."], ["now mark his boat there; where is that stove?", "자, 저기 그의 보트를 보게. 어디가 부서졌나?"], ["In the stern-sheets, man; where he is wont to stand—his stand-point is stove, man!", "고물 쪽 자리일세, 이 사람아. 그가 늘 서 있곤 하던 자리, 그가 발 딛고 서던 바로 그 자리가 부서졌단 말일세, 이 사람아!"], ["Now jump overboard, and sing away, if thou must!", "자, 이제 바다로 뛰어들어, 정 그래야겠거든 실컷 노래나 부르게!\""]], [["“I don’t half understand ye: what’s in the wind?”", "\"무슨 말씀인지 도통 알아듣지 못하겠군요. 무슨 바람이 부는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes, yes, round the Cape of Good Hope is the shortest way to Nantucket,”", "\"그래, 그래, 희망봉을 돌아가는 것이 낸터킷(Nantucket)으로 가는 가장 빠른 길이지.\""], ["soliloquized Starbuck suddenly, heedless of Stubb’s question.", "스타벅이 스터브의 물음에는 아랑곳없이 불쑥 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“The gale that now hammers at us to stave us, we can turn it into a fair wind that will drive us towards home.", "\"지금 우리를 부수려고 두들겨 대는 저 폭풍을, 우리는 우리를 집으로 데려다줄 순풍으로 바꿀 수 있어. 저쪽, 바람받이 편은 온통 파멸의 암흑이지만, 바람그늘 편, 곧 고향 쪽은…"], ["Yonder, to windward, all is blackness of doom; but to leeward, homeward—I see it lightens up there; but not with the lightning.”", "저기가 환히 밝아 오는 게 보여. 그러나 번개로 밝아 오는 것은 아니지.\""]], [["At that moment in one of the intervals of profound darkness, following the flashes, a voice was heard at his side; and almost at the same instant a volley of thunder peals rolled overhead.", "바로 그 순간, 번쩍임에 뒤따른 깊은 어둠의 막간 가운데 하나에서 그의 곁에서 어떤 목소리가 들려왔다. 그리고 거의 같은 찰나에 천둥소리 한 무더기가 머리 위에서 우르릉 굴러갔다."]], [["“Who’s there?”", "\"거기 누구냐?\""]], [["“Old Thunder!”", "\"늙은 천둥이오!\""], ["said Ahab, groping his way along the bulwarks to his pivot-hole; but suddenly finding his path made plain to him by elbowed lances of fire.", "에이해브가 뱃전을 따라 더듬더듬 제 회전축 구멍 쪽으로 길을 찾아가며 말했다. 그러나 문득 팔꿈치처럼 굽이치는 불의 창들이 그의 길을 환히 밝혀 주는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["Now, as the lightning rod to a spire on shore is intended to carry off the perilous fluid into the soil; so the kindred rod which at sea some ships carry to each mast, is intended to conduct it into the water.", "자, 뭍에 선 첨탑의 피뢰침이 그 위험한 전류를 흙 속으로 흘려보내려는 것이듯, 바다에서 어떤 배들이 돛대마다 달아 두는 그 비슷한 막대도 그것을 물속으로 끌어 내리려는 것이다."], ["But as this conductor must descend to considerable depth, that its end may avoid all contact with the hull; and as moreover, if kept constantly towing there, it would be liable to many mishaps, besides interfering not a little with some of the rigging, and more or less impeding the vessel’s way in the water; because of all this, the lower parts of a ship’s lightning-rods are not always overboard; but are generally made in long slender links, so as to be the more readily hauled up into the chains outside, or thrown down into the sea, as occasion may require.", "그러나 이 도체는 그 끝이 선체와 닿는 일이 없도록 상당한 깊이까지 내려가야 하고, 더구나 늘 거기 매달린 채 끌려다니게 되면 여러 사고를 당하기 쉬울뿐더러, 삭구 일부를 적잖이 방해하고 배가 물속을 나아가는 길을 다소간 가로막게 된다. 이런 까닭에 배의 피뢰침 아랫부분은 늘 물속에 잠겨 있지는 않으며, 대개는 길고 가느다란 고리 사슬로 만들어, 필요에 따라 바깥쪽 사슬걸이 위로 더 손쉽게 끌어 올리거나 바다 속으로 던져 내릴 수 있도록 되어 있다."]], [["“The rods!", "\"피뢰 사슬을!"], ["the rods!”", "피뢰 사슬을!\""], ["cried Starbuck to the crew, suddenly admonished to vigilance by the vivid lightning that had just been darting flambeaux, to light Ahab to his post.", "스타벅이 선원들을 향해 외쳤다. 방금 에이해브의 길을 비추려 횃불처럼 번쩍이던 그 생생한 번개에 갑자기 경계심이 일깨워진 것이다."], ["“Are they overboard?", "\"그것들이 물속에 내려져 있느냐?"], ["drop them over, fore and aft.", "던져 내려라, 이물 쪽도 고물 쪽도."], ["Quick!”", "어서!\""]], [["“Avast!”", "\"멈춰라!\""], ["cried Ahab; “let’s have fair play here, though we be the weaker side.", "에이해브가 외쳤다. \"우리가 비록 약한 쪽이라 하나, 여기서는 정정당당하게 겨루도록 하자."], ["Yet I’ll contribute to raise rods on the Himmalehs and Andes, that all the world may be secured; but out on privileges!", "그래도 나는 온 세상이 안전해지도록 히말라야와 안데스에 피뢰침 세우는 일에 기꺼이 한몫 거들겠노라. 허나 특혜 따위는 집어치워라!"], ["Let them be, sir.”", "그것들을 그냥 내버려 두게, 스타벅.\""]], [["“Look aloft!”", "\"위를 보십시오!\""], ["cried Starbuck.", "스타벅이 외쳤다."], ["“The corpusants!", "\"세인트엘모의 불이다!"], ["the corpusants!”", "세인트엘모의 불이야!\""]], [["All the yard-arms were tipped with a pallid fire; and touched at each tri-pointed lightning-rod-end with three tapering white flames, each of the three tall masts was silently burning in that sulphurous air, like three gigantic wax tapers before an altar.", "활대 끝마다 창백한 불이 매달려 있었다. 그리고 세 갈래로 갈라진 피뢰침 끝마다 가늘게 솟구치는 세 줄기 흰 불꽃이 닿아 있어, 세 개의 높은 돛대는 저마다 그 유황 냄새 자욱한 대기 속에서 소리 없이 타오르고 있었으니, 마치 제단 앞에 세워진 세 개의 거대한 밀랍 초와도 같았다."]], [["“Blast the boat!", "\"보트는 될 대로 되라지! 내버려 둬!\""], ["let it go!”", "바로 이 순간 스터브가 외쳤다. 사납게 출렁이는 너울이 그 자신의 작은 보트 밑에서 솟구쳐 올라, 그가 잡아매던 줄을 넘기는 사이에 보트 뱃전이 그의 손을 거세게 짓눌렀던 것이다."], ["cried Stubb at this instant, as a swashing sea heaved up under his own little craft, so that its gunwale violently jammed his hand, as he was passing a lashing.", "\"빌어먹을 것!\" 그러나 갑판 위로 미끄러져 뒤로 넘어지면서, 치켜뜬 그의 눈이 그 불꽃들을 사로잡았다."], ["“Blast it!”—but slipping backward on the deck, his uplifted eyes caught the flames; and immediately shifting his tone he cried—“The corpusants have mercy on us all!”", "그러자 곧장 어조를 바꾸어 그가 외쳤다. \"세인트엘모의 불이여, 우리 모두에게 자비를 베푸소서!\""]], [["To sailors, oaths are household words; they will swear in the trance of the calm, and in the teeth of the tempest; they will imprecate curses from the topsail-yard-arms, when most they teeter over to a seething sea; but in all my voyagings, seldom have I heard a common oath when God’s burning finger has been laid on the ship; when His “Mene, Mene, Tekel Upharsin”", "뱃사람들에게 욕설이란 일상의 말이다. 그들은 잔잔한 황홀경 속에서도 욕을 하고, 폭풍의 이빨 앞에서도 욕을 한다."], ["has been woven into the shrouds and the cordage.", "끓어오르는 바다 위로 가장 아슬아슬하게 흔들릴 때면 윗돛 활대 끝에서 저주를 퍼붓기도 한다. 그러나 내 모든 항해를 통틀어, 하느님의 불타는 손가락이 배 위에 얹혔을 때, 곧 그분의 \"메네 메네 데겔 우바르신\"이 삭구와 밧줄에 새겨졌을 때만큼은, 흔해 빠진 욕설 한마디조차 좀처럼 들어 본 적이 없다."]], [["While this pallidness was burning aloft, few words were heard from the enchanted crew; who in one thick cluster stood on the forecastle, all their eyes gleaming in that pale phosphorescence, like a far away constellation of stars.", "이 창백한 불빛이 위쪽에서 타오르는 동안, 마법에 걸린 선원들의 입에서는 거의 아무 말도 새어 나오지 않았다. 그들은 앞갑판 위에 한데 빽빽이 모여 서서, 그 모든 눈이 저 희미한 인광 속에서 빛나고 있었으니, 마치 아득히 먼 별자리와도 같았다."], ["Relieved against the ghostly light, the gigantic jet negro, Daggoo, loomed up to thrice his real stature, and seemed the black cloud from which the thunder had come.", "그 으스스한 빛을 배경으로, 칠흑같이 검은 거구의 흑인 대구(Daggoo)는 본래 키의 세 곱절로 우뚝 솟아올라, 천둥이 쏟아져 나온 검은 구름처럼 보였다."], ["The parted mouth of Tashtego revealed his shark-white teeth, which strangely gleamed as if they too had been tipped by corpusants; while lit up by the preternatural light, Queequeg’s tattooing burned like Satanic blue flames on his body.", "태시테고(Tashtego)의 벌어진 입은 상어처럼 새하얀 이빨을 드러냈는데, 그 이빨마저 세인트엘모의 불이 끝에 닿은 듯 기이하게 번득였다. 그동안 그 초자연적인 빛을 받은 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 문신은 그의 몸 위에서 사탄의 푸른 불꽃처럼 타올랐다."]], [["The tableau all waned at last with the pallidness aloft; and once more the Pequod and every soul on her decks were wrapped in a pall.", "이 광경은 마침내 위쪽의 창백한 불빛과 더불어 모두 스러져 갔다. 그리하여 피쿼드호와 갑판 위의 모든 영혼은 다시 한 번 검은 장막에 휩싸였다."], ["A moment or two passed, when Starbuck, going forward, pushed against some one.", "한두 순간이 지났을 때, 스타벅이 앞으로 나아가다가 누군가와 부딪쳤다. 스터브였다."], ["It was Stubb.", "\"이제 어떻게 생각하나, 이 사람아."], ["“What thinkest thou now, man; I heard thy cry; it was not the same in the song.”", "자네 외치는 소리를 들었네. 노래 속의 그것과는 달랐어.\""]], [["“No, no, it wasn’t; I said the corpusants have mercy on us all; and I hope they will, still. But do they only have mercy on long faces?—have they no bowels for a laugh? And look ye, Mr. Starbuck—but it’s too dark to look. Hear me, then: I take that mast-head flame we saw for a sign of good luck; for those masts are rooted in a hold that is going to be chock a’ block with sperm-oil, d’ye see; and so, all that sperm will work up into the masts, like sap in a tree. Yes, our three masts will yet be as three spermaceti candles—that’s the good promise we saw.”", "\"아니지요, 아닙니다, 그건 달랐습니다. 저는 세인트엘모의 불이 우리 모두에게 자비를 베풀어 달라고 했지요. 그리고 지금도 여전히 그들이 그래 주기를 바랍니다. 하지만 그들은 그저 시무룩한 얼굴에만 자비를 베푸는 걸까요? 웃음을 위해서는 도무지 인정이 없는 걸까요? 자, 보세요, 스타벅 씨… 하지만 너무 어두워서 보이질 않는군요. 그럼 들어 보세요. 저는 우리가 본 저 돛대 꼭대기의 불꽃을 행운의 징조로 받아들입니다. 저 돛대들은 곧 향유고래 기름으로 가득 들어찰 선창에 뿌리내리고 있으니까요. 아시겠어요? 그러니 그 모든 향유 기름이 나무의 수액처럼 돛대 위로 빨려 올라갈 겁니다. 그래요, 우리 세 돛대는 머지않아 향유고래 기름 양초 세 자루가 될 겁니다. 그게 바로 우리가 본 좋은 약속입니다.\""]], [["At that moment Starbuck caught sight of Stubb’s face slowly beginning to glimmer into sight.", "바로 그 순간 스타벅은 스터브의 얼굴이 서서히 어슴푸레 드러나기 시작하는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Glancing upwards, he cried: “See!", "위쪽을 흘긋 올려다보며 그가 외쳤다."], ["see!”", "\"보게!"], ["and once more the high tapering flames were beheld with what seemed redoubled supernaturalness in their pallor.", "저것 좀 보게!\" 그러자 다시 한 번, 높이 가늘게 솟구친 불꽃들이 그 창백함 속에서 갑절로 짙어진 듯한 초자연성을 띤 채 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“The corpusants have mercy on us all,”", "\"세인트엘모의 불이여, 우리 모두에게 자비를 베푸소서.\""], ["cried Stubb, again.", "스터브가 다시 외쳤다."]], [["At the base of the mainmast, full beneath the doubloon and the flame, the Parsee was kneeling in Ahab’s front, but with his head bowed away from him; while near by, from the arched and overhanging rigging, where they had just been engaged securing a spar, a number of the seamen, arrested by the glare, now cohered together, and hung pendulous, like a knot of numbed wasps from a drooping, orchard twig.", "큰돛대 밑동, 곧 금화와 불꽃 바로 아래에서 파르시인(Parsee)이 에이해브 앞에 무릎 꿇고 있었는데, 그 머리는 그에게서 돌려 숙이고 있었다. 한편 그 가까이, 활처럼 휘어 머리 위로 드리운 삭구에서는, 방금 활대 하나를 붙들어 매던 일을 하다가 그 섬광에 멈춰 선 여러 선원들이, 이제 한데 엉겨 붙어 매달려 있었으니, 마치 축 늘어진 과수원 가지에 마비된 채 한 덩어리로 붙은 말벌 떼와도 같았다."], ["In various enchanted attitudes, like the standing, or stepping, or running skeletons in Herculaneum, others remained rooted to the deck; but all their eyes upcast.", "헤르쿨라네움의 서 있거나 발 내딛거나 달리는 해골들처럼, 갖가지 마법에 걸린 자세로 다른 이들은 갑판에 못 박힌 듯 굳어 있었다. 그러나 그들의 눈은 모두 위를 향하고 있었다."]], [["“Aye, aye, men!”", "\"그래, 그래, 제군들!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Ahab.", "\"저것을 올려다보라. 똑똑히 새겨 두라."], ["“Look up at it; mark it well; the white flame but lights the way to the White Whale!", "저 흰 불꽃은 다름 아닌 흰 고래(White Whale)에게로 가는 길을 밝혀 주는 것이다! 저기 저 큰돛대 사슬을 내게 다오."], ["Hand me those mainmast links there; I would fain feel this pulse, and let mine beat against it; blood against fire!", "나는 이 맥박을 느껴 보고 싶구나. 그리하여 내 맥박이 그것에 맞서 뛰게 하리라."], ["So.”", "피로써 불에 맞서리라! 자, 이렇게.\""]], [["Then turning—the last link held fast in his left hand, he put his foot upon the Parsee; and with fixed upward eye, and high-flung right arm, he stood erect before the lofty tri-pointed trinity of flames.", "그러고는 몸을 돌려, 마지막 사슬 고리를 왼손에 단단히 쥐고서, 그는 한쪽 발을 파르시인 위에 얹었다. 그리고 위를 향해 눈을 고정한 채, 오른팔을 높이 치켜들고서, 그는 우뚝 솟아 세 갈래로 갈라진 불꽃의 삼위일체 앞에 꼿꼿이 섰다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["thou clear spirit of clear fire, whom on these seas I as Persian once did worship, till in the sacramental act so burned by thee, that to this hour I bear the scar; I now know thee, thou clear spirit, and I now know that thy right worship is defiance.", "그대 맑은 불의 맑은 정령이여, 일찍이 나는 이 바다에서 페르시아인으로서 그대를 숭배했더랬다. 그러다 그 성스러운 예식 가운데 그대에게 너무도 심하게 데어, 이 시각까지도 그 흉터를 지니고 있노라."], ["To neither love nor reverence wilt thou be kind; and e’en for hate thou canst but kill; and all are killed.", "이제 나는 그대를 안다, 그대 맑은 정령이여. 그리고 이제 나는 그대를 향한 올바른 숭배가 곧 저항임을 안다."], ["No fearless fool now fronts thee.", "사랑에도 경배에도 그대는 친절하지 않으리라."], ["I own thy speechless, placeless power; but to the last gasp of my earthquake life will dispute its unconditional, unintegral mastery in me.", "그리고 증오에 대해서조차 그대가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 죽이는 것뿐, 그리하여 모두가 죽임을 당한다. 이제 두려움 모르는 그 어떤 바보도 그대 앞에 맞서지 않는다."], ["In the midst of the personified impersonal, a personality stands here.", "나는 그대의 말 없고 자리 없는 권능을 인정한다. 그러나 내 지진 같은 목숨의 마지막 숨이 다할 때까지, 나는 내 안에서 그것이 무조건적이고 완전무결한 지배권을 휘두르는 데 맞서 다투리라."], ["Though but a point at best; whencesoe’er I came; wheresoe’er I go; yet while I earthly live, the queenly personality lives in me, and feels her royal rights.", "인격화된 비인격의 한가운데, 여기 하나의 인격이 서 있다. 비록 기껏해야 한낱 점에 지나지 않을지라도, 내가 어디서 왔든, 어디로 가든, 그래도 내가 이 땅에 살아 있는 한, 그 여왕다운 인격이 내 안에 살아 그 왕다운 권리를 느낀다."], ["But war is pain, and hate is woe.", "그러나 전쟁은 고통이요, 증오는 비애로다."], ["Come in thy lowest form of love, and I will kneel and kiss thee; but at thy highest, come as mere supernal power; and though thou launchest navies of full-freighted worlds, there’s that in here that still remains indifferent.", "그대의 가장 낮은 사랑의 모습으로 오라, 그러면 나는 무릎 꿇고 그대에게 입 맞추리라. 그러나 그대의 가장 높은 모습으로, 한낱 천상의 권능으로서 오거든, 그대가 가득 실은 세계들로 이루어진 함대를 띄워 보낸다 한들, 여기 이 안에는 여전히 무심한 채로 남아 있는 무언가가 있노라."], ["Oh, thou clear spirit, of thy fire thou madest me, and like a true child of fire, I breathe it back to thee.”", "오, 그대 맑은 정령이여, 그대의 불로 그대는 나를 빚었으니, 불의 참된 자식답게, 나는 그것을 그대에게 도로 내뿜노라.\""]], [["[Sudden, repeated flashes of lightning; the nine flames leap lengthwise to thrice their previous height; Ahab, with the rest, closes his eyes, his right hand pressed hard upon them.]", "[갑작스럽게, 거듭하여 번개가 번쩍인다. 아홉 줄기 불꽃이 길게 솟구쳐 이전 높이의 세 곱절에 이른다. 에이해브는 다른 이들과 함께 눈을 감고, 오른손으로 그 위를 힘껏 누른다.]"]], [["“I own thy speechless, placeless power; said I not so?", "\"나는 그대의 말 없고 자리 없는 권능을 인정한다. 내가 그리 말하지 않았던가?"], ["Nor was it wrung from me; nor do I now drop these links.", "허나 그것은 내게서 억지로 짜낸 말이 아니었다. 그리고 나는 지금 이 사슬을 떨어뜨리지 않는다."], ["Thou canst blind; but I can then grope.", "그대는 눈멀게 할 수 있다. 허나 그러면 나는 더듬어 나아가리라."], ["Thou canst consume; but I can then be ashes.", "그대는 태워 없앨 수 있다. 허나 그러면 나는 한 줌 재가 되리라."], ["Take the homage of these poor eyes, and shutter-hands.", "이 가련한 두 눈과, 덧창처럼 닫힌 두 손의 경배를 받아라. 나라면 그것을 받지 않으련만."], ["I would not take it.", "번개가 내 두개골을 꿰뚫고 번쩍인다."], ["The lightning flashes through my skull; mine eye-balls ache and ache; my whole beaten brain seems as beheaded, and rolling on some stunning ground.", "내 두 눈알이 쑤시고 또 쑤신다. 두들겨 맞은 내 머릿골 전체가 마치 목 잘린 듯, 어떤 아찔한 바닥 위를 데굴데굴 구르는 듯하다."], ["Oh, oh!", "오, 오!"], ["Yet blindfold, yet will I talk to thee.", "그래도 눈을 가린 채로, 그래도 나는 그대에게 말하리라."], ["Light though thou be, thou leapest out of darkness; but I am darkness leaping out of light, leaping out of thee!", "그대 빛일지언정, 그대는 어둠에서 솟구쳐 나온다. 그러나 나는 빛에서 솟구쳐 나오는 어둠, 그대에게서 솟구쳐 나오는 어둠이다!"], ["The javelins cease; open eyes; see, or not?", "창들이 멎는다. 두 눈을 떠라."], ["There burn the flames!", "보이느냐, 보이지 않느냐? 저기 불꽃이 타오른다!"], ["Oh, thou magnanimous!", "오, 그대 도량 넓은 이여!"], ["now I do glory in my genealogy.", "이제 나는 내 혈통을 자랑스러워하노라."], ["But thou art but my fiery father; my sweet mother, I know not.", "그러나 그대는 한낱 내 불타는 아비일 뿐, 내 다정한 어미를 나는 알지 못한다."], ["Oh, cruel!", "오, 잔인한 이여!"], ["what hast thou done with her?", "그대는 그녀를 어찌하였는가?"], ["There lies my puzzle; but thine is greater.", "거기에 내 수수께끼가 있다. 허나 그대의 수수께끼가 더 크도다."], ["Thou knowest not how came ye, hence callest thyself unbegotten; certainly knowest not thy beginning, hence callest thyself unbegun.", "그대는 자신이 어찌 생겨났는지 알지 못하니, 그리하여 자신을 낳아진 적 없는 자라 부른다. 분명코 자신의 시작을 알지 못하니, 그리하여 자신을 시작된 적 없는 자라 부른다."], ["I know that of me, which thou knowest not of thyself, oh, thou omnipotent.", "나는 나 자신에 관하여 그대가 그대 자신에 관해 알지 못하는 무언가를 안다, 오, 그대 전능한 이여."], ["There is some unsuffusing thing beyond thee, thou clear spirit, to whom all thy eternity is but time, all thy creativeness mechanical.", "그대 너머에는 스며들지 않는 어떤 것이 있으니, 그대 맑은 정령이여, 그것에게는 그대의 영원함도 한낱 시간에 지나지 않고, 그대의 모든 창조력도 한낱 기계적인 것에 지나지 않는다. 그대를 통하여, 그대의 불타는 자아를 통하여, 내 그을린 두 눈이 그것을 어렴풋이 보노라."], ["Through thee, thy flaming self, my scorched eyes do dimly see it.", "오, 그대 버려진 불꽃이여, 그대 태곳적의 은둔자여, 그대 또한 그대만의 전할 수 없는 수수께끼를, 함께 나눌 수 없는 슬픔을 지니고 있구나."], ["Oh, thou foundling fire, thou hermit immemorial, thou too hast thy incommunicable riddle, thy unparticipated grief.", "여기 다시금 오만한 고뇌로써, 나는 내 아비를 읽어 낸다. 솟구쳐라!"], ["Here again with haughty agony, I read my sire.", "솟구쳐 올라, 하늘을 핥아라! 나는 그대와 함께 솟구친다."], ["Leap! leap up, and lick the sky!", "나는 그대와 함께 타오른다."], ["I leap with thee; I burn with thee; would fain be welded with thee; defyingly I worship thee!”", "나는 기꺼이 그대와 하나로 녹아 붙고 싶다. 저항하며 나는 그대를 숭배하노라!\""]], [["“The boat!", "\"보트!"], ["the boat!”", "보트를 보십시오!\""], ["cried Starbuck, “look at thy boat, old man!”", "스타벅이 외쳤다. \"당신 보트를 보세요, 영감님!\""]], [["Ahab’s harpoon, the one forged at Perth’s fire, remained firmly lashed in its conspicuous crotch, so that it projected beyond his whale-boat’s bow; but the sea that had stove its bottom had caused the loose leather sheath to drop off; and from the keen steel barb there now came a levelled flame of pale, forked fire. As the silent harpoon burned there like a serpent’s tongue, Starbuck grasped Ahab by the arm—“God, God is against thee, old man; forbear! ’tis an ill voyage! ill begun, ill continued; let me square the yards, while we may, old man, and make a fair wind of it homewards, to go on a better voyage than this.”", "퍼스(Perth)의 불에서 벼려진 에이해브의 작살은 그 눈에 띄는 거치대에 단단히 잡아매인 채, 그의 고래잡이 보트 뱃머리 너머로 삐죽 튀어나와 있었다. 그러나 그 보트 바닥을 부수어 놓은 바다가 헐거워진 가죽 칼집을 떨어져 나가게 했고, 이제 그 날카로운 강철 미늘에서 창백하게 갈라진 불의 일직선 화염이 뻗어 나오고 있었다. 그 말 없는 작살이 마치 뱀의 혀처럼 거기서 타오르자, 스타벅이 에이해브의 팔을 움켜잡았다. \"하느님, 하느님이 당신께 맞서고 계십니다, 영감님. 그만두세요! 이건 흉한 항해입니다! 흉하게 시작되어, 흉하게 이어지고 있어요. 아직 그럴 수 있을 때 제가 활대를 바로잡게 해 주세요, 영감님. 그리하여 이 폭풍을 고향으로 향하는 순풍으로 삼아, 이보다 더 나은 항해에 나서게 해 주세요.\""]], [["Overhearing Starbuck, the panic-stricken crew instantly ran to the braces—though not a sail was left aloft.", "스타벅의 말을 엿들은 공포에 사로잡힌 선원들은 즉시 아딧줄로 달려갔다. 위에는 돛 한 폭 남아 있지 않았는데도 말이다."], ["For the moment all the aghast mate’s thoughts seemed theirs; they raised a half mutinous cry.", "그 순간 경악한 일등항해사의 모든 생각이 곧 그들의 생각이 된 듯했다. 그들은 반쯤 반란을 일으킬 듯한 함성을 질렀다."], ["But dashing the rattling lightning links to the deck, and snatching the burning harpoon, Ahab waved it like a torch among them; swearing to transfix with it the first sailor that but cast loose a rope’s end.", "그러나 절그렁대는 번개의 사슬을 갑판에 내동댕이치고, 타오르는 작살을 와락 움켜쥔 에이해브는 그것을 그들 사이로 횃불처럼 휘둘렀다. 그러면서 밧줄 한 가닥이라도 풀어 놓는 첫 번째 선원은 그것으로 꿰뚫어 버리겠노라 으름장을 놓았다."], ["Petrified by his aspect, and still more shrinking from the fiery dart that he held, the men fell back in dismay, and Ahab again spoke:—", "그의 모습에 돌처럼 굳어 버린 데다, 그가 든 그 불타는 화살에 더더욱 움츠러든 선원들은 겁에 질려 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 에이해브는 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“All your oaths to hunt the White Whale are as binding as mine; and heart, soul, and body, lungs and life, old Ahab is bound.", "\"흰 고래를 사냥하겠다는 너희의 모든 맹세는 내 맹세만큼이나 구속력을 지닌다. 그리고 심장도, 영혼도, 육신도, 폐도, 목숨도, 이 늙은 에이해브는 그 맹세에 묶여 있다."], ["And that ye may know to what tune this heart beats; look ye here; thus I blow out the last fear!”", "그리고 이 심장이 어떤 가락에 맞추어 뛰는지 너희가 알 수 있도록, 여기를 보라. 나는 이렇듯 마지막 두려움을 불어 꺼 버리노라!\""], ["And with one blast of his breath he extinguished the flame.", "그러고는 단숨에 입김을 한 번 불어 그 불꽃을 꺼 버렸다."]], [["As in the hurricane that sweeps the plain, men fly the neighborhood of some lone, gigantic elm, whose very height and strength but render it so much the more unsafe, because so much the more a mark for thunderbolts; so at those last words of Ahab’s many of the mariners did run from him in a terror of dismay.", "벌판을 휩쓰는 태풍 속에서 사람들이 어느 외따로 선 거대한 느릅나무 곁을 피해 달아나듯이 — 바로 그 높이와 힘이 도리어 그 나무를 그만큼 더 위태롭게 만드니, 그만큼 더 벼락의 표적이 되기 때문이다 — 그렇듯 에이해브의 그 마지막 말이 떨어지자 많은 뱃사람들이 경악의 공포에 사로잡혀 그에게서 달아났다."]], [["Ahab standing by the helm.", "에이해브가 키 옆에 서 있다."], ["Starbuck approaching him.", "스타벅이 그에게 다가온다."]], [["“We must send down the main-top-sail yard, sir.", "\"큰 윗돛 활대를 내려야 합니다, 선장님."], ["The band is working loose and the lee lift is half-stranded.", "결박이 헐거워지고 있고 바람 아래쪽 받침줄이 반쯤 풀렸습니다."], ["Shall I strike it, sir?”", "내릴까요, 선장님?\""]], [["“Strike nothing; lash it.", "\"아무것도 내리지 마라. 단단히 동여매라."], ["If I had sky-sail poles, I’d sway them up now.”", "내게 하늘돛 장대가 있다면 지금이라도 끌어 올렸을 것이다.\""]], [["“Sir!—in God’s name!—sir?” “Well.”", "\"선장님!—제발 하느님의 이름으로!—선장님?\""]], [["“The anchors are working, sir.", "\"왜 그러느냐.\" \"닻이 흔들리고 있습니다, 선장님."], ["Shall I get them inboard?”", "안으로 끌어들일까요?\""]], [["“Strike nothing, and stir nothing, but lash everything.", "\"아무것도 내리지 말고, 아무것도 건드리지 마라, 다만 모두 단단히 동여매라."], ["The wind rises, but it has not got up to my table-lands yet.", "바람이 거세지고는 있으나, 아직 내 고원 지대까지는 올라오지 못했다."], ["Quick, and see to it.—By masts and keels!", "어서, 가서 처리하라.—돛대와 용골을 두고 맹세하건대!"], ["he takes me for the hunch-backed skipper of some coasting smack.", "이자는 나를 무슨 연안을 떠도는 작은 배의 곱사등이 선장쯤으로 보는 모양이구나."], ["Send down my main-top-sail yard!", "내 큰 윗돛 활대를 내리라니!"], ["Ho, gluepots!", "어이, 풀단지 같은 자들아!"], ["Loftiest trucks were made for wildest winds, and this brain-truck of mine now sails amid the cloud-scud.", "가장 높은 돛머리는 가장 사나운 바람을 위해 만들어진 것이며, 지금 내 이 머릿속 돛머리는 흩날리는 구름 사이를 항해하고 있다."], ["Shall I strike that?", "그것을 내리라고?"], ["Oh, none but cowards send down their brain-trucks in tempest time.", "오, 폭풍이 몰아칠 때 제 머릿속 돛머리를 내리는 자는 겁쟁이뿐이다."], ["What a hooroosh aloft there!", "저 높은 곳에서 웬 야단법석이란 말이냐!"], ["I would e’en take it for sublime, did I not know that the colic is a noisy malady.", "산통(疝痛)이 요란한 병이라는 것을 내가 모른다면, 저 소리를 숭고하다 여겼을 것이다."], ["Oh, take medicine, take medicine!”", "오, 약을 먹어라, 약을 먹어!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 121. Midnight.—The Forecastle Bulwarks.", "# CHAPTER 121. Midnight.—The Forecastle Bulwarks."]], [["Stubb and Flask mounted on them, and passing additional lashings over the anchors there hanging.", "“아니야, 스터브(Stubb)."], ["“No, Stubb; you may pound that knot there as much as you please, but you will never pound into me what you were just now saying.", "거기 그 매듭이야 네 마음껏 두들겨도 좋다만, 방금 네가 한 말만큼은 내 머릿속에 절대 못 박아 넣을걸."], ["And how long ago is it since you said the very contrary?", "그리고 네가 정반대 소리를 한 게 얼마나 됐다고?"], ["Didn’t you once say that whatever ship Ahab sails in, that ship should pay something extra on its insurance policy, just as though it were loaded with powder barrels aft and boxes of lucifers forward?", "언젠가 네가 이런 말을 안 했나? 에이해브(Ahab)가 어느 배를 타든, 그 배는 보험증권에 웃돈을 좀 더 물어야 한다고, 마치 고물에는 화약통을 싣고 이물에는 성냥갑을 잔뜩 실은 배인 것처럼 말이야."], ["Stop, now; didn’t you say so?”", "자, 잠깐만, 네가 그렇게 말하지 않았어?”"]], [["“Well, suppose I did?", "“그래, 내가 그랬다고 치자."], ["What then?", "그게 뭐 어때서?"], ["I’ve part changed my flesh since that time, why not my mind?", "그때 이후로 내 살점도 일부는 갈렸는데, 마음이라고 못 바꿀까?"], ["Besides, supposing we are loaded with powder barrels aft and lucifers forward; how the devil could the lucifers get afire in this drenching spray here?", "게다가 우리가 정말 고물에 화약통을 싣고 이물에 성냥을 실었다 친들, 이렇게 흠뻑 쏟아지는 물보라 속에서 도대체 그 성냥에 어떻게 불이 붙겠어?"], ["Why, my little man, you have pretty red hair, but you couldn’t get afire now.", "이봐, 꼬마 친구, 자네 머리칼이 제법 빨갛긴 해도, 지금은 거기에 불을 붙일 수가 없을걸. 몸 좀 털어 봐."], ["Shake yourself; you’re Aquarius, or the water-bearer, Flask; might fill pitchers at your coat collar.", "자넨 물병자리, 곧 물을 나르는 자야, 플라스크(Flask). 자네 외투 깃에서 물주전자라도 채울 수 있겠어."], ["Don’t you see, then, that for these extra risks the Marine Insurance companies have extra guarantees?", "그러니 모르겠나, 이런 추가 위험에 대해서는 해상보험회사들이 추가 보증을 받는다는 걸?"], ["Here are hydrants, Flask.", "여기 소화전이 있다 이거지, 플라스크."], ["But hark, again, and I’ll answer ye the other thing.", "하지만 다시 들어 봐, 그 다른 문제에 대해 답해 줄 테니."], ["First take your leg off from the crown of the anchor here, though, so I can pass the rope; now listen.", "다만 우선 그 다리부터 여기 닻머리에서 치워 봐, 그래야 밧줄을 넘길 수 있으니까. 자, 들어 봐."], ["What’s the mighty difference between holding a mast’s lightning-rod in the storm, and standing close by a mast that hasn’t got any lightning-rod at all in a storm?", "폭풍 속에서 돛대의 피뢰침을 붙잡고 있는 것과, 폭풍 속에서 피뢰침이라곤 아예 없는 돛대 바로 옆에 서 있는 것 사이에 무슨 대단한 차이가 있겠어?"], ["Don’t you see, you timber-head, that no harm can come to the holder of the rod, unless the mast is first struck?", "모르겠나, 이 멍청아, 돛대가 먼저 벼락을 맞지 않는 한 피뢰침 잡은 사람에게는 아무 해도 없다는 걸?"], ["What are you talking about, then?", "그럼 자넨 도대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거야?"], ["Not one ship in a hundred carries rods, and Ahab,—aye, man, and all of us,—were in no more danger then, in my poor opinion, than all the crews in ten thousand ships now sailing the seas.", "백 척 중에 한 척도 피뢰침을 달고 다니지 않아, 그러니 에이해브도, 그래, 이 친구야, 우리 모두도, 내 변변찮은 생각으로는 지금 바다를 항해하는 만 척의 배에 탄 모든 선원들보다 조금도 더 위험하지 않았던 거야."], ["Why, you King-Post, you, I suppose you would have every man in the world go about with a small lightning-rod running up the corner of his hat, like a militia officer’s skewered feather, and trailing behind like his sash.", "이봐, 이 킹포스트 같은 친구야, 자네는 세상 모든 사람이 민병대 장교의 꼬챙이 꽂은 깃털처럼 모자 모서리에 작은 피뢰침을 세우고, 그 띠처럼 뒤로 질질 끌고 다니길 바라는 모양이지?"], ["Why don’t ye be sensible, Flask?", "좀 분별 있게 굴지그래, 플라스크?"], ["it’s easy to be sensible; why don’t ye, then?", "분별 있게 구는 거야 쉬운 일이잖아. 그런데 왜 안 그래?"], ["any man with half an eye can be sensible.”", "반쪽 눈만 달린 사람도 분별은 차릴 수 있는데.”"]], [["“I don’t know that, Stubb.", "“그건 모르겠는데, 스터브."], ["You sometimes find it rather hard.”", "자네도 더러는 그게 꽤 어렵다고 느끼잖아.”"]], [["“Yes, when a fellow’s soaked through, it’s hard to be sensible, that’s a fact.", "“그래, 사람이 흠뻑 젖었을 땐 분별 차리기가 어렵지, 그건 사실이야."], ["And I am about drenched with this spray.", "그리고 난 이 물보라에 거의 다 젖어 가는 중이고."], ["Never mind; catch the turn there, and pass it.", "신경 쓰지 마. 거기 한 바퀴 돌려서 넘겨."], ["Seems to me we are lashing down these anchors now as if they were never going to be used again.", "내가 보기엔 지금 우리가 이 닻들을 다시는 안 쓸 것처럼 묶어 두고 있는 것 같단 말이야."], ["Tying these two anchors here, Flask, seems like tying a man’s hands behind him.", "여기 이 두 닻을 묶는 게, 플라스크, 사람 손을 등 뒤로 결박하는 것 같단 말이지."], ["And what big generous hands they are, to be sure.", "그것도 참 크고 후한 손들이긴 하지만 말이야."], ["These are your iron fists, hey?", "이게 자네의 쇠주먹들인가, 응?"], ["What a hold they have, too!", "게다가 어찌나 단단히 붙드는지!"], ["I wonder, Flask, whether the world is anchored anywhere; if she is, she swings with an uncommon long cable, though.", "궁금하군, 플라스크, 이 세상은 대체 어디엔가 닻을 내리고 있는 걸까. 만약 그렇다면 세상은 유난히 긴 밧줄을 늘어뜨린 채 흔들리고 있는 셈이지."], ["There, hammer that knot down, and we’ve done.", "자, 그 매듭을 두들겨 박아라, 그럼 끝이다."], ["So; next to touching land, lighting on deck is the most satisfactory.", "됐어. 뭍을 밟는 것 다음으로는 갑판에 내려서는 게 제일 흡족하단 말이야."], ["I say, just wring out my jacket skirts, will ye?", "이봐, 내 웃옷 자락 좀 짜 주겠나?"], ["Thank ye.", "고맙네."], ["They laugh at long-togs so, Flask; but seems to me, a long tailed coat ought always to be worn in all storms afloat.", "사람들이 긴 옷자락을 그렇게들 비웃지, 플라스크. 하지만 내가 보기엔, 바다 위 온갖 폭풍 속에서는 늘 긴 꼬리 외투를 입어야 마땅하단 말이야."], ["The tails tapering down that way, serve to carry off the water, d’ye see.", "그렇게 끝으로 갈수록 가늘어지는 자락이 물을 흘려보내 주거든, 알겠어? 삼각모도 마찬가지지."], ["Same with cocked hats; the cocks form gable-end eave-troughs, Flask.", "그 뾰족한 부분이 박공 처마의 빗물받이 노릇을 한다고, 플라스크."], ["No more monkey-jackets and tarpaulins for me; I must mount a swallow-tail, and drive down a beaver; so.", "나는 이제 짧은 작업복 재킷이니 방수포 외투니 하는 건 안 입을 테야. 제비꼬리 연미복을 걸치고, 비버 가죽 모자를 눌러써야겠어, 이렇게."], ["Halloa!", "어이쿠!"], ["whew!", "휘이!"], ["there goes my tarpaulin overboard; Lord, Lord, that the winds that come from heaven should be so unmannerly!", "저기 내 방수포 모자가 바다로 날아가네. 맙소사, 맙소사, 하늘에서 불어오는 바람이 이렇게 무례할 수가 있나!"], ["This is a nasty night, lad.”", "고약한 밤이야, 이 친구야.”"]], [["# CHAPTER 122. Midnight Aloft.—Thunder and Lightning.", "# CHAPTER 122. Midnight Aloft.—Thunder and Lightning."]], [["The main-top-sail yard.—Tashtego passing new lashings around it.", "『큰 윗돛 활대.—타슈테고(Tashtego)가 새 밧줄을 그 둘레에 감으며.』"]], [["“Um, um, um.", "“음, 음, 음."], ["Stop that thunder!", "저놈의 천둥 좀 멈춰라!"], ["Plenty too much thunder up here.", "이 위는 천둥이 너무 많아."], ["What’s the use of thunder?", "천둥이 무슨 소용이람?"], ["Um, um, um.", "음, 음, 음."], ["We don’t want thunder; we want rum; give us a glass of rum.", "우리는 천둥 따위 바라지 않아. 우리가 바라는 건 럼주야."], ["Um, um, um!” CHAPTER 123.", "럼주 한 잔 다오."], ["The Musket.", "음, 음, 음!”"]], [["During the most violent shocks of the Typhoon, the man at the Pequod’s jaw-bone tiller had several times been reelingly hurled to the deck by its spasmodic motions, even though preventer tackles had been attached to it—for they were slack—because some play to the tiller was indispensable.", "태풍이 가장 사납게 몰아칠 때, 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 턱뼈 키손잡이를 잡고 선 사람은 그 발작 같은 움직임 탓에 몇 번이나 비틀거리며 갑판으로 내동댕이쳐졌다. 보조 도르래를 키손잡이에 매달아 두긴 했으나 — 줄을 느슨하게 풀어 두었기 때문이었다 — 키손잡이에는 어느 정도의 여유가 반드시 필요했던 것이다."]], [["In a severe gale like this, while the ship is but a tossed shuttlecock to the blast, it is by no means uncommon to see the needles in the compasses, at intervals, go round and round.", "이처럼 사나운 강풍 속에서, 배가 돌풍에 이리저리 내던져지는 한낱 셔틀콕 신세로 전락할 때면, 나침반의 바늘이 이따금 빙글빙글 도는 광경을 보는 일은 결코 드물지 않다. 피쿼드호의 나침반도 그러했다."], ["It was thus with the Pequod’s; at almost every shock the helmsman had not failed to notice the whirling velocity with which they revolved upon the cards; it is a sight that hardly anyone can behold without some sort of unwonted emotion.", "거의 매번 충격이 올 때마다 키잡이는 바늘이 나침반 눈금 위에서 빙빙 도는 그 어지러운 속도를 놓치지 않고 보았다. 그것은 어지간한 사람이라면 어떤 낯선 감정에 사로잡히지 않고는 도저히 바라볼 수 없는 광경이다."]], [["Some hours after midnight, the Typhoon abated so much, that through the strenuous exertions of Starbuck and Stubb—one engaged forward and the other aft—the shivered remnants of the jib and fore and main-top-sails were cut adrift from the spars, and went eddying away to leeward, like the feathers of an albatross, which sometimes are cast to the winds when that storm-tossed bird is on the wing.", "자정이 지나 몇 시간 뒤, 태풍이 어지간히 잦아들자, 스타벅(Starbuck)과 스터브(Stubb)의 — 한 사람은 앞쪽에서, 다른 한 사람은 뒤쪽에서 — 안간힘 어린 노고를 통해, 갈가리 찢긴 삼각돛과 앞돛 그리고 큰 윗돛의 너덜너덜한 잔해가 활대에서 잘려 나가, 폭풍에 시달리던 앨버트로스가 날갯짓을 할 때 더러 바람에 흩날려 떨어지는 그 새의 깃털처럼, 소용돌이치며 바람 부는 아래쪽으로 날아가 버렸다."]], [["The three corresponding new sails were now bent and reefed, and a storm-trysail was set further aft; so that the ship soon went through the water with some precision again; and the course—for the present, East-south-east—which he was to steer, if practicable, was once more given to the helmsman.", "이제 거기에 상응하는 새 돛 세 개가 활대에 매달리고 폭을 줄여졌으며, 폭풍용 보조 돛 하나가 더 뒤쪽에 펼쳐졌다. 그리하여 배는 곧 다시금 어느 정도 정확하게 물살을 헤치고 나아갔다."], ["For during the violence of the gale, he had only steered according to its vicissitudes.", "그리고 그가 잡아야 할 항로 — 당분간은 동남동쪽 — 가, 가능하다면, 다시 한번 키잡이에게 주어졌다."], ["But as he was now bringing the ship as near her course as possible, watching the compass meanwhile, lo!", "강풍이 사납게 몰아치는 동안 그는 오직 그 변덕에 맞추어 키를 잡았을 뿐이었기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 그가 나침반을 살피면서 배를 가능한 한 항로 가까이로 끌어가고 있을 때, 보라!"], ["a good sign!", "좋은 징조였다!"], ["the wind seemed coming round astern; aye, the foul breeze became fair!", "바람이 고물 쪽으로 돌아오는 듯했다. 그렇다, 고약하던 바람이 순풍으로 바뀐 것이다!"]], [["Instantly the yards were squared, to the lively song of “Ho!", "곧바로 활대들이 가로로 반듯하게 맞춰졌으니, “호!"], ["the fair wind!", "순풍이다!"], ["oh-ye-ho, cheerly men!”", "어이여어, 기운차게, 친구들아!”"], ["the crew singing for joy, that so promising an event should so soon have falsified the evil portents preceding it.", "하는 흥겨운 노랫소리와 함께였다. 그토록 길조로운 사건이 앞서 닥쳤던 흉조를 이리도 빨리 거짓으로 만들어 준 것이 기뻐, 선원들이 환호하며 노래했다."]], [["In compliance with the standing order of his commander—to report immediately, and at any one of the twenty-four hours, any decided change in the affairs of the deck,—Starbuck had no sooner trimmed the yards to the breeze—however reluctantly and gloomily,—than he mechanically went below to apprise Captain Ahab of the circumstance.", "언제든, 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 어느 때든, 갑판 일에 뚜렷한 변화가 생기면 즉시 보고하라는 선장의 상시 명령에 따라, 스타벅은 — 비록 마지못해, 또 침울한 심정으로나마 — 활대를 바람에 맞추어 손질해 놓자마자, 기계적으로 아래층으로 내려가 에이해브(Ahab) 선장에게 그 사정을 알리려 했다."]], [["Ere knocking at his state-room, he involuntarily paused before it a moment.", "그의 선실 문을 두드리기에 앞서, 그는 자기도 모르게 문 앞에서 잠시 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["The cabin lamp—taking long swings this way and that—was burning fitfully, and casting fitful shadows upon the old man’s bolted door,—a thin one, with fixed blinds inserted, in place of upper panels.", "선실 등불은 — 이쪽저쪽으로 길게 흔들리며 — 일렁이듯 타올라, 노인의 빗장 걸린 문 위로 일렁이는 그림자를 드리우고 있었다. 그 문은 얇은 것으로, 위쪽 판자 대신 붙박이 발이 끼워져 있었다."], ["The isolated subterraneousness of the cabin made a certain humming silence to reign there, though it was hooped round by all the roar of the elements.", "선실은 외따로 떨어진 지하 동굴 같은 곳이어서, 비록 사방이 천지의 온갖 포효로 에워싸여 있었음에도, 그 안에는 어떤 웅웅대는 정적이 감돌고 있었다."], ["The loaded muskets in the rack were shiningly revealed, as they stood upright against the forward bulkhead.", "장전된 머스킷총들이 앞쪽 격벽에 기대어 곧추선 채로 번들거리며 모습을 드러냈다. 스타벅은 정직하고 올곧은 사람이었다."], ["Starbuck was an honest, upright man; but out of Starbuck’s heart, at that instant when he saw the muskets, there strangely evolved an evil thought; but so blent with its neutral or good accompaniments that for the instant he hardly knew it for itself.", "그러나 그 머스킷총을 본 바로 그 순간, 스타벅의 가슴속에서 이상하게도 사악한 생각 하나가 피어올랐다. 다만 그것은 무심하거나 선한 다른 생각들과 너무도 뒤섞여 있어, 그 찰나에 그는 그것이 무엇인지 좀처럼 알아채지 못했다."]], [["“He would have shot me once,”", "“저 사람은 언젠가 나를 쏘려 했지.” 그가 중얼거렸다."], ["he murmured, “yes, there’s the very musket that he pointed at me;—that one with the studded stock; let me touch it—lift it.", "“그래, 바로 저게 그가 내게 겨누었던 그 머스킷총이야. 개머리판에 징이 박힌 저것."], ["Strange, that I, who have handled so many deadly lances, strange, that I should shake so now.", "한번 만져 보자 — 들어 보자. 이상하군, 그토록 많은 살벌한 작살을 다뤄 온 내가, 이상하게도, 지금 이렇게 떨고 있다니."], ["Loaded?", "장전됐나?"], ["I must see.", "봐야겠어."], ["Aye, aye; and powder in the pan;—that’s not good.", "그래, 그래, 약실에 화약도 들어 있군. 좋지 않은 일이야."], ["Best spill it?—wait.", "쏟아 버리는 게 나을까?"], ["I’ll cure myself of this.", "잠깐. 이런 마음일랑 내 스스로 고쳐 보리라."], ["I’ll hold the musket boldly while I think.—I come to report a fair wind to him.", "생각하는 동안 이 총을 대담하게 쥐고 있어야지. 나는 그에게 순풍을 알리러 온 거다."], ["But how fair?", "한데 무슨 순풍이지?"], ["Fair for death and doom,—that’s fair for Moby Dick.", "죽음과 파멸을 향한 순풍, 그것이야말로 모비 딕(Moby Dick)에게 순풍이지."], ["It’s a fair wind that’s only fair for that accursed fish.—The very tube he pointed at me!—the very one; this one—I hold it here; he would have killed me with the very thing I handle now.—Aye and he would fain kill all his crew.", "저 저주받은 물고기에게만 순한 바람, 그게 순풍이라니. 그가 내게 겨누었던 바로 그 총신!"], ["Does he not say he will not strike his spars to any gale?", "바로 그것, 이것 — 내가 여기 쥐고 있는 이것. 그는 지금 내가 손에 든 바로 이것으로 나를 죽이려 했었지."], ["Has he not dashed his heavenly quadrant?", "그래, 그는 제 선원들 모두를 기꺼이 죽이려 들 거야."], ["and in these same perilous seas, gropes he not his way by mere dead reckoning of the error-abounding log?", "그는 어떤 강풍에도 돛대를 내리지 않겠다고 말하지 않았던가? 그 천상의 사분의를 내동댕이치지 않았던가?"], ["and in this very Typhoon, did he not swear that he would have no lightning-rods?", "게다가 이 위험천만한 바다에서, 오류투성이 측정기의 추측 항법에만 의지해 길을 더듬고 있지 않은가? 또 바로 이 태풍 속에서, 피뢰침 따위는 두지 않겠다고 맹세하지 않았던가?"], ["But shall this crazed old man be tamely suffered to drag a whole ship’s company down to doom with him?—Yes, it would make him the wilful murderer of thirty men and more, if this ship come to any deadly harm; and come to deadly harm, my soul swears this ship will, if Ahab have his way.", "한데 이 미쳐 버린 노인이 배 한 척의 선원 전부를 제 파멸 속으로 함께 끌고 들어가도록 고분고분 내버려 두어야 한단 말인가? 그래, 만일 이 배가 어떤 치명적인 화를 입게 된다면, 그는 서른 명 넘는 사람들을 고의로 죽인 살인자가 되는 셈이다."], ["If, then, he were this instant—put aside, that crime would not be his.", "그리고 에이해브가 제멋대로 한다면 이 배가 치명적인 화를 입으리라는 것을, 내 영혼이 맹세컨대 그러하리라. 그렇다면, 만약 그가 바로 이 순간에 — 치워진다면, 그 죄는 그의 것이 되지 않을 텐데."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["is he muttering in his sleep?", "그가 잠결에 중얼거리고 있나?"], ["Yes, just there,—in there, he’s sleeping.", "그래, 바로 저기, 저 안에서, 그는 잠들어 있다."], ["Sleeping?", "잠들어 있다고?"], ["aye, but still alive, and soon awake again.", "그래, 하지만 여전히 살아 있고, 곧 다시 깨어나겠지."], ["I can’t withstand thee, then, old man.", "그렇다면 나는 당신을 당해 낼 수 없소, 노인이여."], ["Not reasoning; not remonstrance; not entreaty wilt thou hearken to; all this thou scornest.", "이치도, 간언도, 애원도 당신은 귀담아듣지 않을 테고, 그 모든 것을 당신은 비웃을 따름이지. 당신 자신의 단호한 명령에 대한 무조건의 복종, 당신이 숨 쉬듯 바라는 건 오직 그것뿐."], ["Flat obedience to thy own flat commands, this is all thou breathest.", "그래, 당신은 사람들이 당신의 맹세를 함께 맹세했다 하고, 우리 모두가 에이해브라 말하지. 위대한 하느님, 그런 일이 없게 하소서!"], ["Aye, and say’st the men have vow’d thy vow; say’st all of us are Ahabs.", "한데 다른 길은 없는가? 합법적인 길은?"], ["Great God forbid!—But is there no other way?", "그를 포로로 잡아 고향까지 끌고 갈까? 무어라!"], ["no lawful way?—Make him a prisoner to be taken home?", "이 노인의 살아 있는 권능을 그의 살아 있는 두 손에서 비틀어 빼앗기를 바란다고? 오직 바보만이 그런 짓을 해 보려 들 테지."], ["What!", "그를 결박했다 치자."], ["hope to wrest this old man’s living power from his own living hands?", "온몸을 밧줄과 닻줄로 칭칭 묶고, 이 선실 바닥의 고리 볼트에 사슬로 붙들어 맸다 치자. 그러면 그는 우리에 갇힌 호랑이보다 더 끔찍해질 거다."], ["Only a fool would try it.", "나는 그 꼴을 견뎌 낼 수 없고, 그 울부짖음에서 결코 달아날 수도 없을 거다."], ["Say he were pinioned even; knotted all over with ropes and hawsers; chained down to ring-bolts on this cabin floor; he would be more hideous than a caged tiger, then.", "그 참기 어려운 긴 항해 동안 온갖 위안도, 잠 그 자체도, 더없이 값진 이성마저도 나를 떠나 버리리라. 그렇다면, 무엇이 남는가?"], ["I could not endure the sight; could not possibly fly his howlings; all comfort, sleep itself, inestimable reason would leave me on the long intolerable voyage.", "육지는 수백 리그나 떨어져 있고, 가장 가까운 곳이라곤 굳게 잠긴 일본뿐이다. 나는 여기 망망대해 위에 홀로 서 있고, 나와 법 사이에는 두 대양과 대륙 하나가 가로놓여 있다."], ["What, then, remains?", "그래, 그래, 그렇고말고."], ["The land is hundreds of leagues away, and locked Japan the nearest.", "하늘이 침대에 누운 살인 미수자에게 벼락을 내리쳐 그 시트와 살갗을 함께 불쏘시개로 만든다면, 하늘은 살인자인가?"], ["I stand alone here upon an open sea, with two oceans and a whole continent between me and law.—Aye, aye, ’tis so.—Is heaven a murderer when its lightning strikes a would-be murderer in his bed, tindering sheets and skin together?—And would I be a murderer, then, if”—and slowly, stealthily, and half sideways looking, he placed the loaded musket’s end against the door.", "그렇다면 나는, 만약—” 그러고는 천천히, 살그머니, 곁눈질로 반쯤 비스듬히 바라보면서, 그는 장전된 머스킷총의 끝을 문에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“On this level, Ahab’s hammock swings within; his head this way.", "“이 높이쯤이면, 에이해브의 해먹이 저 안에서 흔들리고 있겠지. 머리가 이쪽을 향하고 있을 테고."], ["A touch, and Starbuck may survive to hug his wife and child again.—Oh Mary!", "한 번만 건드리면, 스타벅은 살아남아 아내와 자식을 다시 품에 안을 수 있으리라. 오 메리!"], ["Mary!—boy!", "메리! 아들아!"], ["boy!", "아들아!"], ["boy!—But if I wake thee not to death, old man, who can tell to what unsounded deeps Starbuck’s body this day week may sink, with all the crew!", "아들아! 하지만 노인이여, 내가 당신을 죽음으로 깨우지 않는다면, 이 주가 다 가기 전에 스타벅의 몸뚱이가 모든 선원과 함께 그 어떤 가늠할 길 없는 심연으로 가라앉을지, 그 누가 알 수 있겠는가!"], ["Great God, where art Thou?", "위대한 하느님, 당신은 어디 계십니까? 할까?"], ["Shall I?", "내가 할까?"], ["shall I?—The wind has gone down and shifted, sir; the fore and main topsails are reefed and set; she heads her course.”", "바람이 잦아들고 방향을 바꿨습니다, 선장님. 앞돛과 큰 윗돛은 폭을 줄여 펼쳤고, 배는 제 항로로 나아가고 있습니다.”"]], [["“Stern all!", "“전부 후진하라!"], ["Oh Moby Dick, I clutch thy heart at last!”", "오 모비 딕, 내 마침내 네 심장을 움켜쥐었노라!”"]], [["Such were the sounds that now came hurtling from out the old man’s tormented sleep, as if Starbuck’s voice had caused the long dumb dream to speak.", "이런 소리들이 이제 노인의 괴로움에 시달리는 잠 속에서 튀어나와 부딪쳐 왔으니, 마치 스타벅의 목소리가 그 오래도록 입 다물고 있던 꿈을 말하게 만들기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["The yet levelled musket shook like a drunkard’s arm against the panel; Starbuck seemed wrestling with an angel; but turning from the door, he placed the death-tube in its rack, and left the place.", "여전히 겨눠진 머스킷총이 술 취한 자의 팔처럼 문판자에 대고 떨렸다. 스타벅은 천사와 씨름하는 듯했다. 그러나 문에서 돌아서더니, 그는 그 죽음의 총신을 총가에 도로 꽂아 두고 그곳을 떠났다."]], [["“He’s too sound asleep, Mr.", "“저분은 너무 깊이 잠드셨네, 스터브 씨."], ["Stubb; go thou down, and wake him, and tell him.", "자네가 내려가서 깨워 드리고 알려 드리게."], ["I must see to the deck here.", "나는 여기 갑판을 살펴야 하니."], ["Thou know’st what to say.”", "무슨 말을 해야 할지는 자네가 알 테지.”"]], [["# CHAPTER 124. The Needle.", "# CHAPTER 124. The Needle."]], [["Next morning the not-yet-subsided sea rolled in long slow billows of mighty bulk, and striving in the Pequod’s gurgling track, pushed her on like giants’ palms outspread.", "이튿날 아침, 아직 잦아들지 않은 바다가 거대한 부피의 길고 느린 큰 물결로 굽이쳐 와, 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 콸콸거리는 항적 속에서 안간힘을 쓰며, 마치 거인들의 펼친 손바닥처럼 배를 앞으로 밀어 주었다."], ["The strong, unstaggering breeze abounded so, that sky and air seemed vast outbellying sails; the whole world boomed before the wind.", "강하고 흔들림 없는 바람이 어찌나 가득했던지, 하늘과 대기 전체가 거대하게 부풀어 오른 돛처럼 보였고, 온 세상이 바람결에 둥둥 울리며 나아갔다."], ["Muffled in the full morning light, the invisible sun was only known by the spread intensity of his place; where his bayonet rays moved on in stacks.", "환한 아침 빛에 감싸여 보이지 않는 태양은, 다만 그 자리에 퍼진 강렬한 밝기로써만 그 위치를 알 수 있었으니, 그곳에서는 총검 같은 햇살이 다발을 이루어 뻗어 나가고 있었다."], ["Emblazonings, as of crowned Babylonian kings and queens, reigned over everything.", "왕관을 쓴 바빌론의 왕들과 왕비들의 문장(紋章)처럼, 휘황한 빛깔이 만물 위에 군림했다."], ["The sea was as a crucible of molten gold, that bubblingly leaps with light and heat.", "바다는 빛과 열을 머금고 부글거리며 솟구치는 녹은 황금의 도가니와도 같았다."]], [["Long maintaining an enchanted silence, Ahab stood apart; and every time the tetering ship loweringly pitched down her bowsprit, he turned to eye the bright sun’s rays produced ahead; and when she profoundly settled by the stern, he turned behind, and saw the sun’s rearward place, and how the same yellow rays were blending with his undeviating wake.", "오랫동안 마법에 걸린 듯한 침묵을 지키며, 에이해브(Ahab)는 홀로 떨어져 서 있었다. 그리고 배가 시소처럼 흔들리며 뱃머리 사장(斜檣)을 무겁게 아래로 처박을 때마다, 그는 몸을 돌려 앞쪽으로 뻗어 나가는 밝은 햇살을 바라보았고, 배가 고물 쪽으로 깊이 가라앉을 때면, 뒤로 돌아 태양의 뒤편 자리를 바라보며, 똑같은 노란 햇살이 한 치도 어긋남 없는 항적과 어우러지는 모습을 지켜보았다."]], [["“Ha, ha, my ship!", "\"하, 하, 내 배여!"], ["thou mightest well be taken now for the sea-chariot of the sun.", "너는 지금 태양의 바다 전차(戰車)라 불려도 손색이 없겠구나."], ["Ho, ho!", "호, 호!"], ["all ye nations before my prow, I bring the sun to ye!", "내 뱃머리 앞의 온 나라들이여, 내가 너희에게 태양을 가져다주노라!"], ["Yoke on the further billows; hallo!", "저 먼 물결에 멍에를 씌워라. 어이!"], ["a tandem, I drive the sea!”", "두 마리 말을 앞뒤로 이어 매듯, 내가 바다를 몰고 가노라!\""]], [["But suddenly reined back by some counter thought, he hurried towards the helm, huskily demanding how the ship was heading.", "그러나 어떤 반대되는 생각에 문득 고삐를 당긴 듯, 그는 키 쪽으로 서둘러 다가가, 쉰 목소리로 배가 어느 방향으로 가고 있는지를 다그쳐 물었다."]], [["“East-sou-east, sir,”", "\"동남동, 동쪽에서 남쪽으로 치우친 방향입니다, 선장님.\""], ["said the frightened steersman.", "겁먹은 키잡이가 대답했다."]], [["“Thou liest!”", "\"거짓말 마라!\""], ["smiting him with his clenched fist.", "그는 움켜쥔 주먹으로 키잡이를 후려쳤다."], ["“Heading East at this hour in the morning, and the sun astern?”", "\"이 아침 시간에 동쪽으로 가는데, 태양이 고물 뒤에 있다고?\""]], [["Upon this every soul was confounded; for the phenomenon just then observed by Ahab had unaccountably escaped every one else; but its very blinding palpableness must have been the cause.", "이 말에 모든 이의 넋이 빠져나간 듯했다. 방금 에이해브가 알아챈 그 현상이 어찌 된 영문인지 다른 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 바로 그 눈멀 만큼 또렷한 명백함이야말로 그 까닭이었으리라."]], [["Thrusting his head half way into the binnacle, Ahab caught one glimpse of the compasses; his uplifted arm slowly fell; for a moment he almost seemed to stagger.", "머리를 나침반 함(函) 속에 절반쯤 들이밀고, 에이해브는 나침반들을 흘끗 보았다. 그러자 치켜들었던 그의 팔이 천천히 떨어졌고, 한순간 그는 거의 비틀거리는 듯 보였다."], ["Standing behind him Starbuck looked, and lo!", "그의 뒤에 서 있던 스타벅(Starbuck)이 들여다보니, 아니나 다를까!"], ["the two compasses pointed East, and the Pequod was as infallibly going West.", "두 나침반은 모두 동쪽을 가리키고 있었건만, 피쿼드호는 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 서쪽으로 나아가고 있었다."]], [["But ere the first wild alarm could get out abroad among the crew, the old man with a rigid laugh exclaimed, “I have it!", "그러나 첫 번째 거센 경악이 선원들 사이로 퍼져 나가기도 전에, 노인은 굳은 웃음을 지으며 외쳤다. \"알았다!"], ["It has happened before. Mr.", "전에도 이런 일이 있었다."], ["Starbuck, last night’s thunder turned our compasses—that’s all.", "스타벅, 간밤의 천둥이 우리 나침반을 돌려놓은 것뿐이다."], ["Thou hast before now heard of such a thing, I take it.”", "자네도 지금껏 이런 일을 들어 본 적이 있을 테지.\""]], [["“Aye; but never before has it happened to me, sir,”", "\"예. 하지만 제게는 한 번도 이런 일이 일어난 적이 없었습니다, 선장님.\""], ["said the pale mate, gloomily.", "창백한 일등 항해사가 침울하게 말했다."]], [["Here, it must needs be said, that accidents like this have in more than one case occurred to ships in violent storms.", "여기서 한마디 해 두어야 할 것은, 이와 같은 사고가 한두 번이 아니라 여러 차례, 거센 폭풍 속의 배들에게 일어났다는 사실이다. 뱃사람의 나침반 바늘에서 발현되는 자기(磁氣) 에너지는, 누구나 알다시피 하늘에서 보이는 전기와 본질적으로 하나다."], ["The magnetic energy, as developed in the mariner’s needle, is, as all know, essentially one with the electricity beheld in heaven; hence it is not to be much marvelled at, that such things should be.", "그러니 이런 일이 일어난다 한들 그리 놀랄 것은 못 된다. 번개가 실제로 배를 내리쳐 활대와 삭구 일부를 부러뜨린 경우에는, 바늘에 미친 영향이 때로 한층 더 치명적이었다."], ["Instances where the lightning has actually struck the vessel, so as to smite down some of the spars and rigging, the effect upon the needle has at times been still more fatal; all its loadstone virtue being annihilated, so that the before magnetic steel was of no more use than an old wife’s knitting needle.", "그 자석으로서의 효력이 송두리째 사라져, 전에는 자성을 띠었던 강철이 늙은 아낙의 뜨개바늘만큼도 쓸모없어진 것이다. 그러나 어느 경우든, 바늘은 그렇게 망가지거나 잃어버린 본래의 효력을 스스로의 힘으로 다시는 되찾지 못한다."], ["But in either case, the needle never again, of itself, recovers the original virtue thus marred or lost; and if the binnacle compasses be affected, the same fate reaches all the others that may be in the ship; even were the lowermost one inserted into the kelson.", "그리고 나침반 함의 나침반들이 영향을 받으면, 똑같은 운명이 배 안에 있을 법한 다른 모든 나침반에까지 미친다. 설령 가장 밑바닥, 용골판(龍骨板) 속에 박혀 있는 것이라 할지라도 그러하다."]], [["Deliberately standing before the binnacle, and eyeing the transpointed compasses, the old man, with the sharp of his extended hand, now took the precise bearing of the sun, and satisfied that the needles were exactly inverted, shouted out his orders for the ship’s course to be changed accordingly.", "나침반 함 앞에 신중하게 서서, 방향이 뒤바뀐 나침반들을 가만히 살피며, 노인은 이제 쭉 뻗은 손의 날을 세워 태양의 정확한 방위를 가늠하더니, 바늘들이 정확히 거꾸로 되어 있음을 확인하고는, 그에 따라 배의 침로를 바꾸라는 명령을 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["The yards were hard up; and once more the Pequod thrust her undaunted bows into the opposing wind, for the supposed fair one had only been juggling her.", "활대를 한껏 끌어올렸고, 피쿼드호는 또다시 그 굴함 없는 뱃머리를 맞바람 속으로 들이밀었다. 순풍인 줄 알았던 그 바람이 그저 배를 농락하고 있었을 따름이었으니."]], [["Meanwhile, whatever were his own secret thoughts, Starbuck said nothing, but quietly he issued all requisite orders; while Stubb and Flask—who in some small degree seemed then to be sharing his feelings—likewise unmurmuringly acquiesced.", "그동안 스타벅은 제 속내가 무엇이든 한마디도 하지 않은 채, 조용히 필요한 명령을 모두 내렸다. 한편 스텁(Stubb)과 플래스크(Flask)는, 그때만큼은 어느 정도 그의 심정을 함께 나누는 듯 보였는데, 마찬가지로 군말 없이 따랐다."], ["As for the men, though some of them lowly rumbled, their fear of Ahab was greater than their fear of Fate.", "선원들로 말하자면, 몇몇은 낮게 웅성거리기도 했으나, 에이해브에 대한 두려움이 운명에 대한 두려움보다 더 컸다."], ["But as ever before, the pagan harpooneers remained almost wholly unimpressed; or if impressed, it was only with a certain magnetism shot into their congenial hearts from inflexible Ahab’s.", "그러나 늘 그래 왔듯, 이교도 작살잡이들은 거의 아무런 동요도 보이지 않았다. 설령 동요했다 한들, 그것은 다만 굽힐 줄 모르는 에이해브의 마음에서 그들의 기질에 맞는 가슴속으로 쏘아 들어온 어떤 자력(磁力) 때문이었을 뿐이다."]], [["For a space the old man walked the deck in rolling reveries.", "한동안 노인은 굽이치는 상념에 잠겨 갑판을 거닐었다."], ["But chancing to slip with his ivory heel, he saw the crushed copper sight-tubes of the quadrant he had the day before dashed to the deck.", "그러다 우연히 상아 의족 뒤축이 미끄러졌을 때, 그는 전날 갑판에 내동댕이쳐 박살 내 버린 사분의(四分儀)의 으스러진 구리 조준관을 보았다."]], [["“Thou poor, proud heaven-gazer and sun’s pilot!", "\"가련하고도 오만하게 하늘을 우러르던 자여, 태양의 안내자여!"], ["yesterday I wrecked thee, and to-day the compasses would fain have wrecked me.", "어제는 내가 너를 부숴 버렸고, 오늘은 나침반들이 나를 부숴 버리려 들었구나."], ["So, so.", "그래, 그래."], ["But Ahab is lord over the level loadstone yet.", "하지만 에이해브는 아직 저 평평한 자석의 주인이다."], ["Mr. Starbuck—a lance without a pole; a top-maul, and the smallest of the sail-maker’s needles.", "스타벅, 자루 없는 창 한 자루, 윗활대 메, 그리고 돛 만드는 바늘 가운데 가장 작은 것을 가져오게."], ["Quick!”", "어서!\""]], [["Accessory, perhaps, to the impulse dictating the thing he was now about to do, were certain prudential motives, whose object might have been to revive the spirits of his crew by a stroke of his subtile skill, in a matter so wondrous as that of the inverted compasses.", "지금 그가 막 하려는 일을 부추긴 충동에는, 아마도 어떤 신중한 동기들이 곁들여 있었으리라. 그 동기의 목적인즉, 뒤집힌 나침반처럼 그토록 기묘한 사태를 두고 자신의 정묘한 솜씨를 한 번 펼쳐 보임으로써 선원들의 사기를 되살리려는 것이었을 터다."], ["Besides, the old man well knew that to steer by transpointed needles, though clumsily practicable, was not a thing to be passed over by superstitious sailors, without some shudderings and evil portents.", "게다가 노인은, 방향이 뒤바뀐 바늘로 키를 잡는 일이 비록 서투르게나마 가능하긴 해도, 미신에 사로잡힌 뱃사람들에게는 어떤 전율과 불길한 징조 없이 그냥 넘어갈 만한 일이 아님을 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["“Men,” said he, steadily turning upon the crew, as the mate handed him the things he had demanded, “my men, the thunder turned old Ahab’s needles; but out of this bit of steel Ahab can make one of his own, that will point as true as any.”", "\"제군들.\" 일등 항해사가 그가 요구한 물건들을 건네주자, 그는 침착하게 선원들을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"제군들, 천둥이 늙은 에이해브의 바늘들을 돌려놓았다. 하지만 이 강철 토막 하나로 에이해브는 자기만의 바늘을 만들 수 있다. 그것은 그 어떤 바늘 못지않게 정확히 가리킬 것이다.\""]], [["Abashed glances of servile wonder were exchanged by the sailors, as this was said; and with fascinated eyes they awaited whatever magic might follow.", "이 말이 떨어지자, 선원들 사이에는 비굴할 만큼 놀라워하면서도 무안해하는 눈길이 오갔다. 그리고 그들은 홀린 듯한 눈으로, 뒤이어 펼쳐질 어떤 마법이든 기다렸다."], ["But Starbuck looked away.", "그러나 스타벅은 시선을 돌렸다."]], [["With a blow from the top-maul Ahab knocked off the steel head of the lance, and then handing to the mate the long iron rod remaining, bade him hold it upright, without its touching the deck.", "에이해브는 윗활대 메를 한 번 내리쳐 창에서 강철 머리를 떼어 내고는, 남은 긴 쇠막대를 일등 항해사에게 건네며 갑판에 닿지 않게 똑바로 세워 잡으라고 일렀다."], ["Then, with the maul, after repeatedly smiting the upper end of this iron rod, he placed the blunted needle endwise on the top of it, and less strongly hammered that, several times, the mate still holding the rod as before.", "그러고 나서 메로 이 쇠막대 윗부분을 거듭 두들긴 뒤, 무뎌진 바늘을 그 꼭대기에 세로로 세워 놓고, 일등 항해사가 여전히 막대를 잡고 있는 채로, 그것을 좀 더 약하게 여러 차례 망치질했다. 그런 다음 그것을 들고 작고 기이한 동작 몇 가지를 했는데, 그것이 강철에 자성을 띠게 하는 데 꼭 필요한 것이었는지, 아니면 그저 선원들의 경외감을 부추기려는 것이었는지는 알 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Then going through some small strange motions with it—whether indispensable to the magnetizing of the steel, or merely intended to augment the awe of the crew, is uncertain—he called for linen thread; and moving to the binnacle, slipped out the two reversed needles there, and horizontally suspended the sail-needle by its middle, over one of the compass-cards.", "이윽고 그는 무명실을 가져오라 하더니, 나침반 함으로 다가가 그 안에 거꾸로 들어 있던 두 바늘을 빼내고, 돛바늘을 그 한가운데에 실을 매어 나침반 눈금판 하나 위에 수평으로 매달았다. 처음에는 강철 바늘이 양 끝을 떨고 진동하며 빙글빙글 돌았으나, 마침내 제자리에 멈추어 섰다."], ["At first, the steel went round and round, quivering and vibrating at either end; but at last it settled to its place, when Ahab, who had been intently watching for this result, stepped frankly back from the binnacle, and pointing his stretched arm towards it, exclaimed,—“Look ye, for yourselves, if Ahab be not lord of the level loadstone!", "이 결과를 골똘히 지켜보고 있던 에이해브는, 그러자 거리낌 없이 나침반 함에서 한 걸음 물러서더니, 뻗은 팔로 그것을 가리키며 외쳤다. \"직접들 보아라, 에이해브가 저 평평한 자석의 주인이 아닌지!"], ["The sun is East, and that compass swears it!”", "태양은 동쪽에 있는데, 저 나침반이 그렇다고 맹세하고 있지 않느냐!\""]], [["One after another they peered in, for nothing but their own eyes could persuade such ignorance as theirs, and one after another they slunk away.", "하나씩 차례로 그들은 안을 들여다보았다. 그들처럼 무지한 자들을 설득할 수 있는 것은 오직 제 눈뿐이었기 때문이다. 그리고 하나씩 차례로 그들은 슬그머니 물러갔다."]], [["In his fiery eyes of scorn and triumph, you then saw Ahab in all his fatal pride.", "경멸과 승리로 이글거리는 그 두 눈 속에서, 그때 그대들은 그 모든 치명적인 자긍심에 휩싸인 에이해브의 모습을 보았으리라."]], [["# CHAPTER 125. The Log and Line.", "# CHAPTER 125. The Log and Line."]], [["While now the fated Pequod had been so long afloat this voyage, the log and line had but very seldom been in use.", "운명 지어진 피쿼드(Pequod)호가 이번 항해에서 그토록 오래 바다 위를 떠다니는 동안, 측정줄과 측정기는 좀처럼 쓰인 적이 없었다."], ["Owing to a confident reliance upon other means of determining the vessel’s place, some merchantmen, and many whalemen, especially when cruising, wholly neglect to heave the log; though at the same time, and frequently more for form’s sake than anything else, regularly putting down upon the customary slate the course steered by the ship, as well as the presumed average rate of progression every hour.", "배의 위치를 정하는 다른 수단을 자신만만하게 의지한 탓에, 일부 상선들과 많은 포경선들은, 특히 순항할 때면 측정기를 던지는 일을 아예 소홀히 한다. 그러면서도 동시에, 다른 무엇보다 형식을 갖추기 위한 경우가 많지만, 늘 쓰던 석판 위에다 배가 잡은 항로와 매시간 추정한 평균 진행 속도를 규칙적으로 적어 두기는 한다."], ["It had been thus with the Pequod.", "피쿼드호의 사정도 그러했다."], ["The wooden reel and angular log attached hung, long untouched, just beneath the railing of the after bulwarks.", "나무로 된 얼레와 거기 매달린 모난 측정기는 오래도록 손길이 닿지 않은 채 고물 쪽 뱃전 난간 바로 아래에 걸려 있었다."], ["Rains and spray had damped it; sun and wind had warped it; all the elements had combined to rot a thing that hung so idly.", "비와 물보라가 그것을 적셨고, 햇볕과 바람이 그것을 뒤틀어 놓았으며, 그렇게 한가로이 매달려 있던 물건을 썩히려고 온갖 자연의 요소들이 한데 힘을 모았다."], ["But heedless of all this, his mood seized Ahab, as he happened to glance upon the reel, not many hours after the magnet scene, and he remembered how his quadrant was no more, and recalled his frantic oath about the level log and line.", "그러나 이 모든 것에 아랑곳없이, 자석 소동이 있은 지 몇 시간 지나지 않아 우연히 얼레로 눈길이 갔을 때, 에이해브(Ahab)는 어떤 기분에 사로잡혔고, 자신의 사분의(四分儀)가 이제 없다는 사실을 떠올렸으며, 측정기와 측정줄에 대해 미친 듯이 내뱉었던 맹세를 기억해 냈다."], ["The ship was sailing plungingly; astern the billows rolled in riots.", "배는 곤두박질치듯 항해하고 있었고, 고물 뒤로는 파도가 사납게 굽이쳤다."]], [["“Forward, there!", "\"이봐, 거기 앞쪽!"], ["Heave the log!”", "측정기를 던져라!\""]], [["Two seamen came.", "선원 둘이 왔다."], ["The golden-hued Tahitian and the grizzly Manxman.", "황금빛 살결의 타히티 사람과 백발이 성성한 맨섬 사람이었다."], ["“Take the reel, one of ye, I’ll heave.”", "\"너희 중 하나가 얼레를 잡아라. 던지는 건 내가 하겠다.\""]], [["They went towards the extreme stern, on the ship’s lee side, where the deck, with the oblique energy of the wind, was now almost dipping into the creamy, sidelong-rushing sea.", "그들은 배의 바람 아래쪽, 고물의 맨 끝으로 갔는데, 그곳은 바람의 비스듬한 기세에 밀려 갑판이 이제 거의 옆으로 우유처럼 들이닥치는 바다에 잠길 듯했다."]], [["The Manxman took the reel, and holding it high up, by the projecting handle-ends of the spindle, round which the spool of line revolved, so stood with the angular log hanging downwards, till Ahab advanced to him.", "맨섬 사람이 얼레를 잡고서, 줄을 감은 실패가 돌아가는 굴대의 양쪽 튀어나온 손잡이 끝을 쥐어 높이 치켜들고, 모난 측정기가 아래로 늘어진 채로 그렇게 서서 에이해브가 다가오기를 기다렸다."]], [["Ahab stood before him, and was lightly unwinding some thirty or forty turns to form a preliminary hand-coil to toss overboard, when the old Manxman, who was intently eyeing both him and the line, made bold to speak.", "에이해브가 그 앞에 서서, 바다로 던지기 전에 미리 한 묶음을 손에 사리려고 서른 바퀴인지 마흔 바퀴인지를 가볍게 풀어내고 있는데, 그와 줄을 골똘히 살펴보던 늙은 맨섬 사람이 용기를 내어 입을 열었다."]], [["“Sir, I mistrust it; this line looks far gone, long heat and wet have spoiled it.”", "\"선장님, 영 미덥지 못합니다. 이 줄은 너무 삭아 보입니다. 오랜 열기와 습기가 못 쓰게 만들어 놓았습니다.\""]], [["“’Twill hold, old gentleman.", "\"버텨 줄 걸세, 노인장."], ["Long heat and wet, have they spoiled thee?", "오랜 열기와 습기가 자네는 못 쓰게 만들었던가?"], ["Thou seem’st to hold.", "자네는 그래도 버티는 것 같은데."], ["Or, truer perhaps, life holds thee; not thou it.”", "아니, 어쩌면 더 맞는 말로는, 삶이 자네를 붙들고 있는 거겠지. 자네가 삶을 붙들고 있는 게 아니라.\""]], [["“I hold the spool, sir.", "\"저는 실패를 붙들고 있습니다요, 선장님."], ["But just as my captain says.", "하지만 그저 선장님 말씀대로 하지요."], ["With these grey hairs of mine ’tis not worth while disputing, ’specially with a superior, who’ll ne’er confess.”", "이 허연 머리칼을 이고서 따져 봐야 무슨 소용입니까. 더구나 결코 잘못을 인정하지 않을 윗사람과는 말입니다.\""]], [["“What’s that?", "\"그게 무슨 소린가?"], ["There now’s a patched professor in Queen Nature’s granite-founded College; but methinks he’s too subservient.", "여기 자연 여왕(Queen Nature)의 화강암으로 다져진 대학에 헝겊 기운 교수 하나가 있구나. 그런데 보아하니 지나치게 굽실거리는군."], ["Where wert thou born?”", "자넨 어디서 태어났는가?\""]], [["“In the little rocky Isle of Man, sir.”", "\"작고 바위투성이인 맨섬에서 태어났습니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“Excellent!", "\"훌륭하군!"], ["Thou’st hit the world by that.”", "그 한마디로 자네는 세상의 정곡을 찔렀어.\""]], [["“I know not, sir, but I was born there.”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다만, 저는 거기서 태어났습니다.\""]], [["“In the Isle of Man, hey?", "\"맨섬(Isle of Man)이라고?"], ["Well, the other way, it’s good.", "음, 거꾸로 보면 그것 참 그럴듯하군."], ["Here’s a man from Man; a man born in once independent Man, and now unmanned of Man; which is sucked in—by what?", "여기 맨(Man, 사람)에서 온 사람(man)이 있다. 한때 독립국이던 맨에서 태어났으나, 이제는 맨에게서 사내다움(man)을 빼앗긴 사람이라."], ["Up with the reel!", "그게 무엇한테 빨려 들어간 건가?"], ["The dead, blind wall butts all inquiring heads at last.", "얼레를 들어 올려라! 죽어 있는 눈먼 벽은 결국 캐묻는 모든 머리통을 들이받아 버리지."], ["Up with it!", "들어 올려라!"], ["So.”", "그래.\""]], [["The log was heaved.", "측정기가 던져졌다."], ["The loose coils rapidly straightened out in a long dragging line astern, and then, instantly, the reel began to whirl.", "느슨하게 사렸던 줄이 고물 뒤로 길게 끌리며 빠르게 펴졌고, 그러자 곧바로 얼레가 빙글빙글 돌기 시작했다."], ["In turn, jerkingly raised and lowered by the rolling billows, the towing resistance of the log caused the old reelman to stagger strangely.", "굽이치는 파도에 따라 측정기가 끌어당기는 저항이 들쭉날쭉 오르내리면서, 늙은 얼레잡이는 이상하게 비틀거렸다."]], [["“Hold hard!”", "\"꽉 붙들어라!\""]], [["Snap!", "탁!"], ["the overstrained line sagged down in one long festoon; the tugging log was gone.", "지나치게 팽팽해진 줄이 한 가닥 긴 꽃 장식처럼 축 늘어졌다. 끌려가던 측정기는 사라지고 없었다."]], [["“I crush the quadrant, the thunder turns the needles, and now the mad sea parts the log-line.", "\"내가 사분의를 박살내고, 천둥이 바늘을 뒤집어 놓더니, 이제는 미쳐 날뛰는 바다가 측정줄을 끊어 놓는구나."], ["But Ahab can mend all.", "그러나 에이해브는 무엇이든 고칠 수 있다."], ["Haul in here, Tahitian; reel up, Manxman.", "여기 줄을 끌어들여라, 타히티 사람아. 얼레를 감아라, 맨섬 사람아."], ["And look ye, let the carpenter make another log, and mend thou the line.", "그리고 잘 들어라, 목수더러 측정기를 새로 만들게 하고, 자네는 줄을 손보아라."], ["See to it.”", "잊지 말고.\""]], [["“There he goes now; to him nothing’s happened; but to me, the skewer seems loosening out of the middle of the world.", "\"저것 보게, 또 가 버리는군. 저 양반한테는 아무 일도 일어나지 않은 게야."], ["Haul in, haul in, Tahitian!", "그런데 나한테는 세상 한복판에 박힌 꼬챙이가 헐거워져 빠지는 것만 같으니."], ["These lines run whole, and whirling out: come in broken, and dragging slow.", "끌어들여라, 끌어들여, 타히티 사람아! 이 줄들은 멀쩡하게 풀려 빙글빙글 돌아 나가더니만, 끊어진 채 느릿느릿 끌려 들어오는구나."], ["Ha, Pip?", "하, 핍(Pip)이냐?"], ["come to help; eh, Pip?”", "도우러 왔구나, 응, 핍?\""]], [["“Pip?", "\"핍?"], ["whom call ye Pip?", "누구를 핍이라 부르는 거냐?"], ["Pip jumped from the whale-boat.", "핍은 포경 보트에서 뛰어내렸다."], ["Pip’s missing.", "핍은 행방불명이야."], ["Let’s see now if ye haven’t fished him up here, fisherman.", "어디 보자, 자네가 여기서 그 녀석을 낚아 올린 건 아닌지, 어부 양반."], ["It drags hard; I guess he’s holding on.", "묵직하게 끌리는군. 아무래도 그 녀석이 매달려 있는 모양이야."], ["Jerk him, Tahiti!", "흔들어 떼어 내라, 타히티!"], ["Jerk him off; we haul in no cowards here.", "흔들어 떨어뜨려. 여기서는 겁쟁이를 끌어올리지 않는다."], ["Ho!", "호!"], ["there’s his arm just breaking water.", "저기 그 녀석 팔이 막 물 위로 솟아오르는군."], ["A hatchet!", "손도끼다! 손도끼!"], ["a hatchet! cut it off—we haul in no cowards here.", "줄을 잘라 버려라 — 여기서는 겁쟁이를 끌어올리지 않으니까."], ["Captain Ahab!", "에이해브 선장!"], ["sir, sir!", "선장님, 선장님!"], ["here’s Pip, trying to get on board again.”", "여기 핍이 다시 배에 오르려고 하고 있습니다!\""]], [["“Peace, thou crazy loon,”", "\"닥쳐라, 이 미친 멍청이야.\""], ["cried the Manxman, seizing him by the arm.", "맨섬 사람이 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 소리쳤다."], ["“Away from the quarter-deck!”", "\"후갑판에서 썩 물러가!\""]], [["“The greater idiot ever scolds the lesser,”", "\"더 큰 천치가 늘 더 작은 천치를 꾸짖는 법이지.\""], ["muttered Ahab, advancing.", "에이해브가 다가서며 중얼거렸다."], ["“Hands off from that holiness!", "\"그 신성한 것에서 손을 떼라!"], ["Where sayest thou Pip was, boy?", "얘야, 핍이 어디 있다고 했느냐?\""]], [["“Astern there, sir, astern!", "\"저기 고물 쪽이요, 선장님, 고물 쪽! 보세요!"], ["Lo! lo!”", "보세요!\""]], [["“And who art thou, boy?", "\"그런데 얘야, 너는 누구냐?"], ["I see not my reflection in the vacant pupils of thy eyes.", "네 눈의 텅 빈 동공 속에 내 모습이 비치지 않는구나. 오 하느님(God)!"], ["Oh God! that man should be a thing for immortal souls to sieve through!", "사람이 불멸하는 영혼들이 그것을 통해 걸러져야 할 체 같은 것이라니!"], ["Who art thou, boy?”", "너는 누구냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“Bell-boy, sir; ship’s-crier; ding, dong, ding!", "\"종지기 소년입니다, 선장님. 배의 외치는 자이지요."], ["Pip!", "딩, 동, 딩!"], ["Pip!", "핍! 핍!"], ["Pip!", "핍!"], ["One hundred pounds of clay reward for Pip; five feet high—looks cowardly—quickest known by that!", "핍을 찾는 자에게 진흙 백 파운드 상금! 키는 다섯 자 — 겁쟁이처럼 생겼으니 — 그걸로 단박에 알아본다지!"], ["Ding, dong, ding!", "딩, 동, 딩!"], ["Who’s seen Pip the coward?”", "겁쟁이 핍을 본 사람 없소?\""]], [["“There can be no hearts above the snow-line.", "\"만년설 경계선 위에는 심장이 있을 수 없다."], ["Oh, ye frozen heavens!", "오, 너희 얼어붙은 하늘이여!"], ["look down here.", "여기 아래를 굽어보아라."], ["Ye did beget this luckless child, and have abandoned him, ye creative libertines.", "너희가 이 불운한 아이를 낳아 놓고는 버렸구나, 너희 창조의 난봉꾼들아. 이리 오너라, 얘야."], ["Here, boy; Ahab’s cabin shall be Pip’s home henceforth, while Ahab lives.", "에이해브가 사는 동안, 앞으로 에이해브의 선실이 핍의 집이 될 것이다."], ["Thou touchest my inmost centre, boy; thou art tied to me by cords woven of my heart-strings.", "너는 내 가장 깊은 중심을 건드리는구나, 얘야. 너는 내 심장의 현으로 짜인 끈으로 나와 묶여 있다."], ["Come, let’s down.”", "자, 내려가자.\""]], [["“What’s this?", "\"이게 뭐예요?"], ["here’s velvet shark-skin,”", "여기 보드라운 상어 가죽이 있네요.\""], ["intently gazing at Ahab’s hand, and feeling it.", "그는 에이해브의 손을 골똘히 들여다보며 만져 보았다."], ["“Ah, now, had poor Pip but felt so kind a thing as this, perhaps he had ne’er been lost!", "\"아, 이제, 가엾은 핍이 이토록 다정한 것을 한 번이라도 느껴 보았더라면, 어쩌면 영영 잃어버리지 않았을 텐데! 이건 제게는 사람을 붙드는 밧줄 같아 보입니다, 선장님."], ["This seems to me, sir, as a man-rope; something that weak souls may hold by.", "약한 영혼들이 붙잡을 수 있는 무언가 말이에요. 오, 선장님, 늙은 퍼스(Perth)를 불러다가 이 두 손을 단단히 박아 붙이게 해 주세요."], ["Oh, sir, let old Perth now come and rivet these two hands together; the black one with the white, for I will not let this go.”", "검은 손을 흰 손에다 말이에요. 저는 이걸 결코 놓지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, boy, nor will I thee, unless I should thereby drag thee to worse horrors than are here.", "\"오, 얘야, 나도 너를 놓지 않으마. 그렇게 함으로써 내가 너를 여기보다 더 끔찍한 공포 속으로 끌고 들어가는 게 아니라면 말이다."], ["Come, then, to my cabin. Lo!", "그러니 이리 오너라, 내 선실로."], ["ye believers in gods all goodness, and in man all ill, lo you!", "보라! 신들은 온통 선하고 사람은 온통 악하다고 믿는 너희여, 보라!"], ["see the omniscient gods oblivious of suffering man; and man, though idiotic, and knowing not what he does, yet full of the sweet things of love and gratitude.", "전지(全知)한 신들이 고통받는 인간을 잊고 있는 것을 보라. 그리고 인간은, 비록 천치이고 제가 무엇을 하는지조차 모르나, 그래도 사랑과 감사의 달콤한 것들로 가득 차 있는 것을 보라."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["I feel prouder leading thee by thy black hand, than though I grasped an Emperor’s!”", "황제의 손을 잡았을 때보다, 너의 검은 손을 잡고 이끄는 지금이 나는 더 자랑스럽구나!\""]], [["“There go two daft ones now,”", "\"저기 얼빠진 자가 둘이나 가는군.\""], ["muttered the old Manxman.", "늙은 맨섬 사람이 중얼거렸다."], ["“One daft with strength, the other daft with weakness.", "\"하나는 힘이 넘쳐 얼이 빠졌고, 다른 하나는 힘이 모자라 얼이 빠졌어."], ["But here’s the end of the rotten line—all dripping, too.", "그건 그렇고, 여기 삭은 줄의 끄트머리로구나 — 그것도 물이 뚝뚝 흐르는."], ["Mend it, eh?", "이걸 손보라고?"], ["I think we had best have a new line altogether.", "내 생각엔 줄을 통째로 새것으로 바꾸는 게 제일 낫겠는데."], ["I’ll see Mr. Stubb about it.”", "스터브(Stubb) 씨한테 가서 말해 봐야겠어.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 126. The Life-Buoy.", "# CHAPTER 126. The Life-Buoy."]], [["Steering now south-eastward by Ahab’s levelled steel, and her progress solely determined by Ahab’s level log and line; the Pequod held on her path towards the Equator.", "앞으로 향한 에이해브(Ahab)의 수평으로 겨눈 강철에 따라 이제 남동쪽으로 진로를 잡고, 그 항진을 오로지 에이해브의 수평 측정용 측정줄과 측정기에만 의지한 채, 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 적도를 향해 나아가는 길을 굳건히 지켰다."], ["Making so long a passage through such unfrequented waters, descrying no ships, and ere long, sideways impelled by unvarying trade winds, over waves monotonously mild; all these seemed the strange calm things preluding some riotous and desperate scene.", "그토록 인적 드문 바다를 그토록 오래 건너며, 단 한 척의 배도 보이지 않았고, 머지않아 변함없는 무역풍에 옆으로 떠밀려 단조롭도록 잔잔한 물결 위를 흘러가니, 이 모든 것은 어떤 격렬하고 절망적인 장면을 예고하는 기이한 고요처럼 보였다."]], [["At last, when the ship drew near to the outskirts, as it were, of the Equatorial fishing-ground, and in the deep darkness that goes before the dawn, was sailing by a cluster of rocky islets; the watch—then headed by Flask—was startled by a cry so plaintively wild and unearthly—like half-articulated wailings of the ghosts of all Herod’s murdered Innocents—that one and all, they started from their reveries, and for the space of some moments stood, or sat, or leaned all transfixedly listening, like the carved Roman slave, while that wild cry remained within hearing.", "마침내 배가 적도 어장의 이를테면 변두리에 가까워졌을 때, 새벽에 앞서는 짙은 어둠 속에서 바위투성이 작은 섬 무리 곁을 항해하고 있을 때, 그때 플라스크(Flask)가 이끌던 당직들은 한 울음소리에 소스라쳤다. 그것은 더없이 애처롭고 사납고 이 세상의 것 같지 않은 소리였으니, 헤롯에게 학살당한 무죄한 아기들의 유령이 채 말이 되지 못한 채 흐느끼는 듯한 그 소리에, 너나없이 모두가 몽상에서 화들짝 깨어나, 그 사나운 울음이 들리는 동안 내내, 새겨진 로마 노예상처럼 못 박힌 듯 서서, 혹은 앉아서, 혹은 기댄 채 귀 기울이고 있었다."], ["The Christian or civilized part of the crew said it was mermaids, and shuddered; but the pagan harpooneers remained unappalled.", "선원들 가운데 기독교를 믿거나 문명화된 자들은 그것이 인어라고 하며 몸서리쳤으나, 이교도 작살잡이들은 조금도 겁먹지 않았다."], ["Yet the grey Manxman—the oldest mariner of all—declared that the wild thrilling sounds that were heard, were the voices of newly drowned men in the sea.", "그런데 백발의 맨섬 사람, 곧 그들 중 가장 늙은 뱃사람은 들려온 그 사납고 오싹한 소리들이 방금 바다에 빠져 죽은 자들의 목소리라고 단언했다."]], [["Below in his hammock, Ahab did not hear of this till grey dawn, when he came to the deck; it was then recounted to him by Flask, not unaccompanied with hinted dark meanings.", "해먹 속 아래에 있던 에이해브는 잿빛 새벽이 되어 갑판으로 올라오고 나서야 비로소 이 일을 들었다. 그때 플라스크가 그 일을 들려주었는데, 어두운 의미가 은근히 깃들어 있지 않은 것은 아니었다."], ["He hollowly laughed, and thus explained the wonder.", "에이해브는 공허하게 웃고는, 그 괴이한 일을 이렇게 풀어 설명했다."]], [["Those rocky islands the ship had passed were the resort of great numbers of seals, and some young seals that had lost their dams, or some dams that had lost their cubs, must have risen nigh the ship and kept company with her, crying and sobbing with their human sort of wail.", "배가 지나온 그 바위섬들은 수많은 바다표범이 모여 사는 곳이어서, 어미를 잃은 어린 바다표범 몇 마리, 혹은 새끼를 잃은 어미 몇 마리가 배 가까이 떠올라 배를 따라오며, 사람 비슷한 울음으로 울고 흐느꼈으리라는 것이었다."], ["But this only the more affected some of them, because most mariners cherish a very superstitious feeling about seals, arising not only from their peculiar tones when in distress, but also from the human look of their round heads and semi-intelligent faces, seen peeringly uprising from the water alongside.", "그러나 이 설명은 도리어 그들 중 몇몇을 더욱 동요시켰으니, 대부분의 뱃사람은 바다표범에 대해 매우 미신적인 감정을 품고 있기 때문이었다. 그것은 그들이 곤경에 처했을 때 내는 독특한 소리 때문만이 아니라, 둥근 머리와 어딘가 반쯤 영리해 보이는 얼굴이 배 옆 물 위로 빠끔히 솟아오르며 사람 같은 인상을 풍기는 까닭이기도 했다."], ["In the sea, under certain circumstances, seals have more than once been mistaken for men.", "어떤 상황에서는 바다에서 바다표범이 사람으로 오인된 일이 한두 번이 아니었다."]], [["But the bodings of the crew were destined to receive a most plausible confirmation in the fate of one of their number that morning. At sun-rise this man went from his hammock to his mast-head at the fore; and whether it was that he was not yet half waked from his sleep (for sailors sometimes go aloft in a transition state), whether it was thus with the man, there is now no telling; but, be that as it may, he had not been long at his perch, when a cry was heard—a cry and a rushing—and looking up, they saw a falling phantom in the air; and looking down, a little tossed heap of white bubbles in the blue of the sea.", "그러나 선원들의 불길한 예감은 그날 아침 그들 중 한 사람의 운명으로 가장 그럴듯한 확증을 받을 운명이었다. 해 뜰 무렵 이 사람은 해먹에서 일어나 앞돛대 꼭대기의 망대로 올라갔다. 그가 아직 잠에서 반쯤도 깨지 못한 탓이었는지(뱃사람들은 더러 그런 몽롱한 상태로 돛대 위에 오르기도 하니까), 그가 정말 그러했는지는 이제 알 길이 없다. 그러나 어찌 되었든, 그가 망대에 오른 지 얼마 되지 않았을 때 한 외침이 들렸다. 외침과 함께 무언가 빠르게 떨어지는 소리가 들렸다. 위를 올려다보니 허공에서 떨어지는 유령 같은 형체가 보였고, 아래를 내려다보니 파란 바다 위에 작게 일렁이는 하얀 물거품 더미가 보였다."]], [["The life-buoy—a long slender cask—was dropped from the stern, where it always hung obedient to a cunning spring; but no hand rose to seize it, and the sun having long beat upon this cask it had shrunken, so that it slowly filled, and that parched wood also filled at its every pore; and the studded iron-bound cask followed the sailor to the bottom, as if to yield him his pillow, though in sooth but a hard one.", "구명부표, 곧 길고 가느다란 통 하나가 고물에서 떨어졌다. 그것은 늘 거기 매달려 정교한 용수철에 순순히 매여 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그것을 붙잡으려고 솟아오르는 손은 없었고, 오랫동안 햇볕에 달궈진 그 통은 쪼그라들어 천천히 물이 차올랐으며, 바싹 마른 그 나무는 구멍이란 구멍마다 물을 빨아들였다. 그리하여 쇠테를 두르고 못이 박힌 그 통은 마치 그에게 베개를, 비록 실은 딱딱한 베개에 불과할지언정 베개를 마련해 주려는 듯 그 뱃사람을 따라 바닥으로 가라앉았다."]], [["And thus the first man of the Pequod that mounted the mast to look out for the White Whale, on the White Whale’s own peculiar ground; that man was swallowed up in the deep.", "이리하여 흰 고래(White Whale)를 찾으려고, 그것도 흰 고래만의 독특한 영역에서 돛대에 오른 피쿼드호의 첫 사람이, 그 사람이 깊은 바닷속으로 삼켜졌다."], ["But few, perhaps, thought of that at the time.", "그러나 그때 그 일을 떠올린 자는 아마 거의 없었으리라."], ["Indeed, in some sort, they were not grieved at this event, at least as a portent; for they regarded it, not as a foreshadowing of evil in the future, but as the fulfilment of an evil already presaged.", "사실 어떤 의미에서 그들은 이 사건을 슬퍼하지 않았으니, 적어도 하나의 전조로서는 그러했다. 그들은 이를 앞날의 재앙을 예고하는 징조가 아니라, 이미 예고된 재앙이 이루어진 것으로 여겼기 때문이다."], ["They declared that now they knew the reason of those wild shrieks they had heard the night before.", "그들은 이제야 간밤에 들었던 그 사나운 비명의 까닭을 알게 되었다고 단언했다."], ["But again the old Manxman said nay.", "그러나 다시금 늙은 맨섬 사람은 아니라고 했다."]], [["The lost life-buoy was now to be replaced; Starbuck was directed to see to it; but as no cask of sufficient lightness could be found, and as in the feverish eagerness of what seemed the approaching crisis of the voyage, all hands were impatient of any toil but what was directly connected with its final end, whatever that might prove to be; therefore, they were going to leave the ship’s stern unprovided with a buoy, when by certain strange signs and inuendoes Queequeg hinted a hint concerning his coffin.", "이제 잃어버린 구명부표를 대신할 것을 마련해야 했다. 스타벅(Starbuck)이 그 일을 맡으라는 지시를 받았다. 그러나 충분히 가벼운 통을 찾을 수 없었고, 항해의 위기가 다가오는 듯한 그 열병 같은 조바심 속에서 모든 선원이, 그 끝이 무엇으로 드러나든 간에, 그 최후의 목적에 직접 연결되지 않은 어떤 수고도 견디지 못하던 터라, 그들은 배 고물에 부표를 마련해 두지 않은 채 내버려 두려 했다. 바로 그때 기묘한 몸짓과 넌지시 던지는 말로, 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)가 제 관(棺)에 관한 한 가지 암시를 흘렸다."]], [["“A life-buoy of a coffin!”", "\"관으로 만든 구명부표라니!\""], ["cried Starbuck, starting.", "스타벅이 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤다."]], [["“Rather queer, that, I should say,”", "\"그것참 좀 별난 일이로군.\""], ["said Stubb.", "스터브(Stubb)가 말했다."]], [["“It will make a good enough one,”", "\"꽤 쓸 만한 부표가 될 겁니다.\""], ["said Flask, “the carpenter here can arrange it easily.”", "플라스크가 말했다. \"여기 목수가 손쉽게 손볼 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Bring it up; there’s nothing else for it,”", "\"가져오게. 달리 방도가 없네.\""], ["said Starbuck, after a melancholy pause.", "스타벅이 침울하게 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다."], ["“Rig it, carpenter; do not look at me so—the coffin, I mean.", "\"그것을 손보게, 목수. 나를 그렇게 쳐다보지 말게 — 관 말일세."], ["Dost thou hear me?", "내 말 알아듣겠나?"], ["Rig it.”", "손보란 말이네.\""]], [["“And shall I nail down the lid, sir?”", "\"그러면 뚜껑에 못을 박을까요, 나리?\""], ["moving his hand as with a hammer.", "목수가 망치질을 하듯 손을 움직이며 물었다."]], [["“Aye.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“And shall I caulk the seams, sir?”", "\"그러고서 이음매를 메울까요, 나리?\""], ["moving his hand as with a caulking-iron.", "그가 뱃밥 메우는 인두를 쓰듯 손을 움직이며 물었다."]], [["“Aye.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“And shall I then pay over the same with pitch, sir?”", "\"그러면 그 위에 역청을 칠할까요, 나리?\""], ["moving his hand as with a pitch-pot.", "그가 역청 단지를 쓰듯 손을 움직이며 물었다."]], [["“Away!", "\"썩 물러가게!"], ["what possesses thee to this?", "무엇에 씌어 그러는 겐가?"], ["Make a life-buoy of the coffin, and no more.—Mr.", "관으로 구명부표나 만들고, 그 이상은 더 묻지 말게."], ["Stubb, Mr. Flask, come forward with me.”", "— 스터브 씨, 플라스크 씨, 나와 함께 앞으로 갑시다.\""]], [["“He goes off in a huff.", "\"성을 내며 가 버리는군."], ["The whole he can endure; at the parts he baulks.", "일 전체는 그가 견딜 수 있으면서, 부분 부분에서는 영 뒤로 빼지."], ["Now I don’t like this.", "자, 이건 영 마음에 안 들어."], ["I make a leg for Captain Ahab, and he wears it like a gentleman; but I make a bandbox for Queequeg, and he won’t put his head into it.", "에이해브 선장을 위해 다리를 만들어 드렸더니 그분은 신사처럼 차고 다니시는데, 퀴퀘그를 위해 상자 하나를 만들어 줬더니 그 친구는 그 안에 머리를 들이밀려 하질 않거든."], ["Are all my pains to go for nothing with that coffin?", "저 관에 들인 내 수고가 다 헛것이 되고 마는 건가?"], ["And now I’m ordered to make a life-buoy of it.", "그런데 이제는 그걸 구명부표로 만들라는 분부야. 이건 헌 외투를 뒤집는 것과 같아."], ["It’s like turning an old coat; going to bring the flesh on the other side now.", "이제 안감을 겉으로 끄집어내자는 격이지. 난 이런 땜장이 짓 같은 일이 싫어 — 도무지 싫단 말이야."], ["I don’t like this cobbling sort of business—I don’t like it at all; it’s undignified; it’s not my place.", "품위가 없어. 내 할 일이 아니라고."], ["Let tinkers’ brats do tinkerings; we are their betters.", "땜장이 새끼들이나 땜장이 일을 하라지. 우리는 그놈들보다 한 수 위니까."], ["I like to take in hand none but clean, virgin, fair-and-square mathematical jobs, something that regularly begins at the beginning, and is at the middle when midway, and comes to an end at the conclusion; not a cobbler’s job, that’s at an end in the middle, and at the beginning at the end.", "나는 깨끗하고, 처녀처럼 손때 안 묻고, 반듯반듯한 수학 같은 일만 손에 쥐고 싶어. 처음에서 규칙대로 시작해, 중간에 이르면 한가운데에 있고, 결말에 가서 끝나는 그런 일 말이야."], ["It’s the old woman’s tricks to be giving cobbling jobs. Lord!", "한가운데서 끝나고 끝에서 시작하는 땜장이 일 따위는 싫다고."], ["what an affection all old women have for tinkers.", "땜장이 일을 시키는 건 노파들이나 부리는 수작이야."], ["I know an old woman of sixty-five who ran away with a bald-headed young tinker once.", "원, 노파들은 어찌 그리도 하나같이 땜장이를 좋아하는지! 한번은 예순다섯 먹은 노파가 대머리 젊은 땜장이랑 눈이 맞아 달아난 일을 내 알고 있지."], ["And that’s the reason I never would work for lonely widow old women ashore, when I kept my job-shop in the Vineyard; they might have taken it into their lonely old heads to run off with me.", "그래서 나는 바인야드에서 일터를 꾸리던 시절에도, 뭍에서 외로운 과부 노파들 일은 결코 맡으려 하지 않았어. 그 외로운 늙은 머릿속에 나하고 달아날 궁리가 들어찰지도 모르니까."], ["But heigh-ho!", "하지만 에라, 모르겠다!"], ["there are no caps at sea but snow-caps.", "바다엔 눈으로 덮인 봉우리 말고는 늙은이도 없지."], ["Let me see.", "어디 보자."], ["Nail down the lid; caulk the seams; pay over the same with pitch; batten them down tight, and hang it with the snap-spring over the ship’s stern.", "뚜껑에 못을 박고, 이음매를 메우고, 그 위에 역청을 칠하고, 단단히 빗장을 질러 막은 다음, 배 고물에 용수철 고리를 달아 매단다."], ["Were ever such things done before with a coffin?", "관을 가지고 이런 짓을 해 본 자가 일찍이 있었던가?"], ["Some superstitious old carpenters, now, would be tied up in the rigging, ere they would do the job.", "요즘 같으면 미신에 사로잡힌 늙은 목수들은 이런 일을 하느니 차라리 삭구에 제 몸을 묶이는 편을 택할 테지."], ["But I’m made of knotty Aroostook hemlock; I don’t budge.", "하지만 나는 옹이투성이 애루스툭 솔송나무로 빚어진 사람이야. 난 끄떡없어."], ["Cruppered with a coffin!", "관을 엉덩이에 차고 다니다니!"], ["Sailing about with a grave-yard tray!", "묘지 쟁반을 싣고 항해를 하다니!"], ["But never mind.", "하지만 상관없어."], ["We workers in woods make bridal-bedsteads and card-tables, as well as coffins and hearses.", "우리 목수들은 관이며 영구차만 만드는 게 아니라 신방 침대며 카드놀이 탁자도 만든다고. 우리는 달삯으로 일하든, 일감 단위로 일하든, 이문을 보고 일하든 하는 것이지, 우리 일의 까닭이며 연유를 따지는 건 우리 몫이 아니야."], ["We work by the month, or by the job, or by the profit; not for us to ask the why and wherefore of our work, unless it be too confounded cobbling, and then we stash it if we can.", "너무나도 빌어먹을 땜장이 짓이 아닌 다음에야 말이지. 그럴 땐 할 수만 있으면 슬쩍 치워 버리는 거야."], ["Hem!", "흠!"], ["I’ll do the job, now, tenderly.", "이제 그 일을 정성껏 해 주지."], ["I’ll have me—let’s see—how many in the ship’s company, all told?", "어디 — 가만, 배에 탄 사람이 모두 몇이더라?"], ["But I’ve forgotten.", "아차, 잊어버렸군."], ["Any way, I’ll have me thirty separate, Turk’s-headed life-lines, each three feet long hanging all round to the coffin.", "어쨌든 터키 머리 매듭을 단 구명 밧줄을 서른 가닥 따로 마련해서, 저마다 석 자 길이로 관 둘레에 빙 둘러 늘어뜨리자."], ["Then, if the hull go down, there’ll be thirty lively fellows all fighting for one coffin, a sight not seen very often beneath the sun!", "그러면 배가 가라앉을 때 관 하나를 두고 서른 명의 팔팔한 녀석들이 서로 다투게 될 테니, 해 아래 그리 자주 보기 힘든 광경이렷다!"], ["Come hammer, caulking-iron, pitch-pot, and marling-spike!", "자, 망치야, 뱃밥 인두야, 역청 단지야, 밧줄용 송곳아, 다 이리 오너라!"], ["Let’s to it.”", "어디 한번 해 보자꾸나.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 127. The Deck.", "# CHAPTER 127. The Deck."]], [["The coffin laid upon two line-tubs, between the vice-bench and the open hatchway; the Carpenter caulking its seams; the string of twisted oakum slowly unwinding from a large roll of it placed in the bosom of his frock.—Ahab comes slowly from the cabin-gangway, and hears Pip following him.", "갑판. 관은 죔틀 작업대와 열린 승강구 사이, 밧줄통 두 개 위에 놓여 있다. 목수가 그 이음매에 뱃밥을 메워 넣는 중이고, 꼬인 뱃밥 끈은 그의 작업복 가슴팍에 끼워 둔 커다란 뭉치에서 천천히 풀려 나온다. 에이해브가 선실 통로에서 느릿느릿 걸어 나오다가, 핍이 자기를 따라오는 소리를 듣는다."]], [["“Back, lad; I will be with ye again presently.", "\"돌아가거라, 얘야. 곧 다시 너와 함께 있어 주마."], ["He goes!", "가는구나!"], ["Not this hand complies with my humor more genially than that boy.—Middle aisle of a church!", "저 아이만큼 내 기분을 살갑게 맞춰 주는 이 손은 없으렷다. 어느 교회의 가운뎃길 같구나!"], ["What’s here?”", "여기 이게 뭐냐?\""]], [["“Life-buoy, sir.", "\"구명부표입니다, 선장님."], ["Mr. Starbuck’s orders.", "스타벅 항해사님의 지시랍니다."], ["Oh, look, sir!", "아, 보세요, 선장님!"], ["Beware the hatchway!”", "승강구를 조심하세요!\""]], [["“Thank ye, man.", "\"고맙네, 이 사람아."], ["Thy coffin lies handy to the vault.”", "자네 관이 무덤 곁에 잘도 놓여 있구먼.\""]], [["“Sir?", "\"네?"], ["The hatchway?", "승강구 말씀입니까?"], ["oh!", "아!"], ["So it does, sir, so it does.”", "정말 그렇군요, 선장님, 정말 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Art not thou the leg-maker?", "\"자네가 그 다리 만든 자가 아닌가?"], ["Look, did not this stump come from thy shop?”", "보게, 이 의족이 자네 작업장에서 나온 게 아니더냐?\""]], [["“I believe it did, sir; does the ferrule stand, sir?”", "\"그런 것 같습니다, 선장님. 테두리 쇠는 잘 버티고 있는지요?\""]], [["“Well enough.", "\"그럭저럭 쓸 만하네."], ["But art thou not also the undertaker?”", "한데 자네는 장의사 노릇도 하는 게 아닌가?\""]], [["“Aye, sir; I patched up this thing here as a coffin for Queequeg; but they’ve set me now to turning it into something else.”", "\"그렇습니다, 선장님. 제가 여기 이걸 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)의 관으로 손봐 두었는데, 이제 와서 다른 물건으로 바꾸라 하시는군요.\""]], [["“Then tell me; art thou not an arrant, all-grasping, intermeddling, monopolising, heathenish old scamp, to be one day making legs, and the next day coffins to clap them in, and yet again life-buoys out of those same coffins?", "\"그렇다면 말해 보게. 자네야말로 손에 잡히는 건 죄다 거머쥐고, 남의 일에 끼어들고, 혼자 다 해 먹는 그 천하의 이교도 늙은 망나니가 아닌가?"], ["Thou art as unprincipled as the gods, and as much of a jack-of-all-trades.”", "하루는 다리를 만들고, 다음 날엔 그 다리를 처넣을 관을 만들고, 또 그다음엔 그 똑같은 관으로 구명부표를 만들어 내니 말이야. 자네는 신들만큼이나 원칙이 없고, 또 신들만큼이나 못 하는 일이 없는 만물박사로구나.\""]], [["“But I do not mean anything, sir.", "\"하지만 저는 아무 뜻도 없습니다, 선장님."], ["I do as I do.”", "그저 시키는 대로 할 뿐이지요.\""]], [["“The gods again.", "\"또 신들 얘기로구먼."], ["Hark ye, dost thou not ever sing working about a coffin?", "들어 보게, 자네는 관을 손볼 때 노래 한 자락 부르지 않는가?"], ["The Titans, they say, hummed snatches when chipping out the craters for volcanoes; and the grave-digger in the play sings, spade in hand.", "듣자 하니 티탄들은 화산의 분화구를 깎아 낼 때 곡조를 흥얼거렸다 하고, 연극에 나오는 무덤 파는 자도 삽을 손에 든 채 노래를 부른다네."], ["Dost thou never?”", "자네는 영 안 부르는가?\""]], [["“Sing, sir?", "\"노래 말입니까, 선장님?"], ["Do I sing?", "제가 노래를요?"], ["Oh, I’m indifferent enough, sir, for that; but the reason why the grave-digger made music must have been because there was none in his spade, sir.", "아, 그런 건 저야 아무래도 상관없습니다, 선장님. 하지만 무덤 파는 자가 가락을 낸 까닭은 분명 제 삽에는 가락이 없었기 때문일 겁니다, 선장님."], ["But the caulking mallet is full of it.", "한데 이 뱃밥 메우는 망치에는 가락이 그득합니다."], ["Hark to it.”", "들어 보십시오.\""]], [["“Aye, and that’s because the lid there’s a sounding-board; and what in all things makes the sounding-board is this—there’s naught beneath.", "\"그렇지, 그건 저 뚜껑이 울림판 노릇을 하기 때문이지. 그리고 무엇이든 울림판을 만드는 건 바로 이것일세 — 그 아래에 아무것도 없다는 것 말이야."], ["And yet, a coffin with a body in it rings pretty much the same, Carpenter.", "하지만 시신이 든 관도 거의 똑같은 소리를 낸다네, 목수."], ["Hast thou ever helped carry a bier, and heard the coffin knock against the churchyard gate, going in?", "자네는 상여를 메는 일을 거든 적이 있어, 관이 교회 묘지의 문에 부딪치며 안으로 들어갈 때 그 소리를 들어 본 적이 있는가?\""]], [["“Faith, sir, I’ve——”", "\"솔직히 말씀드려, 선장님, 제가——\""]], [["“Faith?", "\"솔직히?"], ["What’s that?”", "그게 무슨 말인가?\""]], [["“Why, faith, sir, it’s only a sort of exclamation-like—that’s all, sir.”", "\"아니, 그저, 선장님, 그건 그냥 일종의 감탄사 같은 겁니다 — 그게 전부입니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“Um, um; go on.”", "\"음, 음. 계속해 보게.\""]], [["“I was about to say, sir, that——”", "\"제가 말씀드리려던 건, 선장님, 그러니까——\""]], [["“Art thou a silk-worm?", "\"자네는 누에인가?"], ["Dost thou spin thy own shroud out of thyself?", "제 몸에서 제 수의(壽衣)를 자아내는 게야?"], ["Look at thy bosom!", "자네 가슴팍을 보게!"], ["Despatch!", "어서 서둘러!"], ["and get these traps out of sight.”", "이 잡동사니들을 눈앞에서 치워 버리게.\""]], [["“He goes aft.", "\"선장님이 고물 쪽으로 가시는군."], ["That was sudden, now; but squalls come sudden in hot latitudes.", "갑작스럽기도 하지. 하지만 무더운 위도에선 돌풍도 갑자기 몰아치는 법이지."], ["I’ve heard that the Isle of Albemarle, one of the Gallipagos, is cut by the Equator right in the middle.", "듣자 하니 갈라파고스 제도 가운데 하나인 앨버말섬은 적도가 그 한복판을 정확히 가른다더군."], ["Seems to me some sort of Equator cuts yon old man, too, right in his middle.", "내 보기엔 어떤 종류의 적도가 저 늙은 양반도 그 한복판을 정확히 가르고 있는 듯해."], ["He’s always under the Line—fiery hot, I tell ye!", "저이는 늘 적도선 아래 있단 말이야 — 불같이 뜨겁지, 암 그렇고말고!"], ["He’s looking this way—come, oakum; quick.", "이쪽을 보고 계시는군 — 자, 뱃밥아, 어서."], ["Here we go again.", "다시 시작하자."], ["This wooden mallet is the cork, and I’m the professor of musical glasses—tap, tap!”", "이 나무망치는 코르크 마개고, 나는 유리 악기를 다루는 명인이렷다 — 탁, 탁!\""]], [["(Ahab to himself.)", "(에이해브가 혼잣말로.)"]], [["“There’s a sight!", "\"저런 광경이라니!"], ["There’s a sound!", "저런 소리라니!"], ["The greyheaded woodpecker tapping the hollow tree!", "저 잿빛 머리 딱따구리가 텅 빈 나무를 쪼아 대고 있구나!"], ["Blind and dumb might well be envied now.", "지금이야말로 눈멀고 귀먹은 자가 부럽기 짝이 없다."], ["See!", "보라!"], ["that thing rests on two line-tubs, full of tow-lines.", "저 물건이 예인줄로 가득 찬 밧줄통 두 개 위에 놓여 있다."], ["A most malicious wag, that fellow.", "참으로 짓궂은 익살꾼이로구나, 저자는."], ["Rat-tat!", "따다닥!"], ["So man’s seconds tick!", "저렇게 사람의 초(秒)가 째깍거리는구나!"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["how immaterial are all materials!", "온갖 물질이란 게 얼마나 덧없는 것인가!"], ["What things real are there, but imponderable thoughts?", "가늠할 수 없는 사념(思念) 말고, 참으로 실재하는 것이 무어란 말인가?"], ["Here now’s the very dreaded symbol of grim death, by a mere hap, made the expressive sign of the help and hope of most endangered life.", "자, 이제 보라, 음산한 죽음의 더없이 무서운 상징이, 한낱 우연에 의해, 가장 위태로운 목숨을 돕고 그 희망을 일러 주는 표지가 되었구나."], ["A life-buoy of a coffin!", "관으로 만든 구명부표라니!"], ["Does it go further?", "이게 더 멀리까지 가는가?"], ["Can it be that in some spiritual sense the coffin is, after all, but an immortality-preserver!", "혹여 어떤 영적인 의미에서, 관이란 결국 불멸을 지켜 주는 것에 지나지 않는 게 아닐까!"], ["I’ll think of that. But no.", "그 점을 곰곰 생각해 봐야겠다."], ["So far gone am I in the dark side of earth, that its other side, the theoretic bright one, seems but uncertain twilight to me.", "아니, 그만두자. 나는 이 땅의 어두운 쪽으로 너무 깊이 와 버려, 그 반대쪽, 곧 이론상의 밝은 쪽은 내게 어슴푸레한 황혼으로밖에 보이지 않는구나."], ["Will ye never have done, Carpenter, with that accursed sound?", "목수, 자네는 그 저주받은 소리를 영영 멈추지 않을 작정인가? 나는 아래로 내려가네."], ["I go below; let me not see that thing here when I return again.", "내가 다시 돌아왔을 때 저 물건이 여기 있는 꼴은 보지 않게 해 주게."], ["Now, then, Pip, we’ll talk this over; I do suck most wondrous philosophies from thee!", "자, 그럼, 핍아, 우리 이 문제를 함께 이야기해 보자꾸나. 나는 네게서 더없이 놀라운 철학을 빨아들이고 있단다!"], ["Some unknown conduits from the unknown worlds must empty into thee!”", "알지 못하는 세계에서 흘러나오는 어떤 미지의 물길들이 분명 네 안으로 쏟아져 들고 있는 게야!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 128. The Pequod Meets The Rachel.", "# CHAPTER 128. The Pequod Meets The Rachel."]], [["Next day, a large ship, the Rachel, was descried, bearing directly down upon the Pequod, all her spars thickly clustering with men.", "이튿날, 큰 배 한 척, 곧 레이철호(Rachel)가 발견되었는데, 그 배는 곧장 피쿼드호(Pequod)를 향해 내려오고 있었고, 활대마다 사람들이 빽빽이 매달려 있었다."], ["At the time the Pequod was making good speed through the water; but as the broad-winged windward stranger shot nigh to her, the boastful sails all fell together as blank bladders that are burst, and all life fled from the smitten hull.", "그때 피쿼드호는 물살을 가르며 제법 빠른 속도로 나아가고 있었다. 그러나 바람을 안고 너른 날개를 펼친 그 낯선 배가 가까이 다가들자, 으스대던 돛들이 일제히 터진 오줌보처럼 푹 꺼졌고, 멈춰 선 그 선체에서는 모든 생기가 빠져나갔다."]], [["“Bad news; she brings bad news,”", "\"나쁜 소식이야. 저 배가 나쁜 소식을 가져오는군.\""], ["muttered the old Manxman.", "늙은 맨섬 사람이 중얼거렸다."], ["But ere her commander, who, with trumpet to mouth, stood up in his boat; ere he could hopefully hail, Ahab’s voice was heard.", "그러나 나팔을 입에 댄 채 자기 보트에 일어서 있던 그 배의 선장이 미처 부푼 마음으로 인사를 건네기도 전에, 에이해브(Ahab)의 목소리가 먼저 울렸다."]], [["“Hast seen the White Whale?”", "\"흰 고래(White Whale)를 보았소?\""]], [["“Aye, yesterday.", "\"그렇소, 어제 보았소."], ["Have ye seen a whale-boat adrift?”", "혹시 떠다니는 고래잡이 보트를 보지 못했소?\""]], [["Throttling his joy, Ahab negatively answered this unexpected question; and would then have fain boarded the stranger, when the stranger captain himself, having stopped his vessel’s way, was seen descending her side.", "기쁨을 목구멍 안으로 짓눌러 삼키며, 에이해브는 이 뜻밖의 물음에 보지 못했노라고 대답했다. 그러고는 기꺼이 그 낯선 배에 건너가 보려던 참인데, 낯선 배의 선장이 직접 자기 배의 진행을 멈추고서 뱃전을 타고 내려오는 모습이 보였다."], ["A few keen pulls, and his boat-hook soon clinched the Pequod’s main-chains, and he sprang to the deck.", "노를 몇 번 세차게 젓자, 그의 보트 갈고리가 어느새 피쿼드호의 주(主)사슬을 단단히 걸어챘고, 그는 갑판 위로 뛰어올랐다."], ["Immediately he was recognised by Ahab for a Nantucketer he knew.", "에이해브는 그를 보자마자 자기가 아는 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람임을 알아보았다."], ["But no formal salutation was exchanged.", "그러나 격식 차린 인사 따위는 오가지 않았다."]], [["“Where was he?—not killed!—not killed!”", "\"녀석이 어디 있었소? 죽지는 않았겠지!"], ["cried Ahab, closely advancing.", "죽지는 않았겠지!\" 에이해브는 바싹 다가서며 외쳤다."], ["“How was it?”", "\"어찌 된 일이오?\""]], [["It seemed that somewhat late on the afternoon of the day previous, while three of the stranger’s boats were engaged with a shoal of whales, which had led them some four or five miles from the ship; and while they were yet in swift chase to windward, the white hump and head of Moby Dick had suddenly loomed up out of the water, not very far to leeward; whereupon, the fourth rigged boat—a reserved one—had been instantly lowered in chase.", "전해진 사연인즉, 전날 오후 다소 늦은 무렵, 그 낯선 배의 보트 세 척이 고래 떼와 맞붙어 그것들에게 이끌려 배에서 사오 마일이나 떨어진 곳까지 가게 되었는데, 한창 바람 부는 쪽으로 빠르게 추격하던 중에, 모비 딕(Moby Dick)의 흰 혹과 머리가 그리 멀지 않은 바람 아래쪽 바다 위로 불쑥 솟아올랐다고 한다. 그리하여 네 번째로 채비된 보트, 곧 예비로 남겨 두었던 보트가 즉각 내려져 추격에 나섰다."], ["After a keen sail before the wind, this fourth boat—the swiftest keeled of all—seemed to have succeeded in fastening—at least, as well as the man at the mast-head could tell anything about it.", "바람을 등지고 한바탕 날렵하게 내달린 끝에, 모든 배 가운데 가장 빠른 용골을 지닌 이 네 번째 보트는 마침내 고래에 작살을 박아 넣은 듯했다. 적어도 돛대 꼭대기에 있던 사람이 알아볼 수 있는 한으로는 그러했다."], ["In the distance he saw the diminished dotted boat; and then a swift gleam of bubbling white water; and after that nothing more; whence it was concluded that the stricken whale must have indefinitely run away with his pursuers, as often happens.", "그는 멀리서 점점 작아지는, 점처럼 보이는 보트를 보았고, 이어 부글거리는 흰 물거품이 잠깐 번뜩이는 것을 보았으며, 그 뒤로는 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그리하여 흔히 그러하듯, 작살에 찔린 고래가 자기를 쫓는 자들을 끌고 끝없이 달아나 버린 모양이라고 짐작되었다."], ["There was some apprehension, but no positive alarm, as yet.", "약간의 불안은 있었으나, 아직 분명한 경보가 울린 것은 아니었다."], ["The recall signals were placed in the rigging; darkness came on; and forced to pick up her three far to windward boats—ere going in quest of the fourth one in the precisely opposite direction—the ship had not only been necessitated to leave that boat to its fate till near midnight, but, for the time, to increase her distance from it.", "귀선 신호기가 삭구에 내걸렸고, 이윽고 어둠이 깔렸다. 정반대 방향으로 떨어진 네 번째 보트를 찾으러 가기에 앞서 바람 부는 쪽 멀리 있던 세 척의 보트부터 먼저 거두어들여야 했으므로, 그 배는 부득이 자정 무렵까지 그 보트를 운명에 내맡겨 두어야 했을 뿐만 아니라, 그동안 오히려 그 보트와의 거리를 더 벌릴 수밖에 없었다."], ["But the rest of her crew being at last safe aboard, she crowded all sail—stunsail on stunsail—after the missing boat; kindling a fire in her try-pots for a beacon; and every other man aloft on the look-out.", "그러나 마침내 나머지 선원들을 무사히 갑판에 거두어들이자, 그 배는 사라진 보트를 뒤쫓아 돛이란 돛은 죄다 펼치고 — 보조돛 위에 또 보조돛을 — 한껏 내달았으며, 봉화로 삼고자 기름 솥에 불을 지피고, 그 밖의 모든 사람을 돛대 위에 올려 망을 보게 했다."], ["But though when she had thus sailed a sufficient distance to gain the presumed place of the absent ones when last seen; though she then paused to lower her spare boats to pull all around her; and not finding anything, had again dashed on; again paused, and lowered her boats; and though she had thus continued doing till daylight; yet not the least glimpse of the missing keel had been seen.", "그렇게 항해하여, 마지막으로 목격되었을 때 사라진 자들이 있었으리라 짐작되는 곳까지 넉넉히 다다랐으면서도, 그리고 거기서 잠시 멈춰 예비 보트들을 내려 사방을 샅샅이 뒤졌으면서도, 아무것도 찾지 못해 다시 내달았다가, 또 멈춰 보트를 내리기를 거듭하며 날이 밝을 때까지 그 짓을 계속했으면서도, 사라진 용골의 그림자조차 끝내 보지 못했다."]], [["The story told, the stranger Captain immediately went on to reveal his object in boarding the Pequod.", "이야기를 마친 낯선 선장은 곧바로 피쿼드호에 건너온 까닭을 털어놓기 시작했다."], ["He desired that ship to unite with his own in the search; by sailing over the sea some four or five miles apart, on parallel lines, and so sweeping a double horizon, as it were.", "그는 피쿼드호가 자기 배와 합세하여 함께 수색해 주기를, 곧 두 배가 사오 마일 간격을 두고 나란한 항로로 바다를 누비며 말하자면 이중의 수평선을 훑어 주기를 바랐다."]], [["“I will wager something now,” whispered Stubb to Flask, “that some one in that missing boat wore off that Captain’s best coat; mayhap, his watch—he’s so cursed anxious to get it back. Who ever heard of two pious whale-ships cruising after one missing whale-boat in the height of the whaling season? See, Flask, only see how pale he looks—pale in the very buttons of his eyes—look—it wasn’t the coat—it must have been the—”", "\"내가 지금 무언가 걸고 장담하겠는데,\" 스터브(Stubb)가 플래스크(Flask)에게 속삭였다. \"저 사라진 보트에 탄 누군가가 저 선장의 제일 좋은 외투를 입고 갔든지, 어쩌면 회중시계를 차고 갔든지 한 걸세. 저토록 미친 듯이 되찾으려 안달하는 걸 보면 말이야. 한창 포경철에 점잖은 고래잡이 배 두 척이 사라진 보트 하나를 찾자고 함께 누비고 다닌다는 소리를 누가 들어 봤겠나? 보게, 플래스크, 그저 저 핼쑥한 낯빛 좀 보라고. 눈동자 단추까지 새파랗게 질렸어. 보게나. 외투 따위가 아니었어. 분명 저건 그러니까……\""]], [["“My boy, my own boy is among them. For God’s sake—I beg, I conjure”—here exclaimed the stranger Captain to Ahab, who thus far had but icily received his petition. “For eight-and-forty hours let me charter your ship—I will gladly pay for it, and roundly pay for it—if there be no other way—for eight-and-forty hours only—only that—you must, oh, you must, and you shall do this thing.”", "\"내 아들, 내 친자식이 그 안에 있소. 제발 부탁이오. 간청하오. 비노니——\" 여기서 낯선 선장이 에이해브를 향해 외쳤다. 에이해브는 그때껏 그의 청을 그저 얼음장같이 받아넘기고 있었다. \"마흔여덟 시간만 당신 배를 빌려 주시오. 기꺼이 값을 치르겠소, 두둑이 치르리다. 달리 방도가 없다면 말이오. 그저 마흔여덟 시간만, 그것만이면 되오. 당신은 반드시, 오, 반드시 그래 줘야만 하오, 또 기어이 그리해 줄 것이오.\""]], [["“His son!”", "\"아들이라니!\""], ["cried Stubb, “oh, it’s his son he’s lost!", "스터브가 외쳤다. \"오, 잃어버린 게 바로 제 아들이었구나!"], ["I take back the coat and watch—what says Ahab?", "외투니 시계니 했던 말은 죄다 거두겠네. 에이해브는 뭐라 하시려나?"], ["We must save that boy.”", "우리는 그 아이를 구해야 해.\""]], [["“He’s drowned with the rest on ’em, last night,”", "\"그 애도 어젯밤 나머지 사람들과 함께 물에 빠져 죽었어.\" 그들 뒤에 서 있던 늙은 맨섬 선원이 말했다."], ["said the old Manx sailor standing behind them; “I heard; all of ye heard their spirits.”", "\"내 들었거든. 자네들도 그들의 혼령 소리를 들었잖나.\""]], [["Now, as it shortly turned out, what made this incident of the Rachel’s the more melancholy, was the circumstance, that not only was one of the Captain’s sons among the number of the missing boat’s crew; but among the number of the other boat’s crews, at the same time, but on the other hand, separated from the ship during the dark vicissitudes of the chase, there had been still another son; as that for a time, the wretched father was plunged to the bottom of the cruellest perplexity; which was only solved for him by his chief mate’s instinctively adopting the ordinary procedure of a whale-ship in such emergencies, that is, when placed between jeopardized but divided boats, always to pick up the majority first. But the captain, for some unknown constitutional reason, had refrained from mentioning all this, and not till forced to it by Ahab’s iciness did he allude to his one yet missing boy; a little lad, but twelve years old, whose father with the earnest but unmisgiving hardihood of a Nantucketer’s paternal love, had thus early sought to initiate him in the perils and wonders of a vocation almost immemorially the destiny of all his race. Nor does it unfrequently occur, that Nantucket captains will send a son of such tender age away from them, for a protracted three or four years’ voyage in some other ship than their own; so that their first knowledge of a whaleman’s career shall be unenervated by any chance display of a father’s natural but untimely partiality, or undue apprehensiveness and concern.", "그런데 곧 드러난 바이지만, 이 레이철호의 사건을 한층 더 비통하게 만든 것은 다음과 같은 사정이었다. 사라진 보트의 선원들 가운데 선장의 아들 하나가 있었을 뿐만 아니라, 같은 시각 다른 보트들의 선원들 가운데에도, 다만 정반대 편으로, 추격의 어두운 곡절 속에서 배와 떨어져 버린 또 하나의 아들이 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 한동안 가련한 아버지는 더없이 잔혹한 진퇴양난의 밑바닥으로 곤두박질쳤고, 이는 오직 그의 일등항해사가 그런 위급한 경우 고래잡이 배의 통상적인 절차, 곧 위태로운 처지에 빠졌으되 서로 갈라진 보트들 사이에 놓이면 언제나 다수가 탄 쪽부터 먼저 거두어들인다는 원칙을 본능적으로 따른 덕분에 비로소 풀렸다. 그러나 선장은 알 수 없는 천성의 이유로 이 모든 사정을 입에 올리기를 삼가고 있다가, 에이해브의 얼음장 같은 태도에 떠밀려서야 비로소 아직 행방이 묘연한 한 아들 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그 아이는 이제 겨우 열두 살짜리 어린 사내였는데, 그 아버지는 낸터킷 사람 특유의 부정(父情), 곧 간절하면서도 흔들림 없는 강인한 사랑으로, 거의 까마득한 옛날부터 자기 종족 모두의 숙명이 되어 온 그 직업의 위험과 경이 속으로 이토록 일찍부터 아들을 들여보내려 했던 것이다. 그리고 낸터킷의 선장들이 그토록 어린 아들을 자기 곁에서 떠나보내, 자기 배가 아닌 다른 어느 배에 태워 길게는 삼사 년에 이르는 항해에 내보내는 일도 드물지 않다. 이는 고래잡이 직업에 대한 아들의 첫 경험이, 아비의 자연스럽지만 때아닌 편애나 지나친 노심초사가 어쩌다 드러나는 일로 인해 무뎌지지 않도록 하려는 것이다."]], [["Meantime, now the stranger was still beseeching his poor boon of Ahab; and Ahab still stood like an anvil, receiving every shock, but without the least quivering of his own.", "그러는 동안에도, 낯선 선장은 여전히 그 가련한 청을 에이해브에게 애원하고 있었다. 그리고 에이해브는 여전히 모루처럼 우뚝 서서, 온갖 충격을 고스란히 받아 내면서도 제 몸은 조금도 떨지 않았다."]], [["“I will not go,” said the stranger, “till you say aye to me. Do to me as you would have me do to you in the like case. For you too have a boy, Captain Ahab—though but a child, and nestling safely at home now—a child of your old age too—Yes, yes, you relent; I see it—run, run, men, now, and stand by to square in the yards.”", "\"가지 않겠소.\" 낯선 선장이 말했다. \"당신이 내게 그러겠노라 말해 줄 때까지는. 같은 처지라면 내가 당신에게 해 주었으면 싶은 그대로 나에게 해 주시오. 당신에게도 아들이 있지 않소, 에이해브 선장. 비록 아직 어린아이이고, 지금쯤 집에서 포근히 잠들어 있겠지만 말이오. 게다가 당신 노년에 얻은 자식이 아니오. 그래, 그래, 당신 마음이 누그러지는구려. 내 눈에 보이오. 어서, 어서들, 자, 활대를 가로로 돌릴 채비를 하라!\""]], [["“Avast,”", "\"멈춰라!\" 에이해브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Ahab—“touch not a rope-yarn”; then in a voice that prolongingly moulded every word—“Captain Gardiner, I will not do it.", "\"밧줄 한 가닥도 건드리지 마라.\" 그러고는 한 마디 한 마디를 길게 빚어내는 목소리로 말을 이었다."], ["Even now I lose time.", "\"가디너 선장, 나는 그리할 수 없소. 지금 이 순간에도 나는 시간을 허비하고 있소."], ["Good-bye, good-bye.", "잘 가시오, 잘 가시오."], ["God bless ye, man, and may I forgive myself, but I must go.", "신께서 당신을 축복하시기를, 그리고 나 자신도 나를 용서할 수 있기를. 하지만 나는 가야만 하오."], ["Mr.", "스타벅(Starbuck) 군, 나침반함의 시계를 보게."], ["Starbuck, look at the binnacle watch, and in three minutes from this present instant warn off all strangers: then brace forward again, and let the ship sail as before.”", "그리고 지금 이 순간으로부터 삼 분 뒤에는 외부인을 모두 내보내게. 그런 뒤 다시 활대를 앞으로 돌려, 배가 전처럼 나아가도록 하게.\""]], [["Hurriedly turning, with averted face, he descended into his cabin, leaving the strange captain transfixed at this unconditional and utter rejection of his so earnest suit.", "서둘러 몸을 돌려 얼굴을 외면한 채, 그는 자기 선실로 내려가 버렸고, 그토록 간절한 청을 무조건적으로, 완전히 거절당한 낯선 선장은 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다."], ["But starting from his enchantment, Gardiner silently hurried to the side; more fell than stepped into his boat, and returned to his ship.", "그러나 가디너는 홀린 듯한 상태에서 퍼뜩 깨어나, 말없이 뱃전으로 황급히 다가갔다. 그러고는 자기 보트로 내려갔다기보다 차라리 굴러떨어지듯 몸을 던지더니, 제 배로 돌아갔다."]], [["Soon the two ships diverged their wakes; and long as the strange vessel was in view, she was seen to yaw hither and thither at every dark spot, however small, on the sea. This way and that her yards were swung round; starboard and larboard, she continued to tack; now she beat against a head sea; and again it pushed her before it; while all the while, her masts and yards were thickly clustered with men, as three tall cherry trees, when the boys are cherrying among the boughs.", "이윽고 두 배의 항적이 서로 갈라졌다. 그리고 그 낯선 배가 시야에 머무는 내내, 바다 위에 떠 있는 거뭇한 점이라면 아무리 작은 것이라도 하나하나 좇아 이리저리 뱃머리를 비트는 모습이 보였다. 이쪽으로 저쪽으로 활대가 휘둘러졌고, 우현으로 좌현으로 그 배는 끊임없이 침로를 바꾸었다. 이제는 맞바람 치는 바다를 거슬러 올라가다가, 또다시 그 물결에 떠밀려 내려가곤 했다. 그러는 내내, 그 배의 돛대와 활대마다 사람들이 빽빽이 매달려 있었으니, 마치 사내아이들이 가지 사이를 누비며 버찌를 따고 있는 키 큰 벚나무 세 그루 같았다."]], [["But by her still halting course and winding, woeful way, you plainly saw that this ship that so wept with spray, still remained without comfort.", "그러나 여전히 머뭇거리며 굽이굽이 구슬프게 나아가는 그 항로를 보면, 물보라로 그토록 흐느끼던 이 배가 여태껏 아무런 위로도 얻지 못했음이 또렷이 드러났다. 그 배는 곧 라헬이었다."], ["She was Rachel, weeping for her children, because they were not.", "자식들을 위해 우는 라헬, 자식들이 이제 더는 세상에 없는 까닭에 우는 라헬이었다. * 라헬: 구약 예레미야서의 한 구절에서 따온 것으로, 라헬은 죽은 자식들을 위해 울며 위로받기를 거부하는 어머니로 그려진다."]], [["# CHAPTER 129. The Cabin.", "# CHAPTER 129. The Cabin."]], [["(Ahab moving to go on deck; Pip catches him by the hand to follow.)", "장. 선실. (에이해브가 갑판으로 올라가려 움직이는데, 핍이 따라가려고 그의 손을 붙잡는다.)"]], [["“Lad, lad, I tell thee thou must not follow Ahab now.", "\"얘야, 얘야, 내 너에게 이르노니 너는 지금 에이해브를 따라와서는 안 된다."], ["The hour is coming when Ahab would not scare thee from him, yet would not have thee by him.", "에이해브가 너를 자기에게서 겁주어 쫓아내지는 않으나, 그렇다고 곁에 두지도 않으려는 때가 다가오고 있다."], ["There is that in thee, poor lad, which I feel too curing to my malady.", "가엾은 아이야, 네 안에는 내 병을 너무도 잘 낫게 하는 무언가가 있구나."], ["Like cures like; and for this hunt, my malady becomes my most desired health.", "같은 것이 같은 것을 고치는 법이니, 이 사냥을 위해서는 내 병이 내가 가장 바라는 건강이 되는 것이다."], ["Do thou abide below here, where they shall serve thee, as if thou wert the captain.", "너는 여기 아래에 머물러라, 마치 네가 선장이라도 된 양 사람들이 너를 섬길 것이다."], ["Aye, lad, thou shalt sit here in my own screwed chair; another screw to it, thou must be.”", "그래, 얘야, 너는 여기 내 나사 박힌 의자에 앉으렴. 너 또한 그 의자에 박힌 또 하나의 나사가 되어야 하느니라.\""]], [["“No, no, no! ye have not a whole body, sir; do ye but use poor me for your one lost leg; only tread upon me, sir; I ask no more, so I remain a part of ye.”", "\"안 됩니다, 안 돼요, 안 돼요! 선장님은 온전한 몸이 아니시잖아요. 그저 이 가엾은 저를 잃어버린 한쪽 다리 삼아 쓰세요. 그저 저를 밟고 디디기만 하세요, 선장님. 그것밖에 청하지 않을 테니, 그러면 저는 선장님의 일부로 남는 거예요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["spite of million villains, this makes me a bigot in the fadeless fidelity of man!—and a black!", "수백만의 악당이 있다 한들, 이 아이는 나로 하여금 인간의 시들지 않는 충심을 맹신하게 만드는구나! 게다가 흑인이고!"], ["and crazy!—but methinks like-cures-like applies to him too; he grows so sane again.”", "게다가 미친 아이가! 허나 같은 것이 같은 것을 고친다는 말은 이 아이에게도 들어맞는 듯하니, 이렇게 다시 제정신을 찾아 가는구나.\""]], [["“They tell me, sir, that Stubb did once desert poor little Pip, whose drowned bones now show white, for all the blackness of his living skin.", "\"사람들이 제게 말하기를, 선장님, 한번은 스터브가 이 가엾은 어린 핍을 버렸다 하더군요. 살아 있는 살갗은 온통 검은데도, 물에 빠진 그 뼈는 이제 하얗게 드러나는 그 핍을 말이에요."], ["But I will never desert ye, sir, as Stubb did him.", "하지만 저는 결코 스터브가 그랬듯 선장님을 버리지 않을 거예요, 선장님."], ["Sir, I must go with ye.”", "선장님, 저는 선장님과 함께 가야만 해요.\""]], [["“If thou speakest thus to me much more, Ahab’s purpose keels up in him.", "\"네가 내게 그런 말을 더 늘어놓으면, 에이해브의 결심이 그의 안에서 용골이 뒤집히듯 뒤집히고 만다."], ["I tell thee no; it cannot be.”", "안 된다고 이르지 않느냐. 그리될 수는 없다.\""]], [["“Oh good master, master, master!", "\"오, 착하신 주인님, 주인님, 주인님!\""]], [["“Weep so, and I will murder thee!", "\"그렇게 울거라, 그러면 내가 너를 죽여 버릴 테다!"], ["have a care, for Ahab too is mad.", "조심해라, 에이해브 또한 미쳐 있으니."], ["Listen, and thou wilt often hear my ivory foot upon the deck, and still know that I am there.", "귀를 기울이거라, 그러면 내 상아 다리가 갑판을 디디는 소리를 자주 듣게 될 것이고, 그래도 내가 거기 있음을 알게 되리라."], ["And now I quit thee.", "그리고 이제 나는 너를 떠난다."], ["Thy hand!—Met!", "네 손을!—맞잡았다!"], ["True art thou, lad, as the circumference to its centre.", "너는 참되구나, 얘야, 원둘레가 그 중심에 충실하듯이."], ["So: God for ever bless thee; and if it come to that,—God for ever save thee, let what will befall.”", "그러니 하느님께서 영원토록 너를 축복하시기를. 그리고 만일 그 지경에 이른다면,—무슨 일이 닥치든, 하느님께서 영원토록 너를 구원하시기를.\""]], [["(Ahab goes; Pip steps one step forward.)", "(에이해브가 떠난다. 핍이 한 걸음 앞으로 나선다.)"]], [["“Here he this instant stood; I stand in his air,—but I’m alone.", "\"여기 그분이 방금 서 계셨지. 나는 그분이 남긴 공기 속에 서 있건만,—나는 혼자구나."], ["Now were even poor Pip here I could endure it, but he’s missing.", "지금 가엾은 핍이라도 여기 있다면 견딜 수 있으련만, 그 애가 보이질 않아."], ["Pip!", "핍!"], ["Pip!", "핍!"], ["Ding, dong, ding!", "딩, 동, 딩!"], ["Who’s seen Pip?", "누가 핍을 봤나요?"], ["He must be up here; let’s try the door.", "그 애는 여기 위에 있을 거야. 문을 한번 열어 보자."], ["What?", "뭐야?"], ["neither lock, nor bolt, nor bar; and yet there’s no opening it.", "자물쇠도, 빗장도, 가로대도 없는데, 그런데도 열리질 않네. 이건 분명 주문(呪文) 탓이야."], ["It must be the spell; he told me to stay here: Aye, and told me this screwed chair was mine.", "그분이 내게 여기 머물라 하셨지. 그래, 그리고 이 나사 박힌 의자가 내 것이라 하셨지."], ["Here, then, I’ll seat me, against the transom, in the ship’s full middle, all her keel and her three masts before me.", "그렇다면 여기, 나는 고물 가로목에 등을 기대고 앉으리라, 배의 한복판에, 그 용골과 세 개의 돛대를 모두 내 앞에 두고서."], ["Here, our old sailors say, in their black seventy-fours great admirals sometimes sit at table, and lord it over rows of captains and lieutenants.", "우리 늙은 선원들이 말하기를, 그들의 검은 칠십사문 군함에서는 위대한 제독들이 이따금 식탁에 앉아, 줄지어 늘어선 함장들과 부관들 위에 군림한다지."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["what’s this?", "이게 뭐지?"], ["epaulets!", "견장이다!"], ["epaulets!", "견장이야!"], ["the epaulets all come crowding!", "견장들이 죄다 몰려오는구나! 술병들을 돌려라."], ["Pass round the decanters; glad to see ye; fill up, monsieurs!", "그대들을 만나니 반갑소. 가득 채우시오, 신사 여러분!"], ["What an odd feeling, now, when a black boy’s host to white men with gold lace upon their coats!—Monsieurs, have ye seen one Pip?—a little negro lad, five feet high, hang-dog look, and cowardly!", "검은 소년이 금몰을 단 외투를 걸친 백인들의 주인 노릇을 하다니, 참으로 묘한 기분이로구나!—신사 여러분, 핍이라는 아이를 보셨소?—키가 5피트(약 1.5미터)에, 풀죽은 낯빛을 하고, 겁쟁이인 어린 흑인 소년 말이오!"], ["Jumped from a whale-boat once;—seen him? No!", "한번은 포경 보트에서 뛰어내린 적이 있지요.—그 애를 보셨소?"], ["Well then, fill up again, captains, and let’s drink shame upon all cowards!", "못 보셨다고! 그렇다면 다시 채우시오, 함장들이여, 그리고 모든 겁쟁이들에게 부끄러움이 있으라 건배합시다!"], ["I name no names.", "이름은 대지 않겠소."], ["Shame upon them!", "그들에게 부끄러움이 있으라!"], ["Put one foot upon the table.", "한 발을 식탁 위에 올려놓으시오."], ["Shame upon all cowards.—Hist!", "모든 겁쟁이들에게 부끄러움이 있으라.—쉿!"], ["above there, I hear ivory—Oh, master!", "저 위에서, 상아 소리가 들린다—오, 주인님!"], ["master!", "주인님!"], ["I am indeed down-hearted when you walk over me.", "당신이 제 머리 위를 걸으실 때면 저는 정말 마음이 무겁습니다."], ["But here I’ll stay, though this stern strikes rocks; and they bulge through; and oysters come to join me.”", "하지만 저는 여기 머물겠어요, 이 고물이 바위에 부딪쳐 깨지고, 그 틈으로 물이 불룩 밀려들어, 굴들이 저와 함께하러 몰려온다 해도.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 130. The Hat.", "# CHAPTER 130. The Hat."]], [["And now that at the proper time and place, after so long and wide a preliminary cruise, Ahab,—all other whaling waters swept—seemed to have chased his foe into an ocean-fold, to slay him the more securely there; now, that he found himself hard by the very latitude and longitude where his tormenting wound had been inflicted; now that a vessel had been spoken which on the very day preceding had actually encountered Moby Dick;—and now that all his successive meetings with various ships contrastingly concurred to show the demoniac indifference with which the white whale tore his hunters, whether sinning or sinned against; now it was that there lurked a something in the old man’s eyes, which it was hardly sufferable for feeble souls to see. As the unsetting polar star, which through the livelong, arctic, six months’ night sustains its piercing, steady, central gaze; so Ahab’s purpose now fixedly gleamed down upon the constant midnight of the gloomy crew. It domineered above them so, that all their bodings, doubts, misgivings, fears, were fain to hide beneath their souls, and not sprout forth a single spear or leaf.", "모자. 이제 그토록 길고 광범위한 예비 항해를 거친 끝에, 마땅한 때와 장소에 이르러, 에이해브는—다른 모든 고래잡이 해역을 샅샅이 훑은 뒤—적을 어느 대양의 후미진 구석으로 몰아넣어 그곳에서 한층 더 확실하게 죽이려는 듯했다. 이제 그는 자신을 괴롭히던 상처가 새겨졌던 바로 그 위도와 경도의 지척에 와 있음을 깨달았다. 이제 바로 전날 실제로 모비 딕(Moby Dick)과 마주쳤던 한 배와 말을 나눈 터였다. 그리고 이제 여러 배들과 잇따라 만나면서 알게 된 사실들이 대조적으로 한데 모여, 그 흰 고래가 자기를 사냥하는 자들을—그들이 죄를 짓는 쪽이든 죄를 당하는 쪽이든 가리지 않고—찢어발기던 악마 같은 무심함을 드러내 보였다. 이제 노인의 두 눈 속에는 무언가가 도사리고 있었으니, 나약한 영혼들이 차마 견디며 바라보기 힘든 것이었다. 기나긴 북극의 여섯 달 밤 내내 그 꿰뚫는 듯한 한결같은 중심의 시선을 지켜 내는, 결코 지지 않는 북극성처럼, 에이해브의 목적도 이제 음울한 선원들의 변함없는 한밤중을 향해 붙박인 채 빛을 쏟아 내렸다. 그것이 그들 위로 어찌나 군림했던지, 그들의 모든 불길한 예감과 의심과 불안과 두려움은 영혼 밑바닥에 숨어들어, 단 한 개의 창끝이나 잎사귀조차 내밀지 못하고 말았다."]], [["In this foreshadowing interval too, all humor, forced or natural, vanished.", "이렇게 무언가를 예고하는 이 막간(幕間)에는 억지로 짜낸 것이든 자연스러운 것이든 모든 익살도 사라졌다."], ["Stubb no more strove to raise a smile; Starbuck no more strove to check one.", "스터브(Stubb)는 더 이상 웃음을 자아내려 애쓰지 않았고, 스타벅(Starbuck)도 더 이상 그것을 막으려 들지 않았다."], ["Alike, joy and sorrow, hope and fear, seemed ground to finest dust, and powdered, for the time, in the clamped mortar of Ahab’s iron soul.", "기쁨과 슬픔, 희망과 두려움이 한결같이 더없이 고운 가루로 빻아져, 그때만큼은 에이해브의 무쇠 같은 영혼이라는 단단히 죈 절구 속에서 가루가 되어 버린 듯했다."], ["Like machines, they dumbly moved about the deck, ever conscious that the old man’s despot eye was on them.", "기계처럼, 그들은 말없이 갑판 위를 오갔으니, 노인의 폭군 같은 눈이 늘 자신들을 향해 있음을 의식하고 있었다."]], [["But did you deeply scan him in his more secret confidential hours; when he thought no glance but one was on him; then you would have seen that even as Ahab’s eyes so awed the crew’s, the inscrutable Parsee’s glance awed his; or somehow, at least, in some wild way, at times affected it.", "그러나 그의 더 은밀하고 사적인 시간을 깊이 들여다보았다면, 곧 그가 단 하나의 시선 외에는 누구의 눈길도 자신에게 닿지 않는다고 여겼던 그 순간을 살펴보았다면, 에이해브의 눈이 선원들의 눈을 그토록 위압했듯이, 그 헤아릴 길 없는 파시교도(Parsee)의 눈길은 에이해브의 눈을 위압했음을, 또는 적어도 어떤 식으로든, 어떤 기이한 방식으로 이따금 그것에 영향을 미쳤음을 보았을 것이다."], ["Such an added, gliding strangeness began to invest the thin Fedallah now; such ceaseless shudderings shook him; that the men looked dubious at him; half uncertain, as it seemed, whether indeed he were a mortal substance, or else a tremulous shadow cast upon the deck by some unseen being’s body.", "이제 그 야윈 페달라(Fedallah)에게는 그처럼 한층 더해진, 미끄러지듯 스며드는 기이함이 깃들기 시작했고, 그처럼 끊임없는 전율이 그를 뒤흔들었기에, 사람들은 그를 미심쩍은 눈으로 바라보았으니, 그가 정말로 죽을 운명을 지닌 살아 있는 존재인지, 아니면 보이지 않는 어떤 존재의 몸뚱이가 갑판 위에 드리운 떨리는 그림자인지를 반쯤 확신하지 못하는 듯했다."], ["And that shadow was always hovering there.", "그리고 그 그림자는 늘 그 자리를 맴돌고 있었다."], ["For not by night, even, had Fedallah ever certainly been known to slumber, or go below.", "페달라가 밤이라 한들 잠을 자거나 아래로 내려가는 모습은 단 한 번도 확실하게 목격된 바 없었기 때문이다."], ["He would stand still for hours: but never sat or leaned; his wan but wondrous eyes did plainly say—We two watchmen never rest.", "그는 몇 시간이고 가만히 서 있곤 했지만, 결코 앉거나 기대지 않았다. 그의 핏기 없으나 경이로운 두 눈은 분명히 이렇게 말하고 있었다—우리 두 파수꾼은 결코 쉬지 않는다."]], [["Nor, at any time, by night or day could the mariners now step upon the deck, unless Ahab was before them; either standing in his pivot-hole, or exactly pacing the planks between two undeviating limits,—the main-mast and the mizen; or else they saw him standing in the cabin-scuttle,—his living foot advanced upon the deck, as if to step; his hat slouched heavily over his eyes; so that however motionless he stood, however the days and nights were added on, that he had not swung in his hammock; yet hidden beneath that slouching hat, they could never tell unerringly whether, for all this, his eyes were really closed at times; or whether he was still intently scanning them; no matter, though he stood so in the scuttle for a whole hour on the stretch, and the unheeded night-damp gathered in beads of dew upon that stone-carved coat and hat. The clothes that the night had wet, the next day’s sunshine dried upon him; and so, day after day, and night after night; he went no more beneath the planks; whatever he wanted from the cabin that thing he sent for.", "또한 어느 때든, 밤이든 낮이든, 에이해브가 그들 앞에 있지 않은 한 선원들은 이제 갑판에 발을 디딜 수 없었으니, 그는 자신의 회전축 구멍에 서 있거나, 큰 돛대와 뒷돛대라는 두 개의 어김없는 한계 사이의 널빤지를 정확히 오가며 거닐었고, 그렇지 않으면 그가 선실 승강구에 서 있는 모습이 보였다. 그의 살아 있는 발은 마치 한 걸음 내디디려는 듯 갑판 위로 내밀어져 있었고, 모자는 두 눈 위로 푹 눌러쓴 채였다. 그래서 그가 아무리 미동도 없이 서 있더라도, 날이 가고 밤이 거듭되어 그가 해먹에 몸을 누인 적이 없을지언정, 그 푹 눌러쓴 모자 밑에 가려진 까닭에, 사람들은 이 모든 와중에 그의 두 눈이 이따금 정말로 감겨 있는지, 아니면 여전히 자신들을 뚫어지게 살피고 있는지를 결코 틀림없이 알아낼 수 없었다. 그가 한 시간 내내 줄곧 승강구에 그렇게 서 있고, 아랑곳하지 않는 밤이슬이 그 돌로 깎아 놓은 듯한 외투와 모자 위에 구슬방울로 맺혀도 그것은 상관없었다. 밤이 적셔 놓은 옷은 이튿날의 햇살이 그의 몸 위에서 말려 주었으니, 그렇게 날이 가고 또 가고, 밤이 가고 또 가도, 그는 더 이상 갑판 널빤지 아래로 내려가지 않았다. 선실에서 무언가가 필요하면 그것을 가져오라고 사람을 시켰다."]], [["He ate in the same open air; that is, his two only meals,—breakfast and dinner: supper he never touched; nor reaped his beard; which darkly grew all gnarled, as unearthed roots of trees blown over, which still grow idly on at naked base, though perished in the upper verdure.", "그는 같은 바깥 공기 속에서 식사를 했으니, 곧 그가 드는 단 두 끼니인 아침과 저녁이었다. 밤참에는 결코 손대지 않았고, 수염도 깎지 않았다."], ["But though his whole life was now become one watch on deck; and though the Parsee’s mystic watch was without intermission as his own; yet these two never seemed to speak—one man to the other—unless at long intervals some passing unmomentous matter made it necessary.", "그 수염은 마치 바람에 뽑혀 넘어진 나무의 드러난 뿌리가, 윗부분의 푸르름은 시들어 죽었어도 헐벗은 밑동에서 하릴없이 계속 자라나듯, 거무스름하게 온통 뒤엉킨 채 자라났다. 그러나 비록 그의 온 삶이 이제 갑판 위에서의 한 번의 불침번이 되어 버렸고, 또 파시교도의 신비로운 불침번도 그 자신의 것과 마찬가지로 한 치의 끊김도 없었을지언정, 이 두 사람은—한 사람이 다른 사람에게—결코 말을 나누는 법이 없는 듯했으니, 오랜 간격을 두고 지나가는 사소한 일이 그것을 불가피하게 만들 때를 제외하고는 그러했다."], ["Though such a potent spell seemed secretly to join the twain; openly, and to the awe-struck crew, they seemed pole-like asunder.", "비록 그처럼 강력한 마법이 은밀히 그 둘을 이어 주는 듯했으나, 겉으로 드러나기로는, 그리고 외경에 사로잡힌 선원들에게는, 둘이 양극처럼 멀리 떨어져 있는 듯 보였다."], ["If by day they chanced to speak one word; by night, dumb men were both, so far as concerned the slightest verbal interchange.", "만일 낮에 그들이 우연히 한마디라도 나눈다면, 밤에는 둘 다 벙어리가 되어, 아주 사소한 말 한 마디 주고받는 일조차 없었다."], ["At times, for longest hours, without a single hail, they stood far parted in the starlight; Ahab in his scuttle, the Parsee by the mainmast; but still fixedly gazing upon each other; as if in the Parsee Ahab saw his forethrown shadow, in Ahab the Parsee his abandoned substance.", "이따금 더없이 긴 시간 동안, 단 한 번의 부름도 없이, 그들은 별빛 속에 멀리 떨어져 서 있었으니, 에이해브는 자신의 승강구에, 파시교도는 큰 돛대 곁에 있었으나, 그래도 서로를 붙박인 듯 응시하고 있었다. 마치 에이해브는 파시교도 속에서 자신이 앞으로 드리운 그림자를 보고, 파시교도는 에이해브 속에서 자신이 버린 실체를 보는 듯했다."]], [["And yet, somehow, did Ahab—in his own proper self, as daily, hourly, and every instant, commandingly revealed to his subordinates,—Ahab seemed an independent lord; the Parsee but his slave.", "그러면서도 어쩐 일인지, 에이해브는—나날이, 매시간, 매 순간 자신의 부하들에게 위엄 있게 드러나는 그 본연의 모습으로—독립된 군주처럼 보였고, 파시교도는 다만 그의 노예처럼 보였다."], ["Still again both seemed yoked together, and an unseen tyrant driving them; the lean shade siding the solid rib.", "그런가 하면 또다시 둘은 한 멍에에 함께 매여 있고, 보이지 않는 폭군이 그들을 몰아가는 듯했으니, 야윈 그림자가 단단한 갈빗대 곁에 붙어 있는 형국이었다."], ["For be this Parsee what he may, all rib and keel was solid Ahab.", "이 파시교도가 무엇이든 간에, 온통 갈빗대요 용골인 것은 단단한 에이해브였기 때문이다."]], [["At the first faintest glimmering of the dawn, his iron voice was heard from aft,—“Man the mast-heads!”—and all through the day, till after sunset and after twilight, the same voice every hour, at the striking of the helmsman’s bell, was heard—“What d’ye see?—sharp!", "새벽의 가장 희미한 첫 어스름이 비칠 무렵이면, 그의 무쇠 같은 목소리가 고물 쪽에서 들려왔다—\"돛대 꼭대기로 올라가라!\" 그리고 온종일, 해가 진 뒤에도, 황혼이 진 뒤에도, 키잡이의 종이 울릴 때마다 매시간 똑같은 목소리가 들려왔다—\"무엇이 보이느냐?—똑똑히!"], ["sharp!”", "똑똑히 보라!\""]], [["But when three or four days had slided by, after meeting the children-seeking Rachel; and no spout had yet been seen; the monomaniac old man seemed distrustful of his crew’s fidelity; at least, of nearly all except the Pagan harpooneers; he seemed to doubt, even, whether Stubb and Flask might not willingly overlook the sight he sought.", "그러나 아이들을 찾아 헤매는 레이철호와 마주친 뒤로 사나흘이 미끄러지듯 흘러갔는데도 아직 물줄기 하나 보이지 않자, 편집광에 사로잡힌 노인은 선원들의 충실함을, 적어도 이교도 작살잡이들을 제외한 거의 모두의 충실함을 미덥지 않게 여기는 듯했다. 심지어 스터브와 플래스크(Flask)마저도 자기가 찾는 그 광경을 일부러 못 본 체 넘기지나 않을까 의심하는 듯했다."], ["But if these suspicions were really his, he sagaciously refrained from verbally expressing them, however his actions might seem to hint them.", "그러나 이런 의심이 정말로 그의 것이었다 한들, 그의 행동이 그것을 넌지시 내비치는 듯 보일지언정, 그는 슬기롭게도 그것을 말로 드러내기를 삼갔다."]], [["“I will have the first sight of the whale myself,”—he said.", "\"고래를 맨 처음 보는 것은 내가 직접 하겠다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Aye!", "\"그렇고말고!"], ["Ahab must have the doubloon!”", "에이해브가 그 금화를 차지해야지!\""], ["and with his own hands he rigged a nest of basketed bowlines; and sending a hand aloft, with a single sheaved block, to secure to the main-mast head, he received the two ends of the downward-reeved rope; and attaching one to his basket prepared a pin for the other end, in order to fasten it at the rail.", "그러고는 제 손으로 바구니처럼 엮은 밧줄 둥지를 만들었다. 그리고 한 선원을 위로 올려 보내 도르래 하나가 달린 활차를 큰 돛대 꼭대기에 고정하게 한 다음, 아래로 꿰어 내린 밧줄의 두 끝을 받아 들었다."], ["This done, with that end yet in his hand and standing beside the pin, he looked round upon his crew, sweeping from one to the other; pausing his glance long upon Daggoo, Queequeg, Tashtego; but shunning Fedallah; and then settling his firm relying eye upon the chief mate, said,—“Take the rope, sir—I give it into thy hands, Starbuck.”", "그러고는 한쪽 끝을 자기 바구니에 매고, 다른 쪽 끝은 난간에 붙들어 매려고 고정 핀을 마련했다. 이 일을 마치고, 그 끝을 아직 손에 쥔 채 핀 곁에 선 그는 선원들을 둘러보며 한 사람에게서 다른 사람에게로 시선을 옮겼고, 다구(Daggoo)와 퀴퀘그(Queequeg)와 타슈테고(Tashtego)에게는 한참 동안 눈길을 멈추었으나 페달라는 피했다."], ["Then arranging his person in the basket, he gave the word for them to hoist him to his perch, Starbuck being the one who secured the rope at last; and afterwards stood near it.", "그러고는 굳건히 믿음을 거는 눈길을 일등항해사에게 머무르며 말했다—\"이 밧줄을 잡게, 자네—내 그것을 자네 손에 맡기네, 스타벅.\" 그런 다음 바구니 안에 몸을 자리 잡고, 자기를 그 횃대까지 끌어 올리라고 명령을 내리니, 마침내 밧줄을 단단히 붙들어 맨 자는 스타벅이었고, 그 후로 그는 밧줄 곁에 서 있었다."], ["And thus, with one hand clinging round the royal mast, Ahab gazed abroad upon the sea for miles and miles,—ahead, astern, this side, and that,—within the wide expanded circle commanded at so great a height.", "그리하여 한 손으로 가장 높은 돛대를 부둥켜안은 채, 에이해브는 그토록 높은 곳에서 한눈에 담기는 그 넓게 펼쳐진 원 안의 바다를 몇 마일이고 몇 마일이고 멀리 내다보았다—앞으로, 뒤로, 이쪽으로, 저쪽으로."]], [["When in working with his hands at some lofty almost isolated place in the rigging, which chances to afford no foothold, the sailor at sea is hoisted up to that spot, and sustained there by the rope; under these circumstances, its fastened end on deck is always given in strict charge to some one man who has the special watch of it.", "선상에서 선원이 삭구(索具)의 어느 높고 거의 외따로 떨어진, 발 디딜 곳이라곤 없는 자리에서 손으로 일을 할 때면, 그는 밧줄에 매달려 그 자리까지 끌어 올려진 채 거기 지탱된다. 이런 경우에 갑판 위에서 그 밧줄을 붙들어 맨 끝은 늘 그것을 특별히 지키는 한 사람에게 엄중히 맡겨진다."], ["Because in such a wilderness of running rigging, whose various different relations aloft cannot always be infallibly discerned by what is seen of them at the deck; and when the deck-ends of these ropes are being every few minutes cast down from the fastenings, it would be but a natural fatality, if, unprovided with a constant watchman, the hoisted sailor should by some carelessness of the crew be cast adrift and fall all swooping to the sea.", "이는 그처럼 어지러이 뒤얽힌 동삭(動索)의 황야에서는, 위쪽에 매여 있는 갖가지 다른 연결 관계를 갑판에서 보이는 것만으로는 늘 어김없이 분간할 수가 없고, 또 이런 밧줄들의 갑판 쪽 끝이 몇 분마다 한 번씩 매듭에서 풀려 내던져지는 까닭에, 만일 변함없는 파수꾼을 두지 않는다면, 끌어 올려진 선원이 선원들의 부주의로 인해 떨어져 나가 바다로 곤두박질쳐 떨어지는 일이 그저 자연스러운 운명이 되고 말 것이기 때문이다. 그러므로 이 일에서 에이해브가 취한 조치는 별스러운 것이 아니었다."], ["So Ahab’s proceedings in this matter were not unusual; the only strange thing about them seemed to be, that Starbuck, almost the one only man who had ever ventured to oppose him with anything in the slightest degree approaching to decision—one of those too, whose faithfulness on the look-out he had seemed to doubt somewhat;—it was strange, that this was the very man he should select for his watchman; freely giving his whole life into such an otherwise distrusted person’s hands.", "다만 한 가지 기이해 보이는 점이 있었으니, 그것은 일찍이 그에게 조금이라도 결연함에 가까운 무언가로 감히 맞서 본 거의 유일한 사람이자—더구나 망보는 일에 대한 충실함을 그가 다소 의심하는 듯했던 바로 그 사람 가운데 하나인—스타벅을, 그가 바로 자신의 파수꾼으로 골랐다는 점이었다. 그토록 미덥지 않게 여기던 그런 사람의 손에 제 목숨을 통째로 선뜻 맡긴 것이다."]], [["Now, the first time Ahab was perched aloft; ere he had been there ten minutes; one of those red-billed savage sea-hawks which so often fly incommodiously close round the manned mast-heads of whalemen in these latitudes; one of these birds came wheeling and screaming round his head in a maze of untrackably swift circlings.", "자, 에이해브가 처음으로 높은 곳에 올라앉았을 때, 그가 거기 올라간 지 십 분도 채 되지 않아, 이 위도에서 고래잡이배의 사람 탄 돛대 꼭대기 둘레를 거추장스러울 만큼 가까이 날아다니기 일쑤인 붉은 부리의 사나운 바다매 가운데 한 마리가, 그의 머리 둘레를 종잡을 수 없이 빠른 원을 그리며 어지러이 빙빙 돌면서 끽끽 울어 댔다."], ["Then it darted a thousand feet straight up into the air; then spiralized downwards, and went eddying again round his head.", "그러더니 그것은 곧장 천 피트나 허공으로 솟구쳐 올랐다가, 다시 나선을 그리며 내려와 그의 머리 둘레를 소용돌이치듯 맴돌았다."]], [["But with his gaze fixed upon the dim and distant horizon, Ahab seemed not to mark this wild bird; nor, indeed, would any one else have marked it much, it being no uncommon circumstance; only now almost the least heedful eye seemed to see some sort of cunning meaning in almost every sight.", "그러나 어슴푸레한 먼 수평선에 시선을 못 박은 채, 에이해브는 이 사나운 새를 알아채지 못하는 듯했다. 사실 다른 어느 누구도 그것을 그다지 눈여겨보지 않았을 터이니, 그것은 드문 일이 아니었기 때문이다. 다만 이제는 거의 그 무엇도 마음에 두지 않는 눈조차도 거의 모든 광경 속에서 어떤 교묘한 의미를 읽어 내는 듯했다."]], [["“Your hat, your hat, sir!”", "\"모자, 모자입니다, 선장님!\""], ["suddenly cried the Sicilian seaman, who being posted at the mizen-mast-head, stood directly behind Ahab, though somewhat lower than his level, and with a deep gulf of air dividing them.", "시칠리아 출신 선원이 별안간 외쳤으니, 그는 뒷돛대 꼭대기에 배치되어 에이해브의 바로 뒤쪽에, 다만 그보다는 다소 낮은 자리에 깊은 허공의 골을 사이에 두고 서 있었다."]], [["But already the sable wing was before the old man’s eyes; the long hooked bill at his head: with a scream, the black hawk darted away with his prize.", "그러나 이미 그 검은 날개는 노인의 두 눈앞에 와 있었고, 길고 갈고리진 부리는 그의 머리께에 닿아 있었다. 끽끽 한 번 울고는, 그 검은 매가 제 노획물을 채어 쏜살같이 날아가 버렸다."]], [["An eagle flew thrice round Tarquin’s head, removing his cap to replace it, and thereupon Tanaquil, his wife, declared that Tarquin would be king of Rome.", "독수리 한 마리가 타르퀴니우스의 머리 둘레를 세 바퀴 돌며 그의 모자를 벗겼다가 다시 씌워 주었고, 그러자 그의 아내 타나퀼은 타르퀴니우스가 로마의 왕이 되리라 선언했다. 그러나 그 징조가 길조로 여겨진 것은 오로지 모자를 도로 씌워 준 까닭이었다."], ["But only by the replacing of the cap was that omen accounted good.", "에이해브의 모자는 결코 되돌아오지 않았으니, 그 사나운 매는 그것을 물고 자꾸자꾸 날아갔다."], ["Ahab’s hat was never restored; the wild hawk flew on and on with it; far in advance of the prow: and at last disappeared; while from the point of that disappearance, a minute black spot was dimly discerned, falling from that vast height into the sea.", "뱃머리보다 훨씬 멀리 앞서 가더니, 마침내 사라져 버렸다. 그리고 그것이 사라진 지점에서, 작디작은 검은 점 하나가 그 까마득한 높이에서 바다로 떨어지는 것이 어렴풋이 보였다."]], [["# CHAPTER 131. The Pequod Meets The Delight.", "# CHAPTER 131. The Pequod Meets The Delight."]], [["The intense Pequod sailed on; the rolling waves and days went by; the life-buoy-coffin still lightly swung; and another ship, most miserably misnamed the Delight, was descried. As she drew nigh, all eyes were fixed upon her broad beams, called shears, which, in some whaling-ships, cross the quarter-deck at the height of eight or nine feet; serving to carry the spare, unrigged, or disabled boats.", "격렬한 피쿼드(Pequod)호는 계속 나아갔다. 일렁이는 파도와 나날이 지나갔다. 구명부표 겸 관(棺)은 여전히 가볍게 흔들렸고, 또 다른 배 한 척이 시야에 들어왔는데, 더없이 가련하게도 딜라이트(Delight)호라는 어울리지 않는 이름을 단 배였다. 그 배가 가까이 다가오자, 모두의 시선이 시어즈라 불리는 그 넓은 가로대에 쏠렸다. 어떤 포경선에서는 이 가로대가 8피트나 9피트(약 2.4~2.7미터) 높이로 뒷갑판을 가로질러, 예비용으로 비치하거나 삭구를 풀어 둔, 또는 못 쓰게 된 보트들을 얹어 두는 데 쓰인다."]], [["Upon the stranger’s shears were beheld the shattered, white ribs, and some few splintered planks, of what had once been a whale-boat; but you now saw through this wreck, as plainly as you see through the peeled, half-unhinged, and bleaching skeleton of a horse.", "낯선 배의 가로대 위에는 한때 포경 보트였던 것의 산산이 부서진 하얀 갈빗대와, 몇 조각 쪼개진 널빤지가 얹혀 있었다. 그러나 그 잔해 너머로는, 마치 살이 벗겨지고 반쯤 어긋난 채 허옇게 바래 가는 말의 골격을 들여다보듯, 또렷이 속이 들여다보였다."]], [["“Hast seen the White Whale?”", "\"흰고래를 본 적 있소?\""]], [["“Look!”", "\"보시오!\""], ["replied the hollow-cheeked captain from his taffrail; and with his trumpet he pointed to the wreck.", "고물 난간에서 두 볼이 움푹 꺼진 선장이 대답했다. 그러고는 자신의 나팔로 그 잔해를 가리켰다."]], [["“Hast killed him?”", "\"그놈을 죽였소?\""]], [["“The harpoon is not yet forged that ever will do that,”", "\"그 일을 해낼 작살은 아직 벼려지지 않았소.\""], ["answered the other, sadly glancing upon a rounded hammock on the deck, whose gathered sides some noiseless sailors were busy in sewing together.", "상대편이 대답하며, 갑판 위에 둥글게 묶인 해먹을 슬픈 눈길로 흘긋 보았는데, 그 모아 놓은 양옆을 몇몇 말없는 선원들이 분주히 꿰매고 있었다."]], [["“Not forged!”", "\"벼려지지 않았다고!\""], ["and snatching Perth’s levelled iron from the crotch, Ahab held it out, exclaiming—“Look ye, Nantucketer; here in this hand I hold his death!", "에이해브(Ahab)는 보트 받침대에서 퍼스(Perth)가 다듬어 놓은 작살을 낚아채더니, 그것을 쑥 내밀며 소리쳤다. \"보시오, 낸터킷(Nantucket) 사람이여."], ["Tempered in blood, and tempered by lightning are these barbs; and I swear to temper them triply in that hot place behind the fin, where the White Whale most feels his accursed life!”", "바로 이 손에 그놈의 죽음을 쥐고 있소이다! 피로 담금질하고, 번개로 담금질한 이 미늘들을, 나는 흰고래가 그 저주받은 목숨을 가장 절실히 느끼는 곳, 곧 지느러미 뒤 저 뜨거운 자리에서 세 곱절로 담금질하겠노라 맹세하오!\""]], [["“Then God keep thee, old man—see’st thou that”—pointing to the hammock—“I bury but one of five stout men, who were alive only yesterday; but were dead ere night. Only that one I bury; the rest were buried before they died; you sail upon their tomb.” Then turning to his crew—“Are ye ready there? place the plank then on the rail, and lift the body; so, then—Oh! God”—advancing towards the hammock with uplifted hands—“may the resurrection and the life——”", "\"그렇다면 하느님께서 그대를 지켜 주시기를, 노인장이여. 저것이 보이시오.\" 선장은 해먹을 가리켰다. \"나는 다섯 명의 건장한 사내 가운데 단 하나만을 묻으려 하오. 바로 어제까지만 해도 살아 있었으나 밤이 되기도 전에 죽어 버린 자들이오. 묻는 것은 오직 저 하나뿐, 나머지는 죽기도 전에 이미 묻혀 버렸소. 그대는 지금 그들의 무덤 위를 항해하고 있는 거요.\" 그러고는 선원들을 돌아보며 말했다. \"거기 준비됐는가? 그렇다면 널빤지를 난간에 걸치고, 시신을 들어 올려라. 자, 그러면—오! 하느님이시여—\" 그는 두 손을 치켜든 채 해먹을 향해 다가갔다. \"부활과 생명이 함께하시기를——\""]], [["“Brace forward!", "\"앞 돛 활대를 돌려라!"], ["Up helm!”", "키를 올려라!\""], ["cried Ahab like lightning to his men.", "에이해브가 번개처럼 선원들에게 외쳤다."]], [["But the suddenly started Pequod was not quick enough to escape the sound of the splash that the corpse soon made as it struck the sea; not so quick, indeed, but that some of the flying bubbles might have sprinkled her hull with their ghostly baptism.", "그러나 갑작스레 움직이기 시작한 피쿼드호도, 곧 시신이 바다에 부딪히며 일으킨 첨벙 소리를 벗어날 만큼 빠르지는 못했다. 그렇다, 그토록 빠르지 못했기에, 흩날린 물거품 몇 방울이 어쩌면 그 유령 같은 세례로 배의 선체를 적셨을지도 모른다."]], [["As Ahab now glided from the dejected Delight, the strange life-buoy hanging at the Pequod’s stern came into conspicuous relief.", "이제 에이해브가 의기소침한 딜라이트호 곁을 미끄러져 지나갈 때, 피쿼드호의 고물에 매달린 그 기묘한 구명부표가 두드러지게 도드라져 보였다."]], [["“Ha!", "\"하!"], ["yonder!", "저기!"], ["look yonder, men!”", "저길 보시오, 사람들이여!\""], ["cried a foreboding voice in her wake.", "그 배 뒤로 불길한 목소리가 외쳤다."], ["“In vain, oh, ye strangers, ye fly our sad burial; ye but turn us your taffrail to show us your coffin!”", "\"헛되도다, 오, 낯선 이들이여, 그대들은 우리의 슬픈 장례를 피해 달아나지만, 우리에게 그대들의 고물을 돌려 보이는 것은 다만 그대들의 관을 우리에게 내보이는 것일 뿐이로다!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 132. The Symphony.", "# CHAPTER 132. The Symphony."]], [["It was a clear steel-blue day.", "맑고 강철빛 도는 푸른 날이었다."], ["The firmaments of air and sea were hardly separable in that all-pervading azure; only, the pensive air was transparently pure and soft, with a woman’s look, and the robust and man-like sea heaved with long, strong, lingering swells, as Samson’s chest in his sleep.", "하늘과 바다의 창공은 사방에 두루 퍼진 그 쪽빛 속에서 좀처럼 구분되지 않았다. 다만 생각에 잠긴 듯한 대기는 여인의 얼굴빛처럼 투명하리만치 순결하고 부드러웠으며, 사내처럼 건장한 바다는 잠든 삼손의 가슴팍처럼 길고 힘차고 여운 있는 너울로 일렁였다."]], [["Hither, and thither, on high, glided the snow-white wings of small, unspeckled birds; these were the gentle thoughts of the feminine air; but to and fro in the deeps, far down in the bottomless blue, rushed mighty leviathans, sword-fish, and sharks; and these were the strong, troubled, murderous thinkings of the masculine sea.", "높은 곳에서는 작고 얼룩 하나 없는 새들의 눈처럼 흰 날개가 이리저리 미끄러져 갔다. 그것은 여성스러운 대기가 품은 부드러운 상념들이었다. 그러나 깊은 바다 속, 저 밑바닥 없는 푸름 속에서는 거대한 리바이어던과 황새치와 상어들이 이리저리 내달렸다. 그것은 남성스러운 바다가 품은 강하고 어지럽고 살기 어린 상념들이었다."]], [["But though thus contrasting within, the contrast was only in shades and shadows without; those two seemed one; it was only the sex, as it were, that distinguished them.", "그러나 안으로는 이렇게 대비를 이루면서도, 겉으로는 그 대비가 다만 빛깔의 농담(濃淡)과 그늘의 차이에 지나지 않았다. 그 둘은 하나처럼 보였으니, 굳이 말하자면 그 둘을 가르는 것은 오직 성(性)뿐이었다."]], [["Aloft, like a royal czar and king, the sun seemed giving this gentle air to this bold and rolling sea; even as bride to groom.", "저 높은 곳에서 황제이자 군왕인 양 태양은 이 부드러운 대기를 저 대담하게 일렁이는 바다에게 내려 주는 듯했으니, 마치 신부를 신랑에게 건네듯 했다."], ["And at the girdling line of the horizon, a soft and tremulous motion—most seen here at the equator—denoted the fond, throbbing trust, the loving alarms, with which the poor bride gave her bosom away.", "그리고 수평선의 띠 두른 경계선 위에서는, 적도 부근에서 가장 잘 보이는 그 부드럽고 떨리는 움직임이, 가련한 신부가 제 가슴을 내어 주며 품은 다정하고 두근거리는 신뢰와 사랑에 찬 떨림을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["Tied up and twisted; gnarled and knotted with wrinkles; haggardly firm and unyielding; his eyes glowing like coals, that still glow in the ashes of ruin; untottering Ahab stood forth in the clearness of the morn; lifting his splintered helmet of a brow to the fair girl’s forehead of heaven.", "꽁꽁 묶이고 비틀린 채, 주름으로 옹이 지고 매듭 진 얼굴, 초췌하리만치 굳세고 굽힘 없는 모습, 폐허의 잿더미 속에서도 여태 타오르는 숯덩이처럼 이글거리는 두 눈 — 흔들림 없는 에이해브가 아침의 맑음 속에 우뚝 섰다. 그 깨어진 투구 같은 이마를 저 아리따운 처녀 같은 하늘의 이마를 향해 치켜들고서."]], [["Oh, immortal infancy, and innocency of the azure!", "오, 쪽빛 하늘의 불멸하는 유년이여, 그 순결함이여!"], ["Invisible winged creatures that frolic all round us!", "우리 주위를 온통 뛰놀며 도는 보이지 않는 날개 달린 피조물들이여!"], ["Sweet childhood of air and sky!", "대기와 하늘의 감미로운 어린 시절이여!"], ["how oblivious were ye of old Ahab’s close-coiled woe!", "너희는 어찌 그리도 늙은 에이해브의 똬리 튼 슬픔에 무심하던가!"], ["But so have I seen little Miriam and Martha, laughing-eyed elves, heedlessly gambol around their old sire; sporting with the circle of singed locks which grew on the marge of that burnt-out crater of his brain.", "그러나 나는 이런 모습을 본 적이 있다 — 웃음 어린 눈을 한 작은 요정들, 미리암과 마사가 늙은 아비 주위를 아무 근심 없이 뛰놀며, 다 타 버린 그 뇌의 분화구 가장자리에 돋아난 그슬린 머리칼의 둘레를 가지고 장난치는 모습을."]], [["Slowly crossing the deck from the scuttle, Ahab leaned over the side and watched how his shadow in the water sank and sank to his gaze, the more and the more that he strove to pierce the profundity.", "승강구에서 갑판을 천천히 가로질러, 에이해브는 뱃전에 몸을 기대고 물속에 비친 제 그림자가 응시하는 눈길 아래로 점점 더, 점점 더 가라앉는 것을 지켜보았다 — 그가 그 깊이를 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰면 애쓸수록 그림자는 더욱더 깊이 잠겨 들었다."], ["But the lovely aromas in that enchanted air did at last seem to dispel, for a moment, the cankerous thing in his soul.", "그러나 그 마법에 걸린 듯한 대기 속의 사랑스러운 향기는 마침내 그의 영혼을 좀먹던 것을 한순간 흩어 버리는 듯했다."], ["That glad, happy air, that winsome sky, did at last stroke and caress him; the step-mother world, so long cruel—forbidding—now threw affectionate arms round his stubborn neck, and did seem to joyously sob over him, as if over one, that however wilful and erring, she could yet find it in her heart to save and to bless.", "그 기쁘고 행복한 대기, 그 매혹적인 하늘이 마침내 그를 쓰다듬고 어루만졌다. 그토록 오래 잔인하고 — 매정하던 — 계모 같은 세상이 이제 그의 완고한 목덜미에 다정한 팔을 두르고, 아무리 제멋대로이고 그릇된 자식일망정 그래도 마음속 한구석에서 구원하고 축복할 길을 찾아내겠다는 듯, 그를 굽어보며 기쁨에 겨워 흐느끼는 것만 같았다."], ["From beneath his slouched hat Ahab dropped a tear into the sea; nor did all the Pacific contain such wealth as that one wee drop.", "푹 눌러쓴 모자 아래로 에이해브는 바다에 눈물 한 방울을 떨어뜨렸다. 태평양을 다 합쳐도 그 작디작은 한 방울만 한 보물은 담겨 있지 않았다."]], [["Starbuck saw the old man; saw him, how he heavily leaned over the side; and he seemed to hear in his own true heart the measureless sobbing that stole out of the centre of the serenity around. Careful not to touch him, or be noticed by him, he yet drew near to him, and stood there.", "스타벅이 그 노인을 보았다. 그가 어찌나 무겁게 뱃전에 몸을 기대고 있는지를 보았다. 그리고 그는 주위에 감도는 고요의 한복판에서 새어 나오는 그 가없는 흐느낌을, 제 진실된 가슴속에서 듣는 듯했다. 그를 건드리지 않으려고, 또 그의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 조심하면서도, 그는 그에게로 다가가 곁에 섰다. 에이해브가 돌아섰다."]], [["Ahab turned.", "\"스타벅!\""]], [["“Starbuck!” “Sir.”", "\"예, 선장님.\""]], [["“Oh, Starbuck!", "\"오, 스타벅!"], ["it is a mild, mild wind, and a mild looking sky.", "온화하구나, 온화한 바람이야, 그리고 온화한 빛깔의 하늘이고."], ["On such a day—very much such a sweetness as this—I struck my first whale—a boy-harpooneer of eighteen!", "바로 이런 날에 — 꼭 이만큼이나 감미롭던 날에 — 나는 첫 고래를 잡았지. 열여덟 살 풋내기 작살잡이로 말이야!"], ["Forty—forty—forty years ago!—ago!", "사십 — 사십 — 사십 년 전 일이야! 전 일이라고!"], ["Forty years of continual whaling!", "사십 년을 쉴 새 없이 고래잡이로 보냈어!"], ["forty years of privation, and peril, and storm-time!", "사십 년의 궁핍과 위험과 폭풍의 세월! 사십 년을 저 무자비한 바다 위에서!"], ["forty years on the pitiless sea!", "사십 년 동안 에이해브는 평화로운 뭍을 저버렸어, 사십 년 동안 깊은 바다의 공포와 전쟁을 벌이려고!"], ["for forty years has Ahab forsaken the peaceful land, for forty years to make war on the horrors of the deep!", "그래, 정말로 그래, 스타벅, 그 사십 년 가운데 뭍에서 보낸 세월은 삼 년도 채 못 돼. 내가 살아온 이 삶을 생각하면, 그것이 얼마나 황량한 고독이었는지, 선장이라는 자리의 그 담장 두른 폐쇄성 — 바깥의 푸른 들녘에서 흘러드는 그 어떤 공감도 좀처럼 들이지 않는 성벽 같은 도시 — 오, 그 피로함이여!"], ["Aye and yes, Starbuck, out of those forty years I have not spent three ashore.", "그 무거움이여! 홀로 명령하는 자의 기니 해안 노예살이 같은 삶이여!"], ["When I think of this life I have led; the desolation of solitude it has been; the masoned, walled-town of a Captain’s exclusiveness, which admits but small entrance to any sympathy from the green country without—oh, weariness! heaviness!", "이 모든 것을 생각하면 — 전에는 그저 어렴풋이 의심만 했을 뿐 이토록 사무치게 알지는 못했던 것을 — 그리고 사십 년 동안 내가 어떻게 메마른 소금 절임만 먹고 살아왔는지를 — 메마른 내 영혼의 양식에 꼭 어울리는 표상이지!"], ["Guinea-coast slavery of solitary command!—when I think of all this; only half-suspected, not so keenly known to me before—and how for forty years I have fed upon dry salted fare—fit emblem of the dry nourishment of my soil!—when the poorest landsman has had fresh fruit to his daily hand, and broken the world’s fresh bread to my mouldy crusts—away, whole oceans away, from that young girl-wife I wedded past fifty, and sailed for Cape Horn the next day, leaving but one dent in my marriage pillow—wife?", "— 가장 가난한 뭍사람조차 날마다 신선한 과일을 손에 쥐고, 세상의 갓 구운 빵을 떼어 먹는 동안, 나는 곰팡이 슨 딱딱한 빵 껍질이나 씹었어 — 저 어린 새색시에게서 멀리, 온 바다만큼이나 멀리 떨어져서 말이야. 쉰을 넘겨 맞아들인 그 색시를, 결혼한 이튿날 케이프 혼을 향해 떠나며, 신혼의 베개에 옴폭 들어간 자국 하나만 남겨 두고 떠나왔지 — 아내라고?"], ["wife?—rather a widow with her husband alive!", "아내? 차라리 남편이 살아 있는 과부라 해야겠지!"], ["Aye, I widowed that poor girl when I married her, Starbuck; and then, the madness, the frenzy, the boiling blood and the smoking brow, with which, for a thousand lowerings old Ahab has furiously, foamingly chased his prey—more a demon than a man!—aye, aye!", "그래, 나는 그 가련한 처녀와 혼인하는 그 순간에 그 애를 과부로 만들어 버린 거야, 스타벅. 그러고 나서, 그 광기, 그 광란, 그 끓어오르는 피와 김 서린 이마로, 천 번이나 보트를 내리며 늙은 에이해브는 미친 듯이, 게거품을 물고 제 먹잇감을 뒤쫓았어 — 사람이라기보다 악마에 가까운 꼴로!"], ["what a forty years’ fool—fool—old fool, has old Ahab been!", "그래, 그래! 사십 년을 얼마나 바보로 — 바보로 — 늙은 바보로 살아온 거냐, 이 늙은 에이해브가!"], ["Why this strife of the chase?", "어찌하여 이 추격의 싸움을 벌이느냐?"], ["why weary, and palsy the arm at the oar, and the iron, and the lance?", "어찌하여 노와 작살과 창을 쥔 팔을 지치게 하고 마비시키느냐?"], ["how the richer or better is Ahab now? Behold.", "지금의 에이해브가 그래서 무엇이 더 부유해지고 더 나아졌단 말이냐?"], ["Oh, Starbuck!", "보라. 오, 스타벅!"], ["is it not hard, that with this weary load I bear, one poor leg should have been snatched from under me?", "가혹하지 않으냐, 이 고단한 짐을 진 채로 그 가련한 한쪽 다리마저 내 몸 밑에서 채여 가 버렸다는 게?"], ["Here, brush this old hair aside; it blinds me, that I seem to weep.", "자, 이 늙은 머리칼을 좀 쓸어 넘겨 다오. 눈을 가려서, 마치 내가 우는 것처럼 보이게 하는구나."], ["Locks so grey did never grow but from out some ashes!", "이렇게 잿빛이 된 머리칼은 어떤 잿더미에서가 아니고서야 결코 돋아나지 않는 법이지!"], ["But do I look very old, so very, very old, Starbuck?", "그런데 내가 정말 늙어 보이느냐, 아주, 아주 늙어 보이느냐, 스타벅?"], ["I feel deadly faint, bowed, and humped, as though I were Adam, staggering beneath the piled centuries since Paradise.", "죽을 만큼 기운이 빠지고, 등이 굽고 곱사등이 된 듯한 기분이야, 마치 내가 아담이라도 된 양, 낙원 이래로 쌓여 온 세월을 짊어지고 비틀거리는 듯해. 하느님!"], ["God!", "하느님!"], ["God!", "하느님!"], ["God!—crack my heart!—stave my brain!—mockery!", "내 심장을 쪼개 주소서! 내 머리를 부숴 주소서!"], ["mockery!", "조롱이로구나!"], ["bitter, biting mockery of grey hairs, have I lived enough joy to wear ye; and seem and feel thus intolerably old?", "조롱이야! 잿빛 머리칼의 쓰디쓴, 살을 에는 조롱이여, 내가 너희를 두를 만큼 충분한 기쁨을 누리며 살아왔던가, 이토록 견딜 수 없으리만치 늙어 보이고 또 그렇게 느껴지도록?"], ["Close!", "가까이!"], ["stand close to me, Starbuck; let me look into a human eye; it is better than to gaze into sea or sky; better than to gaze upon God.", "내게 바싹 다가서라, 스타벅. 사람의 눈을 들여다보게 해 다오."], ["By the green land; by the bright hearth-stone!", "바다나 하늘을 바라보는 것보다 낫고, 하느님을 우러러보는 것보다 낫구나. 저 푸른 뭍을 두고, 저 환한 화롯가의 돌을 두고 맹세하노니!"], ["this is the magic glass, man; I see my wife and my child in thine eye.", "이것이야말로 마법의 거울이야, 이 사람아. 네 눈 속에서 나는 내 아내와 내 자식을 본다."], ["No, no; stay on board, on board!—lower not when I do; when branded Ahab gives chase to Moby Dick.", "아니, 아니. 배에 남아 있어라, 배에!"], ["That hazard shall not be thine.", "내가 보트를 내릴 때 너는 내리지 마라. 낙인찍힌 에이해브가 모비 딕을 뒤쫓을 때 말이다."], ["No, no!", "그 위험은 네 몫이 되어선 안 돼."], ["not with the far away home I see in that eye!”", "아니, 아니! 그 눈 속에서 내가 보는 저 머나먼 고향을 두고서라도, 안 될 일이야!\""]], [["“Oh, my Captain!", "\"오, 선장님!"], ["my Captain!", "선장님!"], ["noble soul!", "고귀한 영혼이여!"], ["grand old heart, after all!", "그래도 역시 위대한 노장의 마음이시여!"], ["why should any one give chase to that hated fish!", "어찌하여 누구든 그 가증스러운 물고기를 뒤쫓아야 한단 말입니까!"], ["Away with me!", "저와 함께 떠나시지요!"], ["let us fly these deadly waters!", "이 죽음의 바다에서 달아납시다!"], ["let us home!", "고향으로 돌아갑시다!"], ["Wife and child, too, are Starbuck’s—wife and child of his brotherly, sisterly, play-fellow youth; even as thine, sir, are the wife and child of thy loving, longing, paternal old age!", "아내와 자식은 이 스타벅에게도 있습니다 — 형제처럼, 누이처럼, 소꿉동무처럼 함께 자란 젊은 날의 아내와 자식이지요. 선장님께도 자애롭고 그리움에 찬 아버지로서의 노년에 둔 아내와 자식이 있는 것처럼 말입니다!"], ["Away!", "떠납시다!"], ["let us away!—this instant let me alter the course!", "우리 떠나요! 지금 당장 항로를 바꾸게 해 주십시오!"], ["How cheerily, how hilariously, O my Captain, would we bowl on our way to see old Nantucket again!", "오, 선장님, 우리가 그리운 낸터킷을 다시 보러 나아가는 길이 얼마나 즐겁고, 얼마나 흥겨울지요!"], ["I think, sir, they have some such mild blue days, even as this, in Nantucket.”", "제 생각엔, 선장님, 낸터킷에도 이렇게 온화하고 푸른 날들이, 바로 오늘 같은 날들이 더러 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“They have, they have.", "\"있고말고, 있고말고."], ["I have seen them—some summer days in the morning.", "나도 본 적이 있어 — 여름날 아침나절에 말이야."], ["About this time—yes, it is his noon nap now—the boy vivaciously wakes; sits up in bed; and his mother tells him of me, of cannibal old me; how I am abroad upon the deep, but will yet come back to dance him again.”", "지금 이맘때쯤이면 — 그래, 지금이 바로 그 아이의 낮잠 시간이겠지 — 그 녀석이 생기 넘치게 깨어나, 잠자리에서 일어나 앉으면, 제 어미가 나에 관해 일러 주겠지, 식인종 같은 이 늙은 나에 관해. 내가 어찌 저 먼 바다에 나가 있는지, 그래도 언젠가는 돌아와 다시 안고 춤춰 줄 거라고 말이야.\""]], [["“’Tis my Mary, my Mary herself!", "\"바로 제 메리예요, 제 메리가 틀림없습니다!"], ["She promised that my boy, every morning, should be carried to the hill to catch the first glimpse of his father’s sail!", "제 아내는 약속했지요, 우리 아이를 아침마다 언덕으로 데려가 제 아비의 돛을 맨 먼저 보게 해 주겠다고요! 그래요, 그래요!"], ["Yes, yes! no more!", "더는 안 됩니다!"], ["it is done!", "끝났어요!"], ["we head for Nantucket!", "낸터킷으로 뱃머리를 돌립시다!"], ["Come, my Captain, study out the course, and let us away!", "자, 선장님, 항로를 잡으세요, 그리고 떠납시다!"], ["See, see!", "보세요, 보세요!"], ["the boy’s face from the window!", "창문 너머 그 아이의 얼굴이!"], ["the boy’s hand on the hill!”", "언덕 위 그 아이의 손이!\""]], [["But Ahab’s glance was averted; like a blighted fruit tree he shook, and cast his last, cindered apple to the soil.", "그러나 에이해브의 시선은 딴 데로 돌려져 있었다. 그는 시들어 버린 과일나무처럼 몸을 떨며, 마지막으로 남은 잿빛 사과 한 알을 땅으로 떨구었다."]], [["“What is it, what nameless, inscrutable, unearthly thing is it; what cozening, hidden lord and master, and cruel, remorseless emperor commands me; that against all natural lovings and longings, I so keep pushing, and crowding, and jamming myself on all the time; recklessly making me ready to do what in my own proper, natural heart, I durst not so much as dare?", "\"이것은 대체 무엇인가, 이름 붙일 수도 없고, 헤아릴 수도 없고, 이 세상 것 같지도 않은 이것은 무엇인가. 어떤 교활하고 숨은 군주이자 주인이, 어떤 잔인하고 무자비한 황제가 나를 호령하기에, 온갖 자연스러운 사랑과 그리움을 거스르고서 나를 이토록 끊임없이 떠밀고, 몰아대고, 짓눌러 가며, 내 본디의 자연스러운 마음으로는 감히 시도조차 못 할 일을 무모하게 해치우도록 채비시키는가?"], ["Is Ahab, Ahab?", "에이해브는 에이해브인가?"], ["Is it I, God, or who, that lifts this arm?", "이 팔을 들어 올리는 것은 나인가, 하느님인가, 아니면 누구인가?"], ["But if the great sun move not of himself; but is as an errand-boy in heaven; nor one single star can revolve, but by some invisible power; how then can this one small heart beat; this one small brain think thoughts; unless God does that beating, does that thinking, does that living, and not I.", "그러나 저 거대한 태양조차 스스로 움직이는 게 아니라 하늘의 심부름꾼 소년에 지나지 않고, 단 하나의 별도 어떤 보이지 않는 힘에 의하지 않고서는 돌지 못한다면, 그렇다면 이 작은 심장 하나가 어찌 뛰며, 이 작은 뇌 하나가 어찌 생각을 떠올릴 수 있단 말인가. 하느님이 그 박동을 뛰게 하고, 그 생각을 하게 하고, 그 삶을 살게 하는 것이지, 내가 하는 것이 아니라면 말이야."], ["By heaven, man, we are turned round and round in this world, like yonder windlass, and Fate is the handspike.", "하늘에 맹세코, 이 사람아, 우리는 이 세상에서 저기 저 양묘기처럼 빙빙 돌려질 뿐이고, 운명이야말로 그 가로장이지."], ["And all the time, lo! that smiling sky, and this unsounded sea!", "그런데 보라, 그 내내 저 미소 짓는 하늘과 이 깊이 모를 바다라니!"], ["Look!", "보라!"], ["see yon Albicore!", "저 다랑어를 보아라!"], ["who put it into him to chase and fang that flying-fish?", "저 녀석에게 누가 저 날치를 뒤쫓아 물어뜯으라 일러 주었느냐?"], ["Where do murderers go, man!", "살인자들은 어디로 가는가, 이 사람아!"], ["Who’s to doom, when the judge himself is dragged to the bar?", "재판관 자신이 피고석으로 끌려 나오는 마당에, 대체 누가 단죄를 한단 말이냐? 그러나 온화하구나, 온화한 바람이야, 그리고 온화한 빛깔의 하늘이고."], ["But it is a mild, mild wind, and a mild looking sky; and the air smells now, as if it blew from a far-away meadow; they have been making hay somewhere under the slopes of the Andes, Starbuck, and the mowers are sleeping among the new-mown hay.", "게다가 대기에서는 지금, 마치 머나먼 풀밭에서 불어온 듯한 냄새가 나는구나. 누군가 안데스 산비탈 어딘가에서 건초를 만들고 있었나 보다, 스타벅, 그리고 풀 베는 이들은 갓 베어 낸 건초 더미 사이에서 잠들어 있겠지."], ["Sleeping?", "잠들었다고?"], ["Aye, toil we how we may, we all sleep at last on the field.", "그래, 우리가 아무리 애써 일한들, 우리 모두 결국엔 들판에 누워 잠들지."], ["Sleep?", "잠든다고?"], ["Aye, and rust amid greenness; as last year’s scythes flung down, and left in the half-cut swaths—Starbuck!”", "그래, 그리고 푸름 속에서 녹슬어 가는 거야. 작년에 내던져진 낫들이 반쯤 베다 만 풀밭에 그대로 버려진 것처럼 말이야 — 스타벅!\""]], [["But blanched to a corpse’s hue with despair, the Mate had stolen away.", "그러나 절망으로 시신의 빛깔이 되도록 핏기가 가신 일등항해사는, 어느새 슬그머니 사라지고 없었다."]], [["Ahab crossed the deck to gaze over on the other side; but started at two reflected, fixed eyes in the water there.", "에이해브는 갑판을 가로질러 반대편 너머를 바라보았다. 그러나 거기 물속에 비친, 한곳에 고정된 두 눈에 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["Fedallah was motionlessly leaning over the same rail.", "페달라가 같은 난간에 미동도 없이 기대어 서 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 133. The Chase—First Day.", "# CHAPTER 133. The Chase—First Day."]], [["That night, in the mid-watch, when the old man—as his wont at intervals—stepped forth from the scuttle in which he leaned, and went to his pivot-hole, he suddenly thrust out his face fiercely, snuffing up the sea air as a sagacious ship’s dog will, in drawing nigh to some barbarous isle.", "그날 밤, 한밤중 당직 시간에, 노인은 — 이따금 그러하듯 — 자신이 기대어 있던 승강구에서 걸어 나와 자기 회전 구멍 자리로 갔다. 그때 그는 돌연 얼굴을 사납게 내밀고는, 야만의 섬에 다가갈 때 영리한 배 안의 개가 그러하듯 바다 공기를 코로 들이켰다."], ["He declared that a whale must be near.", "그는 고래가 가까이 있는 게 분명하다고 단언했다."], ["Soon that peculiar odor, sometimes to a great distance given forth by the living sperm whale, was palpable to all the watch; nor was any mariner surprised when, after inspecting the compass, and then the dog-vane, and then ascertaining the precise bearing of the odor as nearly as possible, Ahab rapidly ordered the ship’s course to be slightly altered, and the sail to be shortened.", "곧 살아 있는 향유고래가 이따금 먼 거리에까지 풍기는 그 특유의 냄새가 당직 선원 모두에게 또렷이 느껴졌다. 그리고 에이해브가 나침반을 살피고, 이어 풍향계를 보고, 그런 다음 가능한 한 정확히 냄새의 방향을 가늠한 뒤 재빨리 배의 항로를 약간 바꾸고 돛을 줄이라 명했을 때, 그 누구도 놀라지 않았다."]], [["The acute policy dictating these movements was sufficiently vindicated at daybreak, by the sight of a long sleek on the sea directly and lengthwise ahead, smooth as oil, and resembling in the pleated watery wrinkles bordering it, the polished metallic-like marks of some swift tide-rip, at the mouth of a deep, rapid stream.", "이런 동작들을 지시한 예리한 판단은 동틀 무렵에 충분히 입증되었다. 바로 앞바다 위로 길게 뻗은 매끄러운 자국이 보였던 것이다. 그것은 기름처럼 반들거렸고, 그 가장자리를 두른 잔주름진 물결무늬가 마치 깊고 빠른 강 어귀에서 일어나는 거센 조류의, 잘 닦은 금속 같은 자취를 닮아 있었다."]], [["“Man the mast-heads!", "\"돛대 꼭대기로 올라가라!"], ["Call all hands!”", "전원 집합!\""]], [["Thundering with the butts of three clubbed handspikes on the forecastle deck, Daggoo roused the sleepers with such judgment claps that they seemed to exhale from the scuttle, so instantaneously did they appear with their clothes in their hands.", "대거가 곤봉처럼 굵은 손잡이 막대 세 개의 밑동으로 앞갑판을 천둥처럼 두드려 잠든 자들을 깨우니, 그 단호한 타격에 그들은 마치 승강구에서 토해진 듯 옷가지를 손에 든 채 순식간에 나타났다."]], [["“What d’ye see?”", "\"뭐가 보이느냐?\""], ["cried Ahab, flattening his face to the sky.", "에이해브가 얼굴을 하늘로 바싹 쳐들며 외쳤다."]], [["“Nothing, nothing sir!”", "\"아무것도, 아무것도 없습니다!\""], ["was the sound hailing down in reply.", "위에서 응답하는 소리가 내려왔다."]], [["“T’gallant sails!—stunsails!", "\"중간 돛대 윗돛을 펴라!"], ["alow and aloft, and on both sides!”", "보조돛도! 아래위로, 양쪽 다!\""]], [["All sail being set, he now cast loose the life-line, reserved for swaying him to the main royal-mast head; and in a few moments they were hoisting him thither, when, while but two thirds of the way aloft, and while peering ahead through the horizontal vacancy between the main-top-sail and top-gallant-sail, he raised a gull-like cry in the air.", "돛이 모두 펴지자, 그는 자신을 주(主) 최상부 돛대 꼭대기로 끌어올리는 데 쓰는 구명줄을 풀어 던졌다. 그러자 잠시 후 그들이 그를 그곳으로 끌어올리기 시작했고, 위로 삼분의 이쯤 올라갔을 무렵, 주 중간돛과 그 윗돛 사이의 수평으로 트인 공간 너머를 응시하던 그가 갈매기 같은 외침을 허공에 띄웠다."], ["“There she blows!—there she blows!", "\"저기 물을 뿜는다! 저기 뿜는다!"], ["A hump like a snow-hill!", "눈 덮인 언덕 같은 혹이다!"], ["It is Moby Dick!”", "모비 딕이다!\""]], [["Fired by the cry which seemed simultaneously taken up by the three look-outs, the men on deck rushed to the rigging to behold the famous whale they had so long been pursuing.", "세 명의 망꾼이 동시에 받아 외치는 듯한 그 고함에 불이 붙은 갑판 위의 선원들은, 그토록 오래 쫓아온 그 이름난 고래를 보려고 삭구로 달려들었다."], ["Ahab had now gained his final perch, some feet above the other look-outs, Tashtego standing just beneath him on the cap of the top-gallant-mast, so that the Indian’s head was almost on a level with Ahab’s heel.", "에이해브는 이제 마지막 자리, 다른 망꾼들보다 몇 자쯤 더 높은 곳에 이르렀다. 태시테고가 바로 그 아래 최상부 돛대 꼭대기의 캡에 서 있었으므로, 그 인디언의 머리는 에이해브의 발꿈치와 거의 같은 높이였다."], ["From this height the whale was now seen some mile or so ahead, at every roll of the sea revealing his high sparkling hump, and regularly jetting his silent spout into the air.", "이 높이에서 보니 고래는 이제 한 마일쯤 앞에 있었고, 바다가 굽이칠 때마다 반짝이는 높은 혹을 드러내며 규칙적으로 소리 없는 물줄기를 허공에 뿜어 올렸다."], ["To the credulous mariners it seemed the same silent spout they had so long ago beheld in the moonlit Atlantic and Indian Oceans.", "잘 믿는 선원들에게는 그것이 오래전 달빛 어린 대서양과 인도양에서 보았던 바로 그 소리 없는 물줄기처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["“And did none of ye see it before?”", "\"너희 중 누구도 그것을 먼저 보지 못했단 말이냐?\""], ["cried Ahab, hailing the perched men all around him.", "에이해브가 사방에 올라가 있는 자들을 향해 외쳤다."]], [["“I saw him almost that same instant, sir, that Captain Ahab did, and I cried out,”", "\"에이해브 선장님께서 보신 거의 그 순간에 저도 그를 보았고, 소리쳤습니다.\""], ["said Tashtego.", "태시테고가 말했다."]], [["“Not the same instant; not the same—no, the doubloon is mine, Fate reserved the doubloon for me.", "\"같은 순간이 아니다. 같은 순간이 아니야 — 그래, 그 금화는 내 것이다."], ["I only; none of ye could have raised the White Whale first.", "운명이 그 금화를 내 몫으로 남겨 둔 것이다. 오직 나만이!"], ["There she blows!—there she blows!—there she blows!", "너희 중 누구도 흰 고래를 먼저 발견할 수 없었다. 저기 물을 뿜는다!"], ["There again!—there again!”", "저기 뿜는다! 저기 뿜는다!"], ["he cried, in long-drawn, lingering, methodic tones, attuned to the gradual prolongings of the whale’s visible jets.", "또 저기다! 또 저기!\""], ["“He’s going to sound! In stunsails!", "그는 길게 끌며, 여운을 남기는 절도 있는 어조로, 고래가 눈에 보이게 점점 길게 뿜어 올리는 물줄기에 맞추어 외쳤다."], ["Down top-gallant-sails!", "\"잠수하려 한다! 보조돛을 거둬라!"], ["Stand by three boats. Mr.", "윗돛을 내려라!"], ["Starbuck, remember, stay on board, and keep the ship.", "보트 세 척, 대기하라. 스타벅, 명심하게, 자네는 배에 남아 배를 지켜라."], ["Helm there!", "키잡이!"], ["Luff, luff a point!", "바람 쪽으로 돌려라, 한 점만!"], ["So; steady, man, steady! There go flukes!", "그래, 침착하게, 침착하게!"], ["No, no; only black water!", "저기 꼬리지느러미가 간다!"], ["All ready the boats there?", "아니, 아니, 그저 검은 물일 뿐이다!"], ["Stand by, stand by!", "보트들 준비됐나?"], ["Lower me, Mr.", "대기, 대기하라!"], ["Starbuck; lower, lower,—quick, quicker!”", "나를 내려라, 스타벅. 내려, 내려라 — 빨리, 더 빨리!\""], ["and he slid through the air to the deck.", "그러고는 허공을 미끄러져 갑판으로 내려왔다."]], [["“He is heading straight to leeward, sir,”", "\"녀석은 곧장 바람 아래쪽으로 향하고 있습니다, 선장님.\" 스터브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Stubb, “right away from us; cannot have seen the ship yet.”", "\"우리에게서 곧장 멀어집니다. 아직 배를 보지 못한 모양입니다.\""]], [["“Be dumb, man!", "\"입 닥쳐라!"], ["Stand by the braces!", "활대 밧줄을 잡아라! 키를 한껏 내려라!"], ["Hard down the helm!—brace up!", "활대를 당겨라! 돛을 떨게 하라!"], ["Shiver her!—shiver her!—So; well that!", "떨게 하라! 그래, 잘했다!"], ["Boats, boats!”", "보트, 보트로!\""]], [["Soon all the boats but Starbuck’s were dropped; all the boat-sails set—all the paddles plying; with rippling swiftness, shooting to leeward; and Ahab heading the onset. A pale, death-glimmer lit up Fedallah’s sunken eyes; a hideous motion gnawed his mouth.", "곧 스타벅의 것만 빼고 모든 보트가 내려졌다. 보트의 돛이 모두 펼쳐지고 — 노가 모두 움직이기 시작했다. 잔물결 일으키는 날쌤으로, 바람 아래쪽을 향해 쏜살같이 내달렸다. 에이해브가 그 돌격의 선두에 섰다. 페달라의 푹 꺼진 두 눈에 창백한, 죽음 같은 빛이 어른거렸다. 흉측한 경련이 그의 입을 갉아먹었다."]], [["Like noiseless nautilus shells, their light prows sped through the sea; but only slowly they neared the foe.", "소리 없는 앵무조개 껍질처럼, 그들의 가벼운 뱃머리가 바다를 가르며 내달렸다. 그러나 적에게 다가가는 속도는 더디기만 했다."], ["As they neared him, the ocean grew still more smooth; seemed drawing a carpet over its waves; seemed a noon-meadow, so serenely it spread.", "그들이 가까워질수록 바다는 한층 더 잔잔해져, 마치 물결 위에 양탄자를 펼쳐 깔아 놓은 듯했고, 정오의 풀밭처럼 고요하게 펼쳐졌다."], ["At length the breathless hunter came so nigh his seemingly unsuspecting prey, that his entire dazzling hump was distinctly visible, sliding along the sea as if an isolated thing, and continually set in a revolving ring of finest, fleecy, greenish foam.", "마침내 숨죽인 사냥꾼은, 아무것도 눈치채지 못한 듯한 그 먹잇감에 바싹 다가갔다. 그리하여 눈부신 혹 전체가 또렷이 보였는데, 그것은 마치 외따로 떨어진 물체처럼 바다 위를 미끄러져 갔고, 더없이 곱고 양털 같은 연둣빛 거품의 도는 고리 속에 끊임없이 둘러싸여 있었다."], ["He saw the vast, involved wrinkles of the slightly projecting head beyond.", "그는 그 너머로 약간 솟아오른 머리의 거대하고 뒤얽힌 주름들을 보았다."], ["Before it, far out on the soft Turkish-rugged waters, went the glistening white shadow from his broad, milky forehead, a musical rippling playfully accompanying the shade; and behind, the blue waters interchangeably flowed over into the moving valley of his steady wake; and on either hand bright bubbles arose and danced by his side.", "그 앞으로, 부드러운 터키 양탄자 같은 물 위 저 멀리, 넓고 젖빛 도는 이마에서 비롯된 번쩍이는 흰 그림자가 나아갔고, 음악 같은 잔물결이 그 그림자를 장난스레 뒤따랐다. 그리고 뒤로는 푸른 물이 서로 번갈아 흘러들어 그 한결같은 항적이 이루는 움직이는 골짜기를 채웠으며, 양옆으로는 밝은 거품이 일어나 그 곁에서 춤추었다."], ["But these were broken again by the light toes of hundreds of gay fowl softly feathering the sea, alternate with their fitful flight; and like to some flag-staff rising from the painted hull of an argosy, the tall but shattered pole of a recent lance projected from the white whale’s back; and at intervals one of the cloud of soft-toed fowls hovering, and to and fro skimming like a canopy over the fish, silently perched and rocked on this pole, the long tail feathers streaming like pennons.", "그러나 이 거품들은, 부드럽게 바다를 깃털로 쓰다듬듯 변덕스럽게 날아오르는 수백 마리 흥겨운 물새의 가벼운 발끝에 다시 부서졌다. 그리고 큰 상선의 그림 그려진 선체에서 솟은 깃대처럼, 최근에 박힌 창의 부러진 긴 자루가 흰 고래의 등에서 삐죽 솟아 있었고, 이따금 그 부드러운 발의 물새 떼 중 한 마리가, 고기 위를 차일처럼 이리저리 스치며 맴돌다가, 소리 없이 이 자루에 내려앉아 흔들거렸으니, 그 긴 꽁지깃이 깃발처럼 나부꼈다."]], [["A gentle joyousness—a mighty mildness of repose in swiftness, invested the gliding whale.", "부드러운 환희가 — 날쌤 속에 깃든 위엄 있는 온화한 평정이 미끄러져 가는 고래를 감싸고 있었다."], ["Not the white bull Jupiter swimming away with ravished Europa clinging to his graceful horns; his lovely, leering eyes sideways intent upon the maid; with smooth bewitching fleetness, rippling straight for the nuptial bower in Crete; not Jove, not that great majesty Supreme! did surpass the glorified White Whale as he so divinely swam.", "납치한 에우로페가 그 우아한 뿔에 매달린 채 헤엄쳐 가던 흰 황소 유피테르도 — 처녀를 곁눈질로 응시하는 그 사랑스럽고 음흉한 눈으로, 매끄럽고 매혹적인 날쌤으로 크레타의 혼례 침실을 향해 잔물결 일으키며 곧장 나아가던 그 황소도 — 유피테르도, 저 위대하고 지엄한 최고의 신조차도, 그토록 신성하게 헤엄치는 영광에 싸인 흰 고래를 능가하지 못했다."]], [["On each soft side—coincident with the parted swell, that but once leaving him, then flowed so wide away—on each bright side, the whale shed off enticings.", "그 부드러운 양옆에서 — 한 번 그를 떠나면 그토록 넓게 흘러가 버리는 갈라진 너울과 어울려 — 그 빛나는 양옆에서, 고래는 유혹의 손길을 흩뿌렸다."], ["No wonder there had been some among the hunters who namelessly transported and allured by all this serenity, had ventured to assail it; but had fatally found that quietude but the vesture of tornadoes.", "이 모든 고요함에 형언할 수 없이 도취되고 홀린 나머지, 감히 그것을 공격하려 들었던 사냥꾼들이 더러 있었던 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그러나 그들은 그 정적이 다름 아닌 폭풍의 겉옷임을 죽음으로써 깨달았던 것이다."], ["Yet calm, enticing calm, oh, whale!", "그럼에도 고요하구나, 유혹하는 고요함이여, 오 고래여!"], ["thou glidest on, to all who for the first time eye thee, no matter how many in that same way thou may’st have bejuggled and destroyed before.", "처음으로 너를 보는 모든 이에게, 너는 미끄러져 나아간다 — 네가 그토록 똑같은 방식으로 전에 얼마나 많은 자들을 홀려 파멸시켰든 아랑곳없이."]], [["And thus, through the serene tranquillities of the tropical sea, among waves whose hand-clappings were suspended by exceeding rapture, Moby Dick moved on, still withholding from sight the full terrors of his submerged trunk, entirely hiding the wrenched hideousness of his jaw.", "이리하여, 열대 바다의 잔잔한 고요 속을, 더없는 황홀에 손뼉 치기를 멈춘 물결 사이를, 모비 딕은 나아갔다. 여전히 물에 잠긴 몸통의 온전한 공포를 시야에서 거두어들이고, 그 비틀린 흉측한 턱을 완전히 감춘 채로."], ["But soon the fore part of him slowly rose from the water; for an instant his whole marbleized body formed a high arch, like Virginia’s Natural Bridge, and warningly waving his bannered flukes in the air, the grand god revealed himself, sounded, and went out of sight.", "그러나 곧 그의 앞부분이 물에서 천천히 솟아올랐다. 한순간 대리석 같은 몸 전체가 버지니아의 자연 다리처럼 높은 아치를 이루었고, 깃발 같은 꼬리지느러미를 경고하듯 허공에 흔들며, 그 위대한 신은 제 모습을 드러내고는 잠수하여 시야에서 사라졌다."], ["Hoveringly halting, and dipping on the wing, the white sea-fowls longingly lingered over the agitated pool that he left.", "흰 바닷새들은 맴돌며 멈칫거리다 날개를 담그듯 내려앉아, 그가 남긴 출렁이는 물웅덩이 위를 그리운 듯 서성거렸다."]], [["With oars apeak, and paddles down, the sheets of their sails adrift, the three boats now stilly floated, awaiting Moby Dick’s reappearance.", "노를 곧추세우고, 짧은 노를 내리고, 돛줄을 풀어 둔 채, 세 척의 보트는 이제 고요히 떠 있었다. 모비 딕이 다시 나타나기를 기다리며."]], [["“An hour,”", "\"한 시간이다.\""], ["said Ahab, standing rooted in his boat’s stern; and he gazed beyond the whale’s place, towards the dim blue spaces and wide wooing vacancies to leeward.", "에이해브가 자기 보트의 고물에 뿌리박은 듯 서서 말했다. 그러고는 고래가 있던 자리 너머, 바람 아래쪽으로 어슴푸레 푸른 공간들과, 넓게 손짓하는 듯한 텅 빈 곳들을 응시했다."], ["It was only an instant; for again his eyes seemed whirling round in his head as he swept the watery circle.", "그것은 한순간일 뿐이었다. 다시 그의 두 눈은 그 물의 원을 휘둘러보면서 머릿속에서 빙글빙글 돌아가는 듯했다."], ["The breeze now freshened; the sea began to swell.", "이제 산들바람이 거세졌고, 바다가 부풀어 오르기 시작했다."]], [["“The birds!—the birds!”", "\"새다! 새들이다!\""], ["cried Tashtego.", "태시테고가 외쳤다."]], [["In long Indian file, as when herons take wing, the white birds were now all flying towards Ahab’s boat; and when within a few yards began fluttering over the water there, wheeling round and round, with joyous, expectant cries.", "왜가리들이 날아오를 때처럼 길게 인디언 행렬을 이루어, 흰 새들이 이제 모두 에이해브의 보트를 향해 날아오고 있었다. 그리고 몇 야드 안쪽에 이르자 그곳 물 위를 퍼덕이며 빙글빙글 맴돌기 시작했으니, 기쁘고 기대에 찬 울음을 토하며 그러했다."], ["Their vision was keener than man’s; Ahab could discover no sign in the sea.", "그들의 시각은 인간의 것보다 예리했다. 에이해브는 바다에서 아무 기척도 찾아낼 수 없었다."], ["But suddenly as he peered down and down into its depths, he profoundly saw a white living spot no bigger than a white weasel, with wonderful celerity uprising, and magnifying as it rose, till it turned, and then there were plainly revealed two long crooked rows of white, glistening teeth, floating up from the undiscoverable bottom.", "그러나 문득, 그가 그 깊디깊은 곳을 응시하던 차에, 그는 저 깊은 데서 흰 족제비만 한 크기의 살아 있는 흰 점을 어렴풋이 보았다. 그것은 놀라운 속도로 솟아올라, 솟으면서 점점 커지더니, 마침내 몸을 돌리자, 알 수 없는 바닥에서 떠오르는 길고 굽은 두 줄의 희고 번쩍이는 이빨이 또렷이 드러났다."], ["It was Moby Dick’s open mouth and scrolled jaw; his vast, shadowed bulk still half blending with the blue of the sea.", "그것은 모비 딕의 벌린 입과 둘둘 말린 턱이었다. 그 거대하고 그늘진 몸뚱이는 여전히 바다의 푸른빛과 반쯤 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["The glittering mouth yawned beneath the boat like an open-doored marble tomb; and giving one sidelong sweep with his steering oar, Ahab whirled the craft aside from this tremendous apparition.", "번쩍이는 입이 문 열린 대리석 무덤처럼 보트 아래에서 아가리를 벌렸다. 에이해브는 키 잡는 노로 한 번 옆으로 휘저어, 이 어마어마한 환영에서 배를 비켜 돌렸다."], ["Then, calling upon Fedallah to change places with him, went forward to the bows, and seizing Perth’s harpoon, commanded his crew to grasp their oars and stand by to stern.", "그러고는 페달라에게 자리를 바꾸라 명하고, 앞으로 나아가 뱃머리로 가서, 퍼스의 작살을 움켜쥐고는, 선원들에게 노를 잡고 고물 쪽으로 대기하라 명했다."]], [["Now, by reason of this timely spinning round the boat upon its axis, its bow, by anticipation, was made to face the whale’s head while yet under water.", "이제, 이렇게 때맞추어 보트를 그 축을 중심으로 돌려놓은 까닭에, 그 뱃머리는 미리 헤아린 대로 아직 물속에 있는 고래의 머리를 마주하게 되었다."], ["But as if perceiving this stratagem, Moby Dick, with that malicious intelligence ascribed to him, sidelingly transplanted himself, as it were, in an instant, shooting his pleated head lengthwise beneath the boat.", "그러나 마치 이 술책을 알아챈 듯이, 모비 딕은 그에게 돌려진 그 음흉한 지능으로, 이를테면 순식간에 몸을 옆으로 옮겨, 주름진 머리를 보트 아래로 길게 들이밀었다."]], [["Through and through; through every plank and each rib, it thrilled for an instant, the whale obliquely lying on his back, in the manner of a biting shark, slowly and feelingly taking its bows full within his mouth, so that the long, narrow, scrolled lower jaw curled high up into the open air, and one of the teeth caught in a row-lock.", "뚫고 또 뚫고, 모든 널빤지와 늑재 하나하나를 관통하며, 한순간 그것은 부르르 떨었다. 고래가 비스듬히 등을 대고 누워, 물어뜯는 상어처럼, 천천히 그리고 느낌을 음미하듯 보트의 뱃머리를 입속에 통째로 받아들이니, 길고 좁다란 둘둘 말린 아래턱이 트인 허공으로 높이 말려 올라가고, 이빨 하나가 노받이에 걸렸다."], ["The bluish pearl-white of the inside of the jaw was within six inches of Ahab’s head, and reached higher than that.", "턱 안쪽의 푸르스름한 진주빛 흰 면이 에이해브의 머리에서 여섯 인치 거리에 있었고, 그보다 더 높이까지 닿았다."], ["In this attitude the White Whale now shook the slight cedar as a mildly cruel cat her mouse.", "이런 자세로 흰 고래는, 짐짓 잔인한 고양이가 쥐를 다루듯, 그 가냘픈 삼나무 보트를 흔들었다."], ["With unastonished eyes Fedallah gazed, and crossed his arms; but the tiger-yellow crew were tumbling over each other’s heads to gain the uttermost stern.", "페달라는 놀라움 없는 눈으로 응시하며 팔짱을 꼈다. 그러나 호랑이처럼 누런 선원들은 서로의 머리를 타고 넘으며 고물 맨 끝으로 몰려갔다."]], [["And now, while both elastic gunwales were springing in and out, as the whale dallied with the doomed craft in this devilish way; and from his body being submerged beneath the boat, he could not be darted at from the bows, for the bows were almost inside of him, as it were; and while the other boats involuntarily paused, as before a quick crisis impossible to withstand, then it was that monomaniac Ahab, furious with this tantalizing vicinity of his foe, which placed him all alive and helpless in the very jaws he hated; frenzied with all this, he seized the long bone with his naked hands, and wildly strove to wrench it from its gripe.", "그리고 이제, 탄력 있는 양쪽 뱃전이 안팎으로 튀어 오르는 동안, 고래가 이 악마 같은 방식으로 운명 지어진 배를 희롱하는 동안, 그리고 그의 몸이 보트 아래에 잠겨 있던 탓에 뱃머리에서 그를 향해 작살을 던질 수 없었으니 — 뱃머리가 말하자면 거의 그의 몸속에 들어가 있었기 때문이다 — 다른 보트들이, 도저히 맞설 수 없는 급박한 위기를 앞둔 듯 저도 모르게 멈춰 선 동안, 그때였다, 편집광 에이해브가, 자기 적이 이토록 약 올리듯 가까이 있는 데에 격분하여 — 자신이 그토록 증오하던 바로 그 아가리 안에 산 채로 무력하게 놓이게 되자 — 이 모든 것에 광란하여, 맨손으로 그 긴 뼈를 움켜쥐고, 그것을 그 물린 데서 비틀어 떼어 내려 미친 듯 안간힘을 썼다."], ["As now he thus vainly strove, the jaw slipped from him; the frail gunwales bent in, collapsed, and snapped, as both jaws, like an enormous shears, sliding further aft, bit the craft completely in twain, and locked themselves fast again in the sea, midway between the two floating wrecks.", "그가 이렇게 헛되이 애쓰는 동안, 턱이 그의 손에서 미끄러졌다. 약한 뱃전이 안으로 휘어지더니, 무너지며 뚝 부러졌고, 두 턱이 거대한 가위처럼 더 뒤쪽으로 미끄러지며 배를 완전히 둘로 물어 끊고는, 두 동강 난 잔해 사이 한가운데서 다시 바다 속에 굳게 맞물렸다."], ["These floated aside, the broken ends drooping, the crew at the stern-wreck clinging to the gunwales, and striving to hold fast to the oars to lash them across.", "잔해들은 부러진 끝을 늘어뜨린 채 옆으로 떠내려갔고, 고물 쪽 잔해에 매달린 선원들은 뱃전을 붙들고서, 노를 단단히 붙잡아 가로질러 묶으려 애썼다."]], [["At that preluding moment, ere the boat was yet snapped, Ahab, the first to perceive the whale’s intent, by the crafty upraising of his head, a movement that loosed his hold for the time; at that moment his hand had made one final effort to push the boat out of the bite.", "그 서막의 순간, 보트가 아직 부러지기 전, 고래가 교활하게 머리를 치켜드는 것을 보고 — 그 동작에 잠시 물린 힘이 느슨해졌는데 — 그 의도를 맨 먼저 알아챈 에이해브는, 바로 그 순간 손으로 보트를 그 물린 데서 밀어내려 마지막 힘을 다해 보았다."], ["But only slipping further into the whale’s mouth, and tilting over sideways as it slipped, the boat had shaken off his hold on the jaw; spilled him out of it, as he leaned to the push; and so he fell flat-faced upon the sea.", "그러나 보트는 고래의 입속으로 더 깊이 미끄러져 들어갈 뿐이었고, 미끄러지면서 옆으로 기울어져, 그가 밀려고 몸을 기울인 차에, 보트는 그가 턱을 붙잡은 손을 떨쳐 버리고 그를 거기서 쏟아 냈으니, 그리하여 그는 얼굴을 처박은 채 바다 위로 떨어졌다."]], [["Ripplingly withdrawing from his prey, Moby Dick now lay at a little distance, vertically thrusting his oblong white head up and down in the billows; and at the same time slowly revolving his whole spindled body; so that when his vast wrinkled forehead rose—some twenty or more feet out of the water—the now rising swells, with all their confluent waves, dazzlingly broke against it; vindictively tossing their shivered spray still higher into the air.* So, in a gale, the but half baffled Channel billows only recoil from the base of the Eddystone, triumphantly to overleap its summit with their scud.", "먹잇감에게서 잔물결을 일으키며 물러난 모비 딕은, 이제 약간 떨어진 곳에 누워, 길쭉한 흰 머리를 너울 속에서 수직으로 위아래로 내밀었다. 동시에 그 방추형 몸 전체를 천천히 돌렸으니, 그리하여 그의 거대한 주름진 이마가 물 위로 스무 자 남짓 솟아오르자, 이제 부풀어 오르던 너울이, 한데 합류한 온 물결을 거느리고, 눈부시게 그 이마에 부딪쳐 부서지며, 산산이 흩어진 물보라를 앙갚음하듯 더 높이 허공으로 내던졌다. 이리하여, 폭풍 속에서, 절반쯤만 가로막힌 해협의 너울이 에디스톤 등대의 밑동에서 그저 물러났다가, 그 물보라로 의기양양하게 그 꼭대기를 뛰어넘는 것이다."]], [["*This motion is peculiar to the sperm whale.", "* 이 동작은 향유고래 특유의 것이다."], ["It receives its designation (pitchpoling) from its being likened to that preliminary up-and-down poise of the whale-lance, in the exercise called pitchpoling, previously described.", "이것은 앞서 설명한 피치폴링이라는 동작에서, 고래잡이 창을 던지기에 앞서 위아래로 가누어 균형 잡는 예비 자세에 빗대어 그 이름(피치폴링)을 얻었다."], ["By this motion the whale must best and most comprehensively view whatever objects may be encircling him.", "이 동작으로 고래는 자기를 둘러싼 어떤 대상이든 가장 잘, 그리고 가장 폭넓게 살필 수 있는 것이다."]], [["But soon resuming his horizontal attitude, Moby Dick swam swiftly round and round the wrecked crew; sideways churning the water in his vengeful wake, as if lashing himself up to still another and more deadly assault.", "그러나 곧 수평 자세를 되찾은 모비 딕은, 난파한 선원들 주위를 빙글빙글 빠르게 헤엄쳤다. 복수심에 찬 항적 속에서 옆으로 물을 휘저으니, 마치 또 한 번의, 더욱 치명적인 공격을 위해 스스로를 채찍질하는 듯했다."], ["The sight of the splintered boat seemed to madden him, as the blood of grapes and mulberries cast before Antiochus’s elephants in the book of Maccabees.", "산산조각 난 보트를 보자 그는 미쳐 날뛰는 듯했으니, 마치 마카베오기에서 안티오코스의 코끼리들 앞에 뿌려진 포도와 오디의 즙이 그러했듯 했다."], ["Meanwhile Ahab half smothered in the foam of the whale’s insolent tail, and too much of a cripple to swim,—though he could still keep afloat, even in the heart of such a whirlpool as that; helpless Ahab’s head was seen, like a tossed bubble which the least chance shock might burst.", "그러는 동안 에이해브는 고래의 오만한 꼬리가 일으킨 거품 속에 반쯤 파묻혀 있었고, 헤엄치기에는 너무도 성치 못한 몸이었으나 — 그래도 그런 소용돌이의 한복판에서조차 여전히 떠 있을 수는 있었다 — 무력한 에이해브의 머리는, 작은 충격에도 터져 버릴 듯 내던져진 거품 방울처럼 보였다."], ["From the boat’s fragmentary stern, Fedallah incuriously and mildly eyed him; the clinging crew, at the other drifting end, could not succor him; more than enough was it for them to look to themselves.", "부서진 보트의 고물 조각에서, 페달라는 무심하고 차분한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 다른 쪽 떠내려가는 끝에 매달린 선원들은 그를 구할 수 없었으니, 제 한 몸 건사하는 것만으로도 벅찼던 것이다."], ["For so revolvingly appalling was the White Whale’s aspect, and so planetarily swift the ever-contracting circles he made, that he seemed horizontally swooping upon them.", "흰 고래의 모습은 그토록 빙빙 돌며 소름 끼쳤고, 점점 좁혀 드는 그 원은 행성처럼 빨라서, 그가 마치 수평으로 그들을 덮쳐 오는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["And though the other boats, unharmed, still hovered hard by; still they dared not pull into the eddy to strike, lest that should be the signal for the instant destruction of the jeopardized castaways, Ahab and all; nor in that case could they themselves hope to escape.", "그리고 무사한 다른 보트들이 여전히 바로 곁에서 맴돌고 있었으나, 그래도 감히 소용돌이 안으로 노 저어 들어가 작살을 던지지는 못했으니, 그것이 위태로운 처지의 조난자들, 곧 에이해브와 모두를 그 자리에서 파멸시키는 신호가 될까 두려웠던 것이다. 게다가 그렇게 되면 그들 자신도 빠져나오기를 바랄 수 없었다."], ["With straining eyes, then, they remained on the outer edge of the direful zone, whose centre had now become the old man’s head.", "그리하여 그들은 핏발 선 눈으로, 그 무서운 구역의 바깥 가장자리에 머물러 있었으니, 그 중심은 이제 노인의 머리가 되어 있었다."]], [["Meantime, from the beginning all this had been descried from the ship’s mast heads; and squaring her yards, she had borne down upon the scene; and was now so nigh, that Ahab in the water hailed her!—“Sail on the”—but that moment a breaking sea dashed on him from Moby Dick, and whelmed him for the time. But struggling out of it again, and chancing to rise on a towering crest, he shouted,—“Sail on the whale!—Drive him off!”", "그러는 동안, 이 모든 일은 처음부터 배의 돛대 꼭대기에서 내려다보이고 있었다. 배는 활대를 가지런히 돌려 그 현장으로 밀고 내려왔고, 이제는 어찌나 가까웠던지, 물속의 에이해브가 배를 향해 외쳐 부를 정도였다. \"고래 위로 배를…….\" 그러나 그 순간 모비 딕에게서 부서진 파도가 그를 덮쳐, 잠시 그를 휩쓸어 버렸다. 그래도 그는 다시 그것을 헤치고 나와, 우연히 우뚝 솟은 물마루 위로 떠오른 차에 외쳤다. \"고래 위로 배를 몰아라! 그를 쫓아내라!\""]], [["The Pequod’s prows were pointed; and breaking up the charmed circle, she effectually parted the white whale from his victim.", "피쿼드호의 뱃머리가 겨냥을 정했다. 그리고 마법에 걸린 듯한 그 원을 깨뜨리며, 배는 흰 고래를 그 희생물로부터 효과적으로 갈라놓았다."], ["As he sullenly swam off, the boats flew to the rescue.", "고래가 시무룩하게 헤엄쳐 가는 동안, 보트들이 구조하러 날아왔다."]], [["Dragged into Stubb’s boat with blood-shot, blinded eyes, the white brine caking in his wrinkles; the long tension of Ahab’s bodily strength did crack, and helplessly he yielded to his body’s doom: for a time, lying all crushed in the bottom of Stubb’s boat, like one trodden under foot of herds of elephants.", "핏발 서 앞이 보이지 않는 눈으로, 주름 사이에 흰 소금이 엉겨 붙은 채, 스터브의 보트로 끌어 올려진 에이해브는, 그토록 오래 팽팽히 당겨졌던 육체의 힘이 끝내 툭 끊어져, 무력하게 제 몸의 운명에 굴복하고 말았다. 한동안 그는 코끼리 떼의 발에 짓밟힌 자처럼, 스터브의 보트 바닥에 짓이겨진 채 누워 있었다."], ["Far inland, nameless wails came from him, as desolate sounds from out ravines.", "그에게서 형언할 수 없는 통곡이, 마치 깊은 골짜기에서 새어 나오는 황량한 소리처럼, 저 먼 내륙으로부터 들려오는 듯했다."]], [["But this intensity of his physical prostration did but so much the more abbreviate it.", "그러나 그의 육체가 이토록 격렬하게 무너졌다는 사실은, 도리어 그만큼 더 그것을 짧게 줄였을 뿐이다."], ["In an instant’s compass, great hearts sometimes condense to one deep pang, the sum total of those shallow pains kindly diffused through feebler men’s whole lives.", "한순간의 폭 안에, 위대한 심장은 이따금 하나의 깊은 격통으로 응축되니, 그것은 더 연약한 인간들의 평생에 걸쳐 어질게 흩뿌려지는 그 얕은 고통들의 총합이다."], ["And so, such hearts, though summary in each one suffering; still, if the gods decree it, in their life-time aggregate a whole age of woe, wholly made up of instantaneous intensities; for even in their pointless centres, those noble natures contain the entire circumferences of inferior souls.", "그리하여 그런 심장들은, 비록 저마다의 고통에서는 한순간에 그칠지라도, 신들이 그리 정한다면, 그 생애 동안 한 시대 전체에 맞먹는 비애를 그러모으니, 그것은 온통 순간적인 격렬함으로 이루어진 것이다. 그 무딘 중심 속에서조차, 그 고귀한 천성들은 못한 영혼들의 둘레 전체를 품고 있기 때문이다."]], [["“The harpoon,”", "\"작살은,\""], ["said Ahab, half way rising, and draggingly leaning on one bended arm—“is it safe?”", "에이해브가 반쯤 몸을 일으키며, 굽힌 한쪽 팔에 힘겹게 기대어 말했다. \"무사한가?\""]], [["“Aye, sir, for it was not darted; this is it,”", "\"예, 선장님, 던지지 않았으니까요. 여기 있습니다.\""], ["said Stubb, showing it.", "스터브가 그것을 내보이며 말했다."]], [["“Lay it before me;—any missing men?”", "\"내 앞에 놓아라. 잃은 사람은 없나?\""]], [["“One, two, three, four, five;—there were five oars, sir, and here are five men.”", "\"하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯 — 노가 다섯이었고, 여기 사람도 다섯입니다.\""]], [["“That’s good.—Help me, man; I wish to stand.", "\"좋다. 나를 부축하게."], ["So, so, I see him!", "일어서고 싶다. 그래, 그래, 그가 보인다!"], ["there!", "저기! 저기!"], ["there!", "여전히 바람 아래쪽으로 가는구나."], ["going to leeward still; what a leaping spout!—Hands off from me!", "저 솟구치는 물줄기 좀 보라! 내게서 손을 떼라!"], ["The eternal sap runs up in Ahab’s bones again!", "영원한 수액이 에이해브의 뼛속에 다시 차오른다! 돛을 펴라!"], ["Set the sail; out oars; the helm!”", "노를 내라! 키를 잡아라!\""]], [["It is often the case that when a boat is stove, its crew, being picked up by another boat, help to work that second boat; and the chase is thus continued with what is called double-banked oars.", "보트가 부서지면, 그 선원들이 다른 보트에 구조되어 그 두 번째 보트를 함께 젓는 일이 흔히 있다. 그리하여 이른바 이중 배치된 노로 추격이 이어지는 것이다."], ["It was thus now.", "지금이 바로 그러했다."], ["But the added power of the boat did not equal the added power of the whale, for he seemed to have treble-banked his every fin; swimming with a velocity which plainly showed, that if now, under these circumstances, pushed on, the chase would prove an indefinitely prolonged, if not a hopeless one; nor could any crew endure for so long a period, such an unintermitted, intense straining at the oar; a thing barely tolerable only in some one brief vicissitude.", "그러나 보트에 더해진 힘은 고래에게 더해진 힘에 미치지 못했으니, 고래는 마치 모든 지느러미를 세 배로 늘려 배치한 듯, 빠른 속도로 헤엄쳤기 때문이다. 그 속도는, 만약 이런 상황에서 추격을 계속 밀어붙인다면, 그것이 절망적이지는 않더라도 한없이 길어지리라는 것을 분명히 보여 주었다."], ["The ship itself, then, as it sometimes happens, offered the most promising intermediate means of overtaking the chase.", "또한 어떤 선원도 그토록 오랜 시간, 그토록 쉼 없이 격렬하게 노에 힘을 쥐어짜는 것을 견뎌 낼 수 없었으니, 그것은 어떤 짧은 한 차례의 위기에서나 겨우 참아 낼 만한 일이었다. 그러므로 이따금 그러하듯, 배 자체가 추격을 따라잡는 가장 유망한 중간 수단이 되어 주었다."], ["Accordingly, the boats now made for her, and were soon swayed up to their cranes—the two parts of the wrecked boat having been previously secured by her—and then hoisting everything to her side, and stacking her canvas high up, and sideways outstretching it with stun-sails, like the double-jointed wings of an albatross; the Pequod bore down in the leeward wake of Moby-Dick.", "이에 따라 보트들은 이제 배를 향해 나아갔고, 곧 기중기에 끌어 올려졌다 — 부서진 보트의 두 조각은 앞서 배에 매어 둔 터였다. 그런 다음 모든 것을 뱃전으로 끌어 올리고, 돛을 높이 쌓아 올리고, 보조돛으로 옆으로 펼쳤으니, 그것은 마치 신천옹의 두 마디 접힌 날개와도 같았다."], ["At the well known, methodic intervals, the whale’s glittering spout was regularly announced from the manned mast-heads; and when he would be reported as just gone down, Ahab would take the time, and then pacing the deck, binnacle-watch in hand, so soon as the last second of the allotted hour expired, his voice was heard.—“Whose is the doubloon now?", "그리하여 피쿼드호는 모비 딕의 바람 아래쪽 항적을 따라 밀고 내려갔다. 잘 알려진 규칙적인 간격마다, 사람을 배치한 돛대 꼭대기에서 고래의 번쩍이는 물줄기가 규칙적으로 알려졌다."], ["D’ye see him?”", "그리고 그가 막 잠수했다고 보고되면, 에이해브는 시간을 재었다. 그러고는 갑판을 거닐며, 나침함 시계를 손에 든 채, 정해진 한 시간의 마지막 초가 지나가자마자 그의 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["and if the reply was, No, sir!", "\"이제 그 금화는 누구 것이냐? 그가 보이느냐?\""], ["straightway he commanded them to lift him to his perch.", "그리고 만약 응답이 \"아닙니다, 선장님!\" 하는 것이면, 그는 즉시 자기를 망대로 끌어 올리라 명했다."], ["In this way the day wore on; Ahab, now aloft and motionless; anon, unrestingly pacing the planks.", "이런 식으로 하루가 저물어 갔다. 에이해브는 어느 때는 망대 위에서 미동도 없이, 또 어느 때는 쉴 새 없이 갑판을 거닐었다."]], [["As he was thus walking, uttering no sound, except to hail the men aloft, or to bid them hoist a sail still higher, or to spread one to a still greater breadth—thus to and fro pacing, beneath his slouched hat, at every turn he passed his own wrecked boat, which had been dropped upon the quarter-deck, and lay there reversed; broken bow to shattered stern.", "이렇게 거니는 동안, 그는 망대 위의 사람들을 부르거나, 돛을 더 높이 올리라 이르거나, 더 넓게 펴라 명하는 것 말고는 아무 소리도 내지 않았다. 이렇게 이리저리 거닐며, 푹 눌러쓴 모자 아래로, 돌아설 때마다 그는 후갑판에 내려놓은 자신의 부서진 보트 곁을 지났으니, 그것은 거기 뒤집힌 채로 — 부러진 뱃머리가 박살 난 고물을 향한 채로 — 놓여 있었다."], ["At last he paused before it; and as in an already over-clouded sky fresh troops of clouds will sometimes sail across, so over the old man’s face there now stole some such added gloom as this.", "마침내 그는 그 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그리고 이미 잔뜩 흐린 하늘 위로 이따금 새로운 구름 떼가 가로질러 흘러가듯, 노인의 얼굴 위로 이제 그런 식의 어떤 어둠이 더하여 슬그머니 깃들었다."]], [["Stubb saw him pause; and perhaps intending, not vainly, though, to evince his own unabated fortitude, and thus keep up a valiant place in his Captain’s mind, he advanced, and eyeing the wreck exclaimed—“The thistle the ass refused; it pricked his mouth too keenly, sir; ha! ha!”", "스터브는 그가 멈춰 서는 것을 보았다. 그리고 아마도, 헛되지 않게, 자신의 꺾이지 않은 불굴의 기개를 드러내어 선장의 마음속에서 용감한 자리를 지켜 두려는 뜻이었던지, 그가 다가와 잔해를 바라보며 외쳤다. \"당나귀가 마다한 엉겅퀴군요. 그놈의 입을 너무 따끔하게 찔렀으니까요, 선장님. 하하! 하하!\""]], [["“What soulless thing is this that laughs before a wreck?", "\"난파선 앞에서 웃다니, 이 영혼 없는 물건은 대체 뭐란 말이냐? 이 사람아, 이 사람아!"], ["Man, man! did I not know thee brave as fearless fire (and as mechanical) I could swear thou wert a poltroon.", "내가 자네를 두려움 모르는 불처럼 용감하다고 (또 그처럼 기계적이라고) 알지 못했더라면, 자네를 비겁한 겁쟁이라 단언했을 것이다."], ["Groan nor laugh should be heard before a wreck.”", "난파선 앞에서는 신음도 웃음도 들려서는 안 되는 법이다.\""]], [["“Aye, sir,”", "\"예, 선장님,\" 스타벅이 다가오며 말했다."], ["said Starbuck drawing near, “’tis a solemn sight; an omen, and an ill one.”", "\"이건 엄숙한 광경입니다. 하나의 징조, 그것도 불길한 징조지요.\""]], [["“Omen?", "\"징조? 징조라고?"], ["omen?—the dictionary!", "그딴 건 사전에나 있는 말이다! 신들이 인간에게 대놓고 말하려 한다면, 그들은 떳떳이 대놓고 말할 것이다."], ["If the gods think to speak outright to man, they will honorably speak outright; not shake their heads, and give an old wives’ darkling hint.—Begone!", "고개를 절레절레 흔들며 노파들의 어두운 암시 따위나 주지는 않는다. 물러가라!"], ["Ye two are the opposite poles of one thing; Starbuck is Stubb reversed, and Stubb is Starbuck; and ye two are all mankind; and Ahab stands alone among the millions of the peopled earth, nor gods nor men his neighbors!", "너희 둘은 한 가지 것의 정반대 양극이다. 스타벅은 뒤집힌 스터브요, 스터브는 스타벅이다."], ["Cold, cold—I shiver!—How now?", "그리고 너희 둘이 곧 온 인류다. 그리고 에이해브는 이 사람 사는 땅 위 수백만 가운데 홀로 서 있으니, 신도 인간도 그의 이웃이 아니다!"], ["Aloft there!", "춥다, 추워 — 몸이 떨리는구나!"], ["D’ye see him?", "어찌 된 일이냐? 거기 망대 위!"], ["Sing out for every spout, though he spout ten times a second!”", "그가 보이느냐? 물줄기를 뿜을 때마다 외쳐라, 그가 일 초에 열 번을 뿜더라도!\""]], [["The day was nearly done; only the hem of his golden robe was rustling.", "하루가 거의 저물어, 다만 그 황금빛 옷자락만이 바스락거릴 뿐이었다."], ["Soon, it was almost dark, but the look-out men still remained unset.", "곧 거의 어두워졌으나, 망꾼들은 여전히 자리에서 내려오지 않았다."]], [["“Can’t see the spout now, sir;—too dark”—cried a voice from the air.", "\"이제 물줄기가 보이지 않습니다, 선장님 — 너무 어둡습니다.\" 허공에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["“How heading when last seen?”", "\"마지막으로 보았을 때 어느 쪽으로 향하고 있었나?\""]], [["“As before, sir,—straight to leeward.”", "\"전과 같습니다, 선장님 — 곧장 바람 아래쪽입니다.\""]], [["“Good!", "\"좋다!"], ["he will travel slower now ’tis night.", "밤이 되었으니 이제 더 느리게 갈 것이다. 최상부 돛과 윗돛의 보조돛을 내려라, 스타벅."], ["Down royals and top-gallant stun-sails, Mr.", "아침이 되기 전에 그를 지나쳐 버려서는 안 된다. 그는 지금 한 항로를 가는 중이니, 잠시 멈춰 표류할지도 모른다."], ["Starbuck.", "키잡이!"], ["We must not run over him before morning; he’s making a passage now, and may heave-to a while.", "바람을 가득 안고 곧장 나아가게 하라! 망대 위!"], ["Helm there!", "내려오라!"], ["keep her full before the wind!—Aloft!", "스터브, 앞돛대 꼭대기에 교대 인원을 보내, 아침까지 사람을 배치해 두게.\" 그러고는 큰 돛대의 금화를 향해 다가서며 말했다."], ["come down!—Mr.", "\"제군들, 이 금은 내 것이다, 내가 그것을 벌었으니까. 그러나 흰 고래가 죽을 때까지 나는 그것을 여기 그대로 두겠다."], ["Stubb, send a fresh hand to the fore-mast head, and see it manned till morning.”—Then advancing towards the doubloon in the main-mast—“Men, this gold is mine, for I earned it; but I shall let it abide here till the White Whale is dead; and then, whosoever of ye first raises him, upon the day he shall be killed, this gold is that man’s; and if on that day I shall again raise him, then, ten times its sum shall be divided among all of ye!", "그런 다음, 그가 죽임당하는 그날에, 너희 중 누구든 그를 가장 먼저 발견하는 자, 그 사람의 것이 이 금이다. 그리고 만약 그날 내가 다시 그를 발견하게 되면, 그때는 이 금의 열 배에 해당하는 액수를 너희 모두에게 나누어 주겠다!"], ["Away now!—the deck is thine, sir!”", "자, 이제 가라! 갑판은 자네 것일세, 선장!\""]], [["And so saying, he placed himself half way within the scuttle, and slouching his hat, stood there till dawn, except when at intervals rousing himself to see how the night wore on.", "이렇게 말하고서, 그는 승강구 안에 반쯤 몸을 들이고, 모자를 푹 눌러쓴 채, 동틀 녘까지 거기 서 있었다. 다만 이따금 몸을 일으켜 밤이 어떻게 깊어 가는지 살필 때만 빼고는."]], [["# CHAPTER 134. The Chase—Second Day.", "# CHAPTER 134. The Chase—Second Day."]], [["At day-break, the three mast-heads were punctually manned afresh.", "날이 밝자, 세 군데 돛대 꼭대기에는 어김없이 다시 망보는 이들이 배치되었다."]], [["“D’ye see him?”", "\"그놈이 보이느냐?\""], ["cried Ahab after allowing a little space for the light to spread.", "에이해브는 빛이 퍼질 약간의 여유를 준 뒤 외쳤다."]], [["“See nothing, sir.”", "\"아무것도 안 보입니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“Turn up all hands and make sail!", "\"전원 갑판으로 불러내고 돛을 올려라! 그놈은 내 짐작보다 더 빨리 움직인다."], ["he travels faster than I thought for;—the top-gallant sails!—aye, they should have been kept on her all night.", "윗돛을 올려라! 그래, 밤새 그 돛을 펴 두었어야 했어."], ["But no matter—’tis but resting for the rush.”", "하지만 상관없다. 한 번 내달리려고 잠시 쉬는 것일 뿐이니.\""]], [["Here be it said, that this pertinacious pursuit of one particular whale, continued through day into night, and through night into day, is a thing by no means unprecedented in the South sea fishery.", "여기서 한 가지 말해 둘 것은, 어느 특정한 고래 한 마리를 이처럼 집요하게 쫓아 낮에서 밤으로, 다시 밤에서 낮으로 추격을 이어 가는 일이 남양 포경업에서 결코 전례 없는 일이 아니라는 점이다."], ["For such is the wonderful skill, prescience of experience, and invincible confidence acquired by some great natural geniuses among the Nantucket commanders; that from the simple observation of a whale when last descried, they will, under certain given circumstances, pretty accurately foretell both the direction in which he will continue to swim for a time, while out of sight, as well as his probable rate of progression during that period.", "낸터킷의 어떤 위대한 타고난 천재 선장들은 놀라운 기량과 경험에서 우러난 예지, 그리고 무엇으로도 꺾을 수 없는 자신감을 갖추고 있어서, 고래가 마지막으로 목격되었을 때의 단순한 관찰만으로도, 특정한 주어진 상황에서라면, 그 고래가 시야에서 사라진 동안 한동안 헤엄쳐 갈 방향뿐만 아니라 그 기간에 나아갈 대략의 속도까지도 꽤 정확히 예측해 낸다. 그리고 이런 경우, 그것은 어느 정도 이런 도선사(導船士)와도 같다."], ["And, in these cases, somewhat as a pilot, when about losing sight of a coast, whose general trending he well knows, and which he desires shortly to return to again, but at some further point; like as this pilot stands by his compass, and takes the precise bearing of the cape at present visible, in order the more certainly to hit aright the remote, unseen headland, eventually to be visited: so does the fisherman, at his compass, with the whale; for after being chased, and diligently marked, through several hours of daylight, then, when night obscures the fish, the creature’s future wake through the darkness is almost as established to the sagacious mind of the hunter, as the pilot’s coast is to him.", "도선사는 그 대체적인 방향을 잘 알고 있고 머지않아 다시, 다만 좀 더 멀리 떨어진 어느 지점에서 돌아가고자 하는 해안이 시야에서 사라지려 할 때, 자기 나침반 곁에 서서 지금 보이는 곶의 정확한 방위를 잰다. 이는 결국 다시 찾아가게 될, 멀리 떨어져 보이지 않는 갑(岬)을 더욱 확실히 제대로 맞히기 위함이다."], ["So that to this hunter’s wondrous skill, the proverbial evanescence of a thing writ in water, a wake, is to all desired purposes well nigh as reliable as the steadfast land.", "어부도 자기 나침반 앞에서 고래를 두고 꼭 그렇게 한다. 여러 시간 환한 대낮에 고래를 쫓으며 부지런히 그 자취를 표시해 둔 뒤라면, 밤이 그 고래를 어둠 속에 가려 버려도, 어둠을 뚫고 나아갈 그 짐승의 앞으로의 항적은 도선사에게 해안이 그러하듯, 영민한 사냥꾼의 마음속에 거의 확정된 것이나 다름없다."], ["And as the mighty iron Leviathan of the modern railway is so familiarly known in its every pace, that, with watches in their hands, men time his rate as doctors that of a baby’s pulse; and lightly say of it, the up train or the down train will reach such or such a spot, at such or such an hour; even so, almost, there are occasions when these Nantucketers time that other Leviathan of the deep, according to the observed humor of his speed; and say to themselves, so many hours hence this whale will have gone two hundred miles, will have about reached this or that degree of latitude or longitude.", "그리하여 이 사냥꾼의 경탄할 만한 기량 앞에서는, 물 위에 쓴 글씨처럼 덧없다는 속담 속의 그 항적조차도, 바라는 모든 목적에 비추어 보면 흔들림 없는 육지만큼이나 거의 믿을 만한 것이 된다. 또한 현대 철도의 강력한 무쇠 리바이어던은 그 한 걸음 한 걸음까지 워낙 친숙하게 알려져 있어서, 사람들이 손에 시계를 들고 마치 의사가 아기의 맥박을 재듯 그 속도를 재며, 상행 열차나 하행 열차가 몇 시쯤이면 어느 지점에 닿을 것이라고 가볍게 말하는데, 거의 그와 같이, 이 낸터킷 사람들도 관찰된 그 속도의 기질에 따라 저 깊은 바다의 또 다른 리바이어던의 시간을 재며, 지금부터 몇 시간 뒤면 이 고래가 200마일을 갔을 것이고, 이러저러한 위도나 경도에 거의 다다라 있을 것이라고 혼잣말하는 경우가 있는 것이다."], ["But to render this acuteness at all successful in the end, the wind and the sea must be the whaleman’s allies; for of what present avail to the becalmed or windbound mariner is the skill that assures him he is exactly ninety-three leagues and a quarter from his port?", "그러나 이 예리함이 끝내 조금이라도 성공을 거두려면 바람과 바다가 포경자의 동맹이 되어 주어야 한다. 자기 항구로부터 정확히 93과 4분의 1리그 떨어져 있음을 일러 주는 기량이, 바람이 잦아들었거나 역풍에 묶인 뱃사람에게 지금 당장 무슨 소용이 있겠는가?"], ["Inferable from these statements, are many collateral subtile matters touching the chase of whales.", "이러한 진술들로부터 고래 추격과 관련된 미묘한 부수적 사항들이 여럿 추론될 수 있다."]], [["The ship tore on; leaving such a furrow in the sea as when a cannon-ball, missent, becomes a plough-share and turns up the level field.", "배는 맹렬히 내달렸다. 잘못 쏘아진 포탄이 보습이 되어 평평한 들판을 갈아엎듯, 바다에 깊은 고랑을 남기며 나아갔다."]], [["“By salt and hemp!”", "\"소금과 삼대에 걸고!\""], ["cried Stubb, “but this swift motion of the deck creeps up one’s legs and tingles at the heart.", "스터브가 외쳤다. \"이 갑판의 빠른 움직임이 다리를 타고 기어올라 심장을 짜릿하게 하는구나."], ["This ship and I are two brave fellows!—Ha, ha!", "이 배와 나는 둘 다 용감한 친구들이렷다! 하, 하!"], ["Some one take me up, and launch me, spine-wise, on the sea,—for by live-oaks!", "누가 날 들어다가, 등뼈를 아래로 해서 바다에 내던져 다오. 떡갈나무에 걸고!"], ["my spine’s a keel.", "내 등뼈가 곧 용골이니."], ["Ha, ha!", "하, 하!"], ["we go the gait that leaves no dust behind!”", "우리는 뒤에 먼지 한 톨 안 남기는 걸음으로 나아간다!\""]], [["“There she blows—she blows!—she blows!—right ahead!”", "\"저기 물을 뿜는다, 뿜어! 뿜는다!"], ["was now the mast-head cry.", "바로 앞쪽이다!\" 이제 돛대 꼭대기에서 외침이 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Aye, aye!”", "\"옳지, 옳지!\" 스터브가 외쳤다."], ["cried Stubb, “I knew it—ye can’t escape—blow on and split your spout, O whale!", "\"내 그럴 줄 알았다. 넌 빠져나갈 수 없어."], ["the mad fiend himself is after ye!", "실컷 뿜어 대라, 네 분수 구멍이 터지도록 뿜어 대라, 오 고래여! 미친 악마 그 자신이 네 뒤를 쫓고 있다!"], ["blow your trump—blister your lungs!—Ahab will dam off your blood, as a miller shuts his watergate upon the stream!”", "나팔을 불어라, 폐가 부르트도록 불어라! 에이해브가 네 피를 막아 버릴 테다, 방앗간 주인이 물길에 수문을 닫아걸듯이!\""]], [["And Stubb did but speak out for well nigh all that crew.", "스터브는 사실상 그 선원 전부의 심정을 대신 말한 것일 뿐이었다."], ["The frenzies of the chase had by this time worked them bubblingly up, like old wine worked anew.", "추격의 광기가 이때쯤이면 그들을 부글부글 끓어오르게 했으니, 마치 오래된 포도주가 새로이 발효되는 것과도 같았다."], ["Whatever pale fears and forebodings some of them might have felt before; these were not only now kept out of sight through the growing awe of Ahab, but they were broken up, and on all sides routed, as timid prairie hares that scatter before the bounding bison.", "그들 중 더러가 전에 느꼈을지 모를 창백한 두려움과 불길한 예감, 그것들은 이제 점점 자라나는 에이해브에 대한 외경(畏敬) 속에 보이지 않게 묻혀 버렸을 뿐만 아니라, 산산이 부서져 사방으로 쫓겨 달아났으니, 마치 껑충대며 달려드는 들소 앞에서 흩어지는 겁 많은 초원의 토끼들과도 같았다."], ["The hand of Fate had snatched all their souls; and by the stirring perils of the previous day; the rack of the past night’s suspense; the fixed, unfearing, blind, reckless way in which their wild craft went plunging towards its flying mark; by all these things, their hearts were bowled along.", "운명의 손이 그들의 영혼을 모조리 낚아채 갔다. 그리고 전날의 들끓는 위험, 지난밤의 긴장이 안겨 준 고문 같은 시간, 그들의 거친 배가 날아가는 표적을 향해 곤두박질치며 나아가던 그 고정되고, 두려움 없고, 눈멀고, 무모한 방식, 이 모든 것에 의해 그들의 마음은 굴러가듯 휩쓸려 갔다."], ["The wind that made great bellies of their sails, and rushed the vessel on by arms invisible as irresistible; this seemed the symbol of that unseen agency which so enslaved them to the race.", "그들의 돛을 큰 배(腹)처럼 부풀려 놓고, 보이지 않으면서도 거역할 수 없는 팔로 배를 떠밀어 내달리게 한 바람, 이것은 그들을 그토록 추격에 옭아맨 저 보이지 않는 힘의 상징인 듯했다."]], [["They were one man, not thirty.", "그들은 서른 명이 아니라 한 사람이었다."], ["For as the one ship that held them all; though it was put together of all contrasting things—oak, and maple, and pine wood; iron, and pitch, and hemp—yet all these ran into each other in the one concrete hull, which shot on its way, both balanced and directed by the long central keel; even so, all the individualities of the crew, this man’s valor, that man’s fear; guilt and guiltiness, all varieties were welded into oneness, and were all directed to that fatal goal which Ahab their one lord and keel did point to.", "그들 모두를 담은 한 척의 배가, 비록 떡갈나무와 단풍나무와 소나무, 쇠와 역청과 삼대처럼 온갖 대조되는 것들로 짜맞추어졌으되, 이 모든 것이 하나의 단단한 선체 안으로 녹아들어, 길게 뻗은 중앙 용골에 의해 균형 잡히고 방향이 잡힌 채 제 길로 내달렸듯이, 꼭 그렇게 선원들의 모든 개별성, 이 사람의 용맹과 저 사람의 두려움, 죄와 죄책감, 그 온갖 다양함이 하나로 용접되어, 그들의 유일한 군주이자 용골인 에이해브가 가리키는 저 치명적인 목표를 향해 모조리 겨누어져 있었다."]], [["The rigging lived.", "삭구(索具)가 살아 움직였다."], ["The mast-heads, like the tops of tall palms, were outspreadingly tufted with arms and legs.", "돛대 꼭대기는 키 큰 야자나무 우듬지처럼, 팔과 다리가 사방으로 술처럼 무성하게 돋아나 있었다."], ["Clinging to a spar with one hand, some reached forth the other with impatient wavings; others, shading their eyes from the vivid sunlight, sat far out on the rocking yards; all the spars in full bearing of mortals, ready and ripe for their fate.", "어떤 이들은 한 손으로 활대를 붙든 채 다른 한 손을 내뻗어 조바심치며 흔들어 댔고, 또 어떤 이들은 눈부신 햇빛을 손으로 가리며 흔들리는 활대 위 멀찍이 앉아 있었다. 모든 활대마다 인간들을 가득 매단 채, 그들 자신의 운명을 향해 준비되고 무르익어 있었다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["how they still strove through that infinite blueness to seek out the thing that might destroy them!", "그들은 저 무한한 푸르름을 뚫고 자신들을 파멸시킬지도 모를 그것을 찾아내려 어찌도 그리 안간힘을 쓰고 있었던가!"]], [["“Why sing ye not out for him, if ye see him?”", "\"그놈이 보이거든 어찌하여 소리쳐 알리지 않느냐?\""], ["cried Ahab, when, after the lapse of some minutes since the first cry, no more had been heard.", "첫 외침이 있은 뒤 몇 분이 지나도록 더는 아무 소리도 들리지 않자, 에이해브가 외쳤다. \"나를 위로 끌어올려라, 제군들."], ["“Sway me up, men; ye have been deceived; not Moby Dick casts one odd jet that way, and then disappears.”", "너희는 속은 것이다. 모비 딕은 저런 식으로 물줄기 한 번 어설피 뿜고 나서 사라지는 놈이 아니다.\""]], [["It was even so; in their headlong eagerness, the men had mistaken some other thing for the whale-spout, as the event itself soon proved; for hardly had Ahab reached his perch; hardly was the rope belayed to its pin on deck, when he struck the key-note to an orchestra, that made the air vibrate as with the combined discharges of rifles.", "과연 그러했다. 앞뒤 가리지 않는 열망에 휩싸여, 선원들은 다른 무언가를 고래의 물줄기로 잘못 본 것이었고, 이는 곧 사건 자체로 증명되었다."], ["The triumphant halloo of thirty buckskin lungs was heard, as—much nearer to the ship than the place of the imaginary jet, less than a mile ahead—Moby Dick bodily burst into view!", "에이해브가 미처 망대에 다다르기도 전에, 밧줄이 갑판의 고정 핀에 채 매이기도 전에, 그가 한 관현악단을 향해 으뜸음을 내질러, 마치 소총들을 한꺼번에 발사한 듯한 소리로 공기를 진동시켰던 것이다."], ["For not by any calm and indolent spoutings; not by the peaceable gush of that mystic fountain in his head, did the White Whale now reveal his vicinity; but by the far more wondrous phenomenon of breaching.", "사슴 가죽처럼 질긴 서른 개의 폐에서 터져 나온 의기양양한 함성이 울려 퍼졌으니, 상상 속 물줄기가 있던 자리보다 배에 훨씬 더 가까운 곳, 1마일도 채 안 되는 앞쪽에서 모비 딕이 그 거대한 몸뚱이를 통째로 드러내며 솟구쳐 올랐던 것이다!"], ["Rising with his utmost velocity from the furthest depths, the Sperm Whale thus booms his entire bulk into the pure element of air, and piling up a mountain of dazzling foam, shows his place to the distance of seven miles and more.", "흰 고래는 이번에는 고요하고 나른한 물뿜기로도, 제 머리에 달린 저 신비로운 샘에서 평화로이 솟구치는 물줄기로도 제 위치를 드러낸 것이 아니라, 훨씬 더 경이로운 현상인 도약(跳躍)으로 그리한 것이었다. 향유고래는 가장 깊은 심연으로부터 최대 속도로 솟구쳐 올라, 이렇듯 제 온몸을 순수한 공기의 원소 속으로 쏘아 올리고, 눈부신 거품의 산을 쌓아 올리며, 7마일이 넘는 먼 거리에까지 제 자리를 알린다."], ["In those moments, the torn, enraged waves he shakes off, seem his mane; in some cases, this breaching is his act of defiance.", "그 순간, 그가 떨쳐 내는 찢기고 성난 파도는 그의 갈기처럼 보이며, 어떤 경우에는 이 도약이 그의 도전 행위가 되기도 한다."]], [["“There she breaches!", "\"저기 솟구친다!"], ["there she breaches!”", "저기 솟구친다!\""], ["was the cry, as in his immeasurable bravadoes the White Whale tossed himself salmon-like to Heaven.", "흰 고래가 그 헤아릴 수 없는 허세 속에서 연어처럼 하늘을 향해 몸을 내던지자, 외침이 터져 나왔다."], ["So suddenly seen in the blue plain of the sea, and relieved against the still bluer margin of the sky, the spray that he raised, for the moment, intolerably glittered and glared like a glacier; and stood there gradually fading and fading away from its first sparkling intensity, to the dim mistiness of an advancing shower in a vale.", "푸른 바다 평원 위에 그토록 돌연히 나타나, 한층 더 푸른 하늘 가장자리를 배경으로 도드라진 채, 그가 일으킨 물보라는 그 순간 견딜 수 없으리만치 빙하처럼 반짝이며 번득였고, 그 자리에 머물러 처음의 그 눈부신 강렬함으로부터 차츰차츰 옅어지고 또 옅어지다가, 마침내 골짜기로 밀려드는 소나기의 어슴푸레한 안개처럼 스러져 갔다."]], [["“Aye, breach your last to the sun, Moby Dick!”", "\"옳지, 해를 향해 마지막 도약을 해 보아라, 모비 딕!\""], ["cried Ahab, “thy hour and thy harpoon are at hand!—Down!", "에이해브가 외쳤다. \"네 시간과 네게 박힐 작살이 지척에 있다!"], ["down all of ye, but one man at the fore.", "내려와라! 모두 내려와라, 다만 앞돛대에 한 사람만 남기고."], ["The boats!—stand by!”", "보트를 내려라! 준비하라!\""]], [["Unmindful of the tedious rope-ladders of the shrouds, the men, like shooting stars, slid to the deck, by the isolated backstays and halyards; while Ahab, less dartingly, but still rapidly was dropped from his perch.", "선원들은 삭구의 더딘 밧줄 사다리는 거들떠보지도 않고, 따로 떨어진 뒷버팀줄과 도르래 줄을 타고 별똥별처럼 갑판으로 미끄러져 내려왔다. 에이해브도 그처럼 쏜살같지는 않았으나 여전히 빠르게 망대에서 내려졌다."]], [["“Lower away,”", "\"보트를 내려라.\" 그는 자기 보트에 닿자마자 외쳤다."], ["he cried, so soon as he had reached his boat—a spare one, rigged the afternoon previous. “Mr.", "그것은 전날 오후에 의장(艤裝)을 갖춘 예비 보트였다."], ["Starbuck, the ship is thine—keep away from the boats, but keep near them.", "\"스타벅 씨, 배는 자네 것일세. 보트들에서 떨어져 있되, 가까이 머물게."], ["Lower, all!”", "모두 내려라!\""]], [["As if to strike a quick terror into them, by this time being the first assailant himself, Moby Dick had turned, and was now coming for the three crews.", "마치 단번에 그들에게 공포를 심어 주려는 듯, 이번에는 모비 딕 자신이 먼저 공격하는 자가 되어, 몸을 돌려 이제 세 보트의 선원들을 향해 다가오고 있었다. 에이해브의 보트가 한가운데 있었다."], ["Ahab’s boat was central; and cheering his men, he told them he would take the whale head-and-head,—that is, pull straight up to his forehead,—a not uncommon thing; for when within a certain limit, such a course excludes the coming onset from the whale’s sidelong vision.", "그는 부하들을 격려하며, 자기는 고래를 정면으로, 곧 그 이마를 향해 곧장 노 저어 가겠노라고 일렀다. 이는 그리 드문 일이 아니었으니, 일정한 거리 안에 들어가기만 하면 그런 항로는 고래의 옆쪽 시야에 다가오는 습격이 드러나지 않게 해 주기 때문이었다."], ["But ere that close limit was gained, and while yet all three boats were plain as the ship’s three masts to his eye; the White Whale churning himself into furious speed, almost in an instant as it were, rushing among the boats with open jaws, and a lashing tail, offered appalling battle on every side; and heedless of the irons darted at him from every boat, seemed only intent on annihilating each separate plank of which those boats were made.", "그러나 그 바싹 좁혀진 거리에 이르기도 전에, 아직 세 보트가 모두 그의 눈에 배의 세 돛대처럼 또렷이 보이는 동안, 흰 고래는 제 몸을 휘저어 맹렬한 속도를 내더니, 거의 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 입을 떡 벌리고 꼬리를 채찍처럼 후려치며 보트들 사이로 돌진해 들어와 사방으로 소름 끼치는 싸움을 걸어왔다. 그리고 보트마다 자기를 향해 던지는 작살에는 아랑곳없이, 오로지 그 보트들을 이루는 널빤지 한 장 한 장을 산산이 부숴 버리는 데만 골몰한 듯했다."], ["But skilfully manœuvred, incessantly wheeling like trained chargers in the field; the boats for a while eluded him; though, at times, but by a plank’s breadth; while all the time, Ahab’s unearthly slogan tore every other cry but his to shreds.", "그러나 보트들은 능숙하게 조종되어, 들판에서 잘 훈련된 군마처럼 쉴 새 없이 빙빙 돌며, 한동안 그를 피해 다녔다. 비록 때로는 그 간격이 널빤지 한 장 너비에 지나지 않았으나, 그러는 내내 에이해브의 섬뜩한 함성은 그의 것을 제외한 다른 모든 외침을 갈가리 찢어 버렸다."]], [["But at last in his untraceable evolutions, the White Whale so crossed and recrossed, and in a thousand ways entangled the slack of the three lines now fast to him, that they foreshortened, and, of themselves, warped the devoted boats towards the planted irons in him; though now for a moment the whale drew aside a little, as if to rally for a more tremendous charge. Seizing that opportunity, Ahab first paid out more line: and then was rapidly hauling and jerking in upon it again—hoping that way to disencumber it of some snarls—when lo!—a sight more savage than the embattled teeth of sharks!", "그러나 마침내 흰 고래는 그 종잡을 수 없는 움직임 속에서 이리저리 거듭 교차하며, 이제 제 몸에 단단히 박힌 세 가닥 밧줄의 느슨한 부분을 천 갈래로 뒤엉키게 만들었고, 그리하여 그 줄들이 짧아지면서 저절로 그 충실한 보트들을 그의 몸에 박힌 작살 쪽으로 끌어당겼다. 그러는가 싶더니 한순간 고래는 마치 한층 더 무시무시한 돌격을 위해 기운을 추스르려는 듯 약간 옆으로 물러났다. 그 기회를 붙잡아, 에이해브는 먼저 밧줄을 더 풀어 주었다가, 다시 그것을 빠르게 끌어당기고 홱홱 잡아채기 시작했으니, 그런 식으로 몇 군데 엉킨 매듭을 풀어 보려는 심산이었다. 그런데 보라! 상어의 이빨이 줄지어 늘어선 것보다 더 흉포한 광경이!"]], [["Caught and twisted—corkscrewed in the mazes of the line, loose harpoons and lances, with all their bristling barbs and points, came flashing and dripping up to the chocks in the bows of Ahab’s boat.", "밧줄의 미로 속에 걸리고 비틀려 나사처럼 휘감긴, 풀려난 작살과 창들이, 그 곤두선 미늘과 날카로운 끝을 모조리 드러낸 채, 번득이며 물방울을 뚝뚝 떨구며 에이해브 보트 뱃머리의 받침대까지 솟구쳐 올라왔다."], ["Only one thing could be done.", "할 수 있는 일은 단 하나뿐이었다."], ["Seizing the boat-knife, he critically reached within—through—and then, without—the rays of steel; dragged in the line beyond, passed it, inboard, to the bowsman, and then, twice sundering the rope near the chocks—dropped the intercepted fagot of steel into the sea; and was all fast again.", "그는 보트 칼을 움켜쥐고, 위태롭게도 강철 날들의 안쪽으로, 그 사이로, 그러고는 바깥쪽으로 손을 뻗어, 그 너머의 밧줄을 끌어당겨 뱃전 안쪽으로 넘겨 이물잡이에게 건넨 뒤, 받침대 가까이에서 밧줄을 두 번 끊어, 가로채인 강철 다발을 바다로 떨어뜨렸다. 그리하여 모든 것이 다시 단단히 묶였다."], ["That instant, the White Whale made a sudden rush among the remaining tangles of the other lines; by so doing, irresistibly dragged the more involved boats of Stubb and Flask towards his flukes; dashed them together like two rolling husks on a surf-beaten beach, and then, diving down into the sea, disappeared in a boiling maelstrom, in which, for a space, the odorous cedar chips of the wrecks danced round and round, like the grated nutmeg in a swiftly stirred bowl of punch.", "바로 그 순간, 흰 고래는 다른 밧줄들의 남은 엉킨 매듭 사이로 돌연 돌진했고, 그렇게 함으로써 더 깊이 얽혀든 스터브와 플래스크의 보트들을 거역할 수 없이 제 꼬리지느러미 쪽으로 끌어당겨, 파도에 시달리는 해변에서 굴러다니는 두 개의 깍지처럼 서로 맞부딪쳐 박살 내고는, 바닷속으로 잠겨 들며 부글거리는 소용돌이 속으로 사라졌다. 그 소용돌이 속에서, 한동안 난파선 잔해의 향기로운 삼나무 부스러기들이, 빠르게 휘저은 펀치 사발 속의 갈아 놓은 육두구처럼 빙글빙글 맴돌았다."]], [["While the two crews were yet circling in the waters, reaching out after the revolving line-tubs, oars, and other floating furniture, while aslope little Flask bobbed up and down like an empty vial, twitching his legs upwards to escape the dreaded jaws of sharks; and Stubb was lustily singing out for some one to ladle him up; and while the old man’s line—now parting—admitted of his pulling into the creamy pool to rescue whom he could;—in that wild simultaneousness of a thousand concreted perils,—Ahab’s yet unstricken boat seemed drawn up towards Heaven by invisible wires,—as, arrow-like, shooting perpendicularly from the sea, the White Whale dashed his broad forehead against its bottom, and sent it, turning over and over, into the air; till it fell again—gunwale downwards—and Ahab and his men struggled out from under it, like seals from a sea-side cave.", "두 보트의 선원들이 아직 물속에서 빙빙 돌며, 떠다니는 밧줄 통과 노, 그 밖에 둥둥 뜬 세간을 붙잡으려 손을 뻗는 동안, 비스듬히 기울어진 자세의 작달막한 플래스크가 빈 약병처럼 까닥까닥 떠올랐다 가라앉았다 하며 상어의 무시무시한 아가리를 피하려 두 다리를 위로 버둥거리는 동안, 스터브가 누구든 자기를 건져 올려 달라고 목청껏 외쳐 대는 동안, 그리고 이제 끊어져 가는 노인의 밧줄이 그에게 건질 수 있는 자를 구하러 그 우윳빛 웅덩이 속으로 노 저어 들어갈 여유를 허락하는 동안, 천 가지 응결된 위험들이 한꺼번에 몰아치는 그 거친 동시성 속에서, 에이해브의 아직 부서지지 않은 보트가 보이지 않는 줄에 의해 하늘로 끌어올려지는 듯했다. 흰 고래가 화살처럼 바다에서 수직으로 솟구쳐 올라, 그 넓은 이마를 보트 밑바닥에 들이받아, 보트를 빙글빙글 뒤집으며 공중으로 날려 보냈던 것이다. 마침내 보트가 다시 떨어져 뱃전이 아래를 향한 채 처박혔고, 에이해브와 그의 부하들은 마치 바닷가 동굴에서 빠져나오는 물범처럼 그 밑에서 허우적거리며 기어 나왔다."]], [["The first uprising momentum of the whale—modifying its direction as he struck the surface—involuntarily launched him along it, to a little distance from the centre of the destruction he had made; and with his back to it, he now lay for a moment slowly feeling with his flukes from side to side; and whenever a stray oar, bit of plank, the least chip or crumb of the boats touched his skin, his tail swiftly drew back, and came sideways smiting the sea.", "고래가 처음 솟구쳐 오른 그 추진력은, 수면을 들이받으면서 방향이 바뀌어, 본의 아니게 그를 수면을 따라 떠밀어, 그가 만들어 낸 파괴의 한가운데서 조금 떨어진 곳으로 데려갔다. 그리고 그곳을 등진 채, 그는 한순간 꼬리지느러미로 이쪽저쪽을 천천히 더듬으며 누워 있었다."], ["But soon, as if satisfied that his work for that time was done, he pushed his pleated forehead through the ocean, and trailing after him the intertangled lines, continued his leeward way at a traveller’s methodic pace.", "그러다 떠도는 노 한 자루나 널빤지 조각, 보트의 아주 작은 부스러기나 조각 하나라도 제 살갗에 닿을라치면, 그 꼬리는 잽싸게 뒤로 물러났다가 옆으로 휘둘러 바다를 후려쳤다. 그러나 곧, 이번 일은 다 끝났다고 흡족해진 듯, 그는 주름진 이마로 대양을 밀어 헤치며, 뒤엉킨 밧줄들을 제 뒤에 끌고서, 나그네의 한결같은 걸음걸이로 바람 부는 쪽을 향한 길을 계속 나아갔다."]], [["As before, the attentive ship having descried the whole fight, again came bearing down to the rescue, and dropping a boat, picked up the floating mariners, tubs, oars, and whatever else could be caught at, and safely landed them on her decks.", "전과 마찬가지로, 그 모든 싸움을 지켜본 주의 깊은 배가 다시금 구조하러 다가와, 보트 한 척을 내려, 물에 떠 있는 선원들과 밧줄 통, 노, 그 밖에 붙잡을 수 있는 것은 무엇이든 건져 올려, 무사히 갑판 위로 올려놓았다."], ["Some sprained shoulders, wrists, and ankles; livid contusions; wrenched harpoons and lances; inextricable intricacies of rope; shattered oars and planks; all these were there; but no fatal or even serious ill seemed to have befallen any one.", "어깨와 손목과 발목을 삔 자들, 검푸르게 멍든 타박상, 뒤틀린 작살과 창, 풀 길 없이 뒤엉킨 밧줄, 산산이 부서진 노와 널빤지, 이 모든 것이 거기 있었으나, 누구에게도 치명적이거나 중한 화는 미치지 않은 듯했다."], ["As with Fedallah the day before, so Ahab was now found grimly clinging to his boat’s broken half, which afforded a comparatively easy float; nor did it so exhaust him as the previous day’s mishap.", "전날의 페달라가 그러했듯, 에이해브도 이번에는 비교적 쉽게 떠 있게 해 준 자기 보트의 부러진 반쪽에 음울하게 매달려 있는 것이 발견되었다. 그리고 그것은 전날의 사고만큼 그를 기진맥진하게 하지는 않았다."]], [["But when he was helped to the deck, all eyes were fastened upon him; as instead of standing by himself he still half-hung upon the shoulder of Starbuck, who had thus far been the foremost to assist him.", "그러나 그가 부축을 받아 갑판으로 올라오자, 모든 눈길이 그에게 쏠렸다. 그가 혼자 서지 못하고, 여태껏 누구보다 앞장서서 그를 부축해 온 스타벅의 어깨에 여전히 반쯤 기대고 있었기 때문이다."], ["His ivory leg had been snapped off, leaving but one short sharp splinter.", "그의 상아 다리는 부러져 나가, 짧고 날카로운 파편 하나만이 남아 있었다."]], [["“Aye, aye, Starbuck, ’tis sweet to lean sometimes, be the leaner who he will; and would old Ahab had leaned oftener than he has.”", "\"옳지, 옳지, 스타벅, 때로는 기대는 것도 달콤한 법이지, 기댈 상대가 누구든 말이야. 늙은 에이해브가 여태보다 더 자주 기대었더라면 좋았을 것을.\""]], [["“The ferrule has not stood, sir,”", "\"테가 버텨 주지 못했군요, 선장님.\""], ["said the carpenter, now coming up; “I put good work into that leg.”", "이제 다가온 목수가 말했다. \"그 다리에는 좋은 솜씨를 들였는데 말입니다.\""]], [["“But no bones broken, sir, I hope,”", "\"하지만 뼈는 부러지지 않으셨겠지요, 선장님.\""], ["said Stubb with true concern.", "스터브가 진심으로 걱정하며 말했다."]], [["“Aye!", "\"그렇다!"], ["and all splintered to pieces, Stubb!—d’ye see it.—But even with a broken bone, old Ahab is untouched; and I account no living bone of mine one jot more me, than this dead one that’s lost.", "게다가 산산조각이 났지, 스터브! 자네도 보지 않는가."], ["Nor white whale, nor man, nor fiend, can so much as graze old Ahab in his own proper and inaccessible being.", "허나 뼈가 부러졌어도 늙은 에이해브는 멀쩡하다. 나는 이 몸에 살아 붙은 뼈 하나가, 잃어버린 이 죽은 뼈보다 조금이라도 더 나라고 여기지 않는다."], ["Can any lead touch yonder floor, any mast scrape yonder roof?—Aloft there!", "흰 고래도, 사람도, 악마도, 제 본연의, 다가갈 수 없는 존재 속에 있는 늙은 에이해브를 털끝만큼도 스치지 못한다. 그 어떤 측연(測鉛)이 저 바닥에 닿을 수 있으며, 그 어떤 돛대가 저 천장을 긁을 수 있겠는가?"], ["which way?”", "거기 위에! 어느 쪽이냐?\""]], [["“Dead to leeward, sir.”", "\"바람 불어가는 쪽으로 곧장입니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“Up helm, then; pile on the sail again, ship keepers!", "\"그렇다면 키를 올려라. 다시 돛을 잔뜩 펴라, 배 지키는 자들아!"], ["down the rest of the spare boats and rig them—Mr.", "나머지 예비 보트들을 내려 의장을 갖춰라."], ["Starbuck away, and muster the boat’s crews.”", "스타벅 씨는 가서 보트 선원들을 소집하게.\""]], [["“Let me first help thee towards the bulwarks, sir.”", "\"먼저 뱃전 쪽으로 부축해 드리겠습니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“Oh, oh, oh!", "\"오, 오, 오!"], ["how this splinter gores me now!", "이 파편이 지금 나를 어찌나 쑤셔 대는지!"], ["Accursed fate!", "저주받은 운명이여!"], ["that the unconquerable captain in the soul should have such a craven mate!”", "정복할 수 없는 영혼의 선장에게 어찌 이리도 겁 많은 부관이 붙어 있단 말인가!\""]], [["“Sir?”", "\"선장님?\""]], [["“My body, man, not thee.", "\"내 몸뚱이를 말한 것이다, 이 사람아, 자네가 아니라."], ["Give me something for a cane—there, that shivered lance will do.", "지팡이 삼을 무언가를 다오. 옳지, 저 부러진 창이면 되겠다."], ["Muster the men.", "사람들을 소집하라."], ["Surely I have not seen him yet.", "분명 내 그를 아직 보지 못했다."], ["By heaven it cannot be!—missing?—quick!", "맙소사, 그럴 리가 없다! 보이지 않는다고?"], ["call them all.”", "어서! 모두 불러 모아라.\""]], [["The old man’s hinted thought was true.", "노인이 넌지시 떠올린 생각은 사실이었다."], ["Upon mustering the company, the Parsee was not there.", "선원들을 소집해 보니, 파시교도가 거기 없었다."]], [["“The Parsee!”", "\"파시교도!\""], ["cried Stubb—“he must have been caught in——”", "스터브가 외쳤다. \"그는 분명 휩쓸린 게 틀림없——\""]], [["“The black vomit wrench thee!—run all of ye above, alow, cabin, forecastle—find him—not gone—not gone!”", "\"검은 토사물이 너를 비틀어 짜 버리기를! 모두 위로, 아래로, 선실로, 선수루로 뛰어가라. 그를 찾아내라. 사라진 게 아니다, 사라진 게 아니야!\""]], [["But quickly they returned to him with the tidings that the Parsee was nowhere to be found.", "그러나 그들은 곧 파시교도를 어디서도 찾을 수 없다는 소식을 들고 그에게 돌아왔다."]], [["“Aye, sir,”", "\"그렇습니다, 선장님.\" 스터브가 말했다."], ["said Stubb—“caught among the tangles of your line—I thought I saw him dragging under.”", "\"선장님의 밧줄이 엉킨 곳에 휘말렸어요. 그가 끌려 들어가는 걸 본 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“My line! my line? Gone?—gone? What means that little word?—What death-knell rings in it, that old Ahab shakes as if he were the belfry. The harpoon, too!—toss over the litter there,—d’ye see it?—the forged iron, men, the white whale’s—no, no, no,—blistered fool! this hand did dart it!—’tis in the fish!—Aloft there! Keep him nailed—Quick!—all hands to the rigging of the boats—collect the oars—harpooneers! the irons, the irons!—hoist the royals higher—a pull on all the sheets!—helm there! steady, steady for your life! I’ll ten times girdle the unmeasured globe; yea and dive straight through it, but I’ll slay him yet!”", "\"내 밧줄이라고? 내 밧줄? 사라졌다고? 사라져? 그 짧은 말이 무슨 뜻이란 말이냐? 그 속에 무슨 조종(弔鐘)이 울리기에, 늙은 에이해브가 마치 제가 종루라도 된 양 이리 떨린단 말인가. 작살도! 거기 잡동사니를 뒤집어 보아라. 보이느냐? 그 벼려 낸 무쇠, 제군들, 흰 고래의 그것이——아니, 아니, 아니다, 부어터질 멍청이 같으니! 이 손이 그걸 던졌지! 그건 고래 몸속에 있다! 거기 위에! 그놈에게서 눈을 떼지 마라. 어서! 전원 보트 의장에 매달려라. 노를 모아라. 작살잡이들아! 작살을, 작살을! 윗돛을 더 높이 올려라. 모든 돛 줄을 잡아당겨라! 거기 키잡이! 침착하게, 목숨을 걸고 침착하게! 나는 이 헤아릴 수 없는 지구를 열 바퀴라도 두르겠다. 아니, 그것을 곧장 꿰뚫고 잠수해서라도, 기어이 그놈을 죽이고 말리라!\""]], [["“Great God!", "\"위대하신 하느님이여! 단 한순간만이라도 모습을 보여 주소서,\""], ["but for one single instant show thyself,”", "스타벅이 외쳤다. \"선장님은 결코, 결코 그놈을 잡지 못하실 겁니다, 노인장."], ["cried Starbuck; “never, never wilt thou capture him, old man—In Jesus’ name no more of this, that’s worse than devil’s madness.", "예수님의 이름으로 비노니, 이런 짓은 그만두십시오. 이건 악마의 광기보다도 더한 것입니다."], ["Two days chased; twice stove to splinters; thy very leg once more snatched from under thee; thy evil shadow gone—all good angels mobbing thee with warnings:—what more wouldst thou have?—Shall we keep chasing this murderous fish till he swamps the last man?", "이틀을 쫓았고, 두 번이나 산산조각으로 부서졌으며, 선장님의 다리마저 또 한 번 몸 아래에서 뜯겨 나갔습니다. 선장님의 사악한 그림자도 사라졌습니다."], ["Shall we be dragged by him to the bottom of the sea?", "온갖 선한 천사들이 떼 지어 경고로 선장님을 둘러싸고 있습니다. 더 무엇을 바라십니까?"], ["Shall we be towed by him to the infernal world?", "이 살인마 같은 물고기가 마지막 한 사람까지 물에 빠뜨려 버릴 때까지 계속 쫓아야 합니까? 그놈에게 끌려 바다 밑바닥까지 가야 합니까?"], ["Oh, oh,—Impiety and blasphemy to hunt him more!”", "그놈에게 이끌려 지옥의 세계로 끌려가야 합니까? 오, 오, 그놈을 더 사냥하는 것은 불경이요 신성모독입니다!\""]], [["“Starbuck, of late I’ve felt strangely moved to thee; ever since that hour we both saw—thou know’st what, in one another’s eyes.", "\"스타벅, 요즈음 나는 자네에게 이상하게 마음이 끌렸다네. 우리 둘이 서로의 눈 속에서, 자네도 알다시피 그것을 본 그 순간 이래로 말이야."], ["But in this matter of the whale, be the front of thy face to me as the palm of this hand—a lipless, unfeatured blank.", "허나 이 고래의 일에서만큼은, 자네 얼굴의 앞면을 내게 이 손바닥처럼 만들어 다오. 입술도 없고 이목구비도 없는 텅 빈 백지로 말이야."], ["Ahab is for ever Ahab, man.", "에이해브는 영원히 에이해브다, 이 사람아."], ["This whole act’s immutably decreed.", "이 모든 막은 바꿀 수 없이 정해져 있다."], ["’Twas rehearsed by thee and me a billion years before this ocean rolled.", "이 대양이 굽이치기 십억 년 전에 자네와 내가 이미 연습한 것이다."], ["Fool!", "어리석은 자여!"], ["I am the Fates’ lieutenant; I act under orders.", "나는 운명의 부관이다. 나는 명령에 따라 움직인다."], ["Look thou, underling!", "보아라, 아랫것이여! 자네가 내 명령에 복종하듯이 말이다."], ["that thou obeyest mine.—Stand round me, men.", "내 둘레에 모여라, 제군들. 너희는 한 늙은이가 그루터기만 남도록 베인 모습을 본다."], ["Ye see an old man cut down to the stump; leaning on a shivered lance; propped up on a lonely foot.", "부러진 창에 기대어, 외로운 한 발로 버티고 선 모습을. 이것이 에이해브다."], ["’Tis Ahab—his body’s part; but Ahab’s soul’s a centipede, that moves upon a hundred legs.", "그의 몸뚱이의 부분이지. 허나 에이해브의 영혼은 백 개의 다리로 움직이는 지네다."], ["I feel strained, half stranded, as ropes that tow dismasted frigates in a gale; and I may look so.", "나는 긴장하여 반쯤 좌초된 느낌이다. 돌풍 속에서 돛대 잃은 군함을 끌고 가는 밧줄처럼."], ["But ere I break, ye’ll hear me crack; and till ye hear that, know that Ahab’s hawser tows his purpose yet.", "내 꼴이 그리 보일지도 모르지. 허나 내가 끊어지기 전에, 너희는 내가 우지끈 부러지는 소리를 들으리라."], ["Believe ye, men, in the things called omens?", "그리고 그 소리를 듣기 전까지는, 에이해브의 닻줄이 아직도 제 목적을 끌고 간다는 것을 알아 두어라."], ["Then laugh aloud, and cry encore!", "제군들아, 너희는 징조라 불리는 것들을 믿느냐?"], ["For ere they drown, drowning things will twice rise to the surface; then rise again, to sink for evermore.", "그렇다면 소리 내어 웃어라, 그리고 한 번 더 외쳐라! 물에 빠져 죽기 전에, 빠져 가는 것들은 두 번 수면 위로 떠오르고, 그러고는 다시 한 번 떠올랐다가, 영영 가라앉는 법이니."], ["So with Moby Dick—two days he’s floated—tomorrow will be the third.", "모비 딕도 그러하다. 이틀을 떠올라 있었으니, 내일이 사흘째가 되리라."], ["Aye, men, he’ll rise once more,—but only to spout his last!", "그렇다, 제군들아, 그놈은 한 번 더 떠오를 것이다. 허나 그것은 다만 마지막 물줄기를 뿜기 위함이리라!"], ["D’ye feel brave men, brave?”", "너희는 용감하냐, 제군들아, 용감하냐?\""]], [["“As fearless fire,”", "\"두려움 없는 불길처럼이요.\""], ["cried Stubb.", "스터브가 외쳤다."]], [["“And as mechanical,”", "\"그리고 기계처럼이지.\" 에이해브가 중얼거렸다."], ["muttered Ahab.", "그러고는 사람들이 앞쪽으로 가자, 그는 계속 중얼거렸다."], ["Then as the men went forward, he muttered on: “The things called omens!", "\"징조라 불리는 것들! 그리고 어제 나는 저기 스타벅에게 부서진 내 보트를 두고 똑같은 말을 했지."], ["And yesterday I talked the same to Starbuck there, concerning my broken boat.", "오! 내 안에 그토록 단단히 박혀 있는 것을, 다른 이들의 마음에서는 어찌도 그리 용감하게 몰아내려 애쓰는가!"], ["Oh!", "파시교도가, 파시교도가 사라졌다고, 사라졌어?"], ["how valiantly I seek to drive out of others’ hearts what’s clinched so fast in mine!—The Parsee—the Parsee!—gone, gone?", "게다가 그는 나보다 먼저 가기로 되어 있었지. 허나 내가 죽기 전에 다시 한번 보이기로 되어 있었거늘."], ["and he was to go before:—but still was to be seen again ere I could perish—How’s that?—There’s a riddle now might baffle all the lawyers backed by the ghosts of the whole line of judges:—like a hawk’s beak it pecks my brain.", "그게 어찌 된 일인가? 여기 한 수수께끼가 있구나, 모든 변호사들이 역대 판사들의 망령을 죄다 등에 업고 덤벼도 풀지 못할 수수께끼가."], ["I’ll, I’ll solve it, though!”", "매의 부리처럼 그것이 내 뇌를 쪼아 댄다. 허나 내가, 내가 기어이 풀고야 말리라!\""]], [["When dusk descended, the whale was still in sight to leeward.", "황혼이 내려앉을 무렵, 고래는 여전히 바람 불어가는 쪽에 보였다."]], [["So once more the sail was shortened, and everything passed nearly as on the previous night; only, the sound of hammers, and the hum of the grindstone was heard till nearly daylight, as the men toiled by lanterns in the complete and careful rigging of the spare boats and sharpening their fresh weapons for the morrow. Meantime, of the broken keel of Ahab’s wrecked craft the carpenter made him another leg; while still as on the night before, slouched Ahab stood fixed within his scuttle; his hid, heliotrope glance anticipatingly gone backward on its dial; sat due eastward for the earliest sun.", "그리하여 다시 한번 돛이 줄여졌고, 모든 것이 전날 밤과 거의 똑같이 흘러갔다. 다만, 망치 소리와 숫돌 가는 소리가 날이 거의 밝을 때까지 들렸으니, 선원들이 등불을 밝히고 예비 보트들의 의장을 완벽하고 꼼꼼하게 갖추며 내일을 위해 새 무기들을 벼리느라 고되게 일했기 때문이다. 그러는 동안, 목수는 에이해브의 부서진 보트의 부러진 용골로 그에게 새 다리를 만들어 주었다. 한편 에이해브는 전날 밤과 다름없이, 구부정한 자세로 자기 승강구 안에 붙박인 듯 서 있었다. 감추어진, 해바라기 같은 그의 시선은 미리 제 해시계 위를 거슬러 돌아가, 가장 이른 해를 맞으려 동쪽을 향한 채로 자리 잡고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 135. The Chase.—Third Day.", "# CHAPTER 135. The Chase.—Third Day."]], [["The morning of the third day dawned fair and fresh, and once more the solitary night-man at the fore-mast-head was relieved by crowds of the daylight look-outs, who dotted every mast and almost every spar.", "셋째 날 아침이 맑고 신선하게 밝아 왔다. 앞돛대 꼭대기에서 홀로 밤을 지새우던 망보기 선원은 다시 한 번, 낮의 망꾼 무리에게 자리를 넘겨주었다. 그들은 모든 돛대와 거의 모든 활대마다 점점이 들어찼다."]], [["“D’ye see him?”", "\"놈이 보이느냐?\""], ["cried Ahab; but the whale was not yet in sight.", "에이해브가 외쳤다. 그러나 고래는 아직 시야에 없었다."]], [["“In his infallible wake, though; but follow that wake, that’s all.", "\"그래도 놈이 남긴 그 틀림없는 항적을 따라가면 된다. 그 항적만 좇아라, 그뿐이다."], ["Helm there; steady, as thou goest, and hast been going.", "키잡이! 그대로 똑바로, 지금껏 가던 대로 침착하게 가라."], ["What a lovely day again!", "오늘도 또 이리 사랑스러운 날이로구나!"], ["were it a new-made world, and made for a summer-house to the angels, and this morning the first of its throwing open to them, a fairer day could not dawn upon that world.", "이 세상이 갓 빚어진 새 세상이라 하여 천사들을 위한 여름 별장으로 지어졌다 한들, 그리고 오늘 아침이 그들에게 처음으로 그 문을 활짝 열어 주는 날이라 한들, 이보다 더 아름다운 날은 그 세상에 밝아 오지 못하리라. 여기 곱씹어 볼 거리가 있구나, 에이해브에게 사색할 겨를이 있다면 말이다."], ["Here’s food for thought, had Ahab time to think; but Ahab never thinks; he only feels, feels, feels; that’s tingling enough for mortal man!", "하지만 에이해브는 결코 사색하지 않는다. 다만 느낄 뿐, 느끼고, 느낄 뿐이다."], ["to think’s audacity.", "그것만으로도 죽어야 할 인간에게는 충분히 짜릿하다!"], ["God only has that right and privilege.", "사색이란 만용이다. 오직 하느님만이 그 권리와 특권을 지닌다."], ["Thinking is, or ought to be, a coolness and a calmness; and our poor hearts throb, and our poor brains beat too much for that.", "사색이란 차가움이자 고요함이거나, 마땅히 그래야 하는 것이다. 그러나 우리의 가련한 심장은 너무 거세게 뛰고, 우리의 가련한 머릿속은 너무 요란하게 박동한다."], ["And yet, I’ve sometimes thought my brain was very calm—frozen calm, this old skull cracks so, like a glass in which the contents turned to ice, and shiver it.", "그렇지만 나는 때로 내 머릿속이 무척 차분하다고, 얼어붙은 듯 차분하다고 여겨 왔다. 이 늙은 두개골이 이렇게 갈라지는 것을 보라, 마치 그 속의 내용물이 얼음으로 변해 잔을 산산이 쪼개 버리는 유리잔처럼."], ["And still this hair is growing now; this moment growing, and heat must breed it; but no, it’s like that sort of common grass that will grow anywhere, between the earthy clefts of Greenland ice or in Vesuvius lava.", "그런데도 이 머리카락은 지금도 자라고 있다. 이 순간에도 자라고 있으니, 열기가 그것을 키워 내야 마땅하건만, 아니다, 이것은 어디서든 자라나는 흔한 풀과 같아서, 그린란드 얼음의 흙 갈라진 틈새든 베수비오의 용암 위든 가리지 않는다."], ["How the wild winds blow it; they whip it about me as the torn shreds of split sails lash the tossed ship they cling to.", "거센 바람이 어찌나 이 머리칼을 휘날리는지, 마치 찢긴 돛 조각들이 그것이 매달린 흔들리는 배를 후려치듯, 머리칼이 내 주위로 채찍질을 해 댄다."], ["A vile wind that has no doubt blown ere this through prison corridors and cells, and wards of hospitals, and ventilated them, and now comes blowing hither as innocent as fleeces.", "비열한 바람이로구나, 의심할 바 없이 이전에 감옥의 복도와 감방을, 그리고 병원의 병동을 헤집고 불어 그곳들을 환기시키고는, 이제 양털처럼 천진하게 이리로 불어오니."], ["Out upon it!—it’s tainted.", "꺼져 버려라! 더럽혀진 바람이다."], ["Were I the wind, I’d blow no more on such a wicked, miserable world.", "내가 바람이라면, 이토록 사악하고 비참한 세상에는 다시는 불지 않으리라."], ["I’d crawl somewhere to a cave, and slink there.", "어딘가 동굴로 기어들어 가 거기 숨어 버리리라."], ["And yet, ’tis a noble and heroic thing, the wind!", "그렇지만 바람이란 고귀하고 영웅적인 것이로다!"], ["who ever conquered it?", "일찍이 누가 그것을 정복한 적이 있던가?"], ["In every fight it has the last and bitterest blow.", "어떤 싸움에서든 마지막의, 그리고 가장 쓰라린 일격은 바람의 몫이다."], ["Run tilting at it, and you but run through it.", "창을 겨누고 달려들어 보라, 그저 그것을 꿰뚫고 지나갈 뿐이리라."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["a coward wind that strikes stark naked men, but will not stand to receive a single blow.", "발가벗은 사내들을 후려치면서도 단 한 번의 반격조차 받아 내려 버티지 않는, 겁쟁이 바람이로다."], ["Even Ahab is a braver thing—a nobler thing than that.", "에이해브조차 그보다는 더 용감한 존재요, 더 고귀한 존재다."], ["Would now the wind but had a body; but all the things that most exasperate and outrage mortal man, all these things are bodiless, but only bodiless as objects, not as agents.", "이제 바람에게 몸뚱이라도 있다면 좋으련만. 그러나 죽어야 할 인간을 가장 분노케 하고 격노케 하는 모든 것들은 죄다 형체가 없으니, 다만 객체로서 형체가 없을 뿐 행위자로서 형체가 없는 것은 아니다."], ["There’s a most special, a most cunning, oh, a most malicious difference!", "거기에 더없이 특별하고, 더없이 교활하며, 오, 더없이 악의에 찬 차이가 있도다!"], ["And yet, I say again, and swear it now, that there’s something all glorious and gracious in the wind.", "그렇지만 다시 말하노니, 지금 맹세컨대, 바람에는 영광스럽고 자애로운 무언가가 깃들어 있다."], ["These warm Trade Winds, at least, that in the clear heavens blow straight on, in strong and steadfast, vigorous mildness; and veer not from their mark, however the baser currents of the sea may turn and tack, and mightiest Mississippies of the land swift and swerve about, uncertain where to go at last.", "적어도 이 따스한 무역풍은, 맑은 하늘에서 굳세고 한결같게, 활기찬 부드러움으로 곧장 불어오며, 바다의 저급한 해류들이 아무리 방향을 틀고 돌아간들, 뭍의 가장 거대한 미시시피 강들이 아무리 빠르게 비틀거리며 끝내 어디로 가야 할지 갈피를 못 잡은들, 결코 제 목표에서 벗어나지 않는다."], ["And by the eternal Poles!", "그리고 영원한 양극을 두고 맹세하노니!"], ["these same Trades that so directly blow my good ship on; these Trades, or something like them—something so unchangeable, and full as strong, blow my keeled soul along!", "내 좋은 배를 이토록 곧게 밀어 주는 이 무역풍, 이 무역풍이, 혹은 그와 비슷한 무언가가, 그토록 변함없고 그만큼 굳센 무언가가, 용골을 단 내 영혼을 앞으로 밀어 가는도다! 자, 가자!"], ["To it!", "위에 있는 자들!"], ["Aloft there!", "무엇이 보이느냐?\""], ["What d’ye see?”", "\"아무것도 없습니다, 선장님.\""]], [["“Nothing, sir.”", "\"아무것도 없다!"], ["“Nothing!", "정오가 코앞인데!"], ["and noon at hand!", "금화가 임자 없이 구걸하는 신세로구나!"], ["The doubloon goes a-begging!", "해를 보라! 그래, 그래, 그래야만 하리라."], ["See the sun!", "내가 놈을 지나쳐 와 버렸구나."], ["Aye, aye, it must be so.", "어쩌다 놈에게 앞길을 내주었단 말이냐?"], ["I’ve oversailed him.", "그래, 이제는 놈이 나를 쫓고 있는 게로구나."], ["How, got the start?", "내가 놈을 쫓는 게 아니라."], ["Aye, he’s chasing me now; not I, him—that’s bad; I might have known it, too.", "그건 나쁜 징조다. 진작 알았어야 했거늘."], ["Fool!", "멍청이!"], ["the lines—the harpoons he’s towing.", "밧줄들이며, 놈이 끌고 다니는 작살들을 떠올렸어야지."], ["Aye, aye, I have run him by last night.", "그래, 그래, 간밤에 내가 놈을 지나쳐 와 버린 거다."], ["About!", "돌려라!"], ["about!", "돌려!"], ["Come down, all of ye, but the regular look outs!", "모두 내려오너라, 정규 망꾼들만 빼고!"], ["Man the braces!”", "아딧줄 당기는 자리에 붙어라!\""]], [["Steering as she had done, the wind had been somewhat on the Pequod’s quarter, so that now being pointed in the reverse direction, the braced ship sailed hard upon the breeze as she rechurned the cream in her own white wake.", "지금껏 가던 대로 항해할 때는 바람이 피쿼드(Pequod)호의 고물 쪽 비스듬한 방향에서 불어오고 있었으나, 이제 뱃머리를 정반대로 돌리자, 아딧줄을 팽팽히 당긴 배는 제가 남긴 새하얀 항적 속의 거품을 다시 휘저으며 바람을 거슬러 힘겹게 나아갔다."]], [["“Against the wind he now steers for the open jaw,”", "\"이제 바람을 거슬러 떡 벌린 아가리를 향해 키를 잡는구나.\""], ["murmured Starbuck to himself, as he coiled the new-hauled main-brace upon the rail.", "스타벅(Starbuck)은 새로 끌어당긴 큰돛 아딧줄을 난간에 감으며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["“God keep us, but already my bones feel damp within me, and from the inside wet my flesh.", "\"하느님, 우리를 지켜 주소서. 하나 벌써부터 내 뼛속이 축축이 젖어 들고, 그 안에서부터 살까지 적셔 오는구나."], ["I misdoubt me that I disobey my God in obeying him!”", "저 사람을 따름으로써 내 하느님을 거역하는 것은 아닐까, 나는 의심스럽다!\""]], [["“Stand by to sway me up!”", "\"나를 끌어올릴 채비를 하라!\""], ["cried Ahab, advancing to the hempen basket.", "에이해브가 삼노끈으로 짠 바구니로 다가서며 외쳤다."], ["“We should meet him soon.”", "\"머지않아 놈과 마주치게 될 것이다.\""]], [["“Aye, aye, sir,”", "\"예, 예, 선장님.\""], ["and straightway Starbuck did Ahab’s bidding, and once more Ahab swung on high.", "스타벅은 곧장 에이해브의 명을 따랐고, 에이해브는 다시 한 번 높은 곳으로 매달려 올라갔다."]], [["A whole hour now passed; gold-beaten out to ages.", "이제 꼬박 한 시간이 흘렀다. 영겁의 세월처럼 얇게 두드려 펴진 시간이었다."], ["Time itself now held long breaths with keen suspense.", "이제 시간 그 자체가 날카로운 긴장 속에 긴 숨을 멈추고 있었다."], ["But at last, some three points off the weather bow, Ahab descried the spout again, and instantly from the three mast-heads three shrieks went up as if the tongues of fire had voiced it.", "그러나 마침내, 바람을 받는 뱃머리에서 세 방위쯤 떨어진 곳에서 에이해브는 다시 물줄기를 발견했고, 그 순간 세 돛대 꼭대기에서 마치 불의 혀들이 그것을 외치기라도 한 듯 세 줄기 비명이 솟구쳐 올랐다."]], [["“Forehead to forehead I meet thee, this third time, Moby Dick!", "\"이마와 이마를 맞대고 너와 마주하노라, 이 세 번째로구나, 모비 딕(Moby Dick)!"], ["On deck there!—brace sharper up; crowd her into the wind’s eye.", "갑판에 있는 자들아! 아딧줄을 더 바짝 당겨라, 배를 바람의 눈 속으로 몰아넣어라."], ["He’s too far off to lower yet, Mr. Starbuck.", "놈은 아직 보트를 내리기엔 너무 멀리 있다, 스타벅 씨."], ["The sails shake!", "돛이 떨리는구나!"], ["Stand over that helmsman with a top-maul!", "저 키잡이 곁에 망치를 들고 버티고 서라! 그래, 그래."], ["So, so; he travels fast, and I must down.", "놈은 빠르게 나아가고, 나는 내려가야 한다."], ["But let me have one more good round look aloft here at the sea; there’s time for that.", "하나 여기 위에서 바다를 한 번만 더 잘 둘러보게 해 다오. 그럴 시간은 있다."], ["An old, old sight, and yet somehow so young; aye, and not changed a wink since I first saw it, a boy, from the sand-hills of Nantucket!", "오래되고 또 오래된 광경이건만, 어쩐지 이리도 젊구나. 그래, 내가 소년 시절 낸터킷(Nantucket)의 모래언덕에서 처음 보았던 그때와 눈 한 번 깜박할 만큼도 변하지 않았구나!"], ["The same!—the same!—the same to Noah as to me.", "한결같다!"], ["There’s a soft shower to leeward.", "한결같아! 노아에게나 내게나 한결같도다."], ["Such lovely leewardings!", "바람 불어가는 쪽에 부드러운 소나기가 내린다."], ["They must lead somewhere—to something else than common land, more palmy than the palms.", "어쩌면 저리도 사랑스러운 바람 아랫녘인가! 저것들은 분명 어딘가로, 흔한 뭍과는 다른 어딘가로, 종려나무보다 더 종려나무다운 곳으로 이어지리라."], ["Leeward!", "바람 아래쪽!"], ["the white whale goes that way; look to windward, then; the better if the bitterer quarter.", "흰고래는 저쪽으로 가는구나. 그러니 바람 부는 쪽을 보아라."], ["But good bye, good bye, old mast-head!", "더 쓰라린 쪽일수록 더 좋으니. 그러나 잘 있거라, 잘 있거라, 늙은 돛대 꼭대기여!"], ["What’s this?—green?", "이게 무엇이냐? 초록빛인가?"], ["aye, tiny mosses in these warped cracks.", "그래, 이 뒤틀린 갈라진 틈마다 자그마한 이끼가 끼었구나."], ["No such green weather stains on Ahab’s head!", "에이해브의 머리에는 이런 초록빛 풍상의 얼룩이 없도다!"], ["There’s the difference now between man’s old age and matter’s.", "인간의 노년과 물질의 노년 사이에는 바로 이런 차이가 있구나."], ["But aye, old mast, we both grow old together; sound in our hulls, though, are we not, my ship?", "하나 그래, 늙은 돛대여, 우리는 함께 늙어 가는구나. 그래도 우리는 선체가 튼튼하지 않으냐, 내 배여?"], ["Aye, minus a leg, that’s all.", "그래, 다리 하나가 모자랄 뿐, 그뿐이다."], ["By heaven this dead wood has the better of my live flesh every way.", "맹세컨대 이 죽은 나무가 어느 모로 보나 내 살아 있는 살보다 낫구나."], ["I can’t compare with it; and I’ve known some ships made of dead trees outlast the lives of men made of the most vital stuff of vital fathers.", "나는 그것에 견줄 수가 없도다. 나는 죽은 나무로 만든 배가, 생기 넘치는 아비에게서 가장 생기 넘치는 재료로 빚어진 인간들의 목숨보다 더 오래 살아남는 것을 본 적이 있다."], ["What’s that he said?", "그가 무어라 했더라?"], ["he should still go before me, my pilot; and yet to be seen again?", "그는 여전히 내 앞을 먼저 가리라고, 내 길잡이가 되리라고 했지. 그런데도 다시 모습을 보이리라고?"], ["But where?", "하나 어디서?"], ["Will I have eyes at the bottom of the sea, supposing I descend those endless stairs?", "끝없는 계단을 내려가 바다 밑바닥에 닿는다 한들, 내게 그것을 볼 눈이 남아 있겠는가?"], ["and all night I’ve been sailing from him, wherever he did sink to.", "게다가 나는 밤새도록 놈이 가라앉은 그곳이 어디든 거기서 멀어지는 쪽으로 항해해 왔거늘."], ["Aye, aye, like many more thou told’st direful truth as touching thyself, O Parsee; but, Ahab, there thy shot fell short.", "그래, 그래, 다른 수많은 일들처럼 너 자신에 관해서도 너는 무시무시한 진실을 말했구나, 오 파시(Parsee)여. 하나 에이해브여, 그 점에서는 네 화살이 빗나가 짧게 떨어졌도다."], ["Good-bye, mast-head—keep a good eye upon the whale, the while I’m gone.", "잘 있거라, 돛대 꼭대기여, 내가 자리를 비운 동안 고래를 잘 지켜보아라."], ["We’ll talk to-morrow, nay, to-night, when the white whale lies down there, tied by head and tail.”", "내일 다시 이야기하자, 아니, 오늘 밤이다, 흰고래가 머리와 꼬리를 묶인 채 저 아래 누워 있을 그때.\""]], [["He gave the word; and still gazing round him, was steadily lowered through the cloven blue air to the deck.", "그는 명령을 내렸다. 그러고는 여전히 주위를 둘러보면서, 갈라진 푸른 대기를 가르며 천천히 갑판으로 내려졌다."]], [["In due time the boats were lowered; but as standing in his shallop’s stern, Ahab just hovered upon the point of the descent, he waved to the mate,—who held one of the tackle-ropes on deck—and bade him pause.", "때가 되어 보트들이 내려졌다. 그러나 작은 보트 고물에 서서 에이해브가 막 내려가려는 찰나에 멈칫하며, 그는 갑판에서 도르래 밧줄 하나를 붙들고 있던 일등항해사에게 손짓하며 잠시 멈추라고 일렀다. \"스타벅!\""]], [["“Starbuck!” “Sir?”", "\"네, 선장님?\""]], [["“For the third time my soul’s ship starts upon this voyage, Starbuck.”", "\"내 영혼의 배가 이 항해를 시작하는 것이 세 번째로구나, 스타벅.\""]], [["“Aye, sir, thou wilt have it so.”", "\"예, 선장님, 선장님께서 그리하시겠다니.\""]], [["“Some ships sail from their ports, and ever afterwards are missing, Starbuck!”", "\"어떤 배들은 제 항구에서 출항한 뒤로 영영 행방을 알 수 없게 되지, 스타벅!\""]], [["“Truth, sir: saddest truth.”", "\"맞습니다, 선장님. 더없이 슬픈 진실이지요.\""]], [["“Some men die at ebb tide; some at low water; some at the full of the flood;—and I feel now like a billow that’s all one crested comb, Starbuck.", "\"어떤 사람들은 썰물에 죽고, 어떤 이들은 물이 낮을 때 죽으며, 어떤 이들은 만조에 죽는다. 그런데 나는 지금, 온통 하나의 마루로 솟구쳐 부서지는 큰 물결처럼 느껴지는구나, 스타벅."], ["I am old;—shake hands with me, man.”", "나는 늙었다. 손을 다오, 친구여.\""]], [["Their hands met; their eyes fastened; Starbuck’s tears the glue.", "두 사람의 손이 마주 잡혔고, 두 사람의 눈이 서로에게 못 박혔다. 스타벅의 눈물이 그 둘을 붙드는 아교가 되었다."]], [["“Oh, my captain, my captain!—noble heart—go not—go not!—see, it’s a brave man that weeps; how great the agony of the persuasion then!”", "\"오, 선장님, 선장님! 고귀한 마음이시여, 가지 마십시오, 가지 마십시오! 보십시오, 우는 것은 용감한 사람입니다. 그러니 만류하려는 이 고뇌가 얼마나 크겠습니까!\""]], [["“Lower away!”—cried Ahab, tossing the mate’s arm from him.", "\"내려라!\" 에이해브는 일등항해사의 팔을 자신에게서 뿌리치며 외쳤다."], ["“Stand by the crew!”", "\"선원들 곁을 지켜라!\""]], [["In an instant the boat was pulling round close under the stern.", "순식간에 보트는 고물 바로 아래쪽으로 빙 돌아 노를 저었다."]], [["“The sharks!", "\"상어다!"], ["the sharks!”", "상어다!\""], ["cried a voice from the low cabin-window there; “O master, my master, come back!”", "저 아래 선실 창에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"오 주인님, 제 주인님, 돌아오세요!\""]], [["But Ahab heard nothing; for his own voice was high-lifted then; and the boat leaped on.", "그러나 에이해브는 아무것도 듣지 못했다. 그때 그 자신의 목소리가 드높이 솟구쳐 있었기 때문이다. 보트는 앞으로 튀어 나갔다."]], [["Yet the voice spake true; for scarce had he pushed from the ship, when numbers of sharks, seemingly rising from out the dark waters beneath the hull, maliciously snapped at the blades of the oars, every time they dipped in the water; and in this way accompanied the boat with their bites.", "그러나 그 목소리는 참된 것을 말한 셈이었다. 그가 배에서 막 밀려 나오자마자, 선체 아래 검은 물속에서 솟아오른 듯한 수많은 상어들이 노가 물에 잠길 때마다 악의에 차서 노깃을 향해 덥석덥석 물어뜯으며, 그렇게 물어뜯음으로 보트를 따라붙었던 것이다."], ["It is a thing not uncommonly happening to the whale-boats in those swarming seas; the sharks at times apparently following them in the same prescient way that vultures hover over the banners of marching regiments in the east.", "이는 그처럼 상어가 우글거리는 바다에서 고래잡이 보트에 그리 드물지 않게 일어나는 일이었다. 상어들은 때로, 동방에서 행군하는 연대의 깃발 위를 독수리들이 맴도는 것과 똑같이 앞일을 내다보는 듯한 방식으로 보트를 뒤따른다."], ["But these were the first sharks that had been observed by the Pequod since the White Whale had been first descried; and whether it was that Ahab’s crew were all such tiger-yellow barbarians, and therefore their flesh more musky to the senses of the sharks—a matter sometimes well known to affect them,—however it was, they seemed to follow that one boat without molesting the others.", "그러나 이놈들은 흰고래가 처음 발견된 이래 피쿼드호가 목격한 첫 상어들이었다. 그리고 에이해브의 선원들이 죄다 그처럼 호랑이 빛 누런 야만인들이라 그 살점이 상어의 감각에 더 사향내처럼 풍긴 까닭인지, 그것이 때로 상어들에게 영향을 미친다는 것은 익히 알려진 사실이거니와, 어쨌든 그 까닭이 무엇이든, 상어들은 다른 보트들은 건드리지 않고 오로지 그 한 척의 보트만 따라붙는 듯했다."]], [["“Heart of wrought steel!” murmured Starbuck gazing over the side, and following with his eyes the receding boat—“canst thou yet ring boldly to that sight?—lowering thy keel among ravening sharks, and followed by them, open-mouthed to the chase; and this the critical third day?—For when three days flow together in one continuous intense pursuit; be sure the first is the morning, the second the noon, and the third the evening and the end of that thing—be that end what it may. Oh! my God! what is this that shoots through me, and leaves me so deadly calm, yet expectant,—fixed at the top of a shudder! Future things swim before me, as in empty outlines and skeletons; all the past is somehow grown dim. Mary, girl! thou fadest in pale glories behind me; boy! I seem to see but thy eyes grown wondrous blue. Strangest problems of life seem clearing; but clouds sweep between—Is my journey’s end coming? My legs feel faint; like his who has footed it all day. Feel thy heart,—beats it yet? Stir thyself, Starbuck!—stave it off—move, move! speak aloud!—Mast-head there! See ye my boy’s hand on the hill?—Crazed;—aloft there!—keep thy keenest eye upon the boats:—mark well the whale!—Ho! again!—drive off that hawk! see! he pecks—he tears the vane”—pointing to the red flag flying at the main-truck—“Ha! he soars away with it!—Where’s the old man now? see’st thou that sight, oh Ahab!—shudder, shudder!”", "\"단련된 강철의 심장이여!\" 스타벅은 뱃전 너머를 응시하며, 멀어져 가는 보트를 눈으로 좇으며 중얼거렸다. \"그대는 저런 광경 앞에서도 여전히 용감하게 종소리를 울릴 수 있단 말인가? 게걸스러운 상어들 사이로 용골을 내리고, 입을 떡 벌린 채 추격에 나선 그놈들에게 뒤쫓기면서, 그것도 운명을 가르는 셋째 날에? 사흘이 하나의 끊임없고 격렬한 추격으로 흘러갈 때면, 분명 첫째 날은 아침이요, 둘째 날은 정오이며, 셋째 날은 저녁, 곧 그 일의 끝이리라. 그 끝이 무엇이든 간에. 오! 하느님! 내 안을 꿰뚫고 지나가며, 나를 이토록 죽은 듯 고요하면서도 무언가를 기다리게 하고, 전율의 정점에 못 박아 두는 이것은 대체 무엇입니까! 미래의 일들이 내 앞을 헤엄쳐 지나가니, 텅 빈 윤곽과 뼈대처럼 떠오릅니다. 지나간 모든 것은 어쩐지 흐릿해졌습니다. 메리, 내 아내여! 그대는 창백한 영광 속에 내 뒤로 희미하게 사라져 가는구나. 아들아! 네 눈동자만이 놀랍도록 푸르러진 채 보이는 듯하구나. 삶의 가장 기이한 수수께끼들이 풀려 나가는 듯하건만, 구름이 그 사이를 휩쓸고 지나간다. 내 여정의 끝이 다가오는가? 다리에 힘이 빠지는구나. 온종일 걸어 다닌 사람의 다리처럼. 네 심장을 짚어 보라. 아직도 뛰는가? 정신 차려라, 스타벅! 떨쳐 버려라, 움직여라, 움직여! 큰 소리로 외쳐라! 돛대 꼭대기에 있는 자여! 언덕 위에 내 아들의 손이 보이느냐? 미쳤구나. 위에 있는 자여! 가장 날카로운 눈으로 보트들을 지켜보아라. 고래를 잘 살펴라! 호! 또구나! 저 매를 쫓아 버려라! 보라! 놈이 쪼아 댄다, 깃발을 찢어 놓는다.\" 그는 큰돛대 꼭대기에 나부끼는 붉은 깃발을 가리켰다. \"하! 놈이 그것을 채어 높이 솟구쳐 날아가는구나! 그 노인은 지금 어디 있는가? 오 에이해브, 그대는 저 광경이 보이는가! 떨어라, 떨어라!\""]], [["The boats had not gone very far, when by a signal from the mast-heads—a downward pointed arm, Ahab knew that the whale had sounded; but intending to be near him at the next rising, he held on his way a little sideways from the vessel; the becharmed crew maintaining the profoundest silence, as the head-beat waves hammered and hammered against the opposing bow.", "보트들이 그리 멀리 나아가지 않았을 때, 돛대 꼭대기에서 보낸 신호로, 곧 아래쪽을 가리키는 팔을 보고서 에이해브는 고래가 잠수했음을 알았다. 그러나 놈이 다음에 떠오를 때 가까이 있으려는 뜻으로, 그는 배에서 약간 비껴난 방향으로 길을 이어 갔다. 정면으로 부딪쳐 오는 파도가 맞은편 뱃머리를 거듭거듭 두드려 대는 동안, 마법에 걸린 듯한 선원들은 더없이 깊은 침묵을 지켰다."]], [["“Drive, drive in your nails, oh ye waves!", "\"못을 박아라, 못을 박아라, 오 너희 파도들이여! 못대가리 끝까지 깊이 박아 넣어라!"], ["to their uttermost heads drive them in!", "그래 봐야 너희는 뚜껑 없는 것을 두드릴 뿐이다."], ["ye but strike a thing without a lid; and no coffin and no hearse can be mine:—and hemp only can kill me!", "내게는 어떤 관도, 어떤 영구차도 없으리라. 그리고 오직 삼노끈만이 나를 죽일 수 있으리라!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!”", "하!\""]], [["Suddenly the waters around them slowly swelled in broad circles; then quickly upheaved, as if sideways sliding from a submerged berg of ice, swiftly rising to the surface. A low rumbling sound was heard; a subterraneous hum; and then all held their breaths; as bedraggled with trailing ropes, and harpoons, and lances, a vast form shot lengthwise, but obliquely from the sea. Shrouded in a thin drooping veil of mist, it hovered for a moment in the rainbowed air; and then fell swamping back into the deep. Crushed thirty feet upwards, the waters flashed for an instant like heaps of fountains, then brokenly sank in a shower of flakes, leaving the circling surface creamed like new milk round the marble trunk of the whale.", "별안간 그들 주위의 물이 넓은 원을 그리며 천천히 부풀어 올랐다. 그러더니 마치 물에 잠긴 빙산에서 옆으로 미끄러져 나오듯 빠르게 솟구쳐 올라, 수면을 향해 날쌔게 떠올랐다. 낮게 우르릉거리는 소리가 들렸다. 땅속에서 울리는 듯한 웅웅거림이었다. 그러자 모두가 숨을 죽였다. 끌려오는 밧줄들과 작살들과 창들에 칭칭 휘감긴 채, 거대한 형체 하나가 바다에서 비스듬히, 그러나 몸을 길게 뻗으며 솟구쳐 나왔다. 얇게 드리운 안개의 면사포에 싸인 채, 그것은 무지개 빛으로 물든 대기 속에 한순간 떠 있다가, 다시 깊은 바다 속으로 곤두박질쳐 잠겨 들었다. 삼십 피트(약 9미터) 높이로 부서져 솟구친 물줄기는 한순간 분수 무더기처럼 번쩍이다가, 이내 자잘한 물보라가 되어 부서져 내렸다. 그리고 빙 둘러진 수면에는 고래의 대리석 같은 몸통을 에워싼 채 갓 짠 우유처럼 거품이 일었다."]], [["“Give way!”", "\"저어라!\" 에이해브가 노잡이들에게 외쳤고, 보트들은 공격을 향해 쏜살같이 앞으로 내달았다."], ["cried Ahab to the oarsmen, and the boats darted forward to the attack; but maddened by yesterday’s fresh irons that corroded in him, Moby Dick seemed combinedly possessed by all the angels that fell from heaven.", "그러나 어제 새로 박힌 작살들이 제 몸속에서 곪아 들어 광란에 빠진 모비 딕은, 하늘에서 떨어진 모든 천사들에게 한꺼번에 사로잡힌 듯했다. 그 넓고 흰 이마를 뒤덮은, 투명한 살갗 아래 용접된 듯 단단한 힘줄의 너른 층층이 한데 얽혀 있는 것처럼 보였다."], ["The wide tiers of welded tendons overspreading his broad white forehead, beneath the transparent skin, looked knitted together; as head on, he came churning his tail among the boats; and once more flailed them apart; spilling out the irons and lances from the two mates’ boats, and dashing in one side of the upper part of their bows, but leaving Ahab’s almost without a scar.", "놈은 정면으로 돌진하며 보트들 사이로 꼬리를 휘저어 댔고, 다시 한 번 그것들을 후려쳐 흩어 놓았다. 두 항해사의 보트에서 작살과 창들을 쏟아 버리고, 그 뱃머리 윗부분 한쪽을 쳐서 부수었으나, 에이해브의 보트만은 거의 상처 하나 입지 않은 채로 남겨 두었다."]], [["While Daggoo and Queequeg were stopping the strained planks; and as the whale swimming out from them, turned, and showed one entire flank as he shot by them again; at that moment a quick cry went up.", "대구(Daggoo)와 퀴케그(Queequeg)가 휜 널빤지의 틈을 틀어막고 있을 때, 그리고 고래가 그들에게서 헤엄쳐 빠져나가 몸을 돌려, 다시 그들 곁을 스쳐 지나며 옆구리 한쪽을 통째로 드러냈을 때, 바로 그 순간 다급한 외침이 솟구쳤다."], ["Lashed round and round to the fish’s back; pinioned in the turns upon turns in which, during the past night, the whale had reeled the involutions of the lines around him, the half torn body of the Parsee was seen; his sable raiment frayed to shreds; his distended eyes turned full upon old Ahab.", "물고기의 등에 칭칭 휘감겨, 지난밤 고래가 제 몸 둘레로 밧줄의 사리를 돌리고 또 돌려 감던 그 겹겹의 휘감김 속에 단단히 결박된 채로, 반쯤 찢긴 파시의 몸뚱이가 모습을 드러냈다. 그의 검은 옷자락은 너덜너덜 해어져 있었고, 부릅뜬 두 눈은 늙은 에이해브를 정면으로 향하고 있었다."]], [["The harpoon dropped from his hand.", "작살이 그의 손에서 떨어졌다."]], [["“Befooled, befooled!”—drawing in a long lean breath—“Aye, Parsee!", "\"속았구나, 속았어!\" 그는 길고 야윈 숨을 들이쉬었다."], ["I see thee again.—Aye, and thou goest before; and this, this then is the hearse that thou didst promise.", "\"그래, 파시여! 다시 너를 보는구나."], ["But I hold thee to the last letter of thy word.", "그래, 너는 앞서가는구나. 그리고 이것이, 바로 이것이 네가 약속했던 그 영구차로구나."], ["Where is the second hearse?", "하나 나는 네 말의 마지막 글자 한 자까지 붙들겠다. 두 번째 영구차는 어디 있느냐?"], ["Away, mates, to the ship!", "가라, 항해사들이여, 배로 가라! 저 보트들은 이제 쓸모가 없다."], ["those boats are useless now; repair them if ye can in time, and return to me; if not, Ahab is enough to die—Down, men!", "시간이 되거든 고칠 수 있으면 고쳐서 내게 돌아오라. 만일 그러지 못한다면, 죽는 데는 에이해브 하나로 족하다."], ["the first thing that but offers to jump from this boat I stand in, that thing I harpoon.", "앉아라, 사람들아! 내가 타고 선 이 보트에서 뛰어내리려 드는 첫 번째 놈이 있다면, 그놈을 내가 작살로 꿰어 버리겠다."], ["Ye are not other men, but my arms and my legs; and so obey me.—Where’s the whale?", "너희는 다른 사람들이 아니라 내 팔이요 내 다리다. 그러니 내게 복종하라."], ["gone down again?”", "고래는 어디 있느냐? 또 가라앉아 버렸느냐?\""]], [["But he looked too nigh the boat; for as if bent upon escaping with the corpse he bore, and as if the particular place of the last encounter had been but a stage in his leeward voyage, Moby Dick was now again steadily swimming forward; and had almost passed the ship,—which thus far had been sailing in the contrary direction to him, though for the present her headway had been stopped. He seemed swimming with his utmost velocity, and now only intent upon pursuing his own straight path in the sea.", "그러나 그는 보트와 너무 가까운 곳만 살피고 있었다. 마치 제가 짊어진 시신을 데리고 달아나기로 작정한 듯, 그리고 마치 마지막 격돌이 벌어진 그 장소가 다만 제 바람 아랫녘 항해의 한 단계에 지나지 않았다는 듯, 모비 딕은 이제 다시 꾸준히 앞으로 헤엄쳐 나아가고 있었다. 놈은 이미 배를 거의 다 지나친 참이었다. 배는 지금껏 놈과 정반대 방향으로 항해해 왔으나, 당장은 그 전진이 멈춰 있던 터였다. 놈은 온 힘을 다해 헤엄치는 듯했고, 이제는 오로지 바다에서 제 곧은 길을 좇는 데에만 골몰해 있었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Ahab,”", "에이해브 선장님,\""], ["cried Starbuck, “not too late is it, even now, the third day, to desist.", "스타벅이 외쳤다. \"지금이라도, 이 셋째 날에라도, 그만두기에 너무 늦은 것은 아닙니다."], ["See!", "보십시오!"], ["Moby Dick seeks thee not.", "모비 딕은 선장님을 찾지 않습니다."], ["It is thou, thou, that madly seekest him!”", "미친 듯이 놈을 찾는 것은 바로 선장님, 선장님이십니다!\""]], [["Setting sail to the rising wind, the lonely boat was swiftly impelled to leeward, by both oars and canvas.", "솟구쳐 오르는 바람에 돛을 펼친 채, 외로운 보트는 노와 돛 양쪽의 힘으로 바람 아랫녘을 향해 빠르게 밀려 나아갔다."], ["And at last when Ahab was sliding by the vessel, so near as plainly to distinguish Starbuck’s face as he leaned over the rail, he hailed him to turn the vessel about, and follow him, not too swiftly, at a judicious interval.", "그리고 마침내 에이해브가 배 곁을 미끄러져 지나며, 난간 너머로 몸을 기울인 스타벅의 얼굴을 또렷이 알아볼 만큼 가까워졌을 때, 그는 스타벅에게 배를 돌려, 너무 빠르지 않게 적당한 간격을 두고 자신을 뒤따르라고 소리쳐 일렀다."], ["Glancing upwards, he saw Tashtego, Queequeg, and Daggoo, eagerly mounting to the three mast-heads; while the oarsmen were rocking in the two staved boats which had but just been hoisted to the side, and were busily at work in repairing them.", "위를 흘끗 올려다보니, 태시테고(Tashtego)와 퀴케그와 대구가 세 돛대 꼭대기로 부지런히 올라가고 있었고, 노잡이들은 방금 막 뱃전으로 끌어올려진, 부서진 두 척의 보트 안에서 흔들거리며 그것들을 고치느라 분주히 일하고 있었다."], ["One after the other, through the port-holes, as he sped, he also caught flying glimpses of Stubb and Flask, busying themselves on deck among bundles of new irons and lances.", "그가 빠르게 지나가는 동안, 그는 또 현창들을 통해 하나하나, 스텁(Stubb)과 플래스크(Flask)가 갑판에서 새 작살과 창 다발들 사이에서 분주히 움직이는 모습을 스치듯 보았다."], ["As he saw all this; as he heard the hammers in the broken boats; far other hammers seemed driving a nail into his heart. But he rallied.", "이 모든 것을 보았을 때, 부서진 보트들에서 망치질 소리를 들었을 때, 전혀 다른 망치들이 그의 심장에 못을 박아 넣는 듯했다."], ["And now marking that the vane or flag was gone from the main-mast-head, he shouted to Tashtego, who had just gained that perch, to descend again for another flag, and a hammer and nails, and so nail it to the mast.", "그러나 그는 마음을 다잡았다. 그리고 이제 큰돛대 꼭대기에서 풍향기, 곧 깃발이 사라진 것을 알아채고, 그는 막 그 횃대에 오른 태시테고에게 다시 내려가 다른 깃발과 망치와 못을 가져다가 돛대에 단단히 박으라고 외쳤다."]], [["Whether fagged by the three days’ running chase, and the resistance to his swimming in the knotted hamper he bore; or whether it was some latent deceitfulness and malice in him: whichever was true, the White Whale’s way now began to abate, as it seemed, from the boat so rapidly nearing him once more; though indeed the whale’s last start had not been so long a one as before. And still as Ahab glided over the waves the unpitying sharks accompanied him; and so pertinaciously stuck to the boat; and so continually bit at the plying oars, that the blades became jagged and crunched, and left small splinters in the sea, at almost every dip.", "사흘에 걸친 끊임없는 추격에 지친 탓인지, 아니면 제가 짊어진 매듭진 짐이 헤엄을 가로막는 데 저항하느라 지쳐서인지, 그것도 아니면 놈 안에 숨은 어떤 기만과 악의 때문인지, 무엇이 참이든 간에, 흰고래의 나아가는 속도가 이제 누그러지기 시작하는 듯했다. 다시 한 번 그토록 빠르게 다가오는 보트로부터 멀어지려는 듯하면서도. 사실 고래가 마지막으로 내달은 거리는 앞서만큼 길지 않았다. 그리고 에이해브가 파도 위를 미끄러져 가는 내내, 무자비한 상어들이 그를 따라붙었다. 그놈들은 어찌나 끈질기게 보트에 들러붙고, 어찌나 줄곧 부지런히 젓는 노를 물어뜯었던지, 노깃이 들쭉날쭉 깨물려 부서지고, 거의 노를 담글 때마다 자잘한 나뭇조각이 바다에 떨어져 나갔다."]], [["“Heed them not!", "\"저놈들을 신경 쓰지 마라!"], ["those teeth but give new rowlocks to your oars.", "저 이빨들은 너희 노에 새 노받이를 달아 줄 뿐이다."], ["Pull on!", "저어라!"], ["’tis the better rest, the shark’s jaw than the yielding water.”", "무르기만 한 물보다는 상어의 턱이 차라리 더 든든한 받침이니.\""]], [["“But at every bite, sir, the thin blades grow smaller and smaller!”", "\"하지만 선장님, 물어뜯길 때마다 가느다란 노깃이 자꾸자꾸 작아집니다!\""]], [["“They will last long enough!", "\"오래 버틸 만큼은 버틸 게다! 저어라!"], ["pull on!—But who can tell”—he muttered—“whether these sharks swim to feast on the whale or on Ahab?—But pull on!", "하나 누가 알겠느냐,\" 그는 중얼거렸다."], ["Aye, all alive, now—we near him.", "\"이 상어들이 고래의 잔치를 위해 헤엄쳐 오는지, 아니면 에이해브의 잔치를 위해 오는지를? 하나 저어라!"], ["The helm!", "그래, 이제 모두 기운을 내라, 놈에게 다가가고 있다."], ["take the helm!", "키! 키를 잡게 해 다오!"], ["let me pass,”—and so saying two of the oarsmen helped him forward to the bows of the still flying boat.", "비켜라.\" 이렇게 말하며, 노잡이 둘이 그를 부축해 여전히 내달리는 보트의 뱃머리로 데려갔다."]], [["At length as the craft was cast to one side, and ran ranging along with the White Whale’s flank, he seemed strangely oblivious of its advance—as the whale sometimes will—and Ahab was fairly within the smoky mountain mist, which, thrown off from the whale’s spout, curled round his great, Monadnock hump; he was even thus close to him; when, with body arched back, and both arms lengthwise high-lifted to the poise, he darted his fierce iron, and his far fiercer curse into the hated whale. As both steel and curse sank to the socket, as if sucked into a morass, Moby Dick sideways writhed; spasmodically rolled his nigh flank against the bow, and, without staving a hole in it, so suddenly canted the boat over, that had it not been for the elevated part of the gunwale to which he then clung, Ahab would once more have been tossed into the sea. As it was, three of the oarsmen—who foreknew not the precise instant of the dart, and were therefore unprepared for its effects—these were flung out; but so fell, that, in an instant two of them clutched the gunwale again, and rising to its level on a combing wave, hurled themselves bodily inboard again; the third man helplessly dropping astern, but still afloat and swimming.", "마침내 보트가 한쪽으로 비껴 던져지듯 돌아, 흰고래의 옆구리와 나란히 줄지어 달리게 되었을 때, 놈은 이상하리만치 그 다가옴을 알아채지 못하는 듯했다. 고래가 이따금 그러하듯이. 그리고 에이해브는 어느새 그 자욱한 산안개 속에 완전히 들어가 있었다. 그것은 고래의 물줄기에서 뿜어져 나와, 모내드녹 산봉우리 같은 놈의 거대한 혹 둘레로 감돌고 있었다. 그는 그렇게나 놈에게 바싹 다가가 있었다. 그때, 몸을 뒤로 한껏 젖히고 두 팔을 길게 높이 치켜들어 겨눈 채로, 그는 그 사나운 작살을, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 더 사나운 저주를 미운 고래에게 던져 박았다. 강철도 저주도 마치 늪 속으로 빨려들 듯 박힌 자리 끝까지 가라앉자, 모비 딕은 옆구리를 비틀며 발작하듯 가까운 쪽 옆구리를 뱃머리에 들이박았다. 그리고 보트에 구멍 하나 내지 않은 채, 어찌나 갑작스레 보트를 기울여 엎었던지, 그 순간 에이해브가 매달려 있던 뱃전의 높직한 부분이 아니었다면, 그는 또 한 번 바다로 내동댕이쳐졌으리라. 실제로 노잡이 셋이 작살이 던져지는 정확한 순간을 미리 알지 못해 그 여파에 대비하지 못한 채 밖으로 튕겨 나갔다. 그러나 떨어지는 모양새가 그러하여, 그중 둘은 순식간에 다시 뱃전을 움켜쥐었고, 부서지는 물결을 타고 그 높이까지 솟아올라 제 몸을 통째로 다시 보트 안으로 던져 넣었다. 세 번째 사내는 속절없이 고물 쪽으로 떨어졌으나, 여전히 물 위에 떠서 헤엄치고 있었다."]], [["Almost simultaneously, with a mighty volition of ungraduated, instantaneous swiftness, the White Whale darted through the weltering sea.", "거의 동시에, 단계 없이 즉각적인 날쌤을 향한 거대한 의지로, 흰고래는 들끓는 바다를 가르며 쏜살같이 내달았다."], ["But when Ahab cried out to the steersman to take new turns with the line, and hold it so; and commanded the crew to turn round on their seats, and tow the boat up to the mark; the moment the treacherous line felt that double strain and tug, it snapped in the empty air!", "그러나 에이해브가 키잡이에게 밧줄을 새로 감아 단단히 붙들고 있으라고 소리치고, 선원들에게는 자리에서 몸을 돌려 보트를 목표 지점까지 끌어당기라고 명령한 순간, 그 믿을 수 없는 밧줄은 그 이중의 팽팽함과 당김을 느끼기가 무섭게, 텅 빈 허공에서 툭 끊어져 버렸다!"]], [["“What breaks in me?", "\"내 안에서 무엇이 끊어졌는가?"], ["Some sinew cracks!—’tis whole again; oars!", "어떤 힘줄이 갈라진다! 다시 멀쩡해졌구나."], ["oars!", "노!"], ["Burst in upon him!”", "노를 저어라! 놈을 향해 덮쳐라!\""]], [["Hearing the tremendous rush of the sea-crashing boat, the whale wheeled round to present his blank forehead at bay; but in that evolution, catching sight of the nearing black hull of the ship; seemingly seeing in it the source of all his persecutions; bethinking it—it may be—a larger and nobler foe; of a sudden, he bore down upon its advancing prow, smiting his jaws amid fiery showers of foam.", "바다를 부수며 내달려 오는 보트의 어마어마한 돌진 소리를 듣고, 고래는 빙 몸을 돌려 그 텅 빈 이마를 들이대며 맞설 태세를 취했다. 그러나 그렇게 몸을 돌리던 중, 다가오는 배의 검은 선체가 눈에 들어오자, 그 안에서 제가 받은 모든 박해의 근원을 본 듯, 그리고 어쩌면 그것을 더 크고 더 고귀한 적으로 여긴 듯, 별안간 놈은 그 다가오는 뱃머리를 향해 덮쳐 들며, 불꽃 같은 거품의 소나기 속에서 제 턱을 부딪쳐 댔다."]], [["Ahab staggered; his hand smote his forehead.", "에이해브가 비틀거렸다. 그의 손이 제 이마를 쳤다."], ["“I grow blind; hands!", "\"눈이 머는구나. 사람들아!"], ["stretch out before me that I may yet grope my way.", "내가 더듬어서라도 길을 찾을 수 있도록 내 앞으로 손을 뻗어 다오."], ["Is’t night?”", "밤인가?\""]], [["“The whale!", "\"고래입니다!"], ["The ship!”", "배입니다!\""], ["cried the cringing oarsmen.", "움츠러든 노잡이들이 외쳤다."]], [["“Oars!", "\"노!"], ["oars!", "노를 저어라!"], ["Slope downwards to thy depths, O sea, that ere it be for ever too late, Ahab may slide this last, last time upon his mark!", "오 바다여, 그대 깊은 곳을 향해 비스듬히 기울어라, 영영 너무 늦어 버리기 전에, 에이해브가 이 마지막, 마지막으로 제 표적을 향해 미끄러져 갈 수 있도록!"], ["I see: the ship!", "보인다, 배가! 배다!"], ["the ship! Dash on, my men!", "내달려라, 내 사람들아!"], ["Will ye not save my ship?”", "너희는 내 배를 구하지 않겠느냐?\""]], [["But as the oarsmen violently forced their boat through the sledge-hammering seas, the before whale-smitten bow-ends of two planks burst through, and in an instant almost, the temporarily disabled boat lay nearly level with the waves; its half-wading, splashing crew, trying hard to stop the gap and bale out the pouring water.", "그러나 노잡이들이 큰 망치로 두드려 대는 듯한 바다를 헤치며 보트를 거세게 몰아가던 그때, 앞서 고래에게 들이받힌 널빤지 두 장의 뱃머리 끝이 터져 부서졌고, 거의 순식간에 일시적으로 못 쓰게 된 보트는 파도와 거의 같은 높이로 가라앉았다. 반쯤 물에 잠긴 채 물을 튀기는 선원들은 그 갈라진 틈을 막고 쏟아져 들어오는 물을 퍼내려 안간힘을 썼다."]], [["Meantime, for that one beholding instant, Tashtego’s mast-head hammer remained suspended in his hand; and the red flag, half-wrapping him as with a plaid, then streamed itself straight out from him, as his own forward-flowing heart; while Starbuck and Stubb, standing upon the bowsprit beneath, caught sight of the down-coming monster just as soon as he.", "한편, 그 한순간 바라보는 동안, 태시테고의 돛대 꼭대기에서 망치를 든 손은 허공에 멈춰 있었다. 그리고 붉은 깃발은 격자무늬 천처럼 그의 몸을 반쯤 감쌌다가, 마치 앞으로 내달리는 그 자신의 심장처럼, 그에게서 곧장 뻗어 나부꼈다. 그 아래 제1사장 위에 서 있던 스타벅과 스텁은 그가 본 바로 그 순간, 내리덮쳐 오는 괴물을 보았다."]], [["“The whale, the whale!", "\"고래다, 고래!"], ["Up helm, up helm!", "키를 올려라, 키를 올려!"], ["Oh, all ye sweet powers of air, now hug me close!", "오, 그대 대기의 모든 정다운 힘들이여, 이제 나를 꼭 안아 다오!"], ["Let not Starbuck die, if die he must, in a woman’s fainting fit.", "스타벅이 죽어야 한다면, 여인의 기절 발작 속에서 죽게 하지는 마소서."], ["Up helm, I say—ye fools, the jaw!", "키를 올리라니까, 이 멍청이들아, 아가리!"], ["the jaw!", "아가리다!"], ["Is this the end of all my bursting prayers?", "이것이 터져 나오던 내 모든 기도의 끝이란 말인가?"], ["all my life-long fidelities?", "평생토록 지켜 온 내 모든 충성의 끝이란 말인가?"], ["Oh, Ahab, Ahab, lo, thy work.", "오, 에이해브, 에이해브, 보라, 그대가 한 일을."], ["Steady!", "침착하라!"], ["helmsman, steady.", "키잡이여, 침착하라."], ["Nay, nay!", "아니, 아니다!"], ["Up helm again!", "다시 키를 올려라!"], ["He turns to meet us!", "놈이 우리를 맞으러 몸을 돌린다!"], ["Oh, his unappeasable brow drives on towards one, whose duty tells him he cannot depart.", "오, 놈의 달랠 길 없는 이마가 한 사람을 향해 돌진해 오는구나, 제 의무가 떠날 수 없다고 일러 주는 한 사람을."], ["My God, stand by me now!”", "하느님, 이제 제 곁에 서 주소서!\""]], [["“Stand not by me, but stand under me, whoever you are that will now help Stubb; for Stubb, too, sticks here.", "\"내 곁에 서지 말고, 내 아래에 서라, 그대가 누구든 이제 스텁을 도우려는 자라면. 스텁 또한 여기 들러붙어 있으니."], ["I grin at thee, thou grinning whale!", "나는 너를 보고 씩 웃노라, 이 씩 웃는 고래야!"], ["Who ever helped Stubb, or kept Stubb awake, but Stubb’s own unwinking eye?", "일찍이 스텁의 깜박이지 않는 제 눈 말고 그 누가 스텁을 도왔으며, 그 누가 스텁을 깨어 있게 했더란 말이냐?"], ["And now poor Stubb goes to bed upon a mattrass that is all too soft; would it were stuffed with brushwood!", "그리고 이제 가련한 스텁은 너무나 푹신한 매트리스 위에 잠자리에 드는구나. 차라리 잔솔가지로 채워졌으면 좋으련만!"], ["I grin at thee, thou grinning whale!", "나는 너를 보고 씩 웃노라, 이 씩 웃는 고래야!"], ["Look ye, sun, moon, and stars!", "보라, 해여, 달이여, 그리고 별들이여!"], ["I call ye assassins of as good a fellow as ever spouted up his ghost.", "나는 너희를 일찍이 제 혼을 뿜어 올린 그 어떤 사내 못지않게 훌륭한 친구의 살인자라 부르노라."], ["For all that, I would yet ring glasses with ye, would ye but hand the cup!", "그럼에도 너희가 잔을 건네 주기만 한다면, 나는 너희와 잔을 맞대고 짠 소리를 울리고 싶구나!"], ["Oh, oh!", "오, 오!"], ["oh, oh!", "오, 오!"], ["thou grinning whale, but there’ll be plenty of gulping soon!", "이 씩 웃는 고래야, 하나 머잖아 실컷 꿀꺽거릴 일이 넘쳐 나겠구나!"], ["Why fly ye not, O Ahab!", "어찌하여 달아나지 않는가, 오 에이해브여!"], ["For me, off shoes and jacket to it; let Stubb die in his drawers!", "나로 말하자면, 신발과 웃옷을 벗어 던지리라. 스텁은 속바지 차림으로 죽게 하라!"], ["A most mouldy and over salted death, though;—cherries!", "그래도 곰팡내 나고 소금기 지나친 죽음이로다."], ["cherries!", "버찌!"], ["cherries!", "버찌! 버찌!"], ["Oh, Flask, for one red cherry ere we die!”", "오, 플래스크, 죽기 전에 빨간 버찌 한 알이라도!\""]], [["“Cherries?", "\"버찌?"], ["I only wish that we were where they grow.", "나는 다만 우리가 그것이 자라는 곳에 있었으면 할 따름이다."], ["Oh, Stubb, I hope my poor mother’s drawn my part-pay ere this; if not, few coppers will now come to her, for the voyage is up.”", "오, 스텁, 나는 우리 가엾은 어머니가 지금쯤 내 봉급 일부를 타 가셨기를 바란다. 그러지 못했다면 이제 어머니께 돌아갈 동전 몇 푼도 변변치 않으리라, 항해가 끝장났으니.\""]], [["From the ship’s bows, nearly all the seamen now hung inactive; hammers, bits of plank, lances, and harpoons, mechanically retained in their hands, just as they had darted from their various employments; all their enchanted eyes intent upon the whale, which from side to side strangely vibrating his predestinating head, sent a broad band of overspreading semicircular foam before him as he rushed.", "배의 뱃머리에서, 이제 거의 모든 선원이 손을 놓은 채 매달려 있었다. 망치며, 널빤지 조각이며, 창이며, 작살이 저마다의 일거리에서 막 던지듯 멈춰 선 그대로, 그들의 손에 기계적으로 쥐어진 채였다."], ["Retribution, swift vengeance, eternal malice were in his whole aspect, and spite of all that mortal man could do, the solid white buttress of his forehead smote the ship’s starboard bow, till men and timbers reeled.", "마법에 걸린 그들의 눈은 모조리 고래를 향해 못 박혀 있었다. 놈은 운명을 예정하는 그 머리를 좌우로 기이하게 진동시키며, 돌진하는 제 앞으로 반원을 그리며 너르게 퍼지는 거품의 띠를 보내고 있었다."], ["Some fell flat upon their faces.", "응징과 신속한 복수와 영원한 악의가 놈의 온 모습에 깃들어 있었고, 죽어야 할 인간이 무엇을 할 수 있든 그 모든 것을 비웃듯, 놈의 이마의 단단한 흰 버팀벽이 배의 우현 뱃머리를 들이받았다."], ["Like dislodged trucks, the heads of the harpooneers aloft shook on their bull-like necks.", "그 충격에 사람들도 목재들도 휘청거렸다. 더러는 얼굴을 박고 납작하게 쓰러졌다."], ["Through the breach, they heard the waters pour, as mountain torrents down a flume.", "제자리에서 빠져나온 도르래처럼, 높은 곳의 작살잡이들의 머리가 황소 같은 목 위에서 흔들렸다. 그 갈라진 틈으로, 그들은 산골짜기의 급류가 도랑을 따라 쏟아지듯 물이 밀려드는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“The ship!", "\"배다!"], ["The hearse!—the second hearse!”", "영구차로구나! 두 번째 영구차로구나!\""], ["cried Ahab from the boat; “its wood could only be American!”", "에이해브가 보트에서 외쳤다. \"그 나무가 미국산이 아닐 리 없으니!\""]], [["Diving beneath the settling ship, the whale ran quivering along its keel; but turning under water, swiftly shot to the surface again, far off the other bow, but within a few yards of Ahab’s boat, where, for a time, he lay quiescent.", "가라앉는 배 밑으로 자맥질하며, 고래는 그 용골을 따라 부르르 떨며 내달렸다. 그러나 물속에서 몸을 돌려, 다시 빠르게 수면으로 솟구쳐 올랐다. 반대편 뱃머리에서 멀찍이, 그러나 에이해브의 보트에서 몇 야드 안 되는 곳이었다. 거기서 놈은 한동안 잠잠히 누워 있었다."]], [["“I turn my body from the sun.", "\"나는 해를 등지고 몸을 돌린다."], ["What ho, Tashtego!", "이봐, 태시테고!"], ["let me hear thy hammer.", "네 망치 소리를 들려 다오."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["ye three unsurrendered spires of mine; thou uncracked keel; and only god-bullied hull; thou firm deck, and haughty helm, and Pole-pointed prow,—death-glorious ship!", "그대 항복하지 않은 나의 세 첨탑이여, 갈라지지 않은 용골이여, 오직 신에게만 들이받힌 선체여, 그대 굳건한 갑판과 도도한 키와 북극을 가리키는 뱃머리여, 죽음으로 영광스러운 배여!"], ["must ye then perish, and without me?", "그렇다면 그대는 나도 없이 스러져야 한단 말인가?"], ["Am I cut off from the last fond pride of meanest shipwrecked captains?", "나는 가장 보잘것없는 난파선 선장들에게 마지막으로 허락된 그 정겨운 자랑마저 빼앗긴 것인가?"], ["Oh, lonely death on lonely life!", "오, 외로운 삶 위의 외로운 죽음이여!"], ["Oh, now I feel my topmost greatness lies in my topmost grief.", "오, 이제 나는 깨닫는다, 내 가장 높은 위대함이 내 가장 깊은 비탄 속에 있음을."], ["Ho, ho!", "호, 호!"], ["from all your furthest bounds, pour ye now in, ye bold billows of my whole foregone life, and top this one piled comber of my death!", "너희 가장 먼 끝에서부터, 이제 밀려들어라, 내 지나온 온 생애의 그대 담대한 큰 물결들이여, 그리고 내 죽음의 이 한 겹 쌓인 마루를 그 위에 얹어 다오!"], ["Towards thee I roll, thou all-destroying but unconquering whale; to the last I grapple with thee; from hell’s heart I stab at thee; for hate’s sake I spit my last breath at thee.", "너를 향해 나는 굴러간다, 모든 것을 파괴하지만 정복당하지는 않는 고래여. 마지막까지 나는 너와 맞붙어 싸운다."], ["Sink all coffins and all hearses to one common pool!", "지옥의 심장으로부터 나는 너를 찌른다. 증오를 위하여 나는 너를 향해 내 마지막 숨을 뱉노라."], ["and since neither can be mine, let me then tow to pieces, while still chasing thee, though tied to thee, thou damned whale!", "모든 관과 모든 영구차를 하나의 공동 웅덩이에 가라앉혀 버려라! 그리고 그 어느 것도 내 것이 될 수 없으니, 나로 하여금 너를 뒤쫓으며 갈가리 찢기게 하라, 너에게 묶인 채로라도, 이 저주받은 고래야!"], ["Thus, I give up the spear!”", "이렇게, 나는 작살을 내던진다!\""]], [["The harpoon was darted; the stricken whale flew forward; with igniting velocity the line ran through the grooves;—ran foul. Ahab stooped to clear it; he did clear it; but the flying turn caught him round the neck, and voicelessly as Turkish mutes bowstring their victim, he was shot out of the boat, ere the crew knew he was gone. Next instant, the heavy eye-splice in the rope’s final end flew out of the stark-empty tub, knocked down an oarsman, and smiting the sea, disappeared in its depths.", "작살이 던져졌다. 작살에 맞은 고래는 앞으로 내달았다. 불붙듯 빠른 속도로 밧줄이 홈을 따라 풀려 나갔다. 그러다 얽혀 버렸다. 에이해브가 그것을 풀려고 몸을 굽혔다. 그는 정말로 풀어냈다. 그러나 날아오르던 밧줄 고리가 그의 목을 휘감았고, 터키의 벙어리 형리들이 제 희생자의 목을 활시위로 조르듯 소리 없이, 그는 선원들이 그가 사라진 것을 알아채기도 전에 보트 밖으로 튕겨 나갔다. 다음 순간, 밧줄의 맨 끝에 무겁게 이어 붙인 고리매듭이 텅 빈 통에서 휙 튀어나와 노잡이 하나를 쓰러뜨리고는, 바다를 후려치며 그 깊은 곳으로 사라져 버렸다."]], [["For an instant, the tranced boat’s crew stood still; then turned.", "한순간, 넋이 나간 보트의 선원들은 꼼짝없이 서 있었다. 그러더니 몸을 돌렸다."], ["“The ship?", "\"배는?"], ["Great God, where is the ship?”", "위대한 하느님이시여, 배는 어디 있습니까?\" 머잖아 그들은 흐릿하고 어지러운 매질 너머로, 가스 같은 신기루 속에서처럼 비스듬히 스러져 가는 배의 환영을 보았다."], ["Soon they through dim, bewildering mediums saw her sidelong fading phantom, as in the gaseous Fata Morgana; only the uppermost masts out of water; while fixed by infatuation, or fidelity, or fate, to their once lofty perches, the pagan harpooneers still maintained their sinking lookouts on the sea.", "가장 높은 돛대들만이 물 밖으로 솟아 있을 뿐이었다. 한때 드높았던 제 횃대에 미혹 때문인지, 충성 때문인지, 운명 때문인지 못 박힌 채, 이교도 작살잡이들은 여전히 가라앉아 가는 망보기 자리를 바다 위에 지키고 있었다."], ["And now, concentric circles seized the lone boat itself, and all its crew, and each floating oar, and every lance-pole, and spinning, animate and inanimate, all round and round in one vortex, carried the smallest chip of the Pequod out of sight.", "그리고 이제, 동심원들이 그 외로운 보트 그 자체를, 그리고 그 모든 선원을, 떠 있는 노 하나하나를, 창대 하나하나를 움켜쥐었다. 그러고는 산 것이든 죽은 것이든 모조리 빙글빙글 돌리며, 하나의 소용돌이 속에서 빙빙 돌려, 피쿼드호의 가장 작은 나뭇조각 하나까지 시야 밖으로 휩쓸어 가 버렸다."]], [["But as the last whelmings intermixingly poured themselves over the sunken head of the Indian at the mainmast, leaving a few inches of the erect spar yet visible, together with long streaming yards of the flag, which calmly undulated, with ironical coincidings, over the destroying billows they almost touched;—at that instant, a red arm and a hammer hovered backwardly uplifted in the open air, in the act of nailing the flag faster and yet faster to the subsiding spar. A sky-hawk that tauntingly had followed the main-truck downwards from its natural home among the stars, pecking at the flag, and incommoding Tashtego there; this bird now chanced to intercept its broad fluttering wing between the hammer and the wood; and simultaneously feeling that etherial thrill, the submerged savage beneath, in his death-gasp, kept his hammer frozen there; and so the bird of heaven, with archangelic shrieks, and his imperial beak thrust upwards, and his whole captive form folded in the flag of Ahab, went down with his ship, which, like Satan, would not sink to hell till she had dragged a living part of heaven along with her, and helmeted herself with it.", "그러나 마지막으로 덮쳐드는 물결들이 한데 뒤섞여, 큰돛대에 매달린 인디언(Indian)의 가라앉은 머리 위로 쏟아져 내려, 곧추선 활대의 몇 인치와 함께 길게 나부끼는 몇 야드의 깃발자락만 남겨 두었을 때, 그 깃발이 거의 닿을 듯한 그 파괴의 큰 물결들 위로 비웃듯 우연한 박자로 잔잔히 너울거리고 있을 때, 바로 그 순간, 붉은 팔 하나와 망치 하나가 깃발을 가라앉아 가는 활대에 더 빠르게, 또 더욱더 빠르게 박으려는 동작으로, 탁 트인 허공 속에 뒤로 젖혀진 채 치켜올려져 있었다. 별들 사이의 제 본향에서부터 큰돛대 꼭대기를 따라 비웃듯 내려와 깃발을 쪼아 대며 거기 태시테고를 괴롭히던 하늘의 매 한 마리가, 마침 이 순간 그 너른 퍼덕이는 날개를 망치와 나무 사이에 끼워 넣었다. 그리고 그 천상의 전율을 느끼는 것과 동시에, 그 아래 물에 잠긴 야만인은 죽음의 헐떡임 속에서 제 망치를 그 자리에 얼어붙은 듯 멈춰 둔 채 있었다. 그리하여 그 하늘의 새는, 대천사 같은 비명을 지르며, 제왕다운 부리를 위로 치켜든 채, 사로잡힌 온몸이 에이해브의 깃발에 휘감긴 채, 제 배와 함께 가라앉아 갔다. 그 배는, 사탄이 그러하듯, 살아 있는 하늘의 한 자락을 함께 끌고 내려가 그것으로 제 머리에 투구를 씌우기 전에는 결코 지옥으로 가라앉으려 하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Now small fowls flew screaming over the yet yawning gulf; a sullen white surf beat against its steep sides; then all collapsed, and the great shroud of the sea rolled on as it rolled five thousand years ago.", "이제 작은 새들이 아직 입을 벌리고 있는 심연 위로 비명을 지르며 날아다녔다. 음울한 흰 파도가 그 가파른 옆구리를 두드렸다. 그러고는 모든 것이 무너져 내렸고, 바다의 거대한 수의는 오천 년 전에 굽이쳤던 그대로 다시 굽이쳐 흘렀다."]], [["Epilogue", "맺음말"]], [["The drama’s done.", "\"나만 홀로 피하였으므로 당신에게 알리려고 왔나이다.\""], ["Why then here does any one step forth?—Because one did survive the wreck.", "욥기."]], [["It so chanced, that after the Parsee’s disappearance, I was he whom the Fates ordained to take the place of Ahab’s bowsman, when that bowsman assumed the vacant post; the same, who, when on the last day the three men were tossed from out of the rocking boat, was dropped astern.", "연극은 끝났다. 그렇다면 어찌하여 여기 누군가가 앞으로 나선단 말인가?"], ["So, floating on the margin of the ensuing scene, and in full sight of it, when the halfspent suction of the sunk ship reached me, I was then, but slowly, drawn towards the closing vortex.", "한 사람이 그 난파에서 살아남았기 때문이다. 마침 파시가 사라진 뒤로, 그 뱃머리잡이가 비어 있던 자리를 맡게 되었을 때, 에이해브의 뱃머리잡이 자리를 대신하도록 운명의 여신들이 정한 자가 바로 나였다."], ["When I reached it, it had subsided to a creamy pool.", "마지막 날에 세 사람이 흔들리는 보트에서 튕겨 나갔을 때, 고물 쪽으로 떨어졌던 바로 그 사람이다."], ["Round and round, then, and ever contracting towards the button-like black bubble at the axis of that slowly wheeling circle, like another Ixion I did revolve.", "그리하여 이어지는 광경의 가장자리에 떠서, 그것을 온전히 눈앞에 둔 채로, 가라앉은 배가 반쯤 다하여 빨아들이는 힘이 내게 미쳐 왔을 때, 나는 그제야, 그러나 천천히, 닫혀 가는 소용돌이를 향해 끌려들었다. 내가 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 그것은 이미 우윳빛 웅덩이로 잦아들어 있었다."], ["Till, gaining that vital centre, the black bubble upward burst; and now, liberated by reason of its cunning spring, and, owing to its great buoyancy, rising with great force, the coffin life-buoy shot lengthwise from the sea, fell over, and floated by my side.", "그리하여 나는 빙글빙글, 점점 더 그 천천히 도는 원의 축에 자리한 단추처럼 검은 거품을 향해 좁혀 들며, 또 하나의 익시온처럼 돌고 또 돌았다. 마침내 그 생명의 중심에 다다랐을 때, 검은 거품이 위로 터져 올랐다."], ["Buoyed up by that coffin, for almost one whole day and night, I floated on a soft and dirgelike main.", "그리고 이제, 그 교묘한 용수철 장치 덕분에 풀려나, 그 엄청난 부력으로 인해 거센 힘으로 솟구쳐 오른 관 모양의 구명부표가 몸을 길게 뻗으며 바다에서 튀어 올라, 옆으로 넘어지더니 내 곁에 둥실 떠올랐다. 그 관에 떠받쳐진 채로, 거의 꼬박 하루 낮과 밤 동안, 나는 부드럽고 만가(輓歌) 같은 바다 위를 떠다녔다."], ["The unharming sharks, they glided by as if with padlocks on their mouths; the savage sea-hawks sailed with sheathed beaks.", "해를 끼치지 않는 상어들은 마치 제 아가리에 자물쇠라도 채운 듯 곁을 미끄러져 지나갔다. 사나운 바다매들은 부리를 칼집에 넣은 듯 날아다녔다."], ["On the second day, a sail drew near, nearer, and picked me up at last.", "둘째 날에, 돛배 하나가 가까이, 더 가까이 다가오더니, 마침내 나를 건져 올렸다."], ["It was the devious-cruising Rachel, that in her retracing search after her missing children, only found another orphan.", "그것은 이리저리 떠돌며 수색하던 레이철호였으니, 잃어버린 제 자식들을 되짚어 찾아 헤매던 끝에, 또 다른 고아 하나만을 찾아낸 것이었다."]]];
